isticum.org/sth1050.html#30986)\n\nPraeterea, gratia inclinat creaturam rationalem in Deum. Si igitur Angelus in gratia creatus fuisset nullus Angelus fuisset a Deo aversus.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30987)\n\nPraeterea, gratia medium est inter naturam et gloriam. Sed Angeli non fuerunt beati in sua creatione. Ergo videtur quod nec etiam creati sint in gratia, sed primo in natura tantum; postea autem adepti sunt gratiam; et ultimo facti sunt beati.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30988)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XII de Civ. Dei, *bonam voluntatem quis fecit in Angelis, nisi ille qui eos cum sua voluntate, idest cum amore casto quo illi adhaerent, creavit, simul in eis condens naturam et largiens gratiam?*\n\n[I q. 62 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30989)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, quamvis super hoc sint diversae opiniones, quibusdam dicentibus quod creati sunt Angeli in naturalibus tantum, aliis vero quod sunt creati in gratia; hoc tamen probabilius videtur tenendum, et magis dictis sanctorum consonum est, quod fuerunt creati in gratia gratum faciente. Sic enim videmus quod omnia quae processu temporis per opus divinae providentiae, creatura sub Deo operante, sunt producta, in prima rerum conditione producta sunt secundum quasdam seminales rationes, ut Augustinus dicit, super Gen. ad Litt.; sicut arbores et animalia et alia huiusmodi. Manifestum est autem quod gratia gratum faciens hoc modo comparatur ad beatitudinem, sicut ratio seminalis in natura ad effectum naturalem, unde I Ioan. III, gratia semen Dei nominatur. Sicut igitur, secundum opinionem Augustini, ponitur quod statim in prima creatione corporalis creaturae inditae sunt ei seminales rationes omnium naturalium effectuum, ita statim a principio sunt Angeli creati in gratia.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30990)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod informitas illa Angeli potest intelligi vel per comparationem ad formationem gloriae, et sic praecessit tempore informitas formationem. Vel per comparationem ad formationem gratiae, et sic non praecessit ordine temporis, sed ordine naturae; sicut etiam de formatione corporali Augustinus ponit.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30991)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod omnis forma inclinat suum subiectum secundum modum naturae eius. Modus autem naturalis intellectualis naturae est, ut libere feratur in ea quae vult. Et ideo inclinatio gratiae non imponit necessitatem, sed habens gratiam potest ea non uti, et peccare.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30992)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, quamvis gratia sit medium inter naturam et gloriam ordine naturae tamen ordine temporis in natura creata non debuit simul esse gloria cum natura, quia est finis operationis ipsius naturae per gratiam adiutae. Gratia autem non se habet ut finis operationis, quia non est ex operibus; sed ut principium bene operandi. Et ideo statim cum natura gratiam dare conveniens fuit.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 62 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30993)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus beatus suam beatitudinem non meruerit. Meritum enim est ex difficultate actus meritorii. Sed nullam difficultatem Angelus habuit ad bene operandum. Ergo bona operatio non fuit ei meritoria.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30994)\n\nPraeterea, naturalibus non meremur. Sed naturale fuit Angelo quod converteretur ad Deum. Ergo per hoc non meruit beatitudinem.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30995)\n\nPraeterea, si Angelus beatus beatitudinem suam meruit, aut ergo antequam eam haberet, aut post. Sed non ante, quia, ut multis videtur, ante non habuit gratiam, sine qua nullum est meritum. Nec etiam post, quia sic etiam modo mereretur, quod videtur esse falsum, quia sic minor Angelus merendo ad superioris Angeli gradum posset pertingere, et non essent stabiles distinctiones graduum gratiae; quod est inconveniens. Non ergo Angelus beatus suam beatitudinem meruit.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30996)\n\nSed contra, Apoc. XXI, dicitur quod mensura Angeli, in illa caelesti Ierusalem, est mensura hominis. Sed homo ad beatitudinem pertingere non potest nisi per meritum. Ergo neque Angelus.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30997)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod soli Deo beatitudo perfecta est naturalis quia idem est sibi esse et beatum esse. Cuiuslibet autem creaturae esse beatum non est natura, sed ultimus finis. Quaelibet autem res ad ultimum finem per suam operationem pertingit. Quae quidem operatio in finem ducens, vel est factiva finis, quando finis non excedit virtutem eius quod operatur propter finem, sicut medicatio est factiva sanitatis, vel est meritoria finis, quando finis excedit virtutem operantis propter finem, unde expectatur finis ex dono alterius. Beatitudo autem ultima excedit et naturam angelicam et humanam, ut ex dictis patet. Unde relinquitur quod tam homo quam Angelus suam beatitudinem meruerit. Et si quidem Angelus in gratia creatus fuit, sine qua nullum est meritum, absque difficultate dicere possumus quod suam beatitudinem meruerit. Et similiter si quis diceret quod qualitercumque gratiam habuerit antequam gloriam. Si vero gratiam non habuit antequam esset beatus, sic oportet dicere quod beatitudinem absque merito habuit, sicut nos gratiam. Quod tamen est contra rationem beatitudinis, quae habet rationem finis, et est praemium virtutis, ut etiam philosophus dicit, in I Ethic. Vel oportet dicere quod Angeli merentur beatitudinem per ea quae iam beati operantur in divinis ministeriis, ut alii dixerunt. Quod tamen est contra rationem meriti, nam meritum habet rationem viae ad finem, ei autem qui iam est in termino, non convenit moveri ad terminum; et sic nullus meretur quod iam habet. Vel oportet dicere quod unus et idem actus conversionis in Deum, inquantum est ex libero arbitrio, est meritorius; et inquantum pertingit ad finem, est fruitio beata. Sed nec hoc etiam videtur esse conveniens, quia liberum arbitrium non est sufficiens causa meriti unde actus non potest esse meritorius secundum quod est ex libero arbitrio, nisi inquantum est gratia informatus; non autem simul potest informari gratia imperfecta, quae est principium merendi, et gratia perfecta, quae est principium fruendi. Unde non videtur esse possibile quod simul fruatur, et suam fruitionem mereatur. Et ideo melius dicendum est quod gratiam habuit Angelus antequam esset beatus, per quam beatitudinem meruit.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30998)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod difficultas bene operandi non est in Angelis ex aliqua contrarietate, vel impedimento naturalis virtutis; sed ex hoc quod opus aliquod bonum est supra virtutem naturae.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30999)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod conversione naturali Angelus non meruit beatitudinem, sed conversione caritatis, quae est per gratiam.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31000)\n\nAd tertium patet responsio ex dictis.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 62 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31001)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non statim post unum actum meritorium beatitudinem habuerit. Difficilius enim est homini bene operari quam Angelo. Sed homo non praemiatur statim post unum actum. Ergo neque Angelus.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31002)\n\nPraeterea, Angelus statim in principio suae creationis, et in instanti, actum aliquem habere potuit, cum etiam corpora naturalia in ipso instanti suae creationis moveri incipiant, et si motus corporis in instanti esse posset, sicut opera intellectus et voluntatis, in primo instanti suae generationis motum haberent. Si ergo Angelus per unum motum suae voluntatis beatitudinem meruit, in primo instanti suae creationis meruit beatitudinem. Si ergo eorum beatitudo non retardatur, statim in primo instanti fuerunt beati.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31003)\n\nPraeterea, inter multum distantia oportet esse multa media. Sed status beatitudinis Angelorum multum distat a statu naturae eorum, medium autem inter utrumque est meritum. Oportuit igitur quod per multa media Angelus ad beatitudinem perveniret.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31004)\n\nSed contra, anima hominis et Angelus similiter ad beatitudinem ordinantur, unde sanctis promittitur aequalitas Angelorum Luc. XX. Sed anima a corpore separata, si habeat meritum beatitudinis, statim beatitudinem consequitur, nisi aliud sit impedimentum. Ergo pari ratione et Angelus. Sed statim in primo actu caritatis habuit meritum beatitudinis. Ergo, cum in eo non esset aliquod impedimentum, statim ad beatitudinem pervenit per solum unum actum meritorium.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31005)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Angelus post primum actum caritatis quo beatitudinem meruit, statim beatus fuit. Cuius ratio est, quia gratia perficit naturam secundum modum naturae, sicut et omnis perfectio recipitur in perfectibili secundum modum eius. Est autem hoc proprium naturae angelicae, quod naturalem perfectionem non per discursum acquirat, sed statim per naturam habeat, sicut supra ostensum est. Sicut autem ex sua natura Angelus habet ordinem ad perfectionem naturalem, ita ex merito habet ordinem ad gloriam. Et ita statim post meritum in Angelo fuit beatitudo consecuta. Meritum autem beatitudinis, non solum in Angelo, sed etiam in homine esse potest per unicum actum, quia quolibet actu caritate informato homo beatitudinem meretur. Unde relinquitur quod statim post unum actum caritate informatum, Angelus beatus fuit.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31006)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod homo secundum suam naturam non statim natus est ultimam perfectionem adipisci, sicut Angelus. Et ideo homini longior via data est ad merendum beatitudinem, quam Angelo.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31007)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Angelus est supra tempus rerum corporalium, unde instantia diversa in his quae ad Angelos pertinent, non accipiuntur nisi secundum successionem in ipsorum actibus. Non autem potuit simul in eis esse actus meritorius beatitudinis, et actus beatitudinis, qui est fruitio; cum unus sit gratiae imperfectae, et alius gratiae consummatae. Unde relinquitur quod oportet diversa instantia accipi, in quorum uno meruerit beatitudinem, et in alio fuerit beatus.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31008)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod de natura Angeli est, quod statim suam perfectionem consequatur ad quam ordinatur. Et ideo non requiritur nisi unus actus meritorius; qui ea ratione medium dici potest, quia secundum ipsum Angelus ad beatitudinem ordinatur.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 62 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31009)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli non sint consecuti gratiam et gloriam secundum quantitatem suorum naturalium. Gratia enim ex mera Dei voluntate datur. Ergo et quantitas gratiae dependet ex voluntate Dei, et non ex quantitate naturalium.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31010)\n\nPraeterea, magis propinquum videtur ad gratiam actus humanus quam natura, quia actus humanus praeparatorius est ad gratiam. Sed gratia non est ex operibus, ut dicitur Rom. XI. Multo igitur minus quantitas gratiae in Angelis est secundum quantitatem naturalium.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31011)\n\nPraeterea, homo et Angelus pariter ordinantur ad beatitudinem vel gratiam. Sed homini non datur plus de gratia secundum gradum naturalium. Ergo nec Angelo.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31012)\n\nSed contra est quod Magister dicit, III dist. II Sent., quod *Angeli qui natura magis subtiles, et sapientia amplius perspicaces creati sunt, hi etiam maioribus gratiae muneribus praediti sunt*.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31013)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod rationabile est quod secundum gradum naturalium Angelis data sint dona gratiarum et perfectio beatitudinis. Cuius quidem ratio ex duobus accipi potest. Primo quidem ex parte ipsius Dei, qui per ordinem suae sapientiae diversos gradus in angelica natura constituit. Sicut autem natura angelica facta est a Deo ad gratiam et beatitudinem consequendam, ita etiam gradus naturae angelicae ad diversos gradus gratiae et gloriae ordinari videntur, ut puta, si aedificator lapides polit ad construendam domum, ex hoc ipso quod aliquos pulchrius et decentius aptat, videtur eos ad honoratiorem partem domus ordinare. Sic igitur videtur quod Deus Angelos quos altioris naturae fecit, ad maiora gratiarum dona et ampliorem beatitudinem ordinaverit. Secundo apparet idem ex parte ipsius Angeli. Non enim Angelus est compositus ex diversis naturis, ut inclinatio unius naturae impetum alterius impediat aut retardet; sicut in homine accidit, in quo motus intellectivae partis aut retardatur aut impeditur ex inclinatione partis sensitivae. Quando autem non est aliquid quod retardet aut impediat, natura secundum totam suam virtutem movetur. Et ideo rationabile est quod Angeli qui meliorem naturam habuerunt, etiam fortius et efficacius ad Deum sint conversi. Hoc autem etiam in hominibus contingit, quod secundum intensionem conversionis in Deum datur maior gratia et gloria. Unde videtur quod Angeli qui habuerunt meliora naturalia, habuerunt plus de gratia et gloria.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31014)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut gratia est ex mera Dei voluntate, ita etiam et natura Angeli. Et sicut naturam Dei voluntas ordinavit ad gratiam ita et gradus naturae ad gradus gratiae.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31015)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod actus rationalis creaturae sunt ab ipsa; sed natura est immediate a Deo. Unde magis videtur quod gratia detur secundum gradum naturae, quam ex operibus.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31016)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod diversitas naturalium aliter est in Angelis, qui differunt specie; et aliter in hominibus, qui differunt solo numero. Differentia enim secundum speciem est propter finem, sed differentia secundum numerum est propter materiam. In homine etiam est aliquid quod potest impedire vel retardare motum intellectivae naturae, non autem in Angelis. Unde non est eadem ratio de utroque.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 62 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31017)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelis beatis non remaneat cognitio et dilectio naturalis. Quia, ut dicitur I Cor. XIII, *cum venerit quod perfectum est, evacuabitur quod ex parte est*. Sed dilectio et cognitio naturalis est imperfecta respectu cognitionis et dilectionis beatae. Ergo adveniente beatitudine, naturalis cognitio et dilectio cessat.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31018)\n\nPraeterea, ubi unum sufficit, aliud superflue existit. Sed sufficit in Angelis beatis cognitio et dilectio gloriae. Superfluum ergo esset quod remaneret in eis cognitio et dilectio naturalis.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31019)\n\nPraeterea, eadem potentia non habet simul duos actus; sicut nec una linea terminatur ex eadem parte ad duo puncta. Sed Angeli beati sunt semper in actu cognitionis et dilectionis beatae, felicitas enim non est secundum habitum, sed secundum actum, ut dicitur in I Ethic. Ergo nunquam in Angelis potest esse cognitio et dilectio naturalis.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31020)\n\nSed contra, quandiu manet natura aliqua, manet operatio eius. Sed beatitudo non tollit naturam; cum sit perfectio eius. Ergo non tollit naturalem cognitionem et dilectionem.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31021)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in Angelis beatis remanet cognitio et dilectio naturalis. Sicut enim se habent principia operationum ad invicem, ita se habent et operationes ipsae. Manifestum est autem quod natura ad beatitudinem comparatur sicut primum ad secundum, quia beatitudo naturae additur. Semper autem oportet salvari primum in secundo. Unde oportet quod natura salvetur in beatitudine. Et similiter oportet quod in actu beatitudinis salvetur actus naturae.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31022)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod perfectio adveniens tollit imperfectionem sibi oppositam. Imperfectio autem naturae non opponitur perfectioni beatitudinis, sed substernitur ei, sicut imperfectio potentiae substernitur perfectioni formae, et non tollitur potentia per formam, sed tollitur privatio, quae opponitur formae. Et similiter etiam imperfectio cognitionis naturalis non opponitur perfectioni cognitionis gloriae, nihil enim prohibet simul aliquid cognoscere per diversa media, sicut simul potest aliquid cognosci per medium probabile, et demonstrativum. Et similiter potest Angelus simul Deum cognoscere per essentiam Dei, quod pertinet ad cognitionem gloriae, et per essentiam propriam, quod pertinet ad cognitionem naturae.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31023)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ea quae sunt beatitudinis, per se sufficiunt. Sed ad hoc quod sint, praeexigunt ea quae sunt naturae, quia nulla beatitudo est per se subsistens, nisi beatitudo increata.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31024)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod duae operationes non possunt esse simul unius potentiae, nisi una ad aliam ordinetur. Cognitio autem et dilectio naturalis ordinantur ad cognitionem et dilectionem gloriae. Unde nihil prohibet in Angelo simul esse et cognitionem et dilectionem naturalem, et cognitionem et dilectionem gloriae.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 62 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31025)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus beatus peccare possit. Beatitudo enim non tollit naturam, ut dictum est. Sed de ratione naturae creatae est quod possit deficere. Ergo Angelus beatus potest peccare.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31026)\n\nPraeterea, potestates rationales sunt ad opposita, ut philosophus dicit. Sed voluntas Angeli beati non desinit esse rationalis. Ergo se habet ad bonum et malum.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31027)\n\nPraeterea, ad libertatem arbitrii pertinet quod homo possit eligere bonum et malum. Sed libertas arbitrii non minuitur in Angelis beatis. Ergo possunt peccare.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31028)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XI super Gen. ad Litt., quod illa natura quae peccare non potest in sanctis Angelis est. Ergo sancti Angeli peccare non possunt.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31029)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Angeli beati peccare non possunt. Cuius ratio est, quia eorum beatitudo in hoc consistit, quod per essentiam Deum vident. Essentia autem Dei est ipsa essentia bonitatis. Unde hoc modo se habet Angelus videns Deum ad ipsum Deum, sicut se habet quicumque non videns Deum ad communem rationem boni. Impossibile est autem quod aliquis quidquam velit vel operetur, nisi attendens ad bonum; vel quod velit divertere a bono, inquantum huiusmodi. Angelus igitur beatus non potest velle vel agere, nisi attendens ad Deum. Sic autem volens vel agens non potest peccare. Unde Angelus beatus nullo modo peccare potest.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31030)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod bonum creatum, in se consideratum, deficere potest. Sed ex coniunctione perfecta ad bonum increatum, qualis est coniunctio beatitudinis, adipiscitur quod peccare non possit, ratione iam dicta.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31031)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod virtutes rationales se habent ad opposita in illis ad quae non ordinantur naturaliter, sed quantum ad illa ad quae naturaliter ordinantur, non se habent ad opposita. Intellectus enim non potest non assentire principiis naturaliter notis, et similiter voluntas non potest non adhaerere bono inquantum est bonum, quia in bonum naturaliter ordinatur sicut in suum obiectum. Voluntas igitur Angeli se habet ad opposita, quantum ad multa facienda vel non facienda. Sed quantum ad ipsum Deum, quem vident esse ipsam essentiam bonitatis, non se habent ad opposita; sed secundum ipsum ad omnia diriguntur, quodcumque oppositorum eligant. Quod sine peccato est.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31032)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod liberum arbitrium sic se habet ad eligendum ea quae sunt ad finem, sicut se habet intellectus ad conclusiones. Manifestum est autem quod ad virtutem intellectus pertinet, ut in diversas conclusiones procedere possit secundum principia data, sed quod in aliquam conclusionem procedat praetermittendo ordinem principiorum, hoc est ex defectu ipsius. Unde quod liberum arbitrium diversa eligere possit servato ordine finis, hoc pertinet ad perfectionem libertatis eius, sed quod eligat aliquid divertendo ab ordine finis, quod est peccare, hoc pertinet ad defectum libertatis. Unde maior libertas arbitrii est in Angelis, qui peccare non possunt, quam in nobis, qui peccare possumus.\n\n## Articulus 9\n\n[I q. 62 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31033)\n\nAd nonum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli beati in beatitudine proficere possint. Caritas enim est principium merendi. Sed in Angelis est perfecta caritas. Ergo Angeli beati possunt mereri. Crescente autem merito, et praemium beatitudinis crescit. Ergo Angeli beati in beatitudine proficere possunt.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31034)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, in libro de Doctr. Christ., quod *Deus utitur nobis ad nostram utilitatem, et ad suam bonitatem*. Et similiter Angelis, quibus utitur in ministeriis spiritualibus; *cum sint administratorii spiritus, in ministerium missi propter eos qui haereditatem capiunt salutis*, ut dicitur Heb. I. Non autem hoc esset ad eorum utilitatem, si per hoc non mererentur nec in beatitudine proficerent. Relinquitur ergo quod Angeli beati et mereri, et in beatitudine proficere possunt.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31035)\n\nPraeterea, ad imperfectionem pertinet quod ille qui non est in summo, non possit proficere. Sed Angeli non sunt in summo. Si ergo ad maius proficere non possunt, videtur quod in eis sit imperfectio et defectus. Quod est inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31036)\n\nSed contra est quod mereri et proficere pertinent ad statum viae. Sed Angeli non sunt viatores, sed comprehensores. Ergo Angeli beati non possunt mereri, nec in beatitudine proficere.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31037)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in unoquoque motu motoris intentio fertur in aliquid determinatum, ad quod mobile perducere intendit, intentio enim est de fine cui repugnat infinitum. Manifestum est autem quod, cum creatura rationalis per suam virtutem consequi non possit suam beatitudinem, quae in visione Dei consistit, ut ex superioribus patet; indiget ut ad beatitudinem a Deo moveatur. Oportet igitur quod sit aliquid determinatum, ad quod quaelibet creatura rationalis dirigatur sicut in ultimum finem. Et hoc quidem determinatum non potest esse, in divina visione, quantum ad ipsum quod videtur, quia summa veritas ab omnibus beatis secundum diversos gradus conspicitur. Sed quantum ad modum visionis, praefigitur diversimode terminus ex intentione dirigentis in finem. Non enim possibile est quod, sicut rationalis creatura producitur ad videndum summam essentiam, ita producatur ad summum modum visionis, qui est comprehensio, hic enim modus soli Deo competere potest, ut ex supra dictis patet. Sed cum infinita efficacia requiratur ad Deum comprehendendum, creaturae vero efficacia in videndo non possit esse nisi finita; ab infinito autem finitum quodlibet infinitis gradibus distet; infinitis modis contingit creaturam rationalem intelligere Deum vel clarius vel minus clare. Et sicut beatitudo consistit in ipsa visione, ita gradus beatitudinis in certo modo visionis. Sic igitur unaquaeque creatura rationalis a Deo perducitur ad finem beatitudinis, ut etiam ad determinatum gradum beatitudinis perducatur ex praedestinatione Dei. Unde consecuto illo gradu, ad altiorem transire non potest.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31038)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod mereri est eius quod movetur ad finem. Movetur autem ad finem creatura rationalis, non solum patiendo, sed etiam operando. Et si quidem finis ille subsit virtuti rationalis creaturae, operatio illa dicetur acquisitiva illius finis, sicut homo meditando acquirit scientiam, si vero finis non sit in potestate eius, sed ab alio expectetur, operatio, erit meritoria finis. Ei autem quod est in ultimo termino, non convenit moveri, sed mutatum esse. Unde caritatis imperfectae, quae est viae, est mereri, caritatis autem perfectae non est mereri, sed potius praemio frui. Sicut et in habitibus acquisitis, operatio praecedens habitum est acquisitiva habitus, quae vero est ex habitu iam acquisito, est operatio iam perfecta cum delectatione. Et similiter actus caritatis perfectae non habet rationem meriti, sed magis est de perfectione praemii.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31039)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod aliquid dicitur utile dupliciter. Uno modo, sicut quod est in via ad finem, et sic utile est meritum beatitudinis. Alio modo, sicut pars est utilis ad totum, ut paries ad domum. Et hoc modo ministeria Angelorum sunt utilia Angelis beatis, inquantum sunt quaedam pars beatitudinis ipsorum, diffundere enim perfectionem habitam in alia, hoc est de ratione perfecti inquantum est perfectum.\n\n[I q. 62 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31040)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, licet Angelus beatus non sit in summo gradu beatitudinis simpliciter, est tamen in ultimo quantum ad seipsum, secundum praedestinationem divinam. Potest tamen augeri Angelorum gaudium de salute eorum qui per ipsorum ministerium salvantur; secundum illud Luc. XV, *gaudium est Angelis Dei super uno peccatore poenitentiam agente*. Sed hoc gaudium ad praemium accidentale pertinet, quod quidem augeri potest usque ad diem iudicii. Unde quidam dicunt quod, quantum ad praemium accidentale, etiam mereri possunt. Sed melius est ut dicatur quod nullo modo aliquis beatus mereri potest, nisi sit simul viator et comprehensor, ut Christus, qui solus fuit viator et comprehensor. Praedictum enim gaudium magis acquirunt ex virtute beatitudinis, quam illud mereantur.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0002","text":"[I q. 62 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30968)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0003","text":"Consequenter investigandum est quomodo Angeli facti sunt in esse gratiae vel gloriae. Et circa hoc quaeruntur novem. Primo, utrum Angeli fuerint in sua creatione beati. Secundo, utrum indiguerint gratia ad hoc quod ad Deum converterentur. Tertio, utrum fuerint creati in gratia. Quarto, utrum suam beatitudinem meruerint. Quinto, utrum statim post meritum beatitudinem adepti fuerint. Sexto, utrum gratiam et gloriam secundum capacitatem suorum naturalium receperint. Septimo, utrum post consecutionem gloriae remanserit in eis dilectio et cognitio naturalis. Octavo, utrum postmodum potuerint peccare. Nono, utrum post adeptionem gloriae potuerint proficere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0005","text":"[I q. 62 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30969)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli fuerint creati beati. Dicitur enim in libro de ecclesiasticis dogmatibus, quod *Angeli qui in illa in qua creati sunt beatitudine perseverant, non natura possident bonum quod habent*. Sunt ergo Angeli creati in beatitudine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0007","text":"[I q. 62 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30970)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0008","text":"Praeterea, natura angelica est nobilior quam creatura corporalis. Sed creatura corporalis statim in principio suae creationis fuit creata formata et perfecta; nec informitas praecessit in ea formationem tempore, sed natura tantum, ut Augustinus dicit, I super Gen. ad Litt. Ergo nec naturam angelicam creavit Deus informem et imperfectam. Sed eius formatio et perfectio est per beatitudinem, secundum quod fruitur Deo. Ergo fuit creata beata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0009","text":"[I q. 62 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30971)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0010","text":"Praeterea, secundum Augustinum, super Gen. ad Litt., ea quae leguntur facta in operibus sex dierum, simul facta fuerunt, et sic oportet quod statim a principio creationis rerum fuerint omnes illi sex dies. Sed in illis sex diebus, secundum eius expositionem, mane fuit cognitio angelica secundum quam cognoverunt verbum et res in verbo. Ergo statim a principio creationis cognoverunt verbum et res in verbo. Sed Angeli beati sunt per hoc quod verbum vident. Ergo statim a principio suae creationis Angeli fuerunt beati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0011","text":"[I q. 62 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30972)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0012","text":"Sed contra, de ratione beatitudinis est stabilitas sive confirmatio in bono. Sed Angeli non statim ut creati sunt, fuerunt confirmati in bono, quod casus quorundam ostendit. Non ergo Angeli in sua creatione fuerunt beati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0013","text":"[I q. 62 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30973)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod nomine beatitudinis intelligitur ultima perfectio rationalis seu intellectualis naturae, et inde est quod naturaliter desideratur, quia unumquodque naturaliter desiderat suam ultimam perfectionem. Ultima autem perfectio rationalis seu intellectualis naturae est duplex. Una quidem, quam potest assequi virtute suae naturae, et haec quodammodo beatitudo vel felicitas dicitur. Unde et Aristoteles perfectissimam hominis contemplationem, qua optimum intelligibile, quod est Deus, contemplari potest in hac vita, dicit esse ultimam hominis felicitatem. Sed super hanc felicitatem est alia felicitas, quam in futuro expectamus, qua videbimus Deum sicuti est. Quod quidem est supra cuiuslibet intellectus creati naturam, ut supra ostensum est. Sic igitur dicendum est quod, quantum ad primam beatitudinem, quam Angelus assequi virtute suae naturae potuit, fuit creatus beatus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0015","text":"Quia perfectionem huiusmodi Angelus non acquirit per aliquem motum discursivum, sicut homo, sed statim ei adest propter suae naturae dignitatem, ut supra dictum est. Sed ultimam beatitudinem, quae facultatem naturae excedit, Angeli non statim in principio suae creationis habuerunt, quia haec beatitudo non est aliquid naturae, sed naturae finis; et ideo non statim eam a principio debuerunt habere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0016","text":"[I q. 62 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30974)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod beatitudo ibi accipitur pro illa perfectione naturali quam Angelus habuit in statu innocentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0018","text":"[I q. 62 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30975)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod creatura corporalis statim in principio suae creationis habere non potuit perfectionem ad quam per suam operationem perducitur, unde, secundum Augustinum, germinatio plantarum ex terra non statim fuit in primis operibus, in quibus virtus sola germinativa plantarum data est terrae. Et similiter creatura angelica in principio suae creationis habuit perfectionem suae naturae; non autem perfectionem ad quam per suam operationem pervenire debebat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0020","text":"[I q. 62 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30976)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Angelus duplicem habet verbi cognitionem, unam naturalem, et aliam gloriae, naturalem quidem, qua cognoscit verbum per eius similitudinem in sua natura relucentem; cognitionem vero gloriae, qua cognoscit verbum per suam essentiam. Et utraque cognoscit Angelus res in verbo, sed naturali quidem cognitione imperfecte, cognitione vero gloriae perfecte. Prima ergo cognitio rerum in verbo affuit Angelo a principio suae creationis, secunda vero non, sed quando facti sunt beati per conversionem ad bonum. Et haec proprie dicitur cognitio matutina.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0023","text":"[I q. 62 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30977)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non indiguerit gratia ad hoc quod converteretur in Deum. Ad ea enim quae naturaliter possumus, gratia non indigemus. Sed naturaliter Angelus convertitur in Deum, quia naturaliter Deum diligit, ut ex supra dictis patet. Ergo Angelus non indiguit gratia ad hoc quod converteretur in Deum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0025","text":"[I q. 62 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30978)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0026","text":"Praeterea, ad ea tantum videmur indigere auxilio, quae sunt difficilia. Sed converti ad Deum non erat difficile Angelo; cum nihil esset in eo quod huic conversioni repugnaret. Ergo Angelus non indiguit auxilio gratiae ad hoc quod converteretur in Deum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0027","text":"[I q. 62 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30979)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0028","text":"Praeterea, converti ad Deum est se ad gratiam praeparare, unde Zach. I, dicitur, *convertimini ad me, et ego convertar ad vos*. Sed nos non indigemus gratia ad hoc quod nos ad gratiam praeparemus, quia sic esset abire in infinitum. Ergo non indiguit gratia Angelus ad hoc quod converteretur in Deum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0029","text":"[I q. 62 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30980)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0030","text":"Sed contra, per conversionem ad Deum Angelus pervenit ad beatitudinem. Si igitur non indiguisset gratia ad hoc quod converteretur in Deum, sequeretur quod non indigeret gratia ad habendam vitam aeternam. Quod est contra illud apostoli, Rom. VI, *gratia Dei vita aeterna*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0031","text":"[I q. 62 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30981)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0032","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Angeli indiguerunt gratia ad hoc quod converterentur in Deum, prout est obiectum beatitudinis. Sicut enim superius dictum est, naturalis motus voluntatis est principium omnium eorum quae volumus. Naturalis autem inclinatio voluntatis est ad id quod est conveniens secundum naturam. Et ideo, si aliquid sit supra naturam, voluntas in id ferri non potest, nisi ab aliquo alio supernaturali principio adiuta. Sicut patet quod ignis habet naturalem inclinationem ad calefaciendum, et ad generandum ignem, sed generare carnem est supra naturalem virtutem ignis, unde ignis ad hoc nullam inclinationem habet, nisi secundum quod movetur ut instrumentum ab anima nutritiva. Ostensum est autem supra, cum de Dei cognitione ageretur, quod videre Deum per essentiam, in quo ultima beatitudo rationalis creaturae consistit, est supra naturam cuiuslibet intellectus creati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0033","text":"Unde nulla creatura rationalis potest habere motum voluntatis ordinatum ad illam beatitudinem, nisi mota a supernaturali agente. Et hoc dicimus auxilium gratiae. Et ideo dicendum est quod Angelus in illam beatitudinem voluntate converti non potuit, nisi per auxilium gratiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0034","text":"[I q. 62 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30982)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0035","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Angelus naturaliter diligit Deum, inquantum est principium naturalis esse. Hic autem loquimur de conversione ad Deum, inquantum est beatificans per suae essentiae visionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0036","text":"[I q. 62 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30983)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0037","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod difficile est quod transcendit potentiam. Sed hoc contingit esse dupliciter. Uno modo, quia transcendit potentiam secundum suum naturalem ordinem. Et tunc, si ad hoc possit pervenire aliquo auxilio, dicitur difficile; si autem nullo modo, dicitur impossibile, sicut impossibile est hominem volare. Alio modo transcendit aliquid potentiam, non secundum ordinem naturalem potentiae, sed propter aliquod impedimentum potentiae adiunctum. Sicut ascendere non est contra naturalem ordinem potentiae animae motivae, quia anima, quantum est de se, nata est movere in quamlibet partem, sed impeditur ab hoc propter corporis gravitatem; unde difficile est homini ascendere. Converti autem ad beatitudinem ultimam, homini quidem est difficile et quia est supra naturam, et quia habet impedimentum ex corruptione corporis et infectione peccati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0038","text":"Sed Angelo est difficile propter hoc solum quod est supernaturale.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0039","text":"[I q. 62 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30984)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0040","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod quilibet motus voluntatis in Deum, potest dici conversio in ipsum. Et ideo triplex est conversio in Deum. Una quidem per dilectionem perfectam, quae est creaturae iam Deo fruentis. Et ad hanc conversionem requiritur gratia consummata. Alia conversio est, quae est meritum beatitudinis. Et ad hanc requiritur habitualis gratia, quae est merendi principium. Tertia conversio est, per quam aliquis praeparat se ad gratiam habendam. Et ad hanc non exigitur aliqua habitualis gratia, sed operatio Dei ad se animam convertentis, secundum illud Thren. ult., *converte nos, domine, ad te, et convertemur*. Unde patet quod non est procedere in infinitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0041","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0042","text":"[I q. 62 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30985)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0043","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli non sint creati in gratia. Dicit enim Augustinus, II super Gen. ad Litt., quod angelica natura primo erat informiter creata, et caelum dicta, postmodum vero formata est, et lux appellata. Sed haec formatio est per gratiam. Ergo non sunt creati in gratia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0044","text":"[I q. 62 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30986)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0045","text":"Praeterea, gratia inclinat creaturam rationalem in Deum. Si igitur Angelus in gratia creatus fuisset nullus Angelus fuisset a Deo aversus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0046","text":"[I q. 62 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30987)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0047","text":"Praeterea, gratia medium est inter naturam et gloriam. Sed Angeli non fuerunt beati in sua creatione. Ergo videtur quod nec etiam creati sint in gratia, sed primo in natura tantum; postea autem adepti sunt gratiam; et ultimo facti sunt beati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0048","text":"[I q. 62 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30988)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0049","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XII de Civ. Dei, *bonam voluntatem quis fecit in Angelis, nisi ille qui eos cum sua voluntate, idest cum amore casto quo illi adhaerent, creavit, simul in eis condens naturam et largiens gratiam?*","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0050","text":"[I q. 62 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30989)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0051","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, quamvis super hoc sint diversae opiniones, quibusdam dicentibus quod creati sunt Angeli in naturalibus tantum, aliis vero quod sunt creati in gratia; hoc tamen probabilius videtur tenendum, et magis dictis sanctorum consonum est, quod fuerunt creati in gratia gratum faciente. Sic enim videmus quod omnia quae processu temporis per opus divinae providentiae, creatura sub Deo operante, sunt producta, in prima rerum conditione producta sunt secundum quasdam seminales rationes, ut Augustinus dicit, super Gen. ad Litt.; sicut arbores et animalia et alia huiusmodi. Manifestum est autem quod gratia gratum faciens hoc modo comparatur ad beatitudinem, sicut ratio seminalis in natura ad effectum naturalem, unde I Ioan. III, gratia semen Dei nominatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0052","text":"Sicut igitur, secundum opinionem Augustini, ponitur quod statim in prima creatione corporalis creaturae inditae sunt ei seminales rationes omnium naturalium effectuum, ita statim a principio sunt Angeli creati in gratia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0053","text":"[I q. 62 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30990)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0054","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod informitas illa Angeli potest intelligi vel per comparationem ad formationem gloriae, et sic praecessit tempore informitas formationem. Vel per comparationem ad formationem gratiae, et sic non praecessit ordine temporis, sed ordine naturae; sicut etiam de formatione corporali Augustinus ponit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0055","text":"[I q. 62 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30991)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0056","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod omnis forma inclinat suum subiectum secundum modum naturae eius. Modus autem naturalis intellectualis naturae est, ut libere feratur in ea quae vult. Et ideo inclinatio gratiae non imponit necessitatem, sed habens gratiam potest ea non uti, et peccare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0057","text":"[I q. 62 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30992)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0058","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, quamvis gratia sit medium inter naturam et gloriam ordine naturae tamen ordine temporis in natura creata non debuit simul esse gloria cum natura, quia est finis operationis ipsius naturae per gratiam adiutae. Gratia autem non se habet ut finis operationis, quia non est ex operibus; sed ut principium bene operandi. Et ideo statim cum natura gratiam dare conveniens fuit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0059","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0060","text":"[I q. 62 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30993)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0061","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus beatus suam beatitudinem non meruerit. Meritum enim est ex difficultate actus meritorii. Sed nullam difficultatem Angelus habuit ad bene operandum. Ergo bona operatio non fuit ei meritoria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0062","text":"[I q. 62 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30994)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0063","text":"Praeterea, naturalibus non meremur. Sed naturale fuit Angelo quod converteretur ad Deum. Ergo per hoc non meruit beatitudinem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0064","text":"[I q. 62 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30995)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0065","text":"Praeterea, si Angelus beatus beatitudinem suam meruit, aut ergo antequam eam haberet, aut post. Sed non ante, quia, ut multis videtur, ante non habuit gratiam, sine qua nullum est meritum. Nec etiam post, quia sic etiam modo mereretur, quod videtur esse falsum, quia sic minor Angelus merendo ad superioris Angeli gradum posset pertingere, et non essent stabiles distinctiones graduum gratiae; quod est inconveniens. Non ergo Angelus beatus suam beatitudinem meruit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0066","text":"[I q. 62 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30996)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0067","text":"Sed contra, Apoc. XXI, dicitur quod mensura Angeli, in illa caelesti Ierusalem, est mensura hominis. Sed homo ad beatitudinem pertingere non potest nisi per meritum. Ergo neque Angelus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0068","text":"[I q. 62 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30997)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0069","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod soli Deo beatitudo perfecta est naturalis quia idem est sibi esse et beatum esse. Cuiuslibet autem creaturae esse beatum non est natura, sed ultimus finis. Quaelibet autem res ad ultimum finem per suam operationem pertingit. Quae quidem operatio in finem ducens, vel est factiva finis, quando finis non excedit virtutem eius quod operatur propter finem, sicut medicatio est factiva sanitatis, vel est meritoria finis, quando finis excedit virtutem operantis propter finem, unde expectatur finis ex dono alterius. Beatitudo autem ultima excedit et naturam angelicam et humanam, ut ex dictis patet. Unde relinquitur quod tam homo quam Angelus suam beatitudinem meruerit. Et si quidem Angelus in gratia creatus fuit, sine qua nullum est meritum, absque difficultate dicere possumus quod suam beatitudinem meruerit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0070","text":"Et similiter si quis diceret quod qualitercumque gratiam habuerit antequam gloriam. Si vero gratiam non habuit antequam esset beatus, sic oportet dicere quod beatitudinem absque merito habuit, sicut nos gratiam. Quod tamen est contra rationem beatitudinis, quae habet rationem finis, et est praemium virtutis, ut etiam philosophus dicit, in I Ethic. Vel oportet dicere quod Angeli merentur beatitudinem per ea quae iam beati operantur in divinis ministeriis, ut alii dixerunt. Quod tamen est contra rationem meriti, nam meritum habet rationem viae ad finem, ei autem qui iam est in termino, non convenit moveri ad terminum; et sic nullus meretur quod iam habet. Vel oportet dicere quod unus et idem actus conversionis in Deum, inquantum est ex libero arbitrio, est meritorius; et inquantum pertingit ad finem, est fruitio beata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0071","text":"Sed nec hoc etiam videtur esse conveniens, quia liberum arbitrium non est sufficiens causa meriti unde actus non potest esse meritorius secundum quod est ex libero arbitrio, nisi inquantum est gratia informatus; non autem simul potest informari gratia imperfecta, quae est principium merendi, et gratia perfecta, quae est principium fruendi. Unde non videtur esse possibile quod simul fruatur, et suam fruitionem mereatur. Et ideo melius dicendum est quod gratiam habuit Angelus antequam esset beatus, per quam beatitudinem meruit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0072","text":"[I q. 62 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30998)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0073","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod difficultas bene operandi non est in Angelis ex aliqua contrarietate, vel impedimento naturalis virtutis; sed ex hoc quod opus aliquod bonum est supra virtutem naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0074","text":"[I q. 62 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30999)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0075","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod conversione naturali Angelus non meruit beatitudinem, sed conversione caritatis, quae est per gratiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0076","text":"[I q. 62 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31000)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0077","text":"Ad tertium patet responsio ex dictis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0078","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0079","text":"[I q. 62 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31001)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0080","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non statim post unum actum meritorium beatitudinem habuerit. Difficilius enim est homini bene operari quam Angelo. Sed homo non praemiatur statim post unum actum. Ergo neque Angelus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0081","text":"[I q. 62 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31002)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0082","text":"Praeterea, Angelus statim in principio suae creationis, et in instanti, actum aliquem habere potuit, cum etiam corpora naturalia in ipso instanti suae creationis moveri incipiant, et si motus corporis in instanti esse posset, sicut opera intellectus et voluntatis, in primo instanti suae generationis motum haberent. Si ergo Angelus per unum motum suae voluntatis beatitudinem meruit, in primo instanti suae creationis meruit beatitudinem. Si ergo eorum beatitudo non retardatur, statim in primo instanti fuerunt beati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0083","text":"[I q. 62 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31003)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0084","text":"Praeterea, inter multum distantia oportet esse multa media. Sed status beatitudinis Angelorum multum distat a statu naturae eorum, medium autem inter utrumque est meritum. Oportuit igitur quod per multa media Angelus ad beatitudinem perveniret.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0085","text":"[I q. 62 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31004)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0086","text":"Sed contra, anima hominis et Angelus similiter ad beatitudinem ordinantur, unde sanctis promittitur aequalitas Angelorum Luc. XX. Sed anima a corpore separata, si habeat meritum beatitudinis, statim beatitudinem consequitur, nisi aliud sit impedimentum. Ergo pari ratione et Angelus. Sed statim in primo actu caritatis habuit meritum beatitudinis. Ergo, cum in eo non esset aliquod impedimentum, statim ad beatitudinem pervenit per solum unum actum meritorium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0087","text":"[I q. 62 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31005)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0088","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Angelus post primum actum caritatis quo beatitudinem meruit, statim beatus fuit. Cuius ratio est, quia gratia perficit naturam secundum modum naturae, sicut et omnis perfectio recipitur in perfectibili secundum modum eius. Est autem hoc proprium naturae angelicae, quod naturalem perfectionem non per discursum acquirat, sed statim per naturam habeat, sicut supra ostensum est. Sicut autem ex sua natura Angelus habet ordinem ad perfectionem naturalem, ita ex merito habet ordinem ad gloriam. Et ita statim post meritum in Angelo fuit beatitudo consecuta. Meritum autem beatitudinis, non solum in Angelo, sed etiam in homine esse potest per unicum actum, quia quolibet actu caritate informato homo beatitudinem meretur. Unde relinquitur quod statim post unum actum caritate informatum, Angelus beatus fuit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0089","text":"[I q. 62 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31006)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0090","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod homo secundum suam naturam non statim natus est ultimam perfectionem adipisci, sicut Angelus. Et ideo homini longior via data est ad merendum beatitudinem, quam Angelo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0091","text":"[I q. 62 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31007)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0092","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Angelus est supra tempus rerum corporalium, unde instantia diversa in his quae ad Angelos pertinent, non accipiuntur nisi secundum successionem in ipsorum actibus. Non autem potuit simul in eis esse actus meritorius beatitudinis, et actus beatitudinis, qui est fruitio; cum unus sit gratiae imperfectae, et alius gratiae consummatae. Unde relinquitur quod oportet diversa instantia accipi, in quorum uno meruerit beatitudinem, et in alio fuerit beatus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0093","text":"[I q. 62 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31008)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0094","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod de natura Angeli est, quod statim suam perfectionem consequatur ad quam ordinatur. Et ideo non requiritur nisi unus actus meritorius; qui ea ratione medium dici potest, quia secundum ipsum Angelus ad beatitudinem ordinatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0095","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0096","text":"[I q. 62 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31009)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0097","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli non sint consecuti gratiam et gloriam secundum quantitatem suorum naturalium. Gratia enim ex mera Dei voluntate datur. Ergo et quantitas gratiae dependet ex voluntate Dei, et non ex quantitate naturalium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0098","text":"[I q. 62 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31010)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0099","text":"Praeterea, magis propinquum videtur ad gratiam actus humanus quam natura, quia actus humanus praeparatorius est ad gratiam. Sed gratia non est ex operibus, ut dicitur Rom. XI. Multo igitur minus quantitas gratiae in Angelis est secundum quantitatem naturalium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0100","text":"[I q. 62 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31011)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0101","text":"Praeterea, homo et Angelus pariter ordinantur ad beatitudinem vel gratiam. Sed homini non datur plus de gratia secundum gradum naturalium. Ergo nec Angelo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0102","text":"[I q. 62 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31012)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0103","text":"Sed contra est quod Magister dicit, III dist. II Sent., quod *Angeli qui natura magis subtiles, et sapientia amplius perspicaces creati sunt, hi etiam maioribus gratiae muneribus praediti sunt*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0104","text":"[I q. 62 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31013)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0105","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod rationabile est quod secundum gradum naturalium Angelis data sint dona gratiarum et perfectio beatitudinis. Cuius quidem ratio ex duobus accipi potest. Primo quidem ex parte ipsius Dei, qui per ordinem suae sapientiae diversos gradus in angelica natura constituit. Sicut autem natura angelica facta est a Deo ad gratiam et beatitudinem consequendam, ita etiam gradus naturae angelicae ad diversos gradus gratiae et gloriae ordinari videntur, ut puta, si aedificator lapides polit ad construendam domum, ex hoc ipso quod aliquos pulchrius et decentius aptat, videtur eos ad honoratiorem partem domus ordinare. Sic igitur videtur quod Deus Angelos quos altioris naturae fecit, ad maiora gratiarum dona et ampliorem beatitudinem ordinaverit. Secundo apparet idem ex parte ipsius Angeli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0106","text":"Non enim Angelus est compositus ex diversis naturis, ut inclinatio unius naturae impetum alterius impediat aut retardet; sicut in homine accidit, in quo motus intellectivae partis aut retardatur aut impeditur ex inclinatione partis sensitivae. Quando autem non est aliquid quod retardet aut impediat, natura secundum totam suam virtutem movetur. Et ideo rationabile est quod Angeli qui meliorem naturam habuerunt, etiam fortius et efficacius ad Deum sint conversi. Hoc autem etiam in hominibus contingit, quod secundum intensionem conversionis in Deum datur maior gratia et gloria. Unde videtur quod Angeli qui habuerunt meliora naturalia, habuerunt plus de gratia et gloria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0107","text":"[I q. 62 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31014)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0108","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut gratia est ex mera Dei voluntate, ita etiam et natura Angeli. Et sicut naturam Dei voluntas ordinavit ad gratiam ita et gradus naturae ad gradus gratiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0109","text":"[I q. 62 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31015)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0110","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod actus rationalis creaturae sunt ab ipsa; sed natura est immediate a Deo. Unde magis videtur quod gratia detur secundum gradum naturae, quam ex operibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0111","text":"[I q. 62 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31016)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0112","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod diversitas naturalium aliter est in Angelis, qui differunt specie; et aliter in hominibus, qui differunt solo numero. Differentia enim secundum speciem est propter finem, sed differentia secundum numerum est propter materiam. In homine etiam est aliquid quod potest impedire vel retardare motum intellectivae naturae, non autem in Angelis. Unde non est eadem ratio de utroque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0113","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0114","text":"[I q. 62 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31017)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0115","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelis beatis non remaneat cognitio et dilectio naturalis. Quia, ut dicitur I Cor. XIII, *cum venerit quod perfectum est, evacuabitur quod ex parte est*. Sed dilectio et cognitio naturalis est imperfecta respectu cognitionis et dilectionis beatae. Ergo adveniente beatitudine, naturalis cognitio et dilectio cessat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0116","text":"[I q. 62 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31018)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0117","text":"Praeterea, ubi unum sufficit, aliud superflue existit. Sed sufficit in Angelis beatis cognitio et dilectio gloriae. Superfluum ergo esset quod remaneret in eis cognitio et dilectio naturalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0118","text":"[I q. 62 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31019)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0119","text":"Praeterea, eadem potentia non habet simul duos actus; sicut nec una linea terminatur ex eadem parte ad duo puncta. Sed Angeli beati sunt semper in actu cognitionis et dilectionis beatae, felicitas enim non est secundum habitum, sed secundum actum, ut dicitur in I Ethic. Ergo nunquam in Angelis potest esse cognitio et dilectio naturalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0120","text":"[I q. 62 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31020)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0121","text":"Sed contra, quandiu manet natura aliqua, manet operatio eius. Sed beatitudo non tollit naturam; cum sit perfectio eius. Ergo non tollit naturalem cognitionem et dilectionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0122","text":"[I q. 62 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31021)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0123","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in Angelis beatis remanet cognitio et dilectio naturalis. Sicut enim se habent principia operationum ad invicem, ita se habent et operationes ipsae. Manifestum est autem quod natura ad beatitudinem comparatur sicut primum ad secundum, quia beatitudo naturae additur. Semper autem oportet salvari primum in secundo. Unde oportet quod natura salvetur in beatitudine. Et similiter oportet quod in actu beatitudinis salvetur actus naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0124","text":"[I q. 62 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31022)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0125","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod perfectio adveniens tollit imperfectionem sibi oppositam. Imperfectio autem naturae non opponitur perfectioni beatitudinis, sed substernitur ei, sicut imperfectio potentiae substernitur perfectioni formae, et non tollitur potentia per formam, sed tollitur privatio, quae opponitur formae. Et similiter etiam imperfectio cognitionis naturalis non opponitur perfectioni cognitionis gloriae, nihil enim prohibet simul aliquid cognoscere per diversa media, sicut simul potest aliquid cognosci per medium probabile, et demonstrativum. Et similiter potest Angelus simul Deum cognoscere per essentiam Dei, quod pertinet ad cognitionem gloriae, et per essentiam propriam, quod pertinet ad cognitionem naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0126","text":"[I q. 62 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31023)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0127","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ea quae sunt beatitudinis, per se sufficiunt. Sed ad hoc quod sint, praeexigunt ea quae sunt naturae, quia nulla beatitudo est per se subsistens, nisi beatitudo increata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0128","text":"[I q. 62 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31024)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0129","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod duae operationes non possunt esse simul unius potentiae, nisi una ad aliam ordinetur. Cognitio autem et dilectio naturalis ordinantur ad cognitionem et dilectionem gloriae. Unde nihil prohibet in Angelo simul esse et cognitionem et dilectionem naturalem, et cognitionem et dilectionem gloriae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0130","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0131","text":"[I q. 62 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31025)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0132","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus beatus peccare possit. Beatitudo enim non tollit naturam, ut dictum est. Sed de ratione naturae creatae est quod possit deficere. Ergo Angelus beatus potest peccare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0133","text":"[I q. 62 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31026)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0134","text":"Praeterea, potestates rationales sunt ad opposita, ut philosophus dicit. Sed voluntas Angeli beati non desinit esse rationalis. Ergo se habet ad bonum et malum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0135","text":"[I q. 62 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31027)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0136","text":"Praeterea, ad libertatem arbitrii pertinet quod homo possit eligere bonum et malum. Sed libertas arbitrii non minuitur in Angelis beatis. Ergo possunt peccare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0137","text":"[I q. 62 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31028)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0138","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XI super Gen. ad Litt., quod illa natura quae peccare non potest in sanctis Angelis est. Ergo sancti Angeli peccare non possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0139","text":"[I q. 62 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31029)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0140","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Angeli beati peccare non possunt. Cuius ratio est, quia eorum beatitudo in hoc consistit, quod per essentiam Deum vident. Essentia autem Dei est ipsa essentia bonitatis. Unde hoc modo se habet Angelus videns Deum ad ipsum Deum, sicut se habet quicumque non videns Deum ad communem rationem boni. Impossibile est autem quod aliquis quidquam velit vel operetur, nisi attendens ad bonum; vel quod velit divertere a bono, inquantum huiusmodi. Angelus igitur beatus non potest velle vel agere, nisi attendens ad Deum. Sic autem volens vel agens non potest peccare. Unde Angelus beatus nullo modo peccare potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0141","text":"[I q. 62 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31030)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0142","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod bonum creatum, in se consideratum, deficere potest. Sed ex coniunctione perfecta ad bonum increatum, qualis est coniunctio beatitudinis, adipiscitur quod peccare non possit, ratione iam dicta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0143","text":"[I q. 62 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31031)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0144","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod virtutes rationales se habent ad opposita in illis ad quae non ordinantur naturaliter, sed quantum ad illa ad quae naturaliter ordinantur, non se habent ad opposita. Intellectus enim non potest non assentire principiis naturaliter notis, et similiter voluntas non potest non adhaerere bono inquantum est bonum, quia in bonum naturaliter ordinatur sicut in suum obiectum. Voluntas igitur Angeli se habet ad opposita, quantum ad multa facienda vel non facienda. Sed quantum ad ipsum Deum, quem vident esse ipsam essentiam bonitatis, non se habent ad opposita; sed secundum ipsum ad omnia diriguntur, quodcumque oppositorum eligant. Quod sine peccato est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0145","text":"[I q. 62 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31032)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0146","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod liberum arbitrium sic se habet ad eligendum ea quae sunt ad finem, sicut se habet intellectus ad conclusiones. Manifestum est autem quod ad virtutem intellectus pertinet, ut in diversas conclusiones procedere possit secundum principia data, sed quod in aliquam conclusionem procedat praetermittendo ordinem principiorum, hoc est ex defectu ipsius. Unde quod liberum arbitrium diversa eligere possit servato ordine finis, hoc pertinet ad perfectionem libertatis eius, sed quod eligat aliquid divertendo ab ordine finis, quod est peccare, hoc pertinet ad defectum libertatis. Unde maior libertas arbitrii est in Angelis, qui peccare non possunt, quam in nobis, qui peccare possumus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0147","text":"## Articulus 9","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0148","text":"[I q. 62 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31033)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0149","text":"Ad nonum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli beati in beatitudine proficere possint. Caritas enim est principium merendi. Sed in Angelis est perfecta caritas. Ergo Angeli beati possunt mereri. Crescente autem merito, et praemium beatitudinis crescit. Ergo Angeli beati in beatitudine proficere possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0150","text":"[I q. 62 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31034)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0151","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, in libro de Doctr. Christ., quod *Deus utitur nobis ad nostram utilitatem, et ad suam bonitatem*. Et similiter Angelis, quibus utitur in ministeriis spiritualibus; *cum sint administratorii spiritus, in ministerium missi propter eos qui haereditatem capiunt salutis*, ut dicitur Heb. I. Non autem hoc esset ad eorum utilitatem, si per hoc non mererentur nec in beatitudine proficerent. Relinquitur ergo quod Angeli beati et mereri, et in beatitudine proficere possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0152","text":"[I q. 62 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31035)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0153","text":"Praeterea, ad imperfectionem pertinet quod ille qui non est in summo, non possit proficere. Sed Angeli non sunt in summo. Si ergo ad maius proficere non possunt, videtur quod in eis sit imperfectio et defectus. Quod est inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0154","text":"[I q. 62 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31036)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0155","text":"Sed contra est quod mereri et proficere pertinent ad statum viae. Sed Angeli non sunt viatores, sed comprehensores. Ergo Angeli beati non possunt mereri, nec in beatitudine proficere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0156","text":"[I q. 62 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31037)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0157","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in unoquoque motu motoris intentio fertur in aliquid determinatum, ad quod mobile perducere intendit, intentio enim est de fine cui repugnat infinitum. Manifestum est autem quod, cum creatura rationalis per suam virtutem consequi non possit suam beatitudinem, quae in visione Dei consistit, ut ex superioribus patet; indiget ut ad beatitudinem a Deo moveatur. Oportet igitur quod sit aliquid determinatum, ad quod quaelibet creatura rationalis dirigatur sicut in ultimum finem. Et hoc quidem determinatum non potest esse, in divina visione, quantum ad ipsum quod videtur, quia summa veritas ab omnibus beatis secundum diversos gradus conspicitur. Sed quantum ad modum visionis, praefigitur diversimode terminus ex intentione dirigentis in finem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0158","text":"Non enim possibile est quod, sicut rationalis creatura producitur ad videndum summam essentiam, ita producatur ad summum modum visionis, qui est comprehensio, hic enim modus soli Deo competere potest, ut ex supra dictis patet. Sed cum infinita efficacia requiratur ad Deum comprehendendum, creaturae vero efficacia in videndo non possit esse nisi finita; ab infinito autem finitum quodlibet infinitis gradibus distet; infinitis modis contingit creaturam rationalem intelligere Deum vel clarius vel minus clare. Et sicut beatitudo consistit in ipsa visione, ita gradus beatitudinis in certo modo visionis. Sic igitur unaquaeque creatura rationalis a Deo perducitur ad finem beatitudinis, ut etiam ad determinatum gradum beatitudinis perducatur ex praedestinatione Dei. Unde consecuto illo gradu, ad altiorem transire non potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0159","text":"[I q. 62 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31038)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0160","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod mereri est eius quod movetur ad finem. Movetur autem ad finem creatura rationalis, non solum patiendo, sed etiam operando. Et si quidem finis ille subsit virtuti rationalis creaturae, operatio illa dicetur acquisitiva illius finis, sicut homo meditando acquirit scientiam, si vero finis non sit in potestate eius, sed ab alio expectetur, operatio, erit meritoria finis. Ei autem quod est in ultimo termino, non convenit moveri, sed mutatum esse. Unde caritatis imperfectae, quae est viae, est mereri, caritatis autem perfectae non est mereri, sed potius praemio frui. Sicut et in habitibus acquisitis, operatio praecedens habitum est acquisitiva habitus, quae vero est ex habitu iam acquisito, est operatio iam perfecta cum delectatione. Et similiter actus caritatis perfectae non habet rationem meriti, sed magis est de perfectione praemii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0161","text":"[I q. 62 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31039)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0162","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod aliquid dicitur utile dupliciter. Uno modo, sicut quod est in via ad finem, et sic utile est meritum beatitudinis. Alio modo, sicut pars est utilis ad totum, ut paries ad domum. Et hoc modo ministeria Angelorum sunt utilia Angelis beatis, inquantum sunt quaedam pars beatitudinis ipsorum, diffundere enim perfectionem habitam in alia, hoc est de ratione perfecti inquantum est perfectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0163","text":"[I q. 62 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31040)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0164","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, licet Angelus beatus non sit in summo gradu beatitudinis simpliciter, est tamen in ultimo quantum ad seipsum, secundum praedestinationem divinam. Potest tamen augeri Angelorum gaudium de salute eorum qui per ipsorum ministerium salvantur; secundum illud Luc. XV, *gaudium est Angelis Dei super uno peccatore poenitentiam agente*. Sed hoc gaudium ad praemium accidentale pertinet, quod quidem augeri potest usque ad diem iudicii. Unde quidam dicunt quod, quantum ad praemium accidentale, etiam mereri possunt. Sed melius est ut dicatur quod nullo modo aliquis beatus mereri potest, nisi sit simul viator et comprehensor, ut Christus, qui solus fuit viator et comprehensor. Praedictum enim gaudium magis acquirunt ex virtute beatitudinis, quam illud mereantur.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9cc83d990778e7e847ecfac753f9914fe69ffbd9237cd8ea415662dfa5e5f9b6","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T144753688009Z_dff5519e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 62","source_sha256":"9cc83d990778e7e847ecfac753f9914fe69ffbd9237cd8ea415662dfa5e5f9b6","sha256":"1183bce23baec189130749057d83ef6962c44557c82a949b9048090533a740c9","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:47:53.688050+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0002","text":"[I q. 62 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30968)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0003","text":"Next, it must be investigated how angels were made in the state of grace or glory. And concerning this, nine questions are asked. First, whether the angels were blessed in their creation. Second, whether they needed grace in order to turn to God. Third, whether they were created in grace. Fourth, whether they merited their beatitude. Fifth, whether they attained beatitude immediately after merit. Sixth, whether they received grace and glory according to the capacity of their natural powers. Seventh, whether, after attaining glory, natural love and knowledge remained in them. Eighth, whether they were thereafter able to sin. Ninth, whether, after attaining glory, they were able to make progress.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0005","text":"[I q. 62 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30969)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0006","text":"The first is approached as follows. It seems that the angels were created blessed. For it is said in the book *On Ecclesiastical Dogmas* that “the angels who persevere in that beatitude in which they were created do not possess the good which they have by nature.” Therefore, the angels were created in beatitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0007","text":"[I q. 62 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30970)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0008","text":"Further, the angelic nature is nobler than a corporeal creature. But a corporeal creature, immediately at the beginning of its creation, was created formed and perfect; nor did formlessness precede formation in it in time, but only by nature, as Augustine says, *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, I. Therefore neither did God create the angelic nature formless and imperfect. But its formation and perfection are through beatitude, insofar as it enjoys God. Therefore it was created blessed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0009","text":"[I q. 62 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30971)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0010","text":"Further, according to Augustine, *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, the things read as having been made in the works of the six days were made simultaneously, and thus it is necessary that, immediately from the beginning of the creation of things, all those six days existed. But in those six days, according to his exposition, morning was angelic knowledge, according to which they knew the Word and things in the Word. Therefore, immediately from the beginning of creation they knew the Word and things in the Word. But angels are blessed by seeing the Word. Therefore, immediately from the beginning of their creation, the angels were blessed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0011","text":"[I q. 62 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30972)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0012","text":"On the contrary, stability or confirmation in the good belongs to the notion of beatitude. But the angels were not confirmed in the good immediately upon being created, as the fall of some shows. Therefore the angels were not blessed in their creation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0013","text":"[I q. 62 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30973)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that by the name of beatitude is understood the ultimate perfection of a rational or intellectual nature, and hence it is naturally desired, because each thing naturally desires its ultimate perfection. Now the ultimate perfection of a rational or intellectual nature is twofold. One kind is that which it can attain by the power of its nature, and this is in some manner called beatitude or happiness. Thus Aristotle too calls the most perfect contemplation of man—by which he can contemplate in this life the highest intelligible object, which is God—the ultimate happiness of man. But above this happiness there is another happiness, which we await in the future, by which we shall see God as he is. This indeed exceeds the nature of every created intellect, as was shown above. Therefore it must be said that, as regards the first beatitude, which the angel could attain by the power of his nature, he was created blessed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0015","text":"For the angel does not acquire such perfection through any discursive movement, as man does, but it is immediately present to him by reason of the dignity of his nature, as was said above. But the angels did not possess, immediately at the beginning of their creation, the ultimate beatitude, which exceeds the capacity of nature, because this beatitude is not something of nature, but the end of nature; and therefore they ought not to have had it immediately from the beginning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0016","text":"[I q. 62 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30974)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that beatitude is there taken for that natural perfection which the angel had in the state of innocence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0018","text":"[I q. 62 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30975)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0019","text":"To the second it must be said that a corporeal creature could not, immediately at the beginning of its creation, have the perfection to which it is brought through its own operation. Hence, according to Augustine, the sprouting of plants from the earth was not immediately present in the first works, in which only the germinative power of plants was given to the earth. Likewise, at the beginning of its creation, the angelic creature had the perfection of its nature, but not the perfection to which it was bound to arrive through its own operation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0020","text":"[I q. 62 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30976)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0021","text":"To the third it must be said that the angel has a twofold knowledge of the Word: one natural, and the other of glory. The natural knowledge is that by which he knows the Word through its likeness shining forth in his nature; but the knowledge of glory is that by which he knows the Word through its essence. By both kinds the angel knows things in the Word, but by natural knowledge imperfectly, and by the knowledge of glory perfectly. Therefore, the first knowledge of things in the Word was present to the angel from the beginning of his creation, but the second was not, except when they were made blessed by turning to the good. And this is properly called morning knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0023","text":"[I q. 62 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30977)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0024","text":"The second is approached as follows. It seems that the angel did not need grace in order to turn to God. For we do not need grace for those things which we can do naturally. But the angel naturally turns to God, because he naturally loves God, as is clear from what has been said above. Therefore the angel did not need grace in order to turn to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0025","text":"[I q. 62 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30978)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0026","text":"Moreover, we seem to need assistance only for those things that are difficult. But it was not difficult for an angel to turn to God, since there was nothing in him that resisted this turning. Therefore, an angel did not need the assistance of grace in order to turn to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0027","text":"[I q. 62 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30979)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0028","text":"Moreover, to turn to God is to prepare oneself for grace; whence it is said in Zechariah 1, *Turn to me, and I will turn to you*. But we do not need grace in order to prepare ourselves for grace, since in this way one would proceed to infinity. Therefore, the angel did not need grace in order to turn to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0029","text":"[I q. 62 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30980)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0030","text":"But on the contrary, by turning to God an angel attains beatitude. Therefore, if he had not needed grace in order to turn to God, it would follow that he would not need grace in order to have eternal life. This is contrary to the saying of the Apostle, Romans 6, *the grace of God [is] eternal life*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0031","text":"[I q. 62 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30981)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0032","text":"I answer that it must be said that angels needed grace in order to turn to God, insofar as He is the object of beatitude. For, as was said above, the natural movement of the will is the principle of all those things that we will. Now the natural inclination of the will is toward that which is suitable according to nature. Therefore, if something is above nature, the will cannot be borne toward it unless assisted by some other supernatural principle. Thus it is clear that fire has a natural inclination to heat and to generate fire, but to generate flesh is above the natural power of fire; hence fire has no inclination to this, except insofar as it is moved as an instrument by the nutritive soul. But it was shown above, when God's knowledge was under discussion, that to see God through His essence, in which the ultimate beatitude of the rational creature consists, is above the nature of every created intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0033","text":"Hence no rational creature can have a movement of the will ordered toward that beatitude unless moved by a supernatural agent. And this we call the assistance of grace. Therefore it must be said that an angel could not turn by will toward that beatitude except through the assistance of grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0034","text":"[I q. 62 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30982)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0035","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that an angel naturally loves God insofar as He is the principle of natural being. But here we are speaking of turning to God insofar as He beatifies through the vision of His essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0036","text":"[I q. 62 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30983)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0037","text":"To the second it must be said that what transcends a power is difficult. But this happens in two ways. In one way, because it transcends a power according to its natural order. And then, if one can attain it by some assistance, it is called difficult; but if in no way, it is called impossible, just as it is impossible for a human being to fly. In another way, something transcends a power not according to the natural order of the power, but on account of some impediment attached to the power. Thus to ascend is not contrary to the natural order of the power of the motive soul, because the soul, as far as it is concerned, is naturally suited to move in any direction; but it is impeded from this by the weight of the body. Hence it is difficult for a human being to ascend. But to turn to ultimate beatitude is difficult for a human being both because it is above nature and because he has an impediment from the corruption of the body and the infection of sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0038","text":"But for an angel it is difficult solely because it is supernatural.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0039","text":"[I q. 62 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30984)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0040","text":"To the third it must be said that every movement of the will toward God can be called a turning toward Him. Therefore there is a threefold turning toward God. One is through perfect love, which belongs to a creature already enjoying God. And consummated grace is required for this turning. Another turning is that which is the merit of beatitude. And habitual grace, which is the principle of meriting, is required for this. The third turning is that by which someone prepares himself to have grace. And for this no habitual grace is required, but rather the operation of God turning the soul toward Himself, according to that saying of the last chapter of Lamentations, *Turn us, O Lord, to thee, and we shall be turned*. Hence it is clear that there is no proceeding to infinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0041","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0042","text":"[I q. 62 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30985)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0043","text":"We proceed thus to the third article. It seems that angels were not created in grace. For Augustine says, in Book II of *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, that the angelic nature was first created without form and called heaven, but afterward was formed and called light. But this formation is through grace. Therefore, they were not created in grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0044","text":"[I q. 62 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30986)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0045","text":"Moreover, grace inclines the rational creature toward God. Therefore, if an angel had been created in grace, no angel would have turned away from God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0046","text":"[I q. 62 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30987)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0047","text":"Moreover, grace is intermediate between nature and glory. But angels were not blessed in their creation. Therefore it seems that neither were they created in grace, but first in nature alone; afterward they acquired grace; and finally they were made blessed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0048","text":"[I q. 62 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30988)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0049","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in Book XII of *The City of God*: “Who produced the good will in the angels, unless He who created them with their will—that is, with the chaste love by which they adhere to Him—at the same time establishing nature in them and bestowing grace?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0050","text":"[I q. 62 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30989)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0051","text":"I answer that it must be said that, although there are diverse opinions on this matter, some saying that the angels were created in natural things alone, but others that they were created in grace, nevertheless it seems more probable that one should hold—and more consonant with the sayings of the saints—that they were created in sanctifying grace. For thus we see that all things which, in the course of time, are produced through the work of divine providence, with the creature acting under God, were produced in the first condition of things according to certain seminal principles, as Augustine says, *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*; as trees and animals and other such things. Now it is manifest that sanctifying grace is related to beatitude in this way, as a seminal principle in nature is related to a natural effect; hence, in 1 John III, grace is called the seed of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0052","text":"Therefore, just as, according to Augustine's opinion, it is held that, immediately in the first creation of corporeal creatures, the seminal principles of all natural effects were implanted in them, so from the beginning the angels were created in grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0053","text":"[I q. 62 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30990)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0054","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that formlessness of the angel can be understood either in comparison with the formation of glory, and thus formlessness preceded formation in time; or in comparison with the formation of grace, and thus it did not precede it in the order of time, but in the order of nature—as Augustine also maintains concerning corporeal formation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0055","text":"[I q. 62 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30991)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0056","text":"To the second, it must be said that every form inclines its subject according to the mode of its nature. But the natural mode of an intellectual nature is that it be borne freely toward those things which it wills. And therefore the inclination of grace does not impose necessity; rather, one who has grace can fail to use it and can sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0057","text":"[I q. 62 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30992)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0058","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although grace is intermediate between nature and glory in the order of nature, nevertheless in the order of time glory ought not to be present simultaneously with nature in a created nature, because it is the end of the operation of nature itself aided by grace. But grace is not related as the end of operation, because it is not from works, but as a principle of acting well. And therefore it was fitting that grace be given immediately with nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0059","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0060","text":"[I q. 62 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30993)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0061","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that a blessed angel did not merit his beatitude. For merit arises from the difficulty of the meritorious act. But an angel had no difficulty in acting well. Therefore a good operation was not meritorious for him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0062","text":"[I q. 62 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30994)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0063","text":"Further, we do not merit by natural things. But it was natural to an angel to turn toward God. Therefore by this he did not merit beatitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0064","text":"[I q. 62 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30995)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0065","text":"Further, if a blessed angel merited his beatitude, then he did so either before he had it or afterward. But not before, because, as seems to many, before then he did not have grace, without which there is no merit. Nor afterward either, because then he would still merit even now, which seems false; for then a lesser angel could, by meriting, attain to the rank of a superior angel, and the distinctions among the degrees of grace would not be stable—which is unfitting. Therefore a blessed angel did not merit his beatitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0066","text":"[I q. 62 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30996)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0067","text":"But on the contrary, in Revelation XXI it is said that the measure of the angel, in that heavenly Jerusalem, is the measure of a man. But man cannot attain beatitude except through merit. Therefore neither can an angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0068","text":"[I q. 62 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30997)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0069","text":"I answer that it must be said that perfect beatitude is natural to God alone, because for Him to be and to be blessed are the same. But for any creature, to be blessed is not its nature, but its ultimate end. Now every thing attains its ultimate end through its operation. And this operation leading to the end either brings about the end, when the end does not exceed the power of that which operates for the end, as medical treatment brings about health; or it merits the end, when the end exceeds the power of the one operating for the end, whence the end is expected from the gift of another. But ultimate beatitude exceeds both angelic and human nature, as is clear from what has been said. Hence it follows that both man and angel have merited their beatitude. And indeed, if the angel was created in grace, without which there is no merit, we can say without difficulty that he merited his beatitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0070","text":"And likewise if someone were to say that he had grace in whatever way before glory. But if he did not have grace before he was blessed, then one must say that he had beatitude without merit, as we have grace. Yet this is contrary to the nature of beatitude, which has the character of an end and is the reward of virtue, as the Philosopher also says in *Ethics* I. Or one must say that angels merit beatitude through those things which, already blessed, they perform in divine ministries, as others have said. Yet this is contrary to the nature of merit, for merit has the character of a way to an end; but it does not befit one who is already at the terminus to be moved toward the terminus. And thus no one merits what he already has. Or one must say that one and the same act of turning toward God, insofar as it is from free choice, is meritorious; and insofar as it attains the end, is blessed enjoyment.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0071","text":"But even this does not seem fitting, because free choice is not a sufficient cause of merit; hence an act cannot be meritorious insofar as it is from free choice, except insofar as it is informed by grace. But it cannot at the same time be informed by imperfect grace, which is the principle of meriting, and by perfect grace, which is the principle of enjoying. Hence it does not seem possible that one should at the same time enjoy and merit one's enjoyment. And therefore it is better to say that the angel had grace before he was blessed, through which he merited beatitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0072","text":"[I q. 62 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30998)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0073","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the difficulty of acting well is not in the angels from any contrariety or impediment of natural power, but from the fact that some good work is above the power of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0074","text":"[I q. 62 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#30999)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0075","text":"To the second, it must be said that by natural conversion the angel did not merit beatitude, but by the conversion of charity, which is through grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0076","text":"[I q. 62 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31000)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0077","text":"The answer to the third is clear from what has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0078","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0079","text":"[I q. 62 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31001)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0080","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth. It seems that the angel did not have beatitude immediately after one meritorious act. For it is more difficult for a human being to act well than for an angel. But a human being is not rewarded immediately after one act. Therefore neither is an angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0081","text":"[I q. 62 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31002)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0082","text":"Moreover, the angel could have had some act immediately at the beginning of his creation, and in an instant, since even natural bodies begin to be moved in the very instant of their creation; and if the motion of a body could be in an instant, as can the operations of intellect and will, they would have motion in the first instant of their generation. Therefore, if the angel merited beatitude through one motion of his will, he merited beatitude in the first instant of his creation. Therefore, if their beatitude is not delayed, they were beatified immediately in the first instant.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0083","text":"[I q. 62 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31003)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0084","text":"Moreover, between things greatly distant there must be many intermediates. But the state of the angels' beatitude is greatly distant from the state of their nature, and merit is intermediate between the two. Therefore it was necessary that the angel should arrive at beatitude through many intermediates.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0085","text":"[I q. 62 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31004)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0086","text":"On the contrary, the human soul and the angel are similarly ordered to beatitude; hence equality with the angels is promised to the saints in Luke XX. But the soul separated from the body, if it has the merit of beatitude, immediately obtains beatitude, unless there be some other impediment. Therefore, by equal reasoning, so too does the angel. But in the first act of charity he immediately had the merit of beatitude. Therefore, since there was no impediment in him, he immediately arrived at beatitude through only one meritorious act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0087","text":"[I q. 62 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31005)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0088","text":"I answer that it must be said that after the first act of charity by which he merited beatitude, the angel was immediately blessed. The reason for this is that grace perfects nature according to the mode of nature, just as every perfection is received in what is perfectible according to its mode. Now it is proper to angelic nature that it acquires natural perfection not through discursive reasoning, but has it immediately by nature, as was shown above. But just as by his nature the angel has an ordering to natural perfection, so by merit he has an ordering to glory. And thus beatitude followed immediately after merit in the angel. Moreover, the merit of beatitude can be present not only in the angel but also in the human being through a single act, because by any act informed by charity a human being merits beatitude. Hence it remains that immediately after one act informed by charity, the angel was blessed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0089","text":"[I q. 62 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31006)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0090","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that according to his nature a human being is not born to attain final perfection immediately, as an angel is. And therefore a longer way was given to the human being for meriting beatitude than to the angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0091","text":"[I q. 62 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31007)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0092","text":"To the second, it must be said that the angel is above the time of corporeal things; hence different instants in those things that pertain to angels are understood only according to succession in their acts. But there could not be simultaneously in them an act meriting beatitude and an act of beatitude, which is enjoyment; since one belongs to imperfect grace and the other to consummated grace. Hence it remains that different instants must be understood, in one of which he merited beatitude, and in the other was blessed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0093","text":"[I q. 62 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31008)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0094","text":"To the third, it must be said that it belongs to the nature of the angel that he immediately attain the perfection to which he is ordered. And therefore only one meritorious act is required, which can be called a medium on that account, because through it the angel is ordered to beatitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0095","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0096","text":"[I q. 62 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31009)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0097","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth point. It seems that the angels did not attain grace and glory according to the measure of their natural endowments. For grace is given from God's mere will. Therefore the measure of grace also depends on God's will, and not on the measure of natural endowments.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0098","text":"[I q. 62 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31010)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0099","text":"Moreover, a human act seems closer to grace than nature is, since a human act is preparatory to grace. But grace is not from works, as is said in Rom. XI. Therefore, much less is the measure of grace in angels according to the measure of their natural endowments.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0100","text":"[I q. 62 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31011)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0101","text":"Moreover, man and angel are alike ordered to beatitude or grace. But more grace is not given to a man according to the degree of his natural endowments. Therefore neither is it given to an angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0102","text":"[I q. 62 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31012)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0103","text":"On the contrary, the Master says, III Sentences, distinction II, that *the angels who were created more subtle by nature and more penetrating in wisdom were also endowed with greater gifts of grace*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0104","text":"[I q. 62 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31013)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0105","text":"I answer that it is reasonable that gifts of graces and the perfection of beatitude should have been given to the angels according to the degree of their natural endowments. The reason for this can be taken from two considerations. First, on the part of God Himself, who, through the order of His wisdom, established diverse grades in the angelic nature. Now, just as angelic nature was made by God for attaining grace and beatitude, so also the grades of angelic nature seem ordered to diverse grades of grace and glory; as, for example, if a builder polishes stones in order to construct a house, by the very fact that he fits some more beautifully and suitably, he seems to order them to a more honorable part of the house. Thus, therefore, it seems that God has ordered the angels whom He made of a higher nature to greater gifts of graces and more ample beatitude. Secondly, the same is apparent on the part of the angel himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0106","text":"For an angel is not composed of diverse natures, such that the inclination of one nature might impede or retard the impetus of the other, as happens in man, in whom the movement of the intellectual part is either retarded or impeded by the inclination of the sensitive part. But when there is nothing to retard or impede, nature is moved according to its whole power. And therefore it is reasonable that those angels who had a better nature should also have been turned to God more strongly and effectively. Now this also occurs among human beings: according to the intensity of their turning to God, greater grace and glory are given. Hence it seems that the angels who had better natural endowments had more grace and glory.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0107","text":"[I q. 62 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31014)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0108","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, just as grace is from God's mere will, so too is the angel's nature. And just as God's will ordered nature to grace, so also it ordered the grades of nature to the grades of grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0109","text":"[I q. 62 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31015)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0110","text":"To the second it must be said that the acts of a rational creature are from the creature itself, but nature is immediately from God. Hence it seems more fitting that grace be given according to the degree of nature than from works.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0111","text":"[I q. 62 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31016)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0112","text":"To the third it must be said that diversity of natural endowments is one way in angels, who differ in species, and another way in human beings, who differ in number alone. For difference according to species is on account of the end, but difference according to number is on account of matter. In man there is also something that can impede or retard the movement of the intellectual nature, but not in angels. Hence the same account does not apply to both.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0113","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0114","text":"[I q. 62 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31017)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0115","text":"We proceed thus to the seventh point. It seems that natural knowledge and love do not remain in the blessed angels. For, as is said in I Cor. XIII, *when that which is perfect has come, that which is in part will be done away with*. But natural love and knowledge are imperfect in comparison with blessed knowledge and love. Therefore, when beatitude comes, natural knowledge and love cease.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0116","text":"[I q. 62 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31018)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0117","text":"Moreover, where one thing suffices, another exists superfluously. But knowledge and love of glory suffice in the blessed angels. Therefore it would be superfluous for natural knowledge and love to remain in them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0118","text":"[I q. 62 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31019)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0119","text":"Moreover, the same power does not simultaneously have two acts, just as one line is not terminated at the same end by two points. But the blessed angels are always in the act of blessed knowledge and love; for happiness is not according to habit, but according to act, as is said in I Ethics. Therefore natural knowledge and love can never be in the angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0120","text":"[I q. 62 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31020)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0121","text":"But on the contrary, so long as a nature remains, its operation remains. But beatitude does not remove nature, since it is its perfection. Therefore, it does not remove natural cognition and love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0122","text":"[I q. 62 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31021)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0123","text":"I answer that it must be said that in the blessed angels natural cognition and love remain. For just as the principles of operations are related to one another, so too are the operations themselves. Now it is evident that nature is related to beatitude as first to second, because beatitude is added to nature. But the first must always be preserved in the second. Hence nature must be preserved in beatitude. And likewise, in the act of beatitude the act of nature must be preserved.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0124","text":"[I q. 62 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31022)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0125","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that an added perfection removes the imperfection opposed to it. But the imperfection of nature is not opposed to the perfection of beatitude, but underlies it, just as the imperfection of potency underlies the perfection of form; and potency is not removed by form, but the privation which is opposed to form is removed. Likewise, the imperfection of natural cognition is not opposed to the perfection of the cognition of glory, for nothing prevents something from being known simultaneously through diverse media, just as something can be known simultaneously through a probable and a demonstrative medium. And likewise an angel can simultaneously know God through the essence of God, which pertains to the cognition of glory, and through its own essence, which pertains to the cognition of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0126","text":"[I q. 62 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31023)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0127","text":"To the second, it must be said that the things belonging to beatitude suffice through themselves. But in order that they be, they presuppose the things belonging to nature, because no beatitude is subsistent through itself except uncreated beatitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0128","text":"[I q. 62 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31024)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0129","text":"To the third, it must be said that two operations of one power cannot exist simultaneously unless one is ordered to the other. But natural cognition and love are ordered to the cognition and love of glory. Hence nothing prevents there being simultaneously in an angel both natural cognition and love, and cognition and love of glory.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0130","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0131","text":"[I q. 62 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31025)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0132","text":"We proceed thus to the eighth article. It seems that a blessed angel can sin. For beatitude does not remove nature, as has been said. But it belongs to the nature of a creature that it can fail. Therefore a blessed angel can sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0133","text":"[I q. 62 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31026)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0134","text":"Further, rational powers are directed to opposites, as the Philosopher says. But the will of a blessed angel does not cease to be rational. Therefore it is directed to good and evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0135","text":"[I q. 62 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31027)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0136","text":"Further, it belongs to freedom of choice that a human being can choose good and evil. But freedom of choice is not diminished in blessed angels. Therefore they can sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0137","text":"[I q. 62 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31028)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0138","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in Book XI of On the Literal Meaning of Genesis, that the nature which cannot sin is in the holy angels. Therefore the holy angels cannot sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0139","text":"[I q. 62 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31029)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0140","text":"I answer that it must be said that blessed angels cannot sin. The reason for this is that their beatitude consists in this: that they see God through His essence. Now the essence of God is the very essence of goodness. Hence an angel seeing God is related to God Himself in the same way as anyone not seeing God is related to the common notion of good. But it is impossible for anyone to will or do anything except by attending to the good, or to will to turn away from the good as such. Therefore a blessed angel cannot will or act except by attending to God. But in so willing or acting, he cannot sin. Hence a blessed angel can in no way sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0141","text":"[I q. 62 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31030)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0142","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that a created good, considered in itself, can fail. But from perfect union with the uncreated good, such as the union of beatitude, it acquires that it cannot sin, for the reason already given.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0143","text":"[I q. 62 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31031)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0144","text":"To the second, it must be said that rational powers are directed to opposites in those matters to which they are not naturally ordered; but with respect to those to which they are naturally ordered, they are not directed to opposites. For the intellect cannot fail to assent to principles naturally known, and likewise the will cannot fail to adhere to the good insofar as it is good, because it is naturally ordered to the good as to its object. Therefore the will of an angel is directed to opposites with respect to many things to be done or not done. But with respect to God Himself, whom they see to be the very essence of goodness, they are not directed to opposites; rather, through Him they are directed to all things, whichever of the opposites they choose. This is without sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0145","text":"[I q. 62 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31032)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0146","text":"To the third, it must be said that free choice is related to choosing those things that are for the end as the intellect is related to conclusions. Now it is manifest that it belongs to the virtue of the intellect that it be able to proceed to diverse conclusions according to given principles; but that it proceed to some conclusion while passing over the order of the principles, this is from a defect of itself. Hence, that free choice can choose diverse things while preserving the order of the end belongs to the perfection of its freedom; but that it choose something by turning aside from the order of the end—which is to sin—belongs to a defect of freedom. Hence there is greater freedom of choice in the angels, who cannot sin, than in us, who can sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0147","text":"## Article 9","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0148","text":"[I q. 62 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31033)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0149","text":"We proceed thus to the ninth article. It seems that blessed angels can advance in beatitude. For charity is the principle of meriting. But there is perfect charity in angels. Therefore blessed angels can merit. But as merit increases, so too does the reward of beatitude increase. Therefore blessed angels can advance in beatitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0150","text":"[I q. 62 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31034)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0151","text":"Further, Augustine says, in the book *On Christian Doctrine*, that *God makes use of us for our benefit and for His goodness*. And likewise of the angels, whom He employs in spiritual ministries; *since they are ministering spirits, sent for service on account of those who shall receive the inheritance of salvation*, as is said in Heb. I. But this would not be for their benefit if through it they neither merited nor advanced in beatitude. Therefore it remains that blessed angels can both merit and advance in beatitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0152","text":"[I q. 62 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31035)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0153","text":"Further, it pertains to imperfection that one who is not at the highest point cannot advance. But angels are not at the highest point. Therefore, if they cannot advance to something greater, it seems that there is imperfection and defect in them. This is unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0154","text":"[I q. 62 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31036)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0155","text":"But on the contrary, to merit and to advance belong to the state of the way. But angels are not wayfarers, but comprehensors. Therefore blessed angels cannot merit, nor advance in beatitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0156","text":"[I q. 62 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31037)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0157","text":"I answer that it must be said that in every movement the intention of the mover is directed toward something determinate, to which it intends to bring the movable thing; for intention concerns an end, to which the infinite is opposed. Now it is manifest that, since a rational creature cannot attain by its own power its beatitude, which consists in the vision of God, as is clear from what has been said above, it needs to be moved by God to beatitude. Therefore there must be something determinate to which every rational creature is directed as to its last end. And this determinate thing cannot, in the divine vision, be with respect to that which is seen itself, because the highest truth is beheld by all the blessed in diverse degrees. But with respect to the mode of vision, a limit is prefigured in diverse ways by the intention of the one directing to the end.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0158","text":"For it is not possible that, just as a rational creature is brought forth to see the highest essence, so it be brought forth to the highest mode of vision, which is comprehension; for this mode can belong to God alone, as is clear from what has been said above. But since infinite efficacy is required to comprehend God, whereas a creature's efficacy in seeing can only be finite; and since every finite thing is distant from the infinite by infinite degrees; a rational creature can understand God in infinitely many ways, either more clearly or less clearly. And just as beatitude consists in the vision itself, so the degree of beatitude consists in a determinate mode of vision. Thus, therefore, every rational creature is brought by God to the end of beatitude in such a way that, by God's predestination, it is also brought to a determinate degree of beatitude. Hence, having attained that degree, it cannot pass to a higher one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0158"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0159","text":"[I q. 62 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31038)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0159"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0160","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that to merit belongs to that which is moved toward an end. Now a rational creature is moved toward an end not only by being acted upon, but also by acting. And if that end falls under the power of the rational creature, that operation will be called acquisitive of that end, just as a human being acquires knowledge by meditating; but if the end is not in its power, but is awaited from another, the operation will be meritorious of the end. But it does not belong to that which is at the final terminus to be moved, but rather to have been moved. Hence, it belongs to imperfect charity, which is of the way, to merit; but it does not belong to perfect charity to merit, but rather to enjoy the reward. Just as, in acquired habits, the operation preceding the habit is acquisitive of the habit, whereas that which proceeds from a habit already acquired is an operation already perfected with delight. And likewise, the act of perfect charity does not have the character of merit, but rather belongs to the perfection of the reward.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0160"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0161","text":"[I q. 62 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31039)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0161"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0162","text":"To the second, it must be said that something is called useful in two ways. In one way, as that which is on the way to an end, and thus the merit of beatitude is useful. In another way, as a part is useful to the whole, as a wall to a house. And in this way the ministries of the angels are useful to the blessed angels, insofar as they are a certain part of their beatitude; for to diffuse a possessed perfection into others belongs to the character of what is perfect insofar as it is perfect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0162"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0163","text":"[I q. 62 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31040)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0163"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0164","text":"To the third, it must be said that although a blessed angel is not in the highest degree of beatitude simply speaking, nevertheless it is in the ultimate degree with respect to itself, according to divine predestination. Yet the joy of the angels can be increased by the salvation of those who are saved through their ministry, according to Luke XV: *there is joy among the angels of God over one sinner doing penance*. But this joy pertains to the accidental reward, which can indeed be increased up to the day of judgment. Hence some say that, with respect to the accidental reward, they can also merit. But it is better to say that in no way can any blessed person merit unless he is at once a wayfarer and a comprehensor, as Christ, who alone was a wayfarer and a comprehensor. For they acquire the aforesaid joy more through the power of beatitude than they merit it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_54:P0164"]}],"source_digest":"628b8acb01f458d3b7ea2b46bce13a5566b4245ad180d084e6b227385a01e3d0","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 62"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 62","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55","title":"First Part · Question 63","order":64,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_55","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 63 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31041)\n\nDeinde considerandum est quomodo Angeli facti sunt mali. Et primo, quantum ad malum culpae; secundo, quantum ad malum poenae. Circa primum quaeruntur novem. Primo, utrum malum culpae in Angelo esse possit. Secundo, cuiusmodi peccata in eis esse possunt. Tertio, quid appetendo Angelus peccavit. Quarto, supposito quod aliqui peccato propriae voluntatis facti sunt mali, utrum aliqui naturaliter sint mali. Quinto, supposito quod non, utrum aliquis eorum in primo instanti suae creationis potuerit esse malus per actum propriae voluntatis. Sexto, supposito quod non, utrum aliqua mora fuerit inter creationem et lapsum. Septimo, utrum supremus inter cadentes, fuerit simpliciter inter omnes Angelos summus. Octavo, utrum peccatum primi Angeli fuerit aliis aliqua causa peccandi. Nono, utrum tot ceciderint, quot remanserunt.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 63 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31042)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod malum culpae in Angelis esse non possit. Quia malum non potest esse nisi in his quae sunt in potentia, ut dicitur in IX Metaphys., subiectum enim privationis est ens in potentia. Sed Angeli, cum sint formae subsistentes, non habent esse in potentia. Ergo in eis non potest esse malum.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31043)\n\nPraeterea, Angeli sunt digniores quam corpora caelestia. Sed in corporibus caelestibus non potest esse malum, ut philosophi dicunt. Ergo neque in Angelis.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31044)\n\nPraeterea, id quod est naturale, semper inest. Sed naturale est Angelis quod moveantur motu dilectionis in Deum. Ergo hoc ab eis removeri non potest. Sed diligendo Deum non peccant. Ergo Angeli peccare non possunt.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31045)\n\nPraeterea, appetitus non est nisi boni, vel apparentis boni. Sed in Angelis non potest esse apparens bonum, quod non sit verum bonum, quia in eis vel omnino error esse non potest, vel saltem non potest praecedere culpam. Ergo Angeli non possunt appetere nisi id quod est vere bonum. Sed nullus, appetendo id quod est vere bonum, peccat. Ergo Angelus appetendo non peccat.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31046)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Iob IV, *in Angelis suis reperit pravitatem*.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31047)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod tam Angelus quam quaecumque creatura rationalis, si in sua sola natura consideretur, potest peccare, et cuicumque creaturae hoc convenit ut peccare non possit, hoc habet ex dono gratiae, non ex conditione naturae. Cuius ratio est, quia peccare nihil est aliud quam declinare a rectitudine actus quam debet habere; sive accipiatur peccatum in naturalibus, sive in artificialibus, sive in moralibus. Solum autem illum actum a rectitudine declinare non contingit, cuius regula est ipsa virtus agentis. Si enim manus artificis esset ipsa regula incisionis, nunquam posset artifex nisi recte lignum incidere, sed si rectitudo incisionis sit ab alia regula, contingit incisionem esse rectam et non rectam. Divina autem voluntas sola est regula sui actus, quia non ad superiorem finem ordinatur. Omnis autem voluntas cuiuslibet creaturae rectitudinem in suo actu non habet, nisi secundum quod regulatur a voluntate divina, ad quam pertinet ultimus finis, sicut quaelibet voluntas inferioris debet regulari secundum voluntatem superioris, ut voluntas militis secundum voluntatem ducis exercitus. Sic igitur in sola voluntate divina peccatum esse non potest, in qualibet autem voluntate creaturae potest esse peccatum, secundum conditionem suae naturae.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31048)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in Angelis non est potentia ad esse naturale. Est tamen in eis potentia secundum intellectivam partem, ad hoc quod convertantur in hoc vel in illud. Et quantum ad hoc, potest in eis esse malum.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31049)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod corpora caelestia non habent operationem nisi naturalem. Et ideo sicut in natura eorum non potest esse corruptionis malum, ita nec in actione naturali eorum potest esse malum inordinationis. Sed supra actionem naturalem in Angelis est actio liberi arbitrii, secundum quam contingit in eis esse malum.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31050)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod naturale est Angelo quod convertatur motu dilectionis in Deum, secundum quod est principium naturalis esse. Sed quod convertatur in ipsum secundum quod est obiectum beatitudinis supernaturalis, hoc est ex amore gratuito, a quo averti potuit peccando.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31051)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod peccatum in actu liberi arbitrii contingit esse dupliciter. Uno modo, ex hoc quod aliquod malum eligitur, sicut homo peccat eligendo adulterium, quod secundum se est malum. Et tale peccatum semper procedit ex aliqua ignorantia vel errore, alioquin id quod est malum, non eligeretur ut bonum. Errat quidem adulter in particulari, eligens hanc delectationem inordinati actus quasi aliquod bonum ad nunc agendum, propter inclinationem passionis aut habitus; etiam si in universali non erret, sed veram de hoc sententiam teneat. Hoc autem modo in Angelo peccatum esse non potuit, quia nec in Angelis sunt passiones, quibus ratio aut intellectus ligetur, ut ex supra dictis patet; nec iterum primum peccatum habitus praecedere potuit ad peccatum inclinans. Alio modo contingit peccare per liberum arbitrium, eligendo aliquid quod secundum se est bonum, sed non cum ordine debitae mensurae aut regulae; ita quod defectus inducens peccatum sit solum ex parte electionis, quae non habet debitum ordinem, non ex parte rei electae; sicut si aliquis eligeret orare, non attendens ad ordinem ab Ecclesia institutum. Et huiusmodi peccatum non praeexigit ignorantiam, sed absentiam solum considerationis eorum quae considerari debent. Et hoc modo Angelus peccavit, convertendo se per liberum arbitrium ad proprium bonum, absque ordine ad regulam divinae voluntatis.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 63 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31052)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelis non possit esse solum peccatum superbiae et invidiae. In quemcumque enim cadit delectatio alicuius peccati, potest cadere peccatum illud. Sed Daemones delectantur etiam in obscenitatibus carnalium peccatorum, ut Augustinus dicit, II de Civ. Dei. Ergo in Daemonibus etiam peccata carnalia possunt esse.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31053)\n\nPraeterea, sicut superbia et invidia sunt peccata spiritualia, ita acedia et avaritia et ira. Sed spiritui conveniunt peccata spiritualia, sicut et carni peccata carnalia. Ergo non solum superbia et invidia in Angelis esse possunt, sed etiam acedia et avaritia.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31054)\n\nPraeterea, secundum Gregorium, in moralibus, ex superbia nascuntur plura vitia, et similiter ex invidia. Posita autem causa, ponitur effectus. Si ergo superbia et invidia in Angelis esse possunt, pari ratione et alia vitia in eis esse possunt.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31055)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XIV libro de Civ. Dei, quod *Diabolus non est fornicator aut ebriosus, neque aliquid huiusmodi, est tamen superbus et invidus*.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31056)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod peccatum aliquod in aliquo esse potest dupliciter, uno modo, secundum reatum; alio modo, secundum affectum. Secundum reatum quidem omnia peccata in Daemonibus esse contingit, quia dum homines ad omnia peccata inducunt, omnium peccatorum reatum incurrunt. Secundum affectum vero illa solum peccata in malis Angelis esse possunt, ad quae contingit affici spiritualem naturam. Spiritualem autem naturam affici non contingit ad bona quae sunt propria corpori, sed ad ea quae in rebus spiritualibus inveniri possunt, nihil enim afficitur nisi ad id quod suae naturae potest esse quodam modo conveniens. In spiritualibus autem bonis non potest esse peccatum dum aliquis ad ea afficitur, nisi per hoc quod in tali affectu superioris regula non servatur. Et hoc est peccatum superbiae, non subdi superiori in eo quo debet. Unde peccatum primum Angeli non potest esse aliud quam superbia. Sed consequenter potuit in eis esse etiam invidia. Eiusdem enim rationis est quod affectus tendat in aliquid appetendum, et quod renitatur opposito. Invidus autem ex hoc de bono alterius dolet, inquantum bonum alterius aestimat sui boni impedimentum. Non autem bonum alterius poterat aestimari impedimentum boni affectati per Angelum malum, nisi inquantum affectavit excellentiam singularem, quae quidem singularitas per alterius excellentiam cessat. Et ideo post peccatum superbiae consecutum est in Angelo peccante malum invidiae, secundum quod de bono hominis doluit; et etiam de excellentia divina, secundum quod eo Deus contra voluntatem ipsius Diaboli utitur in gloriam divinam.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31057)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Daemones non delectantur in obscenitatibus carnalium peccatorum; quasi ipsi afficiantur ad delectationes carnales, sed hoc totum ex invidia procedit, quod in peccatis hominum quibuscumque delectantur, inquantum sunt impedimenta humani boni.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31058)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod avaritia, secundum quod est speciale peccatum, est immoderatus appetitus rerum temporalium quae veniunt in usum vitae humanae, quaecumque pecunia aestimari possunt, et ad ista non afficiuntur Daemones, sicut nec ad delectationes carnales. Unde avaritia proprie sumpta in eis esse non potest. Sed si avaritia dicatur omnis immoderata cupiditas habendi quodcumque bonum creatum, sic avaritia continetur in superbia quae est in Daemonibus. Ira vero cum quadam passione est, sicut et concupiscentia. Unde ipsa in Daemonibus esse non potest nisi metaphorice. Acedia vero est quaedam tristitia, qua homo redditur tardus ad spirituales actus propter corporalem laborem; qui Daemonibus non competit. Et sic patet quod sola superbia et invidia sunt pure spiritualia peccata, quae Daemonibus competere possunt, ita tamen quod invidia non sumatur pro passione, sed pro voluntate renitente bono alterius.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31059)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod sub invidia et superbia, prout in Daemonibus ponuntur, comprehenduntur omnia peccata quae ab illis derivantur.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 63 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31060)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Diabolus non appetierit esse ut Deus. Illud enim quod non cadit in apprehensione, non cadit in appetitu, cum bonum apprehensum moveat appetitum vel sensibilem vel rationalem, vel intellectualem (in solo enim huiusmodi appetitu contingit esse peccatum). Sed creaturam aliquam esse aequalem Deo, non cadit in apprehensione, implicat enim contradictionem, quia necesse est finitum esse infinitum, si aequatur infinito. Ergo Angelus non potuit appetere esse ut Deus.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31061)\n\nPraeterea, illud quod est finis naturae, absque peccato appeti potest. Sed assimilari Deo est finis in quem tendit naturaliter quaelibet creatura. Si ergo Angelus appetiit esse ut Deus, non per aequalitatem, sed per similitudinem, videtur quod in hoc non peccaverit.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31062)\n\nPraeterea, Angelus in maiori plenitudine sapientiae conditus est quam homo. Sed nullus homo, nisi omnino amens, eligit esse aequalis Angelo, nedum Deo, quia electio non est nisi possibilium, de quibus est consilium. Ergo multo minus peccavit Angelus appetendo esse ut Deus.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31063)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Isaiae XIV, ex persona Diaboli, *ascendam in caelum, et ero similis altissimo*. Et Augustinus dicit in libro de quaestionibus Vet. Test., quod elatione inflatus, voluit dici Deus.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31064)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Angelus, absque omni dubio, peccavit appetendo esse ut Deus. Sed hoc potest intelligi dupliciter, uno modo, per aequiparantiam; alio modo, per similitudinem. Primo quidem modo, non potuit appetere esse ut Deus, quia scivit hoc esse impossibile, naturali cognitione; nec primum actum peccandi in ipso praecessit vel habitus vel passio ligans cognoscitivam ipsius virtutem, ut in particulari deficiens eligeret impossibile, sicut in nobis interdum accidit. Et tamen, dato quod esset possibile, hoc esset contra naturale desiderium. Inest enim unicuique naturale desiderium ad conservandum suum esse, quod non conservaretur, si transmutaretur in alteram naturam. Unde nulla res quae est in inferiori gradu naturae, potest appetere superioris naturae gradum, sicut asinus non appetit esse equus, quia si transferretur in gradum superioris naturae, iam ipsum non esset. Sed in hoc imaginatio decipitur, quia enim homo appetit esse in altiori gradu quantum ad aliqua accidentalia, quae possunt crescere absque corruptione subiecti, aestimatur quod possit appetere altiorem gradum naturae, in quem pervenire non posset nisi esse desineret. Manifestum est autem quod Deus excedit Angelum, non secundum aliqua accidentalia, sed secundum gradum naturae, et etiam unus Angelus alium. Unde impossibile est quod Angelus inferior appetat esse aequalis superiori; nedum quod appetat esse aequalis Deo. Appetere autem esse ut Deus per similitudinem, contingit dupliciter. Uno modo, quantum ad id in quo aliquid natum est Deo assimilari. Et sic, si aliquis quantum ad hoc appetat esse Deo similis, non peccat, dummodo similitudinem Dei debito ordine appetat adipisci, ut scilicet eam a Deo habeat. Peccaret vero si quis etiam appeteret secundum iustitiam esse similis Deo, quasi propria virtute, et non ex virtute Dei. Alio vero modo potest aliquis appetere similis esse Deo, quantum ad hoc in quo non natus est assimilari; sicut si quis appeteret creare caelum et terram, quod est proprium Dei; in quo appetitu esset peccatum. Et hoc modo Diabolus appetiit esse ut Deus. Non ut ei assimilaretur quantum ad hoc quod est nulli subesse simpliciter, quia sic etiam suum non esse appeteret, cum nulla creatura esse possit nisi per hoc quod sub Deo esse participat. Sed in hoc appetiit esse similis Deo, quia appetiit ut finem ultimum beatitudinis id ad quod virtute suae naturae poterat pervenire, avertens suum appetitum a beatitudine supernaturali, quae est ex gratia Dei. Vel si appetiit ut ultimum finem illam Dei similitudinem quae datur ex gratia, voluit hoc habere per virtutem suae naturae, non ex divino auxilio secundum Dei dispositionem. Et hoc consonat dictis Anselmi, qui dicit quod appetiit illud ad quod pervenisset si stetisset. Et haec duo quodammodo in idem redeunt, quia secundum utrumque appetiit finalem beatitudinem per suam virtutem habere, quod est proprium Dei. Quia vero quod est per se, est principium et causa eius quod est per aliud, ex hoc etiam consecutum est quod appetiit aliquem principatum super alia habere. In quo etiam perverse voluit Deo assimilari.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 3 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31065)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ad omnia obiecta.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 63 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31066)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aliqui Daemones sint naturaliter mali. Dicit enim Porphyrius, ut Augustinus introducit X de Civ. Dei, quod *est quoddam genus Daemonum natura fallax, simulans deos et animas defunctorum*. Sed esse fallacem est esse malum. Ergo aliqui Daemones sunt naturaliter mali.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31067)\n\nPraeterea, sicut Angeli sunt creati a Deo, ita et homines. Sed aliqui homines sunt naturaliter mali, de quibus dicitur Sap. XII, *erat eorum malitia naturalis*. Ergo et Angeli aliqui possunt esse naturaliter mali.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31068)\n\nPraeterea, aliqua animalia irrationalia habent quasdam naturales malitias, sicut vulpes naturaliter est subdola, et lupus naturaliter est rapax, et tamen sunt creaturae Dei. Ergo et Daemones, licet sint creaturae Dei, possunt esse naturaliter mali.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31069)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *Daemones non sunt natura mali*.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31070)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod omne quod est, inquantum est et naturam habet aliquam, in bonum aliquod naturaliter tendit, utpote ex principio bono existens, quia semper effectus convertitur in suum principium. Contingit autem alicui bono particulari aliquod malum esse adiunctum, sicut igni coniungitur hoc malum quod est esse consumptivum aliorum, sed bono universali nullum malum potest esse adiunctum. Si ergo aliquid sit cuius natura ordinetur in aliquod bonum particulare, potest naturaliter tendere in aliquod malum, non inquantum malum, sed per accidens, inquantum est coniunctum cuidam bono. Si vero aliquid sit cuius natura ordinetur in aliquod bonum secundum communem boni rationem hoc secundum suam naturam non potest tendere in aliquod malum. Manifestum est autem quod quaelibet natura intellectualis habet ordinem in bonum universale, quod potest apprehendere, et quod est obiectum voluntatis. Unde cum Daemones sint substantiae intellectuales, nullo modo possunt habere inclinationem naturalem in aliquod quodcumque malum. Et ideo non possunt esse naturaliter mali.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31071)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus ibidem reprehendit Porphyrium de hoc quod dixit quod Daemones erant naturaliter fallaces, dicens eos non esse naturaliter fallaces, sed propria voluntate. Porphyrius autem hac ratione posuit Daemones esse natura fallaces, quia ponebat Daemones esse animalia habentia naturam sensitivam. Natura autem sensitiva ordinatur ad aliquod bonum particulare, cui potest esse coniunctum malum. Et secundum hoc, aliquam inclinationem naturalem habere possunt ad malum; per accidens tamen, inquantum malum est coniunctum bono.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31072)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod malitia aliquorum hominum potest dici naturalis, vel propter consuetudinem, quae est altera natura; vel propter naturalem inclinationem ex parte naturae sensitivae, ad aliquam inordinatam passionem, sicut quidam dicuntur naturaliter iracundi vel concupiscentes; non autem ex parte naturae intellectualis.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31073)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod animalia bruta secundum naturam sensitivam habent naturalem inclinationem ad quaedam particularia bona, quibus coniuncta sunt aliqua mala; sicut vulpes ad quaerendum victum sagaciter, cui adiungitur dolositas. Unde esse dolosum non est malum vulpi, cum sit ei naturale; sicut nec esse furiosum est malum cani, sicut Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 63 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31074)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Diabolus in primo instanti suae creationis fuerit malus per culpam propriae voluntatis. Dicitur enim Ioan. VIII, de Diabolo, *ille homicida erat ab initio*.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31075)\n\nPraeterea, secundum Augustinum, I super Gen. ad Litt., informitas creaturae non praecessit formationem tempore, sed origine tantum. Per caelum autem quod legitur primo creatum, ut ipse dicit in II libro, intelligitur natura angelica informis; per hoc autem quod dicitur quod Deus dixit, fiat lux, et facta est lux, intelligitur formatio eius per conversionem ad verbum; simul ergo natura Angeli creata est, et facta est lux. Sed simul dum facta est lux, distincta est a tenebris, per quas intelliguntur Angeli peccantes. Ergo in primo instanti suae creationis quidam Angeli fuerunt beati, et quidam peccaverunt.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31076)\n\nPraeterea, peccatum opponitur merito. Sed in primo instanti suae creationis aliqua natura intellectualis potest mereri; sicut anima Christi, vel etiam ipsi boni Angeli. Ergo et Daemones in primo instanti suae creationis potuerunt peccare.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31077)\n\nPraeterea, natura angelica virtuosior est quam natura corporea. Sed res corporalis statim in primo instanti suae creationis incipit habere suam operationem; sicut ignis in primo instanti quo generatus est, incipit moveri sursum. Ergo et Angelus in primo instanti suae creationis potuit operari. Aut ergo habuit operationem rectam, aut non rectam. Si rectam, cum gratiam habuerint, per eam meruerunt beatitudinem. In Angelis autem statim ad meritum sequitur praemium, ut supra dictum est. Ergo fuissent statim beati, et ita nunquam peccassent, quod est falsum. Relinquitur ergo quod in primo instanti, non recte operando, peccaverunt.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31078)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *vidit Deus cuncta quae fecerat, et erant valde bona*. Inter ea autem erant etiam Daemones. Ergo et Daemones aliquando fuerunt boni.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31079)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam posuerunt quod statim Daemones in primo instanti suae creationis mali fuerunt, non quidem per naturam, sed per peccatum propriae voluntatis, quia *ex quo est factus Diabolus, iustitiam recusavit. Cui sententiae*, ut Augustinus dicit, XI de Civ. Dei, *quisquis acquiescit, non cum illis haereticis sapit, idest Manichaeis, qui dicunt quod Diabolus habet naturam mali*. Sed quia haec opinio auctoritati Scripturae contradicit (dicitur enim, sub figura principis Babylonis, de Diabolo, Isaiae XIV, *quomodo cecidisti, Lucifer, qui mane oriebaris?* Et Ezech. XXVIII, *in deliciis Paradisi Dei fuisti* dicitur ad Diabolum sub persona regis Tyri), ideo a magistris haec opinio tanquam erronea rationabiliter reprobata est. Unde aliqui dixerunt quod Angeli in primo instanti suae creationis peccare potuerunt, sed non peccaverunt. Sed haec opinio etiam a quibusdam improbatur ea ratione quia, cum duae operationes se consequuntur, impossibile videtur quod in eodem nunc utraque operatio terminetur. Manifestum est autem quod peccatum Angeli fuit operatio creatione posterior. Terminus autem creationis est ipsum esse Angeli; terminus vero operationis peccati est quod sunt mali. Impossibile ergo videtur quod in primo instanti quo Angelus esse coepit, fuerit malus. Sed haec ratio non videtur sufficiens. Habet enim solum locum in motibus temporalibus, qui successive aguntur; sicut si motus localis sequitur ad alterationem, non potest in eodem instanti terminari alteratio et localis motus. Sed si sunt mutationes instantaneae, simul et in eodem instanti potest esse terminus primae et secundae mutationis; sicut in eodem instanti in quo illuminatur luna a sole, illuminatur aer a luna. Manifestum est autem quod creatio est instantanea; et similiter motus liberi arbitrii in Angelis; non enim indigent collatione et discursu rationis, ut ex supra dictis patet. Unde nihil prohibet simul et in eodem instanti esse terminum creationis, et terminum liberi arbitrii. Et ideo aliter dicendum est, quod impossibile fuit Angelum in primo instanti peccasse per inordinatum actum liberi arbitrii. Quamvis enim res aliqua in primo instanti quo esse incipit, simul incipere possit operari; tamen illa operatio quae simul incipit cum esse rei, est ei ab agente a quo habet esse; sicut moveri sursum inest igni a generante. Unde si aliqua res habeat esse ab agente deficiente, quod possit esse causa defectivae actionis, poterit in primo instanti in quo incipit esse, habere defectivam operationem; sicut si tibia quae nascitur clauda ex debilitate seminis, statim incipiat claudicare. Agens autem quod Angelos in esse produxit, scilicet Deus, non potest esse causa peccati. Unde non potest dici quod Diabolus in primo instanti suae creationis fuerit malus.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31080)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit, XI de Civ. Dei, *cum dicitur quod Diabolus ab initio peccat non ab initio ex quo creatus est, peccare putandus est, sed ab initio peccati*; scilicet quia nunquam a peccato suo recessit.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31081)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod illa distinctio lucis et tenebrarum, secundum quod per tenebras peccata Daemonum intelliguntur, accipienda est secundum Dei praescientiam. Unde Augustinus dicit, XI de Civ. Dei, quod *solus lucem ac tenebras discernere potuit, qui potuit etiam, priusquam caderent, praescire casuros*.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31082)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod quidquid est in merito, est a Deo, et ideo in primo instanti suae creationis Angelus mereri potuit. Sed non est similis ratio de peccato, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31083)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod Deus non discrevit inter Angelos ante aversionem quorundam et conversionem aliorum, ut Augustinus dicit, XI de Civ. Dei, et ideo omnes, in gratia creati in primo instanti meruerunt. Sed quidam eorum statim impedimentum praestiterunt suae beatitudinis, praecedens meritum mortificantes. Et ideo beatitudine quam meruerunt, sunt privati.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 63 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31084)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aliqua mora fuerit inter creationem et lapsum Angeli. Dicitur enim Ezech. XXVIII, *ambulasti perfectus in viis tuis a die conceptionis tuae, donec inventa est iniquitas in te*. Sed ambulatio, cum sit motus continuus, requirit aliquam moram. Ergo aliqua mora fuit inter creationem Diaboli et eius lapsum.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31085)\n\nPraeterea, Origenes dicit quod *serpens antiquus non statim supra pectus et ventrem suum ambulavit*; per quod intelligitur eius peccatum. Ergo Diabolus non statim post primum instans suae creationis peccavit.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31086)\n\nPraeterea, posse peccare commune est homini et Angelo. Fuit autem aliqua mora inter formationem hominis et eius peccatum. Ergo, pari ratione, fuit aliqua mora inter formationem Diaboli et eius peccatum.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 6 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31087)\n\nPraeterea, aliud instans fuit in quo Diabolus peccavit, ab instanti in quo creatus fuit. Sed inter quaelibet duo instantia cadit tempus medium. Ergo aliqua mora fuit inter creationem eius et lapsum.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31088)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Ioan. VIII, de Diabolo, quod *in veritate non stetit*. Et sicut Augustinus dicit, XI de Civ. Dei, *oportet ut hoc sic accipiamus, quod in veritate fuerit, sed non permanserit*.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31089)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa hoc est duplex opinio. Sed probabilior, et sanctorum dictis magis consona est, quod statim post primum instans suae creationis Diabolus peccaverit. Et hoc necesse est dicere, si ponatur quod in primo instanti suae creationis in actum liberi arbitrii proruperit, et cum gratia fuerit creatus, ut supra diximus. Cum enim Angeli per unum actum meritorium ad beatitudinem perveniant, ut supra dictum est; si Diabolus in primo instanti, in gratia creatus, meruit, statim post primum instans beatitudinem accepisset, nisi statim impedimentum praestitisset peccando. Si vero ponatur quod Angelus in gratia creatus non fuerit; vel quod in primo instanti actum liberi arbitrii non potuerit habere; nihil prohibet aliquam moram fuisse inter creationem et lapsum.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31090)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod per motus corporales, qui per tempus mensurantur, quandoque in sacra Scriptura intelliguntur metaphorice motus spirituales instantanei. Et sic per ambulationem intelligitur motus liberi arbitrii tendentis in bonum.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31091)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Origenes dicit quod *serpens antiquus non a principio, nec statim supra pectus* ambulavit, propter primum instans, in quo malus non fuit.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31092)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Angelus habet liberum arbitrium inflexibile post electionem, et ideo nisi statim post primum instans, in quo naturalem motum habuit ad bonum, impedimentum beatitudini praestitisset, fuisset firmatus in bono. Sed non est simile de homine. Et ideo ratio non sequitur.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 6 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31093)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod inter quaelibet duo instantia esse tempus medium, habet veritatem inquantum tempus est continuum, ut probatur in VI Physic. Sed in Angelis, qui non sunt subiecti caelesti motui, qui primo per tempus continuum mensuratur, tempus accipitur pro ipsa successione operationum intellectus, vel etiam affectus. Sic igitur instans primum in Angelis intelligitur respondere operationi mentis angelicae, qua se in seipsam convertit per vespertinam cognitionem, quia in primo die commemoratur vespere, sed non mane. Et haec quidem operatio in omnibus bona fuit. Sed ab hac operatione quidam per matutinam cognitionem ad laudem verbi sunt conversi, quidam vero, in seipsis remanentes, facti sunt nox, per superbiam intumescentes, ut Augustinus dicit, IV super Gen. ad Litt. Et sic prima operatio fuit omnibus communis; sed in secunda sunt discreti. Et ideo in primo instanti omnes fuerunt boni; sed in secundo fuerunt boni a malis distincti.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 63 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31094)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ille Angelus qui fuit supremus inter peccantes, non fuerit supremus inter omnes. Dicitur enim de eo Ezech. XXVIII, *tu Cherub extentus et protegens, posui te in monte sancto Dei*. Sed ordo Cherubim est sub ordine Seraphim, ut Dionysius dicit, VII cap. Ang. Hier. Ergo Angelus qui fuit supremus inter peccantes, non fuit supremus inter omnes.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31095)\n\nPraeterea, Deus fecit naturam intellectualem propter beatitudinem consequendam. Si igitur Angelus qui fuit supremus inter omnes, peccavit, sequitur quod ordinatio divina fuerit frustrata in nobilissima creatura. Quod est inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31096)\n\nPraeterea, quanto aliquid magis inclinatur in aliquid, tanto minus potest ab illo deficere. Sed Angelus quanto est superior, tanto magis inclinatur in Deum. Ergo minus potest a Deo peccando deficere. Et sic videtur quod Angelus qui peccavit, non fuerit supremus inter omnes, sed de inferioribus.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31097)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Gregorius, in homilia de centum ovibus, *quod primus Angelus qui peccavit, dum cunctis agminibus Angelorum praelatus, eorum claritatem transcenderet, ex eorum comparatione clarior fuit*.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31098)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in peccato est duo considerare, scilicet pronitatem ad peccandum; et motivum ad peccandum. Si ergo consideremus in Angelis pronitatem ad peccandum, minus videtur quod peccaverint superiores Angeli, quam inferiores. Et propter hoc Damascenus dicit quod maior eorum qui peccaverunt, fuit terrestri ordini praelatus. Et videtur haec opinio consonare positioni Platonicorum, quam Augustinus recitat in Lib. de Civ. Dei VIII et X. Dicebant enim quod omnes dii erant boni, sed Daemonum quidam boni, quidam mali; deos nominantes substantias intellectuales quae sunt a globo lunari superius, Daemones vero substantias intellectuales quae sunt a globo lunari inferius, superiores hominibus ordine naturae. Nec est abiicienda haec opinio tanquam a fide aliena, quia tota creatura corporalis administratur a Deo per Angelos, ut Augustinus dicit, III de Trin.; unde nihil prohibet dicere inferiores Angelos divinitus distributos esse ad administrandum inferiora corpora, superiores vero ad administrandum corpora superiora, supremos vero ad assistendum Deo. Et secundum hoc Damascenus dicit quod illi qui ceciderunt, fuerunt de inferioribus, in quorum etiam ordine aliqui boni Angeli permanserunt. Si vero consideretur motivum ad peccandum, maius invenitur in superioribus quam in inferioribus. Fuit enim Daemonum peccatum superbia, ut supra dictum est; cuius motivum est excellentia, quae fuit maior in superioribus. Et ideo Gregorius dicit quod ille qui peccavit, fuit superior inter omnes. Et hoc videtur probabilius. Quia peccatum Angeli non processit ex aliqua pronitate, sed ex solo libero arbitrio, unde magis videtur consideranda esse ratio quae sumitur a motivo ad peccandum. Non est tamen inde alii opinioni praeiudicandum, quia etiam in principe inferiorum Angelorum potuit esse aliquod motivum ad peccandum.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31099)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Cherubim interpretatur plenitudo scientiae; Seraphim autem interpretatur ardentes sive incedentes. Et sic patet quod Cherubim denominatur a scientia, quae potest esse cum mortali peccato; Seraphim vero denominatur ab ardore caritatis, quae cum peccato mortali esse non potest. Et ideo primus Angelus peccans non est denominatus Seraphim, sed Cherubim.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31100)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod divina intentio non frustratur nec in his qui peccant, nec in his qui salvantur, utrorumque enim eventum Deus praecognoscit, et ex utroque habet gloriam, dum hos ex sua bonitate salvat, illos ex sua iustitia punit. Ipsa vero creatura intellectualis, dum peccat, a fine debito deficit. Nec hoc est inconveniens in quacumque creatura sublimi, sic enim creatura intellectualis instituta est a Deo, ut in eius arbitrio positum sit agere propter finem.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31101)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod quantacumque inclinatio ad bonum fuerit in supremo Angelo, tamen ei necessitatem non inducebat. Unde potuit per liberum arbitrium eam non sequi.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 63 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31102)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod peccatum primi Angeli peccantis non fuit aliis causa peccandi. Causa enim prior est causato. Sed omnes simul peccaverunt, ut Damascenus dicit. Ergo peccatum unius non fuit aliis causa peccandi.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31103)\n\nPraeterea, primum peccatum Angeli non potest esse nisi superbia, ut supra dictum est. Sed superbia excellentiam quaerit. Magis autem excellentiae repugnat quod aliquis inferiori subdatur, quam superiori, et sic non videtur quod Daemones peccaverint per hoc quod voluerunt subesse alicui superiorum Angelorum, potius quam Deo. Sic autem peccatum unius Angeli fuisset aliis causa peccandi, si eos ad hoc induxisset ut sibi subiicerentur. Non ergo videtur quod peccatum primi Angeli fuerit causa peccandi aliis.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31104)\n\nPraeterea, maius peccatum est velle subesse alteri contra Deum, quam contra Deum alteri velle praeesse, quia minus habet de motivo ad peccandum. Si ergo peccatum primi Angeli fuit aliis causa peccandi in hoc, quod eos ut sibi subiicerentur induxit, gravius peccassent inferiores Angeli quam supremus, quod est contra hoc quod super illud Psalmi CIII, *draco iste quem formasti*, dicit Glossa, *qui ceteris in esse erat excellentior, factus est in malitia maior*. Non ergo peccatum primi Angeli fuit aliis causa peccandi.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31105)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Apoc. XII, quod draco traxit secum tertiam partem stellarum.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31106)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod peccatum primi Angeli fuit aliis causa peccandi, non quidem cogens, sed quadam quasi exhortatione inducens. Cuius signum ex hoc apparet, quod omnes Daemones illi supremo subduntur; ut manifeste apparet per illud quod dicit dominus, Matth. XXV, *ite, maledicti, in ignem aeternum, qui paratus est Diabolo et Angelis eius*. Habet enim hoc ordo divinae iustitiae, ut cuius suggestioni aliquis consentit in culpa, eius potestati subdatur in poena; secundum illud II Petr. II, a quo quis superatus est, huic servus addictus est.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31107)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet simul Daemones peccaverint, tamen peccatum unius potuit esse aliis causa peccandi. Angelus enim non indiget ad eligendum vel exhortandum vel etiam consentiendum, temporis mora; sicut homo, qui deliberatione indiget ad eligendum et ad consentiendum, et locutione vocali ad exhortandum, quorum utrumque tempore agitur. Manifestum est autem quod etiam homo simul dum aliquid iam corde concepit, in eodem instanti incipit loqui. Et in ultimo instanti locutionis, in quo aliquis sensum loquentis capit, potest assentire ei quod dicitur, ut patet maxime in primis conceptionibus, quas quisque probat auditas. Sublato ergo tempore locutionis et deliberationis quod in nobis requiritur, in eodem instanti in quo primus Angelus suam affectionem intelligibili locutione expressit, possibile fuit aliis in eam consentire.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31108)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod superbus, ceteris paribus, magis vult subesse superiori quam inferiori. Sed si aliquam excellentiam consequatur sub inferiori, quam sub superiori consequi non possit, magis eligit inferiori subesse quam superiori. Sic igitur non fuit contra superbiam Daemonum quod subesse inferiori voluerunt, in eius principatum consentientes; ad hoc eum principem et ducem habere volentes, ut virtute naturali suam ultimam beatitudinem consequerentur; praesertim quia supremo Angelo naturae ordine etiam tunc subiecti erant.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31109)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, Angelus non habet aliquid retardans, sed secundum suam totam virtutem movetur in illud ad quod movetur, sive in bonum sive in malum. Quia igitur supremus Angelus maiorem habuit naturalem virtutem quam inferiores, intensiori motu in peccatum prolapsus est. Et ideo factus est etiam in malitia maior.\n\n## Articulus 9\n\n[I q. 63 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31110)\n\nAd nonum sic proceditur. Videtur quod plures peccaverunt de Angelis, quam permanserunt. Quia, ut dicit philosophus, *malum est ut in pluribus, bonum ut in paucioribus*.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31111)\n\nPraeterea, iustitia et peccatum eadem ratione inveniuntur in Angelis et hominibus. Sed in hominibus plures inveniuntur mali quam boni; secundum illud Eccle. I, *stultorum infinitus est numerus*. Ergo pari ratione in Angelis.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31112)\n\nPraeterea, Angeli distinguuntur secundum personas, et secundum ordines. Si igitur plures personae angelicae remanserunt, videtur etiam quod non de omnibus ordinibus aliqui peccaverunt.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31113)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur IV Reg. VI, *plures nobiscum sunt quam cum illis*; quod exponitur de bonis Angelis qui sunt nobiscum in auxilium, et de malis qui nobis adversantur.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31114)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod plures Angeli permanserunt quam peccaverunt. Quia peccatum est contra naturalem inclinationem, ea vero quae contra naturam fiunt, ut in paucioribus accidunt; natura enim consequitur suum effectum vel semper, vel ut in pluribus.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31115)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod philosophus loquitur quantum ad homines, in quibus malum contingit ex hoc quod sequuntur bona sensibilia, quae sunt pluribus nota, deserto bono rationis, quod paucioribus notum est. In Angelis autem non est nisi natura intellectualis. Unde non est similis ratio.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31116)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ad secundum.\n\n[I q. 63 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31117)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod secundum illos qui dicunt quod Diabolus maior fuit de inferiori ordine Angelorum, qui praesunt terrestribus, manifestum est quod non de quolibet ordine ceciderunt, sed de infimo tantum. Secundum vero illos qui ponunt maiorem Diabolum de supremo fuisse ordine, probabile est quod de quolibet ordine aliqui ceciderunt; sicut et in quemlibet ordinem homines assumuntur in supplementum ruinae angelicae. In quo etiam magis comprobatur libertas liberi arbitrii, quae secundum quemlibet gradum creaturae in malum flecti potest. In sacra Scriptura tamen nomina quorundam ordinum, ut Seraphim et thronorum, Daemonibus non attribuuntur; quia haec nomina sumuntur ab ardore caritatis et ab inhabitatione Dei, quae non possunt esse cum peccato mortali. Attribuuntur autem eis nomina Cherubim, potestatum et principatuum, quia haec nomina sumuntur a scientia et potentia, quae bonis malisque possunt esse communia.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0002","text":"[I q. 63 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31041)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est quomodo Angeli facti sunt mali. Et primo, quantum ad malum culpae; secundo, quantum ad malum poenae. Circa primum quaeruntur novem. Primo, utrum malum culpae in Angelo esse possit. Secundo, cuiusmodi peccata in eis esse possunt. Tertio, quid appetendo Angelus peccavit. Quarto, supposito quod aliqui peccato propriae voluntatis facti sunt mali, utrum aliqui naturaliter sint mali. Quinto, supposito quod non, utrum aliquis eorum in primo instanti suae creationis potuerit esse malus per actum propriae voluntatis. Sexto, supposito quod non, utrum aliqua mora fuerit inter creationem et lapsum. Septimo, utrum supremus inter cadentes, fuerit simpliciter inter omnes Angelos summus. Octavo, utrum peccatum primi Angeli fuerit aliis aliqua causa peccandi. Nono, utrum tot ceciderint, quot remanserunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0005","text":"[I q. 63 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31042)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod malum culpae in Angelis esse non possit. Quia malum non potest esse nisi in his quae sunt in potentia, ut dicitur in IX Metaphys., subiectum enim privationis est ens in potentia. Sed Angeli, cum sint formae subsistentes, non habent esse in potentia. Ergo in eis non potest esse malum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0007","text":"[I q. 63 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31043)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0008","text":"Praeterea, Angeli sunt digniores quam corpora caelestia. Sed in corporibus caelestibus non potest esse malum, ut philosophi dicunt. Ergo neque in Angelis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0009","text":"[I q. 63 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31044)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0010","text":"Praeterea, id quod est naturale, semper inest. Sed naturale est Angelis quod moveantur motu dilectionis in Deum. Ergo hoc ab eis removeri non potest. Sed diligendo Deum non peccant. Ergo Angeli peccare non possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0011","text":"[I q. 63 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31045)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0012","text":"Praeterea, appetitus non est nisi boni, vel apparentis boni. Sed in Angelis non potest esse apparens bonum, quod non sit verum bonum, quia in eis vel omnino error esse non potest, vel saltem non potest praecedere culpam. Ergo Angeli non possunt appetere nisi id quod est vere bonum. Sed nullus, appetendo id quod est vere bonum, peccat. Ergo Angelus appetendo non peccat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0013","text":"[I q. 63 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31046)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0014","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Iob IV, *in Angelis suis reperit pravitatem*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0015","text":"[I q. 63 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31047)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod tam Angelus quam quaecumque creatura rationalis, si in sua sola natura consideretur, potest peccare, et cuicumque creaturae hoc convenit ut peccare non possit, hoc habet ex dono gratiae, non ex conditione naturae. Cuius ratio est, quia peccare nihil est aliud quam declinare a rectitudine actus quam debet habere; sive accipiatur peccatum in naturalibus, sive in artificialibus, sive in moralibus. Solum autem illum actum a rectitudine declinare non contingit, cuius regula est ipsa virtus agentis. Si enim manus artificis esset ipsa regula incisionis, nunquam posset artifex nisi recte lignum incidere, sed si rectitudo incisionis sit ab alia regula, contingit incisionem esse rectam et non rectam. Divina autem voluntas sola est regula sui actus, quia non ad superiorem finem ordinatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0017","text":"Omnis autem voluntas cuiuslibet creaturae rectitudinem in suo actu non habet, nisi secundum quod regulatur a voluntate divina, ad quam pertinet ultimus finis, sicut quaelibet voluntas inferioris debet regulari secundum voluntatem superioris, ut voluntas militis secundum voluntatem ducis exercitus. Sic igitur in sola voluntate divina peccatum esse non potest, in qualibet autem voluntate creaturae potest esse peccatum, secundum conditionem suae naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0018","text":"[I q. 63 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31048)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0019","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in Angelis non est potentia ad esse naturale. Est tamen in eis potentia secundum intellectivam partem, ad hoc quod convertantur in hoc vel in illud. Et quantum ad hoc, potest in eis esse malum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0020","text":"[I q. 63 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31049)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0021","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod corpora caelestia non habent operationem nisi naturalem. Et ideo sicut in natura eorum non potest esse corruptionis malum, ita nec in actione naturali eorum potest esse malum inordinationis. Sed supra actionem naturalem in Angelis est actio liberi arbitrii, secundum quam contingit in eis esse malum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0022","text":"[I q. 63 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31050)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0023","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod naturale est Angelo quod convertatur motu dilectionis in Deum, secundum quod est principium naturalis esse. Sed quod convertatur in ipsum secundum quod est obiectum beatitudinis supernaturalis, hoc est ex amore gratuito, a quo averti potuit peccando.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0024","text":"[I q. 63 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31051)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0025","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod peccatum in actu liberi arbitrii contingit esse dupliciter. Uno modo, ex hoc quod aliquod malum eligitur, sicut homo peccat eligendo adulterium, quod secundum se est malum. Et tale peccatum semper procedit ex aliqua ignorantia vel errore, alioquin id quod est malum, non eligeretur ut bonum. Errat quidem adulter in particulari, eligens hanc delectationem inordinati actus quasi aliquod bonum ad nunc agendum, propter inclinationem passionis aut habitus; etiam si in universali non erret, sed veram de hoc sententiam teneat. Hoc autem modo in Angelo peccatum esse non potuit, quia nec in Angelis sunt passiones, quibus ratio aut intellectus ligetur, ut ex supra dictis patet; nec iterum primum peccatum habitus praecedere potuit ad peccatum inclinans.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0026","text":"Alio modo contingit peccare per liberum arbitrium, eligendo aliquid quod secundum se est bonum, sed non cum ordine debitae mensurae aut regulae; ita quod defectus inducens peccatum sit solum ex parte electionis, quae non habet debitum ordinem, non ex parte rei electae; sicut si aliquis eligeret orare, non attendens ad ordinem ab Ecclesia institutum. Et huiusmodi peccatum non praeexigit ignorantiam, sed absentiam solum considerationis eorum quae considerari debent. Et hoc modo Angelus peccavit, convertendo se per liberum arbitrium ad proprium bonum, absque ordine ad regulam divinae voluntatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0027","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0028","text":"[I q. 63 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31052)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0029","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Angelis non possit esse solum peccatum superbiae et invidiae. In quemcumque enim cadit delectatio alicuius peccati, potest cadere peccatum illud. Sed Daemones delectantur etiam in obscenitatibus carnalium peccatorum, ut Augustinus dicit, II de Civ. Dei. Ergo in Daemonibus etiam peccata carnalia possunt esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0030","text":"[I q. 63 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31053)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0031","text":"Praeterea, sicut superbia et invidia sunt peccata spiritualia, ita acedia et avaritia et ira. Sed spiritui conveniunt peccata spiritualia, sicut et carni peccata carnalia. Ergo non solum superbia et invidia in Angelis esse possunt, sed etiam acedia et avaritia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0032","text":"[I q. 63 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31054)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0033","text":"Praeterea, secundum Gregorium, in moralibus, ex superbia nascuntur plura vitia, et similiter ex invidia. Posita autem causa, ponitur effectus. Si ergo superbia et invidia in Angelis esse possunt, pari ratione et alia vitia in eis esse possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0034","text":"[I q. 63 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31055)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0035","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XIV libro de Civ. Dei, quod *Diabolus non est fornicator aut ebriosus, neque aliquid huiusmodi, est tamen superbus et invidus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0036","text":"[I q. 63 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31056)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0037","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod peccatum aliquod in aliquo esse potest dupliciter, uno modo, secundum reatum; alio modo, secundum affectum. Secundum reatum quidem omnia peccata in Daemonibus esse contingit, quia dum homines ad omnia peccata inducunt, omnium peccatorum reatum incurrunt. Secundum affectum vero illa solum peccata in malis Angelis esse possunt, ad quae contingit affici spiritualem naturam. Spiritualem autem naturam affici non contingit ad bona quae sunt propria corpori, sed ad ea quae in rebus spiritualibus inveniri possunt, nihil enim afficitur nisi ad id quod suae naturae potest esse quodam modo conveniens. In spiritualibus autem bonis non potest esse peccatum dum aliquis ad ea afficitur, nisi per hoc quod in tali affectu superioris regula non servatur. Et hoc est peccatum superbiae, non subdi superiori in eo quo debet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0038","text":"Unde peccatum primum Angeli non potest esse aliud quam superbia. Sed consequenter potuit in eis esse etiam invidia. Eiusdem enim rationis est quod affectus tendat in aliquid appetendum, et quod renitatur opposito. Invidus autem ex hoc de bono alterius dolet, inquantum bonum alterius aestimat sui boni impedimentum. Non autem bonum alterius poterat aestimari impedimentum boni affectati per Angelum malum, nisi inquantum affectavit excellentiam singularem, quae quidem singularitas per alterius excellentiam cessat. Et ideo post peccatum superbiae consecutum est in Angelo peccante malum invidiae, secundum quod de bono hominis doluit; et etiam de excellentia divina, secundum quod eo Deus contra voluntatem ipsius Diaboli utitur in gloriam divinam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0039","text":"[I q. 63 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31057)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0040","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Daemones non delectantur in obscenitatibus carnalium peccatorum; quasi ipsi afficiantur ad delectationes carnales, sed hoc totum ex invidia procedit, quod in peccatis hominum quibuscumque delectantur, inquantum sunt impedimenta humani boni.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0041","text":"[I q. 63 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31058)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0042","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod avaritia, secundum quod est speciale peccatum, est immoderatus appetitus rerum temporalium quae veniunt in usum vitae humanae, quaecumque pecunia aestimari possunt, et ad ista non afficiuntur Daemones, sicut nec ad delectationes carnales. Unde avaritia proprie sumpta in eis esse non potest. Sed si avaritia dicatur omnis immoderata cupiditas habendi quodcumque bonum creatum, sic avaritia continetur in superbia quae est in Daemonibus. Ira vero cum quadam passione est, sicut et concupiscentia. Unde ipsa in Daemonibus esse non potest nisi metaphorice. Acedia vero est quaedam tristitia, qua homo redditur tardus ad spirituales actus propter corporalem laborem; qui Daemonibus non competit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0043","text":"Et sic patet quod sola superbia et invidia sunt pure spiritualia peccata, quae Daemonibus competere possunt, ita tamen quod invidia non sumatur pro passione, sed pro voluntate renitente bono alterius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0044","text":"[I q. 63 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31059)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0045","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod sub invidia et superbia, prout in Daemonibus ponuntur, comprehenduntur omnia peccata quae ab illis derivantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0046","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0047","text":"[I q. 63 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31060)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0048","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Diabolus non appetierit esse ut Deus. Illud enim quod non cadit in apprehensione, non cadit in appetitu, cum bonum apprehensum moveat appetitum vel sensibilem vel rationalem, vel intellectualem (in solo enim huiusmodi appetitu contingit esse peccatum). Sed creaturam aliquam esse aequalem Deo, non cadit in apprehensione, implicat enim contradictionem, quia necesse est finitum esse infinitum, si aequatur infinito. Ergo Angelus non potuit appetere esse ut Deus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0049","text":"[I q. 63 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31061)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0050","text":"Praeterea, illud quod est finis naturae, absque peccato appeti potest. Sed assimilari Deo est finis in quem tendit naturaliter quaelibet creatura. Si ergo Angelus appetiit esse ut Deus, non per aequalitatem, sed per similitudinem, videtur quod in hoc non peccaverit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0051","text":"[I q. 63 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31062)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0052","text":"Praeterea, Angelus in maiori plenitudine sapientiae conditus est quam homo. Sed nullus homo, nisi omnino amens, eligit esse aequalis Angelo, nedum Deo, quia electio non est nisi possibilium, de quibus est consilium. Ergo multo minus peccavit Angelus appetendo esse ut Deus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0053","text":"[I q. 63 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31063)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0054","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Isaiae XIV, ex persona Diaboli, *ascendam in caelum, et ero similis altissimo*. Et Augustinus dicit in libro de quaestionibus Vet. Test., quod elatione inflatus, voluit dici Deus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0055","text":"[I q. 63 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31064)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0056","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Angelus, absque omni dubio, peccavit appetendo esse ut Deus. Sed hoc potest intelligi dupliciter, uno modo, per aequiparantiam; alio modo, per similitudinem. Primo quidem modo, non potuit appetere esse ut Deus, quia scivit hoc esse impossibile, naturali cognitione; nec primum actum peccandi in ipso praecessit vel habitus vel passio ligans cognoscitivam ipsius virtutem, ut in particulari deficiens eligeret impossibile, sicut in nobis interdum accidit. Et tamen, dato quod esset possibile, hoc esset contra naturale desiderium. Inest enim unicuique naturale desiderium ad conservandum suum esse, quod non conservaretur, si transmutaretur in alteram naturam. Unde nulla res quae est in inferiori gradu naturae, potest appetere superioris naturae gradum, sicut asinus non appetit esse equus, quia si transferretur in gradum superioris naturae, iam ipsum non esset.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0057","text":"Sed in hoc imaginatio decipitur, quia enim homo appetit esse in altiori gradu quantum ad aliqua accidentalia, quae possunt crescere absque corruptione subiecti, aestimatur quod possit appetere altiorem gradum naturae, in quem pervenire non posset nisi esse desineret. Manifestum est autem quod Deus excedit Angelum, non secundum aliqua accidentalia, sed secundum gradum naturae, et etiam unus Angelus alium. Unde impossibile est quod Angelus inferior appetat esse aequalis superiori; nedum quod appetat esse aequalis Deo. Appetere autem esse ut Deus per similitudinem, contingit dupliciter. Uno modo, quantum ad id in quo aliquid natum est Deo assimilari. Et sic, si aliquis quantum ad hoc appetat esse Deo similis, non peccat, dummodo similitudinem Dei debito ordine appetat adipisci, ut scilicet eam a Deo habeat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0058","text":"Peccaret vero si quis etiam appeteret secundum iustitiam esse similis Deo, quasi propria virtute, et non ex virtute Dei. Alio vero modo potest aliquis appetere similis esse Deo, quantum ad hoc in quo non natus est assimilari; sicut si quis appeteret creare caelum et terram, quod est proprium Dei; in quo appetitu esset peccatum. Et hoc modo Diabolus appetiit esse ut Deus. Non ut ei assimilaretur quantum ad hoc quod est nulli subesse simpliciter, quia sic etiam suum non esse appeteret, cum nulla creatura esse possit nisi per hoc quod sub Deo esse participat. Sed in hoc appetiit esse similis Deo, quia appetiit ut finem ultimum beatitudinis id ad quod virtute suae naturae poterat pervenire, avertens suum appetitum a beatitudine supernaturali, quae est ex gratia Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0059","text":"Vel si appetiit ut ultimum finem illam Dei similitudinem quae datur ex gratia, voluit hoc habere per virtutem suae naturae, non ex divino auxilio secundum Dei dispositionem. Et hoc consonat dictis Anselmi, qui dicit quod appetiit illud ad quod pervenisset si stetisset. Et haec duo quodammodo in idem redeunt, quia secundum utrumque appetiit finalem beatitudinem per suam virtutem habere, quod est proprium Dei. Quia vero quod est per se, est principium et causa eius quod est per aliud, ex hoc etiam consecutum est quod appetiit aliquem principatum super alia habere. In quo etiam perverse voluit Deo assimilari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0060","text":"[I q. 63 a. 3 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31065)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0061","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ad omnia obiecta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0062","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0063","text":"[I q. 63 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31066)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0064","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aliqui Daemones sint naturaliter mali. Dicit enim Porphyrius, ut Augustinus introducit X de Civ. Dei, quod *est quoddam genus Daemonum natura fallax, simulans deos et animas defunctorum*. Sed esse fallacem est esse malum. Ergo aliqui Daemones sunt naturaliter mali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0065","text":"[I q. 63 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31067)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0066","text":"Praeterea, sicut Angeli sunt creati a Deo, ita et homines. Sed aliqui homines sunt naturaliter mali, de quibus dicitur Sap. XII, *erat eorum malitia naturalis*. Ergo et Angeli aliqui possunt esse naturaliter mali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0067","text":"[I q. 63 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31068)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0068","text":"Praeterea, aliqua animalia irrationalia habent quasdam naturales malitias, sicut vulpes naturaliter est subdola, et lupus naturaliter est rapax, et tamen sunt creaturae Dei. Ergo et Daemones, licet sint creaturae Dei, possunt esse naturaliter mali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0069","text":"[I q. 63 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31069)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0070","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *Daemones non sunt natura mali*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0071","text":"[I q. 63 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31070)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0072","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod omne quod est, inquantum est et naturam habet aliquam, in bonum aliquod naturaliter tendit, utpote ex principio bono existens, quia semper effectus convertitur in suum principium. Contingit autem alicui bono particulari aliquod malum esse adiunctum, sicut igni coniungitur hoc malum quod est esse consumptivum aliorum, sed bono universali nullum malum potest esse adiunctum. Si ergo aliquid sit cuius natura ordinetur in aliquod bonum particulare, potest naturaliter tendere in aliquod malum, non inquantum malum, sed per accidens, inquantum est coniunctum cuidam bono. Si vero aliquid sit cuius natura ordinetur in aliquod bonum secundum communem boni rationem hoc secundum suam naturam non potest tendere in aliquod malum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0073","text":"Manifestum est autem quod quaelibet natura intellectualis habet ordinem in bonum universale, quod potest apprehendere, et quod est obiectum voluntatis. Unde cum Daemones sint substantiae intellectuales, nullo modo possunt habere inclinationem naturalem in aliquod quodcumque malum. Et ideo non possunt esse naturaliter mali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0074","text":"[I q. 63 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31071)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0075","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus ibidem reprehendit Porphyrium de hoc quod dixit quod Daemones erant naturaliter fallaces, dicens eos non esse naturaliter fallaces, sed propria voluntate. Porphyrius autem hac ratione posuit Daemones esse natura fallaces, quia ponebat Daemones esse animalia habentia naturam sensitivam. Natura autem sensitiva ordinatur ad aliquod bonum particulare, cui potest esse coniunctum malum. Et secundum hoc, aliquam inclinationem naturalem habere possunt ad malum; per accidens tamen, inquantum malum est coniunctum bono.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0076","text":"[I q. 63 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31072)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0077","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod malitia aliquorum hominum potest dici naturalis, vel propter consuetudinem, quae est altera natura; vel propter naturalem inclinationem ex parte naturae sensitivae, ad aliquam inordinatam passionem, sicut quidam dicuntur naturaliter iracundi vel concupiscentes; non autem ex parte naturae intellectualis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0078","text":"[I q. 63 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31073)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0079","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod animalia bruta secundum naturam sensitivam habent naturalem inclinationem ad quaedam particularia bona, quibus coniuncta sunt aliqua mala; sicut vulpes ad quaerendum victum sagaciter, cui adiungitur dolositas. Unde esse dolosum non est malum vulpi, cum sit ei naturale; sicut nec esse furiosum est malum cani, sicut Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0080","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0081","text":"[I q. 63 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31074)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0082","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Diabolus in primo instanti suae creationis fuerit malus per culpam propriae voluntatis. Dicitur enim Ioan. VIII, de Diabolo, *ille homicida erat ab initio*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0083","text":"[I q. 63 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31075)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0084","text":"Praeterea, secundum Augustinum, I super Gen. ad Litt., informitas creaturae non praecessit formationem tempore, sed origine tantum. Per caelum autem quod legitur primo creatum, ut ipse dicit in II libro, intelligitur natura angelica informis; per hoc autem quod dicitur quod Deus dixit, fiat lux, et facta est lux, intelligitur formatio eius per conversionem ad verbum; simul ergo natura Angeli creata est, et facta est lux. Sed simul dum facta est lux, distincta est a tenebris, per quas intelliguntur Angeli peccantes. Ergo in primo instanti suae creationis quidam Angeli fuerunt beati, et quidam peccaverunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0085","text":"[I q. 63 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31076)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0086","text":"Praeterea, peccatum opponitur merito. Sed in primo instanti suae creationis aliqua natura intellectualis potest mereri; sicut anima Christi, vel etiam ipsi boni Angeli. Ergo et Daemones in primo instanti suae creationis potuerunt peccare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0087","text":"[I q. 63 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31077)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0088","text":"Praeterea, natura angelica virtuosior est quam natura corporea. Sed res corporalis statim in primo instanti suae creationis incipit habere suam operationem; sicut ignis in primo instanti quo generatus est, incipit moveri sursum. Ergo et Angelus in primo instanti suae creationis potuit operari. Aut ergo habuit operationem rectam, aut non rectam. Si rectam, cum gratiam habuerint, per eam meruerunt beatitudinem. In Angelis autem statim ad meritum sequitur praemium, ut supra dictum est. Ergo fuissent statim beati, et ita nunquam peccassent, quod est falsum. Relinquitur ergo quod in primo instanti, non recte operando, peccaverunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0089","text":"[I q. 63 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31078)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0090","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *vidit Deus cuncta quae fecerat, et erant valde bona*. Inter ea autem erant etiam Daemones. Ergo et Daemones aliquando fuerunt boni.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0091","text":"[I q. 63 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31079)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0092","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam posuerunt quod statim Daemones in primo instanti suae creationis mali fuerunt, non quidem per naturam, sed per peccatum propriae voluntatis, quia *ex quo est factus Diabolus, iustitiam recusavit. Cui sententiae*, ut Augustinus dicit, XI de Civ. Dei, *quisquis acquiescit, non cum illis haereticis sapit, idest Manichaeis, qui dicunt quod Diabolus habet naturam mali*. Sed quia haec opinio auctoritati Scripturae contradicit (dicitur enim, sub figura principis Babylonis, de Diabolo, Isaiae XIV, *quomodo cecidisti, Lucifer, qui mane oriebaris?* Et Ezech. XXVIII, *in deliciis Paradisi Dei fuisti* dicitur ad Diabolum sub persona regis Tyri), ideo a magistris haec opinio tanquam erronea rationabiliter reprobata est. Unde aliqui dixerunt quod Angeli in primo instanti suae creationis peccare potuerunt, sed non peccaverunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0093","text":"Sed haec opinio etiam a quibusdam improbatur ea ratione quia, cum duae operationes se consequuntur, impossibile videtur quod in eodem nunc utraque operatio terminetur. Manifestum est autem quod peccatum Angeli fuit operatio creatione posterior. Terminus autem creationis est ipsum esse Angeli; terminus vero operationis peccati est quod sunt mali. Impossibile ergo videtur quod in primo instanti quo Angelus esse coepit, fuerit malus. Sed haec ratio non videtur sufficiens. Habet enim solum locum in motibus temporalibus, qui successive aguntur; sicut si motus localis sequitur ad alterationem, non potest in eodem instanti terminari alteratio et localis motus. Sed si sunt mutationes instantaneae, simul et in eodem instanti potest esse terminus primae et secundae mutationis; sicut in eodem instanti in quo illuminatur luna a sole, illuminatur aer a luna.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0094","text":"Manifestum est autem quod creatio est instantanea; et similiter motus liberi arbitrii in Angelis; non enim indigent collatione et discursu rationis, ut ex supra dictis patet. Unde nihil prohibet simul et in eodem instanti esse terminum creationis, et terminum liberi arbitrii. Et ideo aliter dicendum est, quod impossibile fuit Angelum in primo instanti peccasse per inordinatum actum liberi arbitrii. Quamvis enim res aliqua in primo instanti quo esse incipit, simul incipere possit operari; tamen illa operatio quae simul incipit cum esse rei, est ei ab agente a quo habet esse; sicut moveri sursum inest igni a generante. Unde si aliqua res habeat esse ab agente deficiente, quod possit esse causa defectivae actionis, poterit in primo instanti in quo incipit esse, habere defectivam operationem; sicut si tibia quae nascitur clauda ex debilitate seminis, statim incipiat claudicare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0095","text":"Agens autem quod Angelos in esse produxit, scilicet Deus, non potest esse causa peccati. Unde non potest dici quod Diabolus in primo instanti suae creationis fuerit malus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0096","text":"[I q. 63 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31080)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0097","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit, XI de Civ. Dei, *cum dicitur quod Diabolus ab initio peccat non ab initio ex quo creatus est, peccare putandus est, sed ab initio peccati*; scilicet quia nunquam a peccato suo recessit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0098","text":"[I q. 63 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31081)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0099","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod illa distinctio lucis et tenebrarum, secundum quod per tenebras peccata Daemonum intelliguntur, accipienda est secundum Dei praescientiam. Unde Augustinus dicit, XI de Civ. Dei, quod *solus lucem ac tenebras discernere potuit, qui potuit etiam, priusquam caderent, praescire casuros*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0100","text":"[I q. 63 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31082)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0101","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod quidquid est in merito, est a Deo, et ideo in primo instanti suae creationis Angelus mereri potuit. Sed non est similis ratio de peccato, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0102","text":"[I q. 63 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31083)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0103","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod Deus non discrevit inter Angelos ante aversionem quorundam et conversionem aliorum, ut Augustinus dicit, XI de Civ. Dei, et ideo omnes, in gratia creati in primo instanti meruerunt. Sed quidam eorum statim impedimentum praestiterunt suae beatitudinis, praecedens meritum mortificantes. Et ideo beatitudine quam meruerunt, sunt privati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0104","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0105","text":"[I q. 63 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31084)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0106","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aliqua mora fuerit inter creationem et lapsum Angeli. Dicitur enim Ezech. XXVIII, *ambulasti perfectus in viis tuis a die conceptionis tuae, donec inventa est iniquitas in te*. Sed ambulatio, cum sit motus continuus, requirit aliquam moram. Ergo aliqua mora fuit inter creationem Diaboli et eius lapsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0107","text":"[I q. 63 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31085)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0108","text":"Praeterea, Origenes dicit quod *serpens antiquus non statim supra pectus et ventrem suum ambulavit*; per quod intelligitur eius peccatum. Ergo Diabolus non statim post primum instans suae creationis peccavit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0109","text":"[I q. 63 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31086)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0110","text":"Praeterea, posse peccare commune est homini et Angelo. Fuit autem aliqua mora inter formationem hominis et eius peccatum. Ergo, pari ratione, fuit aliqua mora inter formationem Diaboli et eius peccatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0111","text":"[I q. 63 a. 6 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31087)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0112","text":"Praeterea, aliud instans fuit in quo Diabolus peccavit, ab instanti in quo creatus fuit. Sed inter quaelibet duo instantia cadit tempus medium. Ergo aliqua mora fuit inter creationem eius et lapsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0113","text":"[I q. 63 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31088)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0114","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Ioan. VIII, de Diabolo, quod *in veritate non stetit*. Et sicut Augustinus dicit, XI de Civ. Dei, *oportet ut hoc sic accipiamus, quod in veritate fuerit, sed non permanserit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0115","text":"[I q. 63 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31089)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0116","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa hoc est duplex opinio. Sed probabilior, et sanctorum dictis magis consona est, quod statim post primum instans suae creationis Diabolus peccaverit. Et hoc necesse est dicere, si ponatur quod in primo instanti suae creationis in actum liberi arbitrii proruperit, et cum gratia fuerit creatus, ut supra diximus. Cum enim Angeli per unum actum meritorium ad beatitudinem perveniant, ut supra dictum est; si Diabolus in primo instanti, in gratia creatus, meruit, statim post primum instans beatitudinem accepisset, nisi statim impedimentum praestitisset peccando. Si vero ponatur quod Angelus in gratia creatus non fuerit; vel quod in primo instanti actum liberi arbitrii non potuerit habere; nihil prohibet aliquam moram fuisse inter creationem et lapsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0117","text":"[I q. 63 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31090)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0118","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod per motus corporales, qui per tempus mensurantur, quandoque in sacra Scriptura intelliguntur metaphorice motus spirituales instantanei. Et sic per ambulationem intelligitur motus liberi arbitrii tendentis in bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0119","text":"[I q. 63 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31091)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0120","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Origenes dicit quod *serpens antiquus non a principio, nec statim supra pectus* ambulavit, propter primum instans, in quo malus non fuit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0121","text":"[I q. 63 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31092)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0122","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Angelus habet liberum arbitrium inflexibile post electionem, et ideo nisi statim post primum instans, in quo naturalem motum habuit ad bonum, impedimentum beatitudini praestitisset, fuisset firmatus in bono. Sed non est simile de homine. Et ideo ratio non sequitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0123","text":"[I q. 63 a. 6 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31093)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0124","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod inter quaelibet duo instantia esse tempus medium, habet veritatem inquantum tempus est continuum, ut probatur in VI Physic. Sed in Angelis, qui non sunt subiecti caelesti motui, qui primo per tempus continuum mensuratur, tempus accipitur pro ipsa successione operationum intellectus, vel etiam affectus. Sic igitur instans primum in Angelis intelligitur respondere operationi mentis angelicae, qua se in seipsam convertit per vespertinam cognitionem, quia in primo die commemoratur vespere, sed non mane. Et haec quidem operatio in omnibus bona fuit. Sed ab hac operatione quidam per matutinam cognitionem ad laudem verbi sunt conversi, quidam vero, in seipsis remanentes, facti sunt nox, per superbiam intumescentes, ut Augustinus dicit, IV super Gen. ad Litt. Et sic prima operatio fuit omnibus communis; sed in secunda sunt discreti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0125","text":"Et ideo in primo instanti omnes fuerunt boni; sed in secundo fuerunt boni a malis distincti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0126","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0127","text":"[I q. 63 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31094)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0128","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ille Angelus qui fuit supremus inter peccantes, non fuerit supremus inter omnes. Dicitur enim de eo Ezech. XXVIII, *tu Cherub extentus et protegens, posui te in monte sancto Dei*. Sed ordo Cherubim est sub ordine Seraphim, ut Dionysius dicit, VII cap. Ang. Hier. Ergo Angelus qui fuit supremus inter peccantes, non fuit supremus inter omnes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0129","text":"[I q. 63 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31095)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0130","text":"Praeterea, Deus fecit naturam intellectualem propter beatitudinem consequendam. Si igitur Angelus qui fuit supremus inter omnes, peccavit, sequitur quod ordinatio divina fuerit frustrata in nobilissima creatura. Quod est inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0131","text":"[I q. 63 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31096)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0132","text":"Praeterea, quanto aliquid magis inclinatur in aliquid, tanto minus potest ab illo deficere. Sed Angelus quanto est superior, tanto magis inclinatur in Deum. Ergo minus potest a Deo peccando deficere. Et sic videtur quod Angelus qui peccavit, non fuerit supremus inter omnes, sed de inferioribus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0133","text":"[I q. 63 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31097)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0134","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Gregorius, in homilia de centum ovibus, *quod primus Angelus qui peccavit, dum cunctis agminibus Angelorum praelatus, eorum claritatem transcenderet, ex eorum comparatione clarior fuit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0135","text":"[I q. 63 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31098)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0136","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in peccato est duo considerare, scilicet pronitatem ad peccandum; et motivum ad peccandum. Si ergo consideremus in Angelis pronitatem ad peccandum, minus videtur quod peccaverint superiores Angeli, quam inferiores. Et propter hoc Damascenus dicit quod maior eorum qui peccaverunt, fuit terrestri ordini praelatus. Et videtur haec opinio consonare positioni Platonicorum, quam Augustinus recitat in Lib. de Civ. Dei VIII et X. Dicebant enim quod omnes dii erant boni, sed Daemonum quidam boni, quidam mali; deos nominantes substantias intellectuales quae sunt a globo lunari superius, Daemones vero substantias intellectuales quae sunt a globo lunari inferius, superiores hominibus ordine naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0137","text":"Nec est abiicienda haec opinio tanquam a fide aliena, quia tota creatura corporalis administratur a Deo per Angelos, ut Augustinus dicit, III de Trin.; unde nihil prohibet dicere inferiores Angelos divinitus distributos esse ad administrandum inferiora corpora, superiores vero ad administrandum corpora superiora, supremos vero ad assistendum Deo. Et secundum hoc Damascenus dicit quod illi qui ceciderunt, fuerunt de inferioribus, in quorum etiam ordine aliqui boni Angeli permanserunt. Si vero consideretur motivum ad peccandum, maius invenitur in superioribus quam in inferioribus. Fuit enim Daemonum peccatum superbia, ut supra dictum est; cuius motivum est excellentia, quae fuit maior in superioribus. Et ideo Gregorius dicit quod ille qui peccavit, fuit superior inter omnes. Et hoc videtur probabilius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0138","text":"Quia peccatum Angeli non processit ex aliqua pronitate, sed ex solo libero arbitrio, unde magis videtur consideranda esse ratio quae sumitur a motivo ad peccandum. Non est tamen inde alii opinioni praeiudicandum, quia etiam in principe inferiorum Angelorum potuit esse aliquod motivum ad peccandum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0139","text":"[I q. 63 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31099)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0140","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Cherubim interpretatur plenitudo scientiae; Seraphim autem interpretatur ardentes sive incedentes. Et sic patet quod Cherubim denominatur a scientia, quae potest esse cum mortali peccato; Seraphim vero denominatur ab ardore caritatis, quae cum peccato mortali esse non potest. Et ideo primus Angelus peccans non est denominatus Seraphim, sed Cherubim.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0141","text":"[I q. 63 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31100)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0142","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod divina intentio non frustratur nec in his qui peccant, nec in his qui salvantur, utrorumque enim eventum Deus praecognoscit, et ex utroque habet gloriam, dum hos ex sua bonitate salvat, illos ex sua iustitia punit. Ipsa vero creatura intellectualis, dum peccat, a fine debito deficit. Nec hoc est inconveniens in quacumque creatura sublimi, sic enim creatura intellectualis instituta est a Deo, ut in eius arbitrio positum sit agere propter finem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0143","text":"[I q. 63 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31101)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0144","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod quantacumque inclinatio ad bonum fuerit in supremo Angelo, tamen ei necessitatem non inducebat. Unde potuit per liberum arbitrium eam non sequi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0145","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0146","text":"[I q. 63 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31102)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0147","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod peccatum primi Angeli peccantis non fuit aliis causa peccandi. Causa enim prior est causato. Sed omnes simul peccaverunt, ut Damascenus dicit. Ergo peccatum unius non fuit aliis causa peccandi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0148","text":"[I q. 63 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31103)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0149","text":"Praeterea, primum peccatum Angeli non potest esse nisi superbia, ut supra dictum est. Sed superbia excellentiam quaerit. Magis autem excellentiae repugnat quod aliquis inferiori subdatur, quam superiori, et sic non videtur quod Daemones peccaverint per hoc quod voluerunt subesse alicui superiorum Angelorum, potius quam Deo. Sic autem peccatum unius Angeli fuisset aliis causa peccandi, si eos ad hoc induxisset ut sibi subiicerentur. Non ergo videtur quod peccatum primi Angeli fuerit causa peccandi aliis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0150","text":"[I q. 63 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31104)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0151","text":"Praeterea, maius peccatum est velle subesse alteri contra Deum, quam contra Deum alteri velle praeesse, quia minus habet de motivo ad peccandum. Si ergo peccatum primi Angeli fuit aliis causa peccandi in hoc, quod eos ut sibi subiicerentur induxit, gravius peccassent inferiores Angeli quam supremus, quod est contra hoc quod super illud Psalmi CIII, *draco iste quem formasti*, dicit Glossa, *qui ceteris in esse erat excellentior, factus est in malitia maior*. Non ergo peccatum primi Angeli fuit aliis causa peccandi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0152","text":"[I q. 63 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31105)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0153","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Apoc. XII, quod draco traxit secum tertiam partem stellarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0154","text":"[I q. 63 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31106)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0155","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod peccatum primi Angeli fuit aliis causa peccandi, non quidem cogens, sed quadam quasi exhortatione inducens. Cuius signum ex hoc apparet, quod omnes Daemones illi supremo subduntur; ut manifeste apparet per illud quod dicit dominus, Matth. XXV, *ite, maledicti, in ignem aeternum, qui paratus est Diabolo et Angelis eius*. Habet enim hoc ordo divinae iustitiae, ut cuius suggestioni aliquis consentit in culpa, eius potestati subdatur in poena; secundum illud II Petr. II, a quo quis superatus est, huic servus addictus est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0156","text":"[I q. 63 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31107)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0157","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet simul Daemones peccaverint, tamen peccatum unius potuit esse aliis causa peccandi. Angelus enim non indiget ad eligendum vel exhortandum vel etiam consentiendum, temporis mora; sicut homo, qui deliberatione indiget ad eligendum et ad consentiendum, et locutione vocali ad exhortandum, quorum utrumque tempore agitur. Manifestum est autem quod etiam homo simul dum aliquid iam corde concepit, in eodem instanti incipit loqui. Et in ultimo instanti locutionis, in quo aliquis sensum loquentis capit, potest assentire ei quod dicitur, ut patet maxime in primis conceptionibus, quas quisque probat auditas. Sublato ergo tempore locutionis et deliberationis quod in nobis requiritur, in eodem instanti in quo primus Angelus suam affectionem intelligibili locutione expressit, possibile fuit aliis in eam consentire.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0158","text":"[I q. 63 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31108)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0159","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod superbus, ceteris paribus, magis vult subesse superiori quam inferiori. Sed si aliquam excellentiam consequatur sub inferiori, quam sub superiori consequi non possit, magis eligit inferiori subesse quam superiori. Sic igitur non fuit contra superbiam Daemonum quod subesse inferiori voluerunt, in eius principatum consentientes; ad hoc eum principem et ducem habere volentes, ut virtute naturali suam ultimam beatitudinem consequerentur; praesertim quia supremo Angelo naturae ordine etiam tunc subiecti erant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0160","text":"[I q. 63 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31109)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0161","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, Angelus non habet aliquid retardans, sed secundum suam totam virtutem movetur in illud ad quod movetur, sive in bonum sive in malum. Quia igitur supremus Angelus maiorem habuit naturalem virtutem quam inferiores, intensiori motu in peccatum prolapsus est. Et ideo factus est etiam in malitia maior.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0162","text":"## Articulus 9","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0163","text":"[I q. 63 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31110)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0164","text":"Ad nonum sic proceditur. Videtur quod plures peccaverunt de Angelis, quam permanserunt. Quia, ut dicit philosophus, *malum est ut in pluribus, bonum ut in paucioribus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0165","text":"[I q. 63 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31111)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0166","text":"Praeterea, iustitia et peccatum eadem ratione inveniuntur in Angelis et hominibus. Sed in hominibus plures inveniuntur mali quam boni; secundum illud Eccle. I, *stultorum infinitus est numerus*. Ergo pari ratione in Angelis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0167","text":"[I q. 63 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31112)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0168","text":"Praeterea, Angeli distinguuntur secundum personas, et secundum ordines. Si igitur plures personae angelicae remanserunt, videtur etiam quod non de omnibus ordinibus aliqui peccaverunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0169","text":"[I q. 63 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31113)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0170","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur IV Reg. VI, *plures nobiscum sunt quam cum illis*; quod exponitur de bonis Angelis qui sunt nobiscum in auxilium, et de malis qui nobis adversantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0171","text":"[I q. 63 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31114)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0172","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod plures Angeli permanserunt quam peccaverunt. Quia peccatum est contra naturalem inclinationem, ea vero quae contra naturam fiunt, ut in paucioribus accidunt; natura enim consequitur suum effectum vel semper, vel ut in pluribus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0173","text":"[I q. 63 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31115)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0174","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod philosophus loquitur quantum ad homines, in quibus malum contingit ex hoc quod sequuntur bona sensibilia, quae sunt pluribus nota, deserto bono rationis, quod paucioribus notum est. In Angelis autem non est nisi natura intellectualis. Unde non est similis ratio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0175","text":"[I q. 63 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31116)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0176","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ad secundum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0177","text":"[I q. 63 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31117)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0178","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod secundum illos qui dicunt quod Diabolus maior fuit de inferiori ordine Angelorum, qui praesunt terrestribus, manifestum est quod non de quolibet ordine ceciderunt, sed de infimo tantum. Secundum vero illos qui ponunt maiorem Diabolum de supremo fuisse ordine, probabile est quod de quolibet ordine aliqui ceciderunt; sicut et in quemlibet ordinem homines assumuntur in supplementum ruinae angelicae. In quo etiam magis comprobatur libertas liberi arbitrii, quae secundum quemlibet gradum creaturae in malum flecti potest. In sacra Scriptura tamen nomina quorundam ordinum, ut Seraphim et thronorum, Daemonibus non attribuuntur; quia haec nomina sumuntur ab ardore caritatis et ab inhabitatione Dei, quae non possunt esse cum peccato mortali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0179","text":"Attribuuntur autem eis nomina Cherubim, potestatum et principatuum, quia haec nomina sumuntur a scientia et potentia, quae bonis malisque possunt esse communia.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9e6321e7c96a9f6ed42020eacc6442f06ab77ad63de988655672d05b2864d17b","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T145004175251Z_cabe021b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 63","source_sha256":"9e6321e7c96a9f6ed42020eacc6442f06ab77ad63de988655672d05b2864d17b","sha256":"83804dbb7cb05d936b54eeed79a503088d1029ef180c2e8eeec4f6bd44c0024c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:50:04.175265+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0002","text":"[I q. 63 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31041)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0003","text":"Next, one must consider how angels became evil. And first, as regards the evil of fault; second, as regards the evil of punishment. Concerning the first, nine questions are asked. First, whether the evil of fault can exist in an angel. Second, what kinds of sins can exist in them. Third, by desiring what the angel sinned. Fourth, granted that some became evil through a sin of their own will, whether some are naturally evil. Fifth, granted that they are not, whether any of them could have been evil through an act of his own will in the first instant of his creation. Sixth, granted that they could not, whether there was any interval between creation and the fall. Seventh, whether the highest among those falling was simply the highest among all angels. Eighth, whether the sin of the first angel was in some way a cause of sinning for the others. Ninth, whether as many fell as remained.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0005","text":"[I q. 63 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31042)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the evil of fault cannot exist in angels. For evil can exist only in those things that are in potency, as is said in *Metaphysics* IX; for the subject of privation is a being in potency. But angels, since they are subsistent forms, do not have being in potency. Therefore evil cannot exist in them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0007","text":"[I q. 63 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31043)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0008","text":"Moreover, angels are more noble than the heavenly bodies. But evil cannot exist in the heavenly bodies, as the philosophers say. Therefore neither can it exist in angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0009","text":"[I q. 63 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31044)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0010","text":"Moreover, that which is natural is always present. But it is natural to angels to be moved toward God by a motion of love. Therefore this cannot be removed from them. But in loving God they do not sin. Therefore angels cannot sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0011","text":"[I q. 63 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31045)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0012","text":"Moreover, appetite is only for the good, or for an apparent good. But in angels there cannot be an apparent good that is not a true good, because in them either error cannot exist at all, or at least it cannot precede fault. Therefore angels can desire only that which is truly good. But no one, in desiring that which is truly good, sins. Therefore an angel does not sin by desiring.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0013","text":"[I q. 63 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31046)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0014","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Job IV: *in his angels he found perversity*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0015","text":"[I q. 63 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31047)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0016","text":"I answer that both an angel and any rational creature whatever, if considered in its nature alone, can sin; and whatever creature has the property that it cannot sin has this from the gift of grace, not from the condition of its nature. The reason for this is that to sin is nothing other than to depart from the rectitude that an act ought to have, whether sin is taken in natural things, artificial things, or moral things. But only that act cannot depart from rectitude whose rule is the very power of the agent. For if the hand of the craftsman were itself the rule of cutting, the craftsman could never cut wood except rightly; but if the rectitude of cutting is from another rule, it happens that the cutting is right or not right. But the divine will alone is the rule of its own act, because it is not ordered to a higher end.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0017","text":"Now the will of every creature does not have rectitude in its act except insofar as it is regulated by the divine will, to which the ultimate end belongs, just as every will of an inferior ought to be regulated according to the will of a superior—for example, the will of a soldier according to the will of the commander of an army. Thus, therefore, sin cannot exist in the divine will alone; but in the will of every creature sin can exist, according to the condition of its nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0018","text":"[I q. 63 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31048)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0019","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that in angels there is no potency for natural being. Yet there is in them potency according to the intellectual part, so that they may turn to this or to that. And in this respect evil can exist in them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0020","text":"[I q. 63 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31049)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0021","text":"To the second it must be said that heavenly bodies have no operation except a natural one. And therefore, just as the evil of corruption cannot exist in their nature, so neither can the evil of disorder exist in their natural action. But above natural action, in angels there is an action of free choice, according to which evil can exist in them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0022","text":"[I q. 63 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31050)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0023","text":"To the third it must be said that it is natural to an angel to turn toward God by a motion of love, insofar as He is the principle of natural being. But to turn toward Him insofar as He is the object of supernatural beatitude is from gratuitous love, from which the angel could turn away by sinning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0024","text":"[I q. 63 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31051)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0025","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that sin in an act of free choice can occur in two ways. In one way, from the fact that some evil is chosen, as a man sins by choosing adultery, which is evil in itself. And such sin always proceeds from some ignorance or error; otherwise, that which is evil would not be chosen as good. Indeed, the adulterer errs in the particular, choosing this pleasure of a disordered act as some good to be done now, on account of the inclination of passion or habit; even if he does not err in the universal, but holds a true judgment concerning this. But sin could not be in an angel in this way, because there are neither passions in angels by which reason or intellect may be bound, as is clear from what was said above; nor, again, could a first sin be preceded by a habit inclining toward sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0026","text":"In another way, one can sin through free choice by choosing something which is good in itself, but not with the order of the due measure or rule; so that the defect bringing about the sin is solely on the part of the choice, which lacks due order, and not on the part of the thing chosen; as if someone chose to pray without attending to the order established by the Church. And sin of this sort does not presuppose ignorance, but only the absence of consideration of those things which ought to be considered. And in this way the angel sinned, by turning himself through free choice to his own good, without order to the rule of the divine will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0027","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0028","text":"[I q. 63 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31052)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0029","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that in angels there cannot be only the sin of pride and envy. For in whomever the delight of some sin can occur, that sin can occur. But demons delight even in the obscenities of carnal sinners, as Augustine says in Book II of *The City of God*. Therefore carnal sins also can be in demons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0030","text":"[I q. 63 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31053)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0031","text":"Moreover, just as pride and envy are spiritual sins, so are sloth, avarice, and anger. But spiritual sins belong to a spirit, just as carnal sins belong to flesh. Therefore not only pride and envy can be in angels, but also sloth and avarice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0032","text":"[I q. 63 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31054)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0033","text":"Moreover, according to Gregory, in moral matters several vices are born from pride, and likewise from envy. But when the cause is posited, the effect is posited. Therefore, if pride and envy can be in angels, by equal reasoning the other vices can also be in them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0034","text":"[I q. 63 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31055)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0035","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says in Book XIV of *The City of God* that *the Devil is not a fornicator or a drunkard, nor anything of this sort; yet he is proud and envious*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0036","text":"[I q. 63 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31056)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0037","text":"I answer that it must be said that a sin can be in someone in two ways: in one way, according to guilt; in another way, according to affection. According to guilt, indeed, all sins can be in demons, because, while they induce men to all sins, they incur the guilt of all sins. But according to affection, only those sins can be in evil angels toward which a spiritual nature can be affected. Now a spiritual nature cannot be affected toward goods proper to the body, but toward those things which can be found in spiritual things; for nothing is affected except toward that which can in some way be fitting to its nature. But in spiritual goods there can be no sin while someone is affected toward them, except insofar as, in such affection, the rule of a superior is not observed. And this is the sin of pride: not to be subject to a superior in that in which one ought to be subject.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0038","text":"Hence the first sin of an angel cannot be other than pride. But subsequently envy could also be in them. For it belongs to the same account that affection tend toward something to be desired and that it resist the opposite. Now the envious person grieves over another's good because he judges another's good to be an impediment to his own good. But another's good could not be judged an impediment to the good desired by an evil angel, except insofar as he desired singular excellence, which singularity indeed ceases through another's excellence. And therefore, after the sin of pride, the evil of envy followed in the sinning angel, insofar as he grieved over man's good; and also over divine excellence, insofar as God uses him, against the will of the Devil himself, for divine glory.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0039","text":"[I q. 63 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31057)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0040","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that demons do not delight in the obscenities of carnal sinners as though they themselves were affected toward carnal pleasures; rather, this all proceeds from envy: they delight in whatever sins of men insofar as these are impediments to human good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0041","text":"[I q. 63 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31058)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0042","text":"To the second, it must be said that avarice, insofar as it is a special sin, is the immoderate appetite for temporal things that come into the use of human life, whatever things can be reckoned as money; and demons are not affected toward these, just as neither toward carnal pleasures. Hence avarice, properly taken, cannot be in them. But if avarice is called every immoderate desire for possessing any created good whatsoever, then avarice is contained under the pride which is in demons. Anger, however, is with a certain passion, as is also concupiscence. Hence it cannot itself be in demons except metaphorically. Sloth, moreover, is a certain sadness by which a man is made slow for spiritual acts on account of bodily labor; this does not belong to demons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0043","text":"And thus it is clear that pride and envy alone are purely spiritual sins, which can belong to demons; yet in such a way that envy is not taken for a passion, but for a will resisting another's good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0044","text":"[I q. 63 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31059)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0045","text":"To the third, it must be said that under envy and pride, as they are posited in demons, are comprehended all the sins that are derived from them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0046","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0047","text":"[I q. 63 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31060)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0048","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that the Devil did not desire to be as God. For that which does not fall under apprehension does not fall under appetite, since an apprehended good moves either the sensitive, rational, or intellectual appetite (for sin can occur only in an appetite of this kind). But for some creature to be equal to God does not fall under apprehension, for it implies a contradiction: since, if it is made equal to the infinite, the finite must be infinite. Therefore an angel could not desire to be as God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0049","text":"[I q. 63 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31061)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0050","text":"Moreover, that which is the end of nature can be desired without sin. But to be made like to God is an end toward which every creature naturally tends. Therefore, if the angel desired to be as God, not by equality but by likeness, it seems that he did not sin in this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0051","text":"[I q. 63 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31062)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0052","text":"Moreover, the angel was created in a greater fullness of wisdom than man. But no man, unless wholly insane, chooses to be equal to an angel, much less to God, since choice concerns only possible things, concerning which there is deliberation. Therefore, much less did the angel sin by desiring to be as God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0053","text":"[I q. 63 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31063)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0054","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Isaiah XIV, in the person of the Devil, *I will ascend into heaven, and I will be like the Most High*. And Augustine says in the book *On Questions of the Old Testament* that, swollen with pride, he wished to be called God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0055","text":"[I q. 63 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31064)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0056","text":"I answer that it must be said that the angel, without any doubt, sinned by desiring to be as God. But this can be understood in two ways: in one way, by equality; in another way, by likeness. In the first way, indeed, he could not have desired to be as God, because he knew this to be impossible by natural knowledge; nor was the first act of sinning in him preceded by either a habit or a passion binding his cognitive power, such that, failing in the particular, he would choose the impossible, as sometimes happens in us. And yet, granted that it were possible, this would be contrary to natural desire. For there is in each thing a natural desire to preserve its own being, which would not be preserved if it were changed into another nature. Hence no thing that is in a lower grade of nature can desire the grade of a higher nature, just as an ass does not desire to be a horse, because if it were transferred to the grade of a higher nature, it would no longer be itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0057","text":"But imagination is deceived in this, because, since a man desires to be in a higher grade with respect to certain accidental things, which can increase without the corruption of the subject, it is supposed that he can desire a higher grade of nature, which he could not attain unless he ceased to be. But it is manifest that God exceeds the angel, not according to certain accidental things, but according to the grade of nature; and likewise one angel exceeds another. Hence it is impossible that a lower angel should desire to be equal to a higher one, much less that he should desire to be equal to God. But to desire to be as God by likeness occurs in two ways. In one way, with respect to that in which something is naturally apt to be made like to God. And thus, if anyone desires to be like God in this respect, he does not sin, provided that he desires to attain the likeness of God in due order, namely, that he have it from God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0058","text":"But someone would sin if he desired even according to justice to be like God, as though by his own power and not from the power of God. In another way, however, someone can desire to be like God with respect to that in which he is not naturally apt to be assimilated; as if someone desired to create heaven and earth, which is proper to God: in this desire there would be sin. And in this way the Devil desired to be as God. Not that he might be made like to Him with respect to being subject to no one simply, for thus he would also desire his own non-being, since no creature can be unless by participating in being under God. But in this he desired to be like God: because he desired as the ultimate end of beatitude that to which he could attain by the power of his nature, turning his appetite away from supernatural beatitude, which is from the grace of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0059","text":"Or, if he desired as his ultimate end that likeness to God which is given by grace, he wished to have this through the power of his nature, not from divine aid according to God's disposition. And this accords with the words of Anselm, who says that he desired that to which he would have attained had he stood firm. And these two in some way come back to the same thing, because in either case he desired to have final beatitude through his own power, which is proper to God. But because that which is through itself is the principle and cause of that which is through another, it also followed from this that he desired to have some dominion over others. In this, too, he perversely wished to be like God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0060","text":"[I q. 63 a. 3 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31065)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0061","text":"And through this the answer to all the objections is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0062","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0063","text":"[I q. 63 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31066)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0064","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth article. It seems that some demons are naturally evil. For Porphyry says, as Augustine relates in Book X of *The City of God*, that *there is a certain kind of demons, deceitful by nature, feigning gods and the souls of the dead*. But to be deceitful is to be evil. Therefore, some demons are naturally evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0065","text":"[I q. 63 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31067)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0066","text":"Moreover, just as angels are created by God, so too are human beings. But some human beings are naturally evil, of whom it is said in Wisdom XII, *their malice was natural*. Therefore some angels can also be naturally evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0067","text":"[I q. 63 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31068)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0068","text":"Moreover, some irrational animals have certain natural malices: for example, the fox is naturally crafty and the wolf is naturally rapacious; and yet they are creatures of God. Therefore demons too, although they are creatures of God, can be naturally evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0069","text":"[I q. 63 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31069)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0070","text":"But on the contrary, Dionysius says in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names* that *demons are not evil by nature*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0071","text":"[I q. 63 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31070)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0072","text":"I answer that it must be said that everything that is, insofar as it is and has some nature, naturally tends toward some good, as existing from a good principle, because an effect always returns to its principle. But some evil can be joined to some particular good, just as this evil, namely being consumptive of other things, is joined to fire; but no evil can be joined to the universal good. Therefore, if there is something whose nature is ordered to some particular good, it can naturally tend toward some evil, not insofar as it is evil, but accidentally, insofar as it is joined to a certain good. But if there is something whose nature is ordered to some good according to the common notion of good, this, according to its nature, cannot tend toward any evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0073","text":"Now it is manifest that every intellectual nature has an ordering toward the universal good, which it can apprehend, and which is the object of the will. Hence, since demons are intellectual substances, they can in no way have a natural inclination toward any evil whatsoever. And therefore they cannot be naturally evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0074","text":"[I q. 63 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31071)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0075","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that Augustine there reproves Porphyry for having said that demons were naturally deceitful, saying that they are not naturally deceitful, but deceitful by their own will. Porphyry, however, maintained on this ground that demons were deceitful by nature, because he held demons to be animals having a sensitive nature. But sensitive nature is ordered toward some particular good, with which evil can be conjoined. And in this respect they can have some natural inclination toward evil, yet accidentally, insofar as evil is conjoined to good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0076","text":"[I q. 63 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31072)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0077","text":"To the second, it must be said that the malice of certain men can be called natural, either on account of habit, which is a second nature; or on account of a natural inclination on the part of the sensitive nature toward some disordered passion, just as some are called naturally irascible or concupiscent; but not on the part of the intellectual nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0078","text":"[I q. 63 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31073)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0079","text":"To the third, it must be said that brute animals, according to their sensitive nature, have a natural inclination toward certain particular goods to which certain evils are conjoined; as the fox has an inclination to seek food shrewdly, to which craftiness is joined. Hence to be crafty is not an evil for the fox, since it is natural to it; just as neither is it an evil for a dog to be rabid, as Dionysius says, chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0080","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0081","text":"[I q. 63 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31074)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0082","text":"We proceed to the fifth thus. It seems that the Devil was evil through the fault of his own will in the first instant of his creation. For it is said in John VIII concerning the Devil: *he was a murderer from the beginning*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0083","text":"[I q. 63 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31075)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0084","text":"Further, according to Augustine, in I *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, the formlessness of the creature did not precede its formation in time, but only in origin. Now by the heaven which is read to have been created first, as he himself says in the second book, is understood the unformed angelic nature; while by the words, God said, let light be made, and light was made, is understood its formation through conversion to the Word. Therefore the nature of the angel was created, and light was made, simultaneously. But at the same time as light was made, it was distinguished from the darkness, by which sinful angels are understood. Therefore, in the first instant of their creation, some angels were blessed and some sinned.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0085","text":"[I q. 63 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31076)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0086","text":"Further, sin is opposed to merit. But in the first instant of its creation, an intellectual nature can merit, as the soul of Christ, or even the good angels themselves. Therefore demons too could sin in the first instant of their creation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0087","text":"[I q. 63 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31077)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0088","text":"Further, angelic nature is more powerful than corporeal nature. But a corporeal thing immediately begins to have its operation in the first instant of its creation; just as fire, in the first instant in which it is generated, begins to move upward. Therefore the angel too could operate in the first instant of its creation. Either, then, it had a right operation, or an unrighteous one. If a right one, since they had grace, through it they merited beatitude. But in angels reward immediately follows merit, as was said above. Therefore they would immediately have been blessed, and thus would never have sinned—which is false. It remains, therefore, that in the first instant, by operating not rightly, they sinned.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0089","text":"[I q. 63 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31078)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0090","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Genesis I: *God saw all the things that he had made, and they were very good*. Now demons too were among them. Therefore demons too were at some time good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0091","text":"[I q. 63 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31079)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0092","text":"I answer that it must be said that some have maintained that demons were evil immediately, in the first instant of their creation, not indeed by nature but through the sin of their own will, because *from the moment the Devil was made, he refused justice*. To this opinion, as Augustine says in XI *On the City of God*, *whoever assents does not think with those heretics, namely the Manichaeans, who say that the Devil has a nature of evil*. But because this opinion contradicts the authority of Scripture (for it is said, under the figure of the prince of Babylon, concerning the Devil, in Isaiah XIV, *how have you fallen, Lucifer, who arose in the morning?* And in Ezekiel XXVIII, *you were in the delights of the Paradise of God* is said to the Devil under the person of the king of Tyre), therefore this opinion was reasonably rejected by the masters as erroneous. Hence some said that angels could sin in the first instant of their creation, but did not sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0093","text":"But this opinion too is disapproved by some on the ground that, when two operations follow one another, it seems impossible for both operations to be completed in the same instant. Now it is manifest that the angel's sin was an operation subsequent to creation. But the term of creation is the very being of the angel, whereas the term of the operation of sin is that they are evil. Therefore it seems impossible that, in the first instant in which the angel began to be, it was evil. But this reasoning does not seem sufficient. For it has place only in temporal motions, which are carried out successively; as if local motion follows alteration, the alteration and the local motion cannot be completed in the same instant. But if the changes are instantaneous, the term of the first and of the second change can be simultaneous and in the same instant; just as, in the same instant in which the moon is illuminated by the sun, the air is illuminated by the moon.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0094","text":"Now it is manifest that creation is instantaneous; and likewise the movement of free choice in angels; for they have no need of comparison and discursive reasoning, as is clear from what was said above. Hence nothing prevents the term of creation and the term of free choice from being simultaneous and in the same instant. And therefore it must be said otherwise: that it was impossible for an angel to have sinned in the first instant through a disordered act of free choice. For although a thing can begin to operate at the same time in the first instant in which it begins to be, nevertheless the operation that begins at the same time as the being of the thing is from the agent from which it has being; as upward motion belongs to fire from its generator. Hence if some thing has being from a defective agent, which can be the cause of a defective action, it can have a defective operation in the first instant in which it begins to be; as if a leg that is born lame from the weakness of the seed immediately begins to limp.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0095","text":"But the agent that brought angels into being, namely God, cannot be the cause of sin. Hence it cannot be said that the Devil was evil in the first instant of his creation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0096","text":"[I q. 63 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31080)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0097","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, as Augustine says in *The City of God*, XI, *when it is said that the Devil sins from the beginning, he is not to be thought to sin from the beginning from which he was created, but from the beginning of sin*; namely, because he never withdrew from his sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0098","text":"[I q. 63 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31081)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0099","text":"To the second, it must be said that that distinction of light and darkness, insofar as the sins of the demons are understood by darkness, is to be taken according to God's foreknowledge. Hence Augustine says, in *The City of God*, XI, that *he alone was able to distinguish light and darkness who was also able, before they fell, to foreknow that they would fall*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0100","text":"[I q. 63 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31082)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0101","text":"To the third, it must be said that whatever is in merit is from God, and therefore in the first instant of his creation the angel could merit. But the same reasoning does not apply to sin, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0102","text":"[I q. 63 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31083)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0103","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that God did not distinguish among the angels before the turning away of some and the conversion of others, as Augustine says in *The City of God*, XI; and therefore all, created in grace, merited in the first instant. But some of them immediately presented an impediment to their own beatitude, mortifying their preceding merit. And therefore they are deprived of the beatitude which they merited.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0104","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0105","text":"[I q. 63 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31084)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0106","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth. It seems that there was some delay between the creation and the fall of the angel. For it is said in Ezek. XXVIII: *you walked perfect in your ways from the day of your conception, until iniquity was found in you*. But walking, since it is continuous motion, requires some delay. Therefore there was some delay between the creation of the Devil and his fall.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0107","text":"[I q. 63 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31085)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0108","text":"Further, Origen says that *the ancient serpent did not immediately walk upon his breast and belly*; by which his sin is understood. Therefore the Devil did not sin immediately after the first instant of his creation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0109","text":"[I q. 63 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31086)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0110","text":"Further, the capacity to sin is common to man and angel. But there was some delay between the formation of man and his sin. Therefore, by equal reasoning, there was some delay between the formation of the Devil and his sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0111","text":"[I q. 63 a. 6 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31087)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0112","text":"Further, the instant in which the Devil sinned was different from the instant in which he was created. But between any two instants an intermediate time falls. Therefore there was some delay between his creation and his fall.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0113","text":"[I q. 63 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31088)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0114","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in John VIII of the Devil that *he did not stand in the truth*. And as Augustine says in *The City of God*, XI, *we must understand this thus: that he was in the truth, but did not remain*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0115","text":"[I q. 63 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31089)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0116","text":"I answer that it must be said that there is a twofold opinion concerning this. But the more probable opinion, and the one more consonant with the sayings of the saints, is that the Devil sinned immediately after the first instant of his creation. And this must be said if it is held that in the first instant of his creation he broke forth into an act of free choice, and was created with grace, as we said above. For since angels attain beatitude through one meritorious act, as was said above, if the Devil, created in grace, merited in the first instant, he would have received beatitude immediately after the first instant, unless he had immediately presented an impediment by sinning. But if it is held that the angel was not created in grace, or that he could not have an act of free choice in the first instant, nothing prevents there having been some delay between creation and the fall.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0117","text":"[I q. 63 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31090)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0118","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that by bodily motions, which are measured by time, instantaneous spiritual motions are sometimes understood metaphorically in Sacred Scripture. And thus by walking is understood the motion of free choice tending toward the good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0119","text":"[I q. 63 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31091)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0120","text":"To the second, it must be said that Origen says that *the ancient serpent did not walk upon his breast from the beginning, nor immediately*, on account of the first instant, in which he was not evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0121","text":"[I q. 63 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31092)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0122","text":"To the third, it must be said that an Angel has free choice inflexible after election, and therefore, unless immediately after the first instant, in which he had a natural movement toward the good, he had presented an impediment to beatitude, he would have been confirmed in the good. But it is not similar in the case of man. And therefore the argument does not follow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0123","text":"[I q. 63 a. 6 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31093)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0124","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that there is an intermediate time between any two instants is true insofar as time is continuous, as is proved in Physics VI. But in Angels, who are not subject to the celestial motion which is measured first by continuous time, time is taken for the very succession of operations of intellect, or even of affection. Thus, therefore, the first instant in Angels is understood to correspond to that operation of the angelic mind by which it turns itself toward itself through evening knowledge, because on the first day evening is mentioned, but not morning. And this operation indeed was good in all. But from this operation some were turned, through morning knowledge, to the praise of the Word; whereas others, remaining in themselves, became night, swelling with pride, as Augustine says, On the Literal Meaning of Genesis IV. And thus the first operation was common to all; but in the second they were distinguished.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0125","text":"And therefore in the first instant all were good; but in the second the good were distinguished from the evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0126","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0127","text":"[I q. 63 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31094)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0128","text":"The seventh is approached thus. It seems that the Angel who was highest among those sinning was not highest among all. For it is said of him in Ezekiel XXVIII, *you, an extended and protecting Cherub, I placed you on the holy mountain of God*. But the order of Cherubim is beneath the order of Seraphim, as Dionysius says, in chapter VII of the Angelic Hierarchy. Therefore the Angel who was highest among those sinning was not highest among all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0129","text":"[I q. 63 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31095)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0130","text":"Moreover, God made intellectual nature for the sake of attaining beatitude. Therefore, if the Angel who was highest among all sinned, it follows that the divine ordering was frustrated in the noblest creature. Which is unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0131","text":"[I q. 63 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31096)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0132","text":"Moreover, the more something is inclined toward something, the less it can fail from it. But the higher an Angel is, the more he is inclined toward God. Therefore, he can less fail from God by sinning. And thus it seems that the Angel who sinned was not highest among all, but one of the lower ones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0133","text":"[I q. 63 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31097)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0134","text":"But to the contrary is what Gregory says, in the homily on the hundred sheep: *the first Angel who sinned, since, being set over all the hosts of Angels, he surpassed their brightness, was brighter in comparison with them*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0135","text":"[I q. 63 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31098)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0136","text":"I answer that it must be said that in sin there are two things to consider, namely, proneness to sinning and the motive for sinning. If, therefore, we consider in Angels proneness to sinning, it seems less likely that the higher Angels sinned than the lower. And on this account Damascene says that the greatest of those who sinned was set over the terrestrial order. And this opinion seems to accord with the position of the Platonists, which Augustine recounts in Books VIII and X of The City of God. For they said that all the gods were good, but that among the Demons some were good and some evil; calling gods the intellectual substances that are above the lunar sphere, but Demons the intellectual substances that are beneath the lunar sphere, superior to men in the order of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0137","text":"Nor is this opinion to be rejected as alien to the faith, because the whole corporeal creature is administered by God through Angels, as Augustine says in On the Trinity III; hence nothing prevents one from saying that the lower Angels were divinely assigned to administer lower bodies, the higher to administer higher bodies, and the highest to attend upon God. And accordingly Damascene says that those who fell were among the lower ones, in whose order some good Angels also remained. But if the motive for sinning is considered, it is found to be greater in the higher than in the lower. For the sin of the Demons was pride, as was said above; and its motive is excellence, which was greater in the higher. And therefore Gregory says that he who sinned was highest among all. And this seems more probable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0138","text":"Because the Angel's sin did not proceed from any proneness, but from free choice alone, whence the argument taken from the motive for sinning seems rather to be considered. Nevertheless, no prejudice should thereby be done to the other opinion, because even in the prince of the lower Angels there could have been some motive for sinning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0139","text":"[I q. 63 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31099)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0140","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Cherubim is interpreted as fullness of knowledge; but Seraphim is interpreted as burning or advancing. And thus it is clear that Cherubim is denominated from knowledge, which can exist with mortal sin; whereas Seraphim is denominated from the ardor of charity, which cannot exist with mortal sin. And therefore the first Angel sinning was not called Seraphim, but Cherubim.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0141","text":"[I q. 63 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31100)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0142","text":"To the second, it must be said that the divine intention is not frustrated either in those who sin or in those who are saved, for God foreknows the outcome of both, and from each has glory, while from his goodness he saves these and from his justice punishes those. But the intellectual creature itself, when it sins, falls short of its due end. Nor is this unfitting in any exalted creature, for the intellectual creature was so instituted by God that to act for an end is placed in its own choice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0143","text":"[I q. 63 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31101)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0144","text":"To the third, it must be said that, however great the inclination toward the good may have been in the highest Angel, it nevertheless did not impose necessity upon him. Hence he could, through free choice, not follow it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0145","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0146","text":"[I q. 63 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31102)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0147","text":"We proceed thus to the eighth. It seems that the sin of the first angel to sin was not a cause of sinning for the others. For a cause is prior to its effect. But all sinned simultaneously, as Damascene says. Therefore, the sin of one was not a cause of sinning for the others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0148","text":"[I q. 63 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31103)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0149","text":"Moreover, the first sin of an angel can only have been pride, as was said above. But pride seeks excellence. Now it is more opposed to excellence that someone be subjected to an inferior than to a superior; and thus it does not seem that the demons sinned by wishing to be subject to one of the superior angels rather than to God. Yet the sin of one angel would have been a cause of sinning for the others if he had induced them to subject themselves to him. Therefore, it does not seem that the sin of the first angel was a cause of sinning for the others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0150","text":"[I q. 63 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31104)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0151","text":"Moreover, it is a greater sin to wish to be subject to another against God than, against God, to wish to preside over another, because it has less of a motive for sinning. Therefore, if the sin of the first angel was a cause of sinning for the others in this, that he induced them to subject themselves to him, the inferior angels would have sinned more gravely than the highest, which is contrary to what the Gloss says on that verse of Psalm CIII, *this dragon which you formed*: *he who was more excellent than the others in being was made greater in malice*. Therefore, the sin of the first angel was not a cause of sinning for the others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0152","text":"[I q. 63 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31105)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0153","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Apocalypse XII that the dragon drew after himself a third part of the stars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0154","text":"[I q. 63 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31106)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0155","text":"I answer that it must be said that the sin of the first angel was a cause of sinning for the others, not indeed compelling them, but inducing them by a kind of exhortation. A sign of this is apparent from the fact that all the demons are subject to that highest one, as is manifest from what the Lord says, Matthew XXV: *Depart, ye cursed, into eternal fire, which has been prepared for the Devil and his angels.* For the order of divine justice has it that whoever consents in fault to someone's suggestion is subjected in punishment to his power; according to II Peter II: by whom a man has been overcome, to him he has been bound as a slave.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0156","text":"[I q. 63 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31107)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0157","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although the demons sinned simultaneously, nevertheless the sin of one could have been a cause of sinning for the others. For an angel does not require a delay of time for choosing, exhorting, or even consenting, as a man does, who requires deliberation for choosing and consenting, and vocal speech for exhorting, both of which take place in time. Now it is manifest that even a man, at the same time as he has already conceived something in his heart, begins in that same instant to speak. And in the final instant of speech, in which someone apprehends the meaning of the speaker, he can assent to what is said, as is especially evident in the first conceptions, which each person approves upon hearing them. Therefore, if the time of speech and deliberation required in us is removed, in the same instant in which the first angel expressed his affection by intelligible speech, it was possible for the others to consent to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0158","text":"[I q. 63 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31108)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0158"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0159","text":"To the second, it must be said that the proud man, other things being equal, prefers to be subject to a superior rather than to an inferior. But if he attains some excellence under an inferior which he cannot attain under a superior, he chooses rather to be subject to an inferior than to a superior. Thus, therefore, it was not contrary to the pride of the demons that they wished to be subject to an inferior, consenting to his rule; wishing to have him as prince and leader in order that, by natural power, they might attain their ultimate beatitude; especially since they were even then subject to the highest angel according to the order of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0159"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0160","text":"[I q. 63 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31109)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0160"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0161","text":"To the third, it must be said that, as was said above, an angel has nothing that retards him, but he is moved according to his whole power toward that to which he is moved, whether toward good or toward evil. Therefore, because the highest angel had greater natural power than the inferior angels, he fell into sin with a more intense motion. And therefore he was also made greater in malice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0161"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0162","text":"## Article 9","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0162"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0163","text":"[I q. 63 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31110)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0163"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0164","text":"We proceed thus to the ninth. It seems that more of the angels sinned than remained. For, as the Philosopher says, *evil is among the greater number, good among the fewer*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0164"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0165","text":"[I q. 63 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31111)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0165"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0166","text":"Moreover, justice and sin are found in angels and men on the same account. But among men more are found to be evil than good, according to Ecclesiastes I: *the number of fools is infinite*. Therefore, by equal reasoning, among the angels as well.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0166"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0167","text":"[I q. 63 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31112)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0167"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0168","text":"Moreover, angels are distinguished according to persons and according to orders. Therefore, if more angelic persons remained, it seems also that some did not sin from every order.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0168"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0169","text":"[I q. 63 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31113)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0169"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0170","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in IV Kings VI, *there are more with us than with them*; which is explained concerning the good Angels who are with us to aid us, and the evil ones who oppose us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0170"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0171","text":"[I q. 63 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31114)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0171"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0172","text":"I answer that it must be said that more Angels remained than sinned. For sin is contrary to natural inclination; but things that occur contrary to nature happen in fewer cases. For nature attains its effect either always or in the majority of cases.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0172"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0173","text":"[I q. 63 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31115)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0173"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0174","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the Philosopher speaks with regard to men, in whom evil occurs because they follow sensible goods, which are known to more people, abandoning the good of reason, which is known to fewer. But in Angels there is only an intellectual nature. Hence the reasoning is not the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0174"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0175","text":"[I q. 63 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31116)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0175"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0176","text":"And through this the answer to the second is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0176"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0177","text":"[I q. 63 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31117)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0177"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0178","text":"To the third, it must be said that, according to those who say that the Devil was greater from the lower order of Angels, who preside over earthly things, it is clear that they did not fall from every order, but only from the lowest. But according to those who maintain that the greater Devil was from the highest order, it is probable that some fell from every order; just as men are assumed into every order to make up for the angelic ruin. In this too the freedom of free choice is more clearly proved, since at every grade of creature it can be bent toward evil. In Sacred Scripture, however, the names of certain orders, such as Seraphim and Thrones, are not attributed to demons, because these names are taken from the ardor of charity and from the indwelling of God, which cannot coexist with mortal sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0178"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0179","text":"But the names Cherubim, Powers, and Principalities are attributed to them, because these names are taken from knowledge and power, which can be common to the good and the evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_55:P0179"]}],"source_digest":"2ea452327e53f6feeb77192e3985728217e858c1493f5631e3fdf859b123eb96","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 63"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 63","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56","title":"First Part · Question 64","order":65,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_56","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 64 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31118)\n\nConsequenter quaeritur de poena Daemonum. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, de obtenebratione intellectus. Secundo, de obstinatione voluntatis. Tertio, de dolore ipsorum. Quarto, de loco poenali ipsorum.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 64 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31119)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus Daemonis sit obtenebratus per privationem cognitionis omnis veritatis. Si enim aliquam veritatem cognoscerent, maxime cognoscerent seipsos, quod est cognoscere substantias separatas. Hoc autem eorum miseriae non convenit, cum ad magnam beatitudinem pertinere videatur, intantum quod quidam ultimam beatitudinem hominis posuerunt in cognoscendo substantias separatas. Ergo Daemones privantur omni cognitione veritatis.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31120)\n\nPraeterea, id quod est manifestissimum in natura, videtur esse maxime manifestum Angelis, sive bonis sive malis. Quod enim non sit nobis maxime manifestum, contingit ex debilitate intellectus nostri a phantasmatibus accipientis, sicut ex debilitate oculi noctuae contingit quod non possit videre lumen solis. Sed Daemones non possunt cognoscere Deum, qui est secundum se manifestissimus, cum sit in summo veritatis, eo quod non habent mundum cor, quo solo videtur Deus. Ergo nec alia cognoscere possunt.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31121)\n\nPraeterea, cognitio rerum Angelis conveniens est duplex, secundum Augustinum, scilicet matutina, et vespertina. Sed cognitio matutina non competit Daemonibus, quia non vident res in verbo, nec etiam cognitio vespertina, quia cognitio vespertina refert res cognitas ad laudem creatoris (unde post vespere fit mane, ut dicitur Gen. I). Ergo Daemones non possunt cognitionem de rebus habere.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31122)\n\nPraeterea, Angeli in sua conditione cognoverunt mysterium regni Dei, ut Augustinus dicit, V super Gen. ad Litt. Sed Daemones hac cognitione privati sunt, quia *si cognovissent, nequaquam dominum gloriae crucifixissent*, ut dicitur I Cor. II. Ergo, pari ratione, omni alia cognitione veritatis sunt privati.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31123)\n\nPraeterea, quamcumque veritatem aliquis scit, aut cognoscit eam naturaliter, sicut nos cognoscimus prima principia; aut accipiendo ab alio, sicut quae scimus addiscendo; aut per experientiam longi temporis, sicut quae scimus inveniendo. Sed Daemones non possunt cognoscere veritatem per suam naturam, quia ab eis divisi sunt boni Angeli sicut lux a tenebris, ut Augustinus dicit; omnis autem manifestatio fit per lumen, ut dicitur Ephes. V. Similiter etiam neque per revelationem, neque addiscendo a bonis Angelis, quia non est conventio lucis ad tenebras, ut dicitur II Cor. VI. Neque etiam per experientiam longi temporis, quia experientia a sensu oritur. Ergo nulla in eis est cognitio veritatis.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31124)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *data Daemonibus angelica dona nequaquam mutata esse dicimus, sed sunt integra et splendidissima*. Inter ista autem naturalia dona est cognitio veritatis. Ergo in eis est aliqua veritatis cognitio.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31125)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod duplex est cognitio veritatis, una quidem quae habetur per gratiam; alia vero quae habetur per naturam. Et ista quae habetur per gratiam, est duplex, una quae est speculativa tantum, sicut cum alicui aliqua secreta divinorum revelantur; alia vero quae est affectiva, producens amorem Dei; et haec proprie pertinet ad donum sapientiae. Harum autem trium cognitionum prima in Daemonibus nec est ablata, nec diminuta. Consequitur enim ipsam naturam Angeli, qui secundum suam naturam est quidam intellectus vel mens, propter simplicitatem autem suae substantiae, a natura eius aliquid subtrahi non potest, ut sic per subtractionem naturalium puniatur, sicut homo punitur per subtractionem manus aut pedis aut alicuius huiusmodi. Et ideo dicit Dionysius quod dona naturalia in eis integra manent. Unde naturalis cognitio in eis non est diminuta. Secunda autem cognitio, quae est per gratiam, in speculatione consistens, non est in eis totaliter ablata, sed diminuta, quia de huiusmodi secretis divinis tantum revelatur eis quantum oportet, vel mediantibus Angelis, vel per aliqua temporalia divinae virtutis effecta, ut dicit Augustinus, IX de Civ. Dei; non autem sicut ipsis sanctis Angelis, quibus plura et clarius revelantur in ipso verbo. A tertia vero cognitione sunt totaliter privati, sicut et a caritate.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31126)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod felicitas consistit in applicatione ad id quod superius est. Substantiae autem separatae sunt ordine naturae supra nos, unde aliqualis ratio felicitatis esse potest homini si cognoscat substantias separatas; licet perfecta eius felicitas sit in cognoscendo primam substantiam, scilicet Deum. Sed substantiae separatae cognoscere substantiam separatam est connaturale, sicut et nobis cognoscere naturas sensibiles. Unde sicut in hoc non est felicitas hominis, quod cognoscat naturas sensibiles; ita non est felicitas Angeli in hoc, quod cognoscat substantias separatas.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31127)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod illud quod est manifestissimum in natura, est nobis occultum propter hoc quod excedit proportionem intellectus nostri; et non solum propter hoc quod intellectus noster accipit a phantasmatibus. Excedit autem divina substantia non solum proportionem intellectus humani, sed etiam intellectus angelici. Unde nec ipse Angelus secundum suam naturam, potest cognoscere Dei substantiam. Potest tamen altiorem cognitionem de Deo habere per suam naturam quam homo, propter perfectionem sui intellectus. Et talis cognitio Dei remanet etiam in Daemonibus. Licet enim non habeant puritatem quae est per gratiam, habent tamen puritatem naturae, quae sufficit ad cognitionem Dei quae eis competit ex natura.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31128)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod creatura tenebra est, comparata excellentiae divini luminis, et ideo cognitio creaturae in propria natura, vespertina dicitur. Vespere enim est tenebris adiunctum, habet tamen aliquid de luce, cum autem totaliter deficit lux, est nox. Sic igitur et cognitio rerum in propria natura, quando refertur ad laudem creatoris, ut in bonis Angelis, habet aliquid de luce divina, et potest dici vespertina, si autem non referatur in Deum, sicut in Daemonibus, non dicitur vespertina, sed nocturna. Unde et in Genesi I, legitur quod tenebras quas Deus a luce separavit, vocavit noctem.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31129)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod mysterium regni Dei, quod est impletum per Christum, omnes quidem Angeli a principio aliquo modo cognoverunt; maxime ex quo beatificati sunt visione verbi, quam Daemones nunquam habuerunt. Non tamen omnes Angeli cognoverunt perfecte, neque aequaliter. Unde Daemones multo minus, Christo existente in mundo, perfecte mysterium incarnationis cognoverunt. Non enim innotuit eis, ut Augustinus dicit sicut Angelis sanctis, qui verbi participata aeternitate perfruuntur, sed sicut eis terrendis innotescendum fuit per quaedam temporalia effecta. Si autem perfecte et per certitudinem cognovissent ipsum esse filium Dei, et effectum passionis eius, nunquam dominum gloriae crucifigi procurassent.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31130)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod Daemones tribus modis cognoscunt veritatem aliquam. Uno modo, subtilitate suae naturae, quia licet sint obtenebrati per privationem luminis gratiae, sunt tamen lucidi lumine intellectualis naturae. Secundo, per revelationem a sanctis Angelis; cum quibus non conveniunt quidem per conformitatem voluntatis; conveniunt autem similitudine intellectualis naturae, secundum quam possunt accipere quod ab aliis manifestatur. Tertio modo cognoscunt per experientiam longi temporis; non quasi a sensu accipientes; sed dum in rebus singularibus completur similitudo eius speciei intelligibilis quam sibi naturaliter habent inditam, aliqua cognoscunt praesentia, quae non praecognoverunt futura, ut supra de cognitione Angelorum dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 64 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31131)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntas Daemonum non sit obstinata in malo. Libertas enim arbitrii ad naturam intellectualis naturae pertinet, quae manet in Daemonibus, ut dictum est. Sed libertas arbitrii per se et prius ordinatur ad bonum quam ad malum. Ergo voluntas Daemonis non est ita obstinata in malo, quin possit redire ad bonum.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31132)\n\nPraeterea, maior est misericordia Dei, quae est infinita, quam Daemonis malitia, quae est finita. A malitia autem culpae ad bonitatem iustitiae nullus redit nisi per Dei misericordiam. Ergo etiam Daemones a statu malitiae possunt redire ad statum iustitiae.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31133)\n\nPraeterea, si Daemones habent voluntatem obstinatam in malo, maxime haberent eam obstinatam in peccato quo peccaverunt. Sed illud peccatum in eis nunc non manet, scilicet superbia quia nec motivum manet, scilicet excellentia. Ergo Daemon non est obstinatus in malitia.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31134)\n\nPraeterea, Gregorius dicit quod homo per alium reparari potuit, quia per alium cecidit. Sed Daemones inferiores per primum ceciderunt, ut supra dictum est. Ergo eorum casus per alium reparari potest. Ergo non sunt in malitia obstinati.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31135)\n\nPraeterea, quicumque est in malitia obstinatus, nunquam aliquod bonum opus operatur. Sed Daemon aliqua bona opera facit, confitetur enim veritatem, dicens Christo, scio quia sis sanctus Dei, Marc. I, Daemones etiam credunt et contremiscunt, ut dicitur Iacob. II; Dionysius etiam dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *bonum et optimum concupiscunt, esse, vivere et intelligere*. Ergo non sunt obstinati in malitia.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31136)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo LXXIII, *superbia eorum qui te oderunt, ascendit semper*; quod de Daemonibus exponitur. Ergo semper obstinati in malitia perseverant.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31137)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Origenis positio fuit quod omnis voluntas creaturae, propter libertatem arbitrii, potest flecti et in bonum et in malum, excepta anima Christi propter unionem verbi. Sed haec positio tollit veritatem beatitudinis a sanctis Angelis et hominibus, quia stabilitas sempiterna est de ratione verae beatitudinis; unde et vita aeterna nominatur. Repugnat etiam auctoritati Scripturae sacrae, quae Daemones et homines malos in supplicium aeternum mittendos, bonos autem in vitam aeternam transferendos pronuntiat, Matth. XXV. Unde haec positio est tanquam erronea reputanda; et tenendum est firmiter, secundum fidem Catholicam, quod et voluntas bonorum Angelorum confirmata est in bono, et voluntas Daemonum obstinata est in malo. Causam autem huius obstinationis oportet accipere, non ex gravitate culpae, sed ex conditione naturae status. *Hoc enim est hominibus mors, quod Angelis casus*, ut Damascenus dicit. Manifestum est autem quod omnia mortalia peccata hominum, sive sint magna sive sint parva, ante mortem sunt remissibilia; post mortem vero, irremissibilia, et perpetuo manentia. Ad inquirendum ergo causam huiusmodi obstinationis, considerandum est quod vis appetitiva in omnibus proportionatur apprehensivae a qua movetur, sicut mobile motori. Appetitus enim sensitivus est boni particularis, voluntas vero universalis, ut supra dictum est; sicut etiam sensus apprehensivus est singularium, intellectus vero universalium. Differt autem apprehensio Angeli ab apprehensione hominis in hoc, quod Angelus apprehendit immobiliter per intellectum, sicut et nos immobiliter apprehendimus prima principia, quorum est intellectus, homo vero per rationem apprehendit mobiliter, discurrendo de uno ad aliud, habens viam procedendi ad utrumque oppositorum. Unde et voluntas hominis adhaeret alicui mobiliter, quasi potens etiam ab eo discedere et contrario adhaerere, voluntas autem Angeli adhaeret fixe et immobiliter. Et ideo, si consideretur ante adhaesionem, potest libere adhaerere et huic et opposito (in his scilicet quae non naturaliter vult), sed postquam iam adhaesit, immobiliter adhaeret. Et ideo consuevit dici quod liberum arbitrium hominis flexibile est ad oppositum et ante electionem, et post; liberum autem arbitrium Angeli est flexibile ad utrumque oppositum ante electionem, sed non post. Sic igitur et boni Angeli, semper adhaerentes iustitiae, sunt in illa confirmati, mali vero, peccantes, sunt in peccato obstinati. De obstinatione vero hominum damnatorum infra dicetur.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31138)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod boni et mali Angeli habent liberum arbitrium, sed secundum modum et conditionem suae naturae, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31139)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod misericordia Dei liberat a peccato poenitentes. Illi vero qui poenitentiae capaces non sunt, immobiliter malo adhaerentes per divinam misericordiam non liberantur.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31140)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod adhuc manet in Diabolo peccatum quo primo peccavit, quantum ad appetitum; licet non quantum ad hoc quod credat se posse obtinere. Sicut si aliquis credat se posse facere homicidium, et velit facere, et postea adimatur ei potestas; nihilominus voluntas homicidii in eo manere potest, ut velit fecisse, vel velit facere si posset.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31141)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod non est tota causa quare peccatum hominis sit remissibile, quia alio suggerente peccavit. Et ideo ratio non sequitur.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31142)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod actus Daemonis est duplex. Quidam scilicet ex voluntate deliberata procedens, et hic proprie potest dici actus eius. Et talis actus Daemonis semper est malus, quia etsi aliquando aliquod bonum faciat, non tamen bene facit; sicut dum veritatem dicit ut decipiat, et dum non voluntarie credit et confitetur, sed rerum evidentia coactus. Alius autem actus Daemonis est naturalis; qui bonus esse potest, et attestatur bonitati naturae. Et tamen etiam tali bono actu abutuntur ad malum.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 64 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31143)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod dolor non sit in Daemonibus. Cum enim dolor et gaudium opponantur, non possunt esse simul in eodem. Sed in Daemonibus est gaudium, dicit enim Augustinus, contra Manichaeos, *Diabolus potestatem habet in eos qui Dei praecepta contemnunt, et de hac tam infelici potestate laetatur*. Ergo in Daemonibus non est dolor.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31144)\n\nPraeterea, dolor est causa timoris, de his enim timemus dum futura sunt, de quibus dolemus dum praesentia sunt. Sed in Daemonibus non est timor; secundum illud Iob XLI, *factus est ut nullum timeret*. Ergo in Daemonibus non est dolor.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31145)\n\nPraeterea, dolere de malo est bonum. Sed Daemones non possunt bene facere. Ergo non possunt dolere, ad minus de malo culpae; quod pertinet ad vermem conscientiae.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31146)\n\nSed contra est quod peccatum Daemonis est gravius quam peccatum hominis. Sed homo punitur dolore pro delectatione peccati; secundum illud Apoc. XVIII, *quantum glorificavit se et in deliciis fuit, tantum date ei tormentum et luctum*. Ergo multo magis Diabolus, qui maximo se glorificavit, punitur doloris luctu.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31147)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod timor, dolor, gaudium, et huiusmodi, secundum quod sunt passiones, in Daemonibus esse non possunt, sic enim sunt propriae appetitus sensitivi, qui est virtus in organo corporali. Sed secundum quod nominant simplices actus voluntatis, sic possunt esse in Daemonibus. Et necesse est dicere quod in eis sit dolor. Quia dolor, secundum quod significat simplicem actum voluntatis, nihil est aliud quam renisus voluntatis ad id quod est vel non est. Patet autem quod Daemones multa vellent non esse quae sunt, et esse quae non sunt, vellent enim, cum sint invidi, damnari eos qui salvantur. Unde oportet dicere quod in eis sit dolor, et praecipue quia de ratione poenae est, quod voluntati repugnet. Privantur etiam beatitudine quam naturaliter appetunt; et in multis eorum iniqua voluntas cohibetur.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31148)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod gaudium et dolor de eodem sunt opposita, non autem de diversis. Unde nihil prohibet unum simul dolere de uno, et gaudere de alio; et maxime secundum quod dolor et gaudium important simplices voluntatis actus; quia non solum in diversis, sed etiam in una et eadem re potest esse aliquid quod volumus, et aliquid quod nolumus.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31149)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod sicut in Daemonibus est dolor de praesenti, ita et timor de futuro. Quod autem dicitur, factus est ut nullum timeret, intelligitur de timore Dei cohibente a peccato. Alibi, namque scriptum est quod Daemones credunt et contremiscunt.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31150)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod dolere de malo culpae propter se attestatur voluntatis bonitati, cui malum culpae opponitur. Dolere autem de malo poenae, vel de malo culpae propter poenam, attestatur bonitati naturae, cui malum poenae opponitur. Unde Augustinus dicit, XIX de Civ. Dei, quod *dolor amissi boni in supplicio, testis est naturae bonae*. Daemon ergo, cum perversae sit voluntatis et obstinatae, de malo culpae non dolet.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 64 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31151)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aer iste non sit locus poenalis Daemonum. Daemon enim est natura spiritualis. Natura autem spiritualis non afficitur loco. Ergo nullus locus est Daemonibus poenalis.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31152)\n\nPraeterea, peccatum hominis non est gravius quam peccatum Daemonis. Sed locus poenalis hominis est Infernus. Ergo multo magis Daemonis. Ergo non aer caliginosus.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31153)\n\nPraeterea, Daemones puniuntur poena ignis. Sed in aere caliginoso non est ignis. Ergo aer caliginosus non est locus poenae Daemonum.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31154)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, III super Gen. ad Litt., quod *aer caliginosus est quasi carcer Daemonibus usque ad tempus iudicii*.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31155)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Angeli, secundum suam naturam, medii sunt inter Deum et homines. Habet autem hoc divinae providentiae ratio, quod inferiorum bonum per superiora procuret. Bonum autem hominis dupliciter procuratur per divinam providentiam. Uno modo directe, dum scilicet aliquis inducitur ad bonum et retrahitur a malo, et hoc decenter fit per Angelos bonos. Alio modo indirecte, dum scilicet aliquis exercetur, impugnatus, per impugnationem contrarii. Et hanc procurationem boni humani conveniens fuit per malos Angelos fieri, ne totaliter post peccatum ab utilitate naturalis ordinis exciderent. Sic ergo Daemonibus duplex locus poenalis debetur. Unus quidem ratione suae culpae, et hic est Infernus. Alius autem ratione exercitationis humanae, et sic debetur eis caliginosus aer. Procuratio autem salutis humanae protenditur usque ad diem iudicii, unde et usque tunc durat ministerium Angelorum et exercitatio Daemonum. Unde et usque tunc et boni Angeli ad nos huc mittuntur, et Daemones in hoc aere caliginoso sunt ad nostrum exercitium, licet eorum aliqui etiam nunc in Inferno sint, ad torquendum eos quos ad malum induxerunt; sicut et aliquis boni Angeli sunt cum animabus sanctis in caelo. Sed post diem iudicii omnes mali, tam homines quam Angeli, in Inferno erunt; boni vero in caelo.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31156)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod locus non est poenalis Angelo aut animae, quasi afficiens alterando naturam; sed quasi afficiens voluntatem contristando, dum Angelus vel anima apprehendit se esse in loco non convenienti suae voluntati.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31157)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod anima secundum ordinem naturae non praefertur alteri animae, sicut Daemones ordine naturae praeferuntur hominibus. Unde non est similis ratio.\n\n[I q. 64 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31158)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod aliqui dixerunt usque ad diem iudicii differri poenam sensibilem tam Daemonum quam animarum, et similiter beatitudinem sanctorum differri usque ad diem iudicii; quod est erroneum, et repugnans apostoli sententiae, qui dicit, II Cor. V, *si terrestris domus nostra huius habitationis dissolvatur, domum habemus in caelo*. Alii vero, licet hoc non concedant de animabus, concedunt tamen de Daemonibus. Sed melius est dicendum quod idem iudicium sit de malis animabus et malis Angelis; sicut idem iudicium est de bonis animabus et bonis Angelis. Unde dicendum est quod, sicut locus caelestis pertinet ad gloriam Angelorum, tamen gloria eorum non minuitur cum ad nos veniunt, quia considerant illum locum esse suum (eo modo quo dicimus honorem episcopi non minui dum actu non sedet in cathedra); similiter dicendum est quod Daemones licet non actu alligentur gehennali igni, dum sunt in aere isto caliginoso, tamen ex hoc ipso quod sciunt illam alligationem sibi deberi, eorum poena non diminuitur. Unde dicitur in quadam Glossa Iacobi III, quod *portant secum ignem Gehennae quocumque vadant*. Nec est contra hoc, quod rogaverunt dominum ut non mitteret eos in abyssum, ut dicitur Lucae VIII, quia hoc petierunt reputantes sibi poenam, si excluderentur a loco in quo possunt hominibus nocere. Unde Marci V, dicitur quod *deprecabantur eum ne expelleret eos extra regionem*.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0002","text":"[I q. 64 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31118)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0003","text":"Consequenter quaeritur de poena Daemonum. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, de obtenebratione intellectus. Secundo, de obstinatione voluntatis. Tertio, de dolore ipsorum. Quarto, de loco poenali ipsorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0005","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31119)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus Daemonis sit obtenebratus per privationem cognitionis omnis veritatis. Si enim aliquam veritatem cognoscerent, maxime cognoscerent seipsos, quod est cognoscere substantias separatas. Hoc autem eorum miseriae non convenit, cum ad magnam beatitudinem pertinere videatur, intantum quod quidam ultimam beatitudinem hominis posuerunt in cognoscendo substantias separatas. Ergo Daemones privantur omni cognitione veritatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0007","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31120)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0008","text":"Praeterea, id quod est manifestissimum in natura, videtur esse maxime manifestum Angelis, sive bonis sive malis. Quod enim non sit nobis maxime manifestum, contingit ex debilitate intellectus nostri a phantasmatibus accipientis, sicut ex debilitate oculi noctuae contingit quod non possit videre lumen solis. Sed Daemones non possunt cognoscere Deum, qui est secundum se manifestissimus, cum sit in summo veritatis, eo quod non habent mundum cor, quo solo videtur Deus. Ergo nec alia cognoscere possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0009","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31121)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0010","text":"Praeterea, cognitio rerum Angelis conveniens est duplex, secundum Augustinum, scilicet matutina, et vespertina. Sed cognitio matutina non competit Daemonibus, quia non vident res in verbo, nec etiam cognitio vespertina, quia cognitio vespertina refert res cognitas ad laudem creatoris (unde post vespere fit mane, ut dicitur Gen. I). Ergo Daemones non possunt cognitionem de rebus habere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0011","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31122)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0012","text":"Praeterea, Angeli in sua conditione cognoverunt mysterium regni Dei, ut Augustinus dicit, V super Gen. ad Litt. Sed Daemones hac cognitione privati sunt, quia *si cognovissent, nequaquam dominum gloriae crucifixissent*, ut dicitur I Cor. II. Ergo, pari ratione, omni alia cognitione veritatis sunt privati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0013","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31123)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0014","text":"Praeterea, quamcumque veritatem aliquis scit, aut cognoscit eam naturaliter, sicut nos cognoscimus prima principia; aut accipiendo ab alio, sicut quae scimus addiscendo; aut per experientiam longi temporis, sicut quae scimus inveniendo. Sed Daemones non possunt cognoscere veritatem per suam naturam, quia ab eis divisi sunt boni Angeli sicut lux a tenebris, ut Augustinus dicit; omnis autem manifestatio fit per lumen, ut dicitur Ephes. V. Similiter etiam neque per revelationem, neque addiscendo a bonis Angelis, quia non est conventio lucis ad tenebras, ut dicitur II Cor. VI. Neque etiam per experientiam longi temporis, quia experientia a sensu oritur. Ergo nulla in eis est cognitio veritatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0015","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31124)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0016","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *data Daemonibus angelica dona nequaquam mutata esse dicimus, sed sunt integra et splendidissima*. Inter ista autem naturalia dona est cognitio veritatis. Ergo in eis est aliqua veritatis cognitio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0017","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31125)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0018","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod duplex est cognitio veritatis, una quidem quae habetur per gratiam; alia vero quae habetur per naturam. Et ista quae habetur per gratiam, est duplex, una quae est speculativa tantum, sicut cum alicui aliqua secreta divinorum revelantur; alia vero quae est affectiva, producens amorem Dei; et haec proprie pertinet ad donum sapientiae. Harum autem trium cognitionum prima in Daemonibus nec est ablata, nec diminuta. Consequitur enim ipsam naturam Angeli, qui secundum suam naturam est quidam intellectus vel mens, propter simplicitatem autem suae substantiae, a natura eius aliquid subtrahi non potest, ut sic per subtractionem naturalium puniatur, sicut homo punitur per subtractionem manus aut pedis aut alicuius huiusmodi. Et ideo dicit Dionysius quod dona naturalia in eis integra manent. Unde naturalis cognitio in eis non est diminuta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0019","text":"Secunda autem cognitio, quae est per gratiam, in speculatione consistens, non est in eis totaliter ablata, sed diminuta, quia de huiusmodi secretis divinis tantum revelatur eis quantum oportet, vel mediantibus Angelis, vel per aliqua temporalia divinae virtutis effecta, ut dicit Augustinus, IX de Civ. Dei; non autem sicut ipsis sanctis Angelis, quibus plura et clarius revelantur in ipso verbo. A tertia vero cognitione sunt totaliter privati, sicut et a caritate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0020","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31126)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0021","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod felicitas consistit in applicatione ad id quod superius est. Substantiae autem separatae sunt ordine naturae supra nos, unde aliqualis ratio felicitatis esse potest homini si cognoscat substantias separatas; licet perfecta eius felicitas sit in cognoscendo primam substantiam, scilicet Deum. Sed substantiae separatae cognoscere substantiam separatam est connaturale, sicut et nobis cognoscere naturas sensibiles. Unde sicut in hoc non est felicitas hominis, quod cognoscat naturas sensibiles; ita non est felicitas Angeli in hoc, quod cognoscat substantias separatas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0022","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31127)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0023","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod illud quod est manifestissimum in natura, est nobis occultum propter hoc quod excedit proportionem intellectus nostri; et non solum propter hoc quod intellectus noster accipit a phantasmatibus. Excedit autem divina substantia non solum proportionem intellectus humani, sed etiam intellectus angelici. Unde nec ipse Angelus secundum suam naturam, potest cognoscere Dei substantiam. Potest tamen altiorem cognitionem de Deo habere per suam naturam quam homo, propter perfectionem sui intellectus. Et talis cognitio Dei remanet etiam in Daemonibus. Licet enim non habeant puritatem quae est per gratiam, habent tamen puritatem naturae, quae sufficit ad cognitionem Dei quae eis competit ex natura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0024","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31128)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0025","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod creatura tenebra est, comparata excellentiae divini luminis, et ideo cognitio creaturae in propria natura, vespertina dicitur. Vespere enim est tenebris adiunctum, habet tamen aliquid de luce, cum autem totaliter deficit lux, est nox. Sic igitur et cognitio rerum in propria natura, quando refertur ad laudem creatoris, ut in bonis Angelis, habet aliquid de luce divina, et potest dici vespertina, si autem non referatur in Deum, sicut in Daemonibus, non dicitur vespertina, sed nocturna. Unde et in Genesi I, legitur quod tenebras quas Deus a luce separavit, vocavit noctem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0026","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31129)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0027","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod mysterium regni Dei, quod est impletum per Christum, omnes quidem Angeli a principio aliquo modo cognoverunt; maxime ex quo beatificati sunt visione verbi, quam Daemones nunquam habuerunt. Non tamen omnes Angeli cognoverunt perfecte, neque aequaliter. Unde Daemones multo minus, Christo existente in mundo, perfecte mysterium incarnationis cognoverunt. Non enim innotuit eis, ut Augustinus dicit sicut Angelis sanctis, qui verbi participata aeternitate perfruuntur, sed sicut eis terrendis innotescendum fuit per quaedam temporalia effecta. Si autem perfecte et per certitudinem cognovissent ipsum esse filium Dei, et effectum passionis eius, nunquam dominum gloriae crucifigi procurassent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0028","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31130)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0029","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod Daemones tribus modis cognoscunt veritatem aliquam. Uno modo, subtilitate suae naturae, quia licet sint obtenebrati per privationem luminis gratiae, sunt tamen lucidi lumine intellectualis naturae. Secundo, per revelationem a sanctis Angelis; cum quibus non conveniunt quidem per conformitatem voluntatis; conveniunt autem similitudine intellectualis naturae, secundum quam possunt accipere quod ab aliis manifestatur. Tertio modo cognoscunt per experientiam longi temporis; non quasi a sensu accipientes; sed dum in rebus singularibus completur similitudo eius speciei intelligibilis quam sibi naturaliter habent inditam, aliqua cognoscunt praesentia, quae non praecognoverunt futura, ut supra de cognitione Angelorum dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0030","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0031","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31131)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0032","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntas Daemonum non sit obstinata in malo. Libertas enim arbitrii ad naturam intellectualis naturae pertinet, quae manet in Daemonibus, ut dictum est. Sed libertas arbitrii per se et prius ordinatur ad bonum quam ad malum. Ergo voluntas Daemonis non est ita obstinata in malo, quin possit redire ad bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0033","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31132)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0034","text":"Praeterea, maior est misericordia Dei, quae est infinita, quam Daemonis malitia, quae est finita. A malitia autem culpae ad bonitatem iustitiae nullus redit nisi per Dei misericordiam. Ergo etiam Daemones a statu malitiae possunt redire ad statum iustitiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0035","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31133)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0036","text":"Praeterea, si Daemones habent voluntatem obstinatam in malo, maxime haberent eam obstinatam in peccato quo peccaverunt. Sed illud peccatum in eis nunc non manet, scilicet superbia quia nec motivum manet, scilicet excellentia. Ergo Daemon non est obstinatus in malitia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0037","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31134)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0038","text":"Praeterea, Gregorius dicit quod homo per alium reparari potuit, quia per alium cecidit. Sed Daemones inferiores per primum ceciderunt, ut supra dictum est. Ergo eorum casus per alium reparari potest. Ergo non sunt in malitia obstinati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0039","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31135)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0040","text":"Praeterea, quicumque est in malitia obstinatus, nunquam aliquod bonum opus operatur. Sed Daemon aliqua bona opera facit, confitetur enim veritatem, dicens Christo, scio quia sis sanctus Dei, Marc. I, Daemones etiam credunt et contremiscunt, ut dicitur Iacob. II; Dionysius etiam dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *bonum et optimum concupiscunt, esse, vivere et intelligere*. Ergo non sunt obstinati in malitia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0041","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31136)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0042","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo LXXIII, *superbia eorum qui te oderunt, ascendit semper*; quod de Daemonibus exponitur. Ergo semper obstinati in malitia perseverant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0043","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31137)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0044","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Origenis positio fuit quod omnis voluntas creaturae, propter libertatem arbitrii, potest flecti et in bonum et in malum, excepta anima Christi propter unionem verbi. Sed haec positio tollit veritatem beatitudinis a sanctis Angelis et hominibus, quia stabilitas sempiterna est de ratione verae beatitudinis; unde et vita aeterna nominatur. Repugnat etiam auctoritati Scripturae sacrae, quae Daemones et homines malos in supplicium aeternum mittendos, bonos autem in vitam aeternam transferendos pronuntiat, Matth. XXV. Unde haec positio est tanquam erronea reputanda; et tenendum est firmiter, secundum fidem Catholicam, quod et voluntas bonorum Angelorum confirmata est in bono, et voluntas Daemonum obstinata est in malo. Causam autem huius obstinationis oportet accipere, non ex gravitate culpae, sed ex conditione naturae status.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0045","text":"*Hoc enim est hominibus mors, quod Angelis casus*, ut Damascenus dicit. Manifestum est autem quod omnia mortalia peccata hominum, sive sint magna sive sint parva, ante mortem sunt remissibilia; post mortem vero, irremissibilia, et perpetuo manentia. Ad inquirendum ergo causam huiusmodi obstinationis, considerandum est quod vis appetitiva in omnibus proportionatur apprehensivae a qua movetur, sicut mobile motori. Appetitus enim sensitivus est boni particularis, voluntas vero universalis, ut supra dictum est; sicut etiam sensus apprehensivus est singularium, intellectus vero universalium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0046","text":"Differt autem apprehensio Angeli ab apprehensione hominis in hoc, quod Angelus apprehendit immobiliter per intellectum, sicut et nos immobiliter apprehendimus prima principia, quorum est intellectus, homo vero per rationem apprehendit mobiliter, discurrendo de uno ad aliud, habens viam procedendi ad utrumque oppositorum. Unde et voluntas hominis adhaeret alicui mobiliter, quasi potens etiam ab eo discedere et contrario adhaerere, voluntas autem Angeli adhaeret fixe et immobiliter. Et ideo, si consideretur ante adhaesionem, potest libere adhaerere et huic et opposito (in his scilicet quae non naturaliter vult), sed postquam iam adhaesit, immobiliter adhaeret. Et ideo consuevit dici quod liberum arbitrium hominis flexibile est ad oppositum et ante electionem, et post; liberum autem arbitrium Angeli est flexibile ad utrumque oppositum ante electionem, sed non post.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0047","text":"Sic igitur et boni Angeli, semper adhaerentes iustitiae, sunt in illa confirmati, mali vero, peccantes, sunt in peccato obstinati. De obstinatione vero hominum damnatorum infra dicetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0048","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31138)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0049","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod boni et mali Angeli habent liberum arbitrium, sed secundum modum et conditionem suae naturae, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0050","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31139)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0051","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod misericordia Dei liberat a peccato poenitentes. Illi vero qui poenitentiae capaces non sunt, immobiliter malo adhaerentes per divinam misericordiam non liberantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0052","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31140)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0053","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod adhuc manet in Diabolo peccatum quo primo peccavit, quantum ad appetitum; licet non quantum ad hoc quod credat se posse obtinere. Sicut si aliquis credat se posse facere homicidium, et velit facere, et postea adimatur ei potestas; nihilominus voluntas homicidii in eo manere potest, ut velit fecisse, vel velit facere si posset.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0054","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31141)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0055","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod non est tota causa quare peccatum hominis sit remissibile, quia alio suggerente peccavit. Et ideo ratio non sequitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0056","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31142)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0057","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod actus Daemonis est duplex. Quidam scilicet ex voluntate deliberata procedens, et hic proprie potest dici actus eius. Et talis actus Daemonis semper est malus, quia etsi aliquando aliquod bonum faciat, non tamen bene facit; sicut dum veritatem dicit ut decipiat, et dum non voluntarie credit et confitetur, sed rerum evidentia coactus. Alius autem actus Daemonis est naturalis; qui bonus esse potest, et attestatur bonitati naturae. Et tamen etiam tali bono actu abutuntur ad malum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0058","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0059","text":"[I q. 64 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31143)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0060","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod dolor non sit in Daemonibus. Cum enim dolor et gaudium opponantur, non possunt esse simul in eodem. Sed in Daemonibus est gaudium, dicit enim Augustinus, contra Manichaeos, *Diabolus potestatem habet in eos qui Dei praecepta contemnunt, et de hac tam infelici potestate laetatur*. Ergo in Daemonibus non est dolor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0061","text":"[I q. 64 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31144)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0062","text":"Praeterea, dolor est causa timoris, de his enim timemus dum futura sunt, de quibus dolemus dum praesentia sunt. Sed in Daemonibus non est timor; secundum illud Iob XLI, *factus est ut nullum timeret*. Ergo in Daemonibus non est dolor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0063","text":"[I q. 64 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31145)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0064","text":"Praeterea, dolere de malo est bonum. Sed Daemones non possunt bene facere. Ergo non possunt dolere, ad minus de malo culpae; quod pertinet ad vermem conscientiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0065","text":"[I q. 64 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31146)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0066","text":"Sed contra est quod peccatum Daemonis est gravius quam peccatum hominis. Sed homo punitur dolore pro delectatione peccati; secundum illud Apoc. XVIII, *quantum glorificavit se et in deliciis fuit, tantum date ei tormentum et luctum*. Ergo multo magis Diabolus, qui maximo se glorificavit, punitur doloris luctu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0067","text":"[I q. 64 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31147)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0068","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod timor, dolor, gaudium, et huiusmodi, secundum quod sunt passiones, in Daemonibus esse non possunt, sic enim sunt propriae appetitus sensitivi, qui est virtus in organo corporali. Sed secundum quod nominant simplices actus voluntatis, sic possunt esse in Daemonibus. Et necesse est dicere quod in eis sit dolor. Quia dolor, secundum quod significat simplicem actum voluntatis, nihil est aliud quam renisus voluntatis ad id quod est vel non est. Patet autem quod Daemones multa vellent non esse quae sunt, et esse quae non sunt, vellent enim, cum sint invidi, damnari eos qui salvantur. Unde oportet dicere quod in eis sit dolor, et praecipue quia de ratione poenae est, quod voluntati repugnet. Privantur etiam beatitudine quam naturaliter appetunt; et in multis eorum iniqua voluntas cohibetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0069","text":"[I q. 64 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31148)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0070","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod gaudium et dolor de eodem sunt opposita, non autem de diversis. Unde nihil prohibet unum simul dolere de uno, et gaudere de alio; et maxime secundum quod dolor et gaudium important simplices voluntatis actus; quia non solum in diversis, sed etiam in una et eadem re potest esse aliquid quod volumus, et aliquid quod nolumus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0071","text":"[I q. 64 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31149)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0072","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod sicut in Daemonibus est dolor de praesenti, ita et timor de futuro. Quod autem dicitur, factus est ut nullum timeret, intelligitur de timore Dei cohibente a peccato. Alibi, namque scriptum est quod Daemones credunt et contremiscunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0073","text":"[I q. 64 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31150)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0074","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod dolere de malo culpae propter se attestatur voluntatis bonitati, cui malum culpae opponitur. Dolere autem de malo poenae, vel de malo culpae propter poenam, attestatur bonitati naturae, cui malum poenae opponitur. Unde Augustinus dicit, XIX de Civ. Dei, quod *dolor amissi boni in supplicio, testis est naturae bonae*. Daemon ergo, cum perversae sit voluntatis et obstinatae, de malo culpae non dolet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0075","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0076","text":"[I q. 64 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31151)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0077","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aer iste non sit locus poenalis Daemonum. Daemon enim est natura spiritualis. Natura autem spiritualis non afficitur loco. Ergo nullus locus est Daemonibus poenalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0078","text":"[I q. 64 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31152)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0079","text":"Praeterea, peccatum hominis non est gravius quam peccatum Daemonis. Sed locus poenalis hominis est Infernus. Ergo multo magis Daemonis. Ergo non aer caliginosus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0080","text":"[I q. 64 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31153)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0081","text":"Praeterea, Daemones puniuntur poena ignis. Sed in aere caliginoso non est ignis. Ergo aer caliginosus non est locus poenae Daemonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0082","text":"[I q. 64 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31154)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0083","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, III super Gen. ad Litt., quod *aer caliginosus est quasi carcer Daemonibus usque ad tempus iudicii*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0084","text":"[I q. 64 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31155)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0085","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Angeli, secundum suam naturam, medii sunt inter Deum et homines. Habet autem hoc divinae providentiae ratio, quod inferiorum bonum per superiora procuret. Bonum autem hominis dupliciter procuratur per divinam providentiam. Uno modo directe, dum scilicet aliquis inducitur ad bonum et retrahitur a malo, et hoc decenter fit per Angelos bonos. Alio modo indirecte, dum scilicet aliquis exercetur, impugnatus, per impugnationem contrarii. Et hanc procurationem boni humani conveniens fuit per malos Angelos fieri, ne totaliter post peccatum ab utilitate naturalis ordinis exciderent. Sic ergo Daemonibus duplex locus poenalis debetur. Unus quidem ratione suae culpae, et hic est Infernus. Alius autem ratione exercitationis humanae, et sic debetur eis caliginosus aer.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0086","text":"Procuratio autem salutis humanae protenditur usque ad diem iudicii, unde et usque tunc durat ministerium Angelorum et exercitatio Daemonum. Unde et usque tunc et boni Angeli ad nos huc mittuntur, et Daemones in hoc aere caliginoso sunt ad nostrum exercitium, licet eorum aliqui etiam nunc in Inferno sint, ad torquendum eos quos ad malum induxerunt; sicut et aliquis boni Angeli sunt cum animabus sanctis in caelo. Sed post diem iudicii omnes mali, tam homines quam Angeli, in Inferno erunt; boni vero in caelo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0087","text":"[I q. 64 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31156)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0088","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod locus non est poenalis Angelo aut animae, quasi afficiens alterando naturam; sed quasi afficiens voluntatem contristando, dum Angelus vel anima apprehendit se esse in loco non convenienti suae voluntati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0089","text":"[I q. 64 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31157)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0090","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod anima secundum ordinem naturae non praefertur alteri animae, sicut Daemones ordine naturae praeferuntur hominibus. Unde non est similis ratio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0091","text":"[I q. 64 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31158)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0092","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod aliqui dixerunt usque ad diem iudicii differri poenam sensibilem tam Daemonum quam animarum, et similiter beatitudinem sanctorum differri usque ad diem iudicii; quod est erroneum, et repugnans apostoli sententiae, qui dicit, II Cor. V, *si terrestris domus nostra huius habitationis dissolvatur, domum habemus in caelo*. Alii vero, licet hoc non concedant de animabus, concedunt tamen de Daemonibus. Sed melius est dicendum quod idem iudicium sit de malis animabus et malis Angelis; sicut idem iudicium est de bonis animabus et bonis Angelis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0093","text":"Unde dicendum est quod, sicut locus caelestis pertinet ad gloriam Angelorum, tamen gloria eorum non minuitur cum ad nos veniunt, quia considerant illum locum esse suum (eo modo quo dicimus honorem episcopi non minui dum actu non sedet in cathedra); similiter dicendum est quod Daemones licet non actu alligentur gehennali igni, dum sunt in aere isto caliginoso, tamen ex hoc ipso quod sciunt illam alligationem sibi deberi, eorum poena non diminuitur. Unde dicitur in quadam Glossa Iacobi III, quod *portant secum ignem Gehennae quocumque vadant*. Nec est contra hoc, quod rogaverunt dominum ut non mitteret eos in abyssum, ut dicitur Lucae VIII, quia hoc petierunt reputantes sibi poenam, si excluderentur a loco in quo possunt hominibus nocere. Unde Marci V, dicitur quod *deprecabantur eum ne expelleret eos extra regionem*.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"43a2485b2496a4078cf00f08e5fc43bb19cadb48f43e4b5b7ca36a33a2d6966b","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T145119598080Z_6b41b59f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 64","source_sha256":"43a2485b2496a4078cf00f08e5fc43bb19cadb48f43e4b5b7ca36a33a2d6966b","sha256":"b3f283331a2dd989229bf81984b9284a6808a97f5fc1c355ce7926fa5164004a","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:51:19.598119+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0001","text":"## Proemium","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0002","text":"[I q. 64 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31118)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0003","text":"Consequently, there is inquiry concerning the punishment of the demons. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, concerning the darkening of the intellect. Second, concerning the obstinacy of the will. Third, concerning their pain. Fourth, concerning their place of punishment.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0005","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31119)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the demon's intellect is darkened by the privation of the knowledge of every truth. For if they knew any truth, they would know themselves most of all, which is to know separated substances. But this does not befit their misery, since it seems to pertain to great beatitude, so much so that some have placed man's ultimate beatitude in knowing separated substances. Therefore, the demons are deprived of all knowledge of truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0007","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31120)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0008","text":"Further, that which is most manifest in nature seems to be most manifest to angels, whether good or evil. For that it is not most manifest to us happens from the weakness of our intellect, which receives from phantasms, just as from the weakness of an owl's eye it happens that it cannot see the light of the sun. But demons cannot know God, who is in Himself most manifest, since He is at the summit of truth, because they do not have a pure heart, by which alone God is seen. Therefore neither can they know other things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0009","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31121)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0010","text":"Further, according to Augustine, the knowledge befitting angels is twofold, namely, morning and evening knowledge. But morning knowledge does not belong to demons, because they do not see things in the Word; nor does evening knowledge, because evening knowledge refers things known to the praise of the Creator (whence, after evening, morning comes, as is said in Gen. I). Therefore demons cannot have knowledge of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0011","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31122)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0012","text":"Further, in their creation the angels knew the mystery of the kingdom of God, as Augustine says, V On the Literal Meaning of Genesis. But demons are deprived of this knowledge, because *if they had known, they would never have crucified the Lord of glory*, as is said in I Cor. II. Therefore, by equal reasoning, they are deprived of every other knowledge of truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0013","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31123)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0014","text":"Further, whatever truth anyone knows, he either knows it naturally, as we know first principles; or by receiving it from another, as those things that we know by learning; or through the experience of a long time, as those things that we know by discovery. But demons cannot know truth through their nature, because good angels are divided from them as light from darkness, as Augustine says; but every manifestation is made through light, as is said in Ephes. V. Likewise, neither can they know by revelation, nor by learning from good angels, because there is no fellowship of light with darkness, as is said in II Cor. VI. Nor again through the experience of a long time, because experience arises from sense. Therefore there is no knowledge of truth in them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0015","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31124)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0016","text":"On the contrary, Dionysius says, in chapter IV of On the Divine Names, that *we by no means say that the angelic gifts bestowed upon demons have been changed, but that they are whole and most splendid*. But among these natural gifts is knowledge of truth. Therefore there is some knowledge of truth in them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0017","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31125)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0018","text":"I answer that it must be said that knowledge of truth is twofold: one kind is had through grace, while another is had through nature. And that which is had through grace is twofold: one which is merely speculative, as when certain secrets of divine things are revealed to someone; and another which is affective, producing love of God; and this properly pertains to the gift of wisdom. Of these three kinds of knowledge, the first has neither been taken away from demons nor diminished. For it follows upon the very nature of an angel, who according to his nature is a certain intellect or mind; but, because of the simplicity of his substance, nothing can be taken away from his nature, so that he might thus be punished by the removal of natural things, as man is punished by the removal of a hand or foot or something of this kind. And therefore Dionysius says that their natural gifts remain whole. Hence their natural knowledge is not diminished.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0019","text":"But the second knowledge, which consists in speculation through grace, has not been totally taken away from them, but diminished; because of such divine secrets only so much is revealed to them as is fitting, either through the mediation of angels or through certain temporal effects of divine power, as Augustine says in IX of The City of God; but not as to the holy angels themselves, to whom more things are revealed, and more clearly, in the Word itself. But they are totally deprived of the third knowledge, just as they are of charity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0020","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31126)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0021","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that happiness consists in being joined to that which is higher. But separated substances are above us in the order of nature; hence there can be some account of happiness for man if he knows separated substances, although his perfect happiness consists in knowing the first substance, namely God. But for separated substances to know a separated substance is connatural, just as it is connatural to us to know sensible natures. Hence, just as it is not man's happiness in this, that he knows sensible natures, so neither is it an angel's happiness in this, that he knows separated substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0022","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31127)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0023","text":"To the second, it must be said that what is most manifest in nature is hidden from us because it exceeds the proportion of our intellect, and not only because our intellect receives from phantasms. But the divine substance exceeds not only the proportion of the human intellect, but also that of the angelic intellect. Hence not even an angel, according to his nature, can know God's substance. Nevertheless, through his nature he can have a higher knowledge of God than man, because of the perfection of his intellect. And such knowledge of God remains even in demons. For although they do not have the purity that is through grace, they nevertheless have purity of nature, which suffices for the knowledge of God that befits them by nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0024","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31128)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0025","text":"To the third, it must be said that a creature is darkness, compared with the excellence of the divine light, and therefore the knowledge of a creature in its own nature is called vespertine. For evening is joined to darkness, yet has something of light; but when light wholly fails, it is night. Thus, therefore, the knowledge of things in their own nature, when it is referred to the praise of the Creator, as in the good Angels, has something of divine light, and can be called vespertine; but if it is not referred to God, as in the demons, it is not called vespertine, but nocturnal. Hence also in Genesis 1, it is read that God called the darkness which He separated from the light, night.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0026","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31129)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0027","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the mystery of the kingdom of God, which has been fulfilled through Christ, all the Angels indeed knew in some way from the beginning; especially from the time when they were beatified by the vision of the Word, which the demons never had. Yet not all the Angels knew it perfectly, nor equally. Hence the demons, much less, while Christ was in the world, knew the mystery of the Incarnation perfectly. For it was not made known to them, as Augustine says, as to the holy Angels, who enjoy the participated eternity of the Word; but as it was to be made known to those to be terrified through certain temporal effects. But if they had known perfectly and with certainty that He was the Son of God, and the effect of His Passion, they would never have procured that the Lord of glory be crucified.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0028","text":"[I q. 64 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31130)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0029","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that demons know some truth in three ways. In one way, by the subtlety of their nature, because although they are darkened through the privation of the light of grace, they are nevertheless luminous by the light of intellectual nature. In a second way, through revelation by the holy Angels; with whom they do not indeed agree through conformity of will, but they do agree in the likeness of intellectual nature, according to which they can receive what is manifested by others. In a third way, they know through the experience of a long time; not as though receiving from sense, but while, in singular things, the likeness of that intelligible species which they have naturally impressed upon them is fulfilled, they know certain present things which they did not foreknow as future, as was said above concerning the knowledge of Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0030","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0031","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31131)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0032","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the will of demons is not obstinate in evil. For freedom of choice belongs to the nature of an intellectual nature, which remains in demons, as has been said. But freedom of choice is of itself and priorly ordered toward good rather than toward evil. Therefore the will of a demon is not so obstinate in evil that it cannot return to good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0033","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31132)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0034","text":"Further, the mercy of God, which is infinite, is greater than the malice of a demon, which is finite. But no one returns from the malice of guilt to the goodness of justice except through God's mercy. Therefore demons also can return from a state of malice to a state of justice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0035","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31133)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0036","text":"Further, if demons have a will obstinate in evil, they would have it obstinate above all in the sin by which they sinned. But that sin does not now remain in them, namely pride, because neither does its motive remain, namely excellence. Therefore a demon is not obstinate in malice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0037","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31134)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0038","text":"Further, Gregory says that man could be restored through another, because he fell through another. But the lower demons fell through the first, as was said above. Therefore their fall can be repaired through another. Therefore they are not obstinate in malice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0039","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31135)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0040","text":"Further, whoever is obstinate in malice never performs any good work. But a demon performs certain good works: for he confesses the truth, saying to Christ, “I know that you are the Holy One of God,” Mark 1; demons also believe and tremble, as is said in James 2; Dionysius also says, in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, that *they desire the good and the best: to be, to live, and to understand*. Therefore they are not obstinate in malice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0041","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31136)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0042","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Psalm 73, *the pride of those who hate you has ascended continually*; which is expounded of demons. Therefore they always persevere obstinate in malice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0043","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31137)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0044","text":"I answer that it must be said that Origen's position was that every will of a creature, on account of freedom of choice, can be bent both toward good and toward evil, except the soul of Christ on account of the union of the Word. But this position removes the truth of beatitude from the holy Angels and men, because everlasting stability belongs to the nature of true beatitude; hence it is also called eternal life. It also conflicts with the authority of Sacred Scripture, which pronounces that demons and wicked men are to be sent into eternal punishment, but the good are to be transferred into eternal life, Matthew 25. Hence this position is to be regarded as erroneous; and it must be held firmly, according to the Catholic faith, that both the will of the good Angels is confirmed in good and the will of demons is obstinate in evil. But the cause of this obstinacy must be taken, not from the gravity of the fault, but from the condition of the state of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0045","text":"For, as Damascene says, *death is to men what a fall is to Angels*. Now it is evident that all mortal sins of men, whether great or small, are remissible before death; but after death, they are irremissible and remain perpetually. Therefore, to investigate the cause of such obstinacy, it must be considered that the appetitive power in all things is proportioned to the apprehensive power by which it is moved, as a movable thing to its mover. For the sensitive appetite is of particular good, whereas the will is of universal good, as was said above; just as the apprehensive sense is of singulars, whereas the intellect is of universals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0046","text":"But the apprehension of an Angel differs from the apprehension of a human being in this: that an Angel apprehends immovably through intellect, just as we too apprehend immovably the first principles, which belong to intellect; whereas a human being apprehends movably through reason, proceeding discursively from one thing to another, having a way of proceeding to either of two opposites. Hence too the human will adheres to something movably, as being able also to depart from it and adhere to the contrary; but the will of an Angel adheres fixedly and immovably. And therefore, if it is considered before adherence, it can freely adhere both to this and to the opposite (namely, in those things which it does not will naturally); but after it has already adhered, it adheres immovably. And therefore it is customary to say that human free choice is flexible toward the opposite both before election and after it; but the free choice of an Angel is flexible toward either opposite before election, but not afterward.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0047","text":"Thus, therefore, the good Angels, always adhering to justice, are confirmed in it; but the evil angels, sinning, are obstinate in sin. But concerning the obstinacy of damned men, it will be said below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0048","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31138)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0049","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that the good and the evil Angels have free choice, but according to the mode and condition of their nature, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0050","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31139)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0051","text":"To the second, it must be said that the mercy of God frees the repentant from sin. But those who are not capable of repentance, adhering immovably to evil, are not freed through divine mercy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0052","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31140)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0053","text":"To the third, it must be said that the sin by which the Devil first sinned still remains in him, as regards the appetite, although not as regards his believing that he can attain it. Thus, if someone believes that he can commit homicide, and wishes to commit it, and afterwards the power is taken away from him, nevertheless the will for homicide can remain in him, so that he may wish to have done it, or may wish to do it if he could.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0054","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31141)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0055","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that it is not the whole reason why the sin of man is remissible that he sinned at another's suggestion. And therefore the argument does not follow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0056","text":"[I q. 64 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31142)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0057","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that the act of a demon is twofold. One, namely, proceeding from deliberate will, and this can properly be called his act. And such an act of a demon is always evil, because even if he sometimes does some good, he nevertheless does not do it well; as when he speaks the truth in order to deceive, and when he believes and confesses not voluntarily, but compelled by the evidentness of things. But another act of a demon is natural; and this can be good, and bears witness to the goodness of nature. And yet they also misuse such a good act for evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0058","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0059","text":"[I q. 64 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31143)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0060","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that there is no sorrow in demons. For since sorrow and joy are opposed, they cannot be together in the same subject. But there is joy in demons; for Augustine says, against the Manichaeans, *The Devil has power over those who despise God's commandments, and he rejoices in this so unhappy power.* Therefore there is no sorrow in demons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0061","text":"[I q. 64 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31144)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0062","text":"Moreover, sorrow is a cause of fear; for we fear, while they are future, those things over which we sorrow while they are present. But there is no fear in demons, according to that of Job 41, *he was made so as to fear no one.* Therefore there is no sorrow in demons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0063","text":"[I q. 64 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31145)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0064","text":"Moreover, to sorrow over evil is good. But demons cannot do good. Therefore they cannot sorrow, at least not over the evil of fault; which pertains to the worm of conscience.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0065","text":"[I q. 64 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31146)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0066","text":"But on the contrary, the sin of a demon is graver than the sin of a man. But man is punished with sorrow for the pleasure of sin, according to that of Revelation 18, *As much as she glorified herself and was in delights, so much torment and mourning give her.* Therefore much more is the Devil, who glorified himself most greatly, punished with the mourning of sorrow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0067","text":"[I q. 64 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31147)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0068","text":"I answer that it must be said that fear, sorrow, joy, and things of this sort, insofar as they are passions, cannot be in demons, for thus they are proper to the sensitive appetite, which is a power in a bodily organ. But insofar as they name simple acts of the will, they can be in demons. And it is necessary to say that there is sorrow in them. For sorrow, insofar as it signifies a simple act of the will, is nothing other than the resistance of the will to that which is or is not. Now it is clear that demons would will many things that are to not be, and things that are not to be; for, being envious, they would will those who are saved to be damned. Hence it must be said that there is sorrow in them, and especially because it belongs to the nature of punishment that it be repugnant to the will. They are also deprived of the beatitude which they naturally desire; and in many matters their wicked will is restrained.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0069","text":"[I q. 64 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31148)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0070","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that joy and sorrow concerning the same thing are opposed, but not concerning different things. Hence nothing prevents one from sorrowing at the same time over one thing and rejoicing over another; and especially insofar as sorrow and joy imply simple acts of the will, because not only in different things, but even in one and the same thing, there can be something that we will and something that we do not will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0071","text":"[I q. 64 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31149)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0072","text":"To the second, it must be said that, just as there is sorrow in demons over the present, so also there is fear over the future. But what is said, *he was made so as to fear no one*, is understood of the fear of God that restrains from sin. For elsewhere it is written that the demons believe and tremble.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0073","text":"[I q. 64 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31150)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0074","text":"To the third, it must be said that to grieve over the evil of fault for its own sake bears witness to the goodness of the will, to which the evil of fault is opposed. But to grieve over the evil of punishment, or over the evil of fault on account of punishment, bears witness to the goodness of nature, to which the evil of punishment is opposed. Hence Augustine says, in XIX *On the City of God*, that *pain at a good lost in punishment is a witness to a good nature*. Therefore the demon, since its will is perverse and obstinate, does not grieve over the evil of fault.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0075","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0076","text":"[I q. 64 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31151)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0077","text":"The fourth is approached thus. It seems that this air is not a penal place for demons. For a demon is a spiritual nature. But a spiritual nature is not affected by place. Therefore no place is penal for demons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0078","text":"[I q. 64 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31152)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0079","text":"Further, the sin of a human being is not more grievous than the sin of a demon. But the penal place of a human being is Hell. Therefore, much more so is that of a demon. Therefore it is not the darksome air.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0080","text":"[I q. 64 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31153)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0081","text":"Further, demons are punished by the punishment of fire. But there is no fire in the darksome air. Therefore the darksome air is not the place of punishment for demons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0082","text":"[I q. 64 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31154)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0083","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in III *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, that *the darksome air is as it were a prison for demons until the time of judgment*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0084","text":"[I q. 64 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31155)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0085","text":"I answer that it must be said that angels, according to their nature, are intermediate between God and human beings. Now it belongs to the ordering of divine providence to procure the good of lower things through higher things. But the good of human beings is procured by divine providence in two ways. In one way, directly, namely, when someone is led toward good and drawn back from evil; and this is fittingly done through good angels. In another way, indirectly, namely, when someone is exercised, being assailed, through the assault of an adversary. And it was fitting that this procuring of human good be done through evil angels, lest after sin they should fall away altogether from the usefulness of the natural order. Thus, therefore, a twofold penal place is due to demons. One on account of their fault, and this is Hell. But another on account of the exercise of human beings, and thus the darksome air is due to them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0086","text":"But the procuring of human salvation extends to the day of judgment; hence until then also the ministry of angels and the exercise by demons endure. Hence until then both good angels are sent hither to us, and demons are in this darksome air for our exercise, although some of them are even now in Hell, to torment those whom they led into evil; just as some good angels are with the souls of the saints in heaven. But after the day of judgment all the evil, both human beings and angels, will be in Hell; but the good will be in heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0087","text":"[I q. 64 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31156)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0088","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that a place is penal to an angel or soul not as affecting its nature by alteration, but as affecting its will by causing sorrow, while the angel or soul apprehends itself to be in a place not suited to its will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0089","text":"[I q. 64 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31157)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0090","text":"To the second, it must be said that, according to the order of nature, one soul is not preferred to another soul, as demons are preferred to human beings in the order of nature. Hence the reasoning is not similar.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0091","text":"[I q. 64 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1050.html#31158)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0092","text":"To the third, it must be said that some have said that sensible punishment, both of demons and of souls, is deferred until the day of judgment, and likewise that the beatitude of the saints is deferred until the day of judgment; which is erroneous and repugnant to the statement of the Apostle, who says, II Cor. V, *if our earthly house of this habitation be dissolved, we have a house in heaven*. Others, however, although they do not grant this concerning souls, nevertheless grant it concerning demons. But it is better to say that the judgment concerning evil souls and evil angels is the same; just as the judgment concerning good souls and good angels is the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0093","text":"Hence it must be said that, just as the heavenly place pertains to the glory of angels, yet their glory is not diminished when they come to us, because they consider that place to be theirs (in the way in which we say that a bishop's honor is not diminished while he is not actually sitting in his chair); similarly, it must be said that although demons are not actually bound by the fire of Gehenna while they are in this darksome air, nevertheless from the very fact that they know that that binding is due to them, their punishment is not diminished. Hence it is said in a certain Gloss on James III that *they carry the fire of Gehenna with them wherever they go*. Nor is it contrary to this that they begged the Lord not to send them into the abyss, as is said in Luke VIII, because they asked this, reckoning it a punishment to themselves if they were excluded from the place in which they can harm human beings. Hence in Mark V it is said that *they besought him not to drive them out of the region*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_56:P0093"]}],"source_digest":"f2faab05914d00bdf18f47ada47f32985685e435013ff2f85c01e73f0f10ee25","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 64"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 64","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57","title":"First Part · Question 65","order":66,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_57","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 65 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31159)\n\nPost considerationem spiritualis creaturae, considerandum est de creatura corporali. In cuius productione tria opera Scriptura commemorat, scilicet opus creationis, cum dicitur, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram,* etc.; opus distinctionis, cum dicitur, *divisit lucem a tenebris, et aquas quae sunt supra firmamentum, ab aquis quae sunt sub firmamento*; et opus ornatus, cum dicitur, *fiant luminaria in firmamento* et cetera. Primo ergo considerandum est de opere creationis; secundo, de opere distinctionis; tertio, de opere ornatus. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum creatura corporalis sit a Deo. Secundo, utrum sit facta propter bonitatem Dei. Tertio, utrum sit facta a Deo mediantibus Angelis. Quarto, utrum formae corporum sint ab Angelis, an immediate a Deo.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 65 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31160)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod creatura corporalis non sit a Deo. Dicitur enim Eccle. III, *didici quod omnia quae fecit Deus, perseverant in aeternum*. Sed corpora visibilia non perseverant in aeternum, dicitur enim II Cor. IV, *quae videntur temporalia sunt; quae autem non videntur, aeterna*. Ergo Deus non fecit corpora visibilia.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31161)\n\nPraeterea, Gen. I, dicitur, *vidit Deus cuncta quae fecerat, et erant valde bona*. Sed creaturae corporales sunt malae, experimur enim eas in multis noxias, ut patet in multis serpentibus, in aestu solis, et huiusmodi; ideo autem aliquid dicitur malum, quia nocet. Creaturae igitur corporales non sunt a Deo.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31162)\n\nPraeterea, id quod est a Deo, non retrahit a Deo, sed ducit in ipsum. Sed creaturae corporales retrahunt a Deo, unde apostolus dicit, II Cor. IV, *non contemplantibus nobis quae videntur*. Ergo creaturae corporales non sunt a Deo.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31163)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo CXLV, *qui fecit caelum et terram, mare, et omnia quae in eis sunt*.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31164)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quorundam haereticorum positio est, quod visibilia ista non sunt creata a bono Deo, sed a malo principio. Et ad argumentum sui erroris assumunt quod apostolus dicit II, Cor. IV, *Deus huius saeculi excaecavit mentes infidelium*. Haec autem positio est omnino impossibilis. Si enim diversa in aliquo uniantur, necesse est huius unionis causam esse aliquam, non enim diversa secundum se uniuntur. Et inde est quod, quandocumque in diversis invenitur aliquid unum, oportet quod illa diversa illud unum ab aliqua una causa recipiant; sicut diversa corpora calida habent calorem ab igne. Hoc autem quod est esse, communiter invenitur in omnibus rebus, quantumcumque diversis. Necesse est ergo esse unum essendi principium, a quo esse habeant quaecumque sunt quocumque modo, sive sint invisibilia et spiritualia, sive sint visibilia et corporalia. Dicitur autem Diabolus esse Deus huius saeculi, non creatione, sed quia saeculariter viventes ei serviunt; eo modo loquendi quo apostolus loquitur, ad Philipp. III, *quorum Deus venter est*.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31165)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod omnes creaturae Dei secundum aliquid in aeternum perseverant, ad minus secundum materiam, quia creaturae nunquam in nihilum redigentur, etiam si sint corruptibiles. Sed quanto creaturae magis appropinquant ad Deum, qui est omnino immobilis, tanto magis sunt immobiles. Nam creaturae corruptibiles in perpetuum manent secundum materiam, sed mutantur secundum formam substantialem. Creaturae vero incorruptibiles permanent quidem secundum substantiam, sed sunt mutabiles secundum alia, puta secundum locum, ut corpora caelestia; vel secundum affectiones, ut creaturae spirituales. Quod autem apostolus dicit, *quae videntur, temporalia sunt*, etsi verum sit etiam quantum ad ipsas res in se consideratas, secundum quod omnis creatura visibilis subiacet tempori, vel secundum suum esse vel secundum suum motum; tamen apostolus intendit loqui de visibilibus secundum quod sunt hominis praemia. Nam praemia hominis quae sunt in istis rebus visibilibus, temporaliter transeunt, quae autem sunt in rebus invisibilibus, permanent in aeternum. Unde et supra praemiserat, *aeternum gloriae pondus operatur in nobis*.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31166)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod creatura corporalis, secundum suam naturam, est bona, sed non est bonum universale, sed est quoddam bonum particulare et contractum, secundum quam particularitatem et contractionem sequitur in ea contrarietas, per quam unum contrariatur alteri, licet utrumque in se sit bonum. Quidam autem, aestimantes res non ex earum natura, sed ex suo proprio commodo, quaecumque sibi nociva sunt, simpliciter mala arbitrantur non considerantes quod id quod est uni nocivum quantum ad aliquid, vel alteri vel eidem quantum ad aliquid est proficuum. Quod nequaquam esset, si secundum se corpora essent mala et noxia.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31167)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod creaturae, quantum est de se, non retrahunt a Deo, sed in ipsum ducunt, quia *invisibilia Dei per ea quae facta sunt, intellecta, conspiciuntur*, ut dicitur Rom. I. Sed quod avertant a Deo, hoc est ex culpa eorum qui insipienter eis utuntur. Unde dicitur Sap. XIV, quod *creaturae factae sunt in muscipulam pedibus insipientium*. Et hoc ipsum quod sic a Deo abducunt, attestatur quod sunt a Deo. Non enim abducunt insipientes a Deo, nisi alliciendo secundum aliquid boni in eis existens, quod habent a Deo.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 65 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31168)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod creatura corporalis non sit facta propter Dei bonitatem. Dicitur enim Sap. I, *creavit Deus ut essent omnia*. Ergo omnia sunt creata propter suum proprium esse, et non propter Dei bonitatem.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31169)\n\nPraeterea, bonum habet rationem finis. Ergo id quod est magis bonum in rebus, est finis minus boni. Creatura autem spiritualis comparatur ad corporalem, sicut maius bonum ad minus bonum. Ergo creatura corporalis est propter spiritualem, et non propter Dei bonitatem.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31170)\n\nPraeterea, iustitia non dat inaequalia nisi inaequalibus. Sed Deus est iustus. Ergo ante omnem inaequalitatem a Deo creatam, est inaequalitas a Deo non creata. Sed inaequalitas a Deo non creata, non potest esse nisi quae est ex libero arbitrio. Ergo omnis inaequalitas sequitur ex diversis motibus liberi arbitrii. Creaturae autem corporales sunt inaequales spiritualibus. Ergo creaturae corporales sunt factae propter aliquos motus liberi arbitrii, et non propter Dei bonitatem.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31171)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Prov. XVI, *universa propter semetipsum operatus est dominus*.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31172)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Origenes posuit quod creatura corporalis non est facta ex prima Dei intentione, sed ad poenam creaturae spiritualis peccantis. Posuit enim quod Deus a principio creaturas spirituales solas fecit, et omnes aequales. Quarum, cum essent liberi arbitrii, quaedam conversae sunt in Deum, et secundum quantitatem conversionis sortitae sunt maiorem vel minorem gradum, in sua simplicitate remanentes. Quaedam vero, aversae a Deo, alligatae sunt corporibus diversis, secundum modum aversionis a Deo. Quae quidem positio erronea est. Primo quidem, quia contrariatur Scripturae, quae, enarrata productione cuiuslibet speciei creaturae corporalis subiungit, vidit Deus quia hoc esset bonum; quasi diceret quod unumquodque ideo factum est, quia bonum est ipsum esse. Secundum autem opinionem Origenis, creatura corporalis facta est, non quia bonum est eam esse, sed ut malum alterius puniretur. Secundo, quia sequeretur quod mundi corporalis dispositio quae nunc est, esset a casu. Si enim ideo corpus solis tale factum est, ut congrueret alicui peccato spiritualis creaturae puniendo; si plures creaturae spirituales similiter peccassent sicut illa propter cuius peccatum puniendum ponit solem creatum, sequeretur quod essent plures soles in mundo. Et idem esset de aliis. Haec autem sunt omnino inconvenientia. Unde haec positione remota tanquam erronea, considerandum est quod ex omnibus creaturis constituitur totum universum sicut totum ex partibus. Si autem alicuius totius et partium eius velimus finem assignare, inveniemus primo quidem, quod singulae partes sunt propter suos actus; sicut oculus ad videndum. Secundo vero, quod pars ignobilior est propter nobiliorem; sicut sensus propter intellectum, et pulmo propter cor. Tertio vero, omnes partes sunt propter perfectionem totius, sicut et materia propter formam, partes enim sunt quasi materia totius. Ulterius autem, totus homo est propter aliquem finem extrinsecum, puta ut fruatur Deo. Sic igitur et in partibus universi, unaquaeque creatura est propter suum proprium actum et perfectionem. Secundo autem, creaturae ignobiliores sunt propter nobiliores sicut creaturae quae sunt infra hominem, sunt propter hominem. Ulterius autem, singulae creaturae sunt propter perfectionem totius universi. Ulterius autem, totum universum, cum singulis suis partibus, ordinatur in Deum sicut in finem, inquantum in eis per quandam imitationem divina bonitas repraesentatur ad gloriam Dei, quamvis creaturae rationales speciali quodam modo supra hoc habeant finem Deum, quem attingere possunt sua operatione, cognoscendo et amando. Et sic patet quod divina bonitas est finis omnium corporalium.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31173)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in hoc ipso quod creatura aliqua habet esse, repraesentat divinum esse et bonitatem eius. Et ideo per hoc quod Deus creavit omnia ut essent, non excluditur quin creaverit omnia propter suam bonitatem.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31174)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod finis proximus non excludit finem ultimum. Unde per hoc quod creatura corporalis facta est quodammodo propter spiritualem, non removetur quin sit facta propter Dei bonitatem.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31175)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod aequalitas iustitiae locum habet in retribuendo, iustum enim est quod aequalibus aequalia retribuantur. Non autem habet locum in prima rerum institutione. Sicut enim artifex eiusdem generis lapides in diversis partibus aedificii ponit absque iniustitia, non propter aliquam diversitatem in lapidibus praecedentem, sed attendens ad perfectionem totius aedificii, quae non esset nisi lapides diversimode in aedificio collocarentur; sic et Deus a principio, ut esset perfectio in universo, diversas et inaequales creaturas instituit, secundum suam sapientiam, absque iniustitia, nulla tamen praesupposita meritorum diversitate.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 65 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31176)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod creatura corporalis sit producta a Deo mediantibus Angelis. Sicut enim res gubernantur per divinam sapientiam, ita omnia sunt per Dei sapientiam facta; secundum illud Psalmi CIII, *omnia in sapientia fecisti*. Sed ordinare est sapientis, ut dicitur in principio Metaphys. Unde in gubernatione rerum, inferiora per superiora reguntur quodam ordine, ut Augustinus dicit, III de Trin. Ergo et in rerum productione talis ordo fuit, quod creatura corporalis, tanquam inferior, per spiritualem, tanquam superiorem, est producta.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31177)\n\nPraeterea, diversitas effectuum demonstrat diversitatem causarum, quia idem semper facit idem. Si ergo omnes creaturae, tam spirituales quam corporales, sunt immediate a Deo productae, nulla esset inter creaturas diversitas, nec una magis distaret a Deo quam alia. Quod patet esse falsum, cum propter longe distare a Deo dicat philosophus quaedam corruptibilia esse.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31178)\n\nPraeterea, ad producendum effectum finitum, non requiritur virtus infinita. Sed omne corpus finitum est. Ergo per finitam virtutem spiritualis creaturae produci potuit; et productum fuit, quia in talibus non differt esse et posse; praesertim quia nulla dignitas competens alicui secundum suam naturam, ei denegatur, nisi forte ob culpam.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31179)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*, per quae creatura corporalis intelligitur. Ergo creatura corporalis est immediate a Deo producta.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31180)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam posuerunt gradatim res a Deo processisse, ita scilicet quod ab eo immediate processit prima creatura, et illa produxit aliam; et sic inde usque ad creaturam corpoream. Sed haec positio est impossibilis. Quia prima corporalis creaturae productio est per creationem per quam etiam ipsa materia producitur, imperfectum enim est prius quam perfectum in fieri impossibile est autem aliquid creari nisi a solo Deo. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod quanto aliqua causa est superior, tanto ad plura se extendit in causando. Semper autem id quod substernitur in rebus, invenitur communius quam id quod informat et restringit ipsum, sicut esse quam vivere, et vivere quam intelligere, et materia quam forma. Quanto ergo aliquid est magis substratum, tanto a superiori causa directe procedit. Id ergo quod est primo substratum in omnibus, proprie pertinet ad causalitatem supremae causae. Nulla igitur secunda causa potest aliquid producere, non praesupposito in re producta aliquo quod causatur a superiori causa. Creatio autem est productio alicuius rei secundum suam totam substantiam, nullo praesupposito quod sit vel increatum vel ab aliquo creatum. Unde relinquitur quod nihil potest aliquid creare nisi solus Deus, qui est prima causa. Et ideo ut Moyses ostenderet corpora omnia immediate a Deo creata, dixit, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31181)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in productione rerum est aliquis ordo, non quidem ut una creatura creetur ab alia (hoc enim impossibile est); sed ita quod ex divina sapientia diversi gradus in creaturis, constituuntur.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31182)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ipse Deus unus, absque suae simplicitatis detrimento, diversorum cognoscitivus est, ut supra ostensum est. Et ideo etiam est, secundum diversa cognita, diversorum productorum causa per suam sapientiam, sicut et artifex, apprehendendo diversas formas, producit diversa artificiata.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31183)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod quantitas virtutis agentis non solum mensuratur secundum rem factam, sed etiam secundum modum faciendi, quia unum et idem aliter fit et a maiori, et a minori virtute. Producere autem aliquid finitum hoc modo ut nihil praesupponatur, est virtutis infinitae. Unde nulli creaturae competere potest.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 65 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31184)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod formae corporum sint ab Angelis. Dicit enim Boetius, in libro de Trin., quod *a formis quae sunt sine materia, veniunt formae quae sunt in materia*. Formae autem quae sunt sine materia, sunt substantiae spirituales, formae autem quae sunt in materia, sunt formae corporum. Ergo formae corporum sunt a spiritualibus substantiis.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31185)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod est per participationem, reducitur ad id quod est per essentiam. Sed spirituales substantiae per suam essentiam sunt formae, creaturae autem corporales participant formas. Ergo formae corporalium rerum sunt a spiritualibus substantiis derivatae.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31186)\n\nPraeterea, spirituales substantiae magis habent virtutem causandi quam corpora caelestia. Sed corpora caelestia causant formas in istis inferioribus, unde dicuntur esse generationis et corruptionis causa. Ergo multo magis a spiritualibus substantiis formae quae sunt in materia, derivantur.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31187)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, III de Trin., quod *non est putandum Angelis ad nutum servire hanc corporalem materiam, sed potius Deo*. Illi autem ad nutum dicitur servire corporalis materia, a quo speciem recipit. Non ergo formae corporales sunt ab Angelis, sed a Deo.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31188)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod opinio fuit quorundam quod omnes formae corporales deriventur a substantiis spiritualibus quas Angelos dicimus. Et hoc quidem dupliciter aliqui posuerunt. Plato enim posuit formas quae sunt in materia corporali, derivari et formari a formis sine materia subsistentibus, per modum participationis cuiusdam. Ponebat enim hominem quendam immaterialiter subsistentem, et similiter equum, et sic de aliis, ex quibus constituuntur haec singularia sensibilia, secundum quod in materia corporali remanet quaedam impressio ab illis formis separatis, per modum assimilationis cuiusdam, quam participationem vocabat. Et secundum ordinem formarum ponebant Platonici ordinem substantiarum separatarum, puta quod una substantia separata est quae est equus, quae est causa omnium equorum; supra quam est quaedam vita separata, quam dicebant per se vitam et causam omnis vitae; et ulterius quandam quam nominabant ipsum esse, et causam omnis esse. Avicenna vero et quidam alii non posuerunt formas rerum corporalium in materia per se subsistere, sed solum in intellectu. A formis ergo in intellectu creaturarum spiritualium existentibus (quas quidem ipsi intelligentias, nos autem Angelos dicimus), dicebant procedere omnes formas quae sunt in materia corporali, sicut a formis quae sunt in mente artificis, procedunt formae artificiatorum. Et in idem videtur redire quod quidam moderni haeretici ponunt, dicentes quidem Deum creatorem omnium, sed materiam corporalem a Diabolo formatam et per varias species distinctam. Omnes autem hae opiniones ex una radice processisse videntur. Quaerebant enim causam formarum, ac si ipsae formae fierent secundum seipsas. Sed sicut probat Aristoteles in VII Metaphys., id quod proprie fit, est compositum, formae autem corruptibilium rerum habent ut aliquando sint, aliquando non sint, absque hoc quod ipsae generentur aut corrumpantur, sed compositis generatis aut corruptis, quia etiam formae non habent esse, sed composita habent esse per eas, sic enim alicui competit fieri, sicut et esse. Et ideo, cum simile fiat a suo simili, non est quaerenda causa formarum corporalium aliqua forma immaterialis; sed aliquod compositum, secundum quod hic ignis generatur ab hoc igne. Sic igitur formae corporales causantur, non quasi influxae ab aliqua immateriali forma, sed quasi materia reducta de potentia in actum ab aliquo agente composito. Sed quia agens compositum, quod est corpus, movetur a substantia spirituali creata, ut Augustinus dicit III de Trin.; sequitur ulterius quod etiam formae corporales a substantiis spiritualibus deriventur, non tanquam influentibus formas, sed tanquam moventibus ad formas. Ulterius autem reducuntur in Deum, sicut in primam causam, etiam species angelici intellectus, quae sunt quaedam seminales rationes corporalium formarum. In prima autem corporalis creaturae productione non consideratur aliqua transmutatio de potentia in actum. Et ideo formae corporales quas in prima productione corpora habuerunt, sunt immediate a Deo productae, cui soli ad nutum obedit materia, tanquam propriae causae. Unde ad hoc significandum, Moyses singulis operibus praemittit, dixit Deus fiat hoc vel illud; in quo significatur formatio rerum per verbum Dei facta, a quo, secundum Augustinum, *est omnis forma et compago et concordia partium*.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31189)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Boetius intelligit per formas quae sunt sine materia, rationes rerum quae sunt in mente divina, sicut etiam apostolus dicit, Heb. XI, *fide credimus aptata esse saecula verbo Dei, ut ex invisibilibus visibilia fierent*. Si tamen per formas quae sunt sine materia, intelligit Angelos, dicendum est quod ab eis veniunt formae quae sunt in materia, non per influxum, sed per motum.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31190)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod formae participatae in materia reducuntur, non ad formas aliquas per se subsistentes rationis eiusdem, ut Platonici posuerunt; sed ad formas intelligibiles vel intellectus angelici, a quibus per motum procedunt; vel ulterius ad rationes intellectus divini, a quibus etiam formarum semina sunt rebus creatis indita, ut per motum in actum educi possint.\n\n[I q. 65 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31191)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod corpora caelestia causant formas in istis inferioribus, non influendo, sed movendo.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0002","text":"[I q. 65 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31159)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0003","text":"Post considerationem spiritualis creaturae, considerandum est de creatura corporali. In cuius productione tria opera Scriptura commemorat, scilicet opus creationis, cum dicitur, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram,* etc.; opus distinctionis, cum dicitur, *divisit lucem a tenebris, et aquas quae sunt supra firmamentum, ab aquis quae sunt sub firmamento*; et opus ornatus, cum dicitur, *fiant luminaria in firmamento* et cetera. Primo ergo considerandum est de opere creationis; secundo, de opere distinctionis; tertio, de opere ornatus. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum creatura corporalis sit a Deo. Secundo, utrum sit facta propter bonitatem Dei. Tertio, utrum sit facta a Deo mediantibus Angelis. Quarto, utrum formae corporum sint ab Angelis, an immediate a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0005","text":"[I q. 65 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31160)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod creatura corporalis non sit a Deo. Dicitur enim Eccle. III, *didici quod omnia quae fecit Deus, perseverant in aeternum*. Sed corpora visibilia non perseverant in aeternum, dicitur enim II Cor. IV, *quae videntur temporalia sunt; quae autem non videntur, aeterna*. Ergo Deus non fecit corpora visibilia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0007","text":"[I q. 65 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31161)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0008","text":"Praeterea, Gen. I, dicitur, *vidit Deus cuncta quae fecerat, et erant valde bona*. Sed creaturae corporales sunt malae, experimur enim eas in multis noxias, ut patet in multis serpentibus, in aestu solis, et huiusmodi; ideo autem aliquid dicitur malum, quia nocet. Creaturae igitur corporales non sunt a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0009","text":"[I q. 65 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31162)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0010","text":"Praeterea, id quod est a Deo, non retrahit a Deo, sed ducit in ipsum. Sed creaturae corporales retrahunt a Deo, unde apostolus dicit, II Cor. IV, *non contemplantibus nobis quae videntur*. Ergo creaturae corporales non sunt a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0011","text":"[I q. 65 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31163)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo CXLV, *qui fecit caelum et terram, mare, et omnia quae in eis sunt*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0013","text":"[I q. 65 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31164)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quorundam haereticorum positio est, quod visibilia ista non sunt creata a bono Deo, sed a malo principio. Et ad argumentum sui erroris assumunt quod apostolus dicit II, Cor. IV, *Deus huius saeculi excaecavit mentes infidelium*. Haec autem positio est omnino impossibilis. Si enim diversa in aliquo uniantur, necesse est huius unionis causam esse aliquam, non enim diversa secundum se uniuntur. Et inde est quod, quandocumque in diversis invenitur aliquid unum, oportet quod illa diversa illud unum ab aliqua una causa recipiant; sicut diversa corpora calida habent calorem ab igne. Hoc autem quod est esse, communiter invenitur in omnibus rebus, quantumcumque diversis. Necesse est ergo esse unum essendi principium, a quo esse habeant quaecumque sunt quocumque modo, sive sint invisibilia et spiritualia, sive sint visibilia et corporalia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0015","text":"Dicitur autem Diabolus esse Deus huius saeculi, non creatione, sed quia saeculariter viventes ei serviunt; eo modo loquendi quo apostolus loquitur, ad Philipp. III, *quorum Deus venter est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0016","text":"[I q. 65 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31165)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod omnes creaturae Dei secundum aliquid in aeternum perseverant, ad minus secundum materiam, quia creaturae nunquam in nihilum redigentur, etiam si sint corruptibiles. Sed quanto creaturae magis appropinquant ad Deum, qui est omnino immobilis, tanto magis sunt immobiles. Nam creaturae corruptibiles in perpetuum manent secundum materiam, sed mutantur secundum formam substantialem. Creaturae vero incorruptibiles permanent quidem secundum substantiam, sed sunt mutabiles secundum alia, puta secundum locum, ut corpora caelestia; vel secundum affectiones, ut creaturae spirituales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0018","text":"Quod autem apostolus dicit, *quae videntur, temporalia sunt*, etsi verum sit etiam quantum ad ipsas res in se consideratas, secundum quod omnis creatura visibilis subiacet tempori, vel secundum suum esse vel secundum suum motum; tamen apostolus intendit loqui de visibilibus secundum quod sunt hominis praemia. Nam praemia hominis quae sunt in istis rebus visibilibus, temporaliter transeunt, quae autem sunt in rebus invisibilibus, permanent in aeternum. Unde et supra praemiserat, *aeternum gloriae pondus operatur in nobis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0019","text":"[I q. 65 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31166)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod creatura corporalis, secundum suam naturam, est bona, sed non est bonum universale, sed est quoddam bonum particulare et contractum, secundum quam particularitatem et contractionem sequitur in ea contrarietas, per quam unum contrariatur alteri, licet utrumque in se sit bonum. Quidam autem, aestimantes res non ex earum natura, sed ex suo proprio commodo, quaecumque sibi nociva sunt, simpliciter mala arbitrantur non considerantes quod id quod est uni nocivum quantum ad aliquid, vel alteri vel eidem quantum ad aliquid est proficuum. Quod nequaquam esset, si secundum se corpora essent mala et noxia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0021","text":"[I q. 65 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31167)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod creaturae, quantum est de se, non retrahunt a Deo, sed in ipsum ducunt, quia *invisibilia Dei per ea quae facta sunt, intellecta, conspiciuntur*, ut dicitur Rom. I. Sed quod avertant a Deo, hoc est ex culpa eorum qui insipienter eis utuntur. Unde dicitur Sap. XIV, quod *creaturae factae sunt in muscipulam pedibus insipientium*. Et hoc ipsum quod sic a Deo abducunt, attestatur quod sunt a Deo. Non enim abducunt insipientes a Deo, nisi alliciendo secundum aliquid boni in eis existens, quod habent a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0023","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0024","text":"[I q. 65 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31168)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0025","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod creatura corporalis non sit facta propter Dei bonitatem. Dicitur enim Sap. I, *creavit Deus ut essent omnia*. Ergo omnia sunt creata propter suum proprium esse, et non propter Dei bonitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0026","text":"[I q. 65 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31169)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0027","text":"Praeterea, bonum habet rationem finis. Ergo id quod est magis bonum in rebus, est finis minus boni. Creatura autem spiritualis comparatur ad corporalem, sicut maius bonum ad minus bonum. Ergo creatura corporalis est propter spiritualem, et non propter Dei bonitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0028","text":"[I q. 65 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31170)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0029","text":"Praeterea, iustitia non dat inaequalia nisi inaequalibus. Sed Deus est iustus. Ergo ante omnem inaequalitatem a Deo creatam, est inaequalitas a Deo non creata. Sed inaequalitas a Deo non creata, non potest esse nisi quae est ex libero arbitrio. Ergo omnis inaequalitas sequitur ex diversis motibus liberi arbitrii. Creaturae autem corporales sunt inaequales spiritualibus. Ergo creaturae corporales sunt factae propter aliquos motus liberi arbitrii, et non propter Dei bonitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0030","text":"[I q. 65 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31171)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0031","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Prov. XVI, *universa propter semetipsum operatus est dominus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0032","text":"[I q. 65 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31172)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0033","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Origenes posuit quod creatura corporalis non est facta ex prima Dei intentione, sed ad poenam creaturae spiritualis peccantis. Posuit enim quod Deus a principio creaturas spirituales solas fecit, et omnes aequales. Quarum, cum essent liberi arbitrii, quaedam conversae sunt in Deum, et secundum quantitatem conversionis sortitae sunt maiorem vel minorem gradum, in sua simplicitate remanentes. Quaedam vero, aversae a Deo, alligatae sunt corporibus diversis, secundum modum aversionis a Deo. Quae quidem positio erronea est. Primo quidem, quia contrariatur Scripturae, quae, enarrata productione cuiuslibet speciei creaturae corporalis subiungit, vidit Deus quia hoc esset bonum; quasi diceret quod unumquodque ideo factum est, quia bonum est ipsum esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0034","text":"Secundum autem opinionem Origenis, creatura corporalis facta est, non quia bonum est eam esse, sed ut malum alterius puniretur. Secundo, quia sequeretur quod mundi corporalis dispositio quae nunc est, esset a casu. Si enim ideo corpus solis tale factum est, ut congrueret alicui peccato spiritualis creaturae puniendo; si plures creaturae spirituales similiter peccassent sicut illa propter cuius peccatum puniendum ponit solem creatum, sequeretur quod essent plures soles in mundo. Et idem esset de aliis. Haec autem sunt omnino inconvenientia. Unde haec positione remota tanquam erronea, considerandum est quod ex omnibus creaturis constituitur totum universum sicut totum ex partibus. Si autem alicuius totius et partium eius velimus finem assignare, inveniemus primo quidem, quod singulae partes sunt propter suos actus; sicut oculus ad videndum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0035","text":"Secundo vero, quod pars ignobilior est propter nobiliorem; sicut sensus propter intellectum, et pulmo propter cor. Tertio vero, omnes partes sunt propter perfectionem totius, sicut et materia propter formam, partes enim sunt quasi materia totius. Ulterius autem, totus homo est propter aliquem finem extrinsecum, puta ut fruatur Deo. Sic igitur et in partibus universi, unaquaeque creatura est propter suum proprium actum et perfectionem. Secundo autem, creaturae ignobiliores sunt propter nobiliores sicut creaturae quae sunt infra hominem, sunt propter hominem. Ulterius autem, singulae creaturae sunt propter perfectionem totius universi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0036","text":"Ulterius autem, totum universum, cum singulis suis partibus, ordinatur in Deum sicut in finem, inquantum in eis per quandam imitationem divina bonitas repraesentatur ad gloriam Dei, quamvis creaturae rationales speciali quodam modo supra hoc habeant finem Deum, quem attingere possunt sua operatione, cognoscendo et amando. Et sic patet quod divina bonitas est finis omnium corporalium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0037","text":"[I q. 65 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31173)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0038","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in hoc ipso quod creatura aliqua habet esse, repraesentat divinum esse et bonitatem eius. Et ideo per hoc quod Deus creavit omnia ut essent, non excluditur quin creaverit omnia propter suam bonitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0039","text":"[I q. 65 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31174)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0040","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod finis proximus non excludit finem ultimum. Unde per hoc quod creatura corporalis facta est quodammodo propter spiritualem, non removetur quin sit facta propter Dei bonitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0041","text":"[I q. 65 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31175)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0042","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod aequalitas iustitiae locum habet in retribuendo, iustum enim est quod aequalibus aequalia retribuantur. Non autem habet locum in prima rerum institutione. Sicut enim artifex eiusdem generis lapides in diversis partibus aedificii ponit absque iniustitia, non propter aliquam diversitatem in lapidibus praecedentem, sed attendens ad perfectionem totius aedificii, quae non esset nisi lapides diversimode in aedificio collocarentur; sic et Deus a principio, ut esset perfectio in universo, diversas et inaequales creaturas instituit, secundum suam sapientiam, absque iniustitia, nulla tamen praesupposita meritorum diversitate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0043","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0044","text":"[I q. 65 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31176)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0045","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod creatura corporalis sit producta a Deo mediantibus Angelis. Sicut enim res gubernantur per divinam sapientiam, ita omnia sunt per Dei sapientiam facta; secundum illud Psalmi CIII, *omnia in sapientia fecisti*. Sed ordinare est sapientis, ut dicitur in principio Metaphys. Unde in gubernatione rerum, inferiora per superiora reguntur quodam ordine, ut Augustinus dicit, III de Trin. Ergo et in rerum productione talis ordo fuit, quod creatura corporalis, tanquam inferior, per spiritualem, tanquam superiorem, est producta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0046","text":"[I q. 65 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31177)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0047","text":"Praeterea, diversitas effectuum demonstrat diversitatem causarum, quia idem semper facit idem. Si ergo omnes creaturae, tam spirituales quam corporales, sunt immediate a Deo productae, nulla esset inter creaturas diversitas, nec una magis distaret a Deo quam alia. Quod patet esse falsum, cum propter longe distare a Deo dicat philosophus quaedam corruptibilia esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0048","text":"[I q. 65 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31178)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0049","text":"Praeterea, ad producendum effectum finitum, non requiritur virtus infinita. Sed omne corpus finitum est. Ergo per finitam virtutem spiritualis creaturae produci potuit; et productum fuit, quia in talibus non differt esse et posse; praesertim quia nulla dignitas competens alicui secundum suam naturam, ei denegatur, nisi forte ob culpam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0050","text":"[I q. 65 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31179)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0051","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*, per quae creatura corporalis intelligitur. Ergo creatura corporalis est immediate a Deo producta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0052","text":"[I q. 65 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31180)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0053","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam posuerunt gradatim res a Deo processisse, ita scilicet quod ab eo immediate processit prima creatura, et illa produxit aliam; et sic inde usque ad creaturam corpoream. Sed haec positio est impossibilis. Quia prima corporalis creaturae productio est per creationem per quam etiam ipsa materia producitur, imperfectum enim est prius quam perfectum in fieri impossibile est autem aliquid creari nisi a solo Deo. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod quanto aliqua causa est superior, tanto ad plura se extendit in causando. Semper autem id quod substernitur in rebus, invenitur communius quam id quod informat et restringit ipsum, sicut esse quam vivere, et vivere quam intelligere, et materia quam forma. Quanto ergo aliquid est magis substratum, tanto a superiori causa directe procedit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0054","text":"Id ergo quod est primo substratum in omnibus, proprie pertinet ad causalitatem supremae causae. Nulla igitur secunda causa potest aliquid producere, non praesupposito in re producta aliquo quod causatur a superiori causa. Creatio autem est productio alicuius rei secundum suam totam substantiam, nullo praesupposito quod sit vel increatum vel ab aliquo creatum. Unde relinquitur quod nihil potest aliquid creare nisi solus Deus, qui est prima causa. Et ideo ut Moyses ostenderet corpora omnia immediate a Deo creata, dixit, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0055","text":"[I q. 65 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31181)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0056","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in productione rerum est aliquis ordo, non quidem ut una creatura creetur ab alia (hoc enim impossibile est); sed ita quod ex divina sapientia diversi gradus in creaturis, constituuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0057","text":"[I q. 65 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31182)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0058","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ipse Deus unus, absque suae simplicitatis detrimento, diversorum cognoscitivus est, ut supra ostensum est. Et ideo etiam est, secundum diversa cognita, diversorum productorum causa per suam sapientiam, sicut et artifex, apprehendendo diversas formas, producit diversa artificiata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0059","text":"[I q. 65 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31183)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0060","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod quantitas virtutis agentis non solum mensuratur secundum rem factam, sed etiam secundum modum faciendi, quia unum et idem aliter fit et a maiori, et a minori virtute. Producere autem aliquid finitum hoc modo ut nihil praesupponatur, est virtutis infinitae. Unde nulli creaturae competere potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0061","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0062","text":"[I q. 65 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31184)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0063","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod formae corporum sint ab Angelis. Dicit enim Boetius, in libro de Trin., quod *a formis quae sunt sine materia, veniunt formae quae sunt in materia*. Formae autem quae sunt sine materia, sunt substantiae spirituales, formae autem quae sunt in materia, sunt formae corporum. Ergo formae corporum sunt a spiritualibus substantiis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0064","text":"[I q. 65 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31185)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0065","text":"Praeterea, omne quod est per participationem, reducitur ad id quod est per essentiam. Sed spirituales substantiae per suam essentiam sunt formae, creaturae autem corporales participant formas. Ergo formae corporalium rerum sunt a spiritualibus substantiis derivatae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0066","text":"[I q. 65 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31186)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0067","text":"Praeterea, spirituales substantiae magis habent virtutem causandi quam corpora caelestia. Sed corpora caelestia causant formas in istis inferioribus, unde dicuntur esse generationis et corruptionis causa. Ergo multo magis a spiritualibus substantiis formae quae sunt in materia, derivantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0068","text":"[I q. 65 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31187)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0069","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, III de Trin., quod *non est putandum Angelis ad nutum servire hanc corporalem materiam, sed potius Deo*. Illi autem ad nutum dicitur servire corporalis materia, a quo speciem recipit. Non ergo formae corporales sunt ab Angelis, sed a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0070","text":"[I q. 65 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31188)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0071","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod opinio fuit quorundam quod omnes formae corporales deriventur a substantiis spiritualibus quas Angelos dicimus. Et hoc quidem dupliciter aliqui posuerunt. Plato enim posuit formas quae sunt in materia corporali, derivari et formari a formis sine materia subsistentibus, per modum participationis cuiusdam. Ponebat enim hominem quendam immaterialiter subsistentem, et similiter equum, et sic de aliis, ex quibus constituuntur haec singularia sensibilia, secundum quod in materia corporali remanet quaedam impressio ab illis formis separatis, per modum assimilationis cuiusdam, quam participationem vocabat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0072","text":"Et secundum ordinem formarum ponebant Platonici ordinem substantiarum separatarum, puta quod una substantia separata est quae est equus, quae est causa omnium equorum; supra quam est quaedam vita separata, quam dicebant per se vitam et causam omnis vitae; et ulterius quandam quam nominabant ipsum esse, et causam omnis esse. Avicenna vero et quidam alii non posuerunt formas rerum corporalium in materia per se subsistere, sed solum in intellectu. A formis ergo in intellectu creaturarum spiritualium existentibus (quas quidem ipsi intelligentias, nos autem Angelos dicimus), dicebant procedere omnes formas quae sunt in materia corporali, sicut a formis quae sunt in mente artificis, procedunt formae artificiatorum. Et in idem videtur redire quod quidam moderni haeretici ponunt, dicentes quidem Deum creatorem omnium, sed materiam corporalem a Diabolo formatam et per varias species distinctam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0073","text":"Omnes autem hae opiniones ex una radice processisse videntur. Quaerebant enim causam formarum, ac si ipsae formae fierent secundum seipsas. Sed sicut probat Aristoteles in VII Metaphys., id quod proprie fit, est compositum, formae autem corruptibilium rerum habent ut aliquando sint, aliquando non sint, absque hoc quod ipsae generentur aut corrumpantur, sed compositis generatis aut corruptis, quia etiam formae non habent esse, sed composita habent esse per eas, sic enim alicui competit fieri, sicut et esse. Et ideo, cum simile fiat a suo simili, non est quaerenda causa formarum corporalium aliqua forma immaterialis; sed aliquod compositum, secundum quod hic ignis generatur ab hoc igne. Sic igitur formae corporales causantur, non quasi influxae ab aliqua immateriali forma, sed quasi materia reducta de potentia in actum ab aliquo agente composito.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0074","text":"Sed quia agens compositum, quod est corpus, movetur a substantia spirituali creata, ut Augustinus dicit III de Trin.; sequitur ulterius quod etiam formae corporales a substantiis spiritualibus deriventur, non tanquam influentibus formas, sed tanquam moventibus ad formas. Ulterius autem reducuntur in Deum, sicut in primam causam, etiam species angelici intellectus, quae sunt quaedam seminales rationes corporalium formarum. In prima autem corporalis creaturae productione non consideratur aliqua transmutatio de potentia in actum. Et ideo formae corporales quas in prima productione corpora habuerunt, sunt immediate a Deo productae, cui soli ad nutum obedit materia, tanquam propriae causae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0075","text":"Unde ad hoc significandum, Moyses singulis operibus praemittit, dixit Deus fiat hoc vel illud; in quo significatur formatio rerum per verbum Dei facta, a quo, secundum Augustinum, *est omnis forma et compago et concordia partium*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0076","text":"[I q. 65 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31189)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0077","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Boetius intelligit per formas quae sunt sine materia, rationes rerum quae sunt in mente divina, sicut etiam apostolus dicit, Heb. XI, *fide credimus aptata esse saecula verbo Dei, ut ex invisibilibus visibilia fierent*. Si tamen per formas quae sunt sine materia, intelligit Angelos, dicendum est quod ab eis veniunt formae quae sunt in materia, non per influxum, sed per motum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0078","text":"[I q. 65 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31190)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0079","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod formae participatae in materia reducuntur, non ad formas aliquas per se subsistentes rationis eiusdem, ut Platonici posuerunt; sed ad formas intelligibiles vel intellectus angelici, a quibus per motum procedunt; vel ulterius ad rationes intellectus divini, a quibus etiam formarum semina sunt rebus creatis indita, ut per motum in actum educi possint.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0080","text":"[I q. 65 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31191)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0081","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod corpora caelestia causant formas in istis inferioribus, non influendo, sed movendo.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"08dcd625998702d39c06c09cac9b71e34884ef45976f13a6f8cd18b3101f9c82","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T145221322841Z_ae53efc7","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 65","source_sha256":"08dcd625998702d39c06c09cac9b71e34884ef45976f13a6f8cd18b3101f9c82","sha256":"d5620e165900b48f550c2d31793ba960641cedf132dbcb4dafaebc2e9667a783","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:52:21.322869+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0002","text":"[I q. 65 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31159)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0003","text":"After the consideration of the spiritual creature, there remains to be considered the corporeal creature. In its production Scripture commemorates three works: namely, the work of creation, when it says, *in the beginning God created heaven and earth*, etc.; the work of distinction, when it says, *he divided the light from the darkness, and the waters which are above the firmament from the waters which are beneath the firmament*; and the work of adornment, when it says, *let there be lights in the firmament*, and so forth. Therefore, first the work of creation is to be considered; second, the work of distinction; third, the work of adornment. Concerning the first, four questions are asked. First, whether the corporeal creature is from God. Second, whether it was made for God's goodness. Third, whether it was made by God through the mediation of the angels. Fourth, whether the forms of bodies are from angels, or immediately from God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0005","text":"[I q. 65 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31160)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0006","text":"To the first, one proceeds thus. It seems that the corporeal creature is not from God. For it is said in Ecclesiastes III: *I have learned that all things which God has made endure for ever.* But visible bodies do not endure for ever, for it is said in II Corinthians IV: *the things which are seen are temporal; but the things which are not seen are eternal.* Therefore God did not make visible bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0007","text":"[I q. 65 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31161)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0008","text":"Further, in Genesis I it is said: *God saw all the things that he had made, and they were very good.* But corporeal creatures are evil, for we experience them to be harmful in many ways, as is evident in many serpents, in the heat of the sun, and the like; now a thing is called evil because it harms. Therefore corporeal creatures are not from God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0009","text":"[I q. 65 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31162)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0010","text":"Further, that which is from God does not draw away from God, but leads into him. But corporeal creatures draw away from God; hence the Apostle says, in II Corinthians IV: *while we contemplate not the things which are seen.* Therefore corporeal creatures are not from God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0011","text":"[I q. 65 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31163)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0012","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Psalm CXLV: *who made heaven and earth, the sea, and all things that are in them.*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0013","text":"[I q. 65 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31164)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that the position of certain heretics is that these visible things were not created by the good God, but by an evil principle. And for an argument in support of their error they adduce what the Apostle says in II Corinthians IV: *The god of this world has blinded the minds of unbelievers.* But this position is altogether impossible. For if diverse things are united in something, it is necessary that there be some cause of this union, for things diverse in themselves are not united. Hence it is that, whenever something one is found in diverse things, those diverse things must receive that one thing from some one cause; just as diverse hot bodies have heat from fire. Now being is found commonly in all things, however diverse. Therefore there must be one principle of being, from which whatever things are in whatever way have being, whether they are invisible and spiritual or visible and corporeal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0015","text":"Now the Devil is called the god of this world not by creation, but because those who live worldly lives serve him; according to the manner of speaking by which the Apostle speaks in Philippians III: *whose god is their belly.*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0016","text":"[I q. 65 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31165)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0017","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that all God's creatures endure for ever in some respect, at least with respect to matter, because creatures will never be reduced to nothing, even if they are corruptible. But the more creatures approach God, who is altogether immovable, the more immovable they are. For corruptible creatures remain perpetually with respect to matter, but are changed with respect to substantial form. Incorruptible creatures, however, remain indeed with respect to substance, but are mutable with respect to other things: for example, with respect to place, as heavenly bodies are; or with respect to affections, as spiritual creatures are.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0018","text":"But what the Apostle says, *the things which are seen are temporal*, although it is true even as regards the things themselves considered in themselves, insofar as every visible creature is subject to time, either according to its being or according to its motion; nevertheless, the Apostle intends to speak of visible things insofar as they are rewards of man. For man's rewards which are in these visible things pass away temporally, whereas those which are in invisible things endure for ever. Hence he had also previously stated: *it works in us an eternal weight of glory.*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0019","text":"[I q. 65 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31166)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0020","text":"To the second it must be said that the corporeal creature, according to its nature, is good; but it is not the universal good, but a certain particular and restricted good. According to this particularity and restriction, contrariety follows in it, through which one thing is contrary to another, although each is good in itself. But some, judging things not from their nature but from their own private advantage, judge to be simply evil whatever is harmful to themselves, without considering that what is harmful to one in some respect is beneficial either to another or to the same one in some respect. This would by no means be so if bodies were evil and harmful in themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0021","text":"[I q. 65 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31167)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0022","text":"To the third it must be said that creatures, as far as concerns themselves, do not draw away from God, but lead into him, because *the invisible things of God, understood through the things that have been made, are seen*, as is said in Romans I. But that they turn away from God is due to the fault of those who use them foolishly. Hence it is said in Wisdom XIV that *creatures were made a snare for the feet of the foolish.* And this very fact that they thus lead away from God attests that they are from God. For they do not lead foolish people away from God except by alluring them according to some good existing in them, which they have from God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0023","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0024","text":"[I q. 65 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31168)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0025","text":"We proceed thus to the second [question]. It seems that the corporeal creature was not made for the sake of God's goodness. For it is said in Wisdom 1, *God created that all things might exist*. Therefore, all things were created for the sake of their own proper being, and not for the sake of God's goodness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0026","text":"[I q. 65 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31169)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0027","text":"Moreover, the good has the character of an end. Therefore, that which is better among things is the end of what is less good. But the spiritual creature is compared to the corporeal as a greater good to a lesser good. Therefore, the corporeal creature is for the sake of the spiritual creature, and not for the sake of God's goodness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0028","text":"[I q. 65 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31170)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0029","text":"Moreover, justice does not give unequal things except to unequal persons. But God is just. Therefore, prior to every inequality created by God, there is an inequality not created by God. But an inequality not created by God can only be one that arises from free choice. Therefore, every inequality follows from different movements of free choice. But corporeal creatures are unequal to spiritual creatures. Therefore, corporeal creatures were made on account of certain movements of free choice, and not for the sake of God's goodness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0030","text":"[I q. 65 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31171)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0031","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Proverbs 16: *The Lord has made all things for himself*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0032","text":"[I q. 65 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31172)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0033","text":"I answer that Origen held that the corporeal creature was not made by God's first intention, but for the punishment of the sinning spiritual creature. For he held that God in the beginning made spiritual creatures alone, and all equal. Since these possessed free choice, some were converted to God and, according to the measure of their conversion, obtained a greater or lesser degree, while remaining in their simplicity. But others, turned away from God, were bound to diverse bodies according to the manner of their turning away from God. This position, indeed, is erroneous. First, because it is contrary to Scripture, which, having narrated the production of each species of corporeal creature, adds: God saw that this was good; as though saying that each thing was made for this reason, because its very being is good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0034","text":"But according to Origen's opinion, the corporeal creature was made, not because it is good for it to exist, but so that the evil of another might be punished. Secondly, because it would follow that the present disposition of the corporeal world was due to chance. For if the body of the sun was made such in order that it might be fitting for punishing some sin of a spiritual creature—if several spiritual creatures had sinned in like manner as that one for whose sin, to be punished, he posits that the sun was created—it would follow that there were several suns in the world. And the same would hold concerning other things. But these consequences are wholly unfitting. Therefore, with this position set aside as erroneous, it must be considered that the whole universe is constituted from all creatures, as a whole from its parts. Now, if we should wish to assign an end to any whole and to its parts, we would find, first, that the individual parts are for the sake of their acts; as the eye is for seeing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0035","text":"Secondly, that a less noble part is for the sake of a more noble one; as sense is for the sake of intellect, and the lung for the sake of the heart. Thirdly, all parts are for the sake of the perfection of the whole, just as matter is for the sake of form; for parts are, as it were, the matter of the whole. Furthermore, the whole man is for the sake of some extrinsic end, namely, that he may enjoy God. So too, therefore, among the parts of the universe, each creature is for the sake of its own act and perfection. Secondly, less noble creatures are for the sake of more noble ones, just as creatures that are below man are for the sake of man. Furthermore, individual creatures are for the sake of the perfection of the whole universe.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0036","text":"Furthermore, the whole universe, together with all its parts, is ordered to God as to an end, insofar as in them the divine goodness is represented by a certain imitation, unto the glory of God; although rational creatures, in a certain special manner beyond this, have God as an end, whom they can attain by their operation, by knowing and loving. And thus it is clear that the divine goodness is the end of all corporeal things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0037","text":"[I q. 65 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31173)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0038","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, in the very fact that some creature has being, it represents the divine being and his goodness. And therefore, from the fact that God created all things that they might exist, it is not excluded that he created all things for the sake of his goodness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0039","text":"[I q. 65 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31174)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0040","text":"To the second, it must be said that a proximate end does not exclude the ultimate end. Hence, from the fact that the corporeal creature was made in some manner for the sake of the spiritual creature, it is not removed that it was made for the sake of God's goodness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0041","text":"[I q. 65 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31175)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0042","text":"To the third, it must be said that the equality of justice has a place in rendering recompense; for it is just that equal things be rendered to equals. But it has no place in the first institution of things. For just as an artisan places stones of the same kind in diverse parts of a building without injustice, not on account of some preceding diversity in the stones, but attending to the perfection of the whole building—which would not exist unless the stones were placed in the building in diverse ways—so also God, in the beginning, in order that there might be perfection in the universe, established diverse and unequal creatures according to his wisdom, without injustice, while no diversity of merits was presupposed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0043","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0044","text":"[I q. 65 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31176)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0045","text":"We proceed thus to the third [question]. It seems that the corporeal creature was produced by God through the mediation of the angels. For just as things are governed by divine wisdom, so all things are made by God's wisdom, according to Psalm 103: *You have made all things in wisdom*. But ordering belongs to the wise, as is said at the beginning of the Metaphysics. Hence, in the governance of things, lower things are ruled through higher things in a certain order, as Augustine says in *On the Trinity*, III. Therefore, in the production of things too there was such an order: that the corporeal creature, as lower, was produced through the spiritual creature, as higher.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0046","text":"[I q. 65 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31177)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0047","text":"Moreover, diversity of effects demonstrates diversity of causes, because the same always produces the same. Therefore, if all creatures, both spiritual and corporeal, were produced immediately by God, there would be no diversity among creatures, nor would one be more distant from God than another. But this is plainly false, since the Philosopher says that some things are corruptible because they are far distant from God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0048","text":"[I q. 65 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31178)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0049","text":"Moreover, an infinite power is not required to produce a finite effect. But every body is finite. Therefore it could have been produced by the finite power of a spiritual creature; and it was produced, because in such matters to be and to be able do not differ; especially since no dignity befitting someone according to his nature is denied to him, unless perhaps on account of fault.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0050","text":"[I q. 65 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31179)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0051","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Gen. I, *in the beginning God created heaven and earth*, by which corporeal creature is understood. Therefore corporeal creature was produced immediately by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0052","text":"[I q. 65 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31180)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0053","text":"I answer that it must be said that some have held that things proceeded from God by stages, namely, such that the first creature proceeded immediately from Him, and that creature produced another; and thus thence to corporeal creature. But this position is impossible. For the first production of corporeal creature is through creation, through which matter itself is also produced; for what is imperfect is prior to what is perfect in coming-to-be. But it is impossible for anything to be created except by God alone. For the elucidation of this, it must be considered that the higher a cause is, the more things it extends to in causing. Now that which is subjected in things is always found to be more common than that which informs and restricts it, as being is more common than living, and living than understanding, and matter than form. Therefore, the more something is underlying, the more directly it proceeds from a higher cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0054","text":"Therefore that which is first underlying in all things properly belongs to the causality of the highest cause. Therefore no secondary cause can produce anything without presupposing in the thing produced something which is caused by a higher cause. But creation is the production of a thing according to its whole substance, with nothing presupposed which either is uncreated or is created by something. Hence it remains that nothing can create anything except God alone, who is the first cause. And therefore, in order that Moses might show that all bodies were created immediately by God, he said, *in the beginning God created heaven and earth*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0055","text":"[I q. 65 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31181)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0056","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that there is some order in the production of things, not indeed such that one creature is created by another (for this is impossible), but such that diverse grades in creatures are established from divine wisdom.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0057","text":"[I q. 65 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31182)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0058","text":"To the second it must be said that God Himself, one without detriment to His simplicity, is cognizant of diverse things, as was shown above. And therefore He is also, according to the diverse things known, the cause through His wisdom of diverse products, just as an artisan, by apprehending diverse forms, produces diverse artifacts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0059","text":"[I q. 65 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31183)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0060","text":"To the third it must be said that the magnitude of an agent's power is measured not only according to the thing made, but also according to the mode of making, because one and the same thing is made differently by a greater and by a lesser power. But to produce something finite in such a way that nothing is presupposed is a matter of infinite power. Hence it cannot belong to any creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0061","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0062","text":"[I q. 65 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31184)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0063","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that the forms of bodies are from angels. For Boethius says, in the book *On the Trinity*, that *from forms which are without matter come forms which are in matter*. But forms that are without matter are spiritual substances, whereas forms that are in matter are the forms of bodies. Therefore the forms of bodies are from spiritual substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0064","text":"[I q. 65 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31185)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0065","text":"Moreover, everything which is by participation is reduced to that which is by essence. But spiritual substances are forms through their essence, while corporeal creatures participate in forms. Therefore the forms of corporeal things are derived from spiritual substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0066","text":"[I q. 65 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31186)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0067","text":"Moreover, spiritual substances have a greater power of causing than heavenly bodies. But heavenly bodies cause forms in these lower things, whence they are said to be the cause of generation and corruption. Therefore, much more are the forms that are in matter derived from spiritual substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0068","text":"[I q. 65 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31187)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0069","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in III *On the Trinity*, that *this corporeal matter ought not to be thought to serve the angels at their command, but rather God*. But corporeal matter is said to serve at the command of that from which it receives its species. Therefore corporeal forms are not from angels, but from God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0070","text":"[I q. 65 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31188)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0071","text":"I answer that it must be said that it was the opinion of some that all corporeal forms are derived from the spiritual substances which we call angels. And some indeed maintained this in two ways. For Plato held that forms which are in corporeal matter are derived and formed from subsisting forms without matter, by way of a certain participation. For he posited a certain man subsisting immaterially, and likewise a horse, and so concerning the others, from which these individual sensible things are constituted, insofar as a certain impression from those separate forms remains in corporeal matter by way of a certain assimilation, which he called participation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0072","text":"And according to the order of forms, the Platonists posited an order of separate substances: for example, that there is one separate substance which is horse, which is the cause of all horses; above which is a certain separate life, which they called life itself and the cause of all life; and beyond that one which they named being itself, and the cause of all being. But Avicenna and certain others did not posit that the forms of corporeal things subsist in matter through themselves, but only in the intellect. Therefore, from forms existing in the intellect of spiritual creatures (which they indeed call intelligences, but we call angels), they said that all forms which are in corporeal matter proceed, just as from forms which are in the mind of an artisan proceed the forms of artifacts. And what certain modern heretics maintain seems to come back to the same thing: namely, that God is the creator of all things, but that corporeal matter was formed by the Devil and distinguished into various species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0073","text":"Now all these opinions seem to have proceeded from one root. For they sought the cause of forms, as though the forms themselves came to be through themselves. But, as Aristotle proves in Metaphysics VII, that which properly comes to be is the composite; whereas the forms of corruptible things are such as to be at one time and not to be at another, without their themselves being generated or corrupted, but rather when composites are generated or corrupted. For forms too do not have being, but composites have being through them; for becoming belongs to something in the same way as being does. And therefore, since like is made by its like, one should not seek some immaterial form as the cause of corporeal forms, but rather some composite, according as this fire is generated by this fire. Thus corporeal forms are caused, not as though infused by some immaterial form, but as matter being reduced from potency to act by some composite agent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0074","text":"But because a composite agent, which is a body, is moved by a created spiritual substance, as Augustine says in On the Trinity III, it further follows that corporeal forms too are derived from spiritual substances, not as infusing forms, but as moving toward forms. Further still, the species of the angelic intellect, which are certain seminal reasons of corporeal forms, are reduced to God as to the first cause. But in the first production of corporeal creation, no transmutation from potency to act is considered. And therefore the corporeal forms which bodies had in their first production were produced immediately by God, to whose command alone matter obeys, as to its proper cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0075","text":"Hence, to signify this, Moses prefaces the individual works with: God said, “Let this or that be”; by which the formation of things, made through the Word of God, is signified—from whom, according to Augustine, *is every form and joining and harmony of parts*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0076","text":"[I q. 65 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31189)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0077","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that by forms which are without matter Boethius understands the reasons of things that are in the divine mind, as the Apostle also says, Hebrews XI: *by faith we believe that the ages were fitted together by the word of God, so that visible things might come to be from invisible things*. If, however, by forms which are without matter he understands angels, it must be said that forms which are in matter come from them, not by influx, but by motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0078","text":"[I q. 65 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31190)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0079","text":"To the second, it must be said that forms participated in matter are reduced, not to certain per se subsisting forms of the same account, as the Platonists held, but to the intelligible forms of the angelic intellect, from which they proceed by motion; or, further, to the reasons of the divine intellect, from which seeds of forms also have been implanted in created things, so that they may be brought forth into act through motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0080","text":"[I q. 65 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31191)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0081","text":"To the third, it must be said that the heavenly bodies cause forms in these lower things, not by influx, but by moving.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_57:P0081"]}],"source_digest":"25cb2f1f83341718c50e66ef5e1d816e6d4ca7588fb05d101f61044522594ca4","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 65"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 65","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58","title":"First Part · Question 66","order":67,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_58","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 66 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31192)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de opere distinctionis. Et primo considerandum est de ordine creationis ad distinctionem; secundo, de ipsa distinctione secundum se. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum informitas materiae creatae praecesserit tempore distinctionem ipsius. Secundo, utrum sit una materia omnium corporalium. Tertio, utrum caelum Empyreum sit concreatum materiae informi. Quarto, utrum tempus sit eidem concreatum.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 66 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31193)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod informitas materiae tempore praecesserit formationem ipsius. Dicitur enim Gen. 1, *terra erat inanis et vacua*, sive invisibilis et incomposita, secundum aliam litteram; per quod designatur informitas materiae, ut Augustinus dicit. Ergo materia fuit aliquando informis, antequam formaretur.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31194)\n\nPraeterea, natura in sua operatione Dei operationem imitatur; sicut causa secunda imitatur causam primam. Sed in operatione naturae informitas tempore praecedit formationem. Ergo et in operatione Dei.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31195)\n\nPraeterea, materia potior est accidente, quia materia est pars substantiae. Sed Deus potest facere quod accidens sit sine subiecto; ut patet in sacramento altaris. Ergo potuit facere quod materia esset sine forma.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 1 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31196)\n\nSed contra, imperfectio effectus attestatur imperfectioni agentis. Sed Deus est agens perfectissimum, unde de eo dicitur, Deut. XXXII, *Dei perfecta sunt opera*. Ergo opus ab eo creatum nunquam fuit informe.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 1 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31197)\n\nPraeterea, creaturae corporalis formatio facta fuit per opus distinctionis. Distinctioni autem opponitur confusio, sicut et formationi informitas. Si ergo informitas praecessit tempore formationem materiae, sequitur a principio fuisse confusionem corporalis creaturae, quam antiqui vocaverunt chaos.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31198)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa hoc sunt diversae opiniones sanctorum. Augustinus enim vult quod informitas materiae corporalis non praecesserit tempore formationem ipsius, sed solum origine vel ordine naturae. Alii vero, ut Basilius, Ambrosius et Chrysostomus, volunt quod informitas materiae tempore praecesserit formationem. Et quamvis hae opiniones videantur esse contrariae, tamen parum ab invicem differunt, aliter enim accipit informitatem materiae Augustinus quam alii. Augustinus enim accipit informitatem materiae pro carentia omnis formae. Et sic impossibile est dicere quod informitas materiae tempore praecesserit vel formationem ipsius, vel distinctionem. Et de formatione quidem manifestum est. Si enim materia informis praecessit duratione, haec erat iam in actu, hoc enim duratio importat, creationis enim terminus est ens actu. Ipsum autem quod est actus, est forma. Dicere igitur materiam praecedere sine forma, est dicere ens actu sine actu, quod implicat contradictionem. Nec etiam potest dici quod habuit aliquam formam communem et postmodum supervenerunt ei formae diversae, quibus sit distincta. Quia hoc esset idem cum opinione antiquorum naturalium, qui posuerunt materiam primam esse aliquod corpus in actu, puta ignem, aerem aut aquam, aut aliquod medium. Ex quo sequebatur quod fieri non esset nisi alterari. Quia cum illa forma praecedens daret esse in actu in genere substantiae, et faceret esse hoc aliquid; sequebatur quod superveniens forma non faceret simpliciter ens actu, sed ens actu hoc, quod est proprium formae accidentalis; et sic sequentes formae essent accidentia, secundum quae non attenditur generatio, sed alteratio. Unde oportet dicere quod materia prima neque fuit creata omnino sine forma, neque sub forma una communi, sed sub formis distinctis. Et ita, si informitas materiae referatur ad conditionem primae materiae, quae secundum se non habet aliquam formam, informitas materiae non praecessit formationem seu distinctionem ipsius tempore, ut Augustinus dicit, sed origine seu natura tantum, eo modo quo potentia est prior actu, et pars toto. Alii vero sancti accipiunt informitatem, non secundum quod excludit omnem formam, sed secundum quod excludit istam formositatem et decorem qui nunc apparet in corporea creatura. Et secundum hoc dicunt quod informitas materiae corporalis duratione praecessit formationem eiusdem. Et sic secundum hoc, quantum ad aliquid cum eis Augustinus concordat, et quantum aliquid discordat, ut infra patebit. Et quantum ex littera Genesis I, accipi potest, triplex formositas deerat, propter quod dicebatur creatura corporalis informis. Deerat enim a toto corpore diaphano, quod dicitur caelum, pulchritudo lucis, unde dicitur quod tenebrae erant super faciem abyssi. Deerat autem terrae duplex pulchritudo. Una, quam habet ex hoc quod est aquis discooperta, et quantum ad hoc dicitur quod terra erat inanis, sive invisibilis, quia corporali aspectui patere non poterat, propter aquas undique eam cooperientes. Alia vero, quam habet ex hoc quod est ornata herbis et plantis, et ideo dicitur quod erat vacua, vel incomposita, idest non ornata, secundum aliam litteram. Et sic, cum praemisisset duas naturas creatas, scilicet caelum et terram, informitatem caeli expressit per hoc quod dixit, tenebrae erant super faciem abyssi, secundum quod sub caelo etiam aer includitur, informitatem vero terrae, per hoc quod dixit, terra erat inanis et vacua.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31199)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod terra aliter accipitur in loco isto ab Augustino, et ab aliis sanctis. Augustinus enim vult quod nomine terrae et aquae significetur in hoc loco ipsa materia prima. Non enim poterat Moyses rudi populo primam materiam exprimere, nisi sub similitudine rerum eis notarum. Unde et sub multiplici similitudine eam exprimit, non vocans eam tantum aquam vel tantum terram, ne videatur secundum rei veritatem materia prima esse vel terra vel aqua. Habet tamen similitudinem cum terra, inquantum subsidet formis; et cum aqua, inquantum est apta formari diversis formis. Secundum hoc ergo, dicitur terra inanis et vacua, vel invisibilis et incomposita, quia materia per formam cognoscitur (unde in se considerata dicitur invisibilis vel inanis), et eius potentia per formam repletur (unde et Plato materiam dicit esse locum). Alii vero sancti per terram intelligunt ipsum elementum, quae qualiter, secundum eos, erat informis, dictum est.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31200)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod natura producit effectum in actu de ente in potentia, et ideo oportet ut in eius operatione potentia tempore praecedat actum, et informitas formationem. Sed Deus producit ens actu ex nihilo, et ideo statim potest producere rem perfectam, secundum magnitudinem suae virtutis.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31201)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod accidens, cum sit forma, est actus quidam, materia autem secundum id quod est, est ens in potentia. Unde magis repugnat esse in actu materiae sine forma, quam accidenti sine subiecto.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31202)\n\nAd primum vero quod obiicitur in contrarium, dicendum est quod si, secundum alios sanctos, informitas tempore praecessit formationem materiae, non fuit hoc ex impotentia Dei; sed ex eius sapientia, ut ordo servaretur in rerum conditione, dum ex imperfecto ad perfectum adducerentur.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31203)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod quidam antiquorum naturalium posuerunt confusionem excludentem omnem distinctionem; praeter hoc quod Anaxagoras posuit solum intellectum distinctum et immixtum. Sed ante opus distinctionis Scriptura sacra ponit multiplicem distinctionem. Primo quidem, caeli et terrae (in quo ostenditur distinctio etiam secundum materiam, ut infra patebit), et hoc cum dicit, *in principio Deus creavit caelum et terram*. Secundo, distinctionem elementorum quantum ad formas suas, per hoc quod nominat terram et aquam. Aerem autem et ignem non nominat, quia non est ita manifestum rudibus, quibus Moyses loquebatur, huiusmodi esse corpora, sicut manifestum est de terra et aqua. Quamvis Plato aerem intellexerit significari per hoc quod dicitur spiritus domini (quia etiam aer spiritus dicitur), ignem vero intellexerit significari per caelum (quod igneae naturae esse dixit), ut Augustinus refert in VIII libro de Civ. Dei. Sed Rabbi Moyses, in aliis cum Platone concordans, dicit ignem significari per tenebras, quia, ut dicit, in propria sphaera ignis non lucet. Sed magis videtur esse conveniens quod prius dictum est, quia spiritus domini in Scriptura non nisi pro spiritu sancto consuevit poni. Qui aquis superferri dicitur, non corporaliter, sed sicut voluntas artificis superfertur materiae quam vult formare. Tertia distinctio significatur secundum situm. Quia terra erat sub aquis, quibus invisibilis reddebatur, aer vero, qui est subiectum tenebrarum, significatur fuisse super aquas, per hoc quod dicitur, tenebrae erant super faciem abyssi. Quid autem distinguendum remaneret, ex sequentibus apparebit.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 66 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31204)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod una sit materia informis omnium corporalium. Dicit enim Augustinus, XII Confess., *duo reperio quae fecisti, unum quod erat formatum, alterum quod erat informe*; et hoc dicit esse terram invisibilem et incompositam, per quam dicit significari materiam rerum corporalium. Ergo una est materia omnium corporalium.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31205)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus dicit, in V Metaphys., quod illa quae sunt unum in genere, sunt unum in materia. Sed omnia corporalia conveniunt in genere corporis. Ergo omnium corporalium est una materia.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31206)\n\nPraeterea, diversus actus fit in diversa potentia, et unus in una. Sed omnium corporum est una forma, scilicet corporeitas. Ergo omnium corporalium est materia una.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31207)\n\nPraeterea, materia in se considerata, est solum in potentia. Sed distinctio est per formas. Ergo materia in se considerata, est una tantum omnium corporalium.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31208)\n\nSed contra, quaecumque conveniunt in materia, sunt transmutabilia ad invicem, et agunt et patiuntur ab invicem, ut dicitur in I de Gen. Sed corpora caelestia et inferiora non sic se habent ad invicem. Ergo eorum materia non est una.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31209)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa hoc fuerunt diversae opiniones philosophorum. Plato enim, et omnes philosophi ante Aristotelem, posuerunt omnia corpora esse de natura quatuor elementorum. Unde cum quatuor elementa communicent in una materia, ut mutua generatio et corruptio in eis ostendit; per consequens sequebatur quod omnium corporum sit materia una. Quod autem quaedam corpora sint incorruptibilia, Plato adscribebat non conditioni materiae, sed voluntati artificis, scilicet Dei, quem introducit corporibus caelestibus dicentem, *natura vestra estis dissolubilia, voluntate autem mea indissolubilia, quia voluntas mea maior est nexu vestro*. Hanc autem positionem Aristoteles reprobat, per motus naturales corporum. Cum enim corpus caeleste habeat naturalem motum diversum a naturali motu elementorum, sequitur quod eius natura sit alia a natura quatuor elementorum. Et sicut motus circularis, qui est proprius corporis caelestis, caret contrarietate, motus autem elementorum sunt invicem contrarii, ut qui est sursum ei qui est deorsum, ita corpus caeleste est absque contrarietate, corpora vero elementaria sunt cum contrarietate. Et quia corruptio et generatio sunt ex contrariis, sequitur quod secundum suam naturam corpus caeleste sit incorruptibile, elementa vero sunt corruptibilia. Sed non obstante hac differentia corruptibilitatis et incorruptibilitatis naturalis, Avicebron posuit unam materiam omnium corporum, attendens ad unitatem formae corporalis. Sed si forma corporeitatis esset una forma per se, cui supervenirent aliae formae, quibus corpora distinguuntur, haberet necessitatem quod dicitur. Quia illa forma immutabiliter materiae inhaereret, et quantum ad illam esset omne corpus incorruptibile, sed corruptio accideret per remotionem sequentium formarum, quae non esset corruptio simpliciter, sed secundum quid, quia privationi substerneretur aliquod ens actu. Sicut etiam accidebat antiquis naturalibus, qui ponebant subiectum corporum aliquod ens actu, puta ignem aut aerem aut aliquid huiusmodi. Supposito autem quod nulla forma quae sit in corpore corruptibili remaneat ut substrata generationi et corruptioni, sequitur de necessitate quod non sit eadem materia corporum corruptibilium et incorruptibilium. Materia enim, secundum id quod est, est in potentia ad formam. Oportet ergo quod materia, secundum se considerata, sit in potentia ad formam omnium illorum quorum est materia communis. Per unam autem formam non fit in actu nisi quantum ad illam formam. Remanet ergo in potentia quantum ad omnes alias formas. Nec hoc excluditur, si una illarum formarum sit perfectior et continens in se virtute alias. Quia potentia, quantum est de se, indifferenter se habet ad perfectum et imperfectum, unde sicut quando est sub forma imperfecta, est in potentia ad formam perfectam, ita e converso. Sic ergo materia, secundum quod est sub forma incorruptibilis corporis, erit adhuc in potentia ad formam corruptibilis corporis. Et cum non habeat eam in actu, erit simul sub forma et privatione, quia carentia formae in eo quod est in potentia ad formam, est privatio. Haec autem dispositio est corruptibilis corporis. Impossibile ergo est quod corporis corruptibilis et incorruptibilis per naturam, sit una materia. Nec tamen dicendum est, ut Averroes fingit, quod ipsum corpus caeleste sit materia caeli, ens in potentia ad ubi et non ad esse; et forma eius est substantia separata quae unitur ei ut motor. Quia impossibile est ponere aliquod ens actu, quin vel ipsum totum sit actus et forma, vel habeat actum seu formam. Remota ergo per intellectum substantia separata quae ponitur motor, si corpus caeleste non est habens formam, quod est componi ex forma et subiecto formae, sequitur quod sit totum forma et actus. Omne autem tale est intellectum in actu; quod de corpore caelesti dici non potest, cum sit sensibile. Relinquitur ergo quod materia corporis caelestis, secundum se considerata, non est in potentia nisi ad formam quam habet. Nec refert ad propositum quaecumque sit illa, sive anima, sive aliquid aliud. Unde illa forma sic perficit illam materiam, quod nullo modo in ea remanet potentia ad esse, sed ad ubi tantum, ut Aristoteles dicit. Et sic non est eadem materia corporis caelestis et elementorum, nisi secundum analogiam, secundum quod conveniunt in ratione potentiae.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31210)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus sequitur in hoc opinionem Platonis, non ponentis quintam essentiam. Vel dicendum quod materia informis est una unitate ordinis, sicut omnia corpora sunt unum in ordine creaturae corporeae.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31211)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod si genus consideretur physice, corruptibilia et incorruptibilia non sunt in eodem genere, propter diversum modum potentiae in eis, ut dicitur X Metaphys. Secundum autem logicam considerationem, est unum genus omnium corporum, propter unam rationem corporeitatis.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31212)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod forma corporeitatis non est una in omnibus corporibus, cum non sit alia a formis quibus corpora distinguuntur, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31213)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, cum potentia dicatur ad actum, ens in potentia est diversum ex hoc ipso quod ordinatur ad diversum actum; sicut visus ad colorem, et auditus ad sonum. Unde ex hoc ipso materia caelestis corporis est alia a materia elementi, quia non est in potentia ad formam elementi.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 66 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31214)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod caelum Empyreum non sit concreatum materiae informi. Caelum enim Empyreum, si est aliquid, oportet quod sit corpus sensibile. Omne autem corpus sensibile est mobile. Caelum autem Empyreum non est mobile, quia motus eius deprehenderetur per motum alicuius corporis apparentis; quod minime apparet. Non ergo caelum Empyreum est aliquid materiae informi concreatum.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31215)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, in III de Trin., quod *inferiora corpora per superiora quodam ordine reguntur*. Si ergo caelum Empyreum est quoddam supremum corpus, oportet quod habeat aliquam influentiam in haec inferiora corpora. Sed hoc non videtur, praesertim si ponatur immobile, cum nullum corpus moveat nisi motum. Non est ergo caelum Empyreum materiae informi concreatum.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31216)\n\nSi dicatur quod caelum Empyreum est locus contemplationis, non ordinatum ad naturales effectus, contra, Augustinus dicit, in IV de Trin., quod *nos, secundum quod mente aliquid aeternum capimus, non in hoc mundo sumus*; ex quo patet quod contemplatio mentem supra corporalia elevat. Non ergo contemplationi locus corporeus deputatur.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31217)\n\nPraeterea, inter corpora caelestia invenitur aliquod corpus partim diaphanum et partim lucidum, scilicet caelum sidereum. Invenitur etiam aliquod caelum totum diaphanum, quod aliqui nominant caelum aqueum vel crystallinum. Si ergo est aliud superius caelum, oportet quod sit totum lucidum. Sed hoc esse non potest, quia sic continue aer illuminaretur, nec unquam nox esset. Non ergo caelum Empyreum materiae informi est concreatum.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31218)\n\nSed contra est quod Strabus dicit, quod cum dicitur, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram, caelum dicit non visibile firmamentum, sed Empyreum, idest igneum*.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31219)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod caelum Empyreum non invenitur positum nisi per auctoritates Strabi et Bedae, et iterum per auctoritatem Basilii. In cuius positione quantum ad aliquid conveniunt, scilicet quantum ad hoc quod sit locus beatorum. Dicit enim Strabus, et etiam Beda, quod statim factum Angelis est repletum. Basilius etiam dicit, in II Hexaem., *sicut damnati in tenebras ultimas abiguntur ita remuneratio pro dignis operibus restauratur in ea luce quae est extra mundum, ubi beati quietis domicilium sortientur*. Differunt tamen quantum ad rationem ponendi. Nam Strabus et Beda ponunt caelum Empyreum ea ratione, quia firmamentum, per quod caelum sidereum intelligunt, non in principio sed secunda die dicitur factum. Basilius vero ea ratione ponit, ne videatur simpliciter Deus opus suum a tenebris inchoasse; quod Manichaei calumniantur, Deum veteris testamenti Deum tenebrarum nominantes. Hae autem rationes non sunt multum cogentes. Nam quaestio de firmamento quod legitur factum in secunda die, aliter solvitur, ab Augustino et ab aliis sanctis. Quaestio autem de tenebris solvitur, secundum Augustinum, per hoc quod informitas (quae per tenebras significatur) non praecessit duratione formationem, sed origine. Secundum alios vero, cum tenebrae non sint creatura aliqua, sed privatio lucis, divinam sapientiam attestatur, ut ea quae produxit ex nihilo, primo in statu imperfectionis institueret, et postmodum ea perduceret ad perfectum. Potest autem convenientior ratio sumi ex ipsa conditione gloriae. Expectatur enim in futura remuneratione duplex gloria, scilicet spiritualis, et corporalis, non solum in corporibus humanis glorificandis, sed etiam in toto mundo innovando. Inchoata est autem spiritualis gloria ab ipso mundi principio in beatitudine Angelorum, quorum aequalitas sanctis promittitur. Unde conveniens fuit ut etiam a principio corporalis gloria inchoaretur in aliquo corpore, quod etiam a principio fuerit absque servitute corruptionis et mutabilitatis, et totaliter lucidum; sicut tota creatura corporalis expectatur post resurrectionem futura. Et ideo illud caelum dicitur Empyreum, idest igneum, non ab ardore, sed a splendore. Sciendum est autem quod Augustinus, X de Civ. Dei, dicit quod *Porphyrius discernebat a Daemonibus Angelos, ut aerea loca esse Daemonum, aetherea vero vel Empyrea diceret Angelorum*. Sed Porphyrius, tanquam Platonicus, caelum istud sidereum igneum esse existimabat, et ideo Empyreum nominabat; vel aethereum, secundum quod nomen aetheris sumitur ab inflammatione, et non secundum quod sumitur a velocitate motus, ut Aristoteles dicit. Quod pro tanto dictum sit, ne aliquis opinetur Augustinum caelum Empyreum posuisse sicut nunc ponitur a modernis.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31220)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod corpora sensibilia sunt mobilia secundum ipsum statum mundi, quia per motum creaturae corporalis procuratur electorum multiplicatio. Sed in ultima consummatione gloriae cessabit corporum motus. Et talem oportuit esse a principio dispositionem caeli Empyrei.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31221)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod satis probabile est quod caelum Empyreum, secundum quosdam, cum sit ordinatum ad statum gloriae, non habet influentiam in inferiora corpora, quae sunt sub alio ordine, utpote ordinata ad naturalem rerum decursum. Probabilius tamen videtur dicendum quod, sicut supremi Angeli, qui assistunt, habent influentiam super medios et ultimos, qui mittuntur, quamvis ipsi non mittantur, secundum Dionysium; ita caelum Empyreum habet influentiam super corpora quae moventur, licet ipsum non moveatur. Et propter hoc potest dici quod influit in primum caelum quod movetur, non aliquid transiens et adveniens per motum, sed aliquid fixum et stabile; puta virtutem continendi et causandi, vel aliquid huiusmodi ad dignitatem pertinens.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31222)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod locus corporeus deputatur contemplationi non propter necessitatem, sed propter congruitatem, ut exterior claritas interiori conveniat. Unde Basilius dicit quod *ministrator spiritus non poterat degere in tenebris; sed in luce et laetitia decentem sibi habitum possidebat*.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31223)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, sicut Basilius dicit in II Hexaem., *constat factum esse caelum rotunditate conclusum, habens corpus spissum et adeo validum, ut possit ea quae extrinsecus habentur, ab interioribus separare. Ob hoc necessario post se regionem relictam carentem luce constituit, utpote fulgore qui superradiabat excluso*. Sed quia corpus firmamenti, etsi sit solidum, est tamen diaphanum, quod lumen non impedit (ut patet per hoc, quod lumen stellarum videmus non obstantibus mediis caelis); potest aliter dici quod habet lucem caelum Empyreum non condensatam, ut radios emittat, sicut corpus solis, sed magis subtilem. Vel habet claritatem gloriae, quae non est conformis cum claritate naturali.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 66 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31224)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod tempus non sit concreatum materiae informi. Dicit enim Augustinus, XII Confess., ad Deum loquens, *duo reperio quae fecisti carentia temporibus*, scilicet materiam primam corporalem, et naturam angelicam. Non ergo tempus est concreatum materiae informi.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31225)\n\nPraeterea, tempus dividitur per diem et noctem. Sed a principio nec nox nec dies erat, sed postmodum, cum divisit Deus lucem a tenebris. Ergo a principio non erat tempus.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31226)\n\nPraeterea, tempus est numerus motus firmamenti, quod legitur factum secundo die. Ergo non a principio erat tempus.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31227)\n\nPraeterea, motus est prior tempore. Magis igitur deberet numerari inter primo creata motus, quam tempus.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31228)\n\nPraeterea, sicut tempus est mensura extrinseca, ita et locus. Non ergo magis debet computari inter primo creata tempus, quam locus.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31229)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, super Gen. ad Litt., quod spiritualis et corporalis creatura est creata in principio temporis.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31230)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod communiter dicitur quatuor esse primo creata, scilicet naturam angelicam, caelum Empyreum, materiam corporalem informem, et tempus. Sed attendendum est quod hoc dictum non procedit secundum Augustini opinionem. Augustinus enim ponit duo primo creata, scilicet naturam angelicam et materiam corporalem, nulla mentione facta de caelo Empyreo. Haec autem duo, scilicet natura angelica et materia informis, praecedunt formationem non duratione, sed natura. Et sicut natura praecedunt formationem, ita etiam et motum et tempus. Unde tempus non potest eis connumerari. Procedit autem praedicta connumeratio secundum opinionem aliorum sanctorum, ponentium quod informitas materiae duratione praecessit formationem, et tunc pro illa duratione necesse est ponere tempus aliquod; aliter enim mensura durationis accipi non posset.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31231)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus hoc dicit ea ratione qua natura angelica et materia informis praecedunt origine, seu natura, tempus.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31232)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod sicut, secundum alios sanctos, materia erat quodammodo informis, et postea fuit formata; ita tempus quodammodo fuit informe, et postmodum formatum, et distinctum per diem et noctem.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31233)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, si motus firmamenti non statim a principio incoepit, tunc tempus quod praecessit, non erat numerus motus firmamenti, sed cuiuscumque primi motus. Accidit enim tempori quod sit numerus motus firmamenti, inquantum hic motus est primus motuum, si autem esset alius motus primus, illius motus esset tempus mensura, quia omnia mensurantur primo sui generis. Oportet autem dicere statim a principio fuisse aliquem motum, ad minus secundum successionem conceptionum et affectionum in mente angelica. Motum autem non est intelligere sine tempore, cum nihil aliud sit tempus quam numerus prioris et posterioris in motu.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31234)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod inter primo creata computantur ea quae habent generalem habitudinem ad res. Et ideo computari debuit tempus, quod habet rationem communis mensurae, non autem motus, qui comparatur solum ad subiectum mobile.\n\n[I q. 66 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31235)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod locus intelligitur in caelo Empyreo omnia continente. Et quia locus est de permanentibus, concreatus est totus simul. Tempus autem, quod non est permanens, concreatum est in suo principio, sicut etiam modo nihil est accipere in actu de tempore nisi nunc.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0002","text":"[I q. 66 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31192)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de opere distinctionis. Et primo considerandum est de ordine creationis ad distinctionem; secundo, de ipsa distinctione secundum se. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum informitas materiae creatae praecesserit tempore distinctionem ipsius. Secundo, utrum sit una materia omnium corporalium. Tertio, utrum caelum Empyreum sit concreatum materiae informi. Quarto, utrum tempus sit eidem concreatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0005","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31193)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod informitas materiae tempore praecesserit formationem ipsius. Dicitur enim Gen. 1, *terra erat inanis et vacua*, sive invisibilis et incomposita, secundum aliam litteram; per quod designatur informitas materiae, ut Augustinus dicit. Ergo materia fuit aliquando informis, antequam formaretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0007","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31194)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0008","text":"Praeterea, natura in sua operatione Dei operationem imitatur; sicut causa secunda imitatur causam primam. Sed in operatione naturae informitas tempore praecedit formationem. Ergo et in operatione Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0009","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31195)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0010","text":"Praeterea, materia potior est accidente, quia materia est pars substantiae. Sed Deus potest facere quod accidens sit sine subiecto; ut patet in sacramento altaris. Ergo potuit facere quod materia esset sine forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0011","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31196)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0012","text":"Sed contra, imperfectio effectus attestatur imperfectioni agentis. Sed Deus est agens perfectissimum, unde de eo dicitur, Deut. XXXII, *Dei perfecta sunt opera*. Ergo opus ab eo creatum nunquam fuit informe.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0013","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31197)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0014","text":"Praeterea, creaturae corporalis formatio facta fuit per opus distinctionis. Distinctioni autem opponitur confusio, sicut et formationi informitas. Si ergo informitas praecessit tempore formationem materiae, sequitur a principio fuisse confusionem corporalis creaturae, quam antiqui vocaverunt chaos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0015","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31198)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa hoc sunt diversae opiniones sanctorum. Augustinus enim vult quod informitas materiae corporalis non praecesserit tempore formationem ipsius, sed solum origine vel ordine naturae. Alii vero, ut Basilius, Ambrosius et Chrysostomus, volunt quod informitas materiae tempore praecesserit formationem. Et quamvis hae opiniones videantur esse contrariae, tamen parum ab invicem differunt, aliter enim accipit informitatem materiae Augustinus quam alii. Augustinus enim accipit informitatem materiae pro carentia omnis formae. Et sic impossibile est dicere quod informitas materiae tempore praecesserit vel formationem ipsius, vel distinctionem. Et de formatione quidem manifestum est. Si enim materia informis praecessit duratione, haec erat iam in actu, hoc enim duratio importat, creationis enim terminus est ens actu. Ipsum autem quod est actus, est forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0017","text":"Dicere igitur materiam praecedere sine forma, est dicere ens actu sine actu, quod implicat contradictionem. Nec etiam potest dici quod habuit aliquam formam communem et postmodum supervenerunt ei formae diversae, quibus sit distincta. Quia hoc esset idem cum opinione antiquorum naturalium, qui posuerunt materiam primam esse aliquod corpus in actu, puta ignem, aerem aut aquam, aut aliquod medium. Ex quo sequebatur quod fieri non esset nisi alterari. Quia cum illa forma praecedens daret esse in actu in genere substantiae, et faceret esse hoc aliquid; sequebatur quod superveniens forma non faceret simpliciter ens actu, sed ens actu hoc, quod est proprium formae accidentalis; et sic sequentes formae essent accidentia, secundum quae non attenditur generatio, sed alteratio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0018","text":"Unde oportet dicere quod materia prima neque fuit creata omnino sine forma, neque sub forma una communi, sed sub formis distinctis. Et ita, si informitas materiae referatur ad conditionem primae materiae, quae secundum se non habet aliquam formam, informitas materiae non praecessit formationem seu distinctionem ipsius tempore, ut Augustinus dicit, sed origine seu natura tantum, eo modo quo potentia est prior actu, et pars toto. Alii vero sancti accipiunt informitatem, non secundum quod excludit omnem formam, sed secundum quod excludit istam formositatem et decorem qui nunc apparet in corporea creatura. Et secundum hoc dicunt quod informitas materiae corporalis duratione praecessit formationem eiusdem. Et sic secundum hoc, quantum ad aliquid cum eis Augustinus concordat, et quantum aliquid discordat, ut infra patebit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0019","text":"Et quantum ex littera Genesis I, accipi potest, triplex formositas deerat, propter quod dicebatur creatura corporalis informis. Deerat enim a toto corpore diaphano, quod dicitur caelum, pulchritudo lucis, unde dicitur quod tenebrae erant super faciem abyssi. Deerat autem terrae duplex pulchritudo. Una, quam habet ex hoc quod est aquis discooperta, et quantum ad hoc dicitur quod terra erat inanis, sive invisibilis, quia corporali aspectui patere non poterat, propter aquas undique eam cooperientes. Alia vero, quam habet ex hoc quod est ornata herbis et plantis, et ideo dicitur quod erat vacua, vel incomposita, idest non ornata, secundum aliam litteram.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0020","text":"Et sic, cum praemisisset duas naturas creatas, scilicet caelum et terram, informitatem caeli expressit per hoc quod dixit, tenebrae erant super faciem abyssi, secundum quod sub caelo etiam aer includitur, informitatem vero terrae, per hoc quod dixit, terra erat inanis et vacua.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0021","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31199)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0022","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod terra aliter accipitur in loco isto ab Augustino, et ab aliis sanctis. Augustinus enim vult quod nomine terrae et aquae significetur in hoc loco ipsa materia prima. Non enim poterat Moyses rudi populo primam materiam exprimere, nisi sub similitudine rerum eis notarum. Unde et sub multiplici similitudine eam exprimit, non vocans eam tantum aquam vel tantum terram, ne videatur secundum rei veritatem materia prima esse vel terra vel aqua. Habet tamen similitudinem cum terra, inquantum subsidet formis; et cum aqua, inquantum est apta formari diversis formis. Secundum hoc ergo, dicitur terra inanis et vacua, vel invisibilis et incomposita, quia materia per formam cognoscitur (unde in se considerata dicitur invisibilis vel inanis), et eius potentia per formam repletur (unde et Plato materiam dicit esse locum).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0023","text":"Alii vero sancti per terram intelligunt ipsum elementum, quae qualiter, secundum eos, erat informis, dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0024","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31200)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0025","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod natura producit effectum in actu de ente in potentia, et ideo oportet ut in eius operatione potentia tempore praecedat actum, et informitas formationem. Sed Deus producit ens actu ex nihilo, et ideo statim potest producere rem perfectam, secundum magnitudinem suae virtutis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0026","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31201)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0027","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod accidens, cum sit forma, est actus quidam, materia autem secundum id quod est, est ens in potentia. Unde magis repugnat esse in actu materiae sine forma, quam accidenti sine subiecto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0028","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31202)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0029","text":"Ad primum vero quod obiicitur in contrarium, dicendum est quod si, secundum alios sanctos, informitas tempore praecessit formationem materiae, non fuit hoc ex impotentia Dei; sed ex eius sapientia, ut ordo servaretur in rerum conditione, dum ex imperfecto ad perfectum adducerentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0030","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31203)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0031","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod quidam antiquorum naturalium posuerunt confusionem excludentem omnem distinctionem; praeter hoc quod Anaxagoras posuit solum intellectum distinctum et immixtum. Sed ante opus distinctionis Scriptura sacra ponit multiplicem distinctionem. Primo quidem, caeli et terrae (in quo ostenditur distinctio etiam secundum materiam, ut infra patebit), et hoc cum dicit, *in principio Deus creavit caelum et terram*. Secundo, distinctionem elementorum quantum ad formas suas, per hoc quod nominat terram et aquam. Aerem autem et ignem non nominat, quia non est ita manifestum rudibus, quibus Moyses loquebatur, huiusmodi esse corpora, sicut manifestum est de terra et aqua.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0032","text":"Quamvis Plato aerem intellexerit significari per hoc quod dicitur spiritus domini (quia etiam aer spiritus dicitur), ignem vero intellexerit significari per caelum (quod igneae naturae esse dixit), ut Augustinus refert in VIII libro de Civ. Dei. Sed Rabbi Moyses, in aliis cum Platone concordans, dicit ignem significari per tenebras, quia, ut dicit, in propria sphaera ignis non lucet. Sed magis videtur esse conveniens quod prius dictum est, quia spiritus domini in Scriptura non nisi pro spiritu sancto consuevit poni. Qui aquis superferri dicitur, non corporaliter, sed sicut voluntas artificis superfertur materiae quam vult formare. Tertia distinctio significatur secundum situm. Quia terra erat sub aquis, quibus invisibilis reddebatur, aer vero, qui est subiectum tenebrarum, significatur fuisse super aquas, per hoc quod dicitur, tenebrae erant super faciem abyssi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0033","text":"Quid autem distinguendum remaneret, ex sequentibus apparebit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0034","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0035","text":"[I q. 66 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31204)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0036","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod una sit materia informis omnium corporalium. Dicit enim Augustinus, XII Confess., *duo reperio quae fecisti, unum quod erat formatum, alterum quod erat informe*; et hoc dicit esse terram invisibilem et incompositam, per quam dicit significari materiam rerum corporalium. Ergo una est materia omnium corporalium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0037","text":"[I q. 66 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31205)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0038","text":"Praeterea, philosophus dicit, in V Metaphys., quod illa quae sunt unum in genere, sunt unum in materia. Sed omnia corporalia conveniunt in genere corporis. Ergo omnium corporalium est una materia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0039","text":"[I q. 66 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31206)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0040","text":"Praeterea, diversus actus fit in diversa potentia, et unus in una. Sed omnium corporum est una forma, scilicet corporeitas. Ergo omnium corporalium est materia una.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0041","text":"[I q. 66 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31207)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0042","text":"Praeterea, materia in se considerata, est solum in potentia. Sed distinctio est per formas. Ergo materia in se considerata, est una tantum omnium corporalium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0043","text":"[I q. 66 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31208)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0044","text":"Sed contra, quaecumque conveniunt in materia, sunt transmutabilia ad invicem, et agunt et patiuntur ab invicem, ut dicitur in I de Gen. Sed corpora caelestia et inferiora non sic se habent ad invicem. Ergo eorum materia non est una.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0045","text":"[I q. 66 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31209)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0046","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa hoc fuerunt diversae opiniones philosophorum. Plato enim, et omnes philosophi ante Aristotelem, posuerunt omnia corpora esse de natura quatuor elementorum. Unde cum quatuor elementa communicent in una materia, ut mutua generatio et corruptio in eis ostendit; per consequens sequebatur quod omnium corporum sit materia una. Quod autem quaedam corpora sint incorruptibilia, Plato adscribebat non conditioni materiae, sed voluntati artificis, scilicet Dei, quem introducit corporibus caelestibus dicentem, *natura vestra estis dissolubilia, voluntate autem mea indissolubilia, quia voluntas mea maior est nexu vestro*. Hanc autem positionem Aristoteles reprobat, per motus naturales corporum. Cum enim corpus caeleste habeat naturalem motum diversum a naturali motu elementorum, sequitur quod eius natura sit alia a natura quatuor elementorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0047","text":"Et sicut motus circularis, qui est proprius corporis caelestis, caret contrarietate, motus autem elementorum sunt invicem contrarii, ut qui est sursum ei qui est deorsum, ita corpus caeleste est absque contrarietate, corpora vero elementaria sunt cum contrarietate. Et quia corruptio et generatio sunt ex contrariis, sequitur quod secundum suam naturam corpus caeleste sit incorruptibile, elementa vero sunt corruptibilia. Sed non obstante hac differentia corruptibilitatis et incorruptibilitatis naturalis, Avicebron posuit unam materiam omnium corporum, attendens ad unitatem formae corporalis. Sed si forma corporeitatis esset una forma per se, cui supervenirent aliae formae, quibus corpora distinguuntur, haberet necessitatem quod dicitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0048","text":"Quia illa forma immutabiliter materiae inhaereret, et quantum ad illam esset omne corpus incorruptibile, sed corruptio accideret per remotionem sequentium formarum, quae non esset corruptio simpliciter, sed secundum quid, quia privationi substerneretur aliquod ens actu. Sicut etiam accidebat antiquis naturalibus, qui ponebant subiectum corporum aliquod ens actu, puta ignem aut aerem aut aliquid huiusmodi. Supposito autem quod nulla forma quae sit in corpore corruptibili remaneat ut substrata generationi et corruptioni, sequitur de necessitate quod non sit eadem materia corporum corruptibilium et incorruptibilium. Materia enim, secundum id quod est, est in potentia ad formam. Oportet ergo quod materia, secundum se considerata, sit in potentia ad formam omnium illorum quorum est materia communis. Per unam autem formam non fit in actu nisi quantum ad illam formam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0049","text":"Remanet ergo in potentia quantum ad omnes alias formas. Nec hoc excluditur, si una illarum formarum sit perfectior et continens in se virtute alias. Quia potentia, quantum est de se, indifferenter se habet ad perfectum et imperfectum, unde sicut quando est sub forma imperfecta, est in potentia ad formam perfectam, ita e converso. Sic ergo materia, secundum quod est sub forma incorruptibilis corporis, erit adhuc in potentia ad formam corruptibilis corporis. Et cum non habeat eam in actu, erit simul sub forma et privatione, quia carentia formae in eo quod est in potentia ad formam, est privatio. Haec autem dispositio est corruptibilis corporis. Impossibile ergo est quod corporis corruptibilis et incorruptibilis per naturam, sit una materia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0050","text":"Nec tamen dicendum est, ut Averroes fingit, quod ipsum corpus caeleste sit materia caeli, ens in potentia ad ubi et non ad esse; et forma eius est substantia separata quae unitur ei ut motor. Quia impossibile est ponere aliquod ens actu, quin vel ipsum totum sit actus et forma, vel habeat actum seu formam. Remota ergo per intellectum substantia separata quae ponitur motor, si corpus caeleste non est habens formam, quod est componi ex forma et subiecto formae, sequitur quod sit totum forma et actus. Omne autem tale est intellectum in actu; quod de corpore caelesti dici non potest, cum sit sensibile. Relinquitur ergo quod materia corporis caelestis, secundum se considerata, non est in potentia nisi ad formam quam habet. Nec refert ad propositum quaecumque sit illa, sive anima, sive aliquid aliud.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0051","text":"Unde illa forma sic perficit illam materiam, quod nullo modo in ea remanet potentia ad esse, sed ad ubi tantum, ut Aristoteles dicit. Et sic non est eadem materia corporis caelestis et elementorum, nisi secundum analogiam, secundum quod conveniunt in ratione potentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0052","text":"[I q. 66 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31210)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0053","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus sequitur in hoc opinionem Platonis, non ponentis quintam essentiam. Vel dicendum quod materia informis est una unitate ordinis, sicut omnia corpora sunt unum in ordine creaturae corporeae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0054","text":"[I q. 66 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31211)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0055","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod si genus consideretur physice, corruptibilia et incorruptibilia non sunt in eodem genere, propter diversum modum potentiae in eis, ut dicitur X Metaphys. Secundum autem logicam considerationem, est unum genus omnium corporum, propter unam rationem corporeitatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0056","text":"[I q. 66 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31212)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0057","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod forma corporeitatis non est una in omnibus corporibus, cum non sit alia a formis quibus corpora distinguuntur, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0058","text":"[I q. 66 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31213)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0059","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, cum potentia dicatur ad actum, ens in potentia est diversum ex hoc ipso quod ordinatur ad diversum actum; sicut visus ad colorem, et auditus ad sonum. Unde ex hoc ipso materia caelestis corporis est alia a materia elementi, quia non est in potentia ad formam elementi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0060","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0061","text":"[I q. 66 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31214)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0062","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod caelum Empyreum non sit concreatum materiae informi. Caelum enim Empyreum, si est aliquid, oportet quod sit corpus sensibile. Omne autem corpus sensibile est mobile. Caelum autem Empyreum non est mobile, quia motus eius deprehenderetur per motum alicuius corporis apparentis; quod minime apparet. Non ergo caelum Empyreum est aliquid materiae informi concreatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0063","text":"[I q. 66 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31215)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0064","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, in III de Trin., quod *inferiora corpora per superiora quodam ordine reguntur*. Si ergo caelum Empyreum est quoddam supremum corpus, oportet quod habeat aliquam influentiam in haec inferiora corpora. Sed hoc non videtur, praesertim si ponatur immobile, cum nullum corpus moveat nisi motum. Non est ergo caelum Empyreum materiae informi concreatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0065","text":"[I q. 66 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31216)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0066","text":"Si dicatur quod caelum Empyreum est locus contemplationis, non ordinatum ad naturales effectus, contra, Augustinus dicit, in IV de Trin., quod *nos, secundum quod mente aliquid aeternum capimus, non in hoc mundo sumus*; ex quo patet quod contemplatio mentem supra corporalia elevat. Non ergo contemplationi locus corporeus deputatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0067","text":"[I q. 66 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31217)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0068","text":"Praeterea, inter corpora caelestia invenitur aliquod corpus partim diaphanum et partim lucidum, scilicet caelum sidereum. Invenitur etiam aliquod caelum totum diaphanum, quod aliqui nominant caelum aqueum vel crystallinum. Si ergo est aliud superius caelum, oportet quod sit totum lucidum. Sed hoc esse non potest, quia sic continue aer illuminaretur, nec unquam nox esset. Non ergo caelum Empyreum materiae informi est concreatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0069","text":"[I q. 66 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31218)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0070","text":"Sed contra est quod Strabus dicit, quod cum dicitur, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram, caelum dicit non visibile firmamentum, sed Empyreum, idest igneum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0071","text":"[I q. 66 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31219)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0072","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod caelum Empyreum non invenitur positum nisi per auctoritates Strabi et Bedae, et iterum per auctoritatem Basilii. In cuius positione quantum ad aliquid conveniunt, scilicet quantum ad hoc quod sit locus beatorum. Dicit enim Strabus, et etiam Beda, quod statim factum Angelis est repletum. Basilius etiam dicit, in II Hexaem., *sicut damnati in tenebras ultimas abiguntur ita remuneratio pro dignis operibus restauratur in ea luce quae est extra mundum, ubi beati quietis domicilium sortientur*. Differunt tamen quantum ad rationem ponendi. Nam Strabus et Beda ponunt caelum Empyreum ea ratione, quia firmamentum, per quod caelum sidereum intelligunt, non in principio sed secunda die dicitur factum. Basilius vero ea ratione ponit, ne videatur simpliciter Deus opus suum a tenebris inchoasse; quod Manichaei calumniantur, Deum veteris testamenti Deum tenebrarum nominantes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0073","text":"Hae autem rationes non sunt multum cogentes. Nam quaestio de firmamento quod legitur factum in secunda die, aliter solvitur, ab Augustino et ab aliis sanctis. Quaestio autem de tenebris solvitur, secundum Augustinum, per hoc quod informitas (quae per tenebras significatur) non praecessit duratione formationem, sed origine. Secundum alios vero, cum tenebrae non sint creatura aliqua, sed privatio lucis, divinam sapientiam attestatur, ut ea quae produxit ex nihilo, primo in statu imperfectionis institueret, et postmodum ea perduceret ad perfectum. Potest autem convenientior ratio sumi ex ipsa conditione gloriae. Expectatur enim in futura remuneratione duplex gloria, scilicet spiritualis, et corporalis, non solum in corporibus humanis glorificandis, sed etiam in toto mundo innovando.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0074","text":"Inchoata est autem spiritualis gloria ab ipso mundi principio in beatitudine Angelorum, quorum aequalitas sanctis promittitur. Unde conveniens fuit ut etiam a principio corporalis gloria inchoaretur in aliquo corpore, quod etiam a principio fuerit absque servitute corruptionis et mutabilitatis, et totaliter lucidum; sicut tota creatura corporalis expectatur post resurrectionem futura. Et ideo illud caelum dicitur Empyreum, idest igneum, non ab ardore, sed a splendore. Sciendum est autem quod Augustinus, X de Civ. Dei, dicit quod *Porphyrius discernebat a Daemonibus Angelos, ut aerea loca esse Daemonum, aetherea vero vel Empyrea diceret Angelorum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0075","text":"Sed Porphyrius, tanquam Platonicus, caelum istud sidereum igneum esse existimabat, et ideo Empyreum nominabat; vel aethereum, secundum quod nomen aetheris sumitur ab inflammatione, et non secundum quod sumitur a velocitate motus, ut Aristoteles dicit. Quod pro tanto dictum sit, ne aliquis opinetur Augustinum caelum Empyreum posuisse sicut nunc ponitur a modernis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0076","text":"[I q. 66 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31220)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0077","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod corpora sensibilia sunt mobilia secundum ipsum statum mundi, quia per motum creaturae corporalis procuratur electorum multiplicatio. Sed in ultima consummatione gloriae cessabit corporum motus. Et talem oportuit esse a principio dispositionem caeli Empyrei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0078","text":"[I q. 66 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31221)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0079","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod satis probabile est quod caelum Empyreum, secundum quosdam, cum sit ordinatum ad statum gloriae, non habet influentiam in inferiora corpora, quae sunt sub alio ordine, utpote ordinata ad naturalem rerum decursum. Probabilius tamen videtur dicendum quod, sicut supremi Angeli, qui assistunt, habent influentiam super medios et ultimos, qui mittuntur, quamvis ipsi non mittantur, secundum Dionysium; ita caelum Empyreum habet influentiam super corpora quae moventur, licet ipsum non moveatur. Et propter hoc potest dici quod influit in primum caelum quod movetur, non aliquid transiens et adveniens per motum, sed aliquid fixum et stabile; puta virtutem continendi et causandi, vel aliquid huiusmodi ad dignitatem pertinens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0080","text":"[I q. 66 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31222)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0081","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod locus corporeus deputatur contemplationi non propter necessitatem, sed propter congruitatem, ut exterior claritas interiori conveniat. Unde Basilius dicit quod *ministrator spiritus non poterat degere in tenebris; sed in luce et laetitia decentem sibi habitum possidebat*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0082","text":"[I q. 66 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31223)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0083","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, sicut Basilius dicit in II Hexaem., *constat factum esse caelum rotunditate conclusum, habens corpus spissum et adeo validum, ut possit ea quae extrinsecus habentur, ab interioribus separare. Ob hoc necessario post se regionem relictam carentem luce constituit, utpote fulgore qui superradiabat excluso*. Sed quia corpus firmamenti, etsi sit solidum, est tamen diaphanum, quod lumen non impedit (ut patet per hoc, quod lumen stellarum videmus non obstantibus mediis caelis); potest aliter dici quod habet lucem caelum Empyreum non condensatam, ut radios emittat, sicut corpus solis, sed magis subtilem. Vel habet claritatem gloriae, quae non est conformis cum claritate naturali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0084","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0085","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31224)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0086","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod tempus non sit concreatum materiae informi. Dicit enim Augustinus, XII Confess., ad Deum loquens, *duo reperio quae fecisti carentia temporibus*, scilicet materiam primam corporalem, et naturam angelicam. Non ergo tempus est concreatum materiae informi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0087","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31225)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0088","text":"Praeterea, tempus dividitur per diem et noctem. Sed a principio nec nox nec dies erat, sed postmodum, cum divisit Deus lucem a tenebris. Ergo a principio non erat tempus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0089","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31226)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0090","text":"Praeterea, tempus est numerus motus firmamenti, quod legitur factum secundo die. Ergo non a principio erat tempus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0091","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31227)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0092","text":"Praeterea, motus est prior tempore. Magis igitur deberet numerari inter primo creata motus, quam tempus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0093","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31228)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0094","text":"Praeterea, sicut tempus est mensura extrinseca, ita et locus. Non ergo magis debet computari inter primo creata tempus, quam locus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0095","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31229)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0096","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, super Gen. ad Litt., quod spiritualis et corporalis creatura est creata in principio temporis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0097","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31230)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0098","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod communiter dicitur quatuor esse primo creata, scilicet naturam angelicam, caelum Empyreum, materiam corporalem informem, et tempus. Sed attendendum est quod hoc dictum non procedit secundum Augustini opinionem. Augustinus enim ponit duo primo creata, scilicet naturam angelicam et materiam corporalem, nulla mentione facta de caelo Empyreo. Haec autem duo, scilicet natura angelica et materia informis, praecedunt formationem non duratione, sed natura. Et sicut natura praecedunt formationem, ita etiam et motum et tempus. Unde tempus non potest eis connumerari. Procedit autem praedicta connumeratio secundum opinionem aliorum sanctorum, ponentium quod informitas materiae duratione praecessit formationem, et tunc pro illa duratione necesse est ponere tempus aliquod; aliter enim mensura durationis accipi non posset.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0099","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31231)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0100","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus hoc dicit ea ratione qua natura angelica et materia informis praecedunt origine, seu natura, tempus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0101","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31232)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0102","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod sicut, secundum alios sanctos, materia erat quodammodo informis, et postea fuit formata; ita tempus quodammodo fuit informe, et postmodum formatum, et distinctum per diem et noctem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0103","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31233)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0104","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, si motus firmamenti non statim a principio incoepit, tunc tempus quod praecessit, non erat numerus motus firmamenti, sed cuiuscumque primi motus. Accidit enim tempori quod sit numerus motus firmamenti, inquantum hic motus est primus motuum, si autem esset alius motus primus, illius motus esset tempus mensura, quia omnia mensurantur primo sui generis. Oportet autem dicere statim a principio fuisse aliquem motum, ad minus secundum successionem conceptionum et affectionum in mente angelica. Motum autem non est intelligere sine tempore, cum nihil aliud sit tempus quam numerus prioris et posterioris in motu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0105","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31234)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0106","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod inter primo creata computantur ea quae habent generalem habitudinem ad res. Et ideo computari debuit tempus, quod habet rationem communis mensurae, non autem motus, qui comparatur solum ad subiectum mobile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0107","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31235)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0108","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod locus intelligitur in caelo Empyreo omnia continente. Et quia locus est de permanentibus, concreatus est totus simul. Tempus autem, quod non est permanens, concreatum est in suo principio, sicut etiam modo nihil est accipere in actu de tempore nisi nunc.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"221acd4387b9ade9cc98d0fc84381afdbde0e8ddfa0f6552cb7c25cf779890da","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T145345733513Z_26f9cb8d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 66","source_sha256":"221acd4387b9ade9cc98d0fc84381afdbde0e8ddfa0f6552cb7c25cf779890da","sha256":"e1c6427ed868ee50d2b60de22d5ec0b8f3351c337c0e58aba8d3fcdbfda16f54","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:53:45.733552+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0002","text":"[I q. 66 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31192)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the work of distinction. First, the order of creation with respect to distinction must be considered; second, distinction itself in its own right. Concerning the first, four questions are asked. First, whether the formlessness of created matter preceded its distinction in time. Second, whether there is one matter of all corporeal things. Third, whether the Empyrean heaven was created together with formless matter. Fourth, whether time was created together with it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0005","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31193)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the formlessness of matter preceded its formation in time. For it is said in Gen. 1, *the earth was void and empty*, or invisible and uncomposed, according to another reading; by which the formlessness of matter is signified, as Augustine says. Therefore matter was at some time formless before it was formed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0007","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31194)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0008","text":"Further, nature in its operation imitates the operation of God, just as a secondary cause imitates the first cause. But in the operation of nature, formlessness precedes formation in time. Therefore this is so also in the operation of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0009","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31195)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0010","text":"Further, matter is more excellent than an accident, since matter is a part of substance. But God can bring it about that an accident exists without a subject, as is evident in the sacrament of the altar. Therefore He could bring it about that matter existed without form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0011","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31196)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0012","text":"On the contrary, the imperfection of an effect bears witness to the imperfection of the agent. But God is a most perfect agent; hence it is said of Him, Deut. XXXII, *the works of God are perfect*. Therefore a work created by Him was never formless.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0013","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31197)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0014","text":"Further, the formation of corporeal creature was effected through the work of distinction. But confusion is opposed to distinction, just as formlessness is opposed to formation. Therefore, if formlessness preceded the formation of matter in time, it follows that at the beginning there was a confusion of corporeal creature, which the ancients called chaos.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0015","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31198)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0016","text":"I answer that it must be said that there are diverse opinions of the saints concerning this matter. For Augustine holds that the formlessness of corporeal matter did not precede its formation in time, but only in origin or in the order of nature. Others, however, such as Basil, Ambrose, and Chrysostom, hold that the formlessness of matter preceded its formation in time. And although these opinions seem to be contrary, they differ little from one another, for Augustine understands the formlessness of matter differently from the others. For Augustine understands the formlessness of matter as the lack of every form. And thus it is impossible to say that the formlessness of matter preceded either its formation or its distinction in time. This is manifest indeed concerning formation. For if formless matter preceded in duration, it was already in act; for this is what duration implies, since the terminus of creation is a being in act. But that which is act is form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0017","text":"Therefore, to say that matter precedes without form is to say that a being in act is without act, which involves a contradiction. Nor can it be said that it had some common form and that afterwards diverse forms supervened upon it, by which it was distinguished. For this would be the same as the opinion of the ancient natural philosophers, who held that prime matter was some body in act, for example fire, air, or water, or some intermediate body. From this it followed that coming-to-be was nothing but alteration. For since that preceding form gave being in act in the genus of substance, and made it to be this something, it followed that the supervening form did not make a being simply in act, but a being in act as this, which is proper to accidental form; and thus the subsequent forms would be accidents, according to which generation is not considered, but alteration.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0018","text":"Hence it must be said that prime matter was neither created entirely without form nor under one common form, but under distinct forms. And thus, if the formlessness of matter is referred to the condition of prime matter, which of itself has no form, the formlessness of matter did not precede its formation or distinction in time, as Augustine says, but only in origin or nature, in the way in which potency is prior to act, and a part to the whole. The other saints, however, understand formlessness not insofar as it excludes every form, but insofar as it excludes that comeliness and beauty which now appears in corporeal creature. And according to this, they say that the formlessness of corporeal matter preceded its formation in duration. And so, according to this, Augustine agrees with them in one respect and disagrees with them in another, as will become clear below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0019","text":"And, insofar as can be gathered from the letter of Genesis I, a threefold beauty was lacking, on account of which corporeal creature was said to be formless. For from the whole transparent body, which is called heaven, the beauty of light was lacking; hence it is said that darkness was over the face of the abyss. But the earth lacked a twofold beauty. One, which it has from being uncovered by the waters; and in this respect it is said that the earth was void, or invisible, because it could not lie open to bodily sight, on account of the waters covering it on every side. The other, which it has from being adorned with herbs and plants; and therefore it is said that it was empty, or uncomposed, that is, unadorned, according to another reading.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0020","text":"And so, having premised two created natures, namely heaven and earth, he expressed the formlessness of heaven by saying, darkness was over the face of the abyss, insofar as air too is included under heaven; but the formlessness of earth by saying, the earth was void and empty.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0021","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31199)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0022","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that earth is understood differently in this passage by Augustine and by the other saints. For Augustine holds that by the names earth and water, prime matter itself is signified in this passage. For Moses could not express prime matter to a rude people except under the likeness of things known to them. Hence he also expresses it under a manifold likeness, not calling it only water or only earth, lest it seem that, according to the truth of the thing, prime matter is either earth or water. Yet it has a likeness to earth insofar as it underlies forms, and to water insofar as it is suited to be formed by diverse forms. According to this, therefore, earth is said to be void and empty, or invisible and uncomposed, because matter is known through form (whence, considered in itself, it is said to be invisible or void), and its potency is filled through form (whence Plato also says that matter is place).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0023","text":"But the other saints understand by earth the element itself, which, in what way it was formless according to them, has been stated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0024","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31200)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0025","text":"To the second, it must be said that nature produces an effect in act from being in potency, and therefore it is necessary that, in its operation, potency temporally precede act, and formlessness formation. But God produces being in act from nothing, and therefore can at once produce a thing perfect, according to the magnitude of His power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0026","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31201)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0027","text":"To the third, it must be said that an accident, since it is a form, is a certain act; but matter, according to what it is, is being in potency. Hence it is more repugnant for matter to be in act without form than for an accident to be without a subject.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0028","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31202)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0029","text":"But to the first objection made to the contrary, it must be said that if, according to other saints, formlessness temporally preceded the formation of matter, this was not from God's impotence, but from His wisdom, so that order might be preserved in the production of things, while they were brought from the imperfect to the perfect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0030","text":"[I q. 66 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31203)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0031","text":"To the second, it must be said that some of the ancient natural philosophers posited a confusion excluding every distinction, except that Anaxagoras posited intellect alone as distinct and unmixed. But before the work of distinction, Sacred Scripture posits a multiple distinction. First, indeed, of heaven and earth (wherein a distinction even according to matter is shown, as will appear below), and this when it says, *in the beginning God created heaven and earth*. Second, a distinction of the elements as to their forms, by the fact that it names earth and water. But it does not name air and fire, because it is not so evident to the unlearned, to whom Moses was speaking, that such are bodies, as it is evident concerning earth and water.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0032","text":"Although Plato understood air to be signified by what is said, the spirit of the Lord (because air too is called spirit), and understood fire to be signified by heaven (which he said to be of a fiery nature), as Augustine reports in Book VIII of *The City of God*. But Rabbi Moses, agreeing with Plato in other matters, says that fire is signified by darkness, because, as he says, in its own sphere fire does not shine. But what was said first seems more fitting, because the spirit of the Lord in Scripture is customarily taken only for the Holy Spirit. He is said to be borne over the waters, not bodily, but as the will of an artisan is borne over the matter which he wishes to form. The third distinction is signified according to position. For the earth was beneath the waters, by which it was rendered invisible; but the air, which is the subject of darkness, is signified as having been above the waters, by what is said: darkness was upon the face of the abyss.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0033","text":"But what remained to be distinguished will appear from what follows.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0034","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0035","text":"[I q. 66 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31204)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0036","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that there is one formless matter of all bodily things. For Augustine says, in Book XII of the *Confessions*, *I find two things that You made, one that was formed, the other that was formless*; and he says that this is the invisible and unorganized earth, by which he says the matter of bodily things is signified. Therefore, the matter of all bodily things is one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0037","text":"[I q. 66 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31205)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0038","text":"Further, the Philosopher says, in Book V of the *Metaphysics*, that those things which are one in genus are one in matter. But all bodily things agree in the genus of body. Therefore, there is one matter of all bodily things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0039","text":"[I q. 66 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31206)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0040","text":"Further, a diverse act belongs to a diverse potency, and one act to one potency. But all bodies have one form, namely corporeity. Therefore, the matter of all bodily things is one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0041","text":"[I q. 66 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31207)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0042","text":"Further, matter considered in itself is only in potency. But distinction is by forms. Therefore, matter considered in itself is only one for all bodily things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0043","text":"[I q. 66 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31208)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0044","text":"On the contrary, whatever things agree in matter are mutually transformable, and act upon and are acted upon by one another, as is said in Book I of *On Generation*. But the celestial and lower bodies do not stand in such a relation to one another. Therefore, their matter is not one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0045","text":"[I q. 66 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31209)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0046","text":"I answer that it must be said that concerning this there were diverse opinions among philosophers. For Plato, and all philosophers before Aristotle, held that all bodies are of the nature of the four elements. Hence, since the four elements share one matter, as mutual generation and corruption in them shows, it consequently followed that the matter of all bodies is one. But Plato ascribed the fact that certain bodies are incorruptible not to the condition of matter, but to the will of the maker, namely God, whom he introduces as saying to the celestial bodies: *by your nature you are dissoluble, but by my will indissoluble, because my will is greater than your bond*. Aristotle, however, disproves this position by means of the natural motions of bodies. For since a celestial body has a natural motion different from the natural motion of the elements, it follows that its nature is other than the nature of the four elements.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0047","text":"And just as circular motion, which is proper to a celestial body, lacks contrariety, whereas the motions of the elements are contrary to one another, as upward motion is contrary to downward motion, so the celestial body is without contrariety, whereas elemental bodies involve contrariety. And because corruption and generation arise from contraries, it follows that according to its nature a celestial body is incorruptible, whereas the elements are corruptible. But notwithstanding this difference of natural corruptibility and incorruptibility, Avicebron posited one matter for all bodies, attending to the unity of bodily form. But if the form of corporeity were one form per se, to which the other forms by which bodies are distinguished were added, what is said would necessarily follow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0048","text":"For that form would adhere immutably to matter, and with respect to it every body would be incorruptible; but corruption would occur through the removal of the subsequent forms. This would not be corruption simply speaking, but in a certain respect, because some being in act would underlie the privation. This also happened to the ancient natural philosophers, who posited as the subject of bodies some being in act, for example fire or air or something of this sort. But granted that no form which is in a corruptible body remains as a substrate to generation and corruption, it necessarily follows that the matter of corruptible and incorruptible bodies is not the same. For matter, according to what it is, is in potency to form. Therefore matter, considered in itself, must be in potency to the form of all those things of which it is common matter. But through one form it is made actual only with respect to that form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0049","text":"Therefore it remains in potency with respect to all other forms. Nor is this excluded if one of those forms is more perfect and contains the others within itself by its power. For potency, so far as it is in itself, is disposed indifferently toward the perfect and the imperfect; hence, just as when it is under an imperfect form it is in potency toward a perfect form, so conversely. Thus, therefore, matter, insofar as it is under the form of an incorruptible body, will still be in potency toward the form of a corruptible body. And since it does not have this in act, it will be simultaneously under form and privation, because the lack of a form in that which is in potency toward the form is privation. But this disposition belongs to a corruptible body. Therefore it is impossible that there be one matter of a body corruptible and incorruptible by nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0050","text":"Yet it must not be said, as Averroes imagines, that the heavenly body itself is the matter of heaven, being in potency to where and not to being; and that its form is a separate substance united to it as mover. For it is impossible to posit any being in act without either its whole self being act and form, or having act or form. Therefore, if by intellect the separate substance which is posited as mover is removed, and the heavenly body is not something having form—which is to be composed of form and the subject of form—it follows that it is wholly form and act. But everything of this kind is understood in act; and this cannot be said of the heavenly body, since it is sensible. It remains, therefore, that the matter of the heavenly body, considered in itself, is in potency only to the form which it has. And it makes no difference to the present matter what that form may be, whether soul or something else.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0051","text":"Hence that form so perfects that matter that there in no way remains in it a potency toward being, but only toward where, as Aristotle says. And thus the matter of the heavenly body and that of the elements is not the same, except according to analogy, insofar as they agree in the notion of potency.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0052","text":"[I q. 66 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31210)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0053","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that Augustine here follows Plato's opinion, which does not posit a fifth essence. Or it may be said that formless matter is one by unity of order, just as all bodies are one in the order of corporeal creaturehood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0054","text":"[I q. 66 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31211)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0055","text":"To the second, it must be said that if genus is considered physically, corruptible and incorruptible things are not in the same genus, on account of the different mode of potency in them, as is said in Metaphysics X. But according to logical consideration, there is one genus of all bodies, on account of the one notion of corporeity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0056","text":"[I q. 66 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31212)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0057","text":"To the third, it must be said that the form of corporeity is not one in all bodies, since it is not other than the forms by which bodies are distinguished, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0058","text":"[I q. 66 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31213)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0059","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, since potency is said in relation to act, a being in potency is made different precisely by being ordered to a different act; just as sight is ordered to color and hearing to sound. Hence the very fact that it is not in potency toward the form of an element makes the matter of the heavenly body different from the matter of an element.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0060","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0061","text":"[I q. 66 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31214)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0062","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that the Empyrean heaven was not created together with formless matter. For if the Empyrean heaven is anything, it must be a sensible body. But every sensible body is movable. Yet the Empyrean heaven is not movable, because its motion would be detected through the motion of some apparent body; and this in no way appears. Therefore the Empyrean heaven is not anything created together with formless matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0063","text":"[I q. 66 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31215)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0064","text":"Further, Augustine says, in On the Trinity III, that *the lower bodies are governed by the higher in a certain order*. Therefore, if the Empyrean heaven is some highest body, it must have some influence upon these lower bodies. But this does not appear, especially if it is posited as immovable, since no body moves unless it is moved. Therefore the Empyrean heaven was not created together with formless matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0065","text":"[I q. 66 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31216)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0066","text":"If it is said that the Empyrean heaven is a place of contemplation, not ordered to natural effects, on the contrary, Augustine says, in On the Trinity IV, that *we, insofar as we grasp something eternal with the mind, are not in this world*; from which it is clear that contemplation raises the mind above corporeal things. Therefore no corporeal place is assigned to contemplation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0067","text":"[I q. 66 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31217)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0068","text":"Further, among the heavenly bodies there is found a body partly transparent and partly luminous, namely, the starry heaven. There is also found a heaven wholly transparent, which some call the watery or crystalline heaven. Therefore, if there is another higher heaven, it must be wholly luminous. But this cannot be, because then the air would be continuously illuminated and there would never be night. Therefore the Empyrean heaven was not created together with formless matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0069","text":"[I q. 66 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31218)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0070","text":"But on the contrary is what Strabus says: when it is said, *In the beginning God created heaven and earth*, by heaven he means not the visible firmament, but the Empyrean, that is, the fiery.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0071","text":"[I q. 66 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31219)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0072","text":"I answer that the Empyrean heaven is found to be posited only on the authorities of Strabus and Bede, and again on the authority of Basil. In positing it, they agree in one respect, namely, that it is the place of the blessed. For Strabus, and likewise Bede, say that as soon as it was made, it was filled with angels. Basil also says, in the Hexaemeron II: *just as the damned are driven away into the outer darkness, so the recompense for worthy works is restored in that light which is outside the world, where the blessed shall obtain the dwelling-place of rest.* They differ, however, as to the reason for positing it. For Strabus and Bede posit the Empyrean heaven for this reason: because the firmament, by which they understand the starry heaven, is said to have been made not in the beginning but on the second day. Basil, however, posits it for this reason: lest it seem that God simply began His work from darkness; as the Manichees slanderously assert, calling the God of the Old Testament the god of darkness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0073","text":"But these arguments are not very compelling. For the question concerning the firmament, which is read to have been made on the second day, is otherwise resolved by Augustine and by other saints. The question concerning darkness, however, is resolved, according to Augustine, by the fact that formlessness (which is signified by darkness) did not precede formation in duration, but in origin. According to others, however, since darkness is not any creature, but a privation of light, it bears witness to divine wisdom that He first established in a state of imperfection those things which He produced from nothing, and afterwards brought them to perfection. But a more fitting reason can be taken from the very condition of glory. For in the future recompense a twofold glory is expected, namely, spiritual and bodily, not only in the human bodies to be glorified, but also in the whole world to be renewed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0074","text":"Now spiritual glory was begun from the very beginning of the world in the beatitude of the Angels, whose equality is promised to the saints. Hence it was fitting that, from the beginning, bodily glory also should be begun in some body which from the beginning was without the servitude of corruption and mutability, and wholly luminous; as the whole corporeal creation is expected to be after the resurrection. And therefore that heaven is called Empyrean, that is, fiery, not from heat, but from splendor. But it should be known that Augustine, in Book X of The City of God, says that *Porphyry distinguished Angels from Demons, so as to say that airy places belong to Demons, but ethereal or Empyrean places to Angels*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0075","text":"But Porphyry, as a Platonist, thought that this starry heaven was fiery, and therefore called it Empyrean; or ethereal, insofar as the name aether is taken from blazing, and not insofar as it is taken from the swiftness of motion, as Aristotle says. This has been said to this extent, lest anyone suppose that Augustine posited the Empyrean heaven as it is now posited by the moderns.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0076","text":"[I q. 66 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31220)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0077","text":"Therefore, to the first objection it must be said that sensible bodies are movable according to the present state of the world, because by the motion of corporeal creatures the multiplication of the elect is procured. But in the final consummation of glory, the motion of bodies will cease. And such ought to have been, from the beginning, the disposition of the Empyrean heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0078","text":"[I q. 66 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31221)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0079","text":"To the second it must be said that it is sufficiently probable that, according to some, the Empyrean heaven, since it is ordered to the state of glory, does not have influence upon the lower bodies, which are under another order, namely, as ordered to the natural course of things. Yet it seems more probable that one should say that, just as the highest Angels, who assist, have influence over the middle and lowest, who are sent, although they themselves are not sent, according to Dionysius; so the Empyrean heaven has influence over bodies which are moved, although it itself is not moved. And on this account it can be said that it influences the first heaven which is moved, not with something passing and coming upon it through motion, but with something fixed and stable; for example, a power of containing and causing, or something of this sort pertaining to dignity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0080","text":"[I q. 66 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31222)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0081","text":"To the third it must be said that a bodily place is assigned to contemplation not from necessity, but from fittingness, so that outward brightness may correspond to inward brightness. Hence Basil says that *the ministering spirit could not dwell in darkness; but in light and joy he possessed a habitation fitting to himself*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0082","text":"[I q. 66 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31223)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0083","text":"To the fourth it must be said that, as Basil says in Book II of the Hexaemeron, *it is evident that heaven was made enclosed in roundness, having a dense and so strong a body that it can separate those things which are held outside from those within. For this reason it necessarily established behind itself a region left devoid of light, the radiance which shone above being excluded*. But because the body of the firmament, although solid, is nevertheless transparent, and does not impede light (as is clear from the fact that we see the light of the stars despite the intervening heavens), it can be said otherwise that the Empyrean heaven has light not condensed so as to emit rays, as does the body of the sun, but more subtle. Or it has the brightness of glory, which is not conformed to natural brightness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0084","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0085","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31224)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0086","text":"To the fourth question one proceeds thus. It seems that time was not created together with formless matter. For Augustine says, in Book XII of the Confessions, speaking to God, *I find two things which You made lacking times*, namely, first bodily matter and the angelic nature. Therefore time was not created together with formless matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0087","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31225)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0088","text":"Further, time is divided by day and night. But in the beginning there was neither night nor day, but afterwards, when God divided the light from the darkness. Therefore in the beginning there was no time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0089","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31226)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0090","text":"Further, time is the number of the motion of the firmament, which is read to have been made on the second day. Therefore time was not in the beginning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0091","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31227)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0092","text":"Further, motion is prior to time. Therefore motion ought rather to be numbered among the things first created than time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0093","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31228)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0094","text":"Further, just as time is an extrinsic measure, so also is place. Therefore time ought not to be reckoned among the things first created more than place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0095","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31229)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0096","text":"But against this is what Augustine says, in On the Literal Meaning of Genesis, that spiritual and corporeal creature was created in the beginning of time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0097","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31230)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0098","text":"I answer that it is commonly said that four things were created first, namely, the angelic nature, the Empyrean heaven, formless corporeal matter, and time. But it should be noted that this statement does not proceed according to Augustine's opinion. For Augustine posits that two things were created first, namely, the angelic nature and corporeal matter, with no mention made of the Empyrean heaven. Now these two, namely the angelic nature and formless matter, precede formation not in duration, but by nature. And just as they precede formation by nature, so also motion and time. Hence time cannot be counted among them. But the aforesaid enumeration proceeds according to the opinion of other saints, who hold that the formlessness of matter preceded its formation in duration; and then, for that duration, it is necessary to posit some time, for otherwise no measure of duration could be taken.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0099","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31231)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0100","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that Augustine says this for the reason that the angelic nature and formless matter precede time by origin, or by nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0101","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31232)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0102","text":"To the second, it must be said that, just as, according to the other saints, matter was in some way formless and was afterwards formed, so time was in some way formless and was afterwards formed and distinguished by day and night.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0103","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31233)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0104","text":"To the third, it must be said that, if the motion of the firmament did not begin immediately from the beginning, then the time that preceded was not the number of the motion of the firmament, but of whatever first motion. For it is accidental to time that it be the number of the motion of the firmament, insofar as this motion is the first of motions; but if another motion were first, time would be the measure of that motion, because all things are measured by the first in their genus. Yet it must be said that there was some motion immediately from the beginning, at least according to the succession of conceptions and affections in the angelic mind. But motion cannot be understood without time, since time is nothing other than the number of before and after in motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0105","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31234)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0106","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that among the things first created are counted those that have a general relation to things. And therefore time, which has the character of a common measure, ought to have been counted, but not motion, which is related only to the mobile subject.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0107","text":"[I q. 66 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31235)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0108","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that place is understood in the all-containing Empyrean heaven. And because place is among permanent things, it was co-created all at once in its entirety. But time, which is not permanent, was co-created in its beginning, just as even now nothing can be taken as actual in time except the present instant.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_58:P0108"]}],"source_digest":"55f4e498895d496d5727cf7d8666e4b222375ccb5b6df2ade513e7c94ebee183","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 66"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 66","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59","title":"First Part · Question 67","order":68,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_59","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 67 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31236)\n\nConsequenter considerandum est de opere distinctionis secundum se et primo, de opere primae diei; secundo, de opere secundae diei; tertio, de opere tertiae. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum lux proprie in spiritualibus dici possit. Secundo, utrum lux corporalis sit corpus. Tertio, utrum sit qualitas. Quarto, utrum conveniens fuit prima die fieri lucem.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 67 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31237)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod lux proprie in spiritualibus dicatur. Dicit enim Augustinus, IV super Gen. ad Litt., quod in spiritualibus melior et certior lux est, et quod *Christus non sic dicitur lux quo modo lapis, sed illud proprie, hoc figurative*.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31238)\n\nPraeterea, Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., ponit lumen inter nomina intelligibilia Dei. Nomina autem intelligibilia proprie dicuntur in spiritualibus. Ergo lux proprie dicitur in spiritualibus.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31239)\n\nPraeterea, apostolus dicit, ad Ephes. V, *omne quod manifestatur, lumen est*. Sed manifestatio magis proprie est in spiritualibus quam in corporalibus. Ergo et lux.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31240)\n\nSed contra est quod Ambrosius, in libro de fide, ponit splendorem inter ea quae de Deo metaphorice dicuntur.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31241)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod de aliquo nomine dupliciter convenit loqui, uno modo, secundum primam eius impositionem; alio modo, secundum usum nominis. Sicut patet in nomine visionis, quod primo impositum est ad significandum actum sensus visus; sed propter dignitatem et certitudinem huius sensus, extensum est hoc nomen, secundum usum loquentium, ad omnem cognitionem aliorum sensuum (dicimus enim, vide quomodo sapit, vel quomodo redolet, vel quomodo est calidum); et ulterius etiam ad cognitionem intellectus, secundum illud Matth. V, *beati mundo corde, quoniam ipsi Deum videbunt*. Et similiter dicendum est de nomine lucis. Nam primo quidem est institutum ad significandum id quod facit manifestationem in sensu visus, postmodum autem extensum est ad significandum omne illud quod facit manifestationem secundum quamcumque cognitionem. Si ergo accipiatur nomen luminis secundum suam primam impositionem, metaphorice in spiritualibus dicitur, ut Ambrosius dicit. Si autem accipiatur secundum quod est in usu loquentium ad omnem manifestationem extensum, sic proprie in spiritualibus dicitur.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 1 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31242)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ad obiecta.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 67 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31243)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod lux sit corpus. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro de Lib. Arbit., quod *lux in corporibus primum tenet locum*. Ergo lux est corpus.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31244)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus dicit quod lumen est species ignis. Sed ignis est corpus. Ergo lumen est corpus.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31245)\n\nPraeterea, ferri, intersecari, et reflecti est proprie corporum, haec autem omnia attribuuntur lumini vel radio. Coniunguntur etiam diversi radii et separantur, ut Dionysius dicit, II cap. de Div. Nom., quod etiam videtur non nisi corporibus convenire posse. Ergo lumen est corpus.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31246)\n\nSed contra, duo corpora non possunt simul esse in eodem loco. Sed lumen est simul cum aere. Ergo lumen non est corpus.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31247)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod impossibile est lumen esse corpus. Quod quidem apparet tripliciter. Primo quidem, ex parte loci. Nam locus cuiuslibet corporis est alius a loco alterius corporis, nec est possibile, secundum naturam, duo corpora esse simul in eodem loco, qualiacumque corpora sint; quia contiguum requirit distinctionem in situ. Secundo, apparet idem ex ratione motus. Si enim lumen esset corpus, illuminatio esset motus localis corporis. Nullus autem motus localis corporis potest esse in instanti, quia omne quod movetur localiter, necesse est quod prius perveniat ad medium magnitudinis quam ad extremum. Illuminatio autem fit in instanti. Nec potest dici quod fiat in tempore imperceptibili. Quia in parvo spatio posset tempus latere, in magno autem spatio, puta ab oriente in occidentem, tempus latere non posset, statim enim cum sol est in puncto orientis, illuminatur totum hemisphaerium usque ad punctum oppositum. Est etiam aliud considerandum ex parte motus. Quia omne corpus habet motum naturalem determinatum, motus autem illuminationis est ad omnem partem, nec magis secundum circulum quam secundum rectitudinem. Unde manifestum est quod illuminatio non est motus localis alicuius corporis. Tertio, apparet idem ex parte generationis et corruptionis. Si enim lumen esset corpus, quando aer obtenebrescit per absentiam luminaris, sequeretur quod corpus luminis corrumperetur, et quod materia eius acciperet aliam formam. Quod non apparet, nisi aliquis dicat etiam tenebras esse corpus. Nec etiam apparet ex qua materia tantum corpus, quod replet medium hemisphaerium, quotidie generetur. Ridiculum est etiam dicere quod ad solam absentiam luminaris, tantum corpus corrumpatur. Si quis etiam dicat quod non corrumpitur, sed simul cum sole accedit et circumfertur, quid dici poterit de hoc, quod ad interpositionem alicuius corporis circa candelam, tota domus obscuratur? Nec videtur quod lumen congregetur circa candelam, quia non apparet ibi maior claritas post quam ante. Quia ergo omnia haec non solum rationi, sed sensui etiam repugnant, dicendum est quod impossibile est lumen esse corpus.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31248)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus accipit lucem pro corpore lucido in actu, scilicet pro igne, quod inter quatuor elementa nobilissimum est.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31249)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Aristoteles lumen nominat ignem in propria materia, sicut ignis in materia aerea dicitur flamma, et in materia terrea dicitur carbo. Non tamen est multum curandum de eis exemplis quae Aristoteles inducit in libris logicalibus, quia inducit ea ut probabilia secundum opinionem aliorum.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31250)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod omnia illa attribuuntur lumini metaphorice, sicut etiam possent attribui calori. Quia enim motus localis est naturaliter primus motuum, ut probatur in VIII Physic., utimur nominibus pertinentibus ad motum localem, in alteratione et in omnibus motibus, sicut etiam nomen distantiae derivatum est a loco ad omnia contraria, ut dicitur in X Metaphys.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 67 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31251)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod lux non sit qualitas. Omnis enim qualitas permanet in subiecto etiam postquam agens discesserit; sicut calor in aqua postquam removetur ab igne. Sed lumen non remanet in aere recedente luminari. Ergo lumen non est qualitas.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31252)\n\nPraeterea, omnis qualitas sensibilis habet contrarium; sicut calido contrariatur frigidum, et albo nigrum. Sed lumini nihil est contrarium, tenebra enim est privatio luminis. Ergo lumen non est qualitas sensibilis.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31253)\n\nPraeterea, causa est potior effectu. Sed lux caelestium corporum causat formas substantiales in istis inferioribus. Dat etiam esse spirituale coloribus, quia facit eos visibiles actu. Ergo lux non est aliqua qualitas sensibilis, sed magis substantialis forma, aut spiritualis.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31254)\n\nSed contra est quod Damascenus dicit, in libro I, quod lux est quaedam qualitas.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31255)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam dixerunt quod lumen in aere non habet esse naturale, sicut color in pariete; sed esse intentionale, sicut similitudo coloris in aere. Sed hoc non potest esse, propter duo. Primo quidem, quia lumen denominat aerem, fit enim aer luminosus in actu. Color vero non denominat ipsum, non enim dicitur aer coloratus. Secundo, quia lumen habet effectum in natura, quia per radios solis calefiunt corpora. Intentiones autem non causant transmutationes naturales. Alii vero dixerunt quod lux est forma substantialis solis. Sed hoc etiam apparet impossibile, propter duo. Primo quidem, quia nulla forma substantialis est per se sensibilis, quia quod quid est est obiectum intellectus, ut dicitur in III de anima. Lux autem est secundum se visibilis. Secundo, quia impossibile est ut id quod est forma substantialis in uno, sit forma accidentalis in alio, quia formae substantiali per se convenit constituere in specie; unde semper et in omnibus adest ei. Lux autem non est forma substantialis aeris, alioquin, ea recedente, corrumperetur. Unde non potest esse forma substantialis solis. Dicendum est ergo quod, sicut calor est qualitas activa consequens formam substantialem ignis, ita lux est qualitas activa consequens formam substantialem solis, vel cuiuscumque alterius corporis a se lucentis, si aliquod aliud tale est. Cuius signum est, quod radii diversarum stellarum habent diversos effectus, secundum diversas naturas corporum.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31256)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, cum qualitas consequatur formam substantialem, diversimode se habet subiectum ad receptionem qualitatis, sicut se habet ad receptionem formae. Cum enim materia perfecte recipit formam, firmiter stabilitur etiam qualitas consequens formam; sicut si aqua convertatur in ignem. Cum vero forma substantialis recipitur imperfecte, secundum inchoationem quandam, qualitas consequens manet quidem aliquandiu, sed non semper; sicut patet in aqua calefacta, quae redit ad suam naturam. Sed illuminatio non fit per aliquam transmutationem materiae ad susceptionem formae substantialis, ut fiat quasi inchoatio aliqua formae. Et ideo lumen non remanet nisi ad praesentiam agentis.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31257)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod accidit luci quod non habeat contrarium, inquantum est qualitas naturalis primi corporis alterantis, quod est a contrarietate elongatum.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31258)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut calor agit ad formam ignis quasi instrumentaliter in virtute formae substantialis, ita lumen agit quasi instrumentaliter in virtute corporum caelestium ad producendas formas substantiales, et ad hoc quod faciat colores visibiles actu, inquantum est qualitas primi corporis sensibilis.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 67 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31259)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod inconvenienter lucis productio in prima die ponatur. Est enim lux qualitas quaedam, ut dictum est. Qualitas autem, cum sit accidens, non habet rationem primi, sed magis rationem postremi. Non ergo prima die debet poni productio lucis.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31260)\n\nPraeterea, per lucem distinguitur nox a die. Hoc autem fit per solem, qui ponitur factus die quarta. Ergo non debuit poni productio lucis prima die.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31261)\n\nPraeterea, nox et dies fit per circularem motum corporis lucidi. Sed circularis motus est proprius firmamenti, quod legitur factum die secunda. Ergo non debuit poni in prima die productio lucis distinguentis noctem et diem.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31262)\n\nSi dicatur quod intelligitur de luce spirituali, contra, lux quae legitur facta prima die, facit distinctionem a tenebris. Sed non erant in principio spirituales tenebrae, quia etiam Daemones fuerunt a principio boni, ut supra dictum est. Non ergo prima die debuit poni productio lucis.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31263)\n\nSed contra, id sine quo non potest esse dies, oportuit fieri in prima die. Sed sine luce non potest esse dies. Ergo oportuit lucem fieri prima die.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31264)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod de productione lucis est duplex opinio. Augustino enim videtur quod non fuerit conveniens Moysen praetermisisse spiritualis creaturae productionem. Et ideo dicit quod, cum dicitur, in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram, per caelum intelligitur spiritualis natura adhuc informis, per terram autem intelligitur materia informis corporalis creaturae. Et quia natura spiritualis dignior est quam corporalis, fuit prius formanda. Formatio igitur spiritualis naturae significatur in productione lucis, ut intelligatur de luce spirituali formatio enim naturae spiritualis est per hoc quod illuminatur ut adhaereat verbo Dei. Aliis autem videtur quod sit praetermissa a Moyse productio spiritualis creaturae. Sed huius rationem diversimode assignant. Basilius enim dicit quod Moyses principium narrationis suae fecit a principio quod ad tempus pertinet sensibilium rerum; sed spiritualis natura, idest angelica, praetermittitur, quia fuit ante creata. Chrysostomus autem assignat aliam rationem. Quia Moyses loquebatur rudi populo, qui nihil nisi corporalia poterat capere; quem etiam ab idololatria revocare volebat. Assumpsissent autem idololatriae occasionem, si propositae fuissent eis aliquae substantiae supra omnes corporeas creaturas, eas enim reputassent deos, cum etiam proni essent ad hoc quod solem et lunam et stellas colerent tanquam deos; quod eis inhibetur Deut. IV. Praemissa autem fuerat Gen. I, circa creaturam corporalem multiplex informitas, una quidem in hoc quod dicebatur, terra erat inanis et vacua; alia vero in hoc quod dicebatur, tenebrae erant super faciem abyssi. Necessarium autem fuit ut informitas tenebrarum primo removeretur per lucis productionem, propter duo. Primo quidem, quia lux, ut dictum est, est qualitas primi corporis, unde secundum eam primo fuit mundus formandus. Secundo, propter communitatem lucis, communicant enim in ea inferiora corpora cum superioribus. Sicut autem in cognitione proceditur a communioribus, ita etiam in operatione, nam prius generatur vivum quam animal, et animal quam homo, ut dicitur in libro de Gener. Animal. Sic ergo oportuit ordinem divinae sapientiae manifestari, ut primo inter opera distinctionis produceretur lux, tanquam primi corporis forma, et tanquam communior. Basilius autem ponit tertiam rationem, quia per lucem omnia alia manifestantur. Potest et quarta ratio addi, quae in obiiciendo est tacta, quia dies non potest esse sine luce; unde oportuit in prima die fieri lucem.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31265)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, secundum opinionem quae ponit informitatem materiae duratione praecedere formationem, oportet dicere quod materia a principio fuerit creata sub formis substantialibus; postmodum vero fuerit formata secundum aliquas conditiones accidentales, inter quas primum locum obtinet lux.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31266)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod quidam dicunt lucem illam fuisse quandam nubem lucidam, quae postmodum, facto sole, in materiam praeiacentem rediit. Sed istud non est conveniens. Quia Scriptura in principio Genesis commemorat institutionem naturae, quae postmodum perseverat, unde non debet dici quod aliquid tunc factum fuerit, quod postmodum esse desierit. Et ideo alii dicunt quod illa nubes lucida adhuc remanet, et est coniuncta soli, ut ab eo discerni non possit. Sed secundum hoc, illa nubes superflua remaneret, nihil autem est vanum in operibus Dei. Et ideo alii dicunt quod ex illa nube formatum est corpus solis. Sed hoc etiam dici non potest, si ponatur corpus solis non esse de natura quatuor elementorum, sed esse incorruptibile per naturam quia secundum hoc, materia eius non potest esse sub alia forma. Et ideo est dicendum, ut Dionysius dicit IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod illa lux fuit lux solis, sed adhuc informis, quantum ad hoc, quod iam erat substantia solis, et habebat virtutem illuminativam in communi; sed postmodum data est ei specialis et determinata virtus ad particulares effectus. Et secundum hoc, in productione huius lucis distincta est lux a tenebris, quantum ad tria. Primo quidem, quantum ad causam, secundum quod in substantia solis erat causa luminis, in opacitate autem terrae causa tenebrarum. Secundo, quantum ad locum, quia in uno hemisphaerio erat lumen, in alio tenebrae. Tertio, quantum ad tempus, quia in eodem hemisphaerio secundum unam partem temporis erat lumen, secundum aliam tenebrae. Et hoc est quod dicitur, *lucem vocavit diem, et tenebras noctem*.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31267)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Basilius dicit lucem et tenebras tunc fuisse per emissionem et contractionem luminis, et non per motum. Sed contra hoc obiicit Augustinus quod nulla ratio esset huius vicissitudinis emittendi et retrahendi luminis; cum homines et animalia non essent, quorum usibus hoc deserviret. Et praeterea hoc non habet natura corporis lucidi, ut retrahat lumen in sui praesentia, sed miraculose potest hoc fieri, in prima autem institutione naturae non quaeritur miraculum, sed quid natura rerum habeat, ut Augustinus dicit. Et ideo dicendum est quod duplex est motus in caelo. Unus communis toti caelo, qui facit diem et noctem, et iste videtur institutus primo die. Alius autem est, qui diversificatur per diversa corpora; secundum quos motus fit diversitas dierum ad invicem, et mensium et annorum. Et ideo in prima die fit mentio de sola distinctione noctis et diei, quae fit per motum communem. In quarta autem die fit mentio de diversitate dierum et temporum et annorum, cum dicitur, *ut sint in tempora et dies et annos*; quae quidem diversitas fit per motus proprios.\n\n[I q. 67 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31268)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, informitas non praecedit formationem duratione. Unde oportet dicere quod per lucis productionem intelligatur formatio spiritualis creaturae non quae est per gloriam perfecta, cum qua creata non fuit; sed quae est per gratiam perfecta, cum qua creata fuit, ut dictum est. Per hanc ergo lucem facta est divisio a tenebris, idest ab informitate alterius creaturae non formatae. Vel, si tota creatura simul formata fuit, facta est distinctio a tenebris spiritualibus, non quae tunc essent (quia Diabolus non fuit creatus malus); sed quas Deus futuras praevidit.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0002","text":"[I q. 67 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31236)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0003","text":"Consequenter considerandum est de opere distinctionis secundum se et primo, de opere primae diei; secundo, de opere secundae diei; tertio, de opere tertiae. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum lux proprie in spiritualibus dici possit. Secundo, utrum lux corporalis sit corpus. Tertio, utrum sit qualitas. Quarto, utrum conveniens fuit prima die fieri lucem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0005","text":"[I q. 67 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31237)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod lux proprie in spiritualibus dicatur. Dicit enim Augustinus, IV super Gen. ad Litt., quod in spiritualibus melior et certior lux est, et quod *Christus non sic dicitur lux quo modo lapis, sed illud proprie, hoc figurative*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0007","text":"[I q. 67 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31238)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0008","text":"Praeterea, Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., ponit lumen inter nomina intelligibilia Dei. Nomina autem intelligibilia proprie dicuntur in spiritualibus. Ergo lux proprie dicitur in spiritualibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0009","text":"[I q. 67 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31239)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0010","text":"Praeterea, apostolus dicit, ad Ephes. V, *omne quod manifestatur, lumen est*. Sed manifestatio magis proprie est in spiritualibus quam in corporalibus. Ergo et lux.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0011","text":"[I q. 67 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31240)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod Ambrosius, in libro de fide, ponit splendorem inter ea quae de Deo metaphorice dicuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0013","text":"[I q. 67 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31241)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod de aliquo nomine dupliciter convenit loqui, uno modo, secundum primam eius impositionem; alio modo, secundum usum nominis. Sicut patet in nomine visionis, quod primo impositum est ad significandum actum sensus visus; sed propter dignitatem et certitudinem huius sensus, extensum est hoc nomen, secundum usum loquentium, ad omnem cognitionem aliorum sensuum (dicimus enim, vide quomodo sapit, vel quomodo redolet, vel quomodo est calidum); et ulterius etiam ad cognitionem intellectus, secundum illud Matth. V, *beati mundo corde, quoniam ipsi Deum videbunt*. Et similiter dicendum est de nomine lucis. Nam primo quidem est institutum ad significandum id quod facit manifestationem in sensu visus, postmodum autem extensum est ad significandum omne illud quod facit manifestationem secundum quamcumque cognitionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0015","text":"Si ergo accipiatur nomen luminis secundum suam primam impositionem, metaphorice in spiritualibus dicitur, ut Ambrosius dicit. Si autem accipiatur secundum quod est in usu loquentium ad omnem manifestationem extensum, sic proprie in spiritualibus dicitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0016","text":"[I q. 67 a. 1 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31242)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0017","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ad obiecta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0018","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0019","text":"[I q. 67 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31243)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0020","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod lux sit corpus. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro de Lib. Arbit., quod *lux in corporibus primum tenet locum*. Ergo lux est corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0021","text":"[I q. 67 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31244)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0022","text":"Praeterea, philosophus dicit quod lumen est species ignis. Sed ignis est corpus. Ergo lumen est corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0023","text":"[I q. 67 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31245)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0024","text":"Praeterea, ferri, intersecari, et reflecti est proprie corporum, haec autem omnia attribuuntur lumini vel radio. Coniunguntur etiam diversi radii et separantur, ut Dionysius dicit, II cap. de Div. Nom., quod etiam videtur non nisi corporibus convenire posse. Ergo lumen est corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0025","text":"[I q. 67 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31246)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0026","text":"Sed contra, duo corpora non possunt simul esse in eodem loco. Sed lumen est simul cum aere. Ergo lumen non est corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0027","text":"[I q. 67 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31247)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0028","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod impossibile est lumen esse corpus. Quod quidem apparet tripliciter. Primo quidem, ex parte loci. Nam locus cuiuslibet corporis est alius a loco alterius corporis, nec est possibile, secundum naturam, duo corpora esse simul in eodem loco, qualiacumque corpora sint; quia contiguum requirit distinctionem in situ. Secundo, apparet idem ex ratione motus. Si enim lumen esset corpus, illuminatio esset motus localis corporis. Nullus autem motus localis corporis potest esse in instanti, quia omne quod movetur localiter, necesse est quod prius perveniat ad medium magnitudinis quam ad extremum. Illuminatio autem fit in instanti. Nec potest dici quod fiat in tempore imperceptibili.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0029","text":"Quia in parvo spatio posset tempus latere, in magno autem spatio, puta ab oriente in occidentem, tempus latere non posset, statim enim cum sol est in puncto orientis, illuminatur totum hemisphaerium usque ad punctum oppositum. Est etiam aliud considerandum ex parte motus. Quia omne corpus habet motum naturalem determinatum, motus autem illuminationis est ad omnem partem, nec magis secundum circulum quam secundum rectitudinem. Unde manifestum est quod illuminatio non est motus localis alicuius corporis. Tertio, apparet idem ex parte generationis et corruptionis. Si enim lumen esset corpus, quando aer obtenebrescit per absentiam luminaris, sequeretur quod corpus luminis corrumperetur, et quod materia eius acciperet aliam formam. Quod non apparet, nisi aliquis dicat etiam tenebras esse corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0030","text":"Nec etiam apparet ex qua materia tantum corpus, quod replet medium hemisphaerium, quotidie generetur. Ridiculum est etiam dicere quod ad solam absentiam luminaris, tantum corpus corrumpatur. Si quis etiam dicat quod non corrumpitur, sed simul cum sole accedit et circumfertur, quid dici poterit de hoc, quod ad interpositionem alicuius corporis circa candelam, tota domus obscuratur? Nec videtur quod lumen congregetur circa candelam, quia non apparet ibi maior claritas post quam ante. Quia ergo omnia haec non solum rationi, sed sensui etiam repugnant, dicendum est quod impossibile est lumen esse corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0031","text":"[I q. 67 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31248)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0032","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus accipit lucem pro corpore lucido in actu, scilicet pro igne, quod inter quatuor elementa nobilissimum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0033","text":"[I q. 67 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31249)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0034","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Aristoteles lumen nominat ignem in propria materia, sicut ignis in materia aerea dicitur flamma, et in materia terrea dicitur carbo. Non tamen est multum curandum de eis exemplis quae Aristoteles inducit in libris logicalibus, quia inducit ea ut probabilia secundum opinionem aliorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0035","text":"[I q. 67 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31250)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0036","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod omnia illa attribuuntur lumini metaphorice, sicut etiam possent attribui calori. Quia enim motus localis est naturaliter primus motuum, ut probatur in VIII Physic., utimur nominibus pertinentibus ad motum localem, in alteratione et in omnibus motibus, sicut etiam nomen distantiae derivatum est a loco ad omnia contraria, ut dicitur in X Metaphys.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0037","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0038","text":"[I q. 67 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31251)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0039","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod lux non sit qualitas. Omnis enim qualitas permanet in subiecto etiam postquam agens discesserit; sicut calor in aqua postquam removetur ab igne. Sed lumen non remanet in aere recedente luminari. Ergo lumen non est qualitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0040","text":"[I q. 67 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31252)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0041","text":"Praeterea, omnis qualitas sensibilis habet contrarium; sicut calido contrariatur frigidum, et albo nigrum. Sed lumini nihil est contrarium, tenebra enim est privatio luminis. Ergo lumen non est qualitas sensibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0042","text":"[I q. 67 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31253)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0043","text":"Praeterea, causa est potior effectu. Sed lux caelestium corporum causat formas substantiales in istis inferioribus. Dat etiam esse spirituale coloribus, quia facit eos visibiles actu. Ergo lux non est aliqua qualitas sensibilis, sed magis substantialis forma, aut spiritualis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0044","text":"[I q. 67 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31254)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0045","text":"Sed contra est quod Damascenus dicit, in libro I, quod lux est quaedam qualitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0046","text":"[I q. 67 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31255)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0047","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam dixerunt quod lumen in aere non habet esse naturale, sicut color in pariete; sed esse intentionale, sicut similitudo coloris in aere. Sed hoc non potest esse, propter duo. Primo quidem, quia lumen denominat aerem, fit enim aer luminosus in actu. Color vero non denominat ipsum, non enim dicitur aer coloratus. Secundo, quia lumen habet effectum in natura, quia per radios solis calefiunt corpora. Intentiones autem non causant transmutationes naturales. Alii vero dixerunt quod lux est forma substantialis solis. Sed hoc etiam apparet impossibile, propter duo. Primo quidem, quia nulla forma substantialis est per se sensibilis, quia quod quid est est obiectum intellectus, ut dicitur in III de anima. Lux autem est secundum se visibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0048","text":"Secundo, quia impossibile est ut id quod est forma substantialis in uno, sit forma accidentalis in alio, quia formae substantiali per se convenit constituere in specie; unde semper et in omnibus adest ei. Lux autem non est forma substantialis aeris, alioquin, ea recedente, corrumperetur. Unde non potest esse forma substantialis solis. Dicendum est ergo quod, sicut calor est qualitas activa consequens formam substantialem ignis, ita lux est qualitas activa consequens formam substantialem solis, vel cuiuscumque alterius corporis a se lucentis, si aliquod aliud tale est. Cuius signum est, quod radii diversarum stellarum habent diversos effectus, secundum diversas naturas corporum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0049","text":"[I q. 67 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31256)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0050","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, cum qualitas consequatur formam substantialem, diversimode se habet subiectum ad receptionem qualitatis, sicut se habet ad receptionem formae. Cum enim materia perfecte recipit formam, firmiter stabilitur etiam qualitas consequens formam; sicut si aqua convertatur in ignem. Cum vero forma substantialis recipitur imperfecte, secundum inchoationem quandam, qualitas consequens manet quidem aliquandiu, sed non semper; sicut patet in aqua calefacta, quae redit ad suam naturam. Sed illuminatio non fit per aliquam transmutationem materiae ad susceptionem formae substantialis, ut fiat quasi inchoatio aliqua formae. Et ideo lumen non remanet nisi ad praesentiam agentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0051","text":"[I q. 67 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31257)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0052","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod accidit luci quod non habeat contrarium, inquantum est qualitas naturalis primi corporis alterantis, quod est a contrarietate elongatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0053","text":"[I q. 67 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31258)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0054","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut calor agit ad formam ignis quasi instrumentaliter in virtute formae substantialis, ita lumen agit quasi instrumentaliter in virtute corporum caelestium ad producendas formas substantiales, et ad hoc quod faciat colores visibiles actu, inquantum est qualitas primi corporis sensibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0055","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0056","text":"[I q. 67 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31259)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0057","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod inconvenienter lucis productio in prima die ponatur. Est enim lux qualitas quaedam, ut dictum est. Qualitas autem, cum sit accidens, non habet rationem primi, sed magis rationem postremi. Non ergo prima die debet poni productio lucis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0058","text":"[I q. 67 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31260)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0059","text":"Praeterea, per lucem distinguitur nox a die. Hoc autem fit per solem, qui ponitur factus die quarta. Ergo non debuit poni productio lucis prima die.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0060","text":"[I q. 67 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31261)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0061","text":"Praeterea, nox et dies fit per circularem motum corporis lucidi. Sed circularis motus est proprius firmamenti, quod legitur factum die secunda. Ergo non debuit poni in prima die productio lucis distinguentis noctem et diem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0062","text":"[I q. 67 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31262)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0063","text":"Si dicatur quod intelligitur de luce spirituali, contra, lux quae legitur facta prima die, facit distinctionem a tenebris. Sed non erant in principio spirituales tenebrae, quia etiam Daemones fuerunt a principio boni, ut supra dictum est. Non ergo prima die debuit poni productio lucis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0064","text":"[I q. 67 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31263)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0065","text":"Sed contra, id sine quo non potest esse dies, oportuit fieri in prima die. Sed sine luce non potest esse dies. Ergo oportuit lucem fieri prima die.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0066","text":"[I q. 67 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31264)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0067","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod de productione lucis est duplex opinio. Augustino enim videtur quod non fuerit conveniens Moysen praetermisisse spiritualis creaturae productionem. Et ideo dicit quod, cum dicitur, in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram, per caelum intelligitur spiritualis natura adhuc informis, per terram autem intelligitur materia informis corporalis creaturae. Et quia natura spiritualis dignior est quam corporalis, fuit prius formanda. Formatio igitur spiritualis naturae significatur in productione lucis, ut intelligatur de luce spirituali formatio enim naturae spiritualis est per hoc quod illuminatur ut adhaereat verbo Dei. Aliis autem videtur quod sit praetermissa a Moyse productio spiritualis creaturae. Sed huius rationem diversimode assignant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0068","text":"Basilius enim dicit quod Moyses principium narrationis suae fecit a principio quod ad tempus pertinet sensibilium rerum; sed spiritualis natura, idest angelica, praetermittitur, quia fuit ante creata. Chrysostomus autem assignat aliam rationem. Quia Moyses loquebatur rudi populo, qui nihil nisi corporalia poterat capere; quem etiam ab idololatria revocare volebat. Assumpsissent autem idololatriae occasionem, si propositae fuissent eis aliquae substantiae supra omnes corporeas creaturas, eas enim reputassent deos, cum etiam proni essent ad hoc quod solem et lunam et stellas colerent tanquam deos; quod eis inhibetur Deut. IV. Praemissa autem fuerat Gen. I, circa creaturam corporalem multiplex informitas, una quidem in hoc quod dicebatur, terra erat inanis et vacua; alia vero in hoc quod dicebatur, tenebrae erant super faciem abyssi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0069","text":"Necessarium autem fuit ut informitas tenebrarum primo removeretur per lucis productionem, propter duo. Primo quidem, quia lux, ut dictum est, est qualitas primi corporis, unde secundum eam primo fuit mundus formandus. Secundo, propter communitatem lucis, communicant enim in ea inferiora corpora cum superioribus. Sicut autem in cognitione proceditur a communioribus, ita etiam in operatione, nam prius generatur vivum quam animal, et animal quam homo, ut dicitur in libro de Gener. Animal. Sic ergo oportuit ordinem divinae sapientiae manifestari, ut primo inter opera distinctionis produceretur lux, tanquam primi corporis forma, et tanquam communior. Basilius autem ponit tertiam rationem, quia per lucem omnia alia manifestantur. Potest et quarta ratio addi, quae in obiiciendo est tacta, quia dies non potest esse sine luce; unde oportuit in prima die fieri lucem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0070","text":"[I q. 67 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31265)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0071","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, secundum opinionem quae ponit informitatem materiae duratione praecedere formationem, oportet dicere quod materia a principio fuerit creata sub formis substantialibus; postmodum vero fuerit formata secundum aliquas conditiones accidentales, inter quas primum locum obtinet lux.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0072","text":"[I q. 67 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31266)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0073","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod quidam dicunt lucem illam fuisse quandam nubem lucidam, quae postmodum, facto sole, in materiam praeiacentem rediit. Sed istud non est conveniens. Quia Scriptura in principio Genesis commemorat institutionem naturae, quae postmodum perseverat, unde non debet dici quod aliquid tunc factum fuerit, quod postmodum esse desierit. Et ideo alii dicunt quod illa nubes lucida adhuc remanet, et est coniuncta soli, ut ab eo discerni non possit. Sed secundum hoc, illa nubes superflua remaneret, nihil autem est vanum in operibus Dei. Et ideo alii dicunt quod ex illa nube formatum est corpus solis. Sed hoc etiam dici non potest, si ponatur corpus solis non esse de natura quatuor elementorum, sed esse incorruptibile per naturam quia secundum hoc, materia eius non potest esse sub alia forma. Et ideo est dicendum, ut Dionysius dicit IV cap. de Div.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0074","text":"Nom., quod illa lux fuit lux solis, sed adhuc informis, quantum ad hoc, quod iam erat substantia solis, et habebat virtutem illuminativam in communi; sed postmodum data est ei specialis et determinata virtus ad particulares effectus. Et secundum hoc, in productione huius lucis distincta est lux a tenebris, quantum ad tria. Primo quidem, quantum ad causam, secundum quod in substantia solis erat causa luminis, in opacitate autem terrae causa tenebrarum. Secundo, quantum ad locum, quia in uno hemisphaerio erat lumen, in alio tenebrae. Tertio, quantum ad tempus, quia in eodem hemisphaerio secundum unam partem temporis erat lumen, secundum aliam tenebrae. Et hoc est quod dicitur, *lucem vocavit diem, et tenebras noctem*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0075","text":"[I q. 67 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31267)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0076","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Basilius dicit lucem et tenebras tunc fuisse per emissionem et contractionem luminis, et non per motum. Sed contra hoc obiicit Augustinus quod nulla ratio esset huius vicissitudinis emittendi et retrahendi luminis; cum homines et animalia non essent, quorum usibus hoc deserviret. Et praeterea hoc non habet natura corporis lucidi, ut retrahat lumen in sui praesentia, sed miraculose potest hoc fieri, in prima autem institutione naturae non quaeritur miraculum, sed quid natura rerum habeat, ut Augustinus dicit. Et ideo dicendum est quod duplex est motus in caelo. Unus communis toti caelo, qui facit diem et noctem, et iste videtur institutus primo die. Alius autem est, qui diversificatur per diversa corpora; secundum quos motus fit diversitas dierum ad invicem, et mensium et annorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0077","text":"Et ideo in prima die fit mentio de sola distinctione noctis et diei, quae fit per motum communem. In quarta autem die fit mentio de diversitate dierum et temporum et annorum, cum dicitur, *ut sint in tempora et dies et annos*; quae quidem diversitas fit per motus proprios.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0078","text":"[I q. 67 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31268)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0079","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, informitas non praecedit formationem duratione. Unde oportet dicere quod per lucis productionem intelligatur formatio spiritualis creaturae non quae est per gloriam perfecta, cum qua creata non fuit; sed quae est per gratiam perfecta, cum qua creata fuit, ut dictum est. Per hanc ergo lucem facta est divisio a tenebris, idest ab informitate alterius creaturae non formatae. Vel, si tota creatura simul formata fuit, facta est distinctio a tenebris spiritualibus, non quae tunc essent (quia Diabolus non fuit creatus malus); sed quas Deus futuras praevidit.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"74fbd23a0cac40df843d123bf11bab7489a6d1de204c685b7429318140cd6539","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T145440344946Z_d5cbd490","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 67","source_sha256":"74fbd23a0cac40df843d123bf11bab7489a6d1de204c685b7429318140cd6539","sha256":"ac9a56efa52829f5de397a82c3676ecb4e03cd1ce958c8aa9734d0c020a3dbce","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:54:40.344969+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0002","text":"[I q. 67 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31236)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the work of distinction in itself: first, the work of the first day; second, the work of the second day; third, that of the third. Concerning the first, four questions are asked. First, whether light can properly be spoken of in spiritual things. Second, whether corporeal light is a body. Third, whether it is a quality. Fourth, whether it was fitting that light be made on the first day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0005","text":"[I q. 67 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31237)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that light is properly spoken of in spiritual things. For Augustine says, in Book IV of *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, that in spiritual things light is better and more certain, and that *Christ is not called light in the way a stone is, but the former properly, the latter figuratively*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0007","text":"[I q. 67 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31238)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0008","text":"Further, Dionysius, in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, places light among the intelligible names of God. But intelligible names are properly spoken of in spiritual things. Therefore light is properly spoken of in spiritual things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0009","text":"[I q. 67 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31239)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0010","text":"Further, the Apostle says, in Ephesians V, *everything that is manifested is light*. But manifestation is more properly in spiritual than in corporeal things. Therefore, so also is light.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0011","text":"[I q. 67 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31240)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0012","text":"On the contrary, Ambrose, in the book *On Faith*, places brightness among those things that are said of God metaphorically.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0013","text":"[I q. 67 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31241)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0014","text":"I answer that it is fitting to speak of a name in two ways: in one way, according to its first imposition; in another way, according to the use of the name. This is clear in the name “seeing,” which was first imposed to signify the act of the sense of sight; but because of the dignity and certainty of this sense, this name has been extended, according to common usage, to every cognition of the other senses (for we say, “See how it tastes,” or “how it smells,” or “how hot it is”); and further also to the cognition of the intellect, according to Matthew V: *Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God*. And the same must be said of the name “light.” For it was first instituted to signify that which brings about manifestation in the sense of sight; but afterward it was extended to signify everything that brings about manifestation according to any kind of cognition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0015","text":"Therefore, if the name “light” is taken according to its first imposition, it is spoken of metaphorically in spiritual things, as Ambrose says. But if it is taken as extended in common usage to every manifestation, then it is properly spoken of in spiritual things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0016","text":"[I q. 67 a. 1 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31242)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0017","text":"And through this the response to the objections is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0018","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0019","text":"[I q. 67 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31243)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0020","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that light is a body. For Augustine says, in the book *On Free Choice*, that *light holds the first place among bodies*. Therefore light is a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0021","text":"[I q. 67 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31244)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0022","text":"Further, the Philosopher says that light is a species of fire. But fire is a body. Therefore light is a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0023","text":"[I q. 67 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31245)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0024","text":"Further, to be carried, cut through, and reflected belong properly to bodies; yet all these are attributed to light or to a ray. Moreover, different rays are joined together and separated, as Dionysius says in chapter II of *On the Divine Names*; and this too seems able to belong only to bodies. Therefore light is a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0025","text":"[I q. 67 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31246)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0026","text":"On the contrary, two bodies cannot be simultaneously in the same place. But light is simultaneously with air. Therefore light is not a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0027","text":"[I q. 67 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31247)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0028","text":"I answer that it must be said that it is impossible for light to be a body. This is evident in three ways. First, on the part of place. For the place of any body is distinct from the place of another body, nor is it naturally possible for two bodies to be simultaneously in the same place, whatever bodies they may be; because contact requires distinction of position. Second, the same is evident from the nature of motion. For if light were a body, illumination would be a local motion of a body. But no local motion of a body can occur in an instant, because everything moved locally must necessarily arrive at the middle of the magnitude before arriving at the extremity. But illumination occurs in an instant. Nor can it be said that it occurs in an imperceptible time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0029","text":"For in a small space time could escape notice, but in a large space, for example from east to west, time could not escape notice; for as soon as the sun is at the point of the east, the whole hemisphere as far as the opposite point is illuminated. There is also another consideration on the part of motion. For every body has a determinate natural motion, whereas the motion of illumination is toward every direction, and no more in a circle than in a straight line. Hence it is manifest that illumination is not the local motion of any body. Third, the same is evident on the part of generation and corruption. For if light were a body, when the air is darkened through the absence of a luminary, it would follow that the body of light were corrupted and that its matter received another form. This does not appear, unless someone also says that darkness is a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0030","text":"Nor is it apparent from what matter so great a body, which fills the space of a hemisphere, is generated daily. It is also ridiculous to say that, merely upon the absence of a luminary, so great a body is corrupted. If someone should also say that it is not corrupted, but comes and is carried around together with the sun, what could be said concerning the fact that, upon the interposition of some body around a candle, the whole house is darkened? Nor does it seem that light gathers around the candle, because no greater brightness appears there afterward than before. Therefore, because all these things are repugnant not only to reason but also to sense, it must be said that it is impossible for light to be a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0031","text":"[I q. 67 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31248)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0032","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Augustine takes light for a body actually luminous, namely, fire, which is the noblest among the four elements.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0033","text":"[I q. 67 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31249)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0034","text":"To the second, it must be said that Aristotle calls light fire in its proper matter, just as fire in airy matter is called flame, and in earthly matter is called charcoal. Nevertheless, one should not be greatly concerned about those examples which Aristotle introduces in his logical books, because he introduces them as probable according to the opinion of others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0035","text":"[I q. 67 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31250)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0036","text":"To the third, it must be said that all those things are attributed to light metaphorically, just as they could also be attributed to heat. For since local motion is naturally the first of motions, as is proved in Physics VIII, we use names pertaining to local motion in alteration and in all motions, just as the name of distance, derived from place, is applied to all contraries, as is said in Metaphysics X.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0037","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0038","text":"[I q. 67 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31251)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0039","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that light is not a quality. For every quality remains in its subject even after the agent has departed; as heat remains in water after it is removed from fire. But light does not remain in the air when the luminary withdraws. Therefore light is not a quality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0040","text":"[I q. 67 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31252)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0041","text":"Moreover, every sensible quality has a contrary; as cold is contrary to hot, and black to white. But nothing is contrary to light, for darkness is a privation of light. Therefore light is not a sensible quality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0042","text":"[I q. 67 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31253)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0043","text":"Moreover, a cause is more excellent than its effect. But the light of the heavenly bodies causes substantial forms in these lower things. It also gives spiritual being to colors, because it makes them actually visible. Therefore light is not some sensible quality, but rather a substantial or spiritual form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0044","text":"[I q. 67 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31254)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0045","text":"On the contrary, Damascene says in Book I that light is a certain quality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0046","text":"[I q. 67 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31255)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0047","text":"I answer that it must be said that some have held that light in the air does not have natural being, as color does in a wall, but intentional being, as the likeness of a color does in the air. But this cannot be so, for two reasons. First, because light denominates the air: for the air becomes actually luminous. Color, however, does not denominate it, for the air is not called colored. Second, because light has an effect in nature, since bodies are warmed by the rays of the sun. But intentions do not cause natural transformations. Others, however, have held that light is the substantial form of the sun. But this too appears impossible, for two reasons. First, because no substantial form is sensible through itself, since the what-it-is is the object of the intellect, as is said in On the Soul III. But light is visible in itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0048","text":"Second, because it is impossible that what is a substantial form in one should be an accidental form in another, since it belongs to a substantial form through itself to constitute in a species; hence it is always and in all things present to it. But light is not the substantial form of air; otherwise, when it departed, the air would be corrupted. Hence it cannot be the substantial form of the sun. Therefore it must be said that, just as heat is an active quality consequent upon the substantial form of fire, so light is an active quality consequent upon the substantial form of the sun, or of whatever other body shines by itself, if there is any other such body. A sign of this is that the rays of different stars have different effects, according to the diverse natures of the bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0049","text":"[I q. 67 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31256)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0050","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that, since quality follows upon substantial form, the subject is related in diverse ways to the reception of quality, just as it is related to the reception of form. For when matter perfectly receives form, the quality following upon the form is also firmly established; as when water is converted into fire. But when substantial form is received imperfectly, according to a certain beginning, the consequent quality indeed remains for some time, but not always; as is evident in heated water, which returns to its nature. But illumination does not take place through some transmutation of matter for the reception of substantial form, such that some beginning of form should come about, as it were. And therefore light does not remain except in the presence of the agent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0051","text":"[I q. 67 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31257)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0052","text":"To the second it must be said that it is accidental to light that it has no contrary, insofar as it is the natural quality of the first altering body, which is removed from contrariety.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0053","text":"[I q. 67 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31258)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0054","text":"To the third it must be said that, just as heat acts toward the form of fire instrumentally, as it were, by the power of the substantial form, so light acts instrumentally, as it were, by the power of the heavenly bodies, to produce substantial forms and to make colors actually visible, insofar as it is the quality of the first sensible body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0055","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0056","text":"[I q. 67 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31259)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0057","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that the production of light is inappropriately placed on the first day. For light is a certain quality, as has been said. But quality, since it is an accident, does not have the character of first, but rather the character of last. Therefore the production of light ought not to be placed on the first day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0058","text":"[I q. 67 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31260)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0059","text":"Further, night is distinguished from day by light. But this occurs through the sun, which is said to have been made on the fourth day. Therefore the production of light ought not to be placed on the first day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0060","text":"[I q. 67 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31261)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0061","text":"Further, night and day come about through the circular motion of a luminous body. But circular motion is proper to the firmament, which is read to have been made on the second day. Therefore the production of light distinguishing night and day ought not to be placed on the first day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0062","text":"[I q. 67 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31262)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0063","text":"If it be said that this is understood of spiritual light, on the contrary: the light which is read to have been made on the first day makes a distinction from darkness. But there was no spiritual darkness in the beginning, because even the demons were good in the beginning, as was said above. Therefore the production of light ought not to be placed on the first day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0064","text":"[I q. 67 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31263)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0065","text":"On the contrary, that without which there cannot be a day had to be made on the first day. But without light there cannot be a day. Therefore light had to be made on the first day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0066","text":"[I q. 67 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31264)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0067","text":"I answer that it must be said that there is a twofold opinion concerning the production of light. For Augustine seems to hold that it would not have been fitting for Moses to have passed over the production of the spiritual creature. And therefore he says that, when it is said, “In the beginning God created heaven and earth,” by heaven is understood the spiritual nature still unformed, while by earth is understood the formless matter of corporeal creature. And because spiritual nature is more worthy than corporeal nature, it had to be formed first. Therefore the formation of spiritual nature is signified in the production of light, so that it is understood as spiritual light; for the formation of spiritual nature is through its being illuminated so as to adhere to the Word of God. But to others it seems that the production of the spiritual creature was passed over by Moses. Yet they assign different reasons for this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0068","text":"For Basil says that Moses made the beginning of his narrative from the beginning that pertains to the time of sensible things; but spiritual nature, that is, angelic nature, is passed over because it was created before. Chrysostom, however, assigns another reason. For Moses spoke to a rude people, who could grasp nothing except corporeal things; and he also wished to recall them from idolatry. But they would have taken an occasion for idolatry if certain substances above all corporeal creatures had been set before them; for they would have considered them gods, since they were even inclined to worship the sun and moon and stars as gods, as is forbidden them in Deut. IV. But in Gen. I, with respect to corporeal creature, a manifold formlessness had been set forth: one, indeed, in that it was said, “the earth was void and empty”; another, however, in that it was said, “darkness was upon the face of the deep.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0069","text":"But it was necessary that the formlessness of darkness first be removed through the production of light, for two reasons. First, indeed, because light, as has been said, is the quality of the first body, whence the world had first to be formed according to it. Second, because of the commonality of light: for lower bodies share in it with higher bodies. But just as in knowledge one proceeds from more common things, so also in operation; for a living thing is generated before an animal, and an animal before a human being, as is said in the book On the Generation of Animals. Thus, therefore, it was fitting that the order of divine wisdom be manifested, so that among the works of distinction light should first be produced, as the form of the first body and as the more common. Basil, however, gives a third reason: because through light all other things are manifested. And a fourth reason can be added, which was touched upon in the objection: because day cannot exist without light; whence light had to be made on the first day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0070","text":"[I q. 67 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31265)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0071","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that, according to the opinion which holds that the formlessness of matter preceded its formation in duration, it must be said that matter was created from the beginning under substantial forms; but afterwards it was formed according to certain accidental conditions, among which light holds the first place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0072","text":"[I q. 67 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31266)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0073","text":"To the second, it must be said that some say that that light was a certain luminous cloud, which afterwards, when the sun had been made, returned into the underlying matter. But this is not fitting. For Scripture, at the beginning of Genesis, recounts the institution of nature, which afterwards endures; hence it should not be said that something was then made which afterwards ceased to be. And therefore others say that that luminous cloud still remains, and is joined to the sun, so that it cannot be distinguished from it. But according to this, that cloud would remain superfluous; yet nothing is vain in the works of God. And therefore others say that the body of the sun was formed from that cloud. But this too cannot be said, if it is held that the body of the sun is not of the nature of the four elements, but is incorruptible by nature; because according to this, its matter cannot be under another form. And therefore it must be said, as Dionysius says in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0074","text":"that that light was the light of the sun, but as yet unformed, insofar as the substance of the sun already existed and had an illuminating power in general; but afterwards a special and determinate power for particular effects was given to it. And according to this, in the production of this light, light was distinguished from darkness in three respects. First, as regards the cause: insofar as in the substance of the sun there was the cause of light, while in the opacity of the earth there was the cause of darkness. Second, as regards place: because in one hemisphere there was light, in the other darkness. Third, as regards time: because in the same hemisphere, during one part of time there was light, during another darkness. And this is what is said: *he called the light day, and the darkness night*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0075","text":"[I q. 67 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31267)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0076","text":"To the third, it must be said that Basil says that light and darkness then existed through the emission and contraction of light, and not through motion. But Augustine objects against this that there would be no reason for this alternation of emitting and withdrawing light, since there were no human beings and animals to whose uses this would be of service. And besides, it is not in the nature of a luminous body to withdraw light in its own presence; rather, this can occur miraculously. But in the first institution of nature, a miracle is not sought, but rather what the nature of things possesses, as Augustine says. And therefore it must be said that there is a twofold motion in heaven. One is common to the whole heaven, which produces day and night, and this seems to have been instituted on the first day. But the other is diversified through diverse bodies; according to these motions there comes about the diversity of days among themselves, and of months and years.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0077","text":"And therefore on the first day mention is made only of the distinction between night and day, which occurs through the common motion. But on the fourth day mention is made of the diversity of days and seasons and years, when it is said, *let them be for seasons and days and years*; and this diversity indeed occurs through proper motions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0078","text":"[I q. 67 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31268)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0079","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, according to Augustine, formlessness does not precede formation in duration. Hence it is necessary to say that by the production of light is understood the formation of the spiritual creature—not that which is perfected through glory, with which it was not created, but that which is perfected through grace, with which it was created, as has been said. Therefore through this light a division was made from darkness, that is, from the formlessness of the other creature not formed. Or, if the whole creature was formed simultaneously, a distinction was made from spiritual darkness—not darkness which then existed (for the Devil was not created evil), but darkness which God foresaw would come to be.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_59:P0079"]}],"source_digest":"e503c120b0aefe6fa7d2a081e377c84145c1eeffdeb4e0a70f5c61af84d23eaa","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 67"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 67","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60","title":"First Part · Question 68","order":69,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_60","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 68 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31269)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de opere secundae diei. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum firmamentum sit factum secunda die. Secundo, utrum aliquae aquae sint supra firmamentum. Tertio, utrum firmamentum dividat aquas ab aquis. Quarto, utrum sit unum caelum tantum, vel plures.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 68 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31270)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod firmamentum non sit factum secunda die. Dicitur enim Gen. I, *vocavit Deus firmamentum caelum*. Sed caelum factum est ante omnem diem, ut patet cum dicitur, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*. Ergo firmamentum non est factum secunda die.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31271)\n\nPraeterea, opera sex dierum ordinantur secundum ordinem divinae sapientiae. Non conveniret autem divinae sapientiae, ut posterius faceret quod est naturaliter prius. Firmamentum autem est naturaliter prius aqua et terra, de quibus tamen fit mentio ante formationem lucis, quae fuit prima die. Non ergo firmamentum factum est secunda die.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31272)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod est factum per sex dies, formatum est ex materia prius creata ante omnem diem. Sed firmamentum non potuit formari ex materia praeexistente, quia sic esset generabile et corruptibile. Ergo firmamentum non est factum secunda die.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31273)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *dixit Deus, fiat firmamentum*. Et postea sequitur, *et factum est vespere et mane dies secundus*.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31274)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus docet, in huiusmodi quaestionibus duo sunt observanda. Primo quidem, ut veritas Scripturae inconcusse teneatur. Secundo, cum Scriptura divina multipliciter exponi possit, quod nulli expositioni aliquis ita praecise inhaereat quod, si certa ratione constiterit hoc esse falsum, quod aliquis sensum Scripturae esse asserere praesumat, ne Scriptura ex hoc ab infidelibus derideatur, et ne eis via credendi praecludatur. Sciendum est ergo quod hoc quod legitur firmamentum secunda die factum, dupliciter intelligi potest. Uno modo, de firmamento in quo sunt sidera. Et secundum hoc, oportet nos diversimode exponere secundum diversas opiniones hominum de firmamento. Quidam enim dixerunt firmamentum illud esse ex elementis compositum. Et haec fuit opinio Empedoclis; qui tamen dixit ideo illud corpus indissolubile esse, quia in eius compositione lis non erat sed amicitia tantum. Alii vero dixerunt firmamentum esse de natura quatuor elementorum non quasi ex elementis compositum, sed quasi elementum simplex. Et haec opinio fuit Platonis, qui posuit corpus caeleste esse elementum ignis. Alii vero dixerunt caelum non esse de natura quatuor elementorum, sed esse quintum corpus, praeter quatuor elementa. Et haec est opinio Aristotelis. Secundum igitur primam opinionem, absolute posset concedi quod firmamentum factum fuerit secunda die, etiam secundum suam substantiam. Nam ad opus creationis pertinet producere ipsam elementorum substantiam, ad opus autem distinctionis et ornatus pertinet formare aliqua ex praeexistentibus elementis. Secundum vero opinionem Platonis, non est conveniens quod firmamentum credatur secundum suam substantiam esse factum secunda die. Nam facere firmamentum, secundum hoc, est producere elementum ignis. Productio autem elementorum pertinet ad opus creationis, secundum eos qui ponunt informitatem materiae tempore praecedere formationem, quia formae elementorum sunt quae primo adveniunt materiae. Multo autem minus secundum opinionem Aristotelis poni potest quod firmamentum secundum suam substantiam sit secunda die productum, secundum quod per istos dies successio temporis designatur. Quia caelum, cum sit secundum suam naturam incorruptibile, habet materiam quae non potest subesse alteri formae, unde impossibile est quod firmamentum sit factum ex materia prius tempore existente. Unde productio substantiae firmamenti ad opus creationis pertinet. Sed aliqua formatio eius, secundum has duas opiniones, pertinet ad opus secundae diei, sicut etiam Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod lumen solis fuit informe in primo triduo, et postea fuit in quarta die formatum. Si autem per istos dies non designetur temporis successio, sed solum ordo naturae, ut Augustinus vult; nihil prohibebit dicere, secundum quamcumque harum opinionum formationem secundum substantiam firmamenti ad secundam diem pertinere. Potest autem et alio modo intelligi, ut per firmamentum quod legitur secunda die factum, non intelligatur firmamentum illud in quo fixae sunt stellae, sed illa pars aeris in qua condensantur nubes. Et dicitur firmamentum propter spissitudinem aeris in parte illa, nam quod est spissum et solidum, dicitur esse corpus firmum, ad differentiam corporis mathematici, ut dicit Basilius. Et secundum hanc expositionem, nihil repugnans sequitur cuicumque opinioni. Unde Augustinus, II super Gen. ad Litt., hanc expositionem commendans, dicit, *hanc considerationem laude dignissimam iudico; quod enim dixit, neque est contra fidem, et in promptu, posito documento, credi potest*.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31275)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, secundum Chrysostomum, primo Moyses summarie dixit quid Deus fecit, praemittens, in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram, postea per partes explicavit. Sicut si quis dicat, hic artifex fecit domum istam, et postea subdat, primo fecit fundamenta, et postea erexit parietes, tertio superposuit tectum. Et sic non oportet nos aliud caelum intelligere, cum dicitur, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*; et cum dicitur quod secunda die factum est firmamentum. Potest etiam dici aliud esse caelum quod legitur in principio creatum, et quod legitur secunda die factum. Et hoc diversimode. Nam secundum Augustinum, caelum quod legitur primo die factum, est natura spiritualis informis, caelum autem quod legitur secunda die factum, est caelum corporeum. Secundum vero Bedam et Strabum, caelum quod legitur primo die factum, est caelum Empyreum, firmamentum vero quod legitur secunda die factum, est caelum sidereum. Secundum vero Damascenum, caelum quod legitur prima die factum, est quoddam caelum sphaericum sine stellis, de quo philosophi loquuntur, dicentes ipsum esse nonam sphaeram et mobile primum, quod movetur motu diurno, per firmamentum vero factum secunda die, intelligitur caelum sidereum. Secundum autem aliam expositionem, quam Augustinus tangit, caelum prima die factum, est etiam ipsum caelum sidereum, per firmamentum vero secunda die factum, intelligitur spatium aeris in quo nubes condensantur, quod etiam caelum aequivoce dicitur. Et ideo, ad aequivocationem designandam, signanter dicitur, vocavit Deus firmamentum caelum; sicut et supra dixerat, vocavit lucem diem (quia dies etiam pro spatio vigintiquatuor horarum ponitur). Et idem est in aliis observandum, ut Rabbi Moyses dicit.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31276)\n\nAd secundum et tertium patet solutio ex supra dictis.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 68 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31277)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aquae non sunt supra firmamentum. Aqua enim est naturaliter gravis. Locus autem proprius gravis non est esse sursum, sed solum deorsum. Ergo aquae non sunt supra firmamentum.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31278)\n\nPraeterea, naturaliter aqua est fluida. Sed quod est fluidum, non potest consistere super corpus rotundum, ut experimento patet. Ergo, cum firmamentum sit corpus rotundum, aqua non potest esse supra firmamentum.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31279)\n\nPraeterea, aqua, cum sit elementum, ordinatur ad generationem corporis mixti; sicut imperfectum ordinatur ad perfectum. Sed supra firmamentum non est locus mixtionis, sed supra terram. Ergo frustra aqua esset supra firmamentum. Nihil autem in operibus Dei est frustra. Ergo aquae non sunt supra firmamentum.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31280)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, quod *divisit aquas quae erant supra firmamentum, ab his quae erant sub firmamento*.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31281)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut dicit Augustinus, II super Gen. ad Litt., *maior est Scripturae huius auctoritas quam omnis humani ingenii capacitas. Unde quomodo et quales aquae ibi sint, eas tamen ibi esse, minime dubitamus*. Quales autem sint illae aquae, non eodem modo ab omnibus assignatur. Origenes enim dicit quod aquae illae quae super caelos sunt, sunt spirituales substantiae, unde in Psalmo CXLVIII, dicitur, *aquae quae super caelos sunt, laudent nomen domini*; et Dan. III, *benedicite, aquae omnes quae super caelos sunt, domino*. Sed ad hoc respondet Basilius, in III Hexaem., quod hoc non dicitur eo quod aquae sint rationales creaturae; sed quia *consideratio earum, prudenter a sensum habentibus contemplata, glorificationem perficit creatoris*. Unde ibidem dicitur idem de igne et grandine et huiusmodi, de quibus constat quod non sunt rationales creaturae. Dicendum est ergo quod sunt aquae corporales. Sed quales aquae sint, oportet diversimode definire, secundum diversam de firmamento sententiam. Si enim per firmamentum intelligitur caelum sidereum quod ponitur esse de natura quatuor elementorum, pari ratione et aquae quae super caelos sunt, eiusdem naturae poterunt credi cum elementaribus aquis. Si autem per firmamentum intelligatur caelum sidereum quod non sit de natura quatuor elementorum, tunc et aquae illae quae sunt supra firmamentum, non erunt de natura elementarium aquarum, sed sicut, secundum Strabum, dicitur caelum Empyreum, idest igneum, propter solum splendorem; ita dicetur aliud caelum aqueum propter solam diaphaneitatem, quod est supra caelum sidereum. Posito etiam quod firmamentum sit alterius naturae praeter quatuor elementa, adhuc potest dici quod aquas dividit, si per aquam non elementum aquae, sed materiam informem corporum intelligamus, ut Augustinus dicit, super Gen. contra Manich., quia secundum hoc, quidquid est inter corpora, dividit aquas ab aquis. Si autem per firmamentum intelligatur pars aeris in qua nubes condensantur, sic aquae quae supra firmamentum sunt, sunt aquae quae, vaporabiliter resolutae, supra aliquam partem aeris elevantur ex quibus pluviae generantur. Dicere enim quod aquae vaporabiliter resolutae eleventur supra caelum sidereum, ut quidam dixerunt, quorum opinionem Augustinus tangit in II super Gen. ad Litt. est omnino impossibile. Tum propter soliditatem caeli. Tum propter regionem ignis mediam, quae huiusmodi vapores consumeret. Tum quia locus quo feruntur levia et rara, est infra concavum orbis lunae. Tum etiam quia sensibiliter apparet vapores non elevari usque ad cacumina quorundam montium. Quod etiam dicunt de rarefactione corporis in infinitum, propter hoc quod corpus est in infinitum divisibile, vanum est. Non enim corpus naturale in infinitum dividitur aut rarefit, sed usque ad certum terminum.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31282)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod quibusdam videtur ratio illa solvenda per hoc, quod aquae, quamvis sint naturaliter graves, virtute tamen divina super caelos continentur. Sed hanc solutionem Augustinus excludit, II Lib. super Gen. ad Litt., dicens quod *nunc quemadmodum Deus instituit naturas rerum convenit quaerere; non quid in eis ad miraculum suae potentiae velit operari*. Unde aliter dicendum est quod, secundum duas ultimas opiniones de aquis et firmamento, patet solutio ex praemissis. Secundum autem primam opinionem, oportet ponere alium ordinem in elementis quam Aristoteles ponat; ut quaedam aquae spissae sint circa terram, quaedam vero tenues circa caelum; ut sic se habeant illae ad caelum, sicut istae ad terram. Vel quod per aquam intelligatur materia corporum, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31283)\n\nAd secundum etiam patet solutio ex praemissis, secundum duas ultimas opiniones. Secundum vero primam, respondet Basilius dupliciter. Uno modo, quia non est necessarium ut omne quod in concavo apparet rotundum, sit etiam supra rotundum secundum convexum. Secundo, quia aquae quae sunt supra caelos, non sunt fluidae; sed quasi glaciali soliditate circa caelum firmatae. Unde et a quibusdam dicuntur caelum crystallinum.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31284)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, secundum tertiam opinionem, aquae sunt supra firmamentum vaporabiliter elevatae propter utilitatem pluviarum. Secundum vero secundam opinionem, aquae sunt supra firmamentum, idest caelum totum diaphanum absque stellis. Quod quidam ponunt primum mobile, quod revolvit totum caelum motu diurno, ut operetur per motum diurnum continuitatem generationis, sicut caelum in quo sunt sidera, per motum qui est secundum zodiacum, operatur diversitatem generationis et corruptionis, per accessum et recessum, et per diversas virtutes stellarum. Secundum vero primam opinionem, aquae sunt ibi, ut Basilius dicit, ad contemperandum calorem caelestium corporum. Cuius signum acceperunt aliqui, ut Augustinus dicit, quod stella Saturni, propter vicinitatem aquarum superiorum, est frigidissima.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 68 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31285)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod firmamentum non dividat aquas ab aquis. Unius enim corporis secundum speciem, est unus locus naturalis. Sed omnis aqua omni aquae est eadem specie, ut dicit philosophus. Non ergo aquae ab aquis sunt distinguendae secundum locum.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31286)\n\nSi dicatur quod aquae illae quae sunt supra firmamentum, sunt alterius speciei ab aquis quae sunt sub firmamento, contra, ea quae sunt secundum speciem diversa, non indigent aliquo alio distinguente. Si ergo aquae superiores et inferiores specie differunt, firmamentum eas ab invicem non distinguit.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31287)\n\nPraeterea, illud videtur aquas ab aquis distinguere, quod ex utraque parte ab aquis contingitur; sicut si aliquis paries fabricetur in medio fluminis. Manifestum est autem quod aquae inferiores non pertingunt usque ad firmamentum. Ergo non dividit firmamentum aquas ab aquis.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31288)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *fiat firmamentum in medio aquarum, et dividat aquas ab aquis*.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31289)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod aliquis, considerando superficie tenus litteram Genesis, posset talem imaginationem concipere, secundum quorundam antiquorum philosophorum positionem. Posuerunt enim quidam aquam esse quoddam infinitum corpus, et omnium aliorum corporum principium. Quam quidem immensitatem aquarum accipere posset in nomine abyssi, cum dicitur quod tenebrae erant super faciem abyssi. Ponebant etiam quod istud caelum sensibile quod videmus, non continet infra se omnia corporalia; sed est infinitum aquarum corpus supra caelum. Et ita posset aliquis dicere quod firmamentum caeli dividit aquas exteriores ab aquis interioribus, idest ab omnibus corporibus quae infra caelum continentur, quorum principium aquam ponebant. Sed quia ista positio per veras rationes falsa deprehenditur, non est dicendum hunc esse intellectum Scripturae. Sed considerandum est quod Moyses rudi populo loquebatur, quorum imbecillitati condescendens, illa solum eis proposuit, quae manifeste sensui apparent. Omnes autem, quantumcumque rudes, terram et aquam esse corpora sensu deprehendunt. Aer autem non percipitur ab omnibus esse corpus, intantum quod etiam quidam philosophi aerem dixerunt nihil esse, plenum aere vacuum nominantes. Et ideo Moyses de aqua et terra mentionem facit expressam, aerem autem non expresse nominat, ne rudibus quoddam ignotum proponeret. Ut tamen capacibus veritatem exprimeret, dat locum intelligendi aerem, significans ipsum quasi aquae annexum, cum dicit quod tenebrae erant super faciem abyssi; per quod datur intelligi super faciem aquae esse aliquod corpus diaphanum quod est subiectum lucis et tenebrarum. Sic igitur sive per firmamentum intelligamus caelum in quo sunt sidera, sive spatium aeris nubilosum, convenienter dicitur quod firmamentum dividit aquas ab aquis, secundum quod per aquam materia informis significatur; vel secundum quod omnia corpora diaphana sub nomine aquarum intelliguntur. Nam caelum sidereum distinguit corpora inferiora diaphana a superioribus. Aer vero nubilosus distinguit superiorem aeris partem, in qua generantur pluviae et huiusmodi impressiones, ab inferiori parte aeris, quae aquae connectitur, et sub nomine aquarum intelligitur.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31290)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, si per firmamentum intelligatur caelum sidereum, aquae superiores non sunt eiusdem speciei cum inferioribus. Si autem per firmamentum intelligatur nubilosus aer, tunc utraeque aquae sunt eiusdem speciei. Et deputantur tunc duo loca aquis non eadem ratione; sed locus superior est locus generationis aquarum, locus autem inferior est locus quietis earum.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31291)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, si accipiantur aquae diversae secundum speciem, firmamentum dicitur dividere aquas ab aquis, non sicut causa faciens divisionem; sed sicut terminus utrarumque aquarum.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31292)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Moyses, propter invisibilitatem aeris et similium corporum, omnia huiusmodi corpora sub aquae nomine comprehendit. Et sic manifestum est quod ex utraque parte firmamenti, qualitercumque accepti, sunt aquae.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 68 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31293)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod sit unum caelum tantum. Caelum enim contra terram dividitur, cum dicitur, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*. Sed terra est una tantum. Ergo et caelum est unum tantum.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31294)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod constat ex tota sua materia est unum tantum. Sed caelum est huiusmodi, ut probat philosophus in I de caelo. Ergo caelum est unum tantum.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31295)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid dicitur de pluribus univoce, dicitur de eis secundum unam rationem communem. Sed si sunt plures caeli, caelum dicitur univoce de pluribus, quia si aequivoce, non proprie dicerentur plures caeli. Oportet ergo, si dicuntur plures caeli, quod sit aliqua ratio communis, secundum quam caeli dicantur. Hanc autem non est assignare. Non est ergo dicendum quod sint plures caeli.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31296)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo CXLVIII, *laudate eum, caeli caelorum*.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31297)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa hoc videtur esse quaedam diversitas inter Basilium et Chrysostomum. Dicit enim Chrysostomus non esse nisi unum caelum; et quod pluraliter dicitur, caeli caelorum, hoc est propter proprietatem linguae Hebraeae, in qua consuetum est ut caelum solum pluraliter significetur; sicut sunt etiam multa nomina in Latino quae singulari carent. Basilius autem, et Damascenus sequens eum, dicunt plures esse caelos. Sed haec diversitas magis est in voce quam in re. Nam Chrysostomus unum caelum nominat totum corpus quod est supra terram et aquam, nam etiam aves, quae volant in aere, dicuntur propter hoc volucres caeli. Sed quia in isto corpore sunt multae distinctiones, propter hoc Basilius posuit plures caelos. Ad distinctionem ergo caelorum sciendam, considerandum est quod caelum tripliciter dicitur in Scripturis. Quandoque enim dicitur proprie et naturaliter. Et sic dicitur caelum corpus aliquod sublime, et luminosum actu vel potentia, et incorruptibile per naturam. Et secundum hoc, ponuntur tres caeli. Primum totaliter lucidum, quod vocant Empyreum. Secundum totaliter diaphanum, quod vocant caelum aqueum vel crystallinum. Tertium partim diaphanum et partim lucidum actu, quod vocant caelum sidereum, et dividitur in octo sphaeras, scilicet in sphaeram stellarum fixarum, et septem sphaeras planetarum; quae possunt dici octo caeli. Secundo dicitur caelum per participationem alicuius proprietatis caelestis corporis, scilicet sublimitatis et luminositatis actu vel potentia. Et sic totum illud spatium quod est ab aquis usque ad orbem lunae, Damascenus ponit unum caelum, nominans illud aereum. Et sic, secundum eum, sunt tres caeli, aereum, sidereum, et aliud superius, de quo intelligit quod apostolus legitur raptus usque ad tertium caelum. Sed quia istud spatium continet duo elementa, scilicet ignis et aeris, et in utroque eorum vocatur superior et inferior regio; ideo istud caelum Rabanus distinguit in quatuor, supremam regionem ignis nominans caelum igneum; inferiorem vero regionem, caelum Olympium, ab altitudine cuiusdam montis qui vocatur Olympus; supremam vero regionem aeris vocavit caelum aethereum, propter inflammationem; inferiorem vero regionem, caelum aereum. Et sic, cum isti quatuor caeli tribus superioribus connumerantur, fiunt in universo, secundum Rabanum, septem caeli corporei. Tertio dicitur caelum metaphorice. Et sic quandoque ipsa sancta Trinitas dicitur caelum, propter eius spiritualem sublimitatem et lucem. De quo caelo exponitur Diabolum dixisse, ascendam in caelum, idest ad aequalitatem Dei. Quandoque etiam spiritualia bona in quibus est sanctorum remuneratio, propter eorum eminentiam caeli nominantur; ubi dicitur, *merces vestra multa est in caelis*, ut Augustinus exponit. Quandoque vero tria genera supernaturalium visionum, scilicet corporalis, imaginariae et intellectualis tres caeli nominantur. De quibus Augustinus exponit quod Paulus est raptus usque ad tertium caelum.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31298)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod terra se habet ad caelum, ut centrum ad circumferentiam. Circa unum autem centrum possunt esse multae circumferentiae. Unde una terra existente, multi caeli ponuntur.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31299)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ratio illa procedit de caelo, secundum quod importat universitatem creaturarum corporalium. Sic enim est unum caelum tantum.\n\n[I q. 68 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31300)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in omnibus caelis invenitur communiter sublimitas et aliqua luminositas, ut ex dictis patet.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0002","text":"[I q. 68 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31269)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de opere secundae diei. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum firmamentum sit factum secunda die. Secundo, utrum aliquae aquae sint supra firmamentum. Tertio, utrum firmamentum dividat aquas ab aquis. Quarto, utrum sit unum caelum tantum, vel plures.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0005","text":"[I q. 68 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31270)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod firmamentum non sit factum secunda die. Dicitur enim Gen. I, *vocavit Deus firmamentum caelum*. Sed caelum factum est ante omnem diem, ut patet cum dicitur, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*. Ergo firmamentum non est factum secunda die.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0007","text":"[I q. 68 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31271)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0008","text":"Praeterea, opera sex dierum ordinantur secundum ordinem divinae sapientiae. Non conveniret autem divinae sapientiae, ut posterius faceret quod est naturaliter prius. Firmamentum autem est naturaliter prius aqua et terra, de quibus tamen fit mentio ante formationem lucis, quae fuit prima die. Non ergo firmamentum factum est secunda die.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0009","text":"[I q. 68 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31272)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0010","text":"Praeterea, omne quod est factum per sex dies, formatum est ex materia prius creata ante omnem diem. Sed firmamentum non potuit formari ex materia praeexistente, quia sic esset generabile et corruptibile. Ergo firmamentum non est factum secunda die.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0011","text":"[I q. 68 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31273)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *dixit Deus, fiat firmamentum*. Et postea sequitur, *et factum est vespere et mane dies secundus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0013","text":"[I q. 68 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31274)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus docet, in huiusmodi quaestionibus duo sunt observanda. Primo quidem, ut veritas Scripturae inconcusse teneatur. Secundo, cum Scriptura divina multipliciter exponi possit, quod nulli expositioni aliquis ita praecise inhaereat quod, si certa ratione constiterit hoc esse falsum, quod aliquis sensum Scripturae esse asserere praesumat, ne Scriptura ex hoc ab infidelibus derideatur, et ne eis via credendi praecludatur. Sciendum est ergo quod hoc quod legitur firmamentum secunda die factum, dupliciter intelligi potest. Uno modo, de firmamento in quo sunt sidera. Et secundum hoc, oportet nos diversimode exponere secundum diversas opiniones hominum de firmamento. Quidam enim dixerunt firmamentum illud esse ex elementis compositum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0015","text":"Et haec fuit opinio Empedoclis; qui tamen dixit ideo illud corpus indissolubile esse, quia in eius compositione lis non erat sed amicitia tantum. Alii vero dixerunt firmamentum esse de natura quatuor elementorum non quasi ex elementis compositum, sed quasi elementum simplex. Et haec opinio fuit Platonis, qui posuit corpus caeleste esse elementum ignis. Alii vero dixerunt caelum non esse de natura quatuor elementorum, sed esse quintum corpus, praeter quatuor elementa. Et haec est opinio Aristotelis. Secundum igitur primam opinionem, absolute posset concedi quod firmamentum factum fuerit secunda die, etiam secundum suam substantiam. Nam ad opus creationis pertinet producere ipsam elementorum substantiam, ad opus autem distinctionis et ornatus pertinet formare aliqua ex praeexistentibus elementis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0016","text":"Secundum vero opinionem Platonis, non est conveniens quod firmamentum credatur secundum suam substantiam esse factum secunda die. Nam facere firmamentum, secundum hoc, est producere elementum ignis. Productio autem elementorum pertinet ad opus creationis, secundum eos qui ponunt informitatem materiae tempore praecedere formationem, quia formae elementorum sunt quae primo adveniunt materiae. Multo autem minus secundum opinionem Aristotelis poni potest quod firmamentum secundum suam substantiam sit secunda die productum, secundum quod per istos dies successio temporis designatur. Quia caelum, cum sit secundum suam naturam incorruptibile, habet materiam quae non potest subesse alteri formae, unde impossibile est quod firmamentum sit factum ex materia prius tempore existente. Unde productio substantiae firmamenti ad opus creationis pertinet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0017","text":"Sed aliqua formatio eius, secundum has duas opiniones, pertinet ad opus secundae diei, sicut etiam Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod lumen solis fuit informe in primo triduo, et postea fuit in quarta die formatum. Si autem per istos dies non designetur temporis successio, sed solum ordo naturae, ut Augustinus vult; nihil prohibebit dicere, secundum quamcumque harum opinionum formationem secundum substantiam firmamenti ad secundam diem pertinere. Potest autem et alio modo intelligi, ut per firmamentum quod legitur secunda die factum, non intelligatur firmamentum illud in quo fixae sunt stellae, sed illa pars aeris in qua condensantur nubes. Et dicitur firmamentum propter spissitudinem aeris in parte illa, nam quod est spissum et solidum, dicitur esse corpus firmum, ad differentiam corporis mathematici, ut dicit Basilius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0018","text":"Et secundum hanc expositionem, nihil repugnans sequitur cuicumque opinioni. Unde Augustinus, II super Gen. ad Litt., hanc expositionem commendans, dicit, *hanc considerationem laude dignissimam iudico; quod enim dixit, neque est contra fidem, et in promptu, posito documento, credi potest*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0019","text":"[I q. 68 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31275)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0020","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, secundum Chrysostomum, primo Moyses summarie dixit quid Deus fecit, praemittens, in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram, postea per partes explicavit. Sicut si quis dicat, hic artifex fecit domum istam, et postea subdat, primo fecit fundamenta, et postea erexit parietes, tertio superposuit tectum. Et sic non oportet nos aliud caelum intelligere, cum dicitur, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*; et cum dicitur quod secunda die factum est firmamentum. Potest etiam dici aliud esse caelum quod legitur in principio creatum, et quod legitur secunda die factum. Et hoc diversimode. Nam secundum Augustinum, caelum quod legitur primo die factum, est natura spiritualis informis, caelum autem quod legitur secunda die factum, est caelum corporeum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0021","text":"Secundum vero Bedam et Strabum, caelum quod legitur primo die factum, est caelum Empyreum, firmamentum vero quod legitur secunda die factum, est caelum sidereum. Secundum vero Damascenum, caelum quod legitur prima die factum, est quoddam caelum sphaericum sine stellis, de quo philosophi loquuntur, dicentes ipsum esse nonam sphaeram et mobile primum, quod movetur motu diurno, per firmamentum vero factum secunda die, intelligitur caelum sidereum. Secundum autem aliam expositionem, quam Augustinus tangit, caelum prima die factum, est etiam ipsum caelum sidereum, per firmamentum vero secunda die factum, intelligitur spatium aeris in quo nubes condensantur, quod etiam caelum aequivoce dicitur. Et ideo, ad aequivocationem designandam, signanter dicitur, vocavit Deus firmamentum caelum; sicut et supra dixerat, vocavit lucem diem (quia dies etiam pro spatio vigintiquatuor horarum ponitur).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0022","text":"Et idem est in aliis observandum, ut Rabbi Moyses dicit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0023","text":"[I q. 68 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31276)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0024","text":"Ad secundum et tertium patet solutio ex supra dictis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0025","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0026","text":"[I q. 68 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31277)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0027","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aquae non sunt supra firmamentum. Aqua enim est naturaliter gravis. Locus autem proprius gravis non est esse sursum, sed solum deorsum. Ergo aquae non sunt supra firmamentum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0028","text":"[I q. 68 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31278)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0029","text":"Praeterea, naturaliter aqua est fluida. Sed quod est fluidum, non potest consistere super corpus rotundum, ut experimento patet. Ergo, cum firmamentum sit corpus rotundum, aqua non potest esse supra firmamentum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0030","text":"[I q. 68 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31279)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0031","text":"Praeterea, aqua, cum sit elementum, ordinatur ad generationem corporis mixti; sicut imperfectum ordinatur ad perfectum. Sed supra firmamentum non est locus mixtionis, sed supra terram. Ergo frustra aqua esset supra firmamentum. Nihil autem in operibus Dei est frustra. Ergo aquae non sunt supra firmamentum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0032","text":"[I q. 68 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31280)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0033","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, quod *divisit aquas quae erant supra firmamentum, ab his quae erant sub firmamento*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0034","text":"[I q. 68 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31281)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0035","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut dicit Augustinus, II super Gen. ad Litt., *maior est Scripturae huius auctoritas quam omnis humani ingenii capacitas. Unde quomodo et quales aquae ibi sint, eas tamen ibi esse, minime dubitamus*. Quales autem sint illae aquae, non eodem modo ab omnibus assignatur. Origenes enim dicit quod aquae illae quae super caelos sunt, sunt spirituales substantiae, unde in Psalmo CXLVIII, dicitur, *aquae quae super caelos sunt, laudent nomen domini*; et Dan. III, *benedicite, aquae omnes quae super caelos sunt, domino*. Sed ad hoc respondet Basilius, in III Hexaem., quod hoc non dicitur eo quod aquae sint rationales creaturae; sed quia *consideratio earum, prudenter a sensum habentibus contemplata, glorificationem perficit creatoris*. Unde ibidem dicitur idem de igne et grandine et huiusmodi, de quibus constat quod non sunt rationales creaturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0036","text":"Dicendum est ergo quod sunt aquae corporales. Sed quales aquae sint, oportet diversimode definire, secundum diversam de firmamento sententiam. Si enim per firmamentum intelligitur caelum sidereum quod ponitur esse de natura quatuor elementorum, pari ratione et aquae quae super caelos sunt, eiusdem naturae poterunt credi cum elementaribus aquis. Si autem per firmamentum intelligatur caelum sidereum quod non sit de natura quatuor elementorum, tunc et aquae illae quae sunt supra firmamentum, non erunt de natura elementarium aquarum, sed sicut, secundum Strabum, dicitur caelum Empyreum, idest igneum, propter solum splendorem; ita dicetur aliud caelum aqueum propter solam diaphaneitatem, quod est supra caelum sidereum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0037","text":"Posito etiam quod firmamentum sit alterius naturae praeter quatuor elementa, adhuc potest dici quod aquas dividit, si per aquam non elementum aquae, sed materiam informem corporum intelligamus, ut Augustinus dicit, super Gen. contra Manich., quia secundum hoc, quidquid est inter corpora, dividit aquas ab aquis. Si autem per firmamentum intelligatur pars aeris in qua nubes condensantur, sic aquae quae supra firmamentum sunt, sunt aquae quae, vaporabiliter resolutae, supra aliquam partem aeris elevantur ex quibus pluviae generantur. Dicere enim quod aquae vaporabiliter resolutae eleventur supra caelum sidereum, ut quidam dixerunt, quorum opinionem Augustinus tangit in II super Gen. ad Litt. est omnino impossibile. Tum propter soliditatem caeli. Tum propter regionem ignis mediam, quae huiusmodi vapores consumeret. Tum quia locus quo feruntur levia et rara, est infra concavum orbis lunae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0038","text":"Tum etiam quia sensibiliter apparet vapores non elevari usque ad cacumina quorundam montium. Quod etiam dicunt de rarefactione corporis in infinitum, propter hoc quod corpus est in infinitum divisibile, vanum est. Non enim corpus naturale in infinitum dividitur aut rarefit, sed usque ad certum terminum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0039","text":"[I q. 68 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31282)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0040","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod quibusdam videtur ratio illa solvenda per hoc, quod aquae, quamvis sint naturaliter graves, virtute tamen divina super caelos continentur. Sed hanc solutionem Augustinus excludit, II Lib. super Gen. ad Litt., dicens quod *nunc quemadmodum Deus instituit naturas rerum convenit quaerere; non quid in eis ad miraculum suae potentiae velit operari*. Unde aliter dicendum est quod, secundum duas ultimas opiniones de aquis et firmamento, patet solutio ex praemissis. Secundum autem primam opinionem, oportet ponere alium ordinem in elementis quam Aristoteles ponat; ut quaedam aquae spissae sint circa terram, quaedam vero tenues circa caelum; ut sic se habeant illae ad caelum, sicut istae ad terram. Vel quod per aquam intelligatur materia corporum, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0041","text":"[I q. 68 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31283)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0042","text":"Ad secundum etiam patet solutio ex praemissis, secundum duas ultimas opiniones. Secundum vero primam, respondet Basilius dupliciter. Uno modo, quia non est necessarium ut omne quod in concavo apparet rotundum, sit etiam supra rotundum secundum convexum. Secundo, quia aquae quae sunt supra caelos, non sunt fluidae; sed quasi glaciali soliditate circa caelum firmatae. Unde et a quibusdam dicuntur caelum crystallinum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0043","text":"[I q. 68 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31284)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0044","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, secundum tertiam opinionem, aquae sunt supra firmamentum vaporabiliter elevatae propter utilitatem pluviarum. Secundum vero secundam opinionem, aquae sunt supra firmamentum, idest caelum totum diaphanum absque stellis. Quod quidam ponunt primum mobile, quod revolvit totum caelum motu diurno, ut operetur per motum diurnum continuitatem generationis, sicut caelum in quo sunt sidera, per motum qui est secundum zodiacum, operatur diversitatem generationis et corruptionis, per accessum et recessum, et per diversas virtutes stellarum. Secundum vero primam opinionem, aquae sunt ibi, ut Basilius dicit, ad contemperandum calorem caelestium corporum. Cuius signum acceperunt aliqui, ut Augustinus dicit, quod stella Saturni, propter vicinitatem aquarum superiorum, est frigidissima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0045","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0046","text":"[I q. 68 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31285)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0047","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod firmamentum non dividat aquas ab aquis. Unius enim corporis secundum speciem, est unus locus naturalis. Sed omnis aqua omni aquae est eadem specie, ut dicit philosophus. Non ergo aquae ab aquis sunt distinguendae secundum locum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0048","text":"[I q. 68 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31286)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0049","text":"Si dicatur quod aquae illae quae sunt supra firmamentum, sunt alterius speciei ab aquis quae sunt sub firmamento, contra, ea quae sunt secundum speciem diversa, non indigent aliquo alio distinguente. Si ergo aquae superiores et inferiores specie differunt, firmamentum eas ab invicem non distinguit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0050","text":"[I q. 68 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31287)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0051","text":"Praeterea, illud videtur aquas ab aquis distinguere, quod ex utraque parte ab aquis contingitur; sicut si aliquis paries fabricetur in medio fluminis. Manifestum est autem quod aquae inferiores non pertingunt usque ad firmamentum. Ergo non dividit firmamentum aquas ab aquis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0052","text":"[I q. 68 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31288)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0053","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *fiat firmamentum in medio aquarum, et dividat aquas ab aquis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0054","text":"[I q. 68 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31289)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0055","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod aliquis, considerando superficie tenus litteram Genesis, posset talem imaginationem concipere, secundum quorundam antiquorum philosophorum positionem. Posuerunt enim quidam aquam esse quoddam infinitum corpus, et omnium aliorum corporum principium. Quam quidem immensitatem aquarum accipere posset in nomine abyssi, cum dicitur quod tenebrae erant super faciem abyssi. Ponebant etiam quod istud caelum sensibile quod videmus, non continet infra se omnia corporalia; sed est infinitum aquarum corpus supra caelum. Et ita posset aliquis dicere quod firmamentum caeli dividit aquas exteriores ab aquis interioribus, idest ab omnibus corporibus quae infra caelum continentur, quorum principium aquam ponebant. Sed quia ista positio per veras rationes falsa deprehenditur, non est dicendum hunc esse intellectum Scripturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0056","text":"Sed considerandum est quod Moyses rudi populo loquebatur, quorum imbecillitati condescendens, illa solum eis proposuit, quae manifeste sensui apparent. Omnes autem, quantumcumque rudes, terram et aquam esse corpora sensu deprehendunt. Aer autem non percipitur ab omnibus esse corpus, intantum quod etiam quidam philosophi aerem dixerunt nihil esse, plenum aere vacuum nominantes. Et ideo Moyses de aqua et terra mentionem facit expressam, aerem autem non expresse nominat, ne rudibus quoddam ignotum proponeret. Ut tamen capacibus veritatem exprimeret, dat locum intelligendi aerem, significans ipsum quasi aquae annexum, cum dicit quod tenebrae erant super faciem abyssi; per quod datur intelligi super faciem aquae esse aliquod corpus diaphanum quod est subiectum lucis et tenebrarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0057","text":"Sic igitur sive per firmamentum intelligamus caelum in quo sunt sidera, sive spatium aeris nubilosum, convenienter dicitur quod firmamentum dividit aquas ab aquis, secundum quod per aquam materia informis significatur; vel secundum quod omnia corpora diaphana sub nomine aquarum intelliguntur. Nam caelum sidereum distinguit corpora inferiora diaphana a superioribus. Aer vero nubilosus distinguit superiorem aeris partem, in qua generantur pluviae et huiusmodi impressiones, ab inferiori parte aeris, quae aquae connectitur, et sub nomine aquarum intelligitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0058","text":"[I q. 68 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31290)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0059","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, si per firmamentum intelligatur caelum sidereum, aquae superiores non sunt eiusdem speciei cum inferioribus. Si autem per firmamentum intelligatur nubilosus aer, tunc utraeque aquae sunt eiusdem speciei. Et deputantur tunc duo loca aquis non eadem ratione; sed locus superior est locus generationis aquarum, locus autem inferior est locus quietis earum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0060","text":"[I q. 68 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31291)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0061","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, si accipiantur aquae diversae secundum speciem, firmamentum dicitur dividere aquas ab aquis, non sicut causa faciens divisionem; sed sicut terminus utrarumque aquarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0062","text":"[I q. 68 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31292)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0063","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Moyses, propter invisibilitatem aeris et similium corporum, omnia huiusmodi corpora sub aquae nomine comprehendit. Et sic manifestum est quod ex utraque parte firmamenti, qualitercumque accepti, sunt aquae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0064","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0065","text":"[I q. 68 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31293)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0066","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod sit unum caelum tantum. Caelum enim contra terram dividitur, cum dicitur, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*. Sed terra est una tantum. Ergo et caelum est unum tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0067","text":"[I q. 68 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31294)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0068","text":"Praeterea, omne quod constat ex tota sua materia est unum tantum. Sed caelum est huiusmodi, ut probat philosophus in I de caelo. Ergo caelum est unum tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0069","text":"[I q. 68 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31295)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0070","text":"Praeterea, quidquid dicitur de pluribus univoce, dicitur de eis secundum unam rationem communem. Sed si sunt plures caeli, caelum dicitur univoce de pluribus, quia si aequivoce, non proprie dicerentur plures caeli. Oportet ergo, si dicuntur plures caeli, quod sit aliqua ratio communis, secundum quam caeli dicantur. Hanc autem non est assignare. Non est ergo dicendum quod sint plures caeli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0071","text":"[I q. 68 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31296)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0072","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo CXLVIII, *laudate eum, caeli caelorum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0073","text":"[I q. 68 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31297)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0074","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa hoc videtur esse quaedam diversitas inter Basilium et Chrysostomum. Dicit enim Chrysostomus non esse nisi unum caelum; et quod pluraliter dicitur, caeli caelorum, hoc est propter proprietatem linguae Hebraeae, in qua consuetum est ut caelum solum pluraliter significetur; sicut sunt etiam multa nomina in Latino quae singulari carent. Basilius autem, et Damascenus sequens eum, dicunt plures esse caelos. Sed haec diversitas magis est in voce quam in re. Nam Chrysostomus unum caelum nominat totum corpus quod est supra terram et aquam, nam etiam aves, quae volant in aere, dicuntur propter hoc volucres caeli. Sed quia in isto corpore sunt multae distinctiones, propter hoc Basilius posuit plures caelos. Ad distinctionem ergo caelorum sciendam, considerandum est quod caelum tripliciter dicitur in Scripturis. Quandoque enim dicitur proprie et naturaliter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0075","text":"Et sic dicitur caelum corpus aliquod sublime, et luminosum actu vel potentia, et incorruptibile per naturam. Et secundum hoc, ponuntur tres caeli. Primum totaliter lucidum, quod vocant Empyreum. Secundum totaliter diaphanum, quod vocant caelum aqueum vel crystallinum. Tertium partim diaphanum et partim lucidum actu, quod vocant caelum sidereum, et dividitur in octo sphaeras, scilicet in sphaeram stellarum fixarum, et septem sphaeras planetarum; quae possunt dici octo caeli. Secundo dicitur caelum per participationem alicuius proprietatis caelestis corporis, scilicet sublimitatis et luminositatis actu vel potentia. Et sic totum illud spatium quod est ab aquis usque ad orbem lunae, Damascenus ponit unum caelum, nominans illud aereum. Et sic, secundum eum, sunt tres caeli, aereum, sidereum, et aliud superius, de quo intelligit quod apostolus legitur raptus usque ad tertium caelum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0076","text":"Sed quia istud spatium continet duo elementa, scilicet ignis et aeris, et in utroque eorum vocatur superior et inferior regio; ideo istud caelum Rabanus distinguit in quatuor, supremam regionem ignis nominans caelum igneum; inferiorem vero regionem, caelum Olympium, ab altitudine cuiusdam montis qui vocatur Olympus; supremam vero regionem aeris vocavit caelum aethereum, propter inflammationem; inferiorem vero regionem, caelum aereum. Et sic, cum isti quatuor caeli tribus superioribus connumerantur, fiunt in universo, secundum Rabanum, septem caeli corporei. Tertio dicitur caelum metaphorice. Et sic quandoque ipsa sancta Trinitas dicitur caelum, propter eius spiritualem sublimitatem et lucem. De quo caelo exponitur Diabolum dixisse, ascendam in caelum, idest ad aequalitatem Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0077","text":"Quandoque etiam spiritualia bona in quibus est sanctorum remuneratio, propter eorum eminentiam caeli nominantur; ubi dicitur, *merces vestra multa est in caelis*, ut Augustinus exponit. Quandoque vero tria genera supernaturalium visionum, scilicet corporalis, imaginariae et intellectualis tres caeli nominantur. De quibus Augustinus exponit quod Paulus est raptus usque ad tertium caelum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0078","text":"[I q. 68 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31298)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0079","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod terra se habet ad caelum, ut centrum ad circumferentiam. Circa unum autem centrum possunt esse multae circumferentiae. Unde una terra existente, multi caeli ponuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0080","text":"[I q. 68 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31299)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0081","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ratio illa procedit de caelo, secundum quod importat universitatem creaturarum corporalium. Sic enim est unum caelum tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0082","text":"[I q. 68 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31300)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0083","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in omnibus caelis invenitur communiter sublimitas et aliqua luminositas, ut ex dictis patet.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"6cdfd569bc5e845d352b1092ac9d985f7dd6a089ab4253ddeaa68c7e2534b385","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T145546844731Z_f2e84022","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 68","source_sha256":"6cdfd569bc5e845d352b1092ac9d985f7dd6a089ab4253ddeaa68c7e2534b385","sha256":"bdf01f22dfd9022a0948dd865b69c5cc7afe45c5dc7653983c8921730bac052a","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:55:46.844752+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0002","text":"[I q. 68 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31269)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the work of the second day. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, whether the firmament was made on the second day. Second, whether any waters are above the firmament. Third, whether the firmament divides waters from waters. Fourth, whether there is only one heaven, or several.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0005","text":"[I q. 68 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31270)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first point. It seems that the firmament was not made on the second day. For it is said in Gen. I, *God called the firmament heaven*. But heaven was made before every day, as is clear when it is said, *in the beginning God created heaven and earth*. Therefore the firmament was not made on the second day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0007","text":"[I q. 68 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31271)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0008","text":"Further, the works of the six days are ordered according to the order of divine wisdom. But it would not befit divine wisdom to make later what is naturally prior. Now the firmament is naturally prior to water and earth, which nevertheless are mentioned before the formation of light, which was on the first day. Therefore the firmament was not made on the second day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0009","text":"[I q. 68 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31272)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0010","text":"Further, everything that was made through the six days was formed from matter created beforehand, before every day. But the firmament could not be formed from pre-existing matter, because in that case it would be generable and corruptible. Therefore the firmament was not made on the second day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0011","text":"[I q. 68 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31273)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Gen. I, *God said, Let there be a firmament*. And afterwards follows, *and there was evening and morning, the second day*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0013","text":"[I q. 68 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31274)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0014","text":"I answer that, as Augustine teaches, two things must be observed in questions of this kind. First, that the truth of Scripture be held unshaken. Second, since divine Scripture can be expounded in many ways, that no one adhere so precisely to any exposition that, if it should be established by certain reasoning that this is false, he should presume to assert that it is the sense of Scripture; lest Scripture thereby be ridiculed by unbelievers, and the way of believing be barred to them. It must therefore be known that the statement read, that the firmament was made on the second day, can be understood in two ways. In one way, concerning the firmament in which the stars are. And according to this, we must expound differently according to men's different opinions about the firmament. For some said that that firmament was composed of the elements.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0015","text":"And this was the opinion of Empedocles; who nevertheless said that that body was indissoluble because in its composition there was not strife but friendship alone. Others, however, said that the firmament was of the nature of the four elements, not as though composed of elements, but as though a simple element. And this was Plato's opinion, who held the heavenly body to be an element of fire. Others, however, said that heaven is not of the nature of the four elements, but is a fifth body besides the four elements. And this is Aristotle's opinion. Therefore, according to the first opinion, it could simply be conceded that the firmament was made on the second day, even according to its substance. For it belongs to the work of creation to produce the very substance of the elements, whereas it belongs to the work of distinction and adornment to form certain things from pre-existing elements.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0016","text":"But according to Plato's opinion, it is not fitting to believe that the firmament was made according to its substance on the second day. For to make the firmament, according to this view, is to produce the element of fire. But the production of the elements belongs to the work of creation, according to those who hold that the formlessness of matter temporally preceded formation, because the forms of the elements are those which first come to matter. But much less, according to Aristotle's opinion, can it be held that the firmament was produced according to its substance on the second day, if by these days a succession of time is designated. For heaven, since it is incorruptible according to its nature, has matter which cannot be subject to another form; whence it is impossible that the firmament was made from matter existing previously in time. Hence the production of the substance of the firmament belongs to the work of creation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0017","text":"But some formation of it, according to these two opinions, belongs to the work of the second day, just as Dionysius also says, in ch. IV of *On the Divine Names*, that the light of the sun was unformed during the first three days, and afterwards was formed on the fourth day. But if by these days not a succession of time is designated, but only an order of nature, as Augustine holds, nothing will prevent one from saying, according to whichever of these opinions, that the formation according to the substance of the firmament belongs to the second day. It can also be understood in another way: that by the firmament which is read to have been made on the second day is not understood that firmament in which the stars are fixed, but that part of the air in which clouds are condensed. And it is called a firmament on account of the thickness of the air in that part; for what is thick and solid is said to be a firm body, in distinction from a mathematical body, as Basil says.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0018","text":"And according to this exposition, nothing repugnant follows for any opinion. Hence Augustine, in II *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, commending this exposition, says: *I judge this consideration most worthy of praise; for what he said is neither against the faith, and, with proof adduced, can readily be believed*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0019","text":"[I q. 68 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31275)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0020","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, according to Chrysostom, Moses first stated summarily what God made, prefacing, *in the beginning God created heaven and earth*, and afterwards explained it part by part. As if someone were to say, “This craftsman made this house,” and afterwards add, “First he made the foundations, then he raised the walls, third he set the roof upon it.” And thus we need not understand a different heaven when it is said, *in the beginning God created heaven and earth*, and when it is said that the firmament was made on the second day. It can also be said that the heaven read to have been created in the beginning is different from that read to have been made on the second day. And this in different ways. For according to Augustine, the heaven read to have been made on the first day is unformed spiritual nature, whereas the heaven read to have been made on the second day is corporeal heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0021","text":"But according to Bede and Strabus, the heaven read to have been made on the first day is the empyrean heaven, whereas the firmament read to have been made on the second day is the starry heaven. But according to Damascene, the heaven read to have been made on the first day is a certain spherical heaven without stars, of which philosophers speak, saying that it is the ninth sphere and the first movable, which is moved with diurnal motion; whereas by the firmament made on the second day the starry heaven is understood. But according to another exposition, which Augustine mentions, the heaven made on the first day is likewise the very starry heaven, whereas by the firmament made on the second day is understood the space of air in which clouds are condensed, which is also equivocally called heaven. And therefore, to designate the equivocation, it is said pointedly, God called the firmament heaven; just as above He had said, He called the light day (because day is also used for the space of twenty-four hours).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0022","text":"And the same must be observed in other matters, as Rabbi Moses says.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0023","text":"[I q. 68 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31276)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0024","text":"The solution to the second and third is clear from what has been said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0025","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0026","text":"[I q. 68 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31277)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0027","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the waters are not above the firmament. For water is naturally heavy. But the proper place of a heavy thing is not to be above, but only below. Therefore the waters are not above the firmament.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0028","text":"[I q. 68 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31278)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0029","text":"Further, water is naturally fluid. But that which is fluid cannot remain upon a round body, as is evident by experiment. Therefore, since the firmament is a round body, water cannot be above the firmament.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0030","text":"[I q. 68 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31279)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0031","text":"Further, water, since it is an element, is ordered to the generation of a mixed body, just as the imperfect is ordered to the perfect. But above the firmament there is no place of mixture, but above the earth. Therefore water would be there above the firmament in vain. But nothing in God's works is in vain. Therefore the waters are not above the firmament.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0032","text":"[I q. 68 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31280)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0033","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Gen. I that he *divided the waters which were above the firmament from those which were beneath the firmament*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0034","text":"[I q. 68 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31281)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0035","text":"I answer that, as Augustine says, in II On the Literal Meaning of Genesis, *the authority of this Scripture is greater than the capacity of all human intellect. Hence, however and of whatever kind those waters may be there, we nevertheless do not in the least doubt that they are there*. But what kind of waters those are is not assigned in the same way by everyone. For Origen says that those waters which are above the heavens are spiritual substances; whence in Psalm CXLVIII it is said, *waters that are above the heavens, praise the name of the Lord*; and Dan. III, *all waters that are above the heavens, bless the Lord*. But Basil replies to this, in III Hexaemeron, that this is not said because the waters are rational creatures, but because *the consideration of them, prudently contemplated by those possessing sense, accomplishes the glorification of the Creator*. Hence there the same is said of fire and hail and things of this kind, concerning which it is clear that they are not rational creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0036","text":"It must therefore be said that they are corporeal waters. But what kind of waters they are must be determined in different ways, according to the different view concerning the firmament. For if by the firmament is understood the starry heaven, which is held to be of the nature of the four elements, then by equal reason the waters which are above the heavens may be believed to be of the same nature as elemental waters. But if by the firmament is understood the starry heaven, which is not of the nature of the four elements, then those waters which are above the firmament will not be of the nature of elemental waters; but just as, according to Strabo, the Empyrean heaven is called, that is, fiery, on account of brightness alone, so another heaven will be called watery on account of transparency alone, which is above the starry heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0037","text":"Even if it be posited that the firmament is of another nature besides the four elements, it can still be said that it divides the waters, if by water we understand not the element of water but the formless matter of bodies, as Augustine says, On Genesis against the Manichees; because according to this, whatever is between bodies divides waters from waters. But if by the firmament is understood that part of the air in which clouds are condensed, then the waters which are above the firmament are waters which, dissolved into vapour, are raised above some part of the air, from which rains are generated. For to say that waters dissolved into vapour are raised above the starry heaven, as some have said—whose opinion Augustine touches upon in II On the Literal Meaning of Genesis—is altogether impossible. First, on account of the solidity of heaven. Second, on account of the intermediate region of fire, which would consume vapours of this kind. Third, because the place to which light and rare things are borne is beneath the concavity of the lunar sphere.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0038","text":"Again, because it is sensibly apparent that vapours are not raised even to the summits of certain mountains. What they also say about the rarefaction of a body to infinity, on the ground that body is divisible to infinity, is vain. For a natural body is not divided or rarefied to infinity, but only to a determinate limit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0039","text":"[I q. 68 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31282)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0040","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that some think this argument should be solved by the fact that waters, although naturally heavy, are nevertheless held above the heavens by divine power. But Augustine excludes this solution, in Book II On the Literal Meaning of Genesis, saying that *we must now inquire how God instituted the natures of things, not what He may will to work in them by a miracle of His power*. Hence it must be said otherwise, that according to the last two opinions concerning the waters and the firmament, the solution is clear from what has been said. But according to the first opinion, another order must be posited among the elements than Aristotle posits: namely, that certain dense waters are around the earth, while certain rarefied waters are around heaven, so that those are related to heaven as these are to earth. Or else that by water is understood the matter of bodies, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0041","text":"[I q. 68 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31283)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0042","text":"To the second, too, the solution is clear from what has been said, according to the last two opinions. But according to the first, Basil answers in two ways. In one way, because it is not necessary that everything which appears round in its concavity should also be round above according to its convexity. In another way, because the waters which are above the heavens are not fluid, but are fixed around heaven as if by icy solidity. Hence they are also called by some the crystalline heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0043","text":"[I q. 68 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31284)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0044","text":"To the third, it must be said that, according to the third opinion, the waters are above the firmament, raised into vapour for the usefulness of rains. But according to the second opinion, the waters are above the firmament, that is, the whole transparent heaven without stars. Some posit this as the first moved sphere, which turns the whole heaven with its daily motion, in order to effect through the daily motion the continuity of generation; just as the heaven in which the stars are, through the motion which is according to the zodiac, effects the diversity of generation and corruption, through approach and recession, and through the diverse powers of the stars. But according to the first opinion, the waters are there, as Basil says, to temper the heat of the heavenly bodies. Some have taken as a sign of this, as Augustine says, that the star of Saturn, on account of the proximity of the upper waters, is very cold.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0045","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0046","text":"[I q. 68 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31285)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0047","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that the firmament does not divide waters from waters. For one body according to species has one natural place. But all water is the same in species as all water, as the Philosopher says. Therefore waters are not to be distinguished from waters according to place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0048","text":"[I q. 68 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31286)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0049","text":"If it be said that those waters which are above the firmament are of a different species from the waters which are beneath the firmament, on the contrary, things that are diverse according to species do not require something else to distinguish them. Therefore, if the upper and lower waters differ in species, the firmament does not distinguish them from one another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0050","text":"[I q. 68 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31287)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0051","text":"Moreover, that which distinguishes waters from waters seems to be touched by waters on either side, as if someone were to build a wall in the middle of a river. But it is manifest that the lower waters do not reach as far as the firmament. Therefore the firmament does not divide waters from waters.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0052","text":"[I q. 68 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31288)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0053","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Gen. I, *let there be a firmament in the midst of the waters, and let it divide waters from waters*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0054","text":"[I q. 68 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31289)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0055","text":"I answer that it must be said that someone, considering the letter of Genesis only superficially, could conceive such an imagination, according to the position of certain ancient philosophers. For some held water to be a certain infinite body, and the principle of all other bodies. One could indeed understand that immensity of waters by the name of the abyss, when it is said that darkness was upon the face of the abyss. They also held that this sensible heaven which we see does not contain within itself all corporeal things, but that there is an infinite body of waters above heaven. And thus someone could say that the firmament of heaven divides the outer waters from the inner waters, that is, from all the bodies contained beneath heaven, of which they held water to be the principle. But because this position is discovered to be false by sound arguments, it must not be said that this is the understanding of Scripture.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0056","text":"But it should be considered that Moses was speaking to a rude people, and, condescending to their weakness, proposed to them only those things that manifestly appear to sense. Now all, however rude, perceive by sense that earth and water are bodies. But air is not perceived by all to be a body, so much so that even certain philosophers said that air is nothing, calling a vacuum full of air. And therefore Moses makes explicit mention of water and earth, but does not expressly name air, lest he propose to the unlearned something unknown. Yet, in order to express the truth to those capable of it, he gives occasion for understanding air, signifying it as though joined to water when he says that darkness was upon the face of the abyss; by which it is given to understand that above the face of the water there is some transparent body which is the subject of light and darkness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0057","text":"Thus, therefore, whether by the firmament we understand the heaven in which the stars are, or the cloudy space of air, it is fittingly said that the firmament divides waters from waters, according as formless matter is signified by water; or according as all transparent bodies are understood under the name of waters. For the starry heaven distinguishes the lower transparent bodies from the higher ones. But the cloudy air distinguishes the upper part of the air, in which rains and impressions of this sort are generated, from the lower part of the air, which is joined to water and is understood under the name of waters.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0058","text":"[I q. 68 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31290)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0059","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, if by the firmament the starry heaven is understood, the upper waters are not of the same species as the lower. But if by the firmament the cloudy air is understood, then both waters are of the same species. And in that case two places are assigned to the waters not for the same reason; rather, the upper place is the place of the generation of waters, whereas the lower place is the place of their rest.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0060","text":"[I q. 68 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31291)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0061","text":"To the second, it must be said that, if the waters are taken as diverse according to species, the firmament is said to divide waters from waters, not as a cause effecting the division, but as the boundary of both waters.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0062","text":"[I q. 68 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31292)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0063","text":"To the third, it must be said that Moses, on account of the invisibility of air and similar bodies, comprehended all bodies of this sort under the name of water. And thus it is manifest that on either side of the firmament, however it be understood, there are waters.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0064","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0065","text":"[I q. 68 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31293)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0066","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that there is only one heaven. For heaven is divided against earth, when it is said, *in the beginning God created heaven and earth*. But there is only one earth. Therefore there is also only one heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0067","text":"[I q. 68 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31294)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0068","text":"Moreover, everything that consists of its whole matter is only one. But heaven is of this sort, as the Philosopher proves in I *On the Heavens*. Therefore heaven is only one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0069","text":"[I q. 68 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31295)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0070","text":"Moreover, whatever is said univocally of several things is said of them according to one common account. But if there are several heavens, “heaven” is said univocally of the several; for if equivocally, they would not properly be called several heavens. Therefore, if several heavens are spoken of, there must be some common account according to which they are called heavens. But this cannot be assigned. Therefore it must not be said that there are several heavens.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0071","text":"[I q. 68 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31296)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0072","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Psalm CXLVIII, *praise him, heavens of heavens*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0073","text":"[I q. 68 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31297)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0074","text":"I answer that, on this matter, there seems to be a certain difference between Basil and Chrysostom. For Chrysostom says that there is but one heaven; and that its being said in the plural, heavens of heavens, is owing to a property of the Hebrew language, in which it is customary for heaven to be signified only in the plural; just as there are also many nouns in Latin that lack a singular. But Basil, and Damascene following him, say that there are several heavens. Yet this difference is more in word than in reality. For Chrysostom calls one heaven the whole body which is above the earth and water; for even birds, which fly in the air, are on this account called birds of heaven. But because in this body there are many distinctions, Basil therefore posited several heavens. Therefore, for knowing the distinction of the heavens, it must be considered that heaven is spoken of in Scripture in three ways. For sometimes it is spoken of properly and naturally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0075","text":"And thus heaven is called a certain lofty body, luminous actually or potentially, and incorruptible by nature. And according to this, three heavens are posited. The first, wholly luminous, which they call the Empyrean. The second, wholly diaphanous, which they call the watery or crystalline heaven. The third, partly diaphanous and partly actually luminous, which they call the starry heaven, and which is divided into eight spheres, namely, the sphere of the fixed stars and the seven spheres of the planets; which may be called eight heavens. Secondly, heaven is spoken of by participation in some property of a heavenly body, namely loftiness and luminosity actually or potentially. And thus Damascene posits as one heaven all that space which extends from the waters to the orb of the moon, calling it the aerial heaven. And thus, according to him, there are three heavens: the aerial, the starry, and another higher one, by which he understands that to which the Apostle is read to have been caught up, even to the third heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0076","text":"But because that space contains two elements, namely fire and air, and in each of them an upper and a lower region are spoken of, therefore Rabanus distinguishes this heaven into four: calling the highest region of fire the fiery heaven; but the lower region, the Olympian heaven, from the height of a certain mountain called Olympus; while he called the highest region of air the ethereal heaven, because of its inflammability; and the lower region, the aerial heaven. And thus, when these four heavens are counted together with the three higher ones, there are in all, according to Rabanus, seven corporeal heavens. Thirdly, heaven is spoken of metaphorically. And thus sometimes the Holy Trinity itself is called heaven, on account of its spiritual loftiness and light. Of this heaven the Devil is expounded to have said, I will ascend into heaven, that is, to equality with God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0077","text":"Sometimes also the spiritual goods in which the reward of the saints consists are called heavens, on account of their eminence; where it is said, *your reward is great in the heavens*, as Augustine explains. But sometimes the three kinds of supernatural visions, namely the corporeal, the imaginary, and the intellectual, are called three heavens. Concerning these, Augustine explains that Paul was caught up even to the third heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0078","text":"[I q. 68 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31298)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0079","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that the earth is related to heaven as the center to the circumference. But around one center there can be many circumferences. Hence, while there is one earth, many heavens are posited.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0080","text":"[I q. 68 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31299)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0081","text":"To the second it must be said that that argument proceeds concerning heaven insofar as it implies the totality of corporeal creatures. For in this sense there is only one heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0082","text":"[I q. 68 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31300)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0083","text":"To the third it must be said that in all the heavens loftiness and some luminosity are found in common, as is clear from what has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_60:P0083"]}],"source_digest":"e75d3afe153d61e0be489acef5aac64d6f3445b42a8ce8bd8da07a1dce5d893a","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 68"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 68","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61","title":"First Part · Question 69","order":70,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_61","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 69 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31301)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de opere tertiae diei. Et circa hoc quaeruntur duo. Primo, de congregatione aquarum. Secundo, de productione plantarum.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 69 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31302)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aquarum congregatio non convenienter dicatur facta tertia die. Ea enim quae facta sunt prima et secunda die, verbo factionis exprimuntur, dicitur enim, *dixit Deus fiat lux, et, fiat firmamentum*. Sed tertia dies condividitur duabus primis. Ergo opus tertiae diei debuit exprimi verbo factionis, et non solum verbo congregationis.\n\n[I q. 69 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31303)\n\nPraeterea, terra prius undique erat aquis cooperta, propter hoc enim invisibilis dicebatur. Non erat ergo aliquis locus super terram, in quo aquae congregari possent.\n\n[I q. 69 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31304)\n\nPraeterea, quae non sunt continuata ad invicem, non habent unum locum. Sed non omnes aquae habent ad invicem continuitatem. Ergo non sunt omnes aquae congregatae in unum locum.\n\n[I q. 69 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31305)\n\nPraeterea, congregatio ad motum localem pertinet. Sed aquae naturaliter videntur fluentes, et ad mare currentes. Non ergo fuit necessarium ad hoc divinum praeceptum adhiberi.\n\n[I q. 69 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31306)\n\nPraeterea, terra etiam in principio suae creationis nominatur, cum dicitur, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*. Inconvenienter ergo dicitur tertia die nomen terrae impositum.\n\n[I q. 69 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31307)\n\nSed in contrarium sufficit auctoritas Scripturae.\n\n[I q. 69 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31308)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod hic oportet aliter dicere secundum expositionem Augustini, et aliorum sanctorum. Augustinus enim in omnibus his operibus non ponit durationis ordinem, sed solum originis et naturae. Dicit enim primo creatam naturam spiritualem informem, et naturam corporalem absque omni forma (quam dicit primo significari nomine terrae et aquae), non quia haec informitas formationem praecesserit tempore, sed origine tantum. Neque una formatio, secundum eum, praecessit aliam duratione; sed solum naturae ordine. Secundum quem ordinem necesse fuit ut primo poneretur formatio supremae naturae, scilicet spiritualis, per hoc quod legitur prima die lux facta. Sicut autem spiritualis natura praeeminet corporali, ita superiora corpora praeeminent inferioribus. Unde secundo loco tangitur formatio superiorum corporum, cum dicitur, fiat firmamentum; per quod intelligitur impressio formae caelestis in materiam informem, non prius existentem tempore, sed origine tantum. Tertio vero loco ponitur impressio formarum elementarium in materiam informem, non tempore, sed origine praecedentem. Unde per hoc quod dicitur, *congregentur aquae, et appareat arida*, intelligitur quod materiae corporali impressa est forma substantialis aquae, per quam competit sibi talis motus; et forma substantialis terrae, per quam competit sibi sic videri. Sed secundum alios sanctos, in his operibus etiam ordo durationis attenditur, ponunt enim quod, informitas materiae tempore praecessit formationem, et una formatio aliam. Sed informitas materiae, secundum eos, non intelligitur carentia omnis formae quia iam erat caelum et aqua et terra (quae tria nominantur tanquam manifeste sensu perceptibilia), sed intelligitur informitas materiae carentia debitae distinctionis, et consummatae cuiusdam pulchritudinis. Et secundum haec tria nomina, posuit Scriptura tres informitates. Ad caelum enim, quod est superius, pertinet informitas tenebrarum, quia ex eo est origo luminis. Informitas vero aquae, quae est media, significatur nomine abyssi, quia hoc nomen significat quandam immensitatem inordinatam aquarum, ut Augustinus dicit, contra Faustum. Informitas vero terrae tangitur in hoc quod dicitur, quod terra erat invisibilis vel inanis, per hoc quod erat aquis cooperta. Sic igitur formatio supremi corporis facta est prima die. Et quia tempus sequitur motum caeli, tempus autem est numerus motus supremi corporis; per huiusmodi formationem facta est distinctio temporis, noctis scilicet et diei. Secundo vero die formatum est medium corpus, scilicet aqua, per firmamentum accipiens quandam distinctionem et ordinem (ita quod sub nomine aquae etiam alia comprehenduntur, ut supra dictum est). Tertio vero die formatum est ultimum corpus, scilicet terra, per hoc quod discooperta est aquis; et facta est distinctio in infimo, quae dicitur terrae et maris. Unde satis congrue, sicut informitatem terrae expresserat dicens quod terra erat invisibilis vel inanis, ita eius formationem exprimit per hoc quod dicit, et appareat arida.\n\n[I q. 69 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31309)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, ideo in opere tertiae diei non utitur Scriptura verbo factionis, sicut in praecedentibus operibus, ut ostendatur quod superiores formae, scilicet spirituales Angelorum, et caelestium corporum, sunt perfectae in esse et stabiles; formae vero inferiorum corporum sunt imperfectae et mobiles. Et ideo per congregationem aquarum et apparentiam aridae, impressio talium formarum designatur, *aqua enim est labiliter fluxa, terra stabiliter fixa*, ut ipse dicit in II super Gen. ad Litt. Secundum vero alios, dicendum est quod opus tertiae diei est perfectum secundum solum motum localem. Et ideo non oportuit quod Scriptura uteretur verbo factionis.\n\n[I q. 69 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31310)\n\nAd secundum patet solutio secundum Augustinum, quia non oportet dicere quod terra primo esset cooperta aquis, et postmodum sint aquae congregatae; sed quod in tali congregatione fuerunt productae. Secundum vero alios, tripliciter respondetur, ut Augustinus dicit, I super Gen. ad Litt. Uno modo, ut aquae in maiorem altitudinem sint elevatae in loco ubi sunt congregatae. Nam mare esse altius terra experimento compertum est in mari rubro, ut Basilius dicit. Secundo, ut dicatur quod *rarior aqua velut nebula, terras tegebat, quae congregatione densata est*. Tertio modo, ut dicatur quod *terra potuit aliquas partes praebere concavas, quibus confluentes aquae reciperentur*. Inter quas prima videtur probabilior.\n\n[I q. 69 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31311)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod omnes aquae unum terminum habent, scilicet mare, in quod confluunt vel manifestis vel occultis meatibus. Et propter hoc dicuntur aquae congregari in unum locum. Vel dicit unum locum, non simpliciter, sed per comparationem ad locum terrae siccae; ut sit sensus, congregentur aquae in unum locum, idest, seorsum a terra sicca. Nam ad designandam pluralitatem locorum aquae, subdit quod congregationes aquarum appellavit maria.\n\n[I q. 69 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31312)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod iussio Dei naturalem motum corporibus praebet. Unde dicitur quod suis naturalibus motibus faciunt verbum eius. Vel potest dici quod naturale esset quod aqua undique esset circa terram, sicut aer undique est circa aquam et terram; sed propter necessitatem finis, ut scilicet animalia et plantae essent super terram, oportuit quod aliqua pars terrae esset discooperta aquis. Quod quidem aliqui philosophi attribuunt actioni solis, per elevationem vaporum desiccantis terram. Sed sacra Scriptura attribuit hoc potestati divinae, non solum in Genesi, sed etiam in Iob XXXVIII, ubi ex persona domini dicitur, *circumdedi mare terminis meis; et* Ierem. V, *me ergo non timebitis, ait dominus, qui posui arenam terminum mari?*\n\n[I q. 69 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31313)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, per terram de qua primo fiebat mentio, intelligitur materia prima, nunc autem intelligitur ipsum elementum terrae. Vel potest dici, secundum Basilium, quod primo nominabatur terra secundum naturam suam, nunc autem nominatur ex sua principali proprietate, quae est siccitas. Unde dicitur quod vocavit aridam, terram. Vel potest dici, secundum Rabbi Moysen, quod ubicumque dicitur vocavit, significatur aequivocatio nominis. Unde prius dictum est quod vocavit lucem, diem, propter hoc quod etiam dies vocatur spatium vigintiquatuor horarum, secundum quod ibidem dicitur, factum est vespere et mane dies unus. Similiter dicitur quod firmamentum, idest aerem, vocavit caelum, quia etiam caelum dicitur quod est primo creatum. Similiter etiam dicitur hic quod aridam, idest illam partem quae est discooperta aquis, vocavit terram, prout distinguitur contra mare, quamvis communi nomine terra vocetur, sive sit aquis cooperta, sive discooperta. Intelligitur autem ubique per hoc quod dicitur vocavit, idest, dedit naturam vel proprietatem ut possit sic vocari.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 69 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31314)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod plantarum productio inconvenienter tertia die facta legatur. Plantae enim habent vitam sicut et animalia. Sed productio animalium non ponitur inter opera distinctionis, sed pertinet ad opus ornatus. Ergo nec productio plantarum commemorari debuit in tertia die, quae pertinet ad opus distinctionis.\n\n[I q. 69 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31315)\n\nPraeterea, illud quod pertinet ad maledictionem terrae, non debuit commemorari cum formatione terrae. Sed productio quarundam plantarum pertinet ad maledictionem terrae; secundum illud Gen. III, *maledicta terra in opere tuo, spinas et tribulos germinabit tibi*. Ergo productio plantarum universaliter non debuit commemorari in tertia die, quae pertinet ad formationem terrae.\n\n[I q. 69 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31316)\n\nPraeterea, sicut plantae adhaerent terrae, ita etiam lapides et metalla; et tamen non fit mentio de eis in terrae formatione. Ergo nec plantae fieri debuerunt tertia die.\n\n[I q. 69 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31317)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *protulit terra herbam virentem*; et postea sequitur, *factum est vespere et mane dies tertius*.\n\n[I q. 69 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31318)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, in tertia die informitas terrae removetur. Duplex autem informitas circa terram describebatur, una, quod erat invisibilis vel inanis, quia erat aquis cooperta; alia, quod erat incomposita sive vacua, idest non habens debitum decorem, qui acquiritur terrae ex plantis eam quodammodo vestientibus. Et ideo utraque informitas in hac tertia die removetur, prima quidem, per hoc quod aquae congregatae sunt in unum locum, et apparuit arida; secunda vero, per hoc quod protulit terra herbam virentem. Sed tamen circa productionem plantarum, aliter opinatur Augustinus ab aliis. Alii enim expositores dicunt quod plantae productae sunt actu in suis speciebus in hac tertia die, secundum quod superficies litterae sonat. Augustinus autem, V Sup. Gen. ad Litt., dicit quod *causaliter tunc dictum est produxisse terram herbam et lignum, idest producendi accepisse virtutem*. Et hoc quidem confirmat auctoritate Scripturae. Dicitur enim Gen. II, *istae sunt generationes caeli et terrae, quando creata sunt, in die quo Deus fecit caelum et terram, et omne virgultum agri, antequam oriretur in terra, omnemque herbam regionis, priusquam germinaret*. Ante ergo quam orirentur super terram, factae sunt causaliter in terra. Confirmat autem hoc etiam ratione. Quia in illis primis diebus condidit Deus creaturam originaliter vel causaliter, a quo opere postmodum requievit, qui tamen postmodum, secundum administrationem rerum conditarum per opus propagationis, usque modo operatur. Producere autem plantas ex terra, ad opus propagationis pertinet. Non ergo in tertia die productae sunt plantae in actu, sed causaliter tantum. Quamvis, secundum alios, dici possit quod prima institutio specierum ad opera sex dierum pertinet, sed quod ex speciebus primo institutis generatio similium in specie procedat, hoc iam pertinet ad rerum administrationem. Et hoc est quod Scriptura dicit, antequam oriretur super terram, vel antequam germinaret; idest, antequam ex similibus similia producerentur, sicut nunc naturaliter fieri videmus secundum viam seminationis. Unde signanter Scriptura dicit, germinet terra herbam virentem et facientem semen, quia scilicet sunt productae perfectae species plantarum, ex quibus semina aliarum orirentur. Nec refert ubicumque habeant vim seminativam, utrum scilicet in radice, vel in stipite, vel in fructu.\n\n[I q. 69 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31319)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod vita in plantis est occulta, quia carent motu locali et sensu, quibus animatum ab inanimato maxime distinguitur. Et ideo, quia immobiliter terrae inhaerent, earum productio ponitur quasi quaedam terrae formatio.\n\n[I q. 69 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31320)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod etiam ante illam maledictionem, spinae et tribuli producti erant vel virtute vel actu. Sed non erant producti homini in poenam; ut scilicet terra quam propter cibum coleret, infructuosa quaedam et noxia germinaret. Unde dictum est, *germinabit tibi*.\n\n[I q. 69 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31321)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Moyses ea tantum proposuit quae in manifesto apparent, sicut iam dictum est. Corpora autem mineralia habent generationem occultam in visceribus terrae. Et iterum, non habent manifestam distinctionem a terra, sed quaedam terrae species videntur. Et ideo de eis mentionem non fecit.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0002","text":"[I q. 69 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31301)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de opere tertiae diei. Et circa hoc quaeruntur duo. Primo, de congregatione aquarum. Secundo, de productione plantarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0005","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31302)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aquarum congregatio non convenienter dicatur facta tertia die. Ea enim quae facta sunt prima et secunda die, verbo factionis exprimuntur, dicitur enim, *dixit Deus fiat lux, et, fiat firmamentum*. Sed tertia dies condividitur duabus primis. Ergo opus tertiae diei debuit exprimi verbo factionis, et non solum verbo congregationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0007","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31303)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0008","text":"Praeterea, terra prius undique erat aquis cooperta, propter hoc enim invisibilis dicebatur. Non erat ergo aliquis locus super terram, in quo aquae congregari possent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0009","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31304)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0010","text":"Praeterea, quae non sunt continuata ad invicem, non habent unum locum. Sed non omnes aquae habent ad invicem continuitatem. Ergo non sunt omnes aquae congregatae in unum locum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0011","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31305)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0012","text":"Praeterea, congregatio ad motum localem pertinet. Sed aquae naturaliter videntur fluentes, et ad mare currentes. Non ergo fuit necessarium ad hoc divinum praeceptum adhiberi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0013","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31306)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0014","text":"Praeterea, terra etiam in principio suae creationis nominatur, cum dicitur, *in principio creavit Deus caelum et terram*. Inconvenienter ergo dicitur tertia die nomen terrae impositum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0015","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31307)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0016","text":"Sed in contrarium sufficit auctoritas Scripturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0017","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31308)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0018","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod hic oportet aliter dicere secundum expositionem Augustini, et aliorum sanctorum. Augustinus enim in omnibus his operibus non ponit durationis ordinem, sed solum originis et naturae. Dicit enim primo creatam naturam spiritualem informem, et naturam corporalem absque omni forma (quam dicit primo significari nomine terrae et aquae), non quia haec informitas formationem praecesserit tempore, sed origine tantum. Neque una formatio, secundum eum, praecessit aliam duratione; sed solum naturae ordine. Secundum quem ordinem necesse fuit ut primo poneretur formatio supremae naturae, scilicet spiritualis, per hoc quod legitur prima die lux facta. Sicut autem spiritualis natura praeeminet corporali, ita superiora corpora praeeminent inferioribus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0019","text":"Unde secundo loco tangitur formatio superiorum corporum, cum dicitur, fiat firmamentum; per quod intelligitur impressio formae caelestis in materiam informem, non prius existentem tempore, sed origine tantum. Tertio vero loco ponitur impressio formarum elementarium in materiam informem, non tempore, sed origine praecedentem. Unde per hoc quod dicitur, *congregentur aquae, et appareat arida*, intelligitur quod materiae corporali impressa est forma substantialis aquae, per quam competit sibi talis motus; et forma substantialis terrae, per quam competit sibi sic videri. Sed secundum alios sanctos, in his operibus etiam ordo durationis attenditur, ponunt enim quod, informitas materiae tempore praecessit formationem, et una formatio aliam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0020","text":"Sed informitas materiae, secundum eos, non intelligitur carentia omnis formae quia iam erat caelum et aqua et terra (quae tria nominantur tanquam manifeste sensu perceptibilia), sed intelligitur informitas materiae carentia debitae distinctionis, et consummatae cuiusdam pulchritudinis. Et secundum haec tria nomina, posuit Scriptura tres informitates. Ad caelum enim, quod est superius, pertinet informitas tenebrarum, quia ex eo est origo luminis. Informitas vero aquae, quae est media, significatur nomine abyssi, quia hoc nomen significat