 q. 69 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31310)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0025","text":"Ad secundum patet solutio secundum Augustinum, quia non oportet dicere quod terra primo esset cooperta aquis, et postmodum sint aquae congregatae; sed quod in tali congregatione fuerunt productae. Secundum vero alios, tripliciter respondetur, ut Augustinus dicit, I super Gen. ad Litt. Uno modo, ut aquae in maiorem altitudinem sint elevatae in loco ubi sunt congregatae. Nam mare esse altius terra experimento compertum est in mari rubro, ut Basilius dicit. Secundo, ut dicatur quod *rarior aqua velut nebula, terras tegebat, quae congregatione densata est*. Tertio modo, ut dicatur quod *terra potuit aliquas partes praebere concavas, quibus confluentes aquae reciperentur*. Inter quas prima videtur probabilior.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0026","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31311)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0027","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod omnes aquae unum terminum habent, scilicet mare, in quod confluunt vel manifestis vel occultis meatibus. Et propter hoc dicuntur aquae congregari in unum locum. Vel dicit unum locum, non simpliciter, sed per comparationem ad locum terrae siccae; ut sit sensus, congregentur aquae in unum locum, idest, seorsum a terra sicca. Nam ad designandam pluralitatem locorum aquae, subdit quod congregationes aquarum appellavit maria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0028","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31312)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0029","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod iussio Dei naturalem motum corporibus praebet. Unde dicitur quod suis naturalibus motibus faciunt verbum eius. Vel potest dici quod naturale esset quod aqua undique esset circa terram, sicut aer undique est circa aquam et terram; sed propter necessitatem finis, ut scilicet animalia et plantae essent super terram, oportuit quod aliqua pars terrae esset discooperta aquis. Quod quidem aliqui philosophi attribuunt actioni solis, per elevationem vaporum desiccantis terram. Sed sacra Scriptura attribuit hoc potestati divinae, non solum in Genesi, sed etiam in Iob XXXVIII, ubi ex persona domini dicitur, *circumdedi mare terminis meis; et* Ierem. V, *me ergo non timebitis, ait dominus, qui posui arenam terminum mari?*","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0030","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31313)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0031","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, per terram de qua primo fiebat mentio, intelligitur materia prima, nunc autem intelligitur ipsum elementum terrae. Vel potest dici, secundum Basilium, quod primo nominabatur terra secundum naturam suam, nunc autem nominatur ex sua principali proprietate, quae est siccitas. Unde dicitur quod vocavit aridam, terram. Vel potest dici, secundum Rabbi Moysen, quod ubicumque dicitur vocavit, significatur aequivocatio nominis. Unde prius dictum est quod vocavit lucem, diem, propter hoc quod etiam dies vocatur spatium vigintiquatuor horarum, secundum quod ibidem dicitur, factum est vespere et mane dies unus. Similiter dicitur quod firmamentum, idest aerem, vocavit caelum, quia etiam caelum dicitur quod est primo creatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0032","text":"Similiter etiam dicitur hic quod aridam, idest illam partem quae est discooperta aquis, vocavit terram, prout distinguitur contra mare, quamvis communi nomine terra vocetur, sive sit aquis cooperta, sive discooperta. Intelligitur autem ubique per hoc quod dicitur vocavit, idest, dedit naturam vel proprietatem ut possit sic vocari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0033","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0034","text":"[I q. 69 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31314)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0035","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod plantarum productio inconvenienter tertia die facta legatur. Plantae enim habent vitam sicut et animalia. Sed productio animalium non ponitur inter opera distinctionis, sed pertinet ad opus ornatus. Ergo nec productio plantarum commemorari debuit in tertia die, quae pertinet ad opus distinctionis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0036","text":"[I q. 69 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31315)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0037","text":"Praeterea, illud quod pertinet ad maledictionem terrae, non debuit commemorari cum formatione terrae. Sed productio quarundam plantarum pertinet ad maledictionem terrae; secundum illud Gen. III, *maledicta terra in opere tuo, spinas et tribulos germinabit tibi*. Ergo productio plantarum universaliter non debuit commemorari in tertia die, quae pertinet ad formationem terrae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0038","text":"[I q. 69 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31316)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0039","text":"Praeterea, sicut plantae adhaerent terrae, ita etiam lapides et metalla; et tamen non fit mentio de eis in terrae formatione. Ergo nec plantae fieri debuerunt tertia die.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0040","text":"[I q. 69 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31317)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0041","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *protulit terra herbam virentem*; et postea sequitur, *factum est vespere et mane dies tertius*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0042","text":"[I q. 69 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31318)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0043","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, in tertia die informitas terrae removetur. Duplex autem informitas circa terram describebatur, una, quod erat invisibilis vel inanis, quia erat aquis cooperta; alia, quod erat incomposita sive vacua, idest non habens debitum decorem, qui acquiritur terrae ex plantis eam quodammodo vestientibus. Et ideo utraque informitas in hac tertia die removetur, prima quidem, per hoc quod aquae congregatae sunt in unum locum, et apparuit arida; secunda vero, per hoc quod protulit terra herbam virentem. Sed tamen circa productionem plantarum, aliter opinatur Augustinus ab aliis. Alii enim expositores dicunt quod plantae productae sunt actu in suis speciebus in hac tertia die, secundum quod superficies litterae sonat. Augustinus autem, V Sup. Gen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0044","text":"ad Litt., dicit quod *causaliter tunc dictum est produxisse terram herbam et lignum, idest producendi accepisse virtutem*. Et hoc quidem confirmat auctoritate Scripturae. Dicitur enim Gen. II, *istae sunt generationes caeli et terrae, quando creata sunt, in die quo Deus fecit caelum et terram, et omne virgultum agri, antequam oriretur in terra, omnemque herbam regionis, priusquam germinaret*. Ante ergo quam orirentur super terram, factae sunt causaliter in terra. Confirmat autem hoc etiam ratione. Quia in illis primis diebus condidit Deus creaturam originaliter vel causaliter, a quo opere postmodum requievit, qui tamen postmodum, secundum administrationem rerum conditarum per opus propagationis, usque modo operatur. Producere autem plantas ex terra, ad opus propagationis pertinet. Non ergo in tertia die productae sunt plantae in actu, sed causaliter tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0045","text":"Quamvis, secundum alios, dici possit quod prima institutio specierum ad opera sex dierum pertinet, sed quod ex speciebus primo institutis generatio similium in specie procedat, hoc iam pertinet ad rerum administrationem. Et hoc est quod Scriptura dicit, antequam oriretur super terram, vel antequam germinaret; idest, antequam ex similibus similia producerentur, sicut nunc naturaliter fieri videmus secundum viam seminationis. Unde signanter Scriptura dicit, germinet terra herbam virentem et facientem semen, quia scilicet sunt productae perfectae species plantarum, ex quibus semina aliarum orirentur. Nec refert ubicumque habeant vim seminativam, utrum scilicet in radice, vel in stipite, vel in fructu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0046","text":"[I q. 69 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31319)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0047","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod vita in plantis est occulta, quia carent motu locali et sensu, quibus animatum ab inanimato maxime distinguitur. Et ideo, quia immobiliter terrae inhaerent, earum productio ponitur quasi quaedam terrae formatio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0048","text":"[I q. 69 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31320)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0049","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod etiam ante illam maledictionem, spinae et tribuli producti erant vel virtute vel actu. Sed non erant producti homini in poenam; ut scilicet terra quam propter cibum coleret, infructuosa quaedam et noxia germinaret. Unde dictum est, *germinabit tibi*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0050","text":"[I q. 69 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31321)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0051","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Moyses ea tantum proposuit quae in manifesto apparent, sicut iam dictum est. Corpora autem mineralia habent generationem occultam in visceribus terrae. Et iterum, non habent manifestam distinctionem a terra, sed quaedam terrae species videntur. Et ideo de eis mentionem non fecit.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"5cab092fd99be9389ff516588fde3741d63b610445925a7036c17021c582ab1d","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T145627218027Z_4d6cd30b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 69","source_sha256":"5cab092fd99be9389ff516588fde3741d63b610445925a7036c17021c582ab1d","sha256":"3db735049bad2eef7e7757725dc5537e5a7c6efb821251b64d37383c720c6758","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:56:27.218088+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0002","text":"[I q. 69 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31301)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the work of the third day. And concerning this, two questions are asked. First, concerning the gathering of the waters. Second, concerning the production of plants.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0005","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31302)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the gathering of the waters is not suitably said to have been made on the third day. For those things which were made on the first and second days are expressed by a word of making; for it is said, *God said, Let light be made*, and, *Let a firmament be made*. But the third day is divided along with the first two. Therefore the work of the third day ought to have been expressed by a word of making, and not only by a word of gathering.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0007","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31303)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0008","text":"Further, the earth was previously covered everywhere by waters; for this reason it was called invisible. Therefore there was no place upon the earth in which the waters could be gathered.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0009","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31304)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0010","text":"Further, things which are not continuous with one another do not have one place. But not all waters have continuity with one another. Therefore not all waters are gathered into one place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0011","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31305)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0012","text":"Further, gathering pertains to local motion. But waters seem naturally to flow and to run toward the sea. Therefore it was not necessary for a divine command to be applied for this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0013","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31306)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0014","text":"Further, the earth is also named at the beginning of its creation, when it is said, *In the beginning God created heaven and earth*. Therefore it is unfittingly said that the name of earth was imposed on the third day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0015","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31307)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0016","text":"But, on the contrary, the authority of Scripture suffices.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0017","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31308)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0018","text":"I answer that here one must speak differently according to the exposition of Augustine and of the other saints. For Augustine, in all these works, does not posit an order of duration, but only one of origin and nature. For he says that spiritual nature was first created formless, and bodily nature without every form (which he says is first signified by the name of earth and water), not because this formlessness preceded formation in time, but only in origin. Nor, according to him, did one formation precede another in duration, but only in the order of nature. According to this order it was necessary that the formation of the highest nature, namely the spiritual, be set down first, through what is read: light was made on the first day. But just as spiritual nature surpasses bodily nature, so higher bodies surpass lower ones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0019","text":"Hence, in the second place, the formation of the higher bodies is touched upon, when it is said, let a firmament be made; by which is understood the impression of the heavenly form upon formless matter, not previously existing in time, but only in origin. But in the third place is set down the impression of elemental forms upon formless matter, preceding not in time but in origin. Hence, by what is said, *let the waters be gathered, and let the dry land appear*, it is understood that the substantial form of water was impressed upon bodily matter, through which such motion befits it; and the substantial form of earth, through which it befits it to be seen thus. But according to the other saints, an order of duration is also considered in these works; for they posit that the formlessness of matter preceded formation in time, and that one formation preceded another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0020","text":"But according to them, the formlessness of matter is not understood as the lack of every form, because heaven and water and earth already existed (these three being named as manifestly perceptible by sense); rather, the formlessness of matter is understood as the lack of due distinction and of a certain completed beauty. And according to these three names, Scripture posited three formlessnesses. For to heaven, which is higher, pertains the formlessness of darkness, because from it is the origin of light. But the formlessness of water, which is intermediate, is signified by the name abyss, because this name signifies a certain disordered immensity of waters, as Augustine says in *Against Faustus*. But the formlessness of earth is touched upon in what is said, that the earth was invisible or empty, through its being covered by waters. Thus, therefore, the formation of the highest body was made on the first day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0021","text":"And because time follows the motion of heaven, while time is the number of the motion of the highest body, through such a formation the distinction of time was made, namely, of night and day. But on the second day the intermediate body, namely water, was formed through the firmament, receiving a certain distinction and order (so that under the name of water other things too are comprehended, as was said above). But on the third day the lowest body, namely earth, was formed, through its being uncovered from the waters; and a distinction was made in the lowest, which is called that of earth and sea. Hence quite fittingly, just as it had expressed the formlessness of earth by saying that the earth was invisible or empty, so it expresses its formation through what it says: and let the dry land appear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0022","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31309)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0023","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, according to Augustine, Scripture does not use the word of making in the work of the third day, as in the preceding works, in order to show that the higher forms, namely the spiritual forms of angels and of heavenly bodies, are perfect in being and stable; whereas the forms of lower bodies are imperfect and mobile. And therefore, by the gathering of the waters and the appearing of the dry land, the impression of such forms is designated; *for water is fluidly flowing, earth stably fixed*, as he himself says in II *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*. But according to the others, it must be said that the work of the third day is perfect only according to local motion. And therefore it was not necessary that Scripture use the word of making.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0024","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31310)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0025","text":"The solution to the second is clear according to Augustine, because one need not say that the earth was first covered by waters, and that afterward the waters were gathered together; but rather that they were produced in such a gathering. According to others, however, there are three answers, as Augustine says, I, On the Literal Meaning of Genesis. In one way, that the waters were raised to a greater height in the place where they are gathered. For that the sea is higher than the earth has been ascertained by experience in the Red Sea, as Basil says. Secondly, that it be said that *water, rarer like mist, covered the lands, and was condensed by gathering together*. In the third way, that it be said that *the earth could provide certain concave parts, into which the waters flowing together might be received*. Of these, the first seems more probable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0026","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31311)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0027","text":"To the third it must be said that all waters have one terminus, namely the sea, into which they flow by manifest or hidden channels. And for this reason the waters are said to be gathered into one place. Or he says “one place,” not absolutely, but in comparison with the place of dry land; so that the sense is: let the waters be gathered into one place, that is, apart from dry land. For to designate the plurality of places of water, he adds that he called the gatherings of waters seas.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0028","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31312)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0029","text":"To the fourth it must be said that the command of God bestows natural motion upon bodies. Hence it is said that by their natural motions they perform his word. Or it may be said that it would be natural for water to be everywhere around the earth, just as air is everywhere around water and earth; but on account of the necessity of the end, namely, that animals and plants might be upon the earth, it was necessary that some part of the earth be uncovered by waters. Certain philosophers attribute this to the action of the sun, which dries the earth by the elevation of vapors. But Sacred Scripture attributes this to the divine power, not only in Genesis, but also in Job XXXVIII, where it is said in the person of the Lord, *I surrounded the sea with my bounds*; and in Jeremiah V, *will you not therefore fear me, says the Lord, who set sand as a boundary to the sea?*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0030","text":"[I q. 69 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31313)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0031","text":"To the fifth it must be said that, according to Augustine, by the earth first mentioned, prime matter is understood, whereas now the element of earth itself is understood. Or it can be said, according to Basil, that it was first called earth according to its nature, but now it is named from its principal property, which is dryness. Hence it is said that he called the dry land, earth. Or it can be said, according to Rabbi Moses, that wherever “he called” is said, an equivocation of the name is signified. Hence it was said earlier that he called the light, day, because the space of twenty-four hours is also called day, according as it is there said, evening and morning were made, one day. Similarly, it is said that he called the firmament, that is, the air, heaven, because that which was first created is also called heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0032","text":"Likewise, it is also said here that he called the dry land, that is, that part which is uncovered by waters, earth, insofar as it is distinguished from the sea, although by the common name it is called earth whether it is covered by waters or uncovered. But everywhere by this phrase “he called” is understood: that is, he gave it the nature or property such that it could thus be called.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0033","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0034","text":"[I q. 69 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31314)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0035","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the production of plants is inappropriately read as having taken place on the third day. For plants have life, just as animals do. But the production of animals is not placed among the works of distinction, but pertains to the work of adornment. Therefore neither should the production of plants have been commemorated on the third day, which pertains to the work of distinction.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0036","text":"[I q. 69 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31315)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0037","text":"Further, that which pertains to the curse of the earth ought not to have been commemorated with the formation of the earth. But the production of certain plants pertains to the curse of the earth, according to Genesis III: *cursed is the earth in thy work; it shall bring forth thorns and thistles for thee*. Therefore the production of plants universally ought not to have been commemorated on the third day, which pertains to the formation of the earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0038","text":"[I q. 69 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31316)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0039","text":"Further, just as plants adhere to the earth, so also do stones and metals; yet no mention is made of them in the formation of the earth. Therefore neither ought plants to have been made on the third day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0040","text":"[I q. 69 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31317)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0041","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Genesis I, *the earth brought forth green herb*; and afterward follows, *evening and morning were made, the third day*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0042","text":"[I q. 69 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31318)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0043","text":"I answer that, as was said above, on the third day the formlessness of the earth is removed. Now a twofold formlessness concerning the earth was described: one, that it was invisible or void, because it was covered by waters; the other, that it was unarranged or empty, that is, not having its due beauty, which the earth acquires from plants that in a certain manner clothe it. And therefore both formlessnesses are removed on this third day: the first indeed, by the waters being gathered into one place and the dry land appearing; the second, by the earth bringing forth green herb. Yet concerning the production of plants Augustine holds an opinion different from that of others. For other expositors say that plants were produced actually in their species on this third day, according to the sound of the letter. But Augustine, V, On the Literal Meaning of Genesis,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0044","text":"says that *it was then said causally that the earth brought forth herb and tree, that is, that it received the power of bringing them forth*. And he confirms this by the authority of Scripture. For it is said in Genesis II: *these are the generations of heaven and earth, when they were created, in the day in which God made heaven and earth, and every shrub of the field before it arose in the earth, and every herb of the region before it germinated*. Therefore, before they arose upon the earth, they were made causally in the earth. He also confirms this by reason. For in those first days God established the creature originally or causally, from which work he afterward rested; though afterward, according to the governance of things established through the work of propagation, he works even until now. But bringing forth plants from the earth pertains to the work of propagation. Therefore plants were not produced in act on the third day, but only causally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0045","text":"Although, according to others, it can be said that the first institution of species pertains to the works of the six days, but that from species first instituted the generation of like things in species proceeds, this now pertains to the governance of things. And this is what Scripture says: before it arose upon the earth, or before it germinated; that is, before like things were produced from like things, as we now see occurring naturally by the way of seed. Hence Scripture significantly says, let the earth germinate green herb and making seed, because perfect species of plants were produced, from which the seeds of others would arise. Nor does it matter where they have the power of seed, whether in the root, or in the stem, or in the fruit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0046","text":"[I q. 69 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31319)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0047","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that life in plants is hidden, because they lack local motion and sense, by which the animate is most distinguished from the inanimate. And therefore, because they adhere immovably to the earth, their production is set down as a certain formation of the earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0048","text":"[I q. 69 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31320)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0049","text":"To the second, it must be said that even before that curse, thorns and thistles were produced, either potentially or actually. But they were not produced as a punishment for man; namely, so that the earth which he cultivated for food might germinate certain unfruitful and harmful things. Hence it was said, *it shall germinate for you*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0050","text":"[I q. 69 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31321)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0051","text":"To the third, it must be said that Moses set forth only those things that appear openly, as has already been said. Mineral bodies, however, have a hidden generation in the bowels of the earth. And again, they do not have a manifest distinction from the earth, but appear to be certain species of earth. And therefore he made no mention of them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_61:P0051"]}],"source_digest":"07206d14376622838ce96c0d6c58493711556f8394338992fa5bb51da8abd56f","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 69"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 69","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62","title":"First Part · Question 70","order":71,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_62","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 70 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31322)\n\nConsequenter considerandum est de opere ornatus. Et primo, de singulis diebus secundum se; secundo, de omnibus sex diebus in communi. Circa primum ergo, considerandum est primo de opere quartae diei, secundo, de opere quintae; tertio, de opere sextae; quarto, de iis quae pertinent ad septimum diem. Circa primum quaeruntur tria. Primo, de productione luminarium. Secundo, de fine productionis eorum. Tertio, utrum sint animata.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 70 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31323)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod luminaria non debuerint produci quarta die. Luminaria enim sunt corpora incorruptibilia naturaliter. Ergo eorum materia non potest esse absque formis eorum. Sed eorum materia producta est in opere creationis, ante omnem diem. Ergo et eorum formae. Non ergo sunt facta quarta die.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31324)\n\nPraeterea, luminaria sunt quasi vasa luminis. Sed lux est facta prima die. Ergo luminaria fieri debuerunt prima die, et non quarta.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31325)\n\nPraeterea, sicut plantae fixae sunt in terra, ita luminaria fixa sunt in firmamento, unde Scriptura dicit quod posuit ea in firmamento. Sed productio plantarum simul describitur cum formatione terrae, cui inhaerent. Ergo et productio luminarium simul debuit poni, secunda die, cum productione firmamenti.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31326)\n\nPraeterea, sol et luna et alia luminaria sunt causae plantarum. Sed naturali ordine causa praecedit effectum. Ergo luminaria non debuerunt fieri quarta die, sed tertia vel ante.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31327)\n\nPraeterea, multae stellae, secundum astrologos, sunt luna maiores. Non ergo tantum sol et luna debuerunt poni duo magna luminaria.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31328)\n\nSed in contrarium sufficit auctoritas Scripturae.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31329)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in recapitulatione divinorum operum, Scriptura sic dicit, *igitur perfecti sunt caeli et terra, et omnis ornatus eorum*. In quibus verbis triplex opus intelligi potest, scilicet opus creationis, per quod caelum et terra producta leguntur, sed informia. Et opus distinctionis, per quod caelum et terra sunt perfecta, sive per formas substantiales attributas materiae omnino informi, ut Augustinus vult; sive quantum ad convenientem decorem et ordinem, ut alii sancti dicunt. Et his duobus operibus additur ornatus. Et differt ornatus a perfectione. Nam perfectio caeli et terrae ad ea pertinere videtur quae caelo et terrae sunt intrinseca, ornatus vero ad ea quae sunt a caelo et terra distincta. Sicut homo perficitur per proprias partes et formas, ornatur autem per vestimenta, vel aliquid huiusmodi. Distinctio autem aliquorum maxime manifestatur per motum localem, quo ab invicem separantur. Et ideo ad opus ornatus pertinet productio illarum rerum quae habent motum in caelo et in terra. Sicut autem supra dictum est, de tribus fit mentio in creatione, scilicet de caelo et aqua et terra. Et haec tria etiam formantur per opus distinctionis tribus diebus, primo die, caelum; secundo die distinguuntur aquae; tertio die fit distinctio in terra, maris et aridae. Et similiter in opere ornatus, primo die, qui est quartus, producuntur luminaria, quae moventur in caelo, ad ornatum ipsius. Secundo die, qui est quintus, aves et pisces, ad ornatum medii elementi, quia habent motum in aere et aqua, quae pro uno accipiuntur. Tertio die, qui est sextus, producuntur animalia quae habent motum in terra, ad ornatum ipsius. Sed sciendum est quod in productione luminarium non discordat Augustinus ab aliis sanctis. Dicit enim luminaria esse facta in actu, non in virtute tantum, non enim habet firmamentum virtutem productivam luminarium, sicut habet terra virtutem productivam plantarum. Unde Scriptura non dicit, producat firmamentum luminaria; sicut dicit, germinet terra herbam virentem.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31330)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, nulla difficultas ex hoc oritur. Non enim ponit successionem temporis in istis operibus, et ideo non oportet dicere quod materia luminarium fuerit sub alia forma. Secundum etiam eos qui ponunt caelestia corpora ex natura quatuor elementorum, nulla difficultas accidit, quia potest dici quod sunt formata ex praeiacenti materia, sicut animalia et plantae. Sed secundum eos qui ponunt corpora caelestia esse alterius naturae ab elementis et incorruptibilia per naturam, oportet dicere quod substantia luminarium a principio fuit creata; sed prius erat informis, et nunc formatur; non quidem forma substantiali, sed per collationem determinatae virtutis. Ideo tamen non fit mentio a principio de eis, sed solum quarta die, ut Chrysostomus dicit, ut per hoc removeat populum ab idololatria, ostendens luminaria non esse deos, ex quo nec a principio fuerunt.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31331)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, nulla sequitur difficultas, quia lux de qua prima die facta est mentio, fuit lux spiritualis; nunc autem fit lux corporalis. Si autem lux primo die facta intelligitur lux corporalis, oportet dicere quod lux primo die fuit producta secundum communem lucis naturam, quarto autem die attributa est luminaribus determinata virtus ad determinatos effectus; secundum quod videmus alios effectus habere radium solis, et alios radium lunae, et sic de aliis. Et propter hanc determinationem virtutis, dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod lumen solis, quod primo erat informe, quarto die formatum est.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31332)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, secundum Ptolomaeum, luminaria non sunt fixa in sphaeris, sed habent motum seorsum a motu sphaerarum. Unde Chrysostomus dicit quod non ideo dicitur quod posuit ea in firmamento, quia ibi sint fixa; sed quia iusserit ut ibi essent; sicut posuit hominem in Paradiso, ut ibi esset. Sed secundum opinionem Aristotelis, stellae fixae sunt in orbibus, et non moventur nisi motu orbium, secundum rei veritatem. Tamen motus luminarium sensu percipitur, non autem motus sphaerarum. Moyses autem, rudi populo condescendens, secutus est quae sensibiliter apparent, ut dictum est. Si autem sit aliud firmamentum quod factum est secunda die, ab eo in quo posita sunt sidera, secundum distinctionem naturae, licet sensus non discernat, quem Moyses sequitur, ut dictum est; cessat obiectio. Nam firmamentum factum est secunda die, quantum ad inferiorem partem. In firmamento autem posita sunt sidera quarta die, quantum ad superiorem partem; ut totum pro uno accipiatur, secundum quod sensui apparet.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31333)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, sicut dicit Basilius, praemittitur productio plantarum luminaribus, ad excludendam idololatriam. Qui enim credunt luminaria esse deos, dicunt quod primordialem originem habent plantae a luminaribus. Quamvis, ut Chrysostomus dicit, sicut agricola cooperatur ad productionem plantarum, ita etiam et luminaria per suos motus.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31334)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod, sicut Chrysostomus dicit, dicuntur duo luminaria magna non tam quantitate, quam efficacia et virtute. Quia etsi aliae stellae sint maiores quantitate quam luna, tamen effectus lunae magis sentitur in istis inferioribus. Et etiam secundum sensum maior apparet.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 70 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31335)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod inconvenienter causa productionis luminarium describatur. Dicitur enim Ierem. X, *a signis caeli nolite metuere, quae gentes timent*. Non ergo luminaria in signa facta sunt.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31336)\n\nPraeterea, signum contra causam dividitur. Sed luminaria sunt causa eorum quae hic aguntur. Ergo non sunt signa.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31337)\n\nPraeterea, distinctio temporum et dierum incoepit a primo die. Non ergo facta sunt luminaria in tempora et dies et annos, idest in horum distinctionem.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31338)\n\nPraeterea, nihil fit propter vilius se, quia finis est melior iis quae sunt ad finem. Sed luminaria sunt meliora quam terra. Non ergo facta sunt ut illuminent terram.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31339)\n\nPraeterea, luna non praeest nocti quando est prima. Probabile autem est quod luna facta fuerit prima, sic enim homines incipiunt computare. Ergo luna non est facta ut praesit nocti.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31340)\n\nIn contrarium sufficit auctoritas Scripturae.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31341)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est supra, creatura aliqua corporalis potest dici esse facta vel propter actum proprium, vel propter aliam creaturam, vel propter totum universum, vel propter gloriam Dei. Sed Moyses, ut populum ab idololatria revocaret, illam solam causam tetigit, secundum quod sunt facta ad utilitatem hominum. Unde dicitur Deut. IV, *ne forte, elevatis oculis ad caelum, videas solem et lunam et omnia astra caeli, et errore deceptus adores ea et colas, quae creavit dominus Deus in ministerium cunctis gentibus*. Hoc autem ministerium explicat in principio Genesis per tria. Primo enim provenit utilitas hominibus ex luminaribus quantum ad visum, qui est directivus in operibus, et maxime utilis ad cognoscendas res. Et quantum ad hoc, dicit, *ut luceant in firmamento, et illuminent terram*. Secundo, quantum ad vicissitudines temporum, quibus et fastidium tollitur et valetudo conservatur, et necessaria victui oriuntur quae non essent, si semper esset aut aestas aut hiems. Et quantum ad hoc, dicit, *ut sint in tempora et dies et annos*. Tertio, quantum ad opportunitatem negotiorum et operum, inquantum ex luminaribus caeli accipitur significatio pluviosi temporis vel sereni quae sunt apta diversis negotiis. Et quantum ad hoc dicit, *ut sint in signa*.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31342)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod luminaria sunt in signa corporalium transmutationum, non autem eorum quae dependent ex libero arbitrio.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31343)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod per causam sensibilem quandoque ducimur in cognitionem effectus occulti, sicut et e converso. Unde nihil prohibet causam sensibilem esse signum. Ideo tamen potius dicit signa quam causas, ut occasionem idololatriae tolleret.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31344)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in prima die facta est communis distinctio temporis per diem et noctem, secundum motum diurnum, qui est communis totius caeli; qui potest intelligi incoepisse primo die. Sed speciales distinctiones dierum et temporum, secundum quod dies est calidior die, et tempus tempore, et annus anno, fiunt secundum speciales motus stellarum; qui possunt intelligi quarto die incoepisse.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31345)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod in illuminatione terrae intelligitur utilitas hominis, qui secundum animam praefertur corporibus luminarium. Nihil tamen prohibet dici quod dignior creatura facta est propter inferiorem, non secundum quod in se consideratur sed secundum quod ordinatur ad integritatem universi.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31346)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod luna, quando est perfecta, oritur vespere et occidit mane, et sic praeest nocti. Et satis probabile est quod luna fuerit facta plena; sicut et herbae factae sunt in sua perfectione, facientes semen, et similiter animalia et homo. Licet enim naturali processu ab imperfecto ad perfectum deveniatur, simpliciter tamen perfectum prius est imperfecto. Augustinus tamen hoc non asserit, quia dicit non esse inconveniens quod Deus imperfecta fecerit, quae postmodum ipse perfecit.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 70 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31347)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod luminaria caeli sint animata. Superius enim corpus nobilioribus ornamentis ornari debet. Sed ea quae pertinent ad ornatum inferiorum corporum, sunt animata; scilicet pisces, aves et terrestria animalia. Ergo et luminaria, quae pertinent ad ornatum caeli.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31348)\n\nPraeterea, nobilioris corporis nobilior est forma. Sed sol et luna et alia luminaria sunt nobiliora quam corpora plantarum et animalium. Ergo habent nobiliorem formam. Nobilissima autem forma est anima, quae est principium vitae, quia, ut Augustinus dicit in libro de vera Relig., *quaelibet substantia vivens naturae ordine praefertur substantiae non viventi*. Ergo luminaria caeli sunt animata.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31349)\n\nPraeterea, causa nobilior est effectu. Sed sol et luna et alia luminaria sunt causa vitae, ut patet maxime in animalibus ex putrefactione generatis, quae virtute solis et stellarum vitam consequuntur. Ergo multo magis corpora caelestia vivunt et sunt animata.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31350)\n\nPraeterea, motus caeli et caelestium corporum sunt naturales, ut patet in I de caelo. Motus autem naturalis est a principio intrinseco. Cum igitur principium motus caelestium corporum sit aliqua substantia apprehensiva, quae movetur sicut desiderans a desiderato, ut dicitur in XII Metaphys.; videtur quod principium apprehendens sit principium intrinsecum corporibus caelestibus. Ergo sunt animata.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31351)\n\nPraeterea, primum mobile est caelum. In genere autem mobilium, primum est movens seipsum, ut probatur in VIII Physic., quia quod est per se, prius est eo quod est per aliud. Sola autem animata movent seipsa, ut in eodem libro ostenditur. Ergo corpora caelestia sunt animata.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31352)\n\nSed contra est quod Damascenus dicit in libro II, *nullus animatos caelos vel luminaria aestimet; inanimati enim sunt et insensibiles*.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31353)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa istam quaestionem apud philosophos fuit diversa opinio. Anaxagoras enim, ut Augustinus refert Lib. XVIII de Civ. Dei, *factus est reus apud Athenienses, quia dixit solem esse lapidem ardentem, negans utique ipsum esse Deum*, vel aliquid animatum. Platonici vero posuerunt corpora caelestia animata. Similiter etiam apud doctores fidei, fuit circa hoc diversa opinio. Origenes enim posuit corpora caelestia animata. Hieronymus etiam idem sentire videtur, exponens illud Eccle. I, *lustrans universa, per circuitum pergit spiritus*. Basilius vero et Damascenus asserunt corpora caelestia non esse animata. Augustinus vero sub dubio dereliquit, in neutram partem declinans, ut patet in II supra Gen. ad Litt.; et in Enchirid., ubi etiam dicit quod, si sunt animata caelestia corpora, pertinent ad societatem Angelorum eorum animae. In hac autem opinionum diversitate, ut veritas aliquatenus innotescat, considerandum est quod unio animae et corporis non est propter corpus, sed propter animam, non enim forma est propter materiam, sed e converso. Natura autem et virtus animae deprehenditur ex eius operatione, quae etiam quodammodo est finis eius. Invenitur autem corpus necessarium ad aliquam operationem animae, quae mediante corpore exercetur; sicut patet in operibus animae sensitivae et nutritivae. Unde necesse est tales animas unitas esse corporibus propter suas operationes. Est autem aliqua operatio animae, quae non exercetur corpore mediante, sed tamen ex corpore aliquod adminiculum tali operationi exhibetur; sicut per corpus exhibentur animae humanae phantasmata, quibus indiget ad intelligendum. Unde etiam talem animam necesse est corpori uniri propter suam operationem, licet contingat ipsam separari. Manifestum est autem quod anima caelestis corporis non potest habere operationes nutritivae animae, quae sunt nutrire, augere et generare, huiusmodi enim operationes non competunt corpori incorruptibili per naturam. Similiter etiam nec operationes animae sensitivae corpori caelesti conveniunt, quia omnes sensus fundantur super tactum, qui est apprehensivus qualitatum elementarium. Omnia etiam organa potentiarum sensitivarum requirunt determinatam proportionem secundum commixtionem aliquam elementorum, a quorum natura corpora caelestia ponuntur remota. Relinquitur ergo quod de operationibus animae nulla potest competere animae caelesti nisi duae, intelligere et movere, nam appetere consequitur sensum et intellectum, et cum utroque ordinatur. Intellectualis autem operatio, cum non exerceatur per corpus, non indiget corpore nisi inquantum ei per sensus ministrantur phantasmata. Operationes autem sensitivae animae corporibus caelestibus non conveniunt, ut dictum est. Sic igitur propter operationem intellectualem, anima caelesti corpori non uniretur. Relinquitur ergo quod propter solam motionem. Ad hoc autem quod moveat, non oportet quod uniatur ei ut forma; sed per contactum virtutis, sicut motor unitur mobili. Unde Aristoteles, libro VIII Physic., postquam ostendit quod primum movens seipsum componitur ex duabus partibus, quarum una est movens et alia mota; assignans quomodo hae duae partes uniantur, dicit quod per contactum vel duorum ad invicem, si utrumque sit corpus, vel unius ad alterum et non e converso, si unum sit corpus et aliud non corpus. Platonici etiam animas corporibus uniri non ponebant nisi per contactum virtutis, sicut motor mobili. Et sic per hoc quod Plato ponit corpora caelestia animata, nihil aliud datur intelligi, quam quod substantiae spirituales uniuntur corporibus caelestibus ut motores mobilibus. Quod autem corpora caelestia moveantur ab aliqua substantia apprehendente, et non solum a natura, sicut gravia et levia, patet ex hoc, quod natura non movet nisi ad unum, quo habito quiescit, quod in motu corporum caelestium non apparet. Unde relinquitur quod moventur ab aliqua substantia apprehendente Augustinus etiam dicit, III de Trin., corpora omnia administrari a Deo per spiritum vitae. Sic igitur patet quod corpora caelestia non sunt animata eo modo quo plantae et animalia, sed aequivoce. Unde inter ponentes ea esse animata, et ponentes ea inanimata, parva vel nulla differentia invenitur in re, sed in voce tantum.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31354)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ad ornatum pertinent aliqua secundum proprium motum. Et quantum ad hoc, luminaria caeli conveniunt cum aliis quae ad ornatum pertinent, quia moventur a substantia vivente.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31355)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod nihil prohibet aliquid esse nobilius simpliciter, quod tamen non est nobilius quantum ad aliquid. Forma ergo caelestis corporis, etsi non sit simpliciter nobilior anima animalis, est tamen nobilior quantum ad rationem formae, perficit enim totaliter suam materiam, ut non sit in potentia ad aliam formam; quod anima non facit. Quantum etiam ad motum, moventur corpora caelestia a nobilioribus motoribus.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31356)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod corpus caeleste, cum sit movens motum, habet rationem instrumenti, quod agit in virtute principalis agentis. Et ideo ex virtute sui motoris, qui est substantia vivens, potest causare vitam.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31357)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod motus corporis caelestis est naturalis, non propter principium activum, sed propter principium passivum, quia scilicet habet in sua natura aptitudinem ut tali motu ab intellectu moveatur.\n\n[I q. 70 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31358)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod caelum dicitur movere seipsum, inquantum componitur ex motore et mobili, non sicut ex forma et materia, sed secundum contactum virtutis, ut dictum est. Et hoc etiam modo potest dici quod eius motor est principium intrinsecum, ut sic etiam motus caeli possit dici naturalis ex parte principii activi; sicut motus voluntarius dicitur esse naturalis animali inquantum est animal, ut dicitur in VIII Physic.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0002","text":"[I q. 70 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31322)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0003","text":"Consequenter considerandum est de opere ornatus. Et primo, de singulis diebus secundum se; secundo, de omnibus sex diebus in communi. Circa primum ergo, considerandum est primo de opere quartae diei, secundo, de opere quintae; tertio, de opere sextae; quarto, de iis quae pertinent ad septimum diem. Circa primum quaeruntur tria. Primo, de productione luminarium. Secundo, de fine productionis eorum. Tertio, utrum sint animata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0005","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31323)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod luminaria non debuerint produci quarta die. Luminaria enim sunt corpora incorruptibilia naturaliter. Ergo eorum materia non potest esse absque formis eorum. Sed eorum materia producta est in opere creationis, ante omnem diem. Ergo et eorum formae. Non ergo sunt facta quarta die.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0007","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31324)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0008","text":"Praeterea, luminaria sunt quasi vasa luminis. Sed lux est facta prima die. Ergo luminaria fieri debuerunt prima die, et non quarta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0009","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31325)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0010","text":"Praeterea, sicut plantae fixae sunt in terra, ita luminaria fixa sunt in firmamento, unde Scriptura dicit quod posuit ea in firmamento. Sed productio plantarum simul describitur cum formatione terrae, cui inhaerent. Ergo et productio luminarium simul debuit poni, secunda die, cum productione firmamenti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0011","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31326)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0012","text":"Praeterea, sol et luna et alia luminaria sunt causae plantarum. Sed naturali ordine causa praecedit effectum. Ergo luminaria non debuerunt fieri quarta die, sed tertia vel ante.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0013","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31327)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0014","text":"Praeterea, multae stellae, secundum astrologos, sunt luna maiores. Non ergo tantum sol et luna debuerunt poni duo magna luminaria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0015","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31328)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0016","text":"Sed in contrarium sufficit auctoritas Scripturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0017","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31329)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0018","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in recapitulatione divinorum operum, Scriptura sic dicit, *igitur perfecti sunt caeli et terra, et omnis ornatus eorum*. In quibus verbis triplex opus intelligi potest, scilicet opus creationis, per quod caelum et terra producta leguntur, sed informia. Et opus distinctionis, per quod caelum et terra sunt perfecta, sive per formas substantiales attributas materiae omnino informi, ut Augustinus vult; sive quantum ad convenientem decorem et ordinem, ut alii sancti dicunt. Et his duobus operibus additur ornatus. Et differt ornatus a perfectione. Nam perfectio caeli et terrae ad ea pertinere videtur quae caelo et terrae sunt intrinseca, ornatus vero ad ea quae sunt a caelo et terra distincta. Sicut homo perficitur per proprias partes et formas, ornatur autem per vestimenta, vel aliquid huiusmodi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0019","text":"Distinctio autem aliquorum maxime manifestatur per motum localem, quo ab invicem separantur. Et ideo ad opus ornatus pertinet productio illarum rerum quae habent motum in caelo et in terra. Sicut autem supra dictum est, de tribus fit mentio in creatione, scilicet de caelo et aqua et terra. Et haec tria etiam formantur per opus distinctionis tribus diebus, primo die, caelum; secundo die distinguuntur aquae; tertio die fit distinctio in terra, maris et aridae. Et similiter in opere ornatus, primo die, qui est quartus, producuntur luminaria, quae moventur in caelo, ad ornatum ipsius. Secundo die, qui est quintus, aves et pisces, ad ornatum medii elementi, quia habent motum in aere et aqua, quae pro uno accipiuntur. Tertio die, qui est sextus, producuntur animalia quae habent motum in terra, ad ornatum ipsius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0020","text":"Sed sciendum est quod in productione luminarium non discordat Augustinus ab aliis sanctis. Dicit enim luminaria esse facta in actu, non in virtute tantum, non enim habet firmamentum virtutem productivam luminarium, sicut habet terra virtutem productivam plantarum. Unde Scriptura non dicit, producat firmamentum luminaria; sicut dicit, germinet terra herbam virentem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0021","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31330)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0022","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, nulla difficultas ex hoc oritur. Non enim ponit successionem temporis in istis operibus, et ideo non oportet dicere quod materia luminarium fuerit sub alia forma. Secundum etiam eos qui ponunt caelestia corpora ex natura quatuor elementorum, nulla difficultas accidit, quia potest dici quod sunt formata ex praeiacenti materia, sicut animalia et plantae. Sed secundum eos qui ponunt corpora caelestia esse alterius naturae ab elementis et incorruptibilia per naturam, oportet dicere quod substantia luminarium a principio fuit creata; sed prius erat informis, et nunc formatur; non quidem forma substantiali, sed per collationem determinatae virtutis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0023","text":"Ideo tamen non fit mentio a principio de eis, sed solum quarta die, ut Chrysostomus dicit, ut per hoc removeat populum ab idololatria, ostendens luminaria non esse deos, ex quo nec a principio fuerunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0024","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31331)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0025","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, nulla sequitur difficultas, quia lux de qua prima die facta est mentio, fuit lux spiritualis; nunc autem fit lux corporalis. Si autem lux primo die facta intelligitur lux corporalis, oportet dicere quod lux primo die fuit producta secundum communem lucis naturam, quarto autem die attributa est luminaribus determinata virtus ad determinatos effectus; secundum quod videmus alios effectus habere radium solis, et alios radium lunae, et sic de aliis. Et propter hanc determinationem virtutis, dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod lumen solis, quod primo erat informe, quarto die formatum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0026","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31332)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0027","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, secundum Ptolomaeum, luminaria non sunt fixa in sphaeris, sed habent motum seorsum a motu sphaerarum. Unde Chrysostomus dicit quod non ideo dicitur quod posuit ea in firmamento, quia ibi sint fixa; sed quia iusserit ut ibi essent; sicut posuit hominem in Paradiso, ut ibi esset. Sed secundum opinionem Aristotelis, stellae fixae sunt in orbibus, et non moventur nisi motu orbium, secundum rei veritatem. Tamen motus luminarium sensu percipitur, non autem motus sphaerarum. Moyses autem, rudi populo condescendens, secutus est quae sensibiliter apparent, ut dictum est. Si autem sit aliud firmamentum quod factum est secunda die, ab eo in quo posita sunt sidera, secundum distinctionem naturae, licet sensus non discernat, quem Moyses sequitur, ut dictum est; cessat obiectio. Nam firmamentum factum est secunda die, quantum ad inferiorem partem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0028","text":"In firmamento autem posita sunt sidera quarta die, quantum ad superiorem partem; ut totum pro uno accipiatur, secundum quod sensui apparet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0029","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31333)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0030","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, sicut dicit Basilius, praemittitur productio plantarum luminaribus, ad excludendam idololatriam. Qui enim credunt luminaria esse deos, dicunt quod primordialem originem habent plantae a luminaribus. Quamvis, ut Chrysostomus dicit, sicut agricola cooperatur ad productionem plantarum, ita etiam et luminaria per suos motus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0031","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31334)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0032","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod, sicut Chrysostomus dicit, dicuntur duo luminaria magna non tam quantitate, quam efficacia et virtute. Quia etsi aliae stellae sint maiores quantitate quam luna, tamen effectus lunae magis sentitur in istis inferioribus. Et etiam secundum sensum maior apparet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0033","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0034","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31335)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0035","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod inconvenienter causa productionis luminarium describatur. Dicitur enim Ierem. X, *a signis caeli nolite metuere, quae gentes timent*. Non ergo luminaria in signa facta sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0036","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31336)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0037","text":"Praeterea, signum contra causam dividitur. Sed luminaria sunt causa eorum quae hic aguntur. Ergo non sunt signa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0038","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31337)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0039","text":"Praeterea, distinctio temporum et dierum incoepit a primo die. Non ergo facta sunt luminaria in tempora et dies et annos, idest in horum distinctionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0040","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31338)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0041","text":"Praeterea, nihil fit propter vilius se, quia finis est melior iis quae sunt ad finem. Sed luminaria sunt meliora quam terra. Non ergo facta sunt ut illuminent terram.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0042","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31339)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0043","text":"Praeterea, luna non praeest nocti quando est prima. Probabile autem est quod luna facta fuerit prima, sic enim homines incipiunt computare. Ergo luna non est facta ut praesit nocti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0044","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31340)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0045","text":"In contrarium sufficit auctoritas Scripturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0046","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31341)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0047","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est supra, creatura aliqua corporalis potest dici esse facta vel propter actum proprium, vel propter aliam creaturam, vel propter totum universum, vel propter gloriam Dei. Sed Moyses, ut populum ab idololatria revocaret, illam solam causam tetigit, secundum quod sunt facta ad utilitatem hominum. Unde dicitur Deut. IV, *ne forte, elevatis oculis ad caelum, videas solem et lunam et omnia astra caeli, et errore deceptus adores ea et colas, quae creavit dominus Deus in ministerium cunctis gentibus*. Hoc autem ministerium explicat in principio Genesis per tria. Primo enim provenit utilitas hominibus ex luminaribus quantum ad visum, qui est directivus in operibus, et maxime utilis ad cognoscendas res. Et quantum ad hoc, dicit, *ut luceant in firmamento, et illuminent terram*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0048","text":"Secundo, quantum ad vicissitudines temporum, quibus et fastidium tollitur et valetudo conservatur, et necessaria victui oriuntur quae non essent, si semper esset aut aestas aut hiems. Et quantum ad hoc, dicit, *ut sint in tempora et dies et annos*. Tertio, quantum ad opportunitatem negotiorum et operum, inquantum ex luminaribus caeli accipitur significatio pluviosi temporis vel sereni quae sunt apta diversis negotiis. Et quantum ad hoc dicit, *ut sint in signa*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0049","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31342)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0050","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod luminaria sunt in signa corporalium transmutationum, non autem eorum quae dependent ex libero arbitrio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0051","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31343)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0052","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod per causam sensibilem quandoque ducimur in cognitionem effectus occulti, sicut et e converso. Unde nihil prohibet causam sensibilem esse signum. Ideo tamen potius dicit signa quam causas, ut occasionem idololatriae tolleret.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0053","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31344)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0054","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in prima die facta est communis distinctio temporis per diem et noctem, secundum motum diurnum, qui est communis totius caeli; qui potest intelligi incoepisse primo die. Sed speciales distinctiones dierum et temporum, secundum quod dies est calidior die, et tempus tempore, et annus anno, fiunt secundum speciales motus stellarum; qui possunt intelligi quarto die incoepisse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0055","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31345)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0056","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod in illuminatione terrae intelligitur utilitas hominis, qui secundum animam praefertur corporibus luminarium. Nihil tamen prohibet dici quod dignior creatura facta est propter inferiorem, non secundum quod in se consideratur sed secundum quod ordinatur ad integritatem universi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0057","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31346)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0058","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod luna, quando est perfecta, oritur vespere et occidit mane, et sic praeest nocti. Et satis probabile est quod luna fuerit facta plena; sicut et herbae factae sunt in sua perfectione, facientes semen, et similiter animalia et homo. Licet enim naturali processu ab imperfecto ad perfectum deveniatur, simpliciter tamen perfectum prius est imperfecto. Augustinus tamen hoc non asserit, quia dicit non esse inconveniens quod Deus imperfecta fecerit, quae postmodum ipse perfecit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0059","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0060","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31347)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0061","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod luminaria caeli sint animata. Superius enim corpus nobilioribus ornamentis ornari debet. Sed ea quae pertinent ad ornatum inferiorum corporum, sunt animata; scilicet pisces, aves et terrestria animalia. Ergo et luminaria, quae pertinent ad ornatum caeli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0062","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31348)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0063","text":"Praeterea, nobilioris corporis nobilior est forma. Sed sol et luna et alia luminaria sunt nobiliora quam corpora plantarum et animalium. Ergo habent nobiliorem formam. Nobilissima autem forma est anima, quae est principium vitae, quia, ut Augustinus dicit in libro de vera Relig., *quaelibet substantia vivens naturae ordine praefertur substantiae non viventi*. Ergo luminaria caeli sunt animata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0064","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31349)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0065","text":"Praeterea, causa nobilior est effectu. Sed sol et luna et alia luminaria sunt causa vitae, ut patet maxime in animalibus ex putrefactione generatis, quae virtute solis et stellarum vitam consequuntur. Ergo multo magis corpora caelestia vivunt et sunt animata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0066","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31350)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0067","text":"Praeterea, motus caeli et caelestium corporum sunt naturales, ut patet in I de caelo. Motus autem naturalis est a principio intrinseco. Cum igitur principium motus caelestium corporum sit aliqua substantia apprehensiva, quae movetur sicut desiderans a desiderato, ut dicitur in XII Metaphys.; videtur quod principium apprehendens sit principium intrinsecum corporibus caelestibus. Ergo sunt animata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0068","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31351)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0069","text":"Praeterea, primum mobile est caelum. In genere autem mobilium, primum est movens seipsum, ut probatur in VIII Physic., quia quod est per se, prius est eo quod est per aliud. Sola autem animata movent seipsa, ut in eodem libro ostenditur. Ergo corpora caelestia sunt animata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0070","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31352)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0071","text":"Sed contra est quod Damascenus dicit in libro II, *nullus animatos caelos vel luminaria aestimet; inanimati enim sunt et insensibiles*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0072","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31353)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0073","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa istam quaestionem apud philosophos fuit diversa opinio. Anaxagoras enim, ut Augustinus refert Lib. XVIII de Civ. Dei, *factus est reus apud Athenienses, quia dixit solem esse lapidem ardentem, negans utique ipsum esse Deum*, vel aliquid animatum. Platonici vero posuerunt corpora caelestia animata. Similiter etiam apud doctores fidei, fuit circa hoc diversa opinio. Origenes enim posuit corpora caelestia animata. Hieronymus etiam idem sentire videtur, exponens illud Eccle. I, *lustrans universa, per circuitum pergit spiritus*. Basilius vero et Damascenus asserunt corpora caelestia non esse animata. Augustinus vero sub dubio dereliquit, in neutram partem declinans, ut patet in II supra Gen. ad Litt.; et in Enchirid., ubi etiam dicit quod, si sunt animata caelestia corpora, pertinent ad societatem Angelorum eorum animae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0074","text":"In hac autem opinionum diversitate, ut veritas aliquatenus innotescat, considerandum est quod unio animae et corporis non est propter corpus, sed propter animam, non enim forma est propter materiam, sed e converso. Natura autem et virtus animae deprehenditur ex eius operatione, quae etiam quodammodo est finis eius. Invenitur autem corpus necessarium ad aliquam operationem animae, quae mediante corpore exercetur; sicut patet in operibus animae sensitivae et nutritivae. Unde necesse est tales animas unitas esse corporibus propter suas operationes. Est autem aliqua operatio animae, quae non exercetur corpore mediante, sed tamen ex corpore aliquod adminiculum tali operationi exhibetur; sicut per corpus exhibentur animae humanae phantasmata, quibus indiget ad intelligendum. Unde etiam talem animam necesse est corpori uniri propter suam operationem, licet contingat ipsam separari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0075","text":"Manifestum est autem quod anima caelestis corporis non potest habere operationes nutritivae animae, quae sunt nutrire, augere et generare, huiusmodi enim operationes non competunt corpori incorruptibili per naturam. Similiter etiam nec operationes animae sensitivae corpori caelesti conveniunt, quia omnes sensus fundantur super tactum, qui est apprehensivus qualitatum elementarium. Omnia etiam organa potentiarum sensitivarum requirunt determinatam proportionem secundum commixtionem aliquam elementorum, a quorum natura corpora caelestia ponuntur remota. Relinquitur ergo quod de operationibus animae nulla potest competere animae caelesti nisi duae, intelligere et movere, nam appetere consequitur sensum et intellectum, et cum utroque ordinatur. Intellectualis autem operatio, cum non exerceatur per corpus, non indiget corpore nisi inquantum ei per sensus ministrantur phantasmata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0076","text":"Operationes autem sensitivae animae corporibus caelestibus non conveniunt, ut dictum est. Sic igitur propter operationem intellectualem, anima caelesti corpori non uniretur. Relinquitur ergo quod propter solam motionem. Ad hoc autem quod moveat, non oportet quod uniatur ei ut forma; sed per contactum virtutis, sicut motor unitur mobili. Unde Aristoteles, libro VIII Physic., postquam ostendit quod primum movens seipsum componitur ex duabus partibus, quarum una est movens et alia mota; assignans quomodo hae duae partes uniantur, dicit quod per contactum vel duorum ad invicem, si utrumque sit corpus, vel unius ad alterum et non e converso, si unum sit corpus et aliud non corpus. Platonici etiam animas corporibus uniri non ponebant nisi per contactum virtutis, sicut motor mobili.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0077","text":"Et sic per hoc quod Plato ponit corpora caelestia animata, nihil aliud datur intelligi, quam quod substantiae spirituales uniuntur corporibus caelestibus ut motores mobilibus. Quod autem corpora caelestia moveantur ab aliqua substantia apprehendente, et non solum a natura, sicut gravia et levia, patet ex hoc, quod natura non movet nisi ad unum, quo habito quiescit, quod in motu corporum caelestium non apparet. Unde relinquitur quod moventur ab aliqua substantia apprehendente Augustinus etiam dicit, III de Trin., corpora omnia administrari a Deo per spiritum vitae. Sic igitur patet quod corpora caelestia non sunt animata eo modo quo plantae et animalia, sed aequivoce. Unde inter ponentes ea esse animata, et ponentes ea inanimata, parva vel nulla differentia invenitur in re, sed in voce tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0078","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31354)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0079","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ad ornatum pertinent aliqua secundum proprium motum. Et quantum ad hoc, luminaria caeli conveniunt cum aliis quae ad ornatum pertinent, quia moventur a substantia vivente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0080","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31355)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0081","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod nihil prohibet aliquid esse nobilius simpliciter, quod tamen non est nobilius quantum ad aliquid. Forma ergo caelestis corporis, etsi non sit simpliciter nobilior anima animalis, est tamen nobilior quantum ad rationem formae, perficit enim totaliter suam materiam, ut non sit in potentia ad aliam formam; quod anima non facit. Quantum etiam ad motum, moventur corpora caelestia a nobilioribus motoribus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0082","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31356)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0083","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod corpus caeleste, cum sit movens motum, habet rationem instrumenti, quod agit in virtute principalis agentis. Et ideo ex virtute sui motoris, qui est substantia vivens, potest causare vitam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0084","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31357)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0085","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod motus corporis caelestis est naturalis, non propter principium activum, sed propter principium passivum, quia scilicet habet in sua natura aptitudinem ut tali motu ab intellectu moveatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0086","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31358)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0087","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod caelum dicitur movere seipsum, inquantum componitur ex motore et mobili, non sicut ex forma et materia, sed secundum contactum virtutis, ut dictum est. Et hoc etiam modo potest dici quod eius motor est principium intrinsecum, ut sic etiam motus caeli possit dici naturalis ex parte principii activi; sicut motus voluntarius dicitur esse naturalis animali inquantum est animal, ut dicitur in VIII Physic.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"420a6a67c42e46c86276a7598e9cc2276501b430e8379219062c9aa0bbd7b076","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T145731990851Z_73f0b9e9","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 70","source_sha256":"420a6a67c42e46c86276a7598e9cc2276501b430e8379219062c9aa0bbd7b076","sha256":"ac6e15bf97cc59c2151dec4ae1b203e1736b4f3e5d40b45452b368c1728c58ea","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:57:31.990891+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0002","text":"[I q. 70 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31322)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the work of adornment. And first, the individual days in themselves; second, all six days in common. Concerning the first, therefore, we must consider first the work of the fourth day; second, the work of the fifth; third, the work of the sixth; fourth, those things that pertain to the seventh day. Concerning the first, three questions are asked. First, concerning the production of the lights. Second, concerning the end of their production. Third, whether they are animate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0005","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31323)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the lights ought not to have been produced on the fourth day. For the lights are naturally incorruptible bodies. Therefore, their matter cannot exist without their forms. But their matter was produced in the work of creation, before every day. Therefore, so too were their forms. Therefore, they were not made on the fourth day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0007","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31324)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0008","text":"Further, the lights are, as it were, vessels of light. But light was made on the first day. Therefore, the lights ought to have been made on the first day, and not on the fourth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0009","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31325)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0010","text":"Further, just as plants are fixed in the earth, so the lights are fixed in the firmament, whence Scripture says that He placed them in the firmament. But the production of plants is described together with the formation of the earth, to which they adhere. Therefore, the production of the lights likewise ought to have been placed together, on the second day, with the production of the firmament.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0011","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31326)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0012","text":"Further, the sun and moon and the other lights are causes of plants. But in the natural order, a cause precedes its effect. Therefore, the lights ought not to have been made on the fourth day, but on the third or before.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0013","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31327)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0014","text":"Further, many stars, according to astronomers, are larger than the moon. Therefore, not only the sun and moon ought to have been designated as two great lights.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0015","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31328)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0016","text":"But, to the contrary, the authority of Scripture suffices.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0017","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31329)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0018","text":"I answer that it must be said that, in the recapitulation of the divine works, Scripture says thus: *therefore the heavens and the earth and all their adornment were perfected*. In these words a threefold work can be understood, namely, the work of creation, by which heaven and earth are read to have been produced, but unformed; and the work of distinction, by which heaven and earth are perfected, either through substantial forms attributed to matter wholly unformed, as Augustine holds; or with respect to fitting beauty and order, as the other saints say. And to these two works adornment is added. And adornment differs from perfection. For the perfection of heaven and earth seems to pertain to those things which are intrinsic to heaven and earth, whereas adornment pertains to those things which are distinct from heaven and earth. Thus a man is perfected by his own parts and forms, but is adorned by garments, or something of this sort.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0019","text":"Now the distinction of certain things is most clearly manifested through local motion, by which they are separated from one another. And therefore the production of those things which have motion in heaven and on earth pertains to the work of adornment. But, as was said above, mention is made of three things in creation, namely, heaven and water and earth. And these three are likewise formed by the work of distinction in three days: on the first day, heaven; on the second day, the waters are distinguished; on the third day, distinction is made in the earth, of sea and dry land. And similarly in the work of adornment, on the first day, which is the fourth, the lights are produced, which move in heaven, for its adornment. On the second day, which is the fifth, birds and fish are produced, for the adornment of the middle element, because they have motion in air and water, which are taken as one. On the third day, which is the sixth, animals that have motion on earth are produced, for its adornment.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0020","text":"But it should be known that Augustine does not disagree with the other saints concerning the production of the lights. For he says that the lights were made in act, not in potency only; for the firmament does not have a productive power of lights, as the earth has a productive power of plants. Hence Scripture does not say, “Let the firmament bring forth lights,” as it says, “Let the earth germinate green herb.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0021","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31330)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0022","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that, according to Augustine, no difficulty arises from this. For he does not posit a succession of time in these works, and therefore it is not necessary to say that the matter of the lights was under another form. Likewise, according to those who posit that the heavenly bodies are of the nature of the four elements, no difficulty arises, because it can be said that they were formed from pre-existing matter, as animals and plants were. But according to those who posit that the heavenly bodies are of a nature other than the elements and are incorruptible by nature, it must be said that the substance of the lights was created from the beginning; but it was previously unformed and is now formed, not indeed by a substantial form, but through the bestowal of a determinate power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0023","text":"Yet no mention is made of them in the beginning, but only on the fourth day, as Chrysostom says, so that thereby he might remove the people from idolatry, showing that the lights are not gods, since they were not even from the beginning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0024","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31331)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0025","text":"To the second, it must be said that, according to Augustine, no difficulty follows, because the light of which mention was made as having been made on the first day was spiritual light; but now corporeal light is made. But if the light made on the first day is understood to be corporeal light, one must say that on the first day light was produced according to the common nature of light, whereas on the fourth day a determinate power for determinate effects was attributed to the luminaries; accordingly, we see that the ray of the sun has some effects, and the ray of the moon others, and so with the rest. And on account of this determination of power, Dionysius says, in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, that the light of the sun, which was at first formless, was formed on the fourth day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0026","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31332)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0027","text":"To the third, it must be said that, according to Ptolemy, the luminaries are not fixed in the spheres, but have a motion separate from the motion of the spheres. Hence Chrysostom says that it is not said that He placed them in the firmament because they are fixed there, but because He commanded that they should be there; just as He placed man in Paradise, that he might be there. But according to Aristotle's opinion, the stars are fixed in the orbs, and in reality are moved only by the motion of the orbs. Nevertheless, the motion of the luminaries is perceived by sense, but not the motion of the spheres. Moses, however, condescending to an unlearned people, followed what appears to the senses, as has been said. But if there is another firmament, made on the second day, distinct in nature from that in which the stars are placed—though sense, which Moses follows, as has been said, does not discern it—the objection ceases. For the firmament was made on the second day with respect to its lower part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0028","text":"But the stars were placed in the firmament on the fourth day with respect to its upper part; so that the whole may be taken as one, according as it appears to sense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0029","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31333)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0030","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, as Basil says, the production of plants is set before that of the luminaries in order to exclude idolatry. For those who believe the luminaries to be gods say that plants have their primordial origin from the luminaries. Although, as Chrysostom says, just as the farmer cooperates in the production of plants, so also do the luminaries through their motions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0031","text":"[I q. 70 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31334)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0032","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that, as Chrysostom says, the two luminaries are called great not so much on account of quantity as of efficacy and power. For although other stars may be greater in quantity than the moon, nevertheless the moon's effects are felt more in these lower things. And it also appears greater to sense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0033","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0034","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31335)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0035","text":"We proceed to the second thus. It seems that the cause of the production of the luminaries is described unfittingly. For it is said in Jeremiah 10, *Do not fear the signs of heaven, which the nations fear.* Therefore the luminaries were not made as signs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0036","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31336)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0037","text":"Further, a sign is divided against a cause. But the luminaries are causes of the things that take place here. Therefore they are not signs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0038","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31337)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0039","text":"Further, the distinction of times and days began from the first day. Therefore the luminaries were not made for times and days and years, that is, for the distinction of these.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0040","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31338)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0041","text":"Further, nothing is made for the sake of something more worthless than itself, because the end is better than the things that are for the end. But the luminaries are better than the earth. Therefore they were not made in order to illuminate the earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0042","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31339)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0043","text":"Further, the moon does not rule the night when it is new. But it is probable that the moon was made new, for thus men begin to reckon. Therefore the moon was not made to rule the night.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0044","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31340)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0045","text":"On the contrary, the authority of Scripture suffices.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0046","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31341)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0047","text":"I answer that, as was said above, a corporeal creature can be said to have been made either for its proper act, or for another creature, or for the whole universe, or for the glory of God. But Moses, in order to call the people back from idolatry, touched upon that cause alone according to which they were made for the usefulness of men. Hence it is said in Deuteronomy 4, *lest perhaps, raising your eyes to heaven, you see the sun and moon and all the stars of heaven, and, deceived by error, adore and worship them, which the Lord God created for service to all nations.* He explains this service in the beginning of Genesis in three ways. First, utility comes to men from the luminaries with respect to sight, which directs in works and is especially useful for knowing things. And with respect to this, he says, *that they may shine in the firmament, and illuminate the earth*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0048","text":"Second, with respect to the vicissitudes of times, by which both weariness is removed and health is preserved, and things necessary for sustenance arise which would not arise if it were always either summer or winter. And with respect to this, he says, *that they may be for times and days and years*. Third, with respect to the suitability of affairs and works, insofar as from the luminaries of heaven there is received an indication of rainy or clear weather, which are suitable for different affairs. And with respect to this he says, *that they may be for signs*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0049","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31342)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0050","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that the luminaries are signs of bodily changes, but not of those things that depend on free choice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0051","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31343)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0052","text":"To the second it must be said that through a sensible cause we are sometimes led to knowledge of a hidden effect, and conversely. Hence nothing prevents a sensible cause from being a sign. Yet he says signs rather than causes, in order to remove an occasion of idolatry.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0053","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31344)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0054","text":"To the third it must be said that on the first day there was made a general distinction of time by day and night, according to the diurnal motion, which is common to the whole heaven; and this can be understood to have begun on the first day. But the particular distinctions of days and times—according as one day is hotter than another day, and one season than another season, and one year than another year—are made according to the particular motions of the stars; and these can be understood to have begun on the fourth day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0055","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31345)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0056","text":"To the fourth it must be said that, in the illumination of the earth, the usefulness of man is understood, who according to the soul is preferred to the bodies of the luminaries. Yet nothing prevents its being said that a more worthy creature was made for a lower one, not insofar as it is considered in itself, but insofar as it is ordered to the completeness of the universe.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0057","text":"[I q. 70 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31346)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0058","text":"To the fifth it must be said that the moon, when it is full, rises in the evening and sets in the morning, and thus presides over the night. And it is quite probable that the moon was made full; just as plants were made in their perfection, producing seed, and likewise animals and man. For although by a natural process one comes from the imperfect to the perfect, nevertheless, absolutely speaking, the perfect is prior to the imperfect. Augustine, however, does not assert this, because he says that it is not unfitting that God made imperfect things, which he himself afterwards perfected.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0059","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0060","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31347)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0061","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that the luminaries of heaven are animate. For a higher body ought to be adorned with more noble ornaments. But those things that pertain to the adornment of lower bodies are animate, namely, fishes, birds, and terrestrial animals. Therefore also are the luminaries, which pertain to the adornment of heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0062","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31348)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0063","text":"Further, the form of a more noble body is more noble. But the sun and moon and the other luminaries are more noble than the bodies of plants and animals. Therefore they have a more noble form. But the most noble form is the soul, which is the principle of life, because, as Augustine says in the book *On True Religion*, *every living substance is, in the order of nature, preferred to a non-living substance*. Therefore the luminaries of heaven are animate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0064","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31349)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0065","text":"Further, a cause is more noble than its effect. But the sun and moon and the other luminaries are causes of life, as is especially clear in animals generated from putrefaction, which receive life by the power of the sun and stars. Therefore, much more do the heavenly bodies live and are animate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0066","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31350)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0067","text":"Further, the motions of the heaven and of the heavenly bodies are natural, as is clear in *On the Heavens* I. But natural motion is from an intrinsic principle. Therefore, since the principle of the motion of heavenly bodies is some apprehensive substance, which is moved as desiring by that which is desired, as is said in *Metaphysics* XII, it seems that the apprehending principle is an intrinsic principle in the heavenly bodies. Therefore they are animate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0068","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31351)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0069","text":"Further, the first movable is the heaven. But in the genus of movable things, the first is that which moves itself, as is proved in *Physics* VIII, because that which is through itself is prior to that which is through another. But only animate things move themselves, as is shown in the same book. Therefore the heavenly bodies are animate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0070","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31352)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0071","text":"But against this is what Damascene says in Book II: *let no one suppose the heavens or the luminaries to be animate; for they are inanimate and without sensation*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0072","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31353)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0073","text":"I answer that, concerning this question, there was a diversity of opinion among the philosophers. For Anaxagoras, as Augustine relates in Book XVIII of *The City of God*, *was accused before the Athenians because he said that the sun was a burning stone, thereby denying, of course, that it was God*, or anything animate. But the Platonists held that the heavenly bodies are animate. Likewise, among the doctors of the faith there was a diversity of opinion on this matter. For Origen held that the heavenly bodies are animate. Jerome also seems to have held the same view, in expounding that passage of Eccles. I, *the spirit goes round about, surveying all things*. But Basil and Damascene assert that the heavenly bodies are not animate. Augustine, however, left the matter in doubt, inclining to neither side, as is clear in II *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*; and in the *Enchiridion*, where he also says that, if the heavenly bodies are animate, their souls belong to the society of the Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0074","text":"But amid this diversity of opinions, in order that the truth may become known to some extent, it must be considered that the union of soul and body is not for the sake of the body, but for the sake of the soul; for form is not for the sake of matter, but conversely. Now the nature and power of the soul are discerned from its operation, which is also in a certain way its end. But a body is found necessary for some operation of the soul which is exercised by means of the body; as is clear in the works of the sensitive and nutritive soul. Hence such souls must be united to bodies on account of their operations. But there is some operation of the soul which is not exercised by means of the body, yet from the body some assistance is afforded for such operation; just as through the body phantasms are supplied to the human soul, which it needs in order to understand. Hence such a soul too must be united to a body on account of its operation, although it may be separated from it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0075","text":"Now it is manifest that the soul of a heavenly body cannot have the operations of the nutritive soul, which are to nourish, to grow, and to generate; for operations of this kind do not belong to a body incorruptible by nature. Likewise, neither do the operations of the sensitive soul suit a heavenly body, because all the senses are founded upon touch, which apprehends elemental qualities. Moreover, all the organs of the sensitive powers require a determinate proportion according to some mixture of the elements, from the nature of which heavenly bodies are held to be removed. There remains, therefore, that of the operations of the soul none can belong to a heavenly soul except two: to understand and to move; for to desire follows upon sense and intellect, and is ordered with both. But intellectual operation, since it is not exercised through the body, does not need a body except insofar as phantasms are supplied to it through the senses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0076","text":"But the operations of the sensitive soul do not belong to heavenly bodies, as has been said. Therefore, a soul would not be united to a heavenly body on account of intellectual operation. It remains, then, that it is united on account of motion alone. But in order to move it, it need not be united to it as form; rather, it is united by contact of power, as a mover is united to the movable. Hence Aristotle, in Book VIII of the *Physics*, after showing that the first self-mover is composed of two parts, one of which is moving and the other moved, in explaining how these two parts are united, says that this is by contact either of two things with one another, if both are bodies, or of one with the other and not conversely, if one is a body and the other is not a body. The Platonists likewise did not hold that souls are united to bodies except by contact of power, as a mover to the movable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0077","text":"And thus, by Plato's holding that the heavenly bodies are animate, nothing else is understood than that spiritual substances are united to heavenly bodies as movers to movables. But that heavenly bodies are moved by some apprehending substance, and not merely by nature, as heavy and light things are, is clear from this: nature moves only toward one thing, and, when that is attained, it comes to rest; which does not appear in the motion of heavenly bodies. Hence it remains that they are moved by some apprehending substance. Augustine also says, in III *On the Trinity*, that all bodies are administered by God through the spirit of life. Thus, then, it is clear that heavenly bodies are not animate in the manner in which plants and animals are, but equivocally. Hence among those who hold them to be animate and those who hold them to be inanimate, little or no difference is found in reality, but only in wording.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0078","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31354)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0079","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that certain things belong to adornment according to their proper motion. And in this respect the lights of heaven agree with the other things belonging to adornment, because they are moved by a living substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0080","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31355)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0081","text":"To the second it must be said that nothing prevents something from being nobler simply, which nevertheless is not nobler in a certain respect. Therefore, although the form of a heavenly body is not simply nobler than the soul of an animal, it is nevertheless nobler with respect to the notion of form; for it wholly perfects its matter, so that it is not in potency to another form, which the soul does not do. Likewise, with respect to motion, heavenly bodies are moved by nobler movers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0082","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31356)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0083","text":"To the third it must be said that a heavenly body, since it is a moved mover, has the character of an instrument, which acts by the power of the principal agent. And therefore, by the power of its mover, which is a living substance, it can cause life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0084","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31357)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0085","text":"To the fourth it must be said that the motion of a heavenly body is natural, not on account of an active principle, but on account of a passive principle: namely, because it has in its nature an aptitude to be moved by an intellect with such a motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0086","text":"[I q. 70 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31358)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0087","text":"To the fifth it must be said that heaven is said to move itself insofar as it is composed of mover and movable, not as of form and matter, but according to contact of power, as has been said. And in this way too its mover can be said to be an intrinsic principle, so that the motion of heaven may likewise be called natural on the part of the active principle; just as voluntary motion is said to be natural to an animal insofar as it is an animal, as is said in VIII *Physics*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_62:P0087"]}],"source_digest":"2a2c0aa0d60e294690ce6a20c803fdcddf2459df767c2f4d805be5f2e8233099","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 70"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 70","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63","title":"First Part · Question 71","order":72,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_63","text":"[I q. 71 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31359)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de opere quintae diei. Et videtur quod inconvenienter hoc opus describatur. Illud enim aquae producunt, ad cuius productionem sufficit virtus aquae. Sed virtus aquae non sufficit ad productionem omnium piscium et avium, cum videamus plura eorum generari ex semine. Non ergo convenienter dicitur, *producant aquae reptile animae viventis, et volatile super terram*.\n\n[I q. 71 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31360)\n\nPraeterea, pisces et aves non tantum producuntur ex aqua, sed in eorum compositione videtur magis terra dominari quam aqua, quia corpora eorum naturaliter moventur ad terram; unde et in terra quiescunt. Non ergo convenienter dicitur pisces et aves ex aqua produci.\n\n[I q. 71 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31361)\n\nPraeterea, sicut pisces habent motum in aquis, ita et aves in aere. Si ergo pisces ex aquis producuntur, aves non deberent produci ex aquis, sed ex aere.\n\n[I q. 71 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31362)\n\nPraeterea, non omnes pisces reptant in aquis, cum quidem habeant pedes, quibus gradiuntur in terra, sicut vituli marini. Non ergo sufficienter productio piscium designatur in hoc quod dicitur, *producant aquae reptile animae viventis*.\n\n[I q. 71 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31363)\n\nPraeterea, animalia terrestria sunt perfectiora avibus et piscibus. Quod patet ex hoc quod habent membra magis distincta, et perfectiorem generationem, generant enim animalia, sed pisces et aves generant ova. Perfectiora autem praecedunt in ordine naturae. Non ergo quinta die debuerunt fieri pisces et aves, ante animalia terrestria.\n\n[I q. 71 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31364)\n\nIn contrarium sufficit auctoritas Scripturae.\n\n[I q. 71 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31365)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, opus ornatus per ordinem respondet ordini distinctionis. Unde sicut inter tres dies distinctioni deputatos, media, quae est secunda, deputatur distinctioni medii corporis, scilicet aquae; ita inter tres dies deputatos ad opus ornatus, media, idest quinta, deputatur ad ornatum medii corporis, per productionem avium et piscium. Unde sicut Moyses in quarta die nominat luminaria et lucem, ut designet quod quarta dies respondet primae, in qua dixerat lucem factam; ita in hac quinta die facit mentionem de aquis et de firmamento caeli, ut designet quod quinta dies respondet secundae. Sed sciendum est quod, sicut in productione plantarum differt Augustinus ab aliis, ita et in productione piscium et avium. Alii enim dicunt pisces et aves quinta die esse productos in actu, Augustinus autem dicit, V super Gen. ad Litt., quod quinta die aquarum natura produxit pisces et aves potentialiter.\n\n[I q. 71 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31366)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Avicenna posuit omnia animalia posse generari ex aliquali elementorum commixtione absque semine, etiam per viam naturae. Sed hoc videtur inconveniens. Quia natura determinatis mediis procedit ad suos effectus, unde illa quae naturaliter generantur ex semine, non possunt naturaliter sine semine generari. Et ideo dicendum est aliter, quod in naturali generatione animalium, principium activum est virtus formativa quae est in semine, in iis quae ex semine generantur; loco cuius virtutis, in iis quae ex putrefactione generantur, est virtus caelestis corporis. Materiale autem principium in utrorumque animalium generatione, est aliquod elementum vel elementatum. In prima autem rerum institutione, fuit principium activum verbum Dei, quod ex materia elementari produxit animalia vel in actu, secundum alios sanctos; vel virtute, secundum Augustinum. Non quod aqua aut terra habeat in se virtutem producendi omnia animalia, ut Avicenna posuit, sed quia hoc ipsum quod ex materia elementari, virtute seminis vel stellarum, possunt animalia produci, est ex virtute primitus elementis data.\n\n[I q. 71 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31367)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod corpora avium et piscium possunt dupliciter considerari. Uno modo, secundum se. Et sic necesse est quod in eis magis terrestre elementum dominetur, quia ad hoc quod fiat contemperatio mixtionis in corpore animalis necesse est quod quantitative abundet in eo elementum quod est minus activum, scilicet terra. Sed si considerentur secundum quod sunt nata moveri talibus motibus, sic habent affinitatem quandam cum corporibus in quibus moventur. Et sic eorum generatio hic describitur.\n\n[I q. 71 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31368)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod aer, quia insensibilis est, non per seipsum connumeratur, sed cum aliis, partim quidem cum aqua, quantum ad inferiorem partem, quae exhalationibus aquae ingrossatur; partim etiam cum caelo, quantum ad superiorem partem. Aves autem motum habent in inferiori parte aeris, et ideo sub firmamento caeli volare dicuntur, etiam si firmamentum pro nebuloso aere accipiatur. Et ideo productio avium aquae adscribitur.\n\n[I q. 71 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31369)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod natura de uno extremo ad aliud transit per media. Et ideo inter terrestria et aquatica animalia sunt quaedam media, quae communicant cum utrisque; et computantur cum illis cum quibus magis communicant, secundum id quod cum eis communicant; non secundum id quod communicant cum alio extremo. Tamen, ut includantur omnia huiusmodi quae habent aliquid speciale inter pisces, subiungit, postquam dixerat, *producant aquae reptile animae viventis, creavit Deus cete grandia,* et cetera.\n\n[I q. 71 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31370)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod productio horum animalium ordinatur secundum ordinem corporum quae eis ornantur, magis quam secundum propriam dignitatem. Et tamen, in via generationis, ab imperfectioribus ad perfectiora pervenitur.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0001","text":"[I q. 71 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31359)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0002","text":"Deinde considerandum est de opere quintae diei. Et videtur quod inconvenienter hoc opus describatur. Illud enim aquae producunt, ad cuius productionem sufficit virtus aquae. Sed virtus aquae non sufficit ad productionem omnium piscium et avium, cum videamus plura eorum generari ex semine. Non ergo convenienter dicitur, *producant aquae reptile animae viventis, et volatile super terram*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0003","text":"[I q. 71 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31360)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0004","text":"Praeterea, pisces et aves non tantum producuntur ex aqua, sed in eorum compositione videtur magis terra dominari quam aqua, quia corpora eorum naturaliter moventur ad terram; unde et in terra quiescunt. Non ergo convenienter dicitur pisces et aves ex aqua produci.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0005","text":"[I q. 71 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31361)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0006","text":"Praeterea, sicut pisces habent motum in aquis, ita et aves in aere. Si ergo pisces ex aquis producuntur, aves non deberent produci ex aquis, sed ex aere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0007","text":"[I q. 71 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31362)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0008","text":"Praeterea, non omnes pisces reptant in aquis, cum quidem habeant pedes, quibus gradiuntur in terra, sicut vituli marini. Non ergo sufficienter productio piscium designatur in hoc quod dicitur, *producant aquae reptile animae viventis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0009","text":"[I q. 71 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31363)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0010","text":"Praeterea, animalia terrestria sunt perfectiora avibus et piscibus. Quod patet ex hoc quod habent membra magis distincta, et perfectiorem generationem, generant enim animalia, sed pisces et aves generant ova. Perfectiora autem praecedunt in ordine naturae. Non ergo quinta die debuerunt fieri pisces et aves, ante animalia terrestria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0011","text":"[I q. 71 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31364)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0012","text":"In contrarium sufficit auctoritas Scripturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0013","text":"[I q. 71 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31365)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, opus ornatus per ordinem respondet ordini distinctionis. Unde sicut inter tres dies distinctioni deputatos, media, quae est secunda, deputatur distinctioni medii corporis, scilicet aquae; ita inter tres dies deputatos ad opus ornatus, media, idest quinta, deputatur ad ornatum medii corporis, per productionem avium et piscium. Unde sicut Moyses in quarta die nominat luminaria et lucem, ut designet quod quarta dies respondet primae, in qua dixerat lucem factam; ita in hac quinta die facit mentionem de aquis et de firmamento caeli, ut designet quod quinta dies respondet secundae. Sed sciendum est quod, sicut in productione plantarum differt Augustinus ab aliis, ita et in productione piscium et avium. Alii enim dicunt pisces et aves quinta die esse productos in actu, Augustinus autem dicit, V super Gen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0015","text":"ad Litt., quod quinta die aquarum natura produxit pisces et aves potentialiter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0016","text":"[I q. 71 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31366)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Avicenna posuit omnia animalia posse generari ex aliquali elementorum commixtione absque semine, etiam per viam naturae. Sed hoc videtur inconveniens. Quia natura determinatis mediis procedit ad suos effectus, unde illa quae naturaliter generantur ex semine, non possunt naturaliter sine semine generari. Et ideo dicendum est aliter, quod in naturali generatione animalium, principium activum est virtus formativa quae est in semine, in iis quae ex semine generantur; loco cuius virtutis, in iis quae ex putrefactione generantur, est virtus caelestis corporis. Materiale autem principium in utrorumque animalium generatione, est aliquod elementum vel elementatum. In prima autem rerum institutione, fuit principium activum verbum Dei, quod ex materia elementari produxit animalia vel in actu, secundum alios sanctos; vel virtute, secundum Augustinum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0018","text":"Non quod aqua aut terra habeat in se virtutem producendi omnia animalia, ut Avicenna posuit, sed quia hoc ipsum quod ex materia elementari, virtute seminis vel stellarum, possunt animalia produci, est ex virtute primitus elementis data.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0019","text":"[I q. 71 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31367)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod corpora avium et piscium possunt dupliciter considerari. Uno modo, secundum se. Et sic necesse est quod in eis magis terrestre elementum dominetur, quia ad hoc quod fiat contemperatio mixtionis in corpore animalis necesse est quod quantitative abundet in eo elementum quod est minus activum, scilicet terra. Sed si considerentur secundum quod sunt nata moveri talibus motibus, sic habent affinitatem quandam cum corporibus in quibus moventur. Et sic eorum generatio hic describitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0021","text":"[I q. 71 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31368)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod aer, quia insensibilis est, non per seipsum connumeratur, sed cum aliis, partim quidem cum aqua, quantum ad inferiorem partem, quae exhalationibus aquae ingrossatur; partim etiam cum caelo, quantum ad superiorem partem. Aves autem motum habent in inferiori parte aeris, et ideo sub firmamento caeli volare dicuntur, etiam si firmamentum pro nebuloso aere accipiatur. Et ideo productio avium aquae adscribitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0023","text":"[I q. 71 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31369)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0024","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod natura de uno extremo ad aliud transit per media. Et ideo inter terrestria et aquatica animalia sunt quaedam media, quae communicant cum utrisque; et computantur cum illis cum quibus magis communicant, secundum id quod cum eis communicant; non secundum id quod communicant cum alio extremo. Tamen, ut includantur omnia huiusmodi quae habent aliquid speciale inter pisces, subiungit, postquam dixerat, *producant aquae reptile animae viventis, creavit Deus cete grandia,* et cetera.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0025","text":"[I q. 71 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31370)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0026","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod productio horum animalium ordinatur secundum ordinem corporum quae eis ornantur, magis quam secundum propriam dignitatem. Et tamen, in via generationis, ab imperfectioribus ad perfectiora pervenitur.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"b739e407dc4054cd48f606d6750a7e53ff9010f80a03390913ba50ab10492422","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T145752513162Z_6f058805","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 71","source_sha256":"b739e407dc4054cd48f606d6750a7e53ff9010f80a03390913ba50ab10492422","sha256":"6cb0fa3d99bde86bae4212ead6c38151503efe6f1c764bd920f1206d2f4ee8f6","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:57:52.513182+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0001","text":"[I q. 71 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31359)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0002","text":"Next, we must consider the work of the fifth day. And it seems that this work is described inappropriately. For the waters produce that for whose production the power of water suffices. But the power of water does not suffice for the production of all fish and birds, since we see that many of them are generated from seed. Therefore it is not fittingly said: *Let the waters bring forth the creeping thing of living soul, and the flying thing over the earth*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0003","text":"[I q. 71 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31360)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0004","text":"Moreover, fish and birds are produced not only from water, but in their composition earth seems to predominate more than water, because their bodies are naturally moved toward the earth; hence they also rest upon the earth. Therefore fish and birds are not fittingly said to be produced from water.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0005","text":"[I q. 71 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31361)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0006","text":"Moreover, just as fish have movement in the waters, so birds have it in the air. Therefore, if fish are produced from the waters, birds ought not to be produced from the waters, but from the air.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0007","text":"[I q. 71 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31362)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0008","text":"Moreover, not all fish creep in the waters, since some indeed have feet by which they walk upon the earth, as seals do. Therefore the production of fish is not sufficiently designated in the statement: *Let the waters bring forth the creeping thing of living soul*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0009","text":"[I q. 71 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31363)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0010","text":"Moreover, terrestrial animals are more perfect than birds and fish. This is evident from the fact that they have members more distinct, and a more perfect generation: for animals generate animals, whereas fish and birds generate eggs. But the more perfect precede in the order of nature. Therefore fish and birds ought not to have been made on the fifth day, before terrestrial animals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0011","text":"[I q. 71 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31364)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0012","text":"On the contrary, the authority of Scripture suffices.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0013","text":"[I q. 71 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31365)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that, as was stated above, the work of adornment corresponds in order to the order of distinction. Hence, just as among the three days assigned to distinction, the middle one, namely the second, is assigned to the distinction of the middle body, namely water; so among the three days assigned to the work of adornment, the middle one, that is, the fifth, is assigned to the adornment of the middle body, through the production of birds and fish. Hence, just as Moses on the fourth day names the luminaries and light, in order to indicate that the fourth day corresponds to the first, on which he had said that light was made; so on this fifth day he makes mention of the waters and of the firmament of heaven, in order to indicate that the fifth day corresponds to the second. But it must be known that, just as Augustine differs from the others concerning the production of plants, so also concerning the production of fish and birds. For others say that fish and birds were produced in act on the fifth day, whereas Augustine says, in V *On Genesis*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0015","text":"*to the Letter*, that on the fifth day the nature of water produced fish and birds potentially.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0016","text":"[I q. 71 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31366)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Avicenna held that all animals can be generated from some mixture of the elements without seed, even by way of nature. But this seems unfitting. For nature proceeds to its effects through determinate means; hence those things which are naturally generated from seed cannot naturally be generated without seed. And therefore it must be said otherwise: that in the natural generation of animals, the active principle is the formative power which is in the seed, in those which are generated from seed; in place of which power, in those which are generated from putrefaction, there is the power of the heavenly body. But the material principle in the generation of both kinds of animals is some element or something compounded of elements. In the first institution of things, however, the active principle was the Word of God, which produced animals from elemental matter either in act, according to the other saints; or in power, according to Augustine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0018","text":"Not because water or earth has within itself the power to produce all animals, as Avicenna held, but because the very fact that animals can be produced from elemental matter by the power of seed or of stars is from a power given to the elements from the beginning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0019","text":"[I q. 71 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31367)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0020","text":"To the second, it must be said that the bodies of birds and fish can be considered in two ways. In one way, in themselves. And thus it is necessary that the terrestrial element predominate more in them, because, in order that there be a tempering of mixture in an animal body, it is necessary that the element which is less active, namely earth, abound in it quantitatively. But if they are considered insofar as they are naturally apt to be moved by such movements, then they have a certain affinity with the bodies in which they move. And thus their generation is described here.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0021","text":"[I q. 71 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31368)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0022","text":"To the third, it must be said that air, because it is imperceptible, is not counted separately, but with other things: partly indeed with water, as regards its lower part, which is thickened by the exhalations of water; and partly with heaven, as regards its upper part. But birds have movement in the lower part of the air, and therefore they are said to fly under the firmament of heaven, even if the firmament is understood as cloudy air. And therefore the production of birds is ascribed to water.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0023","text":"[I q. 71 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31369)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0024","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that nature passes from one extreme to another through intermediates. And therefore between terrestrial and aquatic animals there are certain intermediates which share in both; and they are counted with those with which they share more, according to that in which they share with them, not according to that which they share with the other extreme. Nevertheless, in order that all such things which have something special among fish may be included, he adds, after having said, *Let the waters bring forth the creeping thing of living soul*: *God created the great sea creatures*, and so forth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0025","text":"[I q. 71 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31370)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0026","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that the production of these animals is ordered according to the order of the bodies by which they are adorned, rather than according to their own dignity. And yet, in the way of generation, one arrives from the more imperfect at the more perfect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_63:P0026"]}],"source_digest":"3449b8171a133b5043a98244eb5c94e069ce3a8fd544d2addb8c6901ffb4513d","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 71"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 71","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64","title":"First Part · Question 72","order":73,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_64","text":"[I q. 72 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31371)\n\nDeinde quaeritur de opere sextae diei. Et videtur quod inconvenienter describatur. Sicut enim aves et pisces habent viventem animam, ita etiam et animalia terrestria; non autem terrestria animalia sunt ipsa anima vivens. Ergo inconvenienter dicitur, producat terra animam viventem; sed debuit dici, producat terra quadrupedia animae viventis.\n\n[I q. 72 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31372)\n\nPraeterea, genus non debet dividi contra speciem. Sed iumenta et bestiae sub quadrupedibus computantur. Inconvenienter ergo quadrupedia connumerantur iumentis et bestiis.\n\n[I q. 72 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31373)\n\nPraeterea, sicut alia animalia sunt in determinato genere et specie, ita et homo. Sed in factione hominis non fit mentio de suo genere vel specie. Ergo nec in productione aliorum animalium debuit fieri mentio de genere vel specie, cum dicitur, in genere suo, vel specie sua.\n\n[I q. 72 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31374)\n\nPraeterea, animalia terrestria magis sunt similia homini, qui a Deo dicitur benedici, quam aves et pisces. Cum igitur aves et pisces dicantur benedici, multo fortius hoc dici debuit de aliis animalibus.\n\n[I q. 72 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31375)\n\nPraeterea, quaedam animalia generantur ex putrefactione, quae est corruptio quaedam. Corruptio autem non convenit primae institutioni rerum. Non ergo animalia debuerunt in prima rerum institutione produci.\n\n[I q. 72 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31376)\n\nPraeterea, quaedam animalia sunt venenosa et homini noxia. Nihil autem debuit esse homini nocivum ante peccatum. Ergo huiusmodi animalia vel omnino fieri a Deo non debuerunt, qui est bonorum auctor, vel non debuerunt fieri ante peccatum.\n\n[I q. 72 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31377)\n\nIn contrarium sufficit auctoritas Scripturae.\n\n[I q. 72 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31378)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut in die quinto ornatur medium corpus, et respondet secundae diei ita in sexto die ornatur ultimum corpus, scilicet terra, per productionem animalium terrestrium, et respondet tertiae diei. Unde utrobique fit mentio de terra. Et hic etiam, secundum Augustinum animalia terrestria producuntur potentialiter, secundum vero alios sanctos, in actu.\n\n[I q. 72 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31379)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut Basilius dicit, diversus gradus vitae qui in diversis viventibus invenitur, ex modo loquendi Scripturae colligi potest. Plantae enim habent imperfectissimam vitam et occultam. Unde in earum productione nulla mentio fit de vita, sed solum de generatione, quia secundum hanc solum invenitur actus vitae in eis; nutritiva enim et augmentativa generativae deserviunt, ut infra dicetur. Inter animalia vero, perfectiora sunt, communiter loquendo, terrestria avibus et piscibus, non quod pisces memoria careant, ut Basilius dicit, et Augustinus improbat; sed propter distinctionem membrorum, et perfectionem generationis (quantum autem ad aliquas sagacitates, etiam aliqua animalia imperfecta magis vigent, ut apes et formicae). Et ideo pisces vocat, non animam viventem, sed reptile animae viventis, sed terrena animalia vocat animam viventem, propter perfectionem vitae in eis, ac si pisces sint corpora habentia aliquid animae, terrestria vero animalia, propter perfectionem vitae, sint quasi animae dominantes corporibus. Perfectissimus autem gradus vitae est in homine. Et ideo vitam hominis non dicit produci a terra vel aqua, sicut ceterorum animalium, sed a Deo.\n\n[I q. 72 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31380)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod per iumenta, vel pecora, intelliguntur animalia domestica, quae homini serviunt qualitercumque. Per bestias autem intelliguntur animalia saeva, ut ursi et leones. Per reptilia vero, animalia quae vel non habent pedes quibus eleventur a terra, ut serpentes; vel habent breves, quibus parum elevantur, ut lacertae et tortucae et huiusmodi. Sed quia sunt quaedam animalia quae sub nullo horum comprehenduntur, ut cervi et capreae, ut etiam ista comprehenderentur, addidit quadrupedia. Vel quadrupedia praemisit quasi genus, et alia subiunxit quasi species, sunt enim etiam quaedam reptilia quadrupedia, ut lacertae et tortucae.\n\n[I q. 72 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31381)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in aliis animalibus et plantis mentionem fecit de genere et specie, ut designaret generationes similium ex similibus. In homine autem non fuit necessarium ut hoc diceretur, quia quod praemissum fuit de aliis, etiam de homine intelligi potest. Vel quia animalia et plantae producuntur secundum genus et speciem suam, quasi longe a similitudine divina remota, homo autem dicitur formatus ad imaginem et similitudinem Dei.\n\n[I q. 72 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31382)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod benedictio Dei dat virtutem multiplicandi per generationem. Et ideo quod positum est in avibus et piscibus, quae primo occurrunt, non fuit necessarium repeti in terrenis animalibus, sed intelligitur. In hominibus autem iteratur benedictio, quia in eis est quaedam specialis multiplicationis ratio, propter complendum numerum electorum, *et ne quisquam diceret in officio gignendi filios ullum esse peccatum. Plantae vero nullum habent propagandae prolis affectum, ac sine ullo sensu generant, unde indignae iudicatae sunt benedictionis verbis*.\n\n[I q. 72 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31383)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod, cum generatio unius sit corruptio alterius, quod ex corruptione ignobiliorum generentur nobiliora, non repugnat primae rerum institutioni. Unde animalia quae generantur ex corruptione rerum inanimatarum vel plantarum, potuerunt tunc generari. Non autem quae generantur ex corruptione animalium, tunc potuerunt produci, nisi potentialiter tantum.\n\n[I q. 72 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31384)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit in I super Gen. contra Manichaeos, *si in alicuius opificis officinam imperitus intraverit, videt ibi multa instrumenta quorum causas ignorat, et si multum est insipiens, superflua putat. Iam vero si in fornacem incautus ceciderit, aut ferramento aliquo acuto se vulneraverit, noxia existimat ibi esse multa, quorum usum quia novit artifex, insipientiam eius irridet. Sic in hoc mundo quidam audent multa reprehendere, quorum causas non vident, multa enim, etsi domui nostrae non sunt necessaria, eis tamen completur universitatis integritas*. Homo autem ante peccatum ordinate fuisset usus rebus mundi. Unde animalia venenosa ei noxia non fuissent.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0001","text":"[I q. 72 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31371)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0002","text":"Deinde quaeritur de opere sextae diei. Et videtur quod inconvenienter describatur. Sicut enim aves et pisces habent viventem animam, ita etiam et animalia terrestria; non autem terrestria animalia sunt ipsa anima vivens. Ergo inconvenienter dicitur, producat terra animam viventem; sed debuit dici, producat terra quadrupedia animae viventis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0003","text":"[I q. 72 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31372)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0004","text":"Praeterea, genus non debet dividi contra speciem. Sed iumenta et bestiae sub quadrupedibus computantur. Inconvenienter ergo quadrupedia connumerantur iumentis et bestiis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0005","text":"[I q. 72 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31373)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0006","text":"Praeterea, sicut alia animalia sunt in determinato genere et specie, ita et homo. Sed in factione hominis non fit mentio de suo genere vel specie. Ergo nec in productione aliorum animalium debuit fieri mentio de genere vel specie, cum dicitur, in genere suo, vel specie sua.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0007","text":"[I q. 72 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31374)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0008","text":"Praeterea, animalia terrestria magis sunt similia homini, qui a Deo dicitur benedici, quam aves et pisces. Cum igitur aves et pisces dicantur benedici, multo fortius hoc dici debuit de aliis animalibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0009","text":"[I q. 72 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31375)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0010","text":"Praeterea, quaedam animalia generantur ex putrefactione, quae est corruptio quaedam. Corruptio autem non convenit primae institutioni rerum. Non ergo animalia debuerunt in prima rerum institutione produci.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0011","text":"[I q. 72 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31376)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0012","text":"Praeterea, quaedam animalia sunt venenosa et homini noxia. Nihil autem debuit esse homini nocivum ante peccatum. Ergo huiusmodi animalia vel omnino fieri a Deo non debuerunt, qui est bonorum auctor, vel non debuerunt fieri ante peccatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0013","text":"[I q. 72 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31377)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0014","text":"In contrarium sufficit auctoritas Scripturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0015","text":"[I q. 72 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31378)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut in die quinto ornatur medium corpus, et respondet secundae diei ita in sexto die ornatur ultimum corpus, scilicet terra, per productionem animalium terrestrium, et respondet tertiae diei. Unde utrobique fit mentio de terra. Et hic etiam, secundum Augustinum animalia terrestria producuntur potentialiter, secundum vero alios sanctos, in actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0017","text":"[I q. 72 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31379)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0018","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut Basilius dicit, diversus gradus vitae qui in diversis viventibus invenitur, ex modo loquendi Scripturae colligi potest. Plantae enim habent imperfectissimam vitam et occultam. Unde in earum productione nulla mentio fit de vita, sed solum de generatione, quia secundum hanc solum invenitur actus vitae in eis; nutritiva enim et augmentativa generativae deserviunt, ut infra dicetur. Inter animalia vero, perfectiora sunt, communiter loquendo, terrestria avibus et piscibus, non quod pisces memoria careant, ut Basilius dicit, et Augustinus improbat; sed propter distinctionem membrorum, et perfectionem generationis (quantum autem ad aliquas sagacitates, etiam aliqua animalia imperfecta magis vigent, ut apes et formicae).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0019","text":"Et ideo pisces vocat, non animam viventem, sed reptile animae viventis, sed terrena animalia vocat animam viventem, propter perfectionem vitae in eis, ac si pisces sint corpora habentia aliquid animae, terrestria vero animalia, propter perfectionem vitae, sint quasi animae dominantes corporibus. Perfectissimus autem gradus vitae est in homine. Et ideo vitam hominis non dicit produci a terra vel aqua, sicut ceterorum animalium, sed a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0020","text":"[I q. 72 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31380)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0021","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod per iumenta, vel pecora, intelliguntur animalia domestica, quae homini serviunt qualitercumque. Per bestias autem intelliguntur animalia saeva, ut ursi et leones. Per reptilia vero, animalia quae vel non habent pedes quibus eleventur a terra, ut serpentes; vel habent breves, quibus parum elevantur, ut lacertae et tortucae et huiusmodi. Sed quia sunt quaedam animalia quae sub nullo horum comprehenduntur, ut cervi et capreae, ut etiam ista comprehenderentur, addidit quadrupedia. Vel quadrupedia praemisit quasi genus, et alia subiunxit quasi species, sunt enim etiam quaedam reptilia quadrupedia, ut lacertae et tortucae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0022","text":"[I q. 72 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31381)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0023","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in aliis animalibus et plantis mentionem fecit de genere et specie, ut designaret generationes similium ex similibus. In homine autem non fuit necessarium ut hoc diceretur, quia quod praemissum fuit de aliis, etiam de homine intelligi potest. Vel quia animalia et plantae producuntur secundum genus et speciem suam, quasi longe a similitudine divina remota, homo autem dicitur formatus ad imaginem et similitudinem Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0024","text":"[I q. 72 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31382)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0025","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod benedictio Dei dat virtutem multiplicandi per generationem. Et ideo quod positum est in avibus et piscibus, quae primo occurrunt, non fuit necessarium repeti in terrenis animalibus, sed intelligitur. In hominibus autem iteratur benedictio, quia in eis est quaedam specialis multiplicationis ratio, propter complendum numerum electorum, *et ne quisquam diceret in officio gignendi filios ullum esse peccatum. Plantae vero nullum habent propagandae prolis affectum, ac sine ullo sensu generant, unde indignae iudicatae sunt benedictionis verbis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0026","text":"[I q. 72 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31383)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0027","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod, cum generatio unius sit corruptio alterius, quod ex corruptione ignobiliorum generentur nobiliora, non repugnat primae rerum institutioni. Unde animalia quae generantur ex corruptione rerum inanimatarum vel plantarum, potuerunt tunc generari. Non autem quae generantur ex corruptione animalium, tunc potuerunt produci, nisi potentialiter tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0028","text":"[I q. 72 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31384)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0029","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit in I super Gen. contra Manichaeos, *si in alicuius opificis officinam imperitus intraverit, videt ibi multa instrumenta quorum causas ignorat, et si multum est insipiens, superflua putat. Iam vero si in fornacem incautus ceciderit, aut ferramento aliquo acuto se vulneraverit, noxia existimat ibi esse multa, quorum usum quia novit artifex, insipientiam eius irridet. Sic in hoc mundo quidam audent multa reprehendere, quorum causas non vident, multa enim, etsi domui nostrae non sunt necessaria, eis tamen completur universitatis integritas*. Homo autem ante peccatum ordinate fuisset usus rebus mundi. Unde animalia venenosa ei noxia non fuissent.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9d708f5d772dbc29fc58facc103a49b793e24adcf945830e51756563c9e49f7f","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T145815218416Z_42f528ca","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 72","source_sha256":"9d708f5d772dbc29fc58facc103a49b793e24adcf945830e51756563c9e49f7f","sha256":"0b9a29d9759d8715342b9ff714f916a4796ab2883c5959a876579d315547a05a","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:58:15.218435+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0001","text":"[I q. 72 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31371)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0002","text":"Next, there is inquiry concerning the work of the sixth day. And it seems that it is described unfittingly. For just as birds and fish have a living soul, so too do terrestrial animals; yet terrestrial animals are not themselves a living soul. Therefore it is unfittingly said, Let the earth bring forth a living soul; rather, it ought to have been said, Let the earth bring forth four-footed animals of a living soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0003","text":"[I q. 72 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31372)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0004","text":"Further, a genus ought not to be divided against a species. But beasts of burden and wild beasts are included under four-footed animals. Therefore four-footed animals are unfittingly numbered together with beasts of burden and wild beasts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0005","text":"[I q. 72 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31373)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0006","text":"Further, just as the other animals are in a determinate genus and species, so too is man. But in the making of man no mention is made of his genus or species. Therefore neither in the production of the other animals ought mention to have been made of genus or species, when it is said, according to its genus, or according to its species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0007","text":"[I q. 72 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31374)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0008","text":"Further, terrestrial animals are more like man, who is said to be blessed by God, than are birds and fish. Therefore, since birds and fish are said to be blessed, much more ought this to have been said of the other animals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0009","text":"[I q. 72 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31375)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0010","text":"Further, certain animals are generated from putrefaction, which is a kind of corruption. But corruption does not befit the first institution of things. Therefore animals ought not to have been produced in the first institution of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0011","text":"[I q. 72 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31376)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0012","text":"Further, certain animals are venomous and harmful to man. But nothing ought to have been harmful to man before sin. Therefore animals of this kind either ought not at all to have been made by God, who is the author of good things, or ought not to have been made before sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0013","text":"[I q. 72 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31377)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0014","text":"On the contrary, the authority of Scripture suffices.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0015","text":"[I q. 72 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31378)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0016","text":"I answer that, just as on the fifth day the middle body is adorned, and corresponds to the second day, so on the sixth day the last body, namely the earth, is adorned through the production of terrestrial animals, and corresponds to the third day. Hence in both places mention is made of the earth. And here also, according to Augustine, terrestrial animals are produced potentially; according to the other saints, however, actually.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0017","text":"[I q. 72 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31379)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0018","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, as Basil says, the different grade of life found in different living things can be gathered from the manner of speaking of Scripture. For plants have a most imperfect and hidden life. Hence in their production no mention is made of life, but only of generation, because an act of life is found in them only according to this; for the nutritive and augmentative powers serve the generative, as will be said below. But among animals, terrestrial animals are, speaking generally, more perfect than birds and fish—not because fish lack memory, as Basil says and Augustine disproves, but because of the distinction of their members and the perfection of their generation (although, as regards certain shrewdnesses, even some imperfect animals are more vigorous, such as bees and ants).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0019","text":"And therefore he calls fish not a living soul, but a creeping thing of a living soul; but he calls terrestrial animals a living soul, on account of the perfection of life in them, as though fish were bodies having something of soul, whereas terrestrial animals, on account of the perfection of life, were as it were souls ruling bodies. But the most perfect grade of life is in man. And therefore he does not say that the life of man is produced from earth or water, as that of the other animals, but from God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0020","text":"[I q. 72 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31380)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0021","text":"To the second it must be said that by beasts of burden, or cattle, are understood domestic animals, which serve man in whatever way. But by wild beasts are understood fierce animals, such as bears and lions. By creeping things, however, are understood animals which either do not have feet by which they are raised from the earth, as serpents; or have short ones, by which they are raised but little, as lizards and tortoises and things of this sort. But because there are certain animals which are included under none of these, such as stags and roe deer, in order that these too might be included, he added four-footed animals. Or he set forth four-footed animals first as a genus, and added the others as species; for there are also certain creeping things that are four-footed, such as lizards and tortoises.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0022","text":"[I q. 72 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31381)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0023","text":"To the third it must be said that in the case of the other animals and plants he made mention of genus and species, in order to designate the generations of like from like. But in the case of man it was not necessary that this be said, because what was set forth concerning the others can also be understood concerning man. Or because animals and plants are produced according to their genus and species, as though far removed from the divine likeness, whereas man is said to be formed to the image and likeness of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0024","text":"[I q. 72 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31382)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0025","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the blessing of God gives the power of multiplying through generation. And therefore, what was set down concerning birds and fish, which occur first, did not need to be repeated concerning terrestrial animals, but is understood. In human beings, however, the blessing is repeated, because in them there is a certain special reason for multiplication, for the sake of completing the number of the elect, *and lest anyone should say that there is any sin in the office of begetting children. But plants have no inclination to propagate offspring, and generate without any sensation; hence they were judged unworthy of the words of blessing*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0026","text":"[I q. 72 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31383)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0027","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that, since the generation of one thing is the corruption of another, the fact that nobler things are generated from the corruption of less noble things is not contrary to the first institution of things. Hence animals that are generated from the corruption of inanimate things or plants could then have been generated. But those that are generated from the corruption of animals could not then have been produced, except only potentially.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0028","text":"[I q. 72 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31384)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0029","text":"To the sixth, it must be said that, as Augustine says in Book I of On Genesis against the Manicheans, *if an unskilled person enters the workshop of some craftsman, he sees there many instruments whose purposes he does not know, and if he is very foolish, he thinks them superfluous. But if, unwarily, he falls into a furnace or wounds himself on some sharp tool, he judges that there are many harmful things there, the craftsman, because he knows their use, ridiculing his foolishness. Thus, in this world, some dare to criticize many things whose reasons they do not see; for many things, although they are not necessary for our house, nevertheless the integrity of the universe is completed by them*. But before sin, man would have made ordered use of the things of the world. Hence venomous animals would not have been harmful to him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_64:P0029"]}],"source_digest":"40df7545de9d991497c260f9a6ed10a3f311561e76a6b100208fbe950a6e118d","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 72"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 72","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65","title":"First Part · Question 73","order":74,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_65","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 73 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31385)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de iis quae pertinent ad septimum diem. Et circa hoc quaeruntur tria. Primo, de completione operum. Secundo, de requie Dei. Tertio, de benedictione et sanctificatione huius diei.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 73 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31386)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod completio divinorum operum non debeat septimo diei adscribi. Omnia enim quae in hoc saeculo aguntur, ad divina opera pertinent. Sed consummatio saeculi erit in fine mundi, ut habetur Matth. XIII. Tempus etiam incarnationis Christi est cuiusdam completionis tempus, unde dicitur tempus plenitudinis, Gal. IV. Et ipse Christus moriens dixit, consummatum est, ut dicitur Ioan. XIX. Non ergo completio divinorum operum competit diei septimo.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31387)\n\nPraeterea, quicumque complet opus suum, aliquid facit. Sed Deus non legitur septimo die aliquid fecisse, quinimmo ab omni opere quievisse. Ergo completio operum non competit septimo diei.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31388)\n\nPraeterea, non dicitur aliquid esse completum, cui multa superadduntur, nisi forte sint illa superflua, quia perfectum dicitur cui nihil deest eorum quae debet habere. Sed post septimum diem multa sunt facta, et productio multorum individuorum; et etiam quarumdam novarum specierum, quae frequenter apparent, praecipue in animalibus ex putrefactione generatis. Quotidie etiam Deus novas animas creat. Novum etiam fuit incarnationis opus, de quo dicitur Ierem. XXXI, novum faciet dominus super terram. Nova etiam sunt miraculosa opera, de quibus dicitur Eccli. XXXVI, *innova signa, et immuta mirabilia*. Innovabuntur etiam omnia in glorificatione sanctorum, secundum illud Apoc. XXI, *et dixit qui sedebat in throno, ecce nova facio omnia*. Completio ergo divinorum operum non debet attribui septimo diei.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31389)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, *complevit Deus die septimo opus suum quod fecerat*.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31390)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod duplex est rei perfectio, prima, et secunda. Prima quidem perfectio est, secundum quod res in sua substantia est perfecta. Quae quidem perfectio est forma totius, quae ex integritate partium consurgit. Perfectio autem secunda est finis. Finis autem vel est operatio, sicut finis citharistae est citharizare, vel est aliquid ad quod per operationem pervenitur, sicut finis aedificatoris est domus, quam aedificando facit. Prima autem perfectio est causa secundae, quia forma est principium operationis. Ultima autem perfectio, quae est finis totius universi, est perfecta beatitudo sanctorum; quae erit in ultima consummatione saeculi. Prima autem perfectio, quae est in integritate universi, fuit in prima rerum institutione. Et haec deputatur septimo diei.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31391)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, perfectio prima est causa secundae. Ad beatitudinem autem consequendam duo requiruntur, natura et gratia. Ipsa ergo beatitudinis perfectio erit in fine mundi, ut dictum est. Sed ista consummatio praecessit causaliter, quantum ad naturam quidem, in prima rerum institutione, quantum ad gratiam vero, in incarnatione Christi, quia *gratia et veritas per Iesum Christum facta est*, ut dicitur Ioan. I. Sic igitur in septima die fuit consummatio naturae; in incarnatione Christi, consummatio gratiae; in fine mundi, consummatio gloriae.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31392)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod septima die Deus aliquid operatus est, non novam creaturam condendo, sed creaturam administrando, et ad propriam operationem eam movendo, quod iam aliqualiter pertinet ad inchoationem quandam secundae perfectionis. Et ideo consummatio operum, secundum nostram translationem, attribuitur diei septimae. Sed secundum aliam translationem, attribuitur diei sextae. Et utrumque potest stare. Quia consummatio quae est secundum integritatem partium universi, competit sextae diei, consummatio quae est secundum operationem partium, competit septimae. Vel potest dici quod in motu continuo, quandiu aliquid potest moveri ulterius, non dicitur motus perfectus ante quietem, quies enim demonstrat motum consummatum. Deus autem poterat plures creaturas facere, praeter illas quas fecerat sex diebus. Unde hoc ipso quod cessavit novas creaturas condere in septima die, dicitur opus suum consummasse.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31393)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod nihil postmodum a Deo factum est totaliter novum, quin aliqualiter in operibus sex dierum praecesserit. Quaedam enim praeextiterunt materialiter, sicut quod Deus de costa Adae formavit mulierem. Quaedam vero praeextiterunt in operibus sex dierum, non solum materialiter, sed etiam causaliter, sicut individua quae nunc generantur, praecesserunt in primis individuis suarum specierum. Species etiam novae, si quae apparent, praeextiterunt in quibusdam activis virtutibus, sicut et animalia ex putrefactione generata producuntur ex virtutibus stellarum et elementorum quas a principio acceperunt, etiam si novae species talium animalium producantur. Animalia etiam quaedam secundum novam speciem aliquando oriuntur ex commixtione animalium diversorum secundum speciem, sicut cum ex asino et equa generatur mulus, et haec etiam praecesserunt causaliter in operibus sex dierum. Quaedam vero praecesserunt secundum similitudinem; sicut animae quae nunc creantur. Et similiter incarnationis opus, quia, ut dicitur Philipp. II, filius Dei est in similitudinem hominum factus. Gloria etiam spiritualis secundum similitudinem praecessit in Angelis, corporalis vero in caelo, praecipue Empyreo. Unde dicitur Eccle. I, *nihil sub sole novum; iam enim praecessit in saeculis quae fuerunt ante nos*.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 73 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31394)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus septima die non requievit ab omni suo opere. Dicitur enim Ioan. V, *pater meus usque modo operatur, et ego operor*. Non ergo septima die requievit ab omni opere.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31395)\n\nPraeterea, requies motui opponitur; vel labori qui interdum causatur ex motu. Sed Deus immobiliter, et absque labore sua opera produxit. Non ergo dicendum est septima die a suo opere requievisse.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31396)\n\nSi dicatur quod Deus requievit die septima quia fecit hominem requiescere, contra, requies contraponitur eius operationi. Sed quod dicitur, Deus creavit vel fecit hoc vel illud, non exponitur quod Deus hominem fecit creare aut facere. Ergo nec convenienter exponi potest, ut dicatur Deus requievisse, quia fecit hominem requiescere.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31397)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, *requievit Deus die septimo ab omni opere quod patrarat*.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31398)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quies proprie opponitur motui; et per consequens labori, qui ex motu consurgit. Quamvis autem motus proprie acceptus sit corporum, tamen nomen motus etiam ad spiritualia derivatur dupliciter. Uno modo, secundum quod omnis operatio motus dicitur, sic enim et divina bonitas quodammodo movetur et procedit in res, secundum quod se eis communicat, ut Dionysius dicit, II cap. de Div. Nom. Alio modo, desiderium in aliud tendens quidam motus dicitur. Unde et requies dupliciter accipitur, uno modo, pro cessatione ab operibus; alio modo, pro impletione desiderii. Et utroque modo dicitur Deus requievisse die septima. Primo quidem, quia die septima cessavit novas creaturas condere, nihil enim postea fecit, quod non aliquo modo praecesserit in primis operibus, ut dictum est. Alio modo, secundum quod rebus conditis ipse non indigebat, sed seipso fruendo beatus est. Unde post conditionem omnium operum, non dicitur quod in suis operibus requievit, quasi eis ad suam beatitudinem indigens, sed ab eis requievit, utique in seipso, quia ipse sufficit sibi et implet desiderium suum. Et quamvis ab aeterno in seipso requieverit, tamen quod post opera condita in seipso requievit, hoc pertinet ad septimum diem. Et hoc est ab operibus requiescere, ut Augustinus dicit, super Gen. ad Litt.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31399)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Deus usque modo operatur, conservando et administrando creaturam conditam, non autem novam creaturam condendo.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31400)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod requies non opponitur labori sive motui, sed productioni novarum rerum, et desiderio in aliud tendenti, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31401)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut Deus in solo se requiescit, et se fruendo beatus est; ita et nos per solam Dei fruitionem beati efficimur. Et sic etiam facit nos a suis et nostris operibus in seipso requiescere. Est ergo conveniens expositio, ut dicatur Deus requievisse, quia nos requiescere facit. Sed non est haec sola ponenda, sed alia expositio est principalior et prior.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 73 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31402)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod benedictio et sanctificatio non debeatur diei septimae. Tempus enim aliquod consuevit dici benedictum aut sanctum, propter aliquod bonum quod in illo tempore evenit; aut propter hoc quod aliquod malum vitatur sed Deo nihil accrescit aut deperit, sive operetur, sive ab operando cesset. Non ergo specialis benedictio et sanctificatio debetur diei septimae.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31403)\n\nPraeterea, benedictio a bonitate dicitur. Sed bonum est diffusivum et communicativum sui, secundum Dionysium. Ergo magis debuerunt benedici dies in quibus creaturas produxit, quam ille dies in quo a producendis creaturis cessavit.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31404)\n\nPraeterea, superius in singulis creaturis quaedam benedictio commemorata est, dum in singulis operibus dictum est, vidit Deus quod esset bonum. Non oportuit igitur quod post omnium productionem, dies septima benediceretur.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31405)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, *benedixit Deus diei septimo, et sanctificavit illum, quia in illo cessaverat ab omni opere suo*.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31406)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, requies Dei in die septima dupliciter accipitur. Primo quidem, quantum ad hoc, quod cessavit a novis operibus condendis, ita tamen quod creaturam conditam conservat et administrat. Alio modo, secundum quod post opera requievit in seipso. Quantum ergo ad primum, competit septimae diei benedictio. Quia, sicut supra dictum est, benedictio ad multiplicationem pertinet, unde dictum est creaturis quas benedixit, crescite et multiplicamini. Multiplicatio autem rerum fit per administrationem creaturae, secundum quam ex similibus similia generantur. Quantum vero ad secundum, competit septimae diei sanctificatio. Maxime enim sanctificatio cuiuslibet attenditur in hoc quod in Deo requiescit, unde et res Deo dedicatae sanctae dicuntur.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31407)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod non propter hoc dies septimus sanctificatur, quia Deo possit aliquid accrescere vel decrescere, sed quia creaturis aliquid accrescit per multiplicationem et quietem in Deo.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31408)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod in primis sex diebus productae sunt res in suis primis causis. Sed postea ex illis primis causis res multiplicantur et conservantur, quod etiam ad bonitatem divinam pertinet. Cuius etiam perfectio in hoc maxime ostenditur quod in ipsa sola et ipse requiescit, et nos requiescere possumus, ea fruentes.\n\n[I q. 73 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31409)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod bonum quod in singulis diebus commemoratur, pertinet ad primam naturae institutionem, benedictio autem diei septimae pertinet ad naturae propagationem.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0002","text":"[I q. 73 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31385)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de iis quae pertinent ad septimum diem. Et circa hoc quaeruntur tria. Primo, de completione operum. Secundo, de requie Dei. Tertio, de benedictione et sanctificatione huius diei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0005","text":"[I q. 73 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31386)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod completio divinorum operum non debeat septimo diei adscribi. Omnia enim quae in hoc saeculo aguntur, ad divina opera pertinent. Sed consummatio saeculi erit in fine mundi, ut habetur Matth. XIII. Tempus etiam incarnationis Christi est cuiusdam completionis tempus, unde dicitur tempus plenitudinis, Gal. IV. Et ipse Christus moriens dixit, consummatum est, ut dicitur Ioan. XIX. Non ergo completio divinorum operum competit diei septimo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0007","text":"[I q. 73 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31387)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0008","text":"Praeterea, quicumque complet opus suum, aliquid facit. Sed Deus non legitur septimo die aliquid fecisse, quinimmo ab omni opere quievisse. Ergo completio operum non competit septimo diei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0009","text":"[I q. 73 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31388)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0010","text":"Praeterea, non dicitur aliquid esse completum, cui multa superadduntur, nisi forte sint illa superflua, quia perfectum dicitur cui nihil deest eorum quae debet habere. Sed post septimum diem multa sunt facta, et productio multorum individuorum; et etiam quarumdam novarum specierum, quae frequenter apparent, praecipue in animalibus ex putrefactione generatis. Quotidie etiam Deus novas animas creat. Novum etiam fuit incarnationis opus, de quo dicitur Ierem. XXXI, novum faciet dominus super terram. Nova etiam sunt miraculosa opera, de quibus dicitur Eccli. XXXVI, *innova signa, et immuta mirabilia*. Innovabuntur etiam omnia in glorificatione sanctorum, secundum illud Apoc. XXI, *et dixit qui sedebat in throno, ecce nova facio omnia*. Completio ergo divinorum operum non debet attribui septimo diei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0011","text":"[I q. 73 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31389)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, *complevit Deus die septimo opus suum quod fecerat*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0013","text":"[I q. 73 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31390)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod duplex est rei perfectio, prima, et secunda. Prima quidem perfectio est, secundum quod res in sua substantia est perfecta. Quae quidem perfectio est forma totius, quae ex integritate partium consurgit. Perfectio autem secunda est finis. Finis autem vel est operatio, sicut finis citharistae est citharizare, vel est aliquid ad quod per operationem pervenitur, sicut finis aedificatoris est domus, quam aedificando facit. Prima autem perfectio est causa secundae, quia forma est principium operationis. Ultima autem perfectio, quae est finis totius universi, est perfecta beatitudo sanctorum; quae erit in ultima consummatione saeculi. Prima autem perfectio, quae est in integritate universi, fuit in prima rerum institutione. Et haec deputatur septimo diei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0015","text":"[I q. 73 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31391)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0016","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, perfectio prima est causa secundae. Ad beatitudinem autem consequendam duo requiruntur, natura et gratia. Ipsa ergo beatitudinis perfectio erit in fine mundi, ut dictum est. Sed ista consummatio praecessit causaliter, quantum ad naturam quidem, in prima rerum institutione, quantum ad gratiam vero, in incarnatione Christi, quia *gratia et veritas per Iesum Christum facta est*, ut dicitur Ioan. I. Sic igitur in septima die fuit consummatio naturae; in incarnatione Christi, consummatio gratiae; in fine mundi, consummatio gloriae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0017","text":"[I q. 73 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31392)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0018","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod septima die Deus aliquid operatus est, non novam creaturam condendo, sed creaturam administrando, et ad propriam operationem eam movendo, quod iam aliqualiter pertinet ad inchoationem quandam secundae perfectionis. Et ideo consummatio operum, secundum nostram translationem, attribuitur diei septimae. Sed secundum aliam translationem, attribuitur diei sextae. Et utrumque potest stare. Quia consummatio quae est secundum integritatem partium universi, competit sextae diei, consummatio quae est secundum operationem partium, competit septimae. Vel potest dici quod in motu continuo, quandiu aliquid potest moveri ulterius, non dicitur motus perfectus ante quietem, quies enim demonstrat motum consummatum. Deus autem poterat plures creaturas facere, praeter illas quas fecerat sex diebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0019","text":"Unde hoc ipso quod cessavit novas creaturas condere in septima die, dicitur opus suum consummasse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0020","text":"[I q. 73 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31393)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod nihil postmodum a Deo factum est totaliter novum, quin aliqualiter in operibus sex dierum praecesserit. Quaedam enim praeextiterunt materialiter, sicut quod Deus de costa Adae formavit mulierem. Quaedam vero praeextiterunt in operibus sex dierum, non solum materialiter, sed etiam causaliter, sicut individua quae nunc generantur, praecesserunt in primis individuis suarum specierum. Species etiam novae, si quae apparent, praeextiterunt in quibusdam activis virtutibus, sicut et animalia ex putrefactione generata producuntur ex virtutibus stellarum et elementorum quas a principio acceperunt, etiam si novae species talium animalium producantur. Animalia etiam quaedam secundum novam speciem aliquando oriuntur ex commixtione animalium diversorum secundum speciem, sicut cum ex asino et equa generatur mulus, et haec etiam praecesserunt causaliter in operibus sex dierum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0022","text":"Quaedam vero praecesserunt secundum similitudinem; sicut animae quae nunc creantur. Et similiter incarnationis opus, quia, ut dicitur Philipp. II, filius Dei est in similitudinem hominum factus. Gloria etiam spiritualis secundum similitudinem praecessit in Angelis, corporalis vero in caelo, praecipue Empyreo. Unde dicitur Eccle. I, *nihil sub sole novum; iam enim praecessit in saeculis quae fuerunt ante nos*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0023","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0024","text":"[I q. 73 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31394)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0025","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus septima die non requievit ab omni suo opere. Dicitur enim Ioan. V, *pater meus usque modo operatur, et ego operor*. Non ergo septima die requievit ab omni opere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0026","text":"[I q. 73 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31395)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0027","text":"Praeterea, requies motui opponitur; vel labori qui interdum causatur ex motu. Sed Deus immobiliter, et absque labore sua opera produxit. Non ergo dicendum est septima die a suo opere requievisse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0028","text":"[I q. 73 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31396)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0029","text":"Si dicatur quod Deus requievit die septima quia fecit hominem requiescere, contra, requies contraponitur eius operationi. Sed quod dicitur, Deus creavit vel fecit hoc vel illud, non exponitur quod Deus hominem fecit creare aut facere. Ergo nec convenienter exponi potest, ut dicatur Deus requievisse, quia fecit hominem requiescere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0030","text":"[I q. 73 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31397)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0031","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, *requievit Deus die septimo ab omni opere quod patrarat*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0032","text":"[I q. 73 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31398)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0033","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quies proprie opponitur motui; et per consequens labori, qui ex motu consurgit. Quamvis autem motus proprie acceptus sit corporum, tamen nomen motus etiam ad spiritualia derivatur dupliciter. Uno modo, secundum quod omnis operatio motus dicitur, sic enim et divina bonitas quodammodo movetur et procedit in res, secundum quod se eis communicat, ut Dionysius dicit, II cap. de Div. Nom. Alio modo, desiderium in aliud tendens quidam motus dicitur. Unde et requies dupliciter accipitur, uno modo, pro cessatione ab operibus; alio modo, pro impletione desiderii. Et utroque modo dicitur Deus requievisse die septima. Primo quidem, quia die septima cessavit novas creaturas condere, nihil enim postea fecit, quod non aliquo modo praecesserit in primis operibus, ut dictum est. Alio modo, secundum quod rebus conditis ipse non indigebat, sed seipso fruendo beatus est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0034","text":"Unde post conditionem omnium operum, non dicitur quod in suis operibus requievit, quasi eis ad suam beatitudinem indigens, sed ab eis requievit, utique in seipso, quia ipse sufficit sibi et implet desiderium suum. Et quamvis ab aeterno in seipso requieverit, tamen quod post opera condita in seipso requievit, hoc pertinet ad septimum diem. Et hoc est ab operibus requiescere, ut Augustinus dicit, super Gen. ad Litt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0035","text":"[I q. 73 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31399)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0036","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Deus usque modo operatur, conservando et administrando creaturam conditam, non autem novam creaturam condendo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0037","text":"[I q. 73 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31400)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0038","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod requies non opponitur labori sive motui, sed productioni novarum rerum, et desiderio in aliud tendenti, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0039","text":"[I q. 73 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31401)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0040","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut Deus in solo se requiescit, et se fruendo beatus est; ita et nos per solam Dei fruitionem beati efficimur. Et sic etiam facit nos a suis et nostris operibus in seipso requiescere. Est ergo conveniens expositio, ut dicatur Deus requievisse, quia nos requiescere facit. Sed non est haec sola ponenda, sed alia expositio est principalior et prior.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0041","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0042","text":"[I q. 73 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31402)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0043","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod benedictio et sanctificatio non debeatur diei septimae. Tempus enim aliquod consuevit dici benedictum aut sanctum, propter aliquod bonum quod in illo tempore evenit; aut propter hoc quod aliquod malum vitatur sed Deo nihil accrescit aut deperit, sive operetur, sive ab operando cesset. Non ergo specialis benedictio et sanctificatio debetur diei septimae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0044","text":"[I q. 73 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31403)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0045","text":"Praeterea, benedictio a bonitate dicitur. Sed bonum est diffusivum et communicativum sui, secundum Dionysium. Ergo magis debuerunt benedici dies in quibus creaturas produxit, quam ille dies in quo a producendis creaturis cessavit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0046","text":"[I q. 73 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31404)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0047","text":"Praeterea, superius in singulis creaturis quaedam benedictio commemorata est, dum in singulis operibus dictum est, vidit Deus quod esset bonum. Non oportuit igitur quod post omnium productionem, dies septima benediceretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0048","text":"[I q. 73 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31405)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0049","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, *benedixit Deus diei septimo, et sanctificavit illum, quia in illo cessaverat ab omni opere suo*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0050","text":"[I q. 73 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31406)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0051","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, requies Dei in die septima dupliciter accipitur. Primo quidem, quantum ad hoc, quod cessavit a novis operibus condendis, ita tamen quod creaturam conditam conservat et administrat. Alio modo, secundum quod post opera requievit in seipso. Quantum ergo ad primum, competit septimae diei benedictio. Quia, sicut supra dictum est, benedictio ad multiplicationem pertinet, unde dictum est creaturis quas benedixit, crescite et multiplicamini. Multiplicatio autem rerum fit per administrationem creaturae, secundum quam ex similibus similia generantur. Quantum vero ad secundum, competit septimae diei sanctificatio. Maxime enim sanctificatio cuiuslibet attenditur in hoc quod in Deo requiescit, unde et res Deo dedicatae sanctae dicuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0052","text":"[I q. 73 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31407)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0053","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod non propter hoc dies septimus sanctificatur, quia Deo possit aliquid accrescere vel decrescere, sed quia creaturis aliquid accrescit per multiplicationem et quietem in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0054","text":"[I q. 73 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31408)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0055","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod in primis sex diebus productae sunt res in suis primis causis. Sed postea ex illis primis causis res multiplicantur et conservantur, quod etiam ad bonitatem divinam pertinet. Cuius etiam perfectio in hoc maxime ostenditur quod in ipsa sola et ipse requiescit, et nos requiescere possumus, ea fruentes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0056","text":"[I q. 73 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31409)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0057","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod bonum quod in singulis diebus commemoratur, pertinet ad primam naturae institutionem, benedictio autem diei septimae pertinet ad naturae propagationem.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"4787b559f4e09894ea9d7838ba28f440897f80afc5235761dad7d864e31338ff","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T145852147524Z_69d70d44","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 73","source_sha256":"4787b559f4e09894ea9d7838ba28f440897f80afc5235761dad7d864e31338ff","sha256":"0d76fffb5b5c63ef24cb5284135d5d2d0acb1c6ec326feed5e83c173bae6b33a","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:58:52.147547+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0002","text":"[I q. 73 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31385)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider those things that pertain to the seventh day. And concerning this, three questions are asked. First, concerning the completion of the works. Second, concerning God's rest. Third, concerning the blessing and sanctification of this day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0005","text":"[I q. 73 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31386)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the completion of the divine works ought not to be ascribed to the seventh day. For all things that are done in this age pertain to the divine works. But the consummation of the age will be at the end of the world, as is found in Matthew XIII. The time of Christ's incarnation also is a time of a certain completion; whence it is called the time of fullness, Galatians IV. And Christ himself, when dying, said, “It is consummated,” as is said in John XIX. Therefore the completion of the divine works does not belong to the seventh day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0007","text":"[I q. 73 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31387)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0008","text":"Further, whoever completes his work does something. But God is not read to have done anything on the seventh day; indeed, rather, to have rested from every work. Therefore the completion of the works does not belong to the seventh day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0009","text":"[I q. 73 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31388)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0010","text":"Further, a thing to which many things are added is not said to be complete, unless perhaps those things are superfluous, because that is called perfect to which none of those things that it ought to have is lacking. But after the seventh day many things have been made, including the production of many individuals, and also of certain new species, which frequently appear, especially among animals generated from putrefaction. God also creates new souls daily. The work of the incarnation was likewise new, concerning which it is said in Jeremiah XXXI: “The Lord will make a new thing upon the earth.” New too are miraculous works, concerning which it is said in Ecclesiasticus XXXVI: *Renew signs, and change wonders*. All things will also be renewed in the glorification of the saints, according to that in Apocalypse XXI: *And he who sat upon the throne said: Behold, I make all things new*. Therefore the completion of the divine works ought not to be attributed to the seventh day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0011","text":"[I q. 73 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31389)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0012","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Genesis II: *God completed on the seventh day his work which he had made*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0013","text":"[I q. 73 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31390)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0014","text":"I answer that there is a twofold perfection of a thing: the first and the second. The first perfection is that according to which a thing is perfect in its substance. This perfection is the form of the whole, which arises from the completeness of the parts. But the second perfection is the end. Now the end is either an operation, as the end of a cithara-player is to play the cithara; or it is something to which one arrives through operation, as the end of a builder is the house that he makes by building. But the first perfection is the cause of the second, because form is the principle of operation. The ultimate perfection, which is the end of the whole universe, is the perfect beatitude of the saints; and this will be in the final consummation of the age. But the first perfection, which consists in the completeness of the universe, was in the first institution of things. And this is assigned to the seventh day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0015","text":"[I q. 73 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31391)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0016","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, as has been said, the first perfection is the cause of the second. Now, to attain beatitude, two things are required: nature and grace. Therefore the perfection of beatitude itself will be at the end of the world, as has been said. But this consummation preceded causally: as regards nature, indeed, in the first institution of things; but as regards grace, in the incarnation of Christ, because “grace and truth came through Jesus Christ,” as is said in John I. Thus, then, on the seventh day there was the consummation of nature; in the incarnation of Christ, the consummation of grace; at the end of the world, the consummation of glory.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0017","text":"[I q. 73 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31392)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0018","text":"To the second it must be said that on the seventh day God did work something, not by establishing a new creature, but by governing the creature and moving it to its proper operation; and this already pertains in some way to a certain beginning of the second perfection. And therefore, according to our translation, the consummation of the works is attributed to the seventh day. But according to another translation, it is attributed to the sixth day. And both can stand. For the consummation consisting in the completeness of the parts of the universe belongs to the sixth day; the consummation consisting in the operation of the parts belongs to the seventh. Or it can be said that in continuous motion, as long as something can be moved further, the motion is not called perfect before rest; for rest shows that the motion is consummated. But God could have made more creatures besides those which he had made in six days.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0019","text":"Hence, by the very fact that he ceased to establish new creatures on the seventh day, he is said to have consummated his work.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0020","text":"[I q. 73 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31393)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0021","text":"To the third it must be said that nothing was afterwards made by God wholly new, without having in some way preceded in the works of the six days. For some things pre-existed materially, as when God formed woman from Adam's rib. But some pre-existed in the works of the six days not only materially, but also causally, as the individuals now generated preceded in the first individuals of their species. New species also, if any appear, pre-existed in certain active powers, just as animals generated from putrefaction are produced from the powers of the stars and elements which they received from the beginning, even if new species of such animals are produced. Some animals also sometimes arise as a new species from the mixing of animals diverse in species, as when a mule is generated from an ass and a mare; and these too preceded causally in the works of the six days.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0022","text":"But some preceded according to likeness, as do the souls which are now created. And likewise the work of the incarnation, because, as is said in Philippians II, the Son of God was made in the likeness of men. Spiritual glory also preceded according to likeness in the angels, but bodily glory in heaven, especially the Empyrean. Hence it is said in Ecclesiastes I: *Nothing is new under the sun; for it has already preceded in the ages that were before us*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0023","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0024","text":"[I q. 73 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31394)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0025","text":"We proceed thus to the second point. It seems that God did not rest on the seventh day from all His work. For it is said in John V, *My Father works even until now, and I work*. Therefore, He did not rest on the seventh day from all work.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0026","text":"[I q. 73 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31395)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0027","text":"Further, rest is opposed to motion, or to labor, which is sometimes caused by motion. But God produced His works immovably and without labor. Therefore it should not be said that He rested from His work on the seventh day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0028","text":"[I q. 73 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31396)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0029","text":"If it be said that God rested on the seventh day because He made man rest, on the contrary, rest is set in opposition to His operation. But when it is said that God created or made this or that, it is not expounded as meaning that God made man create or make. Therefore neither can it fittingly be expounded by saying that God rested because He made man rest.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0030","text":"[I q. 73 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31397)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0031","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Gen. II, *God rested on the seventh day from all the work which He had accomplished*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0032","text":"[I q. 73 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31398)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0033","text":"I answer that rest is properly opposed to motion, and consequently to labor, which arises from motion. Although motion, properly taken, belongs to bodies, nevertheless the name of motion is also extended to spiritual things in two ways. In one way, insofar as every operation is called motion; thus the divine goodness too is in a certain manner moved and proceeds into things, insofar as it communicates itself to them, as Dionysius says in chapter II of *On the Divine Names*. In another way, desire tending toward another thing is called a kind of motion. Hence rest too is understood in two ways: in one way, as cessation from works; in another way, as the fulfillment of desire. And in both ways God is said to have rested on the seventh day. First, indeed, because on the seventh day He ceased to create new creatures; for afterward He made nothing that had not in some way preceded in the first works, as has been said. In the other way, insofar as He Himself did not need the things created, but is blessed by enjoying Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0034","text":"Hence, after the creation of all works, it is not said that He rested in His works, as though needing them for His blessedness, but that He rested from them, certainly in Himself, because He suffices for Himself and fulfills His own desire. And although from eternity He rested in Himself, nevertheless, that after created works He rested in Himself pertains to the seventh day. And this is to rest from works, as Augustine says, in his *Literal Commentary on Genesis*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0035","text":"[I q. 73 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31399)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0036","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that God works even until now by preserving and governing the created creature, but not by creating a new creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0037","text":"[I q. 73 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31400)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0038","text":"To the second it must be said that rest is opposed not to labor or motion, but to the production of new things, and to desire tending toward another thing, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0039","text":"[I q. 73 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31401)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0040","text":"To the third it must be said that, just as God rests in Himself alone and is blessed by enjoying Himself, so we too are made blessed through the enjoyment of God alone. And thus He also makes us rest in Himself from His works and ours. Therefore it is a fitting exposition to say that God rested because He makes us rest. But this is not the only exposition to be maintained; rather, the other exposition is more principal and prior.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0041","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0042","text":"[I q. 73 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31402)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0043","text":"We proceed thus to the third point. It seems that blessing and sanctification are not due to the seventh day. For a time is customarily called blessed or holy because of some good that occurs in that time, or because some evil is avoided; but nothing accrues to or is lost by God, whether He works or ceases from working. Therefore no special blessing and sanctification are due to the seventh day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0044","text":"[I q. 73 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31403)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0045","text":"Further, blessing is named from goodness. But the good is diffusive and communicative of itself, according to Dionysius. Therefore the days on which He produced creatures ought rather to have been blessed than that day on which He ceased from producing creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0046","text":"[I q. 73 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31404)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0047","text":"Further, above, a certain blessing was mentioned in each of the individual creatures, when in each of the works it was said: God saw that it was good. Therefore it was not necessary that, after the production of all things, the seventh day should be blessed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0048","text":"[I q. 73 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31405)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0049","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Gen. II, *God blessed the seventh day, and sanctified it, because on it He had ceased from all His work*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0050","text":"[I q. 73 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31406)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0051","text":"I answer that, as was said above, God's rest on the seventh day is understood in two ways. First, with respect to this, that He ceased from creating new works, yet so that He preserves and governs the creature created. In another way, according as after His works He rested in Himself. Therefore, with respect to the first, the blessing of the seventh day is fitting. For, as was said above, blessing pertains to multiplication; hence it was said to the creatures which He blessed, increase and multiply. But the multiplication of things comes about through the governance of the creature, according to which like things are generated from like things. With respect to the second, however, the sanctification of the seventh day is fitting. For the sanctification of anything is considered chiefly in this, that it rests in God; hence also things dedicated to God are called holy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0052","text":"[I q. 73 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31407)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0053","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the seventh day is sanctified not because anything can increase or decrease for God, but because something increases for creatures through multiplication and rest in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0054","text":"[I q. 73 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31408)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0055","text":"To the second it must be said that in the first six days things were produced in their first causes. But afterward, from those first causes, things are multiplied and preserved, which also pertains to the divine goodness. Its perfection is also shown most especially in this: that He Himself rests in it alone, and that we can rest, enjoying it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0056","text":"[I q. 73 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31409)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0057","text":"To the third it must be said that the good which is mentioned on the individual days pertains to the first institution of nature, whereas the blessing of the seventh day pertains to the propagation of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_65:P0057"]}],"source_digest":"73da97e715ca0fd26aa27776a3973b459004474ae0e4cab9652bf5eb3d9c21a0","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 73"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 73","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66","title":"First Part · Question 74","order":75,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_66","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 74 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31410)\n\nDeinde quaeritur de omnibus septem diebus in communi. Et quaeruntur tria. Primo, de sufficientia horum dierum. Secundo, utrum sint unus dies, vel plures. Tertio, de quibusdam modis loquendi quibus Scriptura utitur, enarrando opera sex dierum.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 74 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31411)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sufficienter isti dies enumerentur. Non minus enim distinguitur opus creationis ab operibus distinctionis et ornatus, quam haec duo opera ab invicem. Sed alii dies deputantur distinctioni, et alii ornatui. Ergo etiam alii dies debent assignari creationi.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31412)\n\nPraeterea, aer et ignis sunt nobiliora elementa quam terra et aqua. Sed unus dies deputatur distinctioni aquae, et alius distinctioni terrae. Ergo alii dies debent deputari distinctioni ignis et aeris.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31413)\n\nPraeterea, non minus distant volucres et pisces, quam volucres et terrestria animalia. Homo etiam plus distat ab aliis animalibus, quam alia quaecumque animalia ab invicem. Sed alius dies deputatur productioni piscium maris, et alius productioni animalium terrae. Ergo et alius dies debet deputari productioni avium caeli, et alius productioni hominis.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 1 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31414)\n\nSed contra, videtur quod aliqui dies superflue assignentur. Lumen enim ad luminaria se habet ut accidens ad subiectum. Simul autem producitur subiectum cum proprio accidente. Non ergo alia die debuit produci lux, et alia luminaria.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 1 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31415)\n\nPraeterea, dies isti deputantur primae institutioni mundi. Sed in septima die nihil primitus est institutum. Ergo septima dies non debet aliis connumerari.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31416)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ratio distinctionis horum dierum ex praemissis potest esse manifesta. Oportuit enim primo distingui partes mundi; et postmodum singulas partes ornari, per hoc quod quasi suis habitatoribus replentur. Secundum ergo alios sanctos, in creatura corporali tres partes designantur, prima, quae significatur nomine caeli; media, quae significatur nomine aquae; et, infima quae significatur nomine terrae. Unde et secundum Pythagoricos, perfectio in tribus ponitur, principio, medio et fine, ut dicitur in I de caelo. Prima ergo pars distinguitur prima die, et ornatur quarta; media distinguitur secunda die, et ornatur quinta; infima distinguitur tertia die, et ornatur sexta. Augustinus vero convenit quidem cum eis in ultimis tribus diebus, differt autem in tribus primis. Quia secundum eum, in primo die formatur creatura spiritualis, in duobus aliis creatura corporalis; ita quod in secundo corpora superiora, in tertio corpora inferiora. Et sic perfectio divinorum operum respondet perfectioni senarii numeri, qui consurgit ex suis partibus aliquotis simul iunctis; quae quidem partes sunt unum, duo, tria. Una enim dies deputatur formationi creaturae spiritualis, duae formationi creaturae corporalis, et tres ornatui.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31417)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, opus creationis pertinet ad productionem materiae informis, et naturae spiritualis informis. Quae quidem duo sunt extra tempus, ut ipse dicit in XII Confess., et ideo creatio utriusque ponitur ante omnem diem. Sed secundum alios sanctos, potest dici quod opus distinctionis et ornatus attenditur secundum aliquam mutationem creaturae, quae tempore mensuratur. Opus autem creationis consistit in sola divina actione in instanti rerum substantiam producentis. Et ideo quodlibet opus distinctionis et ornatus dicitur factum in die, creatio autem dicitur facta in principio, quod sonat aliquid indivisibile.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31418)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ignis et aer, quia non distinguuntur a vulgo, inter partes mundi non sunt expresse nominata a Moyse; sed computantur cum medio, scilicet aqua, maxime quantum ad inferiorem aeris partem; quantum vero ad superiorem, computantur cum caelo, ut Augustinus dicit.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31419)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod productio animalium recitatur secundum quod sunt in ornatum partium mundi. Et ideo dies productionis animalium distinguuntur vel uniuntur, secundum hanc convenientiam vel differentiam, qua conveniunt vel differunt in ornando aliquam partem mundi.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31420)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod prima die facta est natura lucis in aliquo subiecto. Sed quarto die facta dicuntur luminaria, non quia eorum substantia sit de novo producta; sed quia sunt aliquo modo formata, quo prius non erant, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31421)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod septimae diei, secundum Augustinum, deputatur aliquid post omnia quae sex diebus attribuuntur, scilicet quod Deus a suis operibus in seipso requiescit. Et ideo oportuit post sex dies fieri mentionem de septimo. Secundum vero alios, potest dici quod in septimo die mundus habuit quendam novum statum, ut scilicet ei nihil adderetur de novo. Et ideo post sex dies ponitur septima, deputata cessationi ab opere.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 74 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31422)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod omnes isti dies sint unus dies. Dicitur enim Gen. II, *istae sunt generationes caeli et terrae, quando creata sunt, in die quo fecit dominus caelum et terram, et omne virgultum agri, antequam oriretur in terra*. Unus ergo est dies in quo fecit caelum et terram et omne virgultum agri. Sed caelum et terram fecit in prima die, vel potius ante omnem diem; virgultum autem agri, tertia die. Ergo unus est primus dies et tertius, et pari ratione, omnes alii.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31423)\n\nPraeterea, Eccli. XVIII, dicitur, *qui vivit in aeternum, creavit omnia simul*. Sed hoc non esset, si dies horum operum essent plures, quia plures dies non sunt simul. Ergo non sunt plures dies, sed unus tantum.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31424)\n\nPraeterea, die septimo cessavit Deus a novis operibus condendis. Si ergo septima dies est alia ab aliis diebus, sequitur quod illam diem non fecerit. Quod est inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31425)\n\nPraeterea, totum opus quod uni diei adscribitur, in instanti perfecit, cum in singulis operibus dicatur, dixit, et factum est. Si igitur sequens opus in diem alium reservasset, sequeretur quod in residua parte illius diei cessasset ab opere, quod esset superfluum. Non igitur est alius dies sequentis operis, a die operis praecedentis.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31426)\n\nSed contra est quod Gen. I dicitur, *factum est vespere et mane dies secundus, et dies tertius*, et sic de aliis. Secundum autem et tertium dici non potest, ubi est unum tantum. Ergo non fuit unus dies tantum.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31427)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in hac quaestione Augustinus ab aliis expositoribus dissentit. Augustinus enim vult, et super Gen. ad Litt., et XI de Civ. Dei, et ad Orosium, quod omnes qui dicuntur septem dies, sunt unus dies septempliciter rebus praesentatus. Alii vero expositores sentiunt quod fuerunt septem dies diversi, et non unus tantum. Hae autem duae opiniones, si referantur ad expositionem litterae Genesis, magnam diversitatem habent. Nam secundum Augustinum, per diem intelligitur cognitio mentis angelicae; ut sic primus dies sit cognito primi divini operis, secundus dies cognitio secundi operis, et sic de aliis. Et dicitur unumquodque opus esse factum in aliqua die, quia nihil Deus produxit in rerum natura, quod non impresserit menti angelicae. Quae quidem multa simul potest cognoscere, praecipue in verbo, in quo omnis Angelorum cognitio perficitur et terminatur. Et sic distinguitur dies secundum naturalem ordinem rerum cognitarum, non secundum successionem cognitionis, aut secundum successionem productionis rerum. Cognitio autem angelica proprie et vere dies nominari potest, cum lux, quae est causa diei, proprie in spiritualibus, secundum Augustinum, inveniatur. Secundum vero alios, per istos dies et successio dierum temporalium ostenditur, et successio productionis rerum. Sed si istae duae opiniones referantur ad modum productionis rerum, non invenitur magna differentia. Et hoc propter duo in quibus, exponendo, diversificatur Augustinus ab aliis, ut ex supra dictis patet. Primo quidem, quia Augustinus per terram et aquam prius creatam, intelligit materiam corporalem totaliter informem, per factionem autem firmamenti, et congregationem aquarum, et apparitionem aridae, intelligit impressionem formarum in materiam corporalem. Alii vero sancti per terram et aquam primo creatas, intelligunt ipsa elementa mundi, sub propriis formis existentia, per sequentia autem opera, aliquam distinctionem in corporibus prius existentibus, ut supra dictum est. Secundo autem differunt quantum ad productionem plantarum et animalium, quae alii ponunt in opere sex dierum esse producta in actu; Augustinus vero potentialiter tantum. In hoc ergo quod Augustinus ponit opera sex dierum esse simul facta, sequitur idem modus productionis rerum. Nam secundum utrosque, in prima rerum productione materia erat sub formis substantialibus elementorum, et secundum utrosque, in prima rerum institutione non fuerunt animalia et plantae in actu. Sed remanet differentia quantum ad quatuor. Quia secundum alios sanctos, post primam productionem creaturae, fuit aliquod tempus in quo non erat lux; item in quo non erat firmamentum formatum; item in quo non erat terra discooperta aquis; et in quo non erant formata caeli luminaria, quod est quartum. Quae non oportet ponere secundum expositionem Augustini. Ut igitur neutri sententiae praeiudicetur, utriusque rationibus respondendum est.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31428)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in die in quo creavit Deus caelum et terram, creavit etiam omne virgultum agri, non in actu, sed antequam oriretur super terram, idest potentialiter. Quod Augustinus adscribit tertiae diei, alii vero primae rerum institutioni.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31429)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Deus creavit omnia simul, quantum ad rerum substantiam quodammodo informem. Sed quantum ad formationem quae facta est per distinctionem et ornatum, non simul. Unde signanter utitur verbo creationis.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31430)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in die septimo cessavit Deus a novis operibus condendis, non autem a propagandis quibusdam ex aliis, ad quam propagationem pertinet quod post primum diem alii succedunt.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31431)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod non est ex impotentia Dei, quasi indigentis tempore ad operandum, quod omnia non sunt simul distincta et ornata, sed ut ordo servaretur in rerum institutione. Et ideo oportuit ut diversis statibus mundi diversi dies deservirent. Semper autem per sequens opus novus perfectionis status mundo est additus.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31432)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, ille ordo dierum referendus est ad naturalem ordinem operum quae diebus attribuuntur.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31433)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Scriptura non utatur convenientibus verbis ad exprimendum opera sex dierum. Sicut enim lux et firmamentum et huiusmodi opera per Dei verbum sunt facta, ita caelum et terra, quia omnia per ipsum facta sunt, ut dicitur Ioan. I. Ergo in creatione caeli et terrae debuit fieri mentio de verbo Dei, sicut in aliis operibus.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31434)\n\nPraeterea, aqua est creata a Deo, quae tamen creata non commemoratur. Insufficienter ergo rerum creatio describitur.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31435)\n\nPraeterea, sicut dicitur Gen. I, *vidit Deus cuncta quae fecerat, et erant valde bona*. In singulis ergo operibus debuit dici, vidit Deus quod esset bonum. Inconvenienter ergo praetermittitur in opere creationis, et in opere secundae diei.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31436)\n\nPraeterea, spiritus Dei est Deus. Deo autem non competit ferri, nec situm habere. Inconvenienter ergo dicitur quod *spiritus Dei ferebatur super aquas*.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31437)\n\nPraeterea, nullus facit quod iam factum est. Inconvenienter ergo, postquam dictum est, *dixit Deus, fiat firmamentum, et factum est ita*, subditur, *et fecit Deus firmamentum*. Et similiter in aliis operibus.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31438)\n\nPraeterea, vespere et mane non sufficienter dividunt diem, cum sint plures partes diei. Ergo inconvenienter dicitur quod *factum est vespere et mane dies secundus, vel tertius*.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31439)\n\nPraeterea, secundo et tertio non convenienter correspondet unum, sed primum. Debuit ergo dici, factum est vespere et mane dies primus, ubi dicitur dies unus.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31440)\n\nRespondeo dicendum ad primum, quod secundum Augustinum, persona filii commemoratur tam in prima rerum creatione, quam in rerum distinctione et ornatu; aliter tamen et aliter. Distinctio enim et ornatus pertinet ad rerum formationem. Sicut autem formatio artificiatorum est per formam artis quae est in mente artificis, quae potest dici intelligibile verbum ipsius; ita formatio totius creaturae est per verbum Dei. Et ideo in opere distinctionis et ornatus fit mentio de verbo. In creatione autem commemoratur filius ut principium, cum dicitur, *in principio creavit Deus*, quia per creationem intelligitur productio informis materiae. Secundum vero alios, qui ponunt primo creata elementa sub propriis formis, oportet aliter dici. Basilius enim dicit quod per hoc quod dicitur, dixit Deus, importatur divinum imperium. Prius autem oportuit produci creaturam quae obediret, quam fieri mentionem de divino imperio.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31441)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, per caelum intelligitur spiritualis natura informis; per terram autem materia informis omnium corporum. Et sic nulla creatura est praetermissa. Secundum Basilium vero, ponuntur caelum et terra tanquam duo extrema, ut ex his intelligantur media; praecipue quia omnium mediorum motus vel est ad caelum ut levium, vel ad terram, ut gravium. Alii vero dicunt quod sub nomine terrae comprehendere solet Scriptura omnia quatuor elementa. Unde in Psalmo CXLVIII, postquam dictum est, laudate dominum de terra, subditur, ignis, grando, nix, glacies.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31442)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in opere creationis ponitur aliquid correspondens ei quod dicitur in opere distinctionis et ornatus, vidit Deus hoc vel illud esse bonum. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod spiritus sanctus amor est. *Duo autem sunt*, ut Augustinus dicit I super Gen. ad Litt., *propter quae Deus amat creaturam suam, scilicet ut sit, et ut permaneat. Ut ergo esset quod permaneret, dicitur quod spiritus Dei ferebatur super aquam* (secundum quod per aquam materia informis intelligitur; sicut amor artificis fertur super materiam aliquam, ut ex ea formet opus), *ut autem maneret quod fecerat, dicitur, vidit Deus quod esset bonum*. In hoc enim significatur quaedam complacentia Dei opificis in re facta, non quod alio modo cognosceret, aut placeret ei creatura iam facta, quam antequam faceret. Et sic in utroque opere creationis et formationis, Trinitas personarum insinuatur. In creatione quidem, persona patris per Deum creantem; persona filii, per principium in quo creavit; spiritus sancti, qui superfertur aquis. In formatione vero, persona patris in Deo dicente; persona vero filii, in verbo quo dicitur; persona spiritus sancti, in complacentia qua vidit Deus esse bonum quod factum erat. In opere vero secundae diei non ponitur, vidit Deus quod esset bonum, quia opus distinctionis aquarum tunc inchoatur, et in tertio die perficitur, unde quod ponitur in tertia die, refertur etiam ad secundam. Vel, quia distinctio quae ponitur secunda die, est de his quae non sunt manifesta populo, ideo huiusmodi approbatione Scriptura non utitur. Vel iterum propter hoc, quod per firmamentum intelligitur aer nubilosus, qui non est de partibus permanentibus in universo, seu de partibus principalibus mundi. Et has tres rationes Rabbi Moyses ponit. Quidam autem assignant rationem mysticam ex parte numeri. Quia binarius ab unitate recedit, unde opus secundae diei non approbatur.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31443)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod Rabbi Moyses per spiritum domini intelligit aerem vel ventum, sicut et Plato intellexit. Et dicit quod dicitur spiritus domini, secundum quod Scriptura consuevit ubique flatum ventorum Deo attribuere. Sed secundum sanctos, per spiritum domini intelligitur spiritus sanctus. Qui dicitur superferri aquae, idest materiae informi secundum Augustinum, *ne facienda opera sua propter indigentiae necessitatem putaretur Deus amare, indigentiae enim amor rebus quas diligit subiicitur. Commode, autem factum est, ut prius insinuaretur aliquid inchoatum, cui superferri diceretur, non enim superfertur loco, sed praeexcellente potentia*, ut Augustinus dicit I super Gen. ad Litt. Secundum Basilium vero, super ferebatur elemento aquae; idest, *fovebat et vivificabat aquarum naturam, ad similitudinem gallinae cubantis, vitalem virtutem his quae foventur iniiciens*. Habet enim aqua praecipue vitalem virtutem, quia plurima animalia generantur in aqua, et omnium animalium semina sunt humida. Vita etiam spiritualis datur per aquam Baptismi, unde dicitur Ioan. III, *nisi quis renatus fuerit ex aqua et spiritu sancto*.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31444)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, per illa tria designatur triplex esse rerum, primo quidem esse rerum in verbo, per hoc quod dixit, fiat; secundo, esse rerum in mente angelica per hoc quod dixit, factum est; tertio, esse rerum in propria natura, per hoc quod dixit, fecit. Et quia in primo die describitur formatio Angelorum, non fuit necesse ut ibi adderetur, fecit. Secundum alios vero, potest dici quod in hoc quod dicit, dixit Deus fiat, importatur imperium Dei de faciendo. Per hoc autem quod dicit, factum est, importatur complementum operis. Oportuit autem ut subderetur quomodo factum fuit, praecipue propter illos qui dixerunt omnia visibilia per Angelos facta. Et ideo ad hoc removendum, subditur quod ipse Deus fecit. Unde in singulis operibus, postquam dicitur, et factum est, aliquis actus Dei subditur; vel fecit, vel distinxit, vel vocavit, vel aliquid huiusmodi.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31445)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, per vespere et mane intelligitur vespertina et matutina cognitio in Angelis, de quibus dictum est supra. Vel, secundum Basilium, totum tempus consuevit denominari a principaliori parte, scilicet a die, secundum quod dixit Iacob, dies peregrinationis meae, nulla mentione facta de nocte. Vespere autem et mane ponuntur ut termini diei; cuius mane est principium, sed vespere finis. Vel quia per vespere designatur principium noctis, per mane principium diei. Congruum autem fuit, ut ubi commemoratur prima distinctio rerum, sola principia temporum designarentur. Praemittitur autem vespere, quia, cum a luce dies incoeperit, prius occurrit terminus lucis, quod est vespere, quam terminus tenebrarum et noctis, quod est mane. Vel, secundum Chrysostomum, ut designetur quod dies naturalis non terminatur in vespere, sed in mane.\n\n[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31446)\n\nAd septimum dicendum quod dicitur unus dies in prima diei institutione, ad designandum quod viginti quatuor horarum spatia pertinent ad unum diem. Unde per hoc quod dicitur unus, praefigitur mensura diei naturalis. Vel propter hoc, ut significaret diem consummari per reditum solis ad unum et idem punctum. Vel quia completo septenario dierum, reditur ad primum diem, qui est unus cum octavo. Et has tres rationes Basilius assignat.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0002","text":"[I q. 74 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31410)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0003","text":"Deinde quaeritur de omnibus septem diebus in communi. Et quaeruntur tria. Primo, de sufficientia horum dierum. Secundo, utrum sint unus dies, vel plures. Tertio, de quibusdam modis loquendi quibus Scriptura utitur, enarrando opera sex dierum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0005","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31411)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sufficienter isti dies enumerentur. Non minus enim distinguitur opus creationis ab operibus distinctionis et ornatus, quam haec duo opera ab invicem. Sed alii dies deputantur distinctioni, et alii ornatui. Ergo etiam alii dies debent assignari creationi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0007","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31412)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0008","text":"Praeterea, aer et ignis sunt nobiliora elementa quam terra et aqua. Sed unus dies deputatur distinctioni aquae, et alius distinctioni terrae. Ergo alii dies debent deputari distinctioni ignis et aeris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0009","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31413)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0010","text":"Praeterea, non minus distant volucres et pisces, quam volucres et terrestria animalia. Homo etiam plus distat ab aliis animalibus, quam alia quaecumque animalia ab invicem. Sed alius dies deputatur productioni piscium maris, et alius productioni animalium terrae. Ergo et alius dies debet deputari productioni avium caeli, et alius productioni hominis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0011","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31414)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0012","text":"Sed contra, videtur quod aliqui dies superflue assignentur. Lumen enim ad luminaria se habet ut accidens ad subiectum. Simul autem producitur subiectum cum proprio accidente. Non ergo alia die debuit produci lux, et alia luminaria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0013","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31415)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0014","text":"Praeterea, dies isti deputantur primae institutioni mundi. Sed in septima die nihil primitus est institutum. Ergo septima dies non debet aliis connumerari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0015","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31416)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ratio distinctionis horum dierum ex praemissis potest esse manifesta. Oportuit enim primo distingui partes mundi; et postmodum singulas partes ornari, per hoc quod quasi suis habitatoribus replentur. Secundum ergo alios sanctos, in creatura corporali tres partes designantur, prima, quae significatur nomine caeli; media, quae significatur nomine aquae; et, infima quae significatur nomine terrae. Unde et secundum Pythagoricos, perfectio in tribus ponitur, principio, medio et fine, ut dicitur in I de caelo. Prima ergo pars distinguitur prima die, et ornatur quarta; media distinguitur secunda die, et ornatur quinta; infima distinguitur tertia die, et ornatur sexta. Augustinus vero convenit quidem cum eis in ultimis tribus diebus, differt autem in tribus primis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0017","text":"Quia secundum eum, in primo die formatur creatura spiritualis, in duobus aliis creatura corporalis; ita quod in secundo corpora superiora, in tertio corpora inferiora. Et sic perfectio divinorum operum respondet perfectioni senarii numeri, qui consurgit ex suis partibus aliquotis simul iunctis; quae quidem partes sunt unum, duo, tria. Una enim dies deputatur formationi creaturae spiritualis, duae formationi creaturae corporalis, et tres ornatui.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0018","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31417)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0019","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, opus creationis pertinet ad productionem materiae informis, et naturae spiritualis informis. Quae quidem duo sunt extra tempus, ut ipse dicit in XII Confess., et ideo creatio utriusque ponitur ante omnem diem. Sed secundum alios sanctos, potest dici quod opus distinctionis et ornatus attenditur secundum aliquam mutationem creaturae, quae tempore mensuratur. Opus autem creationis consistit in sola divina actione in instanti rerum substantiam producentis. Et ideo quodlibet opus distinctionis et ornatus dicitur factum in die, creatio autem dicitur facta in principio, quod sonat aliquid indivisibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0020","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31418)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0021","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ignis et aer, quia non distinguuntur a vulgo, inter partes mundi non sunt expresse nominata a Moyse; sed computantur cum medio, scilicet aqua, maxime quantum ad inferiorem aeris partem; quantum vero ad superiorem, computantur cum caelo, ut Augustinus dicit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0022","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31419)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0023","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod productio animalium recitatur secundum quod sunt in ornatum partium mundi. Et ideo dies productionis animalium distinguuntur vel uniuntur, secundum hanc convenientiam vel differentiam, qua conveniunt vel differunt in ornando aliquam partem mundi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0024","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31420)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0025","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod prima die facta est natura lucis in aliquo subiecto. Sed quarto die facta dicuntur luminaria, non quia eorum substantia sit de novo producta; sed quia sunt aliquo modo formata, quo prius non erant, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0026","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31421)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0027","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod septimae diei, secundum Augustinum, deputatur aliquid post omnia quae sex diebus attribuuntur, scilicet quod Deus a suis operibus in seipso requiescit. Et ideo oportuit post sex dies fieri mentionem de septimo. Secundum vero alios, potest dici quod in septimo die mundus habuit quendam novum statum, ut scilicet ei nihil adderetur de novo. Et ideo post sex dies ponitur septima, deputata cessationi ab opere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0028","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0029","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31422)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0030","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod omnes isti dies sint unus dies. Dicitur enim Gen. II, *istae sunt generationes caeli et terrae, quando creata sunt, in die quo fecit dominus caelum et terram, et omne virgultum agri, antequam oriretur in terra*. Unus ergo est dies in quo fecit caelum et terram et omne virgultum agri. Sed caelum et terram fecit in prima die, vel potius ante omnem diem; virgultum autem agri, tertia die. Ergo unus est primus dies et tertius, et pari ratione, omnes alii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0031","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31423)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0032","text":"Praeterea, Eccli. XVIII, dicitur, *qui vivit in aeternum, creavit omnia simul*. Sed hoc non esset, si dies horum operum essent plures, quia plures dies non sunt simul. Ergo non sunt plures dies, sed unus tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0033","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31424)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0034","text":"Praeterea, die septimo cessavit Deus a novis operibus condendis. Si ergo septima dies est alia ab aliis diebus, sequitur quod illam diem non fecerit. Quod est inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0035","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31425)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0036","text":"Praeterea, totum opus quod uni diei adscribitur, in instanti perfecit, cum in singulis operibus dicatur, dixit, et factum est. Si igitur sequens opus in diem alium reservasset, sequeretur quod in residua parte illius diei cessasset ab opere, quod esset superfluum. Non igitur est alius dies sequentis operis, a die operis praecedentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0037","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31426)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0038","text":"Sed contra est quod Gen. I dicitur, *factum est vespere et mane dies secundus, et dies tertius*, et sic de aliis. Secundum autem et tertium dici non potest, ubi est unum tantum. Ergo non fuit unus dies tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0039","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31427)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0040","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in hac quaestione Augustinus ab aliis expositoribus dissentit. Augustinus enim vult, et super Gen. ad Litt., et XI de Civ. Dei, et ad Orosium, quod omnes qui dicuntur septem dies, sunt unus dies septempliciter rebus praesentatus. Alii vero expositores sentiunt quod fuerunt septem dies diversi, et non unus tantum. Hae autem duae opiniones, si referantur ad expositionem litterae Genesis, magnam diversitatem habent. Nam secundum Augustinum, per diem intelligitur cognitio mentis angelicae; ut sic primus dies sit cognito primi divini operis, secundus dies cognitio secundi operis, et sic de aliis. Et dicitur unumquodque opus esse factum in aliqua die, quia nihil Deus produxit in rerum natura, quod non impresserit menti angelicae. Quae quidem multa simul potest cognoscere, praecipue in verbo, in quo omnis Angelorum cognitio perficitur et terminatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0041","text":"Et sic distinguitur dies secundum naturalem ordinem rerum cognitarum, non secundum successionem cognitionis, aut secundum successionem productionis rerum. Cognitio autem angelica proprie et vere dies nominari potest, cum lux, quae est causa diei, proprie in spiritualibus, secundum Augustinum, inveniatur. Secundum vero alios, per istos dies et successio dierum temporalium ostenditur, et successio productionis rerum. Sed si istae duae opiniones referantur ad modum productionis rerum, non invenitur magna differentia. Et hoc propter duo in quibus, exponendo, diversificatur Augustinus ab aliis, ut ex supra dictis patet. Primo quidem, quia Augustinus per terram et aquam prius creatam, intelligit materiam corporalem totaliter informem, per factionem autem firmamenti, et congregationem aquarum, et apparitionem aridae, intelligit impressionem formarum in materiam corporalem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0042","text":"Alii vero sancti per terram et aquam primo creatas, intelligunt ipsa elementa mundi, sub propriis formis existentia, per sequentia autem opera, aliquam distinctionem in corporibus prius existentibus, ut supra dictum est. Secundo autem differunt quantum ad productionem plantarum et animalium, quae alii ponunt in opere sex dierum esse producta in actu; Augustinus vero potentialiter tantum. In hoc ergo quod Augustinus ponit opera sex dierum esse simul facta, sequitur idem modus productionis rerum. Nam secundum utrosque, in prima rerum productione materia erat sub formis substantialibus elementorum, et secundum utrosque, in prima rerum institutione non fuerunt animalia et plantae in actu. Sed remanet differentia quantum ad quatuor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0043","text":"Quia secundum alios sanctos, post primam productionem creaturae, fuit aliquod tempus in quo non erat lux; item in quo non erat firmamentum formatum; item in quo non erat terra discooperta aquis; et in quo non erant formata caeli luminaria, quod est quartum. Quae non oportet ponere secundum expositionem Augustini. Ut igitur neutri sententiae praeiudicetur, utriusque rationibus respondendum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0044","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31428)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0045","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in die in quo creavit Deus caelum et terram, creavit etiam omne virgultum agri, non in actu, sed antequam oriretur super terram, idest potentialiter. Quod Augustinus adscribit tertiae diei, alii vero primae rerum institutioni.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0046","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31429)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0047","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Deus creavit omnia simul, quantum ad rerum substantiam quodammodo informem. Sed quantum ad formationem quae facta est per distinctionem et ornatum, non simul. Unde signanter utitur verbo creationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0048","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31430)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0049","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in die septimo cessavit Deus a novis operibus condendis, non autem a propagandis quibusdam ex aliis, ad quam propagationem pertinet quod post primum diem alii succedunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0050","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31431)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0051","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod non est ex impotentia Dei, quasi indigentis tempore ad operandum, quod omnia non sunt simul distincta et ornata, sed ut ordo servaretur in rerum institutione. Et ideo oportuit ut diversis statibus mundi diversi dies deservirent. Semper autem per sequens opus novus perfectionis status mundo est additus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0052","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31432)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0053","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, ille ordo dierum referendus est ad naturalem ordinem operum quae diebus attribuuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0054","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0055","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31433)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0056","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Scriptura non utatur convenientibus verbis ad exprimendum opera sex dierum. Sicut enim lux et firmamentum et huiusmodi opera per Dei verbum sunt facta, ita caelum et terra, quia omnia per ipsum facta sunt, ut dicitur Ioan. I. Ergo in creatione caeli et terrae debuit fieri mentio de verbo Dei, sicut in aliis operibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0057","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31434)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0058","text":"Praeterea, aqua est creata a Deo, quae tamen creata non commemoratur. Insufficienter ergo rerum creatio describitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0059","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31435)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0060","text":"Praeterea, sicut dicitur Gen. I, *vidit Deus cuncta quae fecerat, et erant valde bona*. In singulis ergo operibus debuit dici, vidit Deus quod esset bonum. Inconvenienter ergo praetermittitur in opere creationis, et in opere secundae diei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0061","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31436)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0062","text":"Praeterea, spiritus Dei est Deus. Deo autem non competit ferri, nec situm habere. Inconvenienter ergo dicitur quod *spiritus Dei ferebatur super aquas*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0063","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31437)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0064","text":"Praeterea, nullus facit quod iam factum est. Inconvenienter ergo, postquam dictum est, *dixit Deus, fiat firmamentum, et factum est ita*, subditur, *et fecit Deus firmamentum*. Et similiter in aliis operibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0065","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31438)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0066","text":"Praeterea, vespere et mane non sufficienter dividunt diem, cum sint plures partes diei. Ergo inconvenienter dicitur quod *factum est vespere et mane dies secundus, vel tertius*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0067","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31439)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0068","text":"Praeterea, secundo et tertio non convenienter correspondet unum, sed primum. Debuit ergo dici, factum est vespere et mane dies primus, ubi dicitur dies unus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0069","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31440)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0070","text":"Respondeo dicendum ad primum, quod secundum Augustinum, persona filii commemoratur tam in prima rerum creatione, quam in rerum distinctione et ornatu; aliter tamen et aliter. Distinctio enim et ornatus pertinet ad rerum formationem. Sicut autem formatio artificiatorum est per formam artis quae est in mente artificis, quae potest dici intelligibile verbum ipsius; ita formatio totius creaturae est per verbum Dei. Et ideo in opere distinctionis et ornatus fit mentio de verbo. In creatione autem commemoratur filius ut principium, cum dicitur, *in principio creavit Deus*, quia per creationem intelligitur productio informis materiae. Secundum vero alios, qui ponunt primo creata elementa sub propriis formis, oportet aliter dici. Basilius enim dicit quod per hoc quod dicitur, dixit Deus, importatur divinum imperium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0071","text":"Prius autem oportuit produci creaturam quae obediret, quam fieri mentionem de divino imperio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0072","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31441)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0073","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, per caelum intelligitur spiritualis natura informis; per terram autem materia informis omnium corporum. Et sic nulla creatura est praetermissa. Secundum Basilium vero, ponuntur caelum et terra tanquam duo extrema, ut ex his intelligantur media; praecipue quia omnium mediorum motus vel est ad caelum ut levium, vel ad terram, ut gravium. Alii vero dicunt quod sub nomine terrae comprehendere solet Scriptura omnia quatuor elementa. Unde in Psalmo CXLVIII, postquam dictum est, laudate dominum de terra, subditur, ignis, grando, nix, glacies.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0074","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31442)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0075","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in opere creationis ponitur aliquid correspondens ei quod dicitur in opere distinctionis et ornatus, vidit Deus hoc vel illud esse bonum. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod spiritus sanctus amor est. *Duo autem sunt*, ut Augustinus dicit I super Gen. ad Litt., *propter quae Deus amat creaturam suam, scilicet ut sit, et ut permaneat. Ut ergo esset quod permaneret, dicitur quod spiritus Dei ferebatur super aquam* (secundum quod per aquam materia informis intelligitur; sicut amor artificis fertur super materiam aliquam, ut ex ea formet opus), *ut autem maneret quod fecerat, dicitur, vidit Deus quod esset bonum*. In hoc enim significatur quaedam complacentia Dei opificis in re facta, non quod alio modo cognosceret, aut placeret ei creatura iam facta, quam antequam faceret. Et sic in utroque opere creationis et formationis, Trinitas personarum insinuatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0076","text":"In creatione quidem, persona patris per Deum creantem; persona filii, per principium in quo creavit; spiritus sancti, qui superfertur aquis. In formatione vero, persona patris in Deo dicente; persona vero filii, in verbo quo dicitur; persona spiritus sancti, in complacentia qua vidit Deus esse bonum quod factum erat. In opere vero secundae diei non ponitur, vidit Deus quod esset bonum, quia opus distinctionis aquarum tunc inchoatur, et in tertio die perficitur, unde quod ponitur in tertia die, refertur etiam ad secundam. Vel, quia distinctio quae ponitur secunda die, est de his quae non sunt manifesta populo, ideo huiusmodi approbatione Scriptura non utitur. Vel iterum propter hoc, quod per firmamentum intelligitur aer nubilosus, qui non est de partibus permanentibus in universo, seu de partibus principalibus mundi. Et has tres rationes Rabbi Moyses ponit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0077","text":"Quidam autem assignant rationem mysticam ex parte numeri. Quia binarius ab unitate recedit, unde opus secundae diei non approbatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0078","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31443)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0079","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod Rabbi Moyses per spiritum domini intelligit aerem vel ventum, sicut et Plato intellexit. Et dicit quod dicitur spiritus domini, secundum quod Scriptura consuevit ubique flatum ventorum Deo attribuere. Sed secundum sanctos, per spiritum domini intelligitur spiritus sanctus. Qui dicitur superferri aquae, idest materiae informi secundum Augustinum, *ne facienda opera sua propter indigentiae necessitatem putaretur Deus amare, indigentiae enim amor rebus quas diligit subiicitur. Commode, autem factum est, ut prius insinuaretur aliquid inchoatum, cui superferri diceretur, non enim superfertur loco, sed praeexcellente potentia*, ut Augustinus dicit I super Gen. ad Litt. Secundum Basilium vero, super ferebatur elemento aquae; idest, *fovebat et vivificabat aquarum naturam, ad similitudinem gallinae cubantis, vitalem virtutem his quae foventur iniiciens*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0080","text":"Habet enim aqua praecipue vitalem virtutem, quia plurima animalia generantur in aqua, et omnium animalium semina sunt humida. Vita etiam spiritualis datur per aquam Baptismi, unde dicitur Ioan. III, *nisi quis renatus fuerit ex aqua et spiritu sancto*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0081","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31444)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0082","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, per illa tria designatur triplex esse rerum, primo quidem esse rerum in verbo, per hoc quod dixit, fiat; secundo, esse rerum in mente angelica per hoc quod dixit, factum est; tertio, esse rerum in propria natura, per hoc quod dixit, fecit. Et quia in primo die describitur formatio Angelorum, non fuit necesse ut ibi adderetur, fecit. Secundum alios vero, potest dici quod in hoc quod dicit, dixit Deus fiat, importatur imperium Dei de faciendo. Per hoc autem quod dicit, factum est, importatur complementum operis. Oportuit autem ut subderetur quomodo factum fuit, praecipue propter illos qui dixerunt omnia visibilia per Angelos facta. Et ideo ad hoc removendum, subditur quod ipse Deus fecit. Unde in singulis operibus, postquam dicitur, et factum est, aliquis actus Dei subditur; vel fecit, vel distinxit, vel vocavit, vel aliquid huiusmodi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0083","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31445)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0084","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, per vespere et mane intelligitur vespertina et matutina cognitio in Angelis, de quibus dictum est supra. Vel, secundum Basilium, totum tempus consuevit denominari a principaliori parte, scilicet a die, secundum quod dixit Iacob, dies peregrinationis meae, nulla mentione facta de nocte. Vespere autem et mane ponuntur ut termini diei; cuius mane est principium, sed vespere finis. Vel quia per vespere designatur principium noctis, per mane principium diei. Congruum autem fuit, ut ubi commemoratur prima distinctio rerum, sola principia temporum designarentur. Praemittitur autem vespere, quia, cum a luce dies incoeperit, prius occurrit terminus lucis, quod est vespere, quam terminus tenebrarum et noctis, quod est mane. Vel, secundum Chrysostomum, ut designetur quod dies naturalis non terminatur in vespere, sed in mane.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0085","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31446)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0086","text":"Ad septimum dicendum quod dicitur unus dies in prima diei institutione, ad designandum quod viginti quatuor horarum spatia pertinent ad unum diem. Unde per hoc quod dicitur unus, praefigitur mensura diei naturalis. Vel propter hoc, ut significaret diem consummari per reditum solis ad unum et idem punctum. Vel quia completo septenario dierum, reditur ad primum diem, qui est unus cum octavo. Et has tres rationes Basilius assignat.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9fb84ed74c46758510503eca35aaea2d575022572b7e9a8247b75b8cb9dedeaa","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T145955208147Z_ff4033e4","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 74","source_sha256":"9fb84ed74c46758510503eca35aaea2d575022572b7e9a8247b75b8cb9dedeaa","sha256":"d20d525f5e295b34b25b80c02175f0e17001f91ac8bf948cc7858ab798cb3924","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T14:59:55.208167+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0002","text":"[I q. 74 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31410)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0003","text":"Next, there is inquiry concerning all seven days in common. And three questions are asked. First, concerning the sufficiency of these days. Second, whether they are one day, or several. Third, concerning certain modes of speaking that Scripture employs in recounting the works of the six days.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0005","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31411)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that these days are not enumerated sufficiently. For the work of creation is distinguished from the works of distinction and adornment no less than these two works are distinguished from one another. But some days are assigned to distinction, and others to adornment. Therefore, other days should also be assigned to creation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0007","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31412)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0008","text":"Further, air and fire are nobler elements than earth and water. But one day is assigned to the distinction of water, and another to the distinction of earth. Therefore, other days should be assigned to the distinction of fire and air.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0009","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31413)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0010","text":"Further, birds and fish differ no less than birds and terrestrial animals. Man also differs more from the other animals than any other animals differ from one another. But one day is assigned to the production of the fish of the sea, and another to the production of the animals of the earth. Therefore, another day should likewise be assigned to the production of the birds of heaven, and another to the production of man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0011","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31414)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0012","text":"On the contrary, it seems that some days are assigned superfluously. For light is related to the luminaries as an accident to its subject. But a subject is produced at the same time as its proper accident. Therefore, light ought not to have been produced on one day, and the luminaries on another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0013","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31415)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0014","text":"Further, these days are assigned to the first institution of the world. But on the seventh day nothing was instituted for the first time. Therefore, the seventh day ought not to be numbered with the others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0015","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31416)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0016","text":"I answer that the reason for the distinction of these days can be evident from what has been said above. For the parts of the world first had to be distinguished, and afterward each part adorned, by being filled, as it were, with its inhabitants. Therefore, according to the other saints, three parts are designated in corporeal creation: the first, which is signified by the name of heaven; the middle, which is signified by the name of water; and the lowest, which is signified by the name of earth. Hence also, according to the Pythagoreans, perfection is placed in three: beginning, middle, and end, as is said in I *On the Heavens*. Therefore, the first part is distinguished on the first day and adorned on the fourth; the middle is distinguished on the second day and adorned on the fifth; the lowest is distinguished on the third day and adorned on the sixth. Augustine, however, agrees with them indeed concerning the last three days, but differs concerning the first three.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0017","text":"For according to him, spiritual creation is formed on the first day, corporeal creation on the other two: so that on the second day the higher bodies, and on the third the lower bodies, are formed. And thus the perfection of the divine works corresponds to the perfection of the number six, which arises from its aliquot parts joined together at once; and these parts are one, two, and three. For one day is assigned to the formation of spiritual creation, two to the formation of corporeal creation, and three to adornment.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0018","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31417)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0019","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, according to Augustine, the work of creation pertains to the production of formless matter and formless spiritual nature. These two are outside time, as he himself says in XII *Confessions*, and therefore the creation of each is set down before every day. But according to the other saints, it can be said that the work of distinction and adornment is considered according to some change in the creature, which is measured by time. The work of creation, however, consists in the divine action alone, producing the substance of things in an instant. And therefore every work of distinction and adornment is said to have been made in a day, whereas creation is said to have been made in the beginning, which signifies something indivisible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0020","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31418)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0021","text":"To the second, it must be said that fire and air, because they are not distinguished by the common people, are not expressly named by Moses among the parts of the world; but they are reckoned with the middle part, namely water—especially as regards the lower part of the air; whereas as regards its upper part, they are reckoned with heaven, as Augustine says.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0022","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31419)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0023","text":"To the third, it must be said that the production of animals is recounted insofar as they serve for the adornment of the parts of the world. And therefore the days of the production of animals are distinguished or united according to this fittingness or difference by which they agree or differ in adorning some part of the world.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0024","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31420)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0025","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that on the first day the nature of light was made in some subject. But on the fourth day the luminaries are said to have been made, not because their substance was produced anew, but because they were in some way formed in which they had not been before, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0026","text":"[I q. 74 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31421)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0027","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that, according to Augustine, something after all that is attributed to the six days is assigned to the seventh day, namely, that God rests from His works in Himself. And therefore mention of the seventh had to be made after the six days. But according to others, it can be said that on the seventh day the world had a certain new state, namely, that nothing new was added to it. And therefore, after the six days, the seventh is set down, assigned to cessation from work.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0028","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0029","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31422)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0030","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that all these days are one day. For it is said in Gen. II, *these are the generations of heaven and earth, when they were created, in the day in which the Lord made heaven and earth, and every shrub of the field, before it sprang up on the earth*. Therefore, there is one day on which He made heaven and earth and every shrub of the field. But He made heaven and earth on the first day, or rather before every day; whereas the shrub of the field, on the third day. Therefore the first and third day are one, and by the same reasoning all the others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0031","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31423)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0032","text":"Further, it is said in Ecclus. XVIII: *He who lives forever created all things at once*. But this would not be so if the days of these works were several, because several days are not simultaneous. Therefore, the days are not several, but only one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0033","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31424)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0034","text":"Further, on the seventh day God ceased from creating new works. Therefore, if the seventh day is different from the other days, it follows that He did not make that day. This is unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0035","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31425)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0036","text":"Further, He completed in an instant the whole work that is ascribed to one day, since in the case of each work it is said, He said, and it was made. Therefore, if He had reserved the following work for another day, it would follow that during the remaining part of that day He ceased from working, which would be superfluous. Therefore, the day of the following work is not different from the day of the preceding work.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0037","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31426)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0038","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Gen. I, *there was evening and morning, the second day, and the third day*, and so concerning the others. But second and third cannot be said where there is only one. Therefore, there was not only one day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0039","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31427)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0040","text":"I answer that it must be said that in this question Augustine differs from the other expositors. For Augustine maintains, both in *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, and in XI of *The City of God*, and in his work addressed to Orosius, that all those called the seven days are one day represented sevenfold in things. But the other expositors hold that there were seven different days, and not merely one. Now these two opinions, if referred to the exposition of the letter of Genesis, have a great difference. For according to Augustine, by day is understood the cognition of the angelic mind; so that the first day is the cognition of the first divine work, the second day the cognition of the second work, and so concerning the others. And each work is said to have been made on some day because God produced nothing in the nature of things without impressing it upon the angelic mind. This mind can indeed know many things simultaneously, especially in the Word, in whom all the cognition of the angels is perfected and terminated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0041","text":"And thus the day is distinguished according to the natural order of the things known, not according to a succession of cognition or according to a succession in the production of things. But angelic cognition can properly and truly be called day, since light, which is the cause of day, is properly found in spiritual things, according to Augustine. According to others, however, by these days both the succession of temporal days and the succession in the production of things are shown. But if these two opinions are referred to the mode of producing things, no great difference is found. And this is because of two matters in which Augustine, in expounding, differs from the others, as is clear from what was said above. First, because by the earth and water created beforehand Augustine understands corporeal matter wholly without form; but by the making of the firmament, the gathering together of the waters, and the appearance of dry land, he understands the impressing of forms upon corporeal matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0042","text":"But the other holy men understand by the earth and water first created the very elements of the world, existing under their proper forms; and by the subsequent works, a certain distinction among bodies previously existing, as was said above. Secondly, they differ concerning the production of plants and animals, which others hold to have been produced actually in the work of the six days; Augustine, however, only potentially. Therefore, in Augustine's holding that the works of the six days were made simultaneously, the same mode of producing things follows. For according to both, in the first production of things matter was under the substantial forms of the elements; and according to both, in the first institution of things animals and plants did not exist actually. But a difference remains with respect to four matters.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0043","text":"For according to the other holy men, after the first production of the creature, there was some time in which there was no light; likewise, in which there was no formed firmament; likewise, in which the earth was not uncovered by the waters; and in which the luminaries of heaven, which is the fourth, were not formed. These need not be posited according to Augustine's exposition. Therefore, so that neither opinion may be prejudiced, the arguments of both must be answered.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0044","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31428)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0045","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that on the day on which God created heaven and earth, He also created every shrub of the field, not actually, but before it sprang up upon the earth, that is, potentially. Augustine attributes this to the third day, but others to the first institution of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0046","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31429)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0047","text":"To the second, it must be said that God created all things simultaneously with respect to the substance of things, in a certain manner without form. But with respect to the formation that took place through distinction and adornment, not simultaneously. Hence he significantly employs the word creation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0048","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31430)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0049","text":"To the third, it must be said that on the seventh day God ceased from creating new works, but not from propagating certain things from others; and to this propagation pertains the fact that, after the first day, others succeed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0050","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31431)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0051","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that it is not from God's impotence, as though He needed time for working, that all things were not simultaneously distinguished and adorned, but in order that order might be preserved in the institution of things. And therefore it was necessary that different days should serve the different states of the world. Yet by each succeeding work a new state of perfection was added to the world.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0052","text":"[I q. 74 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31432)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0053","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that, according to Augustine, that order of days must be referred to the natural order of the works attributed to the days.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0054","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0055","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31433)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0056","text":"We proceed to the third as follows. It seems that Scripture does not use fitting words to express the works of the six days. For just as light and the firmament and works of this kind were made through God's word, so too were heaven and earth, since all things were made through Him, as is said in John 1. Therefore, in the creation of heaven and earth mention ought to have been made of the word of God, as in the other works.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0057","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31434)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0058","text":"Further, water was created by God, yet its creation is not commemorated. Therefore the creation of things is described insufficiently.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0059","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31435)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0060","text":"Further, as is said in Gen. 1, *God saw all the things that He had made, and they were very good*. Therefore, in each individual work it ought to have been said, God saw that it was good. Therefore it is unfittingly omitted in the work of creation and in the work of the second day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0061","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31436)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0062","text":"Further, the Spirit of God is God. But to God it does not belong to be borne along, nor to have a position. Therefore it is unfittingly said that *the Spirit of God was borne over the waters*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0063","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31437)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0064","text":"Further, no one makes what has already been made. Therefore, after it has been said, *God said, Let there be a firmament, and it was so*, it is unfittingly added, *and God made the firmament*. And similarly in the other works.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0065","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31438)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0066","text":"Further, evening and morning do not sufficiently divide the day, since there are more parts of the day. Therefore it is unfittingly said that *there was evening and morning, the second day, or the third*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0067","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 arg. 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31439)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0068","text":"Further, “one” does not correspond fittingly to “second” and “third,” but rather “first” does. Therefore it ought to have been said, there was evening and morning, the first day, where “one day” is said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0069","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31440)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0070","text":"I answer that, as to the first, according to Augustine, the person of the Son is mentioned both in the first creation of things and in the distinction and adornment of things, though differently in each case. For distinction and adornment pertain to the formation of things. Now, just as the formation of artifacts is through the form of the art that is in the mind of the artisan, which can be called his intelligible word, so the formation of the whole creature is through the Word of God. And therefore in the work of distinction and adornment mention is made of the Word. But in creation the Son is commemorated as a principle, when it is said, *In the beginning God created*, because by creation is understood the production of formless matter. But according to others, who hold that the elements were first created under their proper forms, it must be said otherwise. For Basil says that by the words, God said, the divine command is signified.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0071","text":"But it was first necessary for a creature that would obey to be produced, before mention was made of the divine command.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0072","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31441)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0073","text":"To the second, it must be said that, according to Augustine, by heaven is understood the formless spiritual nature; but by earth, the formless matter of all bodies. And thus no creature is omitted. According to Basil, however, heaven and earth are set down as the two extremes, so that the intermediate things may be understood from these; especially because the motion of all intermediate things is either toward heaven, as of light things, or toward earth, as of heavy things. Others, however, say that Scripture is accustomed to comprehend all four elements under the name of earth. Hence in Psalm 148, after it has been said, praise the Lord from the earth, there follows: fire, hail, snow, ice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0074","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31442)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0075","text":"To the third, it must be said that in the work of creation something is set down corresponding to what is said in the work of distinction and adornment: God saw this or that to be good. For the clarification of this, it must be considered that the Holy Spirit is love. *Now there are two things,* as Augustine says in Book I, *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis, for which God loves His creature: namely, that it may be, and that it may remain. Therefore, that there might be that which would remain, it is said that the Spirit of God was borne over the water* (inasmuch as formless matter is understood by water; just as an artisan's love is borne over some matter in order to form a work from it); *but that what He had made might remain, it is said: God saw that it was good.* For in this a certain complacency of God the maker in the thing made is signified—not that He knew in one way, or that the creature already made pleased Him in one way, and before He made it in another. And thus, in both the work of creation and that of formation, the Trinity of Persons is intimated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0076","text":"Indeed, in creation, the Person of the Father is indicated by God creating; the Person of the Son, by the principle in which He created; and that of the Holy Spirit, who is borne over the waters. But in formation, the Person of the Father is in God speaking; the Person of the Son, in the word by which He speaks; and the Person of the Holy Spirit, in the complacency by which God saw that what had been made was good. But in the work of the second day, “God saw that it was good” is not set down, because the work of the distinction of the waters is then begun and is completed on the third day; hence what is set down on the third day is also referred to the second. Or, because the distinction set down on the second day concerns things that are not manifest to the people, Scripture therefore does not use this sort of approval. Or again, on account of this: that by the firmament is understood the cloudy air, which is not among the permanent parts of the universe, or among the principal parts of the world. And Rabbi Moses gives these three reasons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0077","text":"Some, however, assign a mystical reason on the part of number. For the number two recedes from unity; hence the work of the second day is not approved.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0078","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31443)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0079","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that Rabbi Moses understands by the spirit of the Lord the air or wind, as Plato also understood it. And he says that it is called the spirit of the Lord because Scripture is accustomed everywhere to attribute the blowing of winds to God. But according to the saints, by the spirit of the Lord is understood the Holy Spirit. He is said to be borne over the water, that is, over formless matter according to Augustine, *lest God be thought to love the works He was to make because of a necessity arising from need; for the love of need is subject to the things it loves. Yet it was fittingly done that something begun should first be intimated, over which He might be said to be borne; for He is not borne over it in place, but by pre-eminent power,* as Augustine says in Book I, *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis.* According to Basil, however, He was borne over the element of water; that is, *He cherished and enlivened the nature of the waters, after the likeness of a brooding hen, infusing vital power into those things that are cherished.*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0080","text":"For water possesses vital power especially, because very many animals are generated in water, and the seeds of all animals are moist. Spiritual life too is given through the water of Baptism; hence it is said in John 3, *unless someone has been born again of water and the Holy Spirit.*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0081","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31444)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0082","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that, according to Augustine, by those three things the threefold being of things is designated: first, indeed, the being of things in the Word, by that which He said, let it be; second, the being of things in the angelic mind, by that which He said, it was made; third, the being of things in their own nature, by that which He said, He made. And because on the first day the formation of the angels is described, it was not necessary that “He made” be added there. According to others, however, it can be said that in His saying, God said, let it be, God's command concerning making is implied. But by His saying, it was made, the completion of the work is implied. It was necessary, however, that it be added how it was made, especially on account of those who said that all visible things were made by angels. And therefore, to remove this, it is added that God Himself made it. Hence in each of the works, after it is said, and it was made, some act of God follows: either He made, or He distinguished, or He called, or something of this sort.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0083","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31445)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0084","text":"To the sixth, it must be said that, according to Augustine, by evening and morning are understood the evening and morning knowledge in the angels, concerning whom it was said above. Or, according to Basil, the whole time is customarily named from the more principal part, namely, from the day, as Jacob said: the days of my pilgrimage, with no mention made of night. But evening and morning are set down as the boundaries of the day: morning is its beginning, but evening its end. Or because by evening the beginning of night is designated, and by morning the beginning of day. It was fitting, however, that where the first distinction of things is commemorated, only the beginnings of times should be designated. Evening is put first because, since the day began from light, the boundary of light, which is evening, occurs before the boundary of darkness and night, which is morning. Or, according to Chrysostom, so that it may be designated that the natural day is not terminated in the evening, but in the morning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0085","text":"[I q. 74 a. 3 ad 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1065.html#31446)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0086","text":"To the seventh, it must be said that “one day” is said in the institution of the first day, to designate that the space of twenty-four hours belongs to one day. Hence, by the fact that “one” is said, the measure of the natural day is prefigured. Or on this account: that it might signify the day to be completed by the return of the sun to one and the same point. Or because, when the sevenfold number of days has been completed, there is a return to the first day, which is one with the eighth. And Basil gives these three reasons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_66:P0086"]}],"source_digest":"15294bd1683a5509f9233cbbc6c298f23ea18400d550563feb735a12ca5dcbad","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 74"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 74","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67","title":"First Part · Question 75","order":76,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_67","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 75 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31447)\n\nPost considerationem creaturae spiritualis et corporalis, considerandum est de homine, qui ex spirituali et corporali substantia componitur. Et primo, de natura ipsius hominis; secundo, de eius productione. Naturam autem hominis considerare pertinet ad theologum ex parte animae, non autem ex parte corporis, nisi secundum habitudinem quam habet corpus ad animam. Et ideo prima consideratio circa animam versabitur. Et quia, secundum Dionysium, XI cap. Angel. Hier., tria inveniuntur in substantiis spiritualibus, scilicet essentia, virtus et operatio; primo considerabimus ea quae pertinent ad essentiam animae; secundo, ea quae pertinent ad virtutem sive potentias eius; tertio, ea quae pertinent ad operationem eius. Circa primum duplex occurrit consideratio, quarum prima est de ipsa anima secundum se; secunda, de unione eius ad corpus. Circa primum quaeruntur septem. Primo, utrum anima sit corpus. Secundo, utrum anima humana sit aliquid subsistens. Tertio, utrum animae brutorum sint subsistentes. Quarto, utrum anima sit homo; vel magis homo sit aliquid compositum ex anima et corpore. Quinto, utrum sit composita ex materia et forma. Sexto, utrum anima humana sit incorruptibilis. Septimo, utrum anima sit eiusdem speciei cum Angelo.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 75 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31448)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima sit corpus. Anima enim est motor corporis. Non autem est movens non motum. Tum quia videtur quod nihil possit movere nisi moveatur, quia nihil dat alteri quod non habet, sicut quod non est calidum non calefacit. Tum quia, si aliquid est movens non motum, causat motum sempiternum et eodem modo se habentem, ut probatur in VIII Physic., quod non apparet in motu animalis, qui est ab anima. Ergo anima est movens motum. Sed omne movens motum est corpus. Ergo anima est corpus.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31449)\n\nPraeterea, omnis cognitio fit per aliquam similitudinem. Non potest autem esse similitudo corporis ad rem incorpoream. Si igitur anima non esset corpus, non posset cognoscere res corporeas.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31450)\n\nPraeterea, moventis ad motum oportet esse aliquem contactum. Contactus autem non est nisi corporum. Cum igitur anima moveat corpus, videtur quod anima sit corpus.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31451)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, VI de Trin., quod *anima simplex dicitur respectu corporis, quia mole non diffunditur per spatium loci*.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31452)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ad inquirendum de natura animae, oportet praesupponere quod anima dicitur esse primum principium vitae in his quae apud nos vivunt animata enim viventia dicimus, res vero inanimatas vita carentes. Vita autem maxime manifestatur duplici opere, scilicet cognitionis et motus. Horum autem principium antiqui philosophi, imaginationem transcendere non valentes, aliquod corpus ponebant; sola corpora res esse dicentes, et quod non est corpus, nihil esse. Et secundum hoc, animam aliquod corpus esse dicebant. Huius autem opinionis falsitas licet multipliciter ostendi possit tamen uno utemur, quo et communius et certius patet animam corpus non esse. Manifestum est enim quod non quodcumque vitalis operationis principium est anima, sic enim oculus esset anima, cum sit quoddam principium visionis; et idem esset dicendum de aliis animae instrumentis. Sed primum principium vitae dicimus esse animam. Quamvis autem aliquod corpus possit esse quoddam principium vitae, sicut cor est principium vitae in animali; tamen non potest esse primum principium vitae aliquod corpus. Manifestum est enim quod esse principium vitae, vel vivens, non convenit corpori ex hoc quod est corpus, alioquin omne corpus esset vivens, aut principium vitae. Convenit igitur alicui corpori quod sit vivens, vel etiam principium vitae, per hoc quod est tale corpus. Quod autem est actu tale, habet hoc ab aliquo principio quod dicitur actus eius. Anima igitur, quae est primum principium vitae, non est corpus, sed corporis actus, sicut calor, qui est principium calefactionis, non est corpus, sed quidam corporis actus.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31453)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, cum omne quod movetur ab alio moveatur, quod non potest in infinitum procedere, necesse est dicere quod non omne movens movetur. Cum enim moveri sit exire de potentia in actum, movens dat id quod habet mobili, inquantum facit ipsum esse in actu. Sed sicut ostenditur in VIII Physic., est quoddam movens penitus immobile, quod nec per se nec per accidens movetur, et tale movens potest movere motum semper uniformem. Est autem aliud movens, quod non movetur per se, sed movetur per accidens, et propter hoc non movet motum semper uniformem. Et tale movens est anima. Est autem aliud movens, quod per se movetur, scilicet corpus. Et quia antiqui naturales nihil esse credebant nisi corpora, posuerunt quod omne movens movetur, et quod anima per se movetur, et est corpus.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31454)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod non est necessarium quod similitudo rei cognitae sit actu in natura cognoscentis, sed si aliquid sit quod prius est cognoscens in potentia et postea in actu, oportet quod similitudo cogniti non sit actu in natura cognoscentis, sed in potentia tantum; sicut color non est actu in pupilla, sed in potentia tantum. Unde non oportet quod in natura animae sit similitudo rerum corporearum in actu; sed quod sit in potentia ad huiusmodi similitudines. Sed quia antiqui naturales nesciebant distinguere inter actum et potentiam, ponebant animam esse corpus, ad hoc quod cognosceret corpus; et ad hoc quod cognosceret omnia corpora, quod esset composita ex principiis omnium corporum.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31455)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod est duplex contactus, quantitatis et virtutis. Primo modo, corpus non tangitur nisi a corpore. Secundo modo, corpus potest tangi a re incorporea quae movet corpus.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 75 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31456)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima humana non sit aliquid subsistens. Quod enim est subsistens, dicitur hoc aliquid. Anima autem non est hoc aliquid, sed compositum ex anima et corpore. Ergo anima non est aliquid subsistens.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31457)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod est subsistens, potest dici operari. Sed anima non dicitur operari, quia, ut dicitur in I de anima, *dicere animam sentire aut intelligere, simile est ac si dicat eam aliquis texere vel aedificare*. Ergo anima non est aliquid subsistens.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31458)\n\nPraeterea, si anima esset aliquid subsistens, esset aliqua eius operatio sine corpore. Sed nulla est eius operatio sine corpore, nec etiam intelligere, quia non contingit intelligere sine phantasmate, phantasma autem non est sine corpore. Ergo anima humana non est aliquid subsistens.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31459)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, X de Trin. *Quisquis videt mentis naturam et esse substantiam, et non esse corpoream, videt eos qui opinantur eam esse corpoream, ob hoc errare, quod adiungunt ei ea sine quibus nullam possunt cogitare naturam, scilicet corporum phantasias*. Natura ergo mentis humanae non solum est incorporea, sed etiam substantia, scilicet aliquid subsistens.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31460)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere id quod est principium intellectualis operationis, quod dicimus animam hominis, esse quoddam principium incorporeum et subsistens. Manifestum est enim quod homo per intellectum cognoscere potest naturas omnium corporum. Quod autem potest cognoscere aliqua, oportet ut nihil eorum habeat in sua natura, quia illud quod inesset ei naturaliter impediret cognitionem aliorum; sicut videmus quod lingua infirmi quae infecta est cholerico et amaro humore, non potest percipere aliquid dulce, sed omnia videntur ei amara. Si igitur principium intellectuale haberet in se naturam alicuius corporis, non posset omnia corpora cognoscere. Omne autem corpus habet aliquam naturam determinatam. Impossibile est igitur quod principium intellectuale sit corpus. Et similiter impossibile est quod intelligat per organum corporeum, quia etiam natura determinata illius organi corporei prohiberet cognitionem omnium corporum; sicut si aliquis determinatus color sit non solum in pupilla, sed etiam in vase vitreo, liquor infusus eiusdem coloris videtur. Ipsum igitur intellectuale principium, quod dicitur mens vel intellectus, habet operationem per se, cui non communicat corpus. Nihil autem potest per se operari, nisi quod per se subsistit. Non enim est operari nisi entis in actu, unde eo modo aliquid operatur, quo est. Propter quod non dicimus quod calor calefacit, sed calidum. Relinquitur igitur animam humanam, quae dicitur intellectus vel mens, esse aliquid incorporeum et subsistens.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31461)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod hoc aliquid potest accipi dupliciter, uno modo, pro quocumque subsistente, alio modo, pro subsistente completo in natura alicuius speciei. Primo modo, excludit inhaerentiam accidentis et formae materialis, secundo modo, excludit etiam imperfectionem partis. Unde manus posset dici hoc aliquid primo modo, sed non secundo modo. Sic igitur, cum anima humana sit pars speciei humanae, potest dici hoc aliquid primo modo, quasi subsistens, sed non secundo modo, sic enim compositum ex anima et corpore dicitur hoc aliquid.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31462)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod verba illa Aristoteles dicit non secundum propriam sententiam, sed secundum opinionem illorum qui dicebant quod intelligere est moveri; ut patet ex iis quae ibi praemittit. Vel dicendum quod per se agere convenit per se existenti. Sed per se existens quandoque potest dici aliquid si non sit inhaerens ut accidens vel ut forma materialis, etiam si sit pars. Sed proprie et per se subsistens dicitur quod neque est praedicto modo inhaerens, neque est pars. Secundum quem modum oculus aut manus non posset dici per se subsistens; et per consequens nec per se operans. Unde et operationes partium attribuuntur toti per partes. Dicimus enim quod homo videt per oculum, et palpat per manum, aliter quam calidum calefacit per calorem, quia calor nullo modo calefacit, proprie loquendo. Potest igitur dici quod anima intelligit, sicut oculus videt, sed magis proprie dicitur quod homo intelligat per animam.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31463)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod corpus requiritur ad actionem intellectus, non sicut organum quo talis actio exerceatur, sed ratione obiecti, phantasma enim comparatur ad intellectum sicut color ad visum. Sic autem indigere corpore non removet intellectum esse subsistentem, alioquin animal non esset aliquid subsistens, cum indigeat exterioribus sensibilibus ad sentiendum.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 75 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31464)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod animae brutorum animalium sint subsistentes. Homo enim convenit in genere cum aliis animalibus. Sed anima hominis est aliquid subsistens, ut ostensum est. Ergo et animae aliorum animalium sunt subsistentes.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31465)\n\nPraeterea, similiter se habet sensitivum ad sensibilia, sicut intellectivum et intelligibilia. Sed intellectus intelligit intelligibilia sine corpore. Ergo et sensus apprehendit sensibilia sine corpore. Animae autem brutorum animalium sunt sensitivae. Ergo sunt subsistentes, pari ratione qua et anima hominis, quae est intellectiva.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31466)\n\nPraeterea, brutorum animalium anima movet corpus. Corpus autem non movet, sed movetur. Anima ergo bruti animalis habet aliquam operationem sine corpore.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31467)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in libro de Eccl. Dogmat., *solum hominem credimus habere animam substantivam; animalium vero animae non sunt substantivae*.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31468)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod antiqui philosophi nullam distinctionem ponebant inter sensum et intellectum, et utrumque corporeo principio attribuebant, ut dictum est. Plato autem distinxit inter intellectum et sensum; utrumque tamen attribuit principio incorporeo, ponens quod, sicut intelligere, ita et sentire convenit animae secundum seipsam. Et ex hoc sequebatur quod etiam animae brutorum animalium sint subsistentes. Sed Aristoteles posuit quod solum intelligere, inter opera animae, sine organo corporeo exercetur. Sentire vero, et consequentes operationes animae sensitivae, manifeste accidunt cum aliqua corporis immutatione; sicut in videndo immutatur pupilla per speciem coloris; et idem apparet in aliis. Et sic manifestum est quod anima sensitiva non habet aliquam operationem propriam per seipsam, sed omnis operatio sensitivae animae est coniuncti. Ex quo relinquitur quod, cum animae brutorum animalium per se non operentur, non sint subsistentes, similiter enim unumquodque habet esse et operationem.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31469)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod homo, etsi conveniat in genere cum aliis animalibus, specie tamen differt, differentia autem speciei attenditur secundum differentiam formae. Nec oportet quod omnis differentia formae faciat generis diversitatem.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31470)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod sensitivum quodammodo se habet ad sensibilia sicut intellectivum ad intelligibilia, inquantum scilicet utrumque est in potentia ad sua obiecta. Sed quodammodo dissimiliter se habent, inquantum sensitivum patitur a sensibili cum corporis immutatione, unde excellentia sensibilium corrumpit sensum. Quod in intellectu non contingit, nam intellectus intelligens maxima intelligibilium, magis potest postmodum intelligere minora. Si vero in intelligendo fatigetur corpus, hoc est per accidens, in quantum intellectus indiget operatione virium sensitivarum, per quas ei phantasmata praeparantur.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31471)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod vis motiva est duplex. Una quae imperat motum, scilicet appetitiva. Et huius operatio in anima sensitiva non est sine corpore; sed ira et gaudium et omnes huiusmodi passiones sunt cum aliqua corporis immutatione. Alia vis motiva est exequens motum, per quam membra redduntur habilia ad obediendum appetitui, cuius actus non est movere, sed moveri. Unde patet quod movere non est actus animae sensitivae sine corpore.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 75 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31472)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima sit homo. Dicitur enim II ad Cor. IV, *licet is qui foris est noster homo corrumpatur, tamen is qui intus est, renovatur de die in diem*. Sed id quod est intus in homine, est anima. Ergo anima est homo interior.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31473)\n\nPraeterea, anima humana est substantia quaedam. Non autem est substantia universalis. Ergo est substantia particularis. Ergo est hypostasis vel persona. Sed non nisi humana. Ergo anima est homo, nam persona humana est homo.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31474)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus, XIX de Civ. Dei, commendat Varronem, *qui hominem nec animam solam, nec solum corpus, sed animam simul et corpus esse arbitrabatur*.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31475)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod animam esse hominem dupliciter potest intelligi. Uno modo, quod homo sit anima, sed hic homo non sit anima, sed compositum ex anima et corpore, puta Socrates. Quod ideo dico, quia quidam posuerunt solam formam esse de ratione speciei, materiam vero esse partem individui, et non speciei. Quod quidem non potest esse verum. Nam ad naturam speciei pertinet id quod significat definitio. Definitio autem in rebus naturalibus non significat formam tantum, sed formam et materiam. Unde materia est pars speciei in rebus naturalibus, non quidem materia signata, quae est principium individuationis; sed materia communis. Sicut enim de ratione huius hominis est quod sit ex hac anima et his carnibus et his ossibus; ita de ratione hominis est quod sit ex anima et carnibus et ossibus. Oportet enim de substantia speciei esse quidquid est communiter de substantia omnium individuorum sub specie contentorum. Alio vero modo potest intelligi sic, quod etiam haec anima sit hic homo. Et hoc quidem sustineri posset, si poneretur quod animae sensitivae operatio esset eius propria sine corpore, quia omnes operationes quae attribuuntur homini, convenirent soli animae; illud autem est unaquaeque res, quod operatur operationes illius rei. Unde illud est homo, quod operatur operationes hominis. Ostensum est autem quod sentire non est operatio animae tantum. Cum igitur sentire sit quaedam operatio hominis, licet non propria, manifestum est quod homo non est anima tantum, sed est aliquid compositum ex anima et corpore. Plato vero, ponens sentire esse proprium animae, ponere potuit quod homo esset anima utens corpore.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31476)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, secundum philosophum in IX Ethic., illud potissime videtur esse unumquodque, quod est principale in ipso, sicut quod facit rector civitatis, dicitur civitas facere. Et hoc modo aliquando quod est principale in homine, dicitur homo, aliquando quidem pars intellectiva, secundum rei veritatem, quae dicitur homo interior; aliquando vero pars sensitiva cum corpore, secundum aestimationem quorundam, qui solum circa sensibilia detinentur. Et hic dicitur homo exterior.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31477)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod non quaelibet substantia particularis est hypostasis vel persona, sed quae habet completam naturam speciei. Unde manus vel pes non potest dici hypostasis vel persona. Et similiter nec anima, cum sit pars speciei humanae.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 75 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31478)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima sit composita ex materia et forma. Potentia enim contra actum dividitur. Sed omnia quaecumque sunt in actu, participant primum actum, qui Deus est; per cuius participationem omnia sunt et bona et entia et viventia, ut patet per doctrinam Dionysii in libro de Div. Nom. Ergo quaecumque sunt in potentia, participant primam potentiam. Sed prima potentia est materia prima. Cum ergo anima humana sit quodammodo in potentia, quod apparet ex hoc quod homo quandoque est intelligens in potentia; videtur quod anima humana participet materiam primam tanquam partem sui.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31479)\n\nPraeterea, in quocumque inveniuntur proprietates materiae, ibi invenitur materia. Sed in anima inveniuntur proprietates materiae, quae sunt subiici et transmutari, subiicitur enim scientiae et virtuti, et mutatur de ignorantia ad scientiam, et de vitio ad virtutem. Ergo in anima est materia.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31480)\n\nPraeterea, illa quae non habent materiam, non habent causam sui esse, ut dicitur in VIII Metaphys. Sed anima habet causam sui esse, quia creatur a Deo. Ergo anima habet materiam.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31481)\n\nPraeterea, quod non habet materiam, sed est forma tantum, est actus purus et infinitus. Hoc autem solius Dei est. Ergo anima habet materiam.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31482)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus probat, in VII super Gen. ad Litt., quod anima non est facta nec ex materia corporali, nec ex materia spirituali.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31483)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod anima non habet materiam. Et hoc potest considerari dupliciter. Primo quidem, ex ratione animae in communi. Est enim de ratione animae, quod sit forma alicuius corporis. Aut igitur est forma secundum se totam; aut secundum aliquam partem sui. Si secundum se totam, impossibile est quod pars eius sit materia, si dicatur materia aliquod ens in potentia tantum, quia forma, inquantum forma, est actus; id autem quod est in potentia tantum, non potest esse pars actus, cum potentia repugnet actui, utpote contra actum divisa. Si autem sit forma secundum aliquam partem sui, illam partem, dicemus esse animam, et illam materiam cuius primo est actus, dicemus esse primum animatum. Secundo, specialiter ex ratione humanae animae, inquantum est intellectiva. Manifestum est enim quod omne quod recipitur in aliquo, recipitur in eo per modum recipientis. Sic autem cognoscitur unumquodque, sicut forma eius est in cognoscente anima autem intellectiva cognoscit rem aliquam in sua natura absolute, puta lapidem inquantum est lapis absolute. Est igitur forma lapidis absolute, secundum propriam rationem formalem, in anima intellectiva. Anima igitur intellectiva est forma absoluta, non autem aliquid compositum ex materia et forma. Si enim anima intellectiva esset composita ex materia et forma, formae rerum reciperentur in ea ut individuales, et sic non cognosceret nisi singulare, sicut accidit in potentiis sensitivis, quae recipiunt formas rerum in organo corporali, materia enim est principium individuationis formarum. Relinquitur ergo quod anima intellectiva, et omnis intellectualis substantia cognoscens formas absolute, caret compositione formae et materiae.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31484)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod primus actus est universale principium omnium actuum, quia est infinitum, virtualiter in se omnia praehabens, ut dicit Dionysius. Unde participatur a rebus, non sicut pars, sed secundum diffusionem processionis ipsius. Potentia autem, cum sit receptiva actus, oportet quod actui proportionetur. Actus vero recepti, qui procedunt a primo actu infinito et sunt quaedam participationes eius, sunt diversi. Unde non potest esse potentia una quae recipiat omnes actus, sicut est unus actus influens omnes actus participatos, alioquin potentia receptiva adaequaret potentiam activam primi actus. Est autem alia potentia receptiva in anima intellectiva, a potentia receptiva materiae primae, ut patet ex diversitate receptorum, nam materia prima recipit formas individuales, intellectus autem recipit formas absolutas. Unde talis potentia in anima intellectiva existens, non ostendit quod anima sit composita ex materia et forma.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31485)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod subiici et transmutari convenit materiae secundum quod est in potentia. Sicut ergo est alia potentia intellectus, et alia potentia materiae primae, ita est alia ratio subiiciendi et transmutandi. Secundum hoc enim intellectus subiicitur scientiae, et transmutatur de ignorantia ad scientiam, secundum quod est in potentia ad species intelligibiles.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31486)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod forma est causa essendi materiae, et agens, unde agens, inquantum reducit materiam in actum formae transmutando, est ei causa essendi. Si quid autem est forma subsistens, non habet esse per aliquod formale principium, nec habet causam transmutantem de potentia in actum. Unde post verba praemissa, philosophus concludit quod in his quae sunt composita ex materia et forma, *nulla est alia causa nisi movens ex potestate ad actum, quaecumque vero non habent materiam, omnia simpliciter sunt quod vere entia aliquid*.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31487)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod omne participatum comparatur ad participans ut actus eius. Quaecumque autem forma creata per se subsistens ponatur, oportet quod participet esse, quia etiam ipsa vita, vel quidquid sic diceretur, participat ipsum esse, ut dicit Dionysius, V cap. de Div. Nom. Esse autem participatum finitur ad capacitatem participantis. Unde solus Deus, qui est ipsum suum esse, est actus purus et infinitus. In substantiis vero intellectualibus est compositio ex actu et potentia; non quidem ex materia et forma, sed ex forma et esse participato. Unde a quibusdam dicuntur componi ex quo est et quod est, ipsum enim esse est quo aliquid est.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 75 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31488)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima humana sit corruptibilis. Quorum enim est simile principium et similis processus, videtur esse similis finis. Sed simile est principium generationis hominum et iumentorum, quia de terra facta sunt. Similis est etiam vitae processus in utrisque, quia *similiter spirant omnia, et nihil habet homo iumento amplius*, ut dicitur Eccle. III. Ergo, ut ibidem concluditur, *unus est interitus hominis et iumentorum, et aequa utriusque conditio*. Sed anima brutorum animalium est corruptibilis. Ergo et anima humana est corruptibilis.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31489)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod est ex nihilo, vertibile est in nihilum, quia finis debet respondere principio. Sed sicut dicitur Sap. II, *ex nihilo nati sumus*, quod verum est non solum quantum ad corpus, sed etiam quantum ad animam. Ergo ut ibidem concluditur, post hoc erimus tanquam non fuerimus, etiam secundum animam.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31490)\n\nPraeterea, nulla res est sine propria operatione. Sed propria operatio animae, quae est intelligere cum phantasmate, non potest esse sine corpore, nihil enim sine phantasmate intelligit anima; phantasma autem non est sine corpore, ut dicitur in libro de anima. Ergo anima non potest remanere, destructo corpore.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31491)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod animae humanae habent ex bonitate divina quod sint intellectuales et quod habeant substantialem vitam inconsumptibilem.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31492)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere animam humanam, quam dicimus intellectivum principium, esse incorruptibilem. Dupliciter enim aliquid corrumpitur, uno modo, per se; alio modo, per accidens. Impossibile est autem aliquid subsistens generari aut corrumpi per accidens, idest aliquo generato vel corrupto. Sic enim competit alicui generari et corrumpi, sicut et esse, quod per generationem acquiritur et per corruptionem amittitur. Unde quod per se habet esse, non potest generari vel corrumpi nisi per se, quae vero non subsistunt, ut accidentia et formae materiales, dicuntur fieri et corrumpi per generationem et corruptionem compositorum. Ostensum est autem supra quod animae brutorum non sunt per se subsistentes, sed sola anima humana. Unde animae brutorum corrumpuntur, corruptis corporibus, anima autem humana non posset corrumpi, nisi per se corrumperetur. Quod quidem omnino est impossibile non solum de ipsa, sed de quolibet subsistente quod est forma tantum. Manifestum est enim quod id quod secundum se convenit alicui, est inseparabile ab ipso. Esse autem per se convenit formae, quae est actus. Unde materia secundum hoc acquirit esse in actu, quod acquirit formam, secundum hoc autem accidit in ea corruptio, quod separatur forma ab ea. Impossibile est autem quod forma separetur a seipsa. Unde impossibile est quod forma subsistens desinat esse. Dato etiam quod anima esset ex materia et forma composita, ut quidam dicunt, adhuc oporteret ponere eam incorruptibilem. Non enim invenitur corruptio nisi ubi invenitur contrarietas, generationes enim et corruptiones ex contrariis et in contraria sunt; unde corpora caelestia, quia non habent materiam contrarietati subiectam, incorruptibilia sunt. In anima autem intellectiva non potest esse aliqua contrarietas. Recipit enim secundum modum sui esse, ea vero quae in ipsa recipiuntur, sunt absque contrarietate; quia etiam rationes contrariorum in intellectu non sunt contrariae, sed est una scientia contrariorum. Impossibile est ergo quod anima intellectiva sit corruptibilis. Potest etiam huius rei accipi signum ex hoc, quod unumquodque naturaliter suo modo esse desiderat. Desiderium autem in rebus cognoscentibus sequitur cognitionem. Sensus autem non cognoscit esse nisi sub hic et nunc, sed intellectus apprehendit esse absolute, et secundum omne tempus. Unde omne habens intellectum naturaliter desiderat esse semper. Naturale autem desiderium non potest esse inane. Omnis igitur intellectualis substantia est incorruptibilis.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31493)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Salomon inducit rationem illam ex persona insipientium, ut exprimitur Sap. II. Quod ergo dicitur quod homo et alia animalia habent simile generationis principium, verum est quantum ad corpus, similiter enim de terra facta sunt omnia animalia. Non autem quantum ad animam, nam anima brutorum producitur ex virtute aliqua corporea, anima vero humana a Deo. Et ad hoc significandum dicitur Gen., quantum ad alia animalia, producat terra animam viventem, quantum vero ad hominem, dicitur quod inspiravit in faciem eius spiraculum vitae. Et ideo concluditur Eccle. ult., *revertatur pulvis in terram suam, unde erat, et spiritus redeat ad Deum qui dedit illum*. Similiter processus vitae est similis quantum ad corpus; ad quod pertinet quod dicitur in Eccle., *similiter spirant omnia*; et Sap. II, *fumus et flatus est in naribus nostris* et cetera. Sed non est similis processus quantum ad animam, quia homo intelligit, non autem animalia bruta. Unde falsum est quod dicitur, nihil habet homo iumento amplius. Et ideo similis est interitus quantum ad corpus, sed non quantum ad animam.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31494)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut posse creari dicitur aliquid non per potentiam passivam, sed solum per potentiam activam creantis, qui ex nihilo potest aliquid producere; ita cum dicitur aliquid vertibile in nihil, non importatur in creatura potentia ad non esse, sed in creatore potentia ad hoc quod esse non influat. Dicitur autem aliquid corruptibile per hoc, quod inest ei potentia ad non esse.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31495)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod intelligere cum phantasmate est propria operatio animae secundum quod corpori est unita. Separata autem a corpore habebit alium modum intelligendi, similem aliis substantiis a corpore separatis, ut infra melius patebit.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 75 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31496)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima et Angelus sint unius speciei. Unumquodque enim ordinatur ad proprium finem per naturam suae speciei, per quam habet inclinationem ad finem. Sed idem est finis animae et Angeli, scilicet beatitudo aeterna. Ergo sunt unius speciei.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31497)\n\nPraeterea, ultima differentia specifica est nobilissima, quia complet rationem speciei. Sed nihil est nobilius in Angelo et anima quam intellectuale esse. Ergo conveniunt anima et Angelus in ultima differentia specifica. Ergo sunt unius speciei.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31498)\n\nPraeterea, anima ab Angelo differre non videtur nisi per hoc, quod est corpori unita. Corpus autem, cum sit extra essentiam animae, non videtur ad eius speciem pertinere. Ergo anima et Angelus sunt unius speciei.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31499)\n\nSed contra, quorum sunt diversae operationes naturales, ipsa differunt specie. Sed animae et Angeli sunt diversae operationes naturales, quia ut dicit Dionysius, VII cap. de Div. Nom., *mentes angelicae simplices et beatos intellectus habent, non de visibilibus congregantes divinam cognitionem*; cuius contrarium postmodum de anima dicit. Anima igitur et Angelus non sunt unius speciei.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31500)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Origenes posuit omnes animas humanas et Angelos esse unius speciei. Et hoc ideo, quia posuit diversitatem gradus in huiusmodi substantiis inventam, accidentalem, utpote ex libero arbitrio provenientem, ut supra dictum est. Quod non potest esse, quia in substantiis incorporeis non potest esse diversitas secundum numerum absque diversitate secundum speciem, et absque naturali inaequalitate. Quia si non sint compositae ex materia et forma, sed sint formae subsistentes, manifestum est quod necesse erit in eis esse diversitatem in specie. Non enim potest intelligi quod aliqua forma separata sit nisi una unius speciei, sicut si esset albedo separata, non posset esse nisi una tantum; haec enim albedo non differt ab illa nisi per hoc, quod est huius vel illius. Diversitas autem secundum speciem semper habet diversitatem naturalem concomitantem, sicut in speciebus colorum unus est perfectior altero, et similiter in aliis. Et hoc ideo, quia differentiae dividentes genus sunt contrariae; contraria autem se habent secundum perfectum et imperfectum, quia principium contrarietatis est privatio et habitus ut dicitur in X Metaphys. Idem etiam sequeretur, si huiusmodi substantiae essent compositae ex materia et forma. Si enim materia huius distinguitur a materia illius, necesse est quod vel forma sit principium distinctionis materiae, ut scilicet materiae sint diversae propter habitudinem ad diversas formas, et tunc sequitur adhuc diversitas secundum speciem et inaequalitas naturalis. Vel materia erit principium distinctionis formarum; nec poterit dici materia haec alia ab illa, nisi secundum divisionem quantitativam, quae non habet locum in substantiis incorporeis, cuiusmodi sunt Angelus et anima. Unde non potest esse quod Angelus et anima sint unius speciei. Quomodo autem sint plures animae unius speciei infra ostendetur.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31501)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ratio illa procedit de fine proximo et naturali. Beatitudo autem aeterna est finis ultimus et supernaturalis.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31502)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod differentia specifica ultima est nobilissima, inquantum est maxime determinata, per modum quo actus est nobilior potentia. Sic autem intellectuale non est nobilissimum, quia est indeterminatum et commune ad multos intellectualitatis gradus, sicut sensibile ad multos gradus in esse sensibili. Unde sicut non omnia sensibilia sunt unius speciei, ita nec omnia intellectualia.\n\n[I q. 75 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31503)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod corpus non est de essentia animae, sed anima ex natura suae essentiae habet quod sit corpori unibilis. Unde nec proprie anima est in specie; sed compositum. Et hoc ipsum quod anima quodammodo indiget corpore ad suam operationem, ostendit quod anima tenet inferiorem gradum intellectualitatis quam Angelus, qui corpori non unitur.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0002","text":"[I q. 75 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31447)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0003","text":"Post considerationem creaturae spiritualis et corporalis, considerandum est de homine, qui ex spirituali et corporali substantia componitur. Et primo, de natura ipsius hominis; secundo, de eius productione. Naturam autem hominis considerare pertinet ad theologum ex parte animae, non autem ex parte corporis, nisi secundum habitudinem quam habet corpus ad animam. Et ideo prima consideratio circa animam versabitur. Et quia, secundum Dionysium, XI cap. Angel. Hier., tria inveniuntur in substantiis spiritualibus, scilicet essentia, virtus et operatio; primo considerabimus ea quae pertinent ad essentiam animae; secundo, ea quae pertinent ad virtutem sive potentias eius; tertio, ea quae pertinent ad operationem eius. Circa primum duplex occurrit consideratio, quarum prima est de ipsa anima secundum se; secunda, de unione eius ad corpus. Circa primum quaeruntur septem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0004","text":"Primo, utrum anima sit corpus. Secundo, utrum anima humana sit aliquid subsistens. Tertio, utrum animae brutorum sint subsistentes. Quarto, utrum anima sit homo; vel magis homo sit aliquid compositum ex anima et corpore. Quinto, utrum sit composita ex materia et forma. Sexto, utrum anima humana sit incorruptibilis. Septimo, utrum anima sit eiusdem speciei cum Angelo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0005","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0006","text":"[I q. 75 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31448)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0007","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima sit corpus. Anima enim est motor corporis. Non autem est movens non motum. Tum quia videtur quod nihil possit movere nisi moveatur, quia nihil dat alteri quod non habet, sicut quod non est calidum non calefacit. Tum quia, si aliquid est movens non motum, causat motum sempiternum et eodem modo se habentem, ut probatur in VIII Physic., quod non apparet in motu animalis, qui est ab anima. Ergo anima est movens motum. Sed omne movens motum est corpus. Ergo anima est corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0008","text":"[I q. 75 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31449)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0009","text":"Praeterea, omnis cognitio fit per aliquam similitudinem. Non potest autem esse similitudo corporis ad rem incorpoream. Si igitur anima non esset corpus, non posset cognoscere res corporeas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0010","text":"[I q. 75 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31450)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0011","text":"Praeterea, moventis ad motum oportet esse aliquem contactum. Contactus autem non est nisi corporum. Cum igitur anima moveat corpus, videtur quod anima sit corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0012","text":"[I q. 75 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31451)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0013","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, VI de Trin., quod *anima simplex dicitur respectu corporis, quia mole non diffunditur per spatium loci*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0014","text":"[I q. 75 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31452)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0015","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ad inquirendum de natura animae, oportet praesupponere quod anima dicitur esse primum principium vitae in his quae apud nos vivunt animata enim viventia dicimus, res vero inanimatas vita carentes. Vita autem maxime manifestatur duplici opere, scilicet cognitionis et motus. Horum autem principium antiqui philosophi, imaginationem transcendere non valentes, aliquod corpus ponebant; sola corpora res esse dicentes, et quod non est corpus, nihil esse. Et secundum hoc, animam aliquod corpus esse dicebant. Huius autem opinionis falsitas licet multipliciter ostendi possit tamen uno utemur, quo et communius et certius patet animam corpus non esse. Manifestum est enim quod non quodcumque vitalis operationis principium est anima, sic enim oculus esset anima, cum sit quoddam principium visionis; et idem esset dicendum de aliis animae instrumentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0016","text":"Sed primum principium vitae dicimus esse animam. Quamvis autem aliquod corpus possit esse quoddam principium vitae, sicut cor est principium vitae in animali; tamen non potest esse primum principium vitae aliquod corpus. Manifestum est enim quod esse principium vitae, vel vivens, non convenit corpori ex hoc quod est corpus, alioquin omne corpus esset vivens, aut principium vitae. Convenit igitur alicui corpori quod sit vivens, vel etiam principium vitae, per hoc quod est tale corpus. Quod autem est actu tale, habet hoc ab aliquo principio quod dicitur actus eius. Anima igitur, quae est primum principium vitae, non est corpus, sed corporis actus, sicut calor, qui est principium calefactionis, non est corpus, sed quidam corporis actus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0017","text":"[I q. 75 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31453)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0018","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, cum omne quod movetur ab alio moveatur, quod non potest in infinitum procedere, necesse est dicere quod non omne movens movetur. Cum enim moveri sit exire de potentia in actum, movens dat id quod habet mobili, inquantum facit ipsum esse in actu. Sed sicut ostenditur in VIII Physic., est quoddam movens penitus immobile, quod nec per se nec per accidens movetur, et tale movens potest movere motum semper uniformem. Est autem aliud movens, quod non movetur per se, sed movetur per accidens, et propter hoc non movet motum semper uniformem. Et tale movens est anima. Est autem aliud movens, quod per se movetur, scilicet corpus. Et quia antiqui naturales nihil esse credebant nisi corpora, posuerunt quod omne movens movetur, et quod anima per se movetur, et est corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0019","text":"[I q. 75 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31454)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod non est necessarium quod similitudo rei cognitae sit actu in natura cognoscentis, sed si aliquid sit quod prius est cognoscens in potentia et postea in actu, oportet quod similitudo cogniti non sit actu in natura cognoscentis, sed in potentia tantum; sicut color non est actu in pupilla, sed in potentia tantum. Unde non oportet quod in natura animae sit similitudo rerum corporearum in actu; sed quod sit in potentia ad huiusmodi similitudines. Sed quia antiqui naturales nesciebant distinguere inter actum et potentiam, ponebant animam esse corpus, ad hoc quod cognosceret corpus; et ad hoc quod cognosceret omnia corpora, quod esset composita ex principiis omnium corporum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0021","text":"[I q. 75 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31455)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod est duplex contactus, quantitatis et virtutis. Primo modo, corpus non tangitur nisi a corpore. Secundo modo, corpus potest tangi a re incorporea quae movet corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0023","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0024","text":"[I q. 75 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31456)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0025","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima humana non sit aliquid subsistens. Quod enim est subsistens, dicitur hoc aliquid. Anima autem non est hoc aliquid, sed compositum ex anima et corpore. Ergo anima non est aliquid subsistens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0026","text":"[I q. 75 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31457)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0027","text":"Praeterea, omne quod est subsistens, potest dici operari. Sed anima non dicitur operari, quia, ut dicitur in I de anima, *dicere animam sentire aut intelligere, simile est ac si dicat eam aliquis texere vel aedificare*. Ergo anima non est aliquid subsistens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0028","text":"[I q. 75 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31458)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0029","text":"Praeterea, si anima esset aliquid subsistens, esset aliqua eius operatio sine corpore. Sed nulla est eius operatio sine corpore, nec etiam intelligere, quia non contingit intelligere sine phantasmate, phantasma autem non est sine corpore. Ergo anima humana non est aliquid subsistens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0030","text":"[I q. 75 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31459)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0031","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, X de Trin. *Quisquis videt mentis naturam et esse substantiam, et non esse corpoream, videt eos qui opinantur eam esse corpoream, ob hoc errare, quod adiungunt ei ea sine quibus nullam possunt cogitare naturam, scilicet corporum phantasias*. Natura ergo mentis humanae non solum est incorporea, sed etiam substantia, scilicet aliquid subsistens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0032","text":"[I q. 75 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31460)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0033","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere id quod est principium intellectualis operationis, quod dicimus animam hominis, esse quoddam principium incorporeum et subsistens. Manifestum est enim quod homo per intellectum cognoscere potest naturas omnium corporum. Quod autem potest cognoscere aliqua, oportet ut nihil eorum habeat in sua natura, quia illud quod inesset ei naturaliter impediret cognitionem aliorum; sicut videmus quod lingua infirmi quae infecta est cholerico et amaro humore, non potest percipere aliquid dulce, sed omnia videntur ei amara. Si igitur principium intellectuale haberet in se naturam alicuius corporis, non posset omnia corpora cognoscere. Omne autem corpus habet aliquam naturam determinatam. Impossibile est igitur quod principium intellectuale sit corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0034","text":"Et similiter impossibile est quod intelligat per organum corporeum, quia etiam natura determinata illius organi corporei prohiberet cognitionem omnium corporum; sicut si aliquis determinatus color sit non solum in pupilla, sed etiam in vase vitreo, liquor infusus eiusdem coloris videtur. Ipsum igitur intellectuale principium, quod dicitur mens vel intellectus, habet operationem per se, cui non communicat corpus. Nihil autem potest per se operari, nisi quod per se subsistit. Non enim est operari nisi entis in actu, unde eo modo aliquid operatur, quo est. Propter quod non dicimus quod calor calefacit, sed calidum. Relinquitur igitur animam humanam, quae dicitur intellectus vel mens, esse aliquid incorporeum et subsistens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0035","text":"[I q. 75 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31461)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0036","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod hoc aliquid potest accipi dupliciter, uno modo, pro quocumque subsistente, alio modo, pro subsistente completo in natura alicuius speciei. Primo modo, excludit inhaerentiam accidentis et formae materialis, secundo modo, excludit etiam imperfectionem partis. Unde manus posset dici hoc aliquid primo modo, sed non secundo modo. Sic igitur, cum anima humana sit pars speciei humanae, potest dici hoc aliquid primo modo, quasi subsistens, sed non secundo modo, sic enim compositum ex anima et corpore dicitur hoc aliquid.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0037","text":"[I q. 75 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31462)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0038","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod verba illa Aristoteles dicit non secundum propriam sententiam, sed secundum opinionem illorum qui dicebant quod intelligere est moveri; ut patet ex iis quae ibi praemittit. Vel dicendum quod per se agere convenit per se existenti. Sed per se existens quandoque potest dici aliquid si non sit inhaerens ut accidens vel ut forma materialis, etiam si sit pars. Sed proprie et per se subsistens dicitur quod neque est praedicto modo inhaerens, neque est pars. Secundum quem modum oculus aut manus non posset dici per se subsistens; et per consequens nec per se operans. Unde et operationes partium attribuuntur toti per partes. Dicimus enim quod homo videt per oculum, et palpat per manum, aliter quam calidum calefacit per calorem, quia calor nullo modo calefacit, proprie loquendo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0039","text":"Potest igitur dici quod anima intelligit, sicut oculus videt, sed magis proprie dicitur quod homo intelligat per animam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0040","text":"[I q. 75 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31463)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0041","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod corpus requiritur ad actionem intellectus, non sicut organum quo talis actio exerceatur, sed ratione obiecti, phantasma enim comparatur ad intellectum sicut color ad visum. Sic autem indigere corpore non removet intellectum esse subsistentem, alioquin animal non esset aliquid subsistens, cum indigeat exterioribus sensibilibus ad sentiendum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0042","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0043","text":"[I q. 75 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31464)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0044","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod animae brutorum animalium sint subsistentes. Homo enim convenit in genere cum aliis animalibus. Sed anima hominis est aliquid subsistens, ut ostensum est. Ergo et animae aliorum animalium sunt subsistentes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0045","text":"[I q. 75 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31465)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0046","text":"Praeterea, similiter se habet sensitivum ad sensibilia, sicut intellectivum et intelligibilia. Sed intellectus intelligit intelligibilia sine corpore. Ergo et sensus apprehendit sensibilia sine corpore. Animae autem brutorum animalium sunt sensitivae. Ergo sunt subsistentes, pari ratione qua et anima hominis, quae est intellectiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0047","text":"[I q. 75 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31466)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0048","text":"Praeterea, brutorum animalium anima movet corpus. Corpus autem non movet, sed movetur. Anima ergo bruti animalis habet aliquam operationem sine corpore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0049","text":"[I q. 75 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31467)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0050","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in libro de Eccl. Dogmat., *solum hominem credimus habere animam substantivam; animalium vero animae non sunt substantivae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0051","text":"[I q. 75 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31468)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0052","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod antiqui philosophi nullam distinctionem ponebant inter sensum et intellectum, et utrumque corporeo principio attribuebant, ut dictum est. Plato autem distinxit inter intellectum et sensum; utrumque tamen attribuit principio incorporeo, ponens quod, sicut intelligere, ita et sentire convenit animae secundum seipsam. Et ex hoc sequebatur quod etiam animae brutorum animalium sint subsistentes. Sed Aristoteles posuit quod solum intelligere, inter opera animae, sine organo corporeo exercetur. Sentire vero, et consequentes operationes animae sensitivae, manifeste accidunt cum aliqua corporis immutatione; sicut in videndo immutatur pupilla per speciem coloris; et idem apparet in aliis. Et sic manifestum est quod anima sensitiva non habet aliquam operationem propriam per seipsam, sed omnis operatio sensitivae animae est coniuncti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0053","text":"Ex quo relinquitur quod, cum animae brutorum animalium per se non operentur, non sint subsistentes, similiter enim unumquodque habet esse et operationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0054","text":"[I q. 75 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31469)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0055","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod homo, etsi conveniat in genere cum aliis animalibus, specie tamen differt, differentia autem speciei attenditur secundum differentiam formae. Nec oportet quod omnis differentia formae faciat generis diversitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0056","text":"[I q. 75 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31470)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0057","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod sensitivum quodammodo se habet ad sensibilia sicut intellectivum ad intelligibilia, inquantum scilicet utrumque est in potentia ad sua obiecta. Sed quodammodo dissimiliter se habent, inquantum sensitivum patitur a sensibili cum corporis immutatione, unde excellentia sensibilium corrumpit sensum. Quod in intellectu non contingit, nam intellectus intelligens maxima intelligibilium, magis potest postmodum intelligere minora. Si vero in intelligendo fatigetur corpus, hoc est per accidens, in quantum intellectus indiget operatione virium sensitivarum, per quas ei phantasmata praeparantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0058","text":"[I q. 75 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31471)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0059","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod vis motiva est duplex. Una quae imperat motum, scilicet appetitiva. Et huius operatio in anima sensitiva non est sine corpore; sed ira et gaudium et omnes huiusmodi passiones sunt cum aliqua corporis immutatione. Alia vis motiva est exequens motum, per quam membra redduntur habilia ad obediendum appetitui, cuius actus non est movere, sed moveri. Unde patet quod movere non est actus animae sensitivae sine corpore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0060","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0061","text":"[I q. 75 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31472)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0062","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima sit homo. Dicitur enim II ad Cor. IV, *licet is qui foris est noster homo corrumpatur, tamen is qui intus est, renovatur de die in diem*. Sed id quod est intus in homine, est anima. Ergo anima est homo interior.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0063","text":"[I q. 75 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31473)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0064","text":"Praeterea, anima humana est substantia quaedam. Non autem est substantia universalis. Ergo est substantia particularis. Ergo est hypostasis vel persona. Sed non nisi humana. Ergo anima est homo, nam persona humana est homo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0065","text":"[I q. 75 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31474)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0066","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus, XIX de Civ. Dei, commendat Varronem, *qui hominem nec animam solam, nec solum corpus, sed animam simul et corpus esse arbitrabatur*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0067","text":"[I q. 75 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31475)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0068","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod animam esse hominem dupliciter potest intelligi. Uno modo, quod homo sit anima, sed hic homo non sit anima, sed compositum ex anima et corpore, puta Socrates. Quod ideo dico, quia quidam posuerunt solam formam esse de ratione speciei, materiam vero esse partem individui, et non speciei. Quod quidem non potest esse verum. Nam ad naturam speciei pertinet id quod significat definitio. Definitio autem in rebus naturalibus non significat formam tantum, sed formam et materiam. Unde materia est pars speciei in rebus naturalibus, non quidem materia signata, quae est principium individuationis; sed materia communis. Sicut enim de ratione huius hominis est quod sit ex hac anima et his carnibus et his ossibus; ita de ratione hominis est quod sit ex anima et carnibus et ossibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0069","text":"Oportet enim de substantia speciei esse quidquid est communiter de substantia omnium individuorum sub specie contentorum. Alio vero modo potest intelligi sic, quod etiam haec anima sit hic homo. Et hoc quidem sustineri posset, si poneretur quod animae sensitivae operatio esset eius propria sine corpore, quia omnes operationes quae attribuuntur homini, convenirent soli animae; illud autem est unaquaeque res, quod operatur operationes illius rei. Unde illud est homo, quod operatur operationes hominis. Ostensum est autem quod sentire non est operatio animae tantum. Cum igitur sentire sit quaedam operatio hominis, licet non propria, manifestum est quod homo non est anima tantum, sed est aliquid compositum ex anima et corpore. Plato vero, ponens sentire esse proprium animae, ponere potuit quod homo esset anima utens corpore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0070","text":"[I q. 75 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31476)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0071","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, secundum philosophum in IX Ethic., illud potissime videtur esse unumquodque, quod est principale in ipso, sicut quod facit rector civitatis, dicitur civitas facere. Et hoc modo aliquando quod est principale in homine, dicitur homo, aliquando quidem pars intellectiva, secundum rei veritatem, quae dicitur homo interior; aliquando vero pars sensitiva cum corpore, secundum aestimationem quorundam, qui solum circa sensibilia detinentur. Et hic dicitur homo exterior.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0072","text":"[I q. 75 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31477)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0073","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod non quaelibet substantia particularis est hypostasis vel persona, sed quae habet completam naturam speciei. Unde manus vel pes non potest dici hypostasis vel persona. Et similiter nec anima, cum sit pars speciei humanae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0074","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0075","text":"[I q. 75 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31478)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0076","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima sit composita ex materia et forma. Potentia enim contra actum dividitur. Sed omnia quaecumque sunt in actu, participant primum actum, qui Deus est; per cuius participationem omnia sunt et bona et entia et viventia, ut patet per doctrinam Dionysii in libro de Div. Nom. Ergo quaecumque sunt in potentia, participant primam potentiam. Sed prima potentia est materia prima. Cum ergo anima humana sit quodammodo in potentia, quod apparet ex hoc quod homo quandoque est intelligens in potentia; videtur quod anima humana participet materiam primam tanquam partem sui.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0077","text":"[I q. 75 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31479)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0078","text":"Praeterea, in quocumque inveniuntur proprietates materiae, ibi invenitur materia. Sed in anima inveniuntur proprietates materiae, quae sunt subiici et transmutari, subiicitur enim scientiae et virtuti, et mutatur de ignorantia ad scientiam, et de vitio ad virtutem. Ergo in anima est materia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0079","text":"[I q. 75 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31480)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0080","text":"Praeterea, illa quae non habent materiam, non habent causam sui esse, ut dicitur in VIII Metaphys. Sed anima habet causam sui esse, quia creatur a Deo. Ergo anima habet materiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0081","text":"[I q. 75 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31481)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0082","text":"Praeterea, quod non habet materiam, sed est forma tantum, est actus purus et infinitus. Hoc autem solius Dei est. Ergo anima habet materiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0083","text":"[I q. 75 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31482)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0084","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus probat, in VII super Gen. ad Litt., quod anima non est facta nec ex materia corporali, nec ex materia spirituali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0085","text":"[I q. 75 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31483)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0086","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod anima non habet materiam. Et hoc potest considerari dupliciter. Primo quidem, ex ratione animae in communi. Est enim de ratione animae, quod sit forma alicuius corporis. Aut igitur est forma secundum se totam; aut secundum aliquam partem sui. Si secundum se totam, impossibile est quod pars eius sit materia, si dicatur materia aliquod ens in potentia tantum, quia forma, inquantum forma, est actus; id autem quod est in potentia tantum, non potest esse pars actus, cum potentia repugnet actui, utpote contra actum divisa. Si autem sit forma secundum aliquam partem sui, illam partem, dicemus esse animam, et illam materiam cuius primo est actus, dicemus esse primum animatum. Secundo, specialiter ex ratione humanae animae, inquantum est intellectiva. Manifestum est enim quod omne quod recipitur in aliquo, recipitur in eo per modum recipientis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0087","text":"Sic autem cognoscitur unumquodque, sicut forma eius est in cognoscente anima autem intellectiva cognoscit rem aliquam in sua natura absolute, puta lapidem inquantum est lapis absolute. Est igitur forma lapidis absolute, secundum propriam rationem formalem, in anima intellectiva. Anima igitur intellectiva est forma absoluta, non autem aliquid compositum ex materia et forma. Si enim anima intellectiva esset composita ex materia et forma, formae rerum reciperentur in ea ut individuales, et sic non cognosceret nisi singulare, sicut accidit in potentiis sensitivis, quae recipiunt formas rerum in organo corporali, materia enim est principium individuationis formarum. Relinquitur ergo quod anima intellectiva, et omnis intellectualis substantia cognoscens formas absolute, caret compositione formae et materiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0088","text":"[I q. 75 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31484)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0089","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod primus actus est universale principium omnium actuum, quia est infinitum, virtualiter in se omnia praehabens, ut dicit Dionysius. Unde participatur a rebus, non sicut pars, sed secundum diffusionem processionis ipsius. Potentia autem, cum sit receptiva actus, oportet quod actui proportionetur. Actus vero recepti, qui procedunt a primo actu infinito et sunt quaedam participationes eius, sunt diversi. Unde non potest esse potentia una quae recipiat omnes actus, sicut est unus actus influens omnes actus participatos, alioquin potentia receptiva adaequaret potentiam activam primi actus. Est autem alia potentia receptiva in anima intellectiva, a potentia receptiva materiae primae, ut patet ex diversitate receptorum, nam materia prima recipit formas individuales, intellectus autem recipit formas absolutas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0090","text":"Unde talis potentia in anima intellectiva existens, non ostendit quod anima sit composita ex materia et forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0091","text":"[I q. 75 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31485)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0092","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod subiici et transmutari convenit materiae secundum quod est in potentia. Sicut ergo est alia potentia intellectus, et alia potentia materiae primae, ita est alia ratio subiiciendi et transmutandi. Secundum hoc enim intellectus subiicitur scientiae, et transmutatur de ignorantia ad scientiam, secundum quod est in potentia ad species intelligibiles.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0093","text":"[I q. 75 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31486)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0094","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod forma est causa essendi materiae, et agens, unde agens, inquantum reducit materiam in actum formae transmutando, est ei causa essendi. Si quid autem est forma subsistens, non habet esse per aliquod formale principium, nec habet causam transmutantem de potentia in actum. Unde post verba praemissa, philosophus concludit quod in his quae sunt composita ex materia et forma, *nulla est alia causa nisi movens ex potestate ad actum, quaecumque vero non habent materiam, omnia simpliciter sunt quod vere entia aliquid*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0095","text":"[I q. 75 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31487)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0096","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod omne participatum comparatur ad participans ut actus eius. Quaecumque autem forma creata per se subsistens ponatur, oportet quod participet esse, quia etiam ipsa vita, vel quidquid sic diceretur, participat ipsum esse, ut dicit Dionysius, V cap. de Div. Nom. Esse autem participatum finitur ad capacitatem participantis. Unde solus Deus, qui est ipsum suum esse, est actus purus et infinitus. In substantiis vero intellectualibus est compositio ex actu et potentia; non quidem ex materia et forma, sed ex forma et esse participato. Unde a quibusdam dicuntur componi ex quo est et quod est, ipsum enim esse est quo aliquid est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0097","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0098","text":"[I q. 75 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31488)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0099","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima humana sit corruptibilis. Quorum enim est simile principium et similis processus, videtur esse similis finis. Sed simile est principium generationis hominum et iumentorum, quia de terra facta sunt. Similis est etiam vitae processus in utrisque, quia *similiter spirant omnia, et nihil habet homo iumento amplius*, ut dicitur Eccle. III. Ergo, ut ibidem concluditur, *unus est interitus hominis et iumentorum, et aequa utriusque conditio*. Sed anima brutorum animalium est corruptibilis. Ergo et anima humana est corruptibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0100","text":"[I q. 75 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31489)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0101","text":"Praeterea, omne quod est ex nihilo, vertibile est in nihilum, quia finis debet respondere principio. Sed sicut dicitur Sap. II, *ex nihilo nati sumus*, quod verum est non solum quantum ad corpus, sed etiam quantum ad animam. Ergo ut ibidem concluditur, post hoc erimus tanquam non fuerimus, etiam secundum animam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0102","text":"[I q. 75 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31490)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0103","text":"Praeterea, nulla res est sine propria operatione. Sed propria operatio animae, quae est intelligere cum phantasmate, non potest esse sine corpore, nihil enim sine phantasmate intelligit anima; phantasma autem non est sine corpore, ut dicitur in libro de anima. Ergo anima non potest remanere, destructo corpore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0104","text":"[I q. 75 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31491)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0105","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod animae humanae habent ex bonitate divina quod sint intellectuales et quod habeant substantialem vitam inconsumptibilem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0106","text":"[I q. 75 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31492)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0107","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere animam humanam, quam dicimus intellectivum principium, esse incorruptibilem. Dupliciter enim aliquid corrumpitur, uno modo, per se; alio modo, per accidens. Impossibile est autem aliquid subsistens generari aut corrumpi per accidens, idest aliquo generato vel corrupto. Sic enim competit alicui generari et corrumpi, sicut et esse, quod per generationem acquiritur et per corruptionem amittitur. Unde quod per se habet esse, non potest generari vel corrumpi nisi per se, quae vero non subsistunt, ut accidentia et formae materiales, dicuntur fieri et corrumpi per generationem et corruptionem compositorum. Ostensum est autem supra quod animae brutorum non sunt per se subsistentes, sed sola anima humana. Unde animae brutorum corrumpuntur, corruptis corporibus, anima autem humana non posset corrumpi, nisi per se corrumperetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0108","text":"Quod quidem omnino est impossibile non solum de ipsa, sed de quolibet subsistente quod est forma tantum. Manifestum est enim quod id quod secundum se convenit alicui, est inseparabile ab ipso. Esse autem per se convenit formae, quae est actus. Unde materia secundum hoc acquirit esse in actu, quod acquirit formam, secundum hoc autem accidit in ea corruptio, quod separatur forma ab ea. Impossibile est autem quod forma separetur a seipsa. Unde impossibile est quod forma subsistens desinat esse. Dato etiam quod anima esset ex materia et forma composita, ut quidam dicunt, adhuc oporteret ponere eam incorruptibilem. Non enim invenitur corruptio nisi ubi invenitur contrarietas, generationes enim et corruptiones ex contrariis et in contraria sunt; unde corpora caelestia, quia non habent materiam contrarietati subiectam, incorruptibilia sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0109","text":"In anima autem intellectiva non potest esse aliqua contrarietas. Recipit enim secundum modum sui esse, ea vero quae in ipsa recipiuntur, sunt absque contrarietate; quia etiam rationes contrariorum in intellectu non sunt contrariae, sed est una scientia contrariorum. Impossibile est ergo quod anima intellectiva sit corruptibilis. Potest etiam huius rei accipi signum ex hoc, quod unumquodque naturaliter suo modo esse desiderat. Desiderium autem in rebus cognoscentibus sequitur cognitionem. Sensus autem non cognoscit esse nisi sub hic et nunc, sed intellectus apprehendit esse absolute, et secundum omne tempus. Unde omne habens intellectum naturaliter desiderat esse semper. Naturale autem desiderium non potest esse inane. Omnis igitur intellectualis substantia est incorruptibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0110","text":"[I q. 75 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31493)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0111","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Salomon inducit rationem illam ex persona insipientium, ut exprimitur Sap. II. Quod ergo dicitur quod homo et alia animalia habent simile generationis principium, verum est quantum ad corpus, similiter enim de terra facta sunt omnia animalia. Non autem quantum ad animam, nam anima brutorum producitur ex virtute aliqua corporea, anima vero humana a Deo. Et ad hoc significandum dicitur Gen., quantum ad alia animalia, producat terra animam viventem, quantum vero ad hominem, dicitur quod inspiravit in faciem eius spiraculum vitae. Et ideo concluditur Eccle. ult., *revertatur pulvis in terram suam, unde erat, et spiritus redeat ad Deum qui dedit illum*. Similiter processus vitae est similis quantum ad corpus; ad quod pertinet quod dicitur in Eccle., *similiter spirant omnia*; et Sap. II, *fumus et flatus est in naribus nostris* et cetera.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0112","text":"Sed non est similis processus quantum ad animam, quia homo intelligit, non autem animalia bruta. Unde falsum est quod dicitur, nihil habet homo iumento amplius. Et ideo similis est interitus quantum ad corpus, sed non quantum ad animam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0113","text":"[I q. 75 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31494)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0114","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut posse creari dicitur aliquid non per potentiam passivam, sed solum per potentiam activam creantis, qui ex nihilo potest aliquid producere; ita cum dicitur aliquid vertibile in nihil, non importatur in creatura potentia ad non esse, sed in creatore potentia ad hoc quod esse non influat. Dicitur autem aliquid corruptibile per hoc, quod inest ei potentia ad non esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0115","text":"[I q. 75 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31495)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0116","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod intelligere cum phantasmate est propria operatio animae secundum quod corpori est unita. Separata autem a corpore habebit alium modum intelligendi, similem aliis substantiis a corpore separatis, ut infra melius patebit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0117","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0118","text":"[I q. 75 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31496)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0119","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima et Angelus sint unius speciei. Unumquodque enim ordinatur ad proprium finem per naturam suae speciei, per quam habet inclinationem ad finem. Sed idem est finis animae et Angeli, scilicet beatitudo aeterna. Ergo sunt unius speciei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0120","text":"[I q. 75 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31497)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0121","text":"Praeterea, ultima differentia specifica est nobilissima, quia complet rationem speciei. Sed nihil est nobilius in Angelo et anima quam intellectuale esse. Ergo conveniunt anima et Angelus in ultima differentia specifica. Ergo sunt unius speciei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0122","text":"[I q. 75 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31498)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0123","text":"Praeterea, anima ab Angelo differre non videtur nisi per hoc, quod est corpori unita. Corpus autem, cum sit extra essentiam animae, non videtur ad eius speciem pertinere. Ergo anima et Angelus sunt unius speciei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0124","text":"[I q. 75 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31499)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0125","text":"Sed contra, quorum sunt diversae operationes naturales, ipsa differunt specie. Sed animae et Angeli sunt diversae operationes naturales, quia ut dicit Dionysius, VII cap. de Div. Nom., *mentes angelicae simplices et beatos intellectus habent, non de visibilibus congregantes divinam cognitionem*; cuius contrarium postmodum de anima dicit. Anima igitur et Angelus non sunt unius speciei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0126","text":"[I q. 75 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31500)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0127","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Origenes posuit omnes animas humanas et Angelos esse unius speciei. Et hoc ideo, quia posuit diversitatem gradus in huiusmodi substantiis inventam, accidentalem, utpote ex libero arbitrio provenientem, ut supra dictum est. Quod non potest esse, quia in substantiis incorporeis non potest esse diversitas secundum numerum absque diversitate secundum speciem, et absque naturali inaequalitate. Quia si non sint compositae ex materia et forma, sed sint formae subsistentes, manifestum est quod necesse erit in eis esse diversitatem in specie. Non enim potest intelligi quod aliqua forma separata sit nisi una unius speciei, sicut si esset albedo separata, non posset esse nisi una tantum; haec enim albedo non differt ab illa nisi per hoc, quod est huius vel illius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0128","text":"Diversitas autem secundum speciem semper habet diversitatem naturalem concomitantem, sicut in speciebus colorum unus est perfectior altero, et similiter in aliis. Et hoc ideo, quia differentiae dividentes genus sunt contrariae; contraria autem se habent secundum perfectum et imperfectum, quia principium contrarietatis est privatio et habitus ut dicitur in X Metaphys. Idem etiam sequeretur, si huiusmodi substantiae essent compositae ex materia et forma. Si enim materia huius distinguitur a materia illius, necesse est quod vel forma sit principium distinctionis materiae, ut scilicet materiae sint diversae propter habitudinem ad diversas formas, et tunc sequitur adhuc diversitas secundum speciem et inaequalitas naturalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0129","text":"Vel materia erit principium distinctionis formarum; nec poterit dici materia haec alia ab illa, nisi secundum divisionem quantitativam, quae non habet locum in substantiis incorporeis, cuiusmodi sunt Angelus et anima. Unde non potest esse quod Angelus et anima sint unius speciei. Quomodo autem sint plures animae unius speciei infra ostendetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0130","text":"[I q. 75 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31501)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0131","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ratio illa procedit de fine proximo et naturali. Beatitudo autem aeterna est finis ultimus et supernaturalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0132","text":"[I q. 75 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31502)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0133","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod differentia specifica ultima est nobilissima, inquantum est maxime determinata, per modum quo actus est nobilior potentia. Sic autem intellectuale non est nobilissimum, quia est indeterminatum et commune ad multos intellectualitatis gradus, sicut sensibile ad multos gradus in esse sensibili. Unde sicut non omnia sensibilia sunt unius speciei, ita nec omnia intellectualia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0134","text":"[I q. 75 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31503)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0135","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod corpus non est de essentia animae, sed anima ex natura suae essentiae habet quod sit corpori unibilis. Unde nec proprie anima est in specie; sed compositum. Et hoc ipsum quod anima quodammodo indiget corpore ad suam operationem, ostendit quod anima tenet inferiorem gradum intellectualitatis quam Angelus, qui corpori non unitur.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"dcf96ec3e4120bcbfc8056464887cd2a4522d1d99f31867bd7e4c4ffebff9cf4","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T150152089007Z_39b77f5c","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 75","source_sha256":"dcf96ec3e4120bcbfc8056464887cd2a4522d1d99f31867bd7e4c4ffebff9cf4","sha256":"41654fe12e1727f074ea5fed2a207c90b0138ade5c88b26658c23b9b4574881c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:01:52.089021+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0002","text":"[I q. 75 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31447)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0003","text":"After considering the spiritual and corporeal creature, we must consider man, who is composed of spiritual and corporeal substance. And first, his nature; second, his production. Now the consideration of human nature pertains to the theologian on the part of the soul, but not on the part of the body, except according to the relation which the body has to the soul. And therefore the first consideration will concern the soul. And because, according to Dionysius, chapter XI of the *Celestial Hierarchy*, three things are found in spiritual substances, namely essence, power, and operation; first we shall consider those things which pertain to the essence of the soul; second, those which pertain to its power or powers; third, those which pertain to its operation. Concerning the first, a twofold consideration arises, the first concerning the soul itself according to itself; the second concerning its union with the body. Concerning the first, seven questions are asked.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0004","text":"First, whether the soul is a body. Second, whether the human soul is something subsistent. Third, whether the souls of brute animals are subsistent. Fourth, whether the soul is man; or rather whether man is something composed of soul and body. Fifth, whether it is composed of matter and form. Sixth, whether the human soul is incorruptible. Seventh, whether the soul is of the same species as an angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0005","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0006","text":"[I q. 75 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31448)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0007","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the soul is a body. For the soul is the mover of the body. But that which is not moved is not a mover. First, because it seems that nothing can move unless it is moved, since nothing gives another what it does not have, just as what is not hot does not heat. Further, because, if something is a mover not moved, it causes a motion that is everlasting and disposed in the same way, as is proved in *Physics* VIII; but this does not appear in animal motion, which is from the soul. Therefore the soul is a moved mover. But every moved mover is a body. Therefore the soul is a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0008","text":"[I q. 75 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31449)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0009","text":"Moreover, all cognition takes place through some likeness. But there cannot be a likeness of a body to an incorporeal thing. Therefore, if the soul were not a body, it could not know corporeal things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0010","text":"[I q. 75 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31450)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0011","text":"Moreover, there must be some contact of the mover with what is moved. But contact belongs only to bodies. Therefore, since the soul moves the body, it seems that the soul is a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0012","text":"[I q. 75 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31451)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0013","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says in *On the Trinity*, VI, that *the soul is called simple in relation to the body, because it is not spread out by bulk through the space of a place*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0014","text":"[I q. 75 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31452)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0015","text":"I answer that, in order to inquire into the nature of the soul, one must presuppose that the soul is said to be the first principle of life in those things which live among us: for we call living things animate, whereas inanimate things lack life. Now life is manifested most of all by two works, namely, cognition and motion. The ancient philosophers, unable to transcend imagination, held that the principle of these was some body, saying that bodies alone are things, and that what is not a body is nothing. And accordingly, they said that the soul was some body. Although the falsity of this opinion can be shown in many ways, we shall nevertheless use one [argument] by which it is both more commonly and more certainly clear that the soul is not a body. For it is manifest that not every principle of a vital operation is the soul; for thus the eye would be the soul, since it is a certain principle of vision; and the same would have to be said of the other instruments of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0016","text":"But we say that the soul is the first principle of life. Although some body can be a certain principle of life, just as the heart is a principle of life in an animal, nevertheless no body can be the first principle of life. For it is manifest that being a principle of life, or living, does not belong to a body by virtue of its being a body; otherwise every body would be living or a principle of life. Therefore, it belongs to a certain body that it be living, or even a principle of life, by virtue of its being such a body. But whatever is actually such has this from some principle which is called its act. Therefore the soul, which is the first principle of life, is not a body, but an act of a body; just as heat, which is a principle of heating, is not a body, but a certain act of a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0017","text":"[I q. 75 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31453)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0018","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, since everything moved is moved by another, and this cannot proceed to infinity, it is necessary to say that not every mover is moved. For since to be moved is to pass from potency into act, the mover gives to the movable what it has, insofar as it makes it to be in act. But, as is shown in *Physics* VIII, there is a certain mover entirely immobile, which is moved neither through itself nor accidentally; and such a mover can cause motion always uniform. But there is another mover which is not moved through itself, but is moved accidentally; and for this reason it does not cause motion always uniform. And such a mover is the soul. There is, however, another mover which is moved through itself, namely, a body. And because the ancient natural philosophers believed that nothing existed except bodies, they held that every mover is moved, and that the soul is moved through itself and is a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0019","text":"[I q. 75 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31454)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0020","text":"To the second, it must be said that it is not necessary for the likeness of the known thing to be actually in the nature of the knower; but if there is something which is first knowing potentially and afterward actually, it is necessary that the likeness of the known not be actually in the nature of the knower, but only potentially; just as color is not actually in the pupil, but only potentially. Hence it is not necessary that the likeness of corporeal things be actually in the nature of the soul, but [only] that it be in potency to such likenesses. But because the ancient natural philosophers did not know how to distinguish between act and potency, they held the soul to be a body, in order that it might know body; and, in order that it might know all bodies, that it was composed of the principles of all bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0021","text":"[I q. 75 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31455)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0022","text":"To the third, it must be said that there is a twofold contact: of quantity and of power. In the first way, a body is touched only by a body. In the second way, a body can be touched by an incorporeal thing that moves the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0023","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0024","text":"[I q. 75 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31456)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0025","text":"We proceed to the second point as follows. It seems that the human soul is not something subsistent. For that which is subsistent is called a “this something.” But the soul is not a “this something,” but rather the composite of soul and body is. Therefore the soul is not something subsistent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0026","text":"[I q. 75 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31457)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0027","text":"Further, everything that is subsistent can be said to act. But the soul is not said to act, because, as is said in *On the Soul* I, *to say that the soul senses or understands is like saying that it weaves or builds*. Therefore the soul is not something subsistent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0028","text":"[I q. 75 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31458)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0029","text":"Further, if the soul were something subsistent, it would have some operation without the body. But it has no operation without the body, not even understanding, since it is not possible to understand without a phantasm, and a phantasm is not without a body. Therefore the human soul is not something subsistent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0030","text":"[I q. 75 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31459)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0031","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity* X: *Whoever sees the nature of the mind to be both a substance and not corporeal sees that those who suppose it to be corporeal err for this reason: that they join to it those things without which they cannot think of any nature, namely, phantasms of bodies.* Therefore the nature of the human mind is not only incorporeal, but also a substance, namely, something subsistent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0032","text":"[I q. 75 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31460)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0033","text":"I answer that it must be said that that which is the principle of intellectual operation, which we call the soul of man, is a certain incorporeal and subsistent principle. For it is evident that through the intellect man can know the natures of all bodies. But that which can know certain things must have none of them in its nature, because that which were naturally present in it would impede the cognition of others; just as we see that the tongue of a sick person, infected with a choleric and bitter humor, cannot perceive anything sweet, but everything seems bitter to it. Therefore, if the intellectual principle had in itself the nature of any body, it could not know all bodies. But every body has some determinate nature. Therefore it is impossible for the intellectual principle to be a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0034","text":"And likewise, it is impossible that it should understand through a bodily organ, because even the determinate nature of that bodily organ would prevent cognition of all bodies; just as, if a determinate color is present not only in the pupil but also in a glass vessel, a liquid poured into it appears to be of the same color. Therefore the intellectual principle itself, which is called mind or intellect, has an operation through itself in which the body does not share. But nothing can act through itself unless it subsists through itself. For acting belongs only to a being in act; hence, a thing acts in the manner in which it is. For this reason we do not say that heat heats, but that what is hot heats. It remains, therefore, that the human soul, which is called intellect or mind, is something incorporeal and subsistent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0035","text":"[I q. 75 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31461)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0036","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that “this something” can be taken in two ways: in one way, for whatever subsistent thing; in another way, for what subsists complete in the nature of some species. In the first way, it excludes the inherence of an accident and of a material form; in the second way, it also excludes the imperfection of a part. Hence a hand could be called a “this something” in the first way, but not in the second way. Thus, therefore, since the human soul is a part of the human species, it can be called a “this something” in the first way, as subsisting, but not in the second way; for in this way the composite of soul and body is called a “this something.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0037","text":"[I q. 75 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31462)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0038","text":"To the second it must be said that Aristotle says those words not according to his own opinion, but according to the opinion of those who said that to understand is to be moved, as is clear from what he sets forth beforehand there. Or it must be said that to act through itself belongs to that which exists through itself. But that which exists through itself can sometimes be called something if it is not inherent as an accident or as a material form, even if it is a part. But that is called subsistent properly and through itself which neither is inherent in the aforesaid manner nor is a part. In this sense, an eye or a hand could not be called subsistent through itself, and consequently neither acting through itself. Hence the operations of parts are also attributed to the whole through the parts. For we say that a man sees through the eye and touches through the hand, otherwise than that what is hot heats through heat, because heat in no way heats, properly speaking.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0039","text":"Therefore it can be said that the soul understands, just as the eye sees, but more properly it is said that the man understands through the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0040","text":"[I q. 75 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31463)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0041","text":"To the third it must be said that the body is required for the action of the intellect, not as an organ by which such an action is exercised, but by reason of the object; for a phantasm is related to the intellect as color to sight. But to need a body in this way does not remove the intellect's being subsistent; otherwise an animal would not be something subsistent, since it needs external sensibles in order to sense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0042","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0043","text":"[I q. 75 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31464)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0044","text":"We proceed to the third point as follows. It seems that the souls of brute animals are subsistent. For man agrees in genus with other animals. But the soul of man is something subsistent, as has been shown. Therefore the souls of other animals are also subsistent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0045","text":"[I q. 75 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31465)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0046","text":"Further, the sensitive is related to sensibles in the same way as the intellective to intelligibles. But the intellect understands intelligibles without the body. Therefore sense also apprehends sensibles without the body. But the souls of brute animals are sensitive. Therefore they are subsistent, by the same reasoning as the soul of man, which is intellective.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0047","text":"[I q. 75 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31466)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0048","text":"Further, the soul of a brute animal moves the body. But the body does not move, but is moved. Therefore the soul of a brute animal has some operation without the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0049","text":"[I q. 75 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31467)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0050","text":"On the contrary, it is said in the book *On Ecclesiastical Dogmas*: *we believe that man alone has a subsistent soul; but the souls of animals are not subsistent*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0051","text":"[I q. 75 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31468)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0052","text":"I answer that it must be said that the ancient philosophers posited no distinction between sense and intellect, and attributed both to a corporeal principle, as has been said. Plato, however, distinguished between intellect and sense; yet he attributed both to an incorporeal principle, holding that, just as understanding, so also sensing belongs to the soul through itself. And from this it followed that even the souls of brute animals are subsistent. But Aristotle held that understanding alone, among the operations of the soul, is exercised without a corporeal organ. But sensing, and the consequent operations of the sensitive soul, manifestly occur with some alteration of the body; just as in seeing the pupil is altered by the species of color; and the same appears in the other cases. And thus it is manifest that the sensitive soul does not have any operation proper through itself, but every operation of the sensitive soul is of the composite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0053","text":"From this it remains that, since the souls of brute animals do not operate through themselves, they are not subsistent; for in like manner each thing has being and operation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0054","text":"[I q. 75 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31469)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0055","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that although man agrees in genus with the other animals, he nevertheless differs in species; but the difference of species is considered according to the difference of form. Nor is it necessary that every difference of form produce a diversity of genus.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0056","text":"[I q. 75 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31470)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0057","text":"To the second it must be said that the sensitive faculty is in a certain way related to sensibles as the intellective faculty is to intelligibles, namely insofar as each is in potency to its objects. But in a certain way they are related differently, insofar as the sensitive faculty is acted upon by the sensible with an alteration of the body; whence an excess of sensibles corrupts the sense. This does not occur in the intellect, for an intellect understanding the greatest intelligibles can afterward understand lesser ones more fully. But if the body becomes fatigued in understanding, this is accidental, insofar as the intellect needs the operation of the sensitive powers, through which phantasms are prepared for it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0058","text":"[I q. 75 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31471)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0059","text":"To the third it must be said that motive power is twofold. One commands motion, namely, the appetitive power. And its operation in the sensitive soul is not without the body; rather, anger and joy and all passions of this sort occur with some alteration of the body. The other motive power executes motion, through which the members are rendered fit to obey the appetite; its act is not to move, but to be moved. Hence it is clear that to move is not an act of the sensitive soul without the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0060","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0061","text":"[I q. 75 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31472)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0062","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that the soul is man. For it is said in 2 Corinthians 4: *although our outer man is corrupted, yet our inner man is renewed from day to day*. But that which is within man is the soul. Therefore the soul is the inner man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0063","text":"[I q. 75 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31473)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0064","text":"Further, the human soul is a certain substance. But it is not a universal substance. Therefore it is a particular substance. Therefore it is a hypostasis or person. But only a human one. Therefore the soul is man, for a human person is a man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0065","text":"[I q. 75 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31474)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0066","text":"On the contrary, Augustine, in XIX *On the City of God*, commends Varro, *who judged that man is neither the soul alone nor the body alone, but soul and body together*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0067","text":"[I q. 75 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31475)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0068","text":"I answer that it must be said that the soul's being man can be understood in two ways. In one way, that man is soul, but that this man is not soul, but a composite of soul and body—for example, Socrates. I say this because some have held that form alone belongs to the account of the species, whereas matter is a part of the individual and not of the species. This indeed cannot be true. For that which the definition signifies belongs to the nature of the species. But in natural things the definition signifies not form alone, but form and matter. Hence matter is a part of the species in natural things—not designated matter, which is the principle of individuation, but common matter. For just as it belongs to the account of this man that he is constituted from this soul and this flesh and these bones, so it belongs to the account of man that he is constituted from soul and flesh and bones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0069","text":"For whatever belongs commonly to the substance of all the individuals contained under a species must belong to the substance of the species. In another way, however, it can be understood thus: that even this soul is this man. And this could indeed be maintained if it were held that the operation of the sensitive soul is proper to it without the body, because all the operations attributed to man would belong to the soul alone; but each thing is that which performs the operations of that thing. Hence that which performs the operations of man is man. But it has been shown that sensing is not an operation of the soul alone. Therefore, since sensing is a certain operation of man, although not a proper one, it is manifest that man is not the soul alone, but is something composed of soul and body. Plato, however, by holding that sensing is proper to the soul, could hold that man was a soul using a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0070","text":"[I q. 75 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31476)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0071","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, according to the Philosopher in IX *Ethics*, that which is principal in a thing seems most of all to be the thing itself, just as what the ruler of a city does is said to be done by the city. And in this way, that which is principal in man is sometimes called man: sometimes indeed the intellective part, according to the truth of the matter, which is called the inner man; but sometimes the sensitive part together with the body, according to the estimation of some, who are occupied only with sensibles. And this is called the outer man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0072","text":"[I q. 75 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31477)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0073","text":"To the second, it must be said that not every particular substance is a hypostasis or person, but only that which has the complete nature of the species. Hence a hand or a foot cannot be called a hypostasis or person. Likewise, neither can the soul, since it is a part of the human species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0074","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0075","text":"[I q. 75 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31478)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0076","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth. It seems that the soul is composed of matter and form. For potency is divided against act. But all things whatsoever that are in act participate in the first act, which is God; by participation in whom all things are both good and beings and living, as is clear from the teaching of Dionysius in the book On the Divine Names. Therefore, whatever things are in potency participate in the first potency. But the first potency is prime matter. Therefore, since the human soul is in a certain way in potency, as appears from the fact that a human being is sometimes understanding in potency, it seems that the human soul participates in prime matter as a part of itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0077","text":"[I q. 75 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31479)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0078","text":"Further, wherever the properties of matter are found, there matter is found. But the properties of matter, namely to be subject and to be changed, are found in the soul; for it is subject to knowledge and virtue, and is changed from ignorance to knowledge and from vice to virtue. Therefore, there is matter in the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0079","text":"[I q. 75 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31480)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0080","text":"Further, those things which do not have matter do not have a cause of their being, as is said in VIII Metaphysics. But the soul has a cause of its being, because it is created by God. Therefore, the soul has matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0081","text":"[I q. 75 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31481)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0082","text":"Further, what does not have matter, but is form alone, is pure and infinite act. But this belongs to God alone. Therefore, the soul has matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0083","text":"[I q. 75 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31482)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0084","text":"On the contrary, Augustine proves, in VII On the Literal Meaning of Genesis, that the soul is made neither from bodily matter nor from spiritual matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0085","text":"[I q. 75 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31483)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0086","text":"I answer that it must be said that the soul does not have matter. And this can be considered in two ways. First, indeed, from the account of soul in general. For it belongs to the account of soul that it be the form of some body. Therefore, it is either form according to its whole self, or according to some part of itself. If according to its whole self, it is impossible that a part of it should be matter, if matter is said to be some being in potency only; because form, insofar as it is form, is act. But that which is in potency only cannot be a part of act, since potency is opposed to act, as being divided against act. But if it is form according to some part of itself, we shall call that part soul, and we shall call the matter of which it is first the act the first animated thing. Second, especially from the account of the human soul, insofar as it is intellectual. For it is manifest that everything received in something is received in it according to the mode of the receiver.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0087","text":"Now each thing is known according as its form is in the knower. But the intellectual soul knows some thing in its nature absolutely, for example, a stone insofar as it is a stone absolutely. Therefore, the form of the stone absolutely, according to its proper formal account, is in the intellectual soul. Therefore, the intellectual soul is an absolute form, and not something composed of matter and form. For if the intellectual soul were composed of matter and form, the forms of things would be received in it as individual, and thus it would know only the singular, as happens in the sensitive powers, which receive the forms of things in a bodily organ; for matter is the principle of the individuation of forms. It remains, therefore, that the intellectual soul, and every intellectual substance knowing forms absolutely, lacks composition of form and matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0088","text":"[I q. 75 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31484)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0089","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the first act is the universal principle of all acts, because it is infinite, having all things prepossessed in itself virtually, as Dionysius says. Hence it is participated in by things, not as a part, but according to the outpouring of its procession. But potency, since it is receptive of act, must be proportioned to act. Now the received acts which proceed from the infinite first act and are certain participations of it are diverse. Hence there cannot be one potency that receives all acts, just as there is one act infusing all participated acts; otherwise, the receptive potency would equal the active potency of the first act. But the receptive potency in the intellectual soul is other than the receptive potency of prime matter, as is clear from the diversity of what is received; for prime matter receives individual forms, whereas the intellect receives absolute forms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0090","text":"Hence such a potency existing in the intellectual soul does not show that the soul is composed of matter and form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0091","text":"[I q. 75 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31485)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0092","text":"To the second, it must be said that to be subject and to be changed belong to matter according as it is in potency. Therefore, just as the potency of the intellect is other than the potency of prime matter, so there is another account of being subject and of being changed. For the intellect is subject to knowledge and is changed from ignorance to knowledge according as it is in potency with respect to intelligible species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0093","text":"[I q. 75 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31486)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0094","text":"To the third, it must be said that form is the cause of the being of matter, and the agent; hence the agent, insofar as it reduces matter to the act of form by changing it, is a cause of being for it. But if something is a subsisting form, it does not have being through any formal principle, nor does it have a cause changing it from potency to act. Hence, after the words set forth above, the Philosopher concludes that in those things which are composed of matter and form, *there is no other cause except that which moves from potency to act; but whatever things do not have matter are altogether that which truly is something*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0095","text":"[I q. 75 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31487)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0096","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that everything participated is related to the participant as its act. But whatever created subsisting form may be posited, it must participate in being, because even life itself, or whatever might be spoken of in this way, participates in being itself, as Dionysius says, chapter V of On the Divine Names. But participated being is limited to the capacity of the participant. Hence God alone, who is His own being, is pure and infinite act. But in intellectual substances there is composition from act and potency; not indeed from matter and form, but from form and participated being. Hence by some they are said to be composed of that by which a thing is and that which is; for being itself is that by which something is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0097","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0098","text":"[I q. 75 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31488)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0099","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth. It seems that the human soul is corruptible. For things whose principle and process are similar seem to have a similar end. But the principle of the generation of human beings and beasts of burden is similar, because they were made from the earth. Their course of life is likewise similar in both, because *all breathe alike, and man has nothing more than a beast of burden*, as is said in Ecclesiastes III. Therefore, as is concluded there, *there is one destruction of man and of beasts of burden, and the condition of both is equal*. But the soul of brute animals is corruptible. Therefore the human soul too is corruptible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0100","text":"[I q. 75 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31489)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0101","text":"Further, everything that is from nothing is changeable into nothing, because the end ought to correspond to the beginning. But, as is said in Wisdom II, *we were born from nothing*, which is true not only as regards the body, but also as regards the soul. Therefore, as is concluded there, after this we shall be as though we had not been, even according to the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0102","text":"[I q. 75 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31490)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0103","text":"Further, no thing is without its proper operation. But the proper operation of the soul, which is to understand with a phantasm, cannot be without a body; for the soul understands nothing without a phantasm, while a phantasm is not without a body, as is said in the book *On the Soul*. Therefore the soul cannot remain when the body is destroyed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0104","text":"[I q. 75 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31491)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0105","text":"But on the contrary, Dionysius says, in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, that human souls have from the divine goodness that they are intellectual and that they have an incorruptible substantial life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0106","text":"[I q. 75 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31492)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0107","text":"I answer that it must be said that the human soul, which we call the intellectual principle, is incorruptible. For a thing is corrupted in two ways: in one way, through itself; in another way, accidentally. Now it is impossible for anything subsisting to be generated or corrupted accidentally, that is, by something else being generated or corrupted. For generation and corruption belong to a thing in the same way as being does, which is acquired through generation and lost through corruption. Hence, what has being through itself cannot be generated or corrupted except through itself; whereas things that do not subsist, such as accidents and material forms, are said to come to be and to be corrupted through the generation and corruption of composites. But it was shown above that the souls of brute animals are not subsistent through themselves, but the human soul alone is. Hence the souls of brute animals are corrupted when their bodies are corrupted; but the human soul could not be corrupted unless it were corrupted through itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0108","text":"This is altogether impossible, not only in its case, but in the case of any subsisting thing that is a form only. For it is manifest that what belongs to something according to itself is inseparable from it. But being through itself belongs to form, which is act. Hence matter acquires actual being inasmuch as it acquires form, whereas corruption occurs in it inasmuch as form is separated from it. But it is impossible for a form to be separated from itself. Hence it is impossible for a subsisting form to cease to be. Even if the soul were composed of matter and form, as some say, it would still be necessary to hold it incorruptible. For corruption is found only where contrariety is found; for generations and corruptions are from contraries and into contraries. Hence the celestial bodies, because they do not have matter subject to contrariety, are incorruptible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0109","text":"But there can be no contrariety in the intellectual soul. For it receives according to the mode of its being, whereas the things received in it are without contrariety; because even the notions of contraries in the intellect are not contrary, but there is one knowledge of contraries. Therefore it is impossible that the intellectual soul should be corruptible. A sign of this may also be taken from the fact that everything naturally desires to be in its own manner. But desire in knowing things follows cognition. Now sense does not know being except under here and now, but the intellect apprehends being absolutely, and according to all time. Hence everything having intellect naturally desires always to be. But natural desire cannot be vain. Therefore every intellectual substance is incorruptible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0110","text":"[I q. 75 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31493)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0111","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Solomon puts forward that argument in the person of the foolish, as is expressed in Wisdom II. Therefore, what is said, that man and the other animals have a similar principle of generation, is true as regards the body; for all animals were likewise made from the earth. But it is not true as regards the soul, for the soul of brute animals is produced from some corporeal power, whereas the human soul is from God. And to signify this, it is said in Genesis, regarding the other animals, let the earth bring forth the living soul; but regarding man, it is said that He breathed into his face the breath of life. And therefore it is concluded in the last chapter of Ecclesiastes: *let the dust return to its earth, whence it was, and let the spirit return to God who gave it*. Likewise, the course of life is similar as regards the body; to which belongs what is said in Ecclesiastes, *all breathe alike*; and in Wisdom II, *smoke and breath are in our nostrils*, and so forth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0112","text":"But the process is not similar as regards the soul, because man understands, whereas brute animals do not. Hence it is false what is said, that man has nothing more than a beast of burden. And therefore the destruction is similar as regards the body, but not as regards the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0113","text":"[I q. 75 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31494)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0114","text":"To the second, it must be said that, just as a thing is said to be able to be created not through passive power, but only through the active power of the Creator, who can produce something from nothing; so, when something is said to be changeable into nothing, there is not implied in the creature a power toward not-being, but in the Creator a power not to impart being. But a thing is called corruptible by reason of there being in it a power toward not-being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0115","text":"[I q. 75 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31495)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0116","text":"To the third, it must be said that understanding with a phantasm is the proper operation of the soul insofar as it is united to the body. But when separated from the body, it will have another mode of understanding, similar to the other substances separated from body, as will appear more clearly below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0117","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0118","text":"[I q. 75 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31496)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0119","text":"We proceed thus to the seventh. It seems that the soul and an angel are of one species. For everything is ordered to its proper end through the nature of its species, through which it has an inclination toward the end. But the end of the soul and of an angel is the same, namely eternal beatitude. Therefore they are of one species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0120","text":"[I q. 75 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31497)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0121","text":"Moreover, the ultimate specific difference is most noble, because it completes the account of the species. But nothing is more noble in an angel and a soul than being intellectual. Therefore, the soul and the angel agree in their ultimate specific difference. Therefore, they are of one species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0122","text":"[I q. 75 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31498)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0123","text":"Moreover, the soul does not seem to differ from an angel except in this, that it is united to a body. But the body, since it is outside the essence of the soul, does not seem to pertain to its species. Therefore, the soul and the angel are of one species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0124","text":"[I q. 75 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31499)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0125","text":"On the contrary, things whose natural operations are diverse themselves differ in species. But the soul and the angel have diverse natural operations, because, as Dionysius says in chapter VII of *On the Divine Names*, *the angelic minds have simple and blessed intellects, not gathering divine cognition from visible things*; and afterward he says the contrary concerning the soul. Therefore, the soul and the angel are not of one species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0126","text":"[I q. 75 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31500)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0127","text":"I answer that it must be said that Origen held that all human souls and angels are of one species. And this was because he held that the diversity of rank found in such substances is accidental, as proceeding from free choice, as was said above. But this cannot be, because in incorporeal substances there cannot be diversity according to number without diversity according to species, and without natural inequality. For if they are not composed of matter and form, but are subsistent forms, it is manifest that there must be diversity in species among them. For it cannot be understood that there should be more than one separate form of one species; just as, if there were separate whiteness, there could be only one. For this whiteness differs from that one only by being that of this thing or of that thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0128","text":"But diversity according to species always has natural diversity accompanying it, just as among the species of colors one is more perfect than another, and likewise among other things. And this is because the differences dividing a genus are contraries; but contraries are related according to the perfect and the imperfect, because the principle of contrariety is privation and possession, as is said in *Metaphysics* X. The same would also follow if substances of this kind were composed of matter and form. For if the matter of this one is distinguished from the matter of that one, it is necessary either that form be the principle distinguishing matter, namely, that the matters are diverse on account of their relation to diverse forms; and then diversity according to species and natural inequality still follow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0129","text":"Or matter will be the principle distinguishing forms; nor can this matter be said to be other than that matter except according to quantitative division, which has no place in incorporeal substances, such as an angel and a soul. Hence it cannot be that an angel and a soul are of one species. But how there are several souls of one species will be shown below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0130","text":"[I q. 75 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31501)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0131","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that argument proceeds concerning the proximate and natural end. But eternal beatitude is the ultimate and supernatural end.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0132","text":"[I q. 75 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31502)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0133","text":"To the second, it must be said that the ultimate specific difference is most noble insofar as it is most determinate, in the way in which act is nobler than potency. But in this way the intellectual is not most noble, because it is indeterminate and common to many grades of intellectuality, just as the sensible is common to many grades in sensible being. Hence, just as not all sensible things are of one species, so neither are all intellectual things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0134","text":"[I q. 75 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31503)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0135","text":"To the third, it must be said that the body is not of the essence of the soul, but the soul, from the nature of its essence, has it that it can be united to a body. Hence, properly speaking, the soul is not in a species; rather, the composite is. And this very fact, that the soul in some way needs a body for its operation, shows that the soul occupies a lower grade of intellectuality than an angel, which is not united to a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_67:P0135"]}],"source_digest":"2cd598f5f5ea1243d30a5cd1c7f38189a5087ff8b7f1378299a44f382a938654","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 75"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 75","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68","title":"First Part · Question 76","order":77,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_68","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 76 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31504)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de unione animae ad corpus. Et circa hoc quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum intellectivum principium uniatur corpori ut forma. Secundo, utrum intellectivum principium numero multiplicetur secundum multiplicationem corporum; vel sit unus intellectus omnium hominum. Tertio, utrum in corpore cuius forma est principium intellectivum, sit aliqua alia anima. Quarto, utrum sit in eo aliqua alia forma substantialis. Quinto, quale debeat esse corpus cuius intellectivum principium est forma. Sexto, utrum tali corpori uniatur mediante aliquo alio corpore. Septimo, utrum mediante aliquo accidente. Octavo, utrum anima sit tota in qualibet parte corporis.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 76 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31505)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectivum principium non uniatur corpori ut forma. Dicit enim philosophus, in III de anima, quod intellectus est separatus, et quod nullius corporis est actus. Non ergo unitur corpori ut forma.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31506)\n\nPraeterea, omnis forma determinatur secundum naturam materiae cuius est forma, alioquin non requireretur proportio inter materiam et formam. Si ergo intellectus uniretur corpori ut forma, cum omne corpus habeat determinatam naturam, sequeretur quod intellectus haberet determinatam naturam. Et sic non esset omnium cognoscitivus, ut ex superioribus patet, quod est contra rationem intellectus. Non ergo intellectus unitur corpori ut forma.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31507)\n\nPraeterea, quaecumque potentia receptiva est actus alicuius corporis, recipit formam materialiter et individualiter, quia receptum est in recipiente secundum modum recipientis. Sed forma rei intellectae non recipitur in intellectu materialiter et individualiter, sed magis immaterialiter et universaliter, alioquin intellectus non esset cognoscitivus immaterialium et universalium, sed singularium tantum, sicut et sensus. Intellectus ergo non unitur corpori ut forma.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31508)\n\nPraeterea, eiusdem est potentia et actio, idem enim est quod potest agere, et quod agit. Sed actio intellectualis non est alicuius corporis, ut ex superioribus patet. Ergo nec potentia intellectiva est alicuius corporis potentia. Sed virtus sive potentia non potest esse abstractior vel simplicior quam essentia a qua virtus vel potentia derivatur. Ergo nec substantia intellectus est corporis forma.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31509)\n\nPraeterea, id quod per se habet esse, non unitur corpori ut forma, quia forma est quo aliquid est; et sic ipsum esse formae non est ipsius formae secundum se. Sed intellectivum principium habet secundum se esse, et est subsistens, ut supra dictum est. Non ergo unitur corpori ut forma.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 1 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31510)\n\nPraeterea, id quod inest alicui rei secundum se, semper inest ei. Sed formae secundum se inest uniri materiae, non enim per accidens aliquod, sed per essentiam suam est actus materiae; alioquin ex materia et forma non fieret unum substantialiter, sed accidentaliter. Forma ergo non potest esse sine propria materia. Sed intellectivum principium, cum sit incorruptibile, ut supra ostensum est, remanet corpori non unitum, corpore corrupto. Ergo intellectivum principium non unitur corpori ut forma.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31511)\n\nSed contra, secundum philosophum, in VIII Metaphys., differentia sumitur a forma rei. Sed differentia constitutiva hominis est rationale; quod dicitur de homine ratione intellectivi principii. Intellectivum ergo principium est forma hominis.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31512)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere quod intellectus, qui est intellectualis operationis principium, sit humani corporis forma. Illud enim quo primo aliquid operatur, est forma eius cui operatio attribuitur, sicut quo primo sanatur corpus, est sanitas, et quo primo scit anima, est scientia; unde sanitas est forma corporis, et scientia animae. Et huius ratio est, quia nihil agit nisi secundum quod est actu, unde quo aliquid est actu, eo agit. Manifestum est autem quod primum quo corpus vivit, est anima. Et cum vita manifestetur secundum diversas operationes in diversis gradibus viventium, id quo primo operamur unumquodque horum operum vitae, est anima, anima enim est primum quo nutrimur, et sentimus, et movemur secundum locum; et similiter quo primo intelligimus. Hoc ergo principium quo primo intelligimus, sive dicatur intellectus sive anima intellectiva, est forma corporis. Et haec est demonstratio Aristotelis in II de anima. Si quis autem velit dicere animam intellectivam non esse corporis formam, oportet quod inveniat modum quo ista actio quae est intelligere, sit huius hominis actio, experitur enim unusquisque seipsum esse qui intelligit. Attribuitur autem aliqua actio alicui tripliciter, ut patet per philosophum, V Physic., dicitur enim movere aliquid aut agere vel secundum se totum, sicut medicus sanat; aut secundum partem, sicut homo videt per oculum; aut per accidens, sicut dicitur quod album aedificat, quia accidit aedificatori esse album. Cum igitur dicimus Socratem aut Platonem intelligere, manifestum est quod non attribuitur ei per accidens, attribuitur enim ei inquantum est homo, quod essentialiter praedicatur de ipso. Aut ergo oportet dicere quod Socrates intelligit secundum se totum, sicut Plato posuit, dicens hominem esse animam intellectivam, aut oportet dicere quod intellectus sit aliqua pars Socratis. Et primum quidem stare non potest, ut supra ostensum est, propter hoc quod ipse idem homo est qui percipit se et intelligere et sentire, sentire autem non est sine corpore, unde oportet corpus aliquam esse hominis partem. Relinquitur ergo quod intellectus quo Socrates intelligit, est aliqua pars Socratis ita quod intellectus aliquo modo corpori Socratis uniatur. Hanc autem unionem Commentator, in III de anima, dicit esse per speciem intelligibilem. Quae quidem habet duplex subiectum, unum scilicet intellectum possibilem; et aliud ipsa phantasmata quae sunt in organis corporeis. Et sic per speciem intelligibilem continuatur intellectus possibilis corpori huius vel illius hominis. Sed ista continuatio vel unio non sufficit ad hoc quod actio intellectus sit actio Socratis. Et hoc patet per similitudinem in sensu, ex quo Aristoteles procedit ad considerandum ea quae sunt intellectus. Sic enim se habent phantasmata ad intellectum, ut dicitur in III de anima, sicut colores ad visum. Sicut ergo species colorum sunt in visu, ita species phantasmatum sunt in intellectu possibili. Patet autem quod ex hoc quod colores sunt in pariete, quorum similitudines sunt in visu, actio visus non attribuitur parieti, non enim dicimus quod paries videat, sed magis quod videatur. Ex hoc ergo quod species phantasmatum sunt in intellectu possibili, non sequitur quod Socrates, in quo sunt phantasmata, intelligat; sed quod ipse, vel eius phantasmata intelligantur. Quidam autem dicere voluerunt quod intellectus unitur corpori ut motor; et sic ex intellectu et corpore fit unum, ut actio intellectus toti attribui possit. Sed hoc est multipliciter vanum. Primo quidem, quia intellectus non movet corpus nisi per appetitum, cuius motus praesupponit operationem intellectus. Non ergo quia movetur Socrates ab intellectu, ideo intelligit, sed potius e converso, quia intelligit, ideo ab intellectu movetur Socrates. Secundo quia, cum Socrates sit quoddam individuum in natura cuius essentia est una, composita ex materia et forma; si intellectus non sit forma eius, sequitur quod sit praeter essentiam eius; et sic intellectus comparabitur ad totum Socratem sicut motor ad motum. Intelligere autem est actio quiescens in agente, non autem transiens in alterum, sicut calefactio. Non ergo intelligere potest attribui Socrati propter hoc quod est motus ab intellectu. Tertio, quia actio motoris nunquam attribuitur moto nisi sicut instrumento, sicut actio carpentarii serrae. Si igitur intelligere attribuitur Socrati quia est actio motoris eius, sequitur quod attribuatur ei sicut instrumento. Quod est contra philosophum, qui vult quod intelligere non sit per instrumentum corporeum. Quarto quia, licet actio partis attribuatur toti, ut actio oculi homini; nunquam tamen attribuitur alii parti, nisi forte per accidens, non enim dicimus quod manus videat, propter hoc quod oculus videt. Si ergo ex intellectu et Socrate dicto modo fit unum, actio intellectus non potest attribui Socrati. Si vero Socrates est totum quod componitur ex unione intellectus ad reliqua quae sunt Socratis, et tamen intellectus non unitur aliis quae sunt Socratis nisi sicut motor; sequitur quod Socrates non sit unum simpliciter, et per consequens nec ens simpliciter; sic enim aliquid est ens, quomodo et unum. Relinquitur ergo solus modus quem Aristoteles ponit, quod hic homo intelligit, quia principium intellectivum est forma ipsius. Sic ergo ex ipsa operatione intellectus apparet quod intellectivum principium unitur corpori ut forma. Potest etiam idem manifestari ex ratione speciei humanae. Natura enim uniuscuiusque rei ex eius operatione ostenditur. Propria autem operatio hominis, inquantum est homo, est intelligere, per hanc enim omnia animalia transcendit. Unde et Aristoteles, in libro Ethic., in hac operatione, sicut in propria hominis, ultimam felicitatem constituit. Oportet ergo quod homo secundum illud speciem sortiatur, quod est huius operationis principium. Sortitur autem unumquodque speciem per propriam formam. Relinquitur ergo quod intellectivum principium sit propria hominis forma. Sed considerandum est quod, quanto forma est nobilior, tanto magis dominatur materiae corporali, et minus ei immergitur, et magis sua operatione vel virtute excedit eam. Unde videmus quod forma mixti corporis habet aliquam operationem quae non causatur ex qualitatibus elementaribus. Et quanto magis proceditur in nobilitate formarum, tanto magis invenitur virtus formae materiam elementarem excedere, sicut anima vegetabilis plus quam forma metalli, et anima sensibilis plus quam anima vegetabilis. Anima autem humana est ultima in nobilitate formarum. Unde intantum sua virtute excedit materiam corporalem, quod habet aliquam operationem et virtutem in qua nullo modo communicat materia corporalis. Et haec virtus dicitur intellectus. Est autem attendendum quod, si quis poneret animam componi ex materia et forma, nullo modo posset dicere animam esse formam corporis. Cum enim forma sit actus, materia vero sit ens in potentia tantum; nullo modo id quod est ex materia et forma compositum, potest esse alterius forma secundum se totum. Si autem secundum aliquid sui sit forma, id quod est forma dicimus animam, et id cuius est forma dicimus primum animatum, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31513)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut philosophus dicit in II Physic., ultima formarum naturalium, ad quam terminatur consideratio philosophi naturalis, scilicet anima humana, est quidem separata, sed tamen in materia; quod ex hoc probat, quia homo ex materia generat hominem, et sol. Separata quidem est secundum virtutem intellectivam, quia virtus intellectiva non est virtus alicuius organi corporalis, sicut virtus visiva est actus oculi intelligere enim est actus qui non potest exerceri per organum corporale, sicut exercetur visio. Sed in materia est inquantum ipsa anima cuius est haec virtus, est corporis forma, et terminus generationis humanae. Sic ergo philosophus dicit in III de anima quod intellectus est separatus, quia non est virtus alicuius organi corporalis.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31514)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ad secundum et tertium. Sufficit enim ad hoc quod homo possit intelligere omnia per intellectum, et ad hoc quod intellectus intelligat immaterialia et universalia, quod virtus intellectiva non est corporis actus.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31515)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod humana anima non est forma in materia corporali immersa, vel ab ea totaliter comprehensa, propter suam perfectionem. Et ideo nihil prohibet aliquam eius virtutem non esse corporis actum; quamvis anima secundum suam essentiam sit corporis forma.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31516)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod anima illud esse in quo ipsa subsistit, communicat materiae corporali, ex qua et anima intellectiva fit unum, ita quod illud esse quod est totius compositi, est etiam ipsius animae. Quod non accidit in aliis formis, quae non sunt subsistentes. Et propter hoc anima humana remanet in suo esse, destructo corpore, non autem aliae formae.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 1 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31517)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod secundum se convenit animae corpori uniri, sicut secundum se convenit corpori levi esse sursum. Et sicut corpus leve manet quidem leve cum a loco proprio fuerit separatum, cum aptitudine tamen et inclinatione ad proprium locum; ita anima humana manet in suo esse cum fuerit a corpore separata, habens aptitudinem et inclinationem naturalem ad corporis unionem.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 76 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31518)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectivum principium non multiplicetur secundum multiplicationem corporum, sed sit unus intellectus in omnibus hominibus. Nulla enim substantia immaterialis multiplicatur secundum numerum in una specie. Anima autem humana est substantia immaterialis, non enim est composita ex materia et forma, ut supra ostensum est. Non ergo sunt multae in una specie. Sed omnes homines sunt unius speciei. Est ergo unus intellectus omnium hominum.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31519)\n\nPraeterea, remota causa, removetur effectus. Si ergo secundum multiplicationem corporum multiplicarentur animae humanae, consequens videretur quod, remotis corporibus, multitudo animarum non remaneret, sed ex omnibus animabus remaneret aliquod unum solum. Quod est haereticum, periret enim differentia praemiorum et poenarum.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31520)\n\nPraeterea, si intellectus meus est alius ab intellectu tuo, intellectus meus est quoddam individuum, et similiter intellectus tuus, particularia enim sunt quae differunt numero et conveniunt in una specie. Sed omne quod recipitur in aliquo, est in eo per modum recipientis. Ergo species rerum in intellectu meo et tuo reciperentur individualiter, quod est contra rationem intellectus, qui est cognoscitivus universalium.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31521)\n\nPraeterea, intellectum est in intellectu intelligente. Si ergo intellectus meus est alius ab intellectu tuo, oportet quod aliud sit intellectum a me, et aliud sit intellectum a te. Et ita erit individualiter numeratum, et intellectum in potentia tantum, et oportebit abstrahere intentionem communem ab utroque, quia a quibuslibet diversis contingit abstrahere aliquod commune intelligibile. Quod est contra rationem intellectus, quia sic non videretur distingui intellectus a virtute imaginativa. Videtur ergo relinqui quod sit unus intellectus omnium hominum.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31522)\n\nPraeterea, cum discipulus accipit scientiam a magistro, non potest dici quod scientia magistri generet scientiam in discipulo, quia sic etiam scientia esset forma activa, sicut calor; quod patet esse falsum. Videtur ergo quod eadem numero scientia quae est in magistro, communicetur discipulo. Quod esse non potest, nisi sit unus intellectus utriusque. Videtur ergo quod sit unus intellectus discipuli et magistri; et per consequens omnium hominum.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 2 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31523)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, in libro de quantitate animae, *si plures tantum animas humanas dixerim, ipse me ridebo*. Sed maxime videtur anima esse una quantum ad intellectum. Ergo est unus intellectus omnium hominum.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31524)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in II Physic., quod sicut se habent causae universales ad universalia, ita se habent causae particulares ad particularia. Sed impossibile est quod una anima secundum speciem, sit diversorum animalium secundum speciem. Ergo impossibile est quod anima intellectiva una numero, sit diversorum secundum numerum.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31525)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod intellectum esse unum omnium hominum, omnino est impossibile. Et hoc quidem patet, si, secundum Platonis sententiam homo sit ipse intellectus. Sequeretur enim, si Socratis et Platonis est unus intellectus tantum, quod Socrates et Plato sint unus homo; et quod non distinguantur ab invicem nisi per hoc quod est extra essentiam utriusque. Et erit tunc distinctio Socratis et Platonis non alia quam hominis tunicati et cappati, quod est omnino absurdum. Similiter etiam patet hoc esse impossibile, si, secundum sententiam Aristotelis, intellectus ponatur pars, seu potentia, animae quae est hominis forma. Impossibile est enim plurium numero diversorum esse unam formam, sicut impossibile est quod eorum sit unum esse, nam forma est essendi principium. Similiter etiam patet hoc esse impossibile quocumque modo quis ponat unionem intellectus ad hunc et ad illum hominem. Manifestum est enim quod, si sit unum principale agens et duo instrumenta, dici poterit unum agens simpliciter, sed plures actiones, sicut si unus homo tangat diversa duabus manibus, erit unus tangens, sed duo tactus. Si vero e converso instrumentum sit unum et principales agentes diversi, dicentur quidem plures agentes, sed una actio, sicut si multi uno fune trahant navem, erunt multi trahentes, sed unus tractus. Si vero agens principale sit unum et instrumentum unum, dicetur unum agens et una actio, sicut cum faber uno martello percutit, est unus percutiens et una percussio. Manifestum est autem quod, qualitercumque intellectus seu uniatur seu copuletur huic vel illi homini, intellectus inter cetera quae ad hominem pertinent, principalitatem habet, obediunt enim vires sensitivae intellectui, et ei deserviunt. Si ergo poneretur quod essent plures intellectus et sensus unus duorum hominum, puta si duo homines haberent unum oculum; essent quidem plures videntes, sed una visio. Si vero intellectus est unus, quantumcumque diversificentur alia quibus omnibus intellectus utitur quasi instrumentis, nullo modo Socrates et Plato poterunt dici nisi unus intelligens. Et si addamus quod ipsum intelligere, quod est actio intellectus, non fit per aliquod aliud organum, nisi per ipsum intellectum; sequetur ulterius quod sit et agens unum et actio una; idest quod omnes homines sint unus intelligens, et unum intelligere; dico autem respectu eiusdem intelligibilis. Posset autem diversificari actio intellectualis mea et tua per diversitatem phantasmatum, quia scilicet aliud est phantasma lapidis in me et aliud in te, si ipsum phantasma, secundum quod est aliud in me et aliud in te, esset forma intellectus possibilis, quia idem agens secundum diversas formas producit diversas actiones, sicut secundum diversas formas rerum respectu eiusdem oculi sunt diversae visiones. Sed ipsum phantasma non est forma intellectus possibilis, sed species intelligibilis quae a phantasmatibus abstrahitur. In uno autem intellectu a phantasmatibus diversis eiusdem speciei non abstrahitur nisi una species intelligibilis. Sicut in uno homine apparet, in quo possunt esse diversa phantasmata lapidis, et tamen ab omnibus eis abstrahitur una species intelligibilis lapidis, per quam intellectus unius hominis operatione una intelligit naturam lapidis, non obstante diversitate phantasmatum. Si ergo unus intellectus esset omnium hominum, diversitas phantasmatum quae sunt in hoc et in illo, non posset causare diversitatem intellectualis operationis huius et illius hominis, ut Commentator fingit in III de anima. Relinquitur ergo quod omnino impossibile et inconveniens est ponere unum intellectum omnium hominum.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31526)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, licet anima intellectiva non habeat materiam ex qua sit, sicut nec Angelus, tamen est forma materiae alicuius; quod Angelo non convenit. Et ideo secundum divisionem materiae sunt multae animae unius speciei, multi autem Angeli unius speciei omnino esse non possunt.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31527)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod unumquodque hoc modo habet unitatem, quo habet esse, et per consequens idem est iudicium de multiplicatione rei, et de esse ipsius. Manifestum est autem quod anima intellectualis, secundum suum esse, unitur corpori ut forma; et tamen, destructo corpore, remanet anima intellectualis in suo esse. Et eadem ratione multitudo animarum est secundum multitudinem corporum; et tamen, destructis corporibus, remanent animae in suo esse multiplicatae.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31528)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod individuatio intelligentis, aut speciei per quam intelligit, non excludit intelligentiam universalium, alioquin, cum intellectus separati sint quaedam substantiae subsistentes, et per consequens particulares, non possent universalia intelligere. Sed materialitas cognoscentis et speciei per quam cognoscitur, universalis cognitionem impedit. Sicut enim omnis actio est secundum modum formae qua agens agit, ut calefactio secundum modum caloris; ita cognitio est secundum modum speciei qua cognoscens cognoscit. Manifestum est autem quod natura communis distinguitur et multiplicatur secundum principia individuantia, quae sunt ex parte materiae. Si ergo forma per quam fit cognitio, sit materialis, non abstracta a conditionibus materiae, erit similitudo naturae speciei aut generis, secundum quod est distincta et multiplicata per principia individuantia, et ita non poterit cognosci natura rei in sua communitate. Si vero species sit abstracta a conditionibus materiae individualis, erit similitudo naturae absque iis quae ipsam distinguunt et multiplicant, et ita cognoscetur universale. Nec refert, quantum ad hoc, utrum sit unus intellectus vel plures, quia si etiam esset unus tantum, oporteret ipsum esse aliquem quendam, et speciem per quam intelligit esse aliquam quandam.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31529)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, sive intellectus sit unus sive plures, id quod intelligitur est unum. Id enim quod intelligitur non est in intellectu secundum se, sed secundum suam similitudinem, *lapis enim non est in anima, sed species lapidis*, ut dicitur in III de anima. Et tamen lapis est id quod intelligitur, non autem species lapidis, nisi per reflexionem intellectus supra seipsum, alioquin scientiae non essent de rebus, sed de speciebus intelligibilibus. Contingit autem eidem rei diversa secundum diversas formas assimilari. Et quia cognitio fit secundum assimilationem cognoscentis ad rem cognitam, sequitur quod idem a diversis cognoscentibus cognosci contingit, ut patet in sensu, nam plures vident eundem colorem, secundum diversas similitudines. Et similiter plures intellectus intelligunt unam rem intellectam. Sed hoc tantum interest inter sensum et intellectum, secundum sententiam Aristotelis, quod res sentitur secundum illam dispositionem quam extra animam habet, in sua particularitate, natura autem rei quae intelligitur, est quidem extra animam, sed non habet illum modum essendi extra animam, secundum quem intelligitur. Intelligitur enim natura communis seclusis principiis individuantibus; non autem hunc modum essendi habet extra animam. Sed secundum sententiam Platonis, res intellecta eo modo est extra animam quo intelligitur, posuit enim naturas rerum a materia separatas.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31530)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod scientia alia est in discipulo, et alia in magistro. Quomodo autem causetur, in sequentibus ostendetur.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 2 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31531)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod Augustinus intelligit animas non esse plures tantum, quin uniantur in una ratione speciei.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 76 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31532)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod praeter animam intellectivam sint in homine aliae animae per essentiam differentes, scilicet sensitiva et nutritiva. Corruptibile enim et incorruptibile non sunt unius substantiae. Sed anima intellectiva est incorruptibilis; aliae vero animae, scilicet sensitiva et nutritiva, sunt corruptibiles, ut ex superioribus patet. Ergo in homine non potest esse una essentia animae intellectivae et sensitivae et nutritivae.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31533)\n\nSi dicatur quod anima sensitiva in homine est incorruptibilis, *contra, corruptibile et incorruptibile differunt secundum genus*, ut dicitur in X Metaphys. Sed anima sensitiva in equo et leone et aliis brutis animalibus est corruptibilis. Si igitur in homine sit incorruptibilis, non erit eiusdem generis anima sensitiva in homine et bruto. Animal autem dicitur ex eo quod habet animam sensitivam. Neque ergo animal erit unum genus commune hominis et aliorum animalium. Quod est inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31534)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus dicit, in libro de Generat. Animal., quod embryo prius est animal quam homo. Sed hoc esse non posset, si esset eadem essentia animae sensitivae et intellectivae, est enim animal per animam sensitivam, homo vero per animam intellectivam. Non ergo in homine est una essentia animae sensitivae et intellectivae.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31535)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus dicit, in VIII Metaphys., quod genus sumitur a materia, differentia vero a forma. Sed rationale, quod est differentia constitutiva hominis, sumitur ab anima intellectiva; animal vero dicitur ex hoc quod habet corpus animatum anima sensitiva. Anima ergo intellectiva comparatur ad corpus animatum anima sensitiva, sicut forma ad materiam. Non ergo anima intellectiva est eadem per essentiam cum anima sensitiva in homine; sed praesupponit eam sicut materiale suppositum.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31536)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in libro de Eccles. Dogmat., *neque duas animas esse dicimus in homine uno, sicut Iacobus et alii Syrorum scribunt, unam animalem, qua animatur corpus, et immixta sit sanguini, et alteram spiritualem, quae rationi ministret, sed dicimus unam et eandem esse animam in homine, quae et corpus sua societate vivificat, et semetipsam sua ratione disponit*.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31537)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Plato posuit diversas animas esse in corpore uno, etiam secundum organa distinctas, quibus diversa opera vitae attribuebat; dicens vim nutritivam esse in hepate, concupiscibilem in corde, cognoscitivam in cerebro. Quam quidem opinionem Aristoteles reprobat, in libro de anima, quantum ad illas animae partes quae corporeis organis in suis operibus utuntur, ex hoc quod in animalibus quae decisa vivunt, in qualibet parte inveniuntur diversae operationes animae, sicut sensus et appetitus. Hoc autem non esset, si diversa principia operationum animae, tanquam per essentiam diversae, diversis partibus corporis distributa essent. Sed de intellectiva sub dubio videtur relinquere utrum sit separata ab aliis partibus animae solum ratione, an etiam loco. Opinio autem Platonis sustineri utique posset, si poneretur quod anima unitur corpori, non ut forma, sed ut motor, ut posuit Plato. Nihil enim inconveniens sequitur, si idem mobile a diversis motoribus moveatur, praecipue secundum diversas partes. Sed si ponamus animam corpori uniri sicut formam, omnino impossibile videtur plures animas per essentiam differentes in uno corpore esse. Quod quidem triplici ratione manifestari potest. Primo quidem, quia animal non esset simpliciter unum, cuius essent animae plures. Nihil enim est simpliciter unum nisi per formam unam, per quam habet res esse, ab eodem enim habet res quod sit ens et quod sit una; et ideo ea quae denominantur a diversis formis, non sunt unum simpliciter, sicut homo albus. Si igitur homo ab alia forma haberet quod sit vivum, scilicet ab anima vegetabili; et ab alia forma quod sit animal, scilicet ab anima sensibili; et ab alia quod sit homo, scilicet ab anima rationali; sequeretur quod homo non esset unum simpliciter, sicut et Aristoteles argumentatur contra Platonem, in VIII Metaphys., quod si alia esset idea animalis, et alia bipedis, non esset unum simpliciter animal bipes. Et propter hoc, in I de anima, contra ponentes diversas animas in corpore, inquirit quid contineat illas, idest quid faciat ex eis unum. Et non potest dici quod uniantur per corporis unitatem, quia magis anima continet corpus, et facit ipsum esse unum, quam e converso. Secundo, hoc apparet impossibile ex modo praedicationis. Quae enim sumuntur a diversis formis, praedicantur ad invicem vel per accidens, si formae non sint ad invicem ordinatae, puta cum dicimus quod album est dulce, vel, si formae sint ordinatae ad invicem, erit praedicatio per se, in secundo modo dicendi per se, quia subiectum ponitur in definitione praedicati. Sicut superficies praeambula est ad colorem, si ergo dicamus quod corpus superficiatum est coloratum, erit secundus modus praedicationis per se. Si ergo alia forma sit a qua aliquid dicitur animal, et a qua aliquid dicitur homo, sequeretur quod vel unum horum non possit praedicari de altero nisi per accidens, si istae duae formae ad invicem ordinem non habent; vel quod sit ibi praedicatio in secundo modo dicendi per se, si una animarum sit ad aliam praeambula. Utrumque autem horum est manifeste falsum, quia animal per se de homine praedicatur, non per accidens; homo autem non ponitur in definitione animalis, sed e converso. Ergo oportet eandem formam esse per quam aliquid est animal, et per quam aliquid est homo, alioquin homo non vere esset id quod est animal, ut sic animal per se de homine praedicetur. Tertio, apparet hoc esse impossibile per hoc, quod una operatio animae, cum fuerit intensa, impedit aliam. Quod nullo modo contingeret, nisi principium actionum esset per essentiam unum. Sic ergo dicendum quod eadem numero est anima in homine sensitiva et intellectiva et nutritiva. Quomodo autem hoc contingat, de facili considerari potest, si quis differentias specierum et formarum attendat. Inveniuntur enim rerum species et formae differre ab invicem secundum perfectius et minus perfectum, sicut in rerum ordine animata perfectiora sunt inanimatis, et animalia plantis, et homines animalibus brutis, et in singulis horum generum sunt gradus diversi. Et ideo Aristoteles, in VIII Metaphys., assimilat species rerum numeris, qui differunt specie secundum additionem vel subtractionem unitatis. Et in II de anima, comparat diversas animas speciebus figurarum, quarum una continet aliam; sicut pentagonum continet tetragonum, et excedit. Sic igitur anima intellectiva continet in sua virtute quidquid habet anima sensitiva brutorum, et nutritiva plantarum. Sicut ergo superficies quae habet figuram pentagonam, non per aliam figuram est tetragona, et per aliam pentagona; quia superflueret figura tetragona, ex quo in pentagona continetur; ita nec per aliam animam Socrates est homo, et per aliam animal, sed per unam et eandem.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31538)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod anima sensitiva non habet incorruptibilitatem ex hoc quod est sensitiva, sed ex hoc quod est intellectiva, ei incorruptibilitas debetur. Quando ergo anima est sensitiva tantum, corruptibilis est, quando vero cum sensitivo intellectivum habet, est incorruptibilis. Licet enim sensitivum incorruptionem non det, tamen incorruptionem intellectivo auferre non potest.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31539)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod formae non collocantur in genere vel in specie, sed composita. Homo autem corruptibilis est, sicut et alia animalia. Unde differentia secundum corruptibile et incorruptibile, quae est ex parte formarum, non facit hominem secundum genus ab aliis animalibus differre.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31540)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod prius embryo habet animam quae est sensitiva tantum; qua abiecta, advenit perfectior anima, quae est simul sensitiva et intellectiva; ut infra plenius ostendetur.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31541)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod non oportet secundum diversas rationes vel intentiones logicas, quae consequuntur modum intelligendi, diversitatem in rebus naturalibus accipere, quia ratio unum et idem secundum diversos modos apprehendere potest. Quia igitur, ut dictum est, anima intellectiva virtute continet id quod sensitiva habet, et adhuc amplius; potest seorsum ratio considerare quod pertinet ad virtutem sensitivae, quasi quoddam imperfectum et materiale. Et quia hoc invenit commune homini et aliis animalibus, ex hoc rationem generis format. Id vero in quo anima intellectiva sensitiva excedit, accipit quasi formale et completivum, et ex eo format differentiam hominis.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 76 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31542)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in homine sit alia forma praeter animam intellectivam. Dicit enim philosophus, in II de anima, quod *anima est actus corporis physici potentia vitam habentis*. Comparatur igitur anima ad corpus, sicut forma ad materiam. Sed corpus habet aliquam formam substantialem per quam est corpus. Ergo ante animam praecedit in corpore aliqua forma substantialis.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31543)\n\nPraeterea, homo et quodlibet animal est movens seipsum. Omne autem movens seipsum dividitur in duas partes, quarum una est movens, et alia est mota, ut probatur in VIII Physic. Pars autem movens est anima. Ergo oportet quod alia pars sit talis quae possit esse mota. Sed materia prima non potest moveri, ut dicitur in V Physic., cum sit ens solum in potentia, quinimmo omne quod movetur est corpus. Ergo oportet quod in homine et in quolibet animali sit alia forma substantialis, per quam constituatur corpus.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31544)\n\nPraeterea, ordo in formis attenditur secundum habitudinem ad materiam primam, prius enim et posterius dicitur per comparationem ad aliquod principium. Si ergo non esset in homine alia forma substantialis praeter animam rationalem, sed immediate materiae primae inhaereret; sequeretur quod esset in ordine imperfectissimarum formarum, quae immediate inhaerent materiae.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31545)\n\nPraeterea, corpus humanum est corpus mixtum. Mixtio autem non fit secundum materiam tantum, quia tunc esset corruptio sola. Oportet ergo quod remaneant formae elementorum in corpore mixto, quae sunt formae substantiales. Ergo in corpore humano sunt aliae formae substantiales praeter animam intellectivam.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31546)\n\nSed contra, unius rei est unum esse substantiale. Sed forma substantialis dat esse substantiale. Ergo unius rei est una tantum forma substantialis. Anima autem est forma substantialis hominis. Ergo impossibile est quod in homine sit aliqua alia forma substantialis quam anima intellectiva.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31547)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, si poneretur anima intellectiva non uniri corpori ut forma, sed solum ut motor, ut Platonici posuerunt; necesse esset dicere quod in homine esset alia forma substantialis, per quam corpus ab anima mobile in suo esse constitueretur. Sed si anima intellectiva unitur corpori ut forma substantialis, sicut supra iam diximus, impossibile est quod aliqua alia forma substantialis praeter eam inveniatur in homine. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod forma substantialis in hoc a forma accidentali differt quia forma accidentalis non dat esse simpliciter, sed esse tale, sicut calor facit suum subiectum non simpliciter esse, sed esse calidum. Et ideo cum advenit forma accidentalis, non dicitur aliquid fieri vel generari simpliciter, sed fieri tale aut aliquo modo se habens, et similiter cum recedit forma accidentalis, non dicitur aliquid corrumpi simpliciter, sed secundum quid. Forma autem substantialis dat esse simpliciter, et ideo per eius adventum dicitur aliquid simpliciter generari, et per eius recessum simpliciter corrumpi. Et propter hoc antiqui naturales, qui posuerunt materiam primam esse aliquod ens actu, puta ignem aut aerem aut aliquid huiusmodi, dixerunt quod nihil generatur aut corrumpitur simpliciter, sed omne fieri statuerunt alterari, ut dicitur in I Physic. Si igitur ita esset, quod praeter animam intellectivam praeexisteret quaecumque alia forma substantialis in materia, per quam subiectum animae esset ens actu; sequeretur quod anima non daret esse simpliciter; et per consequens quod non esset forma substantialis; et quod per adventum animae non esset generatio simpliciter, neque per eius abscessum corruptio simpliciter, sed solum secundum quid. Quae sunt manifeste falsa. Unde dicendum est quod nulla alia forma substantialis est in homine, nisi sola anima intellectiva; et quod ipsa, sicut virtute continet animam sensitivam et nutritivam, ita virtute continet omnes inferiores formas, et facit ipsa sola quidquid imperfectiores formae in aliis faciunt. Et similiter est dicendum de anima sensitiva in brutis, et de nutritiva in plantis, et universaliter de omnibus formis perfectioribus respectu imperfectiorum.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31548)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Aristoteles non dicit animam esse actum corporis tantum, sed actum corporis physici organici potentia vitam habentis, et quod talis potentia non abiicit animam. Unde manifestum est quod in eo cuius anima dicitur actus, etiam anima includitur; eo modo loquendi quo dicitur quod calor est actus calidi, et lumen est actus lucidi; non quod seorsum sit lucidum sine luce, sed quia est lucidum per lucem. Et similiter dicitur quod anima est actus corporis etc., quia per animam et est corpus, et est organicum, et est potentia vitam habens. Sed actus primus dicitur in potentia respectu actus secundi, qui est operatio. Talis enim potentia est non abiiciens, idest non excludens, animam.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31549)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod anima non movet corpus per esse suum, secundum quod unitur corpori ut forma; sed per potentiam motivam, cuius actus praesupponit iam corpus effectum in actu per animam; ut sic anima secundum vim motivam sit pars movens, et corpus animatum sit pars mota.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31550)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in materia considerantur diversi gradus perfectionis, sicut esse, vivere sentire et intelligere. Semper autem secundum superveniens priori, perfectius est. Forma ergo quae dat solum primum gradum perfectionis materiae, est imperfectissima, sed forma quae dat primum et secundum, et tertium, et sic deinceps, est perfectissima; et tamen materiae immediata.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31551)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod Avicenna posuit formas substantiales elementorum integras remanere in mixto, mixtionem autem fieri secundum quod contrariae qualitates elementorum reducuntur ad medium. Sed hoc est impossibile. Quia diversae formae elementorum non possunt esse nisi in diversis partibus materiae; ad quarum diversitatem oportet intelligi dimensiones, sine quibus materia divisibilis esse non potest. Materia autem dimensioni subiecta non invenitur nisi in corpore. Diversa autem corpora non possunt esse in eodem loco. Unde sequitur quod elementa sint in mixto distincta secundum situm. Et ita non erit vera mixtio, quae est secundum totum, sed mixtio ad sensum, quae est secundum minima iuxta se posita. Averroes autem posuit, in III de caelo, quod formae elementorum, propter sui imperfectionem, sunt mediae inter formas accidentales et substantiales; et ideo recipiunt magis et minus; et ideo remittuntur in mixtione et ad medium reducuntur, et conflatur ex eis una forma. Sed hoc est etiam magis impossibile. Nam esse substantiale cuiuslibet rei in indivisibili consistit; et omnis additio et subtractio variat speciem, sicut in numeris, ut dicitur in VIII Metaphys. Unde impossibile est quod forma substantialis quaecumque recipiat magis et minus. Nec minus est impossibile aliquid esse medium inter substantiam et accidens. Et ideo dicendum est, secundum philosophum in I de Generat., quod formae elementorum manent in mixto non actu, sed virtute. Manent enim qualitates propriae elementorum, licet remissae, in quibus est virtus formarum elementarium. Et huiusmodi qualitas mixtionis est propria dispositio ad formam substantialem corporis mixti, puta formam lapidis, vel animae cuiuscumque.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 76 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31552)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima intellectiva inconvenienter tali corpori uniatur. Materia enim debet esse proportionata formae. Sed anima intellectiva est forma incorruptibilis. Non ergo convenienter unitur corpori corruptibili.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31553)\n\nPraeterea, anima intellectiva est forma maxime immaterialis, cuius signum est, quod habet operationem in qua non communicat materia corporalis. Sed quanto corpus est subtilius, tanto minus habet de materia. Ergo anima deberet subtilissimo corpori uniri, puta igni; et non corpori mixto, et terrestri magis.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31554)\n\nPraeterea, cum forma sit principium speciei, ab una forma non proveniunt diversae species. Sed anima intellectiva est una forma. Ergo non debet uniri corpori quod componitur ex partibus dissimilium specierum.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31555)\n\nPraeterea, perfectioris formae debet esse perfectius susceptibile. Sed anima intellectiva est perfectissima animarum. Cum igitur aliorum animalium corpora habeant naturaliter insita tegumenta, puta pilorum loco vestium, et unguium loco calceamentorum; habeant etiam arma naturaliter sibi data, sicut ungues, dentes et cornua, ergo videtur quod anima intellectiva non debuerit uniri corpori imperfecto tanquam talibus auxiliis privato.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31556)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit philosophus, in II de anima, quod *anima est actus corporis physici organici potentia vitam habentis*.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31557)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum forma non sit propter materiam, sed potius materia propter formam; ex forma oportet rationem accipere quare materia sit talis, et non e converso. Anima autem intellectiva, sicut supra habitum est, secundum naturae ordinem, infimum gradum in substantiis intellectualibus tenet; intantum quod non habet naturaliter sibi inditam notitiam veritatis, sicut Angeli, sed oportet quod eam colligat ex rebus divisibilibus per viam sensus, ut Dionysius dicit, VII cap. de Div. Nom. Natura autem nulli deest in necessariis, unde oportuit quod anima intellectiva non solum haberet virtutem intelligendi, sed etiam virtutem sentiendi. Actio autem sensus non fit sine corporeo instrumento. Oportuit igitur animam intellectivam tali corpori uniri, quod possit esse conveniens organum sensus. Omnes autem alii sensus fundantur supra tactum. Ad organum autem tactus requiritur quod sit medium inter contraria, quae sunt calidum et frigidum, humidum et siccum, et similia, quorum est tactus apprehensivus, sic enim est in potentia ad contraria, et potest ea sentire. Unde quanto organum tactus fuerit magis reductum ad aequalitatem complexionis, tanto perceptibilior erit tactus. Anima autem intellectiva habet completissime virtutem sensitivam, quia quod est inferioris praeexistit perfectius in superiori ut dicit Dionysius in libro de Div. Nom. Unde oportuit corpus cui unitur anima intellectiva, esse corpus mixtum, inter omnia alia magis reductum ad aequalitatem complexionis. Et propter hoc homo inter omnia animalia melioris est tactus. Et inter ipsos homines, qui sunt melioris tactus, sunt melioris intellectus. Cuius signum est, quod molles carne bene aptos mente videmus, ut dicitur in II de anima.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31558)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod hanc obiectionem aliquis forte vellet evadere per hoc, quod diceret corpus hominis ante peccatum incorruptibile fuisse. Sed haec responsio non videtur sufficiens, quia corpus hominis ante peccatum immortale fuit non per naturam, sed per gratiae divinae donum; alioquin immortalitas eius per peccatum sublata non esset, sicut nec immortalitas Daemonis. Et ideo aliter dicendum est, quod in materia duplex conditio invenitur, una quae eligitur ad hoc quod sit conveniens formae; alia quae ex necessitate consequitur prioris dispositionis. Sicut artifex ad formam serrae eligit materiam ferream, aptam ad secandum dura; sed quod dentes serrae hebetari possint et rubiginem contrahere, sequitur ex necessitate materiae. Sic igitur et animae intellectivae debetur corpus quod sit aequalis complexionis, ex hoc autem de necessitate materiae sequitur quod sit corruptibile. Si quis vero dicat quod Deus potuit hanc necessitatem vitare, dicendum est quod in constitutione rerum naturalium non consideratur quid Deus facere possit, sed quid naturae rerum conveniat, ut Augustinus dicit, II super Gen. ad Litt. Providit tamen Deus adhibendo remedium contra mortem per gratiae donum.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31559)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod animae intellectivae non debetur corpus propter ipsam intellectualem operationem secundum se; sed propter sensitivam virtutem, quae requirit organum aequaliter complexionatum. Et ideo oportuit animam intellectivam tali corpori uniri, et non simplici elemento, vel corpori mixto in quo excederet ignis secundum quantitatem, quia non posset esse aequalitas complexionis, propter excedentem ignis activam virtutem. Habet autem hoc corpus aequaliter complexionatum quandam dignitatem, per hoc quod est remotum a contrariis; in quo quodammodo assimilatur corpori caelesti.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31560)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod partes animalis, ut oculus, manus, caro et os, et huiusmodi, non sunt in specie, sed totum, et ideo non potest dici, proprie loquendo, quod sint diversarum specierum, sed quod sint diversarum dispositionum. Et hoc competit animae intellectivae, quae quamvis sit una secundum essentiam, tamen propter sui perfectionem est multiplex in virtute; et ideo, ad diversas operationes, indiget diversis dispositionibus in partibus corporis cui unitur. Et propter hoc videmus quod maior est diversitas partium in animalibus perfectis quam in imperfectis, et in his quam in plantis.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31561)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod anima intellectiva, quia est universalium comprehensiva, habet virtutem ad infinita. Et ideo non potuerunt sibi determinari a natura vel determinatae existimationes naturales, vel etiam determinata auxilia vel defensionum vel tegumentorum; sicut aliis animalibus, quorum animae habent apprehensionem et virtutem ad aliqua particularia determinata. Sed loco horum omnium, homo habet naturaliter rationem, et manus, quae sunt organa organorum, quia per eas homo potest sibi praeparare instrumenta infinitorum modorum, et ad infinitos effectus.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 76 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31562)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima intellectiva uniatur corpori mediantibus aliquibus dispositionibus accidentalibus. Omnis enim forma est in materia sibi propria et disposita. Sed dispositiones ad formam sunt accidentia quaedam. Ergo oportet praeintelligi accidentia aliqua in materia ante formam substantialem, et ita ante animam, cum anima sit quaedam substantialis forma.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31563)\n\nPraeterea, diversae formae unius speciei requirunt diversas materiae partes. Partes autem materiae diversae non possunt intelligi nisi secundum divisionem dimensivarum quantitatum. Ergo oportet intelligere dimensiones in materia ante formas substantiales, quae sunt multae unius speciei.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31564)\n\nPraeterea, spirituale applicatur corporali per contactum virtutis. Virtus autem animae est eius potentia. Ergo videtur quod anima unitur corpori mediante potentia, quae est quoddam accidens.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31565)\n\nSed contra est quod accidens est posterius substantia et tempore et ratione, ut dicitur in VII Metaphys. Non ergo forma accidentalis aliqua potest intelligi in materia ante animam, quae est forma substantialis.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31566)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, si anima uniretur corpori solum ut motor, nihil prohiberet, immo magis necessarium esset esse aliquas dispositiones medias inter animam et corpus, potentiam scilicet ex parte animae, per quam moveret corpus; et aliquam habilitatem ex parte corporis, per quam corpus esset ab anima mobile. Sed si anima intellectiva unitur corpori ut forma substantialis, sicut iam supra dictum est, impossibile est quod aliqua dispositio accidentalis cadat media inter corpus et animam, vel inter quamcumque formam substantialem et materiam suam. Et huius ratio est quia, cum materia sit in potentia ad omnes actus ordine quodam, oportet quod id quod est primum simpliciter in actibus, primo in materia intelligatur. Primum autem inter omnes actus est esse. Impossibile est ergo intelligere materiam prius esse calidam vel quantam, quam esse in actu. Esse autem in actu habet per formam substantialem, quae facit esse simpliciter, ut iam dictum est. Unde impossibile est quod quaecumque dispositiones accidentales praeexistant in materia ante formam substantialem; et per consequens neque ante animam.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31567)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut ex praedictis patet, forma perfectior virtute continet quidquid est inferiorum formarum. Et ideo una et eadem existens, perficit materiam secundum diversos perfectionis gradus. Una enim et eadem forma est per essentiam, per quam homo est ens actu, et per quam est corpus, et per quam est vivum, et per quam est animal, et per quam est homo. Manifestum est autem quod unumquodque genus consequuntur propria accidentia. Sicut ergo materia praeintelligitur perfecta secundum esse ante intellectum corporeitatis, et sic de aliis; ita praeintelliguntur accidentia quae sunt propria entis, ante corporeitatem. Et sic praeintelliguntur dispositiones in materia ante formam, non quantum ad omnem eius effectum, sed quantum ad posteriorem.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31568)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod dimensiones quantitativae sunt accidentia consequentia corporeitatem, quae toti materiae convenit. Unde materia iam intellecta sub corporeitate et dimensionibus, potest intelligi ut distincta in diversas partes, ut sic accipiat diversas formas secundum ulteriores perfectionis gradus. Quamvis enim eadem forma sit secundum essentiam quae diversos perfectionis gradus materiae attribuit, ut dictum est; tamen secundum considerationem rationis differt.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31569)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod substantia spiritualis quae unitur corpori solum ut motor, unitur ei per potentiam vel virtutem. Sed anima intellectiva corpori unitur ut forma per suum esse. Administrat tamen ipsum et movet per suam potentiam et virtutem.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 76 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31570)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima uniatur corpori animalis mediante aliquo corpore. Dicit enim Augustinus, VII super Gen. ad Litt., quod anima per lucem, idest ignem, et aerem, *quae sunt similiora spiritui, corpus administrat*. Ignis autem et aer sunt corpora. Ergo anima unitur corpori humano mediante aliquo corpore.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31571)\n\nPraeterea, id quo subtracto solvitur unio aliquorum unitorum, videtur esse medium inter ea. Sed deficiente spiritu, anima a corpore separatur. Ergo spiritus, qui est quoddam corpus subtile, medium est in unione corporis et animae.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31572)\n\nPraeterea, ea quae sunt multum distantia, non uniuntur nisi per medium. Sed anima intellectiva distat a corpore et quia est incorporea, et quia est incorruptibilis. Ergo videtur quod uniatur ei mediante aliquo quod sit corpus incorruptibile. Et hoc videtur esse aliqua lux caelestis, quae conciliat elementa et redigit in unum.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31573)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in II de anima, quod *non oportet quaerere si unum est anima et corpus, sicut neque ceram et figuram*. Sed figura unitur cerae nullo corpore mediante. Ergo et anima corpori.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31574)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod si anima, secundum Platonicos, corpori uniretur solum ut motor, conveniens esset dicere quod inter animam hominis, vel cuiuscumque animalis, et corpus aliqua alia corpora media intervenirent, convenit enim motori aliquid distans per media magis propinqua movere. Si vero anima unitur corpori ut forma, sicut iam dictum est, impossibile est quod uniatur ei aliquo corpore mediante. Cuius ratio est, quia sic dicitur aliquid unum, quomodo et ens. Forma autem per seipsam facit rem esse in actu, cum per essentiam suam sit actus; nec dat esse per aliquod medium. Unde unitas rei compositae ex materia et forma est per ipsam formam, quae secundum seipsam unitur materiae ut actus eius. Nec est aliquid aliud uniens nisi agens, quod facit materiam esse in actu, ut dicitur in VIII Metaphys. Unde patet esse falsas opiniones eorum qui posuerunt aliqua corpora esse media inter animam et corpus hominis. Quorum quidam Platonici dixerunt quod anima intellectiva habet corpus incorruptibile sibi naturaliter unitum, a quo nunquam separatur, et eo mediante unitur corpori hominis corruptibili. Quidam vero dixerunt quod unitur corpori mediante spiritu corporeo. Alii vero dixerunt quod unitur corpori mediante luce, quam dicunt esse corpus, et de natura quintae essentiae, ita quod anima vegetabilis unitur corpori mediante luce caeli siderei; anima vero sensibilis, mediante luce caeli crystallini; anima vero intellectualis, mediante luce caeli Empyrei. Quod fictitium et derisibile apparet, tum quia lux non est corpus; tum quia quinta essentia non venit materialiter in compositionem corporis mixti, cum sit inalterabilis, sed virtualiter tantum; tum etiam quia anima immediate corpori unitur ut forma materiae.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31575)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus loquitur de anima inquantum movet corpus, unde utitur verbo administrationis. Et verum est quod partes grossiores corporis per subtiliores movet. Et primum instrumentum virtutis motivae est spiritus, ut dicit philosophus in libro de causa motus animalium.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31576)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, subtracto spiritu, deficit unio animae ad corpus, non quia sit medium; sed quia tollitur dispositio per quam corpus est dispositum ad talem unionem. Est tamen spiritus medium in movendo, sicut primum instrumentum motus.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31577)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod anima distat quidem a corpore plurimum, si utriusque conditiones seorsum considerentur, unde si utrumque ipsorum separatim esse haberet, oporteret quod multa media intervenirent. Sed inquantum anima est forma corporis, non habet esse seorsum ab esse corporis; sed per suum esse corpori unitur immediate. Sic enim et quaelibet forma, si consideretur ut actus, habet magnam distantiam a materia, quae est ens in potentia tantum.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 76 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31578)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima non sit tota in qualibet parte corporis. Dicit enim philosophus, in libro de causa motus animalium, *non opus est in unaquaque corporis parte esse animam; sed in quodam principio corporis existente, alia vivere; eo quod simul nata sunt facere proprium motum per naturam*.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31579)\n\nPraeterea, anima est in corpore cuius est actus. Sed est actus corporis organici. Ergo non est nisi in corpore organico. Sed non quaelibet pars corporis hominis est corpus organicum. Ergo anima non est in qualibet parte corporis tota.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31580)\n\nPraeterea, in II de anima dicitur quod sicut se habet pars animae ad partem corporis, ut visus ad pupillam, ita anima tota ad totum corpus animalis. Si igitur tota anima est in qualibet parte corporis, sequitur quod quaelibet pars corporis sit animal.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31581)\n\nPraeterea, omnes potentiae animae in ipsa essentia animae fundantur. Si igitur anima tota est in qualibet parte corporis, sequitur quod omnes potentiae animae sint in qualibet corporis parte, et ita visus erit in aure, et auditus in oculo. Quod est inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 8 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31582)\n\nPraeterea, si in qualibet parte corporis esset tota anima, quaelibet pars corporis immediate dependeret ab anima. Non ergo una pars dependeret ab alia, nec una pars esset principalior quam alia, quod est manifeste falsum. Non ergo anima est in qualibet parte corporis tota.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31583)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in VI de Trin., quod *anima in quocumque corpore et in toto est tota, et in qualibet eius parte tota est*.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31584)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut in aliis iam dictum est, si anima uniretur corpori solum ut motor, posset dici quod non esset in qualibet parte corporis, sed in una tantum, per quam alias moveret. Sed quia anima unitur corpori ut forma, necesse est quod sit in toto, et in qualibet parte corporis. Non enim est forma corporis accidentalis, sed substantialis. Substantialis autem forma non solum est perfectio totius, sed cuiuslibet partis. Cum enim totum consistat ex partibus, forma totius quae non dat esse singulis partibus corporis, est forma quae est compositio et ordo, sicut forma domus, et talis forma est accidentalis. Anima vero est forma substantialis, unde oportet quod sit forma et actus non solum totius, sed cuiuslibet partis. Et ideo, recedente anima, sicut non dicitur animal et homo nisi aequivoce, quemadmodum et animal pictum vel lapideum; ita est de manu et oculo, aut carne et osse, ut philosophus dicit. Cuius signum est, quod nulla pars corporis habet proprium opus, anima recedente, cum tamen omne quod retinet speciem, retineat operationem speciei. Actus autem est in eo cuius est actus. Unde oportet animam esse in toto corpore, et in qualibet eius parte. Et quod tota sit in qualibet parte eius, hinc considerari potest, quia, cum totum sit quod dividitur in partes, secundum triplicem divisionem est triplex totalitas. Est enim quoddam totum quod dividitur in partes quantitativas, sicut tota linea vel totum corpus. Est etiam quoddam totum quod dividitur in partes rationis et essentiae; sicut definitum in partes definitionis, et compositum resolvitur in materiam et formam. Tertium autem totum est potentiale, quod dividitur in partes virtutis. Primus autem totalitatis modus non convenit formis, nisi forte per accidens; et illis solis formis, quae habent indifferentem habitudinem ad totum quantitativum et partes eius. Sicut albedo, quantum est de sui ratione, aequaliter se habet ut sit in tota superficie et in qualibet superficiei parte; et ideo, divisa superficie, dividitur albedo per accidens. Sed forma quae requirit diversitatem in partibus, sicut est anima, et praecipue animalium perfectorum, non aequaliter se habet ad totum et partes, unde non dividitur per accidens per divisionem quantitatis. Sic ergo totalitas quantitativa non potest attribui animae nec per se nec per accidens. Sed totalitas secunda, quae attenditur secundum rationis et essentiae perfectionem, proprie et per se convenit formis. Similiter autem et totalitas virtutis, quia forma est operationis principium. Si ergo quaereretur de albedine, utrum esset tota in tota superficie et in qualibet eius parte, distinguere oporteret. Quia si fiat mentio de totalitate quantitativa, quam habet albedo per accidens, non tota esset in qualibet parte superficiei. Et similiter dicendum est de totalitate virtutis magis enim potest movere visum albedo quae est in tota superficie, quam albedo quae est in aliqua eius particula. Sed si fiat mentio de totalitate speciei et essentiae, tota albedo est in qualibet superficiei parte. Sed quia anima totalitatem quantitativam non habet, nec per se nec per accidens, ut dictum est; sufficit dicere quod anima tota est in qualibet parte corporis secundum totalitatem perfectionis et essentiae; non autem secundum totalitatem virtutis. Quia non secundum quamlibet suam potentiam est in qualibet parte corporis; sed secundum visum in oculo, secundum auditum in aure, et sic de aliis. Tamen attendendum est quod, quia anima requirit diversitatem in partibus, non eodem modo comparatur ad totum et ad partes, sed ad totum quidem primo et per se, sicut ad proprium et proportionatum perfectibile; ad partes autem per posterius, secundum quod habent ordinem ad totum.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31585)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod philosophus loquitur de potentia motiva animae.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31586)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod anima est actus corporis organici, sicut primi et proportionati perfectibilis.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31587)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod animal est quod componitur ex anima et corpore toto, quod est primum et proportionatum eius perfectibile. Sic autem anima non est in parte. Unde non oportet quod pars animalis sit animal.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 8 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31588)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod potentiarum animae quaedam sunt in ea secundum quod excedit totam corporis capacitatem, scilicet intellectus et voluntas, unde huiusmodi potentiae in nulla parte corporis esse dicuntur. Aliae vero potentiae sunt communes animae et corpori, unde talium potentiarum non oportet quod quaelibet sit in quocumque est anima; sed solum in illa parte corporis quae est proportionata ad talis potentiae operationem.\n\n[I q. 76 a. 8 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31589)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod una pars corporis dicitur esse principalior quam alia, propter potentias diversas quarum sunt organa partes corporis. Quae enim est principalioris potentiae organum, est principalior pars corporis, vel quae etiam eidem potentiae principalius deservit.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0002","text":"[I q. 76 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31504)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de unione animae ad corpus. Et circa hoc quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum intellectivum principium uniatur corpori ut forma. Secundo, utrum intellectivum principium numero multiplicetur secundum multiplicationem corporum; vel sit unus intellectus omnium hominum. Tertio, utrum in corpore cuius forma est principium intellectivum, sit aliqua alia anima. Quarto, utrum sit in eo aliqua alia forma substantialis. Quinto, quale debeat esse corpus cuius intellectivum principium est forma. Sexto, utrum tali corpori uniatur mediante aliquo alio corpore. Septimo, utrum mediante aliquo accidente. Octavo, utrum anima sit tota in qualibet parte corporis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0005","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31505)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectivum principium non uniatur corpori ut forma. Dicit enim philosophus, in III de anima, quod intellectus est separatus, et quod nullius corporis est actus. Non ergo unitur corpori ut forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0007","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31506)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0008","text":"Praeterea, omnis forma determinatur secundum naturam materiae cuius est forma, alioquin non requireretur proportio inter materiam et formam. Si ergo intellectus uniretur corpori ut forma, cum omne corpus habeat determinatam naturam, sequeretur quod intellectus haberet determinatam naturam. Et sic non esset omnium cognoscitivus, ut ex superioribus patet, quod est contra rationem intellectus. Non ergo intellectus unitur corpori ut forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0009","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31507)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0010","text":"Praeterea, quaecumque potentia receptiva est actus alicuius corporis, recipit formam materialiter et individualiter, quia receptum est in recipiente secundum modum recipientis. Sed forma rei intellectae non recipitur in intellectu materialiter et individualiter, sed magis immaterialiter et universaliter, alioquin intellectus non esset cognoscitivus immaterialium et universalium, sed singularium tantum, sicut et sensus. Intellectus ergo non unitur corpori ut forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0011","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31508)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0012","text":"Praeterea, eiusdem est potentia et actio, idem enim est quod potest agere, et quod agit. Sed actio intellectualis non est alicuius corporis, ut ex superioribus patet. Ergo nec potentia intellectiva est alicuius corporis potentia. Sed virtus sive potentia non potest esse abstractior vel simplicior quam essentia a qua virtus vel potentia derivatur. Ergo nec substantia intellectus est corporis forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0013","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31509)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0014","text":"Praeterea, id quod per se habet esse, non unitur corpori ut forma, quia forma est quo aliquid est; et sic ipsum esse formae non est ipsius formae secundum se. Sed intellectivum principium habet secundum se esse, et est subsistens, ut supra dictum est. Non ergo unitur corpori ut forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0015","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31510)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0016","text":"Praeterea, id quod inest alicui rei secundum se, semper inest ei. Sed formae secundum se inest uniri materiae, non enim per accidens aliquod, sed per essentiam suam est actus materiae; alioquin ex materia et forma non fieret unum substantialiter, sed accidentaliter. Forma ergo non potest esse sine propria materia. Sed intellectivum principium, cum sit incorruptibile, ut supra ostensum est, remanet corpori non unitum, corpore corrupto. Ergo intellectivum principium non unitur corpori ut forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0017","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31511)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0018","text":"Sed contra, secundum philosophum, in VIII Metaphys., differentia sumitur a forma rei. Sed differentia constitutiva hominis est rationale; quod dicitur de homine ratione intellectivi principii. Intellectivum ergo principium est forma hominis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0019","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31512)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0020","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere quod intellectus, qui est intellectualis operationis principium, sit humani corporis forma. Illud enim quo primo aliquid operatur, est forma eius cui operatio attribuitur, sicut quo primo sanatur corpus, est sanitas, et quo primo scit anima, est scientia; unde sanitas est forma corporis, et scientia animae. Et huius ratio est, quia nihil agit nisi secundum quod est actu, unde quo aliquid est actu, eo agit. Manifestum est autem quod primum quo corpus vivit, est anima. Et cum vita manifestetur secundum diversas operationes in diversis gradibus viventium, id quo primo operamur unumquodque horum operum vitae, est anima, anima enim est primum quo nutrimur, et sentimus, et movemur secundum locum; et similiter quo primo intelligimus. Hoc ergo principium quo primo intelligimus, sive dicatur intellectus sive anima intellectiva, est forma corporis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0021","text":"Et haec est demonstratio Aristotelis in II de anima. Si quis autem velit dicere animam intellectivam non esse corporis formam, oportet quod inveniat modum quo ista actio quae est intelligere, sit huius hominis actio, experitur enim unusquisque seipsum esse qui intelligit. Attribuitur autem aliqua actio alicui tripliciter, ut patet per philosophum, V Physic., dicitur enim movere aliquid aut agere vel secundum se totum, sicut medicus sanat; aut secundum partem, sicut homo videt per oculum; aut per accidens, sicut dicitur quod album aedificat, quia accidit aedificatori esse album. Cum igitur dicimus Socratem aut Platonem intelligere, manifestum est quod non attribuitur ei per accidens, attribuitur enim ei inquantum est homo, quod essentialiter praedicatur de ipso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0022","text":"Aut ergo oportet dicere quod Socrates intelligit secundum se totum, sicut Plato posuit, dicens hominem esse animam intellectivam, aut oportet dicere quod intellectus sit aliqua pars Socratis. Et primum quidem stare non potest, ut supra ostensum est, propter hoc quod ipse idem homo est qui percipit se et intelligere et sentire, sentire autem non est sine corpore, unde oportet corpus aliquam esse hominis partem. Relinquitur ergo quod intellectus quo Socrates intelligit, est aliqua pars Socratis ita quod intellectus aliquo modo corpori Socratis uniatur. Hanc autem unionem Commentator, in III de anima, dicit esse per speciem intelligibilem. Quae quidem habet duplex subiectum, unum scilicet intellectum possibilem; et aliud ipsa phantasmata quae sunt in organis corporeis. Et sic per speciem intelligibilem continuatur intellectus possibilis corpori huius vel illius hominis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0023","text":"Sed ista continuatio vel unio non sufficit ad hoc quod actio intellectus sit actio Socratis. Et hoc patet per similitudinem in sensu, ex quo Aristoteles procedit ad considerandum ea quae sunt intellectus. Sic enim se habent phantasmata ad intellectum, ut dicitur in III de anima, sicut colores ad visum. Sicut ergo species colorum sunt in visu, ita species phantasmatum sunt in intellectu possibili. Patet autem quod ex hoc quod colores sunt in pariete, quorum similitudines sunt in visu, actio visus non attribuitur parieti, non enim dicimus quod paries videat, sed magis quod videatur. Ex hoc ergo quod species phantasmatum sunt in intellectu possibili, non sequitur quod Socrates, in quo sunt phantasmata, intelligat; sed quod ipse, vel eius phantasmata intelligantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0024","text":"Quidam autem dicere voluerunt quod intellectus unitur corpori ut motor; et sic ex intellectu et corpore fit unum, ut actio intellectus toti attribui possit. Sed hoc est multipliciter vanum. Primo quidem, quia intellectus non movet corpus nisi per appetitum, cuius motus praesupponit operationem intellectus. Non ergo quia movetur Socrates ab intellectu, ideo intelligit, sed potius e converso, quia intelligit, ideo ab intellectu movetur Socrates. Secundo quia, cum Socrates sit quoddam individuum in natura cuius essentia est una, composita ex materia et forma; si intellectus non sit forma eius, sequitur quod sit praeter essentiam eius; et sic intellectus comparabitur ad totum Socratem sicut motor ad motum. Intelligere autem est actio quiescens in agente, non autem transiens in alterum, sicut calefactio. Non ergo intelligere potest attribui Socrati propter hoc quod est motus ab intellectu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0025","text":"Tertio, quia actio motoris nunquam attribuitur moto nisi sicut instrumento, sicut actio carpentarii serrae. Si igitur intelligere attribuitur Socrati quia est actio motoris eius, sequitur quod attribuatur ei sicut instrumento. Quod est contra philosophum, qui vult quod intelligere non sit per instrumentum corporeum. Quarto quia, licet actio partis attribuatur toti, ut actio oculi homini; nunquam tamen attribuitur alii parti, nisi forte per accidens, non enim dicimus quod manus videat, propter hoc quod oculus videt. Si ergo ex intellectu et Socrate dicto modo fit unum, actio intellectus non potest attribui Socrati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0026","text":"Si vero Socrates est totum quod componitur ex unione intellectus ad reliqua quae sunt Socratis, et tamen intellectus non unitur aliis quae sunt Socratis nisi sicut motor; sequitur quod Socrates non sit unum simpliciter, et per consequens nec ens simpliciter; sic enim aliquid est ens, quomodo et unum. Relinquitur ergo solus modus quem Aristoteles ponit, quod hic homo intelligit, quia principium intellectivum est forma ipsius. Sic ergo ex ipsa operatione intellectus apparet quod intellectivum principium unitur corpori ut forma. Potest etiam idem manifestari ex ratione speciei humanae. Natura enim uniuscuiusque rei ex eius operatione ostenditur. Propria autem operatio hominis, inquantum est homo, est intelligere, per hanc enim omnia animalia transcendit. Unde et Aristoteles, in libro Ethic., in hac operatione, sicut in propria hominis, ultimam felicitatem constituit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0027","text":"Oportet ergo quod homo secundum illud speciem sortiatur, quod est huius operationis principium. Sortitur autem unumquodque speciem per propriam formam. Relinquitur ergo quod intellectivum principium sit propria hominis forma. Sed considerandum est quod, quanto forma est nobilior, tanto magis dominatur materiae corporali, et minus ei immergitur, et magis sua operatione vel virtute excedit eam. Unde videmus quod forma mixti corporis habet aliquam operationem quae non causatur ex qualitatibus elementaribus. Et quanto magis proceditur in nobilitate formarum, tanto magis invenitur virtus formae materiam elementarem excedere, sicut anima vegetabilis plus quam forma metalli, et anima sensibilis plus quam anima vegetabilis. Anima autem humana est ultima in nobilitate formarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0028","text":"Unde intantum sua virtute excedit materiam corporalem, quod habet aliquam operationem et virtutem in qua nullo modo communicat materia corporalis. Et haec virtus dicitur intellectus. Est autem attendendum quod, si quis poneret animam componi ex materia et forma, nullo modo posset dicere animam esse formam corporis. Cum enim forma sit actus, materia vero sit ens in potentia tantum; nullo modo id quod est ex materia et forma compositum, potest esse alterius forma secundum se totum. Si autem secundum aliquid sui sit forma, id quod est forma dicimus animam, et id cuius est forma dicimus primum animatum, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0029","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31513)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0030","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut philosophus dicit in II Physic., ultima formarum naturalium, ad quam terminatur consideratio philosophi naturalis, scilicet anima humana, est quidem separata, sed tamen in materia; quod ex hoc probat, quia homo ex materia generat hominem, et sol. Separata quidem est secundum virtutem intellectivam, quia virtus intellectiva non est virtus alicuius organi corporalis, sicut virtus visiva est actus oculi intelligere enim est actus qui non potest exerceri per organum corporale, sicut exercetur visio. Sed in materia est inquantum ipsa anima cuius est haec virtus, est corporis forma, et terminus generationis humanae. Sic ergo philosophus dicit in III de anima quod intellectus est separatus, quia non est virtus alicuius organi corporalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0031","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31514)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0032","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ad secundum et tertium. Sufficit enim ad hoc quod homo possit intelligere omnia per intellectum, et ad hoc quod intellectus intelligat immaterialia et universalia, quod virtus intellectiva non est corporis actus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0033","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31515)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0034","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod humana anima non est forma in materia corporali immersa, vel ab ea totaliter comprehensa, propter suam perfectionem. Et ideo nihil prohibet aliquam eius virtutem non esse corporis actum; quamvis anima secundum suam essentiam sit corporis forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0035","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31516)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0036","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod anima illud esse in quo ipsa subsistit, communicat materiae corporali, ex qua et anima intellectiva fit unum, ita quod illud esse quod est totius compositi, est etiam ipsius animae. Quod non accidit in aliis formis, quae non sunt subsistentes. Et propter hoc anima humana remanet in suo esse, destructo corpore, non autem aliae formae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0037","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31517)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0038","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod secundum se convenit animae corpori uniri, sicut secundum se convenit corpori levi esse sursum. Et sicut corpus leve manet quidem leve cum a loco proprio fuerit separatum, cum aptitudine tamen et inclinatione ad proprium locum; ita anima humana manet in suo esse cum fuerit a corpore separata, habens aptitudinem et inclinationem naturalem ad corporis unionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0039","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0040","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31518)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0041","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectivum principium non multiplicetur secundum multiplicationem corporum, sed sit unus intellectus in omnibus hominibus. Nulla enim substantia immaterialis multiplicatur secundum numerum in una specie. Anima autem humana est substantia immaterialis, non enim est composita ex materia et forma, ut supra ostensum est. Non ergo sunt multae in una specie. Sed omnes homines sunt unius speciei. Est ergo unus intellectus omnium hominum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0042","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31519)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0043","text":"Praeterea, remota causa, removetur effectus. Si ergo secundum multiplicationem corporum multiplicarentur animae humanae, consequens videretur quod, remotis corporibus, multitudo animarum non remaneret, sed ex omnibus animabus remaneret aliquod unum solum. Quod est haereticum, periret enim differentia praemiorum et poenarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0044","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31520)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0045","text":"Praeterea, si intellectus meus est alius ab intellectu tuo, intellectus meus est quoddam individuum, et similiter intellectus tuus, particularia enim sunt quae differunt numero et conveniunt in una specie. Sed omne quod recipitur in aliquo, est in eo per modum recipientis. Ergo species rerum in intellectu meo et tuo reciperentur individualiter, quod est contra rationem intellectus, qui est cognoscitivus universalium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0046","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31521)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0047","text":"Praeterea, intellectum est in intellectu intelligente. Si ergo intellectus meus est alius ab intellectu tuo, oportet quod aliud sit intellectum a me, et aliud sit intellectum a te. Et ita erit individualiter numeratum, et intellectum in potentia tantum, et oportebit abstrahere intentionem communem ab utroque, quia a quibuslibet diversis contingit abstrahere aliquod commune intelligibile. Quod est contra rationem intellectus, quia sic non videretur distingui intellectus a virtute imaginativa. Videtur ergo relinqui quod sit unus intellectus omnium hominum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0048","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31522)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0049","text":"Praeterea, cum discipulus accipit scientiam a magistro, non potest dici quod scientia magistri generet scientiam in discipulo, quia sic etiam scientia esset forma activa, sicut calor; quod patet esse falsum. Videtur ergo quod eadem numero scientia quae est in magistro, communicetur discipulo. Quod esse non potest, nisi sit unus intellectus utriusque. Videtur ergo quod sit unus intellectus discipuli et magistri; et per consequens omnium hominum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0050","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31523)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0051","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, in libro de quantitate animae, *si plures tantum animas humanas dixerim, ipse me ridebo*. Sed maxime videtur anima esse una quantum ad intellectum. Ergo est unus intellectus omnium hominum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0052","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31524)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0053","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in II Physic., quod sicut se habent causae universales ad universalia, ita se habent causae particulares ad particularia. Sed impossibile est quod una anima secundum speciem, sit diversorum animalium secundum speciem. Ergo impossibile est quod anima intellectiva una numero, sit diversorum secundum numerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0054","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31525)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0055","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod intellectum esse unum omnium hominum, omnino est impossibile. Et hoc quidem patet, si, secundum Platonis sententiam homo sit ipse intellectus. Sequeretur enim, si Socratis et Platonis est unus intellectus tantum, quod Socrates et Plato sint unus homo; et quod non distinguantur ab invicem nisi per hoc quod est extra essentiam utriusque. Et erit tunc distinctio Socratis et Platonis non alia quam hominis tunicati et cappati, quod est omnino absurdum. Similiter etiam patet hoc esse impossibile, si, secundum sententiam Aristotelis, intellectus ponatur pars, seu potentia, animae quae est hominis forma. Impossibile est enim plurium numero diversorum esse unam formam, sicut impossibile est quod eorum sit unum esse, nam forma est essendi principium. Similiter etiam patet hoc esse impossibile quocumque modo quis ponat unionem intellectus ad hunc et ad illum hominem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0056","text":"Manifestum est enim quod, si sit unum principale agens et duo instrumenta, dici poterit unum agens simpliciter, sed plures actiones, sicut si unus homo tangat diversa duabus manibus, erit unus tangens, sed duo tactus. Si vero e converso instrumentum sit unum et principales agentes diversi, dicentur quidem plures agentes, sed una actio, sicut si multi uno fune trahant navem, erunt multi trahentes, sed unus tractus. Si vero agens principale sit unum et instrumentum unum, dicetur unum agens et una actio, sicut cum faber uno martello percutit, est unus percutiens et una percussio. Manifestum est autem quod, qualitercumque intellectus seu uniatur seu copuletur huic vel illi homini, intellectus inter cetera quae ad hominem pertinent, principalitatem habet, obediunt enim vires sensitivae intellectui, et ei deserviunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0057","text":"Si ergo poneretur quod essent plures intellectus et sensus unus duorum hominum, puta si duo homines haberent unum oculum; essent quidem plures videntes, sed una visio. Si vero intellectus est unus, quantumcumque diversificentur alia quibus omnibus intellectus utitur quasi instrumentis, nullo modo Socrates et Plato poterunt dici nisi unus intelligens. Et si addamus quod ipsum intelligere, quod est actio intellectus, non fit per aliquod aliud organum, nisi per ipsum intellectum; sequetur ulterius quod sit et agens unum et actio una; idest quod omnes homines sint unus intelligens, et unum intelligere; dico autem respectu eiusdem intelligibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0058","text":"Posset autem diversificari actio intellectualis mea et tua per diversitatem phantasmatum, quia scilicet aliud est phantasma lapidis in me et aliud in te, si ipsum phantasma, secundum quod est aliud in me et aliud in te, esset forma intellectus possibilis, quia idem agens secundum diversas formas producit diversas actiones, sicut secundum diversas formas rerum respectu eiusdem oculi sunt diversae visiones. Sed ipsum phantasma non est forma intellectus possibilis, sed species intelligibilis quae a phantasmatibus abstrahitur. In uno autem intellectu a phantasmatibus diversis eiusdem speciei non abstrahitur nisi una species intelligibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0059","text":"Sicut in uno homine apparet, in quo possunt esse diversa phantasmata lapidis, et tamen ab omnibus eis abstrahitur una species intelligibilis lapidis, per quam intellectus unius hominis operatione una intelligit naturam lapidis, non obstante diversitate phantasmatum. Si ergo unus intellectus esset omnium hominum, diversitas phantasmatum quae sunt in hoc et in illo, non posset causare diversitatem intellectualis operationis huius et illius hominis, ut Commentator fingit in III de anima. Relinquitur ergo quod omnino impossibile et inconveniens est ponere unum intellectum omnium hominum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0060","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31526)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0061","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, licet anima intellectiva non habeat materiam ex qua sit, sicut nec Angelus, tamen est forma materiae alicuius; quod Angelo non convenit. Et ideo secundum divisionem materiae sunt multae animae unius speciei, multi autem Angeli unius speciei omnino esse non possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0062","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31527)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0063","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod unumquodque hoc modo habet unitatem, quo habet esse, et per consequens idem est iudicium de multiplicatione rei, et de esse ipsius. Manifestum est autem quod anima intellectualis, secundum suum esse, unitur corpori ut forma; et tamen, destructo corpore, remanet anima intellectualis in suo esse. Et eadem ratione multitudo animarum est secundum multitudinem corporum; et tamen, destructis corporibus, remanent animae in suo esse multiplicatae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0064","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31528)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0065","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod individuatio intelligentis, aut speciei per quam intelligit, non excludit intelligentiam universalium, alioquin, cum intellectus separati sint quaedam substantiae subsistentes, et per consequens particulares, non possent universalia intelligere. Sed materialitas cognoscentis et speciei per quam cognoscitur, universalis cognitionem impedit. Sicut enim omnis actio est secundum modum formae qua agens agit, ut calefactio secundum modum caloris; ita cognitio est secundum modum speciei qua cognoscens cognoscit. Manifestum est autem quod natura communis distinguitur et multiplicatur secundum principia individuantia, quae sunt ex parte materiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0066","text":"Si ergo forma per quam fit cognitio, sit materialis, non abstracta a conditionibus materiae, erit similitudo naturae speciei aut generis, secundum quod est distincta et multiplicata per principia individuantia, et ita non poterit cognosci natura rei in sua communitate. Si vero species sit abstracta a conditionibus materiae individualis, erit similitudo naturae absque iis quae ipsam distinguunt et multiplicant, et ita cognoscetur universale. Nec refert, quantum ad hoc, utrum sit unus intellectus vel plures, quia si etiam esset unus tantum, oporteret ipsum esse aliquem quendam, et speciem per quam intelligit esse aliquam quandam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0067","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31529)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0068","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, sive intellectus sit unus sive plures, id quod intelligitur est unum. Id enim quod intelligitur non est in intellectu secundum se, sed secundum suam similitudinem, *lapis enim non est in anima, sed species lapidis*, ut dicitur in III de anima. Et tamen lapis est id quod intelligitur, non autem species lapidis, nisi per reflexionem intellectus supra seipsum, alioquin scientiae non essent de rebus, sed de speciebus intelligibilibus. Contingit autem eidem rei diversa secundum diversas formas assimilari. Et quia cognitio fit secundum assimilationem cognoscentis ad rem cognitam, sequitur quod idem a diversis cognoscentibus cognosci contingit, ut patet in sensu, nam plures vident eundem colorem, secundum diversas similitudines. Et similiter plures intellectus intelligunt unam rem intellectam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0069","text":"Sed hoc tantum interest inter sensum et intellectum, secundum sententiam Aristotelis, quod res sentitur secundum illam dispositionem quam extra animam habet, in sua particularitate, natura autem rei quae intelligitur, est quidem extra animam, sed non habet illum modum essendi extra animam, secundum quem intelligitur. Intelligitur enim natura communis seclusis principiis individuantibus; non autem hunc modum essendi habet extra animam. Sed secundum sententiam Platonis, res intellecta eo modo est extra animam quo intelligitur, posuit enim naturas rerum a materia separatas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0070","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31530)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0071","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod scientia alia est in discipulo, et alia in magistro. Quomodo autem causetur, in sequentibus ostendetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0072","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31531)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0073","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod Augustinus intelligit animas non esse plures tantum, quin uniantur in una ratione speciei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0074","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0075","text":"[I q. 76 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31532)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0076","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod praeter animam intellectivam sint in homine aliae animae per essentiam differentes, scilicet sensitiva et nutritiva. Corruptibile enim et incorruptibile non sunt unius substantiae. Sed anima intellectiva est incorruptibilis; aliae vero animae, scilicet sensitiva et nutritiva, sunt corruptibiles, ut ex superioribus patet. Ergo in homine non potest esse una essentia animae intellectivae et sensitivae et nutritivae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0077","text":"[I q. 76 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31533)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0078","text":"Si dicatur quod anima sensitiva in homine est incorruptibilis, *contra, corruptibile et incorruptibile differunt secundum genus*, ut dicitur in X Metaphys. Sed anima sensitiva in equo et leone et aliis brutis animalibus est corruptibilis. Si igitur in homine sit incorruptibilis, non erit eiusdem generis anima sensitiva in homine et bruto. Animal autem dicitur ex eo quod habet animam sensitivam. Neque ergo animal erit unum genus commune hominis et aliorum animalium. Quod est inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0079","text":"[I q. 76 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31534)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0080","text":"Praeterea, philosophus dicit, in libro de Generat. Animal., quod embryo prius est animal quam homo. Sed hoc esse non posset, si esset eadem essentia animae sensitivae et intellectivae, est enim animal per animam sensitivam, homo vero per animam intellectivam. Non ergo in homine est una essentia animae sensitivae et intellectivae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0081","text":"[I q. 76 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31535)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0082","text":"Praeterea, philosophus dicit, in VIII Metaphys., quod genus sumitur a materia, differentia vero a forma. Sed rationale, quod est differentia constitutiva hominis, sumitur ab anima intellectiva; animal vero dicitur ex hoc quod habet corpus animatum anima sensitiva. Anima ergo intellectiva comparatur ad corpus animatum anima sensitiva, sicut forma ad materiam. Non ergo anima intellectiva est eadem per essentiam cum anima sensitiva in homine; sed praesupponit eam sicut materiale suppositum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0083","text":"[I q. 76 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31536)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0084","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in libro de Eccles. Dogmat., *neque duas animas esse dicimus in homine uno, sicut Iacobus et alii Syrorum scribunt, unam animalem, qua animatur corpus, et immixta sit sanguini, et alteram spiritualem, quae rationi ministret, sed dicimus unam et eandem esse animam in homine, quae et corpus sua societate vivificat, et semetipsam sua ratione disponit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0085","text":"[I q. 76 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31537)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0086","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Plato posuit diversas animas esse in corpore uno, etiam secundum organa distinctas, quibus diversa opera vitae attribuebat; dicens vim nutritivam esse in hepate, concupiscibilem in corde, cognoscitivam in cerebro. Quam quidem opinionem Aristoteles reprobat, in libro de anima, quantum ad illas animae partes quae corporeis organis in suis operibus utuntur, ex hoc quod in animalibus quae decisa vivunt, in qualibet parte inveniuntur diversae operationes animae, sicut sensus et appetitus. Hoc autem non esset, si diversa principia operationum animae, tanquam per essentiam diversae, diversis partibus corporis distributa essent. Sed de intellectiva sub dubio videtur relinquere utrum sit separata ab aliis partibus animae solum ratione, an etiam loco.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0087","text":"Opinio autem Platonis sustineri utique posset, si poneretur quod anima unitur corpori, non ut forma, sed ut motor, ut posuit Plato. Nihil enim inconveniens sequitur, si idem mobile a diversis motoribus moveatur, praecipue secundum diversas partes. Sed si ponamus animam corpori uniri sicut formam, omnino impossibile videtur plures animas per essentiam differentes in uno corpore esse. Quod quidem triplici ratione manifestari potest. Primo quidem, quia animal non esset simpliciter unum, cuius essent animae plures. Nihil enim est simpliciter unum nisi per formam unam, per quam habet res esse, ab eodem enim habet res quod sit ens et quod sit una; et ideo ea quae denominantur a diversis formis, non sunt unum simpliciter, sicut homo albus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0088","text":"Si igitur homo ab alia forma haberet quod sit vivum, scilicet ab anima vegetabili; et ab alia forma quod sit animal, scilicet ab anima sensibili; et ab alia quod sit homo, scilicet ab anima rationali; sequeretur quod homo non esset unum simpliciter, sicut et Aristoteles argumentatur contra Platonem, in VIII Metaphys., quod si alia esset idea animalis, et alia bipedis, non esset unum simpliciter animal bipes. Et propter hoc, in I de anima, contra ponentes diversas animas in corpore, inquirit quid contineat illas, idest quid faciat ex eis unum. Et non potest dici quod uniantur per corporis unitatem, quia magis anima continet corpus, et facit ipsum esse unum, quam e converso. Secundo, hoc apparet impossibile ex modo praedicationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0089","text":"Quae enim sumuntur a diversis formis, praedicantur ad invicem vel per accidens, si formae non sint ad invicem ordinatae, puta cum dicimus quod album est dulce, vel, si formae sint ordinatae ad invicem, erit praedicatio per se, in secundo modo dicendi per se, quia subiectum ponitur in definitione praedicati. Sicut superficies praeambula est ad colorem, si ergo dicamus quod corpus superficiatum est coloratum, erit secundus modus praedicationis per se. Si ergo alia forma sit a qua aliquid dicitur animal, et a qua aliquid dicitur homo, sequeretur quod vel unum horum non possit praedicari de altero nisi per accidens, si istae duae formae ad invicem ordinem non habent; vel quod sit ibi praedicatio in secundo modo dicendi per se, si una animarum sit ad aliam praeambula.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0090","text":"Utrumque autem horum est manifeste falsum, quia animal per se de homine praedicatur, non per accidens; homo autem non ponitur in definitione animalis, sed e converso. Ergo oportet eandem formam esse per quam aliquid est animal, et per quam aliquid est homo, alioquin homo non vere esset id quod est animal, ut sic animal per se de homine praedicetur. Tertio, apparet hoc esse impossibile per hoc, quod una operatio animae, cum fuerit intensa, impedit aliam. Quod nullo modo contingeret, nisi principium actionum esset per essentiam unum. Sic ergo dicendum quod eadem numero est anima in homine sensitiva et intellectiva et nutritiva. Quomodo autem hoc contingat, de facili considerari potest, si quis differentias specierum et formarum attendat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0091","text":"Inveniuntur enim rerum species et formae differre ab invicem secundum perfectius et minus perfectum, sicut in rerum ordine animata perfectiora sunt inanimatis, et animalia plantis, et homines animalibus brutis, et in singulis horum generum sunt gradus diversi. Et ideo Aristoteles, in VIII Metaphys., assimilat species rerum numeris, qui differunt specie secundum additionem vel subtractionem unitatis. Et in II de anima, comparat diversas animas speciebus figurarum, quarum una continet aliam; sicut pentagonum continet tetragonum, et excedit. Sic igitur anima intellectiva continet in sua virtute quidquid habet anima sensitiva brutorum, et nutritiva plantarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0092","text":"Sicut ergo superficies quae habet figuram pentagonam, non per aliam figuram est tetragona, et per aliam pentagona; quia superflueret figura tetragona, ex quo in pentagona continetur; ita nec per aliam animam Socrates est homo, et per aliam animal, sed per unam et eandem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0093","text":"[I q. 76 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31538)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0094","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod anima sensitiva non habet incorruptibilitatem ex hoc quod est sensitiva, sed ex hoc quod est intellectiva, ei incorruptibilitas debetur. Quando ergo anima est sensitiva tantum, corruptibilis est, quando vero cum sensitivo intellectivum habet, est incorruptibilis. Licet enim sensitivum incorruptionem non det, tamen incorruptionem intellectivo auferre non potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0095","text":"[I q. 76 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31539)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0096","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod formae non collocantur in genere vel in specie, sed composita. Homo autem corruptibilis est, sicut et alia animalia. Unde differentia secundum corruptibile et incorruptibile, quae est ex parte formarum, non facit hominem secundum genus ab aliis animalibus differre.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0097","text":"[I q. 76 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31540)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0098","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod prius embryo habet animam quae est sensitiva tantum; qua abiecta, advenit perfectior anima, quae est simul sensitiva et intellectiva; ut infra plenius ostendetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0099","text":"[I q. 76 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31541)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0100","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod non oportet secundum diversas rationes vel intentiones logicas, quae consequuntur modum intelligendi, diversitatem in rebus naturalibus accipere, quia ratio unum et idem secundum diversos modos apprehendere potest. Quia igitur, ut dictum est, anima intellectiva virtute continet id quod sensitiva habet, et adhuc amplius; potest seorsum ratio considerare quod pertinet ad virtutem sensitivae, quasi quoddam imperfectum et materiale. Et quia hoc invenit commune homini et aliis animalibus, ex hoc rationem generis format. Id vero in quo anima intellectiva sensitiva excedit, accipit quasi formale et completivum, et ex eo format differentiam hominis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0101","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0102","text":"[I q. 76 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31542)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0103","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in homine sit alia forma praeter animam intellectivam. Dicit enim philosophus, in II de anima, quod *anima est actus corporis physici potentia vitam habentis*. Comparatur igitur anima ad corpus, sicut forma ad materiam. Sed corpus habet aliquam formam substantialem per quam est corpus. Ergo ante animam praecedit in corpore aliqua forma substantialis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0104","text":"[I q. 76 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31543)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0105","text":"Praeterea, homo et quodlibet animal est movens seipsum. Omne autem movens seipsum dividitur in duas partes, quarum una est movens, et alia est mota, ut probatur in VIII Physic. Pars autem movens est anima. Ergo oportet quod alia pars sit talis quae possit esse mota. Sed materia prima non potest moveri, ut dicitur in V Physic., cum sit ens solum in potentia, quinimmo omne quod movetur est corpus. Ergo oportet quod in homine et in quolibet animali sit alia forma substantialis, per quam constituatur corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0106","text":"[I q. 76 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31544)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0107","text":"Praeterea, ordo in formis attenditur secundum habitudinem ad materiam primam, prius enim et posterius dicitur per comparationem ad aliquod principium. Si ergo non esset in homine alia forma substantialis praeter animam rationalem, sed immediate materiae primae inhaereret; sequeretur quod esset in ordine imperfectissimarum formarum, quae immediate inhaerent materiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0108","text":"[I q. 76 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31545)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0109","text":"Praeterea, corpus humanum est corpus mixtum. Mixtio autem non fit secundum materiam tantum, quia tunc esset corruptio sola. Oportet ergo quod remaneant formae elementorum in corpore mixto, quae sunt formae substantiales. Ergo in corpore humano sunt aliae formae substantiales praeter animam intellectivam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0110","text":"[I q. 76 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31546)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0111","text":"Sed contra, unius rei est unum esse substantiale. Sed forma substantialis dat esse substantiale. Ergo unius rei est una tantum forma substantialis. Anima autem est forma substantialis hominis. Ergo impossibile est quod in homine sit aliqua alia forma substantialis quam anima intellectiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0112","text":"[I q. 76 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31547)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0113","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, si poneretur anima intellectiva non uniri corpori ut forma, sed solum ut motor, ut Platonici posuerunt; necesse esset dicere quod in homine esset alia forma substantialis, per quam corpus ab anima mobile in suo esse constitueretur. Sed si anima intellectiva unitur corpori ut forma substantialis, sicut supra iam diximus, impossibile est quod aliqua alia forma substantialis praeter eam inveniatur in homine. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod forma substantialis in hoc a forma accidentali differt quia forma accidentalis non dat esse simpliciter, sed esse tale, sicut calor facit suum subiectum non simpliciter esse, sed esse calidum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0114","text":"Et ideo cum advenit forma accidentalis, non dicitur aliquid fieri vel generari simpliciter, sed fieri tale aut aliquo modo se habens, et similiter cum recedit forma accidentalis, non dicitur aliquid corrumpi simpliciter, sed secundum quid. Forma autem substantialis dat esse simpliciter, et ideo per eius adventum dicitur aliquid simpliciter generari, et per eius recessum simpliciter corrumpi. Et propter hoc antiqui naturales, qui posuerunt materiam primam esse aliquod ens actu, puta ignem aut aerem aut aliquid huiusmodi, dixerunt quod nihil generatur aut corrumpitur simpliciter, sed omne fieri statuerunt alterari, ut dicitur in I Physic.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0115","text":"Si igitur ita esset, quod praeter animam intellectivam praeexisteret quaecumque alia forma substantialis in materia, per quam subiectum animae esset ens actu; sequeretur quod anima non daret esse simpliciter; et per consequens quod non esset forma substantialis; et quod per adventum animae non esset generatio simpliciter, neque per eius abscessum corruptio simpliciter, sed solum secundum quid. Quae sunt manifeste falsa. Unde dicendum est quod nulla alia forma substantialis est in homine, nisi sola anima intellectiva; et quod ipsa, sicut virtute continet animam sensitivam et nutritivam, ita virtute continet omnes inferiores formas, et facit ipsa sola quidquid imperfectiores formae in aliis faciunt. Et similiter est dicendum de anima sensitiva in brutis, et de nutritiva in plantis, et universaliter de omnibus formis perfectioribus respectu imperfectiorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0116","text":"[I q. 76 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31548)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0117","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Aristoteles non dicit animam esse actum corporis tantum, sed actum corporis physici organici potentia vitam habentis, et quod talis potentia non abiicit animam. Unde manifestum est quod in eo cuius anima dicitur actus, etiam anima includitur; eo modo loquendi quo dicitur quod calor est actus calidi, et lumen est actus lucidi; non quod seorsum sit lucidum sine luce, sed quia est lucidum per lucem. Et similiter dicitur quod anima est actus corporis etc., quia per animam et est corpus, et est organicum, et est potentia vitam habens. Sed actus primus dicitur in potentia respectu actus secundi, qui est operatio. Talis enim potentia est non abiiciens, idest non excludens, animam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0118","text":"[I q. 76 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31549)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0119","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod anima non movet corpus per esse suum, secundum quod unitur corpori ut forma; sed per potentiam motivam, cuius actus praesupponit iam corpus effectum in actu per animam; ut sic anima secundum vim motivam sit pars movens, et corpus animatum sit pars mota.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0120","text":"[I q. 76 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31550)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0121","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in materia considerantur diversi gradus perfectionis, sicut esse, vivere sentire et intelligere. Semper autem secundum superveniens priori, perfectius est. Forma ergo quae dat solum primum gradum perfectionis materiae, est imperfectissima, sed forma quae dat primum et secundum, et tertium, et sic deinceps, est perfectissima; et tamen materiae immediata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0122","text":"[I q. 76 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31551)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0123","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod Avicenna posuit formas substantiales elementorum integras remanere in mixto, mixtionem autem fieri secundum quod contrariae qualitates elementorum reducuntur ad medium. Sed hoc est impossibile. Quia diversae formae elementorum non possunt esse nisi in diversis partibus materiae; ad quarum diversitatem oportet intelligi dimensiones, sine quibus materia divisibilis esse non potest. Materia autem dimensioni subiecta non invenitur nisi in corpore. Diversa autem corpora non possunt esse in eodem loco. Unde sequitur quod elementa sint in mixto distincta secundum situm. Et ita non erit vera mixtio, quae est secundum totum, sed mixtio ad sensum, quae est secundum minima iuxta se posita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0124","text":"Averroes autem posuit, in III de caelo, quod formae elementorum, propter sui imperfectionem, sunt mediae inter formas accidentales et substantiales; et ideo recipiunt magis et minus; et ideo remittuntur in mixtione et ad medium reducuntur, et conflatur ex eis una forma. Sed hoc est etiam magis impossibile. Nam esse substantiale cuiuslibet rei in indivisibili consistit; et omnis additio et subtractio variat speciem, sicut in numeris, ut dicitur in VIII Metaphys. Unde impossibile est quod forma substantialis quaecumque recipiat magis et minus. Nec minus est impossibile aliquid esse medium inter substantiam et accidens. Et ideo dicendum est, secundum philosophum in I de Generat., quod formae elementorum manent in mixto non actu, sed virtute. Manent enim qualitates propriae elementorum, licet remissae, in quibus est virtus formarum elementarium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0125","text":"Et huiusmodi qualitas mixtionis est propria dispositio ad formam substantialem corporis mixti, puta formam lapidis, vel animae cuiuscumque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0126","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0127","text":"[I q. 76 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31552)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0128","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima intellectiva inconvenienter tali corpori uniatur. Materia enim debet esse proportionata formae. Sed anima intellectiva est forma incorruptibilis. Non ergo convenienter unitur corpori corruptibili.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0129","text":"[I q. 76 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31553)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0130","text":"Praeterea, anima intellectiva est forma maxime immaterialis, cuius signum est, quod habet operationem in qua non communicat materia corporalis. Sed quanto corpus est subtilius, tanto minus habet de materia. Ergo anima deberet subtilissimo corpori uniri, puta igni; et non corpori mixto, et terrestri magis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0131","text":"[I q. 76 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31554)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0132","text":"Praeterea, cum forma sit principium speciei, ab una forma non proveniunt diversae species. Sed anima intellectiva est una forma. Ergo non debet uniri corpori quod componitur ex partibus dissimilium specierum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0133","text":"[I q. 76 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31555)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0134","text":"Praeterea, perfectioris formae debet esse perfectius susceptibile. Sed anima intellectiva est perfectissima animarum. Cum igitur aliorum animalium corpora habeant naturaliter insita tegumenta, puta pilorum loco vestium, et unguium loco calceamentorum; habeant etiam arma naturaliter sibi data, sicut ungues, dentes et cornua, ergo videtur quod anima intellectiva non debuerit uniri corpori imperfecto tanquam talibus auxiliis privato.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0135","text":"[I q. 76 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31556)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0136","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit philosophus, in II de anima, quod *anima est actus corporis physici organici potentia vitam habentis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0137","text":"[I q. 76 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31557)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0138","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum forma non sit propter materiam, sed potius materia propter formam; ex forma oportet rationem accipere quare materia sit talis, et non e converso. Anima autem intellectiva, sicut supra habitum est, secundum naturae ordinem, infimum gradum in substantiis intellectualibus tenet; intantum quod non habet naturaliter sibi inditam notitiam veritatis, sicut Angeli, sed oportet quod eam colligat ex rebus divisibilibus per viam sensus, ut Dionysius dicit, VII cap. de Div. Nom. Natura autem nulli deest in necessariis, unde oportuit quod anima intellectiva non solum haberet virtutem intelligendi, sed etiam virtutem sentiendi. Actio autem sensus non fit sine corporeo instrumento. Oportuit igitur animam intellectivam tali corpori uniri, quod possit esse conveniens organum sensus. Omnes autem alii sensus fundantur supra tactum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0139","text":"Ad organum autem tactus requiritur quod sit medium inter contraria, quae sunt calidum et frigidum, humidum et siccum, et similia, quorum est tactus apprehensivus, sic enim est in potentia ad contraria, et potest ea sentire. Unde quanto organum tactus fuerit magis reductum ad aequalitatem complexionis, tanto perceptibilior erit tactus. Anima autem intellectiva habet completissime virtutem sensitivam, quia quod est inferioris praeexistit perfectius in superiori ut dicit Dionysius in libro de Div. Nom. Unde oportuit corpus cui unitur anima intellectiva, esse corpus mixtum, inter omnia alia magis reductum ad aequalitatem complexionis. Et propter hoc homo inter omnia animalia melioris est tactus. Et inter ipsos homines, qui sunt melioris tactus, sunt melioris intellectus. Cuius signum est, quod molles carne bene aptos mente videmus, ut dicitur in II de anima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0140","text":"[I q. 76 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31558)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0141","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod hanc obiectionem aliquis forte vellet evadere per hoc, quod diceret corpus hominis ante peccatum incorruptibile fuisse. Sed haec responsio non videtur sufficiens, quia corpus hominis ante peccatum immortale fuit non per naturam, sed per gratiae divinae donum; alioquin immortalitas eius per peccatum sublata non esset, sicut nec immortalitas Daemonis. Et ideo aliter dicendum est, quod in materia duplex conditio invenitur, una quae eligitur ad hoc quod sit conveniens formae; alia quae ex necessitate consequitur prioris dispositionis. Sicut artifex ad formam serrae eligit materiam ferream, aptam ad secandum dura; sed quod dentes serrae hebetari possint et rubiginem contrahere, sequitur ex necessitate materiae. Sic igitur et animae intellectivae debetur corpus quod sit aequalis complexionis, ex hoc autem de necessitate materiae sequitur quod sit corruptibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0142","text":"Si quis vero dicat quod Deus potuit hanc necessitatem vitare, dicendum est quod in constitutione rerum naturalium non consideratur quid Deus facere possit, sed quid naturae rerum conveniat, ut Augustinus dicit, II super Gen. ad Litt. Providit tamen Deus adhibendo remedium contra mortem per gratiae donum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0143","text":"[I q. 76 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31559)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0144","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod animae intellectivae non debetur corpus propter ipsam intellectualem operationem secundum se; sed propter sensitivam virtutem, quae requirit organum aequaliter complexionatum. Et ideo oportuit animam intellectivam tali corpori uniri, et non simplici elemento, vel corpori mixto in quo excederet ignis secundum quantitatem, quia non posset esse aequalitas complexionis, propter excedentem ignis activam virtutem. Habet autem hoc corpus aequaliter complexionatum quandam dignitatem, per hoc quod est remotum a contrariis; in quo quodammodo assimilatur corpori caelesti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0145","text":"[I q. 76 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31560)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0146","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod partes animalis, ut oculus, manus, caro et os, et huiusmodi, non sunt in specie, sed totum, et ideo non potest dici, proprie loquendo, quod sint diversarum specierum, sed quod sint diversarum dispositionum. Et hoc competit animae intellectivae, quae quamvis sit una secundum essentiam, tamen propter sui perfectionem est multiplex in virtute; et ideo, ad diversas operationes, indiget diversis dispositionibus in partibus corporis cui unitur. Et propter hoc videmus quod maior est diversitas partium in animalibus perfectis quam in imperfectis, et in his quam in plantis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0147","text":"[I q. 76 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31561)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0148","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod anima intellectiva, quia est universalium comprehensiva, habet virtutem ad infinita. Et ideo non potuerunt sibi determinari a natura vel determinatae existimationes naturales, vel etiam determinata auxilia vel defensionum vel tegumentorum; sicut aliis animalibus, quorum animae habent apprehensionem et virtutem ad aliqua particularia determinata. Sed loco horum omnium, homo habet naturaliter rationem, et manus, quae sunt organa organorum, quia per eas homo potest sibi praeparare instrumenta infinitorum modorum, et ad infinitos effectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0149","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0150","text":"[I q. 76 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31562)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0151","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima intellectiva uniatur corpori mediantibus aliquibus dispositionibus accidentalibus. Omnis enim forma est in materia sibi propria et disposita. Sed dispositiones ad formam sunt accidentia quaedam. Ergo oportet praeintelligi accidentia aliqua in materia ante formam substantialem, et ita ante animam, cum anima sit quaedam substantialis forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0152","text":"[I q. 76 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31563)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0153","text":"Praeterea, diversae formae unius speciei requirunt diversas materiae partes. Partes autem materiae diversae non possunt intelligi nisi secundum divisionem dimensivarum quantitatum. Ergo oportet intelligere dimensiones in materia ante formas substantiales, quae sunt multae unius speciei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0154","text":"[I q. 76 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31564)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0155","text":"Praeterea, spirituale applicatur corporali per contactum virtutis. Virtus autem animae est eius potentia. Ergo videtur quod anima unitur corpori mediante potentia, quae est quoddam accidens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0156","text":"[I q. 76 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31565)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0157","text":"Sed contra est quod accidens est posterius substantia et tempore et ratione, ut dicitur in VII Metaphys. Non ergo forma accidentalis aliqua potest intelligi in materia ante animam, quae est forma substantialis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0158","text":"[I q. 76 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31566)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0159","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, si anima uniretur corpori solum ut motor, nihil prohiberet, immo magis necessarium esset esse aliquas dispositiones medias inter animam et corpus, potentiam scilicet ex parte animae, per quam moveret corpus; et aliquam habilitatem ex parte corporis, per quam corpus esset ab anima mobile. Sed si anima intellectiva unitur corpori ut forma substantialis, sicut iam supra dictum est, impossibile est quod aliqua dispositio accidentalis cadat media inter corpus et animam, vel inter quamcumque formam substantialem et materiam suam. Et huius ratio est quia, cum materia sit in potentia ad omnes actus ordine quodam, oportet quod id quod est primum simpliciter in actibus, primo in materia intelligatur. Primum autem inter omnes actus est esse. Impossibile est ergo intelligere materiam prius esse calidam vel quantam, quam esse in actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0160","text":"Esse autem in actu habet per formam substantialem, quae facit esse simpliciter, ut iam dictum est. Unde impossibile est quod quaecumque dispositiones accidentales praeexistant in materia ante formam substantialem; et per consequens neque ante animam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0161","text":"[I q. 76 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31567)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0162","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut ex praedictis patet, forma perfectior virtute continet quidquid est inferiorum formarum. Et ideo una et eadem existens, perficit materiam secundum diversos perfectionis gradus. Una enim et eadem forma est per essentiam, per quam homo est ens actu, et per quam est corpus, et per quam est vivum, et per quam est animal, et per quam est homo. Manifestum est autem quod unumquodque genus consequuntur propria accidentia. Sicut ergo materia praeintelligitur perfecta secundum esse ante intellectum corporeitatis, et sic de aliis; ita praeintelliguntur accidentia quae sunt propria entis, ante corporeitatem. Et sic praeintelliguntur dispositiones in materia ante formam, non quantum ad omnem eius effectum, sed quantum ad posteriorem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0163","text":"[I q. 76 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31568)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0164","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod dimensiones quantitativae sunt accidentia consequentia corporeitatem, quae toti materiae convenit. Unde materia iam intellecta sub corporeitate et dimensionibus, potest intelligi ut distincta in diversas partes, ut sic accipiat diversas formas secundum ulteriores perfectionis gradus. Quamvis enim eadem forma sit secundum essentiam quae diversos perfectionis gradus materiae attribuit, ut dictum est; tamen secundum considerationem rationis differt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0165","text":"[I q. 76 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31569)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0166","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod substantia spiritualis quae unitur corpori solum ut motor, unitur ei per potentiam vel virtutem. Sed anima intellectiva corpori unitur ut forma per suum esse. Administrat tamen ipsum et movet per suam potentiam et virtutem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0167","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0168","text":"[I q. 76 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31570)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0169","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima uniatur corpori animalis mediante aliquo corpore. Dicit enim Augustinus, VII super Gen. ad Litt., quod anima per lucem, idest ignem, et aerem, *quae sunt similiora spiritui, corpus administrat*. Ignis autem et aer sunt corpora. Ergo anima unitur corpori humano mediante aliquo corpore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0170","text":"[I q. 76 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31571)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0171","text":"Praeterea, id quo subtracto solvitur unio aliquorum unitorum, videtur esse medium inter ea. Sed deficiente spiritu, anima a corpore separatur. Ergo spiritus, qui est quoddam corpus subtile, medium est in unione corporis et animae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0172","text":"[I q. 76 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31572)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0173","text":"Praeterea, ea quae sunt multum distantia, non uniuntur nisi per medium. Sed anima intellectiva distat a corpore et quia est incorporea, et quia est incorruptibilis. Ergo videtur quod uniatur ei mediante aliquo quod sit corpus incorruptibile. Et hoc videtur esse aliqua lux caelestis, quae conciliat elementa et redigit in unum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0174","text":"[I q. 76 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31573)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0175","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in II de anima, quod *non oportet quaerere si unum est anima et corpus, sicut neque ceram et figuram*. Sed figura unitur cerae nullo corpore mediante. Ergo et anima corpori.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0176","text":"[I q. 76 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31574)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0177","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod si anima, secundum Platonicos, corpori uniretur solum ut motor, conveniens esset dicere quod inter animam hominis, vel cuiuscumque animalis, et corpus aliqua alia corpora media intervenirent, convenit enim motori aliquid distans per media magis propinqua movere. Si vero anima unitur corpori ut forma, sicut iam dictum est, impossibile est quod uniatur ei aliquo corpore mediante. Cuius ratio est, quia sic dicitur aliquid unum, quomodo et ens. Forma autem per seipsam facit rem esse in actu, cum per essentiam suam sit actus; nec dat esse per aliquod medium. Unde unitas rei compositae ex materia et forma est per ipsam formam, quae secundum seipsam unitur materiae ut actus eius. Nec est aliquid aliud uniens nisi agens, quod facit materiam esse in actu, ut dicitur in VIII Metaphys.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0178","text":"Unde patet esse falsas opiniones eorum qui posuerunt aliqua corpora esse media inter animam et corpus hominis. Quorum quidam Platonici dixerunt quod anima intellectiva habet corpus incorruptibile sibi naturaliter unitum, a quo nunquam separatur, et eo mediante unitur corpori hominis corruptibili. Quidam vero dixerunt quod unitur corpori mediante spiritu corporeo. Alii vero dixerunt quod unitur corpori mediante luce, quam dicunt esse corpus, et de natura quintae essentiae, ita quod anima vegetabilis unitur corpori mediante luce caeli siderei; anima vero sensibilis, mediante luce caeli crystallini; anima vero intellectualis, mediante luce caeli Empyrei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0179","text":"Quod fictitium et derisibile apparet, tum quia lux non est corpus; tum quia quinta essentia non venit materialiter in compositionem corporis mixti, cum sit inalterabilis, sed virtualiter tantum; tum etiam quia anima immediate corpori unitur ut forma materiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0180","text":"[I q. 76 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31575)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0181","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus loquitur de anima inquantum movet corpus, unde utitur verbo administrationis. Et verum est quod partes grossiores corporis per subtiliores movet. Et primum instrumentum virtutis motivae est spiritus, ut dicit philosophus in libro de causa motus animalium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0182","text":"[I q. 76 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31576)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0183","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, subtracto spiritu, deficit unio animae ad corpus, non quia sit medium; sed quia tollitur dispositio per quam corpus est dispositum ad talem unionem. Est tamen spiritus medium in movendo, sicut primum instrumentum motus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0184","text":"[I q. 76 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31577)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0185","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod anima distat quidem a corpore plurimum, si utriusque conditiones seorsum considerentur, unde si utrumque ipsorum separatim esse haberet, oporteret quod multa media intervenirent. Sed inquantum anima est forma corporis, non habet esse seorsum ab esse corporis; sed per suum esse corpori unitur immediate. Sic enim et quaelibet forma, si consideretur ut actus, habet magnam distantiam a materia, quae est ens in potentia tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0186","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0187","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31578)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0188","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima non sit tota in qualibet parte corporis. Dicit enim philosophus, in libro de causa motus animalium, *non opus est in unaquaque corporis parte esse animam; sed in quodam principio corporis existente, alia vivere; eo quod simul nata sunt facere proprium motum per naturam*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0189","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31579)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0190","text":"Praeterea, anima est in corpore cuius est actus. Sed est actus corporis organici. Ergo non est nisi in corpore organico. Sed non quaelibet pars corporis hominis est corpus organicum. Ergo anima non est in qualibet parte corporis tota.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0191","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31580)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0192","text":"Praeterea, in II de anima dicitur quod sicut se habet pars animae ad partem corporis, ut visus ad pupillam, ita anima tota ad totum corpus animalis. Si igitur tota anima est in qualibet parte corporis, sequitur quod quaelibet pars corporis sit animal.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0193","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31581)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0194","text":"Praeterea, omnes potentiae animae in ipsa essentia animae fundantur. Si igitur anima tota est in qualibet parte corporis, sequitur quod omnes potentiae animae sint in qualibet corporis parte, et ita visus erit in aure, et auditus in oculo. Quod est inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0195","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31582)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0196","text":"Praeterea, si in qualibet parte corporis esset tota anima, quaelibet pars corporis immediate dependeret ab anima. Non ergo una pars dependeret ab alia, nec una pars esset principalior quam alia, quod est manifeste falsum. Non ergo anima est in qualibet parte corporis tota.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0197","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31583)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0198","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in VI de Trin., quod *anima in quocumque corpore et in toto est tota, et in qualibet eius parte tota est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0199","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31584)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0200","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut in aliis iam dictum est, si anima uniretur corpori solum ut motor, posset dici quod non esset in qualibet parte corporis, sed in una tantum, per quam alias moveret. Sed quia anima unitur corpori ut forma, necesse est quod sit in toto, et in qualibet parte corporis. Non enim est forma corporis accidentalis, sed substantialis. Substantialis autem forma non solum est perfectio totius, sed cuiuslibet partis. Cum enim totum consistat ex partibus, forma totius quae non dat esse singulis partibus corporis, est forma quae est compositio et ordo, sicut forma domus, et talis forma est accidentalis. Anima vero est forma substantialis, unde oportet quod sit forma et actus non solum totius, sed cuiuslibet partis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0201","text":"Et ideo, recedente anima, sicut non dicitur animal et homo nisi aequivoce, quemadmodum et animal pictum vel lapideum; ita est de manu et oculo, aut carne et osse, ut philosophus dicit. Cuius signum est, quod nulla pars corporis habet proprium opus, anima recedente, cum tamen omne quod retinet speciem, retineat operationem speciei. Actus autem est in eo cuius est actus. Unde oportet animam esse in toto corpore, et in qualibet eius parte. Et quod tota sit in qualibet parte eius, hinc considerari potest, quia, cum totum sit quod dividitur in partes, secundum triplicem divisionem est triplex totalitas. Est enim quoddam totum quod dividitur in partes quantitativas, sicut tota linea vel totum corpus. Est etiam quoddam totum quod dividitur in partes rationis et essentiae; sicut definitum in partes definitionis, et compositum resolvitur in materiam et formam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0202","text":"Tertium autem totum est potentiale, quod dividitur in partes virtutis. Primus autem totalitatis modus non convenit formis, nisi forte per accidens; et illis solis formis, quae habent indifferentem habitudinem ad totum quantitativum et partes eius. Sicut albedo, quantum est de sui ratione, aequaliter se habet ut sit in tota superficie et in qualibet superficiei parte; et ideo, divisa superficie, dividitur albedo per accidens. Sed forma quae requirit diversitatem in partibus, sicut est anima, et praecipue animalium perfectorum, non aequaliter se habet ad totum et partes, unde non dividitur per accidens per divisionem quantitatis. Sic ergo totalitas quantitativa non potest attribui animae nec per se nec per accidens. Sed totalitas secunda, quae attenditur secundum rationis et essentiae perfectionem, proprie et per se convenit formis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0203","text":"Similiter autem et totalitas virtutis, quia forma est operationis principium. Si ergo quaereretur de albedine, utrum esset tota in tota superficie et in qualibet eius parte, distinguere oporteret. Quia si fiat mentio de totalitate quantitativa, quam habet albedo per accidens, non tota esset in qualibet parte superficiei. Et similiter dicendum est de totalitate virtutis magis enim potest movere visum albedo quae est in tota superficie, quam albedo quae est in aliqua eius particula. Sed si fiat mentio de totalitate speciei et essentiae, tota albedo est in qualibet superficiei parte. Sed quia anima totalitatem quantitativam non habet, nec per se nec per accidens, ut dictum est; sufficit dicere quod anima tota est in qualibet parte corporis secundum totalitatem perfectionis et essentiae; non autem secundum totalitatem virtutis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0204","text":"Quia non secundum quamlibet suam potentiam est in qualibet parte corporis; sed secundum visum in oculo, secundum auditum in aure, et sic de aliis. Tamen attendendum est quod, quia anima requirit diversitatem in partibus, non eodem modo comparatur ad totum et ad partes, sed ad totum quidem primo et per se, sicut ad proprium et proportionatum perfectibile; ad partes autem per posterius, secundum quod habent ordinem ad totum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0205","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31585)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0206","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod philosophus loquitur de potentia motiva animae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0207","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31586)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0208","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod anima est actus corporis organici, sicut primi et proportionati perfectibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0209","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31587)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0210","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod animal est quod componitur ex anima et corpore toto, quod est primum et proportionatum eius perfectibile. Sic autem anima non est in parte. Unde non oportet quod pars animalis sit animal.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0211","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31588)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0212","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod potentiarum animae quaedam sunt in ea secundum quod excedit totam corporis capacitatem, scilicet intellectus et voluntas, unde huiusmodi potentiae in nulla parte corporis esse dicuntur. Aliae vero potentiae sunt communes animae et corpori, unde talium potentiarum non oportet quod quaelibet sit in quocumque est anima; sed solum in illa parte corporis quae est proportionata ad talis potentiae operationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0213","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31589)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0214","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod una pars corporis dicitur esse principalior quam alia, propter potentias diversas quarum sunt organa partes corporis. Quae enim est principalioris potentiae organum, est principalior pars corporis, vel quae etiam eidem potentiae principalius deservit.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"0242219a0617cefe7afe20884bfbe885c40060d0ef9abe8cf6dffa2b3b3c0113","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T150444059055Z_d208441f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 76","source_sha256":"0242219a0617cefe7afe20884bfbe885c40060d0ef9abe8cf6dffa2b3b3c0113","sha256":"88e95c940f5f68290a23c5d36e891bfafd9849fc0ed5b8806fee7dc4c2e6bd24","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:04:44.059071+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0002","text":"[I q. 76 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31504)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0003","text":"Next, consideration must be given to the union of the soul with the body. And concerning this, eight questions are asked. First, whether the intellectual principle is united to the body as form. Second, whether the intellectual principle is multiplied in number according to the multiplication of bodies, or whether there is one intellect for all human beings. Third, whether, in a body whose form is the intellectual principle, there is some other soul. Fourth, whether there is in it some other substantial form. Fifth, what kind of body that whose form is the intellectual principle ought to be. Sixth, whether it is united to such a body by means of some other body. Seventh, whether by means of some accident. Eighth, whether the soul is whole in each part of the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0005","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31505)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the intellectual principle is not united to the body as form. For the Philosopher says, in III *On the Soul*, that the intellect is separate, and that it is the actuality of no body. Therefore it is not united to the body as form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0007","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31506)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0008","text":"Further, every form is determined according to the nature of the matter of which it is the form; otherwise, a proportion between matter and form would not be required. Therefore, if intellect were united to the body as form, since every body has a determinate nature, it would follow that intellect had a determinate nature. And thus it would not be cognizant of all things, as is clear from what has been said above, which is contrary to the nature of intellect. Therefore intellect is not united to the body as form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0009","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31507)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0010","text":"Further, every receptive power that is the actuality of some body receives form materially and individually, because what is received is in the receiver according to the mode of the receiver. But the form of the thing understood is not received in the intellect materially and individually, but rather immaterially and universally; otherwise the intellect would not be cognizant of immaterial and universal things, but only of singulars, just as sense is. Therefore intellect is not united to the body as form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0011","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31508)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0012","text":"Further, power and action belong to the same thing; for that which is able to act is the same as that which acts. But intellectual action is not that of any body, as is clear from what has been said above. Therefore neither is intellectual power a power of any body. But a virtue or power cannot be more abstract or simpler than the essence from which the virtue or power is derived. Therefore neither is the substance of the intellect the form of a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0013","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31509)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0014","text":"Further, that which has being through itself is not united to a body as form, because form is that by which something is; and thus the very being of a form does not belong to the form itself through itself. But the intellectual principle has being through itself, and is subsistent, as was said above. Therefore it is not united to the body as form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0015","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31510)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0016","text":"Further, what belongs to something through itself always belongs to it. But it belongs to form through itself to be united to matter; for it is the actuality of matter not through some accident but through its essence; otherwise, from matter and form one thing would not come to be substantially, but accidentally. Therefore form cannot exist without its proper matter. But the intellectual principle, since it is incorruptible, as was shown above, remains not united to the body when the body is corrupted. Therefore the intellectual principle is not united to the body as form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0017","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31511)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0018","text":"On the contrary, according to the Philosopher, in VIII *Metaphysics*, the difference is taken from the form of a thing. But the constitutive difference of man is rational, which is said of man by reason of the intellectual principle. Therefore the intellectual principle is the form of man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0019","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31512)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0020","text":"I answer that it must be said that the intellect, which is the principle of intellectual operation, is the form of the human body. For that by which something first operates is the form of that to which the operation is attributed, just as that by which the body is first made healthy is health, and that by which the soul first knows is knowledge; hence health is the form of the body, and knowledge of the soul. And the reason for this is that nothing acts except insofar as it is in act; hence, by that by which something is in act, it acts. Now it is manifest that the first thing by which the body lives is the soul. And since life is manifested according to diverse operations in the diverse grades of living things, that by which we first carry out each of these works of life is the soul; for the soul is that by which we are first nourished, and sense, and move in place; and likewise that by which we first understand. Therefore this principle by which we first understand, whether it be called intellect or intellectual soul, is the form of the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0021","text":"And this is Aristotle's demonstration in II *On the Soul*. But if anyone should wish to say that the intellectual soul is not the form of the body, he must find a way in which this action, which is understanding, is the action of this man; for each one experiences himself to be the one who understands. Now an action is attributed to something in three ways, as is clear from the Philosopher, V *Physics*: for something is said to move or act either according to its whole self, as the physician heals; or according to a part, as a man sees through the eye; or accidentally, as a white thing is said to build because it happens that the builder is white. Therefore, when we say that Socrates or Plato understands, it is manifest that this is not attributed to him accidentally; for it is attributed to him insofar as he is a man, which is predicated of him essentially.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0022","text":"Therefore one must either say that Socrates understands according to his whole self, as Plato held, saying that man is an intellectual soul; or one must say that intellect is some part of Socrates. And the first indeed cannot stand, as was shown above, because the very same man is the one who perceives himself both to understand and to sense; but sensing is not without a body, whence the body must be some part of man. It remains, therefore, that the intellect by which Socrates understands is some part of Socrates, such that intellect is in some way united to the body of Socrates. But the Commentator, in III *On the Soul*, says that this union is through the intelligible species. This indeed has a twofold subject: one, namely, the possible intellect; and the other, the phantasms themselves, which are in bodily organs. And thus, through the intelligible species, the possible intellect is conjoined to the body of this or that man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0023","text":"But this conjunction or union is not sufficient for the action of the intellect to be the action of Socrates. And this is clear from a likeness in sense, from which Aristotle proceeds to consider those things that belong to intellect. For phantasms are related to intellect, as is said in III *On the Soul*, as colors are to sight. Therefore, just as species of colors are in sight, so species of phantasms are in the possible intellect. Now it is clear that from the fact that colors are on a wall, whose likenesses are in sight, the action of sight is not attributed to the wall; for we do not say that the wall sees, but rather that it is seen. Therefore, from the fact that species of phantasms are in the possible intellect, it does not follow that Socrates, in whom the phantasms are, understands; but rather that he himself, or his phantasms, are understood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0024","text":"But some have wished to say that intellect is united to the body as mover; and thus from intellect and body one thing comes to be, so that the action of intellect can be attributed to the whole. But this is vain in many ways. First, indeed, because intellect does not move the body except through appetite, the movement of which presupposes the operation of intellect. Therefore it is not because Socrates is moved by intellect that he understands; but rather conversely, because he understands, Socrates is moved by intellect. Second, because, since Socrates is an individual in nature whose essence is one, composed of matter and form, if intellect is not his form, it follows that it is outside his essence; and thus intellect will be related to the whole Socrates as mover to moved. But understanding is an action remaining in the agent, not passing over into another, as heating does. Therefore understanding cannot be attributed to Socrates on account of his being moved by intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0025","text":"Third, because the action of a mover is never attributed to the moved thing except as to an instrument, as the action of a carpenter is attributed to a saw. Therefore, if understanding is attributed to Socrates because it is the action of his mover, it follows that it is attributed to him as to an instrument. This is contrary to the Philosopher, who holds that understanding does not take place through a corporeal instrument. Fourth, because, although the action of a part is attributed to the whole, as the action of the eye to the human being, nevertheless it is never attributed to another part except perhaps accidentally; for we do not say that the hand sees because the eye sees. Therefore, if the intellect and Socrates are made one in the aforesaid manner, the action of the intellect cannot be attributed to Socrates.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0026","text":"But if Socrates is the whole composed from the union of the intellect with the other things that are Socrates, and yet the intellect is united to the other things that are Socrates only as a mover, it follows that Socrates is not one simply, and consequently neither is he a being simply; for a thing is a being in the manner in which it is one. Therefore, the only remaining mode is that which Aristotle posits: that this human being understands because the intellectual principle is his form. Thus, from the very operation of the intellect it appears that the intellectual principle is united to the body as form. The same can also be shown from the account of the human species. For the nature of each thing is shown by its operation. But the proper operation of the human being, insofar as he is human, is understanding; for by this he transcends all animals. Hence Aristotle also, in the book of the Ethics, places ultimate happiness in this operation, as in that which is proper to the human being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0027","text":"Therefore, a human being must receive his species according to that which is the principle of this operation. But each thing receives its species through its proper form. It remains, therefore, that the intellectual principle is the proper form of the human being. But it must be considered that the nobler a form is, the more it dominates corporeal matter, the less it is immersed in it, and the more it exceeds it in its operation or power. Hence we see that the form of a mixed body has some operation that is not caused by the elemental qualities. And the further one proceeds in the nobility of forms, the more one finds the power of the form to exceed elemental matter, as the vegetative soul does more than the form of a metal, and the sensitive soul more than the vegetative soul. But the human soul is the highest in the nobility of forms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0028","text":"Hence it exceeds corporeal matter by its power to such an extent that it has an operation and power in which corporeal matter in no way shares. And this power is called intellect. But it should be noted that, if someone were to posit that the soul is composed of matter and form, he could in no way say that the soul is the form of the body. For since form is act, whereas matter is being only in potency, that which is composed of matter and form can in no way be the form of another according to its whole self. But if according to something of itself it is form, we call that which is form the soul, and that of which it is the form we call the first animate thing, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0029","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31513)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0030","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that, as the Philosopher says in Physics II, the last of natural forms, at which the consideration of the natural philosopher terminates—namely, the human soul—is indeed separate, yet nevertheless in matter. He proves this from the fact that a human being and the sun generate a human being from matter. It is separate indeed according to the intellectual power, because the intellectual power is not the power of any corporeal organ, as the power of sight is the act of the eye; for understanding is an act that cannot be exercised through a corporeal organ, as seeing is exercised. But it is in matter insofar as the soul itself, whose power this is, is the form of the body and the terminus of human generation. Thus, therefore, the Philosopher says in On the Soul III that the intellect is separate because it is not the power of any corporeal organ.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0031","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31514)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0032","text":"And through this the response to the second and third is clear. For it is sufficient, for a human being to be able to understand all things through the intellect, and for the intellect to understand immaterial and universal things, that the intellectual power is not the act of a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0033","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31515)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0034","text":"To the fourth it must be said that the human soul is not a form immersed in corporeal matter, or totally comprehended by it, on account of its perfection. And therefore nothing prevents some power of it from not being the act of a body, although the soul according to its essence is the form of the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0035","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31516)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0036","text":"To the fifth it must be said that the soul communicates to corporeal matter that being in which it itself subsists, from which the intellectual soul and matter become one, such that the being which belongs to the whole composite is also that of the soul itself. This does not occur in other forms, which are not subsistent. And for this reason the human soul remains in its being when the body is destroyed, but other forms do not.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0037","text":"[I q. 76 a. 1 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31517)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0038","text":"To the sixth it must be said that it belongs to the soul in itself to be united to the body, just as it belongs to a light body in itself to be above. And just as a light body indeed remains light when it has been separated from its proper place, yet with an aptitude and inclination toward its proper place, so the human soul remains in its being when it has been separated from the body, having a natural aptitude and inclination toward union with the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0039","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0040","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31518)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0041","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the intellectual principle is not multiplied according to the multiplication of bodies, but that there is one intellect in all human beings. For no immaterial substance is multiplied numerically within one species. But the human soul is an immaterial substance, for it is not composed of matter and form, as was shown above. Therefore, there are not many within one species. But all human beings are of one species. Therefore, there is one intellect of all human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0042","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31519)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0043","text":"Further, when the cause is removed, the effect is removed. Therefore, if human souls were multiplied according to the multiplication of bodies, it would seem to follow that, when the bodies were removed, the multitude of souls would not remain, but from all the souls some one thing alone would remain. This is heretical, for the distinction of rewards and punishments would perish.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0044","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31520)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0045","text":"Further, if my intellect is other than your intellect, my intellect is some individual, and likewise your intellect; for particulars are those which differ in number and agree in one species. But everything received in something is in it according to the mode of the receiver. Therefore, the species of things would be received individually in my intellect and yours, which is contrary to the nature of the intellect, which is cognitive of universals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0046","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31521)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0047","text":"Further, the thing understood is in the intellect that understands. Therefore, if my intellect is other than your intellect, that which is understood by me must be other than that which is understood by you. And thus it will be numerically individuated, and understood only in potency; and it will be necessary to abstract a common intelligible intention from both, because from any diverse things it is possible to abstract some common intelligible. This is contrary to the nature of the intellect, because thus intellect would not seem to be distinguished from the imaginative power. Therefore, it seems to remain that there is one intellect of all human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0048","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31522)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0049","text":"Moreover, when a disciple receives knowledge from a teacher, it cannot be said that the teacher’s knowledge generates knowledge in the disciple, because in that case knowledge too would be an active form, like heat; and this is evidently false. Therefore, it seems that the numerically same knowledge which is in the teacher is communicated to the disciple. But this cannot be unless there is one intellect for both. Therefore, it seems that the intellect of the disciple and the teacher is one; and consequently, that of all human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0050","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31523)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0051","text":"Moreover, Augustine says in the book *On the Quantity of the Soul*: *if I were to say that there are merely several human souls, I myself would laugh at myself*. But the soul seems especially to be one with respect to intellect. Therefore, there is one intellect of all human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0052","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31524)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0053","text":"On the contrary, the Philosopher says in *Physics* II that, as universal causes are related to universals, so particular causes are related to particulars. But it is impossible for one soul in species to belong to animals diverse in species. Therefore, it is impossible for one intellectual soul numerically to belong to things diverse in number.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0054","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31525)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0055","text":"I answer that it is altogether impossible for the intellect to be one for all human beings. This is indeed evident if, according to Plato’s opinion, man himself is the intellect. For if Socrates and Plato had but one intellect, it would follow that Socrates and Plato are one man, and that they are distinguished from one another only by that which is outside the essence of each. The distinction between Socrates and Plato would then be no other than that between a man clothed in a tunic and one clothed in a cape, which is altogether absurd. Likewise, this is evidently impossible if, according to Aristotle’s opinion, intellect is held to be a part, or power, of the soul that is the form of man. For it is impossible for one form to belong to a plurality numerically diverse, just as it is impossible for them to have one being, since form is the principle of being. Likewise, this is evidently impossible in whatever way one may posit the union of intellect with this man and that man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0056","text":"For it is evident that, if there is one principal agent and two instruments, one agent can be spoken of simply, but several actions, as when one man touches different things with two hands: there will be one toucher, but two touchings. But if, conversely, there is one instrument and diverse principal agents, there will indeed be several agents, but one action, as when many men draw a ship with one rope: there will be many drawers, but one drawing. But if there is one principal agent and one instrument, there will be said to be one agent and one action, as when a smith strikes with one hammer, there is one striker and one striking. Now it is evident that, however the intellect may be united or conjoined to this or that man, intellect has preeminence among the other things that pertain to man; for the sensitive powers obey the intellect and serve it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0057","text":"If, therefore, it were posited that there are several intellects and one sense for two human beings—for example, if two men had one eye—there would indeed be several seers, but one vision. But if the intellect is one, however much the other things which the intellect uses as instruments may be diversified, Socrates and Plato can in no way be called other than one understander. And if we add that understanding itself, which is the action of the intellect, does not take place through any other organ than the intellect itself, it will further follow that there are one agent and one action; that is, that all human beings are one understander and one understanding; I mean with respect to the same intelligible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0058","text":"But my intellectual action and yours could be diversified through the diversity of phantasms—namely, because the phantasm of a stone in me is other than that in you—if the phantasm itself, insofar as it is other in me and other in you, were the form of the possible intellect. For the same agent produces diverse actions according to diverse forms, just as, according to the diverse forms of things in relation to the same eye, there are diverse visions. But the phantasm itself is not the form of the possible intellect, but rather the intelligible species that is abstracted from phantasms. Yet in one intellect, from diverse phantasms of the same species, only one intelligible species is abstracted.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0059","text":"This appears in one man, in whom there can be diverse phantasms of a stone, and yet from all of them one intelligible species of stone is abstracted, through which the intellect of one man, by one operation, understands the nature of stone, notwithstanding the diversity of the phantasms. Therefore, if there were one intellect for all human beings, the diversity of phantasms that are in this man and that one could not cause a diversity of the intellectual operation of this and that man, as the Commentator imagines in *On the Soul* III. It remains, therefore, that it is altogether impossible and unfitting to posit one intellect for all human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0060","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31526)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0061","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although the intellectual soul does not have matter from which it is, just as neither does an angel, nevertheless it is the form of some matter; which does not belong to an angel. Therefore, according to the division of matter there are many souls of one species; but many angels of one species cannot exist at all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0062","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31527)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0063","text":"To the second, it must be said that everything has unity in the mode in which it has being, and consequently the judgment concerning the multiplication of a thing is the same as that concerning its being. Now it is evident that the intellectual soul is united to the body according to its being as form; and yet, when the body is destroyed, the intellectual soul remains in its being. And by the same reasoning, the multitude of souls is according to the multitude of bodies; yet, when the bodies have been destroyed, the souls remain multiplied in their being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0064","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31528)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0065","text":"To the third, it must be said that the individuation of the one understanding, or of the species through which it understands, does not exclude the understanding of universals; otherwise, since separate intellects are certain subsisting substances, and consequently particular, they could not understand universals. But the materiality of the knower and of the species through which something is known impedes universal cognition. For just as every action is according to the mode of the form by which the agent acts, as heating is according to the mode of heat, so cognition is according to the mode of the species by which the knower knows. Now it is evident that a common nature is distinguished and multiplied according to individuating principles, which are on the side of matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0066","text":"Therefore, if the form through which cognition takes place is material, not abstracted from the conditions of matter, it will be a likeness of the nature of the species or genus insofar as it is distinguished and multiplied by individuating principles, and thus the nature of the thing will not be able to be known in its commonality. But if the species is abstracted from the conditions of individual matter, it will be a likeness of the nature without those things that distinguish and multiply it, and thus the universal will be known. Nor does it matter in this respect whether there is one intellect or several, because even if there were only one, it would have to be some particular thing, and the species through which it understands some particular thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0067","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31529)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0068","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, whether the intellect is one or several, that which is understood is one. For that which is understood is not in the intellect according to itself, but according to its likeness; for *the stone is not in the soul, but the species of the stone*, as is said in *On the Soul* III. And yet the stone is that which is understood, not the species of the stone, except through the intellect’s reflection upon itself; otherwise, the sciences would not be about things, but about intelligible species. Now the same thing can be assimilated in different ways according to different forms. And because cognition occurs according to the assimilation of the knower to the known thing, it follows that the same thing can be known by diverse knowers, as is clear in sense: for several see the same color according to diverse likenesses. Likewise, several intellects understand one understood thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0069","text":"But, according to Aristotle’s opinion, there is this difference only between sense and intellect: that a thing is sensed according to the disposition which it has outside the soul, in its particularity; whereas the nature of the thing that is understood is indeed outside the soul, but it does not have outside the soul that mode of being according to which it is understood. For the common nature is understood with individuating principles set aside; but it does not have this mode of being outside the soul. But according to Plato’s opinion, the understood thing is outside the soul in the mode in which it is understood; for he posited natures of things separated from matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0070","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31530)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0071","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that the knowledge in the disciple is one thing, and that in the teacher another. But how it is caused will be shown later.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0072","text":"[I q. 76 a. 2 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31531)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0073","text":"To the sixth it must be said that Augustine understands souls not to be many only insofar as they are united in one specific nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0074","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0075","text":"[I q. 76 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31532)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0076","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that, besides the intellective soul, there are in a human being other souls differing in essence, namely the sensitive and the nutritive. For the corruptible and the incorruptible are not of one substance. But the intellective soul is incorruptible; whereas the other souls, namely the sensitive and nutritive, are corruptible, as is clear from what has been said above. Therefore, in a human being there cannot be one essence of the intellective, sensitive, and nutritive soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0077","text":"[I q. 76 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31533)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0078","text":"If it be said that the sensitive soul in a human being is incorruptible: on the contrary, the corruptible and the incorruptible differ in genus, as is said in Metaphysics X. But the sensitive soul in a horse and a lion and other brute animals is corruptible. Therefore, if it is incorruptible in a human being, the sensitive soul in a human being and in a brute will not be of the same genus. But an animal is so called from its having a sensitive soul. Therefore animal will not be one genus common to human beings and other animals. Which is unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0079","text":"[I q. 76 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31534)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0080","text":"Moreover, the Philosopher says, in the book On the Generation of Animals, that the embryo is an animal before it is a human being. But this could not be if the sensitive and intellective soul were of the same essence; for it is an animal through the sensitive soul, but a human being through the intellective soul. Therefore, in a human being the sensitive and intellective soul are not of one essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0081","text":"[I q. 76 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31535)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0082","text":"Moreover, the Philosopher says, in Metaphysics VIII, that genus is taken from matter, whereas difference is taken from form. But rational, which is the constitutive difference of a human being, is taken from the intellective soul; while animal is so called from the fact that it has a body animated by a sensitive soul. Therefore the intellective soul is related to the body animated by a sensitive soul as form to matter. Therefore the intellective soul is not the same in essence as the sensitive soul in a human being; rather, it presupposes it as a material subject.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0083","text":"[I q. 76 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31536)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0084","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in the book On Ecclesiastical Dogmas: *nor do we say that there are two souls in one human being, as James and other Syrians write, one animal soul, by which the body is animated and which is mingled with the blood, and another spiritual soul, which ministers to reason; but we say that the soul in a human being is one and the same, which both gives life to the body by its association and disposes itself by its reason*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0085","text":"[I q. 76 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31537)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0086","text":"I answer that it must be said that Plato posited diverse souls in one body, distinct also according to organs, to which he attributed diverse works of life; saying that the nutritive power is in the liver, the concupiscible in the heart, and the cognitive in the brain. Aristotle indeed reproves this opinion, in the book On the Soul, as regards those parts of the soul which use bodily organs in their operations, on the ground that in animals which live after being cut apart, the diverse operations of the soul, such as sense and appetite, are found in each part. But this would not be so if the diverse principles of the soul's operations, as being diverse in essence, were distributed among diverse parts of the body. But concerning the intellective, he seems to leave it in doubt whether it is separate from the other parts of the soul by reason alone, or also by place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0087","text":"Plato's opinion, however, could indeed be maintained if it were held that the soul is united to the body not as form but as mover, as Plato held. For no incongruity follows if the same movable thing is moved by diverse movers, especially according to diverse parts. But if we posit that the soul is united to the body as form, it seems altogether impossible for several souls differing in essence to be in one body. This can indeed be made clear by three arguments. First, because an animal whose souls were several would not be one simply. For nothing is one simply except through one form, through which a thing has being; for from the same source a thing has both that it is a being and that it is one. Therefore, things denominated from diverse forms are not one simply, as a white human being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0088","text":"If, therefore, a human being had from one form that it is living, namely from the vegetative soul; and from another form that it is an animal, namely from the sensitive soul; and from another that it is a human being, namely from the rational soul; it would follow that a human being was not one simply, just as Aristotle argues against Plato, in Metaphysics VIII, that if the Idea of animal were one thing and that of biped another, a biped animal would not be one simply. And for this reason, in On the Soul I, against those who posit diverse souls in the body, he asks what holds them together, that is, what makes one out of them. Nor can it be said that they are united through the unity of the body, because the soul rather contains the body and makes it to be one than conversely. Second, this appears impossible from the mode of predication.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0089","text":"For those things which are taken from diverse forms are predicated of one another either accidentally, if the forms are not ordered to one another—for example, when we say that the white is sweet—or, if the forms are ordered to one another, the predication will be per se, in the second mode of saying per se, because the subject is placed in the definition of the predicate. Thus surface is preliminary to color; therefore, if we say that a body having a surface is colored, there will be the second mode of per se predication. Therefore, if there is one form by which something is called an animal, and another by which something is called a human being, it would follow either that one of these cannot be predicated of the other except accidentally, if these two forms have no order to one another; or that there is there predication in the second mode of saying per se, if one of the souls is preliminary to the other.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0090","text":"But both of these are manifestly false, because animal is predicated per se of human being, not accidentally; moreover, human being is not placed in the definition of animal, but conversely. Therefore the form by which something is an animal and that by which something is a human being must be the same; otherwise a human being would not truly be that which is an animal, such that animal is predicated per se of human being. Third, this appears impossible from the fact that one operation of the soul, when intensified, impedes another. This would in no way happen unless the principle of the actions were one in essence. Thus, therefore, it must be said that in a human being the sensitive, intellective, and nutritive soul is numerically the same. But how this comes about can easily be considered if one attends to the differences of species and forms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0091","text":"For the species and forms of things are found to differ from one another according to more perfect and less perfect: thus, in the order of things, animate things are more perfect than inanimate, animals than plants, and human beings than brute animals; and within each of these genera there are diverse grades. And therefore Aristotle, in Metaphysics VIII, likens the species of things to numbers, which differ in species by the addition or subtraction of unity. And in On the Soul II, he compares diverse souls to the species of figures, one of which contains another, just as a pentagon contains and exceeds a tetragon. Thus, therefore, the intellective soul contains in its power whatever the sensitive soul of brutes and the nutritive soul of plants have.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0092","text":"Therefore, just as a surface having a pentagonal figure is not tetragonal through one figure and pentagonal through another, because the tetragonal figure would be superfluous, since it is contained in the pentagonal; so neither is Socrates a human being through one soul and an animal through another, but through one and the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0093","text":"[I q. 76 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31538)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0094","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the sensitive soul does not have incorruptibility from the fact that it is sensitive, but from the fact that it is intellective, to which incorruptibility belongs. Therefore, when the soul is sensitive only, it is corruptible; but when it has the intellective together with the sensitive, it is incorruptible. For although the sensitive does not confer incorruptibility, it cannot take incorruptibility away from the intellective.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0095","text":"[I q. 76 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31539)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0096","text":"To the second it must be said that forms are not placed in genus or species, but composites are. But a human being is corruptible, just as other animals are. Hence the difference according to corruptible and incorruptible, which is on the part of the forms, does not make a human being differ in genus from other animals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0097","text":"[I q. 76 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31540)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0098","text":"To the third, it must be said that first the embryo has a soul that is sensitive only; when this is cast aside, a more perfect soul comes, which is at once sensitive and intellective, as will be shown more fully below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0099","text":"[I q. 76 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31541)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0100","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that it is not necessary to posit diversity in natural things according to diverse logical notions or intentions, which follow the mode of understanding, because reason can apprehend one and the same thing in diverse ways. Therefore, since, as has been said, the intellective soul contains by its power what the sensitive soul has, and still more besides, reason can consider separately what pertains to the power of the sensitive soul, as something imperfect and material. And because it finds this common to man and the other animals, from this it forms the notion of genus. But that in which the intellective soul surpasses the sensitive, it takes as formal and completing, and from this it forms the difference of man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0101","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0102","text":"[I q. 76 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31542)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0103","text":"We proceed to the fourth thus. It seems that in man there is another form besides the intellective soul. For the Philosopher says, in *On the Soul* II, that *the soul is the actuality of a physical body having life potentially*. Therefore, the soul is related to the body as form to matter. But the body has some substantial form through which it is a body. Therefore, some substantial form precedes the soul in the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0104","text":"[I q. 76 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31543)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0105","text":"Further, man and every animal is self-moving. But everything self-moving is divided into two parts, one of which moves and the other is moved, as is proved in *Physics* VIII. Now the moving part is the soul. Therefore, the other part must be such as can be moved. But prime matter cannot be moved, as is said in *Physics* V, since it is being only in potency; indeed, everything that is moved is a body. Therefore, in man and in every animal there must be another substantial form through which a body is constituted.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0106","text":"[I q. 76 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31544)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0107","text":"Further, order among forms is considered according to their relation to prime matter, for prior and posterior are spoken of by comparison with some principle. Therefore, if there were no other substantial form in man besides the rational soul, but it adhered immediately to prime matter, it would follow that it was in the order of the most imperfect forms, which adhere immediately to matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0108","text":"[I q. 76 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31545)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0109","text":"Further, the human body is a mixed body. But mixture does not take place according to matter alone, because then there would be corruption alone. Therefore, the forms of the elements, which are substantial forms, must remain in the mixed body. Therefore, there are other substantial forms in the human body besides the intellective soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0110","text":"[I q. 76 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31546)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0111","text":"On the contrary, one thing has one substantial being. But a substantial form gives substantial being. Therefore, one thing has only one substantial form. But the soul is the substantial form of man. Therefore, it is impossible that there be in man any other substantial form than the intellective soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0112","text":"[I q. 76 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31547)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0113","text":"I answer that it must be said that, if the intellective soul were posited as not united to the body as form, but only as mover, as the Platonists posited, it would be necessary to say that in man there was another substantial form through which the body, movable by the soul, would be constituted in its being. But if the intellective soul is united to the body as substantial form, as we have already said above, it is impossible that any other substantial form besides it be found in man. For the evidence of this, it should be considered that a substantial form differs from an accidental form in this: that an accidental form does not give being simply, but being such-and-such; just as heat makes its subject not to be simply, but to be hot.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0114","text":"And therefore, when an accidental form comes, something is not said to come to be or to be generated simply, but to become such-and-such or to be disposed in some way; and similarly, when an accidental form departs, something is not said to be corrupted simply, but in a qualified respect. But a substantial form gives being simply, and therefore by its coming something is said to be generated simply, and by its departure to be corrupted simply. And on account of this the ancient natural philosophers, who posited prime matter to be some being in act, for example fire or air or something of this kind, said that nothing is generated or corrupted simply, but held that every coming-to-be is alteration, as is said in *Physics* I.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0115","text":"Therefore, if it were so that, besides the intellective soul, any other substantial form pre-existed in matter, through which the subject of the soul was a being in act, it would follow that the soul did not give being simply; and consequently that it was not a substantial form; and that by the coming of the soul there was not generation simply, nor by its departure corruption simply, but only in a qualified respect. These things are manifestly false. Hence it must be said that there is no other substantial form in man except the intellective soul alone; and that it itself, just as it contains the sensitive and nutritive soul by its power, so also contains by its power all lower forms, and by itself alone does whatever the more imperfect forms do in others. And the same must likewise be said of the sensitive soul in brute animals, and of the nutritive soul in plants, and universally of all more perfect forms in relation to imperfect ones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0116","text":"[I q. 76 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31548)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0117","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Aristotle does not say that the soul is the actuality of a body merely, but the actuality of a physical organic body having life potentially, and that such potency does not cast aside the soul. Hence it is clear that the soul too is included in that whose actuality the soul is said to be, in the manner of speaking by which heat is said to be the actuality of what is hot, and light the actuality of what is luminous; not because there is separately something luminous without light, but because it is luminous through light. And similarly, the soul is said to be the actuality of a body, etc., because through the soul it both is a body, is organic, and has life potentially. But first actuality is called potency in relation to second actuality, which is operation. For such potency does not cast aside, that is, does not exclude, the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0118","text":"[I q. 76 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31549)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0119","text":"To the second, it must be said that the soul does not move the body through its being, according as it is united to the body as form; but through its motive power, the actuality of which presupposes a body already made actual through the soul; so that the soul according to its motive power is the moving part, and the animated body is the moved part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0120","text":"[I q. 76 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31550)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0121","text":"To the third, it must be said that in matter diverse grades of perfection are considered, such as being, living, sensing, and understanding. But what is subsequent to a prior thing is always more perfect. Therefore, the form that gives only the first grade of perfection to matter is most imperfect; but the form that gives the first and second, and third, and so on, is most perfect; and yet immediate to matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0122","text":"[I q. 76 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31551)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0123","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that Avicenna held that the substantial forms of the elements remain entire in the mixture, while mixture takes place insofar as the contrary qualities of the elements are reduced to a mean. But this is impossible. For the diverse forms of the elements can exist only in diverse parts of matter; for the diversity of these, dimensions must be understood, without which matter cannot be divisible. But matter subject to dimension is found only in a body. But diverse bodies cannot be in the same place. Hence it follows that the elements are distinct in the mixture according to position. And thus there will not be true mixture, which is according to the whole, but mixture to sense, which is according to the least parts placed next to one another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0124","text":"But Averroes held, in Book III of On the Heavens, that the forms of the elements, on account of their imperfection, are intermediate between accidental and substantial forms; and therefore they receive more and less; and therefore they are diminished in mixture and reduced to a mean, and one form is fused from them. But this too is even more impossible. For the substantial being of any thing whatsoever consists in the indivisible; and every addition and subtraction varies the species, as in numbers, as is said in Metaphysics VIII. Hence it is impossible that any substantial form should receive more and less. Nor is it less impossible for something to be intermediate between substance and accident. And therefore it must be said, according to the Philosopher in Book I of On Generation, that the forms of the elements remain in the mixture not actually, but virtually. For the proper qualities of the elements remain, though diminished, and in them is the power of the elemental forms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0125","text":"And a quality of mixture of this sort is the proper disposition for the substantial form of a mixed body, for example, the form of a stone, or of any soul whatsoever.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0126","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0127","text":"[I q. 76 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31552)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0128","text":"The fifth point is approached thus. It seems that the intellectual soul is unsuitably united to such a body. For matter ought to be proportioned to form. But the intellectual soul is an incorruptible form. Therefore, it is not suitably united to a corruptible body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0129","text":"[I q. 76 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31553)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0130","text":"Further, the intellectual soul is a form that is maximally immaterial, a sign of which is that it has an operation in which corporeal matter does not share. But the subtler a body is, the less it has of matter. Therefore, the soul ought to be united to a most subtle body, for example fire, and not to a mixed body, and one more terrestrial.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0131","text":"[I q. 76 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31554)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0132","text":"Further, since form is the principle of species, diverse species do not proceed from one form. But the intellectual soul is one form. Therefore, it ought not to be united to a body composed of parts of dissimilar species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0133","text":"[I q. 76 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31555)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0134","text":"Further, a more perfect form ought to have a more perfect recipient. But the intellectual soul is the most perfect of souls. Therefore, since the bodies of other animals have coverings naturally implanted in them, for example hairs in place of clothing and nails in place of shoes, and also have weapons naturally given to them, such as claws, teeth, and horns, it seems that the intellectual soul ought not to have been united to an imperfect body as though deprived of such aids.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0135","text":"[I q. 76 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31556)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0136","text":"But on the contrary is what the Philosopher says, in Book II of On the Soul, that *the soul is the actuality of a physical organic body having life potentially*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0137","text":"[I q. 76 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31557)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0138","text":"I answer that, since form is not for the sake of matter, but rather matter for the sake of form, the reason why matter is such must be taken from the form, and not conversely. But the intellectual soul, as was held above, according to the order of nature occupies the lowest grade among intellectual substances, to such an extent that it does not have knowledge of truth naturally implanted in it, as angels do, but must gather it from divisible things by way of sense, as Dionysius says in chapter VII of On the Divine Names. But nature fails no one in necessities; hence the intellectual soul had to have not only the power of understanding but also the power of sensing. But the act of sense does not occur without a corporeal instrument. Therefore, the intellectual soul had to be united to such a body as could be a suitable organ of sense. But all the other senses are founded upon touch.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0139","text":"But for the organ of touch it is required that it be intermediate between contraries, which are hot and cold, moist and dry, and the like, of which touch is apprehensive; for thus it is in potency toward contraries and can sense them. Hence, the more the organ of touch is reduced to equality of complexion, the more perceptive touch will be. But the intellectual soul has the sensitive power most completely, because what belongs to the lower pre-exists more perfectly in the higher, as Dionysius says in the book On the Divine Names. Hence the body to which the intellectual soul is united had to be a mixed body, more reduced than all others to equality of complexion. And for this reason, among all animals man has the better touch. And among men themselves, those who have the better touch have the better intellect. A sign of this is that we see those soft in flesh to be well disposed in mind, as is said in Book II of On the Soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0140","text":"[I q. 76 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31558)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0141","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that someone might perhaps wish to evade this objection by saying that the human body before sin was incorruptible. But this response does not seem sufficient, because the human body before sin was immortal not by nature, but by the gift of divine grace; otherwise its immortality would not have been removed through sin, just as neither was the immortality of the demon. And therefore it must be said otherwise, that in matter a twofold condition is found: one that is chosen in order that it may be suitable to the form; another that follows necessarily from the prior disposition. Thus an artisan chooses iron material for the form of a saw, suitable for cutting hard things; but that the teeth of the saw can become blunt and contract rust follows from the necessity of the material. Thus, therefore, a body of equal complexion is due to the intellectual soul; but from the necessity of matter it follows that it is corruptible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0142","text":"But if someone says that God could have avoided this necessity, it must be said that in the constitution of natural things one does not consider what God can do, but what befits the nature of things, as Augustine says in Book II, On the Literal Meaning of Genesis. Nevertheless, God provided a remedy against death by the gift of grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0143","text":"[I q. 76 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31559)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0144","text":"To the second, it must be said that a body is not due to the intellectual soul on account of intellectual operation itself; but on account of the sensitive power, which requires an organ of equally balanced complexion. And therefore the intellectual soul had to be united to such a body, and not to a simple element, or to a mixed body in which fire exceeded in quantity, because there could not be equality of complexion on account of the excessive active power of fire. But this body of equally balanced complexion has a certain dignity through being removed from contraries; in this it is in a certain way assimilated to a celestial body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0145","text":"[I q. 76 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31560)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0146","text":"To the third, it must be said that the parts of an animal, such as the eye, hand, flesh, and bone, and things of this sort, are not in a species, but rather the whole is; and therefore, properly speaking, they cannot be said to be of different species, but rather to be of different dispositions. And this befits the intellective soul, which, although it is one according to essence, is nevertheless multiple in power on account of its perfection; and therefore, for diverse operations, it requires diverse dispositions in the parts of the body to which it is united. And on this account we see that there is a greater diversity of parts in perfect animals than in imperfect ones, and in these than in plants.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0147","text":"[I q. 76 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31561)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0148","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the intellective soul, because it comprehends universals, has power toward infinitely many things. And therefore neither determinate natural estimations nor determinate aids, whether of defenses or coverings, could be determined for it by nature, as for the other animals, whose souls have apprehension and power toward certain determinate particulars. But in place of all these, man naturally has reason and hands, which are the instruments of instruments, because by them man can prepare for himself instruments in infinitely many ways and for infinitely many effects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0149","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0150","text":"[I q. 76 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31562)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0151","text":"The sixth is approached thus. It seems that the intellective soul is united to the body by means of certain accidental dispositions. For every form is in matter proper and disposed to it. But dispositions toward form are certain accidents. Therefore some accidents must be understood to preexist in matter before the substantial form, and thus before the soul, since the soul is a certain substantial form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0152","text":"[I q. 76 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31563)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0153","text":"Moreover, diverse forms of one species require diverse parts of matter. But diverse parts of matter cannot be understood except according to the division of dimensional quantities. Therefore dimensions must be understood in matter before substantial forms, which are many within one species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0154","text":"[I q. 76 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31564)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0155","text":"Moreover, what is spiritual is applied to what is corporeal through contact of power. But the power of the soul is its potency. Therefore it seems that the soul is united to the body by means of a potency, which is a certain accident.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0156","text":"[I q. 76 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31565)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0157","text":"But on the contrary, an accident is posterior to substance both in time and in account, as is said in Metaphysics VII. Therefore no accidental form can be understood in matter before the soul, which is a substantial form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0158","text":"[I q. 76 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31566)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0158"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0159","text":"I answer that it must be said that, if the soul were united to the body only as a mover, nothing would prevent—and indeed it would be more necessary—that there be certain intermediate dispositions between soul and body: namely, a power on the part of the soul through which it would move the body, and some aptitude on the part of the body through which the body would be movable by the soul. But if the intellective soul is united to the body as substantial form, as has already been said above, it is impossible that any accidental disposition should fall between body and soul, or between any substantial form whatsoever and its matter. The reason for this is that, since matter is in potency to all acts in a certain order, that which is simply first among acts must be understood first in matter. But the first among all acts is being. Therefore it is impossible to understand matter first to be hot or quantified, before being in act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0159"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0160","text":"Now it has being in act through the substantial form, which causes being simply, as has already been said. Hence it is impossible that any accidental dispositions whatsoever should preexist in matter before the substantial form, and consequently neither before the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0160"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0161","text":"[I q. 76 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31567)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0161"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0162","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, as is clear from what has been said, a more perfect form contains in its power whatever belongs to the lower forms. And therefore, while being one and the same, it perfects matter according to diverse degrees of perfection. For one and the same form is that through whose essence man is a being in act, and through which he is a body, and through which he is living, and through which he is an animal, and through which he is a man. Now it is manifest that proper accidents follow upon each genus. Therefore, just as matter is understood to be perfected according to being before the understanding of corporeity, and so with the others, so accidents proper to being are understood before corporeity. And thus dispositions are understood in matter before the form, not with respect to every one of its effects, but with respect to a later one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0162"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0163","text":"[I q. 76 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31568)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0163"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0164","text":"To the second, it must be said that quantitative dimensions are accidents consequent upon corporeity, which belongs to the whole of matter. Hence matter, already understood under corporeity and dimensions, can be understood as distinct into diverse parts, so that it may thus receive diverse forms according to further degrees of perfection. For although it is the same form according to essence that attributes diverse degrees of perfection to matter, as has been said, nevertheless it differs according to the consideration of reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0164"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0165","text":"[I q. 76 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31569)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0165"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0166","text":"To the third, it must be said that a spiritual substance which is united to a body only as a mover is united to it through potency or power. But the intellective soul is united to the body as form through its being. It nevertheless administers and moves it through its potency and power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0166"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0167","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0167"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0168","text":"[I q. 76 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31570)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0168"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0169","text":"We proceed thus to the seventh. It seems that the soul is united to the body of an animal by means of some body. For Augustine says, in Book VII On the Literal Meaning of Genesis, that the soul administers the body through light, that is, fire, and air, *which are more like spirit*. But fire and air are bodies. Therefore the soul is united to the human body by means of some body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0169"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0170","text":"[I q. 76 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31571)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0170"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0171","text":"Further, that upon whose removal the union of certain things united is dissolved seems to be a medium between them. But when the spirit fails, the soul is separated from the body. Therefore spirit, which is a certain subtle body, is a medium in the union of body and soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0171"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0172","text":"[I q. 76 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31572)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0172"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0173","text":"Further, things that are very distant are not united except through a medium. But the intellectual soul is distant from the body both because it is incorporeal and because it is incorruptible. Therefore it seems that it is united to it by means of something that is an incorruptible body. And this seems to be some celestial light, which brings the elements together and reduces them to one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0173"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0174","text":"[I q. 76 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31573)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0174"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0175","text":"On the contrary, the Philosopher says, in Book II On the Soul, that *one should not inquire whether soul and body are one, any more than wax and its shape*. But shape is united to wax with no body intervening. Therefore so too is the soul to the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0175"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0176","text":"[I q. 76 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31574)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0176"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0177","text":"I answer that, if the soul, according to the Platonists, were united to the body only as a mover, it would be fitting to say that certain other bodies intervened as media between the soul of a human being, or of any animal whatsoever, and the body; for it is fitting that a mover move something distant through nearer intermediaries. But if the soul is united to the body as form, as has already been said, it is impossible for it to be united to it by means of any body. The reason for this is that a thing is said to be one in the same way as it is said to be a being. But form of itself makes a thing to be actual, since through its essence it is act; nor does it give being through any medium. Hence the unity of a thing composed of matter and form is through the form itself, which is united to matter of itself as its act. Nor is there anything else uniting them except the agent, which makes matter to be actual, as is said in Metaphysics VIII.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0177"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0178","text":"Hence the opinions of those who posited certain bodies as media between the soul and the human body are evidently false. Some Platonists among them said that the intellectual soul has an incorruptible body naturally united to it, from which it is never separated, and that through it the soul is united to the corruptible human body. Others, however, said that it is united to the body by means of a corporeal spirit. Still others said that it is united to the body by means of light, which they say is a body and of the nature of the fifth essence: so that the vegetative soul is united to the body by means of the light of the starry heaven; the sensitive soul, by means of the light of the crystalline heaven; and the intellectual soul, by means of the light of the Empyrean heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0178"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0179","text":"This appears fictitious and ridiculous: both because light is not a body; and because the fifth essence does not enter materially into the composition of a mixed body, since it is unalterable, but only virtually; and also because the soul is immediately united to the body as the form of matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0179"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0180","text":"[I q. 76 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31575)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0180"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0181","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Augustine is speaking of the soul insofar as it moves the body, whence he uses the word “administration.” And it is true that it moves the grosser parts of the body through the subtler ones. And the first instrument of the motive power is spirit, as the Philosopher says in the book On the Cause of Animal Motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0181"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0182","text":"[I q. 76 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31576)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0182"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0183","text":"To the second, it must be said that, when spirit is removed, the union of the soul to the body fails, not because it is a medium, but because the disposition through which the body is disposed for such a union is taken away. Nevertheless, spirit is a medium in moving, as the first instrument of motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0183"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0184","text":"[I q. 76 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31577)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0184"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0185","text":"To the third, it must be said that the soul is indeed very distant from the body, if the conditions of each are considered separately; hence, if each of them had being separately, it would be necessary for many intermediaries to intervene. But insofar as the soul is the form of the body, it does not have being separately from the being of the body; rather, through its being it is united to the body immediately. For thus too every form, if considered as act, has a great distance from matter, which is being only in potency.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0185"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0186","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0186"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0187","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31578)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0187"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0188","text":"We proceed thus to the eighth. It seems that the soul is not whole in every part of the body. For the Philosopher says, in the book On the Cause of Animal Motion: *It is not necessary for the soul to be in every part of the body; rather, being in a certain principle of the body, it causes the other parts to live, because they are naturally fitted simultaneously to make their proper motion*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0188"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0189","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31579)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0189"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0190","text":"Further, the soul is in the body of which it is the act. But it is the act of an organic body. Therefore it is only in an organic body. But not every part of the human body is an organic body. Therefore the soul is not whole in every part of the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0190"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0191","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31580)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0191"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0192","text":"Further, it is said in Book II On the Soul that as a part of the soul is related to a part of the body, as sight to the pupil, so is the whole soul related to the whole animal body. Therefore, if the whole soul is in every part of the body, it follows that every part of the body is an animal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0192"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0193","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31581)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0193"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0194","text":"Further, all the powers of the soul are founded in the very essence of the soul. Therefore, if the whole soul is in each part of the body, it follows that all the powers of the soul are in each part of the body, and thus sight will be in the ear and hearing in the eye. This is unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0194"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0195","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31582)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0195"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0196","text":"Further, if the whole soul were in each part of the body, each part of the body would depend immediately on the soul. Therefore one part would not depend on another, nor would one part be more principal than another, which is manifestly false. Therefore the whole soul is not in each part of the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0196"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0197","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31583)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0197"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0198","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, VI, that *the soul, in whatever body it is, is whole in the whole body, and whole in each of its parts*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0198"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0199","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31584)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0199"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0200","text":"I answer that, as has already been said elsewhere, if the soul were united to the body only as a mover, it could be said that it was not in each part of the body, but only in one part, through which it moved the others. But because the soul is united to the body as form, it must be in the whole body and in each part of the body. For it is not an accidental form of the body, but a substantial form. Now a substantial form is not only the perfection of the whole, but of each part. For since the whole consists of parts, the form of a whole that does not give being to the individual parts of the body is a form consisting in composition and order, as the form of a house; and such a form is accidental. But the soul is a substantial form; hence it must be the form and act not only of the whole, but of each part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0200"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0201","text":"And therefore, when the soul departs, just as an animal and a human being are spoken of only equivocally, in the way that a painted or stone animal is, so it is with a hand and an eye, or flesh and bone, as the Philosopher says. A sign of this is that no part of the body has its proper operation when the soul has departed, although everything that retains its species retains the operation of the species. But an act is in that of which it is the act. Hence the soul must be in the whole body and in each of its parts. And that it is whole in each of its parts can be considered from this: since a whole is what is divided into parts, there is a threefold totality according to a threefold division. For there is a certain whole divided into quantitative parts, such as a whole line or a whole body. There is also a certain whole divided into parts of account and essence, as what is defined into the parts of its definition, and a composite is resolved into matter and form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0201"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0202","text":"But the third whole is potential, which is divided into parts of power. The first mode of totality does not belong to forms except perhaps accidentally, and only to those forms which have an indifferent relation to the quantitative whole and its parts. Thus whiteness, as regards its own account, is related equally to being in the whole surface and in each part of the surface; and therefore, when the surface is divided, the whiteness is divided accidentally. But a form that requires diversity in the parts, as the soul does, and especially the soul of perfect animals, is not related equally to the whole and the parts; hence it is not divided accidentally through the division of quantity. Thus quantitative totality cannot be attributed to the soul either per se or accidentally. But the second totality, which is considered according to the perfection of account and essence, belongs properly and per se to forms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0202"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0203","text":"Likewise also does totality of power, because form is a principle of operation. Therefore, if one were to ask of whiteness whether it was whole in the whole surface and in each part of it, one would have to make a distinction. For if mention is made of quantitative totality, which whiteness has accidentally, it would not be whole in each part of the surface. And the same must be said concerning totality of power: for whiteness over the whole surface can move sight more than whiteness in some small part of it. But if mention is made of totality of species and essence, the whole whiteness is in each part of the surface. But because the soul has no quantitative totality, either per se or accidentally, as has been said, it is enough to say that the soul is whole in each part of the body according to the totality of perfection and essence, but not according to totality of power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0203"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0204","text":"For it is not in each part of the body according to each of its powers, but according to sight in the eye, according to hearing in the ear, and so concerning the others. Yet it should be noted that, because the soul requires diversity in the parts, it is not related to the whole and to the parts in the same way, but to the whole primarily and per se, as to its proper and proportionate perfectible; and to the parts secondarily, insofar as they are ordered to the whole.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0204"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0205","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31585)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0205"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0206","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the Philosopher is speaking of the motive power of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0206"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0207","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31586)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0207"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0208","text":"To the second, it must be said that the soul is the act of an organic body, as of its first and proportionate perfectible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0208"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0209","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31587)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0209"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0210","text":"To the third, it must be said that an animal is what is composed of soul and whole body, which is its first and proportionate perfectible. But the soul is not in a part in this way. Hence it is not necessary that a part of an animal be an animal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0210"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0211","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31588)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0211"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0212","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that certain powers of the soul are in it insofar as it exceeds the entire capacity of the body, namely intellect and will; hence powers of this kind are said to be in no part of the body. But other powers are common to soul and body; hence it is not necessary that each such power be wherever the soul is, but only in that part of the body which is proportioned to the operation of such a power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0212"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0213","text":"[I q. 76 a. 8 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1075.html#31589)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0213"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0214","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that one part of the body is called more principal than another on account of the diverse powers of which the parts of the body are organs. For that which is the organ of a more principal power is a more principal part of the body, as is also that which serves the same power more principally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_68:P0214"]}],"source_digest":"36e00173aad8ea358baabf725f3109ad27d9ca7ab397c7c4a165737570b0f75a","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 76"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 76","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69","title":"First Part · Question 77","order":78,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_69","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 77 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31590)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de his quae pertinent ad potentias animae. Et primo, in generali; secundo, in speciali. Circa primum quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum essentia animae sit eius potentia. Secundo, utrum sit una tantum potentia animae, vel plures. Tertio, quomodo potentiae animae distinguantur. Quarto, de ordine ipsarum ad invicem. Quinto, utrum anima sit subiectum omnium potentiarum. Sexto, utrum potentiae fluant ab essentia animae. Septimo, utrum potentia una oriatur ex alia. Octavo, utrum omnes potentiae animae remaneant in ea post mortem.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31591)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ipsa essentia animae sit eius potentia. Dicit enim Augustinus, in IX de Trin., quod *mens, notitia et amor sunt substantialiter in anima, vel, ut idem dicam, essentialiter*. Et in X dicit quod *memoria, intelligentia et voluntas sunt una vita, una mens, una essentia*.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31592)\n\nPraeterea, anima est nobilior quam materia prima. Sed materia prima est sua potentia. Ergo multo magis anima.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31593)\n\nPraeterea, forma substantialis est simplicior quam accidentalis, cuius signum est, quod forma substantialis non intenditur vel remittitur, sed in indivisibili consistit. Forma autem accidentalis est ipsa sua virtus. Ergo multo magis forma substantialis, quae est anima.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31594)\n\nPraeterea, potentia sensitiva est qua sentimus, et potentia intellectiva qua intelligimus. Sed id quo primo sentimus et intelligimus est anima, secundum philosophum, in II de anima. Ergo anima est suae potentiae.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31595)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod non est de essentia rei, est accidens. Si ergo potentia animae est praeter essentiam eius, sequitur quod sit accidens. Quod est contra Augustinum, in IX de Trin., ubi dicit quod *praedicta non sunt in anima sicut in subiecto, ut color aut figura in corpore, aut ulla alia qualitas aut quantitas, quidquid enim tale est, non excedit subiectum in quo est; mens autem potest etiam alia amare et cognoscere*.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31596)\n\nPraeterea, forma simplex subiectum esse non potest. Anima autem est forma simplex, cum non sit composita ex materia et forma, ut supra dictum est. Non ergo potentia animae potest esse in ipsa sicut in subiecto.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31597)\n\nPraeterea, accidens non est principium substantialis differentiae. Sed sensibile et rationale sunt substantiales differentiae, et sumuntur a sensu et ratione, quae sunt potentiae animae. Ergo potentiae animae non sunt accidentia. Et ita videtur quod potentia animae sit eius essentia.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31598)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, XI cap. Caelest. Hier., quod *caelestes spiritus dividuntur in essentiam, virtutem et operationem*. Multo igitur magis in anima aliud est essentia, et aliud virtus sive potentia.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31599)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod impossibile est dicere quod essentia animae sit eius potentia; licet hoc quidam posuerint. Et hoc dupliciter ostenditur, quantum ad praesens. Primo quia, cum potentia et actus dividant ens et quodlibet genus entis, oportet quod ad idem genus referatur potentia et actus. Et ideo, si actus non est in genere substantiae, potentia quae dicitur ad illum actum, non potest esse in genere substantiae. Operatio autem animae non est in genere substantiae; sed in solo Deo, cuius operatio est eius substantia. Unde Dei potentia, quae est operationis principium, est ipsa Dei essentia. Quod non potest esse verum neque in anima, neque in aliqua creatura; ut supra etiam de Angelo dictum est. Secundo, hoc etiam impossibile apparet in anima. Nam anima secundum suam essentiam est actus. Si ergo ipsa essentia animae esset immediatum operationis principium, semper habens animam actu haberet opera vitae; sicut semper habens animam actu est vivum. Non enim, inquantum est forma, est actus ordinatus ad ulteriorem actum, sed est ultimus terminus generationis. Unde quod sit in potentia adhuc ad alium actum, hoc non competit ei secundum suam essentiam, inquantum est forma; sed secundum suam potentiam. Et sic ipsa anima, secundum quod subest suae potentiae, dicitur actus primus, ordinatus ad actum secundum. Invenitur autem habens animam non semper esse in actu operum vitae. Unde etiam in definitione animae dicitur quod est actus corporis potentia vitam habentis, quae tamen potentia non abiicit animam. Relinquitur ergo quod essentia animae non est eius potentia. Nihil enim est in potentia secundum actum, inquantum est actus.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31600)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus loquitur de mente secundum quod noscit se et amat se. Sic ergo notitia et amor, inquantum referuntur ad ipsam ut cognitam et amatam, substantialiter vel essentialiter sunt in anima, quia ipsa substantia vel essentia animae cognoscitur et amatur. Et similiter intelligendum est quod alibi dicit, quod sunt una vita, una mens, una essentia. Vel, sicut quidam dicunt, haec locutio verificatur secundum modum quo totum potestativum praedicatur de suis partibus, quod medium est inter totum universale et totum integrale. Totum enim universale adest cuilibet parti secundum totam suam essentiam et virtutem, ut animal homini et equo, et ideo proprie de singulis partibus praedicatur. Totum vero integrale non est in qualibet parte, neque secundum totam essentiam, neque secundum totam virtutem. Et ideo nullo modo de singulis partibus praedicatur; sed aliquo modo, licet improprie, praedicatur de omnibus simul, ut si dicamus quod paries, tectum et fundamentum sunt domus. Totum vero potentiale adest singulis partibus secundum totam suam essentiam, sed non secundum totam virtutem. Et ideo quodammodo potest praedicari de qualibet parte; sed non ita proprie sicut totum universale. Et per hunc modum Augustinus dicit quod memoria, intelligentia et voluntas sunt una animae essentia.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31601)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod actus ad quem est in potentia materia prima, est substantialis forma. Et ideo potentia materiae non est aliud quam eius essentia.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31602)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod actio est compositi, sicut et esse, existentis enim est agere. Compositum autem per formam substantialem habet esse substantialiter; per virtutem autem quae consequitur formam substantialem, operatur. Unde sic se habet forma accidentalis activa ad formam substantialem agentis (ut calor ad formam ignis), sicut se habet potentia animae ad animam.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31603)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod hoc ipsum quod forma accidentalis est actionis principium, habet a forma substantiali. Et ideo forma substantialis est primum actionis principium, sed non proximum. Et secundum hoc philosophus dicit quod *id quo intelligimus et sentimus, est anima*.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31604)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod, si accidens accipiatur secundum quod dividitur contra substantiam, sic nihil potest esse medium inter substantiam et accidens, quia dividuntur secundum affirmationem et negationem, scilicet secundum esse in subiecto et non esse in subiecto. Et hoc modo, cum potentia animae non sit eius essentia, oportet quod sit accidens, et est in secunda specie qualitatis. Si vero accipiatur accidens secundum quod ponitur unum quinque universalium, sic aliquid est medium inter substantiam et accidens. Quia ad substantiam pertinet quidquid est essentiale rei, non autem quidquid est extra essentiam, potest sic dici accidens, sed solum id quod non causatur ex principiis essentialibus speciei. Proprium enim non est de essentia rei, sed ex principiis essentialibus speciei causatur, unde medium est inter essentiam et accidens sic dictum. Et hoc modo potentiae animae possunt dici mediae inter substantiam et accidens, quasi proprietates animae naturales. Quod autem Augustinus dicit, quod notitia et amor non sunt in anima sicut accidentia in subiecto, intelligitur secundum modum praedictum, prout comparantur ad animam, non sicut ad amantem et cognoscentem; sed prout comparantur ad eam sicut ad amatam et cognitam. Et hoc modo procedit sua probatio, quia si amor esset in anima amata sicut in subiecto, sequeretur quod accidens transcenderet suum subiectum; cum etiam alia sint amata per animam.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31605)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod anima, licet non sit composita ex materia et forma, habet tamen aliquid de potentialitate admixtum ut supra dictum est. Et ideo potest esse subiectum accidentis. Propositio autem inducta locum habet in Deo, qui est actus purus, in qua materia Boetius eam introducit.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31606)\n\nAd septimum dicendum quod rationale et sensibile, prout sunt differentiae, non sumuntur a potentiis sensus et rationis; sed ab ipsa anima sensitiva et rationali. Quia tamen formae substantiales, quae secundum se sunt nobis ignotae, innotescunt per accidentia; nihil prohibet interdum accidentia loco differentiarum substantialium poni.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 77 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31607)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sint plures potentiae animae. Anima enim intellectiva maxime ad divinam similitudinem accedit. Sed in Deo est una et simplex potentia. Ergo et in anima intellectiva.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31608)\n\nPraeterea, quanto virtus est superior, tanto est magis unita. Sed anima intellectiva excedit omnes alias formas in virtute. Ergo maxime debet habere unam virtutem seu potentiam.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31609)\n\nPraeterea, operari est existentis in actu. Sed per eandem essentiam animae homo habet esse secundum diversos gradus perfectionis, ut supra habitum est. Ergo per eandem potentiam animae operatur diversas operationes diversorum graduum.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31610)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus, in II de anima ponit plures animae potentias.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31611)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est ponere plures animae potentias. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod, sicut philosophus dicit in II de caelo, quae sunt in rebus infima, non possunt consequi perfectam bonitatem, sed aliquam imperfectam consequuntur paucis motibus; superiora vero his adipiscuntur perfectam bonitatem motibus multis; his autem superiora sunt quae adipiscuntur perfectam bonitatem motibus paucis; summa vero perfectio invenitur in his quae absque motu perfectam possident bonitatem. Sicut infime est ad sanitatem dispositus qui non potest perfectam consequi sanitatem, sed aliquam modicam consequitur paucis remediis; melius autem dispositus est qui potest perfectam consequi sanitatem, sed remediis multis; et adhuc melius, qui remediis paucis; optime autem, qui absque remedio perfectam sanitatem habet. Dicendum est ergo quod res quae sunt infra hominem, quaedam particularia bona consequuntur, et ideo quasdam paucas et determinatas operationes habent et virtutes. Homo autem potest consequi universalem et perfectam bonitatem, quia potest adipisci beatitudinem. Est tamen in ultimo gradu, secundum naturam, eorum quibus competit beatitudo, et ideo multis et diversis operationibus et virtutibus indiget anima humana. Angelis vero minor diversitas potentiarum competit. In Deo vero non est aliqua potentia vel actio, praeter eius essentiam. Est et alia ratio quare anima humana abundat diversitate potentiarum, videlicet quia est in confinio spiritualium et corporalium creaturarum, et ideo concurrunt in ipsa virtutes utrarumque creaturarum.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31612)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in hoc ipso magis ad similitudinem Dei accedit anima intellectiva quam creaturae inferiores, quod perfectam bonitatem consequi potest; licet per multa et diversa; in quo deficit a superioribus.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31613)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod virtus unita est superior, si ad aequalia se extendat. Sed virtus multiplicata est superior, si plura ei subiiciantur.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31614)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod unius rei est unum esse substantiale, sed possunt esse operationes plures. Et ideo est una essentia animae, sed potentiae plures.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 77 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31615)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod potentiae non distinguantur per actus et obiecta. Nihil enim determinatur ad speciem per illud quod posterius, vel extrinsecum est. Actus autem est posterior potentia; obiectum autem est extrinsecum. Ergo per ea potentiae non distinguuntur secundum speciem.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31616)\n\nPraeterea, contraria sunt quae maxime differunt. Si igitur potentiae distinguerentur penes obiecta, sequeretur quod contrariorum non esset eadem potentia. Quod patet esse falsum fere in omnibus, nam potentia visiva eadem est albi et nigri, et gustus idem est dulcis et amari.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31617)\n\nPraeterea, remota causa, removetur effectus. Si igitur potentiarum differentia esset ex differentia obiectorum, idem obiectum non pertineret ad diversas potentias. Quod patet esse falsum, nam idem est quod potentia cognoscitiva cognoscit, et appetitiva appetit.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31618)\n\nPraeterea, id quod per se est causa alicuius, in omnibus causat illud. Sed quaedam obiecta diversa, quae pertinent ad diversas potentias, pertinent etiam ad aliquam unam potentiam, sicut sonus et color pertinent ad visum et auditum, quae sunt diversae potentiae; et tamen pertinent ad unam potentiam sensus communis. Non ergo potentiae distinguuntur secundum differentiam obiectorum.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31619)\n\nSed contra, posteriora distinguuntur secundum priora. Sed philosophus dicit II de anima, quod *priores potentiis actus et operationes secundum rationem sunt*; et adhuc his priora sunt opposita, sive obiecta. Ergo potentiae distinguuntur secundum actus et obiecta.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31620)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod potentia, secundum illud quod est potentia, ordinatur ad actum. Unde oportet rationem potentiae accipi ex actu ad quem ordinatur, et per consequens oportet quod ratio potentiae diversificetur, ut diversificatur ratio actus. Ratio autem actus diversificatur secundum diversam rationem obiecti. Omnis enim actio vel est potentiae activae, vel passivae. Obiectum autem comparatur ad actum potentiae passivae, sicut principium et causa movens, color enim inquantum movet visum, est principium visionis. Ad actum autem potentiae activae comparatur obiectum ut terminus et finis, sicut augmentativae virtutis obiectum est quantum perfectum, quod est finis augmenti. Ex his autem duobus actio speciem recipit, scilicet ex principio, vel ex fine seu termino, differt enim calefactio ab infrigidatione, secundum quod haec quidem a calido, scilicet activo, ad calidum; illa autem a frigido ad frigidum procedit. Unde necesse est quod potentiae diversificentur secundum actus et obiecta. Sed tamen considerandum est quod ea quae sunt per accidens, non diversificant speciem. Quia enim coloratum accidit animali, non diversificantur species animalis per differentiam coloris, sed per differentiam eius quod per se accidit animali, per differentiam scilicet animae sensitivae, quae quandoque invenitur cum ratione, quandoque sine ratione. Unde rationale et irrationale sunt differentiae divisivae animalis, diversas eius species constituentes. Sic igitur non quaecumque diversitas obiectorum diversificat potentias animae; sed differentia eius ad quod per se potentia respicit. Sicut sensus per se respicit passibilem qualitatem, quae per se dividitur in colorem, sonum et huiusmodi, et ideo alia potentia sensitiva est coloris, scilicet visus, et alia soni, scilicet auditus. Sed passibili qualitati, ut colorato accidit esse musicum vel grammaticum, vel magnum et parvum, aut hominem vel lapidem. Et ideo penes huiusmodi differentias potentiae animae non distinguuntur.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31621)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod actus, licet sit posterior potentia in esse, est tamen prior in intentione et secundum rationem, sicut finis agente. Obiectum autem, licet sit extrinsecum, est tamen principium vel finis actionis. Principio autem et fini proportionantur ea quae sunt intrinseca rei.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31622)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, si potentia aliqua per se respiceret unum contrariorum sicut obiectum, oporteret quod contrarium ad aliam potentiam pertineret. Sed potentia animae non per se respicit propriam rationem contrarii, sed communem rationem utriusque contrariorum, sicut visus non respicit per se rationem albi, sed rationem coloris. Et hoc ideo, quia unum contrariorum est quodammodo ratio alterius, cum se habeant sicut perfectum et imperfectum.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31623)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod nihil prohibet id quod est subiecto idem esse diversum secundum rationem. Et ideo potest ad diversas potentias animae pertinere.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31624)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod potentia superior per se respicit universaliorem rationem obiecti, quam potentia inferior, quia quanto potentia est superior, tanto ad plura se extendit. Et ideo multa conveniunt in una ratione obiecti, quam per se respicit superior potentia, quae tamen differunt secundum rationes quas per se respiciunt inferiores potentiae. Et inde est quod diversa obiecta pertinent ad diversas inferiores potentias, quae tamen uni superiori potentiae subduntur.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 77 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31625)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in potentiis animae non sit ordo. In his enim quae cadunt sub una divisione, non est prius et posterius, sed sunt naturaliter simul. Sed potentiae animae contra se invicem dividuntur. Ergo inter eas non est ordo.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31626)\n\nPraeterea, potentiae animae comparantur ad obiecta, et ad ipsam animam. Sed ex parte animae, inter eas non est ordo, quia anima est una. Similiter etiam nec ex parte obiectorum, cum sint diversa et penitus disparata, ut patet de colore et sono. In potentiis ergo animae non est ordo.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31627)\n\nPraeterea, in potentiis ordinatis hoc invenitur, quod operatio unius dependet ab operatione alterius. Sed actus unius potentiae animae non dependet ab actu alterius, potest enim visus exire in actum absque auditu, et e converso. Non ergo inter potentias animae est ordo.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31628)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus, in II de anima, comparat partes sive potentias animae figuris. Sed figurae habent ordinem ad invicem. Ergo et potentiae animae.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31629)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum anima sit una, potentiae vero plures; ordine autem quodam ab uno in multitudinem procedatur; necesse est inter potentias animae ordinem esse. Triplex autem ordo inter eas attenditur. Quorum duo considerantur secundum dependentiam unius potentiae ab altera, tertius autem accipitur secundum ordinem obiectorum. Dependentia autem unius potentiae ab altera dupliciter accipi potest, uno modo, secundum naturae ordinem, prout perfecta sunt naturaliter imperfectis priora; alio modo, secundum ordinem generationis et temporis, prout ex imperfecto ad perfectum venitur. Secundum igitur primum potentiarum ordinem, potentiae intellectivae sunt priores potentiis sensitivis, unde dirigunt eas et imperant eis. Et similiter potentiae sensitivae hoc ordine sunt priores potentiis animae nutritivae. Secundum vero ordinem secundum, e converso se habet. Nam potentiae animae nutritivae sunt priores, in via generationis, potentiis animae sensitivae, unde ad earum actiones praeparant corpus. Et similiter est de potentiis sensitivis respectu intellectivarum. Secundum autem ordinem tertium, ordinantur quaedam vires sensitivae ad invicem, scilicet visus, auditus et olfactus. Nam visibile est prius naturaliter, quia est commune superioribus et inferioribus corporibus. Sonus autem audibilis fit in aere, qui est naturaliter prior commixtione elementorum, quam consequitur odor.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31630)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod alicuius generis species se habent secundum prius et posterius, sicut numeri et figurae, quantum ad esse; licet simul esse dicantur inquantum suscipiunt communis generis praedicationem.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31631)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ordo iste potentiarum animae est et ex parte animae, quae secundum ordinem quendam habet aptitudinem ad diversos actus, licet sit una secundum essentiam; et ex parte obiectorum; et etiam ex parte actuum, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31632)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ratio illa procedit de illis potentiis in quibus attenditur ordo solum secundum tertium modum. Illae autem potentiae quae ordinantur secundum alios duos modos, ita se habent quod actus unius dependet ab altera.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 77 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31633)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod omnes potentiae animae sint in anima sicut in subiecto. Sicut enim se habent potentiae corporis ad corpus, ita se habent potentiae animae ad animam. Sed corpus est subiectum corporalium potentiarum. Ergo anima est subiectum potentiarum animae.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31634)\n\nPraeterea, operationes potentiarum animae attribuuntur corpori propter animam, quia, ut dicitur in II de anima, *anima est quo sentimus et intelligimus primum*. Sed propria principia operationum animae sunt potentiae. Ergo potentiae per prius sunt in anima.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31635)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, XII super Gen. ad Litt., quod anima quaedam sentit non per corpus, immo sine corpore, ut est timor et huiusmodi; quaedam vero sentit per corpus. Sed si potentia sensitiva non esset in sola anima sicut in subiecto, nihil posset sine corpore sentire. Ergo anima est subiectum potentiae sensitivae; et pari ratione, omnium aliarum potentiarum.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31636)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in libro de somno et vigilia quod *sentire non est proprium animae neque corporis, sed coniuncti*. Potentia ergo sensitiva est in coniuncto sicut in subiecto. Non ergo sola anima est subiectum omnium potentiarum suarum.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31637)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod illud est subiectum operativae potentiae, quod est potens operari, omne enim accidens denominat proprium subiectum. Idem autem est quod potest operari, et quod operatur. Unde oportet quod eius sit potentia sicut subiecti, cuius est operatio; ut etiam philosophus dicit, in principio de somno et vigilia. Manifestum est autem ex supra dictis quod quaedam operationes sunt animae, quae exercentur sine organo corporali, ut intelligere et velle. Unde potentiae quae sunt harum operationum principia, sunt in anima sicut in subiecto. Quaedam vero operationes sunt animae, quae exercentur per organa corporalia; sicut visio per oculum, et auditus per aurem. Et simile est de omnibus aliis operationibus nutritivae et sensitivae partis. Et ideo potentiae quae sunt talium operationum principia, sunt in coniuncto sicut in subiecto, et non in anima sola.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31638)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod omnes potentiae dicuntur esse animae, non sicut subiecti, sed sicut principii, quia per animam coniunctum habet quod tales operationes operari possit.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31639)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod omnes huiusmodi potentiae per prius sunt in anima quam in coniuncto, non sicut in subiecto, sed sicut in principio.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31640)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod opinio Platonis fuit quod sentire est operatio animae propria, sicut et intelligere. In multis autem quae ad philosophiam pertinent, Augustinus utitur opinionibus Platonis, non asserendo, sed recitando. Tamen, quantum ad praesens pertinet, hoc quod dicitur anima quaedam sentire cum corpore et quaedam sine corpore, dupliciter potest intelligi. Uno modo, quod hoc quod dico cum corpore vel sine corpore, determinet actum sentiendi secundum quod exit a sentiente. Et sic nihil sentit sine corpore, quia actio sentiendi non potest procedere ab anima nisi per organum corporale. Alio modo potest intelligi ita quod praedicta determinent actum sentiendi ex parte obiecti quod sentitur. Et sic quaedam sentit cum corpore, idest in corpore existentia, sicut cum sentit vulnus vel aliquid huiusmodi, quaedam vero sentit sine corpore, idest non existentia in corpore, sed solum in apprehensione animae, sicut cum sentit se tristari vel gaudere de aliquo audito.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 77 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31641)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod potentiae animae non fluant ab eius essentia. Ab uno enim simplici non procedunt diversa. Essentia autem animae est una et simplex. Cum ergo potentiae animae sint multae et diversae, non possunt procedere ab eius essentia.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31642)\n\nPraeterea, illud a quo aliud procedit, est causa eius. Sed essentia animae non potest dici causa potentiarum; ut patet discurrenti per singula causarum genera. Ergo potentiae animae non fluunt ab eius essentia.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31643)\n\nPraeterea, emanatio quendam motum nominat. Sed nihil movetur a seipso, ut probatur in VII libro Physic.; nisi forte ratione partis, sicut animal dicitur moveri a seipso, quia una pars eius est movens et alia mota. Neque etiam anima movetur, ut probatur in I de anima. Non ergo anima causat in se suas potentias.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31644)\n\nSed contra, potentiae animae sunt quaedam proprietates naturales ipsius. Sed subiectum est causa propriorum accidentium, unde et ponitur in definitione accidentis, ut patet in VII Metaphys. Ergo potentiae animae procedunt ab eius essentia sicut a causa.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31645)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod forma substantialis et accidentalis partim conveniunt, et partim differunt. Conveniunt quidem in hoc, quod utraque est actus, et secundum utramque est aliquid quodammodo in actu. Differunt autem in duobus. Primo quidem, quia forma substantialis facit esse simpliciter, et eius subiectum est ens in potentia tantum. Forma autem accidentalis non facit esse simpliciter; sed esse tale, aut tantum, aut aliquo modo se habens, subiectum enim eius est ens in actu. Unde patet quod actualitas per prius invenitur in forma substantiali quam in eius subiecto, et quia primum est causa in quolibet genere, forma substantialis causat esse in actu in suo subiecto. Sed e converso, actualitas per prius invenitur in subiecto formae accidentalis, quam in forma accidentali, unde actualitas formae accidentalis causatur ab actualitate subiecti. Ita quod subiectum, inquantum est in potentia, est susceptivum formae accidentalis, inquantum autem est in actu, est eius productivum. Et hoc dico de proprio et per se accidente, nam respectu accidentis extranei, subiectum est susceptivum tantum; productivum vero talis accidentis est agens extrinsecum. Secundo autem differunt substantialis forma et accidentalis, quia, cum minus principale sit propter principalius, materia est propter formam substantialem; sed e converso, forma accidentalis est propter completionem subiecti. Manifestum est autem ex dictis quod potentiarum animae subiectum est vel ipsa anima sola, quae potest esse subiectum accidentis secundum quod habet aliquid potentialitatis, ut supra dictum est; vel compositum. Compositum autem est in actu per animam. Unde manifestum est quod omnes potentiae animae, sive subiectum earum sit anima sola, sive compositum, fluunt ab essentia animae sicut a principio, quia iam dictum est quod accidens causatur a subiecto secundum quod est actu, et recipitur in eo inquantum est in potentia.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31646)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ab uno simplici possunt naturaliter multa procedere ordine quodam. Et iterum propter diversitatem recipientium. Sic igitur ab una essentia animae procedunt multae et diversae potentiae, tum propter ordinem potentiarum, tum etiam secundum diversitatem organorum corporalium.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31647)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod subiectum est causa proprii accidentis et finalis, et quodammodo activa; et etiam ut materialis, inquantum est susceptivum accidentis. Et ex hoc potest accipi quod essentia animae est causa omnium potentiarum sicut finis et sicut principium activum; quarundam autem sicut susceptivum.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31648)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod emanatio propriorum accidentium a subiecto non est per aliquam transmutationem; sed per aliquam naturalem resultationem, sicut ex uno naturaliter aliud resultat, ut ex luce color.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 77 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31649)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod una potentia animae non oriatur ab alia. Eorum enim quae simul esse incipiunt, unum non oritur ab alio. Sed omnes potentiae animae sunt simul animae concreatae. Ergo una earum ab alia non oritur.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31650)\n\nPraeterea, potentia animae oritur ab anima sicut accidens a subiecto. Sed una potentia animae non potest esse subiectum alterius, quia accidentis non est accidens. Ergo una potentia non oritur ab alia.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31651)\n\nPraeterea, oppositum non oritur a suo opposito, sed unumquodque oritur ex simili secundum speciem. Potentiae autem animae ex opposito dividuntur, sicut diversae species. Ergo una earum non procedit ab alia.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31652)\n\nSed contra, potentiae cognoscuntur per actus. Sed actus unius potentiae causatur ab alio; sicut actus phantasiae ab actu sensus. Ergo una potentia animae causatur ab alia.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31653)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in his quae secundum ordinem naturalem procedunt ab uno, sicut primum est causa omnium, ita quod est primo propinquius, est quodammodo causa eorum quae sunt magis remota. Ostensum est autem supra quod inter potentias animae est multiplex ordo. Et ideo una potentia animae ab essentia animae procedit mediante alia. Sed quia essentia animae comparatur ad potentias et sicut principium activum et finale, et sicut principium susceptivum, vel seorsum per se vel simul cum corpore; agens autem et finis est perfectius, susceptivum autem principium, inquantum huiusmodi, est minus perfectum, consequens est quod potentiae animae quae sunt priores secundum ordinem perfectionis et naturae, sint principia aliarum per modum finis et activi principii. Videmus enim quod sensus est propter intellectum, et non e converso. Sensus etiam est quaedam deficiens participatio intellectus, unde secundum naturalem originem quodammodo est ab intellectu, sicut imperfectum a perfecto. Sed secundum viam susceptivi principii, e converso potentiae imperfectiores inveniuntur principia respectu aliarum, sicut anima, secundum quod habet potentiam sensitivam, consideratur sicut subiectum et materiale quoddam respectu intellectus. Et propter hoc, imperfectiores potentiae sunt priores in via generationis, prius enim animal generatur quam homo.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31654)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut potentia animae ab essentia fluit, non per transmutationem, sed per naturalem quandam resultationem, et est simul cum anima; ita est etiam de una potentia respectu alterius.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31655)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod accidens per se non potest esse subiectum accidentis; sed unum accidens per prius recipitur in substantia quam aliud, sicut quantitas quam qualitas. Et hoc modo unum accidens dicitur esse subiectum alterius, ut superficies coloris, inquantum substantia uno accidente mediante recipit aliud. Et similiter potest dici de potentiis animae.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31656)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod potentiae animae opponuntur ad invicem oppositione perfecti et imperfecti; sicut etiam species numerorum et figurarum. Haec autem oppositio non impedit originem unius ab alio, quia imperfecta naturaliter a perfectis procedunt.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 77 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31657)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod omnes potentiae animae remaneant in anima a corpore separata. Dicitur enim in libro de spiritu et anima, quod *anima recedit a corpore, secum trahens sensum et imaginationem, rationem et intellectum et intelligentiam, concupiscibilitatem et irascibilitatem*.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31658)\n\nPraeterea, potentiae animae sunt eius naturales proprietates. Sed proprium semper inest, et nunquam separatur ab eo cuius est proprium. Ergo potentiae animae sunt in ea etiam post mortem.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31659)\n\nPraeterea, potentiae animae, etiam sensitivae, non debilitantur debilitato corpore, quia, ut dicitur in I de anima, *si senex accipiat oculum iuvenis, videbit utique sicut et iuvenis*. Sed debilitas est via ad corruptionem. Ergo potentiae animae non corrumpuntur corrupto corpore, sed manent in anima separata.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31660)\n\nPraeterea, memoria est potentia animae sensitivae, ut philosophus probat. Sed memoria manet in anima separata, dicitur enim, Luc. XVI, diviti epuloni in Inferno secundum animam existenti, *recordare quia recepisti bona in vita tua*. Ergo memoria manet in anima separata; et per consequens aliae potentiae sensitivae partis.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 8 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31661)\n\nPraeterea, gaudium et tristitia sunt in concupiscibili, quae est potentia sensitivae partis. Manifestum est autem animas separatas tristari et gaudere de praemiis vel poenis quas habent. Ergo vis concupiscibilis manet in anima separata.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 8 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31662)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, XII super Gen. ad Litt., quod sicut anima, cum corpus iacet sine sensu nondum penitus mortuum, videt quaedam secundum imaginariam visionem; ita cum fuerit a corpore penitus separata per mortem. Sed imaginatio est potentia sensitivae partis. Ergo potentia sensitivae partis manet in anima separata; et per consequens omnes aliae potentiae.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31663)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in libro de Eccles. Dogmat., *ex duabus tantum substantiis constat homo, anima cum ratione sua, et carne cum sensibus suis*. Ergo, defuncta carne, potentiae sensitivae non manent.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31664)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut iam dictum est, omnes potentiae animae comparantur ad animam solam sicut ad principium. Sed quaedam potentiae comparantur ad animam solam sicut ad subiectum, ut intellectus et voluntas. Et huiusmodi potentiae necesse est quod maneant in anima, corpore destructo. Quaedam vero potentiae sunt in coniuncto sicut in subiecto, sicut omnes potentiae sensitivae partis et nutritivae. Destructo autem subiecto, non potest accidens remanere. Unde, corrupto coniuncto, non manent huiusmodi potentiae actu; sed virtute tantum manent in anima, sicut in principio vel radice. Et sic falsum est, quod quidam dicunt huiusmodi potentias in anima remanere etiam corpore corrupto. Et multo falsius, quod dicunt etiam actus harum potentiarum remanere in anima separata, quia talium potentiarum nulla est actio nisi per organum corporeum.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31665)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod liber ille auctoritatem non habet. Unde quod ibi scriptum est, eadem facilitate contemnitur, qua dicitur. Tamen potest dici quod trahit secum anima huiusmodi potentias, non actu, sed virtute.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31666)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod huiusmodi potentiae quas dicimus actu in anima separata non manere, non sunt proprietates solius animae, sed coniuncti.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31667)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod dicuntur non debilitari huiusmodi potentiae debilitato corpore, quia anima manet immutabilis, quae est virtuale principium huiusmodi potentiarum.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 8 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31668)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod illa recordatio accipitur eo modo quo Augustinus ponit memoriam in mente; non eo modo quo ponitur pars animae sensitivae.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 8 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31669)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod tristitia et gaudium sunt in anima separata, non secundum appetitum sensitivum, sed secundum appetitum intellectivum; sicut etiam in Angelis.\n\n[I q. 77 a. 8 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31670)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod Augustinus loquitur ibi inquirendo, non asserendo. Unde quaedam ibi dicta retractat.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0002","text":"[I q. 77 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31590)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de his quae pertinent ad potentias animae. Et primo, in generali; secundo, in speciali. Circa primum quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum essentia animae sit eius potentia. Secundo, utrum sit una tantum potentia animae, vel plures. Tertio, quomodo potentiae animae distinguantur. Quarto, de ordine ipsarum ad invicem. Quinto, utrum anima sit subiectum omnium potentiarum. Sexto, utrum potentiae fluant ab essentia animae. Septimo, utrum potentia una oriatur ex alia. Octavo, utrum omnes potentiae animae remaneant in ea post mortem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0005","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31591)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ipsa essentia animae sit eius potentia. Dicit enim Augustinus, in IX de Trin., quod *mens, notitia et amor sunt substantialiter in anima, vel, ut idem dicam, essentialiter*. Et in X dicit quod *memoria, intelligentia et voluntas sunt una vita, una mens, una essentia*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0007","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31592)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0008","text":"Praeterea, anima est nobilior quam materia prima. Sed materia prima est sua potentia. Ergo multo magis anima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0009","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31593)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0010","text":"Praeterea, forma substantialis est simplicior quam accidentalis, cuius signum est, quod forma substantialis non intenditur vel remittitur, sed in indivisibili consistit. Forma autem accidentalis est ipsa sua virtus. Ergo multo magis forma substantialis, quae est anima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0011","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31594)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0012","text":"Praeterea, potentia sensitiva est qua sentimus, et potentia intellectiva qua intelligimus. Sed id quo primo sentimus et intelligimus est anima, secundum philosophum, in II de anima. Ergo anima est suae potentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0013","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31595)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0014","text":"Praeterea, omne quod non est de essentia rei, est accidens. Si ergo potentia animae est praeter essentiam eius, sequitur quod sit accidens. Quod est contra Augustinum, in IX de Trin., ubi dicit quod *praedicta non sunt in anima sicut in subiecto, ut color aut figura in corpore, aut ulla alia qualitas aut quantitas, quidquid enim tale est, non excedit subiectum in quo est; mens autem potest etiam alia amare et cognoscere*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0015","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31596)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0016","text":"Praeterea, forma simplex subiectum esse non potest. Anima autem est forma simplex, cum non sit composita ex materia et forma, ut supra dictum est. Non ergo potentia animae potest esse in ipsa sicut in subiecto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0017","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31597)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0018","text":"Praeterea, accidens non est principium substantialis differentiae. Sed sensibile et rationale sunt substantiales differentiae, et sumuntur a sensu et ratione, quae sunt potentiae animae. Ergo potentiae animae non sunt accidentia. Et ita videtur quod potentia animae sit eius essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0019","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31598)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0020","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, XI cap. Caelest. Hier., quod *caelestes spiritus dividuntur in essentiam, virtutem et operationem*. Multo igitur magis in anima aliud est essentia, et aliud virtus sive potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0021","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31599)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0022","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod impossibile est dicere quod essentia animae sit eius potentia; licet hoc quidam posuerint. Et hoc dupliciter ostenditur, quantum ad praesens. Primo quia, cum potentia et actus dividant ens et quodlibet genus entis, oportet quod ad idem genus referatur potentia et actus. Et ideo, si actus non est in genere substantiae, potentia quae dicitur ad illum actum, non potest esse in genere substantiae. Operatio autem animae non est in genere substantiae; sed in solo Deo, cuius operatio est eius substantia. Unde Dei potentia, quae est operationis principium, est ipsa Dei essentia. Quod non potest esse verum neque in anima, neque in aliqua creatura; ut supra etiam de Angelo dictum est. Secundo, hoc etiam impossibile apparet in anima. Nam anima secundum suam essentiam est actus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0023","text":"Si ergo ipsa essentia animae esset immediatum operationis principium, semper habens animam actu haberet opera vitae; sicut semper habens animam actu est vivum. Non enim, inquantum est forma, est actus ordinatus ad ulteriorem actum, sed est ultimus terminus generationis. Unde quod sit in potentia adhuc ad alium actum, hoc non competit ei secundum suam essentiam, inquantum est forma; sed secundum suam potentiam. Et sic ipsa anima, secundum quod subest suae potentiae, dicitur actus primus, ordinatus ad actum secundum. Invenitur autem habens animam non semper esse in actu operum vitae. Unde etiam in definitione animae dicitur quod est actus corporis potentia vitam habentis, quae tamen potentia non abiicit animam. Relinquitur ergo quod essentia animae non est eius potentia. Nihil enim est in potentia secundum actum, inquantum est actus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0024","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31600)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0025","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus loquitur de mente secundum quod noscit se et amat se. Sic ergo notitia et amor, inquantum referuntur ad ipsam ut cognitam et amatam, substantialiter vel essentialiter sunt in anima, quia ipsa substantia vel essentia animae cognoscitur et amatur. Et similiter intelligendum est quod alibi dicit, quod sunt una vita, una mens, una essentia. Vel, sicut quidam dicunt, haec locutio verificatur secundum modum quo totum potestativum praedicatur de suis partibus, quod medium est inter totum universale et totum integrale. Totum enim universale adest cuilibet parti secundum totam suam essentiam et virtutem, ut animal homini et equo, et ideo proprie de singulis partibus praedicatur. Totum vero integrale non est in qualibet parte, neque secundum totam essentiam, neque secundum totam virtutem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0026","text":"Et ideo nullo modo de singulis partibus praedicatur; sed aliquo modo, licet improprie, praedicatur de omnibus simul, ut si dicamus quod paries, tectum et fundamentum sunt domus. Totum vero potentiale adest singulis partibus secundum totam suam essentiam, sed non secundum totam virtutem. Et ideo quodammodo potest praedicari de qualibet parte; sed non ita proprie sicut totum universale. Et per hunc modum Augustinus dicit quod memoria, intelligentia et voluntas sunt una animae essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0027","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31601)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0028","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod actus ad quem est in potentia materia prima, est substantialis forma. Et ideo potentia materiae non est aliud quam eius essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0029","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31602)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0030","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod actio est compositi, sicut et esse, existentis enim est agere. Compositum autem per formam substantialem habet esse substantialiter; per virtutem autem quae consequitur formam substantialem, operatur. Unde sic se habet forma accidentalis activa ad formam substantialem agentis (ut calor ad formam ignis), sicut se habet potentia animae ad animam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0031","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31603)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0032","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod hoc ipsum quod forma accidentalis est actionis principium, habet a forma substantiali. Et ideo forma substantialis est primum actionis principium, sed non proximum. Et secundum hoc philosophus dicit quod *id quo intelligimus et sentimus, est anima*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0033","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31604)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0034","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod, si accidens accipiatur secundum quod dividitur contra substantiam, sic nihil potest esse medium inter substantiam et accidens, quia dividuntur secundum affirmationem et negationem, scilicet secundum esse in subiecto et non esse in subiecto. Et hoc modo, cum potentia animae non sit eius essentia, oportet quod sit accidens, et est in secunda specie qualitatis. Si vero accipiatur accidens secundum quod ponitur unum quinque universalium, sic aliquid est medium inter substantiam et accidens. Quia ad substantiam pertinet quidquid est essentiale rei, non autem quidquid est extra essentiam, potest sic dici accidens, sed solum id quod non causatur ex principiis essentialibus speciei. Proprium enim non est de essentia rei, sed ex principiis essentialibus speciei causatur, unde medium est inter essentiam et accidens sic dictum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0035","text":"Et hoc modo potentiae animae possunt dici mediae inter substantiam et accidens, quasi proprietates animae naturales. Quod autem Augustinus dicit, quod notitia et amor non sunt in anima sicut accidentia in subiecto, intelligitur secundum modum praedictum, prout comparantur ad animam, non sicut ad amantem et cognoscentem; sed prout comparantur ad eam sicut ad amatam et cognitam. Et hoc modo procedit sua probatio, quia si amor esset in anima amata sicut in subiecto, sequeretur quod accidens transcenderet suum subiectum; cum etiam alia sint amata per animam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0036","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31605)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0037","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod anima, licet non sit composita ex materia et forma, habet tamen aliquid de potentialitate admixtum ut supra dictum est. Et ideo potest esse subiectum accidentis. Propositio autem inducta locum habet in Deo, qui est actus purus, in qua materia Boetius eam introducit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0038","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31606)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0039","text":"Ad septimum dicendum quod rationale et sensibile, prout sunt differentiae, non sumuntur a potentiis sensus et rationis; sed ab ipsa anima sensitiva et rationali. Quia tamen formae substantiales, quae secundum se sunt nobis ignotae, innotescunt per accidentia; nihil prohibet interdum accidentia loco differentiarum substantialium poni.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0040","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0041","text":"[I q. 77 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31607)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0042","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sint plures potentiae animae. Anima enim intellectiva maxime ad divinam similitudinem accedit. Sed in Deo est una et simplex potentia. Ergo et in anima intellectiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0043","text":"[I q. 77 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31608)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0044","text":"Praeterea, quanto virtus est superior, tanto est magis unita. Sed anima intellectiva excedit omnes alias formas in virtute. Ergo maxime debet habere unam virtutem seu potentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0045","text":"[I q. 77 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31609)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0046","text":"Praeterea, operari est existentis in actu. Sed per eandem essentiam animae homo habet esse secundum diversos gradus perfectionis, ut supra habitum est. Ergo per eandem potentiam animae operatur diversas operationes diversorum graduum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0047","text":"[I q. 77 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31610)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0048","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus, in II de anima ponit plures animae potentias.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0049","text":"[I q. 77 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31611)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0050","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est ponere plures animae potentias. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod, sicut philosophus dicit in II de caelo, quae sunt in rebus infima, non possunt consequi perfectam bonitatem, sed aliquam imperfectam consequuntur paucis motibus; superiora vero his adipiscuntur perfectam bonitatem motibus multis; his autem superiora sunt quae adipiscuntur perfectam bonitatem motibus paucis; summa vero perfectio invenitur in his quae absque motu perfectam possident bonitatem. Sicut infime est ad sanitatem dispositus qui non potest perfectam consequi sanitatem, sed aliquam modicam consequitur paucis remediis; melius autem dispositus est qui potest perfectam consequi sanitatem, sed remediis multis; et adhuc melius, qui remediis paucis; optime autem, qui absque remedio perfectam sanitatem habet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0051","text":"Dicendum est ergo quod res quae sunt infra hominem, quaedam particularia bona consequuntur, et ideo quasdam paucas et determinatas operationes habent et virtutes. Homo autem potest consequi universalem et perfectam bonitatem, quia potest adipisci beatitudinem. Est tamen in ultimo gradu, secundum naturam, eorum quibus competit beatitudo, et ideo multis et diversis operationibus et virtutibus indiget anima humana. Angelis vero minor diversitas potentiarum competit. In Deo vero non est aliqua potentia vel actio, praeter eius essentiam. Est et alia ratio quare anima humana abundat diversitate potentiarum, videlicet quia est in confinio spiritualium et corporalium creaturarum, et ideo concurrunt in ipsa virtutes utrarumque creaturarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0052","text":"[I q. 77 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31612)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0053","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in hoc ipso magis ad similitudinem Dei accedit anima intellectiva quam creaturae inferiores, quod perfectam bonitatem consequi potest; licet per multa et diversa; in quo deficit a superioribus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0054","text":"[I q. 77 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31613)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0055","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod virtus unita est superior, si ad aequalia se extendat. Sed virtus multiplicata est superior, si plura ei subiiciantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0056","text":"[I q. 77 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31614)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0057","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod unius rei est unum esse substantiale, sed possunt esse operationes plures. Et ideo est una essentia animae, sed potentiae plures.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0058","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0059","text":"[I q. 77 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31615)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0060","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod potentiae non distinguantur per actus et obiecta. Nihil enim determinatur ad speciem per illud quod posterius, vel extrinsecum est. Actus autem est posterior potentia; obiectum autem est extrinsecum. Ergo per ea potentiae non distinguuntur secundum speciem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0061","text":"[I q. 77 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31616)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0062","text":"Praeterea, contraria sunt quae maxime differunt. Si igitur potentiae distinguerentur penes obiecta, sequeretur quod contrariorum non esset eadem potentia. Quod patet esse falsum fere in omnibus, nam potentia visiva eadem est albi et nigri, et gustus idem est dulcis et amari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0063","text":"[I q. 77 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31617)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0064","text":"Praeterea, remota causa, removetur effectus. Si igitur potentiarum differentia esset ex differentia obiectorum, idem obiectum non pertineret ad diversas potentias. Quod patet esse falsum, nam idem est quod potentia cognoscitiva cognoscit, et appetitiva appetit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0065","text":"[I q. 77 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31618)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0066","text":"Praeterea, id quod per se est causa alicuius, in omnibus causat illud. Sed quaedam obiecta diversa, quae pertinent ad diversas potentias, pertinent etiam ad aliquam unam potentiam, sicut sonus et color pertinent ad visum et auditum, quae sunt diversae potentiae; et tamen pertinent ad unam potentiam sensus communis. Non ergo potentiae distinguuntur secundum differentiam obiectorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0067","text":"[I q. 77 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31619)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0068","text":"Sed contra, posteriora distinguuntur secundum priora. Sed philosophus dicit II de anima, quod *priores potentiis actus et operationes secundum rationem sunt*; et adhuc his priora sunt opposita, sive obiecta. Ergo potentiae distinguuntur secundum actus et obiecta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0069","text":"[I q. 77 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31620)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0070","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod potentia, secundum illud quod est potentia, ordinatur ad actum. Unde oportet rationem potentiae accipi ex actu ad quem ordinatur, et per consequens oportet quod ratio potentiae diversificetur, ut diversificatur ratio actus. Ratio autem actus diversificatur secundum diversam rationem obiecti. Omnis enim actio vel est potentiae activae, vel passivae. Obiectum autem comparatur ad actum potentiae passivae, sicut principium et causa movens, color enim inquantum movet visum, est principium visionis. Ad actum autem potentiae activae comparatur obiectum ut terminus et finis, sicut augmentativae virtutis obiectum est quantum perfectum, quod est finis augmenti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0071","text":"Ex his autem duobus actio speciem recipit, scilicet ex principio, vel ex fine seu termino, differt enim calefactio ab infrigidatione, secundum quod haec quidem a calido, scilicet activo, ad calidum; illa autem a frigido ad frigidum procedit. Unde necesse est quod potentiae diversificentur secundum actus et obiecta. Sed tamen considerandum est quod ea quae sunt per accidens, non diversificant speciem. Quia enim coloratum accidit animali, non diversificantur species animalis per differentiam coloris, sed per differentiam eius quod per se accidit animali, per differentiam scilicet animae sensitivae, quae quandoque invenitur cum ratione, quandoque sine ratione. Unde rationale et irrationale sunt differentiae divisivae animalis, diversas eius species constituentes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0072","text":"Sic igitur non quaecumque diversitas obiectorum diversificat potentias animae; sed differentia eius ad quod per se potentia respicit. Sicut sensus per se respicit passibilem qualitatem, quae per se dividitur in colorem, sonum et huiusmodi, et ideo alia potentia sensitiva est coloris, scilicet visus, et alia soni, scilicet auditus. Sed passibili qualitati, ut colorato accidit esse musicum vel grammaticum, vel magnum et parvum, aut hominem vel lapidem. Et ideo penes huiusmodi differentias potentiae animae non distinguuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0073","text":"[I q. 77 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31621)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0074","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod actus, licet sit posterior potentia in esse, est tamen prior in intentione et secundum rationem, sicut finis agente. Obiectum autem, licet sit extrinsecum, est tamen principium vel finis actionis. Principio autem et fini proportionantur ea quae sunt intrinseca rei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0075","text":"[I q. 77 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31622)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0076","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, si potentia aliqua per se respiceret unum contrariorum sicut obiectum, oporteret quod contrarium ad aliam potentiam pertineret. Sed potentia animae non per se respicit propriam rationem contrarii, sed communem rationem utriusque contrariorum, sicut visus non respicit per se rationem albi, sed rationem coloris. Et hoc ideo, quia unum contrariorum est quodammodo ratio alterius, cum se habeant sicut perfectum et imperfectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0077","text":"[I q. 77 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31623)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0078","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod nihil prohibet id quod est subiecto idem esse diversum secundum rationem. Et ideo potest ad diversas potentias animae pertinere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0079","text":"[I q. 77 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31624)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0080","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod potentia superior per se respicit universaliorem rationem obiecti, quam potentia inferior, quia quanto potentia est superior, tanto ad plura se extendit. Et ideo multa conveniunt in una ratione obiecti, quam per se respicit superior potentia, quae tamen differunt secundum rationes quas per se respiciunt inferiores potentiae. Et inde est quod diversa obiecta pertinent ad diversas inferiores potentias, quae tamen uni superiori potentiae subduntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0081","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0082","text":"[I q. 77 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31625)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0083","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in potentiis animae non sit ordo. In his enim quae cadunt sub una divisione, non est prius et posterius, sed sunt naturaliter simul. Sed potentiae animae contra se invicem dividuntur. Ergo inter eas non est ordo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0084","text":"[I q. 77 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31626)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0085","text":"Praeterea, potentiae animae comparantur ad obiecta, et ad ipsam animam. Sed ex parte animae, inter eas non est ordo, quia anima est una. Similiter etiam nec ex parte obiectorum, cum sint diversa et penitus disparata, ut patet de colore et sono. In potentiis ergo animae non est ordo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0086","text":"[I q. 77 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31627)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0087","text":"Praeterea, in potentiis ordinatis hoc invenitur, quod operatio unius dependet ab operatione alterius. Sed actus unius potentiae animae non dependet ab actu alterius, potest enim visus exire in actum absque auditu, et e converso. Non ergo inter potentias animae est ordo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0088","text":"[I q. 77 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31628)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0089","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus, in II de anima, comparat partes sive potentias animae figuris. Sed figurae habent ordinem ad invicem. Ergo et potentiae animae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0090","text":"[I q. 77 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31629)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0091","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum anima sit una, potentiae vero plures; ordine autem quodam ab uno in multitudinem procedatur; necesse est inter potentias animae ordinem esse. Triplex autem ordo inter eas attenditur. Quorum duo considerantur secundum dependentiam unius potentiae ab altera, tertius autem accipitur secundum ordinem obiectorum. Dependentia autem unius potentiae ab altera dupliciter accipi potest, uno modo, secundum naturae ordinem, prout perfecta sunt naturaliter imperfectis priora; alio modo, secundum ordinem generationis et temporis, prout ex imperfecto ad perfectum venitur. Secundum igitur primum potentiarum ordinem, potentiae intellectivae sunt priores potentiis sensitivis, unde dirigunt eas et imperant eis. Et similiter potentiae sensitivae hoc ordine sunt priores potentiis animae nutritivae. Secundum vero ordinem secundum, e converso se habet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0092","text":"Nam potentiae animae nutritivae sunt priores, in via generationis, potentiis animae sensitivae, unde ad earum actiones praeparant corpus. Et similiter est de potentiis sensitivis respectu intellectivarum. Secundum autem ordinem tertium, ordinantur quaedam vires sensitivae ad invicem, scilicet visus, auditus et olfactus. Nam visibile est prius naturaliter, quia est commune superioribus et inferioribus corporibus. Sonus autem audibilis fit in aere, qui est naturaliter prior commixtione elementorum, quam consequitur odor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0093","text":"[I q. 77 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31630)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0094","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod alicuius generis species se habent secundum prius et posterius, sicut numeri et figurae, quantum ad esse; licet simul esse dicantur inquantum suscipiunt communis generis praedicationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0095","text":"[I q. 77 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31631)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0096","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ordo iste potentiarum animae est et ex parte animae, quae secundum ordinem quendam habet aptitudinem ad diversos actus, licet sit una secundum essentiam; et ex parte obiectorum; et etiam ex parte actuum, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0097","text":"[I q. 77 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31632)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0098","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ratio illa procedit de illis potentiis in quibus attenditur ordo solum secundum tertium modum. Illae autem potentiae quae ordinantur secundum alios duos modos, ita se habent quod actus unius dependet ab altera.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0099","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0100","text":"[I q. 77 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31633)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0101","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod omnes potentiae animae sint in anima sicut in subiecto. Sicut enim se habent potentiae corporis ad corpus, ita se habent potentiae animae ad animam. Sed corpus est subiectum corporalium potentiarum. Ergo anima est subiectum potentiarum animae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0102","text":"[I q. 77 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31634)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0103","text":"Praeterea, operationes potentiarum animae attribuuntur corpori propter animam, quia, ut dicitur in II de anima, *anima est quo sentimus et intelligimus primum*. Sed propria principia operationum animae sunt potentiae. Ergo potentiae per prius sunt in anima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0104","text":"[I q. 77 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31635)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0105","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, XII super Gen. ad Litt., quod anima quaedam sentit non per corpus, immo sine corpore, ut est timor et huiusmodi; quaedam vero sentit per corpus. Sed si potentia sensitiva non esset in sola anima sicut in subiecto, nihil posset sine corpore sentire. Ergo anima est subiectum potentiae sensitivae; et pari ratione, omnium aliarum potentiarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0106","text":"[I q. 77 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31636)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0107","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in libro de somno et vigilia quod *sentire non est proprium animae neque corporis, sed coniuncti*. Potentia ergo sensitiva est in coniuncto sicut in subiecto. Non ergo sola anima est subiectum omnium potentiarum suarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0108","text":"[I q. 77 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31637)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0109","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod illud est subiectum operativae potentiae, quod est potens operari, omne enim accidens denominat proprium subiectum. Idem autem est quod potest operari, et quod operatur. Unde oportet quod eius sit potentia sicut subiecti, cuius est operatio; ut etiam philosophus dicit, in principio de somno et vigilia. Manifestum est autem ex supra dictis quod quaedam operationes sunt animae, quae exercentur sine organo corporali, ut intelligere et velle. Unde potentiae quae sunt harum operationum principia, sunt in anima sicut in subiecto. Quaedam vero operationes sunt animae, quae exercentur per organa corporalia; sicut visio per oculum, et auditus per aurem. Et simile est de omnibus aliis operationibus nutritivae et sensitivae partis. Et ideo potentiae quae sunt talium operationum principia, sunt in coniuncto sicut in subiecto, et non in anima sola.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0110","text":"[I q. 77 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31638)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0111","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod omnes potentiae dicuntur esse animae, non sicut subiecti, sed sicut principii, quia per animam coniunctum habet quod tales operationes operari possit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0112","text":"[I q. 77 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31639)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0113","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod omnes huiusmodi potentiae per prius sunt in anima quam in coniuncto, non sicut in subiecto, sed sicut in principio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0114","text":"[I q. 77 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31640)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0115","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod opinio Platonis fuit quod sentire est operatio animae propria, sicut et intelligere. In multis autem quae ad philosophiam pertinent, Augustinus utitur opinionibus Platonis, non asserendo, sed recitando. Tamen, quantum ad praesens pertinet, hoc quod dicitur anima quaedam sentire cum corpore et quaedam sine corpore, dupliciter potest intelligi. Uno modo, quod hoc quod dico cum corpore vel sine corpore, determinet actum sentiendi secundum quod exit a sentiente. Et sic nihil sentit sine corpore, quia actio sentiendi non potest procedere ab anima nisi per organum corporale. Alio modo potest intelligi ita quod praedicta determinent actum sentiendi ex parte obiecti quod sentitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0116","text":"Et sic quaedam sentit cum corpore, idest in corpore existentia, sicut cum sentit vulnus vel aliquid huiusmodi, quaedam vero sentit sine corpore, idest non existentia in corpore, sed solum in apprehensione animae, sicut cum sentit se tristari vel gaudere de aliquo audito.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0117","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0118","text":"[I q. 77 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31641)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0119","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod potentiae animae non fluant ab eius essentia. Ab uno enim simplici non procedunt diversa. Essentia autem animae est una et simplex. Cum ergo potentiae animae sint multae et diversae, non possunt procedere ab eius essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0120","text":"[I q. 77 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31642)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0121","text":"Praeterea, illud a quo aliud procedit, est causa eius. Sed essentia animae non potest dici causa potentiarum; ut patet discurrenti per singula causarum genera. Ergo potentiae animae non fluunt ab eius essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0122","text":"[I q. 77 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31643)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0123","text":"Praeterea, emanatio quendam motum nominat. Sed nihil movetur a seipso, ut probatur in VII libro Physic.; nisi forte ratione partis, sicut animal dicitur moveri a seipso, quia una pars eius est movens et alia mota. Neque etiam anima movetur, ut probatur in I de anima. Non ergo anima causat in se suas potentias.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0124","text":"[I q. 77 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31644)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0125","text":"Sed contra, potentiae animae sunt quaedam proprietates naturales ipsius. Sed subiectum est causa propriorum accidentium, unde et ponitur in definitione accidentis, ut patet in VII Metaphys. Ergo potentiae animae procedunt ab eius essentia sicut a causa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0126","text":"[I q. 77 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31645)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0127","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod forma substantialis et accidentalis partim conveniunt, et partim differunt. Conveniunt quidem in hoc, quod utraque est actus, et secundum utramque est aliquid quodammodo in actu. Differunt autem in duobus. Primo quidem, quia forma substantialis facit esse simpliciter, et eius subiectum est ens in potentia tantum. Forma autem accidentalis non facit esse simpliciter; sed esse tale, aut tantum, aut aliquo modo se habens, subiectum enim eius est ens in actu. Unde patet quod actualitas per prius invenitur in forma substantiali quam in eius subiecto, et quia primum est causa in quolibet genere, forma substantialis causat esse in actu in suo subiecto. Sed e converso, actualitas per prius invenitur in subiecto formae accidentalis, quam in forma accidentali, unde actualitas formae accidentalis causatur ab actualitate subiecti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0128","text":"Ita quod subiectum, inquantum est in potentia, est susceptivum formae accidentalis, inquantum autem est in actu, est eius productivum. Et hoc dico de proprio et per se accidente, nam respectu accidentis extranei, subiectum est susceptivum tantum; productivum vero talis accidentis est agens extrinsecum. Secundo autem differunt substantialis forma et accidentalis, quia, cum minus principale sit propter principalius, materia est propter formam substantialem; sed e converso, forma accidentalis est propter completionem subiecti. Manifestum est autem ex dictis quod potentiarum animae subiectum est vel ipsa anima sola, quae potest esse subiectum accidentis secundum quod habet aliquid potentialitatis, ut supra dictum est; vel compositum. Compositum autem est in actu per animam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0129","text":"Unde manifestum est quod omnes potentiae animae, sive subiectum earum sit anima sola, sive compositum, fluunt ab essentia animae sicut a principio, quia iam dictum est quod accidens causatur a subiecto secundum quod est actu, et recipitur in eo inquantum est in potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0130","text":"[I q. 77 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31646)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0131","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ab uno simplici possunt naturaliter multa procedere ordine quodam. Et iterum propter diversitatem recipientium. Sic igitur ab una essentia animae procedunt multae et diversae potentiae, tum propter ordinem potentiarum, tum etiam secundum diversitatem organorum corporalium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0132","text":"[I q. 77 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31647)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0133","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod subiectum est causa proprii accidentis et finalis, et quodammodo activa; et etiam ut materialis, inquantum est susceptivum accidentis. Et ex hoc potest accipi quod essentia animae est causa omnium potentiarum sicut finis et sicut principium activum; quarundam autem sicut susceptivum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0134","text":"[I q. 77 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31648)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0135","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod emanatio propriorum accidentium a subiecto non est per aliquam transmutationem; sed per aliquam naturalem resultationem, sicut ex uno naturaliter aliud resultat, ut ex luce color.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0136","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0137","text":"[I q. 77 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31649)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0138","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod una potentia animae non oriatur ab alia. Eorum enim quae simul esse incipiunt, unum non oritur ab alio. Sed omnes potentiae animae sunt simul animae concreatae. Ergo una earum ab alia non oritur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0139","text":"[I q. 77 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31650)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0140","text":"Praeterea, potentia animae oritur ab anima sicut accidens a subiecto. Sed una potentia animae non potest esse subiectum alterius, quia accidentis non est accidens. Ergo una potentia non oritur ab alia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0141","text":"[I q. 77 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31651)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0142","text":"Praeterea, oppositum non oritur a suo opposito, sed unumquodque oritur ex simili secundum speciem. Potentiae autem animae ex opposito dividuntur, sicut diversae species. Ergo una earum non procedit ab alia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0143","text":"[I q. 77 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31652)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0144","text":"Sed contra, potentiae cognoscuntur per actus. Sed actus unius potentiae causatur ab alio; sicut actus phantasiae ab actu sensus. Ergo una potentia animae causatur ab alia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0145","text":"[I q. 77 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31653)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0146","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in his quae secundum ordinem naturalem procedunt ab uno, sicut primum est causa omnium, ita quod est primo propinquius, est quodammodo causa eorum quae sunt magis remota. Ostensum est autem supra quod inter potentias animae est multiplex ordo. Et ideo una potentia animae ab essentia animae procedit mediante alia. Sed quia essentia animae comparatur ad potentias et sicut principium activum et finale, et sicut principium susceptivum, vel seorsum per se vel simul cum corpore; agens autem et finis est perfectius, susceptivum autem principium, inquantum huiusmodi, est minus perfectum, consequens est quod potentiae animae quae sunt priores secundum ordinem perfectionis et naturae, sint principia aliarum per modum finis et activi principii. Videmus enim quod sensus est propter intellectum, et non e converso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0147","text":"Sensus etiam est quaedam deficiens participatio intellectus, unde secundum naturalem originem quodammodo est ab intellectu, sicut imperfectum a perfecto. Sed secundum viam susceptivi principii, e converso potentiae imperfectiores inveniuntur principia respectu aliarum, sicut anima, secundum quod habet potentiam sensitivam, consideratur sicut subiectum et materiale quoddam respectu intellectus. Et propter hoc, imperfectiores potentiae sunt priores in via generationis, prius enim animal generatur quam homo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0148","text":"[I q. 77 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31654)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0149","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut potentia animae ab essentia fluit, non per transmutationem, sed per naturalem quandam resultationem, et est simul cum anima; ita est etiam de una potentia respectu alterius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0150","text":"[I q. 77 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31655)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0151","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod accidens per se non potest esse subiectum accidentis; sed unum accidens per prius recipitur in substantia quam aliud, sicut quantitas quam qualitas. Et hoc modo unum accidens dicitur esse subiectum alterius, ut superficies coloris, inquantum substantia uno accidente mediante recipit aliud. Et similiter potest dici de potentiis animae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0152","text":"[I q. 77 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31656)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0153","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod potentiae animae opponuntur ad invicem oppositione perfecti et imperfecti; sicut etiam species numerorum et figurarum. Haec autem oppositio non impedit originem unius ab alio, quia imperfecta naturaliter a perfectis procedunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0154","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0155","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31657)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0156","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod omnes potentiae animae remaneant in anima a corpore separata. Dicitur enim in libro de spiritu et anima, quod *anima recedit a corpore, secum trahens sensum et imaginationem, rationem et intellectum et intelligentiam, concupiscibilitatem et irascibilitatem*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0157","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31658)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0158","text":"Praeterea, potentiae animae sunt eius naturales proprietates. Sed proprium semper inest, et nunquam separatur ab eo cuius est proprium. Ergo potentiae animae sunt in ea etiam post mortem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0159","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31659)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0160","text":"Praeterea, potentiae animae, etiam sensitivae, non debilitantur debilitato corpore, quia, ut dicitur in I de anima, *si senex accipiat oculum iuvenis, videbit utique sicut et iuvenis*. Sed debilitas est via ad corruptionem. Ergo potentiae animae non corrumpuntur corrupto corpore, sed manent in anima separata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0161","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31660)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0162","text":"Praeterea, memoria est potentia animae sensitivae, ut philosophus probat. Sed memoria manet in anima separata, dicitur enim, Luc. XVI, diviti epuloni in Inferno secundum animam existenti, *recordare quia recepisti bona in vita tua*. Ergo memoria manet in anima separata; et per consequens aliae potentiae sensitivae partis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0163","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31661)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0164","text":"Praeterea, gaudium et tristitia sunt in concupiscibili, quae est potentia sensitivae partis. Manifestum est autem animas separatas tristari et gaudere de praemiis vel poenis quas habent. Ergo vis concupiscibilis manet in anima separata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0165","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31662)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0166","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, XII super Gen. ad Litt., quod sicut anima, cum corpus iacet sine sensu nondum penitus mortuum, videt quaedam secundum imaginariam visionem; ita cum fuerit a corpore penitus separata per mortem. Sed imaginatio est potentia sensitivae partis. Ergo potentia sensitivae partis manet in anima separata; et per consequens omnes aliae potentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0167","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31663)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0168","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in libro de Eccles. Dogmat., *ex duabus tantum substantiis constat homo, anima cum ratione sua, et carne cum sensibus suis*. Ergo, defuncta carne, potentiae sensitivae non manent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0169","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31664)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0170","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut iam dictum est, omnes potentiae animae comparantur ad animam solam sicut ad principium. Sed quaedam potentiae comparantur ad animam solam sicut ad subiectum, ut intellectus et voluntas. Et huiusmodi potentiae necesse est quod maneant in anima, corpore destructo. Quaedam vero potentiae sunt in coniuncto sicut in subiecto, sicut omnes potentiae sensitivae partis et nutritivae. Destructo autem subiecto, non potest accidens remanere. Unde, corrupto coniuncto, non manent huiusmodi potentiae actu; sed virtute tantum manent in anima, sicut in principio vel radice. Et sic falsum est, quod quidam dicunt huiusmodi potentias in anima remanere etiam corpore corrupto. Et multo falsius, quod dicunt etiam actus harum potentiarum remanere in anima separata, quia talium potentiarum nulla est actio nisi per organum corporeum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0171","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31665)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0172","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod liber ille auctoritatem non habet. Unde quod ibi scriptum est, eadem facilitate contemnitur, qua dicitur. Tamen potest dici quod trahit secum anima huiusmodi potentias, non actu, sed virtute.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0173","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31666)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0174","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod huiusmodi potentiae quas dicimus actu in anima separata non manere, non sunt proprietates solius animae, sed coniuncti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0175","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31667)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0176","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod dicuntur non debilitari huiusmodi potentiae debilitato corpore, quia anima manet immutabilis, quae est virtuale principium huiusmodi potentiarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0177","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31668)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0178","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod illa recordatio accipitur eo modo quo Augustinus ponit memoriam in mente; non eo modo quo ponitur pars animae sensitivae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0179","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31669)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0180","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod tristitia et gaudium sunt in anima separata, non secundum appetitum sensitivum, sed secundum appetitum intellectivum; sicut etiam in Angelis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0181","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31670)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0182","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod Augustinus loquitur ibi inquirendo, non asserendo. Unde quaedam ibi dicta retractat.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"dc5ead8925d4fdca79f0630aaff5825b83ee9f25488623a6a4e732e79df0188e","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T150634491854Z_5b2591d8","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 77","source_sha256":"dc5ead8925d4fdca79f0630aaff5825b83ee9f25488623a6a4e732e79df0188e","sha256":"8dae74a8ed231156c8077c4b849b36c61a0beffec55cace35109d8d00666e582","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:06:34.491868+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0002","text":"[I q. 77 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31590)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider those matters that pertain to the powers of the soul. First, in general; second, in particular. Concerning the first, eight questions are asked. First, whether the essence of the soul is its power. Second, whether there is only one power of the soul, or several. Third, how the powers of the soul are distinguished. Fourth, concerning their order in relation to one another. Fifth, whether the soul is the subject of all the powers. Sixth, whether the powers flow from the essence of the soul. Seventh, whether one power arises from another. Eighth, whether all the powers of the soul remain in it after death.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0005","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31591)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the very essence of the soul is its power. For Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, IX, that *mind, knowledge, and love are in the soul substantially, or, to say the same thing, essentially*. And in X he says that *memory, understanding, and will are one life, one mind, one essence*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0007","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31592)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0008","text":"Further, the soul is nobler than prime matter. But prime matter is its own potency. Therefore, much more is the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0009","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31593)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0010","text":"Further, a substantial form is simpler than an accidental form, a sign of which is that a substantial form is not intensified or diminished, but consists in an indivisible point. But an accidental form is its own virtue. Therefore much more is a substantial form, which is the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0011","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31594)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0012","text":"Further, the sensitive power is that by which we sense, and the intellective power that by which we understand. But that by which we first sense and understand is the soul, according to the Philosopher, in *On the Soul*, II. Therefore the soul is its powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0013","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31595)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0014","text":"Further, everything that is not of the essence of a thing is an accident. Therefore, if the power of the soul is beyond its essence, it follows that it is an accident. This is contrary to Augustine, in *On the Trinity*, IX, where he says that *the aforesaid are not in the soul as in a subject, as color or shape in a body, or any other quality or quantity; for whatever is of this sort does not exceed the subject in which it is; but the mind can love and know other things as well*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0015","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31596)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0016","text":"Further, a simple form cannot be a subject. But the soul is a simple form, since it is not composed of matter and form, as was said above. Therefore, the power of the soul cannot be in it as in a subject.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0017","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 arg. 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31597)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0018","text":"Further, an accident is not a principle of a substantial difference. But sensible and rational are substantial differences, and are taken from sense and reason, which are powers of the soul. Therefore, the powers of the soul are not accidents. And thus it seems that the power of the soul is its essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0019","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31598)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0020","text":"But on the contrary, Dionysius says, in chapter XI of the *Celestial Hierarchy*, that *the heavenly spirits are divided into essence, power, and operation*. Therefore, much more in the soul essence is one thing and virtue or power another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0021","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31599)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0022","text":"I answer that it must be said that it is impossible to maintain that the essence of the soul is its power, although some have held this. And this is shown in two ways, as regards the present matter. First, because, since potency and act divide being and every genus of being, potency and act must be referred to the same genus. And therefore, if an act is not in the genus of substance, the potency that is said in relation to that act cannot be in the genus of substance. But the operation of the soul is not in the genus of substance; this is so only in God, whose operation is His substance. Hence the power of God, which is the principle of operation, is the very essence of God. This cannot be true either in the soul or in any creature, as was also said above concerning the angel. Second, this likewise appears impossible in the soul. For the soul, according to its essence, is act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0023","text":"Therefore, if the very essence of the soul were the immediate principle of operation, whatever has a soul would always actually have the works of life, just as whatever has a soul is always actually living. For, insofar as it is a form, it is not an act ordered to a further act, but is the ultimate term of generation. Hence, that it should still be in potency toward another act does not belong to it according to its essence, insofar as it is a form, but according to its power. And thus the soul itself, insofar as it underlies its power, is called first act, ordered to second act. Yet that which has a soul is found not always to be in act as regards the works of life. Hence it is also said in the definition of the soul that it is the act of a body having life potentially, which potency nevertheless does not exclude the soul. It remains, therefore, that the essence of the soul is not its power. For nothing is in potency according to act, insofar as it is act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0024","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31600)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0025","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Augustine speaks of the mind insofar as it knows itself and loves itself. Thus, therefore, knowledge and love, insofar as they are referred to it itself as known and loved, are in the soul substantially or essentially, because the very substance or essence of the soul is known and loved. And it is to be understood similarly that elsewhere he says that they are one life, one mind, one essence. Or, as some say, this expression is verified according to the mode in which a potential whole is predicated of its parts, which is intermediate between a universal whole and an integral whole. For a universal whole is present in each part according to its whole essence and power, as animal is in man and horse, and therefore is properly predicated of the individual parts. But an integral whole is not in each part, neither according to its whole essence nor according to its whole power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0026","text":"And therefore it is in no way predicated of the individual parts; but in some way, though improperly, it is predicated of all of them together, as when we say that wall, roof, and foundation are a house. But a potential whole is present in the individual parts according to its whole essence, but not according to its whole power. And therefore in a certain way it can be predicated of each part, but not as properly as a universal whole. And in this way Augustine says that memory, understanding, and will are one essence of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0027","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31601)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0028","text":"To the second, it must be said that the act toward which prime matter is in potency is substantial form. And therefore the potency of matter is nothing other than its essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0029","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31602)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0030","text":"To the third, it must be said that action belongs to the composite, as does being; for it is what exists that acts. But through the substantial form the composite has being substantially; through the power that follows upon the substantial form, however, it operates. Hence an active accidental form is related to the substantial form of the agent (as heat to the form of fire) as a power of the soul is related to the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0031","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31603)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0032","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the very fact that an accidental form is a principle of action it has from the substantial form. And therefore the substantial form is the first principle of action, but not the proximate one. And according to this the Philosopher says that *that by which we understand and sense is the soul*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0033","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31604)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0034","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that, if accident is taken as it is divided against substance, then nothing can be intermediate between substance and accident, because they are divided according to affirmation and negation, namely, according to being in a subject and not being in a subject. And in this way, since the power of the soul is not its essence, it must be an accident, and it is in the second species of quality. But if accident is taken as it is posited as one of the five universals, then something is intermediate between substance and accident. For whatever is essential to a thing belongs to substance; but not everything outside the essence can thus be called an accident, but only that which is not caused from the essential principles of the species. For a property is not of the essence of a thing, but is caused from the essential principles of the species; hence it is intermediate between essence and accident so called.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0035","text":"And in this way the powers of the soul can be called intermediate between substance and accident, as it were natural properties of the soul. But what Augustine says, namely, that knowledge and love are not in the soul as accidents in a subject, is understood in the aforesaid manner, insofar as they are compared to the soul, not as to one loving and knowing, but insofar as they are compared to it as loved and known. And in this way his proof proceeds, because if love were in the loved soul as in a subject, it would follow that an accident transcended its subject, since other things too are loved by the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0036","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31605)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0037","text":"To the sixth, it must be said that the soul, although it is not composed of matter and form, nevertheless has something of potentiality mixed in, as was said above. And therefore it can be a subject of accident. But the proposition adduced holds in God, who is pure act, in connection with which matter Boethius introduces it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0038","text":"[I q. 77 a. 1 ad 7](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31606)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0039","text":"To the seventh, it must be said that rational and sentient, insofar as they are differences, are not taken from the powers of sense and reason, but from the sensitive and rational soul itself. Yet, since substantial forms, which in themselves are unknown to us, become known through accidents, nothing prevents accidents from sometimes being posited in place of substantial differences.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0040","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0041","text":"[I q. 77 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31607)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0042","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that there are not several powers of the soul. For the intellectual soul most closely approaches the divine likeness. But in God there is one simple power. Therefore there is also one in the intellectual soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0043","text":"[I q. 77 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31608)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0044","text":"Moreover, the higher a power is, the more united it is. But the intellectual soul surpasses all other forms in power. Therefore it ought especially to have one virtue or power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0045","text":"[I q. 77 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31609)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0046","text":"Moreover, to operate belongs to that which exists in act. But by the same essence of the soul man has being according to diverse grades of perfection, as has been treated above. Therefore by the same power of the soul he performs diverse operations of diverse grades.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0047","text":"[I q. 77 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31610)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0048","text":"But on the contrary, the Philosopher, in Book II of *On the Soul*, posits several powers of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0049","text":"[I q. 77 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31611)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0050","text":"I answer that it must be held that there are several powers of the soul. To make this evident, it should be considered that, as the Philosopher says in *On the Heavens* II, things that are lowest among beings cannot attain perfect goodness, but attain some imperfect goodness by a few movements; whereas those above them attain perfect goodness by many movements; and higher than these are those that attain perfect goodness by few movements; but the highest perfection is found in those that possess perfect goodness without movement. Thus, he who is disposed in the lowest way toward health cannot attain perfect health, but attains some slight measure of it by a few remedies; but he is better disposed who can attain perfect health, though by many remedies; and better still, he who does so by few remedies; but best of all, he who has perfect health without any remedy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0051","text":"Therefore it must be said that things below man attain certain particular goods, and consequently have certain few and determinate operations and powers. But man can attain universal and perfect goodness, because he can attain beatitude. Yet he is in the lowest grade, by nature, among those to whom beatitude belongs, and therefore the human soul requires many and diverse operations and powers. But a lesser diversity of powers belongs to angels. In God, however, there is no power or action besides His essence. There is also another reason why the human soul abounds in a diversity of powers: namely, because it is on the boundary between spiritual and corporeal creatures, and therefore the powers of both kinds of creatures concur in it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0052","text":"[I q. 77 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31612)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0053","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that precisely in this the intellectual soul approaches more nearly to the likeness of God than lower creatures do: that it can attain perfect goodness, although through many and diverse means; wherein it falls short of higher beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0054","text":"[I q. 77 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31613)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0055","text":"To the second it must be said that a united power is superior if it extends to equal things. But a multiplied power is superior if more things are subject to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0056","text":"[I q. 77 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31614)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0057","text":"To the third it must be said that one thing has one substantial being, but it can have several operations. Therefore, the soul has one essence, but several powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0058","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0059","text":"[I q. 77 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31615)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0060","text":"We proceed thus to the third article. It seems that the powers are not distinguished by acts and objects. For nothing is determined to a species by that which is subsequent or extrinsic. But an act is subsequent to a power, and an object is extrinsic. Therefore, powers are not distinguished in species by these.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0061","text":"[I q. 77 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31616)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0062","text":"Moreover, contraries are those that differ most greatly. Therefore, if powers were distinguished according to objects, it would follow that there would not be the same power for contraries. This is evidently false in almost all cases, for the same visual power is of white and black, and the same taste is of sweet and bitter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0063","text":"[I q. 77 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31617)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0064","text":"Moreover, when the cause is removed, the effect is removed. Therefore, if the difference of powers arose from the difference of objects, the same object would not belong to diverse powers. This is evidently false, for what the cognitive power knows, the appetitive power desires.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0065","text":"[I q. 77 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31618)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0066","text":"Moreover, that which is per se the cause of something causes it in all cases. But certain diverse objects, which belong to diverse powers, also belong to one power, as sound and color belong to sight and hearing, which are diverse powers; and yet they belong to the one power of common sense. Therefore, powers are not distinguished according to the difference of objects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0067","text":"[I q. 77 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31619)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0068","text":"On the contrary, things that are subsequent are distinguished according to things that are prior. But the Philosopher says in *On the Soul* II that *acts and operations are prior in account to powers*; and prior even to these are opposites, or objects. Therefore, powers are distinguished according to acts and objects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0069","text":"[I q. 77 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31620)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0070","text":"I answer that a power, according to that whereby it is a power, is ordered to an act. Hence the account of a power must be taken from the act to which it is ordered, and consequently the account of a power must be diversified as the account of the act is diversified. Now the account of an act is diversified according to the diverse account of the object. For every action is either of an active or of a passive power. But the object is related to the act of a passive power as a moving principle and cause; for color, insofar as it moves sight, is the principle of vision. But the object is related to the act of an active power as its term and end; thus, the object of the augmentative power is perfected quantity, which is the end of augmentation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0071","text":"Now an action receives its species from these two, namely, from the principle or from the end or term; for heating differs from cooling according as the former proceeds from the hot, namely the active, to the hot, whereas the latter proceeds from the cold to the cold. Hence powers must be diversified according to acts and objects. Yet it must be considered that things which are accidental do not diversify a species. Since being colored is accidental to an animal, the species of animal is not diversified by a difference of color, but by a difference in what belongs per se to an animal, namely, by a difference of the sensitive soul, which is found sometimes with reason and sometimes without reason. Hence rational and irrational are the dividing differences of animal, constituting its diverse species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0072","text":"Thus, then, not every diversity of objects diversifies the powers of the soul, but rather the difference of that to which a power is directed per se. Thus sense is directed per se to a sensible quality, which is per se divided into color, sound, and the like; and therefore one sensitive power is of color, namely sight, and another of sound, namely hearing. But it is accidental to a sensible quality, such as color, to be musical or grammatical, or large and small, or a human being or a stone. And therefore the powers of the soul are not distinguished according to differences of this sort.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0073","text":"[I q. 77 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31621)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0074","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that an act, although it is subsequent to a power in being, is nevertheless prior in intention and according to its account, as an end is to an agent. But an object, although it is extrinsic, is nevertheless the principle or end of an action. Now those things that are intrinsic to a thing are proportioned to the principle and the end.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0075","text":"[I q. 77 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31622)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0076","text":"To the second, it must be said that, if some power were of itself directed to one of the contraries as its object, it would be necessary that the contrary belong to another power. But the power of the soul is not of itself directed to the proper account of a contrary, but to the common account of both contraries, just as sight is not of itself directed to the account of white, but to the account of color. And this is so because one contrary is in some manner the account of the other, since they stand as perfect and imperfect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0077","text":"[I q. 77 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31623)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0078","text":"To the third, it must be said that nothing prevents what is the same in subject from being diverse according to account. And therefore it can belong to diverse powers of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0079","text":"[I q. 77 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31624)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0080","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that a higher power is of itself directed to a more universal account of the object than a lower power, because the higher a power is, the more things it extends to. And therefore many things agree in one account of the object, to which the higher power is of itself directed, though they differ according to the accounts to which the lower powers are of themselves directed. And hence it is that diverse objects belong to diverse lower powers, which nevertheless are subjected to one higher power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0081","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0082","text":"[I q. 77 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31625)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0083","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth article. It seems that there is no order among the powers of the soul. For in those things that fall under one division, there is no prior and posterior, but they are naturally simultaneous. But the powers of the soul are divided against one another. Therefore there is no order among them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0084","text":"[I q. 77 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31626)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0085","text":"Further, the powers of the soul are compared both to objects and to the soul itself. But on the part of the soul, there is no order among them, because the soul is one. Likewise, neither is there order on the part of the objects, since they are diverse and altogether disparate, as is evident concerning color and sound. Therefore there is no order among the powers of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0086","text":"[I q. 77 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31627)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0087","text":"Further, among ordered powers this is found: that the operation of one depends upon the operation of another. But the act of one power of the soul does not depend on the act of another; for sight can proceed into act without hearing, and conversely. Therefore there is no order among the powers of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0088","text":"[I q. 77 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31628)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0089","text":"On the contrary, the Philosopher, in Book II of On the Soul, compares the parts or powers of the soul to figures. But figures have an order among themselves. Therefore the powers of the soul do also.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0090","text":"[I q. 77 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31629)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0091","text":"I answer that, since the soul is one whereas its powers are many, and since one proceeds to multitude by a certain order, it is necessary that there be an order among the powers of the soul. Now a threefold order is discerned among them. Two of these are considered according to the dependence of one power upon another; the third is taken according to the order of objects. The dependence of one power upon another can be understood in two ways: in one way, according to the order of nature, insofar as the perfect are naturally prior to the imperfect; in another way, according to the order of generation and time, insofar as one comes from the imperfect to the perfect. Therefore, according to the first order of powers, the intellectual powers are prior to the sensitive powers; hence they direct and command them. And similarly, in this order, the sensitive powers are prior to the nutritive powers of the soul. But according to the second order, the reverse is the case.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0092","text":"For the nutritive powers of the soul are prior, in the way of generation, to the sensitive powers of the soul; hence they prepare the body for their actions. And it is similar with the sensitive powers in relation to the intellectual powers. According to the third order, however, certain sensitive powers are ordered among themselves, namely sight, hearing, and smell. For the visible is naturally prior, because it is common to higher and lower bodies. But audible sound comes to be in air, which is naturally prior to the mixture of the elements, from which odor follows.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0093","text":"[I q. 77 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31630)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0094","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the species of some genus stand in a relation of prior and posterior, as numbers and figures do, with respect to being; although they are said to exist simultaneously insofar as they receive the predication of a common genus.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0095","text":"[I q. 77 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31631)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0096","text":"To the second, it must be said that this order of the powers of the soul is both on the part of the soul, which according to a certain order has an aptitude for diverse acts, although it is one according to essence; and on the part of the objects; and also on the part of the acts, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0097","text":"[I q. 77 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31632)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0098","text":"To the third, it must be said that that argument proceeds concerning those powers in which order is considered only according to the third mode. But those powers which are ordered according to the other two modes are so related that the act of one depends upon the other.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0099","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0100","text":"[I q. 77 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31633)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0101","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth. It seems that all the powers of the soul are in the soul as in a subject. For as the powers of the body are related to the body, so the powers of the soul are related to the soul. But the body is the subject of bodily powers. Therefore the soul is the subject of the powers of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0102","text":"[I q. 77 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31634)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0103","text":"Further, the operations of the powers of the soul are attributed to the body on account of the soul, because, as is said in *On the Soul* II, *the soul is that by which we first sense and understand*. But the proper principles of the soul's operations are its powers. Therefore the powers are primarily in the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0104","text":"[I q. 77 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31635)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0105","text":"Further, Augustine says, in XII *On Genesis according to the Letter*, that the soul senses certain things not through the body, indeed without the body, as fear and the like; but it senses certain things through the body. But if the sensitive power were not in the soul alone as in its subject, it could sense nothing without the body. Therefore the soul is the subject of the sensitive power; and by equal reasoning, of all the other powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0106","text":"[I q. 77 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31636)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0107","text":"But on the contrary, the Philosopher says, in the book *On Sleep and Waking*, that *sensing is not proper to the soul or to the body, but to the composite*. Therefore the sensitive power is in the composite as in its subject. Therefore the soul alone is not the subject of all its powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0108","text":"[I q. 77 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31637)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0109","text":"I answer that it must be said that the subject of an operative power is that which is capable of operating, for every accident denominates its proper subject. But that which can operate and that which operates are the same. Hence the power must belong, as to its subject, to that to which the operation belongs; as the Philosopher also says at the beginning of *On Sleep and Waking*. Now it is clear from what has been said above that certain operations belong to the soul, which are exercised without a bodily organ, such as understanding and willing. Hence the powers which are the principles of these operations are in the soul as in their subject. But certain operations belong to the soul which are exercised through bodily organs, such as sight through the eye and hearing through the ear. And the same holds concerning all the other operations of the nutritive and sensitive part. Therefore the powers which are the principles of such operations are in the composite as in their subject, and not in the soul alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0110","text":"[I q. 77 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31638)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0111","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that all the powers are said to belong to the soul, not as to a subject, but as to a principle, because through the soul the composite has the ability to perform such operations.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0112","text":"[I q. 77 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31639)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0113","text":"To the second, it must be said that all powers of this kind are in the soul prior to being in the composite, not as in a subject, but as in a principle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0114","text":"[I q. 77 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31640)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0115","text":"To the third, it must be said that Plato's opinion was that sensing is an operation proper to the soul, just as understanding is. But in many matters pertaining to philosophy, Augustine employs the opinions of Plato, not by asserting them, but by recounting them. Nevertheless, as pertains to the present matter, the statement that the soul senses some things with the body and some without the body can be understood in two ways. In one way, so that what I say, “with the body” or “without the body,” determines the act of sensing according as it proceeds from the one sensing. And thus it senses nothing without the body, because the action of sensing cannot proceed from the soul except through a bodily organ. In another way, it can be understood such that the aforesaid expressions determine the act of sensing on the part of the object that is sensed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0116","text":"And thus it senses certain things with the body, that is, as existing in the body, as when it senses a wound or something of this kind; but it senses certain things without the body, that is, not as existing in the body, but only in the apprehension of the soul, as when it senses itself to be saddened or delighted over something heard.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0117","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0118","text":"[I q. 77 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31641)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0119","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth. It seems that the powers of the soul do not flow from its essence. For diverse things do not proceed from one simple thing. But the essence of the soul is one and simple. Therefore, since the powers of the soul are many and diverse, they cannot proceed from its essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0120","text":"[I q. 77 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31642)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0121","text":"Moreover, that from which another proceeds is its cause. But the essence of the soul cannot be called the cause of the powers, as is clear to one who considers the individual kinds of causes. Therefore, the powers of the soul do not flow from its essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0122","text":"[I q. 77 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31643)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0123","text":"Moreover, emanation denotes a certain motion. But nothing is moved by itself, as is proved in Book VII of the Physics, unless perhaps by reason of a part, as an animal is said to be moved by itself because one part of it moves and another is moved. Nor is the soul moved, as is proved in Book I of On the Soul. Therefore the soul does not cause its powers in itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0124","text":"[I q. 77 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31644)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0125","text":"On the contrary, the powers of the soul are certain natural properties of the soul itself. But a subject is the cause of its proper accidents; hence it is also included in the definition of accident, as is clear in Book VII of the Metaphysics. Therefore, the powers of the soul proceed from its essence as from a cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0126","text":"[I q. 77 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31645)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0127","text":"I answer that it must be said that substantial and accidental form partly agree and partly differ. They agree indeed in this, that each is an act, and according to each something is in act in some way. But they differ in two respects. First, because substantial form causes being simply, and its subject is a being in potency only. Accidental form, however, does not cause being simply, but being such, or so much, or disposed in some manner; for its subject is a being in act. Hence it is clear that actuality is found priorly in substantial form rather than in its subject; and because what is first is the cause in every genus, substantial form causes actual being in its subject. But conversely, actuality is found priorly in the subject of accidental form rather than in the accidental form; whence the actuality of accidental form is caused by the actuality of the subject.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0128","text":"Thus the subject, insofar as it is in potency, is receptive of accidental form, whereas insofar as it is in act, it is productive of it. And I say this of a proper and per se accident; for with respect to an extraneous accident, the subject is receptive only, while the productive cause of such an accident is an extrinsic agent. Secondly, substantial and accidental form differ because, since what is less principal exists for the sake of what is more principal, matter exists for the sake of substantial form; but conversely, accidental form exists for the completion of the subject. Now it is evident from what has been said that the subject of the powers of the soul is either the soul alone itself, which can be the subject of an accident insofar as it has some potentiality, as was said above, or the composite. But the composite is in act through the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0129","text":"Hence it is manifest that all the powers of the soul, whether their subject is the soul alone or the composite, flow from the essence of the soul as from a principle, because it has already been said that an accident is caused by a subject insofar as it is in act, and is received in it insofar as it is in potency.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0130","text":"[I q. 77 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31646)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0131","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that many things can naturally proceed from one simple thing in a certain order, and again on account of the diversity of recipients. Thus, therefore, many and diverse powers proceed from the one essence of the soul, both on account of the order of the powers and according to the diversity of bodily organs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0132","text":"[I q. 77 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31647)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0133","text":"To the second, it must be said that the subject is the cause of its proper accident both finally and, in a certain way, actively; and also materially, insofar as it is receptive of the accident. From this it can be gathered that the essence of the soul is the cause of all the powers as an end and as an active principle, but of some as receptive.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0134","text":"[I q. 77 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31648)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0135","text":"To the third, it must be said that the emanation of proper accidents from a subject is not through any change, but through a certain natural resultance, just as one thing naturally results from another, as color from light.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0136","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0137","text":"[I q. 77 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31649)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0138","text":"The seventh point is addressed thus. It seems that one power of the soul does not arise from another. For among those things that begin to be at the same time, one does not arise from another. But all the powers of the soul are created together with the soul. Therefore, one of them does not arise from another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0139","text":"[I q. 77 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31650)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0140","text":"Moreover, a power of the soul arises from the soul as an accident from a subject. But one power of the soul cannot be the subject of another, because an accident does not belong to an accident. Therefore, one power does not arise from another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0141","text":"[I q. 77 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31651)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0142","text":"Moreover, an opposite does not arise from its opposite, but each thing arises from what is specifically similar. But the powers of the soul are divided in opposition, like diverse species. Therefore, one of them does not proceed from another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0143","text":"[I q. 77 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31652)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0144","text":"On the contrary, powers are known through acts. But the act of one power is caused by another, as the act of imagination by the act of sense. Therefore, one power of the soul is caused by another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0145","text":"[I q. 77 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31653)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0146","text":"I answer that, in those things which proceed from one thing according to a natural order, just as the first is the cause of all, so that which is nearer to the first is in some way the cause of those which are more remote. Now it has been shown above that there is a multiple order among the powers of the soul. And therefore one power of the soul proceeds from the essence of the soul by means of another. But because the essence of the soul is related to the powers both as an active and final principle, and as a receptive principle, either separately in itself or together with the body; and since agent and end are more perfect, whereas a receptive principle, as such, is less perfect; it follows that the powers of the soul which are prior according to the order of perfection and nature are the principles of the others in the manner of an end and of an active principle. For we see that sense is for the sake of intellect, and not conversely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0147","text":"Sense, moreover, is a certain deficient participation in intellect; hence, according to natural origin, it is in some way from intellect, as the imperfect from the perfect. But according to the way of a receptive principle, conversely, the more imperfect powers are found to be principles with respect to the others, just as the soul, insofar as it has sensitive power, is considered as a subject and a certain material thing with respect to intellect. And on this account the more imperfect powers are prior in the way of generation; for an animal is generated before a human being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0148","text":"[I q. 77 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31654)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0149","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, just as the power of the soul flows from its essence, not through change, but through a certain natural resultance, and exists simultaneously with the soul; so it is also with one power in relation to another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0150","text":"[I q. 77 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31655)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0151","text":"To the second it must be said that an accident cannot, through itself, be the subject of an accident; but one accident is received in substance prior to another, as quantity prior to quality. And in this way one accident is said to be the subject of another, as surface is of color, insofar as substance receives one accident through the mediation of another. And the same may be said of the powers of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0152","text":"[I q. 77 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31656)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0153","text":"To the third it must be said that the powers of the soul are opposed to one another by the opposition of perfect and imperfect, as also are the species of numbers and figures. But this opposition does not impede the origin of one from another, because imperfect things naturally proceed from perfect things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0154","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0155","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31657)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0156","text":"The eighth is approached thus. It seems that all the powers of the soul remain in the soul separated from the body. For it is said in the book *On Spirit and Soul* that *the soul departs from the body, drawing with it sense and imagination, reason and intellect and intelligence, concupiscibility and irascibility*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0157","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31658)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0158","text":"Further, the powers of the soul are its natural properties. But a property is always present and is never separated from that of which it is a property. Therefore the powers of the soul are in it even after death.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0158"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0159","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31659)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0159"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0160","text":"Further, the powers of the soul, even the sensitive powers, are not weakened when the body is weakened, because, as is said in *On the Soul* I, *if an old man were to receive the eye of a young man, he would surely see just as a young man does*. But weakness is a way toward corruption. Therefore the powers of the soul are not corrupted when the body is corrupted, but remain in the separated soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0160"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0161","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31660)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0161"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0162","text":"Further, memory is a power of the sensitive soul, as the Philosopher proves. But memory remains in the separated soul, for it is said, Luke XVI, to the rich feaster existing in Hell according to his soul: *remember that you received good things in your life*. Therefore memory remains in the separated soul, and consequently the other powers of the sensitive part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0162"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0163","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31661)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0163"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0164","text":"Further, joy and sorrow are in the concupiscible power, which is a power of the sensitive part. But it is manifest that separated souls grieve and rejoice over the rewards or punishments which they have. Therefore the concupiscible power remains in the separated soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0164"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0165","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31662)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0165"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0166","text":"Further, Augustine says, in XII *On Genesis according to the Letter*, that just as the soul, when the body lies without sensation and is not yet wholly dead, sees certain things according to an imaginary vision, so also when it has been wholly separated from the body by death. But imagination is a power of the sensitive part. Therefore a power of the sensitive part remains in the separated soul, and consequently all the other powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0166"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0167","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31663)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0167"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0168","text":"But against this is what is said in the book *On Ecclesiastical Dogmas*: *man consists of only two substances, a soul with its reason, and flesh with its senses*. Therefore, when the flesh has died, the sensitive powers do not remain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0168"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0169","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31664)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0169"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0170","text":"I answer that, as has already been said, all the powers of the soul are related to the soul alone as to their principle. But certain powers are related to the soul alone as to their subject, as intellect and will are. And powers of this kind must remain in the soul when the body has been destroyed. But certain powers are in the composite as in their subject, such as all the powers of the sensitive and nutritive part. Now, once the subject has been destroyed, an accident cannot remain. Hence, when the composite has perished, powers of this kind do not remain actually; rather, they remain in the soul only virtually, as in a principle or root. And thus it is false what some say, namely, that powers of this kind remain in the soul even when the body has perished. And much more false is what they say, namely, that even the acts of these powers remain in the separated soul, because there is no operation of such powers except through a bodily organ.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0170"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0171","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31665)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0171"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0172","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that that book has no authority. Hence, what is written there is dismissed with the same ease with which it is stated. Yet it can be said that the soul carries such powers with it, not actually, but virtually.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0172"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0173","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31666)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0173"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0174","text":"To the second, it must be said that the powers of this kind which we say do not remain actually in the separated soul are not properties of the soul alone, but of the composite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0174"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0175","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31667)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0175"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0176","text":"To the third, it must be said that powers of this kind are said not to be weakened when the body is weakened because the soul remains unchanged, and it is the virtual principle of such powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0176"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0177","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31668)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0177"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0178","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that that recollection is understood in the manner in which Augustine places memory in the mind, not in the manner in which it is posited as a part of the sensitive soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0178"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0179","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31669)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0179"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0180","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that sadness and joy are in the separated soul, not according to the sensitive appetite, but according to the intellective appetite, just as they are also in angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0180"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0181","text":"[I q. 77 a. 8 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31670)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0181"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0182","text":"To the sixth, it must be said that Augustine speaks there by way of inquiry, not assertion. Hence, he retracts certain things said there.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_69:P0182"]}],"source_digest":"8c2cd01c4e36c0bbffa795a87c6953947b482baaf59777141731607777c5de39","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 77"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 77","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70","title":"First Part · Question 78","order":79,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_70","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 78 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31671)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de potentiis animae in speciali. Ad considerationem autem theologi pertinet inquirere specialiter solum de potentiis intellectivis et appetitivis, in quibus virtutes inveniuntur. Sed quia cognitio harum potentiarum quodammodo dependet ex aliis, ideo nostra consideratio de potentiis animae in speciali erit tripartita, primo namque considerandum est de his quae sunt praeambula ad intellectum; secundo, de potentiis intellectivis; tertio, de potentiis appetitivis. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, de generibus potentiarum animae. Secundo, de speciebus vegetativae partis. Tertio, de sensibus exterioribus. Quarto, de sensibus interioribus.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 78 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31672)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sint quinque genera potentiarum animae distinguenda, scilicet vegetativum, sensitivum, appetitivum, motivum secundum locum, et intellectivum. Potentiae enim animae dicuntur partes ipsius. Sed tantum tres partes animae communiter ab omnibus assignantur, scilicet anima vegetabilis, anima sensibilis, et anima rationalis. Ergo tantum tria sunt genera potentiarum animae, et non quinque.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31673)\n\nPraeterea, potentiae animae sunt principia operum vitae. Sed quatuor modis dicitur aliquid vivere. Dicit enim philosophus, in II de anima, *multipliciter ipso vivere dicto, etsi unum aliquod horum insit solum, aliquid dicimus vivere; ut intellectus, et sensus, motus et status secundum locum, adhuc autem motus secundum alimentum, et decrementum et augmentum*. Ergo tantum quatuor sunt genera potentiarum animae, appetitivo excluso.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31674)\n\nPraeterea, ad illud quod est commune omnibus potentiis, non debet deputari aliquod speciale animae genus. Sed appetere convenit cuilibet potentiae animae. Visus enim appetit visibile conveniens, unde dicitur Eccli. XL, *gratiam et speciem desiderabit oculus, et super hoc virides sationes*. Et eadem ratione, quaelibet alia potentia desiderat obiectum sibi conveniens. Ergo non debet poni appetitivum unum speciale genus potentiarum animae.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31675)\n\nPraeterea, principium movens in animalibus est sensus, aut intellectus, aut appetitus, ut dicitur in III de anima. Non ergo motivum debet poni speciale genus animae praeter praedicta.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31676)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in II de anima, *potentias autem dicimus vegetativum sensitivum, appetitivum, motivum secundum locum, et intellectivum*.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31677)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quinque sunt genera potentiarum animae, quae numerata sunt. Tres vero dicuntur animae. Quatuor vero dicuntur modi vivendi. Et huius diversitatis ratio est, quia diversae animae distinguuntur secundum quod diversimode operatio animae supergreditur operationem naturae corporalis, tota enim natura corporalis subiacet animae, et comparatur ad ipsam sicut materia et instrumentum. Est ergo quaedam operatio animae, quae intantum excedit naturam corpoream, quod neque etiam exercetur per organum corporale. Et talis est operatio animae rationalis. Est autem alia operatio animae infra istam, quae quidem fit per organum corporale, non tamen per aliquam corpoream qualitatem. Et talis est operatio animae sensibilis, quia etsi calidum et frigidum, et humidum et siccum, et aliae huiusmodi qualitates corporeae requirantur ad operationem sensus; non tamen ita quod mediante virtute talium qualitatum operatio animae sensibilis procedat; sed requiruntur solum ad debitam dispositionem organi. Infima autem operationum animae est, quae fit per organum corporeum, et virtute corporeae qualitatis. Supergreditur tamen operationem naturae corporeae, quia motiones corporum sunt ab exteriori principio, huiusmodi autem operationes sunt a principio intrinseco; hoc enim commune est omnibus operationibus animae; omne enim animatum aliquo modo movet seipsum. Et talis est operatio animae vegetabilis, digestio enim, et ea quae consequuntur, fit instrumentaliter per actionem caloris, ut dicitur in II de anima. Genera vero potentiarum animae distinguuntur secundum obiecta. Quanto enim potentia est altior, tanto respicit universalius obiectum, ut supra dictum est. Obiectum autem operationis animae in triplici ordine potest considerari. Alicuius enim potentiae animae obiectum est solum corpus animae unitum. Et hoc genus potentiarum animae dicitur vegetativum, non enim vegetativa potentia agit nisi in corpus cui anima unitur. Est autem aliud genus potentiarum animae, quod respicit universalius obiectum, scilicet omne corpus sensibile; et non solum corpus animae unitum. Est autem aliud genus potentiarum animae, quod respicit adhuc universalius obiectum, scilicet non solum corpus sensibile, sed universaliter omne ens. Ex quo patet quod ista duo secunda genera potentiarum animae habent operationem non solum respectu rei coniunctae, sed etiam respectu rei extrinsecae. Cum autem operans oporteat aliquo modo coniungi suo obiecto circa quod operatur, necesse est extrinsecam rem, quae est obiectum operationis animae, secundum duplicem rationem ad animam comparari. Uno modo, secundum quod nata est animae coniungi et in anima esse per suam similitudinem. Et quantum ad hoc, sunt duo genera potentiarum, scilicet sensitivum, respectu obiecti minus communis, quod est corpus sensibile; et intellectivum, respectu obiecti communissimi, quod est ens universale. Alio vero modo, secundum quod ipsa anima inclinatur et tendit in rem exteriorem. Et secundum hanc etiam comparationem, sunt duo genera potentiarum animae, unum quidem, scilicet appetitivum, secundum quod anima comparatur ad rem extrinsecam ut ad finem, qui est primum in intentione; aliud autem motivum secundum locum, prout anima comparatur ad rem exteriorem sicut ad terminum operationis et motus; ad consequendum enim aliquod desideratum et intentum, omne animal movetur. Modi vero vivendi distinguuntur secundum gradus viventium. Quaedam enim viventia sunt, in quibus est tantum vegetativum, sicut in plantis. Quaedam vero, in quibus cum vegetativo est etiam sensitivum, non tamen motivum secundum locum; sicut sunt immobilia animalia, ut conchilia. Quaedam vero sunt, quae supra hoc habent motivum secundum locum; ut perfecta animalia, quae multis indigent ad suam vitam, et ideo indigent motu, ut vitae necessaria procul posita quaerere possint. Quaedam vero viventia sunt, in quibus cum his est intellectivum, scilicet in hominibus. Appetitivum autem non constituit aliquem gradum viventium, quia in quibuscumque est sensus, est etiam appetitus, ut dicitur in II libro de anima.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31678)\n\nEt per hoc solvuntur duo prima obiecta.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31679)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod appetitus naturalis est inclinatio cuiuslibet rei in aliquid, ex natura sua, unde naturali appetitu quaelibet potentia desiderat sibi conveniens. Sed appetitus animalis consequitur formam apprehensam. Et ad huiusmodi appetitum requiritur specialis animae potentia, et non sufficit sola apprehensio. Res enim appetitur prout est in sua natura, non est autem secundum suam naturam in virtute apprehensiva, sed secundum suam similitudinem. Unde patet quod visus appetit naturaliter visibile solum ad suum actum, scilicet ad videndum, animal autem appetit rem visam per vim appetitivam, non solum ad videndum, sed etiam ad alios usus. Si autem non indigeret anima rebus perceptis a sensu, nisi propter actiones sensuum, scilicet ut eas sentiret; non oporteret appetitivum ponere speciale genus inter potentias animae, quia sufficeret appetitus naturalis potentiarum.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31680)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, quamvis sensus et appetitus sint principia moventia in animalibus perfectis, non tamen sensus et appetitus, inquantum huiusmodi, sufficiunt ad movendum, nisi superadderetur eis aliqua virtus, nam in immobilibus animalibus est sensus et appetitus, non tamen habent vim motivam. Haec autem vis motiva non solum est in appetitu et sensu ut imperante motum; sed etiam est in ipsis partibus corporis, ut sint habilia ad obediendum appetitui animae moventis. Cuius signum est, quod quando membra removentur a sua dispositione naturali, non obediunt appetitui ad motum.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 78 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31681)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod inconvenienter partes vegetativae assignentur, scilicet nutritivum, augmentativum, et generativum. Huiusmodi enim vires dicuntur naturales. Sed potentiae animae sunt supra vires naturales. Ergo huiusmodi vires non debent poni potentiae animae.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31682)\n\nPraeterea, ad id quod est commune viventibus et non viventibus, non debet aliqua potentia animae deputari. Sed generatio est communis omnibus generabilibus et corruptibilibus, tam viventibus quam non viventibus. Ergo vis generativa non debet poni potentia animae.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31683)\n\nPraeterea, anima potentior est quam natura corporea. Sed natura corporea eadem virtute activa dat speciem et debitam quantitatem. Ergo multo magis anima. Non est ergo alia potentia animae augmentativa a generativa.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31684)\n\nPraeterea, unaquaeque res conservatur in esse per id per quod esse habet. Sed potentia generativa est per quam acquiritur esse viventis. Ergo per eandem res viva conservatur. Sed ad conservationem rei viventis ordinatur vis nutritiva, ut dicitur in II de anima, est enim potentia potens salvare suscipiens ipsam. Non debet ergo distingui nutritiva potentia a generativa.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31685)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in II de anima, quod opera huius animae sunt generare, et alimento uti, et iterum augmentum facere.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31686)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod tres sunt potentiae vegetativae partis. Vegetativum enim, ut dictum est, habet pro obiecto ipsum corpus vivens per animam, ad quod quidem corpus triplex animae operatio est necessaria. Una quidem, per quam esse acquirat, et ad hoc ordinatur potentia generativa. Alia vero, per quam corpus vivum acquirit debitam quantitatem, et ad hoc ordinatur vis augmentativa. Alia vero, per quam corpus viventis salvatur et in esse, et in quantitate debita, et ad hoc ordinatur vis nutritiva. Est tamen quaedam differentia attendenda inter has potentias. Nam nutritiva et augmentativa habent suum effectum in eo in quo sunt, quia ipsum corpus unitum animae augetur et conservatur per vim augmentativam et nutritivam in eadem anima existentem. Sed vis generativa habet effectum suum, non in eodem corpore, sed in alio, quia nihil est generativum sui ipsius. Et ideo vis generativa quodammodo appropinquat ad dignitatem animae sensitivae, quae habet operationem in res exteriores, licet excellentiori modo et universaliori, supremum enim inferioris naturae attingit id quod est infimum superioris, ut patet per Dionysium, in VII cap. de Div. Nom. Et ideo inter istas tres potentias finalior et principalior et perfectior est generativa, ut dicitur in II de anima, est enim rei iam perfectae facere alteram qualis ipsa est. Generativae autem deserviunt et augmentativa et nutritiva, augmentativae vero nutritiva.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31687)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod huiusmodi vires dicuntur naturales, tum quia habent effectum similem naturae, quae etiam dat esse et quantitatem et conservationem (licet hae vires habeant hoc altiori modo), tum quia hae vires exercent suas actiones instrumentaliter per qualitates activas et passivas, quae sunt naturalium actionum principia.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31688)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod generatio in rebus inanimatis est totaliter ab extrinseco. Sed generatio viventium est quodam altiori modo, per aliquid ipsius viventis, quod est semen, in quo est aliquod principium corporis formativum. Et ideo oportet esse aliquam potentiam rei viventis, per quam semen huiusmodi praeparetur, et haec est vis generativa.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31689)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, quia generatio viventium est ex aliquo semine, oportet quod in principio animal generetur parvae quantitatis. Et propter hoc necesse est quod habeat potentiam animae, per quam ad debitam quantitatem perducatur. Sed corpus inanimatum generatur ex materia determinata ab agente extrinseco, et ideo simul recipit speciem et quantitatem secundum materiae conditionem.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31690)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, sicut iam dictum est, operatio vegetativi principii completur mediante calore, cuius est humidum consumere. Et ideo, ad restaurationem humidi deperditi, necesse est habere potentiam nutritivam, per quam alimentum convertatur in substantiam corporis. Quod etiam est necessarium ad actum virtutis augmentativae et generativae.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 78 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31691)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod inconvenienter distinguantur quinque sensus exteriores. Sensus enim est cognoscitivus accidentium. Sunt autem multa genera accidentium. Cum ergo potentiae distinguantur per obiecta, videtur quod sensus multiplicentur secundum numerum qui est in generibus accidentium.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31692)\n\nPraeterea, magnitudo et figura, et alia quae dicuntur sensibilia communia, non sunt sensibilia per accidens, sed contra ea dividuntur in II de anima. Diversitas autem per se obiectorum diversificat potentias. Cum ergo plus differant magnitudo et figura a colore quam sonus; videtur quod multo magis debeat esse alia potentia sensitiva cognoscitiva magnitudinis aut figurae, quam coloris et soni.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31693)\n\nPraeterea, unus sensus est unius contrarietatis; sicut visus albi et nigri. Sed tactus est cognoscitivus plurium contrarietatum, scilicet calidi et frigidi, humidi et sicci, et huiusmodi. Ergo non est sensus unus, sed plures. Ergo plures sensus sunt quam quinque.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31694)\n\nPraeterea, species non dividitur contra genus. Sed gustus est tactus quidam. Ergo non debet poni alter sensus praeter tactum.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31695)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod *non est alter sensus praeter quinque*.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31696)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod rationem distinctionis et numeri sensuum exteriorum quidam accipere voluerunt ex parte organorum, in quibus aliquod elementorum dominatur, vel aqua, vel aer, vel aliquid huiusmodi. Quidam autem ex parte medii, quod est vel coniunctum, vel extrinsecum; et hoc vel aer, vel aqua, vel aliquid huiusmodi. Quidam autem ex diversa natura sensibilium qualitatum, secundum quod est qualitas simplicis corporis, vel sequens complexionem. Sed nihil istorum conveniens est. Non enim potentiae sunt propter organa, sed organa propter potentias, unde non propter hoc sunt diversae potentiae, quia sunt diversa organa; sed ideo natura instituit diversitatem in organis, ut congruerent diversitati potentiarum. Et similiter diversa media diversis sensibus attribuit, secundum quod erat conveniens ad actus potentiarum. Naturas autem sensibilium qualitatum cognoscere non est sensus, sed intellectus. Accipienda est ergo ratio numeri et distinctionis exteriorum sensuum, secundum illud quod proprie et per se ad sensum pertinet. Est autem sensus quaedam potentia passiva, quae nata est immutari ab exteriori sensibili. Exterius ergo immutativum est quod per se a sensu percipitur, et secundum cuius diversitatem sensitivae potentiae distinguuntur. Est autem duplex immutatio, una naturalis, et alia spiritualis. Naturalis quidem, secundum quod forma immutantis recipitur in immutato secundum esse naturale, sicut calor in calefacto. Spiritualis autem, secundum quod forma immutantis recipitur in immutato secundum esse spirituale; ut forma coloris in pupilla, quae non fit per hoc colorata. Ad operationem autem sensus requiritur immutatio spiritualis, per quam intentio formae sensibilis fiat in organo sensus. Alioquin, si sola immutatio naturalis sufficeret ad sentiendum, omnia corpora naturalia sentirent dum alterantur. Sed in quibusdam sensibus invenitur immutatio spiritualis tantum, sicut in visu. In quibusdam autem, cum immutatione spirituali, etiam naturalis; vel ex parte obiecti tantum, vel etiam ex parte organi. Ex parte autem obiecti, invenitur transmutatio naturalis, secundum locum quidem, in sono, qui est obiectum auditus, nam sonus ex percussione causatur et aeris commotione. Secundum alterationem vero, in odore, qui est obiectum olfactus, oportet enim per calidum alterari aliquo modo corpus, ad hoc quod spiret odorem. Ex parte autem organi, est immutatio naturalis in tactu et gustu, nam et manus tangens calida calefit, et lingua humectatur per humiditatem saporum. Organum vero olfactus aut auditus nulla naturali immutatione immutatur in sentiendo, nisi per accidens. Visus autem, quia est absque immutatione naturali et organi et obiecti, est maxime spiritualis, et perfectior inter omnes sensus, et communior. Et post hoc auditus, et deinde olfactus, qui habent immutationem naturalem ex parte obiecti. Motus tamen localis est perfectior et naturaliter prior quam motus alterationis, ut probatur in VIII Physic. Tactus autem et gustus sunt maxime materiales, de quorum distinctione post dicetur. Et inde est quod alii tres sensus non fiunt per medium coniunctum, ne aliqua naturalis transmutatio pertingat ad organum, ut accidit in his duobus sensibus.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31697)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod non omnia accidentia habent vim immutativam secundum se; sed solae qualitates tertiae speciei, secundum quas contingit alteratio. Et ideo solae huiusmodi qualitates sunt obiecta sensuum, quia, ut dicitur in VII Physic., *secundum eadem alteratur sensus, secundum quae alterantur corpora inanimata*.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31698)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod magnitudo et figura et huiusmodi, quae dicuntur communia sensibilia, sunt media inter sensibilia per accidens et sensibilia propria, quae sunt obiecta sensuum. Nam sensibilia propria primo et per se immutant sensum; cum sint qualitates alterantes. Sensibilia vero communia omnia reducuntur ad quantitatem. Et de magnitudine quidem et numero, patet quod sunt species quantitatis. Figura autem est qualitas circa quantitatem; cum consistat ratio figurae in terminatione magnitudinis. Motus autem et quies sentiuntur, secundum quod subiectum uno modo vel pluribus modis se habet secundum magnitudinem subiecti vel localis distantiae, quantum ad motum augmenti et motum localem; vel etiam secundum sensibiles qualitates, ut in motu alterationis, et sic sentire motum et quietem est quodammodo sentire unum et multa. Quantitas autem est proximum subiectum qualitatis alterativae, ut superficies coloris. Et ideo sensibilia communia non movent sensum primo et per se, sed ratione sensibilis qualitatis; ut superficies ratione coloris. Nec tamen sunt sensibilia per accidens, quia huiusmodi sensibilia aliquam diversitatem faciunt in immutatione sensus. Alio enim modo immutatur sensus a magna superficie, et a parva, quia etiam ipsa albedo dicitur magna vel parva, et ideo dividitur secundum proprium subiectum.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31699)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut philosophus videtur dicere in II de anima, sensus tactus est unus genere, sed dividitur in multos sensus secundum speciem; et propter hoc est diversarum contrarietatum. Qui tamen non separantur ab invicem secundum organum, sed per totum corpus se concomitantur; et ideo eorum distinctio non apparet. Gustus autem, qui est perceptivus dulcis et amari, concomitatur tactum in lingua, non autem per totum corpus; et ideo de facili a tactu distinguitur. Posset tamen dici quod omnes illae contrarietates, et singulae conveniunt in uno genere proximo, et omnes in uno genere communi, quod est obiectum tactus secundum rationem communem. Sed illud genus commune est innominatum; sicut etiam genus proximum calidi et frigidi est innominatum.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31700)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod sensus gustus, secundum dictum philosophi, est quaedam species tactus quae est in lingua tantum. Non autem distinguitur a tactu in genere, sed a tactu quantum ad illas species quae per totum corpus diffunduntur. Si vero tactus sit unus sensus tantum, propter unam rationem communem obiecti, dicendum erit quod secundum rationem diversam immutationis, distinguitur gustus a tactu. Nam tactus immutatur naturali immutatione, et non solum spirituali, quantum ad organum suum, secundum qualitatem quae ei proprie obiicitur. Gustus autem organum non immutatur de necessitate naturali immutatione secundum qualitatem quae ei proprie obiicitur, ut scilicet lingua fiat dulcis vel amara; sed secundum praeambulam qualitatem, in qua fundatur sapor, scilicet secundum humorem, qui est obiectum tactus.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 78 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31701)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod interiores sensus inconvenienter distinguantur. Commune enim non dividitur contra proprium. Ergo sensus communis non debet enumerari inter vires interiores sensitivas, praeter sensus exteriores proprios.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31702)\n\nPraeterea, ad id ad quod sufficit sensus proprius et exterior, non oportet ponere aliquam vim apprehensivam interiorem. Sed ad iudicandum de sensibilibus, sufficiunt sensus proprii et exteriores, unusquisque enim sensus iudicat de proprio obiecto. Similiter etiam videntur sufficere ad hoc quod percipiant suos actus, quia cum actio sensus sit quodammodo medium inter potentiam et obiectum, videtur quod multo magis visus possit suam visionem, tanquam sibi propinquiorem, percipere, quam colorem; et sic de aliis. Non ergo necessarium fuit ad hoc ponere interiorem potentiam, quae dicatur sensus communis.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31703)\n\nPraeterea, secundum philosophum, phantasticum et memorativum sunt passiones primi sensitivi. Sed passio non dividitur contra subiectum. Ergo memoria et phantasia non debent poni aliae potentiae praeter sensum.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31704)\n\nPraeterea, intellectus minus dependet a sensu, quam quaecumque potentia sensitivae partis. Sed intellectus nihil cognoscit nisi accipiendo a sensu, unde dicitur in I posteriorum, quod *quibus deest unus sensus, deficit una scientia*. Ergo multo minus debet poni una potentia sensitivae partis ad percipiendum intentiones quas non percipit sensus, quam vocant aestimativam.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31705)\n\nPraeterea, actus cogitativae, qui est conferre et componere et dividere, et actus reminiscitivae, qui est quodam syllogismo uti ad inquirendum, non minus distant ab actu aestimativae et memorativae, quam actus aestimativae ab actu phantasiae. Debent ergo vel cogitativa et reminiscitiva poni aliae vires praeter aestimativam et memorativam; vel aestimativa et memorativa non debent poni aliae vires praeter phantasiam.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 4 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31706)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus, XII super Gen. ad Litt., ponit tria genera visionum, scilicet corporalem, quae fit per sensum; et spiritualem, quae fit per imaginationem sive phantasiam; et intellectualem, quae fit per intellectum. Non est ergo aliqua vis interior quae sit media inter sensum et intellectum, nisi imaginativa tantum.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31707)\n\nSed contra est quod Avicenna, in suo libro de anima, ponit quinque potentias sensitivas interiores, scilicet sensum communem, phantasiam, imaginativam, aestimativam, et memorativam.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31708)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum natura non deficiat in necessariis, oportet esse tot actiones animae sensitivae, quot sufficiant ad vitam animalis perfecti. Et quaecumque harum actionum non possunt reduci in unum principium, requirunt diversas potentias, cum potentia animae nihil aliud sit quam proximum principium operationis animae. Est autem considerandum quod ad vitam animalis perfecti requiritur quod non solum apprehendat rem apud praesentiam sensibilis, sed etiam apud eius absentiam. Alioquin, cum animalis motus et actio sequantur apprehensionem, non moveretur animal ad inquirendum aliquid absens; cuius contrarium apparet maxime in animalibus perfectis, quae moventur motu processivo; moventur enim ad aliquid absens apprehensum. Oportet ergo quod animal per animam sensitivam non solum recipiat species sensibilium, cum praesentialiter immutatur ab eis; sed etiam eas retineat et conservet. Recipere autem et retinere reducuntur in corporalibus ad diversa principia, nam humida bene recipiunt, et male retinent; e contrario autem est de siccis. Unde, cum potentia sensitiva sit actus organi corporalis, oportet esse aliam potentiam quae recipiat species sensibilium, et quae conservet. Rursus considerandum est quod, si animal moveretur solum propter delectabile et contristabile secundum sensum, non esset necessarium ponere in animali nisi apprehensionem formarum quas percipit sensus, in quibus delectatur aut horret. Sed necessarium est animali ut quaerat aliqua vel fugiat, non solum quia sunt convenientia vel non convenientia ad sentiendum, sed etiam propter aliquas alias commoditates et utilitates, sive nocumenta, sicut ovis videns lupum venientem fugit, non propter indecentiam coloris vel figurae, sed quasi inimicum naturae; et similiter avis colligit paleam, non quia delectet sensum, sed quia est utilis ad nidificandum. Necessarium est ergo animali quod percipiat huiusmodi intentiones, quas non percipit sensus exterior. Et huius perceptionis oportet esse aliquod aliud principium, cum perceptio formarum sensibilium sit ex immutatione sensibilis, non autem perceptio intentionum praedictarum. Sic ergo ad receptionem formarum sensibilium ordinatur sensus proprius et communis, de quorum distinctione post dicetur. Ad harum autem formarum retentionem aut conservationem ordinatur phantasia, sive imaginatio, quae idem sunt, est enim phantasia sive imaginatio quasi thesaurus quidam formarum per sensum acceptarum. Ad apprehendendum autem intentiones quae per sensum non accipiuntur, ordinatur vis aestimativa. Ad conservandum autem eas, vis memorativa, quae est thesaurus quidam huiusmodi intentionum. Cuius signum est, quod principium memorandi fit in animalibus ex aliqua huiusmodi intentione, puta quod est nocivum vel conveniens. Et ipsa ratio praeteriti, quam attendit memoria, inter huiusmodi intentiones computatur. Considerandum est autem quod, quantum ad formas sensibiles, non est differentia inter hominem et alia animalia, similiter enim immutantur a sensibilibus exterioribus. Sed quantum ad intentiones praedictas, differentia est, nam alia animalia percipiunt huiusmodi intentiones solum naturali quodam instinctu, homo autem etiam per quandam collationem. Et ideo quae in aliis animalibus dicitur aestimativa naturalis, in homine dicitur cogitativa, quae per collationem quandam huiusmodi intentiones adinvenit. Unde etiam dicitur ratio particularis, cui medici assignant determinatum organum, scilicet mediam partem capitis, est enim collativa intentionum individualium, sicut ratio intellectiva intentionum universalium. Ex parte autem memorativae, non solum habet memoriam, sicut cetera animalia, in subita recordatione praeteritorum; sed etiam reminiscentiam, quasi syllogistice inquirendo praeteritorum memoriam, secundum individuales intentiones. Avicenna vero ponit quintam potentiam, mediam inter aestimativam et imaginativam, quae componit et dividit formas imaginatas; ut patet cum ex forma imaginata auri et forma imaginata montis componimus unam formam montis aurei, quem nunquam vidimus. Sed ista operatio non apparet in aliis animalibus ab homine, in quo ad hoc sufficit virtus imaginativa. Cui etiam hanc actionem attribuit Averroes, in libro quodam quem fecit de sensu et sensibilibus. Et sic non est necesse ponere nisi quatuor vires interiores sensitivae partis, scilicet sensum communem et imaginationem, aestimativam et memorativam.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31709)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod sensus interior non dicitur communis per praedicationem, sicut genus; sed sicut communis radix et principium exteriorum sensuum.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31710)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod sensus proprius iudicat de sensibili proprio, discernendo ipsum ab aliis quae cadunt sub eodem sensu, sicut discernendo album a nigro vel a viridi. Sed discernere album a dulci non potest neque visus neque gustus, quia oportet quod qui inter aliqua discernit, utrumque cognoscat. Unde oportet ad sensum communem pertinere discretionis iudicium, ad quem referantur, sicut ad communem terminum, omnes apprehensiones sensuum; a quo etiam percipiantur intentiones sensuum, sicut cum aliquis videt se videre. Hoc enim non potest fieri per sensum proprium, qui non cognoscit nisi formam sensibilis a quo immutatur; in qua immutatione perficitur visio, et ex qua immutatione sequitur alia immutatio in sensu communi, qui visionem percipit.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31711)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut una potentia oritur ab anima, alia mediante, ut supra dictum est; ita etiam anima subiicitur alii potentiae, mediante alia. Et secundum hunc modum, phantasticum et memorativum dicuntur passiones primi sensitivi.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31712)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, licet intellectus operatio oriatur a sensu, tamen in re apprehensa per sensum intellectus multa cognoscit quae sensus percipere non potest. Et similiter aestimativa, licet inferiori modo.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31713)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod illam eminentiam habet cogitativa et memorativa in homine, non per id quod est proprium sensitivae partis; sed per aliquam affinitatem et propinquitatem ad rationem universalem, secundum quandam refluentiam. Et ideo non sunt aliae vires, sed eaedem, perfectiores quam sint in aliis animalibus.\n\n[I q. 78 a. 4 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31714)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod Augustinus spiritualem visionem dicit esse, quae fit per similitudines corporum in absentia corporum. Unde patet quod communis est omnibus interioribus apprehensionibus.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0002","text":"[I q. 78 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31671)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de potentiis animae in speciali. Ad considerationem autem theologi pertinet inquirere specialiter solum de potentiis intellectivis et appetitivis, in quibus virtutes inveniuntur. Sed quia cognitio harum potentiarum quodammodo dependet ex aliis, ideo nostra consideratio de potentiis animae in speciali erit tripartita, primo namque considerandum est de his quae sunt praeambula ad intellectum; secundo, de potentiis intellectivis; tertio, de potentiis appetitivis. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, de generibus potentiarum animae. Secundo, de speciebus vegetativae partis. Tertio, de sensibus exterioribus. Quarto, de sensibus interioribus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0005","text":"[I q. 78 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31672)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sint quinque genera potentiarum animae distinguenda, scilicet vegetativum, sensitivum, appetitivum, motivum secundum locum, et intellectivum. Potentiae enim animae dicuntur partes ipsius. Sed tantum tres partes animae communiter ab omnibus assignantur, scilicet anima vegetabilis, anima sensibilis, et anima rationalis. Ergo tantum tria sunt genera potentiarum animae, et non quinque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0007","text":"[I q. 78 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31673)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0008","text":"Praeterea, potentiae animae sunt principia operum vitae. Sed quatuor modis dicitur aliquid vivere. Dicit enim philosophus, in II de anima, *multipliciter ipso vivere dicto, etsi unum aliquod horum insit solum, aliquid dicimus vivere; ut intellectus, et sensus, motus et status secundum locum, adhuc autem motus secundum alimentum, et decrementum et augmentum*. Ergo tantum quatuor sunt genera potentiarum animae, appetitivo excluso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0009","text":"[I q. 78 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31674)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0010","text":"Praeterea, ad illud quod est commune omnibus potentiis, non debet deputari aliquod speciale animae genus. Sed appetere convenit cuilibet potentiae animae. Visus enim appetit visibile conveniens, unde dicitur Eccli. XL, *gratiam et speciem desiderabit oculus, et super hoc virides sationes*. Et eadem ratione, quaelibet alia potentia desiderat obiectum sibi conveniens. Ergo non debet poni appetitivum unum speciale genus potentiarum animae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0011","text":"[I q. 78 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31675)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0012","text":"Praeterea, principium movens in animalibus est sensus, aut intellectus, aut appetitus, ut dicitur in III de anima. Non ergo motivum debet poni speciale genus animae praeter praedicta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0013","text":"[I q. 78 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31676)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0014","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in II de anima, *potentias autem dicimus vegetativum sensitivum, appetitivum, motivum secundum locum, et intellectivum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0015","text":"[I q. 78 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31677)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quinque sunt genera potentiarum animae, quae numerata sunt. Tres vero dicuntur animae. Quatuor vero dicuntur modi vivendi. Et huius diversitatis ratio est, quia diversae animae distinguuntur secundum quod diversimode operatio animae supergreditur operationem naturae corporalis, tota enim natura corporalis subiacet animae, et comparatur ad ipsam sicut materia et instrumentum. Est ergo quaedam operatio animae, quae intantum excedit naturam corpoream, quod neque etiam exercetur per organum corporale. Et talis est operatio animae rationalis. Est autem alia operatio animae infra istam, quae quidem fit per organum corporale, non tamen per aliquam corpoream qualitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0017","text":"Et talis est operatio animae sensibilis, quia etsi calidum et frigidum, et humidum et siccum, et aliae huiusmodi qualitates corporeae requirantur ad operationem sensus; non tamen ita quod mediante virtute talium qualitatum operatio animae sensibilis procedat; sed requiruntur solum ad debitam dispositionem organi. Infima autem operationum animae est, quae fit per organum corporeum, et virtute corporeae qualitatis. Supergreditur tamen operationem naturae corporeae, quia motiones corporum sunt ab exteriori principio, huiusmodi autem operationes sunt a principio intrinseco; hoc enim commune est omnibus operationibus animae; omne enim animatum aliquo modo movet seipsum. Et talis est operatio animae vegetabilis, digestio enim, et ea quae consequuntur, fit instrumentaliter per actionem caloris, ut dicitur in II de anima. Genera vero potentiarum animae distinguuntur secundum obiecta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0018","text":"Quanto enim potentia est altior, tanto respicit universalius obiectum, ut supra dictum est. Obiectum autem operationis animae in triplici ordine potest considerari. Alicuius enim potentiae animae obiectum est solum corpus animae unitum. Et hoc genus potentiarum animae dicitur vegetativum, non enim vegetativa potentia agit nisi in corpus cui anima unitur. Est autem aliud genus potentiarum animae, quod respicit universalius obiectum, scilicet omne corpus sensibile; et non solum corpus animae unitum. Est autem aliud genus potentiarum animae, quod respicit adhuc universalius obiectum, scilicet non solum corpus sensibile, sed universaliter omne ens. Ex quo patet quod ista duo secunda genera potentiarum animae habent operationem non solum respectu rei coniunctae, sed etiam respectu rei extrinsecae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0019","text":"Cum autem operans oporteat aliquo modo coniungi suo obiecto circa quod operatur, necesse est extrinsecam rem, quae est obiectum operationis animae, secundum duplicem rationem ad animam comparari. Uno modo, secundum quod nata est animae coniungi et in anima esse per suam similitudinem. Et quantum ad hoc, sunt duo genera potentiarum, scilicet sensitivum, respectu obiecti minus communis, quod est corpus sensibile; et intellectivum, respectu obiecti communissimi, quod est ens universale. Alio vero modo, secundum quod ipsa anima inclinatur et tendit in rem exteriorem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0020","text":"Et secundum hanc etiam comparationem, sunt duo genera potentiarum animae, unum quidem, scilicet appetitivum, secundum quod anima comparatur ad rem extrinsecam ut ad finem, qui est primum in intentione; aliud autem motivum secundum locum, prout anima comparatur ad rem exteriorem sicut ad terminum operationis et motus; ad consequendum enim aliquod desideratum et intentum, omne animal movetur. Modi vero vivendi distinguuntur secundum gradus viventium. Quaedam enim viventia sunt, in quibus est tantum vegetativum, sicut in plantis. Quaedam vero, in quibus cum vegetativo est etiam sensitivum, non tamen motivum secundum locum; sicut sunt immobilia animalia, ut conchilia. Quaedam vero sunt, quae supra hoc habent motivum secundum locum; ut perfecta animalia, quae multis indigent ad suam vitam, et ideo indigent motu, ut vitae necessaria procul posita quaerere possint.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0021","text":"Quaedam vero viventia sunt, in quibus cum his est intellectivum, scilicet in hominibus. Appetitivum autem non constituit aliquem gradum viventium, quia in quibuscumque est sensus, est etiam appetitus, ut dicitur in II libro de anima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0022","text":"[I q. 78 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31678)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0023","text":"Et per hoc solvuntur duo prima obiecta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0024","text":"[I q. 78 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31679)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0025","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod appetitus naturalis est inclinatio cuiuslibet rei in aliquid, ex natura sua, unde naturali appetitu quaelibet potentia desiderat sibi conveniens. Sed appetitus animalis consequitur formam apprehensam. Et ad huiusmodi appetitum requiritur specialis animae potentia, et non sufficit sola apprehensio. Res enim appetitur prout est in sua natura, non est autem secundum suam naturam in virtute apprehensiva, sed secundum suam similitudinem. Unde patet quod visus appetit naturaliter visibile solum ad suum actum, scilicet ad videndum, animal autem appetit rem visam per vim appetitivam, non solum ad videndum, sed etiam ad alios usus. Si autem non indigeret anima rebus perceptis a sensu, nisi propter actiones sensuum, scilicet ut eas sentiret; non oporteret appetitivum ponere speciale genus inter potentias animae, quia sufficeret appetitus naturalis potentiarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0026","text":"[I q. 78 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31680)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0027","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, quamvis sensus et appetitus sint principia moventia in animalibus perfectis, non tamen sensus et appetitus, inquantum huiusmodi, sufficiunt ad movendum, nisi superadderetur eis aliqua virtus, nam in immobilibus animalibus est sensus et appetitus, non tamen habent vim motivam. Haec autem vis motiva non solum est in appetitu et sensu ut imperante motum; sed etiam est in ipsis partibus corporis, ut sint habilia ad obediendum appetitui animae moventis. Cuius signum est, quod quando membra removentur a sua dispositione naturali, non obediunt appetitui ad motum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0028","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0029","text":"[I q. 78 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31681)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0030","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod inconvenienter partes vegetativae assignentur, scilicet nutritivum, augmentativum, et generativum. Huiusmodi enim vires dicuntur naturales. Sed potentiae animae sunt supra vires naturales. Ergo huiusmodi vires non debent poni potentiae animae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0031","text":"[I q. 78 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31682)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0032","text":"Praeterea, ad id quod est commune viventibus et non viventibus, non debet aliqua potentia animae deputari. Sed generatio est communis omnibus generabilibus et corruptibilibus, tam viventibus quam non viventibus. Ergo vis generativa non debet poni potentia animae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0033","text":"[I q. 78 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31683)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0034","text":"Praeterea, anima potentior est quam natura corporea. Sed natura corporea eadem virtute activa dat speciem et debitam quantitatem. Ergo multo magis anima. Non est ergo alia potentia animae augmentativa a generativa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0035","text":"[I q. 78 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31684)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0036","text":"Praeterea, unaquaeque res conservatur in esse per id per quod esse habet. Sed potentia generativa est per quam acquiritur esse viventis. Ergo per eandem res viva conservatur. Sed ad conservationem rei viventis ordinatur vis nutritiva, ut dicitur in II de anima, est enim potentia potens salvare suscipiens ipsam. Non debet ergo distingui nutritiva potentia a generativa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0037","text":"[I q. 78 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31685)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0038","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in II de anima, quod opera huius animae sunt generare, et alimento uti, et iterum augmentum facere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0039","text":"[I q. 78 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31686)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0040","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod tres sunt potentiae vegetativae partis. Vegetativum enim, ut dictum est, habet pro obiecto ipsum corpus vivens per animam, ad quod quidem corpus triplex animae operatio est necessaria. Una quidem, per quam esse acquirat, et ad hoc ordinatur potentia generativa. Alia vero, per quam corpus vivum acquirit debitam quantitatem, et ad hoc ordinatur vis augmentativa. Alia vero, per quam corpus viventis salvatur et in esse, et in quantitate debita, et ad hoc ordinatur vis nutritiva. Est tamen quaedam differentia attendenda inter has potentias. Nam nutritiva et augmentativa habent suum effectum in eo in quo sunt, quia ipsum corpus unitum animae augetur et conservatur per vim augmentativam et nutritivam in eadem anima existentem. Sed vis generativa habet effectum suum, non in eodem corpore, sed in alio, quia nihil est generativum sui ipsius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0041","text":"Et ideo vis generativa quodammodo appropinquat ad dignitatem animae sensitivae, quae habet operationem in res exteriores, licet excellentiori modo et universaliori, supremum enim inferioris naturae attingit id quod est infimum superioris, ut patet per Dionysium, in VII cap. de Div. Nom. Et ideo inter istas tres potentias finalior et principalior et perfectior est generativa, ut dicitur in II de anima, est enim rei iam perfectae facere alteram qualis ipsa est. Generativae autem deserviunt et augmentativa et nutritiva, augmentativae vero nutritiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0042","text":"[I q. 78 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31687)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0043","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod huiusmodi vires dicuntur naturales, tum quia habent effectum similem naturae, quae etiam dat esse et quantitatem et conservationem (licet hae vires habeant hoc altiori modo), tum quia hae vires exercent suas actiones instrumentaliter per qualitates activas et passivas, quae sunt naturalium actionum principia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0044","text":"[I q. 78 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31688)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0045","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod generatio in rebus inanimatis est totaliter ab extrinseco. Sed generatio viventium est quodam altiori modo, per aliquid ipsius viventis, quod est semen, in quo est aliquod principium corporis formativum. Et ideo oportet esse aliquam potentiam rei viventis, per quam semen huiusmodi praeparetur, et haec est vis generativa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0046","text":"[I q. 78 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31689)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0047","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, quia generatio viventium est ex aliquo semine, oportet quod in principio animal generetur parvae quantitatis. Et propter hoc necesse est quod habeat potentiam animae, per quam ad debitam quantitatem perducatur. Sed corpus inanimatum generatur ex materia determinata ab agente extrinseco, et ideo simul recipit speciem et quantitatem secundum materiae conditionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0048","text":"[I q. 78 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31690)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0049","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, sicut iam dictum est, operatio vegetativi principii completur mediante calore, cuius est humidum consumere. Et ideo, ad restaurationem humidi deperditi, necesse est habere potentiam nutritivam, per quam alimentum convertatur in substantiam corporis. Quod etiam est necessarium ad actum virtutis augmentativae et generativae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0050","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0051","text":"[I q. 78 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31691)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0052","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod inconvenienter distinguantur quinque sensus exteriores. Sensus enim est cognoscitivus accidentium. Sunt autem multa genera accidentium. Cum ergo potentiae distinguantur per obiecta, videtur quod sensus multiplicentur secundum numerum qui est in generibus accidentium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0053","text":"[I q. 78 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31692)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0054","text":"Praeterea, magnitudo et figura, et alia quae dicuntur sensibilia communia, non sunt sensibilia per accidens, sed contra ea dividuntur in II de anima. Diversitas autem per se obiectorum diversificat potentias. Cum ergo plus differant magnitudo et figura a colore quam sonus; videtur quod multo magis debeat esse alia potentia sensitiva cognoscitiva magnitudinis aut figurae, quam coloris et soni.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0055","text":"[I q. 78 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31693)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0056","text":"Praeterea, unus sensus est unius contrarietatis; sicut visus albi et nigri. Sed tactus est cognoscitivus plurium contrarietatum, scilicet calidi et frigidi, humidi et sicci, et huiusmodi. Ergo non est sensus unus, sed plures. Ergo plures sensus sunt quam quinque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0057","text":"[I q. 78 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31694)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0058","text":"Praeterea, species non dividitur contra genus. Sed gustus est tactus quidam. Ergo non debet poni alter sensus praeter tactum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0059","text":"[I q. 78 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31695)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0060","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod *non est alter sensus praeter quinque*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0061","text":"[I q. 78 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31696)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0062","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod rationem distinctionis et numeri sensuum exteriorum quidam accipere voluerunt ex parte organorum, in quibus aliquod elementorum dominatur, vel aqua, vel aer, vel aliquid huiusmodi. Quidam autem ex parte medii, quod est vel coniunctum, vel extrinsecum; et hoc vel aer, vel aqua, vel aliquid huiusmodi. Quidam autem ex diversa natura sensibilium qualitatum, secundum quod est qualitas simplicis corporis, vel sequens complexionem. Sed nihil istorum conveniens est. Non enim potentiae sunt propter organa, sed organa propter potentias, unde non propter hoc sunt diversae potentiae, quia sunt diversa organa; sed ideo natura instituit diversitatem in organis, ut congruerent diversitati potentiarum. Et similiter diversa media diversis sensibus attribuit, secundum quod erat conveniens ad actus potentiarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0063","text":"Naturas autem sensibilium qualitatum cognoscere non est sensus, sed intellectus. Accipienda est ergo ratio numeri et distinctionis exteriorum sensuum, secundum illud quod proprie et per se ad sensum pertinet. Est autem sensus quaedam potentia passiva, quae nata est immutari ab exteriori sensibili. Exterius ergo immutativum est quod per se a sensu percipitur, et secundum cuius diversitatem sensitivae potentiae distinguuntur. Est autem duplex immutatio, una naturalis, et alia spiritualis. Naturalis quidem, secundum quod forma immutantis recipitur in immutato secundum esse naturale, sicut calor in calefacto. Spiritualis autem, secundum quod forma immutantis recipitur in immutato secundum esse spirituale; ut forma coloris in pupilla, quae non fit per hoc colorata. Ad operationem autem sensus requiritur immutatio spiritualis, per quam intentio formae sensibilis fiat in organo sensus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0064","text":"Alioquin, si sola immutatio naturalis sufficeret ad sentiendum, omnia corpora naturalia sentirent dum alterantur. Sed in quibusdam sensibus invenitur immutatio spiritualis tantum, sicut in visu. In quibusdam autem, cum immutatione spirituali, etiam naturalis; vel ex parte obiecti tantum, vel etiam ex parte organi. Ex parte autem obiecti, invenitur transmutatio naturalis, secundum locum quidem, in sono, qui est obiectum auditus, nam sonus ex percussione causatur et aeris commotione. Secundum alterationem vero, in odore, qui est obiectum olfactus, oportet enim per calidum alterari aliquo modo corpus, ad hoc quod spiret odorem. Ex parte autem organi, est immutatio naturalis in tactu et gustu, nam et manus tangens calida calefit, et lingua humectatur per humiditatem saporum. Organum vero olfactus aut auditus nulla naturali immutatione immutatur in sentiendo, nisi per accidens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0065","text":"Visus autem, quia est absque immutatione naturali et organi et obiecti, est maxime spiritualis, et perfectior inter omnes sensus, et communior. Et post hoc auditus, et deinde olfactus, qui habent immutationem naturalem ex parte obiecti. Motus tamen localis est perfectior et naturaliter prior quam motus alterationis, ut probatur in VIII Physic. Tactus autem et gustus sunt maxime materiales, de quorum distinctione post dicetur. Et inde est quod alii tres sensus non fiunt per medium coniunctum, ne aliqua naturalis transmutatio pertingat ad organum, ut accidit in his duobus sensibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0066","text":"[I q. 78 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31697)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0067","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod non omnia accidentia habent vim immutativam secundum se; sed solae qualitates tertiae speciei, secundum quas contingit alteratio. Et ideo solae huiusmodi qualitates sunt obiecta sensuum, quia, ut dicitur in VII Physic., *secundum eadem alteratur sensus, secundum quae alterantur corpora inanimata*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0068","text":"[I q. 78 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31698)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0069","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod magnitudo et figura et huiusmodi, quae dicuntur communia sensibilia, sunt media inter sensibilia per accidens et sensibilia propria, quae sunt obiecta sensuum. Nam sensibilia propria primo et per se immutant sensum; cum sint qualitates alterantes. Sensibilia vero communia omnia reducuntur ad quantitatem. Et de magnitudine quidem et numero, patet quod sunt species quantitatis. Figura autem est qualitas circa quantitatem; cum consistat ratio figurae in terminatione magnitudinis. Motus autem et quies sentiuntur, secundum quod subiectum uno modo vel pluribus modis se habet secundum magnitudinem subiecti vel localis distantiae, quantum ad motum augmenti et motum localem; vel etiam secundum sensibiles qualitates, ut in motu alterationis, et sic sentire motum et quietem est quodammodo sentire unum et multa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0070","text":"Quantitas autem est proximum subiectum qualitatis alterativae, ut superficies coloris. Et ideo sensibilia communia non movent sensum primo et per se, sed ratione sensibilis qualitatis; ut superficies ratione coloris. Nec tamen sunt sensibilia per accidens, quia huiusmodi sensibilia aliquam diversitatem faciunt in immutatione sensus. Alio enim modo immutatur sensus a magna superficie, et a parva, quia etiam ipsa albedo dicitur magna vel parva, et ideo dividitur secundum proprium subiectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0071","text":"[I q. 78 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31699)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0072","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut philosophus videtur dicere in II de anima, sensus tactus est unus genere, sed dividitur in multos sensus secundum speciem; et propter hoc est diversarum contrarietatum. Qui tamen non separantur ab invicem secundum organum, sed per totum corpus se concomitantur; et ideo eorum distinctio non apparet. Gustus autem, qui est perceptivus dulcis et amari, concomitatur tactum in lingua, non autem per totum corpus; et ideo de facili a tactu distinguitur. Posset tamen dici quod omnes illae contrarietates, et singulae conveniunt in uno genere proximo, et omnes in uno genere communi, quod est obiectum tactus secundum rationem communem. Sed illud genus commune est innominatum; sicut etiam genus proximum calidi et frigidi est innominatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0073","text":"[I q. 78 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31700)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0074","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod sensus gustus, secundum dictum philosophi, est quaedam species tactus quae est in lingua tantum. Non autem distinguitur a tactu in genere, sed a tactu quantum ad illas species quae per totum corpus diffunduntur. Si vero tactus sit unus sensus tantum, propter unam rationem communem obiecti, dicendum erit quod secundum rationem diversam immutationis, distinguitur gustus a tactu. Nam tactus immutatur naturali immutatione, et non solum spirituali, quantum ad organum suum, secundum qualitatem quae ei proprie obiicitur. Gustus autem organum non immutatur de necessitate naturali immutatione secundum qualitatem quae ei proprie obiicitur, ut scilicet lingua fiat dulcis vel amara; sed secundum praeambulam qualitatem, in qua fundatur sapor, scilicet secundum humorem, qui est obiectum tactus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0075","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0076","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31701)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0077","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod interiores sensus inconvenienter distinguantur. Commune enim non dividitur contra proprium. Ergo sensus communis non debet enumerari inter vires interiores sensitivas, praeter sensus exteriores proprios.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0078","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31702)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0079","text":"Praeterea, ad id ad quod sufficit sensus proprius et exterior, non oportet ponere aliquam vim apprehensivam interiorem. Sed ad iudicandum de sensibilibus, sufficiunt sensus proprii et exteriores, unusquisque enim sensus iudicat de proprio obiecto. Similiter etiam videntur sufficere ad hoc quod percipiant suos actus, quia cum actio sensus sit quodammodo medium inter potentiam et obiectum, videtur quod multo magis visus possit suam visionem, tanquam sibi propinquiorem, percipere, quam colorem; et sic de aliis. Non ergo necessarium fuit ad hoc ponere interiorem potentiam, quae dicatur sensus communis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0080","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31703)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0081","text":"Praeterea, secundum philosophum, phantasticum et memorativum sunt passiones primi sensitivi. Sed passio non dividitur contra subiectum. Ergo memoria et phantasia non debent poni aliae potentiae praeter sensum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0082","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31704)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0083","text":"Praeterea, intellectus minus dependet a sensu, quam quaecumque potentia sensitivae partis. Sed intellectus nihil cognoscit nisi accipiendo a sensu, unde dicitur in I posteriorum, quod *quibus deest unus sensus, deficit una scientia*. Ergo multo minus debet poni una potentia sensitivae partis ad percipiendum intentiones quas non percipit sensus, quam vocant aestimativam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0084","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31705)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0085","text":"Praeterea, actus cogitativae, qui est conferre et componere et dividere, et actus reminiscitivae, qui est quodam syllogismo uti ad inquirendum, non minus distant ab actu aestimativae et memorativae, quam actus aestimativae ab actu phantasiae. Debent ergo vel cogitativa et reminiscitiva poni aliae vires praeter aestimativam et memorativam; vel aestimativa et memorativa non debent poni aliae vires praeter phantasiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0086","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31706)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0087","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus, XII super Gen. ad Litt., ponit tria genera visionum, scilicet corporalem, quae fit per sensum; et spiritualem, quae fit per imaginationem sive phantasiam; et intellectualem, quae fit per intellectum. Non est ergo aliqua vis interior quae sit media inter sensum et intellectum, nisi imaginativa tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0088","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31707)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0089","text":"Sed contra est quod Avicenna, in suo libro de anima, ponit quinque potentias sensitivas interiores, scilicet sensum communem, phantasiam, imaginativam, aestimativam, et memorativam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0090","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31708)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0091","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum natura non deficiat in necessariis, oportet esse tot actiones animae sensitivae, quot sufficiant ad vitam animalis perfecti. Et quaecumque harum actionum non possunt reduci in unum principium, requirunt diversas potentias, cum potentia animae nihil aliud sit quam proximum principium operationis animae. Est autem considerandum quod ad vitam animalis perfecti requiritur quod non solum apprehendat rem apud praesentiam sensibilis, sed etiam apud eius absentiam. Alioquin, cum animalis motus et actio sequantur apprehensionem, non moveretur animal ad inquirendum aliquid absens; cuius contrarium apparet maxime in animalibus perfectis, quae moventur motu processivo; moventur enim ad aliquid absens apprehensum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0092","text":"Oportet ergo quod animal per animam sensitivam non solum recipiat species sensibilium, cum praesentialiter immutatur ab eis; sed etiam eas retineat et conservet. Recipere autem et retinere reducuntur in corporalibus ad diversa principia, nam humida bene recipiunt, et male retinent; e contrario autem est de siccis. Unde, cum potentia sensitiva sit actus organi corporalis, oportet esse aliam potentiam quae recipiat species sensibilium, et quae conservet. Rursus considerandum est quod, si animal moveretur solum propter delectabile et contristabile secundum sensum, non esset necessarium ponere in animali nisi apprehensionem formarum quas percipit sensus, in quibus delectatur aut horret.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0093","text":"Sed necessarium est animali ut quaerat aliqua vel fugiat, non solum quia sunt convenientia vel non convenientia ad sentiendum, sed etiam propter aliquas alias commoditates et utilitates, sive nocumenta, sicut ovis videns lupum venientem fugit, non propter indecentiam coloris vel figurae, sed quasi inimicum naturae; et similiter avis colligit paleam, non quia delectet sensum, sed quia est utilis ad nidificandum. Necessarium est ergo animali quod percipiat huiusmodi intentiones, quas non percipit sensus exterior. Et huius perceptionis oportet esse aliquod aliud principium, cum perceptio formarum sensibilium sit ex immutatione sensibilis, non autem perceptio intentionum praedictarum. Sic ergo ad receptionem formarum sensibilium ordinatur sensus proprius et communis, de quorum distinctione post dicetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0094","text":"Ad harum autem formarum retentionem aut conservationem ordinatur phantasia, sive imaginatio, quae idem sunt, est enim phantasia sive imaginatio quasi thesaurus quidam formarum per sensum acceptarum. Ad apprehendendum autem intentiones quae per sensum non accipiuntur, ordinatur vis aestimativa. Ad conservandum autem eas, vis memorativa, quae est thesaurus quidam huiusmodi intentionum. Cuius signum est, quod principium memorandi fit in animalibus ex aliqua huiusmodi intentione, puta quod est nocivum vel conveniens. Et ipsa ratio praeteriti, quam attendit memoria, inter huiusmodi intentiones computatur. Considerandum est autem quod, quantum ad formas sensibiles, non est differentia inter hominem et alia animalia, similiter enim immutantur a sensibilibus exterioribus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0095","text":"Sed quantum ad intentiones praedictas, differentia est, nam alia animalia percipiunt huiusmodi intentiones solum naturali quodam instinctu, homo autem etiam per quandam collationem. Et ideo quae in aliis animalibus dicitur aestimativa naturalis, in homine dicitur cogitativa, quae per collationem quandam huiusmodi intentiones adinvenit. Unde etiam dicitur ratio particularis, cui medici assignant determinatum organum, scilicet mediam partem capitis, est enim collativa intentionum individualium, sicut ratio intellectiva intentionum universalium. Ex parte autem memorativae, non solum habet memoriam, sicut cetera animalia, in subita recordatione praeteritorum; sed etiam reminiscentiam, quasi syllogistice inquirendo praeteritorum memoriam, secundum individuales intentiones.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0096","text":"Avicenna vero ponit quintam potentiam, mediam inter aestimativam et imaginativam, quae componit et dividit formas imaginatas; ut patet cum ex forma imaginata auri et forma imaginata montis componimus unam formam montis aurei, quem nunquam vidimus. Sed ista operatio non apparet in aliis animalibus ab homine, in quo ad hoc sufficit virtus imaginativa. Cui etiam hanc actionem attribuit Averroes, in libro quodam quem fecit de sensu et sensibilibus. Et sic non est necesse ponere nisi quatuor vires interiores sensitivae partis, scilicet sensum communem et imaginationem, aestimativam et memorativam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0097","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31709)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0098","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod sensus interior non dicitur communis per praedicationem, sicut genus; sed sicut communis radix et principium exteriorum sensuum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0099","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31710)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0100","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod sensus proprius iudicat de sensibili proprio, discernendo ipsum ab aliis quae cadunt sub eodem sensu, sicut discernendo album a nigro vel a viridi. Sed discernere album a dulci non potest neque visus neque gustus, quia oportet quod qui inter aliqua discernit, utrumque cognoscat. Unde oportet ad sensum communem pertinere discretionis iudicium, ad quem referantur, sicut ad communem terminum, omnes apprehensiones sensuum; a quo etiam percipiantur intentiones sensuum, sicut cum aliquis videt se videre. Hoc enim non potest fieri per sensum proprium, qui non cognoscit nisi formam sensibilis a quo immutatur; in qua immutatione perficitur visio, et ex qua immutatione sequitur alia immutatio in sensu communi, qui visionem percipit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0101","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31711)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0102","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut una potentia oritur ab anima, alia mediante, ut supra dictum est; ita etiam anima subiicitur alii potentiae, mediante alia. Et secundum hunc modum, phantasticum et memorativum dicuntur passiones primi sensitivi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0103","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31712)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0104","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, licet intellectus operatio oriatur a sensu, tamen in re apprehensa per sensum intellectus multa cognoscit quae sensus percipere non potest. Et similiter aestimativa, licet inferiori modo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0105","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31713)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0106","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod illam eminentiam habet cogitativa et memorativa in homine, non per id quod est proprium sensitivae partis; sed per aliquam affinitatem et propinquitatem ad rationem universalem, secundum quandam refluentiam. Et ideo non sunt aliae vires, sed eaedem, perfectiores quam sint in aliis animalibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0107","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31714)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0108","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod Augustinus spiritualem visionem dicit esse, quae fit per similitudines corporum in absentia corporum. Unde patet quod communis est omnibus interioribus apprehensionibus.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"0007eb9e009333d60348c3c3150c66150d7115da912c362de63d2de8cf9535d8","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T150757413230Z_ae9ecd46","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 78","source_sha256":"0007eb9e009333d60348c3c3150c66150d7115da912c362de63d2de8cf9535d8","sha256":"fed5a7642db0e66a61bdfc9336610c277dc2853c29fa235ee75e95885164b677","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:07:57.413253+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0002","text":"[I q. 78 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31671)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0003","text":"Next, the powers of the soul must be considered in particular. Now it pertains to the theologian's consideration to inquire particularly only into the intellective and appetitive powers, in which the virtues are found. But because the cognition of these powers somehow depends upon the others, our consideration of the powers of the soul in particular will be threefold. For first, those things which are preliminary to the intellect must be considered; second, the intellective powers; third, the appetitive powers. Concerning the first, four questions are asked. First, concerning the genera of the soul's powers. Second, concerning the species of the vegetative part. Third, concerning the external senses. Fourth, concerning the internal senses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0005","text":"[I q. 78 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31672)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that five genera of the soul's powers ought not to be distinguished, namely, the vegetative, the sensitive, the appetitive, the locomotive according to place, and the intellective. For the powers of the soul are called its parts. But only three parts of the soul are commonly assigned by all, namely, the vegetative soul, the sensitive soul, and the rational soul. Therefore, there are only three genera of the soul's powers, and not five.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0007","text":"[I q. 78 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31673)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0008","text":"Further, the powers of the soul are principles of the works of life. But something is said to live in four ways. For the Philosopher says, in *On the Soul* II: *since living itself is spoken of in many ways, we say that a thing lives if even one of these alone is present: namely, intellect, and sense, motion and rest according to place, and further motion according to nourishment, decrease, and growth*. Therefore, there are only four genera of the soul's powers, with the appetitive excluded.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0009","text":"[I q. 78 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31674)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0010","text":"Further, no special genus of the soul should be assigned to that which is common to all powers. But to desire belongs to every power of the soul. For sight desires a fitting visible object; whence it is said in Ecclesiasticus XL: *the eye shall desire grace and beauty, and beyond this, green sown fields*. And for the same reason, every other power desires its fitting object. Therefore, the appetitive ought not to be posited as one special genus of the soul's powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0011","text":"[I q. 78 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31675)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0012","text":"Further, the moving principle in animals is sense, or intellect, or appetite, as is said in *On the Soul* III. Therefore, the locomotive ought not to be posited as a special genus of the soul besides those aforesaid.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0013","text":"[I q. 78 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31676)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0014","text":"On the contrary, the Philosopher says, in *On the Soul* II: *we call the powers the vegetative, the sensitive, the appetitive, the locomotive according to place, and the intellective*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0015","text":"[I q. 78 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31677)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0016","text":"I answer that there are five genera of the soul's powers, which have been enumerated. But there are said to be three souls. And there are said to be four modes of living. The reason for this diversity is that the diverse souls are distinguished according as the operation of the soul surpasses in diverse ways the operation of corporeal nature; for all corporeal nature is subject to the soul and is compared to it as matter and instrument. There is, therefore, an operation of the soul which so far exceeds corporeal nature that it is not even exercised through a corporeal organ. Such is the operation of the rational soul. But there is another operation of the soul beneath this, which indeed takes place through a corporeal organ, yet not through any corporeal quality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0017","text":"Such is the operation of the sensitive soul; for although hot and cold, moist and dry, and other corporeal qualities of this sort are required for the operation of sense, it is nevertheless not such that the operation of the sensitive soul proceeds by means of the power of such qualities; rather, they are required only for the due disposition of the organ. But the lowest of the soul's operations is that which takes place through a corporeal organ and by the power of a corporeal quality. Yet it surpasses the operation of corporeal nature, because the motions of bodies are from an extrinsic principle, whereas operations of this kind are from an intrinsic principle; for this is common to all operations of the soul: everything animated in some way moves itself. Such is the operation of the vegetative soul; for digestion, and the things consequent upon it, take place instrumentally through the action of heat, as is said in *On the Soul* II. But the genera of the soul's powers are distinguished according to their objects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0018","text":"For the higher a power is, the more universal an object it regards, as was said above. Now the object of an operation of the soul can be considered in a threefold order. For the object of some power of the soul is only the body united to the soul. And this genus of the soul's powers is called vegetative, for the vegetative power acts only upon the body to which the soul is united. But there is another genus of the soul's powers which regards a more universal object, namely, every sensible body, and not only the body united to the soul. And there is another genus of the soul's powers which regards an object still more universal, namely, not only a sensible body, but universally every being. From this it is clear that these latter two genera of the soul's powers have operation not only with respect to a conjoined thing, but also with respect to an extrinsic thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0019","text":"But since the agent must in some way be conjoined to its object concerning which it operates, the extrinsic thing which is the object of the soul's operation must be compared to the soul in a twofold respect. In one way, according as it is naturally apt to be conjoined to the soul and to be in the soul through its likeness. In this respect there are two genera of powers: namely, the sensitive, with respect to the less common object, which is the sensible body; and the intellective, with respect to the most common object, which is universal being. But in another way, according as the soul itself is inclined and tends toward the extrinsic thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0020","text":"And according to this relation also, there are two genera of the soul's powers: one, namely, the appetitive, according as the soul is related to the extrinsic thing as to an end, which is first in intention; the other, the locomotive according to place, insofar as the soul is related to the extrinsic thing as to the terminus of operation and motion; for in order to attain something desired and intended, every animal moves. But the modes of living are distinguished according to the grades of living things. For there are some living things in which there is only the vegetative power, as in plants. But there are some in which, together with the vegetative, there is also the sensitive, yet not the locomotive according to place, such as immobile animals, for example shellfish. But there are some which, beyond this, have locomotion according to place, such as perfect animals, which need many things for their life and therefore need motion, so that they may be able to seek the necessities of life situated at a distance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0021","text":"But there are some living things in which, together with these, there is the intellective, namely, in human beings. The appetitive, however, does not constitute any grade of living things, because wherever there is sense, there is also appetite, as is said in the second book of *On the Soul*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0022","text":"[I q. 78 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31678)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0023","text":"And by this the first two objections are resolved.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0024","text":"[I q. 78 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31679)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0025","text":"To the third, it must be said that natural appetite is the inclination of any thing toward something, from its nature; hence by natural appetite every power desires what is suitable to itself. But animal appetite follows upon an apprehended form. And for an appetite of this sort, a special power of the soul is required, and apprehension alone does not suffice. For a thing is desired insofar as it is in its own nature; but it is not in the apprehensive power according to its nature, but according to its likeness. Hence it is clear that sight naturally desires the visible only for its act, namely, for seeing; but the animal desires the thing seen through the appetitive power, not only for seeing, but also for other uses. But if the soul did not need things perceived by sense except for the actions of the senses, namely, in order that it might sense them, it would not be necessary to posit the appetitive as a special genus among the powers of the soul, because the natural appetite of the powers would suffice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0026","text":"[I q. 78 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31680)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0027","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, although sense and appetite are moving principles in perfect animals, nevertheless sense and appetite, insofar as they are such, do not suffice for moving unless some power were added to them; for in immobile animals there are sense and appetite, yet they do not have locomotive power. But this motive power is not only in appetite and sense as commanding motion; it is also in the parts of the body themselves, so that they may be apt to obey the appetite of the soul that moves. A sign of this is that, when the limbs are removed from their natural disposition, they do not obey the appetite with respect to motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0028","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0029","text":"[I q. 78 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31681)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0030","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the parts of the vegetative faculty are assigned unfittingly, namely, the nutritive, augmentative, and generative. For powers of this kind are called natural. But the powers of the soul are above natural powers. Therefore powers of this kind ought not to be posited as powers of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0031","text":"[I q. 78 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31682)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0032","text":"Moreover, no power of the soul ought to be assigned to that which is common to living and non-living things. But generation is common to all generable and corruptible things, both living and non-living. Therefore the generative power ought not to be posited as a power of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0033","text":"[I q. 78 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31683)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0034","text":"Moreover, the soul is more powerful than corporeal nature. But corporeal nature by the same active power gives both species and due quantity. Therefore much more so does the soul. Therefore the augmentative power of the soul is not distinct from the generative.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0035","text":"[I q. 78 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31684)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0036","text":"Moreover, each thing is preserved in being by that through which it has being. But the generative power is that through which the being of a living thing is acquired. Therefore by the same power a living thing is preserved. But the nutritive power is ordered to the preservation of a living thing, as is said in *On the Soul* II; for it is a power capable of preserving that which receives it. Therefore the nutritive power ought not to be distinguished from the generative.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0037","text":"[I q. 78 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31685)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0038","text":"But on the contrary, the Philosopher says, in *On the Soul* II, that the operations of this soul are to generate, to use nourishment, and again to effect growth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0039","text":"[I q. 78 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31686)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0040","text":"I answer that it must be said that there are three powers of the vegetative part. For the vegetative, as was said, has as its object the living body itself through the soul; and for this body a threefold operation of the soul is necessary. One, indeed, through which it acquires being, and to this the generative power is ordered. Another, through which the living body acquires due quantity, and to this the augmentative power is ordered. And another, through which the body of the living thing is preserved both in being and in due quantity, and to this the nutritive power is ordered. Yet a certain difference must be noted among these powers. For the nutritive and augmentative powers have their effect in that in which they are, because the very body united to the soul is increased and preserved through the augmentative and nutritive power existing in the same soul. But the generative power has its effect, not in the same body, but in another, because nothing is generative of itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0041","text":"And therefore the generative power in a certain way approaches the dignity of the sensitive soul, which has an operation upon external things, although in a more excellent and more universal manner; for the highest of a lower nature touches that which is lowest of a higher nature, as is clear through Dionysius, in chapter VII of *On the Divine Names*. And therefore among these three powers the generative is more final, more principal, and more perfect, as is said in *On the Soul* II; for it belongs to a thing already perfect to make another such as itself. But the augmentative and nutritive powers serve the generative, and the nutritive serves the augmentative.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0042","text":"[I q. 78 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31687)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0043","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that powers of this kind are called natural, both because they have an effect similar to nature, which also gives being, quantity, and preservation (although these powers have this in a higher manner), and because these powers exercise their actions instrumentally through active and passive qualities, which are principles of natural actions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0044","text":"[I q. 78 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31688)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0045","text":"To the second, it must be said that generation in inanimate things is wholly from without. But the generation of living things is in a certain higher manner, through something of the living thing itself, namely seed, in which there is some principle formative of the body. And therefore there must be some power of the living thing through which such seed is prepared, and this is the generative power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0046","text":"[I q. 78 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31689)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0047","text":"To the third, it must be said that, because the generation of living things is from some seed, it is necessary that in the beginning an animal be generated of small quantity. And for this reason it is necessary that it have a power of the soul through which it may be brought to due quantity. But an inanimate body is generated from matter determined by an external agent, and therefore it receives species and quantity at the same time, according to the condition of the matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0048","text":"[I q. 78 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31690)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0049","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, as has already been stated, the operation of the vegetative principle is completed by means of heat, whose role it is to consume moisture. And therefore, for the restoration of lost moisture, it is necessary to have a nutritive power, through which nourishment is converted into the substance of the body. This is also necessary for the act of the augmentative and generative powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0050","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0051","text":"[I q. 78 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31691)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0052","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that the five external senses are distinguished in an unsuitable manner. For sense is cognitive of accidents. But there are many genera of accidents. Therefore, since powers are distinguished by their objects, it seems that the senses are multiplied according to the number found in the genera of accidents.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0053","text":"[I q. 78 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31692)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0054","text":"Further, magnitude and figure, and the other things called common sensibles, are not sensibles per accidens, but are contrasted with them in *On the Soul* II. But diversity of objects per se diversifies powers. Therefore, since magnitude and figure differ more from color than sound does, it seems that there ought much more to be another sensitive power cognizant of magnitude or figure than of color and sound.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0055","text":"[I q. 78 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31693)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0056","text":"Further, one sense is of one contrariety, as sight is of white and black. But touch is cognitive of several contrarieties, namely, hot and cold, moist and dry, and the like. Therefore, it is not one sense but several. Therefore, there are more senses than five.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0057","text":"[I q. 78 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31694)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0058","text":"Further, a species is not divided against a genus. But taste is a kind of touch. Therefore, another sense besides touch should not be posited.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0059","text":"[I q. 78 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31695)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0060","text":"On the contrary, the Philosopher says, in *On the Soul* III, that *there is no other sense besides the five*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0061","text":"[I q. 78 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31696)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0062","text":"I answer that it must be said that some have wished to derive the reason for the distinction and number of the external senses from the organs, in which some one of the elements predominates—water, air, or something of this sort. Others, however, from the medium, which is either conjoined or extrinsic, and this is air, water, or something of this sort. Others, however, from the diverse nature of sensible qualities, insofar as it is a quality of a simple body or follows upon a complexion. But none of these is suitable. For powers do not exist for the sake of organs, but organs for the sake of powers; hence powers are not diverse because organs are diverse, but nature instituted diversity in the organs so that they might correspond to the diversity of the powers. And likewise it assigned diverse media to diverse senses, insofar as this was suited to the acts of the powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0063","text":"But to cognize the natures of sensible qualities belongs not to sense but to intellect. Therefore, the reason for the number and distinction of the external senses must be taken from that which belongs properly and per se to sense. Now sense is a certain passive power, naturally apt to be changed by an external sensible. Therefore, the external changer is what is perceived per se by sense, and according to the diversity of which the sensitive powers are distinguished. But there is a twofold change: one natural and the other spiritual. Natural change is that according to which the form of the changer is received in the changed thing according to natural being, as heat in what is heated. Spiritual change, however, is that according to which the form of the changer is received in the changed thing according to spiritual being, as the form of color in the pupil, which does not thereby become colored. But for the operation of sense, spiritual change is required, through which an intention of the sensible form comes to be in the organ of sense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0064","text":"Otherwise, if natural change alone sufficed for sensing, all natural bodies would sense while being altered. But in some senses spiritual change alone is found, as in sight. In others, however, along with spiritual change, natural change is also found, either on the part of the object alone or also on the part of the organ. On the part of the object, natural change is found with respect to place in sound, which is the object of hearing; for sound is caused by percussion and the movement of air. With respect to alteration, however, it is found in odor, which is the object of smell; for a body must in some way be altered by heat in order that it may emit an odor. On the part of the organ, however, natural change occurs in touch and taste; for both a hand touching hot things becomes hot, and the tongue is moistened by the moisture of flavors. But the organ of smell or hearing undergoes no natural change in sensing, except per accidens.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0065","text":"Sight, however, because it is without natural change both of organ and of object, is the most spiritual, more perfect among all the senses, and more universal. After it comes hearing, and then smell, which have natural change on the part of the object. Yet local motion is more perfect and naturally prior to the motion of alteration, as is proved in *Physics* VIII. But touch and taste are the most material, and their distinction will be discussed later. Hence it is that the other three senses do not occur through a conjoined medium, lest some natural change reach the organ, as happens in these two senses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0066","text":"[I q. 78 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31697)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0067","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that not all accidents have the power to change of themselves, but only qualities of the third species, according to which alteration occurs. Therefore, only such qualities are objects of the senses, because, as is said in *Physics* VII, *sense is altered according to the same things according to which inanimate bodies are altered*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0068","text":"[I q. 78 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31698)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0069","text":"To the second, it must be said that magnitude and figure and the like, which are called common sensibles, are intermediate between sensibles per accidens and proper sensibles, which are the objects of the senses. For proper sensibles change sense first and per se, since they are altering qualities. But all common sensibles are reduced to quantity. And concerning magnitude and number, it is clear that they are species of quantity. But figure is a quality concerning quantity, since the notion of figure consists in the termination of magnitude. Motion and rest, however, are sensed insofar as the subject is disposed in one way or in several ways according to the magnitude of the subject or of local distance, with respect to the motion of growth and local motion; or also according to sensible qualities, as in the motion of alteration. Thus, to sense motion and rest is in a certain way to sense one and many.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0070","text":"But quantity is the proximate subject of an altering quality, as surface is of color. And therefore common sensibles do not move sense first and per se, but by reason of sensible quality, as surface by reason of color. Nevertheless, they are not sensibles per accidens, because sensibles of this kind produce some diversity in the change of sense. For sense is changed in one way by a large surface and in another by a small one, because whiteness itself is also called large or small, and therefore is divided according to its proper subject.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0071","text":"[I q. 78 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31699)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0072","text":"To the third, it must be said that, as the Philosopher seems to say in *On the Soul* II, the sense of touch is one in genus, but is divided into many senses according to species; and on this account it is of diverse contrarieties. Yet these are not separated from one another according to organ, but accompany one another throughout the whole body; and therefore their distinction does not appear. But taste, which is perceptive of sweet and bitter, accompanies touch in the tongue, but not throughout the whole body; and therefore it is easily distinguished from touch. It could nevertheless be said that all those contrarieties, and each one of them, agree in one proximate genus, and all in one common genus, which is the object of touch according to its common notion. But that common genus is unnamed, just as the proximate genus of hot and cold is also unnamed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0073","text":"[I q. 78 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31700)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0074","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the sense of taste, according to the saying of the Philosopher, is a certain species of touch which is in the tongue alone. Yet it is not distinguished from touch in genus, but from touch as regards those species which are diffused throughout the whole body. But if touch is only one sense, on account of one common account of the object, it must be said that taste is distinguished from touch according to a different account of alteration. For touch is altered by a natural alteration, and not only by a spiritual one, as regards its organ, according to the quality which is properly presented to it. But the organ of taste is not necessarily altered by a natural alteration according to the quality which is properly presented to it, namely, such that the tongue becomes sweet or bitter; but according to the antecedent quality on which flavor is founded, namely according to moisture, which is the object of touch.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0075","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0076","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31701)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0077","text":"The fourth is approached thus. It seems that the interior senses are distinguished inappropriately. For the common is not divided against the proper. Therefore common sense ought not to be enumerated among the interior sensitive powers, in addition to the proper exterior senses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0078","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31702)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0079","text":"Further, for that for which the proper and exterior sense suffices, there is no need to posit any interior apprehensive power. But for judging concerning sensibles, the proper and exterior senses suffice, for each sense judges concerning its proper object. Likewise, they also seem to suffice for perceiving their own acts, because, since the action of sense is in some manner intermediate between the power and the object, it seems that sight can perceive its own seeing, as being nearer to itself, much more than color; and so with the others. Therefore it was not necessary for this purpose to posit an interior power called common sense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0080","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31703)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0081","text":"Further, according to the Philosopher, the imaginative and the memorative are affections of the primary sensitive faculty. But an affection is not divided against its subject. Therefore memory and phantasy ought not to be posited as powers other than sense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0082","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31704)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0083","text":"Further, the intellect depends less on sense than does any power of the sensitive part. But the intellect knows nothing except by receiving from sense; hence it is said in *Posterior Analytics* I that *those who lack one sense lack one science*. Therefore much less ought a power of the sensitive part to be posited for perceiving intentions which sense does not perceive, which they call the estimative power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0084","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31705)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0085","text":"Further, the act of the cogitative power, which is to compare and to combine and to divide, and the act of the reminiscitive power, which is to employ a kind of syllogism in searching, differ no less from the act of the estimative and memorative powers than the act of the estimative power differs from the act of phantasy. Therefore either the cogitative and reminiscitive powers ought to be posited as powers other than the estimative and memorative powers, or the estimative and memorative powers ought not to be posited as powers other than phantasy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0086","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31706)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0087","text":"Further, Augustine, in XII *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, posits three kinds of visions: namely, corporeal, which takes place through sense; spiritual, which takes place through imagination or phantasy; and intellectual, which takes place through intellect. Therefore there is no interior power intermediate between sense and intellect, except the imaginative alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0088","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31707)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0089","text":"But on the contrary, Avicenna, in his book *On the Soul*, posits five interior sensitive powers: namely, common sense, phantasy, imagination, the estimative power, and the memorative power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0090","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31708)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0091","text":"I answer that it must be said that, since nature does not fail in necessary things, there must be as many actions of the sensitive soul as suffice for the life of a perfect animal. And whatever of these actions cannot be reduced to one principle requires diverse powers, since a power of the soul is nothing other than the proximate principle of an operation of the soul. Now it must be considered that for the life of a perfect animal it is required that it apprehend a thing not only in the presence of the sensible, but also in its absence. Otherwise, since an animal's motion and action follow apprehension, the animal would not be moved to seek something absent; the contrary of which appears especially in perfect animals, which are moved by progressive motion; for they are moved toward something absent that has been apprehended.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0092","text":"Therefore the animal must, through the sensitive soul, not only receive the species of sensibles when it is presently altered by them, but also retain and preserve them. But receiving and retaining are reduced to diverse principles in corporeal things, for moist things receive well and retain poorly; the contrary is the case with dry things. Hence, since the sensitive power is the act of a bodily organ, there must be one power that receives the species of sensibles, and another that preserves them. Again, it must be considered that, if an animal were moved only by what is pleasurable and distressing according to sense, it would not be necessary to posit in the animal anything but apprehension of the forms which sense perceives, in which it takes pleasure or from which it recoils.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0093","text":"But it is necessary for an animal to seek or flee certain things not only because they are suitable or unsuitable to be sensed, but also because of certain other advantages and utilities, or harms; just as a sheep, seeing a wolf approaching, flees not because of the unbecomingness of its color or shape, but as from an enemy of nature; and similarly a bird gathers straw not because it delights sense, but because it is useful for nest-building. Therefore it is necessary that an animal perceive intentions of this kind, which exterior sense does not perceive. And there must be some other principle of this perception, since the perception of sensible forms comes about from an alteration by the sensible, but not the perception of the aforesaid intentions. Thus, then, the proper and common senses are ordered to the reception of sensible forms; concerning their distinction, it will be said later.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0094","text":"But phantasy, or imagination—which are the same—is ordered to the retention or preservation of these forms; for phantasy or imagination is as it were a certain treasury of forms received through sense. But the estimative power is ordered to apprehending intentions which are not received through sense. And the memorative power, which is a certain treasury of intentions of this kind, is ordered to preserving them. A sign of this is that the beginning of remembering in animals arises from some such intention—for example, that something is harmful or suitable. And the very notion of the past, which memory attends to, is counted among intentions of this kind. It must be considered, moreover, that as regards sensible forms, there is no difference between man and other animals, for they are altered similarly by exterior sensibles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0095","text":"But as regards the aforesaid intentions, there is a difference: for other animals perceive intentions of this kind solely by a certain natural instinct, whereas man also does so by a certain comparison. And therefore what in other animals is called the natural estimative power is called in man the cogitative power, which discovers such intentions by a certain comparison. Hence it is also called particular reason; physicians assign to it a determinate organ, namely the middle part of the head. For it compares individual intentions, just as the intellectual reason compares universal intentions. On the side of the memorative power, moreover, man not only has memory, as other animals do, in the sudden recollection of past things, but also reminiscence, as though syllogistically seeking the memory of past things according to individual intentions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0096","text":"But Avicenna posits a fifth power, intermediate between the estimative and the imaginative, which combines and divides imagined forms; as is apparent when, from the imagined form of gold and the imagined form of a mountain, we compose one form of a golden mountain, which we have never seen. But this operation does not appear in animals other than man, in whom the imaginative power suffices for this. Averroes also attributes this action to it, in a certain book which he wrote on sense and sensibles. And thus it is not necessary to posit more than four interior powers of the sensitive part: namely, common sense and imagination, the estimative power, and the memorative power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0097","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31709)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0098","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that the internal sense is not called common by predication, as a genus is; but as the common root and principle of the external senses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0099","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31710)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0100","text":"To the second, it must be said that the proper sense judges concerning its proper sensible, distinguishing it from other things which fall under the same sense, as when it distinguishes white from black or from green. But neither sight nor taste can distinguish white from sweet, because whoever distinguishes between certain things must know both. Hence the judgment of discrimination must belong to the common sense, to which, as to a common terminus, all the apprehensions of the senses are referred; and by which the intentions of the senses are also perceived, as when someone sees that he sees. For this cannot be done by the proper sense, which knows only the form of the sensible by which it is altered; in which alteration vision is brought to completion, and from which alteration there follows another alteration in the common sense, which perceives the vision.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0101","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31711)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0102","text":"To the third, it must be said that, just as one power arises from the soul through the mediation of another, as was said above, so too the soul is subject to one power through the mediation of another. And in this manner the phantastic and memorative powers are called passions of the primary sensitive power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0103","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31712)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0104","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, although the operation of the intellect arises from sense, nevertheless, in the thing apprehended through sense, the intellect knows many things that sense cannot perceive. And similarly does the estimative power, though in a lower manner.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0105","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31713)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0106","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that the cogitative and memorative powers possess that eminence in human beings, not by virtue of what is proper to the sensitive part, but through a certain affinity and proximity to universal reason, according to a certain overflow. Therefore, they are not other powers, but the same powers, more perfect than they are in other animals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0107","text":"[I q. 78 a. 4 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31714)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0108","text":"To the sixth, it must be said that Augustine calls spiritual vision that which occurs through likenesses of bodies in the absence of bodies. Hence it is clear that it is common to all the internal apprehensions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_70:P0108"]}],"source_digest":"fc21503e017a63b7bafc5d89a392d3ada91789205c14cf7850083ee2e2d28ccb","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 78"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 78","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71","title":"First Part · Question 79","order":80,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_71","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 79 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31715)\n\nDeinde quaeritur de potentiis intellectivis. Circa quod quaeruntur tredecim. Primo, utrum intellectus sit potentia animae, vel eius essentia. Secundo, si est potentia, utrum sit potentia passiva. Tertio, si est potentia passiva, utrum sit ponere aliquem intellectum agentem. Quarto, utrum sit aliquid animae. Quinto, utrum intellectus agens sit unus omnium. Sexto, utrum memoria sit in intellectu. Septimo, utrum sit alia potentia ab intellectu. Octavo, utrum ratio sit alia potentia ab intellectu. Nono, utrum ratio superior et inferior sint diversae potentiae. Decimo, utrum intelligentia sit alia potentia praeter intellectum. Undecimo, utrum intellectus speculativus et practicus sint diversae potentiae. Duodecimo, utrum synderesis sit aliqua potentia intellectivae partis. Tertiodecimo, utrum conscientia sit aliqua potentia intellectivae partis.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 79 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31716)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus non sit aliqua potentia animae, sed sit ipsa eius essentia. Intellectus idem enim videtur esse quod mens. Sed mens non est potentia animae sed essentia, dicit enim Augustinus, IX de Trin., *mens et spiritus non relative dicuntur, sed essentiam demonstrant*. Ergo intellectus est ipsa essentia animae.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31717)\n\nPraeterea, diversa genera potentiarum animae non uniuntur in aliqua potentia una, sed in sola essentia animae. Appetitivum autem et intellectivum sunt diversa genera potentiarum animae, ut dicitur in II de anima; conveniunt autem in mente, quia Augustinus, X de Trin., ponit intelligentiam et voluntatem in mente. Ergo mens et intellectus est ipsa essentia animae, et non aliqua eius potentia.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31718)\n\nPraeterea, secundum Gregorium, in homilia ascensionis, homo intelligit cum Angelis. Sed Angeli dicuntur mentes et intellectus. Ergo mens et intellectus hominis non est aliqua potentia animae, sed ipsa anima.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31719)\n\nPraeterea, ex hoc convenit alicui substantiae quod sit intellectiva, quia est immaterialis. Sed anima est immaterialis per suam essentiam. Ergo videtur quod anima per suam essentiam sit intellectiva.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31720)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus ponit intellectivum potentiam animae, ut patet in II de anima.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31721)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere, secundum praemissa, quod intellectus sit aliqua potentia animae, et non ipsa animae essentia. Tunc enim solum immediatum principium operationis est ipsa essentia rei operantis, quando ipsa operatio est eius esse, sicut enim potentia se habet ad operationem ut ad suum actum, ita se habet essentia ad esse. In solo Deo autem idem est intelligere quod suum esse. Unde in solo Deo intellectus est eius essentia, in aliis autem creaturis intellectualibus intellectus est quaedam potentia intelligentis.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31722)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod sensus accipitur aliquando pro potentia, aliquando vero pro ipsa anima sensitiva, denominatur enim anima sensitiva nomine principalioris suae potentiae, quae est sensus. Et similiter anima intellectiva quandoque nominatur nomine intellectus, quasi a principaliori sua virtute; sicut dicitur in I de anima, quod intellectus est substantia quaedam. Et etiam hoc modo Augustinus dicit quod mens est spiritus, vel essentia.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31723)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod appetitivum et intellectivum sunt diversa genera potentiarum animae, secundum diversas rationes obiectorum. Sed appetitivum partim convenit cum intellectivo, et partim cum sensitivo, quantum ad modum operandi per organum corporale, vel sine huiusmodi organo, nam appetitus sequitur apprehensionem. Et secundum hoc Augustinus ponit voluntatem in mente, et philosophus in ratione.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31724)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in Angelis non est alia vis nisi intellectiva, et voluntas, quae ad intellectum consequitur. Et propter hoc Angelus dicitur mens vel intellectus, quia tota virtus sua in hoc consistit. Anima autem habet multas alias vires, sicut sensitivas et nutritivas, et ideo non est simile.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31725)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod ipsa immaterialitas substantiae intelligentis creatae non est eius intellectus; sed ex immaterialitate habet virtutem ad intelligendum. Unde non oportet quod intellectus sit substantia animae, sed eius virtus et potentia.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 79 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31726)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus non sit potentia passiva. Patitur enim unumquodque secundum materiam; sed agit ratione formae. Sed virtus intellectiva consequitur immaterialitatem substantiae intelligentis. Ergo videtur quod intellectus non sit potentia passiva.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31727)\n\nPraeterea, potentia intellectiva est incorruptibilis, ut supra dictum est. Sed intellectus si est passivus, est corruptibilis, ut dicitur in III de anima. Ergo potentia intellectiva non est passiva.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31728)\n\nPraeterea, agens est nobilius patiente, ut dicit Augustinus XII super Gen. ad Litt., et Aristoteles in III de anima. Potentiae autem vegetativae partis omnes sunt activae, quae tamen sunt infimae inter potentias animae. Ergo multo magis potentiae intellectivae, quae sunt supremae, omnes sunt activae.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31729)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod *intelligere est pati quoddam*.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31730)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod pati tripliciter dicitur. Uno modo, propriissime, scilicet quando aliquid removetur ab eo quod convenit sibi secundum naturam, aut secundum propriam inclinationem; sicut cum aqua frigiditatem amittit per calefactionem, et cum homo aegrotat aut tristatur. Secundo modo, minus proprie dicitur aliquis pati ex eo quod aliquid ab ipso abiicitur, sive sit ei conveniens, sive non conveniens. Et secundum hoc dicitur pati non solum qui aegrotat, sed etiam qui sanatur; non solum qui tristatur, sed etiam qui laetatur; vel quocumque modo aliquis alteretur vel moveatur. Tertio modo, dicitur aliquid pati communiter, ex hoc solo quod id quod est in potentia ad aliquid, recipit illud ad quod erat in potentia, absque hoc quod aliquid abiiciatur. Secundum quem modum, omne quod exit de potentia in actum, potest dici pati, etiam cum perficitur. Et sic intelligere nostrum est pati. Quod quidem hac ratione apparet. Intellectus enim, sicut supra dictum est, habet operationem circa ens in universali. Considerari ergo potest utrum intellectus sit in actu vel potentia, ex hoc quod consideratur quomodo intellectus se habeat ad ens universale. Invenitur enim aliquis intellectus qui ad ens universale se habet sicut actus totius entis, et talis est intellectus divinus, qui est Dei essentia, in qua originaliter et virtualiter totum ens praeexistit sicut in prima causa. Et ideo intellectus divinus non est in potentia, sed est actus purus. Nullus autem intellectus creatus potest se habere ut actus respectu totius entis universalis, quia sic oporteret quod esset ens infinitum. Unde omnis intellectus creatus, per hoc ipsum quod est, non est actus omnium intelligibilium, sed comparatur ad ipsa intelligibilia sicut potentia ad actum. Potentia autem dupliciter se habet ad actum. Est enim quaedam potentia quae semper est perfecta per actum; sicut diximus de materia corporum caelestium. Quaedam autem potentia est, quae non semper est in actu, sed de potentia procedit in actum; sicut invenitur in generabilibus et corruptibilibus. Intellectus igitur angelicus semper est in actu suorum intelligibilium, propter propinquitatem ad primum intellectum, qui est actus purus, ut supra dictum est. Intellectus autem humanus, qui est infimus in ordine intellectuum, et maxime remotus a perfectione divini intellectus, est in potentia respectu intelligibilium, et in principio est sicut tabula rasa in qua nihil est scriptum, ut philosophus dicit in III de anima. Quod manifeste apparet ex hoc, quod in principio sumus intelligentes solum in potentia, postmodum autem efficimur intelligentes in actu. Sic igitur patet quod intelligere nostrum est quoddam pati, secundum tertium modum passionis. Et per consequens intellectus est potentia passiva.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31731)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit de primo et secundo modo passionis, qui sunt proprii materiae primae. Tertius autem modus passionis est cuiuscumque in potentia existentis quod in actum reducitur.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31732)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod intellectus passivus secundum quosdam dicitur appetitus sensitivus, in quo sunt animae passiones; qui etiam in I Ethic. dicitur rationalis per participationem, quia obedit rationi. Secundum alios autem intellectus passivus dicitur virtus cogitativa, quae nominatur ratio particularis. Et utroque modo passivum accipi potest secundum primos duos modos passionis, inquantum talis intellectus sic dictus, est actus alicuius organi corporalis. Sed intellectus qui est in potentia ad intelligibilia, quem Aristoteles ob hoc nominat intellectum possibilem, non est passivus nisi tertio modo, quia non est actus organi corporalis. Et ideo est incorruptibilis.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31733)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod agens est nobilius patiente, si ad idem actio et passio referantur, non autem semper, si ad diversa. Intellectus autem est vis passiva respectu totius entis universalis. Vegetativum autem est activum respectu cuiusdam entis particularis, scilicet corporis coniuncti. Unde nihil prohibet huiusmodi passivum esse nobilius tali activo.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 79 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31734)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit ponere intellectum agentem. Sicut enim se habet sensus ad sensibilia, ita se habet intellectus noster ad intelligibilia. Sed quia sensus est in potentia ad sensibilia non ponitur sensus agens, sed sensus patiens tantum. Ergo, cum intellectus noster sit in potentia ad intelligibilia, videtur quod non debeat poni intellectus agens, sed possibilis tantum.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31735)\n\nPraeterea, si dicatur quod in sensu etiam est aliquod agens, sicut lumen, contra, lumen requiritur ad visum inquantum facit medium lucidum in actu, nam color ipse secundum se est motivus lucidi. Sed in operatione intellectus non ponitur aliquod medium quod necesse sit fieri in actu. Ergo non est necessarium ponere intellectum agentem.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31736)\n\nPraeterea, similitudo agentis recipitur in patiente secundum modum patientis. Sed intellectus possibilis est virtus immaterialis. Ergo immaterialitas eius sufficit ad hoc quod recipiantur in eo formae immaterialiter. Sed ex hoc ipso aliqua forma est intelligibilis in actu, quod est immaterialis. Ergo nulla necessitas est ponere intellectum agentem, ad hoc quod faciat species intelligibiles in actu.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31737)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod *sicut in omni natura ita et in anima est aliquid quo est omnia fieri, et aliquid quo est omnia facere*. Est ergo ponere intellectum agentem.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31738)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, secundum opinionem Platonis, nulla necessitas erat ponere intellectum agentem ad faciendum intelligibilia in actu; sed forte ad praebendum lumen intelligibile intelligenti, ut infra dicetur. Posuit enim Plato formas rerum naturalium sine materia subsistere, et per consequens eas intelligibiles esse, quia ex hoc est aliquid intelligibile actu, quod est immateriale. Et huiusmodi vocabat species, sive ideas, ex quarum participatione dicebat etiam materiam corporalem formari, ad hoc quod individua naturaliter constituerentur in propriis generibus et speciebus; et intellectus nostros, ad hoc quod de generibus et speciebus rerum scientiam haberent. Sed quia Aristoteles non posuit formas rerum naturalium subsistere sine materia; formae autem in materia existentes non sunt intelligibiles actu, sequebatur quod naturae seu formae rerum sensibilium, quas intelligimus, non essent intelligibiles actu. Nihil autem reducitur de potentia in actum, nisi per aliquod ens actu, sicut sensus fit in actu per sensibile in actu. Oportebat igitur ponere aliquam virtutem ex parte intellectus, quae faceret intelligibilia in actu, per abstractionem specierum a conditionibus materialibus. Et haec est necessitas ponendi intellectum agentem.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31739)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod sensibilia inveniuntur actu extra animam, et ideo non oportuit ponere sensum agentem. Et sic patet quod in parte nutritiva omnes potentiae sunt activae; in parte autem sensitiva, omnes passivae; in parte vero intellectiva est aliquid activum, et aliquid passivum.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31740)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod circa effectum luminis est duplex opinio. Quidam enim dicunt quod lumen requiritur ad visum, ut faciat colores actu visibiles. Et secundum hoc, similiter requiritur, et propter idem, intellectus agens ad intelligendum, propter quod lumen ad videndum. Secundum alios vero, lumen requiritur ad videndum, non propter colores, ut fiant actu visibiles; sed ut medium fiat actu lucidum, ut Commentator dicit in II de anima. Et secundum hoc, similitudo qua Aristoteles assimilat intellectum agentem lumini, attenditur quantum ad hoc, quod sicut hoc est necessarium ad videndum, ita illud ad intelligendum; sed non propter idem.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31741)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, supposito agente, bene contingit diversimode recipi eius similitudinem in diversis propter eorum dispositionem diversam. Sed si agens non praeexistit, nihil ad hoc faciet dispositio recipientis. Intelligibile autem in actu non est aliquid existens in rerum natura, quantum ad naturam rerum sensibilium, quae non subsistunt praeter materiam. Et ideo ad intelligendum non sufficeret immaterialitas intellectus possibilis, nisi adesset intellectus agens, qui faceret intelligibilia in actu per modum abstractionis.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 79 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31742)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus agens non sit aliquid animae nostrae. Intellectus enim agentis effectus est illuminare ad intelligendum. Sed hoc fit per aliquid quod est altius anima; secundum illud Ioan. I, *erat lux vera, quae illuminat omnem hominem venientem in hunc mundum*. Ergo videtur quod intellectus agens non sit aliquid animae.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31743)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus, in III de anima, attribuit intellectui agenti quod non aliquando intelligit et aliquando non intelligit. Sed anima nostra non semper intelligit; sed aliquando intelligit et aliquando non intelligit. Ergo intellectus agens non est aliquid animae nostrae.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31744)\n\nPraeterea, agens et patiens sufficiunt ad agendum. Si igitur intellectus possibilis est aliquid animae nostrae, qui est virtus passiva, et similiter intellectus agens, qui est virtus activa; sequitur quod homo semper poterit intelligere cum voluerit, quod patet esse falsum. Non est ergo intellectus agens aliquid animae nostrae.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31745)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod intellectus agens est substantia actu ens. Nihil autem est respectu eiusdem in actu et in potentia. Si ergo intellectus possibilis, qui est in potentia ad omnia intelligibilia, est aliquid animae nostrae; videtur impossibile quod intellectus agens sit aliquid animae nostrae.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31746)\n\nPraeterea, si intellectus agens est aliquid animae nostrae, oportet quod sit aliqua potentia. Non est enim nec passio nec habitus, nam habitus et passiones non habent rationem agentis respectu passionum animae; sed magis passio est ipsa actio potentiae passivae, habitus autem est aliquid quod ex actibus consequitur. Omnis autem potentia fluit ab essentia animae. Sequeretur ergo quod intellectus agens ab essentia animae procederet. Et sic non inesset animae per participationem ab aliquo superiori intellectu, quod est inconveniens. Non ergo intellectus agens est aliquid animae nostrae.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31747)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, III de anima quod *necesse est in anima has esse differentias*, scilicet intellectum possibilem, et agentem.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31748)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod intellectus agens de quo philosophus loquitur, est aliquid animae. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod supra animam intellectivam humanam necesse est ponere aliquem superiorem intellectum, a quo anima virtutem intelligendi obtineat. Semper enim quod participat aliquid, et quod est mobile, et quod est imperfectum, praeexigit ante se aliquid quod est per essentiam suam tale, et quod est immobile et perfectum. Anima autem humana intellectiva dicitur per participationem intellectualis virtutis, cuius signum est, quod non tota est intellectiva, sed secundum aliquam sui partem. Pertingit etiam ad intelligentiam veritatis cum quodam discursu et motu, arguendo. Habet etiam imperfectam intelligentiam, tum quia non omnia intelligit; tum quia in his quae intelligit, de potentia procedit ad actum. Oportet ergo esse aliquem altiorem intellectum, quo anima iuvetur ad intelligendum. Posuerunt ergo quidam hunc intellectum secundum substantiam separatum, esse intellectum agentem, qui quasi illustrando phantasmata, facit ea intelligibilia actu. Sed, dato quod sit aliquis talis intellectus agens separatus, nihilominus tamen oportet ponere in ipsa anima humana aliquam virtutem ab illo intellectu superiori participatam, per quam anima humana facit intelligibilia in actu. Sicut et in aliis rebus naturalibus perfectis, praeter universales causas agentes, sunt propriae virtutes inditae singulis rebus perfectis, ab universalibus agentibus derivatae, non enim solus sol generat hominem, sed est in homine virtus generativa hominis; et similiter in aliis animalibus perfectis. Nihil autem est perfectius in inferioribus rebus anima humana. Unde oportet dicere quod in ipsa sit aliqua virtus derivata a superiori intellectu, per quam possit phantasmata illustrare. Et hoc experimento cognoscimus, dum percipimus nos abstrahere formas universales a conditionibus particularibus, quod est facere actu intelligibilia. Nulla autem actio convenit alicui rei, nisi per aliquod principium formaliter ei inhaerens; ut supra dictum est, cum de intellectu possibili ageretur. Ergo oportet virtutem quae est principium huius actionis, esse aliquid in anima. Et ideo Aristoteles comparavit intellectum agentem lumini, quod est aliquid receptum in aere. Plato autem intellectum separatum imprimentem in animas nostras, comparavit soli; ut Themistius dicit in commentario tertii de anima. Sed intellectus separatus, secundum nostrae fidei documenta, est ipse Deus, qui est creator animae, et in quo solo beatificatur, ut infra patebit. Unde ab ipso anima humana lumen intellectuale participat, secundum illud Psalmi IV, *signatum est super nos lumen vultus tui, domine*.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31749)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod illa lux vera illuminat sicut causa universalis, a qua anima humana participat quandam particularem virtutem, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31750)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod philosophus illa verba non dicit de intellectu agente, sed de intellectu in actu. Unde supra de ipso praemiserat, idem autem est secundum actum scientia rei. Vel, si intelligatur de intellectu agente, hoc dicitur quia non est ex parte intellectus agentis hoc quod quandoque intelligimus et quandoque non intelligimus; sed ex parte intellectus qui est in potentia.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31751)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, si intellectus agens compararetur ad intellectum possibilem ut obiectum agens ad potentiam, sicut visibile in actu ad visum; sequeretur quod statim omnia intelligeremus, cum intellectus agens sit quo est omnia facere. Nunc autem non se habet ut obiectum, sed ut faciens obiecta in actu, ad quod requiritur, praeter praesentiam intellectus agentis, praesentia phantasmatum, et bona dispositio virium sensitivarum, et exercitium in huiusmodi opere; quia per unum intellectum fiunt etiam alia intellecta, sicut per terminos propositiones, et per prima principia conclusiones. Et quantum ad hoc, non differt utrum intellectus agens sit aliquid animae, vel aliquid separatum.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31752)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod anima intellectiva est quidem actu immaterialis, sed est in potentia ad determinatas species rerum. Phantasmata autem, e converso, sunt quidem actu similitudines specierum quarundam, sed sunt potentia immaterialia. Unde nihil prohibet unam et eandem animam, inquantum est immaterialis in actu, habere aliquam virtutem per quam faciat immaterialia in actu abstrahendo a conditionibus individualis materiae, quae quidem virtus dicitur intellectus agens; et aliam virtutem receptivam huiusmodi specierum, quae dicitur intellectus possibilis, inquantum est in potentia ad huiusmodi species.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31753)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod, cum essentia animae sit immaterialis, a supremo intellectu creata, nihil prohibet virtutem quae a supremo intellectu participatur, per quam abstrahit a materia, ab essentia ipsius procedere, sicut et alias eius potentias.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 79 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31754)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus agens sit unus in omnibus. Nihil enim quod est separatum a corpore, multiplicatur secundum multiplicationem corporum. Sed intellectus agens est separatus, ut dicitur in III de anima. Ergo non multiplicatur in multis corporibus hominum, sed est unus in omnibus.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31755)\n\nPraeterea, intellectus agens facit universale, quod est unum in multis. Sed illud quod est causa unitatis, magis est unum. Ergo intellectus agens est unus in omnibus.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31756)\n\nPraeterea, omnes homines conveniunt in primis conceptionibus intellectus. His autem assentiunt per intellectum agentem. Ergo conveniunt omnes in uno intellectu agente.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31757)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod intellectus agens est sicut lumen. Non autem est idem lumen in diversis illuminatis. Ergo non est idem intellectus agens in diversis hominibus.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31758)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod veritas huius quaestionis dependet ex praemissis. Si enim intellectus agens non esset aliquid animae, sed esset quaedam substantia separata, unus esset intellectus agens omnium hominum. Et hoc intelligunt qui ponunt unitatem intellectus agentis. Si autem intellectus agens sit aliquid animae, ut quaedam virtus ipsius, necesse est dicere quod sint plures intellectus agentes, secundum pluralitatem animarum, quae multiplicantur secundum multiplicationem hominum, ut supra dictum est. Non enim potest esse quod una et eadem virtus numero sit diversarum substantiarum.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31759)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod philosophus probat intellectum agentem esse separatum, per hoc quod possibilis est separatus; quia, ut ipse dicit, agens est honorabilius patiente. Intellectus autem possibilis dicitur separatus, quia non est actus alicuius organi corporalis. Et secundum hunc modum etiam intellectus agens dicitur separatus, non quasi sit aliqua substantia separata.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31760)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod intellectus agens causat universale abstrahendo a materia. Ad hoc autem non requiritur quod sit unus in omnibus habentibus intellectum, sed quod sit unus in omnibus secundum habitudinem ad omnia a quibus abstrahit universale, respectu quorum universale est unum. Et hoc competit intellectui agenti inquantum est immaterialis.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31761)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod omnia quae sunt unius speciei, communicant in actione consequente naturam speciei, et per consequens in virtute, quae est actionis principium, non quod sit eadem numero in omnibus. Cognoscere autem prima intelligibilia est actio consequens speciem humanam. Unde oportet quod omnes homines communicent in virtute quae est principium huius actionis, et haec est virtus intellectus agentis. Non tamen oportet quod sit eadem numero in omnibus. Oportet tamen quod ab uno principio in omnibus derivetur. Et sic illa communicatio hominum in primis intelligibilibus, demonstrat unitatem intellectus separati, quem Plato comparat soli; non autem unitatem intellectus agentis, quem Aristoteles comparat lumini.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 79 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31762)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod memoria non sit in parte intellectiva animae. Dicit enim Augustinus, XII de Trin., quod ad partem superiorem animae pertinent quae non sunt hominibus pecoribusque communia. Sed memoria est hominibus pecoribusque communis, dicit enim ibidem quod *possunt pecora sentire per corporis sensus corporalia, et ea mandare memoriae*. Ergo memoria non pertinet ad partem animae intellectivam.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31763)\n\nPraeterea, memoria praeteritorum est. Sed praeteritum dicitur secundum aliquod determinatum tempus. Memoria igitur est cognoscitiva alicuius sub determinato tempore; quod est cognoscere aliquid sub hic et nunc. Hoc autem non est intellectus, sed sensus. Memoria igitur non est in parte intellectiva, sed solum in parte sensitiva.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31764)\n\nPraeterea, in memoria conservantur species rerum quae actu non cogitantur. Sed hoc non est possibile accidere in intellectu, quia intellectus fit in actu per hoc quod informatur specie intelligibili; intellectum autem esse in actu, est ipsum intelligere in actu; et sic intellectus omnia intelligit in actu, quorum species apud se habet. Non ergo memoria est in parte intellectiva.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31765)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, X de Trin., quod *memoria, intelligentia et voluntas sunt una mens*.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31766)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum de ratione memoriae sit conservare species rerum quae actu non apprehenduntur, hoc primum considerari oportet, utrum species intelligibiles sic in intellectu conservari possint. Posuit enim Avicenna hoc esse impossibile. In parte enim sensitiva dicebat hoc accidere, quantum ad aliquas potentias, inquantum sunt actus organorum corporalium, in quibus conservari possunt aliquae species absque actuali apprehensione. In intellectu autem, qui caret organo corporali, nihil existit nisi intelligibiliter. Unde oportet intelligi in actu illud cuius similitudo in intellectu existit. Sic ergo, secundum ipsum, quam cito aliquis actu desinit intelligere aliquam rem, desinit esse illius rei species in intellectu, sed oportet, si denuo vult illam rem intelligere, quod convertat se ad intellectum agentem, quem ponit substantiam separatam, ut ab illo effluant species intelligibiles in intellectum possibilem. Et ex exercitio et usu convertendi se ad intellectum agentem, relinquitur, secundum ipsum, quaedam habilitas in intellectu possibili convertendi se ad intellectum agentem, quam dicebat esse habitum scientiae. Secundum igitur hanc positionem, nihil conservatur in parte intellectiva, quod non actu intelligatur. Unde non poterit poni memoria in parte intellectiva, secundum hunc modum. Sed haec opinio manifeste repugnat dictis Aristotelis. Dicit enim, in III de anima, quod, cum intellectus possibilis *sic fiat singula ut sciens, dicitur qui secundum actum; et quod hoc accidit cum possit operari per seipsum. Est quidem igitur et tunc potentia quodammodo; non tamen similiter ut ante addiscere aut invenire*. Dicitur autem intellectus possibilis fieri singula, secundum quod recipit species singulorum. Ex hoc ergo quod recipit species intelligibilium, habet quod possit operari cum voluerit, non autem quod semper operetur, quia et tunc est quodammodo in potentia, licet aliter quam ante intelligere; eo scilicet modo quo sciens in habitu est in potentia ad considerandum in actu. Repugnat etiam praedicta positio rationi. Quod enim recipitur in aliquo, recipitur in eo secundum modum recipientis. Intellectus autem est magis stabilis naturae et immobilis, quam materia corporalis. Si ergo materia corporalis formas quas recipit, non solum tenet dum per eas agit in actu, sed etiam postquam agere per eas cessaverit; multo fortius intellectus immobiliter et inamissibiliter recipit species intelligibiles, sive a sensibilibus acceptas, sive etiam ab aliquo superiori intellectu effluxas. Sic igitur, si memoria accipiatur solum pro vi conservativa specierum, oportet dicere memoriam esse in intellectiva parte. Si vero de ratione memoriae sit quod eius obiectum sit praeteritum, ut praeteritum; memoria in parte intellectiva non erit, sed sensitiva tantum, quae est apprehensiva particularium. Praeteritum enim, ut praeteritum, cum significet esse sub determinato tempore, ad conditionem particularis pertinet.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31767)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod memoria, secundum quod est conservativa specierum, non est nobis pecoribusque communis. Species enim conservantur non in parte animae sensitiva tantum, sed magis in coniuncto; cum vis memorativa sit actus organi cuiusdam. Sed intellectus secundum seipsum est conservativus specierum, praeter concomitantiam organi corporalis. Unde philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod *anima est locus specierum, non tota, sed intellectus*.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31768)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod praeteritio potest ad duo referri, scilicet ad obiectum quod cognoscitur; et ad cognitionis actum. Quae quidem duo simul coniunguntur in parte sensitiva, quae est apprehensiva alicuius per hoc quod immutatur a praesenti sensibili, unde simul animal memoratur se prius sensisse in praeterito, et se sensisse quoddam praeteritum sensibile. Sed quantum ad partem intellectivam pertinet, praeteritio accidit, et non per se convenit, ex parte obiecti intellectus. Intelligit enim intellectus hominem, inquantum est homo, homini autem, inquantum est homo, accidit vel in praesenti vel in praeterito vel in futuro esse. Ex parte vero actus, praeteritio per se accipi potest etiam in intellectu, sicut in sensu. Quia intelligere animae nostrae est quidam particularis actus, in hoc vel in illo tempore existens, secundum quod dicitur homo intelligere nunc vel heri vel cras. Et hoc non repugnat intellectualitati, quia huiusmodi intelligere, quamvis sit quoddam particulare, tamen est immaterialis actus, ut supra de intellectu dictum est; et ideo sicut intelligit seipsum intellectus, quamvis ipse sit quidam singularis intellectus, ita intelligit suum intelligere, quod est singularis actus vel in praeterito vel in praesenti vel in futuro existens. Sic igitur salvatur ratio memoriae, quantum ad hoc quod est praeteritorum, in intellectu, secundum quod intelligit se prius intellexisse, non autem secundum quod intelligit praeteritum, prout est hic et nunc.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31769)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod species intelligibilis aliquando est in intellectu in potentia tantum, et tunc dicitur intellectus esse in potentia. Aliquando autem secundum ultimam completionem actus, et tunc intelligit actu. Aliquando medio modo se habet inter potentiam et actum, et tunc dicitur esse intellectus in habitu. Et secundum hunc modum intellectus conservat species, etiam quando actu non intelligit.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 79 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31770)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod alia potentia sit memoria intellectiva, et alia intellectus. Augustinus enim, in X de Trin., ponit in mente memoriam, intelligentiam et voluntatem. Manifestum est autem quod memoria est alia potentia a voluntate. Ergo similiter est alia ab intellectu.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31771)\n\nPraeterea, eadem ratio distinctionis est potentiarum sensitivae partis et intellectivae. Sed memoria in parte sensitiva est alia potentia a sensu, ut supra dictum est. Ergo memoria partis intellectivae est alia potentia ab intellectu.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31772)\n\nPraeterea, secundum Augustinum, memoria, intelligentia et voluntas sunt sibi invicem aequalia, et unum eorum ab alio oritur. Hoc autem esse non posset, si memoria esset eadem potentia cum intellectu. Non est ergo eadem potentia.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31773)\n\nSed contra, de ratione memoriae est, quod sit thesaurus vel locus conservativus specierum. Hoc autem philosophus, in III de anima, attribuit intellectui, ut dictum est. Non ergo in parte intellectiva est alia potentia memoria ab intellectu.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31774)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, potentiae animae distinguuntur secundum diversas rationes obiectorum; eo quod ratio cuiuslibet potentiae consistit in ordine ad id ad quod dicitur, quod est eius obiectum. Dictum est etiam supra quod, si aliqua potentia secundum propriam rationem ordinetur ad aliquod obiectum secundum communem rationem obiecti, non diversificabitur illa potentia secundum diversitates particularium differentiarum, sicut potentia visiva, quae respicit suum obiectum secundum rationem colorati, non diversificatur per diversitatem albi et nigri. Intellectus autem respicit suum obiectum secundum communem rationem entis; eo quod intellectus possibilis est quo est omnia fieri. Unde secundum nullam differentiam entium, diversificatur differentia intellectus possibilis. Diversificatur tamen potentia intellectus agentis, et intellectus possibilis, quia respectu eiusdem obiecti, aliud principium oportet esse potentiam activam, quae facit obiectum esse in actu; et aliud potentiam passivam, quae movetur ab obiecto in actu existente. Et sic potentia activa comparatur ad suum obiectum, ut ens in actu ad ens in potentia, potentia autem passiva comparatur ad suum obiectum e converso, ut ens in potentia ad ens in actu. Sic igitur nulla alia differentia potentiarum in intellectu esse potest, nisi possibilis et agentis. Unde patet quod memoria non est alia potentia ab intellectu, ad rationem enim potentiae passivae pertinet conservare, sicut et recipere.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31775)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, quamvis in III dist. I Sent. dicatur quod memoria, intelligentia et voluntas sint tres vires; tamen hoc non est secundum intentionem Augustini, qui expresse dicit in XIV de Trin., quod *si accipiatur memoria, intelligentia et voluntas, secundum quod semper praesto sunt animae, sive cogitentur sive non cogitentur, ad solam memoriam pertinere videntur. Intelligentiam autem nunc dico qua intelligimus cogitantes; et eam voluntatem, sive amorem vel dilectionem, quae istam prolem parentemque coniungit*. Ex quo patet quod ista tria non accipit Augustinus pro tribus potentiis; sed memoriam accipit pro habituali animae retentione, intelligentiam autem pro actu intellectus, voluntatem autem pro actu voluntatis.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31776)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod praeteritum et praesens possunt esse propriae differentiae potentiarum sensitivarum diversificativae; non autem potentiarum intellectivarum, ratione supra dicta.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31777)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod intelligentia oritur ex memoria, sicut actus ex habitu. Et hoc modo etiam aequatur ei; non autem sicut potentia potentiae.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 79 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31778)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ratio sit alia potentia ab intellectu. In libro enim de spiritu et anima dicitur, *cum ab inferioribus ad superiora ascendere volumus, prius occurrit nobis sensus, deinde imaginatio, deinde ratio, deinde intellectus*. Est ergo alia potentia ratio ab intellectu, sicut imaginatio a ratione.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31779)\n\nPraeterea, Boetius dicit, in libro de Consol., quod intellectus comparatur ad rationem sicut aeternitas ad tempus. Sed non est eiusdem virtutis esse in aeternitate et esse in tempore. Ergo non est eadem potentia ratio et intellectus.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31780)\n\nPraeterea, homo communicat cum Angelis in intellectu, cum brutis vero in sensu. Sed ratio, quae est propria hominis, qua animal rationale dicitur, est alia potentia a sensu. Ergo pari ratione est alia potentia ab intellectu, qui proprie convenit Angelis, unde et intellectuales dicuntur.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31781)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, III super Gen. ad Litt., quod *illud quo homo irrationabilibus animalibus antecellit, est ratio, vel mens, vel intelligentia, vel si quo alio vocabulo commodius appellatur*. Ratio ergo et intellectus et mens sunt una potentia.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31782)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ratio et intellectus in homine non possunt esse diversae potentiae. Quod manifeste cognoscitur, si utriusque actus consideretur. Intelligere enim est simpliciter veritatem intelligibilem apprehendere. Ratiocinari autem est procedere de uno intellecto ad aliud, ad veritatem intelligibilem cognoscendam. Et ideo Angeli, qui perfecte possident, secundum modum suae naturae, cognitionem intelligibilis veritatis, non habent necesse procedere de uno ad aliud; sed simpliciter et absque discursu veritatem rerum apprehendunt, ut Dionysius dicit, VII cap. de Div. Nom. Homines autem ad intelligibilem veritatem cognoscendam perveniunt, procedendo de uno ad aliud, ut ibidem dicitur, et ideo rationales dicuntur. Patet ergo quod ratiocinari comparatur ad intelligere sicut moveri ad quiescere, vel acquirere ad habere, quorum unum est perfecti, aliud autem imperfecti. Et quia motus semper ab immobili procedit, et ad aliquid quietum terminatur; inde est quod ratiocinatio humana, secundum viam inquisitionis vel inventionis, procedit a quibusdam simpliciter intellectis, quae sunt prima principia; et rursus, in via iudicii, resolvendo redit ad prima principia, ad quae inventa examinat. Manifestum est autem quod quiescere et moveri non reducuntur ad diversas potentias, sed ad unam et eandem, etiam in naturalibus rebus, quia per eandem naturam aliquid movetur ad locum, et quiescit in loco. Multo ergo magis per eandem potentiam intelligimus et ratiocinamur. Et sic patet quod in homine eadem potentia est ratio et intellectus.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31783)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod illa enumeratio fit secundum ordinem actuum, non secundum distinctionem potentiarum. Quamvis liber ille non sit magnae auctoritatis.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31784)\n\nAd secundum patet responsio ex dictis. Aeternitas enim comparatur ad tempus, sicut immobile ad mobile. Et ideo Boetius comparavit intellectum aeternitati, rationem vero tempori.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31785)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod alia animalia sunt ita infra hominem, quod non possunt attingere ad cognoscendam veritatem, quam ratio inquirit. Homo vero attingit ad cognoscendam intelligibilem veritatem, quam Angeli cognoscunt; sed imperfecte. Et ideo vis cognoscitiva Angelorum non est alterius generis a vi cognoscitiva rationis, sed comparatur ad ipsam ut perfectum ad imperfectum.\n\n## Articulus 9\n\n[I q. 79 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31786)\n\nAd nonum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ratio superior et inferior sint diversae potentiae. Dicit enim Augustinus, XII de Trin., quod imago Trinitatis est in superiori parte rationis, non autem in inferiori. Sed partes animae sunt ipsae eius potentiae. Ergo duae potentiae sunt ratio superior et inferior.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31787)\n\nPraeterea, nihil oritur a seipso. Sed ratio inferior oritur a superiori, et ab ea regulatur et dirigitur. Ergo ratio superior est alia potentia ab inferiori.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31788)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus dicit, in VI Ethic., quod scientificum animae quo cognoscit anima necessaria, est aliud principium et alia pars animae ab opinativo et ratiocinativo, quo cognoscit contingentia. Et hoc probat per hoc, quia *ad ea quae sunt genere altera, altera genere particula animae* ordinatur; contingens autem et necessarium sunt altera genere, sicut corruptibile et incorruptibile. Cum autem idem sit necessarium quod aeternum, et temporale idem quod contingens; videtur quod idem sit quod philosophus vocat scientificum, et superior pars rationis, quae secundum Augustinum intendit aeternis conspiciendis et consulendis; et quod idem sit quod philosophus vocat ratiocinativum vel opinativum, et inferior ratio, quae secundum Augustinum intendit temporalibus disponendis. Est ergo alia potentia animae ratio superior, et ratio inferior.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 9 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31789)\n\nPraeterea, Damascenus dicit quod *ex imaginatione fit opinio; deinde mens, diiudicans opinionem sive vera sit sive falsa, diiudicat veritatem; unde et mens dicitur a metiendo. De quibus igitur iudicatum est iam et determinatum vere, dicitur intellectus*. Sic igitur opinativum, quod est ratio inferior, est aliud a mente et intellectu, per quod potest intelligi ratio superior.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31790)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XII de Trin., quod ratio superior et inferior non nisi per officia distinguuntur. Non ergo sunt duae potentiae.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31791)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ratio superior et inferior, secundum quod ab Augustino accipiuntur, nullo modo duae potentiae animae esse possunt. Dicit enim quod ratio superior est quae intendit aeternis conspiciendis aut consulendis, conspiciendis quidem, secundum quod ea in seipsis speculatur; consulendis vero, secundum quod ex eis accipit regulas agendorum. Ratio vero inferior ab ipso dicitur, quae intendit temporalibus rebus. Haec autem duo, scilicet temporalia et aeterna, comparantur ad cognitionem nostram hoc modo, quod unum eorum est medium ad cognoscendum alterum. Nam secundum viam inventionis, per res temporales in cognitionem devenimus aeternorum, secundum illud apostoli, ad Rom. I, *invisibilia Dei per ea quae facta sunt, intellecta, conspiciuntur*, in via vero iudicii, per aeterna iam cognita de temporalibus iudicamus, et secundum rationes aeternorum temporalia disponimus. Potest autem contingere quod medium, et id ad quod per medium pervenitur, ad diversos habitus pertineant, sicut principia prima indemonstrabilia pertinent ad habitum intellectus, conclusiones vero ex his deductae ad habitum scientiae. Et ideo ex principiis geometriae contingit aliquid concludere in alia scientia, puta in perspectiva. Sed eadem potentia rationis est, ad quam pertinet et medium et ultimum. Est enim actus rationis quasi quidam motus de uno in aliud perveniens, idem autem est mobile quod pertransiens medium pertingit ad terminum. Unde una et eadem potentia rationis est ratio superior et inferior. Sed distinguuntur, secundum Augustinum, per officia actuum, et secundum diversos habitus, nam superiori rationi attribuitur sapientia, inferiori vero scientia.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31792)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod secundum quamcumque rationem partitionis potest pars dici. Inquantum ergo ratio dividitur secundum diversa officia, ratio superior et inferior partitiones dicuntur, et non quia sunt diversae potentiae.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31793)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ratio inferior dicitur a superiori deduci, vel ab ea regulari, inquantum principia quibus utitur inferior ratio, deducuntur et diriguntur a principiis superioris rationis.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31794)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod scientificum de quo philosophus loquitur non est idem quod ratio superior, nam necessaria scibilia inveniuntur etiam in rebus temporalibus, de quibus est scientia naturalis et mathematica. Opinativum autem et ratiocinativum in minus est quam ratio inferior, quia est contingentium tantum. Nec tamen est simpliciter dicendum quod sit alia potentia qua intellectus cognoscit necessaria, et alia qua cognoscit contingentia, quia utraque cognoscit secundum eandem rationem obiecti, scilicet secundum rationem entis et veri. Unde et necessaria, quae habent perfectum esse in veritate, perfecte cognoscit; utpote ad eorum quidditatem pertingens, per quam propria accidentia de his demonstrat. Contingentia vero imperfecte cognoscit; sicut et habent imperfectum esse et veritatem. Perfectum autem et imperfectum in actu non diversificant potentiam; sed diversificant actus quantum ad modum agendi, et per consequens principia actuum et ipsos habitus. Et ideo philosophus posuit duas particulas animae, scientificum et ratiocinativum, non quia sunt duae potentiae; sed quia distinguuntur secundum diversam aptitudinem ad recipiendum diversos habitus, quorum diversitatem ibi inquirere intendit. Contingentia enim et necessaria, etsi differant secundum propria genera, conveniunt tamen in communi ratione entis, quam respicit intellectus, ad quam diversimode se habent secundum perfectum et imperfectum.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 9 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31795)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod illa distinctio Damasceni est secundum diversitatem actuum, non secundum diversitatem potentiarum. Opinio enim significat actum intellectus qui fertur in unam partem contradictionis cum formidine alterius. Diiudicare vero, vel mensurare, est actus intellectus applicantis principia certa ad examinationem propositorum. Et ex hoc sumitur nomen mentis. Intelligere autem est cum quadam approbatione diiudicatis inhaerere.\n\n## Articulus 10\n\n[I q. 79 a. 10 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31796)\n\nAd decimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intelligentia sit alia potentia ab intellectu. Dicitur enim in libro de spiritu et anima, quod *cum ab inferioribus ad superiora ascendere volumus, prius occurrit nobis sensus, deinde imaginatio, deinde ratio, postea intellectus, et postea intelligentia*. Sed imaginatio et sensus sunt diversae potentiae. Ergo et intellectus et intelligentia.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 10 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31797)\n\nPraeterea, Boetius dicit, in V de Consol., quod *ipsum hominem aliter sensus, aliter imaginatio, aliter ratio, aliter intelligentia intuetur*. Sed intellectus est eadem potentia cum ratione. Ergo videtur quod intelligentia sit alia potentia quam intellectus; sicut ratio est alia potentia quam imaginatio et sensus.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 10 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31798)\n\nPraeterea, actus sunt praevii potentiis, ut dicitur in II de anima. Sed intelligentia est quidam actus ab aliis actibus qui attribuuntur intellectui divisus. Dicit enim Damascenus quod *primus motus intelligentia dicitur; quae vero circa aliquid est intelligentia, intentio vocatur; quae permanens et figurans animam ad id quod intelligitur, excogitatio dicitur; excogitatio vero in eodem manens, et seipsam examinans et diiudicans, phronesis dicitur* (idest sapientia); *phronesis autem dilatata facit cogitationem, idest interius dispositum sermonem; ex quo aiunt provenire sermonem per linguam enarratum*. Ergo videtur quod intelligentia sit quaedam specialis potentia.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 10 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31799)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod *intelligentia indivisibilium est, in quibus non est falsum*. Sed huiusmodi cognoscere pertinet ad intellectum. Ergo intelligentia non est alia potentia praeter intellectum.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 10 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31800)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod hoc nomen intelligentia proprie significat ipsum actum intellectus qui est intelligere. In quibusdam tamen libris de Arabico translatis, substantiae separatae quas nos Angelos dicimus, intelligentiae vocantur; forte propter hoc, quod huiusmodi substantiae semper actu intelligunt. In libris tamen de Graeco translatis, dicuntur intellectus seu mentes. Sic ergo intelligentia ab intellectu non distinguitur sicut potentia a potentia; sed sicut actus a potentia. Invenitur enim talis divisio etiam a philosophis. Quandoque enim ponunt quatuor intellectus, scilicet intellectum agentem, possibilem, et in habitu, et adeptum. Quorum quatuor intellectus agens et possibilis sunt diversae potentiae; sicut et in omnibus est alia potentia activa, et alia passiva. Alia vero tria distinguuntur secundum tres status intellectus possibilis, qui quandoque est in potentia tantum, et sic dicitur possibilis; quandoque autem in actu primo, qui est scientia, et sic dicitur intellectus in habitu; quandoque autem in actu secundo, qui est considerare, et sic dicitur intellectus in actu, sive intellectus adeptus.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 10 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31801)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, si recipi debet illa auctoritas, intelligentia ponitur pro actu intellectus. Et sic dividitur contra intellectum, sicut actus contra potentiam.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 10 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31802)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Boetius accipit intelligentiam pro actu intellectus qui transcendit actum rationis. Unde ibidem dicit quod *ratio tantum humani generis est, sicut intelligentia sola divini*, proprium enim Dei est quod absque omni investigatione omnia intelligat.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 10 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31803)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod omnes illi actus quos Damascenus enumerat, sunt unius potentiae, scilicet intellectivae. Quae primo quidem simpliciter aliquid apprehendit, et hic actus dicitur intelligentia. Secundo vero, id quod apprehendit, ordinat ad aliquid aliud cognoscendum vel operandum, et hic vocatur intentio. Dum vero persistit in inquisitione illius quod intendit, vocatur excogitatio. Dum vero id quod est excogitatum examinat ad aliqua certa, dicitur scire vel sapere; quod est phronesis, vel sapientiae, nam sapientiae est iudicare, ut dicitur in I Metaphys. Ex quo autem habet aliquid pro certo, quasi examinatum, cogitat quomodo possit illud aliis manifestare, et haec est dispositio interioris sermonis; ex qua procedit exterior locutio. Non enim omnis differentia actuum potentias diversificat; sed solum illa quae non potest reduci in idem principium, ut supra dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 11\n\n[I q. 79 a. 11 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31804)\n\nAd undecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus speculativus et practicus sint diversae potentiae. Apprehensivum enim et motivum sunt diversa genera potentiarum, ut patet in II de anima. Sed intellectus speculativus est apprehensivus tantum, intellectus autem practicus est motivus. Ergo sunt diversae potentiae.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 11 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31805)\n\nPraeterea, diversa ratio obiecti diversificat potentiam. Sed obiectum speculativi intellectus est verum, practici autem bonum; quae differunt ratione. Ergo intellectus speculativus et practicus sunt diversae potentiae.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 11 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31806)\n\nPraeterea, in parte intellectiva intellectus practicus comparatur ad speculativum, sicut aestimativa ad imaginativam in parte sensitiva. Sed aestimativa differt ab imaginativa sicut potentia a potentia, ut supra dictum est. Ergo et intellectus practicus a speculativo.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 11 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31807)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in III de anima, quod intellectus speculativus per extensionem fit practicus. Una autem potentia non mutatur in aliam. Ergo intellectus speculativus et practicus non sunt diversae potentiae.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 11 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31808)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod intellectus practicus et speculativus non sunt diversae potentiae. Cuius ratio est quia, ut supra dictum est, id quod accidentaliter se habet ad obiecti rationem quam respicit aliqua potentia, non diversificat potentiam, accidit enim colorato quod sit homo, aut magnum aut parvum; unde omnia huiusmodi eadem visiva potentia apprehenduntur. Accidit autem alicui apprehenso per intellectum, quod ordinetur ad opus, vel non ordinetur. Secundum hoc autem differunt intellectus speculativus et practicus. Nam intellectus speculativus est, qui quod apprehendit, non ordinat ad opus, sed ad solam veritatis considerationem, practicus vero intellectus dicitur, qui hoc quod apprehendit, ordinat ad opus. Et hoc est quod philosophus dicit in III de anima, quod *speculativus differt a practico, fine*. Unde et a fine denominatur uterque, hic quidem speculativus, ille vero practicus, idest operativus.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 11 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31809)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod intellectus practicus est motivus, non quasi exequens motum, sed quasi dirigens ad motum. Quod convenit ei secundum modum suae apprehensionis.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 11 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31810)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod verum et bonum se invicem includunt, nam verum est quoddam bonum, alioquin non esset appetibile; et bonum est quoddam verum, alioquin non esset intelligibile. Sicut igitur obiectum appetitus potest esse verum, inquantum habet rationem boni, sicut cum aliquis appetit veritatem cognoscere; ita obiectum intellectus practici est bonum ordinabile ad opus, sub ratione veri. Intellectus enim practicus veritatem cognoscit, sicut et speculativus; sed veritatem cognitam ordinat ad opus.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 11 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31811)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod multae differentiae diversificant sensitivas potentias, quae non diversificant potentias intellectivas, ut supra dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 12\n\n[I q. 79 a. 12 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31812)\n\nAd duodecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod synderesis sit quaedam specialis potentia ab aliis distincta. Ea enim quae cadunt sub una divisione, videntur esse unius generis. Sed in Glossa Hieronymi Ezech. I, dividitur synderesis contra irascibilem et concupiscibilem et rationalem; quae sunt quaedam potentiae. Ergo synderesis est quaedam potentia.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 12 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31813)\n\nPraeterea, opposita sunt unius generis. Sed synderesis et sensualitas opponi videntur, quia synderesis semper inclinat ad bonum, sensualitas autem semper ad malum; unde per serpentem significatur, ut patet per Augustinum, XII de Trin. Videtur ergo quod synderesis sit potentia, sicut et sensualitas.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 12 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31814)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, in libro de libero arbitrio, quod in naturali iudicatorio adsunt quaedam *regulae et semina virtutum et vera et incommutabilia*, haec autem dicimus synderesim. Cum ergo regulae incommutabiles quibus iudicamus, pertineant ad rationem secundum sui superiorem partem, ut Augustinus dicit XII de Trin.; videtur quod synderesis sit idem quod ratio. Et ita est quaedam potentia.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 12 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31815)\n\n*Sed contra, potentiae rationales se habent ad opposita*, secundum philosophum. Synderesis autem non se habet ad opposita, sed ad bonum tantum inclinat. Ergo synderesis non est potentia. Si enim esset potentia, oporteret quod esset rationalis potentia, non enim invenitur in brutis.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 12 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31816)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod synderesis non est potentia, sed habitus, licet quidam posuerint synderesim esse quandam potentiam ratione altiorem; quidam vero dixerint eam esse ipsam rationem, non ut est ratio, sed ut est natura. Ad huius autem evidentiam, considerandum est quod, sicut supra dictum est, ratiocinatio hominis, cum sit quidam motus, ab intellectu progreditur aliquorum, scilicet naturaliter notorum absque investigatione rationis, sicut a quodam principio immobili, et ad intellectum etiam terminatur, inquantum iudicamus per principia per se naturaliter nota, de his quae ratiocinando invenimus. Constat autem quod, sicut ratio speculativa ratiocinatur de speculativis, ita ratio practica ratiocinatur de operabilibus. Oportet igitur naturaliter nobis esse indita, sicut principia speculabilium, ita et principia operabilium. Prima autem principia speculabilium nobis naturaliter indita, non pertinent ad aliquam specialem potentiam; sed ad quendam specialem habitum, qui dicitur intellectus principiorum, ut patet in VI Ethic. Unde et principia operabilium nobis naturaliter indita, non pertinent ad specialem potentiam; sed ad specialem habitum naturalem, quem dicimus synderesim. Unde et synderesis dicitur instigare ad bonum, et murmurare de malo, inquantum per prima principia procedimus ad inveniendum, et iudicamus inventa. Patet ergo quod synderesis non est potentia, sed habitus naturalis.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 12 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31817)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod illa divisio Hieronymi attenditur secundum diversitatem actuum, non secundum diversitatem potentiarum. Diversi autem actus possunt esse unius potentiae.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 12 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31818)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod similiter oppositio sensualitatis et synderesis attenditur secundum oppositionem actuum; non sicut diversarum specierum unius generis.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 12 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31819)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod huiusmodi incommutabiles rationes sunt prima principia operabilium, circa quae non contingit errare; et attribuuntur rationi sicut potentiae, et synderesi sicut habitui. Unde et utroque, scilicet ratione et synderesi, naturaliter iudicamus.\n\n## Articulus 13\n\n[I q. 79 a. 13 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31820)\n\nAd tertiumdecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod conscientia sit quaedam potentia. Dicit enim Origenes quod *conscientia est spiritus corrector et paedagogus animae sociatus, quo separatur a malis et adhaeret bonis*. Sed spiritus in anima nominat potentiam aliquam, vel ipsam mentem, secundum illud Ephes. IV, *renovamini spiritu mentis vestrae*; vel ipsam imaginationem; unde et imaginaria visio spiritualis vocatur, ut patet per Augustinum, XII super Gen. ad Litt. Est ergo conscientia quaedam potentia.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 13 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31821)\n\nPraeterea, nihil est peccati subiectum nisi potentia animae. Sed conscientia est subiectum peccati, dicitur enim ad Tit. I, de quibusdam, quod *inquinatae sunt eorum mens et conscientia*. Ergo videtur quod conscientia sit potentia.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 13 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31822)\n\nPraeterea, necesse est quod conscientia sit vel actus, vel habitus, vel potentia. Sed non est actus, quia non semper maneret in homine. Nec est habitus, non enim esset unum quid conscientia, sed multa; per multos enim habitus cognoscitivos dirigimur in agendis. Ergo conscientia est potentia.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 13 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31823)\n\nSed contra, conscientia deponi potest, non autem potentia. Ergo conscientia non est potentia.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 13 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31824)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod conscientia, proprie loquendo, non est potentia, sed actus. Et hoc patet tum ex ratione nominis, tum etiam ex his quae secundum communem usum loquendi, conscientiae attribuuntur. Conscientia enim, secundum proprietatem vocabuli, importat ordinem scientiae ad aliquid, nam conscientia dicitur cum alio scientia. Applicatio autem scientiae ad aliquid fit per aliquem actum. Unde ex ista ratione nominis patet quod conscientia sit actus. Idem autem apparet ex his quae conscientiae attribuuntur. Dicitur enim conscientia testificari, ligare vel instigare, et etiam accusare vel remordere sive reprehendere. Et haec omnia consequuntur applicationem alicuius nostrae cognitionis vel scientiae ad ea quae agimus. Quae quidem applicatio fit tripliciter. Uno modo, secundum quod recognoscimus aliquid nos fecisse vel non fecisse, secundum illud Eccle. VII, *scit conscientia tua te crebro maledixisse aliis*, et secundum hoc, conscientia dicitur testificari. Alio modo applicatur secundum quod per nostram conscientiam iudicamus aliquid esse faciendum vel non faciendum, et secundum hoc, dicitur conscientia instigare vel ligare. Tertio modo applicatur secundum quod per conscientiam iudicamus quod aliquid quod est factum, sit bene factum vel non bene factum, et secundum hoc, conscientia dicitur excusare vel accusare, seu remordere. Patet autem quod omnia haec consequuntur actualem applicationem scientiae ad ea quae agimus. Unde proprie loquendo, conscientia nominat actum. Quia tamen habitus est principium actus, quandoque nomen conscientiae attribuitur primo habitui naturali, scilicet synderesi, sicut Hieronymus, in Glossa Ezech. I, synderesim conscientiam nominat; et Basilius naturale iudicatorium; et Damascenus dicit quod est lex intellectus nostri. Consuetum enim est quod causae et effectus per invicem nominentur.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 13 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31825)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod conscientia dicitur spiritus, secundum quod spiritus pro mente ponitur, quia est quoddam mentis dictamen.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 13 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31826)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod inquinatio dicitur esse in conscientia, non sicut in subiecto, sed sicut cognitum in cognitione, inquantum scilicet aliquis scit se esse inquinatum.\n\n[I q. 79 a. 13 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31827)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod actus, etsi non semper maneat in se, semper tamen manet in sua causa, quae est potentia et habitus. Habitus autem ex quibus conscientia informatur, etsi multi sint, omnes tamen efficaciam habent ab uno primo, scilicet ab habitu primorum principiorum, qui dicitur synderesis. Unde specialiter hic habitus interdum conscientia nominatur, ut supra dictum est.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0002","text":"[I q. 79 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31715)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0003","text":"Deinde quaeritur de potentiis intellectivis. Circa quod quaeruntur tredecim. Primo, utrum intellectus sit potentia animae, vel eius essentia. Secundo, si est potentia, utrum sit potentia passiva. Tertio, si est potentia passiva, utrum sit ponere aliquem intellectum agentem. Quarto, utrum sit aliquid animae. Quinto, utrum intellectus agens sit unus omnium. Sexto, utrum memoria sit in intellectu. Septimo, utrum sit alia potentia ab intellectu. Octavo, utrum ratio sit alia potentia ab intellectu. Nono, utrum ratio superior et inferior sint diversae potentiae. Decimo, utrum intelligentia sit alia potentia praeter intellectum. Undecimo, utrum intellectus speculativus et practicus sint diversae potentiae. Duodecimo, utrum synderesis sit aliqua potentia intellectivae partis. Tertiodecimo, utrum conscientia sit aliqua potentia intellectivae partis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0005","text":"[I q. 79 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31716)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus non sit aliqua potentia animae, sed sit ipsa eius essentia. Intellectus idem enim videtur esse quod mens. Sed mens non est potentia animae sed essentia, dicit enim Augustinus, IX de Trin., *mens et spiritus non relative dicuntur, sed essentiam demonstrant*. Ergo intellectus est ipsa essentia animae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0007","text":"[I q. 79 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31717)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0008","text":"Praeterea, diversa genera potentiarum animae non uniuntur in aliqua potentia una, sed in sola essentia animae. Appetitivum autem et intellectivum sunt diversa genera potentiarum animae, ut dicitur in II de anima; conveniunt autem in mente, quia Augustinus, X de Trin., ponit intelligentiam et voluntatem in mente. Ergo mens et intellectus est ipsa essentia animae, et non aliqua eius potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0009","text":"[I q. 79 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31718)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0010","text":"Praeterea, secundum Gregorium, in homilia ascensionis, homo intelligit cum Angelis. Sed Angeli dicuntur mentes et intellectus. Ergo mens et intellectus hominis non est aliqua potentia animae, sed ipsa anima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0011","text":"[I q. 79 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31719)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0012","text":"Praeterea, ex hoc convenit alicui substantiae quod sit intellectiva, quia est immaterialis. Sed anima est immaterialis per suam essentiam. Ergo videtur quod anima per suam essentiam sit intellectiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0013","text":"[I q. 79 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31720)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0014","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus ponit intellectivum potentiam animae, ut patet in II de anima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0015","text":"[I q. 79 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31721)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere, secundum praemissa, quod intellectus sit aliqua potentia animae, et non ipsa animae essentia. Tunc enim solum immediatum principium operationis est ipsa essentia rei operantis, quando ipsa operatio est eius esse, sicut enim potentia se habet ad operationem ut ad suum actum, ita se habet essentia ad esse. In solo Deo autem idem est intelligere quod suum esse. Unde in solo Deo intellectus est eius essentia, in aliis autem creaturis intellectualibus intellectus est quaedam potentia intelligentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0017","text":"[I q. 79 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31722)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0018","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod sensus accipitur aliquando pro potentia, aliquando vero pro ipsa anima sensitiva, denominatur enim anima sensitiva nomine principalioris suae potentiae, quae est sensus. Et similiter anima intellectiva quandoque nominatur nomine intellectus, quasi a principaliori sua virtute; sicut dicitur in I de anima, quod intellectus est substantia quaedam. Et etiam hoc modo Augustinus dicit quod mens est spiritus, vel essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0019","text":"[I q. 79 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31723)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod appetitivum et intellectivum sunt diversa genera potentiarum animae, secundum diversas rationes obiectorum. Sed appetitivum partim convenit cum intellectivo, et partim cum sensitivo, quantum ad modum operandi per organum corporale, vel sine huiusmodi organo, nam appetitus sequitur apprehensionem. Et secundum hoc Augustinus ponit voluntatem in mente, et philosophus in ratione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0021","text":"[I q. 79 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31724)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in Angelis non est alia vis nisi intellectiva, et voluntas, quae ad intellectum consequitur. Et propter hoc Angelus dicitur mens vel intellectus, quia tota virtus sua in hoc consistit. Anima autem habet multas alias vires, sicut sensitivas et nutritivas, et ideo non est simile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0023","text":"[I q. 79 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31725)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0024","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod ipsa immaterialitas substantiae intelligentis creatae non est eius intellectus; sed ex immaterialitate habet virtutem ad intelligendum. Unde non oportet quod intellectus sit substantia animae, sed eius virtus et potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0025","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0026","text":"[I q. 79 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31726)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0027","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus non sit potentia passiva. Patitur enim unumquodque secundum materiam; sed agit ratione formae. Sed virtus intellectiva consequitur immaterialitatem substantiae intelligentis. Ergo videtur quod intellectus non sit potentia passiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0028","text":"[I q. 79 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31727)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0029","text":"Praeterea, potentia intellectiva est incorruptibilis, ut supra dictum est. Sed intellectus si est passivus, est corruptibilis, ut dicitur in III de anima. Ergo potentia intellectiva non est passiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0030","text":"[I q. 79 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31728)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0031","text":"Praeterea, agens est nobilius patiente, ut dicit Augustinus XII super Gen. ad Litt., et Aristoteles in III de anima. Potentiae autem vegetativae partis omnes sunt activae, quae tamen sunt infimae inter potentias animae. Ergo multo magis potentiae intellectivae, quae sunt supremae, omnes sunt activae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0032","text":"[I q. 79 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31729)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0033","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod *intelligere est pati quoddam*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0034","text":"[I q. 79 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31730)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0035","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod pati tripliciter dicitur. Uno modo, propriissime, scilicet quando aliquid removetur ab eo quod convenit sibi secundum naturam, aut secundum propriam inclinationem; sicut cum aqua frigiditatem amittit per calefactionem, et cum homo aegrotat aut tristatur. Secundo modo, minus proprie dicitur aliquis pati ex eo quod aliquid ab ipso abiicitur, sive sit ei conveniens, sive non conveniens. Et secundum hoc dicitur pati non solum qui aegrotat, sed etiam qui sanatur; non solum qui tristatur, sed etiam qui laetatur; vel quocumque modo aliquis alteretur vel moveatur. Tertio modo, dicitur aliquid pati communiter, ex hoc solo quod id quod est in potentia ad aliquid, recipit illud ad quod erat in potentia, absque hoc quod aliquid abiiciatur. Secundum quem modum, omne quod exit de potentia in actum, potest dici pati, etiam cum perficitur. Et sic intelligere nostrum est pati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0036","text":"Quod quidem hac ratione apparet. Intellectus enim, sicut supra dictum est, habet operationem circa ens in universali. Considerari ergo potest utrum intellectus sit in actu vel potentia, ex hoc quod consideratur quomodo intellectus se habeat ad ens universale. Invenitur enim aliquis intellectus qui ad ens universale se habet sicut actus totius entis, et talis est intellectus divinus, qui est Dei essentia, in qua originaliter et virtualiter totum ens praeexistit sicut in prima causa. Et ideo intellectus divinus non est in potentia, sed est actus purus. Nullus autem intellectus creatus potest se habere ut actus respectu totius entis universalis, quia sic oporteret quod esset ens infinitum. Unde omnis intellectus creatus, per hoc ipsum quod est, non est actus omnium intelligibilium, sed comparatur ad ipsa intelligibilia sicut potentia ad actum. Potentia autem dupliciter se habet ad actum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0037","text":"Est enim quaedam potentia quae semper est perfecta per actum; sicut diximus de materia corporum caelestium. Quaedam autem potentia est, quae non semper est in actu, sed de potentia procedit in actum; sicut invenitur in generabilibus et corruptibilibus. Intellectus igitur angelicus semper est in actu suorum intelligibilium, propter propinquitatem ad primum intellectum, qui est actus purus, ut supra dictum est. Intellectus autem humanus, qui est infimus in ordine intellectuum, et maxime remotus a perfectione divini intellectus, est in potentia respectu intelligibilium, et in principio est sicut tabula rasa in qua nihil est scriptum, ut philosophus dicit in III de anima. Quod manifeste apparet ex hoc, quod in principio sumus intelligentes solum in potentia, postmodum autem efficimur intelligentes in actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0038","text":"Sic igitur patet quod intelligere nostrum est quoddam pati, secundum tertium modum passionis. Et per consequens intellectus est potentia passiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0039","text":"[I q. 79 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31731)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0040","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod obiectio illa procedit de primo et secundo modo passionis, qui sunt proprii materiae primae. Tertius autem modus passionis est cuiuscumque in potentia existentis quod in actum reducitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0041","text":"[I q. 79 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31732)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0042","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod intellectus passivus secundum quosdam dicitur appetitus sensitivus, in quo sunt animae passiones; qui etiam in I Ethic. dicitur rationalis per participationem, quia obedit rationi. Secundum alios autem intellectus passivus dicitur virtus cogitativa, quae nominatur ratio particularis. Et utroque modo passivum accipi potest secundum primos duos modos passionis, inquantum talis intellectus sic dictus, est actus alicuius organi corporalis. Sed intellectus qui est in potentia ad intelligibilia, quem Aristoteles ob hoc nominat intellectum possibilem, non est passivus nisi tertio modo, quia non est actus organi corporalis. Et ideo est incorruptibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0043","text":"[I q. 79 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31733)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0044","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod agens est nobilius patiente, si ad idem actio et passio referantur, non autem semper, si ad diversa. Intellectus autem est vis passiva respectu totius entis universalis. Vegetativum autem est activum respectu cuiusdam entis particularis, scilicet corporis coniuncti. Unde nihil prohibet huiusmodi passivum esse nobilius tali activo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0045","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0046","text":"[I q. 79 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31734)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0047","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod non sit ponere intellectum agentem. Sicut enim se habet sensus ad sensibilia, ita se habet intellectus noster ad intelligibilia. Sed quia sensus est in potentia ad sensibilia non ponitur sensus agens, sed sensus patiens tantum. Ergo, cum intellectus noster sit in potentia ad intelligibilia, videtur quod non debeat poni intellectus agens, sed possibilis tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0048","text":"[I q. 79 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31735)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0049","text":"Praeterea, si dicatur quod in sensu etiam est aliquod agens, sicut lumen, contra, lumen requiritur ad visum inquantum facit medium lucidum in actu, nam color ipse secundum se est motivus lucidi. Sed in operatione intellectus non ponitur aliquod medium quod necesse sit fieri in actu. Ergo non est necessarium ponere intellectum agentem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0050","text":"[I q. 79 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31736)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0051","text":"Praeterea, similitudo agentis recipitur in patiente secundum modum patientis. Sed intellectus possibilis est virtus immaterialis. Ergo immaterialitas eius sufficit ad hoc quod recipiantur in eo formae immaterialiter. Sed ex hoc ipso aliqua forma est intelligibilis in actu, quod est immaterialis. Ergo nulla necessitas est ponere intellectum agentem, ad hoc quod faciat species intelligibiles in actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0052","text":"[I q. 79 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31737)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0053","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod *sicut in omni natura ita et in anima est aliquid quo est omnia fieri, et aliquid quo est omnia facere*. Est ergo ponere intellectum agentem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0054","text":"[I q. 79 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31738)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0055","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, secundum opinionem Platonis, nulla necessitas erat ponere intellectum agentem ad faciendum intelligibilia in actu; sed forte ad praebendum lumen intelligibile intelligenti, ut infra dicetur. Posuit enim Plato formas rerum naturalium sine materia subsistere, et per consequens eas intelligibiles esse, quia ex hoc est aliquid intelligibile actu, quod est immateriale. Et huiusmodi vocabat species, sive ideas, ex quarum participatione dicebat etiam materiam corporalem formari, ad hoc quod individua naturaliter constituerentur in propriis generibus et speciebus; et intellectus nostros, ad hoc quod de generibus et speciebus rerum scientiam haberent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0056","text":"Sed quia Aristoteles non posuit formas rerum naturalium subsistere sine materia; formae autem in materia existentes non sunt intelligibiles actu, sequebatur quod naturae seu formae rerum sensibilium, quas intelligimus, non essent intelligibiles actu. Nihil autem reducitur de potentia in actum, nisi per aliquod ens actu, sicut sensus fit in actu per sensibile in actu. Oportebat igitur ponere aliquam virtutem ex parte intellectus, quae faceret intelligibilia in actu, per abstractionem specierum a conditionibus materialibus. Et haec est necessitas ponendi intellectum agentem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0057","text":"[I q. 79 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31739)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0058","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod sensibilia inveniuntur actu extra animam, et ideo non oportuit ponere sensum agentem. Et sic patet quod in parte nutritiva omnes potentiae sunt activae; in parte autem sensitiva, omnes passivae; in parte vero intellectiva est aliquid activum, et aliquid passivum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0059","text":"[I q. 79 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31740)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0060","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod circa effectum luminis est duplex opinio. Quidam enim dicunt quod lumen requiritur ad visum, ut faciat colores actu visibiles. Et secundum hoc, similiter requiritur, et propter idem, intellectus agens ad intelligendum, propter quod lumen ad videndum. Secundum alios vero, lumen requiritur ad videndum, non propter colores, ut fiant actu visibiles; sed ut medium fiat actu lucidum, ut Commentator dicit in II de anima. Et secundum hoc, similitudo qua Aristoteles assimilat intellectum agentem lumini, attenditur quantum ad hoc, quod sicut hoc est necessarium ad videndum, ita illud ad intelligendum; sed non propter idem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0061","text":"[I q. 79 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31741)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0062","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, supposito agente, bene contingit diversimode recipi eius similitudinem in diversis propter eorum dispositionem diversam. Sed si agens non praeexistit, nihil ad hoc faciet dispositio recipientis. Intelligibile autem in actu non est aliquid existens in rerum natura, quantum ad naturam rerum sensibilium, quae non subsistunt praeter materiam. Et ideo ad intelligendum non sufficeret immaterialitas intellectus possibilis, nisi adesset intellectus agens, qui faceret intelligibilia in actu per modum abstractionis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0063","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0064","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31742)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0065","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus agens non sit aliquid animae nostrae. Intellectus enim agentis effectus est illuminare ad intelligendum. Sed hoc fit per aliquid quod est altius anima; secundum illud Ioan. I, *erat lux vera, quae illuminat omnem hominem venientem in hunc mundum*. Ergo videtur quod intellectus agens non sit aliquid animae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0066","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31743)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0067","text":"Praeterea, philosophus, in III de anima, attribuit intellectui agenti quod non aliquando intelligit et aliquando non intelligit. Sed anima nostra non semper intelligit; sed aliquando intelligit et aliquando non intelligit. Ergo intellectus agens non est aliquid animae nostrae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0068","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31744)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0069","text":"Praeterea, agens et patiens sufficiunt ad agendum. Si igitur intellectus possibilis est aliquid animae nostrae, qui est virtus passiva, et similiter intellectus agens, qui est virtus activa; sequitur quod homo semper poterit intelligere cum voluerit, quod patet esse falsum. Non est ergo intellectus agens aliquid animae nostrae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0070","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31745)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0071","text":"Praeterea, philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod intellectus agens est substantia actu ens. Nihil autem est respectu eiusdem in actu et in potentia. Si ergo intellectus possibilis, qui est in potentia ad omnia intelligibilia, est aliquid animae nostrae; videtur impossibile quod intellectus agens sit aliquid animae nostrae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0072","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31746)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0073","text":"Praeterea, si intellectus agens est aliquid animae nostrae, oportet quod sit aliqua potentia. Non est enim nec passio nec habitus, nam habitus et passiones non habent rationem agentis respectu passionum animae; sed magis passio est ipsa actio potentiae passivae, habitus autem est aliquid quod ex actibus consequitur. Omnis autem potentia fluit ab essentia animae. Sequeretur ergo quod intellectus agens ab essentia animae procederet. Et sic non inesset animae per participationem ab aliquo superiori intellectu, quod est inconveniens. Non ergo intellectus agens est aliquid animae nostrae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0074","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31747)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0075","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, III de anima quod *necesse est in anima has esse differentias*, scilicet intellectum possibilem, et agentem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0076","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31748)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0077","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod intellectus agens de quo philosophus loquitur, est aliquid animae. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod supra animam intellectivam humanam necesse est ponere aliquem superiorem intellectum, a quo anima virtutem intelligendi obtineat. Semper enim quod participat aliquid, et quod est mobile, et quod est imperfectum, praeexigit ante se aliquid quod est per essentiam suam tale, et quod est immobile et perfectum. Anima autem humana intellectiva dicitur per participationem intellectualis virtutis, cuius signum est, quod non tota est intellectiva, sed secundum aliquam sui partem. Pertingit etiam ad intelligentiam veritatis cum quodam discursu et motu, arguendo. Habet etiam imperfectam intelligentiam, tum quia non omnia intelligit; tum quia in his quae intelligit, de potentia procedit ad actum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0078","text":"Oportet ergo esse aliquem altiorem intellectum, quo anima iuvetur ad intelligendum. Posuerunt ergo quidam hunc intellectum secundum substantiam separatum, esse intellectum agentem, qui quasi illustrando phantasmata, facit ea intelligibilia actu. Sed, dato quod sit aliquis talis intellectus agens separatus, nihilominus tamen oportet ponere in ipsa anima humana aliquam virtutem ab illo intellectu superiori participatam, per quam anima humana facit intelligibilia in actu. Sicut et in aliis rebus naturalibus perfectis, praeter universales causas agentes, sunt propriae virtutes inditae singulis rebus perfectis, ab universalibus agentibus derivatae, non enim solus sol generat hominem, sed est in homine virtus generativa hominis; et similiter in aliis animalibus perfectis. Nihil autem est perfectius in inferioribus rebus anima humana.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0079","text":"Unde oportet dicere quod in ipsa sit aliqua virtus derivata a superiori intellectu, per quam possit phantasmata illustrare. Et hoc experimento cognoscimus, dum percipimus nos abstrahere formas universales a conditionibus particularibus, quod est facere actu intelligibilia. Nulla autem actio convenit alicui rei, nisi per aliquod principium formaliter ei inhaerens; ut supra dictum est, cum de intellectu possibili ageretur. Ergo oportet virtutem quae est principium huius actionis, esse aliquid in anima. Et ideo Aristoteles comparavit intellectum agentem lumini, quod est aliquid receptum in aere. Plato autem intellectum separatum imprimentem in animas nostras, comparavit soli; ut Themistius dicit in commentario tertii de anima. Sed intellectus separatus, secundum nostrae fidei documenta, est ipse Deus, qui est creator animae, et in quo solo beatificatur, ut infra patebit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0080","text":"Unde ab ipso anima humana lumen intellectuale participat, secundum illud Psalmi IV, *signatum est super nos lumen vultus tui, domine*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0081","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31749)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0082","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod illa lux vera illuminat sicut causa universalis, a qua anima humana participat quandam particularem virtutem, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0083","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31750)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0084","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod philosophus illa verba non dicit de intellectu agente, sed de intellectu in actu. Unde supra de ipso praemiserat, idem autem est secundum actum scientia rei. Vel, si intelligatur de intellectu agente, hoc dicitur quia non est ex parte intellectus agentis hoc quod quandoque intelligimus et quandoque non intelligimus; sed ex parte intellectus qui est in potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0085","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31751)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0086","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, si intellectus agens compararetur ad intellectum possibilem ut obiectum agens ad potentiam, sicut visibile in actu ad visum; sequeretur quod statim omnia intelligeremus, cum intellectus agens sit quo est omnia facere. Nunc autem non se habet ut obiectum, sed ut faciens obiecta in actu, ad quod requiritur, praeter praesentiam intellectus agentis, praesentia phantasmatum, et bona dispositio virium sensitivarum, et exercitium in huiusmodi opere; quia per unum intellectum fiunt etiam alia intellecta, sicut per terminos propositiones, et per prima principia conclusiones. Et quantum ad hoc, non differt utrum intellectus agens sit aliquid animae, vel aliquid separatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0087","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31752)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0088","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod anima intellectiva est quidem actu immaterialis, sed est in potentia ad determinatas species rerum. Phantasmata autem, e converso, sunt quidem actu similitudines specierum quarundam, sed sunt potentia immaterialia. Unde nihil prohibet unam et eandem animam, inquantum est immaterialis in actu, habere aliquam virtutem per quam faciat immaterialia in actu abstrahendo a conditionibus individualis materiae, quae quidem virtus dicitur intellectus agens; et aliam virtutem receptivam huiusmodi specierum, quae dicitur intellectus possibilis, inquantum est in potentia ad huiusmodi species.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0089","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31753)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0090","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod, cum essentia animae sit immaterialis, a supremo intellectu creata, nihil prohibet virtutem quae a supremo intellectu participatur, per quam abstrahit a materia, ab essentia ipsius procedere, sicut et alias eius potentias.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0091","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0092","text":"[I q. 79 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31754)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0093","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus agens sit unus in omnibus. Nihil enim quod est separatum a corpore, multiplicatur secundum multiplicationem corporum. Sed intellectus agens est separatus, ut dicitur in III de anima. Ergo non multiplicatur in multis corporibus hominum, sed est unus in omnibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0094","text":"[I q. 79 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31755)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0095","text":"Praeterea, intellectus agens facit universale, quod est unum in multis. Sed illud quod est causa unitatis, magis est unum. Ergo intellectus agens est unus in omnibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0096","text":"[I q. 79 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31756)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0097","text":"Praeterea, omnes homines conveniunt in primis conceptionibus intellectus. His autem assentiunt per intellectum agentem. Ergo conveniunt omnes in uno intellectu agente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0098","text":"[I q. 79 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31757)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0099","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod intellectus agens est sicut lumen. Non autem est idem lumen in diversis illuminatis. Ergo non est idem intellectus agens in diversis hominibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0100","text":"[I q. 79 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31758)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0101","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod veritas huius quaestionis dependet ex praemissis. Si enim intellectus agens non esset aliquid animae, sed esset quaedam substantia separata, unus esset intellectus agens omnium hominum. Et hoc intelligunt qui ponunt unitatem intellectus agentis. Si autem intellectus agens sit aliquid animae, ut quaedam virtus ipsius, necesse est dicere quod sint plures intellectus agentes, secundum pluralitatem animarum, quae multiplicantur secundum multiplicationem hominum, ut supra dictum est. Non enim potest esse quod una et eadem virtus numero sit diversarum substantiarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0102","text":"[I q. 79 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31759)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0103","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod philosophus probat intellectum agentem esse separatum, per hoc quod possibilis est separatus; quia, ut ipse dicit, agens est honorabilius patiente. Intellectus autem possibilis dicitur separatus, quia non est actus alicuius organi corporalis. Et secundum hunc modum etiam intellectus agens dicitur separatus, non quasi sit aliqua substantia separata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0104","text":"[I q. 79 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31760)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0105","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod intellectus agens causat universale abstrahendo a materia. Ad hoc autem non requiritur quod sit unus in omnibus habentibus intellectum, sed quod sit unus in omnibus secundum habitudinem ad omnia a quibus abstrahit universale, respectu quorum universale est unum. Et hoc competit intellectui agenti inquantum est immaterialis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0106","text":"[I q. 79 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31761)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0107","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod omnia quae sunt unius speciei, communicant in actione consequente naturam speciei, et per consequens in virtute, quae est actionis principium, non quod sit eadem numero in omnibus. Cognoscere autem prima intelligibilia est actio consequens speciem humanam. Unde oportet quod omnes homines communicent in virtute quae est principium huius actionis, et haec est virtus intellectus agentis. Non tamen oportet quod sit eadem numero in omnibus. Oportet tamen quod ab uno principio in omnibus derivetur. Et sic illa communicatio hominum in primis intelligibilibus, demonstrat unitatem intellectus separati, quem Plato comparat soli; non autem unitatem intellectus agentis, quem Aristoteles comparat lumini.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0108","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0109","text":"[I q. 79 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31762)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0110","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod memoria non sit in parte intellectiva animae. Dicit enim Augustinus, XII de Trin., quod ad partem superiorem animae pertinent quae non sunt hominibus pecoribusque communia. Sed memoria est hominibus pecoribusque communis, dicit enim ibidem quod *possunt pecora sentire per corporis sensus corporalia, et ea mandare memoriae*. Ergo memoria non pertinet ad partem animae intellectivam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0111","text":"[I q. 79 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31763)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0112","text":"Praeterea, memoria praeteritorum est. Sed praeteritum dicitur secundum aliquod determinatum tempus. Memoria igitur est cognoscitiva alicuius sub determinato tempore; quod est cognoscere aliquid sub hic et nunc. Hoc autem non est intellectus, sed sensus. Memoria igitur non est in parte intellectiva, sed solum in parte sensitiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0113","text":"[I q. 79 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31764)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0114","text":"Praeterea, in memoria conservantur species rerum quae actu non cogitantur. Sed hoc non est possibile accidere in intellectu, quia intellectus fit in actu per hoc quod informatur specie intelligibili; intellectum autem esse in actu, est ipsum intelligere in actu; et sic intellectus omnia intelligit in actu, quorum species apud se habet. Non ergo memoria est in parte intellectiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0115","text":"[I q. 79 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31765)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0116","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, X de Trin., quod *memoria, intelligentia et voluntas sunt una mens*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0117","text":"[I q. 79 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31766)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0118","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum de ratione memoriae sit conservare species rerum quae actu non apprehenduntur, hoc primum considerari oportet, utrum species intelligibiles sic in intellectu conservari possint. Posuit enim Avicenna hoc esse impossibile. In parte enim sensitiva dicebat hoc accidere, quantum ad aliquas potentias, inquantum sunt actus organorum corporalium, in quibus conservari possunt aliquae species absque actuali apprehensione. In intellectu autem, qui caret organo corporali, nihil existit nisi intelligibiliter. Unde oportet intelligi in actu illud cuius similitudo in intellectu existit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0119","text":"Sic ergo, secundum ipsum, quam cito aliquis actu desinit intelligere aliquam rem, desinit esse illius rei species in intellectu, sed oportet, si denuo vult illam rem intelligere, quod convertat se ad intellectum agentem, quem ponit substantiam separatam, ut ab illo effluant species intelligibiles in intellectum possibilem. Et ex exercitio et usu convertendi se ad intellectum agentem, relinquitur, secundum ipsum, quaedam habilitas in intellectu possibili convertendi se ad intellectum agentem, quam dicebat esse habitum scientiae. Secundum igitur hanc positionem, nihil conservatur in parte intellectiva, quod non actu intelligatur. Unde non poterit poni memoria in parte intellectiva, secundum hunc modum. Sed haec opinio manifeste repugnat dictis Aristotelis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0120","text":"Dicit enim, in III de anima, quod, cum intellectus possibilis *sic fiat singula ut sciens, dicitur qui secundum actum; et quod hoc accidit cum possit operari per seipsum. Est quidem igitur et tunc potentia quodammodo; non tamen similiter ut ante addiscere aut invenire*. Dicitur autem intellectus possibilis fieri singula, secundum quod recipit species singulorum. Ex hoc ergo quod recipit species intelligibilium, habet quod possit operari cum voluerit, non autem quod semper operetur, quia et tunc est quodammodo in potentia, licet aliter quam ante intelligere; eo scilicet modo quo sciens in habitu est in potentia ad considerandum in actu. Repugnat etiam praedicta positio rationi. Quod enim recipitur in aliquo, recipitur in eo secundum modum recipientis. Intellectus autem est magis stabilis naturae et immobilis, quam materia corporalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0121","text":"Si ergo materia corporalis formas quas recipit, non solum tenet dum per eas agit in actu, sed etiam postquam agere per eas cessaverit; multo fortius intellectus immobiliter et inamissibiliter recipit species intelligibiles, sive a sensibilibus acceptas, sive etiam ab aliquo superiori intellectu effluxas. Sic igitur, si memoria accipiatur solum pro vi conservativa specierum, oportet dicere memoriam esse in intellectiva parte. Si vero de ratione memoriae sit quod eius obiectum sit praeteritum, ut praeteritum; memoria in parte intellectiva non erit, sed sensitiva tantum, quae est apprehensiva particularium. Praeteritum enim, ut praeteritum, cum significet esse sub determinato tempore, ad conditionem particularis pertinet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0122","text":"[I q. 79 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31767)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0123","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod memoria, secundum quod est conservativa specierum, non est nobis pecoribusque communis. Species enim conservantur non in parte animae sensitiva tantum, sed magis in coniuncto; cum vis memorativa sit actus organi cuiusdam. Sed intellectus secundum seipsum est conservativus specierum, praeter concomitantiam organi corporalis. Unde philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod *anima est locus specierum, non tota, sed intellectus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0124","text":"[I q. 79 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31768)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0125","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod praeteritio potest ad duo referri, scilicet ad obiectum quod cognoscitur; et ad cognitionis actum. Quae quidem duo simul coniunguntur in parte sensitiva, quae est apprehensiva alicuius per hoc quod immutatur a praesenti sensibili, unde simul animal memoratur se prius sensisse in praeterito, et se sensisse quoddam praeteritum sensibile. Sed quantum ad partem intellectivam pertinet, praeteritio accidit, et non per se convenit, ex parte obiecti intellectus. Intelligit enim intellectus hominem, inquantum est homo, homini autem, inquantum est homo, accidit vel in praesenti vel in praeterito vel in futuro esse. Ex parte vero actus, praeteritio per se accipi potest etiam in intellectu, sicut in sensu. Quia intelligere animae nostrae est quidam particularis actus, in hoc vel in illo tempore existens, secundum quod dicitur homo intelligere nunc vel heri vel cras.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0126","text":"Et hoc non repugnat intellectualitati, quia huiusmodi intelligere, quamvis sit quoddam particulare, tamen est immaterialis actus, ut supra de intellectu dictum est; et ideo sicut intelligit seipsum intellectus, quamvis ipse sit quidam singularis intellectus, ita intelligit suum intelligere, quod est singularis actus vel in praeterito vel in praesenti vel in futuro existens. Sic igitur salvatur ratio memoriae, quantum ad hoc quod est praeteritorum, in intellectu, secundum quod intelligit se prius intellexisse, non autem secundum quod intelligit praeteritum, prout est hic et nunc.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0127","text":"[I q. 79 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31769)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0128","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod species intelligibilis aliquando est in intellectu in potentia tantum, et tunc dicitur intellectus esse in potentia. Aliquando autem secundum ultimam completionem actus, et tunc intelligit actu. Aliquando medio modo se habet inter potentiam et actum, et tunc dicitur esse intellectus in habitu. Et secundum hunc modum intellectus conservat species, etiam quando actu non intelligit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0129","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0130","text":"[I q. 79 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31770)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0131","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod alia potentia sit memoria intellectiva, et alia intellectus. Augustinus enim, in X de Trin., ponit in mente memoriam, intelligentiam et voluntatem. Manifestum est autem quod memoria est alia potentia a voluntate. Ergo similiter est alia ab intellectu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0132","text":"[I q. 79 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31771)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0133","text":"Praeterea, eadem ratio distinctionis est potentiarum sensitivae partis et intellectivae. Sed memoria in parte sensitiva est alia potentia a sensu, ut supra dictum est. Ergo memoria partis intellectivae est alia potentia ab intellectu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0134","text":"[I q. 79 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31772)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0135","text":"Praeterea, secundum Augustinum, memoria, intelligentia et voluntas sunt sibi invicem aequalia, et unum eorum ab alio oritur. Hoc autem esse non posset, si memoria esset eadem potentia cum intellectu. Non est ergo eadem potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0136","text":"[I q. 79 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31773)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0137","text":"Sed contra, de ratione memoriae est, quod sit thesaurus vel locus conservativus specierum. Hoc autem philosophus, in III de anima, attribuit intellectui, ut dictum est. Non ergo in parte intellectiva est alia potentia memoria ab intellectu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0138","text":"[I q. 79 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31774)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0139","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, potentiae animae distinguuntur secundum diversas rationes obiectorum; eo quod ratio cuiuslibet potentiae consistit in ordine ad id ad quod dicitur, quod est eius obiectum. Dictum est etiam supra quod, si aliqua potentia secundum propriam rationem ordinetur ad aliquod obiectum secundum communem rationem obiecti, non diversificabitur illa potentia secundum diversitates particularium differentiarum, sicut potentia visiva, quae respicit suum obiectum secundum rationem colorati, non diversificatur per diversitatem albi et nigri. Intellectus autem respicit suum obiectum secundum communem rationem entis; eo quod intellectus possibilis est quo est omnia fieri. Unde secundum nullam differentiam entium, diversificatur differentia intellectus possibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0140","text":"Diversificatur tamen potentia intellectus agentis, et intellectus possibilis, quia respectu eiusdem obiecti, aliud principium oportet esse potentiam activam, quae facit obiectum esse in actu; et aliud potentiam passivam, quae movetur ab obiecto in actu existente. Et sic potentia activa comparatur ad suum obiectum, ut ens in actu ad ens in potentia, potentia autem passiva comparatur ad suum obiectum e converso, ut ens in potentia ad ens in actu. Sic igitur nulla alia differentia potentiarum in intellectu esse potest, nisi possibilis et agentis. Unde patet quod memoria non est alia potentia ab intellectu, ad rationem enim potentiae passivae pertinet conservare, sicut et recipere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0141","text":"[I q. 79 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31775)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0142","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, quamvis in III dist. I Sent. dicatur quod memoria, intelligentia et voluntas sint tres vires; tamen hoc non est secundum intentionem Augustini, qui expresse dicit in XIV de Trin., quod *si accipiatur memoria, intelligentia et voluntas, secundum quod semper praesto sunt animae, sive cogitentur sive non cogitentur, ad solam memoriam pertinere videntur. Intelligentiam autem nunc dico qua intelligimus cogitantes; et eam voluntatem, sive amorem vel dilectionem, quae istam prolem parentemque coniungit*. Ex quo patet quod ista tria non accipit Augustinus pro tribus potentiis; sed memoriam accipit pro habituali animae retentione, intelligentiam autem pro actu intellectus, voluntatem autem pro actu voluntatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0143","text":"[I q. 79 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31776)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0144","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod praeteritum et praesens possunt esse propriae differentiae potentiarum sensitivarum diversificativae; non autem potentiarum intellectivarum, ratione supra dicta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0145","text":"[I q. 79 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31777)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0146","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod intelligentia oritur ex memoria, sicut actus ex habitu. Et hoc modo etiam aequatur ei; non autem sicut potentia potentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0147","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0148","text":"[I q. 79 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31778)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0149","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ratio sit alia potentia ab intellectu. In libro enim de spiritu et anima dicitur, *cum ab inferioribus ad superiora ascendere volumus, prius occurrit nobis sensus, deinde imaginatio, deinde ratio, deinde intellectus*. Est ergo alia potentia ratio ab intellectu, sicut imaginatio a ratione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0150","text":"[I q. 79 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31779)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0151","text":"Praeterea, Boetius dicit, in libro de Consol., quod intellectus comparatur ad rationem sicut aeternitas ad tempus. Sed non est eiusdem virtutis esse in aeternitate et esse in tempore. Ergo non est eadem potentia ratio et intellectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0152","text":"[I q. 79 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31780)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0153","text":"Praeterea, homo communicat cum Angelis in intellectu, cum brutis vero in sensu. Sed ratio, quae est propria hominis, qua animal rationale dicitur, est alia potentia a sensu. Ergo pari ratione est alia potentia ab intellectu, qui proprie convenit Angelis, unde et intellectuales dicuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0154","text":"[I q. 79 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31781)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0155","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, III super Gen. ad Litt., quod *illud quo homo irrationabilibus animalibus antecellit, est ratio, vel mens, vel intelligentia, vel si quo alio vocabulo commodius appellatur*. Ratio ergo et intellectus et mens sunt una potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0156","text":"[I q. 79 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31782)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0157","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ratio et intellectus in homine non possunt esse diversae potentiae. Quod manifeste cognoscitur, si utriusque actus consideretur. Intelligere enim est simpliciter veritatem intelligibilem apprehendere. Ratiocinari autem est procedere de uno intellecto ad aliud, ad veritatem intelligibilem cognoscendam. Et ideo Angeli, qui perfecte possident, secundum modum suae naturae, cognitionem intelligibilis veritatis, non habent necesse procedere de uno ad aliud; sed simpliciter et absque discursu veritatem rerum apprehendunt, ut Dionysius dicit, VII cap. de Div. Nom. Homines autem ad intelligibilem veritatem cognoscendam perveniunt, procedendo de uno ad aliud, ut ibidem dicitur, et ideo rationales dicuntur. Patet ergo quod ratiocinari comparatur ad intelligere sicut moveri ad quiescere, vel acquirere ad habere, quorum unum est perfecti, aliud autem imperfecti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0158","text":"Et quia motus semper ab immobili procedit, et ad aliquid quietum terminatur; inde est quod ratiocinatio humana, secundum viam inquisitionis vel inventionis, procedit a quibusdam simpliciter intellectis, quae sunt prima principia; et rursus, in via iudicii, resolvendo redit ad prima principia, ad quae inventa examinat. Manifestum est autem quod quiescere et moveri non reducuntur ad diversas potentias, sed ad unam et eandem, etiam in naturalibus rebus, quia per eandem naturam aliquid movetur ad locum, et quiescit in loco. Multo ergo magis per eandem potentiam intelligimus et ratiocinamur. Et sic patet quod in homine eadem potentia est ratio et intellectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0159","text":"[I q. 79 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31783)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0160","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod illa enumeratio fit secundum ordinem actuum, non secundum distinctionem potentiarum. Quamvis liber ille non sit magnae auctoritatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0161","text":"[I q. 79 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31784)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0162","text":"Ad secundum patet responsio ex dictis. Aeternitas enim comparatur ad tempus, sicut immobile ad mobile. Et ideo Boetius comparavit intellectum aeternitati, rationem vero tempori.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0163","text":"[I q. 79 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31785)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0164","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod alia animalia sunt ita infra hominem, quod non possunt attingere ad cognoscendam veritatem, quam ratio inquirit. Homo vero attingit ad cognoscendam intelligibilem veritatem, quam Angeli cognoscunt; sed imperfecte. Et ideo vis cognoscitiva Angelorum non est alterius generis a vi cognoscitiva rationis, sed comparatur ad ipsam ut perfectum ad imperfectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0165","text":"## Articulus 9","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0166","text":"[I q. 79 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31786)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0167","text":"Ad nonum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ratio superior et inferior sint diversae potentiae. Dicit enim Augustinus, XII de Trin., quod imago Trinitatis est in superiori parte rationis, non autem in inferiori. Sed partes animae sunt ipsae eius potentiae. Ergo duae potentiae sunt ratio superior et inferior.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0168","text":"[I q. 79 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31787)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0169","text":"Praeterea, nihil oritur a seipso. Sed ratio inferior oritur a superiori, et ab ea regulatur et dirigitur. Ergo ratio superior est alia potentia ab inferiori.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0170","text":"[I q. 79 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31788)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0171","text":"Praeterea, philosophus dicit, in VI Ethic., quod scientificum animae quo cognoscit anima necessaria, est aliud principium et alia pars animae ab opinativo et ratiocinativo, quo cognoscit contingentia. Et hoc probat per hoc, quia *ad ea quae sunt genere altera, altera genere particula animae* ordinatur; contingens autem et necessarium sunt altera genere, sicut corruptibile et incorruptibile. Cum autem idem sit necessarium quod aeternum, et temporale idem quod contingens; videtur quod idem sit quod philosophus vocat scientificum, et superior pars rationis, quae secundum Augustinum intendit aeternis conspiciendis et consulendis; et quod idem sit quod philosophus vocat ratiocinativum vel opinativum, et inferior ratio, quae secundum Augustinum intendit temporalibus disponendis. Est ergo alia potentia animae ratio superior, et ratio inferior.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0172","text":"[I q. 79 a. 9 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31789)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0173","text":"Praeterea, Damascenus dicit quod *ex imaginatione fit opinio; deinde mens, diiudicans opinionem sive vera sit sive falsa, diiudicat veritatem; unde et mens dicitur a metiendo. De quibus igitur iudicatum est iam et determinatum vere, dicitur intellectus*. Sic igitur opinativum, quod est ratio inferior, est aliud a mente et intellectu, per quod potest intelligi ratio superior.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0174","text":"[I q. 79 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31790)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0175","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XII de Trin., quod ratio superior et inferior non nisi per officia distinguuntur. Non ergo sunt duae potentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0176","text":"[I q. 79 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31791)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0177","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ratio superior et inferior, secundum quod ab Augustino accipiuntur, nullo modo duae potentiae animae esse possunt. Dicit enim quod ratio superior est quae intendit aeternis conspiciendis aut consulendis, conspiciendis quidem, secundum quod ea in seipsis speculatur; consulendis vero, secundum quod ex eis accipit regulas agendorum. Ratio vero inferior ab ipso dicitur, quae intendit temporalibus rebus. Haec autem duo, scilicet temporalia et aeterna, comparantur ad cognitionem nostram hoc modo, quod unum eorum est medium ad cognoscendum alterum. Nam secundum viam inventionis, per res temporales in cognitionem devenimus aeternorum, secundum illud apostoli, ad Rom. I, *invisibilia Dei per ea quae facta sunt, intellecta, conspiciuntur*, in via vero iudicii, per aeterna iam cognita de temporalibus iudicamus, et secundum rationes aeternorum temporalia disponimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0178","text":"Potest autem contingere quod medium, et id ad quod per medium pervenitur, ad diversos habitus pertineant, sicut principia prima indemonstrabilia pertinent ad habitum intellectus, conclusiones vero ex his deductae ad habitum scientiae. Et ideo ex principiis geometriae contingit aliquid concludere in alia scientia, puta in perspectiva. Sed eadem potentia rationis est, ad quam pertinet et medium et ultimum. Est enim actus rationis quasi quidam motus de uno in aliud perveniens, idem autem est mobile quod pertransiens medium pertingit ad terminum. Unde una et eadem potentia rationis est ratio superior et inferior. Sed distinguuntur, secundum Augustinum, per officia actuum, et secundum diversos habitus, nam superiori rationi attribuitur sapientia, inferiori vero scientia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0179","text":"[I q. 79 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31792)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0180","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod secundum quamcumque rationem partitionis potest pars dici. Inquantum ergo ratio dividitur secundum diversa officia, ratio superior et inferior partitiones dicuntur, et non quia sunt diversae potentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0181","text":"[I q. 79 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31793)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0182","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ratio inferior dicitur a superiori deduci, vel ab ea regulari, inquantum principia quibus utitur inferior ratio, deducuntur et diriguntur a principiis superioris rationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0183","text":"[I q. 79 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31794)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0184","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod scientificum de quo philosophus loquitur non est idem quod ratio superior, nam necessaria scibilia inveniuntur etiam in rebus temporalibus, de quibus est scientia naturalis et mathematica. Opinativum autem et ratiocinativum in minus est quam ratio inferior, quia est contingentium tantum. Nec tamen est simpliciter dicendum quod sit alia potentia qua intellectus cognoscit necessaria, et alia qua cognoscit contingentia, quia utraque cognoscit secundum eandem rationem obiecti, scilicet secundum rationem entis et veri. Unde et necessaria, quae habent perfectum esse in veritate, perfecte cognoscit; utpote ad eorum quidditatem pertingens, per quam propria accidentia de his demonstrat. Contingentia vero imperfecte cognoscit; sicut et habent imperfectum esse et veritatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0185","text":"Perfectum autem et imperfectum in actu non diversificant potentiam; sed diversificant actus quantum ad modum agendi, et per consequens principia actuum et ipsos habitus. Et ideo philosophus posuit duas particulas animae, scientificum et ratiocinativum, non quia sunt duae potentiae; sed quia distinguuntur secundum diversam aptitudinem ad recipiendum diversos habitus, quorum diversitatem ibi inquirere intendit. Contingentia enim et necessaria, etsi differant secundum propria genera, conveniunt tamen in communi ratione entis, quam respicit intellectus, ad quam diversimode se habent secundum perfectum et imperfectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0186","text":"[I q. 79 a. 9 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31795)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0187","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod illa distinctio Damasceni est secundum diversitatem actuum, non secundum diversitatem potentiarum. Opinio enim significat actum intellectus qui fertur in unam partem contradictionis cum formidine alterius. Diiudicare vero, vel mensurare, est actus intellectus applicantis principia certa ad examinationem propositorum. Et ex hoc sumitur nomen mentis. Intelligere autem est cum quadam approbatione diiudicatis inhaerere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0188","text":"## Articulus 10","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0189","text":"[I q. 79 a. 10 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31796)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0190","text":"Ad decimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intelligentia sit alia potentia ab intellectu. Dicitur enim in libro de spiritu et anima, quod *cum ab inferioribus ad superiora ascendere volumus, prius occurrit nobis sensus, deinde imaginatio, deinde ratio, postea intellectus, et postea intelligentia*. Sed imaginatio et sensus sunt diversae potentiae. Ergo et intellectus et intelligentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0191","text":"[I q. 79 a. 10 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31797)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0192","text":"Praeterea, Boetius dicit, in V de Consol., quod *ipsum hominem aliter sensus, aliter imaginatio, aliter ratio, aliter intelligentia intuetur*. Sed intellectus est eadem potentia cum ratione. Ergo videtur quod intelligentia sit alia potentia quam intellectus; sicut ratio est alia potentia quam imaginatio et sensus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0193","text":"[I q. 79 a. 10 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31798)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0194","text":"Praeterea, actus sunt praevii potentiis, ut dicitur in II de anima. Sed intelligentia est quidam actus ab aliis actibus qui attribuuntur intellectui divisus. Dicit enim Damascenus quod *primus motus intelligentia dicitur; quae vero circa aliquid est intelligentia, intentio vocatur; quae permanens et figurans animam ad id quod intelligitur, excogitatio dicitur; excogitatio vero in eodem manens, et seipsam examinans et diiudicans, phronesis dicitur* (idest sapientia); *phronesis autem dilatata facit cogitationem, idest interius dispositum sermonem; ex quo aiunt provenire sermonem per linguam enarratum*. Ergo videtur quod intelligentia sit quaedam specialis potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0195","text":"[I q. 79 a. 10 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31799)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0196","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod *intelligentia indivisibilium est, in quibus non est falsum*. Sed huiusmodi cognoscere pertinet ad intellectum. Ergo intelligentia non est alia potentia praeter intellectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0197","text":"[I q. 79 a. 10 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31800)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0198","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod hoc nomen intelligentia proprie significat ipsum actum intellectus qui est intelligere. In quibusdam tamen libris de Arabico translatis, substantiae separatae quas nos Angelos dicimus, intelligentiae vocantur; forte propter hoc, quod huiusmodi substantiae semper actu intelligunt. In libris tamen de Graeco translatis, dicuntur intellectus seu mentes. Sic ergo intelligentia ab intellectu non distinguitur sicut potentia a potentia; sed sicut actus a potentia. Invenitur enim talis divisio etiam a philosophis. Quandoque enim ponunt quatuor intellectus, scilicet intellectum agentem, possibilem, et in habitu, et adeptum. Quorum quatuor intellectus agens et possibilis sunt diversae potentiae; sicut et in omnibus est alia potentia activa, et alia passiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0199","text":"Alia vero tria distinguuntur secundum tres status intellectus possibilis, qui quandoque est in potentia tantum, et sic dicitur possibilis; quandoque autem in actu primo, qui est scientia, et sic dicitur intellectus in habitu; quandoque autem in actu secundo, qui est considerare, et sic dicitur intellectus in actu, sive intellectus adeptus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0200","text":"[I q. 79 a. 10 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31801)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0201","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, si recipi debet illa auctoritas, intelligentia ponitur pro actu intellectus. Et sic dividitur contra intellectum, sicut actus contra potentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0202","text":"[I q. 79 a. 10 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31802)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0203","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Boetius accipit intelligentiam pro actu intellectus qui transcendit actum rationis. Unde ibidem dicit quod *ratio tantum humani generis est, sicut intelligentia sola divini*, proprium enim Dei est quod absque omni investigatione omnia intelligat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0204","text":"[I q. 79 a. 10 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31803)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0205","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod omnes illi actus quos Damascenus enumerat, sunt unius potentiae, scilicet intellectivae. Quae primo quidem simpliciter aliquid apprehendit, et hic actus dicitur intelligentia. Secundo vero, id quod apprehendit, ordinat ad aliquid aliud cognoscendum vel operandum, et hic vocatur intentio. Dum vero persistit in inquisitione illius quod intendit, vocatur excogitatio. Dum vero id quod est excogitatum examinat ad aliqua certa, dicitur scire vel sapere; quod est phronesis, vel sapientiae, nam sapientiae est iudicare, ut dicitur in I Metaphys. Ex quo autem habet aliquid pro certo, quasi examinatum, cogitat quomodo possit illud aliis manifestare, et haec est dispositio interioris sermonis; ex qua procedit exterior locutio. Non enim omnis differentia actuum potentias diversificat; sed solum illa quae non potest reduci in idem principium, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0206","text":"## Articulus 11","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0207","text":"[I q. 79 a. 11 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31804)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0208","text":"Ad undecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus speculativus et practicus sint diversae potentiae. Apprehensivum enim et motivum sunt diversa genera potentiarum, ut patet in II de anima. Sed intellectus speculativus est apprehensivus tantum, intellectus autem practicus est motivus. Ergo sunt diversae potentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0209","text":"[I q. 79 a. 11 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31805)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0210","text":"Praeterea, diversa ratio obiecti diversificat potentiam. Sed obiectum speculativi intellectus est verum, practici autem bonum; quae differunt ratione. Ergo intellectus speculativus et practicus sunt diversae potentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0211","text":"[I q. 79 a. 11 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31806)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0212","text":"Praeterea, in parte intellectiva intellectus practicus comparatur ad speculativum, sicut aestimativa ad imaginativam in parte sensitiva. Sed aestimativa differt ab imaginativa sicut potentia a potentia, ut supra dictum est. Ergo et intellectus practicus a speculativo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0213","text":"[I q. 79 a. 11 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31807)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0214","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in III de anima, quod intellectus speculativus per extensionem fit practicus. Una autem potentia non mutatur in aliam. Ergo intellectus speculativus et practicus non sunt diversae potentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0215","text":"[I q. 79 a. 11 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31808)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0216","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod intellectus practicus et speculativus non sunt diversae potentiae. Cuius ratio est quia, ut supra dictum est, id quod accidentaliter se habet ad obiecti rationem quam respicit aliqua potentia, non diversificat potentiam, accidit enim colorato quod sit homo, aut magnum aut parvum; unde omnia huiusmodi eadem visiva potentia apprehenduntur. Accidit autem alicui apprehenso per intellectum, quod ordinetur ad opus, vel non ordinetur. Secundum hoc autem differunt intellectus speculativus et practicus. Nam intellectus speculativus est, qui quod apprehendit, non ordinat ad opus, sed ad solam veritatis considerationem, practicus vero intellectus dicitur, qui hoc quod apprehendit, ordinat ad opus. Et hoc est quod philosophus dicit in III de anima, quod *speculativus differt a practico, fine*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0217","text":"Unde et a fine denominatur uterque, hic quidem speculativus, ille vero practicus, idest operativus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0218","text":"[I q. 79 a. 11 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31809)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0219","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod intellectus practicus est motivus, non quasi exequens motum, sed quasi dirigens ad motum. Quod convenit ei secundum modum suae apprehensionis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0220","text":"[I q. 79 a. 11 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31810)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0221","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod verum et bonum se invicem includunt, nam verum est quoddam bonum, alioquin non esset appetibile; et bonum est quoddam verum, alioquin non esset intelligibile. Sicut igitur obiectum appetitus potest esse verum, inquantum habet rationem boni, sicut cum aliquis appetit veritatem cognoscere; ita obiectum intellectus practici est bonum ordinabile ad opus, sub ratione veri. Intellectus enim practicus veritatem cognoscit, sicut et speculativus; sed veritatem cognitam ordinat ad opus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0222","text":"[I q. 79 a. 11 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31811)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0223","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod multae differentiae diversificant sensitivas potentias, quae non diversificant potentias intellectivas, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0224","text":"## Articulus 12","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0225","text":"[I q. 79 a. 12 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31812)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0226","text":"Ad duodecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod synderesis sit quaedam specialis potentia ab aliis distincta. Ea enim quae cadunt sub una divisione, videntur esse unius generis. Sed in Glossa Hieronymi Ezech. I, dividitur synderesis contra irascibilem et concupiscibilem et rationalem; quae sunt quaedam potentiae. Ergo synderesis est quaedam potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0227","text":"[I q. 79 a. 12 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31813)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0228","text":"Praeterea, opposita sunt unius generis. Sed synderesis et sensualitas opponi videntur, quia synderesis semper inclinat ad bonum, sensualitas autem semper ad malum; unde per serpentem significatur, ut patet per Augustinum, XII de Trin. Videtur ergo quod synderesis sit potentia, sicut et sensualitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0229","text":"[I q. 79 a. 12 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31814)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0230","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, in libro de libero arbitrio, quod in naturali iudicatorio adsunt quaedam *regulae et semina virtutum et vera et incommutabilia*, haec autem dicimus synderesim. Cum ergo regulae incommutabiles quibus iudicamus, pertineant ad rationem secundum sui superiorem partem, ut Augustinus dicit XII de Trin.; videtur quod synderesis sit idem quod ratio. Et ita est quaedam potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0231","text":"[I q. 79 a. 12 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31815)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0232","text":"*Sed contra, potentiae rationales se habent ad opposita*, secundum philosophum. Synderesis autem non se habet ad opposita, sed ad bonum tantum inclinat. Ergo synderesis non est potentia. Si enim esset potentia, oporteret quod esset rationalis potentia, non enim invenitur in brutis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0233","text":"[I q. 79 a. 12 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31816)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0234","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod synderesis non est potentia, sed habitus, licet quidam posuerint synderesim esse quandam potentiam ratione altiorem; quidam vero dixerint eam esse ipsam rationem, non ut est ratio, sed ut est natura. Ad huius autem evidentiam, considerandum est quod, sicut supra dictum est, ratiocinatio hominis, cum sit quidam motus, ab intellectu progreditur aliquorum, scilicet naturaliter notorum absque investigatione rationis, sicut a quodam principio immobili, et ad intellectum etiam terminatur, inquantum iudicamus per principia per se naturaliter nota, de his quae ratiocinando invenimus. Constat autem quod, sicut ratio speculativa ratiocinatur de speculativis, ita ratio practica ratiocinatur de operabilibus. Oportet igitur naturaliter nobis esse indita, sicut principia speculabilium, ita et principia operabilium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0235","text":"Prima autem principia speculabilium nobis naturaliter indita, non pertinent ad aliquam specialem potentiam; sed ad quendam specialem habitum, qui dicitur intellectus principiorum, ut patet in VI Ethic. Unde et principia operabilium nobis naturaliter indita, non pertinent ad specialem potentiam; sed ad specialem habitum naturalem, quem dicimus synderesim. Unde et synderesis dicitur instigare ad bonum, et murmurare de malo, inquantum per prima principia procedimus ad inveniendum, et iudicamus inventa. Patet ergo quod synderesis non est potentia, sed habitus naturalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0236","text":"[I q. 79 a. 12 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31817)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0237","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod illa divisio Hieronymi attenditur secundum diversitatem actuum, non secundum diversitatem potentiarum. Diversi autem actus possunt esse unius potentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0238","text":"[I q. 79 a. 12 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31818)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0239","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod similiter oppositio sensualitatis et synderesis attenditur secundum oppositionem actuum; non sicut diversarum specierum unius generis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0240","text":"[I q. 79 a. 12 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31819)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0241","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod huiusmodi incommutabiles rationes sunt prima principia operabilium, circa quae non contingit errare; et attribuuntur rationi sicut potentiae, et synderesi sicut habitui. Unde et utroque, scilicet ratione et synderesi, naturaliter iudicamus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0242","text":"## Articulus 13","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0243","text":"[I q. 79 a. 13 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31820)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0244","text":"Ad tertiumdecimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod conscientia sit quaedam potentia. Dicit enim Origenes quod *conscientia est spiritus corrector et paedagogus animae sociatus, quo separatur a malis et adhaeret bonis*. Sed spiritus in anima nominat potentiam aliquam, vel ipsam mentem, secundum illud Ephes. IV, *renovamini spiritu mentis vestrae*; vel ipsam imaginationem; unde et imaginaria visio spiritualis vocatur, ut patet per Augustinum, XII super Gen. ad Litt. Est ergo conscientia quaedam potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0245","text":"[I q. 79 a. 13 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31821)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0246","text":"Praeterea, nihil est peccati subiectum nisi potentia animae. Sed conscientia est subiectum peccati, dicitur enim ad Tit. I, de quibusdam, quod *inquinatae sunt eorum mens et conscientia*. Ergo videtur quod conscientia sit potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0247","text":"[I q. 79 a. 13 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31822)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0248","text":"Praeterea, necesse est quod conscientia sit vel actus, vel habitus, vel potentia. Sed non est actus, quia non semper maneret in homine. Nec est habitus, non enim esset unum quid conscientia, sed multa; per multos enim habitus cognoscitivos dirigimur in agendis. Ergo conscientia est potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0249","text":"[I q. 79 a. 13 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31823)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0250","text":"Sed contra, conscientia deponi potest, non autem potentia. Ergo conscientia non est potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0251","text":"[I q. 79 a. 13 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31824)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0252","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod conscientia, proprie loquendo, non est potentia, sed actus. Et hoc patet tum ex ratione nominis, tum etiam ex his quae secundum communem usum loquendi, conscientiae attribuuntur. Conscientia enim, secundum proprietatem vocabuli, importat ordinem scientiae ad aliquid, nam conscientia dicitur cum alio scientia. Applicatio autem scientiae ad aliquid fit per aliquem actum. Unde ex ista ratione nominis patet quod conscientia sit actus. Idem autem apparet ex his quae conscientiae attribuuntur. Dicitur enim conscientia testificari, ligare vel instigare, et etiam accusare vel remordere sive reprehendere. Et haec omnia consequuntur applicationem alicuius nostrae cognitionis vel scientiae ad ea quae agimus. Quae quidem applicatio fit tripliciter. Uno modo, secundum quod recognoscimus aliquid nos fecisse vel non fecisse, secundum illud Eccle.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0253","text":"VII, *scit conscientia tua te crebro maledixisse aliis*, et secundum hoc, conscientia dicitur testificari. Alio modo applicatur secundum quod per nostram conscientiam iudicamus aliquid esse faciendum vel non faciendum, et secundum hoc, dicitur conscientia instigare vel ligare. Tertio modo applicatur secundum quod per conscientiam iudicamus quod aliquid quod est factum, sit bene factum vel non bene factum, et secundum hoc, conscientia dicitur excusare vel accusare, seu remordere. Patet autem quod omnia haec consequuntur actualem applicationem scientiae ad ea quae agimus. Unde proprie loquendo, conscientia nominat actum. Quia tamen habitus est principium actus, quandoque nomen conscientiae attribuitur primo habitui naturali, scilicet synderesi, sicut Hieronymus, in Glossa Ezech.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0254","text":"I, synderesim conscientiam nominat; et Basilius naturale iudicatorium; et Damascenus dicit quod est lex intellectus nostri. Consuetum enim est quod causae et effectus per invicem nominentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0255","text":"[I q. 79 a. 13 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31825)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0256","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod conscientia dicitur spiritus, secundum quod spiritus pro mente ponitur, quia est quoddam mentis dictamen.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0257","text":"[I q. 79 a. 13 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31826)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0258","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod inquinatio dicitur esse in conscientia, non sicut in subiecto, sed sicut cognitum in cognitione, inquantum scilicet aliquis scit se esse inquinatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0259","text":"[I q. 79 a. 13 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31827)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0260","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod actus, etsi non semper maneat in se, semper tamen manet in sua causa, quae est potentia et habitus. Habitus autem ex quibus conscientia informatur, etsi multi sint, omnes tamen efficaciam habent ab uno primo, scilicet ab habitu primorum principiorum, qui dicitur synderesis. Unde specialiter hic habitus interdum conscientia nominatur, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"0ff1555ffac33c5da98393378c7f94180541216fd0ec4cae91f8d6a5045c0a00","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T151052928810Z_35f786bc","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 79","source_sha256":"0ff1555ffac33c5da98393378c7f94180541216fd0ec4cae91f8d6a5045c0a00","sha256":"830cd495919a6cf6349fb9d9a1b4310224f462776c5753897d26f4a66eed4590","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:10:52.928824+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0002","text":"[I q. 79 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31715)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0003","text":"Next, inquiry is made concerning the intellective powers. Concerning this, thirteen questions are asked. First, whether the intellect is a power of the soul, or its essence. Second, if it is a power, whether it is a passive power. Third, if it is a passive power, whether it is necessary to posit an agent intellect. Fourth, whether it is something of the soul. Fifth, whether the agent intellect is one in all. Sixth, whether memory is in the intellect. Seventh, whether it is a power other than the intellect. Eighth, whether reason is a power other than the intellect. Ninth, whether higher and lower reason are diverse powers. Tenth, whether intelligence is a power other than the intellect. Eleventh, whether the speculative and practical intellects are diverse powers. Twelfth, whether synderesis is some power of the intellective part. Thirteenth, whether conscience is some power of the intellective part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0005","text":"[I q. 79 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31716)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0006","text":"The first is proceeded with thus. It seems that the intellect is not some power of the soul, but is its very essence. For intellect seems to be the same as mind. But mind is not a power of the soul, but its essence; for Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, IX: *mind and spirit are not said relatively, but signify essence*. Therefore intellect is the very essence of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0007","text":"[I q. 79 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31717)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0008","text":"Further, diverse genera of powers of the soul are not united in any one power, but only in the essence of the soul. But the appetitive and intellective are diverse genera of powers of the soul, as is said in *On the Soul*, II; yet they come together in the mind, because Augustine, in *On the Trinity*, X, places intelligence and will in the mind. Therefore mind and intellect are the very essence of the soul, and not some power of it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0009","text":"[I q. 79 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31718)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0010","text":"Further, according to Gregory, in the homily on the Ascension, man understands with the angels. But angels are called minds and intellects. Therefore man's mind and intellect are not some power of the soul, but the soul itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0011","text":"[I q. 79 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31719)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0012","text":"Further, it belongs to a substance to be intellective because it is immaterial. But the soul is immaterial through its essence. Therefore it seems that the soul is intellective through its essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0013","text":"[I q. 79 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31720)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0014","text":"But on the contrary, the Philosopher posits the intellective as a power of the soul, as is clear in *On the Soul*, II.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0015","text":"[I q. 79 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31721)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0016","text":"I answer that it must be said, according to what has been set forth, that the intellect is some power of the soul, and not the very essence of the soul. For only then is the essence itself of the thing operating the immediate principle of an operation, when the operation itself is its being; for just as a power is related to operation as to its act, so essence is related to being. But in God alone, understanding is the same as His being. Hence in God alone the intellect is His essence; but in other intellectual creatures, the intellect is a certain power of the one understanding.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0017","text":"[I q. 79 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31722)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0018","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that sense is sometimes taken for a power, and sometimes for the sensitive soul itself; for the sensitive soul is denominated by the name of its more principal power, which is sense. Similarly, the intellective soul is sometimes named by the name of intellect, as it were from its more principal power; as is said in *On the Soul*, I, that intellect is a certain substance. And Augustine also says in this way that mind is spirit, or essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0019","text":"[I q. 79 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31723)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0020","text":"To the second it must be said that the appetitive and intellective are diverse genera of powers of the soul, according to diverse accounts of their objects. But the appetitive partly agrees with the intellective, and partly with the sensitive, as regards the manner of operating through a bodily organ or without such an organ; for appetite follows apprehension. And accordingly Augustine places the will in the mind, and the Philosopher in reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0021","text":"[I q. 79 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31724)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0022","text":"To the third it must be said that in angels there is no power other than the intellective and the will, which follows upon intellect. And on this account an angel is called mind or intellect, because its whole power consists in this. But the soul has many other powers, such as the sensitive and nutritive; and therefore there is no likeness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0023","text":"[I q. 79 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31725)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0024","text":"To the fourth it must be said that the immateriality itself of a created intelligent substance is not its intellect; rather, from immateriality it has the power to understand. Hence it need not be that the intellect is the substance of the soul, but its virtue and power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0025","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0026","text":"[I q. 79 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31726)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0027","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the intellect is not a passive power. For each thing undergoes [passion] according to matter; but acts by reason of form. But the intellectual power follows upon the immateriality of the intelligent substance. Therefore it seems that the intellect is not a passive power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0028","text":"[I q. 79 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31727)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0029","text":"Moreover, the intellectual power is incorruptible, as was said above. But if the intellect is passive, it is corruptible, as is said in Book III of *On the Soul*. Therefore the intellectual power is not passive.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0030","text":"[I q. 79 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31728)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0031","text":"Moreover, the agent is more noble than the patient, as Augustine says in Book XII of *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, and Aristotle in Book III of *On the Soul*. But all the powers of the vegetative part are active, though they are the lowest among the powers of the soul. Therefore, much more are the intellectual powers, which are the highest, all active.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0032","text":"[I q. 79 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31729)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0033","text":"But on the contrary, the Philosopher says, in Book III of *On the Soul*, that *understanding is a kind of undergoing*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0034","text":"[I q. 79 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31730)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0035","text":"I answer that it must be said that undergoing is spoken of in three ways. In one way, most properly, namely, when something is removed from a thing which belongs to it according to its nature or according to its proper inclination; as when water loses coldness through heating, and when a man falls ill or becomes sad. In a second way, less properly, someone is said to undergo from the fact that something is cast off from him, whether it be suitable to him or unsuitable. And according to this, not only one who falls ill, but also one who is healed, is said to undergo; not only one who becomes sad, but also one who becomes joyful; or whenever someone is altered or moved in any way. In a third way, something is commonly said to undergo solely from the fact that what is in potency to something receives that to which it was in potency, without anything being cast off. According to this mode, everything which goes from potency into act can be said to undergo, even when it is perfected. And thus our understanding is an undergoing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0036","text":"This is apparent for the following reason. For the intellect, as was said above, has its operation concerning being universally. Therefore, whether the intellect is in act or in potency can be considered from the way in which the intellect stands in relation to universal being. For there is found an intellect which stands in relation to universal being as the act of all being; and such is the divine intellect, which is God's essence, in which all being pre-exists originally and virtually, as in the first cause. And therefore the divine intellect is not in potency, but is pure act. But no created intellect can stand as act in respect of the whole of universal being, because thus it would have to be infinite being. Hence every created intellect, by the very fact that it exists, is not the act of all intelligibles, but is related to the intelligibles themselves as potency to act. Now potency is related to act in two ways.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0037","text":"For there is a certain potency which is always perfected by act, as we said concerning the matter of the heavenly bodies. But there is another potency which is not always in act, but proceeds from potency into act, as is found in generable and corruptible things. Therefore the angelic intellect is always in act with respect to its intelligibles, on account of its proximity to the first intellect, which is pure act, as was said above. But the human intellect, which is lowest in the order of intellects and most remote from the perfection of the divine intellect, is in potency with respect to intelligibles, and in the beginning is like a blank tablet on which nothing is written, as the Philosopher says in Book III of *On the Soul*. This is plainly evident from the fact that at first we are intelligent only in potency, but afterwards become intelligent in act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0038","text":"Thus, then, it is clear that our understanding is a kind of undergoing, according to the third mode of undergoing. And consequently the intellect is a passive power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0039","text":"[I q. 79 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31731)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0040","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that objection proceeds from the first and second modes of undergoing, which belong properly to prime matter. But the third mode of undergoing belongs to whatever exists in potency and is brought into act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0041","text":"[I q. 79 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31732)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0042","text":"To the second, it must be said that, according to some, the passive intellect is called the sensitive appetite, in which are the passions of the soul; it is also called rational by participation in Book I of the *Ethics*, because it obeys reason. According to others, however, the passive intellect is called the cogitative power, which is termed particular reason. And in either way the passive can be understood according to the first two modes of undergoing, insofar as such an intellect, so called, is the act of some bodily organ. But the intellect which is in potency with respect to intelligibles, which Aristotle for this reason calls the possible intellect, is passive only in the third way, because it is not the act of a bodily organ. And therefore it is incorruptible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0043","text":"[I q. 79 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31733)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0044","text":"To the third, it must be said that the agent is more noble than the patient if action and passion are referred to the same thing, but not always if they are referred to different things. But the intellect is a passive power with respect to the whole of universal being. The vegetative power, however, is active with respect to a particular being, namely, the conjoined body. Hence nothing prevents such a passive power from being more noble than such an active power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0045","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0046","text":"[I q. 79 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31734)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0047","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that an agent intellect should not be posited. For as sense is related to sensibles, so our intellect is related to intelligibles. But because sense is in potency to sensibles, an agent sense is not posited, but only a patient sense. Therefore, since our intellect is in potency to intelligibles, it seems that an agent intellect ought not to be posited, but only a possible intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0048","text":"[I q. 79 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31735)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0049","text":"Moreover, if it is said that there is also some agent in sense, such as light: on the contrary, light is required for sight insofar as it makes the medium actually luminous, for color itself, according to itself, is a mover of the luminous. But in the operation of the intellect no medium is posited that must be made actual. Therefore, it is not necessary to posit an agent intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0050","text":"[I q. 79 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31736)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0051","text":"Moreover, the likeness of the agent is received in the patient according to the mode of the patient. But the possible intellect is an immaterial power. Therefore, its immateriality suffices for forms to be received in it immaterially. But a form is intelligible in act precisely from the fact that it is immaterial. Therefore, there is no necessity to posit an agent intellect in order that it make intelligible species actual.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0052","text":"[I q. 79 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31737)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0053","text":"But on the contrary, the Philosopher says, in *On the Soul* III, that *as in all nature, so also in the soul, there is something by which all things come to be, and something by which all things are made*. Therefore, an agent intellect must be posited.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0054","text":"[I q. 79 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31738)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0055","text":"I answer that it must be said that, according to Plato's opinion, there was no necessity to posit an agent intellect for making intelligibles actual, but perhaps for providing intelligible light to the one understanding, as will be said below. For Plato held that the forms of natural things subsist without matter, and consequently that they are intelligible, because a thing is actually intelligible from the fact that it is immaterial. And he called such things species, or ideas, by participation in which he said that corporeal matter too is formed, so that natural individuals might be constituted in their proper genera and species; and our intellects, so that they might have knowledge concerning the genera and species of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0056","text":"But because Aristotle did not posit that the forms of natural things subsist without matter, while forms existing in matter are not actually intelligible, it followed that the natures or forms of sensible things, which we understand, were not actually intelligible. But nothing is reduced from potency to act except by some being in act, just as sense is made actual by an actual sensible. Therefore, it was necessary to posit some power on the part of the intellect which would make intelligibles actual, through abstraction of species from material conditions. And this is the necessity for positing an agent intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0057","text":"[I q. 79 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31739)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0058","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that sensibles are found actually outside the soul, and therefore it was not necessary to posit an agent sense. Thus it is clear that, in the nutritive part, all powers are active; but in the sensitive part, all are passive; whereas in the intellectual part there is something active and something passive.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0059","text":"[I q. 79 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31740)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0060","text":"To the second, it must be said that there is a twofold opinion concerning the effect of light. For some say that light is required for sight in order to make colors actually visible. And according to this, similarly, and for the same reason, the agent intellect is required for understanding for which light is required for seeing. But according to others, light is required for seeing, not on account of colors, that they may become actually visible, but in order that the medium may become actually luminous, as the Commentator says in *On the Soul* II. And according to this, the likeness by which Aristotle assimilates the agent intellect to light is considered with respect to this: that, just as the latter is necessary for seeing, so the former is necessary for understanding; but not for the same reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0061","text":"[I q. 79 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31741)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0062","text":"To the third, it must be said that, given an agent, it can indeed happen that its likeness is received differently in different things because of their different dispositions. But if an agent does not pre-exist, the disposition of the recipient will contribute nothing to this. Now an actual intelligible is not something existing in the nature of things, as regards the nature of sensible things, which do not subsist apart from matter. And therefore, for understanding, the immateriality of the possible intellect would not suffice unless the agent intellect were present, which makes intelligibles actual by way of abstraction.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0063","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0064","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31742)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0065","text":"The fourth point is approached thus. It seems that the agent intellect is not something belonging to our soul. For the effect of the agent intellect is to illuminate for understanding. But this is done through something higher than the soul, according to John 1: *there was the true light, which enlightens every human being coming into this world*. Therefore, it seems that the agent intellect is not something belonging to the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0066","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31743)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0067","text":"Moreover, the Philosopher, in *On the Soul* III, attributes to the agent intellect that it does not sometimes understand and sometimes not understand. But our soul does not always understand; rather, it sometimes understands and sometimes does not understand. Therefore, the agent intellect is not something belonging to our soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0068","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31744)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0069","text":"Moreover, agent and patient suffice for acting. Therefore, if the possible intellect is something belonging to our soul, which is a passive power, and likewise the agent intellect, which is an active power, it follows that a human being will always be able to understand whenever he wills—which is clearly false. Therefore, the agent intellect is not something belonging to our soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0070","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31745)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0071","text":"Moreover, the Philosopher says, in *On the Soul* III, that the agent intellect is a substance existing in act. But nothing is, with respect to the same thing, both in act and in potency. Therefore, if the possible intellect, which is in potency to all intelligibles, is something belonging to our soul, it seems impossible that the agent intellect should be something belonging to our soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0072","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31746)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0073","text":"Moreover, if the agent intellect is something of our soul, it must be a power. For it is neither a passion nor a habit, since habits and passions do not have the character of an agent with respect to the passions of the soul; rather, a passion is the very action of a passive power, whereas a habit is something that follows from acts. But every power flows from the essence of the soul. Therefore it would follow that the agent intellect proceeded from the essence of the soul. And thus it would not be present in the soul by participation from some higher intellect, which is unfitting. Therefore the agent intellect is not something of our soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0074","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31747)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0075","text":"On the contrary, the Philosopher says in III *On the Soul* that *it is necessary that these differences be in the soul*, namely, the possible and the agent intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0076","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31748)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0077","text":"I answer that it must be said that the agent intellect of which the Philosopher speaks is something of the soul. To make this evident, it must be considered that above the human intellectual soul it is necessary to posit some higher intellect, from which the soul obtains the power of understanding. For that which participates in something, and that which is movable, and that which is imperfect, always presupposes before itself something that is such through its essence and that is immovable and perfect. But the human soul is called intellectual by participation in intellectual power, a sign of which is that it is not intellectual in its entirety, but according to some part of itself. It also attains to the understanding of truth with a certain discursion and movement, by reasoning. It also has imperfect understanding, both because it does not understand all things, and because, in those things which it understands, it proceeds from potency to act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0078","text":"Therefore there must be some higher intellect by which the soul is assisted in understanding. Accordingly, some have posited that this intellect, separate according to substance, is the agent intellect, which, as it were by illuminating the phantasms, makes them actually intelligible. But, granted that there is some such separate agent intellect, nevertheless it is necessary to posit in the human soul itself some power participated from that higher intellect, through which the human soul makes things actually intelligible. Thus also in other perfect natural things, besides the universal agent causes, there are proper powers bestowed upon individual perfect things, derived from the universal agents; for it is not the sun alone that generates a human being, but in a human being there is a generative power of a human being; and similarly in other perfect animals. But among lower things nothing is more perfect than the human soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0079","text":"Hence it must be said that there is in it some power derived from the higher intellect, through which it can illuminate phantasms. And we know this by experience, when we perceive that we abstract universal forms from particular conditions, which is to make things actually intelligible. But no action belongs to any thing except through some principle formally inhering in it; as was said above, when the possible intellect was under discussion. Therefore the power that is the principle of this action must be something in the soul. And therefore Aristotle compared the agent intellect to light, which is something received in the air. But Plato compared the separate intellect impressing upon our souls to the sun, as Themistius says in his commentary on the third book *On the Soul*. But the separate intellect, according to the teachings of our faith, is God Himself, who is the creator of the soul and in whom alone it is beatified, as will become clear below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0080","text":"Hence the human soul participates from Him in intellectual light, according to that of Psalm IV, *the light of thy countenance, O Lord, is stamped upon us*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0081","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31749)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0082","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that true light illuminates as a universal cause, from which the human soul participates in a certain particular power, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0083","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31750)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0084","text":"To the second, it must be said that the Philosopher does not say those words concerning the agent intellect, but concerning the intellect in act. Hence he had previously stated concerning it: knowledge in act is the same as the thing. Or, if it is understood concerning the agent intellect, this is said because our sometimes understanding and sometimes not understanding is not on the part of the agent intellect, but on the part of the intellect which is in potency.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0085","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31751)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0086","text":"To the third, it must be said that, if the agent intellect were compared to the possible intellect as an acting object to a power, as the actually visible to sight, it would follow that we would understand all things at once, since the agent intellect is that by which all things are made. But now it is not related as an object, but as making objects actual; and for this there is required, besides the presence of the agent intellect, the presence of phantasms, a good disposition of the sensitive powers, and exercise in work of this kind; because through one thing understood other things are also made understood, as propositions through terms, and conclusions through first principles. And in this respect, it makes no difference whether the agent intellect is something of the soul or something separate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0087","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31752)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0088","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the intellectual soul is indeed actually immaterial, but is in potency toward determinate species of things. Phantasms, on the other hand, are indeed actually likenesses of certain species, but are potentially immaterial. Hence nothing prevents one and the same soul, insofar as it is actually immaterial, from having some power through which it makes things actually immaterial by abstracting them from the conditions of individual matter—which power is called the agent intellect—and another power receptive of such species, which is called the possible intellect, insofar as it is in potency toward such species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0089","text":"[I q. 79 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31753)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0090","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that, since the essence of the soul is immaterial, created by the supreme intellect, nothing prevents the power participated from the supreme intellect, through which it abstracts from matter, from proceeding from its essence, just as its other powers do.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0091","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0092","text":"[I q. 79 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31754)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0093","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth. It seems that the agent intellect is one in all. For nothing that is separate from a body is multiplied according to the multiplication of bodies. But the agent intellect is separate, as is said in III *On the Soul*. Therefore it is not multiplied in the many bodies of human beings, but is one in all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0094","text":"[I q. 79 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31755)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0095","text":"Moreover, the agent intellect makes the universal, which is one in many. But that which is the cause of unity is more one. Therefore the agent intellect is one in all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0096","text":"[I q. 79 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31756)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0097","text":"Moreover, all human beings agree in the first conceptions of the intellect. But they assent to these through the agent intellect. Therefore, all agree in one agent intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0098","text":"[I q. 79 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31757)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0099","text":"But on the contrary, the Philosopher says, in *On the Soul* III, that the agent intellect is like light. But light is not the same in different things illuminated. Therefore, the agent intellect is not the same in different human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0100","text":"[I q. 79 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31758)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0101","text":"I answer that the truth of this question depends on what has been set forth above. For if the agent intellect were not something belonging to the soul, but were a certain separate substance, there would be one agent intellect for all human beings. And this is what those who maintain the unity of the agent intellect understand. But if the agent intellect is something belonging to the soul, as a certain power of it, it is necessary to say that there are several agent intellects, according to the plurality of souls, which are multiplied according to the multiplication of human beings, as was said above. For it cannot be that one and the same power numerically belongs to different substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0102","text":"[I q. 79 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31759)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0103","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the Philosopher proves that the agent intellect is separate because the possible intellect is separate; for, as he himself says, the agent is more noble than the patient. Now the possible intellect is said to be separate because it is not the act of any bodily organ. And in this manner the agent intellect too is said to be separate, not as though it were some separate substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0104","text":"[I q. 79 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31760)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0105","text":"To the second it must be said that the agent intellect causes the universal by abstracting from matter. But for this it is not required that it be one in all those possessing intellect, but that it be one in all according to its relation to all things from which it abstracts the universal, with respect to which the universal is one. And this belongs to the agent intellect insofar as it is immaterial.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0106","text":"[I q. 79 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31761)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0107","text":"To the third it must be said that all things which are of one species share in the action consequent upon the nature of the species, and consequently in the power which is the principle of the action—not because it is numerically the same in all. But to know the first intelligibles is an action consequent upon the human species. Hence it is necessary that all human beings share in the power which is the principle of this action, and this is the power of the agent intellect. Yet it need not be numerically the same in all. It must nevertheless be derived in all from one principle. And thus that sharing of human beings in the first intelligibles demonstrates the unity of the separate intellect, which Plato compares to the sun, but not the unity of the agent intellect, which Aristotle compares to light.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0108","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0109","text":"[I q. 79 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31762)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0110","text":"The sixth point is approached thus. It seems that memory is not in the intellectual part of the soul. For Augustine says, in *On the Trinity* XII, that those things which are not common to human beings and beasts belong to the higher part of the soul. But memory is common to human beings and beasts; for he says in the same place that *beasts can perceive bodily things through the senses of the body, and commit them to memory*. Therefore memory does not belong to the intellectual part of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0111","text":"[I q. 79 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31763)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0112","text":"Moreover, memory is of the past. But the past is spoken of according to some determinate time. Therefore memory is cognitive of something under determinate time, which is to cognize something under here and now. But this belongs not to intellect but to sense. Therefore memory is not in the intellectual part, but only in the sensitive part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0113","text":"[I q. 79 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31764)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0114","text":"Moreover, in memory are preserved the species of things which are not actually thought. But this cannot occur in the intellect, because the intellect becomes actual through being informed by an intelligible species; but for the intellect to be actual is itself for it to understand actually; and thus the intellect actually understands all those things whose species it has within itself. Therefore memory is not in the intellectual part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0115","text":"[I q. 79 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31765)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0116","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity* X, that *memory, understanding, and will are one mind*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0117","text":"[I q. 79 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31766)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0118","text":"I answer that, since it belongs to the nature of memory to preserve the species of things that are not actually apprehended, one must first consider whether intelligible species can thus be preserved in the intellect. For Avicenna held this to be impossible. For he said that this occurred in the sensitive part, as regards certain powers, insofar as they are acts of bodily organs, in which certain species can be preserved without actual apprehension. But in the intellect, which lacks a bodily organ, nothing exists except intelligibly. Hence that whose likeness exists in the intellect must be understood actually.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0119","text":"Thus, according to him, as soon as someone actually ceases to understand some thing, the species of that thing ceases to be in the intellect; but if he wishes to understand that thing again, he must turn himself toward the agent intellect, which he posits as a separate substance, so that intelligible species may flow from it into the possible intellect. And from the exercise and practice of turning oneself toward the agent intellect, according to him, a certain aptitude is left in the possible intellect for turning itself toward the agent intellect, which he called the habit of knowledge. Therefore, according to this position, nothing is preserved in the intellectual part which is not actually understood. Hence, in this way, memory cannot be posited in the intellectual part. But this opinion manifestly conflicts with the statements of Aristotle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0120","text":"For he says, in *On the Soul* III, that, when the possible intellect *thus becomes each thing as one who knows, it is called according to act; and this occurs when it is able to operate through itself. It is indeed then in potency in a certain way; yet not similarly as before learning or discovering*. Now the possible intellect is said to become each thing insofar as it receives the species of individual things. Therefore, from receiving the species of intelligibles, it has the ability to operate when it wills, but not to operate always, because even then it is in potency in a certain way, though otherwise than before understanding—namely, in the way in which one who knows habitually is in potency to consider actually. The aforesaid position also conflicts with reason. For what is received in something is received in it according to the mode of the receiver. But the intellect is of a more stable and immovable nature than corporeal matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0121","text":"Therefore, if corporeal matter not only retains the forms which it receives while it acts actually through them, but also after it has ceased to act through them; much more strongly does the intellect receive intelligible species immovably and without loss, whether received from sensibles or even emanating from some higher intellect. Thus, therefore, if memory is understood solely as the power preserving species, one must say that memory is in the intellective part. But if it belongs to the nature of memory that its object is the past, as past, memory will not be in the intellective part, but only in the sensitive part, which apprehends particulars. For the past, as past, since it signifies being under a determinate time, pertains to the condition of the particular.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0122","text":"[I q. 79 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31767)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0123","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that memory, insofar as it preserves species, is not common to us and the brute animals. For species are preserved not in the sensitive part of the soul alone, but rather in the composite; since the memorative power is the act of a certain organ. But the intellect is in itself preservative of species, apart from the accompaniment of a bodily organ. Hence the Philosopher says, in *On the Soul* III, that *the soul is the place of species—not the whole soul, but the intellect*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0124","text":"[I q. 79 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31768)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0125","text":"To the second it must be said that pastness can be referred to two things: namely, to the object that is known, and to the act of knowing. These two are indeed conjoined at once in the sensitive part, which apprehends something by being altered by a present sensible; hence an animal simultaneously remembers that it sensed before in the past, and that it sensed some past sensible. But as regards the intellective part, pastness is accidental, and does not belong per se, on the side of the object of the intellect. For the intellect understands man insofar as he is man; but it is accidental to man, insofar as he is man, to be in the present, in the past, or in the future. On the side of the act, however, pastness can be taken per se even in the intellect, just as in sense. For our soul's understanding is a certain particular act, existing at this or that time, according as a man is said to understand now, or yesterday, or tomorrow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0126","text":"And this is not incompatible with intellectuality, because such understanding, although it is something particular, is nevertheless an immaterial act, as was said above concerning the intellect; and therefore, just as the intellect understands itself, although it itself is a certain singular intellect, so it understands its own understanding, which is a singular act existing in the past, the present, or the future. Thus, therefore, the nature of memory, insofar as it is of past things, is preserved in the intellect, insofar as it understands that it previously understood, but not insofar as it understands the past as it is here and now.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0127","text":"[I q. 79 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31769)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0128","text":"To the third it must be said that the intelligible species is sometimes in the intellect only potentially, and then the intellect is said to be in potency. But sometimes it is there according to the ultimate completion of act, and then it actually understands. And sometimes it stands in a middle manner between potency and act, and then the intellect is said to be in habit. And in this manner the intellect preserves species, even when it does not actually understand.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0129","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0130","text":"[I q. 79 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31770)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0131","text":"We proceed thus to the seventh article. It seems that intellective memory is one power and the intellect another. For Augustine, in *On the Trinity* X, posits memory, understanding, and will in the mind. But it is manifest that memory is a power other than will. Therefore, similarly, it is other than intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0132","text":"[I q. 79 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31771)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0133","text":"Further, the same is the basis for the distinction of the powers of the sensitive and intellective parts. But memory in the sensitive part is a power other than sense, as was said above. Therefore, memory in the intellective part is a power other than intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0134","text":"[I q. 79 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31772)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0135","text":"Further, according to Augustine, memory, understanding, and will are equal to one another, and one arises from another. But this could not be if memory were the same power as intellect. Therefore, it is not the same power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0136","text":"[I q. 79 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31773)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0137","text":"On the contrary, it belongs to the nature of memory that it be a treasury or place preserving species. But the Philosopher, in *On the Soul* III, attributes this to the intellect, as was said. Therefore, in the intellective part memory is not a power other than intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0138","text":"[I q. 79 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31774)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0139","text":"I answer that it must be said that, as was said above, the powers of the soul are distinguished according to diverse accounts of their objects, because the nature of any power consists in its relation to that toward which it is said to be directed, which is its object. It was also said above that, if some power according to its proper nature is ordered to some object according to the common account of the object, that power will not be diversified according to the diversities of particular differences; just as the visual power, which regards its object according to the account of the colored, is not diversified by the difference between white and black. But the intellect regards its object according to the common account of being, because the possible intellect is that by which all things become. Hence, according to no difference among beings is the difference of the possible intellect diversified.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0140","text":"Nevertheless, the power of the agent intellect and that of the possible intellect are diversified, because with respect to the same object there must be one principle that is the active power, which makes the object to be in act, and another that is the passive power, which is moved by the object existing in act. And thus the active power is compared to its object as being in act to being in potency, whereas the passive power is compared conversely to its object as being in potency to being in act. Thus, therefore, there can be no other difference of powers in the intellect than the possible and the agent. Hence it is clear that memory is not a power other than intellect; for it belongs to the nature of a passive power to preserve, just as to receive.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0141","text":"[I q. 79 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31775)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0142","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although in III, dist. I of the *Sentences* it is said that memory, understanding, and will are three powers, nevertheless this is not according to Augustine's intention, for he expressly says in *On the Trinity* XIV that *if memory, understanding, and will are taken insofar as they are always present to the soul, whether they are thought of or not thought of, they seem to pertain to memory alone. But I now call understanding that by which we understand when thinking; and will, or love or affection, that which joins this offspring and parent.* From this it is clear that Augustine does not take these three for three powers; rather, he takes memory for the habitual retention of the soul, understanding for the act of the intellect, and will for the act of the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0143","text":"[I q. 79 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31776)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0144","text":"To the second it must be said that past and present can be proper differences diversifying sensitive powers, but not intellective powers, for the reason stated above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0145","text":"[I q. 79 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31777)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0146","text":"To the third, it must be said that understanding arises from memory, as an act from a habit. And in this way it is also equal to it; but not as a power to a power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0147","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0148","text":"[I q. 79 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31778)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0149","text":"We proceed thus to the eighth. It seems that reason is a power other than intellect. For in the book *On Spirit and Soul* it is said: *when we wish to ascend from lower things to higher, sense first presents itself to us, then imagination, then reason, then intellect*. Therefore reason is a power other than intellect, just as imagination is other than reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0150","text":"[I q. 79 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31779)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0151","text":"Moreover, Boethius says in the book *On Consolation* that intellect is compared to reason as eternity to time. But to be in eternity and to be in time do not belong to the same power. Therefore reason and intellect are not the same power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0152","text":"[I q. 79 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31780)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0153","text":"Moreover, man shares intellect with the angels, but sense with brute animals. But reason, which is proper to man, by which he is called a rational animal, is a power other than sense. Therefore, by equal reasoning, it is a power other than intellect, which properly belongs to angels, whence they too are called intellectual.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0154","text":"[I q. 79 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31781)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0155","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in III *On Genesis according to the Letter*, that *that by which man excels irrational animals is reason, or mind, or intelligence, or whatever other term may more suitably be applied*. Therefore reason, intellect, and mind are one power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0156","text":"[I q. 79 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31782)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0157","text":"I answer that it must be said that reason and intellect in man cannot be diverse powers. This is plainly known if the act of each is considered. For to understand is simply to apprehend intelligible truth. But to reason is to proceed from one thing understood to another, in order to know intelligible truth. And therefore angels, who possess knowledge of intelligible truth perfectly according to the mode of their nature, have no need to proceed from one thing to another; rather, they apprehend the truth of things simply and without discursion, as Dionysius says in chapter VII of *On the Divine Names*. But men arrive at the knowledge of intelligible truth by proceeding from one thing to another, as is said in the same place, and therefore are called rational. It is thus clear that reasoning is related to understanding as being moved to resting, or acquiring to having; one of which belongs to the perfect, the other to the imperfect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0158","text":"And because motion always proceeds from what is immobile and terminates in something at rest, it follows that human reasoning, according to the way of inquiry or discovery, proceeds from certain things understood simply, which are first principles; and again, in the way of judgment, by resolving it returns to first principles, by which it examines what has been discovered. Now it is clear that resting and being moved are not reduced to diverse powers, but to one and the same power, even in natural things, because by the same nature something is moved toward a place and rests in a place. Therefore, much more do we understand and reason by the same power. And thus it is clear that in man reason and intellect are the same power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0158"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0159","text":"[I q. 79 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31783)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0159"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0160","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that enumeration is made according to the order of acts, not according to a distinction of powers. Although that book is not of great authority.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0160"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0161","text":"[I q. 79 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31784)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0161"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0162","text":"The answer to the second is clear from what has been said. For eternity is compared to time as the immobile to the mobile. And therefore Boethius compared intellect to eternity, but reason to time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0162"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0163","text":"[I q. 79 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31785)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0163"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0164","text":"To the third, it must be said that other animals are so far beneath man that they cannot attain to the knowledge of the truth which reason investigates. Man, however, attains to the knowledge of intelligible truth, which angels know, but imperfectly. And therefore the cognitive power of angels is not of another genus from the cognitive power of reason, but is compared to it as the perfect to the imperfect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0164"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0165","text":"## Article 9","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0165"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0166","text":"[I q. 79 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31786)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0166"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0167","text":"We proceed thus to the ninth. It seems that higher and lower reason are diverse powers. For Augustine says, in XII *On the Trinity*, that the image of the Trinity is in the higher part of reason, but not in the lower. But the parts of the soul are its powers themselves. Therefore higher and lower reason are two powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0167"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0168","text":"[I q. 79 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31787)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0168"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0169","text":"Moreover, nothing arises from itself. But lower reason arises from higher reason, and is regulated and directed by it. Therefore higher reason is a power other than lower reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0169"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0170","text":"[I q. 79 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31788)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0170"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0171","text":"Moreover, the Philosopher says, in Ethics VI, that the scientific part of the soul, by which the soul knows necessary things, is a different principle and a different part of the soul from the opinative and ratiocinative part, by which it knows contingent things. And he proves this by the fact that *a part of the soul generically different is ordered to things generically different*; but the contingent and the necessary are generically different, as are the corruptible and the incorruptible. But since the necessary is the same as the eternal, and the temporal the same as the contingent, it seems that what the Philosopher calls the scientific is the same as the higher part of reason, which according to Augustine is directed to beholding and consulting eternal things; and that what the Philosopher calls the ratiocinative or opinative is the same as lower reason, which according to Augustine is directed to disposing temporal things. Therefore higher reason and lower reason are different powers of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0171"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0172","text":"[I q. 79 a. 9 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31789)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0172"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0173","text":"Moreover, Damascene says that *from imagination opinion arises; then the mind, judging the opinion, whether it be true or false, judges the truth; whence the mind is also so called from measuring. But concerning those things which have already been truly judged and determined, there is said to be understanding*. Thus the opinative, which is lower reason, is other than mind and understanding, by which higher reason can be understood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0173"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0174","text":"[I q. 79 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31790)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0174"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0175","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in On the Trinity XII, that higher and lower reason are distinguished only by their offices. Therefore they are not two powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0175"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0176","text":"[I q. 79 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31791)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0176"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0177","text":"I answer that it must be said that higher and lower reason, according as they are understood by Augustine, can in no way be two powers of the soul. For he says that higher reason is that which is directed to beholding or consulting eternal things: to beholding them, indeed, insofar as it contemplates them in themselves; but to consulting them insofar as it receives from them rules for action. But lower reason is called by him that which is directed to temporal things. Now these two, namely temporal and eternal things, are related to our cognition in this way: one of them is a means for knowing the other. For, according to the way of discovery, through temporal things we arrive at knowledge of eternal things, according to that saying of the Apostle, Romans 1: *the invisible things of God are clearly seen, being understood by the things that are made*; but in the way of judgment, through eternal things already known we judge concerning temporal things, and according to eternal principles we dispose temporal things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0177"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0178","text":"But it can happen that the means, and that to which one arrives through the means, pertain to different habits, just as first indemonstrable principles pertain to the habit of understanding, whereas conclusions deduced from them pertain to the habit of science. And therefore from the principles of geometry one can draw a conclusion in another science, for example, in perspective. But it belongs to the same power of reason to deal with both the means and the ultimate. For the act of reason is as it were a movement, proceeding from one thing to another; but it is the same movable thing that, passing through the means, reaches the terminus. Hence higher and lower reason are one and the same power of reason. But they are distinguished, according to Augustine, by the offices of their acts and by different habits; for wisdom is attributed to higher reason, but science to lower reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0178"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0179","text":"[I q. 79 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31792)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0179"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0180","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that a part can be so called according to any ground of division. Therefore, insofar as reason is divided according to different offices, higher and lower reason are called parts, and not because they are different powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0180"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0181","text":"[I q. 79 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31793)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0181"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0182","text":"To the second, it must be said that lower reason is said to be derived from higher reason, or to be regulated by it, insofar as the principles which lower reason uses are derived and directed from the principles of higher reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0182"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0183","text":"[I q. 79 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31794)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0183"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0184","text":"To the third, it must be said that the scientific part of which the Philosopher speaks is not the same as higher reason, for knowable necessary things are also found in temporal things, concerning which natural and mathematical science are concerned. But the opinative and ratiocinative are less extensive than lower reason, because they concern only contingent things. Nevertheless, it must not be said simply that there is one power by which the intellect knows necessary things, and another by which it knows contingent things, because it knows both according to the same formal aspect of the object, namely according to the aspect of being and truth. Hence it knows necessary things, which have perfect being in truth, perfectly, as reaching their quiddity, through which it demonstrates their proper accidents. But it knows contingent things imperfectly, just as they have imperfect being and truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0184"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0185","text":"But the perfect and the imperfect in an act do not differentiate a power; rather, they differentiate acts as to their mode of acting, and consequently the principles of acts and the habits themselves. And therefore the Philosopher posited two parts of the soul, the scientific and the ratiocinative, not because they are two powers, but because they are distinguished according to a different aptitude for receiving different habits, whose diversity he intends there to investigate. For contingent and necessary things, although they differ according to their proper genera, nevertheless agree in the common notion of being, to which the intellect is directed, and to which they stand differently according to the perfect and the imperfect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0185"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0186","text":"[I q. 79 a. 9 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31795)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0186"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0187","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that Damascene's distinction is according to a diversity of acts, not according to a diversity of powers. For opinion signifies an act of the intellect that inclines toward one side of a contradiction with fear of the other. But to judge or to measure is an act of the intellect applying certain principles to the examination of things proposed. And from this the name of mind is taken. But to understand is to adhere, with a certain approval, to things that have been judged.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0187"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0188","text":"## Article 10","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0188"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0189","text":"[I q. 79 a. 10 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31796)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0189"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0190","text":"We proceed thus to the tenth article. It seems that intelligence is a power other than intellect. For it is said in the book On Spirit and Soul that *when we wish to ascend from lower things to higher things, sense first presents itself to us, then imagination, then reason, afterward intellect, and afterward intelligence*. But imagination and sense are different powers. Therefore intellect and intelligence are also different powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0190"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0191","text":"[I q. 79 a. 10 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31797)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0191"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0192","text":"Moreover, Boethius says, in Consolation V, that *sense beholds man in one way, imagination in another, reason in another, and intelligence in another*. But intellect is the same power as reason. Therefore it seems that intelligence is a power other than intellect, just as reason is a power other than imagination and sense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0192"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0193","text":"[I q. 79 a. 10 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31798)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0193"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0194","text":"Moreover, acts are prior to powers, as is said in *On the Soul* II. But intelligence is a certain act distinct from the other acts that are attributed to the intellect. For Damascene says that *the first movement is called intelligence; that which is intelligence concerning something is called intention; that which persists and configures the soul to what is understood is called thought; but thought remaining in the same thing, and examining and judging itself, is called phronesis* (that is, wisdom); *and phronesis, when extended, produces thought, that is, discourse inwardly arranged; from which, they say, discourse narrated through the tongue proceeds*. Therefore, intelligence seems to be a special power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0194"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0195","text":"[I q. 79 a. 10 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31799)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0195"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0196","text":"On the contrary, the Philosopher says, in *On the Soul* III, that *intelligence is of indivisibles, in which there is no falsity*. But to know things of this kind pertains to the intellect. Therefore intelligence is not another power besides the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0196"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0197","text":"[I q. 79 a. 10 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31800)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0197"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0198","text":"I answer that it must be said that the name intelligence properly signifies the very act of the intellect which is to understand. In certain books translated from Arabic, however, the separate substances which we call Angels are called intelligences; perhaps because such substances always understand actually. In books translated from Greek, however, they are called intellects or minds. Therefore intelligence is not distinguished from intellect as power from power, but as act from power. For such a division is found also among the philosophers. For sometimes they posit four intellects: namely, the agent intellect, the possible intellect, the intellect in habit, and the acquired intellect. Of these four, the agent and possible intellects are diverse powers, just as in all things there is one active power and another passive power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0198"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0199","text":"But the other three are distinguished according to three states of the possible intellect, which is sometimes only in potency, and thus is called possible; but sometimes in first act, which is knowledge, and thus is called intellect in habit; and sometimes in second act, which is to consider, and thus is called intellect in act, or acquired intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0199"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0200","text":"[I q. 79 a. 10 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31801)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0200"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0201","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, if that authority is to be accepted, intelligence is posited for the act of the intellect. And thus it is divided against intellect as act against power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0201"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0202","text":"[I q. 79 a. 10 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31802)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0202"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0203","text":"To the second, it must be said that Boethius takes intelligence for the act of the intellect which transcends the act of reason. Hence he says in the same place that *reason belongs only to the human race, just as intelligence belongs only to the divine*; for it is proper to God to understand all things without any investigation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0203"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0204","text":"[I q. 79 a. 10 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31803)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0204"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0205","text":"To the third, it must be said that all those acts which Damascene enumerates belong to one power, namely, the intellective power. This first simply apprehends something, and this act is called intelligence. Secondly, it orders what it apprehends to knowing or doing something else, and this is called intention. But while it persists in the inquiry into that which it intends, it is called thought. But while it examines that which has been thought out in relation to certain things, it is said to know or to be wise; this is phronesis, or wisdom, for it belongs to wisdom to judge, as is said in *Metaphysics* I. But when it has something as certain, as though examined, it thinks how it may make this manifest to others; and this is the arrangement of inward discourse, from which outward speech proceeds. For not every difference of acts diversifies powers, but only that which cannot be reduced to the same principle, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0205"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0206","text":"## Article 11","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0206"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0207","text":"[I q. 79 a. 11 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31804)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0207"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0208","text":"We proceed thus to the eleventh article. It seems that the speculative and practical intellects are diverse powers. For the apprehensive and the motive are diverse genera of powers, as is clear in *On the Soul* II. But the speculative intellect is apprehensive only, whereas the practical intellect is motive. Therefore they are diverse powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0208"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0209","text":"[I q. 79 a. 11 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31805)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0209"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0210","text":"Moreover, a diverse account of the object diversifies a power. But the object of the speculative intellect is the true, whereas that of the practical is the good; and these differ in account. Therefore the speculative and practical intellects are diverse powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0210"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0211","text":"[I q. 79 a. 11 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31806)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0211"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0212","text":"Moreover, in the intellective part the practical intellect is related to the speculative as the estimative power is to the imaginative power in the sensitive part. But the estimative differs from the imaginative as power from power, as was said above. Therefore so too does the practical intellect differ from the speculative.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0212"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0213","text":"[I q. 79 a. 11 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31807)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0213"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0214","text":"On the contrary, it is said in *On the Soul* III that the speculative intellect becomes practical by extension. But one power is not changed into another. Therefore the speculative and practical intellects are not diverse powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0214"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0215","text":"[I q. 79 a. 11 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31808)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0215"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0216","text":"I answer that it must be said that the practical and speculative intellects are not diverse powers. The reason for this is that, as was said above, that which is accidental to the account of the object at which some power is directed does not diversify the power; for it is accidental to what is colored that it be a human being, or large or small; hence all such things are apprehended by the same visual power. But it is accidental to something apprehended by the intellect that it be ordered to work, or not ordered to work. Yet the speculative and practical intellects differ in this respect. For the speculative intellect is that which does not order what it apprehends to work, but only to the consideration of truth; whereas the practical intellect is called that which orders what it apprehends to work. And this is what the Philosopher says in *On the Soul* III: that *the speculative differs from the practical by its end*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0216"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0217","text":"Hence each is also denominated from its end: the one indeed speculative, the other practical, that is, operative.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0217"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0218","text":"[I q. 79 a. 11 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31809)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0218"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0219","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that the practical intellect is motive, not as executing motion, but as directing toward motion. This belongs to it according to the mode of its apprehension.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0219"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0220","text":"[I q. 79 a. 11 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31810)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0220"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0221","text":"To the second it must be said that the true and the good mutually include one another; for the true is a certain good, otherwise it would not be desirable; and the good is a certain true, otherwise it would not be intelligible. Therefore, just as the object of appetite can be the true, insofar as it has the character of good, as when someone desires to know the truth, so the object of the practical intellect is a good orderable to work, under the aspect of the true. For the practical intellect knows truth, just as the speculative intellect does; but it orders the truth known to work.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0221"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0222","text":"[I q. 79 a. 11 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31811)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0222"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0223","text":"To the third it must be said that many differences diversify the sensitive powers which do not diversify the intellectual powers, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0223"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0224","text":"## Article 12","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0224"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0225","text":"[I q. 79 a. 12 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31812)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0225"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0226","text":"We proceed thus to the twelfth. It seems that synderesis is a certain special power distinct from the others. For things that fall under one division seem to be of one genus. But in Jerome's Gloss on Ezekiel I, synderesis is divided off against the irascible, the concupiscible, and the rational, which are certain powers. Therefore synderesis is a certain power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0226"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0227","text":"[I q. 79 a. 12 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31813)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0227"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0228","text":"Moreover, opposites belong to one genus. But synderesis and sensuality seem to be opposed, because synderesis always inclines toward good, whereas sensuality always inclines toward evil; hence it is signified by the serpent, as is clear from Augustine, On the Trinity, XII. Therefore it seems that synderesis is a power, just as sensuality is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0228"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0229","text":"[I q. 79 a. 12 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31814)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0229"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0230","text":"Moreover, Augustine says, in the book On Free Choice, that in natural judgment there are present certain *rules and seeds of virtues, true and unchangeable*; and these we call synderesis. Therefore, since the unchangeable rules by which we judge pertain to reason according to its higher part, as Augustine says in On the Trinity XII, it seems that synderesis is the same as reason. And thus it is a certain power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0230"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0231","text":"[I q. 79 a. 12 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31815)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0231"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0232","text":"*On the contrary, rational powers are related to opposites*, according to the Philosopher. But synderesis is not related to opposites, but inclines only toward good. Therefore synderesis is not a power. For if it were a power, it would have to be a rational power, since it is not found in brute animals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0232"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0233","text":"[I q. 79 a. 12 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31816)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0233"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0234","text":"I answer that it must be said that synderesis is not a power, but a habit, although some have held synderesis to be a certain power higher than reason; while others have said that it is reason itself, not insofar as it is reason, but insofar as it is nature. For the clarification of this, it must be considered that, as was said above, human reasoning, since it is a certain motion, proceeds from the understanding of certain things, namely, things naturally known without the inquiry of reason, as from a certain immovable principle; and it also terminates in understanding, insofar as we judge, through principles naturally known through themselves, concerning those things which we discover by reasoning. But it is clear that, just as speculative reason reasons concerning speculative matters, so practical reason reasons concerning matters to be done. Therefore, just as the principles of speculative matters, so too the principles of matters to be done must be naturally implanted in us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0234"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0235","text":"But the first principles of speculative matters naturally implanted in us do not pertain to any special power, but to a certain special habit, which is called the understanding of principles, as is clear in Ethics VI. Hence the principles of matters to be done naturally implanted in us likewise do not pertain to a special power, but to a special natural habit, which we call synderesis. Hence synderesis is said to urge toward good and to murmur against evil, insofar as through first principles we proceed to discover, and judge the things discovered. Therefore it is clear that synderesis is not a power, but a natural habit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0235"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0236","text":"[I q. 79 a. 12 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31817)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0236"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0237","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that Jerome's division is considered according to the diversity of acts, not according to the diversity of powers. But diverse acts can belong to one power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0237"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0238","text":"[I q. 79 a. 12 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31818)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0238"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0239","text":"To the second it must be said that likewise the opposition of sensuality and synderesis is considered according to the opposition of acts, not as that of diverse species of one genus.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0239"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0240","text":"[I q. 79 a. 12 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31819)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0240"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0241","text":"To the third it must be said that unchangeable principles of this kind are the first principles of things to be done, concerning which it is not possible to err; and they are attributed to reason as a power, and to synderesis as a habit. Hence we judge naturally by both, namely, reason and synderesis.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0241"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0242","text":"## Article 13","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0242"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0243","text":"[I q. 79 a. 13 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31820)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0243"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0244","text":"We proceed thus to the thirteenth article. It seems that conscience is a certain power. For Origen says that *conscience is a corrective spirit and pedagogue joined to the soul, by which it is separated from evils and adheres to goods*. But spirit in the soul denotes some power, or the mind itself, according to Ephesians IV, *be renewed in the spirit of your mind*; or the imagination itself; whence an imaginary vision is also called spiritual, as is clear from Augustine, XII On the Literal Meaning of Genesis. Therefore conscience is a certain power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0244"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0245","text":"[I q. 79 a. 13 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31821)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0245"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0246","text":"Further, nothing is the subject of sin except a power of the soul. But conscience is the subject of sin, for it is said concerning certain people in Titus I that *their mind and conscience are defiled*. Therefore it seems that conscience is a power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0246"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0247","text":"[I q. 79 a. 13 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31822)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0247"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0248","text":"Further, conscience must be either an act, or a habit, or a power. But it is not an act, because it would not always remain in man. Nor is it a habit, for conscience would not be one thing, but many; for we are directed in acting by many cognitive habits. Therefore conscience is a power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0248"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0249","text":"[I q. 79 a. 13 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31823)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0249"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0250","text":"On the contrary, conscience can be laid aside, but a power cannot. Therefore conscience is not a power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0250"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0251","text":"[I q. 79 a. 13 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31824)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0251"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0252","text":"I answer that, properly speaking, conscience is not a power but an act. This is clear both from the meaning of the name and also from those things which, according to common usage of speech, are attributed to conscience. For conscience, according to the propriety of the word, implies an ordering of knowledge to something, for conscience is called knowledge with another. But the application of knowledge to something takes place through some act. Hence from this meaning of the name it is clear that conscience is an act. The same also appears from those things attributed to conscience. For conscience is said to bear witness, to bind or urge, and also to accuse or sting back, or reprove. And all these follow upon the application of some knowledge or science of ours to the things we do. This application indeed occurs in three ways. In one way, insofar as we recognize that we have done or not done something, according to Eccles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0252"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0253","text":"VII, *your conscience knows that you have often cursed others*, and in this respect conscience is said to bear witness. In another way it is applied insofar as through our conscience we judge something to be done or not to be done, and in this respect conscience is said to urge or bind. In a third way it is applied insofar as through conscience we judge that something which has been done has been done well or not well, and in this respect conscience is said to excuse or accuse, or to sting back. But it is clear that all these follow upon the actual application of knowledge to the things we do. Hence, properly speaking, conscience names an act. Yet because a habit is a principle of act, sometimes the name conscience is attributed to the first natural habit, namely synderesis, just as Jerome, in the Gloss on Ezek.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0253"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0254","text":"I, calls synderesis conscience; and Basil calls it the natural judging faculty; and Damascene says that it is the law of our intellect. For it is customary that causes and effects be named from one another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0254"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0255","text":"[I q. 79 a. 13 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31825)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0255"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0256","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that conscience is called spirit insofar as spirit is taken for mind, because it is a certain dictate of the mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0256"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0257","text":"[I q. 79 a. 13 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31826)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0257"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0258","text":"To the second it must be said that defilement is said to be in conscience, not as in a subject, but as the known is in knowledge, namely insofar as someone knows himself to be defiled.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0258"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0259","text":"[I q. 79 a. 13 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31827)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0259"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0260","text":"To the third it must be said that an act, although it does not always remain in itself, nevertheless always remains in its cause, which is power and habit. But the habits from which conscience is informed, although they are many, nevertheless all have their efficacy from one first habit, namely, from the habit of first principles, which is called synderesis. Hence this habit in particular is sometimes called conscience, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_71:P0260"]}],"source_digest":"539c9d8c83ef2a377f28113977ec471ee65ac5a67252e3dc884a7b4dc9d018a2","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 79"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 79","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72","title":"First Part · Question 80","order":81,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_72","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 80 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31828)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de potentiis appetitivis. Et circa hoc consideranda sunt quatuor, primo, de appetitivo in communi; secundo, de sensualitate; tertio, de voluntate; quarto, de libero arbitrio. Circa primum quaeruntur duo. Primo, utrum debeat poni appetitus aliqua specialis potentia animae. Secundo, utrum appetitus dividatur in appetitum sensitivum et intellectivum, sicut in potentias diversas.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 80 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31829)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod appetitus non sit aliqua specialis animae potentia. Ad ea enim quae sunt communia animatis et inanimatis, non est aliqua potentia animae assignanda. Sed appetere est commune animatis et inanimatis quia bonum est quod omnia appetunt, ut dicitur in I Ethic. Ergo appetitus non est specialis potentia animae.\n\n[I q. 80 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31830)\n\nPraeterea, potentiae distinguuntur secundum obiecta. Sed idem est quod cognoscimus et appetimus. Ergo vim appetitivam non oportet esse aliam praeter vim apprehensivam.\n\n[I q. 80 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31831)\n\nPraeterea, commune non distinguitur contra proprium. Sed quaelibet potentia animae appetit quoddam particulare appetibile, scilicet obiectum sibi conveniens. Ergo respectu huius obiecti quod est appetibile in communi, non oportet accipi aliquam potentiam ab aliis distinctam, quae appetitiva dicatur.\n\n[I q. 80 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31832)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus, in II de anima, distinguit appetitivum ab aliis potentiis. Damascenus etiam, in II libro distinguit vires appetitivas a cognitivis.\n\n[I q. 80 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31833)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est ponere quandam potentiam animae appetitivam. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod quamlibet formam sequitur aliqua inclinatio, sicut ignis ex sua forma inclinatur in superiorem locum, et ad hoc quod generet sibi simile. Forma autem in his quae cognitionem participant, altiori modo invenitur quam in his quae cognitione carent. In his enim quae cognitione carent, invenitur tantummodo forma ad unum esse proprium determinans unumquodque, quod etiam naturale uniuscuiusque est. Hanc igitur formam naturalem sequitur naturalis inclinatio, quae appetitus naturalis vocatur. In habentibus autem cognitionem, sic determinatur unumquodque ad proprium esse naturale per formam naturalem, quod tamen est receptivum specierum aliarum rerum, sicut sensus recipit species omnium sensibilium, et intellectus omnium intelligibilium, ut sic anima hominis sit omnia quodammodo secundum sensum et intellectum, in quo quodammodo cognitionem habentia ad Dei similitudinem appropinquant, in quo omnia praeexistunt, sicut Dionysius dicit. Sicut igitur formae altiori modo existunt in habentibus cognitionem supra modum formarum naturalium, ita oportet quod in eis sit inclinatio supra modum inclinationis naturalis, quae dicitur appetitus naturalis. Et haec superior inclinatio pertinet ad vim animae appetitivam, per quam animal appetere potest ea quae apprehendit, non solum ea ad quae inclinatur ex forma naturali. Sic igitur necesse est ponere aliquam potentiam animae appetitivam.\n\n[I q. 80 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31834)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod appetere invenitur in habentibus cognitionem, supra modum communem quo invenitur in omnibus, ut dictum est. Et ideo oportet ad hoc determinari aliquam potentiam animae.\n\n[I q. 80 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31835)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod id quod apprehenditur et appetitur, est idem subiecto, sed differt ratione, apprehenditur enim ut est ens sensibile vel intelligibile; appetitur vero ut est conveniens aut bonum. Diversitas autem rationum in obiectis requiritur ad diversitatem potentiarum; non autem materialis diversitas.\n\n[I q. 80 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31836)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod unaquaeque potentia animae est quaedam forma seu natura, et habet naturalem inclinationem in aliquid. Unde unaquaeque appetit obiectum sibi conveniens naturali appetitu. Supra quem est appetitus animalis consequens apprehensionem, quo appetitur aliquid non ea ratione qua est conveniens ad actum huius vel illius potentiae, utpote visio ad videndum et auditio ad audiendum; sed quia est conveniens simpliciter animali.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 80 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31837)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod appetitus sensitivus et intellectivus non sint diversae potentiae. Potentiae enim non diversificantur per accidentales differentias, ut supra dictum est. Sed accidit appetibili quod sit apprehensum per sensum vel intellectum. Ergo appetitus sensitivus et intellectivus non sunt diversae potentiae.\n\n[I q. 80 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31838)\n\nPraeterea, cognitio intellectiva est universalium, et secundum hoc distinguitur a sensitiva, quae est singularium. Sed ista distinctio non habet locum ex parte appetitivae, cum enim appetitus sit motus ab anima ad res, quae sunt singulares, omnis appetitus videtur esse rei singularis. Non ergo appetitus intellectivus debet distingui a sensitivo.\n\n[I q. 80 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31839)\n\nPraeterea, sicut sub apprehensivo ordinatur appetitivum ut inferior potentia, ita et motivum. Sed non est aliud motivum in homine consequens intellectum, quam in aliis animalibus consequens sensum. Ergo, pari ratione, neque est aliud appetitivum.\n\n[I q. 80 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31840)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus, in III de anima, distinguit duplicem appetitum, et dicit quod appetitus superior movet inferiorem.\n\n[I q. 80 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31841)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere appetitum intellectivum esse aliam potentiam a sensitivo. Potentia enim appetitiva est potentia passiva, quae nata est moveri ab apprehenso, unde appetibile apprehensum est movens non motum, appetitus autem movens motum, ut dicitur in III de anima, et XII Metaphys. Passiva autem et mobilia distinguuntur secundum distinctionem activorum et motivorum, quia oportet motivum esse proportionatum mobili, et activum passivo; et ipsa potentia passiva propriam rationem habet ex ordine ad suum activum. Quia igitur est alterius generis apprehensum per intellectum et apprehensum per sensum, consequens est quod appetitus intellectivus sit alia potentia a sensitivo.\n\n[I q. 80 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31842)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod appetibili non accidit esse apprehensum per sensum vel intellectum, sed per se ei convenit, nam appetibile non movet appetitum nisi inquantum est apprehensum. Unde differentiae apprehensi sunt per se differentiae appetibilis. Unde potentiae appetitivae distinguuntur secundum differentiam apprehensorum, sicut secundum propria obiecta.\n\n[I q. 80 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31843)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod appetitus intellectivus, etsi feratur in res quae sunt extra animam singulares, fertur tamen in eas secundum aliquam rationem universalem; sicut cum appetit aliquid quia est bonum. Unde philosophus dicit in sua rhetorica, quod odium potest esse de aliquo universali, puta cum odio habemus omne latronum genus. Similiter etiam per appetitum intellectivum appetere possumus immaterialia bona, quae sensus non apprehendit; sicut scientiam, virtutes, et alia huiusmodi.\n\n[I q. 80 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31844)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut dicitur in III de anima, opinio universalis non movet nisi mediante particulari, et similiter appetitus superior movet mediante inferiori. Et ideo non est alia vis motiva consequens intellectum et sensum.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0002","text":"[I q. 80 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31828)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de potentiis appetitivis. Et circa hoc consideranda sunt quatuor, primo, de appetitivo in communi; secundo, de sensualitate; tertio, de voluntate; quarto, de libero arbitrio. Circa primum quaeruntur duo. Primo, utrum debeat poni appetitus aliqua specialis potentia animae. Secundo, utrum appetitus dividatur in appetitum sensitivum et intellectivum, sicut in potentias diversas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0005","text":"[I q. 80 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31829)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod appetitus non sit aliqua specialis animae potentia. Ad ea enim quae sunt communia animatis et inanimatis, non est aliqua potentia animae assignanda. Sed appetere est commune animatis et inanimatis quia bonum est quod omnia appetunt, ut dicitur in I Ethic. Ergo appetitus non est specialis potentia animae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0007","text":"[I q. 80 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31830)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0008","text":"Praeterea, potentiae distinguuntur secundum obiecta. Sed idem est quod cognoscimus et appetimus. Ergo vim appetitivam non oportet esse aliam praeter vim apprehensivam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0009","text":"[I q. 80 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31831)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0010","text":"Praeterea, commune non distinguitur contra proprium. Sed quaelibet potentia animae appetit quoddam particulare appetibile, scilicet obiectum sibi conveniens. Ergo respectu huius obiecti quod est appetibile in communi, non oportet accipi aliquam potentiam ab aliis distinctam, quae appetitiva dicatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0011","text":"[I q. 80 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31832)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus, in II de anima, distinguit appetitivum ab aliis potentiis. Damascenus etiam, in II libro distinguit vires appetitivas a cognitivis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0013","text":"[I q. 80 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31833)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est ponere quandam potentiam animae appetitivam. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod quamlibet formam sequitur aliqua inclinatio, sicut ignis ex sua forma inclinatur in superiorem locum, et ad hoc quod generet sibi simile. Forma autem in his quae cognitionem participant, altiori modo invenitur quam in his quae cognitione carent. In his enim quae cognitione carent, invenitur tantummodo forma ad unum esse proprium determinans unumquodque, quod etiam naturale uniuscuiusque est. Hanc igitur formam naturalem sequitur naturalis inclinatio, quae appetitus naturalis vocatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0015","text":"In habentibus autem cognitionem, sic determinatur unumquodque ad proprium esse naturale per formam naturalem, quod tamen est receptivum specierum aliarum rerum, sicut sensus recipit species omnium sensibilium, et intellectus omnium intelligibilium, ut sic anima hominis sit omnia quodammodo secundum sensum et intellectum, in quo quodammodo cognitionem habentia ad Dei similitudinem appropinquant, in quo omnia praeexistunt, sicut Dionysius dicit. Sicut igitur formae altiori modo existunt in habentibus cognitionem supra modum formarum naturalium, ita oportet quod in eis sit inclinatio supra modum inclinationis naturalis, quae dicitur appetitus naturalis. Et haec superior inclinatio pertinet ad vim animae appetitivam, per quam animal appetere potest ea quae apprehendit, non solum ea ad quae inclinatur ex forma naturali. Sic igitur necesse est ponere aliquam potentiam animae appetitivam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0016","text":"[I q. 80 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31834)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod appetere invenitur in habentibus cognitionem, supra modum communem quo invenitur in omnibus, ut dictum est. Et ideo oportet ad hoc determinari aliquam potentiam animae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0018","text":"[I q. 80 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31835)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod id quod apprehenditur et appetitur, est idem subiecto, sed differt ratione, apprehenditur enim ut est ens sensibile vel intelligibile; appetitur vero ut est conveniens aut bonum. Diversitas autem rationum in obiectis requiritur ad diversitatem potentiarum; non autem materialis diversitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0020","text":"[I q. 80 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31836)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod unaquaeque potentia animae est quaedam forma seu natura, et habet naturalem inclinationem in aliquid. Unde unaquaeque appetit obiectum sibi conveniens naturali appetitu. Supra quem est appetitus animalis consequens apprehensionem, quo appetitur aliquid non ea ratione qua est conveniens ad actum huius vel illius potentiae, utpote visio ad videndum et auditio ad audiendum; sed quia est conveniens simpliciter animali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0023","text":"[I q. 80 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31837)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod appetitus sensitivus et intellectivus non sint diversae potentiae. Potentiae enim non diversificantur per accidentales differentias, ut supra dictum est. Sed accidit appetibili quod sit apprehensum per sensum vel intellectum. Ergo appetitus sensitivus et intellectivus non sunt diversae potentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0025","text":"[I q. 80 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31838)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0026","text":"Praeterea, cognitio intellectiva est universalium, et secundum hoc distinguitur a sensitiva, quae est singularium. Sed ista distinctio non habet locum ex parte appetitivae, cum enim appetitus sit motus ab anima ad res, quae sunt singulares, omnis appetitus videtur esse rei singularis. Non ergo appetitus intellectivus debet distingui a sensitivo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0027","text":"[I q. 80 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31839)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0028","text":"Praeterea, sicut sub apprehensivo ordinatur appetitivum ut inferior potentia, ita et motivum. Sed non est aliud motivum in homine consequens intellectum, quam in aliis animalibus consequens sensum. Ergo, pari ratione, neque est aliud appetitivum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0029","text":"[I q. 80 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31840)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0030","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus, in III de anima, distinguit duplicem appetitum, et dicit quod appetitus superior movet inferiorem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0031","text":"[I q. 80 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31841)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0032","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere appetitum intellectivum esse aliam potentiam a sensitivo. Potentia enim appetitiva est potentia passiva, quae nata est moveri ab apprehenso, unde appetibile apprehensum est movens non motum, appetitus autem movens motum, ut dicitur in III de anima, et XII Metaphys. Passiva autem et mobilia distinguuntur secundum distinctionem activorum et motivorum, quia oportet motivum esse proportionatum mobili, et activum passivo; et ipsa potentia passiva propriam rationem habet ex ordine ad suum activum. Quia igitur est alterius generis apprehensum per intellectum et apprehensum per sensum, consequens est quod appetitus intellectivus sit alia potentia a sensitivo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0033","text":"[I q. 80 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31842)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0034","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod appetibili non accidit esse apprehensum per sensum vel intellectum, sed per se ei convenit, nam appetibile non movet appetitum nisi inquantum est apprehensum. Unde differentiae apprehensi sunt per se differentiae appetibilis. Unde potentiae appetitivae distinguuntur secundum differentiam apprehensorum, sicut secundum propria obiecta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0035","text":"[I q. 80 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31843)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0036","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod appetitus intellectivus, etsi feratur in res quae sunt extra animam singulares, fertur tamen in eas secundum aliquam rationem universalem; sicut cum appetit aliquid quia est bonum. Unde philosophus dicit in sua rhetorica, quod odium potest esse de aliquo universali, puta cum odio habemus omne latronum genus. Similiter etiam per appetitum intellectivum appetere possumus immaterialia bona, quae sensus non apprehendit; sicut scientiam, virtutes, et alia huiusmodi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0037","text":"[I q. 80 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31844)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0038","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut dicitur in III de anima, opinio universalis non movet nisi mediante particulari, et similiter appetitus superior movet mediante inferiori. Et ideo non est alia vis motiva consequens intellectum et sensum.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"900574ad1fc38547f52ca6e96af75f4fe59b1f66a10eef3ebfd361cabbaf76bc","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T151116623872Z_ff758c30","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 80","source_sha256":"900574ad1fc38547f52ca6e96af75f4fe59b1f66a10eef3ebfd361cabbaf76bc","sha256":"cad869a91faf3448ba0887492a87c0bf3398892ee637d3ff2aa416244a4a5aab","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:11:16.623969+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0002","text":"[I q. 80 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31828)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the appetitive powers. And concerning this, four things must be considered: first, appetite in general; second, sensuality; third, the will; fourth, free choice. Concerning the first, two questions are asked. First, whether appetite ought to be posited as some special power of the soul. Second, whether appetite is divided into sensitive and intellective appetite, as into diverse powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0005","text":"[I q. 80 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31829)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that appetite is not some special power of the soul. For no power of the soul is to be assigned to those things which are common to animate and inanimate things. But to appetite is common to animate and inanimate things, because the good is that which all things appetite, as is said in Ethics I. Therefore appetite is not a special power of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0007","text":"[I q. 80 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31830)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0008","text":"Further, powers are distinguished according to their objects. But that which we know and that which we appetite are the same. Therefore the appetitive power need not be other than the apprehensive power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0009","text":"[I q. 80 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31831)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0010","text":"Further, the common is not distinguished against the proper. But every power of the soul appetites some particular appetible thing, namely, its suitable object. Therefore, with respect to this object which is the appetible in common, it is not necessary to posit some power distinct from the others, which is called appetitive.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0011","text":"[I q. 80 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31832)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0012","text":"On the contrary, the Philosopher, in On the Soul II, distinguishes the appetitive from the other powers. Damascene also, in Book II, distinguishes the appetitive powers from the cognitive ones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0013","text":"[I q. 80 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31833)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that it is necessary to posit an appetitive power of the soul. For the evidence of this, one must consider that every form is followed by some inclination, just as fire, from its form, is inclined toward a higher place and toward generating its like. But form is found in a higher manner in those things that share in cognition than in those that lack cognition. For in things that lack cognition, there is found only a form determining each thing to one proper being, which is also the natural being of each thing. This natural form, therefore, is followed by a natural inclination, which is called natural appetite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0015","text":"But in those having cognition, each thing is so determined to its proper natural being through its natural form that it is nevertheless receptive of the species of other things; as sense receives the species of all sensibles, and intellect those of all intelligibles, so that the human soul is in a certain manner all things according to sense and intellect. In this way, those having cognition in some manner draw near to the likeness of God, in whom all things pre-exist, as Dionysius says. Therefore, just as forms exist in a higher manner in those having cognition, above the mode of natural forms, so it is necessary that in them there be an inclination above the mode of natural inclination, which is called natural appetite. And this higher inclination belongs to the appetitive power of the soul, through which an animal can appetite those things which it apprehends, and not only those toward which it is inclined from its natural form. Thus, therefore, it is necessary to posit some appetitive power of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0016","text":"[I q. 80 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31834)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that appetite is found in those having cognition above the common mode in which it is found in all things, as has been said. And therefore it is necessary that some power of the soul be determined to this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0018","text":"[I q. 80 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31835)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0019","text":"To the second, it must be said that what is apprehended and what is appetited are the same in subject, but differ in account: for it is apprehended as a sensible or intelligible being, but appetited as suitable or good. But diversity of accounts in objects is required for diversity of powers, whereas material diversity is not.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0020","text":"[I q. 80 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31836)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0021","text":"To the third, it must be said that each power of the soul is a certain form or nature, and has a natural inclination toward something. Hence each appetites its suitable object by natural appetite. Above this is animal appetite, consequent upon apprehension, by which something is appetited not by reason of its being suitable to the act of this or that power—for example, sight for seeing and hearing for hearing—but because it is suitable simply to the animal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0023","text":"[I q. 80 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31837)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0024","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that sensitive and intellective appetite are not diverse powers. For powers are not diversified by accidental differences, as was said above. But it is accidental to the appetible that it be apprehended by sense or by intellect. Therefore sensitive and intellective appetite are not diverse powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0025","text":"[I q. 80 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31838)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0026","text":"Further, intellectual cognition is of universals, and in this respect it is distinguished from sensitive cognition, which is of singulars. But this distinction has no place on the appetitive side; for, since appetite is a movement from the soul toward things, which are singular, every appetite seems to be of a singular thing. Therefore the intellectual appetite ought not to be distinguished from the sensitive.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0027","text":"[I q. 80 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31839)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0028","text":"Further, just as the appetitive is ordered under the apprehensive as an inferior power, so too is the motive power. But there is not in man a motive power consequent upon intellect other than that which in other animals is consequent upon sense. Therefore, by equal reasoning, neither is there another appetitive power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0029","text":"[I q. 80 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31840)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0030","text":"But on the contrary, the Philosopher, in III On the Soul, distinguishes a twofold appetite, and says that the higher appetite moves the lower.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0031","text":"[I q. 80 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31841)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0032","text":"I answer that it must be said that the intellectual appetite is a power other than the sensitive appetite. For the appetitive power is a passive power, naturally apt to be moved by what is apprehended; hence the apprehended appetible is a mover not moved, whereas appetite is a mover moved, as is said in III On the Soul and XII Metaphysics. But passive and movable things are distinguished according to the distinction of active and motive things, because the mover must be proportioned to the movable, and the active to the passive; and the passive power itself has its proper account from its order to its active principle. Therefore, since what is apprehended by intellect and what is apprehended by sense are of another genus, it follows that the intellectual appetite is a power other than the sensitive appetite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0033","text":"[I q. 80 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31842)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0034","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that being apprehended by sense or intellect is not accidental to the appetible, but belongs to it per se; for the appetible does not move the appetite except insofar as it is apprehended. Hence the differences of what is apprehended are per se differences of the appetible. Hence the appetitive powers are distinguished according to the difference of things apprehended, as according to their proper objects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0035","text":"[I q. 80 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31843)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0036","text":"To the second, it must be said that the intellectual appetite, although it is borne toward things outside the soul which are singular, is nevertheless borne toward them according to some universal account; as when it desires something because it is good. Hence the Philosopher says in his Rhetoric that hatred can be of some universal, for example when we hate the whole race of robbers. Likewise, through the intellectual appetite we can desire immaterial goods, which sense does not apprehend, such as knowledge, virtues, and other things of this kind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0037","text":"[I q. 80 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31844)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0038","text":"To the third, it must be said that, as is said in III On the Soul, universal opinion does not move except through the particular, and similarly the higher appetite moves through the lower. And therefore there is not another motive power consequent upon intellect and sense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_72:P0038"]}],"source_digest":"97e97834a9ce405f677513bffd58c2dc9f41bde8999f3c5751226f47254fa738","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 80"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 80","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73","title":"First Part · Question 81","order":82,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_73","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 81 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31845)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de sensualitate. Circa quam quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum sensualitas sit vis appetitiva tantum. Secundo, utrum dividatur sensualitas in irascibilem et concupiscibilem, sicut in diversas potentias. Tertio, utrum irascibilis et concupiscibilis obediant rationi.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 81 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31846)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod sensualitas non solum sit appetitiva, sed etiam cognitiva. Dicit enim Augustinus, XII de Trin., quod *sensualis animae motus, qui in corporis sensus intenditur, nobis pecoribusque communis est*. Sed corporis sensus sub vi cognitiva continentur. Ergo sensualitas est vis cognitiva.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31847)\n\nPraeterea, quae cadunt sub una divisione, videntur esse unius generis. Sed Augustinus, in XII de Trin., dividit sensualitatem contra rationem superiorem et inferiorem; quae ad cognitionem pertinent. Ergo sensualitas etiam est vis cognitiva.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31848)\n\nPraeterea, sensualitas in tentatione hominis tenet locum serpentis. Sed serpens in tentatione primorum parentum se habuit ut nuntians et proponens peccatum; quod est vis cognitivae. Ergo sensualitas est vis cognitiva.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31849)\n\nSed contra est quod sensualitas definitur esse *appetitus rerum ad corpus pertinentium*.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31850)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod nomen sensualitatis sumptum videtur a sensuali motu, de quo Augustinus loquitur XII de Trin., sicut ab actu sumitur nomen potentiae, ut a visione visus. Motus autem sensualis est appetitus apprehensionem sensitivam consequens. Actus enim apprehensivae virtutis non ita proprie dicitur motus, sicut actio appetitus, nam operatio virtutis apprehensivae perficitur in hoc, quod res apprehensae sunt in apprehendente; operatio autem virtutis appetitivae perficitur in hoc, quod appetens inclinatur in rem appetibilem. Et ideo operatio apprehensivae virtutis assimilatur quieti, operatio autem virtutis appetitivae magis assimilatur motui. Unde per sensualem motum intelligitur operatio appetitivae virtutis. Et sic sensualitas est nomen appetitus sensitivi.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31851)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod per hoc quod dicit Augustinus quod sensualis animae motus intenditur in corporis sensus, non datur intelligi quod corporis sensus sub sensualitate comprehendantur, sed magis quod motus sensualitatis sit inclinatio quaedam ad sensus corporis, dum scilicet appetimus ea quae per corporis sensus apprehenduntur. Et sic corporis sensus pertinent ad sensualitatem quasi praeambuli.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31852)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod sensualitas dividitur contra rationem superiorem et inferiorem, inquantum communicant in actu motionis, vis enim cognitiva, ad quam pertinet ratio superior et inferior, est motiva, sicut et appetitiva, ad quam pertinet sensualitas.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31853)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod serpens non solum ostendit et proposuit peccatum, sed etiam, inclinavit in effectum peccati. Et quantum ad hoc, sensualitas per serpentem significatur.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 81 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31854)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod appetitus sensitivus non distinguatur in irascibilem et concupiscibilem, sicut in potentias diversas. Eadem enim potentia animae est unius contrarietatis, ut visus albi et nigri, ut dicitur in II de anima. Sed conveniens et nocivum sunt contraria. Cum ergo concupiscibilis respiciat conveniens, irascibilis vero nocivum, videtur quod eadem potentia animae sit irascibilis et concupiscibilis.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31855)\n\nPraeterea, appetitus sensitivus non est nisi convenientium secundum sensum. Sed conveniens secundum sensum est obiectum concupiscibilis. Ergo nullus appetitus sensitivus est a concupiscibili differens.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31856)\n\nPraeterea, odium est in irascibili, dicit enim Hieronymus, super Matth., *possideamus in irascibili odium vitiorum*. Sed odium, cum contrarietur amori, est in concupiscibili. Ergo eadem vis est concupiscibilis et irascibilis.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31857)\n\nSed contra est quod Gregorius Nyssenus et Damascenus ponunt duas vires, irascibilem et concupiscibilem, partes appetitus sensitivi.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31858)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod appetitus sensitivus est una vis in genere, quae sensualitas dicitur; sed dividitur in duas potentias, quae sunt species appetitus sensitivi, scilicet in irascibilem et concupiscibilem. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerari oportet quod in rebus naturalibus corruptibilibus, non solum oportet esse inclinationem ad consequendum convenientia et refugiendum nociva; sed etiam ad resistendum corrumpentibus et contrariis, quae convenientibus impedimentum praebent et ingerunt nocumenta. Sicut ignis habet naturalem inclinationem non solum ut recedat ab inferiori loco, qui sibi non convenit, et tendat in locum superiorem sibi convenientem; sed etiam quod resistat corrumpentibus et impedientibus. Quia igitur appetitus sensitivus est inclinatio consequens apprehensionem sensitivam, sicut appetitus naturalis est inclinatio consequens formam naturalem; necesse est quod in parte sensitiva sint duae appetitivae potentiae. Una, per quam anima simpliciter inclinatur ad prosequendum ea quae sunt convenientia secundum sensum, et ad refugiendum nociva, et haec dicitur concupiscibilis. Alia vero, per quam animal resistit impugnantibus, quae convenientia impugnant et nocumenta inferunt, et haec vis vocatur irascibilis. Unde dicitur quod eius obiectum est arduum, quia scilicet tendit ad hoc quod superet contraria, et superemineat eis. Hae autem duae inclinationes non reducuntur in unum principium, quia interdum anima tristibus se ingerit, contra inclinationem concupiscibilis, ut secundum inclinationem irascibilis impugnet contraria. Unde etiam passiones irascibilis repugnare videntur passionibus concupiscibilis, nam concupiscentia accensa minuit iram, et ira accensa minuit concupiscentiam, ut in pluribus. Patet etiam ex hoc, quod irascibilis est quasi propugnatrix et defensatrix concupiscibilis, dum insurgit contra ea quae impediunt convenientia, quae concupiscibilis appetit, et ingerunt nociva, quae concupiscibilis refugit. Et propter hoc, omnes passiones irascibilis incipiunt a passionibus concupiscibilis, et in eas terminantur; sicut ira nascitur ex illata tristitia, et vindictam inferens, in laetitiam terminatur. Propter hoc etiam pugnae animalium sunt de concupiscibilibus, scilicet de cibis et venereis, ut dicitur in VIII de animalibus.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31859)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod vis concupiscibilis est et convenientis et inconvenientis. Sed irascibilis est ad resistendum inconvenienti quod impugnat.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31860)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut in apprehensivis virtutibus in parte sensitiva est aliqua vis aestimativa, scilicet quae est perceptiva eorum quae sensum non immutant, ut supra dictum est; ita etiam in appetitu sensitivo est aliqua vis appetens aliquid quod non est conveniens secundum delectationem sensus, sed secundum quod est utile animali ad suam defensionem. Et haec est vis irascibilis.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31861)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod odium simpliciter pertinet ad concupiscibilem; sed ratione impugnationis quae ex odio causatur, potest ad irascibilem pertinere.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 81 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31862)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod irascibilis et concupiscibilis non obediant rationi. Irascibilis enim et concupiscibilis sunt partes sensualitatis. Sed sensualitas non obedit rationi, unde per serpentem significatur, ut dicit Augustinus, XII de Trin. Ergo irascibilis et concupiscibilis non obediunt rationi.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31863)\n\nPraeterea, quod obedit alicui, non repugnat ei. Sed irascibilis et concupiscibilis repugnant rationi; secundum illud apostoli, ad Rom. VII, *video aliam legem in membris meis, repugnantem legi mentis meae*. Ergo irascibilis et concupiscibilis non obediunt rationi.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31864)\n\nPraeterea, sicut rationali parte animae inferior est vis appetitiva, ita etiam et vis sensitiva. Sed sensitiva pars animae non obedit rationi, non enim audimus nec videmus quando volumus. Ergo similiter neque vires sensitivi appetitus, scilicet irascibilis et concupiscibilis, obediunt rationi.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31865)\n\nSed contra est quod Damascenus dicit, quod *obediens et persuasibile rationi dividitur in concupiscentiam et iram*.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31866)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod irascibilis et concupiscibilis obediunt superiori parti, in qua est intellectus sive ratio et voluntas, dupliciter, uno modo quidem, quantum ad rationem; alio vero modo, quantum ad voluntatem. Rationi quidem obediunt quantum ad ipsos suos actus. Cuius ratio est, quia appetitus sensitivus in aliis quidem animalibus natus est moveri ab aestimativa virtute; sicut ovis aestimans lupum inimicum, timet. Loco autem aestimativae virtutis est in homine, sicut supra dictum est, vis cogitativa; quae dicitur a quibusdam ratio particularis, eo quod est collativa intentionum individualium. Unde ab ea natus est moveri in homine appetitus sensitivus. Ipsa autem ratio particularis nata est moveri et dirigi secundum rationem universalem, unde in syllogisticis ex universalibus propositionibus concluduntur conclusiones singulares. Et ideo patet quod ratio universalis imperat appetitui sensitivo, qui distinguitur per concupiscibilem et irascibilem, et hic appetitus ei obedit. Et quia deducere universalia principia in conclusiones singulares, non est opus simplicis intellectus, sed rationis; ideo irascibilis et concupiscibilis magis dicuntur obedire rationi, quam intellectui. Hoc etiam quilibet experiri potest in seipso, applicando enim aliquas universales considerationes, mitigatur ira aut timor aut aliquid huiusmodi, vel etiam instigatur. Voluntati etiam subiacet appetitus sensitivus, quantum ad executionem, quae fit per vim motivam. In aliis enim animalibus statim ad appetitum concupiscibilis et irascibilis sequitur motus sicut ovis, timens lupum statim fugit, quia non est in eis aliquis superior appetitus qui repugnet. Sed homo non statim movetur secundum appetitum irascibilis et concupiscibilis; sed expectatur imperium voluntatis, quod est appetitus superior. In omnibus enim potentiis motivis ordinatis, secundum movens non movet nisi virtute primi moventis, unde appetitus inferior non sufficit movere, nisi appetitus superior consentiat. Et hoc est quod philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod *appetitus superior movet appetitum inferiorem, sicut sphaera superior inferiorem*. Hoc ergo modo irascibilis et concupiscibilis rationi subduntur.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31867)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod sensualitas significatur per serpentem, quantum ad id quod est proprium sibi ex parte sensitivae partis. Irascibilis autem et concupiscibilis magis nominant sensitivum appetitum ex parte actus, ad quem inducuntur ex ratione, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31868)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut philosophus dicit in I politicorum, *est quidem in animali contemplari et despoticum principatum, et politicum, anima quidem enim corpori dominatur despotico principatu; intellectus autem appetitui, politico et regali*. Dicitur enim despoticus principatus, quo aliquis principatur servis, qui non habent facultatem in aliquo resistendi imperio praecipientis, quia nihil sui habent. Principatus autem politicus et regalis dicitur, quo aliquis principatur liberis, qui, etsi subdantur regimini praesidentis, tamen habent aliquid proprium, ex quo possunt reniti praecipientis imperio. Sic igitur anima dicitur dominari corpori despotico principatu, quia corporis membra in nullo resistere possunt imperio animae, sed statim ad appetitum animae movetur manus et pes, et quodlibet membrum quod natum est moveri voluntario motu. Intellectus autem, seu ratio, dicitur principari irascibili et concupiscibili politico principatu, quia appetitus sensibilis habet aliquid proprium, unde potest reniti imperio rationis. Natus est enim moveri appetitus sensitivus, non solum ab aestimativa in aliis animalibus, et cogitativa in homine, quam dirigit universalis ratio; sed etiam ab imaginativa et sensu. Unde experimur irascibilem vel concupiscibilem rationi repugnare, per hoc quod sentimus vel imaginamur aliquod delectabile quod ratio vetat, vel triste quod ratio praecipit. Et sic per hoc quod irascibilis et concupiscibilis in aliquo rationi repugnant, non excluditur quin ei obediant.\n\n[I q. 81 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31869)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod sensus exteriores indigent ad suos actus exterioribus sensibilibus, quibus immutentur, quorum praesentia non est in potestate rationis. Sed vires interiores, tam appetitivae quam apprehensivae, non indigent exterioribus rebus. Et ideo subduntur imperio rationis, quae potest non solum instigare vel mitigare affectus appetitivae virtutis, sed etiam formare imaginativae virtutis phantasmata.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0002","text":"[I q. 81 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31845)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de sensualitate. Circa quam quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum sensualitas sit vis appetitiva tantum. Secundo, utrum dividatur sensualitas in irascibilem et concupiscibilem, sicut in diversas potentias. Tertio, utrum irascibilis et concupiscibilis obediant rationi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0005","text":"[I q. 81 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31846)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod sensualitas non solum sit appetitiva, sed etiam cognitiva. Dicit enim Augustinus, XII de Trin., quod *sensualis animae motus, qui in corporis sensus intenditur, nobis pecoribusque communis est*. Sed corporis sensus sub vi cognitiva continentur. Ergo sensualitas est vis cognitiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0007","text":"[I q. 81 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31847)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0008","text":"Praeterea, quae cadunt sub una divisione, videntur esse unius generis. Sed Augustinus, in XII de Trin., dividit sensualitatem contra rationem superiorem et inferiorem; quae ad cognitionem pertinent. Ergo sensualitas etiam est vis cognitiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0009","text":"[I q. 81 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31848)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0010","text":"Praeterea, sensualitas in tentatione hominis tenet locum serpentis. Sed serpens in tentatione primorum parentum se habuit ut nuntians et proponens peccatum; quod est vis cognitivae. Ergo sensualitas est vis cognitiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0011","text":"[I q. 81 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31849)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod sensualitas definitur esse *appetitus rerum ad corpus pertinentium*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0013","text":"[I q. 81 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31850)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod nomen sensualitatis sumptum videtur a sensuali motu, de quo Augustinus loquitur XII de Trin., sicut ab actu sumitur nomen potentiae, ut a visione visus. Motus autem sensualis est appetitus apprehensionem sensitivam consequens. Actus enim apprehensivae virtutis non ita proprie dicitur motus, sicut actio appetitus, nam operatio virtutis apprehensivae perficitur in hoc, quod res apprehensae sunt in apprehendente; operatio autem virtutis appetitivae perficitur in hoc, quod appetens inclinatur in rem appetibilem. Et ideo operatio apprehensivae virtutis assimilatur quieti, operatio autem virtutis appetitivae magis assimilatur motui. Unde per sensualem motum intelligitur operatio appetitivae virtutis. Et sic sensualitas est nomen appetitus sensitivi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0015","text":"[I q. 81 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31851)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0016","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod per hoc quod dicit Augustinus quod sensualis animae motus intenditur in corporis sensus, non datur intelligi quod corporis sensus sub sensualitate comprehendantur, sed magis quod motus sensualitatis sit inclinatio quaedam ad sensus corporis, dum scilicet appetimus ea quae per corporis sensus apprehenduntur. Et sic corporis sensus pertinent ad sensualitatem quasi praeambuli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0017","text":"[I q. 81 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31852)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0018","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod sensualitas dividitur contra rationem superiorem et inferiorem, inquantum communicant in actu motionis, vis enim cognitiva, ad quam pertinet ratio superior et inferior, est motiva, sicut et appetitiva, ad quam pertinet sensualitas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0019","text":"[I q. 81 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31853)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0020","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod serpens non solum ostendit et proposuit peccatum, sed etiam, inclinavit in effectum peccati. Et quantum ad hoc, sensualitas per serpentem significatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0021","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0022","text":"[I q. 81 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31854)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0023","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod appetitus sensitivus non distinguatur in irascibilem et concupiscibilem, sicut in potentias diversas. Eadem enim potentia animae est unius contrarietatis, ut visus albi et nigri, ut dicitur in II de anima. Sed conveniens et nocivum sunt contraria. Cum ergo concupiscibilis respiciat conveniens, irascibilis vero nocivum, videtur quod eadem potentia animae sit irascibilis et concupiscibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0024","text":"[I q. 81 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31855)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0025","text":"Praeterea, appetitus sensitivus non est nisi convenientium secundum sensum. Sed conveniens secundum sensum est obiectum concupiscibilis. Ergo nullus appetitus sensitivus est a concupiscibili differens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0026","text":"[I q. 81 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31856)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0027","text":"Praeterea, odium est in irascibili, dicit enim Hieronymus, super Matth., *possideamus in irascibili odium vitiorum*. Sed odium, cum contrarietur amori, est in concupiscibili. Ergo eadem vis est concupiscibilis et irascibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0028","text":"[I q. 81 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31857)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0029","text":"Sed contra est quod Gregorius Nyssenus et Damascenus ponunt duas vires, irascibilem et concupiscibilem, partes appetitus sensitivi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0030","text":"[I q. 81 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31858)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0031","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod appetitus sensitivus est una vis in genere, quae sensualitas dicitur; sed dividitur in duas potentias, quae sunt species appetitus sensitivi, scilicet in irascibilem et concupiscibilem. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerari oportet quod in rebus naturalibus corruptibilibus, non solum oportet esse inclinationem ad consequendum convenientia et refugiendum nociva; sed etiam ad resistendum corrumpentibus et contrariis, quae convenientibus impedimentum praebent et ingerunt nocumenta. Sicut ignis habet naturalem inclinationem non solum ut recedat ab inferiori loco, qui sibi non convenit, et tendat in locum superiorem sibi convenientem; sed etiam quod resistat corrumpentibus et impedientibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0032","text":"Quia igitur appetitus sensitivus est inclinatio consequens apprehensionem sensitivam, sicut appetitus naturalis est inclinatio consequens formam naturalem; necesse est quod in parte sensitiva sint duae appetitivae potentiae. Una, per quam anima simpliciter inclinatur ad prosequendum ea quae sunt convenientia secundum sensum, et ad refugiendum nociva, et haec dicitur concupiscibilis. Alia vero, per quam animal resistit impugnantibus, quae convenientia impugnant et nocumenta inferunt, et haec vis vocatur irascibilis. Unde dicitur quod eius obiectum est arduum, quia scilicet tendit ad hoc quod superet contraria, et superemineat eis. Hae autem duae inclinationes non reducuntur in unum principium, quia interdum anima tristibus se ingerit, contra inclinationem concupiscibilis, ut secundum inclinationem irascibilis impugnet contraria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0033","text":"Unde etiam passiones irascibilis repugnare videntur passionibus concupiscibilis, nam concupiscentia accensa minuit iram, et ira accensa minuit concupiscentiam, ut in pluribus. Patet etiam ex hoc, quod irascibilis est quasi propugnatrix et defensatrix concupiscibilis, dum insurgit contra ea quae impediunt convenientia, quae concupiscibilis appetit, et ingerunt nociva, quae concupiscibilis refugit. Et propter hoc, omnes passiones irascibilis incipiunt a passionibus concupiscibilis, et in eas terminantur; sicut ira nascitur ex illata tristitia, et vindictam inferens, in laetitiam terminatur. Propter hoc etiam pugnae animalium sunt de concupiscibilibus, scilicet de cibis et venereis, ut dicitur in VIII de animalibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0034","text":"[I q. 81 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31859)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0035","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod vis concupiscibilis est et convenientis et inconvenientis. Sed irascibilis est ad resistendum inconvenienti quod impugnat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0036","text":"[I q. 81 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31860)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0037","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut in apprehensivis virtutibus in parte sensitiva est aliqua vis aestimativa, scilicet quae est perceptiva eorum quae sensum non immutant, ut supra dictum est; ita etiam in appetitu sensitivo est aliqua vis appetens aliquid quod non est conveniens secundum delectationem sensus, sed secundum quod est utile animali ad suam defensionem. Et haec est vis irascibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0038","text":"[I q. 81 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31861)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0039","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod odium simpliciter pertinet ad concupiscibilem; sed ratione impugnationis quae ex odio causatur, potest ad irascibilem pertinere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0040","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0041","text":"[I q. 81 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31862)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0042","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod irascibilis et concupiscibilis non obediant rationi. Irascibilis enim et concupiscibilis sunt partes sensualitatis. Sed sensualitas non obedit rationi, unde per serpentem significatur, ut dicit Augustinus, XII de Trin. Ergo irascibilis et concupiscibilis non obediunt rationi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0043","text":"[I q. 81 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31863)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0044","text":"Praeterea, quod obedit alicui, non repugnat ei. Sed irascibilis et concupiscibilis repugnant rationi; secundum illud apostoli, ad Rom. VII, *video aliam legem in membris meis, repugnantem legi mentis meae*. Ergo irascibilis et concupiscibilis non obediunt rationi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0045","text":"[I q. 81 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31864)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0046","text":"Praeterea, sicut rationali parte animae inferior est vis appetitiva, ita etiam et vis sensitiva. Sed sensitiva pars animae non obedit rationi, non enim audimus nec videmus quando volumus. Ergo similiter neque vires sensitivi appetitus, scilicet irascibilis et concupiscibilis, obediunt rationi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0047","text":"[I q. 81 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31865)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0048","text":"Sed contra est quod Damascenus dicit, quod *obediens et persuasibile rationi dividitur in concupiscentiam et iram*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0049","text":"[I q. 81 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31866)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0050","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod irascibilis et concupiscibilis obediunt superiori parti, in qua est intellectus sive ratio et voluntas, dupliciter, uno modo quidem, quantum ad rationem; alio vero modo, quantum ad voluntatem. Rationi quidem obediunt quantum ad ipsos suos actus. Cuius ratio est, quia appetitus sensitivus in aliis quidem animalibus natus est moveri ab aestimativa virtute; sicut ovis aestimans lupum inimicum, timet. Loco autem aestimativae virtutis est in homine, sicut supra dictum est, vis cogitativa; quae dicitur a quibusdam ratio particularis, eo quod est collativa intentionum individualium. Unde ab ea natus est moveri in homine appetitus sensitivus. Ipsa autem ratio particularis nata est moveri et dirigi secundum rationem universalem, unde in syllogisticis ex universalibus propositionibus concluduntur conclusiones singulares.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0051","text":"Et ideo patet quod ratio universalis imperat appetitui sensitivo, qui distinguitur per concupiscibilem et irascibilem, et hic appetitus ei obedit. Et quia deducere universalia principia in conclusiones singulares, non est opus simplicis intellectus, sed rationis; ideo irascibilis et concupiscibilis magis dicuntur obedire rationi, quam intellectui. Hoc etiam quilibet experiri potest in seipso, applicando enim aliquas universales considerationes, mitigatur ira aut timor aut aliquid huiusmodi, vel etiam instigatur. Voluntati etiam subiacet appetitus sensitivus, quantum ad executionem, quae fit per vim motivam. In aliis enim animalibus statim ad appetitum concupiscibilis et irascibilis sequitur motus sicut ovis, timens lupum statim fugit, quia non est in eis aliquis superior appetitus qui repugnet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0052","text":"Sed homo non statim movetur secundum appetitum irascibilis et concupiscibilis; sed expectatur imperium voluntatis, quod est appetitus superior. In omnibus enim potentiis motivis ordinatis, secundum movens non movet nisi virtute primi moventis, unde appetitus inferior non sufficit movere, nisi appetitus superior consentiat. Et hoc est quod philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod *appetitus superior movet appetitum inferiorem, sicut sphaera superior inferiorem*. Hoc ergo modo irascibilis et concupiscibilis rationi subduntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0053","text":"[I q. 81 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31867)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0054","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod sensualitas significatur per serpentem, quantum ad id quod est proprium sibi ex parte sensitivae partis. Irascibilis autem et concupiscibilis magis nominant sensitivum appetitum ex parte actus, ad quem inducuntur ex ratione, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0055","text":"[I q. 81 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31868)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0056","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut philosophus dicit in I politicorum, *est quidem in animali contemplari et despoticum principatum, et politicum, anima quidem enim corpori dominatur despotico principatu; intellectus autem appetitui, politico et regali*. Dicitur enim despoticus principatus, quo aliquis principatur servis, qui non habent facultatem in aliquo resistendi imperio praecipientis, quia nihil sui habent. Principatus autem politicus et regalis dicitur, quo aliquis principatur liberis, qui, etsi subdantur regimini praesidentis, tamen habent aliquid proprium, ex quo possunt reniti praecipientis imperio. Sic igitur anima dicitur dominari corpori despotico principatu, quia corporis membra in nullo resistere possunt imperio animae, sed statim ad appetitum animae movetur manus et pes, et quodlibet membrum quod natum est moveri voluntario motu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0057","text":"Intellectus autem, seu ratio, dicitur principari irascibili et concupiscibili politico principatu, quia appetitus sensibilis habet aliquid proprium, unde potest reniti imperio rationis. Natus est enim moveri appetitus sensitivus, non solum ab aestimativa in aliis animalibus, et cogitativa in homine, quam dirigit universalis ratio; sed etiam ab imaginativa et sensu. Unde experimur irascibilem vel concupiscibilem rationi repugnare, per hoc quod sentimus vel imaginamur aliquod delectabile quod ratio vetat, vel triste quod ratio praecipit. Et sic per hoc quod irascibilis et concupiscibilis in aliquo rationi repugnant, non excluditur quin ei obediant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0058","text":"[I q. 81 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31869)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0059","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod sensus exteriores indigent ad suos actus exterioribus sensibilibus, quibus immutentur, quorum praesentia non est in potestate rationis. Sed vires interiores, tam appetitivae quam apprehensivae, non indigent exterioribus rebus. Et ideo subduntur imperio rationis, quae potest non solum instigare vel mitigare affectus appetitivae virtutis, sed etiam formare imaginativae virtutis phantasmata.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"822936d4221fc672ab962006b7182dccb0ec7dc259dbaa613418d6cd5d548baa","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T151205785027Z_b6ea7583","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 81","source_sha256":"822936d4221fc672ab962006b7182dccb0ec7dc259dbaa613418d6cd5d548baa","sha256":"6f7144aa810486944722f3dd1b7327441d0f31279aa9ee6ee2183c4bd43c001c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:12:05.785056+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0002","text":"[I q. 81 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31845)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0003","text":"Next, sensuality must be considered. Concerning it, three questions are asked. First, whether sensuality is an appetitive power only. Second, whether sensuality is divided into the irascible and the concupiscible, as into diverse powers. Third, whether the irascible and concupiscible obey reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0005","text":"[I q. 81 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31846)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that sensuality is not only appetitive, but also cognitive. For Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, XII, that *the movement of the sensual soul, which is directed toward the senses of the body, is common to us and to beasts*. But the senses of the body are contained under the cognitive power. Therefore sensuality is a cognitive power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0007","text":"[I q. 81 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31847)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0008","text":"Moreover, things that fall under one division seem to be of one genus. But Augustine, in *On the Trinity*, XII, divides sensuality against higher and lower reason, which pertain to cognition. Therefore sensuality too is a cognitive power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0009","text":"[I q. 81 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31848)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0010","text":"Moreover, in the temptation of man, sensuality holds the place of the serpent. But in the temptation of the first parents, the serpent acted as announcing and proposing sin, which belongs to a cognitive power. Therefore sensuality is a cognitive power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0011","text":"[I q. 81 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31849)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, sensuality is defined as *the appetite for things pertaining to the body*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0013","text":"[I q. 81 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31850)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that the name sensuality seems to be taken from sensual movement, of which Augustine speaks in *On the Trinity*, XII, just as the name of a power is taken from its act, as sight from seeing. Now sensual movement is the appetite consequent upon sensitive apprehension. For the act of an apprehensive power is not so properly called a movement as is the action of appetite, since the operation of an apprehensive power is completed in this, that the things apprehended are in the apprehender; whereas the operation of an appetitive power is completed in this, that the one who desires is inclined toward the appetible thing. Therefore the operation of an apprehensive power is likened to rest, while the operation of an appetitive power is more likened to movement. Hence by sensual movement is understood the operation of the appetitive power. And thus sensuality is the name of the sensitive appetite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0015","text":"[I q. 81 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31851)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0016","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, by Augustine's saying that the movement of the sensual soul is directed toward the senses of the body, it is not given to understand that the senses of the body are comprehended under sensuality; but rather that the movement of sensuality is a certain inclination toward the senses of the body, namely, while we desire those things that are apprehended through the senses of the body. And thus the senses of the body pertain to sensuality as preliminaries.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0017","text":"[I q. 81 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31852)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0018","text":"To the second, it must be said that sensuality is divided against higher and lower reason insofar as they share in the act of moving; for the cognitive power, to which higher and lower reason pertain, is motive, just as is the appetitive power, to which sensuality pertains.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0019","text":"[I q. 81 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31853)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0020","text":"To the third, it must be said that the serpent not only showed and proposed sin, but also inclined toward the effect of sin. And in this respect sensuality is signified by the serpent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0021","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0022","text":"[I q. 81 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31854)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0023","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the sensitive appetite is not distinguished into the irascible and the concupiscible as into diverse powers. For one and the same power of the soul is concerned with one contrariety, as sight with white and black, as is said in *On the Soul*, II. But the suitable and the harmful are contraries. Therefore, since the concupiscible regards the suitable, whereas the irascible regards the harmful, it seems that the irascible and concupiscible are one and the same power of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0024","text":"[I q. 81 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31855)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0025","text":"Moreover, the sensitive appetite is only of things suitable according to sense. But what is suitable according to sense is the object of the concupiscible power. Therefore, no sensitive appetite differs from the concupiscible power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0026","text":"[I q. 81 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31856)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0027","text":"Moreover, hatred is in the irascible power; for Jerome says, on Matthew, *let us possess in the irascible power hatred of vices*. But hatred, since it is contrary to love, is in the concupiscible power. Therefore, the concupiscible and irascible powers are the same power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0028","text":"[I q. 81 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31857)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0029","text":"But on the contrary, Gregory of Nyssa and Damascene posit two powers, the irascible and the concupiscible, as parts of the sensitive appetite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0030","text":"[I q. 81 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31858)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0031","text":"I answer that it must be said that the sensitive appetite is one power in genus, which is called sensuality; but it is divided into two powers, which are species of the sensitive appetite, namely, the irascible and the concupiscible. For the clarification of this, it must be considered that in corruptible natural things there must be not only an inclination to attain suitable things and to flee harmful things, but also to resist things that corrupt and oppose, which provide an impediment to suitable things and inflict harms. Thus fire has a natural inclination not only to withdraw from the lower place, which is not suitable to it, and to tend toward the higher place suitable to it, but also to resist things that corrupt and impede.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0032","text":"Therefore, since the sensitive appetite is an inclination consequent upon sensitive apprehension, just as the natural appetite is an inclination consequent upon natural form, it is necessary that there be two appetitive powers in the sensitive part. One, through which the soul is simply inclined to pursue those things that are suitable according to sense, and to flee harmful things; and this is called the concupiscible power. But the other is that through which an animal resists assailants that attack suitable things and inflict harms; and this power is called the irascible. Hence its object is said to be the arduous, because it tends to overcome contraries and to prevail over them. But these two inclinations are not reduced to one principle, because sometimes the soul exposes itself to sorrowful things, contrary to the inclination of the concupiscible power, in order, according to the inclination of the irascible power, to attack contraries.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0033","text":"Hence also the passions of the irascible power seem to conflict with the passions of the concupiscible power; for concupiscence when inflamed diminishes anger, and anger when inflamed diminishes concupiscence, as happens for the most part. This is also clear from the fact that the irascible power is, as it were, the champion and defender of the concupiscible power, insofar as it rises up against those things that impede the suitable things which the concupiscible power desires, and inflict the harmful things which the concupiscible power flees. And on this account, all the passions of the irascible power begin from the passions of the concupiscible power and terminate in them; just as anger arises from sorrow inflicted, and, inflicting vengeance, terminates in joy. For this reason also the fights of animals are over concupiscible things, namely, foods and sexual matters, as is said in Book VIII of *On Animals*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0034","text":"[I q. 81 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31859)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0035","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the concupiscible power is concerned with both what is suitable and what is unsuitable. But the irascible power is for resisting the unsuitable thing that attacks.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0036","text":"[I q. 81 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31860)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0037","text":"To the second, it must be said that, just as among the apprehensive powers in the sensitive part there is a certain estimative power, namely, that which perceives things that do not alter the senses, as was said above; so also in the sensitive appetite there is a certain power that desires something which is not suitable according to the pleasure of sense, but insofar as it is useful to the animal for its defense. And this is the irascible power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0038","text":"[I q. 81 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31861)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0039","text":"To the third, it must be said that hatred simply belongs to the concupiscible power; but by reason of the attack caused by hatred, it can belong to the irascible power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0040","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0041","text":"[I q. 81 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31862)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0042","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that the irascible and concupiscible powers do not obey reason. For the irascible and concupiscible powers are parts of sensuality. But sensuality does not obey reason; hence it is signified by the serpent, as Augustine says in Book XII of *On the Trinity*. Therefore, the irascible and concupiscible powers do not obey reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0043","text":"[I q. 81 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31863)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0044","text":"Moreover, what obeys something does not resist it. But the irascible and concupiscible powers resist reason, according to that saying of the Apostle, Romans VII: *I see another law in my members, resisting the law of my mind*. Therefore, the irascible and concupiscible powers do not obey reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0045","text":"[I q. 81 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31864)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0046","text":"Moreover, just as the appetitive power is inferior to the rational part of the soul, so too is the sensitive power. But the sensitive part of the soul does not obey reason, for we do not hear or see when we wish. Therefore, likewise neither do the powers of the sensitive appetite, namely the irascible and concupiscible powers, obey reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0047","text":"[I q. 81 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31865)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0048","text":"But on the contrary, Damascene says that *that which is obedient and amenable to reason is divided into concupiscence and anger*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0049","text":"[I q. 81 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31866)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0050","text":"I answer that the irascible and concupiscible powers obey the higher part, in which are intellect or reason and will, in two ways: in one way, with respect to reason; in another way, with respect to will. They obey reason indeed with respect to their own acts. The reason for this is that, in other animals, the sensitive appetite is naturally moved by the estimative power; thus a sheep, judging a wolf to be an enemy, fears it. But in man, in place of the estimative power, as was said above, there is the cogitative power, which is called by some particular reason, because it compares individual intentions. Hence the sensitive appetite in man is naturally moved by it. But particular reason itself is naturally moved and directed according to universal reason; hence, in syllogistic reasoning, singular conclusions are inferred from universal propositions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0051","text":"And therefore it is clear that universal reason commands the sensitive appetite, which is divided into the concupiscible and irascible, and that this appetite obeys it. And because drawing universal principles into singular conclusions is not the work of simple intellect but of reason, the irascible and concupiscible powers are therefore said to obey reason more than intellect. Anyone can also experience this in himself: for, by applying certain universal considerations, anger or fear or something of this kind is mitigated, or even incited. The sensitive appetite is also subject to the will with respect to execution, which takes place through the motive power. For in other animals, motion immediately follows upon the appetite of the concupiscible and irascible powers; thus a sheep, fearing a wolf, immediately flees, because there is in them no higher appetite that might resist.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0052","text":"But man is not immediately moved according to the appetite of the irascible and concupiscible powers; rather, the command of the will is awaited, which is the higher appetite. For in all ordered motive powers, a secondary mover does not move except by the power of the first mover; hence the lower appetite is not sufficient to move unless the higher appetite consents. And this is what the Philosopher says in *On the Soul*, III: that *the higher appetite moves the lower appetite, as the higher sphere moves the lower*. Therefore, in this way the irascible and concupiscible powers are subject to reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0053","text":"[I q. 81 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31867)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0054","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that sensuality is signified by the serpent, with respect to that which belongs properly to it on the part of the sensitive faculty. But the irascible and concupiscible powers name the sensitive appetite more on the part of its act, to which they are induced by reason, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0055","text":"[I q. 81 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31868)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0056","text":"To the second, it must be said that, as the Philosopher says in *Politics*, I, *in an animal one may observe both despotic rule and political rule: for the soul rules the body by despotic rule, but the intellect rules the appetite by political and royal rule*. For that is called despotic rule by which someone rules slaves, who have no power to resist in any respect the command of the one commanding, because they possess nothing of their own. But political and royal rule is called that by which someone rules free persons, who, although they are subject to the governance of the ruler, nevertheless have something of their own by which they can resist the command of the one commanding. Thus, therefore, the soul is said to rule the body by despotic rule because the members of the body can in no respect resist the command of the soul; rather, at the soul's appetite, the hand and foot immediately move, and every member naturally capable of being moved by voluntary motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0057","text":"But the intellect, or reason, is said to rule the irascible and concupiscible powers by political rule, because the sensitive appetite has something of its own whereby it can resist the command of reason. For the sensitive appetite is naturally moved not only by the estimative power in other animals, and by the cogitative power in man, which universal reason directs, but also by the imaginative power and by sense. Hence we experience the irascible or concupiscible power resisting reason, in that we sense or imagine something pleasant which reason forbids, or something sad which reason commands. And thus, from the fact that the irascible and concupiscible powers in some respect resist reason, it is not excluded that they obey it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0058","text":"[I q. 81 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31869)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0059","text":"To the third, it must be said that the external senses require for their acts external sensibles by which they are altered, and the presence of these is not within the power of reason. But the interior powers, both appetitive and apprehensive, do not require external things. And therefore they are subject to the command of reason, which can not only incite or mitigate the affections of the appetitive power, but can also form phantasms for the imaginative power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_73:P0059"]}],"source_digest":"52dff884959daae770ef5f8d88e3e8747e987130decf9c85a0b525491ed1c440","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 81"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 81","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74","title":"First Part · Question 82","order":83,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_74","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 82 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31870)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de voluntate. Circa quam quaeruntur quinque. Primo, utrum voluntas aliquid ex necessitate appetat. Secundo, utrum omnia ex necessitate appetat. Tertio, utrum sit eminentior potentia quam intellectus. Quarto, utrum voluntas moveat intellectum. Quinto, utrum voluntas distinguatur per irascibilem et concupiscibilem.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 82 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31871)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntas nihil ex necessitate appetat. Dicit enim Augustinus, in V de Civ. Dei, quod si aliquid est necessarium, non est voluntarium. Sed omne quod voluntas appetit, est voluntarium. Ergo nihil quod voluntas appetit, est necessario desideratum.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31872)\n\nPraeterea, potestates rationales, secundum philosophum, se habent ad opposita. Sed voluntas est potestas rationalis, quia, ut dicitur in III de anima, voluntas in ratione est. Ergo voluntas se habet ad opposita. Ad nihil ergo de necessitate determinatur.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31873)\n\nPraeterea, secundum voluntatem sumus domini nostrorum actuum. Sed eius quod ex necessitate est, non sumus domini. Ergo actus voluntatis non potest de necessitate esse.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31874)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in XIII de Trin., quod *beatitudinem omnes una voluntate appetunt*. Si autem non esset necessarium sed contingens, deficeret ad minus in paucioribus. Ergo voluntas ex necessitate aliquid vult.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31875)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necessitas dicitur multipliciter. Necesse est enim quod non potest non esse. Quod quidem convenit alicui, uno modo ex principio intrinseco, sive materiali, sicut cum dicimus quod omne compositum ex contrariis necesse est corrumpi; sive formali, sicut cum dicimus quod necesse est triangulum habere tres angulos aequales duobus rectis. Et haec est necessitas naturalis et absoluta. Alio modo convenit alicui quod non possit non esse, ex aliquo extrinseco, vel fine vel agente. Fine quidem, sicut cum aliquis non potest sine hoc consequi, aut bene consequi finem aliquem, ut cibus dicitur necessarius ad vitam, et equus ad iter. Et haec vocatur necessitas finis; quae interdum etiam utilitas dicitur. Ex agente autem hoc alicui convenit, sicut cum aliquis cogitur ab aliquo agente, ita quod non possit contrarium agere. Et haec vocatur necessitas coactionis. Haec igitur coactionis necessitas omnino repugnat voluntati. Nam hoc dicimus esse violentum, quod est contra inclinationem rei. Ipse autem motus voluntatis est inclinatio quaedam in aliquid. Et ideo sicut dicitur aliquid naturale quia est secundum inclinationem naturae, ita dicitur aliquid voluntarium quia est secundum inclinationem voluntatis. Sicut ergo impossibile est quod aliquid simul sit violentum et naturale; ita impossibile est quod aliquid simpliciter sit coactum sive violentum, et voluntarium. Necessitas autem finis non repugnat voluntati, quando ad finem non potest perveniri nisi uno modo, sicut ex voluntate transeundi mare, fit necessitas in voluntate ut velit navem. Similiter etiam nec necessitas naturalis repugnat voluntati. Quinimmo necesse est quod, sicut intellectus ex necessitate inhaeret primis principiis, ita voluntas ex necessitate inhaereat ultimo fini, qui est beatitudo, finis enim se habet in operativis sicut principium in speculativis, ut dicitur in II Physic. Oportet enim quod illud quod naturaliter alicui convenit et immobiliter, sit fundamentum et principium omnium aliorum, quia natura rei est primum in unoquoque, et omnis motus procedit ab aliquo immobili.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31876)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod verbum Augustini est intelligendum de necessario necessitate coactionis. Necessitas autem naturalis non aufert libertatem voluntatis, ut ipsemet in eodem libro dicit.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31877)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod voluntas secundum quod aliquid naturaliter vult, magis respondet intellectui naturalium principiorum, quam rationi, quae ad opposita se habet. Unde secundum hoc, magis est intellectualis quam rationalis potestas.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31878)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod sumus domini nostrorum actuum secundum quod possumus hoc vel illud eligere. Electio autem non est de fine, sed de his quae sunt ad finem, ut dicitur in III Ethic. Unde appetitus ultimi finis non est de his quorum domini sumus.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 82 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31879)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntas ex necessitate omnia velit quaecumque vult. Dicit enim Dionysius, IV de Div. Nom., quod malum est praeter voluntatem. Ex necessitate ergo voluntas tendit in bonum sibi propositum.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31880)\n\nPraeterea, obiectum voluntatis comparatur ad ipsam sicut movens ad mobile. Sed motus mobilis necessario consequitur ex movente. Ergo videtur quod obiectum voluntatis ex necessitate moveat ipsam.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31881)\n\nPraeterea, sicut apprehensum secundum sensum est obiectum appetitus sensitivi, ita apprehensum secundum intellectum est obiectum intellectivi appetitus, qui dicitur voluntas. Sed apprehensum secundum sensum ex necessitate movet appetitum sensitivum, dicit enim Augustinus, super Gen. ad Litt., quod animalia moventur visis. Ergo videtur quod apprehensum secundum intellectum ex necessitate moveat voluntatem.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31882)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, quod *voluntas est qua peccatur et recte vivitur*, et sic se habet ad opposita. Non ergo ex necessitate vult quaecumque vult.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31883)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod voluntas non ex necessitate vult quaecumque vult. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod sicut intellectus naturaliter et ex necessitate inhaeret primis principiis, ita voluntas ultimo fini, ut iam dictum est. Sunt autem quaedam intelligibilia quae non habent necessariam connexionem ad prima principia; sicut contingentes propositiones, ad quarum remotionem non sequitur remotio primorum principiorum. Et talibus non ex necessitate assentit intellectus. Quaedam autem propositiones sunt necessariae, quae habent connexionem necessariam cum primis principiis; sicut conclusiones demonstrabiles, ad quarum remotionem sequitur remotio primorum principiorum. Et his intellectus ex necessitate assentit, cognita connexione necessaria conclusionum ad principia per demonstrationis deductionem, non autem ex necessitate assentit antequam huiusmodi necessitatem connexionis per demonstrationem cognoscat. Similiter etiam est ex parte voluntatis. Sunt enim quaedam particularia bona, quae non habent necessariam connexionem ad beatitudinem, quia sine his potest aliquis esse beatus, et huiusmodi voluntas non de necessitate inhaeret. Sunt autem quaedam habentia necessariam connexionem ad beatitudinem, quibus scilicet homo Deo inhaeret, in quo solo vera beatitudo consistit. Sed tamen antequam per certitudinem divinae visionis necessitas huiusmodi connexionis demonstretur, voluntas non ex necessitate Deo inhaeret, nec his quae Dei sunt. Sed voluntas videntis Deum per essentiam, de necessitate inhaeret Deo, sicut nunc ex necessitate volumus esse beati. Patet ergo quod voluntas non ex necessitate vult quaecumque vult.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31884)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod voluntas in nihil potest tendere nisi sub ratione boni. Sed quia bonum est multiplex, propter hoc non ex necessitate determinatur ad unum.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31885)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod movens tunc ex necessitate causat motum in mobili, quando potestas moventis excedit mobile, ita quod tota eius possibilitas moventi subdatur. Cum autem possibilitas voluntatis sit respectu boni universalis et perfecti, non subiicitur eius possibilitas tota alicui particulari bono. Et ideo non ex necessitate movetur ab illo.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31886)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod vis sensitiva non est vis collativa diversorum, sicut ratio, sed simpliciter aliquid unum apprehendit. Et ideo secundum illud unum determinate movet appetitum sensitivum. Sed ratio est collativa plurium, et ideo ex pluribus moveri potest appetitus intellectivus, scilicet voluntas, et non ex uno ex necessitate.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 82 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31887)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntas sit altior potentia quam intellectus. Bonum enim et finis est obiectum voluntatis. Sed finis est prima et altissima causarum. Ergo voluntas est prima et altissima potentiarum.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31888)\n\nPraeterea, res naturales inveniuntur procedere de imperfectis ad perfecta. Et hoc etiam in potentiis animae apparet, proceditur enim de sensu ad intellectum, qui est nobilior. Sed naturalis processus est de actu intellectus in actum voluntatis. Ergo voluntas est perfectior et nobilior potentia quam intellectus.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31889)\n\nPraeterea, habitus sunt proportionati potentiis, sicut perfectiones perfectibilibus. Sed habitus quo perficitur voluntas, scilicet caritas, est nobilior habitibus quibus perficitur intellectus, dicitur enim I ad Cor. XIII, *si noverim mysteria omnia, et si habuero omnem fidem, caritatem autem non habeam, nihil sum*. Ergo voluntas est altior potentia quam intellectus.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31890)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus, in X Ethic., ponit altissimam potentiam animae esse intellectum.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31891)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod eminentia alicuius ad alterum potest attendi dupliciter, uno modo, simpliciter; alio modo, secundum quid. Consideratur autem aliquid tale simpliciter, prout est secundum seipsum tale, secundum quid autem, prout dicitur tale secundum respectum ad alterum. Si ergo intellectus et voluntas considerentur secundum se, sic intellectus eminentior invenitur. Et hoc apparet ex comparatione obiectorum ad invicem. Obiectum enim intellectus est simplicius et magis absolutum quam obiectum voluntatis, nam obiectum intellectus est ipsa ratio boni appetibilis; bonum autem appetibile, cuius ratio est in intellectu, est obiectum voluntatis. Quanto autem aliquid est simplicius et abstractius, tanto secundum se est nobilius et altius. Et ideo obiectum intellectus est altius quam obiectum voluntatis. Cum ergo propria ratio potentiae sit secundum ordinem ad obiectum, sequitur quod secundum se et simpliciter intellectus sit altior et nobilior voluntate. Secundum quid autem, et per comparationem ad alterum, voluntas invenitur interdum altior intellectu; ex eo scilicet quod obiectum voluntatis in altiori re invenitur quam obiectum intellectus. Sicut si dicerem auditum esse secundum quid nobiliorem visu, inquantum res aliqua cuius est sonus, nobilior est aliqua re cuius est color, quamvis color sit nobilior et simplicior sono. Ut enim supra dictum est, actio intellectus consistit in hoc quod ratio rei intellectae est in intelligente; actus vero voluntatis perficitur in hoc quod voluntas inclinatur ad ipsam rem prout in se est. Et ideo philosophus dicit, in VI Metaphys., quod bonum et malum, quae sunt obiecta voluntatis, sunt in rebus; verum et falsum, quae sunt obiecta intellectus, sunt in mente. Quando igitur res in qua est bonum, est nobilior ipsa anima, in qua est ratio intellecta; per comparationem ad talem rem, voluntas est altior intellectu. Quando vero res in qua est bonum, est infra animam; tunc etiam per comparationem ad talem rem, intellectus est altior voluntate. Unde melior est amor Dei quam cognitio, e contrario autem melior est cognitio rerum corporalium quam amor. Simpliciter tamen intellectus est nobilior quam voluntas.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31892)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ratio causae accipitur secundum comparationem unius ad alterum, et in tali comparatione ratio boni principalior invenitur, sed verum dicitur magis absolute, et ipsius boni rationem significat. Unde et bonum quoddam verum est. Sed rursus et ipsum verum est quoddam bonum; secundum quod intellectus res quaedam est, et verum finis ipsius. Et inter alios fines iste finis est excellentior; sicut intellectus inter alias potentias.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31893)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod illud quod est prius generatione et tempore, est imperfectius, quia in uno et eodem potentia tempore praecedit actum, et imperfectio perfectionem. Sed illud quod est prius simpliciter et secundum naturae ordinem, est perfectius, sic enim actus est prior potentia. Et hoc modo intellectus est prior voluntate, sicut motivum mobili, et activum passivo, bonum enim intellectum movet voluntatem.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31894)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod illa ratio procedit de voluntate secundum comparationem ad id quod supra animam est. Virtus enim caritatis est qua Deum amamus.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 82 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31895)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntas non moveat intellectum. Movens enim est nobilius et prius moto, quia movens est agens; agens autem est nobilius patiente, ut Augustinus dicit XII super Gen. ad Litt., et philosophus in III de anima. Sed intellectus est prior et nobilior voluntate, ut supra dictum est. Ergo voluntas non movet intellectum.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31896)\n\nPraeterea, movens non movetur a moto, nisi forte per accidens. Sed intellectus movet voluntatem, quia appetibile apprehensum per intellectum est movens non motum; appetitus autem movens motum. Ergo intellectus non movetur a voluntate.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31897)\n\nPraeterea, nihil velle possumus nisi sit intellectum. Si igitur ad intelligendum movet voluntas volendo intelligere, oportebit quod etiam illud velle praecedat aliud intelligere, et illud intelligere aliud velle, et sic in infinitum, quod est impossibile. Non ergo voluntas movet intellectum.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31898)\n\nSed contra est quod Damascenus dicit, quod *in nobis est percipere quamcumque volumus artem, et non percipere*. In nobis autem est aliquid per voluntatem; percipimus autem artes per intellectum. Voluntas ergo movet intellectum.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31899)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod aliquid dicitur movere dupliciter. Uno modo, per modum finis; sicut dicitur quod finis movet efficientem. Et hoc modo intellectus movet voluntatem, quia bonum intellectum est obiectum voluntatis, et movet ipsam ut finis. Alio modo dicitur aliquid movere per modum agentis; sicut alterans movet alteratum, et impellens movet impulsum. Et hoc modo voluntas movet intellectum, et omnes animae vires; ut Anselmus dicit in libro de similitudinibus. Cuius ratio est, quia in omnibus potentiis activis ordinatis, illa potentia quae respicit finem universalem, movet potentias quae respiciunt fines particulares. Et hoc apparet tam in naturalibus quam in politicis. Caelum enim, quod agit ad universalem conservationem generabilium et corruptibilium, movet omnia inferiora corpora, quorum unumquodque agit ad conservationem propriae speciei, vel etiam individui. Rex etiam, qui intendit bonum commune totius regni, movet per suum imperium singulos praepositos civitatum, qui singulis civitatibus curam regiminis impendunt. Obiectum autem voluntatis est bonum et finis in communi. Quaelibet autem potentia comparatur ad aliquod bonum proprium sibi conveniens; sicut visus ad perceptionem coloris, intellectus ad cognitionem veri. Et ideo voluntas per modum agentis movet omnes animae potentias ad suos actus, praeter vires naturales vegetativae partis, quae nostro arbitrio non subduntur.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31900)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod intellectus dupliciter considerari potest, uno modo, secundum quod intellectus est apprehensivus entis et veri universalis; alio modo, secundum quod est quaedam res, et particularis potentia habens determinatum actum. Et similiter voluntas dupliciter considerari potest, uno modo, secundum communitatem sui obiecti, prout scilicet est appetitiva boni communis; alio modo, secundum quod est quaedam determinata animae potentia habens determinatum actum. Si ergo comparentur intellectus et voluntas secundum rationem communitatis obiectorum utriusque, sic dictum est supra quod intellectus est simpliciter altior et nobilior voluntate. Si autem consideretur intellectus secundum communitatem sui obiecti, et voluntas secundum quod est quaedam determinata potentia, sic iterum intellectus est altior et prior voluntate, quia sub ratione entis et veri, quam apprehendit intellectus, continetur voluntas ipsa, et actus eius, et obiectum ipsius. Unde intellectus intelligit voluntatem, et actum eius, et obiectum ipsius, sicut et alia specialia intellecta, ut lapidem aut lignum, quae continentur sub communi ratione entis et veri. Si vero consideretur voluntas secundum communem rationem sui obiecti, quod est bonum, intellectus autem secundum quod est quaedam res et potentia specialis; sic sub communi ratione boni continetur, velut quoddam speciale, et intellectus ipse, et ipsum intelligere, et obiectum eius, quod est verum, quorum quodlibet est quoddam speciale bonum. Et secundum hoc voluntas est altior intellectu, et potest ipsum movere. Ex his ergo apparet ratio quare hae potentiae suis actibus invicem se includunt, quia intellectus intelligit voluntatem velle, et voluntas vult intellectum intelligere. Et simili ratione bonum continetur sub vero, inquantum est quoddam verum intellectum; et verum continetur sub bono, inquantum est quoddam bonum desideratum.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31901)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod intellectus alio modo movet voluntatem, quam voluntas intellectum, ut iam dictum est.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31902)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod non oportet procedere in infinitum, sed statur in intellectu sicut in primo. Omnem enim voluntatis motum necesse est quod praecedat apprehensio, sed non omnem apprehensionem praecedit motus voluntatis; sed principium consiliandi et intelligendi est aliquod intellectivum principium altius intellectu nostro, quod est Deus, ut etiam Aristoteles dicit in VII Ethicae Eudemicae, et per hunc modum ostendit quod non est procedere in infinitum.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 82 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31903)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod irascibilis et concupiscibilis distingui debeant in appetitu superiori, qui est voluntas. Vis enim concupiscibilis dicitur a concupiscendo; et irascibilis ab irascendo. Sed aliqua concupiscentia est quae non potest pertinere ad appetitum sensitivum, sed solum ad intellectivum, qui est voluntas; sicut concupiscentia sapientiae, de qua dicitur Sap. VI, *concupiscentia sapientiae perducit ad regnum perpetuum*. Est etiam quaedam ira quae non potest pertinere ad appetitum sensitivum, sed intellectivum tantum; sicut cum irascimur contra vitia. Unde et Hieronymus, super Matth., monet ut *odium vitiorum possideamus in irascibili*. Ergo irascibilis et concupiscibilis distingui debent in appetitu intellectivo, sicut et in sensitivo.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31904)\n\nPraeterea, secundum quod communiter dicitur, caritas est in concupiscibili, spes autem in irascibili. Non autem possunt esse in appetitu sensitivo, quia non sunt sensibilium obiectorum, sed intelligibilium. Ergo concupiscibilis et irascibilis sunt ponenda in parte intellectiva.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31905)\n\nPraeterea, in libro de spiritu et anima dicitur quod has potentias (scilicet irascibilem et concupiscibilem, et rationalem) *habet anima antequam corpori misceatur*. Sed nulla potentia sensitivae partis est animae tantum, sed coniuncti, ut supra dictum est. Ergo irascibilis et concupiscibilis sunt in voluntate, quae est appetitus intellectivus.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31906)\n\nSed contra est quod Gregorius Nyssenus, dicit, quod irrationalis pars animae dividitur in desiderativum et irascitivum; et idem dicit Damascenus, in libro II. Et philosophus dicit, in III de anima quod *voluntas in ratione est, in irrationali autem parte animae concupiscentia et ira, vel desiderium et animus*.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31907)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod irascibilis et concupiscibilis non sunt partes intellectivi appetitus, qui dicitur voluntas. Quia, sicut supra dictum est, potentia quae ordinatur ad aliquod obiectum secundum communem rationem, non diversificatur per differentias speciales sub illa ratione communi contentas. Sicut quia visus respicit visibile secundum rationem colorati, non multiplicantur visivae potentiae secundum diversas species colorum, si autem esset aliqua potentia quae esset albi inquantum est album, et non inquantum est coloratum, diversificaretur a potentia quae esset nigri inquantum est nigrum. Appetitus autem sensitivus non respicit communem rationem boni, quia nec sensus apprehendit universale. Et ideo secundum diversas rationes particularium bonorum, diversificantur partes appetitus sensitivi, nam concupiscibilis respicit propriam rationem boni, inquantum est delectabile secundum sensum, et conveniens naturae; irascibilis autem respicit rationem boni, secundum quod est repulsivum et impugnativum eius quod infert nocumentum. Sed voluntas respicit bonum sub communi ratione boni. Et ideo non diversificantur in ipsa, quae est appetitus intellectivus, aliquae potentiae appetitivae, ut sit in appetitu intellectivo alia potentia irascibilis, et alia concupiscibilis, sicut etiam ex parte intellectus non multiplicantur vires apprehensivae, licet multiplicentur ex parte sensus.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31908)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod amor, concupiscentia, et huiusmodi, dupliciter accipiuntur. Quandoque quidem secundum quod sunt quaedam passiones, cum quadam scilicet concitatione animi provenientes. Et sic communiter accipiuntur, et hoc modo sunt solum in appetitu sensitivo. Alio modo significant simplicem affectum, absque passione vel animi concitatione. Et sic sunt actus voluntatis. Et hoc etiam modo attribuuntur Angelis et Deo. Sed prout sic accipiuntur, non pertinent ad diversas potentias, sed ad unam tantum potentiam, quae dicitur voluntas.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31909)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ipsa voluntas potest dici irascibilis, prout vult impugnare malum, non ex impetu passionis, sed ex iudicio rationis. Et eodem modo potest dici concupiscibilis, propter desiderium boni. Et sic in irascibili et concupiscibili sunt caritas et spes; idest in voluntate secundum quod habet ordinem ad huiusmodi actus. Sic etiam potest intelligi quod dicitur in libro de spiritu et anima, quod irascibilis et concupiscibilis sunt animae antequam uniatur corpori (ut tamen intelligatur ordo naturae, et non temporis), licet non sit necessarium verbis illius libri fidem adhibere.\n\n[I q. 82 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31910)\n\nUnde patet solutio ad tertium.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0002","text":"[I q. 82 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31870)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de voluntate. Circa quam quaeruntur quinque. Primo, utrum voluntas aliquid ex necessitate appetat. Secundo, utrum omnia ex necessitate appetat. Tertio, utrum sit eminentior potentia quam intellectus. Quarto, utrum voluntas moveat intellectum. Quinto, utrum voluntas distinguatur per irascibilem et concupiscibilem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0005","text":"[I q. 82 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31871)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntas nihil ex necessitate appetat. Dicit enim Augustinus, in V de Civ. Dei, quod si aliquid est necessarium, non est voluntarium. Sed omne quod voluntas appetit, est voluntarium. Ergo nihil quod voluntas appetit, est necessario desideratum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0007","text":"[I q. 82 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31872)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0008","text":"Praeterea, potestates rationales, secundum philosophum, se habent ad opposita. Sed voluntas est potestas rationalis, quia, ut dicitur in III de anima, voluntas in ratione est. Ergo voluntas se habet ad opposita. Ad nihil ergo de necessitate determinatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0009","text":"[I q. 82 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31873)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0010","text":"Praeterea, secundum voluntatem sumus domini nostrorum actuum. Sed eius quod ex necessitate est, non sumus domini. Ergo actus voluntatis non potest de necessitate esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0011","text":"[I q. 82 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31874)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in XIII de Trin., quod *beatitudinem omnes una voluntate appetunt*. Si autem non esset necessarium sed contingens, deficeret ad minus in paucioribus. Ergo voluntas ex necessitate aliquid vult.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0013","text":"[I q. 82 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31875)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necessitas dicitur multipliciter. Necesse est enim quod non potest non esse. Quod quidem convenit alicui, uno modo ex principio intrinseco, sive materiali, sicut cum dicimus quod omne compositum ex contrariis necesse est corrumpi; sive formali, sicut cum dicimus quod necesse est triangulum habere tres angulos aequales duobus rectis. Et haec est necessitas naturalis et absoluta. Alio modo convenit alicui quod non possit non esse, ex aliquo extrinseco, vel fine vel agente. Fine quidem, sicut cum aliquis non potest sine hoc consequi, aut bene consequi finem aliquem, ut cibus dicitur necessarius ad vitam, et equus ad iter. Et haec vocatur necessitas finis; quae interdum etiam utilitas dicitur. Ex agente autem hoc alicui convenit, sicut cum aliquis cogitur ab aliquo agente, ita quod non possit contrarium agere. Et haec vocatur necessitas coactionis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0015","text":"Haec igitur coactionis necessitas omnino repugnat voluntati. Nam hoc dicimus esse violentum, quod est contra inclinationem rei. Ipse autem motus voluntatis est inclinatio quaedam in aliquid. Et ideo sicut dicitur aliquid naturale quia est secundum inclinationem naturae, ita dicitur aliquid voluntarium quia est secundum inclinationem voluntatis. Sicut ergo impossibile est quod aliquid simul sit violentum et naturale; ita impossibile est quod aliquid simpliciter sit coactum sive violentum, et voluntarium. Necessitas autem finis non repugnat voluntati, quando ad finem non potest perveniri nisi uno modo, sicut ex voluntate transeundi mare, fit necessitas in voluntate ut velit navem. Similiter etiam nec necessitas naturalis repugnat voluntati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0016","text":"Quinimmo necesse est quod, sicut intellectus ex necessitate inhaeret primis principiis, ita voluntas ex necessitate inhaereat ultimo fini, qui est beatitudo, finis enim se habet in operativis sicut principium in speculativis, ut dicitur in II Physic. Oportet enim quod illud quod naturaliter alicui convenit et immobiliter, sit fundamentum et principium omnium aliorum, quia natura rei est primum in unoquoque, et omnis motus procedit ab aliquo immobili.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0017","text":"[I q. 82 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31876)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0018","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod verbum Augustini est intelligendum de necessario necessitate coactionis. Necessitas autem naturalis non aufert libertatem voluntatis, ut ipsemet in eodem libro dicit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0019","text":"[I q. 82 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31877)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod voluntas secundum quod aliquid naturaliter vult, magis respondet intellectui naturalium principiorum, quam rationi, quae ad opposita se habet. Unde secundum hoc, magis est intellectualis quam rationalis potestas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0021","text":"[I q. 82 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31878)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod sumus domini nostrorum actuum secundum quod possumus hoc vel illud eligere. Electio autem non est de fine, sed de his quae sunt ad finem, ut dicitur in III Ethic. Unde appetitus ultimi finis non est de his quorum domini sumus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0023","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0024","text":"[I q. 82 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31879)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0025","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntas ex necessitate omnia velit quaecumque vult. Dicit enim Dionysius, IV de Div. Nom., quod malum est praeter voluntatem. Ex necessitate ergo voluntas tendit in bonum sibi propositum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0026","text":"[I q. 82 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31880)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0027","text":"Praeterea, obiectum voluntatis comparatur ad ipsam sicut movens ad mobile. Sed motus mobilis necessario consequitur ex movente. Ergo videtur quod obiectum voluntatis ex necessitate moveat ipsam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0028","text":"[I q. 82 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31881)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0029","text":"Praeterea, sicut apprehensum secundum sensum est obiectum appetitus sensitivi, ita apprehensum secundum intellectum est obiectum intellectivi appetitus, qui dicitur voluntas. Sed apprehensum secundum sensum ex necessitate movet appetitum sensitivum, dicit enim Augustinus, super Gen. ad Litt., quod animalia moventur visis. Ergo videtur quod apprehensum secundum intellectum ex necessitate moveat voluntatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0030","text":"[I q. 82 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31882)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0031","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, quod *voluntas est qua peccatur et recte vivitur*, et sic se habet ad opposita. Non ergo ex necessitate vult quaecumque vult.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0032","text":"[I q. 82 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31883)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0033","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod voluntas non ex necessitate vult quaecumque vult. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod sicut intellectus naturaliter et ex necessitate inhaeret primis principiis, ita voluntas ultimo fini, ut iam dictum est. Sunt autem quaedam intelligibilia quae non habent necessariam connexionem ad prima principia; sicut contingentes propositiones, ad quarum remotionem non sequitur remotio primorum principiorum. Et talibus non ex necessitate assentit intellectus. Quaedam autem propositiones sunt necessariae, quae habent connexionem necessariam cum primis principiis; sicut conclusiones demonstrabiles, ad quarum remotionem sequitur remotio primorum principiorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0034","text":"Et his intellectus ex necessitate assentit, cognita connexione necessaria conclusionum ad principia per demonstrationis deductionem, non autem ex necessitate assentit antequam huiusmodi necessitatem connexionis per demonstrationem cognoscat. Similiter etiam est ex parte voluntatis. Sunt enim quaedam particularia bona, quae non habent necessariam connexionem ad beatitudinem, quia sine his potest aliquis esse beatus, et huiusmodi voluntas non de necessitate inhaeret. Sunt autem quaedam habentia necessariam connexionem ad beatitudinem, quibus scilicet homo Deo inhaeret, in quo solo vera beatitudo consistit. Sed tamen antequam per certitudinem divinae visionis necessitas huiusmodi connexionis demonstretur, voluntas non ex necessitate Deo inhaeret, nec his quae Dei sunt. Sed voluntas videntis Deum per essentiam, de necessitate inhaeret Deo, sicut nunc ex necessitate volumus esse beati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0035","text":"Patet ergo quod voluntas non ex necessitate vult quaecumque vult.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0036","text":"[I q. 82 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31884)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0037","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod voluntas in nihil potest tendere nisi sub ratione boni. Sed quia bonum est multiplex, propter hoc non ex necessitate determinatur ad unum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0038","text":"[I q. 82 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31885)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0039","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod movens tunc ex necessitate causat motum in mobili, quando potestas moventis excedit mobile, ita quod tota eius possibilitas moventi subdatur. Cum autem possibilitas voluntatis sit respectu boni universalis et perfecti, non subiicitur eius possibilitas tota alicui particulari bono. Et ideo non ex necessitate movetur ab illo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0040","text":"[I q. 82 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31886)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0041","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod vis sensitiva non est vis collativa diversorum, sicut ratio, sed simpliciter aliquid unum apprehendit. Et ideo secundum illud unum determinate movet appetitum sensitivum. Sed ratio est collativa plurium, et ideo ex pluribus moveri potest appetitus intellectivus, scilicet voluntas, et non ex uno ex necessitate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0042","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0043","text":"[I q. 82 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31887)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0044","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntas sit altior potentia quam intellectus. Bonum enim et finis est obiectum voluntatis. Sed finis est prima et altissima causarum. Ergo voluntas est prima et altissima potentiarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0045","text":"[I q. 82 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31888)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0046","text":"Praeterea, res naturales inveniuntur procedere de imperfectis ad perfecta. Et hoc etiam in potentiis animae apparet, proceditur enim de sensu ad intellectum, qui est nobilior. Sed naturalis processus est de actu intellectus in actum voluntatis. Ergo voluntas est perfectior et nobilior potentia quam intellectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0047","text":"[I q. 82 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31889)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0048","text":"Praeterea, habitus sunt proportionati potentiis, sicut perfectiones perfectibilibus. Sed habitus quo perficitur voluntas, scilicet caritas, est nobilior habitibus quibus perficitur intellectus, dicitur enim I ad Cor. XIII, *si noverim mysteria omnia, et si habuero omnem fidem, caritatem autem non habeam, nihil sum*. Ergo voluntas est altior potentia quam intellectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0049","text":"[I q. 82 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31890)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0050","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus, in X Ethic., ponit altissimam potentiam animae esse intellectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0051","text":"[I q. 82 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31891)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0052","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod eminentia alicuius ad alterum potest attendi dupliciter, uno modo, simpliciter; alio modo, secundum quid. Consideratur autem aliquid tale simpliciter, prout est secundum seipsum tale, secundum quid autem, prout dicitur tale secundum respectum ad alterum. Si ergo intellectus et voluntas considerentur secundum se, sic intellectus eminentior invenitur. Et hoc apparet ex comparatione obiectorum ad invicem. Obiectum enim intellectus est simplicius et magis absolutum quam obiectum voluntatis, nam obiectum intellectus est ipsa ratio boni appetibilis; bonum autem appetibile, cuius ratio est in intellectu, est obiectum voluntatis. Quanto autem aliquid est simplicius et abstractius, tanto secundum se est nobilius et altius. Et ideo obiectum intellectus est altius quam obiectum voluntatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0053","text":"Cum ergo propria ratio potentiae sit secundum ordinem ad obiectum, sequitur quod secundum se et simpliciter intellectus sit altior et nobilior voluntate. Secundum quid autem, et per comparationem ad alterum, voluntas invenitur interdum altior intellectu; ex eo scilicet quod obiectum voluntatis in altiori re invenitur quam obiectum intellectus. Sicut si dicerem auditum esse secundum quid nobiliorem visu, inquantum res aliqua cuius est sonus, nobilior est aliqua re cuius est color, quamvis color sit nobilior et simplicior sono. Ut enim supra dictum est, actio intellectus consistit in hoc quod ratio rei intellectae est in intelligente; actus vero voluntatis perficitur in hoc quod voluntas inclinatur ad ipsam rem prout in se est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0054","text":"Et ideo philosophus dicit, in VI Metaphys., quod bonum et malum, quae sunt obiecta voluntatis, sunt in rebus; verum et falsum, quae sunt obiecta intellectus, sunt in mente. Quando igitur res in qua est bonum, est nobilior ipsa anima, in qua est ratio intellecta; per comparationem ad talem rem, voluntas est altior intellectu. Quando vero res in qua est bonum, est infra animam; tunc etiam per comparationem ad talem rem, intellectus est altior voluntate. Unde melior est amor Dei quam cognitio, e contrario autem melior est cognitio rerum corporalium quam amor. Simpliciter tamen intellectus est nobilior quam voluntas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0055","text":"[I q. 82 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31892)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0056","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ratio causae accipitur secundum comparationem unius ad alterum, et in tali comparatione ratio boni principalior invenitur, sed verum dicitur magis absolute, et ipsius boni rationem significat. Unde et bonum quoddam verum est. Sed rursus et ipsum verum est quoddam bonum; secundum quod intellectus res quaedam est, et verum finis ipsius. Et inter alios fines iste finis est excellentior; sicut intellectus inter alias potentias.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0057","text":"[I q. 82 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31893)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0058","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod illud quod est prius generatione et tempore, est imperfectius, quia in uno et eodem potentia tempore praecedit actum, et imperfectio perfectionem. Sed illud quod est prius simpliciter et secundum naturae ordinem, est perfectius, sic enim actus est prior potentia. Et hoc modo intellectus est prior voluntate, sicut motivum mobili, et activum passivo, bonum enim intellectum movet voluntatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0059","text":"[I q. 82 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31894)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0060","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod illa ratio procedit de voluntate secundum comparationem ad id quod supra animam est. Virtus enim caritatis est qua Deum amamus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0061","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0062","text":"[I q. 82 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31895)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0063","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod voluntas non moveat intellectum. Movens enim est nobilius et prius moto, quia movens est agens; agens autem est nobilius patiente, ut Augustinus dicit XII super Gen. ad Litt., et philosophus in III de anima. Sed intellectus est prior et nobilior voluntate, ut supra dictum est. Ergo voluntas non movet intellectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0064","text":"[I q. 82 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31896)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0065","text":"Praeterea, movens non movetur a moto, nisi forte per accidens. Sed intellectus movet voluntatem, quia appetibile apprehensum per intellectum est movens non motum; appetitus autem movens motum. Ergo intellectus non movetur a voluntate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0066","text":"[I q. 82 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31897)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0067","text":"Praeterea, nihil velle possumus nisi sit intellectum. Si igitur ad intelligendum movet voluntas volendo intelligere, oportebit quod etiam illud velle praecedat aliud intelligere, et illud intelligere aliud velle, et sic in infinitum, quod est impossibile. Non ergo voluntas movet intellectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0068","text":"[I q. 82 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31898)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0069","text":"Sed contra est quod Damascenus dicit, quod *in nobis est percipere quamcumque volumus artem, et non percipere*. In nobis autem est aliquid per voluntatem; percipimus autem artes per intellectum. Voluntas ergo movet intellectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0070","text":"[I q. 82 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31899)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0071","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod aliquid dicitur movere dupliciter. Uno modo, per modum finis; sicut dicitur quod finis movet efficientem. Et hoc modo intellectus movet voluntatem, quia bonum intellectum est obiectum voluntatis, et movet ipsam ut finis. Alio modo dicitur aliquid movere per modum agentis; sicut alterans movet alteratum, et impellens movet impulsum. Et hoc modo voluntas movet intellectum, et omnes animae vires; ut Anselmus dicit in libro de similitudinibus. Cuius ratio est, quia in omnibus potentiis activis ordinatis, illa potentia quae respicit finem universalem, movet potentias quae respiciunt fines particulares. Et hoc apparet tam in naturalibus quam in politicis. Caelum enim, quod agit ad universalem conservationem generabilium et corruptibilium, movet omnia inferiora corpora, quorum unumquodque agit ad conservationem propriae speciei, vel etiam individui.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0072","text":"Rex etiam, qui intendit bonum commune totius regni, movet per suum imperium singulos praepositos civitatum, qui singulis civitatibus curam regiminis impendunt. Obiectum autem voluntatis est bonum et finis in communi. Quaelibet autem potentia comparatur ad aliquod bonum proprium sibi conveniens; sicut visus ad perceptionem coloris, intellectus ad cognitionem veri. Et ideo voluntas per modum agentis movet omnes animae potentias ad suos actus, praeter vires naturales vegetativae partis, quae nostro arbitrio non subduntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0073","text":"[I q. 82 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31900)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0074","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod intellectus dupliciter considerari potest, uno modo, secundum quod intellectus est apprehensivus entis et veri universalis; alio modo, secundum quod est quaedam res, et particularis potentia habens determinatum actum. Et similiter voluntas dupliciter considerari potest, uno modo, secundum communitatem sui obiecti, prout scilicet est appetitiva boni communis; alio modo, secundum quod est quaedam determinata animae potentia habens determinatum actum. Si ergo comparentur intellectus et voluntas secundum rationem communitatis obiectorum utriusque, sic dictum est supra quod intellectus est simpliciter altior et nobilior voluntate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0075","text":"Si autem consideretur intellectus secundum communitatem sui obiecti, et voluntas secundum quod est quaedam determinata potentia, sic iterum intellectus est altior et prior voluntate, quia sub ratione entis et veri, quam apprehendit intellectus, continetur voluntas ipsa, et actus eius, et obiectum ipsius. Unde intellectus intelligit voluntatem, et actum eius, et obiectum ipsius, sicut et alia specialia intellecta, ut lapidem aut lignum, quae continentur sub communi ratione entis et veri. Si vero consideretur voluntas secundum communem rationem sui obiecti, quod est bonum, intellectus autem secundum quod est quaedam res et potentia specialis; sic sub communi ratione boni continetur, velut quoddam speciale, et intellectus ipse, et ipsum intelligere, et obiectum eius, quod est verum, quorum quodlibet est quoddam speciale bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0076","text":"Et secundum hoc voluntas est altior intellectu, et potest ipsum movere. Ex his ergo apparet ratio quare hae potentiae suis actibus invicem se includunt, quia intellectus intelligit voluntatem velle, et voluntas vult intellectum intelligere. Et simili ratione bonum continetur sub vero, inquantum est quoddam verum intellectum; et verum continetur sub bono, inquantum est quoddam bonum desideratum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0077","text":"[I q. 82 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31901)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0078","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod intellectus alio modo movet voluntatem, quam voluntas intellectum, ut iam dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0079","text":"[I q. 82 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31902)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0080","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod non oportet procedere in infinitum, sed statur in intellectu sicut in primo. Omnem enim voluntatis motum necesse est quod praecedat apprehensio, sed non omnem apprehensionem praecedit motus voluntatis; sed principium consiliandi et intelligendi est aliquod intellectivum principium altius intellectu nostro, quod est Deus, ut etiam Aristoteles dicit in VII Ethicae Eudemicae, et per hunc modum ostendit quod non est procedere in infinitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0081","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0082","text":"[I q. 82 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31903)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0083","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod irascibilis et concupiscibilis distingui debeant in appetitu superiori, qui est voluntas. Vis enim concupiscibilis dicitur a concupiscendo; et irascibilis ab irascendo. Sed aliqua concupiscentia est quae non potest pertinere ad appetitum sensitivum, sed solum ad intellectivum, qui est voluntas; sicut concupiscentia sapientiae, de qua dicitur Sap. VI, *concupiscentia sapientiae perducit ad regnum perpetuum*. Est etiam quaedam ira quae non potest pertinere ad appetitum sensitivum, sed intellectivum tantum; sicut cum irascimur contra vitia. Unde et Hieronymus, super Matth., monet ut *odium vitiorum possideamus in irascibili*. Ergo irascibilis et concupiscibilis distingui debent in appetitu intellectivo, sicut et in sensitivo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0084","text":"[I q. 82 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31904)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0085","text":"Praeterea, secundum quod communiter dicitur, caritas est in concupiscibili, spes autem in irascibili. Non autem possunt esse in appetitu sensitivo, quia non sunt sensibilium obiectorum, sed intelligibilium. Ergo concupiscibilis et irascibilis sunt ponenda in parte intellectiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0086","text":"[I q. 82 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31905)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0087","text":"Praeterea, in libro de spiritu et anima dicitur quod has potentias (scilicet irascibilem et concupiscibilem, et rationalem) *habet anima antequam corpori misceatur*. Sed nulla potentia sensitivae partis est animae tantum, sed coniuncti, ut supra dictum est. Ergo irascibilis et concupiscibilis sunt in voluntate, quae est appetitus intellectivus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0088","text":"[I q. 82 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31906)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0089","text":"Sed contra est quod Gregorius Nyssenus, dicit, quod irrationalis pars animae dividitur in desiderativum et irascitivum; et idem dicit Damascenus, in libro II. Et philosophus dicit, in III de anima quod *voluntas in ratione est, in irrationali autem parte animae concupiscentia et ira, vel desiderium et animus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0090","text":"[I q. 82 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31907)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0091","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod irascibilis et concupiscibilis non sunt partes intellectivi appetitus, qui dicitur voluntas. Quia, sicut supra dictum est, potentia quae ordinatur ad aliquod obiectum secundum communem rationem, non diversificatur per differentias speciales sub illa ratione communi contentas. Sicut quia visus respicit visibile secundum rationem colorati, non multiplicantur visivae potentiae secundum diversas species colorum, si autem esset aliqua potentia quae esset albi inquantum est album, et non inquantum est coloratum, diversificaretur a potentia quae esset nigri inquantum est nigrum. Appetitus autem sensitivus non respicit communem rationem boni, quia nec sensus apprehendit universale.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0092","text":"Et ideo secundum diversas rationes particularium bonorum, diversificantur partes appetitus sensitivi, nam concupiscibilis respicit propriam rationem boni, inquantum est delectabile secundum sensum, et conveniens naturae; irascibilis autem respicit rationem boni, secundum quod est repulsivum et impugnativum eius quod infert nocumentum. Sed voluntas respicit bonum sub communi ratione boni. Et ideo non diversificantur in ipsa, quae est appetitus intellectivus, aliquae potentiae appetitivae, ut sit in appetitu intellectivo alia potentia irascibilis, et alia concupiscibilis, sicut etiam ex parte intellectus non multiplicantur vires apprehensivae, licet multiplicentur ex parte sensus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0093","text":"[I q. 82 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31908)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0094","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod amor, concupiscentia, et huiusmodi, dupliciter accipiuntur. Quandoque quidem secundum quod sunt quaedam passiones, cum quadam scilicet concitatione animi provenientes. Et sic communiter accipiuntur, et hoc modo sunt solum in appetitu sensitivo. Alio modo significant simplicem affectum, absque passione vel animi concitatione. Et sic sunt actus voluntatis. Et hoc etiam modo attribuuntur Angelis et Deo. Sed prout sic accipiuntur, non pertinent ad diversas potentias, sed ad unam tantum potentiam, quae dicitur voluntas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0095","text":"[I q. 82 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31909)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0096","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ipsa voluntas potest dici irascibilis, prout vult impugnare malum, non ex impetu passionis, sed ex iudicio rationis. Et eodem modo potest dici concupiscibilis, propter desiderium boni. Et sic in irascibili et concupiscibili sunt caritas et spes; idest in voluntate secundum quod habet ordinem ad huiusmodi actus. Sic etiam potest intelligi quod dicitur in libro de spiritu et anima, quod irascibilis et concupiscibilis sunt animae antequam uniatur corpori (ut tamen intelligatur ordo naturae, et non temporis), licet non sit necessarium verbis illius libri fidem adhibere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0097","text":"[I q. 82 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31910)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0098","text":"Unde patet solutio ad tertium.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"7da14e4df06538811a9a8381d0b6b4f149119b100d80fd3b048be43edf79c14e","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T151313883765Z_4c2a7612","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 82","source_sha256":"7da14e4df06538811a9a8381d0b6b4f149119b100d80fd3b048be43edf79c14e","sha256":"adfe654be3a3587254ba9726c892eebf2ad1ac285823a6874593834e36eb1b7c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:13:13.883798+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0002","text":"[I q. 82 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31870)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the will. Concerning it, five questions are asked. First, whether the will desires anything of necessity. Second, whether it desires all things of necessity. Third, whether it is a higher power than the intellect. Fourth, whether the will moves the intellect. Fifth, whether the will is distinguished by the irascible and the concupiscible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0005","text":"[I q. 82 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31871)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0006","text":"The first is approached thus. It seems that the will desires nothing of necessity. For Augustine says, in Book V of *The City of God*, that if something is necessary, it is not voluntary. But everything that the will desires is voluntary. Therefore, nothing that the will desires is desired necessarily.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0007","text":"[I q. 82 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31872)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0008","text":"Further, rational powers, according to the Philosopher, are related to opposites. But the will is a rational power, because, as is said in Book III of *On the Soul*, the will is in reason. Therefore, the will is related to opposites. Therefore, it is determined to nothing of necessity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0009","text":"[I q. 82 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31873)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0010","text":"Further, according to the will, we are masters of our acts. But we are not masters of that which is from necessity. Therefore, an act of the will cannot be of necessity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0011","text":"[I q. 82 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31874)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0012","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in Book XIII of *On the Trinity*, that *all desire beatitude by one will*. But if this were not necessary but contingent, it would fail at least in the case of some. Therefore, the will wills something of necessity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0013","text":"[I q. 82 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31875)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that necessity is spoken of in many ways. For that is necessary which cannot not be. Now this belongs to something in one way from an intrinsic principle, whether material, as when we say that every composite of contraries must be corrupted; or formal, as when we say that a triangle must have three angles equal to two right angles. And this is natural and absolute necessity. In another way, that which cannot not be belongs to something from something extrinsic, either an end or an agent. From an end indeed, as when someone cannot attain, or cannot attain well, some end without this, as food is said to be necessary for life, and a horse for a journey. And this is called necessity of the end; which is sometimes also called usefulness. But it belongs to something from an agent, as when someone is compelled by some agent, so that he cannot do the contrary. And this is called necessity of coercion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0015","text":"Therefore, this necessity of coercion is altogether repugnant to the will. For we call that violent which is contrary to the inclination of a thing. But the very movement of the will is a certain inclination toward something. And therefore, just as something is called natural because it is according to the inclination of nature, so something is called voluntary because it is according to the inclination of the will. Therefore, just as it is impossible for something to be at once violent and natural, so it is impossible for something simply to be compelled or violent, and voluntary. But necessity of the end is not repugnant to the will when an end cannot be attained except in one way, just as from the will to cross the sea there arises necessity in the will to will a ship. Likewise, natural necessity too is not repugnant to the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0016","text":"Indeed, it is necessary that, just as the intellect necessarily adheres to first principles, so the will necessarily adheres to the ultimate end, which is beatitude; for the end is related in practical matters as a principle in speculative matters, as is said in Book II of the *Physics*. For that which belongs naturally and immovably to something must be the foundation and principle of all the others, because the nature of a thing is first in each thing, and every movement proceeds from something immovable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0017","text":"[I q. 82 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31876)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0018","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Augustine's statement is to be understood of what is necessary by necessity of coercion. Natural necessity, however, does not take away the freedom of the will, as he himself says in the same book.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0019","text":"[I q. 82 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31877)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0020","text":"To the second, it must be said that insofar as the will naturally wills something, it corresponds more to the intellect of natural principles than to reason, which is related to opposites. Hence, in this respect, it is more an intellectual than a rational power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0021","text":"[I q. 82 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31878)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0022","text":"To the third, it must be said that we are masters of our acts insofar as we can choose this or that. But choice is not of the end, but of those things that are for the end, as is said in Book III of the *Ethics*. Hence, the appetite for the ultimate end is not among those things of which we are masters.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0023","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0024","text":"[I q. 82 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31879)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0025","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the will wills necessarily whatever it wills. For Dionysius says, in *On the Divine Names*, IV, that evil is outside the will. Therefore the will tends necessarily toward the good set before it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0026","text":"[I q. 82 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31880)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0027","text":"Further, the object of the will is related to it as a mover to what is moved. But the motion of what is moved necessarily follows from the mover. Therefore it seems that the object of the will necessarily moves it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0028","text":"[I q. 82 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31881)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0029","text":"Further, just as what is apprehended according to sense is the object of the sensitive appetite, so what is apprehended according to the intellect is the object of the intellective appetite, which is called the will. But what is apprehended according to sense necessarily moves the sensitive appetite; for Augustine says, in *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, that animals are moved by things seen. Therefore it seems that what is apprehended according to the intellect necessarily moves the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0030","text":"[I q. 82 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31882)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0031","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says that *the will is that by which one sins and lives rightly*, and thus it is related to opposites. Therefore it does not necessarily will whatever it wills.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0032","text":"[I q. 82 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31883)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0033","text":"I answer that the will does not necessarily will whatever it wills. To make this evident, it must be considered that, just as the intellect naturally and necessarily adheres to first principles, so the will adheres to the last end, as has already been said. Now there are certain intelligibles that do not have a necessary connection with first principles; such as contingent propositions, upon the removal of which the removal of first principles does not follow. And the intellect does not necessarily assent to such things. But certain propositions are necessary, which have a necessary connection with first principles; such as demonstrable conclusions, upon the removal of which the removal of first principles follows.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0034","text":"And the intellect necessarily assents to these, once the necessary connection of the conclusions to the principles has been known through the process of demonstration; but it does not necessarily assent before it knows through demonstration such necessity of connection. The same is likewise the case on the part of the will. For there are certain particular goods that do not have a necessary connection with beatitude, because one can be happy without them, and to such things the will does not necessarily adhere. But there are certain things having a necessary connection with beatitude, namely, those by which man adheres to God, in whom alone true beatitude consists. Yet before the necessity of such a connection is demonstrated through the certainty of the divine vision, the will does not necessarily adhere to God, nor to those things that are God's. But the will of one seeing God through His essence necessarily adheres to God, just as we now necessarily will to be happy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0035","text":"It is clear, therefore, that the will does not necessarily will whatever it wills.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0036","text":"[I q. 82 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31884)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0037","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the will can tend toward nothing except under the aspect of the good. But because the good is manifold, it is not thereby necessarily determined to one thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0038","text":"[I q. 82 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31885)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0039","text":"To the second, it must be said that a mover then necessarily causes motion in what is moved when the power of the mover exceeds what is moved, so that all its potentiality is subjected to the mover. But since the potentiality of the will is with respect to universal and perfect good, its whole potentiality is not subjected to any particular good. And therefore it is not necessarily moved by it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0040","text":"[I q. 82 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31886)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0041","text":"To the third, it must be said that the sensitive power is not a power that compares diverse things, as reason is, but apprehends simply one thing. And therefore, according to that one thing, it moves the sensitive appetite determinately. But reason compares many things, and therefore the intellective appetite, namely the will, can be moved by many things, and not necessarily by one thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0042","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0043","text":"[I q. 82 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31887)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0044","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that the will is a higher power than the intellect. For the good and the end are the object of the will. But the end is the first and highest of causes. Therefore the will is the first and highest of the powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0045","text":"[I q. 82 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31888)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0046","text":"Further, natural things are found to proceed from the imperfect to the perfect. And this also appears in the powers of the soul, for one proceeds from sense to intellect, which is nobler. But the natural process is from the act of the intellect to the act of the will. Therefore the will is a more perfect and nobler power than the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0047","text":"[I q. 82 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31889)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0048","text":"Further, habits are proportioned to powers, as perfections to perfectible things. But the habit by which the will is perfected, namely charity, is nobler than the habits by which the intellect is perfected; for it is said in I Corinthians XIII: *if I should know all mysteries, and if I should have all faith, but should not have charity, I am nothing*. Therefore the will is a higher power than the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0049","text":"[I q. 82 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31890)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0050","text":"But on the contrary, the Philosopher, in Ethics X, holds that the highest power of the soul is the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0051","text":"[I q. 82 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31891)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0052","text":"I answer that the preeminence of one thing over another can be considered in two ways: in one way, absolutely; in another way, relatively. For something is considered such absolutely insofar as it is such in itself, but relatively insofar as it is called such according to its relation to another. If, therefore, intellect and will are considered in themselves, the intellect is found to be more eminent. And this is apparent from the comparison of their objects with one another. For the object of the intellect is simpler and more absolute than the object of the will, since the object of the intellect is the very ratio of the desirable good; but the desirable good, whose ratio is in the intellect, is the object of the will. Now the simpler and more abstract anything is, the nobler and higher it is in itself. And therefore the object of the intellect is higher than the object of the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0053","text":"Therefore, since the proper ratio of a power is according to its ordering to its object, it follows that in itself and absolutely the intellect is higher and nobler than the will. Relatively, however, and by comparison with another, the will is sometimes found to be higher than the intellect; namely, because the object of the will is found in a higher thing than the object of the intellect. Thus, if I were to say that hearing is relatively nobler than sight, insofar as some thing of which sound is the attribute is nobler than some thing of which color is the attribute, although color is nobler and simpler than sound. For, as was said above, the action of the intellect consists in this, that the ratio of the thing understood is in the one understanding; but the act of the will is perfected in this, that the will is inclined toward the very thing as it is in itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0054","text":"And therefore the Philosopher says, in Metaphysics VI, that good and evil, which are the objects of the will, are in things; whereas truth and falsity, which are the objects of the intellect, are in the mind. Therefore, when the thing in which the good is, is nobler than the soul itself, in which the intelligible ratio is, then, by comparison with such a thing, the will is higher than the intellect. But when the thing in which the good is, is below the soul, then, by comparison with such a thing, the intellect is higher than the will. Hence love of God is better than knowledge of God; conversely, knowledge of corporeal things is better than love of them. Absolutely, however, the intellect is nobler than the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0055","text":"[I q. 82 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31892)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0056","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the notion of cause is taken according to the comparison of one thing with another, and in such a comparison the notion of good is found to be more principal; but the true is spoken of more absolutely, and signifies the very notion of the good. Hence a certain good is true. But conversely, the true itself is a certain good, insofar as the intellect is a certain thing, and truth is its end. And among other ends this end is more excellent, just as the intellect is among the other powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0057","text":"[I q. 82 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31893)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0058","text":"To the second, it must be said that what is prior in generation and time is more imperfect, because in one and the same thing potency temporally precedes act, and imperfection perfection. But what is prior absolutely and according to the order of nature is more perfect; for in this way act is prior to potency. And in this way the intellect is prior to the will, as a mover to what is moved, and as active to passive; for the understood good moves the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0059","text":"[I q. 82 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31894)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0060","text":"To the third, it must be said that that argument proceeds concerning the will in comparison with that which is above the soul. For charity is the virtue by which we love God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0061","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0062","text":"[I q. 82 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31895)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0063","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that the will does not move the intellect. For that which moves is nobler and prior to that which is moved, because that which moves is an agent; but an agent is nobler than that which undergoes, as Augustine says in XII On the Literal Meaning of Genesis, and the Philosopher in On the Soul III. But the intellect is prior to and nobler than the will, as was said above. Therefore the will does not move the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0064","text":"[I q. 82 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31896)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0065","text":"Further, what moves is not moved by what is moved, except perhaps accidentally. But the intellect moves the will, because the appetible thing apprehended by the intellect is a mover not moved; whereas appetite is a mover moved. Therefore the intellect is not moved by the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0066","text":"[I q. 82 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31897)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0067","text":"Further, we can will nothing unless it is understood. If, therefore, the will moves to understanding by willing to understand, it will be necessary that another understanding also precede that willing, and another willing that understanding, and thus to infinity, which is impossible. Therefore the will does not move the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0068","text":"[I q. 82 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31898)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0069","text":"But on the contrary, Damascene says that *it is in us to learn whatever art we will, and not to learn it*. But that which is in us is by the will; and we learn arts through the intellect. Therefore the will moves the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0070","text":"[I q. 82 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31899)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0071","text":"I answer that something is said to move in two ways. In one way, by way of an end; as the end is said to move the efficient cause. And in this way the intellect moves the will, because the understood good is the object of the will, and moves it as an end. In another way, something is said to move by way of an agent; as that which alters moves what is altered, and that which impels moves what is impelled. And in this way the will moves the intellect, and all the powers of the soul, as Anselm says in the book On Similitudes. The reason for this is that, among all ordered active powers, the power which regards the universal end moves the powers which regard particular ends. And this is apparent both in natural and in political matters. For the heaven, which acts for the universal preservation of things capable of generation and corruption, moves all lower bodies, each of which acts for the preservation of its own species, or even of the individual.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0072","text":"Likewise, a king, who intends the common good of the whole kingdom, moves by his command the individual governors of cities, who devote the care of government to the individual cities. But the object of the will is good and end in common. Every power, however, is related to some good proper and suitable to it; as sight to the perception of color, and intellect to the knowledge of truth. And therefore the will, by way of an agent, moves all the powers of the soul to their acts, except the natural powers of the vegetative part, which are not subject to our choice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0073","text":"[I q. 82 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31900)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0074","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that the intellect can be considered in two ways: in one way, insofar as the intellect is apprehensive of universal being and truth; in another way, insofar as it is a certain thing and a particular power having a determinate act. And similarly the will can be considered in two ways: in one way, according to the commonness of its object, namely insofar as it is appetitive of the common good; in another way, insofar as it is a certain determinate power of the soul having a determinate act. If, therefore, intellect and will are compared according to the character of the commonness of the objects of each, then, as was said above, intellect is simply higher and nobler than will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0075","text":"But if intellect is considered according to the commonness of its object, and will insofar as it is a certain determinate power, then again intellect is higher and prior to will, because under the character of being and truth, which intellect apprehends, the will itself, its act, and its object are contained. Hence the intellect understands the will, its act, and its object, just as it understands other particular intelligibles, such as a stone or wood, which are contained under the common character of being and truth. But if will is considered according to the common character of its object, which is good, while intellect is considered insofar as it is a certain thing and special power, then under the common character of good are contained, as something special, both intellect itself, understanding itself, and its object, which is truth, each of which is a certain special good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0076","text":"And in this respect will is higher than intellect, and can move it. From these things, therefore, the reason is apparent why these powers mutually include one another in their acts: because intellect understands that the will wills, and the will wills that the intellect understand. And by a like reason good is contained under truth, insofar as it is a certain understood truth; and truth is contained under good, insofar as it is a certain desired good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0077","text":"[I q. 82 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31901)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0078","text":"To the second it must be said that intellect moves will in one way, and will intellect in another way, as has already been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0079","text":"[I q. 82 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31902)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0080","text":"To the third it must be said that it is not necessary to proceed to infinity, but one stops at the intellect as at a first principle. For every movement of the will must be preceded by apprehension, but not every apprehension is preceded by a movement of the will; rather, the principle of deliberating and understanding is some intellectual principle higher than our intellect, which is God, as Aristotle also says in Book VII of the Eudemian Ethics, and in this way he shows that there is no proceeding to infinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0081","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0082","text":"[I q. 82 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31903)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0083","text":"The fifth point is approached thus. It seems that the irascible and concupiscible should be distinguished in the higher appetite, which is the will. For the concupiscible power is so called from desiring, and the irascible from becoming angry. But there is some desire that cannot belong to the sensitive appetite, but only to the intellectual appetite, which is the will; such as desire for wisdom, concerning which Wisdom VI says, *the desire for wisdom leads to an everlasting kingdom*. There is also a certain anger that cannot belong to the sensitive appetite, but only to the intellectual; as when we are angry against vices. Hence Jerome also, commenting on Matthew, admonishes us that *we should possess hatred of vices in the irascible power*. Therefore the irascible and concupiscible should be distinguished in the intellectual appetite, as they are in the sensitive appetite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0084","text":"[I q. 82 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31904)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0085","text":"Further, as is commonly said, charity is in the concupiscible, but hope in the irascible. But they cannot be in the sensitive appetite, because they are not of sensible objects but of intelligible ones. Therefore the concupiscible and irascible must be posited in the intellectual part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0086","text":"[I q. 82 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31905)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0087","text":"Further, in the book *On Spirit and Soul* it is said that the soul *has these powers—namely, the irascible, concupiscible, and rational—before it is united with the body*. But no power of the sensitive part belongs to the soul alone, but to the composite, as was said above. Therefore the irascible and concupiscible are in the will, which is the intellectual appetite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0088","text":"[I q. 82 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31906)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0089","text":"But on the contrary, Gregory of Nyssa says that the irrational part of the soul is divided into the desiring and the irascible; and Damascene says the same in Book II. And the Philosopher says, in *On the Soul* III, that *the will is in reason, but in the irrational part of the soul are concupiscence and anger, or desire and spirit*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0090","text":"[I q. 82 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31907)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0091","text":"I answer that it must be said that the irascible and concupiscible are not parts of the intellectual appetite, which is called will. For, as was said above, a power ordered to an object according to its common character is not diversified by the specific differences contained under that common character. Thus, because sight regards the visible according to the character of the colored, visual powers are not multiplied according to the various species of colors; but if there were some power of white insofar as it is white, and not insofar as it is colored, it would be diversified from a power of black insofar as it is black. But the sensitive appetite does not regard the common character of good, because sense does not apprehend the universal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0092","text":"And therefore, according to the diverse characters of particular goods, the parts of the sensitive appetite are diversified. For the concupiscible regards the proper character of good, insofar as it is pleasurable according to sense and suited to nature; but the irascible regards the character of good insofar as it repels and assails that which inflicts harm. But the will regards good under the common character of good. And therefore no appetitive powers are diversified within it, which is the intellectual appetite, such that there is one irascible power and another concupiscible power in the intellectual appetite, just as apprehensive powers are not multiplied on the part of the intellect, although they are multiplied on the part of sense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0093","text":"[I q. 82 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31908)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0094","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that love, concupiscence, and such things are taken in two ways. At times, indeed, insofar as they are certain passions arising with a certain stirring of the soul. And thus they are commonly understood, and in this way they are only in the sensitive appetite. In another way, they signify a simple affection, without passion or stirring of the soul. And thus they are acts of the will. And in this way they are also attributed to angels and to God. But insofar as they are thus understood, they do not pertain to diverse powers, but to one power only, which is called will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0095","text":"[I q. 82 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31909)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0096","text":"To the second it must be said that the will itself can be called irascible, insofar as it wills to assail evil, not from the impulse of passion but from the judgment of reason. And in the same way it can be called concupiscible, on account of its desire for good. And thus charity and hope are in the irascible and concupiscible; that is, in the will insofar as it has an ordering toward acts of this kind. Thus also may be understood what is said in the book *On Spirit and Soul*, that the irascible and concupiscible are in the soul before it is united to the body (provided, however, that an order of nature, and not of time, is understood), although it is not necessary to place faith in the words of that book.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0097","text":"[I q. 82 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31910)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0098","text":"Whence the solution to the third objection is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_74:P0098"]}],"source_digest":"8c39e60190b6740e54d25eaf88771ca5b6af8d1b46af3e879c7469330431ec88","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 82"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 82","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75","title":"First Part · Question 83","order":84,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_75","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 83 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31911)\n\nDeinde quaeritur de libero arbitrio. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum homo sit liberi arbitrii. Secundo, quid sit liberum arbitrium, utrum sit potentia, vel actus, vel habitus. Tertio si est potentia, utrum sit appetitiva, vel cognitiva. Quarto, si est appetitiva, utrum sit eadem potentia cum voluntate, vel alia.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 83 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31912)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo non sit liberi arbitrii. Quicumque enim est liberi arbitrii, facit quod vult. Sed homo non facit quod vult, dicitur enim Rom. VII, *non enim quod volo bonum, hoc ago; sed quod odi malum, illud facio*. Ergo homo non est liberi arbitrii.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31913)\n\nPraeterea, quicumque est liberi arbitrii, eius est velle et non velle, operari et non operari. Sed hoc non est hominis, dicitur enim ad Rom. IX, non est volentis, scilicet velle, neque currentis, scilicet currere. Ergo homo non est liberi arbitrii.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31914)\n\n*Praeterea, liberum est quod sui causa est*, ut dicitur in I Metaphys. Quod ergo movetur ab alio, non est liberum. Sed Deus movet voluntatem, dicitur enim Prov. XXI, *cor regis in manu Dei, et quocumque voluerit vertet illud*; et Philipp. II, *Deus est qui operatur in nobis velle et perficere*. Ergo homo non est liberi arbitrii.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31915)\n\nPraeterea, quicumque est liberi arbitrii, est dominus suorum actuum. Sed homo non est dominus suorum actuum, quia, ut dicitur Ierem. X, *non est in homine via eius, nec viri est ut dirigat gressus suos*. Ergo homo non est liberi arbitrii.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31916)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus dicit, in III Ethic., *qualis unusquisque est, talis finis videtur ei*. Sed non est in potestate nostra aliquales esse, sed hoc nobis est a natura. Ergo naturale est nobis quod aliquem finem sequamur. Non ergo ex libero arbitrio.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31917)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Eccli. XV, *Deus ab initio constituit hominem, et reliquit eum in manu consilii sui*. Glossa, idest in libertate arbitrii.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31918)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod homo est liberi arbitrii, alioquin frustra essent consilia, exhortationes, praecepta, prohibitiones, praemia et poenae. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod quaedam agunt absque iudicio, sicut lapis movetur deorsum; et similiter omnia cognitione carentia. Quaedam autem agunt iudicio, sed non libero; sicut animalia bruta. Iudicat enim ovis videns lupum, eum esse fugiendum, naturali iudicio, et non libero, quia non ex collatione, sed ex naturali instinctu hoc iudicat. Et simile est de quolibet iudicio brutorum animalium. Sed homo agit iudicio, quia per vim cognoscitivam iudicat aliquid esse fugiendum vel prosequendum. Sed quia iudicium istud non est ex naturali instinctu in particulari operabili, sed ex collatione quadam rationis; ideo agit libero iudicio, potens in diversa ferri. Ratio enim circa contingentia habet viam ad opposita; ut patet in dialecticis syllogismis, et rhetoricis persuasionibus. Particularia autem operabilia sunt quaedam contingentia, et ideo circa ea iudicium rationis ad diversa se habet, et non est determinatum ad unum. Et pro tanto necesse est quod homo sit liberi arbitrii, ex hoc ipso quod rationalis est.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31919)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, appetitus sensitivus, etsi obediat rationi, tamen potest in aliquo repugnare, concupiscendo contra illud quod ratio dictat. Hoc ergo est bonum quod homo non facit cum vult, scilicet non concupiscere contra rationem, ut Glossa Augustini ibidem dicit.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31920)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod verbum illud apostoli non sic est intelligendum quasi homo non velit et non currat libero arbitrio, sed quia liberum arbitrium ad hoc non est sufficiens, nisi moveatur et iuvetur a Deo.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31921)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod liberum arbitrium est causa sui motus, quia homo per liberum arbitrium seipsum movet ad agendum. Non tamen hoc est de necessitate libertatis, quod sit prima causa sui id quod liberum est, sicut nec ad hoc quod aliquid sit causa alterius, requiritur quod sit prima causa eius. Deus igitur est prima causa movens et naturales causas et voluntarias. Et sicut naturalibus causis, movendo eas, non aufert quin actus earum sint naturales; ita movendo causas voluntarias, non aufert quin actiones earum sint voluntariae, sed potius hoc in eis facit, operatur enim in unoquoque secundum eius proprietatem.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31922)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod dicitur non esse in homine via eius, quantum ad executiones electionum, in quibus homo impediri potest, velit nolit. Electiones autem ipsae sunt in nobis, supposito tamen divino auxilio.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31923)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod qualitas hominis est duplex, una naturalis, et alia superveniens. Naturalis autem qualitas accipi potest vel circa partem intellectivam; vel circa corpus et virtutes corpori annexas. Ex eo igitur quod homo est aliqualis qualitate naturali quae attenditur secundum intellectivam partem, naturaliter homo appetit ultimum finem, scilicet beatitudinem. Qui quidem appetitus naturalis est, et non subiacet libero arbitrio, ut ex supradictis patet. Ex parte vero corporis et virtutum corpori annexarum, potest esse homo aliqualis naturali qualitate, secundum quod est talis complexionis, vel talis dispositionis, ex quacumque impressione corporearum causarum, quae non possunt in intellectivam partem imprimere, eo quod non est alicuius corporis actus. Sic igitur qualis unusquisque est secundum corpoream qualitatem, talis finis videtur ei, quia ex huiusmodi dispositione homo inclinatur ad eligendum aliquid vel repudiandum. Sed istae inclinationes subiacent iudicio rationis, cui obedit inferior appetitus, ut dictum est. Unde per hoc libertati arbitrii non praeiudicatur. Qualitates autem supervenientes sunt sicut habitus et passiones, secundum quae aliquis magis inclinatur in unum quam in alterum. Tamen istae etiam inclinationes subiacent iudicio rationis. Et huiusmodi etiam qualitates ei subiacent, inquantum in nobis est tales qualitates acquirere, vel causaliter vel dispositive, vel a nobis excludere. Et sic nihil est quod libertati arbitrii repugnet.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 83 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31924)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod liberum arbitrium non sit potentia. Arbitrium enim liberum nihil est aliud quam liberum iudicium. Iudicium autem non nominat potentiam, sed actum. Ergo liberum arbitrium non est potentia.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31925)\n\nPraeterea, liberum arbitrium dicitur esse facultas voluntatis et rationis. Facultas autem nominat facilitatem potestatis, quae quidem est per habitum. Ergo liberum arbitrium est habitus. Bernardus etiam dicit quod liberum arbitrium est habitus animae liber sui. Non ergo est potentia.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31926)\n\nPraeterea, nulla potentia naturalis tollitur per peccatum. Sed liberum arbitrium tollitur per peccatum, Augustinus enim dicit quod *homo male utens libero arbitrio, et se perdit et ipsum*. Ergo liberum arbitrium non est potentia.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31927)\n\nSed contra est quod nihil est subiectum habitus, ut videtur, nisi potentia. Sed liberum arbitrium est subiectum gratiae; qua sibi assistente, bonum eligit. Ergo liberum arbitrium est potentia.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31928)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, quamvis liberum arbitrium nominet quendam actum secundum propriam significationem vocabuli; secundum tamen communem usum loquendi, liberum arbitrium dicimus id quod est huius actus principium, scilicet quo homo libere iudicat. Principium autem actus in nobis est et potentia et habitus, dicimur enim aliquid cognoscere et per scientiam, et per intellectivam potentiam. Oportet ergo quod liberum arbitrium vel sit potentia, vel sit habitus, vel sit potentia cum aliquo habitu. Quod autem non sit habitus, neque potentia cum habitu, manifeste apparet ex duobus. Primo quidem, quia si est habitus, oportet quod sit habitus naturalis, hoc enim est naturale homini, quod sit liberi arbitrii. Nullus autem habitus naturalis adest nobis ad ea quae subsunt libero arbitrio, quia ad ea respectu quorum habemus habitus naturales, naturaliter inclinamur, sicut ad assentiendum primis principiis; ea autem ad quae naturaliter inclinamur, non subsunt libero arbitrio, sicut dictum est de appetitu beatitudinis. Unde contra propriam rationem liberi arbitrii est, quod sit habitus naturalis. Contra naturalitatem autem eius est, quod sit habitus non naturalis. Et sic relinquitur quod nullo modo sit habitus. Secundo hoc apparet, quia habitus dicuntur secundum quos *nos habemus ad passiones vel ad actus bene vel male*, ut dicitur in II Ethic., nam per temperantiam bene nos habemus ad concupiscentias, per intemperantiam autem male; per scientiam etiam bene nos habemus ad actum intellectus, dum verum cognoscimus per habitum autem contrarium male. Liberum autem arbitrium indifferenter se habet ad bene eligendum vel male. Unde impossibile est quod liberum arbitrium sit habitus. Relinquitur ergo quod sit potentia.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31929)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod consuetum est potentiam significari nomine actus. Et sic per hunc actum qui est liberum iudicium, nominatur potentia quae est huius actus principium. Alioquin, si liberum arbitrium nominaret actum, non semper maneret in homine.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31930)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod facultas nominat quandoque potestatem expeditam ad operandum. Et sic facultas ponitur in definitione liberi arbitrii. Bernardus autem accipit habitum non secundum quod dividitur contra potentiam, sed secundum quod significat habitudinem quandam, qua aliquo modo se aliquis habet ad actum. Quod quidem est tam per potentiam quam per habitum, nam per potentiam homo se habet ut potens operari, per habitum autem ut aptus ad operandum bene vel male.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31931)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod homo peccando liberum arbitrium dicitur perdidisse, non quantum ad libertatem naturalem, quae est a coactione; sed quantum ad libertatem quae est a culpa et a miseria. De qua infra in tractatu moralium dicetur, in secunda parte huius operis.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 83 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31932)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod liberum arbitrium non sit potentia appetitiva, sed cognitiva. Dicit enim Damascenus quod *cum rationali confestim comitatur liberum arbitrium*. Sed ratio est potentia cognitiva. Ergo liberum arbitrium est potentia cognitiva.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31933)\n\nPraeterea, liberum arbitrium dicitur quasi liberum iudicium. Sed iudicare est actus cognitivae virtutis. Ergo liberum arbitrium est cognitiva potentia.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31934)\n\nPraeterea, ad liberum arbitrium praecipue pertinet electio. Sed electio videtur ad cognitionem pertinere, quia electio importat quandam comparationem unius ad alterum, quod est proprium cognitivae virtutis. Ergo liberum arbitrium est potentia cognitiva.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31935)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in III Ethic., quod *electio est desiderium eorum quae sunt in nobis*. Sed desiderium est actus appetitivae virtutis. Ergo et electio. Liberum autem arbitrium est secundum quod eligimus. Ergo liberum arbitrium est virtus appetitiva.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31936)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod proprium liberi arbitrii est electio, ex hoc enim liberi arbitrii esse dicimur, quod possumus unum recipere, alio recusato, quod est eligere. Et ideo naturam liberi arbitrii ex electione considerare oportet. Ad electionem autem concurrit aliquid ex parte cognitivae virtutis, et aliquid ex parte appetitivae, ex parte quidem cognitivae, requiritur consilium, per quod diiudicatur quid sit alteri praeferendum; ex parte autem appetitivae, requiritur quod appetendo acceptetur id quod per consilium diiudicatur. Et ideo Aristoteles in VI Ethic. sub dubio derelinquit utrum principalius pertineat electio ad vim appetitivam, vel ad vim cognitivam, dicit enim quod electio vel est intellectus appetitivus, vel appetitus intellectivus. Sed in III Ethic. in hoc magis declinat quod sit appetitus intellectivus, nominans electionem desiderium consiliabile. Et huius ratio est, quia proprium obiectum electionis est illud quod est ad finem, hoc autem, inquantum huiusmodi, habet rationem boni quod dicitur utile, unde cum bonum, inquantum huiusmodi, sit obiectum appetitus, sequitur quod electio sit principaliter actus appetitivae virtutis. Et sic liberum arbitrium est appetitiva potentia.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31937)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod potentiae appetitivae concomitantur apprehensivas. Et secundum hoc dicit Damascenus quod *cum rationali confestim comitatur liberum arbitrium*.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31938)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod iudicium est quasi conclusio et determinatio consilii. Determinatur autem consilium, primo quidem per sententiam rationis, et secundo per acceptationem appetitus, unde philosophus dicit, in III Ethic., quod *ex consiliari iudicantes desideramus secundum consilium*. Et hoc modo ipsa electio dicitur quoddam iudicium, a quo nominatur liberum arbitrium.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31939)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ista collatio quae importatur in nomine electionis, pertinet ad consilium praecedens, quod est rationis. Appetitus enim, quamvis non sit collativus, tamen inquantum a vi cognitiva conferente movetur, habet quandam collationis similitudinem, dum unum alteri praeoptat.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 83 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31940)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod liberum arbitrium sit alia potentia a voluntate. Dicit enim Damascenus, in libro II, quod aliud est thelesis, aliud vero bulesis, thelesis autem est voluntas; bulesis autem videtur arbitrium liberum, quia bulesis, secundum ipsum, est voluntas quae est circa aliquid quasi unius per comparationem ad alterum. Ergo videtur quod liberum arbitrium sit alia potentia a voluntate.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31941)\n\nPraeterea, potentiae cognoscuntur per actus. Sed electio, quae est actus liberi arbitrii, est aliud a voluntate, ut dicitur in III Ethic., *quia voluntas est de fine, electio autem de iis quae sunt ad finem*. Ergo liberum arbitrium est alia potentia a voluntate.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31942)\n\nPraeterea, voluntas est appetitus intellectivus. Sed ex parte intellectus sunt duae potentiae, scilicet agens et possibilis. Ergo etiam ex parte appetitus intellectivi debet esse alia potentia praeter voluntatem. Et haec non videtur esse nisi liberum arbitrium. Ergo liberum arbitrium est alia potentia praeter voluntatem.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31943)\n\nSed contra est quod Damascenus dicit, in III libro, quod liberum arbitrium nihil aliud est quam voluntas.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31944)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod potentias appetitivas oportet esse proportionatas potentiis apprehensivis, ut supra dictum est. Sicut autem ex parte apprehensionis intellectivae se habent intellectus et ratio, ita ex parte appetitus intellectivi se habent voluntas et liberum arbitrium, quod nihil aliud est quam vis electiva. Et hoc patet ex habitudine obiectorum et actuum. Nam intelligere importat simplicem acceptionem alicuius rei, unde intelligi dicuntur proprie principia, quae sine collatione per seipsa cognoscuntur. Ratiocinari autem proprie est devenire ex uno in cognitionem alterius, unde proprie de conclusionibus ratiocinamur, quae ex principiis innotescunt. Similiter ex parte appetitus, velle importat simplicem appetitum alicuius rei, unde voluntas dicitur esse de fine, qui propter se appetitur. Eligere autem est appetere aliquid propter alterum consequendum, unde proprie est eorum quae sunt ad finem. Sicut autem se habet in cognitivis principium ad conclusionem, cui propter principia assentimus; ita in appetitivis se habet finis ad ea quae sunt ad finem, quae propter finem appetuntur. Unde manifestum est quod sicut se habet intellectus ad rationem, ita se habet voluntas ad vim electivam, idest ad liberum arbitrium. Ostensum est autem supra quod eiusdem potentiae est intelligere et ratiocinari, sicut eiusdem virtutis est quiescere et moveri. Unde etiam eiusdem potentiae est velle et eligere. Et propter hoc voluntas et liberum arbitrium non sunt duae potentiae, sed una.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31945)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod bulesis distinguitur a thelesi, non propter diversitatem potentiarum, sed propter differentiam actuum.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31946)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod electio et voluntas, idest ipsum velle, sunt diversi actus, sed tamen pertinent ad unam potentiam, sicut etiam intelligere et ratiocinari, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 83 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31947)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod intellectus comparatur ad voluntatem ut movens. Et ideo non oportet in voluntate distinguere agens et possibile.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0002","text":"[I q. 83 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31911)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0003","text":"Deinde quaeritur de libero arbitrio. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum homo sit liberi arbitrii. Secundo, quid sit liberum arbitrium, utrum sit potentia, vel actus, vel habitus. Tertio si est potentia, utrum sit appetitiva, vel cognitiva. Quarto, si est appetitiva, utrum sit eadem potentia cum voluntate, vel alia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0005","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31912)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo non sit liberi arbitrii. Quicumque enim est liberi arbitrii, facit quod vult. Sed homo non facit quod vult, dicitur enim Rom. VII, *non enim quod volo bonum, hoc ago; sed quod odi malum, illud facio*. Ergo homo non est liberi arbitrii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0007","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31913)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0008","text":"Praeterea, quicumque est liberi arbitrii, eius est velle et non velle, operari et non operari. Sed hoc non est hominis, dicitur enim ad Rom. IX, non est volentis, scilicet velle, neque currentis, scilicet currere. Ergo homo non est liberi arbitrii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0009","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31914)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0010","text":"*Praeterea, liberum est quod sui causa est*, ut dicitur in I Metaphys. Quod ergo movetur ab alio, non est liberum. Sed Deus movet voluntatem, dicitur enim Prov. XXI, *cor regis in manu Dei, et quocumque voluerit vertet illud*; et Philipp. II, *Deus est qui operatur in nobis velle et perficere*. Ergo homo non est liberi arbitrii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0011","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31915)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0012","text":"Praeterea, quicumque est liberi arbitrii, est dominus suorum actuum. Sed homo non est dominus suorum actuum, quia, ut dicitur Ierem. X, *non est in homine via eius, nec viri est ut dirigat gressus suos*. Ergo homo non est liberi arbitrii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0013","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31916)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0014","text":"Praeterea, philosophus dicit, in III Ethic., *qualis unusquisque est, talis finis videtur ei*. Sed non est in potestate nostra aliquales esse, sed hoc nobis est a natura. Ergo naturale est nobis quod aliquem finem sequamur. Non ergo ex libero arbitrio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0015","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31917)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0016","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Eccli. XV, *Deus ab initio constituit hominem, et reliquit eum in manu consilii sui*. Glossa, idest in libertate arbitrii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0017","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31918)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0018","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod homo est liberi arbitrii, alioquin frustra essent consilia, exhortationes, praecepta, prohibitiones, praemia et poenae. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod quaedam agunt absque iudicio, sicut lapis movetur deorsum; et similiter omnia cognitione carentia. Quaedam autem agunt iudicio, sed non libero; sicut animalia bruta. Iudicat enim ovis videns lupum, eum esse fugiendum, naturali iudicio, et non libero, quia non ex collatione, sed ex naturali instinctu hoc iudicat. Et simile est de quolibet iudicio brutorum animalium. Sed homo agit iudicio, quia per vim cognoscitivam iudicat aliquid esse fugiendum vel prosequendum. Sed quia iudicium istud non est ex naturali instinctu in particulari operabili, sed ex collatione quadam rationis; ideo agit libero iudicio, potens in diversa ferri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0019","text":"Ratio enim circa contingentia habet viam ad opposita; ut patet in dialecticis syllogismis, et rhetoricis persuasionibus. Particularia autem operabilia sunt quaedam contingentia, et ideo circa ea iudicium rationis ad diversa se habet, et non est determinatum ad unum. Et pro tanto necesse est quod homo sit liberi arbitrii, ex hoc ipso quod rationalis est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0020","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31919)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0021","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, appetitus sensitivus, etsi obediat rationi, tamen potest in aliquo repugnare, concupiscendo contra illud quod ratio dictat. Hoc ergo est bonum quod homo non facit cum vult, scilicet non concupiscere contra rationem, ut Glossa Augustini ibidem dicit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0022","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31920)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0023","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod verbum illud apostoli non sic est intelligendum quasi homo non velit et non currat libero arbitrio, sed quia liberum arbitrium ad hoc non est sufficiens, nisi moveatur et iuvetur a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0024","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31921)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0025","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod liberum arbitrium est causa sui motus, quia homo per liberum arbitrium seipsum movet ad agendum. Non tamen hoc est de necessitate libertatis, quod sit prima causa sui id quod liberum est, sicut nec ad hoc quod aliquid sit causa alterius, requiritur quod sit prima causa eius. Deus igitur est prima causa movens et naturales causas et voluntarias. Et sicut naturalibus causis, movendo eas, non aufert quin actus earum sint naturales; ita movendo causas voluntarias, non aufert quin actiones earum sint voluntariae, sed potius hoc in eis facit, operatur enim in unoquoque secundum eius proprietatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0026","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31922)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0027","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod dicitur non esse in homine via eius, quantum ad executiones electionum, in quibus homo impediri potest, velit nolit. Electiones autem ipsae sunt in nobis, supposito tamen divino auxilio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0028","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31923)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0029","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod qualitas hominis est duplex, una naturalis, et alia superveniens. Naturalis autem qualitas accipi potest vel circa partem intellectivam; vel circa corpus et virtutes corpori annexas. Ex eo igitur quod homo est aliqualis qualitate naturali quae attenditur secundum intellectivam partem, naturaliter homo appetit ultimum finem, scilicet beatitudinem. Qui quidem appetitus naturalis est, et non subiacet libero arbitrio, ut ex supradictis patet. Ex parte vero corporis et virtutum corpori annexarum, potest esse homo aliqualis naturali qualitate, secundum quod est talis complexionis, vel talis dispositionis, ex quacumque impressione corporearum causarum, quae non possunt in intellectivam partem imprimere, eo quod non est alicuius corporis actus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0030","text":"Sic igitur qualis unusquisque est secundum corpoream qualitatem, talis finis videtur ei, quia ex huiusmodi dispositione homo inclinatur ad eligendum aliquid vel repudiandum. Sed istae inclinationes subiacent iudicio rationis, cui obedit inferior appetitus, ut dictum est. Unde per hoc libertati arbitrii non praeiudicatur. Qualitates autem supervenientes sunt sicut habitus et passiones, secundum quae aliquis magis inclinatur in unum quam in alterum. Tamen istae etiam inclinationes subiacent iudicio rationis. Et huiusmodi etiam qualitates ei subiacent, inquantum in nobis est tales qualitates acquirere, vel causaliter vel dispositive, vel a nobis excludere. Et sic nihil est quod libertati arbitrii repugnet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0031","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0032","text":"[I q. 83 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31924)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0033","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod liberum arbitrium non sit potentia. Arbitrium enim liberum nihil est aliud quam liberum iudicium. Iudicium autem non nominat potentiam, sed actum. Ergo liberum arbitrium non est potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0034","text":"[I q. 83 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31925)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0035","text":"Praeterea, liberum arbitrium dicitur esse facultas voluntatis et rationis. Facultas autem nominat facilitatem potestatis, quae quidem est per habitum. Ergo liberum arbitrium est habitus. Bernardus etiam dicit quod liberum arbitrium est habitus animae liber sui. Non ergo est potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0036","text":"[I q. 83 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31926)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0037","text":"Praeterea, nulla potentia naturalis tollitur per peccatum. Sed liberum arbitrium tollitur per peccatum, Augustinus enim dicit quod *homo male utens libero arbitrio, et se perdit et ipsum*. Ergo liberum arbitrium non est potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0038","text":"[I q. 83 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31927)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0039","text":"Sed contra est quod nihil est subiectum habitus, ut videtur, nisi potentia. Sed liberum arbitrium est subiectum gratiae; qua sibi assistente, bonum eligit. Ergo liberum arbitrium est potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0040","text":"[I q. 83 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31928)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0041","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, quamvis liberum arbitrium nominet quendam actum secundum propriam significationem vocabuli; secundum tamen communem usum loquendi, liberum arbitrium dicimus id quod est huius actus principium, scilicet quo homo libere iudicat. Principium autem actus in nobis est et potentia et habitus, dicimur enim aliquid cognoscere et per scientiam, et per intellectivam potentiam. Oportet ergo quod liberum arbitrium vel sit potentia, vel sit habitus, vel sit potentia cum aliquo habitu. Quod autem non sit habitus, neque potentia cum habitu, manifeste apparet ex duobus. Primo quidem, quia si est habitus, oportet quod sit habitus naturalis, hoc enim est naturale homini, quod sit liberi arbitrii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0042","text":"Nullus autem habitus naturalis adest nobis ad ea quae subsunt libero arbitrio, quia ad ea respectu quorum habemus habitus naturales, naturaliter inclinamur, sicut ad assentiendum primis principiis; ea autem ad quae naturaliter inclinamur, non subsunt libero arbitrio, sicut dictum est de appetitu beatitudinis. Unde contra propriam rationem liberi arbitrii est, quod sit habitus naturalis. Contra naturalitatem autem eius est, quod sit habitus non naturalis. Et sic relinquitur quod nullo modo sit habitus. Secundo hoc apparet, quia habitus dicuntur secundum quos *nos habemus ad passiones vel ad actus bene vel male*, ut dicitur in II Ethic., nam per temperantiam bene nos habemus ad concupiscentias, per intemperantiam autem male; per scientiam etiam bene nos habemus ad actum intellectus, dum verum cognoscimus per habitum autem contrarium male.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0043","text":"Liberum autem arbitrium indifferenter se habet ad bene eligendum vel male. Unde impossibile est quod liberum arbitrium sit habitus. Relinquitur ergo quod sit potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0044","text":"[I q. 83 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31929)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0045","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod consuetum est potentiam significari nomine actus. Et sic per hunc actum qui est liberum iudicium, nominatur potentia quae est huius actus principium. Alioquin, si liberum arbitrium nominaret actum, non semper maneret in homine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0046","text":"[I q. 83 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31930)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0047","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod facultas nominat quandoque potestatem expeditam ad operandum. Et sic facultas ponitur in definitione liberi arbitrii. Bernardus autem accipit habitum non secundum quod dividitur contra potentiam, sed secundum quod significat habitudinem quandam, qua aliquo modo se aliquis habet ad actum. Quod quidem est tam per potentiam quam per habitum, nam per potentiam homo se habet ut potens operari, per habitum autem ut aptus ad operandum bene vel male.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0048","text":"[I q. 83 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31931)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0049","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod homo peccando liberum arbitrium dicitur perdidisse, non quantum ad libertatem naturalem, quae est a coactione; sed quantum ad libertatem quae est a culpa et a miseria. De qua infra in tractatu moralium dicetur, in secunda parte huius operis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0050","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0051","text":"[I q. 83 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31932)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0052","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod liberum arbitrium non sit potentia appetitiva, sed cognitiva. Dicit enim Damascenus quod *cum rationali confestim comitatur liberum arbitrium*. Sed ratio est potentia cognitiva. Ergo liberum arbitrium est potentia cognitiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0053","text":"[I q. 83 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31933)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0054","text":"Praeterea, liberum arbitrium dicitur quasi liberum iudicium. Sed iudicare est actus cognitivae virtutis. Ergo liberum arbitrium est cognitiva potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0055","text":"[I q. 83 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31934)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0056","text":"Praeterea, ad liberum arbitrium praecipue pertinet electio. Sed electio videtur ad cognitionem pertinere, quia electio importat quandam comparationem unius ad alterum, quod est proprium cognitivae virtutis. Ergo liberum arbitrium est potentia cognitiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0057","text":"[I q. 83 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31935)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0058","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in III Ethic., quod *electio est desiderium eorum quae sunt in nobis*. Sed desiderium est actus appetitivae virtutis. Ergo et electio. Liberum autem arbitrium est secundum quod eligimus. Ergo liberum arbitrium est virtus appetitiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0059","text":"[I q. 83 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31936)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0060","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod proprium liberi arbitrii est electio, ex hoc enim liberi arbitrii esse dicimur, quod possumus unum recipere, alio recusato, quod est eligere. Et ideo naturam liberi arbitrii ex electione considerare oportet. Ad electionem autem concurrit aliquid ex parte cognitivae virtutis, et aliquid ex parte appetitivae, ex parte quidem cognitivae, requiritur consilium, per quod diiudicatur quid sit alteri praeferendum; ex parte autem appetitivae, requiritur quod appetendo acceptetur id quod per consilium diiudicatur. Et ideo Aristoteles in VI Ethic. sub dubio derelinquit utrum principalius pertineat electio ad vim appetitivam, vel ad vim cognitivam, dicit enim quod electio vel est intellectus appetitivus, vel appetitus intellectivus. Sed in III Ethic. in hoc magis declinat quod sit appetitus intellectivus, nominans electionem desiderium consiliabile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0061","text":"Et huius ratio est, quia proprium obiectum electionis est illud quod est ad finem, hoc autem, inquantum huiusmodi, habet rationem boni quod dicitur utile, unde cum bonum, inquantum huiusmodi, sit obiectum appetitus, sequitur quod electio sit principaliter actus appetitivae virtutis. Et sic liberum arbitrium est appetitiva potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0062","text":"[I q. 83 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31937)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0063","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod potentiae appetitivae concomitantur apprehensivas. Et secundum hoc dicit Damascenus quod *cum rationali confestim comitatur liberum arbitrium*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0064","text":"[I q. 83 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31938)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0065","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod iudicium est quasi conclusio et determinatio consilii. Determinatur autem consilium, primo quidem per sententiam rationis, et secundo per acceptationem appetitus, unde philosophus dicit, in III Ethic., quod *ex consiliari iudicantes desideramus secundum consilium*. Et hoc modo ipsa electio dicitur quoddam iudicium, a quo nominatur liberum arbitrium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0066","text":"[I q. 83 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31939)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0067","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ista collatio quae importatur in nomine electionis, pertinet ad consilium praecedens, quod est rationis. Appetitus enim, quamvis non sit collativus, tamen inquantum a vi cognitiva conferente movetur, habet quandam collationis similitudinem, dum unum alteri praeoptat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0068","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0069","text":"[I q. 83 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31940)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0070","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod liberum arbitrium sit alia potentia a voluntate. Dicit enim Damascenus, in libro II, quod aliud est thelesis, aliud vero bulesis, thelesis autem est voluntas; bulesis autem videtur arbitrium liberum, quia bulesis, secundum ipsum, est voluntas quae est circa aliquid quasi unius per comparationem ad alterum. Ergo videtur quod liberum arbitrium sit alia potentia a voluntate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0071","text":"[I q. 83 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31941)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0072","text":"Praeterea, potentiae cognoscuntur per actus. Sed electio, quae est actus liberi arbitrii, est aliud a voluntate, ut dicitur in III Ethic., *quia voluntas est de fine, electio autem de iis quae sunt ad finem*. Ergo liberum arbitrium est alia potentia a voluntate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0073","text":"[I q. 83 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31942)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0074","text":"Praeterea, voluntas est appetitus intellectivus. Sed ex parte intellectus sunt duae potentiae, scilicet agens et possibilis. Ergo etiam ex parte appetitus intellectivi debet esse alia potentia praeter voluntatem. Et haec non videtur esse nisi liberum arbitrium. Ergo liberum arbitrium est alia potentia praeter voluntatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0075","text":"[I q. 83 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31943)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0076","text":"Sed contra est quod Damascenus dicit, in III libro, quod liberum arbitrium nihil aliud est quam voluntas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0077","text":"[I q. 83 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31944)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0078","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod potentias appetitivas oportet esse proportionatas potentiis apprehensivis, ut supra dictum est. Sicut autem ex parte apprehensionis intellectivae se habent intellectus et ratio, ita ex parte appetitus intellectivi se habent voluntas et liberum arbitrium, quod nihil aliud est quam vis electiva. Et hoc patet ex habitudine obiectorum et actuum. Nam intelligere importat simplicem acceptionem alicuius rei, unde intelligi dicuntur proprie principia, quae sine collatione per seipsa cognoscuntur. Ratiocinari autem proprie est devenire ex uno in cognitionem alterius, unde proprie de conclusionibus ratiocinamur, quae ex principiis innotescunt. Similiter ex parte appetitus, velle importat simplicem appetitum alicuius rei, unde voluntas dicitur esse de fine, qui propter se appetitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0079","text":"Eligere autem est appetere aliquid propter alterum consequendum, unde proprie est eorum quae sunt ad finem. Sicut autem se habet in cognitivis principium ad conclusionem, cui propter principia assentimus; ita in appetitivis se habet finis ad ea quae sunt ad finem, quae propter finem appetuntur. Unde manifestum est quod sicut se habet intellectus ad rationem, ita se habet voluntas ad vim electivam, idest ad liberum arbitrium. Ostensum est autem supra quod eiusdem potentiae est intelligere et ratiocinari, sicut eiusdem virtutis est quiescere et moveri. Unde etiam eiusdem potentiae est velle et eligere. Et propter hoc voluntas et liberum arbitrium non sunt duae potentiae, sed una.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0080","text":"[I q. 83 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31945)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0081","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod bulesis distinguitur a thelesi, non propter diversitatem potentiarum, sed propter differentiam actuum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0082","text":"[I q. 83 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31946)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0083","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod electio et voluntas, idest ipsum velle, sunt diversi actus, sed tamen pertinent ad unam potentiam, sicut etiam intelligere et ratiocinari, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0084","text":"[I q. 83 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31947)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0085","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod intellectus comparatur ad voluntatem ut movens. Et ideo non oportet in voluntate distinguere agens et possibile.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"40820c6870a704cabaf7eeb1aa437bcf2fc5a007b8eae2a0528aec1696dbe44a","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T151406094003Z_de6b54d5","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 83","source_sha256":"40820c6870a704cabaf7eeb1aa437bcf2fc5a007b8eae2a0528aec1696dbe44a","sha256":"3daee05e08e3188f1dde1cbb14e90470a96d8e95cf606fd99a5a2a3928464c88","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:14:06.094018+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0002","text":"[I q. 83 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31911)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0003","text":"Next, free choice is considered. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, whether man has free choice. Second, what free choice is: whether it is a power, or an act, or a habit. Third, if it is a power, whether it is appetitive or cognitive. Fourth, if it is appetitive, whether it is the same power as the will, or another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0005","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31912)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0006","text":"It is proceeded thus to the first. It seems that man does not have free choice. For whoever has free choice does what he wills. But man does not do what he wills, for it is said in Rom. VII, *for the good that I will, I do not do; but the evil that I hate, that I do*. Therefore man does not have free choice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0007","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31913)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0008","text":"Moreover, whoever has free choice, it belongs to him to will and not to will, to act and not to act. But this does not belong to man, for it is said in Rom. IX, it is not of one willing, namely, to will, nor of one running, namely, to run. Therefore man does not have free choice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0009","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31914)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0010","text":"*That is free which is the cause of itself*, as is said in I Metaphysics. Therefore, what is moved by another is not free. But God moves the will, for it is said in Prov. XXI, *the heart of the king is in the hand of God, and wherever He wills, He shall turn it*; and in Philipp. II, *it is God who works in us to will and to accomplish*. Therefore man does not have free choice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0011","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31915)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0012","text":"Moreover, whoever has free choice is master of his own acts. But man is not master of his own acts, because, as is said in Jer. X, *a man's way is not in himself, nor does it belong to a man to direct his steps*. Therefore man does not have free choice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0013","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31916)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0014","text":"Moreover, the Philosopher says, in III Ethics, *such as each one is, such does the end appear to him*. But it is not in our power to be of a certain sort, but this comes to us from nature. Therefore it is natural to us that we follow some end. Therefore not from free choice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0015","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31917)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0016","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Ecclus. XV, *God from the beginning established man, and left him in the hand of his own counsel*. The Gloss: that is, in the freedom of choice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0017","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31918)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0018","text":"I answer that it must be said that man has free choice; otherwise counsels, exhortations, precepts, prohibitions, rewards, and punishments would be in vain. To make this evident, it should be considered that some things act without judgment, as a stone is moved downward; and likewise all things lacking cognition. But some things act with judgment, yet not freely; as brute animals do. For a sheep, seeing a wolf, judges that it should be fled from, by a natural judgment and not a free one, because it judges this not from comparison, but from natural instinct. And the same is true of every judgment of brute animals. But man acts by judgment, because through the cognitive power he judges something to be avoided or pursued. But because this judgment is not from natural instinct in a particular matter of action, but from a certain comparison of reason, therefore he acts by free judgment, being able to be inclined to diverse things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0019","text":"For reason, concerning contingent things, has a way toward opposites; as is clear in dialectical syllogisms and rhetorical persuasions. But particular matters of action are certain contingent things, and therefore concerning them the judgment of reason is disposed in diverse ways and is not determined to one thing. And to this extent it is necessary that man have free choice, from the very fact that he is rational.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0020","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31919)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0021","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, as was said above, the sensitive appetite, although it obeys reason, can nevertheless in some respect resist it, by desiring against that which reason dictates. Therefore this is the good which man does not do when he wills, namely, not to desire against reason, as Augustine's Gloss says there.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0022","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31920)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0023","text":"To the second, it must be said that that word of the Apostle is not to be understood as though man does not will and run by free choice, but because free choice is not sufficient for this unless it is moved and aided by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0024","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31921)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0025","text":"To the third, it must be said that free choice is the cause of its own movement, because through free choice a human being moves himself to act. Yet it is not necessary to freedom that that which is free should be the first cause of itself, just as neither is it required, for something to be the cause of another, that it be its first cause. Therefore God is the first cause moving both natural and voluntary causes. And just as, by moving natural causes, He does not take away the fact that their acts are natural, so too, by moving voluntary causes, He does not take away the fact that their actions are voluntary; rather, He brings this about in them, for He operates in each thing according to its own property.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0026","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31922)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0027","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that it is said that a man's way is not in himself with respect to the execution of his choices, in which a man can be impeded, whether he wills or does not will. But the choices themselves are in us, presupposing, however, divine assistance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0028","text":"[I q. 83 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31923)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0029","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that a human being's quality is twofold: one natural, and the other supervening. Now natural quality may be taken either with regard to the intellective part, or with regard to the body and the powers annexed to the body. Therefore, from the fact that a human being is of a certain sort by a natural quality considered according to the intellective part, a human being naturally desires the ultimate end, namely, beatitude. This appetite is indeed natural and is not subject to free choice, as is clear from what has been said above. But on the part of the body and the powers annexed to the body, a human being can be of a certain sort by a natural quality, insofar as he is of such a complexion or such a disposition, from any impression of bodily causes, which cannot make an impression on the intellective part, since it is not the act of any body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0030","text":"Thus, therefore, as each person is according to bodily quality, such an end appears to him, because by a disposition of this kind a human being is inclined to choose something or reject it. But these inclinations are subject to the judgment of reason, to which the lower appetite obeys, as has been said. Hence free choice is not prejudiced by this. The supervening qualities, however, are such as habits and passions, according to which someone is more inclined to one thing than to another. Yet these inclinations too are subject to the judgment of reason. And qualities of this kind are also subject to him, insofar as it lies in us to acquire such qualities, either causally or dispositively, or to exclude them from ourselves. And thus there is nothing that conflicts with freedom of choice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0031","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0032","text":"[I q. 83 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31924)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0033","text":"We proceed thus to the second article. It seems that free choice is not a power. For free choice is nothing other than free judgment. But judgment denotes not a power, but an act. Therefore free choice is not a power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0034","text":"[I q. 83 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31925)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0035","text":"Moreover, free choice is said to be a faculty of will and reason. But faculty denotes an ease of power, which indeed comes through a habit. Therefore free choice is a habit. Bernard also says that free choice is a habit of the soul, free in itself. Therefore it is not a power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0036","text":"[I q. 83 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31926)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0037","text":"Moreover, no natural power is taken away by sin. But free choice is taken away by sin; for Augustine says that *a man, by using free choice badly, loses both himself and it*. Therefore free choice is not a power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0038","text":"[I q. 83 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31927)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0039","text":"On the contrary, it seems that nothing is the subject of a habit except a power. But free choice is the subject of grace; with grace assisting it, it chooses the good. Therefore free choice is a power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0040","text":"[I q. 83 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31928)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0041","text":"I answer that, although free choice, according to the proper signification of the word, names a certain act, nevertheless, according to common usage in speaking, we call free choice that which is the principle of this act, namely, that by which a human being judges freely. But the principle of an act in us is both a power and a habit, for we are said to know something both through knowledge and through the intellective power. Therefore free choice must either be a power, or be a habit, or be a power together with some habit. But that it is not a habit, nor a power together with a habit, is clearly apparent for two reasons. First, because if it is a habit, it must be a natural habit; for it is natural to a human being to possess free choice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0042","text":"But no natural habit is present in us with respect to those things that are subject to free choice, because we are naturally inclined toward those things with respect to which we have natural habits, as toward assenting to first principles; but those things toward which we are naturally inclined are not subject to free choice, as has been said concerning the appetite for beatitude. Hence it is contrary to the proper notion of free choice that it should be a natural habit. But that it should be a non-natural habit is contrary to its nature. And thus it follows that it is in no way a habit. Secondly, this is apparent because habits are spoken of as those according to which *we are disposed well or badly with respect to passions or acts*, as is said in Ethics II; for through temperance we are well disposed toward concupiscences, but through intemperance badly; likewise, through knowledge we are well disposed toward the act of the intellect, when we know the truth, but through the contrary habit badly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0043","text":"But free choice is indifferent with respect to choosing well or badly. Hence it is impossible that free choice be a habit. Therefore it remains that it is a power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0044","text":"[I q. 83 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31929)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0045","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that it is customary for a power to be signified by the name of an act. And thus, through this act which is free judgment, the power that is the principle of this act is named. Otherwise, if free choice named an act, it would not always remain in a human being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0046","text":"[I q. 83 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31930)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0047","text":"To the second, it must be said that faculty sometimes denotes a power unimpeded in operating. And thus faculty is included in the definition of free choice. But Bernard takes habit not insofar as it is divided against power, but insofar as it signifies a certain relation by which someone is in some manner disposed toward an act. This indeed occurs both through a power and through a habit, for through a power a human being is disposed as able to act, but through a habit as apt to act well or badly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0048","text":"[I q. 83 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31931)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0049","text":"To the third, it must be said that by sinning man is said to have lost free choice, not as regards natural freedom, which is freedom from coercion, but as regards the freedom that is from fault and misery. Of this, it will be spoken below in the treatise on morals, in the second part of this work.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0050","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0051","text":"[I q. 83 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31932)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0052","text":"The third is approached thus. It seems that free choice is not an appetitive power, but a cognitive one. For Damascene says that *free choice immediately accompanies the rational*. But reason is a cognitive power. Therefore free choice is a cognitive power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0053","text":"[I q. 83 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31933)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0054","text":"Further, free choice is called, as it were, free judgment. But judging is an act of the cognitive power. Therefore free choice is a cognitive power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0055","text":"[I q. 83 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31934)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0056","text":"Further, choice pertains especially to free choice. But choice seems to pertain to cognition, because choice implies a certain comparison of one thing with another, which is proper to the cognitive power. Therefore free choice is a cognitive power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0057","text":"[I q. 83 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31935)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0058","text":"But on the contrary, the Philosopher says, in Ethics III, that *choice is desire for things that are in our power*. But desire is an act of the appetitive power. Therefore choice is so as well. Now free choice is that by which we choose. Therefore free choice is an appetitive power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0059","text":"[I q. 83 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31936)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0060","text":"I answer that it must be said that choice is proper to free choice; for we are said to have free choice because we can accept one thing while rejecting another, which is to choose. Therefore the nature of free choice must be considered from choice. Now, something from the side of the cognitive power and something from the side of the appetitive power concur in choice. On the side of cognition, deliberation is required, by which it is judged what ought to be preferred to another; but on the side of appetite, it is required that what is judged through deliberation be accepted by appetite. Therefore Aristotle, in Ethics VI, leaves it doubtful whether choice pertains more principally to the appetitive faculty or to the cognitive faculty; for he says that choice is either appetitive intellect or intellective appetite. But in Ethics III he inclines more to its being intellective appetite, calling choice deliberative desire.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0061","text":"And the reason for this is that the proper object of choice is that which is for an end. This, insofar as it is such, has the character of the good called useful; hence, since the good as such is the object of appetite, it follows that choice is principally an act of the appetitive power. And thus free choice is an appetitive power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0062","text":"[I q. 83 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31937)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0063","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that appetitive powers accompany apprehensive powers. And in this sense Damascene says that *free choice immediately accompanies the rational*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0064","text":"[I q. 83 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31938)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0065","text":"To the second, it must be said that judgment is, as it were, the conclusion and determination of deliberation. But deliberation is determined, first indeed by the verdict of reason, and second by the acceptance of appetite; hence the Philosopher says, in Ethics III, that *having judged by deliberation, we desire in accordance with deliberation*. And in this way choice itself is called a certain judgment, from which free choice takes its name.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0066","text":"[I q. 83 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31939)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0067","text":"To the third, it must be said that this comparison implied in the name of choice pertains to the preceding deliberation, which belongs to reason. For appetite, although it is not comparative, nevertheless, insofar as it is moved by the cognitive power that compares, has a certain likeness of comparison, while it prefers one thing to another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0068","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0069","text":"[I q. 83 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31940)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0070","text":"The fourth is approached thus. It seems that free choice is a power other than the will. For Damascene says, in Book II, that thelēsis is one thing and boulēsis another; but thelēsis is will, whereas boulēsis seems to be free choice, because boulēsis, according to him, is will concerning something as one thing in comparison with another. Therefore it seems that free choice is a power other than the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0071","text":"[I q. 83 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31941)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0072","text":"Further, powers are known through acts. But choice, which is an act of free choice, is other than will, as is said in Ethics III, *because will is concerned with the end, whereas choice is concerned with the things that are for the end*. Therefore free choice is a power other than the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0073","text":"[I q. 83 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31942)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0074","text":"Further, the will is an intellective appetite. But on the part of the intellect there are two powers, namely the active and the possible. Therefore, on the part of the intellective appetite there must likewise be another power besides the will. And this seems to be nothing other than free choice. Therefore free choice is another power besides the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0075","text":"[I q. 83 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31943)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0076","text":"But on the contrary, Damascene says, in Book III, that free choice is nothing other than the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0077","text":"[I q. 83 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31944)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0078","text":"I answer that it must be said that the appetitive powers must be proportioned to the apprehensive powers, as was said above. Now, just as on the part of intellective apprehension intellect and reason are related, so on the part of intellective appetite will and free choice are related, which is nothing other than the elective power. And this is clear from the relation of their objects and acts. For understanding implies the simple apprehension of some thing; hence principles, which are known through themselves without comparison, are properly said to be understood. But reasoning properly consists in arriving from one thing at the knowledge of another; hence we properly reason concerning conclusions, which become known from principles. Similarly, on the part of appetite, willing implies the simple appetite for some thing; hence the will is said to concern the end, which is desired for its own sake.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0079","text":"But choosing is to desire something for the sake of attaining another thing; hence it properly concerns those things that are for the end. Now, just as in cognitive powers a principle is related to a conclusion, to which we assent on account of principles, so in appetitive powers the end is related to those things that are for the end, which are desired on account of the end. Hence it is manifest that, just as intellect is related to reason, so the will is related to the elective power, that is, to free choice. But it was shown above that understanding and reasoning belong to the same power, just as resting and moving belong to the same power. Hence willing and choosing likewise belong to the same power. And for this reason the will and free choice are not two powers, but one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0080","text":"[I q. 83 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31945)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0081","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that bulesis is distinguished from thelesis, not on account of a diversity of powers, but on account of a difference of acts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0082","text":"[I q. 83 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31946)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0083","text":"To the second, it must be said that choice and will, that is, willing itself, are diverse acts, but nevertheless belong to one power, just as understanding and reasoning do, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0084","text":"[I q. 83 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1077.html#31947)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0085","text":"To the third, it must be said that the intellect is related to the will as mover. And therefore it is not necessary to distinguish in the will an active and a possible [principle].","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_75:P0085"]}],"source_digest":"a34c0ffd10e7252c8a880a37e9313c454dddb3200d2c062e0339e9b9d55b0e59","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 83"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 83","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76","title":"First Part · Question 84","order":85,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_76","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 84 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31948)\n\nConsequenter considerandum est de actibus animae, quantum ad potentias intellectivas et appetitivas, aliae enim animae potentiae non pertinent directe ad considerationem theologi. Actus autem appetitivae partis ad considerationem moralis scientiae pertinent, et ideo in secunda parte huius operis de eis tractabitur, in qua considerandum erit de morali materia. Nunc autem de actibus intellectivae partis agetur. In consideratione vero actuum, hoc modo procedemus, primo namque considerandum est quomodo intelligit anima corpori coniuncta; secundo, quomodo intelligit a corpore separata. Prima autem consideratio erit tripartita, primo namque considerabitur quomodo anima intelligit corporalia, quae sunt infra ipsam; secundo, quomodo intelligit seipsam, et ea quae in ipsa sunt; tertio, quomodo intelligit substantias immateriales, quae sunt supra ipsam. Circa cognitionem vero corporalium, tria consideranda occurrunt, primo quidem per quid ea cognoscit; secundo, quomodo et quo ordine; tertio, quid in eis cognoscit. Circa primum quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum anima cognoscat corpora per intellectum. Secundo, utrum intelligat ea per essentiam suam, vel per aliquas species. Tertio, si per aliquas species, utrum species omnium intelligibilium sint ei naturaliter innatae. Quarto, utrum effluant in ipsam ab aliquibus formis immaterialibus separatis. Quinto, utrum anima nostra omnia quae intelligit, videat in rationibus aeternis. Sexto, utrum cognitionem intelligibilem acquirat a sensu. Septimo, utrum intellectus possit actu intelligere per species intelligibiles quas penes se habet, non convertendo se ad phantasmata. Octavo, utrum iudicium intellectus impediatur per impedimentum sensitivarum virtutum.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 84 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31949)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima non cognoscat corpora per intellectum. Dicit enim Augustinus, in II Soliloq. quod *corpora intellectu comprehendi non possunt; nec aliquod corporeum nisi sensibus videri potest*. Dicit etiam, XII super Gen. ad Litt., quod visio intellectualis est eorum quae sunt per essentiam suam in anima. Huiusmodi autem non sunt corpora. Ergo anima per intellectum corpora cognoscere non potest.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31950)\n\nPraeterea, sicut se habet sensus ad intelligibilia, ita se habet intellectus ad sensibilia. Sed anima per sensum nullo modo potest cognoscere spiritualia, quae sunt intelligibilia. Ergo nullo modo per intellectum potest cognoscere corpora, quae sunt sensibilia.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31951)\n\nPraeterea, intellectus est necessariorum et semper eodem modo se habentium. Sed corpora omnia sunt mobilia, et non eodem modo se habentia. Anima ergo per intellectum corpora cognoscere non potest.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31952)\n\nSed contra est quod scientia est in intellectu. Si ergo intellectus non cognoscit corpora, sequitur quod nulla scientia sit de corporibus. Et sic peribit scientia naturalis, quae est de corpore mobili.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31953)\n\nRespondeo dicendum, ad evidentiam huius quaestionis, quod primi philosophi qui de naturis rerum inquisiverunt, putaverunt nihil esse in mundo praeter corpus. Et quia videbant omnia corpora mobilia esse, et putabant ea in continuo fluxu esse, aestimaverunt quod nulla certitudo de rerum veritate haberi posset a nobis. Quod enim est in continuo fluxu, per certitudinem apprehendi non potest, quia prius labitur quam mente diiudicetur, sicut Heraclitus dixit quod *non est possibile aquam fluvii currentis bis tangere*, ut recitat philosophus in IV Metaphys. His autem superveniens Plato, ut posset salvare certam cognitionem veritatis a nobis per intellectum haberi, posuit praeter ista corporalia aliud genus entium a materia et motu separatum, quod nominabat species sive ideas, per quarum participationem unumquodque istorum singularium et sensibilium dicitur vel homo vel equus vel aliquid huiusmodi. Sic ergo dicebat scientias et definitiones et quidquid ad actum intellectus pertinet, non referri ad ista corpora sensibilia, sed ad illa immaterialia et separata; ut sic anima non intelligat ista corporalia, sed intelligat horum corporalium species separatas. Sed hoc dupliciter apparet falsum. Primo quidem quia, cum illae species sint immateriales et immobiles, excluderetur a scientiis cognitio motus et materiae (quod est proprium scientiae naturalis) et demonstratio per causas moventes et materiales. Secundo autem, quia derisibile videtur ut, dum rerum quae nobis manifestae sunt notitiam quaerimus, alia entia in medium afferamus, quae non possunt esse earum substantiae, cum ab eis differant secundum esse, et sic, illis substantiis separatis cognitis, non propter hoc de istis sensibilibus iudicare possemus. Videtur autem in hoc Plato deviasse a veritate, quia, cum aestimaret omnem cognitionem per modum alicuius similitudinis esse, credidit quod forma cogniti ex necessitate sit in cognoscente eo modo quo est in cognito. Consideravit autem quod forma rei intellectae est in intellectu universaliter et immaterialiter et immobiliter, quod ex ipsa operatione intellectus apparet, qui intelligit universaliter et per modum necessitatis cuiusdam; modus enim actionis est secundum modum formae agentis. Et ideo existimavit quod oporteret res intellectas hoc modo in seipsis subsistere, scilicet immaterialiter et immobiliter. Hoc autem necessarium non est. Quia etiam in ipsis sensibilibus videmus quod forma alio modo est in uno sensibilium quam in altero, puta cum in uno est albedo intensior, in alio remissior, et in uno est albedo cum dulcedine, in alio sine dulcedine. Et per hunc etiam modum forma sensibilis alio modo est in re quae est extra animam, et alio modo in sensu, qui suscipit formas sensibilium absque materia, sicut colorem auri sine auro. Et similiter intellectus species, corporum, quae sunt materiales et mobiles, recipit immaterialiter et immobiliter, secundum modum suum, nam receptum est in recipiente per modum recipientis. Dicendum est ergo quod anima per intellectum cognoscit corpora cognitione immateriali, universali et necessaria.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31954)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod verbum Augustini est intelligendum quantum ad ea quibus intellectus cognoscit, non autem quantum ad ea quae cognoscit. Cognoscit enim corpora intelligendo, sed non per corpora, neque per similitudines materiales et corporeas; sed per species immateriales et intelligibiles, quae per sui essentiam in anima esse possunt.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31955)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit XXII de Civit. Dei, non est dicendum quod, sicut sensus cognoscit sola corporalia, ita intellectus cognoscit sola spiritualia, quia sequeretur quod Deus et Angeli corporalia non cognoscerent. Huius autem diversitatis ratio est, quia inferior virtus non se extendit ad ea quae sunt superioris virtutis; sed virtus superior ea quae sunt inferioris virtutis, excellentiori modo operatur.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31956)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod omnis motus supponit aliquid immobile, cum enim transmutatio fit secundum qualitatem, remanet substantia immobilis; et cum transmutatur forma substantialis, remanet materia immobilis. Rerum etiam mutabilium sunt immobiles habitudines, sicut Socrates etsi non semper sedeat, tamen immobiliter est verum quod, quandocumque sedet, in uno loco manet. Et propter hoc nihil prohibet de rebus mobilibus immobilem scientiam habere.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 84 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31957)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima per essentiam suam corporalia intelligat. Dicit enim Augustinus, X de Trin., quod *anima imagines corporum convolvit et rapit factas in semetipsa de semetipsa, dat enim eis formandis quiddam substantiae suae*. Sed per similitudines corporum corpora intelligit. Ergo per essentiam suam, quam dat formandis talibus similitudinibus, et de qua eas format, cognoscit corporalia.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31958)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod *anima quodammodo est omnia*. Cum ergo simile simili cognoscatur, videtur quod anima per seipsam corporalia cognoscat.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31959)\n\nPraeterea, anima est superior corporalibus creaturis. Inferiora autem sunt in superioribus eminentiori modo quam in seipsis, ut Dionysius dicit. Ergo omnes creaturae corporeae nobiliori modo existunt in ipsa substantia animae quam in seipsis. Per suam ergo substantiam potest creaturas corporeas cognoscere.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31960)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, IX de Trin., quod *mens corporearum rerum notitias per sensus corporis colligit*. Ipsa autem anima non est cognoscibilis per corporis sensus. Non ergo cognoscit corporea per suam substantiam.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31961)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod antiqui philosophi posuerunt quod anima per suam essentiam cognoscit corpora. Hoc enim animis omnium communiter inditum fuit, quod simile simili cognoscitur. Existimabant autem quod forma cogniti sit in cognoscente eo modo quo est in re cognita. E contrario tamen Platonici posuerunt. Plato enim, quia perspexit intellectualem animam immaterialem esse et immaterialiter cognoscere, posuit formas rerum cognitarum immaterialiter subsistere. Priores vero naturales, quia considerabant res cognitas esse corporeas et materiales, posuerunt oportere res cognitas etiam in anima cognoscente materialiter esse. Et ideo, ut animae attribuerent omnium cognitionem, posuerunt eam habere naturam communem cum omnibus. Et quia natura principiatorum ex principiis constituitur, attribuerunt animae naturam principii, ita quod qui dixit principium omnium esse ignem, posuit animam esse de natura ignis; et similiter de aere et aqua. Empedocles autem, qui posuit quatuor elementa materialia et duo moventia, ex his etiam dixit animam esse constitutam. Et ita, cum res materialiter in anima ponerent, posuerunt omnem cognitionem animae materialem esse, non discernentes inter intellectum et sensum. Sed haec opinio improbatur. Primo quidem, quia in materiali principio, de quo loquebantur, non existunt principiata nisi in potentia. Non autem cognoscitur aliquid secundum quod est in potentia, sed solum secundum quod est actu, ut patet in IX Metaphys., unde nec ipsa potentia cognoscitur nisi per actum. Sic igitur non sufficeret attribuere animae principiorum naturam ad hoc quod omnia cognosceret, nisi inessent ei naturae et formae singulorum effectuum, puta ossis et carnis et aliorum huiusmodi; ut Aristoteles contra Empedoclem argumentatur in I de anima. Secundo quia, si oporteret rem cognitam materialiter in cognoscente existere, nulla ratio esset quare res quae materialiter extra animam subsistunt, cognitione carerent, puta, si anima igne cognoscit ignem, et ignis etiam qui est extra animam, ignem cognosceret. Relinquitur ergo quod oportet materialia cognita in cognoscente existere non materialiter, sed magis immaterialiter. Et huius ratio est, quia actus cognitionis se extendit ad ea quae sunt extra cognoscentem, cognoscimus enim etiam ea quae extra nos sunt. Per materiam autem determinatur forma rei ad aliquid unum. Unde manifestum est quod ratio cognitionis ex opposito se habet ad rationem materialitatis. Et ideo quae non recipiunt formas nisi materialiter, nullo modo sunt cognoscitiva, sicut plantae; ut dicitur in II libro de anima. Quanto autem aliquid immaterialius habet formam rei cognitae, tanto perfectius cognoscit. Unde et intellectus, qui abstrahit speciem non solum a materia, sed etiam a materialibus conditionibus individuantibus, perfectius cognoscit quam sensus, qui accipit formam rei cognitae sine materia quidem, sed cum materialibus conditionibus. Et inter ipsos sensus, visus est magis cognoscitivus, quia est minus materialis, ut supra dictum est. Et inter ipsos intellectus, tanto quilibet est perfectior, quanto immaterialior. Ex his ergo patet quod, si aliquis intellectus est qui per essentiam suam cognoscit omnia, oportet quod essentia eius habeat in se immaterialiter omnia; sicut antiqui posuerunt essentiam animae actu componi ex principiis omnium materialium, ut cognosceret omnia. Hoc autem est proprium Dei, ut sua essentia sit immaterialiter comprehensiva omnium, prout effectus virtute praeexistunt in causa. Solus igitur Deus per essentiam suam omnia intelligit; non autem anima humana, neque etiam Angelus.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31962)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus ibi loquitur de visione imaginaria, quae fit per imagines corporum. Quibus imaginibus formandis dat anima aliquid suae substantiae, sicut subiectum datur ut informetur per aliquam formam. Et sic de seipsa facit huiusmodi imagines, non quod anima vel aliquid animae convertatur, ut sit haec vel illa imago; sed sicut dicitur de corpore fieri aliquid coloratum, prout informatur colore. Et hic sensus apparet ex his quae sequuntur. Dicit enim quod servat aliquid, scilicet non formatum tali imagine, quod libere de specie talium imaginum iudicet, et hoc dicit esse mentem vel intellectum. Partem autem quae informatur huiusmodi imaginibus scilicet imaginativam, dicit esse communem nobis et bestiis.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31963)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Aristoteles non posuit animam esse actu compositam ex omnibus, sicut antiqui naturales; sed dixit quodammodo animam esse omnia, inquantum est in potentia ad omnia; per sensum quidem ad sensibilia, per intellectum vero ad intelligibilia.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31964)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod quaelibet creatura habet esse finitum et determinatum. Unde essentia superioris creaturae, etsi habeat quandam similitudinem inferioris creaturae prout communicant in aliquo genere, non tamen complete habet similitudinem illius, quia determinatur ad aliquam speciem, praeter quam est species inferioris creaturae. Sed essentia Dei est perfecta similitudo omnium quantum ad omnia quae in rebus inveniuntur, sicut universale principium omnium.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 84 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31965)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima intelligat omnia per species sibi naturaliter inditas. Dicit enim Gregorius, in homilia ascensionis quod *homo habet commune cum Angelis intelligere*. Sed Angeli intelligunt omnia per formas naturaliter inditas, unde in libro de causis dicitur quod *omnis intelligentia est plena formis*. Ergo et anima habet species rerum naturaliter inditas, quibus corporalia intelligit.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31966)\n\nPraeterea, anima intellectiva est nobilior quam materia prima corporalis. Sed materia prima est creata a Deo sub formis ad quas est in potentia. Ergo multo magis anima intellectiva est creata a Deo sub speciebus intelligibilibus. Et sic anima intelligit corporalia per species sibi naturaliter inditas.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31967)\n\nPraeterea, nullus potest verum respondere nisi de eo quod scit. Sed aliquis etiam idiota, non habens scientiam acquisitam, respondet verum de singulis, si tamen ordinate interrogetur, ut narratur in Menone Platonis de quodam. Ergo antequam aliquis acquirat scientiam, habet rerum cognitionem. Quod non esset nisi anima haberet species naturaliter inditas. Intelligit igitur anima res corporeas per species naturaliter inditas.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31968)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in III de anima, de intellectu loquens, quod est sicut tabula in qua nihil est scriptum.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31969)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum forma sit principium actionis, oportet ut eo modo se habeat aliquid ad formam quae est actionis principium, quo se habet ad actionem illam, sicut si moveri sursum est ex levitate, oportet quod in potentia tantum sursum fertur, esse leve solum in potentia, quod autem actu sursum fertur, esse leve in actu. Videmus autem quod homo est quandoque cognoscens in potentia tantum, tam secundum sensum quam secundum intellectum. Et de tali potentia in actum reducitur, ut sentiat quidem, per actiones sensibilium in sensum; ut intelligat autem, per disciplinam aut inventionem. Unde oportet dicere quod anima cognoscitiva sit in potentia tam ad similitudines quae sunt principia sentiendi, quam ad similitudines quae sunt principia intelligendi. Et propter hoc Aristoteles posuit quod intellectus, quo anima intelligit, non habet aliquas species naturaliter inditas, sed est in principio in potentia ad huiusmodi species omnes. Sed quia id quod habet actu formam, interdum non potest agere secundum formam propter aliquod impedimentum, sicut leve si impediatur sursum ferri; propter hoc Plato posuit quod intellectus hominis naturaliter est plenus omnibus speciebus intelligibilibus, sed per unionem corporis impeditur ne possit in actum exire. Sed hoc non videtur convenienter dictum. Primo quidem quia, si habet anima naturalem notitiam omnium, non videtur esse possibile quod huius naturalis notitiae tantam oblivionem capiat, quod nesciat se huiusmodi scientiam habere, nullus enim homo obliviscitur ea quae naturaliter cognoscit, sicut quod omne totum sit maius sua parte, et alia huiusmodi. Praecipue autem hoc videtur inconveniens, si ponatur esse animae naturale corpori uniri, ut supra habitum est, inconveniens enim est quod naturalis operatio alicuius rei totaliter impediatur per id quod est sibi secundum naturam. Secundo, manifeste apparet huius positionis falsitas ex hoc quod, deficiente aliquo sensu, deficit scientia eorum, quae apprehenduntur secundum illum sensum; sicut caecus natus nullam potest habere notitiam de coloribus. Quod non esset, si animae essent naturaliter inditae omnium intelligibilium rationes. Et ideo dicendum est quod anima non cognoscit corporalia per species naturaliter inditas.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31970)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod homo quidem convenit cum Angelis in intelligendo, deficit tamen ab eminentia intellectus eorum, sicut et corpora inferiora, quae tantum existunt secundum Gregorium, deficiunt ab existentia superiorum corporum. Nam materia inferiorum corporum non est completa totaliter per formam, sed est in potentia ad formas quas non habet, materia autem caelestium corporum est totaliter completa per formam, ita quod non est in potentia ad aliam formam, ut supra habitum est. Et similiter intellectus Angeli est perfectus per species intelligibiles secundum suam naturam, intellectus autem humanus est in potentia ad huiusmodi species.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31971)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod materia prima habet esse substantiale per formam, et ideo oportuit quod crearetur sub aliqua forma, alioquin non esset in actu. Sub una tamen forma existens, est in potentia ad alias. Intellectus autem non habet esse substantiale per speciem intelligibilem; et ideo non est simile.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31972)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ordinata interrogatio procedit ex principiis communibus per se notis, ad propria. Per talem autem processum scientia causatur in anima addiscentis. Unde cum verum respondet de his de quibus secundo interrogatur, hoc non est quia prius ea noverit; sed quia tunc ea de novo addiscit. Nihil enim refert utrum ille qui docet, proponendo vel interrogando procedat de principiis communibus ad conclusiones, utrobique enim animus audientis certificatur de posterioribus per priora.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 84 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31973)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod species intelligibiles effluant in animam ab aliquibus formis separatis. Omne enim quod per participationem est tale, causatur ab eo quod est per essentiam tale; sicut quod est ignitum reducitur sicut in causam in ignem. Sed anima intellectiva, secundum quod est actu intelligens, participat ipsa intelligibilia, intellectus enim in actu, quodammodo est intellectum in actu. Ergo ea quae secundum se et per essentiam suam sunt intellecta in actu, sunt causae animae intellectivae quod actu intelligat. Intellecta autem in actu per essentiam suam, sunt formae sine materia existentes. Species igitur intelligibiles quibus anima intelligit, causantur a formis aliquibus separatis.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31974)\n\nPraeterea, intelligibilia se habent ad intellectum, sicut sensibilia ad sensum. Sed sensibilia quae sunt in actu extra animam, sunt causae specierum sensibilium quae sunt in sensu, quibus sentimus. Ergo species intelligibiles quibus intellectus noster intelligit, causantur ab aliquibus actu intelligibilibus extra animam existentibus. Huiusmodi autem non sunt nisi formae a materia separatae. Formae igitur intelligibiles intellectus nostri effluunt ab aliquibus substantiis separatis.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31975)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod est in potentia, reducitur in actum per id quod est actu. Si ergo intellectus noster, prius in potentia existens, postmodum actu intelligat, oportet quod hoc causetur ab aliquo intellectu qui semper est in actu. Hic autem est intellectus separatus. Ergo ab aliquibus substantiis separatis causantur species intelligibiles quibus actu intelligimus.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31976)\n\nSed contra est quia secundum hoc sensibus non indigeremus ad intelligendum. Quod patet esse falsum ex hoc praecipue quod qui caret uno sensu, nullo modo potest habere scientiam de sensibilibus illius sensus.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31977)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam posuerunt species intelligibiles nostri intellectus procedere ab aliquibus formis vel substantiis separatis. Et hoc dupliciter. Plato enim, sicut dictum est, posuit formas rerum sensibilium per se sine materia subsistentes; sicut formam hominis, quam nominabat per se hominem, et formam vel ideam equi, quam nominabat per se equum, et sic de aliis. Has ergo formas separatas ponebat participari et ab anima nostra, et a materia corporali; ab anima quidem nostra ad cognoscendum, a materia vero corporali ad essendum; ut sicut materia corporalis per hoc quod participat ideam lapidis, fit hic lapis, ita intellectus noster per hoc quod participat ideam lapidis, fit intelligens lapidem. Participatio autem ideae fit per aliquam similitudinem ipsius ideae in participante ipsam, per modum quo exemplar participatur ab exemplato. Sicut igitur ponebat formas sensibiles quae sunt in materia corporali, effluere ab ideis sicut quasdam earum similitudines; ita ponebat species intelligibiles nostri intellectus esse similitudines quasdam idearum ab eis effluentes. Et propter hoc, ut supra dictum est, scientias et definitiones ad ideas referebat. Sed quia contra rationem rerum sensibilium est quod earum formae subsistant absque materiis, ut Aristoteles multipliciter probat; ideo Avicenna, hac positione remota, posuit omnium rerum sensibilium intelligibiles species, non quidem per se subsistere absque materia, sed praeexistere immaterialiter in intellectibus separatis; a quorum primo derivantur huiusmodi species in sequentem, et sic de aliis usque ad ultimum intellectum separatum, quem nominat intellectum agentem; a quo, ut ipse dicit, effluunt species intelligibiles in animas nostras, et formae sensibiles in materiam corporalem. Et sic in hoc Avicenna cum Platone concordat, quod species intelligibiles nostri intellectus effluunt a quibusdam formis separatis, quas tamen Plato dicit per se subsistere, Avicenna vero ponit eas in intelligentia agente. Differunt etiam quantum ad hoc, quod Avicenna ponit species intelligibiles non remanere in intellectu nostro postquam desinit actu intelligere; sed indiget ut iterato se convertat ad recipiendum de novo. Unde non ponit scientiam animae naturaliter inditam, sicut Plato, qui ponit participationes idearum immobiliter in anima permanere. Sed secundum hanc positionem sufficiens ratio assignari non posset quare anima nostra corpori uniretur. Non enim potest dici quod anima intellectiva corpori uniatur propter corpus, quia nec forma est propter materiam, nec motor propter mobile, sed potius e converso. Maxime autem videtur corpus esse necessarium animae intellectivae ad eius propriam operationem, quae est intelligere, quia secundum esse suum a corpore non dependet. Si autem anima species intelligibiles secundum suam naturam apta nata esset recipere per influentiam aliquorum separatorum principiorum tantum, et non acciperet eas ex sensibus, non indigeret corpore ad intelligendum, unde frustra corpori uniretur. Si autem dicatur quod indiget anima nostra sensibus ad intelligendum, quibus quodammodo excitetur ad consideranda ea quorum species intelligibiles a principiis separatis recipit; hoc non videtur sufficere. Quia huiusmodi excitatio non videtur necessaria animae nisi inquantum est consopita, secundum Platonicos, quodammodo et obliviosa propter unionem ad corpus, et sic sensus non proficerent animae intellectivae nisi ad tollendum impedimentum quod animae provenit ex corporis unione. Remanet igitur quaerendum quae sit causa unionis animae ad corpus. Si autem dicatur, secundum Avicennam, quod sensus sunt animae necessarii, quia per eos excitatur ut convertat se ad intelligentiam agentem, a qua recipit species; hoc quidem non sufficit. Quia si in natura animae est ut intelligat per species ab intelligentia agente effluxas, sequeretur quod quandoque anima possit se convertere ad intelligentiam agentem ex inclinatione suae naturae, vel etiam excitata per alium sensum, ut convertat se ad intelligentiam agentem ad recipiendum species sensibilium quorum sensum aliquis non habet. Et sic caecus natus posset habere scientiam de coloribus, quod est manifeste falsum. Unde dicendum est quod species intelligibiles quibus anima nostra intelligit, non effluunt a formis separatis.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31978)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod species intelligibiles quas participat noster intellectus, reducuntur sicut in primam causam in aliquod principium per suam essentiam intelligibile, scilicet in Deum. Sed ab illo principio procedunt mediantibus formis rerum sensibilium et materialium, a quibus scientiam colligimus, ut Dionysius dicit.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31979)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod res materiales, secundum esse quod habent extra animam, possunt esse sensibiles actu; non autem actu intelligibiles. Unde non est simile de sensu et intellectu.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31980)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod intellectus noster possibilis reducitur de potentia ad actum per aliquod ens actu, idest per intellectum agentem, qui est virtus quaedam animae nostrae, ut dictum est, non autem per aliquem intellectum separatum, sicut per causam proximam; sed forte sicut per causam remotam.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 84 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31981)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima intellectiva non cognoscat res materiales in rationibus aeternis. Id enim in quo aliquid cognoscitur, ipsum magis et per prius cognoscitur. Sed anima intellectiva hominis, in statu praesentis vitae, non cognoscit rationes aeternas, quia non cognoscit ipsum Deum, in quo rationes aeternae existunt, sed ei sicut ignoto coniungitur, ut Dionysius dicit in I cap. mysticae theologiae. Ergo anima non cognoscit omnia in rationibus aeternis.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31982)\n\nPraeterea, Rom. I, dicitur quod *invisibilia Dei per ea quae facta sunt, conspiciuntur*. Sed inter invisibilia Dei numerantur rationes aeternae. Ergo rationes aeternae per creaturas materiales cognoscuntur, et non e converso.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31983)\n\nPraeterea, rationes aeternae nihil aliud sunt quam ideae, dicit enim Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod *ideae sunt rationes stabiles rerum in mente divina existentes*. Si ergo dicatur quod anima intellectiva cognoscit omnia in rationibus aeternis, redibit opinio Platonis, qui posuit omnem scientiam ab ideis derivari.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31984)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, XII Confess., *si ambo videmus verum esse quod dicis, et ambo videmus verum esse quod dico, ubi quaeso id videmus? Nec ego utique in te, nec tu in me sed ambo in ipsa, quae supra mentes nostras est, incommutabili veritate*. Veritas autem incommutabilis in aeternis rationibus continetur. Ergo anima intellectiva omnia vera cognoscit in rationibus aeternis.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31985)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit in II de Doctr. Christ., *philosophi qui vocantur, si qua forte vera et fidei nostrae accommoda dixerunt, ab eis tanquam ab iniustis possessoribus in usum nostrum vindicanda sunt. Habent enim doctrinae gentilium quaedam simulata et superstitiosa figmenta, quae unusquisque nostrum de societate gentilium exiens, debet evitare*. Et ideo Augustinus, qui doctrinis Platonicorum imbutus fuerat, si qua invenit fidei accommoda in eorum dictis, assumpsit; quae vero invenit fidei nostrae adversa, in melius commutavit. Posuit autem Plato, sicut supra dictum est, formas rerum per se subsistere a materia separatas, quas ideas vocabat, per quarum participationem dicebat intellectum nostrum omnia cognoscere; ut sicut materia corporalis per participationem ideae lapidis fit lapis, ita intellectus noster per participationem eiusdem ideae cognosceret lapidem. Sed quia videtur esse alienum a fide quod formae rerum extra res per se subsistant absque materia, sicut Platonici posuerunt, dicentes per se vitam aut per se sapientiam esse quasdam substantias creatrices, ut Dionysius dicit XI cap. de Div. Nom.; ideo Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., posuit loco harum idearum quas Plato ponebat, rationes omnium creaturarum in mente divina existere, secundum quas omnia formantur, et secundum quas etiam anima humana omnia cognoscit. Cum ergo quaeritur utrum anima humana in rationibus aeternis omnia cognoscat, dicendum est quod aliquid in aliquo dicitur cognosci dupliciter. Uno modo, sicut in obiecto cognito; sicut aliquis videt in speculo ea quorum imagines in speculo resultant. Et hoc modo anima, in statu praesentis vitae, non potest videre omnia in rationibus aeternis; sed sic in rationibus aeternis cognoscunt omnia beati, qui Deum vident et omnia in ipso. Alio modo dicitur aliquid cognosci in aliquo sicut in cognitionis principio; sicut si dicamus quod in sole videntur ea quae videntur per solem. Et sic necesse est dicere quod anima humana omnia cognoscat in rationibus aeternis, per quarum participationem omnia cognoscimus. Ipsum enim lumen intellectuale quod est in nobis, nihil est aliud quam quaedam participata similitudo luminis increati, in quo continentur rationes aeternae. Unde in Psalmo IV, dicitur, *multi dicunt, quis ostendit nobis bona?* Cui quaestioni Psalmista respondet, dicens, *signatum est super nos lumen vultus tui, domine*. Quasi dicat, per ipsam sigillationem divini luminis in nobis, omnia nobis demonstrantur. Quia tamen praeter lumen intellectuale in nobis, exiguntur species intelligibiles a rebus acceptae, ad scientiam de rebus materialibus habendam; ideo non per solam participationem rationum aeternarum de rebus materialibus notitiam habemus, sicut Platonici posuerunt quod sola idearum participatio sufficit ad scientiam habendam. Unde Augustinus dicit, in IV de Trin., *numquid quia philosophi documentis certissimis persuadent aeternis rationibus omnia temporalia fieri, propterea potuerunt in ipsis rationibus perspicere, vel ex ipsis colligere quot sint animalium genera, quae semina singulorum? Nonne ista omnia per locorum ac temporum historiam quaesierunt?* Quod autem Augustinus non sic intellexerit omnia cognosci in rationibus aeternis, vel in incommutabili veritate, quasi ipsae rationes aeternae videantur, patet per hoc quod ipse dicit in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod *rationalis anima non omnis et quaelibet, sed quae sancta et pura fuerit, asseritur illi visioni*, scilicet rationum aeternarum, esse idonea; sicut sunt animae beatorum.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 5 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31986)\n\nEt per haec patet responsio ad obiecta.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 84 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31987)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectiva cognitio non accipiatur a rebus sensibilibus. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod *non est expectanda sinceritas veritatis a corporis sensibus*. Et hoc probat dupliciter. Uno modo, per hoc quod *omne quod corporeus sensus attingit, sine ulla intermissione temporis commutatur, quod autem non manet, percipi non potest*. Alio modo, per hoc quod *omnia quae per corpus sentimus, etiam cum non adsunt sensibus, imagines tamen eorum patimur, ut in somno vel furore; non autem sensibus discernere valemus utrum ipsa sensibilia, vel imagines eorum falsas sentiamus. Nihil autem percipi potest quod a falso non discernitur*. Et sic concludit quod non est expectanda veritas a sensibus. Sed cognitio intellectualis est apprehensiva veritatis. Non ergo cognitio intellectualis est expectanda a sensibus.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31988)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, XII super Gen. ad Litt., *non est putandum facere aliquid corpus in spiritum, tanquam spiritus corpori facienti materiae vice subdatur, omni enim modo praestantior est qui facit, ea re de qua aliquid facit*. Unde concludit quod *imaginem corporis non corpus in spiritu, sed ipse spiritus in seipso facit*. Non ergo intellectualis cognitio a sensibilibus derivatur.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31989)\n\nPraeterea, effectus non se extendit ultra virtutem suae causae. Sed intellectualis cognitio se extendit ultra sensibilia, intelligimus enim quaedam quae sensu percipi non possunt. Intellectualis ergo cognitio non derivatur a rebus sensibilibus.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31990)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus probat, I Metaphys., et in fine Poster., quod principium nostrae cognitionis est a sensu.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31991)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa istam quaestionem triplex fuit philosophorum opinio. Democritus enim posuit quod *nulla est alia causa cuiuslibet nostrae cognitionis, nisi cum ab his corporibus quae cogitamus, veniunt atque intrant imagines in animas nostras*; ut Augustinus dicit in epistola sua ad Dioscorum. Et Aristoteles etiam dicit, in libro de Somn. et Vigil., quod Democritus posuit cognitionem fieri per idola et defluxiones. Et huius positionis ratio fuit, quia tam ipse Democritus quam alii antiqui naturales non ponebant intellectum differre a sensu, ut Aristoteles dicit in libro de anima. Et ideo, quia sensus immutatur a sensibili, arbitrabantur omnem nostram cognitionem fieri per solam immutationem a sensibilibus. Quam quidem immutationem Democritus asserebat fieri per imaginum defluxiones. Plato vero e contrario posuit intellectum differre a sensu; et intellectum quidem esse virtutem immaterialem organo corporeo non utentem in suo actu. Et quia incorporeum non potest immutari a corporeo, posuit quod cognitio intellectualis non fit per immutationem intellectus a sensibilibus, sed per participationem formarum intelligibilium separatarum, ut dictum est. Sensum etiam posuit virtutem quandam per se operantem. Unde nec ipse sensus, cum sit quaedam vis spiritualis, immutatur a sensibilibus, sed organa sensuum a sensibilibus immutantur, ex qua immutatione anima quodammodo excitatur ut in se species sensibilium formet. Et hanc opinionem tangere videtur Augustinus, XII super Gen. ad Litt., ubi dicit quod *corpus non sentit, sed anima per corpus, quo velut nuntio utitur ad formandum in seipsa quod extrinsecus nuntiatur*. Sic igitur secundum Platonis opinionem, neque intellectualis cognitio a sensibili procedit, neque etiam sensibilis totaliter a sensibilibus rebus; sed sensibilia excitant animam sensibilem ad sentiendum, et similiter sensus excitant animam intellectivam ad intelligendum. Aristoteles autem media via processit. Posuit enim cum Platone intellectum differre a sensu. Sed sensum posuit propriam operationem non habere sine communicatione corporis; ita quod sentire non sit actus animae tantum, sed coniuncti. Et similiter posuit de omnibus operationibus sensitivae partis. Quia igitur non est inconveniens quod sensibilia quae sunt extra animam, causent aliquid in coniunctum, in hoc Aristoteles cum Democrito concordavit, quod operationes sensitivae partis causentur per impressionem sensibilium in sensum, non per modum defluxionis, ut Democritus posuit, sed per quandam operationem. Nam et Democritus omnem actionem fieri posuit per influxionem atomorum, ut patet in I de Generat. Intellectum vero posuit Aristoteles habere operationem absque communicatione corporis. Nihil autem corporeum imprimere potest in rem incorpoream. Et ideo ad causandam intellectualem operationem, secundum Aristotelem, non sufficit sola impressio sensibilium corporum, sed requiritur aliquid nobilius, quia agens est honorabilius patiente, ut ipse dicit. Non tamen ita quod intellectualis operatio causetur in nobis ex sola impressione aliquarum rerum superiorum, ut Plato posuit, sed illud superius et nobilius agens quod vocat intellectum agentem, de quo iam supra diximus, facit phantasmata a sensibus accepta intelligibilia in actu, per modum abstractionis cuiusdam. Secundum hoc ergo, ex parte phantasmatum intellectualis operatio a sensu causatur. Sed quia phantasmata non sufficiunt immutare intellectum possibilem, sed oportet quod fiant intelligibilia actu per intellectum agentem; non potest dici quod sensibilis cognitio sit totalis et perfecta causa intellectualis cognitionis, sed magis quodammodo est materia causae.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31992)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod per verba illa Augustini datur intelligi quod veritas non sit totaliter a sensibus expectanda. Requiritur enim lumen intellectus agentis, per quod immutabiliter veritatem in rebus mutabilibus cognoscamus, et discernamus ipsas res a similitudinibus rerum.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31993)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Augustinus ibi non loquitur de intellectuali cognitione, sed de imaginaria. Et quia, secundum Platonis opinionem, vis imaginaria habet operationem quae est animae solius; eadem ratione usus est Augustinus ad ostendendum quod corpora non imprimunt suas similitudines in vim imaginariam, sed hoc facit ipsa anima, qua utitur Aristoteles ad probandum intellectum agentem esse aliquid separatum, quia scilicet agens est honorabilius patiente. Et procul dubio oportet, secundum hanc positionem, in vi imaginativa ponere non solum potentiam passivam, sed etiam activam. Sed si ponamus, secundum opinionem Aristotelis, quod actio virtutis imaginativae sit coniuncti, nulla sequitur difficultas, quia corpus sensibile est nobilius organo animalis, secundum hoc quod comparatur ad ipsum ut ens in actu ad ens in potentia, sicut coloratum in actu ad pupillam, quae colorata est in potentia. Posset tamen dici quod, quamvis prima immutatio virtutis imaginariae sit per motum sensibilium, quia phantasia est motus factus secundum sensum, ut dicitur in libro de anima; tamen est quaedam operatio animae in homine quae dividendo et componendo format diversas rerum imagines, etiam quae non sunt a sensibus acceptae. Et quantum ad hoc possunt accipi verba Augustini.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31994)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod sensitiva cognitio non est tota causa intellectualis cognitionis. Et ideo non est mirum si intellectualis cognitio ultra sensitivam se extendit.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 84 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31995)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus possit actu intelligere per species intelligibiles quas penes se habet, non convertendo se ad phantasmata. Intellectus enim fit in actu per speciem intelligibilem qua informatur. Sed intellectum esse in actu, est ipsum intelligere. Ergo species intelligibiles sufficiunt ad hoc quod intellectus actu intelligat, absque hoc quod ad phantasmata se convertat.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31996)\n\nPraeterea, magis dependet imaginatio a sensu, quam intellectus ab imaginatione. Sed imaginatio potest imaginari actu, absentibus sensibilibus. Ergo multo magis intellectus potest intelligere actu, non convertendo se ad phantasmata.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31997)\n\nPraeterea, incorporalium non sunt aliqua phantasmata, quia imaginatio tempus et continuum non transcendit. Si ergo intellectus noster non posset aliquid intelligere in actu nisi converteretur ad phantasmata, sequeretur quod non posset intelligere incorporeum aliquid. Quod patet esse falsum, intelligimus enim veritatem ipsam, et Deum et Angelos.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31998)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod *nihil sine phantasmate intelligit anima*.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31999)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod impossibile est intellectum nostrum, secundum praesentis vitae statum, quo passibili corpori coniungitur, aliquid intelligere in actu, nisi convertendo se ad phantasmata. Et hoc duobus indiciis apparet. Primo quidem quia, cum intellectus sit vis quaedam non utens corporali organo, nullo modo impediretur in suo actu per laesionem alicuius corporalis organi, si non requireretur ad eius actum actus alicuius potentiae utentis organo corporali. Utuntur autem organo corporali sensus et imaginatio et aliae vires pertinentes ad partem sensitivam. Unde manifestum est quod ad hoc quod intellectus actu intelligat, non solum accipiendo scientiam de novo, sed etiam utendo scientia iam acquisita, requiritur actus imaginationis et ceterarum virtutum. Videmus enim quod, impedito actu virtutis imaginativae per laesionem organi, ut in phreneticis; et similiter impedito actu memorativae virtutis, ut in lethargicis; impeditur homo ab intelligendo in actu etiam ea quorum scientiam praeaccepit. Secundo, quia hoc quilibet in seipso experiri potest, quod quando aliquis conatur aliquid intelligere, format aliqua phantasmata sibi per modum exemplorum, in quibus quasi inspiciat quod intelligere studet. Et inde est etiam quod quando alium volumus facere aliquid intelligere, proponimus ei exempla, ex quibus sibi phantasmata formare possit ad intelligendum. Huius autem ratio est, quia potentia cognoscitiva proportionatur cognoscibili. Unde intellectus angelici, qui est totaliter a corpore separatus, obiectum proprium est substantia intelligibilis a corpore separata; et per huiusmodi intelligibilia materialia cognoscit. Intellectus autem humani, qui est coniunctus corpori, proprium obiectum est quidditas sive natura in materia corporali existens; et per huiusmodi naturas visibilium rerum etiam in invisibilium rerum aliqualem cognitionem ascendit. De ratione autem huius naturae est, quod in aliquo individuo existat, quod non est absque materia corporali, sicut de ratione naturae lapidis est quod sit in hoc lapide, et de ratione naturae equi quod sit in hoc equo, et sic de aliis. Unde natura lapidis, vel cuiuscumque materialis rei, cognosci non potest complete et vere, nisi secundum quod cognoscitur ut in particulari existens. Particulare autem apprehendimus per sensum et imaginationem. Et ideo necesse est ad hoc quod intellectus actu intelligat suum obiectum proprium, quod convertat se ad phantasmata, ut speculetur naturam universalem in particulari existentem. Si autem proprium obiectum intellectus nostri esset forma separata; vel si naturae rerum sensibilium subsisterent non in particularibus, secundum Platonicos; non oporteret quod intellectus noster semper intelligendo converteret se ad phantasmata.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32000)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod species conservatae in intellectu possibili, in eo existunt habitualiter quando actu non intelligit, sicut supra dictum est. Unde ad hoc quod intelligamus in actu, non sufficit ipsa conservatio specierum; sed oportet quod eis utamur secundum quod convenit rebus quarum sunt species, quae sunt naturae in particularibus existentes.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32001)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod etiam ipsum phantasma est similitudo rei particularis, unde non indiget imaginatio aliqua alia similitudine particularis, sicut indiget intellectus.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32002)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod incorporea, quorum non sunt phantasmata, cognoscuntur a nobis per comparationem ad corpora sensibilia, quorum sunt phantasmata. Sicut veritatem intelligimus ex consideratione rei circa quam veritatem speculamur; Deum autem, ut Dionysius dicit, cognoscimus ut causam, et per excessum, et per remotionem; alias etiam incorporeas substantias, in statu praesentis vitae, cognoscere non possumus nisi per remotionem, vel aliquam comparationem ad corporalia. Et ideo cum de huiusmodi aliquid intelligimus, necesse habemus converti ad phantasmata corporum, licet ipsorum non sint phantasmata.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 84 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32003)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod iudicium intellectus non impediatur per ligamentum sensus. Superius enim non dependet ab inferiori. Sed iudicium intellectus est supra sensum. Ergo iudicium intellectus non impeditur per ligamentum sensus.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32004)\n\nPraeterea, syllogizare est actus intellectus. In somno autem ligatur sensus, ut dicitur in libro de Somn. et Vig.; contingit tamen quandoque quod aliquis dormiens syllogizat. Ergo non impeditur iudicium intellectus per ligamentum sensus.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32005)\n\nSed contra est quod in dormiendo ea quae contra licitos mores contingunt, non imputantur ad peccatum; ut Augustinus in XII super Gen. ad Litt. dicit. Hoc autem non esset si homo in dormiendo liberum usum rationis et intellectus haberet. Ergo impeditur rationis usus per ligamentum sensus.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32006)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, proprium obiectum intellectui nostro proportionatum est natura rei sensibilis. Iudicium autem perfectum de re aliqua dari non potest, nisi ea omnia quae ad rem pertinent cognoscantur, et praecipue si ignoretur id quod est terminus et finis iudicii. Dicit autem philosophus, in III de caelo, quod *sicut finis factivae scientiae est opus, ita naturalis scientiae finis est quod videtur principaliter secundum sensum*, faber enim non quaerit cognitionem cultelli nisi propter opus, ut operetur hunc particularem cultellum; et similiter naturalis non quaerit cognoscere naturam lapidis et equi, nisi ut sciat rationes eorum quae videntur secundum sensum. Manifestum est autem quod non posset esse perfectum iudicium fabri de cultello, si opus ignoraret, et similiter non potest esse perfectum iudicium scientiae naturalis de rebus naturalibus, si sensibilia ignorentur. Omnia autem quae in praesenti statu intelligimus, cognoscuntur a nobis per comparationem ad res sensibiles naturales. Unde impossibile est quod sit in nobis iudicium intellectus perfectum, cum ligamento sensus, per quem res sensibiles cognoscimus.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32007)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, quamvis intellectus sit superior sensu, accipit tamen aliquo modo a sensu, et eius obiecta prima et principalia in sensibilibus fundantur. Et ideo necesse est quod impediatur iudicium intellectus ex ligamento sensus.\n\n[I q. 84 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32008)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod sensus ligatur in dormientibus propter evaporationes quasdam et fumositates resolutas, ut dicitur in libro de Somn. et Vig. Et ideo secundum dispositionem huiusmodi evaporationum, contingit esse ligamentum sensus maius vel minus. Quando enim multus fuerit motus vaporum, ligatur non solum sensus, sed etiam imaginatio, ita ut nulla appareant phantasmata; sicut praecipue accidit cum aliquis incipit dormire post multum cibum et potum. Si vero motus vaporum aliquantulum fuerit remissior, apparent phantasmata, sed distorta et inordinata; sicut accidit in febricitantibus. Si vero adhuc magis motus sedetur, apparent phantasmata ordinata; sicut maxime solet contingere in fine dormitionis, et in hominibus sobriis et habentibus fortem imaginationem. Si autem motus vaporum fuerit modicus, non solum imaginatio remanet libera, sed etiam ipse sensus communis ex parte solvitur; ita quod homo iudicat interdum in dormiendo ea quae videt somnia esse, quasi diiudicans inter res et rerum similitudines. Sed tamen ex aliqua parte remanet sensus communis ligatus; et ideo, licet aliquas similitudines discernat a rebus, tamen semper in aliquibus decipitur. Sic igitur per modum quo sensus solvitur et imaginatio in dormiendo, liberatur et iudicium intellectus, non tamen ex toto. Unde illi qui dormiendo syllogizant, cum excitantur, semper recognoscunt se in aliquo defecisse.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0002","text":"[I q. 84 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31948)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0003","text":"Consequenter considerandum est de actibus animae, quantum ad potentias intellectivas et appetitivas, aliae enim animae potentiae non pertinent directe ad considerationem theologi. Actus autem appetitivae partis ad considerationem moralis scientiae pertinent, et ideo in secunda parte huius operis de eis tractabitur, in qua considerandum erit de morali materia. Nunc autem de actibus intellectivae partis agetur. In consideratione vero actuum, hoc modo procedemus, primo namque considerandum est quomodo intelligit anima corpori coniuncta; secundo, quomodo intelligit a corpore separata. Prima autem consideratio erit tripartita, primo namque considerabitur quomodo anima intelligit corporalia, quae sunt infra ipsam; secundo, quomodo intelligit seipsam, et ea quae in ipsa sunt; tertio, quomodo intelligit substantias immateriales, quae sunt supra ipsam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0004","text":"Circa cognitionem vero corporalium, tria consideranda occurrunt, primo quidem per quid ea cognoscit; secundo, quomodo et quo ordine; tertio, quid in eis cognoscit. Circa primum quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum anima cognoscat corpora per intellectum. Secundo, utrum intelligat ea per essentiam suam, vel per aliquas species. Tertio, si per aliquas species, utrum species omnium intelligibilium sint ei naturaliter innatae. Quarto, utrum effluant in ipsam ab aliquibus formis immaterialibus separatis. Quinto, utrum anima nostra omnia quae intelligit, videat in rationibus aeternis. Sexto, utrum cognitionem intelligibilem acquirat a sensu. Septimo, utrum intellectus possit actu intelligere per species intelligibiles quas penes se habet, non convertendo se ad phantasmata. Octavo, utrum iudicium intellectus impediatur per impedimentum sensitivarum virtutum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0005","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0006","text":"[I q. 84 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31949)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0007","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima non cognoscat corpora per intellectum. Dicit enim Augustinus, in II Soliloq. quod *corpora intellectu comprehendi non possunt; nec aliquod corporeum nisi sensibus videri potest*. Dicit etiam, XII super Gen. ad Litt., quod visio intellectualis est eorum quae sunt per essentiam suam in anima. Huiusmodi autem non sunt corpora. Ergo anima per intellectum corpora cognoscere non potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0008","text":"[I q. 84 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31950)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0009","text":"Praeterea, sicut se habet sensus ad intelligibilia, ita se habet intellectus ad sensibilia. Sed anima per sensum nullo modo potest cognoscere spiritualia, quae sunt intelligibilia. Ergo nullo modo per intellectum potest cognoscere corpora, quae sunt sensibilia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0010","text":"[I q. 84 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31951)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0011","text":"Praeterea, intellectus est necessariorum et semper eodem modo se habentium. Sed corpora omnia sunt mobilia, et non eodem modo se habentia. Anima ergo per intellectum corpora cognoscere non potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0012","text":"[I q. 84 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31952)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0013","text":"Sed contra est quod scientia est in intellectu. Si ergo intellectus non cognoscit corpora, sequitur quod nulla scientia sit de corporibus. Et sic peribit scientia naturalis, quae est de corpore mobili.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0014","text":"[I q. 84 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31953)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0015","text":"Respondeo dicendum, ad evidentiam huius quaestionis, quod primi philosophi qui de naturis rerum inquisiverunt, putaverunt nihil esse in mundo praeter corpus. Et quia videbant omnia corpora mobilia esse, et putabant ea in continuo fluxu esse, aestimaverunt quod nulla certitudo de rerum veritate haberi posset a nobis. Quod enim est in continuo fluxu, per certitudinem apprehendi non potest, quia prius labitur quam mente diiudicetur, sicut Heraclitus dixit quod *non est possibile aquam fluvii currentis bis tangere*, ut recitat philosophus in IV Metaphys.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0016","text":"His autem superveniens Plato, ut posset salvare certam cognitionem veritatis a nobis per intellectum haberi, posuit praeter ista corporalia aliud genus entium a materia et motu separatum, quod nominabat species sive ideas, per quarum participationem unumquodque istorum singularium et sensibilium dicitur vel homo vel equus vel aliquid huiusmodi. Sic ergo dicebat scientias et definitiones et quidquid ad actum intellectus pertinet, non referri ad ista corpora sensibilia, sed ad illa immaterialia et separata; ut sic anima non intelligat ista corporalia, sed intelligat horum corporalium species separatas. Sed hoc dupliciter apparet falsum. Primo quidem quia, cum illae species sint immateriales et immobiles, excluderetur a scientiis cognitio motus et materiae (quod est proprium scientiae naturalis) et demonstratio per causas moventes et materiales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0017","text":"Secundo autem, quia derisibile videtur ut, dum rerum quae nobis manifestae sunt notitiam quaerimus, alia entia in medium afferamus, quae non possunt esse earum substantiae, cum ab eis differant secundum esse, et sic, illis substantiis separatis cognitis, non propter hoc de istis sensibilibus iudicare possemus. Videtur autem in hoc Plato deviasse a veritate, quia, cum aestimaret omnem cognitionem per modum alicuius similitudinis esse, credidit quod forma cogniti ex necessitate sit in cognoscente eo modo quo est in cognito. Consideravit autem quod forma rei intellectae est in intellectu universaliter et immaterialiter et immobiliter, quod ex ipsa operatione intellectus apparet, qui intelligit universaliter et per modum necessitatis cuiusdam; modus enim actionis est secundum modum formae agentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0018","text":"Et ideo existimavit quod oporteret res intellectas hoc modo in seipsis subsistere, scilicet immaterialiter et immobiliter. Hoc autem necessarium non est. Quia etiam in ipsis sensibilibus videmus quod forma alio modo est in uno sensibilium quam in altero, puta cum in uno est albedo intensior, in alio remissior, et in uno est albedo cum dulcedine, in alio sine dulcedine. Et per hunc etiam modum forma sensibilis alio modo est in re quae est extra animam, et alio modo in sensu, qui suscipit formas sensibilium absque materia, sicut colorem auri sine auro. Et similiter intellectus species, corporum, quae sunt materiales et mobiles, recipit immaterialiter et immobiliter, secundum modum suum, nam receptum est in recipiente per modum recipientis. Dicendum est ergo quod anima per intellectum cognoscit corpora cognitione immateriali, universali et necessaria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0019","text":"[I q. 84 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31954)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0020","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod verbum Augustini est intelligendum quantum ad ea quibus intellectus cognoscit, non autem quantum ad ea quae cognoscit. Cognoscit enim corpora intelligendo, sed non per corpora, neque per similitudines materiales et corporeas; sed per species immateriales et intelligibiles, quae per sui essentiam in anima esse possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0021","text":"[I q. 84 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31955)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0022","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit XXII de Civit. Dei, non est dicendum quod, sicut sensus cognoscit sola corporalia, ita intellectus cognoscit sola spiritualia, quia sequeretur quod Deus et Angeli corporalia non cognoscerent. Huius autem diversitatis ratio est, quia inferior virtus non se extendit ad ea quae sunt superioris virtutis; sed virtus superior ea quae sunt inferioris virtutis, excellentiori modo operatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0023","text":"[I q. 84 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31956)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0024","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod omnis motus supponit aliquid immobile, cum enim transmutatio fit secundum qualitatem, remanet substantia immobilis; et cum transmutatur forma substantialis, remanet materia immobilis. Rerum etiam mutabilium sunt immobiles habitudines, sicut Socrates etsi non semper sedeat, tamen immobiliter est verum quod, quandocumque sedet, in uno loco manet. Et propter hoc nihil prohibet de rebus mobilibus immobilem scientiam habere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0025","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0026","text":"[I q. 84 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31957)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0027","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima per essentiam suam corporalia intelligat. Dicit enim Augustinus, X de Trin., quod *anima imagines corporum convolvit et rapit factas in semetipsa de semetipsa, dat enim eis formandis quiddam substantiae suae*. Sed per similitudines corporum corpora intelligit. Ergo per essentiam suam, quam dat formandis talibus similitudinibus, et de qua eas format, cognoscit corporalia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0028","text":"[I q. 84 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31958)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0029","text":"Praeterea, philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod *anima quodammodo est omnia*. Cum ergo simile simili cognoscatur, videtur quod anima per seipsam corporalia cognoscat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0030","text":"[I q. 84 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31959)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0031","text":"Praeterea, anima est superior corporalibus creaturis. Inferiora autem sunt in superioribus eminentiori modo quam in seipsis, ut Dionysius dicit. Ergo omnes creaturae corporeae nobiliori modo existunt in ipsa substantia animae quam in seipsis. Per suam ergo substantiam potest creaturas corporeas cognoscere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0032","text":"[I q. 84 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31960)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0033","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, IX de Trin., quod *mens corporearum rerum notitias per sensus corporis colligit*. Ipsa autem anima non est cognoscibilis per corporis sensus. Non ergo cognoscit corporea per suam substantiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0034","text":"[I q. 84 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31961)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0035","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod antiqui philosophi posuerunt quod anima per suam essentiam cognoscit corpora. Hoc enim animis omnium communiter inditum fuit, quod simile simili cognoscitur. Existimabant autem quod forma cogniti sit in cognoscente eo modo quo est in re cognita. E contrario tamen Platonici posuerunt. Plato enim, quia perspexit intellectualem animam immaterialem esse et immaterialiter cognoscere, posuit formas rerum cognitarum immaterialiter subsistere. Priores vero naturales, quia considerabant res cognitas esse corporeas et materiales, posuerunt oportere res cognitas etiam in anima cognoscente materialiter esse. Et ideo, ut animae attribuerent omnium cognitionem, posuerunt eam habere naturam communem cum omnibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0036","text":"Et quia natura principiatorum ex principiis constituitur, attribuerunt animae naturam principii, ita quod qui dixit principium omnium esse ignem, posuit animam esse de natura ignis; et similiter de aere et aqua. Empedocles autem, qui posuit quatuor elementa materialia et duo moventia, ex his etiam dixit animam esse constitutam. Et ita, cum res materialiter in anima ponerent, posuerunt omnem cognitionem animae materialem esse, non discernentes inter intellectum et sensum. Sed haec opinio improbatur. Primo quidem, quia in materiali principio, de quo loquebantur, non existunt principiata nisi in potentia. Non autem cognoscitur aliquid secundum quod est in potentia, sed solum secundum quod est actu, ut patet in IX Metaphys., unde nec ipsa potentia cognoscitur nisi per actum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0037","text":"Sic igitur non sufficeret attribuere animae principiorum naturam ad hoc quod omnia cognosceret, nisi inessent ei naturae et formae singulorum effectuum, puta ossis et carnis et aliorum huiusmodi; ut Aristoteles contra Empedoclem argumentatur in I de anima. Secundo quia, si oporteret rem cognitam materialiter in cognoscente existere, nulla ratio esset quare res quae materialiter extra animam subsistunt, cognitione carerent, puta, si anima igne cognoscit ignem, et ignis etiam qui est extra animam, ignem cognosceret. Relinquitur ergo quod oportet materialia cognita in cognoscente existere non materialiter, sed magis immaterialiter. Et huius ratio est, quia actus cognitionis se extendit ad ea quae sunt extra cognoscentem, cognoscimus enim etiam ea quae extra nos sunt. Per materiam autem determinatur forma rei ad aliquid unum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0038","text":"Unde manifestum est quod ratio cognitionis ex opposito se habet ad rationem materialitatis. Et ideo quae non recipiunt formas nisi materialiter, nullo modo sunt cognoscitiva, sicut plantae; ut dicitur in II libro de anima. Quanto autem aliquid immaterialius habet formam rei cognitae, tanto perfectius cognoscit. Unde et intellectus, qui abstrahit speciem non solum a materia, sed etiam a materialibus conditionibus individuantibus, perfectius cognoscit quam sensus, qui accipit formam rei cognitae sine materia quidem, sed cum materialibus conditionibus. Et inter ipsos sensus, visus est magis cognoscitivus, quia est minus materialis, ut supra dictum est. Et inter ipsos intellectus, tanto quilibet est perfectior, quanto immaterialior.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0039","text":"Ex his ergo patet quod, si aliquis intellectus est qui per essentiam suam cognoscit omnia, oportet quod essentia eius habeat in se immaterialiter omnia; sicut antiqui posuerunt essentiam animae actu componi ex principiis omnium materialium, ut cognosceret omnia. Hoc autem est proprium Dei, ut sua essentia sit immaterialiter comprehensiva omnium, prout effectus virtute praeexistunt in causa. Solus igitur Deus per essentiam suam omnia intelligit; non autem anima humana, neque etiam Angelus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0040","text":"[I q. 84 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31962)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0041","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus ibi loquitur de visione imaginaria, quae fit per imagines corporum. Quibus imaginibus formandis dat anima aliquid suae substantiae, sicut subiectum datur ut informetur per aliquam formam. Et sic de seipsa facit huiusmodi imagines, non quod anima vel aliquid animae convertatur, ut sit haec vel illa imago; sed sicut dicitur de corpore fieri aliquid coloratum, prout informatur colore. Et hic sensus apparet ex his quae sequuntur. Dicit enim quod servat aliquid, scilicet non formatum tali imagine, quod libere de specie talium imaginum iudicet, et hoc dicit esse mentem vel intellectum. Partem autem quae informatur huiusmodi imaginibus scilicet imaginativam, dicit esse communem nobis et bestiis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0042","text":"[I q. 84 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31963)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0043","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Aristoteles non posuit animam esse actu compositam ex omnibus, sicut antiqui naturales; sed dixit quodammodo animam esse omnia, inquantum est in potentia ad omnia; per sensum quidem ad sensibilia, per intellectum vero ad intelligibilia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0044","text":"[I q. 84 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31964)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0045","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod quaelibet creatura habet esse finitum et determinatum. Unde essentia superioris creaturae, etsi habeat quandam similitudinem inferioris creaturae prout communicant in aliquo genere, non tamen complete habet similitudinem illius, quia determinatur ad aliquam speciem, praeter quam est species inferioris creaturae. Sed essentia Dei est perfecta similitudo omnium quantum ad omnia quae in rebus inveniuntur, sicut universale principium omnium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0046","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0047","text":"[I q. 84 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31965)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0048","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima intelligat omnia per species sibi naturaliter inditas. Dicit enim Gregorius, in homilia ascensionis quod *homo habet commune cum Angelis intelligere*. Sed Angeli intelligunt omnia per formas naturaliter inditas, unde in libro de causis dicitur quod *omnis intelligentia est plena formis*. Ergo et anima habet species rerum naturaliter inditas, quibus corporalia intelligit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0049","text":"[I q. 84 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31966)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0050","text":"Praeterea, anima intellectiva est nobilior quam materia prima corporalis. Sed materia prima est creata a Deo sub formis ad quas est in potentia. Ergo multo magis anima intellectiva est creata a Deo sub speciebus intelligibilibus. Et sic anima intelligit corporalia per species sibi naturaliter inditas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0051","text":"[I q. 84 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31967)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0052","text":"Praeterea, nullus potest verum respondere nisi de eo quod scit. Sed aliquis etiam idiota, non habens scientiam acquisitam, respondet verum de singulis, si tamen ordinate interrogetur, ut narratur in Menone Platonis de quodam. Ergo antequam aliquis acquirat scientiam, habet rerum cognitionem. Quod non esset nisi anima haberet species naturaliter inditas. Intelligit igitur anima res corporeas per species naturaliter inditas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0053","text":"[I q. 84 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31968)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0054","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in III de anima, de intellectu loquens, quod est sicut tabula in qua nihil est scriptum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0055","text":"[I q. 84 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31969)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0056","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum forma sit principium actionis, oportet ut eo modo se habeat aliquid ad formam quae est actionis principium, quo se habet ad actionem illam, sicut si moveri sursum est ex levitate, oportet quod in potentia tantum sursum fertur, esse leve solum in potentia, quod autem actu sursum fertur, esse leve in actu. Videmus autem quod homo est quandoque cognoscens in potentia tantum, tam secundum sensum quam secundum intellectum. Et de tali potentia in actum reducitur, ut sentiat quidem, per actiones sensibilium in sensum; ut intelligat autem, per disciplinam aut inventionem. Unde oportet dicere quod anima cognoscitiva sit in potentia tam ad similitudines quae sunt principia sentiendi, quam ad similitudines quae sunt principia intelligendi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0057","text":"Et propter hoc Aristoteles posuit quod intellectus, quo anima intelligit, non habet aliquas species naturaliter inditas, sed est in principio in potentia ad huiusmodi species omnes. Sed quia id quod habet actu formam, interdum non potest agere secundum formam propter aliquod impedimentum, sicut leve si impediatur sursum ferri; propter hoc Plato posuit quod intellectus hominis naturaliter est plenus omnibus speciebus intelligibilibus, sed per unionem corporis impeditur ne possit in actum exire. Sed hoc non videtur convenienter dictum. Primo quidem quia, si habet anima naturalem notitiam omnium, non videtur esse possibile quod huius naturalis notitiae tantam oblivionem capiat, quod nesciat se huiusmodi scientiam habere, nullus enim homo obliviscitur ea quae naturaliter cognoscit, sicut quod omne totum sit maius sua parte, et alia huiusmodi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0058","text":"Praecipue autem hoc videtur inconveniens, si ponatur esse animae naturale corpori uniri, ut supra habitum est, inconveniens enim est quod naturalis operatio alicuius rei totaliter impediatur per id quod est sibi secundum naturam. Secundo, manifeste apparet huius positionis falsitas ex hoc quod, deficiente aliquo sensu, deficit scientia eorum, quae apprehenduntur secundum illum sensum; sicut caecus natus nullam potest habere notitiam de coloribus. Quod non esset, si animae essent naturaliter inditae omnium intelligibilium rationes. Et ideo dicendum est quod anima non cognoscit corporalia per species naturaliter inditas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0059","text":"[I q. 84 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31970)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0060","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod homo quidem convenit cum Angelis in intelligendo, deficit tamen ab eminentia intellectus eorum, sicut et corpora inferiora, quae tantum existunt secundum Gregorium, deficiunt ab existentia superiorum corporum. Nam materia inferiorum corporum non est completa totaliter per formam, sed est in potentia ad formas quas non habet, materia autem caelestium corporum est totaliter completa per formam, ita quod non est in potentia ad aliam formam, ut supra habitum est. Et similiter intellectus Angeli est perfectus per species intelligibiles secundum suam naturam, intellectus autem humanus est in potentia ad huiusmodi species.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0061","text":"[I q. 84 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31971)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0062","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod materia prima habet esse substantiale per formam, et ideo oportuit quod crearetur sub aliqua forma, alioquin non esset in actu. Sub una tamen forma existens, est in potentia ad alias. Intellectus autem non habet esse substantiale per speciem intelligibilem; et ideo non est simile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0063","text":"[I q. 84 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31972)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0064","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ordinata interrogatio procedit ex principiis communibus per se notis, ad propria. Per talem autem processum scientia causatur in anima addiscentis. Unde cum verum respondet de his de quibus secundo interrogatur, hoc non est quia prius ea noverit; sed quia tunc ea de novo addiscit. Nihil enim refert utrum ille qui docet, proponendo vel interrogando procedat de principiis communibus ad conclusiones, utrobique enim animus audientis certificatur de posterioribus per priora.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0065","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0066","text":"[I q. 84 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31973)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0067","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod species intelligibiles effluant in animam ab aliquibus formis separatis. Omne enim quod per participationem est tale, causatur ab eo quod est per essentiam tale; sicut quod est ignitum reducitur sicut in causam in ignem. Sed anima intellectiva, secundum quod est actu intelligens, participat ipsa intelligibilia, intellectus enim in actu, quodammodo est intellectum in actu. Ergo ea quae secundum se et per essentiam suam sunt intellecta in actu, sunt causae animae intellectivae quod actu intelligat. Intellecta autem in actu per essentiam suam, sunt formae sine materia existentes. Species igitur intelligibiles quibus anima intelligit, causantur a formis aliquibus separatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0068","text":"[I q. 84 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31974)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0069","text":"Praeterea, intelligibilia se habent ad intellectum, sicut sensibilia ad sensum. Sed sensibilia quae sunt in actu extra animam, sunt causae specierum sensibilium quae sunt in sensu, quibus sentimus. Ergo species intelligibiles quibus intellectus noster intelligit, causantur ab aliquibus actu intelligibilibus extra animam existentibus. Huiusmodi autem non sunt nisi formae a materia separatae. Formae igitur intelligibiles intellectus nostri effluunt ab aliquibus substantiis separatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0070","text":"[I q. 84 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31975)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0071","text":"Praeterea, omne quod est in potentia, reducitur in actum per id quod est actu. Si ergo intellectus noster, prius in potentia existens, postmodum actu intelligat, oportet quod hoc causetur ab aliquo intellectu qui semper est in actu. Hic autem est intellectus separatus. Ergo ab aliquibus substantiis separatis causantur species intelligibiles quibus actu intelligimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0072","text":"[I q. 84 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31976)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0073","text":"Sed contra est quia secundum hoc sensibus non indigeremus ad intelligendum. Quod patet esse falsum ex hoc praecipue quod qui caret uno sensu, nullo modo potest habere scientiam de sensibilibus illius sensus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0074","text":"[I q. 84 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31977)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0075","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam posuerunt species intelligibiles nostri intellectus procedere ab aliquibus formis vel substantiis separatis. Et hoc dupliciter. Plato enim, sicut dictum est, posuit formas rerum sensibilium per se sine materia subsistentes; sicut formam hominis, quam nominabat per se hominem, et formam vel ideam equi, quam nominabat per se equum, et sic de aliis. Has ergo formas separatas ponebat participari et ab anima nostra, et a materia corporali; ab anima quidem nostra ad cognoscendum, a materia vero corporali ad essendum; ut sicut materia corporalis per hoc quod participat ideam lapidis, fit hic lapis, ita intellectus noster per hoc quod participat ideam lapidis, fit intelligens lapidem. Participatio autem ideae fit per aliquam similitudinem ipsius ideae in participante ipsam, per modum quo exemplar participatur ab exemplato.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0076","text":"Sicut igitur ponebat formas sensibiles quae sunt in materia corporali, effluere ab ideis sicut quasdam earum similitudines; ita ponebat species intelligibiles nostri intellectus esse similitudines quasdam idearum ab eis effluentes. Et propter hoc, ut supra dictum est, scientias et definitiones ad ideas referebat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0077","text":"Sed quia contra rationem rerum sensibilium est quod earum formae subsistant absque materiis, ut Aristoteles multipliciter probat; ideo Avicenna, hac positione remota, posuit omnium rerum sensibilium intelligibiles species, non quidem per se subsistere absque materia, sed praeexistere immaterialiter in intellectibus separatis; a quorum primo derivantur huiusmodi species in sequentem, et sic de aliis usque ad ultimum intellectum separatum, quem nominat intellectum agentem; a quo, ut ipse dicit, effluunt species intelligibiles in animas nostras, et formae sensibiles in materiam corporalem. Et sic in hoc Avicenna cum Platone concordat, quod species intelligibiles nostri intellectus effluunt a quibusdam formis separatis, quas tamen Plato dicit per se subsistere, Avicenna vero ponit eas in intelligentia agente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0078","text":"Differunt etiam quantum ad hoc, quod Avicenna ponit species intelligibiles non remanere in intellectu nostro postquam desinit actu intelligere; sed indiget ut iterato se convertat ad recipiendum de novo. Unde non ponit scientiam animae naturaliter inditam, sicut Plato, qui ponit participationes idearum immobiliter in anima permanere. Sed secundum hanc positionem sufficiens ratio assignari non posset quare anima nostra corpori uniretur. Non enim potest dici quod anima intellectiva corpori uniatur propter corpus, quia nec forma est propter materiam, nec motor propter mobile, sed potius e converso. Maxime autem videtur corpus esse necessarium animae intellectivae ad eius propriam operationem, quae est intelligere, quia secundum esse suum a corpore non dependet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0079","text":"Si autem anima species intelligibiles secundum suam naturam apta nata esset recipere per influentiam aliquorum separatorum principiorum tantum, et non acciperet eas ex sensibus, non indigeret corpore ad intelligendum, unde frustra corpori uniretur. Si autem dicatur quod indiget anima nostra sensibus ad intelligendum, quibus quodammodo excitetur ad consideranda ea quorum species intelligibiles a principiis separatis recipit; hoc non videtur sufficere. Quia huiusmodi excitatio non videtur necessaria animae nisi inquantum est consopita, secundum Platonicos, quodammodo et obliviosa propter unionem ad corpus, et sic sensus non proficerent animae intellectivae nisi ad tollendum impedimentum quod animae provenit ex corporis unione. Remanet igitur quaerendum quae sit causa unionis animae ad corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0080","text":"Si autem dicatur, secundum Avicennam, quod sensus sunt animae necessarii, quia per eos excitatur ut convertat se ad intelligentiam agentem, a qua recipit species; hoc quidem non sufficit. Quia si in natura animae est ut intelligat per species ab intelligentia agente effluxas, sequeretur quod quandoque anima possit se convertere ad intelligentiam agentem ex inclinatione suae naturae, vel etiam excitata per alium sensum, ut convertat se ad intelligentiam agentem ad recipiendum species sensibilium quorum sensum aliquis non habet. Et sic caecus natus posset habere scientiam de coloribus, quod est manifeste falsum. Unde dicendum est quod species intelligibiles quibus anima nostra intelligit, non effluunt a formis separatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0081","text":"[I q. 84 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31978)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0082","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod species intelligibiles quas participat noster intellectus, reducuntur sicut in primam causam in aliquod principium per suam essentiam intelligibile, scilicet in Deum. Sed ab illo principio procedunt mediantibus formis rerum sensibilium et materialium, a quibus scientiam colligimus, ut Dionysius dicit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0083","text":"[I q. 84 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31979)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0084","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod res materiales, secundum esse quod habent extra animam, possunt esse sensibiles actu; non autem actu intelligibiles. Unde non est simile de sensu et intellectu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0085","text":"[I q. 84 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31980)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0086","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod intellectus noster possibilis reducitur de potentia ad actum per aliquod ens actu, idest per intellectum agentem, qui est virtus quaedam animae nostrae, ut dictum est, non autem per aliquem intellectum separatum, sicut per causam proximam; sed forte sicut per causam remotam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0087","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0088","text":"[I q. 84 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31981)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0089","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima intellectiva non cognoscat res materiales in rationibus aeternis. Id enim in quo aliquid cognoscitur, ipsum magis et per prius cognoscitur. Sed anima intellectiva hominis, in statu praesentis vitae, non cognoscit rationes aeternas, quia non cognoscit ipsum Deum, in quo rationes aeternae existunt, sed ei sicut ignoto coniungitur, ut Dionysius dicit in I cap. mysticae theologiae. Ergo anima non cognoscit omnia in rationibus aeternis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0090","text":"[I q. 84 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31982)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0091","text":"Praeterea, Rom. I, dicitur quod *invisibilia Dei per ea quae facta sunt, conspiciuntur*. Sed inter invisibilia Dei numerantur rationes aeternae. Ergo rationes aeternae per creaturas materiales cognoscuntur, et non e converso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0092","text":"[I q. 84 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31983)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0093","text":"Praeterea, rationes aeternae nihil aliud sunt quam ideae, dicit enim Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod *ideae sunt rationes stabiles rerum in mente divina existentes*. Si ergo dicatur quod anima intellectiva cognoscit omnia in rationibus aeternis, redibit opinio Platonis, qui posuit omnem scientiam ab ideis derivari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0094","text":"[I q. 84 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31984)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0095","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, XII Confess., *si ambo videmus verum esse quod dicis, et ambo videmus verum esse quod dico, ubi quaeso id videmus? Nec ego utique in te, nec tu in me sed ambo in ipsa, quae supra mentes nostras est, incommutabili veritate*. Veritas autem incommutabilis in aeternis rationibus continetur. Ergo anima intellectiva omnia vera cognoscit in rationibus aeternis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0096","text":"[I q. 84 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31985)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0097","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit in II de Doctr. Christ., *philosophi qui vocantur, si qua forte vera et fidei nostrae accommoda dixerunt, ab eis tanquam ab iniustis possessoribus in usum nostrum vindicanda sunt. Habent enim doctrinae gentilium quaedam simulata et superstitiosa figmenta, quae unusquisque nostrum de societate gentilium exiens, debet evitare*. Et ideo Augustinus, qui doctrinis Platonicorum imbutus fuerat, si qua invenit fidei accommoda in eorum dictis, assumpsit; quae vero invenit fidei nostrae adversa, in melius commutavit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0098","text":"Posuit autem Plato, sicut supra dictum est, formas rerum per se subsistere a materia separatas, quas ideas vocabat, per quarum participationem dicebat intellectum nostrum omnia cognoscere; ut sicut materia corporalis per participationem ideae lapidis fit lapis, ita intellectus noster per participationem eiusdem ideae cognosceret lapidem. Sed quia videtur esse alienum a fide quod formae rerum extra res per se subsistant absque materia, sicut Platonici posuerunt, dicentes per se vitam aut per se sapientiam esse quasdam substantias creatrices, ut Dionysius dicit XI cap. de Div. Nom.; ideo Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., posuit loco harum idearum quas Plato ponebat, rationes omnium creaturarum in mente divina existere, secundum quas omnia formantur, et secundum quas etiam anima humana omnia cognoscit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0099","text":"Cum ergo quaeritur utrum anima humana in rationibus aeternis omnia cognoscat, dicendum est quod aliquid in aliquo dicitur cognosci dupliciter. Uno modo, sicut in obiecto cognito; sicut aliquis videt in speculo ea quorum imagines in speculo resultant. Et hoc modo anima, in statu praesentis vitae, non potest videre omnia in rationibus aeternis; sed sic in rationibus aeternis cognoscunt omnia beati, qui Deum vident et omnia in ipso. Alio modo dicitur aliquid cognosci in aliquo sicut in cognitionis principio; sicut si dicamus quod in sole videntur ea quae videntur per solem. Et sic necesse est dicere quod anima humana omnia cognoscat in rationibus aeternis, per quarum participationem omnia cognoscimus. Ipsum enim lumen intellectuale quod est in nobis, nihil est aliud quam quaedam participata similitudo luminis increati, in quo continentur rationes aeternae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0100","text":"Unde in Psalmo IV, dicitur, *multi dicunt, quis ostendit nobis bona?* Cui quaestioni Psalmista respondet, dicens, *signatum est super nos lumen vultus tui, domine*. Quasi dicat, per ipsam sigillationem divini luminis in nobis, omnia nobis demonstrantur. Quia tamen praeter lumen intellectuale in nobis, exiguntur species intelligibiles a rebus acceptae, ad scientiam de rebus materialibus habendam; ideo non per solam participationem rationum aeternarum de rebus materialibus notitiam habemus, sicut Platonici posuerunt quod sola idearum participatio sufficit ad scientiam habendam. Unde Augustinus dicit, in IV de Trin., *numquid quia philosophi documentis certissimis persuadent aeternis rationibus omnia temporalia fieri, propterea potuerunt in ipsis rationibus perspicere, vel ex ipsis colligere quot sint animalium genera, quae semina singulorum?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0101","text":"Nonne ista omnia per locorum ac temporum historiam quaesierunt?* Quod autem Augustinus non sic intellexerit omnia cognosci in rationibus aeternis, vel in incommutabili veritate, quasi ipsae rationes aeternae videantur, patet per hoc quod ipse dicit in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod *rationalis anima non omnis et quaelibet, sed quae sancta et pura fuerit, asseritur illi visioni*, scilicet rationum aeternarum, esse idonea; sicut sunt animae beatorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0102","text":"[I q. 84 a. 5 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31986)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0103","text":"Et per haec patet responsio ad obiecta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0104","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0105","text":"[I q. 84 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31987)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0106","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectiva cognitio non accipiatur a rebus sensibilibus. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod *non est expectanda sinceritas veritatis a corporis sensibus*. Et hoc probat dupliciter. Uno modo, per hoc quod *omne quod corporeus sensus attingit, sine ulla intermissione temporis commutatur, quod autem non manet, percipi non potest*. Alio modo, per hoc quod *omnia quae per corpus sentimus, etiam cum non adsunt sensibus, imagines tamen eorum patimur, ut in somno vel furore; non autem sensibus discernere valemus utrum ipsa sensibilia, vel imagines eorum falsas sentiamus. Nihil autem percipi potest quod a falso non discernitur*. Et sic concludit quod non est expectanda veritas a sensibus. Sed cognitio intellectualis est apprehensiva veritatis. Non ergo cognitio intellectualis est expectanda a sensibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0107","text":"[I q. 84 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31988)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0108","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, XII super Gen. ad Litt., *non est putandum facere aliquid corpus in spiritum, tanquam spiritus corpori facienti materiae vice subdatur, omni enim modo praestantior est qui facit, ea re de qua aliquid facit*. Unde concludit quod *imaginem corporis non corpus in spiritu, sed ipse spiritus in seipso facit*. Non ergo intellectualis cognitio a sensibilibus derivatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0109","text":"[I q. 84 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31989)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0110","text":"Praeterea, effectus non se extendit ultra virtutem suae causae. Sed intellectualis cognitio se extendit ultra sensibilia, intelligimus enim quaedam quae sensu percipi non possunt. Intellectualis ergo cognitio non derivatur a rebus sensibilibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0111","text":"[I q. 84 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31990)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0112","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus probat, I Metaphys., et in fine Poster., quod principium nostrae cognitionis est a sensu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0113","text":"[I q. 84 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31991)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0114","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa istam quaestionem triplex fuit philosophorum opinio. Democritus enim posuit quod *nulla est alia causa cuiuslibet nostrae cognitionis, nisi cum ab his corporibus quae cogitamus, veniunt atque intrant imagines in animas nostras*; ut Augustinus dicit in epistola sua ad Dioscorum. Et Aristoteles etiam dicit, in libro de Somn. et Vigil., quod Democritus posuit cognitionem fieri per idola et defluxiones. Et huius positionis ratio fuit, quia tam ipse Democritus quam alii antiqui naturales non ponebant intellectum differre a sensu, ut Aristoteles dicit in libro de anima. Et ideo, quia sensus immutatur a sensibili, arbitrabantur omnem nostram cognitionem fieri per solam immutationem a sensibilibus. Quam quidem immutationem Democritus asserebat fieri per imaginum defluxiones.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0115","text":"Plato vero e contrario posuit intellectum differre a sensu; et intellectum quidem esse virtutem immaterialem organo corporeo non utentem in suo actu. Et quia incorporeum non potest immutari a corporeo, posuit quod cognitio intellectualis non fit per immutationem intellectus a sensibilibus, sed per participationem formarum intelligibilium separatarum, ut dictum est. Sensum etiam posuit virtutem quandam per se operantem. Unde nec ipse sensus, cum sit quaedam vis spiritualis, immutatur a sensibilibus, sed organa sensuum a sensibilibus immutantur, ex qua immutatione anima quodammodo excitatur ut in se species sensibilium formet. Et hanc opinionem tangere videtur Augustinus, XII super Gen. ad Litt., ubi dicit quod *corpus non sentit, sed anima per corpus, quo velut nuntio utitur ad formandum in seipsa quod extrinsecus nuntiatur*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0116","text":"Sic igitur secundum Platonis opinionem, neque intellectualis cognitio a sensibili procedit, neque etiam sensibilis totaliter a sensibilibus rebus; sed sensibilia excitant animam sensibilem ad sentiendum, et similiter sensus excitant animam intellectivam ad intelligendum. Aristoteles autem media via processit. Posuit enim cum Platone intellectum differre a sensu. Sed sensum posuit propriam operationem non habere sine communicatione corporis; ita quod sentire non sit actus animae tantum, sed coniuncti. Et similiter posuit de omnibus operationibus sensitivae partis. Quia igitur non est inconveniens quod sensibilia quae sunt extra animam, causent aliquid in coniunctum, in hoc Aristoteles cum Democrito concordavit, quod operationes sensitivae partis causentur per impressionem sensibilium in sensum, non per modum defluxionis, ut Democritus posuit, sed per quandam operationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0117","text":"Nam et Democritus omnem actionem fieri posuit per influxionem atomorum, ut patet in I de Generat. Intellectum vero posuit Aristoteles habere operationem absque communicatione corporis. Nihil autem corporeum imprimere potest in rem incorpoream. Et ideo ad causandam intellectualem operationem, secundum Aristotelem, non sufficit sola impressio sensibilium corporum, sed requiritur aliquid nobilius, quia agens est honorabilius patiente, ut ipse dicit. Non tamen ita quod intellectualis operatio causetur in nobis ex sola impressione aliquarum rerum superiorum, ut Plato posuit, sed illud superius et nobilius agens quod vocat intellectum agentem, de quo iam supra diximus, facit phantasmata a sensibus accepta intelligibilia in actu, per modum abstractionis cuiusdam. Secundum hoc ergo, ex parte phantasmatum intellectualis operatio a sensu causatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0118","text":"Sed quia phantasmata non sufficiunt immutare intellectum possibilem, sed oportet quod fiant intelligibilia actu per intellectum agentem; non potest dici quod sensibilis cognitio sit totalis et perfecta causa intellectualis cognitionis, sed magis quodammodo est materia causae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0119","text":"[I q. 84 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31992)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0120","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod per verba illa Augustini datur intelligi quod veritas non sit totaliter a sensibus expectanda. Requiritur enim lumen intellectus agentis, per quod immutabiliter veritatem in rebus mutabilibus cognoscamus, et discernamus ipsas res a similitudinibus rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0121","text":"[I q. 84 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31993)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0122","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Augustinus ibi non loquitur de intellectuali cognitione, sed de imaginaria. Et quia, secundum Platonis opinionem, vis imaginaria habet operationem quae est animae solius; eadem ratione usus est Augustinus ad ostendendum quod corpora non imprimunt suas similitudines in vim imaginariam, sed hoc facit ipsa anima, qua utitur Aristoteles ad probandum intellectum agentem esse aliquid separatum, quia scilicet agens est honorabilius patiente. Et procul dubio oportet, secundum hanc positionem, in vi imaginativa ponere non solum potentiam passivam, sed etiam activam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0123","text":"Sed si ponamus, secundum opinionem Aristotelis, quod actio virtutis imaginativae sit coniuncti, nulla sequitur difficultas, quia corpus sensibile est nobilius organo animalis, secundum hoc quod comparatur ad ipsum ut ens in actu ad ens in potentia, sicut coloratum in actu ad pupillam, quae colorata est in potentia. Posset tamen dici quod, quamvis prima immutatio virtutis imaginariae sit per motum sensibilium, quia phantasia est motus factus secundum sensum, ut dicitur in libro de anima; tamen est quaedam operatio animae in homine quae dividendo et componendo format diversas rerum imagines, etiam quae non sunt a sensibus acceptae. Et quantum ad hoc possunt accipi verba Augustini.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0124","text":"[I q. 84 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31994)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0125","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod sensitiva cognitio non est tota causa intellectualis cognitionis. Et ideo non est mirum si intellectualis cognitio ultra sensitivam se extendit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0126","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0127","text":"[I q. 84 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31995)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0128","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus possit actu intelligere per species intelligibiles quas penes se habet, non convertendo se ad phantasmata. Intellectus enim fit in actu per speciem intelligibilem qua informatur. Sed intellectum esse in actu, est ipsum intelligere. Ergo species intelligibiles sufficiunt ad hoc quod intellectus actu intelligat, absque hoc quod ad phantasmata se convertat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0129","text":"[I q. 84 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31996)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0130","text":"Praeterea, magis dependet imaginatio a sensu, quam intellectus ab imaginatione. Sed imaginatio potest imaginari actu, absentibus sensibilibus. Ergo multo magis intellectus potest intelligere actu, non convertendo se ad phantasmata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0131","text":"[I q. 84 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31997)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0132","text":"Praeterea, incorporalium non sunt aliqua phantasmata, quia imaginatio tempus et continuum non transcendit. Si ergo intellectus noster non posset aliquid intelligere in actu nisi converteretur ad phantasmata, sequeretur quod non posset intelligere incorporeum aliquid. Quod patet esse falsum, intelligimus enim veritatem ipsam, et Deum et Angelos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0133","text":"[I q. 84 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31998)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0134","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod *nihil sine phantasmate intelligit anima*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0135","text":"[I q. 84 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31999)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0136","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod impossibile est intellectum nostrum, secundum praesentis vitae statum, quo passibili corpori coniungitur, aliquid intelligere in actu, nisi convertendo se ad phantasmata. Et hoc duobus indiciis apparet. Primo quidem quia, cum intellectus sit vis quaedam non utens corporali organo, nullo modo impediretur in suo actu per laesionem alicuius corporalis organi, si non requireretur ad eius actum actus alicuius potentiae utentis organo corporali. Utuntur autem organo corporali sensus et imaginatio et aliae vires pertinentes ad partem sensitivam. Unde manifestum est quod ad hoc quod intellectus actu intelligat, non solum accipiendo scientiam de novo, sed etiam utendo scientia iam acquisita, requiritur actus imaginationis et ceterarum virtutum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0137","text":"Videmus enim quod, impedito actu virtutis imaginativae per laesionem organi, ut in phreneticis; et similiter impedito actu memorativae virtutis, ut in lethargicis; impeditur homo ab intelligendo in actu etiam ea quorum scientiam praeaccepit. Secundo, quia hoc quilibet in seipso experiri potest, quod quando aliquis conatur aliquid intelligere, format aliqua phantasmata sibi per modum exemplorum, in quibus quasi inspiciat quod intelligere studet. Et inde est etiam quod quando alium volumus facere aliquid intelligere, proponimus ei exempla, ex quibus sibi phantasmata formare possit ad intelligendum. Huius autem ratio est, quia potentia cognoscitiva proportionatur cognoscibili. Unde intellectus angelici, qui est totaliter a corpore separatus, obiectum proprium est substantia intelligibilis a corpore separata; et per huiusmodi intelligibilia materialia cognoscit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0138","text":"Intellectus autem humani, qui est coniunctus corpori, proprium obiectum est quidditas sive natura in materia corporali existens; et per huiusmodi naturas visibilium rerum etiam in invisibilium rerum aliqualem cognitionem ascendit. De ratione autem huius naturae est, quod in aliquo individuo existat, quod non est absque materia corporali, sicut de ratione naturae lapidis est quod sit in hoc lapide, et de ratione naturae equi quod sit in hoc equo, et sic de aliis. Unde natura lapidis, vel cuiuscumque materialis rei, cognosci non potest complete et vere, nisi secundum quod cognoscitur ut in particulari existens. Particulare autem apprehendimus per sensum et imaginationem. Et ideo necesse est ad hoc quod intellectus actu intelligat suum obiectum proprium, quod convertat se ad phantasmata, ut speculetur naturam universalem in particulari existentem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0139","text":"Si autem proprium obiectum intellectus nostri esset forma separata; vel si naturae rerum sensibilium subsisterent non in particularibus, secundum Platonicos; non oporteret quod intellectus noster semper intelligendo converteret se ad phantasmata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0140","text":"[I q. 84 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32000)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0141","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod species conservatae in intellectu possibili, in eo existunt habitualiter quando actu non intelligit, sicut supra dictum est. Unde ad hoc quod intelligamus in actu, non sufficit ipsa conservatio specierum; sed oportet quod eis utamur secundum quod convenit rebus quarum sunt species, quae sunt naturae in particularibus existentes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0142","text":"[I q. 84 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32001)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0143","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod etiam ipsum phantasma est similitudo rei particularis, unde non indiget imaginatio aliqua alia similitudine particularis, sicut indiget intellectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0144","text":"[I q. 84 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32002)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0145","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod incorporea, quorum non sunt phantasmata, cognoscuntur a nobis per comparationem ad corpora sensibilia, quorum sunt phantasmata. Sicut veritatem intelligimus ex consideratione rei circa quam veritatem speculamur; Deum autem, ut Dionysius dicit, cognoscimus ut causam, et per excessum, et per remotionem; alias etiam incorporeas substantias, in statu praesentis vitae, cognoscere non possumus nisi per remotionem, vel aliquam comparationem ad corporalia. Et ideo cum de huiusmodi aliquid intelligimus, necesse habemus converti ad phantasmata corporum, licet ipsorum non sint phantasmata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0146","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0147","text":"[I q. 84 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32003)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0148","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod iudicium intellectus non impediatur per ligamentum sensus. Superius enim non dependet ab inferiori. Sed iudicium intellectus est supra sensum. Ergo iudicium intellectus non impeditur per ligamentum sensus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0149","text":"[I q. 84 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32004)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0150","text":"Praeterea, syllogizare est actus intellectus. In somno autem ligatur sensus, ut dicitur in libro de Somn. et Vig.; contingit tamen quandoque quod aliquis dormiens syllogizat. Ergo non impeditur iudicium intellectus per ligamentum sensus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0151","text":"[I q. 84 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32005)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0152","text":"Sed contra est quod in dormiendo ea quae contra licitos mores contingunt, non imputantur ad peccatum; ut Augustinus in XII super Gen. ad Litt. dicit. Hoc autem non esset si homo in dormiendo liberum usum rationis et intellectus haberet. Ergo impeditur rationis usus per ligamentum sensus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0153","text":"[I q. 84 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32006)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0154","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, proprium obiectum intellectui nostro proportionatum est natura rei sensibilis. Iudicium autem perfectum de re aliqua dari non potest, nisi ea omnia quae ad rem pertinent cognoscantur, et praecipue si ignoretur id quod est terminus et finis iudicii. Dicit autem philosophus, in III de caelo, quod *sicut finis factivae scientiae est opus, ita naturalis scientiae finis est quod videtur principaliter secundum sensum*, faber enim non quaerit cognitionem cultelli nisi propter opus, ut operetur hunc particularem cultellum; et similiter naturalis non quaerit cognoscere naturam lapidis et equi, nisi ut sciat rationes eorum quae videntur secundum sensum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0155","text":"Manifestum est autem quod non posset esse perfectum iudicium fabri de cultello, si opus ignoraret, et similiter non potest esse perfectum iudicium scientiae naturalis de rebus naturalibus, si sensibilia ignorentur. Omnia autem quae in praesenti statu intelligimus, cognoscuntur a nobis per comparationem ad res sensibiles naturales. Unde impossibile est quod sit in nobis iudicium intellectus perfectum, cum ligamento sensus, per quem res sensibiles cognoscimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0156","text":"[I q. 84 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32007)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0157","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, quamvis intellectus sit superior sensu, accipit tamen aliquo modo a sensu, et eius obiecta prima et principalia in sensibilibus fundantur. Et ideo necesse est quod impediatur iudicium intellectus ex ligamento sensus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0158","text":"[I q. 84 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32008)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0159","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod sensus ligatur in dormientibus propter evaporationes quasdam et fumositates resolutas, ut dicitur in libro de Somn. et Vig. Et ideo secundum dispositionem huiusmodi evaporationum, contingit esse ligamentum sensus maius vel minus. Quando enim multus fuerit motus vaporum, ligatur non solum sensus, sed etiam imaginatio, ita ut nulla appareant phantasmata; sicut praecipue accidit cum aliquis incipit dormire post multum cibum et potum. Si vero motus vaporum aliquantulum fuerit remissior, apparent phantasmata, sed distorta et inordinata; sicut accidit in febricitantibus. Si vero adhuc magis motus sedetur, apparent phantasmata ordinata; sicut maxime solet contingere in fine dormitionis, et in hominibus sobriis et habentibus fortem imaginationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0160","text":"Si autem motus vaporum fuerit modicus, non solum imaginatio remanet libera, sed etiam ipse sensus communis ex parte solvitur; ita quod homo iudicat interdum in dormiendo ea quae videt somnia esse, quasi diiudicans inter res et rerum similitudines. Sed tamen ex aliqua parte remanet sensus communis ligatus; et ideo, licet aliquas similitudines discernat a rebus, tamen semper in aliquibus decipitur. Sic igitur per modum quo sensus solvitur et imaginatio in dormiendo, liberatur et iudicium intellectus, non tamen ex toto. Unde illi qui dormiendo syllogizant, cum excitantur, semper recognoscunt se in aliquo defecisse.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"01b3cadabcee6cea3e668f555331af4932301488ec6c39be27b5b6197e400eeb","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T151620285848Z_ad8b4835","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 84","source_sha256":"01b3cadabcee6cea3e668f555331af4932301488ec6c39be27b5b6197e400eeb","sha256":"2f023a7bff09a9a193c22e858045a0407106a55a9737a008c63b9bf817ac9e95","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:16:20.285897+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0002","text":"[I q. 84 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31948)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0003","text":"Consequently, we must consider the acts of the soul with respect to the intellectual and appetitive powers; for the other powers of the soul do not pertain directly to the theologian's consideration. Now the acts of the appetitive part pertain to the consideration of moral science, and therefore they will be treated in the second part of this work, in which moral matter is to be considered. But now the acts of the intellectual part will be treated. In considering the acts, moreover, we shall proceed in this way: first, it must be considered how the soul understands when conjoined to the body; second, how it understands when separated from the body. The first consideration, however, will be threefold: first, it will be considered how the soul understands corporeal things, which are beneath it; second, how it understands itself and those things which are in it; third, how it understands immaterial substances, which are above it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0004","text":"Concerning the cognition of corporeal things, however, three matters present themselves for consideration: first, by what it knows them; second, how and in what order; third, what it knows in them. Concerning the first, eight questions are asked. First, whether the soul knows bodies through the intellect. Second, whether it understands them through its essence, or through certain species. Third, if through certain species, whether the species of all intelligible things are naturally innate in it. Fourth, whether they flow into it from certain separate immaterial forms. Fifth, whether our soul sees in the eternal reasons all that it understands. Sixth, whether it acquires intelligible cognition from sense. Seventh, whether the intellect can actually understand through the intelligible species which it possesses, without turning itself toward phantasms. Eighth, whether the judgment of the intellect is impeded by an impediment of the sensitive powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0005","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0006","text":"[I q. 84 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31949)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0007","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the soul does not know bodies through the intellect. For Augustine says, in *Soliloquies* II, that *bodies cannot be comprehended by the intellect; nor can anything corporeal be seen except by the senses*. He also says, in *On Genesis, to the Letter* XII, that intellectual vision is of those things which are in the soul through their essence. But bodies are not of this sort. Therefore the soul cannot know bodies through the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0008","text":"[I q. 84 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31950)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0009","text":"Further, as sense is related to intelligible things, so intellect is related to sensible things. But the soul can in no way know spiritual things, which are intelligible, through sense. Therefore it can in no way know bodies, which are sensible, through the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0010","text":"[I q. 84 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31951)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0011","text":"Further, the intellect is of things necessary and always disposed in the same way. But all bodies are mobile and are not disposed in the same way. Therefore the soul cannot know bodies through the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0012","text":"[I q. 84 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31952)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0013","text":"But on the contrary, science is in the intellect. Therefore, if the intellect does not know bodies, it follows that there is no science concerning bodies. And thus natural science, which is concerned with mobile body, will perish.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0014","text":"[I q. 84 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31953)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0015","text":"I answer that it must be said that, for the clarification of this question, the first philosophers who investigated the natures of things thought that nothing existed in the world besides body. And because they saw that all bodies were mobile, and thought that they were in continuous flux, they judged that no certainty concerning the truth of things could be had by us. For that which is in continuous flux cannot be apprehended with certainty, because it slips away before it is judged by the mind; as Heraclitus said, that *it is not possible to touch twice the water of a running river*, as the Philosopher relates in *Metaphysics* IV.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0016","text":"But Plato, succeeding these, in order that certain cognition of truth might be preserved as possessed by us through the intellect, posited, besides these corporeal things, another genus of beings separated from matter and motion, which he called species or ideas; by participation in which each of these singular and sensible things is said to be either a man or a horse or something of this kind. Thus, therefore, he said that sciences and definitions and whatever pertains to the act of the intellect are referred not to these sensible bodies, but to those immaterial and separate things; so that the soul does not understand these corporeal things, but understands the separate species of these corporeal things. But this appears false in two ways. First, indeed, because, since those species are immaterial and immobile, cognition of motion and matter—which is proper to natural science—and demonstration through moving and material causes would be excluded from the sciences.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0017","text":"Second, because it seems ridiculous that, while we seek knowledge of things manifest to us, we should introduce other beings, which cannot be their substances, since they differ from them in being; and thus, once those separate substances were known, we would not on that account be able to judge concerning these sensible things. But Plato seems to have departed from the truth in this, that, since he judged every cognition to occur by way of some likeness, he believed that the form of the known thing is necessarily in the knower in the manner in which it is in the known thing. But he considered that the form of the thing understood is in the intellect universally, immaterially, and immovably, which is apparent from the very operation of the intellect, which understands universally and by way of a certain necessity; for the mode of action is according to the mode of the agent's form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0018","text":"And therefore he judged that it was necessary for things understood to subsist in themselves in this way, namely, immaterially and immovably. But this is not necessary. For even in sensible things themselves we see that a form is in one sensible thing in one way and in another in another way: for example, when whiteness is more intense in one, more diminished in another, and in one there is whiteness with sweetness, in another without sweetness. And in this way also a sensible form is in one way in a thing outside the soul, and in another way in sense, which receives the forms of sensible things without matter, as the color of gold without gold. And similarly the intellect receives immaterially and immovably the species of bodies, which are material and mobile, according to its own mode; for what is received is in the receiver according to the mode of the receiver. Therefore it must be said that the soul knows bodies through the intellect by an immaterial, universal, and necessary cognition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0019","text":"[I q. 84 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31954)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0020","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Augustine's statement is to be understood with respect to those things by which the intellect knows, not with respect to those things which it knows. For it knows bodies by understanding, but not through bodies, nor through material and corporeal likenesses; but through immaterial and intelligible species, which can be in the soul through their essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0021","text":"[I q. 84 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31955)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0022","text":"To the second, it must be said that, as Augustine says in *City of God* XXII, it must not be said that, just as sense knows only corporeal things, so intellect knows only spiritual things, for it would follow that God and angels do not know corporeal things. But the reason for this difference is that a lower power does not extend to those things which belong to a higher power; but a higher power operates upon those things which belong to a lower power in a more excellent way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0023","text":"[I q. 84 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31956)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0024","text":"To the third, it must be said that every motion presupposes something immobile: for when a change occurs according to quality, the substance remains immobile; and when the substantial form is changed, matter remains immobile. Even of mutable things there are immutable relations: thus, although Socrates does not always sit, nevertheless it is immutably true that, whenever he sits, he remains in one place. And for this reason nothing prevents us from having immutable science concerning mobile things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0025","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0026","text":"[I q. 84 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31957)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0027","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the soul understands bodily things through its essence. For Augustine says, in X *On the Trinity*, that *the soul enfolds and seizes images of bodies, made within itself from itself; for it gives them, in order to be formed, something of its own substance*. But it understands bodies through likenesses of bodies. Therefore, through its essence, which it gives for the forming of such likenesses, and from which it forms them, it knows bodily things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0028","text":"[I q. 84 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31958)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0029","text":"Further, the Philosopher says, in III *On the Soul*, that *the soul is in a certain manner all things*. Therefore, since like is known by like, it seems that the soul knows bodily things through itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0030","text":"[I q. 84 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31959)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0031","text":"Further, the soul is superior to bodily creatures. But lower things are in higher things in a more eminent mode than in themselves, as Dionysius says. Therefore all bodily creatures exist in a more noble mode in the very substance of the soul than in themselves. Therefore through its substance it can know bodily creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0032","text":"[I q. 84 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31960)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0033","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in IX *On the Trinity*, that *the mind gathers knowledge of bodily things through the senses of the body*. But the soul itself is not knowable through the senses of the body. Therefore it does not know bodily things through its substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0034","text":"[I q. 84 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31961)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0035","text":"I answer that it must be said that the ancient philosophers held that the soul knows bodies through its essence. For this was commonly implanted in the minds of all: that like is known by like. Yet they supposed that the form of the thing known is in the knower in the mode in which it is in the thing known. The Platonists, however, held the contrary. For Plato, since he perceived that the intellectual soul is immaterial and knows immaterially, held that the forms of known things subsist immaterially. But the earlier natural philosophers, because they considered known things to be bodily and material, held that known things must also be in the knowing soul materially. And therefore, in order to attribute knowledge of all things to the soul, they held that it has a nature common with all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0036","text":"And because the nature of things proceeding from principles is constituted from principles, they attributed to the soul the nature of a principle: so that he who said that fire is the principle of all things held the soul to be of the nature of fire; and likewise concerning air and water. Empedocles, however, who held that there are four material elements and two moving principles, likewise said that the soul is constituted from these. And so, since they posited things materially in the soul, they held all knowledge of the soul to be material, failing to distinguish between intellect and sense. But this opinion is disproved. First, indeed, because in the material principle of which they spoke, the things proceeding from it do not exist except potentially. But a thing is not known insofar as it is in potency, but only insofar as it is in act, as is clear in IX *Metaphysics*; whence not even potency itself is known except through act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0037","text":"Thus, therefore, it would not suffice to attribute to the soul the nature of principles in order that it might know all things, unless the natures and forms of individual effects, for example of bone and flesh and other such things, were present in it; as Aristotle argues against Empedocles in I *On the Soul*. Second, because if the thing known had to exist materially in the knower, there would be no reason why things that subsist materially outside the soul should lack knowledge; for example, if the soul knows fire by fire, then the fire that is outside the soul would also know fire. It remains, therefore, that material things known must exist in the knower not materially, but rather immaterially. And the reason for this is that the act of knowledge extends to things that are outside the knower; for we know even those things that are outside us. But through matter the form of a thing is determined to some one thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0038","text":"Hence it is manifest that the nature of knowledge is opposed to the nature of materiality. And therefore things that receive forms only materially are in no way cognitive, as plants; as is said in the second book *On the Soul*. But the more immaterially something has the form of the thing known, the more perfectly it knows. Hence also the intellect, which abstracts the species not only from matter but also from individuating material conditions, knows more perfectly than sense, which receives the form of the thing known indeed without matter, but with material conditions. And among the senses themselves, sight is more cognitive, because it is less material, as was said above. And among intellects themselves, each is more perfect insofar as it is more immaterial.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0039","text":"From these things, therefore, it is clear that if there is some intellect that knows all things through its essence, its essence must have all things in itself immaterially; just as the ancients held that the essence of the soul is actually composed of the principles of all material things, so that it might know all things. But it is proper to God that his essence immaterially comprehends all things, insofar as effects pre-exist in a cause by virtue. Therefore God alone understands all things through his essence; but not the human soul, nor even an angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0040","text":"[I q. 84 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31962)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0041","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Augustine there speaks of imaginary vision, which takes place through images of bodies. For the forming of these images, the soul gives something of its substance, just as a subject is given in order that it may be informed by some form. And thus it makes such images from itself, not because the soul or something of the soul is converted so as to be this or that image, but as something colored is said to come to be from a body, insofar as it is informed by color. And this sense appears from what follows. For he says that it preserves something, namely, not formed by such an image, which may judge freely concerning the species of such images, and he calls this the mind or intellect. But the part that is informed by such images, namely the imaginative power, he says is common to us and the beasts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0042","text":"[I q. 84 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31963)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0043","text":"To the second it must be said that Aristotle did not hold the soul to be actually composed of all things, as the ancient natural philosophers did; but he said that the soul is in a certain manner all things, insofar as it is potentially all things: through sense, indeed, with respect to sensibles, but through intellect with respect to intelligibles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0044","text":"[I q. 84 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31964)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0045","text":"To the third it must be said that every creature has finite and determinate being. Hence the essence of a higher creature, although it has a certain likeness of a lower creature insofar as they share in some genus, nevertheless does not have its likeness completely, because it is determined to some species other than the species of the lower creature. But the essence of God is the perfect likeness of all things with respect to all things that are found in things, as the universal principle of all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0046","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0047","text":"[I q. 84 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31965)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0048","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that the soul understands all things through species naturally implanted in it. For Gregory says, in the homily on the Ascension, that *man has understanding in common with the angels*. But angels understand all things through forms naturally implanted in them; whence it is said in the *Book of Causes* that *every intelligence is full of forms*. Therefore the soul also has naturally implanted species of things, through which it understands bodily things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0049","text":"[I q. 84 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31966)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0050","text":"Moreover, the intellectual soul is nobler than prime corporeal matter. But prime matter was created by God under the forms for which it is in potency. Therefore, much more was the intellectual soul created by God under intelligible species. And thus the soul understands corporeal things through species naturally implanted in it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0051","text":"[I q. 84 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31967)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0052","text":"Moreover, no one can answer truly except concerning what he knows. But even an unlearned person, having no acquired knowledge, answers truly concerning particulars, provided that he is questioned in an orderly way, as is related in Plato's Meno concerning a certain man. Therefore, before someone acquires knowledge, he has cognition of things. This would not be so unless the soul had species naturally implanted in it. Therefore the soul understands corporeal things through species naturally implanted in it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0053","text":"[I q. 84 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31968)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0054","text":"But on the contrary, the Philosopher says, speaking of the intellect in On the Soul III, that it is like a tablet on which nothing is written.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0055","text":"[I q. 84 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31969)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0056","text":"I answer that it must be said that, since form is the principle of action, a thing must be related to the form which is the principle of action in the way in which it is related to that action: thus, if being moved upward is from lightness, that which is borne upward only potentially must be light only potentially, whereas that which is actually borne upward must be actually light. Now we see that a human being is sometimes cognizant only potentially, both with respect to sense and with respect to intellect. And from such potency he is brought into act: in order to sense, indeed, through the actions of sensibles upon sense; but in order to understand, through learning or discovery. Hence it must be said that the cognitive soul is in potency both to the likenesses which are principles of sensing and to the likenesses which are principles of understanding.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0057","text":"And for this reason Aristotle held that the intellect by which the soul understands does not have any species naturally implanted in it, but is at first in potency to all such species. But because that which actually has a form sometimes cannot act in accordance with the form on account of some impediment, as something light if it is prevented from being borne upward, Plato therefore held that the human intellect is naturally full of all intelligible species, but is impeded by its union with the body from being able to proceed into act. But this does not seem to have been stated fittingly. First, indeed, because, if the soul has natural knowledge of all things, it does not seem possible that it should undergo so great a forgetfulness of this natural knowledge that it should not know itself to possess such knowledge; for no human being forgets those things which he knows naturally, such as that every whole is greater than its part, and other such things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0058","text":"But this seems especially unfitting if it is posited that it is natural for the soul to be united to the body, as was treated above; for it is unfitting that the natural operation of a thing should be totally impeded by that which belongs to it according to nature. Second, the falsity of this position is clearly apparent from the fact that, when some sense is lacking, knowledge of those things which are apprehended by that sense is lacking; thus one born blind can have no knowledge of colors. This would not be so if the intelligible accounts of all things were naturally implanted in the soul. And therefore it must be said that the soul does not know corporeal things through species naturally implanted in it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0059","text":"[I q. 84 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31970)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0060","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that man indeed agrees with the angels in understanding, but falls short of the excellence of their intellect, just as lower bodies, which exist only according to Gregory, fall short of the existence of higher bodies. For the matter of lower bodies is not wholly completed by form, but is in potency to forms which it does not possess; whereas the matter of celestial bodies is wholly completed by form, so that it is not in potency to another form, as was treated above. Similarly, the angel's intellect is perfected by intelligible species according to its nature, whereas the human intellect is in potency to such species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0061","text":"[I q. 84 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31971)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0062","text":"To the second it must be said that prime matter has substantial being through form, and therefore it had to be created under some form; otherwise it would not be in act. Yet, existing under one form, it is in potency to others. But intellect does not have substantial being through an intelligible species; and therefore the comparison does not hold.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0063","text":"[I q. 84 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31972)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0064","text":"To the third it must be said that orderly questioning proceeds from common principles known through themselves to proper matters. Through such a process, moreover, knowledge is caused in the soul of the learner. Hence, when he answers truly concerning those matters about which he is questioned secondly, this is not because he knew them beforehand, but because he then learns them anew. For it makes no difference whether the one teaching proceeds, by proposing or by questioning, from common principles to conclusions; for in either case the mind of the hearer is made certain of what is subsequent through what is prior.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0065","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0066","text":"[I q. 84 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31973)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0067","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that intelligible species flow into the soul from certain separate forms. For everything that is such by participation is caused by that which is such by essence; just as what is ignited is referred, as to a cause, to fire. But the intellectual soul, insofar as it is actually understanding, itself participates in intelligibles; for the intellect in act is in some way the thing understood in act. Therefore, those things which are understood in act according to themselves and through their essence are causes of the intellectual soul's actually understanding. But things understood in act through their essence are forms existing without matter. Therefore, the intelligible species by which the soul understands are caused by certain separate forms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0068","text":"[I q. 84 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31974)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0069","text":"Moreover, intelligibles are related to intellect as sensibles are to sense. But sensibles which are actually outside the soul are causes of the sensible species that are in sense, by which we sense. Therefore, the intelligible species by which our intellect understands are caused by certain things actually intelligible existing outside the soul. But such things are nothing other than forms separated from matter. Therefore, the intelligible forms of our intellect flow forth from certain separate substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0070","text":"[I q. 84 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31975)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0071","text":"Moreover, everything which is in potency is reduced to act by that which is in act. Therefore, if our intellect, existing first in potency, subsequently understands in act, this must be caused by some intellect which is always in act. But this is a separate intellect. Therefore, the intelligible species by which we understand in act are caused by certain separate substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0072","text":"[I q. 84 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31976)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0073","text":"On the contrary, according to this we would not need the senses in order to understand. This is shown to be false especially by the fact that one who lacks one sense can in no way have knowledge of the sensibles of that sense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0074","text":"[I q. 84 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31977)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0075","text":"I answer that it must be said that some have held that the intelligible species of our intellect proceed from certain separate forms or substances. And this in two ways. For Plato, as has been said, held that the forms of sensible things subsist through themselves without matter; for example, the form of man, which he called man through himself, and the form or idea of horse, which he called horse through itself, and likewise concerning the others. Therefore, he held that these separate forms are participated both by our soul and by corporeal matter: by our soul indeed, in order to know; but by corporeal matter, in order to be. Thus, just as corporeal matter, by participating in the idea of stone, becomes this stone, so our intellect, by participating in the idea of stone, becomes understanding stone. But participation in an idea takes place through some likeness of the idea itself in that which participates in it, in the manner in which an exemplar is participated by that which is exemplated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0076","text":"Therefore, just as he held that the sensible forms which are in corporeal matter flow forth from the ideas as certain likenesses of them, so he held that the intelligible species of our intellect are certain likenesses of the ideas flowing forth from them. And for this reason, as was said above, he referred sciences and definitions to the ideas.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0077","text":"But because it is contrary to the nature of sensible things that their forms should subsist without matter, as Aristotle proves in many ways, Avicenna, having set aside this position, held that the intelligible species of all sensible things do not indeed subsist through themselves without matter, but pre-exist immaterially in separate intellects. From the first of these such species are derived into the next, and so on through the others down to the last separate intellect, which he calls the agent intellect. From this, as he says, intelligible species flow forth into our souls, and sensible forms into corporeal matter. Thus Avicenna agrees with Plato in this, that the intelligible species of our intellect flow forth from certain separate forms; yet Plato says that these subsist through themselves, whereas Avicenna places them in the agent intelligence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0078","text":"They also differ in this respect, that Avicenna holds that intelligible species do not remain in our intellect after it ceases actually to understand; rather, it needs to turn again in order to receive anew. Hence he does not hold knowledge to be naturally implanted in the soul, as Plato does, who holds that participations in the ideas remain immovably in the soul. But according to this position, no sufficient reason could be assigned why our soul is united to a body. For it cannot be said that the intellectual soul is united to the body for the sake of the body, because neither is form for the sake of matter, nor a mover for the sake of the movable; rather, the converse is the case. But the body seems especially necessary to the intellectual soul for its proper operation, which is understanding, because in its being it does not depend on the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0079","text":"But if the soul were by its nature naturally suited to receive intelligible species only through the influence of certain separate principles, and did not receive them from the senses, it would not need a body in order to understand, and thus would be united to the body in vain. But if it is said that our soul needs the senses in order to understand, by which it is in some way aroused to consider those things whose intelligible species it receives from separate principles, this does not seem sufficient. For such arousal does not seem necessary to the soul except insofar as it is, according to the Platonists, in some way slumbering and forgetful on account of its union with the body; and thus the senses would benefit the intellectual soul only by removing an impediment that comes to the soul from its union with the body. Therefore, it still remains to inquire what is the cause of the union of the soul with the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0080","text":"But if it is said, according to Avicenna, that the senses are necessary to the soul because through them it is aroused to turn to the agent intelligence, from which it receives the species, this indeed is not sufficient. For if it belongs to the nature of the soul to understand through species flowing forth from the agent intelligence, it would follow that the soul could at some time turn to the agent intelligence from the inclination of its own nature, or even when aroused through another sense, so as to turn to the agent intelligence to receive the species of sensibles for which one does not possess the sense. And thus one born blind could have knowledge of colors, which is manifestly false. Hence it must be said that the intelligible species by which our soul understands do not flow forth from separate forms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0081","text":"[I q. 84 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31978)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0082","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the intelligible species in which our intellect participates are reduced, as to their first cause, to a principle intelligible through its essence, namely, to God. But from that principle they proceed by means of the forms of sensible and material things, from which we gather knowledge, as Dionysius says.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0083","text":"[I q. 84 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31979)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0084","text":"To the second, it must be said that material things, according to the being they have outside the soul, can be actually sensible, but not actually intelligible. Hence the case of sense and intellect is not alike.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0085","text":"[I q. 84 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31980)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0086","text":"To the third, it must be said that our possible intellect is reduced from potency to act by some being in act, that is, by the agent intellect, which is a certain power of our soul, as has been said; not, however, by some separate intellect as by a proximate cause, but perhaps as by a remote cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0087","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0088","text":"[I q. 84 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31981)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0089","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth. It seems that the intellectual soul does not know material things in the eternal reasons. For that in which something is known is itself known more and priorly. But the intellectual soul of man, in the state of the present life, does not know the eternal reasons, because it does not know God Himself, in whom the eternal reasons exist, but is united to Him as to one unknown, as Dionysius says in chapter 1 of the Mystical Theology. Therefore the soul does not know all things in the eternal reasons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0090","text":"[I q. 84 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31982)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0091","text":"Further, it is said in Rom. I that *the invisible things of God are seen through those things which have been made*. But among the invisible things of God are numbered the eternal reasons. Therefore the eternal reasons are known through material creatures, and not conversely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0092","text":"[I q. 84 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31983)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0093","text":"Further, the eternal reasons are nothing other than ideas; for Augustine says, in the book Eighty-Three Questions, that *ideas are the stable reasons of things existing in the divine mind*. Therefore, if it is said that the intellectual soul knows all things in the eternal reasons, the opinion of Plato will return, who held that all knowledge is derived from ideas.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0094","text":"[I q. 84 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31984)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0095","text":"On the contrary is what Augustine says in Confessions XII: *if we both see that what you say is true, and we both see that what I say is true, where, I ask, do we see this? Certainly not I in you, nor you in me, but both of us in that immutable truth itself, which is above our minds*. But immutable truth is contained in the eternal reasons. Therefore the intellectual soul knows all true things in the eternal reasons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0096","text":"[I q. 84 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31985)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0097","text":"I answer that, as Augustine says in Book II of *On Christian Doctrine*, “the philosophers who are so called, if perchance they have said things true and suited to our faith, are to be claimed for our use from them as from unjust possessors. For the teachings of the gentiles contain certain feigned and superstitious fabrications, which each one of us, departing from the society of the gentiles, ought to avoid.” And therefore Augustine, who had been steeped in the teachings of the Platonists, accepted from their statements whatever he found suited to the faith; but whatever he found contrary to our faith, he changed for the better.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0098","text":"Now Plato held, as was said above, that the forms of things subsist through themselves, separated from matter; and he called them ideas, by participation in which he said that our intellect knows all things. Thus, just as corporeal matter becomes a stone by participation in the idea of stone, so our intellect would know a stone by participation in that same idea. But because it seems foreign to the faith that the forms of things should subsist through themselves outside things and without matter, as the Platonists held, saying that life-in-itself or wisdom-in-itself are certain creative substances, as Dionysius says in chapter XI of *On the Divine Names*; therefore Augustine, in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, held that, in place of these ideas which Plato posited, the intelligible principles of all creatures exist in the divine mind, according to which all things are formed, and according to which the human soul also knows all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0099","text":"Therefore, when it is asked whether the human soul knows all things in the eternal intelligible principles, it must be said that something is said to be known in something in two ways. In one way, as in the known object; as someone sees in a mirror those things whose images are reflected in the mirror. And in this way the soul, in the state of the present life, cannot see all things in the eternal intelligible principles; but in this way the blessed, who see God and all things in Him, know all things in the eternal intelligible principles. In another way, something is said to be known in something as in a principle of knowing; as if we were to say that those things which are seen by means of the sun are seen in the sun. And thus it is necessary to say that the human soul knows all things in the eternal intelligible principles, by participation in which we know all things. For the intellectual light itself which is in us is nothing other than a certain participated likeness of the uncreated light, in which the eternal intelligible principles are contained.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0100","text":"Hence in Psalm IV it is said, “Many say, Who will show us good things?” To this question the Psalmist replies, saying, “The light of thy countenance, O Lord, is marked upon us.” As if to say, through the very impressing of the divine light upon us, all things are shown to us. Yet because, besides the intellectual light in us, intelligible species received from things are required in order to have knowledge of material things, therefore we do not have knowledge of material things through participation in the eternal intelligible principles alone, as the Platonists held, that participation in the ideas alone suffices for having knowledge. Hence Augustine says, in Book IV of *On the Trinity*, “Is it because philosophers, by most certain proofs, persuade us that all temporal things are made according to eternal intelligible principles, that they were therefore able to discern in those principles themselves, or to gather from them, how many kinds of animals there are, what the seeds of each are?”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0101","text":"“Did they not seek all these things through the history of places and times?” That Augustine did not understand all things to be known in the eternal intelligible principles, or in unchangeable truth, in such a way that the eternal intelligible principles themselves are seen, is clear from the fact that he himself says in the book *Eighty-Three Questions* that “not every rational soul, whatsoever it may be, but one which has been holy and pure, is declared fit for that vision,” namely, of the eternal intelligible principles; such as are the souls of the blessed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0102","text":"[I q. 84 a. 5 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31986)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0103","text":"And by these things the answer to the objections is evident.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0104","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0105","text":"[I q. 84 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31987)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0106","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth article. It seems that intellectual knowledge is not received from sensible things. For Augustine says, in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, that “purity of truth is not to be expected from the senses of the body.” And he proves this in two ways. First, from the fact that “everything which bodily sense touches is changed without any interval of time; but what does not remain cannot be perceived.” Secondly, from the fact that “all the things which we sense through the body, even when they are not present to the senses, we nevertheless undergo images of them, as in sleep or madness; yet by the senses we are unable to discern whether we sense the sensible things themselves or their false images. But nothing can be perceived which is not distinguished from the false.” And thus he concludes that truth is not to be expected from the senses. But intellectual knowledge apprehends truth. Therefore intellectual knowledge is not to be expected from the senses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0107","text":"[I q. 84 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31988)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0108","text":"Further, Augustine says, in Book XII of *The Literal Meaning of Genesis*, “It must not be thought that a body makes anything in a spirit, as though a spirit were subjected, in the manner of matter, to a body that makes; for in every way that which makes is more excellent than the thing from which it makes something.” Hence he concludes that “it is not the body that makes the image of the body in the spirit, but the spirit itself makes it in itself.” Therefore intellectual knowledge is not derived from sensible things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0109","text":"[I q. 84 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31989)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0110","text":"Further, an effect does not extend beyond the power of its cause. But intellectual knowledge extends beyond sensible things, for we understand certain things that cannot be perceived by sense. Therefore intellectual knowledge is not derived from sensible things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0111","text":"[I q. 84 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31990)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0112","text":"On the contrary, the Philosopher proves, in Book I of the *Metaphysics* and at the end of the *Posterior Analytics*, that the principle of our knowledge is from sense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0113","text":"[I q. 84 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31991)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0114","text":"I answer that there were three opinions of philosophers concerning this question. For Democritus held that “there is no other cause of any of our knowledge except when images come and enter into our souls from those bodies about which we think,” as Augustine says in his letter to Dioscorus. Aristotle too says, in the book *On Sleep and Waking*, that Democritus held that knowledge comes about through idols and emanations. The reason for this position was that Democritus himself, as well as the other ancient natural philosophers, did not hold that intellect differs from sense, as Aristotle says in the book *On the Soul*. And therefore, because sense is altered by the sensible object, they thought that all our knowledge comes about through alteration by sensible things alone. Democritus indeed asserted that this alteration comes about through emanations of images.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0115","text":"Plato, on the other hand, held that intellect differs from sense, and that intellect is an immaterial power which does not use a bodily organ in its act. And because the incorporeal cannot be altered by the corporeal, he held that intellectual knowledge does not come about through alteration of the intellect by sensible things, but through participation in separated intelligible forms, as has been said. He also held that sense is a certain power operating through itself. Hence sense itself, since it is a certain spiritual power, is not altered by sensible things; rather, the organs of the senses are altered by sensible things, and from this alteration the soul is in some way aroused to form the species of sensible things in itself. And Augustine seems to touch upon this opinion in Book XII of *The Literal Meaning of Genesis*, where he says that “it is not the body that senses, but the soul through the body, which it uses as a kind of messenger for forming in itself what is reported from without.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0116","text":"Thus, according to Plato’s opinion, neither intellectual knowledge proceeds from the sensible, nor does even sensible knowledge proceed wholly from sensible things; rather, sensible things arouse the sensitive soul to sense, and similarly the senses arouse the intellective soul to understand. Aristotle, however, proceeded by a middle way. For with Plato he held that intellect differs from sense. But he held that sense has no proper operation without communication with the body, so that sensing is not the act of the soul alone, but of the composite. And he held the same concerning all the operations of the sensitive part. Therefore, since it is not unfitting that sensible things outside the soul cause something in the composite, in this Aristotle agreed with Democritus: that the operations of the sensitive part are caused by the impression of sensible things upon sense—not by way of emanation, as Democritus held, but through a certain operation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0117","text":"For Democritus held that every action comes about through the influx of atoms, as is clear in Book I of *On Generation*. But Aristotle held that intellect has an operation without communication with the body. Yet nothing corporeal can make an impression upon an incorporeal thing. And therefore, to cause intellectual operation, according to Aristotle, the impression of sensible bodies alone does not suffice; rather, something more noble is required, because the agent is more honorable than the patient, as he himself says. Yet this is not such that intellectual operation is caused in us solely by the impression of certain higher things, as Plato held; rather, that higher and more noble agent, which he calls the agent intellect, of which we have already spoken above, makes the phantasms received from the senses actually intelligible, by a certain mode of abstraction. Accordingly, from the side of the phantasms, intellectual operation is caused by sense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0118","text":"But because phantasms do not suffice to alter the possible intellect, but must be made actually intelligible by the agent intellect, it cannot be said that sensible knowledge is the total and perfect cause of intellectual knowledge; rather, it is in a certain way the matter of the cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0119","text":"[I q. 84 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31992)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0120","text":"Therefore, to the first objection it must be said that by those words of Augustine it is conveyed that truth is not to be expected wholly from the senses. For the light of the agent intellect is required, through which we know truth unchangeably in changeable things, and distinguish the things themselves from likenesses of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0121","text":"[I q. 84 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31993)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0122","text":"To the second, it must be said that Augustine there is speaking not of intellectual cognition, but of imaginative cognition. And because, according to Plato’s opinion, the imaginative power has an operation that belongs to the soul alone, Augustine employed the same line of reasoning to show that bodies do not impress their likenesses upon the imaginative power, but that the soul itself does this; Aristotle uses it to prove that the agent intellect is something separate, namely because an agent is more noble than what is acted upon. And undoubtedly, according to this position, one must posit in the imaginative power not only a passive potency but also an active one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0123","text":"But if we posit, according to Aristotle’s opinion, that the action of the imaginative power belongs to the composite, no difficulty follows, because a sensible body is more noble than an animal organ, insofar as it is compared to it as being in act to being in potency, as what is actually colored is to the pupil, which is potentially colored. Yet it could be said that, although the first alteration of the imaginative power is through the motion of sensible things, because phantasy is a motion produced according to sensation, as is said in the book *On the Soul*; nevertheless, there is a certain operation of the soul in man which, by dividing and combining, forms diverse images of things, even images not received from the senses. And Augustine’s words may be understood in this respect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0124","text":"[I q. 84 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31994)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0125","text":"To the third, it must be said that sensitive cognition is not the whole cause of intellectual cognition. And therefore it is not surprising if intellectual cognition extends beyond sensitive cognition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0126","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0127","text":"[I q. 84 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31995)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0128","text":"We proceed thus to the seventh. It seems that the intellect can actually understand through the intelligible species which it has within itself, without turning itself toward phantasms. For the intellect becomes actual through the intelligible species by which it is informed. But for the intellect to be in act is itself to understand. Therefore intelligible species suffice for the intellect actually to understand, without its turning itself toward phantasms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0129","text":"[I q. 84 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31996)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0130","text":"Moreover, imagination depends more upon sense than intellect does upon imagination. But imagination can imagine actually when sensibles are absent. Therefore, much more can the intellect understand actually without turning itself toward phantasms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0131","text":"[I q. 84 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31997)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0132","text":"Moreover, there are no phantasms of incorporeal things, because imagination does not transcend time and the continuous. Therefore, if our intellect could not understand anything actually unless it turned itself toward phantasms, it would follow that it could not understand anything incorporeal. But this is clearly false, for we understand truth itself, and God, and angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0133","text":"[I q. 84 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31998)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0134","text":"But against this is what the Philosopher says in *On the Soul* III, that *the soul understands nothing without a phantasm*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0135","text":"[I q. 84 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#31999)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0136","text":"I answer that it must be said that it is impossible for our intellect, according to the state of the present life, in which it is joined to a passible body, actually to understand anything except by turning itself toward phantasms. And this is evident from two indications. First, because, since the intellect is a power not using a bodily organ, it would in no way be impeded in its act by damage to some bodily organ, if the act of a power using a bodily organ were not required for its act. But the senses and imagination and the other powers pertaining to the sensitive part use a bodily organ. Hence it is manifest that, in order for the intellect actually to understand, not only in acquiring knowledge anew but also in using knowledge already acquired, the act of imagination and of the other powers is required.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0137","text":"For we see that, when the act of the imaginative power is impeded through injury to its organ, as in the frenzied; and likewise when the act of the memorative power is impeded, as in the lethargic; a man is impeded from actually understanding even those things the knowledge of which he had previously received. Secondly, because anyone can experience in himself that, when he endeavors to understand something, he forms for himself certain phantasms by way of examples, in which he may, as it were, look upon what he strives to understand. And hence also, when we wish to make another understand something, we propose examples to him, from which he may form phantasms for himself in order to understand. The reason for this is that a cognitive power is proportioned to the knowable. Hence the proper object of the angelic intellect, which is wholly separated from body, is an intelligible substance separated from body; and through intelligibles of this kind it knows material things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0138","text":"But the proper object of the human intellect, which is joined to a body, is the quiddity or nature existing in corporeal matter; and through such natures of visible things it rises even to some knowledge of invisible things. Now it belongs to the nature of such a nature that it exist in some individual, which is not without corporeal matter; just as it belongs to the nature of a stone that it be in this stone, and to the nature of a horse that it be in this horse, and so with other things. Hence the nature of a stone, or of any material thing whatever, cannot be known completely and truly except insofar as it is known as existing in the particular. But we apprehend the particular through sense and imagination. And therefore, in order that the intellect actually understand its proper object, it must turn itself toward phantasms, so that it may contemplate the universal nature as existing in the particular.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0139","text":"But if the proper object of our intellect were a separate form, or if the natures of sensible things subsisted not in particulars, according to the Platonists, our intellect would not need always, in understanding, to turn itself toward phantasms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0140","text":"[I q. 84 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32000)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0141","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the species preserved in the possible intellect exist in it habitually when it does not actually understand, as was said above. Hence, in order that we understand actually, the mere preservation of the species does not suffice; rather, we must use them in the manner fitting to the things of which they are species, namely, natures existing in particulars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0142","text":"[I q. 84 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32001)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0143","text":"To the second, it must be said that the phantasm itself is also a likeness of a particular thing; hence imagination does not require some other likeness of the particular, as the intellect does.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0144","text":"[I q. 84 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32002)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0145","text":"To the third it must be said that incorporeal things, of which there are no phantasms, are known by us through comparison with sensible bodies, of which there are phantasms. Thus we understand truth from consideration of the thing concerning whose truth we inquire; but God, as Dionysius says, we know as cause, and through excess, and through removal; nor can we know other incorporeal substances, in the state of the present life, except through removal or some comparison with bodily things. And therefore, when we understand something of this sort, we must turn to phantasms of bodies, although they are not phantasms of those things themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0146","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0147","text":"[I q. 84 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32003)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0148","text":"We proceed to the eighth as follows. It seems that the judgment of the intellect is not impeded by a binding of the senses. For what is higher does not depend on what is lower. But the judgment of the intellect is above sense. Therefore the judgment of the intellect is not impeded by a binding of the senses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0149","text":"[I q. 84 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32004)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0150","text":"Moreover, to syllogize is an act of the intellect. But in sleep the senses are bound, as is said in the book On Sleep and Waking; yet it sometimes happens that someone who is sleeping syllogizes. Therefore the judgment of the intellect is not impeded by a binding of the senses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0151","text":"[I q. 84 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32005)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0152","text":"On the contrary, things that occur in sleep contrary to lawful morals are not imputed as sin, as Augustine says in XII On the Literal Meaning of Genesis. But this would not be so if a human being had the free use of reason and intellect while sleeping. Therefore the use of reason is impeded by a binding of the senses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0153","text":"[I q. 84 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32006)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0154","text":"I answer that it must be said that, as was stated, the proper object proportioned to our intellect is the nature of a sensible thing. But a perfect judgment about any thing cannot be given unless all the things pertaining to the matter are known, and especially if that which is the term and end of the judgment is unknown. Now the Philosopher says, in III On the Heavens, that *just as the end of productive science is a work, so the end of natural science is that which appears principally according to sense*; for the craftsman does not seek knowledge of a knife except for the sake of the work, so that he may make this particular knife; and likewise the natural philosopher does not seek to know the nature of stone and horse except in order that he may know the accounts of those things which are seen according to sense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0155","text":"But it is manifest that the craftsman's judgment about a knife could not be perfect if he did not know the work; and likewise the judgment of natural science concerning natural things cannot be perfect if sensible things are unknown. But all the things that we understand in the present state are known by us through comparison with natural sensible things. Hence it is impossible that there be in us a perfect judgment of the intellect when the senses, through which we know sensible things, are bound.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0156","text":"[I q. 84 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32007)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0157","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, although the intellect is higher than sense, it nevertheless receives in some manner from sense, and its first and principal objects are founded in sensible things. And therefore the judgment of the intellect must be impeded by a binding of the senses.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0158","text":"[I q. 84 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32008)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0158"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0159","text":"To the second it must be said that sense is bound in those who are asleep because of certain evaporations and dispersed vaporous exhalations, as is said in the book On Sleep and Waking. And therefore, according to the disposition of such evaporations, the binding of sense may be greater or less. For when the movement of vapors is great, not only sense but also imagination is bound, so that no phantasms appear; as happens especially when someone begins to sleep after much food and drink. But if the movement of vapors is somewhat more relaxed, phantasms appear, but distorted and disordered, as happens to those suffering from fever. But if the movement is stilled still more, ordered phantasms appear, as most often happens at the end of sleep, and in sober persons and those with a strong imagination.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0159"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0160","text":"But if the movement of vapors is slight, not only does imagination remain free, but even common sense itself is in part released; so that a person sometimes judges while sleeping that the things he sees are dreams, as though distinguishing between things and likenesses of things. Yet common sense remains bound in some respect; and therefore, although he distinguishes some likenesses from things, he is nevertheless always deceived in some matters. Thus, according to the manner in which sense and imagination are released in sleep, the judgment of the intellect is released as well, though not wholly. Hence those who syllogize while sleeping, when they wake up, always recognize that they have failed in some respect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_76:P0160"]}],"source_digest":"e941a260f016e5b6ce4f12e2dbc269787bdd905553b7d3b0aa42c5f5dd96f9e9","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 84"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 84","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77","title":"First Part · Question 85","order":86,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_77","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 85 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32009)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de modo et ordine intelligendi. Et circa hoc quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum intellectus noster intelligat abstrahendo species a phantasmatibus. Secundo, utrum species intelligibiles abstractae a phantasmatibus, se habeant ad intellectum nostrum ut quod intelligitur, vel sicut id quo intelligitur. Tertio, utrum intellectus noster naturaliter intelligat prius magis universale. Quarto, utrum intellectus noster possit multa simul intelligere. Quinto, utrum intellectus noster intelligat componendo et dividendo. Sexto, utrum intellectus possit errare. Septimo, utrum unus possit eandem rem melius intelligere quam alius. Octavo, utrum intellectus noster per prius cognoscat indivisibile quam divisibile.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 85 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32010)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus noster non intelligat res corporeas et materiales per abstractionem a phantasmatibus. Quicumque enim intellectus intelligit rem aliter quam sit, est falsus. Formae autem rerum materialium non sunt abstractae a particularibus, quorum similitudines sunt phantasmata. Si ergo intelligamus res materiales per abstractionem specierum a phantasmatibus, erit falsitas in intellectu nostro.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32011)\n\nPraeterea, res materiales sunt res naturales, in quarum definitione cadit materia. Sed nihil potest intelligi sine eo quod cadit in definitione eius. Ergo res materiales non possunt intelligi sine materia. Sed materia est individuationis principium. Ergo res materiales non possunt intelligi per abstractionem universalis a particulari, quod est abstrahere species intelligibiles a phantasmatibus.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32012)\n\nPraeterea, in III de anima dicitur quod phantasmata se habent ad animam intellectivam sicut colores ad visum. Sed visio non fit per abstractionem aliquarum specierum a coloribus, sed per hoc quod colores imprimunt in visum. Ergo nec intelligere contingit per hoc quod aliquid abstrahatur a phantasmatibus, sed per hoc quod phantasmata imprimunt in intellectum.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32013)\n\nPraeterea, ut dicitur in III de anima, in intellectiva anima sunt duo, scilicet intellectus possibilis, et agens. Sed abstrahere a phantasmatibus species intelligibiles non pertinet ad intellectum possibilem, sed recipere species iam abstractas. Sed nec etiam videtur pertinere ad intellectum agentem, qui se habet ad phantasmata sicut lumen ad colores, quod non abstrahit aliquid a coloribus, sed magis eis influit. Ergo nullo modo intelligimus abstrahendo a phantasmatibus.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32014)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus, in III de anima, dicit quod *intellectus intelligit species in phantasmatibus*. Non ergo eas abstrahendo.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32015)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in III de anima, quod *sicut res sunt separabiles a materia, sic circa intellectum sunt*. Ergo oportet quod materialia intelligantur inquantum a materia abstrahuntur, et a similitudinibus materialibus, quae sunt phantasmata.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32016)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, obiectum cognoscibile proportionatur virtuti cognoscitivae. Est autem triplex gradus cognoscitivae virtutis. Quaedam enim cognoscitiva virtus est actus organi corporalis, scilicet sensus. Et ideo obiectum cuiuslibet sensitivae potentiae est forma prout in materia corporali existit. Et quia huiusmodi materia est individuationis principium, ideo omnis potentia sensitivae partis est cognoscitiva particularium tantum. Quaedam autem virtus cognoscitiva est quae neque est actus organi corporalis, neque est aliquo modo corporali materiae coniuncta, sicut intellectus angelicus. Et ideo huius virtutis cognoscitivae obiectum est forma sine materia subsistens, etsi enim materialia cognoscant, non tamen nisi in immaterialibus ea intuentur, scilicet vel in seipsis vel in Deo. Intellectus autem humanus medio modo se habet, non enim est actus alicuius organi, sed tamen est quaedam virtus animae, quae est forma corporis, ut ex supra dictis patet. Et ideo proprium eius est cognoscere formam in materia quidem corporali individualiter existentem, non tamen prout est in tali materia. Cognoscere vero id quod est in materia individuali, non prout est in tali materia, est abstrahere formam a materia individuali, quam repraesentant phantasmata. Et ideo necesse est dicere quod intellectus noster intelligit materialia abstrahendo a phantasmatibus; et per materialia sic considerata in immaterialium aliqualem cognitionem devenimus, sicut e contra Angeli per immaterialia materialia cognoscunt. Plato vero, attendens solum ad immaterialitatem intellectus humani, non autem ad hoc quod est corpori quodammodo unitus, posuit obiectum intellectus ideas separatas; et quod intelligimus, non quidem abstrahendo, sed magis abstracta participando, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32017)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod abstrahere contingit dupliciter. Uno modo, per modum compositionis et divisionis; sicut cum intelligimus aliquid non esse in alio, vel esse separatum ab eo. Alio modo, per modum simplicis et absolutae considerationis; sicut cum intelligimus unum, nihil considerando de alio. Abstrahere igitur per intellectum ea quae secundum rem non sunt abstracta, secundum primum modum abstrahendi, non est absque falsitate. Sed secundo modo abstrahere per intellectum quae non sunt abstracta secundum rem, non habet falsitatem; ut in sensibilibus manifeste apparet. Si enim intelligamus vel dicamus colorem non inesse corpori colorato, vel esse separatum ab eo, erit falsitas in opinione vel in oratione. Si vero consideremus colorem et proprietates eius, nihil considerantes de pomo colorato; vel quod sic intelligimus, etiam voce exprimamus; erit absque falsitate opinionis et orationis. Pomum enim non est de ratione coloris; et ideo nihil prohibet colorem intelligi, nihil intelligendo de pomo. Similiter dico quod ea quae pertinent ad rationem speciei cuiuslibet rei materialis, puta lapidis aut hominis aut equi, possunt considerari sine principiis individualibus, quae non sunt de ratione speciei. Et hoc est abstrahere universale a particulari, vel speciem intelligibilem a phantasmatibus, considerare scilicet naturam speciei absque consideratione individualium principiorum, quae per phantasmata repraesentantur. Cum ergo dicitur quod intellectus est falsus qui intelligit rem aliter quam sit, verum est si ly aliter referatur ad rem intellectam. Tunc enim intellectus est falsus, quando intelligit rem esse aliter quam sit. Unde falsus esset intellectus, si sic abstraheret speciem lapidis a materia, ut intelligeret eam non esse in materia, ut Plato posuit. Non est autem verum quod proponitur, si ly aliter accipiatur ex parte intelligentis. Est enim absque falsitate ut alius sit modus intelligentis in intelligendo, quam modus rei in existendo, quia intellectum est in intelligente immaterialiter, per modum intellectus; non autem materialiter, per modum rei materialis.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32018)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod quidam putaverunt quod species rei naturalis sit forma solum, et quod materia non sit pars speciei. Sed secundum hoc, in definitionibus rerum naturalium non poneretur materia. Et ideo aliter dicendum est, quod materia est duplex, scilicet communis, et signata vel individualis, communis quidem, ut caro et os; individualis autem, ut hae carnes et haec ossa. Intellectus igitur abstrahit speciem rei naturalis a materia sensibili individuali, non autem a materia sensibili communi. Sicut speciem hominis abstrahit ab his carnibus et his ossibus, quae non sunt de ratione speciei, sed sunt partes individui, ut dicitur in VII Metaphys.; et ideo sine eis considerari potest. Sed species hominis non potest abstrahi per intellectum a carnibus et ossibus. Species autem mathematicae possunt abstrahi per intellectum a materia sensibili non solum individuali, sed etiam communi; non tamen a materia intelligibili communi, sed solum individuali. Materia enim sensibilis dicitur materia corporalis secundum quod subiacet qualitatibus sensibilibus, scilicet calido et frigido, duro et molli, et huiusmodi. Materia vero intelligibilis dicitur substantia secundum quod subiacet quantitati. Manifestum est autem quod quantitas prius inest substantiae quam qualitates sensibiles. Unde quantitates, ut numeri et dimensiones et figurae, quae sunt terminationes quantitatum, possunt considerari absque qualitatibus sensibilibus, quod est eas abstrahi a materia sensibili, non tamen possunt considerari sine intellectu substantiae quantitati subiectae, quod esset eas abstrahi a materia intelligibili communi. Possunt tamen considerari sine hac vel illa substantia; quod est eas abstrahi a materia intelligibili individuali. Quaedam vero sunt quae possunt abstrahi etiam a materia intelligibili communi, sicut ens, unum, potentia et actus, et alia huiusmodi, quae etiam esse possunt absque omni materia, ut patet in substantiis immaterialibus. Et quia Plato non consideravit quod dictum est de duplici modo abstractionis, omnia quae diximus abstrahi per intellectum, posuit abstracta esse secundum rem.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32019)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod colores habent eundem modum existendi prout sunt in materia corporali individuali, sicut et potentia visiva, et ideo possunt imprimere suam similitudinem in visum. Sed phantasmata, cum sint similitudines individuorum, et existant in organis corporeis, non habent eundem modum existendi quem habet intellectus humanus, ut ex dictis patet; et ideo non possunt sua virtute imprimere in intellectum possibilem. Sed virtute intellectus agentis resultat quaedam similitudo in intellectu possibili ex conversione intellectus agentis supra phantasmata, quae quidem est repraesentativa eorum quorum sunt phantasmata, solum quantum ad naturam speciei. Et per hunc modum dicitur abstrahi species intelligibilis a phantasmatibus, non quod aliqua eadem numero forma, quae prius fuit in phantasmatibus, postmodum fiat in intellectu possibili, ad modum quo corpus accipitur ab uno loco et transfertur ad alterum.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32020)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod phantasmata et illuminantur ab intellectu agente; et iterum ab eis, per virtutem intellectus agentis, species intelligibiles abstrahuntur. Illuminantur quidem, quia, sicut pars sensitiva ex coniunctione ad intellectivam efficitur virtuosior, ita phantasmata ex virtute intellectus agentis redduntur habilia ut ab eis intentiones intelligibiles abstrahantur. Abstrahit autem intellectus agens species intelligibiles a phantasmatibus, inquantum per virtutem intellectus agentis accipere possumus in nostra consideratione naturas specierum sine individualibus conditionibus, secundum quarum similitudines intellectus possibilis informatur.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32021)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod intellectus noster et abstrahit species intelligibiles a phantasmatibus, inquantum considerat naturas rerum in universali; et tamen intelligit eas in phantasmatibus, quia non potest intelligere etiam ea quorum species abstrahit, nisi convertendo se ad phantasmata, ut supra dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 85 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32022)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod species intelligibiles a phantasmatibus abstractae, se habeant ad intellectum nostrum sicut id quod intelligitur. Intellectum enim in actu est in intelligente, quia intellectum in actu est ipse intellectus in actu. Sed nihil de re intellecta est in intellectu actu intelligente, nisi species intelligibilis abstracta. Ergo huiusmodi species est ipsum intellectum in actu.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32023)\n\nPraeterea, intellectum in actu oportet in aliquo esse, alioquin nihil esset. Sed non est in re quae est extra animam, quia, cum res extra animam sit materialis, nihil quod est in ea, potest esse intellectum in actu. Relinquitur ergo quod intellectum in actu sit in intellectu. Et ita nihil est aliud quam species intelligibilis praedicta.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32024)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus dicit, in I Periherm., quod *voces sunt notae earum quae sunt in anima passionum*. Sed voces significant res intellectas, id enim voce significamus quod intelligimus. Ergo ipsae passiones animae, scilicet species intelligibiles, sunt ea quae intelliguntur in actu.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32025)\n\nSed contra, species intelligibilis se habet ad intellectum, sicut species sensibilis ad sensum. Sed species sensibilis non est illud quod sentitur, sed magis id quo sensus sentit. Ergo species intelligibilis non est quod intelligitur actu, sed id quo intelligit intellectus.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32026)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam posuerunt quod vires cognoscitivae quae sunt in nobis, nihil cognoscunt nisi proprias passiones; puta quod sensus non sentit nisi passionem sui organi. Et secundum hoc, intellectus nihil intelligit nisi suam passionem, idest speciem intelligibilem in se receptam. Et secundum hoc, species huiusmodi est ipsum quod intelligitur. Sed haec opinio manifeste apparet falsa ex duobus. Primo quidem, quia eadem sunt quae intelligimus, et de quibus sunt scientiae. Si igitur ea quae intelligimus essent solum species quae sunt in anima, sequeretur quod scientiae omnes non essent de rebus quae sunt extra animam, sed solum de speciebus intelligibilibus quae sunt in anima; sicut secundum Platonicos omnes scientiae sunt de ideis, quas ponebant esse intellecta in actu. Secundo, quia sequeretur error antiquorum dicentium quod *omne quod videtur est verum*; et sic quod contradictoriae essent simul verae. Si enim potentia non cognoscit nisi propriam passionem, de ea solum iudicat. Sic autem videtur aliquid, secundum quod potentia cognoscitiva afficitur. Semper ergo iudicium potentiae cognoscitivae erit de eo quod iudicat, scilicet de propria passione, secundum quod est; et ita omne iudicium erit verum. Puta si gustus non sentit nisi propriam passionem, cum aliquis habens sanum gustum iudicat mel esse dulce, vere iudicabit; et similiter si ille qui habet gustum infectum, iudicet mel esse amarum, vere iudicabit, uterque enim iudicat secundum quod gustus eius afficitur. Et sic sequitur quod omnis opinio aequaliter erit vera, et universaliter omnis acceptio. Et ideo dicendum est quod species intelligibilis se habet ad intellectum ut quo intelligit intellectus. Quod sic patet. Cum enim sit duplex actio, sicut dicitur IX Metaphys., una quae manet in agente, ut videre et intelligere, altera quae transit in rem exteriorem, ut calefacere et secare; utraque fit secundum aliquam formam. Et sicut forma secundum quam provenit actio tendens in rem exteriorem, est similitudo obiecti actionis, ut calor calefacientis est similitudo calefacti; similiter forma secundum quam provenit actio manens in agente, est similitudo obiecti. Unde similitudo rei visibilis est secundum quam visus videt; et similitudo rei intellectae, quae est species intelligibilis, est forma secundum quam intellectus intelligit. Sed quia intellectus supra seipsum reflectitur, secundum eandem reflexionem intelligit et suum intelligere, et speciem qua intelligit. Et sic species intellectiva secundario est id quod intelligitur. Sed id quod intelligitur primo, est res cuius species intelligibilis est similitudo. Et hoc etiam patet ex antiquorum opinione, qui ponebant simile simili cognosci. Ponebant enim quod anima per terram quae in ipsa erat, cognosceret terram quae extra ipsam erat; et sic de aliis. Si ergo accipiamus speciem terrae loco terrae, secundum doctrinam Aristotelis, qui dicit quod *lapis non est in anima, sed species lapidis*; sequetur quod anima per species intelligibiles cognoscat res quae sunt extra animam.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32027)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod intellectum est in intelligente per suam similitudinem. Et per hunc modum dicitur quod intellectum in actu est intellectus in actu, inquantum similitudo rei intellectae est forma intellectus; sicut similitudo rei sensibilis est forma sensus in actu. Unde non sequitur quod species intelligibilis abstracta sit id quod actu intelligitur, sed quod sit similitudo eius.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32028)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, cum dicitur intellectum in actu, duo importantur, scilicet res quae intelligitur, et hoc quod est ipsum intelligi. Et similiter cum dicitur universale abstractum, duo intelliguntur, scilicet ipsa natura rei, et abstractio seu universalitas. Ipsa igitur natura cui accidit vel intelligi vel abstrahi, vel intentio universalitatis, non est nisi in singularibus; sed hoc ipsum quod est intelligi vel abstrahi, vel intentio universalitatis, est in intellectu. Et hoc possumus videre per simile in sensu. Visus enim videt colorem pomi sine eius odore. Si ergo quaeratur ubi sit color qui videtur sine odore manifestum est quod color qui videtur, non est nisi in pomo; sed quod sit sine odore perceptus, hoc accidit ei ex parte visus, inquantum in visu est similitudo coloris et non odoris. Similiter humanitas quae intelligitur, non est nisi in hoc vel in illo homine, sed quod humanitas apprehendatur sine individualibus conditionibus, quod est ipsam abstrahi, ad quod sequitur intentio universalitatis, accidit humanitatis secundum quod percipitur ab intellectu, in quo est similitudo naturae speciei, et non individualium principiorum.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32029)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in parte sensitiva invenitur duplex operatio. Una secundum solam immutationem, et sic perficitur operatio sensus per hoc quod immutatur a sensibili. Alia operatio est formatio, secundum quod vis imaginativa format sibi aliquod idolum rei absentis, vel etiam nunquam visae. Et utraque haec operatio coniungitur in intellectu. Nam primo quidem consideratur passio intellectus possibilis secundum quod informatur specie intelligibili. Qua quidem formatus, format secundo vel definitionem vel divisionem vel compositionem, quae per vocem significatur. Unde ratio quam significat nomen, est definitio; et enuntiatio significat compositionem et divisionem intellectus. Non ergo voces significant ipsas species intelligibiles; sed ea quae intellectus sibi format ad iudicandum de rebus exterioribus.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 85 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32030)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod magis universalia non sint priora in nostra cognitione intellectuali. Quia ea quae sunt priora et notiora secundum naturam, sunt posteriora et minus nota secundum nos. Sed universalia sunt priora secundum naturam, quia prius est a quo non convertitur subsistendi consequentia. Ergo universalia sunt posteriora in cognitione nostri intellectus.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32031)\n\nPraeterea, composita sunt priora quoad nos quam simplicia. Sed universalia sunt simpliciora. Ergo sunt posterius nota quoad nos.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32032)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus dicit, in I Physic. quod definitum prius cadit in cognitione nostra quam partes definitionis. Sed universaliora sunt partes definitionis minus universalium, sicut animal est pars definitionis hominis. Ergo universalia sunt posterius nota quoad nos.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32033)\n\nPraeterea, per effectus devenimus in causas et principia. Sed universalia sunt quaedam principia. Ergo universalia sunt posterius nota quoad nos.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32034)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in I Physic., quod *ex universalibus in singularia oportet devenire*.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32035)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in cognitione nostri intellectus duo oportet considerare. Primo quidem, quod cognitio intellectiva aliquo modo a sensitiva primordium sumit. Et quia sensus est singularium, intellectus autem universalium; necesse est quod cognitio singularium, quoad nos, prior sit quam universalium cognitio. Secundo oportet considerare quod intellectus noster de potentia in actum procedit. Omne autem quod procedit de potentia in actum, prius pervenit ad actum incompletum, qui est medius inter potentiam et actum, quam ad actum perfectum. Actus autem perfectus ad quem pervenit intellectus, est scientia completa, per quam distincte et determinate res cognoscuntur. Actus autem incompletus est scientia imperfecta, per quam sciuntur res indistincte sub quadam confusione, quod enim sic cognoscitur, secundum quid cognoscitur in actu, et quodammodo in potentia. Unde philosophus dicit, in I Physic., quod *sunt primo nobis manifesta et certa confusa magis; posterius autem cognoscimus distinguendo distincte principia et elementa*. Manifestum est autem quod cognoscere aliquid in quo plura continentur, sine hoc quod habeatur propria notitia uniuscuiusque eorum quae continentur in illo, est cognoscere aliquid sub confusione quadam. Sic autem potest cognosci tam totum universale, in quo partes continentur in potentia, quam etiam totum integrale, utrumque enim totum potest cognosci in quadam confusione, sine hoc quod partes distincte cognoscantur. Cognoscere autem distincte id quod continetur in toto universali, est habere cognitionem de re minus communi. Sicut cognoscere animal indistincte, est cognoscere animal inquantum est animal, cognoscere autem animal distincte, est cognoscere animal inquantum est animal rationale vel irrationale, quod est cognoscere hominem vel leonem. Prius igitur occurrit intellectui nostro cognoscere animal quam cognoscere hominem, et eadem ratio est si comparemus quodcumque magis universale ad minus universale. Et quia sensus exit de potentia in actum sicut et intellectus, idem etiam ordo cognitionis apparet in sensu. Nam prius secundum sensum diiudicamus magis commune quam minus commune, et secundum locum et secundum tempus. Secundum locum quidem, sicut, cum aliquid videtur a remotis, prius deprehenditur esse corpus, quam deprehendatur esse animal; et prius deprehenditur esse animal, quam deprehendatur esse homo; et prius homo, quam Socrates vel Plato. Secundum tempus autem, quia puer a principio prius distinguit hominem a non homine, quam distinguat hunc hominem ab alio homine; et ideo *pueri a principio appellant omnes viros patres, posterius autem determinant unumquemque*, ut dicitur in I Physic. Et huius ratio manifesta est. Quia qui scit aliquid indistincte, adhuc est in potentia ut sciat distinctionis principium; sicut qui scit genus, est in potentia ut sciat differentiam. Et sic patet quod cognitio indistincta media est inter potentiam et actum. Est ergo dicendum quod cognitio singularium est prior quoad nos quam cognitio universalium, sicut cognitio sensitiva quam cognitio intellectiva. Sed tam secundum sensum quam secundum intellectum, cognitio magis communis est prior quam cognitio minus communis.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32036)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod universale dupliciter potest considerari. Uno modo, secundum quod natura universalis consideratur simul cum intentione universalitatis. Et cum intentio universalitatis, ut scilicet unum et idem habeat habitudinem ad multa, proveniat ex abstractione intellectus, oportet quod secundum hunc modum universale sit posterius. Unde in I de anima dicitur quod *animal universale aut nihil est, aut posterius est*. Sed secundum Platonem, qui posuit universalia subsistentia, secundum hanc considerationem universale esset prius quam particularia, quae secundum eum non sunt nisi per participationem universalium subsistentium, quae dicuntur ideae. Alio modo potest considerari quantum ad ipsam naturam, scilicet animalitatis vel humanitatis, prout invenitur in particularibus. Et sic dicendum est quod duplex est ordo naturae. Unus secundum viam generationis et temporis, secundum quam viam, ea quae sunt imperfecta et in potentia, sunt priora. Et hoc modo magis commune est prius secundum naturam, quod apparet manifeste in generatione hominis et animalis; nam prius generatur animal quam homo, ut dicitur in libro de Generat. Animal. Alius est ordo perfectionis, sive intentionis naturae; sicut actus simpliciter est prius secundum naturam quam potentia, et perfectum prius quam imperfectum. Et per hunc modum, minus commune est prius secundum naturam quam magis commune, ut homo quam animal, naturae enim intentio non sistit in generatione animalis, sed intendit generare hominem.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32037)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod universale magis commune comparatur ad minus commune ut totum et ut pars. Ut totum quidem, secundum quod in magis universali non solum continetur in potentia minus universale, sed etiam alia; ut sub animali non solum homo, sed etiam equus. Ut pars autem, secundum quod minus commune continet in sui ratione non solum magis commune, sed etiam alia; ut homo non solum animal, sed etiam rationale. Sic igitur animal consideratum in se, prius est in nostra cognitione quam homo; sed homo est prius in nostra cognitione quam quod animal sit pars rationis eius.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32038)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod pars aliqua dupliciter potest cognosci. Uno modo absolute, secundum quod in se est, et sic nihil prohibet prius cognoscere partes quam totum, ut lapides quam domum. Alio modo, secundum quod sunt partes huius totius, et sic necesse est quod prius cognoscamus totum quam partes; prius enim cognoscimus domum quadam confusa cognitione, quam distinguamus singulas partes eius. Sic igitur dicendum est quod definientia, absolute considerata, sunt prius nota quam definitum, alioquin non notificaretur definitum per ea. Sed secundum quod sunt partes definitionis, sic sunt posterius nota, prius enim cognoscimus hominem quadam confusa cognitione, quam sciamus distinguere omnia quae sunt de hominis ratione.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32039)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod universale, secundum quod accipitur cum intentione universalitatis, est quidem quodammodo principium cognoscendi, prout intentio universalitatis consequitur modum intelligendi qui est per abstractionem. Non autem est necesse quod omne quod est principium cognoscendi, sit principium essendi, ut Plato existimavit, cum quandoque cognoscamus causam per effectum, et substantiam per accidentia. Unde universale sic acceptum, secundum sententiam Aristotelis, non est principium essendi, neque substantia, ut patet in VII Metaphys. Si autem consideremus ipsam naturam generis et speciei prout est in singularibus, sic quodammodo habet rationem principii formalis respectu singularium, nam singulare est propter materiam, ratio autem speciei sumitur ex forma. Sed natura generis comparatur ad naturam speciei magis per modum materialis principii, quia natura generis sumitur ab eo quod est materiale in re, ratio vero speciei ab eo quod est formale; sicut ratio animalis a sensitivo, ratio vero hominis ab intellectivo. Et inde est quod ultima naturae intentio est ad speciem, non autem ad individuum, neque ad genus, quia forma est finis generationis, materia vero est propter formam. Non autem oportet quod cuiuslibet causae vel principii cognitio sit posterior quoad nos, cum quandoque cognoscamus per causas sensibiles, effectus ignotos; quandoque autem e converso.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 85 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32040)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod possimus multa simul intelligere. Intellectus enim est supra tempus. Sed prius et posterius ad tempus pertinent. Ergo intellectus non intelligit diversa secundum prius et posterius, sed simul.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32041)\n\nPraeterea, nihil prohibet diversas formas non oppositas simul eidem actu inesse, sicut odorem et colorem pomo. Sed species intelligibiles non sunt oppositae. Ergo nihil prohibet intellectum unum simul fieri in actu secundum diversas species intelligibiles et sic potest multa simul intelligere.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32042)\n\nPraeterea, intellectus simul intelligit aliquod totum, ut hominem vel domum. Sed in quolibet toto continentur multae partes. Ergo intellectus simul multa intelligit.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32043)\n\nPraeterea, non potest cognosci differentia unius ad alterum, nisi simul utrumque apprehendatur, ut dicitur in libro de anima, et eadem ratio est de quacumque alia comparatione. Sed intellectus noster cognoscit differentiam et comparationem unius ad alterum. Ergo cognoscit multa simul.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32044)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in libro Topic., quod *intelligere est unum solum, scire vero multa*.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32045)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod intellectus quidem potest multa intelligere per modum unius, non autem multa per modum multorum, dico autem per modum unius vel multorum, per unam vel plures species intelligibiles. Nam modus cuiusque actionis consequitur formam quae est actionis principium. Quaecumque ergo intellectus potest intelligere sub una specie, simul intelligere potest, et inde est quod Deus omnia simul videt, quia omnia videt per unum, quod est essentia sua. Quaecumque vero intellectus per diversas species intelligit, non simul intelligit. Et huius ratio est, quia impossibile est idem subiectum perfici simul pluribus formis unius generis et diversarum specierum, sicut impossibile est quod idem corpus secundum idem simul coloretur diversis coloribus, vel figuretur diversis figuris. Omnes autem species intelligibiles sunt unius generis, quia sunt perfectiones unius intellectivae potentiae; licet res quarum sunt species, sint diversorum generum. Impossibile est ergo quod idem intellectus simul perficiatur diversis speciebus intelligibilibus, ad intelligendum diversa in actu.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32046)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod intellectus est supra tempus quod est numerus motus corporalium rerum. Sed ipsa pluralitas specierum intelligibilium causat vicissitudinem quandam intelligibilium operationum, secundum quam una operatio est prior altera. Et hanc vicissitudinem Augustinus nominat tempus, cum dicit, VIII super Gen. ad Litt., quod *Deus movet creaturam spiritualem per tempus*.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32047)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod non solum oppositae formae non possunt esse simul in eodem subiecto, sed nec quaecumque formae eiusdem generis, licet non sint oppositae, sicut patet per exemplum inductum de coloribus et figuris.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32048)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod partes possunt intelligi dupliciter. Uno modo, sub quadam confusione, prout sunt in toto, et sic cognoscuntur per unam formam totius, et sic simul cognoscuntur. Alio modo, cognitione distincta, secundum quod quaelibet cognoscitur per suam speciem, et sic non simul intelliguntur.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32049)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod quando intellectus intelligit differentiam vel comparationem unius ad alterum, cognoscit utrumque differentium vel comparatorum sub ratione ipsius comparationis vel differentiae; sicut dictum est quod cognoscit partes sub ratione totius.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 85 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32050)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus noster non intelligat componendo et dividendo. Compositio enim et divisio non est nisi multorum. Sed intellectus non potest simul multa intelligere. Ergo non potest intelligere componendo et dividendo.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32051)\n\nPraeterea, omni compositioni et divisioni adiungitur tempus praesens, praeteritum vel futurum. Sed intellectus abstrahit a tempore, sicut etiam ab aliis particularibus conditionibus. Ergo intellectus non intelligit componendo et dividendo.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32052)\n\nPraeterea, intellectus intelligit per assimilationem ad res. Sed compositio et divisio nihil est in rebus, nihil enim invenitur in rebus nisi res quae significatur per praedicatum et subiectum, quae est una et eadem si compositio est vera; homo enim est vere id quod est animal. Ergo intellectus non componit et dividit.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32053)\n\nSed contra, voces significant conceptiones intellectus, ut dicit philosophus in I Periherm. Sed in vocibus est compositio et divisio; ut patet in propositionibus affirmativis et negativis. Ergo intellectus componit et dividit.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32054)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod intellectus humanus necesse habet intelligere componendo et dividendo. Cum enim intellectus humanus exeat de potentia in actum, similitudinem quandam habet cum rebus generabilibus, quae non statim perfectionem suam habent, sed eam successive acquirunt. Et similiter intellectus humanus non statim in prima apprehensione capit perfectam rei cognitionem; sed primo apprehendit aliquid de ipsa, puta quidditatem ipsius rei, quae est primum et proprium obiectum intellectus; et deinde intelligit proprietates et accidentia et habitudines circumstantes rei essentiam. Et secundum hoc, necesse habet unum apprehensum alii componere vel dividere; et ex una compositione vel divisione ad aliam procedere, quod est ratiocinari. Intellectus autem angelicus et divinus se habet sicut res incorruptibiles, quae statim a principio habent suam totam perfectionem. Unde intellectus angelicus et divinus statim perfecte totam rei cognitionem habet. Unde in cognoscendo quidditatem rei, cognoscit de re simul quidquid nos cognoscere possumus componendo et dividendo et ratiocinando. Et ideo intellectus humanus cognoscit componendo et dividendo, sicut et ratiocinando. Intellectus autem divinus et angelicus cognoscunt quidem compositionem et divisionem et ratiocinationem, non componendo et dividendo et ratiocinando, sed per intellectum simplicis quidditatis.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32055)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod compositio et divisio intellectus secundum quandam differentiam vel comparationem fit. Unde sic intellectus cognoscit multa componendo et dividendo, sicut cognoscendo differentiam vel comparationem rerum.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32056)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod intellectus et abstrahit a phantasmatibus; et tamen non intelligit actu nisi convertendo se ad phantasmata, sicut supra dictum est. Et ex ea parte qua se ad phantasmata convertit, compositioni et divisioni intellectus adiungitur tempus.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32057)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod similitudo rei recipitur in intellectu secundum modum intellectus, et non secundum modum rei. Unde compositioni et divisioni intellectus respondet quidem aliquid ex parte rei; tamen non eodem modo se habet in re, sicut in intellectu. Intellectus enim humani proprium obiectum est quidditas rei materialis, quae sub sensu et imaginatione cadit. Invenitur autem duplex compositio in re materiali. Prima quidem, formae ad materiam, et huic respondet compositio intellectus qua totum universale de sua parte praedicatur; nam genus sumitur a materia communi, differentia vero completiva speciei a forma, particulare vero a materia individuali. Secunda vero compositio est accidentis ad subiectum, et huic reali compositioni respondet compositio intellectus secundum quam praedicatur accidens de subiecto, ut cum dicitur, homo est albus. Tamen differt compositio intellectus a compositione rei, nam ea quae componuntur in re, sunt diversa; compositio autem intellectus est signum identitatis eorum quae componuntur. Non enim intellectus sic componit, ut dicat quod homo est albedo; sed dicit quod homo est albus, idest habens albedinem, idem autem est subiecto quod est homo, et quod est habens albedinem. Et simile est de compositione formae et materiae, nam animal significat id quod habet naturam sensitivam, rationale vero quod habet naturam intellectivam, homo vero quod habet utrumque, Socrates vero quod habet omnia haec cum materia individuali; et secundum hanc identitatis rationem, intellectus noster unum componit alteri praedicando.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 85 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32058)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus possit esse falsus. Dicit enim philosophus, in VI Metaphys., quod *verum et falsum sunt in mente*. Mens autem et intellectus idem sunt, ut supra dictum est. Ergo falsitas est in intellectu.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32059)\n\nPraeterea, opinio et ratiocinatio ad intellectum pertinent. Sed in utraque istarum invenitur falsitas. Ergo potest esse falsitas in intellectu.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32060)\n\nPraeterea, peccatum in parte intellectiva est. Sed peccatum cum falsitate est, errant enim qui operantur malum, ut dicitur Prov. XIV. Ergo falsitas potest esse in intellectu.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32061)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod *omnis qui fallitur, id in quo fallitur non intelligit*. Et philosophus dicit, in libro de anima, quod *intellectus semper est rectus*.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32062)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod philosophus, in III de anima, comparat, quantum ad hoc, intellectum sensui. Sensus enim circa proprium obiectum non decipitur, sicut visus circa colorem; nisi forte per accidens, ex impedimento circa organum contingente, sicut cum gustus febrientium dulcia iudicat amara, propter hoc quod lingua malis humoribus est repleta. Circa sensibilia vero communia decipitur sensus, sicut in diiudicando de magnitudine vel figura; ut cum iudicat solem esse pedalem, qui tamen est maior terra. Et multo magis decipitur circa sensibilia per accidens; ut cum iudicat fel esse mel, propter coloris similitudinem. Et huius ratio est in evidenti. Quia ad proprium obiectum unaquaeque potentia per se ordinatur, secundum quod ipsa. Quae autem sunt huiusmodi, semper eodem modo se habent. Unde manente potentia, non deficit eius iudicium circa proprium obiectum. Obiectum autem proprium intellectus est quidditas rei. Unde circa quidditatem rei, per se loquendo, intellectus non fallitur. Sed circa ea quae circumstant rei essentiam vel quidditatem, intellectus potest falli, dum unum ordinat ad aliud, vel componendo vel dividendo vel etiam ratiocinando. Et propter hoc etiam circa illas propositiones errare non potest, quae statim cognoscuntur cognita terminorum quidditate, sicut accidit circa prima principia, ex quibus etiam accidit infallibilitas veritatis, secundum certitudinem scientiae, circa conclusiones. Per accidens tamen contingit intellectum decipi circa quod quid est in rebus compositis; non ex parte organi, quia intellectus non est virtus utens organo; sed ex parte compositionis intervenientis circa definitionem, dum vel definitio unius rei est falsa de alia, sicut definitio circuli de triangulo, vel dum aliqua definitio in seipsa est falsa, implicans compositionem impossibilium, ut si accipiatur hoc ut definitio alicuius rei, animal rationale alatum. Unde in rebus simplicibus, in quarum definitionibus compositio intervenire non potest, non possumus decipi; sed deficimus in totaliter non attingendo, sicut dicitur in IX Metaphys.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32063)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod falsitatem dicit esse philosophus in mente secundum compositionem et divisionem.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32064)\n\nEt similiter dicendum est ad secundum, de opinione et ratiocinatione.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32065)\n\nEt ad tertium, de errore peccantium, qui consistit in applicatione ad appetibile. Sed in absoluta consideratione quidditatis rei, et eorum quae per eam cognoscuntur, intellectus nunquam decipitur. Et sic loquuntur auctoritates in contrarium inductae.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 85 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32066)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod unam et eandem rem unus alio melius intelligere non possit. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., *quisquis ullam rem aliter quam est intelligit non eam intelligit. Quare non est dubitandum esse perfectam intelligentiam, qua praestantior esse non possit; et ideo non per infinitum ire quod quaelibet res intelligitur; nec eam posse alium alio plus intelligere*.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32067)\n\nPraeterea, intellectus intelligendo verus est. Veritas autem, cum sit aequalitas quaedam intellectus et rei, non recipit magis et minus, non enim proprie dicitur aliquid magis et minus aequale. Ergo neque magis et minus aliquid intelligi dicitur.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32068)\n\nPraeterea, intellectus est id quod est formalissimum in homine. Sed differentia formae causat differentiam speciei. Si igitur unus homo magis alio intelligit, videtur quod non sint unius speciei.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32069)\n\nSed contra est quod per experimentum inveniuntur aliqui aliis profundius intelligentes; sicut profundius intelligit qui conclusionem aliquam potest reducere in prima principia et causas primas, quam qui potest reducere solum in causas proximas.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32070)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod aliquem intelligere unam et eandem rem magis quam alium, potest intelligi dupliciter. Uno modo, sic quod ly magis determinet actum intelligendi ex parte rei intellectae. Et sic non potest unus eandem rem magis intelligere quam alius, quia si intelligeret eam aliter esse quam sit, vel melius vel peius, falleretur, et non intelligeret ut arguit Augustinus. Alio modo potest intelligi ut determinet actum intelligendi ex parte intelligentis. Et sic unus alio potest eandem rem melius intelligere, quia est melioris virtutis in intelligendo; sicut melius videt visione corporali rem aliquam qui est perfectioris virtutis, et in quo virtus visiva est perfectior. Hoc autem circa intellectum contingit dupliciter. Uno quidem modo, ex parte ipsius intellectus, qui est perfectior. Manifestum est enim quod quanto corpus est melius dispositum, tanto meliorem sortitur animam, quod manifeste apparet in his quae sunt secundum speciem diversa. Cuius ratio est, quia actus et forma recipitur in materia secundum materiae capacitatem. Unde cum etiam in hominibus quidam habeant corpus melius dispositum, sortiuntur animam maioris virtutis in intelligendo, unde dicitur in II de anima quod *molles carne bene aptos mente videmus*. Alio modo contingit hoc ex parte inferiorum virtutum, quibus intellectus indiget ad sui operationem, illi enim in quibus virtus imaginativa et cogitativa et memorativa est melius disposita, sunt melius dispositi ad intelligendum.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32071)\n\nAd primum ergo patet solutio ex dictis.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32072)\n\nEt similiter ad secundum, veritas enim intellectus in hoc consistit, quod intelligatur res esse sicuti est.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32073)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod differentia formae quae non provenit nisi ex diversa dispositione materiae, non facit diversitatem secundum speciem, sed solum secundum numerum; sunt enim diversorum individuorum diversae formae, secundum materiam diversificatae.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 85 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32074)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus noster per prius cognoscat indivisibile quam divisibile. Dicit enim philosophus, in I Physic., quod *intelligimus et scimus ex principiorum et elementorum cognitione*. Sed indivisibilia sunt principia et elementa divisibilium. Ergo per prius sunt nobis nota indivisibilia quam divisibilia.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32075)\n\nPraeterea, id quod ponitur in definitione alicuius, per prius cognoscitur a nobis, quia definitio est ex prioribus et notioribus, ut dicitur in VI Topic. Sed indivisibile ponitur in definitione divisibilis, sicut *linea enim*, ut Euclides dicit, *est longitudo sine latitudine, cuius extremitates sunt duo puncta*. Et unitas ponitur in definitione numeri, quia numerus est multitudo mensurata per unum, ut dicitur in X Metaphys. Ergo intellectus noster per prius intelligit indivisibile quam divisibile.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32076)\n\nPraeterea, simile simili cognoscitur. Sed indivisibile est magis simile intellectui quam divisibile, quia intellectus est simplex, ut dicitur in III de anima. Ergo intellectus noster prius cognoscit indivisibile.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32077)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in III de anima, quod *indivisibile monstratur sicut privatio*. Sed privatio per posterius cognoscitur. Ergo et indivisibile.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32078)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod obiectum intellectus nostri, secundum praesentem statum, est quidditas rei materialis, quam a phantasmatibus abstrahit, ut ex praemissis patet. Et quia id quod est primo et per se cognitum a virtute cognoscitiva, est proprium eius obiectum, considerari potest quo ordine indivisibile intelligatur a nobis, ex eius habitudine ad huiusmodi quidditatem. Dicitur autem indivisibile tripliciter, ut dicitur in III de anima. Uno modo, sicut continuum est indivisibile, quia est indivisum in actu, licet sit divisibile in potentia. Et huiusmodi indivisibile prius est intellectum a nobis quam eius divisio, quae est in partes, quia cognitio confusa est prior quam distincta, ut dictum est. Alio modo dicitur indivisibile secundum speciem, sicut ratio hominis est quoddam indivisibile. Et hoc etiam modo indivisibile est prius intellectum quam divisio eius in partes rationis, ut supra dictum est, et iterum prius quam intellectus componat et dividat, affirmando vel negando. Et huius ratio est, quia huiusmodi duplex indivisibile intellectus secundum se intelligit, sicut proprium obiectum. Tertio modo dicitur indivisibile quod est omnino indivisibile, ut punctus et unitas, quae nec actu nec potentia dividuntur. Et huiusmodi indivisibile per posterius cognoscitur, per privationem divisibilis. Unde punctum privative definitur, punctum est cuius pars non est, et similiter ratio unius est quod sit indivisibile, ut dicitur in X Metaphys. Et huius ratio est, quia tale indivisibile habet quandam oppositionem ad rem corporalem, cuius quidditatem primo et per se intellectus accipit. Si autem intellectus noster intelligeret per participationem indivisibilium separatorum, ut Platonici posuerunt, sequeretur quod indivisibile huiusmodi esset primo intellectum, quia secundum Platonicos, priora prius participantur a rebus.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32079)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in accipiendo scientiam, non semper principia et elementa sunt priora, quia quandoque ex effectibus sensibilibus devenimus in cognitionem principiorum et causarum intelligibilium. Sed in complemento scientiae, semper scientia effectuum dependet ex cognitione principiorum et elementorum, quia, ut ibidem dicit philosophus, *tunc opinamur nos scire, cum principiata possumus in causas resolvere*.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32080)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod punctum non ponitur in definitione lineae communiter sumptae, manifestum est enim quod in linea infinita, et etiam in circulari, non est punctum nisi in potentia. Sed Euclides definit lineam finitam rectam, et ideo posuit punctum in definitione lineae, sicut terminum in definitione terminati. Unitas vero est mensura numeri, et ideo ponitur in definitione numeri mensurati. Non autem ponitur in definitione divisibilis, sed magis e converso.\n\n[I q. 85 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32081)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod similitudo per quam intelligimus, est species cogniti in cognoscente. Et ideo non secundum similitudinem naturae ad potentiam cognoscitivam est aliquid prius cognitum, sed per convenientiam ad obiectum, alioquin magis visus cognosceret auditum quam colorem.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0002","text":"[I q. 85 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32009)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de modo et ordine intelligendi. Et circa hoc quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum intellectus noster intelligat abstrahendo species a phantasmatibus. Secundo, utrum species intelligibiles abstractae a phantasmatibus, se habeant ad intellectum nostrum ut quod intelligitur, vel sicut id quo intelligitur. Tertio, utrum intellectus noster naturaliter intelligat prius magis universale. Quarto, utrum intellectus noster possit multa simul intelligere. Quinto, utrum intellectus noster intelligat componendo et dividendo. Sexto, utrum intellectus possit errare. Septimo, utrum unus possit eandem rem melius intelligere quam alius. Octavo, utrum intellectus noster per prius cognoscat indivisibile quam divisibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0005","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32010)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus noster non intelligat res corporeas et materiales per abstractionem a phantasmatibus. Quicumque enim intellectus intelligit rem aliter quam sit, est falsus. Formae autem rerum materialium non sunt abstractae a particularibus, quorum similitudines sunt phantasmata. Si ergo intelligamus res materiales per abstractionem specierum a phantasmatibus, erit falsitas in intellectu nostro.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0007","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32011)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0008","text":"Praeterea, res materiales sunt res naturales, in quarum definitione cadit materia. Sed nihil potest intelligi sine eo quod cadit in definitione eius. Ergo res materiales non possunt intelligi sine materia. Sed materia est individuationis principium. Ergo res materiales non possunt intelligi per abstractionem universalis a particulari, quod est abstrahere species intelligibiles a phantasmatibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0009","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32012)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0010","text":"Praeterea, in III de anima dicitur quod phantasmata se habent ad animam intellectivam sicut colores ad visum. Sed visio non fit per abstractionem aliquarum specierum a coloribus, sed per hoc quod colores imprimunt in visum. Ergo nec intelligere contingit per hoc quod aliquid abstrahatur a phantasmatibus, sed per hoc quod phantasmata imprimunt in intellectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0011","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32013)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0012","text":"Praeterea, ut dicitur in III de anima, in intellectiva anima sunt duo, scilicet intellectus possibilis, et agens. Sed abstrahere a phantasmatibus species intelligibiles non pertinet ad intellectum possibilem, sed recipere species iam abstractas. Sed nec etiam videtur pertinere ad intellectum agentem, qui se habet ad phantasmata sicut lumen ad colores, quod non abstrahit aliquid a coloribus, sed magis eis influit. Ergo nullo modo intelligimus abstrahendo a phantasmatibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0013","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32014)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0014","text":"Praeterea, philosophus, in III de anima, dicit quod *intellectus intelligit species in phantasmatibus*. Non ergo eas abstrahendo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0015","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32015)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0016","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in III de anima, quod *sicut res sunt separabiles a materia, sic circa intellectum sunt*. Ergo oportet quod materialia intelligantur inquantum a materia abstrahuntur, et a similitudinibus materialibus, quae sunt phantasmata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0017","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32016)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0018","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, obiectum cognoscibile proportionatur virtuti cognoscitivae. Est autem triplex gradus cognoscitivae virtutis. Quaedam enim cognoscitiva virtus est actus organi corporalis, scilicet sensus. Et ideo obiectum cuiuslibet sensitivae potentiae est forma prout in materia corporali existit. Et quia huiusmodi materia est individuationis principium, ideo omnis potentia sensitivae partis est cognoscitiva particularium tantum. Quaedam autem virtus cognoscitiva est quae neque est actus organi corporalis, neque est aliquo modo corporali materiae coniuncta, sicut intellectus angelicus. Et ideo huius virtutis cognoscitivae obiectum est forma sine materia subsistens, etsi enim materialia cognoscant, non tamen nisi in immaterialibus ea intuentur, scilicet vel in seipsis vel in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0019","text":"Intellectus autem humanus medio modo se habet, non enim est actus alicuius organi, sed tamen est quaedam virtus animae, quae est forma corporis, ut ex supra dictis patet. Et ideo proprium eius est cognoscere formam in materia quidem corporali individualiter existentem, non tamen prout est in tali materia. Cognoscere vero id quod est in materia individuali, non prout est in tali materia, est abstrahere formam a materia individuali, quam repraesentant phantasmata. Et ideo necesse est dicere quod intellectus noster intelligit materialia abstrahendo a phantasmatibus; et per materialia sic considerata in immaterialium aliqualem cognitionem devenimus, sicut e contra Angeli per immaterialia materialia cognoscunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0020","text":"Plato vero, attendens solum ad immaterialitatem intellectus humani, non autem ad hoc quod est corpori quodammodo unitus, posuit obiectum intellectus ideas separatas; et quod intelligimus, non quidem abstrahendo, sed magis abstracta participando, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0021","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32017)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0022","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod abstrahere contingit dupliciter. Uno modo, per modum compositionis et divisionis; sicut cum intelligimus aliquid non esse in alio, vel esse separatum ab eo. Alio modo, per modum simplicis et absolutae considerationis; sicut cum intelligimus unum, nihil considerando de alio. Abstrahere igitur per intellectum ea quae secundum rem non sunt abstracta, secundum primum modum abstrahendi, non est absque falsitate. Sed secundo modo abstrahere per intellectum quae non sunt abstracta secundum rem, non habet falsitatem; ut in sensibilibus manifeste apparet. Si enim intelligamus vel dicamus colorem non inesse corpori colorato, vel esse separatum ab eo, erit falsitas in opinione vel in oratione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0023","text":"Si vero consideremus colorem et proprietates eius, nihil considerantes de pomo colorato; vel quod sic intelligimus, etiam voce exprimamus; erit absque falsitate opinionis et orationis. Pomum enim non est de ratione coloris; et ideo nihil prohibet colorem intelligi, nihil intelligendo de pomo. Similiter dico quod ea quae pertinent ad rationem speciei cuiuslibet rei materialis, puta lapidis aut hominis aut equi, possunt considerari sine principiis individualibus, quae non sunt de ratione speciei. Et hoc est abstrahere universale a particulari, vel speciem intelligibilem a phantasmatibus, considerare scilicet naturam speciei absque consideratione individualium principiorum, quae per phantasmata repraesentantur. Cum ergo dicitur quod intellectus est falsus qui intelligit rem aliter quam sit, verum est si ly aliter referatur ad rem intellectam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0024","text":"Tunc enim intellectus est falsus, quando intelligit rem esse aliter quam sit. Unde falsus esset intellectus, si sic abstraheret speciem lapidis a materia, ut intelligeret eam non esse in materia, ut Plato posuit. Non est autem verum quod proponitur, si ly aliter accipiatur ex parte intelligentis. Est enim absque falsitate ut alius sit modus intelligentis in intelligendo, quam modus rei in existendo, quia intellectum est in intelligente immaterialiter, per modum intellectus; non autem materialiter, per modum rei materialis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0025","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32018)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0026","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod quidam putaverunt quod species rei naturalis sit forma solum, et quod materia non sit pars speciei. Sed secundum hoc, in definitionibus rerum naturalium non poneretur materia. Et ideo aliter dicendum est, quod materia est duplex, scilicet communis, et signata vel individualis, communis quidem, ut caro et os; individualis autem, ut hae carnes et haec ossa. Intellectus igitur abstrahit speciem rei naturalis a materia sensibili individuali, non autem a materia sensibili communi. Sicut speciem hominis abstrahit ab his carnibus et his ossibus, quae non sunt de ratione speciei, sed sunt partes individui, ut dicitur in VII Metaphys.; et ideo sine eis considerari potest. Sed species hominis non potest abstrahi per intellectum a carnibus et ossibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0027","text":"Species autem mathematicae possunt abstrahi per intellectum a materia sensibili non solum individuali, sed etiam communi; non tamen a materia intelligibili communi, sed solum individuali. Materia enim sensibilis dicitur materia corporalis secundum quod subiacet qualitatibus sensibilibus, scilicet calido et frigido, duro et molli, et huiusmodi. Materia vero intelligibilis dicitur substantia secundum quod subiacet quantitati. Manifestum est autem quod quantitas prius inest substantiae quam qualitates sensibiles. Unde quantitates, ut numeri et dimensiones et figurae, quae sunt terminationes quantitatum, possunt considerari absque qualitatibus sensibilibus, quod est eas abstrahi a materia sensibili, non tamen possunt considerari sine intellectu substantiae quantitati subiectae, quod esset eas abstrahi a materia intelligibili communi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0028","text":"Possunt tamen considerari sine hac vel illa substantia; quod est eas abstrahi a materia intelligibili individuali. Quaedam vero sunt quae possunt abstrahi etiam a materia intelligibili communi, sicut ens, unum, potentia et actus, et alia huiusmodi, quae etiam esse possunt absque omni materia, ut patet in substantiis immaterialibus. Et quia Plato non consideravit quod dictum est de duplici modo abstractionis, omnia quae diximus abstrahi per intellectum, posuit abstracta esse secundum rem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0029","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32019)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0030","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod colores habent eundem modum existendi prout sunt in materia corporali individuali, sicut et potentia visiva, et ideo possunt imprimere suam similitudinem in visum. Sed phantasmata, cum sint similitudines individuorum, et existant in organis corporeis, non habent eundem modum existendi quem habet intellectus humanus, ut ex dictis patet; et ideo non possunt sua virtute imprimere in intellectum possibilem. Sed virtute intellectus agentis resultat quaedam similitudo in intellectu possibili ex conversione intellectus agentis supra phantasmata, quae quidem est repraesentativa eorum quorum sunt phantasmata, solum quantum ad naturam speciei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0031","text":"Et per hunc modum dicitur abstrahi species intelligibilis a phantasmatibus, non quod aliqua eadem numero forma, quae prius fuit in phantasmatibus, postmodum fiat in intellectu possibili, ad modum quo corpus accipitur ab uno loco et transfertur ad alterum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0032","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32020)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0033","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod phantasmata et illuminantur ab intellectu agente; et iterum ab eis, per virtutem intellectus agentis, species intelligibiles abstrahuntur. Illuminantur quidem, quia, sicut pars sensitiva ex coniunctione ad intellectivam efficitur virtuosior, ita phantasmata ex virtute intellectus agentis redduntur habilia ut ab eis intentiones intelligibiles abstrahantur. Abstrahit autem intellectus agens species intelligibiles a phantasmatibus, inquantum per virtutem intellectus agentis accipere possumus in nostra consideratione naturas specierum sine individualibus conditionibus, secundum quarum similitudines intellectus possibilis informatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0034","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32021)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0035","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod intellectus noster et abstrahit species intelligibiles a phantasmatibus, inquantum considerat naturas rerum in universali; et tamen intelligit eas in phantasmatibus, quia non potest intelligere etiam ea quorum species abstrahit, nisi convertendo se ad phantasmata, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0036","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0037","text":"[I q. 85 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32022)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0038","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod species intelligibiles a phantasmatibus abstractae, se habeant ad intellectum nostrum sicut id quod intelligitur. Intellectum enim in actu est in intelligente, quia intellectum in actu est ipse intellectus in actu. Sed nihil de re intellecta est in intellectu actu intelligente, nisi species intelligibilis abstracta. Ergo huiusmodi species est ipsum intellectum in actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0039","text":"[I q. 85 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32023)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0040","text":"Praeterea, intellectum in actu oportet in aliquo esse, alioquin nihil esset. Sed non est in re quae est extra animam, quia, cum res extra animam sit materialis, nihil quod est in ea, potest esse intellectum in actu. Relinquitur ergo quod intellectum in actu sit in intellectu. Et ita nihil est aliud quam species intelligibilis praedicta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0041","text":"[I q. 85 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32024)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0042","text":"Praeterea, philosophus dicit, in I Periherm., quod *voces sunt notae earum quae sunt in anima passionum*. Sed voces significant res intellectas, id enim voce significamus quod intelligimus. Ergo ipsae passiones animae, scilicet species intelligibiles, sunt ea quae intelliguntur in actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0043","text":"[I q. 85 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32025)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0044","text":"Sed contra, species intelligibilis se habet ad intellectum, sicut species sensibilis ad sensum. Sed species sensibilis non est illud quod sentitur, sed magis id quo sensus sentit. Ergo species intelligibilis non est quod intelligitur actu, sed id quo intelligit intellectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0045","text":"[I q. 85 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32026)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0046","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam posuerunt quod vires cognoscitivae quae sunt in nobis, nihil cognoscunt nisi proprias passiones; puta quod sensus non sentit nisi passionem sui organi. Et secundum hoc, intellectus nihil intelligit nisi suam passionem, idest speciem intelligibilem in se receptam. Et secundum hoc, species huiusmodi est ipsum quod intelligitur. Sed haec opinio manifeste apparet falsa ex duobus. Primo quidem, quia eadem sunt quae intelligimus, et de quibus sunt scientiae. Si igitur ea quae intelligimus essent solum species quae sunt in anima, sequeretur quod scientiae omnes non essent de rebus quae sunt extra animam, sed solum de speciebus intelligibilibus quae sunt in anima; sicut secundum Platonicos omnes scientiae sunt de ideis, quas ponebant esse intellecta in actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0047","text":"Secundo, quia sequeretur error antiquorum dicentium quod *omne quod videtur est verum*; et sic quod contradictoriae essent simul verae. Si enim potentia non cognoscit nisi propriam passionem, de ea solum iudicat. Sic autem videtur aliquid, secundum quod potentia cognoscitiva afficitur. Semper ergo iudicium potentiae cognoscitivae erit de eo quod iudicat, scilicet de propria passione, secundum quod est; et ita omne iudicium erit verum. Puta si gustus non sentit nisi propriam passionem, cum aliquis habens sanum gustum iudicat mel esse dulce, vere iudicabit; et similiter si ille qui habet gustum infectum, iudicet mel esse amarum, vere iudicabit, uterque enim iudicat secundum quod gustus eius afficitur. Et sic sequitur quod omnis opinio aequaliter erit vera, et universaliter omnis acceptio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0048","text":"Et ideo dicendum est quod species intelligibilis se habet ad intellectum ut quo intelligit intellectus. Quod sic patet. Cum enim sit duplex actio, sicut dicitur IX Metaphys., una quae manet in agente, ut videre et intelligere, altera quae transit in rem exteriorem, ut calefacere et secare; utraque fit secundum aliquam formam. Et sicut forma secundum quam provenit actio tendens in rem exteriorem, est similitudo obiecti actionis, ut calor calefacientis est similitudo calefacti; similiter forma secundum quam provenit actio manens in agente, est similitudo obiecti. Unde similitudo rei visibilis est secundum quam visus videt; et similitudo rei intellectae, quae est species intelligibilis, est forma secundum quam intellectus intelligit. Sed quia intellectus supra seipsum reflectitur, secundum eandem reflexionem intelligit et suum intelligere, et speciem qua intelligit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0049","text":"Et sic species intellectiva secundario est id quod intelligitur. Sed id quod intelligitur primo, est res cuius species intelligibilis est similitudo. Et hoc etiam patet ex antiquorum opinione, qui ponebant simile simili cognosci. Ponebant enim quod anima per terram quae in ipsa erat, cognosceret terram quae extra ipsam erat; et sic de aliis. Si ergo accipiamus speciem terrae loco terrae, secundum doctrinam Aristotelis, qui dicit quod *lapis non est in anima, sed species lapidis*; sequetur quod anima per species intelligibiles cognoscat res quae sunt extra animam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0050","text":"[I q. 85 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32027)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0051","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod intellectum est in intelligente per suam similitudinem. Et per hunc modum dicitur quod intellectum in actu est intellectus in actu, inquantum similitudo rei intellectae est forma intellectus; sicut similitudo rei sensibilis est forma sensus in actu. Unde non sequitur quod species intelligibilis abstracta sit id quod actu intelligitur, sed quod sit similitudo eius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0052","text":"[I q. 85 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32028)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0053","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, cum dicitur intellectum in actu, duo importantur, scilicet res quae intelligitur, et hoc quod est ipsum intelligi. Et similiter cum dicitur universale abstractum, duo intelliguntur, scilicet ipsa natura rei, et abstractio seu universalitas. Ipsa igitur natura cui accidit vel intelligi vel abstrahi, vel intentio universalitatis, non est nisi in singularibus; sed hoc ipsum quod est intelligi vel abstrahi, vel intentio universalitatis, est in intellectu. Et hoc possumus videre per simile in sensu. Visus enim videt colorem pomi sine eius odore. Si ergo quaeratur ubi sit color qui videtur sine odore manifestum est quod color qui videtur, non est nisi in pomo; sed quod sit sine odore perceptus, hoc accidit ei ex parte visus, inquantum in visu est similitudo coloris et non odoris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0054","text":"Similiter humanitas quae intelligitur, non est nisi in hoc vel in illo homine, sed quod humanitas apprehendatur sine individualibus conditionibus, quod est ipsam abstrahi, ad quod sequitur intentio universalitatis, accidit humanitatis secundum quod percipitur ab intellectu, in quo est similitudo naturae speciei, et non individualium principiorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0055","text":"[I q. 85 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32029)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0056","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in parte sensitiva invenitur duplex operatio. Una secundum solam immutationem, et sic perficitur operatio sensus per hoc quod immutatur a sensibili. Alia operatio est formatio, secundum quod vis imaginativa format sibi aliquod idolum rei absentis, vel etiam nunquam visae. Et utraque haec operatio coniungitur in intellectu. Nam primo quidem consideratur passio intellectus possibilis secundum quod informatur specie intelligibili. Qua quidem formatus, format secundo vel definitionem vel divisionem vel compositionem, quae per vocem significatur. Unde ratio quam significat nomen, est definitio; et enuntiatio significat compositionem et divisionem intellectus. Non ergo voces significant ipsas species intelligibiles; sed ea quae intellectus sibi format ad iudicandum de rebus exterioribus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0057","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0058","text":"[I q. 85 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32030)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0059","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod magis universalia non sint priora in nostra cognitione intellectuali. Quia ea quae sunt priora et notiora secundum naturam, sunt posteriora et minus nota secundum nos. Sed universalia sunt priora secundum naturam, quia prius est a quo non convertitur subsistendi consequentia. Ergo universalia sunt posteriora in cognitione nostri intellectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0060","text":"[I q. 85 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32031)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0061","text":"Praeterea, composita sunt priora quoad nos quam simplicia. Sed universalia sunt simpliciora. Ergo sunt posterius nota quoad nos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0062","text":"[I q. 85 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32032)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0063","text":"Praeterea, philosophus dicit, in I Physic. quod definitum prius cadit in cognitione nostra quam partes definitionis. Sed universaliora sunt partes definitionis minus universalium, sicut animal est pars definitionis hominis. Ergo universalia sunt posterius nota quoad nos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0064","text":"[I q. 85 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32033)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0065","text":"Praeterea, per effectus devenimus in causas et principia. Sed universalia sunt quaedam principia. Ergo universalia sunt posterius nota quoad nos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0066","text":"[I q. 85 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32034)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0067","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in I Physic., quod *ex universalibus in singularia oportet devenire*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0068","text":"[I q. 85 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32035)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0069","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in cognitione nostri intellectus duo oportet considerare. Primo quidem, quod cognitio intellectiva aliquo modo a sensitiva primordium sumit. Et quia sensus est singularium, intellectus autem universalium; necesse est quod cognitio singularium, quoad nos, prior sit quam universalium cognitio. Secundo oportet considerare quod intellectus noster de potentia in actum procedit. Omne autem quod procedit de potentia in actum, prius pervenit ad actum incompletum, qui est medius inter potentiam et actum, quam ad actum perfectum. Actus autem perfectus ad quem pervenit intellectus, est scientia completa, per quam distincte et determinate res cognoscuntur. Actus autem incompletus est scientia imperfecta, per quam sciuntur res indistincte sub quadam confusione, quod enim sic cognoscitur, secundum quid cognoscitur in actu, et quodammodo in potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0070","text":"Unde philosophus dicit, in I Physic., quod *sunt primo nobis manifesta et certa confusa magis; posterius autem cognoscimus distinguendo distincte principia et elementa*. Manifestum est autem quod cognoscere aliquid in quo plura continentur, sine hoc quod habeatur propria notitia uniuscuiusque eorum quae continentur in illo, est cognoscere aliquid sub confusione quadam. Sic autem potest cognosci tam totum universale, in quo partes continentur in potentia, quam etiam totum integrale, utrumque enim totum potest cognosci in quadam confusione, sine hoc quod partes distincte cognoscantur. Cognoscere autem distincte id quod continetur in toto universali, est habere cognitionem de re minus communi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0071","text":"Sicut cognoscere animal indistincte, est cognoscere animal inquantum est animal, cognoscere autem animal distincte, est cognoscere animal inquantum est animal rationale vel irrationale, quod est cognoscere hominem vel leonem. Prius igitur occurrit intellectui nostro cognoscere animal quam cognoscere hominem, et eadem ratio est si comparemus quodcumque magis universale ad minus universale. Et quia sensus exit de potentia in actum sicut et intellectus, idem etiam ordo cognitionis apparet in sensu. Nam prius secundum sensum diiudicamus magis commune quam minus commune, et secundum locum et secundum tempus. Secundum locum quidem, sicut, cum aliquid videtur a remotis, prius deprehenditur esse corpus, quam deprehendatur esse animal; et prius deprehenditur esse animal, quam deprehendatur esse homo; et prius homo, quam Socrates vel Plato.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0072","text":"Secundum tempus autem, quia puer a principio prius distinguit hominem a non homine, quam distinguat hunc hominem ab alio homine; et ideo *pueri a principio appellant omnes viros patres, posterius autem determinant unumquemque*, ut dicitur in I Physic. Et huius ratio manifesta est. Quia qui scit aliquid indistincte, adhuc est in potentia ut sciat distinctionis principium; sicut qui scit genus, est in potentia ut sciat differentiam. Et sic patet quod cognitio indistincta media est inter potentiam et actum. Est ergo dicendum quod cognitio singularium est prior quoad nos quam cognitio universalium, sicut cognitio sensitiva quam cognitio intellectiva. Sed tam secundum sensum quam secundum intellectum, cognitio magis communis est prior quam cognitio minus communis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0073","text":"[I q. 85 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32036)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0074","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod universale dupliciter potest considerari. Uno modo, secundum quod natura universalis consideratur simul cum intentione universalitatis. Et cum intentio universalitatis, ut scilicet unum et idem habeat habitudinem ad multa, proveniat ex abstractione intellectus, oportet quod secundum hunc modum universale sit posterius. Unde in I de anima dicitur quod *animal universale aut nihil est, aut posterius est*. Sed secundum Platonem, qui posuit universalia subsistentia, secundum hanc considerationem universale esset prius quam particularia, quae secundum eum non sunt nisi per participationem universalium subsistentium, quae dicuntur ideae. Alio modo potest considerari quantum ad ipsam naturam, scilicet animalitatis vel humanitatis, prout invenitur in particularibus. Et sic dicendum est quod duplex est ordo naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0075","text":"Unus secundum viam generationis et temporis, secundum quam viam, ea quae sunt imperfecta et in potentia, sunt priora. Et hoc modo magis commune est prius secundum naturam, quod apparet manifeste in generatione hominis et animalis; nam prius generatur animal quam homo, ut dicitur in libro de Generat. Animal. Alius est ordo perfectionis, sive intentionis naturae; sicut actus simpliciter est prius secundum naturam quam potentia, et perfectum prius quam imperfectum. Et per hunc modum, minus commune est prius secundum naturam quam magis commune, ut homo quam animal, naturae enim intentio non sistit in generatione animalis, sed intendit generare hominem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0076","text":"[I q. 85 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32037)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0077","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod universale magis commune comparatur ad minus commune ut totum et ut pars. Ut totum quidem, secundum quod in magis universali non solum continetur in potentia minus universale, sed etiam alia; ut sub animali non solum homo, sed etiam equus. Ut pars autem, secundum quod minus commune continet in sui ratione non solum magis commune, sed etiam alia; ut homo non solum animal, sed etiam rationale. Sic igitur animal consideratum in se, prius est in nostra cognitione quam homo; sed homo est prius in nostra cognitione quam quod animal sit pars rationis eius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0078","text":"[I q. 85 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32038)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0079","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod pars aliqua dupliciter potest cognosci. Uno modo absolute, secundum quod in se est, et sic nihil prohibet prius cognoscere partes quam totum, ut lapides quam domum. Alio modo, secundum quod sunt partes huius totius, et sic necesse est quod prius cognoscamus totum quam partes; prius enim cognoscimus domum quadam confusa cognitione, quam distinguamus singulas partes eius. Sic igitur dicendum est quod definientia, absolute considerata, sunt prius nota quam definitum, alioquin non notificaretur definitum per ea. Sed secundum quod sunt partes definitionis, sic sunt posterius nota, prius enim cognoscimus hominem quadam confusa cognitione, quam sciamus distinguere omnia quae sunt de hominis ratione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0080","text":"[I q. 85 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32039)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0081","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod universale, secundum quod accipitur cum intentione universalitatis, est quidem quodammodo principium cognoscendi, prout intentio universalitatis consequitur modum intelligendi qui est per abstractionem. Non autem est necesse quod omne quod est principium cognoscendi, sit principium essendi, ut Plato existimavit, cum quandoque cognoscamus causam per effectum, et substantiam per accidentia. Unde universale sic acceptum, secundum sententiam Aristotelis, non est principium essendi, neque substantia, ut patet in VII Metaphys. Si autem consideremus ipsam naturam generis et speciei prout est in singularibus, sic quodammodo habet rationem principii formalis respectu singularium, nam singulare est propter materiam, ratio autem speciei sumitur ex forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0082","text":"Sed natura generis comparatur ad naturam speciei magis per modum materialis principii, quia natura generis sumitur ab eo quod est materiale in re, ratio vero speciei ab eo quod est formale; sicut ratio animalis a sensitivo, ratio vero hominis ab intellectivo. Et inde est quod ultima naturae intentio est ad speciem, non autem ad individuum, neque ad genus, quia forma est finis generationis, materia vero est propter formam. Non autem oportet quod cuiuslibet causae vel principii cognitio sit posterior quoad nos, cum quandoque cognoscamus per causas sensibiles, effectus ignotos; quandoque autem e converso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0083","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0084","text":"[I q. 85 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32040)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0085","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod possimus multa simul intelligere. Intellectus enim est supra tempus. Sed prius et posterius ad tempus pertinent. Ergo intellectus non intelligit diversa secundum prius et posterius, sed simul.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0086","text":"[I q. 85 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32041)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0087","text":"Praeterea, nihil prohibet diversas formas non oppositas simul eidem actu inesse, sicut odorem et colorem pomo. Sed species intelligibiles non sunt oppositae. Ergo nihil prohibet intellectum unum simul fieri in actu secundum diversas species intelligibiles et sic potest multa simul intelligere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0088","text":"[I q. 85 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32042)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0089","text":"Praeterea, intellectus simul intelligit aliquod totum, ut hominem vel domum. Sed in quolibet toto continentur multae partes. Ergo intellectus simul multa intelligit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0090","text":"[I q. 85 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32043)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0091","text":"Praeterea, non potest cognosci differentia unius ad alterum, nisi simul utrumque apprehendatur, ut dicitur in libro de anima, et eadem ratio est de quacumque alia comparatione. Sed intellectus noster cognoscit differentiam et comparationem unius ad alterum. Ergo cognoscit multa simul.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0092","text":"[I q. 85 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32044)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0093","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in libro Topic., quod *intelligere est unum solum, scire vero multa*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0094","text":"[I q. 85 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32045)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0095","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod intellectus quidem potest multa intelligere per modum unius, non autem multa per modum multorum, dico autem per modum unius vel multorum, per unam vel plures species intelligibiles. Nam modus cuiusque actionis consequitur formam quae est actionis principium. Quaecumque ergo intellectus potest intelligere sub una specie, simul intelligere potest, et inde est quod Deus omnia simul videt, quia omnia videt per unum, quod est essentia sua. Quaecumque vero intellectus per diversas species intelligit, non simul intelligit. Et huius ratio est, quia impossibile est idem subiectum perfici simul pluribus formis unius generis et diversarum specierum, sicut impossibile est quod idem corpus secundum idem simul coloretur diversis coloribus, vel figuretur diversis figuris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0096","text":"Omnes autem species intelligibiles sunt unius generis, quia sunt perfectiones unius intellectivae potentiae; licet res quarum sunt species, sint diversorum generum. Impossibile est ergo quod idem intellectus simul perficiatur diversis speciebus intelligibilibus, ad intelligendum diversa in actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0097","text":"[I q. 85 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32046)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0098","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod intellectus est supra tempus quod est numerus motus corporalium rerum. Sed ipsa pluralitas specierum intelligibilium causat vicissitudinem quandam intelligibilium operationum, secundum quam una operatio est prior altera. Et hanc vicissitudinem Augustinus nominat tempus, cum dicit, VIII super Gen. ad Litt., quod *Deus movet creaturam spiritualem per tempus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0099","text":"[I q. 85 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32047)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0100","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod non solum oppositae formae non possunt esse simul in eodem subiecto, sed nec quaecumque formae eiusdem generis, licet non sint oppositae, sicut patet per exemplum inductum de coloribus et figuris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0101","text":"[I q. 85 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32048)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0102","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod partes possunt intelligi dupliciter. Uno modo, sub quadam confusione, prout sunt in toto, et sic cognoscuntur per unam formam totius, et sic simul cognoscuntur. Alio modo, cognitione distincta, secundum quod quaelibet cognoscitur per suam speciem, et sic non simul intelliguntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0103","text":"[I q. 85 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32049)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0104","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod quando intellectus intelligit differentiam vel comparationem unius ad alterum, cognoscit utrumque differentium vel comparatorum sub ratione ipsius comparationis vel differentiae; sicut dictum est quod cognoscit partes sub ratione totius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0105","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0106","text":"[I q. 85 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32050)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0107","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus noster non intelligat componendo et dividendo. Compositio enim et divisio non est nisi multorum. Sed intellectus non potest simul multa intelligere. Ergo non potest intelligere componendo et dividendo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0108","text":"[I q. 85 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32051)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0109","text":"Praeterea, omni compositioni et divisioni adiungitur tempus praesens, praeteritum vel futurum. Sed intellectus abstrahit a tempore, sicut etiam ab aliis particularibus conditionibus. Ergo intellectus non intelligit componendo et dividendo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0110","text":"[I q. 85 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32052)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0111","text":"Praeterea, intellectus intelligit per assimilationem ad res. Sed compositio et divisio nihil est in rebus, nihil enim invenitur in rebus nisi res quae significatur per praedicatum et subiectum, quae est una et eadem si compositio est vera; homo enim est vere id quod est animal. Ergo intellectus non componit et dividit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0112","text":"[I q. 85 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32053)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0113","text":"Sed contra, voces significant conceptiones intellectus, ut dicit philosophus in I Periherm. Sed in vocibus est compositio et divisio; ut patet in propositionibus affirmativis et negativis. Ergo intellectus componit et dividit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0114","text":"[I q. 85 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32054)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0115","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod intellectus humanus necesse habet intelligere componendo et dividendo. Cum enim intellectus humanus exeat de potentia in actum, similitudinem quandam habet cum rebus generabilibus, quae non statim perfectionem suam habent, sed eam successive acquirunt. Et similiter intellectus humanus non statim in prima apprehensione capit perfectam rei cognitionem; sed primo apprehendit aliquid de ipsa, puta quidditatem ipsius rei, quae est primum et proprium obiectum intellectus; et deinde intelligit proprietates et accidentia et habitudines circumstantes rei essentiam. Et secundum hoc, necesse habet unum apprehensum alii componere vel dividere; et ex una compositione vel divisione ad aliam procedere, quod est ratiocinari. Intellectus autem angelicus et divinus se habet sicut res incorruptibiles, quae statim a principio habent suam totam perfectionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0116","text":"Unde intellectus angelicus et divinus statim perfecte totam rei cognitionem habet. Unde in cognoscendo quidditatem rei, cognoscit de re simul quidquid nos cognoscere possumus componendo et dividendo et ratiocinando. Et ideo intellectus humanus cognoscit componendo et dividendo, sicut et ratiocinando. Intellectus autem divinus et angelicus cognoscunt quidem compositionem et divisionem et ratiocinationem, non componendo et dividendo et ratiocinando, sed per intellectum simplicis quidditatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0117","text":"[I q. 85 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32055)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0118","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod compositio et divisio intellectus secundum quandam differentiam vel comparationem fit. Unde sic intellectus cognoscit multa componendo et dividendo, sicut cognoscendo differentiam vel comparationem rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0119","text":"[I q. 85 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32056)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0120","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod intellectus et abstrahit a phantasmatibus; et tamen non intelligit actu nisi convertendo se ad phantasmata, sicut supra dictum est. Et ex ea parte qua se ad phantasmata convertit, compositioni et divisioni intellectus adiungitur tempus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0121","text":"[I q. 85 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32057)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0122","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod similitudo rei recipitur in intellectu secundum modum intellectus, et non secundum modum rei. Unde compositioni et divisioni intellectus respondet quidem aliquid ex parte rei; tamen non eodem modo se habet in re, sicut in intellectu. Intellectus enim humani proprium obiectum est quidditas rei materialis, quae sub sensu et imaginatione cadit. Invenitur autem duplex compositio in re materiali. Prima quidem, formae ad materiam, et huic respondet compositio intellectus qua totum universale de sua parte praedicatur; nam genus sumitur a materia communi, differentia vero completiva speciei a forma, particulare vero a materia individuali. Secunda vero compositio est accidentis ad subiectum, et huic reali compositioni respondet compositio intellectus secundum quam praedicatur accidens de subiecto, ut cum dicitur, homo est albus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0123","text":"Tamen differt compositio intellectus a compositione rei, nam ea quae componuntur in re, sunt diversa; compositio autem intellectus est signum identitatis eorum quae componuntur. Non enim intellectus sic componit, ut dicat quod homo est albedo; sed dicit quod homo est albus, idest habens albedinem, idem autem est subiecto quod est homo, et quod est habens albedinem. Et simile est de compositione formae et materiae, nam animal significat id quod habet naturam sensitivam, rationale vero quod habet naturam intellectivam, homo vero quod habet utrumque, Socrates vero quod habet omnia haec cum materia individuali; et secundum hanc identitatis rationem, intellectus noster unum componit alteri praedicando.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0124","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0125","text":"[I q. 85 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32058)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0126","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus possit esse falsus. Dicit enim philosophus, in VI Metaphys., quod *verum et falsum sunt in mente*. Mens autem et intellectus idem sunt, ut supra dictum est. Ergo falsitas est in intellectu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0127","text":"[I q. 85 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32059)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0128","text":"Praeterea, opinio et ratiocinatio ad intellectum pertinent. Sed in utraque istarum invenitur falsitas. Ergo potest esse falsitas in intellectu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0129","text":"[I q. 85 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32060)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0130","text":"Praeterea, peccatum in parte intellectiva est. Sed peccatum cum falsitate est, errant enim qui operantur malum, ut dicitur Prov. XIV. Ergo falsitas potest esse in intellectu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0131","text":"[I q. 85 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32061)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0132","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod *omnis qui fallitur, id in quo fallitur non intelligit*. Et philosophus dicit, in libro de anima, quod *intellectus semper est rectus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0133","text":"[I q. 85 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32062)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0134","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod philosophus, in III de anima, comparat, quantum ad hoc, intellectum sensui. Sensus enim circa proprium obiectum non decipitur, sicut visus circa colorem; nisi forte per accidens, ex impedimento circa organum contingente, sicut cum gustus febrientium dulcia iudicat amara, propter hoc quod lingua malis humoribus est repleta. Circa sensibilia vero communia decipitur sensus, sicut in diiudicando de magnitudine vel figura; ut cum iudicat solem esse pedalem, qui tamen est maior terra. Et multo magis decipitur circa sensibilia per accidens; ut cum iudicat fel esse mel, propter coloris similitudinem. Et huius ratio est in evidenti. Quia ad proprium obiectum unaquaeque potentia per se ordinatur, secundum quod ipsa. Quae autem sunt huiusmodi, semper eodem modo se habent. Unde manente potentia, non deficit eius iudicium circa proprium obiectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0135","text":"Obiectum autem proprium intellectus est quidditas rei. Unde circa quidditatem rei, per se loquendo, intellectus non fallitur. Sed circa ea quae circumstant rei essentiam vel quidditatem, intellectus potest falli, dum unum ordinat ad aliud, vel componendo vel dividendo vel etiam ratiocinando. Et propter hoc etiam circa illas propositiones errare non potest, quae statim cognoscuntur cognita terminorum quidditate, sicut accidit circa prima principia, ex quibus etiam accidit infallibilitas veritatis, secundum certitudinem scientiae, circa conclusiones.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0136","text":"Per accidens tamen contingit intellectum decipi circa quod quid est in rebus compositis; non ex parte organi, quia intellectus non est virtus utens organo; sed ex parte compositionis intervenientis circa definitionem, dum vel definitio unius rei est falsa de alia, sicut definitio circuli de triangulo, vel dum aliqua definitio in seipsa est falsa, implicans compositionem impossibilium, ut si accipiatur hoc ut definitio alicuius rei, animal rationale alatum. Unde in rebus simplicibus, in quarum definitionibus compositio intervenire non potest, non possumus decipi; sed deficimus in totaliter non attingendo, sicut dicitur in IX Metaphys.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0137","text":"[I q. 85 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32063)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0138","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod falsitatem dicit esse philosophus in mente secundum compositionem et divisionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0139","text":"[I q. 85 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32064)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0140","text":"Et similiter dicendum est ad secundum, de opinione et ratiocinatione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0141","text":"[I q. 85 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32065)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0142","text":"Et ad tertium, de errore peccantium, qui consistit in applicatione ad appetibile. Sed in absoluta consideratione quidditatis rei, et eorum quae per eam cognoscuntur, intellectus nunquam decipitur. Et sic loquuntur auctoritates in contrarium inductae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0143","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0144","text":"[I q. 85 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32066)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0145","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod unam et eandem rem unus alio melius intelligere non possit. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., *quisquis ullam rem aliter quam est intelligit non eam intelligit. Quare non est dubitandum esse perfectam intelligentiam, qua praestantior esse non possit; et ideo non per infinitum ire quod quaelibet res intelligitur; nec eam posse alium alio plus intelligere*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0146","text":"[I q. 85 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32067)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0147","text":"Praeterea, intellectus intelligendo verus est. Veritas autem, cum sit aequalitas quaedam intellectus et rei, non recipit magis et minus, non enim proprie dicitur aliquid magis et minus aequale. Ergo neque magis et minus aliquid intelligi dicitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0148","text":"[I q. 85 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32068)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0149","text":"Praeterea, intellectus est id quod est formalissimum in homine. Sed differentia formae causat differentiam speciei. Si igitur unus homo magis alio intelligit, videtur quod non sint unius speciei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0150","text":"[I q. 85 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32069)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0151","text":"Sed contra est quod per experimentum inveniuntur aliqui aliis profundius intelligentes; sicut profundius intelligit qui conclusionem aliquam potest reducere in prima principia et causas primas, quam qui potest reducere solum in causas proximas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0152","text":"[I q. 85 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32070)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0153","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod aliquem intelligere unam et eandem rem magis quam alium, potest intelligi dupliciter. Uno modo, sic quod ly magis determinet actum intelligendi ex parte rei intellectae. Et sic non potest unus eandem rem magis intelligere quam alius, quia si intelligeret eam aliter esse quam sit, vel melius vel peius, falleretur, et non intelligeret ut arguit Augustinus. Alio modo potest intelligi ut determinet actum intelligendi ex parte intelligentis. Et sic unus alio potest eandem rem melius intelligere, quia est melioris virtutis in intelligendo; sicut melius videt visione corporali rem aliquam qui est perfectioris virtutis, et in quo virtus visiva est perfectior. Hoc autem circa intellectum contingit dupliciter. Uno quidem modo, ex parte ipsius intellectus, qui est perfectior.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0154","text":"Manifestum est enim quod quanto corpus est melius dispositum, tanto meliorem sortitur animam, quod manifeste apparet in his quae sunt secundum speciem diversa. Cuius ratio est, quia actus et forma recipitur in materia secundum materiae capacitatem. Unde cum etiam in hominibus quidam habeant corpus melius dispositum, sortiuntur animam maioris virtutis in intelligendo, unde dicitur in II de anima quod *molles carne bene aptos mente videmus*. Alio modo contingit hoc ex parte inferiorum virtutum, quibus intellectus indiget ad sui operationem, illi enim in quibus virtus imaginativa et cogitativa et memorativa est melius disposita, sunt melius dispositi ad intelligendum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0155","text":"[I q. 85 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32071)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0156","text":"Ad primum ergo patet solutio ex dictis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0157","text":"[I q. 85 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32072)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0158","text":"Et similiter ad secundum, veritas enim intellectus in hoc consistit, quod intelligatur res esse sicuti est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0159","text":"[I q. 85 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32073)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0160","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod differentia formae quae non provenit nisi ex diversa dispositione materiae, non facit diversitatem secundum speciem, sed solum secundum numerum; sunt enim diversorum individuorum diversae formae, secundum materiam diversificatae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0161","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0162","text":"[I q. 85 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32074)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0163","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus noster per prius cognoscat indivisibile quam divisibile. Dicit enim philosophus, in I Physic., quod *intelligimus et scimus ex principiorum et elementorum cognitione*. Sed indivisibilia sunt principia et elementa divisibilium. Ergo per prius sunt nobis nota indivisibilia quam divisibilia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0164","text":"[I q. 85 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32075)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0165","text":"Praeterea, id quod ponitur in definitione alicuius, per prius cognoscitur a nobis, quia definitio est ex prioribus et notioribus, ut dicitur in VI Topic. Sed indivisibile ponitur in definitione divisibilis, sicut *linea enim*, ut Euclides dicit, *est longitudo sine latitudine, cuius extremitates sunt duo puncta*. Et unitas ponitur in definitione numeri, quia numerus est multitudo mensurata per unum, ut dicitur in X Metaphys. Ergo intellectus noster per prius intelligit indivisibile quam divisibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0166","text":"[I q. 85 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32076)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0167","text":"Praeterea, simile simili cognoscitur. Sed indivisibile est magis simile intellectui quam divisibile, quia intellectus est simplex, ut dicitur in III de anima. Ergo intellectus noster prius cognoscit indivisibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0168","text":"[I q. 85 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32077)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0169","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in III de anima, quod *indivisibile monstratur sicut privatio*. Sed privatio per posterius cognoscitur. Ergo et indivisibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0170","text":"[I q. 85 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32078)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0171","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod obiectum intellectus nostri, secundum praesentem statum, est quidditas rei materialis, quam a phantasmatibus abstrahit, ut ex praemissis patet. Et quia id quod est primo et per se cognitum a virtute cognoscitiva, est proprium eius obiectum, considerari potest quo ordine indivisibile intelligatur a nobis, ex eius habitudine ad huiusmodi quidditatem. Dicitur autem indivisibile tripliciter, ut dicitur in III de anima. Uno modo, sicut continuum est indivisibile, quia est indivisum in actu, licet sit divisibile in potentia. Et huiusmodi indivisibile prius est intellectum a nobis quam eius divisio, quae est in partes, quia cognitio confusa est prior quam distincta, ut dictum est. Alio modo dicitur indivisibile secundum speciem, sicut ratio hominis est quoddam indivisibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0172","text":"Et hoc etiam modo indivisibile est prius intellectum quam divisio eius in partes rationis, ut supra dictum est, et iterum prius quam intellectus componat et dividat, affirmando vel negando. Et huius ratio est, quia huiusmodi duplex indivisibile intellectus secundum se intelligit, sicut proprium obiectum. Tertio modo dicitur indivisibile quod est omnino indivisibile, ut punctus et unitas, quae nec actu nec potentia dividuntur. Et huiusmodi indivisibile per posterius cognoscitur, per privationem divisibilis. Unde punctum privative definitur, punctum est cuius pars non est, et similiter ratio unius est quod sit indivisibile, ut dicitur in X Metaphys. Et huius ratio est, quia tale indivisibile habet quandam oppositionem ad rem corporalem, cuius quidditatem primo et per se intellectus accipit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0173","text":"Si autem intellectus noster intelligeret per participationem indivisibilium separatorum, ut Platonici posuerunt, sequeretur quod indivisibile huiusmodi esset primo intellectum, quia secundum Platonicos, priora prius participantur a rebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0174","text":"[I q. 85 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32079)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0175","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in accipiendo scientiam, non semper principia et elementa sunt priora, quia quandoque ex effectibus sensibilibus devenimus in cognitionem principiorum et causarum intelligibilium. Sed in complemento scientiae, semper scientia effectuum dependet ex cognitione principiorum et elementorum, quia, ut ibidem dicit philosophus, *tunc opinamur nos scire, cum principiata possumus in causas resolvere*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0176","text":"[I q. 85 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32080)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0177","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod punctum non ponitur in definitione lineae communiter sumptae, manifestum est enim quod in linea infinita, et etiam in circulari, non est punctum nisi in potentia. Sed Euclides definit lineam finitam rectam, et ideo posuit punctum in definitione lineae, sicut terminum in definitione terminati. Unitas vero est mensura numeri, et ideo ponitur in definitione numeri mensurati. Non autem ponitur in definitione divisibilis, sed magis e converso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0178","text":"[I q. 85 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32081)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0179","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod similitudo per quam intelligimus, est species cogniti in cognoscente. Et ideo non secundum similitudinem naturae ad potentiam cognoscitivam est aliquid prius cognitum, sed per convenientiam ad obiectum, alioquin magis visus cognosceret auditum quam colorem.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"8b60b7c065bbf590fba13db8e40e8a5da5186a7305be10004e4c516a1a4faa32","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T151827165825Z_a8b9c899","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 85","source_sha256":"8b60b7c065bbf590fba13db8e40e8a5da5186a7305be10004e4c516a1a4faa32","sha256":"66e485b28949caeb806a894ca9b1f3a564af0b9a37471cf8971df726bfb916f1","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:18:27.165874+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0002","text":"[I q. 85 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32009)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the mode and order of understanding. And concerning this, eight questions are asked. First, whether our intellect understands by abstracting species from phantasms. Second, whether intelligible species abstracted from phantasms stand in relation to our intellect as that which is understood, or as that by which it understands. Third, whether our intellect naturally understands the more universal first. Fourth, whether our intellect can understand many things simultaneously. Fifth, whether our intellect understands by composing and dividing. Sixth, whether the intellect can err. Seventh, whether one person can understand the same thing better than another. Eighth, whether our intellect knows the indivisible before the divisible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0005","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32010)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that our intellect does not understand corporeal and material things through abstraction from phantasms. For every intellect that understands a thing otherwise than it is, is false. But the forms of material things are not abstracted from the particulars, the likenesses of which are phantasms. Therefore, if we understand material things by abstraction of species from phantasms, there will be falsity in our intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0007","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32011)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0008","text":"Further, material things are natural things, in whose definition matter falls. But nothing can be understood without that which falls in its definition. Therefore material things cannot be understood without matter. But matter is the principle of individuation. Therefore material things cannot be understood through abstraction of the universal from the particular, which is to abstract intelligible species from phantasms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0009","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32012)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0010","text":"Further, it is said in *On the Soul* III that phantasms stand in relation to the intellectual soul as colors do to sight. But vision does not occur through the abstraction of any species from colors, but through colors making an impression on sight. Therefore neither does understanding occur through something being abstracted from phantasms, but through phantasms making an impression on the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0011","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32013)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0012","text":"Further, as is said in *On the Soul* III, there are two things in the intellectual soul, namely, the possible intellect and the agent intellect. But to abstract intelligible species from phantasms does not belong to the possible intellect, but to receive species already abstracted. But neither does it seem to belong to the agent intellect, which stands in relation to phantasms as light does to colors, and which does not abstract anything from colors, but rather acts upon them. Therefore we by no means understand by abstracting from phantasms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0013","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32014)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0014","text":"Further, the Philosopher says in *On the Soul* III that *the intellect understands species in phantasms*. Therefore, not by abstracting them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0015","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32015)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0016","text":"On the contrary, it is said in *On the Soul* III that *as things are separable from matter, so they are with respect to the intellect*. Therefore material things must be understood insofar as they are abstracted from matter and from material likenesses, which are phantasms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0017","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32016)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0018","text":"I answer that, as was said above, the knowable object is proportioned to the cognitive power. Now there is a threefold degree of cognitive power. For one cognitive power is the act of a bodily organ, namely, sense. And therefore the object of every sensitive power is form as existing in bodily matter. And because matter of this kind is the principle of individuation, every power of the sensitive part is cognitive only of particulars. But there is a cognitive power which is neither the act of a bodily organ nor joined in any way to bodily matter, as is the angelic intellect. And therefore the object of this cognitive power is a form subsisting without matter; and although angels know material things, nevertheless they behold them only in immaterial things, namely, either in themselves or in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0019","text":"But the human intellect is disposed in an intermediate way: for it is not the act of any organ, yet it is a power of the soul which is the form of the body, as is clear from what has been said above. And therefore it is proper to it to know form indeed as existing individually in bodily matter, but not insofar as it is in such matter. But to know that which is in individual matter, not insofar as it is in such matter, is to abstract form from individual matter, which phantasms represent. And therefore it is necessary to say that our intellect understands material things by abstracting from phantasms; and through material things thus considered we arrive at some knowledge of immaterial things, just as, conversely, angels know material things through immaterial things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0020","text":"Plato, however, attending only to the immateriality of the human intellect, and not to the fact that it is in some way united to a body, posited separate Ideas as the object of the intellect; and held that we understand, not indeed by abstracting, but rather by participating in things already abstracted, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0021","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32017)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0022","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that abstraction occurs in two ways. In one way, by way of composition and division; as when we understand that something is not in another thing, or is separate from it. In another way, by way of simple and absolute consideration; as when we understand one thing, considering nothing about another. Therefore, to abstract by the intellect things which in reality are not abstract, according to the first mode of abstracting, is not without falsity. But to abstract by the intellect, according to the second mode, things which are not abstract in reality involves no falsity; as is manifest in sensibles. For if we understand or say that color does not inhere in a colored body, or is separate from it, there will be falsity in the opinion or statement.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0023","text":"But if we consider color and its properties, considering nothing about the colored apple; or if we express in speech what we thus understand; this will be without falsity of opinion and statement. For the apple does not belong to the account of color; and therefore nothing prevents color from being understood while nothing is understood about the apple. Similarly, I say that those things which belong to the account of the species of any material thing, for instance of a stone, a human being, or a horse, can be considered without the individual principles, which do not belong to the account of the species. And this is to abstract the universal from the particular, or the intelligible species from phantasms: namely, to consider the nature of the species without consideration of the individual principles represented by phantasms. Therefore, when it is said that the intellect is false which understands a thing otherwise than it is, this is true if the word *otherwise* is referred to the thing understood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0024","text":"For then the intellect is false when it understands a thing to be otherwise than it is. Hence the intellect would be false if it abstracted the species of stone from matter in such a way as to understand it not to be in matter, as Plato held. But what is proposed is not true if the word *otherwise* is taken on the side of the one understanding. For it is without falsity that the mode of the one understanding in understanding differs from the mode of the thing in existing, because what is understood is in the one understanding immaterially, according to the mode of intellect, and not materially, according to the mode of a material thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0025","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32018)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0026","text":"To the second, it must be said that some have thought that the species of a natural thing is the form alone, and that matter is not a part of the species. But on this account matter would not be placed in the definitions of natural things. And therefore it must be said otherwise: that matter is twofold, namely common, and designated or individual; common indeed, as flesh and bone; individual, however, as these flesh and these bones. Therefore the intellect abstracts the species of a natural thing from individual sensible matter, but not from common sensible matter. Just as it abstracts the species of man from these flesh and these bones, which are not of the definition of the species, but are parts of the individual, as is said in Metaphysics VII; and therefore it can be considered without them. But the species of man cannot be abstracted by the intellect from flesh and bones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0027","text":"Mathematical species, however, can be abstracted by the intellect from sensible matter not only individual but also common; yet not from common intelligible matter, but only from individual intelligible matter. For sensible matter is called corporeal matter insofar as it underlies sensible qualities, namely hot and cold, hard and soft, and the like. But intelligible matter is called substance insofar as it underlies quantity. Now it is manifest that quantity inheres in substance prior to sensible qualities. Hence quantities, such as numbers and dimensions and figures, which are the determinations of quantities, can be considered without sensible qualities, which is to abstract them from sensible matter; yet they cannot be considered without the understanding of the substance subject to quantity, which would be to abstract them from common intelligible matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0028","text":"They can, however, be considered without this or that substance; which is to abstract them from individual intelligible matter. But there are certain things that can be abstracted even from common intelligible matter, such as being, one, potency, and act, and other such things, which can also exist without all matter, as is clear in immaterial substances. And because Plato did not consider what has been said concerning the twofold mode of abstraction, he held that all the things which we have said are abstracted by the intellect are abstract in reality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0029","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32019)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0030","text":"To the third, it must be said that colors have the same mode of existing insofar as they are in individual corporeal matter as does the visual power, and therefore they can impress their likeness upon sight. But phantasms, since they are likenesses of individuals and exist in bodily organs, do not have the same mode of existing as the human intellect has, as is clear from what has been said; and therefore they cannot by their own power make an impression upon the possible intellect. But by the power of the agent intellect a certain likeness results in the possible intellect from the turning of the agent intellect toward the phantasms; and this likeness represents those things of which the phantasms are phantasms, only with respect to the nature of the species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0031","text":"And in this manner the intelligible species is said to be abstracted from phantasms, not because some form numerically the same, which was first in the phantasms, subsequently comes to be in the possible intellect, in the manner in which a body is taken from one place and transferred to another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0032","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32020)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0033","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that phantasms are illuminated by the agent intellect; and again, intelligible species are abstracted from them by the power of the agent intellect. They are illuminated indeed because, just as the sensitive part is made more powerful by conjunction with the intellectual part, so phantasms are rendered apt, through the power of the agent intellect, for intelligible intentions to be abstracted from them. But the agent intellect abstracts intelligible species from phantasms insofar as, through the power of the agent intellect, we are able in our consideration to receive the natures of species without individual conditions, by the likenesses of which the possible intellect is informed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0034","text":"[I q. 85 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32021)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0035","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that our intellect both abstracts intelligible species from phantasms, insofar as it considers the natures of things universally, and nevertheless understands them in phantasms, because it cannot understand even those things whose species it abstracts except by turning itself toward phantasms, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0036","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0037","text":"[I q. 85 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32022)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0038","text":"To the second, the procedure is thus. It seems that intelligible species abstracted from phantasms are related to our intellect as that which is understood. For the actually understood is in the one understanding, because the actually understood is the intellect itself in act. But nothing of the thing understood is in the intellect actually understanding except the abstracted intelligible species. Therefore such a species is itself the actually understood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0039","text":"[I q. 85 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32023)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0040","text":"Moreover, the actually understood must be in something, otherwise it would be nothing. But it is not in the thing which is outside the soul, because, since the thing outside the soul is material, nothing that is in it can be actually understood. Therefore it remains that the actually understood is in the intellect. And thus it is nothing other than the aforesaid intelligible species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0041","text":"[I q. 85 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32024)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0042","text":"Moreover, the Philosopher says, in I Perihermeneias, that *spoken sounds are signs of the passions that are in the soul*. But spoken sounds signify things understood, for by a spoken sound we signify what we understand. Therefore the passions of the soul themselves, namely intelligible species, are the things actually understood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0043","text":"[I q. 85 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32025)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0044","text":"On the contrary, the intelligible species is related to the intellect as the sensible species to sense. But the sensible species is not that which is sensed, but rather that by which sense senses. Therefore the intelligible species is not what is actually understood, but that by which the intellect understands.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0045","text":"[I q. 85 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32026)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0046","text":"I answer that it must be said that some have maintained that the cognitive powers which are in us cognize nothing except their own passions; for example, that sense senses only the affection of its own organ. And accordingly, the intellect understands nothing except its own passion, that is, the intelligible species received in it. And accordingly, such a species is itself what is understood. But this opinion manifestly appears false for two reasons. First, because the things that we understand are the same as those concerning which there are sciences. Therefore, if the things that we understand were only the species that are in the soul, it would follow that all sciences would not be about things that are outside the soul, but only about intelligible species that are in the soul; just as according to the Platonists all sciences are about ideas, which they held to be things actually understood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0047","text":"Second, because the error of the ancients who said that *everything which appears is true* would follow; and thus that contradictories would be true at the same time. For if a power cognizes only its own passion, it judges only concerning that. But something appears according as the cognitive power is affected. Therefore the judgment of the cognitive power will always be concerning what it judges, namely its own passion, according as it is; and thus every judgment will be true. For example, if taste senses only its own passion, when someone with a sound taste judges honey to be sweet, he will judge truly; and likewise, if one who has a corrupted taste judges honey to be bitter, he will judge truly, for each judges according as his taste is affected. And thus it follows that every opinion will be equally true, and universally every apprehension.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0048","text":"And therefore it must be said that the intelligible species is related to the intellect as that by which the intellect understands. This is clear as follows. For since there is a twofold action, as is said in Metaphysics IX: one that remains in the agent, such as seeing and understanding; the other that passes into an external thing, such as heating and cutting; each takes place according to some form. And just as the form according to which an action tending toward an external thing proceeds is a likeness of the object of the action, as the heat of what heats is a likeness of what is heated; likewise, the form according to which an action remaining in the agent proceeds is a likeness of the object. Hence the likeness of a visible thing is that according to which sight sees; and the likeness of the thing understood, which is the intelligible species, is the form according to which the intellect understands. But because the intellect reflects upon itself, by the same reflection it understands both its own understanding and the species by which it understands.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0049","text":"And thus the intelligible species is secondarily that which is understood. But that which is understood primarily is the thing of which the intelligible species is a likeness. And this is also evident from the opinion of the ancients, who held that like is known by like. For they held that the soul, through the earth that was in it, knew the earth that was outside it; and likewise concerning other things. If therefore, in accordance with the teaching of Aristotle, who says that *the stone is not in the soul, but the species of the stone*, we take the species of earth in place of earth, it will follow that the soul knows, through intelligible species, things that are outside the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0050","text":"[I q. 85 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32027)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0051","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that the thing understood is in the one understanding through its likeness. And in this way it is said that the actually understood is the intellect in act, insofar as the likeness of the thing understood is the form of the intellect; just as the likeness of the sensible thing is the form of sense in act. Hence it does not follow that the abstracted intelligible species is that which is actually understood, but that it is its likeness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0052","text":"[I q. 85 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32028)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0053","text":"To the second, it must be said that, when “the actually understood” is spoken of, two things are implied: namely, the thing which is understood, and that which is understanding itself. And similarly, when “the abstract universal” is spoken of, two things are understood: namely, the nature of the thing itself, and abstraction or universality. Therefore the nature itself, to which being understood, or being abstracted, or the intention of universality accrues, exists only in singulars; but this very thing, namely being understood or being abstracted, or the intention of universality, exists in the intellect. And we can see this through a likeness in sense. For sight sees the color of an apple without its odor. Therefore, if it is asked where the color is that is seen without odor, it is manifest that the color which is seen exists only in the apple; but that it is perceived without odor accrues to it on the part of sight, insofar as in sight there is a likeness of color and not of odor.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0054","text":"Similarly, humanity which is understood exists only in this or that man; but that humanity is apprehended without individual conditions—which is for it itself to be abstracted, and upon which the intention of universality follows—accrues to humanity according as it is perceived by the intellect, in which there is a likeness of the nature of the species, and not of individual principles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0055","text":"[I q. 85 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32029)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0056","text":"To the third, it must be said that a twofold operation is found in the sensitive part. One is according to alteration alone, and thus the operation of sense is completed by its being altered by the sensible object. The other operation is formation, according as the imaginative power forms for itself some image of an absent thing, or even of one never seen. And both these operations are conjoined in the intellect. For first the reception of the possible intellect is considered, according as it is informed by the intelligible species. Having been thus informed, it secondly forms either a definition or a division or a composition, which is signified by the spoken word. Hence the account signified by a name is a definition; and an enunciation signifies the composition and division of the intellect. Therefore spoken words do not signify the intelligible species themselves, but those things which the intellect forms for itself in order to judge concerning external things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0057","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0058","text":"[I q. 85 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32030)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0059","text":"We proceed to the third as follows. It seems that the more universal things are not prior in our intellectual cognition. For those things which are prior and better known according to nature are posterior and less known according to us. But universals are prior according to nature, because that is prior from which the consequence of subsisting is not convertible. Therefore universals are posterior in the cognition of our intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0060","text":"[I q. 85 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32031)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0061","text":"Moreover, composite things are prior with respect to us than simple things. But universals are simpler. Therefore they are known later with respect to us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0062","text":"[I q. 85 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32032)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0063","text":"Moreover, the Philosopher says, in *Physics* I, that what is defined falls into our cognition before the parts of the definition. But more universal things are parts of the definition of less universal things, just as animal is a part of the definition of man. Therefore universals are known later with respect to us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0064","text":"[I q. 85 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32033)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0065","text":"Moreover, through effects we arrive at causes and principles. But universals are certain principles. Therefore universals are known later with respect to us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0066","text":"[I q. 85 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32034)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0067","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in *Physics* I that *one must proceed from universals to singulars*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0068","text":"[I q. 85 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32035)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0069","text":"I answer that, two things must be considered in the cognition of our intellect. First, indeed, that intellectual cognition in some way takes its origin from sensitive cognition. And because sense is of singulars, whereas intellect is of universals, the cognition of singulars must, with respect to us, be prior to the cognition of universals. Second, it must be considered that our intellect proceeds from potency to act. But everything which proceeds from potency to act reaches an incomplete act, which is intermediate between potency and act, before it reaches perfect act. Now the perfect act which the intellect reaches is complete knowledge, through which things are known distinctly and determinately. But incomplete act is imperfect knowledge, through which things are known indistinctly under a certain confusion; for what is thus known is known in act in a certain respect, and in some way in potency.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0070","text":"Hence the Philosopher says, in *Physics* I, that *things more confused are first manifest and certain to us; but later, by distinguishing, we know distinctly the principles and elements*. Now it is manifest that to know something in which many things are contained, without having proper knowledge of each of those things which are contained in it, is to know something under a certain confusion. In this way, both the universal whole, in which the parts are contained potentially, and also the integral whole can be known; for each whole can be known in a certain confusion, without the parts being known distinctly. But to know distinctly that which is contained in the universal whole is to have cognition of a less common thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0071","text":"Thus, to know animal indistinctly is to know animal insofar as it is animal; but to know animal distinctly is to know animal insofar as it is rational or irrational animal, which is to know man or lion. Therefore our intellect first encounters the cognition of animal before the cognition of man; and the same account holds if we compare any more universal thing with a less universal one. And because sense proceeds from potency to act just as intellect does, the same order of cognition also appears in sense. For according to sense we judge the more common before the less common, both according to place and according to time. According to place, indeed, as when something is seen from afar, it is first apprehended to be a body before it is apprehended to be an animal; and it is first apprehended to be an animal before it is apprehended to be a man; and man before Socrates or Plato.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0072","text":"According to time, however, because from the beginning a child distinguishes a man from a non-man before distinguishing this man from another man; and therefore *children at first call all men fathers, but later distinguish each one*, as is said in *Physics* I. And the reason for this is manifest. For one who knows something indistinctly is still in potency to know the principle of distinction; just as one who knows the genus is in potency to know the difference. And thus it is clear that indistinct cognition is intermediate between potency and act. Therefore it must be said that the cognition of singulars is prior with respect to us to the cognition of universals, just as sensitive cognition is prior to intellectual cognition. But both according to sense and according to intellect, cognition of the more common is prior to cognition of the less common.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0073","text":"[I q. 85 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32036)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0074","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that the universal can be considered in two ways. In one way, insofar as the universal nature is considered together with the intention of universality. And since the intention of universality—namely, that one and the same thing bears a relation to many—arises from the abstraction of the intellect, it is necessary that, in this way, the universal be posterior. Hence it is said in I *On the Soul* that *the universal animal either is nothing, or is posterior*. But according to Plato, who posited subsisting universals, under this consideration the universal would be prior to particulars, which according to him exist only through participation in subsisting universals, which are called ideas. In another way it can be considered with respect to the nature itself, namely, of animality or humanity, insofar as it is found in particulars. And thus it must be said that there is a twofold order of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0075","text":"One is according to the order of generation and time, according to which order those things that are imperfect and in potency are prior. And in this way, the more common is prior by nature, as is manifestly apparent in the generation of man and animal; for an animal is generated before a man, as is said in the book *On the Generation of Animals*. The other is the order of perfection, or of nature's intention; just as act is simply prior by nature to potency, and the perfect to the imperfect. And in this way, the less common is prior by nature to the more common, as man to animal; for nature's intention does not stop at the generation of an animal, but intends to generate a man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0076","text":"[I q. 85 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32037)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0077","text":"To the second it must be said that the more common universal is compared to the less common as a whole and as a part. As a whole indeed, insofar as under the more universal there is contained in potency not only the less universal, but also others; thus under animal there are not only man, but also horse. But as a part, insofar as the less common contains in its notion not only the more common, but also other things; thus man contains not only animal, but also rational. Therefore, animal considered in itself is prior in our knowledge to man; but man is prior in our knowledge to the fact that animal is part of its notion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0078","text":"[I q. 85 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32038)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0079","text":"To the third it must be said that a part can be known in two ways. In one way, absolutely, insofar as it is in itself, and thus nothing prevents parts from being known before the whole, as stones before a house. In another way, insofar as they are parts of this whole, and thus it is necessary that we know the whole before the parts; for we first know a house by a certain confused knowledge before we distinguish its individual parts. Therefore it must be said that the defining elements, considered absolutely, are known before the defined thing, otherwise the defined thing would not be made known through them. But insofar as they are parts of the definition, they are known later; for we first know man by a certain confused knowledge before we know how to distinguish all that belongs to the notion of man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0080","text":"[I q. 85 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32039)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0081","text":"To the fourth it must be said that the universal, insofar as it is taken together with the intention of universality, is indeed in some way a principle of knowing, insofar as the intention of universality follows the mode of understanding that is through abstraction. But it is not necessary that everything which is a principle of knowing be a principle of being, as Plato supposed, since sometimes we know a cause through an effect, and a substance through accidents. Hence the universal so taken, according to Aristotle's opinion, is not a principle of being, nor a substance, as is clear in VII *Metaphysics*. But if we consider the very nature of genus and species insofar as it is in singulars, then it has in some way the character of a formal principle with respect to singulars; for the singular is on account of matter, whereas the notion of species is taken from form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0082","text":"But the nature of genus is related to the nature of species more in the manner of a material principle, because the nature of genus is taken from what is material in the thing, whereas the notion of species is taken from what is formal; just as the notion of animal is from the sensitive, whereas the notion of man is from the intellective. And hence nature's ultimate intention is directed toward the species, but not toward the individual, nor toward the genus, because form is the end of generation, whereas matter is for the sake of form. But it is not necessary that knowledge of every cause or principle be posterior with respect to us, since sometimes we know unknown effects through sensible causes, but sometimes conversely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0083","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0084","text":"[I q. 85 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32040)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0085","text":"The fourth point is approached thus. It seems that we can understand many things simultaneously. For the intellect is above time. But before and after pertain to time. Therefore the intellect does not understand different things according to before and after, but simultaneously.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0086","text":"[I q. 85 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32041)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0087","text":"Further, nothing prevents diverse, non-opposed forms from inhering simultaneously in the same thing in act, as odor and color in an apple. But intelligible species are not opposed. Therefore nothing prevents one intellect from becoming actual simultaneously according to diverse intelligible species, and thus it can understand many things simultaneously.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0088","text":"[I q. 85 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32042)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0089","text":"Further, the intellect understands some whole simultaneously, such as man or a house. But many parts are contained in every whole. Therefore the intellect understands many things simultaneously.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0090","text":"[I q. 85 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32043)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0091","text":"Further, the difference of one thing from another cannot be known unless both are apprehended simultaneously, as is said in the book *On the Soul*, and the same reasoning holds concerning any other comparison. But our intellect knows the difference and comparison of one thing to another. Therefore it knows many things simultaneously.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0092","text":"[I q. 85 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32044)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0093","text":"But to the contrary is what is said in the book *Topics*, that *understanding is of one thing only, whereas knowing is of many*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0094","text":"[I q. 85 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32045)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0095","text":"I answer that it must be said that the intellect can indeed understand many things in the manner of one, but not many things in the manner of many. But I mean by the manner of one or of many, through one or several intelligible species. For the mode of every action follows the form which is the principle of the action. Therefore, whatever things the intellect can understand under one species, it can understand simultaneously; and hence God sees all things simultaneously, because He sees all things through one thing, which is His essence. But whatever things the intellect understands through diverse species, it does not understand simultaneously. And the reason for this is that it is impossible for the same subject to be perfected simultaneously by several forms of one genus and of diverse species, just as it is impossible for the same body, in the same respect, to be colored simultaneously by diverse colors, or figured by diverse figures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0096","text":"But all intelligible species are of one genus, because they are perfections of one intellective power, although the things of which they are species are of diverse genera. Therefore it is impossible for the same intellect to be perfected simultaneously by diverse intelligible species, so as actually to understand diverse things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0097","text":"[I q. 85 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32046)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0098","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that the intellect is above time, which is the number of the motion of corporeal things. But the very plurality of intelligible species causes a certain succession of intelligible operations, according to which one operation is prior to another. And Augustine calls this succession time, when he says, in Book VIII of *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, that *God moves the spiritual creature through time*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0099","text":"[I q. 85 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32047)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0100","text":"To the second, it must be said that not only can opposed forms not exist simultaneously in the same subject, but neither can any forms of the same genus, even if they are not opposed, as is clear from the example given concerning colors and figures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0101","text":"[I q. 85 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32048)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0102","text":"To the third, it must be said that parts can be understood in two ways. In one way, under a certain confusion, insofar as they are in the whole; and thus they are known through the one form of the whole, and thus they are known simultaneously. In another way, by distinct knowledge, insofar as each is known through its own species; and thus they are not understood simultaneously.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0103","text":"[I q. 85 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32049)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0104","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that when the intellect understands the difference or comparison of one thing to another, it knows both of the things differing or compared under the character of the comparison or difference itself; just as it was said that it knows the parts under the character of the whole.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0105","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0106","text":"[I q. 85 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32050)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0107","text":"We proceed to the fifth as follows. It seems that our intellect does not understand by composing and dividing. For composition and division are only of many things. But the intellect cannot understand many things simultaneously. Therefore, it cannot understand by composing and dividing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0108","text":"[I q. 85 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32051)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0109","text":"Moreover, every composition and division is joined to present, past, or future time. But the intellect abstracts from time, just as it does from other particular conditions. Therefore, the intellect does not understand by composing and dividing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0110","text":"[I q. 85 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32052)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0111","text":"Moreover, the intellect understands by assimilation to things. But composition and division are nothing in things; for nothing is found in things except the thing signified by the predicate and subject, which is one and the same if the composition is true; for a human being is truly that which an animal is. Therefore, the intellect does not compose and divide.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0112","text":"[I q. 85 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32053)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0113","text":"On the contrary, words signify conceptions of the intellect, as the Philosopher says in Book I of *On Interpretation*. But in words there are composition and division, as is clear in affirmative and negative propositions. Therefore, the intellect composes and divides.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0114","text":"[I q. 85 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32054)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0115","text":"I answer that it must be said that the human intellect must understand by composing and dividing. For since the human intellect passes from potency into act, it bears a certain likeness to generable things, which do not at once possess their perfection, but acquire it successively. Likewise, the human intellect does not at once, in its first apprehension, grasp perfect knowledge of a thing; rather, it first apprehends something of it, for example, the quiddity of the thing itself, which is the first and proper object of the intellect; and then it understands the properties, accidents, and relations surrounding the essence of the thing. And accordingly, it must compose or divide one apprehended thing with another; and proceed from one composition or division to another, which is to reason. But the angelic and divine intellects are as incorruptible things, which have their whole perfection at once from the beginning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0116","text":"Hence the angelic and divine intellect has at once perfect and complete knowledge of a thing. Hence, in knowing the quiddity of a thing, it knows concerning the thing at the same time whatever we can know by composing and dividing and reasoning. And therefore the human intellect knows by composing and dividing, just as by reasoning. But the divine and angelic intellects indeed know composition and division and reasoning, not by composing and dividing and reasoning, but through the understanding of simple quiddity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0117","text":"[I q. 85 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32055)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0118","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that composition and division of the intellect occur according to a certain difference or comparison. Hence, in composing and dividing, the intellect knows many things in the same way as in knowing the difference or comparison of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0119","text":"[I q. 85 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32056)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0120","text":"To the second, it must be said that the intellect both abstracts from phantasms and nevertheless does not understand actually except by turning itself toward phantasms, as was said above. And on the side on which it turns itself toward phantasms, time is joined to the composition and division of the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0121","text":"[I q. 85 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32057)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0122","text":"To the third it must be said that the likeness of a thing is received in the intellect according to the mode of the intellect, and not according to the mode of the thing. Hence something on the part of the thing does indeed correspond to the composition and division of the intellect; nevertheless, it is not disposed in the thing in the same way as in the intellect. For the proper object of the human intellect is the quiddity of a material thing, which falls under sense and imagination. Now a twofold composition is found in a material thing. The first is that of form to matter, and to this corresponds the composition of the intellect by which the universal whole is predicated of its part; for genus is taken from common matter, whereas the difference completing the species is taken from form, and the particular from individual matter. The second composition is that of accident to subject, and to this real composition corresponds the composition of the intellect according to which an accident is predicated of a subject, as when it is said, man is white.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0123","text":"Nevertheless, the composition of the intellect differs from the composition of the thing, for those things which are composed in the thing are diverse; but the composition of the intellect is a sign of the identity of those things which are composed. For the intellect does not compose in such a way as to say that man is whiteness; rather, it says that man is white, that is, having whiteness; but that which is man and that which has whiteness are the same in subject. And it is similar concerning the composition of form and matter: for animal signifies that which has a sensitive nature, rational that which has an intellective nature, man that which has both, and Socrates that which has all these with individual matter; and according to this character of identity, our intellect composes one thing with another by predicating it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0124","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0125","text":"[I q. 85 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32058)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0126","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth. It seems that the intellect can be false. For the Philosopher says, in Metaphysics VI, that *the true and the false are in the mind*. But mind and intellect are the same, as was said above. Therefore falsity is in the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0127","text":"[I q. 85 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32059)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0128","text":"Moreover, opinion and reasoning belong to the intellect. But falsity is found in each of these. Therefore falsity can be in the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0129","text":"[I q. 85 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32060)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0130","text":"Moreover, sin is in the intellective part. But sin is accompanied by falsity, for those who do evil err, as is said in Proverbs XIV. Therefore falsity can be in the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0131","text":"[I q. 85 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32061)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0132","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in the book of Eighty-Three Questions, that *everyone who is deceived does not understand that in which he is deceived*. And the Philosopher says, in the book On the Soul, that *the intellect is always right*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0133","text":"[I q. 85 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32062)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0134","text":"I answer that it must be said that the Philosopher, in On the Soul III, compares the intellect with sense in this respect. For sense is not deceived concerning its proper object, as sight is not concerning color—except perhaps accidentally, owing to an impediment occurring in the organ, as when the taste of those with a fever judges sweet things to be bitter, because the tongue is filled with bad humors. But sense is deceived concerning common sensibles, as in judging magnitude or figure; as when it judges the sun to be a foot in size, though it is greater than the earth. And it is deceived much more concerning sensibles per accidens; as when it judges gall to be honey because of similarity of color. And the reason for this is evident. For each power is ordered per se to its proper object, according as it is itself. But things of this sort are always disposed in the same way. Hence, so long as the power remains, its judgment concerning its proper object does not fail.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0135","text":"But the proper object of the intellect is the quiddity of a thing. Hence, speaking per se, the intellect is not mistaken concerning the quiddity of a thing. But concerning those things that surround a thing's essence or quiddity, the intellect can be mistaken, while it orders one thing to another, whether by composing or dividing or even by reasoning. And for this reason it also cannot err concerning those propositions that are known at once when the quiddity of the terms is known, as occurs concerning first principles; from which there also results the infallibility of truth, according to the certitude of science, concerning conclusions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0136","text":"Yet it happens accidentally that the intellect is deceived concerning what a thing is in composite things: not on the part of an organ, because the intellect is not a power using an organ, but on the part of a composition entering into the definition, either because the definition of one thing is false of another, as the definition of a circle is of a triangle, or because some definition is in itself false, implying a composition of impossibilities, as if this were taken as the definition of some thing: a winged rational animal. Hence, in simple things, in whose definitions composition cannot enter, we cannot be deceived; but we fall short by not attaining them at all, as is said in Metaphysics IX.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0137","text":"[I q. 85 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32063)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0138","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that the Philosopher says falsity is in the mind according to composition and division.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0139","text":"[I q. 85 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32064)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0140","text":"And the same must be said to the second, concerning opinion and reasoning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0141","text":"[I q. 85 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32065)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0142","text":"And to the third, concerning the error of those who sin, which consists in application to the appetible. But in the absolute consideration of the quiddity of a thing, and of those things which are known through it, the intellect is never deceived. And thus the authorities adduced to the contrary speak.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0143","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0144","text":"[I q. 85 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32066)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0145","text":"We proceed thus to the seventh [article]. It seems that one person cannot understand one and the same thing better than another. For Augustine says, in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, “whoever understands any thing otherwise than it is does not understand it. Wherefore it must not be doubted that there is a perfect understanding, than which there cannot be a more excellent; and therefore that it does not go on to infinity that any thing is understood; nor that one can understand it more than another.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0146","text":"[I q. 85 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32067)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0147","text":"Further, the intellect, in understanding, is true. But truth, since it is a certain equality of intellect and thing, does not admit of more and less; for something is not properly said to be more or less equal. Therefore neither is something said to be understood more or less.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0148","text":"[I q. 85 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32068)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0149","text":"Further, the intellect is what is most formal in man. But a difference of form causes a difference of species. Therefore, if one man understands more than another, it seems that they are not of one species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0150","text":"[I q. 85 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32069)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0151","text":"But on the contrary, by experience some are found to understand more deeply than others; just as one who can reduce some conclusion to first principles and first causes understands more deeply than one who can reduce it only to proximate causes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0152","text":"[I q. 85 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32070)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0153","text":"I answer that one person's understanding one and the same thing more than another's can be understood in two ways. In one way, such that the word “more” determines the act of understanding on the part of the thing understood. And thus one cannot understand the same thing more than another, because if he understood it to be otherwise than it is, whether better or worse, he would be mistaken and would not understand, as Augustine argues. In another way, it can be understood as determining the act of understanding on the part of the one understanding. And thus one person can understand the same thing better than another, because he has a better power in understanding; just as one who has a more perfect power, and in whom the visual power is more perfect, sees some thing better by bodily sight. This occurs concerning the intellect in two ways. In one way indeed, on the part of the intellect itself, which is more perfect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0154","text":"For it is manifest that the better disposed a body is, the better soul it obtains; which is manifestly apparent in things that are diverse according to species. The reason for this is that act and form are received in matter according to the capacity of matter. Hence, since even among human beings some have a body better disposed, they obtain a soul of greater power in understanding; whence it is said in *On the Soul* II that “we see those soft in flesh to be well endowed in mind.” In another way this occurs on the part of the lower powers, which the intellect needs for its operation; for those in whom the imaginative, cogitative, and memorative power is better disposed are better disposed for understanding.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0155","text":"[I q. 85 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32071)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0156","text":"The solution to the first is therefore clear from what has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0157","text":"[I q. 85 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32072)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0158","text":"And likewise to the second: for the truth of the intellect consists in this, that the thing is understood to be as it is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0158"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0159","text":"[I q. 85 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32073)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0159"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0160","text":"To the third, it must be said that a difference of form which arises only from a diverse disposition of matter does not make diversity according to species, but only according to number; for distinct individuals have distinct forms, diversified according to matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0160"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0161","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0161"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0162","text":"[I q. 85 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32074)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0162"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0163","text":"We proceed thus to the eighth [article]. It seems that our intellect knows the indivisible before the divisible. For the Philosopher says, in *Physics* I, that “we understand and know from knowledge of principles and elements.” But indivisibles are the principles and elements of divisibles. Therefore indivisibles are known to us before divisibles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0163"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0164","text":"[I q. 85 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32075)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0164"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0165","text":"Further, that which is placed in the definition of something is known by us beforehand, because a definition is from things prior and better known, as is said in *Topics* VI. But the indivisible is placed in the definition of the divisible; for “a line,” as Euclid says, “is a length without breadth, whose extremities are two points.” And unity is placed in the definition of number, because number is a multitude measured by one, as is said in *Metaphysics* X. Therefore our intellect understands the indivisible before the divisible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0165"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0166","text":"[I q. 85 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32076)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0166"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0167","text":"Further, like is known by like. But the indivisible is more like the intellect than the divisible, because the intellect is simple, as is said in *On the Soul* III. Therefore our intellect knows the indivisible first.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0167"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0168","text":"[I q. 85 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32077)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0168"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0169","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in *On the Soul* III that *the indivisible is shown as a privation*. But privation is known subsequently. Therefore, so too is the indivisible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0169"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0170","text":"[I q. 85 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32078)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0170"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0171","text":"I answer that it must be said that the object of our intellect, according to the present state, is the quiddity of a material thing, which it abstracts from phantasms, as is clear from what has been said above. And because that which is first and through itself known by a cognitive power is its proper object, the order in which the indivisible is understood by us can be considered from its relation to such a quiddity. Now, the indivisible is spoken of in three ways, as is said in *On the Soul* III. In one way, as the continuous is indivisible, because it is undivided in act, although divisible in potency. And such an indivisible is understood by us before its division, which is into parts, because confused cognition is prior to distinct cognition, as has been said. In another way, something is called indivisible according to species, as the notion of man is something indivisible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0171"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0172","text":"And in this way also the indivisible is understood before its division into parts of the notion, as was said above, and again before the intellect composes and divides, by affirming or denying. And the reason for this is that the intellect understands such a twofold indivisible through itself, as its proper object. In a third way, that is called indivisible which is altogether indivisible, as a point and unity, which are divided neither in act nor in potency. And such an indivisible is known subsequently, through the privation of the divisible. Hence a point is defined privatively: a point is that whose part does not exist; and similarly, the notion of one is that it be indivisible, as is said in *Metaphysics* X. And the reason for this is that such an indivisible has a certain opposition to a corporeal thing, the quiddity of which the intellect first and through itself apprehends.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0172"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0173","text":"But if our intellect understood through participation in separate indivisibles, as the Platonists held, it would follow that an indivisible of this kind would be understood first, because according to the Platonists, prior things are participated in first by things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0173"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0174","text":"[I q. 85 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32079)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0174"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0175","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that in acquiring knowledge, principles and elements are not always prior, because sometimes from sensible effects we arrive at knowledge of intelligible principles and causes. But in the completion of knowledge, knowledge of effects always depends on knowledge of principles and elements, because, as the Philosopher says in the same place, *then do we judge that we know, when we can resolve the things derived from principles into their causes*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0175"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0176","text":"[I q. 85 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32080)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0176"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0177","text":"To the second, it must be said that a point is not placed in the definition of line taken universally; for it is manifest that in an infinite line, and also in a circular one, there is no point except potentially. But Euclid defines a finite straight line, and therefore he placed a point in the definition of a line, as a terminus in the definition of what is terminated. Unity, however, is the measure of number, and therefore it is placed in the definition of measured number. But it is not placed in the definition of the divisible, but rather the reverse.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0177"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0178","text":"[I q. 85 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32081)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0178"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0179","text":"To the third, it must be said that the likeness through which we understand is the species of the thing known in the knower. And therefore, something is not known first according to the likeness of its nature to the cognitive power, but through its suitability to the object; otherwise sight would know hearing more than color.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_77:P0179"]}],"source_digest":"9188a5dffdb14edd0c4902cd6960004358a50a486f5e83f411fccc7c7b53aa68","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 85"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 85","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78","title":"First Part · Question 86","order":87,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_78","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 86 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32082)\n\nDeinde considerandum est quid intellectus noster in rebus materialibus cognoscat. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum cognoscat singularia. Secundo, utrum cognoscat infinita. Tertio, utrum cognoscat contingentia. Quarto, utrum cognoscat futura.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 86 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32083)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus noster cognoscat singularia. Quicumque enim cognoscit compositionem, cognoscit extrema compositionis. Sed intellectus noster cognoscit hanc compositionem. Socrates est homo, eius enim est propositionem formare. Ergo intellectus noster cognoscit hoc singulare quod est Socrates.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32084)\n\nPraeterea, intellectus practicus dirigit ad agendum. Sed actus sunt circa singularia. Ergo cognoscit singularia.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32085)\n\nPraeterea, intellectus noster intelligit seipsum. Ipse autem est quoddam singulare, alioquin non haberet aliquem actum; actus enim singularium sunt. Ergo intellectus noster cognoscit singulare.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32086)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid potest virtus inferior, potest superior. Sed sensus cognoscit singulare. Ergo multo magis intellectus.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32087)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit philosophus, in I Physic., quod *universale secundum rationem est notum, singulare autem secundum sensum*.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32088)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod singulare in rebus materialibus intellectus noster directe et primo cognoscere non potest. Cuius ratio est, quia principium singularitatis in rebus materialibus est materia individualis, intellectus autem noster, sicut supra dictum est, intelligit abstrahendo speciem intelligibilem ab huiusmodi materia. Quod autem a materia individuali abstrahitur, est universale. Unde intellectus noster directe non est cognoscitivus nisi universalium. Indirecte autem, et quasi per quandam reflexionem, potest cognoscere singulare, quia, sicut supra dictum est, etiam postquam species intelligibiles abstraxit, non potest secundum eas actu intelligere nisi convertendo se ad phantasmata, in quibus species intelligibiles intelligit, ut dicitur in III de anima. Sic igitur ipsum universale per speciem intelligibilem directe intelligit; indirecte autem singularia, quorum sunt phantasmata. Et hoc modo format hanc propositionem, Socrates est homo.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32089)\n\nUnde patet solutio ad primum.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32090)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod electio particularis operabilis est quasi conclusio syllogismi intellectus practici, ut dicitur in VII Ethic. Ex universali autem propositione directe non potest concludi singularis, nisi mediante aliqua singulari propositione assumpta. Unde universalis ratio intellectus practici non movet nisi mediante particulari apprehensione sensitivae partis, ut dicitur in III de anima.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32091)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod singulare non repugnat intelligibilitati inquantum est singulare, sed inquantum est materiale, quia nihil intelligitur nisi immaterialiter. Et ideo si sit aliquod singulare immateriale, sicut est intellectus, hoc non repugnat intelligibilitati.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32092)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod virtus superior potest illud quod potest virtus inferior, sed eminentiori modo. Unde id quod cognoscit sensus materialiter et concrete, quod est cognoscere singulare directe, hoc cognoscit intellectus immaterialiter et abstracte, quod est cognoscere universale.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 86 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32093)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus noster possit cognoscere infinita. Deus enim excedit omnia infinita. Sed intellectus noster potest cognoscere Deum, ut supra dictum est. Ergo multo magis potest cognoscere omnia alia infinita.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32094)\n\nPraeterea, intellectus noster natus est cognoscere genera et species. Sed quorundam generum sunt infinitae species, sicut numeri, proportionis et figurae. Ergo intellectus noster potest cognoscere infinita.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32095)\n\nPraeterea, si unum corpus non impediret aliud ab existendo in uno et eodem loco, nihil prohiberet infinita corpora in uno loco esse. Sed una species intelligibilis non prohibet aliam ab existendo simul in eodem intellectu, contingit enim multa scire in habitu. Ergo nihil prohibet intellectum nostrum infinitorum scientiam habere in habitu.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32096)\n\nPraeterea, intellectus, cum non sit virtus materiae corporalis, ut supra dictum est, videtur esse potentia infinita. Sed virtus infinita potest super infinita. Ergo intellectus noster potest cognoscere infinita.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32097)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in I Physic., quod *infinitum, inquantum est infinitum, est ignotum*.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32098)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum potentia proportionetur suo obiecto, oportet hoc modo se habere intellectum ad infinitum, sicut se habet eius obiectum, quod est quidditas rei materialis. In rebus autem materialibus non invenitur infinitum in actu, sed solum in potentia, secundum quod unum succedit alteri, ut dicitur in III Physic. Et ideo in intellectu nostro invenitur infinitum in potentia, in accipiendo scilicet unum post aliud, quia nunquam intellectus noster tot intelligit, quin possit plura intelligere. Actu autem vel habitu non potest cognoscere infinita intellectus noster. Actu quidem non, quia intellectus noster non potest simul actu cognoscere nisi quod per unam speciem cognoscit. Infinitum autem non habet unam speciem, alioquin haberet rationem totius et perfecti. Et ideo non potest intelligi nisi accipiendo partem post partem, ut ex eius definitione patet in III Physic., *est enim infinitum cuius quantitatem accipientibus semper est aliquid extra accipere*, et sic infinitum cognosci non posset actu, nisi omnes partes eius numerarentur, quod est impossibile. Et eadem ratione non possumus intelligere infinita in habitu. In nobis enim habitualis cognitio causatur ex actuali consideratione, intelligendo enim efficimur scientes, ut dicitur in II Ethic. Unde non possemus habere habitum infinitorum secundum distinctam cognitionem, nisi consideravissemus omnia infinita, numerando ea secundum cognitionis successionem, quod est impossibile. Et ita nec actu nec habitu intellectus noster potest cognoscere infinita, sed in potentia tantum, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32099)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, Deus dicitur infinitus sicut forma quae non est terminata per aliquam materiam, in rebus autem materialibus aliquid dicitur infinitum per privationem formalis terminationis. Et quia forma secundum se nota est, materia autem sine forma ignota, inde est quod infinitum materiale est secundum se ignotum. Infinitum autem formale, quod est Deus, est secundum se notum, ignotum autem quoad nos, propter defectum intellectus nostri, qui secundum statum praesentis vitae habet naturalem aptitudinem ad materialia cognoscenda. Et ideo in praesenti Deum cognoscere non possumus nisi per materiales effectus. In futuro autem tolletur defectus intellectus nostri per gloriam, et tunc ipsum Deum in sua essentia videre poterimus, tamen absque comprehensione.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32100)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod intellectus noster natus est cognoscere species per abstractionem a phantasmatibus. Et ideo illas species numerorum et figurarum quas quis non est imaginatus, non potest cognoscere nec actu nec habitu, nisi forte in genere et in principiis universalibus; quod est cognoscere in potentia et confuse.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32101)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, si duo corpora essent in uno loco, vel plura, non oporteret quod successive subintrarent locum, ut sic per ipsam subintrationis successionem numerarentur locata. Sed species intelligibiles ingrediuntur intellectum nostrum successive, quia non multa simul actu intelliguntur. Et ideo oportet numeratas, et non infinitas species esse in intellectu nostro.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32102)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod sicut intellectus noster est infinitus virtute, ita infinitum cognoscit. Est enim virtus eius infinita, secundum quod non terminatur per materiam corporalem. Et est cognoscitivus universalis, quod est abstractum a materia individuali, et per consequens non finitur ad aliquod individuum, sed, quantum est de se, ad infinita individua se extendit.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 86 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32103)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus non sit cognoscitivus contingentium. Quia, ut dicitur in VI Ethic., intellectus et sapientia et scientia non sunt contingentium, sed necessariorum.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32104)\n\nPraeterea, sicut dicitur in IV Physic., *ea quae quandoque sunt et quandoque non sunt, tempore mensurantur*. Intellectus autem a tempore abstrahit, sicut et ab aliis conditionibus materiae. Cum igitur proprium contingentium sit quandoque esse et quandoque non esse, videtur quod contingentia non cognoscantur ab intellectu.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32105)\n\nSed contra, omnis scientia est in intellectu. Sed quaedam scientiae sunt de contingentibus; sicut scientiae morales, quae sunt de actibus humanis subiectis libero arbitrio; et etiam scientiae naturales, quantum ad partem quae tractat de generabilibus et corruptibilibus. Ergo intellectus est cognoscitivus contingentium.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32106)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod contingentia dupliciter possunt considerari. Uno modo, secundum quod contingentia sunt. Alio modo, secundum quod in eis aliquid necessitatis invenitur, nihil enim est adeo contingens, quin in se aliquid necessarium habeat. Sicut hoc ipsum quod est Socratem currere, in se quidem contingens est; sed habitudo cursus ad motum est necessaria, necessarium enim est Socratem moveri, si currit. Est autem unumquodque contingens ex parte materiae, quia contingens est quod potest esse et non esse; potentia autem pertinet ad materiam. Necessitas autem consequitur rationem formae, quia ea quae consequuntur ad formam, ex necessitate insunt. Materia autem est individuationis principium, ratio autem universalis accipitur secundum abstractionem formae a materia particulari. Dictum autem est supra quod per se et directe intellectus est universalium; sensus autem singularium, quorum etiam indirecte quodammodo est intellectus, ut supra dictum est. Sic igitur contingentia, prout sunt contingentia, cognoscuntur directe quidem sensu, indirecte autem ab intellectu, rationes autem universales et necessariae contingentium cognoscuntur per intellectum. Unde si attendantur rationes universales scibilium, omnes scientiae sunt de necessariis. Si autem attendantur ipsae res, sic quaedam scientia est de necessariis, quaedam vero de contingentibus.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 3 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32107)\n\nEt per hoc patet solutio ad obiecta.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 86 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32108)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus noster cognoscat futura. Intellectus enim noster cognoscit per species intelligibiles, quae abstrahunt ab hic et nunc, et ita se habent indifferenter ad omne tempus. Sed potest cognoscere praesentia. Ergo potest cognoscere futura.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32109)\n\nPraeterea, homo quando alienatur a sensibus, aliqua futura cognoscere potest; ut patet in dormientibus et phreneticis. Sed quando alienatur a sensibus, magis viget intellectu. Ergo intellectus, quantum est de se, est cognoscitivus futurorum.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32110)\n\nPraeterea, cognitio intellectiva hominis efficacior est quam cognitio quaecumque brutorum animalium. Sed quaedam animalia sunt quae cognoscunt quaedam futura; sicut corniculae frequenter crocitantes significant pluviam mox futuram. Ergo multo magis intellectus humanus potest futura cognoscere.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32111)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Eccle. VIII, *multa hominis afflictio, qui ignorat praeterita, et futura nullo potest scire nuntio*.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32112)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod de cognitione futurorum eodem modo distinguendum est, sicut de cognitione contingentium. Nam ipsa futura ut sub tempore cadunt, sunt singularia, quae intellectus humanus non cognoscit nisi per reflexionem, ut supra dictum est. Rationes autem futurorum possunt esse universales, et intellectu perceptibiles, et de eis etiam possunt esse scientiae. Ut tamen communiter de cognitione futurorum loquamur, sciendum est quod futura dupliciter cognosci possunt, uno modo, in seipsis; alio modo, in suis causis. In seipsis quidem futura cognosci non possunt nisi a Deo; cui etiam sunt praesentia dum in cursu rerum sunt futura, inquantum eius aeternus intuitus simul fertur supra totum temporis cursum, ut supra dictum est cum de Dei scientia ageretur. Sed prout sunt in suis causis, cognosci possunt etiam a nobis. Et si quidem in suis causis sint ut ex quibus ex necessitate proveniant, cognoscuntur per certitudinem scientiae; sicut astrologus praecognoscit eclipsim futuram. Si autem sic sint in suis causis ut ab eis proveniant ut in pluribus, sic cognosci possunt per quandam coniecturam vel magis vel minus certam, secundum quod causae sunt vel magis vel minus inclinatae ad effectus.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32113)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ratio illa procedit de cognitione quae fit per rationes universales causarum, ex quibus futura cognosci possunt secundum modum ordinis effectus ad causam.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32114)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit XII Confess., anima habet quandam vim sortis, ut ex sui natura possit futura cognoscere, et ideo quando retrahitur a corporeis sensibus, et quodammodo revertitur ad seipsam, fit particeps notitiae futurorum. Et haec quidem opinio rationabilis esset, si poneremus quod anima acciperet cognitionem rerum secundum participationem idearum, sicut Platonici posuerunt, quia sic anima ex sui natura cognosceret universales causas omnium effectuum, sed impeditur per corpus; unde quando a corporis sensibus abstrahitur, futura cognoscit. Sed quia iste modus cognoscendi non est connaturalis intellectui nostro, sed magis ut cognitionem a sensibus accipiat; ideo non est secundum naturam animae quod futura cognoscat cum a sensibus alienatur; sed magis per impressionem aliquarum causarum superiorum spiritualium et corporalium. Spiritualium quidem, sicut cum virtute divina ministerio Angelorum intellectus humanus illuminatur, et phantasmata ordinantur ad futura aliqua cognoscenda; vel etiam cum per operationem Daemonum fit aliqua commotio in phantasia ad praesignandum aliqua futura quae Daemones cognoscunt, ut supra dictum est. Huiusmodi autem impressiones spiritualium causarum magis nata est anima humana suscipere cum a sensibus alienatur, quia per hoc propinquior fit substantiis spiritualibus, et magis libera ab exterioribus inquietudinibus. Contingit autem et hoc per impressionem superiorum causarum corporalium. Manifestum est enim quod corpora superiora imprimunt in corpora inferiora. Unde cum vires sensitivae sint actus corporalium organorum, consequens est quod ex impressione caelestium corporum immutetur quodammodo phantasia. Unde cum caelestia corpora sint causa multorum futurorum, fiunt in imaginatione aliqua signa quorundam futurorum. Haec autem signa magis percipiuntur in nocte et a dormientibus, quam de die et a vigilantibus, quia, ut dicitur in libro de Somn. et Vigil., *quae deferuntur de die, dissolvuntur magis; plus est enim sine turbatione aer noctis, eo quod silentiores sunt noctes. Et in corpore faciunt sensum propter somnum, quia parvi motus interiores magis sentiuntur a dormientibus quam a vigilantibus. Hi vero motus faciunt phantasmata, ex quibus praevidentur futura*.\n\n[I q. 86 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32115)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod animalia bruta non habent aliquid supra phantasiam quod ordinet phantasmata, sicut habent homines rationem; et ideo phantasia brutorum animalium totaliter sequitur impressionem caelestem. Et ideo ex motibus huiusmodi animalium magis possunt cognosci quaedam futura, ut pluvia et huiusmodi, quam ex motibus hominum, qui moventur per consilium rationis. Unde philosophus dicit, in libro de Somn. et Vigil., quod *quidam imprudentissimi sunt maxime praevidentes, nam intelligentia horum non est curis affecta, sed tanquam deserta et vacua ab omnibus, et mota secundum movens ducitur*.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0002","text":"[I q. 86 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32082)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est quid intellectus noster in rebus materialibus cognoscat. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum cognoscat singularia. Secundo, utrum cognoscat infinita. Tertio, utrum cognoscat contingentia. Quarto, utrum cognoscat futura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0005","text":"[I q. 86 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32083)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus noster cognoscat singularia. Quicumque enim cognoscit compositionem, cognoscit extrema compositionis. Sed intellectus noster cognoscit hanc compositionem. Socrates est homo, eius enim est propositionem formare. Ergo intellectus noster cognoscit hoc singulare quod est Socrates.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0007","text":"[I q. 86 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32084)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0008","text":"Praeterea, intellectus practicus dirigit ad agendum. Sed actus sunt circa singularia. Ergo cognoscit singularia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0009","text":"[I q. 86 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32085)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0010","text":"Praeterea, intellectus noster intelligit seipsum. Ipse autem est quoddam singulare, alioquin non haberet aliquem actum; actus enim singularium sunt. Ergo intellectus noster cognoscit singulare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0011","text":"[I q. 86 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32086)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0012","text":"Praeterea, quidquid potest virtus inferior, potest superior. Sed sensus cognoscit singulare. Ergo multo magis intellectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0013","text":"[I q. 86 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32087)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0014","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit philosophus, in I Physic., quod *universale secundum rationem est notum, singulare autem secundum sensum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0015","text":"[I q. 86 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32088)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod singulare in rebus materialibus intellectus noster directe et primo cognoscere non potest. Cuius ratio est, quia principium singularitatis in rebus materialibus est materia individualis, intellectus autem noster, sicut supra dictum est, intelligit abstrahendo speciem intelligibilem ab huiusmodi materia. Quod autem a materia individuali abstrahitur, est universale. Unde intellectus noster directe non est cognoscitivus nisi universalium. Indirecte autem, et quasi per quandam reflexionem, potest cognoscere singulare, quia, sicut supra dictum est, etiam postquam species intelligibiles abstraxit, non potest secundum eas actu intelligere nisi convertendo se ad phantasmata, in quibus species intelligibiles intelligit, ut dicitur in III de anima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0017","text":"Sic igitur ipsum universale per speciem intelligibilem directe intelligit; indirecte autem singularia, quorum sunt phantasmata. Et hoc modo format hanc propositionem, Socrates est homo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0018","text":"[I q. 86 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32089)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0019","text":"Unde patet solutio ad primum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0020","text":"[I q. 86 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32090)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0021","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod electio particularis operabilis est quasi conclusio syllogismi intellectus practici, ut dicitur in VII Ethic. Ex universali autem propositione directe non potest concludi singularis, nisi mediante aliqua singulari propositione assumpta. Unde universalis ratio intellectus practici non movet nisi mediante particulari apprehensione sensitivae partis, ut dicitur in III de anima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0022","text":"[I q. 86 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32091)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0023","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod singulare non repugnat intelligibilitati inquantum est singulare, sed inquantum est materiale, quia nihil intelligitur nisi immaterialiter. Et ideo si sit aliquod singulare immateriale, sicut est intellectus, hoc non repugnat intelligibilitati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0024","text":"[I q. 86 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32092)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0025","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod virtus superior potest illud quod potest virtus inferior, sed eminentiori modo. Unde id quod cognoscit sensus materialiter et concrete, quod est cognoscere singulare directe, hoc cognoscit intellectus immaterialiter et abstracte, quod est cognoscere universale.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0026","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0027","text":"[I q. 86 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32093)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0028","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus noster possit cognoscere infinita. Deus enim excedit omnia infinita. Sed intellectus noster potest cognoscere Deum, ut supra dictum est. Ergo multo magis potest cognoscere omnia alia infinita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0029","text":"[I q. 86 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32094)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0030","text":"Praeterea, intellectus noster natus est cognoscere genera et species. Sed quorundam generum sunt infinitae species, sicut numeri, proportionis et figurae. Ergo intellectus noster potest cognoscere infinita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0031","text":"[I q. 86 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32095)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0032","text":"Praeterea, si unum corpus non impediret aliud ab existendo in uno et eodem loco, nihil prohiberet infinita corpora in uno loco esse. Sed una species intelligibilis non prohibet aliam ab existendo simul in eodem intellectu, contingit enim multa scire in habitu. Ergo nihil prohibet intellectum nostrum infinitorum scientiam habere in habitu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0033","text":"[I q. 86 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32096)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0034","text":"Praeterea, intellectus, cum non sit virtus materiae corporalis, ut supra dictum est, videtur esse potentia infinita. Sed virtus infinita potest super infinita. Ergo intellectus noster potest cognoscere infinita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0035","text":"[I q. 86 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32097)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0036","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in I Physic., quod *infinitum, inquantum est infinitum, est ignotum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0037","text":"[I q. 86 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32098)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0038","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum potentia proportionetur suo obiecto, oportet hoc modo se habere intellectum ad infinitum, sicut se habet eius obiectum, quod est quidditas rei materialis. In rebus autem materialibus non invenitur infinitum in actu, sed solum in potentia, secundum quod unum succedit alteri, ut dicitur in III Physic. Et ideo in intellectu nostro invenitur infinitum in potentia, in accipiendo scilicet unum post aliud, quia nunquam intellectus noster tot intelligit, quin possit plura intelligere. Actu autem vel habitu non potest cognoscere infinita intellectus noster. Actu quidem non, quia intellectus noster non potest simul actu cognoscere nisi quod per unam speciem cognoscit. Infinitum autem non habet unam speciem, alioquin haberet rationem totius et perfecti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0039","text":"Et ideo non potest intelligi nisi accipiendo partem post partem, ut ex eius definitione patet in III Physic., *est enim infinitum cuius quantitatem accipientibus semper est aliquid extra accipere*, et sic infinitum cognosci non posset actu, nisi omnes partes eius numerarentur, quod est impossibile. Et eadem ratione non possumus intelligere infinita in habitu. In nobis enim habitualis cognitio causatur ex actuali consideratione, intelligendo enim efficimur scientes, ut dicitur in II Ethic. Unde non possemus habere habitum infinitorum secundum distinctam cognitionem, nisi consideravissemus omnia infinita, numerando ea secundum cognitionis successionem, quod est impossibile. Et ita nec actu nec habitu intellectus noster potest cognoscere infinita, sed in potentia tantum, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0040","text":"[I q. 86 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32099)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0041","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, Deus dicitur infinitus sicut forma quae non est terminata per aliquam materiam, in rebus autem materialibus aliquid dicitur infinitum per privationem formalis terminationis. Et quia forma secundum se nota est, materia autem sine forma ignota, inde est quod infinitum materiale est secundum se ignotum. Infinitum autem formale, quod est Deus, est secundum se notum, ignotum autem quoad nos, propter defectum intellectus nostri, qui secundum statum praesentis vitae habet naturalem aptitudinem ad materialia cognoscenda. Et ideo in praesenti Deum cognoscere non possumus nisi per materiales effectus. In futuro autem tolletur defectus intellectus nostri per gloriam, et tunc ipsum Deum in sua essentia videre poterimus, tamen absque comprehensione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0042","text":"[I q. 86 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32100)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0043","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod intellectus noster natus est cognoscere species per abstractionem a phantasmatibus. Et ideo illas species numerorum et figurarum quas quis non est imaginatus, non potest cognoscere nec actu nec habitu, nisi forte in genere et in principiis universalibus; quod est cognoscere in potentia et confuse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0044","text":"[I q. 86 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32101)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0045","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, si duo corpora essent in uno loco, vel plura, non oporteret quod successive subintrarent locum, ut sic per ipsam subintrationis successionem numerarentur locata. Sed species intelligibiles ingrediuntur intellectum nostrum successive, quia non multa simul actu intelliguntur. Et ideo oportet numeratas, et non infinitas species esse in intellectu nostro.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0046","text":"[I q. 86 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32102)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0047","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod sicut intellectus noster est infinitus virtute, ita infinitum cognoscit. Est enim virtus eius infinita, secundum quod non terminatur per materiam corporalem. Et est cognoscitivus universalis, quod est abstractum a materia individuali, et per consequens non finitur ad aliquod individuum, sed, quantum est de se, ad infinita individua se extendit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0048","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0049","text":"[I q. 86 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32103)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0050","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus non sit cognoscitivus contingentium. Quia, ut dicitur in VI Ethic., intellectus et sapientia et scientia non sunt contingentium, sed necessariorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0051","text":"[I q. 86 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32104)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0052","text":"Praeterea, sicut dicitur in IV Physic., *ea quae quandoque sunt et quandoque non sunt, tempore mensurantur*. Intellectus autem a tempore abstrahit, sicut et ab aliis conditionibus materiae. Cum igitur proprium contingentium sit quandoque esse et quandoque non esse, videtur quod contingentia non cognoscantur ab intellectu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0053","text":"[I q. 86 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32105)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0054","text":"Sed contra, omnis scientia est in intellectu. Sed quaedam scientiae sunt de contingentibus; sicut scientiae morales, quae sunt de actibus humanis subiectis libero arbitrio; et etiam scientiae naturales, quantum ad partem quae tractat de generabilibus et corruptibilibus. Ergo intellectus est cognoscitivus contingentium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0055","text":"[I q. 86 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32106)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0056","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod contingentia dupliciter possunt considerari. Uno modo, secundum quod contingentia sunt. Alio modo, secundum quod in eis aliquid necessitatis invenitur, nihil enim est adeo contingens, quin in se aliquid necessarium habeat. Sicut hoc ipsum quod est Socratem currere, in se quidem contingens est; sed habitudo cursus ad motum est necessaria, necessarium enim est Socratem moveri, si currit. Est autem unumquodque contingens ex parte materiae, quia contingens est quod potest esse et non esse; potentia autem pertinet ad materiam. Necessitas autem consequitur rationem formae, quia ea quae consequuntur ad formam, ex necessitate insunt. Materia autem est individuationis principium, ratio autem universalis accipitur secundum abstractionem formae a materia particulari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0057","text":"Dictum autem est supra quod per se et directe intellectus est universalium; sensus autem singularium, quorum etiam indirecte quodammodo est intellectus, ut supra dictum est. Sic igitur contingentia, prout sunt contingentia, cognoscuntur directe quidem sensu, indirecte autem ab intellectu, rationes autem universales et necessariae contingentium cognoscuntur per intellectum. Unde si attendantur rationes universales scibilium, omnes scientiae sunt de necessariis. Si autem attendantur ipsae res, sic quaedam scientia est de necessariis, quaedam vero de contingentibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0058","text":"[I q. 86 a. 3 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32107)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0059","text":"Et per hoc patet solutio ad obiecta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0060","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0061","text":"[I q. 86 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32108)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0062","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus noster cognoscat futura. Intellectus enim noster cognoscit per species intelligibiles, quae abstrahunt ab hic et nunc, et ita se habent indifferenter ad omne tempus. Sed potest cognoscere praesentia. Ergo potest cognoscere futura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0063","text":"[I q. 86 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32109)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0064","text":"Praeterea, homo quando alienatur a sensibus, aliqua futura cognoscere potest; ut patet in dormientibus et phreneticis. Sed quando alienatur a sensibus, magis viget intellectu. Ergo intellectus, quantum est de se, est cognoscitivus futurorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0065","text":"[I q. 86 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32110)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0066","text":"Praeterea, cognitio intellectiva hominis efficacior est quam cognitio quaecumque brutorum animalium. Sed quaedam animalia sunt quae cognoscunt quaedam futura; sicut corniculae frequenter crocitantes significant pluviam mox futuram. Ergo multo magis intellectus humanus potest futura cognoscere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0067","text":"[I q. 86 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32111)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0068","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Eccle. VIII, *multa hominis afflictio, qui ignorat praeterita, et futura nullo potest scire nuntio*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0069","text":"[I q. 86 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32112)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0070","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod de cognitione futurorum eodem modo distinguendum est, sicut de cognitione contingentium. Nam ipsa futura ut sub tempore cadunt, sunt singularia, quae intellectus humanus non cognoscit nisi per reflexionem, ut supra dictum est. Rationes autem futurorum possunt esse universales, et intellectu perceptibiles, et de eis etiam possunt esse scientiae. Ut tamen communiter de cognitione futurorum loquamur, sciendum est quod futura dupliciter cognosci possunt, uno modo, in seipsis; alio modo, in suis causis. In seipsis quidem futura cognosci non possunt nisi a Deo; cui etiam sunt praesentia dum in cursu rerum sunt futura, inquantum eius aeternus intuitus simul fertur supra totum temporis cursum, ut supra dictum est cum de Dei scientia ageretur. Sed prout sunt in suis causis, cognosci possunt etiam a nobis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0071","text":"Et si quidem in suis causis sint ut ex quibus ex necessitate proveniant, cognoscuntur per certitudinem scientiae; sicut astrologus praecognoscit eclipsim futuram. Si autem sic sint in suis causis ut ab eis proveniant ut in pluribus, sic cognosci possunt per quandam coniecturam vel magis vel minus certam, secundum quod causae sunt vel magis vel minus inclinatae ad effectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0072","text":"[I q. 86 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32113)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0073","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ratio illa procedit de cognitione quae fit per rationes universales causarum, ex quibus futura cognosci possunt secundum modum ordinis effectus ad causam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0074","text":"[I q. 86 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32114)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0075","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit XII Confess., anima habet quandam vim sortis, ut ex sui natura possit futura cognoscere, et ideo quando retrahitur a corporeis sensibus, et quodammodo revertitur ad seipsam, fit particeps notitiae futurorum. Et haec quidem opinio rationabilis esset, si poneremus quod anima acciperet cognitionem rerum secundum participationem idearum, sicut Platonici posuerunt, quia sic anima ex sui natura cognosceret universales causas omnium effectuum, sed impeditur per corpus; unde quando a corporis sensibus abstrahitur, futura cognoscit. Sed quia iste modus cognoscendi non est connaturalis intellectui nostro, sed magis ut cognitionem a sensibus accipiat; ideo non est secundum naturam animae quod futura cognoscat cum a sensibus alienatur; sed magis per impressionem aliquarum causarum superiorum spiritualium et corporalium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0076","text":"Spiritualium quidem, sicut cum virtute divina ministerio Angelorum intellectus humanus illuminatur, et phantasmata ordinantur ad futura aliqua cognoscenda; vel etiam cum per operationem Daemonum fit aliqua commotio in phantasia ad praesignandum aliqua futura quae Daemones cognoscunt, ut supra dictum est. Huiusmodi autem impressiones spiritualium causarum magis nata est anima humana suscipere cum a sensibus alienatur, quia per hoc propinquior fit substantiis spiritualibus, et magis libera ab exterioribus inquietudinibus. Contingit autem et hoc per impressionem superiorum causarum corporalium. Manifestum est enim quod corpora superiora imprimunt in corpora inferiora. Unde cum vires sensitivae sint actus corporalium organorum, consequens est quod ex impressione caelestium corporum immutetur quodammodo phantasia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0077","text":"Unde cum caelestia corpora sint causa multorum futurorum, fiunt in imaginatione aliqua signa quorundam futurorum. Haec autem signa magis percipiuntur in nocte et a dormientibus, quam de die et a vigilantibus, quia, ut dicitur in libro de Somn. et Vigil., *quae deferuntur de die, dissolvuntur magis; plus est enim sine turbatione aer noctis, eo quod silentiores sunt noctes. Et in corpore faciunt sensum propter somnum, quia parvi motus interiores magis sentiuntur a dormientibus quam a vigilantibus. Hi vero motus faciunt phantasmata, ex quibus praevidentur futura*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0078","text":"[I q. 86 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32115)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0079","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod animalia bruta non habent aliquid supra phantasiam quod ordinet phantasmata, sicut habent homines rationem; et ideo phantasia brutorum animalium totaliter sequitur impressionem caelestem. Et ideo ex motibus huiusmodi animalium magis possunt cognosci quaedam futura, ut pluvia et huiusmodi, quam ex motibus hominum, qui moventur per consilium rationis. Unde philosophus dicit, in libro de Somn. et Vigil., quod *quidam imprudentissimi sunt maxime praevidentes, nam intelligentia horum non est curis affecta, sed tanquam deserta et vacua ab omnibus, et mota secundum movens ducitur*.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"bfea6acb48e7b802ba4b3a11849227c75f3b038eb20aa7b7e9fb29f09dd040d3","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T151915746240Z_73024e18","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 86","source_sha256":"bfea6acb48e7b802ba4b3a11849227c75f3b038eb20aa7b7e9fb29f09dd040d3","sha256":"9a8f4dff4eafb04b7df725749f9a6202462fa1042a23d967a327627f75863610","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:19:15.746257+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0002","text":"[I q. 86 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32082)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0003","text":"Next, one must consider what our intellect knows in material things. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, whether it knows singulars. Second, whether it knows infinites. Third, whether it knows contingents. Fourth, whether it knows future things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0005","text":"[I q. 86 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32083)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that our intellect knows singulars. For whoever knows a composition knows the extremes of the composition. But our intellect knows this composition: Socrates is a human being; for it belongs to it to form a proposition. Therefore our intellect knows this singular, Socrates.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0007","text":"[I q. 86 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32084)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0008","text":"Moreover, the practical intellect directs toward acting. But acts concern singulars. Therefore it knows singulars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0009","text":"[I q. 86 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32085)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0010","text":"Moreover, our intellect understands itself. But it itself is a certain singular; otherwise, it would not have any act, for acts belong to singulars. Therefore our intellect knows a singular.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0011","text":"[I q. 86 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32086)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0012","text":"Moreover, whatever a lower power can do, a higher power can do. But sense knows the singular. Therefore, much more does the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0013","text":"[I q. 86 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32087)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0014","text":"But to the contrary is what the Philosopher says in Physics I: *the universal is known according to reason, but the singular according to sense*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0015","text":"[I q. 86 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32088)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0016","text":"I answer that it must be said that our intellect cannot know the singular in material things directly and primarily. The reason for this is that the principle of singularity in material things is individual matter, whereas our intellect, as was said above, understands by abstracting the intelligible species from matter of this sort. But what is abstracted from individual matter is universal. Hence our intellect is directly cognizant only of universals. Indirectly, however, and as it were by a certain reflection, it can know the singular, because, as was said above, even after it has abstracted intelligible species, it cannot actually understand through them except by turning itself toward phantasms, in which it understands the intelligible species, as is said in On the Soul III.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0017","text":"Thus, therefore, it understands the universal itself directly through the intelligible species; but indirectly it understands the singulars of which there are phantasms. And in this way it forms this proposition: Socrates is a human being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0018","text":"[I q. 86 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32089)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0019","text":"Hence the reply to the first is evident.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0020","text":"[I q. 86 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32090)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0021","text":"To the second, it must be said that the choice of a particular thing to be done is as it were the conclusion of a syllogism of the practical intellect, as is said in Ethics VII. But from a universal proposition a singular proposition cannot be concluded directly, except through some assumed singular proposition. Hence the universal reason of the practical intellect does not move except through the mediation of a particular apprehension of the sensitive part, as is said in On the Soul III.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0022","text":"[I q. 86 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32091)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0023","text":"To the third, it must be said that the singular is not opposed to intelligibility insofar as it is singular, but insofar as it is material, because nothing is understood except immaterially. And therefore, if there is some immaterial singular, as the intellect is, this is not opposed to intelligibility.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0024","text":"[I q. 86 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32092)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0025","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that a higher power can do what a lower power can do, but in a more eminent way. Hence what sense knows materially and concretely, which is to know the singular directly, intellect knows immaterially and abstractly, which is to know the universal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0026","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0027","text":"[I q. 86 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32093)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0028","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that our intellect can know infinities. For God exceeds all infinities. But our intellect can know God, as was said above. Therefore, much more can it know all other infinities.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0029","text":"[I q. 86 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32094)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0030","text":"Further, our intellect is naturally apt to know genera and species. But some genera have infinite species, such as numbers, proportions, and figures. Therefore our intellect can know infinities.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0031","text":"[I q. 86 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32095)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0032","text":"Further, if one body did not prevent another from existing in one and the same place, nothing would prevent infinite bodies from being in one place. But one intelligible species does not prevent another from existing simultaneously in the same intellect; for it happens that one knows many things habitually. Therefore nothing prevents our intellect from having habitual knowledge of infinities.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0033","text":"[I q. 86 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32096)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0034","text":"Further, since intellect is not a power of corporeal matter, as was said above, it seems to be an infinite power. But an infinite power can extend to infinities. Therefore our intellect can know infinities.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0035","text":"[I q. 86 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32097)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0036","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Physics I that *the infinite, insofar as it is infinite, is unknowable*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0037","text":"[I q. 86 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32098)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0038","text":"I answer that it must be said that, since a power is proportioned to its object, intellect must be related to the infinite in the same way as its object is related to it, namely, the quiddity of a material thing. Now, in material things the infinite is not found in act, but only in potency, insofar as one succeeds another, as is said in Physics III. And therefore the infinite is found in our intellect in potency, namely in taking one thing after another, because our intellect never understands so many things that it cannot understand more. But our intellect cannot know infinities either actually or habitually. Not actually, indeed, because our intellect cannot know actually at once except what it knows through one species. But the infinite does not have one species; otherwise it would have the character of a whole and of something perfect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0039","text":"And therefore it cannot be understood except by taking part after part, as is clear from its definition in Physics III: *for the infinite is that whose quantity, for those taking it, always has something outside to be taken*; and thus the infinite could not be known actually unless all its parts were counted, which is impossible. And for the same reason we cannot understand infinities habitually. For habitual knowledge in us is caused by actual consideration; for by understanding we become knowing, as is said in Ethics II. Hence we could not have a habit of infinities according to distinct knowledge unless we had considered all infinities, counting them according to the succession of cognition, which is impossible. And thus our intellect can know infinities neither actually nor habitually, but only potentially, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0040","text":"[I q. 86 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32099)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0041","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, as was said above, God is called infinite as a form which is not limited by any matter; but in material things something is called infinite through the privation of formal limitation. And because form is known in itself, whereas matter without form is unknown, it follows from this that the material infinite is in itself unknowable. But the formal infinite, which is God, is in itself knowable, yet unknown with respect to us, on account of the defect of our intellect, which, according to the state of the present life, has a natural aptitude for knowing material things. And therefore at present we cannot know God except through material effects. But in the future the defect of our intellect will be removed through glory, and then we shall be able to see God Himself in His essence, yet without comprehension.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0042","text":"[I q. 86 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32100)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0043","text":"To the second, it must be said that our intellect is naturally apt to know species by abstraction from phantasms. And therefore the species of numbers and figures that someone has not imagined, he cannot know either actually or habitually, except perhaps in their genus and in universal principles; which is to know potentially and confusedly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0044","text":"[I q. 86 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32101)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0045","text":"To the third, it must be said that, if two or more bodies were in one place, it would not be necessary for them to enter the place successively, so that the things placed there would be counted through the very succession of their entering. But intelligible species enter our intellect successively, because many things are not understood actually at the same time. And therefore it is necessary that there be numbered, and not infinite, species in our intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0046","text":"[I q. 86 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32102)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0047","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, just as our intellect is infinite in power, so it knows the infinite. For its power is infinite insofar as it is not limited by corporeal matter. And it is cognizant of the universal, which is abstracted from individual matter, and consequently is not limited to any individual, but, of itself, extends to infinite individuals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0048","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0049","text":"[I q. 86 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32103)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0050","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that the intellect is not cognizant of contingents. For, as is said in *Ethics* VI, intellect and wisdom and science are not concerned with contingents, but with necessities.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0051","text":"[I q. 86 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32104)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0052","text":"Further, as is said in *Physics* IV, *those things which sometimes are and sometimes are not are measured by time*. But the intellect abstracts from time, just as it does from the other conditions of matter. Therefore, since it is proper to contingents sometimes to be and sometimes not to be, it seems that contingents are not known by the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0053","text":"[I q. 86 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32105)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0054","text":"On the contrary, all science is in the intellect. But some sciences are concerned with contingents, such as the moral sciences, which concern human acts subject to free choice; and likewise the natural sciences, as regards the part that treats of things generable and corruptible. Therefore the intellect is cognizant of contingents.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0055","text":"[I q. 86 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32106)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0056","text":"I answer that contingents can be considered in two ways. In one way, insofar as they are contingent. In another way, insofar as some necessity is found in them; for nothing is so contingent that it does not have something necessary in itself. Thus, the very fact that Socrates runs is in itself contingent; but the relation of running to motion is necessary, for it is necessary that Socrates be moved if he runs. Now, each thing is contingent on the part of matter, because that is contingent which can be and not be; and potency pertains to matter. Necessity, however, follows the notion of form, because those things which follow upon form are present of necessity. Matter, moreover, is the principle of individuation, whereas the universal notion is received according to the abstraction of form from particular matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0057","text":"But it was said above that, through itself and directly, the intellect is of universals; whereas sense is of singulars, of which the intellect too is, in a certain way, indirectly cognizant, as was said above. Thus, therefore, contingents, insofar as they are contingent, are known directly indeed by sense, but indirectly by the intellect; whereas the universal and necessary notions of contingents are known through the intellect. Hence, if the universal notions of knowable things are considered, all sciences are concerned with necessities. But if the things themselves are considered, then some science is concerned with necessities, and some with contingents.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0058","text":"[I q. 86 a. 3 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32107)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0059","text":"And through this the solution to the objections is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0060","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0061","text":"[I q. 86 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32108)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0062","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that our intellect knows future things. For our intellect knows through intelligible species, which abstract from here and now, and thus are indifferent to every time. But it can know present things. Therefore it can know future things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0063","text":"[I q. 86 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32109)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0064","text":"Further, when a man is withdrawn from the senses, he can know certain future things; as is evident in sleepers and the frenzied. But when he is withdrawn from the senses, he is more vigorous in intellect. Therefore, so far as it is concerned, the intellect is cognizant of future things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0065","text":"[I q. 86 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32110)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0066","text":"Further, man's intellectual cognition is more efficacious than any cognition of brute animals. But there are certain animals that know certain future things; thus, little crows, frequently cawing, signify rain soon to come. Therefore, all the more can the human intellect know future things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0067","text":"[I q. 86 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32111)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0068","text":"On the contrary is what is said in Ecclesiastes VIII: *Great is the affliction of man, who is ignorant of the past and can know the future by no messenger*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0069","text":"[I q. 86 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32112)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0070","text":"I answer that concerning the knowledge of future things, a distinction must be made in the same way as concerning the knowledge of contingents. For future things themselves, insofar as they fall under time, are singulars, which the human intellect does not know except by reflection, as was said above. But the notions of future things can be universal and perceptible by the intellect, and there can also be sciences concerning them. Nevertheless, to speak generally concerning the knowledge of future things, it must be known that future things can be known in two ways: in one way, in themselves; in another way, in their causes. Future things cannot be known in themselves except by God, to whom they are also present while, in the course of things, they are future, insofar as His eternal gaze is borne simultaneously over the whole course of time, as was said above when the knowledge of God was being treated. But insofar as they are in their causes, they can also be known by us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0071","text":"And if they are in their causes as proceeding from them of necessity, they are known with the certainty of science; just as an astronomer foreknows a future eclipse. But if they are in their causes such that they proceed from them for the most part, then they can be known by a certain conjecture, more or less certain according as the causes are more or less inclined toward their effects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0072","text":"[I q. 86 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32113)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0073","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that argument proceeds concerning the cognition that comes about through universal accounts of causes, from which future things can be known according to the mode of the order of effect to cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0074","text":"[I q. 86 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32114)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0075","text":"To the second it must be said that, as Augustine says in Confessions XII, the soul has a certain power of lot, such that from its own nature it can know future things; and therefore, when it is withdrawn from bodily senses and in some manner returns to itself, it becomes a participant in knowledge of future things. And this opinion would indeed be reasonable if we held that the soul received cognition of things through participation in the Ideas, as the Platonists held; for thus the soul would by its own nature know the universal causes of all effects, but is impeded by the body. Hence, when it is abstracted from the senses of the body, it knows future things. But because this mode of knowing is not connatural to our intellect, but rather that it receive cognition from the senses, therefore it is not according to the nature of the soul that it know future things when it is removed from the senses, but rather through the impression of certain higher spiritual and bodily causes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0076","text":"Spiritual causes indeed, as when by divine power, through the ministry of angels, the human intellect is illuminated, and phantasms are ordered for knowing certain future things; or even when, through the operation of demons, some movement occurs in the imagination for fore-signifying certain future things which demons know, as was said above. The human soul is naturally more apt to receive impressions of spiritual causes of this sort when it is removed from the senses, because through this it becomes nearer to spiritual substances and freer from external disturbances. But this also occurs through the impression of higher bodily causes. For it is manifest that higher bodies make an impression upon lower bodies. Hence, since the sensitive powers are acts of bodily organs, it follows that the imagination is in some manner altered by the impression of heavenly bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0077","text":"Hence, since heavenly bodies are the cause of many future things, certain signs of certain future things come to be in the imagination. But these signs are perceived more at night and by those asleep than by day and by those awake, because, as is said in the book On Sleep and Waking, *the things conveyed during the day are more dissolved; for the air of night is more free from disturbance, since nights are quieter. And they produce sensation in the body on account of sleep, because small interior movements are perceived more by those asleep than by those awake. These movements, moreover, produce phantasms, from which future things are foreseen*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0078","text":"[I q. 86 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32115)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0079","text":"To the third it must be said that brute animals do not have anything above imagination to order phantasms, as human beings have reason; and therefore the imagination of brute animals wholly follows the heavenly impression. And therefore from movements of animals of this sort certain future things, such as rain and the like, can be known more readily than from the movements of human beings, who are moved by the counsel of reason. Hence the Philosopher says, in the book On Sleep and Waking, that *certain very unintelligent persons are especially foreseeing; for their intelligence is not affected by cares, but, as though deserted and empty of all things, it is led when moved according to the mover*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_78:P0079"]}],"source_digest":"a3fb30bd3f809047ea9ed1feab706f41703ff39494e73c5d2b46d011d113b9a1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 86"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 86","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79","title":"First Part · Question 87","order":88,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_79","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 87 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32116)\n\nDeinde considerandum est quomodo anima intellectiva cognoscat seipsam, et ea quae in se sunt. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum cognoscat seipsam per suam essentiam. Secundo, quomodo cognoscat habitus in se existentes. Tertio, quomodo intellectus cognoscat proprium actum. Quarto, quomodo cognoscat actum voluntatis.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 87 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32117)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima intellectiva seipsam cognoscat per suam essentiam. Dicit enim Augustinus, IX de Trin., quod *mens seipsam novit per seipsam, quoniam est incorporea*.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32118)\n\nPraeterea, Angelus et anima humana conveniunt in genere intellectualis substantiae. Sed Angelus intelligit seipsum per essentiam suam. Ergo et anima humana.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32119)\n\n*Praeterea, in his quae sunt sine materia, idem est intellectus et quod intelligitur*, ut dicitur III de anima. Sed mens humana est sine materia, non enim est actus corporis alicuius, ut supra dictum est. Ergo in mente humana est idem intellectus et quod intelligitur. Ergo intelligit se per essentiam suam.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32120)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in III de anima, quod *intellectus intelligit seipsum sicut et alia*. Sed alia non intelligit per essentias eorum, sed per eorum similitudines. Ergo neque se intelligit per essentiam suam.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32121)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod unumquodque cognoscibile est secundum quod est in actu, et non secundum quod est in potentia, ut dicitur in IX Metaphys., sic enim aliquid est ens et verum, quod sub cognitione cadit, prout actu est. Et hoc quidem manifeste apparet in rebus sensibilibus, non enim visus percipit coloratum in potentia, sed solum coloratum in actu. Et similiter intellectus manifestum est quod, inquantum est cognoscitivus rerum materialium, non cognoscit nisi quod est actu, et inde est quod non cognoscit materiam primam nisi secundum proportionem ad formam, ut dicitur in I Physic. Unde et in substantiis immaterialibus, secundum quod unaquaeque earum se habet ad hoc quod sit in actu per essentiam suam, ita se habet ad hoc quod sit per suam essentiam intelligibilis. Essentia igitur Dei, quae est actus purus et perfectus, est simpliciter et perfecte secundum seipsam intelligibilis. Unde Deus per suam essentiam non solum seipsum, sed etiam omnia intelligit. Angeli autem essentia est quidem in genere intelligibilium ut actus, non tamen ut actus purus neque completus. Unde eius intelligere non completur per essentiam suam, etsi enim per essentiam suam se intelligat Angelus, tamen non omnia potest per essentiam suam cognoscere, sed cognoscit alia a se per eorum similitudines. Intellectus autem humanus se habet in genere rerum intelligibilium ut ens in potentia tantum, sicut et materia prima se habet in genere rerum sensibilium, unde possibilis nominatur. Sic igitur in sua essentia consideratus, se habet ut potentia intelligens. Unde ex seipso habet virtutem ut intelligat, non autem ut intelligatur, nisi secundum id quod fit actu. Sic enim etiam Platonici posuerunt ordinem entium intelligibilium supra ordinem intellectuum, quia intellectus non intelligit nisi per participationem intelligibilis; participans autem est infra participatum, secundum eos. Si igitur intellectus humanus fieret actu per participationem formarum intelligibilium separatarum, ut Platonici posuerunt per huiusmodi participationem rerum incorporearum intellectus humanus seipsum intelligeret. Sed quia connaturale est intellectui nostro, secundum statum praesentis vitae, quod ad materialia et sensibilia respiciat, sicut supra dictum est; consequens est ut sic seipsum intelligat intellectus noster, secundum quod fit actu per species a sensibilibus abstractas per lumen intellectus agentis, quod est actus ipsorum intelligibilium, et eis mediantibus intellectus possibilis. Non ergo per essentiam suam, sed per actum suum se cognoscit intellectus noster. Et hoc dupliciter. Uno quidem modo, particulariter, secundum quod Socrates vel Plato percipit se habere animam intellectivam, ex hoc quod percipit se intelligere. Alio modo, in universali, secundum quod naturam humanae mentis ex actu intellectus consideramus. Sed verum est quod iudicium et efficacia huius cognitionis per quam naturam animae cognoscimus, competit nobis secundum derivationem luminis intellectus nostri a veritate divina, in qua rationes omnium rerum continentur, sicut supra dictum est. Unde et Augustinus dicit, in IX de Trin., *intuemur inviolabilem veritatem, ex qua perfecte, quantum possumus, definimus non qualis sit uniuscuiusque hominis mens, sed qualis esse sempiternis rationibus debeat*. Est autem differentia inter has duas cognitiones. Nam ad primam cognitionem de mente habendam, sufficit ipsa mentis praesentia, quae est principium actus ex quo mens percipit seipsam. Et ideo dicitur se cognoscere per suam praesentiam. Sed ad secundam cognitionem de mente habendam, non sufficit eius praesentia, sed requiritur diligens et subtilis inquisitio. Unde et multi naturam animae ignorant, et multi etiam circa naturam animae erraverunt. Propter quod Augustinus dicit, X de Trin., de tali inquisitione mentis, *non velut absentem se quaerat mens cernere; sed praesentem quaerat discernere*, idest cognoscere differentiam suam ab aliis rebus, quod est cognoscere quidditatem et naturam suam.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32122)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod mens seipsam per seipsam novit, quia tandem in sui ipsius cognitionem pervenit, licet per suum actum, ipsa enim est quae cognoscitur, quia ipsa seipsam amat, ut ibidem subditur. Potest enim aliquid dici per se notum dupliciter, vel quia per nihil aliud in eius notitiam devenitur, sicut dicuntur prima principia per se nota; vel quia non sunt cognoscibilia per accidens, sicut color est per se visibilis, substantia autem per accidens.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32123)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod essentia Angeli est sicut actus in genere intelligibilium, et ideo se habet et ut intellectus, et ut intellectum. Unde Angelus suam essentiam per seipsum apprehendit. Non autem intellectus humanus, qui vel est omnino in potentia respectu intelligibilium, sicut intellectus possibilis; vel est actus intelligibilium quae abstrahuntur a phantasmatibus, sicut intellectus agens.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32124)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod verbum illud philosophi universaliter verum est in omni intellectu. Sicut enim sensus in actu est sensibile, propter similitudinem sensibilis, quae est forma sensus in actu; ita intellectus in actu est intellectum in actu, propter similitudinem rei intellectae, quae est forma intellectus in actu. Et ideo intellectus humanus, qui fit in actu per speciem rei intellectae, per eandem speciem intelligitur, sicut per formam suam. Idem autem est dicere quod in his *quae sunt sine materia, idem est intellectus et quod intelligitur*, ac si diceretur quod in his quae sunt intellecta in actu, idem est intellectus et quod intelligitur, per hoc enim aliquid est intellectum in actu, quod est sine materia. Sed in hoc est differentia, quia quorundam essentiae sunt sine materia, sicut substantiae separatae quas Angelos dicimus, quarum unaquaeque et est intellecta et est intelligens, sed quaedam res sunt quarum essentiae non sunt sine materia, sed solum similitudines ab eis abstractae. Unde et Commentator dicit, in III de anima, quod propositio inducta non habet veritatem nisi in substantiis separatis, verificatur enim quodammodo in eis quod non verificatur in aliis, ut dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 87 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32125)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus noster cognoscat habitus animae per essentiam eorum. Dicit enim Augustinus, XIII de Trin., *non sic videtur fides in corde in quo est, sicut anima alterius hominis ex motibus corporis videtur; sed eam tenet certissima scientia, clamatque conscientia*. Et eadem ratio est de aliis habitibus animae. Ergo habitus animae non cognoscuntur per actus, sed per seipsos.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32126)\n\nPraeterea, res materiales, quae sunt extra animam, cognoscuntur per hoc quod similitudines earum sunt praesentialiter in anima; et ideo dicuntur per suas similitudines cognosci. Sed habitus animae praesentialiter per suam essentiam sunt in anima. Ergo per suam essentiam cognoscuntur.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32127)\n\n*Praeterea, propter quod unumquodque tale, et illud magis*. Sed res aliae cognoscuntur ab anima propter habitus et species intelligibiles. Ergo ista magis per seipsa ab anima cognoscuntur.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32128)\n\nSed contra, habitus sunt principia actuum, sicut et potentiae. Sed sicut dicitur II de anima, *priores potentiis, secundum rationem, actus et operationes sunt*. Ergo eadem ratione sunt priores habitibus. Et ita habitus per actus cognoscuntur, sicut et potentiae.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32129)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod habitus quodammodo est medium inter potentiam puram et purum actum. Iam autem dictum est quod nihil cognoscitur nisi secundum quod est actu. Sic ergo inquantum habitus deficit ab actu perfecto, deficit ab hoc, ut non sit per seipsum cognoscibilis, sed necesse est quod per actum suum cognoscatur, sive dum aliquis percipit se habere habitum, per hoc quod percipit se producere actum proprium habitus; sive dum aliquis inquirit naturam et rationem habitus, ex consideratione actus. Et prima quidem cognitio habitus fit per ipsam praesentiam habitus, quia ex hoc ipso quod est praesens, actum causat, in quo statim percipitur. Secunda autem cognitio habitus fit per studiosam inquisitionem, sicut supra dictum est de mente.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32130)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, etsi fides non cognoscatur per exteriores corporis motus, percipitur tamen etiam ab eo in quo est, per interiorem actum cordis. Nullus enim fidem se habere scit, nisi per hoc quod se credere percipit.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32131)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod habitus sunt praesentes in intellectu nostro, non sicut obiecta intellectus (quia obiectum intellectus nostri, secundum statum praesentis vitae, est natura rei materialis, ut supra dictum est); sed sunt praesentes in intellectu ut quibus intellectus intelligit.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32132)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, cum dicitur, *propter quod unumquodque, illud magis*, veritatem habet, si intelligatur in his quae sunt unius ordinis, puta in uno genere causae, puta si dicatur quod sanitas est propter vitam, sequitur quod vita sit magis desiderabilis. Si autem accipiantur ea quae sunt diversorum ordinum, non habet veritatem, ut si dicatur quod sanitas est propter medicinam, non ideo sequitur quod medicina sit magis desiderabilis, quia sanitas est in ordine finium, medicina autem in ordine causarum efficientium. Sic igitur si accipiamus duo, quorum utrumque sit per se in ordine obiectorum cognitionis; illud propter quod aliud cognoscitur, erit magis notum, sicut principia conclusionibus. Sed habitus non est de ordine obiectorum, inquantum est habitus; nec propter habitum aliqua cognoscuntur sicut propter obiectum cognitum, sed sicut propter dispositionem vel formam qua cognoscens cognoscit, et ideo ratio non sequitur.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 87 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32133)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus non cognoscat proprium actum. Illud enim proprie cognoscitur, quod est obiectum cognoscitivae virtutis. Sed actus differt ab obiecto. Ergo intellectus non cognoscit suum actum.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32134)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid cognoscitur, aliquo actu cognoscitur. Si igitur intellectus cognoscit actum suum, aliquo actu cognoscit illum; et iterum illum actum alio actu. Erit ergo procedere in infinitum, quod videtur impossibile.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32135)\n\nPraeterea, sicut se habet sensus ad actum suum, ita et intellectus. Sed sensus proprius non sentit actum suum, sed hoc pertinet ad sensum communem, ut dicitur in libro de anima. Ergo neque intellectus intelligit actum suum.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32136)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, X de Trin., *intelligo me intelligere*.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32137)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut iam dictum est, unumquodque cognoscitur secundum quod est actu. Ultima autem perfectio intellectus est eius operatio, non enim est sicut actio tendens in alterum, quae sit perfectio operati, sicut aedificatio aedificati; sed manet in operante ut perfectio et actus eius, ut dicitur in IX Metaphys. Hoc igitur est primum quod de intellectu intelligitur, scilicet ipsum eius intelligere. Sed circa hoc diversi intellectus diversimode se habent. Est enim aliquis intellectus, scilicet divinus, qui est ipsum suum intelligere. Et sic in Deo idem est quod intelligat se intelligere et quod intelligat suam essentiam, quia sua essentia est suum intelligere. Est autem alius intellectus, scilicet angelicus, qui non est suum intelligere, sicut supra dictum est, sed tamen primum obiectum sui intelligere est eius essentia. Unde etsi aliud sit in Angelo, secundum rationem, quod intelligat se intelligere, et quod intelligat suam essentiam, tamen simul et uno actu utrumque intelligit, quia hoc quod est intelligere suam essentiam, est propria perfectio suae essentiae; simul autem et uno actu intelligitur res cum sua perfectione. Est autem alius intellectus, scilicet humanus, qui nec est suum intelligere, nec sui intelligere est obiectum primum ipsa eius essentia, sed aliquid extrinsecum, scilicet natura materialis rei. Et ideo id quod primo cognoscitur ab intellectu humano, est huiusmodi obiectum; et secundario cognoscitur ipse actus quo cognoscitur obiectum; et per actum cognoscitur ipse intellectus, cuius est perfectio ipsum intelligere. Et ideo philosophus dicit quod obiecta praecognoscuntur actibus, et actus potentiis.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32138)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod obiectum intellectus est commune quoddam, scilicet ens et verum, sub quo comprehenditur etiam ipse actus intelligendi. Unde intellectus potest suum actum intelligere. Sed non primo, quia nec primum obiectum intellectus nostri, secundum praesentem statum, est quodlibet ens et verum; sed ens et verum consideratum in rebus materialibus, ut dictum est; ex quibus in cognitionem omnium aliorum devenit.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32139)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ipsum intelligere humanum non est actus et perfectio naturae intellectae materialis, ut sic possit uno actu intelligi natura rei materialis et ipsum intelligere, sicut uno actu intelligitur res cum sua perfectione. Unde alius est actus quo intellectus intelligit lapidem, et alius est actus quo intelligit se intelligere lapidem, et sic inde. Nec est inconveniens in intellectu esse infinitum in potentia, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32140)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod sensus proprius sentit secundum immutationem materialis organi a sensibili exteriori. Non est autem possibile quod aliquid materiale immutet seipsum; sed unum immutatur ab alio. Et ideo actus sensus proprii percipitur per sensum communem. Sed intellectus non intelligit per materialem immutationem organi, et ideo non est simile.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 87 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32141)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus non intelligat actum voluntatis. Nihil enim cognoscitur ab intellectu, nisi sit aliquo modo praesens in intellectu. Sed actus voluntatis non est praesens in intellectu, cum sint diversae potentiae. Ergo actus voluntatis non cognoscitur ab intellectu.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32142)\n\nPraeterea, actus habet speciem ab obiecto. Sed obiectum voluntatis differt ab obiecto intellectus. Ergo et actus voluntatis speciem habet diversam ab obiecto intellectus. Non ergo cognoscitur ab intellectu.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32143)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus, in libro X Confess., attribuit affectionibus animae quod cognoscuntur neque per imagines, sicut corpora; *neque per praesentiam, sicut artes, sed per quasdam notiones*. Non videtur autem quod possint esse aliae notiones rerum in anima nisi vel essentiae rerum cognitarum, vel earum similitudines. Ergo impossibile videtur quod intellectus cognoscat affectiones animae, quae sunt actus voluntatis.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32144)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, X de Trin., *intelligo me velle*.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32145)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, actus voluntatis nihil aliud est quam inclinatio quaedam consequens formam intellectam, sicut appetitus naturalis est inclinatio consequens formam naturalem. Inclinatio autem cuiuslibet rei est in ipsa re per modum eius. Unde inclinatio naturalis est naturaliter in re naturali; et inclinatio quae est appetitus sensibilis, est sensibiliter in sentiente; et similiter inclinatio intelligibilis, quae est actus voluntatis, est intelligibiliter in intelligente, sicut in principio et in proprio subiecto. Unde et philosophus hoc modo loquendi utitur in III de anima, quod voluntas in ratione est. Quod autem intelligibiliter est in aliquo intelligente, consequens est ut ab eo intelligatur. Unde actus voluntatis intelligitur ab intellectu, et inquantum aliquis percipit se velle; et inquantum aliquis cognoscit naturam huius actus, et per consequens naturam eius principii, quod est habitus vel potentia.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32146)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ratio illa procederet, si voluntas et intellectus, sicut sunt diversae potentiae, ita etiam subiecto differrent, sic enim quod est in voluntate, esset absens ab intellectu. Nunc autem, cum utrumque radicetur in una substantia animae, et unum sit quodammodo principium alterius, consequens est ut quod est in voluntate, sit etiam quodammodo in intellectu.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32147)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod bonum et verum, quae sunt obiecta voluntatis et intellectus, differunt quidem ratione, verumtamen unum eorum continetur sub alio, ut supra dictum est, nam verum est quoddam bonum, et bonum est quoddam verum. Et ideo quae sunt voluntatis cadunt sub intellectu; et quae sunt intellectus possunt cadere sub voluntate.\n\n[I q. 87 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32148)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod affectus animae non sunt in intellectu neque per similitudinem tantum, sicut corpora; neque per praesentiam ut in subiecto, sicut artes; sed sicut principiatum in principio, in quo habetur notio principiati. Et ideo Augustinus dicit affectus animae esse in memoria per quasdam notiones.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0002","text":"[I q. 87 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32116)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est quomodo anima intellectiva cognoscat seipsam, et ea quae in se sunt. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum cognoscat seipsam per suam essentiam. Secundo, quomodo cognoscat habitus in se existentes. Tertio, quomodo intellectus cognoscat proprium actum. Quarto, quomodo cognoscat actum voluntatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0005","text":"[I q. 87 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32117)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima intellectiva seipsam cognoscat per suam essentiam. Dicit enim Augustinus, IX de Trin., quod *mens seipsam novit per seipsam, quoniam est incorporea*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0007","text":"[I q. 87 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32118)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0008","text":"Praeterea, Angelus et anima humana conveniunt in genere intellectualis substantiae. Sed Angelus intelligit seipsum per essentiam suam. Ergo et anima humana.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0009","text":"[I q. 87 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32119)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0010","text":"*Praeterea, in his quae sunt sine materia, idem est intellectus et quod intelligitur*, ut dicitur III de anima. Sed mens humana est sine materia, non enim est actus corporis alicuius, ut supra dictum est. Ergo in mente humana est idem intellectus et quod intelligitur. Ergo intelligit se per essentiam suam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0011","text":"[I q. 87 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32120)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in III de anima, quod *intellectus intelligit seipsum sicut et alia*. Sed alia non intelligit per essentias eorum, sed per eorum similitudines. Ergo neque se intelligit per essentiam suam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0013","text":"[I q. 87 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32121)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod unumquodque cognoscibile est secundum quod est in actu, et non secundum quod est in potentia, ut dicitur in IX Metaphys., sic enim aliquid est ens et verum, quod sub cognitione cadit, prout actu est. Et hoc quidem manifeste apparet in rebus sensibilibus, non enim visus percipit coloratum in potentia, sed solum coloratum in actu. Et similiter intellectus manifestum est quod, inquantum est cognoscitivus rerum materialium, non cognoscit nisi quod est actu, et inde est quod non cognoscit materiam primam nisi secundum proportionem ad formam, ut dicitur in I Physic. Unde et in substantiis immaterialibus, secundum quod unaquaeque earum se habet ad hoc quod sit in actu per essentiam suam, ita se habet ad hoc quod sit per suam essentiam intelligibilis. Essentia igitur Dei, quae est actus purus et perfectus, est simpliciter et perfecte secundum seipsam intelligibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0015","text":"Unde Deus per suam essentiam non solum seipsum, sed etiam omnia intelligit. Angeli autem essentia est quidem in genere intelligibilium ut actus, non tamen ut actus purus neque completus. Unde eius intelligere non completur per essentiam suam, etsi enim per essentiam suam se intelligat Angelus, tamen non omnia potest per essentiam suam cognoscere, sed cognoscit alia a se per eorum similitudines. Intellectus autem humanus se habet in genere rerum intelligibilium ut ens in potentia tantum, sicut et materia prima se habet in genere rerum sensibilium, unde possibilis nominatur. Sic igitur in sua essentia consideratus, se habet ut potentia intelligens. Unde ex seipso habet virtutem ut intelligat, non autem ut intelligatur, nisi secundum id quod fit actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0016","text":"Sic enim etiam Platonici posuerunt ordinem entium intelligibilium supra ordinem intellectuum, quia intellectus non intelligit nisi per participationem intelligibilis; participans autem est infra participatum, secundum eos. Si igitur intellectus humanus fieret actu per participationem formarum intelligibilium separatarum, ut Platonici posuerunt per huiusmodi participationem rerum incorporearum intellectus humanus seipsum intelligeret. Sed quia connaturale est intellectui nostro, secundum statum praesentis vitae, quod ad materialia et sensibilia respiciat, sicut supra dictum est; consequens est ut sic seipsum intelligat intellectus noster, secundum quod fit actu per species a sensibilibus abstractas per lumen intellectus agentis, quod est actus ipsorum intelligibilium, et eis mediantibus intellectus possibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0017","text":"Non ergo per essentiam suam, sed per actum suum se cognoscit intellectus noster. Et hoc dupliciter. Uno quidem modo, particulariter, secundum quod Socrates vel Plato percipit se habere animam intellectivam, ex hoc quod percipit se intelligere. Alio modo, in universali, secundum quod naturam humanae mentis ex actu intellectus consideramus. Sed verum est quod iudicium et efficacia huius cognitionis per quam naturam animae cognoscimus, competit nobis secundum derivationem luminis intellectus nostri a veritate divina, in qua rationes omnium rerum continentur, sicut supra dictum est. Unde et Augustinus dicit, in IX de Trin., *intuemur inviolabilem veritatem, ex qua perfecte, quantum possumus, definimus non qualis sit uniuscuiusque hominis mens, sed qualis esse sempiternis rationibus debeat*. Est autem differentia inter has duas cognitiones.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0018","text":"Nam ad primam cognitionem de mente habendam, sufficit ipsa mentis praesentia, quae est principium actus ex quo mens percipit seipsam. Et ideo dicitur se cognoscere per suam praesentiam. Sed ad secundam cognitionem de mente habendam, non sufficit eius praesentia, sed requiritur diligens et subtilis inquisitio. Unde et multi naturam animae ignorant, et multi etiam circa naturam animae erraverunt. Propter quod Augustinus dicit, X de Trin., de tali inquisitione mentis, *non velut absentem se quaerat mens cernere; sed praesentem quaerat discernere*, idest cognoscere differentiam suam ab aliis rebus, quod est cognoscere quidditatem et naturam suam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0019","text":"[I q. 87 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32122)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0020","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod mens seipsam per seipsam novit, quia tandem in sui ipsius cognitionem pervenit, licet per suum actum, ipsa enim est quae cognoscitur, quia ipsa seipsam amat, ut ibidem subditur. Potest enim aliquid dici per se notum dupliciter, vel quia per nihil aliud in eius notitiam devenitur, sicut dicuntur prima principia per se nota; vel quia non sunt cognoscibilia per accidens, sicut color est per se visibilis, substantia autem per accidens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0021","text":"[I q. 87 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32123)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0022","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod essentia Angeli est sicut actus in genere intelligibilium, et ideo se habet et ut intellectus, et ut intellectum. Unde Angelus suam essentiam per seipsum apprehendit. Non autem intellectus humanus, qui vel est omnino in potentia respectu intelligibilium, sicut intellectus possibilis; vel est actus intelligibilium quae abstrahuntur a phantasmatibus, sicut intellectus agens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0023","text":"[I q. 87 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32124)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0024","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod verbum illud philosophi universaliter verum est in omni intellectu. Sicut enim sensus in actu est sensibile, propter similitudinem sensibilis, quae est forma sensus in actu; ita intellectus in actu est intellectum in actu, propter similitudinem rei intellectae, quae est forma intellectus in actu. Et ideo intellectus humanus, qui fit in actu per speciem rei intellectae, per eandem speciem intelligitur, sicut per formam suam. Idem autem est dicere quod in his *quae sunt sine materia, idem est intellectus et quod intelligitur*, ac si diceretur quod in his quae sunt intellecta in actu, idem est intellectus et quod intelligitur, per hoc enim aliquid est intellectum in actu, quod est sine materia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0025","text":"Sed in hoc est differentia, quia quorundam essentiae sunt sine materia, sicut substantiae separatae quas Angelos dicimus, quarum unaquaeque et est intellecta et est intelligens, sed quaedam res sunt quarum essentiae non sunt sine materia, sed solum similitudines ab eis abstractae. Unde et Commentator dicit, in III de anima, quod propositio inducta non habet veritatem nisi in substantiis separatis, verificatur enim quodammodo in eis quod non verificatur in aliis, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0026","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0027","text":"[I q. 87 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32125)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0028","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus noster cognoscat habitus animae per essentiam eorum. Dicit enim Augustinus, XIII de Trin., *non sic videtur fides in corde in quo est, sicut anima alterius hominis ex motibus corporis videtur; sed eam tenet certissima scientia, clamatque conscientia*. Et eadem ratio est de aliis habitibus animae. Ergo habitus animae non cognoscuntur per actus, sed per seipsos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0029","text":"[I q. 87 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32126)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0030","text":"Praeterea, res materiales, quae sunt extra animam, cognoscuntur per hoc quod similitudines earum sunt praesentialiter in anima; et ideo dicuntur per suas similitudines cognosci. Sed habitus animae praesentialiter per suam essentiam sunt in anima. Ergo per suam essentiam cognoscuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0031","text":"[I q. 87 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32127)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0032","text":"*Praeterea, propter quod unumquodque tale, et illud magis*. Sed res aliae cognoscuntur ab anima propter habitus et species intelligibiles. Ergo ista magis per seipsa ab anima cognoscuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0033","text":"[I q. 87 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32128)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0034","text":"Sed contra, habitus sunt principia actuum, sicut et potentiae. Sed sicut dicitur II de anima, *priores potentiis, secundum rationem, actus et operationes sunt*. Ergo eadem ratione sunt priores habitibus. Et ita habitus per actus cognoscuntur, sicut et potentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0035","text":"[I q. 87 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32129)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0036","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod habitus quodammodo est medium inter potentiam puram et purum actum. Iam autem dictum est quod nihil cognoscitur nisi secundum quod est actu. Sic ergo inquantum habitus deficit ab actu perfecto, deficit ab hoc, ut non sit per seipsum cognoscibilis, sed necesse est quod per actum suum cognoscatur, sive dum aliquis percipit se habere habitum, per hoc quod percipit se producere actum proprium habitus; sive dum aliquis inquirit naturam et rationem habitus, ex consideratione actus. Et prima quidem cognitio habitus fit per ipsam praesentiam habitus, quia ex hoc ipso quod est praesens, actum causat, in quo statim percipitur. Secunda autem cognitio habitus fit per studiosam inquisitionem, sicut supra dictum est de mente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0037","text":"[I q. 87 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32130)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0038","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, etsi fides non cognoscatur per exteriores corporis motus, percipitur tamen etiam ab eo in quo est, per interiorem actum cordis. Nullus enim fidem se habere scit, nisi per hoc quod se credere percipit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0039","text":"[I q. 87 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32131)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0040","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod habitus sunt praesentes in intellectu nostro, non sicut obiecta intellectus (quia obiectum intellectus nostri, secundum statum praesentis vitae, est natura rei materialis, ut supra dictum est); sed sunt praesentes in intellectu ut quibus intellectus intelligit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0041","text":"[I q. 87 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32132)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0042","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, cum dicitur, *propter quod unumquodque, illud magis*, veritatem habet, si intelligatur in his quae sunt unius ordinis, puta in uno genere causae, puta si dicatur quod sanitas est propter vitam, sequitur quod vita sit magis desiderabilis. Si autem accipiantur ea quae sunt diversorum ordinum, non habet veritatem, ut si dicatur quod sanitas est propter medicinam, non ideo sequitur quod medicina sit magis desiderabilis, quia sanitas est in ordine finium, medicina autem in ordine causarum efficientium. Sic igitur si accipiamus duo, quorum utrumque sit per se in ordine obiectorum cognitionis; illud propter quod aliud cognoscitur, erit magis notum, sicut principia conclusionibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0043","text":"Sed habitus non est de ordine obiectorum, inquantum est habitus; nec propter habitum aliqua cognoscuntur sicut propter obiectum cognitum, sed sicut propter dispositionem vel formam qua cognoscens cognoscit, et ideo ratio non sequitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0044","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0045","text":"[I q. 87 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32133)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0046","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus non cognoscat proprium actum. Illud enim proprie cognoscitur, quod est obiectum cognoscitivae virtutis. Sed actus differt ab obiecto. Ergo intellectus non cognoscit suum actum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0047","text":"[I q. 87 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32134)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0048","text":"Praeterea, quidquid cognoscitur, aliquo actu cognoscitur. Si igitur intellectus cognoscit actum suum, aliquo actu cognoscit illum; et iterum illum actum alio actu. Erit ergo procedere in infinitum, quod videtur impossibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0049","text":"[I q. 87 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32135)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0050","text":"Praeterea, sicut se habet sensus ad actum suum, ita et intellectus. Sed sensus proprius non sentit actum suum, sed hoc pertinet ad sensum communem, ut dicitur in libro de anima. Ergo neque intellectus intelligit actum suum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0051","text":"[I q. 87 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32136)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0052","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, X de Trin., *intelligo me intelligere*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0053","text":"[I q. 87 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32137)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0054","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut iam dictum est, unumquodque cognoscitur secundum quod est actu. Ultima autem perfectio intellectus est eius operatio, non enim est sicut actio tendens in alterum, quae sit perfectio operati, sicut aedificatio aedificati; sed manet in operante ut perfectio et actus eius, ut dicitur in IX Metaphys. Hoc igitur est primum quod de intellectu intelligitur, scilicet ipsum eius intelligere. Sed circa hoc diversi intellectus diversimode se habent. Est enim aliquis intellectus, scilicet divinus, qui est ipsum suum intelligere. Et sic in Deo idem est quod intelligat se intelligere et quod intelligat suam essentiam, quia sua essentia est suum intelligere. Est autem alius intellectus, scilicet angelicus, qui non est suum intelligere, sicut supra dictum est, sed tamen primum obiectum sui intelligere est eius essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0055","text":"Unde etsi aliud sit in Angelo, secundum rationem, quod intelligat se intelligere, et quod intelligat suam essentiam, tamen simul et uno actu utrumque intelligit, quia hoc quod est intelligere suam essentiam, est propria perfectio suae essentiae; simul autem et uno actu intelligitur res cum sua perfectione. Est autem alius intellectus, scilicet humanus, qui nec est suum intelligere, nec sui intelligere est obiectum primum ipsa eius essentia, sed aliquid extrinsecum, scilicet natura materialis rei. Et ideo id quod primo cognoscitur ab intellectu humano, est huiusmodi obiectum; et secundario cognoscitur ipse actus quo cognoscitur obiectum; et per actum cognoscitur ipse intellectus, cuius est perfectio ipsum intelligere. Et ideo philosophus dicit quod obiecta praecognoscuntur actibus, et actus potentiis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0056","text":"[I q. 87 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32138)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0057","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod obiectum intellectus est commune quoddam, scilicet ens et verum, sub quo comprehenditur etiam ipse actus intelligendi. Unde intellectus potest suum actum intelligere. Sed non primo, quia nec primum obiectum intellectus nostri, secundum praesentem statum, est quodlibet ens et verum; sed ens et verum consideratum in rebus materialibus, ut dictum est; ex quibus in cognitionem omnium aliorum devenit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0058","text":"[I q. 87 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32139)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0059","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ipsum intelligere humanum non est actus et perfectio naturae intellectae materialis, ut sic possit uno actu intelligi natura rei materialis et ipsum intelligere, sicut uno actu intelligitur res cum sua perfectione. Unde alius est actus quo intellectus intelligit lapidem, et alius est actus quo intelligit se intelligere lapidem, et sic inde. Nec est inconveniens in intellectu esse infinitum in potentia, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0060","text":"[I q. 87 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32140)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0061","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod sensus proprius sentit secundum immutationem materialis organi a sensibili exteriori. Non est autem possibile quod aliquid materiale immutet seipsum; sed unum immutatur ab alio. Et ideo actus sensus proprii percipitur per sensum communem. Sed intellectus non intelligit per materialem immutationem organi, et ideo non est simile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0062","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0063","text":"[I q. 87 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32141)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0064","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus non intelligat actum voluntatis. Nihil enim cognoscitur ab intellectu, nisi sit aliquo modo praesens in intellectu. Sed actus voluntatis non est praesens in intellectu, cum sint diversae potentiae. Ergo actus voluntatis non cognoscitur ab intellectu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0065","text":"[I q. 87 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32142)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0066","text":"Praeterea, actus habet speciem ab obiecto. Sed obiectum voluntatis differt ab obiecto intellectus. Ergo et actus voluntatis speciem habet diversam ab obiecto intellectus. Non ergo cognoscitur ab intellectu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0067","text":"[I q. 87 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32143)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0068","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus, in libro X Confess., attribuit affectionibus animae quod cognoscuntur neque per imagines, sicut corpora; *neque per praesentiam, sicut artes, sed per quasdam notiones*. Non videtur autem quod possint esse aliae notiones rerum in anima nisi vel essentiae rerum cognitarum, vel earum similitudines. Ergo impossibile videtur quod intellectus cognoscat affectiones animae, quae sunt actus voluntatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0069","text":"[I q. 87 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32144)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0070","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, X de Trin., *intelligo me velle*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0071","text":"[I q. 87 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32145)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0072","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, actus voluntatis nihil aliud est quam inclinatio quaedam consequens formam intellectam, sicut appetitus naturalis est inclinatio consequens formam naturalem. Inclinatio autem cuiuslibet rei est in ipsa re per modum eius. Unde inclinatio naturalis est naturaliter in re naturali; et inclinatio quae est appetitus sensibilis, est sensibiliter in sentiente; et similiter inclinatio intelligibilis, quae est actus voluntatis, est intelligibiliter in intelligente, sicut in principio et in proprio subiecto. Unde et philosophus hoc modo loquendi utitur in III de anima, quod voluntas in ratione est. Quod autem intelligibiliter est in aliquo intelligente, consequens est ut ab eo intelligatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0073","text":"Unde actus voluntatis intelligitur ab intellectu, et inquantum aliquis percipit se velle; et inquantum aliquis cognoscit naturam huius actus, et per consequens naturam eius principii, quod est habitus vel potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0074","text":"[I q. 87 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32146)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0075","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ratio illa procederet, si voluntas et intellectus, sicut sunt diversae potentiae, ita etiam subiecto differrent, sic enim quod est in voluntate, esset absens ab intellectu. Nunc autem, cum utrumque radicetur in una substantia animae, et unum sit quodammodo principium alterius, consequens est ut quod est in voluntate, sit etiam quodammodo in intellectu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0076","text":"[I q. 87 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32147)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0077","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod bonum et verum, quae sunt obiecta voluntatis et intellectus, differunt quidem ratione, verumtamen unum eorum continetur sub alio, ut supra dictum est, nam verum est quoddam bonum, et bonum est quoddam verum. Et ideo quae sunt voluntatis cadunt sub intellectu; et quae sunt intellectus possunt cadere sub voluntate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0078","text":"[I q. 87 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32148)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0079","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod affectus animae non sunt in intellectu neque per similitudinem tantum, sicut corpora; neque per praesentiam ut in subiecto, sicut artes; sed sicut principiatum in principio, in quo habetur notio principiati. Et ideo Augustinus dicit affectus animae esse in memoria per quasdam notiones.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"f0abae3d3c5582e09a57f842019e32557fa7df42513e3b17072e64d16b72c1c1","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T152016611241Z_1c698a46","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 87","source_sha256":"f0abae3d3c5582e09a57f842019e32557fa7df42513e3b17072e64d16b72c1c1","sha256":"465f8cf600ca98e95406fd7746e12ce99dcba877c1703f5ed17c8cdd22ab15cd","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:20:16.611253+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0001","text":"## Preamble","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0002","text":"[I q. 87 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32116)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0003","text":"Next we must consider how the intellectual soul knows itself, and the things that are in it. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, whether it knows itself through its essence. Second, how it knows the habits existing in it. Third, how the intellect knows its own act. Fourth, how it knows the act of the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0005","text":"[I q. 87 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32117)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the intellectual soul knows itself through its essence. For Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, IX, that *the mind knows itself through itself, because it is incorporeal*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0007","text":"[I q. 87 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32118)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0008","text":"Further, an angel and the human soul agree in the genus of intellectual substance. But an angel understands itself through its essence. Therefore so also does the human soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0009","text":"[I q. 87 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32119)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0010","text":"*Further, in those things which are without matter, the intellect and that which is understood are the same*, as is said in *On the Soul*, III. But the human mind is without matter, for it is not the act of any body, as was said above. Therefore, in the human mind the intellect and that which is understood are the same. Therefore, it understands itself through its essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0011","text":"[I q. 87 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32120)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in *On the Soul*, III, that *the intellect understands itself just as it does other things*. But it does not understand other things through their essences, but through their likenesses. Therefore neither does it understand itself through its essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0013","text":"[I q. 87 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32121)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that each thing is knowable insofar as it is in act, and not insofar as it is in potency, as is said in *Metaphysics*, IX; for in this way something is being and true, which falls under cognition, insofar as it is actually. And this is clearly apparent in sensible things: for sight does not perceive what is colored in potency, but only what is colored in act. And similarly, it is manifest that the intellect, insofar as it is cognizant of material things, knows only what is in act; and hence it does not know prime matter except according to its proportion to form, as is said in *Physics*, I. Hence also in immaterial substances, according as each of them is related to being in act through its essence, so it is related to being intelligible through its essence. Therefore the essence of God, which is pure and perfect act, is simply and perfectly intelligible through itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0015","text":"Hence God understands through his essence not only himself, but also all things. But the essence of an angel is indeed an act in the genus of intelligibles, yet not as pure or complete act. Hence its understanding is not completed through its essence; for although an angel understands itself through its essence, nevertheless it cannot know all things through its essence, but knows things other than itself through their likenesses. But the human intellect is in the genus of intelligible things as a being only in potency, just as prime matter is in the genus of sensible things; hence it is called possible. Thus, considered in its essence, it stands as an intellect in potency. Hence it has from itself the power to understand, but not to be understood, except according to that whereby it becomes actual.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0016","text":"For thus the Platonists also posited the order of intelligible beings above the order of intellects, because the intellect understands only through participation in the intelligible; but that which participates is below that which is participated in, according to them. Therefore, if the human intellect were made actual through participation in separate intelligible forms, as the Platonists held, the human intellect would understand itself through such participation in incorporeal things. But because it is connatural to our intellect, according to the state of the present life, to look to material and sensible things, as was said above, it follows that our intellect understands itself in this way: according as it is made actual through species abstracted from sensibles by the light of the agent intellect, which is the act of those intelligibles, and, through them, of the possible intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0017","text":"Therefore our intellect knows itself not through its essence, but through its act. And this in two ways. In one way, particularly, according as Socrates or Plato perceives himself to have an intellectual soul from the fact that he perceives himself to understand. In another way, universally, according as we consider the nature of the human mind from the act of the intellect. But it is true that the judgment and efficacy of this cognition, through which we know the nature of the soul, belong to us according to the derivation of the light of our intellect from the divine truth, in which the notions of all things are contained, as was said above. Hence Augustine also says, in *On the Trinity*, IX: *we behold inviolable truth, from which we define perfectly, insofar as we can, not what each individual human mind is like, but what it ought to be according to the eternal notions*. But there is a difference between these two cognitions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0018","text":"For to have the first cognition concerning the mind, the very presence of the mind suffices, which is the principle of the act through which the mind perceives itself. And therefore it is said to know itself through its presence. But to have the second cognition concerning the mind, its presence does not suffice, but careful and subtle inquiry is required. Hence many are ignorant of the nature of the soul, and many have even erred concerning the nature of the soul. On account of which Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, X, concerning such an inquiry into the mind: *let not the mind seek to discern itself as though absent; but let it seek to discern itself as present*—that is, to know its difference from other things, which is to know its quiddity and nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0019","text":"[I q. 87 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32122)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0020","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the mind knows itself through itself, because it finally arrives at cognition of itself, although through its act; for it itself is what is known, because it loves itself, as is added in the same place. For something can be said to be known through itself in two ways: either because its cognition is not reached through anything else, as first principles are said to be known through themselves; or because they are not knowable per accidens, as color is visible per se, but substance per accidens.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0021","text":"[I q. 87 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32123)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0022","text":"To the second, it must be said that the essence of an angel is as act in the genus of intelligibles, and therefore it stands both as intellect and as what is understood. Hence an angel apprehends its essence through itself. But not the human intellect, which either is altogether in potency with respect to intelligibles, as the possible intellect; or is the act of intelligibles abstracted from phantasms, as the agent intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0023","text":"[I q. 87 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32124)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0024","text":"To the third, it must be said that that statement of the Philosopher is universally true in every intellect. For just as sense in act is the sensible in act, on account of the likeness of the sensible, which is the form of sense in act, so intellect in act is the intelligible in act, on account of the likeness of the thing understood, which is the form of intellect in act. And therefore the human intellect, which becomes actual through the species of the thing understood, is understood through that same species, as through its form. But to say that in those *which are without matter, the intellect and that which is understood are the same* is the same as to say that in those which are understood in act, the intellect and that which is understood are the same; for it is through being without matter that something is understood in act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0025","text":"But herein lies the difference: that the essences of some things are without matter, as are the separate substances which we call Angels, each of which both is understood and is understanding; whereas there are certain things whose essences are not without matter, but only likenesses abstracted from them are. Hence the Commentator also says, in III On the Soul, that the proposition adduced has truth only in separate substances; for in some manner that is verified in them which is not verified in others, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0026","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0027","text":"[I q. 87 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32125)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0028","text":"The second point is approached thus. It seems that our intellect knows the habits of the soul through their essence. For Augustine says, in XIII On the Trinity, *faith is not seen in the heart in which it is, as another man's soul is seen from bodily movements; rather, it is held by most certain knowledge, and conscience cries it aloud.* And the same reasoning holds concerning the other habits of the soul. Therefore, the habits of the soul are known not through acts, but through themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0029","text":"[I q. 87 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32126)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0030","text":"Moreover, material things, which are outside the soul, are known because their likenesses are present in the soul; and therefore they are said to be known through their likenesses. But the habits of the soul are present in the soul through their essence. Therefore they are known through their essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0031","text":"[I q. 87 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32127)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0032","text":"*Moreover, that on account of which each thing is such, is itself more so.* But other things are known by the soul on account of habits and intelligible species. Therefore these are more known by the soul through themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0033","text":"[I q. 87 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32128)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0034","text":"On the contrary, habits are principles of acts, just as powers are. But, as is said in II On the Soul, *acts and operations are prior in account to powers.* Therefore, by the same reasoning, they are prior to habits. And thus habits are known through acts, just as powers are.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0035","text":"[I q. 87 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32129)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0036","text":"I answer that it must be said that a habit is in some way intermediate between pure potency and pure act. Now it has already been said that nothing is known except insofar as it is in act. Therefore, insofar as a habit falls short of perfect act, it falls short of being knowable through itself; rather, it must be known through its act—either when someone perceives that he has a habit by perceiving that he produces the proper act of the habit; or when someone investigates the nature and account of the habit from consideration of the act. The first knowledge of a habit indeed comes about through the very presence of the habit, because by the very fact that it is present, it causes an act in which it is immediately perceived. But the second knowledge of a habit comes about through diligent inquiry, as was said above concerning the mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0037","text":"[I q. 87 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32130)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0038","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that, although faith is not known through the external movements of the body, it is nevertheless perceived even by the one in whom it is, through an interior act of the heart. For no one knows that he has faith except by perceiving that he believes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0039","text":"[I q. 87 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32131)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0040","text":"To the second it must be said that habits are present in our intellect, not as objects of the intellect (for the object of our intellect, according to the state of the present life, is the nature of a material thing, as was said above), but they are present in the intellect as that by which the intellect understands.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0041","text":"[I q. 87 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32132)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0042","text":"To the third it must be said that when it is said, *that on account of which each thing is such, is itself more so*, this is true if it is understood of things that are of one order, for example, in one genus of cause; for example, if it is said that health is on account of life, it follows that life is more desirable. But if things that are of diverse orders are taken, it is not true; as if it were said that health is on account of medicine, it does not therefore follow that medicine is more desirable, because health is in the order of ends, whereas medicine is in the order of efficient causes. Therefore, if we take two things, each of which is through itself in the order of objects of knowledge, that on account of which the other is known will be more known, as principles are more known than conclusions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0043","text":"But a habit is not in the order of objects insofar as it is a habit; nor are things known on account of a habit as on account of a known object, but as on account of a disposition or form by which the knower knows; and therefore the argument does not follow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0044","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0045","text":"[I q. 87 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32133)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0046","text":"The third point is approached thus. It seems that the intellect does not know its own act. For that is properly known which is the object of a cognitive power. But an act differs from an object. Therefore the intellect does not know its own act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0047","text":"[I q. 87 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32134)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0048","text":"Moreover, whatever is known is known by some act. If, therefore, the intellect knows its act, it knows it by some act; and then that act by another act. Therefore there will be an infinite regress, which seems impossible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0049","text":"[I q. 87 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32135)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0050","text":"Moreover, as sense is related to its act, so also is intellect. But the proper sense does not sense its own act; rather, this pertains to the common sense, as is said in the book *On the Soul*. Therefore, neither does the intellect understand its own act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0051","text":"[I q. 87 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32136)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0052","text":"But to the contrary, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, X: *I understand that I understand*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0053","text":"[I q. 87 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32137)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0054","text":"I answer that, as has already been said, each thing is known insofar as it is in act. Now the ultimate perfection of the intellect is its operation; for it is not like an action tending toward another thing, which is the perfection of the thing acted upon, as building is the perfection of what is built; rather, it remains in the agent as its perfection and act, as is said in *Metaphysics* IX. Therefore, the first thing understood concerning the intellect is its very act of understanding. But different intellects are differently situated with respect to this. For there is an intellect, namely the divine intellect, which is its very act of understanding. And thus in God it is the same thing that He understands that He understands and that He understands His essence, because His essence is His understanding. But there is another intellect, namely the angelic, which is not its understanding, as was said above; nevertheless, the first object of its understanding is its essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0055","text":"Hence, although in an angel it is distinct in notion that it understands itself to understand and that it understands its essence, nevertheless it understands both simultaneously and by one act, because understanding its essence is the proper perfection of its essence; moreover, a thing is understood simultaneously and by one act with its perfection. But there is another intellect, namely the human, which neither is its understanding nor has as the first object of its understanding its own essence, but something extrinsic, namely, the nature of a material thing. And therefore what is first known by the human intellect is an object of this sort; and secondarily there is known the very act by which the object is known; and through the act the intellect itself is known, of which understanding itself is the perfection. And therefore the Philosopher says that objects are known beforehand to acts, and acts to powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0056","text":"[I q. 87 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32138)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0057","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the object of the intellect is something common, namely being and truth, under which even the very act of understanding is comprehended. Hence the intellect can understand its own act. But not first, because neither is the first object of our intellect, in its present state, just any being and truth; rather, it is being and truth considered in material things, as has been said; and from these it arrives at the knowledge of all other things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0058","text":"[I q. 87 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32139)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0059","text":"To the second, it must be said that human understanding itself is not the act and perfection of the material nature understood, such that the nature of a material thing and understanding itself could be understood by one act, as a thing is understood by one act with its perfection. Hence the act by which the intellect understands a stone is one act, and the act by which it understands that it understands the stone is another act, and so on. Nor is it unfitting for there to be an infinite in potency in the intellect, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0060","text":"[I q. 87 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32140)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0061","text":"To the third, it must be said that the proper sense senses through the alteration of a material organ by an external sensible object. But it is not possible for anything material to alter itself; rather, one thing is altered by another. Therefore the act of the proper sense is perceived by the common sense. But the intellect does not understand through a material alteration of an organ, and therefore the case is not similar.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0062","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0063","text":"[I q. 87 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32141)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0064","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth point. It seems that the intellect does not understand the act of the will. For nothing is known by the intellect unless it is present in some way in the intellect. But the act of the will is not present in the intellect, since they are different powers. Therefore the act of the will is not known by the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0065","text":"[I q. 87 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32142)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0066","text":"Moreover, an act has its species from its object. But the object of the will differs from the object of the intellect. Therefore the act of the will also has a species different from the object of the intellect. Therefore it is not known by the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0067","text":"[I q. 87 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32143)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0068","text":"Moreover, Augustine, in book X of the *Confessions*, attributes to the affections of the soul that they are known neither through images, as bodies are, *nor through presence, as arts are, but through certain notions*. But it does not seem that there can be other notions of things in the soul besides either the essences of known things or their likenesses. Therefore it seems impossible that the intellect should know the affections of the soul, which are acts of the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0069","text":"[I q. 87 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32144)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0070","text":"But to the contrary, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, X: *I understand that I will*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0071","text":"[I q. 87 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32145)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0072","text":"I answer that, as was said above, the act of the will is nothing other than a certain inclination consequent upon an understood form, just as natural appetite is an inclination consequent upon a natural form. Now the inclination of any thing is in that thing according to its mode. Hence natural inclination is naturally in a natural thing; and the inclination that is sensible appetite is sensibly in the one sensing; and likewise intelligible inclination, which is the act of the will, is intelligibly in the one understanding, as in its principle and proper subject. Hence the Philosopher uses this manner of speaking in *On the Soul* III, that the will is in reason. But whatever is intelligibly in one understanding consequently is understood by it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0073","text":"Hence the act of the will is understood by the intellect, both insofar as someone perceives himself to will, and insofar as someone knows the nature of this act and, consequently, the nature of its principle, which is a habit or power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0074","text":"[I q. 87 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32146)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0075","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that this argument would proceed if the will and the intellect, just as they are diverse powers, also differed in subject; for in that case what is in the will would be absent from the intellect. But now, since both are rooted in one substance of the soul, and one is in some manner the principle of the other, it follows that what is in the will is also in some manner in the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0076","text":"[I q. 87 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32147)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0077","text":"To the second, it must be said that the good and the true, which are the objects of the will and the intellect, differ indeed in account; nevertheless, one of them is contained under the other, as was said above. For the true is a certain good, and the good is a certain true. And therefore the things that belong to the will fall under the intellect; and the things that belong to the intellect can fall under the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0078","text":"[I q. 87 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32148)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0079","text":"To the third, it must be said that the affections of the soul are in the intellect neither merely by likeness, as bodies are, nor by presence as in a subject, as the arts are; but as what is principiated is in its principle, in which the notion of the principiated is possessed. And therefore Augustine says that the affections of the soul are in memory through certain notions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_79:P0079"]}],"source_digest":"21cf0e38e4d84bf0ccd3ab678730c5d66a0a54b87dfdcb5678e10d65ddd957b1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 87"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 87","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80","title":"First Part · Question 88","order":89,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_80","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 88 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32149)\n\nDeinde considerandum est quomodo anima humana cognoscat ea quae supra se sunt, scilicet immateriales substantias. Et circa hoc quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum anima humana, secundum statum praesentis vitae, possit intelligere substantias immateriales quas Angelos dicimus, per seipsas. Secundo, utrum possit ad earum notitiam pervenire per cognitionem rerum materialium. Tertio, utrum Deus sit id quod primo a nobis cognoscitur.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 88 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32150)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima humana, secundum statum vitae praesentis, possit intelligere substantias immateriales per seipsas. Dicit enim Augustinus, in IX de Trin., *mens ipsa, sicut corporearum rerum notitias per sensus corporis colligit, sic incorporearum rerum per semetipsam*. Huiusmodi autem sunt substantiae immateriales. Ergo mens substantias immateriales intelligit.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32151)\n\nPraeterea, simile simili cognoscitur. Sed magis assimilatur mens humana rebus immaterialibus quam materialibus, cum ipsa mens sit immaterialis, ut ex supradictis patet. Cum ergo mens nostra intelligat res materiales, multo magis intelligit res immateriales.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32152)\n\nPraeterea, quod ea quae sunt secundum se maxime sensibilia, non maxime sentiantur a nobis, provenit ex hoc quod excellentiae sensibilium corrumpunt sensum. Sed excellentiae intelligibilium non corrumpunt intellectum, ut dicitur in III de anima. Ergo ea quae sunt secundum se maxime intelligibilia, sunt etiam maxime intelligibilia nobis. Sed cum res materiales non sint intelligibiles nisi quia facimus eas intelligibiles actu, abstrahendo a materia; manifestum est quod magis sint secundum se intelligibiles substantiae quae secundum suam naturam sunt immateriales. Ergo multo magis intelliguntur a nobis quam res materiales.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32153)\n\nPraeterea, Commentator dicit, in II Metaphys., quod si substantiae abstractae non possent intelligi a nobis, *tunc natura otiose egisset, quia fecit illud quod est naturaliter in se intellectum, non intellectum ab aliquo*. Sed nihil est otiosum sive frustra in natura. Ergo substantiae immateriales possunt intelligi a nobis.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32154)\n\nPraeterea, sicut se habet sensus ad sensibilia, ita se habet intellectus ad intelligibilia. Sed visus noster potest videre omnia corpora, sive sint superiora et incorruptibilia, sive sint inferiora et corruptibilia. Ergo intellectus noster potest intelligere omnes substantias intelligibiles, et superiores et immateriales.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32155)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Sap. IX, *quae in caelis sunt, quis investigabit?* In caelis autem dicuntur huiusmodi substantiae esse; secundum illud Matth. XVIII, *Angeli eorum in caelis* et cetera. Ergo non possunt substantiae immateriales per investigationem humanam cognosci.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32156)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod secundum opinionem Platonis, substantiae immateriales non solum a nobis intelliguntur, sed etiam sunt prima a nobis intellecta. Posuit enim Plato formas immateriales subsistentes, quas ideas vocabat, esse propria obiecta nostri intellectus, et ita primo et per se intelliguntur a nobis. Applicatur tamen animae cognitio rebus materialibus, secundum quod intellectui permiscetur phantasia et sensus. Unde quanto magis intellectus fuerit depuratus, tanto magis percipit immaterialium intelligibilem veritatem. Sed secundum Aristotelis sententiam, quam magis experimur, intellectus noster, secundum statum praesentis vitae, naturalem respectum habet ad naturas rerum materialium; unde nihil intelligit nisi convertendo se ad phantasmata, ut ex dictis patet. Et sic manifestum est quod substantias immateriales, quae sub sensu et imaginatione non cadunt, primo et per se, secundum modum cognitionis nobis expertum, intelligere non possumus. Sed tamen Averroes, in Comment. tertii de anima, ponit quod in fine in hac vita homo pervenire potest ad hoc quod intelligat substantias separatas, per continuationem vel unionem cuiusdam substantiae separatae nobis, quam vocat intellectum agentem, qui quidem, cum sit substantia separata, naturaliter substantias separatas intelligit. Unde cum fuerit nobis perfecte unitus, sic ut per eum perfecte intelligere possimus, intelligemus et nos substantias separatas; sicut nunc per intellectum possibilem nobis unitum intelligimus res materiales. Ponit autem intellectum agentem sic nobis uniri. Cum enim nos intelligamus per intellectum agentem et per intelligibilia speculata, ut patet cum conclusiones intelligimus per principia intellecta; necesse est quod intellectus agens comparetur ad intellecta speculata vel sicut agens principale ad instrumenta, vel sicut forma ad materiam. His enim duobus modis attribuitur actio aliqua duobus principiis, principali quidem agenti et instrumento, sicut sectio artifici et serrae; formae autem et subiecto, sicut calefactio calori et igni. Sed utroque modo intellectus agens comparabitur ad intelligibilia speculata sicut perfectio ad perfectibile, et actus ad potentiam. Simul autem recipitur in aliquo perfectum et perfectio; sicut visibile in actu et lumen in pupilla. Simul igitur in intellectu possibili recipiuntur intellecta speculata et intellectus agens. Et quanto plura intellecta speculata recipimus, tanto magis appropinquamus ad hoc quod intellectus agens perfecte uniatur nobis. Ita quod cum omnia intellecta speculata cognoverimus, intellectus agens perfecte unietur nobis; et poterimus per eum omnia cognoscere materialia et immaterialia. Et in hoc ponit ultimam hominis felicitatem. Nec refert, quantum ad propositum pertinet, utrum in illo statu felicitatis intellectus possibilis intelligat substantias separatas per intellectum agentem, ut ipse sentit, vel, ut ipse imponit Alexandro, intellectus possibilis nunquam intelligat substantias separatas (propter hoc quod ponit intellectum possibilem corruptibilem), sed homo intelligat substantias separatas per intellectum agentem. Sed praedicta stare non possunt. Primo quidem quia, si intellectus agens est substantia separata, impossibile est quod per ipsam formaliter intelligamus, quia id quo formaliter agens agit, est forma et actus agentis; cum omne agens agat inquantum est actu. Sicut etiam supra dictum est circa intellectum possibilem. Secundo quia, secundum modum praedictum, intellectus agens, si est substantia separata, non uniretur nobis secundum suam substantiam; sed solum lumen eius, secundum quod participatur in intellectis speculatis; et non quantum ad alias actiones intellectus agentis, ut possimus per hoc intelligere substantias immateriales. Sicut dum videmus colores illuminatos a sole, non unitur nobis substantia solis, ut possimus actiones solis agere; sed solum nobis unitur lumen solis ad visionem colorum. Tertio, quia dato quod secundum modum praedictum uniretur nobis substantia intellectus agentis, tamen ipsi non ponunt quod intellectus agens totaliter uniatur nobis secundum unum intelligibile vel duo, sed secundum omnia intellecta speculata. Sed omnia intellecta speculata deficiunt a virtute intellectus agentis, quia multo plus est intelligere substantias separatas, quam intelligere omnia materialia. Unde manifestum est quod etiam intellectis omnibus materialibus, non sic uniretur intellectus agens nobis, ut possemus intelligere per eum substantias separatas. Quarto, quia intelligere omnia intellecta materialia vix contingit alicui in hoc mundo; et sic nullus, vel pauci ad felicitatem pervenirent. Quod est contra philosophum, in I Ethic., qui dicit quod *felicitas est quoddam bonum commune, quod potest pervenire omnibus non orbatis ad virtutem*. Est etiam contra rationem quod finem alicuius speciei ut in paucioribus consequantur ea quae continentur sub specie. Quinto, quia philosophus dicit expresse, in I Ethic., quod *felicitas est operatio secundum perfectam virtutem*. Et enumeratis multis virtutibus, in decimo, concludit quod felicitas ultima, consistens in cognitione maximorum intelligibilium, est secundum virtutem sapientiae, quam posuerat in sexto esse caput scientiarum speculativarum. Unde patet quod Aristoteles posuit ultimam felicitatem hominis in cognitione substantiarum separatarum, qualis potest haberi per scientias speculativas, et non per continuationem intellectus agentis a quibusdam confictam. Sexto, quia supra ostensum est quod intellectus agens non est substantia separata, sed virtus quaedam animae, ad eadem active se extendens, ad quae se extendit intellectus possibilis receptive, quia, ut dicitur in III de anima, intellectus possibilis est quo est omnia fieri, intellectus agens quo est omnia facere. Uterque ergo intellectus se extendit, secundum statum praesentis vitae, ad materialia sola; quae intellectus agens facit intelligibilia actu, et recipiuntur in intellectu possibili. Unde secundum statum praesentis vitae, neque per intellectum possibilem, neque per intellectum agentem, possumus intelligere substantias immateriales secundum seipsas.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32157)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ex illa auctoritate Augustini haberi potest quod illud quod mens nostra de cognitione incorporalium rerum accipere potest, per seipsam cognoscere possit. Et hoc adeo verum est, ut etiam apud philosophos dicatur quod scientia de anima est principium quoddam ad cognoscendum substantias separatas. Per hoc enim quod anima nostra cognoscit seipsam, pertingit ad cognitionem aliquam habendam de substantiis incorporeis, qualem eam contingit habere, non quod simpliciter et perfecte eas cognoscat, cognoscendo seipsam.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32158)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod similitudo naturae non est ratio sufficiens ad cognitionem, alioquin oporteret dicere quod Empedocles dixit, quod anima esset de natura omnium, ad hoc quod omnia cognosceret. Sed requiritur ad cognoscendum, ut sit similitudo rei cognitae in cognoscente quasi quaedam forma ipsius. Intellectus autem noster possibilis, secundum statum praesentis vitae, est natus informari similitudinibus rerum materialium a phantasmatibus abstractis, et ideo cognoscit magis materialia quam substantias immateriales.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32159)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod requiritur aliqua proportio obiecti ad potentiam cognoscitivam, ut activi ad passivum, et perfectionis ad perfectibile. Unde quod excellentia sensibilia non capiantur a sensu, non sola ratio est quia corrumpunt organa sensibilia; sed etiam quia sunt improportionata potentiis sensitivis. Et hoc modo substantiae immateriales sunt improportionatae intellectui nostro, secundum praesentem statum, ut non possint ab eo intelligi.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32160)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod illa ratio Commentatoris multipliciter deficit. Primo quidem, quia non sequitur quod, si substantiae separatae non intelliguntur a nobis, non intelligantur ab aliquo intellectu, intelliguntur enim a seipsis, et a se invicem. Secundo, quia non est finis substantiarum separatarum ut intelligantur a nobis. Illud autem otiose et frustra esse dicitur, quod non consequitur finem ad quem est. Et sic non sequitur substantias immateriales esse frustra, etiam si nullo modo intelligerentur a nobis.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32161)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod eodem modo sensus cognoscit et superiora et inferiora corpora, scilicet per immutationem organi a sensibili. Non autem eodem modo intelliguntur a nobis substantiae materiales, quae intelliguntur per modum abstractionis; et substantiae immateriales, quae non possunt sic a nobis intelligi, quia non sunt earum aliqua phantasmata.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 88 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32162)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus noster per cognitionem rerum materialium possit pervenire ad intelligendum substantias immateriales. Dicit enim Dionysius, I cap. Cael. Hier., quod *non est possibile humanae menti ad immaterialem illam sursum excitari caelestium hierarchiarum contemplationem, nisi secundum se materiali manuductione utatur*. Relinquitur ergo quod per materialia manuduci possumus ad intelligendum substantias immateriales.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32163)\n\nPraeterea, scientia est in intellectu. Sed scientiae et definitiones sunt de substantiis immaterialibus, definit enim Damascenus Angelum; et de Angelis aliqua documenta traduntur tam in theologicis quam in philosophicis disciplinis. Ergo substantiae immateriales intelligi possunt a nobis.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32164)\n\nPraeterea, anima humana est de genere substantiarum immaterialium. Sed ipsa intelligi potest a nobis per actum suum, quo intelligit materialia. Ergo et aliae substantiae immateriales intelligi possunt a nobis per suos effectus in rebus materialibus.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32165)\n\nPraeterea, illa sola causa per suos effectus comprehendi non potest, quae in infinitum distat a suis effectibus. Hoc autem solius Dei est proprium. Ergo aliae substantiae immateriales creatae intelligi possunt a nobis per res materiales.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32166)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, I cap. de Div. Nom., quod *sensibilibus intelligibilia, et compositis simplicia, et corporalibus incorporalia apprehendi non possunt*.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32167)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut Averroes narrat in III de anima, quidam Avempace nomine, posuit quod per intellectum substantiarum materialium pervenire possumus, secundum vera philosophiae principia, ad intelligendum substantias immateriales. Cum enim intellectus noster natus sit abstrahere quidditatem rei materialis a materia, si iterum in illa quidditate sit aliquid materiae, poterit iterato abstrahere, et cum hoc in infinitum non procedat, tandem pervenire poterit ad intelligendum aliquam quidditatem quae sit omnino sine materia. Et hoc est intelligere substantiam immaterialem. Quod quidem efficaciter diceretur, si substantiae immateriales essent formae et species horum materialium, ut Platonici posuerunt. Hoc autem non posito, sed supposito quod substantiae immateriales sint omnino alterius rationis a quidditatibus materialium rerum; quantumcumque intellectus noster abstrahat quidditatem rei materialis a materia, nunquam perveniet ad aliquid simile substantiae immateriali. Et ideo per substantias materiales non possumus perfecte substantias immateriales intelligere.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32168)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ex rebus materialibus ascendere possumus in aliqualem cognitionem immaterialium rerum, non tamen in perfectam, quia non est sufficiens comparatio rerum materialium ad immateriales, sed similitudines si quae a materialibus accipiuntur ad immaterialia intelligenda, sunt multum dissimiles, ut Dionysius dicit, II cap. Cael. Hier.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32169)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod de superioribus rebus in scientiis maxime tractatur per viam remotionis, sic enim corpora caelestia notificat Aristoteles per negationem proprietatum inferiorum corporum. Unde multo magis immateriales substantiae a nobis cognosci non possunt, ut earum quidditates apprehendamus, sed de eis nobis in scientiis documenta traduntur per viam remotionis et alicuius habitudinis ad res materiales.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32170)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod anima humana intelligit seipsam per suum intelligere, quod est actus proprius eius, perfecte demonstrans virtutem eius et naturam. Sed neque per hoc, neque per alia quae in rebus materialibus inveniuntur, perfecte cognosci potest immaterialium substantiarum virtus et natura, quia huiusmodi non adaequant earum virtutes.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32171)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod substantiae immateriales creatae in genere quidem naturali non conveniunt cum substantiis materialibus, quia non est in eis eadem ratio potentiae et materiae, conveniunt tamen cum eis in genere logico, quia etiam substantiae immateriales sunt in praedicamento substantiae, cum earum quidditas non sit earum esse. Sed Deus non convenit cum rebus materialibus neque secundum genus naturale, neque secundum genus logicum, quia Deus nullo modo est in genere, ut supra dictum est. Unde per similitudines rerum materialium aliquid affirmative potest cognosci de Angelis secundum rationem communem, licet non secundum rationem speciei; de Deo autem nullo modo.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 88 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32172)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus sit primum quod a mente humana cognoscitur. Illud enim in quo omnia alia cognoscuntur, et per quod de aliis iudicamus, est primo cognitum a nobis; sicut lux ab oculo, et principia prima ab intellectu. Sed omnia in luce primae veritatis cognoscimus, et per eam de omnibus iudicamus; ut dicit Augustinus in libro de Trin., et in libro de vera Relig. Ergo Deus est id quod primo cognoscitur a nobis.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32173)\n\nPraeterea, propter quod unumquodque, et illud magis. Sed Deus est causa omnis nostrae cognitionis, ipse enim est *lux vera, quae illuminat omnem hominem venientem in hunc mundum*, ut dicitur Ioan. I. Ergo Deus est id quod primo et maxime est cognitum nobis.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32174)\n\nPraeterea, id quod primo cognoscitur in imagine, est exemplar quo imago formatur. Sed in mente nostra est Dei imago, ut Augustinus dicit. Ergo id quod primo cognoscitur in mente nostra est Deus.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32175)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Ioan. I, *Deum nemo vidit unquam*.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32176)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum intellectus humanus, secundum statum praesentis vitae, non possit intelligere substantias immateriales creatas, ut dictum est; multo minus potest intelligere essentiam substantiae increatae. Unde simpliciter dicendum est quod Deus non est primum quod a nobis cognoscitur; sed magis per creaturas in Dei cognitionem pervenimus, secundum illud apostoli ad Rom. I, *invisibilia Dei per ea quae facta sunt, intellecta, conspiciuntur*. Primum autem quod intelligitur a nobis secundum statum praesentis vitae, est quidditas rei materialis, quae est nostri intellectus obiectum, ut multoties supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32177)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in luce primae veritatis omnia intelligimus et iudicamus, inquantum ipsum lumen intellectus nostri, sive naturale sive gratuitum, nihil aliud est quam quaedam impressio veritatis primae, ut supra dictum est. Unde cum ipsum lumen intellectus nostri non se habeat ad intellectum nostrum sicut quod intelligitur, sed sicut quo intelligitur; multo minus Deus est id quod primo a nostro intellectu intelligitur.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32178)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod propter quod unumquodque, illud magis, intelligendum est in his quae sunt unius ordinis, ut supra dictum est. Propter Deum autem alia cognoscuntur, non sicut propter primum cognitum, sed sicut propter primam cognoscitivae virtutis causam.\n\n[I q. 88 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32179)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, si in anima nostra esset perfecta imago Dei, sicut filius est perfecta imago patris, statim mens nostra intelligeret Deum. Est autem imago imperfecta. Unde ratio non sequitur.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0002","text":"[I q. 88 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32149)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est quomodo anima humana cognoscat ea quae supra se sunt, scilicet immateriales substantias. Et circa hoc quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum anima humana, secundum statum praesentis vitae, possit intelligere substantias immateriales quas Angelos dicimus, per seipsas. Secundo, utrum possit ad earum notitiam pervenire per cognitionem rerum materialium. Tertio, utrum Deus sit id quod primo a nobis cognoscitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0005","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32150)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima humana, secundum statum vitae praesentis, possit intelligere substantias immateriales per seipsas. Dicit enim Augustinus, in IX de Trin., *mens ipsa, sicut corporearum rerum notitias per sensus corporis colligit, sic incorporearum rerum per semetipsam*. Huiusmodi autem sunt substantiae immateriales. Ergo mens substantias immateriales intelligit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0007","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32151)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0008","text":"Praeterea, simile simili cognoscitur. Sed magis assimilatur mens humana rebus immaterialibus quam materialibus, cum ipsa mens sit immaterialis, ut ex supradictis patet. Cum ergo mens nostra intelligat res materiales, multo magis intelligit res immateriales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0009","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32152)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0010","text":"Praeterea, quod ea quae sunt secundum se maxime sensibilia, non maxime sentiantur a nobis, provenit ex hoc quod excellentiae sensibilium corrumpunt sensum. Sed excellentiae intelligibilium non corrumpunt intellectum, ut dicitur in III de anima. Ergo ea quae sunt secundum se maxime intelligibilia, sunt etiam maxime intelligibilia nobis. Sed cum res materiales non sint intelligibiles nisi quia facimus eas intelligibiles actu, abstrahendo a materia; manifestum est quod magis sint secundum se intelligibiles substantiae quae secundum suam naturam sunt immateriales. Ergo multo magis intelliguntur a nobis quam res materiales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0011","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32153)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0012","text":"Praeterea, Commentator dicit, in II Metaphys., quod si substantiae abstractae non possent intelligi a nobis, *tunc natura otiose egisset, quia fecit illud quod est naturaliter in se intellectum, non intellectum ab aliquo*. Sed nihil est otiosum sive frustra in natura. Ergo substantiae immateriales possunt intelligi a nobis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0013","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32154)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0014","text":"Praeterea, sicut se habet sensus ad sensibilia, ita se habet intellectus ad intelligibilia. Sed visus noster potest videre omnia corpora, sive sint superiora et incorruptibilia, sive sint inferiora et corruptibilia. Ergo intellectus noster potest intelligere omnes substantias intelligibiles, et superiores et immateriales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0015","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32155)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0016","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Sap. IX, *quae in caelis sunt, quis investigabit?* In caelis autem dicuntur huiusmodi substantiae esse; secundum illud Matth. XVIII, *Angeli eorum in caelis* et cetera. Ergo non possunt substantiae immateriales per investigationem humanam cognosci.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0017","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32156)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0018","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod secundum opinionem Platonis, substantiae immateriales non solum a nobis intelliguntur, sed etiam sunt prima a nobis intellecta. Posuit enim Plato formas immateriales subsistentes, quas ideas vocabat, esse propria obiecta nostri intellectus, et ita primo et per se intelliguntur a nobis. Applicatur tamen animae cognitio rebus materialibus, secundum quod intellectui permiscetur phantasia et sensus. Unde quanto magis intellectus fuerit depuratus, tanto magis percipit immaterialium intelligibilem veritatem. Sed secundum Aristotelis sententiam, quam magis experimur, intellectus noster, secundum statum praesentis vitae, naturalem respectum habet ad naturas rerum materialium; unde nihil intelligit nisi convertendo se ad phantasmata, ut ex dictis patet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0019","text":"Et sic manifestum est quod substantias immateriales, quae sub sensu et imaginatione non cadunt, primo et per se, secundum modum cognitionis nobis expertum, intelligere non possumus. Sed tamen Averroes, in Comment. tertii de anima, ponit quod in fine in hac vita homo pervenire potest ad hoc quod intelligat substantias separatas, per continuationem vel unionem cuiusdam substantiae separatae nobis, quam vocat intellectum agentem, qui quidem, cum sit substantia separata, naturaliter substantias separatas intelligit. Unde cum fuerit nobis perfecte unitus, sic ut per eum perfecte intelligere possimus, intelligemus et nos substantias separatas; sicut nunc per intellectum possibilem nobis unitum intelligimus res materiales. Ponit autem intellectum agentem sic nobis uniri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0020","text":"Cum enim nos intelligamus per intellectum agentem et per intelligibilia speculata, ut patet cum conclusiones intelligimus per principia intellecta; necesse est quod intellectus agens comparetur ad intellecta speculata vel sicut agens principale ad instrumenta, vel sicut forma ad materiam. His enim duobus modis attribuitur actio aliqua duobus principiis, principali quidem agenti et instrumento, sicut sectio artifici et serrae; formae autem et subiecto, sicut calefactio calori et igni. Sed utroque modo intellectus agens comparabitur ad intelligibilia speculata sicut perfectio ad perfectibile, et actus ad potentiam. Simul autem recipitur in aliquo perfectum et perfectio; sicut visibile in actu et lumen in pupilla. Simul igitur in intellectu possibili recipiuntur intellecta speculata et intellectus agens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0021","text":"Et quanto plura intellecta speculata recipimus, tanto magis appropinquamus ad hoc quod intellectus agens perfecte uniatur nobis. Ita quod cum omnia intellecta speculata cognoverimus, intellectus agens perfecte unietur nobis; et poterimus per eum omnia cognoscere materialia et immaterialia. Et in hoc ponit ultimam hominis felicitatem. Nec refert, quantum ad propositum pertinet, utrum in illo statu felicitatis intellectus possibilis intelligat substantias separatas per intellectum agentem, ut ipse sentit, vel, ut ipse imponit Alexandro, intellectus possibilis nunquam intelligat substantias separatas (propter hoc quod ponit intellectum possibilem corruptibilem), sed homo intelligat substantias separatas per intellectum agentem. Sed praedicta stare non possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0022","text":"Primo quidem quia, si intellectus agens est substantia separata, impossibile est quod per ipsam formaliter intelligamus, quia id quo formaliter agens agit, est forma et actus agentis; cum omne agens agat inquantum est actu. Sicut etiam supra dictum est circa intellectum possibilem. Secundo quia, secundum modum praedictum, intellectus agens, si est substantia separata, non uniretur nobis secundum suam substantiam; sed solum lumen eius, secundum quod participatur in intellectis speculatis; et non quantum ad alias actiones intellectus agentis, ut possimus per hoc intelligere substantias immateriales. Sicut dum videmus colores illuminatos a sole, non unitur nobis substantia solis, ut possimus actiones solis agere; sed solum nobis unitur lumen solis ad visionem colorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0023","text":"Tertio, quia dato quod secundum modum praedictum uniretur nobis substantia intellectus agentis, tamen ipsi non ponunt quod intellectus agens totaliter uniatur nobis secundum unum intelligibile vel duo, sed secundum omnia intellecta speculata. Sed omnia intellecta speculata deficiunt a virtute intellectus agentis, quia multo plus est intelligere substantias separatas, quam intelligere omnia materialia. Unde manifestum est quod etiam intellectis omnibus materialibus, non sic uniretur intellectus agens nobis, ut possemus intelligere per eum substantias separatas. Quarto, quia intelligere omnia intellecta materialia vix contingit alicui in hoc mundo; et sic nullus, vel pauci ad felicitatem pervenirent. Quod est contra philosophum, in I Ethic., qui dicit quod *felicitas est quoddam bonum commune, quod potest pervenire omnibus non orbatis ad virtutem*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0024","text":"Est etiam contra rationem quod finem alicuius speciei ut in paucioribus consequantur ea quae continentur sub specie. Quinto, quia philosophus dicit expresse, in I Ethic., quod *felicitas est operatio secundum perfectam virtutem*. Et enumeratis multis virtutibus, in decimo, concludit quod felicitas ultima, consistens in cognitione maximorum intelligibilium, est secundum virtutem sapientiae, quam posuerat in sexto esse caput scientiarum speculativarum. Unde patet quod Aristoteles posuit ultimam felicitatem hominis in cognitione substantiarum separatarum, qualis potest haberi per scientias speculativas, et non per continuationem intellectus agentis a quibusdam confictam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0025","text":"Sexto, quia supra ostensum est quod intellectus agens non est substantia separata, sed virtus quaedam animae, ad eadem active se extendens, ad quae se extendit intellectus possibilis receptive, quia, ut dicitur in III de anima, intellectus possibilis est quo est omnia fieri, intellectus agens quo est omnia facere. Uterque ergo intellectus se extendit, secundum statum praesentis vitae, ad materialia sola; quae intellectus agens facit intelligibilia actu, et recipiuntur in intellectu possibili. Unde secundum statum praesentis vitae, neque per intellectum possibilem, neque per intellectum agentem, possumus intelligere substantias immateriales secundum seipsas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0026","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32157)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0027","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ex illa auctoritate Augustini haberi potest quod illud quod mens nostra de cognitione incorporalium rerum accipere potest, per seipsam cognoscere possit. Et hoc adeo verum est, ut etiam apud philosophos dicatur quod scientia de anima est principium quoddam ad cognoscendum substantias separatas. Per hoc enim quod anima nostra cognoscit seipsam, pertingit ad cognitionem aliquam habendam de substantiis incorporeis, qualem eam contingit habere, non quod simpliciter et perfecte eas cognoscat, cognoscendo seipsam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0028","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32158)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0029","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod similitudo naturae non est ratio sufficiens ad cognitionem, alioquin oporteret dicere quod Empedocles dixit, quod anima esset de natura omnium, ad hoc quod omnia cognosceret. Sed requiritur ad cognoscendum, ut sit similitudo rei cognitae in cognoscente quasi quaedam forma ipsius. Intellectus autem noster possibilis, secundum statum praesentis vitae, est natus informari similitudinibus rerum materialium a phantasmatibus abstractis, et ideo cognoscit magis materialia quam substantias immateriales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0030","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32159)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0031","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod requiritur aliqua proportio obiecti ad potentiam cognoscitivam, ut activi ad passivum, et perfectionis ad perfectibile. Unde quod excellentia sensibilia non capiantur a sensu, non sola ratio est quia corrumpunt organa sensibilia; sed etiam quia sunt improportionata potentiis sensitivis. Et hoc modo substantiae immateriales sunt improportionatae intellectui nostro, secundum praesentem statum, ut non possint ab eo intelligi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0032","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32160)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0033","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod illa ratio Commentatoris multipliciter deficit. Primo quidem, quia non sequitur quod, si substantiae separatae non intelliguntur a nobis, non intelligantur ab aliquo intellectu, intelliguntur enim a seipsis, et a se invicem. Secundo, quia non est finis substantiarum separatarum ut intelligantur a nobis. Illud autem otiose et frustra esse dicitur, quod non consequitur finem ad quem est. Et sic non sequitur substantias immateriales esse frustra, etiam si nullo modo intelligerentur a nobis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0034","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32161)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0035","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod eodem modo sensus cognoscit et superiora et inferiora corpora, scilicet per immutationem organi a sensibili. Non autem eodem modo intelliguntur a nobis substantiae materiales, quae intelliguntur per modum abstractionis; et substantiae immateriales, quae non possunt sic a nobis intelligi, quia non sunt earum aliqua phantasmata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0036","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0037","text":"[I q. 88 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32162)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0038","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod intellectus noster per cognitionem rerum materialium possit pervenire ad intelligendum substantias immateriales. Dicit enim Dionysius, I cap. Cael. Hier., quod *non est possibile humanae menti ad immaterialem illam sursum excitari caelestium hierarchiarum contemplationem, nisi secundum se materiali manuductione utatur*. Relinquitur ergo quod per materialia manuduci possumus ad intelligendum substantias immateriales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0039","text":"[I q. 88 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32163)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0040","text":"Praeterea, scientia est in intellectu. Sed scientiae et definitiones sunt de substantiis immaterialibus, definit enim Damascenus Angelum; et de Angelis aliqua documenta traduntur tam in theologicis quam in philosophicis disciplinis. Ergo substantiae immateriales intelligi possunt a nobis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0041","text":"[I q. 88 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32164)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0042","text":"Praeterea, anima humana est de genere substantiarum immaterialium. Sed ipsa intelligi potest a nobis per actum suum, quo intelligit materialia. Ergo et aliae substantiae immateriales intelligi possunt a nobis per suos effectus in rebus materialibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0043","text":"[I q. 88 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32165)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0044","text":"Praeterea, illa sola causa per suos effectus comprehendi non potest, quae in infinitum distat a suis effectibus. Hoc autem solius Dei est proprium. Ergo aliae substantiae immateriales creatae intelligi possunt a nobis per res materiales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0045","text":"[I q. 88 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32166)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0046","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, I cap. de Div. Nom., quod *sensibilibus intelligibilia, et compositis simplicia, et corporalibus incorporalia apprehendi non possunt*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0047","text":"[I q. 88 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32167)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0048","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut Averroes narrat in III de anima, quidam Avempace nomine, posuit quod per intellectum substantiarum materialium pervenire possumus, secundum vera philosophiae principia, ad intelligendum substantias immateriales. Cum enim intellectus noster natus sit abstrahere quidditatem rei materialis a materia, si iterum in illa quidditate sit aliquid materiae, poterit iterato abstrahere, et cum hoc in infinitum non procedat, tandem pervenire poterit ad intelligendum aliquam quidditatem quae sit omnino sine materia. Et hoc est intelligere substantiam immaterialem. Quod quidem efficaciter diceretur, si substantiae immateriales essent formae et species horum materialium, ut Platonici posuerunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0049","text":"Hoc autem non posito, sed supposito quod substantiae immateriales sint omnino alterius rationis a quidditatibus materialium rerum; quantumcumque intellectus noster abstrahat quidditatem rei materialis a materia, nunquam perveniet ad aliquid simile substantiae immateriali. Et ideo per substantias materiales non possumus perfecte substantias immateriales intelligere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0050","text":"[I q. 88 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32168)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0051","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ex rebus materialibus ascendere possumus in aliqualem cognitionem immaterialium rerum, non tamen in perfectam, quia non est sufficiens comparatio rerum materialium ad immateriales, sed similitudines si quae a materialibus accipiuntur ad immaterialia intelligenda, sunt multum dissimiles, ut Dionysius dicit, II cap. Cael. Hier.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0052","text":"[I q. 88 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32169)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0053","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod de superioribus rebus in scientiis maxime tractatur per viam remotionis, sic enim corpora caelestia notificat Aristoteles per negationem proprietatum inferiorum corporum. Unde multo magis immateriales substantiae a nobis cognosci non possunt, ut earum quidditates apprehendamus, sed de eis nobis in scientiis documenta traduntur per viam remotionis et alicuius habitudinis ad res materiales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0054","text":"[I q. 88 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32170)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0055","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod anima humana intelligit seipsam per suum intelligere, quod est actus proprius eius, perfecte demonstrans virtutem eius et naturam. Sed neque per hoc, neque per alia quae in rebus materialibus inveniuntur, perfecte cognosci potest immaterialium substantiarum virtus et natura, quia huiusmodi non adaequant earum virtutes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0056","text":"[I q. 88 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32171)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0057","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod substantiae immateriales creatae in genere quidem naturali non conveniunt cum substantiis materialibus, quia non est in eis eadem ratio potentiae et materiae, conveniunt tamen cum eis in genere logico, quia etiam substantiae immateriales sunt in praedicamento substantiae, cum earum quidditas non sit earum esse. Sed Deus non convenit cum rebus materialibus neque secundum genus naturale, neque secundum genus logicum, quia Deus nullo modo est in genere, ut supra dictum est. Unde per similitudines rerum materialium aliquid affirmative potest cognosci de Angelis secundum rationem communem, licet non secundum rationem speciei; de Deo autem nullo modo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0058","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0059","text":"[I q. 88 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32172)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0060","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus sit primum quod a mente humana cognoscitur. Illud enim in quo omnia alia cognoscuntur, et per quod de aliis iudicamus, est primo cognitum a nobis; sicut lux ab oculo, et principia prima ab intellectu. Sed omnia in luce primae veritatis cognoscimus, et per eam de omnibus iudicamus; ut dicit Augustinus in libro de Trin., et in libro de vera Relig. Ergo Deus est id quod primo cognoscitur a nobis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0061","text":"[I q. 88 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32173)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0062","text":"Praeterea, propter quod unumquodque, et illud magis. Sed Deus est causa omnis nostrae cognitionis, ipse enim est *lux vera, quae illuminat omnem hominem venientem in hunc mundum*, ut dicitur Ioan. I. Ergo Deus est id quod primo et maxime est cognitum nobis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0063","text":"[I q. 88 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32174)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0064","text":"Praeterea, id quod primo cognoscitur in imagine, est exemplar quo imago formatur. Sed in mente nostra est Dei imago, ut Augustinus dicit. Ergo id quod primo cognoscitur in mente nostra est Deus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0065","text":"[I q. 88 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32175)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0066","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Ioan. I, *Deum nemo vidit unquam*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0067","text":"[I q. 88 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32176)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0068","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum intellectus humanus, secundum statum praesentis vitae, non possit intelligere substantias immateriales creatas, ut dictum est; multo minus potest intelligere essentiam substantiae increatae. Unde simpliciter dicendum est quod Deus non est primum quod a nobis cognoscitur; sed magis per creaturas in Dei cognitionem pervenimus, secundum illud apostoli ad Rom. I, *invisibilia Dei per ea quae facta sunt, intellecta, conspiciuntur*. Primum autem quod intelligitur a nobis secundum statum praesentis vitae, est quidditas rei materialis, quae est nostri intellectus obiectum, ut multoties supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0069","text":"[I q. 88 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32177)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0070","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in luce primae veritatis omnia intelligimus et iudicamus, inquantum ipsum lumen intellectus nostri, sive naturale sive gratuitum, nihil aliud est quam quaedam impressio veritatis primae, ut supra dictum est. Unde cum ipsum lumen intellectus nostri non se habeat ad intellectum nostrum sicut quod intelligitur, sed sicut quo intelligitur; multo minus Deus est id quod primo a nostro intellectu intelligitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0071","text":"[I q. 88 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32178)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0072","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod propter quod unumquodque, illud magis, intelligendum est in his quae sunt unius ordinis, ut supra dictum est. Propter Deum autem alia cognoscuntur, non sicut propter primum cognitum, sed sicut propter primam cognoscitivae virtutis causam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0073","text":"[I q. 88 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32179)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0074","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, si in anima nostra esset perfecta imago Dei, sicut filius est perfecta imago patris, statim mens nostra intelligeret Deum. Est autem imago imperfecta. Unde ratio non sequitur.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"eff2c987c1648b024cdb9a96f358f06884a1040b54f760164d0a344d98d1395b","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T152117427067Z_de138b9b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 88","source_sha256":"eff2c987c1648b024cdb9a96f358f06884a1040b54f760164d0a344d98d1395b","sha256":"5152a0d96728d248f2f8fe98cd5fce81ed6b9e4246eb31a2f7e69b53daaaaaaa","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:21:17.427080+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0002","text":"[I q. 88 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32149)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider how the human soul knows those things that are above it, namely, immaterial substances. And concerning this, three questions are asked. First, whether the human soul, in the state of the present life, can understand through themselves the immaterial substances that we call Angels. Second, whether it can arrive at knowledge of them through the cognition of material things. Third, whether God is that which is first known by us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0005","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32150)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0006","text":"The first is approached thus. It seems that the human soul, in the state of the present life, can understand immaterial substances through themselves. For Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, IX: *the mind itself, just as it gathers knowledge of corporeal things through the senses of the body, so also [it gathers knowledge] of incorporeal things through itself.* Now immaterial substances are of this kind. Therefore the mind understands immaterial substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0007","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32151)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0008","text":"Further, like is known by like. But the human mind is more assimilated to immaterial things than to material things, since the mind itself is immaterial, as is clear from what has been said above. Therefore, since our mind understands material things, much more does it understand immaterial things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0009","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32152)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0010","text":"Further, the fact that those things which are in themselves most sensible are not most sensed by us arises from this: that the excellences of sensible things corrupt the sense. But the excellences of intelligible things do not corrupt the intellect, as is said in *On the Soul*, III. Therefore, those things which are in themselves most intelligible are also most intelligible to us. But since material things are not intelligible except because we make them actually intelligible by abstracting from matter, it is manifest that those substances which are immaterial according to their nature are more intelligible in themselves. Therefore they are understood by us much more than material things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0011","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32153)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0012","text":"Further, the Commentator says, in *Metaphysics*, II, that if abstract substances could not be understood by us, *then nature would have acted in vain, because it made that which is by nature understood in itself not understood by anyone.* But nothing in nature is idle or in vain. Therefore immaterial substances can be understood by us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0013","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32154)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0014","text":"Further, as sense stands to sensibles, so intellect stands to intelligibles. But our sight can see all bodies, whether they are higher and incorruptible or lower and corruptible. Therefore our intellect can understand all intelligible substances, both the higher and immaterial ones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0015","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32155)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0016","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Wisdom IX: *Who shall investigate the things that are in heaven?* But substances of this sort are said to be in heaven, according to Matthew XVIII: *Their Angels [are] in heaven*, and so forth. Therefore immaterial substances cannot be known by human investigation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0017","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32156)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0018","text":"I answer that, according to Plato's opinion, immaterial substances are not only understood by us, but are also the first things understood by us. For Plato held that subsisting immaterial forms, which he called ideas, are the proper objects of our intellect, and thus are first and through themselves understood by us. Yet the soul's cognition is applied to material things insofar as imagination and sense are mingled with the intellect. Hence, the more the intellect has been purified, the more it perceives the intelligible truth of immaterial things. But according to Aristotle's view, which we experience more, our intellect, in the state of the present life, has a natural relation to the natures of material things; hence it understands nothing except by turning itself toward phantasms, as is clear from what has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0019","text":"And thus it is manifest that we cannot understand first and through themselves, according to the mode of cognition experienced by us, immaterial substances which do not fall under sense and imagination. Nevertheless Averroes, in his Commentary on the third book of *On the Soul*, holds that at the end, in this life, a human being can attain to understanding separate substances through the continuation or union with us of a certain separate substance, which he calls the agent intellect; and this, since it is a separate substance, naturally understands separate substances. Hence, when it has been perfectly united to us, so that through it we can understand perfectly, we too shall understand separate substances; just as now, through the possible intellect united to us, we understand material things. He holds, moreover, that the agent intellect is united to us in this way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0020","text":"For since we understand through the agent intellect and through speculated intelligibles, as is clear when we understand conclusions through understood principles, it is necessary that the agent intellect be related to speculated intelligibles either as a principal agent to instruments, or as form to matter. For in these two ways an action is attributed to two principles: to a principal agent and an instrument, as cutting to the artisan and the saw; and to a form and subject, as heating to heat and fire. But in either way the agent intellect will be related to speculated intelligibles as perfection to the perfectible, and act to potency. Now the perfected thing and its perfection are received simultaneously in something, as the actually visible and light are [received] in the pupil. Therefore speculated intelligibles and the agent intellect are received simultaneously in the possible intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0021","text":"And the more speculated intelligibles we receive, the more we approach the point at which the agent intellect is perfectly united to us. Thus, when we have known all speculated intelligibles, the agent intellect will be perfectly united to us, and through it we shall be able to know all material and immaterial things. And in this he places the ultimate happiness of man. Nor does it matter, as concerns the present question, whether in that state of happiness the possible intellect understands separate substances through the agent intellect, as he himself holds, or whether, as he attributes to Alexander, the possible intellect never understands separate substances (because he holds the possible intellect to be corruptible), but man understands separate substances through the agent intellect. But the foregoing cannot stand.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0022","text":"First, indeed, because if the agent intellect is a separate substance, it is impossible for us to understand formally through it, because that by which an agent acts formally is the form and act of the agent, since every agent acts insofar as it is in act. This was likewise said above concerning the possible intellect. Second, because, in the aforesaid manner, the agent intellect, if it is a separate substance, would not be united to us according to its substance, but only its light, insofar as it is participated in the speculated intelligibles; and not as regards the other actions of the agent intellect, so that through this we might understand immaterial substances. Thus, when we see colours illuminated by the sun, the substance of the sun is not united to us, so that we may perform the actions of the sun; but only the light of the sun is united to us for the vision of colours.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0023","text":"Third, because, granted that the substance of the agent intellect were united to us in the aforesaid manner, still they do not hold that the agent intellect is totally united to us in accordance with one intelligible or two, but in accordance with all speculated intelligibles. But all speculated intelligibles fall short of the power of the agent intellect, because to understand separate substances is much greater than to understand all material things. Hence it is manifest that, even with all material things understood, the agent intellect would not be so united to us that we could understand separate substances through it. Fourth, because to understand all material intelligibles scarcely happens to anyone in this world; and thus no one, or few, would attain happiness. This is contrary to the Philosopher, who says in *Ethics*, I, that *happiness is a certain common good, which can be attained by all not deprived of virtue*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0024","text":"It is also contrary to reason that those contained under a species should attain the end of some species only in fewer cases. Fifth, because the Philosopher says expressly, in *Ethics*, I, that *happiness is an operation according to perfect virtue.* And after enumerating many virtues, in the tenth [book], he concludes that ultimate happiness, consisting in the cognition of the greatest intelligibles, is according to the virtue of wisdom, which he had set down in the sixth [book] as the head of the speculative sciences. Hence it is clear that Aristotle placed man's ultimate happiness in the cognition of separate substances, such as can be had through the speculative sciences, and not through a continuation of the agent intellect fabricated by some.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0025","text":"Sixth, because it was shown above that the agent intellect is not a separate substance, but a certain power of the soul, extending itself actively to the same things to which the possible intellect extends itself receptively; because, as is said in *On the Soul* III, the possible intellect is that by which all things come to be, and the agent intellect that by which all things are made. Therefore, according to the state of the present life, each intellect extends only to material things: these the agent intellect makes actually intelligible, and they are received in the possible intellect. Hence, according to the state of the present life, neither through the possible intellect nor through the agent intellect can we understand immaterial substances in themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0026","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32157)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0027","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that from that authority of Augustine it can be had that whatever our mind can acquire concerning the cognition of incorporeal things, it can know through itself. And this is so true that even among the philosophers it is said that the science concerning the soul is a certain principle for knowing separate substances. For through the fact that our soul knows itself, it attains to some cognition of incorporeal substances, such as it is able to have; not that by knowing itself it knows them simply and perfectly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0028","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32158)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0029","text":"To the second it must be said that likeness of nature is not a sufficient ground for cognition; otherwise one would have to say what Empedocles said, namely, that the soul was of the nature of all things in order that it might know all things. But for cognition it is required that there be in the knower a likeness of the thing known, as a certain form of that thing itself. Now our possible intellect, according to the state of the present life, is naturally fitted to be informed by likenesses of material things abstracted from phantasms; and therefore it knows material things more than immaterial substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0030","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32159)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0031","text":"To the third it must be said that a certain proportion of the object to the cognitive power is required, as of the active to the passive and of the perfecting to the perfectible. Hence, the fact that exceedingly sensible things are not grasped by sense is not due solely to their corrupting the sensory organs, but also to their being disproportionate to the sensitive powers. And in this way immaterial substances are disproportionate to our intellect in its present state, so that they cannot be understood by it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0032","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32160)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0033","text":"To the fourth it must be said that that argument of the Commentator fails in several ways. First, because it does not follow that, if separate substances are not understood by us, they are not understood by any intellect; for they are understood by themselves and by one another. Second, because it is not the end of separate substances to be understood by us. Now that is said to be idle and in vain which does not attain the end for which it exists. Thus it does not follow that immaterial substances are in vain, even if they were in no way understood by us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0034","text":"[I q. 88 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32161)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0035","text":"To the fifth it must be said that sense knows both higher and lower bodies in the same way, namely, through an alteration of the organ by the sensible object. But material substances, which are understood by way of abstraction, and immaterial substances, which cannot thus be understood by us because there are no phantasms of them, are not understood by us in the same way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0036","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0037","text":"[I q. 88 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32162)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0038","text":"We proceed to the second as follows. It seems that our intellect can arrive at understanding immaterial substances through cognition of material things. For Dionysius says, in chapter 1 of the *Celestial Hierarchy*, that *it is not possible for the human mind to be raised up to that immaterial contemplation of the heavenly hierarchies, unless it employs a material guidance suited to itself*. Therefore it follows that through material things we can be guided to understanding immaterial substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0039","text":"[I q. 88 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32163)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0040","text":"Further, science is in the intellect. But sciences and definitions concern immaterial substances: Damascene defines an angel, and certain teachings concerning angels are handed down both in theological and in philosophical disciplines. Therefore immaterial substances can be understood by us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0041","text":"[I q. 88 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32164)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0042","text":"Further, the human soul is of the genus of immaterial substances. But it can be understood by us through its act, by which it understands material things. Therefore other immaterial substances too can be understood by us through their effects in material things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0043","text":"[I q. 88 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32165)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0044","text":"Further, only that cause cannot be comprehended through its effects which is infinitely distant from its effects. But this is proper to God alone. Therefore other created immaterial substances can be understood by us through material things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0045","text":"[I q. 88 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32166)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0046","text":"But on the contrary, Dionysius says, in chapter 1 of *The Divine Names*, that *intelligible things cannot be apprehended through sensible things, nor simple things through composite things, nor incorporeal things through bodily things*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0047","text":"[I q. 88 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32167)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0048","text":"I answer that, as Averroes relates in *On the Soul* III, a certain man named Avempace held that through the intellect of material substances we can arrive, according to the true principles of philosophy, at understanding immaterial substances. For since our intellect is naturally fitted to abstract the quiddity of a material thing from matter, if there is again something of matter in that quiddity, it will be able to abstract again; and since this does not proceed to infinity, it will finally be able to arrive at understanding some quiddity that is altogether without matter. And this is to understand an immaterial substance. This indeed would be said effectively if immaterial substances were the forms and species of these material things, as the Platonists held.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0049","text":"But this is not posited, but rather supposed: that immaterial substances are of an altogether different notion from the quiddities of material things. However much our intellect abstracts the quiddity of a material thing from matter, it will never arrive at anything similar to an immaterial substance. And therefore through material substances we cannot perfectly understand immaterial substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0050","text":"[I q. 88 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32168)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0051","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that from material things we can ascend to some sort of knowledge of immaterial things, though not to perfect knowledge, because the comparison of material things to immaterial ones is not sufficient; rather, the likenesses, if any are taken from material things for understanding immaterial things, are very dissimilar, as Dionysius says, ch. 2 of the Celestial Hierarchy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0052","text":"[I q. 88 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32169)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0053","text":"To the second, it must be said that in the sciences higher things are chiefly treated by way of removal; for thus Aristotle makes the heavenly bodies known through the negation of the properties of lower bodies. Hence, still more, immaterial substances cannot be known by us so as to apprehend their quiddities; but teachings concerning them are handed down to us in the sciences by way of removal and of some relation to material things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0054","text":"[I q. 88 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32170)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0055","text":"To the third, it must be said that the human soul understands itself through its own understanding, which is its proper act, perfectly manifesting its power and nature. But neither through this nor through other things found in material things can the power and nature of immaterial substances be perfectly known, because things of this sort do not equal their powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0056","text":"[I q. 88 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32171)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0057","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that created immaterial substances do not agree with material substances in natural genus, because there is not in them the same notion of potency and matter; yet they agree with them in logical genus, because immaterial substances too are in the predicament of substance, since their quiddity is not their being. But God does not agree with material things either according to natural genus or according to logical genus, because God is in no way in a genus, as was said above. Hence, through likenesses of material things something can be known affirmatively of angels according to the common notion, though not according to the specific notion; but of God, in no way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0058","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0059","text":"[I q. 88 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32172)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0060","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that God is the first thing known by the human mind. For that in which all other things are known, and through which we judge concerning other things, is first known by us; as light is by the eye, and first principles by the intellect. But we know all things in the light of the first truth, and through it judge concerning all things; as Augustine says in the book On the Trinity and in the book On True Religion. Therefore God is that which is first known by us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0061","text":"[I q. 88 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32173)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0062","text":"Further, that on account of which each thing is, is itself more so. But God is the cause of all our knowledge, for he himself is *the true light, which enlightens every human being coming into this world*, as is said in John 1. Therefore God is that which is first and most known to us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0063","text":"[I q. 88 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32174)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0064","text":"Further, that which is first known in an image is the exemplar by which the image is formed. But in our mind there is an image of God, as Augustine says. Therefore that which is first known in our mind is God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0065","text":"[I q. 88 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32175)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0066","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in John 1, *No one has ever seen God*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0067","text":"[I q. 88 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32176)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0068","text":"I answer that it must be said that, since the human intellect, according to the state of the present life, cannot understand created immaterial substances, as has been said, much less can it understand the essence of the uncreated substance. Hence it must be said without qualification that God is not the first thing known by us; rather, we arrive at the knowledge of God through creatures, according to that saying of the Apostle in Romans 1: *the invisible things of God are clearly seen, being understood through the things that have been made*. But the first thing understood by us according to the state of the present life is the quiddity of a material thing, which is the object of our intellect, as has often been said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0069","text":"[I q. 88 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32177)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0070","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that we understand and judge all things in the light of the first truth, insofar as the very light of our intellect, whether natural or gratuitous, is nothing other than a certain impression of the first truth, as was said above. Hence, since the very light of our intellect is not related to our intellect as that which is understood, but as that by which something is understood, much less is God that which is first understood by our intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0071","text":"[I q. 88 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32178)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0072","text":"To the second, it must be said that the statement, “that on account of which each thing is, is itself more so,” must be understood of things that are of one order, as was said above. But other things are known on account of God, not as on account of the first known, but as on account of the first cause of the cognitive power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0073","text":"[I q. 88 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32179)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0074","text":"To the third, it must be said that, if there were in our soul a perfect image of God, just as the Son is a perfect image of the Father, our mind would immediately understand God. But it is an imperfect image. Hence the reasoning does not follow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_80:P0074"]}],"source_digest":"018e2d0d0c9379f80f69dc82d8e931c201f241fd3e9548ed859a0392c2e78878","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 88"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 88","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81","title":"First Part · Question 89","order":90,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_81","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 89 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32180)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de cognitione animae separatae. Et circa hoc quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum anima separata a corpore possit intelligere. Secundo, utrum intelligat substantias separatas. Tertio, utrum intelligat omnia naturalia. Quarto, utrum cognoscat singularia. Quinto, utrum habitus scientiae hic acquisitae remaneat in anima separata. Sexto, utrum possit uti habitu scientiae hic acquisitae. Septimo, utrum distantia localis impediat cognitionem animae separatae. Octavo, utrum animae separatae a corporibus cognoscant ea quae hic aguntur.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 89 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32181)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima separata nihil omnino intelligere possit. Dicit enim philosophus, in I de anima, quod *intelligere corrumpitur, interius quodam corrupto*. Sed omnia interiora hominis corrumpuntur per mortem. Ergo et ipsum intelligere corrumpitur.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32182)\n\nPraeterea, anima humana impeditur ab intelligendo per ligamentum sensus, et perturbata imaginatione, sicut supra dictum est. Sed morte totaliter sensus et imaginatio corrumpuntur, ut ex supra dictis patet. Ergo anima post mortem nihil intelligit.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32183)\n\nPraeterea, si anima separata intelligit, oportet quod per aliquas species intelligat. Sed non intelligit per species innatas, quia a principio est sicut tabula in qua nihil est scriptum. Neque per species quas abstrahat a rebus, quia non habet organa sensus et imaginationis, quibus mediantibus species intelligibiles abstrahuntur a rebus. Neque etiam per species prius abstractas, et in anima conservatas, quia sic anima pueri nihil intelligeret post mortem. Neque etiam per species intelligibiles divinitus influxas, haec enim cognitio non esset naturalis, de qua nunc agitur, sed gratiae. Ergo anima separata a corpore nihil intelligit.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32184)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in I de anima, quod *si non est aliqua operationum animae propria, non contingit ipsam separari*. Contingit autem ipsam separari. Ergo habet aliquam operationem propriam; et maxime eam quae est intelligere. Intelligit ergo sine corpore existens.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32185)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ista quaestio difficultatem habet ex hoc quod anima, quandiu est corpori coniuncta, non potest aliquid intelligere nisi convertendo se ad phantasmata, ut per experimentum patet. Si autem hoc non est ex natura animae, sed per accidens hoc convenit ei ex eo quod corpori alligatur, sicut Platonici posuerunt, de facili quaestio solvi posset. Nam remoto impedimento corporis, rediret anima ad suam naturam, ut intelligeret intelligibilia simpliciter, non convertendo se ad phantasmata, sicut est de aliis substantiis separatis. Sed secundum hoc, non esset anima corpori unita propter melius animae, si peius intelligeret corpori unita quam separata; sed hoc esset solum propter melius corporis, quod est irrationabile, cum materia sit propter formam, et non e converso. Si autem ponamus quod anima ex sua natura habeat ut intelligat convertendo se ad phantasmata, cum natura animae per mortem corporis non mutetur, videtur quod anima naturaliter nihil possit intelligere, cum non sint ei praesto phantasmata ad quae convertatur. Et ideo ad hanc difficultatem tollendam, considerandum est quod, cum nihil operetur nisi inquantum est actu, modus operandi uniuscuiusque rei sequitur modum essendi ipsius. Habet autem anima alium modum essendi cum unitur corpori, et cum fuerit a corpore separata, manente tamen eadem animae natura; non ita quod uniri corpori sit ei accidentale, sed per rationem suae naturae corpori unitur; sicut nec levis natura mutatur cum est in loco proprio, quod est ei naturale, et cum est extra proprium locum, quod est ei praeter naturam. Animae igitur secundum illum modum essendi quo corpori est unita, competit modus intelligendi per conversionem ad phantasmata corporum, quae in corporeis organis sunt, cum autem fuerit a corpore separata, competit ei modus intelligendi per conversionem ad ea quae sunt intelligibilia simpliciter, sicut et aliis substantiis separatis. Unde modus intelligendi per conversionem ad phantasmata est animae naturalis, sicut et corpori uniri, sed esse separatum a corpore est praeter rationem suae naturae, et similiter intelligere sine conversione ad phantasmata est ei praeter naturam. Et ideo ad hoc unitur corpori, ut sit et operetur secundum naturam suam. Sed hoc rursus habet dubitationem. Cum enim natura semper ordinetur ad id quod melius est; est autem melior modus intelligendi per conversionem ad intelligibilia simpliciter, quam per conversionem ad phantasmata, debuit sic a Deo institui animae natura, ut modus intelligendi nobilior ei esset naturalis, et non indigeret corpori propter hoc uniri. Considerandum est igitur quod, etsi intelligere per conversionem ad superiora sit simpliciter nobilius quam intelligere per conversionem ad phantasmata; tamen ille modus intelligendi, prout erat possibilis animae, erat imperfectior. Quod sic patet. In omnibus enim substantiis intellectualibus invenitur virtus intellectiva per influentiam divini luminis. Quod quidem in primo principio est unum et simplex; et quanto magis creaturae intellectuales distant a primo principio, tanto magis dividitur illud lumen et diversificatur, sicut accidit in lineis a centro egredientibus. Et inde est quod Deus per unam suam essentiam omnia intelligit; superiores autem intellectualium substantiarum, etsi per plures formas intelligant, tamen intelligunt per pauciores, et magis universales, et virtuosiores ad comprehensionem rerum, propter efficaciam virtutis intellectivae quae est in eis; in inferioribus autem sunt formae plures, et minus universales, et minus efficaces ad comprehensionem rerum, inquantum deficiunt a virtute intellectiva superiorum. Si ergo inferiores substantiae haberent formas in illa universalitate in qua habent superiores, quia non sunt tantae efficaciae in intelligendo, non acciperent per eas perfectam cognitionem de rebus, sed in quadam communitate et confusione. Quod aliqualiter apparet in hominibus, nam qui sunt debilioris intellectus, per universales conceptiones magis intelligentium non accipiunt perfectam cognitionem, nisi eis singula in speciali explicentur. Manifestum est autem inter substantias intellectuales, secundum naturae ordinem, infimas esse animas humanas. Hoc autem perfectio universi exigebat, ut diversi gradus in rebus essent. Si igitur animae humanae sic essent institutae a Deo ut intelligerent per modum qui competit substantiis separatis, non haberent cognitionem perfectam, sed confusam in communi. Ad hoc ergo quod perfectam et propriam cognitionem de rebus habere possent, sic naturaliter sunt institutae ut corporibus uniantur, et sic ab ipsis rebus sensibilibus propriam de eis cognitionem accipiant; sicut homines rudes ad scientiam induci non possunt nisi per sensibilia exempla. Sic ergo patet quod propter melius animae est ut corpori uniatur, et intelligat per conversionem ad phantasmata; et tamen esse potest separata, et alium modum intelligendi habere.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32186)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, si diligenter verba philosophi discutiantur, philosophus hoc dixit ex quadam suppositione prius facta, scilicet quod intelligere sit quidam motus coniuncti, sicut et sentire, nondum enim differentiam ostenderat inter intellectum et sensum. Vel potest dici quod loquitur de illo modo intelligendi qui est per conversionem ad phantasmata.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32187)\n\nDe quo etiam procedit secunda ratio.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32188)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod anima separata non intelligit per species innatas; nec per species quas tunc abstrahit; nec solum per species conservatas, ut obiectio probat, sed per species ex influentia divini luminis participatas, quarum anima fit particeps sicut et aliae substantiae separatae, quamvis inferiori modo. Unde tam cito cessante conversione ad corpus, ad superiora convertitur. Nec tamen propter hoc cognitio non est naturalis, quia Deus est auctor non solum influentiae gratuiti luminis, sed etiam naturalis.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 89 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32189)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima separata non intelligat substantias separatas. Perfectior enim est anima corpori coniuncta, quam a corpore separata, cum anima sit naturaliter pars humanae naturae; quaelibet autem pars perfectior est in suo toto. Sed anima coniuncta corpori non intelligit substantias separatas, ut supra habitum est. Ergo multo minus cum fuerit a corpore separata.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32190)\n\nPraeterea, omne quod cognoscitur, vel cognoscitur per sui praesentiam, vel per suam speciem. Sed substantiae separatae non possunt cognosci ab anima per suam praesentiam, quia nihil illabitur animae nisi solus Deus. Neque etiam per aliquas species quas anima ab Angelo abstrahere possit, quia Angelus simplicior est quam anima. Ergo nullo modo anima separata potest cognoscere substantias separatas.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32191)\n\nPraeterea, quidam philosophi posuerunt in cognitione separatarum substantiarum consistere ultimam hominis felicitatem. Si ergo anima separata potest intelligere substantias separatas, ex sola sua separatione consequitur felicitatem. Quod est inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32192)\n\nSed contra est quod animae separatae cognoscunt alias animas separatas; sicut dives in Inferno positus vidit Lazarum et Abraham, Luc. XVI. Ergo vident etiam et Daemones et Angelos animae separatae.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32193)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit in IX de Trin., *mens nostra cognitionem rerum incorporearum per seipsam accipit*, idest cognoscendo seipsam, sicut supra dictum est. Per hoc ergo quod anima separata cognoscit seipsam, accipere possumus qualiter cognoscit alias substantias separatas. Dictum est autem quod quandiu anima corpori est unita, intelligit convertendo se ad phantasmata. Et ideo nec seipsam potest intelligere nisi inquantum fit actu intelligens per speciem a phantasmatibus abstractam, sic enim per actum suum intelligit seipsam, ut supra dictum est. Sed cum fuerit a corpore separata, intelliget non convertendo se ad phantasmata, sed ad ea quae sunt secundum se intelligibilia, unde seipsam per seipsam intelliget. Est autem commune omni substantiae separatae quod intelligat id quod est supra se, et id quod est infra se, per modum suae substantiae, sic enim intelligitur aliquid secundum quod est in intelligente; est autem aliquid in altero per modum eius in quo est. Modus autem substantiae animae separatae est infra modum substantiae angelicae, sed est conformis modo aliarum animarum separatarum. Et ideo de aliis animabus separatis perfectam cognitionem habet; de Angelis autem imperfectam et deficientem, loquendo de cognitione naturali animae separatae. De cognitione autem gloriae est alia ratio.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32194)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod anima separata est quidem imperfectior, si consideretur natura qua communicat cum natura corporis, sed tamen quodammodo est liberior ad intelligendum, inquantum per gravedinem et occupationem corporis a puritate intelligentiae impeditur.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32195)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod anima separata intelligit Angelos per similitudines divinitus impressas. Quae tamen deficiunt a perfecta repraesentatione eorum, propter hoc quod animae natura est inferior quam Angeli.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32196)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in cognitione substantiarum separatarum non quarumcumque, consistit ultima hominis felicitas, sed solius Dei, qui non potest videri nisi per gratiam. In cognitione vero aliarum substantiarum separatarum est magna felicitas, etsi non ultima, si tamen perfecte intelligantur. Sed anima separata naturali cognitione non perfecte eas intelligit, ut dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 89 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32197)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima separata omnia naturalia cognoscat. In substantiis enim separatis sunt rationes omnium rerum naturalium. Sed animae separatae cognoscunt substantias separatas. Ergo cognoscunt omnia naturalia.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32198)\n\nPraeterea, qui intelligit magis intelligibile, multo magis potest intelligere minus intelligibile. Sed anima separata intelligit substantias separatas, quae sunt maxima intelligibilium. Ergo multo magis potest intelligere omnia naturalia, quae sunt minus intelligibilia.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 3 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32199)\n\nSed contra, in Daemonibus magis viget naturalis cognitio quam in anima separata. Sed Daemones non omnia naturalia cognoscunt; sed multa addiscunt per longi temporis experientiam, ut Isidorus dicit. Ergo neque animae separatae omnia naturalia cognoscunt.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 3 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32200)\n\nPraeterea, si anima statim cum est separata, omnia naturalia cognosceret, frustra homines studerent ad rerum scientiam capessendam. Hoc autem est inconveniens. Non ergo anima separata omnia naturalia cognoscit.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32201)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, anima separata intelligit per species quas recipit ex influentia divini luminis, sicut et Angeli, sed tamen, quia natura animae est infra naturam Angeli, cui iste modus cognoscendi est connaturalis, anima separata per huiusmodi species non accipit perfectam rerum cognitionem, sed quasi in communi et confusam. Sicut igitur se habent Angeli ad perfectam cognitionem rerum naturalium per huiusmodi species, ita animae separatae ad imperfectam et confusam. Angeli autem per huiusmodi species cognoscunt cognitione perfecta omnia naturalia, quia omnia quae Deus fecit in propriis naturis, fecit in intelligentia angelica, ut dicit Augustinus, super Gen. ad Litt. Unde et animae separatae de omnibus naturalibus cognitionem habent, non certam et propriam, sed communem et confusam.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32202)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod nec ipse Angelus per suam substantiam cognoscit omnia naturalia, sed per species quasdam, ut supra dictum est. Et ideo non propter hoc sequitur quod anima cognoscat omnia naturalia, quia cognoscit quoquo modo substantiam separatam.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32203)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut anima separata non perfecte intelligit substantias separatas ita nec omnia naturalia perfecte cognoscit, sed sub quadam confusione, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32204)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Isidorus loquitur de cognitione futurorum; quae nec Angeli nec Daemones nec animae separatae cognoscunt, nisi vel in suis causis, vel per revelationem divinam. Nos autem loquimur de cognitione naturalium.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32205)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod cognitio quae acquiritur hic per studium, est propria et perfecta; illa autem est confusa. Unde non sequitur quod studium addiscendi sit frustra.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 89 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32206)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima separata non cognoscat singularia. Nulla enim potentia cognoscitiva remanet in anima separata nisi intellectus, ut ex supra dictis patet. Sed intellectus non est cognoscitivus singularium, ut supra habitum est. Ergo anima separata singularia non cognoscit.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32207)\n\nPraeterea, magis est determinata cognitio qua cognoscitur aliquid in singulari, quam illa qua cognoscitur aliquid in universali. Sed anima separata non habet determinatam cognitionem de speciebus rerum naturalium. Multo igitur minus cognoscit singularia.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32208)\n\nPraeterea, si cognoscit singularia, et non per sensum, pari ratione omnia singularia cognosceret. Sed non cognoscit omnia singularia. Ergo nulla cognoscit.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32209)\n\nSed contra est quod dives in Inferno positus dixit, habeo quinque fratres, ut habetur Luc. XVI.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32210)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod animae separatae aliqua singularia cognoscunt, sed non omnia, etiam quae sunt praesentia. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod duplex est modus intelligendi. Unus per abstractionem a phantasmatibus, et secundum istum modum singularia per intellectum cognosci non possunt directe, sed indirecte, sicut supra dictum est. Alius modus intelligendi est per influentiam specierum a Deo, et per istum modum intellectus potest singularia cognoscere. Sicut enim ipse Deus per suam essentiam, inquantum est causa universalium et individualium principiorum, cognoscit omnia et universalia et singularia, ut supra dictum est; ita substantiae separatae per species, quae sunt quaedam participatae similitudines illius divinae essentiae, possunt singularia cognoscere. In hoc tamen est differentia inter Angelos et animas separatas, quia Angeli per huiusmodi species habent perfectam et propriam cognitionem de rebus, animae vero separatae confusam. Unde Angeli, propter efficaciam sui intellectus per huiusmodi species non solum naturas rerum in speciali cognoscere possunt, sed etiam singularia sub speciebus contenta. Animae vero separatae non possunt cognoscere per huiusmodi species nisi solum singularia illa ad quae quodammodo determinantur, vel per praecedentem cognitionem, vel per aliquam affectionem, vel per naturalem habitudinem, vel per divinam ordinationem, quia omne quod recipitur in aliquo, determinatur in eo secundum modum recipientis.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32211)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod intellectus per viam abstractionis non est cognoscitivus singularium. Sic autem anima separata non intelligit, sed sicut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32212)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ad illarum rerum species vel individua cognitio animae separatae determinatur, ad quae anima separata habet aliquam determinatam habitudinem, sicut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32213)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod anima separata non se habet aequaliter ad omnia singularia, sed ad quaedam habet aliquam habitudinem quam non habet ad alia. Et ideo non est aequalis ratio ut omnia singularia cognoscat.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 89 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32214)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod habitus scientiae hic acquisitae non remaneat in anima separata. Dicit enim apostolus, I ad Cor. XIII, scientia destruetur.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32215)\n\nPraeterea, quidam minus boni in hoc mundo scientia pollent, aliis magis bonis carentibus scientia. Si ergo habitus scientiae permaneret etiam post mortem in anima, sequeretur quod aliqui minus boni etiam in futuro statu essent potiores aliquibus magis bonis. Quod videtur inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32216)\n\nPraeterea, animae separatae habebunt scientiam per influentiam divini luminis. Si igitur scientia hic acquisita in anima separata remaneat, sequetur quod duae erunt formae unius speciei in eodem subiecto. Quod est impossibile.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32217)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus dicit, in libro Praedicament., quod *habitus est qualitas difficile mobilis; sed ab aegritudine, vel ab aliquo huiusmodi, quandoque corrumpitur scientia*. Sed nulla est ita fortis immutatio in hac vita, sicut immutatio quae est per mortem. Ergo videtur quod habitus scientiae per mortem corrumpatur.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32218)\n\nSed contra est quod Hieronymus dicit, in epistola ad Paulinum, *discamus in terris, quorum scientia nobis perseveret in caelo*.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32219)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam posuerunt habitum scientiae non esse in ipso intellectu, sed in viribus sensitivis, scilicet imaginativa, cogitativa et memorativa; et quod species intelligibiles non conservantur in intellectu possibili. Et si haec opinio vera esset, sequeretur quod, destructo corpore, totaliter habitus scientiae hic acquisitae destrueretur. Sed quia scientia est in intellectu, qui est locus specierum, ut dicitur in III de anima; oportet quod habitus scientiae hic acquisitae partim sit in praedictis viribus sensitivis, et partim in ipso intellectu. Et hoc potest considerari ex ipsis actibus ex quibus habitus scientiae acquiritur, nam *habitus sunt similes actibus ex quibus acquiruntur*, ut dicitur in II Ethic. Actus autem intellectus ex quibus in praesenti vita scientia acquiritur, sunt per conversionem intellectus ad phantasmata, quae sunt in praedictis viribus sensitivis. Unde per tales actus et ipsi intellectui possibili acquiritur facultas quaedam ad considerandum per species susceptas; et in praedictis inferioribus viribus acquiritur quaedam habilitas ut facilius per conversionem ad ipsas intellectus possit intelligibilia speculari. Sed sicut actus intellectus principaliter quidem et formaliter est in ipso intellectu, materialiter autem et dispositive in inferioribus viribus, idem etiam dicendum est de habitu. Quantum ergo ad id quod aliquis praesentis scientiae habet in inferioribus viribus, non remanebit in anima separata, sed quantum ad id quod habet in ipso intellectu, necesse est ut remaneat. Quia, ut dicitur in libro de longitudine et brevitate vitae, dupliciter corrumpitur aliqua forma, uno modo, per se, quando corrumpitur a suo contrario, ut calidum a frigido; alio modo, per accidens, scilicet per corruptionem subiecti. Manifestum est autem quod per corruptionem subiecti, scientia quae est in intellectu humano, corrumpi non potest, cum intellectus sit incorruptibilis, ut supra ostensum est. Similiter etiam nec per contrarium corrumpi possunt species intelligibiles quae sunt in intellectu possibili, quia intentioni intelligibili nihil est contrarium; et praecipue quantum ad simplicem intelligentiam, qua intelligitur quod quid est. Sed quantum ad operationem qua intellectus componit et dividit, vel etiam ratiocinatur, sic invenitur contrarietas in intellectu, secundum quod falsum in propositione vel in argumentatione est contrarium vero. Et hoc modo interdum scientia corrumpitur per contrarium, dum scilicet aliquis per falsam argumentationem abducitur a scientia veritatis. Et ideo philosophus, in libro praedicto, ponit duos modos quibus scientia per se corrumpitur, scilicet oblivionem, ex parte memorativae, et deceptionem, ex parte argumentationis falsae. Sed hoc non habet locum in anima separata. Unde dicendum est quod habitus scientiae, secundum quod est in intellectu manet in anima separata.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32220)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod apostolus non loquitur ibi de scientia quantum ad habitum, sed quantum ad cognitionis actum. Unde ad huius probationem inducit, nunc cognosco ex parte.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32221)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut secundum staturam corporis aliquis minus bonus erit maior aliquo magis bono; ita nihil prohibet aliquem minus bonum habere aliquem scientiae habitum in futuro, quem non habet aliquis magis bonus. Sed tamen hoc quasi nullius momenti est in comparatione ad alias praerogativas quas meliores habebunt.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32222)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod utraque scientia non est unius rationis. Unde nullum inconveniens sequitur.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32223)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod ratio illa procedit de corruptione scientiae quantum ad id quod habet ex parte sensitivarum virium.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 89 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32224)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod actus scientiae hic acquisitae non maneat in anima separata. Dicit enim philosophus, in I de anima, quod corrupto corpore, anima neque reminiscitur neque amat. Sed considerare ea quae prius aliquis novit, est reminisci. Ergo anima separata non potest habere actum scientiae quam hic acquisivit.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32225)\n\nPraeterea, species intelligibiles non erunt potentiores in anima separata quam sint in anima corpori unita. Sed per species intelligibiles non possumus modo intelligere, nisi convertendo nos super phantasmata, sicut supra habitum est. Ergo nec anima separata hoc poterit. Et ita nullo modo per species intelligibiles hic acquisitas anima separata intelligere poterit.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32226)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus dicit, in II Ethic., quod *habitus similes actus reddunt actibus per quos acquiruntur*. Sed habitus scientiae hic acquiritur per actus intellectus convertentis se supra phantasmata. Ergo non potest alios actus reddere. Sed tales actus non competunt animae separatae. Ergo anima separata non habebit aliquem actum scientiae hic acquisitae.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32227)\n\nSed contra est quod Luc. XVI, dicitur ad divitem in Inferno positum, *recordare quia recepisti bona in vita tua*.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32228)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in actu est duo considerare, scilicet speciem actus, et modum ipsius. Et species quidem actus consideratur ex obiecto in quod actus cognoscitivae virtutis dirigitur per speciem, quae est obiecti similitudo, sed modus actus pensatur ex virtute agentis. Sicut quod aliquis videat lapidem, contingit ex specie lapidis quae est in oculo, sed quod acute videat, contingit ex virtute visiva oculi. Cum igitur species intelligibiles maneant in anima separata, sicut dictum est; status autem animae separatae non sit idem sicut modo est, sequitur quod secundum species intelligibiles hic acquisitas, anima separata intelligere possit quae prius intellexit; non tamen eodem modo, scilicet per conversionem ad phantasmata, sed per modum convenientem animae separatae. Et ita manet quidem in anima separata actus scientiae hic acquisitae, sed non secundum eundem modum.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32229)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod philosophus loquitur de reminiscentia, secundum quod memoria pertinet ad partem sensitivam, non autem secundum quod memoria est quodammodo in intellectu, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32230)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod diversus modus intelligendi non provenit ex diversa virtute specierum, sed ex diverso statu animae intelligentis.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32231)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod actus per quos acquiritur habitus, sunt similes actibus quos habitus causant, quantum ad speciem actus, non autem quantum ad modum agendi. Nam operari iusta, sed non iuste, idest delectabiliter, causat habitum iustitiae politicae, per quem delectabiliter operamur.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 89 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32232)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod distantia localis impediat cognitionem animae separatae. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro de cura pro mortuis agenda, quod *animae mortuorum ibi sunt, ubi ea quae hic fiunt scire non possunt*. Sciunt autem ea quae apud eos aguntur. Ergo distantia localis impedit cognitionem animae separatae.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32233)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, in libro de divinatione Daemonum, quod *Daemones, propter celeritatem motus, aliqua nobis ignota denuntiant*. Sed agilitas motus ad hoc nihil faceret, si distantia localis cognitionem Daemonis non impediret. Multo igitur magis distantia localis impedit cognitionem animae separatae, quae est inferior secundum naturam quam Daemon.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32234)\n\nPraeterea, sicut distat aliquis secundum locum, ita secundum tempus sed distantia temporis impedit cognitionem animae separatae, non enim cognoscunt futura. Ergo videtur quod etiam distantia secundum locum animae separatae cognitionem impediat.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32235)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Luc. XVI, quod *dives cum esset in tormentis, elevans oculos suos, vidit Abraham a longe*. Ergo distantia localis non impedit animae separatae cognitionem.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32236)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam posuerunt quod anima separata cognosceret singularia abstrahendo a sensibilibus. Quod si esset verum, posset dici quod distantia localis impediret animae separatae cognitionem, requireretur enim quod vel sensibilia agerent in animam separatam, vel anima separata in sensibilia; et quantum ad utrumque, requireretur distantia determinata. Sed praedicta positio est impossibilis, quia abstractio specierum a sensibilibus fit mediantibus sensibus et aliis potentiis sensitivis, quae in anima separata actu non manent. Intelligit autem anima separata singularia per influxum specierum ex divino lumine, quod quidem lumen aequaliter se habet ad propinquum et distans. Unde distantia localis nullo modo impedit animae separatae cognitionem.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32237)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus non dicit quod propter hoc quod ibi sunt animae mortuorum, ea quae hic sunt videre non possunt, ut localis distantia huius ignorantiae causa esse credatur, sed hoc potest propter aliquid aliud contingere, ut infra dicetur.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32238)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Augustinus ibi loquitur secundum opinionem illam qua aliqui posuerunt quod Daemones habent corpora naturaliter sibi unita, secundum quam positionem, etiam potentias sensitivas habere possunt, ad quarum cognitionem requiritur determinata distantia. Et hanc opinionem etiam in eodem libro Augustinus expresse tangit, licet eam magis recitando quam asserendo tangere videatur, ut patet per ea quae dicit XXI libro de Civ. Dei.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32239)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod futura, quae distant secundum tempus, non sunt entia in actu. Unde in seipsis non sunt cognoscibilia, quia sicut deficit aliquid ab entitate, ita deficit a cognoscibilitate. Sed ea quae sunt distantia secundum locum, sunt entia in actu, et secundum se cognoscibilia. Unde non est eadem ratio de distantia locali, et de distantia temporis.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 89 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32240)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod animae separatae cognoscant ea quae hic aguntur. Nisi enim ea cognoscerent, de eis curam non haberent. Sed habent curam de his quae hic aguntur; secundum illud Luc. XVI, *habeo quinque fratres, ut testificetur illis, ne et ipsi veniant in hunc locum tormentorum*. Ergo animae separatae cognoscunt ea quae hic aguntur.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32241)\n\nPraeterea, frequenter mortui vivis apparent, vel dormientibus vel vigilantibus, et eos admonent de iis quae hic aguntur; sicut Samuel apparuit Sauli, ut habetur I Reg. XXVIII. Sed hoc non esset si ea quae hic sunt non cognoscerent. Ergo ea quae hic aguntur cognoscunt.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32242)\n\nPraeterea, animae separatae cognoscunt ea quae apud eas aguntur. Si ergo quae apud nos aguntur non cognoscerent, impediretur earum cognitio per localem distantiam. Quod supra negatum est.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32243)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Iob XIV, *sive fuerint filii eius nobiles, sive ignobiles, non intelliget*.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32244)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, secundum naturalem cognitionem, de qua nunc hic agitur, animae mortuorum nesciunt quae hic aguntur. Et huius ratio ex dictis accipi potest. Quia anima separata cognoscit singularia per hoc quod quodammodo determinata est ad illa, vel per vestigium alicuius praecedentis cognitionis seu affectionis, vel per ordinationem divinam. Animae autem mortuorum, secundum ordinationem divinam, et secundum modum essendi, segregatae sunt a conversatione viventium, et coniunctae conversationi spiritualium substantiarum quae sunt a corpore separatae. Unde ea quae apud nos aguntur ignorant. Et hanc rationem assignat Gregorius in XII Moralium, dicens, *mortui vita in carne viventium post eos, qualiter disponatur, nesciunt, quia vita spiritus longe est a vita carnis; et sicut corporea atque incorporea diversa sunt genere, ita sunt distincta cognitione*. Et hoc etiam Augustinus videtur tangere in libro de cura pro mortuis agenda, dicens quod *animae mortuorum rebus viventium non intersunt*. Sed quantum ad animas beatorum, videtur esse differentia inter Gregorium et Augustinum. Nam Gregorius ibidem subdit, *quod tamen de animabus sanctis sentiendum non est, quia quae intus omnipotentis Dei claritatem vident, nullo modo credendum est quod sit foris aliquid quod ignorent*. Augustinus vero, in libro de cura pro mortuis agenda, expresse dicit quod *nesciunt mortui, etiam sancti, quid agant vivi et eorum filii*, ut habetur in Glossa, super illud, *Abraham nescivit nos*, Isaiae LXIII. Quod quidem confirmat per hoc quod a matre sua non visitabatur, nec in tristitiis consolabatur, sicut quando vivebat; nec est probabile ut sit facta vita feliciore crudelior. Et per hoc quod dominus promisit Iosiae regi quod prius moreretur ne videret mala quae erant populo superventura, ut habetur IV Reg. XXII. Sed Augustinus hoc dubitando dicit, unde praemittit, *ut volet, accipiat quisque quod dicam*. Gregorius autem assertive, quod patet per hoc quod dicit, *nullo modo credendum est*. Magis tamen videtur, secundum sententiam Gregorii, quod animae sanctorum Deum videntes, omnia praesentia quae hic aguntur cognoscant. Sunt enim Angelis aequales, de quibus etiam Augustinus asserit quod ea quae apud vivos aguntur non ignorant. Sed quia sanctorum animae sunt perfectissime iustitiae divinae coniunctae, nec tristantur, nec rebus viventium se ingerunt, nisi secundum quod iustitiae divinae dispositio exigit.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32245)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod animae mortuorum possunt habere curam de rebus viventium, etiam si ignorent eorum statum; sicut nos curam habemus de mortuis, eis suffragia impendendo, quamvis eorum statum ignoremus. Possunt etiam facta viventium non per seipsos cognoscere, sed vel per animas eorum qui hinc ad eos accedunt; vel per Angelos seu Daemones; vel etiam spiritu Dei revelante, sicut Augustinus in eodem libro dicit.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32246)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod hoc quod mortui viventibus apparent qualitercumque, vel contingit per specialem Dei dispensationem, ut animae mortuorum rebus viventium intersint, et est inter divina miracula computandum. Vel huiusmodi apparitiones fiunt per operationes Angelorum bonorum vel malorum, etiam ignorantibus mortuis, sicut etiam vivi ignorantes aliis viventibus apparent in somnis, ut Augustinus dicit in libro praedicto. Unde et de Samuele dici potest quod ipse apparuit per revelationem divinam; secundum hoc quod dicitur Eccli. XLVI, quod *dormivit, et notum fecit regi finem vitae suae*. Vel illa apparitio fuit procurata per Daemones, si tamen Ecclesiastici auctoritas non recipiatur, propter hoc quod inter canonicas Scripturas apud Hebraeos non habetur.\n\n[I q. 89 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32247)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ignorantia huiusmodi non contingit ex locali distantia, sed propter causam praedictam.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0002","text":"[I q. 89 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32180)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de cognitione animae separatae. Et circa hoc quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum anima separata a corpore possit intelligere. Secundo, utrum intelligat substantias separatas. Tertio, utrum intelligat omnia naturalia. Quarto, utrum cognoscat singularia. Quinto, utrum habitus scientiae hic acquisitae remaneat in anima separata. Sexto, utrum possit uti habitu scientiae hic acquisitae. Septimo, utrum distantia localis impediat cognitionem animae separatae. Octavo, utrum animae separatae a corporibus cognoscant ea quae hic aguntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0005","text":"[I q. 89 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32181)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima separata nihil omnino intelligere possit. Dicit enim philosophus, in I de anima, quod *intelligere corrumpitur, interius quodam corrupto*. Sed omnia interiora hominis corrumpuntur per mortem. Ergo et ipsum intelligere corrumpitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0007","text":"[I q. 89 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32182)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0008","text":"Praeterea, anima humana impeditur ab intelligendo per ligamentum sensus, et perturbata imaginatione, sicut supra dictum est. Sed morte totaliter sensus et imaginatio corrumpuntur, ut ex supra dictis patet. Ergo anima post mortem nihil intelligit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0009","text":"[I q. 89 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32183)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0010","text":"Praeterea, si anima separata intelligit, oportet quod per aliquas species intelligat. Sed non intelligit per species innatas, quia a principio est sicut tabula in qua nihil est scriptum. Neque per species quas abstrahat a rebus, quia non habet organa sensus et imaginationis, quibus mediantibus species intelligibiles abstrahuntur a rebus. Neque etiam per species prius abstractas, et in anima conservatas, quia sic anima pueri nihil intelligeret post mortem. Neque etiam per species intelligibiles divinitus influxas, haec enim cognitio non esset naturalis, de qua nunc agitur, sed gratiae. Ergo anima separata a corpore nihil intelligit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0011","text":"[I q. 89 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32184)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in I de anima, quod *si non est aliqua operationum animae propria, non contingit ipsam separari*. Contingit autem ipsam separari. Ergo habet aliquam operationem propriam; et maxime eam quae est intelligere. Intelligit ergo sine corpore existens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0013","text":"[I q. 89 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32185)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ista quaestio difficultatem habet ex hoc quod anima, quandiu est corpori coniuncta, non potest aliquid intelligere nisi convertendo se ad phantasmata, ut per experimentum patet. Si autem hoc non est ex natura animae, sed per accidens hoc convenit ei ex eo quod corpori alligatur, sicut Platonici posuerunt, de facili quaestio solvi posset. Nam remoto impedimento corporis, rediret anima ad suam naturam, ut intelligeret intelligibilia simpliciter, non convertendo se ad phantasmata, sicut est de aliis substantiis separatis. Sed secundum hoc, non esset anima corpori unita propter melius animae, si peius intelligeret corpori unita quam separata; sed hoc esset solum propter melius corporis, quod est irrationabile, cum materia sit propter formam, et non e converso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0015","text":"Si autem ponamus quod anima ex sua natura habeat ut intelligat convertendo se ad phantasmata, cum natura animae per mortem corporis non mutetur, videtur quod anima naturaliter nihil possit intelligere, cum non sint ei praesto phantasmata ad quae convertatur. Et ideo ad hanc difficultatem tollendam, considerandum est quod, cum nihil operetur nisi inquantum est actu, modus operandi uniuscuiusque rei sequitur modum essendi ipsius. Habet autem anima alium modum essendi cum unitur corpori, et cum fuerit a corpore separata, manente tamen eadem animae natura; non ita quod uniri corpori sit ei accidentale, sed per rationem suae naturae corpori unitur; sicut nec levis natura mutatur cum est in loco proprio, quod est ei naturale, et cum est extra proprium locum, quod est ei praeter naturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0016","text":"Animae igitur secundum illum modum essendi quo corpori est unita, competit modus intelligendi per conversionem ad phantasmata corporum, quae in corporeis organis sunt, cum autem fuerit a corpore separata, competit ei modus intelligendi per conversionem ad ea quae sunt intelligibilia simpliciter, sicut et aliis substantiis separatis. Unde modus intelligendi per conversionem ad phantasmata est animae naturalis, sicut et corpori uniri, sed esse separatum a corpore est praeter rationem suae naturae, et similiter intelligere sine conversione ad phantasmata est ei praeter naturam. Et ideo ad hoc unitur corpori, ut sit et operetur secundum naturam suam. Sed hoc rursus habet dubitationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0017","text":"Cum enim natura semper ordinetur ad id quod melius est; est autem melior modus intelligendi per conversionem ad intelligibilia simpliciter, quam per conversionem ad phantasmata, debuit sic a Deo institui animae natura, ut modus intelligendi nobilior ei esset naturalis, et non indigeret corpori propter hoc uniri. Considerandum est igitur quod, etsi intelligere per conversionem ad superiora sit simpliciter nobilius quam intelligere per conversionem ad phantasmata; tamen ille modus intelligendi, prout erat possibilis animae, erat imperfectior. Quod sic patet. In omnibus enim substantiis intellectualibus invenitur virtus intellectiva per influentiam divini luminis. Quod quidem in primo principio est unum et simplex; et quanto magis creaturae intellectuales distant a primo principio, tanto magis dividitur illud lumen et diversificatur, sicut accidit in lineis a centro egredientibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0018","text":"Et inde est quod Deus per unam suam essentiam omnia intelligit; superiores autem intellectualium substantiarum, etsi per plures formas intelligant, tamen intelligunt per pauciores, et magis universales, et virtuosiores ad comprehensionem rerum, propter efficaciam virtutis intellectivae quae est in eis; in inferioribus autem sunt formae plures, et minus universales, et minus efficaces ad comprehensionem rerum, inquantum deficiunt a virtute intellectiva superiorum. Si ergo inferiores substantiae haberent formas in illa universalitate in qua habent superiores, quia non sunt tantae efficaciae in intelligendo, non acciperent per eas perfectam cognitionem de rebus, sed in quadam communitate et confusione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0019","text":"Quod aliqualiter apparet in hominibus, nam qui sunt debilioris intellectus, per universales conceptiones magis intelligentium non accipiunt perfectam cognitionem, nisi eis singula in speciali explicentur. Manifestum est autem inter substantias intellectuales, secundum naturae ordinem, infimas esse animas humanas. Hoc autem perfectio universi exigebat, ut diversi gradus in rebus essent. Si igitur animae humanae sic essent institutae a Deo ut intelligerent per modum qui competit substantiis separatis, non haberent cognitionem perfectam, sed confusam in communi. Ad hoc ergo quod perfectam et propriam cognitionem de rebus habere possent, sic naturaliter sunt institutae ut corporibus uniantur, et sic ab ipsis rebus sensibilibus propriam de eis cognitionem accipiant; sicut homines rudes ad scientiam induci non possunt nisi per sensibilia exempla.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0020","text":"Sic ergo patet quod propter melius animae est ut corpori uniatur, et intelligat per conversionem ad phantasmata; et tamen esse potest separata, et alium modum intelligendi habere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0021","text":"[I q. 89 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32186)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0022","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, si diligenter verba philosophi discutiantur, philosophus hoc dixit ex quadam suppositione prius facta, scilicet quod intelligere sit quidam motus coniuncti, sicut et sentire, nondum enim differentiam ostenderat inter intellectum et sensum. Vel potest dici quod loquitur de illo modo intelligendi qui est per conversionem ad phantasmata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0023","text":"[I q. 89 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32187)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0024","text":"De quo etiam procedit secunda ratio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0025","text":"[I q. 89 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32188)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0026","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod anima separata non intelligit per species innatas; nec per species quas tunc abstrahit; nec solum per species conservatas, ut obiectio probat, sed per species ex influentia divini luminis participatas, quarum anima fit particeps sicut et aliae substantiae separatae, quamvis inferiori modo. Unde tam cito cessante conversione ad corpus, ad superiora convertitur. Nec tamen propter hoc cognitio non est naturalis, quia Deus est auctor non solum influentiae gratuiti luminis, sed etiam naturalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0027","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0028","text":"[I q. 89 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32189)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0029","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima separata non intelligat substantias separatas. Perfectior enim est anima corpori coniuncta, quam a corpore separata, cum anima sit naturaliter pars humanae naturae; quaelibet autem pars perfectior est in suo toto. Sed anima coniuncta corpori non intelligit substantias separatas, ut supra habitum est. Ergo multo minus cum fuerit a corpore separata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0030","text":"[I q. 89 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32190)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0031","text":"Praeterea, omne quod cognoscitur, vel cognoscitur per sui praesentiam, vel per suam speciem. Sed substantiae separatae non possunt cognosci ab anima per suam praesentiam, quia nihil illabitur animae nisi solus Deus. Neque etiam per aliquas species quas anima ab Angelo abstrahere possit, quia Angelus simplicior est quam anima. Ergo nullo modo anima separata potest cognoscere substantias separatas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0032","text":"[I q. 89 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32191)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0033","text":"Praeterea, quidam philosophi posuerunt in cognitione separatarum substantiarum consistere ultimam hominis felicitatem. Si ergo anima separata potest intelligere substantias separatas, ex sola sua separatione consequitur felicitatem. Quod est inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0034","text":"[I q. 89 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32192)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0035","text":"Sed contra est quod animae separatae cognoscunt alias animas separatas; sicut dives in Inferno positus vidit Lazarum et Abraham, Luc. XVI. Ergo vident etiam et Daemones et Angelos animae separatae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0036","text":"[I q. 89 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32193)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0037","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit in IX de Trin., *mens nostra cognitionem rerum incorporearum per seipsam accipit*, idest cognoscendo seipsam, sicut supra dictum est. Per hoc ergo quod anima separata cognoscit seipsam, accipere possumus qualiter cognoscit alias substantias separatas. Dictum est autem quod quandiu anima corpori est unita, intelligit convertendo se ad phantasmata. Et ideo nec seipsam potest intelligere nisi inquantum fit actu intelligens per speciem a phantasmatibus abstractam, sic enim per actum suum intelligit seipsam, ut supra dictum est. Sed cum fuerit a corpore separata, intelliget non convertendo se ad phantasmata, sed ad ea quae sunt secundum se intelligibilia, unde seipsam per seipsam intelliget.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0038","text":"Est autem commune omni substantiae separatae quod intelligat id quod est supra se, et id quod est infra se, per modum suae substantiae, sic enim intelligitur aliquid secundum quod est in intelligente; est autem aliquid in altero per modum eius in quo est. Modus autem substantiae animae separatae est infra modum substantiae angelicae, sed est conformis modo aliarum animarum separatarum. Et ideo de aliis animabus separatis perfectam cognitionem habet; de Angelis autem imperfectam et deficientem, loquendo de cognitione naturali animae separatae. De cognitione autem gloriae est alia ratio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0039","text":"[I q. 89 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32194)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0040","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod anima separata est quidem imperfectior, si consideretur natura qua communicat cum natura corporis, sed tamen quodammodo est liberior ad intelligendum, inquantum per gravedinem et occupationem corporis a puritate intelligentiae impeditur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0041","text":"[I q. 89 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32195)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0042","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod anima separata intelligit Angelos per similitudines divinitus impressas. Quae tamen deficiunt a perfecta repraesentatione eorum, propter hoc quod animae natura est inferior quam Angeli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0043","text":"[I q. 89 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32196)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0044","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in cognitione substantiarum separatarum non quarumcumque, consistit ultima hominis felicitas, sed solius Dei, qui non potest videri nisi per gratiam. In cognitione vero aliarum substantiarum separatarum est magna felicitas, etsi non ultima, si tamen perfecte intelligantur. Sed anima separata naturali cognitione non perfecte eas intelligit, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0045","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0046","text":"[I q. 89 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32197)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0047","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima separata omnia naturalia cognoscat. In substantiis enim separatis sunt rationes omnium rerum naturalium. Sed animae separatae cognoscunt substantias separatas. Ergo cognoscunt omnia naturalia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0048","text":"[I q. 89 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32198)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0049","text":"Praeterea, qui intelligit magis intelligibile, multo magis potest intelligere minus intelligibile. Sed anima separata intelligit substantias separatas, quae sunt maxima intelligibilium. Ergo multo magis potest intelligere omnia naturalia, quae sunt minus intelligibilia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0050","text":"[I q. 89 a. 3 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32199)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0051","text":"Sed contra, in Daemonibus magis viget naturalis cognitio quam in anima separata. Sed Daemones non omnia naturalia cognoscunt; sed multa addiscunt per longi temporis experientiam, ut Isidorus dicit. Ergo neque animae separatae omnia naturalia cognoscunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0052","text":"[I q. 89 a. 3 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32200)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0053","text":"Praeterea, si anima statim cum est separata, omnia naturalia cognosceret, frustra homines studerent ad rerum scientiam capessendam. Hoc autem est inconveniens. Non ergo anima separata omnia naturalia cognoscit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0054","text":"[I q. 89 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32201)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0055","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, anima separata intelligit per species quas recipit ex influentia divini luminis, sicut et Angeli, sed tamen, quia natura animae est infra naturam Angeli, cui iste modus cognoscendi est connaturalis, anima separata per huiusmodi species non accipit perfectam rerum cognitionem, sed quasi in communi et confusam. Sicut igitur se habent Angeli ad perfectam cognitionem rerum naturalium per huiusmodi species, ita animae separatae ad imperfectam et confusam. Angeli autem per huiusmodi species cognoscunt cognitione perfecta omnia naturalia, quia omnia quae Deus fecit in propriis naturis, fecit in intelligentia angelica, ut dicit Augustinus, super Gen. ad Litt. Unde et animae separatae de omnibus naturalibus cognitionem habent, non certam et propriam, sed communem et confusam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0056","text":"[I q. 89 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32202)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0057","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod nec ipse Angelus per suam substantiam cognoscit omnia naturalia, sed per species quasdam, ut supra dictum est. Et ideo non propter hoc sequitur quod anima cognoscat omnia naturalia, quia cognoscit quoquo modo substantiam separatam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0058","text":"[I q. 89 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32203)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0059","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut anima separata non perfecte intelligit substantias separatas ita nec omnia naturalia perfecte cognoscit, sed sub quadam confusione, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0060","text":"[I q. 89 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32204)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0061","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Isidorus loquitur de cognitione futurorum; quae nec Angeli nec Daemones nec animae separatae cognoscunt, nisi vel in suis causis, vel per revelationem divinam. Nos autem loquimur de cognitione naturalium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0062","text":"[I q. 89 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32205)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0063","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod cognitio quae acquiritur hic per studium, est propria et perfecta; illa autem est confusa. Unde non sequitur quod studium addiscendi sit frustra.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0064","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0065","text":"[I q. 89 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32206)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0066","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima separata non cognoscat singularia. Nulla enim potentia cognoscitiva remanet in anima separata nisi intellectus, ut ex supra dictis patet. Sed intellectus non est cognoscitivus singularium, ut supra habitum est. Ergo anima separata singularia non cognoscit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0067","text":"[I q. 89 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32207)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0068","text":"Praeterea, magis est determinata cognitio qua cognoscitur aliquid in singulari, quam illa qua cognoscitur aliquid in universali. Sed anima separata non habet determinatam cognitionem de speciebus rerum naturalium. Multo igitur minus cognoscit singularia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0069","text":"[I q. 89 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32208)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0070","text":"Praeterea, si cognoscit singularia, et non per sensum, pari ratione omnia singularia cognosceret. Sed non cognoscit omnia singularia. Ergo nulla cognoscit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0071","text":"[I q. 89 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32209)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0072","text":"Sed contra est quod dives in Inferno positus dixit, habeo quinque fratres, ut habetur Luc. XVI.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0073","text":"[I q. 89 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32210)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0074","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod animae separatae aliqua singularia cognoscunt, sed non omnia, etiam quae sunt praesentia. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod duplex est modus intelligendi. Unus per abstractionem a phantasmatibus, et secundum istum modum singularia per intellectum cognosci non possunt directe, sed indirecte, sicut supra dictum est. Alius modus intelligendi est per influentiam specierum a Deo, et per istum modum intellectus potest singularia cognoscere. Sicut enim ipse Deus per suam essentiam, inquantum est causa universalium et individualium principiorum, cognoscit omnia et universalia et singularia, ut supra dictum est; ita substantiae separatae per species, quae sunt quaedam participatae similitudines illius divinae essentiae, possunt singularia cognoscere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0075","text":"In hoc tamen est differentia inter Angelos et animas separatas, quia Angeli per huiusmodi species habent perfectam et propriam cognitionem de rebus, animae vero separatae confusam. Unde Angeli, propter efficaciam sui intellectus per huiusmodi species non solum naturas rerum in speciali cognoscere possunt, sed etiam singularia sub speciebus contenta. Animae vero separatae non possunt cognoscere per huiusmodi species nisi solum singularia illa ad quae quodammodo determinantur, vel per praecedentem cognitionem, vel per aliquam affectionem, vel per naturalem habitudinem, vel per divinam ordinationem, quia omne quod recipitur in aliquo, determinatur in eo secundum modum recipientis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0076","text":"[I q. 89 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32211)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0077","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod intellectus per viam abstractionis non est cognoscitivus singularium. Sic autem anima separata non intelligit, sed sicut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0078","text":"[I q. 89 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32212)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0079","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ad illarum rerum species vel individua cognitio animae separatae determinatur, ad quae anima separata habet aliquam determinatam habitudinem, sicut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0080","text":"[I q. 89 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32213)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0081","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod anima separata non se habet aequaliter ad omnia singularia, sed ad quaedam habet aliquam habitudinem quam non habet ad alia. Et ideo non est aequalis ratio ut omnia singularia cognoscat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0082","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0083","text":"[I q. 89 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32214)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0084","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod habitus scientiae hic acquisitae non remaneat in anima separata. Dicit enim apostolus, I ad Cor. XIII, scientia destruetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0085","text":"[I q. 89 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32215)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0086","text":"Praeterea, quidam minus boni in hoc mundo scientia pollent, aliis magis bonis carentibus scientia. Si ergo habitus scientiae permaneret etiam post mortem in anima, sequeretur quod aliqui minus boni etiam in futuro statu essent potiores aliquibus magis bonis. Quod videtur inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0087","text":"[I q. 89 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32216)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0088","text":"Praeterea, animae separatae habebunt scientiam per influentiam divini luminis. Si igitur scientia hic acquisita in anima separata remaneat, sequetur quod duae erunt formae unius speciei in eodem subiecto. Quod est impossibile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0089","text":"[I q. 89 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32217)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0090","text":"Praeterea, philosophus dicit, in libro Praedicament., quod *habitus est qualitas difficile mobilis; sed ab aegritudine, vel ab aliquo huiusmodi, quandoque corrumpitur scientia*. Sed nulla est ita fortis immutatio in hac vita, sicut immutatio quae est per mortem. Ergo videtur quod habitus scientiae per mortem corrumpatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0091","text":"[I q. 89 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32218)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0092","text":"Sed contra est quod Hieronymus dicit, in epistola ad Paulinum, *discamus in terris, quorum scientia nobis perseveret in caelo*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0093","text":"[I q. 89 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32219)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0094","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam posuerunt habitum scientiae non esse in ipso intellectu, sed in viribus sensitivis, scilicet imaginativa, cogitativa et memorativa; et quod species intelligibiles non conservantur in intellectu possibili. Et si haec opinio vera esset, sequeretur quod, destructo corpore, totaliter habitus scientiae hic acquisitae destrueretur. Sed quia scientia est in intellectu, qui est locus specierum, ut dicitur in III de anima; oportet quod habitus scientiae hic acquisitae partim sit in praedictis viribus sensitivis, et partim in ipso intellectu. Et hoc potest considerari ex ipsis actibus ex quibus habitus scientiae acquiritur, nam *habitus sunt similes actibus ex quibus acquiruntur*, ut dicitur in II Ethic.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0095","text":"Actus autem intellectus ex quibus in praesenti vita scientia acquiritur, sunt per conversionem intellectus ad phantasmata, quae sunt in praedictis viribus sensitivis. Unde per tales actus et ipsi intellectui possibili acquiritur facultas quaedam ad considerandum per species susceptas; et in praedictis inferioribus viribus acquiritur quaedam habilitas ut facilius per conversionem ad ipsas intellectus possit intelligibilia speculari. Sed sicut actus intellectus principaliter quidem et formaliter est in ipso intellectu, materialiter autem et dispositive in inferioribus viribus, idem etiam dicendum est de habitu. Quantum ergo ad id quod aliquis praesentis scientiae habet in inferioribus viribus, non remanebit in anima separata, sed quantum ad id quod habet in ipso intellectu, necesse est ut remaneat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0096","text":"Quia, ut dicitur in libro de longitudine et brevitate vitae, dupliciter corrumpitur aliqua forma, uno modo, per se, quando corrumpitur a suo contrario, ut calidum a frigido; alio modo, per accidens, scilicet per corruptionem subiecti. Manifestum est autem quod per corruptionem subiecti, scientia quae est in intellectu humano, corrumpi non potest, cum intellectus sit incorruptibilis, ut supra ostensum est. Similiter etiam nec per contrarium corrumpi possunt species intelligibiles quae sunt in intellectu possibili, quia intentioni intelligibili nihil est contrarium; et praecipue quantum ad simplicem intelligentiam, qua intelligitur quod quid est. Sed quantum ad operationem qua intellectus componit et dividit, vel etiam ratiocinatur, sic invenitur contrarietas in intellectu, secundum quod falsum in propositione vel in argumentatione est contrarium vero.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0097","text":"Et hoc modo interdum scientia corrumpitur per contrarium, dum scilicet aliquis per falsam argumentationem abducitur a scientia veritatis. Et ideo philosophus, in libro praedicto, ponit duos modos quibus scientia per se corrumpitur, scilicet oblivionem, ex parte memorativae, et deceptionem, ex parte argumentationis falsae. Sed hoc non habet locum in anima separata. Unde dicendum est quod habitus scientiae, secundum quod est in intellectu manet in anima separata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0098","text":"[I q. 89 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32220)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0099","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod apostolus non loquitur ibi de scientia quantum ad habitum, sed quantum ad cognitionis actum. Unde ad huius probationem inducit, nunc cognosco ex parte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0100","text":"[I q. 89 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32221)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0101","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut secundum staturam corporis aliquis minus bonus erit maior aliquo magis bono; ita nihil prohibet aliquem minus bonum habere aliquem scientiae habitum in futuro, quem non habet aliquis magis bonus. Sed tamen hoc quasi nullius momenti est in comparatione ad alias praerogativas quas meliores habebunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0102","text":"[I q. 89 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32222)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0103","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod utraque scientia non est unius rationis. Unde nullum inconveniens sequitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0104","text":"[I q. 89 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32223)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0105","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod ratio illa procedit de corruptione scientiae quantum ad id quod habet ex parte sensitivarum virium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0106","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0107","text":"[I q. 89 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32224)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0108","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod actus scientiae hic acquisitae non maneat in anima separata. Dicit enim philosophus, in I de anima, quod corrupto corpore, anima neque reminiscitur neque amat. Sed considerare ea quae prius aliquis novit, est reminisci. Ergo anima separata non potest habere actum scientiae quam hic acquisivit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0109","text":"[I q. 89 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32225)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0110","text":"Praeterea, species intelligibiles non erunt potentiores in anima separata quam sint in anima corpori unita. Sed per species intelligibiles non possumus modo intelligere, nisi convertendo nos super phantasmata, sicut supra habitum est. Ergo nec anima separata hoc poterit. Et ita nullo modo per species intelligibiles hic acquisitas anima separata intelligere poterit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0111","text":"[I q. 89 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32226)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0112","text":"Praeterea, philosophus dicit, in II Ethic., quod *habitus similes actus reddunt actibus per quos acquiruntur*. Sed habitus scientiae hic acquiritur per actus intellectus convertentis se supra phantasmata. Ergo non potest alios actus reddere. Sed tales actus non competunt animae separatae. Ergo anima separata non habebit aliquem actum scientiae hic acquisitae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0113","text":"[I q. 89 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32227)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0114","text":"Sed contra est quod Luc. XVI, dicitur ad divitem in Inferno positum, *recordare quia recepisti bona in vita tua*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0115","text":"[I q. 89 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32228)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0116","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in actu est duo considerare, scilicet speciem actus, et modum ipsius. Et species quidem actus consideratur ex obiecto in quod actus cognoscitivae virtutis dirigitur per speciem, quae est obiecti similitudo, sed modus actus pensatur ex virtute agentis. Sicut quod aliquis videat lapidem, contingit ex specie lapidis quae est in oculo, sed quod acute videat, contingit ex virtute visiva oculi. Cum igitur species intelligibiles maneant in anima separata, sicut dictum est; status autem animae separatae non sit idem sicut modo est, sequitur quod secundum species intelligibiles hic acquisitas, anima separata intelligere possit quae prius intellexit; non tamen eodem modo, scilicet per conversionem ad phantasmata, sed per modum convenientem animae separatae. Et ita manet quidem in anima separata actus scientiae hic acquisitae, sed non secundum eundem modum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0117","text":"[I q. 89 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32229)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0118","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod philosophus loquitur de reminiscentia, secundum quod memoria pertinet ad partem sensitivam, non autem secundum quod memoria est quodammodo in intellectu, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0119","text":"[I q. 89 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32230)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0120","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod diversus modus intelligendi non provenit ex diversa virtute specierum, sed ex diverso statu animae intelligentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0121","text":"[I q. 89 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32231)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0122","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod actus per quos acquiritur habitus, sunt similes actibus quos habitus causant, quantum ad speciem actus, non autem quantum ad modum agendi. Nam operari iusta, sed non iuste, idest delectabiliter, causat habitum iustitiae politicae, per quem delectabiliter operamur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0123","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0124","text":"[I q. 89 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32232)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0125","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod distantia localis impediat cognitionem animae separatae. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro de cura pro mortuis agenda, quod *animae mortuorum ibi sunt, ubi ea quae hic fiunt scire non possunt*. Sciunt autem ea quae apud eos aguntur. Ergo distantia localis impedit cognitionem animae separatae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0126","text":"[I q. 89 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32233)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0127","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, in libro de divinatione Daemonum, quod *Daemones, propter celeritatem motus, aliqua nobis ignota denuntiant*. Sed agilitas motus ad hoc nihil faceret, si distantia localis cognitionem Daemonis non impediret. Multo igitur magis distantia localis impedit cognitionem animae separatae, quae est inferior secundum naturam quam Daemon.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0128","text":"[I q. 89 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32234)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0129","text":"Praeterea, sicut distat aliquis secundum locum, ita secundum tempus sed distantia temporis impedit cognitionem animae separatae, non enim cognoscunt futura. Ergo videtur quod etiam distantia secundum locum animae separatae cognitionem impediat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0130","text":"[I q. 89 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32235)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0131","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Luc. XVI, quod *dives cum esset in tormentis, elevans oculos suos, vidit Abraham a longe*. Ergo distantia localis non impedit animae separatae cognitionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0132","text":"[I q. 89 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32236)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0133","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam posuerunt quod anima separata cognosceret singularia abstrahendo a sensibilibus. Quod si esset verum, posset dici quod distantia localis impediret animae separatae cognitionem, requireretur enim quod vel sensibilia agerent in animam separatam, vel anima separata in sensibilia; et quantum ad utrumque, requireretur distantia determinata. Sed praedicta positio est impossibilis, quia abstractio specierum a sensibilibus fit mediantibus sensibus et aliis potentiis sensitivis, quae in anima separata actu non manent. Intelligit autem anima separata singularia per influxum specierum ex divino lumine, quod quidem lumen aequaliter se habet ad propinquum et distans. Unde distantia localis nullo modo impedit animae separatae cognitionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0134","text":"[I q. 89 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32237)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0135","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus non dicit quod propter hoc quod ibi sunt animae mortuorum, ea quae hic sunt videre non possunt, ut localis distantia huius ignorantiae causa esse credatur, sed hoc potest propter aliquid aliud contingere, ut infra dicetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0136","text":"[I q. 89 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32238)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0137","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Augustinus ibi loquitur secundum opinionem illam qua aliqui posuerunt quod Daemones habent corpora naturaliter sibi unita, secundum quam positionem, etiam potentias sensitivas habere possunt, ad quarum cognitionem requiritur determinata distantia. Et hanc opinionem etiam in eodem libro Augustinus expresse tangit, licet eam magis recitando quam asserendo tangere videatur, ut patet per ea quae dicit XXI libro de Civ. Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0138","text":"[I q. 89 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32239)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0139","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod futura, quae distant secundum tempus, non sunt entia in actu. Unde in seipsis non sunt cognoscibilia, quia sicut deficit aliquid ab entitate, ita deficit a cognoscibilitate. Sed ea quae sunt distantia secundum locum, sunt entia in actu, et secundum se cognoscibilia. Unde non est eadem ratio de distantia locali, et de distantia temporis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0140","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0141","text":"[I q. 89 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32240)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0142","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod animae separatae cognoscant ea quae hic aguntur. Nisi enim ea cognoscerent, de eis curam non haberent. Sed habent curam de his quae hic aguntur; secundum illud Luc. XVI, *habeo quinque fratres, ut testificetur illis, ne et ipsi veniant in hunc locum tormentorum*. Ergo animae separatae cognoscunt ea quae hic aguntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0143","text":"[I q. 89 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32241)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0144","text":"Praeterea, frequenter mortui vivis apparent, vel dormientibus vel vigilantibus, et eos admonent de iis quae hic aguntur; sicut Samuel apparuit Sauli, ut habetur I Reg. XXVIII. Sed hoc non esset si ea quae hic sunt non cognoscerent. Ergo ea quae hic aguntur cognoscunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0145","text":"[I q. 89 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32242)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0146","text":"Praeterea, animae separatae cognoscunt ea quae apud eas aguntur. Si ergo quae apud nos aguntur non cognoscerent, impediretur earum cognitio per localem distantiam. Quod supra negatum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0147","text":"[I q. 89 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32243)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0148","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Iob XIV, *sive fuerint filii eius nobiles, sive ignobiles, non intelliget*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0149","text":"[I q. 89 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32244)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0150","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, secundum naturalem cognitionem, de qua nunc hic agitur, animae mortuorum nesciunt quae hic aguntur. Et huius ratio ex dictis accipi potest. Quia anima separata cognoscit singularia per hoc quod quodammodo determinata est ad illa, vel per vestigium alicuius praecedentis cognitionis seu affectionis, vel per ordinationem divinam. Animae autem mortuorum, secundum ordinationem divinam, et secundum modum essendi, segregatae sunt a conversatione viventium, et coniunctae conversationi spiritualium substantiarum quae sunt a corpore separatae. Unde ea quae apud nos aguntur ignorant. Et hanc rationem assignat Gregorius in XII Moralium, dicens, *mortui vita in carne viventium post eos, qualiter disponatur, nesciunt, quia vita spiritus longe est a vita carnis; et sicut corporea atque incorporea diversa sunt genere, ita sunt distincta cognitione*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0151","text":"Et hoc etiam Augustinus videtur tangere in libro de cura pro mortuis agenda, dicens quod *animae mortuorum rebus viventium non intersunt*. Sed quantum ad animas beatorum, videtur esse differentia inter Gregorium et Augustinum. Nam Gregorius ibidem subdit, *quod tamen de animabus sanctis sentiendum non est, quia quae intus omnipotentis Dei claritatem vident, nullo modo credendum est quod sit foris aliquid quod ignorent*. Augustinus vero, in libro de cura pro mortuis agenda, expresse dicit quod *nesciunt mortui, etiam sancti, quid agant vivi et eorum filii*, ut habetur in Glossa, super illud, *Abraham nescivit nos*, Isaiae LXIII. Quod quidem confirmat per hoc quod a matre sua non visitabatur, nec in tristitiis consolabatur, sicut quando vivebat; nec est probabile ut sit facta vita feliciore crudelior.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0152","text":"Et per hoc quod dominus promisit Iosiae regi quod prius moreretur ne videret mala quae erant populo superventura, ut habetur IV Reg. XXII. Sed Augustinus hoc dubitando dicit, unde praemittit, *ut volet, accipiat quisque quod dicam*. Gregorius autem assertive, quod patet per hoc quod dicit, *nullo modo credendum est*. Magis tamen videtur, secundum sententiam Gregorii, quod animae sanctorum Deum videntes, omnia praesentia quae hic aguntur cognoscant. Sunt enim Angelis aequales, de quibus etiam Augustinus asserit quod ea quae apud vivos aguntur non ignorant. Sed quia sanctorum animae sunt perfectissime iustitiae divinae coniunctae, nec tristantur, nec rebus viventium se ingerunt, nisi secundum quod iustitiae divinae dispositio exigit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0153","text":"[I q. 89 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32245)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0154","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod animae mortuorum possunt habere curam de rebus viventium, etiam si ignorent eorum statum; sicut nos curam habemus de mortuis, eis suffragia impendendo, quamvis eorum statum ignoremus. Possunt etiam facta viventium non per seipsos cognoscere, sed vel per animas eorum qui hinc ad eos accedunt; vel per Angelos seu Daemones; vel etiam spiritu Dei revelante, sicut Augustinus in eodem libro dicit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0155","text":"[I q. 89 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32246)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0156","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod hoc quod mortui viventibus apparent qualitercumque, vel contingit per specialem Dei dispensationem, ut animae mortuorum rebus viventium intersint, et est inter divina miracula computandum. Vel huiusmodi apparitiones fiunt per operationes Angelorum bonorum vel malorum, etiam ignorantibus mortuis, sicut etiam vivi ignorantes aliis viventibus apparent in somnis, ut Augustinus dicit in libro praedicto. Unde et de Samuele dici potest quod ipse apparuit per revelationem divinam; secundum hoc quod dicitur Eccli. XLVI, quod *dormivit, et notum fecit regi finem vitae suae*. Vel illa apparitio fuit procurata per Daemones, si tamen Ecclesiastici auctoritas non recipiatur, propter hoc quod inter canonicas Scripturas apud Hebraeos non habetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0157","text":"[I q. 89 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32247)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0158","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ignorantia huiusmodi non contingit ex locali distantia, sed propter causam praedictam.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"cf3ce2715662da2b3ef1920424effe81e0eafe3aea08ed8a96a6658c7281ca77","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T152256394555Z_2f9ba7e2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 89","source_sha256":"cf3ce2715662da2b3ef1920424effe81e0eafe3aea08ed8a96a6658c7281ca77","sha256":"82cb577e5420216369641a4ad7a787d2982dd4cf9f6a113b617a755243c56453","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:22:56.394600+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0002","text":"[I q. 89 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32180)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0003","text":"Next, the cognition of the separated soul must be considered. And concerning this, eight questions are asked. First, whether a soul separated from the body can understand. Second, whether it understands separate substances. Third, whether it understands all natural things. Fourth, whether it knows singulars. Fifth, whether the habit of knowledge acquired here remains in the separated soul. Sixth, whether it can use the habit of knowledge acquired here. Seventh, whether local distance impedes the cognition of the separated soul. Eighth, whether souls separated from bodies know the things that are done here.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0005","text":"[I q. 89 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32181)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that a separated soul cannot understand anything at all. For the Philosopher says, in *On the Soul* I, that *understanding is corrupted when something internal is corrupted*. But all the internal things of a human being are corrupted through death. Therefore understanding itself too is corrupted.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0007","text":"[I q. 89 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32182)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0008","text":"Further, the human soul is impeded from understanding by the binding of sensation, and when imagination is disturbed, as was said above. But by death both sensation and imagination are totally corrupted, as is clear from what was said above. Therefore after death the soul understands nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0009","text":"[I q. 89 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32183)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0010","text":"Further, if the separated soul understands, it must understand through certain species. But it does not understand through innate species, because at the beginning it is like a tablet on which nothing is written. Nor through species which it abstracts from things, because it does not have the organs of sensation and imagination, through whose mediation intelligible species are abstracted from things. Nor even through species previously abstracted and preserved in the soul, because in that case the soul of a child would understand nothing after death. Nor again through intelligible species divinely infused, for this cognition would not be natural, which is what is now under discussion, but of grace. Therefore the soul separated from the body understands nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0011","text":"[I q. 89 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32184)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0012","text":"On the contrary, the Philosopher says, in *On the Soul* I, that *if none of the soul’s operations is proper to it, it cannot be separated*. But it can be separated. Therefore it has some proper operation; and especially that which is understanding. Therefore it understands while existing without a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0013","text":"[I q. 89 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32185)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0014","text":"I answer that this question has a difficulty from the fact that the soul, so long as it is joined to the body, cannot understand anything except by turning itself to phantasms, as is evident through experience. But if this does not belong to the nature of the soul, but belongs to it accidentally from its being bound to the body, as the Platonists maintained, the question could easily be solved. For once the impediment of the body were removed, the soul would return to its nature, so as to understand intelligibles simply, without turning itself to phantasms, just as is the case with the other separate substances. But according to this, the soul would not be united to the body for the sake of the soul’s greater good, if it understood worse when united to the body than when separated; rather, this would be only for the sake of the body’s greater good—which is unreasonable, since matter is for the sake of form, and not conversely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0015","text":"But if we posit that the soul has from its nature that it understands by turning itself to phantasms, since the nature of the soul is not changed through the death of the body, it seems that the soul can naturally understand nothing, since phantasms to which it might turn are not at hand for it. And therefore, in order to remove this difficulty, it must be considered that, since nothing operates except insofar as it is in act, the mode of operation of each thing follows its mode of being. Now the soul has one mode of being when it is united to the body, and another when it has been separated from the body, while the same nature of the soul remains; not in such a way that being united to the body is accidental to it, but it is united to the body by the account of its nature; just as the nature of something light is not changed when it is in its proper place, which is natural to it, and when it is outside its proper place, which is contrary to its nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0016","text":"Therefore, according to that mode of being by which it is united to the body, there belongs to the soul a mode of understanding by turning itself to the phantasms of bodies, which are in bodily organs; but when it has been separated from the body, there belongs to it a mode of understanding by turning itself to things that are intelligible simply, as also belongs to the other separate substances. Hence the mode of understanding by turning itself to phantasms is natural to the soul, just as being united to the body is; but being separated from the body is outside the account of its nature, and likewise understanding without turning itself to phantasms is contrary to its nature. And therefore it is united to the body in order that it may be and operate according to its nature. But this in turn involves a difficulty.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0017","text":"For since nature is always ordered to what is better, and the mode of understanding by turning itself to intelligibles simply is better than the mode of understanding by turning itself to phantasms, the nature of the soul ought to have been so established by God that the more noble mode of understanding would be natural to it, and that it would not need to be united to the body for this purpose. Therefore it must be considered that, although understanding by turning itself to higher things is simply more noble than understanding by turning itself to phantasms, nevertheless that mode of understanding, insofar as it was possible for the soul, was more imperfect. This is clear as follows. For in all intellectual substances an intellectual power is found through the influence of the divine light. This light is one and simple indeed in the first principle; and the more intellectual creatures are distant from the first principle, the more that light is divided and diversified, as happens in lines proceeding from a center.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0018","text":"And hence it is that God understands all things through his one essence; but the higher intellectual substances, although they understand through several forms, nevertheless understand through fewer, more universal forms, and forms more powerful for the comprehension of things, on account of the efficacy of the intellectual power that is in them. But in the lower substances the forms are more numerous, less universal, and less effective for the comprehension of things, insofar as they fall short of the intellectual power of the higher substances. If, therefore, the lower substances had forms in that universality in which the higher substances have them, because they are not of such efficacy in understanding, they would not receive through them perfect cognition of things, but only in a certain commonness and confusion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0019","text":"This is apparent to some extent in human beings, for those who are of weaker intellect do not receive perfect cognition through the universal conceptions of those who understand more, unless individual matters are explained to them specifically. Now it is clear that, among intellectual substances, human souls are lowest according to the order of nature. But the perfection of the universe required that there be diverse grades in things. If, therefore, human souls had been so established by God that they understood in the manner that belongs to separate substances, they would not have perfect cognition, but a confused cognition in common. Therefore, in order that they might be able to have perfect and proper cognition of things, they are naturally so established that they are united to bodies, and thus receive from sensible things themselves proper cognition concerning them; just as unlearned people cannot be led to knowledge except through sensible examples.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0020","text":"Thus, therefore, it is clear that it is for the soul’s greater good that it be united to the body and understand by turning itself to phantasms; and yet it can be separated and have another mode of understanding.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0021","text":"[I q. 89 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32186)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0022","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that, if the words of the Philosopher are carefully examined, the Philosopher said this on the basis of a certain supposition previously made, namely, that understanding is a certain movement of the composite, just as sensing is; for he had not yet shown the difference between intellect and sense. Or it may be said that he speaks of that mode of understanding which is through turning itself to phantasms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0023","text":"[I q. 89 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32187)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0024","text":"The second argument also proceeds concerning this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0025","text":"[I q. 89 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32188)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0026","text":"To the third, it must be said that the separated soul does not understand through innate species; nor through species which it then abstracts; nor only through conserved species, as the objection proves, but through species participated from the influx of the divine light, of which the soul becomes a participant just as the other separated substances do, although in an inferior mode. Hence, as soon as its turning toward the body ceases, it turns toward higher things. Yet on this account its knowledge is not unnatural, because God is the author not only of the influx of gratuitous light, but also of natural light.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0027","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0028","text":"[I q. 89 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32189)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0029","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the separated soul does not understand separated substances. For the soul united to the body is more perfect than when separated from the body, since the soul is naturally a part of human nature; but every part is more perfect in its whole. But the soul united to the body does not understand separated substances, as has been established above. Therefore, much less does it do so when it has been separated from the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0030","text":"[I q. 89 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32190)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0031","text":"Further, everything that is known is known either through its presence or through its species. But separated substances cannot be known by the soul through their presence, because nothing enters into the soul except God alone. Nor can they be known through any species which the soul could abstract from an angel, because an angel is simpler than the soul. Therefore, in no way can the separated soul know separated substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0032","text":"[I q. 89 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32191)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0033","text":"Further, certain philosophers have held that man's ultimate happiness consists in the knowledge of separated substances. Therefore, if the separated soul can understand separated substances, it attains happiness from its separation alone. This is unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0034","text":"[I q. 89 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32192)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0035","text":"But on the contrary, separated souls know other separated souls; just as the rich man, placed in Hell, saw Lazarus and Abraham, Luke XVI. Therefore separated souls also see demons and angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0036","text":"[I q. 89 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32193)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0037","text":"I answer that, as Augustine says in IX *On the Trinity*, *our mind receives knowledge of incorporeal things through itself*, that is, by knowing itself, as was said above. Therefore, from the fact that the separated soul knows itself, we can gather how it knows other separated substances. Now it has been said that, so long as the soul is united to the body, it understands by turning itself toward phantasms. And therefore it cannot understand even itself except insofar as it is made actually understanding through a species abstracted from phantasms; for thus through its act it understands itself, as was said above. But when it has been separated from the body, it will understand not by turning itself toward phantasms, but toward those things which are intelligible in themselves; hence it will understand itself through itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0038","text":"But it is common to every separated substance that it understands what is above it and what is below it according to the mode of its substance; for something is understood according as it is in the one understanding, while something is in another according to the mode of that in which it is. Now the mode of the substance of the separated soul is below the mode of the angelic substance, but it is conformed to the mode of other separated souls. And therefore it has perfect knowledge of other separated souls; but of angels, imperfect and deficient knowledge, speaking of the natural knowledge of the separated soul. But concerning knowledge of glory, the account is otherwise.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0039","text":"[I q. 89 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32194)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0040","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the separated soul is indeed more imperfect, if the nature which it shares with the nature of the body is considered; yet in a certain way it is freer for understanding, insofar as it is impeded from the purity of understanding by the weight and occupation of the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0041","text":"[I q. 89 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32195)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0042","text":"To the second, it must be said that the separated soul understands angels through likenesses divinely impressed. Yet these fall short of perfectly representing them, because the nature of the soul is inferior to that of an angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0043","text":"[I q. 89 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32196)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0044","text":"To the third, it must be said that man's ultimate happiness consists not in the knowledge of just any separated substances, but of God alone, who cannot be seen except through grace. Indeed, in the knowledge of other separated substances there is great happiness, though not ultimate happiness, provided that they are understood perfectly. But the separated soul does not understand them perfectly by natural knowledge, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0045","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0046","text":"[I q. 89 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32197)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0047","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that the separated soul knows all natural things. For in separated substances are the intelligible accounts of all natural things. But separated souls know separated substances. Therefore, they know all natural things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0048","text":"[I q. 89 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32198)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0049","text":"Furthermore, one who understands what is more intelligible can understand, much more, what is less intelligible. But the separated soul understands separated substances, which are the most intelligible of things. Therefore, much more can it understand all natural things, which are less intelligible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0050","text":"[I q. 89 a. 3 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32199)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0051","text":"But on the contrary, natural knowledge is more vigorous in demons than in the separated soul. But demons do not know all natural things; rather, they learn many things through the experience of a long time, as Isidore says. Therefore neither do separated souls know all natural things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0052","text":"[I q. 89 a. 3 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32200)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0053","text":"Furthermore, if the soul, as soon as it is separated, knew all natural things, men would study in vain to acquire knowledge of things. But this is unfitting. Therefore the separated soul does not know all natural things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0054","text":"[I q. 89 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32201)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0055","text":"I answer that it must be said that, as was said above, the separated soul understands through species which it receives from the influx of the divine light, as do also the angels; yet, because the nature of the soul is below the nature of an angel, to whom this mode of knowing is connatural, the separated soul does not receive through such species a perfect knowledge of things, but one that is, as it were, general and confused. Therefore, as angels are related to the perfect knowledge of natural things through such species, so separated souls are related to imperfect and confused knowledge. But angels through such species know all natural things with perfect knowledge, because all that God made in their proper natures, He made in the angelic intelligence, as Augustine says, On the Literal Meaning of Genesis. Hence separated souls also have knowledge of all natural things, not certain and proper, but general and confused.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0056","text":"[I q. 89 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32202)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0057","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that not even an angel himself knows all natural things through his substance, but through certain species, as was said above. And therefore it does not follow from this that the soul knows all natural things because it knows in some way a separated substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0058","text":"[I q. 89 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32203)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0059","text":"To the second, it must be said that, just as the separated soul does not perfectly understand separated substances, so neither does it perfectly know all natural things, but under a certain confusion, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0060","text":"[I q. 89 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32204)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0061","text":"To the third, it must be said that Isidore speaks of knowledge of future things, which neither angels nor demons nor separated souls know, except either in their causes or through divine revelation. But we are speaking of knowledge of natural things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0062","text":"[I q. 89 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32205)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0063","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the knowledge acquired here through study is proper and perfect; but that other is confused. Hence it does not follow that the effort of learning is in vain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0064","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0065","text":"[I q. 89 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32206)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0066","text":"We proceed to the fourth as follows. It seems that the separated soul does not know singulars. For no cognitive power remains in the separated soul except the intellect, as is clear from what has been said above. But the intellect is not cognizant of singulars, as has been maintained above. Therefore the separated soul does not know singulars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0067","text":"[I q. 89 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32207)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0068","text":"Furthermore, the knowledge by which something is known as singular is more determinate than that by which something is known as universal. But the separated soul does not have determinate knowledge of the species of natural things. Therefore, much less does it know singulars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0069","text":"[I q. 89 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32208)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0070","text":"Furthermore, if it knows singulars, and not through sense, by the same reasoning it would know all singulars. But it does not know all singulars. Therefore it knows none.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0071","text":"[I q. 89 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32209)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0072","text":"But on the contrary, the rich man, placed in Hell, said: I have five brothers, as is found in Luke XVI.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0073","text":"[I q. 89 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32210)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0074","text":"I answer that separated souls know certain singulars, but not all, even those that are present. To make this evident, it must be considered that there is a twofold mode of understanding. One is through abstraction from phantasms, and according to this mode singulars cannot be known directly by the intellect, but indirectly, as was said above. The other mode of understanding is through the influx of species from God, and according to this mode the intellect can know singulars. For just as God Himself, through His essence, insofar as He is the cause of universal and individual principles, knows all things, both universals and singulars, as was said above; so separated substances, through species that are certain participated likenesses of that divine essence, can know singulars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0075","text":"Yet in this there is a difference between angels and separated souls: namely, that angels through such species have perfect and proper knowledge of things, whereas separated souls have confused knowledge. Hence angels, because of the efficacy of their intellect, can through such species know not only the natures of things specifically, but also the singulars contained under the species. Separated souls, however, cannot know through such species except only those singulars toward which they are in some way determined, whether through preceding knowledge, or through some affection, or through a natural relation, or through divine ordination; because everything received in something is determined in it according to the mode of the recipient.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0076","text":"[I q. 89 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32211)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0077","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the intellect does not know singulars by way of abstraction. But the separated soul does not understand in this way, but as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0078","text":"[I q. 89 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32212)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0079","text":"To the second, it must be said that the knowledge of the separated soul is determined to the species or individuals of those things toward which the separated soul has some determinate relation, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0080","text":"[I q. 89 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32213)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0081","text":"To the third, it must be said that the separated soul is not related equally to all singulars, but has some relation to certain ones that it does not have to others. And therefore there is not an equal reason for it to know all singulars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0082","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0083","text":"[I q. 89 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32214)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0084","text":"The fifth point is approached thus. It seems that the habit of knowledge acquired here does not remain in the separated soul. For the Apostle says, in I Corinthians XIII, “knowledge shall be destroyed.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0085","text":"[I q. 89 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32215)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0086","text":"Further, certain less good persons in this world abound in knowledge, while others who are better lack knowledge. Therefore, if the habit of knowledge were to remain in the soul even after death, it would follow that some less good persons would be more eminent than some better persons even in the future state. This seems unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0087","text":"[I q. 89 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32216)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0088","text":"Further, separated souls will have knowledge through the influx of divine light. Therefore, if knowledge acquired here remains in the separated soul, it will follow that there are two forms of one species in the same subject. This is impossible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0089","text":"[I q. 89 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32217)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0090","text":"Further, the Philosopher says, in the book of the Categories, that *a habit is a quality difficult to change; but knowledge is sometimes destroyed by illness, or something of this kind*. But no change in this life is as great as the change that occurs through death. Therefore it seems that the habit of knowledge is destroyed by death.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0091","text":"[I q. 89 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32218)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0092","text":"But to the contrary, Jerome says, in the letter to Paulinus, *let us learn on earth those things the knowledge of which may endure for us in heaven*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0093","text":"[I q. 89 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32219)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0094","text":"I answer that some have held that the habit of knowledge is not in the intellect itself, but in the sensitive powers, namely, the imaginative, cogitative, and memorative powers; and that intelligible species are not preserved in the possible intellect. And if this opinion were true, it would follow that, once the body was destroyed, the habit of knowledge acquired here would be wholly destroyed. But because knowledge is in the intellect, which is the place of species, as is said in *On the Soul* III, the habit of knowledge acquired here must be partly in the aforesaid sensitive powers and partly in the intellect itself. This can be considered from the very acts by which the habit of knowledge is acquired; for *habits are like the acts from which they are acquired*, as is said in the Ethics II.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0095","text":"Now the acts of intellect by which knowledge is acquired in the present life occur through the conversion of the intellect toward the phantasms that are in the aforesaid sensitive powers. Hence, through such acts, the possible intellect itself acquires a certain facility for considering through the species received; and in the aforesaid lower powers a certain aptitude is acquired, so that through conversion toward them the intellect may more easily contemplate intelligibles. But just as the act of intellect is principally and formally in the intellect itself, while materially and dispositively it is in the lower powers, so the same must also be said of the habit. Therefore, as regards that which someone has of present knowledge in the lower powers, it will not remain in the separated soul; but as regards that which he has in the intellect itself, it must remain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0096","text":"For, as is said in the book *On Length and Shortness of Life*, a form is corrupted in two ways: in one way, through itself, when it is corrupted by its contrary, as heat by cold; in another way, accidentally, namely through the corruption of its subject. Now it is evident that knowledge which is in the human intellect cannot be corrupted through the corruption of its subject, since the intellect is incorruptible, as was shown above. Likewise, the intelligible species that are in the possible intellect cannot be corrupted by a contrary, because nothing is contrary to the intelligible intention; and this is especially so as regards simple understanding, by which what a thing is is understood. But as regards the operation by which the intellect composes and divides, or also reasons, contrariety is found in the intellect, insofar as the false in a proposition or in an argumentation is contrary to the true.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0097","text":"And in this way knowledge is sometimes corrupted by its contrary, namely when someone is led away from knowledge of the truth through false argumentation. Therefore the Philosopher, in the aforesaid book, posits two ways in which knowledge is corrupted through itself: namely, forgetfulness, on the part of the memorative power, and deception, on the part of false argumentation. But this has no place in the separated soul. Hence it must be said that the habit of knowledge, insofar as it is in the intellect, remains in the separated soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0098","text":"[I q. 89 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32220)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0099","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that the Apostle does not speak there of knowledge as regards the habit, but as regards the act of cognition. Hence, to prove this, he adduces: now I know in part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0100","text":"[I q. 89 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32221)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0101","text":"To the second it must be said that, just as according to bodily stature someone less good will be greater than someone better, so nothing prevents someone less good from having in the future a habit of knowledge which someone better does not have. Yet this is, as it were, of no importance in comparison with the other prerogatives which the better will have.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0102","text":"[I q. 89 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32222)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0103","text":"To the third it must be said that the two kinds of knowledge are not of one and the same character. Hence no incongruity follows.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0104","text":"[I q. 89 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32223)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0105","text":"To the fourth it must be said that that argument proceeds concerning the corruption of knowledge as regards that which it has on the part of the sensitive powers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0106","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0107","text":"[I q. 89 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32224)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0108","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth. It seems that the act of knowledge acquired here does not remain in the separated soul. For the Philosopher says, in *On the Soul* I, that when the body is corrupted, the soul neither recollects nor loves. But to consider those things which one previously knew is to recollect. Therefore the separated soul cannot have an act of knowledge which it acquired here.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0109","text":"[I q. 89 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32225)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0110","text":"Moreover, intelligible species will not be more powerful in the separated soul than they are in the soul united to the body. But through intelligible species we cannot now understand except by turning ourselves toward phantasms, as was established above. Therefore neither will the separated soul be able to do this. And thus the separated soul will in no way be able to understand through intelligible species acquired here.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0111","text":"[I q. 89 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32226)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0112","text":"Moreover, the Philosopher says, in *Ethics* II, that *similar habits produce acts similar to the acts through which they are acquired*. But the habit of knowledge is acquired here through acts of the intellect turning itself toward phantasms. Therefore it cannot produce other acts. But such acts do not belong to the separated soul. Therefore the separated soul will not have any act of knowledge acquired here.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0113","text":"[I q. 89 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32227)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0114","text":"But on the contrary, in Luke XVI it is said to the rich man placed in Hell: *remember that you received good things in your lifetime*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0115","text":"[I q. 89 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32228)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0116","text":"I answer that it must be said that in an act two things are to be considered: namely, the species of the act and its mode. The species of the act is considered from the object toward which the act of the cognitive power is directed through the species, which is a likeness of the object; but the mode of the act is weighed from the power of the agent. Thus, that someone sees a stone comes about from the species of the stone which is in the eye, but that he sees sharply comes about from the visual power of the eye. Therefore, since intelligible species remain in the separated soul, as has been said, whereas the state of the separated soul is not the same as it is now, it follows that, according to the intelligible species acquired here, the separated soul can understand those things which it understood before; yet not in the same mode, namely by turning toward phantasms, but in a mode suited to the separated soul. And thus the act of knowledge acquired here does indeed remain in the separated soul, but not according to the same mode.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0117","text":"[I q. 89 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32229)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0118","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that the Philosopher speaks of recollection insofar as memory pertains to the sensitive part, but not insofar as memory is in some way in the intellect, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0119","text":"[I q. 89 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32230)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0120","text":"To the second it must be said that the diverse mode of understanding does not arise from a diverse power of the species, but from the diverse state of the understanding soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0121","text":"[I q. 89 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32231)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0122","text":"To the third, it must be said that the acts through which a habit is acquired are similar to the acts that habits cause, as regards the species of the act, but not as regards the mode of acting. For performing just things, but not justly—that is, delightfully—causes the habit of political justice, through which we act delightfully.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0123","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0124","text":"[I q. 89 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32232)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0125","text":"The seventh point is approached thus. It seems that local distance impedes the cognition of the separated soul. For Augustine says, in the book *On Care for the Dead*, that *the souls of the dead are there, where they cannot know what is done here*. But they know what is done among them. Therefore local distance impedes the cognition of the separated soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0126","text":"[I q. 89 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32233)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0127","text":"Further, Augustine says, in the book *On the Divination of Demons*, that *demons announce to us certain things unknown to us because of the swiftness of their motion*. But swiftness of motion would contribute nothing to this if local distance did not impede a demon's cognition. Therefore, much more does local distance impede the cognition of the separated soul, which is inferior by nature to a demon.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0128","text":"[I q. 89 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32234)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0129","text":"Further, just as someone is distant in place, so also in time. But distance in time impedes the cognition of the separated soul, for they do not know future things. Therefore it seems that distance in place also impedes the cognition of the separated soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0130","text":"[I q. 89 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32235)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0131","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Luke XVI that *when the rich man was in torments, lifting up his eyes, he saw Abraham from afar*. Therefore local distance does not impede the cognition of the separated soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0132","text":"[I q. 89 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32236)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0133","text":"I answer that it must be said that some have held that the separated soul knows singulars by abstracting from sensibles. If this were true, it could be said that local distance impedes the cognition of the separated soul; for it would be required either that sensibles act upon the separated soul, or that the separated soul act upon sensibles; and with respect to either, a determinate distance would be required. But the aforesaid position is impossible, because the abstraction of species from sensibles takes place by means of the senses and other sensitive powers, which do not remain actually in the separated soul. But the separated soul understands singulars through an influx of species from the divine light, and this light is related equally to what is near and what is distant. Hence local distance in no way impedes the cognition of the separated soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0134","text":"[I q. 89 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32237)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0135","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that Augustine does not say that, because the souls of the dead are there, they cannot see the things that are here, as though local distance were to be believed the cause of this ignorance; rather, this can happen for some other reason, as will be said below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0136","text":"[I q. 89 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32238)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0137","text":"To the second it must be said that Augustine there speaks according to the opinion by which some held that demons have bodies naturally united to them, according to which position they can also have sensitive powers, for whose cognition a determinate distance is required. And Augustine also expressly touches upon this opinion in the same book, although he seems to mention it more by recounting it than by asserting it, as is clear from what he says in Book XXI of *The City of God*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0138","text":"[I q. 89 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32239)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0139","text":"To the third it must be said that future things, which are distant in time, are not beings in act. Hence they are not knowable in themselves, because as something falls short of being, so it falls short of knowability. But things distant in place are beings in act, and knowable in themselves. Hence the account is not the same for local distance and for distance in time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0140","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0141","text":"[I q. 89 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32240)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0142","text":"The eighth point is approached thus. It seems that separated souls know the things that are done here. For unless they knew them, they would not have care for them. But they have care for the things that are done here, according to Luke XVI: *I have five brothers, that he may testify to them, lest they too come into this place of torments*. Therefore separated souls know the things that are done here.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0143","text":"[I q. 89 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32241)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0144","text":"Further, the dead frequently appear to the living, whether sleeping or waking, and warn them about the things that are done here; as Samuel appeared to Saul, as is related in I Kings XXVIII. But this would not be so if they did not know the things that are here. Therefore they know the things that are done here.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0145","text":"[I q. 89 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32242)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0146","text":"Moreover, separated souls know those things which take place among them. Therefore, if they did not know those things which take place among us, their knowledge would be impeded by local distance. This was denied above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0147","text":"[I q. 89 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32243)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0148","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Job XIV: *whether his children be noble or ignoble, he shall not understand*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0149","text":"[I q. 89 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32244)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0150","text":"I answer that it must be said that, according to natural knowledge, which is what is now under discussion here, the souls of the dead do not know what takes place here. And the reason for this can be gathered from what has been said. For a separated soul knows singulars insofar as it is in some way determined to them, either through the trace of some preceding knowledge or affection, or through divine ordering. But the souls of the dead, according to divine ordering and according to their mode of being, are separated from the society of the living and joined to the society of spiritual substances that are separated from the body. Hence they are ignorant of what takes place among us. And Gregory assigns this reason in Book XII of the *Moralia*, saying: *the dead do not know how the life of those living in the flesh after them is ordered, because the life of the spirit is far from the life of the flesh; and just as corporeal and incorporeal things differ in genus, so they are distinct in knowledge*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0151","text":"And Augustine also seems to touch upon this in the book *On Care for the Dead*, saying that *the souls of the dead do not take part in the affairs of the living*. But with respect to the souls of the blessed, there seems to be a difference between Gregory and Augustine. For Gregory adds in the same place: *yet this is not to be thought concerning the souls of the saints, because it is in no way to be believed that those who see within the brightness of almighty God should be ignorant of anything that is outside*. Augustine, however, in the book *On Care for the Dead*, expressly says that *the dead, even the saints, do not know what the living and their children do*, as is found in the Gloss on the words, *Abraham did not know us*, Isaiah LXIII. He confirms this by the fact that he was not visited by his mother, nor comforted in his sorrows, as when she was alive; nor is it probable that she became more cruel through having become happier in life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0152","text":"And by the fact that the Lord promised King Josiah that he would die beforehand, lest he see the evils that were to come upon the people, as is found in IV Kings XXII. But Augustine says this doubtfully; hence he prefaces it: *let each one take what I shall say as he will*. Gregory, however, says it assertively, as is evident from his saying: *it is in no way to be believed*. Nevertheless, according to Gregory's opinion, it seems more likely that the souls of the saints, seeing God, know all present things that take place here. For they are equal to the angels, concerning whom Augustine also affirms that they are not ignorant of what takes place among the living. But because the souls of the saints are most perfectly joined to divine justice, they neither grieve nor concern themselves with the affairs of the living, except insofar as the disposition of divine justice requires.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0153","text":"[I q. 89 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32245)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0154","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the souls of the dead can have care for the affairs of the living, even if they are ignorant of their condition; just as we have care for the dead by offering suffrages for them, although we are ignorant of their condition. They can also know the deeds of the living not by themselves, but either through the souls of those who go from here to them; or through angels or demons; or even by God's spirit revealing them, as Augustine says in the same book.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0155","text":"[I q. 89 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32246)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0156","text":"To the second, it must be said that the fact that the dead appear to the living in any manner either occurs through a special divine dispensation, so that the souls of the dead take part in the affairs of the living, and this is to be counted among divine miracles; or such apparitions occur through the operations of good or evil angels, even without the dead knowing, just as living persons who are unaware also appear in dreams to other living persons, as Augustine says in the aforesaid book. Hence it can also be said concerning Samuel that he himself appeared through divine revelation, according to what is said in Ecclesiasticus XLVI, that *he slept, and made known to the king the end of his life*. Or that apparition was procured by demons, if indeed the authority of Ecclesiasticus is not received, on account of the fact that it is not held among the canonical Scriptures by the Hebrews.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0157","text":"[I q. 89 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1084.html#32247)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0158","text":"To the third, it must be said that ignorance of this sort does not occur from local distance, but on account of the aforesaid cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_81:P0158"]}],"source_digest":"0a50607c4b53b89a61b2e5c88d20a11a3bb77f873638d5cb69ae5c32835e8de0","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 89"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 89","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82","title":"First Part · Question 90","order":91,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_82","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 90 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32248)\n\nPost praemissa considerandum est de prima hominis productione. Et circa hoc consideranda sunt quatuor, primo considerandum est de productione ipsius hominis; secundo, de fine productionis; tertio, de statu et conditione hominis primo producti; quarto, de loco eius. Circa productionem autem consideranda sunt tria, primo, de productione hominis quantum ad animam; secundo, quantum ad corpus viri; tertio, quantum ad productionem mulieris. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum anima humana sit aliquid factum, vel sit de substantia ipsius Dei. Secundo, supposito quod sit facta, utrum sit creata. Tertio, utrum sit facta mediantibus Angelis. Quarto, utrum sit facta ante corpus.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 90 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32249)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima non sit facta, sed sit de substantia Dei. Dicitur enim Gen. II, *formavit Deus hominem de limo terrae, et inspiravit in faciem eius spiraculum vitae, et factus est homo in animam viventem*. Sed ille qui spirat, aliquid a se emittit. Ergo anima qua homo vivit, est aliquid de substantia Dei.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32250)\n\nPraeterea, sicut supra habitum est, anima est forma simplex. Sed forma est actus. Ergo anima est actus purus, quod est solius Dei. Ergo anima est de substantia Dei.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32251)\n\nPraeterea, quaecumque sunt, et nullo modo differunt, sunt idem. Sed Deus et mens sunt, et nullo modo differunt, quia oporteret quod aliquibus differentiis differrent, et sic essent composita. Ergo Deus et mens humana idem sunt.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32252)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus, in libro de origine animae, enumerat quaedam quae dicit esse multum aperteque perversa, et fidei Catholicae adversa; inter quae primum est, quod quidam dixerunt *Deum animam non de nihilo, sed de seipso fecisse*.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32253)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod dicere animam esse de substantia Dei, manifestam improbabilitatem continet. Ut enim ex dictis patet, anima humana est quandoque intelligens in potentia, et scientiam quodammodo a rebus acquirit, et habet diversas potentias, quae omnia aliena sunt a Dei natura, qui est actus purus, et nihil ab alio accipiens, et nullam in se diversitatem habens, ut supra probatum est. Sed hic error principium habuisse videtur ex duabus positionibus antiquorum. Primi enim qui naturas rerum considerare incoeperunt, imaginationem transcendere non valentes, nihil praeter corpora esse posuerunt. Et ideo Deum dicebant esse quoddam corpus, quod aliorum corporum iudicabant esse principium. Et quia animam ponebant esse de natura illius corporis quod dicebant esse principium, ut dicitur in I de anima, per consequens sequebatur quod anima esset de natura Dei. Iuxta quam positionem etiam Manichaei, Deum esse quandam lucem corpoream existimantes, quandam partem illius lucis animam esse posuerunt corpori alligatam. Secundo vero processum fuit ad hoc, quod aliqui aliquid incorporeum esse apprehenderunt, non tamen a corpore separatum, sed corporis formam. Unde et Varro dixit quod *Deus est anima mundum motu et ratione gubernans*; ut Augustinus narrat, VII de Civ. Dei. Sic igitur illius totalis animae partem aliqui posuerunt animam hominis, sicut homo est pars totius mundi; non valentes intellectu pertingere ad distinguendos spiritualium substantiarum gradus, nisi secundum distinctiones corporum. Haec autem omnia sunt impossibilia, ut supra probatum est. Unde manifeste falsum est animam esse de substantia Dei.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32254)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod inspirare non est accipiendum corporaliter, sed idem est Deum inspirare, quod spiritum facere. Quamvis et homo corporaliter spirans non emittat aliquid de sua substantia, sed de natura extranea.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32255)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod anima, etsi sit forma simplex secundum suam essentiam, non tamen est suum esse, sed est ens per participationem; ut ex supra dictis patet. Et ideo non est actus purus, sicut Deus.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32256)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod differens, proprie acceptum, aliquo differt, unde ibi quaeritur differentia, ubi est convenientia. Et propter hoc oportet differentia esse composita quodammodo, cum in aliquo differant, et in aliquo conveniant. Sed secundum hoc, licet omne differens sit diversum, non tamen omne diversum est differens; ut dicitur in X Metaphys. Nam simplicia diversa sunt seipsis, non autem differunt aliquibus differentiis, ex quibus componantur. Sicut homo et asinus differunt rationali et irrationali differentia, de quibus non est dicere quod ulterius aliis differentiis differant.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 90 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32257)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima non sit producta in esse per creationem. Quod enim in se habet aliquid materiale, fit ex materia. Sed anima habet in se aliquid materiale, cum non sit actus purus. Ergo anima est facta ex materia. Non ergo est creata.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32258)\n\nPraeterea, omnis actus materiae alicuius videtur educi de potentia materiae, cum enim materia sit in potentia ad actum, actus quilibet praeexistit in materia in potentia. Sed anima est actus materiae corporalis, ut ex eius definitione apparet. Ergo anima educitur de potentia materiae.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32259)\n\nPraeterea, anima est forma quaedam. Si igitur anima fit per creationem, pari ratione omnes aliae formae. Et sic nulla forma exibit in esse per generationem. Quod est inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32260)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *creavit Deus hominem ad imaginem suam*. Est autem homo ad imaginem Dei secundum animam. Ergo anima exivit in esse per creationem.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32261)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod anima rationalis non potest fieri nisi per creationem, quod non est verum de aliis formis. Cuius ratio est quia, cum fieri sit via ad esse, hoc modo alicui competit fieri, sicut ei competit esse. Illud autem proprie dicitur esse, quod ipsum habet esse, quasi in suo esse subsistens, unde solae substantiae proprie et vere dicuntur entia. Accidens vero non habet esse, sed eo aliquid est, et hac ratione ens dicitur; sicut albedo dicitur ens, quia ea aliquid est album. Et propter hoc dicitur in VII Metaphys., quod accidens dicitur magis entis quam ens. Et eadem ratio est de omnibus aliis formis non subsistentibus. Et ideo nulli formae non subsistenti proprie competit fieri, sed dicuntur fieri per hoc quod composita subsistentia fiunt. Anima autem rationalis est forma subsistens, ut supra habitum est. Unde sibi proprie competit esse et fieri. Et quia non potest fieri ex materia praeiacente, neque corporali, quia sic esset naturae corporeae; neque spirituali, quia sic substantiae spirituales in invicem transmutarentur, necesse est dicere quod non fiat nisi per creationem.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32262)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in anima est sicut materiale ipsa simplex essentia, formale autem in ipsa est esse participatum, quod quidem ex necessitate simul est cum essentia animae, quia esse per se consequitur ad formam. Et eadem ratio esset, si poneretur composita ex quadam materia spirituali, ut quidam dicunt. Quia illa materia non est in potentia ad aliam formam, sicut nec materia caelestis corporis, alioquin anima esset corruptibilis. Unde nullo modo anima potest fieri ex materia praeiacente.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32263)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod actum extrahi de potentia materiae, nihil aliud est quam aliquid fieri actu, quod prius erat in potentia. Sed quia anima rationalis non habet esse suum dependens a materia corporali, sed habet esse subsistens, et excedit capacitatem materiae corporalis, ut supra dictum est; propterea non educitur de potentia materiae.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32264)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod non est simile de anima rationali, et de aliis formis, ut dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 90 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32265)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima rationalis non sit producta a Deo immediate, sed mediantibus Angelis. Maior enim ordo est in spiritualibus quam in corporalibus. Sed corpora inferiora producuntur per corpora superiora, ut Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom. Ergo et inferiores spiritus, qui sunt animae rationales, producuntur per spiritus superiores, qui sunt Angeli.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32266)\n\nPraeterea, finis rerum respondet principio, Deus enim est principium et finis rerum. Ergo et exitus rerum a principio respondet reductioni rerum in finem. Sed infima reducuntur per prima, ut Dionysius dicit. Ergo et infima procedunt in esse per prima, scilicet animae per Angelos.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32267)\n\n*Praeterea, perfectum est quod potest sibi simile facere*, ut dicitur in IV Meteor. Sed spirituales substantiae sunt multo magis perfectae quam corporales. Cum ergo corpora faciant sibi similia secundum speciem, multo magis Angeli poterunt facere aliquid infra se secundum speciem naturae, scilicet animam rationalem.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32268)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, quod *Deus ipse inspiravit in faciem hominis spiraculum vitae*.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32269)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam posuerunt quod Angeli, secundum quod operantur in virtute Dei, causant animas rationales. Sed hoc est omnino impossibile, et a fide alienum. Ostensum est enim quod anima rationalis non potest produci nisi per creationem. Solus autem Deus potest creare. Quia solius primi agentis est agere, nullo praesupposito, cum semper agens secundum praesupponat aliquid a primo agente, ut supra habitum est. Quod autem agit aliquid ex aliquo praesupposito, agit transmutando. Et ideo nullum aliud agens agit nisi transmutando; sed solus Deus agit creando. Et quia anima rationalis non potest produci per transmutationem alicuius materiae, ideo non potest produci nisi a Deo immediate.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32270)\n\nEt per hoc patet solutio ad obiecta. Nam quod corpora causant vel sibi similia vel inferiora, et quod superiora reducunt inferiora, totum hoc provenit per quandam transmutationem.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 90 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32271)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima humana fuerit producta ante corpus. Opus enim creationis praecessit opus distinctionis et ornatus, ut supra habitum est. Sed anima producta est in esse per creationem; corpus autem factum est in fine ornatus. Ergo anima hominis producta est ante corpus.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32272)\n\nPraeterea, anima rationalis magis convenit cum Angelis quam cum animalibus brutis. Sed Angeli creati fuerunt ante corpora, vel statim a principio cum corporali materia; corpus autem hominis formatum est sexto die, quando et bruta animalia sunt producta. Ergo anima hominis fuit creata ante corpus.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32273)\n\nPraeterea, finis proportionatur principio. Sed anima in fine remanet post corpus. Ergo et in principio fuit creata ante corpus.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32274)\n\nSed contra est quod actus proprius fit in potentia propria. Cum ergo anima sit proprius actus corporis, anima producta est in corpore.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32275)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Origenes posuit non solum animam primi hominis, sed animas omnium hominum ante corpora simul cum Angelis creatas; propter hoc quod credidit omnes spirituales substantias, tam animas quam Angelos, aequales esse secundum suae naturae conditionem, sed solum merito distare; sic ut quaedam earum corporibus alligarentur, quae sunt animae hominum vel caelestium corporum; quaedam vero in sui puritate, secundum diversos ordines, remanerent. De qua opinione supra iam diximus, et ideo relinquatur ad praesens. Augustinus vero, in VII super Gen. ad Litt., dicit quod anima primi hominis ante corpus cum Angelis est creata, propter aliam rationem. Quia scilicet ponit quod corpus hominis in illis operibus sex dierum non fuit productum in actu, sed solum secundum causales rationes, quod non potest de anima dici; quia nec ex aliqua materia corporali aut spirituali praeexistente facta fuit, nec ex aliqua virtute creata produci potuit. Et ideo videtur quod ipsamet anima in operibus sex dierum, in quibus omnia facta fuerunt, simul cum Angelis fuerit creata; et quod postmodum propria voluntate inclinata fuit ad corpus administrandum. Sed hoc non dicit asserendo, ut eius verba demonstrant. Dicit enim, *credatur, si nulla Scripturarum auctoritas seu veritatis ratio contradicit, hominem ita factum sexto die, ut corporis quidem humani ratio causalis in elementis mundi, anima vero iam ipsa crearetur*. Posset autem hoc utique tolerari secundum eos qui ponunt quod anima habet per se speciem et naturam completam, et quod non unitur corpori ut forma, sed solum ad ipsum administrandum. Si autem anima unitur corpori ut forma, et est naturaliter pars humanae naturae, hoc omnino esse non potest. Manifestum est enim quod Deus primas res instituit in perfecto statu suae naturae, secundum quod uniuscuiusque rei species exigebat. Anima autem, cum sit pars humanae naturae, non habet naturalem perfectionem nisi secundum quod est corpori unita. Unde non fuisset conveniens animam sine corpore creari. Sustinendo ergo opinionem Augustini de operibus sex dierum, dici poterit quod anima humana praecessit in operibus sex dierum secundum quandam similitudinem generis, prout convenit cum Angelis in intellectuali natura; ipsa vero fuit creata simul cum corpore. Secundum alios vero sanctos, tam anima quam corpus primi hominis in operibus sex dierum sunt producta.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32276)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, si natura animae haberet integram speciem, ita quod secundum se crearetur, ratio illa procederet, ut per se in principio crearetur. Sed quia naturaliter est forma corporis, non fuit seorsum creanda, sed debuit creari in corpore.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32277)\n\nEt similiter est dicendum ad secundum. Nam anima si per se speciem haberet, magis conveniret cum Angelis. Sed inquantum est forma corporis, pertinet ad genus animalium, ut formale principium.\n\n[I q. 90 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32278)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod animam remanere post corpus, accidit per defectum corporis, qui est mors. Qui quidem defectus in principio creationis animae, esse non debuit.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0002","text":"[I q. 90 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32248)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0003","text":"Post praemissa considerandum est de prima hominis productione. Et circa hoc consideranda sunt quatuor, primo considerandum est de productione ipsius hominis; secundo, de fine productionis; tertio, de statu et conditione hominis primo producti; quarto, de loco eius. Circa productionem autem consideranda sunt tria, primo, de productione hominis quantum ad animam; secundo, quantum ad corpus viri; tertio, quantum ad productionem mulieris. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum anima humana sit aliquid factum, vel sit de substantia ipsius Dei. Secundo, supposito quod sit facta, utrum sit creata. Tertio, utrum sit facta mediantibus Angelis. Quarto, utrum sit facta ante corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0005","text":"[I q. 90 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32249)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima non sit facta, sed sit de substantia Dei. Dicitur enim Gen. II, *formavit Deus hominem de limo terrae, et inspiravit in faciem eius spiraculum vitae, et factus est homo in animam viventem*. Sed ille qui spirat, aliquid a se emittit. Ergo anima qua homo vivit, est aliquid de substantia Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0007","text":"[I q. 90 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32250)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0008","text":"Praeterea, sicut supra habitum est, anima est forma simplex. Sed forma est actus. Ergo anima est actus purus, quod est solius Dei. Ergo anima est de substantia Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0009","text":"[I q. 90 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32251)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0010","text":"Praeterea, quaecumque sunt, et nullo modo differunt, sunt idem. Sed Deus et mens sunt, et nullo modo differunt, quia oporteret quod aliquibus differentiis differrent, et sic essent composita. Ergo Deus et mens humana idem sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0011","text":"[I q. 90 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32252)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus, in libro de origine animae, enumerat quaedam quae dicit esse multum aperteque perversa, et fidei Catholicae adversa; inter quae primum est, quod quidam dixerunt *Deum animam non de nihilo, sed de seipso fecisse*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0013","text":"[I q. 90 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32253)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod dicere animam esse de substantia Dei, manifestam improbabilitatem continet. Ut enim ex dictis patet, anima humana est quandoque intelligens in potentia, et scientiam quodammodo a rebus acquirit, et habet diversas potentias, quae omnia aliena sunt a Dei natura, qui est actus purus, et nihil ab alio accipiens, et nullam in se diversitatem habens, ut supra probatum est. Sed hic error principium habuisse videtur ex duabus positionibus antiquorum. Primi enim qui naturas rerum considerare incoeperunt, imaginationem transcendere non valentes, nihil praeter corpora esse posuerunt. Et ideo Deum dicebant esse quoddam corpus, quod aliorum corporum iudicabant esse principium. Et quia animam ponebant esse de natura illius corporis quod dicebant esse principium, ut dicitur in I de anima, per consequens sequebatur quod anima esset de natura Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0015","text":"Iuxta quam positionem etiam Manichaei, Deum esse quandam lucem corpoream existimantes, quandam partem illius lucis animam esse posuerunt corpori alligatam. Secundo vero processum fuit ad hoc, quod aliqui aliquid incorporeum esse apprehenderunt, non tamen a corpore separatum, sed corporis formam. Unde et Varro dixit quod *Deus est anima mundum motu et ratione gubernans*; ut Augustinus narrat, VII de Civ. Dei. Sic igitur illius totalis animae partem aliqui posuerunt animam hominis, sicut homo est pars totius mundi; non valentes intellectu pertingere ad distinguendos spiritualium substantiarum gradus, nisi secundum distinctiones corporum. Haec autem omnia sunt impossibilia, ut supra probatum est. Unde manifeste falsum est animam esse de substantia Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0016","text":"[I q. 90 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32254)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod inspirare non est accipiendum corporaliter, sed idem est Deum inspirare, quod spiritum facere. Quamvis et homo corporaliter spirans non emittat aliquid de sua substantia, sed de natura extranea.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0018","text":"[I q. 90 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32255)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod anima, etsi sit forma simplex secundum suam essentiam, non tamen est suum esse, sed est ens per participationem; ut ex supra dictis patet. Et ideo non est actus purus, sicut Deus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0020","text":"[I q. 90 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32256)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod differens, proprie acceptum, aliquo differt, unde ibi quaeritur differentia, ubi est convenientia. Et propter hoc oportet differentia esse composita quodammodo, cum in aliquo differant, et in aliquo conveniant. Sed secundum hoc, licet omne differens sit diversum, non tamen omne diversum est differens; ut dicitur in X Metaphys. Nam simplicia diversa sunt seipsis, non autem differunt aliquibus differentiis, ex quibus componantur. Sicut homo et asinus differunt rationali et irrationali differentia, de quibus non est dicere quod ulterius aliis differentiis differant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0023","text":"[I q. 90 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32257)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima non sit producta in esse per creationem. Quod enim in se habet aliquid materiale, fit ex materia. Sed anima habet in se aliquid materiale, cum non sit actus purus. Ergo anima est facta ex materia. Non ergo est creata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0025","text":"[I q. 90 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32258)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0026","text":"Praeterea, omnis actus materiae alicuius videtur educi de potentia materiae, cum enim materia sit in potentia ad actum, actus quilibet praeexistit in materia in potentia. Sed anima est actus materiae corporalis, ut ex eius definitione apparet. Ergo anima educitur de potentia materiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0027","text":"[I q. 90 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32259)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0028","text":"Praeterea, anima est forma quaedam. Si igitur anima fit per creationem, pari ratione omnes aliae formae. Et sic nulla forma exibit in esse per generationem. Quod est inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0029","text":"[I q. 90 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32260)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0030","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *creavit Deus hominem ad imaginem suam*. Est autem homo ad imaginem Dei secundum animam. Ergo anima exivit in esse per creationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0031","text":"[I q. 90 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32261)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0032","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod anima rationalis non potest fieri nisi per creationem, quod non est verum de aliis formis. Cuius ratio est quia, cum fieri sit via ad esse, hoc modo alicui competit fieri, sicut ei competit esse. Illud autem proprie dicitur esse, quod ipsum habet esse, quasi in suo esse subsistens, unde solae substantiae proprie et vere dicuntur entia. Accidens vero non habet esse, sed eo aliquid est, et hac ratione ens dicitur; sicut albedo dicitur ens, quia ea aliquid est album. Et propter hoc dicitur in VII Metaphys., quod accidens dicitur magis entis quam ens. Et eadem ratio est de omnibus aliis formis non subsistentibus. Et ideo nulli formae non subsistenti proprie competit fieri, sed dicuntur fieri per hoc quod composita subsistentia fiunt. Anima autem rationalis est forma subsistens, ut supra habitum est. Unde sibi proprie competit esse et fieri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0033","text":"Et quia non potest fieri ex materia praeiacente, neque corporali, quia sic esset naturae corporeae; neque spirituali, quia sic substantiae spirituales in invicem transmutarentur, necesse est dicere quod non fiat nisi per creationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0034","text":"[I q. 90 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32262)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0035","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in anima est sicut materiale ipsa simplex essentia, formale autem in ipsa est esse participatum, quod quidem ex necessitate simul est cum essentia animae, quia esse per se consequitur ad formam. Et eadem ratio esset, si poneretur composita ex quadam materia spirituali, ut quidam dicunt. Quia illa materia non est in potentia ad aliam formam, sicut nec materia caelestis corporis, alioquin anima esset corruptibilis. Unde nullo modo anima potest fieri ex materia praeiacente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0036","text":"[I q. 90 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32263)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0037","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod actum extrahi de potentia materiae, nihil aliud est quam aliquid fieri actu, quod prius erat in potentia. Sed quia anima rationalis non habet esse suum dependens a materia corporali, sed habet esse subsistens, et excedit capacitatem materiae corporalis, ut supra dictum est; propterea non educitur de potentia materiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0038","text":"[I q. 90 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32264)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0039","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod non est simile de anima rationali, et de aliis formis, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0040","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0041","text":"[I q. 90 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32265)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0042","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima rationalis non sit producta a Deo immediate, sed mediantibus Angelis. Maior enim ordo est in spiritualibus quam in corporalibus. Sed corpora inferiora producuntur per corpora superiora, ut Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom. Ergo et inferiores spiritus, qui sunt animae rationales, producuntur per spiritus superiores, qui sunt Angeli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0043","text":"[I q. 90 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32266)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0044","text":"Praeterea, finis rerum respondet principio, Deus enim est principium et finis rerum. Ergo et exitus rerum a principio respondet reductioni rerum in finem. Sed infima reducuntur per prima, ut Dionysius dicit. Ergo et infima procedunt in esse per prima, scilicet animae per Angelos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0045","text":"[I q. 90 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32267)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0046","text":"*Praeterea, perfectum est quod potest sibi simile facere*, ut dicitur in IV Meteor. Sed spirituales substantiae sunt multo magis perfectae quam corporales. Cum ergo corpora faciant sibi similia secundum speciem, multo magis Angeli poterunt facere aliquid infra se secundum speciem naturae, scilicet animam rationalem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0047","text":"[I q. 90 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32268)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0048","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, quod *Deus ipse inspiravit in faciem hominis spiraculum vitae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0049","text":"[I q. 90 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32269)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0050","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam posuerunt quod Angeli, secundum quod operantur in virtute Dei, causant animas rationales. Sed hoc est omnino impossibile, et a fide alienum. Ostensum est enim quod anima rationalis non potest produci nisi per creationem. Solus autem Deus potest creare. Quia solius primi agentis est agere, nullo praesupposito, cum semper agens secundum praesupponat aliquid a primo agente, ut supra habitum est. Quod autem agit aliquid ex aliquo praesupposito, agit transmutando. Et ideo nullum aliud agens agit nisi transmutando; sed solus Deus agit creando. Et quia anima rationalis non potest produci per transmutationem alicuius materiae, ideo non potest produci nisi a Deo immediate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0051","text":"[I q. 90 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32270)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0052","text":"Et per hoc patet solutio ad obiecta. Nam quod corpora causant vel sibi similia vel inferiora, et quod superiora reducunt inferiora, totum hoc provenit per quandam transmutationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0053","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0054","text":"[I q. 90 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32271)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0055","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima humana fuerit producta ante corpus. Opus enim creationis praecessit opus distinctionis et ornatus, ut supra habitum est. Sed anima producta est in esse per creationem; corpus autem factum est in fine ornatus. Ergo anima hominis producta est ante corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0056","text":"[I q. 90 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32272)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0057","text":"Praeterea, anima rationalis magis convenit cum Angelis quam cum animalibus brutis. Sed Angeli creati fuerunt ante corpora, vel statim a principio cum corporali materia; corpus autem hominis formatum est sexto die, quando et bruta animalia sunt producta. Ergo anima hominis fuit creata ante corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0058","text":"[I q. 90 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32273)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0059","text":"Praeterea, finis proportionatur principio. Sed anima in fine remanet post corpus. Ergo et in principio fuit creata ante corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0060","text":"[I q. 90 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32274)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0061","text":"Sed contra est quod actus proprius fit in potentia propria. Cum ergo anima sit proprius actus corporis, anima producta est in corpore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0062","text":"[I q. 90 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32275)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0063","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Origenes posuit non solum animam primi hominis, sed animas omnium hominum ante corpora simul cum Angelis creatas; propter hoc quod credidit omnes spirituales substantias, tam animas quam Angelos, aequales esse secundum suae naturae conditionem, sed solum merito distare; sic ut quaedam earum corporibus alligarentur, quae sunt animae hominum vel caelestium corporum; quaedam vero in sui puritate, secundum diversos ordines, remanerent. De qua opinione supra iam diximus, et ideo relinquatur ad praesens. Augustinus vero, in VII super Gen. ad Litt., dicit quod anima primi hominis ante corpus cum Angelis est creata, propter aliam rationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0064","text":"Quia scilicet ponit quod corpus hominis in illis operibus sex dierum non fuit productum in actu, sed solum secundum causales rationes, quod non potest de anima dici; quia nec ex aliqua materia corporali aut spirituali praeexistente facta fuit, nec ex aliqua virtute creata produci potuit. Et ideo videtur quod ipsamet anima in operibus sex dierum, in quibus omnia facta fuerunt, simul cum Angelis fuerit creata; et quod postmodum propria voluntate inclinata fuit ad corpus administrandum. Sed hoc non dicit asserendo, ut eius verba demonstrant. Dicit enim, *credatur, si nulla Scripturarum auctoritas seu veritatis ratio contradicit, hominem ita factum sexto die, ut corporis quidem humani ratio causalis in elementis mundi, anima vero iam ipsa crearetur*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0065","text":"Posset autem hoc utique tolerari secundum eos qui ponunt quod anima habet per se speciem et naturam completam, et quod non unitur corpori ut forma, sed solum ad ipsum administrandum. Si autem anima unitur corpori ut forma, et est naturaliter pars humanae naturae, hoc omnino esse non potest. Manifestum est enim quod Deus primas res instituit in perfecto statu suae naturae, secundum quod uniuscuiusque rei species exigebat. Anima autem, cum sit pars humanae naturae, non habet naturalem perfectionem nisi secundum quod est corpori unita. Unde non fuisset conveniens animam sine corpore creari. Sustinendo ergo opinionem Augustini de operibus sex dierum, dici poterit quod anima humana praecessit in operibus sex dierum secundum quandam similitudinem generis, prout convenit cum Angelis in intellectuali natura; ipsa vero fuit creata simul cum corpore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0066","text":"Secundum alios vero sanctos, tam anima quam corpus primi hominis in operibus sex dierum sunt producta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0067","text":"[I q. 90 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32276)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0068","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, si natura animae haberet integram speciem, ita quod secundum se crearetur, ratio illa procederet, ut per se in principio crearetur. Sed quia naturaliter est forma corporis, non fuit seorsum creanda, sed debuit creari in corpore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0069","text":"[I q. 90 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32277)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0070","text":"Et similiter est dicendum ad secundum. Nam anima si per se speciem haberet, magis conveniret cum Angelis. Sed inquantum est forma corporis, pertinet ad genus animalium, ut formale principium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0071","text":"[I q. 90 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32278)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0072","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod animam remanere post corpus, accidit per defectum corporis, qui est mors. Qui quidem defectus in principio creationis animae, esse non debuit.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"8600ef1f7a5330ffba774ca7a306b1fff0785b48b9f11b281d1ce0215ed5585e","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T152340525390Z_be228fcb","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 90","source_sha256":"8600ef1f7a5330ffba774ca7a306b1fff0785b48b9f11b281d1ce0215ed5585e","sha256":"62deb470c0b0f25f3781347f3d53283beee1c57bf58a41ffa9ee77d4915de2e5","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:23:40.525439+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0002","text":"[I q. 90 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32248)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0003","text":"After the foregoing, consideration must be given to the first production of man. And concerning this, four things must be considered: first, the production of man himself; second, the end of his production; third, the state and condition of man when first produced; fourth, his place. Concerning his production, however, three things must be considered: first, the production of man with respect to the soul; second, with respect to the body of the man; third, with respect to the production of the woman. Concerning the first, four questions are asked. First, whether the human soul is something made, or is of the very substance of God. Second, granted that it is made, whether it is created. Third, whether it is made through the mediation of the angels. Fourth, whether it is made before the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0005","text":"[I q. 90 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32249)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the soul is not made, but is of the substance of God. For it is said in Gen. II: *God formed man from the slime of the earth, and breathed into his face the breath of life, and man became a living soul*. But one who breathes emits something from himself. Therefore the soul by which man lives is something of the substance of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0007","text":"[I q. 90 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32250)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0008","text":"Further, as was held above, the soul is a simple form. But form is act. Therefore the soul is pure act, which belongs to God alone. Therefore the soul is of the substance of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0009","text":"[I q. 90 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32251)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0010","text":"Further, whatever things exist and in no way differ are the same. But God and mind exist and in no way differ, because they would have to differ by some differences, and thus would be composite. Therefore God and the human mind are the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0011","text":"[I q. 90 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32252)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0012","text":"On the contrary, Augustine, in the book *On the Origin of the Soul*, enumerates certain things which he says are very openly perverse and contrary to the Catholic faith; among these the first is that certain persons said that *God made the soul, not from nothing, but from Himself*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0013","text":"[I q. 90 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32253)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0014","text":"I answer that to say that the soul is of the substance of God entails a manifest impossibility. For, as is clear from what has been said, the human soul is sometimes understanding in potency, and in some manner acquires knowledge from things, and has diverse powers—all of which are foreign to the nature of God, who is pure act, receiving nothing from another, and having no diversity in Himself, as was proved above. But this error seems to have had its origin in two positions of the ancients. For the first who began to consider the natures of things, being unable to transcend imagination, posited that nothing exists except bodies. And therefore they said that God is a certain body, which they judged to be the principle of other bodies. And because they posited that the soul is of the nature of that body which they said was the principle, as is said in *On the Soul* I, it consequently followed that the soul was of the nature of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0015","text":"In keeping with this position, the Manicheans also, thinking God to be a certain corporeal light, posited that the soul is a certain part of that light bound to the body. Secondly, however, the process went so far that some apprehended something incorporeal, yet not separate from body, but as the form of a body. Hence Varro also said that *God is a soul governing the world by motion and reason*; as Augustine relates, *City of God* VII. Thus, some posited the soul of man as a part of that total soul, just as man is a part of the whole world; being unable by intellect to attain to distinguishing the grades of spiritual substances except according to the distinctions of bodies. But all these things are impossible, as was proved above. Hence it is manifestly false that the soul is of the substance of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0016","text":"[I q. 90 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32254)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that breathing in is not to be understood corporeally, but for God to breathe in is the same as to make a spirit. Although even a man breathing corporeally does not emit something of his own substance, but of an extraneous nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0018","text":"[I q. 90 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32255)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0019","text":"To the second, it must be said that, although the soul is a simple form according to its essence, nevertheless it is not its own being, but is a being by participation, as is clear from what has been said above. And therefore it is not pure act, as God is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0020","text":"[I q. 90 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32256)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0021","text":"To the third, it must be said that what differs, properly understood, differs by something; hence difference is sought where there is agreement. And for this reason differences must in some way be composite, since they differ in something and agree in something. But according to this, although everything that differs is diverse, nevertheless not everything diverse differs; as is said in *Metaphysics* X. For simple things are diverse through themselves, but do not differ by certain differences from which they might be composed. Thus man and ass differ by the rational and irrational difference, concerning which it is not to be said that they further differ by other differences.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0023","text":"[I q. 90 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32257)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0024","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the soul is not brought into being by creation. For whatever has something material in itself comes to be from matter. But the soul has something material in itself, since it is not pure act. Therefore the soul is made from matter. Therefore it is not created.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0025","text":"[I q. 90 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32258)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0026","text":"Moreover, every act of some matter seems to be drawn out from the potentiality of matter; for, since matter is in potency to act, any act pre-exists in matter potentially. But the soul is the act of bodily matter, as is apparent from its definition. Therefore the soul is drawn out from the potentiality of matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0027","text":"[I q. 90 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32259)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0028","text":"Moreover, the soul is a certain form. If, therefore, the soul comes to be through creation, then by equal reasoning so do all other forms. And thus no form will come forth into being through generation. This is unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0029","text":"[I q. 90 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32260)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0030","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Gen. I: *God created man in His own image*. Now man is in the image of God according to the soul. Therefore the soul came forth into being through creation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0031","text":"[I q. 90 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32261)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0032","text":"I answer that it must be said that the rational soul can come to be only through creation, which is not true of other forms. The reason for this is that, since coming to be is a way to being, coming to be belongs to something in the same way as being belongs to it. Now that is properly said to be which itself has being, as subsisting, so to speak, in its own being; hence only substances are properly and truly called beings. But an accident does not have being; rather, by it something is, and for this reason it is called a being; just as whiteness is called a being because by it something is white. And on this account it is said in VII Metaphysics that an accident is called more “of a being” than a being. The same account applies to all other non-subsistent forms. Therefore, coming to be does not properly belong to any non-subsistent form; rather, they are said to come to be because the subsistent composites come to be. But the rational soul is a subsistent form, as was established above. Hence being and coming to be properly belong to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0033","text":"And because it cannot come to be from pre-existing matter—neither bodily matter, for in that case it would be of a bodily nature; nor spiritual matter, for in that case spiritual substances would be transformed into one another—it is necessary to say that it comes to be only through creation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0034","text":"[I q. 90 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32262)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0035","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that in the soul its simple essence itself is as the material element, whereas in it the formal element is participated being; and this necessarily exists simultaneously with the essence of the soul, because being follows upon form of itself. And the same reasoning would hold if it were posited that it is composed of a certain spiritual matter, as some say. For that matter is not in potency to another form, just as neither is the matter of a heavenly body; otherwise the soul would be corruptible. Hence in no way can the soul come to be from pre-existing matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0036","text":"[I q. 90 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32263)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0037","text":"To the second, it must be said that for an act to be drawn out from the potentiality of matter is nothing other than for something to become actual which was previously potential. But because the rational soul does not have its being dependent on bodily matter, but has subsistent being, and exceeds the capacity of bodily matter, as was said above, therefore it is not drawn out from the potentiality of matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0038","text":"[I q. 90 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32264)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0039","text":"To the third, it must be said that the rational soul and the other forms are not alike, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0040","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0041","text":"[I q. 90 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32265)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0042","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that the rational soul is not produced immediately by God, but through the mediation of angels. For there is a greater order among spiritual things than among bodily things. But lower bodies are produced through higher bodies, as Dionysius says in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*. Therefore lower spirits also, which are rational souls, are produced through higher spirits, which are angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0043","text":"[I q. 90 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32266)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0044","text":"Moreover, the end of things corresponds to their beginning, for God is the beginning and end of things. Therefore the going forth of things from their beginning also corresponds to the leading back of things to their end. But the lowest are led back through the first, as Dionysius says. Therefore the lowest likewise proceed into being through the first, namely, souls through angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0045","text":"[I q. 90 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32267)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0046","text":"*Moreover, that is perfect which can make something like itself*, as is said in IV *Meteorology*. But spiritual substances are much more perfect than bodily ones. Therefore, since bodies make things like themselves according to species, angels will much more be able to make something beneath themselves according to the species of nature, namely, a rational soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0047","text":"[I q. 90 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32268)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0048","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Gen. II that *God Himself breathed into the face of man the breath of life*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0049","text":"[I q. 90 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32269)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0050","text":"I answer that some have held that angels, insofar as they operate by the power of God, cause rational souls. But this is altogether impossible and alien to the faith. For it has been shown that the rational soul cannot be produced except by creation. But God alone can create. For to act without anything presupposed belongs to the first agent alone, since a secondary agent always presupposes something from the first agent, as was held above. But what acts from something presupposed acts by transmuting. And therefore no other agent acts except by transmuting; but God alone acts by creating. And because the rational soul cannot be produced through the transmutation of any matter, it cannot be produced except immediately by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0051","text":"[I q. 90 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32270)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0052","text":"And through this the solution to the objections is clear. For that bodies cause either things like themselves or inferior things, and that superior things reduce inferior things, all this comes about through a certain transmutation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0053","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0054","text":"[I q. 90 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32271)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0055","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth article. It seems that the human soul was produced before the body. For the work of creation preceded the work of distinction and adornment, as was held above. But the soul was brought into being by creation; whereas the body was made at the end of the adornment. Therefore the soul of man was produced before the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0056","text":"[I q. 90 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32272)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0057","text":"Further, the rational soul agrees more with angels than with brute animals. But angels were created before bodies, or immediately at the beginning together with corporeal matter; whereas the human body was formed on the sixth day, when brute animals too were produced. Therefore the soul of man was created before the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0058","text":"[I q. 90 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32273)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0059","text":"Further, the end is proportioned to the beginning. But the soul remains at the end after the body. Therefore at the beginning too it was created before the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0060","text":"[I q. 90 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32274)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0061","text":"But on the contrary, a proper act comes to be in its proper potency. Therefore, since the soul is the proper act of the body, the soul was produced in the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0062","text":"[I q. 90 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32275)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0063","text":"I answer that Origen held that not only the soul of the first man, but the souls of all men, were created before bodies together with the angels; on the ground that he believed all spiritual substances, both souls and angels, to be equal as regards the condition of their nature, but to differ only by merit: such that some of them would be bound to bodies, namely the souls of men or of heavenly bodies; while others would remain in their purity, according to diverse orders. We have already spoken above about this opinion, and therefore let it be set aside for the present. Augustine, however, in Book VII On the Literal Meaning of Genesis, says that the soul of the first man was created before the body together with the angels, for another reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0064","text":"Namely, because he holds that the body of man was not produced actually in those works of the six days, but only according to causal reasons; which cannot be said of the soul, because it was neither made from any pre-existing corporeal or spiritual matter, nor could it be produced by any created power. And therefore it seems that the soul itself was created in the works of the six days, in which all things were made, together with the angels; and that afterward, by its own will, it was inclined to administer the body. But he does not state this as an assertion, as his words show. For he says: *let it be believed, if no authority of the Scriptures or reason of truth contradicts, that man was so made on the sixth day that, indeed, the causal reason of the human body was in the elements of the world, while the soul itself was already created*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0065","text":"This, however, could indeed be tolerated according to those who hold that the soul has through itself a complete species and nature, and that it is not united to the body as form, but only in order to administer it. But if the soul is united to the body as form, and is naturally a part of human nature, this cannot at all be. For it is clear that God established the first things in the perfect state of their nature, according as the species of each thing required. But the soul, since it is a part of human nature, does not have natural perfection except insofar as it is united to the body. Hence it would not have been fitting for the soul to be created without a body. Therefore, maintaining Augustine's opinion concerning the works of the six days, it may be said that the human soul preceded in the works of the six days according to a certain likeness of genus, insofar as it agrees with angels in intellectual nature; but it itself was created together with the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0066","text":"According to the other saints, however, both the soul and the body of the first man were produced in the works of the six days.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0067","text":"[I q. 90 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32276)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0068","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that, if the nature of the soul had a complete species, such that it were created according to itself, that argument would proceed, namely, that it would be created by itself in the beginning. But because it is naturally the form of the body, it was not to be created separately, but had to be created in the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0069","text":"[I q. 90 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32277)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0070","text":"And the same must be said to the second. For if the soul had a species through itself, it would agree more with angels. But insofar as it is the form of the body, it belongs to the genus of animals, as their formal principle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0071","text":"[I q. 90 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32278)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0072","text":"To the third it must be said that the soul's remaining after the body happens through a defect of the body, which is death. This defect, indeed, ought not to have been at the beginning of the soul's creation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_82:P0072"]}],"source_digest":"175f990f620f7466ff5386d4ef09af7c05a85c852b2d2274ef743f3c2b5b2cb7","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 90"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 90","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83","title":"First Part · Question 91","order":92,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_83","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 91 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32279)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de productione corporis primi hominis. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, de materia ex qua productum est. Secundo, de auctore a quo productum est. Tertio, de dispositione quae ei per productionem est attributa. Quarto, de modo et ordine productionis ipsius.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 91 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32280)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod corpus primi hominis non sit factum de limo terrae. Maioris enim virtutis est facere aliquid ex nihilo, quam ex aliquo, quia plus distat ab actu non ens quam ens in potentia. Sed cum homo sit dignissima creaturarum inferiorum, decuit ut virtus Dei maxime ostenderetur in productione corporis eius. Ergo non debuit fieri ex limo terrae, sed ex nihilo.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32281)\n\nPraeterea, corpora caelestia sunt nobiliora terrenis. Sed corpus humanum habet maximam nobilitatem, cum perficiatur a nobilissima forma, quae est anima rationalis. Ergo non debuit fieri de corpore terrestri, sed magis de corpore caelesti.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32282)\n\nPraeterea, ignis et aer sunt nobiliora corpora quam terra et aqua, quod ex eorum subtilitate apparet. Cum igitur corpus humanum sit dignissimum, magis debuit fieri ex igne et ex aere quam ex limo terrae.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32283)\n\nPraeterea, corpus humanum est compositum ex quatuor elementis. Non ergo est factum ex limo terrae, sed ex omnibus elementis.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32284)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, *formavit Deus hominem de limo terrae*.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32285)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum Deus perfectus sit, operibus suis perfectionem dedit secundum eorum modum; secundum illud Deut. XXXII, *Dei perfecta sunt opera*. Ipse autem simpliciter perfectus est, ex hoc quod omnia in se praehabet, non per modum compositionis, sed simpliciter et unite, ut Dionysius dicit, eo modo quo diversi effectus praeexistunt in causa, secundum unam eius essentiam. Ista autem perfectio ad Angelos quidem derivatur, secundum quod omnia sunt in eorum cognitione quae sunt a Deo in natura producta, per formas diversas. Ad hominem vero derivatur inferiori modo huiusmodi perfectio. Non enim in sua cognitione naturali habet omnium naturalium notitiam; sed est ex rebus omnibus quodammodo compositus, dum de genere spiritualium substantiarum habet in se animam rationalem, de similitudine vero caelestium corporum habet elongationem a contrariis per maximam aequalitatem complexionis, elementa vero secundum substantiam. Ita tamen quod superiora elementa praedominantur in eo secundum virtutem, scilicet ignis et aer, quia vita praecipue consistit in calido, quod est ignis, et humido, quod est aeris. Inferiora vero elementa abundant in eo secundum substantiam, aliter enim non posset esse mixtionis aequalitas, nisi inferiora elementa, quae sunt minoris virtutis, secundum quantitatem in homine abundarent. Et ideo dicitur corpus hominis de limo terrae formatum, quia limus dicitur terra aquae permixta. Et propter hoc homo dicitur minor mundus, quia omnes creaturae mundi quodammodo inveniuntur in eo.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32286)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod virtus Dei creantis manifestata est in corpore hominis, dum eius materia est per creationem producta. Oportuit autem ut ex materia quatuor elementorum fieret corpus humanum, ut homo haberet convenientiam cum inferioribus corporibus, quasi medium quoddam existens inter spirituales et corporales substantias.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32287)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, quamvis corpus caeleste sit simpliciter nobilius terrestri corpore, tamen quantum ad actus animae rationalis, est minus conveniens. Nam anima rationalis accipit notitiam veritatis quodammodo per sensus; quorum organa formari non possunt ex corpore caelesti, cum sit impassibile. Nec est verum quod quidam dicunt aliquid de quinta essentia materialiter ad compositionem humani corporis advenire, ponentes animam uniri corpori mediante quadam luce. Primo enim, falsum est quod dicunt, lucem esse corpus. Secundo vero, impossibile est aliquid de quinta essentia vel a corpore caelesti dividi, vel elementis permisceri, propter caelestis corporis impassibilitatem. Unde non venit in compositionem mixtorum corporum, nisi secundum suae virtutis effectum.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32288)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, si ignis et aer, quae sunt maioris virtutis in agendo, etiam secundum quantitatem in compositione humani corporis abundarent, omnino ad se traherent alia, et non posset fieri aequalitas commixtionis, quae est necessaria in compositione hominis ad bonitatem sensus tactus, qui est fundamentum sensuum aliorum. Oportet enim organum cuiuslibet sensus non habere in actu contraria quorum sensus est perceptivus, sed in potentia tantum. Vel ita quod omnino careat toto genere contrariorum, sicut pupilla caret colore, ut sit in potentia ad omnes colores, quod in organo tactus non erat possibile, cum sit compositum ex elementis, quorum qualitates percipit tactus. Vel ita quod organum sit medium inter contraria, ut necesse est in tactu accidere, medium enim est quodammodo in potentia ad extrema.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32289)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod in limo terrae est terra, et aqua conglutinans partes terrae. De aliis autem elementis Scriptura mentionem non fecit, tum quia minus abundant secundum quantitatem in corpore hominis, ut dictum est; tum etiam quia in tota rerum productione, de igne et aere, quae sensu non percipiuntur a rudibus mentionem non fecit Scriptura, quae rudi populo tradebatur.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 91 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32290)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod corpus humanum non sit immediate a Deo productum. Dicit enim Augustinus, in III de Trin., quod corporalia disponuntur a Deo per angelicam creaturam. Sed corpus humanum formatum fuit ex materia corporali, ut dictum est. Ergo debuit produci mediantibus Angelis, et non immediate a Deo.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32291)\n\nPraeterea, quod fieri potest virtute creata, non est necessarium quod immediate producatur a Deo. Sed corpus humanum produci potest per virtutem creatam caelestis corporis, nam et quaedam animalia ex putrefactione generantur per virtutem activam corporis caelestis; et Albumasar dicit quod in locis in quibus nimis abundat calor aut frigus, homines non generantur, sed in locis temperatis tantum. Ergo non oportuit quod immediate corpus humanum formaretur a Deo.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32292)\n\nPraeterea, nihil fit ex materia corporali nisi per aliquam materiae transmutationem. Sed omnis transmutatio corporalis causatur ex motu caelestis corporis, qui est primus motuum. Cum igitur corpus humanum sit productum ex materia corporali, videtur quod ad eius formationem aliquid operatum fuerit corpus caeleste.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32293)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, super Gen. ad Litt., quod homo factus est secundum corpus, in operibus sex dierum, secundum causales rationes quas Deus inseruit creaturae corporali; postmodum vero fuit formatum in actu. Sed quod praeexistit in corporali creatura secundum causales rationes, per aliquam virtutem corpoream produci potest. Ergo corpus humanum productum est aliqua virtute creata, et non immediate a Deo.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32294)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Eccli. XVII, *Deus de terra creavit hominem*.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32295)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod prima formatio humani corporis non potuit esse per aliquam virtutem creatam, sed immediate a Deo. Posuerunt siquidem aliqui formas quae sunt in materia corporali, a quibusdam formis immaterialibus derivari. Sed hanc opinionem repellit philosophus, in VII Metaphys., per hoc quod formis non competit per se fieri, sed composito, ut supra expositum est; et quia oportet agens esse simile facto non convenit quod forma pura, quae est sine materia, producat formam quae est in materia, quae non fit nisi per hoc quod compositum fit. Et ideo oportet quod forma quae est in materia, sit causa formae quae est in materia, secundum quod compositum a composito generatur. Deus autem, quamvis omnino sit immaterialis, tamen solus est qui sua virtute materiam producere potest creando. Unde ipsius solius est formam producere in materia absque adminiculo praecedentis formae materialis. Et propter hoc, Angeli non possunt transmutare corpora ad formam aliquam, nisi adhibitis seminibus quibusdam, ut Augustinus dicit in III de Trin. Quia igitur corpus humanum nunquam formatum fuerat, cuius virtute per viam generationis aliud simile in specie formaretur, necesse fuit quod primum corpus hominis immediate formaretur a Deo.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32296)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, etsi Angeli aliquod ministerium Deo exhibeant in his quae circa corpora operatur; aliqua tamen Deus in creatura corporea facit, quae nullo modo Angeli facere possunt; sicut quod suscitat mortuos, et illuminat caecos. Secundum quam virtutem etiam corpus primi hominis de limo terrae formavit. Potuit tamen fieri ut aliquod ministerium in formatione corporis primi hominis Angeli exhiberent; sicut exhibebunt in ultima resurrectione, pulveres colligendo.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32297)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod animalia perfecta, quae generantur ex semine, non possunt generari per solam virtutem caelestis corporis, ut Avicenna fingit; licet ad eorum generationem naturalem cooperetur virtus caelestis corporis, prout philosophus dicit, in II Physic., quod *homo generat hominem ex materia, et sol*. Et exinde est quod exigitur locus temperatus ad generationem hominum et aliorum animalium perfectorum. Sufficit autem virtus caelestium corporum ad generandum quaedam animalia imperfectiora ex materia disposita, manifestum est enim quod plura requiruntur ad productionem rei perfectae, quam ad productionem rei imperfectae.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32298)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod motus caeli est causa transmutationum naturalium, non tamen transmutationum quae fiunt praeter naturae ordinem, et sola virtute divina, sicut quod mortui resuscitantur, quod caeci illuminantur. Quibus est simile quod homo ex limo terrae formatur.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32299)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod secundum rationes causales in creaturis dicitur aliquid praeexistere dupliciter. Uno modo, secundum potentiam activam et passivam, ut non solum ex materia praeexistenti fieri possit, sed etiam ut aliqua praeexistens creatura hoc facere possit. Alio modo, secundum potentiam passivam tantum, ut scilicet de materia praeexistenti fieri possit a Deo. Et hoc modo, secundum Augustinum, corpus hominis praeextitit in operibus productis secundum causales rationes.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 91 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32300)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod corpus hominis non habuerit convenientem dispositionem. Cum enim homo sit nobilissimum animalium, corpus hominis debuit esse dispositum optime ad ea quae sunt propria animalis, scilicet ad sensum et motum. Sed quaedam animalia inveniuntur acutioris sensus quam homo, et velocioris motus; sicut canes melius odorant, et aves velocius moventur. Ergo corpus hominis non est convenienter dispositum.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32301)\n\nPraeterea, perfectum est cui nihil deest. Sed plura desunt humano corpori quam corporibus aliorum animalium, quae habent tegumenta et arma naturalia ad sui protectionem, quae homini desunt. Ergo corpus humanum est imperfectissime dispositum.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32302)\n\nPraeterea, homo plus distat a plantis quam ab animalibus brutis. Sed plantae habent staturam rectam; animalia autem bruta pronam. Ergo homo non debuit habere staturam rectam.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32303)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Eccle. VII, *Deus fecit hominem rectum*.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32304)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod omnes res naturales productae sunt ab arte divina, unde sunt quodammodo artificiata ipsius Dei. Quilibet autem artifex intendit suo operi dispositionem optimam inducere, non simpliciter, sed per comparationem ad finem. Et si talis dispositio habet secum adiunctum aliquem defectum, artifex non curat. Sicut artifex qui facit serram ad secandum, facit eam ex ferro, ut sit idonea ad secandum; nec curat eam facere ex vitro, quae est pulchrior materia, quia talis pulchritudo esset impedimentum finis. Sic igitur Deus unicuique rei naturali dedit optimam dispositionem, non quidem simpliciter, sed secundum ordinem ad proprium finem. Et hoc est quod philosophus dicit, in II Physic., *et quia dignius est sic, non tamen simpliciter, sed ad uniuscuiusque substantiam*. Finis autem proximus humani corporis est anima rationalis et operationes ipsius, materia enim est propter formam, et instrumenta propter actiones agentis. Dico ergo quod Deus instituit corpus humanum in optima dispositione secundum convenientiam ad talem formam et ad tales operationes. Et si aliquis defectus in dispositione humani corporis esse videtur, considerandum est quod talis defectus sequitur ex necessitate materiae, ad ea quae requiruntur in corpore ut sit debita proportio ipsius ad animam et ad animae operationes.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32305)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod tactus, qui est fundamentum aliorum sensuum, est perfectior in homine quam in aliquo alio animali, et propter hoc oportuit quod homo haberet temperatissimam complexionem inter omnia animalia. Praecedit etiam homo omnia alia animalia, quantum ad vires sensitivas interiores; sicut ex supra dictis apparet. Ex quadam autem necessitate contingit quod, quantum ad aliquos exteriores sensus, homo ab aliis animalibus deficiat. Sicut homo, inter omnia animalia, habet pessimum olfactum. Necessarium enim fuit quod homo, inter omnia animalia, respectu sui corporis haberet maximum cerebrum, tum ut liberius in eo perficerentur operationes interiorum virium sensitivarum, quae sunt necessariae ad intellectus operationem, ut supra dictum est; tum etiam ut frigiditas cerebri temperaret calorem cordis, quem necesse est in homine abundare, ad hoc quod homo sit rectae staturae. Magnitudo autem cerebri, propter eius humiditatem, est impedimentum olfactus, qui requirit siccitatem. Et similiter potest assignari ratio quare quaedam animalia sunt acutioris visus et subtilioris auditus quam homo, propter impedimentum horum sensuum quod necesse est consequi in homine ex perfecta complexionis aequalitate. Et eadem etiam ratio est assignanda de hoc quod quaedam animalia sunt homine velociora, cui excellentiae velocitatis repugnat aequalitas humanae complexionis.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32306)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod cornua et ungulae, quae sunt quorundam animalium arma, et spissitudo corii, et multitudo pilorum aut plumarum, quae sunt tegumenta animalium, attestantur abundantiae terrestris elementi; quae repugnat aequalitati et teneritudini complexionis humanae. Et ideo haec homini non competebant. Sed loco horum habet rationem et manus, quibus potest parare sibi arma et tegumenta et alia vitae necessaria, infinitis modis. Unde et manus, in III de anima, dicitur organum organorum. Et hoc etiam magis competebat rationali naturae, quae est infinitarum conceptionum, ut haberet facultatem infinita instrumenta sibi parandi.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32307)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod habere staturam rectam conveniens fuit homini propter quatuor. Primo quidem, quia sensus sunt dati homini non solum ad vitae necessaria procuranda, sicut aliis animalibus; sed etiam ad cognoscendum. Unde, cum cetera animalia non delectentur in sensibilibus nisi per ordinem ad cibos et venerea, solus homo delectatur in ipsa pulchritudine sensibilium secundum seipsam. Et ideo, quia sensus praecipue vigent in facie, alia animalia habent faciem pronam ad terram, quasi ad cibum quaerendum et providendum sibi de victu, homo vero habet faciem erectam, ut per sensus, et praecipue per visum, qui est subtilior et plures differentias rerum ostendit, libere possit ex omni parte sensibilia cognoscere, et caelestia et terrena, ut ex omnibus intelligibilem colligat veritatem. Secundo, ut interiores vires liberius suas operationes habeant, dum cerebrum, in quo quodammodo perficiuntur, non est depressum, sed super omnes partes corporis elevatum. Tertio, quia oporteret quod, si homo haberet pronam staturam, uteretur manibus loco anteriorum pedum. Et sic utilitas manuum ad diversa opera perficienda cessaret. Quarto, quia, si haberet pronam staturam, et uteretur manibus loco anteriorum pedum, oporteret quod cibum caperet ore. Et ita haberet os oblongum, et labia dura et grossa, et linguam etiam duram, ne ab exterioribus laederetur, sicut patet in aliis animalibus. Et talis dispositio omnino impediret locutionem, quae est proprium opus rationis. Et tamen homo staturam rectam habens, maxime distat a plantis. Nam homo habet superius sui, idest caput, versus superius mundi, et inferius sui versus inferius mundi, et ideo est optime dispositus secundum dispositionem totius. Plantae vero habent superius sui versus inferius mundi (nam radices sunt ori proportionales), inferius autem sui versus superius mundi. Animalia vero bruta medio modo, nam superius animalis est pars qua accipit alimentum, inferius autem est pars qua emittit superfluum.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 91 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32308)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod inconvenienter corporis humani productio in Scriptura describatur. Sicut enim corpus humanum est factum a Deo, ita et alia opera sex dierum. Sed in aliis operibus dicitur, *dixit Deus, fiat, et factum est*. Ergo similiter dici debuit de hominis productione.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32309)\n\nPraeterea, corpus humanum a Deo immediate est factum, ut supra dictum est. Ergo inconvenienter dicitur, faciamus hominem.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32310)\n\nPraeterea, forma humani corporis est ipsa anima, quae est spiraculum vitae. Inconvenienter ergo, postquam dixerat, *formavit Deus hominem de limo terrae*, subiunxit, *et inspiravit in faciem eius spiraculum vitae*.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32311)\n\nPraeterea, anima, quae est spiraculum vitae, est in toto corpore, et principaliter in corde. Non ergo debuit dicere, quod *inspiravit in faciem eius spiraculum vitae*.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32312)\n\nPraeterea, sexus masculinus et femininus pertinent ad corpus, imago vero Dei ad animam. Sed anima, secundum Augustinum, fuit facta ante corpus. Inconvenienter ergo cum dixisset, *ad imaginem suam fecit illum*, addidit, *masculum et feminam creavit eos*.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32313)\n\nIn contrarium est auctoritas Scripturae.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32314)\n\nRespondeo dicendum ad primum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit in VI super Gen. ad Litt., non in hoc praeeminet homo aliis rebus, quod Deus ipse fecit hominem, quasi alia ipse non fecerit; cum scriptum sit, *opera manuum tuarum sunt caeli*, et alibi, *aridam fundaverunt manus eius*, sed in hoc quod ad imaginem Dei factus est homo. Utitur tamen Scriptura in productione hominis speciali modo loquendi, ad ostendendum quod alia propter hominem facta sunt. Ea enim quae principaliter intendimus, cum maiori deliberatione et studio consuevimus facere.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32315)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod non est intelligendum Deum Angelis dixisse, faciamus hominem; ut quidam perverse intellexerunt. Sed hoc dicitur ad significandum pluralitatem divinarum personarum, quarum imago expressius invenitur in homine.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32316)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod quidam intellexerunt corpus hominis prius tempore formatum, et postmodum Deum formato iam corpori animam infudisse. Sed contra rationem perfectionis primae institutionis rerum est, quod Deus vel corpus sine anima, vel animam sine corpore fecerit, cum utrumque sit pars humanae naturae. Et hoc etiam est magis inconveniens de corpore, quod dependet ex anima, et non e converso. Et ideo ad hoc excludendum, quidam posuerunt quod, cum dicitur, formavit Deus hominem, intelligitur productio corporis simul cum anima; quod autem additur, et inspiravit in faciem eius spiraculum vitae, intelligitur de spiritu sancto; sicut et dominus insufflavit in apostolos, dicens, accipite spiritum sanctum, Ioan. XX. Sed haec expositio, ut dicit Augustinus in libro de Civ. Dei, excluditur per verba Scripturae. Nam subditur ad praedicta, *et factus est homo in animam viventem*, quod apostolus, I ad Cor. XV, non ad vitam spiritualem, sed ad vitam animalem refert. Per spiraculum ergo vitae intelligitur anima, ut sic quod dicitur, *inspiravit in faciem eius spiraculum vitae*, sit quasi expositio eius quod praemiserat; nam anima est corporis forma.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32317)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, quia operationes vitae magis manifestantur in facie hominis, propter sensus ibi existentes; ideo dicit in faciem hominis inspiratum esse spiraculum vitae.\n\n[I q. 91 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32318)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, omnia opera sex dierum simul sunt facta. Unde animam primi hominis, quam ponit simul factam cum Angelis, non ponit factam ante sextum diem; sed in ipso sexto die ponit esse factam et animam primi hominis in actu, et corpus eius secundum rationes causales. Alii vero doctores ponunt et animam et corpus hominis factum sexto die in actu.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0002","text":"[I q. 91 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32279)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de productione corporis primi hominis. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, de materia ex qua productum est. Secundo, de auctore a quo productum est. Tertio, de dispositione quae ei per productionem est attributa. Quarto, de modo et ordine productionis ipsius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0005","text":"[I q. 91 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32280)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod corpus primi hominis non sit factum de limo terrae. Maioris enim virtutis est facere aliquid ex nihilo, quam ex aliquo, quia plus distat ab actu non ens quam ens in potentia. Sed cum homo sit dignissima creaturarum inferiorum, decuit ut virtus Dei maxime ostenderetur in productione corporis eius. Ergo non debuit fieri ex limo terrae, sed ex nihilo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0007","text":"[I q. 91 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32281)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0008","text":"Praeterea, corpora caelestia sunt nobiliora terrenis. Sed corpus humanum habet maximam nobilitatem, cum perficiatur a nobilissima forma, quae est anima rationalis. Ergo non debuit fieri de corpore terrestri, sed magis de corpore caelesti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0009","text":"[I q. 91 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32282)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0010","text":"Praeterea, ignis et aer sunt nobiliora corpora quam terra et aqua, quod ex eorum subtilitate apparet. Cum igitur corpus humanum sit dignissimum, magis debuit fieri ex igne et ex aere quam ex limo terrae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0011","text":"[I q. 91 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32283)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0012","text":"Praeterea, corpus humanum est compositum ex quatuor elementis. Non ergo est factum ex limo terrae, sed ex omnibus elementis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0013","text":"[I q. 91 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32284)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0014","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, *formavit Deus hominem de limo terrae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0015","text":"[I q. 91 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32285)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum Deus perfectus sit, operibus suis perfectionem dedit secundum eorum modum; secundum illud Deut. XXXII, *Dei perfecta sunt opera*. Ipse autem simpliciter perfectus est, ex hoc quod omnia in se praehabet, non per modum compositionis, sed simpliciter et unite, ut Dionysius dicit, eo modo quo diversi effectus praeexistunt in causa, secundum unam eius essentiam. Ista autem perfectio ad Angelos quidem derivatur, secundum quod omnia sunt in eorum cognitione quae sunt a Deo in natura producta, per formas diversas. Ad hominem vero derivatur inferiori modo huiusmodi perfectio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0017","text":"Non enim in sua cognitione naturali habet omnium naturalium notitiam; sed est ex rebus omnibus quodammodo compositus, dum de genere spiritualium substantiarum habet in se animam rationalem, de similitudine vero caelestium corporum habet elongationem a contrariis per maximam aequalitatem complexionis, elementa vero secundum substantiam. Ita tamen quod superiora elementa praedominantur in eo secundum virtutem, scilicet ignis et aer, quia vita praecipue consistit in calido, quod est ignis, et humido, quod est aeris. Inferiora vero elementa abundant in eo secundum substantiam, aliter enim non posset esse mixtionis aequalitas, nisi inferiora elementa, quae sunt minoris virtutis, secundum quantitatem in homine abundarent. Et ideo dicitur corpus hominis de limo terrae formatum, quia limus dicitur terra aquae permixta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0018","text":"Et propter hoc homo dicitur minor mundus, quia omnes creaturae mundi quodammodo inveniuntur in eo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0019","text":"[I q. 91 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32286)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0020","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod virtus Dei creantis manifestata est in corpore hominis, dum eius materia est per creationem producta. Oportuit autem ut ex materia quatuor elementorum fieret corpus humanum, ut homo haberet convenientiam cum inferioribus corporibus, quasi medium quoddam existens inter spirituales et corporales substantias.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0021","text":"[I q. 91 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32287)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0022","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, quamvis corpus caeleste sit simpliciter nobilius terrestri corpore, tamen quantum ad actus animae rationalis, est minus conveniens. Nam anima rationalis accipit notitiam veritatis quodammodo per sensus; quorum organa formari non possunt ex corpore caelesti, cum sit impassibile. Nec est verum quod quidam dicunt aliquid de quinta essentia materialiter ad compositionem humani corporis advenire, ponentes animam uniri corpori mediante quadam luce. Primo enim, falsum est quod dicunt, lucem esse corpus. Secundo vero, impossibile est aliquid de quinta essentia vel a corpore caelesti dividi, vel elementis permisceri, propter caelestis corporis impassibilitatem. Unde non venit in compositionem mixtorum corporum, nisi secundum suae virtutis effectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0023","text":"[I q. 91 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32288)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0024","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, si ignis et aer, quae sunt maioris virtutis in agendo, etiam secundum quantitatem in compositione humani corporis abundarent, omnino ad se traherent alia, et non posset fieri aequalitas commixtionis, quae est necessaria in compositione hominis ad bonitatem sensus tactus, qui est fundamentum sensuum aliorum. Oportet enim organum cuiuslibet sensus non habere in actu contraria quorum sensus est perceptivus, sed in potentia tantum. Vel ita quod omnino careat toto genere contrariorum, sicut pupilla caret colore, ut sit in potentia ad omnes colores, quod in organo tactus non erat possibile, cum sit compositum ex elementis, quorum qualitates percipit tactus. Vel ita quod organum sit medium inter contraria, ut necesse est in tactu accidere, medium enim est quodammodo in potentia ad extrema.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0025","text":"[I q. 91 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32289)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0026","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod in limo terrae est terra, et aqua conglutinans partes terrae. De aliis autem elementis Scriptura mentionem non fecit, tum quia minus abundant secundum quantitatem in corpore hominis, ut dictum est; tum etiam quia in tota rerum productione, de igne et aere, quae sensu non percipiuntur a rudibus mentionem non fecit Scriptura, quae rudi populo tradebatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0027","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0028","text":"[I q. 91 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32290)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0029","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod corpus humanum non sit immediate a Deo productum. Dicit enim Augustinus, in III de Trin., quod corporalia disponuntur a Deo per angelicam creaturam. Sed corpus humanum formatum fuit ex materia corporali, ut dictum est. Ergo debuit produci mediantibus Angelis, et non immediate a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0030","text":"[I q. 91 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32291)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0031","text":"Praeterea, quod fieri potest virtute creata, non est necessarium quod immediate producatur a Deo. Sed corpus humanum produci potest per virtutem creatam caelestis corporis, nam et quaedam animalia ex putrefactione generantur per virtutem activam corporis caelestis; et Albumasar dicit quod in locis in quibus nimis abundat calor aut frigus, homines non generantur, sed in locis temperatis tantum. Ergo non oportuit quod immediate corpus humanum formaretur a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0032","text":"[I q. 91 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32292)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0033","text":"Praeterea, nihil fit ex materia corporali nisi per aliquam materiae transmutationem. Sed omnis transmutatio corporalis causatur ex motu caelestis corporis, qui est primus motuum. Cum igitur corpus humanum sit productum ex materia corporali, videtur quod ad eius formationem aliquid operatum fuerit corpus caeleste.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0034","text":"[I q. 91 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32293)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0035","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, super Gen. ad Litt., quod homo factus est secundum corpus, in operibus sex dierum, secundum causales rationes quas Deus inseruit creaturae corporali; postmodum vero fuit formatum in actu. Sed quod praeexistit in corporali creatura secundum causales rationes, per aliquam virtutem corpoream produci potest. Ergo corpus humanum productum est aliqua virtute creata, et non immediate a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0036","text":"[I q. 91 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32294)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0037","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Eccli. XVII, *Deus de terra creavit hominem*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0038","text":"[I q. 91 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32295)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0039","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod prima formatio humani corporis non potuit esse per aliquam virtutem creatam, sed immediate a Deo. Posuerunt siquidem aliqui formas quae sunt in materia corporali, a quibusdam formis immaterialibus derivari. Sed hanc opinionem repellit philosophus, in VII Metaphys., per hoc quod formis non competit per se fieri, sed composito, ut supra expositum est; et quia oportet agens esse simile facto non convenit quod forma pura, quae est sine materia, producat formam quae est in materia, quae non fit nisi per hoc quod compositum fit. Et ideo oportet quod forma quae est in materia, sit causa formae quae est in materia, secundum quod compositum a composito generatur. Deus autem, quamvis omnino sit immaterialis, tamen solus est qui sua virtute materiam producere potest creando. Unde ipsius solius est formam producere in materia absque adminiculo praecedentis formae materialis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0040","text":"Et propter hoc, Angeli non possunt transmutare corpora ad formam aliquam, nisi adhibitis seminibus quibusdam, ut Augustinus dicit in III de Trin. Quia igitur corpus humanum nunquam formatum fuerat, cuius virtute per viam generationis aliud simile in specie formaretur, necesse fuit quod primum corpus hominis immediate formaretur a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0041","text":"[I q. 91 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32296)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0042","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, etsi Angeli aliquod ministerium Deo exhibeant in his quae circa corpora operatur; aliqua tamen Deus in creatura corporea facit, quae nullo modo Angeli facere possunt; sicut quod suscitat mortuos, et illuminat caecos. Secundum quam virtutem etiam corpus primi hominis de limo terrae formavit. Potuit tamen fieri ut aliquod ministerium in formatione corporis primi hominis Angeli exhiberent; sicut exhibebunt in ultima resurrectione, pulveres colligendo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0043","text":"[I q. 91 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32297)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0044","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod animalia perfecta, quae generantur ex semine, non possunt generari per solam virtutem caelestis corporis, ut Avicenna fingit; licet ad eorum generationem naturalem cooperetur virtus caelestis corporis, prout philosophus dicit, in II Physic., quod *homo generat hominem ex materia, et sol*. Et exinde est quod exigitur locus temperatus ad generationem hominum et aliorum animalium perfectorum. Sufficit autem virtus caelestium corporum ad generandum quaedam animalia imperfectiora ex materia disposita, manifestum est enim quod plura requiruntur ad productionem rei perfectae, quam ad productionem rei imperfectae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0045","text":"[I q. 91 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32298)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0046","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod motus caeli est causa transmutationum naturalium, non tamen transmutationum quae fiunt praeter naturae ordinem, et sola virtute divina, sicut quod mortui resuscitantur, quod caeci illuminantur. Quibus est simile quod homo ex limo terrae formatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0047","text":"[I q. 91 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32299)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0048","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod secundum rationes causales in creaturis dicitur aliquid praeexistere dupliciter. Uno modo, secundum potentiam activam et passivam, ut non solum ex materia praeexistenti fieri possit, sed etiam ut aliqua praeexistens creatura hoc facere possit. Alio modo, secundum potentiam passivam tantum, ut scilicet de materia praeexistenti fieri possit a Deo. Et hoc modo, secundum Augustinum, corpus hominis praeextitit in operibus productis secundum causales rationes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0049","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0050","text":"[I q. 91 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32300)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0051","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod corpus hominis non habuerit convenientem dispositionem. Cum enim homo sit nobilissimum animalium, corpus hominis debuit esse dispositum optime ad ea quae sunt propria animalis, scilicet ad sensum et motum. Sed quaedam animalia inveniuntur acutioris sensus quam homo, et velocioris motus; sicut canes melius odorant, et aves velocius moventur. Ergo corpus hominis non est convenienter dispositum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0052","text":"[I q. 91 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32301)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0053","text":"Praeterea, perfectum est cui nihil deest. Sed plura desunt humano corpori quam corporibus aliorum animalium, quae habent tegumenta et arma naturalia ad sui protectionem, quae homini desunt. Ergo corpus humanum est imperfectissime dispositum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0054","text":"[I q. 91 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32302)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0055","text":"Praeterea, homo plus distat a plantis quam ab animalibus brutis. Sed plantae habent staturam rectam; animalia autem bruta pronam. Ergo homo non debuit habere staturam rectam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0056","text":"[I q. 91 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32303)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0057","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Eccle. VII, *Deus fecit hominem rectum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0058","text":"[I q. 91 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32304)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0059","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod omnes res naturales productae sunt ab arte divina, unde sunt quodammodo artificiata ipsius Dei. Quilibet autem artifex intendit suo operi dispositionem optimam inducere, non simpliciter, sed per comparationem ad finem. Et si talis dispositio habet secum adiunctum aliquem defectum, artifex non curat. Sicut artifex qui facit serram ad secandum, facit eam ex ferro, ut sit idonea ad secandum; nec curat eam facere ex vitro, quae est pulchrior materia, quia talis pulchritudo esset impedimentum finis. Sic igitur Deus unicuique rei naturali dedit optimam dispositionem, non quidem simpliciter, sed secundum ordinem ad proprium finem. Et hoc est quod philosophus dicit, in II Physic., *et quia dignius est sic, non tamen simpliciter, sed ad uniuscuiusque substantiam*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0060","text":"Finis autem proximus humani corporis est anima rationalis et operationes ipsius, materia enim est propter formam, et instrumenta propter actiones agentis. Dico ergo quod Deus instituit corpus humanum in optima dispositione secundum convenientiam ad talem formam et ad tales operationes. Et si aliquis defectus in dispositione humani corporis esse videtur, considerandum est quod talis defectus sequitur ex necessitate materiae, ad ea quae requiruntur in corpore ut sit debita proportio ipsius ad animam et ad animae operationes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0061","text":"[I q. 91 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32305)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0062","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod tactus, qui est fundamentum aliorum sensuum, est perfectior in homine quam in aliquo alio animali, et propter hoc oportuit quod homo haberet temperatissimam complexionem inter omnia animalia. Praecedit etiam homo omnia alia animalia, quantum ad vires sensitivas interiores; sicut ex supra dictis apparet. Ex quadam autem necessitate contingit quod, quantum ad aliquos exteriores sensus, homo ab aliis animalibus deficiat. Sicut homo, inter omnia animalia, habet pessimum olfactum. Necessarium enim fuit quod homo, inter omnia animalia, respectu sui corporis haberet maximum cerebrum, tum ut liberius in eo perficerentur operationes interiorum virium sensitivarum, quae sunt necessariae ad intellectus operationem, ut supra dictum est; tum etiam ut frigiditas cerebri temperaret calorem cordis, quem necesse est in homine abundare, ad hoc quod homo sit rectae staturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0063","text":"Magnitudo autem cerebri, propter eius humiditatem, est impedimentum olfactus, qui requirit siccitatem. Et similiter potest assignari ratio quare quaedam animalia sunt acutioris visus et subtilioris auditus quam homo, propter impedimentum horum sensuum quod necesse est consequi in homine ex perfecta complexionis aequalitate. Et eadem etiam ratio est assignanda de hoc quod quaedam animalia sunt homine velociora, cui excellentiae velocitatis repugnat aequalitas humanae complexionis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0064","text":"[I q. 91 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32306)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0065","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod cornua et ungulae, quae sunt quorundam animalium arma, et spissitudo corii, et multitudo pilorum aut plumarum, quae sunt tegumenta animalium, attestantur abundantiae terrestris elementi; quae repugnat aequalitati et teneritudini complexionis humanae. Et ideo haec homini non competebant. Sed loco horum habet rationem et manus, quibus potest parare sibi arma et tegumenta et alia vitae necessaria, infinitis modis. Unde et manus, in III de anima, dicitur organum organorum. Et hoc etiam magis competebat rationali naturae, quae est infinitarum conceptionum, ut haberet facultatem infinita instrumenta sibi parandi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0066","text":"[I q. 91 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32307)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0067","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod habere staturam rectam conveniens fuit homini propter quatuor. Primo quidem, quia sensus sunt dati homini non solum ad vitae necessaria procuranda, sicut aliis animalibus; sed etiam ad cognoscendum. Unde, cum cetera animalia non delectentur in sensibilibus nisi per ordinem ad cibos et venerea, solus homo delectatur in ipsa pulchritudine sensibilium secundum seipsam. Et ideo, quia sensus praecipue vigent in facie, alia animalia habent faciem pronam ad terram, quasi ad cibum quaerendum et providendum sibi de victu, homo vero habet faciem erectam, ut per sensus, et praecipue per visum, qui est subtilior et plures differentias rerum ostendit, libere possit ex omni parte sensibilia cognoscere, et caelestia et terrena, ut ex omnibus intelligibilem colligat veritatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0068","text":"Secundo, ut interiores vires liberius suas operationes habeant, dum cerebrum, in quo quodammodo perficiuntur, non est depressum, sed super omnes partes corporis elevatum. Tertio, quia oporteret quod, si homo haberet pronam staturam, uteretur manibus loco anteriorum pedum. Et sic utilitas manuum ad diversa opera perficienda cessaret. Quarto, quia, si haberet pronam staturam, et uteretur manibus loco anteriorum pedum, oporteret quod cibum caperet ore. Et ita haberet os oblongum, et labia dura et grossa, et linguam etiam duram, ne ab exterioribus laederetur, sicut patet in aliis animalibus. Et talis dispositio omnino impediret locutionem, quae est proprium opus rationis. Et tamen homo staturam rectam habens, maxime distat a plantis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0069","text":"Nam homo habet superius sui, idest caput, versus superius mundi, et inferius sui versus inferius mundi, et ideo est optime dispositus secundum dispositionem totius. Plantae vero habent superius sui versus inferius mundi (nam radices sunt ori proportionales), inferius autem sui versus superius mundi. Animalia vero bruta medio modo, nam superius animalis est pars qua accipit alimentum, inferius autem est pars qua emittit superfluum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0070","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0071","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32308)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0072","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod inconvenienter corporis humani productio in Scriptura describatur. Sicut enim corpus humanum est factum a Deo, ita et alia opera sex dierum. Sed in aliis operibus dicitur, *dixit Deus, fiat, et factum est*. Ergo similiter dici debuit de hominis productione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0073","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32309)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0074","text":"Praeterea, corpus humanum a Deo immediate est factum, ut supra dictum est. Ergo inconvenienter dicitur, faciamus hominem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0075","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32310)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0076","text":"Praeterea, forma humani corporis est ipsa anima, quae est spiraculum vitae. Inconvenienter ergo, postquam dixerat, *formavit Deus hominem de limo terrae*, subiunxit, *et inspiravit in faciem eius spiraculum vitae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0077","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32311)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0078","text":"Praeterea, anima, quae est spiraculum vitae, est in toto corpore, et principaliter in corde. Non ergo debuit dicere, quod *inspiravit in faciem eius spiraculum vitae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0079","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32312)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0080","text":"Praeterea, sexus masculinus et femininus pertinent ad corpus, imago vero Dei ad animam. Sed anima, secundum Augustinum, fuit facta ante corpus. Inconvenienter ergo cum dixisset, *ad imaginem suam fecit illum*, addidit, *masculum et feminam creavit eos*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0081","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32313)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0082","text":"In contrarium est auctoritas Scripturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0083","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32314)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0084","text":"Respondeo dicendum ad primum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit in VI super Gen. ad Litt., non in hoc praeeminet homo aliis rebus, quod Deus ipse fecit hominem, quasi alia ipse non fecerit; cum scriptum sit, *opera manuum tuarum sunt caeli*, et alibi, *aridam fundaverunt manus eius*, sed in hoc quod ad imaginem Dei factus est homo. Utitur tamen Scriptura in productione hominis speciali modo loquendi, ad ostendendum quod alia propter hominem facta sunt. Ea enim quae principaliter intendimus, cum maiori deliberatione et studio consuevimus facere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0085","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32315)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0086","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod non est intelligendum Deum Angelis dixisse, faciamus hominem; ut quidam perverse intellexerunt. Sed hoc dicitur ad significandum pluralitatem divinarum personarum, quarum imago expressius invenitur in homine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0087","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32316)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0088","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod quidam intellexerunt corpus hominis prius tempore formatum, et postmodum Deum formato iam corpori animam infudisse. Sed contra rationem perfectionis primae institutionis rerum est, quod Deus vel corpus sine anima, vel animam sine corpore fecerit, cum utrumque sit pars humanae naturae. Et hoc etiam est magis inconveniens de corpore, quod dependet ex anima, et non e converso. Et ideo ad hoc excludendum, quidam posuerunt quod, cum dicitur, formavit Deus hominem, intelligitur productio corporis simul cum anima; quod autem additur, et inspiravit in faciem eius spiraculum vitae, intelligitur de spiritu sancto; sicut et dominus insufflavit in apostolos, dicens, accipite spiritum sanctum, Ioan. XX. Sed haec expositio, ut dicit Augustinus in libro de Civ. Dei, excluditur per verba Scripturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0089","text":"Nam subditur ad praedicta, *et factus est homo in animam viventem*, quod apostolus, I ad Cor. XV, non ad vitam spiritualem, sed ad vitam animalem refert. Per spiraculum ergo vitae intelligitur anima, ut sic quod dicitur, *inspiravit in faciem eius spiraculum vitae*, sit quasi expositio eius quod praemiserat; nam anima est corporis forma.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0090","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32317)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0091","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, quia operationes vitae magis manifestantur in facie hominis, propter sensus ibi existentes; ideo dicit in faciem hominis inspiratum esse spiraculum vitae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0092","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32318)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0093","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, omnia opera sex dierum simul sunt facta. Unde animam primi hominis, quam ponit simul factam cum Angelis, non ponit factam ante sextum diem; sed in ipso sexto die ponit esse factam et animam primi hominis in actu, et corpus eius secundum rationes causales. Alii vero doctores ponunt et animam et corpus hominis factum sexto die in actu.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"66c9ef1d320ea6ed34e964814e16b80e25263ae98fa17006dac5c9079b5f36ed","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T152443986167Z_2b5bf5e0","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 91","source_sha256":"66c9ef1d320ea6ed34e964814e16b80e25263ae98fa17006dac5c9079b5f36ed","sha256":"5da471d26c8a6aaed26ccd4df4417c3101f73afcb82067f17339ced4d0f17280","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:24:43.986229+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0002","text":"[I q. 91 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32279)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the production of the body of the first man. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, concerning the matter from which it was produced. Second, concerning the agent by whom it was produced. Third, concerning the disposition attributed to it through its production. Fourth, concerning the mode and order of its production.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0005","text":"[I q. 91 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32280)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the body of the first man was not made from the slime of the earth. For it is of greater power to make something from nothing than from something, because non-being is farther from actuality than being in potency. But since man is the most worthy of the lower creatures, it was fitting that the power of God should be most greatly displayed in the production of his body. Therefore, it ought not to have been made from the slime of the earth, but from nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0007","text":"[I q. 91 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32281)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0008","text":"Further, heavenly bodies are nobler than earthly ones. But the human body possesses the greatest nobility, since it is perfected by the noblest form, which is the rational soul. Therefore, it ought not to have been made from an earthly body, but rather from a heavenly body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0009","text":"[I q. 91 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32282)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0010","text":"Further, fire and air are nobler bodies than earth and water, as is apparent from their subtlety. Therefore, since the human body is most worthy, it ought rather to have been made from fire and air than from the slime of the earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0011","text":"[I q. 91 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32283)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0012","text":"Further, the human body is composed of the four elements. Therefore, it was not made from the slime of the earth, but from all the elements.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0013","text":"[I q. 91 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32284)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0014","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Gen. II, *God formed man from the slime of the earth*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0015","text":"[I q. 91 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32285)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0016","text":"I answer that, since God is perfect, He bestowed perfection upon His works according to their mode; according to that of Deut. XXXII, *the works of God are perfect*. Now He Himself is simply perfect, because He prepossesses all things in Himself, not by way of composition, but simply and unitedly, as Dionysius says, in the manner in which diverse effects pre-exist in a cause according to its one essence. But this perfection is indeed derived to the angels, insofar as all things that are produced by God in nature are in their cognition through diverse forms. Yet such perfection is derived to man in an inferior way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0017","text":"For in his natural cognition he does not have knowledge of all natural things; but he is in a certain way composed from all things, since from the genus of spiritual substances he has within himself a rational soul; from the likeness of heavenly bodies, he has remoteness from contraries through the greatest equality of complexion; and the elements according to substance. Yet in such a way that the higher elements, namely fire and air, predominate in him according to power, because life consists especially in the hot, which belongs to fire, and the moist, which belongs to air. But the lower elements abound in him according to substance; otherwise, there could not be equality of mixture unless the lower elements, which are of lesser power, abounded in man according to quantity. And therefore the body of man is said to have been formed from the slime of the earth, because slime is called earth mixed with water.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0018","text":"And for this reason man is called a lesser world, because all the creatures of the world are found in him in a certain way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0019","text":"[I q. 91 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32286)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0020","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the power of God creating was manifested in the body of man, inasmuch as its matter was produced by creation. But it was necessary that the human body be made from the matter of the four elements, so that man might have a fittingness with lower bodies, existing as a kind of mean between spiritual and corporeal substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0021","text":"[I q. 91 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32287)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0022","text":"To the second, it must be said that, although a heavenly body is simply nobler than an earthly body, nevertheless it is less suitable with respect to the acts of the rational soul. For the rational soul receives knowledge of truth in a certain way through the senses, whose organs cannot be formed from a heavenly body, since it is impassible. Nor is it true, as some say, that something of the fifth essence comes materially into the composition of the human body, holding that the soul is united to the body through a certain light. For first, what they say—that light is a body—is false. Second, it is impossible for anything of the fifth essence or of a heavenly body either to be divided or to be mixed with the elements, because of the impassibility of the heavenly body. Hence it does not enter into the composition of mixed bodies, except according to the effect of its power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0023","text":"[I q. 91 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32288)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0024","text":"To the third, it must be said that if fire and air, which are of greater power in acting, also abounded according to quantity in the composition of the human body, they would wholly draw the other elements to themselves, and equality of mixture could not come about, which is necessary in the composition of man for the goodness of the sense of touch, which is the foundation of the other senses. For the organ of any sense ought not actually to possess the contraries of which that sense is perceptive, but only potentially. Or it must either wholly lack the entire genus of contraries, as the pupil lacks color, so that it may be in potency to all colors—which was not possible in the organ of touch, since it is composed of elements whose qualities touch perceives—or the organ must be intermediate between the contraries, as must occur in touch; for the intermediate is in a certain way in potency to the extremes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0025","text":"[I q. 91 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32289)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0026","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that in the slime of the earth there is earth, and water binding together the parts of the earth. But Scripture made no mention of the other elements, both because they are less abundant in quantity in the human body, as has been said; and also because, in the whole production of things, Scripture made no mention of fire and air, which are not perceived by the senses of the unlearned, to whom Scripture was handed down.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0027","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0028","text":"[I q. 91 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32290)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0029","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the human body was not produced immediately by God. For Augustine says, in *On the Trinity* III, that bodily things are disposed by God through angelic creatures. But the human body was formed from bodily matter, as has been said. Therefore it ought to have been produced through the mediation of angels, and not immediately by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0030","text":"[I q. 91 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32291)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0031","text":"Further, what can be brought about by created power need not be produced immediately by God. But the human body can be produced by the created power of a heavenly body; for certain animals are also generated from putrefaction through the active power of a heavenly body; and Albumasar says that in places where heat or cold is too abundant, human beings are not generated, but only in temperate places. Therefore it was not necessary that the human body be formed immediately by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0032","text":"[I q. 91 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32292)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0033","text":"Further, nothing is made from bodily matter except through some transformation of matter. But every bodily transformation is caused by the motion of a heavenly body, which is the first of motions. Therefore, since the human body was produced from bodily matter, it seems that a heavenly body effected something in its formation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0034","text":"[I q. 91 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32293)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0035","text":"Further, Augustine says, in *The Literal Meaning of Genesis*, that man was made as regards the body, in the works of the six days, according to the causal reasons which God implanted in bodily creation; but afterward he was formed in act. But what pre-exists in bodily creation according to causal reasons can be produced by some bodily power. Therefore the human body was produced by some created power, and not immediately by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0036","text":"[I q. 91 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32294)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0037","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Ecclesiasticus XVII, *God created man from the earth*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0038","text":"[I q. 91 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32295)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0039","text":"I answer that it must be said that the first formation of the human body could not have been by any created power, but immediately by God. For some have held that the forms which are in bodily matter are derived from certain immaterial forms. But the Philosopher refutes this opinion, in *Metaphysics* VII, on the ground that to forms it does not belong, through themselves, to be made, but rather to the composite, as was explained above; and because the agent must be like the thing made, it is not fitting that a pure form, which is without matter, should produce a form which is in matter, which does not come to be except by the composite coming to be. And therefore a form which is in matter must be the cause of a form which is in matter, insofar as composite is generated from composite. But God, although He is altogether immaterial, is nevertheless alone able by His power to produce matter by creating. Hence it belongs to Him alone to produce a form in matter without the aid of a preceding material form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0040","text":"And for this reason angels cannot transform bodies into any form unless certain seeds are employed, as Augustine says in *On the Trinity* III. Therefore, since a human body had never been formed by whose power another similar in species might be formed by way of generation, it was necessary that the first body of man be formed immediately by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0041","text":"[I q. 91 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32296)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0042","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that although angels render some ministry to God in those things which He works concerning bodies, nevertheless God does certain things in bodily creation which angels can in no way do; for example, that He raises the dead and gives sight to the blind. By the same power He also formed the body of the first man from the slime of the earth. Yet it could have been that angels rendered some ministry in the formation of the body of the first man, just as they will render it in the final resurrection, by gathering the dusts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0043","text":"[I q. 91 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32297)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0044","text":"To the second, it must be said that perfect animals, which are generated from seed, cannot be generated by the power of a heavenly body alone, as Avicenna imagines; although the power of a heavenly body cooperates in their natural generation, insofar as the Philosopher says, in *Physics* II, that *man generates man from matter, and the sun*. Hence it is that a temperate place is required for the generation of human beings and other perfect animals. But the power of heavenly bodies suffices to generate certain more imperfect animals from disposed matter; for it is evident that more things are required for the production of a perfect thing than for the production of an imperfect thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0045","text":"[I q. 91 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32298)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0046","text":"To the third, it must be said that the motion of heaven is the cause of natural transformations, but not of transformations which occur outside the order of nature and by divine power alone, such as the raising of the dead and the giving of sight to the blind. Similar to these is the formation of man from the slime of the earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0047","text":"[I q. 91 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32299)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0048","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that something is said to pre-exist in creatures according to causal reasons in two ways. In one way, according to active and passive power, so that it can not only be made from pre-existing matter, but also so that some pre-existing creature can make it. In another way, according to passive power alone, namely, so that it can be made by God from pre-existing matter. And in this way, according to Augustine, the body of man pre-existed in the works produced according to causal reasons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0049","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0050","text":"[I q. 91 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32300)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0051","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that the human body did not have a suitable disposition. For since man is the noblest of animals, the human body ought to have been disposed in the best way for those things that are proper to an animal, namely, sensation and movement. But some animals are found to have keener sensation than man, and swifter movement; thus dogs smell better, and birds move more swiftly. Therefore the human body is not suitably disposed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0052","text":"[I q. 91 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32301)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0053","text":"Further, that to which nothing is lacking is perfect. But more things are lacking to the human body than to the bodies of other animals, which have natural coverings and weapons for their protection, things which man lacks. Therefore the human body is disposed in the most imperfect way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0054","text":"[I q. 91 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32302)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0055","text":"Further, man differs more from plants than from brute animals. But plants have an upright stature, whereas brute animals have a prone one. Therefore man ought not to have had an upright stature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0056","text":"[I q. 91 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32303)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0057","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Eccles. VII, *God made man upright*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0058","text":"[I q. 91 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32304)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0059","text":"I answer that it must be said that all natural things are produced by divine art, whence they are in a certain manner artifacts of God Himself. Now every craftsman intends to introduce into his work the best disposition, not absolutely, but in relation to its end. And if such a disposition has some defect conjoined with it, the craftsman does not care. Thus, a craftsman making a saw for cutting makes it of iron, so that it may be fit for cutting; nor does he care to make it of glass, which is a more beautiful material, because such beauty would be an impediment to the end. Thus, therefore, God gave to each natural thing the best disposition, not indeed absolutely, but according to its order to its proper end. And this is what the Philosopher says in *Physics* II: *and because it is more fitting thus, not however absolutely, but in relation to the substance of each thing*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0060","text":"But the proximate end of the human body is the rational soul and its operations; for matter exists for the sake of form, and instruments for the actions of an agent. Therefore I say that God established the human body in the best disposition according to its suitability for such a form and such operations. And if any defect seems to exist in the disposition of the human body, it must be considered that such a defect follows from the necessity of matter, in relation to those things required in the body so that there may be its due proportion to the soul and to the operations of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0061","text":"[I q. 91 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32305)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0062","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that touch, which is the foundation of the other senses, is more perfect in man than in any other animal; and on this account it was necessary that man should have the most temperate complexion among all animals. Man also surpasses all other animals with respect to the interior sensitive powers, as is clear from what has been said above. But from a certain necessity it comes about that, with respect to certain external senses, man falls short of other animals. Thus man, among all animals, has the worst sense of smell. For it was necessary that man, among all animals, should have, in relation to his body, the largest brain: both so that the operations of the interior sensitive powers, which are necessary for the operation of the intellect, as was said above, might be carried out in it more freely; and also so that the coldness of the brain might temper the heat of the heart, which must abound in man in order that man may be of upright stature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0063","text":"But the size of the brain, because of its moisture, is an impediment to smell, which requires dryness. And similarly, a reason can be assigned why certain animals have keener sight and more acute hearing than man, on account of the impediment to these senses which must follow in man from the perfect equality of complexion. And the same reason must also be assigned for the fact that certain animals are swifter than man, since equality of human complexion is incompatible with this excellence of swiftness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0064","text":"[I q. 91 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32306)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0065","text":"To the second, it must be said that horns and hoofs, which are the weapons of certain animals, and thickness of hide, and abundance of hair or feathers, which are the coverings of animals, attest an abundance of the earthly element; and this is incompatible with the equality and tenderness of human complexion. And therefore these did not befit man. But in place of these he has reason and hands, by which he can provide himself with weapons and coverings and the other necessities of life, in infinitely many ways. Hence too the hand, in *On the Soul* III, is called the organ of organs. And this also was more fitting to a rational nature, which is of infinite conceptions, namely, that it should have the capacity to provide itself with infinite instruments.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0066","text":"[I q. 91 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32307)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0067","text":"To the third, it must be said that it was fitting for man to have an upright stature for four reasons. First, because the senses are given to man not only for procuring the necessities of life, as to other animals, but also for knowing. Hence, since the other animals take delight in sensible things only in relation to food and sexual matters, man alone takes delight in the very beauty of sensible things in itself. And therefore, because the senses flourish especially in the face, the other animals have their faces inclined toward the earth, as though to seek food and provide themselves with sustenance; but man has an erect face, so that through the senses, and especially through sight, which is more subtle and displays more differences of things, he may freely be able to know sensibles from every side, both heavenly and earthly, so as to gather intelligible truth from all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0068","text":"Second, so that the interior powers may have their operations more freely, since the brain, in which they are in some manner carried out, is not depressed but raised above all parts of the body. Third, because, if man had a prone stature, he would use his hands in place of his forefeet. And thus the usefulness of the hands for carrying out diverse works would cease. Fourth, because, if he had a prone stature and used his hands in place of his forefeet, it would be necessary for him to take food with his mouth. And thus he would have an elongated mouth, hard and thick lips, and likewise a hard tongue, lest they be injured by external things, as is evident in other animals. And such a disposition would altogether impede speech, which is the proper work of reason. And yet man, having an upright stature, differs most greatly from plants.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0069","text":"For man has his upper part, that is, his head, toward the upper part of the world, and his lower part toward the lower part of the world; and therefore he is best disposed according to the disposition of the whole. But plants have their upper part toward the lower part of the world (for their roots are proportionate to the mouth), and their lower part toward the upper part of the world. Brute animals, however, are in an intermediate manner; for the upper part of an animal is that part by which it takes in nourishment, while the lower part is that by which it emits what is superfluous.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0070","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0071","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32308)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0072","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that the production of the human body is described unsuitably in Scripture. For just as the human body was made by God, so too were the other works of the six days. But concerning the other works it is said, *God said, Let it be, and it was made*. Therefore something similar ought to have been said concerning the production of man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0073","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32309)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0074","text":"Moreover, the human body was made immediately by God, as was said above. Therefore it is unfittingly said, “Let us make man.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0075","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32310)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0076","text":"Moreover, the form of the human body is the soul itself, which is the breath of life. Therefore, after saying, *God formed man from the slime of the earth*, it was unfittingly added, *and breathed into his face the breath of life*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0077","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32311)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0078","text":"Moreover, the soul, which is the breath of life, is in the whole body, and principally in the heart. Therefore He ought not to have said that He *breathed into his face the breath of life*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0079","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32312)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0080","text":"Moreover, the male and female sexes pertain to the body, but the image of God to the soul. But, according to Augustine, the soul was made before the body. Therefore, after saying, *in His own image He made him*, it was unfittingly added, *male and female He created them*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0081","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32313)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0082","text":"On the contrary, there is the authority of Scripture.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0083","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32314)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0084","text":"I answer that, with regard to the first, as Augustine says in Book VI of *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, man does not surpass other things in this, that God Himself made man, as though He had not Himself made other things; since it is written, *the heavens are the works of Thy hands*, and elsewhere, *His hands founded the dry land*; but rather in this, that man was made in the image of God. Nevertheless, Scripture employs a special manner of speaking in the production of man, in order to show that other things were made for man's sake. For those things which we chiefly intend, we are accustomed to make with greater deliberation and care.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0085","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32315)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0086","text":"To the second, it must be said that it is not to be understood that God said to the angels, “Let us make man,” as some have perversely understood. Rather, this is said to signify the plurality of the divine persons, whose image is found more expressly in man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0087","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32316)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0088","text":"To the third, it must be said that some have understood the body of man to have been formed first in time, and that afterward God infused a soul into the body already formed. But it is contrary to the principle of the perfection of the first institution of things that God should have made either a body without a soul or a soul without a body, since each is a part of human nature. And this is even more unfitting in the case of the body, which depends upon the soul, and not conversely. Therefore, in order to exclude this, some held that, when it is said, God formed man, the production of the body together with the soul is understood; while what is added, and breathed into his face the breath of life, is understood of the Holy Spirit, just as the Lord also breathed upon the apostles, saying, Receive the Holy Spirit, John XX. But this exposition, as Augustine says in the book *On the City of God*, is excluded by the words of Scripture.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0089","text":"For there is added to the foregoing, *and man was made into a living soul*, which the Apostle, in I Corinthians XV, refers not to spiritual life but to animal life. Therefore, by the breath of life the soul is understood, so that the statement, *He breathed into his face the breath of life*, is, as it were, an exposition of what he had previously set forth; for the soul is the form of the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0090","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32317)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0091","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, because the operations of life are manifested more clearly in the face of man, on account of the senses existing there, it is therefore said that the breath of life was breathed into the face of man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0092","text":"[I q. 91 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32318)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0093","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that, according to Augustine, all the works of the six days were made simultaneously. Hence he does not hold that the soul of the first man, which he posits as made simultaneously with the angels, was made before the sixth day; but he holds that on the sixth day itself both the soul of the first man was made in act and his body according to causal reasons. Other doctors, however, hold that both the soul and the body of man were made in act on the sixth day.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_83:P0093"]}],"source_digest":"b10c1b4c5b4b3b4e8d5b9fcc785c63ee9f7de5ab6f2f317f22e65fe0588a913e","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 91"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 91","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84","title":"First Part · Question 92","order":93,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_84","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 92 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32319)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de productione mulieris. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum in illa rerum productione debuerit mulier produci. Secundo, utrum debuerit fieri de viro. Tertio, utrum de costa viri. Quarto, utrum facta fuerit immediate a Deo.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 92 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32320)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod mulier non debuit produci in prima rerum productione. Dicit enim philosophus, in libro de Generat. Animal., quod *femina est mas occasionatus*. Sed nihil occasionatum et deficiens debuit esse in prima rerum institutione. Ergo in illa prima rerum institutione mulier producenda non fuit.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32321)\n\nPraeterea, subiectio et minoratio ex peccato est subsecuta, nam, ad mulierem dictum est post peccatum, Gen. III, *sub viri potestate eris*; et Gregorius dicit quod, *ubi non delinquimus, omnes pares sumus*. Sed mulier naturaliter est minoris virtutis et dignitatis quam vir, semper enim honorabilius est agens patiente, ut dicit Augustinus XII super Gen. ad Litt. Ergo non debuit mulier produci in prima rerum productione ante peccatum.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32322)\n\nPraeterea, occasiones peccatorum sunt amputandae. Sed Deus praescivit quod mulier esset futura viro in occasionem peccati. Ergo non debuit mulierem producere.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32323)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, *non est bonum hominem esse solum; faciamus ei adiutorium simile sibi*.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32324)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necessarium fuit feminam fieri, sicut Scriptura dicit, in adiutorium viri, non quidem in adiutorium alicuius alterius operis, ut quidam dixerunt, cum ad quodlibet aliud opus convenientius iuvari possit vir per alium virum quam per mulierem; sed in adiutorium generationis. Quod manifestius videri potest, si in viventibus modus generationis consideretur. Sunt enim quaedam viventia, quae in seipsis non habent virtutem activam generationis, sed ab agente alterius speciei generantur; sicut plantae et animalia quae generantur sine semine ex materia convenienti per virtutem activam caelestium corporum. Quaedam vero habent virtutem generationis activam et passivam coniunctam; sicut accidit in plantis quae generantur ex semine. Non enim est in plantis aliquod nobilius opus vitae quam generatio, unde convenienter omni tempore in eis virtuti passivae coniungitur virtus activa generationis. Animalibus vero perfectis competit virtus activa generationis secundum sexum masculinum, virtus vero passiva secundum sexum femininum. Et quia est aliquod opus vitae nobilius in animalibus quam generatio, ad quod eorum vita principaliter ordinatur; ideo non omni tempore sexus masculinus feminino coniungitur in animalibus perfectis, sed solum tempore coitus; ut imaginemur per coitum sic fieri unum ex mare et femina, sicut in planta omni tempore coniunguntur vis masculina et feminina, etsi in quibusdam plus abundet una harum, in quibusdam plus altera. Homo autem adhuc ordinatur ad nobilius opus vitae, quod est intelligere. Et ideo adhuc in homine debuit esse maiori ratione distinctio utriusque virtutis, ut seorsum produceretur femina a mare, et tamen carnaliter coniungerentur in unum ad generationis opus. Et ideo statim post formationem mulieris, dicitur Gen. II, *erunt duo in carne una*.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32325)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod per respectum ad naturam particularem, femina est aliquid deficiens et occasionatum. Quia virtus activa quae est in semine maris, intendit producere sibi simile perfectum, secundum masculinum sexum, sed quod femina generetur, hoc est propter virtutis activae debilitatem, vel propter aliquam materiae indispositionem, vel etiam propter aliquam transmutationem ab extrinseco, puta a ventis Australibus, qui sunt humidi, ut dicitur in libro de Generat. Animal. Sed per comparationem ad naturam universalem, femina non est aliquid occasionatum, sed est de intentione naturae ad opus generationis ordinata. Intentio autem naturae universalis dependet ex Deo, qui est universalis auctor naturae. Et ideo instituendo naturam, non solum marem, sed etiam feminam produxit.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32326)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod duplex est subiectio. Una servilis, secundum quam praesidens utitur subiecto ad sui ipsius utilitatem et talis subiectio introducta est post peccatum. Est autem alia subiectio oeconomica vel civilis, secundum quam praesidens utitur subiectis ad eorum utilitatem et bonum. Et ista subiectio fuisset etiam ante peccatum, defuisset enim bonum ordinis in humana multitudine, si quidam per alios sapientiores gubernati non fuissent. Et sic ex tali subiectione naturaliter femina subiecta est viro, quia naturaliter in homine magis abundat discretio rationis. Nec inaequalitas hominum excluditur per innocentiae statum, ut infra dicetur.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32327)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, si omnia ex quibus homo sumpsit occasionem peccandi, Deus subtraxisset a mundo, remansisset universum imperfectum. Nec debuit bonum commune tolli, ut vitaretur particulare malum, praesertim cum Deus sit adeo potens, ut quodlibet malum possit ordinare in bonum.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 92 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32328)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod mulier non debuit fieri ex viro. Sexus enim communis est homini et aliis animalibus. Sed in aliis animalibus feminae non sunt factae ex maribus. Ergo nec in homine fieri debuit.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32329)\n\nPraeterea, eorum quae sunt eiusdem speciei, eadem est materia. Sed mas et femina sunt eiusdem speciei. Cum igitur vir fuerit factus ex limo terrae, ex eodem debuit fieri femina, et non ex viro.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32330)\n\nPraeterea, mulier facta est in adiutorium viro ad generationem. Sed nimia propinquitas reddit personam ad hoc ineptam, unde personae propinquae a matrimonio excluduntur, ut patet Levit. XVIII. Ergo mulier non debuit fieri ex viro.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32331)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Eccli. XVII, creavit ex ipso, scilicet viro, adiutorium sibi simile, idest mulierem.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32332)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod conveniens fuit mulierem, in prima rerum institutione, ex viro formari, magis quam in aliis animalibus. Primo quidem, ut in hoc quaedam dignitas primo homini servaretur, ut, secundum Dei similitudinem, esset ipse principium totius suae speciei, sicut Deus est principium totius universi. Unde et Paulus dicit, Act. XVII, quod *Deus fecit ex uno omne genus hominum*. Secundo, ut vir magis diligeret mulierem, et ei inseparabilius inhaereret, dum cognosceret eam ex se esse productam. Unde dicitur Gen. II, *de viro sumpta est, quamobrem relinquet homo patrem et matrem, et adhaerebit uxori suae*. Et hoc maxime necessarium fuit in specie humana, in qua mas et femina commanent per totam vitam, quod non contingit in aliis animalibus. Tertio quia, ut philosophus dicit in VIII Ethic., mas et femina coniunguntur in hominibus non solum propter necessitatem generationis, ut in aliis animalibus; sed etiam propter domesticam vitam, in qua sunt alia opera viri et feminae, et in qua vir est caput mulieris. Unde convenienter ex viro formata est femina, sicut ex suo principio. Quarto est ratio sacramentalis; figuratur enim per hoc quod Ecclesia a Christo sumit principium. Unde apostolus dicit, ad Ephes. V, *sacramentum hoc magnum est, ego autem dico in Christo et in Ecclesia*.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32333)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ad primum.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32334)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod materia est ex qua aliquid fit. Natura autem creata habet determinatum principium; et, cum sit determinata ad unum, etiam habet determinatum processum, unde ex determinata materia producit aliquid in determinata specie. Sed virtus divina, cum sit infinita, potest idem secundum speciem ex quacumque materia facere; sicut virum ex limo terrae, et mulierem ex viro.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32335)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ex naturali generatione contrahitur quaedam propinquitas quae matrimonium impedit. Sed mulier non est producta a viro per naturalem generationem, sed sola virtute divina, unde Eva non dicitur filia Adae. Et propter hoc, ratio non sequitur.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 92 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32336)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod mulier non debuerit formari de costa viri. Costa enim viri fuit multo minor quam corpus mulieris. Sed ex minori non potest fieri maius, nisi vel per additionem, quod si fuisset, magis ex illo addito mulier formata diceretur quam de costa; vel etiam per rarefactionem, quia, ut dicit Augustinus, super Gen. ad Litt., *non est possibile ut aliquod corpus crescat, nisi rarescat*. Non autem invenitur corpus mulieris rarius quam viri, ad minus in ea proportione quam habet costa ad corpus Evae. Ergo Eva non fuit formata de costa Adae.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32337)\n\nPraeterea, in operibus primo creatis non fuit aliquid superfluum. Costa ergo Adae fuit de perfectione corporis eius. Ergo, ea subtracta, remansit imperfectum. Quod videtur inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32338)\n\nPraeterea, costa non potest separari ab homine sine dolore. Sed dolor non fuit ante peccatum. Ergo costa non debuit separari a viro, ut ex ea mulier formaretur.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32339)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, *aedificavit dominus Deus costam quam tulerat de Adam, in mulierem*.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32340)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod conveniens fuit mulierem formari de costa viri. Primo quidem, ad significandum quod inter virum et mulierem debet esse socialis coniunctio. Neque enim mulier debet dominari in virum, et ideo non est formata de capite. Neque debet a viro despici, tanquam serviliter subiecta, et ideo non est formata de pedibus. Secundo, propter sacramentum, quia de latere Christi dormientis in cruce fluxerunt sacramenta, idest sanguis et aqua, quibus est Ecclesia instituta.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32341)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod quidam dicunt per multiplicationem materiae absque alterius additione, formatum fuisse corpus mulieris; ad modum quo dominus quinque panes multiplicavit. Sed hoc est omnino impossibile. Multiplicatio enim praedicta aut accidit secundum transmutationem substantiae ipsius materiae; aut secundum transmutationem dimensionum eius. Non autem secundum transmutationem substantiae ipsius materiae, tum quia materia in se considerata, est omnino intransmutabilis, utpote existens in potentia, et habens solum rationem subiecti; tum etiam quia multitudo et magnitudo sunt praeter essentiam ipsius materiae. Et ideo nullo modo potest multiplicatio materiae intelligi, eadem materia manente absque additione, nisi per hoc quod maiores dimensiones accipiat. Hoc autem est rarefieri, scilicet materiam eandem accipere maiores dimensiones, ut philosophus dicit in IV Physic. Dicere ergo materiam multiplicari absque rarefactione, est ponere contradictoria simul, scilicet definitionem absque definito. Unde, cum non appareat rarefactio in talibus multiplicationibus, necesse est ponere additionem materiae, vel per creationem; vel, quod probabilius est, per conversionem. Unde Augustinus dicit, super Ioan., quod *hoc modo Christus ex quinque panibus satiavit quinque millia hominum, quomodo ex paucis granis producit multitudinem segetum*; quod fit per conversionem alimenti. Dicitur tamen vel ex quinque panibus turbas pavisse, vel ex costa mulierem formasse, quia additio facta est ad materiam praeexistentem costae vel panum.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32342)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod costa illa fuit de perfectione Adae, non prout erat individuum quoddam, sed prout erat principium speciei, sicut semen est de perfectione generantis, quod operatione naturali cum delectatione resolvitur. Unde multo magis virtute divina corpus mulieris potuit de costa viri formari absque dolore.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32343)\n\nEt per hoc patet solutio ad tertium.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 92 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32344)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod mulier non fuerit immediate formata a Deo. Nullum enim individuum ex simili secundum speciem productum, fit immediate a Deo. Sed mulier facta est de viro, qui est eiusdem speciei cum ipsa. Ergo non est facta immediate a Deo.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32345)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, III de Trin., quod corporalia dispensantur a Deo per Angelos. Sed corpus mulieris ex materia corporali est formatum. Ergo est factum per ministerium Angelorum, et non immediate a Deo.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32346)\n\nPraeterea, ea quae praeextiterunt in creaturis secundum rationes causales, producuntur virtute alicuius creaturae, et non immediate a Deo. Sed secundum causales rationes corpus mulieris in primis operibus productum fuit, ut Augustinus dicit IX super Gen. ad Litt. Ergo non fuit producta mulier immediate a Deo.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32347)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in eodem libro, *formare vel aedificare costam ut mulier esset, non potuit nisi Deus, a quo universa natura subsistit*.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32348)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, uniuscuiusque speciei generatio naturalis est ex determinata materia. Materia autem ex qua naturaliter generatur homo, est semen humanum viri vel feminae. Unde ex alia quacumque materia individuum humanae speciei generari non potest naturaliter. Solus autem Deus, qui est naturae institutor, potest praeter naturae ordinem res in esse producere. Et ideo solus Deus potuit vel virum de limo terrae, vel mulierem de costa viri formare.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32349)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ratio illa procedit, quando individuum generatur ex simili secundum speciem, generatione naturali.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32350)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit IX super Gen. ad Litt., an ministerium Angeli exhibuerint Deo in formatione mulieris, nescimus, certum tamen est quod, sicut corpus viri de limo non fuit formatum per Angelos, ita nec corpus mulieris de costa viri.\n\n[I q. 92 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32351)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus in eodem libro dicit, *non habuit prima rerum conditio ut femina omnino sic fieret; sed tantum hoc habuit, ut sic fieri posset*. Et ideo secundum causales rationes praeextitit corpus mulieris in primis operibus, non secundum potentiam activam, sed secundum potentiam passivam tantum, in ordine ad potentiam activam creatoris.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0002","text":"[I q. 92 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32319)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de productione mulieris. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum in illa rerum productione debuerit mulier produci. Secundo, utrum debuerit fieri de viro. Tertio, utrum de costa viri. Quarto, utrum facta fuerit immediate a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0005","text":"[I q. 92 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32320)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod mulier non debuit produci in prima rerum productione. Dicit enim philosophus, in libro de Generat. Animal., quod *femina est mas occasionatus*. Sed nihil occasionatum et deficiens debuit esse in prima rerum institutione. Ergo in illa prima rerum institutione mulier producenda non fuit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0007","text":"[I q. 92 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32321)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0008","text":"Praeterea, subiectio et minoratio ex peccato est subsecuta, nam, ad mulierem dictum est post peccatum, Gen. III, *sub viri potestate eris*; et Gregorius dicit quod, *ubi non delinquimus, omnes pares sumus*. Sed mulier naturaliter est minoris virtutis et dignitatis quam vir, semper enim honorabilius est agens patiente, ut dicit Augustinus XII super Gen. ad Litt. Ergo non debuit mulier produci in prima rerum productione ante peccatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0009","text":"[I q. 92 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32322)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0010","text":"Praeterea, occasiones peccatorum sunt amputandae. Sed Deus praescivit quod mulier esset futura viro in occasionem peccati. Ergo non debuit mulierem producere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0011","text":"[I q. 92 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32323)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, *non est bonum hominem esse solum; faciamus ei adiutorium simile sibi*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0013","text":"[I q. 92 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32324)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necessarium fuit feminam fieri, sicut Scriptura dicit, in adiutorium viri, non quidem in adiutorium alicuius alterius operis, ut quidam dixerunt, cum ad quodlibet aliud opus convenientius iuvari possit vir per alium virum quam per mulierem; sed in adiutorium generationis. Quod manifestius videri potest, si in viventibus modus generationis consideretur. Sunt enim quaedam viventia, quae in seipsis non habent virtutem activam generationis, sed ab agente alterius speciei generantur; sicut plantae et animalia quae generantur sine semine ex materia convenienti per virtutem activam caelestium corporum. Quaedam vero habent virtutem generationis activam et passivam coniunctam; sicut accidit in plantis quae generantur ex semine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0015","text":"Non enim est in plantis aliquod nobilius opus vitae quam generatio, unde convenienter omni tempore in eis virtuti passivae coniungitur virtus activa generationis. Animalibus vero perfectis competit virtus activa generationis secundum sexum masculinum, virtus vero passiva secundum sexum femininum. Et quia est aliquod opus vitae nobilius in animalibus quam generatio, ad quod eorum vita principaliter ordinatur; ideo non omni tempore sexus masculinus feminino coniungitur in animalibus perfectis, sed solum tempore coitus; ut imaginemur per coitum sic fieri unum ex mare et femina, sicut in planta omni tempore coniunguntur vis masculina et feminina, etsi in quibusdam plus abundet una harum, in quibusdam plus altera. Homo autem adhuc ordinatur ad nobilius opus vitae, quod est intelligere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0016","text":"Et ideo adhuc in homine debuit esse maiori ratione distinctio utriusque virtutis, ut seorsum produceretur femina a mare, et tamen carnaliter coniungerentur in unum ad generationis opus. Et ideo statim post formationem mulieris, dicitur Gen. II, *erunt duo in carne una*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0017","text":"[I q. 92 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32325)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0018","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod per respectum ad naturam particularem, femina est aliquid deficiens et occasionatum. Quia virtus activa quae est in semine maris, intendit producere sibi simile perfectum, secundum masculinum sexum, sed quod femina generetur, hoc est propter virtutis activae debilitatem, vel propter aliquam materiae indispositionem, vel etiam propter aliquam transmutationem ab extrinseco, puta a ventis Australibus, qui sunt humidi, ut dicitur in libro de Generat. Animal. Sed per comparationem ad naturam universalem, femina non est aliquid occasionatum, sed est de intentione naturae ad opus generationis ordinata. Intentio autem naturae universalis dependet ex Deo, qui est universalis auctor naturae. Et ideo instituendo naturam, non solum marem, sed etiam feminam produxit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0019","text":"[I q. 92 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32326)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod duplex est subiectio. Una servilis, secundum quam praesidens utitur subiecto ad sui ipsius utilitatem et talis subiectio introducta est post peccatum. Est autem alia subiectio oeconomica vel civilis, secundum quam praesidens utitur subiectis ad eorum utilitatem et bonum. Et ista subiectio fuisset etiam ante peccatum, defuisset enim bonum ordinis in humana multitudine, si quidam per alios sapientiores gubernati non fuissent. Et sic ex tali subiectione naturaliter femina subiecta est viro, quia naturaliter in homine magis abundat discretio rationis. Nec inaequalitas hominum excluditur per innocentiae statum, ut infra dicetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0021","text":"[I q. 92 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32327)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, si omnia ex quibus homo sumpsit occasionem peccandi, Deus subtraxisset a mundo, remansisset universum imperfectum. Nec debuit bonum commune tolli, ut vitaretur particulare malum, praesertim cum Deus sit adeo potens, ut quodlibet malum possit ordinare in bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0023","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0024","text":"[I q. 92 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32328)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0025","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod mulier non debuit fieri ex viro. Sexus enim communis est homini et aliis animalibus. Sed in aliis animalibus feminae non sunt factae ex maribus. Ergo nec in homine fieri debuit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0026","text":"[I q. 92 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32329)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0027","text":"Praeterea, eorum quae sunt eiusdem speciei, eadem est materia. Sed mas et femina sunt eiusdem speciei. Cum igitur vir fuerit factus ex limo terrae, ex eodem debuit fieri femina, et non ex viro.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0028","text":"[I q. 92 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32330)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0029","text":"Praeterea, mulier facta est in adiutorium viro ad generationem. Sed nimia propinquitas reddit personam ad hoc ineptam, unde personae propinquae a matrimonio excluduntur, ut patet Levit. XVIII. Ergo mulier non debuit fieri ex viro.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0030","text":"[I q. 92 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32331)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0031","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Eccli. XVII, creavit ex ipso, scilicet viro, adiutorium sibi simile, idest mulierem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0032","text":"[I q. 92 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32332)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0033","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod conveniens fuit mulierem, in prima rerum institutione, ex viro formari, magis quam in aliis animalibus. Primo quidem, ut in hoc quaedam dignitas primo homini servaretur, ut, secundum Dei similitudinem, esset ipse principium totius suae speciei, sicut Deus est principium totius universi. Unde et Paulus dicit, Act. XVII, quod *Deus fecit ex uno omne genus hominum*. Secundo, ut vir magis diligeret mulierem, et ei inseparabilius inhaereret, dum cognosceret eam ex se esse productam. Unde dicitur Gen. II, *de viro sumpta est, quamobrem relinquet homo patrem et matrem, et adhaerebit uxori suae*. Et hoc maxime necessarium fuit in specie humana, in qua mas et femina commanent per totam vitam, quod non contingit in aliis animalibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0034","text":"Tertio quia, ut philosophus dicit in VIII Ethic., mas et femina coniunguntur in hominibus non solum propter necessitatem generationis, ut in aliis animalibus; sed etiam propter domesticam vitam, in qua sunt alia opera viri et feminae, et in qua vir est caput mulieris. Unde convenienter ex viro formata est femina, sicut ex suo principio. Quarto est ratio sacramentalis; figuratur enim per hoc quod Ecclesia a Christo sumit principium. Unde apostolus dicit, ad Ephes. V, *sacramentum hoc magnum est, ego autem dico in Christo et in Ecclesia*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0035","text":"[I q. 92 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32333)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0036","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ad primum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0037","text":"[I q. 92 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32334)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0038","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod materia est ex qua aliquid fit. Natura autem creata habet determinatum principium; et, cum sit determinata ad unum, etiam habet determinatum processum, unde ex determinata materia producit aliquid in determinata specie. Sed virtus divina, cum sit infinita, potest idem secundum speciem ex quacumque materia facere; sicut virum ex limo terrae, et mulierem ex viro.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0039","text":"[I q. 92 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32335)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0040","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ex naturali generatione contrahitur quaedam propinquitas quae matrimonium impedit. Sed mulier non est producta a viro per naturalem generationem, sed sola virtute divina, unde Eva non dicitur filia Adae. Et propter hoc, ratio non sequitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0041","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0042","text":"[I q. 92 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32336)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0043","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod mulier non debuerit formari de costa viri. Costa enim viri fuit multo minor quam corpus mulieris. Sed ex minori non potest fieri maius, nisi vel per additionem, quod si fuisset, magis ex illo addito mulier formata diceretur quam de costa; vel etiam per rarefactionem, quia, ut dicit Augustinus, super Gen. ad Litt., *non est possibile ut aliquod corpus crescat, nisi rarescat*. Non autem invenitur corpus mulieris rarius quam viri, ad minus in ea proportione quam habet costa ad corpus Evae. Ergo Eva non fuit formata de costa Adae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0044","text":"[I q. 92 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32337)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0045","text":"Praeterea, in operibus primo creatis non fuit aliquid superfluum. Costa ergo Adae fuit de perfectione corporis eius. Ergo, ea subtracta, remansit imperfectum. Quod videtur inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0046","text":"[I q. 92 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32338)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0047","text":"Praeterea, costa non potest separari ab homine sine dolore. Sed dolor non fuit ante peccatum. Ergo costa non debuit separari a viro, ut ex ea mulier formaretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0048","text":"[I q. 92 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32339)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0049","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, *aedificavit dominus Deus costam quam tulerat de Adam, in mulierem*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0050","text":"[I q. 92 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32340)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0051","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod conveniens fuit mulierem formari de costa viri. Primo quidem, ad significandum quod inter virum et mulierem debet esse socialis coniunctio. Neque enim mulier debet dominari in virum, et ideo non est formata de capite. Neque debet a viro despici, tanquam serviliter subiecta, et ideo non est formata de pedibus. Secundo, propter sacramentum, quia de latere Christi dormientis in cruce fluxerunt sacramenta, idest sanguis et aqua, quibus est Ecclesia instituta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0052","text":"[I q. 92 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32341)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0053","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod quidam dicunt per multiplicationem materiae absque alterius additione, formatum fuisse corpus mulieris; ad modum quo dominus quinque panes multiplicavit. Sed hoc est omnino impossibile. Multiplicatio enim praedicta aut accidit secundum transmutationem substantiae ipsius materiae; aut secundum transmutationem dimensionum eius. Non autem secundum transmutationem substantiae ipsius materiae, tum quia materia in se considerata, est omnino intransmutabilis, utpote existens in potentia, et habens solum rationem subiecti; tum etiam quia multitudo et magnitudo sunt praeter essentiam ipsius materiae. Et ideo nullo modo potest multiplicatio materiae intelligi, eadem materia manente absque additione, nisi per hoc quod maiores dimensiones accipiat. Hoc autem est rarefieri, scilicet materiam eandem accipere maiores dimensiones, ut philosophus dicit in IV Physic.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0054","text":"Dicere ergo materiam multiplicari absque rarefactione, est ponere contradictoria simul, scilicet definitionem absque definito. Unde, cum non appareat rarefactio in talibus multiplicationibus, necesse est ponere additionem materiae, vel per creationem; vel, quod probabilius est, per conversionem. Unde Augustinus dicit, super Ioan., quod *hoc modo Christus ex quinque panibus satiavit quinque millia hominum, quomodo ex paucis granis producit multitudinem segetum*; quod fit per conversionem alimenti. Dicitur tamen vel ex quinque panibus turbas pavisse, vel ex costa mulierem formasse, quia additio facta est ad materiam praeexistentem costae vel panum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0055","text":"[I q. 92 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32342)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0056","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod costa illa fuit de perfectione Adae, non prout erat individuum quoddam, sed prout erat principium speciei, sicut semen est de perfectione generantis, quod operatione naturali cum delectatione resolvitur. Unde multo magis virtute divina corpus mulieris potuit de costa viri formari absque dolore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0057","text":"[I q. 92 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32343)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0058","text":"Et per hoc patet solutio ad tertium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0059","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0060","text":"[I q. 92 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32344)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0061","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod mulier non fuerit immediate formata a Deo. Nullum enim individuum ex simili secundum speciem productum, fit immediate a Deo. Sed mulier facta est de viro, qui est eiusdem speciei cum ipsa. Ergo non est facta immediate a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0062","text":"[I q. 92 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32345)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0063","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, III de Trin., quod corporalia dispensantur a Deo per Angelos. Sed corpus mulieris ex materia corporali est formatum. Ergo est factum per ministerium Angelorum, et non immediate a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0064","text":"[I q. 92 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32346)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0065","text":"Praeterea, ea quae praeextiterunt in creaturis secundum rationes causales, producuntur virtute alicuius creaturae, et non immediate a Deo. Sed secundum causales rationes corpus mulieris in primis operibus productum fuit, ut Augustinus dicit IX super Gen. ad Litt. Ergo non fuit producta mulier immediate a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0066","text":"[I q. 92 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32347)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0067","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in eodem libro, *formare vel aedificare costam ut mulier esset, non potuit nisi Deus, a quo universa natura subsistit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0068","text":"[I q. 92 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32348)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0069","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, uniuscuiusque speciei generatio naturalis est ex determinata materia. Materia autem ex qua naturaliter generatur homo, est semen humanum viri vel feminae. Unde ex alia quacumque materia individuum humanae speciei generari non potest naturaliter. Solus autem Deus, qui est naturae institutor, potest praeter naturae ordinem res in esse producere. Et ideo solus Deus potuit vel virum de limo terrae, vel mulierem de costa viri formare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0070","text":"[I q. 92 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32349)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0071","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ratio illa procedit, quando individuum generatur ex simili secundum speciem, generatione naturali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0072","text":"[I q. 92 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32350)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0073","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit IX super Gen. ad Litt., an ministerium Angeli exhibuerint Deo in formatione mulieris, nescimus, certum tamen est quod, sicut corpus viri de limo non fuit formatum per Angelos, ita nec corpus mulieris de costa viri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0074","text":"[I q. 92 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32351)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0075","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus in eodem libro dicit, *non habuit prima rerum conditio ut femina omnino sic fieret; sed tantum hoc habuit, ut sic fieri posset*. Et ideo secundum causales rationes praeextitit corpus mulieris in primis operibus, non secundum potentiam activam, sed secundum potentiam passivam tantum, in ordine ad potentiam activam creatoris.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"fbe7ebc3c8cda9ffce5de753ad089751b46539dbf67b9ad40d613b7395459c41","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T152528920417Z_e6d2206b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 92","source_sha256":"fbe7ebc3c8cda9ffce5de753ad089751b46539dbf67b9ad40d613b7395459c41","sha256":"147a4b5ffe41d2c4bcb51ba3e9da77f2ed535d3cda779f85e992253b2f564ab0","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:25:28.920436+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0002","text":"[I q. 92 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32319)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0003","text":"Next, the production of woman must be considered. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, whether woman ought to have been produced in that production of things. Second, whether she ought to have been made from man. Third, whether from the rib of man. Fourth, whether she was made immediately by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0005","text":"[I q. 92 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32320)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that woman ought not to have been produced in the first production of things. For the Philosopher says, in the book *On the Generation of Animals*, that *the female is an occasioned male*. But nothing occasioned and deficient ought to have existed in the first institution of things. Therefore, in that first institution of things, woman was not to be produced.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0007","text":"[I q. 92 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32321)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0008","text":"Moreover, subjection and diminution followed from sin; for after sin it was said to the woman, Gen. III, *you shall be under the power of your husband*; and Gregory says that *where we do not sin, we are all equals*. But woman is naturally of lesser strength and dignity than man; for the agent is always more honorable than the patient, as Augustine says in XII *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*. Therefore, woman ought not to have been produced in the first production of things before sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0009","text":"[I q. 92 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32322)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0010","text":"Moreover, occasions of sins are to be removed. But God foresaw that woman would be an occasion of sin to man. Therefore, He ought not to have produced woman.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0011","text":"[I q. 92 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32323)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0012","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Gen. II, *it is not good for man to be alone; let us make for him a helper like himself*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0013","text":"[I q. 92 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32324)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0014","text":"I answer that it was necessary for woman to be made, as Scripture says, as a help to man—not, indeed, as a help in some other work, as some have said, since for any other work a man can be helped more suitably by another man than by a woman—but as a help in generation. This can be seen more clearly if the mode of generation in living things is considered. For there are certain living things that do not have in themselves an active power of generation, but are generated by an agent of another species; such as plants and animals that are generated without seed from suitable matter through the active power of the heavenly bodies. But some have the active and passive powers of generation joined together, as occurs in plants that are generated from seed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0015","text":"For in plants there is no work of life nobler than generation; hence, fittingly, the active power of generation is joined to the passive power in them at all times. But in perfect animals, the active power of generation belongs to the male sex, while the passive power belongs to the female sex. And because there is in animals a work of life nobler than generation, to which their life is principally ordered, therefore in perfect animals the male sex is not joined to the female at all times, but only at the time of intercourse; so that we may conceive that through intercourse the male and female thus become one, just as in a plant the male and female powers are joined together at all times, although in some one of these predominates more, and in others the other. But man is further ordered to a nobler work of life, which is understanding.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0016","text":"And therefore in man there ought, for an even greater reason, to be a distinction of the two powers, so that the woman might be produced separately from the man, and yet they might be joined carnally into one for the work of generation. And therefore, immediately after the formation of woman, it is said in Gen. II, *the two shall be one flesh*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0017","text":"[I q. 92 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32325)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0018","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that in relation to particular nature, the female is something deficient and occasioned. For the active power that is in the seed of the male intends to produce something like itself, perfect according to the male sex; but that a female is generated is owing to a weakness of the active power, or to some indisposition of the matter, or even to some alteration from without—for example, from the south winds, which are moist, as is said in the book *On the Generation of Animals*. But in comparison with universal nature, the female is not something occasioned, but is intended by nature and ordered to the work of generation. Now the intention of universal nature depends on God, who is the universal author of nature. And therefore, in instituting nature, He produced not only the male but also the female.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0019","text":"[I q. 92 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32326)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0020","text":"To the second, it must be said that there is a twofold subjection. One is servile, according to which the ruler uses the subject for his own benefit; and such subjection was introduced after sin. But there is another subjection, economic or civil, according to which the ruler uses subjects for their benefit and good. And this subjection would have existed even before sin; for the good of order would have been lacking in the human multitude if some had not been governed by others wiser than themselves. And thus, by such subjection, woman is naturally subject to man, because in man discretion of reason naturally abounds more. Nor is inequality among human beings excluded by the state of innocence, as will be said below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0021","text":"[I q. 92 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32327)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0022","text":"To the third, it must be said that if God had removed from the world all things from which man took an occasion of sinning, the universe would have remained imperfect. Nor ought the common good to be taken away in order to avoid a particular evil, especially since God is so powerful that He can order any evil to good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0023","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0024","text":"[I q. 92 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32328)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0025","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that woman ought not to have been made from man. For sex is common to man and the other animals. But among the other animals females are not made from males. Therefore neither ought she to have been made in man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0026","text":"[I q. 92 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32329)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0027","text":"Further, things that are of the same species have the same matter. But male and female are of the same species. Therefore, since man was made from the slime of the earth, woman ought to have been made from the same, and not from man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0028","text":"[I q. 92 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32330)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0029","text":"Further, woman was made as a help to man for generation. But excessive closeness of kin renders a person unsuitable for this, whence closely related persons are excluded from marriage, as is clear from Leviticus XVIII. Therefore woman ought not to have been made from man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0030","text":"[I q. 92 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32331)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0031","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Ecclesiasticus XVII: “He created from him,” namely from man, “a help like unto himself,” that is, woman.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0032","text":"[I q. 92 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32332)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0033","text":"I answer that it was fitting that woman, in the first institution of things, be formed from man, more so than in the other animals. First indeed, in order that by this a certain dignity might be preserved to the first man: that, according to the likeness of God, he himself might be the principle of his entire species, as God is the principle of the whole universe. Hence Paul also says, Acts XVII, that *God made from one every race of men*. Second, so that man might love woman more and cleave to her more inseparably, knowing that she had been brought forth from himself. Hence it is said in Genesis II: *she was taken from man, wherefore a man shall leave father and mother, and shall cleave to his wife*. And this was especially necessary in the human species, in which male and female live together throughout their entire life, which does not occur in the other animals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0034","text":"Third, because, as the Philosopher says in Ethics VIII, male and female are joined among human beings not only for the necessity of generation, as in the other animals, but also for domestic life, in which there are distinct works of man and woman, and in which man is the head of woman. Hence woman was fittingly formed from man, as from her principle. Fourth, there is a sacramental reason; for by this is figured that the Church takes its beginning from Christ. Hence the Apostle says, in Ephesians V: *this is a great sacrament, but I speak in Christ and in the Church*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0035","text":"[I q. 92 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32333)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0036","text":"And through this the answer to the first is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0037","text":"[I q. 92 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32334)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0038","text":"To the second it must be said that matter is that from which something is made. But created nature has a determinate principle; and, since it is determined to one thing, it also has a determinate process, whence from determinate matter it produces something in a determinate species. But divine power, since it is infinite, can make the same thing in species from any matter whatsoever; just as man from the slime of the earth, and woman from man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0039","text":"[I q. 92 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32335)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0040","text":"To the third it must be said that a certain kinship that impedes marriage is contracted through natural generation. But woman was not produced from man through natural generation, but by divine power alone; whence Eve is not called Adam's daughter. And on this account, the argument does not follow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0041","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0042","text":"[I q. 92 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32336)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0043","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that woman ought not to have been formed from the rib of man. For the rib of man was much smaller than the body of woman. But a greater thing cannot be made from a smaller, except either by addition, in which case woman would be said to be formed more from that which was added than from the rib; or also by rarefaction, because, as Augustine says, in *On Genesis according to the Letter*, *it is not possible for any body to grow unless it be rarefied*. But the body of woman is not found to be more rarefied than that of man, at least not in the proportion which the rib bears to the body of Eve. Therefore Eve was not formed from Adam's rib.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0044","text":"[I q. 92 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32337)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0045","text":"Further, in the works first created there was nothing superfluous. Therefore Adam's rib belonged to the perfection of his body. Therefore, when it was removed, something imperfect remained. Which seems unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0046","text":"[I q. 92 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32338)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0047","text":"Further, a rib cannot be separated from a human being without pain. But there was no pain before sin. Therefore the rib ought not to have been separated from man, so that woman might be formed from it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0048","text":"[I q. 92 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32339)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0049","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Gen. II: *the Lord God built the rib which He had taken from Adam into a woman*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0050","text":"[I q. 92 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32340)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0051","text":"I answer that it was fitting for woman to be formed from the rib of man. First indeed, to signify that there ought to be a social union between man and woman. For woman ought not to dominate man, and therefore she was not formed from his head. Nor ought she to be despised by man, as though servilely subject, and therefore she was not formed from his feet. Second, on account of the sacrament: because from the side of Christ sleeping on the cross flowed the sacraments, namely blood and water, by which the Church was established.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0052","text":"[I q. 92 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32341)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0053","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that some say that the body of woman was formed by a multiplication of matter without the addition of anything else, in the manner in which the Lord multiplied the five loaves. But this is altogether impossible. For the aforesaid multiplication occurs either through a transformation of the substance of the matter itself, or through a transformation of its dimensions. But it does not occur through a transformation of the substance of the matter itself, both because matter considered in itself is altogether unchangeable, as existing in potency and having only the character of a subject; and also because multitude and magnitude are outside the essence of matter itself. Therefore, in no way can a multiplication of matter be understood, with the same matter remaining without addition, except by its receiving greater dimensions. But this is to be rarefied, namely, for the same matter to receive greater dimensions, as the Philosopher says in IV Physics.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0054","text":"Therefore, to say that matter is multiplied without rarefaction is to posit contradictories simultaneously, namely, the definition without the defined. Hence, since no rarefaction appears in such multiplications, it is necessary to posit an addition of matter, either through creation or, what is more probable, through conversion. Hence Augustine says, on John, that *Christ in this way satisfied five thousand men from five loaves, as He produces a multitude of crops from a few grains*; which occurs through the conversion of food. Yet He is said either to have fed the crowds from five loaves, or to have formed woman from a rib, because the addition was made to the pre-existing matter of the rib or of the loaves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0055","text":"[I q. 92 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32342)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0056","text":"To the second, it must be said that that rib belonged to Adam's perfection, not insofar as he was a certain individual, but insofar as he was a principle of the species, just as semen belongs to the perfection of the generator, which is discharged by a natural operation with pleasure. Hence, much more could the body of woman be formed from the rib of man by divine power without pain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0057","text":"[I q. 92 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32343)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0058","text":"And through this the solution to the third is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0059","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0060","text":"[I q. 92 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32344)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0061","text":"We proceed to the fourth as follows. It seems that woman was not immediately formed by God. For no individual produced from a like thing according to species is made immediately by God. But woman was made from man, who is of the same species as she. Therefore, she was not made immediately by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0062","text":"[I q. 92 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32345)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0063","text":"Further, Augustine says, in III On the Trinity, that corporeal things are administered by God through angels. But the body of woman was formed from corporeal matter. Therefore, it was made through the ministry of angels, and not immediately by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0064","text":"[I q. 92 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32346)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0065","text":"Further, those things which pre-existed in creatures according to causal reasons are produced by the power of some creature, and not immediately by God. But according to causal reasons the body of woman was produced in the first works, as Augustine says in IX On the Literal Meaning of Genesis. Therefore, woman was not produced immediately by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0066","text":"[I q. 92 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32347)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0067","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in the same book: *to form or build the rib so that there might be a woman could be done only by God, by whom all nature subsists*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0068","text":"[I q. 92 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32348)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0069","text":"I answer that, as was said above, the natural generation of each species is from determinate matter. Now the matter from which a human being is naturally generated is the human semen of man or woman. Hence, from any other matter whatever, an individual of the human species cannot be naturally generated. But God alone, who is the institutor of nature, can produce things in being outside the order of nature. And therefore God alone could form either man from the slime of the earth, or woman from the rib of man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0070","text":"[I q. 92 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32349)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0071","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that argument proceeds when an individual is generated from a like thing according to species, by natural generation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0072","text":"[I q. 92 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32350)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0073","text":"To the second, it must be said that, as Augustine says in Book IX of *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, whether angels rendered God a ministry in the formation of the woman, we do not know; nevertheless, it is certain that, just as the body of the man was not formed from mud by angels, so neither was the body of the woman formed from the rib of the man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0074","text":"[I q. 92 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32351)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0075","text":"To the third, it must be said that, as Augustine says in the same book, *the first condition of things did not have it that woman should be made altogether thus; but it had only this, that she could be made thus*. And therefore, according to causal principles, the body of the woman pre-existed in the first works, not according to active power, but only according to passive power, in relation to the active power of the Creator.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_84:P0075"]}],"source_digest":"c7ab6ca7151e23b190245d283ab5dcd3f8b950ca63c7958a91d68c314e4abda0","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 92"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 92","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85","title":"First Part · Question 93","order":94,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_85","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 93 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32352)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de fine sive termino productionis hominis, prout dicitur factus ad imaginem et similitudinem Dei. Et circa hoc quaeruntur novem. Primo, utrum in homine sit imago Dei. Secundo, utrum imago Dei sit in irrationalibus creaturis. Tertio, utrum imago Dei sit magis in Angelo quam in homine. Quarto, utrum imago Dei sit in omni homine. Quinto, utrum in homine sit imago Dei per comparationem ad essentiam, vel ad personas divinas omnes, aut unam earum. Sexto, utrum imago Dei inveniatur in homine solum secundum mentem. Septimo, utrum imago Dei sit in homine secundum potentias, aut secundum habitus, aut actus. Octavo, utrum per comparationem ad omnia obiecta. Nono, de differentia imaginis et similitudinis.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 93 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32353)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod imago Dei non sit in homine. Dicitur enim Isaiae XL, *cui similem fecistis Deum; aut quam imaginem ponetis ei?*\n\n[I q. 93 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32354)\n\nPraeterea, esse Dei imaginem est proprium primogeniti, de quo dicit apostolus, ad Colos. I, *qui est imago Dei invisibilis, primogenitus omnis creaturae*. Non ergo in homine invenitur Dei imago.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32355)\n\nPraeterea, Hilarius dicit, in libro de Synod., quod *imago est eius rei ad quam imaginatur, species indifferens*; et iterum dicit quod *imago est rei ad rem coaequandam indiscreta et unita similitudo*. Sed non est species indifferens Dei et hominis; nec potest esse aequalitas hominis ad Deum. Ergo in homine non potest esse imago Dei.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32356)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *faciamus hominem ad imaginem et similitudinem nostram*.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32357)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit in libro octoginta trium quaest., *ubi est imago, continuo est et similitudo; sed ubi est similitudo, non continuo est imago*. Ex quo patet quod similitudo est de ratione imaginis, et quod imago aliquid addit supra rationem similitudinis, scilicet quod sit ex alio expressum, imago enim dicitur ex eo quod agitur ad imitationem alterius. Unde ovum, quantumcumque sit alteri ovo simile et aequale, quia tamen non est expressum ex illo, non dicitur imago eius. Aequalitas autem non est de ratione imaginis, quia, ut Augustinus ibidem dicit, *ubi est imago, non continuo est aequalitas*; ut patet in imagine alicuius in speculo relucente. Est tamen de ratione perfectae imaginis, nam in perfecta imagine non deest aliquid imagini, quod insit illi de quo expressa est. Manifestum est autem quod in homine invenitur aliqua Dei similitudo, quae deducitur a Deo sicut ab exemplari, non tamen est similitudo secundum aequalitatem, quia in infinitum excedit exemplar hoc tale exemplatum. Et ideo in homine dicitur esse imago Dei, non tamen perfecta, sed imperfecta. Et hoc significat Scriptura, cum dicit hominem factum ad imaginem Dei, praepositio enim ad accessum quendam significat, qui competit rei distanti.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32358)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod propheta loquitur de corporalibus imaginibus ab homine fabricatis, et ideo signanter dicit, *quam imaginem ponetis ei?* Sed Deus ipse sibi in homine posuit spiritualem imaginem.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32359)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod primogenitus omnis creaturae est imago Dei perfecta, perfecte implens illud cuius imago est, et ideo dicitur imago, et nunquam ad imaginem. Homo vero et propter similitudinem dicitur imago; et propter imperfectionem similitudinis, dicitur ad imaginem. Et quia similitudo perfecta Dei non potest esse nisi in identitate naturae, imago Dei est in filio suo primogenito sicut imago regis in filio sibi connaturali; in homine autem sicut in aliena natura, sicut imago regis in nummo argenteo; ut patet per Augustinum in libro de decem chordis.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32360)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, cum unum sit ens indivisum, eo modo dicitur species indifferens, quo una. Unum autem dicitur aliquid non solum numero aut specie aut genere, sed etiam secundum analogiam vel proportionem quandam, et sic est unitas vel convenientia creaturae ad Deum. Quod autem dicit rei ad rem coaequandam, pertinet ad rationem perfectae imaginis.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 93 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32361)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod imago Dei inveniatur in irrationalibus creaturis. Dicit enim Dionysius, in libro de Div. Nom., *habent causata causarum suarum contingentes imagines*. Sed Deus est causa non solum rationalium creaturarum, sed etiam irrationalium. Ergo imago Dei invenitur in irrationalibus creaturis.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32362)\n\nPraeterea, quanto est expressior similitudo in aliquo, tanto magis accedit ad rationem imaginis. Sed Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod radius solaris maxime habet similitudinem divinae bonitatis. Ergo est ad imaginem Dei.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32363)\n\nPraeterea, quanto aliquid est magis perfectum in bonitate, tanto magis est Deo simile. Sed totum universum est perfectius in bonitate quam homo, quia etsi bona sint singula, tamen simul omnia dicuntur valde bona, Gen. I. Ergo totum universum est ad imaginem Dei, et non solum homo.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32364)\n\nPraeterea, Boetius in libro de Consol., dicit de Deo, *mundum mente gerens, similique in imagine formans*. Ergo totus mundus est ad imaginem Dei, et non solum rationalis creatura.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32365)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, VI super Gen. ad Litt., *hoc excellit in homine, quia Deus ad imaginem suam hominem fecit, propter hoc quod dedit ei mentem intellectualem, qua praestat pecoribus*. Ea ergo quae non habent intellectum, non sunt ad imaginem Dei.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32366)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod non quaelibet similitudo, etiam si sit expressa ex altero, sufficit ad rationem imaginis. Si enim similitudo sit secundum genus tantum, vel secundum aliquod accidens commune, non propter hoc dicetur aliquid esse ad imaginem alterius, non enim posset dici quod vermis qui oritur ex homine, sit imago hominis propter similitudinem generis; neque iterum potest dici quod, si aliquid fiat album ad similitudinem alterius, quod propter hoc sit ad eius imaginem, quia album est accidens commune pluribus speciebus. Requiritur autem ad rationem imaginis quod sit similitudo secundum speciem, sicut imago regis est in filio suo, vel ad minus secundum aliquod accidens proprium speciei, et praecipue secundum figuram, sicut hominis imago dicitur esse in cupro. Unde signanter Hilarius dicit quod imago est species indifferens. Manifestum est autem quod similitudo speciei attenditur secundum ultimam differentiam. Assimilantur autem aliqua Deo, primo quidem, et maxime communiter, inquantum sunt; secundo vero, inquantum vivunt; tertio vero, inquantum sapiunt vel intelligunt. Quae, ut Augustinus dicit in libro octoginta trium quaest., *ita sunt Deo similitudine proxima, ut in creaturis nihil sit propinquius*. Sic ergo patet quod solae intellectuales creaturae, proprie loquendo, sunt ad imaginem Dei.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32367)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod omne imperfectum est quaedam participatio perfecti. Et ideo etiam ea quae deficiunt a ratione imaginis, inquantum tamen aliqualem Dei similitudinem habent, participant aliquid de ratione imaginis. Et ideo Dionysius dicit quod causata habent causarum contingentes imagines, idest quantum contingit ea habere, et non simpliciter.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32368)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Dionysius assimilat radium solarem divinae bonitati quantum ad causalitatem; non secundum dignitatem naturae, quae requiritur ad rationem imaginis.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32369)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod universum est perfectius in bonitate quam intellectualis creatura extensive et diffusive. Sed intensive et collective similitudo divinae perfectionis magis invenitur in intellectuali creatura, quae est capax summi boni. Vel dicendum quod pars non dividitur contra totum, sed contra aliam partem. Unde cum dicitur quod sola natura intellectualis est ad imaginem Dei, non excluditur quin universum, secundum aliquam sui partem, sit ad imaginem Dei; sed excluduntur aliae partes universi.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32370)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod imago accipitur a Boetio secundum rationem similitudinis qua artificiatum imitatur speciem artis quae est in mente artificis, sic autem quaelibet creatura est imago rationis exemplaris quam habet in mente divina. Sic autem non loquimur nunc de imagine, sed secundum quod attenditur secundum similitudinem in natura; prout scilicet primo enti assimilantur omnia, inquantum sunt entia; et primae vitae inquantum sunt viventia; et summae sapientiae, inquantum sunt intelligentia.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 93 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32371)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non sit magis ad imaginem Dei quam homo. Dicit enim Augustinus, in sermone de imagine, quod Deus nulli alii creaturae dedit quod sit ad imaginem suam, nisi homini. Non ergo verum est quod Angelus magis dicatur ad imaginem Dei quam homo.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32372)\n\nPraeterea, secundum Augustinum, in libro octoginta trium quaest., *homo ita est ad imaginem Dei, ut, nulla interposita creatura, formetur a Deo. Et ideo nihil est illi coniunctius*. Sed imago Dei dicitur aliqua creatura, inquantum Deo coniungitur. Ergo Angelus non est magis ad imaginem Dei quam homo.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32373)\n\nPraeterea, creatura dicitur ad imaginem Dei, inquantum est intellectualis naturae. Sed intellectualis natura non intenditur nec remittitur, non enim est de genere accidentis, cum sit in genere substantiae. Ergo Angelus non est magis ad imaginem Dei quam homo.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32374)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Gregorius, in quadam homilia, quod *Angelus dicitur signaculum similitudinis, quia in eo similitudo divinae imaginis magis insinuatur expressa*.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32375)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod de imagine Dei loqui dupliciter possumus. Uno modo, quantum ad id in quo primo consideratur ratio imaginis, quod est intellectualis natura. Et sic imago Dei est magis in Angelis quam sit in hominibus, quia intellectualis natura perfectior est in eis, ut ex supra dictis patet. Secundo potest considerari imago Dei in homine, quantum ad id in quo secundario consideratur, prout scilicet in homine invenitur quaedam Dei imitatio, inquantum scilicet homo est de homine, sicut Deus de Deo; et inquantum anima hominis est tota in toto corpore eius, et iterum tota in qualibet parte ipsius, sicut Deus se habet ad mundum. Et secundum haec et similia, magis invenitur Dei imago in homine quam in Angelo. Sed quantum ad hoc non attenditur per se ratio divinae imaginis in homine, nisi praesupposita prima imitatione, quae est secundum intellectualem naturam, alioquin etiam animalia bruta essent ad imaginem Dei. Et ideo, cum quantum ad intellectualem naturam Angelus sit magis ad imaginem Dei quam homo, simpliciter concedendum est Angelum magis esse ad imaginem Dei; hominem autem secundum quid.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32376)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus excludit a Dei imagine alias inferiores creaturas intellectu carentes, non autem Angelos.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32377)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut ignis dicitur esse subtilissimum corporum secundum suam speciem, cum tamen unus ignis sit alio subtilior; ita dicitur quod nihil est coniunctius Deo quam mens humana, secundum genus intellectualis naturae; quia, sicut ipse supra praemiserat, *quae sapiunt, ita sunt illi similitudine proxima, ut in creaturis nihil sit propinquius*. Unde per hoc non excluditur quin Angelus sit magis ad Dei imaginem.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32378)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, cum dicitur quod substantia non recipit magis et minus, non intelligitur quod una species substantiae non sit perfectior quam alia, sed quod unum et idem individuum non participet suam speciem quandoque magis, quandoque minus. Nec etiam a diversis individuis participatur species substantiae secundum magis et minus.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 93 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32379)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod imago Dei non inveniatur in quolibet homine. Dicit enim apostolus, I ad Cor. XI, quod *vir est imago Dei, mulier autem est imago viri*. Cum ergo mulier sit individuum humanae speciei, non cuilibet individuo convenit esse imaginem Dei.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32380)\n\nPraeterea, apostolus dicit, Rom. VIII, quod *illos quos Deus praescivit conformes fieri imagini filii sui, hos praedestinavit*. Sed non omnes homines praedestinati sunt. Ergo non omnes homines habent conformitatem imaginis.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32381)\n\nPraeterea, similitudo est de ratione imaginis, ut supra dictum est. Sed per peccatum fit homo Deo dissimilis. Ergo amittit Dei imaginem.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32382)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo XXXVIII, *veruntamen in imagine pertransit homo*.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32383)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum homo secundum intellectualem naturam ad imaginem Dei esse dicatur, secundum hoc est maxime ad imaginem Dei, secundum quod intellectualis natura Deum maxime imitari potest. Imitatur autem intellectualis natura maxime Deum quantum ad hoc, quod Deus seipsum intelligit et amat. Unde imago Dei tripliciter potest considerari in homine. Uno quidem modo, secundum quod homo habet aptitudinem naturalem ad intelligendum et amandum Deum, et haec aptitudo consistit in ipsa natura mentis, quae est communis omnibus hominibus. Alio modo, secundum quod homo actu vel habitu Deum cognoscit et amat, sed tamen imperfecte, et haec est imago per conformitatem gratiae. Tertio modo, secundum quod homo Deum actu cognoscit et amat perfecte, et sic attenditur imago secundum similitudinem gloriae. Unde super illud Psalmi IV, *signatum est super nos lumen vultus tui, domine*, Glossa distinguit triplicem imaginem, scilicet creationis, recreationis et similitudinis. Prima ergo imago invenitur in omnibus hominibus; secunda in iustis tantum; tertia vero solum in beatis.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32384)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod tam in viro quam in muliere invenitur Dei imago quantum ad id in quo principaliter ratio imaginis consistit, scilicet quantum ad intellectualem naturam. Unde Gen. I, cum dixisset, *ad imaginem Dei creavit illum*, scilicet hominem, subdidit, *masculum et feminam creavit eos*, et dixit pluraliter eos, ut Augustinus dicit, ne intelligatur in uno individuo uterque sexus fuisse coniunctus. Sed quantum ad aliquid secundario imago Dei invenitur in viro, secundum quod non invenitur in muliere, nam vir est principium mulieris et finis, sicut Deus est principium et finis totius creaturae. Unde cum apostolus dixisset quod *vir imago et gloria est Dei, mulier autem est gloria viri*; ostendit quare hoc dixerit, subdens, *non enim vir est ex muliere, sed mulier ex viro; et vir non est creatus propter mulierem, sed mulier propter virum*.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32385)\n\nAd secundum et tertium dicendum quod illae rationes procedunt de imagine quae est secundum conformitatem gratiae et gloriae.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 93 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32386)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in homine non sit imago Dei quantum ad Trinitatem divinarum personarum. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro de fide ad Petrum, *una est sanctae Trinitatis essentialiter divinitas, et imago ad quam factus est homo*. Et Hilarius, in V de Trin., dicit quod *homo fit ad communem Trinitatis imaginem*. Est ergo in homine imago Dei quantum ad essentiam, et non quantum ad Trinitatem personarum.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32387)\n\nPraeterea, in libro de Eccles. Dogmat. dicitur quod imago Dei attenditur in homine secundum aeternitatem. Damascenus etiam dicit quod *hominem esse ad imaginem Dei, significat intellectuale, et arbitrio liberum, et per se potestativum*. Gregorius etiam Nyssenus dicit quod, cum Scriptura dixit hominem factum ad imaginem Dei, *aequale est ac si diceret humanam naturam omnis boni factam esse participem; bonitatis enim plenitudo divinitas est*. Haec autem omnia non pertinent ad distinctionem personarum, sed magis ad essentiae unitatem. Ergo in homine est imago Dei, non secundum Trinitatem personarum, sed secundum essentiae unitatem.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32388)\n\nPraeterea, imago ducit in cognitionem eius cuius est imago. Si igitur in homine est imago Dei secundum Trinitatem personarum, cum homo per naturalem rationem seipsum cognoscere possit, sequeretur quod per naturalem cognitionem posset homo cognoscere Trinitatem divinarum personarum. Quod est falsum, ut supra ostensum est.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32389)\n\nPraeterea, nomen imaginis non cuilibet trium personarum convenit, sed soli filio, dicit enim Augustinus, in VI de Trin., quod *solus filius est imago patris*. Si igitur in homine attenderetur Dei imago secundum personam, non esset in homine imago totius Trinitatis, sed filii tantum.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32390)\n\nSed contra est quod Hilarius, in IV de Trin., per hoc quod homo dicitur ad imaginem Dei factus, ostendit pluralitatem divinarum personarum.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32391)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra habitum est, distinctio divinarum personarum non est nisi secundum originem, vel potius secundum relationes originis. Non autem est idem modus originis in omnibus, sed modus originis uniuscuiusque est secundum convenientiam suae naturae, aliter enim producuntur animata, aliter inanimata; aliter animalia, atque aliter plantae. Unde manifestum est quod distinctio divinarum personarum est secundum quod divinae naturae convenit. Unde esse ad imaginem Dei secundum imitationem divinae naturae, non excludit hoc quod est esse ad imaginem Dei secundum repraesentationem trium personarum; sed magis unum ad alterum sequitur. Sic igitur dicendum est in homine esse imaginem Dei et quantum ad naturam divinam, et quantum ad Trinitatem personarum, nam et in ipso Deo in tribus personis una existit natura.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32392)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ad duo prima.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32393)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ratio illa procederet, si imago Dei esset in homine perfecte repraesentans Deum. Sed, sicut Augustinus dicit in XV de Trin., maxima est differentia huius Trinitatis quae est in nobis, ad Trinitatem divinam. Et ideo, ut ipse ibidem dicit, *Trinitatem quae in nobis est, videmus potius quam credimus, Deum vero esse Trinitatem, credimus potius quam videmus*.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32394)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod quidam dixerunt in homine esse solum imaginem filii. Sed hoc improbat Augustinus, in XII de Trin. Primo quidem, per hoc quod, cum secundum aequalitatem essentiae filius sit patri similis, necesse est, si homo sit factus ad similitudinem filii, quod sit factus ad similitudinem patris. Secundo quia, si homo esset factus solum ad imaginem filii, non diceret pater, *faciamus hominem ad imaginem et similitudinem nostram*, sed tuam. Cum ergo dicitur, ad imaginem Dei fecit illum, non est intelligendum quod pater fecerit hominem solum ad imaginem filii, qui est Deus, ut quidam exposuerunt, sed intelligendum est quod Deus Trinitas fecit hominem ad imaginem suam, idest totius Trinitatis. Cum autem dicitur quod Deus fecit hominem ad imaginem suam, dupliciter potest intelligi. Uno modo, quod haec praepositio ad designet terminum factionis, ut sit sensus, faciamus hominem taliter, ut sit in eo imago. Alio modo, haec praepositio ad potest designare causam exemplarem; sicut cum dicitur, iste liber est factus ad illum. Et sic imago Dei est ipsa essentia divina, quae abusive imago dicitur, secundum quod imago ponitur pro exemplari. Vel, secundum quod quidam dicunt, divina essentia dicitur imago, quia secundum eam una persona aliam imitatur.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 93 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32395)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod imago Dei non sit in homine solum secundum mentem. Dicit enim apostolus, I ad Cor. XI, quod vir est imago Dei. Sed vir non est solum mens. Ergo imago Dei non attenditur solum secundum mentem.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32396)\n\nPraeterea, Gen. I, *creavit Deus hominem ad imaginem suam, ad imaginem Dei creavit illum, masculum et feminam creavit eos*. Sed distinctio masculi et feminae est secundum corpus. Ergo etiam secundum corpus attenditur Dei imago in homine, et non secundum mentem tantum.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32397)\n\nPraeterea, imago praecipue videtur attendi secundum figuram. Sed figura ad corpus pertinet. Ergo imago Dei attenditur in homine etiam secundum corpus, et non secundum mentem tantum.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 6 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32398)\n\nPraeterea, secundum Augustinum, XII super Gen. ad Litt., triplex visio invenitur in nobis, scilicet corporalis, spiritualis sive imaginaria, et intellectualis. Si ergo secundum visionem intellectualem, quae ad mentem pertinet, est aliqua Trinitas in nobis, secundum quam sumus ad imaginem Dei; pari ratione et in aliis visionibus.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32399)\n\nSed contra est quod apostolus dicit, ad Eph. IV, *renovamini spiritu mentis vestrae, et induite novum hominem*, ex quo datur intelligi quod renovatio nostra, quae fit secundum novi hominis indumentum, ad mentem pertinet. Sed ad Col. III, dicit, *induentes novum hominem, qui renovatur in agnitionem Dei, secundum imaginem eius qui creavit eum*, ubi renovationem quae est secundum novi hominis indumentum, attribuit imagini Dei. Esse ergo ad imaginem Dei pertinet solum ad mentem.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32400)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum in omnibus creaturis sit aliqualis Dei similitudo, in sola creatura rationali invenitur similitudo Dei per modum imaginis, ut supra dictum est, in aliis autem creaturis per modum vestigii. Id autem in quo creatura rationalis excedit alias creaturas, est intellectus sive mens. Unde relinquitur quod nec in ipsa rationali creatura invenitur Dei imago, nisi secundum mentem. In aliis vero partibus, si quas habet rationalis creatura, invenitur similitudo vestigii; sicut et in ceteris rebus quibus secundum partes huiusmodi assimilatur. Cuius ratio manifeste cognosci potest, si attendatur modus quo repraesentat vestigium, et quo repraesentat imago. Imago enim repraesentat secundum similitudinem speciei, ut dictum est. Vestigium autem repraesentat per modum effectus qui sic repraesentat suam causam, quod tamen ad speciei similitudinem non pertingit, impressiones enim quae ex motu animalium relinquuntur, dicuntur vestigia; et similiter cinis dicitur vestigium ignis; et desolatio terrae, vestigium hostilis exercitus. Potest ergo huiusmodi differentia attendi inter creaturas rationales et alias creaturas, et quantum ad hoc quod in creaturis repraesentatur similitudo divinae naturae, et quantum ad hoc quod in eis repraesentatur similitudo Trinitatis increatae. Nam quantum ad similitudinem divinae naturae pertinet, creaturae rationales videntur quodammodo ad repraesentationem speciei pertingere, inquantum imitantur Deum non solum in hoc quod est et vivit, sed etiam in hoc quod intelligit, ut supra dictum est. Aliae vero creaturae non intelligunt; sed apparet in eis quoddam vestigium intellectus producentis, si earum dispositio consideretur. Similiter, cum increata Trinitas distinguatur secundum processionem verbi a dicente, et amoris ab utroque, ut supra habitum est; in creatura rationali, in qua invenitur processio verbi secundum intellectum, et processio amoris secundum voluntatem, potest dici imago Trinitatis increatae per quandam repraesentationem speciei. In aliis autem creaturis non invenitur principium verbi, et verbum, et amor; sed apparet in eis quoddam vestigium quod haec inveniantur in causa producente. Nam hoc ipsum quod creatura habet substantiam modificatam et finitam, demonstrat quod sit a quodam principio; species vero eius demonstrat verbum facientis, sicut forma domus demonstrat conceptionem artificis; ordo vero demonstrat amorem producentis, quo effectus ordinatur ad bonum, sicut usus aedificii demonstrat artificis voluntatem. Sic igitur in homine invenitur Dei similitudo per modum imaginis secundum mentem; sed secundum alias partes eius, per modum vestigii.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32401)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod homo dicitur imago Dei, non quia ipse essentialiter sit imago, sed quia in eo est Dei imago impressa secundum mentem; sicut denarius dicitur imago Caesaris, inquantum habet Caesaris imaginem. Unde non oportet quod secundum quamlibet partem hominis accipiatur Dei imago.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32402)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit XII de Trin., quidam imaginem Trinitatis in homine posuerunt, non secundum unum individuum, sed secundum plura; dicentes quod *vir patris personam intimat; filii vero personam, quod de viro ita processit ut de illo nasceretur; atque ita tertiam personam, velut spiritum sanctum, dicunt esse mulierem, quae ita de viro processit ut non ipsa esset filius aut filia*. Quod prima facie absurdum videtur. Primo quidem, quia sequeretur quod spiritus sanctus esset principium filii, sicut mulier est principium prolis quae nascitur de viro. Secundo, quia unus homo non esset nisi ad imaginem unius personae. Tertio, quia secundum hoc Scriptura de imagine Dei in homine mentionem facere non debuisset, nisi producta iam prole. Et ideo dicendum est quod Scriptura, postquam dixerat, ad imaginem Dei creavit illum, addidit, masculum et feminam creavit eos, non ut imago Dei secundum distinctiones sexuum attendatur; sed quia imago Dei utrique sexui est communis, cum sit secundum mentem, in qua non est distinctio sexuum. Unde apostolus, ad Col. III, postquam dixerat, secundum imaginem eius qui creavit illum, subdit, ubi non est masculus et femina.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32403)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, quamvis imago Dei in homine non accipiatur secundum figuram corpoream, tamen *corpus hominis, quia solum inter terrenorum animalium corpora non pronum in alvum prostratum est, sed tale est ut ad contemplandum caelum sit aptius, magis in hoc ad imaginem et similitudinem Dei, quam cetera corpora animalium, factum iure videri potest*; ut Augustinus dicit in libro octoginta trium quaest. Quod tamen non est sic intelligendum, quasi in corpore hominis sit imago Dei, sed quia ipsa figura humani corporis repraesentat imaginem Dei in anima, per modum vestigii.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 6 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32404)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod tam in visione corporali quam in visione imaginaria invenitur quaedam Trinitas, ut Augustinus dicit in libro de Trin. In visione enim corporali est quidem primo species exterioris corporis; secundo vero ipsa visio, quae fit per impressionem cuiusdam similitudinis praedictae speciei in visum; tertio est ibi intentio voluntatis applicans visum ad videndum, et eum in re visa detinens. Similiter etiam in visione imaginaria invenitur primo quidem species in memoria reservata; secundo ipsa imaginaria visio, quae provenit ex hoc quod acies animae, idest ipsa vis imaginaria, informatur secundum praedictam speciem; tertio vero invenitur intentio voluntatis coniungens utrumque. Sed utraque Trinitas deficit a ratione divinae imaginis. Nam ipsa species exterioris corporis est extra naturam animae, species autem quae est in memoria, etsi non sit extra animam, est tamen adventitia animae, et ita utrobique deficit repraesentatio connaturalitatis et coaeternitatis divinarum personarum. Visio vero corporalis non procedit tantum a specie exterioris corporis, sed simul cum hoc a sensu videntis, et similiter visio imaginaria non solum procedit a specie quae in memoria conservatur, sed etiam a virtute imaginativa, et ita per hoc non repraesentatur convenienter processio filii a solo patre. Intentio vero voluntatis, quae coniungit duo praedicta, non ex eis procedit, neque in visione corporea neque in spirituali, unde non convenienter repraesentatur processio spiritus sancti a patre et filio.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 93 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32405)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod imago Dei non inveniatur in anima secundum actus. Dicit enim Augustinus, XI de Civ. Dei, quod homo factus est ad imaginem Dei, secundum quod *sumus, et nos esse novimus, et id esse ac nosse diligimus*. Sed esse non significat actum. Ergo imago Dei non attenditur in anima secundum actus.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32406)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus, in IX de Trin., assignat imaginem Dei in anima secundum haec tria, quae sunt mens, notitia et amor. Mens autem non significat actum; sed magis potentiam, vel etiam essentiam intellectivae animae. Ergo imago Dei non attenditur secundum actus.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32407)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus, X de Trin., assignat imaginem Trinitatis in anima secundum memoriam, intelligentiam et voluntatem. Sed haec tria sunt vires naturales animae, ut Magister dicit, III distinctione I libri Sent. Ergo imago attenditur secundum potentias, et non secundum actus.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 7 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32408)\n\nPraeterea, imago Trinitatis semper manet in anima. Sed actus non semper manet. Ergo imago Dei non attenditur in anima secundum actus.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32409)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus, XI de Trin., assignat Trinitatem in inferioribus animae partibus secundum actualem visionem sensibilem et imaginariam. Ergo et Trinitas quae est in mente, secundum quam homo est ad imaginem Dei, debet attendi secundum actualem visionem.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32410)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, ad rationem imaginis pertinet aliqualis repraesentatio speciei. Si ergo imago Trinitatis divinae debet accipi in anima, oportet quod secundum illud principaliter attendatur, quod maxime accedit, prout possibile est, ad repraesentandum speciem divinarum personarum. Divinae autem personae distinguuntur secundum processionem verbi a dicente, et amoris connectentis utrumque. Verbum autem in anima nostra sine actuali cogitatione esse non potest, ut Augustinus dicit XIV de Trin. Et ideo primo et principaliter attenditur imago Trinitatis in mente secundum actus, prout scilicet ex notitia quam habemus, cogitando interius verbum formamus, et ex hoc in amorem prorumpimus. Sed quia principia actuum sunt habitus et potentiae; unumquodque autem virtualiter est in suo principio, secundario, et quasi ex consequenti, imago Trinitatis potest attendi in anima secundum potentias, et praecipue secundum habitus, prout in eis scilicet actus virtualiter existunt.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32411)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod esse nostrum ad imaginem Dei pertinet, quod est nobis proprium supra alia animalia; quod quidem esse competit nobis inquantum mentem habemus. Et ideo eadem est haec Trinitas cum illa quam Augustinus ponit in IX de Trin., quae consistit in mente, notitia et amore.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32412)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Augustinus hanc Trinitatem primo adinvenit in mente. Sed quia mens, etsi se totam quodammodo cognoscat, etiam quodammodo se ignorat, prout scilicet est ab aliis distincta; et sic etiam se quaerit, ut Augustinus consequenter probat in X de Trin., ideo, quasi notitia non totaliter menti coaequetur, accipit in anima tria quaedam propria mentis, scilicet memoriam, intelligentiam et voluntatem, quae nullus ignorat se habere, et in istis tribus potius imaginem Trinitatis assignat, quasi prima assignatio sit quodammodo deficiens.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32413)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus probat XIV de Trin., intelligere dicimur et velle seu amare aliqua, et quando de his cogitamus, et quando de his non cogitamus. Sed quando sine cogitatione sunt, ad solam memoriam pertinent; quae nihil est aliud, secundum ipsum, quam habitualis retentio notitiae et amoris. Sed quia, ut ipse dicit, *verbum ibi esse sine cogitatione non potest (cogitamus enim omne quod dicimus etiam illo interiori verbo quod ad nullius gentis pertinet linguam), in tribus potius illis imago ista cognoscitur, memoria scilicet, intelligentia et voluntate. Hanc autem nunc dico intelligentiam, qua intelligimus cogitantes; et eam voluntatem sive amorem vel dilectionem, quae istam prolem parentemque coniungit*. Ex quo patet quod imaginem divinae Trinitatis potius ponit in intelligentia et voluntate actuali, quam secundum quod sunt in habituali retentione memoriae, licet etiam quantum ad hoc, aliquo modo sit imago Trinitatis in anima, ut ibidem dicitur. Et sic patet quod memoria, intelligentia et voluntas non sunt tres vires, ut in sententiis dicitur.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 7 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32414)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod aliquis respondere posset per hoc quod Augustinus dicit XIV de Trin., quod *mens semper sui meminit, semper se intelligit et amat*. Quod quidam sic intelligunt, quasi animae adsit actualis intelligentia et amor sui ipsius. Sed hunc intellectum excludit per hoc quod subdit, quod *non semper se cogitat discretam ab his quae non sunt quod ipsa*. Et sic patet quod anima semper intelligit et amat se, non actualiter, sed habitualiter. Quamvis etiam dici possit quod, percipiendo actum suum, seipsam intelligit quandocumque aliquid intelligit. Sed quia non semper est actu intelligens, ut patet in dormiente, ideo oportet dicere quod actus, etsi non semper maneant in seipsis, manent tamen semper in suis principiis, scilicet potentiis et habitibus. Unde Augustinus dicit, XIV de Trin., *si secundum hoc facta est ad imaginem Dei anima rationalis, quod uti ratione atque intellectu ad intelligendum et conspiciendum Deum potest, ab initio quo esse coepit, fuit in ea Dei imago*.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 93 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32415)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod imago divinae Trinitatis sit in anima non solum per comparationem ad obiectum quod est Deus. Imago enim divinae Trinitatis invenitur in anima, sicut dictum est, secundum quod verbum in nobis procedit a dicente et amor ab utroque. Sed hoc invenitur in nobis secundum quodcumque obiectum. Ergo secundum quodcumque obiectum invenitur in mente nostra imago divinae Trinitatis.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32416)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, in XII de Trin., quod *cum quaerimus in anima Trinitatem, in tota quaerimus, non separantes actionem rationalem in temporalibus a contemplatione aeternorum*. Ergo etiam secundum temporalia obiecta invenitur imago Trinitatis in anima.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32417)\n\nPraeterea, quod Deum intelligamus et amemus, convenit nobis secundum gratiae donum. Si igitur secundum memoriam, intelligentiam et voluntatem seu dilectionem Dei, attendatur imago Trinitatis in anima, non erit imago Dei in homine secundum naturam, sed secundum gratiam. Et sic non erit omnibus communis.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32418)\n\nPraeterea, sancti qui sunt in patria, maxime conformantur imagini Dei secundum gloriae visionem, unde dicitur, II ad Cor. III, *in eandem imaginem transformamur, a claritate in claritatem*. Sed secundum visionem gloriae temporalia cognoscuntur. Ergo etiam per comparationem ad temporalia, Dei imago attenditur in nobis.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32419)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XIV de Trin., quod *non propterea est Dei imago in mente, quia sui meminit, et intelligit et diligit se, sed quia potest etiam meminisse, intelligere et amare Deum, a quo facta est*. Multo igitur minus secundum alia obiecta attenditur imago Dei in mente.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32420)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, imago importat similitudinem utcumque pertingentem ad speciei repraesentationem. Unde oportet quod imago divinae Trinitatis attendatur in anima secundum aliquid quod repraesentat divinas personas repraesentatione speciei, sicut est possibile creaturae. Distinguuntur autem divinae personae, ut dictum est, secundum processionem verbi a dicente, et amoris ab utroque. Verbum autem Dei nascitur de Deo secundum notitiam sui ipsius, et amor procedit a Deo secundum quod seipsum amat. Manifestum est autem quod diversitas obiectorum diversificat speciem verbi et amoris, non enim idem est specie in corde hominis verbum conceptum de lapide et de equo, nec idem specie amor. Attenditur igitur divina imago in homine secundum verbum conceptum de Dei notitia, et amorem exinde derivatum. Et sic imago Dei attenditur in anima secundum quod fertur, vel nata est ferri in Deum. Fertur autem in aliquid mens dupliciter, uno modo, directe et immediate; alio modo, indirecte et mediate, sicut cum aliquis, videndo imaginem hominis in speculo, dicitur ferri in ipsum hominem. Et ideo Augustinus dicit, in XIV de Trin., quod *mens meminit sui, intelligit se, et diligit se, hoc si cernimus, cernimus Trinitatem; nondum quidem Deum, sed iam imaginem Dei*. Sed hoc est, non quia fertur mens in seipsam absolute, sed prout per hoc ulterius potest ferri in Deum; ut patet per auctoritatem supra inductam.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32421)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ad rationem imaginis, non solum oportet attendere quod aliquid procedat ab aliquo; sed etiam quid a quo procedat, scilicet quod verbum Dei procedit a notitia de Deo.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32422)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod in tota quidem anima invenitur aliqua Trinitas, non quidem ita quod praeter actionem temporalium et contemplationem aeternorum, *quaeratur aliquod tertium quo Trinitas impleatur*, prout ibidem subditur. Sed in illa parte rationis quae derivatur a parte temporalium, etsi *Trinitas inveniri possit, non tamen imago Dei potest inveniri*, ut postea dicitur, quia huiusmodi temporalium notitia adventitia est animae. Et habitus etiam ipsi quibus temporalia cognoscuntur, non semper adsunt; sed quandoque quidem praesentialiter adsunt, quandoque autem secundum memoriam tantum, etiam postquam adesse incipiunt. Sicut patet de fide, quae temporaliter nobis advenit in praesenti, in statu autem futurae beatitudinis iam non erit fides, sed memoria fidei.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32423)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod meritoria Dei cognitio et dilectio non est nisi per gratiam. Est tamen aliqua Dei cognitio et dilectio naturalis, ut supra habitum est. Et hoc etiam ipsum naturale est, quod mens ad intelligendum Deum ratione uti potest, secundum quod imaginem Dei semper diximus permanere in mente, sive haec imago Dei ita sit obsoleta, quasi obumbrata, ut pene nulla sit, ut in his qui non habent usum rationis; sive sit obscura atque deformis, ut in peccatoribus; sive sit clara et pulchra, ut in iustis, sicut Augustinus dicit, XIV de Trin.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 8 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32424)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod secundum visionem gloriae, temporalia videbuntur in ipso Deo; et ideo huiusmodi temporalium visio ad Dei imaginem pertinebit. Et hoc est quod Augustinus dicit, XIV de Trin., quod *in illa natura cui mens feliciter adhaerebit, immutabile videbit omne quod viderit*. Nam et in ipso verbo increato sunt rationes omnium creaturarum.\n\n## Articulus 9\n\n[I q. 93 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32425)\n\nAd nonum sic proceditur. Videtur quod similitudo ab imagine non convenienter distinguatur. Genus enim non convenienter distinguitur a specie. Sed similitudo comparatur ad imaginem, ut genus ad speciem, quia *ubi est imago, ibi est continuo similitudo, sed non convertitur*, ut dicitur in libro octoginta trium quaest. Ergo inconvenienter similitudo ab imagine distinguitur.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32426)\n\nPraeterea, ratio imaginis attenditur non solum secundum repraesentationem divinarum personarum, sed etiam secundum repraesentationem divinae essentiae, ad quam repraesentationem pertinet immortalitas et indivisibilitas. Non ergo convenienter dicitur quod *similitudo est in essentia, quia est immortalis et indivisibilis; imago autem in aliis*.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32427)\n\nPraeterea, imago Dei in homine est triplex, scilicet naturae, gratiae et gloriae, ut supra habitum est. Sed innocentia et iustitia ad gratiam pertinent. Inconvenienter ergo dicitur quod *imago accipitur secundum memoriam, intelligentiam et voluntatem; similitudo autem secundum innocentiam et iustitiam*.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 9 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32428)\n\nPraeterea, cognitio veritatis ad intelligentiam pertinet, amor autem virtutis ad voluntatem, quae sunt duae partes imaginis. Non ergo convenienter dicitur quod *imago sit in cognitione veritatis, similitudo in dilectione virtutis*.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32429)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in libro octoginta trium quaest., *sunt qui non frustra intelligunt duo dicta esse ad imaginem et similitudinem; cum, si una res esset, unum nomen sufficere potuisset*.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32430)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod similitudo quaedam unitas est, unum enim in qualitate similitudinem causat, ut dicitur in V Metaphys. Unum autem, cum sit de transcendentibus, et commune est omnibus, et ad singula potest aptari; sicut et bonum et verum. Unde, sicut bonum alicui rei particulari potest comparari ut praeambulum ad ipsam, et ut subsequens, prout designat aliquam perfectionem ipsius; ita etiam est de comparatione similitudinis ad imaginem. Est enim bonum praeambulum ad hominem, secundum quod homo est quoddam particulare bonum, et rursus bonum subsequitur ad hominem, inquantum aliquem hominem specialiter dicimus esse bonum, propter perfectionem virtutis. Et similiter similitudo consideratur ut praeambulum ad imaginem, inquantum est communius quam imago, ut supra dictum est, consideratur etiam ut subsequens ad imaginem, inquantum significat quandam imaginis perfectionem; dicimus enim imaginem alicuius esse similem vel non similem ei cuius est imago, inquantum perfecte vel imperfecte repraesentat ipsum. Sic ergo similitudo potest ab imagine distingui dupliciter. Uno modo, prout est praeambula ad ipsam, et in pluribus existens. Et sic similitudo attenditur secundum ea quae sunt communiora proprietatibus naturae intellectualis, secundum quas proprie attenditur imago. Et secundum hoc dicitur in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod spiritus, idest mens, ad imaginem Dei, nullo dubitante, factus est, cetera autem hominis, scilicet quae pertinent ad inferiores partes animae, vel etiam ad ipsum corpus, ad similitudinem facta esse aliqui volunt. Secundum hoc etiam in libro de quantitate animae dicitur quod similitudo Dei attenditur in anima, inquantum est incorruptibilis, nam corruptibile et incorruptibile sunt differentiae entis communis. Alio modo potest considerari similitudo, secundum quod significat imaginis expressionem et perfectionem. Et secundum hoc Damascenus dicit quod *id quod est secundum imaginem, intellectuale significat, et arbitrio liberum per se potestativum, quod autem secundum similitudinem, virtutis, secundum quod homini possibile est inesse, similitudinem*. Et ad idem refertur quod similitudo dicitur ad dilectionem virtutis pertinere, non enim est virtus sine dilectione virtutis.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32431)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod similitudo non distinguitur ab imagine secundum communem rationem similitudinis (sic enim includitur in ratione ipsius imaginis), sed secundum quod aliqua similitudo deficit a ratione imaginis, vel etiam est imaginis perfectiva.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32432)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod essentia animae pertinet ad imaginem, prout repraesentat divinam essentiam secundum ea quae sunt propria intellectualis naturae, non autem secundum conditiones consequentes ens in communi, ut est esse simplicem et indissolubilem.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32433)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod etiam virtutes quaedam naturaliter insunt animae, ad minus secundum quaedam earum semina, et secundum has posset attendi similitudo naturalis. Quamvis non sit inconveniens ut id quod secundum assignationem unam dicitur imago, secundum aliam dicatur similitudo.\n\n[I q. 93 a. 9 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32434)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod dilectio verbi, quod est amata notitia, pertinet ad rationem imaginis, sed dilectio virtutis pertinet ad similitudinem, sicut et virtus.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0002","text":"[I q. 93 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32352)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de fine sive termino productionis hominis, prout dicitur factus ad imaginem et similitudinem Dei. Et circa hoc quaeruntur novem. Primo, utrum in homine sit imago Dei. Secundo, utrum imago Dei sit in irrationalibus creaturis. Tertio, utrum imago Dei sit magis in Angelo quam in homine. Quarto, utrum imago Dei sit in omni homine. Quinto, utrum in homine sit imago Dei per comparationem ad essentiam, vel ad personas divinas omnes, aut unam earum. Sexto, utrum imago Dei inveniatur in homine solum secundum mentem. Septimo, utrum imago Dei sit in homine secundum potentias, aut secundum habitus, aut actus. Octavo, utrum per comparationem ad omnia obiecta. Nono, de differentia imaginis et similitudinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0005","text":"[I q. 93 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32353)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod imago Dei non sit in homine. Dicitur enim Isaiae XL, *cui similem fecistis Deum; aut quam imaginem ponetis ei?*","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0007","text":"[I q. 93 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32354)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0008","text":"Praeterea, esse Dei imaginem est proprium primogeniti, de quo dicit apostolus, ad Colos. I, *qui est imago Dei invisibilis, primogenitus omnis creaturae*. Non ergo in homine invenitur Dei imago.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0009","text":"[I q. 93 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32355)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0010","text":"Praeterea, Hilarius dicit, in libro de Synod., quod *imago est eius rei ad quam imaginatur, species indifferens*; et iterum dicit quod *imago est rei ad rem coaequandam indiscreta et unita similitudo*. Sed non est species indifferens Dei et hominis; nec potest esse aequalitas hominis ad Deum. Ergo in homine non potest esse imago Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0011","text":"[I q. 93 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32356)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *faciamus hominem ad imaginem et similitudinem nostram*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0013","text":"[I q. 93 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32357)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit in libro octoginta trium quaest., *ubi est imago, continuo est et similitudo; sed ubi est similitudo, non continuo est imago*. Ex quo patet quod similitudo est de ratione imaginis, et quod imago aliquid addit supra rationem similitudinis, scilicet quod sit ex alio expressum, imago enim dicitur ex eo quod agitur ad imitationem alterius. Unde ovum, quantumcumque sit alteri ovo simile et aequale, quia tamen non est expressum ex illo, non dicitur imago eius. Aequalitas autem non est de ratione imaginis, quia, ut Augustinus ibidem dicit, *ubi est imago, non continuo est aequalitas*; ut patet in imagine alicuius in speculo relucente. Est tamen de ratione perfectae imaginis, nam in perfecta imagine non deest aliquid imagini, quod insit illi de quo expressa est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0015","text":"Manifestum est autem quod in homine invenitur aliqua Dei similitudo, quae deducitur a Deo sicut ab exemplari, non tamen est similitudo secundum aequalitatem, quia in infinitum excedit exemplar hoc tale exemplatum. Et ideo in homine dicitur esse imago Dei, non tamen perfecta, sed imperfecta. Et hoc significat Scriptura, cum dicit hominem factum ad imaginem Dei, praepositio enim ad accessum quendam significat, qui competit rei distanti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0016","text":"[I q. 93 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32358)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod propheta loquitur de corporalibus imaginibus ab homine fabricatis, et ideo signanter dicit, *quam imaginem ponetis ei?* Sed Deus ipse sibi in homine posuit spiritualem imaginem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0018","text":"[I q. 93 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32359)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod primogenitus omnis creaturae est imago Dei perfecta, perfecte implens illud cuius imago est, et ideo dicitur imago, et nunquam ad imaginem. Homo vero et propter similitudinem dicitur imago; et propter imperfectionem similitudinis, dicitur ad imaginem. Et quia similitudo perfecta Dei non potest esse nisi in identitate naturae, imago Dei est in filio suo primogenito sicut imago regis in filio sibi connaturali; in homine autem sicut in aliena natura, sicut imago regis in nummo argenteo; ut patet per Augustinum in libro de decem chordis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0020","text":"[I q. 93 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32360)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, cum unum sit ens indivisum, eo modo dicitur species indifferens, quo una. Unum autem dicitur aliquid non solum numero aut specie aut genere, sed etiam secundum analogiam vel proportionem quandam, et sic est unitas vel convenientia creaturae ad Deum. Quod autem dicit rei ad rem coaequandam, pertinet ad rationem perfectae imaginis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0023","text":"[I q. 93 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32361)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod imago Dei inveniatur in irrationalibus creaturis. Dicit enim Dionysius, in libro de Div. Nom., *habent causata causarum suarum contingentes imagines*. Sed Deus est causa non solum rationalium creaturarum, sed etiam irrationalium. Ergo imago Dei invenitur in irrationalibus creaturis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0025","text":"[I q. 93 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32362)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0026","text":"Praeterea, quanto est expressior similitudo in aliquo, tanto magis accedit ad rationem imaginis. Sed Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod radius solaris maxime habet similitudinem divinae bonitatis. Ergo est ad imaginem Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0027","text":"[I q. 93 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32363)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0028","text":"Praeterea, quanto aliquid est magis perfectum in bonitate, tanto magis est Deo simile. Sed totum universum est perfectius in bonitate quam homo, quia etsi bona sint singula, tamen simul omnia dicuntur valde bona, Gen. I. Ergo totum universum est ad imaginem Dei, et non solum homo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0029","text":"[I q. 93 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32364)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0030","text":"Praeterea, Boetius in libro de Consol., dicit de Deo, *mundum mente gerens, similique in imagine formans*. Ergo totus mundus est ad imaginem Dei, et non solum rationalis creatura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0031","text":"[I q. 93 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32365)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0032","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, VI super Gen. ad Litt., *hoc excellit in homine, quia Deus ad imaginem suam hominem fecit, propter hoc quod dedit ei mentem intellectualem, qua praestat pecoribus*. Ea ergo quae non habent intellectum, non sunt ad imaginem Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0033","text":"[I q. 93 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32366)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0034","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod non quaelibet similitudo, etiam si sit expressa ex altero, sufficit ad rationem imaginis. Si enim similitudo sit secundum genus tantum, vel secundum aliquod accidens commune, non propter hoc dicetur aliquid esse ad imaginem alterius, non enim posset dici quod vermis qui oritur ex homine, sit imago hominis propter similitudinem generis; neque iterum potest dici quod, si aliquid fiat album ad similitudinem alterius, quod propter hoc sit ad eius imaginem, quia album est accidens commune pluribus speciebus. Requiritur autem ad rationem imaginis quod sit similitudo secundum speciem, sicut imago regis est in filio suo, vel ad minus secundum aliquod accidens proprium speciei, et praecipue secundum figuram, sicut hominis imago dicitur esse in cupro. Unde signanter Hilarius dicit quod imago est species indifferens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0035","text":"Manifestum est autem quod similitudo speciei attenditur secundum ultimam differentiam. Assimilantur autem aliqua Deo, primo quidem, et maxime communiter, inquantum sunt; secundo vero, inquantum vivunt; tertio vero, inquantum sapiunt vel intelligunt. Quae, ut Augustinus dicit in libro octoginta trium quaest., *ita sunt Deo similitudine proxima, ut in creaturis nihil sit propinquius*. Sic ergo patet quod solae intellectuales creaturae, proprie loquendo, sunt ad imaginem Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0036","text":"[I q. 93 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32367)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0037","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod omne imperfectum est quaedam participatio perfecti. Et ideo etiam ea quae deficiunt a ratione imaginis, inquantum tamen aliqualem Dei similitudinem habent, participant aliquid de ratione imaginis. Et ideo Dionysius dicit quod causata habent causarum contingentes imagines, idest quantum contingit ea habere, et non simpliciter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0038","text":"[I q. 93 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32368)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0039","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Dionysius assimilat radium solarem divinae bonitati quantum ad causalitatem; non secundum dignitatem naturae, quae requiritur ad rationem imaginis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0040","text":"[I q. 93 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32369)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0041","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod universum est perfectius in bonitate quam intellectualis creatura extensive et diffusive. Sed intensive et collective similitudo divinae perfectionis magis invenitur in intellectuali creatura, quae est capax summi boni. Vel dicendum quod pars non dividitur contra totum, sed contra aliam partem. Unde cum dicitur quod sola natura intellectualis est ad imaginem Dei, non excluditur quin universum, secundum aliquam sui partem, sit ad imaginem Dei; sed excluduntur aliae partes universi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0042","text":"[I q. 93 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32370)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0043","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod imago accipitur a Boetio secundum rationem similitudinis qua artificiatum imitatur speciem artis quae est in mente artificis, sic autem quaelibet creatura est imago rationis exemplaris quam habet in mente divina. Sic autem non loquimur nunc de imagine, sed secundum quod attenditur secundum similitudinem in natura; prout scilicet primo enti assimilantur omnia, inquantum sunt entia; et primae vitae inquantum sunt viventia; et summae sapientiae, inquantum sunt intelligentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0044","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0045","text":"[I q. 93 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32371)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0046","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non sit magis ad imaginem Dei quam homo. Dicit enim Augustinus, in sermone de imagine, quod Deus nulli alii creaturae dedit quod sit ad imaginem suam, nisi homini. Non ergo verum est quod Angelus magis dicatur ad imaginem Dei quam homo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0047","text":"[I q. 93 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32372)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0048","text":"Praeterea, secundum Augustinum, in libro octoginta trium quaest., *homo ita est ad imaginem Dei, ut, nulla interposita creatura, formetur a Deo. Et ideo nihil est illi coniunctius*. Sed imago Dei dicitur aliqua creatura, inquantum Deo coniungitur. Ergo Angelus non est magis ad imaginem Dei quam homo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0049","text":"[I q. 93 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32373)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0050","text":"Praeterea, creatura dicitur ad imaginem Dei, inquantum est intellectualis naturae. Sed intellectualis natura non intenditur nec remittitur, non enim est de genere accidentis, cum sit in genere substantiae. Ergo Angelus non est magis ad imaginem Dei quam homo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0051","text":"[I q. 93 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32374)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0052","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Gregorius, in quadam homilia, quod *Angelus dicitur signaculum similitudinis, quia in eo similitudo divinae imaginis magis insinuatur expressa*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0053","text":"[I q. 93 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32375)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0054","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod de imagine Dei loqui dupliciter possumus. Uno modo, quantum ad id in quo primo consideratur ratio imaginis, quod est intellectualis natura. Et sic imago Dei est magis in Angelis quam sit in hominibus, quia intellectualis natura perfectior est in eis, ut ex supra dictis patet. Secundo potest considerari imago Dei in homine, quantum ad id in quo secundario consideratur, prout scilicet in homine invenitur quaedam Dei imitatio, inquantum scilicet homo est de homine, sicut Deus de Deo; et inquantum anima hominis est tota in toto corpore eius, et iterum tota in qualibet parte ipsius, sicut Deus se habet ad mundum. Et secundum haec et similia, magis invenitur Dei imago in homine quam in Angelo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0055","text":"Sed quantum ad hoc non attenditur per se ratio divinae imaginis in homine, nisi praesupposita prima imitatione, quae est secundum intellectualem naturam, alioquin etiam animalia bruta essent ad imaginem Dei. Et ideo, cum quantum ad intellectualem naturam Angelus sit magis ad imaginem Dei quam homo, simpliciter concedendum est Angelum magis esse ad imaginem Dei; hominem autem secundum quid.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0056","text":"[I q. 93 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32376)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0057","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus excludit a Dei imagine alias inferiores creaturas intellectu carentes, non autem Angelos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0058","text":"[I q. 93 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32377)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0059","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut ignis dicitur esse subtilissimum corporum secundum suam speciem, cum tamen unus ignis sit alio subtilior; ita dicitur quod nihil est coniunctius Deo quam mens humana, secundum genus intellectualis naturae; quia, sicut ipse supra praemiserat, *quae sapiunt, ita sunt illi similitudine proxima, ut in creaturis nihil sit propinquius*. Unde per hoc non excluditur quin Angelus sit magis ad Dei imaginem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0060","text":"[I q. 93 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32378)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0061","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, cum dicitur quod substantia non recipit magis et minus, non intelligitur quod una species substantiae non sit perfectior quam alia, sed quod unum et idem individuum non participet suam speciem quandoque magis, quandoque minus. Nec etiam a diversis individuis participatur species substantiae secundum magis et minus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0062","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0063","text":"[I q. 93 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32379)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0064","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod imago Dei non inveniatur in quolibet homine. Dicit enim apostolus, I ad Cor. XI, quod *vir est imago Dei, mulier autem est imago viri*. Cum ergo mulier sit individuum humanae speciei, non cuilibet individuo convenit esse imaginem Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0065","text":"[I q. 93 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32380)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0066","text":"Praeterea, apostolus dicit, Rom. VIII, quod *illos quos Deus praescivit conformes fieri imagini filii sui, hos praedestinavit*. Sed non omnes homines praedestinati sunt. Ergo non omnes homines habent conformitatem imaginis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0067","text":"[I q. 93 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32381)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0068","text":"Praeterea, similitudo est de ratione imaginis, ut supra dictum est. Sed per peccatum fit homo Deo dissimilis. Ergo amittit Dei imaginem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0069","text":"[I q. 93 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32382)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0070","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo XXXVIII, *veruntamen in imagine pertransit homo*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0071","text":"[I q. 93 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32383)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0072","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum homo secundum intellectualem naturam ad imaginem Dei esse dicatur, secundum hoc est maxime ad imaginem Dei, secundum quod intellectualis natura Deum maxime imitari potest. Imitatur autem intellectualis natura maxime Deum quantum ad hoc, quod Deus seipsum intelligit et amat. Unde imago Dei tripliciter potest considerari in homine. Uno quidem modo, secundum quod homo habet aptitudinem naturalem ad intelligendum et amandum Deum, et haec aptitudo consistit in ipsa natura mentis, quae est communis omnibus hominibus. Alio modo, secundum quod homo actu vel habitu Deum cognoscit et amat, sed tamen imperfecte, et haec est imago per conformitatem gratiae. Tertio modo, secundum quod homo Deum actu cognoscit et amat perfecte, et sic attenditur imago secundum similitudinem gloriae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0073","text":"Unde super illud Psalmi IV, *signatum est super nos lumen vultus tui, domine*, Glossa distinguit triplicem imaginem, scilicet creationis, recreationis et similitudinis. Prima ergo imago invenitur in omnibus hominibus; secunda in iustis tantum; tertia vero solum in beatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0074","text":"[I q. 93 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32384)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0075","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod tam in viro quam in muliere invenitur Dei imago quantum ad id in quo principaliter ratio imaginis consistit, scilicet quantum ad intellectualem naturam. Unde Gen. I, cum dixisset, *ad imaginem Dei creavit illum*, scilicet hominem, subdidit, *masculum et feminam creavit eos*, et dixit pluraliter eos, ut Augustinus dicit, ne intelligatur in uno individuo uterque sexus fuisse coniunctus. Sed quantum ad aliquid secundario imago Dei invenitur in viro, secundum quod non invenitur in muliere, nam vir est principium mulieris et finis, sicut Deus est principium et finis totius creaturae. Unde cum apostolus dixisset quod *vir imago et gloria est Dei, mulier autem est gloria viri*; ostendit quare hoc dixerit, subdens, *non enim vir est ex muliere, sed mulier ex viro; et vir non est creatus propter mulierem, sed mulier propter virum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0076","text":"[I q. 93 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32385)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0077","text":"Ad secundum et tertium dicendum quod illae rationes procedunt de imagine quae est secundum conformitatem gratiae et gloriae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0078","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0079","text":"[I q. 93 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32386)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0080","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in homine non sit imago Dei quantum ad Trinitatem divinarum personarum. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro de fide ad Petrum, *una est sanctae Trinitatis essentialiter divinitas, et imago ad quam factus est homo*. Et Hilarius, in V de Trin., dicit quod *homo fit ad communem Trinitatis imaginem*. Est ergo in homine imago Dei quantum ad essentiam, et non quantum ad Trinitatem personarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0081","text":"[I q. 93 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32387)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0082","text":"Praeterea, in libro de Eccles. Dogmat. dicitur quod imago Dei attenditur in homine secundum aeternitatem. Damascenus etiam dicit quod *hominem esse ad imaginem Dei, significat intellectuale, et arbitrio liberum, et per se potestativum*. Gregorius etiam Nyssenus dicit quod, cum Scriptura dixit hominem factum ad imaginem Dei, *aequale est ac si diceret humanam naturam omnis boni factam esse participem; bonitatis enim plenitudo divinitas est*. Haec autem omnia non pertinent ad distinctionem personarum, sed magis ad essentiae unitatem. Ergo in homine est imago Dei, non secundum Trinitatem personarum, sed secundum essentiae unitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0083","text":"[I q. 93 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32388)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0084","text":"Praeterea, imago ducit in cognitionem eius cuius est imago. Si igitur in homine est imago Dei secundum Trinitatem personarum, cum homo per naturalem rationem seipsum cognoscere possit, sequeretur quod per naturalem cognitionem posset homo cognoscere Trinitatem divinarum personarum. Quod est falsum, ut supra ostensum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0085","text":"[I q. 93 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32389)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0086","text":"Praeterea, nomen imaginis non cuilibet trium personarum convenit, sed soli filio, dicit enim Augustinus, in VI de Trin., quod *solus filius est imago patris*. Si igitur in homine attenderetur Dei imago secundum personam, non esset in homine imago totius Trinitatis, sed filii tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0087","text":"[I q. 93 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32390)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0088","text":"Sed contra est quod Hilarius, in IV de Trin., per hoc quod homo dicitur ad imaginem Dei factus, ostendit pluralitatem divinarum personarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0089","text":"[I q. 93 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32391)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0090","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra habitum est, distinctio divinarum personarum non est nisi secundum originem, vel potius secundum relationes originis. Non autem est idem modus originis in omnibus, sed modus originis uniuscuiusque est secundum convenientiam suae naturae, aliter enim producuntur animata, aliter inanimata; aliter animalia, atque aliter plantae. Unde manifestum est quod distinctio divinarum personarum est secundum quod divinae naturae convenit. Unde esse ad imaginem Dei secundum imitationem divinae naturae, non excludit hoc quod est esse ad imaginem Dei secundum repraesentationem trium personarum; sed magis unum ad alterum sequitur. Sic igitur dicendum est in homine esse imaginem Dei et quantum ad naturam divinam, et quantum ad Trinitatem personarum, nam et in ipso Deo in tribus personis una existit natura.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0091","text":"[I q. 93 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32392)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0092","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ad duo prima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0093","text":"[I q. 93 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32393)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0094","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ratio illa procederet, si imago Dei esset in homine perfecte repraesentans Deum. Sed, sicut Augustinus dicit in XV de Trin., maxima est differentia huius Trinitatis quae est in nobis, ad Trinitatem divinam. Et ideo, ut ipse ibidem dicit, *Trinitatem quae in nobis est, videmus potius quam credimus, Deum vero esse Trinitatem, credimus potius quam videmus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0095","text":"[I q. 93 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32394)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0096","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod quidam dixerunt in homine esse solum imaginem filii. Sed hoc improbat Augustinus, in XII de Trin. Primo quidem, per hoc quod, cum secundum aequalitatem essentiae filius sit patri similis, necesse est, si homo sit factus ad similitudinem filii, quod sit factus ad similitudinem patris. Secundo quia, si homo esset factus solum ad imaginem filii, non diceret pater, *faciamus hominem ad imaginem et similitudinem nostram*, sed tuam. Cum ergo dicitur, ad imaginem Dei fecit illum, non est intelligendum quod pater fecerit hominem solum ad imaginem filii, qui est Deus, ut quidam exposuerunt, sed intelligendum est quod Deus Trinitas fecit hominem ad imaginem suam, idest totius Trinitatis. Cum autem dicitur quod Deus fecit hominem ad imaginem suam, dupliciter potest intelligi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0097","text":"Uno modo, quod haec praepositio ad designet terminum factionis, ut sit sensus, faciamus hominem taliter, ut sit in eo imago. Alio modo, haec praepositio ad potest designare causam exemplarem; sicut cum dicitur, iste liber est factus ad illum. Et sic imago Dei est ipsa essentia divina, quae abusive imago dicitur, secundum quod imago ponitur pro exemplari. Vel, secundum quod quidam dicunt, divina essentia dicitur imago, quia secundum eam una persona aliam imitatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0098","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0099","text":"[I q. 93 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32395)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0100","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod imago Dei non sit in homine solum secundum mentem. Dicit enim apostolus, I ad Cor. XI, quod vir est imago Dei. Sed vir non est solum mens. Ergo imago Dei non attenditur solum secundum mentem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0101","text":"[I q. 93 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32396)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0102","text":"Praeterea, Gen. I, *creavit Deus hominem ad imaginem suam, ad imaginem Dei creavit illum, masculum et feminam creavit eos*. Sed distinctio masculi et feminae est secundum corpus. Ergo etiam secundum corpus attenditur Dei imago in homine, et non secundum mentem tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0103","text":"[I q. 93 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32397)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0104","text":"Praeterea, imago praecipue videtur attendi secundum figuram. Sed figura ad corpus pertinet. Ergo imago Dei attenditur in homine etiam secundum corpus, et non secundum mentem tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0105","text":"[I q. 93 a. 6 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32398)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0106","text":"Praeterea, secundum Augustinum, XII super Gen. ad Litt., triplex visio invenitur in nobis, scilicet corporalis, spiritualis sive imaginaria, et intellectualis. Si ergo secundum visionem intellectualem, quae ad mentem pertinet, est aliqua Trinitas in nobis, secundum quam sumus ad imaginem Dei; pari ratione et in aliis visionibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0107","text":"[I q. 93 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32399)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0108","text":"Sed contra est quod apostolus dicit, ad Eph. IV, *renovamini spiritu mentis vestrae, et induite novum hominem*, ex quo datur intelligi quod renovatio nostra, quae fit secundum novi hominis indumentum, ad mentem pertinet. Sed ad Col. III, dicit, *induentes novum hominem, qui renovatur in agnitionem Dei, secundum imaginem eius qui creavit eum*, ubi renovationem quae est secundum novi hominis indumentum, attribuit imagini Dei. Esse ergo ad imaginem Dei pertinet solum ad mentem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0109","text":"[I q. 93 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32400)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0110","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum in omnibus creaturis sit aliqualis Dei similitudo, in sola creatura rationali invenitur similitudo Dei per modum imaginis, ut supra dictum est, in aliis autem creaturis per modum vestigii. Id autem in quo creatura rationalis excedit alias creaturas, est intellectus sive mens. Unde relinquitur quod nec in ipsa rationali creatura invenitur Dei imago, nisi secundum mentem. In aliis vero partibus, si quas habet rationalis creatura, invenitur similitudo vestigii; sicut et in ceteris rebus quibus secundum partes huiusmodi assimilatur. Cuius ratio manifeste cognosci potest, si attendatur modus quo repraesentat vestigium, et quo repraesentat imago. Imago enim repraesentat secundum similitudinem speciei, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0111","text":"Vestigium autem repraesentat per modum effectus qui sic repraesentat suam causam, quod tamen ad speciei similitudinem non pertingit, impressiones enim quae ex motu animalium relinquuntur, dicuntur vestigia; et similiter cinis dicitur vestigium ignis; et desolatio terrae, vestigium hostilis exercitus. Potest ergo huiusmodi differentia attendi inter creaturas rationales et alias creaturas, et quantum ad hoc quod in creaturis repraesentatur similitudo divinae naturae, et quantum ad hoc quod in eis repraesentatur similitudo Trinitatis increatae. Nam quantum ad similitudinem divinae naturae pertinet, creaturae rationales videntur quodammodo ad repraesentationem speciei pertingere, inquantum imitantur Deum non solum in hoc quod est et vivit, sed etiam in hoc quod intelligit, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0112","text":"Aliae vero creaturae non intelligunt; sed apparet in eis quoddam vestigium intellectus producentis, si earum dispositio consideretur. Similiter, cum increata Trinitas distinguatur secundum processionem verbi a dicente, et amoris ab utroque, ut supra habitum est; in creatura rationali, in qua invenitur processio verbi secundum intellectum, et processio amoris secundum voluntatem, potest dici imago Trinitatis increatae per quandam repraesentationem speciei. In aliis autem creaturis non invenitur principium verbi, et verbum, et amor; sed apparet in eis quoddam vestigium quod haec inveniantur in causa producente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0113","text":"Nam hoc ipsum quod creatura habet substantiam modificatam et finitam, demonstrat quod sit a quodam principio; species vero eius demonstrat verbum facientis, sicut forma domus demonstrat conceptionem artificis; ordo vero demonstrat amorem producentis, quo effectus ordinatur ad bonum, sicut usus aedificii demonstrat artificis voluntatem. Sic igitur in homine invenitur Dei similitudo per modum imaginis secundum mentem; sed secundum alias partes eius, per modum vestigii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0114","text":"[I q. 93 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32401)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0115","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod homo dicitur imago Dei, non quia ipse essentialiter sit imago, sed quia in eo est Dei imago impressa secundum mentem; sicut denarius dicitur imago Caesaris, inquantum habet Caesaris imaginem. Unde non oportet quod secundum quamlibet partem hominis accipiatur Dei imago.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0116","text":"[I q. 93 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32402)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0117","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit XII de Trin., quidam imaginem Trinitatis in homine posuerunt, non secundum unum individuum, sed secundum plura; dicentes quod *vir patris personam intimat; filii vero personam, quod de viro ita processit ut de illo nasceretur; atque ita tertiam personam, velut spiritum sanctum, dicunt esse mulierem, quae ita de viro processit ut non ipsa esset filius aut filia*. Quod prima facie absurdum videtur. Primo quidem, quia sequeretur quod spiritus sanctus esset principium filii, sicut mulier est principium prolis quae nascitur de viro. Secundo, quia unus homo non esset nisi ad imaginem unius personae. Tertio, quia secundum hoc Scriptura de imagine Dei in homine mentionem facere non debuisset, nisi producta iam prole.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0118","text":"Et ideo dicendum est quod Scriptura, postquam dixerat, ad imaginem Dei creavit illum, addidit, masculum et feminam creavit eos, non ut imago Dei secundum distinctiones sexuum attendatur; sed quia imago Dei utrique sexui est communis, cum sit secundum mentem, in qua non est distinctio sexuum. Unde apostolus, ad Col. III, postquam dixerat, secundum imaginem eius qui creavit illum, subdit, ubi non est masculus et femina.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0119","text":"[I q. 93 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32403)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0120","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, quamvis imago Dei in homine non accipiatur secundum figuram corpoream, tamen *corpus hominis, quia solum inter terrenorum animalium corpora non pronum in alvum prostratum est, sed tale est ut ad contemplandum caelum sit aptius, magis in hoc ad imaginem et similitudinem Dei, quam cetera corpora animalium, factum iure videri potest*; ut Augustinus dicit in libro octoginta trium quaest. Quod tamen non est sic intelligendum, quasi in corpore hominis sit imago Dei, sed quia ipsa figura humani corporis repraesentat imaginem Dei in anima, per modum vestigii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0121","text":"[I q. 93 a. 6 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32404)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0122","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod tam in visione corporali quam in visione imaginaria invenitur quaedam Trinitas, ut Augustinus dicit in libro de Trin. In visione enim corporali est quidem primo species exterioris corporis; secundo vero ipsa visio, quae fit per impressionem cuiusdam similitudinis praedictae speciei in visum; tertio est ibi intentio voluntatis applicans visum ad videndum, et eum in re visa detinens. Similiter etiam in visione imaginaria invenitur primo quidem species in memoria reservata; secundo ipsa imaginaria visio, quae provenit ex hoc quod acies animae, idest ipsa vis imaginaria, informatur secundum praedictam speciem; tertio vero invenitur intentio voluntatis coniungens utrumque. Sed utraque Trinitas deficit a ratione divinae imaginis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0123","text":"Nam ipsa species exterioris corporis est extra naturam animae, species autem quae est in memoria, etsi non sit extra animam, est tamen adventitia animae, et ita utrobique deficit repraesentatio connaturalitatis et coaeternitatis divinarum personarum. Visio vero corporalis non procedit tantum a specie exterioris corporis, sed simul cum hoc a sensu videntis, et similiter visio imaginaria non solum procedit a specie quae in memoria conservatur, sed etiam a virtute imaginativa, et ita per hoc non repraesentatur convenienter processio filii a solo patre. Intentio vero voluntatis, quae coniungit duo praedicta, non ex eis procedit, neque in visione corporea neque in spirituali, unde non convenienter repraesentatur processio spiritus sancti a patre et filio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0124","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0125","text":"[I q. 93 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32405)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0126","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod imago Dei non inveniatur in anima secundum actus. Dicit enim Augustinus, XI de Civ. Dei, quod homo factus est ad imaginem Dei, secundum quod *sumus, et nos esse novimus, et id esse ac nosse diligimus*. Sed esse non significat actum. Ergo imago Dei non attenditur in anima secundum actus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0127","text":"[I q. 93 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32406)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0128","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus, in IX de Trin., assignat imaginem Dei in anima secundum haec tria, quae sunt mens, notitia et amor. Mens autem non significat actum; sed magis potentiam, vel etiam essentiam intellectivae animae. Ergo imago Dei non attenditur secundum actus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0129","text":"[I q. 93 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32407)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0130","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus, X de Trin., assignat imaginem Trinitatis in anima secundum memoriam, intelligentiam et voluntatem. Sed haec tria sunt vires naturales animae, ut Magister dicit, III distinctione I libri Sent. Ergo imago attenditur secundum potentias, et non secundum actus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0131","text":"[I q. 93 a. 7 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32408)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0132","text":"Praeterea, imago Trinitatis semper manet in anima. Sed actus non semper manet. Ergo imago Dei non attenditur in anima secundum actus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0133","text":"[I q. 93 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32409)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0134","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus, XI de Trin., assignat Trinitatem in inferioribus animae partibus secundum actualem visionem sensibilem et imaginariam. Ergo et Trinitas quae est in mente, secundum quam homo est ad imaginem Dei, debet attendi secundum actualem visionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0135","text":"[I q. 93 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32410)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0136","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, ad rationem imaginis pertinet aliqualis repraesentatio speciei. Si ergo imago Trinitatis divinae debet accipi in anima, oportet quod secundum illud principaliter attendatur, quod maxime accedit, prout possibile est, ad repraesentandum speciem divinarum personarum. Divinae autem personae distinguuntur secundum processionem verbi a dicente, et amoris connectentis utrumque. Verbum autem in anima nostra sine actuali cogitatione esse non potest, ut Augustinus dicit XIV de Trin. Et ideo primo et principaliter attenditur imago Trinitatis in mente secundum actus, prout scilicet ex notitia quam habemus, cogitando interius verbum formamus, et ex hoc in amorem prorumpimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0137","text":"Sed quia principia actuum sunt habitus et potentiae; unumquodque autem virtualiter est in suo principio, secundario, et quasi ex consequenti, imago Trinitatis potest attendi in anima secundum potentias, et praecipue secundum habitus, prout in eis scilicet actus virtualiter existunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0138","text":"[I q. 93 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32411)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0139","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod esse nostrum ad imaginem Dei pertinet, quod est nobis proprium supra alia animalia; quod quidem esse competit nobis inquantum mentem habemus. Et ideo eadem est haec Trinitas cum illa quam Augustinus ponit in IX de Trin., quae consistit in mente, notitia et amore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0140","text":"[I q. 93 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32412)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0141","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Augustinus hanc Trinitatem primo adinvenit in mente. Sed quia mens, etsi se totam quodammodo cognoscat, etiam quodammodo se ignorat, prout scilicet est ab aliis distincta; et sic etiam se quaerit, ut Augustinus consequenter probat in X de Trin., ideo, quasi notitia non totaliter menti coaequetur, accipit in anima tria quaedam propria mentis, scilicet memoriam, intelligentiam et voluntatem, quae nullus ignorat se habere, et in istis tribus potius imaginem Trinitatis assignat, quasi prima assignatio sit quodammodo deficiens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0142","text":"[I q. 93 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32413)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0143","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus probat XIV de Trin., intelligere dicimur et velle seu amare aliqua, et quando de his cogitamus, et quando de his non cogitamus. Sed quando sine cogitatione sunt, ad solam memoriam pertinent; quae nihil est aliud, secundum ipsum, quam habitualis retentio notitiae et amoris. Sed quia, ut ipse dicit, *verbum ibi esse sine cogitatione non potest (cogitamus enim omne quod dicimus etiam illo interiori verbo quod ad nullius gentis pertinet linguam), in tribus potius illis imago ista cognoscitur, memoria scilicet, intelligentia et voluntate. Hanc autem nunc dico intelligentiam, qua intelligimus cogitantes; et eam voluntatem sive amorem vel dilectionem, quae istam prolem parentemque coniungit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0144","text":"Ex quo patet quod imaginem divinae Trinitatis potius ponit in intelligentia et voluntate actuali, quam secundum quod sunt in habituali retentione memoriae, licet etiam quantum ad hoc, aliquo modo sit imago Trinitatis in anima, ut ibidem dicitur. Et sic patet quod memoria, intelligentia et voluntas non sunt tres vires, ut in sententiis dicitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0145","text":"[I q. 93 a. 7 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32414)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0146","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod aliquis respondere posset per hoc quod Augustinus dicit XIV de Trin., quod *mens semper sui meminit, semper se intelligit et amat*. Quod quidam sic intelligunt, quasi animae adsit actualis intelligentia et amor sui ipsius. Sed hunc intellectum excludit per hoc quod subdit, quod *non semper se cogitat discretam ab his quae non sunt quod ipsa*. Et sic patet quod anima semper intelligit et amat se, non actualiter, sed habitualiter. Quamvis etiam dici possit quod, percipiendo actum suum, seipsam intelligit quandocumque aliquid intelligit. Sed quia non semper est actu intelligens, ut patet in dormiente, ideo oportet dicere quod actus, etsi non semper maneant in seipsis, manent tamen semper in suis principiis, scilicet potentiis et habitibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0147","text":"Unde Augustinus dicit, XIV de Trin., *si secundum hoc facta est ad imaginem Dei anima rationalis, quod uti ratione atque intellectu ad intelligendum et conspiciendum Deum potest, ab initio quo esse coepit, fuit in ea Dei imago*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0148","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0149","text":"[I q. 93 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32415)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0150","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod imago divinae Trinitatis sit in anima non solum per comparationem ad obiectum quod est Deus. Imago enim divinae Trinitatis invenitur in anima, sicut dictum est, secundum quod verbum in nobis procedit a dicente et amor ab utroque. Sed hoc invenitur in nobis secundum quodcumque obiectum. Ergo secundum quodcumque obiectum invenitur in mente nostra imago divinae Trinitatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0151","text":"[I q. 93 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32416)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0152","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, in XII de Trin., quod *cum quaerimus in anima Trinitatem, in tota quaerimus, non separantes actionem rationalem in temporalibus a contemplatione aeternorum*. Ergo etiam secundum temporalia obiecta invenitur imago Trinitatis in anima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0153","text":"[I q. 93 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32417)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0154","text":"Praeterea, quod Deum intelligamus et amemus, convenit nobis secundum gratiae donum. Si igitur secundum memoriam, intelligentiam et voluntatem seu dilectionem Dei, attendatur imago Trinitatis in anima, non erit imago Dei in homine secundum naturam, sed secundum gratiam. Et sic non erit omnibus communis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0155","text":"[I q. 93 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32418)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0156","text":"Praeterea, sancti qui sunt in patria, maxime conformantur imagini Dei secundum gloriae visionem, unde dicitur, II ad Cor. III, *in eandem imaginem transformamur, a claritate in claritatem*. Sed secundum visionem gloriae temporalia cognoscuntur. Ergo etiam per comparationem ad temporalia, Dei imago attenditur in nobis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0157","text":"[I q. 93 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32419)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0158","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XIV de Trin., quod *non propterea est Dei imago in mente, quia sui meminit, et intelligit et diligit se, sed quia potest etiam meminisse, intelligere et amare Deum, a quo facta est*. Multo igitur minus secundum alia obiecta attenditur imago Dei in mente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0159","text":"[I q. 93 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32420)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0160","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, imago importat similitudinem utcumque pertingentem ad speciei repraesentationem. Unde oportet quod imago divinae Trinitatis attendatur in anima secundum aliquid quod repraesentat divinas personas repraesentatione speciei, sicut est possibile creaturae. Distinguuntur autem divinae personae, ut dictum est, secundum processionem verbi a dicente, et amoris ab utroque. Verbum autem Dei nascitur de Deo secundum notitiam sui ipsius, et amor procedit a Deo secundum quod seipsum amat. Manifestum est autem quod diversitas obiectorum diversificat speciem verbi et amoris, non enim idem est specie in corde hominis verbum conceptum de lapide et de equo, nec idem specie amor. Attenditur igitur divina imago in homine secundum verbum conceptum de Dei notitia, et amorem exinde derivatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0161","text":"Et sic imago Dei attenditur in anima secundum quod fertur, vel nata est ferri in Deum. Fertur autem in aliquid mens dupliciter, uno modo, directe et immediate; alio modo, indirecte et mediate, sicut cum aliquis, videndo imaginem hominis in speculo, dicitur ferri in ipsum hominem. Et ideo Augustinus dicit, in XIV de Trin., quod *mens meminit sui, intelligit se, et diligit se, hoc si cernimus, cernimus Trinitatem; nondum quidem Deum, sed iam imaginem Dei*. Sed hoc est, non quia fertur mens in seipsam absolute, sed prout per hoc ulterius potest ferri in Deum; ut patet per auctoritatem supra inductam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0162","text":"[I q. 93 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32421)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0163","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ad rationem imaginis, non solum oportet attendere quod aliquid procedat ab aliquo; sed etiam quid a quo procedat, scilicet quod verbum Dei procedit a notitia de Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0164","text":"[I q. 93 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32422)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0165","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod in tota quidem anima invenitur aliqua Trinitas, non quidem ita quod praeter actionem temporalium et contemplationem aeternorum, *quaeratur aliquod tertium quo Trinitas impleatur*, prout ibidem subditur. Sed in illa parte rationis quae derivatur a parte temporalium, etsi *Trinitas inveniri possit, non tamen imago Dei potest inveniri*, ut postea dicitur, quia huiusmodi temporalium notitia adventitia est animae. Et habitus etiam ipsi quibus temporalia cognoscuntur, non semper adsunt; sed quandoque quidem praesentialiter adsunt, quandoque autem secundum memoriam tantum, etiam postquam adesse incipiunt. Sicut patet de fide, quae temporaliter nobis advenit in praesenti, in statu autem futurae beatitudinis iam non erit fides, sed memoria fidei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0166","text":"[I q. 93 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32423)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0167","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod meritoria Dei cognitio et dilectio non est nisi per gratiam. Est tamen aliqua Dei cognitio et dilectio naturalis, ut supra habitum est. Et hoc etiam ipsum naturale est, quod mens ad intelligendum Deum ratione uti potest, secundum quod imaginem Dei semper diximus permanere in mente, sive haec imago Dei ita sit obsoleta, quasi obumbrata, ut pene nulla sit, ut in his qui non habent usum rationis; sive sit obscura atque deformis, ut in peccatoribus; sive sit clara et pulchra, ut in iustis, sicut Augustinus dicit, XIV de Trin.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0168","text":"[I q. 93 a. 8 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32424)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0169","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod secundum visionem gloriae, temporalia videbuntur in ipso Deo; et ideo huiusmodi temporalium visio ad Dei imaginem pertinebit. Et hoc est quod Augustinus dicit, XIV de Trin., quod *in illa natura cui mens feliciter adhaerebit, immutabile videbit omne quod viderit*. Nam et in ipso verbo increato sunt rationes omnium creaturarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0170","text":"## Articulus 9","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0171","text":"[I q. 93 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32425)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0172","text":"Ad nonum sic proceditur. Videtur quod similitudo ab imagine non convenienter distinguatur. Genus enim non convenienter distinguitur a specie. Sed similitudo comparatur ad imaginem, ut genus ad speciem, quia *ubi est imago, ibi est continuo similitudo, sed non convertitur*, ut dicitur in libro octoginta trium quaest. Ergo inconvenienter similitudo ab imagine distinguitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0173","text":"[I q. 93 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32426)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0174","text":"Praeterea, ratio imaginis attenditur non solum secundum repraesentationem divinarum personarum, sed etiam secundum repraesentationem divinae essentiae, ad quam repraesentationem pertinet immortalitas et indivisibilitas. Non ergo convenienter dicitur quod *similitudo est in essentia, quia est immortalis et indivisibilis; imago autem in aliis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0175","text":"[I q. 93 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32427)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0176","text":"Praeterea, imago Dei in homine est triplex, scilicet naturae, gratiae et gloriae, ut supra habitum est. Sed innocentia et iustitia ad gratiam pertinent. Inconvenienter ergo dicitur quod *imago accipitur secundum memoriam, intelligentiam et voluntatem; similitudo autem secundum innocentiam et iustitiam*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0177","text":"[I q. 93 a. 9 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32428)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0178","text":"Praeterea, cognitio veritatis ad intelligentiam pertinet, amor autem virtutis ad voluntatem, quae sunt duae partes imaginis. Non ergo convenienter dicitur quod *imago sit in cognitione veritatis, similitudo in dilectione virtutis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0179","text":"[I q. 93 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32429)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0180","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in libro octoginta trium quaest., *sunt qui non frustra intelligunt duo dicta esse ad imaginem et similitudinem; cum, si una res esset, unum nomen sufficere potuisset*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0181","text":"[I q. 93 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32430)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0182","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod similitudo quaedam unitas est, unum enim in qualitate similitudinem causat, ut dicitur in V Metaphys. Unum autem, cum sit de transcendentibus, et commune est omnibus, et ad singula potest aptari; sicut et bonum et verum. Unde, sicut bonum alicui rei particulari potest comparari ut praeambulum ad ipsam, et ut subsequens, prout designat aliquam perfectionem ipsius; ita etiam est de comparatione similitudinis ad imaginem. Est enim bonum praeambulum ad hominem, secundum quod homo est quoddam particulare bonum, et rursus bonum subsequitur ad hominem, inquantum aliquem hominem specialiter dicimus esse bonum, propter perfectionem virtutis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0183","text":"Et similiter similitudo consideratur ut praeambulum ad imaginem, inquantum est communius quam imago, ut supra dictum est, consideratur etiam ut subsequens ad imaginem, inquantum significat quandam imaginis perfectionem; dicimus enim imaginem alicuius esse similem vel non similem ei cuius est imago, inquantum perfecte vel imperfecte repraesentat ipsum. Sic ergo similitudo potest ab imagine distingui dupliciter. Uno modo, prout est praeambula ad ipsam, et in pluribus existens. Et sic similitudo attenditur secundum ea quae sunt communiora proprietatibus naturae intellectualis, secundum quas proprie attenditur imago. Et secundum hoc dicitur in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod spiritus, idest mens, ad imaginem Dei, nullo dubitante, factus est, cetera autem hominis, scilicet quae pertinent ad inferiores partes animae, vel etiam ad ipsum corpus, ad similitudinem facta esse aliqui volunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0184","text":"Secundum hoc etiam in libro de quantitate animae dicitur quod similitudo Dei attenditur in anima, inquantum est incorruptibilis, nam corruptibile et incorruptibile sunt differentiae entis communis. Alio modo potest considerari similitudo, secundum quod significat imaginis expressionem et perfectionem. Et secundum hoc Damascenus dicit quod *id quod est secundum imaginem, intellectuale significat, et arbitrio liberum per se potestativum, quod autem secundum similitudinem, virtutis, secundum quod homini possibile est inesse, similitudinem*. Et ad idem refertur quod similitudo dicitur ad dilectionem virtutis pertinere, non enim est virtus sine dilectione virtutis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0185","text":"[I q. 93 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32431)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0186","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod similitudo non distinguitur ab imagine secundum communem rationem similitudinis (sic enim includitur in ratione ipsius imaginis), sed secundum quod aliqua similitudo deficit a ratione imaginis, vel etiam est imaginis perfectiva.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0187","text":"[I q. 93 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32432)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0188","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod essentia animae pertinet ad imaginem, prout repraesentat divinam essentiam secundum ea quae sunt propria intellectualis naturae, non autem secundum conditiones consequentes ens in communi, ut est esse simplicem et indissolubilem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0189","text":"[I q. 93 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32433)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0190","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod etiam virtutes quaedam naturaliter insunt animae, ad minus secundum quaedam earum semina, et secundum has posset attendi similitudo naturalis. Quamvis non sit inconveniens ut id quod secundum assignationem unam dicitur imago, secundum aliam dicatur similitudo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0191","text":"[I q. 93 a. 9 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32434)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0192","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod dilectio verbi, quod est amata notitia, pertinet ad rationem imaginis, sed dilectio virtutis pertinet ad similitudinem, sicut et virtus.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"d96e7b114c627fadada705fa94252ae910e93bedd25c39c23e8ca266b426ee40","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T152746341322Z_b9cbcae2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 93","source_sha256":"d96e7b114c627fadada705fa94252ae910e93bedd25c39c23e8ca266b426ee40","sha256":"4194c287f73101da2ee8217490a1354dfa80a169099bb602ca1c8c0a0713a646","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:27:46.341334+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0002","text":"[I q. 93 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32352)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the end or term of the production of man, insofar as he is said to be made to the image and likeness of God. And concerning this, nine questions are asked. First, whether there is an image of God in man. Second, whether the image of God is in irrational creatures. Third, whether the image of God is more in an angel than in man. Fourth, whether the image of God is in every man. Fifth, whether in man there is an image of God by comparison with the essence, or with all the divine persons, or with one of them. Sixth, whether the image of God is found in man only according to the mind. Seventh, whether the image of God is in man according to powers, or according to habits, or acts. Eighth, whether by comparison with all objects. Ninth, concerning the difference between image and likeness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0005","text":"[I q. 93 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32353)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the image of God is not in man. For it is said in Isaiah 40: *To whom have you likened God, or what image will you set before him?*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0007","text":"[I q. 93 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32354)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0008","text":"Further, to be the image of God is proper to the firstborn, of whom the Apostle says, in Colossians 1: *who is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of every creature.* Therefore the image of God is not found in man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0009","text":"[I q. 93 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32355)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0010","text":"Further, Hilary says, in the book *On the Synods*, that *an image is an undifferentiated form of that thing according to which it is imaged*; and again he says that *an image is an undistinguishable and united likeness of one thing to another thing to be made equal to it*. But the form of God and man is not undifferentiated; nor can there be equality of man to God. Therefore there cannot be an image of God in man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0011","text":"[I q. 93 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32356)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0012","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Genesis 1: *Let us make man to our image and likeness.*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0013","text":"[I q. 93 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32357)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0014","text":"I answer that, as Augustine says in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, *where there is an image, there is at once also a likeness; but where there is a likeness, there is not at once an image*. From this it is clear that likeness belongs to the notion of image, and that image adds something above the notion of likeness, namely, that it be expressed from another; for an image is so called from the fact that it is made in imitation of another. Hence an egg, however similar and equal it may be to another egg, nevertheless, since it is not expressed from that one, is not called its image. Equality, however, does not belong to the notion of image, because, as Augustine says in the same place, *where there is an image, there is not at once equality*; as is clear in the image of someone reflected in a mirror. Yet it belongs to the notion of a perfect image, for in a perfect image there is lacking to the image nothing that is present in that from which it is expressed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0015","text":"Now it is evident that in man some likeness of God is found, which is derived from God as from an exemplar; yet it is not a likeness according to equality, because the exemplar infinitely exceeds such an exemplatum. And therefore the image of God is said to be in man, yet not perfectly but imperfectly. And this Scripture signifies when it says that man was made *to* the image of God; for the preposition *to* signifies a certain approach, which befits a thing that is distant.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0016","text":"[I q. 93 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32358)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the prophet speaks of corporeal images fashioned by man, and therefore he says pointedly: *What image will you set before him?* But God himself has placed in man a spiritual image of himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0018","text":"[I q. 93 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32359)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0019","text":"To the second, it must be said that the firstborn of every creature is the perfect image of God, perfectly fulfilling that of which he is the image, and therefore he is called “image,” and never “to the image.” But man, on account of likeness, is called an image; and on account of the imperfection of likeness, is said to be *to the image*. And because the perfect likeness of God cannot be except in identity of nature, the image of God is in his firstborn Son as the image of a king in a son naturally akin to him; but in man, as in an alien nature, as the image of a king on a silver coin; as is clear from Augustine in the book *On the Ten Strings*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0020","text":"[I q. 93 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32360)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0021","text":"To the third, it must be said that, since one is undivided being, it is called an undifferentiated form in the manner in which it is one. Now something is called one not only in number or species or genus, but also according to a certain analogy or proportion; and in this way there is unity or agreement of creature with God. But what he says, “of one thing to another thing to be made equal to it,” pertains to the notion of a perfect image.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0023","text":"[I q. 93 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32361)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0024","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the image of God is found in irrational creatures. For Dionysius says, in the book *On the Divine Names*: *effects have contingent images of their causes*. But God is the cause not only of rational creatures, but also of irrational ones. Therefore the image of God is found in irrational creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0025","text":"[I q. 93 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32362)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0026","text":"Further, the more expressive a likeness in something is, the more it approaches the character of an image. But Dionysius says, in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, that a solar ray has the greatest likeness to the divine goodness. Therefore it is to the image of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0027","text":"[I q. 93 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32363)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0028","text":"Further, the more perfect something is in goodness, the more like God it is. But the whole universe is more perfect in goodness than man, because, although individual things are good, nevertheless all things together are said to be very good, Gen. I. Therefore the whole universe is to the image of God, and not man alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0029","text":"[I q. 93 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32364)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0030","text":"Further, Boethius, in the book *On Consolation*, says of God, *bearing the world in his mind and forming it in a like image*. Therefore the whole world is to the image of God, and not the rational creature alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0031","text":"[I q. 93 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32365)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0032","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in VI *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, *this excels in man: that God made man in His own image, because He gave him an intellectual mind, by which he surpasses the beasts*. Therefore those things that do not have intellect are not to the image of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0033","text":"[I q. 93 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32366)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0034","text":"I answer that it must be said that not every likeness, even if it is expressed from another, suffices for the character of an image. For if the likeness is only according to genus, or according to some common accident, something will not on that account be said to be to the image of another. For one could not say that a worm arising from a man is an image of a man by reason of likeness in genus; nor again can it be said that, if something is made white in likeness to another, it is on that account to its image, because white is an accident common to many species. But for the character of an image it is required that there be a likeness according to species, as the image of a king is in his son; or at least according to some accident proper to the species, and especially according to figure, as the image of a man is said to be in bronze. Hence Hilary significantly says that an image is an undifferentiated species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0035","text":"Now it is manifest that likeness of species is considered according to the ultimate difference. But some things are likened to God, first indeed, and most commonly, insofar as they are; secondly, insofar as they live; thirdly, insofar as they are wise or understand. These, as Augustine says in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, *are so nearest to God in likeness that among creatures nothing is closer*. Thus, therefore, it is clear that intellectual creatures alone, properly speaking, are to the image of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0036","text":"[I q. 93 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32367)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0037","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that every imperfect thing is a certain participation in the perfect. And therefore even those things that fall short of the character of an image, insofar as they nevertheless have some likeness of God, participate in something of the character of an image. And therefore Dionysius says that caused things have images of their causes commensurate with them, that is, insofar as it befits them to have them, and not simply.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0038","text":"[I q. 93 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32368)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0039","text":"To the second it must be said that Dionysius likens the solar ray to the divine goodness with respect to causality, not according to the dignity of nature, which is required for the character of an image.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0040","text":"[I q. 93 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32369)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0041","text":"To the third it must be said that the universe is more perfect in goodness than an intellectual creature extensively and diffusively. But intensively and collectively, the likeness of divine perfection is found more in the intellectual creature, which is capable of the highest good. Or it must be said that a part is not divided against the whole, but against another part. Hence, when it is said that intellectual nature alone is to the image of God, it is not excluded that the universe, according to some part of itself, is to the image of God; but the other parts of the universe are excluded.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0042","text":"[I q. 93 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32370)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0043","text":"To the fourth it must be said that Boethius takes image according to the character of likeness by which the thing made imitates the form of the art which is in the mind of the artificer; and in this way every creature is an image of the exemplary reason which He has in the divine mind. But we are not now speaking of image in this way, but according as it is considered according to likeness in nature: namely, insofar as all things are likened to the first being, in that they are beings; and to the first life, in that they are living; and to the highest wisdom, in that they are intelligent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0044","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0045","text":"[I q. 93 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32371)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0046","text":"We proceed thus to the third article. It seems that an angel is not more to the image of God than man. For Augustine says, in the sermon *On the Image*, that God gave to no other creature than man that it should be to His image. Therefore it is not true that an angel is said to be more to the image of God than man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0047","text":"[I q. 93 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32372)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0048","text":"Further, according to Augustine, in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, *man is so to the image of God that, with no creature interposed, he is formed by God. And therefore nothing is more closely joined to Him*. But a creature is called an image of God insofar as it is joined to God. Therefore an angel is not more to the image of God than man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0049","text":"[I q. 93 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32373)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0050","text":"Moreover, a creature is said to be to the image of God insofar as it is of an intellectual nature. But an intellectual nature is not increased or diminished, for it is not in the genus of accident, since it is in the genus of substance. Therefore an angel is not more to the image of God than a human being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0051","text":"[I q. 93 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32374)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0052","text":"On the contrary, Gregory says in a certain homily that *an angel is called the seal of likeness, because in him the likeness of the divine image is shown forth more expressly*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0053","text":"[I q. 93 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32375)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0054","text":"I answer that we can speak of the image of God in two ways. In one way, with respect to that in which the character of image is first considered, namely, intellectual nature. And thus the image of God is more in angels than it is in human beings, because intellectual nature is more perfect in them, as is clear from what has been said above. In a second way, the image of God can be considered in a human being with respect to that in which it is considered secondarily, namely, insofar as a certain imitation of God is found in a human being: insofar, that is, as a human being is from a human being, as God is from God; and insofar as the human soul is wholly in its whole body, and again wholly in each part of it, just as God is related to the world. And according to these and similar points, the image of God is found more in a human being than in an angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0055","text":"But the character of the divine image in a human being is not considered per se in this respect, unless the first imitation is presupposed, which is according to intellectual nature; otherwise even brute animals would be to the image of God. And therefore, since with respect to intellectual nature an angel is more to the image of God than a human being, it must be granted without qualification that an angel is more to the image of God; whereas a human being is so in a qualified sense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0056","text":"[I q. 93 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32376)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0057","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that Augustine excludes from the image of God the other lower creatures lacking intellect, but not angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0058","text":"[I q. 93 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32377)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0059","text":"To the second, it must be said that, just as fire is said to be the most subtle of bodies according to its species, although one fire is more subtle than another, so it is said that nothing is more closely joined to God than the human mind, according to the genus of intellectual nature; because, as he himself had set forth above, *things that are wise are so near to him in likeness that among creatures nothing is closer*. Hence this does not exclude an angel's being more to the image of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0060","text":"[I q. 93 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32378)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0061","text":"To the third, it must be said that, when it is said that substance does not admit of more and less, this is not understood to mean that one species of substance is not more perfect than another, but that one and the same individual does not participate in its species at one time more and at another time less. Nor is the species of substance participated in according to more and less even by different individuals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0062","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0063","text":"[I q. 93 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32379)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0064","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that the image of God is not found in every human being. For the Apostle says, in 1 Corinthians 11, that *man is the image of God, but woman is the image of man*. Therefore, since woman is an individual of the human species, it does not belong to every individual to be an image of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0065","text":"[I q. 93 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32380)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0066","text":"Moreover, the Apostle says, Romans 8, that *those whom God foreknew to be made conformable to the image of his Son, these he predestined*. But not all human beings are predestined. Therefore not all human beings have conformity of image.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0067","text":"[I q. 93 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32381)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0068","text":"Moreover, likeness belongs to the character of image, as was said above. But through sin a human being becomes unlike God. Therefore he loses the image of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0069","text":"[I q. 93 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32382)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0070","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Psalm 38: *yet man passes by in an image*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0071","text":"[I q. 93 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32383)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0072","text":"I answer that, since a human being is said to be to the image of God according to intellectual nature, he is most of all to the image of God insofar as intellectual nature can most of all imitate God. Now intellectual nature imitates God most of all in this respect: that God understands and loves himself. Hence the image of God can be considered in a human being in three ways. In one way, insofar as a human being has a natural aptitude for understanding and loving God; and this aptitude consists in the very nature of the mind, which is common to all human beings. In another way, insofar as a human being actually or habitually knows and loves God, yet imperfectly; and this is the image through conformity of grace. In a third way, insofar as a human being actually knows and loves God perfectly; and thus the image is considered according to the likeness of glory.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0073","text":"Hence, on that passage of Psalm IV, *the light of thy countenance, O Lord, is signed upon us*, the Gloss distinguishes a threefold image, namely, that of creation, re-creation, and likeness. The first image, therefore, is found in all human beings; the second only in the just; but the third only in the blessed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0074","text":"[I q. 93 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32384)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0075","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that in both man and woman the image of God is found as regards that in which the notion of image principally consists, namely, as regards intellectual nature. Hence, in Gen. I, when it had said, *in the image of God he created him*, namely, the human being, it added, *male and female he created them*, and it said “them” in the plural, as Augustine says, lest it be understood that both sexes had been joined in one individual. But in a certain secondary respect the image of God is found in man in a way in which it is not found in woman, for man is the principle and end of woman, just as God is the principle and end of every creature. Hence, when the Apostle had said that *man is the image and glory of God, but woman is the glory of man*, he showed why he had said this, adding: *for man is not from woman, but woman from man; and man was not created for woman, but woman for man*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0076","text":"[I q. 93 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32385)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0077","text":"To the second and third, it must be said that those arguments proceed concerning the image which is according to conformity of grace and glory.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0078","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0079","text":"[I q. 93 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32386)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0080","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth. It seems that in the human being there is not an image of God with respect to the Trinity of divine persons. For Augustine says, in the book *On Faith to Peter*: *the divinity of the Holy Trinity and the image according to which the human being was made are essentially one*. And Hilary, in *On the Trinity* V, says that *the human being is made according to the common image of the Trinity*. Therefore, the image of God is in the human being with respect to the essence, and not with respect to the Trinity of persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0081","text":"[I q. 93 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32387)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0082","text":"Moreover, in the book *On Ecclesiastical Dogmas* it is said that the image of God is considered in the human being according to eternity. Damascene also says that *for the human being to be according to the image of God signifies being intellectual, free in choice, and self-governing*. Gregory of Nyssa likewise says that, when Scripture said that the human being was made in the image of God, *it is equivalent to saying that human nature was made a participant in every good; for divinity is the fullness of goodness*. But all these things pertain not to the distinction of persons, but rather to the unity of essence. Therefore, the image of God is in the human being not according to the Trinity of persons, but according to the unity of essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0083","text":"[I q. 93 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32388)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0084","text":"Moreover, an image leads to knowledge of that of which it is an image. Therefore, if the image of God is in the human being according to the Trinity of persons, since the human being can know himself through natural reason, it would follow that through natural knowledge a human being could know the Trinity of divine persons. This is false, as was shown above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0085","text":"[I q. 93 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32389)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0086","text":"Moreover, the name of image does not belong to each of the three persons, but to the Son alone; for Augustine says, in *On the Trinity* VI, that *the Son alone is the image of the Father*. Therefore, if the image of God were considered in the human being according to person, the image in the human being would not be of the whole Trinity, but of the Son alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0087","text":"[I q. 93 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32390)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0088","text":"But on the contrary, Hilary, in *On the Trinity* IV, shows from the fact that the human being is said to have been made in the image of God the plurality of divine persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0089","text":"[I q. 93 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32391)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0090","text":"I answer that it must be said that, as was held above, the distinction of the divine persons is only according to origin, or rather according to relations of origin. But the mode of origin is not the same in all things; rather, the mode of origin of each thing is according to the suitability of its nature: for animate things are produced in one way, inanimate things in another; animals in one way, and plants in another. Hence it is clear that the distinction of the divine persons is according to what befits the divine nature. Therefore, being in the image of God according to imitation of the divine nature does not exclude being in the image of God according to representation of the three persons; rather, one follows from the other. Thus, therefore, it must be said that in the human being there is an image of God both with respect to the divine nature and with respect to the Trinity of persons, for even in God himself one nature exists in three persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0091","text":"[I q. 93 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32392)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0092","text":"And through this the reply to the first two is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0093","text":"[I q. 93 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32393)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0094","text":"To the third, it must be said that that argument would proceed if the image of God in the human being perfectly represented God. But, as Augustine says in *On the Trinity* XV, there is a very great difference between this Trinity which is in us and the divine Trinity. And therefore, as he himself says in the same place, *we see the Trinity which is in us rather than believe it, whereas we believe that God is Trinity rather than see it*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0095","text":"[I q. 93 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32394)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0096","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that some have said that in the human being there is only the image of the Son. But Augustine refutes this in *On the Trinity* XII. First, because, since the Son is like the Father according to equality of essence, it is necessary, if the human being is made according to the likeness of the Son, that he be made according to the likeness of the Father. Second, because, if the human being had been made only in the image of the Son, the Father would not say, *Let us make the human being in our image and likeness*, but “in yours.” Therefore, when it is said, “In the image of God he made him,” it is not to be understood that the Father made the human being only in the image of the Son, who is God, as some have expounded it; but it is to be understood that God the Trinity made the human being in his image, that is, in the image of the whole Trinity. But when it is said that God made the human being in his image, this can be understood in two ways.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0097","text":"In one way, because this preposition *ad* designates the terminus of the making, so that the sense is: let us make man such that there may be in him an image. In another way, this preposition *ad* can designate the exemplar cause; as when it is said, this book was made after that one. And thus the image of God is the divine essence itself, which is improperly called an image, insofar as image is taken for exemplar. Or, according to what some say, the divine essence is called an image because, according to it, one person imitates another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0098","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0099","text":"[I q. 93 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32395)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0100","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth. It seems that the image of God is not in man only according to the mind. For the Apostle says, in I Corinthians XI, that man is the image of God. But man is not the mind alone. Therefore, the image of God is not considered only according to the mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0101","text":"[I q. 93 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32396)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0102","text":"Moreover, Gen. I says, *God created man in His own image; in the image of God He created him; male and female He created them*. But the distinction of male and female is according to the body. Therefore, the image of God in man is also considered according to the body, and not according to the mind alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0103","text":"[I q. 93 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32397)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0104","text":"Moreover, an image seems to be considered chiefly according to figure. But figure pertains to the body. Therefore, the image of God in man is considered also according to the body, and not according to the mind alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0105","text":"[I q. 93 a. 6 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32398)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0106","text":"Moreover, according to Augustine, in XII *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, a threefold vision is found in us: namely, corporeal, spiritual or imaginative, and intellectual. Therefore, if according to intellectual vision, which pertains to the mind, there is some Trinity in us, according to which we are in the image of God, then by equal reason there is also in the other visions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0107","text":"[I q. 93 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32399)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0108","text":"But on the contrary, the Apostle says, to the Ephesians IV, *be renewed in the spirit of your mind, and put on the new man*, from which it is understood that our renewal, which takes place according to the putting on of the new man, pertains to the mind. But in Colossians III he says, *putting on the new man, who is renewed unto the knowledge of God, according to the image of Him who created him*, where he attributes to the image of God the renewal which is according to the putting on of the new man. Therefore, to be in the image of God pertains only to the mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0109","text":"[I q. 93 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32400)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0110","text":"I answer that, since in all creatures there is some likeness of God, in the rational creature alone is the likeness of God found by way of image, as stated above; whereas in other creatures it is found by way of trace. Now that in which the rational creature surpasses other creatures is intellect or mind. Hence it follows that not even in the rational creature itself is the image of God found except according to the mind. But in its other parts, if the rational creature has any, a likeness by way of trace is found; just as in the other things to which it is assimilated according to such parts. The reason for this can be clearly known if one considers the manner in which a trace represents, and the manner in which an image represents. For an image represents according to likeness of species, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0111","text":"But a trace represents in the manner of an effect which so represents its cause that it nevertheless does not attain to likeness of species; for the marks left by the movement of animals are called traces; and likewise ashes are called a trace of fire, and the desolation of a land, a trace of a hostile army. Therefore, such a difference can be considered between rational creatures and other creatures, both with respect to the fact that in creatures a likeness of the divine nature is represented, and with respect to the fact that in them a likeness of the uncreated Trinity is represented. For with respect to the likeness of the divine nature, rational creatures seem in some manner to attain to a representation of species, insofar as they imitate God not only in being and living, but also in understanding, as stated above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0112","text":"But other creatures do not understand; rather, if their arrangement is considered, some trace of the producing intellect appears in them. Similarly, since the uncreated Trinity is distinguished according to the procession of the Word from the speaker, and of Love from both, as has been treated above; in the rational creature, in which a procession of the word is found according to intellect, and a procession of love according to will, an image of the uncreated Trinity can be said to exist through a certain representation of species. But in other creatures there is found no principle of word, and word, and love; rather, there appears in them some trace that these are found in the producing cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0113","text":"For the very fact that a creature has a modified and finite substance shows that it is from some principle; while its species shows forth the word of the maker, just as the form of a house shows forth the conception of the craftsman; and its order shows forth the love of the producer, by which the effect is ordered to the good, just as the use of a building shows forth the will of the craftsman. Thus, therefore, in man the likeness of God is found by way of image according to the mind; but according to his other parts, by way of trace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0114","text":"[I q. 93 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32401)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0115","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that man is called the image of God, not because he himself is essentially an image, but because the image of God is impressed upon him according to the mind; just as a denarius is called the image of Caesar, insofar as it bears Caesar's image. Hence it is not necessary that the image of God be understood according to every part of man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0116","text":"[I q. 93 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32402)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0117","text":"To the second, it must be said that, as Augustine says in XII *On the Trinity*, some have posited the image of the Trinity in man not according to one individual, but according to several, saying that *the man signifies the person of the Father; the woman, indeed, the person of the Son, because she proceeded from the man in such a way as to be born from him; and thus they say that the third person, as it were the Holy Spirit, is the woman, who proceeded from the man in such a way that she herself was not son or daughter*. This seems absurd at first sight. First, indeed, because it would follow that the Holy Spirit is the principle of the Son, just as the woman is the principle of offspring born from the man. Second, because one man would be in the image of only one person. Third, because according to this, Scripture should not have made mention of the image of God in man unless offspring had already been produced.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0118","text":"And therefore it must be said that Scripture, after saying, He created him in the image of God, added, male and female He created them, not in order that the image of God be considered according to the distinctions of the sexes, but because the image of God is common to both sexes, since it is according to the mind, in which there is no distinction of sexes. Hence the Apostle, in Colossians III, after saying, according to the image of Him who created him, adds, where there is neither male nor female.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0119","text":"[I q. 93 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32403)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0120","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although the image of God in man is not understood according to bodily figure, nevertheless *the body of man, because alone among the bodies of terrestrial animals it is not cast down prone toward the belly, but is such as to be more suited for contemplating heaven, can rightly be seen, in this respect, to have been made more after the image and likeness of God than the other bodies of animals*; as Augustine says in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*. Yet this is not to be understood as though the image of God were in the human body, but because the very figure of the human body represents the image of God in the soul, by way of trace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0121","text":"[I q. 93 a. 6 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32404)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0122","text":"To the fourth it must be said that both in corporeal vision and in imaginative vision a certain Trinity is found, as Augustine says in the book *On the Trinity*. For in corporeal vision there is, first, the species of the external body; second, the vision itself, which comes about through the impression upon the visual power of a certain likeness of the aforesaid species; third, there is the intention of the will, applying the visual power to seeing and holding it fast upon the thing seen. Likewise, also in imaginative vision there is found, first, the species preserved in memory; second, imaginative vision itself, which results from the fact that the gaze of the soul—that is, the imaginative power itself—is informed according to the aforesaid species; third, the intention of the will is found joining the two. But each Trinity falls short of the character of the divine image.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0123","text":"For the species itself of the external body is outside the nature of the soul; whereas the species which is in memory, although it is not outside the soul, is nevertheless adventitious to the soul; and thus in both cases the representation of the connaturality and coeternity of the divine persons is lacking. Corporeal vision, moreover, does not proceed only from the species of the external body, but together with this from the sense of the one seeing; and similarly imaginative vision proceeds not only from the species preserved in memory, but also from the imaginative power; and thus by this the procession of the Son from the Father alone is not suitably represented. But the intention of the will, which joins the two aforesaid things, does not proceed from them, neither in corporeal nor in spiritual vision; whence the procession of the Holy Spirit from the Father and the Son is not suitably represented.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0124","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0125","text":"[I q. 93 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32405)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0126","text":"We proceed thus to the seventh. It seems that the image of God is not found in the soul according to acts. For Augustine says, in XI *On the City of God*, that man was made to the image of God, insofar as *we are, and know that we are, and love this being and knowing*. But being does not signify an act. Therefore the image of God is not considered in the soul according to acts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0127","text":"[I q. 93 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32406)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0128","text":"Further, Augustine, in IX *On the Trinity*, assigns the image of God in the soul according to these three: mind, knowledge, and love. But mind does not signify an act, but rather a power, or even the essence of the intellectual soul. Therefore the image of God is not considered according to acts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0129","text":"[I q. 93 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32407)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0130","text":"Further, Augustine, in X *On the Trinity*, assigns the image of the Trinity in the soul according to memory, understanding, and will. But these three are the natural powers of the soul, as the Master says in distinction I of book III of the *Sentences*. Therefore the image is considered according to powers, and not according to acts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0131","text":"[I q. 93 a. 7 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32408)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0132","text":"Further, the image of the Trinity always remains in the soul. But an act does not always remain. Therefore the image of God is not considered in the soul according to acts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0133","text":"[I q. 93 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32409)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0134","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine, in XI *On the Trinity*, assigns a Trinity in the lower parts of the soul according to actual sensible and imaginative vision. Therefore the Trinity which is in the mind, according to which man is to the image of God, must likewise be considered according to actual vision.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0135","text":"[I q. 93 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32410)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0136","text":"I answer that it must be said that, as was said above, a certain representation of a species belongs to the character of an image. Therefore, if the image of the divine Trinity is to be understood in the soul, it must be considered principally according to that which comes closest, insofar as possible, to representing the species of the divine persons. Now the divine persons are distinguished according to the procession of the Word from the one speaking, and of the love connecting both. But in our soul a word cannot exist without actual thought, as Augustine says in XIV *On the Trinity*. And therefore the image of the Trinity is considered first and principally in the mind according to acts: namely, insofar as, from the knowledge which we have, by inwardly thinking we form a word, and from this break forth into love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0137","text":"But because the principles of acts are habits and powers, and because everything exists virtually in its principle, secondarily, and as though consequently, the image of the Trinity can be considered in the soul according to powers, and especially according to habits, insofar as acts exist in them virtually.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0138","text":"[I q. 93 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32411)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0139","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that our being to the image of God belongs to that which is proper to us above the other animals; and this being belongs to us insofar as we have mind. And therefore this Trinity is the same as that which Augustine sets forth in IX *On the Trinity*, which consists in mind, knowledge, and love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0140","text":"[I q. 93 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32412)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0141","text":"To the second it must be said that Augustine first discovered this Trinity in the mind. But because the mind, although it knows itself wholly in a certain way, is also in a certain way ignorant of itself, namely insofar as it is distinct from other things—and thus also seeks itself, as Augustine subsequently proves in X *On the Trinity*—therefore, as though knowledge did not wholly equal the mind, he takes in the soul three things proper to the mind, namely memory, understanding, and will, which no one is ignorant that he has; and in these three he assigns the image of the Trinity rather, as though the first assignment were in a certain way deficient.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0142","text":"[I q. 93 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32413)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0143","text":"To the third it must be said that, as Augustine proves in XIV *On the Trinity*, we are said to understand and to will or love certain things both when we think of them and when we do not think of them. But when they are without thought, they pertain to memory alone, which, according to him, is nothing other than the habitual retention of knowledge and love. But because, as he says, *a word cannot be there without thought (for we think everything that we say, even by that inner word which belongs to the language of no nation), this image is rather recognized in those three, namely memory, understanding, and will. But I now call understanding that by which we understand while thinking; and that will, or love or affection, which joins this offspring and its parent*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0144","text":"From this it is clear that he places the image of the divine Trinity rather in actual understanding and will than according to their being in the habitual retention of memory, although even in this respect there is in some way an image of the Trinity in the soul, as is said in the same place. And thus it is clear that memory, understanding, and will are not three powers, as is said in the *Sentences*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0145","text":"[I q. 93 a. 7 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32414)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0146","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that someone could answer through what Augustine says in *On the Trinity*, XIV: that *the mind always remembers itself, always understands and loves itself*. Some understand this as though actual understanding and love of itself are present to the soul. But he excludes this interpretation by adding that it *does not always think of itself as distinct from those things that are not what it itself is*. Thus it is clear that the soul always understands and loves itself, not actually, but habitually. Although it can also be said that, by perceiving its own act, it understands itself whenever it understands something. But because it is not always actually understanding, as is evident in one asleep, it must therefore be said that acts, even if they do not always remain in themselves, nevertheless always remain in their principles, namely, powers and habits.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0147","text":"Hence Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, XIV: *if the rational soul was made according to the image of God in that it can use reason and intellect to understand and behold God, then from the beginning at which it began to exist, the image of God was in it*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0148","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0149","text":"[I q. 93 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32415)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0150","text":"The eighth is approached thus. It seems that the image of the divine Trinity is in the soul not only by comparison with the object which is God. For the image of the divine Trinity is found in the soul, as has been said, insofar as a word in us proceeds from one speaking, and love from both. But this is found in us with respect to any object whatsoever. Therefore, with respect to any object whatsoever, the image of the divine Trinity is found in our mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0151","text":"[I q. 93 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32416)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0152","text":"Further, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, XII, that *when we seek the Trinity in the soul, we seek it in the whole soul, not separating rational action in temporal things from the contemplation of eternal things*. Therefore, even in respect of temporal objects, the image of the Trinity is found in the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0153","text":"[I q. 93 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32417)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0154","text":"Further, that we understand and love God belongs to us according to the gift of grace. Therefore, if the image of the Trinity in the soul is considered according to the memory, understanding, and will or love of God, the image of God will not be in man according to nature, but according to grace. And thus it will not be common to all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0155","text":"[I q. 93 a. 8 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32418)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0156","text":"Further, the saints who are in the homeland are most conformed to the image of God according to the vision of glory; hence it is said in II Corinthians III: *we are transformed into the same image, from glory to glory*. But according to the vision of glory temporal things are known. Therefore, even by comparison with temporal things, the image of God is considered in us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0157","text":"[I q. 93 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32419)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0158","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, XIV, that *the mind is not the image of God because it remembers itself, and understands and loves itself, but because it can also remember, understand, and love God, by whom it was made*. Much less, therefore, is the image of God considered in the mind with respect to other objects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0158"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0159","text":"[I q. 93 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32420)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0159"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0160","text":"I answer that it must be said that, as was stated above, an image implies a likeness in some way attaining to the representation of a species. Hence the image of the divine Trinity must be considered in the soul according to something that represents the divine persons by a representation of species, insofar as this is possible for a creature. But the divine persons are distinguished, as has been said, according to the procession of the Word from the speaker, and of love from both. Now the Word of God is born from God according to His knowledge of Himself, and love proceeds from God insofar as He loves Himself. But it is manifest that diversity of objects diversifies the species of word and love; for the word conceived in the heart of man concerning a stone and that concerning a horse are not the same in species, nor is the love the same in species. Therefore, the divine image is considered in man according to the word conceived from the knowledge of God, and the love derived therefrom.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0160"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0161","text":"And thus the image of God is considered in the soul insofar as it is borne, or is by nature apt to be borne, toward God. Now the mind is borne toward something in two ways: in one way, directly and immediately; in another way, indirectly and mediately, as when someone, seeing the image of a man in a mirror, is said to be borne toward the man himself. And therefore Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, XIV, that *the mind remembers itself, understands itself, and loves itself; if we discern this, we discern a Trinity—not yet indeed God, but already the image of God*. But this is so, not because the mind is borne toward itself absolutely, but insofar as through this it can further be borne toward God, as is clear from the authority cited above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0161"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0162","text":"[I q. 93 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32421)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0162"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0163","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that for the notion of an image, one must consider not only that something proceeds from something, but also what proceeds from what: namely, that the Word of God proceeds from knowledge of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0163"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0164","text":"[I q. 93 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32422)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0164"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0165","text":"To the second, it must be said that a certain Trinity is indeed found in the whole soul, yet not such that, apart from action concerning temporal things and contemplation of eternal things, *some third thing is sought by which the Trinity is completed*, as is added in the same place. But in that part of reason which is derived from the part concerned with temporal things, although *a Trinity can be found, nevertheless the image of God cannot be found*, as is said later, because knowledge of temporal things of this kind is adventitious to the soul. Moreover, the habits themselves by which temporal things are known are not always present; rather, at times they are present actually, and at times only according to memory, even after they begin to be present. This is clear in the case of faith, which comes to us temporally in the present life; but in the state of future blessedness there will no longer be faith, but memory of faith.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0165"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0166","text":"[I q. 93 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32423)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0166"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0167","text":"To the third, it must be said that meritorious knowledge and love of God are only through grace. Yet there is some natural knowledge and love of God, as has been treated above. And this itself is also natural: that the mind can use reason to understand God, according to which we have always said that the image of God remains in the mind—whether this image of God be so worn away, as if overshadowed, that it is almost nonexistent, as in those who do not have the use of reason; or whether it be obscure and deformed, as in sinners; or whether it be clear and beautiful, as in the just, as Augustine says in *On the Trinity*, XIV.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0167"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0168","text":"[I q. 93 a. 8 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32424)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0168"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0169","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that according to the vision of glory, temporal things will be seen in God himself; and therefore the vision of temporal things of this kind will pertain to the image of God. And this is what Augustine says, in Book XIV *On the Trinity*, that “in that nature to which the mind will happily adhere, it will see as immutable everything that it sees.” For in the uncreated Word itself there are the intelligible grounds of all creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0169"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0170","text":"## Article 9","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0170"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0171","text":"[I q. 93 a. 9 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32425)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0171"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0172","text":"The ninth point is approached as follows. It seems that likeness is not suitably distinguished from image. For a genus is not suitably distinguished from a species. But likeness is related to image as genus to species, because “where there is an image, there is at once a likeness, but not conversely,” as is said in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*. Therefore likeness is unsuitably distinguished from image.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0172"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0173","text":"[I q. 93 a. 9 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32426)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0173"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0174","text":"Further, the notion of image is considered not only according to the representation of the divine persons, but also according to the representation of the divine essence, to which representation immortality and indivisibility belong. Therefore it is not suitably said that “likeness is in the essence, because it is immortal and indivisible; but image is in the other things.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0174"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0175","text":"[I q. 93 a. 9 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32427)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0175"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0176","text":"Further, the image of God in man is threefold, namely, that of nature, grace, and glory, as was held above. But innocence and justice pertain to grace. Therefore it is unsuitably said that “image is taken according to memory, understanding, and will; but likeness according to innocence and justice.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0176"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0177","text":"[I q. 93 a. 9 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32428)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0177"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0178","text":"Further, knowledge of truth pertains to the understanding, whereas love of virtue pertains to the will, which are two parts of the image. Therefore it is not suitably said that “image is in the knowledge of truth, likeness in the love of virtue.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0178"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0179","text":"[I q. 93 a. 9 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32429)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0179"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0180","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, “there are those who not without reason understand that two things are said: ‘in the image’ and ‘in the likeness’; for, if it were one thing, one name could have sufficed.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0180"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0181","text":"[I q. 93 a. 9 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32430)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0181"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0182","text":"I answer that likeness is a certain unity; for unity in quality causes likeness, as is said in *Metaphysics* V. But unity, since it is among the transcendentals, is both common to all things and can be applied to individual things, just as goodness and truth can. Hence, just as goodness can be related to some particular thing both as preceding it and as following it, insofar as it denotes some perfection of that thing, so too is it with the relation of likeness to image. For goodness precedes man, according as man is a certain particular good; and again goodness follows man, insofar as we say that a certain man is especially good on account of the perfection of virtue.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0182"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0183","text":"And likewise likeness is considered as preceding image, insofar as it is more common than image, as was said above; it is also considered as following image, insofar as it signifies a certain perfection of the image. For we say that the image of someone is like or unlike that of which it is the image, insofar as it represents it perfectly or imperfectly. Thus, then, likeness can be distinguished from image in two ways. In one way, insofar as it precedes it and exists in more things. And thus likeness is considered according to those things which are more common than the properties of the intellectual nature, according to which image is properly considered. And according to this it is said in the book *Eighty-Three Questions* that the spirit, that is, the mind, was made in the image of God, as no one doubts; but some wish the other things of man—namely, those that pertain to the lower parts of the soul, or even to the body itself—to have been made in the likeness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0183"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0184","text":"According to this, it is also said in the book *On the Quantity of the Soul* that the likeness of God is considered in the soul insofar as it is incorruptible; for corruptible and incorruptible are differences of being in common. In another way, likeness can be considered insofar as it signifies the expression and perfection of the image. And according to this Damascene says that “that which is according to the image signifies the intellectual and free in choice, having power of itself; but that which is according to the likeness signifies a likeness in virtue, insofar as it can be present in man.” And to the same point belongs the statement that likeness pertains to the love of virtue; for there is no virtue without love of virtue.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0184"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0185","text":"[I q. 93 a. 9 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32431)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0185"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0186","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that likeness is not distinguished from image according to the common notion of likeness (for in this way it is included in the notion of image itself), but insofar as some likeness falls short of the notion of image, or is even perfective of image.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0186"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0187","text":"[I q. 93 a. 9 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32432)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0187"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0188","text":"To the second, it must be said that the essence of the soul pertains to image insofar as it represents the divine essence according to those things that are proper to the intellectual nature, but not according to conditions consequent upon being in common, such as being simple and indissoluble.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0188"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0189","text":"[I q. 93 a. 9 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32433)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0189"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0190","text":"To the third, it must be said that certain virtues are also naturally present in the soul, at least according to certain seeds of them, and according to these a natural likeness could be considered. Although it is not unfitting that what is called image according to one assignment be called likeness according to another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0190"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0191","text":"[I q. 93 a. 9 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32434)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0191"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0192","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that love of the word, which is loved knowledge, pertains to the notion of image; but love of virtue pertains to likeness, as does virtue itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_85:P0192"]}],"source_digest":"2fdb01e8cd02ca854fe0408418fb264931b76db5ec8e04734bcd6224e16472b6","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 93"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 93","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86","title":"First Part · Question 94","order":95,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_86","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 94 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32435)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de statu vel conditione primi hominis. Et primo, quantum ad animam; secundo, quantum ad corpus. Circa primum consideranda sunt duo, primo, de conditione hominis quantum ad intellectum; secundo, de conditione hominis quantum ad voluntatem. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum primus homo viderit Deum per essentiam. Secundo, utrum videre potuerit substantias separatas, idest Angelos. Tertio, utrum habuerit omnium scientiam. Quarto, utrum potuerit errare vel decipi.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 94 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32436)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod primus homo per essentiam Deum viderit. Beatitudo enim hominis in visione divinae essentiae consistit. Sed primus homo, in Paradiso conversans, beatam et omnium divitem habuit vitam, ut Damascenus dicit in II libro. Et Augustinus dicit, in XIV de Civ. Dei, *si homines habebant affectus suos quales nunc habemus, quomodo erant beati in illo inenarrabilis beatitudinis loco, idest Paradiso?* Ergo primus homo in Paradiso vidit Deum per essentiam.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32437)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, XIV de Civ. Dei, quod *primo homini non aberat quidquam quod bona voluntas adipisceretur*. Sed nihil melius bona voluntas adipisci potest quam divinae essentiae visionem. Ergo homo per essentiam Deum videbat.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32438)\n\nPraeterea, visio Dei per essentiam est qua videtur Deus sine medio et sine aenigmate. Sed homo in statu innocentiae vidit Deum sine medio; ut Magister dicit in I distinctione IV libri Sent. Vidit etiam sine aenigmate, quia aenigma obscuritatem importat, ut Augustinus dicit, XV de Trin.; obscuritas autem introducta est per peccatum. Ergo homo in primo statu vidit Deum per essentiam.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32439)\n\nSed contra est quod apostolus dicit, I ad Cor. XV, quod *non prius quod spirituale est, sed quod animale*. Sed maxime spirituale est videre Deum per essentiam. Ergo primus homo, in primo statu animalis vitae, Deum per essentiam non vidit.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32440)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod primus homo Deum per essentiam non vidit, secundum communem statum illius vitae; nisi forte dicatur quod viderit eum in raptu, quando Deus immisit soporem in Adam, ut dicitur Gen. II. Et huius ratio est quia, cum divina essentia sit ipsa beatitudo, hoc modo se habet intellectus videntis divinam essentiam ad Deum, sicut se habet quilibet homo ad beatitudinem. Manifestum est autem quod nullus homo potest per voluntatem a beatitudine averti, naturaliter enim, et ex necessitate, homo vult beatitudinem, et fugit miseriam. Unde nullus videns Deum per essentiam, potest voluntate averti a Deo, quod est peccare. Et propter hoc, omnes videntes Deum per essentiam, sic in amore Dei stabiliuntur, quod in aeternum peccare non possunt. Cum ergo Adam peccaverit, manifestum est quod Deum per essentiam non videbat. Cognoscebat tamen Deum quadam altiori cognitione quam nos cognoscamus, et sic quodammodo eius cognitio media erat inter cognitionem praesentis status, et cognitionem patriae, qua Deus per essentiam videtur. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod visio Dei per essentiam dividitur contra visionem Dei per creaturam. Quanto autem aliqua creatura est altior et Deo similior, tanto per eam Deus clarius videtur, sicut homo perfectius videtur per speculum in quo expressius imago eius resultat. Et sic patet quod multo eminentius videtur Deus per intelligibiles effectus, quam per sensibiles et corporeos. A consideratione autem plena et lucida intelligibilium effectuum impeditur homo in statu praesenti, per hoc quod distrahitur a sensibilibus, et circa ea occupatur. Sed, sicut dicitur Eccle. VII, Deus fecit hominem rectum. Haec autem fuit rectitudo hominis divinitus instituti, ut inferiora superioribus subderentur, et superiora ab inferioribus non impedirentur. Unde homo primus non impediebatur per res exteriores a clara et firma contemplatione intelligibilium effectuum, quos ex irradiatione primae veritatis percipiebat, sive naturali cognitione sive gratuita. Unde dicit Augustinus, in XI super Gen. ad Litt., quod *fortassis Deus primis hominibus antea loquebatur, sicut cum Angelis loquitur, ipsa incommutabili veritate illustrans mentes eorum; etsi non tanta participatione divinae essentiae, quantam capiunt Angeli*. Sic igitur per huiusmodi intelligibiles effectus Dei, Deum clarius cognoscebat quam modo cognoscamus.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32441)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod homo in Paradiso beatus fuit, non illa perfecta beatitudine in quam transferendus erat, quae in divinae essentiae visione consistit, habebat tamen beatam vitam secundum quendam modum, ut Augustinus dicit XI super Gen. ad Litt., inquantum habebat integritatem et perfectionem quandam naturalem.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32442)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod bona voluntas est ordinata voluntas. Non autem fuisset primi hominis ordinata voluntas, si in statu meriti habere voluisset quod ei promittebatur pro praemio.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32443)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod duplex est medium. Quoddam, in quo simul videtur quod per medium videri dicitur; sicut cum homo videtur per speculum, et simul videtur cum ipso speculo. Aliud medium est, per cuius notitiam in aliquid ignotum devenimus; sicut est medium demonstrationis. Et sine tali medio Deus videbatur, non tamen sine primo medio. Non enim oportebat primum hominem pervenire in Dei cognitionem per demonstrationem sumptam ab aliquo effectu, sicut nobis est necessarium; sed simul in effectibus, praecipue intelligibilibus, suo modo Deum cognoscebat. Similiter etiam est considerandum quod obscuritas quae importatur in nomine aenigmatis, dupliciter potest accipi. Uno modo, secundum quod quaelibet creatura est quoddam obscurum, si comparetur ad immensitatem divinae claritatis, et sic Adam videbat Deum in aenigmate, quia videbat Deum per effectum creatum. Alio modo potest accipi obscuritas quae consecuta est ex peccato, prout scilicet impeditur homo a consideratione intelligibilium per sensibilium occupationem, et secundum hoc, non vidit Deum in aenigmate.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 94 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32444)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Adam in statu innocentiae Angelos per essentiam viderit. Dicit enim Gregorius, in IV Dialog., *in Paradiso quippe assueverat homo verbis Dei perfrui, beatorum Angelorum spiritibus cordis munditia et celsitudine visionis interesse*.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32445)\n\nPraeterea, anima in statu praesenti impeditur a cognitione substantiarum separatarum, ex hoc quod est unita corpori corruptibili, quod aggravat animam, ut dicitur Sap. IX. Unde et anima separata substantias separatas videre potest, ut supra dictum est. Sed anima primi hominis non aggravabatur a corpore, cum non esset corruptibile. Ergo poterat videre substantias separatas.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32446)\n\nPraeterea, una substantia separata cognoscit aliam cognoscendo seipsam, ut dicitur in libro de causis. Sed anima primi hominis cognoscebat seipsam. Ergo cognoscebat substantias separatas.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32447)\n\nSed contra, anima Adae fuit eiusdem naturae cum animabus nostris. Sed animae nostrae non possunt nunc intelligere substantias separatas. Ergo nec anima primi hominis potuit.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32448)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod status animae hominis distingui potest dupliciter. Uno modo, secundum diversum modum naturalis esse, et hoc modo distinguitur status animae separatae, a statu animae coniunctae corpori. Alio modo distinguitur status animae secundum integritatem et corruptionem, servato eodem modo essendi secundum naturam, et sic status innocentiae distinguitur a statu hominis post peccatum. Anima enim hominis in statu innocentiae erat corpori perficiendo et gubernando accommodata, sicut et nunc, unde dicitur primus homo factus fuisse in animam viventem, idest corpori vitam dantem, scilicet animalem. Sed huius vitae integritatem habebat, inquantum corpus erat totaliter animae subditum, in nullo ipsam impediens, ut supra dictum est. Manifestum est autem ex praemissis quod ex hoc quod anima est accommodata ad corporis gubernationem et perfectionem secundum animalem vitam, competit animae nostrae talis modus intelligendi, qui est per conversionem ad phantasmata. Unde et hic modus intelligendi etiam animae primi hominis competebat. Secundum autem hunc modum intelligendi, motus quidam invenitur in anima, ut Dionysius dicit IV cap. de Div. Nom., secundum tres gradus. Quorum primus est, secundum quod a rebus exterioribus congregatur anima ad seipsam; secundus autem est, prout anima ascendit ad hoc quod uniatur virtutibus superioribus unitis, scilicet Angelis; tertius autem gradus est, secundum quod ulterius manuducitur ad bonum quod est supra omnia, scilicet Deum. Secundum igitur primum processum animae, qui est a rebus exterioribus ad seipsam, perficitur animae cognitio. Quia scilicet intellectualis operatio animae naturalem ordinem habet ad ea quae sunt extra, ut supra dictum est, et ita per eorum cognitionem perfecte cognosci potest nostra intellectualis operatio, sicut actus per obiectum. Et per ipsam intellectualem operationem perfecte potest cognosci humanus intellectus, sicut potentia per proprium actum. Sed in secundo processu non invenitur perfecta cognitio. Quia, cum Angelus non intelligat per conversionem ad phantasmata, sed longe eminentiori modo, ut supra dictum est; praedictus modus cognoscendi, quo anima cognoscit seipsam, non sufficienter ducit in Angeli cognitionem. Multo autem minus tertius processus ad perfectam notitiam terminatur, quia etiam ipsi Angeli, per hoc quod cognoscunt seipsos, non possunt pertingere ad cognitionem divinae substantiae propter eius excessum. Sic igitur anima primi hominis non poterat videre Angelos per essentiam. Sed tamen excellentiorem modum cognitionis habebat de eis, quam nos habeamus, quia eius cognitio erat magis certa et fixa circa interiora intelligibilia, quam cognitio nostra. Et propter tantam eminentiam dicit Gregorius quod intererat Angelorum spiritibus.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32449)\n\nUnde patet solutio ad primum.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32450)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod hoc quod anima primi hominis deficiebat ab intellectu substantiarum separatarum, non erat ex aggravatione corporis; sed ex hoc quod obiectum ei connaturale erat deficiens ab excellentia substantiarum separatarum. Nos autem deficimus propter utrumque.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32451)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod anima primi hominis non poterat per cognitionem sui ipsius pertingere ad cognoscendas substantias separatas, ut supra dictum est, quia etiam unaquaeque substantia separata cognoscit aliam per modum sui ipsius.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 94 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32452)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod primus homo non habuerit scientiam omnium. Aut enim habuit talem scientiam per species acquisitas, aut per species connaturales, aut per species infusas. Non autem per species acquisitas, huiusmodi enim cognitio ab experientia causatur, ut dicitur in I Metaphys.; ipse autem non tunc fuerat omnia expertus. Similiter etiam nec per species connaturales, quia erat eiusdem naturae nobiscum; anima autem nostra est sicut tabula in qua nihil est scriptum, ut dicitur in III de anima. Si autem per species infusas, ergo scientia eius quam habebat de rebus, non erat eiusdem rationis cum scientia nostra, quam a rebus acquirimus.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32453)\n\nPraeterea, in omnibus individuis eiusdem speciei est idem modus consequendi perfectionem. Sed alii homines non statim in sui principio habent omnium scientiam, sed eam per temporis successionem acquirunt secundum suum modum. Ergo nec Adam, statim formatus, habuit omnium scientiam.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32454)\n\nPraeterea, status praesentis vitae homini conceditur ut in eo proficiat anima et quantum ad cognitionem, et quantum ad meritum; propter hoc enim anima corpori videtur esse unita. Sed homo in statu illo profecisset quantum ad meritum. Ergo etiam profecisset quantum ad cognitionem rerum. Non ergo habuit omnium rerum scientiam.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32455)\n\nSed contra est quod ipse imposuit nomina animalibus, ut dicitur Gen. II. Nomina autem debent naturis rerum congruere. Ergo Adam scivit naturas omnium animalium, et pari ratione, habuit omnium aliorum scientiam.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32456)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod naturali ordine perfectum praecedit imperfectum, sicut et actus potentiam, quia ea quae sunt in potentia, non reducuntur ad actum nisi per aliquod ens actu. Et quia res primitus a Deo institutae sunt, non solum ut in seipsis essent, sed etiam ut essent aliorum principia; ideo productae sunt in statu perfecto, in quo possent esse principia aliorum. Homo autem potest esse principium alterius non solum per generationem corporalem, sed etiam per instructionem et gubernationem. Et ideo, sicut primus homo institutus est in statu perfecto quantum ad corpus, ut statim posset generare; ita etiam institutus est in statu perfecto quantum ad animam, ut statim posset alios instruere et gubernare. Non potest autem aliquis instruere, nisi habeat scientiam. Et ideo primus homo sic institutus est a Deo, ut haberet omnium scientiam in quibus homo natus est instrui. Et haec sunt omnia illa quae virtualiter existunt in primis principiis per se notis, quaecumque scilicet naturaliter homines cognoscere possunt. Ad gubernationem autem vitae propriae et aliorum, non solum requiritur cognitio eorum quae naturaliter sciri possunt, sed etiam cognitio eorum quae naturalem cognitionem excedunt; eo quod vita hominis ordinatur ad quendam finem supernaturalem; sicut nobis, ad gubernationem vitae nostrae, necessarium est cognoscere quae fidei sunt. Unde et de his supernaturalibus tantam cognitionem primus homo accepit, quanta erat necessaria ad gubernationem vitae humanae secundum statum illum. Alia vero, quae nec naturali hominis studio cognosci possunt, nec sunt necessaria ad gubernationem vitae humanae, primus homo non cognovit; sicut sunt cogitationes hominum, futura contingentia, et quaedam singularia, puta quot lapilli iaceant in flumine, et alia huiusmodi.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32457)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod primus homo habuit scientiam omnium per species a Deo infusas. Nec tamen scientia illa fuit alterius rationis a scientia nostra; sicut nec oculi quos caeco nato Christus dedit, fuerunt alterius rationis ab oculis quos natura produxit.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32458)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Adam debebat aliquid habere perfectionis, inquantum erat primus homo, quod ceteris hominibus non competit; ut ex dictis patet.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32459)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Adam in scientia naturalium scibilium non profecisset quantum ad numerum scitorum, sed quantum ad modum sciendi, quia quae sciebat intellectualiter, scivisset postmodum per experimentum. Quantum vero ad supernaturalia cognita, profecisset etiam quantum ad numerum, per novas revelationes; sicut et Angeli proficiunt per novas illuminationes. Nec tamen est simile de profectu meriti, et scientiae, quia unus homo non est alteri principium merendi, sicut est sciendi.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 94 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32460)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo in primo statu decipi potuisset. Dicit enim apostolus, I ad Tim. II, quod *mulier seducta in praevaricatione fuit*.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32461)\n\nPraeterea, Magister dicit, XXI dist. II Sent., quod *ideo mulier non horruit serpentem loquentem, quia officium loquendi eum accepisse a Deo putavit*. Sed hoc falsum erat. Ergo mulier decepta fuit ante peccatum.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32462)\n\nPraeterea, naturale est quod quanto aliquid remotius videtur, tanto minus videtur. Sed natura oculi non est contracta per peccatum. Ergo hoc idem in statu innocentiae contigisset. Fuisset ergo homo deceptus circa quantitatem rei visae, sicut et modo.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32463)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, XII super Gen. ad Litt., quod in somno adhaeret anima similitudini tanquam ipsi rei. Sed homo in statu innocentiae comedisset, et per consequens dormivisset et somniasset. Ergo deceptus fuisset, adhaerendo similitudinibus tanquam rebus.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32464)\n\nPraeterea, primus homo nescivisset cogitationes hominum et futura contingentia, ut dictum est. Si igitur aliquis super his sibi falsum diceret, deceptus fuisset.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32465)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, *approbare vera pro falsis, non est natura instituti hominis, sed poena damnati*.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32466)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam dixerunt quod in nomine deceptionis duo possunt intelligi, scilicet qualiscumque existimatio levis, qua aliquis adhaeret falso tanquam vero, sine assensu credulitatis; et iterum firma credulitas. Quantum ergo ad ea quorum scientiam Adam habebat, neutro istorum modorum homo decipi poterat ante peccatum. Sed quantum ad ea quorum scientiam non habebat, decipi poterat, large accepta deceptione pro existimatione qualicumque sine assensu credulitatis. Quod ideo dicunt, quia existimare falsum in talibus, non est noxium homini; et ex quo temere assensus non adhibetur, non est culpabile. Sed haec positio non convenit integritati primi status, quia, ut Augustinus dicit XIV de Civit. Dei, *in illo statu erat devitatio tranquilla peccati, qua manente, nullum malum omnino esse poterat*. Manifestum est autem quod, sicut verum est bonum intellectus, ita falsum est malum eius, ut dicitur in VI Ethic. Unde non poterat esse quod, innocentia manente, intellectus hominis alicui falso acquiesceret quasi vero. Sicut enim in membris corporis primi hominis erat quidem carentia perfectionis alicuius, puta claritatis, non tamen aliquod malum inesse poterat; ita in intellectu poterat esse carentia notitiae alicuius, nulla tamen poterat ibi esse existimatio falsi. Quod etiam ex ipsa rectitudine primi status apparet, secundum quam, quandiu anima maneret Deo subdita, tandiu in homine inferiora superioribus subderentur, nec superiora per inferiora impedirentur. Manifestum est autem ex praemissis quod intellectus circa proprium obiectum semper verus est. Unde ex seipso nunquam decipitur, sed omnis deceptio accidit in intellectu ex aliquo inferiori, puta phantasia vel aliquo huiusmodi. Unde videmus quod, quando naturale iudicatorium non est ligatum, non decipimur per huiusmodi apparitiones, sed solum quando ligatur, ut patet in dormientibus. Unde manifestum est quod rectitudo primi status non compatiebatur aliquam deceptionem circa intellectum.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32467)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod illa seductio mulieris, etsi praecesserit peccatum operis, subsecuta tamen est peccatum internae elationis. Dicit enim Augustinus, XI super Gen. ad Litt., quod *mulier verbis serpentis non crederet, nisi iam inesset menti eius amor propriae potestatis, et quaedam de se superba praesumptio*.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32468)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod mulier putavit serpentem hoc accepisse loquendi officium, non per naturam, sed aliqua supernaturali operatione. Quamvis non sit necessarium auctoritatem Magistri sententiarum sequi in hac parte.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32469)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, si aliquid repraesentatum fuisset sensui vel phantasiae primi hominis aliter quam sit in rerum natura, non tamen deciperetur, quia per rationem veritatem diiudicaret.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32470)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod id quod accidit in somno, non imputatur homini, quia non habet usum rationis, qui est proprius hominis actus.\n\n[I q. 94 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32471)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod alicui dicenti falsum de contingentibus futuris vel cogitationibus cordium, homo in statu innocentiae non credidisset ita esse, sed credidisset quod hoc esset possibile, et hoc non esset existimare falsum. Vel potest dici quod divinitus ei subventum fuisset, ne deciperetur in his quorum scientiam non habebat. Nec est instantia, quam quidam afferunt, quod in tentatione non fuit ei subventum ne deciperetur, licet tunc maxime indigeret. Quia iam praecesserat peccatum in animo, et ad divinum auxilium recursum non habuit.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0002","text":"[I q. 94 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32435)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de statu vel conditione primi hominis. Et primo, quantum ad animam; secundo, quantum ad corpus. Circa primum consideranda sunt duo, primo, de conditione hominis quantum ad intellectum; secundo, de conditione hominis quantum ad voluntatem. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum primus homo viderit Deum per essentiam. Secundo, utrum videre potuerit substantias separatas, idest Angelos. Tertio, utrum habuerit omnium scientiam. Quarto, utrum potuerit errare vel decipi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0005","text":"[I q. 94 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32436)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod primus homo per essentiam Deum viderit. Beatitudo enim hominis in visione divinae essentiae consistit. Sed primus homo, in Paradiso conversans, beatam et omnium divitem habuit vitam, ut Damascenus dicit in II libro. Et Augustinus dicit, in XIV de Civ. Dei, *si homines habebant affectus suos quales nunc habemus, quomodo erant beati in illo inenarrabilis beatitudinis loco, idest Paradiso?* Ergo primus homo in Paradiso vidit Deum per essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0007","text":"[I q. 94 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32437)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0008","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, XIV de Civ. Dei, quod *primo homini non aberat quidquam quod bona voluntas adipisceretur*. Sed nihil melius bona voluntas adipisci potest quam divinae essentiae visionem. Ergo homo per essentiam Deum videbat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0009","text":"[I q. 94 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32438)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0010","text":"Praeterea, visio Dei per essentiam est qua videtur Deus sine medio et sine aenigmate. Sed homo in statu innocentiae vidit Deum sine medio; ut Magister dicit in I distinctione IV libri Sent. Vidit etiam sine aenigmate, quia aenigma obscuritatem importat, ut Augustinus dicit, XV de Trin.; obscuritas autem introducta est per peccatum. Ergo homo in primo statu vidit Deum per essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0011","text":"[I q. 94 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32439)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod apostolus dicit, I ad Cor. XV, quod *non prius quod spirituale est, sed quod animale*. Sed maxime spirituale est videre Deum per essentiam. Ergo primus homo, in primo statu animalis vitae, Deum per essentiam non vidit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0013","text":"[I q. 94 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32440)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod primus homo Deum per essentiam non vidit, secundum communem statum illius vitae; nisi forte dicatur quod viderit eum in raptu, quando Deus immisit soporem in Adam, ut dicitur Gen. II. Et huius ratio est quia, cum divina essentia sit ipsa beatitudo, hoc modo se habet intellectus videntis divinam essentiam ad Deum, sicut se habet quilibet homo ad beatitudinem. Manifestum est autem quod nullus homo potest per voluntatem a beatitudine averti, naturaliter enim, et ex necessitate, homo vult beatitudinem, et fugit miseriam. Unde nullus videns Deum per essentiam, potest voluntate averti a Deo, quod est peccare. Et propter hoc, omnes videntes Deum per essentiam, sic in amore Dei stabiliuntur, quod in aeternum peccare non possunt. Cum ergo Adam peccaverit, manifestum est quod Deum per essentiam non videbat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0015","text":"Cognoscebat tamen Deum quadam altiori cognitione quam nos cognoscamus, et sic quodammodo eius cognitio media erat inter cognitionem praesentis status, et cognitionem patriae, qua Deus per essentiam videtur. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod visio Dei per essentiam dividitur contra visionem Dei per creaturam. Quanto autem aliqua creatura est altior et Deo similior, tanto per eam Deus clarius videtur, sicut homo perfectius videtur per speculum in quo expressius imago eius resultat. Et sic patet quod multo eminentius videtur Deus per intelligibiles effectus, quam per sensibiles et corporeos. A consideratione autem plena et lucida intelligibilium effectuum impeditur homo in statu praesenti, per hoc quod distrahitur a sensibilibus, et circa ea occupatur. Sed, sicut dicitur Eccle. VII, Deus fecit hominem rectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0016","text":"Haec autem fuit rectitudo hominis divinitus instituti, ut inferiora superioribus subderentur, et superiora ab inferioribus non impedirentur. Unde homo primus non impediebatur per res exteriores a clara et firma contemplatione intelligibilium effectuum, quos ex irradiatione primae veritatis percipiebat, sive naturali cognitione sive gratuita. Unde dicit Augustinus, in XI super Gen. ad Litt., quod *fortassis Deus primis hominibus antea loquebatur, sicut cum Angelis loquitur, ipsa incommutabili veritate illustrans mentes eorum; etsi non tanta participatione divinae essentiae, quantam capiunt Angeli*. Sic igitur per huiusmodi intelligibiles effectus Dei, Deum clarius cognoscebat quam modo cognoscamus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0017","text":"[I q. 94 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32441)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0018","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod homo in Paradiso beatus fuit, non illa perfecta beatitudine in quam transferendus erat, quae in divinae essentiae visione consistit, habebat tamen beatam vitam secundum quendam modum, ut Augustinus dicit XI super Gen. ad Litt., inquantum habebat integritatem et perfectionem quandam naturalem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0019","text":"[I q. 94 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32442)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod bona voluntas est ordinata voluntas. Non autem fuisset primi hominis ordinata voluntas, si in statu meriti habere voluisset quod ei promittebatur pro praemio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0021","text":"[I q. 94 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32443)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod duplex est medium. Quoddam, in quo simul videtur quod per medium videri dicitur; sicut cum homo videtur per speculum, et simul videtur cum ipso speculo. Aliud medium est, per cuius notitiam in aliquid ignotum devenimus; sicut est medium demonstrationis. Et sine tali medio Deus videbatur, non tamen sine primo medio. Non enim oportebat primum hominem pervenire in Dei cognitionem per demonstrationem sumptam ab aliquo effectu, sicut nobis est necessarium; sed simul in effectibus, praecipue intelligibilibus, suo modo Deum cognoscebat. Similiter etiam est considerandum quod obscuritas quae importatur in nomine aenigmatis, dupliciter potest accipi. Uno modo, secundum quod quaelibet creatura est quoddam obscurum, si comparetur ad immensitatem divinae claritatis, et sic Adam videbat Deum in aenigmate, quia videbat Deum per effectum creatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0023","text":"Alio modo potest accipi obscuritas quae consecuta est ex peccato, prout scilicet impeditur homo a consideratione intelligibilium per sensibilium occupationem, et secundum hoc, non vidit Deum in aenigmate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0024","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0025","text":"[I q. 94 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32444)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0026","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Adam in statu innocentiae Angelos per essentiam viderit. Dicit enim Gregorius, in IV Dialog., *in Paradiso quippe assueverat homo verbis Dei perfrui, beatorum Angelorum spiritibus cordis munditia et celsitudine visionis interesse*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0027","text":"[I q. 94 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32445)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0028","text":"Praeterea, anima in statu praesenti impeditur a cognitione substantiarum separatarum, ex hoc quod est unita corpori corruptibili, quod aggravat animam, ut dicitur Sap. IX. Unde et anima separata substantias separatas videre potest, ut supra dictum est. Sed anima primi hominis non aggravabatur a corpore, cum non esset corruptibile. Ergo poterat videre substantias separatas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0029","text":"[I q. 94 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32446)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0030","text":"Praeterea, una substantia separata cognoscit aliam cognoscendo seipsam, ut dicitur in libro de causis. Sed anima primi hominis cognoscebat seipsam. Ergo cognoscebat substantias separatas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0031","text":"[I q. 94 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32447)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0032","text":"Sed contra, anima Adae fuit eiusdem naturae cum animabus nostris. Sed animae nostrae non possunt nunc intelligere substantias separatas. Ergo nec anima primi hominis potuit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0033","text":"[I q. 94 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32448)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0034","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod status animae hominis distingui potest dupliciter. Uno modo, secundum diversum modum naturalis esse, et hoc modo distinguitur status animae separatae, a statu animae coniunctae corpori. Alio modo distinguitur status animae secundum integritatem et corruptionem, servato eodem modo essendi secundum naturam, et sic status innocentiae distinguitur a statu hominis post peccatum. Anima enim hominis in statu innocentiae erat corpori perficiendo et gubernando accommodata, sicut et nunc, unde dicitur primus homo factus fuisse in animam viventem, idest corpori vitam dantem, scilicet animalem. Sed huius vitae integritatem habebat, inquantum corpus erat totaliter animae subditum, in nullo ipsam impediens, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0035","text":"Manifestum est autem ex praemissis quod ex hoc quod anima est accommodata ad corporis gubernationem et perfectionem secundum animalem vitam, competit animae nostrae talis modus intelligendi, qui est per conversionem ad phantasmata. Unde et hic modus intelligendi etiam animae primi hominis competebat. Secundum autem hunc modum intelligendi, motus quidam invenitur in anima, ut Dionysius dicit IV cap. de Div. Nom., secundum tres gradus. Quorum primus est, secundum quod a rebus exterioribus congregatur anima ad seipsam; secundus autem est, prout anima ascendit ad hoc quod uniatur virtutibus superioribus unitis, scilicet Angelis; tertius autem gradus est, secundum quod ulterius manuducitur ad bonum quod est supra omnia, scilicet Deum. Secundum igitur primum processum animae, qui est a rebus exterioribus ad seipsam, perficitur animae cognitio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0036","text":"Quia scilicet intellectualis operatio animae naturalem ordinem habet ad ea quae sunt extra, ut supra dictum est, et ita per eorum cognitionem perfecte cognosci potest nostra intellectualis operatio, sicut actus per obiectum. Et per ipsam intellectualem operationem perfecte potest cognosci humanus intellectus, sicut potentia per proprium actum. Sed in secundo processu non invenitur perfecta cognitio. Quia, cum Angelus non intelligat per conversionem ad phantasmata, sed longe eminentiori modo, ut supra dictum est; praedictus modus cognoscendi, quo anima cognoscit seipsam, non sufficienter ducit in Angeli cognitionem. Multo autem minus tertius processus ad perfectam notitiam terminatur, quia etiam ipsi Angeli, per hoc quod cognoscunt seipsos, non possunt pertingere ad cognitionem divinae substantiae propter eius excessum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0037","text":"Sic igitur anima primi hominis non poterat videre Angelos per essentiam. Sed tamen excellentiorem modum cognitionis habebat de eis, quam nos habeamus, quia eius cognitio erat magis certa et fixa circa interiora intelligibilia, quam cognitio nostra. Et propter tantam eminentiam dicit Gregorius quod intererat Angelorum spiritibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0038","text":"[I q. 94 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32449)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0039","text":"Unde patet solutio ad primum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0040","text":"[I q. 94 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32450)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0041","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod hoc quod anima primi hominis deficiebat ab intellectu substantiarum separatarum, non erat ex aggravatione corporis; sed ex hoc quod obiectum ei connaturale erat deficiens ab excellentia substantiarum separatarum. Nos autem deficimus propter utrumque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0042","text":"[I q. 94 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32451)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0043","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod anima primi hominis non poterat per cognitionem sui ipsius pertingere ad cognoscendas substantias separatas, ut supra dictum est, quia etiam unaquaeque substantia separata cognoscit aliam per modum sui ipsius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0044","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0045","text":"[I q. 94 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32452)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0046","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod primus homo non habuerit scientiam omnium. Aut enim habuit talem scientiam per species acquisitas, aut per species connaturales, aut per species infusas. Non autem per species acquisitas, huiusmodi enim cognitio ab experientia causatur, ut dicitur in I Metaphys.; ipse autem non tunc fuerat omnia expertus. Similiter etiam nec per species connaturales, quia erat eiusdem naturae nobiscum; anima autem nostra est sicut tabula in qua nihil est scriptum, ut dicitur in III de anima. Si autem per species infusas, ergo scientia eius quam habebat de rebus, non erat eiusdem rationis cum scientia nostra, quam a rebus acquirimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0047","text":"[I q. 94 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32453)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0048","text":"Praeterea, in omnibus individuis eiusdem speciei est idem modus consequendi perfectionem. Sed alii homines non statim in sui principio habent omnium scientiam, sed eam per temporis successionem acquirunt secundum suum modum. Ergo nec Adam, statim formatus, habuit omnium scientiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0049","text":"[I q. 94 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32454)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0050","text":"Praeterea, status praesentis vitae homini conceditur ut in eo proficiat anima et quantum ad cognitionem, et quantum ad meritum; propter hoc enim anima corpori videtur esse unita. Sed homo in statu illo profecisset quantum ad meritum. Ergo etiam profecisset quantum ad cognitionem rerum. Non ergo habuit omnium rerum scientiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0051","text":"[I q. 94 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32455)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0052","text":"Sed contra est quod ipse imposuit nomina animalibus, ut dicitur Gen. II. Nomina autem debent naturis rerum congruere. Ergo Adam scivit naturas omnium animalium, et pari ratione, habuit omnium aliorum scientiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0053","text":"[I q. 94 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32456)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0054","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod naturali ordine perfectum praecedit imperfectum, sicut et actus potentiam, quia ea quae sunt in potentia, non reducuntur ad actum nisi per aliquod ens actu. Et quia res primitus a Deo institutae sunt, non solum ut in seipsis essent, sed etiam ut essent aliorum principia; ideo productae sunt in statu perfecto, in quo possent esse principia aliorum. Homo autem potest esse principium alterius non solum per generationem corporalem, sed etiam per instructionem et gubernationem. Et ideo, sicut primus homo institutus est in statu perfecto quantum ad corpus, ut statim posset generare; ita etiam institutus est in statu perfecto quantum ad animam, ut statim posset alios instruere et gubernare. Non potest autem aliquis instruere, nisi habeat scientiam. Et ideo primus homo sic institutus est a Deo, ut haberet omnium scientiam in quibus homo natus est instrui.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0055","text":"Et haec sunt omnia illa quae virtualiter existunt in primis principiis per se notis, quaecumque scilicet naturaliter homines cognoscere possunt. Ad gubernationem autem vitae propriae et aliorum, non solum requiritur cognitio eorum quae naturaliter sciri possunt, sed etiam cognitio eorum quae naturalem cognitionem excedunt; eo quod vita hominis ordinatur ad quendam finem supernaturalem; sicut nobis, ad gubernationem vitae nostrae, necessarium est cognoscere quae fidei sunt. Unde et de his supernaturalibus tantam cognitionem primus homo accepit, quanta erat necessaria ad gubernationem vitae humanae secundum statum illum. Alia vero, quae nec naturali hominis studio cognosci possunt, nec sunt necessaria ad gubernationem vitae humanae, primus homo non cognovit; sicut sunt cogitationes hominum, futura contingentia, et quaedam singularia, puta quot lapilli iaceant in flumine, et alia huiusmodi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0056","text":"[I q. 94 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32457)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0057","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod primus homo habuit scientiam omnium per species a Deo infusas. Nec tamen scientia illa fuit alterius rationis a scientia nostra; sicut nec oculi quos caeco nato Christus dedit, fuerunt alterius rationis ab oculis quos natura produxit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0058","text":"[I q. 94 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32458)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0059","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Adam debebat aliquid habere perfectionis, inquantum erat primus homo, quod ceteris hominibus non competit; ut ex dictis patet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0060","text":"[I q. 94 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32459)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0061","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Adam in scientia naturalium scibilium non profecisset quantum ad numerum scitorum, sed quantum ad modum sciendi, quia quae sciebat intellectualiter, scivisset postmodum per experimentum. Quantum vero ad supernaturalia cognita, profecisset etiam quantum ad numerum, per novas revelationes; sicut et Angeli proficiunt per novas illuminationes. Nec tamen est simile de profectu meriti, et scientiae, quia unus homo non est alteri principium merendi, sicut est sciendi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0062","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0063","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32460)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0064","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo in primo statu decipi potuisset. Dicit enim apostolus, I ad Tim. II, quod *mulier seducta in praevaricatione fuit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0065","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32461)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0066","text":"Praeterea, Magister dicit, XXI dist. II Sent., quod *ideo mulier non horruit serpentem loquentem, quia officium loquendi eum accepisse a Deo putavit*. Sed hoc falsum erat. Ergo mulier decepta fuit ante peccatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0067","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32462)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0068","text":"Praeterea, naturale est quod quanto aliquid remotius videtur, tanto minus videtur. Sed natura oculi non est contracta per peccatum. Ergo hoc idem in statu innocentiae contigisset. Fuisset ergo homo deceptus circa quantitatem rei visae, sicut et modo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0069","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32463)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0070","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, XII super Gen. ad Litt., quod in somno adhaeret anima similitudini tanquam ipsi rei. Sed homo in statu innocentiae comedisset, et per consequens dormivisset et somniasset. Ergo deceptus fuisset, adhaerendo similitudinibus tanquam rebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0071","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32464)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0072","text":"Praeterea, primus homo nescivisset cogitationes hominum et futura contingentia, ut dictum est. Si igitur aliquis super his sibi falsum diceret, deceptus fuisset.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0073","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32465)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0074","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, *approbare vera pro falsis, non est natura instituti hominis, sed poena damnati*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0075","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32466)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0076","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam dixerunt quod in nomine deceptionis duo possunt intelligi, scilicet qualiscumque existimatio levis, qua aliquis adhaeret falso tanquam vero, sine assensu credulitatis; et iterum firma credulitas. Quantum ergo ad ea quorum scientiam Adam habebat, neutro istorum modorum homo decipi poterat ante peccatum. Sed quantum ad ea quorum scientiam non habebat, decipi poterat, large accepta deceptione pro existimatione qualicumque sine assensu credulitatis. Quod ideo dicunt, quia existimare falsum in talibus, non est noxium homini; et ex quo temere assensus non adhibetur, non est culpabile. Sed haec positio non convenit integritati primi status, quia, ut Augustinus dicit XIV de Civit. Dei, *in illo statu erat devitatio tranquilla peccati, qua manente, nullum malum omnino esse poterat*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0077","text":"Manifestum est autem quod, sicut verum est bonum intellectus, ita falsum est malum eius, ut dicitur in VI Ethic. Unde non poterat esse quod, innocentia manente, intellectus hominis alicui falso acquiesceret quasi vero. Sicut enim in membris corporis primi hominis erat quidem carentia perfectionis alicuius, puta claritatis, non tamen aliquod malum inesse poterat; ita in intellectu poterat esse carentia notitiae alicuius, nulla tamen poterat ibi esse existimatio falsi. Quod etiam ex ipsa rectitudine primi status apparet, secundum quam, quandiu anima maneret Deo subdita, tandiu in homine inferiora superioribus subderentur, nec superiora per inferiora impedirentur. Manifestum est autem ex praemissis quod intellectus circa proprium obiectum semper verus est. Unde ex seipso nunquam decipitur, sed omnis deceptio accidit in intellectu ex aliquo inferiori, puta phantasia vel aliquo huiusmodi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0078","text":"Unde videmus quod, quando naturale iudicatorium non est ligatum, non decipimur per huiusmodi apparitiones, sed solum quando ligatur, ut patet in dormientibus. Unde manifestum est quod rectitudo primi status non compatiebatur aliquam deceptionem circa intellectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0079","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32467)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0080","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod illa seductio mulieris, etsi praecesserit peccatum operis, subsecuta tamen est peccatum internae elationis. Dicit enim Augustinus, XI super Gen. ad Litt., quod *mulier verbis serpentis non crederet, nisi iam inesset menti eius amor propriae potestatis, et quaedam de se superba praesumptio*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0081","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32468)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0082","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod mulier putavit serpentem hoc accepisse loquendi officium, non per naturam, sed aliqua supernaturali operatione. Quamvis non sit necessarium auctoritatem Magistri sententiarum sequi in hac parte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0083","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32469)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0084","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, si aliquid repraesentatum fuisset sensui vel phantasiae primi hominis aliter quam sit in rerum natura, non tamen deciperetur, quia per rationem veritatem diiudicaret.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0085","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32470)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0086","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod id quod accidit in somno, non imputatur homini, quia non habet usum rationis, qui est proprius hominis actus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0087","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32471)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0088","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod alicui dicenti falsum de contingentibus futuris vel cogitationibus cordium, homo in statu innocentiae non credidisset ita esse, sed credidisset quod hoc esset possibile, et hoc non esset existimare falsum. Vel potest dici quod divinitus ei subventum fuisset, ne deciperetur in his quorum scientiam non habebat. Nec est instantia, quam quidam afferunt, quod in tentatione non fuit ei subventum ne deciperetur, licet tunc maxime indigeret. Quia iam praecesserat peccatum in animo, et ad divinum auxilium recursum non habuit.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"8a09ca8fbb52fd8863368819e4602716b88e441a84deeeba14b8e43837d13bf8","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T152848471936Z_5c19dfff","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 94","source_sha256":"8a09ca8fbb52fd8863368819e4602716b88e441a84deeeba14b8e43837d13bf8","sha256":"4bdef4e27d97e3b49e627051686456f4a82ca3eb9b582f8c1d8fa8a86125821b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:28:48.471953+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0002","text":"[I q. 94 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32435)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0003","text":"Next, the state or condition of the first man must be considered. First, as regards the soul; second, as regards the body. Concerning the first, two things must be considered: first, the condition of man as regards the intellect; second, the condition of man as regards the will. Concerning the first, four questions are asked. First, whether the first man saw God through His essence. Second, whether he could see the separate substances, that is, the Angels. Third, whether he had knowledge of all things. Fourth, whether he could err or be deceived.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0005","text":"[I q. 94 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32436)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0006","text":"The first is approached thus. It seems that the first man saw God through His essence. For man's beatitude consists in the vision of the divine essence. But the first man, dwelling in Paradise, had a blessed life, rich in all things, as Damascene says in Book II. And Augustine says, in XIV *On the City of God*, *if human beings had their affections such as we now have, how were they blessed in that place of ineffable blessedness, that is, Paradise?* Therefore the first man in Paradise saw God through His essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0007","text":"[I q. 94 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32437)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0008","text":"Moreover, Augustine says, in XIV *On the City of God*, that *the first man lacked nothing which a good will might attain*. But a good will can attain nothing better than the vision of the divine essence. Therefore man saw God through His essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0009","text":"[I q. 94 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32438)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0010","text":"Moreover, the vision of God through His essence is that by which God is seen without a medium and without an enigma. But man in the state of innocence saw God without a medium, as the Master says in Book I, distinction 4, of the *Sentences*. He also saw without an enigma, because an enigma implies obscurity, as Augustine says in XV *On the Trinity*; but obscurity was introduced through sin. Therefore man in the first state saw God through His essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0011","text":"[I q. 94 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32439)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0012","text":"On the contrary, the Apostle says, in I Corinthians XV, that *that which is spiritual is not first, but that which is animal*. But to see God through His essence is most spiritual. Therefore the first man, in the first state of animal life, did not see God through His essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0013","text":"[I q. 94 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32440)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that the first man did not see God through His essence, according to the common state of that life; unless perhaps it be said that he saw Him in rapture, when God cast sleep upon Adam, as is said in Genesis II. The reason for this is that, since the divine essence is beatitude itself, the intellect seeing the divine essence is related to God in the same way as any man is related to beatitude. Now it is manifest that no man can turn away from beatitude by his will; for naturally and necessarily man wills beatitude and flees misery. Hence no one seeing God through His essence can turn away from God by the will, which is to sin. And on this account, all who see God through His essence are so established in the love of God that they cannot sin for eternity. Therefore, since Adam sinned, it is manifest that he did not see God through His essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0015","text":"Nevertheless, he knew God by a certain higher knowledge than we know Him, and thus in some way his knowledge was intermediate between the knowledge of the present state and the knowledge of the fatherland, by which God is seen through His essence. For the elucidation of this, it must be considered that the vision of God through His essence is divided against the vision of God through a creature. Now the higher and more like to God some creature is, the more clearly God is seen through it, just as a man is seen more perfectly through a mirror in which his image is reflected more distinctly. Thus it is evident that God is seen far more eminently through intelligible effects than through sensible and corporeal ones. But in the present state man is hindered from a full and clear consideration of intelligible effects because he is distracted by sensible things and occupied with them. But, as is said in Ecclesiastes VII, God made man upright.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0016","text":"Now this was the uprightness of man as divinely instituted: that lower things should be subject to higher things, and higher things should not be hindered by lower things. Hence the first man was not hindered by external things from a clear and firm contemplation of the intelligible effects which he perceived from the irradiation of the first truth, whether by natural or gratuitous knowledge. Hence Augustine says, in XI *On Genesis according to the Letter*, that *perhaps God previously spoke to the first human beings as He speaks with the Angels, illuminating their minds by that unchangeable truth itself; though not by so great a participation in the divine essence as the Angels receive*. Thus, therefore, through such intelligible effects of God, he knew God more clearly than we now know Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0017","text":"[I q. 94 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32441)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0018","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that man in Paradise was blessed, not with that perfect beatitude to which he was to be transferred, which consists in the vision of the divine essence; yet he had a blessed life in a certain manner, as Augustine says in XI *On Genesis according to the Letter*, insofar as he had a certain natural integrity and perfection.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0019","text":"[I q. 94 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32442)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0020","text":"To the second it must be said that a good will is an ordered will. But the will of the first man would not have been ordered if, in the state of merit, he had wished to have that which was promised him as a reward.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0021","text":"[I q. 94 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32443)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0022","text":"To the third it must be said that there is a twofold medium. One is that in which the thing said to be seen through a medium is seen at the same time; as when a man is seen through a mirror, and is at the same time seen along with the mirror itself. Another medium is that through the knowledge of which we arrive at something unknown; such is the medium of demonstration. And God was seen without such a medium, though not without the first medium. For it was not necessary for the first man to arrive at the knowledge of God through a demonstration taken from some effect, as is necessary for us; rather, in the effects themselves, especially intelligible ones, he knew God in his own manner at the same time. Likewise, it must also be considered that the obscurity implied in the name of enigma can be understood in two ways. In one way, insofar as every creature is a certain obscurity when compared with the immensity of the divine brightness; and in this way Adam saw God in an enigma, because he saw God through a created effect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0023","text":"In another way, the obscurity resulting from sin can be understood, namely insofar as man is hindered from the consideration of intelligible things by occupation with sensible things; and in this respect, he did not see God in an enigma.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0024","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0025","text":"[I q. 94 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32444)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0026","text":"The second point is approached thus. It seems that Adam, in the state of innocence, saw the Angels through their essence. For Gregory says, in *Dialogues* IV: “For in Paradise man had become accustomed to delight in the words of God, to be present among the spirits of the blessed Angels through purity of heart and loftiness of vision.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0027","text":"[I q. 94 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32445)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0028","text":"Moreover, in the present state the soul is impeded from knowledge of separated substances by the fact that it is united to a corruptible body, which weighs down the soul, as is said in Wisdom IX. Hence also the separated soul can see separated substances, as was said above. But the soul of the first man was not weighed down by the body, since it was not corruptible. Therefore, it could see separated substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0029","text":"[I q. 94 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32446)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0030","text":"Moreover, one separated substance knows another by knowing itself, as is said in the *Book of Causes*. But the soul of the first man knew itself. Therefore, it knew separated substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0031","text":"[I q. 94 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32447)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0032","text":"On the contrary, Adam's soul was of the same nature as our souls. But our souls cannot now understand separated substances. Therefore neither could the soul of the first man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0033","text":"[I q. 94 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32448)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0034","text":"I answer that the state of a human soul can be distinguished in two ways. In one way, according to a different mode of natural being; and in this way the state of the separated soul is distinguished from the state of the soul joined to the body. In another way, the state of the soul is distinguished according to integrity and corruption, while the same mode of being according to nature is preserved; and thus the state of innocence is distinguished from the state of man after sin. For the human soul in the state of innocence was fitted for perfecting and governing the body, just as it is now; hence the first man is said to have been made into a living soul, that is, one giving life to the body, namely animal life. But it possessed the integrity of this life insofar as the body was wholly subject to the soul, hindering it in no respect, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0035","text":"Now it is evident from what has been said that, from the fact that the soul is fitted for governing and perfecting the body according to animal life, there belongs to our soul such a mode of understanding as is through turning to phantasms. Hence this mode of understanding also belonged to the soul of the first man. But according to this mode of understanding, a certain movement is found in the soul, as Dionysius says in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, according to three stages. The first of these is insofar as the soul is gathered from external things to itself; the second is insofar as the soul ascends so as to be united to the higher united powers, namely, the Angels; and the third stage is insofar as it is further led by the hand to the good that is above all things, namely God. Therefore, according to the first process of the soul, which is from external things to itself, the soul's knowledge is perfected.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0036","text":"Namely, because the intellectual operation of the soul has a natural order to those things that are outside, as was said above, and thus through their knowledge our intellectual operation can be perfectly known, as an act through its object. And through the intellectual operation itself the human intellect can be perfectly known, as a power through its proper act. But in the second process perfect knowledge is not found. For, since an Angel does not understand through turning to phantasms, but in a far more eminent mode, as was said above, the aforesaid mode of knowing, by which the soul knows itself, does not sufficiently lead to knowledge of an Angel. But much less does the third process terminate in perfect knowledge, because even the Angels themselves, through the fact that they know themselves, cannot attain to knowledge of the divine substance on account of its excess.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0037","text":"Thus, therefore, the soul of the first man could not see Angels through their essence. Nevertheless, it had a more excellent mode of knowledge concerning them than we have, because its knowledge was more certain and fixed regarding interior intelligibles than ours is. And on account of so great an eminence Gregory says that it was present among the spirits of the Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0038","text":"[I q. 94 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32449)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0039","text":"Hence the solution to the first is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0040","text":"[I q. 94 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32450)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0041","text":"To the second, it must be said that the fact that the soul of the first man fell short of understanding separated substances was not due to the burden of the body, but to the fact that its connatural object fell short of the excellence of separated substances. But we fall short on account of both.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0042","text":"[I q. 94 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32451)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0043","text":"To the third, it must be said that the soul of the first man could not, through knowledge of itself, attain to knowing separated substances, as was said above, because each separated substance also knows another according to the mode of itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0044","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0045","text":"[I q. 94 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32452)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0046","text":"The third point is approached thus. It seems that the first man did not have knowledge of all things. For he had such knowledge either through acquired species, or through connatural species, or through infused species. But not through acquired species, for knowledge of this kind is caused by experience, as is said in *Metaphysics* I; but he had not then experienced all things. Likewise, neither was it through connatural species, because he was of the same nature as we are; but our soul is like a tablet on which nothing is written, as is said in *On the Soul* III. But if through infused species, then his knowledge, which he had of things, was not of the same kind as our knowledge, which we acquire from things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0047","text":"[I q. 94 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32453)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0048","text":"Moreover, in all individuals of the same species there is the same mode of attaining perfection. But other men do not at once, at their beginning, have knowledge of all things, but acquire it through the succession of time according to their mode. Therefore neither did Adam, immediately upon being formed, have knowledge of all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0049","text":"[I q. 94 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32454)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0050","text":"Moreover, the state of the present life is granted to man so that in it the soul may make progress both with respect to knowledge and with respect to merit; for this reason, indeed, the soul appears to be united to the body. But man would have made progress in that state with respect to merit. Therefore he would also have made progress with respect to knowledge of things. Therefore he did not have knowledge of all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0051","text":"[I q. 94 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32455)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0052","text":"But on the contrary, he himself gave names to the animals, as is said in Gen. II. Now names ought to correspond to the natures of things. Therefore Adam knew the natures of all animals, and by equal reasoning, he had knowledge of all other things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0053","text":"[I q. 94 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32456)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0054","text":"I answer that it must be said that, in the natural order, the perfect precedes the imperfect, just as act precedes potency, because those things that are in potency are not brought into act except through some being in act. And since things were first instituted by God not only so that they might exist in themselves, but also so that they might be principles of other things, they were therefore produced in a perfect state, in which they could be principles of other things. Now man can be the principle of another not only through bodily generation, but also through instruction and governance. And therefore, just as the first man was instituted in a perfect state with respect to the body, so that he might at once be able to generate, so also he was instituted in a perfect state with respect to the soul, so that he might at once be able to instruct and govern others. But no one can instruct unless he has knowledge. And therefore the first man was so instituted by God that he had knowledge of all those things in which man is born to be instructed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0055","text":"And these are all those things that exist virtually in the first self-evident principles, namely, whatever men can naturally know. But for the governance of one's own life and that of others, there is required not only knowledge of those things that can be naturally known, but also knowledge of those things that exceed natural knowledge; because human life is ordered to a certain supernatural end, just as it is necessary for us, for the governance of our life, to know those things that belong to faith. Hence the first man also received concerning these supernatural things as much knowledge as was necessary for the governance of human life according to that state. But the first man did not know other things, which can neither be known by man's natural study nor are necessary for the governance of human life; such as men's thoughts, contingent future events, and certain singular things—for example, how many stones lie in a river, and other such things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0056","text":"[I q. 94 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32457)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0057","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the first man had knowledge of all things through species infused by God. Yet that knowledge was not of a different kind from our knowledge, just as neither were the eyes that Christ gave to one born blind of a different kind from the eyes produced by nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0058","text":"[I q. 94 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32458)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0059","text":"To the second, it must be said that Adam ought to have had some perfection, insofar as he was the first man, which does not belong to other men, as is clear from what has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0060","text":"[I q. 94 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32459)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0061","text":"To the third, it must be said that Adam would not have made progress in knowledge of naturally knowable things with respect to the number of things known, but with respect to the mode of knowing, because what he knew intellectually he would afterwards have known through experience. But with respect to supernatural things known, he would have made progress also with respect to number, through new revelations; just as the angels make progress through new illuminations. Yet progress in merit and in knowledge is not alike, because one man is not the principle of another's meriting, as he is of another's knowing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0062","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0063","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32460)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0064","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that man could have been deceived in the first state. For the Apostle says, in I Tim. II, that *the woman, having been seduced, was in transgression*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0065","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32461)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0066","text":"Moreover, the Master says, XXI dist. II Sent., that *the woman did not recoil from the speaking serpent because she thought that it had received from God the office of speaking*. But this was false. Therefore the woman was deceived before sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0067","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32462)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0068","text":"Moreover, it is natural that the more remote something is seen, the less it is seen. But the nature of the eye was not impaired through sin. Therefore the same thing would have occurred in the state of innocence. Therefore man would have been deceived concerning the magnitude of a thing seen, just as now.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0069","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32463)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0070","text":"Moreover, Augustine says, in XII On the Literal Meaning of Genesis, that in sleep the soul adheres to a likeness as though to the thing itself. But man in the state of innocence would have eaten, and consequently would have slept and dreamed. Therefore he would have been deceived, adhering to likenesses as though to things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0071","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32464)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0072","text":"Moreover, the first man would not have known men's thoughts and contingent future events, as has been said. Therefore, if someone had told him something false about these matters, he would have been deceived.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0073","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32465)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0074","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says: *to approve false things as true is not the nature of man as instituted, but the punishment of man condemned*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0075","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32466)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0076","text":"I answer that some have said that two things can be understood under the name of deception: namely, any kind of slight estimation, by which someone adheres to the false as though to the true, without the assent of belief; and again, firm belief. Therefore, with respect to those things of which Adam had knowledge, man could not be deceived in either of these ways before sin. But with respect to those things of which he did not have knowledge, he could be deceived, taking deception broadly for any sort of estimation without the assent of belief. They say this because to estimate falsely in such matters is not harmful to man; and since assent is not rashly given, it is not blameworthy. But this position does not accord with the integrity of the first state, because, as Augustine says in *The City of God*, XIV, *in that state there was a tranquil avoidance of sin, which remaining, no evil whatsoever could exist*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0077","text":"Now it is clear that, just as the true is the good of the intellect, so the false is its evil, as is said in *Ethics* VI. Hence it could not be that, while innocence remained, the intellect of man should assent to something false as though it were true. For just as in the bodily members of the first man there was indeed a lack of some perfection, for example brightness, yet no evil could be present; so in the intellect there could be a lack of some knowledge, yet no estimation of the false could be there. This is also evident from the very rectitude of the first state, according to which, as long as the soul remained subject to God, so long would the lower things in man be subject to the higher, nor would the higher be impeded by the lower. Now it is clear from what has been said above that the intellect is always true concerning its proper object. Hence it is never deceived by itself, but all deception happens in the intellect from something lower, for example imagination or something of this kind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0078","text":"Hence we see that, when natural judgment is not bound, we are not deceived by apparitions of this kind, but only when it is bound, as is clear in those who sleep. Hence it is clear that the rectitude of the first state did not admit any deception concerning the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0079","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32467)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0080","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that seduction of the woman, although it preceded the sin of the deed, nevertheless followed the sin of inward pride. For Augustine says, in XI *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, that *the woman would not have believed the serpent's words unless love of her own power and a certain proud presumption concerning herself had already been present in her mind*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0081","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32468)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0082","text":"To the second, it must be said that the woman thought that the serpent had received this office of speaking, not by nature, but by some supernatural operation. Although it is not necessary to follow the authority of the Master of the Sentences on this point.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0083","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32469)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0084","text":"To the third, it must be said that, if something had been presented to the sense or imagination of the first man otherwise than it is in the nature of things, he nevertheless would not have been deceived, because by reason he would have judged the truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0085","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32470)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0086","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that what happens in sleep is not imputed to a man, because he does not have the use of reason, which is the act proper to man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0087","text":"[I q. 94 a. 4 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32471)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0088","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that a man in the state of innocence would not have believed that it was so if someone had said something false concerning future contingents or the thoughts of hearts; rather, he would have believed that this was possible, and this would not have been to estimate falsely. Or it can be said that he would have been aided by God, lest he be deceived in matters of which he had no knowledge. Nor is there force in the objection that some raise, that in temptation he was not aided lest he be deceived, although then he had the greatest need. For sin had already preceded in his mind, and he had not had recourse to divine help.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_86:P0088"]}],"source_digest":"d0904c5424380b086590cc315665329420a35f369059bbad9378da7d23368681","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 94"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 94","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87","title":"First Part · Question 95","order":96,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_87","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 95 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32472)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de his quae pertinent ad voluntatem primi hominis. Et circa hoc consideranda sunt duo, primo quidem, de gratia et iustitia primi hominis; secundo, de usu iustitiae quantum ad dominium super alia. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum primus homo creatus fuerit in gratia. Secundo, utrum in statu innocentiae habuerit animae passiones. Tertio, utrum habuit virtutes omnes. Quarto, utrum opera eius fuissent aeque efficacia ad merendum, sicut modo sunt.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 95 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32473)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod primus homo non fuerit creatus in gratia. Apostolus enim, I Cor. XV, distinguens Adam a Christo, dicit, *factus est primus Adam in animam viventem; novissimus autem in spiritum vivificantem*. Sed vivificatio spiritus est per gratiam. Ergo hoc est proprium Christi, quod fuerit factus in gratia.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32474)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, in libro de quaestionibus veteris et novi testamenti, quod *Adam non habuit spiritum sanctum*. Sed quicumque habet gratiam, habet spiritum sanctum. Ergo Adam non fuit creatus in gratia.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32475)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, in libro de correptione et gratia, quod *Deus sic ordinavit Angelorum et hominum vitam, ut prius in eis ostenderet quid posset eorum liberum arbitrium, deinde quid posset suae gratiae beneficium, iustitiaeque iudicium*. Primo ergo condidit hominem et Angelum in sola naturali arbitrii libertate, et postmodum eis gratiam contulit.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32476)\n\nPraeterea, Magister dicit, in XXIV distinctione libri II Sent., quod *homini in creatione datum est auxilium per quod stare poterat, sed non poterat proficere*. Quicumque autem habet gratiam, potest proficere per meritum. Ergo primus homo non fuit creatus in gratia.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32477)\n\nPraeterea, ad hoc quod aliquis accipiat gratiam, requiritur consensus ex parte recipientis, cum per hoc perficiatur matrimonium quoddam spirituale inter Deum et animam. Sed consensus in gratiam esse non potest nisi prius existentis. Ergo homo non accepit gratiam in primo instanti suae creationis.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 1 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32478)\n\nPraeterea, natura plus distat a gratia quam gratia a gloria, quae nihil est aliud quam gratia consummata. Sed in homine gratia praecessit gloriam. Ergo multo magis natura praecessit gratiam.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32479)\n\nSed contra, homo et Angelus aequaliter ordinantur ad gratiam. Sed Angelus est creatus in gratia, dicit enim Augustinus, XII de Civ. Dei, quod *Deus simul erat in eis condens naturam et largiens gratiam*. Ergo et homo creatus fuit in gratia.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32480)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam dicunt quod primus homo non fuit quidem creatus in gratia, sed tamen postmodum gratia fuit sibi collata antequam peccasset, plurimae enim sanctorum auctoritates attestantur hominem in statu innocentiae gratiam habuisse. Sed quod etiam fuerit conditus in gratia, ut alii dicunt, videtur requirere ipsa rectitudo primi status, in qua Deus hominem fecit, secundum illud Eccle. VII, *Deus fecit hominem rectum*. Erat enim haec rectitudo secundum hoc, quod ratio subdebatur Deo, rationi vero inferiores vires, et animae corpus. Prima autem subiectio erat causa et secundae et tertiae, quandiu enim ratio manebat Deo subiecta, inferiora ei subdebantur, ut Augustinus dicit. Manifestum est autem quod illa subiectio corporis ad animam, et inferiorum virium ad rationem, non erat naturalis, alioquin post peccatum mansisset, cum etiam in Daemonibus data naturalia post peccatum permanserint, ut Dionysius dicit cap. IV de Div. Nom. Unde manifestum est quod et illa prima subiectio, qua ratio Deo subdebatur, non erat solum secundum naturam, sed secundum supernaturale donum gratiae, non enim potest esse quod effectus sit potior quam causa. Unde Augustinus dicit, XIII de Civ. Dei, quod *posteaquam praecepti facta transgressio est, confestim, gratia deserente divina, de corporum suorum nuditate confusi sunt, senserunt enim motum inobedientis carnis suae, tanquam reciprocam poenam inobedientiae suae*. Ex quo datur intelligi, si deserente gratia soluta est obedientia carnis ad animam, quod per gratiam in anima existentem inferiora ei subdebantur.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32481)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod apostolus illa verba inducit ad ostendendum esse corpus spirituale, si est corpus animale, quia vita spiritualis corporis incoepit in Christo, qui est primogenitus ex mortuis, sicut vita corporis animalis incoepit in Adam. Non ergo ex verbis apostoli habetur quod Adam non fuit spiritualis secundum animam; sed quod non fuit spiritualis secundum corpus.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32482)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit in eodem loco, non negatur quin aliquo modo fuerit in Adam spiritus sanctus, sicut et in aliis iustis, sed quod non sic fuerit in eo, sicut nunc est in fidelibus, qui admittuntur ad perceptionem haereditatis aeternae statim post mortem.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32483)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ex illa auctoritate Augustini non habetur quod Angelus vel homo prius fuerit creatus in naturali libertate arbitrii, quam habuisset gratiam, sed quod prius ostendit quid in eis posset liberum arbitrium ante confirmationem, et quid postmodum consecuti sunt per auxilium gratiae confirmantis.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32484)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod Magister loquitur secundum opinionem illorum qui posuerunt hominem non esse creatum in gratia, sed in naturalibus tantum. Vel potest dici quod, etsi homo fuerit creatus in gratia, non tamen habuit ex creatione naturae quod posset proficere per meritum, sed ex superadditione gratiae.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32485)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod, cum motus voluntatis non sit continuus, nihil prohibet etiam in primo instanti suae creationis primum hominem gratiae consensisse.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 1 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32486)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod gloriam meremur per actum gratiae, non autem gratiam per actum naturae. Unde non est similis ratio.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 95 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32487)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in primo homine non fuerint animae passiones. Secundum enim passiones animae contingit quod caro concupiscit adversus spiritum. Sed hoc non erat in statu innocentiae. Ergo in statu innocentiae non erant animae passiones.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32488)\n\nPraeterea, anima Adae erat nobilior quam corpus. Sed corpus Adae fuit impassibile. Ergo nec in anima eius fuerunt passiones.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32489)\n\nPraeterea, per virtutem moralem comprimuntur animae passiones. Sed in Adam fuit virtus moralis perfecta. Ergo totaliter passiones excludebantur ab eo.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32490)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, XIV de Civ. Dei, quod erat in eis amor imperturbatus in Deum, et quaedam aliae animae passiones.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32491)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod passiones animae sunt in appetitu sensuali, cuius obiectum est bonum et malum. Unde omnium passionum animae quaedam ordinantur ad bonum, ut amor et gaudium; quaedam ad malum, ut timor et dolor. Et quia in primo statu nullum malum aderat nec imminebat; nec aliquod bonum aberat, quod cuperet bona voluntas pro tempore illo habendum, ut patet per Augustinum XIV de Civ. Dei, omnes illae passiones quae respiciunt malum, in Adam non erant ut timor et dolor et huiusmodi; similiter nec illae passiones quae respiciunt bonum non habitum et nunc habendum, ut cupiditas aestuans. Illae vero passiones quae possunt esse boni praesentis, ut gaudium et amor; vel quae sunt futuri boni ut suo tempore habendi, ut desiderium et spes non affligens; fuerunt in statu innocentiae. Aliter tamen quam in nobis. Nam in nobis appetitus sensualis, in quo sunt passiones, non totaliter subest rationi, unde passiones quandoque sunt in nobis praevenientes iudicium rationis, et impedientes; quandoque vero ex iudicio rationis consequentes, prout sensualis appetitus aliqualiter rationi obedit. In statu vero innocentiae inferior appetitus erat rationi totaliter subiectus, unde non erant in eo passiones animae, nisi ex rationis iudicio consequentes.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32492)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod caro concupiscit adversus spiritum, per hoc quod passiones rationi repugnant, quod in statu innocentiae non erat.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32493)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod corpus humanum in statu innocentiae erat impassibile quantum ad passiones quae removent dispositionem naturalem, ut infra dicetur. Et similiter anima fuit impassibilis quantum ad passiones quae impediunt rationem.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32494)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod perfecta virtus moralis non totaliter tollit passiones, sed ordinat eas, *temperati enim est concupiscere sicut oportet, et quae oportet*, ut dicitur in III Ethic.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 95 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32495)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Adam non habuerit omnes virtutes. Quaedam enim virtutes ordinantur ad refraenandam immoderantiam passionum; sicut per temperantiam refraenatur immoderata concupiscentia, et per fortitudinem immoderatus timor. Sed immoderantia passionum non erat in statu innocentiae. Ergo nec dictae virtutes.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32496)\n\nPraeterea, quaedam virtutes sunt circa passiones respicientes malum; ut mansuetudo circa iras, et fortitudo circa timores. Sed tales passiones non erant in statu innocentiae, ut dictum est. Ergo nec huiusmodi virtutes.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32497)\n\nPraeterea, poenitentia est quaedam virtus respiciens peccatum prius commissum. Misericordia etiam est quaedam virtus respiciens miseriam. Sed in statu innocentiae non erat peccatum nec miseria. Ergo neque huiusmodi virtutes.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32498)\n\nPraeterea, perseverantia est quaedam virtus. Sed hanc Adam non habuit; ut sequens peccatum ostendit. Ergo non habuit omnes virtutes.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32499)\n\nPraeterea, fides quaedam virtus est. Sed haec in statu innocentiae non fuit, importat enim aenigmaticam cognitionem, quae perfectioni primi status repugnare videtur.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32500)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in quadam homilia, *princeps vitiorum devicit Adam de limo terrae ad imaginem Dei factum, pudicitia armatum, temperantia compositum, claritate splendidum*.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32501)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod homo in statu innocentiae aliqualiter habuit omnes virtutes. Et hoc ex dictis potest esse manifestum. Dictum est enim supra quod talis erat rectitudo primi status, quod ratio erat Deo subiecta, inferiores autem vires rationi. Virtutes autem nihil aliud sunt quam perfectiones quaedam, quibus ratio ordinatur in Deum, et inferiores vires disponuntur secundum regulam rationis; ut magis patebit cum de virtutibus agetur. Unde rectitudo primi status exigebat ut homo aliqualiter omnes virtutes haberet. Sed considerandum est quod virtutum quaedam sunt, quae de sui ratione nullam imperfectionem important, ut caritas et iustitia. Et huiusmodi virtutes fuerunt in statu innocentiae simpliciter, et quantum ad habitum et quantum ad actum. Quaedam vero sunt, quae de sui ratione imperfectionem important, vel ex parte actus vel ex parte materiae. Et si huiusmodi imperfectio non repugnat perfectioni primi status, nihilominus huiusmodi virtutes poterant esse in primo statu; sicut fides, quae est eorum quae non videntur, et spes, quae est eorum quae non habentur. Perfectio enim primi status non se extendebat ad hoc, ut videret Deum per essentiam, et ut haberet eum cum fruitione finalis beatitudinis, unde fides et spes esse poterant in primo statu, et quantum ad habitum et quantum ad actum. Si vero imperfectio quae est de ratione virtutis alicuius, repugnat perfectioni primi status, poterat huiusmodi virtus ibi esse secundum habitum, sed non secundum actum, ut patet de poenitentia, quae est dolor de peccato commisso, et de misericordia, quae est dolor de miseria aliena; perfectioni enim primi status repugnat tam dolor, quam culpa et miseria. Unde huiusmodi virtutes erant in primo homine secundum habitum, sed non secundum actum, erat enim primus homo sic dispositus, ut si peccatum praecessisset, doleret; et similiter si miseriam in alio videret, eam pro posse repelleret. Sicut philosophus dicit, in IV Ethic., quod verecundia, quae est de turpi facto, *contingit studioso solum sub conditione, est enim sic dispositus, quod verecundaretur si turpe aliquid committeret*.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32502)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod accidit temperantiae et fortitudini quod superabundantiam passionum repellat, inquantum invenit passiones superabundantes in subiecto. Sed per se convenit huiusmodi virtutibus passiones moderari.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32503)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod illae passiones ad malum ordinatae, repugnant perfectioni primi status, quae habent respectum ad malum in ipso qui afficitur passione, ut timor et dolor. Sed passiones quae respiciunt malum in altero, non repugnant perfectioni primi status, poterat enim homo in primo statu habere odio malitiam Daemonum, sicut et diligere bonitatem Dei. Unde et virtutes quae circa tales passiones essent, possent esse in primo statu, et quantum ad habitum et quantum ad actum. Quae vero sunt circa passiones respicientes malum in eodem subiecto, si circa huiusmodi solas passiones sunt, non poterant esse in primo statu secundum actum, sed solum secundum habitum; sicut de poenitentia et misericordia dictum est. Sed sunt quaedam virtutes quae non sunt circa has passiones solum, sed etiam circa alias; sicut temperantia, quae non solum est circa tristitias, sed etiam circa delectationes; et fortitudo, quae non solum est circa timores, sed etiam circa audaciam et spem. Unde poterat esse in primo statu actus temperantiae, secundum quod est moderativa delectationum; et similiter fortitudo, secundum quod est moderativa audaciae sive spei; non autem secundum quod moderantur tristitiam et timorem.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32504)\n\nAd tertium patet solutio ex his quae dicta sunt.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32505)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod perseverantia dupliciter sumitur. Uno modo, prout est quaedam virtus, et sic significat quendam habitum, quo quis eligit perseverare in bono. Et sic Adam perseverantiam habuit. Alio modo, prout est circumstantia virtutis, et sic significat continuationem quandam virtutis absque interruptione. Et hoc modo Adam perseverantiam non habuit.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32506)\n\nAd quintum patet responsio per ea quae dicta sunt.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 95 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32507)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod opera primi hominis fuerint minus efficacia ad merendum quam opera nostra. Gratia enim ex Dei misericordia datur, quae magis indigentibus subvenit magis. Sed nos indigemus magis gratia quam primus homo in statu innocentiae. Ergo copiosius infunditur nobis gratia. Quae cum sit radix meriti, opera nostra efficaciora ad merendum redduntur.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32508)\n\nPraeterea, ad meritum requiritur pugna quaedam et difficultas. Dicitur enim II ad Tim. II, *non coronabitur nisi qui legitime certaverit*. Et philosophus dicit, in II Ethic., quod *virtus est circa difficile et bonum*. Sed nunc est maior pugna et difficultas. Ergo et maior efficacia ad merendum.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32509)\n\nPraeterea, Magister dicit, XXIV distinctione II libri Sent., quod *homo non meruisset, tentationi resistendo; nunc autem meretur qui tentationi resistit*. Ergo efficaciora sunt opera nostra ad merendum quam in primo statu.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32510)\n\nSed contra est quia secundum hoc, homo esset melioris conditionis post peccatum.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32511)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quantitas meriti ex duobus potest pensari. Uno modo, ex radice caritatis et gratiae. Et talis quantitas meriti respondet praemio essentiali, quod consistit in Dei fruitione, qui enim ex maiori caritate aliquid facit, perfectius Deo fruetur. Alio modo pensari potest quantitas meriti ex quantitate operis. Quae quidem est duplex, scilicet absoluta, et proportionalis. Vidua enim quae misit duo aera minuta in gazophylacium; minus opus fecit quantitate absoluta, quam illi qui magna munera posuerunt, sed quantitate proportionali vidua plus fecit, secundum sententiam domini, quia magis eius facultatem superabat. Utraque tamen quantitas meriti respondet praemio accidentali; quod est gaudium de bono creato. Sic igitur dicendum quod efficaciora fuissent hominis opera ad merendum in statu innocentiae quam post peccatum, si attendatur quantitas meriti ex parte gratiae; quae tunc copiosior fuisset, nullo obstaculo in natura humana invento. Similiter etiam, si consideretur absoluta quantitas operis, quia, cum homo esset maioris virtutis, maiora opera fecisset. Sed si consideretur quantitas proportionalis, maior invenitur ratio meriti post peccatum, propter hominis imbecillitatem, magis enim excedit parvum opus potestatem eius qui cum difficultate operatur illud, quam opus magnum potestatem eius qui sine difficultate operatur.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32512)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod homo post peccatum ad plura indiget gratia quam ante peccatum, sed non magis. Quia homo, etiam ante peccatum, indigebat gratia ad vitam aeternam consequendam, quae est principalis necessitas gratiae. Sed homo post peccatum, super hoc, indiget gratia etiam ad peccati remissionem, et infirmitatis sustentationem.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32513)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod difficultas et pugna pertinent ad quantitatem meriti, secundum quantitatem operis proportionalem, ut dictum est. Et est signum promptitudinis voluntatis, quae conatur ad id quod est sibi difficile, promptitudo autem voluntatis causatur ex magnitudine caritatis. Et tamen potest contingere quod aliquis ita prompta voluntate faciat opus aliquod facile, sicut alius difficile, quia paratus esset facere etiam quod sibi esset difficile. Difficultas tamen actualis, inquantum est poenalis, habet etiam quod sit satisfactoria pro peccato.\n\n[I q. 95 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32514)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod resistere tentationi primo homini non fuisset meritorium, secundum opinionem ponentium quod gratiam non haberet; sicut nec modo est meritorium non habenti gratiam. Sed in hoc est differentia, quia in primo statu nihil erat interius impellens ad malum, sicut modo est, unde magis tunc poterat homo resistere tentationi sine gratia, quam modo.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0002","text":"[I q. 95 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32472)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de his quae pertinent ad voluntatem primi hominis. Et circa hoc consideranda sunt duo, primo quidem, de gratia et iustitia primi hominis; secundo, de usu iustitiae quantum ad dominium super alia. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum primus homo creatus fuerit in gratia. Secundo, utrum in statu innocentiae habuerit animae passiones. Tertio, utrum habuit virtutes omnes. Quarto, utrum opera eius fuissent aeque efficacia ad merendum, sicut modo sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0005","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32473)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod primus homo non fuerit creatus in gratia. Apostolus enim, I Cor. XV, distinguens Adam a Christo, dicit, *factus est primus Adam in animam viventem; novissimus autem in spiritum vivificantem*. Sed vivificatio spiritus est per gratiam. Ergo hoc est proprium Christi, quod fuerit factus in gratia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0007","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32474)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0008","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, in libro de quaestionibus veteris et novi testamenti, quod *Adam non habuit spiritum sanctum*. Sed quicumque habet gratiam, habet spiritum sanctum. Ergo Adam non fuit creatus in gratia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0009","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32475)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0010","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, in libro de correptione et gratia, quod *Deus sic ordinavit Angelorum et hominum vitam, ut prius in eis ostenderet quid posset eorum liberum arbitrium, deinde quid posset suae gratiae beneficium, iustitiaeque iudicium*. Primo ergo condidit hominem et Angelum in sola naturali arbitrii libertate, et postmodum eis gratiam contulit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0011","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32476)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0012","text":"Praeterea, Magister dicit, in XXIV distinctione libri II Sent., quod *homini in creatione datum est auxilium per quod stare poterat, sed non poterat proficere*. Quicumque autem habet gratiam, potest proficere per meritum. Ergo primus homo non fuit creatus in gratia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0013","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32477)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0014","text":"Praeterea, ad hoc quod aliquis accipiat gratiam, requiritur consensus ex parte recipientis, cum per hoc perficiatur matrimonium quoddam spirituale inter Deum et animam. Sed consensus in gratiam esse non potest nisi prius existentis. Ergo homo non accepit gratiam in primo instanti suae creationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0015","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32478)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0016","text":"Praeterea, natura plus distat a gratia quam gratia a gloria, quae nihil est aliud quam gratia consummata. Sed in homine gratia praecessit gloriam. Ergo multo magis natura praecessit gratiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0017","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32479)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0018","text":"Sed contra, homo et Angelus aequaliter ordinantur ad gratiam. Sed Angelus est creatus in gratia, dicit enim Augustinus, XII de Civ. Dei, quod *Deus simul erat in eis condens naturam et largiens gratiam*. Ergo et homo creatus fuit in gratia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0019","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32480)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0020","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam dicunt quod primus homo non fuit quidem creatus in gratia, sed tamen postmodum gratia fuit sibi collata antequam peccasset, plurimae enim sanctorum auctoritates attestantur hominem in statu innocentiae gratiam habuisse. Sed quod etiam fuerit conditus in gratia, ut alii dicunt, videtur requirere ipsa rectitudo primi status, in qua Deus hominem fecit, secundum illud Eccle. VII, *Deus fecit hominem rectum*. Erat enim haec rectitudo secundum hoc, quod ratio subdebatur Deo, rationi vero inferiores vires, et animae corpus. Prima autem subiectio erat causa et secundae et tertiae, quandiu enim ratio manebat Deo subiecta, inferiora ei subdebantur, ut Augustinus dicit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0021","text":"Manifestum est autem quod illa subiectio corporis ad animam, et inferiorum virium ad rationem, non erat naturalis, alioquin post peccatum mansisset, cum etiam in Daemonibus data naturalia post peccatum permanserint, ut Dionysius dicit cap. IV de Div. Nom. Unde manifestum est quod et illa prima subiectio, qua ratio Deo subdebatur, non erat solum secundum naturam, sed secundum supernaturale donum gratiae, non enim potest esse quod effectus sit potior quam causa. Unde Augustinus dicit, XIII de Civ. Dei, quod *posteaquam praecepti facta transgressio est, confestim, gratia deserente divina, de corporum suorum nuditate confusi sunt, senserunt enim motum inobedientis carnis suae, tanquam reciprocam poenam inobedientiae suae*. Ex quo datur intelligi, si deserente gratia soluta est obedientia carnis ad animam, quod per gratiam in anima existentem inferiora ei subdebantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0022","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32481)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0023","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod apostolus illa verba inducit ad ostendendum esse corpus spirituale, si est corpus animale, quia vita spiritualis corporis incoepit in Christo, qui est primogenitus ex mortuis, sicut vita corporis animalis incoepit in Adam. Non ergo ex verbis apostoli habetur quod Adam non fuit spiritualis secundum animam; sed quod non fuit spiritualis secundum corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0024","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32482)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0025","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit in eodem loco, non negatur quin aliquo modo fuerit in Adam spiritus sanctus, sicut et in aliis iustis, sed quod non sic fuerit in eo, sicut nunc est in fidelibus, qui admittuntur ad perceptionem haereditatis aeternae statim post mortem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0026","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32483)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0027","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ex illa auctoritate Augustini non habetur quod Angelus vel homo prius fuerit creatus in naturali libertate arbitrii, quam habuisset gratiam, sed quod prius ostendit quid in eis posset liberum arbitrium ante confirmationem, et quid postmodum consecuti sunt per auxilium gratiae confirmantis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0028","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32484)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0029","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod Magister loquitur secundum opinionem illorum qui posuerunt hominem non esse creatum in gratia, sed in naturalibus tantum. Vel potest dici quod, etsi homo fuerit creatus in gratia, non tamen habuit ex creatione naturae quod posset proficere per meritum, sed ex superadditione gratiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0030","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32485)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0031","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod, cum motus voluntatis non sit continuus, nihil prohibet etiam in primo instanti suae creationis primum hominem gratiae consensisse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0032","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32486)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0033","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod gloriam meremur per actum gratiae, non autem gratiam per actum naturae. Unde non est similis ratio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0034","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0035","text":"[I q. 95 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32487)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0036","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in primo homine non fuerint animae passiones. Secundum enim passiones animae contingit quod caro concupiscit adversus spiritum. Sed hoc non erat in statu innocentiae. Ergo in statu innocentiae non erant animae passiones.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0037","text":"[I q. 95 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32488)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0038","text":"Praeterea, anima Adae erat nobilior quam corpus. Sed corpus Adae fuit impassibile. Ergo nec in anima eius fuerunt passiones.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0039","text":"[I q. 95 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32489)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0040","text":"Praeterea, per virtutem moralem comprimuntur animae passiones. Sed in Adam fuit virtus moralis perfecta. Ergo totaliter passiones excludebantur ab eo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0041","text":"[I q. 95 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32490)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0042","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, XIV de Civ. Dei, quod erat in eis amor imperturbatus in Deum, et quaedam aliae animae passiones.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0043","text":"[I q. 95 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32491)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0044","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod passiones animae sunt in appetitu sensuali, cuius obiectum est bonum et malum. Unde omnium passionum animae quaedam ordinantur ad bonum, ut amor et gaudium; quaedam ad malum, ut timor et dolor. Et quia in primo statu nullum malum aderat nec imminebat; nec aliquod bonum aberat, quod cuperet bona voluntas pro tempore illo habendum, ut patet per Augustinum XIV de Civ. Dei, omnes illae passiones quae respiciunt malum, in Adam non erant ut timor et dolor et huiusmodi; similiter nec illae passiones quae respiciunt bonum non habitum et nunc habendum, ut cupiditas aestuans. Illae vero passiones quae possunt esse boni praesentis, ut gaudium et amor; vel quae sunt futuri boni ut suo tempore habendi, ut desiderium et spes non affligens; fuerunt in statu innocentiae. Aliter tamen quam in nobis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0045","text":"Nam in nobis appetitus sensualis, in quo sunt passiones, non totaliter subest rationi, unde passiones quandoque sunt in nobis praevenientes iudicium rationis, et impedientes; quandoque vero ex iudicio rationis consequentes, prout sensualis appetitus aliqualiter rationi obedit. In statu vero innocentiae inferior appetitus erat rationi totaliter subiectus, unde non erant in eo passiones animae, nisi ex rationis iudicio consequentes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0046","text":"[I q. 95 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32492)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0047","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod caro concupiscit adversus spiritum, per hoc quod passiones rationi repugnant, quod in statu innocentiae non erat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0048","text":"[I q. 95 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32493)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0049","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod corpus humanum in statu innocentiae erat impassibile quantum ad passiones quae removent dispositionem naturalem, ut infra dicetur. Et similiter anima fuit impassibilis quantum ad passiones quae impediunt rationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0050","text":"[I q. 95 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32494)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0051","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod perfecta virtus moralis non totaliter tollit passiones, sed ordinat eas, *temperati enim est concupiscere sicut oportet, et quae oportet*, ut dicitur in III Ethic.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0052","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0053","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32495)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0054","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Adam non habuerit omnes virtutes. Quaedam enim virtutes ordinantur ad refraenandam immoderantiam passionum; sicut per temperantiam refraenatur immoderata concupiscentia, et per fortitudinem immoderatus timor. Sed immoderantia passionum non erat in statu innocentiae. Ergo nec dictae virtutes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0055","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32496)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0056","text":"Praeterea, quaedam virtutes sunt circa passiones respicientes malum; ut mansuetudo circa iras, et fortitudo circa timores. Sed tales passiones non erant in statu innocentiae, ut dictum est. Ergo nec huiusmodi virtutes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0057","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32497)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0058","text":"Praeterea, poenitentia est quaedam virtus respiciens peccatum prius commissum. Misericordia etiam est quaedam virtus respiciens miseriam. Sed in statu innocentiae non erat peccatum nec miseria. Ergo neque huiusmodi virtutes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0059","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32498)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0060","text":"Praeterea, perseverantia est quaedam virtus. Sed hanc Adam non habuit; ut sequens peccatum ostendit. Ergo non habuit omnes virtutes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0061","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32499)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0062","text":"Praeterea, fides quaedam virtus est. Sed haec in statu innocentiae non fuit, importat enim aenigmaticam cognitionem, quae perfectioni primi status repugnare videtur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0063","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32500)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0064","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in quadam homilia, *princeps vitiorum devicit Adam de limo terrae ad imaginem Dei factum, pudicitia armatum, temperantia compositum, claritate splendidum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0065","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32501)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0066","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod homo in statu innocentiae aliqualiter habuit omnes virtutes. Et hoc ex dictis potest esse manifestum. Dictum est enim supra quod talis erat rectitudo primi status, quod ratio erat Deo subiecta, inferiores autem vires rationi. Virtutes autem nihil aliud sunt quam perfectiones quaedam, quibus ratio ordinatur in Deum, et inferiores vires disponuntur secundum regulam rationis; ut magis patebit cum de virtutibus agetur. Unde rectitudo primi status exigebat ut homo aliqualiter omnes virtutes haberet. Sed considerandum est quod virtutum quaedam sunt, quae de sui ratione nullam imperfectionem important, ut caritas et iustitia. Et huiusmodi virtutes fuerunt in statu innocentiae simpliciter, et quantum ad habitum et quantum ad actum. Quaedam vero sunt, quae de sui ratione imperfectionem important, vel ex parte actus vel ex parte materiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0067","text":"Et si huiusmodi imperfectio non repugnat perfectioni primi status, nihilominus huiusmodi virtutes poterant esse in primo statu; sicut fides, quae est eorum quae non videntur, et spes, quae est eorum quae non habentur. Perfectio enim primi status non se extendebat ad hoc, ut videret Deum per essentiam, et ut haberet eum cum fruitione finalis beatitudinis, unde fides et spes esse poterant in primo statu, et quantum ad habitum et quantum ad actum. Si vero imperfectio quae est de ratione virtutis alicuius, repugnat perfectioni primi status, poterat huiusmodi virtus ibi esse secundum habitum, sed non secundum actum, ut patet de poenitentia, quae est dolor de peccato commisso, et de misericordia, quae est dolor de miseria aliena; perfectioni enim primi status repugnat tam dolor, quam culpa et miseria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0068","text":"Unde huiusmodi virtutes erant in primo homine secundum habitum, sed non secundum actum, erat enim primus homo sic dispositus, ut si peccatum praecessisset, doleret; et similiter si miseriam in alio videret, eam pro posse repelleret. Sicut philosophus dicit, in IV Ethic., quod verecundia, quae est de turpi facto, *contingit studioso solum sub conditione, est enim sic dispositus, quod verecundaretur si turpe aliquid committeret*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0069","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32502)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0070","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod accidit temperantiae et fortitudini quod superabundantiam passionum repellat, inquantum invenit passiones superabundantes in subiecto. Sed per se convenit huiusmodi virtutibus passiones moderari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0071","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32503)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0072","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod illae passiones ad malum ordinatae, repugnant perfectioni primi status, quae habent respectum ad malum in ipso qui afficitur passione, ut timor et dolor. Sed passiones quae respiciunt malum in altero, non repugnant perfectioni primi status, poterat enim homo in primo statu habere odio malitiam Daemonum, sicut et diligere bonitatem Dei. Unde et virtutes quae circa tales passiones essent, possent esse in primo statu, et quantum ad habitum et quantum ad actum. Quae vero sunt circa passiones respicientes malum in eodem subiecto, si circa huiusmodi solas passiones sunt, non poterant esse in primo statu secundum actum, sed solum secundum habitum; sicut de poenitentia et misericordia dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0073","text":"Sed sunt quaedam virtutes quae non sunt circa has passiones solum, sed etiam circa alias; sicut temperantia, quae non solum est circa tristitias, sed etiam circa delectationes; et fortitudo, quae non solum est circa timores, sed etiam circa audaciam et spem. Unde poterat esse in primo statu actus temperantiae, secundum quod est moderativa delectationum; et similiter fortitudo, secundum quod est moderativa audaciae sive spei; non autem secundum quod moderantur tristitiam et timorem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0074","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32504)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0075","text":"Ad tertium patet solutio ex his quae dicta sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0076","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32505)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0077","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod perseverantia dupliciter sumitur. Uno modo, prout est quaedam virtus, et sic significat quendam habitum, quo quis eligit perseverare in bono. Et sic Adam perseverantiam habuit. Alio modo, prout est circumstantia virtutis, et sic significat continuationem quandam virtutis absque interruptione. Et hoc modo Adam perseverantiam non habuit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0078","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32506)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0079","text":"Ad quintum patet responsio per ea quae dicta sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0080","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0081","text":"[I q. 95 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32507)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0082","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod opera primi hominis fuerint minus efficacia ad merendum quam opera nostra. Gratia enim ex Dei misericordia datur, quae magis indigentibus subvenit magis. Sed nos indigemus magis gratia quam primus homo in statu innocentiae. Ergo copiosius infunditur nobis gratia. Quae cum sit radix meriti, opera nostra efficaciora ad merendum redduntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0083","text":"[I q. 95 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32508)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0084","text":"Praeterea, ad meritum requiritur pugna quaedam et difficultas. Dicitur enim II ad Tim. II, *non coronabitur nisi qui legitime certaverit*. Et philosophus dicit, in II Ethic., quod *virtus est circa difficile et bonum*. Sed nunc est maior pugna et difficultas. Ergo et maior efficacia ad merendum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0085","text":"[I q. 95 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32509)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0086","text":"Praeterea, Magister dicit, XXIV distinctione II libri Sent., quod *homo non meruisset, tentationi resistendo; nunc autem meretur qui tentationi resistit*. Ergo efficaciora sunt opera nostra ad merendum quam in primo statu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0087","text":"[I q. 95 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32510)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0088","text":"Sed contra est quia secundum hoc, homo esset melioris conditionis post peccatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0089","text":"[I q. 95 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32511)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0090","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quantitas meriti ex duobus potest pensari. Uno modo, ex radice caritatis et gratiae. Et talis quantitas meriti respondet praemio essentiali, quod consistit in Dei fruitione, qui enim ex maiori caritate aliquid facit, perfectius Deo fruetur. Alio modo pensari potest quantitas meriti ex quantitate operis. Quae quidem est duplex, scilicet absoluta, et proportionalis. Vidua enim quae misit duo aera minuta in gazophylacium; minus opus fecit quantitate absoluta, quam illi qui magna munera posuerunt, sed quantitate proportionali vidua plus fecit, secundum sententiam domini, quia magis eius facultatem superabat. Utraque tamen quantitas meriti respondet praemio accidentali; quod est gaudium de bono creato.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0091","text":"Sic igitur dicendum quod efficaciora fuissent hominis opera ad merendum in statu innocentiae quam post peccatum, si attendatur quantitas meriti ex parte gratiae; quae tunc copiosior fuisset, nullo obstaculo in natura humana invento. Similiter etiam, si consideretur absoluta quantitas operis, quia, cum homo esset maioris virtutis, maiora opera fecisset. Sed si consideretur quantitas proportionalis, maior invenitur ratio meriti post peccatum, propter hominis imbecillitatem, magis enim excedit parvum opus potestatem eius qui cum difficultate operatur illud, quam opus magnum potestatem eius qui sine difficultate operatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0092","text":"[I q. 95 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32512)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0093","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod homo post peccatum ad plura indiget gratia quam ante peccatum, sed non magis. Quia homo, etiam ante peccatum, indigebat gratia ad vitam aeternam consequendam, quae est principalis necessitas gratiae. Sed homo post peccatum, super hoc, indiget gratia etiam ad peccati remissionem, et infirmitatis sustentationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0094","text":"[I q. 95 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32513)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0095","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod difficultas et pugna pertinent ad quantitatem meriti, secundum quantitatem operis proportionalem, ut dictum est. Et est signum promptitudinis voluntatis, quae conatur ad id quod est sibi difficile, promptitudo autem voluntatis causatur ex magnitudine caritatis. Et tamen potest contingere quod aliquis ita prompta voluntate faciat opus aliquod facile, sicut alius difficile, quia paratus esset facere etiam quod sibi esset difficile. Difficultas tamen actualis, inquantum est poenalis, habet etiam quod sit satisfactoria pro peccato.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0096","text":"[I q. 95 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32514)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0097","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod resistere tentationi primo homini non fuisset meritorium, secundum opinionem ponentium quod gratiam non haberet; sicut nec modo est meritorium non habenti gratiam. Sed in hoc est differentia, quia in primo statu nihil erat interius impellens ad malum, sicut modo est, unde magis tunc poterat homo resistere tentationi sine gratia, quam modo.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"dfa99914073a8c2a822a6690de30081388a436f33ed2be73b57353502d73b4eb","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T152949487758Z_58105053","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 95","source_sha256":"dfa99914073a8c2a822a6690de30081388a436f33ed2be73b57353502d73b4eb","sha256":"632747fa76a142094c05c774063299dc6c39201a98f27ca75d56b1794ebe9ff6","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:29:49.487777+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0002","text":"[I q. 95 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32472)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider those matters that pertain to the will of the first man. And concerning this, two things are to be considered: first, the grace and justice of the first man; second, the use of justice with respect to dominion over other things. Concerning the first, four questions are asked. First, whether the first man was created in grace. Second, whether in the state of innocence he had passions of the soul. Third, whether he had all the virtues. Fourth, whether his works would have been equally efficacious for meriting as they are now.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0005","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32473)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the first man was not created in grace. For the Apostle, in I Cor. XV, distinguishing Adam from Christ, says, *the first Adam was made into a living soul; but the last into a life-giving spirit*. But the giving of life to the spirit is through grace. Therefore, it is proper to Christ that he was made in grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0007","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32474)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0008","text":"Further, Augustine says, in the book On Questions of the Old and New Testament, that *Adam did not have the Holy Spirit*. But whoever has grace has the Holy Spirit. Therefore Adam was not created in grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0009","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32475)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0010","text":"Further, Augustine says, in the book On Rebuke and Grace, that *God so ordered the life of angels and men that He might first show in them what their free choice could do, and then what the benefit of His grace and the judgment of justice could do*. Therefore He first created man and angel in natural freedom of choice alone, and afterwards bestowed grace upon them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0011","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32476)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0012","text":"Further, the Master says, in the twenty-fourth distinction of Book II of the Sentences, that *in creation man was given aid by which he could stand, but by which he could not make progress*. But whoever has grace can make progress through merit. Therefore the first man was not created in grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0013","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32477)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0014","text":"Further, in order that someone receive grace, consent is required on the part of the recipient, since by this a certain spiritual marriage between God and the soul is brought to completion. But consent to grace cannot exist except in one who already exists. Therefore man did not receive grace in the first instant of his creation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0015","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32478)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0016","text":"Further, nature is more distant from grace than grace is from glory, which is nothing other than consummated grace. But in man grace preceded glory. Therefore nature all the more preceded grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0017","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32479)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0018","text":"But on the contrary, man and angel are equally ordered to grace. But the angel was created in grace, for Augustine says, in XII of The City of God, that *God was at once in them, creating nature and bestowing grace*. Therefore man too was created in grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0019","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32480)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0020","text":"I answer that some say that the first man was indeed not created in grace, but that grace was nevertheless afterwards bestowed upon him before he sinned; for very many authorities of the saints attest that man had grace in the state of innocence. But that he was also made in grace, as others say, seems to be required by the very rectitude of the first state in which God made man, according to Eccles. VII, *God made man upright*. For this rectitude consisted in the fact that reason was subject to God, the lower powers to reason, and the body to the soul. Now the first subjection was the cause both of the second and of the third; for as long as reason remained subject to God, the lower things were subject to it, as Augustine says.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0021","text":"But it is evident that that subjection of the body to the soul, and of the lower powers to reason, was not natural; otherwise it would have remained after sin, since even the natural endowments given to demons remained after sin, as Dionysius says in ch. IV of The Divine Names. Hence it is evident that that first subjection, by which reason was subject to God, was not according to nature alone, but according to the supernatural gift of grace; for it cannot be that the effect is more powerful than the cause. Hence Augustine says, in XIII of The City of God, that *after the transgression of the commandment had been committed, immediately, divine grace forsaking them, they were confounded at the nakedness of their bodies; for they felt the motion of their disobedient flesh, as a reciprocal punishment for their disobedience*. From this it is given to understand that, if upon grace's departure the obedience of the flesh to the soul was dissolved, then by grace existing in the soul the lower things were subject to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0022","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32481)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0023","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the Apostle introduces those words to show that there is a spiritual body if there is an animal body, because the spiritual life of the body began in Christ, who is the firstborn from the dead, just as the life of the animal body began in Adam. Therefore it is not had from the Apostle's words that Adam was not spiritual according to the soul, but that he was not spiritual according to the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0024","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32482)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0025","text":"To the second, it must be said that, as Augustine says in the same place, it is not denied that the Holy Spirit was in Adam in some manner, as also in the other just persons, but that He was not in him as He is now in the faithful, who are admitted to the reception of the eternal inheritance immediately after death.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0026","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32483)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0027","text":"To the third, it must be said that from that authority of Augustine one does not obtain that an angel or a human being was first created in the natural freedom of choice before he had grace, but rather that he first showed what free choice could do in them before confirmation, and what afterward they attained through the help of confirming grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0028","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32484)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0029","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the Master speaks according to the opinion of those who held that man was not created in grace, but only in natural endowments. Or it can be said that, even if man was created in grace, nevertheless he did not have from the creation of his nature the ability to advance through merit, but from the superaddition of grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0030","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32485)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0031","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that, since the movement of the will is not continuous, nothing prevents the first man, even in the first instant of his creation, from having consented to grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0032","text":"[I q. 95 a. 1 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32486)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0033","text":"To the sixth, it must be said that we merit glory through an act of grace, but not grace through an act of nature. Hence the reasoning is not similar.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0034","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0035","text":"[I q. 95 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32487)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0036","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that there were no passions of the soul in the first man. For it is on account of the passions of the soul that the flesh lusts against the spirit. But this was not so in the state of innocence. Therefore, in the state of innocence there were no passions of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0037","text":"[I q. 95 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32488)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0038","text":"Further, Adam's soul was nobler than his body. But Adam's body was impassible. Therefore there were no passions in his soul either.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0039","text":"[I q. 95 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32489)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0040","text":"Further, the passions of the soul are restrained by moral virtue. But in Adam there was perfect moral virtue. Therefore passions were wholly excluded from him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0041","text":"[I q. 95 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32490)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0042","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in Book XIV of The City of God, that there was in them undisturbed love toward God, and certain other passions of the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0043","text":"[I q. 95 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32491)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0044","text":"I answer that the passions of the soul are in the sensitive appetite, the object of which is good and evil. Hence, among all the passions of the soul, some are ordered toward good, such as love and joy; some toward evil, such as fear and pain. And because in the first state no evil was present or threatened; nor was any good absent which a good will desired to have at that time, as is clear from Augustine, Book XIV of The City of God, all those passions that regard evil were not in Adam, such as fear and pain and the like; likewise, neither were those passions that regard a good not possessed and now to be possessed, such as ardent desire. But those passions which can concern a present good, such as joy and love; or which concern a future good to be possessed in its own time, such as desire and non-afflicting hope, were in the state of innocence. Yet in a different way than in us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0045","text":"For in us the sensitive appetite, in which the passions are, is not wholly subject to reason; hence the passions are sometimes in us antecedent to the judgment of reason, and impede it; but sometimes they follow upon the judgment of reason, insofar as the sensitive appetite obeys reason in some measure. But in the state of innocence the lower appetite was wholly subject to reason; hence there were no passions of the soul in it except those following upon the judgment of reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0046","text":"[I q. 95 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32492)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0047","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the flesh lusts against the spirit insofar as the passions resist reason, which was not the case in the state of innocence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0048","text":"[I q. 95 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32493)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0049","text":"To the second, it must be said that the human body in the state of innocence was impassible with respect to passions that remove its natural disposition, as will be said below. And likewise the soul was impassible with respect to passions that impede reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0050","text":"[I q. 95 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32494)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0051","text":"To the third, it must be said that perfect moral virtue does not wholly remove the passions, but orders them; for “it belongs to the temperate man to desire as he ought, and what he ought,” as is said in Ethics III.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0052","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0053","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32495)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0054","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that Adam did not possess all the virtues. For certain virtues are directed to restraining the immoderacy of passions; thus immoderate concupiscence is restrained by temperance, and immoderate fear by fortitude. But there was no immoderacy of passions in the state of innocence. Therefore neither were there the aforesaid virtues.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0055","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32496)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0056","text":"Further, certain virtues concern passions having regard to evil; as meekness concerns angers, and fortitude fears. But such passions were not in the state of innocence, as has been said. Therefore neither were such virtues.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0057","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32497)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0058","text":"Further, repentance is a certain virtue concerning a sin previously committed. Mercy likewise is a certain virtue concerning misery. But in the state of innocence there was neither sin nor misery. Therefore neither were there virtues of this kind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0059","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32498)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0060","text":"Further, perseverance is a certain virtue. But Adam did not possess this, as the subsequent sin shows. Therefore he did not possess all the virtues.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0061","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32499)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0062","text":"Further, faith is a certain virtue. But this was not present in the state of innocence, for it entails an enigmatic cognition, which seems incompatible with the perfection of the first state.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0063","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32500)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0064","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says in a certain homily: “The prince of vices overcame Adam, made from the slime of the earth in the image of God, armed with chastity, composed by temperance, resplendent with brightness.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0065","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32501)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0066","text":"I answer that man in the state of innocence possessed all the virtues in a certain respect. And this can be made clear from what has been said. For it was said above that such was the rectitude of the first state that reason was subject to God, while the lower powers were subject to reason. But virtues are nothing other than certain perfections by which reason is ordered to God and the lower powers are disposed according to the rule of reason, as will be clearer when we treat of the virtues. Hence the rectitude of the first state required that man should possess all the virtues in a certain respect. But it must be considered that certain virtues are such as, by their very nature, imply no imperfection, as charity and justice. And virtues of this kind were in the state of innocence simply, both as to habit and as to act. But there are others which, by their very nature, imply imperfection, either on the part of the act or on the part of the matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0067","text":"And if an imperfection of this kind is not incompatible with the perfection of the first state, nonetheless such virtues could be in the first state; as faith, which is of things not seen, and hope, which is of things not possessed. For the perfection of the first state did not extend to this: that he should see God through his essence, and that he should possess him with the enjoyment of final beatitude. Hence faith and hope could be in the first state, both as to habit and as to act. But if the imperfection that belongs to the nature of some virtue is incompatible with the perfection of the first state, such a virtue could be there according to habit, but not according to act, as is clear concerning repentance, which is sorrow over sin committed, and mercy, which is sorrow over another's misery; for both sorrow and fault and misery are incompatible with the perfection of the first state.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0068","text":"Hence virtues of this kind were in the first man according to habit, but not according to act; for the first man was so disposed that, if sin had preceded, he would have grieved; and likewise, if he saw misery in another, he would repel it to the extent of his power. As the Philosopher says in Ethics IV, that shame, which concerns a shameful deed, “befalls the virtuous man only conditionally; for he is so disposed that he would be ashamed if he committed something shameful.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0069","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32502)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0070","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that it is accidental to temperance and fortitude to repel an excess of passions, insofar as they find passions excessive in the subject. But it belongs to such virtues per se to moderate the passions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0071","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32503)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0072","text":"To the second, it must be said that those passions ordered toward evil are incompatible with the perfection of the first state which have regard to evil in the one affected by the passion, such as fear and sorrow. But passions that have regard to evil in another are not incompatible with the perfection of the first state; for man in the first state could hate the malice of demons, just as he could love the goodness of God. Hence also the virtues that would concern such passions could be in the first state, both as to habit and as to act. But those which concern passions having regard to evil in the same subject, if they concern only passions of this kind, could not be in the first state according to act, but only according to habit; as has been said concerning repentance and mercy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0073","text":"But there are certain virtues that concern not these passions alone, but others also; as temperance, which concerns not only sorrows but also pleasures; and fortitude, which concerns not only fears but also daring and hope. Hence, in the first state there could have been acts of temperance, insofar as it moderates pleasures; and likewise of fortitude, insofar as it moderates daring or hope; but not insofar as they moderate sorrow and fear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0074","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32504)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0075","text":"The solution to the third is clear from what has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0076","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32505)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0077","text":"To the fourth it must be said that perseverance is taken in two ways. In one way, insofar as it is a certain virtue, and thus it signifies a certain habit by which someone chooses to persevere in good. And in this way Adam had perseverance. In another way, insofar as it is a circumstance of virtue, and thus it signifies a certain continuation of virtue without interruption. And in this way Adam did not have perseverance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0078","text":"[I q. 95 a. 3 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32506)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0079","text":"The response to the fifth is clear through the things that have been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0080","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0081","text":"[I q. 95 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32507)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0082","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that the works of the first man were less efficacious for meriting than our works. For grace is given from God's mercy, which comes to the aid of those more in need more abundantly. But we are more in need of grace than the first man in the state of innocence. Therefore grace is infused into us more abundantly. And since it is the root of merit, our works are rendered more efficacious for meriting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0083","text":"[I q. 95 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32508)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0084","text":"Moreover, a certain struggle and difficulty are required for merit. For it is said in 2 Timothy 2, *he shall not be crowned unless he has competed lawfully*. And the Philosopher says, in Ethics II, that *virtue is concerned with the difficult and the good*. But now there is greater struggle and difficulty. Therefore there is also greater efficacy for meriting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0085","text":"[I q. 95 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32509)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0086","text":"Moreover, the Master says, in distinction 24 of Book II of the Sentences, that *man would not have merited by resisting temptation; but now he who resists temptation merits*. Therefore our works are more efficacious for meriting than in the first state.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0087","text":"[I q. 95 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32510)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0088","text":"But to the contrary, according to this, man would be in a better condition after sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0089","text":"[I q. 95 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32511)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0090","text":"I answer that the quantity of merit can be weighed from two things. In one way, from the root of charity and grace. And such a quantity of merit corresponds to the essential reward, which consists in the enjoyment of God; for he who does something from greater charity will enjoy God more perfectly. In another way, the quantity of merit can be weighed from the quantity of the work. This is twofold, namely, absolute and proportional. For the widow who cast two small coins into the treasury did a lesser work in absolute quantity than those who placed great gifts there; but in proportional quantity the widow did more, according to the Lord's judgment, because it exceeded her means more. Yet each quantity of merit corresponds to an accidental reward, which is joy concerning a created good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0091","text":"Thus, therefore, it must be said that man's works would have been more efficacious for meriting in the state of innocence than after sin, if the quantity of merit is considered on the part of grace, which then would have been more abundant, no obstacle having been found in human nature. Likewise also, if the absolute quantity of the work is considered, because, since man was of greater strength, he would have done greater works. But if the proportional quantity is considered, a greater ground of merit is found after sin, on account of man's weakness; for a small work exceeds more the power of one who performs it with difficulty than a great work exceeds the power of one who performs it without difficulty.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0092","text":"[I q. 95 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32512)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0093","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that after sin man needs grace for more things than before sin, but not more greatly. For man, even before sin, needed grace in order to attain eternal life, which is the principal necessity for grace. But after sin man, in addition to this, needs grace also for the remission of sin and the support of weakness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0094","text":"[I q. 95 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32513)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0095","text":"To the second it must be said that difficulty and struggle pertain to the quantity of merit according to the proportional quantity of the work, as has been said. And it is a sign of the readiness of the will, which strives toward that which is difficult for it; moreover, readiness of will is caused by the greatness of charity. Yet it can happen that someone with so ready a will does some easy work as another does a difficult one, because he would be prepared to do even what was difficult for him. Actual difficulty, however, insofar as it is penal, also has the character of being satisfactory for sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0096","text":"[I q. 95 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32514)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0097","text":"To the third it must be said that for the first man to resist temptation would not have been meritorious, according to the opinion of those who hold that he did not have grace; just as neither now is it meritorious for one who does not have grace. But there is this difference: because in the first state there was nothing inwardly impelling toward evil, as there is now; hence then man could resist temptation without grace more than he can now.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_87:P0097"]}],"source_digest":"3a03cdc41cf7f260e9dd14375724696d6402a51941afd77105663432bddf49e6","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 95"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 95","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88","title":"First Part · Question 96","order":97,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_88","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 96 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32515)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de dominio quod competebat homini in statu innocentiae. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum homo in statu innocentiae animalibus dominaretur. Secundo, utrum dominaretur omni creaturae. Tertio, utrum in statu innocentiae omnes homines fuissent aequales. Quarto, utrum in illo statu homo hominibus dominaretur.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 96 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32516)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Adam in statu innocentiae animalibus non dominabatur. Dicit enim Augustinus, IX super Gen. ad Litt., quod ministerio Angelorum animalia sunt adducta ad Adam, ut eis nomina imponeret. Non autem fuisset ibi necessarium Angelorum ministerium, si homo per seipsum animalibus dominabatur. Non ergo in statu innocentiae habuit dominium homo super alia animalia.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32517)\n\nPraeterea, ea quae ad invicem discordant, non recte sub uno dominio congregantur. Sed multa animalia naturaliter ad invicem discordant, sicut ovis et lupus. Ergo omnia animalia sub hominis dominio non continebantur.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32518)\n\nPraeterea, Hieronymus dicit quod *homini ante peccatum non indigenti, Deus animalium dominationem dedit, praesciebat enim hominem adminiculo animalium adiuvandum fore post lapsum*. Ergo ad minus usus dominii super animalia non competebat homini ante peccatum.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32519)\n\nPraeterea, proprium domini esse videtur praecipere. Sed praeceptum non recte fertur nisi ad habentem rationem. Ergo homo non habebat dominium super animalia irrationalia.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32520)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, de homine, *praesit piscibus maris, et volatilibus caeli, et bestiis terrae*.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32521)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, inobedientia ad hominem eorum quae ei debent esse subiecta, subsecuta est in poenam eius, eo quod ipse fuit inobediens Deo. Et ideo in statu innocentiae, ante inobedientiam praedictam, nihil ei repugnabat quod naturaliter deberet ei esse subiectum. Omnia autem animalia sunt homini naturaliter subiecta. Quod apparet ex tribus. Primo quidem, ex ipso naturae processu. Sicut enim in generatione rerum intelligitur quidam ordo quo proceditur de imperfecto ad perfectum (nam materia est propter formam, et forma imperfectior propter perfectiorem), ita etiam est in usu rerum naturalium, nam imperfectiora cedunt in usum perfectorum; plantae enim utuntur terra ad sui nutrimentum, animalia vero plantis, et homines plantis et animalibus. Unde naturaliter homo dominatur animalibus. Et propter hoc philosophus dicit, in I Politic., quod venatio sylvestrium animalium est iusta et naturalis, quia per eam homo vindicat sibi quod est naturaliter suum. Secundo apparet hoc ex ordine divinae providentiae, quae semper inferiora per superiora gubernat. Unde, cum homo sit supra cetera animalia, utpote ad imaginem Dei factus, convenienter eius gubernationi alia animalia subduntur. Tertio apparet idem ex proprietate hominis, et aliorum animalium. In aliis enim animalibus invenitur, secundum aestimationem naturalem, quaedam participatio prudentiae ad aliquos particulares actus, in homine autem invenitur universalis prudentia, quae est ratio omnium agibilium. Omne autem quod est per participationem, subditur ei quod est per essentiam et universaliter. Unde patet quod naturalis est subiectio aliorum animalium ad hominem.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32522)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in subiectos multa potest facere superior potestas, quae non potest facere inferior. Angelus autem est naturaliter superior homine. Unde aliquis effectus poterat fieri circa animalia virtute angelica, qui non poterat fieri potestate humana; scilicet quod statim omnia animalia congregarentur.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32523)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod quidam dicunt quod animalia quae nunc sunt ferocia et occidunt alia animalia, in statu illo fuissent mansueta non solum circa hominem, sed etiam circa alia animalia. Sed hoc est omnino irrationabile. Non enim per peccatum hominis natura animalium est mutata, ut quibus nunc naturale est comedere aliorum animalium carnes, tunc vixissent de herbis, sicut leones et falcones. Nec Glossa Bedae dicit, Gen. I, quod ligna et herbae datae sunt omnibus animalibus et avibus in cibum, sed quibusdam. Fuisset ergo naturalis discordia inter quaedam animalia. Nec tamen propter hoc subtraherentur dominio hominis; sicut nec nunc propter hoc subtrahuntur dominio Dei, cuius providentia hoc totum dispensatur. Et huius providentiae homo executor fuisset, ut etiam nunc apparet in animalibus domesticis, ministrantur enim falconibus domesticis per homines gallinae in cibum.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32524)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod homines in statu innocentiae non indigebant animalibus ad necessitatem corporalem, neque ad tegumentum, quia nudi erant, et non erubescebant, nullo instante inordinatae concupiscentiae motu; neque ad cibum, quia lignis Paradisi vescebantur; neque ad vehiculum, propter corporis robur. Indigebant tamen eis ad experimentalem cognitionem sumendam de naturis eorum. Quod significatum est per hoc, quod Deus ad eum animalia adduxit, ut eis nomina imponeret, quae eorum naturas designant.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32525)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod alia animalia habent quandam participationem prudentiae et rationis secundum aestimationem naturalem; ex qua contingit quod grues sequuntur ducem, et apes obediunt regi. Et sic tunc omnia animalia per seipsa homini obedivissent, sicut nunc quaedam domestica ei obediunt.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 96 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32526)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo non habuisset dominium super omnes alias creaturas. Angelus enim naturaliter est maioris potestatis quam homo. Sed, sicut dicit Augustinus III de Trin., *materia corporalis non obedivisset ad nutum etiam sanctis Angelis*. Ergo multo minus homini in statu innocentiae.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32527)\n\nPraeterea, in plantis non sunt de viribus animae nisi nutritiva et augmentativa et generativa. Hae autem non sunt natae obedire rationi; ut in uno et eodem homine apparet. Ergo, cum dominium competat homini secundum rationem, videtur quod plantis homo in statu innocentiae non dominaretur.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32528)\n\nPraeterea, quicumque dominatur alicui rei, potest illam rem mutare. Sed homo non potuisset mutare cursum caelestium corporum, hoc enim solius Dei est, ut Dionysius dicit in epistola ad Polycarpum. Ergo non dominabatur eis.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32529)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, de homine, *praesit universae creaturae*.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32530)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in homine quodammodo sunt omnia, et ideo secundum modum quo dominatur his quae in seipso sunt, secundum hunc modum competit ei dominari aliis. Est autem in homine quatuor considerare, scilicet rationem, secundum quam convenit cum Angelis; vires sensitivas, secundum quas convenit cum animalibus; vires naturales, secundum quas convenit cum plantis; et ipsum corpus, secundum quod convenit cum rebus inanimatis. Ratio autem in homine habet locum dominantis, et non subiecti dominio. Unde homo Angelis non dominabatur in primo statu, et quod dicitur omni creaturae, intelligitur quae non est ad imaginem Dei. Viribus autem sensitivis, sicut irascibili et concupiscibili, quae aliqualiter obediunt rationi, dominatur anima imperando. Unde et in statu innocentiae animalibus aliis per imperium dominabatur. Viribus autem naturalibus, et ipsi corpori, homo dominatur non quidem imperando, sed utendo. Et sic etiam homo in statu innocentiae dominabatur plantis et rebus inanimatis, non per imperium vel immutationem, sed absque impedimento utendo eorum auxilio.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 2 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32531)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ad obiecta.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 96 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32532)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod homines in statu innocentiae omnes fuissent aequales. Dicit enim Gregorius quod, *ubi non delinquimus, omnes pares sumus*. Sed in statu innocentiae non erat delictum. Ergo omnes erant pares.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32533)\n\nPraeterea, similitudo et aequalitas est ratio mutuae dilectionis; secundum illud Eccli. XIII, *omne animal diligit sibi simile, sic et omnis homo proximum sibi*. In illo autem statu inter homines abundabat dilectio, quae est vinculum pacis. Ergo omnes fuissent pares in statu innocentiae.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32534)\n\nPraeterea, cessante causa, cessat effectus. Sed causa inaequalitatis inter homines videtur nunc esse, ex parte quidem Dei, quod quosdam pro meritis praemiat, quosdam vero punit, ex parte vero naturae, quia propter naturae defectum quidam nascuntur debiles et orbati, quidam autem fortes et perfecti. Quae in primo statu non fuissent.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32535)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Rom. XIII, *quae a Deo sunt, ordinata sunt*. Ordo autem maxime videtur in disparitate consistere, dicit enim Augustinus, XIX de Civ. Dei, *ordo est parium dispariumque rerum sua cuique loca tribuens dispositio*. Ergo in primo statu, qui decentissimus fuisset, disparitas inveniretur.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32536)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere aliquam disparitatem in primo statu fuisse, ad minus quantum ad sexum, quia sine diversitate sexus, generatio non fuisset. Similiter etiam quantum ad aetatem, sic enim quidam ex aliis nascebantur; nec illi qui miscebantur, steriles erant. Sed et secundum animam diversitas fuisset, et quantum ad iustitiam et quantum ad scientiam. Non enim ex necessitate homo operabatur, sed per liberum arbitrium; ex quo homo habet quod possit magis et minus animum applicare ad aliquid faciendum vel volendum vel cognoscendum. Unde quidam magis profecissent in iustitia et scientia quam alii. Ex parte etiam corporis, poterat esse disparitas. Non enim erat exemptum corpus humanum totaliter a legibus naturae, quin ex exterioribus agentibus aliquod commodum aut auxilium reciperet magis et minus, cum etiam et cibis eorum vita sustentaretur. Et sic nihil prohibet dicere quin secundum diversam dispositionem aeris et diversum situm stellarum, aliqui robustiores corpore generarentur quam alii, et maiores et pulchriores et melius complexionati. Ita tamen quod in illis qui excederentur, nullus esset defectus sive peccatum, sive circa animam sive circa corpus.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32537)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Gregorius per verba illa intendit excludere disparitatem quae est secundum differentiam iustitiae et peccati; ex qua contingit quod aliqui poenaliter sunt sub aliis coercendi.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32538)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod aequalitas est causa quod dilectio mutua sit aequalis. Sed tamen inter inaequales potest esse maior dilectio quam inter aequales, licet non aequaliter utrinque respondeat. Pater enim plus diligit filium naturaliter, quam frater fratrem; licet filius non tantundem diligat patrem, sicut ab eo diligitur.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32539)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod causa disparitatis poterat esse et ex parte Dei, non quidem ut puniret quosdam et quosdam praemiaret; sed ut quosdam plus, quosdam minus sublimaret, ut pulchritudo ordinis magis in hominibus reluceret. Et etiam ex parte naturae poterat disparitas causari secundum praedictum modum, absque aliquo defectu naturae.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 96 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32540)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo in statu innocentiae homini non dominabatur. Dicit enim Augustinus, XIX de Civ. Dei, *hominem rationalem, ad imaginem suam factum, non voluit Deus nisi irrationabilibus dominari; non hominem homini, sed hominem pecori*.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32541)\n\nPraeterea, illud quod est introductum in poenam peccati, non fuisset in statu innocentiae. Sed hominem subesse homini, introductum est in poenam peccati, dictum est enim mulieri post peccatum, sub potestate viri eris, ut dicitur Gen. III. Ergo in statu innocentiae non erat homo homini subiectus.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32542)\n\nPraeterea, subiectio libertati opponitur. Sed libertas est unum de praecipuis bonis, quod in statu innocentiae non defuisset, quando nihil aberat quod bona voluntas cupere posset, ut Augustinus dicit XIV de Civ. Dei. Ergo homo homini in statu innocentiae non dominabatur.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32543)\n\nSed contra, conditio hominum in statu innocentiae non erat dignior quam conditio Angelorum. Sed inter Angelos quidam aliis dominantur, unde et unus ordo dominationum vocatur. Ergo non est contra dignitatem status innocentiae, quod homo homini dominaretur.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32544)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod dominium accipitur dupliciter. Uno modo, secundum quod opponitur servituti, et sic dominus dicitur cui aliquis subditur ut servus. Alio modo accipitur dominium, secundum quod communiter refertur ad subiectum qualitercumque, et sic etiam ille qui habet officium gubernandi et dirigendi liberos, dominus dici potest. Primo ergo modo accepto dominio, in statu innocentiae homo homini non dominaretur, sed secundo modo accepto dominio, in statu innocentiae homo homini dominari potuisset. Cuius ratio est, quia servus in hoc differt a libero, quod liber est causa sui, ut dicitur in principio Metaphys.; servus autem ordinatur ad alium. Tunc ergo aliquis dominatur alicui ut servo, quando eum cui dominatur ad propriam utilitatem sui, scilicet dominantis, refert. Et quia unicuique est appetibile proprium bonum, et per consequens contristabile est unicuique quod illud bonum quod deberet esse suum, cedat alteri tantum; ideo tale dominium non potest esse sine poena subiectorum. Propter quod, in statu innocentiae non fuisset tale dominium hominis ad hominem. Tunc vero dominatur aliquis alteri ut libero, quando dirigit ipsum ad proprium bonum eius qui dirigitur, vel ad bonum commune. Et tale dominium hominis, ad hominem in statu innocentiae fuisset, propter duo. Primo quidem, quia homo naturaliter est animal sociale, unde homines in statu innocentiae socialiter vixissent. Socialis autem vita multorum esse non posset, nisi aliquis praesideret, qui ad bonum commune intenderet, multi enim per se intendunt ad multa, unus vero ad unum. Et ideo philosophus dicit, in principio Politic., quod quandocumque multa ordinantur ad unum, semper invenitur unum ut principale et dirigens. Secundo quia, si unus homo habuisset super alium supereminentiam scientiae et iustitiae, inconveniens fuisset nisi hoc exequeretur in utilitatem aliorum; secundum quod dicitur I Petr. IV, *unusquisque gratiam quam accepit, in alterutrum illam administrantes*. Unde Augustinus dicit, XIX de Civ. Dei, quod *iusti non dominandi cupiditate imperant, sed officio consulendi, hoc naturalis ordo praescribit, ita Deus hominem condidit*.\n\n[I q. 96 a. 4 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32545)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ad omnia obiecta, quae procedunt de primo modo dominii.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0002","text":"[I q. 96 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32515)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de dominio quod competebat homini in statu innocentiae. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum homo in statu innocentiae animalibus dominaretur. Secundo, utrum dominaretur omni creaturae. Tertio, utrum in statu innocentiae omnes homines fuissent aequales. Quarto, utrum in illo statu homo hominibus dominaretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0005","text":"[I q. 96 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32516)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Adam in statu innocentiae animalibus non dominabatur. Dicit enim Augustinus, IX super Gen. ad Litt., quod ministerio Angelorum animalia sunt adducta ad Adam, ut eis nomina imponeret. Non autem fuisset ibi necessarium Angelorum ministerium, si homo per seipsum animalibus dominabatur. Non ergo in statu innocentiae habuit dominium homo super alia animalia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0007","text":"[I q. 96 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32517)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0008","text":"Praeterea, ea quae ad invicem discordant, non recte sub uno dominio congregantur. Sed multa animalia naturaliter ad invicem discordant, sicut ovis et lupus. Ergo omnia animalia sub hominis dominio non continebantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0009","text":"[I q. 96 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32518)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0010","text":"Praeterea, Hieronymus dicit quod *homini ante peccatum non indigenti, Deus animalium dominationem dedit, praesciebat enim hominem adminiculo animalium adiuvandum fore post lapsum*. Ergo ad minus usus dominii super animalia non competebat homini ante peccatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0011","text":"[I q. 96 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32519)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0012","text":"Praeterea, proprium domini esse videtur praecipere. Sed praeceptum non recte fertur nisi ad habentem rationem. Ergo homo non habebat dominium super animalia irrationalia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0013","text":"[I q. 96 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32520)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0014","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, de homine, *praesit piscibus maris, et volatilibus caeli, et bestiis terrae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0015","text":"[I q. 96 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32521)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, inobedientia ad hominem eorum quae ei debent esse subiecta, subsecuta est in poenam eius, eo quod ipse fuit inobediens Deo. Et ideo in statu innocentiae, ante inobedientiam praedictam, nihil ei repugnabat quod naturaliter deberet ei esse subiectum. Omnia autem animalia sunt homini naturaliter subiecta. Quod apparet ex tribus. Primo quidem, ex ipso naturae processu. Sicut enim in generatione rerum intelligitur quidam ordo quo proceditur de imperfecto ad perfectum (nam materia est propter formam, et forma imperfectior propter perfectiorem), ita etiam est in usu rerum naturalium, nam imperfectiora cedunt in usum perfectorum; plantae enim utuntur terra ad sui nutrimentum, animalia vero plantis, et homines plantis et animalibus. Unde naturaliter homo dominatur animalibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0017","text":"Et propter hoc philosophus dicit, in I Politic., quod venatio sylvestrium animalium est iusta et naturalis, quia per eam homo vindicat sibi quod est naturaliter suum. Secundo apparet hoc ex ordine divinae providentiae, quae semper inferiora per superiora gubernat. Unde, cum homo sit supra cetera animalia, utpote ad imaginem Dei factus, convenienter eius gubernationi alia animalia subduntur. Tertio apparet idem ex proprietate hominis, et aliorum animalium. In aliis enim animalibus invenitur, secundum aestimationem naturalem, quaedam participatio prudentiae ad aliquos particulares actus, in homine autem invenitur universalis prudentia, quae est ratio omnium agibilium. Omne autem quod est per participationem, subditur ei quod est per essentiam et universaliter. Unde patet quod naturalis est subiectio aliorum animalium ad hominem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0018","text":"[I q. 96 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32522)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0019","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in subiectos multa potest facere superior potestas, quae non potest facere inferior. Angelus autem est naturaliter superior homine. Unde aliquis effectus poterat fieri circa animalia virtute angelica, qui non poterat fieri potestate humana; scilicet quod statim omnia animalia congregarentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0020","text":"[I q. 96 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32523)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0021","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod quidam dicunt quod animalia quae nunc sunt ferocia et occidunt alia animalia, in statu illo fuissent mansueta non solum circa hominem, sed etiam circa alia animalia. Sed hoc est omnino irrationabile. Non enim per peccatum hominis natura animalium est mutata, ut quibus nunc naturale est comedere aliorum animalium carnes, tunc vixissent de herbis, sicut leones et falcones. Nec Glossa Bedae dicit, Gen. I, quod ligna et herbae datae sunt omnibus animalibus et avibus in cibum, sed quibusdam. Fuisset ergo naturalis discordia inter quaedam animalia. Nec tamen propter hoc subtraherentur dominio hominis; sicut nec nunc propter hoc subtrahuntur dominio Dei, cuius providentia hoc totum dispensatur. Et huius providentiae homo executor fuisset, ut etiam nunc apparet in animalibus domesticis, ministrantur enim falconibus domesticis per homines gallinae in cibum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0022","text":"[I q. 96 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32524)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0023","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod homines in statu innocentiae non indigebant animalibus ad necessitatem corporalem, neque ad tegumentum, quia nudi erant, et non erubescebant, nullo instante inordinatae concupiscentiae motu; neque ad cibum, quia lignis Paradisi vescebantur; neque ad vehiculum, propter corporis robur. Indigebant tamen eis ad experimentalem cognitionem sumendam de naturis eorum. Quod significatum est per hoc, quod Deus ad eum animalia adduxit, ut eis nomina imponeret, quae eorum naturas designant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0024","text":"[I q. 96 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32525)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0025","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod alia animalia habent quandam participationem prudentiae et rationis secundum aestimationem naturalem; ex qua contingit quod grues sequuntur ducem, et apes obediunt regi. Et sic tunc omnia animalia per seipsa homini obedivissent, sicut nunc quaedam domestica ei obediunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0026","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0027","text":"[I q. 96 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32526)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0028","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo non habuisset dominium super omnes alias creaturas. Angelus enim naturaliter est maioris potestatis quam homo. Sed, sicut dicit Augustinus III de Trin., *materia corporalis non obedivisset ad nutum etiam sanctis Angelis*. Ergo multo minus homini in statu innocentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0029","text":"[I q. 96 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32527)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0030","text":"Praeterea, in plantis non sunt de viribus animae nisi nutritiva et augmentativa et generativa. Hae autem non sunt natae obedire rationi; ut in uno et eodem homine apparet. Ergo, cum dominium competat homini secundum rationem, videtur quod plantis homo in statu innocentiae non dominaretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0031","text":"[I q. 96 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32528)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0032","text":"Praeterea, quicumque dominatur alicui rei, potest illam rem mutare. Sed homo non potuisset mutare cursum caelestium corporum, hoc enim solius Dei est, ut Dionysius dicit in epistola ad Polycarpum. Ergo non dominabatur eis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0033","text":"[I q. 96 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32529)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0034","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, de homine, *praesit universae creaturae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0035","text":"[I q. 96 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32530)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0036","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in homine quodammodo sunt omnia, et ideo secundum modum quo dominatur his quae in seipso sunt, secundum hunc modum competit ei dominari aliis. Est autem in homine quatuor considerare, scilicet rationem, secundum quam convenit cum Angelis; vires sensitivas, secundum quas convenit cum animalibus; vires naturales, secundum quas convenit cum plantis; et ipsum corpus, secundum quod convenit cum rebus inanimatis. Ratio autem in homine habet locum dominantis, et non subiecti dominio. Unde homo Angelis non dominabatur in primo statu, et quod dicitur omni creaturae, intelligitur quae non est ad imaginem Dei. Viribus autem sensitivis, sicut irascibili et concupiscibili, quae aliqualiter obediunt rationi, dominatur anima imperando. Unde et in statu innocentiae animalibus aliis per imperium dominabatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0037","text":"Viribus autem naturalibus, et ipsi corpori, homo dominatur non quidem imperando, sed utendo. Et sic etiam homo in statu innocentiae dominabatur plantis et rebus inanimatis, non per imperium vel immutationem, sed absque impedimento utendo eorum auxilio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0038","text":"[I q. 96 a. 2 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32531)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0039","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ad obiecta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0040","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0041","text":"[I q. 96 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32532)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0042","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod homines in statu innocentiae omnes fuissent aequales. Dicit enim Gregorius quod, *ubi non delinquimus, omnes pares sumus*. Sed in statu innocentiae non erat delictum. Ergo omnes erant pares.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0043","text":"[I q. 96 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32533)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0044","text":"Praeterea, similitudo et aequalitas est ratio mutuae dilectionis; secundum illud Eccli. XIII, *omne animal diligit sibi simile, sic et omnis homo proximum sibi*. In illo autem statu inter homines abundabat dilectio, quae est vinculum pacis. Ergo omnes fuissent pares in statu innocentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0045","text":"[I q. 96 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32534)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0046","text":"Praeterea, cessante causa, cessat effectus. Sed causa inaequalitatis inter homines videtur nunc esse, ex parte quidem Dei, quod quosdam pro meritis praemiat, quosdam vero punit, ex parte vero naturae, quia propter naturae defectum quidam nascuntur debiles et orbati, quidam autem fortes et perfecti. Quae in primo statu non fuissent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0047","text":"[I q. 96 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32535)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0048","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Rom. XIII, *quae a Deo sunt, ordinata sunt*. Ordo autem maxime videtur in disparitate consistere, dicit enim Augustinus, XIX de Civ. Dei, *ordo est parium dispariumque rerum sua cuique loca tribuens dispositio*. Ergo in primo statu, qui decentissimus fuisset, disparitas inveniretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0049","text":"[I q. 96 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32536)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0050","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere aliquam disparitatem in primo statu fuisse, ad minus quantum ad sexum, quia sine diversitate sexus, generatio non fuisset. Similiter etiam quantum ad aetatem, sic enim quidam ex aliis nascebantur; nec illi qui miscebantur, steriles erant. Sed et secundum animam diversitas fuisset, et quantum ad iustitiam et quantum ad scientiam. Non enim ex necessitate homo operabatur, sed per liberum arbitrium; ex quo homo habet quod possit magis et minus animum applicare ad aliquid faciendum vel volendum vel cognoscendum. Unde quidam magis profecissent in iustitia et scientia quam alii. Ex parte etiam corporis, poterat esse disparitas. Non enim erat exemptum corpus humanum totaliter a legibus naturae, quin ex exterioribus agentibus aliquod commodum aut auxilium reciperet magis et minus, cum etiam et cibis eorum vita sustentaretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0051","text":"Et sic nihil prohibet dicere quin secundum diversam dispositionem aeris et diversum situm stellarum, aliqui robustiores corpore generarentur quam alii, et maiores et pulchriores et melius complexionati. Ita tamen quod in illis qui excederentur, nullus esset defectus sive peccatum, sive circa animam sive circa corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0052","text":"[I q. 96 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32537)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0053","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Gregorius per verba illa intendit excludere disparitatem quae est secundum differentiam iustitiae et peccati; ex qua contingit quod aliqui poenaliter sunt sub aliis coercendi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0054","text":"[I q. 96 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32538)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0055","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod aequalitas est causa quod dilectio mutua sit aequalis. Sed tamen inter inaequales potest esse maior dilectio quam inter aequales, licet non aequaliter utrinque respondeat. Pater enim plus diligit filium naturaliter, quam frater fratrem; licet filius non tantundem diligat patrem, sicut ab eo diligitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0056","text":"[I q. 96 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32539)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0057","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod causa disparitatis poterat esse et ex parte Dei, non quidem ut puniret quosdam et quosdam praemiaret; sed ut quosdam plus, quosdam minus sublimaret, ut pulchritudo ordinis magis in hominibus reluceret. Et etiam ex parte naturae poterat disparitas causari secundum praedictum modum, absque aliquo defectu naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0058","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0059","text":"[I q. 96 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32540)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0060","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo in statu innocentiae homini non dominabatur. Dicit enim Augustinus, XIX de Civ. Dei, *hominem rationalem, ad imaginem suam factum, non voluit Deus nisi irrationabilibus dominari; non hominem homini, sed hominem pecori*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0061","text":"[I q. 96 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32541)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0062","text":"Praeterea, illud quod est introductum in poenam peccati, non fuisset in statu innocentiae. Sed hominem subesse homini, introductum est in poenam peccati, dictum est enim mulieri post peccatum, sub potestate viri eris, ut dicitur Gen. III. Ergo in statu innocentiae non erat homo homini subiectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0063","text":"[I q. 96 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32542)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0064","text":"Praeterea, subiectio libertati opponitur. Sed libertas est unum de praecipuis bonis, quod in statu innocentiae non defuisset, quando nihil aberat quod bona voluntas cupere posset, ut Augustinus dicit XIV de Civ. Dei. Ergo homo homini in statu innocentiae non dominabatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0065","text":"[I q. 96 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32543)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0066","text":"Sed contra, conditio hominum in statu innocentiae non erat dignior quam conditio Angelorum. Sed inter Angelos quidam aliis dominantur, unde et unus ordo dominationum vocatur. Ergo non est contra dignitatem status innocentiae, quod homo homini dominaretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0067","text":"[I q. 96 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32544)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0068","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod dominium accipitur dupliciter. Uno modo, secundum quod opponitur servituti, et sic dominus dicitur cui aliquis subditur ut servus. Alio modo accipitur dominium, secundum quod communiter refertur ad subiectum qualitercumque, et sic etiam ille qui habet officium gubernandi et dirigendi liberos, dominus dici potest. Primo ergo modo accepto dominio, in statu innocentiae homo homini non dominaretur, sed secundo modo accepto dominio, in statu innocentiae homo homini dominari potuisset. Cuius ratio est, quia servus in hoc differt a libero, quod liber est causa sui, ut dicitur in principio Metaphys.; servus autem ordinatur ad alium. Tunc ergo aliquis dominatur alicui ut servo, quando eum cui dominatur ad propriam utilitatem sui, scilicet dominantis, refert.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0069","text":"Et quia unicuique est appetibile proprium bonum, et per consequens contristabile est unicuique quod illud bonum quod deberet esse suum, cedat alteri tantum; ideo tale dominium non potest esse sine poena subiectorum. Propter quod, in statu innocentiae non fuisset tale dominium hominis ad hominem. Tunc vero dominatur aliquis alteri ut libero, quando dirigit ipsum ad proprium bonum eius qui dirigitur, vel ad bonum commune. Et tale dominium hominis, ad hominem in statu innocentiae fuisset, propter duo. Primo quidem, quia homo naturaliter est animal sociale, unde homines in statu innocentiae socialiter vixissent. Socialis autem vita multorum esse non posset, nisi aliquis praesideret, qui ad bonum commune intenderet, multi enim per se intendunt ad multa, unus vero ad unum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0070","text":"Et ideo philosophus dicit, in principio Politic., quod quandocumque multa ordinantur ad unum, semper invenitur unum ut principale et dirigens. Secundo quia, si unus homo habuisset super alium supereminentiam scientiae et iustitiae, inconveniens fuisset nisi hoc exequeretur in utilitatem aliorum; secundum quod dicitur I Petr. IV, *unusquisque gratiam quam accepit, in alterutrum illam administrantes*. Unde Augustinus dicit, XIX de Civ. Dei, quod *iusti non dominandi cupiditate imperant, sed officio consulendi, hoc naturalis ordo praescribit, ita Deus hominem condidit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0071","text":"[I q. 96 a. 4 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32545)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0072","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ad omnia obiecta, quae procedunt de primo modo dominii.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"dc8bc11bfd52e232a68788dc7675ef4832853496f047cf9f603cc0d4eb6af739","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T153036901235Z_77ef6dab","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 96","source_sha256":"dc8bc11bfd52e232a68788dc7675ef4832853496f047cf9f603cc0d4eb6af739","sha256":"3e8758bbd449c2c9f9417ed5114b7167b91ec366d24fe4679d3b84c0ae8420cc","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:30:36.901269+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0002","text":"[I q. 96 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32515)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the dominion that belonged to man in the state of innocence. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, whether man in the state of innocence had dominion over the animals. Second, whether he had dominion over every creature. Third, whether in the state of innocence all men would have been equal. Fourth, whether in that state man had dominion over men.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0005","text":"[I q. 96 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32516)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that Adam did not have dominion over the animals in the state of innocence. For Augustine says, in IX *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, that the animals were brought to Adam by the ministry of angels, so that he might impose names upon them. But the ministry of angels would not have been necessary there if man had dominion over the animals by himself. Therefore, in the state of innocence man did not have dominion over the other animals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0007","text":"[I q. 96 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32517)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0008","text":"Further, things that are discordant with one another are not rightly gathered under one dominion. But many animals are naturally discordant with one another, as the sheep and the wolf. Therefore, not all animals were contained under man's dominion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0009","text":"[I q. 96 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32518)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0010","text":"Further, Jerome says that *God gave dominion over the animals to man before sin, when he had no need of them; for He foreknew that after the fall man would be aided by the assistance of animals*. Therefore, at least the use of dominion over the animals did not belong to man before sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0011","text":"[I q. 96 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32519)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0012","text":"Further, it seems proper to a master to command. But a command is not rightly given except to one possessing reason. Therefore, man did not have dominion over irrational animals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0013","text":"[I q. 96 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32520)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0014","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Gen. I, concerning man: *let him rule over the fishes of the sea, and the birds of the air, and the beasts of the earth*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0015","text":"[I q. 96 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32521)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0016","text":"I answer that, as was said above, the disobedience to man of those things which ought to be subject to him followed as his punishment, because he himself was disobedient to God. And therefore, in the state of innocence, before the aforesaid disobedience, nothing resisted him which by nature ought to be subject to him. Now all animals are naturally subject to man. This is clear from three considerations. First, from the very process of nature. For just as in the generation of things a certain order is understood by which one proceeds from the imperfect to the perfect (for matter is for the sake of form, and a more imperfect form for the sake of a more perfect one), so also it is in the use of natural things, for the more imperfect yield to the use of the more perfect; plants use the earth for their nourishment, animals indeed use plants, and men use plants and animals. Hence man naturally has dominion over animals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0017","text":"And on this account the Philosopher says, in I *Politics*, that the hunting of wild animals is just and natural, because by it man claims for himself what is naturally his. Second, this is clear from the order of divine providence, which always governs lower things through higher things. Hence, since man is above the other animals, as having been made in the image of God, other animals are fittingly subjected to his governance. Third, the same is clear from the property of man and of the other animals. For in other animals there is found, according to natural estimation, a certain participation in prudence for certain particular acts; but in man there is found universal prudence, which is the reason of all things to be done. But everything that is by participation is subject to that which is by essence and universally. Hence it is clear that the subjection of the other animals to man is natural.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0018","text":"[I q. 96 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32522)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0019","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that a higher power can do many things with respect to subjects which a lower power cannot do. But an angel is naturally higher than man. Hence, by angelic power, an effect could be brought about concerning the animals which could not be brought about by human power, namely, that all the animals should immediately be gathered together.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0020","text":"[I q. 96 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32523)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0021","text":"To the second, it must be said that some say that animals which are now fierce and kill other animals would in that state have been tame not only toward man, but also toward other animals. But this is altogether unreasonable. For through man's sin the nature of animals was not changed, so that those for which it is now natural to eat the flesh of other animals would then have lived on herbs, as lions and falcons. Nor does Bede's Gloss say, on Gen. I, that trees and herbs were given to all animals and birds for food, but to some. There would therefore have been natural discord among certain animals. Yet on this account they would not have been withdrawn from man's dominion; just as neither are they now on this account withdrawn from the dominion of God, by whose providence this whole matter is disposed. And man would have been the executor of this providence, as is apparent even now in domestic animals; for hens are provided by men as food for domestic falcons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0022","text":"[I q. 96 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32524)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0023","text":"To the third, it must be said that men in the state of innocence did not need animals for bodily necessity: neither for clothing, because they were naked and were not ashamed, no disordered movement of concupiscence pressing upon them; nor for food, because they fed on the trees of Paradise; nor for conveyance, because of the strength of their bodies. Yet they needed them in order to acquire experimental knowledge of their natures. This was signified by the fact that God brought the animals to him, so that he might impose names upon them, which designate their natures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0024","text":"[I q. 96 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32525)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0025","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the other animals have a certain participation in prudence and reason according to natural estimation; from which it comes about that cranes follow a leader and bees obey a king. And thus then all animals would have obeyed man of themselves, just as now certain domestic animals obey him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0026","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0027","text":"[I q. 96 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32526)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0028","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that man would not have had dominion over all other creatures. For an angel is naturally of greater power than man. But, as Augustine says in *On the Trinity*, III, *corporeal matter would not have obeyed at the nod even of holy Angels*. Therefore, much less would it have obeyed man in the state of innocence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0029","text":"[I q. 96 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32527)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0030","text":"Further, in plants there are no powers of the soul except the nutritive, augmentative, and generative. But these are not naturally apt to obey reason, as is evident in one and the same man. Therefore, since dominion belongs to man according to reason, it seems that man in the state of innocence would not have had dominion over plants.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0031","text":"[I q. 96 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32528)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0032","text":"Further, whoever has dominion over some thing can change that thing. But man could not have changed the course of the heavenly bodies, for this belongs to God alone, as Dionysius says in the letter to Polycarp. Therefore, he did not have dominion over them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0033","text":"[I q. 96 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32529)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0034","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Genesis I, concerning man, *let him preside over every creature*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0035","text":"[I q. 96 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32530)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0036","text":"I answer that it must be said that in man there are in a certain way all things, and therefore, according to the manner in which he has dominion over those things which are in himself, in that manner it belongs to him to have dominion over others. Now four things are to be considered in man: namely, reason, according to which he agrees with the Angels; the sensitive powers, according to which he agrees with animals; the natural powers, according to which he agrees with plants; and the body itself, according to which he agrees with inanimate things. But reason in man holds the place of ruler, and not of that which is subject to dominion. Hence man did not have dominion over the Angels in the first state, and what is said, every creature, is understood of that which is not in the image of God. But the soul has dominion by commanding over the sensitive powers, such as the irascible and concupiscible, which obey reason in some measure. Hence even in the state of innocence he had dominion over the other animals by command.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0037","text":"But man has dominion over the natural powers and over the body itself, not indeed by commanding, but by using. And thus also man in the state of innocence had dominion over plants and inanimate things, not by command or alteration, but by using their aid without impediment.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0038","text":"[I q. 96 a. 2 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32531)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0039","text":"And through this the answer to the objections is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0040","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0041","text":"[I q. 96 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32532)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0042","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that all men in the state of innocence would have been equal. For Gregory says that, *where we do not sin, we are all equal*. But in the state of innocence there was no sin. Therefore, all were equal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0043","text":"[I q. 96 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32533)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0044","text":"Further, likeness and equality are a ground of mutual love, according to Ecclesiasticus XIII: *every animal loves its like; so too every man his neighbor*. But in that state love, which is the bond of peace, abounded among men. Therefore, all would have been equal in the state of innocence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0045","text":"[I q. 96 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32534)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0046","text":"Further, when the cause ceases, the effect ceases. But the cause of inequality among men now seems to be, on God's part indeed, that He rewards some according to their merits, while He punishes others; but on nature's part, because through a defect of nature some are born weak and deprived, whereas others are strong and perfect. These would not have been in the first state.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0047","text":"[I q. 96 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32535)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0048","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Romans XIII, *those things which are from God are ordered*. But order seems to consist especially in disparity; for Augustine says, in *The City of God*, XIX, *order is a disposition which assigns to equal and unequal things their proper places*. Therefore, in the first state, which would have been most fitting, disparity would be found.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0049","text":"[I q. 96 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32536)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0050","text":"I answer that it must be said that there would have been some inequality in the first state, at least as regards sex, because without diversity of sex there would have been no generation. Likewise also as regards age, for in this way some would have been born from others; nor would those who had intercourse have been sterile. But there would also have been diversity with respect to the soul, both as regards justice and as regards knowledge. For man did not act by necessity, but by free choice; from which man has it that he can apply his mind more or less to doing or willing or knowing something. Hence some would have made greater progress in justice and knowledge than others. On the part of the body too, there could have been inequality. For the human body was not wholly exempt from the laws of nature, so as not to receive more or less benefit or assistance from external agents, since their life was also sustained by food.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0051","text":"And thus nothing prevents our saying that, according to a different disposition of the air and a different position of the stars, some would have been generated more robust in body than others, and greater and more beautiful and better complexioned. Yet in those who were surpassed there would have been no defect or sin, either concerning the soul or concerning the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0052","text":"[I q. 96 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32537)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0053","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that by those words Gregory intends to exclude the inequality that is according to the difference between justice and sin; from which it comes about that some are to be restrained under others as a punishment.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0054","text":"[I q. 96 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32538)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0055","text":"To the second it must be said that equality is the cause of mutual love being equal. Yet among unequals there can be greater love than among equals, although it does not correspond equally on both sides. For a father naturally loves his son more than a brother loves his brother, although the son does not love his father as much as he is loved by him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0056","text":"[I q. 96 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32539)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0057","text":"To the third it must be said that the cause of inequality could have been on the part of God also, not indeed in order to punish some and reward others, but in order to elevate some more and some less, so that the beauty of order might shine forth more in human beings. And inequality could also have been caused on the part of nature according to the aforesaid manner, without any defect of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0058","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0059","text":"[I q. 96 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32540)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0060","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that in the state of innocence man did not exercise dominion over man. For Augustine says, in XIX *On the City of God*, that *God did not will that the rational human being, made in His image, should have dominion except over irrational beings: not man over man, but man over beast*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0061","text":"[I q. 96 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32541)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0062","text":"Moreover, that which was introduced as a punishment for sin would not have existed in the state of innocence. But for man to be subject to man was introduced as a punishment for sin; for it was said to the woman after sin, “You shall be under the power of the man,” as is stated in Gen. III. Therefore, in the state of innocence man was not subject to man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0063","text":"[I q. 96 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32542)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0064","text":"Moreover, subjection is opposed to freedom. But freedom is one of the chief goods, which would not have been lacking in the state of innocence, when nothing was absent that a good will could desire, as Augustine says in XIV *On the City of God*. Therefore, in the state of innocence man did not exercise dominion over man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0065","text":"[I q. 96 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32543)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0066","text":"But on the contrary, the condition of human beings in the state of innocence was not more dignified than the condition of the angels. But among the angels some exercise dominion over others, whence one order is called that of dominations. Therefore, it is not contrary to the dignity of the state of innocence that man should exercise dominion over man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0067","text":"[I q. 96 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32544)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0068","text":"I answer that dominion is understood in two ways. In one way, insofar as it is opposed to servitude, and thus a master is said to be one to whom someone is subject as a slave. In another way dominion is understood insofar as it is commonly referred to a subject in whatever manner, and thus even one who has the office of governing and directing free persons can be called a master. Therefore, with dominion understood in the first way, in the state of innocence man would not exercise dominion over man; but with dominion understood in the second way, in the state of innocence man could exercise dominion over man. The reason for this is that a slave differs from a free person in this: that a free person is a cause of himself, as is said at the beginning of the *Metaphysics*; whereas a slave is ordered to another. Therefore, one exercises dominion over another as a slave when he refers the one over whom he exercises dominion to his own utility, namely, that of the one exercising dominion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0069","text":"And because each person's own good is desirable to him, and consequently it is distressing to each person that the good which ought to be his should accrue to another alone, therefore such dominion cannot exist without punishment to those subject to it. On this account, in the state of innocence there would not have been such dominion of man over man. But one exercises dominion over another as a free person when he directs him to the proper good of the one directed, or to the common good. And such dominion of man over man would have existed in the state of innocence, for two reasons. First, because man is naturally a social animal; hence human beings in the state of innocence would have lived socially. But the social life of many could not exist unless someone presided, intending the common good; for many by themselves attend to many things, whereas one attends to one thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0070","text":"And therefore the Philosopher says, at the beginning of the *Politics*, that whenever many things are ordered to one, one is always found as principal and directing. Second, because if one man had possessed superiority over another in knowledge and justice, it would have been unfitting unless he put this into effect for the benefit of others; according as it is said in I Pet. IV, *each administering to one another the grace which he has received*. Hence Augustine says, in XIX *On the City of God*, that *the just rule not from a desire to dominate, but from the office of providing counsel; natural order prescribes this; thus God created man*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0071","text":"[I q. 96 a. 4 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32545)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0072","text":"And through this the response to all the objections is clear, which proceed from the first mode of dominion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_88:P0072"]}],"source_digest":"6de18d3e5dd1f470fbb686001547e5d91b203e3d412f819375ecfd192bb6f47f","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 96"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 96","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89","title":"First Part · Question 97","order":98,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_89","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 97 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32546)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de his quae pertinent ad statum primi hominis secundum corpus. Et primo, quantum ad conservationem individui; secundo, quantum ad conservationem speciei. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum homo in statu innocentiae esset immortalis. Secundo, utrum esset impassibilis. Tertio, utrum indigeret cibis. Quarto, utrum per lignum vitae immortalitatem consequeretur.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 97 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32547)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo in statu innocentiae non erat immortalis. Mortale enim ponitur in definitione hominis. Sed remota definitione, aufertur definitum. Ergo si homo erat, non poterat esse immortalis.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32548)\n\nPraeterea, corruptibile et incorruptibile genere differunt, ut dicitur in X Metaphys. Sed eorum quae differunt genere, non est transmutatio in invicem. Si ergo primus homo fuit incorruptibilis, non posset homo in statu isto esse corruptibilis.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32549)\n\nPraeterea, si homo in statu innocentiae fuit immortalis, aut hoc habuit per naturam, aut per gratiam. Sed non per naturam, quia, cum natura eadem maneat secundum speciem, nunc quoque esset immortalis. Similiter nec per gratiam, quia primus homo gratiam per poenitentiam recuperavit, secundum illud Sap. X, *eduxit illum a delicto suo*; ergo immortalitatem recuperasset; quod patet esse falsum. Non ergo homo erat immortalis in statu innocentiae.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32550)\n\nPraeterea, immortalitas promittitur homini in praemium; secundum illud Apoc. XXI, *mors ultra non erit*. Sed homo non fuit conditus in statu praemii, sed ut praemium mereretur. Ergo homo in statu innocentiae non fuit immortalis.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32551)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur ad Rom. V, quod *per peccatum intravit mors in mundum*. Ergo ante peccatum homo erat immortalis.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32552)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod aliquid potest dici incorruptibile tripliciter. Uno modo, ex parte materiae, eo scilicet quod vel non habet materiam, sicut Angelus; vel habet materiam quae non est in potentia nisi ad unam formam, sicut corpus caeleste. Et hoc dicitur secundum naturam incorruptibile. Alio modo dicitur aliquid incorruptibile ex parte formae, quia scilicet rei corruptibili per naturam, inhaeret aliqua dispositio per quam totaliter a corruptione prohibetur. Et hoc dicitur incorruptibile secundum gloriam, quia, ut dicit Augustinus in epistola ad Dioscorum, *tam potenti natura Deus fecit animam, ut ex eius beatitudine redundet in corpus plenitudo sanitatis*, idest incorruptionis vigor. Tertio modo dicitur aliquid incorruptibile ex parte causae efficientis. Et hoc modo homo in statu innocentiae fuisset incorruptibilis et immortalis. Quia, ut Augustinus dicit in libro de quaest. Vet. et Nov. Test., *Deus hominem fecit, qui quandiu non peccaret, immortalitate vigeret, ut ipse sibi auctor esset aut ad vitam aut ad mortem*. Non enim corpus eius erat indissolubile per aliquem immortalitatis vigorem in eo existentem; sed inerat animae vis quaedam supernaturaliter divinitus data, per quam poterat corpus ab omni corruptione praeservare, quandiu ipsa Deo subiecta mansisset. Quod rationabiliter factum est. Quia enim anima rationalis excedit proportionem corporalis materiae, ut supra dictum est. Conveniens fuit ut in principio ei virtus daretur, per quam corpus conservare posset supra naturam corporalis materiae.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32553)\n\nAd primum ergo et secundum dicendum quod rationes illae procedunt de incorruptibili et immortali per naturam.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32554)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod vis illa praeservandi corpus a corruptione, non erat animae humanae naturalis, sed per donum gratiae. Et quamvis gratiam recuperaverit ad remissionem culpae et meritum gloriae, non tamen ad amissae immortalitatis effectum. Hoc enim reservabatur Christo, per quem naturae defectus in melius reparandus erat, ut infra dicetur.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32555)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod differt immortalitas gloriae, quae promittitur in praemium, ab immortalitate quae fuit homini collata in statu innocentiae.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 97 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32556)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo in statu innocentiae fuisset passibilis. Sentire enim est pati quoddam. Sed homo in statu innocentiae fuisset sensibilis. Ergo fuisset passibilis.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32557)\n\nPraeterea, somnus passio quaedam est. Sed homo in statu innocentiae dormivisset; secundum illud Gen. II, *immisit Deus soporem in Adam*. Ergo fuisset passibilis.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32558)\n\nPraeterea, ibidem subditur quod tulit unam de costis eius. Ergo fuisset passibilis etiam per abscissionem partis.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32559)\n\nPraeterea, corpus hominis molle fuit. Sed molle naturaliter passivum est a duro. Ergo si corpori primi hominis obvium fuisset aliquod corpus durum, ab eo passum fuisset. Et sic primus homo fuit passibilis.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32560)\n\nSed contra est quia, si fuit passibilis, fuit etiam corruptibilis, quia passio, magis facta, abiicit a substantia.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32561)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod passio dupliciter dicitur. Uno modo, proprie, et sic pati dicitur quod a sua naturali dispositione removetur. Passio enim est effectus actionis, in rebus autem naturalibus contraria agunt et patiuntur ad invicem, quorum unum removet alterum a sua naturali dispositione. Alio modo, dicitur passio communiter, secundum quamcumque mutationem, etiam si pertineat ad perfectionem naturae; sicut intelligere vel sentire dicitur pati quoddam. Hoc igitur secundo modo, homo in statu innocentiae passibilis erat, et patiebatur, et secundum animam et secundum corpus. Primo autem modo dicta passione, erat impassibilis et secundum animam et secundum corpus, sicut et immortalis, poterat enim passionem prohibere, sicut et mortem, si absque peccato perstitisset.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32562)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ad duo prima. Nam sentire et dormire non removent hominem a naturali dispositione, sed ad bonum naturae ordinantur.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32563)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, costa illa fuit in Adam, inquantum erat principium humani generis; sicut semen est in homine, inquantum est principium per generationem. Sicut igitur decisio seminis non est cum passione quae removeat hominem a naturali dispositione, ita etiam est dicendum de separatione illius costae.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32564)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod corpus hominis in statu innocentiae poterat praeservari ne pateretur laesionem ab aliquo duro, partim quidem per propriam rationem, per quam poterat nociva vitare; partim etiam per divinam providentiam, quae sic ipsum tuebatur, ut nihil ei occurreret ex improviso, a quo laederetur.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 97 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32565)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo in statu innocentiae non indigebat cibis. Cibus enim necessarius est homini ad restaurationem deperditi. Sed in corpore Adae, ut videtur, nulla fiebat deperditio, quia incorruptibile erat. Ergo non erat ei cibus necessarius.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32566)\n\nPraeterea, cibus est necessarius ad nutriendum. Sed nutritio non est sine passione. Cum ergo corpus hominis esset impassibile, non erat ei cibus necessarius, ut videtur.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32567)\n\nPraeterea, cibus dicitur esse nobis necessarius ad vitae conservationem. Sed Adam aliter vitam poterat conservare, quia si non peccaret, non moreretur. Ergo cibus non erat ei necessarius.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32568)\n\nPraeterea, ad sumptionem cibi sequitur emissio superfluitatum, quae habent quandam turpitudinem non convenientem dignitati primi status. Ergo videtur quod homo in primo statu cibis non uteretur.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32569)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, *de omni ligno quod est in Paradiso, comedetis*.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32570)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod homo in statu innocentiae habuit vitam animalem cibis indigentem; post resurrectionem vero habebit vitam spiritualem cibis non indigentem. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod anima rationalis et anima est et spiritus. Dicitur autem esse anima secundum illud quod est sibi commune et aliis animabus, quod est vitam corpori dare, unde dicitur Gen. II, *factus est homo in animam viventem*, idest vitam corpori dantem. Sed spiritus dicitur secundum illud quod est proprium sibi et non aliis animabus, quod scilicet habeat virtutem intellectivam immaterialem. In primo igitur statu anima rationalis communicabat corpori id quod competit ei inquantum est anima, et ideo corpus illud dicebatur animale, inquantum scilicet habebat vitam ab anima. Primum autem principium vitae in istis inferioribus, ut dicitur in libro de anima, est anima vegetabilis, cuius opera sunt alimento uti et generare et augeri. Et ideo haec opera homini in primo statu competebant. In ultimo vero statu post resurrectionem, anima communicabit quodammodo corpori ea quae sunt sibi propria inquantum est spiritus, immortalitatem quidem, quantum ad omnes; impassibilitatem vero et gloriam et virtutem, quantum ad bonos, quorum corpora spiritualia dicentur. Unde post resurrectionem homines cibis non indigebunt, sed in statu innocentiae eis indigebant.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32571)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut dicit Augustinus in libro de quaest. Vet. et Nov. Test., *quomodo immortale corpus habebat, quod cibo sustentabatur? Immortale enim non eget esca neque potu*. Dictum est enim supra quod immortalitas primi status erat secundum vim quandam supernaturalem in anima residentem; non autem secundum aliquam dispositionem corpori inhaerentem. Unde per actionem caloris aliquid de humido illius corporis poterat deperdi; et ne totaliter consumeretur, necesse erat per assumptionem cibi homini subveniri.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32572)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod in nutritione est quaedam passio et alteratio, scilicet ex parte alimenti, quod convertitur in substantiam eius quod alitur. Unde ex hoc non potest concludi quod corpus hominis fuerit passibile, sed quod cibus assumptus erat passibilis. Quamvis etiam talis passio esset ad perfectionem naturae.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32573)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, si homo sibi non subveniret de cibo, peccaret; sicut peccavit sumendo vetitum cibum. Simul enim sibi praeceptum fuit ut a ligno scientiae boni et mali abstineret, et ut de omni alio ligno Paradisi vesceretur.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32574)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod quidam dicunt quod homo in statu innocentiae non assumpsisset de cibo nisi quantum fuisset ei necessarium, unde non fuisset ibi superfluitatum emissio. Sed hoc irrationabile videtur, quod in cibo assumpto non esset aliqua faeculentia, quae non esset apta ut converteretur in hominis nutrimentum. Unde oportebat superfluitates emitti. Tamen fuisset divinitus provisum ut nulla ex hoc indecentia esset.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 97 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32575)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod lignum vitae non poterat esse causa immortalitatis. Nihil enim potest agere ultra suam speciem, effectus enim non excedit causam. Sed lignum vitae erat corruptibile, alioquin non potuisset in nutrimentum assumi, quia alimentum convertitur in substantiam nutriti, ut dictum est. Ergo lignum vitae incorruptibilitatem seu immortalitatem conferre non poterat.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32576)\n\nPraeterea, effectus qui causantur a virtutibus plantarum et aliarum naturalium rerum, sunt naturales. Si ergo lignum vitae immortalitatem causasset, fuisset illa immortalitas naturalis.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32577)\n\nPraeterea, hoc videtur redire in fabulas antiquorum, qui dixerunt quod dii qui comedebant de quodam cibo, facti sunt immortales, quos irridet philosophus in III Metaphys.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 4 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32578)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. III, *ne forte mittat manum suam et sumat de ligno vitae, et comedat et vivat in aeternum*.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 4 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32579)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus in libro de quaest. Vet. et Nov. Test., dicit, *gustus arboris vitae corruptionem corporis inhibebat, denique etiam post peccatum potuit insolubilis manere, si permissum esset illi edere de arbore vitae*.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32580)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod lignum vitae quodammodo immortalitatem causabat, non autem simpliciter. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod duo remedia ad conservationem vitae habebat homo in primo statu, contra duos defectus. Primus enim defectus est deperditio humidi per actionem caloris naturalis, qui est animae instrumentum. Et contra hunc defectum subveniebatur homini per esum aliorum lignorum Paradisi, sicut et nunc subvenitur nobis, per cibos quos sumimus. Secundus autem defectus est quia, ut philosophus dicit I de Generat., illud quod generatur ex aliquo extraneo, adiunctum ei quod prius erat humido praeexistenti, imminuit virtutem activam speciei, sicut aqua adiuncta vino, primo quidem convertitur in saporem vini, sed secundum quod magis et magis additur, diminuit vini fortitudinem, et tandem vinum fit aquosum. Sic igitur videmus quod in principio virtus activa speciei est adeo fortis, quod potest convertere de alimento non solum quod sufficit ad restaurationem deperditi, sed etiam quod sufficit ad augmentum. Postmodum vero quod aggeneratur non sufficit ad augmentum, sed solum ad restaurationem deperditi. Tandem vero, in statu senectutis, nec ad hoc sufficit, unde sequitur decrementum, et finaliter naturalis dissolutio corporis. Et contra hunc defectum subveniebatur homini per lignum vitae, habebat enim virtutem fortificandi virtutem speciei contra debilitatem provenientem ex admixtione extranei. Unde Augustinus dicit, in XIV de Civ. Dei, quod *cibus aderat homini ne esuriret, potus ne sitiret, et lignum vitae ne senectus eum dissolveret*. Et in libro de quaest. Vet. et Nov. Test., dicit quod *vitae arbor medicinae modo corruptionem hominum prohibebat*. Non tamen simpliciter immortalitatem causabat. Quia neque virtus quae inerat animae ad conservandum corpus, causabatur ex ligno vitae, neque etiam poterat immortalitatis dispositionem corpori praestare, ut nunquam dissolvi posset. Quod ex hoc patet, quia virtus cuiuscumque corporis est finita. Unde non poterat virtus ligni vitae ad hoc se extendere ut daret corpori virtutem Durandi tempore infinito, sed usque ad determinatum tempus. Manifestum est enim quod, quanto aliqua virtus est maior, tanto imprimit durabiliorem effectum. Unde cum virtus ligni vitae esset finita, semel sumptum praeservabat a corruptione usque ad determinatum tempus; quo finito, vel homo translatus fuisset ad spiritualem vitam, vel indiguisset iterum sumere de ligno vitae.\n\n[I q. 97 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32581)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ab obiecta. Nam primae rationes concludunt quod non causabat incorruptibilitatem simpliciter. Aliae vero concludunt quod causabat incorruptibilitatem impediendo corruptionem, secundum modum praedictum.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0002","text":"[I q. 97 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32546)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de his quae pertinent ad statum primi hominis secundum corpus. Et primo, quantum ad conservationem individui; secundo, quantum ad conservationem speciei. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum homo in statu innocentiae esset immortalis. Secundo, utrum esset impassibilis. Tertio, utrum indigeret cibis. Quarto, utrum per lignum vitae immortalitatem consequeretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0005","text":"[I q. 97 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32547)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo in statu innocentiae non erat immortalis. Mortale enim ponitur in definitione hominis. Sed remota definitione, aufertur definitum. Ergo si homo erat, non poterat esse immortalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0007","text":"[I q. 97 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32548)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0008","text":"Praeterea, corruptibile et incorruptibile genere differunt, ut dicitur in X Metaphys. Sed eorum quae differunt genere, non est transmutatio in invicem. Si ergo primus homo fuit incorruptibilis, non posset homo in statu isto esse corruptibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0009","text":"[I q. 97 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32549)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0010","text":"Praeterea, si homo in statu innocentiae fuit immortalis, aut hoc habuit per naturam, aut per gratiam. Sed non per naturam, quia, cum natura eadem maneat secundum speciem, nunc quoque esset immortalis. Similiter nec per gratiam, quia primus homo gratiam per poenitentiam recuperavit, secundum illud Sap. X, *eduxit illum a delicto suo*; ergo immortalitatem recuperasset; quod patet esse falsum. Non ergo homo erat immortalis in statu innocentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0011","text":"[I q. 97 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32550)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0012","text":"Praeterea, immortalitas promittitur homini in praemium; secundum illud Apoc. XXI, *mors ultra non erit*. Sed homo non fuit conditus in statu praemii, sed ut praemium mereretur. Ergo homo in statu innocentiae non fuit immortalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0013","text":"[I q. 97 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32551)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0014","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur ad Rom. V, quod *per peccatum intravit mors in mundum*. Ergo ante peccatum homo erat immortalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0015","text":"[I q. 97 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32552)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod aliquid potest dici incorruptibile tripliciter. Uno modo, ex parte materiae, eo scilicet quod vel non habet materiam, sicut Angelus; vel habet materiam quae non est in potentia nisi ad unam formam, sicut corpus caeleste. Et hoc dicitur secundum naturam incorruptibile. Alio modo dicitur aliquid incorruptibile ex parte formae, quia scilicet rei corruptibili per naturam, inhaeret aliqua dispositio per quam totaliter a corruptione prohibetur. Et hoc dicitur incorruptibile secundum gloriam, quia, ut dicit Augustinus in epistola ad Dioscorum, *tam potenti natura Deus fecit animam, ut ex eius beatitudine redundet in corpus plenitudo sanitatis*, idest incorruptionis vigor. Tertio modo dicitur aliquid incorruptibile ex parte causae efficientis. Et hoc modo homo in statu innocentiae fuisset incorruptibilis et immortalis. Quia, ut Augustinus dicit in libro de quaest. Vet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0017","text":"et Nov. Test., *Deus hominem fecit, qui quandiu non peccaret, immortalitate vigeret, ut ipse sibi auctor esset aut ad vitam aut ad mortem*. Non enim corpus eius erat indissolubile per aliquem immortalitatis vigorem in eo existentem; sed inerat animae vis quaedam supernaturaliter divinitus data, per quam poterat corpus ab omni corruptione praeservare, quandiu ipsa Deo subiecta mansisset. Quod rationabiliter factum est. Quia enim anima rationalis excedit proportionem corporalis materiae, ut supra dictum est. Conveniens fuit ut in principio ei virtus daretur, per quam corpus conservare posset supra naturam corporalis materiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0018","text":"[I q. 97 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32553)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0019","text":"Ad primum ergo et secundum dicendum quod rationes illae procedunt de incorruptibili et immortali per naturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0020","text":"[I q. 97 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32554)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod vis illa praeservandi corpus a corruptione, non erat animae humanae naturalis, sed per donum gratiae. Et quamvis gratiam recuperaverit ad remissionem culpae et meritum gloriae, non tamen ad amissae immortalitatis effectum. Hoc enim reservabatur Christo, per quem naturae defectus in melius reparandus erat, ut infra dicetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0022","text":"[I q. 97 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32555)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0023","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod differt immortalitas gloriae, quae promittitur in praemium, ab immortalitate quae fuit homini collata in statu innocentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0024","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0025","text":"[I q. 97 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32556)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0026","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo in statu innocentiae fuisset passibilis. Sentire enim est pati quoddam. Sed homo in statu innocentiae fuisset sensibilis. Ergo fuisset passibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0027","text":"[I q. 97 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32557)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0028","text":"Praeterea, somnus passio quaedam est. Sed homo in statu innocentiae dormivisset; secundum illud Gen. II, *immisit Deus soporem in Adam*. Ergo fuisset passibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0029","text":"[I q. 97 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32558)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0030","text":"Praeterea, ibidem subditur quod tulit unam de costis eius. Ergo fuisset passibilis etiam per abscissionem partis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0031","text":"[I q. 97 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32559)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0032","text":"Praeterea, corpus hominis molle fuit. Sed molle naturaliter passivum est a duro. Ergo si corpori primi hominis obvium fuisset aliquod corpus durum, ab eo passum fuisset. Et sic primus homo fuit passibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0033","text":"[I q. 97 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32560)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0034","text":"Sed contra est quia, si fuit passibilis, fuit etiam corruptibilis, quia passio, magis facta, abiicit a substantia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0035","text":"[I q. 97 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32561)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0036","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod passio dupliciter dicitur. Uno modo, proprie, et sic pati dicitur quod a sua naturali dispositione removetur. Passio enim est effectus actionis, in rebus autem naturalibus contraria agunt et patiuntur ad invicem, quorum unum removet alterum a sua naturali dispositione. Alio modo, dicitur passio communiter, secundum quamcumque mutationem, etiam si pertineat ad perfectionem naturae; sicut intelligere vel sentire dicitur pati quoddam. Hoc igitur secundo modo, homo in statu innocentiae passibilis erat, et patiebatur, et secundum animam et secundum corpus. Primo autem modo dicta passione, erat impassibilis et secundum animam et secundum corpus, sicut et immortalis, poterat enim passionem prohibere, sicut et mortem, si absque peccato perstitisset.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0037","text":"[I q. 97 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32562)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0038","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ad duo prima. Nam sentire et dormire non removent hominem a naturali dispositione, sed ad bonum naturae ordinantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0039","text":"[I q. 97 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32563)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0040","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, costa illa fuit in Adam, inquantum erat principium humani generis; sicut semen est in homine, inquantum est principium per generationem. Sicut igitur decisio seminis non est cum passione quae removeat hominem a naturali dispositione, ita etiam est dicendum de separatione illius costae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0041","text":"[I q. 97 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32564)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0042","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod corpus hominis in statu innocentiae poterat praeservari ne pateretur laesionem ab aliquo duro, partim quidem per propriam rationem, per quam poterat nociva vitare; partim etiam per divinam providentiam, quae sic ipsum tuebatur, ut nihil ei occurreret ex improviso, a quo laederetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0043","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0044","text":"[I q. 97 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32565)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0045","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo in statu innocentiae non indigebat cibis. Cibus enim necessarius est homini ad restaurationem deperditi. Sed in corpore Adae, ut videtur, nulla fiebat deperditio, quia incorruptibile erat. Ergo non erat ei cibus necessarius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0046","text":"[I q. 97 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32566)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0047","text":"Praeterea, cibus est necessarius ad nutriendum. Sed nutritio non est sine passione. Cum ergo corpus hominis esset impassibile, non erat ei cibus necessarius, ut videtur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0048","text":"[I q. 97 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32567)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0049","text":"Praeterea, cibus dicitur esse nobis necessarius ad vitae conservationem. Sed Adam aliter vitam poterat conservare, quia si non peccaret, non moreretur. Ergo cibus non erat ei necessarius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0050","text":"[I q. 97 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32568)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0051","text":"Praeterea, ad sumptionem cibi sequitur emissio superfluitatum, quae habent quandam turpitudinem non convenientem dignitati primi status. Ergo videtur quod homo in primo statu cibis non uteretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0052","text":"[I q. 97 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32569)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0053","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, *de omni ligno quod est in Paradiso, comedetis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0054","text":"[I q. 97 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32570)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0055","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod homo in statu innocentiae habuit vitam animalem cibis indigentem; post resurrectionem vero habebit vitam spiritualem cibis non indigentem. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod anima rationalis et anima est et spiritus. Dicitur autem esse anima secundum illud quod est sibi commune et aliis animabus, quod est vitam corpori dare, unde dicitur Gen. II, *factus est homo in animam viventem*, idest vitam corpori dantem. Sed spiritus dicitur secundum illud quod est proprium sibi et non aliis animabus, quod scilicet habeat virtutem intellectivam immaterialem. In primo igitur statu anima rationalis communicabat corpori id quod competit ei inquantum est anima, et ideo corpus illud dicebatur animale, inquantum scilicet habebat vitam ab anima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0056","text":"Primum autem principium vitae in istis inferioribus, ut dicitur in libro de anima, est anima vegetabilis, cuius opera sunt alimento uti et generare et augeri. Et ideo haec opera homini in primo statu competebant. In ultimo vero statu post resurrectionem, anima communicabit quodammodo corpori ea quae sunt sibi propria inquantum est spiritus, immortalitatem quidem, quantum ad omnes; impassibilitatem vero et gloriam et virtutem, quantum ad bonos, quorum corpora spiritualia dicentur. Unde post resurrectionem homines cibis non indigebunt, sed in statu innocentiae eis indigebant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0057","text":"[I q. 97 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32571)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0058","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut dicit Augustinus in libro de quaest. Vet. et Nov. Test., *quomodo immortale corpus habebat, quod cibo sustentabatur? Immortale enim non eget esca neque potu*. Dictum est enim supra quod immortalitas primi status erat secundum vim quandam supernaturalem in anima residentem; non autem secundum aliquam dispositionem corpori inhaerentem. Unde per actionem caloris aliquid de humido illius corporis poterat deperdi; et ne totaliter consumeretur, necesse erat per assumptionem cibi homini subveniri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0059","text":"[I q. 97 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32572)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0060","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod in nutritione est quaedam passio et alteratio, scilicet ex parte alimenti, quod convertitur in substantiam eius quod alitur. Unde ex hoc non potest concludi quod corpus hominis fuerit passibile, sed quod cibus assumptus erat passibilis. Quamvis etiam talis passio esset ad perfectionem naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0061","text":"[I q. 97 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32573)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0062","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, si homo sibi non subveniret de cibo, peccaret; sicut peccavit sumendo vetitum cibum. Simul enim sibi praeceptum fuit ut a ligno scientiae boni et mali abstineret, et ut de omni alio ligno Paradisi vesceretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0063","text":"[I q. 97 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32574)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0064","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod quidam dicunt quod homo in statu innocentiae non assumpsisset de cibo nisi quantum fuisset ei necessarium, unde non fuisset ibi superfluitatum emissio. Sed hoc irrationabile videtur, quod in cibo assumpto non esset aliqua faeculentia, quae non esset apta ut converteretur in hominis nutrimentum. Unde oportebat superfluitates emitti. Tamen fuisset divinitus provisum ut nulla ex hoc indecentia esset.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0065","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0066","text":"[I q. 97 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32575)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0067","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod lignum vitae non poterat esse causa immortalitatis. Nihil enim potest agere ultra suam speciem, effectus enim non excedit causam. Sed lignum vitae erat corruptibile, alioquin non potuisset in nutrimentum assumi, quia alimentum convertitur in substantiam nutriti, ut dictum est. Ergo lignum vitae incorruptibilitatem seu immortalitatem conferre non poterat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0068","text":"[I q. 97 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32576)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0069","text":"Praeterea, effectus qui causantur a virtutibus plantarum et aliarum naturalium rerum, sunt naturales. Si ergo lignum vitae immortalitatem causasset, fuisset illa immortalitas naturalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0070","text":"[I q. 97 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32577)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0071","text":"Praeterea, hoc videtur redire in fabulas antiquorum, qui dixerunt quod dii qui comedebant de quodam cibo, facti sunt immortales, quos irridet philosophus in III Metaphys.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0072","text":"[I q. 97 a. 4 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32578)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0073","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. III, *ne forte mittat manum suam et sumat de ligno vitae, et comedat et vivat in aeternum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0074","text":"[I q. 97 a. 4 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32579)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0075","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus in libro de quaest. Vet. et Nov. Test., dicit, *gustus arboris vitae corruptionem corporis inhibebat, denique etiam post peccatum potuit insolubilis manere, si permissum esset illi edere de arbore vitae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0076","text":"[I q. 97 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32580)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0077","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod lignum vitae quodammodo immortalitatem causabat, non autem simpliciter. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod duo remedia ad conservationem vitae habebat homo in primo statu, contra duos defectus. Primus enim defectus est deperditio humidi per actionem caloris naturalis, qui est animae instrumentum. Et contra hunc defectum subveniebatur homini per esum aliorum lignorum Paradisi, sicut et nunc subvenitur nobis, per cibos quos sumimus. Secundus autem defectus est quia, ut philosophus dicit I de Generat., illud quod generatur ex aliquo extraneo, adiunctum ei quod prius erat humido praeexistenti, imminuit virtutem activam speciei, sicut aqua adiuncta vino, primo quidem convertitur in saporem vini, sed secundum quod magis et magis additur, diminuit vini fortitudinem, et tandem vinum fit aquosum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0078","text":"Sic igitur videmus quod in principio virtus activa speciei est adeo fortis, quod potest convertere de alimento non solum quod sufficit ad restaurationem deperditi, sed etiam quod sufficit ad augmentum. Postmodum vero quod aggeneratur non sufficit ad augmentum, sed solum ad restaurationem deperditi. Tandem vero, in statu senectutis, nec ad hoc sufficit, unde sequitur decrementum, et finaliter naturalis dissolutio corporis. Et contra hunc defectum subveniebatur homini per lignum vitae, habebat enim virtutem fortificandi virtutem speciei contra debilitatem provenientem ex admixtione extranei. Unde Augustinus dicit, in XIV de Civ. Dei, quod *cibus aderat homini ne esuriret, potus ne sitiret, et lignum vitae ne senectus eum dissolveret*. Et in libro de quaest. Vet. et Nov. Test., dicit quod *vitae arbor medicinae modo corruptionem hominum prohibebat*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0079","text":"Non tamen simpliciter immortalitatem causabat. Quia neque virtus quae inerat animae ad conservandum corpus, causabatur ex ligno vitae, neque etiam poterat immortalitatis dispositionem corpori praestare, ut nunquam dissolvi posset. Quod ex hoc patet, quia virtus cuiuscumque corporis est finita. Unde non poterat virtus ligni vitae ad hoc se extendere ut daret corpori virtutem Durandi tempore infinito, sed usque ad determinatum tempus. Manifestum est enim quod, quanto aliqua virtus est maior, tanto imprimit durabiliorem effectum. Unde cum virtus ligni vitae esset finita, semel sumptum praeservabat a corruptione usque ad determinatum tempus; quo finito, vel homo translatus fuisset ad spiritualem vitam, vel indiguisset iterum sumere de ligno vitae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0080","text":"[I q. 97 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32581)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0081","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ab obiecta. Nam primae rationes concludunt quod non causabat incorruptibilitatem simpliciter. Aliae vero concludunt quod causabat incorruptibilitatem impediendo corruptionem, secundum modum praedictum.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"fd340df334a7fc6a5611601c036c0c8261776b0f2d322fb1afa682ad01074ccc","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T153144064737Z_f7f2042e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 97","source_sha256":"fd340df334a7fc6a5611601c036c0c8261776b0f2d322fb1afa682ad01074ccc","sha256":"8dd76abe24a4fca354f865fdbe4cda491d77467b55489ed7b50f93d15eaa1bbf","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:31:44.064829+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0002","text":"[I q. 97 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32546)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider those things that pertain to the state of the first man with respect to the body. First, as to the preservation of the individual; second, as to the preservation of the species. Concerning the first, four questions are asked. First, whether man in the state of innocence was immortal. Second, whether he was impassible. Third, whether he needed food. Fourth, whether he would attain immortality through the tree of life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0005","text":"[I q. 97 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32547)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that man in the state of innocence was not immortal. For mortal is included in the definition of man. But once the definition is removed, the thing defined is taken away. Therefore, if he was a man, he could not be immortal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0007","text":"[I q. 97 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32548)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0008","text":"Further, corruptible and incorruptible differ in genus, as is said in *Metaphysics* X. But among those that differ in genus, there is no transformation into one another. Therefore, if the first man was incorruptible, man in this state could not be corruptible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0009","text":"[I q. 97 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32549)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0010","text":"Further, if man in the state of innocence was immortal, he had this either by nature or by grace. But not by nature, because, since nature remains the same according to species, he would now also be immortal. Likewise, not by grace, because the first man recovered grace through penance, according to Wisdom X, *she brought him out of his transgression*; therefore he would have recovered immortality, which is evidently false. Therefore man was not immortal in the state of innocence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0011","text":"[I q. 97 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32550)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0012","text":"Further, immortality is promised to man as a reward, according to Apocalypse XXI, *death shall be no more*. But man was not created in a state of reward, but so as to merit a reward. Therefore man in the state of innocence was not immortal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0013","text":"[I q. 97 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32551)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0014","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Romans V that *through sin death entered into the world*. Therefore, before sin man was immortal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0015","text":"[I q. 97 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32552)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0016","text":"I answer that something can be called incorruptible in three ways. In one way, on the part of its matter, namely, because it either has no matter, as an angel does; or it has matter that is in potency only to one form, as a heavenly body does. And this is called incorruptible according to nature. In another way, something is called incorruptible on the part of its form, namely, because to a thing corruptible by nature there adheres some disposition by which it is wholly prevented from corruption. And this is called incorruptible according to glory, because, as Augustine says in his letter to Dioscorus, *God made the soul of so great a power by nature that from its beatitude there overflows into the body a fullness of health*, that is, the strength of incorruption. In a third way, something is called incorruptible on the part of its efficient cause. And in this way man in the state of innocence would have been incorruptible and immortal. For, as Augustine says in the book *On Questions of the Old*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0017","text":"and New Testament, *God made man such that, so long as he did not sin, he would flourish in immortality, so that he himself might be the author for himself either of life or of death*. For his body was not indissoluble through some vigor of immortality existing in it; rather, there was in the soul a certain power supernaturally given by God, through which it could preserve the body from every corruption, so long as it itself remained subject to God. This was fittingly done. For because the rational soul exceeds the proportion of bodily matter, as was said above, it was fitting that in the beginning it should be given a power through which it could preserve the body above the nature of bodily matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0018","text":"[I q. 97 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32553)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0019","text":"To the first and second, therefore, it must be said that those arguments proceed concerning what is incorruptible and immortal by nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0020","text":"[I q. 97 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32554)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0021","text":"To the third, it must be said that that power of preserving the body from corruption was not natural to the human soul, but was through the gift of grace. And although he recovered grace for the remission of guilt and the merit of glory, nevertheless he did not recover it for the effect of lost immortality. For this was reserved for Christ, through whom the defect of nature was to be repaired for the better, as will be said below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0022","text":"[I q. 97 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32555)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0023","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the immortality of glory, which is promised as a reward, differs from the immortality that was bestowed upon man in the state of innocence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0024","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0025","text":"[I q. 97 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32556)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0026","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that man in the state of innocence would have been passible. For to sense is a kind of being acted upon. But man in the state of innocence would have been capable of sensation. Therefore he would have been passible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0027","text":"[I q. 97 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32557)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0028","text":"Moreover, sleep is a kind of passion. But man in the state of innocence would have slept; according to Genesis II, *God cast a deep sleep upon Adam*. Therefore he would have been passible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0029","text":"[I q. 97 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32558)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0030","text":"Moreover, it is added in the same place that He took one of his ribs. Therefore he would have been passible also through the cutting off of a part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0031","text":"[I q. 97 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32559)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0032","text":"Moreover, the human body was soft. But what is soft is naturally passive in relation to what is hard. Therefore, if some hard body had come into contact with the body of the first man, it would have suffered from it. And thus the first man was passible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0033","text":"[I q. 97 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32560)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0034","text":"But on the contrary, if he was passible, he was also corruptible, because passion, when increased, casts away from substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0035","text":"[I q. 97 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32561)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0036","text":"I answer that passion is spoken of in two ways. In one way, properly, and thus that is said to suffer which is removed from its natural disposition. For passion is the effect of action; but in natural things, contraries act and are acted upon mutually, one of which removes the other from its natural disposition. In another way, passion is spoken of commonly, according to any change whatsoever, even if it pertains to the perfection of nature; just as to understand or to sense is called a kind of being acted upon. Therefore, in this second way, man in the state of innocence was passible, and suffered, both according to soul and according to body. But according to passion spoken of in the first way, he was impassible both according to soul and according to body, just as he was immortal; for he could have prevented passion, just as death, had he persevered without sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0037","text":"[I q. 97 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32562)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0038","text":"And through this the reply to the first two is clear. For sensing and sleeping do not remove a man from his natural disposition, but are ordered to the good of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0039","text":"[I q. 97 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32563)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0040","text":"To the third, it must be said that, as was said above, that rib was in Adam insofar as he was the principle of the human race, just as semen is in man insofar as he is a principle through generation. Therefore, just as the emission of semen is not accompanied by a passion that removes a man from his natural disposition, so also must it be said concerning the separation of that rib.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0041","text":"[I q. 97 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32564)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0042","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the human body in the state of innocence could be preserved from suffering injury from anything hard, partly indeed through its own reason, by which it could avoid harmful things; and partly also through divine providence, which so protected it that nothing would encounter it unexpectedly by which it might be injured.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0043","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0044","text":"[I q. 97 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32565)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0045","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that man in the state of innocence did not need food. For food is necessary to man for the restoration of what has been lost. But in Adam's body, as it seems, no loss took place, because it was incorruptible. Therefore food was not necessary for him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0046","text":"[I q. 97 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32566)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0047","text":"Moreover, food is necessary for nourishment. But nourishment is not without passion. Therefore, since the human body was impassible, food was not necessary for it, as it seems.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0048","text":"[I q. 97 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32567)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0049","text":"Moreover, food is said to be necessary to us for the preservation of life. But Adam could preserve life otherwise, because, if he had not sinned, he would not have died. Therefore food was not necessary for him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0050","text":"[I q. 97 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32568)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0051","text":"Moreover, the emission of superfluities follows upon the consumption of food, and these have a certain unbecomingness not suited to the dignity of the first state. Therefore it seems that in the first state man would not have used foods.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0052","text":"[I q. 97 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32569)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0053","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Gen. II: *of every tree that is in Paradise, you shall eat*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0054","text":"[I q. 97 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32570)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0055","text":"I answer that it must be said that in the state of innocence man had an animal life needing foods; but after the resurrection he will have a spiritual life not needing foods. For the evidence of this, it must be considered that the rational soul is both soul and spirit. Now it is called soul according to that which it has in common with the other souls, namely, to give life to the body; whence it is said in Gen. II, *man was made into a living soul*, that is, giving life to the body. But it is called spirit according to that which is proper to it and not to the other souls, namely, that it has an immaterial intellective power. Therefore, in the first state the rational soul communicated to the body that which belongs to it insofar as it is a soul, and therefore that body was called animal, namely insofar as it had life from the soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0056","text":"Now the first principle of life in these lower things, as is said in the book *On the Soul*, is the vegetative soul, whose operations are to use food, to generate, and to grow. And therefore these operations belonged to man in the first state. But in the final state after the resurrection, the soul will in a certain way communicate to the body those things which are proper to it insofar as it is spirit: immortality indeed, as to all; but impassibility, glory, and power, as to the good, whose bodies will be called spiritual. Hence after the resurrection men will not need foods, but in the state of innocence they needed them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0057","text":"[I q. 97 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32571)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0058","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, as Augustine says in the book *On Questions of the Old and New Testament*, *how did he have an immortal body, which was sustained by food? For an immortal thing does not need food or drink.* For it was said above that the immortality of the first state was according to a certain supernatural power residing in the soul, but not according to any disposition inhering in the body. Hence, through the action of heat, something of the moisture of that body could be lost; and lest it be entirely consumed, it was necessary that man be supplied by the taking of food.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0059","text":"[I q. 97 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32572)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0060","text":"To the second it must be said that in nutrition there is a certain passion and alteration, namely on the part of the food, which is converted into the substance of that which is nourished. Hence from this it cannot be concluded that the body of man was passible, but that the food taken was passible. Although even such a passion was for the perfection of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0061","text":"[I q. 97 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32573)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0062","text":"To the third it must be said that, if man did not provide himself with food, he would sin; just as he sinned by taking forbidden food. For he was at the same time commanded to abstain from the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, and to eat from every other tree of Paradise.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0063","text":"[I q. 97 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32574)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0064","text":"To the fourth it must be said that some say that man in the state of innocence would not have taken food except in so far as it was necessary for him, whence there would have been no emission of superfluities there. But this seems unreasonable: that in food taken there would not be some dregs that were not fit to be converted into man's nourishment. Hence it was necessary for superfluities to be emitted. Yet it would have been divinely provided that no unseemliness would result from this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0065","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0066","text":"[I q. 97 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32575)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0067","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that the tree of life could not be a cause of immortality. For nothing can act beyond its species, since an effect does not exceed its cause. But the tree of life was corruptible; otherwise it could not have been taken as nourishment, because food is converted into the substance of that which is nourished, as has been said. Therefore the tree of life could not confer incorruptibility or immortality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0068","text":"[I q. 97 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32576)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0069","text":"Moreover, effects caused by the powers of plants and other natural things are natural. Therefore, if the tree of life had caused immortality, that immortality would have been natural.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0070","text":"[I q. 97 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32577)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0071","text":"Moreover, this seems to return to the fables of the ancients, who said that the gods who ate of a certain food were made immortal; Aristotle mocks them in Metaphysics III.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0072","text":"[I q. 97 a. 4 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32578)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0073","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Gen. III: *lest perhaps he put forth his hand and take of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0074","text":"[I q. 97 a. 4 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32579)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0075","text":"Moreover, Augustine, in the book *On Questions of the Old and New Testament*, says: *the tasting of the tree of life restrained the corruption of the body; indeed, even after sin, he could have remained indissoluble, if he had been permitted to eat of the tree of life*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0076","text":"[I q. 97 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32580)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0077","text":"I answer that it must be said that the tree of life caused immortality in a certain way, but not simply. To make this evident, one must consider that man in the first state had two remedies for the preservation of life, against two defects. For the first defect is the loss of moisture through the action of natural heat, which is the instrument of the soul. And against this defect man was aided by eating the other trees of Paradise, just as we too are now aided by the foods which we take. But the second defect is that, as the Philosopher says in I *On Generation*, that which is generated from something extraneous, when added to the moisture that previously existed, diminishes the active power of the species; just as water added to wine is at first indeed converted into the flavor of wine, but insofar as more and more is added, it diminishes the strength of the wine, and at length the wine becomes watery.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0078","text":"Thus, therefore, we see that in the beginning the active power of the species is so strong that it can convert from nourishment not only what suffices for the restoration of what has been lost, but also what suffices for growth. Later, however, what is added does not suffice for growth, but only for the restoration of what has been lost. But finally, in the state of old age, it does not suffice even for this; whence decrease follows, and finally the natural dissolution of the body. And against this defect man was aided by the tree of life, for it had the power of strengthening the power of the species against the weakness arising from the admixture of what is extraneous. Hence Augustine says, in XIV *On the City of God*, that *food was present for man, lest he should hunger; drink, lest he should thirst; and the tree of life, lest old age should dissolve him*. And in the book *On Questions of the Old and New Testament*, he says that *the tree of life, in the manner of a medicine, prevented the corruption of men*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0079","text":"Nevertheless, it did not cause immortality simply. For neither was the power that was in the soul for preserving the body caused by the tree of life, nor could it bestow upon the body a disposition of immortality such that it could never be dissolved. This is clear from the fact that the power of any body whatsoever is finite. Hence the power of the tree of life could not extend so far as to give the body the power of enduring for an infinite time, but only up to a determinate time. For it is manifest that the greater any power is, the more lasting an effect it impresses. Hence, since the power of the tree of life was finite, when taken once it preserved one from corruption up to a determinate time; when that time was completed, either man would have been transferred to spiritual life, or he would have needed to take again from the tree of life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0080","text":"[I q. 97 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32581)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0081","text":"And through this the answer to the objections is clear. For the first arguments conclude that it did not cause incorruptibility simply. But the others conclude that it caused incorruptibility by preventing corruption, according to the aforesaid manner.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_89:P0081"]}],"source_digest":"355d415f9338d7800c0599eeb2e32793c42849a4c1b13977a9b5b9b110cc8288","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 97"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 97","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90","title":"First Part · Question 98","order":99,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_90","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 98 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32582)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de his quae pertinent ad conservationem speciei. Et primo, de ipsa generatione; secundo, de conditione prolis genitae. Circa primum quaeruntur duo. Primo, utrum in statu innocentiae fuisset generatio. Secundo, utrum fuisset generatio per coitum.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 98 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32583)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in statu innocentiae non fuisset generatio. Generationi enim corruptio est contraria, ut dicitur in V Physic. Contraria autem sunt circa idem. In statu autem innocentiae non fuisset corruptio. Ergo neque generatio.\n\n[I q. 98 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32584)\n\nPraeterea, generatio ordinatur ad hoc quod conservetur in specie quod secundum individuum conservari non potest, unde et in illis individuis quae in perpetuum durant, generatio non invenitur. Sed in statu innocentiae homo in perpetuum absque morte vixisset. Ergo in statu innocentiae generatio, non fuisset.\n\n[I q. 98 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32585)\n\nPraeterea, per generationem homines multiplicantur. Sed multiplicatis dominis, necesse est fieri possessionum divisionem, ad evitandam confusionem dominii. Ergo, cum homo sit institutus dominus animalium, facta multiplicatione humani generis per generationem, secuta fuisset divisio dominii. Quod videtur esse contrarium iuri naturali, secundum quod omnia sunt communia, ut Isidorus dicit. Non ergo fuisset generatio in statu innocentiae.\n\n[I q. 98 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32586)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *crescite et multiplicamini, et replete terram*. Huiusmodi autem multiplicatio absque nova generatione fieri non potuisset, cum duo tantum fuerint primitus instituti. Ergo in primo statu generatio fuisset.\n\n[I q. 98 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32587)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in statu innocentiae fuisset generatio ad multiplicationem humani generis, alioquin peccatum hominis fuisset valde necessarium, ex quo tantum bonum consecutum est. Est ergo considerandum quod homo, secundum suam naturam, est constitutus quasi medium quoddam inter creaturas corruptibiles et incorruptibiles, nam anima eius est naturaliter incorruptibilis, corpus vero naturaliter corruptibile. Est autem considerandum quod alio modo intentio naturae fertur ad corruptibiles, et incorruptibiles creaturas. Id enim per se videtur esse de intentione naturae, quod est semper et perpetuum. Quod autem est solum secundum aliquod tempus, non videtur esse principaliter de intentione naturae, sed quasi ad aliud ordinatum, alioquin, eo corrupto, naturae intentio cassaretur. Quia igitur in rebus corruptibilibus nihil est perpetuum et semper manens nisi species, bonum speciei est de principali intentione naturae, ad cuius conservationem naturalis generatio ordinatur. Substantiae vero incorruptibiles manent semper non solum secundum speciem, sed etiam secundum individua, et ideo etiam ipsa individua sunt de principali intentione naturae. Sic igitur homini ex parte corporis, quod corruptibile est secundum naturam suam, competit generatio. Ex parte vero animae, quae incorruptibilis est, competit ei quod multitudo individuorum sit per se intenta a natura, vel potius a naturae auctore, qui solus est humanarum animarum creator. Et ideo, ad multiplicationem humani generis, generationem in humano genere statuit, etiam in statu innocentiae.\n\n[I q. 98 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32588)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod corpus hominis in statu innocentiae, quantum erat de se, corruptibile erat, sed potuit praeservari a corruptione per animam. Et ideo non fuit homini subtrahenda generatio, quae debetur corruptibilibus rebus.\n\n[I q. 98 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32589)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod generatio in statu innocentiae, etsi non fuisset propter conservationem speciei, fuisset tamen propter multiplicationem individuorum.\n\n[I q. 98 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32590)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in statu isto, multiplicatis dominis, necesse est fieri divisionem possessionum, quia communitas possessionis est occasio discordiae, ut philosophus dicit in II Politic. Sed in statu innocentiae fuissent voluntates hominum sic ordinatae, quod absque omni periculo discordiae communiter usi fuissent, secundum quod unicuique eorum competeret, rebus quae eorum dominio subdebantur, cum hoc etiam modo apud multos bonos viros observetur.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 98 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32591)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in statu innocentiae non fuisset generatio per coitum. Quia, ut Damascenus dicit, primus homo erat in Paradiso terrestri sicut Angelus quidam. Sed in futuro resurrectionis statu, quando erunt homines Angelis similes, neque nubent neque nubentur, ut dicitur Matth. XXII. Ergo neque in Paradiso fuisset generatio per coitum.\n\n[I q. 98 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32592)\n\nPraeterea, primi homines in perfecta aetate conditi fuerunt. Si igitur in eis ante peccatum generatio fuisset per coitum, fuissent etiam in Paradiso carnaliter coniuncti. Quod patet esse falsum, secundum Scripturam.\n\n[I q. 98 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32593)\n\nPraeterea, in coniunctione carnali maxime efficitur homo similis bestiis, propter vehementiam delectationis, unde etiam continentia laudatur, per quam homines ab huiusmodi delectationibus abstinent. Sed bestiis homo comparatur propter peccatum, secundum illud Psalmi XLVIII, *homo cum in honore esset, non intellexit, comparatus est iumentis insipientibus, et similis factus est illis*. Ergo ante peccatum non fuisset maris et feminae carnalis coniunctio.\n\n[I q. 98 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32594)\n\nPraeterea, in statu innocentiae nulla fuisset corruptio. Sed per coitum corrumpitur integritas virginalis. Ergo coitus in statu innocentiae non fuisset.\n\n[I q. 98 a. 2 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32595)\n\nSed contra est quod Deus ante peccatum masculum et feminam fecit, ut dicitur Gen. I et II. Nihil autem est frustra in operibus Dei. Ergo etiam si homo non peccasset, fuisset coitus, ad quem distinctio sexuum ordinatur.\n\n[I q. 98 a. 2 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32596)\n\nPraeterea, Gen. II, dicitur mulier esse facta in adiutorium viri. Sed non ad aliud nisi ad generationem quae fit per coitum, quia ad quodlibet aliud opus, convenientius adiuvari posset vir per virum quam per feminam. Ergo etiam in statu innocentiae fuisset generatio per coitum.\n\n[I q. 98 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32597)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam antiquorum doctorum, considerantes concupiscentiae foeditatem quae invenitur in coitu in isto statu, posuerunt quod in statu innocentiae non fuisset generatio per coitum. Unde Gregorius Nyssenus dicit in libro quem fecit de homine, quod in Paradiso aliter fuisset multiplicatum genus humanum, sicut multiplicati sunt Angeli, absque concubitu, per operationem divinae virtutis. Et dicit quod Deus ante peccatum fecit masculum et feminam, respiciens ad modum generationis qui futurus erat post peccatum, cuius Deus praescius erat. Sed hoc non dicitur rationabiliter. Ea enim quae sunt naturalia homini, neque subtrahuntur neque dantur homini per peccatum. Manifestum est autem quod homini, secundum animalem vitam, quam etiam ante peccatum habebat, ut supra dictum est, naturale est generare per coitum, sicut et ceteris animalibus perfectis. Et hoc declarant naturalia membra ad hunc usum deputata. Et ideo non est dicendum quod usus horum membrorum naturalium non fuisset ante peccatum, sicut et ceterorum membrorum. Sunt igitur in coitu duo consideranda, secundum praesentem statum. Unum quod naturae est, scilicet coniunctio maris et feminae ad generandum. In omni enim generatione requiritur virtus activa et passiva. Unde, cum in omnibus in quibus est distinctio sexuum, virtus activa sit in mare, virtus vero passiva in femina; naturae ordo exigit ut ad generandum conveniant per coitum mas et femina. Aliud autem quod considerari potest, est quaedam deformitas immoderatae concupiscentiae. Quae in statu innocentiae non fuisset quando inferiores vires omnino rationi subdebantur. Unde Augustinus dicit, in XIV de Civ. Dei, *absit ut suspicemur non potuisse prolem seri sine libidinis morbo. Sed eo voluntatis nutu moverentur illa membra quo cetera, et sine ardore et illecebroso stimulo, cum tranquillitate animi et corporis*.\n\n[I q. 98 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32598)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod homo in Paradiso fuisset sicut Angelus per spiritualem mentem, cum tamen haberet, vitam animalem quantum ad corpus. Sed post resurrectionem erit homo similis Angelo, spiritualis effectus et secundum animam et secundum corpus. Unde non est similis ratio.\n\n[I q. 98 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32599)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit IX super Gen. ad Litt., ideo primi parentes in Paradiso non coierunt, quia, formata muliere, post modicum propter peccatum de Paradiso eiecti sunt, vel quia expectabatur divina auctoritas ad determinatum tempus commixtionis, a qua acceperunt universale mandatum.\n\n[I q. 98 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32600)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod bestiae carent ratione. Unde secundum hoc homo in coitu bestialis efficitur, quod delectationem coitus et fervorem concupiscentiae ratione moderari non potest. Sed in statu innocentiae nihil huiusmodi fuisset quod ratione non moderaretur, non quia esset minor delectatio secundum sensum, ut quidam dicunt (fuisset enim tanto maior delectatio sensibilis, quanto esset purior natura, et corpus magis sensibile); sed quia vis concupiscibilis non ita inordinate se effudisset super huiusmodi delectatione, regulata per rationem, ad quam non pertinet ut sit minor delectatio in sensu, sed ut vis concupiscibilis non immoderate delectationi inhaereat; et dico immoderate, praeter mensuram rationis. Sicut sobrius in cibo moderate assumpto non minorem habet delectationem quam gulosus; sed minus eius concupiscibilis super huiusmodi delectatione requiescit. Et hoc sonant verba Augustini, quae a statu innocentiae non excludunt magnitudinem delectationis, sed ardorem libidinis et inquietudinem animi. Et ideo continentia in statu innocentiae non fuisset laudabilis, quae in tempore isto laudatur non propter defectum fecunditatis, sed propter remotionem inordinatae libidinis. Tunc autem fuisset fecunditas absque libidine.\n\n[I q. 98 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32601)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit XIV de Civ. Dei, *in illo statu nulla corruptione integritatis infunderetur gremio maritus uxoris. Ita enim potuit utero coniugis, salva integritate feminei genitalis, virile semen immitti, sicut nunc potest, eadem integritate salva, ex utero virginis fluxus menstrui cruoris emitti. Ut enim ad pariendum non doloris gemitus, sed maturitatis impulsus feminea viscera relaxaret; sic ad concipiendum non libidinis appetitus, sed voluntarius usus naturam utramque coniungeret*.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0002","text":"[I q. 98 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32582)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de his quae pertinent ad conservationem speciei. Et primo, de ipsa generatione; secundo, de conditione prolis genitae. Circa primum quaeruntur duo. Primo, utrum in statu innocentiae fuisset generatio. Secundo, utrum fuisset generatio per coitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0005","text":"[I q. 98 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32583)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in statu innocentiae non fuisset generatio. Generationi enim corruptio est contraria, ut dicitur in V Physic. Contraria autem sunt circa idem. In statu autem innocentiae non fuisset corruptio. Ergo neque generatio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0007","text":"[I q. 98 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32584)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0008","text":"Praeterea, generatio ordinatur ad hoc quod conservetur in specie quod secundum individuum conservari non potest, unde et in illis individuis quae in perpetuum durant, generatio non invenitur. Sed in statu innocentiae homo in perpetuum absque morte vixisset. Ergo in statu innocentiae generatio, non fuisset.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0009","text":"[I q. 98 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32585)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0010","text":"Praeterea, per generationem homines multiplicantur. Sed multiplicatis dominis, necesse est fieri possessionum divisionem, ad evitandam confusionem dominii. Ergo, cum homo sit institutus dominus animalium, facta multiplicatione humani generis per generationem, secuta fuisset divisio dominii. Quod videtur esse contrarium iuri naturali, secundum quod omnia sunt communia, ut Isidorus dicit. Non ergo fuisset generatio in statu innocentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0011","text":"[I q. 98 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32586)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. I, *crescite et multiplicamini, et replete terram*. Huiusmodi autem multiplicatio absque nova generatione fieri non potuisset, cum duo tantum fuerint primitus instituti. Ergo in primo statu generatio fuisset.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0013","text":"[I q. 98 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32587)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in statu innocentiae fuisset generatio ad multiplicationem humani generis, alioquin peccatum hominis fuisset valde necessarium, ex quo tantum bonum consecutum est. Est ergo considerandum quod homo, secundum suam naturam, est constitutus quasi medium quoddam inter creaturas corruptibiles et incorruptibiles, nam anima eius est naturaliter incorruptibilis, corpus vero naturaliter corruptibile. Est autem considerandum quod alio modo intentio naturae fertur ad corruptibiles, et incorruptibiles creaturas. Id enim per se videtur esse de intentione naturae, quod est semper et perpetuum. Quod autem est solum secundum aliquod tempus, non videtur esse principaliter de intentione naturae, sed quasi ad aliud ordinatum, alioquin, eo corrupto, naturae intentio cassaretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0015","text":"Quia igitur in rebus corruptibilibus nihil est perpetuum et semper manens nisi species, bonum speciei est de principali intentione naturae, ad cuius conservationem naturalis generatio ordinatur. Substantiae vero incorruptibiles manent semper non solum secundum speciem, sed etiam secundum individua, et ideo etiam ipsa individua sunt de principali intentione naturae. Sic igitur homini ex parte corporis, quod corruptibile est secundum naturam suam, competit generatio. Ex parte vero animae, quae incorruptibilis est, competit ei quod multitudo individuorum sit per se intenta a natura, vel potius a naturae auctore, qui solus est humanarum animarum creator. Et ideo, ad multiplicationem humani generis, generationem in humano genere statuit, etiam in statu innocentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0016","text":"[I q. 98 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32588)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod corpus hominis in statu innocentiae, quantum erat de se, corruptibile erat, sed potuit praeservari a corruptione per animam. Et ideo non fuit homini subtrahenda generatio, quae debetur corruptibilibus rebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0018","text":"[I q. 98 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32589)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod generatio in statu innocentiae, etsi non fuisset propter conservationem speciei, fuisset tamen propter multiplicationem individuorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0020","text":"[I q. 98 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32590)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in statu isto, multiplicatis dominis, necesse est fieri divisionem possessionum, quia communitas possessionis est occasio discordiae, ut philosophus dicit in II Politic. Sed in statu innocentiae fuissent voluntates hominum sic ordinatae, quod absque omni periculo discordiae communiter usi fuissent, secundum quod unicuique eorum competeret, rebus quae eorum dominio subdebantur, cum hoc etiam modo apud multos bonos viros observetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0023","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32591)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in statu innocentiae non fuisset generatio per coitum. Quia, ut Damascenus dicit, primus homo erat in Paradiso terrestri sicut Angelus quidam. Sed in futuro resurrectionis statu, quando erunt homines Angelis similes, neque nubent neque nubentur, ut dicitur Matth. XXII. Ergo neque in Paradiso fuisset generatio per coitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0025","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32592)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0026","text":"Praeterea, primi homines in perfecta aetate conditi fuerunt. Si igitur in eis ante peccatum generatio fuisset per coitum, fuissent etiam in Paradiso carnaliter coniuncti. Quod patet esse falsum, secundum Scripturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0027","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32593)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0028","text":"Praeterea, in coniunctione carnali maxime efficitur homo similis bestiis, propter vehementiam delectationis, unde etiam continentia laudatur, per quam homines ab huiusmodi delectationibus abstinent. Sed bestiis homo comparatur propter peccatum, secundum illud Psalmi XLVIII, *homo cum in honore esset, non intellexit, comparatus est iumentis insipientibus, et similis factus est illis*. Ergo ante peccatum non fuisset maris et feminae carnalis coniunctio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0029","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32594)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0030","text":"Praeterea, in statu innocentiae nulla fuisset corruptio. Sed per coitum corrumpitur integritas virginalis. Ergo coitus in statu innocentiae non fuisset.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0031","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32595)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0032","text":"Sed contra est quod Deus ante peccatum masculum et feminam fecit, ut dicitur Gen. I et II. Nihil autem est frustra in operibus Dei. Ergo etiam si homo non peccasset, fuisset coitus, ad quem distinctio sexuum ordinatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0033","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32596)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0034","text":"Praeterea, Gen. II, dicitur mulier esse facta in adiutorium viri. Sed non ad aliud nisi ad generationem quae fit per coitum, quia ad quodlibet aliud opus, convenientius adiuvari posset vir per virum quam per feminam. Ergo etiam in statu innocentiae fuisset generatio per coitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0035","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32597)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0036","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam antiquorum doctorum, considerantes concupiscentiae foeditatem quae invenitur in coitu in isto statu, posuerunt quod in statu innocentiae non fuisset generatio per coitum. Unde Gregorius Nyssenus dicit in libro quem fecit de homine, quod in Paradiso aliter fuisset multiplicatum genus humanum, sicut multiplicati sunt Angeli, absque concubitu, per operationem divinae virtutis. Et dicit quod Deus ante peccatum fecit masculum et feminam, respiciens ad modum generationis qui futurus erat post peccatum, cuius Deus praescius erat. Sed hoc non dicitur rationabiliter. Ea enim quae sunt naturalia homini, neque subtrahuntur neque dantur homini per peccatum. Manifestum est autem quod homini, secundum animalem vitam, quam etiam ante peccatum habebat, ut supra dictum est, naturale est generare per coitum, sicut et ceteris animalibus perfectis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0037","text":"Et hoc declarant naturalia membra ad hunc usum deputata. Et ideo non est dicendum quod usus horum membrorum naturalium non fuisset ante peccatum, sicut et ceterorum membrorum. Sunt igitur in coitu duo consideranda, secundum praesentem statum. Unum quod naturae est, scilicet coniunctio maris et feminae ad generandum. In omni enim generatione requiritur virtus activa et passiva. Unde, cum in omnibus in quibus est distinctio sexuum, virtus activa sit in mare, virtus vero passiva in femina; naturae ordo exigit ut ad generandum conveniant per coitum mas et femina. Aliud autem quod considerari potest, est quaedam deformitas immoderatae concupiscentiae. Quae in statu innocentiae non fuisset quando inferiores vires omnino rationi subdebantur. Unde Augustinus dicit, in XIV de Civ. Dei, *absit ut suspicemur non potuisse prolem seri sine libidinis morbo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0038","text":"Sed eo voluntatis nutu moverentur illa membra quo cetera, et sine ardore et illecebroso stimulo, cum tranquillitate animi et corporis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0039","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32598)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0040","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod homo in Paradiso fuisset sicut Angelus per spiritualem mentem, cum tamen haberet, vitam animalem quantum ad corpus. Sed post resurrectionem erit homo similis Angelo, spiritualis effectus et secundum animam et secundum corpus. Unde non est similis ratio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0041","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32599)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0042","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit IX super Gen. ad Litt., ideo primi parentes in Paradiso non coierunt, quia, formata muliere, post modicum propter peccatum de Paradiso eiecti sunt, vel quia expectabatur divina auctoritas ad determinatum tempus commixtionis, a qua acceperunt universale mandatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0043","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32600)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0044","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod bestiae carent ratione. Unde secundum hoc homo in coitu bestialis efficitur, quod delectationem coitus et fervorem concupiscentiae ratione moderari non potest. Sed in statu innocentiae nihil huiusmodi fuisset quod ratione non moderaretur, non quia esset minor delectatio secundum sensum, ut quidam dicunt (fuisset enim tanto maior delectatio sensibilis, quanto esset purior natura, et corpus magis sensibile); sed quia vis concupiscibilis non ita inordinate se effudisset super huiusmodi delectatione, regulata per rationem, ad quam non pertinet ut sit minor delectatio in sensu, sed ut vis concupiscibilis non immoderate delectationi inhaereat; et dico immoderate, praeter mensuram rationis. Sicut sobrius in cibo moderate assumpto non minorem habet delectationem quam gulosus; sed minus eius concupiscibilis super huiusmodi delectatione requiescit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0045","text":"Et hoc sonant verba Augustini, quae a statu innocentiae non excludunt magnitudinem delectationis, sed ardorem libidinis et inquietudinem animi. Et ideo continentia in statu innocentiae non fuisset laudabilis, quae in tempore isto laudatur non propter defectum fecunditatis, sed propter remotionem inordinatae libidinis. Tunc autem fuisset fecunditas absque libidine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0046","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32601)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0047","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit XIV de Civ. Dei, *in illo statu nulla corruptione integritatis infunderetur gremio maritus uxoris. Ita enim potuit utero coniugis, salva integritate feminei genitalis, virile semen immitti, sicut nunc potest, eadem integritate salva, ex utero virginis fluxus menstrui cruoris emitti. Ut enim ad pariendum non doloris gemitus, sed maturitatis impulsus feminea viscera relaxaret; sic ad concipiendum non libidinis appetitus, sed voluntarius usus naturam utramque coniungeret*.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"eda302ced6a9f1ab58b9bd5dfa81ff36e9d62b9f22d468090f3d7c964d5e11b2","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T153217619843Z_759a54b1","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 98","source_sha256":"eda302ced6a9f1ab58b9bd5dfa81ff36e9d62b9f22d468090f3d7c964d5e11b2","sha256":"016c30487af944a1e49674caa2e7d558e98d2073343b965b1ae2ad00932ba57e","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:32:17.619858+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0002","text":"[I q. 98 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32582)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider those things that pertain to the preservation of the species. First, generation itself; second, the condition of the offspring generated. Concerning the first, two questions are asked. First, whether there would have been generation in the state of innocence. Second, whether there would have been generation through intercourse.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0005","text":"[I q. 98 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32583)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that there would not have been generation in the state of innocence. For corruption is contrary to generation, as is said in Physics V. But contraries concern the same thing. Now, in the state of innocence there would have been no corruption. Therefore, neither generation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0007","text":"[I q. 98 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32584)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0008","text":"Further, generation is ordered to the preservation in the species of what cannot be preserved according to the individual; hence generation is not found in those individuals that endure perpetually. But in the state of innocence man would have lived perpetually without death. Therefore, there would have been no generation in the state of innocence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0009","text":"[I q. 98 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32585)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0010","text":"Further, through generation human beings are multiplied. But when masters are multiplied, a division of possessions must come about, in order to avoid confusion of ownership. Therefore, since man was established as master of the animals, once the human race had been multiplied through generation, a division of dominion would have followed. This seems to be contrary to natural right, according to which all things are common, as Isidore says. Therefore, there would not have been generation in the state of innocence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0011","text":"[I q. 98 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32586)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Gen. I, *increase and multiply, and fill the earth*. But such multiplication could not have occurred without new generation, since only two were established at first. Therefore, in the first state there would have been generation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0013","text":"[I q. 98 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32587)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that in the state of innocence there would have been generation for the multiplication of the human race; otherwise, man's sin would have been very necessary, from which so great a good has followed. Therefore, it must be considered that man, according to his nature, is constituted as a kind of mean between corruptible and incorruptible creatures: for his soul is naturally incorruptible, but his body naturally corruptible. It must also be considered that the intention of nature is directed in one way toward corruptible creatures and in another toward incorruptible creatures. For that which is always and perpetual seems to belong per se to the intention of nature. But that which exists only for some time does not seem to belong principally to the intention of nature, but to be ordered, as it were, to something else; otherwise, when it was corrupted, nature's intention would fail.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0015","text":"Therefore, because among corruptible things nothing is perpetual and always abiding except the species, the good of the species belongs to the principal intention of nature, and natural generation is ordered to its preservation. But incorruptible substances remain always not only according to the species, but also according to individuals; and therefore the individuals themselves also belong to the principal intention of nature. Thus, then, on the part of his body, which is corruptible according to its nature, generation belongs to man. But on the part of his soul, which is incorruptible, it belongs to him that the multitude of individuals be intended per se by nature, or rather by the author of nature, who alone is the creator of human souls. And therefore, for the multiplication of the human race, He established generation in the human race, even in the state of innocence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0016","text":"[I q. 98 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32588)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the body of man in the state of innocence, as far as it was concerned, was corruptible, but it could be preserved from corruption by the soul. And therefore generation, which is due to corruptible things, was not to be taken away from man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0018","text":"[I q. 98 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32589)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0019","text":"To the second, it must be said that generation in the state of innocence, although it would not have been for the preservation of the species, would nevertheless have been for the multiplication of individuals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0020","text":"[I q. 98 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32590)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0021","text":"To the third, it must be said that in the present state, when masters are multiplied, a division of possessions must come about, because community of possessions is an occasion of discord, as the Philosopher says in Politics II. But in the state of innocence, human wills would have been so ordered that, without any danger of discord, they would have used in common, according as it befitted each of them, the things subjected to their dominion, since this too is observed in this way among many good men.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0023","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32591)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0024","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that there would not have been generation through intercourse in the state of innocence. For, as Damascene says, the first man was in the earthly Paradise as a certain angel. But in the future state of resurrection, when human beings will be like angels, they will neither marry nor be given in marriage, as is said in Matt. XXII. Therefore, neither would there have been generation through intercourse in Paradise.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0025","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32592)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0026","text":"Moreover, the first human beings were created at a perfect age. Therefore, if generation through intercourse had existed in them before sin, they would also have been carnally joined in Paradise. But this is clearly false, according to Scripture.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0027","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32593)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0028","text":"Moreover, in carnal union a human being is made most like the beasts, on account of the intensity of pleasure; whence continence too is praised, by which human beings abstain from pleasures of this kind. But man is compared to beasts on account of sin, according to that verse of Psalm XLVIII: *man, when he was in honor, did not understand; he was compared to senseless beasts, and became like them*. Therefore, before sin there would have been no carnal union of male and female.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0029","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32594)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0030","text":"Moreover, in the state of innocence there would have been no corruption. But through intercourse virginal integrity is corrupted. Therefore, intercourse would not have existed in the state of innocence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0031","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 s. c. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32595)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0032","text":"On the contrary, God made male and female before sin, as is said in Genesis I and II. But nothing is without purpose in the works of God. Therefore, even if man had not sinned, there would have been intercourse, to which the distinction of the sexes is ordered.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0033","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 s. c. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32596)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0034","text":"Moreover, in Genesis II, the woman is said to have been made as a helper for the man. But not for anything other than generation, which takes place through intercourse; because for any other work, a man could be helped more suitably by a man than by a woman. Therefore, even in the state of innocence there would have been generation through intercourse.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0035","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32597)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0036","text":"I answer that certain ancient doctors, considering the foulness of concupiscence found in intercourse in the present state, held that in the state of innocence there would not have been generation through intercourse. Hence Gregory of Nyssa says, in the book which he wrote on man, that in Paradise the human race would have been multiplied otherwise, just as the angels were multiplied, without sexual union, through the operation of divine power. And he says that God made male and female before sin, having regard to the mode of generation that was to be after sin, of which God had foreknowledge. But this is not said reasonably. For those things that are natural to man are neither taken away from nor given to man through sin. Now it is manifest that for man, according to animal life, which he also had before sin, as was said above, it is natural to generate through intercourse, as also for the other perfect animals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0037","text":"And the natural members assigned to this use make this clear. And therefore it should not be said that the use of these natural members would not have existed before sin, any more than that of the other members. Thus, in intercourse, according to the present state, two things are to be considered. One is that which belongs to nature, namely, the union of male and female for generation. For in every generation an active and a passive power are required. Hence, since in all things in which there is a distinction of the sexes, the active power is in the male and the passive power in the female, the order of nature requires that male and female come together through intercourse in order to generate. But another thing that may be considered is a certain deformity of immoderate concupiscence. This would not have existed in the state of innocence, when the lower powers were wholly subject to reason. Hence Augustine says, in *The City of God*, XIV: *Far be it from us to suspect that offspring could not have been sown without the disease of lust.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0038","text":"But those members would have been moved by the prompting of the will by which the others are moved, and without ardor and enticing stimulus, with tranquility of mind and body*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0039","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32598)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0040","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that in Paradise man would have been like an angel by his spiritual mind, while nevertheless having animal life as regards his body. But after the resurrection man will be like an angel, having become spiritual both as regards soul and as regards body. Hence the reasoning is not the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0041","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32599)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0042","text":"To the second, it must be said that, as Augustine says in IX *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, the first parents did not have intercourse in Paradise because, after the woman had been formed, they were cast out of Paradise shortly afterward on account of sin; or because divine authority was awaited for the determined time of intercourse, from which they had received the universal command.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0043","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32600)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0044","text":"To the third, it must be said that beasts lack reason. Hence man becomes beastly in intercourse in this respect: that he cannot moderate by reason the pleasure of intercourse and the ardor of concupiscence. But in the state of innocence there would have been nothing of this sort that was not moderated by reason—not because there would have been less pleasure according to sense, as some say (for sensible pleasure would have been so much greater as nature was purer and the body more sensitive), but because the concupiscible power would not have poured itself out so inordinately upon such pleasure, being regulated by reason. It does not belong to reason that there be less pleasure in sense, but that the concupiscible power not cling immoderately to pleasure; and I say immoderately, beyond the measure of reason. Just as a sober person, in food moderately taken, has no less pleasure than a glutton; but his concupiscible power rests less upon such pleasure.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0045","text":"And this is what Augustine's words signify: they do not exclude from the state of innocence greatness of pleasure, but the ardor of lust and the restlessness of the mind. And therefore continence would not have been praiseworthy in the state of innocence; it is praised in the present time not because of a lack of fecundity, but because of the removal of disordered lust. But then there would have been fecundity without lust.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0046","text":"[I q. 98 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32601)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0047","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, as Augustine says in *The City of God*, XIV: *In that state no corruption of integrity would be inflicted upon the wife's womb by her husband. For the male seed could have been introduced into the womb of the spouse, with the integrity of the female genital preserved, just as now, with that same integrity preserved, the flow of menstrual blood can be emitted from the womb of a virgin. For just as, for giving birth, not groans of pain but the impulse of maturity would relax the female organs; so, for conceiving, not the appetite of lust but voluntary use would join both natures*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_90:P0047"]}],"source_digest":"71547bdd0edef7e08043e4452367ef7c45ecef28bc65828bec25228f1cffe177","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 98"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 98","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91","title":"First Part · Question 99","order":100,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_91","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 99 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32602)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de conditione prolis generandae. Et primo, quantum ad corpus; secundo, quantum ad iustitiam; tertio, quantum ad scientiam. Circa primum quaeruntur duo. Primo, utrum in statu innocentiae pueri mox geniti habuissent perfectam virtutem corpoream. Secundo, utrum omnes fuissent nati in sexu masculino.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 99 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32603)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod pueri in statu innocentiae, mox nati, virtutem perfectam habuissent ad motum membrorum. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro de Bapt. Parvul., quod *infirmitati mentis congruit haec infirmitas corporis*, quae scilicet in pueris apparet. Sed in statu innocentiae nulla fuisset infirmitas mentis. Ergo neque talis infirmitas corporis fuisset in parvulis.\n\n[I q. 99 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32604)\n\nPraeterea, quaedam animalia statim cum nascuntur, habent virtutem sufficientem ad usum membrorum. Sed homo est nobilior aliis animalibus. Ergo multo magis est naturale homini quod statim natus virtutem habeat ad usum membrorum. Et ita videtur esse poena ex peccato consequens.\n\n[I q. 99 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32605)\n\nPraeterea, non posse consequi delectabile propositum, afflictionem inducit. Sed si pueri non habuissent virtutem ad movendum membra, frequenter accidisset quod non possent consequi aliquod delectabile eis propositum. Ergo fuisset in eis afflictio; quae non poterat esse ante peccatum. Non ergo in statu innocentiae defuisset pueris virtus ad movendum membra.\n\n[I q. 99 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32606)\n\nPraeterea, defectus senectutis videtur correspondere defectui pueritiae. Sed in statu innocentiae non fuisset defectus senectutis. Ergo neque etiam defectus pueritiae.\n\n[I q. 99 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32607)\n\nSed contra est quod omne generatum prius est imperfectum quam perficiatur. Sed pueri in statu innocentiae fuissent per generationem producti. Ergo a principio imperfecti fuissent et quantitate et virtute corporis.\n\n[I q. 99 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32608)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ea quae sunt supra naturam, sola fide tenemus; quod autem credimus, auctoritati debemus. Unde in omnibus asserendis sequi debemus naturam rerum, praeter ea quae auctoritate divina traduntur, quae sunt supra naturam. Manifestum est autem naturale hoc esse, utpote et principiis humanae naturae competens, quod pueri mox nati non habeant sufficientem virtutem ad movendum membra. Quia homo naturaliter habet cerebrum maius in quantitate, secundum proportionem sui corporis, quam cetera animalia. Unde naturale est quod, propter maximam humiditatem cerebri in pueris, nervi, qui sunt instrumenta motus, non sunt idonei ad movendum membra. Ex alia vero parte nulli Catholico dubium est quin divina virtute fieri possit, ut pueri mox nati perfectam virtutem habeant ad motum membrorum. Constat autem per auctoritatem Scripturae, quod Deus fecit hominem rectum, et haec rectitudo consistit, ut Augustinus dicit, in perfecta subiectione corporis ad animam. Sicut ergo in primo statu non poterat esse in membris hominis aliquid quod repugnaret ordinatae hominis voluntati, ita membra hominis deficere non poterant humanae voluntati. Voluntas autem hominis ordinata est quae tendit in actus sibi convenientes. Non sunt autem iidem actus convenientes homini secundum quamlibet aetatem. Dicendum est ergo quod pueri mox nati non habuissent sufficientem virtutem ad movendum membra ad quoslibet actus; sed ad actus pueritiae convenientes, puta ad sugendum ubera, et ad alia huiusmodi.\n\n[I q. 99 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32609)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus loquitur de ista infirmitate quae nunc in pueris apparet etiam quantum ad actus eorum pueritiae convenientes; ut patet per ea quae praemittit, quod *iuxta se iacentibus mammis, magis possunt esurientes flere quam sugere*.\n\n[I q. 99 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32610)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod hoc quod quaedam animalia statim nata habent usum membrorum, non est ex eorum nobilitate, cum quaedam animalia perfectiora hoc non habeant, sed hoc eis contingit ex siccitate cerebri, et quia actus proprii talium animalium sunt imperfecti, ad quos etiam parva virtus sufficere potest.\n\n[I q. 99 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32611)\n\nAd tertium patet solutio per ea quae dicta sunt in corpore. Vel potest dici quod nihil appetivissent, nisi ordinata voluntate convenisset res secundum statum suum.\n\n[I q. 99 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32612)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod homo in statu innocentiae generatus fuisset, sed non fuisset corruptus. Et ideo in statu illo potuissent esse aliqui defectus pueriles, qui consequuntur generationem; non autem defectus seniles, qui ordinantur ad corruptionem.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 99 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32613)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in primo statu feminae natae non fuissent. Dicit enim philosophus, in libro II de Generat. Animal., quod femina est mas occasionatus, quasi praeter intentionem naturae proveniens. Sed in statu illo nihil evenisset innaturale in hominis generatione. Ergo feminae natae non fuissent.\n\n[I q. 99 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32614)\n\nPraeterea, omne agens generat sibi simile, nisi impediatur vel propter defectum virtutis, vel propter indispositionem materiae, sicut parvus ignis non potest comburere ligna viridia. In generatione autem vis activa est in mare. Cum igitur in statu innocentiae nullus fuisset defectus virtutis ex parte maris, nec indispositio materiae ex parte feminae, videtur quod semper masculi nati fuissent.\n\n[I q. 99 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32615)\n\nPraeterea, in statu innocentiae generatio ad multiplicationem hominum ordinabatur. Sed sufficienter homines multiplicari potuissent per primum hominem et per primam feminam, ex quo in perpetuum victuri erant. Ergo non fuit necessarium quod in statu innocentiae feminae nascerentur.\n\n[I q. 99 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32616)\n\nSed contra est quod sic natura processisset in generando, sicut eam Deus instituit. Sed Deus instituit marem et feminam in natura humana, ut dicitur Gen. I et II. Ergo etiam in statu illo fuissent mares et feminae generati.\n\n[I q. 99 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32617)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod nihil eorum quae ad complementum humanae naturae pertinent, in statu innocentiae defuisset. Sicut autem ad perfectionem universi pertinent diversi gradus rerum, ita etiam diversitas sexus est ad perfectionem humanae naturae. Et ideo in statu innocentiae uterque sexus per generationem productus fuisset.\n\n[I q. 99 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32618)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod femina dicitur mas occasionatus, quia est praeter intentionem naturae particularis, non autem praeter intentionem naturae universalis, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 99 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32619)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod generatio feminae non solum contingit ex defectu virtutis activae vel indispositione materiae, ut obiectio tangit. Sed quandoque quidem ex aliquo accidenti extrinseco; sicut philosophus dicit, in libro de animalibus, quod *ventus Septentrionalis coadiuvat ad generationem masculorum, Australis vero ad generationem feminarum*. Quandoque etiam ex conceptione animae, ad quam de facili immutatur corpus. Et praecipue in statu innocentiae hoc esse poterat, quando corpus magis erat animae subiectum; ut scilicet secundum voluntatem generantis, distingueretur sexus in prole.\n\n[I q. 99 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32620)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod proles fuisset genita vivens vita animali, ad quam sicut pertinet alimento uti, sic etiam generare. Unde conveniebat quod omnes generarent, et non solum primi parentes. Ad quod consequens videtur quod tot fuissent generatae feminae, quot et mares.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0002","text":"[I q. 99 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32602)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de conditione prolis generandae. Et primo, quantum ad corpus; secundo, quantum ad iustitiam; tertio, quantum ad scientiam. Circa primum quaeruntur duo. Primo, utrum in statu innocentiae pueri mox geniti habuissent perfectam virtutem corpoream. Secundo, utrum omnes fuissent nati in sexu masculino.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0005","text":"[I q. 99 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32603)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod pueri in statu innocentiae, mox nati, virtutem perfectam habuissent ad motum membrorum. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro de Bapt. Parvul., quod *infirmitati mentis congruit haec infirmitas corporis*, quae scilicet in pueris apparet. Sed in statu innocentiae nulla fuisset infirmitas mentis. Ergo neque talis infirmitas corporis fuisset in parvulis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0007","text":"[I q. 99 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32604)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0008","text":"Praeterea, quaedam animalia statim cum nascuntur, habent virtutem sufficientem ad usum membrorum. Sed homo est nobilior aliis animalibus. Ergo multo magis est naturale homini quod statim natus virtutem habeat ad usum membrorum. Et ita videtur esse poena ex peccato consequens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0009","text":"[I q. 99 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32605)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0010","text":"Praeterea, non posse consequi delectabile propositum, afflictionem inducit. Sed si pueri non habuissent virtutem ad movendum membra, frequenter accidisset quod non possent consequi aliquod delectabile eis propositum. Ergo fuisset in eis afflictio; quae non poterat esse ante peccatum. Non ergo in statu innocentiae defuisset pueris virtus ad movendum membra.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0011","text":"[I q. 99 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32606)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0012","text":"Praeterea, defectus senectutis videtur correspondere defectui pueritiae. Sed in statu innocentiae non fuisset defectus senectutis. Ergo neque etiam defectus pueritiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0013","text":"[I q. 99 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32607)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0014","text":"Sed contra est quod omne generatum prius est imperfectum quam perficiatur. Sed pueri in statu innocentiae fuissent per generationem producti. Ergo a principio imperfecti fuissent et quantitate et virtute corporis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0015","text":"[I q. 99 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32608)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ea quae sunt supra naturam, sola fide tenemus; quod autem credimus, auctoritati debemus. Unde in omnibus asserendis sequi debemus naturam rerum, praeter ea quae auctoritate divina traduntur, quae sunt supra naturam. Manifestum est autem naturale hoc esse, utpote et principiis humanae naturae competens, quod pueri mox nati non habeant sufficientem virtutem ad movendum membra. Quia homo naturaliter habet cerebrum maius in quantitate, secundum proportionem sui corporis, quam cetera animalia. Unde naturale est quod, propter maximam humiditatem cerebri in pueris, nervi, qui sunt instrumenta motus, non sunt idonei ad movendum membra. Ex alia vero parte nulli Catholico dubium est quin divina virtute fieri possit, ut pueri mox nati perfectam virtutem habeant ad motum membrorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0017","text":"Constat autem per auctoritatem Scripturae, quod Deus fecit hominem rectum, et haec rectitudo consistit, ut Augustinus dicit, in perfecta subiectione corporis ad animam. Sicut ergo in primo statu non poterat esse in membris hominis aliquid quod repugnaret ordinatae hominis voluntati, ita membra hominis deficere non poterant humanae voluntati. Voluntas autem hominis ordinata est quae tendit in actus sibi convenientes. Non sunt autem iidem actus convenientes homini secundum quamlibet aetatem. Dicendum est ergo quod pueri mox nati non habuissent sufficientem virtutem ad movendum membra ad quoslibet actus; sed ad actus pueritiae convenientes, puta ad sugendum ubera, et ad alia huiusmodi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0018","text":"[I q. 99 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32609)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0019","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus loquitur de ista infirmitate quae nunc in pueris apparet etiam quantum ad actus eorum pueritiae convenientes; ut patet per ea quae praemittit, quod *iuxta se iacentibus mammis, magis possunt esurientes flere quam sugere*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0020","text":"[I q. 99 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32610)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0021","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod hoc quod quaedam animalia statim nata habent usum membrorum, non est ex eorum nobilitate, cum quaedam animalia perfectiora hoc non habeant, sed hoc eis contingit ex siccitate cerebri, et quia actus proprii talium animalium sunt imperfecti, ad quos etiam parva virtus sufficere potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0022","text":"[I q. 99 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32611)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0023","text":"Ad tertium patet solutio per ea quae dicta sunt in corpore. Vel potest dici quod nihil appetivissent, nisi ordinata voluntate convenisset res secundum statum suum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0024","text":"[I q. 99 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32612)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0025","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod homo in statu innocentiae generatus fuisset, sed non fuisset corruptus. Et ideo in statu illo potuissent esse aliqui defectus pueriles, qui consequuntur generationem; non autem defectus seniles, qui ordinantur ad corruptionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0026","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0027","text":"[I q. 99 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32613)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0028","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in primo statu feminae natae non fuissent. Dicit enim philosophus, in libro II de Generat. Animal., quod femina est mas occasionatus, quasi praeter intentionem naturae proveniens. Sed in statu illo nihil evenisset innaturale in hominis generatione. Ergo feminae natae non fuissent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0029","text":"[I q. 99 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32614)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0030","text":"Praeterea, omne agens generat sibi simile, nisi impediatur vel propter defectum virtutis, vel propter indispositionem materiae, sicut parvus ignis non potest comburere ligna viridia. In generatione autem vis activa est in mare. Cum igitur in statu innocentiae nullus fuisset defectus virtutis ex parte maris, nec indispositio materiae ex parte feminae, videtur quod semper masculi nati fuissent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0031","text":"[I q. 99 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32615)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0032","text":"Praeterea, in statu innocentiae generatio ad multiplicationem hominum ordinabatur. Sed sufficienter homines multiplicari potuissent per primum hominem et per primam feminam, ex quo in perpetuum victuri erant. Ergo non fuit necessarium quod in statu innocentiae feminae nascerentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0033","text":"[I q. 99 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32616)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0034","text":"Sed contra est quod sic natura processisset in generando, sicut eam Deus instituit. Sed Deus instituit marem et feminam in natura humana, ut dicitur Gen. I et II. Ergo etiam in statu illo fuissent mares et feminae generati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0035","text":"[I q. 99 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32617)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0036","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod nihil eorum quae ad complementum humanae naturae pertinent, in statu innocentiae defuisset. Sicut autem ad perfectionem universi pertinent diversi gradus rerum, ita etiam diversitas sexus est ad perfectionem humanae naturae. Et ideo in statu innocentiae uterque sexus per generationem productus fuisset.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0037","text":"[I q. 99 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32618)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0038","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod femina dicitur mas occasionatus, quia est praeter intentionem naturae particularis, non autem praeter intentionem naturae universalis, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0039","text":"[I q. 99 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32619)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0040","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod generatio feminae non solum contingit ex defectu virtutis activae vel indispositione materiae, ut obiectio tangit. Sed quandoque quidem ex aliquo accidenti extrinseco; sicut philosophus dicit, in libro de animalibus, quod *ventus Septentrionalis coadiuvat ad generationem masculorum, Australis vero ad generationem feminarum*. Quandoque etiam ex conceptione animae, ad quam de facili immutatur corpus. Et praecipue in statu innocentiae hoc esse poterat, quando corpus magis erat animae subiectum; ut scilicet secundum voluntatem generantis, distingueretur sexus in prole.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0041","text":"[I q. 99 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32620)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0042","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod proles fuisset genita vivens vita animali, ad quam sicut pertinet alimento uti, sic etiam generare. Unde conveniebat quod omnes generarent, et non solum primi parentes. Ad quod consequens videtur quod tot fuissent generatae feminae, quot et mares.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"ff587c4436cfe71e67a4032591ebc48720e7f71d161b835ae5314f68dc57d7d8","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T153241988106Z_26b3287d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 99","source_sha256":"ff587c4436cfe71e67a4032591ebc48720e7f71d161b835ae5314f68dc57d7d8","sha256":"3c4125afce3ee16ad55e6278756e0e8c99371460fc99ad618f66c2d89970e3e6","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:32:41.988123+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0002","text":"[I q. 99 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32602)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the condition of the offspring to be generated. First, as to the body; second, as to justice; third, as to knowledge. Concerning the first, two questions are asked. First, whether in the state of innocence children, immediately upon being born, would have had perfect bodily power. Second, whether all would have been born of the male sex.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0005","text":"[I q. 99 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32603)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that children in the state of innocence, immediately upon being born, would have had perfect power for the movement of their limbs. For Augustine says, in the book On the Baptism of Infants, that *this infirmity of body corresponds to infirmity of mind*, namely that which appears in children. But in the state of innocence there would have been no infirmity of mind. Therefore neither would there have been such bodily infirmity in little children.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0007","text":"[I q. 99 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32604)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0008","text":"Further, certain animals, immediately when they are born, have power sufficient for the use of their limbs. But man is nobler than other animals. Therefore it is much more natural to man that, immediately upon being born, he should have power for the use of his limbs. And thus this seems to be a punishment consequent upon sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0009","text":"[I q. 99 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32605)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0010","text":"Further, being unable to attain a pleasurable object proposed induces affliction. But if children had not had the power to move their limbs, it would frequently have happened that they could not attain some pleasurable object proposed to them. Therefore there would have been affliction in them; which could not have been before sin. Therefore, in the state of innocence, children would not have lacked the power to move their limbs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0011","text":"[I q. 99 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32606)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0012","text":"Further, the defect of old age seems to correspond to the defect of childhood. But in the state of innocence there would have been no defect of old age. Therefore neither would there have been a defect of childhood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0013","text":"[I q. 99 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32607)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0014","text":"On the contrary, everything generated is first imperfect before it is perfected. But children in the state of innocence would have been produced by generation. Therefore at the beginning they would have been imperfect both in bodily size and in power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0015","text":"[I q. 99 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32608)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0016","text":"I answer that those things which are above nature we hold by faith alone; but what we believe, we owe to authority. Hence, in all assertions we ought to follow the nature of things, except in those matters handed down by divine authority, which are above nature. Now it is manifestly natural, as being suited to the principles of human nature, that children immediately upon being born should not have power sufficient for moving their limbs. For man naturally has a brain greater in size, in proportion to his body, than other animals. Hence it is natural that, on account of the very great moisture of the brain in children, the nerves, which are instruments of movement, are not fit for moving the limbs. On the other hand, no Catholic doubts that by divine power it can come about that children immediately upon being born have perfect power for the movement of their limbs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0017","text":"But it is established by the authority of Scripture that God made man upright, and this uprightness consists, as Augustine says, in the perfect subjection of the body to the soul. Therefore, just as in the first state there could not be anything in the members of man that resisted the ordered will of man, so the members of man could not fail the human will. Now the will of man is ordered when it tends toward acts appropriate to itself. But the same acts are not appropriate to man at every age. Therefore it must be said that children immediately upon being born would not have had power sufficient to move their limbs for every sort of act, but for acts appropriate to childhood, for example, to suck the breasts, and other such acts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0018","text":"[I q. 99 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32609)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0019","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Augustine speaks of that infirmity which now appears in children even with respect to acts appropriate to their childhood; as is clear from what he prefaces, namely, that *with the breasts lying close beside them, they who are hungry are more able to cry than to suck*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0020","text":"[I q. 99 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32610)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0021","text":"To the second, it must be said that the fact that certain animals immediately upon being born have the use of their limbs does not arise from their nobility, since certain more perfect animals do not have this; rather, this happens to them because of the dryness of their brain, and because the proper acts of such animals are imperfect, for which even a small power can suffice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0022","text":"[I q. 99 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32611)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0023","text":"To the third, the solution is clear from what has been said in the body. Or it may be said that they would have desired nothing unless the thing suited their state according to an ordered will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0024","text":"[I q. 99 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32612)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0025","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that man would have been generated in the state of innocence, but would not have been corrupted. And therefore, in that state there could have been certain childish defects, which follow upon generation; but not senile defects, which are ordered to corruption.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0026","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0027","text":"[I q. 99 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32613)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0028","text":"The second point is treated thus. It seems that females would not have been born in the first state. For the Philosopher says, in book II of *On the Generation of Animals*, that a female is an accidental male, as though coming to be apart from nature's intention. But in that state nothing unnatural would have occurred in the generation of man. Therefore, females would not have been born.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0029","text":"[I q. 99 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32614)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0030","text":"Further, every agent generates something like itself, unless it is impeded either by a defect of power or by an indisposition of matter, just as a small fire cannot burn green wood. But in generation the active power is in the male. Therefore, since in the state of innocence there would have been no defect of power on the part of the male, nor indisposition of matter on the part of the female, it seems that males would always have been born.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0031","text":"[I q. 99 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32615)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0032","text":"Further, in the state of innocence generation was ordered to the multiplication of human beings. But human beings could have been sufficiently multiplied through the first man and the first woman, since they were to live forever. Therefore, it was not necessary that females should be born in the state of innocence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0033","text":"[I q. 99 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32616)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0034","text":"On the contrary, nature would have proceeded in generating as God had established it. But God established male and female in human nature, as is said in Genesis I and II. Therefore, in that state too males and females would have been generated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0035","text":"[I q. 99 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32617)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0036","text":"I answer that it must be said that nothing belonging to the completion of human nature would have been lacking in the state of innocence. Now, just as diverse grades of things belong to the perfection of the universe, so too diversity of sex belongs to the perfection of human nature. And therefore, in the state of innocence both sexes would have been produced through generation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0037","text":"[I q. 99 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32618)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0038","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that a female is called an accidental male because she is apart from the intention of particular nature, but not apart from the intention of universal nature, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0039","text":"[I q. 99 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32619)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0040","text":"To the second, it must be said that the generation of a female does not occur solely from a defect of the active power or from an indisposition of matter, as the objection suggests. But sometimes it occurs from some extrinsic accident; as the Philosopher says, in the book *On Animals*, that *the north wind assists in the generation of males, whereas the south wind assists in the generation of females*. Sometimes too it occurs from the conception of the soul, by which the body is easily changed. And especially in the state of innocence this could be so, when the body was more subject to the soul; namely, so that according to the will of the generator, sex might be distinguished in the offspring.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0041","text":"[I q. 99 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32620)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0042","text":"To the third, it must be said that offspring would have been begotten living an animal life, to which, just as using food belongs, so also does generating. Hence it was fitting that all should generate, and not only the first parents. From which it seems to follow that as many females as males would have been generated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_91:P0042"]}],"source_digest":"a9973bdcf158b34988b6bdf5d075c9bef8f709f0500f3d1c746668425c96f502","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 99"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 99","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92","title":"First Part · Question 100","order":101,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_92","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 100 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32621)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de conditione prolis generandae quantum ad iustitiam. Et circa hoc quaeruntur duo. Primo, utrum homines fuissent nati cum iustitia. Secundo, utrum nascerentur in iustitia confirmati.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 100 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32622)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homines non fuissent cum iustitia nati. Dicit enim Hugo de sancto Victore quod *primus homo ante peccatum generaret quidem filios sine peccato, sed non paternae iustitiae haeredes*.\n\n[I q. 100 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32623)\n\nPraeterea, iustitia est per gratiam, ut apostolus dicit ad Rom. V. Sed gratia non transfunditur, quia sic esset naturalis; sed a solo Deo infunditur. Ergo pueri cum iustitia nati non fuissent.\n\n[I q. 100 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32624)\n\nPraeterea, iustitia in anima est. Sed anima non est ex traduce. Ergo nec iustitia traducta fuisset a parentibus in filios.\n\n[I q. 100 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32625)\n\nSed contra est quod Anselmus dicit, in libro de conceptu Virg., *quod simul cum rationalem haberent animam, iusti essent quos generaret homo, si non peccaret*.\n\n[I q. 100 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32626)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod naturaliter homo generat sibi simile secundum speciem. Unde quaecumque accidentia consequuntur naturam speciei, in his necesse est quod filii parentibus similentur, nisi sit error in operatione naturae, qui in statu innocentiae non fuisset. In accidentibus autem individualibus non est necesse quod filii parentibus similentur. Iustitia autem originalis, in qua primus homo conditus fuit, fuit accidens naturae speciei, non quasi ex principiis speciei causatum, sed sicut quoddam donum divinitus datum toti naturae. Et hoc apparet, quia opposita sunt unius generis, peccatum autem originale, quod opponitur illi iustitiae, dicitur esse peccatum naturae; unde traducitur a parente in posteros. Et propter hoc etiam filii parentibus assimilati fuissent quantum ad originalem iustitiam.\n\n[I q. 100 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32627)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod verbum Hugonis est intelligendum non quantum ad habitum iustitiae, sed quantum ad executionem actus.\n\n[I q. 100 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32628)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod quidam dicunt quod pueri non fuissent nati cum iustitia gratuita, quae est merendi principium, sed cum iustitia originali. Sed cum radix originalis iustitiae, in cuius rectitudine factus est homo, consistat in subiectione supernaturali rationis ad Deum, quae est per gratiam gratum facientem, ut supra dictum est; necesse est dicere quod, si pueri nati fuissent in originali iustitia, quod etiam nati fuissent cum gratia; sicut et de primo homine supra diximus quod fuit cum gratia conditus. Non tamen fuisset propter hoc gratia naturalis, quia non fuisset transfusa per virtutem seminis, sed fuisset collata homini statim cum habuisset animam rationalem. Sicut etiam statim cum corpus est dispositum infunditur a Deo anima rationalis, quae tamen non est ex traduce.\n\n[I q. 100 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32629)\n\nUnde patet solutio ad tertium.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 100 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32630)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod pueri in statu innocentiae nati fuissent in iustitia confirmati. Dicit enim Gregorius, IV Moralium, super illud, *somno meo requiescerem etc., si parentem primum nulla putredo peccati corrumperet, nequaquam ex se filios Gehennae generaret; sed hi qui nunc per redemptorem salvandi sunt, soli ab illo electi nascerentur*. Ergo nascerentur omnes in iustitia confirmati.\n\n[I q. 100 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32631)\n\nPraeterea, Anselmus dicit, in libro cur Deus homo, quod *si primi parentes sic vixissent ut tentati non peccassent, ita confirmarentur cum omni propagine sua, ut ultra peccare non possent*. Ergo pueri nascerentur in iustitia confirmati.\n\n[I q. 100 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32632)\n\nPraeterea, bonum est potentius quam malum. Sed propter peccatum primi hominis consecuta est necessitas peccandi in his qui nascuntur ex eo. Ergo si primus homo in iustitia perstitisset, derivaretur ad posteros necessitas observandi iustitiam.\n\n[I q. 100 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32633)\n\nPraeterea, Angelus adhaerens Deo aliis peccantibus, statim est in iustitia confirmatus, ut ulterius peccare non posset. Ergo similiter et homo, si tentationi restitisset, confirmatus fuisset. Sed qualis ipse fuit, tales alios generasset. Ergo et eius filii confirmati in iustitia nascerentur.\n\n[I q. 100 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32634)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XIV de Civ. Dei, *tam felix universa esset humana societas si nec illi*, scilicet primi parentes, *malum quod in posteros traiicerent, nec quisquam ex stirpe eorum iniquitatem committeret, quae damnationem reciperet*. Ex quo datur intelligi quod, etiam si primi homines non peccassent, aliqui ex eorum stirpe potuissent iniquitatem committere. Non ergo nascerentur in iustitia confirmati.\n\n[I q. 100 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32635)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod non videtur possibile quod pueri in statu innocentiae nascerentur in iustitia confirmati. Manifestum est enim quod pueri in sua nativitate non habuissent plus perfectionis quam eorum parentes in statu generationis. Parentes autem, quandiu generassent, non fuissent confirmati in iustitia. Ex hoc enim creatura rationalis in iustitia confirmatur, quod efficitur beata per apertam Dei visionem, cui viso non potest non inhaerere, cum ipse sit ipsa essentia bonitatis, a qua nullus potest averti, cum nihil desideretur et ametur nisi sub ratione boni. Et hoc dico secundum legem communem, quia ex aliquo privilegio speciali secus accidere potest, sicut creditur de virgine matre Dei. Quam cito autem Adam ad illam beatitudinem pervenisset quod Deum per essentiam videret, efficeretur spiritualis et mente et corpore, et animalis vita cessaret, in qua sola generationis usus fuisset. Unde manifestum est quod parvuli non nascerentur in iustitia confirmati.\n\n[I q. 100 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32636)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, si Adam non peccasset, non generaret ex se filios Gehennae, ita scilicet quod ab ipso peccatum contraherent, quod est causa Gehennae. Possent tamen fieri filii Gehennae per liberum arbitrium peccando. Vel, si filii Gehennae non fierent per peccatum, hoc non esset propter hoc, quia essent in iustitia confirmati; sed propter divinam providentiam, per quam a peccato conservarentur immunes.\n\n[I q. 100 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32637)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Anselmus hoc non dicit asserendo, sed opinando. Quod patet ex ipso modo loquendi, cum dicit, videtur quod, si vixissent et cetera.\n\n[I q. 100 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32638)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ratio ista non est efficax, quamvis per eam Anselmus motus fuisse videatur, ut ex eius verbis apparet. Non enim sic per peccatum primi parentis eius posteri necessitatem peccandi incurrunt, ut ad iustitiam redire non possint, quod est tantum in damnatis. Unde nec ita necessitatem non peccandi transmisisset ad posteros, quod omnino peccare non possent, quod est tantum in beatis.\n\n[I q. 100 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32639)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod non est simile de homine et Angelo. Nam homo habet liberum arbitrium vertibile et ante electionem et post, non autem Angelus, sicut supra dictum est, cum de Angelis ageretur.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0002","text":"[I q. 100 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32621)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de conditione prolis generandae quantum ad iustitiam. Et circa hoc quaeruntur duo. Primo, utrum homines fuissent nati cum iustitia. Secundo, utrum nascerentur in iustitia confirmati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0005","text":"[I q. 100 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32622)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homines non fuissent cum iustitia nati. Dicit enim Hugo de sancto Victore quod *primus homo ante peccatum generaret quidem filios sine peccato, sed non paternae iustitiae haeredes*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0007","text":"[I q. 100 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32623)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0008","text":"Praeterea, iustitia est per gratiam, ut apostolus dicit ad Rom. V. Sed gratia non transfunditur, quia sic esset naturalis; sed a solo Deo infunditur. Ergo pueri cum iustitia nati non fuissent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0009","text":"[I q. 100 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32624)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0010","text":"Praeterea, iustitia in anima est. Sed anima non est ex traduce. Ergo nec iustitia traducta fuisset a parentibus in filios.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0011","text":"[I q. 100 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32625)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod Anselmus dicit, in libro de conceptu Virg., *quod simul cum rationalem haberent animam, iusti essent quos generaret homo, si non peccaret*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0013","text":"[I q. 100 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32626)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod naturaliter homo generat sibi simile secundum speciem. Unde quaecumque accidentia consequuntur naturam speciei, in his necesse est quod filii parentibus similentur, nisi sit error in operatione naturae, qui in statu innocentiae non fuisset. In accidentibus autem individualibus non est necesse quod filii parentibus similentur. Iustitia autem originalis, in qua primus homo conditus fuit, fuit accidens naturae speciei, non quasi ex principiis speciei causatum, sed sicut quoddam donum divinitus datum toti naturae. Et hoc apparet, quia opposita sunt unius generis, peccatum autem originale, quod opponitur illi iustitiae, dicitur esse peccatum naturae; unde traducitur a parente in posteros. Et propter hoc etiam filii parentibus assimilati fuissent quantum ad originalem iustitiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0015","text":"[I q. 100 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32627)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0016","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod verbum Hugonis est intelligendum non quantum ad habitum iustitiae, sed quantum ad executionem actus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0017","text":"[I q. 100 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32628)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0018","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod quidam dicunt quod pueri non fuissent nati cum iustitia gratuita, quae est merendi principium, sed cum iustitia originali. Sed cum radix originalis iustitiae, in cuius rectitudine factus est homo, consistat in subiectione supernaturali rationis ad Deum, quae est per gratiam gratum facientem, ut supra dictum est; necesse est dicere quod, si pueri nati fuissent in originali iustitia, quod etiam nati fuissent cum gratia; sicut et de primo homine supra diximus quod fuit cum gratia conditus. Non tamen fuisset propter hoc gratia naturalis, quia non fuisset transfusa per virtutem seminis, sed fuisset collata homini statim cum habuisset animam rationalem. Sicut etiam statim cum corpus est dispositum infunditur a Deo anima rationalis, quae tamen non est ex traduce.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0019","text":"[I q. 100 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32629)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0020","text":"Unde patet solutio ad tertium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0021","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0022","text":"[I q. 100 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32630)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0023","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod pueri in statu innocentiae nati fuissent in iustitia confirmati. Dicit enim Gregorius, IV Moralium, super illud, *somno meo requiescerem etc., si parentem primum nulla putredo peccati corrumperet, nequaquam ex se filios Gehennae generaret; sed hi qui nunc per redemptorem salvandi sunt, soli ab illo electi nascerentur*. Ergo nascerentur omnes in iustitia confirmati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0024","text":"[I q. 100 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32631)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0025","text":"Praeterea, Anselmus dicit, in libro cur Deus homo, quod *si primi parentes sic vixissent ut tentati non peccassent, ita confirmarentur cum omni propagine sua, ut ultra peccare non possent*. Ergo pueri nascerentur in iustitia confirmati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0026","text":"[I q. 100 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32632)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0027","text":"Praeterea, bonum est potentius quam malum. Sed propter peccatum primi hominis consecuta est necessitas peccandi in his qui nascuntur ex eo. Ergo si primus homo in iustitia perstitisset, derivaretur ad posteros necessitas observandi iustitiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0028","text":"[I q. 100 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32633)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0029","text":"Praeterea, Angelus adhaerens Deo aliis peccantibus, statim est in iustitia confirmatus, ut ulterius peccare non posset. Ergo similiter et homo, si tentationi restitisset, confirmatus fuisset. Sed qualis ipse fuit, tales alios generasset. Ergo et eius filii confirmati in iustitia nascerentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0030","text":"[I q. 100 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32634)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0031","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XIV de Civ. Dei, *tam felix universa esset humana societas si nec illi*, scilicet primi parentes, *malum quod in posteros traiicerent, nec quisquam ex stirpe eorum iniquitatem committeret, quae damnationem reciperet*. Ex quo datur intelligi quod, etiam si primi homines non peccassent, aliqui ex eorum stirpe potuissent iniquitatem committere. Non ergo nascerentur in iustitia confirmati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0032","text":"[I q. 100 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32635)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0033","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod non videtur possibile quod pueri in statu innocentiae nascerentur in iustitia confirmati. Manifestum est enim quod pueri in sua nativitate non habuissent plus perfectionis quam eorum parentes in statu generationis. Parentes autem, quandiu generassent, non fuissent confirmati in iustitia. Ex hoc enim creatura rationalis in iustitia confirmatur, quod efficitur beata per apertam Dei visionem, cui viso non potest non inhaerere, cum ipse sit ipsa essentia bonitatis, a qua nullus potest averti, cum nihil desideretur et ametur nisi sub ratione boni. Et hoc dico secundum legem communem, quia ex aliquo privilegio speciali secus accidere potest, sicut creditur de virgine matre Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0034","text":"Quam cito autem Adam ad illam beatitudinem pervenisset quod Deum per essentiam videret, efficeretur spiritualis et mente et corpore, et animalis vita cessaret, in qua sola generationis usus fuisset. Unde manifestum est quod parvuli non nascerentur in iustitia confirmati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0035","text":"[I q. 100 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32636)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0036","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, si Adam non peccasset, non generaret ex se filios Gehennae, ita scilicet quod ab ipso peccatum contraherent, quod est causa Gehennae. Possent tamen fieri filii Gehennae per liberum arbitrium peccando. Vel, si filii Gehennae non fierent per peccatum, hoc non esset propter hoc, quia essent in iustitia confirmati; sed propter divinam providentiam, per quam a peccato conservarentur immunes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0037","text":"[I q. 100 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32637)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0038","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Anselmus hoc non dicit asserendo, sed opinando. Quod patet ex ipso modo loquendi, cum dicit, videtur quod, si vixissent et cetera.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0039","text":"[I q. 100 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32638)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0040","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ratio ista non est efficax, quamvis per eam Anselmus motus fuisse videatur, ut ex eius verbis apparet. Non enim sic per peccatum primi parentis eius posteri necessitatem peccandi incurrunt, ut ad iustitiam redire non possint, quod est tantum in damnatis. Unde nec ita necessitatem non peccandi transmisisset ad posteros, quod omnino peccare non possent, quod est tantum in beatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0041","text":"[I q. 100 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32639)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0042","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod non est simile de homine et Angelo. Nam homo habet liberum arbitrium vertibile et ante electionem et post, non autem Angelus, sicut supra dictum est, cum de Angelis ageretur.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"6bbcd0d43a11d3d6d464f441b7373f535050a29a90f5f3266c4af06f306f8365","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T153305910779Z_b4fd4fe5","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 100","source_sha256":"6bbcd0d43a11d3d6d464f441b7373f535050a29a90f5f3266c4af06f306f8365","sha256":"79336178fecc3a2329430071e8295122c8df6ae26dd0b72cfc41050493b27814","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:33:05.910866+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0002","text":"[I q. 100 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32621)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the condition of offspring to be generated with respect to justice. And concerning this, two questions are asked. First, whether human beings would have been born with justice. Second, whether they would have been born confirmed in justice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0005","text":"[I q. 100 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32622)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that human beings would not have been born with justice. For Hugh of Saint Victor says that *the first man before sin would indeed have begotten children without sin, but not heirs of paternal justice*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0007","text":"[I q. 100 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32623)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0008","text":"Moreover, justice is by grace, as the Apostle says in Romans V. But grace is not transmitted; for thus it would be natural; rather, it is infused by God alone. Therefore children would not have been born with justice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0009","text":"[I q. 100 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32624)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0010","text":"Moreover, justice is in the soul. But the soul is not by transmission. Therefore neither would justice have been transmitted from parents to children.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0011","text":"[I q. 100 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32625)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0012","text":"On the contrary, Anselm says, in the book *On the Conception of the Virgin*, *that those whom man would beget, if he did not sin, would be just at the same time as they had a rational soul*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0013","text":"[I q. 100 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32626)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that man naturally begets one like himself according to species. Hence, with respect to whatever accidents follow the nature of the species, children must in these be like their parents, unless there be an error in the operation of nature—which would not have existed in the state of innocence. But with respect to individual accidents, children need not be like their parents. Now original justice, in which the first man was created, was an accident of the nature of the species, not as though caused from the principles of the species, but as a certain gift divinely given to the whole nature. And this is apparent, because opposites belong to one genus; but original sin, which is opposed to that justice, is said to be a sin of nature. Hence it is transmitted from parent to descendants. And for this reason, children would also have been made like their parents with respect to original justice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0015","text":"[I q. 100 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32627)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0016","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that Hugh's statement is to be understood not with respect to the habit of justice, but with respect to the exercise of the act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0017","text":"[I q. 100 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32628)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0018","text":"To the second, it must be said that some say that children would not have been born with gratuitous justice, which is the principle of meriting, but with original justice. But since the root of original justice, in whose rectitude man was made, consists in the supernatural subjection of reason to God, which is through grace making pleasing, as was said above, it is necessary to say that, if children had been born in original justice, they would also have been born with grace; just as we said above concerning the first man that he was created with grace. Yet grace would not thereby have been natural, because it would not have been transmitted through the power of the seed, but would have been conferred upon man as soon as he had a rational soul. Just as, when the body is disposed, the rational soul is immediately infused by God, although it is not by transmission.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0019","text":"[I q. 100 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32629)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0020","text":"Hence the answer to the third is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0021","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0022","text":"[I q. 100 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32630)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0023","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that children born in the state of innocence would have been born confirmed in justice. For Gregory says, in *Morals*, IV, commenting on the words, *I would rest in my sleep, etc.*: *if no corruption of sin had corrupted the first parent, he would by no means have begotten from himself children of Gehenna; but those who are now to be saved through the Redeemer would have been born chosen by him alone*. Therefore all would have been born confirmed in justice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0024","text":"[I q. 100 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32631)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0025","text":"Moreover, Anselm says, in the book *Why God Became Man*, that *if the first parents had so lived that, when tempted, they did not sin, they would have been so confirmed, together with all their progeny, that they could no longer sin*. Therefore children would be born confirmed in justice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0026","text":"[I q. 100 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32632)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0027","text":"Moreover, good is more powerful than evil. But as a result of the sin of the first man, a necessity of sinning followed in those who are born from him. Therefore, if the first man had persevered in justice, a necessity of observing justice would be derived to his descendants.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0028","text":"[I q. 100 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32633)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0029","text":"Moreover, the angel who adhered to God while others sinned was immediately confirmed in justice, so that he could not thereafter sin. Therefore likewise man too, if he had resisted temptation, would have been confirmed. But such as he was, such others would he have begotten. Therefore his children too would be born confirmed in justice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0030","text":"[I q. 100 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32634)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0031","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in Book XIV of *The City of God*: *the whole of human society would be so happy if neither they*, namely the first parents, *had transmitted to their descendants the evil, nor anyone from their stock had committed the iniquity, which would receive damnation*. From this it is given to understand that, even if the first human beings had not sinned, some from their stock could have committed iniquity. Therefore they would not be born confirmed in justice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0032","text":"[I q. 100 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32635)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0033","text":"I answer that it must be said that it does not seem possible for children, in the state of innocence, to be born confirmed in justice. For it is manifest that children at their birth would not have had more perfection than their parents in the state of generation. But the parents, for as long as they generated, would not have been confirmed in justice. For by this is a rational creature confirmed in justice: that it is made blessed through the open vision of God; and, upon seeing Him, it cannot fail to adhere to Him, since He is the very essence of goodness, from which no one can turn away, since nothing is desired or loved except under the aspect of the good. And I say this according to the common law, because by some special privilege it can happen otherwise, as is believed concerning the Virgin Mother of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0034","text":"But as soon as Adam had attained that blessedness by which he would see God through His essence, he would become spiritual both in mind and in body, and animal life would cease, in which alone there would have been use of generation. Hence it is manifest that little children would not be born confirmed in justice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0035","text":"[I q. 100 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32636)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0036","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, if Adam had not sinned, he would not have begotten from himself children of Gehenna, namely in such a way that they would contract sin from him, which is the cause of Gehenna. Yet they could become children of Gehenna by sinning through free choice. Or, if they did not become children of Gehenna through sin, this would not be because they were confirmed in justice, but because, through divine providence, they would be preserved free from sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0037","text":"[I q. 100 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32637)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0038","text":"To the second, it must be said that Anselm does not say this by asserting it, but by offering an opinion. This is clear from his very manner of speaking, when he says, it seems that, if they had lived, and so forth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0039","text":"[I q. 100 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32638)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0040","text":"To the third, it must be said that this argument is not cogent, although Anselm seems to have been moved by it, as appears from his words. For the descendants of the first parent do not so incur through his sin a necessity of sinning that they cannot return to justice; that belongs only to the damned. Hence neither would he have transmitted to his descendants such a necessity of not sinning that they could not sin at all; that belongs only to the blessed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0041","text":"[I q. 100 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32639)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0042","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the case of man and angel is not alike. For man has free choice capable of turning both before and after choice, but the angel does not, as was said above when the angels were under discussion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_92:P0042"]}],"source_digest":"5d191e4123bb0fd0bc8df496ae0c8d1cdaed088eee449263bae5ffa66f797d6a","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 100"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 100","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93","title":"First Part · Question 101","order":102,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_93","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 101 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32640)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de conditione prolis generandae quantum ad scientiam. Et circa hoc quaeruntur duo. Primo, utrum pueri nascerentur in scientia perfecti. Secundo, utrum statim post nativitatem habuissent perfectum usum rationis.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 101 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32641)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in statu innocentiae pueri nati fuissent in scientia perfecti. Qualis enim fuit Adam, tales filios generasset. Sed Adam fuit in scientia perfectus, ut supra dictum est. Ergo filii nascerentur ab eo in scientia perfecti.\n\n[I q. 101 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32642)\n\nPraeterea, ignorantia ex peccato causatur, ut Beda dicit. Sed ignorantia est privatio scientiae. Ergo ante peccatum pueri mox nati omnem scientiam habuissent.\n\n[I q. 101 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32643)\n\nPraeterea, pueri mox nati iustitiam habuissent. Sed ad iustitiam requiritur scientia, quae dirigit in agendis. Ergo etiam scientiam habuissent.\n\n[I q. 101 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32644)\n\nSed contra est quod anima nostra per naturam est sicut tabula rasa in qua nihil est scriptum, ut dicitur in III de anima. Sed eadem animae natura est modo, quae tunc fuisset. Ergo animae puerorum in principio scientia caruissent.\n\n[I q. 101 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32645)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, de his quae sunt supra naturam, soli auctoritati creditur, unde ubi auctoritas deficit, sequi debemus naturae conditionem. Est autem naturale homini ut scientiam per sensus acquirat, sicut supra dictum est, et ideo anima unitur corpori, quia indiget eo ad suam propriam operationem; quod non esset, si statim a principio scientiam haberet non acquisitam per sensitivas virtutes. Et ideo dicendum est quod pueri in statu innocentiae non nascerentur perfecti in scientia; sed eam in processu temporis absque difficultate acquisivissent, inveniendo vel addiscendo.\n\n[I q. 101 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32646)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod esse perfectum in scientia fuit individuale accidens primi parentis, inquantum scilicet ipse instituebatur ut pater et instructor totius humani generis. Et ideo quantum ad hoc, non generabat filios similes sibi; sed solum quantum ad accidentia naturalia vel gratuita totius naturae.\n\n[I q. 101 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32647)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ignorantia est privatio scientiae quae debet haberi pro tempore illo. Quod in pueris mox natis non fuisset, habuissent enim scientiam quae eis competebat secundum tempus illud. Unde ignorantia in eis non fuisset, sed nescientia respectu aliquorum. Quam etiam Dionysius ponit in Angelis sanctis, in VII Cael. Hier.\n\n[I q. 101 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32648)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod pueri habuissent sufficientem scientiam ad dirigendum eos in operibus iustitiae in quibus homines diriguntur per universalia principia iuris; quam multo plenius tunc habuissent quam nunc naturaliter habemus, et similiter aliorum universalium principiorum.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 101 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32649)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod pueri in statu innocentiae mox nati habuissent perfectum usum rationis. Nunc enim pueri perfectum usum rationis non habent propter hoc, quod anima per corpus aggravatur. Hoc autem tunc non erat, quia, ut dicitur Sap. IX, *corpus quod corrumpitur, aggravat animam*. Ergo ante peccatum et corruptionem a peccato subsecutam, pueri mox nati perfectum usum rationis habuissent.\n\n[I q. 101 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32650)\n\nPraeterea, quaedam alia animalia mox nata habent naturalis industriae usum, sicut agnus statim fugit lupum. Multo ergo magis homines in statu innocentiae mox nati habuissent usum perfectum rationis.\n\n[I q. 101 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32651)\n\nSed contra est quod natura procedit ab imperfecto ad perfectum in omnibus generatis. Ergo pueri non statim a principio habuissent perfectum usum rationis.\n\n[I q. 101 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32652)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut ex supra dictis patet, usus rationis dependet quodammodo ex usu virium sensitivarum, unde ligato sensu, et impeditis interioribus viribus sensitivis, homo perfectum usum rationis non habet, ut patet in dormientibus et phreneticis. Vires autem sensitivae sunt virtutes quaedam corporalium organorum, et ideo, impeditis earum organis, necesse est quod earum actus impediantur, et per consequens rationis usus. In pueris autem est impedimentum harum virium, propter nimiam humiditatem cerebri. Et ideo in eis non est perfectus usus rationis, sicut nec aliorum membrorum. Et ideo pueri in statu innocentiae non habuissent perfectum usum rationis, sicut habituri erant in perfecta aetate. Habuissent tamen perfectiorem quam nunc, quantum ad ea quae ad eos pertinebant quantum ad statum illum; sicut et de usu membrorum superius est dictum.\n\n[I q. 101 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32653)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod aggravatio additur ex corruptione corporis in hoc, quod usus rationis impeditur quantum ad ea etiam quae pertinent ad hominem secundum quamcumque aetatem.\n\n[I q. 101 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32654)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod etiam alia animalia non habent ita perfectum usum industriae naturalis statim a principio, sicut postea. Quod ex hoc patet, quod aves docent volare pullos suos, et similia in aliis generibus animalium inveniuntur. Et tamen in homine est speciale impedimentum propter abundantiam humiditatis cerebri, ut supra dictum est.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0002","text":"[I q. 101 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32640)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de conditione prolis generandae quantum ad scientiam. Et circa hoc quaeruntur duo. Primo, utrum pueri nascerentur in scientia perfecti. Secundo, utrum statim post nativitatem habuissent perfectum usum rationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0005","text":"[I q. 101 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32641)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in statu innocentiae pueri nati fuissent in scientia perfecti. Qualis enim fuit Adam, tales filios generasset. Sed Adam fuit in scientia perfectus, ut supra dictum est. Ergo filii nascerentur ab eo in scientia perfecti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0007","text":"[I q. 101 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32642)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0008","text":"Praeterea, ignorantia ex peccato causatur, ut Beda dicit. Sed ignorantia est privatio scientiae. Ergo ante peccatum pueri mox nati omnem scientiam habuissent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0009","text":"[I q. 101 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32643)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0010","text":"Praeterea, pueri mox nati iustitiam habuissent. Sed ad iustitiam requiritur scientia, quae dirigit in agendis. Ergo etiam scientiam habuissent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0011","text":"[I q. 101 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32644)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod anima nostra per naturam est sicut tabula rasa in qua nihil est scriptum, ut dicitur in III de anima. Sed eadem animae natura est modo, quae tunc fuisset. Ergo animae puerorum in principio scientia caruissent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0013","text":"[I q. 101 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32645)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, de his quae sunt supra naturam, soli auctoritati creditur, unde ubi auctoritas deficit, sequi debemus naturae conditionem. Est autem naturale homini ut scientiam per sensus acquirat, sicut supra dictum est, et ideo anima unitur corpori, quia indiget eo ad suam propriam operationem; quod non esset, si statim a principio scientiam haberet non acquisitam per sensitivas virtutes. Et ideo dicendum est quod pueri in statu innocentiae non nascerentur perfecti in scientia; sed eam in processu temporis absque difficultate acquisivissent, inveniendo vel addiscendo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0015","text":"[I q. 101 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32646)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0016","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod esse perfectum in scientia fuit individuale accidens primi parentis, inquantum scilicet ipse instituebatur ut pater et instructor totius humani generis. Et ideo quantum ad hoc, non generabat filios similes sibi; sed solum quantum ad accidentia naturalia vel gratuita totius naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0017","text":"[I q. 101 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32647)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0018","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ignorantia est privatio scientiae quae debet haberi pro tempore illo. Quod in pueris mox natis non fuisset, habuissent enim scientiam quae eis competebat secundum tempus illud. Unde ignorantia in eis non fuisset, sed nescientia respectu aliquorum. Quam etiam Dionysius ponit in Angelis sanctis, in VII Cael. Hier.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0019","text":"[I q. 101 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32648)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0020","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod pueri habuissent sufficientem scientiam ad dirigendum eos in operibus iustitiae in quibus homines diriguntur per universalia principia iuris; quam multo plenius tunc habuissent quam nunc naturaliter habemus, et similiter aliorum universalium principiorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0021","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0022","text":"[I q. 101 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32649)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0023","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod pueri in statu innocentiae mox nati habuissent perfectum usum rationis. Nunc enim pueri perfectum usum rationis non habent propter hoc, quod anima per corpus aggravatur. Hoc autem tunc non erat, quia, ut dicitur Sap. IX, *corpus quod corrumpitur, aggravat animam*. Ergo ante peccatum et corruptionem a peccato subsecutam, pueri mox nati perfectum usum rationis habuissent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0024","text":"[I q. 101 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32650)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0025","text":"Praeterea, quaedam alia animalia mox nata habent naturalis industriae usum, sicut agnus statim fugit lupum. Multo ergo magis homines in statu innocentiae mox nati habuissent usum perfectum rationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0026","text":"[I q. 101 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32651)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0027","text":"Sed contra est quod natura procedit ab imperfecto ad perfectum in omnibus generatis. Ergo pueri non statim a principio habuissent perfectum usum rationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0028","text":"[I q. 101 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32652)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0029","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut ex supra dictis patet, usus rationis dependet quodammodo ex usu virium sensitivarum, unde ligato sensu, et impeditis interioribus viribus sensitivis, homo perfectum usum rationis non habet, ut patet in dormientibus et phreneticis. Vires autem sensitivae sunt virtutes quaedam corporalium organorum, et ideo, impeditis earum organis, necesse est quod earum actus impediantur, et per consequens rationis usus. In pueris autem est impedimentum harum virium, propter nimiam humiditatem cerebri. Et ideo in eis non est perfectus usus rationis, sicut nec aliorum membrorum. Et ideo pueri in statu innocentiae non habuissent perfectum usum rationis, sicut habituri erant in perfecta aetate. Habuissent tamen perfectiorem quam nunc, quantum ad ea quae ad eos pertinebant quantum ad statum illum; sicut et de usu membrorum superius est dictum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0030","text":"[I q. 101 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32653)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0031","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod aggravatio additur ex corruptione corporis in hoc, quod usus rationis impeditur quantum ad ea etiam quae pertinent ad hominem secundum quamcumque aetatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0032","text":"[I q. 101 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32654)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0033","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod etiam alia animalia non habent ita perfectum usum industriae naturalis statim a principio, sicut postea. Quod ex hoc patet, quod aves docent volare pullos suos, et similia in aliis generibus animalium inveniuntur. Et tamen in homine est speciale impedimentum propter abundantiam humiditatis cerebri, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"1011a205273fce14242e9c5d606419543b80cc64a8ebdf381a73a9284fe5c107","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T153325594074Z_3d96cdf1","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 101","source_sha256":"1011a205273fce14242e9c5d606419543b80cc64a8ebdf381a73a9284fe5c107","sha256":"577b5746cca92a606465f450c758be1f3125a62508af911ea5c214794a7eb1fa","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:33:25.594112+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0002","text":"[I q. 101 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32640)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0003","text":"Next, the condition of the offspring to be generated must be considered with regard to knowledge. And concerning this, two questions are asked. First, whether children would have been born perfect in knowledge. Second, whether immediately after birth they would have had the perfect use of reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0005","text":"[I q. 101 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32641)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that, in the state of innocence, children would have been born perfect in knowledge. For such as Adam was, such children would he have begotten. But Adam was perfect in knowledge, as stated above. Therefore children would have been born of him perfect in knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0007","text":"[I q. 101 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32642)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0008","text":"Further, ignorance is caused by sin, as Bede says. But ignorance is the privation of knowledge. Therefore, before sin, children immediately upon being born would have possessed all knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0009","text":"[I q. 101 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32643)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0010","text":"Further, children immediately upon being born would have possessed justice. But knowledge, which directs in actions, is required for justice. Therefore they would also have possessed knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0011","text":"[I q. 101 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32644)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0012","text":"On the contrary, our soul is by nature like a blank tablet on which nothing is written, as is said in *On the Soul*, III. But the nature of the soul is the same now as it would have been then. Therefore the souls of children would have lacked knowledge in the beginning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0013","text":"[I q. 101 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32645)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0014","text":"I answer that, as stated above, concerning those things which are above nature, authority alone is believed; hence, where authority fails, we must follow the condition of nature. Now it is natural to man to acquire knowledge through the senses, as stated above, and therefore the soul is united to the body because it needs it for its own operation; which would not be the case if from the very beginning it possessed knowledge not acquired through the sensitive powers. And therefore it must be said that children in the state of innocence would not have been born perfect in knowledge; but in the course of time they would have acquired it without difficulty, by discovery or learning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0015","text":"[I q. 101 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32646)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0016","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that being perfect in knowledge was an individual accident of the first parent, namely insofar as he was established as father and instructor of the entire human race. And therefore, in this respect, he did not beget children like himself, but only with respect to the natural or gratuitous accidents of the whole nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0017","text":"[I q. 101 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32647)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0018","text":"To the second it must be said that ignorance is the privation of knowledge which ought to be possessed at that time. This would not have been so in children immediately upon being born, for they would have possessed the knowledge appropriate to them according to that time. Hence ignorance would not have been in them, but lack of knowledge with respect to certain things. Dionysius also posits this in the holy Angels, in *The Celestial Hierarchy*, VII.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0019","text":"[I q. 101 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32648)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0020","text":"To the third it must be said that children would have had sufficient knowledge to direct them in works of justice, in which men are directed by universal principles of law; and they would then have possessed this much more fully than we now possess it naturally, and likewise the other universal principles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0021","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0022","text":"[I q. 101 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32649)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0023","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that children in the state of innocence, immediately upon being born, would have had the perfect use of reason. For children now do not have the perfect use of reason because the soul is weighed down by the body. But this was not then the case, because, as is said in Wisdom IX, *the body that is corrupted weighs down the soul*. Therefore, before sin and the corruption consequent upon sin, children immediately upon being born would have had the perfect use of reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0024","text":"[I q. 101 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32650)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0025","text":"Moreover, certain other animals, as soon as they are born, have the use of natural instinct, just as a lamb immediately flees a wolf. Therefore, much more would human beings in the state of innocence, as soon as born, have had the perfect use of reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0026","text":"[I q. 101 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32651)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0027","text":"But on the contrary, nature proceeds from the imperfect to the perfect in all things that are generated. Therefore, children would not immediately, from the beginning, have had the perfect use of reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0028","text":"[I q. 101 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32652)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0029","text":"I answer that it must be said that, as is clear from what has been said above, the use of reason depends in a certain way on the use of the sensitive powers. Hence, when sensation is bound and the interior sensitive powers are impeded, a human being does not have the perfect use of reason, as is evident in sleepers and the frenzied. Now the sensitive powers are certain powers of bodily organs; and therefore, when their organs are impeded, it is necessary that their acts be impeded, and consequently the use of reason. But in children there is an impediment to these powers because of the excessive moisture of the brain. Therefore, in them there is not the perfect use of reason, any more than there is of the other limbs. And so children in the state of innocence would not have had the perfect use of reason, such as they were to have at perfect age. Yet they would have had a more perfect use than they do now, with respect to those things that pertained to them according to that state, just as has been said above concerning the use of the limbs.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0030","text":"[I q. 101 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32653)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0031","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that aggravation is added from the corruption of the body in this respect: that the use of reason is impeded even regarding those things that pertain to a human being at whatever age.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0032","text":"[I q. 101 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32654)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0033","text":"To the second, it must be said that other animals too do not have so perfect a use of natural instinct immediately from the beginning as they do later. This is evident from the fact that birds teach their young to fly, and similar things are found in other kinds of animals. And yet in the human being there is a special impediment on account of the abundance of moisture in the brain, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_93:P0033"]}],"source_digest":"3753a14492f1110489aeb445e9e0e31a36e860091cf7f3fd3a2c5ba0b5a27639","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 101"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 101","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94","title":"First Part · Question 102","order":103,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_94","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 102 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32655)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de loco hominis, qui est Paradisus. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum Paradisus sit locus corporeus. Secundo, utrum sit conveniens locus habitationis humanae. Tertio, ad quid homo in Paradiso positus fuit. Quarto, utrum in Paradiso debuit fieri.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 102 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32656)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Paradisus non sit locus corporeus. Dicit enim Beda quod *Paradisus pertingit usque ad lunarem circulum*. Sed nullus locus terrenus talis esse potest, tum quia contra naturam terrae esset quod tantum elevaretur; tum etiam quia sub globo lunari est regio ignis, qui terram consumeret. Non est ergo Paradisus locus corporeus.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32657)\n\nPraeterea, Scriptura commemorat quatuor flumina in Paradiso oriri, ut patet Gen. II. Illa autem flumina quae ibi nominantur, alibi habent manifestas origines; ut patet etiam per philosophum in libro Meteor. Ergo Paradisus non est locus corporeus.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32658)\n\nPraeterea, aliqui diligentissime inquisierunt omnia loca terrae habitabilis, qui tamen nullam mentionem faciunt de loco Paradisi. Ergo non videtur esse locus corporeus.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32659)\n\nPraeterea, in Paradiso describitur lignum vitae esse. Sed lignum vitae est aliquid spirituale, dicitur enim Prov. III, de sapientia, quod est lignum vitae his qui apprehendunt eam. Ergo et Paradisus non est locus corporeus, sed spiritualis.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32660)\n\nPraeterea, si Paradisus est locus corporalis, oportet quod et ligna Paradisi sint corporalia. Sed hoc non videtur, cum corporalia ligna sint producta tertio die; de plantatione autem lignorum Paradisi legitur Gen. II, post opera sex dierum. Ergo Paradisus non est locus corporeus.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32661)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, VIII super Gen. ad Litt., *tres sunt de Paradiso generales sententiae, una eorum qui tantummodo corporaliter Paradisum intelligi volunt; alia eorum qui spiritualiter tantum; tertia eorum qui utroque modo Paradisum accipiunt, quam mihi fateor placere sententiam*.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32662)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit XIII de Civ. Dei, *quae commode dici possunt de intelligendo spiritualiter Paradiso, nemine prohibente dicantur; dum tamen et illius historiae fidelissima veritas rerum gestarum narratione commendata credatur*. Ea enim quae de Paradiso in Scriptura dicuntur, per modum narrationis historicae proponuntur, in omnibus autem quae sic Scriptura tradit, est pro fundamento tenenda veritas historiae, et desuper spirituales expositiones fabricandae. Est ergo Paradisus, ut Isidorus dicit in libro Etymol., *locus in orientis partibus constitutus, cuius vocabulum a Graeco in Latinum vertitur hortus*. Convenienter autem in parte Orientali dicitur situs. Quia credendum est quod in nobilissimo loco totius terrae sit constitutus. Cum autem oriens sit dextera caeli, ut patet per philosophum in II de caelo; dextera autem est nobilior quam sinistra; conveniens fuit ut in Orientali parte Paradisus terrenus institueretur a Deo.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32663)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Bedae verbum non est verum, si secundum sensum manifestum intelligatur. Potest tamen exponi quod usque ad locum lunaris globi ascendit, non secundum situs eminentiam, sed secundum similitudinem, quia est ibi perpetua aeris temperies, ut Isidorus dicit, et in hoc assimilatur corporibus caelestibus, quae sunt absque contrarietate. Magis tamen fit mentio de lunari globo quam de aliis sphaeris, quia lunaris globus est terminus caelestium corporum versus nos; et luna etiam est magis terrae affinis inter omnia corpora caelestia; unde et quasdam tenebras nebulosas habet, quasi accedens ad opacitatem. Quidam autem dicunt quod Paradisus pertingebat usque ad lunarem globum, idest usque ad medium aeris interstitium, in quo generantur pluviae et venti et huiusmodi, quia dominium super huiusmodi evaporationes maxime attribuitur lunae. Sed secundum hoc, locus ille non esset conveniens habitationi humanae, tum quia ibi est maxima intemperies; tum quia non est contemperatus complexioni humanae, sicut aer inferior magis terrae vicinus.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32664)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit VIII super Gen. ad Litt., *credendum est, quoniam locus Paradisi a cognitione hominum est remotissimus, flumina, quorum fontes noti esse dicuntur, alicubi isse sub terras, et post tractus prolixarum regionum, locis aliis erupisse. Nam hoc solere nonnullas aquas facere, quis ignorat?*\n\n[I q. 102 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32665)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod locus ille seclusus est a nostra habitatione aliquibus impedimentis vel montium, vel marium, vel alicuius aestuosae regionis, quae pertransiri non potest. Et ideo scriptores locorum de hoc loco mentionem non fecerunt.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32666)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod lignum vitae est quaedam materialis arbor, sic dicta quia eius fructus habebat virtutem conservandi vitam, ut supra dictum est. Et tamen aliquid significabat spiritualiter, sicut et petra in deserto fuit aliquod materiale, et tamen significavit Christum. Similiter etiam lignum scientiae boni et mali materialis arbor fuit, sic nominata propter eventum futurum, quia post eius esum homo, per experimentum poenae, didicit quid interesset inter obedientiae bonum et inobedientiae malum. Et tamen spiritualiter potuit significare liberum arbitrium, ut quidam dicunt.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32667)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, tertio die productae sunt plantae non in actu, sed secundum quasdam rationes seminales; sed post opera sex dierum productae sunt plantae tam Paradisi quam aliae in actu. Secundum alios vero sanctos, oportet dicere quod omnes plantae productae sunt in actu tertio die, et etiam ligna Paradisi, sed quod dicitur de plantatione lignorum Paradisi post opera sex dierum, intelligitur per recapitulationem esse dictum. Unde littera nostra habet, *plantaverat dominus Deus Paradisum voluptatis a principio*.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 102 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32668)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Paradisus non fuerit locus conveniens habitationi humanae. Homo enim et Angelus similiter ad beatitudinem ordinantur. Sed Angelus statim a principio factus est habitator loci beatorum, scilicet caeli Empyrei. Ergo etiam ibi debuit institui habitatio hominis.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32669)\n\nPraeterea, si locus aliquis debetur homini, aut debetur ei ratione animae, aut ratione corporis. Si ratione animae, debetur ei pro loco caelum, qui videtur esse locus naturalis animae, cum omnibus insitus sit appetitus caeli. Ratione autem corporis, non debetur ei alius locus quam aliis animalibus. Ergo Paradisus nullo modo fuit locus conveniens habitationi humanae.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32670)\n\nPraeterea, frustra est locus in quo nullum locatum continetur. Sed post peccatum Paradisus non est locus habitationis humanae. Ergo, si est locus habitationi humanae congruus, in vanum videtur a Deo institutus fuisse.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32671)\n\nPraeterea, homini, cum sit temperatae complexionis congruus est locus temperatus. Sed locus Paradisi non est locus temperatus, dicitur enim esse sub aequinoctiali circulo, qui locus videtur esse calidissimus, cum bis in anno sol pertranseat super summitatem capitum eorum qui ibi habitant. Ergo Paradisus non est locus congruus habitationi humanae.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32672)\n\nSed contra est quod Damascenus dicit de Paradiso, quod *est divina regio, et digna eius qui secundum imaginem Dei erat, conversatio*.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32673)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, homo sic erat incorruptibilis et immortalis, non quia corpus eius dispositionem incorruptibilitatis haberet, sed quia inerat animae vis quaedam ad praeservandum corpus a corruptione. Corrumpi autem potest corpus humanum et ab interiori et ab exteriori. Ab interiori quidem corrumpitur per consumptionem humidi, et per senectutem, ut supra dictum est, cui corruptioni occurrere poterat primus homo per esum ciborum. Inter ea vero quae exterius corrumpunt, praecipuum videtur esse distemperatus aer, unde huic corruptioni maxime occurritur per temperiem aeris. In Paradiso autem utrumque invenitur, quia, ut Damascenus dicit, *est locus temperato et tenuissimo et purissimo aere circumfulgens, plantis semper floridis comatus*. Unde manifestum est quod Paradisus est locus conveniens habitationi humanae, secundum primae immortalitatis statum.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32674)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod caelum Empyreum est supremum corporalium locorum, et est extra omnem mutabilitatem. Et quantum ad primum horum, est locus congruus naturae angelicae, quia, sicut Augustinus dicit in III de Trin., *Deus regit creaturam corporalem per spiritualem*; unde conveniens est quod spiritualis natura sit supra omnem corporalem constituta, sicut ei praesidens. Quantum autem ad secundum, convenit statui beatitudinis, qui est firmatus in summa stabilitate. Sic igitur locus beatitudinis congruit Angelo secundum naturam suam, unde ibi creatus est. Non autem congruit homini secundum suam naturam, cum non praesideat toti corporali creaturae per modum gubernationis, sed competit ei solum ratione beatitudinis. Unde non est positus a principio in caelo Empyreo; sed illuc transferendus erat in statu finalis beatitudinis.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32675)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ridiculum est dicere quod animae, aut alicui spirituali substantiae, sit aliquis locus naturalis, sed per congruentiam quandam aliquis specialis locus creaturae incorporali attribuitur. Paradisus ergo terrestris erat locus congruens homini et quantum ad animam et quantum ad corpus, inquantum scilicet in anima erat vis praeservandi corpus humanum a corruptione. Quod non competebat aliis animalibus. Et ideo, ut Damascenus dicit, *in Paradiso nullum irrationalium habitabat*, licet ex quadam dispensatione animalia fuerint illuc divinitus adducta ad Adam, et serpens illuc accesserit per operationem Diaboli.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32676)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod non propter hoc locus est frustra, quia non est ibi hominum habitatio post peccatum, sicut etiam non frustra fuit homini attributa immortalitas quaedam, quam conservaturus non erat. Per huiusmodi enim ostenditur benignitas Dei ad hominem, et quid homo peccando amiserit. Quamvis, ut dicitur, nunc Enoch et Elias in illo Paradiso habitent.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32677)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod illi qui dicunt Paradisum esse sub circulo aequinoctiali, opinantur sub circulo illo esse locum temperatissimum, propter aequalitatem dierum et noctium omni tempore; et quia sol nunquam multum ab eis elongatur, ut sit apud eos superabundantia frigoris; nec iterum est apud eos, ut dicunt, superabundantia caloris, quia etsi sol pertranseat super eorum capita, non tamen diu moratur ibi in hac dispositione. Aristoteles tamen, in libro Meteor., expresse dicit quod regio illa est inhabitabilis propter aestum. Quod videtur probabilius, quia terrae per quas nunquam sol pertransit in directum capitis, sunt intemperatae in calore propter solam vicinitatem solis. Quidquid autem de hoc sit, credendum est Paradisum in loco temperatissimo constitutum esse, vel sub aequinoctiali vel alibi.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 102 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32678)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo non sit positus in Paradiso ut operaretur et custodiret illum. Quod enim introductum est in poenam peccati, non fuisset in Paradiso in statu innocentiae. Sed agricultura introducta est in poenam peccati, ut dicitur Gen. III. Ergo homo non fuit positus in Paradiso ut operaretur ipsum.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32679)\n\nPraeterea, custodia non est necessaria, ubi non timetur violentus invasor. Sed in Paradiso nullus timebatur violentus invasor. Ergo non erat necessarium ut Paradisum custodiret.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32680)\n\nPraeterea, si homo positus est in Paradiso ut operaretur et custodiret ipsum, videtur sequi quod homo factus sit propter Paradisum, et non e converso, quod videtur esse falsum. Ergo homo non est positus in Paradiso ut operaretur et custodiret illum.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32681)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, *tulit dominus Deus hominem, et posuit illum in Paradiso voluptatis, ut operaretur et custodiret illum*.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32682)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit VIII super Gen. ad Litt., verbum istud Genesis dupliciter potest intelligi. Uno modo sic, quod Deus posuit hominem in Paradiso, ut ipse Deus operaretur et custodiret hominem, operaretur, inquam, iustificando ipsum, cuius operatio si ab homine cesset, continuo obtenebratur, sicut aer obtenebratur si cesset influentia luminis; ut custodiret vero ab omni corruptione et malo. Alio modo potest intelligi, ut homo operaretur et custodiret Paradisum. Nec tamen illa operatio esset laboriosa, sicut post peccatum, sed fuisset iucunda, propter experientiam virtutis naturae. Custodia etiam illa non esset contra invasores, sed esset ad hoc quod homo sibi Paradisum custodiret, ne ipsum peccando amitteret. Et hoc totum in bonum hominis cedebat, et sic Paradisus ordinatur ad bonum hominis, et non e converso.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 3 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32683)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ad obiecta.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 102 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32684)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo factus fuerit in Paradiso. Angelus enim in loco suae habitationis creatus fuit, scilicet in caelo Empyreo. Sed Paradisus fuit locus congruus habitationi humanae ante peccatum. Ergo videtur quod in Paradiso homo debuit fieri.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32685)\n\nPraeterea, alia animalia conservantur in loco suae generationis; sicut pisces in aquis, et animalia gressibilia in terra, unde producta sunt. Homo autem conservatus fuisset in Paradiso, ut dictum est. Ergo in Paradiso fieri debuit.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32686)\n\nPraeterea, mulier in Paradiso facta fuit. Sed vir dignior est muliere. Ergo multo magis vir debuit fieri in Paradiso.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32687)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, *tulit Deus hominem, et posuit eum in Paradiso*.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32688)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Paradisus fuit locus congruus habitationi humanae, quantum ad incorruptionem primi status. Incorruptio autem illa non erat hominis secundum naturam, sed ex supernaturali Dei dono. Ut ergo hoc gratiae Dei imputaretur, non humanae naturae, Deus hominem extra Paradisum fecit, et postea ipsum in Paradiso posuit, ut habitaret ibi toto tempore animalis vitae, postmodum, cum spiritualem vitam adeptus esset, transferendus in caelum.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32689)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod caelum Empyreum est locus congruus Angelis etiam quantum ad eorum naturam, et ideo ibi sunt creati.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32690)\n\nEt similiter dicendum ad secundum. Loca enim illa congruunt animalibus secundum suam naturam.\n\n[I q. 102 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32691)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod mulier facta fuit in Paradiso non propter dignitatem suam, sed propter dignitatem principii ex quo corpus eius formabatur. Quia similiter et filii in Paradiso fuissent nati, in quo parentes iam erant positi.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0002","text":"[I q. 102 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32655)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de loco hominis, qui est Paradisus. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum Paradisus sit locus corporeus. Secundo, utrum sit conveniens locus habitationis humanae. Tertio, ad quid homo in Paradiso positus fuit. Quarto, utrum in Paradiso debuit fieri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0005","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32656)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Paradisus non sit locus corporeus. Dicit enim Beda quod *Paradisus pertingit usque ad lunarem circulum*. Sed nullus locus terrenus talis esse potest, tum quia contra naturam terrae esset quod tantum elevaretur; tum etiam quia sub globo lunari est regio ignis, qui terram consumeret. Non est ergo Paradisus locus corporeus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0007","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32657)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0008","text":"Praeterea, Scriptura commemorat quatuor flumina in Paradiso oriri, ut patet Gen. II. Illa autem flumina quae ibi nominantur, alibi habent manifestas origines; ut patet etiam per philosophum in libro Meteor. Ergo Paradisus non est locus corporeus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0009","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32658)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0010","text":"Praeterea, aliqui diligentissime inquisierunt omnia loca terrae habitabilis, qui tamen nullam mentionem faciunt de loco Paradisi. Ergo non videtur esse locus corporeus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0011","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32659)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0012","text":"Praeterea, in Paradiso describitur lignum vitae esse. Sed lignum vitae est aliquid spirituale, dicitur enim Prov. III, de sapientia, quod est lignum vitae his qui apprehendunt eam. Ergo et Paradisus non est locus corporeus, sed spiritualis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0013","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32660)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0014","text":"Praeterea, si Paradisus est locus corporalis, oportet quod et ligna Paradisi sint corporalia. Sed hoc non videtur, cum corporalia ligna sint producta tertio die; de plantatione autem lignorum Paradisi legitur Gen. II, post opera sex dierum. Ergo Paradisus non est locus corporeus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0015","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32661)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0016","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, VIII super Gen. ad Litt., *tres sunt de Paradiso generales sententiae, una eorum qui tantummodo corporaliter Paradisum intelligi volunt; alia eorum qui spiritualiter tantum; tertia eorum qui utroque modo Paradisum accipiunt, quam mihi fateor placere sententiam*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0017","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32662)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0018","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit XIII de Civ. Dei, *quae commode dici possunt de intelligendo spiritualiter Paradiso, nemine prohibente dicantur; dum tamen et illius historiae fidelissima veritas rerum gestarum narratione commendata credatur*. Ea enim quae de Paradiso in Scriptura dicuntur, per modum narrationis historicae proponuntur, in omnibus autem quae sic Scriptura tradit, est pro fundamento tenenda veritas historiae, et desuper spirituales expositiones fabricandae. Est ergo Paradisus, ut Isidorus dicit in libro Etymol., *locus in orientis partibus constitutus, cuius vocabulum a Graeco in Latinum vertitur hortus*. Convenienter autem in parte Orientali dicitur situs. Quia credendum est quod in nobilissimo loco totius terrae sit constitutus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0019","text":"Cum autem oriens sit dextera caeli, ut patet per philosophum in II de caelo; dextera autem est nobilior quam sinistra; conveniens fuit ut in Orientali parte Paradisus terrenus institueretur a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0020","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32663)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0021","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Bedae verbum non est verum, si secundum sensum manifestum intelligatur. Potest tamen exponi quod usque ad locum lunaris globi ascendit, non secundum situs eminentiam, sed secundum similitudinem, quia est ibi perpetua aeris temperies, ut Isidorus dicit, et in hoc assimilatur corporibus caelestibus, quae sunt absque contrarietate. Magis tamen fit mentio de lunari globo quam de aliis sphaeris, quia lunaris globus est terminus caelestium corporum versus nos; et luna etiam est magis terrae affinis inter omnia corpora caelestia; unde et quasdam tenebras nebulosas habet, quasi accedens ad opacitatem. Quidam autem dicunt quod Paradisus pertingebat usque ad lunarem globum, idest usque ad medium aeris interstitium, in quo generantur pluviae et venti et huiusmodi, quia dominium super huiusmodi evaporationes maxime attribuitur lunae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0022","text":"Sed secundum hoc, locus ille non esset conveniens habitationi humanae, tum quia ibi est maxima intemperies; tum quia non est contemperatus complexioni humanae, sicut aer inferior magis terrae vicinus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0023","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32664)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0024","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit VIII super Gen. ad Litt., *credendum est, quoniam locus Paradisi a cognitione hominum est remotissimus, flumina, quorum fontes noti esse dicuntur, alicubi isse sub terras, et post tractus prolixarum regionum, locis aliis erupisse. Nam hoc solere nonnullas aquas facere, quis ignorat?*","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0025","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32665)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0026","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod locus ille seclusus est a nostra habitatione aliquibus impedimentis vel montium, vel marium, vel alicuius aestuosae regionis, quae pertransiri non potest. Et ideo scriptores locorum de hoc loco mentionem non fecerunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0027","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32666)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0028","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod lignum vitae est quaedam materialis arbor, sic dicta quia eius fructus habebat virtutem conservandi vitam, ut supra dictum est. Et tamen aliquid significabat spiritualiter, sicut et petra in deserto fuit aliquod materiale, et tamen significavit Christum. Similiter etiam lignum scientiae boni et mali materialis arbor fuit, sic nominata propter eventum futurum, quia post eius esum homo, per experimentum poenae, didicit quid interesset inter obedientiae bonum et inobedientiae malum. Et tamen spiritualiter potuit significare liberum arbitrium, ut quidam dicunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0029","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32667)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0030","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod, secundum Augustinum, tertio die productae sunt plantae non in actu, sed secundum quasdam rationes seminales; sed post opera sex dierum productae sunt plantae tam Paradisi quam aliae in actu. Secundum alios vero sanctos, oportet dicere quod omnes plantae productae sunt in actu tertio die, et etiam ligna Paradisi, sed quod dicitur de plantatione lignorum Paradisi post opera sex dierum, intelligitur per recapitulationem esse dictum. Unde littera nostra habet, *plantaverat dominus Deus Paradisum voluptatis a principio*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0031","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0032","text":"[I q. 102 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32668)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0033","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Paradisus non fuerit locus conveniens habitationi humanae. Homo enim et Angelus similiter ad beatitudinem ordinantur. Sed Angelus statim a principio factus est habitator loci beatorum, scilicet caeli Empyrei. Ergo etiam ibi debuit institui habitatio hominis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0034","text":"[I q. 102 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32669)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0035","text":"Praeterea, si locus aliquis debetur homini, aut debetur ei ratione animae, aut ratione corporis. Si ratione animae, debetur ei pro loco caelum, qui videtur esse locus naturalis animae, cum omnibus insitus sit appetitus caeli. Ratione autem corporis, non debetur ei alius locus quam aliis animalibus. Ergo Paradisus nullo modo fuit locus conveniens habitationi humanae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0036","text":"[I q. 102 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32670)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0037","text":"Praeterea, frustra est locus in quo nullum locatum continetur. Sed post peccatum Paradisus non est locus habitationis humanae. Ergo, si est locus habitationi humanae congruus, in vanum videtur a Deo institutus fuisse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0038","text":"[I q. 102 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32671)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0039","text":"Praeterea, homini, cum sit temperatae complexionis congruus est locus temperatus. Sed locus Paradisi non est locus temperatus, dicitur enim esse sub aequinoctiali circulo, qui locus videtur esse calidissimus, cum bis in anno sol pertranseat super summitatem capitum eorum qui ibi habitant. Ergo Paradisus non est locus congruus habitationi humanae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0040","text":"[I q. 102 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32672)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0041","text":"Sed contra est quod Damascenus dicit de Paradiso, quod *est divina regio, et digna eius qui secundum imaginem Dei erat, conversatio*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0042","text":"[I q. 102 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32673)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0043","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, homo sic erat incorruptibilis et immortalis, non quia corpus eius dispositionem incorruptibilitatis haberet, sed quia inerat animae vis quaedam ad praeservandum corpus a corruptione. Corrumpi autem potest corpus humanum et ab interiori et ab exteriori. Ab interiori quidem corrumpitur per consumptionem humidi, et per senectutem, ut supra dictum est, cui corruptioni occurrere poterat primus homo per esum ciborum. Inter ea vero quae exterius corrumpunt, praecipuum videtur esse distemperatus aer, unde huic corruptioni maxime occurritur per temperiem aeris. In Paradiso autem utrumque invenitur, quia, ut Damascenus dicit, *est locus temperato et tenuissimo et purissimo aere circumfulgens, plantis semper floridis comatus*. Unde manifestum est quod Paradisus est locus conveniens habitationi humanae, secundum primae immortalitatis statum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0044","text":"[I q. 102 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32674)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0045","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod caelum Empyreum est supremum corporalium locorum, et est extra omnem mutabilitatem. Et quantum ad primum horum, est locus congruus naturae angelicae, quia, sicut Augustinus dicit in III de Trin., *Deus regit creaturam corporalem per spiritualem*; unde conveniens est quod spiritualis natura sit supra omnem corporalem constituta, sicut ei praesidens. Quantum autem ad secundum, convenit statui beatitudinis, qui est firmatus in summa stabilitate. Sic igitur locus beatitudinis congruit Angelo secundum naturam suam, unde ibi creatus est. Non autem congruit homini secundum suam naturam, cum non praesideat toti corporali creaturae per modum gubernationis, sed competit ei solum ratione beatitudinis. Unde non est positus a principio in caelo Empyreo; sed illuc transferendus erat in statu finalis beatitudinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0046","text":"[I q. 102 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32675)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0047","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ridiculum est dicere quod animae, aut alicui spirituali substantiae, sit aliquis locus naturalis, sed per congruentiam quandam aliquis specialis locus creaturae incorporali attribuitur. Paradisus ergo terrestris erat locus congruens homini et quantum ad animam et quantum ad corpus, inquantum scilicet in anima erat vis praeservandi corpus humanum a corruptione. Quod non competebat aliis animalibus. Et ideo, ut Damascenus dicit, *in Paradiso nullum irrationalium habitabat*, licet ex quadam dispensatione animalia fuerint illuc divinitus adducta ad Adam, et serpens illuc accesserit per operationem Diaboli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0048","text":"[I q. 102 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32676)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0049","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod non propter hoc locus est frustra, quia non est ibi hominum habitatio post peccatum, sicut etiam non frustra fuit homini attributa immortalitas quaedam, quam conservaturus non erat. Per huiusmodi enim ostenditur benignitas Dei ad hominem, et quid homo peccando amiserit. Quamvis, ut dicitur, nunc Enoch et Elias in illo Paradiso habitent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0050","text":"[I q. 102 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32677)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0051","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod illi qui dicunt Paradisum esse sub circulo aequinoctiali, opinantur sub circulo illo esse locum temperatissimum, propter aequalitatem dierum et noctium omni tempore; et quia sol nunquam multum ab eis elongatur, ut sit apud eos superabundantia frigoris; nec iterum est apud eos, ut dicunt, superabundantia caloris, quia etsi sol pertranseat super eorum capita, non tamen diu moratur ibi in hac dispositione. Aristoteles tamen, in libro Meteor., expresse dicit quod regio illa est inhabitabilis propter aestum. Quod videtur probabilius, quia terrae per quas nunquam sol pertransit in directum capitis, sunt intemperatae in calore propter solam vicinitatem solis. Quidquid autem de hoc sit, credendum est Paradisum in loco temperatissimo constitutum esse, vel sub aequinoctiali vel alibi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0052","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0053","text":"[I q. 102 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32678)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0054","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo non sit positus in Paradiso ut operaretur et custodiret illum. Quod enim introductum est in poenam peccati, non fuisset in Paradiso in statu innocentiae. Sed agricultura introducta est in poenam peccati, ut dicitur Gen. III. Ergo homo non fuit positus in Paradiso ut operaretur ipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0055","text":"[I q. 102 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32679)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0056","text":"Praeterea, custodia non est necessaria, ubi non timetur violentus invasor. Sed in Paradiso nullus timebatur violentus invasor. Ergo non erat necessarium ut Paradisum custodiret.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0057","text":"[I q. 102 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32680)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0058","text":"Praeterea, si homo positus est in Paradiso ut operaretur et custodiret ipsum, videtur sequi quod homo factus sit propter Paradisum, et non e converso, quod videtur esse falsum. Ergo homo non est positus in Paradiso ut operaretur et custodiret illum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0059","text":"[I q. 102 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32681)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0060","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, *tulit dominus Deus hominem, et posuit illum in Paradiso voluptatis, ut operaretur et custodiret illum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0061","text":"[I q. 102 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32682)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0062","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit VIII super Gen. ad Litt., verbum istud Genesis dupliciter potest intelligi. Uno modo sic, quod Deus posuit hominem in Paradiso, ut ipse Deus operaretur et custodiret hominem, operaretur, inquam, iustificando ipsum, cuius operatio si ab homine cesset, continuo obtenebratur, sicut aer obtenebratur si cesset influentia luminis; ut custodiret vero ab omni corruptione et malo. Alio modo potest intelligi, ut homo operaretur et custodiret Paradisum. Nec tamen illa operatio esset laboriosa, sicut post peccatum, sed fuisset iucunda, propter experientiam virtutis naturae. Custodia etiam illa non esset contra invasores, sed esset ad hoc quod homo sibi Paradisum custodiret, ne ipsum peccando amitteret. Et hoc totum in bonum hominis cedebat, et sic Paradisus ordinatur ad bonum hominis, et non e converso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0063","text":"[I q. 102 a. 3 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32683)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0064","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ad obiecta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0065","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0066","text":"[I q. 102 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32684)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0067","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo factus fuerit in Paradiso. Angelus enim in loco suae habitationis creatus fuit, scilicet in caelo Empyreo. Sed Paradisus fuit locus congruus habitationi humanae ante peccatum. Ergo videtur quod in Paradiso homo debuit fieri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0068","text":"[I q. 102 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32685)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0069","text":"Praeterea, alia animalia conservantur in loco suae generationis; sicut pisces in aquis, et animalia gressibilia in terra, unde producta sunt. Homo autem conservatus fuisset in Paradiso, ut dictum est. Ergo in Paradiso fieri debuit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0070","text":"[I q. 102 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32686)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0071","text":"Praeterea, mulier in Paradiso facta fuit. Sed vir dignior est muliere. Ergo multo magis vir debuit fieri in Paradiso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0072","text":"[I q. 102 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32687)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0073","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, *tulit Deus hominem, et posuit eum in Paradiso*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0074","text":"[I q. 102 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32688)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0075","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Paradisus fuit locus congruus habitationi humanae, quantum ad incorruptionem primi status. Incorruptio autem illa non erat hominis secundum naturam, sed ex supernaturali Dei dono. Ut ergo hoc gratiae Dei imputaretur, non humanae naturae, Deus hominem extra Paradisum fecit, et postea ipsum in Paradiso posuit, ut habitaret ibi toto tempore animalis vitae, postmodum, cum spiritualem vitam adeptus esset, transferendus in caelum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0076","text":"[I q. 102 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32689)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0077","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod caelum Empyreum est locus congruus Angelis etiam quantum ad eorum naturam, et ideo ibi sunt creati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0078","text":"[I q. 102 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32690)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0079","text":"Et similiter dicendum ad secundum. Loca enim illa congruunt animalibus secundum suam naturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0080","text":"[I q. 102 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32691)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0081","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod mulier facta fuit in Paradiso non propter dignitatem suam, sed propter dignitatem principii ex quo corpus eius formabatur. Quia similiter et filii in Paradiso fuissent nati, in quo parentes iam erant positi.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"d7e4eda63f8dd00e162143c64f4dcbd8b175bd24ec3abd8ebf8f385f18b2407a","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T153427373091Z_9706ca14","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 102","source_sha256":"d7e4eda63f8dd00e162143c64f4dcbd8b175bd24ec3abd8ebf8f385f18b2407a","sha256":"a790473f36081d652c64db7772596505aa8fd8120155410709ae3078ef60e392","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:34:27.373175+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0002","text":"[I q. 102 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32655)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0003","text":"Next, consideration must be given to the place of man, which is Paradise. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, whether Paradise is a corporeal place. Second, whether it is a suitable place for human habitation. Third, for what purpose man was placed in Paradise. Fourth, whether man ought to have been made in Paradise.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0005","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32656)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that Paradise is not a corporeal place. For Bede says that *Paradise extends as far as the lunar sphere*. But no earthly place can be such, both because it would be contrary to the nature of earth that it should be raised so high, and also because beneath the sphere of the moon is the region of fire, which would consume the earth. Therefore Paradise is not a corporeal place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0007","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32657)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0008","text":"Moreover, Scripture records that four rivers rise in Paradise, as is clear from Gen. II. But those rivers which are named there have manifest sources elsewhere, as is also clear through the Philosopher in the book *Meteorology*. Therefore Paradise is not a corporeal place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0009","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32658)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0010","text":"Moreover, some have investigated most diligently all the places of the habitable earth, yet they make no mention of the place of Paradise. Therefore it does not seem to be a corporeal place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0011","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32659)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0012","text":"Moreover, the tree of life is described as being in Paradise. But the tree of life is something spiritual; for it is said in Prov. III, concerning wisdom, that it is a tree of life to those who lay hold of her. Therefore Paradise too is not a corporeal place, but a spiritual one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0013","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32660)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0014","text":"Moreover, if Paradise is a corporeal place, the trees of Paradise must also be corporeal. But this does not seem to be so, since corporeal trees were produced on the third day, whereas the planting of the trees of Paradise is read of in Gen. II, after the works of the six days. Therefore Paradise is not a corporeal place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0015","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32661)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0016","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in VIII *On Genesis according to the Letter*: *there are three general opinions concerning Paradise: one, of those who wish Paradise to be understood only corporeally; another, of those who understand it only spiritually; a third, of those who take Paradise in both ways—which opinion I confess pleases me*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0017","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32662)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0018","text":"I answer that, as Augustine says in XIII *On the City of God*, *whatever may suitably be said about understanding Paradise spiritually, let it be said, with no one forbidding; provided nevertheless that the most faithful truth of that history, commended by the narration of deeds done, be also believed*. For the things said in Scripture concerning Paradise are set forth by way of historical narration; but in all things that Scripture hands down in this way, the truth of the history must be held as the foundation, and spiritual expositions built upon it. Paradise, therefore, as Isidore says in the book *Etymologies*, is *a place situated in the eastern regions, whose name is translated from Greek into Latin as “garden.”* It is fittingly said to be situated in the eastern part, because it must be believed to be established in the noblest place of the whole earth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0019","text":"But since the east is the right side of heaven, as is clear from the Philosopher in II *On the Heavens*, and the right is nobler than the left, it was fitting that the earthly Paradise should be established by God in the eastern part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0020","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32663)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0021","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Bede’s statement is not true if it is understood according to its manifest sense. Yet it can be explained to mean that it rises as far as the place of the lunar sphere, not according to eminence of position, but according to likeness, because there is there a perpetual temperateness of the air, as Isidore says; and in this it is likened to the heavenly bodies, which are without contrariety. Yet mention is made of the lunar sphere rather than of the other spheres, because the lunar sphere is the boundary of the heavenly bodies toward us; and the moon also is, among all heavenly bodies, more akin to the earth; hence it also has certain cloudy obscurities, as though approaching opacity. But some say that Paradise extended as far as the lunar sphere, that is, as far as the middle region of the air, in which rains and winds and such things are generated, because dominion over such exhalations is attributed especially to the moon.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0022","text":"But according to this, that place would not be suitable for human habitation, both because there is very great intemperateness there, and because it is not tempered to the human complexion, as the lower air nearer to the earth is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0023","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32664)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0024","text":"To the second it must be said that, as Augustine says in VIII *On Genesis according to the Letter*, *it must be believed that, since the place of Paradise is most remote from human knowledge, the rivers whose sources are said to be known went somewhere beneath the earth, and after traversing expanses of lengthy regions, burst forth in other places. For who does not know that some waters are accustomed to do this?*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0025","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32665)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0026","text":"To the third, it must be said that that place is cut off from our habitation by certain impediments, whether of mountains, or of seas, or of some torrid region which cannot be crossed. And therefore writers on places have made no mention of this place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0027","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32666)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0028","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the tree of life is a certain material tree, so called because its fruit had the power of preserving life, as was said above. And yet it signified something spiritually, just as the rock in the desert was something material, and yet signified Christ. Likewise, the tree of the knowledge of good and evil was also a material tree, so named on account of the future event, because after eating from it man, through the experience of punishment, learned what difference there was between the good of obedience and the evil of disobedience. And yet spiritually it could signify free choice, as some say.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0029","text":"[I q. 102 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32667)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0030","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that, according to Augustine, on the third day plants were produced not in actuality, but according to certain seminal reasons; but after the works of the six days, both the plants of Paradise and the others were produced in actuality. According to other saints, however, it must be said that all plants were produced in actuality on the third day, including also the trees of Paradise; but what is said concerning the planting of the trees of Paradise after the works of the six days is to be understood as said by recapitulation. Hence our text has, *the Lord God had planted a paradise of delight from the beginning*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0031","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0032","text":"[I q. 102 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32668)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0033","text":"The second point is approached thus. It seems that Paradise was not a suitable place for human habitation. For man and angel are similarly ordered to beatitude. But the angel was made, from the very beginning, an inhabitant of the place of the blessed, namely, the Empyrean heaven. Therefore the habitation of man too ought to have been established there.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0034","text":"[I q. 102 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32669)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0035","text":"Moreover, if any place is due to man, it is due to him either by reason of the soul or by reason of the body. If by reason of the soul, heaven is due to him as a place, which seems to be the natural place of the soul, since the desire for heaven is implanted in everyone. But by reason of the body, no place is due to him other than to the other animals. Therefore Paradise was in no way a suitable place for human habitation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0036","text":"[I q. 102 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32670)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0037","text":"Moreover, a place in which no thing placed is contained is in vain. But after sin Paradise is not a place of human habitation. Therefore, if it is a place suited to human habitation, it seems to have been established by God in vain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0038","text":"[I q. 102 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32671)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0039","text":"Moreover, for man, since he is of a temperate complexion, a temperate place is suitable. But the place of Paradise is not a temperate place, for it is said to be beneath the equinoctial circle, which place seems to be the hottest, since twice a year the sun passes over the crowns of the heads of those who dwell there. Therefore Paradise is not a place suitable for human habitation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0040","text":"[I q. 102 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32672)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0041","text":"On the contrary, Damascene says of Paradise that *it is a divine region, and a dwelling worthy of him who was according to the image of God*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0042","text":"[I q. 102 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32673)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0043","text":"I answer that, as was said above, man was incorruptible and immortal in such a way, not because his body had a disposition of incorruptibility, but because there was in the soul a certain power to preserve the body from corruption. Now the human body can be corrupted both from within and from without. From within, indeed, it is corrupted by the consumption of moisture and by old age, as was said above; the first man could counter this corruption by eating food. But among those things that corrupt from without, the chief seems to be untempered air; hence this corruption is most especially countered by the temperateness of the air. In Paradise, moreover, both are found, because, as Damascene says, *it is a place shining round about with temperate, most rarefied, and most pure air, adorned with ever-flowering plants*. Hence it is clear that Paradise is a place suitable for human habitation, according to the state of original immortality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0044","text":"[I q. 102 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32674)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0045","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the Empyrean heaven is the highest of bodily places, and is outside all changeability. And as regards the first of these, it is a place suited to the angelic nature, because, as Augustine says in *On the Trinity*, III, *God governs the bodily creature through the spiritual*; hence it is fitting that spiritual nature be established above every bodily nature, as presiding over it. As regards the second, however, it is suited to the state of beatitude, which is established in the highest stability. Thus, then, the place of beatitude is suited to the angel according to his nature, whence he was created there. But it is not suited to man according to his nature, since he does not preside over the whole bodily creature by way of governance, but it belongs to him only by reason of beatitude. Hence he was not placed from the beginning in the Empyrean heaven, but was to be transferred thither in the state of final beatitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0046","text":"[I q. 102 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32675)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0047","text":"To the second, it must be said that it is ridiculous to say that a soul, or any spiritual substance, has some natural place; rather, by a certain fittingness, a special place is attributed to an incorporeal creature. Therefore terrestrial Paradise was a place suitable to man both with respect to the soul and with respect to the body, insofar as there was in the soul a power to preserve the human body from corruption. This did not belong to the other animals. And therefore, as Damascene says, *none of the irrational animals dwelt in Paradise*, although by a certain dispensation animals were divinely brought thither to Adam, and the serpent entered there through the operation of the Devil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0048","text":"[I q. 102 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32676)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0049","text":"To the third, it must be said that the place is not useless on this account, because there is no human habitation there after sin; just as a certain immortality, which he was not going to preserve, was not uselessly bestowed upon man. For by such things God's benevolence toward man is shown, and what man lost by sinning. Although, as is said, Enoch and Elijah now dwell in that Paradise.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0050","text":"[I q. 102 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32677)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0051","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that those who say that Paradise is under the equinoctial circle suppose that beneath that circle there is a most temperate place, on account of the equality of days and nights at all times; and because the sun is never very far removed from them, so that there is an excess of cold among them; nor again, as they say, is there among them an excess of heat, because, although the sun passes over their heads, it nevertheless does not remain long there in this disposition. Aristotle, however, in the book *Meteor.*, expressly says that that region is uninhabitable because of the heat. This seems more probable, because lands through which the sun never passes directly overhead are intemperate in heat solely because of the sun's proximity. But whatever may be the case concerning this, it must be believed that Paradise was established in a most temperate place, whether under the equinoctial circle or elsewhere.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0052","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0053","text":"[I q. 102 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32678)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0054","text":"We proceed to the third thus. It seems that man was not placed in Paradise in order to work and guard it. For what was introduced as a punishment of sin would not have been in Paradise in the state of innocence. But agriculture was introduced as a punishment of sin, as is said in Gen. III. Therefore man was not placed in Paradise in order to work it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0055","text":"[I q. 102 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32679)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0056","text":"Moreover, guarding is not necessary where no violent invader is feared. But in Paradise no violent invader was feared. Therefore it was not necessary that he should guard Paradise.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0057","text":"[I q. 102 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32680)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0058","text":"Moreover, if man was placed in Paradise in order to work and guard it, it seems to follow that man was made for the sake of Paradise, and not conversely, which seems to be false. Therefore man was not placed in Paradise in order to work and guard it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0059","text":"[I q. 102 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32681)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0060","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Gen. II, *the Lord God took man, and placed him in the Paradise of delight, to work and guard it*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0061","text":"[I q. 102 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32682)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0062","text":"I answer that, as Augustine says in VIII *super Gen. ad Litt.*, this saying of Genesis can be understood in two ways. In one way, that God placed man in Paradise so that God Himself might work and guard man: work him, that is, by justifying him; and if this working ceases in man, he is immediately darkened, just as the air is darkened if the influx of light ceases; and guard him from all corruption and evil. In another way, it can be understood so that man might work and guard Paradise. Yet that working would not be laborious, as after sin, but would have been pleasant, on account of the experience of natural power. Nor would that guarding be against invaders, but would be for this purpose: that man might preserve Paradise for himself, lest by sinning he lose it. And all this redounded to man's good; and thus Paradise is ordered to man's good, and not conversely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0063","text":"[I q. 102 a. 3 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32683)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0064","text":"And through this the response to the objections is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0065","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0066","text":"[I q. 102 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32684)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0067","text":"We proceed to the fourth thus. It seems that man was made in Paradise. For the angel was created in the place of his dwelling, namely, in the empyrean heaven. But Paradise was a place suitable for human dwelling before sin. Therefore it seems that man ought to have been made in Paradise.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0068","text":"[I q. 102 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32685)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0069","text":"Moreover, other animals are preserved in the place of their generation: as fish in the waters, and walking animals on the earth, from which they were produced. But man would have been preserved in Paradise, as was said. Therefore he ought to have been made in Paradise.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0070","text":"[I q. 102 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32686)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0071","text":"Moreover, woman was made in Paradise. But man is more worthy than woman. Therefore, much more ought man to have been made in Paradise.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0072","text":"[I q. 102 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32687)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0073","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Gen. II, *God took the man and placed him in Paradise*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0074","text":"[I q. 102 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32688)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0075","text":"I answer that Paradise was a place suited to human habitation, as regards the incorruption of the first state. But that incorruption was not natural to man, but was from the supernatural gift of God. Therefore, so that this might be attributed to the grace of God and not to human nature, God made man outside Paradise, and afterwards placed him in Paradise, that he might dwell there throughout the whole time of the animal life, thereafter, when he had attained the spiritual life, to be transferred to heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0076","text":"[I q. 102 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32689)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0077","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that the Empyrean heaven is a place suited to angels also with respect to their nature, and therefore they were created there.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0078","text":"[I q. 102 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32690)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0079","text":"And the same must be said in reply to the second. For those places are suited to animals according to their nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0080","text":"[I q. 102 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1090.html#32691)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0081","text":"To the third it must be said that woman was made in Paradise not on account of her own dignity, but on account of the dignity of the principle from which her body was formed. For likewise children would have been born in Paradise, in which their parents had already been placed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_94:P0081"]}],"source_digest":"7090efb7e67ec87494c620941114ff8168f0f1571d5ca15d4ed7bc6401075a9b","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 102"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 102","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95","title":"First Part · Question 103","order":104,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_95","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 103 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32692)\n\nPostquam praemissum est de creatione rerum et distinctione earum, restat nunc tertio considerandum de rerum gubernatione. Et primo, in communi; secundo, in speciali de effectibus gubernationis. Circa primum quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum mundus ab aliquo gubernetur. Secundo, quis sit finis gubernationis ipsius. Tertio, utrum gubernetur ab uno. Quarto, de effectibus gubernationis. Quinto, utrum omnia divinae gubernationi subsint. Sexto, utrum omnia immediate gubernentur a Deo. Septimo, utrum divina gubernatio cassetur in aliquo. Octavo, utrum aliquid divinae providentiae contranitatur.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 103 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32693)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod mundus non gubernetur ab aliquo. Illorum enim est gubernari, quae moventur vel operantur propter finem. Sed res naturales, quae sunt magna pars mundi, non moventur aut operantur propter finem, quia non cognoscunt finem. Ergo mundus non gubernatur.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32694)\n\nPraeterea, eorum est proprie gubernari, quae ad aliquid moventur. Sed mundus non videtur ad aliquid moveri, sed in se stabilitatem habet. Ergo non gubernatur.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32695)\n\nPraeterea, id quod in se habet necessitatem qua determinatur ad unum, non indiget exteriori gubernante. Sed principaliores mundi partes quadam necessitate determinantur ad unum in suis actibus et motibus. Ergo mundus gubernatione non indiget.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32696)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Sap. XIV, *tu autem, pater, gubernas omnia providentia*. Et Boetius dicit, in libro de Consol., *o qui perpetua mundum ratione gubernas*.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32697)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam antiqui philosophi gubernationem mundo subtraxerunt, dicentes omnia fortuito agi. Sed haec positio ostenditur esse impossibilis ex duobus. Primo quidem, ex eo quod apparet in ipsis rebus. Videmus enim in rebus naturalibus provenire quod melius est, aut semper aut in pluribus, quod non contingeret, nisi per aliquam providentiam res naturales dirigerentur ad finem boni, quod est gubernare. Unde ipse ordo certus rerum manifeste demonstrat gubernationem mundi, sicut si quis intraret domum bene ordinatam, ex ipsa domus ordinatione ordinatoris rationem perpenderet; ut, ab Aristotele dictum, Tullius introducit in libro de natura deorum. Secundo autem apparet idem ex consideratione divinae bonitatis, per quam res in esse productae sunt, ut ex supra dictis patet. Cum enim optimi sit optima producere, non convenit summae Dei bonitati quod res productas ad perfectum non perducat. Ultima autem perfectio uniuscuiusque est in consecutione finis. Unde ad divinam bonitatem pertinet ut, sicut produxit res in esse, ita etiam eas ad finem perducat. Quod est gubernare.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32698)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod aliquid movetur vel operatur propter finem dupliciter. Uno modo, sicut agens seipsum in finem, ut homo et aliae creaturae rationales, et talium est cognoscere rationem finis, et eorum quae sunt ad finem. Aliquid autem dicitur moveri vel operari propter finem, quasi ab alio actum vel directum in finem, sicut sagitta movetur ad signum directa a sagittante, qui cognoscit finem, non autem sagitta. Unde sicut motus sagittae ad determinatum finem demonstrat aperte quod sagitta dirigitur ab aliquo cognoscente; ita certus cursus naturalium rerum cognitione carentium, manifeste declarat mundum ratione aliqua gubernari.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32699)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod in omnibus rebus creatis est aliquid stabile, ad minus prima materia; et aliquid ad motum pertinens, ut sub motu etiam operationem comprehendamus. Et quantum ad utrumque, res indiget gubernatione, quia hoc ipsum quod in rebus est stabile, in nihilum decideret (quia ex nihilo est), nisi manu gubernatoris servaretur, ut infra patebit.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32700)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod necessitas naturalis inhaerens rebus quae determinantur ad unum, est impressio quaedam Dei dirigentis ad finem, sicut necessitas qua sagitta agitur ut ad certum signum tendat, est impressio sagittantis, et non sagittae. Sed in hoc differt, quia id quod creaturae a Deo recipiunt, est earum natura; quod autem ab homine rebus naturalibus imprimitur praeter earum naturam, ad violentiam pertinet. Unde sicut necessitas violentiae in motu sagittae demonstrat sagittantis directionem; ita necessitas naturalis creaturarum demonstrat divinae providentiae gubernationem.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 103 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32701)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod finis gubernationis mundi non sit aliquid extra mundum existens. Illud enim est finis gubernationis rei, ad quod res gubernata perducitur. Sed illud ad quod res aliqua perducitur, est aliquod bonum in ipsa re, sicut infirmus perducitur ad sanitatem, quae est aliquod bonum in ipso. Ergo finis gubernationis rerum non est aliquod bonum extrinsecum, sed aliquod bonum in ipsis rebus existens.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32702)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus dicit, I Ethic., quod *finium quidam sunt operationes, quidam opera*, idest operata. Sed nihil extrinsecum a toto universo potest esse operatum, operatio autem est in ipsis operantibus. Ergo nihil extrinsecum potest esse finis gubernationis rerum.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32703)\n\nPraeterea, bonum multitudinis videtur esse ordo et pax, quae est tranquillitas ordinis. Ut Augustinus dicit XIX de Civ. Dei. Sed mundus in quadam rerum multitudine consistit. Ergo finis gubernationis mundi est pacificus ordo, qui est in ipsis rebus. Non ergo finis gubernationis rerum est quoddam bonum extrinsecum.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32704)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Prov. XVI, *universa propter se operatus est dominus*. Ipse autem est extra totum ordinem universi. Ergo finis rerum est quoddam bonum extrinsecum.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32705)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum finis respondeat principio, non potest fieri ut, principio cognito, quid sit rerum finis ignoretur. Cum igitur principium rerum sit aliquid extrinsecum a toto universo, scilicet Deus, ut ex supra dictis patet; necesse est quod etiam finis rerum sit quoddam bonum extrinsecum. Et hoc ratione apparet. Manifestum est enim quod bonum habet rationem finis. Unde finis particularis alicuius rei est quoddam bonum particulare, finis autem universalis rerum omnium est quoddam bonum universale. Bonum autem universale est quod est per se et per suam essentiam bonum, quod est ipsa essentia bonitatis, bonum autem particulare est quod est participative bonum. Manifestum est autem quod in tota universitate creaturarum nullum est bonum quod non sit participative bonum. Unde illud bonum quod est finis totius universi, oportet quod sit extrinsecum a toto universo.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32706)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod bonum aliquod consequimur multipliciter, uno modo, sicut formam in nobis existentem, ut sanitatem aut scientiam; alio modo, ut aliquid per nos operatum, sicut aedificator consequitur finem faciendo domum; alio modo, sicut aliquod bonum habitum vel possessum, ut ille qui emit, consequitur finem possidendo agrum. Unde nihil prohibet illud ad quod perducitur universum, esse quoddam bonum extrinsecum.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32707)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod philosophus loquitur de finibus artium, quarum quaedam habent pro finibus operationes ipsas, sicut citharistae finis est citharizare; quaedam vero habent pro fine quoddam operatum, sicut aedificatoris finis non est aedificare, sed domus. Contingit autem aliquid extrinsecum esse finem non solum sicut operatum, sed etiam sicut possessum seu habitum, vel etiam sicut repraesentatum, sicut si dicamus quod Hercules est finis imaginis, quae fit ad eum repraesentandum. Sic igitur potest dici quod bonum extrinsecum a toto universo est finis gubernationis rerum sicut habitum et repraesentatum, quia ad hoc unaquaeque res tendit, ut participet ipsum, et assimiletur ei, quantum potest.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32708)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod finis quidem universi est aliquod bonum in ipso existens, scilicet ordo ipsius universi, hoc autem bonum non est ultimus finis, sed ordinatur ad bonum extrinsecum ut ad ultimum finem; sicut etiam ordo exercitus ordinatur ad ducem, ut dicitur in XII Metaphys.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 103 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32709)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod mundus non gubernetur ab uno. De causa enim per effectus iudicamus. Sed in gubernatione rerum apparet quod res non uniformiter moventur et operantur, quaedam enim contingenter, quaedam vero ex necessitate, et secundum alias diversitates. Ergo mundus non gubernatur ab uno.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32710)\n\nPraeterea, ea quae gubernantur ab uno, a se invicem non dissentiunt nisi propter imperitiam aut impotentiam gubernantis, quae a Deo sunt procul. Sed res creatae a se invicem dissentiunt, et contra se invicem pugnant; ut in contrariis apparet. Non ergo mundus gubernatur ab uno.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32711)\n\nPraeterea, in natura semper invenitur quod melius est. Sed melius est simul esse duos quam unum, ut dicitur Eccle. IV. Ergo mundus non gubernatur ab uno, sed a pluribus.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32712)\n\nSed contra est quod unum Deum et unum dominum confitemur; secundum illud apostoli I ad Cor. VIII, *nobis est unus Deus, pater, et dominus unus*. Quorum utrumque ad gubernationem pertinet, nam ad dominum pertinet gubernatio subditorum; et Dei nomen ex providentia sumitur, ut supra dictum est. Ergo mundus gubernatur ab uno.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32713)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere quod mundus ab uno gubernetur. Cum enim finis gubernationis mundi sit quod est essentialiter bonum, quod est optimum, necesse est quod mundi gubernatio sit optima. Optima autem gubernatio est quae fit per unum. Cuius ratio est, quia gubernatio nihil aliud est quam directio gubernatorum ad finem, qui est aliquod bonum. Unitas autem pertinet ad rationem bonitatis; ut Boetius probat, in III de Consol., per hoc quod. Sicut omnia desiderant bonum, ita desiderant unitatem, sine qua esse non possunt. Nam unumquodque intantum est, inquantum unum est, unde videmus quod res repugnant suae divisioni quantum possunt, et quod dissolutio uniuscuiusque rei provenit ex defectu illius rei. Et ideo id ad quod tendit intentio multitudinem gubernantis, est unitas sive pax. Unitatis autem causa per se est unum. Manifestum est enim quod plures multa unire et concordare non possunt, nisi ipsi aliquo modo uniantur. Illud autem quod est per se unum, potest convenientius et melius esse causa unitatis, quam multi uniti. Unde multitudo melius gubernatur per unum quam per plures. Relinquitur ergo quod gubernatio mundi, quae est optima, sit ab uno gubernante. Et hoc est quod philosophus dicit in XII Metaphys., *entia nolunt disponi male, nec bonum pluralitas principatuum, unus ergo princeps*.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32714)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod motus est actus mobilis a movente. Difformitas ergo motuum est ex diversitate mobilium, quam requirit perfectio universi, ut supra dictum est; non ex diversitate gubernantium.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32715)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod contraria, etsi dissentiant quantum ad fines proximos, conveniunt tamen quantum ad finem ultimum, prout concluduntur sub uno ordine universi.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32716)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in particularibus bonis duo sunt meliora quam unum, sed ei quod est essentialiter bonum, non potest fieri aliqua additio bonitatis.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 103 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32717)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod effectus gubernationis mundi sit unus tantum, et non plures. Effectus enim gubernationis esse videtur id quod per gubernationem in rebus gubernatis causatur. Hoc autem est unum, scilicet bonum ordinis; ut in exercitu patet. Ergo gubernationis mundi est unus effectus.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32718)\n\nPraeterea, ab uno natum est unum tantum procedere. Sed mundus gubernatur ab uno, ut ostensum est. Ergo et gubernationis effectus est unus tantum.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32719)\n\nPraeterea, si effectus gubernationis non est unus tantum propter unitatem gubernantis, oportet quod multiplicetur secundum multitudinem gubernatorum. Haec autem sunt nobis innumerabilia. Ergo gubernationis effectus non possunt comprehendi sub aliquo certo numero.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32720)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, quod *deitas providentia et bonitate perfecta omnia continet, et seipsa implet*. Gubernatio autem ad providentiam pertinet. Ergo gubernationis divinae sunt aliqui determinati effectus.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32721)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod effectus cuiuslibet actionis ex fine eius pensari potest, nam per operationem efficitur ut pertingatur ad finem. Finis autem gubernationis mundi est bonum essentiale, ad cuius participationem et assimilationem omnia tendunt. Effectus igitur gubernationis potest accipi tripliciter. Uno modo, ex parte ipsius finis, et sic est unus effectus gubernationis, scilicet assimilari summo bono. Alio modo potest considerari effectus gubernationis secundum ea quibus ad Dei assimilationem creatura perducitur. Et sic in generali sunt duo effectus gubernationis. Creatura enim assimilatur Deo quantum ad duo, scilicet quantum ad id quod Deus bonus est, inquantum creatura est bona; et quantum ad hoc quod Deus est aliis causa bonitatis, inquantum una creatura movet aliam ad bonitatem. Unde duo sunt effectus gubernationis, scilicet conservatio rerum in bono, et motio earum ad bonum. Tertio modo potest considerari effectus gubernationis in particulari, et sic sunt nobis innumerabiles.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32722)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ordo universi includit in se et conservationem rerum diversarum a Deo institutarum, et motionem earum, quia secundum haec duo invenitur ordo in rebus, secundum scilicet quod una est melior alia, et secundum quod una ab alia movetur.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32723)\n\nAd alia duo patet responsio per ea quae dicta sunt.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 103 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32724)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non omnia divinae gubernationi subdantur. Dicitur enim Eccle. IX, *vidi sub sole nec velocium esse cursum, nec fortium bellum, nec sapientium panem, nec doctorum divitias, nec artificum gratiam, sed tempus casumque in omnibus*. Quae autem gubernationi alicuius subsunt, non sunt casualia. Ergo ea quae sunt sub sole, non subduntur divinae gubernationi.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32725)\n\nPraeterea, apostolus, I ad Cor. IX, dicit quod *non est Deo cura de bobus*. Sed unicuique est cura eorum quae gubernantur ab ipso. Non ergo omnia subduntur divinae gubernationi.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32726)\n\nPraeterea, illud quod seipsum gubernare potest, non videtur alterius gubernatione indigere. Sed creatura rationalis seipsam gubernare potest, cum habeat dominium sui actus, et per se agat; et non solum agatur ab alio, quod videtur esse eorum quae gubernantur. Ergo non omnia subsunt divinae gubernationi.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32727)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, V de Civ. Dei, quod *Deus non solum caelum et terram, nec solum hominem et Angelum, sed nec exigui et contemptibilis animantis viscera, nec avis pennulam, nec herbae flosculum, nec arboris folium, sine suarum partium convenientia dereliquit*. Omnia ergo eius gubernationi subduntur.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32728)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod secundum eandem rationem competit Deo esse gubernatorem rerum, et causam earum, quia eiusdem est rem producere, et ei perfectionem dare, quod ad gubernantem pertinet. Deus autem est causa non quidem particularis unius generis rerum, sed universalis totius entis, ut supra ostensum est. Unde sicut nihil potest esse quod non sit a Deo creatum, ita nihil potest esse quod eius gubernationi non subdatur. Patet etiam hoc idem ex ratione finis. Intantum enim alicuius gubernatio se extendit, inquantum se extendere potest finis gubernationis. Finis autem divinae gubernationis est ipsa sua bonitas, ut supra ostensum est. Unde cum nihil esse possit quod non ordinetur in divinam bonitatem sicut in finem, ut ex supra dictis patet; impossibile est quod aliquod entium subtrahatur gubernationi divinae. Stulta igitur fuit opinio dicentium quod haec inferiora corruptibilia, vel etiam singularia, aut etiam res humanae non gubernantur a Deo. Ex quorum persona dicitur Ezech. IX, *dereliquit dominus terram*.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32729)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod sub sole dicuntur esse ea quae secundum motum solis generantur et corrumpuntur. In quibus omnibus casus invenitur; non ita quod omnia quae in eis fiunt, sint casualia; sed quia in quolibet eorum aliquid casuale inveniri potest. Et hoc ipsum quod aliquid casuale invenitur in huiusmodi rebus, demonstrat ea alicuius gubernationi esse subiecta. Nisi enim huiusmodi corruptibilia ab aliquo superiori gubernarentur, nihil intenderent, maxime quae non cognoscunt, et sic non eveniret in eis aliquid praeter intentionem, quod facit rationem casus. Unde ad ostendendum quod casualia secundum ordinem alicuius superioris causae proveniunt, non dicit simpliciter quod vidit casum esse in omnibus, sed dicit tempus et casum; quia scilicet secundum aliquem ordinem temporis, casuales defectus inveniuntur in his rebus.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32730)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod gubernatio est quaedam mutatio gubernatorum a gubernante. Omnis autem motus est actus mobilis a movente, ut dicitur in III Physic. Omnis autem actus proportionatur ei cuius est actus. Et sic oportet quod diversa mobilia diversimode moveantur, etiam secundum motionem unius motoris. Sic igitur secundum unam artem Dei gubernantis, res diversimode gubernantur, secundum earum diversitatem. Quaedam enim secundum suam naturam sunt per se agentia, tanquam habentia dominium sui actus, et ista gubernantur a Deo non solum per hoc quod moventur ab ipso Deo in eis interius operante, sed etiam per hoc quod ab eo inducuntur ad bonum et retrahuntur a malo per praecepta et prohibitiones, praemia et poenas. Hoc autem modo non gubernantur a Deo creaturae irrationales, quae tantum aguntur, et non agunt. Cum ergo apostolus dicit quod Deo non est cura de bobus, non totaliter subtrahit boves a cura gubernationis divinae; sed solum quantum ad modum qui proprie competit rationali creaturae.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32731)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod creatura rationalis gubernat seipsam per intellectum et voluntatem, quorum utrumque indiget regi et perfici ab intellectu et voluntate Dei. Et ideo supra gubernationem qua creatura rationalis gubernat seipsam tanquam domina sui actus, indiget gubernari a Deo.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 103 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32732)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod omnia immediate gubernentur a Deo. Gregorius enim Nyssenus reprehendit opinionem Platonis, qui divisit providentiam in tria, primam quidem primi Dei, qui providet rebus caelestibus, et universalibus omnibus; secundam vero providentiam esse dixit secundorum deorum, qui caelum circumeunt, scilicet respectu eorum quae sunt in generatione et corruptione; tertiam vero providentiam dixit quorundam Daemonum, qui sunt custodes circa terram humanarum actionum. Ergo videtur quod omnia immediate a Deo gubernentur.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32733)\n\nPraeterea, melius est aliquid fieri per unum quam per multa, si sit possibile, ut dicitur in VIII Physic. Sed Deus potest per seipsum absque mediis causis omnia gubernare. Ergo videtur quod omnia immediate gubernet.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32734)\n\nPraeterea, nihil in Deo est deficiens et imperfectum. Sed ad defectum gubernatoris pertinere videtur quod mediantibus aliquibus gubernet, sicut rex terrenus, quia non sufficit ad omnia agenda, nec ubique est praesens in suo regno, propter hoc oportet quod habeat suae gubernationis ministros. Ergo Deus immediate omnia gubernat.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32735)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in III de Trin., *quemadmodum corpora crassiora et inferiora per subtiliora et potentiora quodam ordine reguntur; ita omnia corpora per spiritum vitae rationalem, et spiritus vitae rationalis desertor atque peccator per spiritum vitae rationalem pium et iustum, et ille per ipsum Deum*.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32736)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in gubernatione duo sunt consideranda, scilicet ratio gubernationis, quae est ipsa providentia; et executio. Quantum igitur ad rationem gubernationis pertinet, Deus immediate omnia gubernat, quantum autem pertinet ad executionem gubernationis, Deus gubernat quaedam mediantibus aliis. Cuius ratio est quia, cum Deus sit ipsa essentia bonitatis, unumquodque attribuendum est Deo secundum sui optimum. Optimum autem in omni genere vel ratione vel cognitione practica, qualis est ratio gubernationis, in hoc consistit, quod particularia cognoscantur, in quibus est actus, sicut optimus medicus est, non qui considerat sola universalia, sed qui potest etiam considerare minima particularium; et idem patet in ceteris. Unde oportet dicere quod Deus omnium etiam minimorum particularium rationem gubernationis habeat. Sed cum per gubernationem res quae gubernantur sint ad perfectionem perducendae; tanto erit melior gubernatio, quanto maior perfectio a gubernante rebus gubernatis communicatur. Maior autem perfectio est quod aliquid in se sit bonum, et etiam sit aliis causa bonitatis, quam si esset solummodo in se bonum. Et ideo sic Deus gubernat res, ut quasdam aliarum in gubernando causas instituat, sicut si aliquis magister discipulos suos non solum scientes faceret, sed etiam aliorum doctores.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32737)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod opinio Platonis reprehenditur, quia etiam quantum ad rationem gubernationis, posuit Deum non immediate omnia gubernare. Quod patet per hoc, quod divisit in tria providentiam, quae est ratio gubernationis.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32738)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, si solus Deus gubernaret, subtraheretur perfectio causalis a rebus. Unde non totum fieret per unum, quod fit per multa.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32739)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod non solum pertinet ad imperfectionem regis terreni quod executores habeat suae gubernationis, sed etiam ad regis dignitatem, quia ex ordine ministrorum potestas regia praeclarior redditur.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 103 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32740)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aliquid praeter ordinem divinae gubernationis contingere possit. Dicit enim Boetius, in III de Consol., quod *Deus per bonum cuncta disponit*. Si ergo nihil in rebus contingit praeter ordinem divinae gubernationis, sequeretur quod nihil esset malum in rebus.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32741)\n\nPraeterea, nihil est casuale quod evenit secundum praeordinationem alicuius gubernantis. Si igitur nihil accidit in rebus praeter ordinem gubernationis divinae, sequitur quod nihil in rebus sit fortuitum et casuale.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32742)\n\nPraeterea, ordo divinae gubernationis est certus et immutabilis, quia est secundum rationem aeternam. Si igitur nihil possit contingere in rebus praeter ordinem divinae gubernationis, sequitur quod omnia ex necessitate eveniant, et nihil sit in rebus contingens, quod est inconveniens. Potest igitur in rebus aliquid contingere praeter ordinem gubernationis divinae.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32743)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Esther XIII, *domine Deus, rex omnipotens, in ditione tua cuncta sunt posita, et non est qui possit resistere tuae voluntati*.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32744)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod praeter ordinem alicuius particularis causae, aliquis effectus evenire potest; non autem praeter ordinem causae universalis. Cuius ratio est, quia praeter ordinem particularis causae nihil provenit nisi ex aliqua alia causa impediente, quam quidem causam necesse est reducere in primam causam universalem, sicut indigestio contingit praeter ordinem virtutis nutritivae, ex aliquo impedimento, puta ex grossitie cibi, quam necesse est reducere in aliquam aliam causam, et sic usque ad causam primam universalem. Cum igitur Deus sit prima causa universalis non unius generis tantum, sed universaliter totius entis; impossibile est quod aliquid contingat praeter ordinem divinae gubernationis, sed ex hoc ipso quod aliquid ex una parte videtur exire ab ordine divinae providentiae qui consideratur secundum aliquam particularem causam, necesse est quod in eundem ordinem relabatur secundum aliam causam.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32745)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod nihil invenitur in mundo quod sit totaliter malum, quia malum semper fundatur in bono, ut supra ostensum est. Et ideo res aliqua dicitur mala, per hoc quod exit ab ordine alicuius particularis boni. Si autem totaliter exiret ab ordine gubernationis divinae, totaliter nihil esset.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32746)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod aliqua dicuntur esse casualia in rebus, per ordinem ad causas particulares, extra quarum ordinem fiunt. Sed quantum ad divinam providentiam pertinet, nihil fit casu in mundo, ut Augustinus dicit in libro octoginta trium quaest.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32747)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod dicuntur aliqui effectus contingentes, per comparationem ad proximas causas, quae in suis effectibus deficere possunt, non propter hoc quod aliquid fieri possit extra totum ordinem gubernationis divinae. Quia hoc ipsum quod aliquid contingit praeter ordinem causae proximae, est ex aliqua causa subiecta gubernationi divinae.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 103 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32748)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aliquid possit reniti contra ordinem gubernationis divinae. Dicitur enim Isaiae III, *lingua eorum et adinventiones eorum contra dominum*.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32749)\n\nPraeterea, nullus rex iuste punit eos qui eius ordinationi non repugnant. Si igitur nihil contraniteretur divinae ordinationi, nullus iuste puniretur a Deo.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32750)\n\nPraeterea, quaelibet res est subiecta ordini divinae gubernationis. Sed una res ab alia impugnatur. Ergo aliqua sunt quae contranituntur divinae gubernationi.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32751)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Boetius, in III de Consol., *non est aliquid quod summo huic bono vel velit vel possit obsistere. Est igitur summum bonum quod regit cuncta fortiter, suaviterque disponit*; ut dicitur Sap. VIII, de divina sapientia.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32752)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ordo divinae providentiae dupliciter potest considerari, uno modo in generali, secundum scilicet quod progreditur a causa gubernativa totius; alio modo in speciali, secundum scilicet quod progreditur ex aliqua causa particulari, quae est executiva divinae gubernationis. Primo igitur modo, nihil contranititur ordini divinae gubernationis. Quod ex duobus patet. Primo quidem, ex hoc quod ordo divinae gubernationis totaliter in bonum tendit, et unaquaeque res in sua operatione et conatu non tendit nisi ad bonum, nullus enim respiciens ad malum operatur, ut Dionysius dicit. Alio modo apparet idem ex hoc quod, sicut supra dictum est, omnis inclinatio alicuius rei vel naturalis vel voluntaria, nihil est aliud quam quaedam impressio a primo movente, sicut inclinatio sagittae ad signum determinatum, nihil aliud est quam quaedam impressio a sagittante. Unde omnia quae agunt vel naturaliter vel voluntarie, quasi propria sponte perveniunt in id ad quod divinitus ordinantur. Et ideo dicitur Deus omnia disponere suaviter.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32753)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod dicuntur aliqui vel cogitare vel loqui vel agere contra Deum, non quia totaliter renitantur ordini divinae gubernationis, quia etiam peccantes intendunt aliquod bonum, sed quia contranituntur cuidam determinato bono, quod est eis conveniens secundum suam naturam aut statum. Et ideo puniuntur iuste a Deo.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32754)\n\nEt per hoc patet solutio ad secundum.\n\n[I q. 103 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32755)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ex hoc quod una res alteri contrapugnat, ostenditur quod aliquid reniti potest ordini qui est ex aliqua causa particulari, non autem ordini qui dependet a causa universali totius.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0002","text":"[I q. 103 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32692)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0003","text":"Postquam praemissum est de creatione rerum et distinctione earum, restat nunc tertio considerandum de rerum gubernatione. Et primo, in communi; secundo, in speciali de effectibus gubernationis. Circa primum quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum mundus ab aliquo gubernetur. Secundo, quis sit finis gubernationis ipsius. Tertio, utrum gubernetur ab uno. Quarto, de effectibus gubernationis. Quinto, utrum omnia divinae gubernationi subsint. Sexto, utrum omnia immediate gubernentur a Deo. Septimo, utrum divina gubernatio cassetur in aliquo. Octavo, utrum aliquid divinae providentiae contranitatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0005","text":"[I q. 103 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32693)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod mundus non gubernetur ab aliquo. Illorum enim est gubernari, quae moventur vel operantur propter finem. Sed res naturales, quae sunt magna pars mundi, non moventur aut operantur propter finem, quia non cognoscunt finem. Ergo mundus non gubernatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0007","text":"[I q. 103 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32694)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0008","text":"Praeterea, eorum est proprie gubernari, quae ad aliquid moventur. Sed mundus non videtur ad aliquid moveri, sed in se stabilitatem habet. Ergo non gubernatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0009","text":"[I q. 103 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32695)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0010","text":"Praeterea, id quod in se habet necessitatem qua determinatur ad unum, non indiget exteriori gubernante. Sed principaliores mundi partes quadam necessitate determinantur ad unum in suis actibus et motibus. Ergo mundus gubernatione non indiget.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0011","text":"[I q. 103 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32696)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Sap. XIV, *tu autem, pater, gubernas omnia providentia*. Et Boetius dicit, in libro de Consol., *o qui perpetua mundum ratione gubernas*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0013","text":"[I q. 103 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32697)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam antiqui philosophi gubernationem mundo subtraxerunt, dicentes omnia fortuito agi. Sed haec positio ostenditur esse impossibilis ex duobus. Primo quidem, ex eo quod apparet in ipsis rebus. Videmus enim in rebus naturalibus provenire quod melius est, aut semper aut in pluribus, quod non contingeret, nisi per aliquam providentiam res naturales dirigerentur ad finem boni, quod est gubernare. Unde ipse ordo certus rerum manifeste demonstrat gubernationem mundi, sicut si quis intraret domum bene ordinatam, ex ipsa domus ordinatione ordinatoris rationem perpenderet; ut, ab Aristotele dictum, Tullius introducit in libro de natura deorum. Secundo autem apparet idem ex consideratione divinae bonitatis, per quam res in esse productae sunt, ut ex supra dictis patet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0015","text":"Cum enim optimi sit optima producere, non convenit summae Dei bonitati quod res productas ad perfectum non perducat. Ultima autem perfectio uniuscuiusque est in consecutione finis. Unde ad divinam bonitatem pertinet ut, sicut produxit res in esse, ita etiam eas ad finem perducat. Quod est gubernare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0016","text":"[I q. 103 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32698)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod aliquid movetur vel operatur propter finem dupliciter. Uno modo, sicut agens seipsum in finem, ut homo et aliae creaturae rationales, et talium est cognoscere rationem finis, et eorum quae sunt ad finem. Aliquid autem dicitur moveri vel operari propter finem, quasi ab alio actum vel directum in finem, sicut sagitta movetur ad signum directa a sagittante, qui cognoscit finem, non autem sagitta. Unde sicut motus sagittae ad determinatum finem demonstrat aperte quod sagitta dirigitur ab aliquo cognoscente; ita certus cursus naturalium rerum cognitione carentium, manifeste declarat mundum ratione aliqua gubernari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0018","text":"[I q. 103 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32699)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod in omnibus rebus creatis est aliquid stabile, ad minus prima materia; et aliquid ad motum pertinens, ut sub motu etiam operationem comprehendamus. Et quantum ad utrumque, res indiget gubernatione, quia hoc ipsum quod in rebus est stabile, in nihilum decideret (quia ex nihilo est), nisi manu gubernatoris servaretur, ut infra patebit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0020","text":"[I q. 103 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32700)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod necessitas naturalis inhaerens rebus quae determinantur ad unum, est impressio quaedam Dei dirigentis ad finem, sicut necessitas qua sagitta agitur ut ad certum signum tendat, est impressio sagittantis, et non sagittae. Sed in hoc differt, quia id quod creaturae a Deo recipiunt, est earum natura; quod autem ab homine rebus naturalibus imprimitur praeter earum naturam, ad violentiam pertinet. Unde sicut necessitas violentiae in motu sagittae demonstrat sagittantis directionem; ita necessitas naturalis creaturarum demonstrat divinae providentiae gubernationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0023","text":"[I q. 103 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32701)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod finis gubernationis mundi non sit aliquid extra mundum existens. Illud enim est finis gubernationis rei, ad quod res gubernata perducitur. Sed illud ad quod res aliqua perducitur, est aliquod bonum in ipsa re, sicut infirmus perducitur ad sanitatem, quae est aliquod bonum in ipso. Ergo finis gubernationis rerum non est aliquod bonum extrinsecum, sed aliquod bonum in ipsis rebus existens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0025","text":"[I q. 103 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32702)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0026","text":"Praeterea, philosophus dicit, I Ethic., quod *finium quidam sunt operationes, quidam opera*, idest operata. Sed nihil extrinsecum a toto universo potest esse operatum, operatio autem est in ipsis operantibus. Ergo nihil extrinsecum potest esse finis gubernationis rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0027","text":"[I q. 103 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32703)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0028","text":"Praeterea, bonum multitudinis videtur esse ordo et pax, quae est tranquillitas ordinis. Ut Augustinus dicit XIX de Civ. Dei. Sed mundus in quadam rerum multitudine consistit. Ergo finis gubernationis mundi est pacificus ordo, qui est in ipsis rebus. Non ergo finis gubernationis rerum est quoddam bonum extrinsecum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0029","text":"[I q. 103 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32704)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0030","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Prov. XVI, *universa propter se operatus est dominus*. Ipse autem est extra totum ordinem universi. Ergo finis rerum est quoddam bonum extrinsecum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0031","text":"[I q. 103 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32705)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0032","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum finis respondeat principio, non potest fieri ut, principio cognito, quid sit rerum finis ignoretur. Cum igitur principium rerum sit aliquid extrinsecum a toto universo, scilicet Deus, ut ex supra dictis patet; necesse est quod etiam finis rerum sit quoddam bonum extrinsecum. Et hoc ratione apparet. Manifestum est enim quod bonum habet rationem finis. Unde finis particularis alicuius rei est quoddam bonum particulare, finis autem universalis rerum omnium est quoddam bonum universale. Bonum autem universale est quod est per se et per suam essentiam bonum, quod est ipsa essentia bonitatis, bonum autem particulare est quod est participative bonum. Manifestum est autem quod in tota universitate creaturarum nullum est bonum quod non sit participative bonum. Unde illud bonum quod est finis totius universi, oportet quod sit extrinsecum a toto universo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0033","text":"[I q. 103 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32706)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0034","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod bonum aliquod consequimur multipliciter, uno modo, sicut formam in nobis existentem, ut sanitatem aut scientiam; alio modo, ut aliquid per nos operatum, sicut aedificator consequitur finem faciendo domum; alio modo, sicut aliquod bonum habitum vel possessum, ut ille qui emit, consequitur finem possidendo agrum. Unde nihil prohibet illud ad quod perducitur universum, esse quoddam bonum extrinsecum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0035","text":"[I q. 103 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32707)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0036","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod philosophus loquitur de finibus artium, quarum quaedam habent pro finibus operationes ipsas, sicut citharistae finis est citharizare; quaedam vero habent pro fine quoddam operatum, sicut aedificatoris finis non est aedificare, sed domus. Contingit autem aliquid extrinsecum esse finem non solum sicut operatum, sed etiam sicut possessum seu habitum, vel etiam sicut repraesentatum, sicut si dicamus quod Hercules est finis imaginis, quae fit ad eum repraesentandum. Sic igitur potest dici quod bonum extrinsecum a toto universo est finis gubernationis rerum sicut habitum et repraesentatum, quia ad hoc unaquaeque res tendit, ut participet ipsum, et assimiletur ei, quantum potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0037","text":"[I q. 103 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32708)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0038","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod finis quidem universi est aliquod bonum in ipso existens, scilicet ordo ipsius universi, hoc autem bonum non est ultimus finis, sed ordinatur ad bonum extrinsecum ut ad ultimum finem; sicut etiam ordo exercitus ordinatur ad ducem, ut dicitur in XII Metaphys.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0039","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0040","text":"[I q. 103 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32709)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0041","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod mundus non gubernetur ab uno. De causa enim per effectus iudicamus. Sed in gubernatione rerum apparet quod res non uniformiter moventur et operantur, quaedam enim contingenter, quaedam vero ex necessitate, et secundum alias diversitates. Ergo mundus non gubernatur ab uno.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0042","text":"[I q. 103 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32710)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0043","text":"Praeterea, ea quae gubernantur ab uno, a se invicem non dissentiunt nisi propter imperitiam aut impotentiam gubernantis, quae a Deo sunt procul. Sed res creatae a se invicem dissentiunt, et contra se invicem pugnant; ut in contrariis apparet. Non ergo mundus gubernatur ab uno.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0044","text":"[I q. 103 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32711)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0045","text":"Praeterea, in natura semper invenitur quod melius est. Sed melius est simul esse duos quam unum, ut dicitur Eccle. IV. Ergo mundus non gubernatur ab uno, sed a pluribus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0046","text":"[I q. 103 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32712)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0047","text":"Sed contra est quod unum Deum et unum dominum confitemur; secundum illud apostoli I ad Cor. VIII, *nobis est unus Deus, pater, et dominus unus*. Quorum utrumque ad gubernationem pertinet, nam ad dominum pertinet gubernatio subditorum; et Dei nomen ex providentia sumitur, ut supra dictum est. Ergo mundus gubernatur ab uno.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0048","text":"[I q. 103 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32713)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0049","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere quod mundus ab uno gubernetur. Cum enim finis gubernationis mundi sit quod est essentialiter bonum, quod est optimum, necesse est quod mundi gubernatio sit optima. Optima autem gubernatio est quae fit per unum. Cuius ratio est, quia gubernatio nihil aliud est quam directio gubernatorum ad finem, qui est aliquod bonum. Unitas autem pertinet ad rationem bonitatis; ut Boetius probat, in III de Consol., per hoc quod. Sicut omnia desiderant bonum, ita desiderant unitatem, sine qua esse non possunt. Nam unumquodque intantum est, inquantum unum est, unde videmus quod res repugnant suae divisioni quantum possunt, et quod dissolutio uniuscuiusque rei provenit ex defectu illius rei. Et ideo id ad quod tendit intentio multitudinem gubernantis, est unitas sive pax. Unitatis autem causa per se est unum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0050","text":"Manifestum est enim quod plures multa unire et concordare non possunt, nisi ipsi aliquo modo uniantur. Illud autem quod est per se unum, potest convenientius et melius esse causa unitatis, quam multi uniti. Unde multitudo melius gubernatur per unum quam per plures. Relinquitur ergo quod gubernatio mundi, quae est optima, sit ab uno gubernante. Et hoc est quod philosophus dicit in XII Metaphys., *entia nolunt disponi male, nec bonum pluralitas principatuum, unus ergo princeps*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0051","text":"[I q. 103 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32714)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0052","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod motus est actus mobilis a movente. Difformitas ergo motuum est ex diversitate mobilium, quam requirit perfectio universi, ut supra dictum est; non ex diversitate gubernantium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0053","text":"[I q. 103 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32715)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0054","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod contraria, etsi dissentiant quantum ad fines proximos, conveniunt tamen quantum ad finem ultimum, prout concluduntur sub uno ordine universi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0055","text":"[I q. 103 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32716)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0056","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in particularibus bonis duo sunt meliora quam unum, sed ei quod est essentialiter bonum, non potest fieri aliqua additio bonitatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0057","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0058","text":"[I q. 103 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32717)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0059","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod effectus gubernationis mundi sit unus tantum, et non plures. Effectus enim gubernationis esse videtur id quod per gubernationem in rebus gubernatis causatur. Hoc autem est unum, scilicet bonum ordinis; ut in exercitu patet. Ergo gubernationis mundi est unus effectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0060","text":"[I q. 103 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32718)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0061","text":"Praeterea, ab uno natum est unum tantum procedere. Sed mundus gubernatur ab uno, ut ostensum est. Ergo et gubernationis effectus est unus tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0062","text":"[I q. 103 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32719)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0063","text":"Praeterea, si effectus gubernationis non est unus tantum propter unitatem gubernantis, oportet quod multiplicetur secundum multitudinem gubernatorum. Haec autem sunt nobis innumerabilia. Ergo gubernationis effectus non possunt comprehendi sub aliquo certo numero.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0064","text":"[I q. 103 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32720)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0065","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, quod *deitas providentia et bonitate perfecta omnia continet, et seipsa implet*. Gubernatio autem ad providentiam pertinet. Ergo gubernationis divinae sunt aliqui determinati effectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0066","text":"[I q. 103 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32721)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0067","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod effectus cuiuslibet actionis ex fine eius pensari potest, nam per operationem efficitur ut pertingatur ad finem. Finis autem gubernationis mundi est bonum essentiale, ad cuius participationem et assimilationem omnia tendunt. Effectus igitur gubernationis potest accipi tripliciter. Uno modo, ex parte ipsius finis, et sic est unus effectus gubernationis, scilicet assimilari summo bono. Alio modo potest considerari effectus gubernationis secundum ea quibus ad Dei assimilationem creatura perducitur. Et sic in generali sunt duo effectus gubernationis. Creatura enim assimilatur Deo quantum ad duo, scilicet quantum ad id quod Deus bonus est, inquantum creatura est bona; et quantum ad hoc quod Deus est aliis causa bonitatis, inquantum una creatura movet aliam ad bonitatem. Unde duo sunt effectus gubernationis, scilicet conservatio rerum in bono, et motio earum ad bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0068","text":"Tertio modo potest considerari effectus gubernationis in particulari, et sic sunt nobis innumerabiles.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0069","text":"[I q. 103 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32722)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0070","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ordo universi includit in se et conservationem rerum diversarum a Deo institutarum, et motionem earum, quia secundum haec duo invenitur ordo in rebus, secundum scilicet quod una est melior alia, et secundum quod una ab alia movetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0071","text":"[I q. 103 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32723)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0072","text":"Ad alia duo patet responsio per ea quae dicta sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0073","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0074","text":"[I q. 103 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32724)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0075","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non omnia divinae gubernationi subdantur. Dicitur enim Eccle. IX, *vidi sub sole nec velocium esse cursum, nec fortium bellum, nec sapientium panem, nec doctorum divitias, nec artificum gratiam, sed tempus casumque in omnibus*. Quae autem gubernationi alicuius subsunt, non sunt casualia. Ergo ea quae sunt sub sole, non subduntur divinae gubernationi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0076","text":"[I q. 103 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32725)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0077","text":"Praeterea, apostolus, I ad Cor. IX, dicit quod *non est Deo cura de bobus*. Sed unicuique est cura eorum quae gubernantur ab ipso. Non ergo omnia subduntur divinae gubernationi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0078","text":"[I q. 103 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32726)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0079","text":"Praeterea, illud quod seipsum gubernare potest, non videtur alterius gubernatione indigere. Sed creatura rationalis seipsam gubernare potest, cum habeat dominium sui actus, et per se agat; et non solum agatur ab alio, quod videtur esse eorum quae gubernantur. Ergo non omnia subsunt divinae gubernationi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0080","text":"[I q. 103 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32727)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0081","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, V de Civ. Dei, quod *Deus non solum caelum et terram, nec solum hominem et Angelum, sed nec exigui et contemptibilis animantis viscera, nec avis pennulam, nec herbae flosculum, nec arboris folium, sine suarum partium convenientia dereliquit*. Omnia ergo eius gubernationi subduntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0082","text":"[I q. 103 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32728)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0083","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod secundum eandem rationem competit Deo esse gubernatorem rerum, et causam earum, quia eiusdem est rem producere, et ei perfectionem dare, quod ad gubernantem pertinet. Deus autem est causa non quidem particularis unius generis rerum, sed universalis totius entis, ut supra ostensum est. Unde sicut nihil potest esse quod non sit a Deo creatum, ita nihil potest esse quod eius gubernationi non subdatur. Patet etiam hoc idem ex ratione finis. Intantum enim alicuius gubernatio se extendit, inquantum se extendere potest finis gubernationis. Finis autem divinae gubernationis est ipsa sua bonitas, ut supra ostensum est. Unde cum nihil esse possit quod non ordinetur in divinam bonitatem sicut in finem, ut ex supra dictis patet; impossibile est quod aliquod entium subtrahatur gubernationi divinae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0084","text":"Stulta igitur fuit opinio dicentium quod haec inferiora corruptibilia, vel etiam singularia, aut etiam res humanae non gubernantur a Deo. Ex quorum persona dicitur Ezech. IX, *dereliquit dominus terram*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0085","text":"[I q. 103 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32729)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0086","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod sub sole dicuntur esse ea quae secundum motum solis generantur et corrumpuntur. In quibus omnibus casus invenitur; non ita quod omnia quae in eis fiunt, sint casualia; sed quia in quolibet eorum aliquid casuale inveniri potest. Et hoc ipsum quod aliquid casuale invenitur in huiusmodi rebus, demonstrat ea alicuius gubernationi esse subiecta. Nisi enim huiusmodi corruptibilia ab aliquo superiori gubernarentur, nihil intenderent, maxime quae non cognoscunt, et sic non eveniret in eis aliquid praeter intentionem, quod facit rationem casus. Unde ad ostendendum quod casualia secundum ordinem alicuius superioris causae proveniunt, non dicit simpliciter quod vidit casum esse in omnibus, sed dicit tempus et casum; quia scilicet secundum aliquem ordinem temporis, casuales defectus inveniuntur in his rebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0087","text":"[I q. 103 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32730)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0088","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod gubernatio est quaedam mutatio gubernatorum a gubernante. Omnis autem motus est actus mobilis a movente, ut dicitur in III Physic. Omnis autem actus proportionatur ei cuius est actus. Et sic oportet quod diversa mobilia diversimode moveantur, etiam secundum motionem unius motoris. Sic igitur secundum unam artem Dei gubernantis, res diversimode gubernantur, secundum earum diversitatem. Quaedam enim secundum suam naturam sunt per se agentia, tanquam habentia dominium sui actus, et ista gubernantur a Deo non solum per hoc quod moventur ab ipso Deo in eis interius operante, sed etiam per hoc quod ab eo inducuntur ad bonum et retrahuntur a malo per praecepta et prohibitiones, praemia et poenas. Hoc autem modo non gubernantur a Deo creaturae irrationales, quae tantum aguntur, et non agunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0089","text":"Cum ergo apostolus dicit quod Deo non est cura de bobus, non totaliter subtrahit boves a cura gubernationis divinae; sed solum quantum ad modum qui proprie competit rationali creaturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0090","text":"[I q. 103 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32731)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0091","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod creatura rationalis gubernat seipsam per intellectum et voluntatem, quorum utrumque indiget regi et perfici ab intellectu et voluntate Dei. Et ideo supra gubernationem qua creatura rationalis gubernat seipsam tanquam domina sui actus, indiget gubernari a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0092","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0093","text":"[I q. 103 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32732)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0094","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod omnia immediate gubernentur a Deo. Gregorius enim Nyssenus reprehendit opinionem Platonis, qui divisit providentiam in tria, primam quidem primi Dei, qui providet rebus caelestibus, et universalibus omnibus; secundam vero providentiam esse dixit secundorum deorum, qui caelum circumeunt, scilicet respectu eorum quae sunt in generatione et corruptione; tertiam vero providentiam dixit quorundam Daemonum, qui sunt custodes circa terram humanarum actionum. Ergo videtur quod omnia immediate a Deo gubernentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0095","text":"[I q. 103 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32733)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0096","text":"Praeterea, melius est aliquid fieri per unum quam per multa, si sit possibile, ut dicitur in VIII Physic. Sed Deus potest per seipsum absque mediis causis omnia gubernare. Ergo videtur quod omnia immediate gubernet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0097","text":"[I q. 103 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32734)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0098","text":"Praeterea, nihil in Deo est deficiens et imperfectum. Sed ad defectum gubernatoris pertinere videtur quod mediantibus aliquibus gubernet, sicut rex terrenus, quia non sufficit ad omnia agenda, nec ubique est praesens in suo regno, propter hoc oportet quod habeat suae gubernationis ministros. Ergo Deus immediate omnia gubernat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0099","text":"[I q. 103 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32735)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0100","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, in III de Trin., *quemadmodum corpora crassiora et inferiora per subtiliora et potentiora quodam ordine reguntur; ita omnia corpora per spiritum vitae rationalem, et spiritus vitae rationalis desertor atque peccator per spiritum vitae rationalem pium et iustum, et ille per ipsum Deum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0101","text":"[I q. 103 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32736)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0102","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in gubernatione duo sunt consideranda, scilicet ratio gubernationis, quae est ipsa providentia; et executio. Quantum igitur ad rationem gubernationis pertinet, Deus immediate omnia gubernat, quantum autem pertinet ad executionem gubernationis, Deus gubernat quaedam mediantibus aliis. Cuius ratio est quia, cum Deus sit ipsa essentia bonitatis, unumquodque attribuendum est Deo secundum sui optimum. Optimum autem in omni genere vel ratione vel cognitione practica, qualis est ratio gubernationis, in hoc consistit, quod particularia cognoscantur, in quibus est actus, sicut optimus medicus est, non qui considerat sola universalia, sed qui potest etiam considerare minima particularium; et idem patet in ceteris. Unde oportet dicere quod Deus omnium etiam minimorum particularium rationem gubernationis habeat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0103","text":"Sed cum per gubernationem res quae gubernantur sint ad perfectionem perducendae; tanto erit melior gubernatio, quanto maior perfectio a gubernante rebus gubernatis communicatur. Maior autem perfectio est quod aliquid in se sit bonum, et etiam sit aliis causa bonitatis, quam si esset solummodo in se bonum. Et ideo sic Deus gubernat res, ut quasdam aliarum in gubernando causas instituat, sicut si aliquis magister discipulos suos non solum scientes faceret, sed etiam aliorum doctores.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0104","text":"[I q. 103 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32737)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0105","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod opinio Platonis reprehenditur, quia etiam quantum ad rationem gubernationis, posuit Deum non immediate omnia gubernare. Quod patet per hoc, quod divisit in tria providentiam, quae est ratio gubernationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0106","text":"[I q. 103 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32738)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0107","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, si solus Deus gubernaret, subtraheretur perfectio causalis a rebus. Unde non totum fieret per unum, quod fit per multa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0108","text":"[I q. 103 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32739)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0109","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod non solum pertinet ad imperfectionem regis terreni quod executores habeat suae gubernationis, sed etiam ad regis dignitatem, quia ex ordine ministrorum potestas regia praeclarior redditur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0110","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0111","text":"[I q. 103 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32740)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0112","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aliquid praeter ordinem divinae gubernationis contingere possit. Dicit enim Boetius, in III de Consol., quod *Deus per bonum cuncta disponit*. Si ergo nihil in rebus contingit praeter ordinem divinae gubernationis, sequeretur quod nihil esset malum in rebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0113","text":"[I q. 103 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32741)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0114","text":"Praeterea, nihil est casuale quod evenit secundum praeordinationem alicuius gubernantis. Si igitur nihil accidit in rebus praeter ordinem gubernationis divinae, sequitur quod nihil in rebus sit fortuitum et casuale.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0115","text":"[I q. 103 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32742)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0116","text":"Praeterea, ordo divinae gubernationis est certus et immutabilis, quia est secundum rationem aeternam. Si igitur nihil possit contingere in rebus praeter ordinem divinae gubernationis, sequitur quod omnia ex necessitate eveniant, et nihil sit in rebus contingens, quod est inconveniens. Potest igitur in rebus aliquid contingere praeter ordinem gubernationis divinae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0117","text":"[I q. 103 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32743)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0118","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Esther XIII, *domine Deus, rex omnipotens, in ditione tua cuncta sunt posita, et non est qui possit resistere tuae voluntati*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0119","text":"[I q. 103 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32744)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0120","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod praeter ordinem alicuius particularis causae, aliquis effectus evenire potest; non autem praeter ordinem causae universalis. Cuius ratio est, quia praeter ordinem particularis causae nihil provenit nisi ex aliqua alia causa impediente, quam quidem causam necesse est reducere in primam causam universalem, sicut indigestio contingit praeter ordinem virtutis nutritivae, ex aliquo impedimento, puta ex grossitie cibi, quam necesse est reducere in aliquam aliam causam, et sic usque ad causam primam universalem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0121","text":"Cum igitur Deus sit prima causa universalis non unius generis tantum, sed universaliter totius entis; impossibile est quod aliquid contingat praeter ordinem divinae gubernationis, sed ex hoc ipso quod aliquid ex una parte videtur exire ab ordine divinae providentiae qui consideratur secundum aliquam particularem causam, necesse est quod in eundem ordinem relabatur secundum aliam causam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0122","text":"[I q. 103 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32745)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0123","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod nihil invenitur in mundo quod sit totaliter malum, quia malum semper fundatur in bono, ut supra ostensum est. Et ideo res aliqua dicitur mala, per hoc quod exit ab ordine alicuius particularis boni. Si autem totaliter exiret ab ordine gubernationis divinae, totaliter nihil esset.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0124","text":"[I q. 103 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32746)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0125","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod aliqua dicuntur esse casualia in rebus, per ordinem ad causas particulares, extra quarum ordinem fiunt. Sed quantum ad divinam providentiam pertinet, nihil fit casu in mundo, ut Augustinus dicit in libro octoginta trium quaest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0126","text":"[I q. 103 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32747)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0127","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod dicuntur aliqui effectus contingentes, per comparationem ad proximas causas, quae in suis effectibus deficere possunt, non propter hoc quod aliquid fieri possit extra totum ordinem gubernationis divinae. Quia hoc ipsum quod aliquid contingit praeter ordinem causae proximae, est ex aliqua causa subiecta gubernationi divinae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0128","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0129","text":"[I q. 103 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32748)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0130","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aliquid possit reniti contra ordinem gubernationis divinae. Dicitur enim Isaiae III, *lingua eorum et adinventiones eorum contra dominum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0131","text":"[I q. 103 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32749)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0132","text":"Praeterea, nullus rex iuste punit eos qui eius ordinationi non repugnant. Si igitur nihil contraniteretur divinae ordinationi, nullus iuste puniretur a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0133","text":"[I q. 103 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32750)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0134","text":"Praeterea, quaelibet res est subiecta ordini divinae gubernationis. Sed una res ab alia impugnatur. Ergo aliqua sunt quae contranituntur divinae gubernationi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0135","text":"[I q. 103 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32751)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0136","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Boetius, in III de Consol., *non est aliquid quod summo huic bono vel velit vel possit obsistere. Est igitur summum bonum quod regit cuncta fortiter, suaviterque disponit*; ut dicitur Sap. VIII, de divina sapientia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0137","text":"[I q. 103 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32752)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0138","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ordo divinae providentiae dupliciter potest considerari, uno modo in generali, secundum scilicet quod progreditur a causa gubernativa totius; alio modo in speciali, secundum scilicet quod progreditur ex aliqua causa particulari, quae est executiva divinae gubernationis. Primo igitur modo, nihil contranititur ordini divinae gubernationis. Quod ex duobus patet. Primo quidem, ex hoc quod ordo divinae gubernationis totaliter in bonum tendit, et unaquaeque res in sua operatione et conatu non tendit nisi ad bonum, nullus enim respiciens ad malum operatur, ut Dionysius dicit. Alio modo apparet idem ex hoc quod, sicut supra dictum est, omnis inclinatio alicuius rei vel naturalis vel voluntaria, nihil est aliud quam quaedam impressio a primo movente, sicut inclinatio sagittae ad signum determinatum, nihil aliud est quam quaedam impressio a sagittante.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0139","text":"Unde omnia quae agunt vel naturaliter vel voluntarie, quasi propria sponte perveniunt in id ad quod divinitus ordinantur. Et ideo dicitur Deus omnia disponere suaviter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0140","text":"[I q. 103 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32753)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0141","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod dicuntur aliqui vel cogitare vel loqui vel agere contra Deum, non quia totaliter renitantur ordini divinae gubernationis, quia etiam peccantes intendunt aliquod bonum, sed quia contranituntur cuidam determinato bono, quod est eis conveniens secundum suam naturam aut statum. Et ideo puniuntur iuste a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0142","text":"[I q. 103 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32754)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0143","text":"Et per hoc patet solutio ad secundum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0144","text":"[I q. 103 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32755)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0145","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ex hoc quod una res alteri contrapugnat, ostenditur quod aliquid reniti potest ordini qui est ex aliqua causa particulari, non autem ordini qui dependet a causa universali totius.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"00b7d4acb9603ed9c9807f835eb170914bd8d8ff4387e5a7dc483875bc1e1e2e","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T153559316797Z_fbb264a9","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 103","source_sha256":"00b7d4acb9603ed9c9807f835eb170914bd8d8ff4387e5a7dc483875bc1e1e2e","sha256":"4d9517ab6a1321f33b4c6bfcd59eaf3dd828dcb1487cad8c4d7cd3feabf639d2","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:35:59.316863+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0002","text":"[I q. 103 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32692)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0003","text":"Having set forth concerning the creation of things and their distinction, there remains now, thirdly, the consideration of the governance of things. First, in general; second, in particular, concerning the effects of governance. Concerning the first, eight questions are asked. First, whether the world is governed by someone. Second, what is the end of its governance. Third, whether it is governed by one. Fourth, concerning the effects of governance. Fifth, whether all things are subject to divine governance. Sixth, whether all things are governed immediately by God. Seventh, whether divine governance fails in anything. Eighth, whether anything resists divine providence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0005","text":"[I q. 103 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32693)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the world is not governed by anyone. For those things are governed which are moved or act for an end. But natural things, which are a great part of the world, are not moved or do not act for an end, because they do not know the end. Therefore the world is not governed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0007","text":"[I q. 103 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32694)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0008","text":"Moreover, those things properly are governed which are moved toward something. But the world does not seem to be moved toward something, but has stability in itself. Therefore it is not governed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0009","text":"[I q. 103 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32695)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0010","text":"Moreover, that which has within itself a necessity by which it is determined to one thing does not need an external governor. But the principal parts of the world are by a certain necessity determined to one thing in their acts and motions. Therefore the world does not need governance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0011","text":"[I q. 103 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32696)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0012","text":"But to the contrary is what is said in Wisdom XIV: *but thou, O Father, governest all things by providence*. And Boethius says, in the book *On Consolation*: *O thou who governest the world by eternal reason*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0013","text":"[I q. 103 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32697)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that certain ancient philosophers took governance away from the world, saying that all things happen by chance. But this position is shown to be impossible on two grounds. First indeed, from what appears in things themselves. For we see that, in natural things, what is better comes about either always or in the greater number of cases; which would not happen unless natural things were directed by some providence to the end of the good, which is to govern. Hence the very definite order of things manifestly demonstrates the governance of the world, just as, if someone were to enter a well-ordered house, from the very ordering of the house he would infer the plan of its arranger; as Tully relates in the book *On the Nature of the Gods*, having been said by Aristotle. Secondly, moreover, the same appears from consideration of divine goodness, through which things were brought forth into being, as is clear from what has been said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0015","text":"For since it belongs to the best to produce the best things, it does not accord with God's supreme goodness that He should not bring the things produced to perfection. But the ultimate perfection of each thing consists in attaining its end. Hence it belongs to divine goodness that, as He brought things forth into being, so also He brings them to their end. And this is to govern.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0016","text":"[I q. 103 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32698)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that something is moved or acts for an end in two ways. In one way, as an agent moving itself toward an end, as a human being and other rational creatures; and it belongs to such things to know the nature of the end and of those things that are for the end. But something is said to be moved or to act for an end as being driven or directed to an end by another, just as an arrow is moved toward the mark when directed by the archer, who knows the end, but the arrow does not. Hence, just as the motion of an arrow toward a determinate end openly demonstrates that the arrow is directed by someone who knows, so the definite course of natural things lacking cognition manifestly declares that the world is governed by some reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0018","text":"[I q. 103 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32699)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0019","text":"To the second, it must be said that in all created things there is something stable, at least prime matter; and something pertaining to motion, so that under motion we also comprehend operation. And with respect to both, a thing needs governance, because that very thing which is stable in things would fall into nothingness (because it is from nothing), unless it were preserved by the hand of the governor, as will be clear below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0020","text":"[I q. 103 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32700)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0021","text":"To the third, it must be said that the natural necessity inhering in things that are determined to one thing is a certain impression of God directing them to an end, just as the necessity by which an arrow is impelled to tend toward a definite mark is an impression of the archer, and not of the arrow. But there is this difference: what creatures receive from God is their nature; whereas what is impressed by man upon natural things beyond their nature pertains to violence. Hence, just as the necessity of violence in the motion of an arrow demonstrates the direction of the archer, so the natural necessity of creatures demonstrates the governance of divine providence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0023","text":"[I q. 103 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32701)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0024","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the end of the governance of the world is not something existing outside the world. For that is the end of the governance of a thing to which the governed thing is brought. But that to which a thing is brought is some good in the thing itself, just as a sick person is brought to health, which is some good in him. Therefore the end of the governance of things is not some extrinsic good, but some good existing in the things themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0025","text":"[I q. 103 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32702)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0026","text":"Moreover, the Philosopher says, in *Ethics* I, that *some ends are operations, while others are works*, that is, things produced. But nothing extrinsic to the whole universe can be produced, whereas an operation is in those who operate themselves. Therefore, nothing extrinsic can be the end of the governance of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0027","text":"[I q. 103 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32703)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0028","text":"Moreover, the good of a multitude seems to be order and peace, which is the tranquillity of order, as Augustine says in *The City of God* XIX. But the world consists in a certain multitude of things. Therefore, the end of the governance of the world is peaceful order, which is in the things themselves. Therefore, the end of the governance of things is not some extrinsic good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0029","text":"[I q. 103 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32704)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0030","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Prov. XVI, *the Lord has made all things for himself*. But he himself is outside the whole order of the universe. Therefore, the end of things is some extrinsic good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0031","text":"[I q. 103 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32705)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0032","text":"I answer that it must be said that, since the end corresponds to the principle, it cannot come about that, once the principle is known, what the end of things is should be unknown. Therefore, since the principle of things is something extrinsic to the whole universe, namely God, as is clear from what has been said above, it is necessary that the end of things also be some extrinsic good. And this is apparent by reason. For it is manifest that the good has the character of an end. Hence the particular end of any thing is some particular good, whereas the universal end of all things is some universal good. But universal good is that which is good through itself and through its own essence, which is the very essence of goodness; whereas particular good is that which is good by participation. Now it is manifest that in the whole universality of creatures there is no good that is not good by participation. Hence that good which is the end of the whole universe must be extrinsic to the whole universe.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0033","text":"[I q. 103 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32706)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0034","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that we attain some good in many ways: in one way, as a form existing in us, such as health or knowledge; in another way, as something produced by us, as a builder attains his end by making a house; in another way, as some good had or possessed, as one who buys attains his end by possessing a field. Hence nothing prevents that to which the universe is brought from being some extrinsic good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0035","text":"[I q. 103 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32707)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0036","text":"To the second it must be said that the Philosopher speaks of the ends of arts, some of which have the operations themselves as their ends, as the end of a lyre-player is to play the lyre; while others have as their end some thing produced, as the end of a builder is not building, but a house. But something extrinsic can be an end not only as something produced, but also as something possessed or had, or even as something represented, as if we were to say that Hercules is the end of an image which is made to represent him. Thus, therefore, it can be said that the good extrinsic to the whole universe is the end of the governance of things as possessed and represented, because each thing tends to this, that it may participate in it and be assimilated to it, as far as it can.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0037","text":"[I q. 103 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32708)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0038","text":"To the third it must be said that the end of the universe is indeed some good existing in it, namely, the order of the universe itself; but this good is not the ultimate end, but is ordered to an extrinsic good as to its ultimate end, just as the order of an army is ordered to its commander, as is said in *Metaphysics* XII.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0039","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0040","text":"[I q. 103 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32709)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0041","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that the world is not governed by one. For we judge concerning a cause through its effects. But in the governance of things it appears that things are not moved and do not act uniformly: for some do so contingently, whereas others do so necessarily, and according to other differences. Therefore, the world is not governed by one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0042","text":"[I q. 103 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32710)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0043","text":"Moreover, things governed by one do not disagree with one another except because of the ignorance or powerlessness of the governor, which are far from God. But created things disagree with one another and fight against one another, as is apparent in contraries. Therefore, the world is not governed by one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0044","text":"[I q. 103 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32711)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0045","text":"Moreover, in nature that which is better is always found. But it is better for two to be together than for one, as is said in Eccles. IV. Therefore, the world is not governed by one, but by many.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0046","text":"[I q. 103 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32712)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0047","text":"On the contrary, we confess one God and one Lord, according to that saying of the Apostle in I Cor. VIII: *for us there is one God, the Father, and one Lord*. Both of these pertain to governance, for governance of subjects pertains to a lord; and the name of God is taken from providence, as was said above. Therefore, the world is governed by one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0048","text":"[I q. 103 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32713)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0049","text":"I answer that it must be said that the world is governed by one. For since the end of the governance of the world is that which is essentially good, which is the best, it is necessary that the governance of the world be the best. But the best governance is that which is carried out by one. The reason for this is that governance is nothing other than the direction of those governed toward an end, which is some good. Now unity pertains to the notion of goodness; as Boethius proves, in III of the *Consolation*, by this, that. Just as all things desire the good, so they desire unity, without which they cannot exist. For each thing exists insofar as it is one, whence we see that things resist their division as much as they can, and that the dissolution of each thing arises from a defect of that thing. And therefore that toward which the intention of one governing a multitude tends is unity or peace. But the per se cause of unity is one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0050","text":"For it is manifest that many cannot unite and make concordant many things unless they themselves are in some way united. But that which is one per se can more fittingly and better be the cause of unity than many united. Hence a multitude is better governed by one than by many. There remains, therefore, that the governance of the world, which is the best, is by one governor. And this is what the Philosopher says in XII *Metaphysics*: *beings do not wish to be badly disposed, nor is a plurality of rulers good; therefore, one ruler*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0051","text":"[I q. 103 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32714)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0052","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that motion is the act of the movable thing from the mover. Therefore, the dissimilarity of motions is from the diversity of movable things, which the perfection of the universe requires, as was said above; not from a diversity of governors.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0053","text":"[I q. 103 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32715)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0054","text":"To the second it must be said that contraries, although they disagree as regards proximate ends, nevertheless agree as regards the ultimate end, insofar as they are included under one order of the universe.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0055","text":"[I q. 103 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32716)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0056","text":"To the third it must be said that in particular goods two are better than one, but to that which is essentially good no addition of goodness can be made.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0057","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0058","text":"[I q. 103 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32717)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0059","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that the effect of the governance of the world is only one, and not several. For the effect of governance appears to be that which is caused in the governed things through governance. But this is one, namely, the good of order; as is evident in an army. Therefore, the governance of the world has one effect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0060","text":"[I q. 103 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32718)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0061","text":"Moreover, from one, only one is naturally apt to proceed. But the world is governed by one, as has been shown. Therefore, the effect of governance too is only one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0062","text":"[I q. 103 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32719)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0063","text":"Moreover, if the effect of governance is not only one on account of the unity of the governor, it must be multiplied according to the multitude of governors. But these are innumerable to us. Therefore, the effects of governance cannot be comprehended under any definite number.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0064","text":"[I q. 103 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32720)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0065","text":"But on the contrary, Dionysius says that *the deity, by providence and perfect goodness, contains all things and fills them with itself*. But governance pertains to providence. Therefore, divine governance has certain determinate effects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0066","text":"[I q. 103 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32721)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0067","text":"I answer that it must be said that the effect of any action can be weighed from its end, for through an operation it is brought about that the end be attained. Now the end of the governance of the world is the essential good, toward participation in and assimilation to which all things tend. Therefore, the effect of governance can be taken in three ways. In one way, on the part of the end itself, and thus there is one effect of governance, namely, to be assimilated to the highest good. In another way, the effect of governance can be considered according to those things by which a creature is brought to assimilation to God. And thus, in general, there are two effects of governance. For a creature is assimilated to God in two respects: namely, with respect to the fact that God is good, insofar as the creature is good; and with respect to this, that God is a cause of goodness for others, insofar as one creature moves another to goodness. Hence there are two effects of governance, namely, the preservation of things in good, and their motion toward good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0068","text":"In a third way, the effect of governance can be considered in particular, and thus they are innumerable to us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0069","text":"[I q. 103 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32722)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0070","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the order of the universe includes within itself both the preservation of the diverse things established by God and their motion, because according to these two, order is found in things: namely, according as one is better than another, and according as one is moved by another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0071","text":"[I q. 103 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32723)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0072","text":"The response to the other two is evident from what has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0073","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0074","text":"[I q. 103 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32724)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0075","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth article. It seems that not all things are subject to divine governance. For it is said in Eccles. IX, *I saw under the sun that the race is not to the swift, nor battle to the strong, nor bread to the wise, nor riches to the learned, nor favor to the skilled, but time and chance [befall] all things*. But things subject to someone's governance are not chance occurrences. Therefore, the things that are under the sun are not subject to divine governance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0076","text":"[I q. 103 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32725)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0077","text":"Further, the Apostle says, in I Cor. IX, that *God has no care for oxen*. But everyone has care for those things which are governed by him. Therefore, not all things are subject to divine governance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0078","text":"[I q. 103 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32726)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0079","text":"Further, that which can govern itself does not seem to need another's governance. But a rational creature can govern itself, since it has mastery over its own act, and acts through itself; and is not merely acted upon by another, which seems to belong to things that are governed. Therefore, not all things are subject to divine governance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0080","text":"[I q. 103 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32727)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0081","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in V *On the City of God*, that *God has not left without the fittingness of their parts not only heaven and earth, nor only man and angel, but not even the entrails of a tiny and contemptible living creature, nor a bird's little feather, nor the small flower of a plant, nor the leaf of a tree*. Therefore all things are subject to His governance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0082","text":"[I q. 103 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32728)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0083","text":"I answer that it must be said that, according to the same account, it belongs to God to be the governor of things and to be their cause, because it belongs to the same one to produce a thing and to give it perfection, which pertains to a governor. Now God is the cause, not indeed a particular cause of one genus of things, but the universal cause of all being, as was shown above. Hence, just as nothing can exist that is not created by God, so nothing can exist that is not subject to His governance. This same point is also clear from the nature of the end. For the governance of something extends as far as the end of the governance can extend. But the end of divine governance is His own goodness itself, as was shown above. Hence, since nothing can exist that is not ordered to divine goodness as to an end, as is clear from what was said above, it is impossible that any being should be withdrawn from divine governance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0084","text":"Therefore, the opinion of those who said that these lower corruptible things, or even singular things, or even human affairs, are not governed by God, was foolish. In their person it is said in Ezek. IX, *The Lord has forsaken the earth*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0085","text":"[I q. 103 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32729)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0086","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that those things are said to be under the sun which are generated and corrupted according to the motion of the sun. Chance is found in all of these; not in such a way that all things which occur in them are by chance, but because something chance-like can be found in each of them. And the very fact that something chance-like is found in things of this sort shows that they are subject to some governance. For unless such corruptible things were governed by some higher being, they would tend toward nothing, especially since they do not know; and thus nothing would occur in them apart from intention, which constitutes the notion of chance. Hence, to show that chance occurrences arise according to the order of some higher cause, he does not say simply that he saw chance in all things, but says time and chance; namely, because according to some order of time, chance defects are found in these things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0087","text":"[I q. 103 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32730)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0088","text":"To the second, it must be said that governance is a certain motion of the governed by the governor. But every motion is the act of what is movable, from a mover, as is said in III *Physics*. Now every act is proportioned to that of which it is the act. And thus it is necessary that diverse movable things be moved in diverse ways, even under the motion of one mover. Therefore, according to the one art of God governing, things are governed in diverse ways, according to their diversity. For some things by their nature are agents through themselves, as having mastery over their own act; and these are governed by God not only in that they are moved by God Himself working inwardly in them, but also in that they are led by Him to good and withdrawn from evil through precepts and prohibitions, rewards and punishments. But irrational creatures, which are only acted upon and do not act, are not governed by God in this way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0089","text":"Therefore, when the Apostle says that God has no care for oxen, he does not entirely withdraw oxen from the care of divine governance, but only as regards the mode that properly belongs to the rational creature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0090","text":"[I q. 103 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32731)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0091","text":"To the third, it must be said that a rational creature governs itself through intellect and will, each of which needs to be ruled and perfected by the intellect and will of God. And therefore, beyond the governance by which the rational creature governs itself as mistress of its own act, it needs to be governed by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0092","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0093","text":"[I q. 103 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32732)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0094","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth article. It seems that all things are governed immediately by God. For Gregory of Nyssa reproves the opinion of Plato, who divided providence into three: the first, indeed, of the first God, who provides for heavenly things and for all universals; but the second providence, he said, belonged to the secondary gods, who move around the heaven, namely with respect to things that are in generation and corruption; and the third providence, he said, belonged to certain demons, who are guardians around the earth of human actions. Therefore it seems that all things are governed immediately by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0095","text":"[I q. 103 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32733)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0096","text":"Further, it is better for something to be done through one than through many, if this is possible, as is said in VIII *Physics*. But God can govern all things through Himself without intermediate causes. Therefore it seems that He governs all things immediately.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0097","text":"[I q. 103 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32734)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0098","text":"Moreover, there is nothing deficient and imperfect in God. But it seems to pertain to a governor's deficiency that he govern through intermediaries, as does an earthly king, because he is not sufficient for doing all things, nor is he everywhere present in his kingdom; for this reason it is necessary that he have ministers of his governance. Therefore God governs all things immediately.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0099","text":"[I q. 103 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32735)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0100","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity* III: *just as grosser and lower bodies are governed in a certain order through subtler and more powerful ones, so all bodies are governed through the rational spirit of life; and the apostate and sinful rational spirit of life through the pious and just rational spirit of life; and that one through God himself*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0101","text":"[I q. 103 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32736)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0102","text":"I answer that it must be said that two things must be considered in governance: namely, the plan of governance, which is providence itself; and its execution. Therefore, as regards the plan of governance, God governs all things immediately; but as regards the execution of governance, God governs some things through others. The reason for this is that, since God is the very essence of goodness, everything must be attributed to God according to what is best in it. Now what is best in every genus, whether of reason or of practical knowledge, such as the plan of governance, consists in this: that particulars, in which action lies, are known; just as the best physician is not one who considers universals alone, but one who can also consider the least particulars; and the same is clear in other cases. Hence it must be said that God has the plan of governance even of all the least particulars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0103","text":"But since through governance the things governed must be brought to perfection, governance will be so much the better as a greater perfection is communicated by the governor to the things governed. Now it is a greater perfection for something to be good in itself and also to be a cause of goodness for others, than if it were good in itself alone. And therefore God so governs things as to establish some as causes of others in governing, just as if a teacher made his disciples not only knowledgeable, but also teachers of others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0104","text":"[I q. 103 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32737)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0105","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Plato's opinion is reproved because, even as regards the plan of governance, he held that God does not immediately govern all things. This is clear from the fact that he divided providence, which is the plan of governance, into three.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0106","text":"[I q. 103 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32738)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0107","text":"To the second it must be said that, if God alone governed, causal perfection would be taken away from things. Hence the whole would not be done through one which is done through many.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0108","text":"[I q. 103 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32739)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0109","text":"To the third it must be said that it pertains not only to the imperfection of an earthly king that he have executors of his governance, but also to the king's dignity, because through the order of ministers royal power is rendered more illustrious.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0110","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0111","text":"[I q. 103 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32740)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0112","text":"We proceed thus to the seventh. It seems that something can happen outside the order of divine governance. For Boethius says, in *On the Consolation* III, that *God disposes all things through the good*. Therefore, if nothing happens in things outside the order of divine governance, it would follow that there would be nothing evil in things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0113","text":"[I q. 103 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32741)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0114","text":"Moreover, nothing is accidental which occurs according to the preordination of some governor. Therefore, if nothing happens in things outside the order of divine governance, it follows that nothing in things is fortuitous and accidental.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0115","text":"[I q. 103 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32742)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0116","text":"Moreover, the order of divine governance is certain and immutable, because it is according to eternal reason. Therefore, if nothing could happen in things outside the order of divine governance, it follows that all things happen of necessity, and that nothing in things is contingent, which is unfitting. Therefore something can happen in things outside the order of divine governance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0117","text":"[I q. 103 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32743)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0118","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Esther XIII: *Lord God, almighty King, all things are placed under thy dominion, and there is none who can resist thy will*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0119","text":"[I q. 103 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32744)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0120","text":"I answer that it must be said that an effect can occur outside the order of some particular cause, but not outside the order of the universal cause. The reason for this is that nothing comes about outside the order of a particular cause except through some other impeding cause, which cause must be reduced to the first universal cause; just as indigestion occurs outside the order of the nutritive power through some impediment, for instance through the coarseness of food, which must be reduced to some other cause, and so on up to the first universal cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0121","text":"Since, therefore, God is the first universal cause, not of one genus only, but universally of the whole of being, it is impossible that anything should occur outside the order of divine governance; rather, from the very fact that something appears on one side to depart from the order of divine providence considered according to some particular cause, it must fall back into the same order according to another cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0122","text":"[I q. 103 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32745)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0123","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that nothing is found in the world which is wholly evil, because evil is always founded upon good, as was shown above. And therefore a thing is called evil inasmuch as it departs from the order of some particular good. But if it were to depart wholly from the order of divine governance, it would be wholly nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0124","text":"[I q. 103 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32746)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0125","text":"To the second, it must be said that certain things are said to be casual among things in relation to particular causes, outside whose order they come about. But as pertains to divine providence, nothing happens by chance in the world, as Augustine says in the book of Eighty-Three Questions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0126","text":"[I q. 103 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32747)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0127","text":"To the third, it must be said that certain effects are called contingent in comparison with proximate causes, which can fail in their effects, not because something can come to be outside the whole order of divine governance. For the very fact that something occurs outside the order of a proximate cause is from some cause subject to divine governance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0128","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0129","text":"[I q. 103 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32748)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0130","text":"We proceed thus to the eighth article. It seems that something can resist the order of divine governance. For it is said in Isaiah III, *their tongue and their inventions are against the Lord*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0131","text":"[I q. 103 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32749)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0132","text":"Moreover, no king justly punishes those who do not oppose his ordinance. Therefore, if nothing opposed the divine ordinance, no one would be justly punished by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0133","text":"[I q. 103 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32750)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0134","text":"Moreover, every thing is subject to the order of divine governance. But one thing is assailed by another. Therefore, there are some things that resist divine governance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0135","text":"[I q. 103 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32751)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0136","text":"But on the contrary is what Boethius says, in III of the Consolation: *there is nothing that either wills or is able to oppose this highest good. Therefore it is the highest good which rules all things strongly and disposes them sweetly*; as is said in Wisdom VIII concerning divine wisdom.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0137","text":"[I q. 103 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32752)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0138","text":"I answer that the order of divine providence can be considered in two ways: in one way generally, namely, insofar as it proceeds from the governing cause of the whole; in another way specially, namely, insofar as it proceeds from some particular cause, which is an executor of divine governance. In the first way, therefore, nothing resists the order of divine governance. This is evident from two things. First, indeed, from the fact that the order of divine governance tends wholly toward good, and every thing in its operation and striving tends only toward good—for no one acting with a view to evil acts, as Dionysius says. In another way, the same is evident from the fact that, as was said above, every inclination of any thing, whether natural or voluntary, is nothing other than a certain impression from the first mover, just as the inclination of an arrow toward a determinate mark is nothing other than a certain impression from the archer.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0139","text":"Hence all things which act either naturally or voluntarily arrive, as though of their own accord, at that to which they are divinely ordered. And therefore God is said to dispose all things sweetly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0140","text":"[I q. 103 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32753)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0141","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that some are said to think or speak or act against God, not because they wholly resist the order of divine governance, since even those who sin intend some good, but because they resist a certain determinate good which is fitting for them according to their nature or state. And therefore they are justly punished by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0142","text":"[I q. 103 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32754)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0143","text":"And through this the solution to the second is evident.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0144","text":"[I q. 103 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32755)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0145","text":"To the third, it must be said that from the fact that one thing is opposed to another, it is shown that something can resist the order that is from some particular cause, but not the order that depends on the universal cause of the whole.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_95:P0145"]}],"source_digest":"22a14eda8951cf7ba4a3de71e15c4a95038f422c8d37ca5cb6e783c7ff5246a1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 103"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 103","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96","title":"First Part · Question 104","order":105,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_96","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 104 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32756)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de effectibus divinae gubernationis in speciali. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum creaturae indigeant ut conserventur in esse a Deo. Secundo, utrum conserventur a Deo immediate. Tertio, utrum Deus possit aliquid redigere in nihilum. Quarto, utrum aliquid in nihilum redigatur.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 104 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32757)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod creaturae non indigeant ut a Deo conserventur in esse. Quod enim non potest non esse, non indiget ut conservetur in esse, sicut quod non potest abscedere, non indiget ut conservetur ne abscedat. Sed quaedam creaturae sunt quae secundum sui naturam non possunt non esse. Ergo non omnes creaturae indigent ut a Deo conserventur in esse. Probatio mediae. Quod per se inest alicui, necesse est ei inesse, et oppositum eius impossibile est ei inesse, sicut necessarium est binarium esse parem, et impossibile est eum esse imparem. Esse autem per se consequitur ad formam, quia unumquodque secundum hoc est ens actu, quod habet formam. Quaedam autem creaturae sunt, quae sunt formae quaedam subsistentes, sicut de Angelis dictum est; et sic per se inest eis esse. Et eadem ratio est de illis quorum materia non est in potentia nisi ad unam formam, sicut supra dictum est de corporibus caelestibus. Huiusmodi ergo creaturae secundum suam naturam ex necessitate sunt, et non possunt non esse, potentia enim ad non esse non potest fundari neque in forma, quam per se sequitur esse; neque in materia existente sub forma quam non potest amittere, cum non sit in potentia ad aliam formam.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32758)\n\nPraeterea, Deus est potentior quolibet creato agente. Sed aliquod creatum agens potest communicare suo effectui ut conservetur in esse, etiam eius operatione cessante, sicut cessante actione aedificatoris, remanet domus; et cessante actione ignis, remanet aqua calefacta per aliquod tempus. Ergo multo magis Deus potest suae creaturae conferre quod conservetur in esse, sua operatione cessante.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32759)\n\nPraeterea, nullum violentum potest contingere absque aliqua causa agente. Sed tendere ad non esse est innaturale et violentum cuilibet creaturae, quia quaelibet creatura naturaliter appetit esse. Ergo nulla creatura potest tendere in non esse, nisi aliquo agente ad corruptionem. Sed quaedam sunt ad quorum corruptionem nihil agere potest; sicut spirituales substantiae, et corpora caelestia. Ergo huiusmodi creaturae non possunt tendere in non esse, etiam Dei operatione cessante.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32760)\n\nPraeterea, si Deus conservat res in esse, hoc erit per aliquam actionem. Per quamlibet autem actionem agentis, si sit efficax, aliquid fit in effectu. Oportet igitur quod per actionem Dei conservantis aliquid fiat in creatura. Sed hoc non videtur. Non enim per huiusmodi actionem fit ipsum esse creaturae, quia quod iam est, non fit. Neque iterum aliquid aliud superadditum, quia vel non continue Deus conservaret creaturam in esse, vel continue aliquid adderetur creaturae, quod est inconveniens. Non igitur creaturae conservantur in esse a Deo.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32761)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Heb. I, *portans omnia verbo virtutis suae*.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32762)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere, et secundum fidem et secundum rationem, quod creaturae conservantur in esse a Deo. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod aliquid conservatur ab altero dupliciter. Uno modo, indirecte et per accidens, sicut ille dicitur rem conservare, qui removet corrumpens; puta si aliquis puerum custodiat ne cadat in ignem, dicitur eum conservare. Et sic etiam Deus dicitur aliqua conservare, sed non omnia, quia quaedam sunt quae non habent corrumpentia, quae necesse sit removere ad rei conservationem. Alio modo dicitur aliquid rem aliquam conservare per se et directe, inquantum scilicet illud quod conservatur, dependet a conservante, ut sine eo esse non possit. Et hoc modo omnes creaturae indigent divina conservatione. Dependet enim esse cuiuslibet creaturae a Deo, ita quod nec ad momentum subsistere possent, sed in nihilum redigerentur, nisi operatione divinae virtutis conservarentur in esse, sicut Gregorius dicit. Et hoc sic perspici potest. Omnis enim effectus dependet a sua causa, secundum quod est causa eius. Sed considerandum est quod aliquod agens est causa sui effectus secundum fieri tantum, et non directe secundum esse eius. Quod quidem contingit et in artificialibus, et in rebus naturalibus. Aedificator enim est causa domus quantum ad eius fieri, non autem directe quantum ad esse eius. Manifestum est enim quod esse domus consequitur formam eius, forma autem domus est compositio et ordo, quae quidem forma consequitur naturalem virtutem quarundam rerum. Sicut enim coquus coquit cibum adhibendo aliquam virtutem naturalem activam, scilicet ignis; ita aedificator facit domum adhibendo caementum, lapides et ligna, quae sunt susceptiva et conservativa talis compositionis et ordinis. Unde esse domus dependet ex naturis harum rerum, sicut fieri domus dependet ex actione aedificatoris. Et simili ratione est considerandum in rebus naturalibus. Quia si aliquod agens non est causa formae inquantum huiusmodi, non erit per se causa esse quod consequitur ad talem formam, sed erit causa effectus secundum fieri tantum. Manifestum est autem quod, si aliqua duo sunt eiusdem speciei, unum non potest esse per se causa formae alterius, inquantum est talis forma, quia sic esset causa formae propriae, cum sit eadem ratio utriusque. Sed potest esse causa huiusmodi formae secundum quod est in materia, idest quod haec materia acquirat hanc formam. Et hoc est esse causa secundum fieri; sicut cum homo generat hominem, et ignis ignem. Et ideo quandocumque naturalis effectus est natus impressionem agentis recipere secundum eandem rationem secundum quam est in agente, tunc fieri effectus dependet ab agente, non autem esse ipsius. Sed aliquando effectus non est natus recipere impressionem agentis secundum eandem rationem secundum quam est in agente, sicut patet in omnibus agentibus quae non agunt simile secundum speciem; sicut caelestia corpora sunt causa generationis inferiorum corporum dissimilium secundum speciem. Et tale agens potest esse causa formae secundum rationem talis formae, et non solum secundum quod acquiritur in hac materia, et ideo est causa non solum fiendi, sed essendi. Sicut igitur fieri rei non potest remanere, cessante actione agentis quod est causa effectus secundum fieri; ita nec esse rei potest remanere, cessante actione agentis quod est causa effectus non solum secundum fieri, sed etiam secundum esse. Et haec est ratio quare aqua calefacta retinet calorem, cessante actione ignis; non autem remanet aer illuminatus, nec ad momentum, cessante actione solis. Quia scilicet materia aquae susceptiva est caloris ignis secundum eandem rationem qua est in igne, unde si perfecte perducatur ad formam ignis, retinebit calorem semper; si autem imperfecte participet aliquid de forma ignis secundum quandam inchoationem, calor non semper remanebit, sed ad tempus, propter debilem participationem principii caloris. Aer autem nullo modo natus est recipere lumen secundum eandem rationem secundum quam est in sole, ut scilicet recipiat formam solis, quae est principium luminis, et ideo, quia non habet radicem in aere, statim cessat lumen, cessante actione solis. Sic autem se habet omnis creatura ad Deum, sicut aer ad solem illuminantem. Sicut enim sol est lucens per suam naturam, aer autem fit luminosus participando lumen a sole, non tamen participando naturam solis; ita solus Deus est ens per essentiam suam, quia eius essentia est suum esse; omnis autem creatura est ens participative, non quod sua essentia sit eius esse. Et ideo, ut Augustinus dicit IV super Gen. ad Litt., *virtus Dei ab eis quae creata sunt regendis si cessaret aliquando, simul et illorum cessaret species, omnisque natura concideret*. Et in VIII eiusdem libri dicit quod, *sicut aer praesente lumine fit lucidus, sic homo, Deo sibi praesente, illuminatur, absente autem, continuo tenebratur*.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32763)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod esse per se consequitur formam creaturae, supposito tamen influxu Dei, sicut lumen sequitur diaphanum aeris, supposito influxu solis. Unde potentia ad non esse in spiritualibus creaturis et corporibus caelestibus, magis est in Deo, qui potest subtrahere suum influxum, quam in forma vel in materia talium creaturarum.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32764)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Deus non potest communicare alicui creaturae ut conservetur in esse, sua operatione cessante; sicut non potest ei communicare quod non sit causa esse illius. Intantum enim indiget creatura conservari a Deo, inquantum esse effectus dependet a causa essendi. Unde non est simile de agente quod non est causa essendi, sed fieri tantum.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32765)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ratio illa procedit de conservatione quae est per remotionem corrumpentis; qua non indigent omnes creaturae, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32766)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod conservatio rerum a Deo non est per aliquam novam actionem; sed per continuationem actionis qua dat esse, quae quidem actio est sine motu et tempore. Sicut etiam conservatio luminis in aere est per continuatum influxum a sole.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 104 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32767)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus immediate omnem creaturam conservet. Eadem enim actione Deus est conservator rerum, qua et creator, ut dictum est. Sed Deus immediate est creator omnium. Ergo immediate est etiam conservator.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32768)\n\nPraeterea, unaquaeque res magis est proxima sibi, quam rei alteri. Sed non potest communicari alicui creaturae quod conservet seipsam. Ergo multo minus potest ei communicari quod conservet aliam. Ergo Deus omnia conservat absque aliqua media causa conservante.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32769)\n\nPraeterea, effectus conservatur in esse ab eo quod est causa eius non solum secundum fieri, sed etiam secundum esse. Sed omnes causae creatae, ut videtur, non sunt causae suorum effectuum nisi secundum fieri, non sunt enim causae nisi movendo, ut supra habitum est. Ergo non sunt causae conservantes suos effectus in esse.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32770)\n\nSed contra est quod per idem conservatur res, per quod habet esse. Sed Deus dat esse rebus mediantibus aliquibus causis mediis. Ergo etiam res in esse conservat mediantibus aliquibus causis.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32771)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, dupliciter aliquid rem aliquam in esse conservat, uno modo, indirecte et per accidens, per hoc quod removet vel impedit actionem corrumpentis; alio modo, directe et per se, quia ab eo dependet esse alterius, sicut a causa dependet esse effectus. Utroque autem modo aliqua res creata invenitur esse alterius conservativa. Manifestum est enim quod etiam in rebus corporalibus multa sunt quae impediunt actiones corrumpentium, et per hoc dicuntur rerum conservativa; sicut sal impedit carnes a putrefactione, et simile est in multis aliis. Invenitur etiam quod ab aliqua creatura dependet aliquis effectus secundum suum esse. Cum enim sunt multae causae ordinatae, necesse est quod effectus dependeat primo quidem et principaliter a causa prima; secundario vero ab omnibus causis mediis. Et ideo principaliter quidem prima causa est effectus conservativa; secundario vero omnes mediae causae, et tanto magis quanto causa fuerit altior et primae causae proximior. Unde superioribus causis, etiam in corporalibus rebus, attribuitur conservatio et permanentia rerum, sicut philosophus dicit, in XII Metaphys., quod primus motus, scilicet diurnus, est causa continuitatis generationis; secundus autem motus, qui est per zodiacum, est causa diversitatis quae est secundum generationem et corruptionem. Et similiter astrologi, attribuunt Saturno, qui est supremus planetarum; res fixas et permanentes. Sic igitur dicendum est quod Deus conservat res quasdam in esse, mediantibus aliquibus causis.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32772)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Deus immediate omnia creavit, sed in ipsa rerum creatione ordinem in rebus instituit, ut quaedam ab aliis dependerent, per quas secundario conservarentur in esse; praesupposita tamen principali conservatione, quae est ab ipso.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32773)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, cum propria causa sit conservativa effectus ab ea dependentis; sicut nulli effectui praestari potest quod sit causa sui ipsius, potest tamen ei praestari quod sit causa alterius; ita etiam nulli effectui praestari potest quod sit sui ipsius conservativus, potest tamen ei praestari quod sit conservativus alterius.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32774)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod nulla creatura potest esse causa alterius, quantum ad hoc quod acquirat novam formam vel dispositionem, nisi per modum alicuius mutationis, quia semper agit praesupposito aliquo subiecto. Sed postquam formam vel dispositionem induxit in effectu, absque alia immutatione effectus, huiusmodi formam vel dispositionem conservat. Sicut in aere, prout illuminatur de novo, intelligitur quaedam mutatio; sed conservatio luminis est absque aeris immutatione, ex sola praesentia illuminantis.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 104 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32775)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non possit aliquid in nihilum redigere. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod *Deus non est causa tendendi in non esse*. Hoc autem contingeret, si aliquam creaturam redigeret in nihilum. Ergo Deus non potest aliquid in nihilum redigere.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32776)\n\nPraeterea, Deus est causa rerum ut sint, per suam bonitatem, quia, ut Augustinus dicit in libro de Doct. Christ., *inquantum Deus bonus est, sumus*. Sed Deus non potest non esse bonus. Ergo non potest facere ut res non sint. Quod faceret, si eas in nihilum redigeret.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32777)\n\nPraeterea, si Deus in nihilum aliqua redigeret, oporteret quod hoc fieret per aliquam actionem. Sed hoc non potest esse, quia omnis actio terminatur ad aliquod ens; unde etiam actio corrumpentis terminatur ad aliquid generatum, quia generatio unius est corruptio alterius. Ergo Deus non potest aliquid in nihilum redigere.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32778)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Ierem. X, *corripe me, domine, verumtamen in iudicio, et non in furore tuo, ne forte ad nihilum redigas me*.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32779)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam posuerunt quod Deus res in esse produxit agendo de necessitate naturae. Quod si esset verum, Deus non posset rem aliquam in nihilum redigere; sicut non potest a sua natura mutari. Sed, sicut supra est habitum, haec positio est falsa, et a fide Catholica penitus aliena, quae confitetur Deum res libera voluntate produxisse in esse, secundum illud Psalmi, *omnia quaecumque voluit dominus, fecit*. Hoc igitur quod Deus creaturae esse communicat, ex Dei voluntate dependet. Nec aliter res in esse conservat, nisi inquantum eis continue influit esse. Ut dictum est. Sicut ergo antequam res essent, potuit eis non communicare esse, et sic eas non facere; ita postquam iam factae sunt, potest eis non influere esse, et sic esse desisterent. Quod est eas in nihilum redigere.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32780)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod non esse non habet causam per se, quia nihil potest esse causa nisi inquantum est ens; ens autem, per se loquendo, est causa essendi. Sic igitur Deus non potest esse causa tendendi in non esse; sed hoc habet creatura ex seipsa, inquantum est de nihilo. Sed per accidens Deus potest esse causa quod res in nihilum redigantur, subtrahendo scilicet suam actionem a rebus.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32781)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod bonitas Dei est causa rerum, non quasi ex necessitate naturae, quia divina bonitas non dependet ex rebus creatis; sed per liberam voluntatem. Unde sicut potuit sine praeiudicio bonitatis suae, res non producere in esse; ita absque detrimento suae bonitatis, potest res in esse non conservare.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32782)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, si Deus rem aliquam redigeret in nihilum, hoc non esset per aliquam actionem; sed per hoc quod ab agendo cessaret.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 104 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32783)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aliquid in nihilum redigatur. Finis enim respondet principio. Sed a principio nihil erat nisi Deus. Ergo ad hunc finem res perducentur, ut nihil sit nisi Deus. Et ita creaturae in nihilum redigentur.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32784)\n\nPraeterea, omnis creatura habet potentiam finitam. Sed nulla potentia finita se extendit ad infinitum, unde in VIII Physic. probatur quod potentia finita non potest movere tempore infinito. Ergo nulla creatura potest durare in infinitum. Et ita quandoque in nihilum redigetur.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32785)\n\nPraeterea, forma et accidentia non habent materiam partem sui. Sed quandoque desinunt esse. Ergo in nihilum rediguntur.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32786)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Eccle. III. *Didici quod omnia opera quae fecit Deus, perseverant in aeternum*.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32787)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod eorum quae a Deo fiunt circa creaturam, quaedam proveniunt secundum naturalem cursum rerum; quaedam vero miraculose operatur praeter ordinem naturalem creaturis inditum, ut infra dicetur. Quae autem facturus est Deus secundum ordinem naturalem rebus inditum, considerari possunt ex ipsis rerum naturis, quae vero miraculose fiunt, ordinantur ad gratiae manifestationem, secundum illud apostoli I ad Cor. XII, *unicuique datur manifestatio spiritus ad utilitatem*; et postmodum, inter cetera, subdit de miraculorum operatione. Creaturarum autem naturae hoc demonstrant, ut nulla earum in nihilum redigatur, quia vel sunt immateriales, et sic in eis non est potentia ad non esse; vel sunt materiales, et sic saltem remanent semper secundum materiam, quae incorruptibilis est, utpote subiectum existens generationis et corruptionis. Redigere etiam aliquid in nihilum, non pertinet ad gratiae manifestationem, cum magis per hoc divina potentia et bonitas ostendatur, quod res in esse conservat. Unde simpliciter dicendum est quod nihil omnino in nihilum redigetur.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32788)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod hoc quod res in esse productae sunt, postquam non fuerunt, declarat potentiam producentis. Sed quod in nihilum redigerentur, huiusmodi manifestationem impediret, cum Dei potentia in hoc maxime ostendatur, quod res in esse conservat, secundum illud apostoli Heb. I, *portans omnia verbo virtutis suae*.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32789)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod potentia creaturae ad essendum est receptiva tantum; sed potentia activa est ipsius Dei, a quo est influxus essendi. Unde quod res in infinitum durent, sequitur infinitatem divinae virtutis. Determinatur tamen quibusdam rebus virtus ad manendum tempore determinato, inquantum impediri possunt ne percipiant influxum essendi qui est ab eo, ex aliquo contrario agente, cui finita virtus non potest resistere tempore infinito, sed solum tempore determinato. Et ideo ea quae non habent contrarium, quamvis habeant finitam virtutem, perseverant in aeternum.\n\n[I q. 104 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32790)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod formae et accidentia non sunt entia completa, cum non subsistant, sed quodlibet eorum est aliquid entis, sic enim ens dicitur, quia eo aliquid est. Et tamen eo modo quo sunt, non omnino in nihilum rediguntur; non quia aliqua pars eorum remaneat, sed remanent in potentia materiae vel subiecti.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0002","text":"[I q. 104 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32756)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de effectibus divinae gubernationis in speciali. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum creaturae indigeant ut conserventur in esse a Deo. Secundo, utrum conserventur a Deo immediate. Tertio, utrum Deus possit aliquid redigere in nihilum. Quarto, utrum aliquid in nihilum redigatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0005","text":"[I q. 104 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32757)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod creaturae non indigeant ut a Deo conserventur in esse. Quod enim non potest non esse, non indiget ut conservetur in esse, sicut quod non potest abscedere, non indiget ut conservetur ne abscedat. Sed quaedam creaturae sunt quae secundum sui naturam non possunt non esse. Ergo non omnes creaturae indigent ut a Deo conserventur in esse. Probatio mediae. Quod per se inest alicui, necesse est ei inesse, et oppositum eius impossibile est ei inesse, sicut necessarium est binarium esse parem, et impossibile est eum esse imparem. Esse autem per se consequitur ad formam, quia unumquodque secundum hoc est ens actu, quod habet formam. Quaedam autem creaturae sunt, quae sunt formae quaedam subsistentes, sicut de Angelis dictum est; et sic per se inest eis esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0007","text":"Et eadem ratio est de illis quorum materia non est in potentia nisi ad unam formam, sicut supra dictum est de corporibus caelestibus. Huiusmodi ergo creaturae secundum suam naturam ex necessitate sunt, et non possunt non esse, potentia enim ad non esse non potest fundari neque in forma, quam per se sequitur esse; neque in materia existente sub forma quam non potest amittere, cum non sit in potentia ad aliam formam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0008","text":"[I q. 104 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32758)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0009","text":"Praeterea, Deus est potentior quolibet creato agente. Sed aliquod creatum agens potest communicare suo effectui ut conservetur in esse, etiam eius operatione cessante, sicut cessante actione aedificatoris, remanet domus; et cessante actione ignis, remanet aqua calefacta per aliquod tempus. Ergo multo magis Deus potest suae creaturae conferre quod conservetur in esse, sua operatione cessante.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0010","text":"[I q. 104 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32759)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0011","text":"Praeterea, nullum violentum potest contingere absque aliqua causa agente. Sed tendere ad non esse est innaturale et violentum cuilibet creaturae, quia quaelibet creatura naturaliter appetit esse. Ergo nulla creatura potest tendere in non esse, nisi aliquo agente ad corruptionem. Sed quaedam sunt ad quorum corruptionem nihil agere potest; sicut spirituales substantiae, et corpora caelestia. Ergo huiusmodi creaturae non possunt tendere in non esse, etiam Dei operatione cessante.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0012","text":"[I q. 104 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32760)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0013","text":"Praeterea, si Deus conservat res in esse, hoc erit per aliquam actionem. Per quamlibet autem actionem agentis, si sit efficax, aliquid fit in effectu. Oportet igitur quod per actionem Dei conservantis aliquid fiat in creatura. Sed hoc non videtur. Non enim per huiusmodi actionem fit ipsum esse creaturae, quia quod iam est, non fit. Neque iterum aliquid aliud superadditum, quia vel non continue Deus conservaret creaturam in esse, vel continue aliquid adderetur creaturae, quod est inconveniens. Non igitur creaturae conservantur in esse a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0014","text":"[I q. 104 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32761)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0015","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Heb. I, *portans omnia verbo virtutis suae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0016","text":"[I q. 104 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32762)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0017","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod necesse est dicere, et secundum fidem et secundum rationem, quod creaturae conservantur in esse a Deo. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod aliquid conservatur ab altero dupliciter. Uno modo, indirecte et per accidens, sicut ille dicitur rem conservare, qui removet corrumpens; puta si aliquis puerum custodiat ne cadat in ignem, dicitur eum conservare. Et sic etiam Deus dicitur aliqua conservare, sed non omnia, quia quaedam sunt quae non habent corrumpentia, quae necesse sit removere ad rei conservationem. Alio modo dicitur aliquid rem aliquam conservare per se et directe, inquantum scilicet illud quod conservatur, dependet a conservante, ut sine eo esse non possit. Et hoc modo omnes creaturae indigent divina conservatione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0018","text":"Dependet enim esse cuiuslibet creaturae a Deo, ita quod nec ad momentum subsistere possent, sed in nihilum redigerentur, nisi operatione divinae virtutis conservarentur in esse, sicut Gregorius dicit. Et hoc sic perspici potest. Omnis enim effectus dependet a sua causa, secundum quod est causa eius. Sed considerandum est quod aliquod agens est causa sui effectus secundum fieri tantum, et non directe secundum esse eius. Quod quidem contingit et in artificialibus, et in rebus naturalibus. Aedificator enim est causa domus quantum ad eius fieri, non autem directe quantum ad esse eius. Manifestum est enim quod esse domus consequitur formam eius, forma autem domus est compositio et ordo, quae quidem forma consequitur naturalem virtutem quarundam rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0019","text":"Sicut enim coquus coquit cibum adhibendo aliquam virtutem naturalem activam, scilicet ignis; ita aedificator facit domum adhibendo caementum, lapides et ligna, quae sunt susceptiva et conservativa talis compositionis et ordinis. Unde esse domus dependet ex naturis harum rerum, sicut fieri domus dependet ex actione aedificatoris. Et simili ratione est considerandum in rebus naturalibus. Quia si aliquod agens non est causa formae inquantum huiusmodi, non erit per se causa esse quod consequitur ad talem formam, sed erit causa effectus secundum fieri tantum. Manifestum est autem quod, si aliqua duo sunt eiusdem speciei, unum non potest esse per se causa formae alterius, inquantum est talis forma, quia sic esset causa formae propriae, cum sit eadem ratio utriusque. Sed potest esse causa huiusmodi formae secundum quod est in materia, idest quod haec materia acquirat hanc formam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0020","text":"Et hoc est esse causa secundum fieri; sicut cum homo generat hominem, et ignis ignem. Et ideo quandocumque naturalis effectus est natus impressionem agentis recipere secundum eandem rationem secundum quam est in agente, tunc fieri effectus dependet ab agente, non autem esse ipsius. Sed aliquando effectus non est natus recipere impressionem agentis secundum eandem rationem secundum quam est in agente, sicut patet in omnibus agentibus quae non agunt simile secundum speciem; sicut caelestia corpora sunt causa generationis inferiorum corporum dissimilium secundum speciem. Et tale agens potest esse causa formae secundum rationem talis formae, et non solum secundum quod acquiritur in hac materia, et ideo est causa non solum fiendi, sed essendi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0021","text":"Sicut igitur fieri rei non potest remanere, cessante actione agentis quod est causa effectus secundum fieri; ita nec esse rei potest remanere, cessante actione agentis quod est causa effectus non solum secundum fieri, sed etiam secundum esse. Et haec est ratio quare aqua calefacta retinet calorem, cessante actione ignis; non autem remanet aer illuminatus, nec ad momentum, cessante actione solis. Quia scilicet materia aquae susceptiva est caloris ignis secundum eandem rationem qua est in igne, unde si perfecte perducatur ad formam ignis, retinebit calorem semper; si autem imperfecte participet aliquid de forma ignis secundum quandam inchoationem, calor non semper remanebit, sed ad tempus, propter debilem participationem principii caloris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0022","text":"Aer autem nullo modo natus est recipere lumen secundum eandem rationem secundum quam est in sole, ut scilicet recipiat formam solis, quae est principium luminis, et ideo, quia non habet radicem in aere, statim cessat lumen, cessante actione solis. Sic autem se habet omnis creatura ad Deum, sicut aer ad solem illuminantem. Sicut enim sol est lucens per suam naturam, aer autem fit luminosus participando lumen a sole, non tamen participando naturam solis; ita solus Deus est ens per essentiam suam, quia eius essentia est suum esse; omnis autem creatura est ens participative, non quod sua essentia sit eius esse. Et ideo, ut Augustinus dicit IV super Gen. ad Litt., *virtus Dei ab eis quae creata sunt regendis si cessaret aliquando, simul et illorum cessaret species, omnisque natura concideret*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0023","text":"Et in VIII eiusdem libri dicit quod, *sicut aer praesente lumine fit lucidus, sic homo, Deo sibi praesente, illuminatur, absente autem, continuo tenebratur*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0024","text":"[I q. 104 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32763)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0025","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod esse per se consequitur formam creaturae, supposito tamen influxu Dei, sicut lumen sequitur diaphanum aeris, supposito influxu solis. Unde potentia ad non esse in spiritualibus creaturis et corporibus caelestibus, magis est in Deo, qui potest subtrahere suum influxum, quam in forma vel in materia talium creaturarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0026","text":"[I q. 104 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32764)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0027","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Deus non potest communicare alicui creaturae ut conservetur in esse, sua operatione cessante; sicut non potest ei communicare quod non sit causa esse illius. Intantum enim indiget creatura conservari a Deo, inquantum esse effectus dependet a causa essendi. Unde non est simile de agente quod non est causa essendi, sed fieri tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0028","text":"[I q. 104 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32765)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0029","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ratio illa procedit de conservatione quae est per remotionem corrumpentis; qua non indigent omnes creaturae, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0030","text":"[I q. 104 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32766)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0031","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod conservatio rerum a Deo non est per aliquam novam actionem; sed per continuationem actionis qua dat esse, quae quidem actio est sine motu et tempore. Sicut etiam conservatio luminis in aere est per continuatum influxum a sole.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0032","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0033","text":"[I q. 104 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32767)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0034","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus immediate omnem creaturam conservet. Eadem enim actione Deus est conservator rerum, qua et creator, ut dictum est. Sed Deus immediate est creator omnium. Ergo immediate est etiam conservator.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0035","text":"[I q. 104 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32768)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0036","text":"Praeterea, unaquaeque res magis est proxima sibi, quam rei alteri. Sed non potest communicari alicui creaturae quod conservet seipsam. Ergo multo minus potest ei communicari quod conservet aliam. Ergo Deus omnia conservat absque aliqua media causa conservante.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0037","text":"[I q. 104 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32769)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0038","text":"Praeterea, effectus conservatur in esse ab eo quod est causa eius non solum secundum fieri, sed etiam secundum esse. Sed omnes causae creatae, ut videtur, non sunt causae suorum effectuum nisi secundum fieri, non sunt enim causae nisi movendo, ut supra habitum est. Ergo non sunt causae conservantes suos effectus in esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0039","text":"[I q. 104 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32770)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0040","text":"Sed contra est quod per idem conservatur res, per quod habet esse. Sed Deus dat esse rebus mediantibus aliquibus causis mediis. Ergo etiam res in esse conservat mediantibus aliquibus causis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0041","text":"[I q. 104 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32771)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0042","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, dupliciter aliquid rem aliquam in esse conservat, uno modo, indirecte et per accidens, per hoc quod removet vel impedit actionem corrumpentis; alio modo, directe et per se, quia ab eo dependet esse alterius, sicut a causa dependet esse effectus. Utroque autem modo aliqua res creata invenitur esse alterius conservativa. Manifestum est enim quod etiam in rebus corporalibus multa sunt quae impediunt actiones corrumpentium, et per hoc dicuntur rerum conservativa; sicut sal impedit carnes a putrefactione, et simile est in multis aliis. Invenitur etiam quod ab aliqua creatura dependet aliquis effectus secundum suum esse. Cum enim sunt multae causae ordinatae, necesse est quod effectus dependeat primo quidem et principaliter a causa prima; secundario vero ab omnibus causis mediis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0043","text":"Et ideo principaliter quidem prima causa est effectus conservativa; secundario vero omnes mediae causae, et tanto magis quanto causa fuerit altior et primae causae proximior. Unde superioribus causis, etiam in corporalibus rebus, attribuitur conservatio et permanentia rerum, sicut philosophus dicit, in XII Metaphys., quod primus motus, scilicet diurnus, est causa continuitatis generationis; secundus autem motus, qui est per zodiacum, est causa diversitatis quae est secundum generationem et corruptionem. Et similiter astrologi, attribuunt Saturno, qui est supremus planetarum; res fixas et permanentes. Sic igitur dicendum est quod Deus conservat res quasdam in esse, mediantibus aliquibus causis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0044","text":"[I q. 104 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32772)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0045","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Deus immediate omnia creavit, sed in ipsa rerum creatione ordinem in rebus instituit, ut quaedam ab aliis dependerent, per quas secundario conservarentur in esse; praesupposita tamen principali conservatione, quae est ab ipso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0046","text":"[I q. 104 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32773)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0047","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, cum propria causa sit conservativa effectus ab ea dependentis; sicut nulli effectui praestari potest quod sit causa sui ipsius, potest tamen ei praestari quod sit causa alterius; ita etiam nulli effectui praestari potest quod sit sui ipsius conservativus, potest tamen ei praestari quod sit conservativus alterius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0048","text":"[I q. 104 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32774)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0049","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod nulla creatura potest esse causa alterius, quantum ad hoc quod acquirat novam formam vel dispositionem, nisi per modum alicuius mutationis, quia semper agit praesupposito aliquo subiecto. Sed postquam formam vel dispositionem induxit in effectu, absque alia immutatione effectus, huiusmodi formam vel dispositionem conservat. Sicut in aere, prout illuminatur de novo, intelligitur quaedam mutatio; sed conservatio luminis est absque aeris immutatione, ex sola praesentia illuminantis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0050","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0051","text":"[I q. 104 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32775)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0052","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non possit aliquid in nihilum redigere. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., quod *Deus non est causa tendendi in non esse*. Hoc autem contingeret, si aliquam creaturam redigeret in nihilum. Ergo Deus non potest aliquid in nihilum redigere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0053","text":"[I q. 104 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32776)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0054","text":"Praeterea, Deus est causa rerum ut sint, per suam bonitatem, quia, ut Augustinus dicit in libro de Doct. Christ., *inquantum Deus bonus est, sumus*. Sed Deus non potest non esse bonus. Ergo non potest facere ut res non sint. Quod faceret, si eas in nihilum redigeret.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0055","text":"[I q. 104 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32777)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0056","text":"Praeterea, si Deus in nihilum aliqua redigeret, oporteret quod hoc fieret per aliquam actionem. Sed hoc non potest esse, quia omnis actio terminatur ad aliquod ens; unde etiam actio corrumpentis terminatur ad aliquid generatum, quia generatio unius est corruptio alterius. Ergo Deus non potest aliquid in nihilum redigere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0057","text":"[I q. 104 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32778)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0058","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Ierem. X, *corripe me, domine, verumtamen in iudicio, et non in furore tuo, ne forte ad nihilum redigas me*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0059","text":"[I q. 104 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32779)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0060","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam posuerunt quod Deus res in esse produxit agendo de necessitate naturae. Quod si esset verum, Deus non posset rem aliquam in nihilum redigere; sicut non potest a sua natura mutari. Sed, sicut supra est habitum, haec positio est falsa, et a fide Catholica penitus aliena, quae confitetur Deum res libera voluntate produxisse in esse, secundum illud Psalmi, *omnia quaecumque voluit dominus, fecit*. Hoc igitur quod Deus creaturae esse communicat, ex Dei voluntate dependet. Nec aliter res in esse conservat, nisi inquantum eis continue influit esse. Ut dictum est. Sicut ergo antequam res essent, potuit eis non communicare esse, et sic eas non facere; ita postquam iam factae sunt, potest eis non influere esse, et sic esse desisterent. Quod est eas in nihilum redigere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0061","text":"[I q. 104 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32780)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0062","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod non esse non habet causam per se, quia nihil potest esse causa nisi inquantum est ens; ens autem, per se loquendo, est causa essendi. Sic igitur Deus non potest esse causa tendendi in non esse; sed hoc habet creatura ex seipsa, inquantum est de nihilo. Sed per accidens Deus potest esse causa quod res in nihilum redigantur, subtrahendo scilicet suam actionem a rebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0063","text":"[I q. 104 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32781)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0064","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod bonitas Dei est causa rerum, non quasi ex necessitate naturae, quia divina bonitas non dependet ex rebus creatis; sed per liberam voluntatem. Unde sicut potuit sine praeiudicio bonitatis suae, res non producere in esse; ita absque detrimento suae bonitatis, potest res in esse non conservare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0065","text":"[I q. 104 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32782)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0066","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, si Deus rem aliquam redigeret in nihilum, hoc non esset per aliquam actionem; sed per hoc quod ab agendo cessaret.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0067","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0068","text":"[I q. 104 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32783)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0069","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod aliquid in nihilum redigatur. Finis enim respondet principio. Sed a principio nihil erat nisi Deus. Ergo ad hunc finem res perducentur, ut nihil sit nisi Deus. Et ita creaturae in nihilum redigentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0070","text":"[I q. 104 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32784)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0071","text":"Praeterea, omnis creatura habet potentiam finitam. Sed nulla potentia finita se extendit ad infinitum, unde in VIII Physic. probatur quod potentia finita non potest movere tempore infinito. Ergo nulla creatura potest durare in infinitum. Et ita quandoque in nihilum redigetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0072","text":"[I q. 104 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32785)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0073","text":"Praeterea, forma et accidentia non habent materiam partem sui. Sed quandoque desinunt esse. Ergo in nihilum rediguntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0074","text":"[I q. 104 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32786)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0075","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Eccle. III. *Didici quod omnia opera quae fecit Deus, perseverant in aeternum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0076","text":"[I q. 104 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32787)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0077","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod eorum quae a Deo fiunt circa creaturam, quaedam proveniunt secundum naturalem cursum rerum; quaedam vero miraculose operatur praeter ordinem naturalem creaturis inditum, ut infra dicetur. Quae autem facturus est Deus secundum ordinem naturalem rebus inditum, considerari possunt ex ipsis rerum naturis, quae vero miraculose fiunt, ordinantur ad gratiae manifestationem, secundum illud apostoli I ad Cor. XII, *unicuique datur manifestatio spiritus ad utilitatem*; et postmodum, inter cetera, subdit de miraculorum operatione. Creaturarum autem naturae hoc demonstrant, ut nulla earum in nihilum redigatur, quia vel sunt immateriales, et sic in eis non est potentia ad non esse; vel sunt materiales, et sic saltem remanent semper secundum materiam, quae incorruptibilis est, utpote subiectum existens generationis et corruptionis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0078","text":"Redigere etiam aliquid in nihilum, non pertinet ad gratiae manifestationem, cum magis per hoc divina potentia et bonitas ostendatur, quod res in esse conservat. Unde simpliciter dicendum est quod nihil omnino in nihilum redigetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0079","text":"[I q. 104 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32788)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0080","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod hoc quod res in esse productae sunt, postquam non fuerunt, declarat potentiam producentis. Sed quod in nihilum redigerentur, huiusmodi manifestationem impediret, cum Dei potentia in hoc maxime ostendatur, quod res in esse conservat, secundum illud apostoli Heb. I, *portans omnia verbo virtutis suae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0081","text":"[I q. 104 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32789)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0082","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod potentia creaturae ad essendum est receptiva tantum; sed potentia activa est ipsius Dei, a quo est influxus essendi. Unde quod res in infinitum durent, sequitur infinitatem divinae virtutis. Determinatur tamen quibusdam rebus virtus ad manendum tempore determinato, inquantum impediri possunt ne percipiant influxum essendi qui est ab eo, ex aliquo contrario agente, cui finita virtus non potest resistere tempore infinito, sed solum tempore determinato. Et ideo ea quae non habent contrarium, quamvis habeant finitam virtutem, perseverant in aeternum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0083","text":"[I q. 104 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32790)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0084","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod formae et accidentia non sunt entia completa, cum non subsistant, sed quodlibet eorum est aliquid entis, sic enim ens dicitur, quia eo aliquid est. Et tamen eo modo quo sunt, non omnino in nihilum rediguntur; non quia aliqua pars eorum remaneat, sed remanent in potentia materiae vel subiecti.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"a0b8ed97b73be4bb99bd6da27efcc89da7d69e7de5f31e41024a50e55f3df0f9","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T153655286098Z_9021e23d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 104","source_sha256":"a0b8ed97b73be4bb99bd6da27efcc89da7d69e7de5f31e41024a50e55f3df0f9","sha256":"fb9de4096ef9c9130e4e9e6cd9c9eb33850a6d4a7f824439fa28c204fddf5bf2","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:36:55.286117+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0002","text":"[I q. 104 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32756)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0003","text":"Next, the effects of divine governance must be considered in particular. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, whether creatures need to be preserved in being by God. Second, whether they are preserved by God immediately. Third, whether God can reduce something to nothing. Fourth, whether anything is reduced to nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0005","text":"[I q. 104 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32757)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that creatures do not need to be preserved in being by God. For what cannot not be does not need to be preserved in being, just as what cannot depart does not need to be kept from departing. But there are certain creatures which, according to their nature, cannot not be. Therefore, not all creatures need to be preserved in being by God. Proof of the minor. What belongs to something through itself must belong to it, and its opposite cannot belong to it; just as it is necessary that two be even, and impossible that it be odd. But being follows upon form through itself, because each thing is actually a being in virtue of having form. Now there are certain creatures which are subsisting forms, as was said of the angels; and thus being belongs to them through itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0007","text":"And the same reasoning applies to those whose matter is in potency only to one form, as was said above concerning the celestial bodies. Therefore, creatures of this sort exist by necessity according to their nature, and cannot not be; for potency to non-being can be founded neither in form, upon which being follows through itself, nor in matter existing under a form which it cannot lose, since it is not in potency to another form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0008","text":"[I q. 104 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32758)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0009","text":"Further, God is more powerful than any created agent. But some created agent can communicate to its effect that it be preserved in being even when its operation has ceased: thus, when the action of the builder has ceased, the house remains; and when the action of fire has ceased, water heated by it remains for some time. Therefore, much more can God confer upon His creature that it be preserved in being when His operation has ceased.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0010","text":"[I q. 104 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32759)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0011","text":"Further, nothing violent can occur without some acting cause. But to tend toward non-being is unnatural and violent for every creature, since every creature naturally desires being. Therefore no creature can tend toward non-being unless something acts toward its corruption. But there are some things toward whose corruption nothing can act, such as spiritual substances and celestial bodies. Therefore creatures of this sort cannot tend toward non-being, even if God's operation were to cease.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0012","text":"[I q. 104 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32760)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0013","text":"Further, if God preserves things in being, this will be through some action. But by every action of an agent, if it is efficacious, something comes to be in the effect. Therefore, through the action of God preserving, something must come to be in the creature. But this does not seem to be so. For through such an action the creature's very being does not come to be, because what already is does not come to be. Nor again does something else added come to be, because either God would not preserve the creature in being continuously, or something would continuously be added to the creature, which is unfitting. Therefore creatures are not preserved in being by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0014","text":"[I q. 104 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32761)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0015","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Heb. I, *upholding all things by the word of his power*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0016","text":"[I q. 104 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32762)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0017","text":"I answer that it must be said, both according to faith and according to reason, that creatures are preserved in being by God. For the clarification of this, it should be considered that one thing is preserved by another in two ways. In one way, indirectly and accidentally, as one is said to preserve a thing who removes that which corrupts it; for example, if someone guards a child lest he fall into fire, he is said to preserve him. And in this way God is also said to preserve certain things, but not all, because there are some things which have no corrupting causes that need to be removed for the preservation of the thing. In another way, something is said to preserve a thing through itself and directly, namely, insofar as what is preserved depends upon the preserver, so that it cannot be without it. And in this way all creatures need divine preservation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0018","text":"For the being of every creature depends upon God, such that they could not subsist even for a moment, but would be reduced to nothing, unless they were preserved in being by the operation of divine power, as Gregory says. And this can be discerned as follows. For every effect depends upon its cause insofar as it is its cause. But it must be considered that some agent is the cause of its effect only with respect to its coming-to-be, and not directly with respect to its being. This occurs both in artificial things and in natural things. For the builder is the cause of the house with respect to its coming-to-be, but not directly with respect to its being. For it is manifest that the being of the house follows upon its form; but the form of the house is composition and order, and this form follows upon the natural power of certain things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0019","text":"For just as a cook cooks food by employing some natural active power, namely, that of fire, so a builder makes a house by employing cement, stones, and timber, which are receptive of and preservative of such a composition and order. Hence the being of the house depends upon the natures of these things, just as the coming-to-be of the house depends upon the action of the builder. And a similar consideration applies in natural things. For if some agent is not the cause of a form as such, it will not be per se the cause of the being that follows upon such a form, but will be the cause of the effect only with respect to its coming-to-be. Now it is manifest that, if two things are of the same species, one cannot be per se the cause of the form of the other insofar as it is such a form, because it would thus be the cause of its own form, since the intelligible account is the same in both. But it can be the cause of such a form insofar as it is in matter, that is, that this matter acquires this form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0020","text":"And this is to be a cause with respect to coming-to-be, as when a man generates a man, and fire generates fire. Therefore, whenever a natural effect is apt to receive the impression of the agent according to the same intelligible account according to which it is in the agent, then the coming-to-be of the effect depends upon the agent, but not its being. But sometimes an effect is not apt to receive the impression of the agent according to the same intelligible account according to which it is in the agent, as is evident in all agents that do not produce a like thing according to species; for example, the celestial bodies are the cause of the generation of lower bodies dissimilar according to species. And such an agent can be the cause of the form according to the intelligible account of such a form, and not only insofar as it is acquired in this matter; and therefore it is the cause not only of coming-to-be, but also of being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0021","text":"Therefore, just as the coming-to-be of a thing cannot remain when the action of the agent that is the cause of the effect with respect to coming-to-be ceases, so neither can the being of a thing remain when the action of the agent that is the cause of the effect not only with respect to coming-to-be but also with respect to being ceases. And this is the reason why heated water retains heat when the action of fire has ceased; but illuminated air does not remain, not even for a moment, when the action of the sun has ceased. For the matter of water is receptive of the heat of fire according to the same intelligible account according to which it is in fire; hence, if it is brought perfectly to the form of fire, it will retain heat always. But if it participates imperfectly in something of the form of fire according to a certain inception, the heat will not remain always, but for a time, on account of its weak participation in the principle of heat.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0022","text":"But air is in no way naturally apt to receive light according to the same intelligible account according to which it is in the sun, namely, so as to receive the form of the sun, which is the principle of light; and therefore, because it has no root in the air, light immediately ceases when the action of the sun ceases. Now every creature stands in relation to God as air to the illuminating sun. For just as the sun is luminous through its nature, whereas air becomes luminous by participating in light from the sun, yet not by participating in the nature of the sun; so God alone is being through His essence, because His essence is His being; whereas every creature is a being by participation, not because its essence is its being. And therefore, as Augustine says in IV *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, *if the power of God in governing the things that have been created were ever to cease, at the same time their species would cease, and every nature would collapse*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0023","text":"And in VIII of the same book he says that, *just as air becomes bright when light is present, so man is illuminated when God is present to him; but when He is absent, he is forthwith darkened*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0024","text":"[I q. 104 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32763)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0025","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that being through itself follows upon the form of a creature, supposing nevertheless the influx of God, just as light follows upon the diaphanousness of the air, supposing the influx of the sun. Hence the potentiality for not-being in spiritual creatures and heavenly bodies is more in God, who can withdraw His influx, than in the form or matter of such creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0026","text":"[I q. 104 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32764)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0027","text":"To the second, it must be said that God cannot communicate to any creature that it be preserved in being while His operation ceases; just as He cannot communicate to it that He not be the cause of its being. For a creature needs to be preserved by God insofar as the being of an effect depends upon the cause of being. Hence the case is not similar with an agent that is not a cause of being, but only of coming-to-be.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0028","text":"[I q. 104 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32765)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0029","text":"To the third, it must be said that that argument proceeds concerning preservation which is through the removal of a corrupting agent; and not all creatures need this, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0030","text":"[I q. 104 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32766)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0031","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the preservation of things by God is not through some new action, but through the continuation of the action by which He gives being; and this action is without motion and time. Just as the preservation of light in the air is through a continued influx from the sun.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0032","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0033","text":"[I q. 104 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32767)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0034","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that God preserves every creature immediately. For God is the conservator of things by the same action by which He is their creator, as has been said. But God is immediately the creator of all things. Therefore He is also immediately their conservator.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0035","text":"[I q. 104 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32768)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0036","text":"Further, each thing is closer to itself than to another thing. But it cannot be communicated to any creature that it preserve itself. Therefore, much less can it be communicated to it that it preserve another. Therefore God preserves all things without any intervening preserving cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0037","text":"[I q. 104 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32769)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0038","text":"Further, an effect is preserved in being by that which is its cause not only with respect to coming-to-be, but also with respect to being. But all created causes, as seems to be the case, are causes of their effects only with respect to coming-to-be; for they are causes only by moving, as was established above. Therefore they are not causes preserving their effects in being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0039","text":"[I q. 104 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32770)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0040","text":"On the contrary, a thing is preserved by that through which it has being. But God gives being to things by means of certain intermediate causes. Therefore He also preserves things in being by means of certain causes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0041","text":"[I q. 104 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32771)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0042","text":"I answer that, as has been said, something preserves a thing in being in two ways. In one way, indirectly and accidentally, by removing or impeding the action of a corrupting agent; in another way, directly and through itself, because the being of another depends upon it, just as the being of an effect depends upon a cause. In both ways, however, one created thing is found to be preservative of another. For it is manifest that even among bodily things there are many things that impede the actions of corrupting agents, and through this are called preservative of things; just as salt prevents meats from putrefaction, and it is similar in many other cases. It is also found that some effect depends upon some creature with respect to its being. For, when there are many ordered causes, it is necessary that an effect depend first and principally upon the first cause, and secondarily upon all the intermediate causes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0043","text":"And therefore the first cause is indeed principally preservative of the effect; secondarily, however, all the intermediate causes are so, and the more so insofar as a cause is higher and closer to the first cause. Hence preservation and permanence of things are attributed to higher causes, even among bodily things; as the Philosopher says in Metaphysics XII, that the first motion, namely the daily motion, is the cause of the continuity of generation, whereas the second motion, which is through the zodiac, is the cause of the diversity that is according to generation and corruption. And similarly astrologers attribute fixed and permanent things to Saturn, which is the highest of the planets. Thus, therefore, it must be said that God preserves certain things in being through the mediation of certain causes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0044","text":"[I q. 104 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32772)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0045","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that God created all things immediately, but in the very creation of things He established an order among things, such that some depended upon others, through which they might be preserved in being secondarily; nevertheless presupposing the principal preservation, which is from Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0046","text":"[I q. 104 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32773)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0047","text":"To the second, it must be said that, since a proper cause is preservative of the effect dependent upon it, just as it cannot be granted to any effect that it be the cause of itself, though it can be granted to it that it be the cause of another, so also it cannot be granted to any effect that it be preservative of itself, though it can be granted to it that it be preservative of another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0048","text":"[I q. 104 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32774)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0049","text":"To the third it must be said that no creature can be the cause of another, as regards its acquiring a new form or disposition, except by way of some change, because it always acts with some subject presupposed. But after it has introduced a form or disposition into the effect, it preserves such form or disposition without a further change of the effect. Thus, in air, insofar as it is newly illuminated, a certain change is understood; but the preservation of light is without change in the air, from the mere presence of that which illuminates.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0050","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0051","text":"[I q. 104 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32775)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0052","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that God cannot reduce something to nothing. For Augustine says, in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, that *God is not the cause of tending toward non-being*. But this would occur if He reduced any creature to nothing. Therefore God cannot reduce something to nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0053","text":"[I q. 104 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32776)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0054","text":"Further, God is the cause of things' being through His goodness, because, as Augustine says in the book *On Christian Doctrine*, *insofar as God is good, we are*. But God cannot fail to be good. Therefore He cannot bring it about that things are not. Yet He would do this if He reduced them to nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0055","text":"[I q. 104 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32777)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0056","text":"Further, if God reduced some things to nothing, it would be necessary for this to take place through some action. But this cannot be, because every action terminates in some being; hence even the action of that which corrupts terminates in something generated, because the generation of one is the corruption of another. Therefore God cannot reduce something to nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0057","text":"[I q. 104 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32778)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0058","text":"But against this is what is said in Jer. X: *Correct me, O Lord, yet with judgment, and not in Thy wrath, lest perhaps Thou reduce me to nothing*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0059","text":"[I q. 104 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32779)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0060","text":"I answer that it must be said that some have maintained that God brought things into being by acting from the necessity of nature. If this were true, God could not reduce any thing to nothing, just as He cannot be changed from His nature. But, as has been maintained above, this position is false and utterly foreign to the Catholic faith, which confesses that God freely brought things into being by His will, according to that saying of the Psalm: *the Lord has done all things whatsoever He willed*. Therefore, the fact that God communicates being to a creature depends upon God's will. Nor does He preserve things in being otherwise than insofar as He continually pours being into them, as has been said. Therefore, just as before things existed He was able not to communicate being to them, and thus not to make them, so after they have already been made He can cease to pour being into them, and thus they would cease to be. And this is to reduce them to nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0061","text":"[I q. 104 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32780)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0062","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that non-being does not have a cause through itself, because nothing can be a cause except insofar as it is a being; but being, speaking through itself, is a cause of being. Thus God cannot be the cause of tending toward non-being; but the creature has this from itself, insofar as it is from nothing. But accidentally God can be the cause that things are reduced to nothing, namely by withdrawing His action from things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0063","text":"[I q. 104 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32781)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0064","text":"To the second it must be said that the goodness of God is the cause of things, not as though from the necessity of nature, because the divine goodness does not depend upon created things, but through free will. Hence, just as He was able, without prejudice to His goodness, not to bring things into being, so without detriment to His goodness He can fail to preserve things in being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0065","text":"[I q. 104 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32782)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0066","text":"To the third it must be said that, if God reduced some thing to nothing, this would not be through some action, but through His ceasing to act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0067","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0068","text":"[I q. 104 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32783)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0069","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that something is reduced to nothing. For the end corresponds to the beginning. But in the beginning there was nothing but God. Therefore things will be brought to this end, that there will be nothing but God. And thus creatures will be reduced to nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0070","text":"[I q. 104 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32784)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0071","text":"Further, every creature has finite power. But no finite power extends to infinity; hence in *Physics* VIII it is proved that a finite power cannot move for an infinite time. Therefore no creature can endure to infinity. And thus at some time it will be reduced to nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0072","text":"[I q. 104 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32785)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0073","text":"Moreover, form and accidents do not have matter as a part of themselves. But sometimes they cease to be. Therefore they are reduced to nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0074","text":"[I q. 104 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32786)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0075","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Eccles. III: *I have learned that all the works which God has made endure forever*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0076","text":"[I q. 104 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32787)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0077","text":"I answer that, among those things which are done by God concerning the creature, some occur according to the natural course of things; but others He works miraculously, beyond the natural order bestowed upon creatures, as will be said below. Now, what God is going to do according to the natural order bestowed upon things can be considered from the natures of things themselves; but those things which are done miraculously are ordered to the manifestation of grace, according to that saying of the Apostle, I Cor. XII: *to each is given the manifestation of the Spirit for profit*; and afterward, among other things, he adds concerning the working of miracles. But the natures of creatures show this: that none of them is reduced to nothing, because either they are immaterial, and thus in them there is no potentiality for not-being; or they are material, and thus at least they always remain with respect to matter, which is incorruptible, as existing as the subject of generation and corruption.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0078","text":"Moreover, to reduce something to nothing does not pertain to the manifestation of grace, since divine power and goodness are shown more by the fact that He preserves things in being. Hence it must be said simply that nothing whatever will be reduced to nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0079","text":"[I q. 104 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32788)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0080","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the fact that things were brought forth into being after they had not been declares the power of the producer. But that they should be reduced to nothing would impede such a manifestation, since the power of God is shown most especially in this, that He preserves things in being, according to that saying of the Apostle, Heb. I: *upholding all things by the word of His power*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0081","text":"[I q. 104 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32789)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0082","text":"To the second, it must be said that the power of a creature for being is only receptive; but the active power is God's own, from whom comes the influx of being. Hence, that things endure to infinity follows from the infinity of the divine power. Yet for certain things the power to remain is determined to a determinate time, insofar as they can be impeded from receiving the influx of being which is from Him, by some contrary agent, which a finite power cannot resist for an infinite time, but only for a determinate time. And therefore those things which have no contrary, although they have finite power, endure forever.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0083","text":"[I q. 104 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32790)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0084","text":"To the third, it must be said that forms and accidents are not complete beings, since they do not subsist, but each of them is something of being; for thus it is called a being, because something is by it. And yet, in the manner in which they are, they are not altogether reduced to nothing: not because some part of them remains, but because they remain in the potentiality of matter or of a subject.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_96:P0084"]}],"source_digest":"da4c58017cbfd517fb04c010eb8081a399f5fbe7022b4c4c2a56d44f2849f517","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 104"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 104","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97","title":"First Part · Question 105","order":106,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_97","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 105 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32791)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de secundo effectu gubernationis divinae qui est mutatio creaturarum. Et primo, de mutatione creaturarum a Deo; secundo, de mutatione unius creaturae ab alia. Circa primum quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum Deus immediate possit movere materiam ad formam. Secundo, utrum immediate possit movere aliquod corpus. Tertio, utrum possit movere intellectum. Quarto, utrum possit movere voluntatem. Quinto, utrum Deus operetur in omni operante. Sexto, utrum possit aliquid facere praeter ordinem rebus inditum. Septimo, utrum omnia quae sic Deus facit, sint miracula. Octavo, de diversitate miraculorum.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 105 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32792)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non possit immediate movere materiam ad formam. Sicut enim probat philosophus in VII Metaphys., formam in hac materia nihil facere potest nisi forma quae est in materia, quia simile facit sibi simile. Sed Deus non est forma in materia. Ergo non potest causare formam in materia.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32793)\n\nPraeterea, si aliquod agens se habeat ad multa, nullum eorum producet, nisi determinetur ad ipsum per aliquid aliud, ut enim in III de anima dicitur, universalis opinio non movet nisi mediante aliqua particulari apprehensione. Sed virtus divina est universalis causa omnium. Ergo non potest producere aliquam particularem formam, nisi mediante aliquo particulari agente.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32794)\n\nPraeterea, sicut esse commune dependet a prima causa universali, ita esse determinatum dependet a determinatis causis particularibus, ut supra habitum est. Sed determinatum esse alicuius rei est per propriam eius formam. Ergo propriae rerum formae non producuntur a Deo, nisi mediantibus causis particularibus.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32795)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, *formavit Deus hominem de limo terrae*.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32796)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deus immediate potest movere materiam ad formam. Quia ens in potentia passiva reduci potest in actum, a potentia activa quae eam sub sua potestate continet. Cum igitur materia contineatur sub potestate divina, utpote a Deo producta, potest reduci in actum per divinam potentiam. Et hoc est moveri materiam ad formam, quia forma nihil aliud est quam actus materiae.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32797)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod effectus aliquis invenitur assimilari causae agenti dupliciter. Uno modo, secundum eandem speciem; ut homo generatur ab homine, et ignis ab igne. Alio modo, secundum virtualem continentiam, prout scilicet forma effectus virtualiter continetur in causa, et sic animalia ex putrefactione generata, et plantae et corpora mineralia assimilantur soli et stellis, quorum virtute generantur. Sic igitur effectus causae agenti similatur secundum totum illud ad quod se extendit virtus agentis. Virtus autem Dei se extendit ad formam et materiam, ut supra habitum est. Unde compositum quod generatur, similatur Deo secundum virtualem continentiam, sicut similatur composito generanti per similitudinem speciei. Unde sicut compositum generans potest movere materiam ad formam generando compositum sibi simile, ita et Deus. Non autem aliqua alia forma non in materia existens, quia materia non continetur in virtute alterius substantiae separatae. Et ideo Daemones et Angeli operantur circa haec visibilia, non quidem imprimendo formas, sed adhibendo corporalia semina.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32798)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ratio illa procederet, si Deus ageret, ex necessitate naturae. Sed quia agit per voluntatem et intellectum, qui cognoscit rationes proprias omnium formarum, et non solum universales; inde est quod potest determinate hanc vel illam formam materiae imprimere.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32799)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod hoc ipsum quod causae secundae ordinantur ad determinatos effectus est illis a Deo. Unde Deus, quia alias causas ordinat ad determinatos effectus, potest etiam determinatos effectus producere per seipsum.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 105 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32800)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non possit immediate movere aliquod corpus. Cum enim movens et motum oporteat esse simul, ut probatur in VII Physic., oportet esse contactum quendam moventis et moti. Sed non potest esse contactus Dei, et corporis alicuius, dicit enim Dionysius, in I cap. de Div. Nom., quod *Dei non est aliquis tactus*. Ergo Deus non potest immediate movere aliquod corpus.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32801)\n\nPraeterea, Deus est movens non motum. Tale autem est appetibile apprehensum. Movet igitur Deus sicut desideratum et apprehensum. Sed non apprehenditur nisi ab intellectu, qui non est corpus, nec virtus corporis. Ergo Deus non potest movere aliquod corpus immediate.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32802)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus probat in VIII Physic., quod potentia infinita movet in instanti. Sed impossibile est aliquod corpus in instanti moveri, quia, cum omnis motus sit inter opposita, sequeretur quod duo opposita simul inessent eidem; quod est impossibile. Ergo corpus non potest immediate moveri a potentia infinita. Potentia autem Dei est infinita, ut supra habitum est. Ergo Deus non potest immediate movere aliquod corpus.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32803)\n\nSed contra, opera sex dierum Deus fecit immediate; in quibus continetur motus corporum, ut patet per hoc quod dicitur Gen. I, *congregentur aquae in locum unum*. Ergo Deus immediate potest movere corpus.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32804)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod erroneum est dicere Deum non posse facere per seipsum omnes determinatos effectus qui fiunt per quamcumque causam creatam. Unde cum corpora moveantur immediate a causis creatis, nulli debet venire in dubium quin Deus possit movere immediate quodcumque corpus. Et hoc quidem consequens est ad ea quae supra dicta sunt. Nam omnis motus corporis cuiuscumque vel consequitur ad aliquam formam, sicut motus localis gravium et levium consequitur formam quae datur a generante, ratione cuius generans dicitur movens, vel est via ad formam aliquam, sicut calefactio est via ad formam ignis. Eiusdem autem est imprimere formam, et disponere ad formam, et dare motum consequentem formam, ignis enim non solum generat alium ignem, sed etiam calefacit, et sursum movet. Cum igitur Deus possit immediate formam materiae imprimere, consequens est ut possit, secundum quemcumque motum, corpus quodcumque movere.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32805)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod duplex est tactus, scilicet corporalis, sicut duo corpora se tangunt; et virtualis, sicut dicitur quod contristans tangit contristatum. Secundum igitur primum contactum, Deus, cum sit incorporeus, nec tangit nec tangitur. Secundum autem virtualem contactum, tangit quidem movendo creaturas, sed non tangitur, quia nullius creaturae virtus naturalis potest ad ipsum pertingere. Et sic intellexit Dionysius quod non est tactus Dei, ut scilicet tangatur.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32806)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod movet Deus sicut desideratum et intellectum. Sed non oportet quod semper moveat sicut desideratum et intellectum ab eo quod movetur; sed sicut desideratum et notum a seipso; quia omnia operatur propter suam bonitatem.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32807)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod philosophus in VIII Physic. intendit probare quod virtus primi motoris non sit virtus in magnitudine, tali ratione, virtus primi motoris est infinita (quod probat per hoc quod potest movere tempore infinito); virtus autem infinita, si esset in aliqua magnitudine, moveret in non tempore, quod est impossibile; ergo oportet quod infinita virtus primi motoris sit non in magnitudine. Ex quo patet quod corpus moveri in non tempore, non consequitur nisi virtutem infinitam in magnitudine. Cuius ratio est, quia omnis virtus quae est in magnitudine, movet secundum se totam, cum moveat per necessitatem naturae. Potentia autem infinita improportionabiliter excedit quamlibet potentiam finitam. Quanto autem maior est potentia moventis, tanto est maior velocitas motus. Cum igitur potentia finita moveat tempore determinato, sequitur quod potentia infinita non moveat in aliquo tempore, quia cuiuscumque temporis ad aliud tempus est aliqua proportio. Sed virtus quae non est in magnitudine, est virtus alicuius intelligentis, qui operatur in effectibus secundum quod eis convenit. Et ideo, cum corpori non possit esse conveniens moveri in non tempore, non sequitur quod moveat in non tempore.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 105 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32808)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non moveat immediate intellectum creatum. Actio enim intellectus est ab eo in quo est, non enim transit in exteriorem materiam, ut dicitur in IX Metaphys. Actio autem eius quod movetur ab alio, non est ab eo in quo est, sed a movente. Non ergo intellectus movetur ab alio. Et ita videtur quod Deus non possit movere intellectum.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32809)\n\nPraeterea, id quod habet in se principium sufficiens sui motus, non movetur ab alio. Sed motus intellectus est ipsum intelligere eius, sicut dicitur quod intelligere vel sentire est motus quidam, secundum philosophum, in III de anima. Sufficiens autem principium intelligendi est lumen intelligibile inditum intellectui. Ergo non movetur ab alio.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32810)\n\nPraeterea, sicut sensus movetur a sensibili, ita intellectus ab intelligibili. Sed Deus non est intelligibilis nobis, sed nostrum intellectum excedit. Ergo Deus non potest movere nostrum intellectum.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32811)\n\nSed contra, docens movet intellectum addiscentis. Sed Deus docet hominem scientiam, sicut dicitur in Psalmo. Ergo Deus movet intellectum hominis.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32812)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut in motibus corporalibus movens dicitur quod dat formam quae est principium motus; ita dicitur movere intellectum, quod causat formam quae est principium intellectualis operationis, quae dicitur motus intellectus. Operationis autem intellectus est duplex principium in intelligente, unum scilicet quod est ipsa virtus intellectualis, quod quidem principium est etiam in intelligente in potentia; aliud autem est principium intelligendi in actu, scilicet similitudo rei intellectae in intelligente. Dicitur ergo aliquid movere intellectum, sive det intelligenti virtutem ad intelligendum, sive imprimat ei similitudinem rei intellectae. Utroque autem modo Deus movet intellectum creatum. Ipse enim est primum ens immateriale. Et quia intellectualitas consequitur immaterialitatem, sequitur quod ipse sit primum intelligens. Unde cum primum in quolibet ordine sit causa eorum quae consequuntur, sequitur quod ab ipso sit omnis virtus intelligendi. Similiter cum ipse sit primum ens, et omnia entia praeexistant in ipso sicut in prima causa, oportet quod sint in eo intelligibiliter secundum modum eius. Sicut enim omnes rationes rerum intelligibiles primo existunt in Deo, et ab derivantur in alios intellectus, ut actu intelligant; sic etiam derivantur in creaturas ut subsistant. Sic igitur Deus movet intellectum creatum, inquantum dat ei virtutem ad intelligendum, vel naturalem vel superadditam; et inquantum imprimit ei species intelligibiles; et utrumque tenet et conservat in esse.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32813)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod operatio intellectualis est quidem ab intellectu in quo est, sicut a causa secunda, sed a Deo sicut a causa prima. Ab ipso enim datur intelligenti quod intelligere possit.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32814)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod lumen intellectuale, simul cum similitudine rei intellectae, est sufficiens principium intelligendi; secundarium tamen, et a primo principio dependens.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32815)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod intelligibile movet intellectum nostrum, inquantum quodammodo imprimit ei suam similitudinem, per quam intelligi potest. Sed similitudines quas Deus imprimit intellectui creato, non sufficiunt ad ipsum Deum intelligendum per essentiam, ut supra habitum est. Unde movet intellectum creatum, cum tamen non sit ei intelligibilis, ut dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 105 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32816)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non possit movere voluntatem creatam. Omne enim quod movetur ab extrinseco, cogitur. Sed voluntas non potest cogi. Ergo non movetur ab aliquo extrinseco. Et ita non potest moveri a Deo.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32817)\n\nPraeterea, Deus non potest facere quod contradictoria sint simul vera. Hoc autem sequeretur, si voluntatem moveret, nam voluntarie moveri est ex se moveri, et non ab alio. Ergo Deus non potest voluntatem movere.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32818)\n\nPraeterea, motus magis attribuitur moventi quam mobili, unde homicidium non attribuitur lapidi, sed proiicienti. Si igitur Deus moveat voluntatem, sequitur quod opera voluntaria non imputentur homini ad meritum vel demeritum. Hoc autem est falsum. Non igitur Deus movet voluntatem.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32819)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur ad Philipp. II, *Deus est qui operatur in nobis velle et perficere*.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32820)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut intellectus, ut dictum est, movetur ab obiecto, et ab eo qui dedit virtutem intelligendi; ita voluntas movetur ab obiecto, quod est bonum, et ab eo qui creat virtutem volendi. Potest autem voluntas moveri sicut ab obiecto, a quocumque bono; non tamen sufficienter et efficaciter nisi a Deo. Non enim sufficienter aliquid potest movere aliquod mobile, nisi virtus activa moventis excedat, vel saltem adaequet virtutem passivam mobilis. Virtus autem passiva voluntatis se extendit ad bonum in universali, est enim eius obiectum bonum universale, sicut et intellectus obiectum est ens universale. Quodlibet autem bonum creatum est quoddam particulare bonum, solus autem Deus est bonum universale. Unde ipse solus implet voluntatem, et sufficienter eam movet ut obiectum. Similiter autem et virtus volendi a solo Deo causatur. Velle enim nihil aliud est quam inclinatio quaedam in obiectum voluntatis, quod est bonum universale. Inclinare autem in bonum universale est primi moventis cui proportionatur ultimus finis, sicut in rebus humanis dirigere ad bonum commune est eius qui praeest multitudini. Unde utroque modo proprium est Dei movere voluntatem, sed maxime secundo modo, interius eam inclinando.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32821)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod illud quod movetur ab altero dicitur cogi, si moveatur contra inclinationem propriam, sed si moveatur ab alio quod sibi dat propriam inclinationem, non dicitur cogi; sicut grave, cum movetur deorsum a generante, non cogitur. Sic igitur Deus, movendo voluntatem, non cogit ipsam, quia dat ei eius propriam inclinationem.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32822)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod moveri voluntarie est moveri ex se, idest a principio intrinseco, sed illud principium intrinsecum potest esse ab alio principio extrinseco. Et sic moveri ex se non repugnat ei quod movetur ab alio.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32823)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, si voluntas ita moveretur ab alio quod ex se nullatenus moveretur, opera voluntatis non imputarentur ad meritum vel demeritum. Sed quia per hoc quod movetur ab alio, non excluditur quin moveatur ex se, ut dictum est; ideo per consequens non tollitur ratio meriti vel demeriti.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 105 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32824)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non operetur in omni operante. Nulla enim insufficientia est Deo attribuenda. Si igitur Deus operatur in omni operante, sufficienter in quolibet operatur. Superfluum igitur esset quod agens creatum aliquid operaretur.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32825)\n\nPraeterea, una operatio non est simul a duobus operantibus, sicut nec unus numero motus potest esse duorum mobilium. Si igitur operatio creaturae est a Deo in creatura operante, non potest esse simul a creatura. Et ita nulla creatura aliquid operatur.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32826)\n\nPraeterea, faciens dicitur esse causa operationis facti, inquantum dat ei formam qua operatur. Si igitur Deus est causa operationis rerum factarum ab ipso, hoc erit inquantum dat eis virtutem operandi. Sed hoc est a principio, quando rem facit. Ergo videtur quod ulterius non operetur in creatura operante.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32827)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Isaiae XXVI, *omnia opera nostra operatus es in nobis, domine*.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32828)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Deum operari in quolibet operante aliqui sic intellexerunt, quod nulla virtus creata aliquid operaretur in rebus, sed solus Deus immediate omnia operaretur; puta quod ignis non calefaceret, sed Deus in igne, et similiter de omnibus aliis. Hoc autem est impossibile. Primo quidem, quia sic subtraheretur ordo causae et causati a rebus creatis. Quod pertinet ad impotentiam creantis, ex virtute enim agentis est, quod suo effectui det virtutem agendi. Secundo, quia virtutes operativae quae in rebus inveniuntur, frustra essent rebus attributae, si per eas nihil operarentur. Quinimmo omnes res creatae viderentur quodammodo esse frustra, si propria operatione destituerentur, cum omnis res sit propter suam operationem. Semper enim imperfectum est propter perfectius, sicut igitur materia est propter formam, ita forma, quae est actus primus, est propter suam operationem, quae est actus secundus; et sic operatio est finis rei creatae. Sic igitur intelligendum est Deum operari in rebus, quod tamen ipsae res propriam habeant operationem. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod, cum sint causarum quatuor genera, materia quidem non est principium actionis, sed se habet ut subiectum recipiens actionis effectum. Finis vero et agens et forma se habent ut actionis principium, sed ordine quodam. Nam primo quidem, principium actionis est finis, qui movet agentem; secundo vero, agens; tertio autem, forma eius quod ab agente applicatur ad agendum (quamvis et ipsum agens per formam suam agat); ut patet in artificialibus. Artifex enim movetur ad agendum a fine, qui est ipsum operatum, puta arca vel lectus; et applicat ad actionem securim quae incidit per suum acumen. Sic igitur secundum haec tria Deus in quolibet operante operatur. Primo quidem, secundum rationem finis. Cum enim omnis operatio sit propter aliquod bonum verum vel apparens; nihil autem est vel apparet bonum, nisi secundum quod participat aliquam similitudinem summi boni, quod est Deus; sequitur quod ipse Deus sit cuiuslibet operationis causa ut finis. Similiter etiam considerandum est quod, si sint multa agentia ordinata, semper secundum agens agit in virtute primi, nam primum agens movet secundum ad agendum. Et secundum hoc, omnia agunt in virtute ipsius Dei; et ita ipse est causa actionum omnium agentium. Tertio, considerandum est quod Deus movet non solum res ad operandum, quasi applicando formas et virtutes rerum ad operationem, sicut etiam artifex applicat securim ad scindendum, qui tamen interdum formam securi non tribuit; sed etiam dat formam creaturis agentibus, et eas tenet in esse. Unde non solum est causa actionum inquantum dat formam quae est principium actionis, sicut generans dicitur esse causa motus gravium et levium; sed etiam sicut conservans formas et virtutes rerum; prout sol dicitur esse causa manifestationis colorum, inquantum dat et conservat lumen, quo manifestantur colores. Et quia forma rei est intra rem, et tanto magis quanto consideratur ut prior et universalior; et ipse Deus est proprie causa ipsius esse universalis in rebus omnibus, quod inter omnia est magis intimum rebus; sequitur quod Deus in omnibus intime operetur. Et propter hoc in sacra Scriptura operationes naturae Deo attribuuntur quasi operanti in natura; secundum illud Iob X, *pelle et carnibus vestisti me, ossibus et nervis compegisti me*.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32829)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Deus sufficienter operatur in rebus ad modum primi agentis, nec propter hoc superfluit operatio secundorum agentium.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32830)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod una actio non procedit a duobus agentibus unius ordinis, sed nihil prohibet quin una et eadem actio procedat a primo et secundo agente.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32831)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Deus non solum dat formas rebus, sed etiam conservat eas in esse, et applicat eas ad agendum, et est finis omnium actionum, ut dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 105 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32832)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non possit facere aliquid praeter ordinem rebus inditum. Dicit enim Augustinus, XXVI contra Faustum, *Deus, conditor et creator omnium naturarum, nihil contra naturam facit*. Sed hoc videtur esse contra naturam, quod est praeter ordinem naturaliter rebus inditum. Ergo Deus non potest facere aliquid praeter ordinem rebus inditum.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32833)\n\nPraeterea, sicut ordo iustitiae est a Deo, ita et ordo naturae. Sed Deus non potest facere aliquid praeter ordinem iustitiae, faceret enim tunc aliquid iniustum. Ergo non potest facere aliquid praeter ordinem naturae.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32834)\n\nPraeterea, ordinem naturae Deus instituit. Si igitur praeter ordinem naturae Deus aliquid faciat, videtur quod ipse sit mutabilis. Quod est inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32835)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XXVI contra Faustum, quod *Deus aliquando aliquid facit contra solitum cursum naturae*.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32836)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod a qualibet causa derivatur aliquis ordo in suos effectus, cum quaelibet causa habeat rationem principii. Et ideo secundum multiplicationem causarum, multiplicantur et ordines, quorum unus continetur sub altero, sicut et causa continetur sub causa. Unde causa superior non continetur sub ordine causae inferioris, sed e converso. Cuius exemplum apparet in rebus humanis, nam ex patrefamilias dependet ordo domus, qui continetur sub ordine civitatis, qui procedit a civitatis rectore, cum et hic contineatur sub ordine regis, a quo totum regnum ordinatur. Si ergo ordo rerum consideretur prout dependet a prima causa, sic contra rerum ordinem Deus facere non potest, sic enim si faceret, faceret contra suam praescientiam aut voluntatem aut bonitatem. Si vero consideretur rerum ordo prout dependet a qualibet secundarum causarum, sic Deus potest facere praeter ordinem rerum. Quia ordini secundarum causarum ipse non est subiectus, sed talis ordo ei subiicitur, quasi ab eo procedens non per necessitatem naturae, sed per arbitrium voluntatis, potuisset enim et alium ordinem rerum instituere. Unde et potest praeter hunc ordinem institutum agere, cum voluerit; puta agendo effectus secundarum causarum sine ipsis, vel producendo aliquos effectus ad quos causae secundae non se extendunt. Unde et Augustinus dicit, XXVI contra Faustum, quod *Deus contra solitum cursum naturae facit; sed contra summam legem tam nullo modo facit, quam contra seipsum non facit*.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32837)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, cum aliquid contingit in rebus naturalibus praeter naturam inditam, hoc potest dupliciter contingere. Uno modo, per actionem agentis qui inclinationem naturalem non dedit, sicut cum homo movet corpus grave sursum, quod non habet ab eo ut moveatur deorsum, et hoc est contra naturam. Alio modo, per actionem illius agentis a quo dependet actio naturalis. Et hoc non est contra naturam, ut patet in fluxu et refluxu maris, qui non est contra naturam, quamvis sit praeter motum naturalem aquae, quae movetur deorsum; est enim ex impressione caelestis corporis, a quo dependet naturalis inclinatio inferiorum corporum. Cum igitur naturae ordo sit a Deo rebus inditus, si quid praeter hunc ordinem faciat, non est contra naturam. Unde Augustinus dicit, XXVI contra Faustum, quod *id est cuique rei naturale, quod ille fecerit a quo est omnis modus, numerus et ordo naturae*.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32838)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ordo iustitiae est secundum relationem ad causam primam, quae est regula omnis iustitiae. Et ideo praeter hunc ordinem, Deus nihil facere potest.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32839)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Deus sic rebus certum ordinem indidit, ut tamen sibi reservaret quid ipse aliquando aliter ex causa esset facturus. Unde cum praeter hunc ordinem agit, non mutatur.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 105 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32840)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non omnia quae Deus facit praeter ordinem naturalem rerum, sint miracula. Creatio enim mundi, et etiam animarum, et iustificatio impii fiunt a Deo praeter ordinem naturalem, non enim fiunt per actionem alicuius causae naturalis. Et tamen haec miracula non dicuntur. Ergo non omnia quae facit Deus praeter ordinem naturalem rerum, sunt miracula.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32841)\n\nPraeterea, miraculum dicitur aliquid arduum et insolitum supra facultatem naturae et spem admirantis proveniens. Sed quaedam fiunt praeter naturae ordinem, quae tamen non sunt ardua, sunt enim in minimis rebus, sicut in restauratione gemmarum, vel sanatione aegrorum. Nec etiam sunt insolita, cum frequenter eveniant, sicut cum infirmi in plateis ponebantur ut ad umbram Petri sanarentur. Nec etiam sunt supra facultatem naturae, ut cum aliqui sanantur a febribus. Nec etiam supra spem, sicut resurrectionem mortuorum omnes speramus, quae tamen fiet praeter ordinem naturae. Ergo non omnia quae fiunt praeter naturae ordinem, sunt miracula.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32842)\n\nPraeterea, miraculi nomen ab admiratione sumitur. Sed admiratio est de rebus sensui manifestis. Sed quandoque aliqua accidunt praeter ordinem naturalem in rebus sensui non manifestis, sicut cum apostoli facti sunt scientes, neque invenientes neque discentes. Ergo non omnia quae fiunt praeter ordinem naturae, sunt miracula.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32843)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XXVI contra Faustum, quod *cum Deus aliquid facit contra cognitum nobis cursum solitumque naturae, magnalia, vel mirabilia nominantur*.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32844)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod nomen miraculi ab admiratione sumitur. Admiratio autem consurgit, cum effectus sunt manifesti et causa occulta; sicut aliquis admiratur cum videt eclipsim solis et ignorat causam, ut dicitur in principio Metaphys. Potest autem causa effectus alicuius apparentis alicui esse nota, quae tamen est aliis incognita. Unde aliquid est mirum uni, quod non est mirum aliis; sicut eclipsim solis miratur rusticus, non autem astrologus. Miraculum autem dicitur quasi admiratione plenum, quod scilicet habet causam simpliciter et omnibus occultam. Haec autem est Deus. Unde illa quae a Deo fiunt praeter causas nobis notas, miracula dicuntur.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32845)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod creatio, et iustificatio impii, etsi a solo Deo fiant, non tamen, proprie loquendo, miracula dicuntur. Quia non sunt nata fieri per alias causas, et ita non contingunt praeter ordinem naturae, cum haec ad ordinem naturae non pertineant.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32846)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod arduum dicitur miraculum, non propter dignitatem rei in qua fit; sed quia excedit facultatem naturae. Similiter etiam insolitum dicitur, non quia frequenter non eveniat sed quia est praeter naturalem consuetudinem. Supra facultatem autem naturae dicitur aliquid, non solum propter substantiam facti sed etiam propter modum et ordinem faciendi. Supra spem etiam naturae miraculum esse dicitur; non supra spem gratiae, quae est ex fide, per quam credimus resurrectionem futuram.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32847)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod scientia apostolorum, quamvis secundum se non fuerit manifesta, manifestabatur tamen in effectibus, ex quibus mirabilis apparebat.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 105 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32848)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod unum miraculum non sit maius alio. Dicit enim Augustinus, in epistola ad Volusianum, *in rebus mirabiliter factis tota ratio facti est potentia facientis*. Sed eadem potentia, scilicet Dei, fiunt omnia miracula. Ergo unum non est maius alio.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32849)\n\nPraeterea, potentia Dei est infinita. Sed infinitum improportionabiliter excedit omne finitum. Ergo non magis est mirandum quod faciat hunc effectum, quam illum. Ergo unum miraculum non est maius altero.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32850)\n\nSed contra est quod dominus dicit, Ioan. XIV, de operibus miraculosis loquens, *opera quae ego facio, et ipse faciet, et maiora horum faciet*.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32851)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod nihil potest dici miraculum ex comparatione potentiae divinae, quia quodcumque factum, divinae potentiae comparatum, est minimum; secundum illud Isaiae XL, *ecce gentes quasi stilla situlae, et quasi momentum staterae reputatae sunt*. Sed dicitur aliquid miraculum per comparationem ad facultatem naturae, quam excedit. Et ideo secundum quod magis excedit facultatem naturae, secundum hoc maius miraculum dicitur. Excedit autem aliquid facultatem naturae tripliciter. Uno modo, quantum ad substantiam facti, sicut quod duo corpora sint simul, vel quod sol retrocedat, aut quod corpus humanum glorificetur; quod nullo modo natura facere potest. Et ista tenent summum gradum in miraculis. Secundo aliquid excedit facultatem naturae, non quantum ad id quod fit, sed quantum ad id in quo fit, sicut resuscitatio mortuorum, et illuminatio caecorum, et similia. Potest enim natura causare vitam, sed non in mortuo, et potest praestare visum, sed non caeco. Et haec tenent secundum locum in miraculis. Tertio modo excedit aliquid facultatem naturae, quantum ad modum et ordinem faciendi, sicut cum aliquis subito per virtutem divinam a febre curatur absque curatione et consueto processu naturae in talibus, et cum statim aer divina virtute in pluvias densatur absque naturalibus causis, sicut factum est ad preces Samuelis et Eliae. Et huiusmodi tenent infimum locum in miraculis. Quaelibet tamen horum habent diversos gradus, secundum quod diversimode excedunt facultatem naturae.\n\n[I q. 105 a. 8 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32852)\n\nEt per hoc patet solutio ad obiecta, quae procedunt ex parte divinae potentiae.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0002","text":"[I q. 105 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32791)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de secundo effectu gubernationis divinae qui est mutatio creaturarum. Et primo, de mutatione creaturarum a Deo; secundo, de mutatione unius creaturae ab alia. Circa primum quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum Deus immediate possit movere materiam ad formam. Secundo, utrum immediate possit movere aliquod corpus. Tertio, utrum possit movere intellectum. Quarto, utrum possit movere voluntatem. Quinto, utrum Deus operetur in omni operante. Sexto, utrum possit aliquid facere praeter ordinem rebus inditum. Septimo, utrum omnia quae sic Deus facit, sint miracula. Octavo, de diversitate miraculorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0005","text":"[I q. 105 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32792)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non possit immediate movere materiam ad formam. Sicut enim probat philosophus in VII Metaphys., formam in hac materia nihil facere potest nisi forma quae est in materia, quia simile facit sibi simile. Sed Deus non est forma in materia. Ergo non potest causare formam in materia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0007","text":"[I q. 105 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32793)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0008","text":"Praeterea, si aliquod agens se habeat ad multa, nullum eorum producet, nisi determinetur ad ipsum per aliquid aliud, ut enim in III de anima dicitur, universalis opinio non movet nisi mediante aliqua particulari apprehensione. Sed virtus divina est universalis causa omnium. Ergo non potest producere aliquam particularem formam, nisi mediante aliquo particulari agente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0009","text":"[I q. 105 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32794)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0010","text":"Praeterea, sicut esse commune dependet a prima causa universali, ita esse determinatum dependet a determinatis causis particularibus, ut supra habitum est. Sed determinatum esse alicuius rei est per propriam eius formam. Ergo propriae rerum formae non producuntur a Deo, nisi mediantibus causis particularibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0011","text":"[I q. 105 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32795)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Gen. II, *formavit Deus hominem de limo terrae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0013","text":"[I q. 105 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32796)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deus immediate potest movere materiam ad formam. Quia ens in potentia passiva reduci potest in actum, a potentia activa quae eam sub sua potestate continet. Cum igitur materia contineatur sub potestate divina, utpote a Deo producta, potest reduci in actum per divinam potentiam. Et hoc est moveri materiam ad formam, quia forma nihil aliud est quam actus materiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0015","text":"[I q. 105 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32797)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0016","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod effectus aliquis invenitur assimilari causae agenti dupliciter. Uno modo, secundum eandem speciem; ut homo generatur ab homine, et ignis ab igne. Alio modo, secundum virtualem continentiam, prout scilicet forma effectus virtualiter continetur in causa, et sic animalia ex putrefactione generata, et plantae et corpora mineralia assimilantur soli et stellis, quorum virtute generantur. Sic igitur effectus causae agenti similatur secundum totum illud ad quod se extendit virtus agentis. Virtus autem Dei se extendit ad formam et materiam, ut supra habitum est. Unde compositum quod generatur, similatur Deo secundum virtualem continentiam, sicut similatur composito generanti per similitudinem speciei. Unde sicut compositum generans potest movere materiam ad formam generando compositum sibi simile, ita et Deus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0017","text":"Non autem aliqua alia forma non in materia existens, quia materia non continetur in virtute alterius substantiae separatae. Et ideo Daemones et Angeli operantur circa haec visibilia, non quidem imprimendo formas, sed adhibendo corporalia semina.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0018","text":"[I q. 105 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32798)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ratio illa procederet, si Deus ageret, ex necessitate naturae. Sed quia agit per voluntatem et intellectum, qui cognoscit rationes proprias omnium formarum, et non solum universales; inde est quod potest determinate hanc vel illam formam materiae imprimere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0020","text":"[I q. 105 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32799)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod hoc ipsum quod causae secundae ordinantur ad determinatos effectus est illis a Deo. Unde Deus, quia alias causas ordinat ad determinatos effectus, potest etiam determinatos effectus producere per seipsum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0023","text":"[I q. 105 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32800)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non possit immediate movere aliquod corpus. Cum enim movens et motum oporteat esse simul, ut probatur in VII Physic., oportet esse contactum quendam moventis et moti. Sed non potest esse contactus Dei, et corporis alicuius, dicit enim Dionysius, in I cap. de Div. Nom., quod *Dei non est aliquis tactus*. Ergo Deus non potest immediate movere aliquod corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0025","text":"[I q. 105 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32801)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0026","text":"Praeterea, Deus est movens non motum. Tale autem est appetibile apprehensum. Movet igitur Deus sicut desideratum et apprehensum. Sed non apprehenditur nisi ab intellectu, qui non est corpus, nec virtus corporis. Ergo Deus non potest movere aliquod corpus immediate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0027","text":"[I q. 105 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32802)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0028","text":"Praeterea, philosophus probat in VIII Physic., quod potentia infinita movet in instanti. Sed impossibile est aliquod corpus in instanti moveri, quia, cum omnis motus sit inter opposita, sequeretur quod duo opposita simul inessent eidem; quod est impossibile. Ergo corpus non potest immediate moveri a potentia infinita. Potentia autem Dei est infinita, ut supra habitum est. Ergo Deus non potest immediate movere aliquod corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0029","text":"[I q. 105 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32803)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0030","text":"Sed contra, opera sex dierum Deus fecit immediate; in quibus continetur motus corporum, ut patet per hoc quod dicitur Gen. I, *congregentur aquae in locum unum*. Ergo Deus immediate potest movere corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0031","text":"[I q. 105 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32804)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0032","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod erroneum est dicere Deum non posse facere per seipsum omnes determinatos effectus qui fiunt per quamcumque causam creatam. Unde cum corpora moveantur immediate a causis creatis, nulli debet venire in dubium quin Deus possit movere immediate quodcumque corpus. Et hoc quidem consequens est ad ea quae supra dicta sunt. Nam omnis motus corporis cuiuscumque vel consequitur ad aliquam formam, sicut motus localis gravium et levium consequitur formam quae datur a generante, ratione cuius generans dicitur movens, vel est via ad formam aliquam, sicut calefactio est via ad formam ignis. Eiusdem autem est imprimere formam, et disponere ad formam, et dare motum consequentem formam, ignis enim non solum generat alium ignem, sed etiam calefacit, et sursum movet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0033","text":"Cum igitur Deus possit immediate formam materiae imprimere, consequens est ut possit, secundum quemcumque motum, corpus quodcumque movere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0034","text":"[I q. 105 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32805)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0035","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod duplex est tactus, scilicet corporalis, sicut duo corpora se tangunt; et virtualis, sicut dicitur quod contristans tangit contristatum. Secundum igitur primum contactum, Deus, cum sit incorporeus, nec tangit nec tangitur. Secundum autem virtualem contactum, tangit quidem movendo creaturas, sed non tangitur, quia nullius creaturae virtus naturalis potest ad ipsum pertingere. Et sic intellexit Dionysius quod non est tactus Dei, ut scilicet tangatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0036","text":"[I q. 105 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32806)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0037","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod movet Deus sicut desideratum et intellectum. Sed non oportet quod semper moveat sicut desideratum et intellectum ab eo quod movetur; sed sicut desideratum et notum a seipso; quia omnia operatur propter suam bonitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0038","text":"[I q. 105 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32807)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0039","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod philosophus in VIII Physic. intendit probare quod virtus primi motoris non sit virtus in magnitudine, tali ratione, virtus primi motoris est infinita (quod probat per hoc quod potest movere tempore infinito); virtus autem infinita, si esset in aliqua magnitudine, moveret in non tempore, quod est impossibile; ergo oportet quod infinita virtus primi motoris sit non in magnitudine. Ex quo patet quod corpus moveri in non tempore, non consequitur nisi virtutem infinitam in magnitudine. Cuius ratio est, quia omnis virtus quae est in magnitudine, movet secundum se totam, cum moveat per necessitatem naturae. Potentia autem infinita improportionabiliter excedit quamlibet potentiam finitam. Quanto autem maior est potentia moventis, tanto est maior velocitas motus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0040","text":"Cum igitur potentia finita moveat tempore determinato, sequitur quod potentia infinita non moveat in aliquo tempore, quia cuiuscumque temporis ad aliud tempus est aliqua proportio. Sed virtus quae non est in magnitudine, est virtus alicuius intelligentis, qui operatur in effectibus secundum quod eis convenit. Et ideo, cum corpori non possit esse conveniens moveri in non tempore, non sequitur quod moveat in non tempore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0041","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0042","text":"[I q. 105 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32808)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0043","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non moveat immediate intellectum creatum. Actio enim intellectus est ab eo in quo est, non enim transit in exteriorem materiam, ut dicitur in IX Metaphys. Actio autem eius quod movetur ab alio, non est ab eo in quo est, sed a movente. Non ergo intellectus movetur ab alio. Et ita videtur quod Deus non possit movere intellectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0044","text":"[I q. 105 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32809)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0045","text":"Praeterea, id quod habet in se principium sufficiens sui motus, non movetur ab alio. Sed motus intellectus est ipsum intelligere eius, sicut dicitur quod intelligere vel sentire est motus quidam, secundum philosophum, in III de anima. Sufficiens autem principium intelligendi est lumen intelligibile inditum intellectui. Ergo non movetur ab alio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0046","text":"[I q. 105 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32810)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0047","text":"Praeterea, sicut sensus movetur a sensibili, ita intellectus ab intelligibili. Sed Deus non est intelligibilis nobis, sed nostrum intellectum excedit. Ergo Deus non potest movere nostrum intellectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0048","text":"[I q. 105 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32811)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0049","text":"Sed contra, docens movet intellectum addiscentis. Sed Deus docet hominem scientiam, sicut dicitur in Psalmo. Ergo Deus movet intellectum hominis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0050","text":"[I q. 105 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32812)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0051","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut in motibus corporalibus movens dicitur quod dat formam quae est principium motus; ita dicitur movere intellectum, quod causat formam quae est principium intellectualis operationis, quae dicitur motus intellectus. Operationis autem intellectus est duplex principium in intelligente, unum scilicet quod est ipsa virtus intellectualis, quod quidem principium est etiam in intelligente in potentia; aliud autem est principium intelligendi in actu, scilicet similitudo rei intellectae in intelligente. Dicitur ergo aliquid movere intellectum, sive det intelligenti virtutem ad intelligendum, sive imprimat ei similitudinem rei intellectae. Utroque autem modo Deus movet intellectum creatum. Ipse enim est primum ens immateriale. Et quia intellectualitas consequitur immaterialitatem, sequitur quod ipse sit primum intelligens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0052","text":"Unde cum primum in quolibet ordine sit causa eorum quae consequuntur, sequitur quod ab ipso sit omnis virtus intelligendi. Similiter cum ipse sit primum ens, et omnia entia praeexistant in ipso sicut in prima causa, oportet quod sint in eo intelligibiliter secundum modum eius. Sicut enim omnes rationes rerum intelligibiles primo existunt in Deo, et ab derivantur in alios intellectus, ut actu intelligant; sic etiam derivantur in creaturas ut subsistant. Sic igitur Deus movet intellectum creatum, inquantum dat ei virtutem ad intelligendum, vel naturalem vel superadditam; et inquantum imprimit ei species intelligibiles; et utrumque tenet et conservat in esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0053","text":"[I q. 105 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32813)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0054","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod operatio intellectualis est quidem ab intellectu in quo est, sicut a causa secunda, sed a Deo sicut a causa prima. Ab ipso enim datur intelligenti quod intelligere possit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0055","text":"[I q. 105 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32814)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0056","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod lumen intellectuale, simul cum similitudine rei intellectae, est sufficiens principium intelligendi; secundarium tamen, et a primo principio dependens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0057","text":"[I q. 105 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32815)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0058","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod intelligibile movet intellectum nostrum, inquantum quodammodo imprimit ei suam similitudinem, per quam intelligi potest. Sed similitudines quas Deus imprimit intellectui creato, non sufficiunt ad ipsum Deum intelligendum per essentiam, ut supra habitum est. Unde movet intellectum creatum, cum tamen non sit ei intelligibilis, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0059","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0060","text":"[I q. 105 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32816)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0061","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non possit movere voluntatem creatam. Omne enim quod movetur ab extrinseco, cogitur. Sed voluntas non potest cogi. Ergo non movetur ab aliquo extrinseco. Et ita non potest moveri a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0062","text":"[I q. 105 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32817)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0063","text":"Praeterea, Deus non potest facere quod contradictoria sint simul vera. Hoc autem sequeretur, si voluntatem moveret, nam voluntarie moveri est ex se moveri, et non ab alio. Ergo Deus non potest voluntatem movere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0064","text":"[I q. 105 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32818)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0065","text":"Praeterea, motus magis attribuitur moventi quam mobili, unde homicidium non attribuitur lapidi, sed proiicienti. Si igitur Deus moveat voluntatem, sequitur quod opera voluntaria non imputentur homini ad meritum vel demeritum. Hoc autem est falsum. Non igitur Deus movet voluntatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0066","text":"[I q. 105 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32819)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0067","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur ad Philipp. II, *Deus est qui operatur in nobis velle et perficere*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0068","text":"[I q. 105 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32820)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0069","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut intellectus, ut dictum est, movetur ab obiecto, et ab eo qui dedit virtutem intelligendi; ita voluntas movetur ab obiecto, quod est bonum, et ab eo qui creat virtutem volendi. Potest autem voluntas moveri sicut ab obiecto, a quocumque bono; non tamen sufficienter et efficaciter nisi a Deo. Non enim sufficienter aliquid potest movere aliquod mobile, nisi virtus activa moventis excedat, vel saltem adaequet virtutem passivam mobilis. Virtus autem passiva voluntatis se extendit ad bonum in universali, est enim eius obiectum bonum universale, sicut et intellectus obiectum est ens universale. Quodlibet autem bonum creatum est quoddam particulare bonum, solus autem Deus est bonum universale. Unde ipse solus implet voluntatem, et sufficienter eam movet ut obiectum. Similiter autem et virtus volendi a solo Deo causatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0070","text":"Velle enim nihil aliud est quam inclinatio quaedam in obiectum voluntatis, quod est bonum universale. Inclinare autem in bonum universale est primi moventis cui proportionatur ultimus finis, sicut in rebus humanis dirigere ad bonum commune est eius qui praeest multitudini. Unde utroque modo proprium est Dei movere voluntatem, sed maxime secundo modo, interius eam inclinando.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0071","text":"[I q. 105 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32821)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0072","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod illud quod movetur ab altero dicitur cogi, si moveatur contra inclinationem propriam, sed si moveatur ab alio quod sibi dat propriam inclinationem, non dicitur cogi; sicut grave, cum movetur deorsum a generante, non cogitur. Sic igitur Deus, movendo voluntatem, non cogit ipsam, quia dat ei eius propriam inclinationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0073","text":"[I q. 105 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32822)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0074","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod moveri voluntarie est moveri ex se, idest a principio intrinseco, sed illud principium intrinsecum potest esse ab alio principio extrinseco. Et sic moveri ex se non repugnat ei quod movetur ab alio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0075","text":"[I q. 105 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32823)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0076","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, si voluntas ita moveretur ab alio quod ex se nullatenus moveretur, opera voluntatis non imputarentur ad meritum vel demeritum. Sed quia per hoc quod movetur ab alio, non excluditur quin moveatur ex se, ut dictum est; ideo per consequens non tollitur ratio meriti vel demeriti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0077","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0078","text":"[I q. 105 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32824)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0079","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non operetur in omni operante. Nulla enim insufficientia est Deo attribuenda. Si igitur Deus operatur in omni operante, sufficienter in quolibet operatur. Superfluum igitur esset quod agens creatum aliquid operaretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0080","text":"[I q. 105 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32825)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0081","text":"Praeterea, una operatio non est simul a duobus operantibus, sicut nec unus numero motus potest esse duorum mobilium. Si igitur operatio creaturae est a Deo in creatura operante, non potest esse simul a creatura. Et ita nulla creatura aliquid operatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0082","text":"[I q. 105 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32826)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0083","text":"Praeterea, faciens dicitur esse causa operationis facti, inquantum dat ei formam qua operatur. Si igitur Deus est causa operationis rerum factarum ab ipso, hoc erit inquantum dat eis virtutem operandi. Sed hoc est a principio, quando rem facit. Ergo videtur quod ulterius non operetur in creatura operante.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0084","text":"[I q. 105 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32827)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0085","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Isaiae XXVI, *omnia opera nostra operatus es in nobis, domine*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0086","text":"[I q. 105 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32828)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0087","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Deum operari in quolibet operante aliqui sic intellexerunt, quod nulla virtus creata aliquid operaretur in rebus, sed solus Deus immediate omnia operaretur; puta quod ignis non calefaceret, sed Deus in igne, et similiter de omnibus aliis. Hoc autem est impossibile. Primo quidem, quia sic subtraheretur ordo causae et causati a rebus creatis. Quod pertinet ad impotentiam creantis, ex virtute enim agentis est, quod suo effectui det virtutem agendi. Secundo, quia virtutes operativae quae in rebus inveniuntur, frustra essent rebus attributae, si per eas nihil operarentur. Quinimmo omnes res creatae viderentur quodammodo esse frustra, si propria operatione destituerentur, cum omnis res sit propter suam operationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0088","text":"Semper enim imperfectum est propter perfectius, sicut igitur materia est propter formam, ita forma, quae est actus primus, est propter suam operationem, quae est actus secundus; et sic operatio est finis rei creatae. Sic igitur intelligendum est Deum operari in rebus, quod tamen ipsae res propriam habeant operationem. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod, cum sint causarum quatuor genera, materia quidem non est principium actionis, sed se habet ut subiectum recipiens actionis effectum. Finis vero et agens et forma se habent ut actionis principium, sed ordine quodam. Nam primo quidem, principium actionis est finis, qui movet agentem; secundo vero, agens; tertio autem, forma eius quod ab agente applicatur ad agendum (quamvis et ipsum agens per formam suam agat); ut patet in artificialibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0089","text":"Artifex enim movetur ad agendum a fine, qui est ipsum operatum, puta arca vel lectus; et applicat ad actionem securim quae incidit per suum acumen. Sic igitur secundum haec tria Deus in quolibet operante operatur. Primo quidem, secundum rationem finis. Cum enim omnis operatio sit propter aliquod bonum verum vel apparens; nihil autem est vel apparet bonum, nisi secundum quod participat aliquam similitudinem summi boni, quod est Deus; sequitur quod ipse Deus sit cuiuslibet operationis causa ut finis. Similiter etiam considerandum est quod, si sint multa agentia ordinata, semper secundum agens agit in virtute primi, nam primum agens movet secundum ad agendum. Et secundum hoc, omnia agunt in virtute ipsius Dei; et ita ipse est causa actionum omnium agentium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0090","text":"Tertio, considerandum est quod Deus movet non solum res ad operandum, quasi applicando formas et virtutes rerum ad operationem, sicut etiam artifex applicat securim ad scindendum, qui tamen interdum formam securi non tribuit; sed etiam dat formam creaturis agentibus, et eas tenet in esse. Unde non solum est causa actionum inquantum dat formam quae est principium actionis, sicut generans dicitur esse causa motus gravium et levium; sed etiam sicut conservans formas et virtutes rerum; prout sol dicitur esse causa manifestationis colorum, inquantum dat et conservat lumen, quo manifestantur colores. Et quia forma rei est intra rem, et tanto magis quanto consideratur ut prior et universalior; et ipse Deus est proprie causa ipsius esse universalis in rebus omnibus, quod inter omnia est magis intimum rebus; sequitur quod Deus in omnibus intime operetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0091","text":"Et propter hoc in sacra Scriptura operationes naturae Deo attribuuntur quasi operanti in natura; secundum illud Iob X, *pelle et carnibus vestisti me, ossibus et nervis compegisti me*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0092","text":"[I q. 105 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32829)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0093","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Deus sufficienter operatur in rebus ad modum primi agentis, nec propter hoc superfluit operatio secundorum agentium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0094","text":"[I q. 105 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32830)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0095","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod una actio non procedit a duobus agentibus unius ordinis, sed nihil prohibet quin una et eadem actio procedat a primo et secundo agente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0096","text":"[I q. 105 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32831)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0097","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Deus non solum dat formas rebus, sed etiam conservat eas in esse, et applicat eas ad agendum, et est finis omnium actionum, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0098","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0099","text":"[I q. 105 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32832)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0100","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Deus non possit facere aliquid praeter ordinem rebus inditum. Dicit enim Augustinus, XXVI contra Faustum, *Deus, conditor et creator omnium naturarum, nihil contra naturam facit*. Sed hoc videtur esse contra naturam, quod est praeter ordinem naturaliter rebus inditum. Ergo Deus non potest facere aliquid praeter ordinem rebus inditum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0101","text":"[I q. 105 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32833)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0102","text":"Praeterea, sicut ordo iustitiae est a Deo, ita et ordo naturae. Sed Deus non potest facere aliquid praeter ordinem iustitiae, faceret enim tunc aliquid iniustum. Ergo non potest facere aliquid praeter ordinem naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0103","text":"[I q. 105 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32834)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0104","text":"Praeterea, ordinem naturae Deus instituit. Si igitur praeter ordinem naturae Deus aliquid faciat, videtur quod ipse sit mutabilis. Quod est inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0105","text":"[I q. 105 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32835)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0106","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XXVI contra Faustum, quod *Deus aliquando aliquid facit contra solitum cursum naturae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0107","text":"[I q. 105 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32836)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0108","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod a qualibet causa derivatur aliquis ordo in suos effectus, cum quaelibet causa habeat rationem principii. Et ideo secundum multiplicationem causarum, multiplicantur et ordines, quorum unus continetur sub altero, sicut et causa continetur sub causa. Unde causa superior non continetur sub ordine causae inferioris, sed e converso. Cuius exemplum apparet in rebus humanis, nam ex patrefamilias dependet ordo domus, qui continetur sub ordine civitatis, qui procedit a civitatis rectore, cum et hic contineatur sub ordine regis, a quo totum regnum ordinatur. Si ergo ordo rerum consideretur prout dependet a prima causa, sic contra rerum ordinem Deus facere non potest, sic enim si faceret, faceret contra suam praescientiam aut voluntatem aut bonitatem. Si vero consideretur rerum ordo prout dependet a qualibet secundarum causarum, sic Deus potest facere praeter ordinem rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0109","text":"Quia ordini secundarum causarum ipse non est subiectus, sed talis ordo ei subiicitur, quasi ab eo procedens non per necessitatem naturae, sed per arbitrium voluntatis, potuisset enim et alium ordinem rerum instituere. Unde et potest praeter hunc ordinem institutum agere, cum voluerit; puta agendo effectus secundarum causarum sine ipsis, vel producendo aliquos effectus ad quos causae secundae non se extendunt. Unde et Augustinus dicit, XXVI contra Faustum, quod *Deus contra solitum cursum naturae facit; sed contra summam legem tam nullo modo facit, quam contra seipsum non facit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0110","text":"[I q. 105 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32837)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0111","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, cum aliquid contingit in rebus naturalibus praeter naturam inditam, hoc potest dupliciter contingere. Uno modo, per actionem agentis qui inclinationem naturalem non dedit, sicut cum homo movet corpus grave sursum, quod non habet ab eo ut moveatur deorsum, et hoc est contra naturam. Alio modo, per actionem illius agentis a quo dependet actio naturalis. Et hoc non est contra naturam, ut patet in fluxu et refluxu maris, qui non est contra naturam, quamvis sit praeter motum naturalem aquae, quae movetur deorsum; est enim ex impressione caelestis corporis, a quo dependet naturalis inclinatio inferiorum corporum. Cum igitur naturae ordo sit a Deo rebus inditus, si quid praeter hunc ordinem faciat, non est contra naturam. Unde Augustinus dicit, XXVI contra Faustum, quod *id est cuique rei naturale, quod ille fecerit a quo est omnis modus, numerus et ordo naturae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0112","text":"[I q. 105 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32838)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0113","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ordo iustitiae est secundum relationem ad causam primam, quae est regula omnis iustitiae. Et ideo praeter hunc ordinem, Deus nihil facere potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0114","text":"[I q. 105 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32839)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0115","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Deus sic rebus certum ordinem indidit, ut tamen sibi reservaret quid ipse aliquando aliter ex causa esset facturus. Unde cum praeter hunc ordinem agit, non mutatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0116","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0117","text":"[I q. 105 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32840)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0118","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non omnia quae Deus facit praeter ordinem naturalem rerum, sint miracula. Creatio enim mundi, et etiam animarum, et iustificatio impii fiunt a Deo praeter ordinem naturalem, non enim fiunt per actionem alicuius causae naturalis. Et tamen haec miracula non dicuntur. Ergo non omnia quae facit Deus praeter ordinem naturalem rerum, sunt miracula.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0119","text":"[I q. 105 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32841)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0120","text":"Praeterea, miraculum dicitur aliquid arduum et insolitum supra facultatem naturae et spem admirantis proveniens. Sed quaedam fiunt praeter naturae ordinem, quae tamen non sunt ardua, sunt enim in minimis rebus, sicut in restauratione gemmarum, vel sanatione aegrorum. Nec etiam sunt insolita, cum frequenter eveniant, sicut cum infirmi in plateis ponebantur ut ad umbram Petri sanarentur. Nec etiam sunt supra facultatem naturae, ut cum aliqui sanantur a febribus. Nec etiam supra spem, sicut resurrectionem mortuorum omnes speramus, quae tamen fiet praeter ordinem naturae. Ergo non omnia quae fiunt praeter naturae ordinem, sunt miracula.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0121","text":"[I q. 105 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32842)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0122","text":"Praeterea, miraculi nomen ab admiratione sumitur. Sed admiratio est de rebus sensui manifestis. Sed quandoque aliqua accidunt praeter ordinem naturalem in rebus sensui non manifestis, sicut cum apostoli facti sunt scientes, neque invenientes neque discentes. Ergo non omnia quae fiunt praeter ordinem naturae, sunt miracula.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0123","text":"[I q. 105 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32843)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0124","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, XXVI contra Faustum, quod *cum Deus aliquid facit contra cognitum nobis cursum solitumque naturae, magnalia, vel mirabilia nominantur*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0125","text":"[I q. 105 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32844)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0126","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod nomen miraculi ab admiratione sumitur. Admiratio autem consurgit, cum effectus sunt manifesti et causa occulta; sicut aliquis admiratur cum videt eclipsim solis et ignorat causam, ut dicitur in principio Metaphys. Potest autem causa effectus alicuius apparentis alicui esse nota, quae tamen est aliis incognita. Unde aliquid est mirum uni, quod non est mirum aliis; sicut eclipsim solis miratur rusticus, non autem astrologus. Miraculum autem dicitur quasi admiratione plenum, quod scilicet habet causam simpliciter et omnibus occultam. Haec autem est Deus. Unde illa quae a Deo fiunt praeter causas nobis notas, miracula dicuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0127","text":"[I q. 105 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32845)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0128","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod creatio, et iustificatio impii, etsi a solo Deo fiant, non tamen, proprie loquendo, miracula dicuntur. Quia non sunt nata fieri per alias causas, et ita non contingunt praeter ordinem naturae, cum haec ad ordinem naturae non pertineant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0129","text":"[I q. 105 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32846)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0130","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod arduum dicitur miraculum, non propter dignitatem rei in qua fit; sed quia excedit facultatem naturae. Similiter etiam insolitum dicitur, non quia frequenter non eveniat sed quia est praeter naturalem consuetudinem. Supra facultatem autem naturae dicitur aliquid, non solum propter substantiam facti sed etiam propter modum et ordinem faciendi. Supra spem etiam naturae miraculum esse dicitur; non supra spem gratiae, quae est ex fide, per quam credimus resurrectionem futuram.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0131","text":"[I q. 105 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32847)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0132","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod scientia apostolorum, quamvis secundum se non fuerit manifesta, manifestabatur tamen in effectibus, ex quibus mirabilis apparebat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0133","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0134","text":"[I q. 105 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32848)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0135","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod unum miraculum non sit maius alio. Dicit enim Augustinus, in epistola ad Volusianum, *in rebus mirabiliter factis tota ratio facti est potentia facientis*. Sed eadem potentia, scilicet Dei, fiunt omnia miracula. Ergo unum non est maius alio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0136","text":"[I q. 105 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32849)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0137","text":"Praeterea, potentia Dei est infinita. Sed infinitum improportionabiliter excedit omne finitum. Ergo non magis est mirandum quod faciat hunc effectum, quam illum. Ergo unum miraculum non est maius altero.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0138","text":"[I q. 105 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32850)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0139","text":"Sed contra est quod dominus dicit, Ioan. XIV, de operibus miraculosis loquens, *opera quae ego facio, et ipse faciet, et maiora horum faciet*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0140","text":"[I q. 105 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32851)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0141","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod nihil potest dici miraculum ex comparatione potentiae divinae, quia quodcumque factum, divinae potentiae comparatum, est minimum; secundum illud Isaiae XL, *ecce gentes quasi stilla situlae, et quasi momentum staterae reputatae sunt*. Sed dicitur aliquid miraculum per comparationem ad facultatem naturae, quam excedit. Et ideo secundum quod magis excedit facultatem naturae, secundum hoc maius miraculum dicitur. Excedit autem aliquid facultatem naturae tripliciter. Uno modo, quantum ad substantiam facti, sicut quod duo corpora sint simul, vel quod sol retrocedat, aut quod corpus humanum glorificetur; quod nullo modo natura facere potest. Et ista tenent summum gradum in miraculis. Secundo aliquid excedit facultatem naturae, non quantum ad id quod fit, sed quantum ad id in quo fit, sicut resuscitatio mortuorum, et illuminatio caecorum, et similia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0142","text":"Potest enim natura causare vitam, sed non in mortuo, et potest praestare visum, sed non caeco. Et haec tenent secundum locum in miraculis. Tertio modo excedit aliquid facultatem naturae, quantum ad modum et ordinem faciendi, sicut cum aliquis subito per virtutem divinam a febre curatur absque curatione et consueto processu naturae in talibus, et cum statim aer divina virtute in pluvias densatur absque naturalibus causis, sicut factum est ad preces Samuelis et Eliae. Et huiusmodi tenent infimum locum in miraculis. Quaelibet tamen horum habent diversos gradus, secundum quod diversimode excedunt facultatem naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0143","text":"[I q. 105 a. 8 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32852)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0144","text":"Et per hoc patet solutio ad obiecta, quae procedunt ex parte divinae potentiae.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"670490015f303a3184cc48dfbe14423e28e6fde31f7feae97f3e7c2415b2c89c","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T153826670170Z_a2e4eda6","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 105","source_sha256":"670490015f303a3184cc48dfbe14423e28e6fde31f7feae97f3e7c2415b2c89c","sha256":"5e61cc351e080d68444bfdd61c0a08694daeb7102c917c75b40ad877074c30da","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:38:26.670199+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0002","text":"[I q. 105 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32791)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the second effect of divine governance, which is the change of creatures. First, the change of creatures by God; second, the change of one creature by another. Concerning the first, eight questions are asked. First, whether God can immediately move matter to form. Second, whether He can immediately move a body. Third, whether He can move the intellect. Fourth, whether He can move the will. Fifth, whether God works in every agent. Sixth, whether He can do anything outside the order bestowed upon things. Seventh, whether all the things which God does in this way are miracles. Eighth, concerning the diversity of miracles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0005","text":"[I q. 105 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32792)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that God cannot immediately move matter to form. For, as the Philosopher proves in VII Metaphysics, nothing can bring about a form in this matter except a form that is in matter, because like makes like itself. But God is not a form in matter. Therefore, He cannot cause form in matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0007","text":"[I q. 105 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32793)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0008","text":"Moreover, if an agent is related to many things, it will produce none of them unless it is determined to it by something else; for, as is said in III On the Soul, a universal opinion does not move except through some particular apprehension. But the divine power is the universal cause of all things. Therefore, it cannot produce any particular form except through some particular agent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0009","text":"[I q. 105 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32794)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0010","text":"Moreover, just as common being depends upon the first universal cause, so determinate being depends upon determinate particular causes, as has been held above. But the determinate being of a thing is through its proper form. Therefore, the proper forms of things are not produced by God except through particular causes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0011","text":"[I q. 105 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32795)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Gen. II, *God formed man from the slime of the earth*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0013","text":"[I q. 105 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32796)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0014","text":"I answer that God can immediately move matter to form. For a being in passive potency can be reduced to act by an active potency that contains it under its power. Therefore, since matter is contained under the divine power, as having been produced by God, it can be reduced to act through divine power. And this is for matter to be moved to form, because form is nothing other than the act of matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0015","text":"[I q. 105 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32797)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0016","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that an effect is found to be assimilated to an efficient cause in two ways. In one way, according to the same species; as man is generated by man, and fire by fire. In another way, according to virtual containment, namely insofar as the form of the effect is contained virtually in the cause; and thus animals generated from putrefaction, and plants and mineral bodies, are assimilated to the sun and stars, by whose power they are generated. Thus, therefore, an effect is assimilated to its efficient cause according to all that to which the power of the agent extends. But the power of God extends to form and matter, as has been held above. Hence the composite which is generated is assimilated to God according to virtual containment, just as it is assimilated to the generating composite by likeness of species. Hence, just as a generating composite can move matter to form by generating a composite like itself, so also can God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0017","text":"But no other form not existing in matter can do so, because matter is not contained in the power of another separate substance. And therefore demons and angels operate concerning these visible things, not indeed by impressing forms, but by applying bodily seeds.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0018","text":"[I q. 105 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32798)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0019","text":"To the second, it must be said that that argument would proceed if God acted from the necessity of nature. But because He acts through will and intellect, which knows the proper notions of all forms, and not only universal ones, it follows that He can determinately impress this or that form upon matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0020","text":"[I q. 105 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32799)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0021","text":"To the third, it must be said that this very fact—that secondary causes are ordered to determinate effects—is from God. Hence God, because He orders other causes to determinate effects, can also produce determinate effects through Himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0023","text":"[I q. 105 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32800)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0024","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that God cannot immediately move a body. For since the mover and the moved must be together, as is proved in VII Physics, there must be some contact of mover and moved. But there cannot be contact between God and any body, for Dionysius says, in chapter I of The Divine Names, that *there is no contact in God*. Therefore, God cannot immediately move any body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0025","text":"[I q. 105 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32801)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0026","text":"Moreover, God is a mover not moved. But such is the apprehended appetible. Therefore God moves as desired and apprehended. But He is apprehended only by the intellect, which is not a body, nor a power of a body. Therefore God cannot move any body immediately.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0027","text":"[I q. 105 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32802)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0028","text":"Moreover, the Philosopher proves in Physics VIII that an infinite power moves in an instant. But it is impossible for any body to be moved in an instant, because, since every motion is between opposites, it would follow that two opposites were in the same thing at once; which is impossible. Therefore a body cannot be moved immediately by an infinite power. But the power of God is infinite, as has been established above. Therefore God cannot immediately move any body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0029","text":"[I q. 105 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32803)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0030","text":"But on the contrary, God made the works of the six days immediately; among these is included the motion of bodies, as is clear from what is said in Gen. I: *let the waters be gathered together into one place*. Therefore God can immediately move a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0031","text":"[I q. 105 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32804)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0032","text":"I answer that it must be said that it is erroneous to say that God cannot by Himself produce all the determinate effects that are produced by any created cause. Hence, since bodies are moved immediately by created causes, no one ought to doubt that God can immediately move any body whatsoever. And this follows from what has been said above. For every motion of any body either follows upon some form, as the local motion of heavy and light things follows upon the form bestowed by the generator, by reason of which the generator is called a mover; or it is a way to some form, as heating is a way to the form of fire. But it belongs to the same thing to impress a form, to dispose toward a form, and to give the motion consequent upon the form; for fire not only generates another fire, but also heats and moves upward.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0033","text":"Therefore, since God can immediately impress form upon matter, it follows that He can move any body according to any kind of motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0034","text":"[I q. 105 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32805)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0035","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that there is a twofold contact: namely, bodily contact, as two bodies touch one another; and virtual contact, as one who causes sorrow is said to touch one made sorrowful. Therefore, according to the first kind of contact, God, since He is incorporeal, neither touches nor is touched. But according to virtual contact, He does indeed touch by moving creatures, but is not touched, because the natural power of no creature can reach unto Him. And Dionysius understood in this way that there is no contact of God, namely, such that He is touched.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0036","text":"[I q. 105 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32806)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0037","text":"To the second it must be said that God moves as desired and understood. But it is not necessary that He always move as desired and understood by that which is moved, but as desired and known by Himself; because He works all things for the sake of His goodness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0038","text":"[I q. 105 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32807)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0039","text":"To the third it must be said that the Philosopher in Physics VIII intends to prove that the power of the first mover is not a power in magnitude, by the following reasoning: the power of the first mover is infinite (which he proves from the fact that it can move for an infinite time); but an infinite power, if it were in some magnitude, would move in no time, which is impossible; therefore the infinite power of the first mover must not be in magnitude. From this it is clear that a body being moved in no time follows only from an infinite power in magnitude. The reason for this is that every power which is in magnitude moves according to its whole self, since it moves by the necessity of nature. But infinite power exceeds any finite power beyond proportion. Now, the greater the power of the mover, the greater the speed of the motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0040","text":"Therefore, since finite power moves in a determinate time, it follows that infinite power does not move in any time, because there is some proportion of any time to another time. But a power which is not in magnitude is the power of an intelligent being, who operates in effects according as befits them. And therefore, since it cannot be fitting for a body to be moved in no time, it does not follow that it moves in no time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0041","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0042","text":"[I q. 105 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32808)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0043","text":"We proceed thus to the third article. It seems that God does not immediately move the created intellect. For the action of the intellect is from that in which it is; for it does not pass into external matter, as is said in Metaphysics IX. But the action of that which is moved by another is not from that in which it is, but from the mover. Therefore the intellect is not moved by another. And thus it seems that God cannot move the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0044","text":"[I q. 105 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32809)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0045","text":"Moreover, that which has within itself a sufficient principle of its motion is not moved by another. But the motion of the intellect is its very understanding, just as understanding or sensing is said to be a certain motion, according to the Philosopher, in On the Soul III. But the intelligible light instilled into the intellect is a sufficient principle of understanding. Therefore it is not moved by another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0046","text":"[I q. 105 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32810)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0047","text":"Moreover, just as sense is moved by the sensible, so intellect is moved by the intelligible. But God is not intelligible to us, but exceeds our intellect. Therefore God cannot move our intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0048","text":"[I q. 105 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32811)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0049","text":"But on the contrary, one who teaches moves the intellect of the learner. But God teaches man knowledge, as is said in the Psalm. Therefore God moves the intellect of man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0050","text":"[I q. 105 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32812)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0051","text":"I answer that it must be said that, just as in bodily motions that which gives the form which is the principle of motion is said to move, so that which causes the form that is the principle of intellectual operation, which is called the motion of the intellect, is said to move the intellect. Now there is a twofold principle of the operation of the intellect in one who understands: one, namely, which is the intellectual power itself; and this principle is also in one who understands potentially; but the other is the principle of understanding actually, namely, the likeness of the thing understood in the one who understands. Therefore, something is said to move the intellect, whether it gives the one who understands the power to understand, or impresses upon him the likeness of the thing understood. But in both ways God moves the created intellect. For He Himself is the first immaterial being. And since intellectuality follows upon immateriality, it follows that He is the first understanding being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0052","text":"Hence, since the first in any order is the cause of those things which follow, it follows that every power of understanding is from Him. Likewise, since He is the first being, and all beings pre-exist in Him as in the first cause, they must be in Him intelligibly according to His mode. For just as all the intelligible notions of things first exist in God, and are derived from Him into other intellects, so that they may understand actually, so too they are derived into creatures so that they may subsist. Thus, therefore, God moves the created intellect, insofar as He gives it the power to understand, whether natural or superadded; and insofar as He impresses intelligible species upon it; and He holds and preserves both in being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0053","text":"[I q. 105 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32813)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0054","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that intellectual operation is indeed from the intellect in which it is, as from a secondary cause, but from God as from the first cause. For from Him it is given to the one who understands that he can understand.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0055","text":"[I q. 105 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32814)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0056","text":"To the second it must be said that the intellectual light, together with the likeness of the thing understood, is a sufficient principle of understanding; yet it is secondary and dependent upon the first principle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0057","text":"[I q. 105 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32815)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0058","text":"To the third it must be said that the intelligible moves our intellect insofar as it in some way impresses its likeness upon it, through which it can be understood. But the likenesses which God impresses upon the created intellect are not sufficient for understanding God Himself through His essence, as was held above. Hence He moves the created intellect, although He is not intelligible to it, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0059","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0060","text":"[I q. 105 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32816)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0061","text":"We proceed to the fourth as follows. It seems that God cannot move the created will. For everything moved from without is compelled. But the will cannot be compelled. Therefore it is not moved by anything external. And thus it cannot be moved by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0062","text":"[I q. 105 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32817)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0063","text":"Further, God cannot bring it about that contradictories are true at the same time. But this would follow if He moved the will, for to be moved voluntarily is to be moved by oneself, and not by another. Therefore God cannot move the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0064","text":"[I q. 105 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32818)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0065","text":"Further, motion is attributed more to the mover than to the movable; hence homicide is not attributed to the stone, but to the one throwing it. Therefore, if God moves the will, it follows that voluntary works are not imputed to man for merit or demerit. But this is false. Therefore God does not move the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0066","text":"[I q. 105 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32819)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0067","text":"But on the contrary is what is said in Philippians II, *God is the one who works in us both to will and to accomplish*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0068","text":"[I q. 105 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32820)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0069","text":"I answer that it must be said that, just as the intellect, as was said, is moved by the object and by him who gave the power of understanding, so the will is moved by the object, which is the good, and by him who creates the power of willing. Now the will can be moved as by an object by any good whatsoever; yet not sufficiently and efficaciously except by God. For something cannot sufficiently move any movable thing unless the active power of the mover exceeds, or at least equals, the passive power of the movable thing. But the passive power of the will extends to good in general; for its object is universal good, just as the object of the intellect is universal being. But every created good is a particular good, whereas God alone is universal good. Hence He alone fulfills the will, and sufficiently moves it as an object. Likewise, the power of willing is caused by God alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0070","text":"For willing is nothing other than a certain inclination toward the object of the will, which is universal good. But to incline toward universal good belongs to the first mover, to whom the ultimate end is proportioned, just as in human affairs to direct toward the common good belongs to him who presides over the multitude. Hence in both ways it is proper to God to move the will, but most especially in the second way, by inclining it inwardly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0071","text":"[I q. 105 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32821)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0072","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that which is moved by another is said to be compelled if it is moved contrary to its own inclination; but if it is moved by another that gives it its own inclination, it is not said to be compelled; just as a heavy thing, when it is moved downward by that which generates it, is not compelled. Thus, therefore, God, in moving the will, does not compel it, because He gives it its own inclination.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0073","text":"[I q. 105 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32822)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0074","text":"To the second, it must be said that to be moved voluntarily is to be moved from oneself, that is, from an intrinsic principle; but that intrinsic principle can be from another, extrinsic principle. And thus being moved from oneself is not incompatible with being moved by another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0075","text":"[I q. 105 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32823)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0076","text":"To the third, it must be said that, if the will were so moved by another that it were in no way moved of itself, the works of the will would not be imputed for merit or demerit. But because, by the fact that it is moved by another, it is not excluded that it is moved of itself, as has been said, the character of merit or demerit is consequently not removed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0077","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0078","text":"[I q. 105 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32824)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0079","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth. It seems that God does not operate in every agent. For no insufficiency is to be attributed to God. Therefore, if God operates in every agent, He operates sufficiently in each. Therefore it would be superfluous for a created agent to perform anything.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0080","text":"[I q. 105 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32825)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0081","text":"Moreover, one operation is not simultaneously from two agents, just as one numerically identical motion cannot belong to two things moved. Therefore, if the operation of a creature is from God in the creature operating, it cannot simultaneously be from the creature. And thus no creature performs anything.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0082","text":"[I q. 105 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32826)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0083","text":"Moreover, a maker is said to be the cause of the operation of the thing made, insofar as it gives it the form by which it operates. Therefore, if God is the cause of the operation of things made by Him, this will be insofar as He gives them the power to operate. But this occurs at the beginning, when He makes the thing. Therefore it seems that He does not further operate in the creature operating.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0084","text":"[I q. 105 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32827)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0085","text":"But to the contrary, it is said in Isaiah XXVI: *you have wrought all our works in us, O Lord*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0086","text":"[I q. 105 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32828)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0087","text":"I answer that some have understood God's operating in every agent in this way: that no created power would perform anything in things, but God alone would immediately perform all things; for example, that fire would not heat, but God would do so in the fire, and likewise concerning all other things. But this is impossible. First, indeed, because in this way the order of cause and caused would be removed from created things. This pertains to the impotence of the creator; for it belongs to the power of an agent to give its effect the power of acting. Second, because the operative powers found in things would be attributed to things in vain if they performed nothing through them. Indeed, all created things would seem in some way to exist in vain if they were deprived of their proper operation, since every thing exists for the sake of its operation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0088","text":"For the imperfect is always for the sake of the more perfect. Therefore, just as matter is for the sake of form, so form, which is first act, is for the sake of its operation, which is second act; and thus operation is the end of a created thing. Therefore, God must be understood to operate in things in such a way that the things themselves nevertheless have their proper operation. For evidence of this, it must be considered that, since there are four kinds of causes, matter is not a principle of action, but stands as a subject receiving the effect of the action. But the end, the agent, and the form stand as principles of action, yet in a certain order. For first, the principle of action is the end, which moves the agent; second, the agent; but third, the form of that which is applied by the agent to acting (although the agent itself also acts through its form); as is evident in artificial things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0089","text":"For an artisan is moved to act by the end, which is the thing itself produced, for example, a chest or a bed; and he applies to the action an axe which cuts by its sharpness. Therefore, according to these three, God operates in every agent. First, indeed, according to the character of an end. For since every operation is for the sake of some real or apparent good, and nothing is or appears good except insofar as it participates in some likeness of the highest good, which is God, it follows that God Himself is the cause of every operation as end. Likewise, it must also be considered that, if there are many ordered agents, the second agent always acts in the power of the first, for the first agent moves the second to act. And according to this, all things act in the power of God Himself; and thus He is the cause of the actions of all agents.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0090","text":"Third, it must be considered that God moves things not only to operate, as though applying the forms and powers of things to operation, just as an artisan also applies an axe to splitting, though he does not always confer the form of the axe; but He also gives form to acting creatures and keeps them in being. Hence He is the cause of actions not only insofar as He gives the form which is the principle of action, just as a generator is said to be the cause of the motion of heavy and light things; but also as preserving the forms and powers of things, just as the sun is said to be the cause of the manifestation of colors, insofar as it gives and preserves the light by which colors are manifested. And because the form of a thing is within the thing, and all the more so insofar as it is considered as prior and more universal; and God Himself is properly the cause of universal being itself in all things, which among all things is most intimate to things; it follows that God operates intimately in all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0091","text":"And for this reason in Sacred Scripture the operations of nature are attributed to God as operating in nature, according to that passage in Job X: *you clothed me with skin and flesh; you bound me together with bones and sinews*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0092","text":"[I q. 105 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32829)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0093","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that God operates sufficiently in things in the manner of a first agent, and for this reason the operation of secondary agents is not superfluous.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0094","text":"[I q. 105 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32830)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0095","text":"To the second, it must be said that one action does not proceed from two agents of one order, but nothing prevents one and the same action from proceeding from a first and a second agent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0096","text":"[I q. 105 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32831)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0097","text":"To the third, it must be said that God not only gives forms to things, but also preserves them in being, applies them to acting, and is the end of all actions, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0098","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0099","text":"[I q. 105 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32832)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0100","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth. It seems that God cannot do anything outside the order implanted in things. For Augustine says, in *Against Faustus*, XXVI: *God, the maker and creator of all natures, does nothing contrary to nature*. But that which is outside the order naturally implanted in things seems to be contrary to nature. Therefore God cannot do anything outside the order implanted in things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0101","text":"[I q. 105 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32833)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0102","text":"Further, just as the order of justice is from God, so also is the order of nature. But God cannot do anything outside the order of justice, for then he would do something unjust. Therefore he cannot do anything outside the order of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0103","text":"[I q. 105 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32834)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0104","text":"Further, God established the order of nature. Therefore, if God were to do anything outside the order of nature, it seems that he himself would be mutable. This is unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0105","text":"[I q. 105 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32835)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0106","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in *Against Faustus*, XXVI, that *God sometimes does something contrary to the customary course of nature*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0107","text":"[I q. 105 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32836)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0108","text":"I answer that it must be said that from every cause some order is derived into its effects, since every cause has the character of a principle. Therefore, according to the multiplication of causes, orders too are multiplied, one of which is contained under another, just as a cause is contained under a cause. Hence a higher cause is not contained under the order of a lower cause, but conversely. An example of this appears in human affairs: for the order of the household depends upon the head of the household, and it is contained under the order of the city, which proceeds from the ruler of the city; and this too is contained under the order of the king, by whom the whole kingdom is ordered. Therefore, if the order of things is considered insofar as it depends on the first cause, then God cannot act against the order of things; for if he did so, he would act against his own foreknowledge, or will, or goodness. But if the order of things is considered insofar as it depends on any of the secondary causes, then God can act outside the order of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0109","text":"For he is not subject to the order of secondary causes, but such an order is subject to him, as proceeding from him not by the necessity of nature but by the choice of his will; for he could have established another order of things. Hence he can also act outside this established order when he wills: for example, by producing the effects of secondary causes without those causes themselves, or by producing certain effects to which secondary causes do not extend. Hence Augustine also says, in *Against Faustus*, XXVI, that *God acts contrary to the customary course of nature; but he no more acts contrary to the supreme law than he acts contrary to himself*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0110","text":"[I q. 105 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32837)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0111","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, when something occurs in natural things outside the nature implanted in them, this can occur in two ways. In one way, through the action of an agent that did not give the natural inclination, as when a man moves a heavy body upward, though it does not have from him that it be moved downward; and this is contrary to nature. In another way, through the action of that agent upon whom natural action depends. And this is not contrary to nature, as is clear in the ebb and flow of the sea, which is not contrary to nature, although it is outside the natural motion of water, which moves downward; for it results from the influence of a heavenly body, upon which the natural inclination of lower bodies depends. Therefore, since the order of nature has been implanted in things by God, if he does anything outside this order, it is not contrary to nature. Hence Augustine says, in *Against Faustus*, XXVI, that *whatsoever he has made is natural to each thing, he from whom is every measure, number, and order of nature*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0112","text":"[I q. 105 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32838)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0113","text":"To the second, it must be said that the order of justice is according to relation to the first cause, which is the rule of all justice. Therefore God can do nothing outside this order.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0114","text":"[I q. 105 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32839)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0115","text":"To the third, it must be said that God so implanted a determinate order in things that he nevertheless reserved to himself what he himself would at some time do otherwise for a cause. Hence, when he acts outside this order, he is not changed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0116","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0117","text":"[I q. 105 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32840)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0118","text":"We proceed thus to the seventh. It seems that not all things which God does outside the natural order of things are miracles. For the creation of the world, and also of souls, and the justification of the ungodly are done by God outside the natural order, since they are not done through the action of any natural cause. Yet these are not called miracles. Therefore not all things which God does outside the natural order of things are miracles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0119","text":"[I q. 105 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32841)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0120","text":"Further, a miracle is called something arduous and unusual, occurring beyond the power of nature and the expectation of the one who wonders. But some things occur outside the order of nature which nevertheless are not arduous, for they are in very small matters, as in the restoration of gems or the healing of the sick. Nor are they unusual, since they happen frequently, as when sick persons were placed in the streets so that they might be healed by Peter's shadow. Nor are they beyond the power of nature, as when some are healed of fevers. Nor are they beyond expectation, as we all hope for the resurrection of the dead, which nevertheless will occur outside the order of nature. Therefore not all things which occur outside the order of nature are miracles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0121","text":"[I q. 105 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32842)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0122","text":"Further, the name of a miracle is taken from wonder. But wonder concerns things manifest to the senses. Yet sometimes certain things occur outside the natural order in matters not manifest to the senses, as when the apostles were made knowing, neither by discovering nor by learning. Therefore not all things that occur outside the order of nature are miracles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0123","text":"[I q. 105 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32843)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0124","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in Book XXVI against Faustus, that *when God does something contrary to the course and customary order of nature known to us, these are called mighty works or wonders*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0125","text":"[I q. 105 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32844)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0126","text":"I answer that it must be said that the name of a miracle is taken from wonder. Now wonder arises when effects are manifest and the cause is hidden; as someone wonders when he sees an eclipse of the sun and does not know its cause, as is said at the beginning of the *Metaphysics*. But the cause of some apparent effect can be known to one person while remaining unknown to others. Hence something is wonderful to one person which is not wonderful to others; thus the peasant wonders at an eclipse of the sun, but not the astronomer. A miracle, however, is called, as it were, full of wonder, namely, that which has a cause simply and to all hidden. But this is God. Hence those things which are done by God outside causes known to us are called miracles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0127","text":"[I q. 105 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32845)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0128","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that creation and the justification of the ungodly, although they are done by God alone, are not, properly speaking, called miracles. For they are not naturally apt to be done through other causes, and thus they do not occur outside the order of nature, since these do not belong to the order of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0129","text":"[I q. 105 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32846)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0130","text":"To the second, it must be said that a miracle is called difficult, not on account of the dignity of the thing in which it occurs, but because it exceeds the capacity of nature. Likewise, it is called unusual not because it does not occur frequently, but because it is outside natural custom. Moreover, something is said to be above the capacity of nature not only on account of the substance of what is done, but also on account of the mode and order of doing. A miracle is also said to be beyond the hope of nature, but not beyond the hope of grace, which is from faith, through which we believe in the future resurrection.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0131","text":"[I q. 105 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32847)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0132","text":"To the third, it must be said that the knowledge of the apostles, although it was not manifest in itself, was nevertheless made manifest in its effects, from which it appeared wondrous.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0133","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0134","text":"[I q. 105 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32848)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0135","text":"We proceed thus to the eighth. It seems that one miracle is not greater than another. For Augustine says, in the letter to Volusianus, *in things wonderfully done, the whole reason for the deed is the power of the doer*. But all miracles are done by the same power, namely that of God. Therefore one is not greater than another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0136","text":"[I q. 105 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32849)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0137","text":"Further, the power of God is infinite. But the infinite exceeds every finite thing without proportion. Therefore it is no more wonderful that He should produce this effect than that one. Therefore one miracle is not greater than another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0138","text":"[I q. 105 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32850)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0139","text":"But on the contrary, the Lord says, speaking of miraculous works, John XIV: *the works that I do, he also shall do, and greater than these shall he do*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0140","text":"[I q. 105 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32851)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0141","text":"I answer that it must be said that nothing can be called a miracle by comparison with the divine power, because whatever is made, compared with the divine power, is least; according to that of Isaiah XL: *behold, the nations are as a drop from a bucket, and are accounted as the small dust of the balance*. But something is called a miracle by comparison with the capacity of nature, which it exceeds. Therefore, according as it exceeds the capacity of nature more, so it is called a greater miracle. Now something exceeds the capacity of nature in three ways. In one way, as to the substance of what is done, as when two bodies are together, or when the sun goes backward, or when a human body is glorified; which nature can in no way do. And these hold the highest grade among miracles. Secondly, something exceeds the capacity of nature, not as to what is done, but as to that in which it is done, as the resurrection of the dead, the giving of sight to the blind, and the like.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0142","text":"For nature can cause life, but not in a dead person, and it can bestow sight, but not on a blind person. And these hold the second place among miracles. In a third way, something exceeds the capacity of nature as to the mode and order of doing, as when someone is suddenly cured of a fever by divine power without treatment and the customary process of nature in such matters, and when the air is immediately condensed into rain by divine power without natural causes, as happened at the prayers of Samuel and Elijah. And things of this kind hold the lowest place among miracles. Yet each of these has diverse grades, according as they exceed the capacity of nature in diverse ways.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0143","text":"[I q. 105 a. 8 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32852)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0144","text":"And through this the solution to the objections is clear, which proceed on the part of the divine power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_97:P0144"]}],"source_digest":"0703672e18dff4d08004b2b9d3478f9f7c8367d757edb6f12338fa949fd19ce2","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 105"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 105","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98","title":"First Part · Question 106","order":107,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_98","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 106 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32853)\n\nDeinde considerandum est quomodo una creatura moveat aliam. Erit autem haec consideratio tripartita, ut primo consideremus quomodo Angeli moveant, qui sunt creaturae pure spirituales; secundo, quomodo corpora moveant; tertio, quomodo homines, qui sunt ex spirituali et corporali natura compositi. Circa primum tria consideranda occurrunt, primo, quomodo Angelus agat in Angelum; secundo, quomodo in creaturam corporalem; tertio, quomodo in homines. Circa primum, considerare oportet de illuminatione, et locutione Angelorum, et ordinatione eorum ad invicem, tam bonorum, quam malorum. Circa illuminationem quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum unus Angelus moveat intellectum alterius illuminando. Secundo, utrum unus moveat voluntatem alterius. Tertio, utrum inferior Angelus possit illuminare superiorem. Quarto, utrum superior Angelus illuminet inferiorem de omnibus quae cognoscit.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 106 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32854)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod unus Angelus non illuminet alium. Angeli enim eandem beatitudinem possident nunc, quam nos in futuro expectamus. Sed tunc unus homo non illuminabit alium; secundum illud Ierem. XXXI, *non docebit ultra vir proximum suum, et vir fratrem suum*. Ergo etiam neque nunc unus Angelus illuminat alium.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32855)\n\nPraeterea, triplex est lumen in Angelis, naturae, gratiae et gloriae. Sed Angelus illuminatur lumine naturae, a creante; lumine gratiae, a iustificante; lumine gloriae, a beatificante; quod totum Dei est. Ergo unus Angelus non illuminat alium.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32856)\n\nPraeterea, lumen est forma quaedam mentis. Sed mens rationalis a solo Deo formatur, nulla interposita creatura, ut Augustinus dicit in libro octoginta trium quaest. Ergo unus Angelus non illuminat mentem alterius.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32857)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Dionysius, VIII cap. Cael. Hier., quod *Angeli secundae hierarchiae purgantur et illuminantur et perficiuntur per Angelos primae hierarchiae*.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32858)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod unus Angelus illuminat alium. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod lumen, secundum quod ad intellectum pertinet, nihil est aliud quam quaedam manifestatio veritatis; secundum illud ad Ephes. V, *omne quod manifestatur, lumen est*. Unde illuminare nihil aliud est quam manifestationem cognitae veritatis alteri tradere; secundum quem modum apostolus dicit, ad Ephes. III, *mihi, omnium sanctorum minimo, data est gratia haec, illuminare omnes quae sit dispensatio sacramenti absconditi a saeculis in Deo*. Sic igitur unus Angelus dicitur illuminare alium, inquantum ei manifestat veritatem quam ipse cognoscit. Unde Dionysius dicit, VII cap. Cael. Hier., quod *theologi plane monstrant caelestium substantiarum ornatus a supremis mentibus doceri deificas scientias*. Cum autem ad intelligendum duo concurrant, ut supra diximus, scilicet virtus intellectiva, et similitudo rei intellectae; secundum haec duo unus Angelus alteri veritatem notam notificare potest. Primo quidem, fortificando virtutem intellectivam eius. Sicut enim virtus imperfectioris corporis confortatur ex situali propinquitate perfectioris corporis, ut minus calidum crescit in calore ex praesentia magis calidi; ita virtus intellectiva inferioris Angeli confortatur ex conversione superioris Angeli ad ipsum. Hoc enim facit in spiritualibus ordo conversionis, quod facit in corporalibus ordo localis propinquitatis. Secundo autem unus Angelus alteri manifestat veritatem, ex parte similitudinis intellectae. Superior enim Angelus notitiam veritatis accipit in universali quadam conceptione, ad quam capiendam inferioris Angeli intellectus non esset sufficiens, sed est ei connaturale ut magis particulariter veritatem accipiat. Superior ergo Angelus veritatem quam universaliter concipit, quodammodo distinguit, ut ab inferiori capi possit; et sic eam cognoscendam illi proponit. Sicut etiam apud nos, doctores, quod in summa capiunt, multipliciter distinguunt, providentes capacitati aliorum. Et hoc est quod Dionysius dicit, XV cap. Cael. Hier., *unaquaeque substantia intellectualis datam sibi a diviniore uniformem intelligentiam, provida virtute dividit et multiplicat, ad inferioris sursum ductricem analogiam*.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32859)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod omnes Angeli, tam superiores quam inferiores, immediate vident Dei essentiam; et quantum ad hoc, unus non docet alium. De hac enim doctrina propheta loquitur, unde dicit *non docebit vir fratrem suum, dicens, cognosce dominum. Omnes enim cognoscent me, a minimo eorum usque ad maximum*. Sed rationes divinorum operum, quae in Deo cognoscuntur sicut in causa, omnes quidem Deus in seipso cognoscit, quia seipsum comprehendit, aliorum vero Deum videntium tanto unusquisque in Deo plures rationes cognoscit, quanto eum perfectius videt. Unde superior Angelus plura in Deo de rationibus divinorum operum cognoscit quam inferior; et de his eum illuminat. Et hoc est quod dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *Angeli existentium illuminantur rationibus*.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32860)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod unus Angelus non illuminat alium tradendo ei lumen naturae vel gratiae vel gloriae; sed confortando lumen naturale ipsius, et manifestando ei veritatem de his quae pertinent ad statum naturae, gratiae et gloriae, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32861)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod rationalis mens formatur immediate a Deo, vel sicut imago ab exemplari, quia non est facta ad alterius imaginem quam Dei, vel sicut subiectum ab ultima forma completiva, quia semper mens creata reputatur informis, nisi ipsi primae veritati inhaereat. Aliae vero illuminationes, quae sunt ab homine vel Angelo, sunt quasi dispositiones ad ultimam formam.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 106 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32862)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod unus Angelus possit movere voluntatem alterius. Quia secundum Dionysium, sicut unus Angelus illuminat alium, ita purgat et perficit; ut patet ex auctoritate supra inducta. Sed purgatio et perfectio videntur pertinere ad voluntatem, nam purgatio videtur esse a sordibus culpae, quae pertinet ad voluntatem; perfectio autem videtur esse per consecutionem finis, qui est obiectum voluntatis. Ergo unus Angelus potest movere voluntatem alterius.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32863)\n\nPraeterea, sicut Dionysius dicit VII cap. Cael. Hier., *nomina Angelorum designant eorum proprietates*. Seraphim autem incendentes dicuntur, aut calefacientes, quod est per amorem, qui ad voluntatem pertinet. Unus ergo Angelus movet voluntatem alterius.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32864)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod appetitus superior movet appetitum inferiorem. Sed sicut intellectus Angeli superioris superior est, ita etiam appetitus. Ergo videtur quod superior Angelus possit immutare voluntatem alterius.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32865)\n\nSed contra, eius est immutare voluntatem, cuius est iustificare, cum iustitia sit rectitudo voluntatis. Sed solus Deus est qui iustificat. Ergo unus Angelus non potest mutare voluntatem alterius.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32866)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, voluntas immutatur dupliciter, uno modo, ex parte obiecti; alio modo ex parte ipsius potentiae. Ex parte quidem obiecti, movet voluntatem et ipsum bonum quod est voluntatis obiectum, sicut appetibile movet appetitum; et ille qui demonstrat obiectum, puta qui demonstrat aliquid esse bonum. Sed sicut supra dictum est, alia quidem bona aliqualiter inclinant voluntatem; sed nihil sufficienter movet voluntatem, nisi bonum universale, quod est Deus. Et hoc bonum solus ipse ostendit, ut per essentiam videatur a beatis, qui dicenti Moysi, *ostende mihi gloriam tuam*, respondit, *ego ostendam tibi omne bonum*, ut habetur Exod. XXXIII. Angelus ergo non sufficienter movet voluntatem, neque ut obiectum, neque ut ostendens obiectum. Sed inclinat eam, ut amabile quoddam, et ut manifestans aliqua bona creata ordinata in Dei bonitatem. Et per hoc inclinare potest ad amorem creaturae vel Dei, per modum suadentis. Ex parte vero ipsius potentiae, voluntas nullo modo potest moveri nisi a Deo. Operatio enim voluntatis est inclinatio quaedam volentis in volitum. Hanc autem inclinationem solus ille immutare potest, qui virtutem volendi creaturae contulit, sicut et naturalem inclinationem solum illud agens potest mutare, quod potest dare virtutem quam consequitur inclinatio naturalis. Solus autem Deus est qui potentiam volendi tribuit creaturae, quia ipse solus est auctor intellectualis naturae. Unde unus Angelus voluntatem alterius movere non potest.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32867)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod secundum modum illuminationis, est accipienda et purgatio et perfectio. Et quia Deus illuminat immutando intellectum et voluntatem, purgat a defectibus intellectus et voluntatis, et perficit in finem intellectus et voluntatis. Angeli autem illuminatio refertur ad intellectum, ut dictum est. Et ideo etiam purgatio Angeli intelligitur a defectu intellectus, qui est nescientia; perfectio autem est consummatio in finem intellectus, qui est veritas cognita. Et hoc est quod dicit Dionysius, VI cap. Eccl. Hier., quod *in caelesti hierarchia purgatio est in subiectis essentiis tanquam ignotorum illuminatio in perfectiorem eas scientiam ducens*. Sicut si dicamus visum corporalem purgari, inquantum removentur tenebrae; illuminari vero, inquantum perfunditur lumine; perfici vero, secundum quod perducitur ad cognitionem colorati.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32868)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod unus Angelus potest inducere alium ad amorem Dei per modum persuadentis, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32869)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod philosophus loquitur de appetitu inferiori sensitivo, qui potest moveri a superiori intellectivo, quia pertinet ad eandem naturam animae, et quia inferior appetitus est virtus in organo corporali. Quod in Angelis locum non habet.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 106 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32870)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus inferior superiorem illuminare possit. Ecclesiastica enim hierarchia derivata est a caelesti, et eam repraesentat, unde et superna Ierusalem dicitur mater nostra, Gal. IV. Sed in Ecclesia etiam superiores illuminantur ab inferioribus et docentur; secundum illud apostoli, I ad Cor. XIV, *potestis omnes per singulos prophetare, ut omnes discant, et omnes exhortentur*. Ergo et in caelesti hierarchia superiores ab inferioribus possunt illuminari.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32871)\n\nPraeterea, sicut ordo corporalium substantiarum dependet ex Dei voluntate, ita et ordo substantiarum spiritualium. Sed sicut dictum est, Deus quandoque praeter ordinem substantiarum corporalium operatur. Ergo quandoque etiam operatur praeter ordinem spiritualium substantiarum, illuminando inferiores non per medios superiores. Sic ergo inferiores illuminati a Deo, possunt superiores illuminare.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32872)\n\nPraeterea, unus Angelus alium illuminat, ad quem se convertit, ut supra dictum est. Sed cum ista conversio sit voluntaria, potest supremus Angelus ad infimum se convertere, mediis praetermissis. Ergo potest eum immediate illuminare, et ita potest illuminare superiores.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32873)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius dicit *hanc legem esse divinitatis immobiliter firmatam, ut inferiora reducantur in Deum per superiora*.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32874)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod inferiores Angeli nunquam illuminant superiores, sed semper ab eis illuminantur. Cuius ratio est quia, sicut supra dictum est, ordo continetur sub ordine, sicut causa continetur sub causa. Unde sicut ordinatur causa ad causam, ita ordo ad ordinem. Et ideo non est inconveniens, si aliquando aliquid fiat praeter ordinem inferioris causae, ad ordinandum in superiorem causam, sicut in rebus humanis praetermittitur mandatum praesidis, ut obediatur principi. Et ita contingit ut praeter ordinem naturae corporalis, aliquid Deus miraculose operetur, ad ordinandum homines in eius cognitionem. Sed praetermissio ordinis qui debetur spiritualibus substantiis, in nullo pertinet ad ordinationem hominum in Deum, cum operationes Angelorum non sint nobis manifestae, sicut operationes visibilium corporum. Et ideo ordo qui convenit spiritualibus substantiis, nunquam a Deo praetermittitur, quin semper inferiora moveantur per superiora, et non e converso.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32875)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ecclesiastica hierarchia imitatur caelestem aliqualiter, sed non perfecte consequitur eius similitudinem. In caelesti enim hierarchia tota ratio ordinis est ex propinquitate ad Deum. Et ideo illi qui sunt Deo propinquiores, sunt et gradu sublimiores, et scientia clariores, et propter hoc superiores nunquam ab inferioribus illuminantur. Sed in ecclesiastica hierarchia, interdum qui sunt Deo per sanctitatem propinquiores, sunt gradu infimi, et scientia non eminentes, et quidam in uno etiam secundum scientiam eminent, et in alio deficiunt. Et propter hoc superiores ab inferioribus doceri possunt.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32876)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod non est similis ratio de hoc quod Deus agat praeter ordinem naturae corporalis, et naturae spiritualis, ut dictum est. Unde ratio non sequitur.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32877)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Angelus voluntate convertitur ad alium Angelum illuminandum; sed voluntas Angeli semper regulatur lege divina, quae ordinem in Angelis instituit.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 106 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32878)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus superior non illuminet inferiorem de omnibus quae ipse novit. Dicit enim Dionysius, XII cap. Cael. Hier., quod Angeli superiores habent scientiam magis universalem, inferiores vero magis particularem et subiectam. Sed plura continentur sub scientia universali quam sub particulari. Ergo non omnia quae sciunt superiores Angeli, cognoscunt inferiores per superiorum illuminationem.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32879)\n\nPraeterea, Magister dicit, in XI distinctione II Sent., quod superiores Angeli cognoverunt a saeculis mysterium incarnationis, inferioribus vero ignotum fuit usquequo completum est. Quod videtur per hoc quod, quibusdam Angelis quaerentibus, quis est iste rex gloriae? Quasi ignorantibus, alii respondent, quasi scientes, dominus virtutum ipse est rex gloriae, ut Dionysius exponit VII cap. Cael. Hier. Hoc autem non esset, si superiores Angeli illuminarent inferiores de omnibus quae ipsi cognoscunt. Non ergo eos illuminant de omnibus sibi notis.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32880)\n\nPraeterea, si omnia superiores Angeli inferioribus annuntiant quae cognoscunt, nihil inferioribus ignotum remanet, quod superiores cognoscant. Non ergo de cetero superiores poterunt illuminare inferiores. Quod videtur inconveniens. Non ergo superiores de omnibus inferiores illuminant.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32881)\n\nSed contra est quod Gregorius dicit, quod *in illa caelesti patria, licet quaedam data sint excellenter, nihil tamen possidetur singulariter*. Et Dionysius dicit, XV cap. Cael. Hier., quod *unaquaeque caelestis essentia intelligentiam sibi a superiori datam, inferiori communicat*; ut patet ex auctoritate supra inducta.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32882)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod omnes creaturae ex divina bonitate participant ut bonum quod habent, in alia diffundant, nam de ratione boni est quod se aliis communicet. Et inde est etiam quod agentia corporalia similitudinem suam aliis tradunt, quantum possibile est. Quanto igitur aliqua agentia magis in participatione divinae bonitatis constituuntur, tanto magis perfectiones suas nituntur in alios transfundere, quantum possibile est. Unde beatus Petrus monet eos qui divinam bonitatem per gratiam participant, dicens, I Petr. IV, *unusquisque, sicut accepit gratiam, in alterutrum illam administrantes, sicut boni dispensatores multiformis gratiae Dei*. Multo igitur magis sancti Angeli, qui sunt in plenissima participatione divinae bonitatis, quidquid a Deo percipiunt, subiectis impartiuntur. Non tamen recipitur ab inferioribus ita excellenter sicut est in superioribus. Et ideo superiores semper remanent in altiori ordine, et perfectiorem scientiam habentes. Sicut unam et eandem rem plenius intelligit magister, quam discipulus qui ab eo addiscit.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32883)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod superiorum Angelorum scientia dicitur esse universalior, quantum ad eminentiorem modum intelligendi.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32884)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod verbum Magistri non sic est intelligendum, quod inferiores Angeli penitus ignoraverint mysterium incarnationis; sed quia non ita plene cognoverunt sicut superiores, et in eius cognitione postmodum profecerunt, dum illud mysterium impleretur.\n\n[I q. 106 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32885)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, usque ad diem iudicii, semper nova aliqua supremis Angelis revelantur divinitus de his quae pertinent ad dispositionem mundi, et praecipue ad salutem electorum. Unde semper remanet unde superiores Angeli inferiores illuminent.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0002","text":"[I q. 106 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32853)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est quomodo una creatura moveat aliam. Erit autem haec consideratio tripartita, ut primo consideremus quomodo Angeli moveant, qui sunt creaturae pure spirituales; secundo, quomodo corpora moveant; tertio, quomodo homines, qui sunt ex spirituali et corporali natura compositi. Circa primum tria consideranda occurrunt, primo, quomodo Angelus agat in Angelum; secundo, quomodo in creaturam corporalem; tertio, quomodo in homines. Circa primum, considerare oportet de illuminatione, et locutione Angelorum, et ordinatione eorum ad invicem, tam bonorum, quam malorum. Circa illuminationem quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum unus Angelus moveat intellectum alterius illuminando. Secundo, utrum unus moveat voluntatem alterius. Tertio, utrum inferior Angelus possit illuminare superiorem. Quarto, utrum superior Angelus illuminet inferiorem de omnibus quae cognoscit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0005","text":"[I q. 106 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32854)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod unus Angelus non illuminet alium. Angeli enim eandem beatitudinem possident nunc, quam nos in futuro expectamus. Sed tunc unus homo non illuminabit alium; secundum illud Ierem. XXXI, *non docebit ultra vir proximum suum, et vir fratrem suum*. Ergo etiam neque nunc unus Angelus illuminat alium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0007","text":"[I q. 106 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32855)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0008","text":"Praeterea, triplex est lumen in Angelis, naturae, gratiae et gloriae. Sed Angelus illuminatur lumine naturae, a creante; lumine gratiae, a iustificante; lumine gloriae, a beatificante; quod totum Dei est. Ergo unus Angelus non illuminat alium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0009","text":"[I q. 106 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32856)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0010","text":"Praeterea, lumen est forma quaedam mentis. Sed mens rationalis a solo Deo formatur, nulla interposita creatura, ut Augustinus dicit in libro octoginta trium quaest. Ergo unus Angelus non illuminat mentem alterius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0011","text":"[I q. 106 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32857)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Dionysius, VIII cap. Cael. Hier., quod *Angeli secundae hierarchiae purgantur et illuminantur et perficiuntur per Angelos primae hierarchiae*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0013","text":"[I q. 106 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32858)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod unus Angelus illuminat alium. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod lumen, secundum quod ad intellectum pertinet, nihil est aliud quam quaedam manifestatio veritatis; secundum illud ad Ephes. V, *omne quod manifestatur, lumen est*. Unde illuminare nihil aliud est quam manifestationem cognitae veritatis alteri tradere; secundum quem modum apostolus dicit, ad Ephes. III, *mihi, omnium sanctorum minimo, data est gratia haec, illuminare omnes quae sit dispensatio sacramenti absconditi a saeculis in Deo*. Sic igitur unus Angelus dicitur illuminare alium, inquantum ei manifestat veritatem quam ipse cognoscit. Unde Dionysius dicit, VII cap. Cael. Hier., quod *theologi plane monstrant caelestium substantiarum ornatus a supremis mentibus doceri deificas scientias*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0015","text":"Cum autem ad intelligendum duo concurrant, ut supra diximus, scilicet virtus intellectiva, et similitudo rei intellectae; secundum haec duo unus Angelus alteri veritatem notam notificare potest. Primo quidem, fortificando virtutem intellectivam eius. Sicut enim virtus imperfectioris corporis confortatur ex situali propinquitate perfectioris corporis, ut minus calidum crescit in calore ex praesentia magis calidi; ita virtus intellectiva inferioris Angeli confortatur ex conversione superioris Angeli ad ipsum. Hoc enim facit in spiritualibus ordo conversionis, quod facit in corporalibus ordo localis propinquitatis. Secundo autem unus Angelus alteri manifestat veritatem, ex parte similitudinis intellectae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0016","text":"Superior enim Angelus notitiam veritatis accipit in universali quadam conceptione, ad quam capiendam inferioris Angeli intellectus non esset sufficiens, sed est ei connaturale ut magis particulariter veritatem accipiat. Superior ergo Angelus veritatem quam universaliter concipit, quodammodo distinguit, ut ab inferiori capi possit; et sic eam cognoscendam illi proponit. Sicut etiam apud nos, doctores, quod in summa capiunt, multipliciter distinguunt, providentes capacitati aliorum. Et hoc est quod Dionysius dicit, XV cap. Cael. Hier., *unaquaeque substantia intellectualis datam sibi a diviniore uniformem intelligentiam, provida virtute dividit et multiplicat, ad inferioris sursum ductricem analogiam*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0017","text":"[I q. 106 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32859)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0018","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod omnes Angeli, tam superiores quam inferiores, immediate vident Dei essentiam; et quantum ad hoc, unus non docet alium. De hac enim doctrina propheta loquitur, unde dicit *non docebit vir fratrem suum, dicens, cognosce dominum. Omnes enim cognoscent me, a minimo eorum usque ad maximum*. Sed rationes divinorum operum, quae in Deo cognoscuntur sicut in causa, omnes quidem Deus in seipso cognoscit, quia seipsum comprehendit, aliorum vero Deum videntium tanto unusquisque in Deo plures rationes cognoscit, quanto eum perfectius videt. Unde superior Angelus plura in Deo de rationibus divinorum operum cognoscit quam inferior; et de his eum illuminat. Et hoc est quod dicit Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *Angeli existentium illuminantur rationibus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0019","text":"[I q. 106 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32860)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod unus Angelus non illuminat alium tradendo ei lumen naturae vel gratiae vel gloriae; sed confortando lumen naturale ipsius, et manifestando ei veritatem de his quae pertinent ad statum naturae, gratiae et gloriae, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0021","text":"[I q. 106 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32861)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod rationalis mens formatur immediate a Deo, vel sicut imago ab exemplari, quia non est facta ad alterius imaginem quam Dei, vel sicut subiectum ab ultima forma completiva, quia semper mens creata reputatur informis, nisi ipsi primae veritati inhaereat. Aliae vero illuminationes, quae sunt ab homine vel Angelo, sunt quasi dispositiones ad ultimam formam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0023","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0024","text":"[I q. 106 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32862)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0025","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod unus Angelus possit movere voluntatem alterius. Quia secundum Dionysium, sicut unus Angelus illuminat alium, ita purgat et perficit; ut patet ex auctoritate supra inducta. Sed purgatio et perfectio videntur pertinere ad voluntatem, nam purgatio videtur esse a sordibus culpae, quae pertinet ad voluntatem; perfectio autem videtur esse per consecutionem finis, qui est obiectum voluntatis. Ergo unus Angelus potest movere voluntatem alterius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0026","text":"[I q. 106 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32863)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0027","text":"Praeterea, sicut Dionysius dicit VII cap. Cael. Hier., *nomina Angelorum designant eorum proprietates*. Seraphim autem incendentes dicuntur, aut calefacientes, quod est per amorem, qui ad voluntatem pertinet. Unus ergo Angelus movet voluntatem alterius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0028","text":"[I q. 106 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32864)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0029","text":"Praeterea, philosophus dicit, in III de anima, quod appetitus superior movet appetitum inferiorem. Sed sicut intellectus Angeli superioris superior est, ita etiam appetitus. Ergo videtur quod superior Angelus possit immutare voluntatem alterius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0030","text":"[I q. 106 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32865)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0031","text":"Sed contra, eius est immutare voluntatem, cuius est iustificare, cum iustitia sit rectitudo voluntatis. Sed solus Deus est qui iustificat. Ergo unus Angelus non potest mutare voluntatem alterius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0032","text":"[I q. 106 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32866)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0033","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, voluntas immutatur dupliciter, uno modo, ex parte obiecti; alio modo ex parte ipsius potentiae. Ex parte quidem obiecti, movet voluntatem et ipsum bonum quod est voluntatis obiectum, sicut appetibile movet appetitum; et ille qui demonstrat obiectum, puta qui demonstrat aliquid esse bonum. Sed sicut supra dictum est, alia quidem bona aliqualiter inclinant voluntatem; sed nihil sufficienter movet voluntatem, nisi bonum universale, quod est Deus. Et hoc bonum solus ipse ostendit, ut per essentiam videatur a beatis, qui dicenti Moysi, *ostende mihi gloriam tuam*, respondit, *ego ostendam tibi omne bonum*, ut habetur Exod. XXXIII. Angelus ergo non sufficienter movet voluntatem, neque ut obiectum, neque ut ostendens obiectum. Sed inclinat eam, ut amabile quoddam, et ut manifestans aliqua bona creata ordinata in Dei bonitatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0034","text":"Et per hoc inclinare potest ad amorem creaturae vel Dei, per modum suadentis. Ex parte vero ipsius potentiae, voluntas nullo modo potest moveri nisi a Deo. Operatio enim voluntatis est inclinatio quaedam volentis in volitum. Hanc autem inclinationem solus ille immutare potest, qui virtutem volendi creaturae contulit, sicut et naturalem inclinationem solum illud agens potest mutare, quod potest dare virtutem quam consequitur inclinatio naturalis. Solus autem Deus est qui potentiam volendi tribuit creaturae, quia ipse solus est auctor intellectualis naturae. Unde unus Angelus voluntatem alterius movere non potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0035","text":"[I q. 106 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32867)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0036","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod secundum modum illuminationis, est accipienda et purgatio et perfectio. Et quia Deus illuminat immutando intellectum et voluntatem, purgat a defectibus intellectus et voluntatis, et perficit in finem intellectus et voluntatis. Angeli autem illuminatio refertur ad intellectum, ut dictum est. Et ideo etiam purgatio Angeli intelligitur a defectu intellectus, qui est nescientia; perfectio autem est consummatio in finem intellectus, qui est veritas cognita. Et hoc est quod dicit Dionysius, VI cap. Eccl. Hier., quod *in caelesti hierarchia purgatio est in subiectis essentiis tanquam ignotorum illuminatio in perfectiorem eas scientiam ducens*. Sicut si dicamus visum corporalem purgari, inquantum removentur tenebrae; illuminari vero, inquantum perfunditur lumine; perfici vero, secundum quod perducitur ad cognitionem colorati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0037","text":"[I q. 106 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32868)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0038","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod unus Angelus potest inducere alium ad amorem Dei per modum persuadentis, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0039","text":"[I q. 106 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32869)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0040","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod philosophus loquitur de appetitu inferiori sensitivo, qui potest moveri a superiori intellectivo, quia pertinet ad eandem naturam animae, et quia inferior appetitus est virtus in organo corporali. Quod in Angelis locum non habet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0041","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0042","text":"[I q. 106 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32870)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0043","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus inferior superiorem illuminare possit. Ecclesiastica enim hierarchia derivata est a caelesti, et eam repraesentat, unde et superna Ierusalem dicitur mater nostra, Gal. IV. Sed in Ecclesia etiam superiores illuminantur ab inferioribus et docentur; secundum illud apostoli, I ad Cor. XIV, *potestis omnes per singulos prophetare, ut omnes discant, et omnes exhortentur*. Ergo et in caelesti hierarchia superiores ab inferioribus possunt illuminari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0044","text":"[I q. 106 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32871)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0045","text":"Praeterea, sicut ordo corporalium substantiarum dependet ex Dei voluntate, ita et ordo substantiarum spiritualium. Sed sicut dictum est, Deus quandoque praeter ordinem substantiarum corporalium operatur. Ergo quandoque etiam operatur praeter ordinem spiritualium substantiarum, illuminando inferiores non per medios superiores. Sic ergo inferiores illuminati a Deo, possunt superiores illuminare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0046","text":"[I q. 106 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32872)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0047","text":"Praeterea, unus Angelus alium illuminat, ad quem se convertit, ut supra dictum est. Sed cum ista conversio sit voluntaria, potest supremus Angelus ad infimum se convertere, mediis praetermissis. Ergo potest eum immediate illuminare, et ita potest illuminare superiores.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0048","text":"[I q. 106 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32873)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0049","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius dicit *hanc legem esse divinitatis immobiliter firmatam, ut inferiora reducantur in Deum per superiora*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0050","text":"[I q. 106 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32874)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0051","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod inferiores Angeli nunquam illuminant superiores, sed semper ab eis illuminantur. Cuius ratio est quia, sicut supra dictum est, ordo continetur sub ordine, sicut causa continetur sub causa. Unde sicut ordinatur causa ad causam, ita ordo ad ordinem. Et ideo non est inconveniens, si aliquando aliquid fiat praeter ordinem inferioris causae, ad ordinandum in superiorem causam, sicut in rebus humanis praetermittitur mandatum praesidis, ut obediatur principi. Et ita contingit ut praeter ordinem naturae corporalis, aliquid Deus miraculose operetur, ad ordinandum homines in eius cognitionem. Sed praetermissio ordinis qui debetur spiritualibus substantiis, in nullo pertinet ad ordinationem hominum in Deum, cum operationes Angelorum non sint nobis manifestae, sicut operationes visibilium corporum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0052","text":"Et ideo ordo qui convenit spiritualibus substantiis, nunquam a Deo praetermittitur, quin semper inferiora moveantur per superiora, et non e converso.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0053","text":"[I q. 106 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32875)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0054","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ecclesiastica hierarchia imitatur caelestem aliqualiter, sed non perfecte consequitur eius similitudinem. In caelesti enim hierarchia tota ratio ordinis est ex propinquitate ad Deum. Et ideo illi qui sunt Deo propinquiores, sunt et gradu sublimiores, et scientia clariores, et propter hoc superiores nunquam ab inferioribus illuminantur. Sed in ecclesiastica hierarchia, interdum qui sunt Deo per sanctitatem propinquiores, sunt gradu infimi, et scientia non eminentes, et quidam in uno etiam secundum scientiam eminent, et in alio deficiunt. Et propter hoc superiores ab inferioribus doceri possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0055","text":"[I q. 106 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32876)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0056","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod non est similis ratio de hoc quod Deus agat praeter ordinem naturae corporalis, et naturae spiritualis, ut dictum est. Unde ratio non sequitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0057","text":"[I q. 106 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32877)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0058","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Angelus voluntate convertitur ad alium Angelum illuminandum; sed voluntas Angeli semper regulatur lege divina, quae ordinem in Angelis instituit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0059","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0060","text":"[I q. 106 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32878)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0061","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus superior non illuminet inferiorem de omnibus quae ipse novit. Dicit enim Dionysius, XII cap. Cael. Hier., quod Angeli superiores habent scientiam magis universalem, inferiores vero magis particularem et subiectam. Sed plura continentur sub scientia universali quam sub particulari. Ergo non omnia quae sciunt superiores Angeli, cognoscunt inferiores per superiorum illuminationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0062","text":"[I q. 106 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32879)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0063","text":"Praeterea, Magister dicit, in XI distinctione II Sent., quod superiores Angeli cognoverunt a saeculis mysterium incarnationis, inferioribus vero ignotum fuit usquequo completum est. Quod videtur per hoc quod, quibusdam Angelis quaerentibus, quis est iste rex gloriae? Quasi ignorantibus, alii respondent, quasi scientes, dominus virtutum ipse est rex gloriae, ut Dionysius exponit VII cap. Cael. Hier. Hoc autem non esset, si superiores Angeli illuminarent inferiores de omnibus quae ipsi cognoscunt. Non ergo eos illuminant de omnibus sibi notis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0064","text":"[I q. 106 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32880)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0065","text":"Praeterea, si omnia superiores Angeli inferioribus annuntiant quae cognoscunt, nihil inferioribus ignotum remanet, quod superiores cognoscant. Non ergo de cetero superiores poterunt illuminare inferiores. Quod videtur inconveniens. Non ergo superiores de omnibus inferiores illuminant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0066","text":"[I q. 106 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32881)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0067","text":"Sed contra est quod Gregorius dicit, quod *in illa caelesti patria, licet quaedam data sint excellenter, nihil tamen possidetur singulariter*. Et Dionysius dicit, XV cap. Cael. Hier., quod *unaquaeque caelestis essentia intelligentiam sibi a superiori datam, inferiori communicat*; ut patet ex auctoritate supra inducta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0068","text":"[I q. 106 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32882)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0069","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod omnes creaturae ex divina bonitate participant ut bonum quod habent, in alia diffundant, nam de ratione boni est quod se aliis communicet. Et inde est etiam quod agentia corporalia similitudinem suam aliis tradunt, quantum possibile est. Quanto igitur aliqua agentia magis in participatione divinae bonitatis constituuntur, tanto magis perfectiones suas nituntur in alios transfundere, quantum possibile est. Unde beatus Petrus monet eos qui divinam bonitatem per gratiam participant, dicens, I Petr. IV, *unusquisque, sicut accepit gratiam, in alterutrum illam administrantes, sicut boni dispensatores multiformis gratiae Dei*. Multo igitur magis sancti Angeli, qui sunt in plenissima participatione divinae bonitatis, quidquid a Deo percipiunt, subiectis impartiuntur. Non tamen recipitur ab inferioribus ita excellenter sicut est in superioribus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0070","text":"Et ideo superiores semper remanent in altiori ordine, et perfectiorem scientiam habentes. Sicut unam et eandem rem plenius intelligit magister, quam discipulus qui ab eo addiscit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0071","text":"[I q. 106 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32883)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0072","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod superiorum Angelorum scientia dicitur esse universalior, quantum ad eminentiorem modum intelligendi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0073","text":"[I q. 106 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32884)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0074","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod verbum Magistri non sic est intelligendum, quod inferiores Angeli penitus ignoraverint mysterium incarnationis; sed quia non ita plene cognoverunt sicut superiores, et in eius cognitione postmodum profecerunt, dum illud mysterium impleretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0075","text":"[I q. 106 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32885)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0076","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, usque ad diem iudicii, semper nova aliqua supremis Angelis revelantur divinitus de his quae pertinent ad dispositionem mundi, et praecipue ad salutem electorum. Unde semper remanet unde superiores Angeli inferiores illuminent.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"e162f1caedd4a0d28836db3fd1d5b476b18b84073ad4e6dc4e5c3967ac351a39","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T153922038141Z_ee674fae","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 106","source_sha256":"e162f1caedd4a0d28836db3fd1d5b476b18b84073ad4e6dc4e5c3967ac351a39","sha256":"2e9ecb4aabaed2c62161b5826c4643ec5a803aef50f12cf43eff52999abc1b7e","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:39:22.038201+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0002","text":"[I q. 106 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32853)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider how one creature moves another. This consideration will be threefold: first, that we may consider how angels move, since they are purely spiritual creatures; second, how they move bodies; third, how they move human beings, who are composed of spiritual and corporeal nature. Concerning the first, three points present themselves for consideration: first, how an angel acts upon an angel; second, how upon a corporeal creature; third, how upon human beings. Concerning the first, we must consider the illumination and speech of angels, and their ordering toward one another, both of the good and of the evil. Concerning illumination, four questions are asked. First, whether one angel moves another's intellect by illuminating it. Second, whether one moves another's will. Third, whether an inferior angel can illuminate a superior one. Fourth, whether a superior angel illuminates an inferior one concerning all that it knows.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0005","text":"[I q. 106 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32854)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that one angel does not illuminate another. For angels now possess the same beatitude that we await in the future. But then one human being will not illuminate another, according to Jeremiah 31, *a man shall no longer teach his neighbor, and a man his brother*. Therefore neither does one angel now illuminate another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0007","text":"[I q. 106 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32855)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0008","text":"Further, there is a threefold light in angels: that of nature, grace, and glory. But an angel is illuminated with the light of nature by the Creator; with the light of grace by the Justifier; and with the light of glory by the Beatifier; all of which belongs to God. Therefore one angel does not illuminate another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0009","text":"[I q. 106 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32856)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0010","text":"Further, light is a certain form of the mind. But the rational mind is formed by God alone, with no creature intervening, as Augustine says in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*. Therefore one angel does not illuminate another's mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0011","text":"[I q. 106 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32857)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, Dionysius says, in chapter 8 of *The Celestial Hierarchy*, that *the angels of the second hierarchy are purified and illuminated and perfected through the angels of the first hierarchy*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0013","text":"[I q. 106 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32858)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0014","text":"I answer that one angel illuminates another. For the evidence of this, it must be considered that light, insofar as it pertains to the intellect, is nothing other than a certain manifestation of truth; according to Ephesians 5, *everything that is manifested is light*. Hence to illuminate is nothing other than to impart to another the manifestation of a truth known; in which sense the Apostle says, in Ephesians 3, *to me, the least of all the saints, this grace was given, to illuminate all as to what is the dispensation of the mystery hidden from the ages in God*. Thus, therefore, one angel is said to illuminate another insofar as it manifests to it the truth which it itself knows. Hence Dionysius says, in chapter 7 of *The Celestial Hierarchy*, that *the theologians plainly show that the adornments of the heavenly substances are taught divine sciences by the supreme minds*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0015","text":"But since, as we have said above, two things concur for understanding, namely, the intellectual power and the likeness of the thing understood, in respect of these two one angel can make known to another a truth known. First indeed, by strengthening its intellectual power. For just as the power of a more imperfect body is strengthened by the local proximity of a more perfect body—so that what is less hot increases in heat through the presence of what is hotter—so the intellectual power of an inferior angel is strengthened by the turning of a superior angel toward it. For the order of turning has in spiritual things the effect which the order of local proximity has in corporeal things. Second, one angel manifests truth to another on the part of the likeness understood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0016","text":"For a superior angel receives knowledge of truth in a certain universal conception, for grasping which the intellect of an inferior angel would not be sufficient; rather, it is connatural to it to receive truth more particularly. Therefore the superior angel in some way distinguishes the truth which it conceives universally, so that it may be grasped by the inferior angel; and thus it presents it to that angel to be known. Just as among us also teachers, what they grasp in summary, distinguish in many ways, making provision for the capacity of others. And this is what Dionysius says, in chapter 15 of *The Celestial Hierarchy*: *each intellectual substance divides and multiplies, by provident power, the uniform understanding given to it by one more divine, according to an upward-leading analogy of the inferior*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0017","text":"[I q. 106 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32859)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0018","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that all angels, both superior and inferior, see God's essence immediately; and in this respect one does not teach another. For the prophet speaks of this teaching, whence he says, *a man shall not teach his brother, saying, Know the Lord. For all shall know me, from the least of them even to the greatest*. But the intelligible grounds of divine works, which are known in God as in their cause, God indeed knows all in Himself, because He comprehends Himself; whereas of those who see God, each knows in God more such intelligible grounds the more perfectly he sees Him. Hence the superior angel knows in God more concerning the intelligible grounds of divine works than the inferior one, and illuminates it concerning these. And this is what Dionysius says, in chapter 4 of *On the Divine Names*, that *angels are illuminated by the intelligible grounds of existing things*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0019","text":"[I q. 106 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32860)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0020","text":"To the second, it must be said that one angel does not illuminate another by imparting to it the light of nature, grace, or glory, but by strengthening its natural light and manifesting to it the truth concerning those things which pertain to the state of nature, grace, and glory, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0021","text":"[I q. 106 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32861)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0022","text":"To the third, it must be said that the rational mind is formed immediately by God, either as an image by its exemplar, because it is not made in the image of anything other than God; or as a subject by its ultimate completing form, because a created mind is always considered formless unless it adheres to the First Truth itself. But other illuminations, which are from a human being or an angel, are as it were dispositions toward the ultimate form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0023","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0024","text":"[I q. 106 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32862)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0025","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that one Angel can move the will of another. For according to Dionysius, just as one Angel illuminates another, so too he purifies and perfects him; as is clear from the authority cited above. But purification and perfection seem to pertain to the will: for purification seems to be from the stains of fault, which pertains to the will; whereas perfection seems to be through attainment of the end, which is the object of the will. Therefore one Angel can move the will of another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0026","text":"[I q. 106 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32863)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0027","text":"Moreover, as Dionysius says in chapter VII of the *Celestial Hierarchy*, *the names of the Angels designate their properties*. Now Seraphim are said to be kindling, or warming, which is through love, and this pertains to the will. Therefore one Angel moves the will of another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0028","text":"[I q. 106 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32864)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0029","text":"Moreover, the Philosopher says, in book III of *On the Soul*, that the higher appetite moves the lower appetite. But just as the intellect of a higher Angel is higher, so also is his appetite. Therefore it seems that a higher Angel can change the will of another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0030","text":"[I q. 106 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32865)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0031","text":"On the contrary, it belongs to him to change the will to whom it belongs to justify, since justice is the rectitude of the will. But God alone is he who justifies. Therefore one Angel cannot change the will of another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0032","text":"[I q. 106 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32866)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0033","text":"I answer that, as was said above, the will is changed in two ways: in one way, on the part of the object; in another way, on the part of the power itself. On the part of the object, both the good itself, which is the object of the will, moves the will, just as the appetible moves the appetite; and also he who displays the object, for example, he who displays that something is good. But, as was said above, other goods do indeed incline the will in some way; but nothing sufficiently moves the will except the universal good, which is God. And He alone displays this good, so that it is seen through essence by the blessed, to whom, when Moses said, *show me thy glory*, He replied, *I will show thee all good*, as is found in Exodus XXXIII. Therefore an Angel does not sufficiently move the will, either as object or as displaying the object. But he inclines it, as something lovable, and as manifesting certain created goods ordered to God's goodness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0034","text":"And through this he can incline it to love of a creature or of God, in the manner of one persuading. But on the part of the power itself, the will can in no way be moved except by God. For the operation of the will is a certain inclination of the one willing toward the thing willed. But only he can change this inclination who bestowed upon the creature the power of willing, just as only that agent can change a natural inclination which can give the power upon which the natural inclination follows. Now God alone bestows the power of willing upon the creature, because He alone is the author of intellectual nature. Hence one Angel cannot move the will of another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0035","text":"[I q. 106 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32867)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0036","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that purification and perfection are to be understood according to the mode of illumination. And because God illuminates by changing the intellect and the will, He purifies from the defects of intellect and will, and perfects unto the end of intellect and will. But the illumination of an Angel is referred to the intellect, as was said. And therefore the purification of an Angel is also understood as from the defect of intellect, which is ignorance; whereas perfection is consummation in the end of intellect, which is known truth. And this is what Dionysius says in chapter VI of the *Ecclesiastical Hierarchy*, that *in the celestial hierarchy purification is in the subject essences, as illumination of things unknown leading them into more perfect knowledge*. It is as if we were to say that bodily sight is purified insofar as darkness is removed; illuminated, indeed, insofar as it is suffused with light; and perfected insofar as it is brought to knowledge of what is colored.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0037","text":"[I q. 106 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32868)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0038","text":"To the second it must be said that one Angel can induce another to the love of God in the manner of one persuading, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0039","text":"[I q. 106 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32869)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0040","text":"To the third it must be said that the Philosopher speaks of the lower sensitive appetite, which can be moved by the higher intellectual appetite, because it pertains to the same nature of the soul, and because the lower appetite is a power in a bodily organ. This has no place in Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0041","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0042","text":"[I q. 106 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32870)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0043","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that a lower Angel can illuminate a higher one. For the ecclesiastical hierarchy is derived from the celestial one and represents it; whence the heavenly Jerusalem is also called our mother, Galatians IV. But in the Church, even superiors are illuminated and taught by inferiors; according to that saying of the Apostle, I Corinthians XIV, *you can all prophesy one by one, so that all may learn and all may be exhorted*. Therefore in the celestial hierarchy too, superiors can be illuminated by inferiors.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0044","text":"[I q. 106 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32871)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0045","text":"Moreover, just as the order of bodily substances depends upon God's will, so too does the order of spiritual substances. But, as was said, God sometimes acts outside the order of bodily substances. Therefore He also sometimes acts outside the order of spiritual substances, illuminating lower beings not through higher intermediaries. Thus, therefore, inferiors illuminated by God can illuminate superiors.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0046","text":"[I q. 106 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32872)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0047","text":"Moreover, one Angel illuminates another toward whom he turns, as was said above. But since this turning is voluntary, the highest Angel can turn himself toward the lowest, passing over the intermediates. Therefore he can illuminate him immediately, and thus can illuminate superiors.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0048","text":"[I q. 106 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32873)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0049","text":"But on the contrary, Dionysius says that *this law of divinity is immovably established: that lower things are led back to God through higher things*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0050","text":"[I q. 106 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32874)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0051","text":"I answer that it must be said that lower Angels never illuminate higher ones, but are always illuminated by them. The reason for this is that, as was said above, an order is contained under an order, just as a cause is contained under a cause. Hence, just as cause is ordered to cause, so order is ordered to order. And therefore it is not unfitting if something is at times done outside the order of a lower cause, in order to be ordered to a higher cause; just as in human affairs the command of a president is set aside in order that the prince may be obeyed. And thus it happens that, outside the order of corporeal nature, God miraculously performs something, in order to order men to the knowledge of Him. But the setting aside of the order due to spiritual substances in no way pertains to the ordering of men to God, since the operations of Angels are not manifest to us as are the operations of visible bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0052","text":"And therefore the order fitting spiritual substances is never set aside by God, but lower things are always moved through higher things, and not conversely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0053","text":"[I q. 106 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32875)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0054","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the ecclesiastical hierarchy imitates the heavenly one in some measure, but does not perfectly attain its likeness. For in the heavenly hierarchy the whole ground of order derives from proximity to God. And therefore those who are nearer to God are also higher in rank and clearer in knowledge; and for this reason the higher are never illuminated by the lower. But in the ecclesiastical hierarchy, those who are nearer to God in holiness are sometimes lowest in rank and not outstanding in knowledge; and some even excel in one respect according to knowledge and fall short in another. And for this reason superiors can be taught by inferiors.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0055","text":"[I q. 106 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32876)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0056","text":"To the second it must be said that there is not a similar account concerning God's acting outside the order of corporeal nature and of spiritual nature, as has been said. Hence the argument does not follow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0057","text":"[I q. 106 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32877)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0058","text":"To the third it must be said that an Angel turns by will toward illuminating another Angel; but the will of an Angel is always regulated by the divine law, which established order among the Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0059","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0060","text":"[I q. 106 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32878)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0061","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that a higher Angel does not illuminate a lower one concerning all that he himself knows. For Dionysius says, in chapter XII of *The Celestial Hierarchy*, that higher Angels have knowledge more universal, whereas lower ones have knowledge more particular and subject. But more things are contained under universal knowledge than under particular knowledge. Therefore lower Angels do not know, through the illumination of the higher, everything that the higher Angels know.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0062","text":"[I q. 106 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32879)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0063","text":"Moreover, the Master says, in distinction XI of the Second Book of the *Sentences*, that the higher Angels knew from the ages the mystery of the Incarnation, but it was unknown to the lower until it was accomplished. This appears from the fact that, when certain Angels ask, Who is this king of glory? as though ignorant, others answer, as though knowing, The Lord of hosts, he is the king of glory, as Dionysius expounds in chapter VII of *The Celestial Hierarchy*. But this would not be so if the higher Angels illuminated the lower concerning everything that they themselves know. Therefore they do not illuminate them concerning everything known to themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0064","text":"[I q. 106 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32880)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0065","text":"Moreover, if the higher Angels announce to the lower all that they know, nothing known by the higher remains unknown to the lower. Therefore thereafter the higher will not be able to illuminate the lower. This seems unfitting. Therefore the higher do not illuminate the lower concerning all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0066","text":"[I q. 106 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32881)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0067","text":"But on the contrary, Gregory says that *in that heavenly homeland, although certain things are given excellently, nevertheless nothing is possessed individually*. And Dionysius says, in chapter XV of *The Celestial Hierarchy*, that *each heavenly essence communicates to an inferior the understanding given to it by a superior*; as is clear from the authority cited above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0068","text":"[I q. 106 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32882)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0069","text":"I answer that it must be said that all creatures participate in the divine goodness such that they diffuse to others the good which they have, for it belongs to the nature of the good to communicate itself to others. And hence it is also that corporeal agents impart their likeness to others, as far as possible. Therefore, the more certain agents are established in participation in the divine goodness, the more they strive to transfuse their perfections into others, as far as possible. Hence blessed Peter exhorts those who participate in the divine goodness through grace, saying, in I Peter IV, *each one, as he has received grace, administering it to one another, as good stewards of the manifold grace of God*. Therefore, much more do the holy Angels, who are in the fullest participation in the divine goodness, impart to those subject to them whatever they receive from God. Yet it is not received by the lower as excellently as it is in the higher.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0070","text":"And therefore the higher always remain in a higher order and possess more perfect knowledge. Just as a teacher understands one and the same thing more fully than the disciple who learns from him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0071","text":"[I q. 106 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32883)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0072","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the knowledge of the higher Angels is called more universal with respect to a more eminent mode of understanding.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0073","text":"[I q. 106 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32884)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0074","text":"To the second it must be said that the Master's statement is not to be understood as though the lower Angels had been altogether ignorant of the mystery of the Incarnation; but rather because they did not know it as fully as the higher Angels, and afterwards advanced in its knowledge, while that mystery was being fulfilled.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0075","text":"[I q. 106 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32885)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0076","text":"To the third it must be said that, up to the day of judgment, certain new things are always divinely revealed to the highest Angels concerning those matters that pertain to the ordering of the world, and especially to the salvation of the elect. Hence there always remains something by which the higher Angels may enlighten the lower.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_98:P0076"]}],"source_digest":"fbf4a6e3233e081c059278b32828e492c03f3fbd6c1c0bd28c5e3bdaa3199144","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 106"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 106","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99","title":"First Part · Question 107","order":108,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC_P_99","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 107 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32886)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de locutionibus Angelorum. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quinque. Primo, utrum unus Angelus loquatur alii. Secundo, utrum inferior superiori. Tertio, utrum Angelus Deo. Quarto, utrum in locutione Angeli aliquid distantia localis operetur. Quinto, utrum locutionem unius Angeli ad alterum omnes cognoscant.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 107 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32887)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod unus Angelus alteri non loquatur. Dicit enim Gregorius, XVIII Moral., quod *in statu resurrectionis uniuscuiusque mentem ab alterius oculis membrorum corpulentia non abscondit*. Multo igitur minus mens unius Angeli absconditur ab altero. Sed locutio est ad manifestandum alteri quod latet in mente. Non igitur oportet quod unus Angelus alteri loquatur.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32888)\n\nPraeterea, duplex est locutio, interior, per quam aliquis sibi ipsi loquitur; et exterior, per quam aliquis loquitur alteri. Exterior autem locutio fit per aliquod sensibile signum, puta voce vel nutu vel aliquo corporis membro, puta lingua vel digito, quae Angelis competere non possunt. Ergo unus Angelus alteri non loquitur.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32889)\n\nPraeterea, loquens excitat audientem ut attendat suae locutioni. Sed non videtur per quid unus Angelus excitet alium ad attendendum, hoc enim fit apud nos aliquo sensibili signo. Ergo unus Angelus non loquitur alteri.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32890)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur I Cor. XIII, *si linguis hominum loquar et Angelorum*.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32891)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in Angelis est aliqua locutio, sed, sicut dicit Gregorius II Moral., *dignum est ut mens nostra, qualitatem corporeae locutionis excedens, ad sublimes atque incognitos modos locutionis intimae suspendatur*. Ad intelligendum igitur qualiter unus Angelus alii loquatur, considerandum est quod, sicut supra diximus cum de actibus et potentiis animae ageretur, voluntas movet intellectum ad suam operationem. Intelligibile autem est in intellectu tripliciter, primo quidem, habitualiter, vel secundum memoriam, ut Augustinus dicit; secundo autem, ut in actu consideratum vel conceptum; tertio, ut ad aliud relatum. Manifestum est autem quod de primo gradu in secundum transfertur intelligibile per imperium voluntatis, unde in definitione habitus dicitur, quo quis utitur cum voluerit. Similiter autem et de secundo gradu transfertur in tertium per voluntatem, nam per voluntatem conceptus mentis ordinatur ad alterum, puta vel ad agendum aliquid, vel ad manifestandum alteri. Quando autem mens convertit se ad actu considerandum quod habet in habitu, loquitur aliquis sibi ipsi, nam ipse conceptus mentis interius verbum vocatur. Ex hoc vero quod conceptus mentis angelicae ordinatur ad manifestandum alteri, per voluntatem ipsius Angeli, conceptus mentis unius Angeli innotescit alteri, et sic loquitur unus Angelus alteri. Nihil est enim aliud loqui ad alterum, quam conceptum mentis alteri manifestare.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32892)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in nobis interior mentis conceptus quasi duplici obstaculo clauditur. Primo quidem, ipsa voluntate, quae conceptum intellectus potest retinere interius, vel ad extra ordinare. Et quantum ad hoc, mentem unius nullus alius potest videre nisi solus Deus; secundum illud I Cor. II, *quae sunt hominis, nemo novit nisi spiritus hominis, qui in ipso est*. Secundo autem clauditur mens hominis ab alio homine per grossitiem corporis. Unde cum etiam voluntas ordinat conceptum mentis ad manifestandum alteri, non statim cognoscitur ab alio, sed oportet aliquod signum sensibile adhibere. Et hoc est quod Gregorius dicit, II Moral., *alienis oculis intra secretum mentis, quasi post parietem corporis stamus, sed cum manifestare nosmetipsos cupimus, quasi per linguae ianuam egredimur, ut quales sumus intrinsecus, ostendamus*. Hoc autem obstaculum non habet Angelus. Et ideo quam cito vult manifestare suum conceptum, statim alius cognoscit.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32893)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod locutio exterior quae fit per vocem, est nobis necessaria propter obstaculum corporis. Unde non convenit Angelo, sed sola locutio interior; ad quam pertinet non solum quod loquatur sibi interius concipiendo, sed etiam quod ordinet per voluntatem ad alterius manifestationem. Et sic lingua Angelorum metaphorice dicitur ipsa virtus Angeli, qua conceptum suum manifestat.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32894)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, quantum ad Angelos bonos, qui semper se invicem vident in verbo, non esset necessarium ponere aliquid excitativum, quia sicut unus semper videt alium, ita semper videt quidquid in eo est ad se ordinatum. Sed quia etiam in statu naturae conditae sibi invicem loqui poterant, et mali Angeli etiam nunc sibi invicem loquuntur; dicendum est quod, sicut sensus movetur a sensibili, ita intellectus movetur ab intelligibili. Sicut ergo per signum sensibile excitatur sensus, ita per aliquam virtutem intelligibilem potest excitari mens Angeli ad attendendum.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 107 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32895)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod inferior Angelus superiori non loquatur. Quia super illud I Cor. XIII, *si linguis hominum loquar et Angelorum*, dicit Glossa quod locutiones Angelorum sunt illuminationes, quibus superiores illuminant inferiores. Sed inferiores nunquam illuminant superiores, ut supra dictum est. Ergo nec inferiores superioribus loquuntur.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32896)\n\nPraeterea, supra dictum est quod illuminare nihil est aliud quam illud quod est alicui manifestum, alteri manifestare. Sed hoc idem est loqui. Ergo idem est loqui, et illuminare, et sic idem quod prius.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32897)\n\nPraeterea, Gregorius dicit, II Moral., quod *Deus ad Angelos loquitur, eo ipso quod eorum cordibus occulta sua invisibilia ostendit*. Sed hoc ipsum est illuminare. Ergo omnis Dei locutio est illuminatio. Pari ergo ratione, omnis Angeli locutio est illuminatio. Nullo ergo modo Angelus inferior superiori loqui potest.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32898)\n\nSed contra est quod, sicut Dionysius exponit VII Cael. Hier., inferiores Angeli superioribus dixerunt, quis est iste rex gloriae?\n\n[I q. 107 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32899)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Angeli inferiores superioribus loqui possunt. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod omnis illuminatio est locutio in Angelis, sed non omnis locutio est illuminatio. Quia sicut dictum est, Angelum loqui Angelo nihil aliud est quam conceptum suum ordinare ad hoc ut ei innotescat, per propriam voluntatem. Ea vero quae mente concipiuntur, ad duplex principium referri possunt, scilicet ad ipsum Deum, qui est prima veritas; et ad voluntatem intelligentis, per quam aliquid actu consideramus. Quia vero veritas est lumen intellectus, et regula omnis veritatis est ipse Deus; manifestatio eius quod mente concipitur, secundum quod dependet a prima veritate, et locutio est et illuminatio; puta si unus homo dicat alii, caelum est a Deo creatum, vel, homo est animal. Sed manifestatio eorum quae dependent ex voluntate intelligentis, non potest dici illuminatio, sed locutio tantum; puta si aliquis alteri dicat, volo hoc addiscere, volo hoc vel illud facere. Cuius ratio est, quia voluntas creata non est lux, nec regula veritatis, sed participans lucem, unde communicare ea quae sunt a voluntate creata, inquantum huiusmodi, non est illuminare. Non enim pertinet ad perfectionem intellectus mei, quid tu velis, vel quid tu intelligas, cognoscere, sed solum quid rei veritas habeat. Manifestum est autem quod Angeli dicuntur superiores vel inferiores per comparationem ad hoc principium quod est Deus. Et ideo illuminatio, quae dependet a principio quod est Deus, solum per superiores Angelos ad inferiores deducitur. Sed in ordine ad principium quod est voluntas, ipse volens est primus et supremus. Et ideo manifestatio eorum quae ad voluntatem pertinent, per ipsum volentem deducitur ad alios quoscumque. Et quantum ad hoc, et superiores inferioribus, et inferiores superioribus loquuntur.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32900)\n\nEt per hoc patet solutio ad primum, et ad secundum.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32901)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod omnis Dei locutio ad Angelos est illuminatio, quia cum voluntas Dei sit regula veritatis, etiam scire quid Deus velit, pertinet ad perfectionem et illuminationem mentis creatae. Sed non est eadem ratio de voluntate Angeli, ut dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 107 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32902)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus Deo non loquatur. Locutio enim est ad manifestandum aliquid alteri. Sed Angelus nihil potest manifestare Deo, qui omnia novit. Ergo Angelus non loquitur Deo.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32903)\n\nPraeterea, loqui est ordinare conceptum intellectus ad alterum, ut dictum est. Sed Angelus semper conceptum suae mentis ordinat in Deum. Si ergo aliquando Deo loquitur, semper Deo loquitur, quod potest videri alicui inconveniens, cum aliquando Angelus Angelo loquatur. Videtur ergo quod Angelus nunquam loquatur Deo.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32904)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Zachar. I, *respondit Angelus domini, et dixit, domine exercituum, usquequo non misereberis Ierusalem?* Loquitur ergo Angelus Deo.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32905)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, locutio Angeli est per hoc, quod conceptus mentis ordinatur ad alterum. Sed aliquid ordinatur ad alterum dupliciter. Uno modo, ad hoc quod communicet alteri; sicut in rebus naturalibus agens ordinatur ad patiens et in locutione humana doctor ordinatur ad discipulum. Et quantum ad hoc, nullo modo Angelus loquitur Deo, neque de his quae ad rerum veritatem pertinent, neque de his quae dependent a voluntate creata, quia Deus est omnis veritatis et omnis voluntatis principium et conditor. Alio modo ordinatur aliquid ad alterum, ut ab eo aliquid accipiat; sicut in rebus naturalibus passivum ad agens, et in locutione humana discipulus ad magistrum. Et hoc modo Angelus loquitur Deo, vel consultando divinam voluntatem de agendis; vel eius excellentiam, quam nunquam comprehendit, admirando; sicut Gregorius dicit, II Moral., quod *Angeli loquuntur Deo, cum per hoc quod super semetipsos respiciunt, in motum admirationis surgunt*.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32906)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod locutio non semper est ad manifestandum alteri; sed quandoque ad hoc ordinatur finaliter, ut loquenti aliquid manifestetur; sicut cum discipulus quaerit aliquid a magistro.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32907)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod locutione qua Angeli loquuntur Deo laudantes ipsum et admirantes, semper Angeli Deo loquuntur. Sed locutione qua eius sapientiam consulunt super agendis, tunc ei loquuntur, quando aliquod novum per eos agendum occurrit, super quo desiderant illuminari.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 107 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32908)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod localis distantia operetur aliquid in locutione angelica. Sicut enim dicit Damascenus, *Angelus ubi est, ibi operatur*. Locutio autem est quaedam operatio Angeli. Cum ergo Angelus sit in determinato loco, videtur quod usque ad determinatam loci distantiam Angelus loqui possit.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32909)\n\nPraeterea, clamor loquentis fit propter distantiam audientis. Sed Isaiae VI dicitur de Seraphim, quod clamabat alter ad alterum. Ergo videtur quod in locutione Angelorum aliquid operetur localis distantia.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32910)\n\nSed contra est quod, sicut dicitur Luc. XVI, dives in Inferno positus loquebatur Abrahae, non impediente locali distantia. Multo igitur minus localis distantia potest impedire locutionem unius Angeli ad alterum.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32911)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod locutio Angeli in intellectuali operatione consistit, ut ex dictis patet. Intellectualis autem operatio Angeli omnino abstracta est a loco et tempore, nam etiam nostra intellectualis operatio est per abstractionem ab hic et nunc, nisi per accidens ex parte phantasmatum, quae in Angelis nulla sunt. In eo autem quod est omnino abstractum a loco et tempore, nihil operatur neque temporis diversitas, neque loci distantia. Unde in locutione Angeli nullum impedimentum facit distantia loci.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32912)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod locutio Angeli, sicut dictum est, est locutio interior, quae tamen ab alio percipitur, et ideo est in Angelo loquente, et per consequens ubi est Angelus loquens. Sed sicut distantia localis non impedit quin unus Angelus alium videre possit; ita etiam non impedit quin percipiat quod in eo ad se ordinatur, quod est eius locutionem percipere.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32913)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod clamor ille non est vocis corporeae, qui fit propter distantiam loci; sed significat magnitudinem rei quae dicebatur, vel magnitudinem affectus, secundum quod dicit Gregorius, II Moral., *tanto quisque minus clamat, quanto minus desiderat*.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 107 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32914)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod locutionem unius Angeli ad alterum omnes cognoscant. Quod enim unius hominis locutionem non omnes audiant, facit inaequalis loci distantia. Sed in locutione Angeli nihil operatur localis distantia, ut dictum est. Ergo uno Angelo loquente ad alterum, omnes percipiunt.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32915)\n\nPraeterea, omnes Angeli communicant in virtute intelligendi. Si ergo conceptus mentis unius ordinatus ad alterum cognoscitur ab uno, pari ratione cognoscitur ab aliis.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32916)\n\nPraeterea, illuminatio est quaedam species locutionis. Sed illuminatio unius Angeli ab altero, pervenit ad omnes Angelos, quia, ut Dionysius dicit XV cap. Cael. Hier., *unaquaeque caelestis essentia intelligentiam sibi traditam aliis communicat*. Ergo et locutio unius Angeli ad alterum, ad omnes perducitur.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32917)\n\nSed contra est quod unus homo potest alteri soli loqui. Multo igitur magis hoc in Angelis esse potest.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32918)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, conceptus mentis unius Angeli percipi potest ab altero, per hoc quod ille cuius est conceptus, sua voluntate ordinat ipsum ad alterum. Potest autem ex aliqua causa ordinari aliquid ad unum, et non ad alterum. Et ideo potest conceptus unius ab aliquo uno cognosci, et non ab aliis. Et sic locutionem unius Angeli ad alterum potest percipere unus absque aliis, non quidem impediente distantia locali, sed hoc faciente voluntaria ordinatione, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32919)\n\nUnde patet responsio ad primum et secundum.\n\n[I q. 107 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32920)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod illuminatio est de his quae emanant a prima regula veritatis, quae est principium commune omnium Angelorum, et ideo illuminationes sunt omnibus communes. Sed locutio potest esse de his quae ordinantur ad principium voluntatis creatae, quod est proprium unicuique Angelo, et ideo non oportet quod huiusmodi locutiones sint omnibus communes.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0002","text":"[I q. 107 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32886)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de locutionibus Angelorum. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quinque. Primo, utrum unus Angelus loquatur alii. Secundo, utrum inferior superiori. Tertio, utrum Angelus Deo. Quarto, utrum in locutione Angeli aliquid distantia localis operetur. Quinto, utrum locutionem unius Angeli ad alterum omnes cognoscant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0005","text":"[I q. 107 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32887)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod unus Angelus alteri non loquatur. Dicit enim Gregorius, XVIII Moral., quod *in statu resurrectionis uniuscuiusque mentem ab alterius oculis membrorum corpulentia non abscondit*. Multo igitur minus mens unius Angeli absconditur ab altero. Sed locutio est ad manifestandum alteri quod latet in mente. Non igitur oportet quod unus Angelus alteri loquatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0007","text":"[I q. 107 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32888)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0008","text":"Praeterea, duplex est locutio, interior, per quam aliquis sibi ipsi loquitur; et exterior, per quam aliquis loquitur alteri. Exterior autem locutio fit per aliquod sensibile signum, puta voce vel nutu vel aliquo corporis membro, puta lingua vel digito, quae Angelis competere non possunt. Ergo unus Angelus alteri non loquitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0009","text":"[I q. 107 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32889)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0010","text":"Praeterea, loquens excitat audientem ut attendat suae locutioni. Sed non videtur per quid unus Angelus excitet alium ad attendendum, hoc enim fit apud nos aliquo sensibili signo. Ergo unus Angelus non loquitur alteri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0011","text":"[I q. 107 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32890)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur I Cor. XIII, *si linguis hominum loquar et Angelorum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0013","text":"[I q. 107 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32891)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in Angelis est aliqua locutio, sed, sicut dicit Gregorius II Moral., *dignum est ut mens nostra, qualitatem corporeae locutionis excedens, ad sublimes atque incognitos modos locutionis intimae suspendatur*. Ad intelligendum igitur qualiter unus Angelus alii loquatur, considerandum est quod, sicut supra diximus cum de actibus et potentiis animae ageretur, voluntas movet intellectum ad suam operationem. Intelligibile autem est in intellectu tripliciter, primo quidem, habitualiter, vel secundum memoriam, ut Augustinus dicit; secundo autem, ut in actu consideratum vel conceptum; tertio, ut ad aliud relatum. Manifestum est autem quod de primo gradu in secundum transfertur intelligibile per imperium voluntatis, unde in definitione habitus dicitur, quo quis utitur cum voluerit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0015","text":"Similiter autem et de secundo gradu transfertur in tertium per voluntatem, nam per voluntatem conceptus mentis ordinatur ad alterum, puta vel ad agendum aliquid, vel ad manifestandum alteri. Quando autem mens convertit se ad actu considerandum quod habet in habitu, loquitur aliquis sibi ipsi, nam ipse conceptus mentis interius verbum vocatur. Ex hoc vero quod conceptus mentis angelicae ordinatur ad manifestandum alteri, per voluntatem ipsius Angeli, conceptus mentis unius Angeli innotescit alteri, et sic loquitur unus Angelus alteri. Nihil est enim aliud loqui ad alterum, quam conceptum mentis alteri manifestare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0016","text":"[I q. 107 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32892)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in nobis interior mentis conceptus quasi duplici obstaculo clauditur. Primo quidem, ipsa voluntate, quae conceptum intellectus potest retinere interius, vel ad extra ordinare. Et quantum ad hoc, mentem unius nullus alius potest videre nisi solus Deus; secundum illud I Cor. II, *quae sunt hominis, nemo novit nisi spiritus hominis, qui in ipso est*. Secundo autem clauditur mens hominis ab alio homine per grossitiem corporis. Unde cum etiam voluntas ordinat conceptum mentis ad manifestandum alteri, non statim cognoscitur ab alio, sed oportet aliquod signum sensibile adhibere. Et hoc est quod Gregorius dicit, II Moral., *alienis oculis intra secretum mentis, quasi post parietem corporis stamus, sed cum manifestare nosmetipsos cupimus, quasi per linguae ianuam egredimur, ut quales sumus intrinsecus, ostendamus*. Hoc autem obstaculum non habet Angelus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0018","text":"Et ideo quam cito vult manifestare suum conceptum, statim alius cognoscit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0019","text":"[I q. 107 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32893)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod locutio exterior quae fit per vocem, est nobis necessaria propter obstaculum corporis. Unde non convenit Angelo, sed sola locutio interior; ad quam pertinet non solum quod loquatur sibi interius concipiendo, sed etiam quod ordinet per voluntatem ad alterius manifestationem. Et sic lingua Angelorum metaphorice dicitur ipsa virtus Angeli, qua conceptum suum manifestat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0021","text":"[I q. 107 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32894)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0022","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, quantum ad Angelos bonos, qui semper se invicem vident in verbo, non esset necessarium ponere aliquid excitativum, quia sicut unus semper videt alium, ita semper videt quidquid in eo est ad se ordinatum. Sed quia etiam in statu naturae conditae sibi invicem loqui poterant, et mali Angeli etiam nunc sibi invicem loquuntur; dicendum est quod, sicut sensus movetur a sensibili, ita intellectus movetur ab intelligibili. Sicut ergo per signum sensibile excitatur sensus, ita per aliquam virtutem intelligibilem potest excitari mens Angeli ad attendendum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0023","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0024","text":"[I q. 107 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32895)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0025","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod inferior Angelus superiori non loquatur. Quia super illud I Cor. XIII, *si linguis hominum loquar et Angelorum*, dicit Glossa quod locutiones Angelorum sunt illuminationes, quibus superiores illuminant inferiores. Sed inferiores nunquam illuminant superiores, ut supra dictum est. Ergo nec inferiores superioribus loquuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0026","text":"[I q. 107 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32896)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0027","text":"Praeterea, supra dictum est quod illuminare nihil est aliud quam illud quod est alicui manifestum, alteri manifestare. Sed hoc idem est loqui. Ergo idem est loqui, et illuminare, et sic idem quod prius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0028","text":"[I q. 107 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32897)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0029","text":"Praeterea, Gregorius dicit, II Moral., quod *Deus ad Angelos loquitur, eo ipso quod eorum cordibus occulta sua invisibilia ostendit*. Sed hoc ipsum est illuminare. Ergo omnis Dei locutio est illuminatio. Pari ergo ratione, omnis Angeli locutio est illuminatio. Nullo ergo modo Angelus inferior superiori loqui potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0030","text":"[I q. 107 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32898)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0031","text":"Sed contra est quod, sicut Dionysius exponit VII Cael. Hier., inferiores Angeli superioribus dixerunt, quis est iste rex gloriae?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0032","text":"[I q. 107 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32899)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0033","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Angeli inferiores superioribus loqui possunt. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod omnis illuminatio est locutio in Angelis, sed non omnis locutio est illuminatio. Quia sicut dictum est, Angelum loqui Angelo nihil aliud est quam conceptum suum ordinare ad hoc ut ei innotescat, per propriam voluntatem. Ea vero quae mente concipiuntur, ad duplex principium referri possunt, scilicet ad ipsum Deum, qui est prima veritas; et ad voluntatem intelligentis, per quam aliquid actu consideramus. Quia vero veritas est lumen intellectus, et regula omnis veritatis est ipse Deus; manifestatio eius quod mente concipitur, secundum quod dependet a prima veritate, et locutio est et illuminatio; puta si unus homo dicat alii, caelum est a Deo creatum, vel, homo est animal.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0034","text":"Sed manifestatio eorum quae dependent ex voluntate intelligentis, non potest dici illuminatio, sed locutio tantum; puta si aliquis alteri dicat, volo hoc addiscere, volo hoc vel illud facere. Cuius ratio est, quia voluntas creata non est lux, nec regula veritatis, sed participans lucem, unde communicare ea quae sunt a voluntate creata, inquantum huiusmodi, non est illuminare. Non enim pertinet ad perfectionem intellectus mei, quid tu velis, vel quid tu intelligas, cognoscere, sed solum quid rei veritas habeat. Manifestum est autem quod Angeli dicuntur superiores vel inferiores per comparationem ad hoc principium quod est Deus. Et ideo illuminatio, quae dependet a principio quod est Deus, solum per superiores Angelos ad inferiores deducitur. Sed in ordine ad principium quod est voluntas, ipse volens est primus et supremus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0035","text":"Et ideo manifestatio eorum quae ad voluntatem pertinent, per ipsum volentem deducitur ad alios quoscumque. Et quantum ad hoc, et superiores inferioribus, et inferiores superioribus loquuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0036","text":"[I q. 107 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32900)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0037","text":"Et per hoc patet solutio ad primum, et ad secundum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0038","text":"[I q. 107 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32901)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0039","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod omnis Dei locutio ad Angelos est illuminatio, quia cum voluntas Dei sit regula veritatis, etiam scire quid Deus velit, pertinet ad perfectionem et illuminationem mentis creatae. Sed non est eadem ratio de voluntate Angeli, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0040","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0041","text":"[I q. 107 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32902)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0042","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus Deo non loquatur. Locutio enim est ad manifestandum aliquid alteri. Sed Angelus nihil potest manifestare Deo, qui omnia novit. Ergo Angelus non loquitur Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0043","text":"[I q. 107 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32903)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0044","text":"Praeterea, loqui est ordinare conceptum intellectus ad alterum, ut dictum est. Sed Angelus semper conceptum suae mentis ordinat in Deum. Si ergo aliquando Deo loquitur, semper Deo loquitur, quod potest videri alicui inconveniens, cum aliquando Angelus Angelo loquatur. Videtur ergo quod Angelus nunquam loquatur Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0045","text":"[I q. 107 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32904)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0046","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Zachar. I, *respondit Angelus domini, et dixit, domine exercituum, usquequo non misereberis Ierusalem?* Loquitur ergo Angelus Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0047","text":"[I q. 107 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32905)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0048","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, locutio Angeli est per hoc, quod conceptus mentis ordinatur ad alterum. Sed aliquid ordinatur ad alterum dupliciter. Uno modo, ad hoc quod communicet alteri; sicut in rebus naturalibus agens ordinatur ad patiens et in locutione humana doctor ordinatur ad discipulum. Et quantum ad hoc, nullo modo Angelus loquitur Deo, neque de his quae ad rerum veritatem pertinent, neque de his quae dependent a voluntate creata, quia Deus est omnis veritatis et omnis voluntatis principium et conditor. Alio modo ordinatur aliquid ad alterum, ut ab eo aliquid accipiat; sicut in rebus naturalibus passivum ad agens, et in locutione humana discipulus ad magistrum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0049","text":"Et hoc modo Angelus loquitur Deo, vel consultando divinam voluntatem de agendis; vel eius excellentiam, quam nunquam comprehendit, admirando; sicut Gregorius dicit, II Moral., quod *Angeli loquuntur Deo, cum per hoc quod super semetipsos respiciunt, in motum admirationis surgunt*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0050","text":"[I q. 107 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32906)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0051","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod locutio non semper est ad manifestandum alteri; sed quandoque ad hoc ordinatur finaliter, ut loquenti aliquid manifestetur; sicut cum discipulus quaerit aliquid a magistro.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0052","text":"[I q. 107 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32907)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0053","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod locutione qua Angeli loquuntur Deo laudantes ipsum et admirantes, semper Angeli Deo loquuntur. Sed locutione qua eius sapientiam consulunt super agendis, tunc ei loquuntur, quando aliquod novum per eos agendum occurrit, super quo desiderant illuminari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0054","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0055","text":"[I q. 107 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32908)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0056","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod localis distantia operetur aliquid in locutione angelica. Sicut enim dicit Damascenus, *Angelus ubi est, ibi operatur*. Locutio autem est quaedam operatio Angeli. Cum ergo Angelus sit in determinato loco, videtur quod usque ad determinatam loci distantiam Angelus loqui possit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0057","text":"[I q. 107 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32909)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0058","text":"Praeterea, clamor loquentis fit propter distantiam audientis. Sed Isaiae VI dicitur de Seraphim, quod clamabat alter ad alterum. Ergo videtur quod in locutione Angelorum aliquid operetur localis distantia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0059","text":"[I q. 107 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32910)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0060","text":"Sed contra est quod, sicut dicitur Luc. XVI, dives in Inferno positus loquebatur Abrahae, non impediente locali distantia. Multo igitur minus localis distantia potest impedire locutionem unius Angeli ad alterum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0061","text":"[I q. 107 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32911)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0062","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod locutio Angeli in intellectuali operatione consistit, ut ex dictis patet. Intellectualis autem operatio Angeli omnino abstracta est a loco et tempore, nam etiam nostra intellectualis operatio est per abstractionem ab hic et nunc, nisi per accidens ex parte phantasmatum, quae in Angelis nulla sunt. In eo autem quod est omnino abstractum a loco et tempore, nihil operatur neque temporis diversitas, neque loci distantia. Unde in locutione Angeli nullum impedimentum facit distantia loci.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0063","text":"[I q. 107 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32912)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0064","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod locutio Angeli, sicut dictum est, est locutio interior, quae tamen ab alio percipitur, et ideo est in Angelo loquente, et per consequens ubi est Angelus loquens. Sed sicut distantia localis non impedit quin unus Angelus alium videre possit; ita etiam non impedit quin percipiat quod in eo ad se ordinatur, quod est eius locutionem percipere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0065","text":"[I q. 107 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32913)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0066","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod clamor ille non est vocis corporeae, qui fit propter distantiam loci; sed significat magnitudinem rei quae dicebatur, vel magnitudinem affectus, secundum quod dicit Gregorius, II Moral., *tanto quisque minus clamat, quanto minus desiderat*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0067","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0068","text":"[I q. 107 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32914)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0069","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod locutionem unius Angeli ad alterum omnes cognoscant. Quod enim unius hominis locutionem non omnes audiant, facit inaequalis loci distantia. Sed in locutione Angeli nihil operatur localis distantia, ut dictum est. Ergo uno Angelo loquente ad alterum, omnes percipiunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0070","text":"[I q. 107 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32915)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0071","text":"Praeterea, omnes Angeli communicant in virtute intelligendi. Si ergo conceptus mentis unius ordinatus ad alterum cognoscitur ab uno, pari ratione cognoscitur ab aliis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0072","text":"[I q. 107 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32916)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0073","text":"Praeterea, illuminatio est quaedam species locutionis. Sed illuminatio unius Angeli ab altero, pervenit ad omnes Angelos, quia, ut Dionysius dicit XV cap. Cael. Hier., *unaquaeque caelestis essentia intelligentiam sibi traditam aliis communicat*. Ergo et locutio unius Angeli ad alterum, ad omnes perducitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0074","text":"[I q. 107 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32917)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0075","text":"Sed contra est quod unus homo potest alteri soli loqui. Multo igitur magis hoc in Angelis esse potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0076","text":"[I q. 107 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32918)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0077","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, conceptus mentis unius Angeli percipi potest ab altero, per hoc quod ille cuius est conceptus, sua voluntate ordinat ipsum ad alterum. Potest autem ex aliqua causa ordinari aliquid ad unum, et non ad alterum. Et ideo potest conceptus unius ab aliquo uno cognosci, et non ab aliis. Et sic locutionem unius Angeli ad alterum potest percipere unus absque aliis, non quidem impediente distantia locali, sed hoc faciente voluntaria ordinatione, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0078","text":"[I q. 107 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32919)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0079","text":"Unde patet responsio ad primum et secundum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0080","text":"[I q. 107 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32920)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0081","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod illuminatio est de his quae emanant a prima regula veritatis, quae est principium commune omnium Angelorum, et ideo illuminationes sunt omnibus communes. Sed locutio potest esse de his quae ordinantur ad principium voluntatis creatae, quod est proprium unicuique Angelo, et ideo non oportet quod huiusmodi locutiones sint omnibus communes.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"922bc3a9a4f1f363ef0a49b02db098c79943308285c681cfacfc87eec600378c","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T154012174593Z_94abd605","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 107","source_sha256":"922bc3a9a4f1f363ef0a49b02db098c79943308285c681cfacfc87eec600378c","sha256":"56b6bd8827a96b0786ef5cee668e809ec39434cf978c096b8c5bd80f52535fad","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:40:12.174613+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0002","text":"[I q. 107 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32886)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the speech of Angels. And concerning this, five questions are asked. First, whether one Angel speaks to another. Second, whether an inferior Angel speaks to a superior. Third, whether an Angel speaks to God. Fourth, whether local distance has any effect in the speech of Angels. Fifth, whether all know the speech of one Angel to another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0005","text":"[I q. 107 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32887)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that one Angel does not speak to another. For Gregory says, in *Morals*, XVIII, that *in the state of resurrection the corporeity of the members does not conceal the mind of one from the eyes of another*. Therefore, much less is the mind of one Angel concealed from another. But speech is for manifesting to another what lies hidden in the mind. Therefore, it is not necessary that one Angel speak to another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0007","text":"[I q. 107 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32888)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0008","text":"Further, speech is twofold: interior, by which someone speaks to himself; and exterior, by which someone speaks to another. But exterior speech takes place through some sensible sign, for instance by voice or gesture or some bodily member, such as the tongue or finger, which cannot belong to Angels. Therefore, one Angel does not speak to another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0009","text":"[I q. 107 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32889)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0010","text":"Further, one who speaks arouses the hearer to attend to his speech. But it does not appear by what means one Angel may arouse another to attend, for among us this is done by some sensible sign. Therefore, one Angel does not speak to another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0011","text":"[I q. 107 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32890)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in I Cor. XIII: *if I speak with the tongues of men and of Angels*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0013","text":"[I q. 107 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32891)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0014","text":"I answer that there is some speech in Angels; but, as Gregory says in *Morals*, II, *it is fitting that our mind, surpassing the quality of bodily speech, be raised up to the lofty and unknown modes of inward speech*. Therefore, in order to understand how one Angel speaks to another, it must be considered that, as we said above when treating of the acts and powers of the soul, the will moves the intellect to its operation. Now the intelligible is in the intellect in three ways: first, habitually, or according to memory, as Augustine says; second, as considered or conceived in act; third, as referred to another. Now it is manifest that the intelligible is transferred from the first grade to the second by the command of the will; hence it is said in the definition of habit that it is that which one uses when one wills.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0015","text":"Likewise, too, it is transferred from the second grade to the third by the will, for by the will the concept of the mind is ordered to another, for example, either to doing something or to manifesting it to another. But when the mind turns itself to considering in act that which it has in habit, someone speaks to himself, for this very concept of the mind is called the inward word. But from the fact that the concept of an angelic mind is ordered, through the will of the Angel himself, to being manifested to another, the concept of the mind of one Angel becomes known to another, and thus one Angel speaks to another. For to speak to another is nothing other than to manifest one's mental concept to another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0016","text":"[I q. 107 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32892)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that in us the interior concept of the mind is enclosed, as it were, by a twofold obstacle. First, by the will itself, which can retain the concept of the intellect inwardly or order it outwardly. And in this respect, no one else can see the mind of another except God alone, according to I Cor. II: *no one knows the things of a man except the spirit of the man which is in him*. Second, the mind of a man is enclosed from another man by the grossness of the body. Hence, even when the will orders the concept of the mind to be manifested to another, it is not immediately known by the other, but some sensible sign must be employed. And this is what Gregory says in *Morals*, II: *we stand before the eyes of others within the secret place of the mind, as though behind the wall of the body; but when we desire to manifest ourselves, we go out, as it were, through the door of the tongue, so that we may show what we are inwardly*. But an Angel does not have this obstacle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0018","text":"And therefore, as soon as he wills to manifest his concept, another immediately knows it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0019","text":"[I q. 107 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32893)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0020","text":"To the second, it must be said that exterior speech, which takes place through voice, is necessary to us because of the obstacle of the body. Hence it does not befit an Angel, but only interior speech does; and to this belongs not only that he speak inwardly to himself by conceiving, but also that by his will he order it to be manifested to another. Thus the tongue of Angels is spoken of metaphorically as the very power of an Angel by which he manifests his concept.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0021","text":"[I q. 107 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32894)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0022","text":"To the third, it must be said that, as regards the good Angels, who always see one another in the Word, it would not be necessary to posit anything arousing, because just as one always sees another, so he always sees whatever in him is ordered to himself. But since they could speak to one another even in the state of created nature, and evil Angels also speak to one another now, it must be said that, just as sense is moved by the sensible, so intellect is moved by the intelligible. Therefore, just as sense is aroused by a sensible sign, so the mind of an Angel can be aroused to attend by some intelligible power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0023","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0024","text":"[I q. 107 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32895)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0025","text":"The second point is approached thus. It seems that an inferior Angel does not speak to a superior one. For on the passage I Cor. XIII, *if I speak with the tongues of men and of Angels*, the Gloss says that the speech of Angels consists in illuminations, by which superiors illuminate inferiors. But inferiors never illuminate superiors, as was said above. Therefore neither do inferiors speak to superiors.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0026","text":"[I q. 107 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32896)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0027","text":"Further, it was said above that to illuminate is nothing other than to make manifest to another that which is manifest to oneself. But this is the same as to speak. Therefore to speak and to illuminate are the same, and thus likewise as before.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0028","text":"[I q. 107 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32897)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0029","text":"Further, Gregory says, *Morals*, II, that *God speaks to Angels by the very fact that He shows to their hearts His hidden invisible things*. But this very thing is to illuminate. Therefore every speech of God is illumination. By equal reasoning, then, every speech of an Angel is illumination. Therefore in no way can an inferior Angel speak to a superior.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0030","text":"[I q. 107 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32898)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0031","text":"On the contrary, as Dionysius explains in *Celestial Hierarchy*, VII, the inferior Angels said to the superior ones, Who is this King of glory?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0032","text":"[I q. 107 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32899)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0033","text":"I answer that inferior Angels can speak to superior Angels. To make this evident, it must be considered that every illumination is speech among Angels, but not every speech is illumination. For, as was said, for one Angel to speak to another is nothing other than to order his concept toward making it known to the other, by his own will. Now the things conceived in the mind can be referred to a twofold principle: namely, to God Himself, who is the first truth; and to the will of the one understanding, through which we actually consider something. But because truth is the light of the intellect, and God Himself is the rule of all truth, the manifestation of that which is conceived in the mind, insofar as it depends upon the first truth, is both speech and illumination; for example, if one man says to another, the heaven was created by God, or, man is an animal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0034","text":"But the manifestation of those things which depend on the will of the one understanding cannot be called illumination, but only speech; for example, if someone says to another, I wish to learn this, I wish to do this or that. The reason for this is that a created will is not light, nor the rule of truth, but participates in light; hence, to communicate those things which are from the created will, as such, is not to illuminate. For it does not belong to the perfection of my intellect to know what you will, or what you understand, but only what the truth of the thing is. Now it is evident that Angels are called superior or inferior by comparison to that principle which is God. And therefore illumination, which depends on the principle that is God, is conveyed only through superior Angels to inferior ones. But in relation to the principle that is the will, the very one who wills is first and supreme.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0035","text":"And therefore the manifestation of those things which pertain to the will is conveyed through the one who wills himself to any others whatsoever. And in this respect, both superiors speak to inferiors, and inferiors to superiors.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0036","text":"[I q. 107 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32900)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0037","text":"And through this the solution to the first and second is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0038","text":"[I q. 107 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32901)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0039","text":"To the third, it must be said that every speech of God to the Angels is illumination, because, since the will of God is the rule of truth, even to know what God wills belongs to the perfection and illumination of the created mind. But the same reasoning does not hold concerning the will of an Angel, as was said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0040","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0041","text":"[I q. 107 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32902)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0042","text":"The third point is approached thus. It seems that an Angel does not speak to God. For speech is for making something manifest to another. But an Angel can make nothing manifest to God, who knows all things. Therefore an Angel does not speak to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0043","text":"[I q. 107 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32903)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0044","text":"Further, to speak is to order a concept of the intellect toward another, as was said. But an Angel always orders the concept of his mind toward God. Therefore, if he ever speaks to God, he always speaks to God, which may seem unfitting to someone, since at times an Angel speaks to an Angel. Therefore it seems that an Angel never speaks to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0045","text":"[I q. 107 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32904)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0046","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Zech. I, *the Angel of the Lord answered and said, O Lord of hosts, how long will You not have mercy on Jerusalem?* Therefore an Angel speaks to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0047","text":"[I q. 107 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32905)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0048","text":"I answer that, as was said, the speech of an Angel takes place through the ordering of a concept of the mind toward another. But something is ordered toward another in two ways. In one way, in order that it communicate something to the other; as in natural things an agent is ordered toward a patient, and in human speech a teacher is ordered toward a disciple. And in this respect an Angel in no way speaks to God, neither concerning those things that pertain to the truth of things nor concerning those that depend upon created will, because God is the principle and creator of all truth and of every will. In another way, something is ordered toward another in order to receive something from it; as in natural things the passive is ordered toward the agent, and in human speech the disciple toward the teacher.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0049","text":"And in this way an angel speaks to God: either by consulting the divine will concerning things to be done; or by admiring His excellence, which it never comprehends; as Gregory says in *Morals*, II, that *angels speak to God when, by looking above themselves, they rise into a movement of admiration*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0050","text":"[I q. 107 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32906)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0051","text":"Therefore, to the first [objection] it must be said that speech is not always for making something manifest to another; rather, it is sometimes ordered finally to making something manifest to the speaker, as when a disciple asks something of a master.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0052","text":"[I q. 107 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32907)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0053","text":"To the second, it must be said that by the speech whereby angels speak to God, praising and admiring Him, angels always speak to God. But by the speech whereby they consult His wisdom concerning things to be done, they then speak to Him when some new thing to be done by them occurs, concerning which they desire to be enlightened.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0054","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0055","text":"[I q. 107 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32908)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0056","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth [article]. It seems that local distance has some effect in angelic speech. For, as Damascene says, *an angel works where it is*. But speech is a certain operation of an angel. Therefore, since an angel is in a determinate place, it seems that an angel can speak as far as a determinate distance of place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0057","text":"[I q. 107 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32909)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0058","text":"Further, the cry of one speaking occurs because of the distance of the hearer. But it is said of the Seraphim in Isaiah VI that one cried to another. Therefore, it seems that local distance has some effect in the speech of angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0059","text":"[I q. 107 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32910)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0060","text":"But on the contrary, as is said in Luke XVI, the rich man, placed in Hell, spoke to Abraham, local distance not hindering [him]. Therefore, much less can local distance impede the speech of one angel to another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0061","text":"[I q. 107 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32911)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0062","text":"I answer that it must be said that the speech of an angel consists in an intellectual operation, as is clear from what has been said. But an angel's intellectual operation is altogether abstracted from place and time; for even our intellectual operation is through abstraction from the here and now, except accidentally on the part of phantasms, of which there are none in angels. But in that which is altogether abstracted from place and time, neither diversity of time nor distance of place has any effect. Hence, in the speech of an angel, distance of place causes no impediment.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0063","text":"[I q. 107 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32912)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0064","text":"Therefore, to the first [objection] it must be said that the speech of an angel, as has been said, is interior speech, which nevertheless is perceived by another; and therefore it is in the speaking angel, and consequently where the speaking angel is. But just as local distance does not prevent one angel from being able to see another, so neither does it prevent him from perceiving that which in the other is ordered toward him, which is to perceive his speech.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0065","text":"[I q. 107 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32913)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0066","text":"To the second, it must be said that that cry is not one of bodily voice, which is made on account of distance of place; rather, it signifies the greatness of the thing that was being said, or the greatness of affection, according as Gregory says in *Morals*, II: *the less each person desires, the less he cries out*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0067","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0068","text":"[I q. 107 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32914)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0069","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth [article]. It seems that all know the speech of one angel to another. For the unequal distance of place is what causes not everyone to hear one man's speech. But local distance has no effect in angelic speech, as has been said. Therefore, when one angel speaks to another, all perceive it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0070","text":"[I q. 107 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32915)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0071","text":"Further, all angels share in the power of understanding. Therefore, if the concept of one mind ordered toward another is known by one, by equal reasoning it is known by the others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0072","text":"[I q. 107 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32916)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0073","text":"Moreover, illumination is a certain species of speech. But the illumination of one Angel by another reaches all the Angels, because, as Dionysius says in chapter 15 of the *Celestial Hierarchy*, *each celestial essence communicates to others the understanding transmitted to it*. Therefore the speech of one Angel to another is also conveyed to all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0074","text":"[I q. 107 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32917)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0075","text":"On the contrary, one man can speak to another alone. Therefore, much more can this be the case among Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0076","text":"[I q. 107 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32918)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0077","text":"I answer that, as was said above, the concept of the mind of one Angel can be perceived by another, through the fact that he whose concept it is directs it, by his will, to the other. Now for some reason something can be directed to one and not to another. And therefore the concept of one can be known by one and not by others. Thus one can perceive the speech of one Angel to another without the others doing so, not indeed because of an impediment of local distance, but because voluntary direction produces this, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0078","text":"[I q. 107 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32919)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0079","text":"Hence the answer to the first and second is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0080","text":"[I q. 107 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32920)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0081","text":"To the third, it must be said that illumination concerns those things that emanate from the first rule of truth, which is the common principle of all the Angels; and therefore illuminations are common to all. But speech can concern those things that are directed to the principle of created will, which is proper to each Angel; and therefore it is not necessary that speech of this sort be common to all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC_P_99:P0081"]}],"source_digest":"6b3ce2915cbfd02b14586db15681733b05770e4e20a267c580a52c1ec85a7f4f","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 107"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 107","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100","title":"First Part · Question 108","order":109,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC__100","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 108 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32921)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de ordinatione Angelorum secundum hierarchias et ordines, dictum est enim quod superiores inferiores illuminant, et non e converso. Et circa hoc quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum omnes Angeli sint unius hierarchiae. Secundo, utrum in una hierarchia sit unus tantum ordo. Tertio, utrum in uno ordine sint plures Angeli. Quarto, utrum distinctio hierarchiarum et ordinum sit a natura. Quinto, de nominibus et proprietatibus singulorum ordinum. Sexto, de comparatione ordinum ad invicem. Septimo, utrum ordines durent post diem iudicii. Octavo, utrum homines assumantur ad ordines Angelorum.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 108 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32922)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod omnes Angeli sint unius hierarchiae. Cum enim Angeli sint supremi inter creaturas, oportet dicere quod sint optime dispositi. Sed optima dispositio est multitudinis secundum quod continetur sub uno principatu; ut patet per philosophum, XII Metaphys., et in III Politic. Cum ergo hierarchia nihil sit aliud quam sacer principatus, videtur quod omnes Angeli sint unius hierarchiae.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32923)\n\nPraeterea, Dionysius dicit, in III cap. Cael. Hier., quod *hierarchia est ordo, scientia et actio*. Sed omnes Angeli conveniunt in uno ordine ad Deum, quem cognoscunt, et a quo in suis actionibus regulantur. Ergo omnes Angeli sunt unius hierarchiae.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32924)\n\nPraeterea, sacer principatus, qui dicitur hierarchia, invenitur in hominibus et Angelis. Sed omnes homines sunt unius hierarchiae. Ergo etiam omnes Angeli sunt unius hierarchiae.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32925)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius, VI cap. Cael. Hier., distinguit tres hierarchias Angelorum.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32926)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod hierarchia est sacer principatus, ut dictum est. In nomine autem principatus duo intelliguntur, scilicet ipse princeps, et multitudo ordinata sub principe. Quia igitur unus est Deus princeps non solum omnium Angelorum, sed etiam hominum, et totius creaturae; ideo non solum omnium Angelorum, sed etiam totius rationalis creaturae, quae sacrorum particeps esse potest, una est hierarchia, secundum quod Augustinus dicit, in XII de Civ. Dei *duas esse civitates, hoc est societates, unam in bonis Angelis et hominibus, alteram in malis*. Sed si consideretur principatus ex parte multitudinis ordinatae sub principe, sic unus principatus dicitur secundum quod multitudo uno et eodem modo potest gubernationem principis recipere. Quae vero non possunt secundum eundem modum gubernari a principe, ad diversos principatus pertinent, sicut sub uno rege sunt diversae civitates, quae diversis reguntur legibus et ministris. Manifestum est autem quod homines alio modo divinas illuminationes percipiunt quam Angeli, nam Angeli percipiunt eas in intelligibili puritate, homines vero percipiunt eas sub sensibilium similitudinibus, ut Dionysius dicit I cap. Cael. Hier. Et ideo oportuit distingui humanam hierarchiam ab angelica. Et per eundem modum in Angelis tres hierarchiae distinguuntur. Dictum est enim supra, dum de cognitione Angelorum ageretur, quod superiores Angeli habent universaliorem cognitionem veritatis quam inferiores. Huiusmodi autem universalis acceptio cognitionis secundum tres gradus in Angelis distingui potest. Possunt enim rationes rerum de quibus Angeli illuminantur, considerari tripliciter. Primo quidem, secundum quod procedunt a primo principio universali, quod est Deus, et iste modus convenit primae hierarchiae, quae immediate ad Deum extenditur, et quasi in vestibulis Dei collocatur, ut Dionysius dicit VII cap. Cael. Hier. Secundo vero, prout huiusmodi rationes dependent ab universalibus causis creatis, quae iam aliquo modo multiplicantur, et hic modus convenit secundae hierarchiae. Tertio autem modo, secundum quod huiusmodi rationes applicantur singulis rebus, et prout dependent a propriis causis, et hic modus convenit infimae hierarchiae. Quod plenius patebit, cum de singulis ordinibus agetur. Sic igitur distinguuntur hierarchiae ex parte multitudinis subiectae. Unde manifestum est eos errare, et contra intentionem Dionysii loqui, qui ponunt in divinis personis hierarchiam quam vocant supercaelestem. In divinis enim personis est quidam ordo naturae, sed non hierarchiae. Nam, ut Dionysius dicit III cap. Cael. Hier., *ordo hierarchiae est alios quidem purgari et illuminari et perfici, alios autem purgare et illuminare et perficere*. Quod absit ut in divinis personis ponamus.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32927)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ratio illa procedit de principatu ex parte principis, quia optimum est quod multitudo regatur ab uno principe, ut philosophus in praedictis locis intendit.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32928)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, quantum ad cognitionem ipsius Dei, quem omnes uno modo, scilicet per essentiam, vident, non distinguuntur in Angelis hierarchiae, sed quantum ad rationes rerum creatarum, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32929)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod omnes homines sunt unius speciei, et unus modus intelligendi est eis connaturalis, non sic autem est in Angelis. Unde non est similis ratio.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 108 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32930)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in una hierarchia non sint plures ordines. Multiplicata enim definitione, multiplicatur et definitum. Sed hierarchia, ut Dionysius dicit, est ordo. Si ergo sunt multi ordines, non erit una hierarchia, sed multae.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32931)\n\nPraeterea, diversi ordines sunt diversi gradus. Sed gradus in spiritualibus constituuntur secundum diversa dona spiritualia. Sed in Angelis omnia dona spiritualia sunt communia, quia nihil ibi singulariter possidetur. Ergo non sunt diversi ordines Angelorum.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32932)\n\nPraeterea, in ecclesiastica hierarchia distinguuntur ordines secundum purgare, illuminare et perficere, nam ordo diaconorum est purgativus, sacerdotum illuminativus, episcoporum perfectivus, ut Dionysius dicit V cap. Eccles. Hier. Sed quilibet Angelus purgat, illuminat et perficit. Non ergo est distinctio ordinum in Angelis.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32933)\n\nSed contra est quod apostolus dicit ad Ephes. I, quod Deus constituit Christum hominem supra *omnem principatum et potestatem et virtutem et dominationem*; qui sunt diversi ordines Angelorum, et quidam eorum ad unam hierarchiam pertinent, ut infra patebit.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32934)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, una hierarchia est unus principatus, idest una multitudo ordinata uno modo sub principis gubernatione. Non autem esset multitudo ordinata, sed confusa, si in multitudine diversi ordines non essent. Ipsa ergo ratio hierarchiae requirit ordinum diversitatem. Quae quidem diversitas ordinum secundum diversa officia et actus consideratur. Sicut patet quod in una civitate sunt diversi ordines secundum diversos actus, nam alius est ordo iudicantium, alius pugnantium, alius laborantium in agris, et sic de aliis. Sed quamvis multi sint unius civitatis ordines, omnes tamen ad tres possunt reduci, secundum quod quaelibet multitudo perfecta habet principium, medium et finem. Unde et in civitatibus triplex ordo hominum invenitur, quidam enim sunt supremi, ut optimates; quidam autem sunt infimi, ut vilis populus; quidam autem sunt medii, ut populus honorabilis. Sic igitur et in qualibet hierarchia angelica ordines distinguuntur secundum diversos actus et officia; et omnis ista diversitas ad tria reducitur, scilicet ad summum, medium et infimum. Et propter hoc in qualibet hierarchia Dionysius ponit tres ordines.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32935)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ordo dupliciter dicitur. Uno modo, ipsa ordinatio comprehendens sub se diversos gradus, et hoc modo hierarchia dicitur ordo. Alio modo dicitur ordo gradus unus, et sic dicuntur plures ordines unius hierarchiae.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32936)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod in societate Angelorum omnia possidentur communiter; sed tamen quaedam excellentius habentur a quibusdam quam ab aliis. Unumquodque autem perfectius habetur ab eo qui potest illud communicare, quam ab eo qui non potest, sicut perfectius est calidum quod potest calefacere, quam quod non potest; et perfectius scit qui potest docere, quam qui non potest. Et quanto perfectius donum aliquis communicare potest, tanto in perfectiori gradu est, sicut in perfectiori gradu magisterii est qui potest docere altiorem scientiam. Et secundum hanc similitudinem consideranda est diversitas graduum vel ordinum in Angelis, secundum diversa officia et actus.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32937)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod inferior Angelus est superior supremo homine nostrae hierarchiae; secundum illud Matth. XI, *qui minor est in regno caelorum, maior est illo*, scilicet Ioanne Baptista, quo nullus maior inter natos mulierum surrexit. Unde minor Angelus caelestis hierarchiae potest non solum purgare sed illuminare et perficere, et altiori modo quam ordines nostrae hierarchiae. Et sic secundum distinctionem harum actionum non distinguuntur caelestes ordines; sed secundum alias differentias actionum.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 108 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32938)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod in uno ordine non sint plures Angeli. Dictum est enim supra omnes Angelos inaequales esse ad invicem. Sed unius ordinis esse dicuntur quae sunt aequalia. Ergo plures Angeli non sunt unius ordinis.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32939)\n\nPraeterea, quod potest sufficienter fieri per unum, superfluum est quod fiat per multa. Sed illud quod pertinet ad unum officium angelicum, sufficienter potest fieri per unum Angelum; multo magis quam per unum solem sufficienter fit quod pertinet ad officium solis, quanto perfectior est Angelus caelesti corpore. Si ergo ordines distinguuntur secundum officia, ut dictum est, superfluum est quod sint plures Angeli unius ordinis.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32940)\n\nPraeterea, supra dictum est quod omnes Angeli sunt inaequales. Si ergo plures Angeli sint unius ordinis, puta tres vel quatuor, infimus superioris ordinis magis conveniet cum supremo inferioris quam cum supremo sui ordinis. Et sic non videtur quod magis sit unius ordinis cum hoc, quam cum illo. Non igitur sunt plures Angeli unius ordinis.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32941)\n\nSed contra est quod Isaiae VI dicitur, quod Seraphim clamabant alter ad alterum. Sunt ergo plures Angeli in uno ordine Seraphim.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32942)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ille qui perfecte cognoscit res aliquas, potest usque ad minima et actus et virtutes et naturas earum distinguere. Qui autem cognoscit eas imperfecte, non potest distinguere nisi in universali, quae quidem distinctio fit per pauciora. Sicut qui imperfecte cognoscit res naturales, distinguit earum ordines in universali, ponens in uno ordine caelestia corpora, in alio corpora inferiora inanimata, in alio plantas, in alio animalia, qui autem perfectius cognosceret res naturales, posset distinguere et in ipsis corporibus caelestibus diversos ordines, et in singulis aliorum. Nos autem imperfecte Angelos cognoscimus, et eorum officia, ut Dionysius dicit VI cap. Cael. Hier. Unde non possumus distinguere officia et ordines Angelorum, nisi in communi; secundum quem modum, multi Angeli sub uno ordine continentur. Si autem perfecte cognosceremus officia Angelorum, et eorum distinctiones, perfecte sciremus quod quilibet Angelus habet suum proprium officium et suum proprium ordinem in rebus, multo magis quam quaelibet stella, etsi nos lateat.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32943)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod omnes Angeli unius ordinis sunt aliquo modo aequales, quantum ad communem similitudinem secundum quam constituuntur in uno ordine, sed simpliciter non sunt aequales. Unde Dionysius dicit, X cap. Cael. Hier., quod in uno et eodem ordine Angelorum, est accipere primos, medios et ultimos.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32944)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod illa specialis distinctio ordinum et officiorum secundum quam quilibet Angelus habet proprium officium et ordinem, est nobis ignota.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32945)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut in superficie quae partim est alba et partim nigra, duae partes quae sunt in confinio albi et nigri, magis conveniunt secundum situm quam aliquae duae partes albae, minus tamen secundum qualitatem; ita duo Angeli qui sunt in terminis duorum ordinum, magis secum conveniunt secundum propinquitatem naturae, quam unus eorum cum aliquibus aliis sui ordinis; minus autem secundum idoneitatem ad similia officia, quae quidem idoneitas usque ad aliquem certum terminum protenditur.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 108 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32946)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod distinctio hierarchiarum et ordinum non sit a natura in Angelis. Hierarchia enim dicitur sacer principatus, et in definitione eius Dionysius ponit quod *deiforme, quantum possibile est, similat*. Sed sanctitas et deiformitas est in Angelis per gratiam, non per naturam. Ergo distinctio hierarchiarum et ordinum in Angelis est per gratiam, non per naturam.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32947)\n\nPraeterea, Seraphim dicuntur ardentes, vel incendentes, ut Dionysius dicit VII cap. Cael. Hier. Hoc autem videtur ad caritatem pertinere, quae non est a natura, sed a gratia, *diffunditur enim in cordibus nostris per spiritum sanctum, qui datus est nobis*, ut dicitur ad Rom. V. *Quod non solum ad sanctos homines pertinet, sed etiam de sanctis Angelis dici potest*, ut Augustinus dicit XII de Civ. Dei. Ergo ordines in Angelis non sunt a natura, sed a gratia.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32948)\n\nPraeterea, hierarchia ecclesiastica exemplatur a caelesti. Sed ordines in hominibus non sunt per naturam, sed per donum gratiae, non enim est a natura quod unus est episcopus, et alius est sacerdos, et alius diaconus. Ergo neque in Angelis sunt ordines a natura, sed a gratia tantum.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32949)\n\nSed contra est quod Magister dicit, IX dist. II Sent., quod *ordo Angelorum dicitur multitudo caelestium spirituum, qui inter se aliquo munere gratiae similantur, sicut et naturalium datorum participatione conveniunt*. Distinctio ergo ordinum in Angelis est non solum secundum dona gratuita, sed etiam secundum dona naturalia.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32950)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ordo gubernationis, qui est ordo multitudinis sub principatu existentis, attenditur per respectum ad finem. Finis autem Angelorum potest accipi dupliciter. Uno modo, secundum facultatem suae naturae, ut scilicet cognoscant et ament Deum naturali cognitione et amore. Et secundum respectum ad hunc finem, distinguuntur ordines Angelorum secundum naturalia dona. Alio modo potest accipi finis angelicae multitudinis supra naturalem facultatem eorum, qui consistit in visione divinae essentiae, et in immobili fruitione bonitatis ipsius; ad quem finem pertingere non possunt nisi per gratiam. Unde secundum respectum ad hunc finem, ordines distinguuntur in Angelis completive quidem secundum dona gratuita, dispositive autem secundum dona naturalia, quia Angelis data sunt dona gratuita secundum capacitatem naturalium, quod non est in hominibus, ut supra dictum est. Unde in hominibus distinguuntur ordines secundum dona gratuita tantum, et non secundum naturam.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 4 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32951)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ad obiecta.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 108 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32952)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ordines Angelorum non convenienter nominentur. Omnes enim caelestes spiritus dicuntur et Angeli et virtutes caelestes. Sed nomina communia inconvenienter aliquibus appropriantur. Ergo inconvenienter nominatur unus ordo Angelorum, et alius virtutum.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32953)\n\nPraeterea, esse dominum est proprium Dei; secundum illud Psal. XCIX *scitote quoniam dominus ipse est Deus*. Ergo inconvenienter unus ordo caelestium spirituum dominationes vocatur.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32954)\n\nPraeterea, nomen dominationis ad gubernationem pertinere videtur. Similiter autem et nomen principatuum, et potestatum. Inconvenienter ergo tribus ordinibus haec tria nomina imponuntur.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32955)\n\nPraeterea, Archangeli dicuntur quasi principes Angeli. Non ergo hoc nomen debet imponi alii ordini quam ordini principatuum.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 5 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32956)\n\nPraeterea, nomen Seraphim imponitur ab ardore qui ad caritatem pertinet, nomen autem Cherubim imponitur a scientia. Caritas autem et scientia sunt dona communia omnibus Angelis. Non ergo debent esse nomina specialium ordinum.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 5 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32957)\n\nPraeterea, throni dicuntur sedes. Sed ex hoc ipso Deus in creatura rationali sedere dicitur, quod ipsum cognoscit et amat. Non ergo debet esse alius ordo thronorum ab ordine Cherubim et Seraphim. Sic igitur videtur quod inconvenienter ordines Angelorum nominentur.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32958)\n\nSed contra est auctoritas sacrae Scripturae, quae sic eos nominat. Nomen enim Seraphim ponitur Isaiae VI; nomen Cherubim Ezech. I; nomen thronorum, Coloss. I; dominationes autem et virtutes et potestates et principatus ponuntur Ephes. I; nomen autem Archangeli ponitur in canonica Iudae, nomina autem Angelorum in pluribus Scripturae locis.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32959)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in nominatione angelicorum ordinum, considerare oportet quod *propria nomina singulorum ordinum proprietates eorum designant*, ut Dionysius dicit VII cap. Cael. Hier. Ad videndum autem quae sit proprietas cuiuslibet ordinis, considerare oportet quod in rebus ordinatis tripliciter aliquid esse contingit, scilicet per proprietatem, per excessum, et per participationem. Per proprietatem autem dicitur esse aliquid in re aliqua, quod adaequatur et proportionatur naturae ipsius. Per excessum autem, quando illud quod attribuitur alicui, est minus quam res cui attribuitur, sed tamen convenit illi rei per quendam excessum; sicut dictum est de omnibus nominibus quae attribuuntur Deo. Per participationem autem, quando illud quod attribuitur alicui, non plenarie invenitur in eo, sed deficienter; sicut sancti homines participative dicuntur dii. Si ergo aliquid nominari debeat nomine designante proprietatem ipsius, non debet nominari ab eo quod imperfecte participat, neque ab eo quod excedenter habet; sed ab eo quod est sibi quasi coaequatum. Sicut si quis velit proprie nominare hominem, dicet eum substantiam rationalem, non autem substantiam intellectualem, quod est proprium nomen Angeli, quia simplex intelligentia convenit Angelo per proprietatem, homini vero per participationem; neque substantiam sensibilem, quod est nomen bruti proprium, quia sensus est minus quam id quod est proprium homini, et convenit homini excedenter prae aliis animalibus. Sic igitur considerandum est in ordinibus Angelorum, quod omnes spirituales perfectiones sunt omnibus Angelis communes et quod omnes abundantius existunt in superioribus quam in inferioribus. Sed cum in ipsis etiam perfectionibus sit quidam gradus, superior perfectio attribuitur superiori ordini per proprietatem, inferiori vero per participationem, e converso autem inferior attribuitur inferiori per proprietatem, superiori autem per excessum. Et ita superior ordo a superiori perfectione nominatur. Sic igitur Dionysius exponit ordinum nomina secundum convenientiam ad spirituales perfectiones eorum. Gregorius vero, in expositione horum nominum, magis attendere videtur exteriora ministeria. Dicit enim, quod *Angeli dicuntur qui minima nuntiant; Archangeli, qui summa; virtutes per quas miracula fiunt; potestates quibus adversae potestates repelluntur; principatus, qui ipsis bonis spiritibus praesunt*.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32960)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Angelus nuntius dicitur. Omnes ergo caelestes spiritus, inquantum sunt manifestatores divinorum, Angeli vocantur. Sed superiores Angeli habent quandam excellentiam in hac manifestatione, a qua superiores ordines nominantur. Infimus autem Angelorum ordo nullam excellentiam supra communem manifestationem addit, et ideo a simplici manifestatione nominatur. Et sic nomen commune remanet infimo ordini quasi proprium, ut dicit Dionysius V cap. Cael. Hier. Vel potest dici quod infimus ordo specialiter dicitur ordo Angelorum, quia immediate nobis annuntiant. Virtus autem dupliciter accipi potest. Uno modo, communiter, secundum quod est media inter essentiam et operationem, et sic omnes caelestes spiritus nominantur caelestes virtutes, sicut et caelestes essentiae. Alio modo, secundum quod importat quendam excessum fortitudinis, et sic est proprium nomen ordinis. Unde Dionysius dicit, VIII cap. Cael. Hier., quod nomen virtutum significat quandam virilem et inconcussam fortitudinem, primo quidem ad omnes operationes divinas eis convenientes; secundo, ad suscipiendum divina. Et ita significat quod sine aliquo timore aggrediuntur divina quae ad eos pertinent, quod videtur ad fortitudinem animi pertinere.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32961)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut dicit Dionysius XII cap. de Div. Nom., *dominatio laudatur in Deo singulariter per quendam excessum, sed per participationem, divina eloquia vocant dominos principaliores ornatus, per quos inferiores ex donis eius accipiunt*. Unde et Dionysius dicit in VIII cap. Cael. Hier., quod nomen dominationum primo quidem significat quandam libertatem, quae est a servili conditione et pedestri subiectione, sicut plebs subiicitur, et a tyrannica oppressione, quam interdum etiam maiores patiuntur. Secundo significat quandam rigidam et inflexibilem gubernationem, quae ad nullum servilem actum inclinatur, neque ad aliquem actum subiectorum vel oppressorum a tyrannis. Tertio significat appetitum et participationem veri dominii, quod est in Deo. Et similiter nomen cuiuslibet ordinis significat participationem eius quod est in Deo; sicut nomen virtutum significat participationem divinae virtutis; et sic de aliis.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32962)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod nomen dominationis, et potestatis, et principatus, diversimode ad gubernationem pertinet. Nam domini est solummodo praecipere de agendis. Et ideo Gregorius dicit quod *quaedam Angelorum agmina, pro eo quod eis cetera ad obediendum subiecta sunt, dominationes vocantur*. Nomen vero potestatis ordinationem quandam designat; secundum illud apostoli ad Rom. XIII, *qui potestati resistit, Dei ordinationi resistit*. Et ideo Dionysius dicit quod nomen potestatis significat quandam ordinationem et circa susceptionem divinorum, et circa actiones divinas quas superiores in inferiores agunt, eas sursum ducendo. Ad ordinem ergo potestatum pertinet ordinare quae a subditis sint agenda. Principari vero, ut Gregorius dicit, est inter reliquos priorem existere, quasi primi sint in executione eorum quae imperantur. Et ideo Dionysius dicit, IX cap. Cael. Hier., quod nomen principatuum significat ductivum cum ordine sacro. Illi enim qui alios ducunt, primi inter eos existentes, principes proprie vocantur secundum illud Psalmi LXVII, *praevenerunt principes coniuncti psallentibus*.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32963)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod Archangeli, secundum Dionysium, medii sunt inter principatus et Angelos. Medium autem comparatum uni extremo, videtur alterum, inquantum participat naturam utriusque, sicut tepidum respectu calidi est frigidum, respectu vero frigidi est calidum. Sic et Archangeli dicuntur quasi principes Angeli, quia respectu Angelorum sunt principes, respectu vero principatuum sunt Angeli. Secundum Gregorium autem, dicuntur Archangeli ex eo quod principantur soli ordini Angelorum, quasi magna nuntiantes. Principatus autem dicuntur ex eo quod principantur omnibus caelestibus virtutibus divinas iussiones explentibus.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 5 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32964)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod nomen Seraphim non imponitur tantum a caritate, sed a caritatis excessu, quem importat nomen ardoris vel incendii. Unde Dionysius, VII cap. Cael. Hier., exponit nomen Seraphim secundum proprietates ignis, in quo est excessus caliditatis. In igne autem tria possumus considerare. Primo quidem, motum, qui est sursum, et qui est continuus. Per quod significatur quod indeclinabiliter moventur in Deum. Secundo vero, virtutem activam eius, quae est calidum. Quod quidem non simpliciter invenitur in igne, sed cum quadam acuitate, quia maxime est penetrativus in agendo, et pertingit usque ad minima; et iterum cum quodam superexcedenti fervore. Et per hoc significatur actio huiusmodi Angelorum, quam in subditos potenter exercent, eos in similem fervorem excitantes, et totaliter eos per incendium purgantes. Tertio consideratur in igne claritas eius. Et hoc significat quod huiusmodi Angeli in seipsis habent inextinguibilem lucem, et quod alios perfecte illuminant. Similiter etiam nomen Cherubim imponitur a quodam excessu scientiae, unde interpretatur plenitudo scientiae. Quod Dionysius exponit quantum ad quatuor, primo quidem, quantum ad perfectam Dei visionem; secundo, quantum ad plenam susceptionem divini luminis; tertio, quantum ad hoc, quod in ipso Deo contemplantur pulchritudinem ordinis rerum a Deo derivatam; quarto, quantum ad hoc, quod ipsi pleni existentes huiusmodi cognitione, eam copiose in alios effundunt.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 5 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32965)\n\nAd sextum dicendum quod ordo thronorum habet excellentiam prae inferioribus ordinibus in hoc, quod immediate in Deo rationes divinorum operum cognoscere possunt. Sed Cherubim habent excellentiam scientiae; Seraphim vero excellentiam ardoris. Et licet in his duabus excellentiis includatur tertia, non tamen in illa quae est thronorum, includuntur aliae duae. Et ideo ordo thronorum distinguitur ab ordine Cherubim et Seraphim. Hoc enim est commune in omnibus, quod excellentia inferioris continetur in excellentia superioris, et non e converso. Exponit autem Dionysius nomen thronorum, per convenientiam ad materiales sedes. In quibus est quatuor considerare. Primo quidem, situm, quia sedes supra terram elevantur. Et sic ipsi Angeli qui throni dicuntur, elevantur usque ad hoc, quod in Deo immediate rationes rerum cognoscant. Secundo in materialibus sedibus consideratur firmitas, quia in ipsis aliquis firmiter sedet. Hic autem est e converso, nam ipsi Angeli firmantur per Deum. Tertio, quia sedes suscipit sedentem, et in ea deferri potest. Sic et isti Angeli suscipiunt Deum in seipsis, et eum quodammodo ad inferiores ferunt. Quarto, ex figura, quia sedes ex una parte est aperta ad suscipiendum sedentem. Ita et isti Angeli sunt per promptitudinem aperti ad suscipiendum Deum, et famulandum ipsi.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 108 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32966)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod inconvenienter gradus ordinum assignentur. Ordo enim praelatorum videtur esse supremus. Sed dominationes, principatus et potestates ex ipsis nominibus praelationem quandam habent. Ergo isti ordines debent esse inter omnes supremi.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32967)\n\nPraeterea, quanto aliquis ordo est Deo propinquior, tanto est superior. Sed ordo thronorum videtur esse Deo propinquissimus, nihil enim coniungitur propinquius sedenti, quam sua sedes. Ergo ordo thronorum est altissimus.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32968)\n\nPraeterea, scientia est prior quam amor; et intellectus videtur esse altior quam voluntas. Ergo et ordo Cherubim videtur esse altior quam ordo Seraphim.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 6 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32969)\n\nPraeterea, Gregorius ponit principatus supra potestates. Non ergo collocantur immediate supra Archangelos, ut Dionysius dicit.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32970)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius ponit, in prima quidem hierarchia. Seraphim ut primos, Cherubim ut medios, thronos ut ultimos; in media vero, dominationes ut primos, virtutes ut medios, potestates ut ultimos; in ultima, principatus ut primos, Archangelos ut medios, Angelos ut ultimos.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32971)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod gradus angelicorum ordinum assignant et Gregorius et Dionysius, quantum ad alia quidem convenienter, sed quantum ad principatus et virtutes differenter. Nam Dionysius collocat virtutes sub dominationibus et supra potestates, principatus autem sub potestatibus et supra Archangelos, Gregorius autem ponit principatus in medio dominationum et potestatum, virtutes vero in medio potestatum et Archangelorum. Et utraque assignatio fulcimentum habere potest ex auctoritate apostoli. Qui, medios ordines ascendendo enumerans, dicit, Ephes. I, quod Deus constituit illum, scilicet Christum, ad dexteram suam in caelestibus, supra omnem principatum et potestatem et virtutem et dominationem, ubi virtutem ponit inter potestatem et dominationem, secundum assignationem Dionysii. Sed ad Coloss. I, enumerans eosdem ordines descendendo, dicit, sive throni, sive dominationes, sive principatus, sive potestates, omnia per ipsum et in ipso creata sunt, ubi principatus ponit medios inter dominationes et potestates, secundum assignationem Gregorii. Primo igitur videamus rationem assignationis Dionysii. In qua considerandum est quod, sicut supra dictum est, prima hierarchia accipit rationes rerum in ipso Deo; secunda vero in causis universalibus; tertia vero secundum determinationem ad speciales effectus. Et quia Deus est finis non solum angelicorum ministeriorum, sed etiam totius creaturae, ad primam hierarchiam pertinet consideratio finis; ad mediam vero dispositio universalis de agendis; ad ultimam autem applicatio dispositionis ad effectum, quae est operis executio; haec enim tria manifestum est in qualibet operatione inveniri. Et ideo Dionysius, ex nominibus ordinum proprietates illorum considerans, illos ordines in prima hierarchia posuit, quorum nomina imponuntur per respectum ad Deum, scilicet Seraphim et Cherubim et thronos. Illos vero ordines posuit in media hierarchia, quorum nomina designant communem quandam gubernationem sive dispositionem, scilicet dominationes, virtutes et potestates. Illos vero ordines posuit in tertia hierarchia, quorum nomina designant operis executionem, scilicet principatus, Angelos et Archangelos. In respectu autem ad finem, tria considerari possunt, nam primo, aliquis considerat finem; secundo vero, perfectam finis cognitionem accipit; tertio vero, intentionem suam in ipso defigit; quorum secundum ex additione se habet ad primum, et tertium ad utrumque. Et quia Deus est finis creaturarum sicut dux est finis exercitus, ut dicitur in XII Metaphys., potest aliquid simile huius ordinis considerari in rebus humanis, nam quidam sunt qui hoc habent dignitatis, ut per seipsos familiariter accedere possunt ad regem vel ducem; quidam vero super hoc habent, ut etiam secreta eius cognoscant; alii vero insuper circa ipsum semper inhaerent, quasi ei coniuncti. Et secundum hanc similitudinem accipere possumus dispositionem ordinum primae hierarchiae. Nam throni elevantur ad hoc, quod Deum familiariter in seipsis recipiant, secundum quod rationes rerum in ipso immediate cognoscere possunt, quod est proprium totius primae hierarchiae. Cherubim vero supereminenter divina secreta cognoscunt. Seraphim vero excellunt in hoc quod est omnium supremum, scilicet Deo ipsi uniri. Ut sic ab eo quod est commune toti hierarchiae, denominetur ordo thronorum; sicut ab eo quod est commune omnibus caelestibus spiritibus, denominatur ordo Angelorum. Ad gubernationis autem rationem tria pertinent. Quorum primum est definitio eorum quae agenda sunt, quod est proprium dominationum. Secundum autem est praebere facultatem ad implendum, quod pertinet ad virtutes. Tertium autem est ordinare qualiter ea quae praecepta vel definita sunt, impleri possint, ut aliqui exequantur, et hoc pertinet ad potestates. Executio autem angelicorum ministeriorum consistit in annuntiando divina. In executione autem cuiuslibet actus, sunt quidam quasi incipientes actionem et alios ducentes, sicut in cantu praecentores, et in bello illi qui alios ducunt et dirigunt, et hoc pertinet ad principatus. Alii vero sunt qui simpliciter exequuntur, et hoc pertinet ad Angelos. Alii vero medio modo se habent, quod ad Archangelos pertinet, ut supra dictum est. Invenitur autem congrua haec ordinum assignatio. Nam semper summum inferioris ordinis affinitatem habet cum ultimo superioris; sicut infima animalia parum distant a plantis. Primus autem ordo est divinarum personarum, qui terminatur ad spiritum sanctum, qui est amor procedens, cum quo affinitatem habet supremus ordo primae hierarchiae, ab incendio amoris denominatus. Infimus autem ordo primae hierarchiae est thronorum, qui ex suo nomine habent quandam affinitatem cum dominationibus, nam throni dicuntur, secundum Gregorium, per quos Deus sua iudicia exercet; accipiunt enim divinas illuminationes per convenientiam ad immediate illuminandum secundam hierarchiam, ad quam pertinet dispositio divinorum ministeriorum. Ordo vero potestatum affinitatem habet cum ordine principatuum, nam cum potestatum sit ordinationem subiectis imponere, haec ordinatio statim in nomine principatuum designatur, qui sunt primi in executione divinorum ministeriorum, utpote praesidentes gubernationi gentium et regnorum, quod est primum et praecipuum in divinis ministeriis; nam bonum gentis est divinius quam bonum unius hominis. Unde dicitur Dan. X, *princeps regni Persarum restitit mihi*. Dispositio etiam ordinum quam Gregorius ponit, congruitatem habet. Nam cum dominationes sint definientes et praecipientes ea quae ad divina ministeria pertinent, ordines eis subiecti disponuntur secundum dispositionem eorum in quos divina ministeria exercentur ut autem Augustinus dicit in III de Trin., *corpora quodam ordine reguntur, inferiora per superiora, et omnia per spiritualem creaturam; et spiritus malus per spiritum bonum*. Primus ergo ordo post dominationes dicitur principatuum, qui etiam bonis spiritibus principantur. Deinde potestates, per quas arcentur mali spiritus, sicut per potestates terrenas arcentur malefactores, ut habetur Rom. XIII. Post quas sunt virtutes, quae habent potestatem super corporalem naturam in operatione miraculorum. Post quas sunt Archangeli et Angeli, qui nuntiant hominibus vel magna, quae sunt supra rationem; vel parva, ad quae ratio se extendere potest.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32972)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in Angelis potius est quod subiiciuntur Deo, quam quod inferioribus praesident, et hoc derivatur ex illo. Et ideo ordines nominati a praelatione non sunt supremi, sed magis ordines nominati a conversione ad Deum.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32973)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod illa propinquitas ad Deum quae designatur nomine thronorum, convenit etiam Cherubim et Seraphim, et excellentius, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32974)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, cognitio est secundum quod cognita sunt in cognoscente; amor autem secundum quod amans unitur rei amatae. Superiora autem nobiliori modo sunt in seipsis quam in inferioribus, inferiora vero nobiliori modo in superioribus quam in seipsis. Et ideo inferiorum quidem cognitio praeeminet dilectioni, superiorum autem dilectio, et praecipue Dei, praeeminet cognitioni.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 6 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32975)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, si quis diligenter consideret dispositiones ordinum secundum Dionysium et Gregorium, parum vel nihil differunt, si ad rem referantur. Exponit enim Gregorius principatuum nomen ex hoc, quod bonis spiritibus praesunt, et hoc convenit virtutibus, secundum quod in nomine virtutum intelligitur quaedam fortitudo dans efficaciam inferioribus spiritibus ad exequenda divina ministeria. Rursus virtutes, secundum Gregorium, videntur esse idem quod principatus secundum Dionysium. Nam hoc est primum in divinis ministeriis, miracula facere, per hoc enim paratur via Annuntiationi Archangelorum et Angelorum.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 108 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32976)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ordines non remanebunt post diem iudicii. Dicit enim apostolus, I ad Cor. XV, quod Christus evacuabit omnem principatum et potestatem, cum tradiderit regnum Deo et patri, quod erit in ultima consummatione. Pari ergo ratione, in illo statu omnes alii ordines evacuabuntur.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32977)\n\nPraeterea, ad officium angelicorum ordinum pertinet purgare, illuminare et perficere. Sed post diem iudicii unus Angelus non purgabit aut illuminabit aut perficiet alium, quia non proficient amplius in scientia. Ergo frustra ordines angelici remanerent.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32978)\n\nPraeterea, apostolus dicit, ad Heb. I, de Angelis, quod *omnes sunt administratorii spiritus, in ministerium missi propter eos qui haereditatem capiunt salutis*, ex quo patet quod officia Angelorum ordinantur ad hoc, quod homines ad salutem adducantur. Sed omnes electi usque ad diem iudicii salutem consequuntur. Non ergo post diem iudicii remanebunt officia et ordines Angelorum.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32979)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Iudic. V, stellae manentes in ordine et cursu suo, quod exponitur de Angelis. Ergo Angeli semper in suis ordinibus remanebunt.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32980)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in ordinibus angelicis duo possunt considerari, scilicet distinctio graduum, et executio officiorum. Distinctio autem graduum est in Angelis secundum differentiam gratiae et naturae, ut supra dictum est. Et utraque differentia semper in Angelis remanebit. Non enim posset naturarum differentia ab eis auferri, nisi eis corruptis, differentia etiam gloriae erit in eis semper, secundum differentiam meriti praecedentis. Executio autem officiorum angelicorum aliquo modo remanebit post diem iudicii, et aliquo modo cessabit. Cessabit quidem, secundum quod eorum officia ordinantur ad perducendum aliquos ad finem, remanebit autem, secundum quod convenit in ultima finis consecutione. Sicut etiam alia sunt officia militarium ordinum in pugna, et in triumpho.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32981)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod principatus et potestates evacuabuntur in illa finali consummatione quantum ad hoc, quod alios ad finem perducant, quia consecuto iam fine, non est necessarium tendere in finem. Et haec ratio intelligitur ex verbis apostoli, dicentis, cum tradiderit regnum Deo et patri, idest, cum perduxerit fideles ad fruendum ipso Deo.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32982)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod actiones Angelorum super alios Angelos considerandae sunt secundum similitudinem actionum intelligibilium quae sunt in nobis. Inveniuntur autem in nobis multae intelligibiles actiones quae sunt ordinatae secundum ordinem causae et causati; sicut cum per multa media gradatim in unam conclusionem devenimus. Manifestum est autem quod cognitio conclusionis dependet ex omnibus mediis praecedentibus, non solum quantum ad novam acquisitionem scientiae, sed etiam quantum ad scientiae conservationem. Cuius signum est quod, si quis oblivisceretur aliquod praecedentium mediorum, opinionem quidem vel fidem de conclusione posset habere, sed non scientiam, ordine causarum ignorato. Sic igitur, cum inferiores Angeli rationes divinorum operum cognoscant per lumen superiorum Angelorum, dependet eorum cognitio ex lumine superiorum, non solum quantum ad novam acquisitionem scientiae, sed etiam quantum ad cognitionis conservationem. Licet ergo post iudicium non proficiant inferiores Angeli in cognitione aliquarum rerum, non tamen propter hoc excluditur quin a superioribus illuminentur.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32983)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, etsi post diem iudicii homines non sint ulterius ad salutem adducendi per ministerium Angelorum; tamen illi qui iam salutem erunt consecuti, aliquam illustrationem habebunt per Angelorum officia.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 108 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32984)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homines non assumantur ad ordines Angelorum. Hierarchia enim humana continetur sub infima hierarchiarum caelestium, sicut infima sub media, et media sub prima. Sed Angeli infimae hierarchiae nunquam transferentur in mediam aut in primam. Ergo neque homines transferentur ad ordines Angelorum.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32985)\n\nPraeterea, ordinibus Angelorum aliqua officia competunt, utpote custodire, miracula facere, Daemones arcere, et huiusmodi, quae non videntur convenire animabus sanctorum. Ergo non transferentur ad ordines Angelorum.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32986)\n\nPraeterea, sicut boni Angeli inducunt ad bonum, ita Daemones inducunt ad malum. Sed erroneum est dicere quod animae hominum malorum convertantur in Daemones, hoc enim Chrysostomus reprobat, super Matth. Ergo non videtur quod animae sanctorum transferantur ad ordines Angelorum.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32987)\n\nSed contra est quod dominus dicit, Matth. XXII, de sanctis, quod *erunt sicut Angeli Dei in caelo*.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32988)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, ordines Angelorum distinguuntur et secundum conditionem naturae, et secundum dona gratiae. Si ergo considerentur Angelorum ordines solum quantum ad gradum naturae, sic homines nullo modo assumi possunt ad ordines Angelorum, quia semper remanebit naturarum distinctio. Quam quidam considerantes, posuerunt quod nullo modo homines transferri possunt ad aequalitatem Angelorum. Quod est erroneum, repugnat enim promissioni Christi, dicentis, Lucae XX, quod filii resurrectionis erunt aequales Angelis in caelis. Illud enim quod est ex parte naturae, se habet ut materiale in ratione ordinis, completivum vero est quod est ex dono gratiae, quae dependet ex liberalitate Dei, non ex ordine naturae. Et ideo per donum gratiae homines mereri possunt tantam gloriam, ut Angelis aequentur secundum singulos Angelorum gradus. Quod est homines ad ordines Angelorum assumi. Quidam tamen dicunt quod ad ordines Angelorum non assumuntur omnes qui salvantur, sed soli virgines vel perfecti; alii vero suum ordinem constituent, quasi condivisum toti societati Angelorum. Sed hoc est contra Augustinum, qui dicit XII de Civ. Dei, quod non erunt duae societates hominum et Angelorum, sed una, quia omnium beatitudo est adhaerere uni Deo.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32989)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod gratia Angelis datur secundum proportionem naturalium; non autem sic est de hominibus, ut supra dictum est. Et ideo sicut inferiores Angeli non possunt transferri ad naturalem gradum superiorum, ita nec ad gratuitum. Homines vero possunt ad gratuitum conscendere, sed non ad naturalem.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32990)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Angeli, secundum naturae ordinem, medii sunt inter nos et Deum. Et ideo, secundum legem communem, per eos administrantur non solum res humanae, sed etiam omnia corporalia. Homines autem sancti, etiam post hanc vitam, sunt eiusdem naturae nobiscum. Unde secundum legem communem, non administrant humana, nec rebus vivorum intersunt, ut Augustinus dicit in libro de cura pro mortuis agenda. Ex quadam tamen speciali dispensatione interdum aliquibus sanctis conceditur, vel vivis vel mortuis, huiusmodi officia exercere, vel miracula faciendo, vel Daemones arcendo, vel aliquid huiusmodi; sicut Augustinus in eodem libro dicit.\n\n[I q. 108 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32991)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod homines ad poenam Daemonum transferri, non est erroneum, sed quidam erronee posuerunt Daemones nihil aliud esse quam animas defunctorum. Et hoc Chrysostomus reprobat.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0002","text":"[I q. 108 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32921)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de ordinatione Angelorum secundum hierarchias et ordines, dictum est enim quod superiores inferiores illuminant, et non e converso. Et circa hoc quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum omnes Angeli sint unius hierarchiae. Secundo, utrum in una hierarchia sit unus tantum ordo. Tertio, utrum in uno ordine sint plures Angeli. Quarto, utrum distinctio hierarchiarum et ordinum sit a natura. Quinto, de nominibus et proprietatibus singulorum ordinum. Sexto, de comparatione ordinum ad invicem. Septimo, utrum ordines durent post diem iudicii. Octavo, utrum homines assumantur ad ordines Angelorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0005","text":"[I q. 108 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32922)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod omnes Angeli sint unius hierarchiae. Cum enim Angeli sint supremi inter creaturas, oportet dicere quod sint optime dispositi. Sed optima dispositio est multitudinis secundum quod continetur sub uno principatu; ut patet per philosophum, XII Metaphys., et in III Politic. Cum ergo hierarchia nihil sit aliud quam sacer principatus, videtur quod omnes Angeli sint unius hierarchiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0007","text":"[I q. 108 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32923)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0008","text":"Praeterea, Dionysius dicit, in III cap. Cael. Hier., quod *hierarchia est ordo, scientia et actio*. Sed omnes Angeli conveniunt in uno ordine ad Deum, quem cognoscunt, et a quo in suis actionibus regulantur. Ergo omnes Angeli sunt unius hierarchiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0009","text":"[I q. 108 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32924)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0010","text":"Praeterea, sacer principatus, qui dicitur hierarchia, invenitur in hominibus et Angelis. Sed omnes homines sunt unius hierarchiae. Ergo etiam omnes Angeli sunt unius hierarchiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0011","text":"[I q. 108 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32925)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius, VI cap. Cael. Hier., distinguit tres hierarchias Angelorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0013","text":"[I q. 108 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32926)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod hierarchia est sacer principatus, ut dictum est. In nomine autem principatus duo intelliguntur, scilicet ipse princeps, et multitudo ordinata sub principe. Quia igitur unus est Deus princeps non solum omnium Angelorum, sed etiam hominum, et totius creaturae; ideo non solum omnium Angelorum, sed etiam totius rationalis creaturae, quae sacrorum particeps esse potest, una est hierarchia, secundum quod Augustinus dicit, in XII de Civ. Dei *duas esse civitates, hoc est societates, unam in bonis Angelis et hominibus, alteram in malis*. Sed si consideretur principatus ex parte multitudinis ordinatae sub principe, sic unus principatus dicitur secundum quod multitudo uno et eodem modo potest gubernationem principis recipere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0015","text":"Quae vero non possunt secundum eundem modum gubernari a principe, ad diversos principatus pertinent, sicut sub uno rege sunt diversae civitates, quae diversis reguntur legibus et ministris. Manifestum est autem quod homines alio modo divinas illuminationes percipiunt quam Angeli, nam Angeli percipiunt eas in intelligibili puritate, homines vero percipiunt eas sub sensibilium similitudinibus, ut Dionysius dicit I cap. Cael. Hier. Et ideo oportuit distingui humanam hierarchiam ab angelica. Et per eundem modum in Angelis tres hierarchiae distinguuntur. Dictum est enim supra, dum de cognitione Angelorum ageretur, quod superiores Angeli habent universaliorem cognitionem veritatis quam inferiores. Huiusmodi autem universalis acceptio cognitionis secundum tres gradus in Angelis distingui potest. Possunt enim rationes rerum de quibus Angeli illuminantur, considerari tripliciter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0016","text":"Primo quidem, secundum quod procedunt a primo principio universali, quod est Deus, et iste modus convenit primae hierarchiae, quae immediate ad Deum extenditur, et quasi in vestibulis Dei collocatur, ut Dionysius dicit VII cap. Cael. Hier. Secundo vero, prout huiusmodi rationes dependent ab universalibus causis creatis, quae iam aliquo modo multiplicantur, et hic modus convenit secundae hierarchiae. Tertio autem modo, secundum quod huiusmodi rationes applicantur singulis rebus, et prout dependent a propriis causis, et hic modus convenit infimae hierarchiae. Quod plenius patebit, cum de singulis ordinibus agetur. Sic igitur distinguuntur hierarchiae ex parte multitudinis subiectae. Unde manifestum est eos errare, et contra intentionem Dionysii loqui, qui ponunt in divinis personis hierarchiam quam vocant supercaelestem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0017","text":"In divinis enim personis est quidam ordo naturae, sed non hierarchiae. Nam, ut Dionysius dicit III cap. Cael. Hier., *ordo hierarchiae est alios quidem purgari et illuminari et perfici, alios autem purgare et illuminare et perficere*. Quod absit ut in divinis personis ponamus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0018","text":"[I q. 108 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32927)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0019","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ratio illa procedit de principatu ex parte principis, quia optimum est quod multitudo regatur ab uno principe, ut philosophus in praedictis locis intendit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0020","text":"[I q. 108 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32928)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0021","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, quantum ad cognitionem ipsius Dei, quem omnes uno modo, scilicet per essentiam, vident, non distinguuntur in Angelis hierarchiae, sed quantum ad rationes rerum creatarum, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0022","text":"[I q. 108 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32929)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0023","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod omnes homines sunt unius speciei, et unus modus intelligendi est eis connaturalis, non sic autem est in Angelis. Unde non est similis ratio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0024","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0025","text":"[I q. 108 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32930)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0026","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in una hierarchia non sint plures ordines. Multiplicata enim definitione, multiplicatur et definitum. Sed hierarchia, ut Dionysius dicit, est ordo. Si ergo sunt multi ordines, non erit una hierarchia, sed multae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0027","text":"[I q. 108 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32931)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0028","text":"Praeterea, diversi ordines sunt diversi gradus. Sed gradus in spiritualibus constituuntur secundum diversa dona spiritualia. Sed in Angelis omnia dona spiritualia sunt communia, quia nihil ibi singulariter possidetur. Ergo non sunt diversi ordines Angelorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0029","text":"[I q. 108 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32932)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0030","text":"Praeterea, in ecclesiastica hierarchia distinguuntur ordines secundum purgare, illuminare et perficere, nam ordo diaconorum est purgativus, sacerdotum illuminativus, episcoporum perfectivus, ut Dionysius dicit V cap. Eccles. Hier. Sed quilibet Angelus purgat, illuminat et perficit. Non ergo est distinctio ordinum in Angelis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0031","text":"[I q. 108 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32933)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0032","text":"Sed contra est quod apostolus dicit ad Ephes. I, quod Deus constituit Christum hominem supra *omnem principatum et potestatem et virtutem et dominationem*; qui sunt diversi ordines Angelorum, et quidam eorum ad unam hierarchiam pertinent, ut infra patebit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0033","text":"[I q. 108 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32934)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0034","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, una hierarchia est unus principatus, idest una multitudo ordinata uno modo sub principis gubernatione. Non autem esset multitudo ordinata, sed confusa, si in multitudine diversi ordines non essent. Ipsa ergo ratio hierarchiae requirit ordinum diversitatem. Quae quidem diversitas ordinum secundum diversa officia et actus consideratur. Sicut patet quod in una civitate sunt diversi ordines secundum diversos actus, nam alius est ordo iudicantium, alius pugnantium, alius laborantium in agris, et sic de aliis. Sed quamvis multi sint unius civitatis ordines, omnes tamen ad tres possunt reduci, secundum quod quaelibet multitudo perfecta habet principium, medium et finem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0035","text":"Unde et in civitatibus triplex ordo hominum invenitur, quidam enim sunt supremi, ut optimates; quidam autem sunt infimi, ut vilis populus; quidam autem sunt medii, ut populus honorabilis. Sic igitur et in qualibet hierarchia angelica ordines distinguuntur secundum diversos actus et officia; et omnis ista diversitas ad tria reducitur, scilicet ad summum, medium et infimum. Et propter hoc in qualibet hierarchia Dionysius ponit tres ordines.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0036","text":"[I q. 108 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32935)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0037","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ordo dupliciter dicitur. Uno modo, ipsa ordinatio comprehendens sub se diversos gradus, et hoc modo hierarchia dicitur ordo. Alio modo dicitur ordo gradus unus, et sic dicuntur plures ordines unius hierarchiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0038","text":"[I q. 108 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32936)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0039","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod in societate Angelorum omnia possidentur communiter; sed tamen quaedam excellentius habentur a quibusdam quam ab aliis. Unumquodque autem perfectius habetur ab eo qui potest illud communicare, quam ab eo qui non potest, sicut perfectius est calidum quod potest calefacere, quam quod non potest; et perfectius scit qui potest docere, quam qui non potest. Et quanto perfectius donum aliquis communicare potest, tanto in perfectiori gradu est, sicut in perfectiori gradu magisterii est qui potest docere altiorem scientiam. Et secundum hanc similitudinem consideranda est diversitas graduum vel ordinum in Angelis, secundum diversa officia et actus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0040","text":"[I q. 108 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32937)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0041","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod inferior Angelus est superior supremo homine nostrae hierarchiae; secundum illud Matth. XI, *qui minor est in regno caelorum, maior est illo*, scilicet Ioanne Baptista, quo nullus maior inter natos mulierum surrexit. Unde minor Angelus caelestis hierarchiae potest non solum purgare sed illuminare et perficere, et altiori modo quam ordines nostrae hierarchiae. Et sic secundum distinctionem harum actionum non distinguuntur caelestes ordines; sed secundum alias differentias actionum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0042","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0043","text":"[I q. 108 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32938)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0044","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod in uno ordine non sint plures Angeli. Dictum est enim supra omnes Angelos inaequales esse ad invicem. Sed unius ordinis esse dicuntur quae sunt aequalia. Ergo plures Angeli non sunt unius ordinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0045","text":"[I q. 108 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32939)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0046","text":"Praeterea, quod potest sufficienter fieri per unum, superfluum est quod fiat per multa. Sed illud quod pertinet ad unum officium angelicum, sufficienter potest fieri per unum Angelum; multo magis quam per unum solem sufficienter fit quod pertinet ad officium solis, quanto perfectior est Angelus caelesti corpore. Si ergo ordines distinguuntur secundum officia, ut dictum est, superfluum est quod sint plures Angeli unius ordinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0047","text":"[I q. 108 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32940)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0048","text":"Praeterea, supra dictum est quod omnes Angeli sunt inaequales. Si ergo plures Angeli sint unius ordinis, puta tres vel quatuor, infimus superioris ordinis magis conveniet cum supremo inferioris quam cum supremo sui ordinis. Et sic non videtur quod magis sit unius ordinis cum hoc, quam cum illo. Non igitur sunt plures Angeli unius ordinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0049","text":"[I q. 108 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32941)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0050","text":"Sed contra est quod Isaiae VI dicitur, quod Seraphim clamabant alter ad alterum. Sunt ergo plures Angeli in uno ordine Seraphim.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0051","text":"[I q. 108 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32942)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0052","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ille qui perfecte cognoscit res aliquas, potest usque ad minima et actus et virtutes et naturas earum distinguere. Qui autem cognoscit eas imperfecte, non potest distinguere nisi in universali, quae quidem distinctio fit per pauciora. Sicut qui imperfecte cognoscit res naturales, distinguit earum ordines in universali, ponens in uno ordine caelestia corpora, in alio corpora inferiora inanimata, in alio plantas, in alio animalia, qui autem perfectius cognosceret res naturales, posset distinguere et in ipsis corporibus caelestibus diversos ordines, et in singulis aliorum. Nos autem imperfecte Angelos cognoscimus, et eorum officia, ut Dionysius dicit VI cap. Cael. Hier. Unde non possumus distinguere officia et ordines Angelorum, nisi in communi; secundum quem modum, multi Angeli sub uno ordine continentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0053","text":"Si autem perfecte cognosceremus officia Angelorum, et eorum distinctiones, perfecte sciremus quod quilibet Angelus habet suum proprium officium et suum proprium ordinem in rebus, multo magis quam quaelibet stella, etsi nos lateat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0054","text":"[I q. 108 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32943)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0055","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod omnes Angeli unius ordinis sunt aliquo modo aequales, quantum ad communem similitudinem secundum quam constituuntur in uno ordine, sed simpliciter non sunt aequales. Unde Dionysius dicit, X cap. Cael. Hier., quod in uno et eodem ordine Angelorum, est accipere primos, medios et ultimos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0056","text":"[I q. 108 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32944)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0057","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod illa specialis distinctio ordinum et officiorum secundum quam quilibet Angelus habet proprium officium et ordinem, est nobis ignota.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0058","text":"[I q. 108 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32945)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0059","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut in superficie quae partim est alba et partim nigra, duae partes quae sunt in confinio albi et nigri, magis conveniunt secundum situm quam aliquae duae partes albae, minus tamen secundum qualitatem; ita duo Angeli qui sunt in terminis duorum ordinum, magis secum conveniunt secundum propinquitatem naturae, quam unus eorum cum aliquibus aliis sui ordinis; minus autem secundum idoneitatem ad similia officia, quae quidem idoneitas usque ad aliquem certum terminum protenditur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0060","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0061","text":"[I q. 108 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32946)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0062","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod distinctio hierarchiarum et ordinum non sit a natura in Angelis. Hierarchia enim dicitur sacer principatus, et in definitione eius Dionysius ponit quod *deiforme, quantum possibile est, similat*. Sed sanctitas et deiformitas est in Angelis per gratiam, non per naturam. Ergo distinctio hierarchiarum et ordinum in Angelis est per gratiam, non per naturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0063","text":"[I q. 108 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32947)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0064","text":"Praeterea, Seraphim dicuntur ardentes, vel incendentes, ut Dionysius dicit VII cap. Cael. Hier. Hoc autem videtur ad caritatem pertinere, quae non est a natura, sed a gratia, *diffunditur enim in cordibus nostris per spiritum sanctum, qui datus est nobis*, ut dicitur ad Rom. V. *Quod non solum ad sanctos homines pertinet, sed etiam de sanctis Angelis dici potest*, ut Augustinus dicit XII de Civ. Dei. Ergo ordines in Angelis non sunt a natura, sed a gratia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0065","text":"[I q. 108 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32948)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0066","text":"Praeterea, hierarchia ecclesiastica exemplatur a caelesti. Sed ordines in hominibus non sunt per naturam, sed per donum gratiae, non enim est a natura quod unus est episcopus, et alius est sacerdos, et alius diaconus. Ergo neque in Angelis sunt ordines a natura, sed a gratia tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0067","text":"[I q. 108 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32949)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0068","text":"Sed contra est quod Magister dicit, IX dist. II Sent., quod *ordo Angelorum dicitur multitudo caelestium spirituum, qui inter se aliquo munere gratiae similantur, sicut et naturalium datorum participatione conveniunt*. Distinctio ergo ordinum in Angelis est non solum secundum dona gratuita, sed etiam secundum dona naturalia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0069","text":"[I q. 108 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32950)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0070","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ordo gubernationis, qui est ordo multitudinis sub principatu existentis, attenditur per respectum ad finem. Finis autem Angelorum potest accipi dupliciter. Uno modo, secundum facultatem suae naturae, ut scilicet cognoscant et ament Deum naturali cognitione et amore. Et secundum respectum ad hunc finem, distinguuntur ordines Angelorum secundum naturalia dona. Alio modo potest accipi finis angelicae multitudinis supra naturalem facultatem eorum, qui consistit in visione divinae essentiae, et in immobili fruitione bonitatis ipsius; ad quem finem pertingere non possunt nisi per gratiam. Unde secundum respectum ad hunc finem, ordines distinguuntur in Angelis completive quidem secundum dona gratuita, dispositive autem secundum dona naturalia, quia Angelis data sunt dona gratuita secundum capacitatem naturalium, quod non est in hominibus, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0071","text":"Unde in hominibus distinguuntur ordines secundum dona gratuita tantum, et non secundum naturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0072","text":"[I q. 108 a. 4 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32951)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0073","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ad obiecta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0074","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0075","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32952)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0076","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ordines Angelorum non convenienter nominentur. Omnes enim caelestes spiritus dicuntur et Angeli et virtutes caelestes. Sed nomina communia inconvenienter aliquibus appropriantur. Ergo inconvenienter nominatur unus ordo Angelorum, et alius virtutum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0077","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32953)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0078","text":"Praeterea, esse dominum est proprium Dei; secundum illud Psal. XCIX *scitote quoniam dominus ipse est Deus*. Ergo inconvenienter unus ordo caelestium spirituum dominationes vocatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0079","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32954)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0080","text":"Praeterea, nomen dominationis ad gubernationem pertinere videtur. Similiter autem et nomen principatuum, et potestatum. Inconvenienter ergo tribus ordinibus haec tria nomina imponuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0081","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32955)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0082","text":"Praeterea, Archangeli dicuntur quasi principes Angeli. Non ergo hoc nomen debet imponi alii ordini quam ordini principatuum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0083","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32956)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0084","text":"Praeterea, nomen Seraphim imponitur ab ardore qui ad caritatem pertinet, nomen autem Cherubim imponitur a scientia. Caritas autem et scientia sunt dona communia omnibus Angelis. Non ergo debent esse nomina specialium ordinum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0085","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32957)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0086","text":"Praeterea, throni dicuntur sedes. Sed ex hoc ipso Deus in creatura rationali sedere dicitur, quod ipsum cognoscit et amat. Non ergo debet esse alius ordo thronorum ab ordine Cherubim et Seraphim. Sic igitur videtur quod inconvenienter ordines Angelorum nominentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0087","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32958)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0088","text":"Sed contra est auctoritas sacrae Scripturae, quae sic eos nominat. Nomen enim Seraphim ponitur Isaiae VI; nomen Cherubim Ezech. I; nomen thronorum, Coloss. I; dominationes autem et virtutes et potestates et principatus ponuntur Ephes. I; nomen autem Archangeli ponitur in canonica Iudae, nomina autem Angelorum in pluribus Scripturae locis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0089","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32959)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0090","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in nominatione angelicorum ordinum, considerare oportet quod *propria nomina singulorum ordinum proprietates eorum designant*, ut Dionysius dicit VII cap. Cael. Hier. Ad videndum autem quae sit proprietas cuiuslibet ordinis, considerare oportet quod in rebus ordinatis tripliciter aliquid esse contingit, scilicet per proprietatem, per excessum, et per participationem. Per proprietatem autem dicitur esse aliquid in re aliqua, quod adaequatur et proportionatur naturae ipsius. Per excessum autem, quando illud quod attribuitur alicui, est minus quam res cui attribuitur, sed tamen convenit illi rei per quendam excessum; sicut dictum est de omnibus nominibus quae attribuuntur Deo. Per participationem autem, quando illud quod attribuitur alicui, non plenarie invenitur in eo, sed deficienter; sicut sancti homines participative dicuntur dii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0091","text":"Si ergo aliquid nominari debeat nomine designante proprietatem ipsius, non debet nominari ab eo quod imperfecte participat, neque ab eo quod excedenter habet; sed ab eo quod est sibi quasi coaequatum. Sicut si quis velit proprie nominare hominem, dicet eum substantiam rationalem, non autem substantiam intellectualem, quod est proprium nomen Angeli, quia simplex intelligentia convenit Angelo per proprietatem, homini vero per participationem; neque substantiam sensibilem, quod est nomen bruti proprium, quia sensus est minus quam id quod est proprium homini, et convenit homini excedenter prae aliis animalibus. Sic igitur considerandum est in ordinibus Angelorum, quod omnes spirituales perfectiones sunt omnibus Angelis communes et quod omnes abundantius existunt in superioribus quam in inferioribus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0092","text":"Sed cum in ipsis etiam perfectionibus sit quidam gradus, superior perfectio attribuitur superiori ordini per proprietatem, inferiori vero per participationem, e converso autem inferior attribuitur inferiori per proprietatem, superiori autem per excessum. Et ita superior ordo a superiori perfectione nominatur. Sic igitur Dionysius exponit ordinum nomina secundum convenientiam ad spirituales perfectiones eorum. Gregorius vero, in expositione horum nominum, magis attendere videtur exteriora ministeria. Dicit enim, quod *Angeli dicuntur qui minima nuntiant; Archangeli, qui summa; virtutes per quas miracula fiunt; potestates quibus adversae potestates repelluntur; principatus, qui ipsis bonis spiritibus praesunt*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0093","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32960)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0094","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Angelus nuntius dicitur. Omnes ergo caelestes spiritus, inquantum sunt manifestatores divinorum, Angeli vocantur. Sed superiores Angeli habent quandam excellentiam in hac manifestatione, a qua superiores ordines nominantur. Infimus autem Angelorum ordo nullam excellentiam supra communem manifestationem addit, et ideo a simplici manifestatione nominatur. Et sic nomen commune remanet infimo ordini quasi proprium, ut dicit Dionysius V cap. Cael. Hier. Vel potest dici quod infimus ordo specialiter dicitur ordo Angelorum, quia immediate nobis annuntiant. Virtus autem dupliciter accipi potest. Uno modo, communiter, secundum quod est media inter essentiam et operationem, et sic omnes caelestes spiritus nominantur caelestes virtutes, sicut et caelestes essentiae. Alio modo, secundum quod importat quendam excessum fortitudinis, et sic est proprium nomen ordinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0095","text":"Unde Dionysius dicit, VIII cap. Cael. Hier., quod nomen virtutum significat quandam virilem et inconcussam fortitudinem, primo quidem ad omnes operationes divinas eis convenientes; secundo, ad suscipiendum divina. Et ita significat quod sine aliquo timore aggrediuntur divina quae ad eos pertinent, quod videtur ad fortitudinem animi pertinere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0096","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32961)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0097","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut dicit Dionysius XII cap. de Div. Nom., *dominatio laudatur in Deo singulariter per quendam excessum, sed per participationem, divina eloquia vocant dominos principaliores ornatus, per quos inferiores ex donis eius accipiunt*. Unde et Dionysius dicit in VIII cap. Cael. Hier., quod nomen dominationum primo quidem significat quandam libertatem, quae est a servili conditione et pedestri subiectione, sicut plebs subiicitur, et a tyrannica oppressione, quam interdum etiam maiores patiuntur. Secundo significat quandam rigidam et inflexibilem gubernationem, quae ad nullum servilem actum inclinatur, neque ad aliquem actum subiectorum vel oppressorum a tyrannis. Tertio significat appetitum et participationem veri dominii, quod est in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0098","text":"Et similiter nomen cuiuslibet ordinis significat participationem eius quod est in Deo; sicut nomen virtutum significat participationem divinae virtutis; et sic de aliis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0099","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32962)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0100","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod nomen dominationis, et potestatis, et principatus, diversimode ad gubernationem pertinet. Nam domini est solummodo praecipere de agendis. Et ideo Gregorius dicit quod *quaedam Angelorum agmina, pro eo quod eis cetera ad obediendum subiecta sunt, dominationes vocantur*. Nomen vero potestatis ordinationem quandam designat; secundum illud apostoli ad Rom. XIII, *qui potestati resistit, Dei ordinationi resistit*. Et ideo Dionysius dicit quod nomen potestatis significat quandam ordinationem et circa susceptionem divinorum, et circa actiones divinas quas superiores in inferiores agunt, eas sursum ducendo. Ad ordinem ergo potestatum pertinet ordinare quae a subditis sint agenda. Principari vero, ut Gregorius dicit, est inter reliquos priorem existere, quasi primi sint in executione eorum quae imperantur. Et ideo Dionysius dicit, IX cap. Cael.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0101","text":"Hier., quod nomen principatuum significat ductivum cum ordine sacro. Illi enim qui alios ducunt, primi inter eos existentes, principes proprie vocantur secundum illud Psalmi LXVII, *praevenerunt principes coniuncti psallentibus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0102","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32963)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0103","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod Archangeli, secundum Dionysium, medii sunt inter principatus et Angelos. Medium autem comparatum uni extremo, videtur alterum, inquantum participat naturam utriusque, sicut tepidum respectu calidi est frigidum, respectu vero frigidi est calidum. Sic et Archangeli dicuntur quasi principes Angeli, quia respectu Angelorum sunt principes, respectu vero principatuum sunt Angeli. Secundum Gregorium autem, dicuntur Archangeli ex eo quod principantur soli ordini Angelorum, quasi magna nuntiantes. Principatus autem dicuntur ex eo quod principantur omnibus caelestibus virtutibus divinas iussiones explentibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0104","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32964)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0105","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod nomen Seraphim non imponitur tantum a caritate, sed a caritatis excessu, quem importat nomen ardoris vel incendii. Unde Dionysius, VII cap. Cael. Hier., exponit nomen Seraphim secundum proprietates ignis, in quo est excessus caliditatis. In igne autem tria possumus considerare. Primo quidem, motum, qui est sursum, et qui est continuus. Per quod significatur quod indeclinabiliter moventur in Deum. Secundo vero, virtutem activam eius, quae est calidum. Quod quidem non simpliciter invenitur in igne, sed cum quadam acuitate, quia maxime est penetrativus in agendo, et pertingit usque ad minima; et iterum cum quodam superexcedenti fervore. Et per hoc significatur actio huiusmodi Angelorum, quam in subditos potenter exercent, eos in similem fervorem excitantes, et totaliter eos per incendium purgantes. Tertio consideratur in igne claritas eius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0106","text":"Et hoc significat quod huiusmodi Angeli in seipsis habent inextinguibilem lucem, et quod alios perfecte illuminant. Similiter etiam nomen Cherubim imponitur a quodam excessu scientiae, unde interpretatur plenitudo scientiae. Quod Dionysius exponit quantum ad quatuor, primo quidem, quantum ad perfectam Dei visionem; secundo, quantum ad plenam susceptionem divini luminis; tertio, quantum ad hoc, quod in ipso Deo contemplantur pulchritudinem ordinis rerum a Deo derivatam; quarto, quantum ad hoc, quod ipsi pleni existentes huiusmodi cognitione, eam copiose in alios effundunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0107","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32965)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0108","text":"Ad sextum dicendum quod ordo thronorum habet excellentiam prae inferioribus ordinibus in hoc, quod immediate in Deo rationes divinorum operum cognoscere possunt. Sed Cherubim habent excellentiam scientiae; Seraphim vero excellentiam ardoris. Et licet in his duabus excellentiis includatur tertia, non tamen in illa quae est thronorum, includuntur aliae duae. Et ideo ordo thronorum distinguitur ab ordine Cherubim et Seraphim. Hoc enim est commune in omnibus, quod excellentia inferioris continetur in excellentia superioris, et non e converso. Exponit autem Dionysius nomen thronorum, per convenientiam ad materiales sedes. In quibus est quatuor considerare. Primo quidem, situm, quia sedes supra terram elevantur. Et sic ipsi Angeli qui throni dicuntur, elevantur usque ad hoc, quod in Deo immediate rationes rerum cognoscant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0109","text":"Secundo in materialibus sedibus consideratur firmitas, quia in ipsis aliquis firmiter sedet. Hic autem est e converso, nam ipsi Angeli firmantur per Deum. Tertio, quia sedes suscipit sedentem, et in ea deferri potest. Sic et isti Angeli suscipiunt Deum in seipsis, et eum quodammodo ad inferiores ferunt. Quarto, ex figura, quia sedes ex una parte est aperta ad suscipiendum sedentem. Ita et isti Angeli sunt per promptitudinem aperti ad suscipiendum Deum, et famulandum ipsi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0110","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0111","text":"[I q. 108 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32966)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0112","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod inconvenienter gradus ordinum assignentur. Ordo enim praelatorum videtur esse supremus. Sed dominationes, principatus et potestates ex ipsis nominibus praelationem quandam habent. Ergo isti ordines debent esse inter omnes supremi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0113","text":"[I q. 108 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32967)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0114","text":"Praeterea, quanto aliquis ordo est Deo propinquior, tanto est superior. Sed ordo thronorum videtur esse Deo propinquissimus, nihil enim coniungitur propinquius sedenti, quam sua sedes. Ergo ordo thronorum est altissimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0115","text":"[I q. 108 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32968)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0116","text":"Praeterea, scientia est prior quam amor; et intellectus videtur esse altior quam voluntas. Ergo et ordo Cherubim videtur esse altior quam ordo Seraphim.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0117","text":"[I q. 108 a. 6 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32969)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0118","text":"Praeterea, Gregorius ponit principatus supra potestates. Non ergo collocantur immediate supra Archangelos, ut Dionysius dicit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0119","text":"[I q. 108 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32970)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0120","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius ponit, in prima quidem hierarchia. Seraphim ut primos, Cherubim ut medios, thronos ut ultimos; in media vero, dominationes ut primos, virtutes ut medios, potestates ut ultimos; in ultima, principatus ut primos, Archangelos ut medios, Angelos ut ultimos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0121","text":"[I q. 108 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32971)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0122","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod gradus angelicorum ordinum assignant et Gregorius et Dionysius, quantum ad alia quidem convenienter, sed quantum ad principatus et virtutes differenter. Nam Dionysius collocat virtutes sub dominationibus et supra potestates, principatus autem sub potestatibus et supra Archangelos, Gregorius autem ponit principatus in medio dominationum et potestatum, virtutes vero in medio potestatum et Archangelorum. Et utraque assignatio fulcimentum habere potest ex auctoritate apostoli. Qui, medios ordines ascendendo enumerans, dicit, Ephes. I, quod Deus constituit illum, scilicet Christum, ad dexteram suam in caelestibus, supra omnem principatum et potestatem et virtutem et dominationem, ubi virtutem ponit inter potestatem et dominationem, secundum assignationem Dionysii. Sed ad Coloss.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0123","text":"I, enumerans eosdem ordines descendendo, dicit, sive throni, sive dominationes, sive principatus, sive potestates, omnia per ipsum et in ipso creata sunt, ubi principatus ponit medios inter dominationes et potestates, secundum assignationem Gregorii. Primo igitur videamus rationem assignationis Dionysii. In qua considerandum est quod, sicut supra dictum est, prima hierarchia accipit rationes rerum in ipso Deo; secunda vero in causis universalibus; tertia vero secundum determinationem ad speciales effectus. Et quia Deus est finis non solum angelicorum ministeriorum, sed etiam totius creaturae, ad primam hierarchiam pertinet consideratio finis; ad mediam vero dispositio universalis de agendis; ad ultimam autem applicatio dispositionis ad effectum, quae est operis executio; haec enim tria manifestum est in qualibet operatione inveniri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0124","text":"Et ideo Dionysius, ex nominibus ordinum proprietates illorum considerans, illos ordines in prima hierarchia posuit, quorum nomina imponuntur per respectum ad Deum, scilicet Seraphim et Cherubim et thronos. Illos vero ordines posuit in media hierarchia, quorum nomina designant communem quandam gubernationem sive dispositionem, scilicet dominationes, virtutes et potestates. Illos vero ordines posuit in tertia hierarchia, quorum nomina designant operis executionem, scilicet principatus, Angelos et Archangelos. In respectu autem ad finem, tria considerari possunt, nam primo, aliquis considerat finem; secundo vero, perfectam finis cognitionem accipit; tertio vero, intentionem suam in ipso defigit; quorum secundum ex additione se habet ad primum, et tertium ad utrumque.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0125","text":"Et quia Deus est finis creaturarum sicut dux est finis exercitus, ut dicitur in XII Metaphys., potest aliquid simile huius ordinis considerari in rebus humanis, nam quidam sunt qui hoc habent dignitatis, ut per seipsos familiariter accedere possunt ad regem vel ducem; quidam vero super hoc habent, ut etiam secreta eius cognoscant; alii vero insuper circa ipsum semper inhaerent, quasi ei coniuncti. Et secundum hanc similitudinem accipere possumus dispositionem ordinum primae hierarchiae. Nam throni elevantur ad hoc, quod Deum familiariter in seipsis recipiant, secundum quod rationes rerum in ipso immediate cognoscere possunt, quod est proprium totius primae hierarchiae. Cherubim vero supereminenter divina secreta cognoscunt. Seraphim vero excellunt in hoc quod est omnium supremum, scilicet Deo ipsi uniri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0126","text":"Ut sic ab eo quod est commune toti hierarchiae, denominetur ordo thronorum; sicut ab eo quod est commune omnibus caelestibus spiritibus, denominatur ordo Angelorum. Ad gubernationis autem rationem tria pertinent. Quorum primum est definitio eorum quae agenda sunt, quod est proprium dominationum. Secundum autem est praebere facultatem ad implendum, quod pertinet ad virtutes. Tertium autem est ordinare qualiter ea quae praecepta vel definita sunt, impleri possint, ut aliqui exequantur, et hoc pertinet ad potestates. Executio autem angelicorum ministeriorum consistit in annuntiando divina. In executione autem cuiuslibet actus, sunt quidam quasi incipientes actionem et alios ducentes, sicut in cantu praecentores, et in bello illi qui alios ducunt et dirigunt, et hoc pertinet ad principatus. Alii vero sunt qui simpliciter exequuntur, et hoc pertinet ad Angelos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0127","text":"Alii vero medio modo se habent, quod ad Archangelos pertinet, ut supra dictum est. Invenitur autem congrua haec ordinum assignatio. Nam semper summum inferioris ordinis affinitatem habet cum ultimo superioris; sicut infima animalia parum distant a plantis. Primus autem ordo est divinarum personarum, qui terminatur ad spiritum sanctum, qui est amor procedens, cum quo affinitatem habet supremus ordo primae hierarchiae, ab incendio amoris denominatus. Infimus autem ordo primae hierarchiae est thronorum, qui ex suo nomine habent quandam affinitatem cum dominationibus, nam throni dicuntur, secundum Gregorium, per quos Deus sua iudicia exercet; accipiunt enim divinas illuminationes per convenientiam ad immediate illuminandum secundam hierarchiam, ad quam pertinet dispositio divinorum ministeriorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0128","text":"Ordo vero potestatum affinitatem habet cum ordine principatuum, nam cum potestatum sit ordinationem subiectis imponere, haec ordinatio statim in nomine principatuum designatur, qui sunt primi in executione divinorum ministeriorum, utpote praesidentes gubernationi gentium et regnorum, quod est primum et praecipuum in divinis ministeriis; nam bonum gentis est divinius quam bonum unius hominis. Unde dicitur Dan. X, *princeps regni Persarum restitit mihi*. Dispositio etiam ordinum quam Gregorius ponit, congruitatem habet. Nam cum dominationes sint definientes et praecipientes ea quae ad divina ministeria pertinent, ordines eis subiecti disponuntur secundum dispositionem eorum in quos divina ministeria exercentur ut autem Augustinus dicit in III de Trin., *corpora quodam ordine reguntur, inferiora per superiora, et omnia per spiritualem creaturam; et spiritus malus per spiritum bonum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0129","text":"Primus ergo ordo post dominationes dicitur principatuum, qui etiam bonis spiritibus principantur. Deinde potestates, per quas arcentur mali spiritus, sicut per potestates terrenas arcentur malefactores, ut habetur Rom. XIII. Post quas sunt virtutes, quae habent potestatem super corporalem naturam in operatione miraculorum. Post quas sunt Archangeli et Angeli, qui nuntiant hominibus vel magna, quae sunt supra rationem; vel parva, ad quae ratio se extendere potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0130","text":"[I q. 108 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32972)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0131","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in Angelis potius est quod subiiciuntur Deo, quam quod inferioribus praesident, et hoc derivatur ex illo. Et ideo ordines nominati a praelatione non sunt supremi, sed magis ordines nominati a conversione ad Deum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0132","text":"[I q. 108 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32973)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0133","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod illa propinquitas ad Deum quae designatur nomine thronorum, convenit etiam Cherubim et Seraphim, et excellentius, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0134","text":"[I q. 108 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32974)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0135","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, cognitio est secundum quod cognita sunt in cognoscente; amor autem secundum quod amans unitur rei amatae. Superiora autem nobiliori modo sunt in seipsis quam in inferioribus, inferiora vero nobiliori modo in superioribus quam in seipsis. Et ideo inferiorum quidem cognitio praeeminet dilectioni, superiorum autem dilectio, et praecipue Dei, praeeminet cognitioni.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0136","text":"[I q. 108 a. 6 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32975)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0137","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, si quis diligenter consideret dispositiones ordinum secundum Dionysium et Gregorium, parum vel nihil differunt, si ad rem referantur. Exponit enim Gregorius principatuum nomen ex hoc, quod bonis spiritibus praesunt, et hoc convenit virtutibus, secundum quod in nomine virtutum intelligitur quaedam fortitudo dans efficaciam inferioribus spiritibus ad exequenda divina ministeria. Rursus virtutes, secundum Gregorium, videntur esse idem quod principatus secundum Dionysium. Nam hoc est primum in divinis ministeriis, miracula facere, per hoc enim paratur via Annuntiationi Archangelorum et Angelorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0138","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0139","text":"[I q. 108 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32976)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0140","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ordines non remanebunt post diem iudicii. Dicit enim apostolus, I ad Cor. XV, quod Christus evacuabit omnem principatum et potestatem, cum tradiderit regnum Deo et patri, quod erit in ultima consummatione. Pari ergo ratione, in illo statu omnes alii ordines evacuabuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0141","text":"[I q. 108 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32977)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0142","text":"Praeterea, ad officium angelicorum ordinum pertinet purgare, illuminare et perficere. Sed post diem iudicii unus Angelus non purgabit aut illuminabit aut perficiet alium, quia non proficient amplius in scientia. Ergo frustra ordines angelici remanerent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0143","text":"[I q. 108 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32978)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0144","text":"Praeterea, apostolus dicit, ad Heb. I, de Angelis, quod *omnes sunt administratorii spiritus, in ministerium missi propter eos qui haereditatem capiunt salutis*, ex quo patet quod officia Angelorum ordinantur ad hoc, quod homines ad salutem adducantur. Sed omnes electi usque ad diem iudicii salutem consequuntur. Non ergo post diem iudicii remanebunt officia et ordines Angelorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0145","text":"[I q. 108 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32979)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0146","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Iudic. V, stellae manentes in ordine et cursu suo, quod exponitur de Angelis. Ergo Angeli semper in suis ordinibus remanebunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0147","text":"[I q. 108 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32980)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0148","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in ordinibus angelicis duo possunt considerari, scilicet distinctio graduum, et executio officiorum. Distinctio autem graduum est in Angelis secundum differentiam gratiae et naturae, ut supra dictum est. Et utraque differentia semper in Angelis remanebit. Non enim posset naturarum differentia ab eis auferri, nisi eis corruptis, differentia etiam gloriae erit in eis semper, secundum differentiam meriti praecedentis. Executio autem officiorum angelicorum aliquo modo remanebit post diem iudicii, et aliquo modo cessabit. Cessabit quidem, secundum quod eorum officia ordinantur ad perducendum aliquos ad finem, remanebit autem, secundum quod convenit in ultima finis consecutione. Sicut etiam alia sunt officia militarium ordinum in pugna, et in triumpho.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0149","text":"[I q. 108 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32981)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0150","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod principatus et potestates evacuabuntur in illa finali consummatione quantum ad hoc, quod alios ad finem perducant, quia consecuto iam fine, non est necessarium tendere in finem. Et haec ratio intelligitur ex verbis apostoli, dicentis, cum tradiderit regnum Deo et patri, idest, cum perduxerit fideles ad fruendum ipso Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0151","text":"[I q. 108 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32982)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0152","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod actiones Angelorum super alios Angelos considerandae sunt secundum similitudinem actionum intelligibilium quae sunt in nobis. Inveniuntur autem in nobis multae intelligibiles actiones quae sunt ordinatae secundum ordinem causae et causati; sicut cum per multa media gradatim in unam conclusionem devenimus. Manifestum est autem quod cognitio conclusionis dependet ex omnibus mediis praecedentibus, non solum quantum ad novam acquisitionem scientiae, sed etiam quantum ad scientiae conservationem. Cuius signum est quod, si quis oblivisceretur aliquod praecedentium mediorum, opinionem quidem vel fidem de conclusione posset habere, sed non scientiam, ordine causarum ignorato.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0153","text":"Sic igitur, cum inferiores Angeli rationes divinorum operum cognoscant per lumen superiorum Angelorum, dependet eorum cognitio ex lumine superiorum, non solum quantum ad novam acquisitionem scientiae, sed etiam quantum ad cognitionis conservationem. Licet ergo post iudicium non proficiant inferiores Angeli in cognitione aliquarum rerum, non tamen propter hoc excluditur quin a superioribus illuminentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0154","text":"[I q. 108 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32983)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0155","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, etsi post diem iudicii homines non sint ulterius ad salutem adducendi per ministerium Angelorum; tamen illi qui iam salutem erunt consecuti, aliquam illustrationem habebunt per Angelorum officia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0156","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0157","text":"[I q. 108 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32984)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0158","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homines non assumantur ad ordines Angelorum. Hierarchia enim humana continetur sub infima hierarchiarum caelestium, sicut infima sub media, et media sub prima. Sed Angeli infimae hierarchiae nunquam transferentur in mediam aut in primam. Ergo neque homines transferentur ad ordines Angelorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0159","text":"[I q. 108 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32985)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0160","text":"Praeterea, ordinibus Angelorum aliqua officia competunt, utpote custodire, miracula facere, Daemones arcere, et huiusmodi, quae non videntur convenire animabus sanctorum. Ergo non transferentur ad ordines Angelorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0161","text":"[I q. 108 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32986)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0162","text":"Praeterea, sicut boni Angeli inducunt ad bonum, ita Daemones inducunt ad malum. Sed erroneum est dicere quod animae hominum malorum convertantur in Daemones, hoc enim Chrysostomus reprobat, super Matth. Ergo non videtur quod animae sanctorum transferantur ad ordines Angelorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0163","text":"[I q. 108 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32987)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0164","text":"Sed contra est quod dominus dicit, Matth. XXII, de sanctis, quod *erunt sicut Angeli Dei in caelo*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0165","text":"[I q. 108 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32988)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0166","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, ordines Angelorum distinguuntur et secundum conditionem naturae, et secundum dona gratiae. Si ergo considerentur Angelorum ordines solum quantum ad gradum naturae, sic homines nullo modo assumi possunt ad ordines Angelorum, quia semper remanebit naturarum distinctio. Quam quidam considerantes, posuerunt quod nullo modo homines transferri possunt ad aequalitatem Angelorum. Quod est erroneum, repugnat enim promissioni Christi, dicentis, Lucae XX, quod filii resurrectionis erunt aequales Angelis in caelis. Illud enim quod est ex parte naturae, se habet ut materiale in ratione ordinis, completivum vero est quod est ex dono gratiae, quae dependet ex liberalitate Dei, non ex ordine naturae. Et ideo per donum gratiae homines mereri possunt tantam gloriam, ut Angelis aequentur secundum singulos Angelorum gradus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0167","text":"Quod est homines ad ordines Angelorum assumi. Quidam tamen dicunt quod ad ordines Angelorum non assumuntur omnes qui salvantur, sed soli virgines vel perfecti; alii vero suum ordinem constituent, quasi condivisum toti societati Angelorum. Sed hoc est contra Augustinum, qui dicit XII de Civ. Dei, quod non erunt duae societates hominum et Angelorum, sed una, quia omnium beatitudo est adhaerere uni Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0168","text":"[I q. 108 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32989)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0169","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod gratia Angelis datur secundum proportionem naturalium; non autem sic est de hominibus, ut supra dictum est. Et ideo sicut inferiores Angeli non possunt transferri ad naturalem gradum superiorum, ita nec ad gratuitum. Homines vero possunt ad gratuitum conscendere, sed non ad naturalem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0170","text":"[I q. 108 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32990)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0171","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Angeli, secundum naturae ordinem, medii sunt inter nos et Deum. Et ideo, secundum legem communem, per eos administrantur non solum res humanae, sed etiam omnia corporalia. Homines autem sancti, etiam post hanc vitam, sunt eiusdem naturae nobiscum. Unde secundum legem communem, non administrant humana, nec rebus vivorum intersunt, ut Augustinus dicit in libro de cura pro mortuis agenda. Ex quadam tamen speciali dispensatione interdum aliquibus sanctis conceditur, vel vivis vel mortuis, huiusmodi officia exercere, vel miracula faciendo, vel Daemones arcendo, vel aliquid huiusmodi; sicut Augustinus in eodem libro dicit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0172","text":"[I q. 108 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32991)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0173","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod homines ad poenam Daemonum transferri, non est erroneum, sed quidam erronee posuerunt Daemones nihil aliud esse quam animas defunctorum. Et hoc Chrysostomus reprobat.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9d118e460cf682b765cfef8b7e5436a2b4c3327c4f3d1e2228228c46a3648856","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T154219063994Z_11c1325a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 108","source_sha256":"9d118e460cf682b765cfef8b7e5436a2b4c3327c4f3d1e2228228c46a3648856","sha256":"13ed6cbf0431cb22b6f621ad969e084351ac3bdcbaa533480149419026c89b41","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:42:19.064069+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0002","text":"[I q. 108 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32921)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0003","text":"Next, consideration must be given to the ordering of the angels according to hierarchies and orders; for it has been said that the higher illuminate the lower, and not conversely. And concerning this, eight questions are asked. First, whether all angels belong to one hierarchy. Second, whether there is only one order in one hierarchy. Third, whether there are several angels in one order. Fourth, whether the distinction of hierarchies and orders is from nature. Fifth, concerning the names and properties of the individual orders. Sixth, concerning the comparison of the orders with one another. Seventh, whether the orders endure after the day of judgment. Eighth, whether men are assumed into the orders of angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0005","text":"[I q. 108 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32922)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that all angels belong to one hierarchy. For since angels are highest among creatures, it must be said that they are most excellently disposed. But the best disposition is that of a multitude insofar as it is contained under one rule; as is clear from the Philosopher, *Metaphysics* XII, and *Politics* III. Therefore, since hierarchy is nothing other than sacred rule, it seems that all angels belong to one hierarchy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0007","text":"[I q. 108 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32923)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0008","text":"Further, Dionysius says, in chapter III of *The Celestial Hierarchy*, that *hierarchy is order, knowledge, and action*. But all angels agree in one order toward God, whom they know and by whom they are regulated in their actions. Therefore all angels belong to one hierarchy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0009","text":"[I q. 108 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32924)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0010","text":"Further, sacred rule, which is called hierarchy, is found among men and angels. But all men belong to one hierarchy. Therefore all angels likewise belong to one hierarchy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0011","text":"[I q. 108 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32925)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0012","text":"On the contrary, Dionysius, in chapter VI of *The Celestial Hierarchy*, distinguishes three hierarchies of angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0013","text":"[I q. 108 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32926)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that hierarchy is sacred rule, as has been said. Now, in the name of rule two things are understood, namely, the ruler himself and the multitude ordered under the ruler. Therefore, because God is one ruler not only of all angels, but also of men and of the whole creation, there is one hierarchy not only of all angels but also of the whole rational creature that can partake in sacred things, according as Augustine says in *The City of God* XII that *there are two cities, that is, societies: one among good angels and men, the other among evil ones*. But if rule is considered on the part of the multitude ordered under the ruler, then a rule is called one insofar as the multitude can receive the ruler's governance in one and the same way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0015","text":"But those which cannot be governed by the ruler in the same manner pertain to different rules, just as under one king there are diverse cities, which are governed by different laws and ministers. Now it is clear that men receive divine illuminations in one way and angels in another; for angels receive them in intelligible purity, whereas men receive them under likenesses of sensible things, as Dionysius says in chapter I of *The Celestial Hierarchy*. And therefore the human hierarchy had to be distinguished from the angelic. And in the same way three hierarchies are distinguished among angels. For it was said above, when the knowledge of angels was under discussion, that the higher angels have a more universal knowledge of truth than the lower. Now such a universal reception of knowledge can be distinguished in angels according to three degrees. For the intelligible accounts of things concerning which angels are illuminated can be considered in three ways.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0016","text":"First, indeed, according as they proceed from the first universal principle, which is God; and this mode belongs to the first hierarchy, which extends immediately to God and is, as it were, placed in the vestibules of God, as Dionysius says in chapter VII of *The Celestial Hierarchy*. Second, according as such accounts depend on universal created causes, which are already in some manner multiplied; and this mode belongs to the second hierarchy. But third, according as such accounts are applied to individual things and according as they depend upon proper causes; and this mode belongs to the lowest hierarchy. This will be more fully clear when the individual orders are discussed. Thus, therefore, hierarchies are distinguished on the part of the multitude subject to them. Hence it is clear that those err, and speak contrary to the intention of Dionysius, who posit in the divine persons a hierarchy which they call supercelestial.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0017","text":"For in the divine persons there is a certain order of nature, but not of hierarchy. For, as Dionysius says in chapter III of *The Celestial Hierarchy*, *the order of hierarchy is that some be purified and illuminated and perfected, while others purify and illuminate and perfect*. Far be it from us to posit this in the divine persons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0018","text":"[I q. 108 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32927)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0019","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that this argument proceeds concerning rule on the part of the ruler, because it is best that a multitude be governed by one ruler, as the Philosopher intends in the aforesaid places.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0020","text":"[I q. 108 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32928)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0021","text":"To the second, it must be said that, as regards the knowledge of God himself, whom all see in one way, namely through the essence, the hierarchies among angels are not distinguished; but they are distinguished as regards the intelligible accounts of created things, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0022","text":"[I q. 108 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32929)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0023","text":"To the third, it must be said that all men are of one species, and one mode of understanding is connatural to them; but this is not so among angels. Hence the reasoning is not similar.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0024","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0025","text":"[I q. 108 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32930)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0026","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that there are not several orders in one hierarchy. For when the definition is multiplied, the thing defined is also multiplied. But hierarchy, as Dionysius says, is an order. Therefore, if there are many orders, there will not be one hierarchy, but many.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0027","text":"[I q. 108 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32931)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0028","text":"Further, diverse orders are diverse grades. But grades among spiritual things are constituted according to diverse spiritual gifts. But among the Angels all spiritual gifts are common, because nothing is possessed there individually. Therefore there are not diverse orders of Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0029","text":"[I q. 108 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32932)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0030","text":"Further, in the ecclesiastical hierarchy the orders are distinguished according to purifying, illuminating, and perfecting; for the order of deacons is purifying, that of priests illuminating, and that of bishops perfecting, as Dionysius says in chapter V of the Ecclesiastical Hierarchy. But every Angel purifies, illuminates, and perfects. Therefore there is no distinction of orders among the Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0031","text":"[I q. 108 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32933)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0032","text":"On the contrary, the Apostle says in Ephesians I that God established Christ as man above *every principality and power and virtue and dominion*; these are diverse orders of Angels, and some of them belong to one hierarchy, as will become clear below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0033","text":"[I q. 108 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32934)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0034","text":"I answer that, as has been said, one hierarchy is one principality, that is, one multitude ordered in one way under the governance of a prince. But a multitude would not be ordered, but confused, if there were not diverse orders in the multitude. Therefore, the very notion of hierarchy requires a diversity of orders. This diversity of orders is considered according to diverse offices and acts. Thus it is evident that in one city there are diverse orders according to diverse acts: for one is the order of those judging, another of those fighting, another of those laboring in the fields, and so of the others. But although there are many orders in one city, all can nevertheless be reduced to three, insofar as every perfect multitude has a beginning, a middle, and an end.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0035","text":"Hence also in cities a threefold order of men is found: some are supreme, such as the nobles; some are lowest, such as the common populace; and some are intermediate, such as the honorable populace. So, therefore, in every angelic hierarchy the orders are distinguished according to diverse acts and offices; and all this diversity is reduced to three, namely, to the highest, the middle, and the lowest. And for this reason Dionysius places three orders in every hierarchy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0036","text":"[I q. 108 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32935)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0037","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that “order” is spoken of in two ways. In one way, it is the ordering itself, comprehending under itself diverse grades; and in this way hierarchy is called an order. In another way, one grade is called an order; and thus several orders are said to belong to one hierarchy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0038","text":"[I q. 108 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32936)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0039","text":"To the second it must be said that in the society of Angels all things are possessed in common; yet some things are possessed more excellently by some than by others. But each thing is possessed more perfectly by one who can communicate it than by one who cannot, just as that which is hot and can heat is more perfect than that which cannot; and one who can teach knows more perfectly than one who cannot. And the more perfectly someone can communicate a gift, the more perfect is his grade, just as he who can teach a higher science is in a more perfect grade of mastery. And according to this likeness, the diversity of grades or orders among the Angels must be considered, according to diverse offices and acts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0040","text":"[I q. 108 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32937)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0041","text":"To the third it must be said that the lowest Angel is higher than the highest man of our hierarchy; according to Matthew XI, *he who is lesser in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he*, namely John the Baptist, than whom no greater has arisen among those born of women. Hence the least Angel of the heavenly hierarchy can not only purify but also illuminate and perfect, and in a higher manner than the orders of our hierarchy. And thus the heavenly orders are not distinguished according to the distinction of these actions, but according to other differences of actions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0042","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0043","text":"[I q. 108 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32938)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0044","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that there are not several Angels in one order. For it was said above that all Angels are unequal to one another. But things that are equal are said to be of one order. Therefore several Angels are not of one order.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0045","text":"[I q. 108 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32939)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0046","text":"Further, what can be sufficiently done by one, it is superfluous for it to be done by many. But that which belongs to one angelic office can be sufficiently done by one Angel, much more so than that which belongs to the office of the sun is sufficiently done by one sun, insofar as an Angel is more perfect than a heavenly body. Therefore, if orders are distinguished according to offices, as has been said, it is superfluous that there should be several Angels of one order.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0047","text":"[I q. 108 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32940)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0048","text":"Further, it was said above that all Angels are unequal. Therefore, if several Angels are of one order, for instance three or four, the lowest of the higher order will have more in common with the highest of the lower order than with the highest of its own order. And thus it does not seem that it is more of one order with the latter than with the former. Therefore there are not several Angels of one order.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0049","text":"[I q. 108 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32941)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0050","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Isaiah VI that the Seraphim cried one to another. Therefore there are several Angels in the one order of Seraphim.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0051","text":"[I q. 108 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32942)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0052","text":"I answer that, one who knows certain things perfectly can distinguish even their least acts, powers, and natures. But one who knows them imperfectly can distinguish only in a universal way, and such distinction is made through fewer [features]. Thus, one who knows natural things imperfectly distinguishes their orders universally, placing heavenly bodies in one order, lower inanimate bodies in another, plants in another, and animals in another. But one who knew natural things more perfectly could distinguish diverse orders even among the heavenly bodies themselves, and within each of the others.\n\nNow we know Angels and their offices imperfectly, as Dionysius says in chapter VI of the *Celestial Hierarchy*. Hence we cannot distinguish the offices and orders of Angels except in common; according to which mode many Angels are contained under one order.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0053","text":"But if we knew perfectly the offices of the Angels and their distinctions, we would know perfectly that each Angel has his own proper office and his own proper order among things, much more so than any star, although this is hidden from us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0054","text":"[I q. 108 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32943)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0055","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that all the Angels of one order are in some way equal, as regards the common likeness by which they are constituted in one order; but absolutely speaking, they are not equal. Hence Dionysius says, in chapter X of the *Celestial Hierarchy*, that in one and the same order of Angels there are to be found first, middle, and last.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0056","text":"[I q. 108 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32944)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0057","text":"To the second, it must be said that that particular distinction of orders and offices, according to which each Angel has a proper office and order, is unknown to us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0058","text":"[I q. 108 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32945)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0059","text":"To the third, it must be said that, just as on a surface that is partly white and partly black, two parts that lie on the boundary of white and black agree more in position than any two white parts, but less in quality; so two Angels who are at the boundaries of two orders agree more with one another in closeness of nature than one of them does with certain others of his own order, but less in aptitude for similar offices. This aptitude extends as far as some definite limit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0060","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0061","text":"[I q. 108 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32946)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0062","text":"The fourth point is approached thus. It seems that the distinction of hierarchies and orders is not from nature in Angels. For hierarchy is called a sacred principality, and Dionysius includes in its definition that it *assimilates [itself] to God, as far as possible*. But holiness and Godlikeness are in Angels through grace, not through nature. Therefore, the distinction of hierarchies and orders in Angels is through grace, not through nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0063","text":"[I q. 108 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32947)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0064","text":"Further, the Seraphim are called burning, or kindling, as Dionysius says in chapter VII of the *Celestial Hierarchy*. But this seems to pertain to charity, which is not from nature but from grace; for *it is poured forth in our hearts through the Holy Spirit, who has been given to us*, as is said in Romans V. *This pertains not only to holy men, but can also be said of the holy Angels*, as Augustine says in XII of *The City of God*. Therefore, the orders among Angels are not from nature but from grace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0065","text":"[I q. 108 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32948)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0066","text":"Further, the ecclesiastical hierarchy is modeled upon the heavenly. But orders among men are not through nature, but through the gift of grace; for it is not by nature that one is a bishop, another a priest, and another a deacon. Therefore, neither among Angels are the orders from nature, but from grace alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0067","text":"[I q. 108 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32949)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0068","text":"But on the contrary, the Master says, in distinction IX of book II of the *Sentences*, that *the order of Angels is called a multitude of heavenly spirits who are like one another by some gift of grace, just as they agree by participation in natural gifts*. Therefore, the distinction of orders among Angels is not only according to gratuitous gifts, but also according to natural gifts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0069","text":"[I q. 108 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32950)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0070","text":"I answer that the order of governance, which is the order of a multitude existing under a principality, is considered in relation to an end. Now the end of Angels can be taken in two ways. In one way, according to the capacity of their nature: namely, that they know and love God by natural knowledge and love. And in relation to this end, the orders of Angels are distinguished according to natural gifts. In another way, the end of the angelic multitude can be taken as exceeding their natural capacity, consisting in the vision of the divine essence and in the unchangeable enjoyment of His goodness; they cannot attain this end except through grace. Hence, in relation to this end, the orders among Angels are distinguished consummately according to gratuitous gifts, but dispositively according to natural gifts, because gratuitous gifts were given to the Angels according to the capacity of their natural gifts. This is not so in human beings, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0071","text":"Hence among human beings the orders are distinguished only according to gratuitous gifts, and not according to nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0072","text":"[I q. 108 a. 4 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32951)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0073","text":"And through this the reply to the objections is evident.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0074","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0075","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32952)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0076","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth. It seems that the orders of Angels are not suitably named. For all heavenly spirits are called both Angels and heavenly virtues. But common names are unsuitably appropriated to some. Therefore one order is unsuitably called that of Angels, and another that of virtues.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0077","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32953)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0078","text":"Further, to be lord is proper to God; according to Psalm XCIX, *know that the Lord himself is God*. Therefore one order of heavenly spirits is unsuitably called dominations.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0079","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32954)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0080","text":"Further, the name of domination seems to pertain to governing. But likewise the name of principalities and of powers does as well. Therefore these three names are unsuitably imposed on three orders.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0081","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32955)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0082","text":"Further, Archangels are called as it were principal Angels. Therefore this name ought not to be imposed upon an order other than the order of principalities.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0083","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32956)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0084","text":"Further, the name Seraphim is imposed from ardor, which pertains to charity, whereas the name Cherubim is imposed from knowledge. But charity and knowledge are gifts common to all Angels. Therefore they ought not to be names of special orders.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0085","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 arg. 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32957)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0086","text":"Further, thrones are called seats. But God is said to sit in a rational creature precisely because it knows and loves him. Therefore there ought not to be an order of thrones distinct from the order of Cherubim and Seraphim. Thus, therefore, it seems that the orders of Angels are unsuitably named.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0087","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32958)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0088","text":"On the contrary, there is the authority of Sacred Scripture, which names them thus. For the name Seraphim is set down in Isaiah VI; the name Cherubim in Ezekiel I; the name thrones in Colossians I; while dominations and virtues and powers and principalities are set down in Ephesians I; the name Archangel is set down in the canonical epistle of Jude, while the names of Angels occur in many places of Scripture.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0089","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32959)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0090","text":"I answer that, in the naming of the angelic orders, it must be considered that *the proper names of the individual orders designate their properties*, as Dionysius says in chapter VII of the *Celestial Hierarchy*. But in order to see what is the property of each order, it must be considered that in ordered things something may occur in three ways, namely, by property, by excess, and by participation. Now something is said to be in a thing by property when it is adequate and proportionate to its nature. But it is by excess when that which is attributed to something is less than the thing to which it is attributed, yet nevertheless belongs to that thing by a certain excess; as was said concerning all names that are attributed to God. But it is by participation when that which is attributed to something is not found in it fully, but deficiently; as holy men are called gods by participation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0091","text":"Therefore, if something ought to be named by a name designating its property, it ought not to be named from that which it participates imperfectly, nor from that which it possesses excessively, but from that which is, as it were, equal to it. Thus, if someone wishes properly to name a human being, he will call him a rational substance, but not an intellectual substance, which is the proper name of an Angel, because simple understanding belongs to an Angel by property, but to a human being by participation; nor a sensible substance, which is the proper name of a brute animal, because sense is less than that which is proper to a human being, and belongs to a human being by excess above other animals. Thus, therefore, concerning the orders of Angels, it must be considered that all spiritual perfections are common to all Angels, and that all exist more abundantly in the higher than in the lower.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0092","text":"But since there is also a certain grade among the perfections themselves, the higher perfection is attributed to the higher order by property, but to the lower by participation; conversely, the lower is attributed to the lower by property, but to the higher by excess. And thus the higher order is named from the higher perfection. Thus Dionysius expounds the names of the orders according to their suitability to their spiritual perfections. Gregory, however, in his exposition of these names, seems rather to attend to their external ministries. For he says that *Angels are so called because they announce lesser things; Archangels, greater things; virtues, through whom miracles are wrought; powers, by whom adverse powers are repelled; principalities, who preside over the good spirits themselves*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0093","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32960)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0094","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that an Angel is called a messenger. Therefore all heavenly spirits, insofar as they are revealers of divine things, are called Angels. But the higher Angels have a certain excellence in this manifestation, from which the higher orders are named. But the lowest order of Angels adds no excellence beyond common manifestation, and therefore is named from simple manifestation. And thus the common name remains, as it were, proper to the lowest order, as Dionysius says in chapter V of the *Celestial Hierarchy*. Or it can be said that the lowest order is specially called the order of Angels because they announce to us immediately. But virtue can be taken in two ways. In one way, commonly, insofar as it is intermediate between essence and operation, and thus all heavenly spirits are called heavenly virtues, just as also heavenly essences. In another way, insofar as it implies a certain excess of strength, and thus it is the proper name of an order.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0095","text":"Hence Dionysius says, in chapter VIII of the *Celestial Hierarchy*, that the name of virtues signifies a certain manly and unshaken strength: first, indeed, with respect to all the divine operations suitable to them; second, with respect to receiving divine things. And thus it signifies that they approach without any fear the divine things that pertain to them, which seems to pertain to strength of soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0096","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32961)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0097","text":"To the second it must be said that, as Dionysius says in chapter XII of *On the Divine Names*, *dominion is praised in God singularly by a certain excess, but by participation the divine utterances call the more principal adornments lords, through whom those beneath receive from His gifts*. Hence Dionysius also says in chapter VIII of *The Celestial Hierarchy* that the name of dominations first indeed signifies a certain freedom, which is from servile condition and pedestrian subjection, such as the common people are subjected to, and from tyrannical oppression, which even the greater sometimes suffer. Second, it signifies a certain rigid and inflexible governance, which bends to no servile act, nor to any act of those subjected or oppressed by tyrants. Third, it signifies an appetite for and participation in true dominion, which is in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0098","text":"And likewise the name of any order signifies participation in that which is in God; just as the name of Virtues signifies participation in the divine virtue; and so with the others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0099","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32962)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0100","text":"To the third it must be said that the name of domination, power, and principality pertains to governance in diverse ways. For it belongs to a lord merely to command concerning things to be done. And therefore Gregory says that *certain hosts of Angels, because the rest are subjected to them for obedience, are called dominations*. But the name of power denotes a certain ordering; according to that saying of the Apostle in Romans XIII, *he who resists the power resists the ordinance of God*. And therefore Dionysius says that the name of power signifies a certain ordering both concerning the reception of divine things and concerning the divine actions which the higher exercise upon the lower, leading them upward. Therefore, it pertains to the order of Powers to order what is to be done by subjects. But to preside, as Gregory says, is to be first among the rest, as though they are first in the execution of what is commanded. And therefore Dionysius says, in chapter IX of *The Celestial*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0101","text":"*Hierarchy*, that the name of principalities signifies leadership together with sacred order. For those who lead others, being first among them, are properly called princes, according to that saying of Psalm LXVII, *the princes went before, joined with those singing psalms*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0102","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32963)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0103","text":"To the fourth it must be said that Archangels, according to Dionysius, are intermediate between Principalities and Angels. But what is intermediate, compared with one extreme, appears to be the other, insofar as it participates in the nature of both; just as the lukewarm, in relation to the hot, is cold, but in relation to the cold is hot. Thus Archangels are called, as it were, princely Angels, because in relation to Angels they are princes, but in relation to Principalities they are Angels. According to Gregory, however, they are called Archangels from the fact that they preside over the order of Angels alone, as if announcing great things. But Principalities are so called from the fact that they preside over all the heavenly virtues carrying out the divine commands.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0104","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32964)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0105","text":"To the fifth it must be said that the name Seraphim is imposed not merely from charity, but from an excess of charity, which the name of burning or conflagration conveys. Hence Dionysius, in chapter VII of *The Celestial Hierarchy*, explains the name Seraphim according to the properties of fire, in which there is an excess of heat. Now in fire we can consider three things. First, its motion, which is upward and continuous. By this is signified that they are moved toward God without turning aside. Second, its active power, which is hot. This indeed is not found in fire simply, but with a certain sharpness, because it is most penetrating in acting and reaches even to the least things; and again with a certain exceeding fervor. And by this is signified the action of Angels of this kind, which they powerfully exercise upon their subjects, stirring them up to a like fervor and wholly purging them by conflagration. Third, its brightness is considered in fire.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0106","text":"And this signifies that Angels of this kind have in themselves an inextinguishable light, and that they perfectly illuminate others. Likewise, too, the name Cherubim is imposed from a certain excess of knowledge, whence it is interpreted as fullness of knowledge. Dionysius explains this with respect to four things: first, with respect to the perfect vision of God; second, with respect to the full reception of divine light; third, with respect to the fact that in God Himself they contemplate the beauty of the order of things derived from God; fourth, with respect to the fact that, being themselves full of such knowledge, they pour it forth abundantly into others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0107","text":"[I q. 108 a. 5 ad 6](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32965)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0108","text":"To the sixth it must be said that the order of Thrones has excellence above the lower orders in this: that they can immediately know in God the intelligible accounts of divine works. But the Cherubim have excellence of knowledge, whereas the Seraphim have excellence of ardor. And although the third is included in these two excellences, the other two are nevertheless not included in that which belongs to the Thrones. Therefore the order of Thrones is distinct from the order of Cherubim and Seraphim. For this is common in all: that the excellence of the lower is contained in the excellence of the higher, and not conversely. Dionysius explains the name of Thrones by their likeness to material seats. In these four things are to be considered. First, position, because seats are raised above the earth. Thus the Angels themselves who are called Thrones are raised to this: that they may immediately know in God the intelligible accounts of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0109","text":"Second, in material seats firmness is considered, because someone sits firmly upon them. But here it is the converse, for the Angels themselves are made firm by God. Third, because a seat receives the one sitting and he can be borne upon it. Thus these Angels too receive God in themselves and in some manner bear Him to the lower. Fourth, from its shape, because a seat is open on one side to receive the one sitting. So too these Angels are open through readiness to receive God and to minister to Him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0110","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0111","text":"[I q. 108 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32966)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0112","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth. It seems that the ranks of the orders are assigned inappropriately. For the order of prelates seems to be supreme. But Dominations, Principalities, and Powers, from their very names, possess a certain prelacy. Therefore these orders ought to be supreme among all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0113","text":"[I q. 108 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32967)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0114","text":"Moreover, the nearer an order is to God, the higher it is. But the order of Thrones seems to be nearest to God, for nothing is joined more closely to one sitting than his seat. Therefore the order of Thrones is the highest.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0115","text":"[I q. 108 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32968)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0116","text":"Moreover, knowledge is prior to love, and the intellect seems to be higher than the will. Therefore the order of Cherubim also seems to be higher than the order of Seraphim.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0117","text":"[I q. 108 a. 6 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32969)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0118","text":"Moreover, Gregory places Principalities above Powers. Therefore they are not placed immediately above Archangels, as Dionysius says.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0119","text":"[I q. 108 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32970)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0120","text":"But on the contrary, Dionysius places in the first hierarchy Seraphim as first, Cherubim as intermediate, Thrones as last; in the middle, Dominations as first, Virtues as intermediate, Powers as last; in the last, Principalities as first, Archangels as intermediate, Angels as last.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0121","text":"[I q. 108 a. 6, body](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32971)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0122","text":"I answer that the degrees of the angelic orders are assigned by both Gregory and Dionysius: as to the other orders, indeed, fittingly; but differently as to Principalities and Virtues. For Dionysius places Virtues below Dominions and above Powers, but Principalities below Powers and above Archangels; whereas Gregory places Principalities in the middle between Dominions and Powers, and Virtues in the middle between Powers and Archangels. And each assignment can have support from the authority of the Apostle. For he, enumerating the middle orders in ascending order, says, Eph. I, that God has set him, namely Christ, at his right hand in the heavenly places, above every Principality and Power and Virtue and Dominion, where he places Virtue between Power and Dominion, in accordance with Dionysius's assignment. But in Coloss.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0123","text":"I, enumerating the same orders in descending order, he says: whether thrones, or dominions, or principalities, or powers, all things were created through him and in him; where he places Principalities in the middle between Dominions and Powers, in accordance with Gregory's assignment. First, therefore, let us consider the rationale of Dionysius's assignment. In it one must consider that, as was said above, the first hierarchy receives the notions of things in God himself; the second, however, in universal causes; and the third according to their determination to particular effects. And because God is the end not only of angelic ministries but also of the whole creation, consideration of the end belongs to the first hierarchy; universal ordering concerning things to be done belongs to the middle one; but application of the ordering to the effect, which is the execution of the work, belongs to the last. For it is manifest that these three are found in every operation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0124","text":"And therefore Dionysius, considering from the names of the orders their properties, placed in the first hierarchy those orders whose names are imposed by reference to God, namely Seraphim, Cherubim, and Thrones. But he placed in the middle hierarchy those orders whose names signify a certain common governance or ordering, namely Dominions, Virtues, and Powers. And he placed in the third hierarchy those orders whose names signify the execution of a work, namely Principalities, Angels, and Archangels. Now, in relation to the end, three things can be considered: for first, someone considers the end; second, he receives perfect knowledge of the end; third, he fixes his intention upon it; of which the second stands by addition to the first, and the third to both.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0125","text":"And because God is the end of creatures as a commander is the end of an army, as is said in Metaphysics XII, something similar to this order may be considered in human affairs. For some have the dignity that they can of themselves approach the king or commander familiarly; others have in addition to this that they also know his secrets; while still others, beyond this, always cleave about him, as though joined to him. And according to this likeness we can understand the disposition of the orders of the first hierarchy. For Thrones are raised up to this, that they receive God familiarly in themselves, insofar as they can immediately know in him the notions of things; and this is proper to the whole first hierarchy. Cherubim, however, know the divine secrets in a supereminent manner. But Seraphim excel in that which is supreme of all, namely, being united to God himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0126","text":"Thus the order of Thrones is denominated from that which is common to the whole hierarchy, just as the order of Angels is denominated from that which is common to all heavenly spirits. Now three things pertain to the character of governance. The first of these is the determination of things to be done, which is proper to Dominions. The second is to provide the capacity for fulfilling them, which belongs to Virtues. The third is to order how the things commanded or determined may be fulfilled, so that some may execute them; and this belongs to Powers. But the execution of angelic ministries consists in announcing divine things. In the execution of any act, however, some are as it were initiators of the action and leaders of others, as precentors in singing, and in war those who lead and direct others; and this belongs to Principalities. But others simply execute, and this belongs to Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0127","text":"But others hold a middle position, which belongs to Archangels, as was said above. Moreover, this assignment of the orders is found to be fitting. For the highest of an inferior order always has an affinity with the lowest of a superior order, just as the lowest animals differ little from plants. Now the first order is that of the divine persons, which terminates in the Holy Spirit, who is proceeding love; and the highest order of the first hierarchy, denominated from the burning of love, has an affinity with him. But the lowest order of the first hierarchy is that of Thrones, which by its name has a certain affinity with Dominions. For Thrones are so called, according to Gregory, because through them God exercises his judgments; for they receive divine illuminations in a manner suitable for immediately illuminating the second hierarchy, to which belongs the ordering of divine ministries.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0128","text":"But the order of Powers has an affinity with the order of Principalities, since it belongs to Powers to impose order upon subjects; and this ordering is immediately signified in the name of Principalities, who are first in the execution of divine ministries, as presiding over the governance of nations and kingdoms, which is first and principal among divine ministries. For the good of a nation is more divine than the good of one man. Hence it is said, Dan. X: *the prince of the kingdom of the Persians withstood me*. The disposition of the orders which Gregory sets forth also has fittingness. For since Dominions determine and command those things which pertain to divine ministries, the orders subject to them are disposed according to the disposition of those upon whom the divine ministries are exercised. But, as Augustine says in On the Trinity III, *bodies are governed in a certain order, the lower by the higher, and all by spiritual creature; and the evil spirit by the good spirit*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0129","text":"Therefore the first order after Dominions is called that of Principalities, who also preside over good spirits. Then come Powers, through whom evil spirits are restrained, just as evildoers are restrained through earthly powers, as is found in Rom. XIII. After these come Virtues, which have power over bodily nature in the working of miracles. After these come Archangels and Angels, who announce to human beings either great things, which are above reason, or small things, to which reason can extend.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0130","text":"[I q. 108 a. 6, reply to 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32972)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0131","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that in Angels, their being subject to God is more important than their presiding over inferiors, and the latter is derived from the former. Therefore, the orders named from prelacy are not the highest, but rather the orders named from conversion to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0132","text":"[I q. 108 a. 6, reply to 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32973)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0133","text":"To the second, it must be said that that proximity to God which is signified by the name of Thrones also belongs to Cherubim and Seraphim, and more excellently, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0134","text":"[I q. 108 a. 6, reply to 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32974)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0135","text":"To the third, it must be said that, as was said above, knowledge is according as the things known are in the knower; but love is according as the lover is united to the thing loved. Now higher things are in themselves in a more noble manner than in lower things; but lower things are in higher things in a more noble manner than in themselves. And therefore knowledge of lower things indeed exceeds love of them, but love of higher things, and especially of God, exceeds knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0136","text":"[I q. 108 a. 6, reply to 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32975)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0137","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, if anyone carefully considers the dispositions of the orders according to Dionysius and Gregory, they differ little or not at all, if they are referred to the reality. For Gregory explains the name of Principalities from the fact that they preside over good spirits; and this befits Virtues, insofar as in the name of Virtues is understood a certain strength giving efficacy to inferior spirits for carrying out divine ministries. Again, Virtues according to Gregory seem to be the same as Principalities according to Dionysius. For to work miracles is first among divine ministries; for by this the way is prepared for the announcement of Archangels and Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0138","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0139","text":"[I q. 108 a. 7, objection 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32976)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0140","text":"We proceed thus to the seventh. It seems that the orders will not remain after the day of judgment. For the Apostle says, I Cor. XV, that Christ will do away with every principality and power when he has delivered the kingdom to God and the Father, which will be at the final consummation. Therefore, by the same reasoning, in that state all the other orders will be done away with.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0141","text":"[I q. 108 a. 7, objection 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32977)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0142","text":"Further, it belongs to the office of the angelic orders to cleanse, illuminate, and perfect. But after the day of judgment one Angel will not cleanse or illuminate or perfect another, because they will no longer advance in knowledge. Therefore the angelic orders would remain in vain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0143","text":"[I q. 108 a. 7, objection 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32978)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0144","text":"Further, the Apostle says, Heb. I, concerning the Angels, that *are they not all ministering spirits, sent for ministry on account of those who shall receive the inheritance of salvation?* From this it is evident that the offices of Angels are ordered to bringing human beings to salvation. But all the elect attain salvation by the day of judgment. Therefore, after the day of judgment, the offices and orders of Angels will not remain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0145","text":"[I q. 108 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32979)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0146","text":"On the contrary, it is said in Judges V: “the stars remained in their order and course,” which is expounded concerning the Angels. Therefore the Angels will always remain in their orders.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0147","text":"[I q. 108 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32980)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0148","text":"I answer that, in the angelic orders, two things may be considered: namely, the distinction of grades, and the execution of offices. The distinction of grades in the Angels is according to the difference of grace and nature, as was said above. And both differences will always remain in the Angels. For the difference of natures could not be taken away from them except by their being corrupted; likewise, the difference of glory will be in them forever, according to the difference of preceding merit. But the execution of angelic offices will in one way remain after the day of judgment, and in another way cease. It will indeed cease insofar as their offices are ordered toward leading some to the end; but it will remain insofar as it befits the final attainment of the end. Just as the offices of military orders are one thing in battle and another in triumph.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0149","text":"[I q. 108 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32981)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0150","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that principalities and powers will be done away with in that final consummation insofar as they lead others to the end, because, the end having now been attained, it is not necessary to tend toward the end. And this reasoning is understood from the words of the Apostle, saying: “when he shall have delivered up the kingdom to God and the Father,” that is, when he shall have led the faithful to enjoy God himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0151","text":"[I q. 108 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32982)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0152","text":"To the second, it must be said that the actions of Angels upon other Angels must be considered according to the likeness of the intelligible actions that are in us. Now many intelligible actions are found in us which are ordered according to the order of cause and effect; as when, through many intermediates, we arrive step by step at one conclusion. But it is manifest that knowledge of the conclusion depends on all the preceding intermediates, not only as regards the new acquisition of knowledge, but also as regards the preservation of knowledge. A sign of this is that, if someone were to forget one of the preceding intermediates, he could indeed have opinion or faith concerning the conclusion, but not knowledge, the order of causes being unknown.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0153","text":"Thus, since the lower Angels know the reasons of divine works through the light of the higher Angels, their knowledge depends on the light of the higher Angels, not only as regards the new acquisition of knowledge, but also as regards the preservation of knowledge. Therefore, although after judgment the lower Angels do not advance in the knowledge of certain things, it is nevertheless not thereby excluded that they are illuminated by the higher Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0154","text":"[I q. 108 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32983)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0155","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although after the day of judgment human beings are no longer to be led to salvation through the ministry of Angels, nevertheless those who will already have attained salvation will have some illumination through the offices of Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0156","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0157","text":"[I q. 108 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32984)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0158","text":"We proceed thus to the eighth. It seems that human beings are not assumed into the orders of Angels. For the human hierarchy is contained under the lowest of the celestial hierarchies, as the lowest under the middle, and the middle under the first. But the Angels of the lowest hierarchy will never be transferred into the middle or into the first. Therefore neither will human beings be transferred to the orders of Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0158"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0159","text":"[I q. 108 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32985)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0159"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0160","text":"Moreover, certain offices belong to the orders of Angels, such as guarding, performing miracles, repelling Demons, and the like, which do not seem to befit the souls of the saints. Therefore they will not be transferred to the orders of Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0160"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0161","text":"[I q. 108 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32986)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0161"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0162","text":"Moreover, just as good Angels induce toward good, so Demons induce toward evil. But it is erroneous to say that the souls of wicked human beings are changed into Demons; for Chrysostom reproves this, on Matthew. Therefore it does not seem that the souls of the saints are transferred to the orders of Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0162"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0163","text":"[I q. 108 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32987)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0163"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0164","text":"On the contrary, the Lord says, Matthew XXII, concerning the saints, that *they will be like the Angels of God in heaven*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0164"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0165","text":"[I q. 108 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32988)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0165"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0166","text":"I answer that, as was said above, the orders of Angels are distinguished both according to the condition of nature and according to the gifts of grace. If, therefore, the orders of Angels are considered solely as regards the grade of nature, then human beings can in no way be assumed into the orders of Angels, because the distinction of natures will always remain. Some, considering this, maintained that human beings can in no way be transferred to equality with Angels. This is erroneous, for it conflicts with the promise of Christ, who says, in Luke XX, that the children of the resurrection will be equal to the Angels in heaven. For that which is on the part of nature stands as material in the notion of order, whereas that which is from the gift of grace is completing; and grace depends on the liberality of God, not on the order of nature. Therefore, through the gift of grace, human beings can merit such great glory that they are made equal to the Angels according to each of the grades of Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0166"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0167","text":"And this is for human beings to be assumed into the orders of Angels. Yet some say that not all who are saved are assumed into the orders of Angels, but only virgins or the perfect; while others say that they will establish their own order, as though shared alongside the whole society of Angels. But this is contrary to Augustine, who says in XII *On the City of God* that there will not be two societies, of human beings and Angels, but one, because the beatitude of all is to adhere to the one God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0167"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0168","text":"[I q. 108 a. 8 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32989)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0168"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0169","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that grace is given to the angels according to the proportion of their natural gifts; but this is not so with human beings, as was said above. And therefore, just as the lower angels cannot be transferred to the natural rank of the higher, so neither can they be transferred to the gratuitous rank. Human beings, however, can ascend to the gratuitous rank, but not to the natural one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0169"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0170","text":"[I q. 108 a. 8 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32990)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0170"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0171","text":"To the second, it must be said that the angels, according to the order of nature, are intermediaries between us and God. And therefore, according to the common law, not only human affairs but also all corporeal things are administered through them. Holy human beings, however, even after this life, are of the same nature as we are. Hence, according to the common law, they do not administer human affairs, nor do they take part in the affairs of the living, as Augustine says in the book On Care to Be Taken for the Dead. Yet, by a certain special dispensation, some saints are sometimes granted, whether living or dead, to exercise offices of this sort, either by performing miracles, or by warding off demons, or by doing something of this kind; as Augustine says in the same book.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0171"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0172","text":"[I q. 108 a. 8 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32991)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0172"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__100:P0173","text":"To the third, it must be said that it is not erroneous that human beings are transferred to the punishment of demons; but some have erroneously maintained that demons are nothing other than the souls of the dead. And Chrysostom rejects this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__100:P0173"]}],"source_digest":"038b1d7ff8e34a4f17577a175a09396072687025899249a9956ab49beebccd16","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 108"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 108","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101","title":"First Part · Question 109","order":110,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC__101","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 109 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32992)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de ordinatione malorum Angelorum. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum ordines sint in Daemonibus. Secundo, utrum in eis sit praelatio. Tertio, utrum unus illuminet alium. Quarto, utrum subiiciantur praelationi bonorum Angelorum.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 109 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32993)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ordines non sint in Daemonibus. Ordo enim pertinet ad rationem boni, sicut et modus et species, ut Augustinus dicit in libro de natura boni; et e contrario inordinatio pertinet ad rationem mali. Sed in bonis Angelis nihil est inordinatum. Ergo in malis Angelis non sunt aliqui ordines.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32994)\n\nPraeterea, ordines angelici sub aliqua hierarchia continentur. Sed Daemones non sunt sub aliqua hierarchia, quae est sacer principatus, cum ab omni sanctitate sint vacui. Ergo in Daemonibus non sunt ordines.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32995)\n\nPraeterea, Daemones de singulis ordinibus Angelorum ceciderunt, ut communiter dicitur. Si ergo aliqui Daemones dicuntur esse alicuius ordinis, quia de illo ordine ceciderunt; videtur quod deberent eis attribui nomina singulorum ordinum. Nunquam autem invenitur quod dicantur Seraphim, vel throni, vel dominationes. Ergo, pari ratione, non sunt in aliquibus ordinibus.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32996)\n\nSed contra est quod apostolus dicit, ad Ephes. ult., quod *est nobis colluctatio adversus principes et potestates, adversus mundi rectores tenebrarum harum*.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32997)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut iam dictum est, ordo angelicus consideratur et secundum gradum naturae, et secundum gradum gratiae. Gratia vero habet duplicem statum, scilicet imperfectum, qui est status merendi; et perfectum, qui est status gloriae consummatae. Si ergo considerentur ordines angelici quantum ad perfectionem gloriae, sic Daemones neque sunt in ordinibus angelicis, neque unquam fuerunt. Si autem considerentur quantum ad id quod est gratiae imperfectae, sic Daemones fuerunt quidem aliquando in ordinibus Angelorum, sed ab eis ceciderunt; secundum illud quod supra posuimus, omnes Angelos in gratia creatos fuisse. Si autem considerentur quantum ad id quod est naturae, sic adhuc sunt in ordinibus, quia data naturalia non amiserunt, ut Dionysius dicit.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32998)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod bonum potest inveniri sine malo; sed malum non potest inveniri sine bono, ut supra habitum est. Et ideo Daemones, inquantum habent naturam bonam, ordinati sunt.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32999)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ordinatio Daemonum, si consideretur ex parte Dei ordinantis, est sacra, utitur enim Daemonibus propter seipsum. Sed ex parte voluntatis Daemonum, non est sacra, quia abutuntur sua natura ad malum.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33000)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod nomen Seraphim imponitur ab ardore caritatis, nomen autem thronorum ab inhabitatione divina, nomen autem dominationum importat libertatem quandam, quae omnia opponuntur peccato. Et ideo peccantibus Angelis huiusmodi nomina non attribuuntur.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 109 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33001)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Daemonibus non sit praelatio. Omnis enim praelatio est secundum aliquem ordinem iustitiae. Sed Daemones totaliter a iustitia ceciderunt. Ergo in eis non est praelatio.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33002)\n\nPraeterea, ubi non est obedientia et subiectio, non est praelatio. Haec autem sine concordia esse non possunt; quae in Daemonibus nulla est, secundum illud Prov. XIII, *inter superbos semper sunt iurgia*. Ergo in Daemonibus non est praelatio.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33003)\n\nPraeterea, si in eis est aliqua praelatio, aut hoc pertinet ad eorum naturam, aut ad eorum culpam vel poenam. Sed non ad eorum naturam, quia subiectio et servitus non est ex natura, sed est ex peccato subsecuta. Nec pertinet ad culpam vel poenam, quia sic superiores Daemones, qui magis peccaverunt, inferioribus subderentur. Non ergo est praelatio in Daemonibus.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33004)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Glossa, I ad Cor. XV, *quandiu durat mundus, Angeli Angelis, homines hominibus, et Daemones Daemonibus praesunt*.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33005)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum actio sequatur naturam rei, quorumcumque naturae sunt ordinatae, oportet quod etiam actiones sub invicem ordinentur. Sicut patet in rebus corporalibus, quia enim inferiora corpora naturali ordine sunt infra corpora caelestia actiones et motus eorum subduntur actionibus et motibus caelestium corporum. Manifestum est autem ex praemissis quod Daemonum quidam naturali ordine sub aliis constituuntur. Unde et actiones eorum sub actionibus superiorum sunt. Et hoc est quod rationem praelationis facit, ut scilicet actio subditi subdatur actioni praelati. Sic igitur ipsa naturalis dispositio Daemonum requirit quod sit in eis praelatio. Convenit etiam hoc divinae sapientiae, quae nihil in universo inordinatum relinquit, *quae attingit a fine usque ad finem fortiter, et disponit omnia suaviter*, ut dicitur Sap. VIII.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33006)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod praelatio Daemonum non fundatur super eorum iustitia, sed super iustitia Dei cuncta ordinantis.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33007)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod concordia Daemonum, qua quidam aliis obediunt, non est ex amicitia quam inter se habeant; sed ex communi nequitia, qua homines odiunt, et Dei iustitiae repugnant. Est enim proprium hominum impiorum, ut eis se adiungant et subiiciant, ad propriam nequitiam exequendam, quos potiores viribus vident.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33008)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Daemones non sunt aequales secundum naturam, unde in eis est naturalis praelatio. Quod in hominibus non contingit, qui natura sunt pares. Quod autem superioribus inferiores subdantur, non est ad bonum superiorum, sed magis ad malum eorum; quia cum mala facere maxime ad miseriam pertineat, praeesse in malis est esse magis miserum.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 109 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33009)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Daemonibus sit illuminatio. Illuminatio enim consistit in manifestatione veritatis. Sed unus Daemon potest alteri veritatem manifestare, quia superiores magis acumine naturalis scientiae vigent. Ergo superiores Daemones possunt inferiores illuminare.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33010)\n\nPraeterea, corpus quod superabundat in lumine, potest illuminare corpus quod in lumine deficit; sicut sol lunam. Sed superiores Daemones magis abundant in participatione luminis naturalis. Ergo videtur quod superiores Daemones possunt inferiores illuminare.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33011)\n\nSed contra, illuminatio cum purgatione est et perfectione, ut supra dictum est. Sed purgare non convenit Daemonibus; secundum illud Eccli. XXXIV, *ab immundo quid mundabitur?* Ergo etiam neque illuminare.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33012)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in Daemonibus non potest esse illuminatio proprie. Dictum est enim supra quod illuminatio proprie est manifestatio veritatis, secundum quod habet ordinem ad Deum, qui illuminat omnem intellectum. Alia autem manifestatio veritatis potest esse locutio; sicut cum unus Angelus alteri suum conceptum manifestat. Perversitas autem Daemonum hoc habet, quod unus alium non intendit ordinare ad Deum, sed magis ab ordine divino abducere. Et ideo unus Daemon alium non illuminat; sed unus alii suum conceptum per modum locutionis intimare potest.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33013)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod non quaelibet veritatis manifestatio habet rationem illuminationis, sed solum quae dicta est.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33014)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod secundum ea quae ad naturalem cognitionem pertinent, non est necessaria manifestatio veritatis neque in Angelis neque in Daemonibus, quia, sicut supra dictum est, statim a principio suae conditionis omnia cognoverunt quae ad naturalem cognitionem pertinent. Et ideo maior plenitudo naturalis luminis quae est in superioribus Daemonibus, non potest esse ratio illuminationis.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 109 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33015)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod boni Angeli non habeant praelationem super malos. Praelatio enim Angelorum praecipue attenditur secundum illuminationes. Sed mali Angeli, cum sint tenebrae, non illuminantur a bonis. Ergo boni Angeli non habent praelationem super malos.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33016)\n\nPraeterea, ad negligentiam praesidentis pertinere videntur ea quae per subditos male fiunt. Sed Daemones multa mala faciunt. Si igitur subsunt praelationi bonorum Angelorum, videtur in Angelis bonis esse aliqua negligentia. Quod est inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33017)\n\nPraeterea, praelatio Angelorum sequitur naturae ordinem, ut supra dictum est. Sed si Daemones de singulis ordinibus ceciderunt, ut communiter dicitur, multi Daemones multis bonis Angelis sunt superiores ordine naturae. Non ergo boni Angeli praelationem habent super omnes malos.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33018)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, III de Trin., quod *spiritus vitae desertor atque peccator regitur per spiritum vitae rationalem, pium et iustum*. Et Gregorius dicit quod *potestates dicuntur Angeli quorum ditioni virtutes adversae subiectae sunt*.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33019)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod totus ordo praelationis primo et originaliter est in Deo, et participatur a creaturis secundum quod Deo magis appropinquant, illae enim creaturae super alias influentiam habent, quae sunt perfectiores et Deo propinquiores. Maxima autem perfectio, et per quam maxime Deo appropinquatur, est creaturarum fruentium Deo, sicut sunt sancti Angeli, qua perfectione Daemones privantur. Et ideo boni Angeli super malos praelationem habent, et per eos reguntur.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33020)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod per sanctos Angelos multa de divinis mysteriis Daemonibus revelantur, cum divina iustitia exigit ut per Daemones aliqua fiant vel ad punitionem malorum, vel ad exercitationem bonorum, sicut in rebus humanis assessores iudicis revelant tortoribus eius sententiam. Huiusmodi autem revelationes, si ad Angelos revelantes comparentur, illuminationes sunt, quia ordinant eas ad Deum. Ex parte vero Daemonum, non sunt illuminationes, quia eas in Deum non ordinant, sed ad expletionem propriae iniquitatis.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33021)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod sancti Angeli sunt ministri divinae sapientiae. Unde sicut divina sapientia permittit aliqua mala fieri per malos Angelos vel homines, propter bona quae ex eis elicit; ita et boni Angeli non totaliter cohibent malos a nocendo.\n\n[I q. 109 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33022)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Angelus qui est inferior ordine naturae, praeest Daemonibus, quamvis superioribus ordine naturae; quia virtus divinae iustitiae, cui inhaerent boni Angeli, potior est quam virtus naturalis Angelorum. Unde et apud homines, spiritualis iudicat omnia, ut dicitur I ad Cor. II. Et philosophus dicit, in libro Ethic., quod *virtuosus est regula et mensura omnium humanorum actuum*.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0002","text":"[I q. 109 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32992)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de ordinatione malorum Angelorum. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum ordines sint in Daemonibus. Secundo, utrum in eis sit praelatio. Tertio, utrum unus illuminet alium. Quarto, utrum subiiciantur praelationi bonorum Angelorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0005","text":"[I q. 109 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32993)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod ordines non sint in Daemonibus. Ordo enim pertinet ad rationem boni, sicut et modus et species, ut Augustinus dicit in libro de natura boni; et e contrario inordinatio pertinet ad rationem mali. Sed in bonis Angelis nihil est inordinatum. Ergo in malis Angelis non sunt aliqui ordines.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0007","text":"[I q. 109 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32994)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0008","text":"Praeterea, ordines angelici sub aliqua hierarchia continentur. Sed Daemones non sunt sub aliqua hierarchia, quae est sacer principatus, cum ab omni sanctitate sint vacui. Ergo in Daemonibus non sunt ordines.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0009","text":"[I q. 109 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32995)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0010","text":"Praeterea, Daemones de singulis ordinibus Angelorum ceciderunt, ut communiter dicitur. Si ergo aliqui Daemones dicuntur esse alicuius ordinis, quia de illo ordine ceciderunt; videtur quod deberent eis attribui nomina singulorum ordinum. Nunquam autem invenitur quod dicantur Seraphim, vel throni, vel dominationes. Ergo, pari ratione, non sunt in aliquibus ordinibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0011","text":"[I q. 109 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32996)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod apostolus dicit, ad Ephes. ult., quod *est nobis colluctatio adversus principes et potestates, adversus mundi rectores tenebrarum harum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0013","text":"[I q. 109 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32997)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut iam dictum est, ordo angelicus consideratur et secundum gradum naturae, et secundum gradum gratiae. Gratia vero habet duplicem statum, scilicet imperfectum, qui est status merendi; et perfectum, qui est status gloriae consummatae. Si ergo considerentur ordines angelici quantum ad perfectionem gloriae, sic Daemones neque sunt in ordinibus angelicis, neque unquam fuerunt. Si autem considerentur quantum ad id quod est gratiae imperfectae, sic Daemones fuerunt quidem aliquando in ordinibus Angelorum, sed ab eis ceciderunt; secundum illud quod supra posuimus, omnes Angelos in gratia creatos fuisse. Si autem considerentur quantum ad id quod est naturae, sic adhuc sunt in ordinibus, quia data naturalia non amiserunt, ut Dionysius dicit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0015","text":"[I q. 109 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32998)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0016","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod bonum potest inveniri sine malo; sed malum non potest inveniri sine bono, ut supra habitum est. Et ideo Daemones, inquantum habent naturam bonam, ordinati sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0017","text":"[I q. 109 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32999)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0018","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ordinatio Daemonum, si consideretur ex parte Dei ordinantis, est sacra, utitur enim Daemonibus propter seipsum. Sed ex parte voluntatis Daemonum, non est sacra, quia abutuntur sua natura ad malum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0019","text":"[I q. 109 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33000)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0020","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod nomen Seraphim imponitur ab ardore caritatis, nomen autem thronorum ab inhabitatione divina, nomen autem dominationum importat libertatem quandam, quae omnia opponuntur peccato. Et ideo peccantibus Angelis huiusmodi nomina non attribuuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0021","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0022","text":"[I q. 109 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33001)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0023","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Daemonibus non sit praelatio. Omnis enim praelatio est secundum aliquem ordinem iustitiae. Sed Daemones totaliter a iustitia ceciderunt. Ergo in eis non est praelatio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0024","text":"[I q. 109 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33002)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0025","text":"Praeterea, ubi non est obedientia et subiectio, non est praelatio. Haec autem sine concordia esse non possunt; quae in Daemonibus nulla est, secundum illud Prov. XIII, *inter superbos semper sunt iurgia*. Ergo in Daemonibus non est praelatio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0026","text":"[I q. 109 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33003)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0027","text":"Praeterea, si in eis est aliqua praelatio, aut hoc pertinet ad eorum naturam, aut ad eorum culpam vel poenam. Sed non ad eorum naturam, quia subiectio et servitus non est ex natura, sed est ex peccato subsecuta. Nec pertinet ad culpam vel poenam, quia sic superiores Daemones, qui magis peccaverunt, inferioribus subderentur. Non ergo est praelatio in Daemonibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0028","text":"[I q. 109 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33004)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0029","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Glossa, I ad Cor. XV, *quandiu durat mundus, Angeli Angelis, homines hominibus, et Daemones Daemonibus praesunt*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0030","text":"[I q. 109 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33005)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0031","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum actio sequatur naturam rei, quorumcumque naturae sunt ordinatae, oportet quod etiam actiones sub invicem ordinentur. Sicut patet in rebus corporalibus, quia enim inferiora corpora naturali ordine sunt infra corpora caelestia actiones et motus eorum subduntur actionibus et motibus caelestium corporum. Manifestum est autem ex praemissis quod Daemonum quidam naturali ordine sub aliis constituuntur. Unde et actiones eorum sub actionibus superiorum sunt. Et hoc est quod rationem praelationis facit, ut scilicet actio subditi subdatur actioni praelati. Sic igitur ipsa naturalis dispositio Daemonum requirit quod sit in eis praelatio. Convenit etiam hoc divinae sapientiae, quae nihil in universo inordinatum relinquit, *quae attingit a fine usque ad finem fortiter, et disponit omnia suaviter*, ut dicitur Sap. VIII.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0032","text":"[I q. 109 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33006)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0033","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod praelatio Daemonum non fundatur super eorum iustitia, sed super iustitia Dei cuncta ordinantis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0034","text":"[I q. 109 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33007)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0035","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod concordia Daemonum, qua quidam aliis obediunt, non est ex amicitia quam inter se habeant; sed ex communi nequitia, qua homines odiunt, et Dei iustitiae repugnant. Est enim proprium hominum impiorum, ut eis se adiungant et subiiciant, ad propriam nequitiam exequendam, quos potiores viribus vident.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0036","text":"[I q. 109 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33008)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0037","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Daemones non sunt aequales secundum naturam, unde in eis est naturalis praelatio. Quod in hominibus non contingit, qui natura sunt pares. Quod autem superioribus inferiores subdantur, non est ad bonum superiorum, sed magis ad malum eorum; quia cum mala facere maxime ad miseriam pertineat, praeesse in malis est esse magis miserum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0038","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0039","text":"[I q. 109 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33009)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0040","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod in Daemonibus sit illuminatio. Illuminatio enim consistit in manifestatione veritatis. Sed unus Daemon potest alteri veritatem manifestare, quia superiores magis acumine naturalis scientiae vigent. Ergo superiores Daemones possunt inferiores illuminare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0041","text":"[I q. 109 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33010)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0042","text":"Praeterea, corpus quod superabundat in lumine, potest illuminare corpus quod in lumine deficit; sicut sol lunam. Sed superiores Daemones magis abundant in participatione luminis naturalis. Ergo videtur quod superiores Daemones possunt inferiores illuminare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0043","text":"[I q. 109 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33011)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0044","text":"Sed contra, illuminatio cum purgatione est et perfectione, ut supra dictum est. Sed purgare non convenit Daemonibus; secundum illud Eccli. XXXIV, *ab immundo quid mundabitur?* Ergo etiam neque illuminare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0045","text":"[I q. 109 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33012)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0046","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in Daemonibus non potest esse illuminatio proprie. Dictum est enim supra quod illuminatio proprie est manifestatio veritatis, secundum quod habet ordinem ad Deum, qui illuminat omnem intellectum. Alia autem manifestatio veritatis potest esse locutio; sicut cum unus Angelus alteri suum conceptum manifestat. Perversitas autem Daemonum hoc habet, quod unus alium non intendit ordinare ad Deum, sed magis ab ordine divino abducere. Et ideo unus Daemon alium non illuminat; sed unus alii suum conceptum per modum locutionis intimare potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0047","text":"[I q. 109 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33013)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0048","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod non quaelibet veritatis manifestatio habet rationem illuminationis, sed solum quae dicta est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0049","text":"[I q. 109 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33014)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0050","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod secundum ea quae ad naturalem cognitionem pertinent, non est necessaria manifestatio veritatis neque in Angelis neque in Daemonibus, quia, sicut supra dictum est, statim a principio suae conditionis omnia cognoverunt quae ad naturalem cognitionem pertinent. Et ideo maior plenitudo naturalis luminis quae est in superioribus Daemonibus, non potest esse ratio illuminationis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0051","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0052","text":"[I q. 109 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33015)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0053","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod boni Angeli non habeant praelationem super malos. Praelatio enim Angelorum praecipue attenditur secundum illuminationes. Sed mali Angeli, cum sint tenebrae, non illuminantur a bonis. Ergo boni Angeli non habent praelationem super malos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0054","text":"[I q. 109 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33016)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0055","text":"Praeterea, ad negligentiam praesidentis pertinere videntur ea quae per subditos male fiunt. Sed Daemones multa mala faciunt. Si igitur subsunt praelationi bonorum Angelorum, videtur in Angelis bonis esse aliqua negligentia. Quod est inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0056","text":"[I q. 109 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33017)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0057","text":"Praeterea, praelatio Angelorum sequitur naturae ordinem, ut supra dictum est. Sed si Daemones de singulis ordinibus ceciderunt, ut communiter dicitur, multi Daemones multis bonis Angelis sunt superiores ordine naturae. Non ergo boni Angeli praelationem habent super omnes malos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0058","text":"[I q. 109 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33018)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0059","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, III de Trin., quod *spiritus vitae desertor atque peccator regitur per spiritum vitae rationalem, pium et iustum*. Et Gregorius dicit quod *potestates dicuntur Angeli quorum ditioni virtutes adversae subiectae sunt*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0060","text":"[I q. 109 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33019)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0061","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod totus ordo praelationis primo et originaliter est in Deo, et participatur a creaturis secundum quod Deo magis appropinquant, illae enim creaturae super alias influentiam habent, quae sunt perfectiores et Deo propinquiores. Maxima autem perfectio, et per quam maxime Deo appropinquatur, est creaturarum fruentium Deo, sicut sunt sancti Angeli, qua perfectione Daemones privantur. Et ideo boni Angeli super malos praelationem habent, et per eos reguntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0062","text":"[I q. 109 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33020)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0063","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod per sanctos Angelos multa de divinis mysteriis Daemonibus revelantur, cum divina iustitia exigit ut per Daemones aliqua fiant vel ad punitionem malorum, vel ad exercitationem bonorum, sicut in rebus humanis assessores iudicis revelant tortoribus eius sententiam. Huiusmodi autem revelationes, si ad Angelos revelantes comparentur, illuminationes sunt, quia ordinant eas ad Deum. Ex parte vero Daemonum, non sunt illuminationes, quia eas in Deum non ordinant, sed ad expletionem propriae iniquitatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0064","text":"[I q. 109 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33021)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0065","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod sancti Angeli sunt ministri divinae sapientiae. Unde sicut divina sapientia permittit aliqua mala fieri per malos Angelos vel homines, propter bona quae ex eis elicit; ita et boni Angeli non totaliter cohibent malos a nocendo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0066","text":"[I q. 109 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33022)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0067","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Angelus qui est inferior ordine naturae, praeest Daemonibus, quamvis superioribus ordine naturae; quia virtus divinae iustitiae, cui inhaerent boni Angeli, potior est quam virtus naturalis Angelorum. Unde et apud homines, spiritualis iudicat omnia, ut dicitur I ad Cor. II. Et philosophus dicit, in libro Ethic., quod *virtuosus est regula et mensura omnium humanorum actuum*.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9a1782aedc1f409f026b6066132341097776e742f6d25e78b8db9c2fa74076a7","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T154253918521Z_88cd75d9","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 109","source_sha256":"9a1782aedc1f409f026b6066132341097776e742f6d25e78b8db9c2fa74076a7","sha256":"fd498c2f3dd46dfd8c9f683fecc8ab76c076fa72e05392395004f69fecdcb557","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:42:53.918532+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0002","text":"[I q. 109 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32992)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0003","text":"Next, the ordering of the evil angels must be considered. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, whether there are orders among the demons. Second, whether there is prelacy among them. Third, whether one illuminates another. Fourth, whether they are subject to the prelacy of the good angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0005","text":"[I q. 109 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32993)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0006","text":"The first is proceeded with thus. It seems that there are no orders among the demons. For order pertains to the notion of good, as do mode and species, as Augustine says in the book *On the Nature of the Good*; and, conversely, disorder pertains to the notion of evil. But among the good angels nothing is disordered. Therefore among the evil angels there are no orders.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0007","text":"[I q. 109 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32994)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0008","text":"Further, the angelic orders are contained under some hierarchy. But the demons are not under any hierarchy, which is a sacred principality, since they are devoid of all holiness. Therefore there are no orders among the demons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0009","text":"[I q. 109 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32995)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0010","text":"Further, demons fell from each of the orders of angels, as is commonly said. Therefore, if some demons are said to be of a certain order because they fell from that order, it seems that the names of the individual orders ought to be attributed to them. Yet one never finds them called Seraphim, or Thrones, or Dominations. Therefore, for the same reason, they are not in any orders.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0011","text":"[I q. 109 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32996)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, the Apostle says, at the end of Ephesians, that *our struggle is against principalities and powers, against the rulers of the world of this darkness*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0013","text":"[I q. 109 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32997)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0014","text":"I answer that, as has already been said, angelic order is considered both according to the grade of nature and according to the grade of grace. Grace, however, has a twofold state: namely, an imperfect one, which is the state of meriting; and a perfect one, which is the state of consummated glory. If, therefore, the angelic orders are considered as to the perfection of glory, then demons neither are in the angelic orders nor ever were. But if they are considered as to that which belongs to imperfect grace, then demons were indeed at one time in the orders of angels, but fell from them; according to what we have set forth above, that all angels were created in grace. But if they are considered as to that which belongs to nature, then they are still in the orders, because they have not lost their natural endowments, as Dionysius says.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0015","text":"[I q. 109 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32998)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0016","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that good can be found without evil; but evil cannot be found without good, as was established above. And therefore demons, insofar as they have a good nature, are ordered.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0017","text":"[I q. 109 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#32999)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0018","text":"To the second, it must be said that the ordering of demons, if considered on the part of God who orders, is sacred; for He makes use of demons for His own sake. But on the part of the will of the demons, it is not sacred, because they misuse their nature for evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0019","text":"[I q. 109 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33000)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0020","text":"To the third, it must be said that the name Seraphim is imposed from the ardor of charity, whereas the name Thrones is imposed from divine indwelling, and the name Dominations signifies a certain freedom—all of which are opposed to sin. And therefore such names are not attributed to sinning angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0021","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0022","text":"[I q. 109 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33001)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0023","text":"The second is proceeded with thus. It seems that there is no prelacy among the demons. For every prelacy is according to some order of justice. But demons have fallen entirely from justice. Therefore there is no prelacy among them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0024","text":"[I q. 109 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33002)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0025","text":"Moreover, where there is no obedience and subjection, there is no prelacy. But these cannot exist without concord; and among the Demons there is none, according to Proverbs XIII: *among the proud there are always quarrels*. Therefore there is no prelacy among the Demons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0026","text":"[I q. 109 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33003)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0027","text":"Moreover, if there is any prelacy among them, this pertains either to their nature, or to their fault or punishment. But it does not pertain to their nature, because subjection and servitude are not from nature, but followed upon sin. Nor does it pertain to fault or punishment, because in this way the superior Demons, who sinned more, would be subjected to the inferior ones. Therefore there is no prelacy among the Demons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0028","text":"[I q. 109 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33004)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0029","text":"On the contrary, the Gloss says, on I Corinthians XV: *as long as the world endures, Angels preside over Angels, men over men, and Demons over Demons*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0030","text":"[I q. 109 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33005)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0031","text":"I answer that, since action follows the nature of a thing, wherever natures are ordered, the actions too must be ordered among themselves. This is evident in corporeal things: for because lower bodies are, by natural order, beneath heavenly bodies, their actions and motions are subject to the actions and motions of heavenly bodies. Now it is clear from what has been said that some of the Demons are established by natural order under others. Hence their actions too are under the actions of their superiors. And this is what constitutes the notion of prelacy: namely, that the action of the subject be subject to the action of the prelate. Thus, therefore, the very natural disposition of the Demons requires that there be prelacy among them. This also accords with divine wisdom, which leaves nothing disordered in the universe, *which reaches mightily from end to end, and orders all things sweetly*, as is said in Wisdom VIII.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0032","text":"[I q. 109 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33006)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0033","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the prelacy of the Demons is founded not upon their justice, but upon the justice of God ordering all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0034","text":"[I q. 109 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33007)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0035","text":"To the second it must be said that the concord of the Demons, by which some obey others, does not arise from friendship that they have among themselves, but from their common wickedness, by which they hate men and resist the justice of God. For it is characteristic of wicked men that they join and subject themselves to those whom they see to be more powerful in strength, in order to carry out their own wickedness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0036","text":"[I q. 109 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33008)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0037","text":"To the third it must be said that the Demons are not equal according to nature; hence there is natural prelacy among them. This does not occur among men, who are equal by nature. But the fact that inferiors are subjected to superiors is not for the good of the superiors, but rather for their evil; because, since to do evil pertains most especially to misery, to preside in evils is to be more miserable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0038","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0039","text":"[I q. 109 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33009)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0040","text":"We proceed to the third thus. It seems that there is illumination among the Demons. For illumination consists in the manifestation of truth. But one Demon can manifest truth to another, because the superiors are stronger in the acuteness of natural knowledge. Therefore the superior Demons can illuminate the inferior ones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0041","text":"[I q. 109 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33010)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0042","text":"Moreover, a body that abounds in light can illuminate a body deficient in light, as the sun does the moon. But the superior Demons abound more in participation in natural light. Therefore it seems that the superior Demons can illuminate the inferior ones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0043","text":"[I q. 109 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33011)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0044","text":"On the contrary, illumination is with purification and perfection, as was said above. But to purify does not belong to Demons, according to Ecclesiasticus XXXIV: *what shall be cleansed by one who is unclean?* Therefore neither does it belong to them to illuminate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0045","text":"[I q. 109 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33012)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0046","text":"I answer that there cannot properly be illumination among the Demons. For it was said above that illumination, properly speaking, is the manifestation of truth insofar as it has an ordering to God, who illuminates every intellect. But another manifestation of truth can be speech, as when one Angel manifests his concept to another. Yet the perversity of the Demons is such that one does not intend to order another to God, but rather to draw him away from the divine order. And therefore one Demon does not illuminate another; but one can disclose his concept to another by way of speech.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0047","text":"[I q. 109 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33013)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0048","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that not every manifestation of truth has the character of illumination, but only that which has been stated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0049","text":"[I q. 109 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33014)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0050","text":"To the second, it must be said that, with respect to those things that pertain to natural knowledge, a manifestation of the truth is necessary neither in the Angels nor in the Demons, because, as was said above, from the very beginning of their creation they knew at once all things that pertain to natural knowledge. And therefore the greater fullness of natural light which is in the higher Demons cannot be a reason for illumination.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0051","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0052","text":"[I q. 109 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33015)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0053","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that the good Angels do not have preeminence over the evil ones. For the preeminence of Angels is considered chiefly according to illuminations. But evil Angels, since they are darkness, are not illuminated by the good. Therefore good Angels do not have preeminence over the evil ones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0054","text":"[I q. 109 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33016)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0055","text":"Further, those things which are done badly by subjects seem to pertain to the negligence of the one presiding. But Demons do many evil things. Therefore, if they are subject to the preeminence of good Angels, there seems to be some negligence in the good Angels. Which is unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0056","text":"[I q. 109 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33017)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0057","text":"Further, the preeminence of Angels follows the order of nature, as was said above. But if Demons fell from each of the orders, as is commonly said, many Demons are superior in the order of nature to many good Angels. Therefore good Angels do not have preeminence over all the evil ones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0058","text":"[I q. 109 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33018)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0059","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in Book III of *On the Trinity*, that *the apostate and sinful spirit of life is governed by the rational, pious, and just spirit of life*. And Gregory says that *the Powers are called those Angels to whose dominion the adverse powers are subject*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0060","text":"[I q. 109 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33019)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0061","text":"I answer that the whole order of preeminence is first and originally in God, and is participated in by creatures according as they draw nearer to God; for those creatures have influence over others which are more perfect and nearer to God. But the greatest perfection, and that by which one draws nearest to God, belongs to creatures enjoying God, such as the holy Angels, of which perfection Demons are deprived. And therefore good Angels have preeminence over the evil ones, and the latter are governed through them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0062","text":"[I q. 109 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33020)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0063","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that through the holy Angels many things concerning the divine mysteries are revealed to Demons, when divine justice requires that certain things be done through Demons, either for the punishment of the wicked or for the exercise of the good, just as in human affairs the judge's assessors disclose his sentence to his executioners. Such revelations, however, if compared with the Angels who reveal them, are illuminations, because they order them to God. But on the part of the Demons, they are not illuminations, because they do not order them to God, but to the fulfillment of their own iniquity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0064","text":"[I q. 109 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33021)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0065","text":"To the second, it must be said that the holy Angels are ministers of divine wisdom. Hence, just as divine wisdom permits certain evils to be done by evil Angels or men, on account of the goods which it draws forth from them, so also the good Angels do not wholly restrain the evil ones from doing harm.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0066","text":"[I q. 109 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33022)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__101:P0067","text":"To the third, it must be said that an Angel who is inferior in the order of nature presides over Demons, even those superior in the order of nature, because the power of divine justice, to which the good Angels adhere, is more potent than the natural power of Angels. Hence also among human beings, the spiritual person judges all things, as is said in I Corinthians II. And the Philosopher says, in the book of the *Ethics*, that *the virtuous man is the rule and measure of all human acts*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__101:P0067"]}],"source_digest":"ea1dc4cb113d09980c8307c345e8036be747e7f42f72a679d21b5421189ee724","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 109"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 109","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102","title":"First Part · Question 110","order":111,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC__102","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 110 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33023)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de praesidentia Angelorum super creaturam corporalem. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum creatura corporalis administretur per Angelos. Secundo, utrum creatura corporalis obediat Angelis ad nutum. Tertio, utrum Angeli sua virtute possint immediate movere corpora localiter. Quarto, utrum Angeli boni vel mali possint facere miracula.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 110 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33024)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod creatura corporalis non administretur per Angelos. Res enim quae habent determinatum modum operandi, non indigent gubernari ab aliquo praesidente, ideo enim indigemus gubernari, ne aliter operemur quam oporteat. Sed res corporales habent determinatas actiones ex naturis sibi divinitus datis. Non ergo indigent gubernatione Angelorum.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33025)\n\nPraeterea, inferiora in entibus gubernantur per superiora. Sed in corporibus quaedam dicuntur inferiora, quaedam superiora. Inferiora igitur gubernantur per superiora. Non ergo est necessarium quod gubernentur per Angelos.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33026)\n\nPraeterea, diversi ordines Angelorum distinguuntur secundum diversa officia. Sed si creaturae corporales administrantur per Angelos, tot erunt officia Angelorum, quot sunt species rerum. Ergo etiam tot erunt ordines Angelorum, quot sunt species rerum. Quod est contra supra dicta. Non ergo corporalis creatura administratur per Angelos.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33027)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, III de Trin., quod *omnia corpora reguntur per spiritum vitae rationalem*. Et Gregorius dicit, in IV Dial., quod *in hoc mundo visibili nihil nisi per creaturam invisibilem disponi potest*.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33028)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod tam in rebus humanis quam in rebus naturalibus, hoc communiter invenitur, quod potestas particularis gubernatur et regitur a potestate universali; sicut potestas ballivi gubernatur per potestatem regis. Et in Angelis etiam est dictum quod superiores Angeli, qui praesunt inferioribus, habent scientiam magis universalem. Manifestum est autem quod virtus cuiuslibet corporis est magis particularis quam virtus spiritualis substantiae, nam omnis forma corporalis est forma individuata per materiam, et determinata ad hic et nunc; formae autem immateriales sunt absolutae et intelligibiles. Et ideo sicut inferiores Angeli, qui habent formas minus universales, reguntur per superiores; ita omnia corporalia reguntur per Angelos et hoc non solum a sanctis doctoribus ponitur, sed etiam ab omnibus philosophis qui incorporeas substantias posuerunt.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33029)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod res corporales habent determinatas actiones, sed has actiones non exercent nisi secundum quod moventur, quia proprium corporis est quod non agat nisi motum. Et ideo oportet quod creatura corporalis a spirituali moveatur.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33030)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ratio ista procedit secundum opinionem Aristotelis, qui posuit quod corpora caelestia moventur a substantiis spiritualibus; quarum numerum conatus fuit assignare secundum numerum motuum qui apparent in corporibus caelestibus. Sed non posuit quod essent aliquae substantiae spirituales quae haberent immediatam praesidentiam supra inferiora corpora, nisi forte animas humanas. Et hoc ideo, quia non consideravit aliquas operationes in inferioribus corporibus exerceri nisi naturales, ad quas sufficiebat motus corporum caelestium. Sed quia nos ponimus multa in corporibus inferioribus fieri praeter naturales actiones corporum, ad quae non sufficiunt virtutes caelestium corporum; ideo secundum nos, necesse est ponere quod Angeli habeant immediatam praesidentiam non solum supra caelestia corpora, sed etiam supra corpora inferiora.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33031)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod de substantiis immaterialibus diversimode philosophi sunt locuti. Plato enim posuit substantias immateriales esse rationes et species sensibilium corporum, et quasdam aliis universaliores, et ideo posuit substantias immateriales habere praesidentiam immediatam circa omnia sensibilia corpora, et diversas circa diversa. Aristoteles autem posuit quod substantiae immateriales non sunt species corporum sensibilium, sed aliquid altius et universalius, et ideo non attribuit eis immediatam praesidentiam supra singula corpora, sed solum supra universalia agentia, quae sunt corpora caelestia. Avicenna vero mediam viam secutus est. Posuit enim cum Platone, aliquam substantiam spiritualem praesidentem immediate sphaerae activorum et passivorum; eo quod, sicut Plato ponebat quod formae horum sensibilium derivantur a substantiis immaterialibus, ita etiam Avicenna hoc posuit. Sed in hoc a Platone differt, quod posuit unam tantum substantiam immaterialem praesidentem omnibus corporibus inferioribus, quam vocavit intelligentiam agentem. Doctores autem sancti posuerunt, sicut et Platonici, diversis rebus corporeis diversas substantias spirituales esse praepositas. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., *unaquaeque res visibilis in hoc mundo habet angelicam potestatem sibi praepositam*. Et Damascenus dicit, *Diabolus erat ex iis angelicis virtutibus quae praeerant terrestri ordini*. Et Origenes dicit, super illud Num. XXII, *cum vidisset asina Angelum, quod opus est mundo Angelis, qui praesunt super bestias, et praesunt animalium nativitati, et virgultorum et plantationum et ceterarum rerum incrementis*. Sed hoc non est ponendum propter hoc, quod secundum suam naturam unus Angelus magis se habeat ad praesidendum animalibus quam plantis, quia quilibet Angelus, etiam minimus, habet altiorem virtutem et universaliorem quam aliquod genus corporalium. Sed est ex ordine divinae sapientiae, quae diversis rebus diversos rectores praeposuit. Nec tamen propter hoc sequitur quod sint plures ordines Angelorum quam novem, quia, sicut supra dictum est, ordines distinguuntur secundum generalia officia. Unde sicut, secundum Gregorium, ad ordinem potestatum pertinent omnes Angeli qui habent proprie praesidentiam super Daemones; ita ad ordinem virtutum pertinere videntur omnes Angeli qui habent praesidentiam super res pure corporeas; horum enim ministerio interdum etiam miracula fiunt.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 110 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33032)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod materia corporalis obediat Angelis ad nutum. Maior est enim virtus Angeli quam virtus animae. Sed conceptioni animae obedit materia corporalis, immutatur enim corpus hominis ex conceptione animae, ad calorem et frigus, et quandoque usque ad sanitatem et aegritudinem. Ergo multo magis secundum conceptionem Angeli, materia corporalis transmutatur.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33033)\n\nPraeterea, quidquid potest virtus inferior, potest virtus superior. Sed virtus Angeli est superior quam virtus corporalis. Corpus autem sua virtute potest transmutare materiam corporalem ad formam aliquam; sicut cum ignis generat ignem. Ergo multo fortius virtute sua Angeli possunt materiam corporalem transmutare ad aliquam formam.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33034)\n\nPraeterea, tota natura corporalis administratur per Angelos, ut dictum est, et sic videtur quod corpora se habet ad Angelos sicut instrumenta; nam ratio instrumenti est quod sit movens motum. Sed in effectibus aliquid invenitur ex virtute principalium agentium, quod non potest esse per virtutem instrumenti; et hoc est id quod est principalius in effectu. Sicut digestio nutrimenti est per virtutem caloris naturalis, qui est instrumentum animae nutritivae; sed quod generetur caro viva, est ex virtute animae. Similiter quod secetur lignum, pertinet ad serram; sed quod perveniatur ad formam lecti, est ex ratione artis. Ergo forma substantialis, quae est principalius in effectibus corporalibus, est ex virtute Angelorum. Materia ergo obedit, ad sui informationem, Angelis.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33035)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, III de Trin., *non est putandum istis transgressoribus Angelis ad nutum servire hanc visibilium rerum materiam, sed soli Deo*.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33036)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Platonici posuerunt formas quae sunt in materia, causari ex immaterialibus formis, quia formas materiales ponebant esse participationes quasdam immaterialium formarum. Et hos, quantum ad aliquid, secutus est Avicenna, qui posuit omnes formas quae sunt in materia, procedere a conceptione intelligentiae, et quod agentia corporalia sunt solum disponentia ad formas. Qui in hoc videntur fuisse decepti, quia existimaverunt formam quasi aliquid per se factum, ut sic ab aliquo formali principio procederet. Sed sicut philosophus probat in VII Metaphys., hoc quod proprie fit, est compositum, hoc enim proprie est quasi subsistens. Forma autem non dicitur ens quasi ipsa sit, sed sicut quo aliquid est, et sic per consequens nec forma proprie fit; eius enim est fieri, cuius est esse, cum fieri nihil aliud sit quam via in esse. Manifestum est autem quod factum est simile facienti, quia omne agens agit sibi simile. Et ideo id quod facit res naturales, habet similitudinem cum composito, vel quia est compositum, sicut ignis generat ignem; vel quia totum compositum, et quantum ad materiam et quantum ad formam, est in virtute ipsius; quod est proprium Dei. Sic igitur omnis informatio materiae vel est a Deo immediate, vel ab aliquo agente corporali; non autem immediate ab Angelo.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33037)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod anima nostra unitur corpori ut forma; et sic non est mirum, si formaliter transmutatur ex conceptione ipsius; praesertim cum motus sensitivi appetitus, qui fit cum quadam transmutatione corporali, subdatur imperio rationis. Angelus autem non sic se habet ad corpora naturalia. Unde ratio non sequitur.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33038)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod id quod potest virtus inferior, potest superior non eodem, sed excellentiori modo, sicut intellectus cognoscit sensibilia excellentiori modo quam sensus. Et sic Angelus excellentiori modo transmutat materiam corporalem quam agentia corporalia, scilicet movendo ipsa agentia corporalia, tanquam causa superior.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33039)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod nihil prohibet ex virtute Angelorum aliquos effectus sequi in rebus naturalibus, ad quae agentia corporalia non sufficerent. Sed hoc non est obedire materiam Angelis ad nutum; sicut nec coquis obedit materia ad nutum, quia aliquem modum decoctionis operantur per ignem secundum aliquam artis moderationem, quam ignis per se non faceret, cum reducere materiam in actum formae substantialis, non excedat virtutem corporalis agentis, quia simile natum est sibi simile facere.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 110 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33040)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod corpora non obediant Angelis ad motum localem. Motus enim localis corporum naturalium sequitur formas eorum. Sed Angeli non causant formas corporum naturalium, ut dictum est. Ergo neque possunt causare in eis motum localem.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33041)\n\nPraeterea, in VIII Physic. probatur quod motus localis est primus motuum. Sed Angeli non possunt causare alios motus, transmutando materiam formaliter. Ergo neque etiam possunt causare motum localem.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33042)\n\nPraeterea, membra corporalia obediunt conceptioni animae ad motum localem, inquantum habent in seipsis aliquod principium vitae. In corporibus autem naturalibus non est aliquod principium vitae. Ergo non obediunt Angelis ad motum localem.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33043)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, III de Trin., quod Angeli adhibent semina corporalia ad aliquos effectus producendos. Sed hoc non possunt facere nisi localiter movendo. Ergo corpora obediunt eis ad motum localem.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33044)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut Dionysius dicit VII cap. de Div. Nom., *divina sapientia coniungit fines primorum principiis secundorum*, ex quo patet quod natura inferior in sui supremo attingitur a natura superiori. Natura autem corporalis est infra naturam spiritualem. Inter omnes autem motus corporeos perfectior est motus localis, ut probatur in VIII Physic., cuius ratio est, quia mobile secundum locum non est in potentia ad aliquid intrinsecum, inquantum huiusmodi, sed solum ad aliquid extrinsecum, scilicet ad locum. Et ideo natura corporalis nata est moveri immediate a natura spirituali secundum locum. Unde et philosophi posuerunt suprema corpora moveri localiter a spiritualibus substantiis. Unde videmus quod anima movet corpus primo et principaliter locali motu.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33045)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in corporibus sunt alii motus locales praeter eos qui consequuntur formas, sicut fluxus et refluxus maris non consequitur formam substantialem aquae, sed virtutem lunae. Et multo magis aliqui motus locales consequi possunt virtutem spiritualium substantiarum.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33046)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Angeli, causando motum localem tanquam priorem, per eum causare possunt alios motus, adhibendo scilicet agentia corporalia ad huiusmodi effectus producendos; sicut faber adhibet ignem ad emollitionem ferri.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33047)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Angeli habent virtutem minus contractam quam animae. Unde virtus motiva animae contrahitur ad corpus unitum, quod per eam vivificatur, quo mediante alia potest movere. Sed virtus Angeli non est contracta ad aliquod corpus. Unde potest corpora non coniuncta localiter movere.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 110 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33048)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli possunt facere miracula. Dicit enim Gregorius, *virtutes vocantur illi spiritus, per quos signa et miracula frequentius fiunt*.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33049)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, in libro octoginta trium quaestionum, *quod magi faciunt miracula per privatos contractus, boni Christiani per publicam iustitiam, mali Christiani per signa publicae iustitiae*. Sed magi faciunt miracula per hoc quod exaudiuntur a Daemonibus, ut ipse alibi in eodem libro dicit. Ergo Daemones miracula possunt facere. Ergo multo magis Angeli boni.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33050)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus in eodem libro dicit quod *omnia quae visibiliter fiunt, etiam per inferiores potestates aeris huius non absurde fieri posse creduntur*. Sed quando effectus aliquis naturalium causarum producitur absque ordine naturalis causae, hoc dicimus esse miraculum; puta cum aliquis sanatur a febre, non per operationem naturae. Ergo Angeli et Daemones possunt facere miracula.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33051)\n\nPraeterea, virtus superior non subditur ordini inferioris causae. Sed natura corporalis est inferior Angelo. Ergo Angelus potest operari praeter ordinem corporalium agentium. Quod est miracula facere.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33052)\n\nSed contra est quod de Deo dicitur in Psalmo, *qui facit mirabilia magna solus*.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33053)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod miraculum proprie dicitur, cum aliquid fit praeter ordinem naturae. Sed non sufficit ad rationem miraculi si aliquid fiat praeter ordinem naturae alicuius particularis, quia sic, cum aliquis proiicit lapidem sursum, miraculum faceret, cum hoc sit praeter ordinem naturae lapidis. Ex hoc ergo aliquid dicitur esse miraculum, quod fit praeter ordinem totius naturae creatae. Hoc autem non potest facere nisi Deus, quia quidquid facit Angelus, vel quaecumque alia creatura, propria virtute, hoc fit secundum ordinem naturae creatae; et sic non est miraculum. Unde relinquitur quod solus Deus miracula facere possit.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33054)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Angeli aliqui dicuntur miracula facere, vel quia ad eorum desiderium Deus miracula facit, sicut et sancti homines dicuntur miracula facere. Vel quia aliquod ministerium exhibent in miraculis quae fiunt; sicut colligendo pulveres in resurrectione communi, vel huiusmodi aliquid agendo.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33055)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod miracula simpliciter loquendo, dicuntur, ut dictum est, cum aliqua fiunt praeter ordinem totius naturae creatae. Sed quia non omnis virtus naturae creatae est nota nobis, ideo cum aliquid fit praeter ordinem naturae creatae nobis notae, per virtutem creatam nobis ignotam, est miraculum quoad nos. Sic igitur cum Daemones aliquid faciunt sua virtute naturali, miracula dicuntur non simpliciter, sed quoad nos. Et hoc modo magi per Daemones miracula faciunt. Et dicuntur fieri per privatos contractus, quia quaelibet virtus creaturae in universo se habet ut virtus alicuius privatae personae in civitate; unde cum magus aliquid facit per pactum initum cum Daemone, hoc fit quasi per quendam privatum contractum. Sed iustitia divina est in toto universo sicut lex publica in civitate. Et ideo boni Christiani, inquantum per iustitiam divinam miracula faciunt, dicuntur facere miracula per publicam iustitiam. Mali autem Christiani per signa publicae iustitiae, sicut invocando nomen Christi, vel exhibendo aliqua sacramenta.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33056)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod spirituales potestates possunt facere ea quae visibiliter fiunt in hoc mundo, adhibendo corporalia semina per motum localem.\n\n[I q. 110 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33057)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod, licet Angeli possint aliquid facere praeter ordinem naturae corporalis, non tamen possunt aliquid facere praeter ordinem totius creaturae, quod exigitur ad rationem miraculi, ut dictum est.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0002","text":"[I q. 110 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33023)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de praesidentia Angelorum super creaturam corporalem. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum creatura corporalis administretur per Angelos. Secundo, utrum creatura corporalis obediat Angelis ad nutum. Tertio, utrum Angeli sua virtute possint immediate movere corpora localiter. Quarto, utrum Angeli boni vel mali possint facere miracula.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0005","text":"[I q. 110 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33024)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod creatura corporalis non administretur per Angelos. Res enim quae habent determinatum modum operandi, non indigent gubernari ab aliquo praesidente, ideo enim indigemus gubernari, ne aliter operemur quam oporteat. Sed res corporales habent determinatas actiones ex naturis sibi divinitus datis. Non ergo indigent gubernatione Angelorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0007","text":"[I q. 110 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33025)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0008","text":"Praeterea, inferiora in entibus gubernantur per superiora. Sed in corporibus quaedam dicuntur inferiora, quaedam superiora. Inferiora igitur gubernantur per superiora. Non ergo est necessarium quod gubernentur per Angelos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0009","text":"[I q. 110 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33026)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0010","text":"Praeterea, diversi ordines Angelorum distinguuntur secundum diversa officia. Sed si creaturae corporales administrantur per Angelos, tot erunt officia Angelorum, quot sunt species rerum. Ergo etiam tot erunt ordines Angelorum, quot sunt species rerum. Quod est contra supra dicta. Non ergo corporalis creatura administratur per Angelos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0011","text":"[I q. 110 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33027)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, III de Trin., quod *omnia corpora reguntur per spiritum vitae rationalem*. Et Gregorius dicit, in IV Dial., quod *in hoc mundo visibili nihil nisi per creaturam invisibilem disponi potest*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0013","text":"[I q. 110 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33028)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod tam in rebus humanis quam in rebus naturalibus, hoc communiter invenitur, quod potestas particularis gubernatur et regitur a potestate universali; sicut potestas ballivi gubernatur per potestatem regis. Et in Angelis etiam est dictum quod superiores Angeli, qui praesunt inferioribus, habent scientiam magis universalem. Manifestum est autem quod virtus cuiuslibet corporis est magis particularis quam virtus spiritualis substantiae, nam omnis forma corporalis est forma individuata per materiam, et determinata ad hic et nunc; formae autem immateriales sunt absolutae et intelligibiles. Et ideo sicut inferiores Angeli, qui habent formas minus universales, reguntur per superiores; ita omnia corporalia reguntur per Angelos et hoc non solum a sanctis doctoribus ponitur, sed etiam ab omnibus philosophis qui incorporeas substantias posuerunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0015","text":"[I q. 110 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33029)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0016","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod res corporales habent determinatas actiones, sed has actiones non exercent nisi secundum quod moventur, quia proprium corporis est quod non agat nisi motum. Et ideo oportet quod creatura corporalis a spirituali moveatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0017","text":"[I q. 110 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33030)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0018","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ratio ista procedit secundum opinionem Aristotelis, qui posuit quod corpora caelestia moventur a substantiis spiritualibus; quarum numerum conatus fuit assignare secundum numerum motuum qui apparent in corporibus caelestibus. Sed non posuit quod essent aliquae substantiae spirituales quae haberent immediatam praesidentiam supra inferiora corpora, nisi forte animas humanas. Et hoc ideo, quia non consideravit aliquas operationes in inferioribus corporibus exerceri nisi naturales, ad quas sufficiebat motus corporum caelestium. Sed quia nos ponimus multa in corporibus inferioribus fieri praeter naturales actiones corporum, ad quae non sufficiunt virtutes caelestium corporum; ideo secundum nos, necesse est ponere quod Angeli habeant immediatam praesidentiam non solum supra caelestia corpora, sed etiam supra corpora inferiora.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0019","text":"[I q. 110 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33031)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0020","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod de substantiis immaterialibus diversimode philosophi sunt locuti. Plato enim posuit substantias immateriales esse rationes et species sensibilium corporum, et quasdam aliis universaliores, et ideo posuit substantias immateriales habere praesidentiam immediatam circa omnia sensibilia corpora, et diversas circa diversa. Aristoteles autem posuit quod substantiae immateriales non sunt species corporum sensibilium, sed aliquid altius et universalius, et ideo non attribuit eis immediatam praesidentiam supra singula corpora, sed solum supra universalia agentia, quae sunt corpora caelestia. Avicenna vero mediam viam secutus est. Posuit enim cum Platone, aliquam substantiam spiritualem praesidentem immediate sphaerae activorum et passivorum; eo quod, sicut Plato ponebat quod formae horum sensibilium derivantur a substantiis immaterialibus, ita etiam Avicenna hoc posuit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0021","text":"Sed in hoc a Platone differt, quod posuit unam tantum substantiam immaterialem praesidentem omnibus corporibus inferioribus, quam vocavit intelligentiam agentem. Doctores autem sancti posuerunt, sicut et Platonici, diversis rebus corporeis diversas substantias spirituales esse praepositas. Dicit enim Augustinus, in libro octoginta trium quaest., *unaquaeque res visibilis in hoc mundo habet angelicam potestatem sibi praepositam*. Et Damascenus dicit, *Diabolus erat ex iis angelicis virtutibus quae praeerant terrestri ordini*. Et Origenes dicit, super illud Num. XXII, *cum vidisset asina Angelum, quod opus est mundo Angelis, qui praesunt super bestias, et praesunt animalium nativitati, et virgultorum et plantationum et ceterarum rerum incrementis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0022","text":"Sed hoc non est ponendum propter hoc, quod secundum suam naturam unus Angelus magis se habeat ad praesidendum animalibus quam plantis, quia quilibet Angelus, etiam minimus, habet altiorem virtutem et universaliorem quam aliquod genus corporalium. Sed est ex ordine divinae sapientiae, quae diversis rebus diversos rectores praeposuit. Nec tamen propter hoc sequitur quod sint plures ordines Angelorum quam novem, quia, sicut supra dictum est, ordines distinguuntur secundum generalia officia. Unde sicut, secundum Gregorium, ad ordinem potestatum pertinent omnes Angeli qui habent proprie praesidentiam super Daemones; ita ad ordinem virtutum pertinere videntur omnes Angeli qui habent praesidentiam super res pure corporeas; horum enim ministerio interdum etiam miracula fiunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0023","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0024","text":"[I q. 110 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33032)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0025","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod materia corporalis obediat Angelis ad nutum. Maior est enim virtus Angeli quam virtus animae. Sed conceptioni animae obedit materia corporalis, immutatur enim corpus hominis ex conceptione animae, ad calorem et frigus, et quandoque usque ad sanitatem et aegritudinem. Ergo multo magis secundum conceptionem Angeli, materia corporalis transmutatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0026","text":"[I q. 110 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33033)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0027","text":"Praeterea, quidquid potest virtus inferior, potest virtus superior. Sed virtus Angeli est superior quam virtus corporalis. Corpus autem sua virtute potest transmutare materiam corporalem ad formam aliquam; sicut cum ignis generat ignem. Ergo multo fortius virtute sua Angeli possunt materiam corporalem transmutare ad aliquam formam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0028","text":"[I q. 110 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33034)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0029","text":"Praeterea, tota natura corporalis administratur per Angelos, ut dictum est, et sic videtur quod corpora se habet ad Angelos sicut instrumenta; nam ratio instrumenti est quod sit movens motum. Sed in effectibus aliquid invenitur ex virtute principalium agentium, quod non potest esse per virtutem instrumenti; et hoc est id quod est principalius in effectu. Sicut digestio nutrimenti est per virtutem caloris naturalis, qui est instrumentum animae nutritivae; sed quod generetur caro viva, est ex virtute animae. Similiter quod secetur lignum, pertinet ad serram; sed quod perveniatur ad formam lecti, est ex ratione artis. Ergo forma substantialis, quae est principalius in effectibus corporalibus, est ex virtute Angelorum. Materia ergo obedit, ad sui informationem, Angelis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0030","text":"[I q. 110 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33035)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0031","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, III de Trin., *non est putandum istis transgressoribus Angelis ad nutum servire hanc visibilium rerum materiam, sed soli Deo*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0032","text":"[I q. 110 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33036)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0033","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Platonici posuerunt formas quae sunt in materia, causari ex immaterialibus formis, quia formas materiales ponebant esse participationes quasdam immaterialium formarum. Et hos, quantum ad aliquid, secutus est Avicenna, qui posuit omnes formas quae sunt in materia, procedere a conceptione intelligentiae, et quod agentia corporalia sunt solum disponentia ad formas. Qui in hoc videntur fuisse decepti, quia existimaverunt formam quasi aliquid per se factum, ut sic ab aliquo formali principio procederet. Sed sicut philosophus probat in VII Metaphys., hoc quod proprie fit, est compositum, hoc enim proprie est quasi subsistens. Forma autem non dicitur ens quasi ipsa sit, sed sicut quo aliquid est, et sic per consequens nec forma proprie fit; eius enim est fieri, cuius est esse, cum fieri nihil aliud sit quam via in esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0034","text":"Manifestum est autem quod factum est simile facienti, quia omne agens agit sibi simile. Et ideo id quod facit res naturales, habet similitudinem cum composito, vel quia est compositum, sicut ignis generat ignem; vel quia totum compositum, et quantum ad materiam et quantum ad formam, est in virtute ipsius; quod est proprium Dei. Sic igitur omnis informatio materiae vel est a Deo immediate, vel ab aliquo agente corporali; non autem immediate ab Angelo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0035","text":"[I q. 110 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33037)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0036","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod anima nostra unitur corpori ut forma; et sic non est mirum, si formaliter transmutatur ex conceptione ipsius; praesertim cum motus sensitivi appetitus, qui fit cum quadam transmutatione corporali, subdatur imperio rationis. Angelus autem non sic se habet ad corpora naturalia. Unde ratio non sequitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0037","text":"[I q. 110 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33038)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0038","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod id quod potest virtus inferior, potest superior non eodem, sed excellentiori modo, sicut intellectus cognoscit sensibilia excellentiori modo quam sensus. Et sic Angelus excellentiori modo transmutat materiam corporalem quam agentia corporalia, scilicet movendo ipsa agentia corporalia, tanquam causa superior.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0039","text":"[I q. 110 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33039)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0040","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod nihil prohibet ex virtute Angelorum aliquos effectus sequi in rebus naturalibus, ad quae agentia corporalia non sufficerent. Sed hoc non est obedire materiam Angelis ad nutum; sicut nec coquis obedit materia ad nutum, quia aliquem modum decoctionis operantur per ignem secundum aliquam artis moderationem, quam ignis per se non faceret, cum reducere materiam in actum formae substantialis, non excedat virtutem corporalis agentis, quia simile natum est sibi simile facere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0041","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0042","text":"[I q. 110 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33040)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0043","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod corpora non obediant Angelis ad motum localem. Motus enim localis corporum naturalium sequitur formas eorum. Sed Angeli non causant formas corporum naturalium, ut dictum est. Ergo neque possunt causare in eis motum localem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0044","text":"[I q. 110 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33041)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0045","text":"Praeterea, in VIII Physic. probatur quod motus localis est primus motuum. Sed Angeli non possunt causare alios motus, transmutando materiam formaliter. Ergo neque etiam possunt causare motum localem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0046","text":"[I q. 110 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33042)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0047","text":"Praeterea, membra corporalia obediunt conceptioni animae ad motum localem, inquantum habent in seipsis aliquod principium vitae. In corporibus autem naturalibus non est aliquod principium vitae. Ergo non obediunt Angelis ad motum localem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0048","text":"[I q. 110 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33043)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0049","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, III de Trin., quod Angeli adhibent semina corporalia ad aliquos effectus producendos. Sed hoc non possunt facere nisi localiter movendo. Ergo corpora obediunt eis ad motum localem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0050","text":"[I q. 110 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33044)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0051","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut Dionysius dicit VII cap. de Div. Nom., *divina sapientia coniungit fines primorum principiis secundorum*, ex quo patet quod natura inferior in sui supremo attingitur a natura superiori. Natura autem corporalis est infra naturam spiritualem. Inter omnes autem motus corporeos perfectior est motus localis, ut probatur in VIII Physic., cuius ratio est, quia mobile secundum locum non est in potentia ad aliquid intrinsecum, inquantum huiusmodi, sed solum ad aliquid extrinsecum, scilicet ad locum. Et ideo natura corporalis nata est moveri immediate a natura spirituali secundum locum. Unde et philosophi posuerunt suprema corpora moveri localiter a spiritualibus substantiis. Unde videmus quod anima movet corpus primo et principaliter locali motu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0052","text":"[I q. 110 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33045)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0053","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in corporibus sunt alii motus locales praeter eos qui consequuntur formas, sicut fluxus et refluxus maris non consequitur formam substantialem aquae, sed virtutem lunae. Et multo magis aliqui motus locales consequi possunt virtutem spiritualium substantiarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0054","text":"[I q. 110 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33046)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0055","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Angeli, causando motum localem tanquam priorem, per eum causare possunt alios motus, adhibendo scilicet agentia corporalia ad huiusmodi effectus producendos; sicut faber adhibet ignem ad emollitionem ferri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0056","text":"[I q. 110 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33047)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0057","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Angeli habent virtutem minus contractam quam animae. Unde virtus motiva animae contrahitur ad corpus unitum, quod per eam vivificatur, quo mediante alia potest movere. Sed virtus Angeli non est contracta ad aliquod corpus. Unde potest corpora non coniuncta localiter movere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0058","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0059","text":"[I q. 110 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33048)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0060","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli possunt facere miracula. Dicit enim Gregorius, *virtutes vocantur illi spiritus, per quos signa et miracula frequentius fiunt*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0061","text":"[I q. 110 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33049)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0062","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, in libro octoginta trium quaestionum, *quod magi faciunt miracula per privatos contractus, boni Christiani per publicam iustitiam, mali Christiani per signa publicae iustitiae*. Sed magi faciunt miracula per hoc quod exaudiuntur a Daemonibus, ut ipse alibi in eodem libro dicit. Ergo Daemones miracula possunt facere. Ergo multo magis Angeli boni.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0063","text":"[I q. 110 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33050)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0064","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus in eodem libro dicit quod *omnia quae visibiliter fiunt, etiam per inferiores potestates aeris huius non absurde fieri posse creduntur*. Sed quando effectus aliquis naturalium causarum producitur absque ordine naturalis causae, hoc dicimus esse miraculum; puta cum aliquis sanatur a febre, non per operationem naturae. Ergo Angeli et Daemones possunt facere miracula.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0065","text":"[I q. 110 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33051)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0066","text":"Praeterea, virtus superior non subditur ordini inferioris causae. Sed natura corporalis est inferior Angelo. Ergo Angelus potest operari praeter ordinem corporalium agentium. Quod est miracula facere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0067","text":"[I q. 110 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33052)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0068","text":"Sed contra est quod de Deo dicitur in Psalmo, *qui facit mirabilia magna solus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0069","text":"[I q. 110 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33053)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0070","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod miraculum proprie dicitur, cum aliquid fit praeter ordinem naturae. Sed non sufficit ad rationem miraculi si aliquid fiat praeter ordinem naturae alicuius particularis, quia sic, cum aliquis proiicit lapidem sursum, miraculum faceret, cum hoc sit praeter ordinem naturae lapidis. Ex hoc ergo aliquid dicitur esse miraculum, quod fit praeter ordinem totius naturae creatae. Hoc autem non potest facere nisi Deus, quia quidquid facit Angelus, vel quaecumque alia creatura, propria virtute, hoc fit secundum ordinem naturae creatae; et sic non est miraculum. Unde relinquitur quod solus Deus miracula facere possit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0071","text":"[I q. 110 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33054)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0072","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Angeli aliqui dicuntur miracula facere, vel quia ad eorum desiderium Deus miracula facit, sicut et sancti homines dicuntur miracula facere. Vel quia aliquod ministerium exhibent in miraculis quae fiunt; sicut colligendo pulveres in resurrectione communi, vel huiusmodi aliquid agendo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0073","text":"[I q. 110 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33055)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0074","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod miracula simpliciter loquendo, dicuntur, ut dictum est, cum aliqua fiunt praeter ordinem totius naturae creatae. Sed quia non omnis virtus naturae creatae est nota nobis, ideo cum aliquid fit praeter ordinem naturae creatae nobis notae, per virtutem creatam nobis ignotam, est miraculum quoad nos. Sic igitur cum Daemones aliquid faciunt sua virtute naturali, miracula dicuntur non simpliciter, sed quoad nos. Et hoc modo magi per Daemones miracula faciunt. Et dicuntur fieri per privatos contractus, quia quaelibet virtus creaturae in universo se habet ut virtus alicuius privatae personae in civitate; unde cum magus aliquid facit per pactum initum cum Daemone, hoc fit quasi per quendam privatum contractum. Sed iustitia divina est in toto universo sicut lex publica in civitate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0075","text":"Et ideo boni Christiani, inquantum per iustitiam divinam miracula faciunt, dicuntur facere miracula per publicam iustitiam. Mali autem Christiani per signa publicae iustitiae, sicut invocando nomen Christi, vel exhibendo aliqua sacramenta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0076","text":"[I q. 110 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33056)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0077","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod spirituales potestates possunt facere ea quae visibiliter fiunt in hoc mundo, adhibendo corporalia semina per motum localem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0078","text":"[I q. 110 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33057)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0079","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod, licet Angeli possint aliquid facere praeter ordinem naturae corporalis, non tamen possunt aliquid facere praeter ordinem totius creaturae, quod exigitur ad rationem miraculi, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"3f100370fa2f5d046ef1cb26bdd147bbdbf46f4a4447dcecd7f19287c22a998f","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T154348775133Z_37576db4","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 110","source_sha256":"3f100370fa2f5d046ef1cb26bdd147bbdbf46f4a4447dcecd7f19287c22a998f","sha256":"7ce643c4d0db6289a70db9e9044ba39880c98428877adea80847ad724971265c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:43:48.775156+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0002","text":"[I q. 110 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33023)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0003","text":"Next, consideration must be given to the governance of the angels over bodily creatures. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, whether bodily creatures are administered by angels. Second, whether bodily creatures obey angels at their command. Third, whether angels can by their own power immediately move bodies locally. Fourth, whether good or evil angels can work miracles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0005","text":"[I q. 110 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33024)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0006","text":"The first is approached thus. It seems that bodily creatures are not administered by angels. For things that have a determinate mode of operating do not need to be governed by any presiding authority; for we need to be governed lest we act otherwise than we ought. But bodily things have determinate actions from the natures divinely given to them. Therefore, they do not need the governance of angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0007","text":"[I q. 110 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33025)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0008","text":"Further, lower things among beings are governed by higher things. But among bodies, some are called lower and some higher. Therefore the lower are governed by the higher. Therefore it is not necessary that they be governed by angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0009","text":"[I q. 110 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33026)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0010","text":"Further, the diverse orders of angels are distinguished according to diverse offices. But if bodily creatures are administered by angels, there will be as many offices of angels as there are species of things. Therefore, there will also be as many orders of angels as there are species of things. This is contrary to what was said above. Therefore, bodily creatures are not administered by angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0011","text":"[I q. 110 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33027)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0012","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, III, that *all bodies are governed by a rational spirit of life*. And Gregory says, in the *Dialogues*, IV, that *in this visible world nothing can be ordered except through an invisible creature*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0013","text":"[I q. 110 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33028)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that, both in human affairs and in natural things, this is commonly found: that a particular power is governed and ruled by a universal power, just as the power of a bailiff is governed by the power of a king. And it has also been said concerning angels that the higher angels, who preside over the lower, have more universal knowledge. Now it is manifest that the power of any body is more particular than the power of a spiritual substance, for every bodily form is a form individuated by matter and determined to here and now; whereas immaterial forms are absolute and intelligible. And therefore, just as the lower angels, which have less universal forms, are ruled by the higher, so all bodily things are ruled by angels. And this is maintained not only by the holy doctors, but also by all philosophers who posited incorporeal substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0015","text":"[I q. 110 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33029)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0016","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that bodily things have determinate actions, but they do not exercise these actions except insofar as they are moved, because it is proper to a body not to act except when moved. And therefore bodily creatures must be moved by spiritual creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0017","text":"[I q. 110 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33030)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0018","text":"To the second, it must be said that this argument proceeds according to the opinion of Aristotle, who held that the heavenly bodies are moved by spiritual substances, whose number he endeavored to assign according to the number of motions appearing in the heavenly bodies. But he did not hold that there were any spiritual substances having immediate governance over lower bodies, except perhaps human souls. And this was because he did not consider that any operations were exercised in lower bodies except natural ones, for which the motion of the heavenly bodies sufficed. But because we hold that many things come about in lower bodies beyond the natural actions of bodies, for which the powers of heavenly bodies do not suffice, it is therefore necessary, according to us, to hold that angels have immediate governance not only over heavenly bodies but also over lower bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0019","text":"[I q. 110 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33031)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0020","text":"To the third, it must be said that philosophers have spoken in different ways concerning immaterial substances. For Plato held that immaterial substances are the concepts and species of sensible bodies, and that some are more universal than others; and therefore he held that immaterial substances have immediate governance over all sensible bodies, and diverse substances over diverse bodies. Aristotle, however, held that immaterial substances are not the species of sensible bodies, but something higher and more universal; and therefore he did not attribute to them immediate governance over individual bodies, but only over universal agents, which are the heavenly bodies. Avicenna, indeed, followed a middle way. For with Plato he held that a certain spiritual substance immediately presides over the sphere of things active and passive, because, just as Plato held that the forms of these sensible things are derived from immaterial substances, so Avicenna also held this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0021","text":"But in this he differs from Plato: that he held that only one immaterial substance presides over all lower bodies, which he called the active intelligence. The holy doctors, however, held, as did the Platonists, that diverse spiritual substances are set over diverse bodily things. For Augustine says, in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, *every visible thing in this world has an angelic power set over it*. And Damascene says, *the Devil was among those angelic powers that presided over the terrestrial order*. And Origen says, on Num. XXII, *when the ass saw the angel, what need has the world of angels, who preside over beasts, and preside over the birth of animals, and the growth of shrubs and plantings and other things*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0022","text":"But this is not to be held because, according to his nature, one angel is more suited than another to preside over animals rather than plants, because every angel, even the least, has a higher and more universal power than any genus of bodily things. Rather, it is from the order of divine wisdom, which has set diverse rulers over diverse things. Yet it does not follow from this that there are more than nine orders of angels, because, as was said above, the orders are distinguished according to general offices. Hence, just as, according to Gregory, all angels who have proper governance over demons belong to the order of Powers, so all angels who have governance over purely bodily things seem to belong to the order of Virtues; for by their ministry miracles are sometimes also worked.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0023","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0024","text":"[I q. 110 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33032)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0025","text":"We proceed thus to the second point. It seems that corporeal matter obeys the angels at their command. For the power of an angel is greater than the power of the soul. But corporeal matter obeys the soul's conception: for the human body is altered by the soul's conception, as to heat and cold, and sometimes even unto health and sickness. Therefore, much more is corporeal matter transformed according to an angel's conception.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0026","text":"[I q. 110 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33033)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0027","text":"Moreover, whatever an inferior power can do, a superior power can do. But the power of an angel is superior to corporeal power. Now a body can, by its own power, transform corporeal matter into some form; as when fire generates fire. Therefore, much more powerfully can angels, by their own power, transform corporeal matter into some form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0028","text":"[I q. 110 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33034)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0029","text":"Moreover, all corporeal nature is administered through angels, as has been said; and thus it seems that bodies are related to angels as instruments are, for the nature of an instrument is that it is a moved mover. But among effects something is found that derives from the power of the principal agents, which cannot be from the power of the instrument; and this is what is more principal in the effect. Thus the digestion of nourishment is by the power of natural heat, which is an instrument of the nutritive soul; but that living flesh is generated is from the power of the soul. Likewise, that wood be cut belongs to the saw; but that the form of a bed be attained belongs to the notion of art. Therefore the substantial form, which is more principal among corporeal effects, is from the power of angels. Matter, therefore, obeys angels, as to its being informed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0030","text":"[I q. 110 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33035)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0031","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in Book III of *On the Trinity*, *it must not be thought that the matter of these visible things serves those transgressing angels at their command, but God alone*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0032","text":"[I q. 110 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33036)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0033","text":"I answer that it must be said that the Platonists held that the forms which are in matter are caused by immaterial forms, because they held material forms to be certain participations of immaterial forms. Avicenna followed them in this respect to some extent: he held that all forms which are in matter proceed from the conception of an intelligence, and that corporeal agents only dispose matter for forms. In this they seem to have been deceived, because they supposed form to be as it were something made per se, so that it would proceed from some formal principle. But, as the Philosopher proves in Book VII of the *Metaphysics*, that which is properly made is the composite, for this is properly as it were subsistent. Form, however, is not called a being as though it itself is, but as that by which something is; and consequently neither is form properly made. For to that belongs becoming, to which belongs being, since becoming is nothing other than a way into being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0034","text":"Now it is manifest that what is made is like the maker, because every agent acts so as to produce something like itself. And therefore that which makes natural things has a likeness to the composite: either because it is composite, as fire generates fire; or because the whole composite, both as regards matter and as regards form, is in its power—which is proper to God. Thus every informing of matter is either immediately from God or from some corporeal agent, but not immediately from an angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0035","text":"[I q. 110 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33037)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0036","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that our soul is united to the body as form; and thus it is not surprising if the body is formally transformed by its conception, especially since the movement of the sensitive appetite, which occurs with a certain corporeal transformation, is subject to the command of reason. But an angel is not related in this way to natural bodies. Hence the argument does not follow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0037","text":"[I q. 110 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33038)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0038","text":"To the second it must be said that what an inferior power can do, a superior power can do not in the same way, but in a more excellent way; just as the intellect knows sensibles in a more excellent way than sense does. Thus an angel transforms corporeal matter in a more excellent way than corporeal agents do, namely, by moving the corporeal agents themselves, as a superior cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0039","text":"[I q. 110 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33039)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0040","text":"To the third it must be said that nothing prevents certain effects from following in natural things through the power of angels, effects for which corporeal agents would not suffice. But this is not for matter to obey angels at their command; just as matter does not obey cooks at their command either, because they bring about some mode of cooking through fire according to a certain regulation of art, which fire of itself would not accomplish. For to reduce matter into the actuality of substantial form does not exceed the power of a corporeal agent, because like is naturally apt to make like.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0041","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0042","text":"[I q. 110 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33040)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0043","text":"We proceed thus to the third point. It seems that bodies do not obey angels with respect to local motion. For the local motion of natural bodies follows upon their forms. But angels do not cause the forms of natural bodies, as has been said. Therefore neither can they cause local motion in them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0044","text":"[I q. 110 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33041)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0045","text":"Moreover, it is proved in Book VIII of the *Physics* that local motion is the first of motions. But angels cannot cause the other motions by formally transforming matter. Therefore neither can they cause local motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0046","text":"[I q. 110 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33042)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0047","text":"Moreover, bodily members obey the soul's conception with respect to local motion, insofar as they have in themselves some principle of life. But in natural bodies there is no principle of life. Therefore they do not obey angels with respect to local motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0048","text":"[I q. 110 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33043)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0049","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in Book III *On the Trinity*, that Angels employ bodily seeds in producing certain effects. But they cannot do this except by moving [them] locally. Therefore bodies obey them with respect to local motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0050","text":"[I q. 110 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33044)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0051","text":"I answer that it must be said that, as Dionysius says in chapter VII of *On the Divine Names*, *divine wisdom joins the ends of first things to the beginnings of second things*, from which it is clear that a lower nature is touched in its highest part by a higher nature. Now bodily nature is beneath spiritual nature. But among all bodily motions, local motion is the more perfect, as is proved in Book VIII of the *Physics*; the reason for this is that what is movable according to place is not, as such, in potency to anything intrinsic, but only to something extrinsic, namely, to place. And therefore bodily nature is naturally apt to be moved immediately in place by spiritual nature. Hence the philosophers also held that the highest bodies are moved locally by spiritual substances. Hence we see that the soul moves the body first and principally by local motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0052","text":"[I q. 110 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33045)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0053","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that in bodies there are local motions other than those which follow upon forms, just as the ebb and flow of the sea does not follow upon the substantial form of water, but upon the power of the moon. And much more can certain local motions follow upon the power of spiritual substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0054","text":"[I q. 110 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33046)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0055","text":"To the second, it must be said that Angels, by causing local motion as something prior, can through it cause other motions, namely, by applying bodily agents for producing effects of this sort; just as a smith applies fire to soften iron.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0056","text":"[I q. 110 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33047)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0057","text":"To the third, it must be said that Angels have a power less contracted than souls. Hence the motive power of the soul is contracted to the united body, which is enlivened by it, and by means of which it can move other things. But the power of an Angel is not contracted to any body. Hence it can move locally bodies not joined [to it].","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0058","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0059","text":"[I q. 110 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33048)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0060","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that Angels can perform miracles. For Gregory says, *those spirits are called virtues through whom signs and miracles are more frequently performed*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0061","text":"[I q. 110 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33049)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0062","text":"Further, Augustine says, in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, *that magicians perform miracles through private compacts, good Christians through public justice, evil Christians through the signs of public justice*. But magicians perform miracles because they are heard by demons, as he himself says elsewhere in the same book. Therefore demons can perform miracles. Therefore, much more, good Angels can [do so].","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0063","text":"[I q. 110 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33050)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0064","text":"Further, Augustine says in the same book that *all things which are done visibly are not unreasonably believed to be able to be done even through the lower powers of this air*. But when some effect of natural causes is produced apart from the order of the natural cause, we call this a miracle; for example, when someone is healed of a fever, not through the operation of nature. Therefore Angels and demons can perform miracles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0065","text":"[I q. 110 a. 4 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33051)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0066","text":"Further, a higher power is not subject to the order of a lower cause. But bodily nature is lower than an Angel. Therefore an Angel can act outside the order of bodily agents. But this is to perform miracles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0067","text":"[I q. 110 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33052)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0068","text":"But on the contrary, it is said of God in the Psalm, *who alone does great wonders*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0069","text":"[I q. 110 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33053)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0070","text":"I answer that it must be said that a miracle is properly so called when something is done outside the order of nature. But for the notion of a miracle it is not sufficient that something be done outside the order of the nature of some particular thing; for thus, when someone throws a stone upward, he would perform a miracle, since this is outside the order of the nature of a stone. Therefore something is called a miracle from the fact that it is done outside the order of the whole of created nature. But this none can do except God, because whatever an Angel, or any other creature, does by its own power, this is done according to the order of created nature; and thus it is not a miracle. Hence it remains that God alone can perform miracles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0071","text":"[I q. 110 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33054)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0072","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that some Angels are said to perform miracles either because God performs miracles at their desire, just as holy men too are said to perform miracles; or because they render some service in the miracles that are performed, as by gathering dust in the general resurrection, or by doing something of this sort.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0073","text":"[I q. 110 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33055)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0074","text":"To the second, it must be said that miracles, speaking simply, are so called, as has been said, when certain things are done outside the order of the whole of created nature. But because not every power of created nature is known to us, when something is done outside the order of created nature known to us, by a created power unknown to us, it is a miracle with respect to us. Thus, therefore, when demons do something by their natural power, they are called miracles not simply, but with respect to us. And in this way magicians work miracles through demons. And they are said to be done through private contracts, because every power of a creature in the universe is related as the power of some private person in a city; hence, when a magician does something through a pact entered into with a demon, this is done as though through a certain private contract. But divine justice is in the whole universe as public law in a city.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0075","text":"And therefore good Christians, insofar as they work miracles through divine justice, are said to work miracles through public justice. But bad Christians [work them] through the signs of public justice, such as by invoking the name of Christ, or by administering certain sacraments.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0076","text":"[I q. 110 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33056)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0077","text":"To the third, it must be said that spiritual powers can bring about those things which visibly occur in this world, by applying bodily seeds through local motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0078","text":"[I q. 110 a. 4 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33057)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__102:P0079","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that, although angels can do something outside the order of corporeal nature, they nevertheless cannot do anything outside the order of the whole of creation, which is required for the notion of a miracle, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__102:P0079"]}],"source_digest":"eeae6f8122b02310c82ae6ffbf776666a865954d7b537a2fdf1da3eee1828456","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 110"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 110","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103","title":"First Part · Question 111","order":112,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC__103","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 111 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33058)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de actione Angelorum in homines. Et primo, quantum possint eos immutare sua virtute naturali; secundo, quomodo mittantur a Deo ad ministerium hominum; tertio, quomodo custodiant homines. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum Angelus possit illuminare intellectum hominis. Secundo, utrum possit immutare affectum eius. Tertio, utrum possit immutare imaginationem eius. Quarto, utrum possit immutare sensum eius.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 111 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33059)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non possit illuminare hominem. Homo enim illuminatur per fidem, unde Dionysius, in Eccles. Hier., illuminationem attribuit Baptismo, qui est fidei sacramentum. Sed fides immediate est a Deo; secundum illud ad Ephes. II, *gratia estis salvati per fidem, et non ex vobis; Dei enim donum est*. Ergo homo non illuminatur ab Angelo, sed immediate a Deo.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33060)\n\nPraeterea, super illud Rom. I, *Deus illis manifestavit*, dicit Glossa quod *non solum ratio naturalis ad hoc profuit ut divina hominibus manifestarentur, sed etiam Deus illis revelavit per opus suum*, scilicet per creaturam. Sed utrumque est a Deo immediate, scilicet ratio naturalis, et creatura. Ergo Deus immediate illuminat hominem.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33061)\n\nPraeterea, quicumque illuminatur, cognoscit suam illuminationem. Sed homines non percipiunt se ab Angelis illuminari. Ergo non illuminantur ab eis.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33062)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius probat, in IV cap. Cael. Hier., quod revelationes divinorum perveniunt ad homines mediantibus Angelis. Huiusmodi autem revelationes sunt illuminationes, ut supra dictum est. Ergo homines illuminantur per Angelos.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33063)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum divinae providentiae ordo habeat ut actionibus superiorum inferiora subdantur, ut supra dictum est; sicut inferiores Angeli illuminantur per superiores, ita homines qui sunt Angelis inferiores, per eos illuminantur. Sed modus utriusque illuminationis quodammodo est similis, et quodammodo diversus. Dictum est enim supra quod illuminatio, quae est manifestatio divinae veritatis, secundum duo attenditur, scilicet secundum quod intellectus inferior confortatur per actionem intellectus superioris; et secundum quod proponuntur intellectui inferiori species intelligibiles quae sunt in superiori, ut capi possint ab inferiori. Et hoc quidem in Angelis fit, secundum quod superior Angelus veritatem universalem conceptam dividit secundum capacitatem inferioris Angeli, ut supra dictum est. Sed intellectus humanus non potest ipsam intelligibilem veritatem nudam capere, quia connaturale est ei ut intelligat per conversionem ad phantasmata, ut supra dictum est. Et ideo intelligibilem veritatem proponunt Angeli hominibus sub similitudinibus sensibilium; secundum illud quod dicit Dionysius, I cap. Cael. Hier., quod *impossibile est aliter nobis lucere divinum radium, nisi varietate sacrorum velaminum circumvelatum*. Ex alia vero parte, intellectus humanus, tanquam inferior, fortificatur per actionem intellectus angelici. Et secundum haec duo attenditur illuminatio qua homo illuminatur ab Angelo.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33064)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ad fidem duo concurrunt. Primo quidem habitus intellectus, quo disponitur ad obediendum voluntati tendenti in divinam veritatem, intellectus enim assentit veritati fidei, non quasi convictus ratione, sed quasi imperatus a voluntate; nullus enim credit nisi volens, ut Augustinus dicit. Et quantum ad hoc, fides est a solo Deo. Secundo requiritur ad fidem, quod credibilia proponantur credenti. Et hoc quidem fit per hominem, secundum quod fides est ex auditu, ut dicitur Rom. X; sed per Angelos principaliter, per quos hominibus revelantur divina. Unde Angeli operantur aliquid ad illuminationem fidei. Et tamen homines illuminantur ab Angelis non solum de credendis, sed etiam de agendis.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33065)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ratio naturalis, quae est immediate a Deo, potest per Angelum confortari, ut dictum est. Et similiter ex speciebus a creaturis acceptis, tanto altior elicitur intelligibilis veritas, quanto intellectus humanus fuerit fortior. Et sic per Angelum adiuvatur homo, ut ex creaturis perfectius in divinam cognitionem deveniat.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33066)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod operatio intellectualis, et illuminatio, dupliciter possunt considerari. Uno modo, ex parte rei intellectae, et sic quicumque intelligit vel illuminatur, cognoscit se intelligere vel illuminari; quia cognoscit rem sibi esse manifestam. Alio modo, ex parte principii, et sic non quicumque intelligit aliquam veritatem, cognoscit quid sit intellectus, qui est principium intellectualis operationis. Et similiter non quicumque illuminatur ab Angelo, cognoscit se ab Angelo illuminari.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 111 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33067)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli possint immutare voluntatem hominis. Quia super illud Heb. I, *qui facit Angelos suos spiritus, et ministros suos flammam ignis*, dicit Glossa quod *ignis sunt, dum spiritu fervent, et vitia nostra urunt*. Sed hoc non esset, nisi voluntatem immutarent. Ergo Angeli possunt immutare voluntatem.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33068)\n\nPraeterea, Beda dicit quod *Diabolus non est immissor malarum cogitationum, sed incensor*. Damascenus autem ulterius dicit quod etiam est immissor, dicit enim in II libro, quod *omnis malitia et immundae passiones ex Daemonibus excogitatae sunt, et immittere homini sunt concessi*. Et pari ratione, Angeli boni immittunt et incendunt bonas cogitationes. Sed hoc non possent facere, nisi immutarent voluntatem. Ergo immutant voluntatem.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33069)\n\nPraeterea, Angelus, sicut est dictum, illuminat intellectum hominis mediantibus phantasmatibus. Sed sicut phantasia, quae deservit intellectui, potest immutari ab Angelo; ita et appetitus sensitivus qui deservit voluntati, quia et ipse etiam est vis utens organo corporali. Ergo sicut illuminat intellectum, ita potest immutare voluntatem.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33070)\n\nSed contra est quod immutare voluntatem est proprium Dei; secundum illud Prov. XXI, *cor regis in manu domini; quocumque voluerit, vertet illud*.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33071)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod voluntas potest immutari dupliciter. Uno modo, ab interiori. Et sic cum motus voluntatis non sit aliud quam inclinatio voluntatis in rem volitam, solius Dei est sic immutare voluntatem, qui dat naturae intellectuali virtutem talis inclinationis. Sicut enim inclinatio naturalis non est nisi a Deo qui dat naturam; ita inclinatio voluntaria non est nisi a Deo, qui causat voluntatem. Alio modo movetur voluntas ab exteriori. Et hoc in Angelo est quidem uno modo tantum, scilicet a bono apprehenso per intellectum. Unde secundum quod aliquis est causa quod aliquid apprehendatur ut bonum ad appetendum, secundum hoc movet voluntatem. Et sic etiam solus Deus efficaciter potest movere voluntatem; Angelus autem et homo per modum suadentis, ut supra dictum est. Sed praeter hunc modum, etiam aliter movetur in hominibus voluntas ab exteriori, scilicet ex passione existente circa appetitum sensitivum; sicut ex concupiscentia vel ira inclinatur voluntas ad aliquid volendum. Et sic etiam Angeli, inquantum possunt concitare huiusmodi passiones, possunt voluntatem movere. Non tamen ex necessitate, quia voluntas semper remanet libera ad consentiendum vel resistendum passioni.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33072)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ministri Dei, vel homines vel Angeli, dicuntur urere vitia, et inflammare ad virtutes, per modum persuasionis.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33073)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Daemones non possunt immittere cogitationes, interius eas causando, cum usus cogitativae virtutis subiaceat voluntati. Dicitur tamen Diabolus incensor cogitationum, inquantum incitat ad cogitandum, vel ad appetendum cogitata, per modum persuadentis, vel passionem concitantis. Et hoc ipsum incendere Damascenus vocat immittere, quia talis operatio interius fit. Sed bonae cogitationes attribuuntur altiori principio, scilicet Deo; licet Angelorum ministerio procurentur.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33074)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod intellectus humanus, secundum praesentem statum, non potest intelligere nisi convertendo se ad phantasmata; sed voluntas humana potest aliquid velle ex iudicio rationis, non sequendo passionem appetitus sensitivi. Unde non est simile.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 111 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33075)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non possit immutare imaginationem hominis. Phantasia enim, ut dicitur in libro de anima, est motus factus a sensu secundum actum. Sed si fieret per immutationem Angeli, non fieret a sensu secundum actum. Ergo est contra rationem phantasiae, quae est actus imaginativae virtutis, ut sit per immutationem Angeli.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33076)\n\nPraeterea, formae quae sunt in imaginatione, cum sint spirituales, sunt nobiliores formis quae sunt in materia sensibili. Sed Angelus non potest imprimere formas in materia sensibili, ut dictum est. Ergo non potest imprimere formas in imaginatione. Et ita non potest eam immutare.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33077)\n\nPraeterea, secundum Augustinum, XII super Gen. ad Litt., *commixtione alterius spiritus fieri potest ut ea quae ipse scit, per huiusmodi imagines ei cui miscetur ostendat, sive intelligenti, sive ut ab alio intellecta pandantur*. Sed non videtur quod Angelus possit misceri imaginationi humanae; neque quod imaginatio possit capere intelligibilia, quae Angelus cognoscit. Ergo videtur quod Angelus non possit mutare imaginationem.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33078)\n\nPraeterea, in visione imaginaria homo adhaeret similitudinibus rerum quasi ipsis rebus. Sed in hoc est quaedam deceptio. Cum ergo Angelus bonus non possit esse causa deceptionis, videtur quod non possit causare imaginariam visionem, imaginationem immutando.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33079)\n\nSed contra est quod ea quae apparent in somnis, videntur imaginaria visione. Sed Angeli revelant aliqua in somnis; ut patet Matth. I et II, de Angelo qui Ioseph in somnis apparuit. Ergo Angelus potest imaginationem movere.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33080)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Angelus, tam bonus quam malus, virtute naturae suae potest movere imaginationem hominis. Quod quidem sic considerari potest. Dictum est enim supra quod natura corporalis obedit Angelo ad motum localem. Illa ergo quae ex motu locali aliquorum corporum possunt causari, subsunt virtuti naturali Angelorum. Manifestum est autem quod apparitiones imaginariae causantur interdum in nobis ex locali mutatione corporalium spirituum et humorum. Unde Aristoteles, in Lib. de Somn. et Vig., assignans causam apparitionis somniorum, dicit quod, cum animal dormit, descendente plurimo sanguine ad principium sensitivum, simul descendunt motus, idest impressiones relictae ex sensibilium motionibus, quae in spiritibus sensualibus conservantur, et movent principium sensitivum, ita quod fit quaedam apparitio, ac si tunc principium sensitivum a rebus ipsis exterioribus mutaretur. Et tanta potest esse commotio spirituum et humorum, quod huiusmodi apparitiones etiam vigilantibus fiant; sicut patet in phreneticis, et in aliis huiusmodi. Sicut igitur hoc fit per naturalem commotionem humorum; et quandoque etiam per voluntatem hominis, qui voluntarie imaginatur quod prius senserat, ita etiam hoc potest fieri virtute Angeli boni vel mali, quandoque quidem cum alienatione a corporeis sensibus, quandoque autem absque tali alienatione.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33081)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod primum principium phantasiae est a sensu secundum actum, non enim possumus imaginari quae nullo modo sensimus, vel secundum totum vel secundum partem; sicut caecus natus non potest imaginari colorem. Sed aliquando imaginatio informatur, ut actus phantastici motus consurgat, ab impressionibus interius conservatis, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33082)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Angelus transmutat imaginationem, non quidem imprimendo aliquam formam imaginariam nullo modo per sensum prius acceptam (non enim posset facere quod caecus imaginaretur colores), sed hoc facit per motum localem spirituum et humorum, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33083)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod commixtio illa spiritus angelici ad imaginationem humanam, non est per essentiam, sed per effectum quem praedicto modo in imaginatione facit; cui demonstrat quae ipse novit, non tamen eo modo quo ipse novit.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33084)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod Angelus causans aliquam imaginariam visionem, quandoque quidem simul intellectum illuminat, ut cognoscat quid per huiusmodi similitudines significetur, et tunc nulla est deceptio. Quandoque vero per operationem Angeli solummodo similitudines rerum apparent in imaginatione, nec tamen tunc causatur deceptio ab Angelo, sed ex defectu intellectus eius cui talia apparent. Sicut nec Christus fuit causa deceptionis in hoc quod multa turbis in parabolis proposuit, quae non exposuit eis.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 111 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33085)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non possit immutare sensum humanum. Operatio enim sensitiva est operatio vitae. Huiusmodi autem operatio non est a principio extrinseco. Non ergo operatio sensitiva potest causari ab Angelo.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33086)\n\nPraeterea, virtus sensitiva est nobilior quam nutritiva. Sed Angelus, ut videtur, non potest mutare virtutem nutritivam; sicut nec alias formas naturales. Ergo neque virtutem sensitivam immutare potest.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33087)\n\nPraeterea, sensus naturaliter movetur a sensibili. Sed Angelus non potest immutare naturae ordinem, ut supra dictum est. Ergo Angelus non potest immutare sensum, sed semper sensus a sensibili immutatur.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33088)\n\nSed contra est quod Angeli qui subverterunt Sodomam, *percusserunt Sodomitas caecitate (vel aorasia), ut ostium domus invenire non possent*, ut dicitur Gen. XIX. Et simile legitur IV Reg. VI, de Syris quos Elisaeus duxit in Samariam.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33089)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod sensus immutatur dupliciter. Uno modo, ab exteriori; sicut cum mutatur a sensibili. Alio modo, ab interiori, videmus enim quod, perturbatis spiritibus et humoribus immutatur sensus; lingua enim infirmi, quia plena est cholerico humore, omnia sentit ut amara; et simile contingit in aliis sensibus. Utroque autem modo Angelus potest immutare sensum hominis sua naturali virtute. Potest enim Angelus opponere exterius sensui sensibile aliquod, vel a natura formatum, vel aliquod de novo formando; sicut facit dum corpus assumit, ut supra dictum est. Similiter etiam potest interius commovere spiritus et humores, ut supra dictum est, ex quibus sensus diversimode immutentur.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33090)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod principium sensitivae operationis non potest esse absque principio interiori, quod est potentia sensitiva, sed illud interius principium potest multipliciter ab exteriori principio commoveri, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33091)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod etiam per commotionem interiorem spirituum et humorum, potest Angelus aliquid operari ad immutandum actum potentiae nutritivae. Et similiter potentiae appetitivae, et sensitivae, et cuiuscumque potentiae corporali organo utentis.\n\n[I q. 111 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33092)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod praeter ordinem totius creaturae Angelus facere non potest, sed praeter ordinem alicuius particularis naturae facere potest, cum tali ordini non subdatur. Et sic quodam singulari modo potest sensum immutare, praeter modum communem.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0002","text":"[I q. 111 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33058)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de actione Angelorum in homines. Et primo, quantum possint eos immutare sua virtute naturali; secundo, quomodo mittantur a Deo ad ministerium hominum; tertio, quomodo custodiant homines. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum Angelus possit illuminare intellectum hominis. Secundo, utrum possit immutare affectum eius. Tertio, utrum possit immutare imaginationem eius. Quarto, utrum possit immutare sensum eius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0005","text":"[I q. 111 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33059)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non possit illuminare hominem. Homo enim illuminatur per fidem, unde Dionysius, in Eccles. Hier., illuminationem attribuit Baptismo, qui est fidei sacramentum. Sed fides immediate est a Deo; secundum illud ad Ephes. II, *gratia estis salvati per fidem, et non ex vobis; Dei enim donum est*. Ergo homo non illuminatur ab Angelo, sed immediate a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0007","text":"[I q. 111 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33060)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0008","text":"Praeterea, super illud Rom. I, *Deus illis manifestavit*, dicit Glossa quod *non solum ratio naturalis ad hoc profuit ut divina hominibus manifestarentur, sed etiam Deus illis revelavit per opus suum*, scilicet per creaturam. Sed utrumque est a Deo immediate, scilicet ratio naturalis, et creatura. Ergo Deus immediate illuminat hominem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0009","text":"[I q. 111 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33061)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0010","text":"Praeterea, quicumque illuminatur, cognoscit suam illuminationem. Sed homines non percipiunt se ab Angelis illuminari. Ergo non illuminantur ab eis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0011","text":"[I q. 111 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33062)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius probat, in IV cap. Cael. Hier., quod revelationes divinorum perveniunt ad homines mediantibus Angelis. Huiusmodi autem revelationes sunt illuminationes, ut supra dictum est. Ergo homines illuminantur per Angelos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0013","text":"[I q. 111 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33063)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum divinae providentiae ordo habeat ut actionibus superiorum inferiora subdantur, ut supra dictum est; sicut inferiores Angeli illuminantur per superiores, ita homines qui sunt Angelis inferiores, per eos illuminantur. Sed modus utriusque illuminationis quodammodo est similis, et quodammodo diversus. Dictum est enim supra quod illuminatio, quae est manifestatio divinae veritatis, secundum duo attenditur, scilicet secundum quod intellectus inferior confortatur per actionem intellectus superioris; et secundum quod proponuntur intellectui inferiori species intelligibiles quae sunt in superiori, ut capi possint ab inferiori. Et hoc quidem in Angelis fit, secundum quod superior Angelus veritatem universalem conceptam dividit secundum capacitatem inferioris Angeli, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0015","text":"Sed intellectus humanus non potest ipsam intelligibilem veritatem nudam capere, quia connaturale est ei ut intelligat per conversionem ad phantasmata, ut supra dictum est. Et ideo intelligibilem veritatem proponunt Angeli hominibus sub similitudinibus sensibilium; secundum illud quod dicit Dionysius, I cap. Cael. Hier., quod *impossibile est aliter nobis lucere divinum radium, nisi varietate sacrorum velaminum circumvelatum*. Ex alia vero parte, intellectus humanus, tanquam inferior, fortificatur per actionem intellectus angelici. Et secundum haec duo attenditur illuminatio qua homo illuminatur ab Angelo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0016","text":"[I q. 111 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33064)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0017","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ad fidem duo concurrunt. Primo quidem habitus intellectus, quo disponitur ad obediendum voluntati tendenti in divinam veritatem, intellectus enim assentit veritati fidei, non quasi convictus ratione, sed quasi imperatus a voluntate; nullus enim credit nisi volens, ut Augustinus dicit. Et quantum ad hoc, fides est a solo Deo. Secundo requiritur ad fidem, quod credibilia proponantur credenti. Et hoc quidem fit per hominem, secundum quod fides est ex auditu, ut dicitur Rom. X; sed per Angelos principaliter, per quos hominibus revelantur divina. Unde Angeli operantur aliquid ad illuminationem fidei. Et tamen homines illuminantur ab Angelis non solum de credendis, sed etiam de agendis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0018","text":"[I q. 111 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33065)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0019","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ratio naturalis, quae est immediate a Deo, potest per Angelum confortari, ut dictum est. Et similiter ex speciebus a creaturis acceptis, tanto altior elicitur intelligibilis veritas, quanto intellectus humanus fuerit fortior. Et sic per Angelum adiuvatur homo, ut ex creaturis perfectius in divinam cognitionem deveniat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0020","text":"[I q. 111 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33066)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0021","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod operatio intellectualis, et illuminatio, dupliciter possunt considerari. Uno modo, ex parte rei intellectae, et sic quicumque intelligit vel illuminatur, cognoscit se intelligere vel illuminari; quia cognoscit rem sibi esse manifestam. Alio modo, ex parte principii, et sic non quicumque intelligit aliquam veritatem, cognoscit quid sit intellectus, qui est principium intellectualis operationis. Et similiter non quicumque illuminatur ab Angelo, cognoscit se ab Angelo illuminari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0022","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0023","text":"[I q. 111 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33067)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0024","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli possint immutare voluntatem hominis. Quia super illud Heb. I, *qui facit Angelos suos spiritus, et ministros suos flammam ignis*, dicit Glossa quod *ignis sunt, dum spiritu fervent, et vitia nostra urunt*. Sed hoc non esset, nisi voluntatem immutarent. Ergo Angeli possunt immutare voluntatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0025","text":"[I q. 111 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33068)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0026","text":"Praeterea, Beda dicit quod *Diabolus non est immissor malarum cogitationum, sed incensor*. Damascenus autem ulterius dicit quod etiam est immissor, dicit enim in II libro, quod *omnis malitia et immundae passiones ex Daemonibus excogitatae sunt, et immittere homini sunt concessi*. Et pari ratione, Angeli boni immittunt et incendunt bonas cogitationes. Sed hoc non possent facere, nisi immutarent voluntatem. Ergo immutant voluntatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0027","text":"[I q. 111 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33069)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0028","text":"Praeterea, Angelus, sicut est dictum, illuminat intellectum hominis mediantibus phantasmatibus. Sed sicut phantasia, quae deservit intellectui, potest immutari ab Angelo; ita et appetitus sensitivus qui deservit voluntati, quia et ipse etiam est vis utens organo corporali. Ergo sicut illuminat intellectum, ita potest immutare voluntatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0029","text":"[I q. 111 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33070)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0030","text":"Sed contra est quod immutare voluntatem est proprium Dei; secundum illud Prov. XXI, *cor regis in manu domini; quocumque voluerit, vertet illud*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0031","text":"[I q. 111 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33071)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0032","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod voluntas potest immutari dupliciter. Uno modo, ab interiori. Et sic cum motus voluntatis non sit aliud quam inclinatio voluntatis in rem volitam, solius Dei est sic immutare voluntatem, qui dat naturae intellectuali virtutem talis inclinationis. Sicut enim inclinatio naturalis non est nisi a Deo qui dat naturam; ita inclinatio voluntaria non est nisi a Deo, qui causat voluntatem. Alio modo movetur voluntas ab exteriori. Et hoc in Angelo est quidem uno modo tantum, scilicet a bono apprehenso per intellectum. Unde secundum quod aliquis est causa quod aliquid apprehendatur ut bonum ad appetendum, secundum hoc movet voluntatem. Et sic etiam solus Deus efficaciter potest movere voluntatem; Angelus autem et homo per modum suadentis, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0033","text":"Sed praeter hunc modum, etiam aliter movetur in hominibus voluntas ab exteriori, scilicet ex passione existente circa appetitum sensitivum; sicut ex concupiscentia vel ira inclinatur voluntas ad aliquid volendum. Et sic etiam Angeli, inquantum possunt concitare huiusmodi passiones, possunt voluntatem movere. Non tamen ex necessitate, quia voluntas semper remanet libera ad consentiendum vel resistendum passioni.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0034","text":"[I q. 111 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33072)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0035","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ministri Dei, vel homines vel Angeli, dicuntur urere vitia, et inflammare ad virtutes, per modum persuasionis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0036","text":"[I q. 111 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33073)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0037","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Daemones non possunt immittere cogitationes, interius eas causando, cum usus cogitativae virtutis subiaceat voluntati. Dicitur tamen Diabolus incensor cogitationum, inquantum incitat ad cogitandum, vel ad appetendum cogitata, per modum persuadentis, vel passionem concitantis. Et hoc ipsum incendere Damascenus vocat immittere, quia talis operatio interius fit. Sed bonae cogitationes attribuuntur altiori principio, scilicet Deo; licet Angelorum ministerio procurentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0038","text":"[I q. 111 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33074)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0039","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod intellectus humanus, secundum praesentem statum, non potest intelligere nisi convertendo se ad phantasmata; sed voluntas humana potest aliquid velle ex iudicio rationis, non sequendo passionem appetitus sensitivi. Unde non est simile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0040","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0041","text":"[I q. 111 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33075)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0042","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non possit immutare imaginationem hominis. Phantasia enim, ut dicitur in libro de anima, est motus factus a sensu secundum actum. Sed si fieret per immutationem Angeli, non fieret a sensu secundum actum. Ergo est contra rationem phantasiae, quae est actus imaginativae virtutis, ut sit per immutationem Angeli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0043","text":"[I q. 111 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33076)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0044","text":"Praeterea, formae quae sunt in imaginatione, cum sint spirituales, sunt nobiliores formis quae sunt in materia sensibili. Sed Angelus non potest imprimere formas in materia sensibili, ut dictum est. Ergo non potest imprimere formas in imaginatione. Et ita non potest eam immutare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0045","text":"[I q. 111 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33077)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0046","text":"Praeterea, secundum Augustinum, XII super Gen. ad Litt., *commixtione alterius spiritus fieri potest ut ea quae ipse scit, per huiusmodi imagines ei cui miscetur ostendat, sive intelligenti, sive ut ab alio intellecta pandantur*. Sed non videtur quod Angelus possit misceri imaginationi humanae; neque quod imaginatio possit capere intelligibilia, quae Angelus cognoscit. Ergo videtur quod Angelus non possit mutare imaginationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0047","text":"[I q. 111 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33078)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0048","text":"Praeterea, in visione imaginaria homo adhaeret similitudinibus rerum quasi ipsis rebus. Sed in hoc est quaedam deceptio. Cum ergo Angelus bonus non possit esse causa deceptionis, videtur quod non possit causare imaginariam visionem, imaginationem immutando.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0049","text":"[I q. 111 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33079)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0050","text":"Sed contra est quod ea quae apparent in somnis, videntur imaginaria visione. Sed Angeli revelant aliqua in somnis; ut patet Matth. I et II, de Angelo qui Ioseph in somnis apparuit. Ergo Angelus potest imaginationem movere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0051","text":"[I q. 111 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33080)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0052","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Angelus, tam bonus quam malus, virtute naturae suae potest movere imaginationem hominis. Quod quidem sic considerari potest. Dictum est enim supra quod natura corporalis obedit Angelo ad motum localem. Illa ergo quae ex motu locali aliquorum corporum possunt causari, subsunt virtuti naturali Angelorum. Manifestum est autem quod apparitiones imaginariae causantur interdum in nobis ex locali mutatione corporalium spirituum et humorum. Unde Aristoteles, in Lib. de Somn. et Vig., assignans causam apparitionis somniorum, dicit quod, cum animal dormit, descendente plurimo sanguine ad principium sensitivum, simul descendunt motus, idest impressiones relictae ex sensibilium motionibus, quae in spiritibus sensualibus conservantur, et movent principium sensitivum, ita quod fit quaedam apparitio, ac si tunc principium sensitivum a rebus ipsis exterioribus mutaretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0053","text":"Et tanta potest esse commotio spirituum et humorum, quod huiusmodi apparitiones etiam vigilantibus fiant; sicut patet in phreneticis, et in aliis huiusmodi. Sicut igitur hoc fit per naturalem commotionem humorum; et quandoque etiam per voluntatem hominis, qui voluntarie imaginatur quod prius senserat, ita etiam hoc potest fieri virtute Angeli boni vel mali, quandoque quidem cum alienatione a corporeis sensibus, quandoque autem absque tali alienatione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0054","text":"[I q. 111 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33081)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0055","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod primum principium phantasiae est a sensu secundum actum, non enim possumus imaginari quae nullo modo sensimus, vel secundum totum vel secundum partem; sicut caecus natus non potest imaginari colorem. Sed aliquando imaginatio informatur, ut actus phantastici motus consurgat, ab impressionibus interius conservatis, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0056","text":"[I q. 111 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33082)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0057","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Angelus transmutat imaginationem, non quidem imprimendo aliquam formam imaginariam nullo modo per sensum prius acceptam (non enim posset facere quod caecus imaginaretur colores), sed hoc facit per motum localem spirituum et humorum, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0058","text":"[I q. 111 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33083)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0059","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod commixtio illa spiritus angelici ad imaginationem humanam, non est per essentiam, sed per effectum quem praedicto modo in imaginatione facit; cui demonstrat quae ipse novit, non tamen eo modo quo ipse novit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0060","text":"[I q. 111 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33084)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0061","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod Angelus causans aliquam imaginariam visionem, quandoque quidem simul intellectum illuminat, ut cognoscat quid per huiusmodi similitudines significetur, et tunc nulla est deceptio. Quandoque vero per operationem Angeli solummodo similitudines rerum apparent in imaginatione, nec tamen tunc causatur deceptio ab Angelo, sed ex defectu intellectus eius cui talia apparent. Sicut nec Christus fuit causa deceptionis in hoc quod multa turbis in parabolis proposuit, quae non exposuit eis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0062","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0063","text":"[I q. 111 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33085)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0064","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non possit immutare sensum humanum. Operatio enim sensitiva est operatio vitae. Huiusmodi autem operatio non est a principio extrinseco. Non ergo operatio sensitiva potest causari ab Angelo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0065","text":"[I q. 111 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33086)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0066","text":"Praeterea, virtus sensitiva est nobilior quam nutritiva. Sed Angelus, ut videtur, non potest mutare virtutem nutritivam; sicut nec alias formas naturales. Ergo neque virtutem sensitivam immutare potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0067","text":"[I q. 111 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33087)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0068","text":"Praeterea, sensus naturaliter movetur a sensibili. Sed Angelus non potest immutare naturae ordinem, ut supra dictum est. Ergo Angelus non potest immutare sensum, sed semper sensus a sensibili immutatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0069","text":"[I q. 111 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33088)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0070","text":"Sed contra est quod Angeli qui subverterunt Sodomam, *percusserunt Sodomitas caecitate (vel aorasia), ut ostium domus invenire non possent*, ut dicitur Gen. XIX. Et simile legitur IV Reg. VI, de Syris quos Elisaeus duxit in Samariam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0071","text":"[I q. 111 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33089)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0072","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod sensus immutatur dupliciter. Uno modo, ab exteriori; sicut cum mutatur a sensibili. Alio modo, ab interiori, videmus enim quod, perturbatis spiritibus et humoribus immutatur sensus; lingua enim infirmi, quia plena est cholerico humore, omnia sentit ut amara; et simile contingit in aliis sensibus. Utroque autem modo Angelus potest immutare sensum hominis sua naturali virtute. Potest enim Angelus opponere exterius sensui sensibile aliquod, vel a natura formatum, vel aliquod de novo formando; sicut facit dum corpus assumit, ut supra dictum est. Similiter etiam potest interius commovere spiritus et humores, ut supra dictum est, ex quibus sensus diversimode immutentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0073","text":"[I q. 111 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33090)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0074","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod principium sensitivae operationis non potest esse absque principio interiori, quod est potentia sensitiva, sed illud interius principium potest multipliciter ab exteriori principio commoveri, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0075","text":"[I q. 111 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33091)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0076","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod etiam per commotionem interiorem spirituum et humorum, potest Angelus aliquid operari ad immutandum actum potentiae nutritivae. Et similiter potentiae appetitivae, et sensitivae, et cuiuscumque potentiae corporali organo utentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0077","text":"[I q. 111 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33092)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0078","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod praeter ordinem totius creaturae Angelus facere non potest, sed praeter ordinem alicuius particularis naturae facere potest, cum tali ordini non subdatur. Et sic quodam singulari modo potest sensum immutare, praeter modum communem.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"ca53d62d136790a3f71bed868d4e30f11a261b5e4a5609d964aff1e0ae37024f","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T154436934385Z_e4ae5ce0","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 111","source_sha256":"ca53d62d136790a3f71bed868d4e30f11a261b5e4a5609d964aff1e0ae37024f","sha256":"f7e54f1e7f08a0ce5b52d4cba24e1763d404ff432bba8f1e7bc0252b46960943","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:44:36.934397+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0002","text":"[I q. 111 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33058)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the action of the Angels upon human beings. First, how far they can change them by their natural power; second, how they are sent by God for the ministry of human beings; third, how they guard human beings. Concerning the first, four questions are asked. First, whether an Angel can illuminate a human being's intellect. Second, whether he can change his affection. Third, whether he can change his imagination. Fourth, whether he can change his sense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0005","text":"[I q. 111 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33059)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that an Angel cannot illuminate a human being. For a human being is illuminated through faith; hence Dionysius, in the *Ecclesiastical Hierarchy*, attributes illumination to Baptism, which is the sacrament of faith. But faith is immediately from God; according to Ephesians II, *by grace you have been saved through faith, and this is not from yourselves; for it is the gift of God*. Therefore a human being is not illuminated by an Angel, but immediately by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0007","text":"[I q. 111 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33060)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0008","text":"Further, on Romans I, *God manifested it to them*, the Gloss says that *not only did natural reason avail for this, so that divine things might be manifested to human beings, but God also revealed them to them through His work*, namely, through the creature. But both are immediately from God, namely natural reason and the creature. Therefore God immediately illuminates the human being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0009","text":"[I q. 111 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33061)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0010","text":"Further, whoever is illuminated knows his own illumination. But human beings do not perceive that they are illuminated by Angels. Therefore they are not illuminated by them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0011","text":"[I q. 111 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33062)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0012","text":"On the contrary, Dionysius proves, in chapter IV of the *Celestial Hierarchy*, that revelations of divine things reach human beings through the mediation of Angels. But revelations of this kind are illuminations, as was said above. Therefore human beings are illuminated through Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0013","text":"[I q. 111 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33063)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0014","text":"I answer that, since the order of divine providence is such that lower things are subject to the actions of higher things, as was said above: just as the lower Angels are illuminated by the higher, so human beings, who are lower than Angels, are illuminated by them. But the mode of each illumination is in some way similar, and in some way different. For it was said above that illumination, which is the manifestation of divine truth, is considered in two respects: namely, insofar as the lower intellect is strengthened through the action of the higher intellect; and insofar as intelligible species that are in the higher are proposed to the lower intellect, so that they may be grasped by the lower. And this indeed takes place among the Angels, insofar as the higher Angel divides the universal truth conceived according to the capacity of the lower Angel, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0015","text":"But the human intellect cannot grasp intelligible truth itself naked, because it is connatural to it to understand by turning toward phantasms, as was said above. And therefore the Angels propose intelligible truth to human beings under likenesses of sensible things; according to what Dionysius says, in chapter I of the *Celestial Hierarchy*, that *it is impossible for the divine ray to shine upon us otherwise than enveloped in a variety of sacred veils*. On the other hand, the human intellect, as lower, is strengthened through the action of the angelic intellect. And according to these two respects is the illumination by which a human being is illuminated by an Angel considered.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0016","text":"[I q. 111 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33064)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0017","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that two things concur for faith. First, the habit of the intellect, by which it is disposed to obey the will tending toward divine truth; for the intellect assents to the truth of faith, not as though compelled by reason, but as commanded by the will. For no one believes unless willing, as Augustine says. And in this respect faith is from God alone. Second, it is required for faith that the things to be believed be proposed to the believer. And this indeed is done through a human being, insofar as faith is from hearing, as is said in Romans X; but principally through Angels, through whom divine things are revealed to human beings. Hence Angels effect something toward the illumination of faith. Yet human beings are illuminated by Angels not only concerning things to be believed, but also concerning things to be done.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0018","text":"[I q. 111 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33065)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0019","text":"To the second, it must be said that natural reason, which is immediately from God, can be strengthened through an Angel, as was said. And similarly, from species received from creatures, an intelligible truth is elicited the higher the stronger the human intellect has been. And thus a human being is helped through an Angel, so as to attain more perfectly, from creatures, to divine knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0020","text":"[I q. 111 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33066)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0021","text":"To the third, it must be said that intellectual operation and illumination can be considered in two ways. In one way, on the part of the thing understood; and thus whoever understands or is illuminated knows that he understands or is illuminated, because he knows the thing to be manifest to him. In another way, on the part of the principle; and thus not everyone who understands some truth knows what the intellect is, which is the principle of intellectual operation. And likewise, not everyone who is illuminated by an Angel knows that he is illuminated by an Angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0022","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0023","text":"[I q. 111 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33067)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0024","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that Angels can change the human will. For on Hebrews I, *who makes His Angels spirits, and His ministers a flame of fire*, the Gloss says that *they are fire when they burn with the spirit and burn up our vices*. But this would not be so unless they changed the will. Therefore Angels can change the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0025","text":"[I q. 111 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33068)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0026","text":"Moreover, Bede says that *the Devil is not an instiller of evil thoughts, but an inciter*. But Damascene further says that he is also an instiller; for he says in the second book that *every wickedness and unclean passion has been devised by the Demons, and they have been permitted to instil them into man*. And by equal reasoning, the good Angels instil and incite good thoughts. But they could not do this unless they changed the will. Therefore they change the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0027","text":"[I q. 111 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33069)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0028","text":"Moreover, an Angel, as has been said, illuminates the human intellect by means of phantasms. But just as the imagination, which serves the intellect, can be changed by an Angel, so also can the sensitive appetite, which serves the will, since it too is a power using a bodily organ. Therefore, just as he illuminates the intellect, so he can change the will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0029","text":"[I q. 111 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33070)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0030","text":"On the contrary, to change the will is proper to God; according to Proverbs 21: *The heart of the king is in the hand of the Lord; wherever He wills, He will turn it*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0031","text":"[I q. 111 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33071)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0032","text":"I answer that the will can be changed in two ways. In one way, from within. And thus, since the movement of the will is nothing other than the inclination of the will toward the thing willed, it belongs to God alone to change the will in this way, for He gives the intellectual nature the power of such an inclination. For just as natural inclination is from none but God, who gives nature, so voluntary inclination is from none but God, who causes the will. In another way, the will is moved from without. And in an Angel this occurs in only one way, namely, by means of a good apprehended by the intellect. Hence, insofar as someone is the cause that something is apprehended as good to be desired, to that extent he moves the will. And thus God alone can move the will efficaciously; but an Angel and a human being do so in the manner of one persuading, as was said above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0033","text":"But besides this mode, in human beings the will is also moved from without in another way, namely, by a passion existing in the sensitive appetite; just as from concupiscence or anger the will is inclined to will something. And thus Angels too, insofar as they can stir up passions of this kind, can move the will. Yet not of necessity, because the will always remains free to consent to or resist the passion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0034","text":"[I q. 111 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33072)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0035","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the ministers of God, whether human beings or Angels, are said to burn away vices and to inflame toward virtues by way of persuasion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0036","text":"[I q. 111 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33073)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0037","text":"To the second, it must be said that Demons cannot instil thoughts by causing them inwardly, since the use of the cogitative power is subject to the will. Yet the Devil is called an inciter of thoughts insofar as he incites one to think, or to desire the things thought, in the manner of one persuading or stirring up a passion. And Damascene calls this very inciting an instilling, because such an operation takes place inwardly. But good thoughts are attributed to a higher principle, namely, God, although they are procured through the ministry of Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0038","text":"[I q. 111 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33074)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0039","text":"To the third, it must be said that the human intellect, in its present state, cannot understand except by turning to phantasms; but the human will can will something on the judgment of reason, without following the passion of the sensitive appetite. Hence there is no likeness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0040","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0041","text":"[I q. 111 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33075)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0042","text":"We proceed to the third point as follows. It seems that an Angel cannot change a human being's imagination. For phantasia, as is said in the book *On the Soul*, is a movement produced by actual sensation. But if it were produced through the change effected by an Angel, it would not be produced by actual sensation. Therefore it is contrary to the nature of phantasia, which is the act of the imaginative power, for it to be through the change effected by an Angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0043","text":"[I q. 111 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33076)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0044","text":"Moreover, the forms that are in the imagination, since they are spiritual, are nobler than the forms that are in sensible matter. But an Angel cannot impress forms upon sensible matter, as has been said. Therefore he cannot impress forms upon the imagination. And thus he cannot change it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0045","text":"[I q. 111 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33077)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0046","text":"Moreover, according to Augustine, in XII *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, *by the mingling of another spirit, it can come about that, through images of this kind, he shows to the one with whom he is mingled those things which he himself knows, whether to one understanding them, or so that they may be disclosed as understood by another*. But it does not seem that an Angel can be mingled with the human imagination, nor that the imagination can receive intelligibles, which the Angel knows. Therefore it seems that an Angel cannot change the imagination.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0047","text":"[I q. 111 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33078)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0048","text":"Moreover, in an imaginative vision a human being adheres to likenesses of things as though to the things themselves. But in this there is a certain deception. Therefore, since a good Angel cannot be a cause of deception, it seems that he cannot cause an imaginative vision by changing the imagination.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0049","text":"[I q. 111 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33079)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0050","text":"But on the contrary, those things which appear in dreams are seen by an imaginative vision. But angels reveal certain things in dreams, as is clear in Matthew I and II, concerning the angel who appeared to Joseph in a dream. Therefore an angel can move the imagination.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0051","text":"[I q. 111 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33080)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0052","text":"I answer that it must be said that an angel, whether good or evil, can by the power of its nature move a human being's imagination. This can be considered as follows. For it was said above that corporeal nature obeys an angel with respect to local motion. Therefore, those things that can be caused by the local motion of certain bodies are subject to the natural power of angels. Now it is evident that imaginative appearances are sometimes caused in us by the local alteration of corporeal spirits and humors. Hence Aristotle, in the book On Sleep and Waking, assigning the cause of the appearance of dreams, says that when an animal sleeps, as a great quantity of blood descends to the sensitive principle, motions also descend together with it—that is, impressions left behind by the motions of sensibles—which are preserved in the sensitive spirits and move the sensitive principle, so that a certain appearance comes about, as though the sensitive principle were then being altered by the external things themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0053","text":"And the commotion of spirits and humors can be so great that such appearances occur even to those awake, as is clear in the frenzied and in others of this kind. Therefore, just as this occurs through a natural commotion of the humors, and sometimes also through the will of a human being, who voluntarily imagines what he had previously sensed, so too it can occur by the power of a good or evil angel: sometimes indeed with alienation from the corporeal senses, but sometimes without such alienation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0054","text":"[I q. 111 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33081)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0055","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that the first principle of phantasy is from sense in act; for we cannot imagine things that we have in no way sensed, either as a whole or in part, just as one born blind cannot imagine color. But sometimes the imagination is informed, so that a motion of phantastic act arises, by impressions preserved within, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0056","text":"[I q. 111 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33082)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0057","text":"To the second, it must be said that an angel changes the imagination not by impressing any imaginative form not previously received in any way through sense—for it could not make a blind person imagine colors—but does this through the local motion of spirits and humors, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0058","text":"[I q. 111 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33083)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0059","text":"To the third, it must be said that that mingling of the angelic spirit with the human imagination is not by essence but by the effect which it produces in the imagination in the aforesaid manner; to it the angel shows what it itself knows, though not in the manner in which it itself knows.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0060","text":"[I q. 111 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33084)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0061","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that an angel causing some imaginative vision sometimes indeed illuminates the intellect at the same time, so that it may know what is signified through such likenesses, and then there is no deception. But sometimes, through the operation of the angel alone, likenesses of things appear in the imagination; yet even then deception is not caused by the angel, but by a defect of the intellect of the one to whom such things appear. Just as Christ too was not the cause of deception in proposing many things to the crowds in parables which he did not explain to them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0062","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0063","text":"[I q. 111 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33085)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0064","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that an angel cannot alter human sense. For sensitive operation is an operation of life. But an operation of this kind is not from an extrinsic principle. Therefore sensitive operation cannot be caused by an angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0065","text":"[I q. 111 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33086)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0066","text":"Moreover, the sensitive power is nobler than the nutritive power. But an angel, it seems, cannot change the nutritive power, just as it cannot change other natural forms. Therefore neither can it alter the sensitive power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0067","text":"[I q. 111 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33087)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0068","text":"Moreover, sense is naturally moved by the sensible. But an angel cannot alter the order of nature, as was said above. Therefore an angel cannot alter sense; rather, sense is always altered by the sensible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0069","text":"[I q. 111 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33088)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0070","text":"But on the contrary, the angels who overthrew Sodom *struck the Sodomites with blindness (or aorasia), so that they could not find the door of the house*, as is said in Genesis XIX. And something similar is read in IV Kings VI, concerning the Syrians whom Elisha led into Samaria.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0071","text":"[I q. 111 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33089)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0072","text":"I answer that it must be said that sense is altered in two ways. In one way, from without, as when it is altered by the sensible. In another way, from within; for we see that, when the spirits and humors are disturbed, sense is altered. For the tongue of a sick person, because it is full of choleric humor, tastes all things as bitter; and something similar happens in the other senses. In both ways an angel can alter human sense by its natural power. For an angel can externally set before sense some sensible thing, either one formed by nature or one newly forming, as it does when it assumes a body, as was said above. Likewise, it can also internally stir the spirits and humors, as was said above, from which the senses are altered in diverse ways.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0073","text":"[I q. 111 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33090)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0074","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that the principle of sensitive operation cannot exist without an interior principle, which is the sensitive power; but that interior principle can be moved in many ways by an exterior principle, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0075","text":"[I q. 111 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33091)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0076","text":"To the second, it must be said that, even through an interior stirring of the spirits and humors, an angel can do something to alter the act of the nutritive power; and likewise [the act] of the appetitive and sensitive powers, and of any power that uses a bodily organ.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0077","text":"[I q. 111 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33092)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__103:P0078","text":"To the third, it must be said that an angel cannot act outside the order of the whole of creation, but can act outside the order of some particular nature, since he is not subject to such an order. And thus, in a certain singular manner, he can alter the senses beyond the common mode.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__103:P0078"]}],"source_digest":"3cac48c6bcaf63592d0a3597a8ad4697a8dbe6649a5715dea1fb3d355eb8b601","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 111"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 111","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104","title":"First Part · Question 112","order":113,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC__104","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 112 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33093)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de missione Angelorum. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum aliqui Angeli mittantur in ministerium. Secundo, utrum omnes mittantur. Tertio, utrum illi qui mittuntur, assistant. Quarto, de quibus ordinibus mittantur.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 112 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33094)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli in ministerium non mittantur. Omnis enim missio est ad aliquem determinatum locum. Sed actiones intellectuales non determinant aliquem locum, quia intellectus abstrahit ab hic et nunc. Cum igitur actiones angelicae sint intellectuales, videtur quod Angeli ad suas actiones agendas non mittantur.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33095)\n\nPraeterea, caelum Empyreum est locus pertinens ad dignitatem Angelorum. Si igitur ad nos mittantur in ministerium, videtur quod eorum dignitati aliquid depereat. Quod est inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33096)\n\nPraeterea, exterior occupatio impedit sapientiae contemplationem, unde dicitur Eccli. XXXVIII, *qui minoratur actu, percipiet sapientiam*. Si igitur Angeli aliqui mittuntur ad exteriora ministeria, videtur quod retardentur a contemplatione. Sed tota eorum beatitudo in contemplatione Dei consistit. Si ergo mitterentur, eorum beatitudo minueretur. Quod est inconveniens.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33097)\n\nPraeterea, ministrare est inferioris, unde dicitur Lucae XXII, *quis maior est, qui recumbit, an ille qui ministrat? Nonne qui recumbit?* Sed Angeli sunt maiores nobis ordine naturae. Ergo non mittuntur in ministerium nostrum.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33098)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Exod. XXIII, *ecce ego mittam Angelum meum, qui praecedat te*.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33099)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ex supra dictis manifestum esse potest quod aliqui Angeli in ministerium mittuntur a Deo. Ut enim supra dictum est, cum de missione divinarum personarum ageretur, ille mitti dicitur, qui aliquo modo ab alio procedit, ut incipiat esse ubi prius non erat, vel ubi prius erat, per alium modum. Filius enim aut spiritus sanctus mitti dicitur, ut a patre procedens per originem; et incipit esse novo modo, idest per gratiam vel per naturam assumptam, ubi prius erat per deitatis praesentiam. Dei enim proprium est ubique esse, quia cum sit universale agens, eius virtus attingit omnia entia; unde est in omnibus rebus, ut supra dictum est. Virtus autem Angeli, cum sit particulare agens, non attingit totum universum; sed sic attingit unum, quod non attingit aliud. Et ideo ita est hic, quod non alibi. Manifestum est autem per supra dicta, quod creatura corporalis per Angelos administratur. Cum igitur aliquid est fiendum per aliquem Angelum circa aliquam creaturam corpoream, de novo applicatur Angelus illi corpori sua virtute; et sic Angelus de novo incipit ibi esse. Et hoc totum procedit ex imperio divino. Unde sequitur, secundum praemissa, quod Angelus a Deo mittatur. Sed actio quam Angelus missus exercet, procedit a Deo sicut a primo principio, cuius nutu et auctoritate Angeli operantur; et in Deum reducitur sicut in ultimum finem. Et hoc facit rationem ministri, nam minister est sicut instrumentum intelligens; instrumentum autem ab alio movetur, et eius actio ad aliud ordinatur. Unde actiones Angelorum ministeria vocantur; et propter hoc dicuntur in ministerium mitti.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33100)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod aliqua operatio dupliciter dicitur intellectualis. Uno modo, quasi in ipso intellectu consistens, ut contemplatio. Et talis operatio non determinat sibi locum, immo, ut Augustinus dicit IV de Trin., *etiam nos, secundum quod aliquid aeternum mente sapimus, non in hoc mundo sumus*. Alio modo dicitur aliqua actio intellectualis, quia est ab aliquo intellectu regulata et imperata. Et sic manifestum est quod operationes intellectuales interdum determinant sibi loca.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33101)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod caelum Empyreum pertinet ad dignitatem Angeli secundum congruentiam quandam, quia congruum est ut supremum corporum naturae quae est supra omnia corpora, attribuatur. Non tamen Angelus aliquid dignitatis accipit a caelo Empyreo. Et ideo quando actu non est in caelo Empyreo, nihil eius dignitati subtrahitur, sicut nec regi, quando non actu sedet in regali solio, quod congruit eius dignitati.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33102)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod in nobis exterior occupatio puritatem contemplationis impedit, quia actioni insistimus secundum sensitivas vires, quarum actiones cum intenduntur, retardantur actiones intellectivae virtutis. Sed Angelus per solam intellectualem operationem regulat suas actiones exteriores. Unde actiones exteriores in nullo impediunt eius contemplationem, quia duarum actionum quarum una est regula et ratio alterius, una non impedit, sed iuvat aliam. Unde Gregorius dicit, in II Moral., quod *Angeli non sic foris exeunt, ut internae contemplationis gaudiis priventur*.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33103)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod Angeli in suis actionibus exterioribus ministrant principaliter Deo, et secundario nobis. Non quia nos sumus superiores eis, simpliciter loquendo, sed quilibet homo vel Angelus, inquantum adhaerendo Deo fit unus spiritus cum Deo, est superior omni creatura. Unde apostolus, ad Philipp. II, dicit, *superiores sibi invicem arbitrantes*.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 112 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33104)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod omnes Angeli in ministerium mittantur. Dicit enim apostolus, ad Heb. I, *omnes sunt administratorii spiritus, in ministerium missi*.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33105)\n\nPraeterea, inter ordines supremus est ordo Seraphim, ut ex supra dictis patet. Sed Seraphim est missus ad purgandum labia prophetae, ut habetur Isaiae VI. Ergo multo magis inferiores Angeli mittuntur.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33106)\n\nPraeterea, divinae personae in infinitum excedunt omnes ordines Angelorum. Sed divinae personae mittuntur, ut supra dictum est. Ergo multo magis quicumque supremi Angeli.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33107)\n\nPraeterea, si superiores Angeli non mittuntur ad exterius ministerium, hoc non est nisi quia superiores Angeli exequuntur divina ministeria per inferiores. Sed cum omnes Angeli sint inaequales, ut supra dictum est, quilibet Angelus habet inferiorem Angelum, praeter ultimum. Ergo unus Angelus solus ministraret in ministerium missus. Quod est contra id quod dicitur Daniel VII, *millia millium ministrabant ei*.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33108)\n\nSed contra est quod Gregorius dicit, referens sententiam Dionysii, *superiora agmina usum exterioris ministerii nequaquam habent*.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33109)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut ex supra dictis patet, hoc habet ordo divinae providentiae, non solum in Angelis, sed etiam in toto universo, quod inferiora per superiora administrantur, sed ab hoc ordine in rebus corporalibus aliquando ex divina dispensatione receditur, propter altiorem ordinem, secundum scilicet quod expedit ad gratiae manifestationem. Quod enim caecus natus fuit illuminatus, quod Lazarus fuit suscitatus, immediate a Deo factum est, absque aliqua actione caelestium corporum. Sed et Angeli boni et mali possunt aliquid in istis corporibus operari praeter actionem caelestium corporum, condensando nubes in pluvias, et aliqua huiusmodi faciendo. Neque alicui debet esse dubium quin Deus immediate hominibus aliqua revelare posset, non mediantibus Angelis; et superiores Angeli, non mediantibus inferioribus. Et secundum hanc considerationem, quidam dixerunt quod, secundum communem legem, superiores non mittuntur, sed inferiores tantum; sed ex aliqua dispensatione divina, interdum etiam superiores mittuntur. Sed hoc non videtur rationabile. Quia ordo angelicus attenditur secundum dona gratiarum. Ordo autem gratiae non habet alium superiorem ordinem, propter quem praetermitti debeat, sicut praetermittitur ordo naturae propter ordinem gratiae. Considerandum est etiam quod ordo naturae in operationibus miraculorum praetermittitur, propter fidei confirmationem. Ad quam nihil valeret, si praetermitteretur ordo angelicus, quia hoc a nobis percipi non posset. Nihil etiam est ita magnum in ministeriis divinis, quod per inferiores ordines exerceri non possit. Unde Gregorius dicit quod *qui summa annuntiant, Archangeli vocantur. Hinc est quod ad virginem Mariam Gabriel Archangelus mittitur*. Quod tamen fuit summum inter omnia divina ministeria, ut ibidem subditur. Et ideo simpliciter dicendum est, cum Dionysio, quod superiores Angeli nunquam ad exterius ministerium mittuntur.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33110)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut in missionibus divinarum personarum aliqua est visibilis, quae attenditur secundum creaturam corpoream; aliqua invisibilis, quae fit secundum spiritualem effectum, ita in missionibus Angelorum aliqua dicitur exterior, quae scilicet est ad aliquod ministerium circa corporalia exhibendum, et secundum hanc missionem non omnes mittuntur; alia est interior, secundum intellectuales effectus, prout scilicet unus Angelus illuminat alium, et sic omnes Angeli mittuntur. Vel aliter dicendum quod apostolus inducit illud ad probandum quod Christus sit maior Angelis per quos data est lex; ut sic ostendat excellentiam novae legis ad veterem. Unde non oportet quod intelligatur nisi de Angelis ministerii, per quos data est lex.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33111)\n\nAd secundum dicendum, secundum Dionysium, quod ille Angelus qui missus est ad purgandum labia prophetae, fuit de inferioribus Angelis; sed dictus est Seraphim, idest incendens, aequivoce, propter hoc quod venerat ad incendendum labia prophetae. Vel dicendum quod superiores Angeli communicant propria dona, a quibus denominantur, mediantibus inferioribus Angelis. Sic igitur unus de Seraphim dictus est purgasse incendio labia prophetae, non quia hoc ipse immediate fecerit, sed quia inferior Angelus virtute eius hoc fecit. Sicut Papa dicitur absolvere aliquem, etiam si per alium officium absolutionis impendat.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33112)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod divinae personae non mittuntur in ministerium, sed aequivoce mitti dicuntur; ut ex praedictis patet.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33113)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod in divinis ministeriis est multiplex gradus. Unde nihil prohibet etiam inaequales Angelos immediate ad ministeria mitti; ita tamen quod superiores mittantur ad altiora ministeria, inferiores vero ad inferiora.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 112 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33114)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod etiam Angeli qui mittuntur, assistant. Dicit enim Gregorius, in homilia, *et mittuntur igitur Angeli, et assistunt, quia etsi circumscriptus est angelicus spiritus, summus tamen spiritus ipse, qui Deus est, circumscriptus non est*.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33115)\n\nPraeterea, Angelus Tobiae in ministerium missus fuit. Sed tamen ipse dixit, *ego sum Raphael Angelus, unus ex septem qui adstamus ante Deum*, ut habetur Tobiae XII. Ergo Angeli qui mittuntur, assistunt.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33116)\n\nPraeterea, quilibet Angelus beatus propinquior est Deo quam Satan. Sed Satan assistit Deo; secundum quod dicitur Iob I, *cum assisterent filii Dei coram domino, affuit inter eos et Satan*. Ergo multo magis Angeli qui mittuntur in ministerium, assistunt.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33117)\n\nPraeterea, si inferiores Angeli non assistunt, hoc non est nisi quia non immediate, sed per superiores Angelos divinas illuminationes recipiunt. Sed quilibet Angelus per superiorem divinas illuminationes suscipit, excepto eo qui est inter omnes supremus. Ergo solus supremus Angelus assisteret. Quod est contra illud quod habetur Dan. VII, *decies millies centena millia assistebant ei*. Ergo etiam illi qui ministrant, assistunt.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33118)\n\nSed contra est quod Gregorius dicit, XVII Moral., super illud Iob, *numquid est numerus militum eius? Assistunt, inquit, illae potestates, quae ad quaedam hominibus nuntianda non exeunt*. Ergo illi qui in ministerium mittuntur, non assistunt.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33119)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Angeli introducuntur assistentes et administrantes, ad similitudinem eorum qui alicui regi famulantur. Quorum aliqui semper ei assistunt, et eius praecepta immediate audiunt. Alii vero sunt, ad quos praecepta regalia per assistentes nuntiantur, sicut illi qui administrationi civitatum praeficiuntur, et hi dicuntur ministrantes, sed non assistentes. Considerandum est ergo quod omnes Angeli divinam essentiam immediate vident, et quantum ad hoc, omnes etiam qui ministrant, assistere dicuntur. Unde Gregorius dicit in II Moral., quod *semper assistere, aut videre faciem patris possunt, qui ad ministerium exterius mittuntur pro nostra salute*. Sed non omnes Angeli secreta divinorum mysteriorum in ipsa claritate divinae essentiae percipere possunt; sed soli superiores, per quos inferioribus denuntiantur. Et secundum hoc, soli superiores, qui sunt primae hierarchiae, assistere dicuntur, cuius proprium dicit esse Dionysius immediate a Deo illuminari.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33120)\n\nEt per hoc patet solutio ad primum et secundum, quae procedunt de primo modo assistendi.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33121)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Satan non dicitur astitisse, sed inter assistentes affuisse describitur, quia, ut Gregorius dicit II Moral., *etsi beatitudinem perdidit, naturam tamen Angelis similem non amisit*.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33122)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod omnes assistentes aliqua immediate vident in claritate divinae essentiae; et ideo totius primae hierarchiae proprium esse dicitur immediate illuminari a Deo. Sed superiores eorum plura percipiunt quam inferiores, de quibus illuminant alios, sicut etiam inter eos qui assistunt regi, plura scit de secretis regis unus quam alius.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 112 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33123)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli secundae hierarchiae omnes mittantur. Angeli enim omnes vel assistunt vel ministrant; secundum quod habetur Dan. VII. Sed Angeli secundae hierarchiae non assistunt, illuminantur enim per Angelos primae hierarchiae, sicut dicit Dionysius VIII cap. Cael. Hier. Omnes ergo Angeli secundae hierarchiae in ministerium mittuntur.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33124)\n\nPraeterea, Gregorius dicit, XVII Moral., quod *plures sunt qui ministrant, quam qui assistunt*. Sed hoc non esset, si Angeli secundae hierarchiae in ministerium non mitterentur. Ergo omnes Angeli secundae hierarchiae in ministerium mittuntur.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33125)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, quod *dominationes sunt maiores omni subiectione*. Sed mitti in ministerium, ad subiectionem pertinet. Ergo dominationes in ministerium non mittuntur.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33126)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, mitti ad exterius ministerium proprie convenit Angelo, secundum quod ex divino imperio operatur circa aliquam creaturam corporalem; quod quidem pertinet ad executionem divini ministerii. Proprietates autem Angelorum ex eorum nominibus manifestantur, ut Dionysius dicit VII cap. Cael. Hier. Et ideo Angeli illorum ordinum ad exterius ministerium mittuntur, ex quorum nominibus aliqua executio datur intelligi. In nomine autem dominationum non importatur aliqua executio, sed sola dispositio et imperium de exequendis. Sed in nominibus inferiorum ordinum intelligitur aliqua executio, nam Angeli et Archangeli denominantur a denuntiando; virtutes et potestates dicuntur per respectum ad aliquem actum; principis etiam est, ut Gregorius dicit, inter alios operantes priorem existere. Unde ad hos quinque ordines pertinet in exterius ministerium mitti, non autem ad quatuor superiores.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33127)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod dominationes computantur quidem inter Angelos ministrantes, non sicut exequentes ministerium, sed sicut disponentes et mandantes quid per alios fieri debeat. Sicut architectores in artificiis nihil manu operantur, sed solum disponunt et praecipiunt quid alii debeant operari.\n\n[I q. 112 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33128)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod de numero assistentium et ministrantium duplex ratio haberi potest. Gregorius enim dicit plures esse ministrantes quam assistentes. Intelligit enim quod dicitur, millia millium ministrabant ei, non esse dictum multiplicative, sed partitive; ac si diceretur, millia de numero millium. Et sic ministrantium numerus ponitur indefinitus, ad significandum excessum; assistentium vero finitus, cum subditur, et decies millies centena millia assistebant ei. Et hoc procedit secundum rationem Platonicorum, qui dicebant quod quanto aliqua sunt uni primo principio propinquiora, tanto sunt minoris multitudinis, sicut quanto numerus est propinquior unitati, tanto est multitudine minor. Et haec opinio salvatur quantum ad numerum ordinum, dum sex ministrant, et tres assistunt. Sed Dionysius ponit, XIV cap. Cael. Hier., quod multitudo Angelorum transcendit omnem materialem multitudinem; ut scilicet, sicut corpora superiora transcendunt corpora inferiora magnitudine quasi in immensum, ita superiores naturae incorporeae transcendunt multitudine omnes naturas corporeas; quia quod est melius, est magis a Deo intentum et multiplicatum. Et secundum hoc, cum assistentes sint superiores ministrantibus, plures erunt assistentes quam ministrantes. Unde secundum hoc, millia millium legitur multiplicativae, ac si diceretur, millies millia. Et quia decies centum sunt mille, si diceretur, decies centena millia, daretur intelligi quod tot essent assistentes, quot ministrantes, sed quia dicitur, decies millies centena millia, multo plures dicuntur esse assistentes quam ministrantes. Nec tamen hoc pro tanto dicitur, quia tantus solum sit Angelorum numerus, sed multo maior, quia omnem materialem multitudinem excedit. Quod significatur per multiplicationem maximorum numerorum supra seipsos, scilicet denarii, centenarii et millenarii; ut Dionysius ibidem dicit.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0002","text":"[I q. 112 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33093)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de missione Angelorum. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum aliqui Angeli mittantur in ministerium. Secundo, utrum omnes mittantur. Tertio, utrum illi qui mittuntur, assistant. Quarto, de quibus ordinibus mittantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0005","text":"[I q. 112 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33094)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli in ministerium non mittantur. Omnis enim missio est ad aliquem determinatum locum. Sed actiones intellectuales non determinant aliquem locum, quia intellectus abstrahit ab hic et nunc. Cum igitur actiones angelicae sint intellectuales, videtur quod Angeli ad suas actiones agendas non mittantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0007","text":"[I q. 112 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33095)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0008","text":"Praeterea, caelum Empyreum est locus pertinens ad dignitatem Angelorum. Si igitur ad nos mittantur in ministerium, videtur quod eorum dignitati aliquid depereat. Quod est inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0009","text":"[I q. 112 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33096)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0010","text":"Praeterea, exterior occupatio impedit sapientiae contemplationem, unde dicitur Eccli. XXXVIII, *qui minoratur actu, percipiet sapientiam*. Si igitur Angeli aliqui mittuntur ad exteriora ministeria, videtur quod retardentur a contemplatione. Sed tota eorum beatitudo in contemplatione Dei consistit. Si ergo mitterentur, eorum beatitudo minueretur. Quod est inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0011","text":"[I q. 112 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33097)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0012","text":"Praeterea, ministrare est inferioris, unde dicitur Lucae XXII, *quis maior est, qui recumbit, an ille qui ministrat? Nonne qui recumbit?* Sed Angeli sunt maiores nobis ordine naturae. Ergo non mittuntur in ministerium nostrum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0013","text":"[I q. 112 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33098)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0014","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Exod. XXIII, *ecce ego mittam Angelum meum, qui praecedat te*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0015","text":"[I q. 112 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33099)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ex supra dictis manifestum esse potest quod aliqui Angeli in ministerium mittuntur a Deo. Ut enim supra dictum est, cum de missione divinarum personarum ageretur, ille mitti dicitur, qui aliquo modo ab alio procedit, ut incipiat esse ubi prius non erat, vel ubi prius erat, per alium modum. Filius enim aut spiritus sanctus mitti dicitur, ut a patre procedens per originem; et incipit esse novo modo, idest per gratiam vel per naturam assumptam, ubi prius erat per deitatis praesentiam. Dei enim proprium est ubique esse, quia cum sit universale agens, eius virtus attingit omnia entia; unde est in omnibus rebus, ut supra dictum est. Virtus autem Angeli, cum sit particulare agens, non attingit totum universum; sed sic attingit unum, quod non attingit aliud. Et ideo ita est hic, quod non alibi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0017","text":"Manifestum est autem per supra dicta, quod creatura corporalis per Angelos administratur. Cum igitur aliquid est fiendum per aliquem Angelum circa aliquam creaturam corpoream, de novo applicatur Angelus illi corpori sua virtute; et sic Angelus de novo incipit ibi esse. Et hoc totum procedit ex imperio divino. Unde sequitur, secundum praemissa, quod Angelus a Deo mittatur. Sed actio quam Angelus missus exercet, procedit a Deo sicut a primo principio, cuius nutu et auctoritate Angeli operantur; et in Deum reducitur sicut in ultimum finem. Et hoc facit rationem ministri, nam minister est sicut instrumentum intelligens; instrumentum autem ab alio movetur, et eius actio ad aliud ordinatur. Unde actiones Angelorum ministeria vocantur; et propter hoc dicuntur in ministerium mitti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0018","text":"[I q. 112 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33100)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0019","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod aliqua operatio dupliciter dicitur intellectualis. Uno modo, quasi in ipso intellectu consistens, ut contemplatio. Et talis operatio non determinat sibi locum, immo, ut Augustinus dicit IV de Trin., *etiam nos, secundum quod aliquid aeternum mente sapimus, non in hoc mundo sumus*. Alio modo dicitur aliqua actio intellectualis, quia est ab aliquo intellectu regulata et imperata. Et sic manifestum est quod operationes intellectuales interdum determinant sibi loca.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0020","text":"[I q. 112 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33101)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0021","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod caelum Empyreum pertinet ad dignitatem Angeli secundum congruentiam quandam, quia congruum est ut supremum corporum naturae quae est supra omnia corpora, attribuatur. Non tamen Angelus aliquid dignitatis accipit a caelo Empyreo. Et ideo quando actu non est in caelo Empyreo, nihil eius dignitati subtrahitur, sicut nec regi, quando non actu sedet in regali solio, quod congruit eius dignitati.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0022","text":"[I q. 112 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33102)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0023","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod in nobis exterior occupatio puritatem contemplationis impedit, quia actioni insistimus secundum sensitivas vires, quarum actiones cum intenduntur, retardantur actiones intellectivae virtutis. Sed Angelus per solam intellectualem operationem regulat suas actiones exteriores. Unde actiones exteriores in nullo impediunt eius contemplationem, quia duarum actionum quarum una est regula et ratio alterius, una non impedit, sed iuvat aliam. Unde Gregorius dicit, in II Moral., quod *Angeli non sic foris exeunt, ut internae contemplationis gaudiis priventur*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0024","text":"[I q. 112 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33103)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0025","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod Angeli in suis actionibus exterioribus ministrant principaliter Deo, et secundario nobis. Non quia nos sumus superiores eis, simpliciter loquendo, sed quilibet homo vel Angelus, inquantum adhaerendo Deo fit unus spiritus cum Deo, est superior omni creatura. Unde apostolus, ad Philipp. II, dicit, *superiores sibi invicem arbitrantes*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0026","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0027","text":"[I q. 112 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33104)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0028","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod omnes Angeli in ministerium mittantur. Dicit enim apostolus, ad Heb. I, *omnes sunt administratorii spiritus, in ministerium missi*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0029","text":"[I q. 112 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33105)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0030","text":"Praeterea, inter ordines supremus est ordo Seraphim, ut ex supra dictis patet. Sed Seraphim est missus ad purgandum labia prophetae, ut habetur Isaiae VI. Ergo multo magis inferiores Angeli mittuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0031","text":"[I q. 112 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33106)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0032","text":"Praeterea, divinae personae in infinitum excedunt omnes ordines Angelorum. Sed divinae personae mittuntur, ut supra dictum est. Ergo multo magis quicumque supremi Angeli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0033","text":"[I q. 112 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33107)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0034","text":"Praeterea, si superiores Angeli non mittuntur ad exterius ministerium, hoc non est nisi quia superiores Angeli exequuntur divina ministeria per inferiores. Sed cum omnes Angeli sint inaequales, ut supra dictum est, quilibet Angelus habet inferiorem Angelum, praeter ultimum. Ergo unus Angelus solus ministraret in ministerium missus. Quod est contra id quod dicitur Daniel VII, *millia millium ministrabant ei*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0035","text":"[I q. 112 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33108)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0036","text":"Sed contra est quod Gregorius dicit, referens sententiam Dionysii, *superiora agmina usum exterioris ministerii nequaquam habent*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0037","text":"[I q. 112 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33109)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0038","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut ex supra dictis patet, hoc habet ordo divinae providentiae, non solum in Angelis, sed etiam in toto universo, quod inferiora per superiora administrantur, sed ab hoc ordine in rebus corporalibus aliquando ex divina dispensatione receditur, propter altiorem ordinem, secundum scilicet quod expedit ad gratiae manifestationem. Quod enim caecus natus fuit illuminatus, quod Lazarus fuit suscitatus, immediate a Deo factum est, absque aliqua actione caelestium corporum. Sed et Angeli boni et mali possunt aliquid in istis corporibus operari praeter actionem caelestium corporum, condensando nubes in pluvias, et aliqua huiusmodi faciendo. Neque alicui debet esse dubium quin Deus immediate hominibus aliqua revelare posset, non mediantibus Angelis; et superiores Angeli, non mediantibus inferioribus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0039","text":"Et secundum hanc considerationem, quidam dixerunt quod, secundum communem legem, superiores non mittuntur, sed inferiores tantum; sed ex aliqua dispensatione divina, interdum etiam superiores mittuntur. Sed hoc non videtur rationabile. Quia ordo angelicus attenditur secundum dona gratiarum. Ordo autem gratiae non habet alium superiorem ordinem, propter quem praetermitti debeat, sicut praetermittitur ordo naturae propter ordinem gratiae. Considerandum est etiam quod ordo naturae in operationibus miraculorum praetermittitur, propter fidei confirmationem. Ad quam nihil valeret, si praetermitteretur ordo angelicus, quia hoc a nobis percipi non posset. Nihil etiam est ita magnum in ministeriis divinis, quod per inferiores ordines exerceri non possit. Unde Gregorius dicit quod *qui summa annuntiant, Archangeli vocantur. Hinc est quod ad virginem Mariam Gabriel Archangelus mittitur*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0040","text":"Quod tamen fuit summum inter omnia divina ministeria, ut ibidem subditur. Et ideo simpliciter dicendum est, cum Dionysio, quod superiores Angeli nunquam ad exterius ministerium mittuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0041","text":"[I q. 112 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33110)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0042","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut in missionibus divinarum personarum aliqua est visibilis, quae attenditur secundum creaturam corpoream; aliqua invisibilis, quae fit secundum spiritualem effectum, ita in missionibus Angelorum aliqua dicitur exterior, quae scilicet est ad aliquod ministerium circa corporalia exhibendum, et secundum hanc missionem non omnes mittuntur; alia est interior, secundum intellectuales effectus, prout scilicet unus Angelus illuminat alium, et sic omnes Angeli mittuntur. Vel aliter dicendum quod apostolus inducit illud ad probandum quod Christus sit maior Angelis per quos data est lex; ut sic ostendat excellentiam novae legis ad veterem. Unde non oportet quod intelligatur nisi de Angelis ministerii, per quos data est lex.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0043","text":"[I q. 112 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33111)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0044","text":"Ad secundum dicendum, secundum Dionysium, quod ille Angelus qui missus est ad purgandum labia prophetae, fuit de inferioribus Angelis; sed dictus est Seraphim, idest incendens, aequivoce, propter hoc quod venerat ad incendendum labia prophetae. Vel dicendum quod superiores Angeli communicant propria dona, a quibus denominantur, mediantibus inferioribus Angelis. Sic igitur unus de Seraphim dictus est purgasse incendio labia prophetae, non quia hoc ipse immediate fecerit, sed quia inferior Angelus virtute eius hoc fecit. Sicut Papa dicitur absolvere aliquem, etiam si per alium officium absolutionis impendat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0045","text":"[I q. 112 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33112)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0046","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod divinae personae non mittuntur in ministerium, sed aequivoce mitti dicuntur; ut ex praedictis patet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0047","text":"[I q. 112 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33113)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0048","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod in divinis ministeriis est multiplex gradus. Unde nihil prohibet etiam inaequales Angelos immediate ad ministeria mitti; ita tamen quod superiores mittantur ad altiora ministeria, inferiores vero ad inferiora.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0049","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0050","text":"[I q. 112 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33114)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0051","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod etiam Angeli qui mittuntur, assistant. Dicit enim Gregorius, in homilia, *et mittuntur igitur Angeli, et assistunt, quia etsi circumscriptus est angelicus spiritus, summus tamen spiritus ipse, qui Deus est, circumscriptus non est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0052","text":"[I q. 112 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33115)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0053","text":"Praeterea, Angelus Tobiae in ministerium missus fuit. Sed tamen ipse dixit, *ego sum Raphael Angelus, unus ex septem qui adstamus ante Deum*, ut habetur Tobiae XII. Ergo Angeli qui mittuntur, assistunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0054","text":"[I q. 112 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33116)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0055","text":"Praeterea, quilibet Angelus beatus propinquior est Deo quam Satan. Sed Satan assistit Deo; secundum quod dicitur Iob I, *cum assisterent filii Dei coram domino, affuit inter eos et Satan*. Ergo multo magis Angeli qui mittuntur in ministerium, assistunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0056","text":"[I q. 112 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33117)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0057","text":"Praeterea, si inferiores Angeli non assistunt, hoc non est nisi quia non immediate, sed per superiores Angelos divinas illuminationes recipiunt. Sed quilibet Angelus per superiorem divinas illuminationes suscipit, excepto eo qui est inter omnes supremus. Ergo solus supremus Angelus assisteret. Quod est contra illud quod habetur Dan. VII, *decies millies centena millia assistebant ei*. Ergo etiam illi qui ministrant, assistunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0058","text":"[I q. 112 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33118)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0059","text":"Sed contra est quod Gregorius dicit, XVII Moral., super illud Iob, *numquid est numerus militum eius? Assistunt, inquit, illae potestates, quae ad quaedam hominibus nuntianda non exeunt*. Ergo illi qui in ministerium mittuntur, non assistunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0060","text":"[I q. 112 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33119)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0061","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Angeli introducuntur assistentes et administrantes, ad similitudinem eorum qui alicui regi famulantur. Quorum aliqui semper ei assistunt, et eius praecepta immediate audiunt. Alii vero sunt, ad quos praecepta regalia per assistentes nuntiantur, sicut illi qui administrationi civitatum praeficiuntur, et hi dicuntur ministrantes, sed non assistentes. Considerandum est ergo quod omnes Angeli divinam essentiam immediate vident, et quantum ad hoc, omnes etiam qui ministrant, assistere dicuntur. Unde Gregorius dicit in II Moral., quod *semper assistere, aut videre faciem patris possunt, qui ad ministerium exterius mittuntur pro nostra salute*. Sed non omnes Angeli secreta divinorum mysteriorum in ipsa claritate divinae essentiae percipere possunt; sed soli superiores, per quos inferioribus denuntiantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0062","text":"Et secundum hoc, soli superiores, qui sunt primae hierarchiae, assistere dicuntur, cuius proprium dicit esse Dionysius immediate a Deo illuminari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0063","text":"[I q. 112 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33120)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0064","text":"Et per hoc patet solutio ad primum et secundum, quae procedunt de primo modo assistendi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0065","text":"[I q. 112 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33121)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0066","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Satan non dicitur astitisse, sed inter assistentes affuisse describitur, quia, ut Gregorius dicit II Moral., *etsi beatitudinem perdidit, naturam tamen Angelis similem non amisit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0067","text":"[I q. 112 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33122)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0068","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod omnes assistentes aliqua immediate vident in claritate divinae essentiae; et ideo totius primae hierarchiae proprium esse dicitur immediate illuminari a Deo. Sed superiores eorum plura percipiunt quam inferiores, de quibus illuminant alios, sicut etiam inter eos qui assistunt regi, plura scit de secretis regis unus quam alius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0069","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0070","text":"[I q. 112 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33123)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0071","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli secundae hierarchiae omnes mittantur. Angeli enim omnes vel assistunt vel ministrant; secundum quod habetur Dan. VII. Sed Angeli secundae hierarchiae non assistunt, illuminantur enim per Angelos primae hierarchiae, sicut dicit Dionysius VIII cap. Cael. Hier. Omnes ergo Angeli secundae hierarchiae in ministerium mittuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0072","text":"[I q. 112 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33124)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0073","text":"Praeterea, Gregorius dicit, XVII Moral., quod *plures sunt qui ministrant, quam qui assistunt*. Sed hoc non esset, si Angeli secundae hierarchiae in ministerium non mitterentur. Ergo omnes Angeli secundae hierarchiae in ministerium mittuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0074","text":"[I q. 112 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33125)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0075","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, quod *dominationes sunt maiores omni subiectione*. Sed mitti in ministerium, ad subiectionem pertinet. Ergo dominationes in ministerium non mittuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0076","text":"[I q. 112 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33126)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0077","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, mitti ad exterius ministerium proprie convenit Angelo, secundum quod ex divino imperio operatur circa aliquam creaturam corporalem; quod quidem pertinet ad executionem divini ministerii. Proprietates autem Angelorum ex eorum nominibus manifestantur, ut Dionysius dicit VII cap. Cael. Hier. Et ideo Angeli illorum ordinum ad exterius ministerium mittuntur, ex quorum nominibus aliqua executio datur intelligi. In nomine autem dominationum non importatur aliqua executio, sed sola dispositio et imperium de exequendis. Sed in nominibus inferiorum ordinum intelligitur aliqua executio, nam Angeli et Archangeli denominantur a denuntiando; virtutes et potestates dicuntur per respectum ad aliquem actum; principis etiam est, ut Gregorius dicit, inter alios operantes priorem existere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0078","text":"Unde ad hos quinque ordines pertinet in exterius ministerium mitti, non autem ad quatuor superiores.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0079","text":"[I q. 112 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33127)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0080","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod dominationes computantur quidem inter Angelos ministrantes, non sicut exequentes ministerium, sed sicut disponentes et mandantes quid per alios fieri debeat. Sicut architectores in artificiis nihil manu operantur, sed solum disponunt et praecipiunt quid alii debeant operari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0081","text":"[I q. 112 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33128)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0082","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod de numero assistentium et ministrantium duplex ratio haberi potest. Gregorius enim dicit plures esse ministrantes quam assistentes. Intelligit enim quod dicitur, millia millium ministrabant ei, non esse dictum multiplicative, sed partitive; ac si diceretur, millia de numero millium. Et sic ministrantium numerus ponitur indefinitus, ad significandum excessum; assistentium vero finitus, cum subditur, et decies millies centena millia assistebant ei. Et hoc procedit secundum rationem Platonicorum, qui dicebant quod quanto aliqua sunt uni primo principio propinquiora, tanto sunt minoris multitudinis, sicut quanto numerus est propinquior unitati, tanto est multitudine minor. Et haec opinio salvatur quantum ad numerum ordinum, dum sex ministrant, et tres assistunt. Sed Dionysius ponit, XIV cap. Cael.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0083","text":"Hier., quod multitudo Angelorum transcendit omnem materialem multitudinem; ut scilicet, sicut corpora superiora transcendunt corpora inferiora magnitudine quasi in immensum, ita superiores naturae incorporeae transcendunt multitudine omnes naturas corporeas; quia quod est melius, est magis a Deo intentum et multiplicatum. Et secundum hoc, cum assistentes sint superiores ministrantibus, plures erunt assistentes quam ministrantes. Unde secundum hoc, millia millium legitur multiplicativae, ac si diceretur, millies millia. Et quia decies centum sunt mille, si diceretur, decies centena millia, daretur intelligi quod tot essent assistentes, quot ministrantes, sed quia dicitur, decies millies centena millia, multo plures dicuntur esse assistentes quam ministrantes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0084","text":"Nec tamen hoc pro tanto dicitur, quia tantus solum sit Angelorum numerus, sed multo maior, quia omnem materialem multitudinem excedit. Quod significatur per multiplicationem maximorum numerorum supra seipsos, scilicet denarii, centenarii et millenarii; ut Dionysius ibidem dicit.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"771d5f8ab197955420e245b7610481cb975c48211080a80b73ddfd31f9d3c478","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T154530903704Z_f4144c03","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 112","source_sha256":"771d5f8ab197955420e245b7610481cb975c48211080a80b73ddfd31f9d3c478","sha256":"4bf8d1f58ed120a6be81f3e85121722bbc690614c72dc23ec18de563b421b318","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:45:30.903741+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0002","text":"[I q. 112 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33093)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0003","text":"Next, consideration must be given to the mission of the angels. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, whether any angels are sent in ministry. Second, whether all are sent. Third, whether those who are sent assist. Fourth, from which orders they are sent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0005","text":"[I q. 112 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33094)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that angels are not sent in ministry. For every mission is to some determinate place. But intellectual actions do not determine any place, because the intellect abstracts from here and now. Therefore, since angelic actions are intellectual, it seems that angels are not sent to carry out their actions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0007","text":"[I q. 112 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33095)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0008","text":"Further, the empyrean heaven is a place pertaining to the dignity of angels. Therefore, if they are sent to us in ministry, it seems that something is lost from their dignity. Which is unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0009","text":"[I q. 112 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33096)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0010","text":"Further, external occupation impedes the contemplation of wisdom; whence it is said in Ecclesiasticus XXXVIII, *he who is diminished in action shall receive wisdom*. Therefore, if any angels are sent to external ministries, it seems that they are hindered from contemplation. But their whole beatitude consists in the contemplation of God. Therefore, if they were sent, their beatitude would be diminished. Which is unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0011","text":"[I q. 112 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33097)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0012","text":"Further, to minister belongs to an inferior; whence it is said in Luke XXII, *which is greater, he who reclines, or he who ministers? Is it not he who reclines?* But angels are greater than us in the order of nature. Therefore, they are not sent in ministry to us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0013","text":"[I q. 112 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33098)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0014","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Exodus XXIII, *behold, I shall send my angel, who may go before you*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0015","text":"[I q. 112 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33099)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0016","text":"I answer that it must be said that from what has been said above it can be clear that some angels are sent by God in ministry. For, as was said above when the mission of the divine persons was being treated, that is said to be sent which proceeds in some way from another, so that it begins to be where previously it was not, or where previously it was, in another mode. For the Son or the Holy Spirit is said to be sent as proceeding from the Father by origin; and he begins to be in a new mode, that is, through grace or assumed nature, where previously he was through the presence of the Godhead. For it is proper to God to be everywhere, because, since he is a universal agent, his power reaches all beings; whence he is in all things, as was said above. But the power of an angel, since it is a particular agent, does not reach the whole universe; rather, it so reaches one thing that it does not reach another. And therefore it is here in such a way that it is not elsewhere.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0017","text":"Moreover, it is clear from what has been said above that corporeal creation is administered through angels. Therefore, when something is to be done by some angel concerning some corporeal creature, the angel is newly applied by his power to that body; and thus the angel newly begins to be there. And all this proceeds from the divine command. Whence it follows, according to the foregoing, that the angel is sent by God. But the action which the sent angel performs proceeds from God as from its first principle, by whose will and authority angels operate; and it is referred back to God as to its ultimate end. And this constitutes the nature of a minister, for a minister is like an intelligent instrument; but an instrument is moved by another, and its action is ordered to another. Whence the actions of angels are called ministries; and for this reason they are said to be sent in ministry.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0018","text":"[I q. 112 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33100)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0019","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that an operation is called intellectual in two ways. In one way, as consisting in the intellect itself, as contemplation. And such an operation does not determine a place for itself; indeed, as Augustine says in *On the Trinity*, IV, *even we, insofar as we savor something eternal in the mind, are not in this world*. In another way, an action is called intellectual because it is regulated and commanded by some intellect. And thus it is clear that intellectual operations sometimes determine places for themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0020","text":"[I q. 112 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33101)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0021","text":"To the second it must be said that the empyrean heaven pertains to the dignity of an angel by a certain fittingness, because it is fitting that the highest of bodily natures, which is above all bodies, be assigned to him. Yet an angel receives no dignity from the empyrean heaven. And therefore, when he is not actually in the empyrean heaven, nothing is taken away from his dignity, just as nothing is taken away from a king when he is not actually seated on the royal throne, which befits his dignity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0022","text":"[I q. 112 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33102)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0023","text":"To the third it must be said that, in us, external occupation impedes the purity of contemplation, because we attend to action according to the sensitive powers, whose actions, when they are intensified, retard the actions of the intellective power. But an angel regulates his external actions by intellectual operation alone. Hence external actions in no way impede his contemplation, because, of two actions of which one is the rule and reason of the other, the one does not impede but helps the other. Whence Gregory says, in *Morals*, II, that *angels do not go forth outwardly in such a way as to be deprived of the joys of inward contemplation*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0024","text":"[I q. 112 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33103)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0025","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that Angels in their exterior actions minister principally to God, and secondarily to us. Not because we are superior to them, speaking simply, but because every man or Angel, insofar as by adhering to God he becomes one spirit with God, is superior to every creature. Hence the Apostle says, in Philippians II, *deeming one another superior to themselves*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0026","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0027","text":"[I q. 112 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33104)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0028","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that all Angels are sent in ministry. For the Apostle says, in Hebrews I, *are they not all ministering spirits, sent for ministry*?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0029","text":"[I q. 112 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33105)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0030","text":"Further, among the orders, the highest is the order of the Seraphim, as is clear from what was said above. But a Seraph was sent to cleanse the lips of the prophet, as is found in Isaiah VI. Therefore, much more are the lower Angels sent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0031","text":"[I q. 112 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33106)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0032","text":"Further, the divine persons infinitely exceed all orders of Angels. But the divine persons are sent, as was said above. Therefore, much more are any of the highest Angels [sent].","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0033","text":"[I q. 112 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33107)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0034","text":"Further, if the higher Angels are not sent for exterior ministry, this can only be because the higher Angels carry out the divine ministries through the lower ones. But since all Angels are unequal, as was said above, every Angel has an Angel lower than himself, except the last. Therefore one Angel alone would minister when sent in ministry. This is contrary to what is said in Daniel VII: *thousands of thousands ministered to him*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0035","text":"[I q. 112 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33108)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0036","text":"On the contrary, Gregory says, reporting the opinion of Dionysius: *the higher hosts by no means have the use of exterior ministry*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0037","text":"[I q. 112 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33109)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0038","text":"I answer that, as is clear from what was said above, this belongs to the order of divine providence, not only among Angels but also in the whole universe: that lower things are administered through higher things. But this order is sometimes departed from in bodily things by divine dispensation, on account of a higher order, namely, insofar as this is expedient for the manifestation of grace. For the fact that the man born blind was enlightened, that Lazarus was raised, was done immediately by God, without any action of the heavenly bodies. But both good and evil Angels can also do something in these bodies apart from the action of heavenly bodies, by condensing clouds into rain and doing things of this kind. Nor should anyone doubt that God could immediately reveal certain things to human beings, without the mediation of Angels; and likewise the higher Angels, without the mediation of lower ones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0039","text":"And according to this consideration, some have said that, according to the common law, the higher ones are not sent, but only the lower; yet by some divine dispensation, even the higher ones are sometimes sent. But this does not seem reasonable. For the angelic order is considered according to the gifts of graces. But the order of grace has no other higher order for the sake of which it ought to be passed over, as the order of nature is passed over for the order of grace. It must also be considered that the order of nature is passed over in miraculous operations for the confirmation of faith. For this would contribute nothing if the angelic order were passed over, since this could not be perceived by us. Nor is there anything so great in the divine ministries that it could not be carried out through the lower orders. Hence Gregory says that *those who announce the highest things are called Archangels. Hence it is that the Archangel Gabriel is sent to the Virgin Mary*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0040","text":"Yet this was the highest among all the divine ministries, as is added there. And therefore it must be said simply, with Dionysius, that the higher Angels are never sent for exterior ministry.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0041","text":"[I q. 112 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33110)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0042","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, just as among the missions of the divine persons one is visible, which is considered according to a bodily creature, and another invisible, which takes place according to a spiritual effect, so among the missions of Angels one is called exterior, namely that which is for rendering some ministry concerning bodily things; and according to this mission not all are sent. Another is interior, according to intellectual effects, namely insofar as one Angel illuminates another; and thus all Angels are sent. Or it may be said otherwise that the Apostle adduces this in order to prove that Christ is greater than the Angels through whom the law was given, so that he may thus show the excellence of the new law over the old. Hence it need only be understood of the Angels of ministry, through whom the law was given.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0043","text":"[I q. 112 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33111)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0044","text":"To the second, it must be said, according to Dionysius, that the Angel who was sent to cleanse the lips of the prophet was among the lower Angels; but he was called a Seraph, that is, kindling, equivocally, because he had come to kindle the lips of the prophet. Or it may be said that the higher Angels communicate their proper gifts, from which they are named, through the lower Angels. Thus, one of the Seraphim is said to have cleansed the lips of the prophet with fire, not because he himself did this immediately, but because a lower Angel did this by his power. Just as the Pope is said to absolve someone, even if he bestows the office of absolution through another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0045","text":"[I q. 112 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33112)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0046","text":"To the third, it must be said that the divine persons are not sent in ministry, but are said to be sent equivocally, as is clear from the foregoing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0047","text":"[I q. 112 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33113)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0048","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that in the divine ministries there is a manifold gradation. Hence nothing prevents even unequal Angels from being sent immediately to ministries; yet in such a way that the higher are sent to higher ministries, and the lower to lower ones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0049","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0050","text":"[I q. 112 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33114)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0051","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that even the Angels who are sent assist. For Gregory says, in a homily, *therefore Angels are sent and assist, because, although the angelic spirit is circumscribed, yet the supreme spirit itself, which is God, is not circumscribed*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0052","text":"[I q. 112 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33115)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0053","text":"Further, the Angel of Tobias was sent for ministry. Yet he himself said, *I am Raphael the Angel, one of the seven who stand before God*, as is found in Tobit XII. Therefore the Angels who are sent assist.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0054","text":"[I q. 112 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33116)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0055","text":"Further, every blessed Angel is nearer to God than Satan. But Satan assists God, according as it is said in Job I, *when the sons of God stood before the Lord, Satan also was present among them*. Therefore, much more do the Angels who are sent in ministry assist.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0056","text":"[I q. 112 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33117)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0057","text":"Further, if the lower Angels do not assist, this is only because they receive divine illuminations not immediately, but through the higher Angels. But every Angel receives divine illuminations through a higher one, except him who is supreme among all. Therefore only the supreme Angel would assist. This is contrary to what is found in Dan. VII, *ten thousand times a hundred thousand stood before him*. Therefore even those who minister assist.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0058","text":"[I q. 112 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33118)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0059","text":"But on the contrary, Gregory says, in the seventeenth book of the Morals, on that passage of Job, *Is there any number of his armies?* “Those powers,” he says, “assist which do not go forth to announce certain things to men.” Therefore those who are sent for ministry do not assist.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0060","text":"[I q. 112 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33119)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0061","text":"I answer that Angels are introduced as assisting and administering, after the likeness of those who serve a king. Some of these always stand by him and hear his commands immediately. Others, however, are those to whom royal commands are announced through those who stand by, as those who are set over the administration of cities; and these are called ministering, but not assisting. It must therefore be considered that all Angels immediately see the divine essence, and in this respect all, even those who minister, are said to assist. Hence Gregory says in the second book of the Morals that *those who are sent outwardly for our salvation can always assist, or see the face of the Father*. But not all Angels can perceive the secrets of the divine mysteries in the very brightness of the divine essence; rather, only the higher ones can, through whom they are announced to the lower ones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0062","text":"And according to this, only the higher ones, who belong to the first hierarchy, are said to assist; Dionysius says that its proper characteristic is to be illuminated immediately by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0063","text":"[I q. 112 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33120)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0064","text":"And through this the solution to the first and second objections is clear, since they proceed according to the first mode of assisting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0065","text":"[I q. 112 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33121)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0066","text":"To the third it must be said that Satan is not said to have stood by, but is described as having been present among those standing by, because, as Gregory says in the second book of the Morals, *although he lost blessedness, he nevertheless did not lose a nature similar to the Angels*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0067","text":"[I q. 112 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33122)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0068","text":"To the fourth it must be said that all those assisting see certain things immediately in the brightness of the divine essence; and therefore it is said to be proper to the whole first hierarchy to be illuminated immediately by God. But their higher members perceive more than the lower ones, concerning which they illuminate others, just as also among those who stand by a king, one knows more than another of the king's secrets.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0069","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0070","text":"[I q. 112 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33123)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0071","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that all the Angels of the second hierarchy are sent. For all Angels either assist or minister, according as is found in Dan. VII. But the Angels of the second hierarchy do not assist, for they are illuminated through the Angels of the first hierarchy, as Dionysius says in chapter VIII of the *Celestial Hierarchy*. Therefore all the Angels of the second hierarchy are sent for ministry.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0072","text":"[I q. 112 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33124)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0073","text":"Moreover, Gregory says, in the seventeenth book of the Morals, that *there are more who minister than who assist*. But this would not be so if the Angels of the second hierarchy were not sent forth for ministry. Therefore all the Angels of the second hierarchy are sent forth for ministry.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0074","text":"[I q. 112 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33125)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0075","text":"On the contrary, Dionysius says that *dominions are greater than every subjection*. But to be sent forth for ministry pertains to subjection. Therefore dominions are not sent forth for ministry.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0076","text":"[I q. 112 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33126)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0077","text":"I answer that, as was said above, to be sent forth for an external ministry properly befits an Angel, insofar as he acts, by divine command, upon some bodily creature; and this pertains to the execution of the divine ministry. Now the properties of the Angels are made manifest from their names, as Dionysius says in chapter VII of the Celestial Hierarchy. And therefore the Angels of those orders are sent forth for external ministry from whose names some execution is understood. But in the name of dominions no execution is implied, but only disposition and command concerning the things to be executed. But in the names of the lower orders some execution is understood: for Angels and Archangels are denominated from announcing; virtues and powers are so called in relation to some act; and it also belongs to a prince, as Gregory says, to stand first among the other workers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0078","text":"Hence it belongs to these five orders to be sent forth for external ministry, but not to the four higher ones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0079","text":"[I q. 112 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33127)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0080","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that dominions are indeed counted among the ministering Angels, not as executing the ministry, but as disposing and commanding what ought to be done by others. Just as architects in crafts do nothing with their hands, but only arrange and command what others ought to do.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0081","text":"[I q. 112 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33128)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0082","text":"To the second, it must be said that a twofold account can be given of the number of those who assist and those who minister. For Gregory says that there are more who minister than who assist. For he understands the statement, “thousands of thousands ministered to him,” not to have been said multiplicatively, but partitively; as though it were said, thousands out of the number of thousands. And thus the number of those ministering is set down as indefinite, in order to signify excess; but that of those assisting as finite, when it is added, “and ten thousand times a hundred thousand stood before him.” And this proceeds according to the reasoning of the Platonists, who said that the closer certain things are to one first principle, the smaller they are in multitude; just as the closer a number is to unity, the smaller it is in multitude. And this opinion is maintained as regards the number of orders, while six minister and three assist. But Dionysius holds, in chapter XIV of the Celestial","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0083","text":"Hierarchy, that the multitude of Angels surpasses every material multitude; namely, so that, just as higher bodies surpass lower bodies in magnitude as it were immeasurably, so higher incorporeal natures surpass all bodily natures in multitude; because what is better is more intended and multiplied by God. And according to this, since those assisting are higher than those ministering, there will be more assisting than ministering. Hence, according to this, “thousands of thousands” is read multiplicatively, as though it said “a thousand times thousands.” And because ten times one hundred are a thousand, if it were said “ten times a hundred thousand,” it would be understood that there were as many assisting as ministering; but because it is said, “ten thousand times a hundred thousand,” those assisting are said to be far more numerous than those ministering.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__104:P0084","text":"Nevertheless, this is not said because the number of Angels is only so great, but because it is much greater, since it exceeds every material multitude. This is signified by the multiplication of the greatest numbers by themselves, namely, ten, one hundred, and one thousand; as Dionysius says in the same place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__104:P0084"]}],"source_digest":"0e1b046e763462819c91b33351ac9f085f0963fa16c9e8b6b0963bc390d6c619","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 112"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 112","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105","title":"First Part · Question 113","order":114,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC__105","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 113 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33129)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de custodia bonorum Angelorum, et de impugnatione malorum. Et circa primum quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum homines ab Angelis custodiantur. Secundo, utrum singulis hominibus singuli Angeli ad custodiam deputentur. Tertio, utrum custodia pertineat solum ad ultimum ordinem Angelorum. Quarto, utrum omni homini conveniat habere Angelum custodem. Quinto, quando incipiat custodia Angeli circa hominem. Sexto, utrum Angelus semper custodiat hominem. Septimo, utrum doleat de perditione custoditi. Octavo, utrum inter Angelos sit pugna ratione custodiae.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 113 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33130)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homines non custodiantur ab Angelis. Custodes enim deputantur aliquibus vel quia nesciunt, vel quia non possunt custodire seipsos; sicut pueris et infirmis. Sed homo potest custodire seipsum per liberum arbitrium; et scit, per naturalem cognitionem legis naturalis. Ergo homo non custoditur ab Angelo.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33131)\n\nPraeterea, ubi adest fortior custodia, infirmior superfluere videtur. Sed homines custodiuntur a Deo; secundum illud Psalmi CXX, *non dormitabit neque dormiet qui custodit Israel*. Ergo non est necessarium quod homo custodiatur ab Angelo.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33132)\n\nPraeterea, perditio custoditi redundat in negligentiam custodis, unde dicitur cuidam, III Reg. XX, *custodi virum istum, qui si lapsus fuerit, erit anima tua pro anima eius*. Sed multi homines quotidie pereunt, in peccatum cadentes, quibus Angeli subvenire possent vel visibiliter apparendo, vel miracula faciendo, vel aliquo simili modo. Essent ergo negligentes Angeli, si eorum custodiae homines essent commissi, quod patet esse falsum. Non igitur Angeli sunt hominum custodes.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33133)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo, *Angelis suis mandavit de te, ut custodiant te in omnibus viis tuis*.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33134)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, secundum rationem divinae providentiae, hoc in rebus omnibus invenitur, quod mobilia et variabilia per immobilia et invariabilia moventur et regulantur; sicut omnia corporalia per substantias spirituales immobiles, et corpora inferiora per corpora superiora, quae sunt invariabilia secundum substantiam. Sed et nos ipsi regulamur circa conclusiones in quibus possumus diversimode opinari, per principia quae invariabiliter tenemus. Manifestum est autem quod in rebus agendis cognitio et affectus hominis multipliciter variari et deficere possunt a bono. Et ideo necessarium fuit quod hominibus Angeli ad custodiam deputarentur, per quos regularentur et moverentur ad bonum.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33135)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod per liberum arbitrium potest homo aliqualiter malum vitare, sed non sufficienter, quia infirmatur circa affectum boni, propter multiplices animae passiones. Similiter etiam universalis cognitio naturalis legis, quae homini naturaliter adest, aliqualiter dirigit hominem ad bonum, sed non sufficienter, quia in applicando universalia principia iuris ad particularia opera, contingit hominem multipliciter deficere. Unde dicitur Sap. IX, *cogitationes mortalium timidae, et incertae providentiae nostrae*. Et ideo necessaria fuit homini custodia Angelorum.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33136)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ad bene operandum duo requiruntur. Primo quidem, quod affectus inclinetur ad bonum, quod quidem fit in nobis per habitum virtutis moralis. Secundo autem, quod ratio inveniat congruas vias ad perficiendum bonum virtutis, quod quidem philosophus attribuit prudentiae. Quantum ergo ad primum, Deus immediate custodit hominem, infundendo ei gratiam et virtutes. Quantum autem ad secundum, Deus custodit hominem sicut universalis instructor, cuius instructio ad hominem provenit mediantibus Angelis, ut supra habitum est.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33137)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut homines a naturali instinctu boni discedunt propter passionem peccati; ita etiam discedunt ab instigatione bonorum Angelorum, quae fit invisibiliter per hoc quod homines illuminant ad bene agendum. Unde quod homines pereunt, non est imputandum negligentiae Angelorum, sed malitiae hominum. Quod autem aliquando, praeter legem communem, hominibus visibiliter apparent, ex speciali Dei gratia est, sicut etiam quod praeter ordinem naturae miracula fiunt.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 113 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33138)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non singuli homines a singulis Angelis custodiantur. Angelus enim est virtuosior quam homo. Sed unus homo sufficit ad custodiam multorum hominum. Ergo multo magis unus Angelus multos homines potest custodire.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33139)\n\nPraeterea, inferiora reducuntur in Deum a superioribus per media, ut Dionysius dicit. Sed cum omnes Angeli sint inaequales, ut supra dictum est, solus unus Angelus est inter quem et homines non est aliquis medius. Ergo unus Angelus solus est qui immediate custodit homines.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33140)\n\nPraeterea, maiores Angeli maioribus officiis deputantur. Sed non est maius officium custodire unum hominem quam alium, cum omnes homines natura sint pares. Cum ergo omnium Angelorum sit unus maior alio, secundum Dionysium, videtur quod diversi homines non custodiantur a diversis Angelis.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33141)\n\nSed contra est quod Hieronymus, exponens illud Matth. XVIII, *Angeli eorum in caelis*, dicit, *magna est dignitas animarum, ut unaquaeque habeat, ab ortu nativitatis, in custodiam sui Angelum delegatum*.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33142)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod singulis hominibus singuli Angeli ad custodiam deputantur. Cuius ratio est, quia Angelorum custodia est quaedam executio divinae providentiae circa homines. Providentia autem Dei aliter se habet ad homines, et ad alias corruptibiles creaturas, quia aliter se habent ad incorruptibilitatem. Homines enim non solum sunt incorruptibiles quantum ad communem speciem, sed etiam quantum ad proprias formas singulorum, quae sunt animae rationales, quod de aliis rebus corruptibilibus dici non potest. Manifestum est autem quod providentia Dei principaliter est circa illa quae perpetuo manent, circa ea vero quae transeunt, providentia Dei est inquantum ordinat ipsa ad res perpetuas. Sic igitur providentia Dei comparatur ad singulos homines, sicut comparatur ad singula genera vel species corruptibilium rerum. Sed secundum Gregorium, diversi ordines deputantur diversis rerum generibus; puta potestates ad arcendos Daemones, virtutes ad miracula facienda in rebus corporeis. Et probabile est quod diversis speciebus rerum diversi Angeli eiusdem ordinis praeficiantur. Unde etiam rationabile est ut diversis hominibus diversi Angeli ad custodiam deputentur.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33143)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod alicui homini adhibetur custos dupliciter. Uno modo, inquantum est homo singularis, et sic uni homini debetur unus custos, et interdum plures deputantur ad custodiam unius. Alio modo, inquantum est pars alicuius collegii, et sic toti collegio unus homo ad custodiam praeponitur, ad quem pertinet providere ea quae pertinent ad unum hominem in ordine ad totum collegium; sicut sunt ea quae exterius aguntur, de quibus alii aedificantur vel scandalizantur. Angelorum autem custodia deputatur hominibus etiam quantum ad invisibilia et occulta, quae pertinent ad singulorum salutem secundum seipsos. Unde singulis hominibus singuli Angeli deputantur ad custodiam.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33144)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, Angeli primae hierarchiae omnes quantum ad aliqua illuminantur immediate a Deo, sed quaedam sunt de quibus illuminantur superiores tantum immediate a Deo, quae inferioribus revelant. Et idem etiam in inferioribus ordinibus considerandum est, nam aliquis infimus Angelus illuminatur quantum ad quaedam ab aliquo supremo, et quantum ad aliqua ab eo qui immediate sibi praefertur. Et sic etiam possibile est quod aliquis Angelus immediate illuminet hominem, qui tamen habet aliquos Angelos sub se, quos illuminat.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33145)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, quamvis homines natura sint pares, tamen inaequalitas in eis invenitur, secundum quod ex divina providentia quidam ordinantur ad maius, et quidam ad minus; secundum illud quod dicitur Eccli. XXXIII, *in multitudine disciplinae domini separavit eos, ex ipsis benedixit et exaltavit, ex ipsis maledixit et humiliavit*. Et sic maius officium est custodire unum hominem quam alium.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 113 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33146)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod custodire homines non pertineat solum ad infimum ordinem Angelorum. Dicit enim Chrysostomus quod hoc quod dicitur Matth. XVIII, *Angeli eorum in caelo etc., intelligitur non de quibuscumque Angelis, sed de supereminentibus*. Ergo supereminentes Angeli custodiunt homines.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33147)\n\nPraeterea, apostolus, ad Heb. I, dicit quod *Angeli sunt in ministerium missi propter eos qui haereditatem capiunt salutis*, et sic videtur quod missio Angelorum ad custodiam hominum ordinetur. Sed quinque ordines in exterius ministerium mittuntur, ut supra dictum est. Ergo omnes Angeli quinque ordinum custodiae hominum deputantur.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33148)\n\nPraeterea, ad custodiam hominum maxime videtur esse necessarium arcere Daemones, quod pertinet ad potestates, secundum Gregorium; et miracula facere, quod pertinet ad virtutes. Ergo illi etiam ordines deputantur ad custodiam, et non solum infimus.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33149)\n\nSed contra est quod in Psalmo custodia hominum attribuitur Angelis; quorum ordo est infimus, secundum Dionysium.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33150)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, homini custodia dupliciter adhibetur. Uno modo custodia particularis, secundum quod singulis hominibus singuli Angeli ad custodiam deputantur. Et talis custodia pertinet ad infimum ordinem Angelorum, quorum, secundum Gregorium, est minima nuntiare; hoc autem videtur esse minimum in officiis Angelorum, procurare ea quae ad unius hominis tantum salutem pertinent. Alia vero est custodia universalis. Et haec multiplicatur secundum diversos ordines, nam quanto agens fuerit universalius, tanto est superius. Sic igitur custodia humanae multitudinis pertinet ad ordinem principatuum, vel forte ad Archangelos, qui dicuntur principes Angeli, unde et Michael, quem Archangelum dicimus, unus de principibus dicitur Dan. X. Ulterius autem super omnes naturas corporeas habent custodiam virtutes. Et ulterius etiam super Daemones habent custodiam potestates. Et ulterius etiam super bonos spiritus habent custodiam principatus, secundum Gregorium.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33151)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod verbum Chrysostomi potest intelligi, ut loquatur de supremis in ordine infimo Angelorum, quia, ut Dionysius dicit, in quolibet ordine sunt primi, medii et ultimi. Est autem probabile quod maiores Angeli deputentur ad custodiam eorum qui sunt ad maiorem gradum gloriae a Deo electi.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33152)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod non omnes Angeli qui mittuntur, habent particularem custodiam super singulos homines; sed quidam ordines habent universalem custodiam, magis vel minus, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33153)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod etiam inferiores Angeli exercent officia superiorum, inquantum aliquid de dono eorum participant, et se habent ad superiores sicut executores virtutis eorum. Et per hunc modum etiam Angeli infimi ordinis possunt et arcere Daemones, et miracula facere.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 113 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33154)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non omnibus hominibus Angeli ad custodiam deputentur. Dicitur enim de Christo, Philipp. II, quod est *in similitudinem hominum factus, et habitu inventus ut homo*. Si igitur omnibus hominibus Angeli ad custodiam deputantur, etiam Christus Angelum custodem habuisset. Sed hoc videtur inconveniens, cum Christus sit maior omnibus Angelis. Non ergo omnibus hominibus Angeli ad custodiam deputantur.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33155)\n\nPraeterea, omnium hominum primus fuit Adam. Sed sibi non competebat habere Angelum custodem, ad minus in statu innocentiae, quia tunc nullis periculis angustiabatur. Ergo Angeli non praeficiuntur ad custodiam omnibus hominibus.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33156)\n\nPraeterea, hominibus Angeli ad custodiam deputantur, ut per eos manuducantur ad vitam aeternam, et incitentur ad bene operandum, et muniantur contra insultus Daemonum. Sed homines praesciti ad damnationem, nunquam perveniunt ad vitam aeternam. Infideles etiam, etsi interdum bona opera faciant, non tamen bene faciunt, quia non recta intentione faciunt, fides enim intentionem dirigit, ut Augustinus dicit. Antichristi etiam adventus erit secundum operationem Satanae, ut dicitur II ad Thessal. II. Non ergo omnibus hominibus Angeli ad custodiam deputantur.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33157)\n\nSed contra est auctoritas Hieronymi supra inducta, qui dicit quod *unaquaeque anima ad sui custodiam habet Angelum deputatum*.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33158)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod homo in statu vitae istius constitutus, est quasi in quadam via, qua debet tendere ad patriam. In qua quidem via multa pericula homini imminent, tum ab interiori, tum ab exteriori; secundum illud Psalmi CXLI, *in via hac qua ambulabam, absconderunt laqueum mihi*. Et ideo sicut hominibus per viam non tutam ambulantibus dantur custodes, ita et cuilibet homini, quandiu viator est, custos Angelus deputatur. Quando autem iam ad terminum viae pervenerit, iam non habebit Angelum custodem; sed habebit in regno Angelum conregnantem, in Inferno Daemonem punientem.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33159)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Christus, secundum quod homo, immediate regulabatur a verbo Dei, unde non indigebat custodia Angelorum. Et iterum secundum animam erat comprehensor; sed ratione passibilitatis corporis, erat viator. Et secundum hoc, non debebatur ei Angelus custos, tanquam superior; sed Angelus minister, tanquam inferior. Unde dicitur Matth. IV, quod *accesserunt Angeli et ministrabant ei*.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33160)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod homo in statu innocentiae non patiebatur aliquod periculum ab interiori, quia interius erant omnia ordinata, ut supra dictum est, sed imminebat ei periculum ab exteriori, propter insidias Daemonum; ut rei probavit eventus. Et ideo indigebat custodia Angelorum.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33161)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut praesciti et infideles, et etiam Antichristus, non privantur interiori auxilio naturalis rationis; ita etiam non privantur exteriori auxilio toti naturae humanae divinitus concesso, scilicet custodia Angelorum. Per quam etsi non iuventur quantum ad hoc quod vitam aeternam bonis operibus mereantur, iuvantur tamen quantum ad hoc, quod ab aliquibus malis retrahuntur, quibus et sibi ipsis et aliis nocere possunt. Nam et ipsi Daemones arcentur per bonos Angelos, ne noceant quantum volunt. Et similiter Antichristus non tantum nocebit, quantum vellet.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 113 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33162)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non deputetur homini ad custodiam a sua nativitate. Angeli enim mittuntur *in ministerium, propter eos qui haereditatem capiunt salutis*, ut apostolus, ad Heb. dicit. Sed homines incipiunt haereditatem capere salutis, quando baptizantur. Ergo Angelus deputatur homini ad custodiam a tempore Baptismi, et non a tempore nativitatis.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33163)\n\nPraeterea, homines ab Angelis custodiuntur, inquantum ab eis illuminantur per modum doctrinae. Sed pueri mox nati non sunt capaces doctrinae, quia non habent usum rationis. Ergo pueris mox natis non deputantur Angeli custodes.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33164)\n\nPraeterea, pueri in materno utero existentes habent animam rationalem aliquo tempore, sicut et post nativitatem ex utero. Sed cum sunt in materno utero, non deputantur eis Angeli ad custodiam, ut videtur, quia neque etiam ministri Ecclesiae eos sacramentis imbuunt. Non ergo statim a nativitate hominibus Angeli ad custodiam deputantur.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33165)\n\nSed contra est quod Hieronymus dicit, quod *unaquaeque anima, ab ortu nativitatis, habet in custodiam sui Angelum deputatum*.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33166)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut Origenes dicit super Matthaeum, super hoc est duplex opinio. Quidam enim dixerunt quod Angelus ad custodiam homini deputatur a tempore Baptismi, alii vero quod a tempore nativitatis. Et hanc opinionem Hieronymus approbat; et rationabiliter. Beneficia enim quae dantur homini divinitus ex eo quod est Christianus, incipiunt a tempore Baptismi; sicut perceptio Eucharistiae, et alia huiusmodi. Sed ea quae providentur homini a Deo, inquantum habet naturam rationalem, ex tunc ei exhibentur, ex quo nascendo talem naturam accipit. Et tale beneficium est custodia Angelorum, ut ex praemissis patet. Unde statim a nativitate habet homo Angelum ad sui custodiam deputatum.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33167)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Angeli mittuntur in ministerium, efficaciter quidem propter eos solos qui haereditatem capiunt salutis, si consideretur ultimus effectus custodiae, qui est perceptio haereditatis. Nihilominus tamen et aliis ministerium Angelorum non subtrahitur, quamvis in eis hanc efficaciam non habeat, quod perducantur ad salutem. Efficax tamen est circa eos Angelorum ministerium, inquantum a multis malis retrahuntur.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33168)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod officium custodiae ordinatur quidem ad illuminationem doctrinae, sicut ad ultimum et principalem effectum. Nihilominus tamen multos alios effectus habet, qui pueris competunt, scilicet arcere Daemones, et alia nocumenta tam corporalia quam spiritualia prohibere.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33169)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod puer quandiu est in materno utero, non totaliter est a matre separatus, sed per quandam colligationem est quodammodo adhuc aliquid eius, sicut et fructus pendens in arbore, est aliquid arboris. Et ideo probabiliter dici potest quod Angelus qui est in custodia matris, custodiat prolem in matris utero existentem. Sed in nativitate, quando separatur a matre, Angelus ei ad custodiam deputatur, ut Hieronymus dicit.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 113 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33170)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus custos quandoque deserat hominem cuius custodiae deputatur. Dicitur enim Ierem. li, ex persona Angelorum, *curavimus Babylonem, et non est curata, derelinquamus ergo eam*. Et Isaiae V, *auferam sepem eius, et erit in conculcationem*; Glossa, *idest Angelorum custodiam*.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33171)\n\nPraeterea, principalius custodit Deus quam Angelus. Sed Deus aliquando hominem derelinquit; secundum illud Psalmi XXI, *Deus, Deus meus, respice in me, quare me dereliquisti?* Ergo multo magis Angelus custos hominem derelinquit.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33172)\n\nPraeterea, sicut dicit Damascenus, *Angeli, cum sunt hic nobiscum, non sunt in caelo*. Sed aliquando sunt in caelo. Ergo aliquando nos derelinquunt.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33173)\n\nSed contra, Daemones nos semper impugnant; secundum illud I Petri V, *adversarius vester Diabolus tanquam leo rugiens circuit, quaerens quem devoret*. Ergo multo magis boni Angeli semper nos custodiunt.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33174)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod custodia Angelorum, ut ex supra dictis patet, est quaedam executio divinae providentiae circa homines facta. Manifestum est autem quod nec homo, nec res aliqua, totaliter divinae providentiae subtrahitur, inquantum enim aliquid participat de esse, intantum subditur universali providentiae entium. Sed intantum Deus, secundum ordinem suae providentiae, dicitur hominem derelinquere, inquantum permittit hominem pati aliquem defectum vel poenae vel culpae. Similiter etiam dicendum est quod Angelus custos nunquam totaliter dimittit hominem, sed ad aliquid interdum eum dimittit; prout scilicet non impedit quin subdatur alicui tribulationi, vel etiam quin cadat in peccatum, secundum ordinem divinorum iudiciorum. Et secundum hoc Babylon et domus Israel ab Angelis derelictae dicuntur, quia Angeli earum custodes non impediverunt quin tribulationibus subderentur.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33175)\n\nEt per hoc patet solutio ad primum et secundum.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33176)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Angelus, etsi interdum derelinquat hominem loco, non tamen derelinquit eum quantum ad effectum custodiae, quia etiam cum est in caelo, cognoscit quid circa hominem agatur; nec indiget mora temporis ad motum localem, sed statim potest adesse.\n\n## Articulus 7\n\n[I q. 113 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33177)\n\nAd septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli doleant de malis eorum quos custodiunt. Dicitur enim Isaiae XXXIII, *Angeli pacis amare flebunt*. Sed fletus est signum doloris et tristitiae. Ergo Angeli tristantur de malis hominum quos custodiunt.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33178)\n\nPraeterea, tristitia est, ut Augustinus dicit, *de his quae nobis nolentibus accidunt*. Sed perditio hominis custoditi est contra voluntatem Angeli custodis. Ergo tristantur Angeli de perditione hominum.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33179)\n\nPraeterea, sicut gaudio contrariatur tristitia, ita poenitentiae contrariatur peccatum. Sed Angeli gaudent de peccatore poenitentiam agente, ut habetur Lucae XV. Ergo tristantur de iusto in peccatum cadente.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 7 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33180)\n\nPraeterea, super illud Num. XVIII; quidquid offerunt primitiarum etc., dicit Glossa Origenis; *trahuntur Angeli in iudicium, utrum ex ipsorum negligentia, an hominum ignavia lapsi sint*. Sed quilibet rationabiliter dolet de malis propter quae in iudicium tractus est. Ergo Angeli dolent de peccatis hominum.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33181)\n\nSed contra, ubi est tristitia et dolor, non est perfecta felicitas, unde dicitur Apoc. XXI, *mors ultra non erit, neque luctus, neque clamor, neque ullus dolor*. Sed Angeli sunt perfecte beati. Ergo de nullo dolent.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33182)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod Angeli non dolent neque de peccatis, neque de poenis hominum. Tristitia enim et dolor, secundum Augustinum, non est nisi de his quae contrariantur voluntati. Nihil autem accidit in mundo quod sit contrarium voluntati Angelorum et aliorum beatorum, quia voluntas eorum totaliter inhaeret ordini divinae iustitiae; nihil autem fit in mundo, nisi quod per divinam iustitiam fit aut permittitur. Et ideo, simpliciter loquendo, nihil fit in mundo contra voluntatem beatorum. Ut enim philosophus dicit in III Ethic. illud dicitur simpliciter voluntarium, quod aliquis vult in particulari, secundum quod agitur, consideratis scilicet omnibus quae circumstant, quamvis in universali consideratum non esset voluntarium, sicut nauta non vult proiectionem mercium in mare, absolute et universaliter considerando, sed imminente periculo salutis hoc vult. Unde magis est hoc voluntarium quam involuntarium, ut ibidem dicitur. Sic igitur Angeli peccata et poenas hominum, universaliter et absolute loquendo, non volunt, volunt tamen quod circa hoc ordo divinae iustitiae servetur, secundum quem quidam poenis subduntur, et peccare permittuntur.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33183)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod verbum illud Isaiae potest intelligi de Angelis, idest nuntiis, Ezechiae, qui fleverunt propter verba Rabsacis; de quibus habetur Isaiae XXXVII. Et hoc secundum litteralem sensum. Secundum vero allegoricum, Angeli pacis sunt apostoli et alii praedicatores, qui flent pro peccatis hominum. Si vero secundum sensum anagogicum exponatur de Angelis beatis, tunc metaphorica erit locutio, ad designandum quod Angeli volunt in universali hominum salutem. Sic enim Deo et Angelis huiusmodi passiones attribuuntur.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33184)\n\nAd secundum patet solutio per ea quae dicta sunt.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33185)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod tam in poenitentia hominum, quam in peccato, manet una ratio gaudii Angelis, scilicet impletio ordinis divinae providentiae.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 7 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33186)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod Angeli ducuntur in iudicium pro peccatis hominum, non quasi rei, sed quasi testes, ad convincendum homines de eorum ignavia.\n\n## Articulus 8\n\n[I q. 113 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33187)\n\nAd octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod inter Angelos non possit esse pugna seu discordia. Dicitur enim Iob XXV, *qui facit concordiam in sublimibus*. Sed pugna opponitur concordiae. Ergo in sublimibus Angelis non est pugna.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33188)\n\nPraeterea, ubi est perfecta caritas et iusta praelatio, non potest esse pugna. Sed hoc totum est in Angelis. Ergo in Angelis non est pugna.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33189)\n\nPraeterea, si Angeli dicuntur pugnare pro eis quos custodiunt, necesse est quod unus Angelus foveat unam partem, et alius aliam. Sed si una pars habet iustitiam, e contra alia pars habet iniustitiam. Ergo sequitur quod Angelus bonus sit fautor iniustitiae, quod est inconveniens. Ergo inter bonos Angelos non est pugna.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33190)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Dan. X, ex persona Gabrielis, *princeps regni Persarum restitit mihi viginti et uno diebus*. Hic autem princeps Persarum erat Angelus regno Persarum in custodiam deputatus. Ergo unus bonus Angelus resistit alii, et sic inter eos est pugna.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33191)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ista quaestio movetur occasione horum verborum Danielis. Quae quidem Hieronymus exponit, dicens principem regni Persarum esse Angelum qui se opposuit liberationi populi Israelitici, pro quo Daniel orabat, Gabriele preces eius Deo praesentante. Haec autem resistentia potuit fieri, quia princeps aliquis Daemonum Iudaeos in Persidem ductos ad peccatum induxerat, per quod impedimentum praestabatur orationi Danielis, pro eodem populo deprecantis. Sed secundum Gregorium, XVII Moral., princeps regni Persarum bonus Angelus fuit, custodiae regni illius deputatus. Ad videndum igitur qualiter unus Angelus alteri resistere dicitur, considerandum est quod divina iudicia circa diversa regna et diversos homines, per Angelos exercentur. In suis autem actionibus Angeli secundum divinam sententiam regulantur. Contingit autem quandoque quod in diversis regnis, vel in diversis hominibus, contraria merita vel demerita inveniuntur, ut unus alteri subdatur aut praesit. Quid autem super hoc ordo divinae sapientiae habeat, cognoscere non possunt nisi Deo revelante, unde necesse habent super his sapientiam Dei consulere. Sic igitur inquantum de contrariis meritis et sibi repugnantibus, divinam consulunt voluntatem, resistere sibi invicem dicuntur, non quia sint eorum contrariae voluntates, cum in hoc omnes concordent, quod Dei sententia impleatur; sed quia ea de quibus consulunt, sunt repugnantia.\n\n[I q. 113 a. 8 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33192)\n\nEt per hoc patet solutio ad obiecta.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0002","text":"[I q. 113 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33129)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de custodia bonorum Angelorum, et de impugnatione malorum. Et circa primum quaeruntur octo. Primo, utrum homines ab Angelis custodiantur. Secundo, utrum singulis hominibus singuli Angeli ad custodiam deputentur. Tertio, utrum custodia pertineat solum ad ultimum ordinem Angelorum. Quarto, utrum omni homini conveniat habere Angelum custodem. Quinto, quando incipiat custodia Angeli circa hominem. Sexto, utrum Angelus semper custodiat hominem. Septimo, utrum doleat de perditione custoditi. Octavo, utrum inter Angelos sit pugna ratione custodiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0005","text":"[I q. 113 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33130)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homines non custodiantur ab Angelis. Custodes enim deputantur aliquibus vel quia nesciunt, vel quia non possunt custodire seipsos; sicut pueris et infirmis. Sed homo potest custodire seipsum per liberum arbitrium; et scit, per naturalem cognitionem legis naturalis. Ergo homo non custoditur ab Angelo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0007","text":"[I q. 113 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33131)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0008","text":"Praeterea, ubi adest fortior custodia, infirmior superfluere videtur. Sed homines custodiuntur a Deo; secundum illud Psalmi CXX, *non dormitabit neque dormiet qui custodit Israel*. Ergo non est necessarium quod homo custodiatur ab Angelo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0009","text":"[I q. 113 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33132)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0010","text":"Praeterea, perditio custoditi redundat in negligentiam custodis, unde dicitur cuidam, III Reg. XX, *custodi virum istum, qui si lapsus fuerit, erit anima tua pro anima eius*. Sed multi homines quotidie pereunt, in peccatum cadentes, quibus Angeli subvenire possent vel visibiliter apparendo, vel miracula faciendo, vel aliquo simili modo. Essent ergo negligentes Angeli, si eorum custodiae homines essent commissi, quod patet esse falsum. Non igitur Angeli sunt hominum custodes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0011","text":"[I q. 113 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33133)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in Psalmo, *Angelis suis mandavit de te, ut custodiant te in omnibus viis tuis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0013","text":"[I q. 113 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33134)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, secundum rationem divinae providentiae, hoc in rebus omnibus invenitur, quod mobilia et variabilia per immobilia et invariabilia moventur et regulantur; sicut omnia corporalia per substantias spirituales immobiles, et corpora inferiora per corpora superiora, quae sunt invariabilia secundum substantiam. Sed et nos ipsi regulamur circa conclusiones in quibus possumus diversimode opinari, per principia quae invariabiliter tenemus. Manifestum est autem quod in rebus agendis cognitio et affectus hominis multipliciter variari et deficere possunt a bono. Et ideo necessarium fuit quod hominibus Angeli ad custodiam deputarentur, per quos regularentur et moverentur ad bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0015","text":"[I q. 113 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33135)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0016","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod per liberum arbitrium potest homo aliqualiter malum vitare, sed non sufficienter, quia infirmatur circa affectum boni, propter multiplices animae passiones. Similiter etiam universalis cognitio naturalis legis, quae homini naturaliter adest, aliqualiter dirigit hominem ad bonum, sed non sufficienter, quia in applicando universalia principia iuris ad particularia opera, contingit hominem multipliciter deficere. Unde dicitur Sap. IX, *cogitationes mortalium timidae, et incertae providentiae nostrae*. Et ideo necessaria fuit homini custodia Angelorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0017","text":"[I q. 113 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33136)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0018","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ad bene operandum duo requiruntur. Primo quidem, quod affectus inclinetur ad bonum, quod quidem fit in nobis per habitum virtutis moralis. Secundo autem, quod ratio inveniat congruas vias ad perficiendum bonum virtutis, quod quidem philosophus attribuit prudentiae. Quantum ergo ad primum, Deus immediate custodit hominem, infundendo ei gratiam et virtutes. Quantum autem ad secundum, Deus custodit hominem sicut universalis instructor, cuius instructio ad hominem provenit mediantibus Angelis, ut supra habitum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0019","text":"[I q. 113 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33137)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0020","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut homines a naturali instinctu boni discedunt propter passionem peccati; ita etiam discedunt ab instigatione bonorum Angelorum, quae fit invisibiliter per hoc quod homines illuminant ad bene agendum. Unde quod homines pereunt, non est imputandum negligentiae Angelorum, sed malitiae hominum. Quod autem aliquando, praeter legem communem, hominibus visibiliter apparent, ex speciali Dei gratia est, sicut etiam quod praeter ordinem naturae miracula fiunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0021","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0022","text":"[I q. 113 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33138)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0023","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non singuli homines a singulis Angelis custodiantur. Angelus enim est virtuosior quam homo. Sed unus homo sufficit ad custodiam multorum hominum. Ergo multo magis unus Angelus multos homines potest custodire.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0024","text":"[I q. 113 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33139)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0025","text":"Praeterea, inferiora reducuntur in Deum a superioribus per media, ut Dionysius dicit. Sed cum omnes Angeli sint inaequales, ut supra dictum est, solus unus Angelus est inter quem et homines non est aliquis medius. Ergo unus Angelus solus est qui immediate custodit homines.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0026","text":"[I q. 113 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33140)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0027","text":"Praeterea, maiores Angeli maioribus officiis deputantur. Sed non est maius officium custodire unum hominem quam alium, cum omnes homines natura sint pares. Cum ergo omnium Angelorum sit unus maior alio, secundum Dionysium, videtur quod diversi homines non custodiantur a diversis Angelis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0028","text":"[I q. 113 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33141)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0029","text":"Sed contra est quod Hieronymus, exponens illud Matth. XVIII, *Angeli eorum in caelis*, dicit, *magna est dignitas animarum, ut unaquaeque habeat, ab ortu nativitatis, in custodiam sui Angelum delegatum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0030","text":"[I q. 113 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33142)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0031","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod singulis hominibus singuli Angeli ad custodiam deputantur. Cuius ratio est, quia Angelorum custodia est quaedam executio divinae providentiae circa homines. Providentia autem Dei aliter se habet ad homines, et ad alias corruptibiles creaturas, quia aliter se habent ad incorruptibilitatem. Homines enim non solum sunt incorruptibiles quantum ad communem speciem, sed etiam quantum ad proprias formas singulorum, quae sunt animae rationales, quod de aliis rebus corruptibilibus dici non potest. Manifestum est autem quod providentia Dei principaliter est circa illa quae perpetuo manent, circa ea vero quae transeunt, providentia Dei est inquantum ordinat ipsa ad res perpetuas. Sic igitur providentia Dei comparatur ad singulos homines, sicut comparatur ad singula genera vel species corruptibilium rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0032","text":"Sed secundum Gregorium, diversi ordines deputantur diversis rerum generibus; puta potestates ad arcendos Daemones, virtutes ad miracula facienda in rebus corporeis. Et probabile est quod diversis speciebus rerum diversi Angeli eiusdem ordinis praeficiantur. Unde etiam rationabile est ut diversis hominibus diversi Angeli ad custodiam deputentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0033","text":"[I q. 113 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33143)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0034","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod alicui homini adhibetur custos dupliciter. Uno modo, inquantum est homo singularis, et sic uni homini debetur unus custos, et interdum plures deputantur ad custodiam unius. Alio modo, inquantum est pars alicuius collegii, et sic toti collegio unus homo ad custodiam praeponitur, ad quem pertinet providere ea quae pertinent ad unum hominem in ordine ad totum collegium; sicut sunt ea quae exterius aguntur, de quibus alii aedificantur vel scandalizantur. Angelorum autem custodia deputatur hominibus etiam quantum ad invisibilia et occulta, quae pertinent ad singulorum salutem secundum seipsos. Unde singulis hominibus singuli Angeli deputantur ad custodiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0035","text":"[I q. 113 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33144)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0036","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut dictum est, Angeli primae hierarchiae omnes quantum ad aliqua illuminantur immediate a Deo, sed quaedam sunt de quibus illuminantur superiores tantum immediate a Deo, quae inferioribus revelant. Et idem etiam in inferioribus ordinibus considerandum est, nam aliquis infimus Angelus illuminatur quantum ad quaedam ab aliquo supremo, et quantum ad aliqua ab eo qui immediate sibi praefertur. Et sic etiam possibile est quod aliquis Angelus immediate illuminet hominem, qui tamen habet aliquos Angelos sub se, quos illuminat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0037","text":"[I q. 113 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33145)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0038","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, quamvis homines natura sint pares, tamen inaequalitas in eis invenitur, secundum quod ex divina providentia quidam ordinantur ad maius, et quidam ad minus; secundum illud quod dicitur Eccli. XXXIII, *in multitudine disciplinae domini separavit eos, ex ipsis benedixit et exaltavit, ex ipsis maledixit et humiliavit*. Et sic maius officium est custodire unum hominem quam alium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0039","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0040","text":"[I q. 113 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33146)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0041","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod custodire homines non pertineat solum ad infimum ordinem Angelorum. Dicit enim Chrysostomus quod hoc quod dicitur Matth. XVIII, *Angeli eorum in caelo etc., intelligitur non de quibuscumque Angelis, sed de supereminentibus*. Ergo supereminentes Angeli custodiunt homines.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0042","text":"[I q. 113 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33147)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0043","text":"Praeterea, apostolus, ad Heb. I, dicit quod *Angeli sunt in ministerium missi propter eos qui haereditatem capiunt salutis*, et sic videtur quod missio Angelorum ad custodiam hominum ordinetur. Sed quinque ordines in exterius ministerium mittuntur, ut supra dictum est. Ergo omnes Angeli quinque ordinum custodiae hominum deputantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0044","text":"[I q. 113 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33148)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0045","text":"Praeterea, ad custodiam hominum maxime videtur esse necessarium arcere Daemones, quod pertinet ad potestates, secundum Gregorium; et miracula facere, quod pertinet ad virtutes. Ergo illi etiam ordines deputantur ad custodiam, et non solum infimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0046","text":"[I q. 113 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33149)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0047","text":"Sed contra est quod in Psalmo custodia hominum attribuitur Angelis; quorum ordo est infimus, secundum Dionysium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0048","text":"[I q. 113 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33150)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0049","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, homini custodia dupliciter adhibetur. Uno modo custodia particularis, secundum quod singulis hominibus singuli Angeli ad custodiam deputantur. Et talis custodia pertinet ad infimum ordinem Angelorum, quorum, secundum Gregorium, est minima nuntiare; hoc autem videtur esse minimum in officiis Angelorum, procurare ea quae ad unius hominis tantum salutem pertinent. Alia vero est custodia universalis. Et haec multiplicatur secundum diversos ordines, nam quanto agens fuerit universalius, tanto est superius. Sic igitur custodia humanae multitudinis pertinet ad ordinem principatuum, vel forte ad Archangelos, qui dicuntur principes Angeli, unde et Michael, quem Archangelum dicimus, unus de principibus dicitur Dan. X. Ulterius autem super omnes naturas corporeas habent custodiam virtutes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0050","text":"Et ulterius etiam super Daemones habent custodiam potestates. Et ulterius etiam super bonos spiritus habent custodiam principatus, secundum Gregorium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0051","text":"[I q. 113 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33151)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0052","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod verbum Chrysostomi potest intelligi, ut loquatur de supremis in ordine infimo Angelorum, quia, ut Dionysius dicit, in quolibet ordine sunt primi, medii et ultimi. Est autem probabile quod maiores Angeli deputentur ad custodiam eorum qui sunt ad maiorem gradum gloriae a Deo electi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0053","text":"[I q. 113 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33152)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0054","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod non omnes Angeli qui mittuntur, habent particularem custodiam super singulos homines; sed quidam ordines habent universalem custodiam, magis vel minus, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0055","text":"[I q. 113 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33153)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0056","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod etiam inferiores Angeli exercent officia superiorum, inquantum aliquid de dono eorum participant, et se habent ad superiores sicut executores virtutis eorum. Et per hunc modum etiam Angeli infimi ordinis possunt et arcere Daemones, et miracula facere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0057","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0058","text":"[I q. 113 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33154)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0059","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod non omnibus hominibus Angeli ad custodiam deputentur. Dicitur enim de Christo, Philipp. II, quod est *in similitudinem hominum factus, et habitu inventus ut homo*. Si igitur omnibus hominibus Angeli ad custodiam deputantur, etiam Christus Angelum custodem habuisset. Sed hoc videtur inconveniens, cum Christus sit maior omnibus Angelis. Non ergo omnibus hominibus Angeli ad custodiam deputantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0060","text":"[I q. 113 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33155)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0061","text":"Praeterea, omnium hominum primus fuit Adam. Sed sibi non competebat habere Angelum custodem, ad minus in statu innocentiae, quia tunc nullis periculis angustiabatur. Ergo Angeli non praeficiuntur ad custodiam omnibus hominibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0062","text":"[I q. 113 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33156)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0063","text":"Praeterea, hominibus Angeli ad custodiam deputantur, ut per eos manuducantur ad vitam aeternam, et incitentur ad bene operandum, et muniantur contra insultus Daemonum. Sed homines praesciti ad damnationem, nunquam perveniunt ad vitam aeternam. Infideles etiam, etsi interdum bona opera faciant, non tamen bene faciunt, quia non recta intentione faciunt, fides enim intentionem dirigit, ut Augustinus dicit. Antichristi etiam adventus erit secundum operationem Satanae, ut dicitur II ad Thessal. II. Non ergo omnibus hominibus Angeli ad custodiam deputantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0064","text":"[I q. 113 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33157)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0065","text":"Sed contra est auctoritas Hieronymi supra inducta, qui dicit quod *unaquaeque anima ad sui custodiam habet Angelum deputatum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0066","text":"[I q. 113 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33158)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0067","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod homo in statu vitae istius constitutus, est quasi in quadam via, qua debet tendere ad patriam. In qua quidem via multa pericula homini imminent, tum ab interiori, tum ab exteriori; secundum illud Psalmi CXLI, *in via hac qua ambulabam, absconderunt laqueum mihi*. Et ideo sicut hominibus per viam non tutam ambulantibus dantur custodes, ita et cuilibet homini, quandiu viator est, custos Angelus deputatur. Quando autem iam ad terminum viae pervenerit, iam non habebit Angelum custodem; sed habebit in regno Angelum conregnantem, in Inferno Daemonem punientem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0068","text":"[I q. 113 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33159)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0069","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Christus, secundum quod homo, immediate regulabatur a verbo Dei, unde non indigebat custodia Angelorum. Et iterum secundum animam erat comprehensor; sed ratione passibilitatis corporis, erat viator. Et secundum hoc, non debebatur ei Angelus custos, tanquam superior; sed Angelus minister, tanquam inferior. Unde dicitur Matth. IV, quod *accesserunt Angeli et ministrabant ei*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0070","text":"[I q. 113 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33160)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0071","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod homo in statu innocentiae non patiebatur aliquod periculum ab interiori, quia interius erant omnia ordinata, ut supra dictum est, sed imminebat ei periculum ab exteriori, propter insidias Daemonum; ut rei probavit eventus. Et ideo indigebat custodia Angelorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0072","text":"[I q. 113 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33161)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0073","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut praesciti et infideles, et etiam Antichristus, non privantur interiori auxilio naturalis rationis; ita etiam non privantur exteriori auxilio toti naturae humanae divinitus concesso, scilicet custodia Angelorum. Per quam etsi non iuventur quantum ad hoc quod vitam aeternam bonis operibus mereantur, iuvantur tamen quantum ad hoc, quod ab aliquibus malis retrahuntur, quibus et sibi ipsis et aliis nocere possunt. Nam et ipsi Daemones arcentur per bonos Angelos, ne noceant quantum volunt. Et similiter Antichristus non tantum nocebit, quantum vellet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0074","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0075","text":"[I q. 113 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33162)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0076","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus non deputetur homini ad custodiam a sua nativitate. Angeli enim mittuntur *in ministerium, propter eos qui haereditatem capiunt salutis*, ut apostolus, ad Heb. dicit. Sed homines incipiunt haereditatem capere salutis, quando baptizantur. Ergo Angelus deputatur homini ad custodiam a tempore Baptismi, et non a tempore nativitatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0077","text":"[I q. 113 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33163)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0078","text":"Praeterea, homines ab Angelis custodiuntur, inquantum ab eis illuminantur per modum doctrinae. Sed pueri mox nati non sunt capaces doctrinae, quia non habent usum rationis. Ergo pueris mox natis non deputantur Angeli custodes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0079","text":"[I q. 113 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33164)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0080","text":"Praeterea, pueri in materno utero existentes habent animam rationalem aliquo tempore, sicut et post nativitatem ex utero. Sed cum sunt in materno utero, non deputantur eis Angeli ad custodiam, ut videtur, quia neque etiam ministri Ecclesiae eos sacramentis imbuunt. Non ergo statim a nativitate hominibus Angeli ad custodiam deputantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0081","text":"[I q. 113 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33165)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0082","text":"Sed contra est quod Hieronymus dicit, quod *unaquaeque anima, ab ortu nativitatis, habet in custodiam sui Angelum deputatum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0083","text":"[I q. 113 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33166)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0084","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut Origenes dicit super Matthaeum, super hoc est duplex opinio. Quidam enim dixerunt quod Angelus ad custodiam homini deputatur a tempore Baptismi, alii vero quod a tempore nativitatis. Et hanc opinionem Hieronymus approbat; et rationabiliter. Beneficia enim quae dantur homini divinitus ex eo quod est Christianus, incipiunt a tempore Baptismi; sicut perceptio Eucharistiae, et alia huiusmodi. Sed ea quae providentur homini a Deo, inquantum habet naturam rationalem, ex tunc ei exhibentur, ex quo nascendo talem naturam accipit. Et tale beneficium est custodia Angelorum, ut ex praemissis patet. Unde statim a nativitate habet homo Angelum ad sui custodiam deputatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0085","text":"[I q. 113 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33167)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0086","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Angeli mittuntur in ministerium, efficaciter quidem propter eos solos qui haereditatem capiunt salutis, si consideretur ultimus effectus custodiae, qui est perceptio haereditatis. Nihilominus tamen et aliis ministerium Angelorum non subtrahitur, quamvis in eis hanc efficaciam non habeat, quod perducantur ad salutem. Efficax tamen est circa eos Angelorum ministerium, inquantum a multis malis retrahuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0087","text":"[I q. 113 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33168)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0088","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod officium custodiae ordinatur quidem ad illuminationem doctrinae, sicut ad ultimum et principalem effectum. Nihilominus tamen multos alios effectus habet, qui pueris competunt, scilicet arcere Daemones, et alia nocumenta tam corporalia quam spiritualia prohibere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0089","text":"[I q. 113 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33169)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0090","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod puer quandiu est in materno utero, non totaliter est a matre separatus, sed per quandam colligationem est quodammodo adhuc aliquid eius, sicut et fructus pendens in arbore, est aliquid arboris. Et ideo probabiliter dici potest quod Angelus qui est in custodia matris, custodiat prolem in matris utero existentem. Sed in nativitate, quando separatur a matre, Angelus ei ad custodiam deputatur, ut Hieronymus dicit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0091","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0092","text":"[I q. 113 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33170)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0093","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angelus custos quandoque deserat hominem cuius custodiae deputatur. Dicitur enim Ierem. li, ex persona Angelorum, *curavimus Babylonem, et non est curata, derelinquamus ergo eam*. Et Isaiae V, *auferam sepem eius, et erit in conculcationem*; Glossa, *idest Angelorum custodiam*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0094","text":"[I q. 113 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33171)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0095","text":"Praeterea, principalius custodit Deus quam Angelus. Sed Deus aliquando hominem derelinquit; secundum illud Psalmi XXI, *Deus, Deus meus, respice in me, quare me dereliquisti?* Ergo multo magis Angelus custos hominem derelinquit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0096","text":"[I q. 113 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33172)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0097","text":"Praeterea, sicut dicit Damascenus, *Angeli, cum sunt hic nobiscum, non sunt in caelo*. Sed aliquando sunt in caelo. Ergo aliquando nos derelinquunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0098","text":"[I q. 113 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33173)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0099","text":"Sed contra, Daemones nos semper impugnant; secundum illud I Petri V, *adversarius vester Diabolus tanquam leo rugiens circuit, quaerens quem devoret*. Ergo multo magis boni Angeli semper nos custodiunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0100","text":"[I q. 113 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33174)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0101","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod custodia Angelorum, ut ex supra dictis patet, est quaedam executio divinae providentiae circa homines facta. Manifestum est autem quod nec homo, nec res aliqua, totaliter divinae providentiae subtrahitur, inquantum enim aliquid participat de esse, intantum subditur universali providentiae entium. Sed intantum Deus, secundum ordinem suae providentiae, dicitur hominem derelinquere, inquantum permittit hominem pati aliquem defectum vel poenae vel culpae. Similiter etiam dicendum est quod Angelus custos nunquam totaliter dimittit hominem, sed ad aliquid interdum eum dimittit; prout scilicet non impedit quin subdatur alicui tribulationi, vel etiam quin cadat in peccatum, secundum ordinem divinorum iudiciorum. Et secundum hoc Babylon et domus Israel ab Angelis derelictae dicuntur, quia Angeli earum custodes non impediverunt quin tribulationibus subderentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0102","text":"[I q. 113 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33175)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0103","text":"Et per hoc patet solutio ad primum et secundum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0104","text":"[I q. 113 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33176)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0105","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Angelus, etsi interdum derelinquat hominem loco, non tamen derelinquit eum quantum ad effectum custodiae, quia etiam cum est in caelo, cognoscit quid circa hominem agatur; nec indiget mora temporis ad motum localem, sed statim potest adesse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0106","text":"## Articulus 7","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0107","text":"[I q. 113 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33177)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0108","text":"Ad septimum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Angeli doleant de malis eorum quos custodiunt. Dicitur enim Isaiae XXXIII, *Angeli pacis amare flebunt*. Sed fletus est signum doloris et tristitiae. Ergo Angeli tristantur de malis hominum quos custodiunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0109","text":"[I q. 113 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33178)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0110","text":"Praeterea, tristitia est, ut Augustinus dicit, *de his quae nobis nolentibus accidunt*. Sed perditio hominis custoditi est contra voluntatem Angeli custodis. Ergo tristantur Angeli de perditione hominum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0111","text":"[I q. 113 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33179)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0112","text":"Praeterea, sicut gaudio contrariatur tristitia, ita poenitentiae contrariatur peccatum. Sed Angeli gaudent de peccatore poenitentiam agente, ut habetur Lucae XV. Ergo tristantur de iusto in peccatum cadente.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0113","text":"[I q. 113 a. 7 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33180)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0114","text":"Praeterea, super illud Num. XVIII; quidquid offerunt primitiarum etc., dicit Glossa Origenis; *trahuntur Angeli in iudicium, utrum ex ipsorum negligentia, an hominum ignavia lapsi sint*. Sed quilibet rationabiliter dolet de malis propter quae in iudicium tractus est. Ergo Angeli dolent de peccatis hominum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0115","text":"[I q. 113 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33181)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0116","text":"Sed contra, ubi est tristitia et dolor, non est perfecta felicitas, unde dicitur Apoc. XXI, *mors ultra non erit, neque luctus, neque clamor, neque ullus dolor*. Sed Angeli sunt perfecte beati. Ergo de nullo dolent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0117","text":"[I q. 113 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33182)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0118","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod Angeli non dolent neque de peccatis, neque de poenis hominum. Tristitia enim et dolor, secundum Augustinum, non est nisi de his quae contrariantur voluntati. Nihil autem accidit in mundo quod sit contrarium voluntati Angelorum et aliorum beatorum, quia voluntas eorum totaliter inhaeret ordini divinae iustitiae; nihil autem fit in mundo, nisi quod per divinam iustitiam fit aut permittitur. Et ideo, simpliciter loquendo, nihil fit in mundo contra voluntatem beatorum. Ut enim philosophus dicit in III Ethic. illud dicitur simpliciter voluntarium, quod aliquis vult in particulari, secundum quod agitur, consideratis scilicet omnibus quae circumstant, quamvis in universali consideratum non esset voluntarium, sicut nauta non vult proiectionem mercium in mare, absolute et universaliter considerando, sed imminente periculo salutis hoc vult.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0119","text":"Unde magis est hoc voluntarium quam involuntarium, ut ibidem dicitur. Sic igitur Angeli peccata et poenas hominum, universaliter et absolute loquendo, non volunt, volunt tamen quod circa hoc ordo divinae iustitiae servetur, secundum quem quidam poenis subduntur, et peccare permittuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0120","text":"[I q. 113 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33183)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0121","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod verbum illud Isaiae potest intelligi de Angelis, idest nuntiis, Ezechiae, qui fleverunt propter verba Rabsacis; de quibus habetur Isaiae XXXVII. Et hoc secundum litteralem sensum. Secundum vero allegoricum, Angeli pacis sunt apostoli et alii praedicatores, qui flent pro peccatis hominum. Si vero secundum sensum anagogicum exponatur de Angelis beatis, tunc metaphorica erit locutio, ad designandum quod Angeli volunt in universali hominum salutem. Sic enim Deo et Angelis huiusmodi passiones attribuuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0122","text":"[I q. 113 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33184)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0123","text":"Ad secundum patet solutio per ea quae dicta sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0124","text":"[I q. 113 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33185)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0125","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod tam in poenitentia hominum, quam in peccato, manet una ratio gaudii Angelis, scilicet impletio ordinis divinae providentiae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0126","text":"[I q. 113 a. 7 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33186)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0127","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod Angeli ducuntur in iudicium pro peccatis hominum, non quasi rei, sed quasi testes, ad convincendum homines de eorum ignavia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0128","text":"## Articulus 8","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0129","text":"[I q. 113 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33187)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0130","text":"Ad octavum sic proceditur. Videtur quod inter Angelos non possit esse pugna seu discordia. Dicitur enim Iob XXV, *qui facit concordiam in sublimibus*. Sed pugna opponitur concordiae. Ergo in sublimibus Angelis non est pugna.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0131","text":"[I q. 113 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33188)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0132","text":"Praeterea, ubi est perfecta caritas et iusta praelatio, non potest esse pugna. Sed hoc totum est in Angelis. Ergo in Angelis non est pugna.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0133","text":"[I q. 113 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33189)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0134","text":"Praeterea, si Angeli dicuntur pugnare pro eis quos custodiunt, necesse est quod unus Angelus foveat unam partem, et alius aliam. Sed si una pars habet iustitiam, e contra alia pars habet iniustitiam. Ergo sequitur quod Angelus bonus sit fautor iniustitiae, quod est inconveniens. Ergo inter bonos Angelos non est pugna.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0135","text":"[I q. 113 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33190)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0136","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Dan. X, ex persona Gabrielis, *princeps regni Persarum restitit mihi viginti et uno diebus*. Hic autem princeps Persarum erat Angelus regno Persarum in custodiam deputatus. Ergo unus bonus Angelus resistit alii, et sic inter eos est pugna.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0137","text":"[I q. 113 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33191)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0138","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ista quaestio movetur occasione horum verborum Danielis. Quae quidem Hieronymus exponit, dicens principem regni Persarum esse Angelum qui se opposuit liberationi populi Israelitici, pro quo Daniel orabat, Gabriele preces eius Deo praesentante. Haec autem resistentia potuit fieri, quia princeps aliquis Daemonum Iudaeos in Persidem ductos ad peccatum induxerat, per quod impedimentum praestabatur orationi Danielis, pro eodem populo deprecantis. Sed secundum Gregorium, XVII Moral., princeps regni Persarum bonus Angelus fuit, custodiae regni illius deputatus. Ad videndum igitur qualiter unus Angelus alteri resistere dicitur, considerandum est quod divina iudicia circa diversa regna et diversos homines, per Angelos exercentur. In suis autem actionibus Angeli secundum divinam sententiam regulantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0139","text":"Contingit autem quandoque quod in diversis regnis, vel in diversis hominibus, contraria merita vel demerita inveniuntur, ut unus alteri subdatur aut praesit. Quid autem super hoc ordo divinae sapientiae habeat, cognoscere non possunt nisi Deo revelante, unde necesse habent super his sapientiam Dei consulere. Sic igitur inquantum de contrariis meritis et sibi repugnantibus, divinam consulunt voluntatem, resistere sibi invicem dicuntur, non quia sint eorum contrariae voluntates, cum in hoc omnes concordent, quod Dei sententia impleatur; sed quia ea de quibus consulunt, sunt repugnantia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0140","text":"[I q. 113 a. 8 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33192)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0141","text":"Et per hoc patet solutio ad obiecta.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"200e1912d3c14ce9c034108c8b341ebe3d41a90eef3e544226d1975182971c0b","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T154656207229Z_e074b7ee","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 113","source_sha256":"200e1912d3c14ce9c034108c8b341ebe3d41a90eef3e544226d1975182971c0b","sha256":"f24d41f03d216a00c6b3e64e18c0317cd2dbbd3fc3df6bbf4d527374dbbbeaba","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:46:56.207248+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0002","text":"[I q. 113 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33129)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the guardianship of the good Angels and the assault of the evil ones. And concerning the first, eight questions are asked. First, whether human beings are guarded by Angels. Second, whether individual Angels are assigned to guard individual human beings. Third, whether guardianship belongs only to the lowest order of Angels. Fourth, whether it is fitting for every human being to have a guardian Angel. Fifth, when an Angel's guardianship of a human being begins. Sixth, whether an Angel always guards a human being. Seventh, whether he grieves over the loss of the one guarded. Eighth, whether there is conflict among Angels on account of guardianship.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0005","text":"[I q. 113 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33130)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that human beings are not guarded by Angels. For guardians are assigned to some either because they do not know how, or because they cannot guard themselves, as in the case of children and the infirm. But a human being can guard himself by free choice; and he knows by the natural knowledge of the natural law. Therefore a human being is not guarded by an Angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0007","text":"[I q. 113 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33131)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0008","text":"Further, where a stronger guardianship is present, a weaker one seems superfluous. But human beings are guarded by God, according to that saying of Psalm 120, *He who guards Israel will neither slumber nor sleep*. Therefore it is not necessary that a human being be guarded by an Angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0009","text":"[I q. 113 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33132)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0010","text":"Further, the loss of one guarded redounds to the negligence of the guardian; hence it is said to someone, in 3 Kings 20, *Guard this man; if he should escape, your life shall be for his life*. But many human beings perish daily, falling into sin, though Angels could assist them either by appearing visibly, or by performing miracles, or in some similar manner. Therefore Angels would be negligent if human beings were entrusted to their guardianship, which is clearly false. Therefore Angels are not the guardians of human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0011","text":"[I q. 113 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33133)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0012","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in the Psalm, *He gave his Angels charge concerning you, that they might guard you in all your ways*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0013","text":"[I q. 113 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33134)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0014","text":"I answer that it must be said that, according to the order of divine providence, this is found in all things: that movable and variable things are moved and regulated by immovable and invariable things; just as all bodily things are moved by immovable spiritual substances, and lower bodies by higher bodies, which are invariable according to substance. But we ourselves also are regulated, with regard to conclusions about which we can hold opinions in different ways, by principles that we hold in an invariable manner. Now it is manifest that in matters of action a human being's knowledge and affection can be varied in many ways and can fail from the good. And therefore it was necessary that Angels be assigned to human beings for their guardianship, through whom they might be regulated and moved toward the good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0015","text":"[I q. 113 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33135)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0016","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that by free choice a human being can avoid evil to some extent, but not sufficiently, because he is weakened in his affection for the good by the many passions of the soul. Likewise, the universal knowledge of the natural law, which is naturally present in a human being, directs him toward the good to some extent, but not sufficiently, because in applying the universal principles of law to particular actions, a human being can fail in many ways. Hence Wisdom 9 says, *The thoughts of mortals are timid, and our providences uncertain*. And therefore the guardianship of Angels was necessary for a human being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0017","text":"[I q. 113 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33136)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0018","text":"To the second, it must be said that two things are required for acting well. First, that affection be inclined toward the good; and this takes place in us through the habit of moral virtue. Second, that reason discover fitting ways for accomplishing the good of virtue; and this the Philosopher attributes to prudence. Therefore, as regards the first, God immediately guards a human being, by infusing grace and virtues into him. But as regards the second, God guards a human being as the universal instructor, whose instruction reaches a human being through the mediation of Angels, as was established above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0019","text":"[I q. 113 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33137)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0020","text":"To the third, it must be said that, just as human beings depart from the natural inclination toward the good on account of the passion of sin, so too do they depart from the prompting of good Angels, which takes place invisibly through their enlightening human beings to act well. Hence the fact that human beings perish is not to be imputed to the negligence of Angels, but to the malice of human beings. But the fact that Angels sometimes appear visibly to human beings, beyond the common law, is from the special grace of God, just as miracles also occur beyond the order of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0021","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0022","text":"[I q. 113 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33138)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0023","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that individual human beings are not guarded by individual Angels. For an Angel is more powerful than a human being. But one human being suffices for the guardianship of many human beings. Therefore, much more can one Angel guard many human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0024","text":"[I q. 113 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33139)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0025","text":"Moreover, lower things are reduced to God by higher things through intermediaries, as Dionysius says. But since all Angels are unequal, as was stated above, there is only one Angel between whom and human beings there is no intermediary. Therefore, only one Angel immediately guards human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0026","text":"[I q. 113 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33140)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0027","text":"Moreover, greater Angels are assigned to greater offices. But it is not a greater office to guard one human being than another, since all human beings are equal by nature. Therefore, since among all Angels one is greater than another, according to Dionysius, it seems that different human beings are not guarded by different Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0028","text":"[I q. 113 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33141)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0029","text":"But on the contrary, Jerome, expounding that passage of Matthew XVIII, *Their Angels in heaven*, says: *Great is the dignity of souls, such that each one has, from the birth of its nativity, an Angel assigned for its guardianship*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0030","text":"[I q. 113 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33142)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0031","text":"I answer that it must be said that individual Angels are assigned to the guardianship of individual human beings. The reason for this is that the guardianship of Angels is a certain execution of divine providence concerning human beings. But the providence of God is related differently to human beings and to other corruptible creatures, because they are related differently to incorruptibility. For human beings are incorruptible not only as regards the common species, but also as regards the proper forms of individuals, which are rational souls; and this cannot be said of other corruptible things. Now it is manifest that the providence of God is principally concerned with those things which remain perpetually; but concerning things which pass away, God's providence consists in ordering them to perpetual things. Thus, therefore, God's providence is related to individual human beings as it is related to individual genera or species of corruptible things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0032","text":"But according to Gregory, different orders are assigned to different kinds of things: for example, the Powers to repelling demons, the Virtues to working miracles in bodily things. And it is probable that different Angels of the same order are set over different species of things. Hence it is also reasonable that different Angels be assigned to the guardianship of different human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0033","text":"[I q. 113 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33143)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0034","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that a guardian is assigned to a human being in two ways. In one way, insofar as he is a particular human being; and thus one guardian is due to one human being, and sometimes several are assigned to the guardianship of one. In another way, insofar as he is part of some community; and thus one human being is set over the guardianship of the whole community, and it pertains to him to provide for those things which concern one human being in relation to the whole community; such as those things done outwardly, by which others are edified or scandalized. But the guardianship of Angels is assigned to human beings also with respect to invisible and hidden things, which pertain to the salvation of individuals in themselves. Hence individual Angels are assigned to the guardianship of individual human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0035","text":"[I q. 113 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33144)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0036","text":"To the second, it must be said that, as has been said, all the Angels of the first hierarchy are immediately illuminated by God concerning certain things; but there are certain things concerning which only the higher ones are immediately illuminated by God, which they reveal to the lower ones. And the same must also be considered in the lower orders: for some lowest Angel is illuminated concerning certain things by some highest Angel, and concerning certain things by the one who is immediately set over him. Thus it is also possible that some Angel immediately illuminates a human being, although he has some Angels under himself, whom he illuminates.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0037","text":"[I q. 113 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33145)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0038","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although human beings are equal by nature, nevertheless inequality is found among them, insofar as by divine providence some are ordered to greater things and some to lesser things, according to what is said in Ecclesiasticus XXXIII: *In the multitude of the Lord's discipline he separated them; of them he blessed and exalted, of them he cursed and humbled.* And thus it is a greater office to guard one human being than another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0039","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0040","text":"[I q. 113 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33146)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0041","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that guarding human beings does not belong only to the lowest order of Angels. For Chrysostom says that what is said in Matthew XVIII, *Their Angels in heaven, etc.*, is understood not of just any Angels, but of the preeminent ones. Therefore, preeminent Angels guard human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0042","text":"[I q. 113 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33147)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0043","text":"Moreover, the Apostle, in Hebrews I, says that *Angels are sent in ministry for the sake of those who shall receive the inheritance of salvation*, and thus it seems that the sending of Angels is ordered to the guardianship of human beings. But five orders are sent into external ministry, as was said above. Therefore, all the Angels of the five orders are assigned to the guardianship of human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0044","text":"[I q. 113 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33148)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0045","text":"Moreover, for the guardianship of human beings it seems especially necessary to repel demons, which pertains to the Powers, according to Gregory; and to perform miracles, which pertains to the Virtues. Therefore, those orders also are assigned to guardianship, and not only the lowest.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0046","text":"[I q. 113 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33149)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0047","text":"But on the contrary, in the Psalm the guardianship of human beings is attributed to the Angels, whose order is the lowest, according to Dionysius.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0048","text":"[I q. 113 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33150)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0049","text":"I answer that it must be said that, as was stated above, guardianship is assigned to man in two ways. In one way, a particular guardianship, according as individual Angels are assigned to the guardianship of individual human beings. And such guardianship belongs to the lowest order of Angels, whose office, according to Gregory, is to announce the least things; but this seems to be the least among the offices of the Angels: to procure those things which pertain only to the salvation of one man. The other is a universal guardianship. And this is multiplied according to the diverse orders, for the more universal an agent is, the higher it is. Thus, therefore, the guardianship of the human multitude belongs to the order of Principalities, or perhaps to the Archangels, who are called prince Angels; whence also Michael, whom we call an Archangel, is called one of the princes in Dan. X. Further, the Virtues have guardianship over all corporeal natures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0050","text":"And further, the Powers also have guardianship over the Demons. And further, the Principalities have guardianship over the good spirits, according to Gregory.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0051","text":"[I q. 113 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33151)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0052","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the saying of Chrysostom can be understood as speaking of those highest in the lowest order of Angels, because, as Dionysius says, in every order there are first, middle, and last. Now it is probable that the greater Angels are assigned to the guardianship of those who are chosen by God for a greater degree of glory.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0053","text":"[I q. 113 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33152)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0054","text":"To the second it must be said that not all Angels who are sent have particular guardianship over individual human beings; but certain orders have universal guardianship, more or less, as has been stated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0055","text":"[I q. 113 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33153)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0056","text":"To the third it must be said that even the lower Angels exercise the offices of the higher, insofar as they participate in something of their gift, and they stand in relation to the higher as executors of their power. And in this way even the Angels of the lowest order can both repel Demons and work miracles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0057","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0058","text":"[I q. 113 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33154)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0059","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth article. It seems that Angels are not assigned to the guardianship of all human beings. For it is said of Christ, Phil. II, that he was *made in the likeness of men, and found in habit as a man*. Therefore, if Angels are assigned to the guardianship of all human beings, Christ too would have had a guardian Angel. But this seems unfitting, since Christ is greater than all Angels. Therefore, Angels are not assigned to the guardianship of all human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0060","text":"[I q. 113 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33155)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0061","text":"Further, the first of all human beings was Adam. But it did not belong to him to have a guardian Angel, at least in the state of innocence, because then he was afflicted by no dangers. Therefore, Angels are not appointed to the guardianship of all human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0062","text":"[I q. 113 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33156)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0063","text":"Further, Angels are assigned to the guardianship of human beings so that through them they may be led by the hand to eternal life, and may be incited to work well, and may be defended against the assaults of Demons. But human beings foreknown unto damnation never attain eternal life. Likewise, unbelievers, even if they sometimes perform good works, nevertheless do not act well, because they do not act with a right intention; for faith directs intention, as Augustine says. Moreover, the coming of Antichrist will be according to the working of Satan, as is said in II Thess. II. Therefore, Angels are not assigned to the guardianship of all human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0064","text":"[I q. 113 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33157)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0065","text":"But on the contrary is the authority of Jerome cited above, who says that *each soul has an Angel assigned for its guardianship*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0066","text":"[I q. 113 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33158)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0067","text":"I answer that it must be said that a man established in the state of this life is as it were on a certain road, by which he ought to tend toward the homeland. On this road, indeed, many dangers threaten man, both from within and from without; according to that saying of Psalm CXLI: *In this way in which I walked, they hid a snare for me*. And therefore, just as guards are given to human beings walking along an unsafe road, so also to each human being, as long as he is a wayfarer, a guardian Angel is assigned. But when he has already arrived at the end of the road, he will no longer have a guardian Angel; rather, in the kingdom he will have an Angel reigning with him, and in Hell a Demon punishing him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0068","text":"[I q. 113 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33159)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0069","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Christ, insofar as he was man, was immediately ruled by the Word of God; hence he did not need the guardianship of Angels. And again, according to his soul he was one who had attained; but by reason of the passibility of his body, he was a wayfarer. And according to this, a guardian Angel was not due to him as a superior, but an Angel ministering to him as an inferior. Hence it is said in Matt. IV that *the Angels came and ministered to him*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0070","text":"[I q. 113 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33160)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0071","text":"To the second it must be said that man in the state of innocence suffered no danger from within, because all things within were ordered, as was stated above; but danger threatened him from without, on account of the snares of the Demons, as the outcome of the matter proved. And therefore he needed the guardianship of Angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0072","text":"[I q. 113 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33161)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0073","text":"To the third it must be said that, just as the foreknown and the unbelievers, and also Antichrist, are not deprived of the interior aid of natural reason, so neither are they deprived of the exterior aid divinely bestowed upon the whole of human nature, namely, the guardianship of the Angels. Although through this they are not helped so far as to merit eternal life by good works, they are nevertheless helped so far as to be drawn back from certain evils by which they can harm both themselves and others. For even the demons themselves are restrained by the good Angels, lest they harm as much as they wish. And likewise Antichrist will not harm as much as he would wish.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0074","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0075","text":"[I q. 113 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33162)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0076","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth. It seems that an Angel is not assigned to a human being for guardianship from his birth. For Angels are sent *in ministry, for those who shall receive the inheritance of salvation*, as the Apostle says in Hebrews. But human beings begin to receive the inheritance of salvation when they are baptized. Therefore, an Angel is assigned to a human being for guardianship from the time of Baptism, and not from the time of birth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0077","text":"[I q. 113 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33163)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0078","text":"Further, human beings are guarded by Angels insofar as they are enlightened by them in the manner of teaching. But children newly born are not capable of teaching, because they do not have the use of reason. Therefore, guardian Angels are not assigned to children newly born.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0079","text":"[I q. 113 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33164)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0080","text":"Further, children existing in the maternal womb have a rational soul for some time, just as they do after birth from the womb. But while they are in the maternal womb, Angels are not assigned to them for guardianship, as it seems, because neither do the ministers of the Church imbue them with the sacraments. Therefore, Angels are not assigned to human beings for guardianship immediately from birth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0081","text":"[I q. 113 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33165)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0082","text":"But to the contrary, Jerome says that *each soul, from the birth of its nativity, has an Angel assigned for its guardianship*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0083","text":"[I q. 113 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33166)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0084","text":"I answer that, as Origen says on Matthew, there is a twofold opinion concerning this. For some have said that an Angel is assigned to a human being for guardianship from the time of Baptism, but others, from the time of birth. And Jerome approves this latter opinion, and reasonably so. For the benefits that are divinely given to a human being from the fact that he is a Christian begin from the time of Baptism, such as reception of the Eucharist and other things of this kind. But those things which are provided by God for a human being insofar as he has a rational nature are bestowed upon him from the time when, by being born, he receives such a nature. And the guardianship of Angels is such a benefit, as is clear from what has been said. Hence, immediately from birth a human being has an Angel assigned for his guardianship.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0085","text":"[I q. 113 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33167)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0086","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Angels are sent in ministry effectively indeed only for those who receive the inheritance of salvation, if the ultimate effect of guardianship is considered, which is the reception of the inheritance. Nevertheless, the ministry of Angels is not withdrawn even from others, although it does not have in them this efficacy, that they be brought to salvation. Yet the ministry of Angels is effective with respect to them insofar as they are drawn back from many evils.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0087","text":"[I q. 113 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33168)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0088","text":"To the second it must be said that the office of guardianship is indeed ordered to the enlightenment of teaching as to its ultimate and principal effect. Nevertheless, it has many other effects that befit children, namely, to ward off demons and to prevent other harms, both bodily and spiritual.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0089","text":"[I q. 113 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33169)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0090","text":"To the third it must be said that, as long as a child is in the maternal womb, he is not wholly separated from the mother, but through a certain connection he is still in some way something of her, just as fruit hanging on a tree is something of the tree. And therefore it can probably be said that the Angel who is in the mother's guardianship guards the offspring existing in the mother's womb. But at birth, when he is separated from the mother, an Angel is assigned to him for guardianship, as Jerome says.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0091","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0092","text":"[I q. 113 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33170)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0093","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth. It seems that a guardian Angel sometimes abandons the human being to whose guardianship he is assigned. For it is said in Jeremiah 51, from the person of the Angels: *We have treated Babylon, and she has not been healed; therefore let us abandon her.* And in Isaiah 5: *I will remove her hedge, and she shall be trampled down*; the Gloss says, *that is, the guardianship of Angels*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0094","text":"[I q. 113 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33171)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0095","text":"Further, God guards more principally than an Angel. But God sometimes abandons a human being, according to that of Psalm 21: *God, my God, look upon me: why have you abandoned me?* Therefore, much more does a guardian Angel abandon a human being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0096","text":"[I q. 113 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33172)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0097","text":"Moreover, as Damascene says, *when Angels are here with us, they are not in heaven*. But sometimes they are in heaven. Therefore, sometimes they abandon us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0098","text":"[I q. 113 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33173)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0099","text":"On the contrary, the demons always assail us; according to I Peter V, *your adversary the Devil, like a roaring lion, goes about, seeking whom he may devour*. Therefore, much more do the good Angels always guard us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0100","text":"[I q. 113 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33174)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0101","text":"I answer that it must be said that the guardianship of the Angels, as is clear from what has been said above, is a certain execution of divine providence exercised concerning human beings. Now it is manifest that neither man nor any thing is altogether withdrawn from divine providence; for insofar as something participates in being, to that extent it is subject to the universal providence of beings. But God is said, according to the order of His providence, to abandon a man insofar as He permits the man to suffer some defect either of punishment or of fault. Likewise, it must also be said that the guardian Angel never altogether leaves a man, but sometimes leaves him as to something; namely, insofar as he does not prevent him from being subjected to some tribulation, or even from falling into sin, according to the order of divine judgments. And in this respect Babylon and the house of Israel are said to have been abandoned by the Angels, because their guardian Angels did not prevent them from being subjected to tribulations.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0102","text":"[I q. 113 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33175)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0103","text":"And through this the solution to the first and second is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0104","text":"[I q. 113 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33176)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0105","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although an Angel sometimes leaves a man as to place, he nevertheless does not leave him as to the effect of guardianship, because even when he is in heaven, he knows what is taking place concerning the man; nor does he need a lapse of time for local motion, but can be present immediately.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0106","text":"## Article 7","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0107","text":"[I q. 113 a. 7 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33177)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0108","text":"We proceed thus to the seventh. It seems that Angels grieve over the evils of those whom they guard. For it is said in Isaiah XXXIII, *the Angels of peace will weep bitterly*. But weeping is a sign of pain and sorrow. Therefore, Angels are saddened by the evils of the human beings whom they guard.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0109","text":"[I q. 113 a. 7 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33178)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0110","text":"Moreover, sorrow is, as Augustine says, *concerning those things which happen to us against our will*. But the perdition of the man guarded is contrary to the will of the guardian Angel. Therefore, Angels are saddened by the perdition of human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0111","text":"[I q. 113 a. 7 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33179)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0112","text":"Moreover, just as sorrow is contrary to joy, so sin is contrary to repentance. But Angels rejoice over a sinner doing penance, as is found in Luke XV. Therefore, they are saddened by a just man falling into sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0113","text":"[I q. 113 a. 7 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33180)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0114","text":"Moreover, on that passage, Num. XVIII, “whatever they offer of the firstfruits,” etc., Origen's Gloss says: *Angels are brought to judgment, whether those persons have fallen through their negligence or through the sloth of human beings*. But everyone reasonably grieves over evils on account of which he is brought to judgment. Therefore, Angels grieve over human sins.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0115","text":"[I q. 113 a. 7 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33181)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0116","text":"On the contrary, where there are sorrow and pain, there is not perfect happiness; hence it is said in Apocalypse XXI, *there shall be no more death, neither mourning, nor crying, nor any pain*. But Angels are perfectly blessed. Therefore, they grieve over nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0117","text":"[I q. 113 a. 7 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33182)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0118","text":"I answer that it must be said that Angels do not grieve either over the sins or over the punishments of human beings. For sorrow and pain, according to Augustine, concern only those things that are contrary to the will. But nothing happens in the world that is contrary to the will of Angels and of the other blessed, because their will wholly adheres to the order of divine justice; and nothing takes place in the world except what is done or permitted through divine justice. And therefore, speaking simply, nothing takes place in the world against the will of the blessed. For, as the Philosopher says in Ethics III, that is called voluntary simply which someone wills in the particular, according as it is being done, with all the surrounding circumstances considered, although considered universally it would not be voluntary; just as a sailor does not will the throwing of merchandise into the sea, considering it absolutely and universally, but, when danger to safety threatens, wills this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0119","text":"Hence this is more voluntary than involuntary, as is said in the same place. Thus Angels do not will the sins and punishments of human beings, speaking universally and absolutely; nevertheless, they will that the order of divine justice be preserved concerning this, according to which some are subjected to punishments and are permitted to sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0120","text":"[I q. 113 a. 7 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33183)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0121","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that that word of Isaiah can be understood of the angels, that is, the messengers, of Hezekiah, who wept on account of the words of Rabshakeh; concerning whom there is an account in Isaiah XXXVII. And this is according to the literal sense. But according to the allegorical sense, the angels of peace are the apostles and other preachers, who weep for the sins of men. But if it is expounded according to the anagogical sense concerning the blessed angels, then the expression will be metaphorical, to signify that the angels will universally the salvation of men. For in this way such passions are attributed to God and to the angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0122","text":"[I q. 113 a. 7 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33184)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0123","text":"To the second, the solution is clear from what has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0124","text":"[I q. 113 a. 7 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33185)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0125","text":"To the third, it must be said that, both in the repentance of men and in sin, one ground of joy remains for the angels, namely, the fulfillment of the order of divine providence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0126","text":"[I q. 113 a. 7 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33186)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0127","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that angels are brought into judgment on account of the sins of men, not as defendants, but as witnesses, to convict men of their negligence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0128","text":"## Article 8","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0129","text":"[I q. 113 a. 8 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33187)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0130","text":"We proceed thus to the eighth. It seems that there cannot be battle or discord among the angels. For it is said in Job XXV, *who makes concord in the heights*. But battle is opposed to concord. Therefore, among the angels on high there is no battle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0131","text":"[I q. 113 a. 8 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33188)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0132","text":"Further, where there is perfect charity and just prelation, there cannot be battle. But all this is in the angels. Therefore, there is no battle among the angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0133","text":"[I q. 113 a. 8 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33189)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0134","text":"Further, if angels are said to fight for those whom they guard, it is necessary that one angel favor one side and another another. But if one side has justice, then, on the contrary, the other side has injustice. Therefore it follows that a good angel is a supporter of injustice, which is unfitting. Therefore there is no battle among the good angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0135","text":"[I q. 113 a. 8 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33190)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0136","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Dan. X, in the person of Gabriel, *the prince of the kingdom of the Persians resisted me for twenty-one days*. But this prince of the Persians was an angel assigned to the guardianship of the kingdom of the Persians. Therefore one good angel resists another, and thus there is battle among them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0137","text":"[I q. 113 a. 8 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33191)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0138","text":"I answer that this question is raised on the occasion of these words of Daniel. Jerome indeed expounds them by saying that the prince of the kingdom of the Persians was an angel who opposed the liberation of the Israelite people, for whom Daniel was praying, Gabriel presenting his prayers to God. But this resistance could have occurred because some prince of the demons had led into sin the Jews who had been taken into Persia, through which an impediment was furnished to the prayer of Daniel, who was praying for that same people. But according to Gregory, *Morals*, XVII, the prince of the kingdom of the Persians was a good angel, assigned to the guardianship of that kingdom. Therefore, to see how one angel is said to resist another, one must consider that divine judgments concerning diverse kingdoms and diverse men are carried out through angels. But in their actions angels are regulated according to the divine sentence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0139","text":"Now it sometimes happens that in different kingdoms, or in different men, contrary merits or demerits are found, such that one is subjected to another or set over another. But what order divine wisdom has concerning this, they cannot know unless it is revealed by God; hence they must consult the wisdom of God concerning these matters. Thus, therefore, insofar as they consult the divine will concerning contrary and mutually conflicting merits, they are said to resist one another, not because their wills are contrary—for in this all agree, that the sentence of God be fulfilled—but because the matters concerning which they consult are conflicting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0140","text":"[I q. 113 a. 8 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33192)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__105:P0141","text":"And through this the solution to the objections is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__105:P0141"]}],"source_digest":"1e1e7bc952b78117f628376982148aa6ab67fc3ed39059bda1c97a8fef9330fe","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 113"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 113","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106","title":"First Part · Question 114","order":115,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC__106","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 114 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33193)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de impugnatione Daemonum. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quinque. Primo, utrum homines a Daemonibus impugnentur. Secundo, utrum tentare sit proprium Diaboli. Tertio, utrum omnia peccata hominum ex impugnatione sive tentatione Daemonum proveniant. Quarto, utrum possint vera miracula facere ad seducendum. Quinto, utrum Daemones qui ab hominibus superantur, ab impugnatione hominum arceantur.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 114 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33194)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homines non impugnentur a Daemonibus. Angeli enim deputantur ad hominum custodiam, missi a Deo. Sed Daemones non mittuntur a Deo, cum Daemonum intentio sit perdere animas, Dei autem salvare. Ergo Daemones non deputantur ad hominum impugnationem.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33195)\n\nPraeterea, non est aequa conditio pugnae, ut infirmus contra fortem, ignarus contra astutum exponatur ad bellum. Sed homines sunt infirmi et ignari; Daemones autem potentes et astuti. Non est ergo permittendum a Deo, qui est omnis iustitiae auctor, ut homines a Daemonibus impugnentur.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33196)\n\nPraeterea, ad exercitium hominum sufficit impugnatio carnis et mundi. Sed Deus permittit electos suos impugnari propter eorum exercitium. Ergo non videtur necessarium quod a Daemonibus impugnentur.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33197)\n\nSed contra est quod apostolus dicit, ad Ephes. VI, quod *non est nobis colluctatio adversus carnem et sanguinem, sed adversus principes et potestates, adversus mundi rectores tenebrarum harum, contra spiritualia nequitiae in caelestibus*.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33198)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa impugnationem Daemonum duo est considerare, scilicet ipsam impugnationem, et impugnationis ordinem. Impugnatio quidem ipsa ex Daemonum malitia procedit, qui propter invidiam profectum hominum impedire nituntur; et propter superbiam divinae potestatis similitudinem usurpant, deputando sibi ministros determinatos ad hominum impugnationem, sicut et Angeli Deo ministrant in determinatis officiis ad hominum salutem. Sed ordo impugnationis ipsius est a Deo, qui ordinate novit malis uti, ad bona ea ordinando. Sed ex parte Angelorum, tam ipsa custodia quam ordo custodiae reducitur ad Deum, sicut ad primum auctorem.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33199)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod mali Angeli impugnant homines dupliciter. Uno modo, instigando ad peccatum. Et sic non mittuntur a Deo ad impugnandum, sed aliquando permittuntur, secundum Dei iusta iudicia. Aliquando autem impugnant homines puniendo. Et sic mittuntur a Deo; sicut missus est spiritus mendax ad puniendum Achab regem Israel, ut dicitur III Reg. ult. Poena enim refertur in Deum, sicut in primum auctorem. Et tamen Daemones ad puniendum missi, alia intentione puniunt, quam mittantur, nam ipsi puniunt ex odio vel invidia; mittuntur autem a Deo propter eius iustitiam.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33200)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod ad hoc quod non sit inaequalis pugnae conditio, fit ex parte hominis recompensatio, principaliter quidem per auxilium divinae gratiae; secundario autem per custodiam Angelorum. Unde IV Reg., Elisaeus dixit ad ministrum suum, *noli timere, plures enim nobiscum sunt, quam cum illis*.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33201)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod infirmitati humanae sufficeret ad exercitium impugnatio quae est a carne et mundo, sed malitiae Daemonum non sufficit, quae utroque utitur ad hominum impugnationem. Sed tamen ex divina ordinatione hoc provenit in gloriam electorum.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 114 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33202)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod tentare non sit proprium Diaboli. Dicitur enim Deus tentare; secundum illud Gen. XXII, *tentavit Deus Abraham*. Tentat etiam caro, et mundus. Et etiam homo dicitur tentare Deum, et hominem. Ergo non est proprium Daemonis tentare.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33203)\n\nPraeterea, tentare est ignorantis. Sed Daemones sciunt quid circa homines agatur. Ergo Daemones non tentant.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33204)\n\nPraeterea, tentatio est via in peccatum. Peccatum autem in voluntate consistit. Cum ergo Daemones non possint voluntatem hominis immutare, ut per supra dicta patet; videtur quod ad eos non pertineat tentare.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33205)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur I ad Thessal. III, *ne forte tentaverit vos is qui tentat*; Glossa, idest Diabolus, cuius officium est tentare.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33206)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod tentare est proprie experimentum sumere de aliquo. Experimentum autem sumitur de aliquo, ut sciatur aliquid circa ipsum, et ideo proximus finis cuiuslibet tentantis est scientia. Sed quandoque ulterius ex scientia quaeritur aliquis alius finis, vel bonus vel malus, bonus quidem, sicut cum aliquis vult scire qualis aliquis sit, vel quantum ad scientiam vel quantum ad virtutem, ut eum promoveat; malus autem, quando hoc scire vult, ut eum decipiat vel subvertat. Et per hunc modum potest accipi quomodo tentare diversis diversimode attribuatur. Homo enim tentare dicitur, quandoque quidem ut sciat tantum, et propter hoc, tentare Deum dicitur esse peccatum; quia homo, quasi incertus, experiri praesumit Dei virtutem. Quandoque vero tentat ut iuvet, quandoque vero, ut noceat. Diabolus autem semper tentat ut noceat, in peccatum praecipitando. Et secundum hoc, dicitur proprium officium eius tentare, nam etsi homo aliquando sic tentet, hoc agit inquantum est minister Diaboli. Deus autem tentare dicitur ut sciat, eo modo loquendi quo dicitur scire quod facit alios scire. Unde dicitur Deut. XII, *tentat vos dominus Deus vester, ut palam fiat utrum diligatis eum*. Caro autem et mundus dicuntur tentare instrumentaliter, seu materialiter, inquantum scilicet potest cognosci qualis sit homo, ex hoc quod sequitur vel repugnat concupiscentiis carnis, et ex hoc quod contemnit prospera mundi et adversa; quibus etiam Diabolus utitur ad tentandum.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33207)\n\nEt sic patet solutio ad primum.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33208)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Daemones sciunt ea quae exterius aguntur circa homines, sed interiorem hominis conditionem solus Deus novit, qui est spirituum ponderator, ex qua aliqui sunt magis proni ad unum vitium quam ad aliud. Et ideo Diabolus tentat explorando interiorem conditionem hominis, ut de illo vitio tentet, ad quod homo magis pronus est.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33209)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod Daemon, etsi non possit immutare voluntatem, potest tamen, ut supra dictum est, aliqualiter immutare inferiores hominis vires; ex quibus etsi non cogitur voluntas, tamen inclinatur.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 114 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33210)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod omnia peccata procedant ex tentatione Diaboli. Dicit enim Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *multitudo Daemonum est causa omnium malorum et sibi et aliis*. Et Damascenus dicit quod *omnis malitia et omnis immunditia a Diabolo excogitatae sunt*.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33211)\n\nPraeterea, de quolibet peccatore dici posset quod dominus de Iudaeis dicit, Ioan. VIII, *vos ex patre Diabolo estis*. Hoc autem est inquantum ipsi ex Diaboli suggestione peccabant. Omne ergo peccatum est ex suggestione Diaboli.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33212)\n\nPraeterea, sicut Angeli deputantur ad custodiam hominum, ita Daemones ad impugnationem. Sed omnia bona quae facimus, ex suggestione bonorum Angelorum procedunt, quia divina ad nos mediantibus Angelis perferuntur. Ergo et omnia mala quae facimus, proveniunt ex suggestione Diaboli.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33213)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in libro de Eccles. Dogmat., *non omnes cogitationes nostrae malae a Diabolo excitantur, sed aliquoties ex nostri arbitrii motu emergunt*.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33214)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod causa alicuius potest dici aliquid dupliciter, uno modo, directe, alio modo, indirecte. Indirecte quidem, sicut cum aliquod agens causans aliquam dispositionem ad aliquem effectum, dicitur esse occasionaliter et indirecte causa illius effectus; sicut si dicatur quod ille qui siccat ligna, est causa combustionis eorum. Et hoc modo dicendum est quod Diabolus est causa omnium peccatorum nostrorum, quia ipse instigavit primum hominem ad peccandum, ex cuius peccato consecuta est in toto genere humano quaedam pronitas ad omnia peccata. Et per hunc modum intelligenda sunt verba Damasceni et Dionysii. Directe autem dicitur esse aliquid causa alicuius, quod operatur directe ad illud. Et hoc modo Diabolus non est causa omnis peccati non enim omnia peccata committuntur Diabolo instigante, sed quaedam ex libertate arbitrii et carnis corruptione. Quia, ut Origenes dicit, etiam si Diabolus non esset, homines haberent appetitum ciborum et venereorum et huiusmodi; circa quae multa inordinatio contingit, nisi per rationem talis appetitus refraenetur; et maxime, supposita corruptione naturae. Refraenare autem et ordinare huiusmodi appetitum, subiacet libero arbitrio. Sic ergo non est necessarium omnia peccata ex instinctu Diaboli provenire. Si qua tamen ex instinctu eius proveniunt, ad ea complenda *eo blandimento decipiuntur homines nunc, quo primi parentes*, ut Isidorus dicit.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33215)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ad primum.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33216)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, si qua peccata absque instinctu Diaboli perpetrantur, per ea tamen fiunt homines filii Diaboli, inquantum ipsum primo peccantem imitantur.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33217)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod homo potest per seipsum ruere in peccatum, sed ad meritum proficere non potest nisi auxilio divino, quod homini exhibetur mediante ministerio Angelorum. Et ideo ad omnia bona nostra cooperantur Angeli, non tamen omnia nostra peccata procedunt ex Daemonum suggestione. Quamvis nullum genus peccati sit, quod non interdum ex Daemonum suggestione proveniat.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 114 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33218)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Daemones non possint homines seducere per aliqua miracula vera. Operatio enim Daemonum maxime vigebit in operibus Antichristi. Sed sicut apostolus dicit II ad Thessal. II, *eius adventus est secundum operationem Satanae, in omni virtute et signis et prodigiis mendacibus*. Ergo multo magis alio tempore per Daemones non fiunt nisi signa mendacia.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33219)\n\nPraeterea, vera miracula per aliquam corporum immutationem fiunt. Sed Daemones non possunt immutare corpus in aliam naturam, dicit enim Augustinus, XVIII de Civ. Dei, *nec corpus quidem humanum ulla ratione crediderim Daemonum arte vel potestate in membra bestialia posse converti*. Ergo Daemones vera miracula facere non possunt.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33220)\n\nPraeterea, argumentum efficaciam non habet, quod se habet ad opposita. Si ergo miracula vera possunt fieri a Daemonibus ad falsitatem persuadendam, non erunt efficacia ad veritatem fidei confirmandam. Quod est inconveniens, cum dicatur Marci ult., *domino cooperante, et sermonem confirmante sequentibus signis*.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33221)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, libro octoginta trium quaest., quod *magicis artibus fiunt miracula plerumque similia illis miraculis quae fiunt per servos Dei*.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33222)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut ex supra dictis patet, si miraculum proprie accipiatur, Daemones miracula facere non possunt, nec aliqua creatura, sed solus Deus, quia miraculum proprie dicitur quod fit praeter ordinem totius naturae creatae, sub quo ordine continetur omnis virtus creaturae. Dicitur tamen quandoque miraculum large, quod excedit humanam facultatem et considerationem. Et sic Daemones possunt facere miracula, quae scilicet homines mirantur, inquantum eorum facultatem et cognitionem excedunt. Nam et unus homo, inquantum facit aliquid quod est supra facultatem et cognitionem alterius, ducit alium in admirationem sui operis, ut quodammodo miraculum videatur operari. Sciendum est tamen quod, quamvis huiusmodi opera Daemonum, quae nobis miracula videntur, ad veram rationem miraculi non pertingant; sunt tamen quandoque verae res. Sicut magi Pharaonis per virtutem Daemonum veros serpentes et ranas fecerunt. *Et quando ignis de caelo cecidit et familiam Iob cum gregibus pecorum uno impetu consumpsit, et turbo domum deiiciens filios eius occidit, quae fuerunt opera Satanae, phantasmata non fuerunt*, ut Augustinus dicit, XX de Civ. Dei.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33223)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit ibidem, Antichristi opera possunt dici esse signa mendacii, *vel quia mortales sensus per phantasmata decepturus est, ut quod non facit, videatur facere, vel quia, si sint vera prodigia, ad mendacium tamen pertrahent credituros*.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33224)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, materia corporalis non obedit Angelis bonis seu malis ad nutum, ut Daemones sua virtute possint transmutare materiam de forma in formam, sed possunt adhibere quaedam semina quae in elementis mundi inveniuntur, ad huiusmodi effectus complendos, ut Augustinus dicit III de Trin. Et ideo dicendum est quod omnes transmutationes corporalium rerum quae possunt fieri per aliquas virtutes naturales, ad quas pertinent praedicta semina, possunt fieri per operationem Daemonum, huiusmodi seminibus adhibitis; sicut cum aliquae res transmutantur in serpentes vel ranas, quae per putrefactionem generari possunt. Illae vero transmutationes corporalium rerum quae non possunt virtute naturae fieri, nullo modo operatione Daemonum, secundum rei veritatem, perfici possunt; sicut quod corpus humanum mutetur in corpus bestiale, aut quod corpus hominis mortuum reviviscat. Et si aliquando aliquid tale operatione Daemonum fieri videatur, hoc non est secundum rei veritatem, sed secundum apparentiam tantum. Quod quidem potest dupliciter contingere. Uno modo, ab interiori; secundum quod Daemon potest mutare phantasiam hominis, et etiam sensus corporeos, ut aliquid videatur aliter quam sit, sicut supra dictum est. Et hoc etiam interdum fieri dicitur virtute aliquarum rerum corporalium. Alio modo, ab exteriori. Cum enim ipse possit formare corpus ex aere cuiuscumque formae et figurae, ut illud assumens in eo visibiliter appareat; potest eadem ratione circumponere cuicumque rei corporeae quamcumque formam corpoream, ut in eius specie videatur. Et hoc est quod Augustinus dicit XVIII de Civ. Dei, quod *phantasticum hominis, quod etiam cogitando sive somniando per rerum innumerabilium genera variatur, velut corporatum in alicuius animalis effigie, sensibus apparet alienis*. Quod non est sic intelligendum, quod ipsa vis phantastica hominis, aut species eius, eadem numero incorporata alterius sensibus ostendatur, sed quia Daemon qui in phantasia unius hominis format aliquam speciem, ipse etiam potest similem speciem alterius sensibus offerre.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33225)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit in libro octoginta trium quaest., *cum talia faciunt magi qualia sancti, diverso fine et diverso iure fiunt. Illi enim faciunt, quaerentes gloriam suam, isti, quaerentes gloriam Dei. Et illi faciunt per quaedam privata commercia; isti autem publica administratione, et iussu Dei, cui cuncta creatura subiecta est*.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 114 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33226)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Daemon qui superatur ab aliquo, non propter hoc ab impugnatione arceatur. Christus enim efficacissime suum tentatorem vicit. Sed tamen postea eum impugnavit, ad occisionem eius Iudaeos incitando. Ergo non est verum quod Diabolus victus ab impugnatione cesset.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33227)\n\nPraeterea, infligere poenam ei qui in pugna succumbit, est incitare ad acrius impugnandum. Hoc autem non pertinet ad Dei misericordiam. Ergo Daemones superati non arcentur.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33228)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur Matth. IV, tunc reliquit eum Diabolus, scilicet Christum superantem.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33229)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam dicunt quod Daemon superatus nullum hominum potest de cetero tentare, nec de eodem nec de alio peccato. Quidam autem dicunt quod potest alios tentare, sed non eundem. Et hoc probabilius dicitur, si tamen intelligatur usque ad aliquod tempus, unde et Lucae IV dicitur quod, *consummata omni tentatione, Diabolus recessit a Christo usque ad tempus*. Et huius ratio est duplex. Una est ex parte divinae clementiae, quia, ut Chrysostomus dicit, super Matth., *non tandiu homines Diabolus tentat, quandiu vult, sed quandiu Deus permittit; quia etsi permittat paulisper tentare, tamen repellit, propter infirmam naturam*. Alia ratio sumitur ex astutia Diaboli, unde Ambrosius dicit, super Lucam, quod *Diabolus instare formidat, quia frequentius refugit triumphari*. Quod tamen aliquando Diabolus redeat ad eum quem dimisit, patet per illud quod dicitur Matth. XII, *revertar in domum meam, unde exivi*.\n\n[I q. 114 a. 5 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33230)\n\nEt per hoc patet solutio ad obiecta.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0002","text":"[I q. 114 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33193)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de impugnatione Daemonum. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quinque. Primo, utrum homines a Daemonibus impugnentur. Secundo, utrum tentare sit proprium Diaboli. Tertio, utrum omnia peccata hominum ex impugnatione sive tentatione Daemonum proveniant. Quarto, utrum possint vera miracula facere ad seducendum. Quinto, utrum Daemones qui ab hominibus superantur, ab impugnatione hominum arceantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0005","text":"[I q. 114 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33194)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homines non impugnentur a Daemonibus. Angeli enim deputantur ad hominum custodiam, missi a Deo. Sed Daemones non mittuntur a Deo, cum Daemonum intentio sit perdere animas, Dei autem salvare. Ergo Daemones non deputantur ad hominum impugnationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0007","text":"[I q. 114 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33195)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0008","text":"Praeterea, non est aequa conditio pugnae, ut infirmus contra fortem, ignarus contra astutum exponatur ad bellum. Sed homines sunt infirmi et ignari; Daemones autem potentes et astuti. Non est ergo permittendum a Deo, qui est omnis iustitiae auctor, ut homines a Daemonibus impugnentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0009","text":"[I q. 114 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33196)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0010","text":"Praeterea, ad exercitium hominum sufficit impugnatio carnis et mundi. Sed Deus permittit electos suos impugnari propter eorum exercitium. Ergo non videtur necessarium quod a Daemonibus impugnentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0011","text":"[I q. 114 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33197)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0012","text":"Sed contra est quod apostolus dicit, ad Ephes. VI, quod *non est nobis colluctatio adversus carnem et sanguinem, sed adversus principes et potestates, adversus mundi rectores tenebrarum harum, contra spiritualia nequitiae in caelestibus*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0013","text":"[I q. 114 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33198)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0014","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa impugnationem Daemonum duo est considerare, scilicet ipsam impugnationem, et impugnationis ordinem. Impugnatio quidem ipsa ex Daemonum malitia procedit, qui propter invidiam profectum hominum impedire nituntur; et propter superbiam divinae potestatis similitudinem usurpant, deputando sibi ministros determinatos ad hominum impugnationem, sicut et Angeli Deo ministrant in determinatis officiis ad hominum salutem. Sed ordo impugnationis ipsius est a Deo, qui ordinate novit malis uti, ad bona ea ordinando. Sed ex parte Angelorum, tam ipsa custodia quam ordo custodiae reducitur ad Deum, sicut ad primum auctorem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0015","text":"[I q. 114 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33199)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0016","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod mali Angeli impugnant homines dupliciter. Uno modo, instigando ad peccatum. Et sic non mittuntur a Deo ad impugnandum, sed aliquando permittuntur, secundum Dei iusta iudicia. Aliquando autem impugnant homines puniendo. Et sic mittuntur a Deo; sicut missus est spiritus mendax ad puniendum Achab regem Israel, ut dicitur III Reg. ult. Poena enim refertur in Deum, sicut in primum auctorem. Et tamen Daemones ad puniendum missi, alia intentione puniunt, quam mittantur, nam ipsi puniunt ex odio vel invidia; mittuntur autem a Deo propter eius iustitiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0017","text":"[I q. 114 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33200)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0018","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod ad hoc quod non sit inaequalis pugnae conditio, fit ex parte hominis recompensatio, principaliter quidem per auxilium divinae gratiae; secundario autem per custodiam Angelorum. Unde IV Reg., Elisaeus dixit ad ministrum suum, *noli timere, plures enim nobiscum sunt, quam cum illis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0019","text":"[I q. 114 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33201)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0020","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod infirmitati humanae sufficeret ad exercitium impugnatio quae est a carne et mundo, sed malitiae Daemonum non sufficit, quae utroque utitur ad hominum impugnationem. Sed tamen ex divina ordinatione hoc provenit in gloriam electorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0021","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0022","text":"[I q. 114 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33202)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0023","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod tentare non sit proprium Diaboli. Dicitur enim Deus tentare; secundum illud Gen. XXII, *tentavit Deus Abraham*. Tentat etiam caro, et mundus. Et etiam homo dicitur tentare Deum, et hominem. Ergo non est proprium Daemonis tentare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0024","text":"[I q. 114 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33203)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0025","text":"Praeterea, tentare est ignorantis. Sed Daemones sciunt quid circa homines agatur. Ergo Daemones non tentant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0026","text":"[I q. 114 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33204)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0027","text":"Praeterea, tentatio est via in peccatum. Peccatum autem in voluntate consistit. Cum ergo Daemones non possint voluntatem hominis immutare, ut per supra dicta patet; videtur quod ad eos non pertineat tentare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0028","text":"[I q. 114 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33205)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0029","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur I ad Thessal. III, *ne forte tentaverit vos is qui tentat*; Glossa, idest Diabolus, cuius officium est tentare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0030","text":"[I q. 114 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33206)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0031","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod tentare est proprie experimentum sumere de aliquo. Experimentum autem sumitur de aliquo, ut sciatur aliquid circa ipsum, et ideo proximus finis cuiuslibet tentantis est scientia. Sed quandoque ulterius ex scientia quaeritur aliquis alius finis, vel bonus vel malus, bonus quidem, sicut cum aliquis vult scire qualis aliquis sit, vel quantum ad scientiam vel quantum ad virtutem, ut eum promoveat; malus autem, quando hoc scire vult, ut eum decipiat vel subvertat. Et per hunc modum potest accipi quomodo tentare diversis diversimode attribuatur. Homo enim tentare dicitur, quandoque quidem ut sciat tantum, et propter hoc, tentare Deum dicitur esse peccatum; quia homo, quasi incertus, experiri praesumit Dei virtutem. Quandoque vero tentat ut iuvet, quandoque vero, ut noceat. Diabolus autem semper tentat ut noceat, in peccatum praecipitando.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0032","text":"Et secundum hoc, dicitur proprium officium eius tentare, nam etsi homo aliquando sic tentet, hoc agit inquantum est minister Diaboli. Deus autem tentare dicitur ut sciat, eo modo loquendi quo dicitur scire quod facit alios scire. Unde dicitur Deut. XII, *tentat vos dominus Deus vester, ut palam fiat utrum diligatis eum*. Caro autem et mundus dicuntur tentare instrumentaliter, seu materialiter, inquantum scilicet potest cognosci qualis sit homo, ex hoc quod sequitur vel repugnat concupiscentiis carnis, et ex hoc quod contemnit prospera mundi et adversa; quibus etiam Diabolus utitur ad tentandum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0033","text":"[I q. 114 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33207)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0034","text":"Et sic patet solutio ad primum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0035","text":"[I q. 114 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33208)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0036","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Daemones sciunt ea quae exterius aguntur circa homines, sed interiorem hominis conditionem solus Deus novit, qui est spirituum ponderator, ex qua aliqui sunt magis proni ad unum vitium quam ad aliud. Et ideo Diabolus tentat explorando interiorem conditionem hominis, ut de illo vitio tentet, ad quod homo magis pronus est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0037","text":"[I q. 114 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33209)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0038","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod Daemon, etsi non possit immutare voluntatem, potest tamen, ut supra dictum est, aliqualiter immutare inferiores hominis vires; ex quibus etsi non cogitur voluntas, tamen inclinatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0039","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0040","text":"[I q. 114 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33210)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0041","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod omnia peccata procedant ex tentatione Diaboli. Dicit enim Dionysius, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *multitudo Daemonum est causa omnium malorum et sibi et aliis*. Et Damascenus dicit quod *omnis malitia et omnis immunditia a Diabolo excogitatae sunt*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0042","text":"[I q. 114 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33211)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0043","text":"Praeterea, de quolibet peccatore dici posset quod dominus de Iudaeis dicit, Ioan. VIII, *vos ex patre Diabolo estis*. Hoc autem est inquantum ipsi ex Diaboli suggestione peccabant. Omne ergo peccatum est ex suggestione Diaboli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0044","text":"[I q. 114 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33212)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0045","text":"Praeterea, sicut Angeli deputantur ad custodiam hominum, ita Daemones ad impugnationem. Sed omnia bona quae facimus, ex suggestione bonorum Angelorum procedunt, quia divina ad nos mediantibus Angelis perferuntur. Ergo et omnia mala quae facimus, proveniunt ex suggestione Diaboli.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0046","text":"[I q. 114 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33213)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0047","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in libro de Eccles. Dogmat., *non omnes cogitationes nostrae malae a Diabolo excitantur, sed aliquoties ex nostri arbitrii motu emergunt*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0048","text":"[I q. 114 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33214)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0049","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod causa alicuius potest dici aliquid dupliciter, uno modo, directe, alio modo, indirecte. Indirecte quidem, sicut cum aliquod agens causans aliquam dispositionem ad aliquem effectum, dicitur esse occasionaliter et indirecte causa illius effectus; sicut si dicatur quod ille qui siccat ligna, est causa combustionis eorum. Et hoc modo dicendum est quod Diabolus est causa omnium peccatorum nostrorum, quia ipse instigavit primum hominem ad peccandum, ex cuius peccato consecuta est in toto genere humano quaedam pronitas ad omnia peccata. Et per hunc modum intelligenda sunt verba Damasceni et Dionysii. Directe autem dicitur esse aliquid causa alicuius, quod operatur directe ad illud. Et hoc modo Diabolus non est causa omnis peccati non enim omnia peccata committuntur Diabolo instigante, sed quaedam ex libertate arbitrii et carnis corruptione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0050","text":"Quia, ut Origenes dicit, etiam si Diabolus non esset, homines haberent appetitum ciborum et venereorum et huiusmodi; circa quae multa inordinatio contingit, nisi per rationem talis appetitus refraenetur; et maxime, supposita corruptione naturae. Refraenare autem et ordinare huiusmodi appetitum, subiacet libero arbitrio. Sic ergo non est necessarium omnia peccata ex instinctu Diaboli provenire. Si qua tamen ex instinctu eius proveniunt, ad ea complenda *eo blandimento decipiuntur homines nunc, quo primi parentes*, ut Isidorus dicit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0051","text":"[I q. 114 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33215)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0052","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ad primum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0053","text":"[I q. 114 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33216)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0054","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, si qua peccata absque instinctu Diaboli perpetrantur, per ea tamen fiunt homines filii Diaboli, inquantum ipsum primo peccantem imitantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0055","text":"[I q. 114 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33217)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0056","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod homo potest per seipsum ruere in peccatum, sed ad meritum proficere non potest nisi auxilio divino, quod homini exhibetur mediante ministerio Angelorum. Et ideo ad omnia bona nostra cooperantur Angeli, non tamen omnia nostra peccata procedunt ex Daemonum suggestione. Quamvis nullum genus peccati sit, quod non interdum ex Daemonum suggestione proveniat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0057","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0058","text":"[I q. 114 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33218)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0059","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Daemones non possint homines seducere per aliqua miracula vera. Operatio enim Daemonum maxime vigebit in operibus Antichristi. Sed sicut apostolus dicit II ad Thessal. II, *eius adventus est secundum operationem Satanae, in omni virtute et signis et prodigiis mendacibus*. Ergo multo magis alio tempore per Daemones non fiunt nisi signa mendacia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0060","text":"[I q. 114 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33219)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0061","text":"Praeterea, vera miracula per aliquam corporum immutationem fiunt. Sed Daemones non possunt immutare corpus in aliam naturam, dicit enim Augustinus, XVIII de Civ. Dei, *nec corpus quidem humanum ulla ratione crediderim Daemonum arte vel potestate in membra bestialia posse converti*. Ergo Daemones vera miracula facere non possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0062","text":"[I q. 114 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33220)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0063","text":"Praeterea, argumentum efficaciam non habet, quod se habet ad opposita. Si ergo miracula vera possunt fieri a Daemonibus ad falsitatem persuadendam, non erunt efficacia ad veritatem fidei confirmandam. Quod est inconveniens, cum dicatur Marci ult., *domino cooperante, et sermonem confirmante sequentibus signis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0064","text":"[I q. 114 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33221)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0065","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, libro octoginta trium quaest., quod *magicis artibus fiunt miracula plerumque similia illis miraculis quae fiunt per servos Dei*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0066","text":"[I q. 114 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33222)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0067","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut ex supra dictis patet, si miraculum proprie accipiatur, Daemones miracula facere non possunt, nec aliqua creatura, sed solus Deus, quia miraculum proprie dicitur quod fit praeter ordinem totius naturae creatae, sub quo ordine continetur omnis virtus creaturae. Dicitur tamen quandoque miraculum large, quod excedit humanam facultatem et considerationem. Et sic Daemones possunt facere miracula, quae scilicet homines mirantur, inquantum eorum facultatem et cognitionem excedunt. Nam et unus homo, inquantum facit aliquid quod est supra facultatem et cognitionem alterius, ducit alium in admirationem sui operis, ut quodammodo miraculum videatur operari. Sciendum est tamen quod, quamvis huiusmodi opera Daemonum, quae nobis miracula videntur, ad veram rationem miraculi non pertingant; sunt tamen quandoque verae res.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0068","text":"Sicut magi Pharaonis per virtutem Daemonum veros serpentes et ranas fecerunt. *Et quando ignis de caelo cecidit et familiam Iob cum gregibus pecorum uno impetu consumpsit, et turbo domum deiiciens filios eius occidit, quae fuerunt opera Satanae, phantasmata non fuerunt*, ut Augustinus dicit, XX de Civ. Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0069","text":"[I q. 114 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33223)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0070","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit ibidem, Antichristi opera possunt dici esse signa mendacii, *vel quia mortales sensus per phantasmata decepturus est, ut quod non facit, videatur facere, vel quia, si sint vera prodigia, ad mendacium tamen pertrahent credituros*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0071","text":"[I q. 114 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33224)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0072","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, materia corporalis non obedit Angelis bonis seu malis ad nutum, ut Daemones sua virtute possint transmutare materiam de forma in formam, sed possunt adhibere quaedam semina quae in elementis mundi inveniuntur, ad huiusmodi effectus complendos, ut Augustinus dicit III de Trin. Et ideo dicendum est quod omnes transmutationes corporalium rerum quae possunt fieri per aliquas virtutes naturales, ad quas pertinent praedicta semina, possunt fieri per operationem Daemonum, huiusmodi seminibus adhibitis; sicut cum aliquae res transmutantur in serpentes vel ranas, quae per putrefactionem generari possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0073","text":"Illae vero transmutationes corporalium rerum quae non possunt virtute naturae fieri, nullo modo operatione Daemonum, secundum rei veritatem, perfici possunt; sicut quod corpus humanum mutetur in corpus bestiale, aut quod corpus hominis mortuum reviviscat. Et si aliquando aliquid tale operatione Daemonum fieri videatur, hoc non est secundum rei veritatem, sed secundum apparentiam tantum. Quod quidem potest dupliciter contingere. Uno modo, ab interiori; secundum quod Daemon potest mutare phantasiam hominis, et etiam sensus corporeos, ut aliquid videatur aliter quam sit, sicut supra dictum est. Et hoc etiam interdum fieri dicitur virtute aliquarum rerum corporalium. Alio modo, ab exteriori.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0074","text":"Cum enim ipse possit formare corpus ex aere cuiuscumque formae et figurae, ut illud assumens in eo visibiliter appareat; potest eadem ratione circumponere cuicumque rei corporeae quamcumque formam corpoream, ut in eius specie videatur. Et hoc est quod Augustinus dicit XVIII de Civ. Dei, quod *phantasticum hominis, quod etiam cogitando sive somniando per rerum innumerabilium genera variatur, velut corporatum in alicuius animalis effigie, sensibus apparet alienis*. Quod non est sic intelligendum, quod ipsa vis phantastica hominis, aut species eius, eadem numero incorporata alterius sensibus ostendatur, sed quia Daemon qui in phantasia unius hominis format aliquam speciem, ipse etiam potest similem speciem alterius sensibus offerre.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0075","text":"[I q. 114 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33225)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0076","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit in libro octoginta trium quaest., *cum talia faciunt magi qualia sancti, diverso fine et diverso iure fiunt. Illi enim faciunt, quaerentes gloriam suam, isti, quaerentes gloriam Dei. Et illi faciunt per quaedam privata commercia; isti autem publica administratione, et iussu Dei, cui cuncta creatura subiecta est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0077","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0078","text":"[I q. 114 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33226)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0079","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod Daemon qui superatur ab aliquo, non propter hoc ab impugnatione arceatur. Christus enim efficacissime suum tentatorem vicit. Sed tamen postea eum impugnavit, ad occisionem eius Iudaeos incitando. Ergo non est verum quod Diabolus victus ab impugnatione cesset.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0080","text":"[I q. 114 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33227)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0081","text":"Praeterea, infligere poenam ei qui in pugna succumbit, est incitare ad acrius impugnandum. Hoc autem non pertinet ad Dei misericordiam. Ergo Daemones superati non arcentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0082","text":"[I q. 114 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33228)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0083","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur Matth. IV, tunc reliquit eum Diabolus, scilicet Christum superantem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0084","text":"[I q. 114 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33229)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0085","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam dicunt quod Daemon superatus nullum hominum potest de cetero tentare, nec de eodem nec de alio peccato. Quidam autem dicunt quod potest alios tentare, sed non eundem. Et hoc probabilius dicitur, si tamen intelligatur usque ad aliquod tempus, unde et Lucae IV dicitur quod, *consummata omni tentatione, Diabolus recessit a Christo usque ad tempus*. Et huius ratio est duplex. Una est ex parte divinae clementiae, quia, ut Chrysostomus dicit, super Matth., *non tandiu homines Diabolus tentat, quandiu vult, sed quandiu Deus permittit; quia etsi permittat paulisper tentare, tamen repellit, propter infirmam naturam*. Alia ratio sumitur ex astutia Diaboli, unde Ambrosius dicit, super Lucam, quod *Diabolus instare formidat, quia frequentius refugit triumphari*. Quod tamen aliquando Diabolus redeat ad eum quem dimisit, patet per illud quod dicitur Matth.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0086","text":"XII, *revertar in domum meam, unde exivi*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0087","text":"[I q. 114 a. 5 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33230)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0088","text":"Et per hoc patet solutio ad obiecta.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"0948986f34c88979083b252c609ab8f9df72f89bedf2d8639e2f74845160f63e","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T154757847477Z_8afb1e4c","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 114","source_sha256":"0948986f34c88979083b252c609ab8f9df72f89bedf2d8639e2f74845160f63e","sha256":"b05497e80b92a4238a92b90c61fc7090a5af86ca7544482e516d291886ce5b90","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:47:57.847530+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0002","text":"[I q. 114 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33193)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0003","text":"Next, consideration must be given to the assault of the demons. And concerning this, five questions are asked. First, whether human beings are assailed by demons. Second, whether tempting is proper to the Devil. Third, whether all human sins arise from the assault or temptation of demons. Fourth, whether they can perform true miracles in order to deceive. Fifth, whether demons who are overcome by human beings are kept back from assailing human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0005","text":"[I q. 114 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33194)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that human beings are not assailed by demons. For angels are assigned to the guardianship of human beings, being sent by God. But demons are not sent by God, since the intention of demons is to destroy souls, whereas God's is to save them. Therefore demons are not assigned to the assault of human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0007","text":"[I q. 114 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33195)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0008","text":"Moreover, the condition of combat is not equitable if the weak are set for war against the strong, the ignorant against the cunning. But human beings are weak and ignorant, whereas demons are powerful and cunning. Therefore it ought not to be permitted by God, who is the author of all justice, that human beings be assailed by demons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0009","text":"[I q. 114 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33196)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0010","text":"Moreover, for the exercise of human beings, the assault of the flesh and the world suffices. But God permits His elect to be assailed for their exercise. Therefore it does not seem necessary that they be assailed by demons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0011","text":"[I q. 114 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33197)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0012","text":"On the contrary, the Apostle says, in Ephesians VI, that *our struggle is not against flesh and blood, but against principalities and powers, against the rulers of the world of this darkness, against the spiritual things of wickedness in the heavenly places*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0013","text":"[I q. 114 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33198)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0014","text":"I answer that two things must be considered concerning the assault of demons: namely, the assault itself and the order of the assault. The assault itself indeed proceeds from the malice of demons, who, through envy, strive to impede the progress of human beings; and through pride, they usurp a likeness of the divine power, assigning to themselves determined ministers for the assault of human beings, just as angels minister to God in determined offices for the salvation of human beings. But the order of this assault is from God, who knows how to use evils in an ordered way, by ordering them to goods. But on the part of the angels, both the guardianship itself and the order of guardianship are referred back to God as to the first author.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0015","text":"[I q. 114 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33199)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0016","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that evil angels assail human beings in two ways. In one way, by inciting them to sin. And thus they are not sent by God to assail; rather, they are sometimes permitted, according to God's just judgments. But at other times they assail human beings by punishing them. And thus they are sent by God; just as a lying spirit was sent to punish Ahab, king of Israel, as is said in III Kings, at the end. For punishment is referred to God as to its first author. And yet the demons sent to punish punish with an intention other than that for which they are sent; for they themselves punish out of hatred or envy, whereas they are sent by God on account of His justice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0017","text":"[I q. 114 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33200)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0018","text":"To the second, it must be said that, in order that the condition of combat not be unequal, recompense is made on the part of the human being: principally indeed through the help of divine grace, and secondarily through the guardianship of angels. Hence, in IV Kings, Elisha said to his servant, *do not fear, for there are more with us than with them*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0019","text":"[I q. 114 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33201)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0020","text":"To the third, it must be said that for human weakness the assault that comes from the flesh and the world would suffice for exercise; but it does not suffice for the malice of demons, which uses both for the assault of human beings. Yet, by divine ordering, this results in the glory of the elect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0021","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0022","text":"[I q. 114 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33202)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0023","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that tempting is not proper to the Devil. For God is said to tempt, according to Genesis XXII: *God tempted Abraham*. The flesh and the world also tempt. And a human being is also said to tempt God and another human being. Therefore it is not proper to a demon to tempt.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0024","text":"[I q. 114 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33203)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0025","text":"Moreover, to tempt is the act of one who is ignorant. But demons know what is done concerning men. Therefore demons do not tempt.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0026","text":"[I q. 114 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33204)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0027","text":"Moreover, temptation is a way to sin. But sin consists in the will. Therefore, since demons cannot change a man's will, as is clear from what has been said above, it seems that to tempt does not pertain to them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0028","text":"[I q. 114 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33205)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0029","text":"On the contrary, it is said in 1 Thess. III, *lest perhaps he who tempts should have tempted you*; the Gloss says, that is, the Devil, whose office it is to tempt.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0030","text":"[I q. 114 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33206)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0031","text":"I answer that it must be said that to tempt is properly to make trial of something. But trial is made of something in order that something concerning it may be known; and therefore the proximate end of every tempter is knowledge. But sometimes, beyond knowledge, some other end is sought, either good or evil: good indeed, as when someone wishes to know what sort of person another is, either with respect to knowledge or with respect to virtue, in order to advance him; but evil, when he wishes to know this in order to deceive or overthrow him. And in this way it may be understood how to tempt is attributed differently to different persons. For a man is said to tempt, sometimes indeed merely in order to know; and for this reason, to tempt God is said to be a sin, because man, as though uncertain, presumes to test God's power. But sometimes he tempts in order to help, and sometimes in order to harm. The Devil, however, always tempts in order to harm, precipitating into sin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0032","text":"And according to this, tempting is called his proper office; for even if a man sometimes tempts in this way, he does so insofar as he is a minister of the Devil. But God is said to tempt in order that He may know, according to that manner of speaking by which He is said to know what He causes others to know. Hence it is said in Deut. XII, *the Lord your God tempts you, that it may become manifest whether you love Him*. But the flesh and the world are said to tempt instrumentally, or materially, namely, insofar as it can be known what sort of man someone is from the fact that he follows or resists the concupiscences of the flesh, and from the fact that he despises the prosperities and adversities of the world; and the Devil also uses these in tempting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0033","text":"[I q. 114 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33207)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0034","text":"And thus the solution to the first is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0035","text":"[I q. 114 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33208)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0036","text":"To the second, it must be said that demons know those things which are done externally concerning men, but God alone knows the interior condition of man, He who weighs spirits; and from this condition some are more inclined to one vice than to another. And therefore the Devil tempts by exploring the interior condition of man, so that he may tempt him by that vice to which the man is more inclined.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0037","text":"[I q. 114 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33209)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0038","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although a demon cannot change the will, nevertheless, as was said above, he can in some way change man's lower powers; from which, although the will is not compelled, it is nevertheless inclined.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0039","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0040","text":"[I q. 114 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33210)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0041","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that all sins proceed from the temptation of the Devil. For Dionysius says, in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, that *the multitude of demons is the cause of all evils, both for itself and for others*. And Damascene says that *every malice and every uncleanness were devised by the Devil*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0042","text":"[I q. 114 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33211)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0043","text":"Moreover, of every sinner it could be said what the Lord says of the Jews, John VIII: *you are of your father the Devil*. But this is insofar as they sinned by the Devil's suggestion. Therefore every sin is from the suggestion of the Devil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0044","text":"[I q. 114 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33212)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0045","text":"Moreover, just as angels are assigned to the guardianship of men, so demons are assigned to their assault. But all the good things that we do proceed from the suggestion of good angels, because divine things are brought to us through the mediation of angels. Therefore all the evils that we do also arise from the suggestion of the Devil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0046","text":"[I q. 114 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33213)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0047","text":"On the contrary, it is said in the book *On Ecclesiastical Dogmas*: *Not all our evil thoughts are stirred up by the Devil, but sometimes they arise from the movement of our own choice*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0048","text":"[I q. 114 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33214)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0049","text":"I answer that the cause of something can be called something in two ways: in one way, directly; in another way, indirectly. Indirectly indeed, as when some agent causing a disposition toward some effect is said to be the occasional and indirect cause of that effect; as if one were to say that he who dries wood is the cause of its burning. And in this way it must be said that the Devil is the cause of all our sins, because he incited the first man to sin, from whose sin there followed in the whole human race a certain proneness to all sins. And the words of Damascene and Dionysius are to be understood in this way. But something is said to be the direct cause of something else when it operates directly toward it. And in this way the Devil is not the cause of every sin, for not all sins are committed at the Devil's instigation, but some from the freedom of the will and the corruption of the flesh.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0050","text":"For, as Origen says, even if the Devil did not exist, men would have an appetite for foods and venereal things and the like; concerning which much disorder occurs unless such appetite is restrained by reason; and especially given the corruption of nature. But restraining and ordering such an appetite is subject to free choice. Therefore it is not necessary that all sins proceed from the Devil's prompting. Yet if any proceed from his prompting, men are deceived into accomplishing them *by the same allurement by which the first parents [were deceived]*, as Isidore says.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0051","text":"[I q. 114 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33215)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0052","text":"And through this the answer to the first objection is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0053","text":"[I q. 114 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33216)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0054","text":"To the second, it must be said that, if any sins are perpetrated without the Devil's prompting, nevertheless through them men become sons of the Devil, insofar as they imitate him, who sinned first.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0055","text":"[I q. 114 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33217)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0056","text":"To the third, it must be said that a man can fall into sin by himself, but he cannot advance to merit except by divine aid, which is furnished to man through the ministry of the Angels. And therefore the Angels cooperate in all our good things; yet not all our sins proceed from the suggestion of the demons. Although there is no kind of sin that does not sometimes proceed from the suggestion of demons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0057","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0058","text":"[I q. 114 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33218)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0059","text":"The fourth point is approached thus. It seems that demons cannot deceive men by any true miracles. For the operation of demons will be most vigorous in the works of Antichrist. But as the Apostle says in 2 Thessalonians 2, *his coming is according to the operation of Satan, in all power and signs and lying wonders*. Therefore, much more at another time are only lying signs performed through demons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0060","text":"[I q. 114 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33219)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0061","text":"Further, true miracles occur through some change of bodies. But demons cannot change a body into another nature, for Augustine says, in Book XVIII of *The City of God*, *nor indeed would I believe on any grounds that a human body can, by the art or power of demons, be transformed into the limbs of beasts*. Therefore demons cannot perform true miracles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0062","text":"[I q. 114 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33220)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0063","text":"Further, an argument that applies to opposites has no efficacy. Therefore, if true miracles can be performed by demons to persuade men of falsehood, they will not be efficacious for confirming the truth of faith. But this is unfitting, since it is said at the end of Mark, *the Lord cooperating, and confirming the word by the signs that followed*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0064","text":"[I q. 114 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33221)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0065","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, that *by magical arts miracles are often performed similar to those miracles that are performed through the servants of God*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0066","text":"[I q. 114 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33222)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0067","text":"I answer that, as is clear from what has been said above, if miracle is taken properly, demons cannot perform miracles, nor can any creature, but God alone; because a miracle is properly called that which is done outside the order of all created nature, under which order every power of a creature is contained. Yet sometimes that which exceeds human capacity and consideration is called a miracle in a broad sense. And thus demons can perform miracles, namely, such as men marvel at insofar as they exceed their capacity and knowledge. For even one man, insofar as he does something beyond another's capacity and knowledge, leads the other into wonder at his work, so that he may seem in some way to work a miracle. Yet it must be known that, although works of demons of this kind, which seem to us to be miracles, do not attain to the true notion of miracle, they are nevertheless sometimes real things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0068","text":"Thus Pharaoh's magicians, through the power of demons, made real serpents and frogs. *And when fire fell from heaven and consumed Job's household together with the herds of cattle in one blow, and a whirlwind, casting down the house, killed his children—these were works of Satan, not phantasms*, as Augustine says in Book XX of *The City of God*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0069","text":"[I q. 114 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33223)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0070","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that, as Augustine says in the same place, the works of Antichrist can be called signs of falsehood, *either because he will deceive mortal senses through phantasms, so that he may seem to do what he does not do; or because, if they are true prodigies, they will nevertheless draw those who believe toward falsehood*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0071","text":"[I q. 114 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33224)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0072","text":"To the second, it must be said that, as was said above, corporeal matter does not obey good or evil angels at their command, so that demons might by their own power transform matter from form into form; but they can employ certain seeds that are found in the elements of the world for bringing about effects of this kind, as Augustine says in Book III of *On the Trinity*. And therefore it must be said that all transformations of corporeal things which can be effected by certain natural powers, to which the aforesaid seeds belong, can be effected by the operation of demons, with such seeds employed; as when certain things are transformed into serpents or frogs, which can be generated through putrefaction.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0073","text":"But those transformations of corporeal things which cannot be effected by the power of nature can in no way be accomplished by the operation of demons, according to the truth of the thing; as, for example, that a human body be changed into the body of a beast, or that the dead body of a man come back to life. And if at times something of this sort seems to be done by the operation of demons, this is not according to the truth of the thing, but only according to appearance. This can indeed happen in two ways. In one way, from within: insofar as a demon can alter a man's imagination, and even the bodily senses, so that something appears otherwise than it is, as was said above. And this too is sometimes said to occur through the power of certain corporeal things. In another way, from without.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0074","text":"For since he can form a body out of air in whatever form and figure, so that, assuming it, he may appear visibly in it; by the same reasoning he can place around any corporeal thing whatever corporeal form, so that it may be seen under its appearance. And this is what Augustine says in *The City of God*, XVIII: that *the phantasm of a man, which is also varied, whether in thought or in dreaming, through innumerable kinds of things, as though embodied in the likeness of some animal, appears to the senses of others*. This is not to be understood as though the very imaginative power of a man, or its species, numerically the same, were embodied and shown to the senses of another; but because the demon who forms some species in the imagination of one man can also offer a similar species to the senses of another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0075","text":"[I q. 114 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33225)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0076","text":"To the third, it must be said that, as Augustine says in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*, *when magicians do such things as saints do, they are done with a different end and by a different right. For the former do them, seeking their own glory; the latter, seeking the glory of God. And the former do them through certain private dealings; but the latter through public administration and by the command of God, to whom every creature is subject*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0077","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0078","text":"[I q. 114 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33226)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0079","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth. It seems that a demon who is overcome is not thereby kept from attacking. For Christ most effectively conquered His tempter. Yet afterward he attacked Him, by inciting the Jews to kill Him. Therefore, it is not true that the Devil, when conquered, ceases from attacking.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0080","text":"[I q. 114 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33227)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0081","text":"Moreover, to inflict punishment on one who succumbs in battle is to incite him to attack more fiercely. But this does not pertain to God's mercy. Therefore, conquered demons are not kept away.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0082","text":"[I q. 114 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33228)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0083","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in Matthew IV: then the Devil left him, namely, Christ as conqueror.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0084","text":"[I q. 114 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33229)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0085","text":"I answer that some say that a demon once overcome can thereafter tempt no human being, neither concerning the same sin nor another. But others say that he can tempt others, but not the same person. And this is said more probably, provided, however, that it be understood as applying up to a certain time; whence Luke IV says that, *when every temptation had been completed, the Devil departed from Christ until a time*. And the reason for this is twofold. One is on the part of divine clemency, because, as Chrysostom says on Matthew, *the Devil does not tempt men for as long as he wishes, but for as long as God permits; for although He permits him to tempt for a little while, nevertheless He drives him away, on account of weak nature*. The other reason is drawn from the Devil's cunning; hence Ambrose says, on Luke, that *the Devil fears to press on, because he more often shrinks from being vanquished*. Yet that the Devil sometimes returns to him whom he has left is clear from what is said in Matthew","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0086","text":"XII: *I will return to my house, from which I came out*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0087","text":"[I q. 114 a. 5 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33230)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__106:P0088","text":"And through this the solution to the objections is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__106:P0088"]}],"source_digest":"aeeb28c198cc1437183cceeb0204934a1f5f65fb7e58092f6834360e21747164","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 114"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 114","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107","title":"First Part · Question 115","order":116,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC__107","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 115 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33231)\n\nConsequenter considerandum est de actione corporalis creaturae; et fato, quod aliquibus corporibus attribuitur. Circa actiones corporales quaeruntur sex. Primo, utrum aliquod corpus sit activum. Secundo, utrum in corporibus sint aliquae seminales rationes. Tertio, utrum corpora caelestia sint causa eorum quae hic per inferiora corpora fiunt. Quarto, utrum sint causa humanorum actuum. Quinto, utrum eorum actionibus Daemones subdantur. Sexto, utrum caelestia corpora imponant necessitatem his quae eorum actionibus subduntur.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 115 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33232)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nullum corpus sit activum. Dicit enim Augustinus quod *in rebus invenitur aliquid actum et non agens, sicut sunt corpora; aliquid agens et non actum, sicut Deus; aliquid agens et actum, sicut substantiae spirituales*.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33233)\n\nPraeterea, omne agens, excepto primo agente, in suo opere indiget subiecto quod sit susceptibile suae actionis. Sed infra substantiam corporalem non est substantia quae sit susceptibilis suae actionis, quia haec substantia tenet infimum gradum in entibus. Ergo substantia corporalis non est activa.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33234)\n\nPraeterea, omnis substantia corporalis concluditur quantitate. Sed quantitas impedit substantiam a motu et actione, quia comprehendit eam, et mergitur in ea, sicut impeditur aer nubilosus a perceptione luminis. Et huius signum est, quod quanto magis accreverit quantitas corporis, tanto est ponderosius et gravius ad hoc quod moveatur. Ergo nulla substantia corporalis est activa.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33235)\n\nPraeterea, omne agens habet virtutem agendi ex propinquitate ad primum activum. Sed a primo activo, quod est simplicissimum, remotissima sunt corpora, quae sunt maxime composita. Ergo nullum corpus est agens.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33236)\n\nPraeterea, si aliquod corpus est agens, aut agit ad formam substantialem; aut ad formam accidentalem. Sed non ad formam substantialem, quia non invenitur in corporibus principium actionis nisi aliqua qualitas activa, quae est accidens; accidens autem non potest esse causa formae substantialis, cum causa sit potior quam effectus. Similiter etiam neque ad formam accidentalem, *quia accidens non se extendit ultra suum subiectum*, ut Augustinus dicit IX de Trin. Ergo nullum corpus est activum.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33237)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius, XV cap. Cael. Hier., inter ceteras proprietates corporei ignis, dicit quod *ad susceptas materias manifestat sui ipsius magnitudinem activus et potens*.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33238)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod sensibiliter apparet aliqua corpora esse activa. Sed circa corporum actiones tripliciter aliqui erraverunt. Fuerunt enim aliqui qui totaliter corporibus actiones subtraxerunt. Et haec est opinio Avicebron in libro fontis vitae, ubi per rationes quae tactae sunt, probare nititur quod nullum corpus agit, sed omnes actiones quae videntur esse corporum, sunt actiones cuiusdam virtutis spiritualis quae penetrat per omnia corpora; ita quod ignis, secundum eum, non calefacit, sed virtus spiritualis penetrans per ipsum. Et videtur haec opinio derivata esse ab opinione Platonis. Nam Plato posuit omnes formas quae sunt in materia corporali, esse participatas et determinatas et contractas ad hanc materiam; formas vero separatas esse absolutas et quasi universales; et ideo illas formas separatas dicebat esse causas formarum quae sunt in materia. Secundum hoc ergo quod forma quae est in materia corporali, determinata est ad hanc materiam individuatam per quantitatem, ponebat Avicebron quod a quantitate, prout est individuationis principium, retinetur et arcetur forma corporalis, ne possit se extendere per actionem in aliam materiam; sed solum forma spiritualis et immaterialis, quae non est coarctata per quantitatem, potest effluere per actionem in aliud. Sed ista ratio non concludit quod forma corporalis non sit agens, sed quod non sit agens universale. Secundum enim quod participatur aliquid, secundum hoc est necessarium quod participetur id quod est proprium ei, sicut quantum participatur de lumine, tantum participatur de ratione visibilis. Agere autem, quod nihil est aliud quam facere aliquid actu, est per se proprium actus, inquantum est actus, unde et omne agens agit sibi simile. Sic ergo ex hoc quod aliquid est forma non determinata per materiam quantitati subiectam, habet quod sit agens indeterminatum et universale, ex hoc vero quod est determinata ad hanc materiam, habet quod sit agens contractum et particulare. Unde si esset forma ignis separata, ut Platonici posuerunt, esset aliquo modo causa omnis ignitionis. Sed haec forma ignis quae est in hac materia corporali, est causa huius ignitionis quae est ab hoc corpore in hoc corpus. Unde et fit talis actio per contactum duorum corporum. Sed tamen haec opinio Avicebron superexcedit opinionem Platonis. Nam Plato ponebat solum formas substantiales separatas; accidentia vero reducebat ad principia materialia quae sunt magnum et parvum, quae ponebat esse prima contraria, sicut et alii rarum et densum. Et ideo tam Plato quam Avicenna, in aliquo ipsum sequens, ponebant quod agentia corporalia agunt secundum formas accidentales, disponendo materiam ad formam substantialem; sed ultima perfectio, quae est per introductionem formae substantialis est a principio immateriali. Et haec est secunda opinio de actione corporum, de qua supra dictum est, cum de creatione ageretur. Tertia vero opinio fuit Democriti, qui ponebat actionem esse per effluxionem atomorum a corpore agente, et passionem esse per receptionem eorundem in poris corporis patientis. Quam opinionem improbat Aristoteles in I de Generat. Sequeretur enim quod corpus non pateretur per totum, et quod quantitas corporis agentis diminueretur ex hoc quod agit, quae sunt manifeste falsa. Dicendum est ergo quod corpus agit secundum quod est actu, in aliud corpus secundum quod est in potentia.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33239)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod dictum Augustini est intelligendum de tota natura corporali simul accepta, quae non habet aliquam inferiorem naturam infra se, in quam agat, sicut natura spiritualis in corporalem, et natura increata in creatam. Sed tamen unum corpus est infra alterum, inquantum est in potentia ad id quod habet aliud in actu.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33240)\n\nEt per hoc patet solutio ad secundum. Sciendum est tamen quod, cum Avicebron sic argumentatur, est aliquid quod est movens non motum, scilicet primus factor rerum, ergo, ex opposito, est aliquid quod est motum et patiens tantum, quod concedendum est. Sed hoc est materia prima, quae est potentia pura, sicut Deus est actus purus. Corpus autem componitur ex potentia et actu, et ideo est agens et patiens.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33241)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod quantitas non impedit formam corporalem omnino ab actione, sicut dictum est, sed impedit eam ne sit agens universale, inquantum forma individuatur prout est in materia quantitati subiecta. Signum tamen quod inducitur de ponderositate corporum, non est ad propositum. Primo quidem, quia additio quantitatis non est causa gravitatis; ut probatur in IV de caelo et mundo. Secundo, quia falsum est quod ponderositas facit tardiorem motum, immo quanto aliquid est gravius, tanto magis movetur motu proprio. Tertio, quia actio non fit per motum localem, ut Democritus posuit; sed per hoc quod aliquid reducitur de potentia in actum.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33242)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod corpus non est id quod maxime distat a Deo, participat enim aliquid de similitudine divini esse, secundum formam quam habet. Sed id quod maxime distat a Deo, est materia prima; quae nullo modo est agens, cum sit in potentia tantum.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33243)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod corpus agit et ad formam accidentalem, et ad formam substantialem. Qualitas enim activa, ut calor, etsi sit accidens, agit tamen in virtute formae substantialis, sicut eius instrumentum; et ideo potest agere ad formam substantialem; sicut et calor naturalis, inquantum est instrumentum animae, agit ad generationem carnis. Ad accidens vero agit propria virtute. Nec est contra rationem accidentis, quod excedat suum subiectum in agendo, sed quod excedat in essendo, nisi forsan quis imaginetur idem accidens numero defluere ab agente in patiens, sicut Democritus ponebat fieri actionem per defluxum atomorum.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 115 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33244)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in materia corporali non sint aliquae rationes seminales. Ratio enim importat aliquid secundum esse spirituale. Sed in materia corporali non est aliquid spiritualiter, sed materialiter tantum, secundum scilicet modum eius in quo est. Ergo in materia corporali non sunt seminales rationes.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33245)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, III de Trin., quod Daemones quaedam opera faciunt adhibendo occultis motibus quaedam semina, quae in elementis cognoscunt. Sed ea quae per motum localem adhibentur, sunt corpora, non rationes. Ergo inconvenienter dicitur quod sunt in corporali materia seminales rationes.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33246)\n\nPraeterea, semen est principium activum. Sed in materia corporali non est aliquod principium activum, cum materiae non competat agere, ut dictum est. Ergo in materia corporali non sunt seminales rationes.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33247)\n\nPraeterea, in materia corporali dicuntur esse quaedam causales rationes, quae videntur sufficere ad rerum productionem. Sed seminales rationes sunt aliae a causalibus, quia praeter seminales rationes fiunt miracula, non autem praeter causales. Ergo inconvenienter dicitur quod seminales rationes sunt in materia corporali.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33248)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, III de Trin., *omnium rerum quae corporaliter visibiliterque nascuntur, occulta quaedam semina in istis corporeis mundi huius elementis latent*.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33249)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod denominationes consueverunt fieri a perfectiori, ut dicitur in II de anima. In tota autem natura corporea perfectiora sunt corpora viva, unde et ipsum nomen naturae translatum est a rebus viventibus ad omnes res naturales. Nam ipsum nomen naturae, ut philosophus dicit in V Metaphys., primo impositum fuit ad significandum generationem viventium, quae nativitas dicitur, et quia viventia generantur ex principio coniuncto, sicut fructus ex arbore, et foetus ex matre, cui colligatur, consequenter tractum est nomen naturae ad omne principium motus quod est in eo quod movetur. Manifestum est autem quod principium activum et passivum generationis rerum viventium sunt semina ex quibus viventia generantur. Et ideo convenienter Augustinus omnes virtutes activas et passivas quae sunt principia generationum et motuum naturalium, seminales rationes vocat. Huiusmodi autem virtutes activae et passivae in multiplici ordine considerari possunt. Nam primo quidem, ut Augustinus dicit VI super Gen. ad Litt., sunt principaliter et originaliter in ipso verbo Dei, secundum rationes ideales. Secundo vero, sunt in elementis mundi, ubi simul a principio productae sunt, sicut in universalibus causis. Tertio vero modo, sunt in iis quae ex universalibus causis secundum successiones temporum producuntur, sicut in hac planta et in hoc animali, tanquam in particularibus causis. Quarto modo, sunt in seminibus quae ex animalibus et plantis producuntur. Quae iterum comparantur ad alios effectus particulares, sicut primordiales causae universales ad primos effectus productos.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33250)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod huiusmodi virtutes activae et passivae rerum naturalium, etsi non possint dici rationes secundum quod sunt in materia corporali; possunt tamen dici rationes per comparationem ad suam originem, secundum quod deducuntur a rationibus idealibus.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33251)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod huiusmodi virtutes activae vel passivae sunt in aliquibus partibus corporalibus; quae dum adhibentur per motum localem ad aliquos effectus complendos, dicuntur semina adhiberi per Daemones.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33252)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod semen maris est principium activum in generatione animalis. Sed potest etiam dici semen id quod est ex parte feminae, quod est principium passivum. Et sic sub semine comprehendi possunt vires activae et passivae.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33253)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod ex verbis Augustini de huiusmodi rationibus seminalibus loquentis, satis accipi potest quod ipsae rationes seminales sunt etiam rationes causales, sicut et semen est quaedam causa, dicit enim in III de Trin., quod *sicut matres gravidae sunt foetibus, sic ipse mundus est gravidus causis nascentium*. Sed tamen rationes ideales possunt dici causales, non autem proprie loquendo seminales, quia semen non est principium separatum, et praeter huiusmodi rationes non fiunt miracula. Similiter etiam neque praeter virtutes passivas creaturae inditas, ut ex ea fieri possit quidquid Deus mandaverit. Sed praeter virtutes activas naturales, et potentias passivas quae ordinantur ad huiusmodi virtutes activas, dicuntur fieri miracula, dum dicitur quod fiunt praeter rationes seminales.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 115 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33254)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod corpora caelestia non sint causa eorum quae hic in inferioribus corporibus fiunt. Dicit enim Damascenus, nos autem dicimus quoniam ipsa, scilicet corpora caelestia, *non sunt causa alicuius eorum quae fiunt, neque corruptionis eorum quae corrumpuntur, signa autem sunt magis imbrium et aeris transmutationis*.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33255)\n\nPraeterea, ad faciendum aliquid, sufficit agens et materia. Sed in istis inferioribus invenitur materia patiens, inveniuntur etiam contraria agentia, scilicet calidum et frigidum et huiusmodi. Ergo non est necessarium, ad causandum ea quae hic inferius fiunt, causalitatem caelestibus corporibus attribuere.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33256)\n\nPraeterea, agens agit sibi simile. Sed videmus quod omnia quae fiunt hic inferius, fiunt per hoc quod calefiunt et frigidantur, et humectantur et desiccantur, et aliis huiusmodi qualitatibus alterantur, quae non inveniuntur in corporibus caelestibus. Ergo corpora caelestia non sunt causa eorum quae hic fiunt.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33257)\n\nPraeterea, sicut Augustinus dicit V de Civ. Dei, *nihil est magis corporeum quam corporis sexus*. Sed corporis sexus non causatur ex corporibus caelestibus, cuius signum esse videtur, quod duorum geminorum sub una constellatione natorum, unus est masculus et alter est femina. Ergo corpora caelestia non sunt causa rerum corporalium quae hic fiunt.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33258)\n\nSed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, III de Trin., quod *corpora crassiora et inferiora per subtiliora et potentiora quodam ordine reguntur*. Et Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *lumen solis ad generationem sensibilium corporum confert, et ad vitam ipsa movet, et nutrit et auget et perficit*.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33259)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, cum omnis multitudo ab unitate procedat; quod autem immobile est, uno modo se habet, quod vero movetur, multiformiter, considerandum est, in tota natura, quod omnis motus ab immobili procedit. Et ideo quanto aliqua sunt immobiliora, tanto sunt magis causa eorum quae sunt magis mobilia. Corpora autem caelestia sunt inter alia corpora magis immobilia, non enim moventur nisi motu locali. Et ideo motus horum inferiorum corporum qui sunt varii et multiformes, reducuntur in motum corporis caelestis, sicut in causam.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33260)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod dictum Damasceni intelligendum est, quod corpora caelestia non sunt prima causa generationis et corruptionis eorum quae hic fiunt; sicut dicebant illi qui ponebant corpora caelestia esse deos.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33261)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod principia activa in istis inferioribus corporibus non inveniuntur nisi qualitates activae elementorum, quae sunt calidum et frigidum et huiusmodi. Et si sic esset quod formae substantiales inferiorum corporum non diversificarentur nisi secundum huiusmodi accidentia, quorum principia rarum et densum antiqui naturales posuerunt; non oporteret super haec inferiora corpora aliquod principium activum ponere, sed ipsa sufficerent ad agendum. Sed recte considerantibus apparet quod huiusmodi accidentia se habent sicut materiales dispositiones ad formas substantiales naturalium corporum. Materia autem non sufficit ad agendum. Et ideo oportet super has materiales dispositiones ponere aliquod principium activum. Unde Platonici posuerunt species separatas, secundum quarum participationem inferiora corpora substantiales formas consequuntur. Sed hoc non videtur sufficere. Quia species separatae semper eodem modo se haberent, cum ponantur immobiles, et sic sequeretur quod non esset aliqua variatio circa generationem et corruptionem inferiorum corporum; quod patet esse falsum. Unde secundum philosophum, in II de Gen., necesse est ponere aliquod principium activum mobile, quod per sui praesentiam et absentiam causet varietatem circa generationem et corruptionem inferiorum corporum. Et huiusmodi sunt corpora caelestia. Et ideo quidquid in istis inferioribus generat, movet ad speciem sicut instrumentum caelestis corporis; secundum quod dicitur in II Physic., quod *homo generat hominem, et sol*.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33262)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod corpora caelestia inferioribus corporibus non sunt similia similitudine speciei; sed inquantum sua universali virtute continent in se quidquid in inferioribus corporibus generatur; secundum quem modum dicimus etiam omnia esse Deo similia.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33263)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod actiones corporum caelestium diversimode recipiuntur in inferioribus corporibus secundum diversam materiae dispositionem. Contingit autem quandoque quod materia conceptus humani non est disposita totaliter ad masculinum sexum; unde partim formatur in masculum, partim in feminam. Unde ad hoc introducitur ab Augustino, ad repellendum scilicet divinationem quae fit per astra, quia effectus astrorum variantur etiam in rebus corporeis, secundum diversam materiae dispositionem.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 115 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33264)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod corpora caelestia sint causa humanorum actuum. Corpora enim caelestia, cum moveantur a spiritualibus substantiis, sicut supra dictum est, agunt in virtute earum quasi instrumenta. Sed illae substantiae spirituales sunt superiores animabus nostris. Ergo videtur quod possint imprimere in animas nostras, et sic causare actus humanos.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33265)\n\nPraeterea, omne multiforme reducitur in aliquod uniforme principium. Sed actus humani sunt varii et multiformes. Ergo videtur quod reducantur in uniformes motus caelestium corporum, sicut in sua principia.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33266)\n\nPraeterea, astrologi frequenter vera annuntiant de eventibus bellorum, et aliis humanis actibus, quorum principia sunt intellectus et voluntas. Quod facere non possent secundum caelestia corpora, nisi essent humanorum actuum causa. Sunt ergo corpora caelestia humanorum actuum causa.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33267)\n\nSed contra est quod Damascenus dicit, quod *corpora caelestia humanorum actuum nequaquam sunt causa*.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33268)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod corpora caelestia in corpora quidem imprimunt directe et per se, sicut iam dictum est. In vires autem animae quae sunt actus organorum corporeorum, directe quidem, sed per accidens, quia necesse est huiusmodi actus harum potentiarum impediri secundum impedimenta organorum, sicut oculus turbatus non bene videt. Unde si intellectus et voluntas essent vires corporeis organis alligatae, sicut posuerunt aliqui, dicentes quod intellectus non differt a sensu; ex necessitate sequeretur quod corpora caelestia essent causa electionum et actuum humanorum. Et ex hoc sequeretur quod homo naturali instinctu ageretur ad suas actiones, sicut cetera animalia, in quibus non sunt nisi vires animae corporeis organis alligatae, nam illud quod fit in istis inferioribus ex impressione corporum caelestium, naturaliter agitur. Et ita sequeretur quod homo non esset liberi arbitrii, sed haberet actiones determinatas, sicut et ceterae res naturales. Quae manifeste sunt falsa, et conversationi humanae contraria. Sciendum est tamen quod indirecte et per accidens impressiones corporum caelestium ad intellectum et voluntatem pertingere possunt; inquantum scilicet tam intellectus quam voluntas aliquo modo ab inferioribus viribus accipiunt, quae organis corporeis alligantur. Sed circa hoc diversimode se habent intellectus et voluntas. Nam intellectus ex necessitate accipit ab inferioribus viribus apprehensivis, unde turbata vi imaginativa vel cogitativa vel memorativa, ex necessitate turbatur actio intellectus. Sed voluntas non ex necessitate sequitur inclinationem appetitus inferioris, licet enim passiones quae sunt in irascibili et concupiscibili, habeant quandam vim ad inclinandam voluntatem; tamen in potestate voluntatis remanet sequi passiones, vel eas refutare. Et ideo impressio caelestium corporum, secundum quam immutari possunt inferiores vires, minus pertingit ad voluntatem, quae est proxima causa humanorum actuum, quam ad intellectum. Ponere igitur caelestia corpora esse causam humanorum actuum, est proprium illorum qui dicunt intellectum non differre a sensu. Unde quidam eorum dicebant quod *talis est voluntas in hominibus, qualem in diem inducit pater virorum deorumque*. Quia ergo constat intellectum et voluntatem non esse actus organorum corporeorum, impossibile est quod corpora caelestia sint causa humanorum actuum.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33269)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod spirituales substantiae quae caelestia corpora movent, in corporalia quidem agunt mediantibus caelestibus corporibus, sed in intellectum humanum agunt immediate illuminando. Voluntatem autem immutare non possunt, ut supra habitum est.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33270)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut multiformitas corporalium motuum reducitur sicut in causam in uniformitatem motuum caelestium; ita multiformitas actuum qui sunt ab intellectu et voluntate, reducitur in principium uniforme quod est intellectus et voluntas divina.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33271)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod plures hominum sequuntur passiones, quae sunt motus sensitivi appetitus, ad quas cooperari possunt corpora caelestia, pauci autem sunt sapientes, qui huiusmodi passionibus resistant. Et ideo astrologi ut in pluribus vera possunt praedicere, et maxime in communi. Non autem in speciali, quia nihil prohibet aliquem hominem per liberum arbitrium passionibus resistere. Unde et ipsi astrologi dicunt quod sapiens homo dominatur astris, inquantum scilicet dominatur suis passionibus.\n\n## Articulus 5\n\n[I q. 115 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33272)\n\nAd quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod corpora caelestia possint imprimere in ipsos Daemones. Daemones enim secundum certa augmenta lunae aliquos homines vexant, qui et propter hoc lunatici dicuntur; ut patet Matth. IV et XVII. Sed hoc non esset, nisi corporibus caelestibus subiacerent. Ergo Daemones subiacent actionibus caelestium corporum.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33273)\n\nPraeterea, necromantici observant certas constellationes ad invocandos Daemones. Non autem per corpora caelestia invocarentur, si non eis subiacerent. Ergo Daemones subiacent actionibus caelestium corporum.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33274)\n\nPraeterea, corpora caelestia virtuosiora sunt quam corpora inferiora. Sed quibusdam inferioribus corporibus Daemones arcentur, scilicet herbis et lapidibus et animantibus, et quibusdam *sonis certis ac vocibus, et figurationibus atque figmentis*, ut a Porphyrio dictum Augustinus introducit in X de Civ. Dei. Ergo multo magis Daemones subduntur actioni caelestium corporum.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33275)\n\nSed contra est quod Daemones sunt superiores ordine naturae quam corpora caelestia. Agens autem est superius patiente, ut Augustinus dicit XII super Gen. ad Litt. Ergo Daemones non subiiciuntur actioni corporum caelestium.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33276)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa Daemones fuit triplex opinio. Prima Peripateticorum, qui posuerunt Daemones non esse; sed ea quae attribuuntur Daemonibus, secundum artem necromanticam, fiunt virtute caelestium corporum. Et hoc est quod Augustinus, X de Civ. Dei, introducit dictum a Porphyrio, quod *fabricantur in terra ab hominibus potestates idoneae siderum variis effectibus exequendis*. Sed haec positio est manifeste falsa. Experimento enim scitur multa per Daemones fieri, ad quae nullo modo virtus caelestium corporum sufficeret; puta quod arreptitii loquuntur lingua ignota, quod recitant versus et auctoritates quas nunquam sciverunt, quod necromantici faciunt statuas loqui et moveri, et similia. Ex quibus Platonici moti fuerunt ut ponerent Daemones esse animalia corpore aerea, animo passiva; ut ab Apuleio dictum Augustinus introducit VIII de Civ. Dei. Et haec est secunda opinio, secundum quam dici posset quod Daemones hoc modo subduntur corporibus caelestibus, sicut et de hominibus dictum est. Sed haec opinio ex superioribus patet esse falsa, dicimus enim Daemones esse substantias intellectuales corporibus non unitas. Unde patet quod non subduntur actioni caelestium corporum, nec per se nec per accidens, nec directe nec indirecte.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33277)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod hoc quod Daemones secundum certa augmenta lunae homines vexant, contingit propter duo. Primo quidem, ad hoc quod infament creaturam Dei, scilicet lunam, ut Hieronymus et Chrysostomus dicunt. Secundo quia, cum non possint operari nisi mediantibus naturalibus virtutibus, ut supra dictum est; in suis operibus considerant corporum aptitudines ad effectus intentos. Manifestum est autem quod *cerebrum humidissimum est omnium partium corporis*, ut Aristoteles dicit, et ideo maxime subiicitur operationi lunae, quae ex sua proprietate habet movere humorem. In cerebro autem perficiuntur vires animales, et ideo Daemones secundum certa augmenta lunae perturbant hominis phantasiam, quando considerant cerebrum ad hoc esse dispositum.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33278)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod Daemones advocati in certis constellationibus, propter duo veniunt. Primo quidem, ut homines in hunc errorem inducant, quod credant aliquod numen esse in stellis. Secundo, quia considerant secundum aliquas certas constellationes materiam corporalem magis esse dispositam ad effectus pro quibus advocantur.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33279)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit XXI de Civ. Dei, *Daemones alliciuntur per varia genera lapidum, herbarum, lignorum, animalium, carminum, rituum, non ut animalia cibis, sed ut spiritus signis*; inquantum scilicet haec eis exhibentur in signum divini honoris, cuius ipsi sunt cupidi.\n\n## Articulus 6\n\n[I q. 115 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33280)\n\nAd sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod corpora caelestia imponant necessitatem iis quae eorum actioni subduntur. Causa enim sufficienti posita, necesse est effectum poni. Sed corpora caelestia sunt sufficiens causa suorum effectuum. Cum igitur corpora caelestia, cum suis motibus et dispositionibus, ponantur sicut ex necessitate entia; videtur quod effectus eorum ex necessitate consequantur.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33281)\n\nPraeterea, effectus agentis ex necessitate sequitur in materia, quando virtus agentis tanta fuerit quod possit sibi subiicere totaliter materiam. Sed tota materia inferiorum corporum subiicitur virtuti caelestium corporum, tanquam excellentiori. Ergo ex necessitate effectus caelestium corporum recipitur in materia corporali.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33282)\n\nPraeterea, si effectus caelestis corporis non ex necessitate proveniat, hoc est propter aliquam causam impedientem. Sed quamlibet causam corpoream quae impedire posset effectum caelestis corporis, necesse est reduci in aliquod caeleste principium, cum caelestia corpora sint causa omnium quae hic fiunt. Ergo, cum et illud caeleste principium sit necessarium, sequitur quod necesse sit impediri effectum alterius corporis caelestis. Et sic omnia quae hic contingunt, ex necessitate eveniunt.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33283)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in libro de Somn. et Vigil., *quod neque eorum quae in corporibus sunt signorum caelestium, velut aquarum et ventorum, inconveniens est multa non evenire*. Sic ergo non omnes effectus caelestium corporum ex necessitate eveniunt.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33284)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ista quaestio partim quidem absoluta est, secundum praemissa; partim autem difficultatem habet. Ostensum enim est quod, quamvis ex impressione corporum caelestium fiant aliquae inclinationes in natura corporali, voluntas tamen non ex necessitate sequitur has inclinationes. Et ideo nihil prohibet per voluntariam actionem impediri effectum caelestium corporum, non solum in ipso homine, sed etiam in aliis rebus ad quas hominum operatio se extendit. Sed nullum tale principium invenitur in rebus naturalibus, quod habeat libertatem sequendi vel non sequendi impressiones caelestes. Unde videtur quod in talibus, ad minus, omnia ex necessitate proveniant, secundum antiquam quorundam rationem, qui, supponentes omne quod est causam habere, et quod, posita causa, ex necessitate ponitur effectus, concludebant, quod omnia ex necessitate contingant. Quam quidem opinionem repellit Aristoteles in VI Metaphys., secundum duo quae ipsi supponunt. Primo enim, non est verum quod, posita quacumque causa, necesse sit effectum poni. Sunt enim quaedam causae quae ordinantur ad suos effectus non ex necessitate, sed ut in pluribus, quae quandoque deficiunt in minori parte. Sed quia huiusmodi causae non deficiunt in minori parte, nisi propter aliquam causam impedientem, videtur adhuc praedictum inconveniens non vitari, quia et ipsum impedimentum talis causae ex necessitate contingit. Et ideo, secundo, oportet dicere quod omne quod est per se, habet causam, quod autem est per accidens, non habet causam, quia non est vere ens, cum non sit vere unum. Album enim causam habet, similiter et musicum; sed album musicum non habet causam, quia non est vere ens, neque vere unum. Manifestum est autem quod causa impediens actionem alicuius causae ordinatae ad suum effectum ut in pluribus, concurrit ei interdum per accidens, unde talis concursus non habet causam, inquantum est per accidens. Et propter hoc, id quod ex tali concursu sequitur, non reducitur in aliquam causam praeexistentem, ex qua ex necessitate sequatur. Sicut quod aliquod corpus terrestre ignitum in superiori parte aeris generetur et deorsum cadat, habet causam aliquam virtutem caelestem, et similiter etiam quod in superficie terrae sit aliqua materia combustibilis, potest reduci in aliquod caeleste principium. Sed quod ignis cadens huic materiae occurrat et comburat eam, non habet causam aliquod caeleste corpus, sed est per accidens. Et sic patet quod non omnes effectus caelestium corporum sunt ex necessitate.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33285)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod corpora caelestia sunt causa inferiorum effectuum mediantibus causis particularibus inferioribus, quae deficere possunt in minori parte.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33286)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod virtus corporis caelestis non est infinita. Unde requirit determinatam dispositionem in materia ad inducendum suum effectum, et quantum ad distantiam loci, et quantum ad alias conditiones. Et ideo sicut distantia loci impedit effectum caelestis corporis (non enim sol eundem caloris effectum habet in Dacia, quem habet in Aethiopia); ita et grossities materiae, vel frigiditas aut caliditas, aut alia huiusmodi dispositio, impedire potest effectum corporis caelestis.\n\n[I q. 115 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33287)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod licet causa impediens effectum alterius causae, reducatur in aliquod caeleste corpus sicut in causam; tamen concursus duarum causarum, cum sit per accidens, non reducitur in causam caelestem, ut dictum est.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0002","text":"[I q. 115 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33231)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0003","text":"Consequenter considerandum est de actione corporalis creaturae; et fato, quod aliquibus corporibus attribuitur. Circa actiones corporales quaeruntur sex. Primo, utrum aliquod corpus sit activum. Secundo, utrum in corporibus sint aliquae seminales rationes. Tertio, utrum corpora caelestia sint causa eorum quae hic per inferiora corpora fiunt. Quarto, utrum sint causa humanorum actuum. Quinto, utrum eorum actionibus Daemones subdantur. Sexto, utrum caelestia corpora imponant necessitatem his quae eorum actionibus subduntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0005","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33232)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nullum corpus sit activum. Dicit enim Augustinus quod *in rebus invenitur aliquid actum et non agens, sicut sunt corpora; aliquid agens et non actum, sicut Deus; aliquid agens et actum, sicut substantiae spirituales*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0007","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33233)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0008","text":"Praeterea, omne agens, excepto primo agente, in suo opere indiget subiecto quod sit susceptibile suae actionis. Sed infra substantiam corporalem non est substantia quae sit susceptibilis suae actionis, quia haec substantia tenet infimum gradum in entibus. Ergo substantia corporalis non est activa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0009","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33234)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0010","text":"Praeterea, omnis substantia corporalis concluditur quantitate. Sed quantitas impedit substantiam a motu et actione, quia comprehendit eam, et mergitur in ea, sicut impeditur aer nubilosus a perceptione luminis. Et huius signum est, quod quanto magis accreverit quantitas corporis, tanto est ponderosius et gravius ad hoc quod moveatur. Ergo nulla substantia corporalis est activa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0011","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33235)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0012","text":"Praeterea, omne agens habet virtutem agendi ex propinquitate ad primum activum. Sed a primo activo, quod est simplicissimum, remotissima sunt corpora, quae sunt maxime composita. Ergo nullum corpus est agens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0013","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33236)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0014","text":"Praeterea, si aliquod corpus est agens, aut agit ad formam substantialem; aut ad formam accidentalem. Sed non ad formam substantialem, quia non invenitur in corporibus principium actionis nisi aliqua qualitas activa, quae est accidens; accidens autem non potest esse causa formae substantialis, cum causa sit potior quam effectus. Similiter etiam neque ad formam accidentalem, *quia accidens non se extendit ultra suum subiectum*, ut Augustinus dicit IX de Trin. Ergo nullum corpus est activum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0015","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33237)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0016","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius, XV cap. Cael. Hier., inter ceteras proprietates corporei ignis, dicit quod *ad susceptas materias manifestat sui ipsius magnitudinem activus et potens*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0017","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33238)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0018","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod sensibiliter apparet aliqua corpora esse activa. Sed circa corporum actiones tripliciter aliqui erraverunt. Fuerunt enim aliqui qui totaliter corporibus actiones subtraxerunt. Et haec est opinio Avicebron in libro fontis vitae, ubi per rationes quae tactae sunt, probare nititur quod nullum corpus agit, sed omnes actiones quae videntur esse corporum, sunt actiones cuiusdam virtutis spiritualis quae penetrat per omnia corpora; ita quod ignis, secundum eum, non calefacit, sed virtus spiritualis penetrans per ipsum. Et videtur haec opinio derivata esse ab opinione Platonis. Nam Plato posuit omnes formas quae sunt in materia corporali, esse participatas et determinatas et contractas ad hanc materiam; formas vero separatas esse absolutas et quasi universales; et ideo illas formas separatas dicebat esse causas formarum quae sunt in materia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0019","text":"Secundum hoc ergo quod forma quae est in materia corporali, determinata est ad hanc materiam individuatam per quantitatem, ponebat Avicebron quod a quantitate, prout est individuationis principium, retinetur et arcetur forma corporalis, ne possit se extendere per actionem in aliam materiam; sed solum forma spiritualis et immaterialis, quae non est coarctata per quantitatem, potest effluere per actionem in aliud. Sed ista ratio non concludit quod forma corporalis non sit agens, sed quod non sit agens universale. Secundum enim quod participatur aliquid, secundum hoc est necessarium quod participetur id quod est proprium ei, sicut quantum participatur de lumine, tantum participatur de ratione visibilis. Agere autem, quod nihil est aliud quam facere aliquid actu, est per se proprium actus, inquantum est actus, unde et omne agens agit sibi simile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0020","text":"Sic ergo ex hoc quod aliquid est forma non determinata per materiam quantitati subiectam, habet quod sit agens indeterminatum et universale, ex hoc vero quod est determinata ad hanc materiam, habet quod sit agens contractum et particulare. Unde si esset forma ignis separata, ut Platonici posuerunt, esset aliquo modo causa omnis ignitionis. Sed haec forma ignis quae est in hac materia corporali, est causa huius ignitionis quae est ab hoc corpore in hoc corpus. Unde et fit talis actio per contactum duorum corporum. Sed tamen haec opinio Avicebron superexcedit opinionem Platonis. Nam Plato ponebat solum formas substantiales separatas; accidentia vero reducebat ad principia materialia quae sunt magnum et parvum, quae ponebat esse prima contraria, sicut et alii rarum et densum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0021","text":"Et ideo tam Plato quam Avicenna, in aliquo ipsum sequens, ponebant quod agentia corporalia agunt secundum formas accidentales, disponendo materiam ad formam substantialem; sed ultima perfectio, quae est per introductionem formae substantialis est a principio immateriali. Et haec est secunda opinio de actione corporum, de qua supra dictum est, cum de creatione ageretur. Tertia vero opinio fuit Democriti, qui ponebat actionem esse per effluxionem atomorum a corpore agente, et passionem esse per receptionem eorundem in poris corporis patientis. Quam opinionem improbat Aristoteles in I de Generat. Sequeretur enim quod corpus non pateretur per totum, et quod quantitas corporis agentis diminueretur ex hoc quod agit, quae sunt manifeste falsa. Dicendum est ergo quod corpus agit secundum quod est actu, in aliud corpus secundum quod est in potentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0022","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33239)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0023","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod dictum Augustini est intelligendum de tota natura corporali simul accepta, quae non habet aliquam inferiorem naturam infra se, in quam agat, sicut natura spiritualis in corporalem, et natura increata in creatam. Sed tamen unum corpus est infra alterum, inquantum est in potentia ad id quod habet aliud in actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0024","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33240)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0025","text":"Et per hoc patet solutio ad secundum. Sciendum est tamen quod, cum Avicebron sic argumentatur, est aliquid quod est movens non motum, scilicet primus factor rerum, ergo, ex opposito, est aliquid quod est motum et patiens tantum, quod concedendum est. Sed hoc est materia prima, quae est potentia pura, sicut Deus est actus purus. Corpus autem componitur ex potentia et actu, et ideo est agens et patiens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0026","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33241)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0027","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod quantitas non impedit formam corporalem omnino ab actione, sicut dictum est, sed impedit eam ne sit agens universale, inquantum forma individuatur prout est in materia quantitati subiecta. Signum tamen quod inducitur de ponderositate corporum, non est ad propositum. Primo quidem, quia additio quantitatis non est causa gravitatis; ut probatur in IV de caelo et mundo. Secundo, quia falsum est quod ponderositas facit tardiorem motum, immo quanto aliquid est gravius, tanto magis movetur motu proprio. Tertio, quia actio non fit per motum localem, ut Democritus posuit; sed per hoc quod aliquid reducitur de potentia in actum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0028","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33242)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0029","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod corpus non est id quod maxime distat a Deo, participat enim aliquid de similitudine divini esse, secundum formam quam habet. Sed id quod maxime distat a Deo, est materia prima; quae nullo modo est agens, cum sit in potentia tantum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0030","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33243)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0031","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod corpus agit et ad formam accidentalem, et ad formam substantialem. Qualitas enim activa, ut calor, etsi sit accidens, agit tamen in virtute formae substantialis, sicut eius instrumentum; et ideo potest agere ad formam substantialem; sicut et calor naturalis, inquantum est instrumentum animae, agit ad generationem carnis. Ad accidens vero agit propria virtute. Nec est contra rationem accidentis, quod excedat suum subiectum in agendo, sed quod excedat in essendo, nisi forsan quis imaginetur idem accidens numero defluere ab agente in patiens, sicut Democritus ponebat fieri actionem per defluxum atomorum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0032","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0033","text":"[I q. 115 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33244)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0034","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod in materia corporali non sint aliquae rationes seminales. Ratio enim importat aliquid secundum esse spirituale. Sed in materia corporali non est aliquid spiritualiter, sed materialiter tantum, secundum scilicet modum eius in quo est. Ergo in materia corporali non sunt seminales rationes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0035","text":"[I q. 115 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33245)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0036","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, III de Trin., quod Daemones quaedam opera faciunt adhibendo occultis motibus quaedam semina, quae in elementis cognoscunt. Sed ea quae per motum localem adhibentur, sunt corpora, non rationes. Ergo inconvenienter dicitur quod sunt in corporali materia seminales rationes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0037","text":"[I q. 115 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33246)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0038","text":"Praeterea, semen est principium activum. Sed in materia corporali non est aliquod principium activum, cum materiae non competat agere, ut dictum est. Ergo in materia corporali non sunt seminales rationes.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0039","text":"[I q. 115 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33247)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0040","text":"Praeterea, in materia corporali dicuntur esse quaedam causales rationes, quae videntur sufficere ad rerum productionem. Sed seminales rationes sunt aliae a causalibus, quia praeter seminales rationes fiunt miracula, non autem praeter causales. Ergo inconvenienter dicitur quod seminales rationes sunt in materia corporali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0041","text":"[I q. 115 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33248)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0042","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, III de Trin., *omnium rerum quae corporaliter visibiliterque nascuntur, occulta quaedam semina in istis corporeis mundi huius elementis latent*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0043","text":"[I q. 115 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33249)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0044","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod denominationes consueverunt fieri a perfectiori, ut dicitur in II de anima. In tota autem natura corporea perfectiora sunt corpora viva, unde et ipsum nomen naturae translatum est a rebus viventibus ad omnes res naturales. Nam ipsum nomen naturae, ut philosophus dicit in V Metaphys., primo impositum fuit ad significandum generationem viventium, quae nativitas dicitur, et quia viventia generantur ex principio coniuncto, sicut fructus ex arbore, et foetus ex matre, cui colligatur, consequenter tractum est nomen naturae ad omne principium motus quod est in eo quod movetur. Manifestum est autem quod principium activum et passivum generationis rerum viventium sunt semina ex quibus viventia generantur. Et ideo convenienter Augustinus omnes virtutes activas et passivas quae sunt principia generationum et motuum naturalium, seminales rationes vocat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0045","text":"Huiusmodi autem virtutes activae et passivae in multiplici ordine considerari possunt. Nam primo quidem, ut Augustinus dicit VI super Gen. ad Litt., sunt principaliter et originaliter in ipso verbo Dei, secundum rationes ideales. Secundo vero, sunt in elementis mundi, ubi simul a principio productae sunt, sicut in universalibus causis. Tertio vero modo, sunt in iis quae ex universalibus causis secundum successiones temporum producuntur, sicut in hac planta et in hoc animali, tanquam in particularibus causis. Quarto modo, sunt in seminibus quae ex animalibus et plantis producuntur. Quae iterum comparantur ad alios effectus particulares, sicut primordiales causae universales ad primos effectus productos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0046","text":"[I q. 115 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33250)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0047","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod huiusmodi virtutes activae et passivae rerum naturalium, etsi non possint dici rationes secundum quod sunt in materia corporali; possunt tamen dici rationes per comparationem ad suam originem, secundum quod deducuntur a rationibus idealibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0048","text":"[I q. 115 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33251)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0049","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod huiusmodi virtutes activae vel passivae sunt in aliquibus partibus corporalibus; quae dum adhibentur per motum localem ad aliquos effectus complendos, dicuntur semina adhiberi per Daemones.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0050","text":"[I q. 115 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33252)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0051","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod semen maris est principium activum in generatione animalis. Sed potest etiam dici semen id quod est ex parte feminae, quod est principium passivum. Et sic sub semine comprehendi possunt vires activae et passivae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0052","text":"[I q. 115 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33253)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0053","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod ex verbis Augustini de huiusmodi rationibus seminalibus loquentis, satis accipi potest quod ipsae rationes seminales sunt etiam rationes causales, sicut et semen est quaedam causa, dicit enim in III de Trin., quod *sicut matres gravidae sunt foetibus, sic ipse mundus est gravidus causis nascentium*. Sed tamen rationes ideales possunt dici causales, non autem proprie loquendo seminales, quia semen non est principium separatum, et praeter huiusmodi rationes non fiunt miracula. Similiter etiam neque praeter virtutes passivas creaturae inditas, ut ex ea fieri possit quidquid Deus mandaverit. Sed praeter virtutes activas naturales, et potentias passivas quae ordinantur ad huiusmodi virtutes activas, dicuntur fieri miracula, dum dicitur quod fiunt praeter rationes seminales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0054","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0055","text":"[I q. 115 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33254)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0056","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod corpora caelestia non sint causa eorum quae hic in inferioribus corporibus fiunt. Dicit enim Damascenus, nos autem dicimus quoniam ipsa, scilicet corpora caelestia, *non sunt causa alicuius eorum quae fiunt, neque corruptionis eorum quae corrumpuntur, signa autem sunt magis imbrium et aeris transmutationis*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0057","text":"[I q. 115 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33255)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0058","text":"Praeterea, ad faciendum aliquid, sufficit agens et materia. Sed in istis inferioribus invenitur materia patiens, inveniuntur etiam contraria agentia, scilicet calidum et frigidum et huiusmodi. Ergo non est necessarium, ad causandum ea quae hic inferius fiunt, causalitatem caelestibus corporibus attribuere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0059","text":"[I q. 115 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33256)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0060","text":"Praeterea, agens agit sibi simile. Sed videmus quod omnia quae fiunt hic inferius, fiunt per hoc quod calefiunt et frigidantur, et humectantur et desiccantur, et aliis huiusmodi qualitatibus alterantur, quae non inveniuntur in corporibus caelestibus. Ergo corpora caelestia non sunt causa eorum quae hic fiunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0061","text":"[I q. 115 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33257)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0062","text":"Praeterea, sicut Augustinus dicit V de Civ. Dei, *nihil est magis corporeum quam corporis sexus*. Sed corporis sexus non causatur ex corporibus caelestibus, cuius signum esse videtur, quod duorum geminorum sub una constellatione natorum, unus est masculus et alter est femina. Ergo corpora caelestia non sunt causa rerum corporalium quae hic fiunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0063","text":"[I q. 115 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33258)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0064","text":"Sed contra est quod Augustinus dicit, III de Trin., quod *corpora crassiora et inferiora per subtiliora et potentiora quodam ordine reguntur*. Et Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod *lumen solis ad generationem sensibilium corporum confert, et ad vitam ipsa movet, et nutrit et auget et perficit*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0065","text":"[I q. 115 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33259)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0066","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, cum omnis multitudo ab unitate procedat; quod autem immobile est, uno modo se habet, quod vero movetur, multiformiter, considerandum est, in tota natura, quod omnis motus ab immobili procedit. Et ideo quanto aliqua sunt immobiliora, tanto sunt magis causa eorum quae sunt magis mobilia. Corpora autem caelestia sunt inter alia corpora magis immobilia, non enim moventur nisi motu locali. Et ideo motus horum inferiorum corporum qui sunt varii et multiformes, reducuntur in motum corporis caelestis, sicut in causam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0067","text":"[I q. 115 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33260)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0068","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod dictum Damasceni intelligendum est, quod corpora caelestia non sunt prima causa generationis et corruptionis eorum quae hic fiunt; sicut dicebant illi qui ponebant corpora caelestia esse deos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0069","text":"[I q. 115 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33261)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0070","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod principia activa in istis inferioribus corporibus non inveniuntur nisi qualitates activae elementorum, quae sunt calidum et frigidum et huiusmodi. Et si sic esset quod formae substantiales inferiorum corporum non diversificarentur nisi secundum huiusmodi accidentia, quorum principia rarum et densum antiqui naturales posuerunt; non oporteret super haec inferiora corpora aliquod principium activum ponere, sed ipsa sufficerent ad agendum. Sed recte considerantibus apparet quod huiusmodi accidentia se habent sicut materiales dispositiones ad formas substantiales naturalium corporum. Materia autem non sufficit ad agendum. Et ideo oportet super has materiales dispositiones ponere aliquod principium activum. Unde Platonici posuerunt species separatas, secundum quarum participationem inferiora corpora substantiales formas consequuntur. Sed hoc non videtur sufficere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0071","text":"Quia species separatae semper eodem modo se haberent, cum ponantur immobiles, et sic sequeretur quod non esset aliqua variatio circa generationem et corruptionem inferiorum corporum; quod patet esse falsum. Unde secundum philosophum, in II de Gen., necesse est ponere aliquod principium activum mobile, quod per sui praesentiam et absentiam causet varietatem circa generationem et corruptionem inferiorum corporum. Et huiusmodi sunt corpora caelestia. Et ideo quidquid in istis inferioribus generat, movet ad speciem sicut instrumentum caelestis corporis; secundum quod dicitur in II Physic., quod *homo generat hominem, et sol*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0072","text":"[I q. 115 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33262)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0073","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod corpora caelestia inferioribus corporibus non sunt similia similitudine speciei; sed inquantum sua universali virtute continent in se quidquid in inferioribus corporibus generatur; secundum quem modum dicimus etiam omnia esse Deo similia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0074","text":"[I q. 115 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33263)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0075","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod actiones corporum caelestium diversimode recipiuntur in inferioribus corporibus secundum diversam materiae dispositionem. Contingit autem quandoque quod materia conceptus humani non est disposita totaliter ad masculinum sexum; unde partim formatur in masculum, partim in feminam. Unde ad hoc introducitur ab Augustino, ad repellendum scilicet divinationem quae fit per astra, quia effectus astrorum variantur etiam in rebus corporeis, secundum diversam materiae dispositionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0076","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0077","text":"[I q. 115 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33264)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0078","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod corpora caelestia sint causa humanorum actuum. Corpora enim caelestia, cum moveantur a spiritualibus substantiis, sicut supra dictum est, agunt in virtute earum quasi instrumenta. Sed illae substantiae spirituales sunt superiores animabus nostris. Ergo videtur quod possint imprimere in animas nostras, et sic causare actus humanos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0079","text":"[I q. 115 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33265)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0080","text":"Praeterea, omne multiforme reducitur in aliquod uniforme principium. Sed actus humani sunt varii et multiformes. Ergo videtur quod reducantur in uniformes motus caelestium corporum, sicut in sua principia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0081","text":"[I q. 115 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33266)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0082","text":"Praeterea, astrologi frequenter vera annuntiant de eventibus bellorum, et aliis humanis actibus, quorum principia sunt intellectus et voluntas. Quod facere non possent secundum caelestia corpora, nisi essent humanorum actuum causa. Sunt ergo corpora caelestia humanorum actuum causa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0083","text":"[I q. 115 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33267)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0084","text":"Sed contra est quod Damascenus dicit, quod *corpora caelestia humanorum actuum nequaquam sunt causa*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0085","text":"[I q. 115 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33268)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0086","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod corpora caelestia in corpora quidem imprimunt directe et per se, sicut iam dictum est. In vires autem animae quae sunt actus organorum corporeorum, directe quidem, sed per accidens, quia necesse est huiusmodi actus harum potentiarum impediri secundum impedimenta organorum, sicut oculus turbatus non bene videt. Unde si intellectus et voluntas essent vires corporeis organis alligatae, sicut posuerunt aliqui, dicentes quod intellectus non differt a sensu; ex necessitate sequeretur quod corpora caelestia essent causa electionum et actuum humanorum. Et ex hoc sequeretur quod homo naturali instinctu ageretur ad suas actiones, sicut cetera animalia, in quibus non sunt nisi vires animae corporeis organis alligatae, nam illud quod fit in istis inferioribus ex impressione corporum caelestium, naturaliter agitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0087","text":"Et ita sequeretur quod homo non esset liberi arbitrii, sed haberet actiones determinatas, sicut et ceterae res naturales. Quae manifeste sunt falsa, et conversationi humanae contraria. Sciendum est tamen quod indirecte et per accidens impressiones corporum caelestium ad intellectum et voluntatem pertingere possunt; inquantum scilicet tam intellectus quam voluntas aliquo modo ab inferioribus viribus accipiunt, quae organis corporeis alligantur. Sed circa hoc diversimode se habent intellectus et voluntas. Nam intellectus ex necessitate accipit ab inferioribus viribus apprehensivis, unde turbata vi imaginativa vel cogitativa vel memorativa, ex necessitate turbatur actio intellectus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0088","text":"Sed voluntas non ex necessitate sequitur inclinationem appetitus inferioris, licet enim passiones quae sunt in irascibili et concupiscibili, habeant quandam vim ad inclinandam voluntatem; tamen in potestate voluntatis remanet sequi passiones, vel eas refutare. Et ideo impressio caelestium corporum, secundum quam immutari possunt inferiores vires, minus pertingit ad voluntatem, quae est proxima causa humanorum actuum, quam ad intellectum. Ponere igitur caelestia corpora esse causam humanorum actuum, est proprium illorum qui dicunt intellectum non differre a sensu. Unde quidam eorum dicebant quod *talis est voluntas in hominibus, qualem in diem inducit pater virorum deorumque*. Quia ergo constat intellectum et voluntatem non esse actus organorum corporeorum, impossibile est quod corpora caelestia sint causa humanorum actuum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0089","text":"[I q. 115 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33269)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0090","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod spirituales substantiae quae caelestia corpora movent, in corporalia quidem agunt mediantibus caelestibus corporibus, sed in intellectum humanum agunt immediate illuminando. Voluntatem autem immutare non possunt, ut supra habitum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0091","text":"[I q. 115 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33270)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0092","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut multiformitas corporalium motuum reducitur sicut in causam in uniformitatem motuum caelestium; ita multiformitas actuum qui sunt ab intellectu et voluntate, reducitur in principium uniforme quod est intellectus et voluntas divina.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0093","text":"[I q. 115 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33271)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0094","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod plures hominum sequuntur passiones, quae sunt motus sensitivi appetitus, ad quas cooperari possunt corpora caelestia, pauci autem sunt sapientes, qui huiusmodi passionibus resistant. Et ideo astrologi ut in pluribus vera possunt praedicere, et maxime in communi. Non autem in speciali, quia nihil prohibet aliquem hominem per liberum arbitrium passionibus resistere. Unde et ipsi astrologi dicunt quod sapiens homo dominatur astris, inquantum scilicet dominatur suis passionibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0095","text":"## Articulus 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0096","text":"[I q. 115 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33272)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0097","text":"Ad quintum sic proceditur. Videtur quod corpora caelestia possint imprimere in ipsos Daemones. Daemones enim secundum certa augmenta lunae aliquos homines vexant, qui et propter hoc lunatici dicuntur; ut patet Matth. IV et XVII. Sed hoc non esset, nisi corporibus caelestibus subiacerent. Ergo Daemones subiacent actionibus caelestium corporum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0098","text":"[I q. 115 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33273)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0099","text":"Praeterea, necromantici observant certas constellationes ad invocandos Daemones. Non autem per corpora caelestia invocarentur, si non eis subiacerent. Ergo Daemones subiacent actionibus caelestium corporum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0100","text":"[I q. 115 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33274)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0101","text":"Praeterea, corpora caelestia virtuosiora sunt quam corpora inferiora. Sed quibusdam inferioribus corporibus Daemones arcentur, scilicet herbis et lapidibus et animantibus, et quibusdam *sonis certis ac vocibus, et figurationibus atque figmentis*, ut a Porphyrio dictum Augustinus introducit in X de Civ. Dei. Ergo multo magis Daemones subduntur actioni caelestium corporum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0102","text":"[I q. 115 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33275)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0103","text":"Sed contra est quod Daemones sunt superiores ordine naturae quam corpora caelestia. Agens autem est superius patiente, ut Augustinus dicit XII super Gen. ad Litt. Ergo Daemones non subiiciuntur actioni corporum caelestium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0104","text":"[I q. 115 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33276)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0105","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa Daemones fuit triplex opinio. Prima Peripateticorum, qui posuerunt Daemones non esse; sed ea quae attribuuntur Daemonibus, secundum artem necromanticam, fiunt virtute caelestium corporum. Et hoc est quod Augustinus, X de Civ. Dei, introducit dictum a Porphyrio, quod *fabricantur in terra ab hominibus potestates idoneae siderum variis effectibus exequendis*. Sed haec positio est manifeste falsa. Experimento enim scitur multa per Daemones fieri, ad quae nullo modo virtus caelestium corporum sufficeret; puta quod arreptitii loquuntur lingua ignota, quod recitant versus et auctoritates quas nunquam sciverunt, quod necromantici faciunt statuas loqui et moveri, et similia. Ex quibus Platonici moti fuerunt ut ponerent Daemones esse animalia corpore aerea, animo passiva; ut ab Apuleio dictum Augustinus introducit VIII de Civ. Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0106","text":"Et haec est secunda opinio, secundum quam dici posset quod Daemones hoc modo subduntur corporibus caelestibus, sicut et de hominibus dictum est. Sed haec opinio ex superioribus patet esse falsa, dicimus enim Daemones esse substantias intellectuales corporibus non unitas. Unde patet quod non subduntur actioni caelestium corporum, nec per se nec per accidens, nec directe nec indirecte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0107","text":"[I q. 115 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33277)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0108","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod hoc quod Daemones secundum certa augmenta lunae homines vexant, contingit propter duo. Primo quidem, ad hoc quod infament creaturam Dei, scilicet lunam, ut Hieronymus et Chrysostomus dicunt. Secundo quia, cum non possint operari nisi mediantibus naturalibus virtutibus, ut supra dictum est; in suis operibus considerant corporum aptitudines ad effectus intentos. Manifestum est autem quod *cerebrum humidissimum est omnium partium corporis*, ut Aristoteles dicit, et ideo maxime subiicitur operationi lunae, quae ex sua proprietate habet movere humorem. In cerebro autem perficiuntur vires animales, et ideo Daemones secundum certa augmenta lunae perturbant hominis phantasiam, quando considerant cerebrum ad hoc esse dispositum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0109","text":"[I q. 115 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33278)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0110","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod Daemones advocati in certis constellationibus, propter duo veniunt. Primo quidem, ut homines in hunc errorem inducant, quod credant aliquod numen esse in stellis. Secundo, quia considerant secundum aliquas certas constellationes materiam corporalem magis esse dispositam ad effectus pro quibus advocantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0111","text":"[I q. 115 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33279)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0112","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, sicut Augustinus dicit XXI de Civ. Dei, *Daemones alliciuntur per varia genera lapidum, herbarum, lignorum, animalium, carminum, rituum, non ut animalia cibis, sed ut spiritus signis*; inquantum scilicet haec eis exhibentur in signum divini honoris, cuius ipsi sunt cupidi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0113","text":"## Articulus 6","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0114","text":"[I q. 115 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33280)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0115","text":"Ad sextum sic proceditur. Videtur quod corpora caelestia imponant necessitatem iis quae eorum actioni subduntur. Causa enim sufficienti posita, necesse est effectum poni. Sed corpora caelestia sunt sufficiens causa suorum effectuum. Cum igitur corpora caelestia, cum suis motibus et dispositionibus, ponantur sicut ex necessitate entia; videtur quod effectus eorum ex necessitate consequantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0116","text":"[I q. 115 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33281)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0117","text":"Praeterea, effectus agentis ex necessitate sequitur in materia, quando virtus agentis tanta fuerit quod possit sibi subiicere totaliter materiam. Sed tota materia inferiorum corporum subiicitur virtuti caelestium corporum, tanquam excellentiori. Ergo ex necessitate effectus caelestium corporum recipitur in materia corporali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0118","text":"[I q. 115 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33282)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0119","text":"Praeterea, si effectus caelestis corporis non ex necessitate proveniat, hoc est propter aliquam causam impedientem. Sed quamlibet causam corpoream quae impedire posset effectum caelestis corporis, necesse est reduci in aliquod caeleste principium, cum caelestia corpora sint causa omnium quae hic fiunt. Ergo, cum et illud caeleste principium sit necessarium, sequitur quod necesse sit impediri effectum alterius corporis caelestis. Et sic omnia quae hic contingunt, ex necessitate eveniunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0120","text":"[I q. 115 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33283)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0121","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in libro de Somn. et Vigil., *quod neque eorum quae in corporibus sunt signorum caelestium, velut aquarum et ventorum, inconveniens est multa non evenire*. Sic ergo non omnes effectus caelestium corporum ex necessitate eveniunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0122","text":"[I q. 115 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33284)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0123","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ista quaestio partim quidem absoluta est, secundum praemissa; partim autem difficultatem habet. Ostensum enim est quod, quamvis ex impressione corporum caelestium fiant aliquae inclinationes in natura corporali, voluntas tamen non ex necessitate sequitur has inclinationes. Et ideo nihil prohibet per voluntariam actionem impediri effectum caelestium corporum, non solum in ipso homine, sed etiam in aliis rebus ad quas hominum operatio se extendit. Sed nullum tale principium invenitur in rebus naturalibus, quod habeat libertatem sequendi vel non sequendi impressiones caelestes. Unde videtur quod in talibus, ad minus, omnia ex necessitate proveniant, secundum antiquam quorundam rationem, qui, supponentes omne quod est causam habere, et quod, posita causa, ex necessitate ponitur effectus, concludebant, quod omnia ex necessitate contingant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0124","text":"Quam quidem opinionem repellit Aristoteles in VI Metaphys., secundum duo quae ipsi supponunt. Primo enim, non est verum quod, posita quacumque causa, necesse sit effectum poni. Sunt enim quaedam causae quae ordinantur ad suos effectus non ex necessitate, sed ut in pluribus, quae quandoque deficiunt in minori parte. Sed quia huiusmodi causae non deficiunt in minori parte, nisi propter aliquam causam impedientem, videtur adhuc praedictum inconveniens non vitari, quia et ipsum impedimentum talis causae ex necessitate contingit. Et ideo, secundo, oportet dicere quod omne quod est per se, habet causam, quod autem est per accidens, non habet causam, quia non est vere ens, cum non sit vere unum. Album enim causam habet, similiter et musicum; sed album musicum non habet causam, quia non est vere ens, neque vere unum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0125","text":"Manifestum est autem quod causa impediens actionem alicuius causae ordinatae ad suum effectum ut in pluribus, concurrit ei interdum per accidens, unde talis concursus non habet causam, inquantum est per accidens. Et propter hoc, id quod ex tali concursu sequitur, non reducitur in aliquam causam praeexistentem, ex qua ex necessitate sequatur. Sicut quod aliquod corpus terrestre ignitum in superiori parte aeris generetur et deorsum cadat, habet causam aliquam virtutem caelestem, et similiter etiam quod in superficie terrae sit aliqua materia combustibilis, potest reduci in aliquod caeleste principium. Sed quod ignis cadens huic materiae occurrat et comburat eam, non habet causam aliquod caeleste corpus, sed est per accidens. Et sic patet quod non omnes effectus caelestium corporum sunt ex necessitate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0126","text":"[I q. 115 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33285)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0127","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod corpora caelestia sunt causa inferiorum effectuum mediantibus causis particularibus inferioribus, quae deficere possunt in minori parte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0128","text":"[I q. 115 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33286)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0129","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod virtus corporis caelestis non est infinita. Unde requirit determinatam dispositionem in materia ad inducendum suum effectum, et quantum ad distantiam loci, et quantum ad alias conditiones. Et ideo sicut distantia loci impedit effectum caelestis corporis (non enim sol eundem caloris effectum habet in Dacia, quem habet in Aethiopia); ita et grossities materiae, vel frigiditas aut caliditas, aut alia huiusmodi dispositio, impedire potest effectum corporis caelestis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0130","text":"[I q. 115 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33287)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0131","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod licet causa impediens effectum alterius causae, reducatur in aliquod caeleste corpus sicut in causam; tamen concursus duarum causarum, cum sit per accidens, non reducitur in causam caelestem, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"78fe808c0160fc33aceb7a38bae3fb2c0449ae244dab89af4d64d71ddfe175bd","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T154929440366Z_0ec117cb","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 115","source_sha256":"78fe808c0160fc33aceb7a38bae3fb2c0449ae244dab89af4d64d71ddfe175bd","sha256":"97245c1ff231f5f759e05e2377b80e0705dcdbe804173573d78f03a945045974","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:49:29.440394+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0002","text":"[I q. 115 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33231)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the action of corporeal creatures, and fate, which is attributed to certain bodies. Concerning corporeal actions, six questions are asked. First, whether any body is active. Second, whether there are any seminal reasons in bodies. Third, whether the heavenly bodies are causes of those things which occur here through lower bodies. Fourth, whether they are causes of human acts. Fifth, whether demons are subject to their actions. Sixth, whether heavenly bodies impose necessity upon those things which are subject to their actions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0005","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33232)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0006","text":"We proceed to the first as follows. It seems that no body is active. For Augustine says that *among things there is found something acted upon and not acting, as bodies are; something acting and not acted upon, as God is; and something acting and acted upon, as spiritual substances are*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0007","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33233)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0008","text":"Further, every agent, except the first agent, requires in its operation a subject capable of receiving its action. But beneath corporeal substance there is no substance capable of receiving its action, because this substance holds the lowest rank among beings. Therefore corporeal substance is not active.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0009","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33234)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0010","text":"Further, every corporeal substance is enclosed by quantity. But quantity impedes substance from motion and action, because it comprehends it and is immersed in it, just as cloudy air is impeded from the perception of light. And a sign of this is that, the more the quantity of a body has increased, the more weighty and heavy it is for being moved. Therefore no corporeal substance is active.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0011","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33235)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0012","text":"Further, every agent has the power to act from proximity to the first active principle. But bodies, which are most composite, are most remote from the first active principle, which is most simple. Therefore no body is an agent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0013","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33236)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0014","text":"Further, if some body is an agent, it either acts toward a substantial form or toward an accidental form. But not toward a substantial form, because no principle of action is found in bodies except some active quality, which is an accident; but an accident cannot be the cause of a substantial form, since the cause is more eminent than the effect. Likewise, neither does it act toward an accidental form, *because an accident does not extend beyond its subject*, as Augustine says in IX On the Trinity. Therefore no body is active.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0015","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33237)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0016","text":"On the contrary, Dionysius, in chapter XV of the *Celestial Hierarchy*, among the other properties of corporeal fire, says that *in relation to the materials it receives, active and powerful, it manifests the greatness of itself*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0017","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33238)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0018","text":"I answer that it must be said that it is sensibly apparent that certain bodies are active. But concerning the actions of bodies, some have erred in three ways. For there were some who entirely denied actions to bodies. This is the opinion of Avicebron in the book *Fount of Life*, where, by the arguments that have been touched upon, he endeavors to prove that no body acts, but that all actions which appear to be those of bodies are actions of some spiritual power which penetrates through all bodies; so that fire, according to him, does not heat, but rather a spiritual power penetrating through it. And this opinion seems to have been derived from Plato's opinion. For Plato held that all forms which are in corporeal matter are participated, determined, and contracted to this matter; whereas separated forms are absolute and, as it were, universal. Therefore he said that those separated forms are causes of the forms which are in matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0019","text":"Accordingly, because a form which is in corporeal matter is determined to this individuated matter through quantity, Avicebron held that the corporeal form is retained and held back by quantity, insofar as quantity is the principle of individuation, so that it cannot extend itself through action into another matter; but only a spiritual and immaterial form, which is not restricted by quantity, can flow forth through action into another. But this argument does not establish that a corporeal form is not an agent, but that it is not a universal agent. For according as something is participated, so it is necessary that that which is proper to it be participated; just as, insofar as light is participated, so much of the character of the visible is participated. But to act, which is nothing other than to make something actual, belongs per se to act insofar as it is act; whence every agent acts so as to produce something like itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0020","text":"Thus, therefore, from the fact that something is a form not determined by matter subject to quantity, it has that it is an indeterminate and universal agent; but from the fact that it is determined to this matter, it has that it is a contracted and particular agent. Hence, if the form of fire were separate, as the Platonists held, it would in some way be the cause of every ignition. But this form of fire, which is in this corporeal matter, is the cause of this ignition, which is from this body into this body. Hence such action comes about through the contact of two bodies. Yet this opinion of Avicebron exceeds Plato's opinion. For Plato posited only substantial forms as separate; but he reduced accidents to material principles, namely, the great and the small, which he posited as the first contraries, just as others posited the rare and the dense.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0021","text":"And therefore both Plato and Avicenna, following him in some respect, held that corporeal agents act according to accidental forms, disposing matter for substantial form; but the ultimate perfection, which is through the introduction of substantial form, is from an immaterial principle. And this is the second opinion concerning the action of bodies, of which there was discussion above when creation was being treated. The third opinion was that of Democritus, who held that action occurs through the outflow of atoms from the acting body, and passion through the reception of these same atoms in the pores of the patient body. Aristotle refutes this opinion in I *On Generation*. For it would follow that a body would not be affected throughout its whole, and that the quantity of the acting body would be diminished by its acting—both of which are manifestly false. It must therefore be said that a body acts, insofar as it is in act, upon another body insofar as it is in potency.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0022","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33239)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0023","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Augustine's statement is to be understood of the whole corporeal nature taken together, which does not have beneath itself any lower nature upon which it acts, as spiritual nature acts upon corporeal nature, and uncreated nature upon created nature. Yet one body is beneath another, insofar as it is in potency to that which another has in act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0024","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33240)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0025","text":"And through this the answer to the second objection is clear. Yet it should be known that, when Avicebron argues thus: there is something that moves and is not moved, namely the first maker of things; therefore, conversely, there is something that is moved and only undergoes, this must be conceded. But this is prime matter, which is pure potency, just as God is pure act. A body, however, is composed of potency and act, and therefore acts and undergoes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0026","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33241)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0027","text":"To the third, it must be said that quantity does not altogether prevent corporeal form from acting, as has been said, but prevents it from being a universal agent, insofar as form is individuated as it is in matter subject to quantity. Yet the sign introduced concerning the heaviness of bodies is not to the point. First, indeed, because the addition of quantity is not the cause of gravity, as is proved in *On the Heavens and the World*, IV. Second, because it is false that heaviness makes motion slower; indeed, the heavier something is, the more it is moved by its proper motion. Third, because action does not take place through local motion, as Democritus held; but through something's being reduced from potency to act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0028","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33242)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0029","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that a body is not that which is most distant from God, for it participates in some likeness of the divine being, according to the form that it has. But that which is most distant from God is prime matter, which is in no way an agent, since it is in potency only.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0030","text":"[I q. 115 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33243)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0031","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that a body acts both toward an accidental form and toward a substantial form. For an active quality, such as heat, although it is an accident, nevertheless acts by the power of the substantial form, as its instrument; and therefore it can act toward a substantial form; just as natural heat, insofar as it is an instrument of the soul, acts toward the generation of flesh. But it acts toward an accident by its own power. Nor is it contrary to the nature of an accident that it exceed its subject in acting, but that it exceed it in being—unless perhaps someone imagines that the same accident numerically flows from agent into patient, as Democritus held that action took place through the efflux of atoms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0032","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0033","text":"[I q. 115 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33244)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0034","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that there are no seminal reasons in corporeal matter. For a reason implies something according to spiritual being. But in corporeal matter there is nothing spiritually, but only materially, namely according to the mode of that in which it is. Therefore there are no seminal reasons in corporeal matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0035","text":"[I q. 115 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33245)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0036","text":"Moreover, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, III, that demons perform certain works by applying, through hidden motions, certain seeds which they know to be in the elements. But those things which are applied by local motion are bodies, not reasons. Therefore it is unfittingly said that there are seminal reasons in corporeal matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0037","text":"[I q. 115 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33246)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0038","text":"Moreover, seed is an active principle. But there is no active principle in corporeal matter, since acting does not belong to matter, as has been said. Therefore there are no seminal reasons in corporeal matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0039","text":"[I q. 115 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33247)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0040","text":"Moreover, certain causal reasons are said to be in corporeal matter, and these seem sufficient for the production of things. But seminal reasons are distinct from causal reasons, because miracles occur beyond seminal reasons, but not beyond causal reasons. Therefore it is unfittingly said that seminal reasons are in corporeal matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0041","text":"[I q. 115 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33248)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0042","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in *On the Trinity*, III: *Certain hidden seeds of all things that are born corporeally and visibly lie concealed in these corporeal elements of this world*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0043","text":"[I q. 115 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33249)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0044","text":"I answer that denominations are customarily made from what is more perfect, as is said in *On the Soul*, II. Now in the whole of corporeal nature, living bodies are more perfect; hence the very name of nature was transferred from living things to all natural things. For the very name of nature, as the Philosopher says in *Metaphysics*, V, was first imposed to signify the generation of living things, which is called birth; and because living things are generated from a conjoined principle, as fruit from a tree and an offspring from its mother, to whom it is attached, consequently the name of nature was extended to every principle of motion that is in that which is moved. Now it is evident that the active and passive principles of the generation of living things are the seeds from which living things are generated. And therefore Augustine suitably calls all the active and passive powers that are principles of natural generations and motions, seminal reasons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0045","text":"Such active and passive powers, moreover, can be considered in a multiple order. For first, as Augustine says in *The Literal Commentary on Genesis*, VI, they are principally and originally in the Word of God itself, according to ideal reasons. Second, they are in the elements of the world, where they were produced together from the beginning, as in universal causes. Third, they are in those things which are produced from universal causes according to the succession of times, as in this plant and in this animal, as in particular causes. Fourth, they are in the seeds that are produced from animals and plants. These in turn are compared to other particular effects as the primordial universal causes are to the first produced effects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0046","text":"[I q. 115 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33250)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0047","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that such active and passive powers of natural things, although they cannot be called reasons insofar as they are in corporeal matter, can nevertheless be called reasons by comparison with their origin, insofar as they are derived from ideal reasons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0048","text":"[I q. 115 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33251)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0049","text":"To the second, it must be said that powers of this kind, active or passive, are in certain bodily parts; when these are employed by demons through local motion for the completion of certain effects, seeds are said to be employed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0050","text":"[I q. 115 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33252)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0051","text":"To the third, it must be said that the seed of the male is the active principle in the generation of an animal. But that which is on the part of the female, which is the passive principle, can also be called seed. And thus active and passive powers can be included under seed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0052","text":"[I q. 115 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33253)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0053","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that from the words of Augustine speaking of seminal reasons of this kind, it can sufficiently be understood that the seminal reasons themselves are also causal reasons, just as seed too is a certain cause; for he says in book III of *On the Trinity* that *as mothers are pregnant with offspring, so the world itself is pregnant with the causes of things being born*. Yet ideal reasons can be called causal, but, properly speaking, not seminal, because seed is not a separate principle; and apart from reasons of this kind miracles do not occur. Likewise, neither do they occur apart from the passive powers bestowed upon a creature, so that from it may be made whatever God shall command. But miracles are said to occur apart from the active natural powers, and the passive powers that are ordered to such active powers, when they are said to occur apart from seminal reasons.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0054","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0055","text":"[I q. 115 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33254)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0056","text":"The third point is approached thus. It seems that heavenly bodies are not the cause of those things that occur here in lower bodies. For Damascene says: But we say that they, namely the heavenly bodies, *are not the cause of any of the things that occur, nor of the corruption of things that are corrupted, but are rather signs of rains and of a change of the air*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0057","text":"[I q. 115 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33255)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0058","text":"Further, for making something, an agent and matter suffice. But among these lower things passive matter is found, and contrary agents are also found, namely hot and cold and things of this kind. Therefore, in order to cause the things that occur here below, it is not necessary to attribute causality to heavenly bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0059","text":"[I q. 115 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33256)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0060","text":"Further, an agent acts upon what is like itself. But we see that all things that occur here below occur through being heated and cooled, moistened and dried, and altered by other qualities of this kind, which are not found in heavenly bodies. Therefore heavenly bodies are not the cause of the things that occur here.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0061","text":"[I q. 115 a. 3 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33257)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0062","text":"Further, as Augustine says in book V of *The City of God*, *nothing is more bodily than the sex of the body*. But bodily sex is not caused by heavenly bodies, as seems to be indicated by the fact that, of two twins born under one constellation, one is male and the other female. Therefore heavenly bodies are not the cause of bodily things that occur here.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0063","text":"[I q. 115 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33258)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0064","text":"But on the contrary, Augustine says, in book III of *On the Trinity*, that *the grosser and lower bodies are governed in a certain order by the subtler and more powerful*. And Dionysius says, in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, that *the light of the sun contributes to the generation of sensible bodies, and moves them toward life, and nourishes, increases, and perfects them*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0065","text":"[I q. 115 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33259)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0066","text":"I answer that, since every multitude proceeds from unity, and what is immobile is disposed in one way, whereas what is moved is disposed in many ways, it must be considered, throughout the whole of nature, that every motion proceeds from what is immobile. And therefore, the more immobile certain things are, the more they are the cause of things that are more movable. Now heavenly bodies are more immobile among other bodies, for they are moved only by local motion. And therefore the motions of these lower bodies, which are various and manifold, are reduced to the motion of a heavenly body as to their cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0067","text":"[I q. 115 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33260)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0068","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Damascene's statement is to be understood as meaning that heavenly bodies are not the first cause of the generation and corruption of the things that occur here, as those said who held heavenly bodies to be gods.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0069","text":"[I q. 115 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33261)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0070","text":"To the second, it must be said that the active principles in these lower bodies are found to be only the active qualities of the elements, namely hot and cold and things of this kind. And if it were so that the substantial forms of lower bodies were diversified only according to accidents of this kind, the principles of which the ancient natural philosophers posited as rare and dense, it would not be necessary to posit any active principle over and above these lower bodies, but they themselves would suffice for acting. But to those who consider rightly, it is apparent that accidents of this kind stand as material dispositions toward the substantial forms of natural bodies. But matter does not suffice for acting. And therefore it is necessary to posit some active principle above these material dispositions. Hence the Platonists posited separate species, by participation in which lower bodies attain substantial forms. But this does not seem to suffice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0071","text":"For the separate species would always be disposed in the same way, since they are posited as immobile, and thus it would follow that there would be no variation in the generation and corruption of lower bodies; which is plainly false. Hence, according to the Philosopher, in book II of *On Generation*, it is necessary to posit some mobile active principle which, through its presence and absence, causes variation in the generation and corruption of lower bodies. And heavenly bodies are of this kind. And therefore whatever generates among these lower things moves toward the species as an instrument of a heavenly body; according as it is said in book II of the *Physics* that *man generates man, and the sun*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0072","text":"[I q. 115 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33262)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0073","text":"To the third, it must be said that the heavenly bodies are not like the lower bodies by a likeness of species, but insofar as by their universal power they contain within themselves whatever is generated in the lower bodies; in which manner we also say that all things are like God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0074","text":"[I q. 115 a. 3 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33263)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0075","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the actions of heavenly bodies are received in different ways in lower bodies according to the diverse disposition of matter. Now it sometimes happens that the matter of a human conception is not disposed entirely toward the male sex; hence it is formed partly as male, partly as female. Hence Augustine introduces this for the purpose, namely, of repelling divination that is carried out through the stars, because the effects of the stars vary even in corporeal things according to the diverse disposition of matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0076","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0077","text":"[I q. 115 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33264)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0078","text":"The fourth is approached thus. It seems that heavenly bodies are the cause of human acts. For heavenly bodies, since they are moved by spiritual substances, as was said above, act by the power of those substances as instruments. But those spiritual substances are superior to our souls. Therefore, it seems that they can make an impression upon our souls, and thus cause human acts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0079","text":"[I q. 115 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33265)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0080","text":"Further, everything manifold is reduced to some uniform principle. But human acts are varied and manifold. Therefore, it seems that they are reduced to the uniform motions of heavenly bodies as to their principles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0081","text":"[I q. 115 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33266)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0082","text":"Further, astrologers frequently announce truly concerning the outcomes of wars and other human acts, whose principles are intellect and will. They could not do this on the basis of heavenly bodies unless the latter were causes of human acts. Therefore, heavenly bodies are causes of human acts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0083","text":"[I q. 115 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33267)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0084","text":"But on the contrary, Damascene says that *heavenly bodies are by no means causes of human acts*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0085","text":"[I q. 115 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33268)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0086","text":"I answer that it must be said that heavenly bodies indeed make an impression directly and per se upon bodies, as has already been said. But upon the powers of the soul which are acts of bodily organs, they act directly indeed, but per accidens, because it is necessary that the acts of such powers be impeded according to the impediments of the organs, just as a disturbed eye does not see well. Hence, if intellect and will were powers bound to bodily organs, as some have held, saying that intellect does not differ from sense, it would follow of necessity that heavenly bodies were the cause of human choices and acts. And from this it would follow that a human being would be moved to his actions by natural instinct, like the other animals, in which there are only powers of the soul bound to bodily organs; for that which occurs in these lower things from the impression of heavenly bodies occurs naturally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0087","text":"And thus it would follow that man would not possess free choice, but would have determined actions, just as other natural things do. These consequences are manifestly false and contrary to human life. Yet it should be known that, indirectly and per accidens, impressions of heavenly bodies can reach intellect and will, namely insofar as both intellect and will in some way receive from the lower powers, which are bound to bodily organs. But intellect and will are differently situated in this respect. For intellect receives from the lower apprehensive powers of necessity; hence, when the imaginative, cogitative, or memorative power is disturbed, the action of the intellect is necessarily disturbed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0088","text":"But the will does not of necessity follow the inclination of the lower appetite. For although the passions that are in the irascible and concupiscible powers have a certain force for inclining the will, it nevertheless remains within the power of the will to follow the passions or to reject them. And therefore the impression of heavenly bodies, according to which the lower powers can be changed, reaches the will—which is the proximate cause of human acts—less than it reaches the intellect. Therefore, to maintain that heavenly bodies are the cause of human acts is proper to those who say that intellect does not differ from sense. Hence some of them said that *such is the will in men as the father of men and gods brings on each day*. Therefore, since it is clear that intellect and will are not acts of bodily organs, it is impossible that heavenly bodies be the cause of human acts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0089","text":"[I q. 115 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33269)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0090","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the spiritual substances that move the heavenly bodies act upon corporeal things by means of heavenly bodies, but they act immediately upon the human intellect by illuminating it. But they cannot change the will, as was established above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0091","text":"[I q. 115 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33270)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0092","text":"To the second, it must be said that, just as the manifoldness of bodily motions is reduced, as to its cause, to the uniformity of heavenly motions, so the manifoldness of acts that proceed from intellect and will is reduced to the uniform principle that is the divine intellect and will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0093","text":"[I q. 115 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33271)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0094","text":"To the third, it must be said that most human beings follow the passions, which are motions of the sensitive appetite, with which heavenly bodies can cooperate; but few are wise, who resist passions of this kind. And therefore astrologers can predict truth in most cases, and especially in general matters. But not in particular cases, because nothing prevents a person from resisting the passions through free choice. Hence astrologers themselves say that a wise man rules the stars, insofar as he rules his passions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0095","text":"## Article 5","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0096","text":"[I q. 115 a. 5 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33272)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0097","text":"We proceed thus to the fifth article. It seems that the heavenly bodies can make an impression upon the demons themselves. For demons torment certain men according to certain increases of the moon, and for this reason they are called lunatics; as is clear from Matt. IV and XVII. But this would not be so unless they were subject to the heavenly bodies. Therefore demons are subject to the actions of the heavenly bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0098","text":"[I q. 115 a. 5 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33273)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0099","text":"Further, necromancers observe certain constellations for invoking demons. But they would not be invoked through the heavenly bodies if they were not subject to them. Therefore demons are subject to the actions of the heavenly bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0100","text":"[I q. 115 a. 5 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33274)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0101","text":"Further, heavenly bodies are more powerful than lower bodies. But demons are warded off by certain lower bodies, namely, herbs and stones and animals, and by certain *definite sounds and words, and configurations and fabrications*, as Augustine reports Porphyry as having said in X of the City of God. Therefore, much more are demons subject to the action of heavenly bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0102","text":"[I q. 115 a. 5 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33275)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0103","text":"On the contrary, demons are higher in the order of nature than heavenly bodies. But an agent is higher than that which suffers, as Augustine says in XII, On the Literal Meaning of Genesis. Therefore demons are not subject to the action of heavenly bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0104","text":"[I q. 115 a. 5 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33276)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0105","text":"I answer that there were three opinions concerning demons. The first was that of the Peripatetics, who held that demons do not exist; rather, those things which are attributed to demons according to the art of necromancy are done by the power of heavenly bodies. And this is what Augustine, in X of the City of God, reports Porphyry as having said: that *powers fit for carrying out the various effects of the stars are fashioned on earth by men*. But this position is manifestly false. For experience shows that many things are done by demons, for which the power of heavenly bodies would in no way suffice; for example, that possessed persons speak an unknown language, that they recite verses and authorities which they have never known, that necromancers make statues speak and move, and the like. Moved by these facts, the Platonists held that demons are animals with an aerial body and a passive soul, as Augustine reports Apuleius as having said in VIII of the City of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0106","text":"And this is the second opinion, according to which it could be said that demons are subject to heavenly bodies in this way, just as was said concerning men. But this opinion is shown by what has been said above to be false; for we say that demons are intellectual substances not united to bodies. Hence it is clear that they are not subject to the action of heavenly bodies, neither per se nor per accidens, neither directly nor indirectly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0107","text":"[I q. 115 a. 5 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33277)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0108","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that demons torment men according to certain increases of the moon for two reasons. First, in order that they may defame God's creature, namely the moon, as Jerome and Chrysostom say. Second, because, since they cannot act except through natural powers, as was said above, in their works they consider the aptitudes of bodies for the intended effects. Now it is evident that *the brain is the moistest of all the parts of the body*, as Aristotle says, and therefore it is especially subject to the operation of the moon, which by its property has the power to move moisture. But the animal powers are perfected in the brain; and therefore demons, according to certain increases of the moon, disturb a man's imagination when they find the brain disposed for this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0109","text":"[I q. 115 a. 5 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33278)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0110","text":"To the second it must be said that demons summoned under certain constellations come for two reasons. First, in order to lead men into this error, that they may believe there is some divinity in the stars. Second, because they observe that, under certain constellations, bodily matter is more disposed toward the effects for which they are summoned.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0111","text":"[I q. 115 a. 5 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33279)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0112","text":"To the third it must be said that, as Augustine says in XXI of the City of God, *demons are enticed by various kinds of stones, herbs, woods, animals, incantations, rites, not as animals by food, but as spirits by signs*; namely, insofar as these are offered to them as a sign of divine honor, of which they themselves are desirous.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0113","text":"## Article 6","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0114","text":"[I q. 115 a. 6 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33280)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0115","text":"We proceed thus to the sixth article. It seems that heavenly bodies impose necessity upon those things that are subject to their action. For, once a sufficient cause has been posited, the effect must be posited. But heavenly bodies are a sufficient cause of their effects. Therefore, since heavenly bodies, together with their motions and dispositions, are posited as existing of necessity, it seems that their effects follow of necessity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0116","text":"[I q. 115 a. 6 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33281)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0117","text":"Further, the effect of an agent follows of necessity in matter when the power of the agent is so great that it can totally subject matter to itself. But all the matter of lower bodies is subject to the power of heavenly bodies, as to a more excellent power. Therefore the effect of heavenly bodies is received of necessity in bodily matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0118","text":"[I q. 115 a. 6 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33282)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0119","text":"Further, if the effect of a heavenly body does not result of necessity, this is because of some impeding cause. But every bodily cause that could impede the effect of a heavenly body must be reduced to some heavenly principle, since heavenly bodies are the cause of all things that happen here. Therefore, since that heavenly principle too is necessary, it follows that the effect of another heavenly body must necessarily be impeded. And thus all things that occur here happen of necessity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0120","text":"[I q. 115 a. 6 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33283)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0121","text":"On the contrary, the Philosopher says, in the book On Sleep and Waking, “that it is not unfitting that many of the celestial signs of things existing in bodies, such as waters and winds, should not come to pass.” Therefore not all effects of heavenly bodies occur of necessity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0122","text":"[I q. 115 a. 6 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33284)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0123","text":"I answer that this question is indeed partly settled by what has been said, but partly it presents a difficulty. For it has been shown that, although certain inclinations in corporeal nature arise from the influence of heavenly bodies, the will nevertheless does not follow these inclinations of necessity. And therefore nothing prevents the effect of heavenly bodies from being impeded by voluntary action, not only in man himself, but also in other things to which human activity extends. But in natural things no such principle is found that has freedom to follow or not to follow celestial influences. Hence it seems that, at least in such things, all things happen of necessity, according to the ancient reasoning of certain persons who, supposing that everything which exists has a cause, and that, when the cause is posited, the effect is posited of necessity, concluded that all things happen of necessity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0124","text":"Aristotle rejects this opinion in Metaphysics VI, on the basis of two points which they assume. First, it is not true that, once any cause whatsoever is posited, the effect must be posited. For some causes are ordered to their effects not by necessity, but for the most part; and these sometimes fail in the lesser part. But since causes of this kind fail in the lesser part only because of some impeding cause, it still seems that the aforesaid difficulty is not avoided, because the very impediment of such a cause happens of necessity. And therefore, secondly, one must say that everything which is per se has a cause, whereas what is per accidens has no cause, because it is not truly a being, since it is not truly one. For white has a cause, and likewise musical; but white-musical has no cause, because it is not truly a being, nor truly one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0125","text":"Now it is evident that a cause impeding the action of some cause ordered to its effect for the most part sometimes coincides with it per accidens; hence such a concurrence has no cause, insofar as it is per accidens. And on this account, that which follows from such a concurrence is not reduced to any pre-existing cause from which it follows of necessity. Thus, that some earthly body be ignited in the upper part of the air and fall downward has as its cause some celestial power; and likewise, that there be some combustible matter on the surface of the earth can be reduced to some celestial principle. But that the falling fire encounter this matter and burn it has no heavenly body as its cause, but is per accidens. Thus it is clear that not all effects of heavenly bodies are from necessity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0126","text":"[I q. 115 a. 6 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33285)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0127","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that heavenly bodies are the cause of lower effects through particular lower causes, which can fail in the lesser part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0128","text":"[I q. 115 a. 6 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33286)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0129","text":"To the second, it must be said that the power of a heavenly body is not infinite. Hence it requires a determinate disposition in matter in order to produce its effect, both with respect to distance of place and with respect to other conditions. And therefore, just as distance of place impedes the effect of a heavenly body—for the sun does not have the same effect of heat in Dacia as it has in Ethiopia—so too the density of matter, or coldness or heat, or some other such disposition, can impede the effect of a heavenly body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0130","text":"[I q. 115 a. 6 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33287)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__107:P0131","text":"To the third, it must be said that although a cause impeding the effect of another cause is reduced to some heavenly body as to a cause, nevertheless the concurrence of the two causes, since it is per accidens, is not reduced to a celestial cause, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__107:P0131"]}],"source_digest":"bb758163b00eae8fbd1b6b12a5ef88ee27cda1a8e5cda62718f4ca1d0abac117","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 115"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 115","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108","title":"First Part · Question 116","order":117,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC__108","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 116 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33288)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de fato. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, an fatum sit. Secundo, in quo sit. Tertio, utrum sit immobile. Quarto, utrum omnia subsint fato.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 116 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33289)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod fatum nihil sit. Dicit enim Gregorius, in homilia Epiphaniae, *absit a fidelium cordibus ut fatum esse aliquid dicant*.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33290)\n\nPraeterea, ea quae fato aguntur, non sunt improvisa, quia, ut Augustinus dicit V de Civ. Dei, *fatum a fando dictum intelligimus, idest a loquendo*; ut ea fato fieri dicantur, quae ab aliquo determinante sunt ante praelocuta. Quae autem sunt provisa, non sunt fortuita neque casualia. Si igitur res fato aguntur, excludetur casus et fortuna a rebus.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33291)\n\nSed contra quod non est, non definitur. Sed Boetius, in IV de Consol., definit fatum, dicens quod *fatum est inhaerens rebus mobilibus dispositio, per quam providentia suis quaeque nectit ordinibus*. Ergo fatum aliquid est.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33292)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod in rebus inferioribus videntur quaedam a fortuna vel casu provenire. Contingit autem quandoque quod aliquid, ad inferiores causas relatum, est fortuitum vel casuale, quod tamen, relatum ad causam aliquam superiorem, invenitur esse per se intentum. Sicut si duo servi alicuius domini mittantur ab eo ad eundem locum, uno de altero ignorante; concursus duorum servorum, si ad ipsos servos referatur, casualis est, quia accidit praeter utriusque intentionem; si autem referatur ad dominum, qui hoc praeordinavit, non est casuale, sed per se intentum. Fuerunt igitur aliqui qui huiusmodi casualia et fortuita, quae in his inferioribus accidunt, in nullam superiorem causam reducere voluerunt. Et hi fatum et providentiam negaverunt; ut de Tullio Augustinus recitat in V de Civ. Dei. Quod est contra ea quae superius de providentia dicta sunt. Quidam vero omnia fortuita et casualia quae in istis inferioribus accidunt, sive in rebus naturalibus sive in rebus humanis, reducere voluerunt in superiorem causam, idest in caelestia corpora. Et secundum hos, fatum nihil aliud est quam *dispositio siderum in qua quisque conceptus est vel natus*. Sed hoc stare non potest, propter duo. Primo quidem, quantum ad res humanas. Quia iam ostensum est quod humani actus non subduntur actioni caelestium corporum, nisi per accidens et indirecte. Causa autem fatalis, cum habeat ordinationem super ea quae fato aguntur, necesse est quod sit directe et per se causa eius quod agitur. Secundo, quantum ad omnia quae per accidens aguntur. Dictum est enim supra quod id quod est per accidens, non est proprie ens neque unum. Omnis autem naturae actio terminatur ad aliquid unum. Unde impossibile est quod id quod est per accidens, sit effectus per se alicuius naturalis principii agentis. Nulla ergo natura per se hoc facere potest, quod intendens fodere sepulcrum, inveniat thesaurum. Manifestum est autem quod corpus caeleste agit per modum naturalis principii, unde et effectus eius in hoc mundo sunt naturales. Impossibile est ergo quod aliqua virtus activa caelestis corporis sit causa eorum quae hic aguntur per accidens, sive a casu sive a fortuna. Et ideo dicendum est quod ea quae hic per accidens aguntur, sive in rebus naturalibus sive in rebus humanis, reducuntur in aliquam causam praeordinantem, quae est providentia divina. Quia nihil prohibet id quod est per accidens, accipi ut unum ab aliquo intellectu, alioquin intellectus formare non posset hanc propositionem, fodiens sepulcrum invenit thesaurum. Et sicut hoc potest intellectus apprehendere, ita potest efficere, sicut si aliquis sciens in quo loco sit thesaurus absconditus, instiget aliquem rusticum hoc ignorantem, ut ibi fodiat sepulcrum. Et sic nihil prohibet ea quae hic per accidens aguntur, ut fortuita vel casualia, reduci in aliquam causam ordinantem, quae per intellectum agat; et praecipue intellectum divinum. Nam solus Deus potest voluntatem immutare, ut supra habitum est. Et per consequens ordinatio humanorum actuum, quorum principium est voluntas, soli Deo attribui debet. Sic igitur inquantum omnia quae hic aguntur, divinae providentiae subduntur, tanquam per eam praeordinata et quasi praelocuta, fatum ponere possumus, licet hoc nomine sancti doctores uti recusaverint, propter eos qui ad vim positionis siderum hoc nomen retorquebant. Unde Augustinus dicit, in V de Civ. Dei, *si propterea quisquam res humanas fato tribuit, quia ipsam Dei voluntatem vel potestatem fati nomine appellat, sententiam teneat, linguam corrigat*. Et sic etiam Gregorius fatum esse negat.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33293)\n\nUnde patet solutio ad primum.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33294)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod nihil prohibet aliqua esse fortuita vel casualia per comparationem ad causas proximas, non tamen per comparationem ad divinam providentiam, sic enim nihil temere fit in mundo, ut Augustinus dicit in libro octoginta trium quaest.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 116 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33295)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod fatum non sit in rebus creatis. Dicit enim Augustinus, V de Civ. Dei, quod *ipsa Dei voluntas vel potestas fati nomine appellatur*. Sed voluntas et potestas Dei non est in creaturis, sed in Deo. Ergo fatum non est in rebus creatis, sed in Deo.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33296)\n\nPraeterea, fatum comparatur ad ea quae ex fato aguntur, ut causa; ut ipse modus loquendi ostendit. Sed causa universalis per se eorum quae hic per accidens aguntur, est solus Deus, ut supra dictum est. Ergo fatum est in Deo, et non in rebus creatis.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33297)\n\nPraeterea, si fatum est in creaturis, aut est substantia, aut accidens, et quodcumque horum detur, oportet quod multiplicetur secundum creaturarum multitudinem. Cum ergo fatum videatur esse unum tantum, videtur quod fatum non sit in creaturis, sed in Deo.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33298)\n\nSed contra est quod Boetius dicit, in IV de Consol. quod *fatum est dispositio rebus mobilibus inhaerens*.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33299)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut ex praedictis patet, divina providentia per causas medias suos effectus exequitur. Potest ergo ipsa ordinatio effectuum dupliciter considerari. Uno modo, secundum quod est in ipso Deo, et sic ipsa ordinatio effectuum vocatur providentia. Secundum vero quod praedicta ordinatio consideratur in mediis causis a Deo ordinatis ad aliquos effectus producendos, sic habet rationem fati. Et hoc est quod Boetius dicit, IV de Consol., *sive famulantibus quibusdam providentiae divinae spiritibus fatum exercetur; seu anima, seu tota inserviente natura, sive caelestibus siderum motibus, seu angelica virtute, seu Daemonum varia solertia, seu aliquibus eorum, seu omnibus, fatalis series texitur*, de quibus omnibus per singula in praecedentibus dictum est. Sic ergo est manifestum quod fatum est in ipsis causis creatis, inquantum sunt ordinatae a Deo ad effectus producendos.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33300)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod ipsa ordinatio causarum secundarum, quam Augustinus seriem causarum nominat, non habet rationem fati, nisi secundum quod dependet a Deo. Et ideo causaliter Dei potestas vel voluntas dici potest fatum. Essentialiter vero fatum est ipsa dispositio seu series, idest ordo, causarum secundarum.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33301)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod intantum fatum habet rationem causae, inquantum et ipsae causae secundae, quarum ordinatio fatum vocatur.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33302)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod fatum dicitur dispositio, non quae est in genere qualitatis; sed secundum quod dispositio designat ordinem, qui non est substantia, sed relatio. Qui quidem ordo, si consideretur per comparationem ad suum principium, est unus, et sic dicitur unum fatum. Si autem consideretur per comparationem ad effectus, vel ad ipsas causas medias, sic multiplicatur, per quem modum poeta dixit, *te tua fata trahunt*.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 116 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33303)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod fatum non sit immobile. Dicit enim Boetius, in IV de Consol., *uti est ad intellectum ratiocinatio, ad id quod est id quod gignitur, ad aeternitatem tempus, ad punctum medium circulus; ita est fati series mobilis ad providentiae stabilem simplicitatem*.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33304)\n\nPraeterea, sicut philosophus dicit in II Topic., *motis nobis, moventur ea quae in nobis sunt. Sed fatum est dispositio inhaerens rebus mobilibus*, ut Boetius dicit. Ergo fatum est mobile.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33305)\n\nPraeterea, si fatum est immobile, ea quae subduntur fato, immobiliter et ex necessitate eveniunt. Sed talia maxime videntur esse contingentia, quae fato attribuuntur. Ergo nihil erit contingens in rebus, sed omnia ex necessitate evenient.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33306)\n\nSed contra est quod Boetius dicit, quod fatum est immobilis dispositio.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33307)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod dispositio secundarum causarum, quam fatum dicimus, potest dupliciter considerari, uno modo, secundum ipsas causas secundas, quae sic disponuntur seu ordinantur; alio modo, per relationem ad primum principium a quo ordinantur, scilicet Deum. Quidam ergo posuerunt ipsam seriem seu dispositionem causarum esse secundum se necessariam, ita quod omnia ex necessitate contingerent; propter hoc, quod quilibet effectus habet causam, et causa posita necesse est effectum poni. Sed hoc patet esse falsum, per ea quae supra dicta sunt. Alii vero e contrario posuerunt fatum esse mobile, etiam secundum quod a divina providentia dependet. Unde Aegyptii dicebant quibusdam sacrificiis fatum posse mutari, ut Gregorius Nyssenus dicit. Sed hoc supra exclusum est, quia immobilitati divinae providentiae repugnat. Et ideo dicendum est quod fatum, secundum considerationem secundarum causarum, mobile est, sed secundum quod subest divinae providentiae, immobilitatem sortitur, non quidem absolutae necessitatis, sed conditionatae; secundum quod dicimus hanc conditionalem esse veram vel necessariam, si Deus praescivit hoc futurum, erit. Unde cum Boetius dixisset fati seriem esse mobilem, post pauca subdit, *quae cum ab immobilis providentiae proficiscatur exordiis, ipsam quoque immutabilem esse necesse est*.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 3 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33308)\n\nEt per hoc patet responsio ad obiecta.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 116 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33309)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod omnia fato subdantur. Dicit enim Boetius, in IV de Consol., *series fati caelum et sidera movet, elementa in se invicem temperat, et alterna format transmutatione; eadem nascentia occidentiaque omnia per similes foetuum seminumque renovat progressus; haec actus fortunasque hominum indissolubili causarum connexione constringit*. Nihil ergo excipi videtur, quod sub fati serie non contineatur.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33310)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, in V de Civ. Dei, quod fatum aliquid est, secundum quod ad voluntatem et potestatem Dei refertur. Sed voluntas Dei est causa omnium quae fiunt, ut Augustinus dicit in III de Trin. Ergo omnia subduntur fato.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33311)\n\nPraeterea, fatum, secundum Boetium, est *dispositio rebus mobilibus inhaerens*. Sed omnes creaturae sunt mutabiles, et solus Deus vere immutabilis, ut supra habitum est. Ergo in omnibus creaturis est fatum.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33312)\n\nSed contra est quod Boetius dicit, in IV de Consol., quod *quaedam quae sub providentia locata sunt, fati seriem superant*.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33313)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, fatum est ordinatio secundarum causarum ad effectus divinitus provisos. Quaecumque igitur causis secundis subduntur, ea subduntur et fato. Si qua vero sunt quae immediate a Deo fiunt, cum non subdantur secundis causis, non subduntur fato; sicut creatio rerum, glorificatio spiritualium substantiarum, et alia huiusmodi. Et hoc est quod Boetius dicit, quod *ea quae sunt primae divinitati propinqua, stabiliter fixa, fatalis ordinem mobilitatis excedunt. Ex quo etiam patet quod quanto aliquid longius a prima mente discedit, nexibus fati maioribus implicatur*; quia magis subiicitur necessitati secundarum causarum.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33314)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod omnia illa quae ibi tanguntur, fiunt a Deo mediantibus causis secundis; et ideo sub fati serie continentur. Sed non est eadem ratio de omnibus aliis, ut supra dictum est.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33315)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod fatum refertur ad voluntatem et potestatem Dei, sicut ad primum principium. Unde non oportet quod quidquid subiicitur voluntati divinae vel potestati, subiiciatur fato, ut dictum est.\n\n[I q. 116 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33316)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod, quamvis omnes creaturae sint aliquo modo mutabiles, tamen aliquae earum non procedunt a causis creatis mutabilibus. Et ideo non subiiciuntur fato, ut dictum est.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0002","text":"[I q. 116 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33288)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de fato. Et circa hoc quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, an fatum sit. Secundo, in quo sit. Tertio, utrum sit immobile. Quarto, utrum omnia subsint fato.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0005","text":"[I q. 116 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33289)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod fatum nihil sit. Dicit enim Gregorius, in homilia Epiphaniae, *absit a fidelium cordibus ut fatum esse aliquid dicant*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0007","text":"[I q. 116 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33290)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0008","text":"Praeterea, ea quae fato aguntur, non sunt improvisa, quia, ut Augustinus dicit V de Civ. Dei, *fatum a fando dictum intelligimus, idest a loquendo*; ut ea fato fieri dicantur, quae ab aliquo determinante sunt ante praelocuta. Quae autem sunt provisa, non sunt fortuita neque casualia. Si igitur res fato aguntur, excludetur casus et fortuna a rebus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0009","text":"[I q. 116 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33291)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0010","text":"Sed contra quod non est, non definitur. Sed Boetius, in IV de Consol., definit fatum, dicens quod *fatum est inhaerens rebus mobilibus dispositio, per quam providentia suis quaeque nectit ordinibus*. Ergo fatum aliquid est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0011","text":"[I q. 116 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33292)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0012","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod in rebus inferioribus videntur quaedam a fortuna vel casu provenire. Contingit autem quandoque quod aliquid, ad inferiores causas relatum, est fortuitum vel casuale, quod tamen, relatum ad causam aliquam superiorem, invenitur esse per se intentum. Sicut si duo servi alicuius domini mittantur ab eo ad eundem locum, uno de altero ignorante; concursus duorum servorum, si ad ipsos servos referatur, casualis est, quia accidit praeter utriusque intentionem; si autem referatur ad dominum, qui hoc praeordinavit, non est casuale, sed per se intentum. Fuerunt igitur aliqui qui huiusmodi casualia et fortuita, quae in his inferioribus accidunt, in nullam superiorem causam reducere voluerunt. Et hi fatum et providentiam negaverunt; ut de Tullio Augustinus recitat in V de Civ. Dei. Quod est contra ea quae superius de providentia dicta sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0013","text":"Quidam vero omnia fortuita et casualia quae in istis inferioribus accidunt, sive in rebus naturalibus sive in rebus humanis, reducere voluerunt in superiorem causam, idest in caelestia corpora. Et secundum hos, fatum nihil aliud est quam *dispositio siderum in qua quisque conceptus est vel natus*. Sed hoc stare non potest, propter duo. Primo quidem, quantum ad res humanas. Quia iam ostensum est quod humani actus non subduntur actioni caelestium corporum, nisi per accidens et indirecte. Causa autem fatalis, cum habeat ordinationem super ea quae fato aguntur, necesse est quod sit directe et per se causa eius quod agitur. Secundo, quantum ad omnia quae per accidens aguntur. Dictum est enim supra quod id quod est per accidens, non est proprie ens neque unum. Omnis autem naturae actio terminatur ad aliquid unum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0014","text":"Unde impossibile est quod id quod est per accidens, sit effectus per se alicuius naturalis principii agentis. Nulla ergo natura per se hoc facere potest, quod intendens fodere sepulcrum, inveniat thesaurum. Manifestum est autem quod corpus caeleste agit per modum naturalis principii, unde et effectus eius in hoc mundo sunt naturales. Impossibile est ergo quod aliqua virtus activa caelestis corporis sit causa eorum quae hic aguntur per accidens, sive a casu sive a fortuna. Et ideo dicendum est quod ea quae hic per accidens aguntur, sive in rebus naturalibus sive in rebus humanis, reducuntur in aliquam causam praeordinantem, quae est providentia divina. Quia nihil prohibet id quod est per accidens, accipi ut unum ab aliquo intellectu, alioquin intellectus formare non posset hanc propositionem, fodiens sepulcrum invenit thesaurum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0015","text":"Et sicut hoc potest intellectus apprehendere, ita potest efficere, sicut si aliquis sciens in quo loco sit thesaurus absconditus, instiget aliquem rusticum hoc ignorantem, ut ibi fodiat sepulcrum. Et sic nihil prohibet ea quae hic per accidens aguntur, ut fortuita vel casualia, reduci in aliquam causam ordinantem, quae per intellectum agat; et praecipue intellectum divinum. Nam solus Deus potest voluntatem immutare, ut supra habitum est. Et per consequens ordinatio humanorum actuum, quorum principium est voluntas, soli Deo attribui debet. Sic igitur inquantum omnia quae hic aguntur, divinae providentiae subduntur, tanquam per eam praeordinata et quasi praelocuta, fatum ponere possumus, licet hoc nomine sancti doctores uti recusaverint, propter eos qui ad vim positionis siderum hoc nomen retorquebant. Unde Augustinus dicit, in V de Civ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0016","text":"Dei, *si propterea quisquam res humanas fato tribuit, quia ipsam Dei voluntatem vel potestatem fati nomine appellat, sententiam teneat, linguam corrigat*. Et sic etiam Gregorius fatum esse negat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0017","text":"[I q. 116 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33293)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0018","text":"Unde patet solutio ad primum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0019","text":"[I q. 116 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33294)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0020","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod nihil prohibet aliqua esse fortuita vel casualia per comparationem ad causas proximas, non tamen per comparationem ad divinam providentiam, sic enim nihil temere fit in mundo, ut Augustinus dicit in libro octoginta trium quaest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0021","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0022","text":"[I q. 116 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33295)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0023","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod fatum non sit in rebus creatis. Dicit enim Augustinus, V de Civ. Dei, quod *ipsa Dei voluntas vel potestas fati nomine appellatur*. Sed voluntas et potestas Dei non est in creaturis, sed in Deo. Ergo fatum non est in rebus creatis, sed in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0024","text":"[I q. 116 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33296)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0025","text":"Praeterea, fatum comparatur ad ea quae ex fato aguntur, ut causa; ut ipse modus loquendi ostendit. Sed causa universalis per se eorum quae hic per accidens aguntur, est solus Deus, ut supra dictum est. Ergo fatum est in Deo, et non in rebus creatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0026","text":"[I q. 116 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33297)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0027","text":"Praeterea, si fatum est in creaturis, aut est substantia, aut accidens, et quodcumque horum detur, oportet quod multiplicetur secundum creaturarum multitudinem. Cum ergo fatum videatur esse unum tantum, videtur quod fatum non sit in creaturis, sed in Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0028","text":"[I q. 116 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33298)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0029","text":"Sed contra est quod Boetius dicit, in IV de Consol. quod *fatum est dispositio rebus mobilibus inhaerens*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0030","text":"[I q. 116 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33299)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0031","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut ex praedictis patet, divina providentia per causas medias suos effectus exequitur. Potest ergo ipsa ordinatio effectuum dupliciter considerari. Uno modo, secundum quod est in ipso Deo, et sic ipsa ordinatio effectuum vocatur providentia. Secundum vero quod praedicta ordinatio consideratur in mediis causis a Deo ordinatis ad aliquos effectus producendos, sic habet rationem fati. Et hoc est quod Boetius dicit, IV de Consol., *sive famulantibus quibusdam providentiae divinae spiritibus fatum exercetur; seu anima, seu tota inserviente natura, sive caelestibus siderum motibus, seu angelica virtute, seu Daemonum varia solertia, seu aliquibus eorum, seu omnibus, fatalis series texitur*, de quibus omnibus per singula in praecedentibus dictum est. Sic ergo est manifestum quod fatum est in ipsis causis creatis, inquantum sunt ordinatae a Deo ad effectus producendos.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0032","text":"[I q. 116 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33300)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0033","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod ipsa ordinatio causarum secundarum, quam Augustinus seriem causarum nominat, non habet rationem fati, nisi secundum quod dependet a Deo. Et ideo causaliter Dei potestas vel voluntas dici potest fatum. Essentialiter vero fatum est ipsa dispositio seu series, idest ordo, causarum secundarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0034","text":"[I q. 116 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33301)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0035","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod intantum fatum habet rationem causae, inquantum et ipsae causae secundae, quarum ordinatio fatum vocatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0036","text":"[I q. 116 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33302)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0037","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod fatum dicitur dispositio, non quae est in genere qualitatis; sed secundum quod dispositio designat ordinem, qui non est substantia, sed relatio. Qui quidem ordo, si consideretur per comparationem ad suum principium, est unus, et sic dicitur unum fatum. Si autem consideretur per comparationem ad effectus, vel ad ipsas causas medias, sic multiplicatur, per quem modum poeta dixit, *te tua fata trahunt*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0038","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0039","text":"[I q. 116 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33303)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0040","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod fatum non sit immobile. Dicit enim Boetius, in IV de Consol., *uti est ad intellectum ratiocinatio, ad id quod est id quod gignitur, ad aeternitatem tempus, ad punctum medium circulus; ita est fati series mobilis ad providentiae stabilem simplicitatem*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0041","text":"[I q. 116 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33304)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0042","text":"Praeterea, sicut philosophus dicit in II Topic., *motis nobis, moventur ea quae in nobis sunt. Sed fatum est dispositio inhaerens rebus mobilibus*, ut Boetius dicit. Ergo fatum est mobile.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0043","text":"[I q. 116 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33305)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0044","text":"Praeterea, si fatum est immobile, ea quae subduntur fato, immobiliter et ex necessitate eveniunt. Sed talia maxime videntur esse contingentia, quae fato attribuuntur. Ergo nihil erit contingens in rebus, sed omnia ex necessitate evenient.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0045","text":"[I q. 116 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33306)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0046","text":"Sed contra est quod Boetius dicit, quod fatum est immobilis dispositio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0047","text":"[I q. 116 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33307)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0048","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod dispositio secundarum causarum, quam fatum dicimus, potest dupliciter considerari, uno modo, secundum ipsas causas secundas, quae sic disponuntur seu ordinantur; alio modo, per relationem ad primum principium a quo ordinantur, scilicet Deum. Quidam ergo posuerunt ipsam seriem seu dispositionem causarum esse secundum se necessariam, ita quod omnia ex necessitate contingerent; propter hoc, quod quilibet effectus habet causam, et causa posita necesse est effectum poni. Sed hoc patet esse falsum, per ea quae supra dicta sunt. Alii vero e contrario posuerunt fatum esse mobile, etiam secundum quod a divina providentia dependet. Unde Aegyptii dicebant quibusdam sacrificiis fatum posse mutari, ut Gregorius Nyssenus dicit. Sed hoc supra exclusum est, quia immobilitati divinae providentiae repugnat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0049","text":"Et ideo dicendum est quod fatum, secundum considerationem secundarum causarum, mobile est, sed secundum quod subest divinae providentiae, immobilitatem sortitur, non quidem absolutae necessitatis, sed conditionatae; secundum quod dicimus hanc conditionalem esse veram vel necessariam, si Deus praescivit hoc futurum, erit. Unde cum Boetius dixisset fati seriem esse mobilem, post pauca subdit, *quae cum ab immobilis providentiae proficiscatur exordiis, ipsam quoque immutabilem esse necesse est*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0050","text":"[I q. 116 a. 3 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33308)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0051","text":"Et per hoc patet responsio ad obiecta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0052","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0053","text":"[I q. 116 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33309)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0054","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod omnia fato subdantur. Dicit enim Boetius, in IV de Consol., *series fati caelum et sidera movet, elementa in se invicem temperat, et alterna format transmutatione; eadem nascentia occidentiaque omnia per similes foetuum seminumque renovat progressus; haec actus fortunasque hominum indissolubili causarum connexione constringit*. Nihil ergo excipi videtur, quod sub fati serie non contineatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0055","text":"[I q. 116 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33310)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0056","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, in V de Civ. Dei, quod fatum aliquid est, secundum quod ad voluntatem et potestatem Dei refertur. Sed voluntas Dei est causa omnium quae fiunt, ut Augustinus dicit in III de Trin. Ergo omnia subduntur fato.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0057","text":"[I q. 116 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33311)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0058","text":"Praeterea, fatum, secundum Boetium, est *dispositio rebus mobilibus inhaerens*. Sed omnes creaturae sunt mutabiles, et solus Deus vere immutabilis, ut supra habitum est. Ergo in omnibus creaturis est fatum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0059","text":"[I q. 116 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33312)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0060","text":"Sed contra est quod Boetius dicit, in IV de Consol., quod *quaedam quae sub providentia locata sunt, fati seriem superant*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0061","text":"[I q. 116 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33313)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0062","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est, fatum est ordinatio secundarum causarum ad effectus divinitus provisos. Quaecumque igitur causis secundis subduntur, ea subduntur et fato. Si qua vero sunt quae immediate a Deo fiunt, cum non subdantur secundis causis, non subduntur fato; sicut creatio rerum, glorificatio spiritualium substantiarum, et alia huiusmodi. Et hoc est quod Boetius dicit, quod *ea quae sunt primae divinitati propinqua, stabiliter fixa, fatalis ordinem mobilitatis excedunt. Ex quo etiam patet quod quanto aliquid longius a prima mente discedit, nexibus fati maioribus implicatur*; quia magis subiicitur necessitati secundarum causarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0063","text":"[I q. 116 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33314)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0064","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod omnia illa quae ibi tanguntur, fiunt a Deo mediantibus causis secundis; et ideo sub fati serie continentur. Sed non est eadem ratio de omnibus aliis, ut supra dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0065","text":"[I q. 116 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33315)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0066","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod fatum refertur ad voluntatem et potestatem Dei, sicut ad primum principium. Unde non oportet quod quidquid subiicitur voluntati divinae vel potestati, subiiciatur fato, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0067","text":"[I q. 116 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33316)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0068","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod, quamvis omnes creaturae sint aliquo modo mutabiles, tamen aliquae earum non procedunt a causis creatis mutabilibus. Et ideo non subiiciuntur fato, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"3cbb38e5a71f263ae4f591656c9c3d01f7ea91b7bd04c14ab0360c4340568111","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T155014364380Z_85b4f254","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 116","source_sha256":"3cbb38e5a71f263ae4f591656c9c3d01f7ea91b7bd04c14ab0360c4340568111","sha256":"af6e2edfa0a1b0489a0b94336991da740122195bfa1978b36fea74f2339745da","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:50:14.364414+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0002","text":"[I q. 116 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33288)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0003","text":"Next, fate must be considered. And concerning this, four questions are asked. First, whether fate exists. Second, in what it exists. Third, whether it is immovable. Fourth, whether all things are subject to fate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0005","text":"[I q. 116 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33289)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that fate is nothing. For Gregory says, in the homily on the Epiphany, *far be it from the hearts of the faithful that they should say fate is something*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0007","text":"[I q. 116 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33290)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0008","text":"Moreover, things that are done by fate are not unforeseen, because, as Augustine says in V of *The City of God*, *we understand fate to be so called from speaking, that is, from talking*; so that those things are said to happen by fate which have been spoken beforehand by someone determining them. But things that are foreseen are neither fortuitous nor chance occurrences. Therefore, if things are done by fate, chance and fortune will be excluded from things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0009","text":"[I q. 116 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33291)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0010","text":"On the contrary, what does not exist is not defined. But Boethius, in IV of *The Consolation*, defines fate, saying that *fate is a disposition inherent in movable things, through which providence binds each thing in its own orders*. Therefore fate is something.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0011","text":"[I q. 116 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33292)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0012","text":"I answer that it must be said that in lower things some things seem to arise from fortune or chance. Now it sometimes happens that something, when referred to lower causes, is fortuitous or chance, which nevertheless, when referred to some higher cause, is found to be intended through itself. For example, if two servants of a certain master are sent by him to the same place, each being unaware of the other: the meeting of the two servants, if referred to the servants themselves, is chance, because it happens beyond the intention of either; but if it is referred to the master, who preordained this, it is not chance, but intended through itself. There were therefore some who did not wish to reduce such chance and fortuitous events, which occur among these lower things, to any higher cause. And these denied fate and providence; as Augustine recounts concerning Tully in V of *The City of God*. This is contrary to what has been said above concerning providence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0013","text":"But some wished to reduce all fortuitous and chance events which occur in these lower things, whether in natural things or in human affairs, to a higher cause, namely, the heavenly bodies. And according to these, fate is nothing other than *the disposition of the stars under which each person was conceived or born*. But this cannot stand, for two reasons. First, with respect to human affairs. For it has already been shown that human acts are not subject to the action of the heavenly bodies except accidentally and indirectly. But a fatal cause, since it has ordering over those things which are done by fate, must be directly and through itself the cause of what is done. Second, with respect to all things that happen accidentally. For it has been said above that what is accidental is not properly being or one. But every action of nature terminates in something one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0014","text":"Hence it is impossible that what is accidental should be the effect through itself of any natural active principle. Therefore no nature can through itself bring it about that one intending to dig a grave finds a treasure. But it is manifest that a heavenly body acts in the manner of a natural principle, and thus its effects in this world are natural. Therefore it is impossible that any active power of a heavenly body should be the cause of those things which happen here accidentally, whether by chance or by fortune. And therefore it must be said that those things which happen here accidentally, whether in natural things or in human affairs, are reduced to some preordaining cause, which is divine providence. For nothing prevents what is accidental from being taken as one by some intellect; otherwise the intellect could not form this proposition: one digging a grave finds a treasure.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0015","text":"And just as the intellect can apprehend this, so it can bring it about, as if someone knowing in what place a treasure is hidden should urge a peasant, who is ignorant of this, to dig a grave there. And thus nothing prevents those things which happen here accidentally, as fortuitous or chance events, from being reduced to some ordering cause that acts through intellect; and especially through the divine intellect. For God alone can change the will, as has been maintained above. And consequently, the ordering of human acts, the principle of which is the will, ought to be attributed to God alone. Thus, therefore, insofar as all things that happen here are subject to divine providence, as preordained by it and, as it were, spoken beforehand, we can posit fate, although the holy doctors refused to use this name, because of those who twisted this name toward the force of the position of the stars. Hence Augustine says, in V of *The City of God*,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0016","text":"*if anyone for this reason attributes human affairs to fate, because he calls the very will or power of God by the name of fate, let him retain the opinion, but correct the language*. And thus Gregory too denies that fate exists.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0017","text":"[I q. 116 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33293)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0018","text":"Hence the solution to the first is evident.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0019","text":"[I q. 116 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33294)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0020","text":"To the second, it must be said that nothing prevents certain things from being fortuitous or chance in relation to proximate causes, though not in relation to divine providence; for thus nothing happens rashly in the world, as Augustine says in the book *Eighty-Three Questions*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0021","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0022","text":"[I q. 116 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33295)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0023","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that fate is not in created things. For Augustine says, in V of *The City of God*, that *the very will or power of God is called by the name of fate*. But the will and power of God are not in creatures, but in God. Therefore fate is not in created things, but in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0024","text":"[I q. 116 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33296)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0025","text":"Moreover, fate is compared to those things that are done by fate as a cause, as the very manner of speaking shows. But the universal cause, through itself, of those things that are done here accidentally is God alone, as was said above. Therefore fate is in God, and not in created things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0026","text":"[I q. 116 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33297)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0027","text":"Moreover, if fate is in creatures, it is either substance or accident; and whichever of these is granted, it must be multiplied according to the multitude of creatures. Therefore, since fate seems to be only one, it seems that fate is not in creatures, but in God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0028","text":"[I q. 116 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33298)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0029","text":"On the contrary, Boethius says, in *On the Consolation* IV, that *fate is a disposition inhering in movable things*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0030","text":"[I q. 116 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33299)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0031","text":"I answer that, as is clear from what has been said, divine providence carries out its effects through intermediate causes. Therefore the ordering of effects itself can be considered in two ways. In one way, insofar as it is in God himself; and thus the ordering of effects itself is called providence. But insofar as the aforesaid ordering is considered in the intermediate causes ordered by God to produce certain effects, it has the character of fate. And this is what Boethius says, in *On the Consolation* IV: *whether fate is exercised by certain spirits serving divine providence; or whether, with soul or the whole of serving nature, whether by the heavenly motions of the stars, or by angelic power, or by the various skill of demons, or by some of these or all of them, the fateful series is woven*—concerning all of which, one by one, it has been spoken in what precedes. Thus, therefore, it is manifest that fate is in created causes themselves, insofar as they are ordered by God to produce effects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0032","text":"[I q. 116 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33300)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0033","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the very ordering of secondary causes, which Augustine calls the series of causes, does not have the character of fate except insofar as it depends on God. And therefore God's power or will can be called fate causally. But essentially, fate is the very disposition or series, that is, order, of secondary causes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0034","text":"[I q. 116 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33301)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0035","text":"To the second it must be said that fate has the character of a cause only insofar as the secondary causes themselves do, the ordering of which is called fate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0036","text":"[I q. 116 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33302)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0037","text":"To the third it must be said that fate is called a disposition, not one that is in the genus of quality, but insofar as disposition designates an order, which is not a substance but a relation. Indeed, if this order is considered in comparison with its principle, it is one, and thus one fate is spoken of. But if it is considered in comparison with its effects, or with the intermediate causes themselves, it is multiplied; in which sense the poet said, *your fates draw you on*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0038","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0039","text":"[I q. 116 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33303)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0040","text":"The third point is approached thus. It seems that fate is not immovable. For Boethius says, in *On the Consolation* IV: *as reasoning is to understanding, as that which is generated is to that which is, as time is to eternity, as a circle is to its central point; so is the movable series of fate to the stable simplicity of providence*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0041","text":"[I q. 116 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33304)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0042","text":"Moreover, as the Philosopher says in *Topics* II, *when we are moved, the things that are in us are moved*. But fate is a disposition inhering in movable things, as Boethius says. Therefore fate is movable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0043","text":"[I q. 116 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33305)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0044","text":"Moreover, if fate is immovable, the things subject to fate occur immovably and by necessity. But those things that are attributed to fate seem especially to be contingent. Therefore there will be nothing contingent among things, but all things will occur by necessity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0045","text":"[I q. 116 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33306)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0046","text":"On the contrary, Boethius says that fate is an immovable disposition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0047","text":"[I q. 116 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33307)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0048","text":"I answer that the disposition of secondary causes, which we call fate, can be considered in two ways: in one way, according to the secondary causes themselves, which are thus disposed or ordered; in another way, by relation to the first principle by which they are ordered, namely God. Therefore, some have held that the series or disposition of causes itself is necessary in itself, so that all things would happen by necessity; on the ground that every effect has a cause, and, once the cause is posited, the effect must be posited. But this is clearly false, from what has been said above. Others, on the contrary, have held fate to be movable, even insofar as it depends on divine providence. Hence the Egyptians said that fate could be changed by certain sacrifices, as Gregory of Nyssa says. But this has been excluded above, because it is incompatible with the immovability of divine providence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0049","text":"And therefore it must be said that fate, according to the consideration of secondary causes, is movable; but according as it is subject to divine providence, it obtains immovability—not indeed that of absolute necessity, but of conditional necessity. Thus we say that this conditional proposition is true or necessary: if God foreknew that this would be, it will be. Hence, when Boethius had said that the series of fate is movable, a little later he adds: *since it proceeds from the beginnings of immovable providence, it too must necessarily be immutable*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0050","text":"[I q. 116 a. 3 ad arg.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33308)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0051","text":"And through this the reply to the objections is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0052","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0053","text":"[I q. 116 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33309)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0054","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth article. It seems that all things are subject to fate. For Boethius says, in Book IV of the *Consolation*: *the series of fate moves heaven and the stars, tempers the elements among themselves, and fashions them by alternating transformation; it renews all things that come to birth and pass away through similar progressions of offspring and seeds; it binds the actions and fortunes of men by an indissoluble connection of causes*. Therefore, nothing seems to be excepted that is not contained under the series of fate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0055","text":"[I q. 116 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33310)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0056","text":"Further, Augustine says, in Book V of the *City of God*, that fate is something, according as it is referred to the will and power of God. But the will of God is the cause of all things that come to pass, as Augustine says in Book III of *On the Trinity*. Therefore all things are subject to fate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0057","text":"[I q. 116 a. 4 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33311)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0058","text":"Further, fate, according to Boethius, is *a disposition adhering to movable things*. But all creatures are mutable, and God alone is truly immutable, as was established above. Therefore fate is in all creatures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0059","text":"[I q. 116 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33312)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0060","text":"But on the contrary, Boethius says, in Book IV of the *Consolation*, that *certain things which are placed under providence surpass the series of fate*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0061","text":"[I q. 116 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33313)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0062","text":"I answer that it must be said that, as was stated above, fate is the ordering of secondary causes toward effects divinely provided for. Therefore, whatever things are subject to secondary causes are also subject to fate. But if there are any things that are done immediately by God, since they are not subject to secondary causes, they are not subject to fate; for example, the creation of things, the glorification of spiritual substances, and other things of this kind. And this is what Boethius says: *those things which are close to the first divinity, firmly fixed, surpass the order of fatal mobility. From this it is also clear that the farther something departs from the first mind, the more greatly it is entangled in the bonds of fate*; because it is more subject to the necessity of secondary causes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0063","text":"[I q. 116 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33314)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0064","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that all those things touched upon there are done by God through the mediation of secondary causes; and therefore they are contained under the series of fate. But the same account does not hold for all other things, as was stated above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0065","text":"[I q. 116 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33315)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0066","text":"To the second, it must be said that fate is referred to the will and power of God as to its first principle. Hence it does not follow that whatever is subject to the divine will or power is subject to fate, as has been stated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0067","text":"[I q. 116 a. 4 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33316)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__108:P0068","text":"To the third, it must be said that, although all creatures are in some way mutable, nevertheless some of them do not proceed from mutable created causes. And therefore they are not subject to fate, as has been stated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__108:P0068"]}],"source_digest":"5c40467418e80a3b172d4ac753e85ffb8f1c6004de50bbfab63eb1c1e8729206","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 116"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 116","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109","title":"First Part · Question 117","order":118,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC__109","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 117 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33317)\n\nPostea considerandum est de his quae pertinent ad actionem hominis, qui est compositus ex spirituali et corporali creatura. Et primo considerandum est de actione hominis; secundo, de propagatione hominis ex homine. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum unus homo possit docere alium, causando in ipso scientiam. Secundo, utrum homo possit docere Angelum. Tertio, utrum homo per virtutem suae animae possit immutare materiam corporalem. Quarto, utrum anima hominis separata possit movere corpora motu locali.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 117 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33318)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo non possit alium docere. Dicit enim dominus, Matth. XXIII, *nolite vocari Rabbi*; ubi dicit Glossa Hieronymi, *ne divinum honorem hominibus tribuatis*. Esse ergo magistrum pertinet proprie ad divinum honorem. Sed docere est proprium magistri. Homo ergo non potest docere, sed hoc est proprium Dei.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33319)\n\nPraeterea, si homo alium docet, hoc non est nisi inquantum agit per scientiam suam ad causandum scientiam in alio. Sed qualitas per quam aliquis agit ad faciendum sibi simile, est qualitas activa. Ergo sequitur quod scientia sit qualitas activa, sicut et calor.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33320)\n\nPraeterea, ad scientiam requiritur lumen intelligibile, et species rei intellectae. Sed neutrum istorum potest causare unus homo in alio. Ergo unus homo non potest docendo causare scientiam in alio.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33321)\n\nPraeterea, doctor nihil agit ad discipulum nisi quod proponit ei quaedam signa, vel vocibus aliquid significando, vel nutibus. Sed proponendo signa non potest aliquis alium docere, causando in eo scientiam. Quia aut proponit signa rerum notarum; aut rerum ignotarum. Si rerum notarum, ille ergo cui signa proponuntur, iam habet scientiam, et eam non acquirit a magistro. Si autem rerum ignotarum, per huiusmodi signa nihil addiscit, sicut si aliquis proponeret alicui Latino verba Graeca, quorum significationem ignoraret, per hoc eum docere non posset. Nullo ergo modo unus homo potest, alium docendo, scientiam in eo causare.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33322)\n\nSed contra est quod apostolus dicit, I ad Tim. II, *in quo positus sum ego praedicator et apostolus, doctor gentium in fide et veritate*.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33323)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod circa hoc diversae fuerunt opiniones. Averroes enim, in Comment. III de anima, posuit unum intellectum possibilem esse omnium hominum, ut supra dictum est. Et ex hoc sequebatur quod eaedem species intelligibiles sint omnium hominum. Et secundum hoc, ponit quod unus homo per doctrinam non causat aliam scientiam in altero ab ea quam ipse habet; sed communicat ei eandem scientiam quam ipse habet, per hoc quod movet eum ad ordinandum phantasmata in anima sua, ad hoc quod sint disposita convenienter ad intelligibilem apprehensionem. Quae quidem opinio quantum ad hoc vera est, quod est eadem scientia in discipulo et magistro, si consideretur identitas secundum unitatem rei scitae, eadem enim rei veritas est quam cognoscit et discipulus et magister. Sed quantum ad hoc quod ponit esse unum intellectum possibilem omnium hominum, et easdem species intelligibiles, differentes solum secundum diversa phantasmata; falsa est eius opinio, ut supra habitum est. Alia est opinio Platonicorum, qui posuerunt quod scientia inest a principio animabus nostris per participationem formarum separatarum, sicut supra habitum est; sed anima ex unione corporis impeditur ne possit considerare libere ea quorum scientiam habet. Et secundum hoc, discipulus a magistro non acquirit scientiam de novo, sed ab eo excitatur ad considerandum ea quorum scientiam habet; ut sic addiscere nihil aliud sit quam reminisci. Sicut etiam ponebant quod agentia naturalia solummodo disponunt ad susceptionem formarum, quas acquirit materia corporalis per participationem specierum separatarum. Sed contra hoc supra ostensum est quod intellectus possibilis animae humanae est in potentia pura ad intelligibilia, secundum quod Aristoteles dicit in III de anima. Et ideo aliter dicendum est, quod docens causat scientiam in addiscente, reducendo ipsum de potentia in actum, sicut dicitur in VIII Physic. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod effectuum qui sunt ab exteriori principio, aliquis est ab exteriori principio tantum; sicut forma domus causatur in materia solum ab arte. Aliquis autem effectus est quandoque quidem ab exteriori principio, quandoque autem ab interiori; sicut sanitas causatur in infirmo quandoque ab exteriori principio, scilicet ab arte medicinae; quandoque autem ab interiori principio ut cum aliquis sanatur per virtutem naturae. Et in talibus effectibus sunt duo attendenda. Primo quidem, quod ars imitatur naturam in sua operatione, sicut enim natura sanat infirmum alterando, digerendo, et expellendo materiam quae causat morbum, ita et ars. Secundo attendendum est, quod principium exterius, scilicet ars, non operatur sicut principale agens, sed sicut coadiuvans agens principale, quod est principium interius, confortando ipsum, et ministrando ei instrumenta et auxilia, quibus utatur ad effectum producendum, sicut medicus confortat naturam, et adhibet ei cibos et medicinas, quibus natura utatur ad finem intentum. Scientia autem acquiritur in homine et ab interiori principio, ut patet in eo qui per inventionem propriam scientiam acquirit; et a principio exteriori, ut patet in eo qui addiscit. Inest enim unicuique homini quoddam principium scientiae, scilicet lumen intellectus agentis, per quod cognoscuntur statim a principio naturaliter quaedam universalia principia omnium scientiarum. Cum autem aliquis huiusmodi universalia principia applicat ad aliqua particularia, quorum memoriam et experimentum per sensum accipit; per inventionem propriam acquirit scientiam eorum quae nesciebat, ex notis ad ignota procedens. Unde et quilibet docens, ex his quae discipulus novit, ducit eum in cognitionem eorum quae ignorabat; secundum quod dicitur in I Poster., quod *omnis doctrina et omnis disciplina ex praeexistenti fit cognitione*. Ducit autem magister discipulum ex praecognitis in cognitionem ignotorum, dupliciter. Primo quidem, proponendo ei aliqua auxilia vel instrumenta, quibus intellectus eius utatur ad scientiam acquirendam, puta cum proponit ei aliquas propositiones minus universales, quas tamen ex praecognitis discipulus diiudicare potest; vel cum proponit ei aliqua sensibilia exempla, vel similia, vel opposita, vel aliqua huiusmodi ex quibus intellectus addiscentis manuducitur in cognitionem veritatis ignotae. Alio modo, cum confortat intellectum addiscentis; non quidem aliqua virtute activa quasi superioris naturae, sicut supra dictum est de Angelis illuminantibus, quia omnes humani intellectus sunt unius gradus in ordine naturae; sed inquantum proponit discipulo ordinem principiorum ad conclusiones, qui forte per seipsum non haberet tantam virtutem collativam, ut ex principiis posset conclusiones deducere. Et ideo dicitur in I Poster., quod demonstratio est syllogismus faciens scire. Et per hunc modum ille qui demonstrat, auditorem scientem facit.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33324)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut iam dictum est, homo docens solummodo exterius ministerium adhibet, sicut medicus sanans, sed sicut natura interior est principalis causa sanationis, ita et interius lumen intellectus est principalis causa scientiae. Utrumque autem horum est a Deo. Et ideo sicut de Deo dicitur, qui sanat omnes infirmitates tuas; ita de eo dicitur, qui docet hominem scientiam, inquantum lumen vultus eius super nos signatur, per quod nobis omnia ostenduntur.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33325)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod doctor non causat scientiam in discipulo per modum agentis naturalis, ut Averroes obiicit. Unde non oportet quod scientia sit qualitas activa, sed est principium quo aliquis dirigitur in docendo, sicut ars est principium quo aliquis dirigitur in operando.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33326)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod magister non causat lumen intelligibile in discipulo, nec directe species intelligibiles, sed movet discipulum per suam doctrinam ad hoc, quod ipse per virtutem sui intellectus formet intelligibiles conceptiones, quarum signa sibi proponit exterius.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33327)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod signa quae magister discipulo proponit, sunt rerum notarum in universali, et sub quadam confusione; sed ignotarum in particulari, et sub quadam distinctione. Et ideo cum quisque per seipsum scientiam acquirit, non potest dici docere seipsum, vel esse sui ipsius magister, quia non praeexistit in eo scientia completa, qualis requiritur in magistro.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 117 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33328)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homines possint docere Angelos. Dicit enim apostolus, ad Ephes. III, *ut innotescat principibus et potestatibus in caelestibus per Ecclesiam multiformis sapientia Dei*. Sed Ecclesia est congregatio hominum fidelium. Ergo Angelis per homines aliqua innotescunt.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33329)\n\nPraeterea, Angeli superiores, qui immediate de divinis a Deo illuminantur, inferiores Angelos instruere possunt, ut supra dictum est. Sed aliqui homines immediate de divinis per Dei verbum sunt instructi; sicut maxime patet de apostolis, secundum illud ad Heb. I, *novissime, diebus istis, locutus est nobis in filio*. Ergo aliqui homines aliquos Angelos docere potuerunt.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33330)\n\nPraeterea, inferiores Angeli a superioribus instruuntur. Sed quidam homines superiores sunt aliquibus Angelis, cum ad supremos ordines Angelorum aliqui homines assumantur, ut Gregorius dicit in quadam homilia. Ergo aliqui inferiores Angeli per aliquos homines de divinis instrui possunt.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33331)\n\nSed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod omnes divinae illuminationes perferuntur ad homines mediantibus Angelis. Non ergo Angeli instruuntur per homines de divinis.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33332)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra habitum est, inferiores Angeli loqui quidem possunt superioribus Angelis, manifestando eis suas cogitationes; sed de rebus divinis superiores ab inferioribus nunquam illuminantur. Manifestum est autem quod eo modo quo inferiores Angeli superioribus subduntur, supremi homines subduntur etiam infimis Angelorum. Quod patet per id quod dominus dicit, Matth. XI, *inter natos mulierum non surrexit maior Ioanne Baptista; sed qui minor est in regno caelorum, maior est illo*. Sic igitur de rebus divinis ab hominibus Angeli nunquam illuminantur. Cogitationes tamen suorum cordium homines Angelis per modum locutionis manifestare possunt, quia secreta cordium scire solius Dei est.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33333)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus, V super Gen. ad Litt., sic exponit illam apostoli auctoritatem. Praemiserat enim apostolus, *mihi, omnium sanctorum minimo, data est gratia haec, illuminare omnes quae sit dispensatio sacramenti absconditi a saeculis in Deo. Ita dico absconditi, ut tamen innotesceret principibus et potestatibus in caelestibus, per Ecclesiam scilicet, multiformis sapientia Dei*. Quasi dicat, ita hoc sacramentum erat absconditum hominibus, ut tamen Ecclesiae caelesti, quae continetur in principibus et potestatibus, hoc sacramentum notum esset *a saeculis, non ante saecula, quia ibi primitus Ecclesia fuit, quo post resurrectionem et ista Ecclesia hominum congreganda est*. Potest tamen et aliter dici, quod *illud quod absconditum est, non tantum in Deo innotescit Angelis, verum etiam hic eis apparet, cum efficitur atque propalatur*, ut Augustinus ibidem subdit. Et sic dum per apostolos impleta sunt Christi et Ecclesiae mysteria, Angelis aliqua apparuerunt de huiusmodi mysteriis, quae ante erant eis occulta. Et per hunc modum potest intelligi quod Hieronymus dicit, quod, apostolis praedicantibus, Angeli aliqua mysteria cognoverunt, quia scilicet per praedicationem apostolorum huiusmodi mysteria explebantur in rebus ipsis, sicut praedicante Paulo convertebantur gentes; de quo apostolus ibi loquitur.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33334)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod apostoli instruebantur immediate a verbo Dei, non secundum eius divinitatem, sed inquantum eius humanitas loquebatur. Unde ratio non sequitur.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33335)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod aliqui homines, etiam in statu viae, sunt maiores aliquibus Angelis, non quidem actu, sed virtute; inquantum scilicet habent caritatem tantae virtutis, ut possint mereri maiorem beatitudinis gradum quam quidam Angeli habeant. Sicut si dicamus semen alicuius magnae arboris esse maius virtute quam aliquam parvam arborem, cum tamen multo minus sit in actu.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 117 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33336)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo per virtutem animae possit corporalem materiam immutare. Dicit enim Gregorius, II Dialog., quod *sancti miracula aliquando ex prece faciunt, aliquando ex potestate, sicut Petrus, qui Tabitham mortuam orando suscitavit, Ananiam et Saphiram mentientes morti increpando tradidit*. Sed in operatione miraculorum fit aliqua immutatio materiae corporalis. Ergo homines virtute suae animae possunt materiam corporalem immutare.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33337)\n\nPraeterea, super illud ad Gal. III, *quis vos fascinavit veritati non obedire?* Dicit Glossa quod *quidam habent* oculos urentes, qui solo aspectu inficiunt alios, et maxime pueros. Sed hoc non esset, nisi virtus animae posset materiam corporalem immutare. Ergo homo per virtutem suae animae potest materiam corporalem immutare.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33338)\n\nPraeterea, corpus humanum est nobilius quam alia inferiora corpora. Sed per apprehensionem animae humanae immutatur corpus humanum ad calorem et frigus, ut patet in irascentibus et timentibus; et quandoque etiam haec immutatio pervenit usque ad aegritudinem et mortem. Ergo multo magis anima hominis potest sua virtute materiam corporalem immutare.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33339)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in III de Trin., quod *materia corporalis soli Deo obedit ad nutum*.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33340)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est materia corporalis non immutatur ad formam, nisi vel ab agente aliquo composito ex materia et forma; vel ab ipso Deo, in quo virtualiter et materia et forma praeexistit, sicut in primordiali causa utriusque. Unde et de Angelis supra dictum est quod materiam corporalem immutare non possunt naturali virtute, nisi applicando corporalia agentia ad effectus aliquos producendos. Multo igitur minus anima sua virtute naturali potest immutare materiam corporalem, nisi mediantibus aliquibus corporibus.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33341)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod sancti dicuntur miracula facere ex potestate gratiae, non naturae. Quod patet per illud quod Gregorius ibidem dicit, *qui filii Dei ex potestate sunt, ut dicit Ioannes, quid mirum si signa facere ex potestate valeant?*\n\n[I q. 117 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33342)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod fascinationis causam assignavit Avicenna ex hoc, quod materia corporalis nata est obedire spirituali substantiae magis quam contrariis agentibus in natura. Et ideo quando anima fuerit fortis in sua imaginatione, corporalis materia immutatur secundum eam. Et hanc dicit esse causam oculi fascinantis. Sed supra ostensum est quod materia corporalis non obedit substantiae spirituali ad nutum, nisi soli creatori. Et ideo melius dicendum est, quod ex forti imaginatione animae immutantur spiritus corporis coniuncti. Quae quidem immutatio spirituum maxime fit in oculis, ad quos subtiliores spiritus perveniunt. Oculi autem inficiunt aerem continuum usque ad determinatum spatium, per quem modum specula, si fuerint nova et pura, contrahunt quandam impuritatem ex aspectu mulieris menstruatae, ut Aristoteles dicit in libro de Somn. et Vig. Sic igitur cum aliqua anima fuerit vehementer commota ad malitiam, sicut maxime in vetulabus contingit, efficitur secundum modum praedictum aspectus eius venenosus et noxius, et maxime pueris, qui habent corpus tenerum, et de facili receptivum impressionis. Possibile est etiam quod ex Dei permissione, vel etiam ex aliquo facto occulto, cooperetur ad hoc malignitas Daemonum, cum quibus vetulae sortilegae aliquod foedus habent.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33343)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod anima corpori humano unitur ut forma, et appetitus sensitivus, qui obedit aliqualiter rationi, ut supra dictum est, est actus alicuius organi corporalis. Et ideo oportet quod ad apprehensionem animae humanae, commoveatur appetitus sensitivus cum aliqua operatione corporali. Ad exteriora vero corpora immutanda apprehensio animae humanae non sufficit, nisi mediante immutatione proprii corporis, ut dictum est.\n\n## Articulus 4\n\n[I q. 117 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33344)\n\nAd quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima hominis separata possit corpora saltem localiter movere. Substantiae enim spirituali naturaliter obedit corpus ad motum localem, ut supra dictum est. Sed anima separata est substantia spiritualis. Ergo suo imperio potest exteriora corpora movere.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33345)\n\nPraeterea, in itinerario Clementis dicitur, narrante Niceta ad Petrum, quod Simon magus per magicas artes pueri a se interfecti animam retinebat, per quam magicas operationes efficiebat. Sed hoc esse non potuisset sine aliqua corporum transmutatione, ad minus locali. Ergo anima separata habet virtutem localiter movendi corpora.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33346)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in libro de anima quod anima non potest movere quodcumque corpus, sed solummodo proprium.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33347)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod anima separata sua naturali virtute non potest movere aliquod corpus. Manifestum est enim quod, cum anima est corpori unita, non movet corpus nisi vivificatum, unde si aliquod membrum corporis mortificetur, non obedit animae ad motum localem. Manifestum est autem quod ab anima separata nullum corpus vivificatur. Unde nullum corpus obedit ei ad motum localem, quantum est ex virtute suae naturae, supra quam potest aliquid ei conferri virtute divina.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33348)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod substantiae quaedam spirituales sunt, quarum virtutes non determinantur ad aliqua corpora, sicut sunt Angeli, qui sunt naturaliter a corporibus absoluti, et ideo diversa corpora eis possunt obedire ad motum. Si tamen alicuius substantiae separatae virtus motiva determinetur naturaliter ad movendum aliquod corpus, non poterit illa substantia movere aliquod corpus maius, sed minus, sicut, secundum philosophos, motor inferioris caeli non posset movere caelum superius. Unde cum anima secundum suam naturam determinetur ad movendum corpus cuius est forma, nullum aliud corpus sua naturali virtute movere potest.\n\n[I q. 117 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33349)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod, sicut dicit Augustinus X de Civ. Dei, et Chrysostomus super Matth., frequenter Daemones simulant se esse animas mortuorum, ad confirmandum gentilium errorem, qui hoc credebant. Et ideo credibile est quod Simon magus illudebatur ab aliquo Daemone, qui simulabat se esse animam pueri quem ipse occiderat.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0002","text":"[I q. 117 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33317)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0003","text":"Postea considerandum est de his quae pertinent ad actionem hominis, qui est compositus ex spirituali et corporali creatura. Et primo considerandum est de actione hominis; secundo, de propagatione hominis ex homine. Circa primum quaeruntur quatuor. Primo, utrum unus homo possit docere alium, causando in ipso scientiam. Secundo, utrum homo possit docere Angelum. Tertio, utrum homo per virtutem suae animae possit immutare materiam corporalem. Quarto, utrum anima hominis separata possit movere corpora motu locali.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0005","text":"[I q. 117 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33318)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo non possit alium docere. Dicit enim dominus, Matth. XXIII, *nolite vocari Rabbi*; ubi dicit Glossa Hieronymi, *ne divinum honorem hominibus tribuatis*. Esse ergo magistrum pertinet proprie ad divinum honorem. Sed docere est proprium magistri. Homo ergo non potest docere, sed hoc est proprium Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0007","text":"[I q. 117 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33319)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0008","text":"Praeterea, si homo alium docet, hoc non est nisi inquantum agit per scientiam suam ad causandum scientiam in alio. Sed qualitas per quam aliquis agit ad faciendum sibi simile, est qualitas activa. Ergo sequitur quod scientia sit qualitas activa, sicut et calor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0009","text":"[I q. 117 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33320)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0010","text":"Praeterea, ad scientiam requiritur lumen intelligibile, et species rei intellectae. Sed neutrum istorum potest causare unus homo in alio. Ergo unus homo non potest docendo causare scientiam in alio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0011","text":"[I q. 117 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33321)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0012","text":"Praeterea, doctor nihil agit ad discipulum nisi quod proponit ei quaedam signa, vel vocibus aliquid significando, vel nutibus. Sed proponendo signa non potest aliquis alium docere, causando in eo scientiam. Quia aut proponit signa rerum notarum; aut rerum ignotarum. Si rerum notarum, ille ergo cui signa proponuntur, iam habet scientiam, et eam non acquirit a magistro. Si autem rerum ignotarum, per huiusmodi signa nihil addiscit, sicut si aliquis proponeret alicui Latino verba Graeca, quorum significationem ignoraret, per hoc eum docere non posset. Nullo ergo modo unus homo potest, alium docendo, scientiam in eo causare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0013","text":"[I q. 117 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33322)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0014","text":"Sed contra est quod apostolus dicit, I ad Tim. II, *in quo positus sum ego praedicator et apostolus, doctor gentium in fide et veritate*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0015","text":"[I q. 117 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33323)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod circa hoc diversae fuerunt opiniones. Averroes enim, in Comment. III de anima, posuit unum intellectum possibilem esse omnium hominum, ut supra dictum est. Et ex hoc sequebatur quod eaedem species intelligibiles sint omnium hominum. Et secundum hoc, ponit quod unus homo per doctrinam non causat aliam scientiam in altero ab ea quam ipse habet; sed communicat ei eandem scientiam quam ipse habet, per hoc quod movet eum ad ordinandum phantasmata in anima sua, ad hoc quod sint disposita convenienter ad intelligibilem apprehensionem. Quae quidem opinio quantum ad hoc vera est, quod est eadem scientia in discipulo et magistro, si consideretur identitas secundum unitatem rei scitae, eadem enim rei veritas est quam cognoscit et discipulus et magister.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0017","text":"Sed quantum ad hoc quod ponit esse unum intellectum possibilem omnium hominum, et easdem species intelligibiles, differentes solum secundum diversa phantasmata; falsa est eius opinio, ut supra habitum est. Alia est opinio Platonicorum, qui posuerunt quod scientia inest a principio animabus nostris per participationem formarum separatarum, sicut supra habitum est; sed anima ex unione corporis impeditur ne possit considerare libere ea quorum scientiam habet. Et secundum hoc, discipulus a magistro non acquirit scientiam de novo, sed ab eo excitatur ad considerandum ea quorum scientiam habet; ut sic addiscere nihil aliud sit quam reminisci. Sicut etiam ponebant quod agentia naturalia solummodo disponunt ad susceptionem formarum, quas acquirit materia corporalis per participationem specierum separatarum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0018","text":"Sed contra hoc supra ostensum est quod intellectus possibilis animae humanae est in potentia pura ad intelligibilia, secundum quod Aristoteles dicit in III de anima. Et ideo aliter dicendum est, quod docens causat scientiam in addiscente, reducendo ipsum de potentia in actum, sicut dicitur in VIII Physic. Ad cuius evidentiam, considerandum est quod effectuum qui sunt ab exteriori principio, aliquis est ab exteriori principio tantum; sicut forma domus causatur in materia solum ab arte. Aliquis autem effectus est quandoque quidem ab exteriori principio, quandoque autem ab interiori; sicut sanitas causatur in infirmo quandoque ab exteriori principio, scilicet ab arte medicinae; quandoque autem ab interiori principio ut cum aliquis sanatur per virtutem naturae. Et in talibus effectibus sunt duo attendenda.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0019","text":"Primo quidem, quod ars imitatur naturam in sua operatione, sicut enim natura sanat infirmum alterando, digerendo, et expellendo materiam quae causat morbum, ita et ars. Secundo attendendum est, quod principium exterius, scilicet ars, non operatur sicut principale agens, sed sicut coadiuvans agens principale, quod est principium interius, confortando ipsum, et ministrando ei instrumenta et auxilia, quibus utatur ad effectum producendum, sicut medicus confortat naturam, et adhibet ei cibos et medicinas, quibus natura utatur ad finem intentum. Scientia autem acquiritur in homine et ab interiori principio, ut patet in eo qui per inventionem propriam scientiam acquirit; et a principio exteriori, ut patet in eo qui addiscit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0020","text":"Inest enim unicuique homini quoddam principium scientiae, scilicet lumen intellectus agentis, per quod cognoscuntur statim a principio naturaliter quaedam universalia principia omnium scientiarum. Cum autem aliquis huiusmodi universalia principia applicat ad aliqua particularia, quorum memoriam et experimentum per sensum accipit; per inventionem propriam acquirit scientiam eorum quae nesciebat, ex notis ad ignota procedens. Unde et quilibet docens, ex his quae discipulus novit, ducit eum in cognitionem eorum quae ignorabat; secundum quod dicitur in I Poster., quod *omnis doctrina et omnis disciplina ex praeexistenti fit cognitione*. Ducit autem magister discipulum ex praecognitis in cognitionem ignotorum, dupliciter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0021","text":"Primo quidem, proponendo ei aliqua auxilia vel instrumenta, quibus intellectus eius utatur ad scientiam acquirendam, puta cum proponit ei aliquas propositiones minus universales, quas tamen ex praecognitis discipulus diiudicare potest; vel cum proponit ei aliqua sensibilia exempla, vel similia, vel opposita, vel aliqua huiusmodi ex quibus intellectus addiscentis manuducitur in cognitionem veritatis ignotae. Alio modo, cum confortat intellectum addiscentis; non quidem aliqua virtute activa quasi superioris naturae, sicut supra dictum est de Angelis illuminantibus, quia omnes humani intellectus sunt unius gradus in ordine naturae; sed inquantum proponit discipulo ordinem principiorum ad conclusiones, qui forte per seipsum non haberet tantam virtutem collativam, ut ex principiis posset conclusiones deducere. Et ideo dicitur in I Poster., quod demonstratio est syllogismus faciens scire.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0022","text":"Et per hunc modum ille qui demonstrat, auditorem scientem facit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0023","text":"[I q. 117 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33324)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0024","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod, sicut iam dictum est, homo docens solummodo exterius ministerium adhibet, sicut medicus sanans, sed sicut natura interior est principalis causa sanationis, ita et interius lumen intellectus est principalis causa scientiae. Utrumque autem horum est a Deo. Et ideo sicut de Deo dicitur, qui sanat omnes infirmitates tuas; ita de eo dicitur, qui docet hominem scientiam, inquantum lumen vultus eius super nos signatur, per quod nobis omnia ostenduntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0025","text":"[I q. 117 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33325)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0026","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod doctor non causat scientiam in discipulo per modum agentis naturalis, ut Averroes obiicit. Unde non oportet quod scientia sit qualitas activa, sed est principium quo aliquis dirigitur in docendo, sicut ars est principium quo aliquis dirigitur in operando.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0027","text":"[I q. 117 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33326)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0028","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod magister non causat lumen intelligibile in discipulo, nec directe species intelligibiles, sed movet discipulum per suam doctrinam ad hoc, quod ipse per virtutem sui intellectus formet intelligibiles conceptiones, quarum signa sibi proponit exterius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0029","text":"[I q. 117 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33327)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0030","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod signa quae magister discipulo proponit, sunt rerum notarum in universali, et sub quadam confusione; sed ignotarum in particulari, et sub quadam distinctione. Et ideo cum quisque per seipsum scientiam acquirit, non potest dici docere seipsum, vel esse sui ipsius magister, quia non praeexistit in eo scientia completa, qualis requiritur in magistro.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0031","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0032","text":"[I q. 117 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33328)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0033","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod homines possint docere Angelos. Dicit enim apostolus, ad Ephes. III, *ut innotescat principibus et potestatibus in caelestibus per Ecclesiam multiformis sapientia Dei*. Sed Ecclesia est congregatio hominum fidelium. Ergo Angelis per homines aliqua innotescunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0034","text":"[I q. 117 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33329)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0035","text":"Praeterea, Angeli superiores, qui immediate de divinis a Deo illuminantur, inferiores Angelos instruere possunt, ut supra dictum est. Sed aliqui homines immediate de divinis per Dei verbum sunt instructi; sicut maxime patet de apostolis, secundum illud ad Heb. I, *novissime, diebus istis, locutus est nobis in filio*. Ergo aliqui homines aliquos Angelos docere potuerunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0036","text":"[I q. 117 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33330)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0037","text":"Praeterea, inferiores Angeli a superioribus instruuntur. Sed quidam homines superiores sunt aliquibus Angelis, cum ad supremos ordines Angelorum aliqui homines assumantur, ut Gregorius dicit in quadam homilia. Ergo aliqui inferiores Angeli per aliquos homines de divinis instrui possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0038","text":"[I q. 117 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33331)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0039","text":"Sed contra est quod Dionysius dicit, IV cap. de Div. Nom., quod omnes divinae illuminationes perferuntur ad homines mediantibus Angelis. Non ergo Angeli instruuntur per homines de divinis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0040","text":"[I q. 117 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33332)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0041","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra habitum est, inferiores Angeli loqui quidem possunt superioribus Angelis, manifestando eis suas cogitationes; sed de rebus divinis superiores ab inferioribus nunquam illuminantur. Manifestum est autem quod eo modo quo inferiores Angeli superioribus subduntur, supremi homines subduntur etiam infimis Angelorum. Quod patet per id quod dominus dicit, Matth. XI, *inter natos mulierum non surrexit maior Ioanne Baptista; sed qui minor est in regno caelorum, maior est illo*. Sic igitur de rebus divinis ab hominibus Angeli nunquam illuminantur. Cogitationes tamen suorum cordium homines Angelis per modum locutionis manifestare possunt, quia secreta cordium scire solius Dei est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0042","text":"[I q. 117 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33333)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0043","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Augustinus, V super Gen. ad Litt., sic exponit illam apostoli auctoritatem. Praemiserat enim apostolus, *mihi, omnium sanctorum minimo, data est gratia haec, illuminare omnes quae sit dispensatio sacramenti absconditi a saeculis in Deo. Ita dico absconditi, ut tamen innotesceret principibus et potestatibus in caelestibus, per Ecclesiam scilicet, multiformis sapientia Dei*. Quasi dicat, ita hoc sacramentum erat absconditum hominibus, ut tamen Ecclesiae caelesti, quae continetur in principibus et potestatibus, hoc sacramentum notum esset *a saeculis, non ante saecula, quia ibi primitus Ecclesia fuit, quo post resurrectionem et ista Ecclesia hominum congreganda est*. Potest tamen et aliter dici, quod *illud quod absconditum est, non tantum in Deo innotescit Angelis, verum etiam hic eis apparet, cum efficitur atque propalatur*, ut Augustinus ibidem subdit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0044","text":"Et sic dum per apostolos impleta sunt Christi et Ecclesiae mysteria, Angelis aliqua apparuerunt de huiusmodi mysteriis, quae ante erant eis occulta. Et per hunc modum potest intelligi quod Hieronymus dicit, quod, apostolis praedicantibus, Angeli aliqua mysteria cognoverunt, quia scilicet per praedicationem apostolorum huiusmodi mysteria explebantur in rebus ipsis, sicut praedicante Paulo convertebantur gentes; de quo apostolus ibi loquitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0045","text":"[I q. 117 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33334)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0046","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod apostoli instruebantur immediate a verbo Dei, non secundum eius divinitatem, sed inquantum eius humanitas loquebatur. Unde ratio non sequitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0047","text":"[I q. 117 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33335)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0048","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod aliqui homines, etiam in statu viae, sunt maiores aliquibus Angelis, non quidem actu, sed virtute; inquantum scilicet habent caritatem tantae virtutis, ut possint mereri maiorem beatitudinis gradum quam quidam Angeli habeant. Sicut si dicamus semen alicuius magnae arboris esse maius virtute quam aliquam parvam arborem, cum tamen multo minus sit in actu.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0049","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0050","text":"[I q. 117 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33336)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0051","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod homo per virtutem animae possit corporalem materiam immutare. Dicit enim Gregorius, II Dialog., quod *sancti miracula aliquando ex prece faciunt, aliquando ex potestate, sicut Petrus, qui Tabitham mortuam orando suscitavit, Ananiam et Saphiram mentientes morti increpando tradidit*. Sed in operatione miraculorum fit aliqua immutatio materiae corporalis. Ergo homines virtute suae animae possunt materiam corporalem immutare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0052","text":"[I q. 117 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33337)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0053","text":"Praeterea, super illud ad Gal. III, *quis vos fascinavit veritati non obedire?* Dicit Glossa quod *quidam habent* oculos urentes, qui solo aspectu inficiunt alios, et maxime pueros. Sed hoc non esset, nisi virtus animae posset materiam corporalem immutare. Ergo homo per virtutem suae animae potest materiam corporalem immutare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0054","text":"[I q. 117 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33338)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0055","text":"Praeterea, corpus humanum est nobilius quam alia inferiora corpora. Sed per apprehensionem animae humanae immutatur corpus humanum ad calorem et frigus, ut patet in irascentibus et timentibus; et quandoque etiam haec immutatio pervenit usque ad aegritudinem et mortem. Ergo multo magis anima hominis potest sua virtute materiam corporalem immutare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0056","text":"[I q. 117 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33339)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0057","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in III de Trin., quod *materia corporalis soli Deo obedit ad nutum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0058","text":"[I q. 117 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33340)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0059","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, sicut supra dictum est materia corporalis non immutatur ad formam, nisi vel ab agente aliquo composito ex materia et forma; vel ab ipso Deo, in quo virtualiter et materia et forma praeexistit, sicut in primordiali causa utriusque. Unde et de Angelis supra dictum est quod materiam corporalem immutare non possunt naturali virtute, nisi applicando corporalia agentia ad effectus aliquos producendos. Multo igitur minus anima sua virtute naturali potest immutare materiam corporalem, nisi mediantibus aliquibus corporibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0060","text":"[I q. 117 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33341)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0061","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod sancti dicuntur miracula facere ex potestate gratiae, non naturae. Quod patet per illud quod Gregorius ibidem dicit, *qui filii Dei ex potestate sunt, ut dicit Ioannes, quid mirum si signa facere ex potestate valeant?*","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0062","text":"[I q. 117 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33342)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0063","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod fascinationis causam assignavit Avicenna ex hoc, quod materia corporalis nata est obedire spirituali substantiae magis quam contrariis agentibus in natura. Et ideo quando anima fuerit fortis in sua imaginatione, corporalis materia immutatur secundum eam. Et hanc dicit esse causam oculi fascinantis. Sed supra ostensum est quod materia corporalis non obedit substantiae spirituali ad nutum, nisi soli creatori. Et ideo melius dicendum est, quod ex forti imaginatione animae immutantur spiritus corporis coniuncti. Quae quidem immutatio spirituum maxime fit in oculis, ad quos subtiliores spiritus perveniunt. Oculi autem inficiunt aerem continuum usque ad determinatum spatium, per quem modum specula, si fuerint nova et pura, contrahunt quandam impuritatem ex aspectu mulieris menstruatae, ut Aristoteles dicit in libro de Somn. et Vig.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0064","text":"Sic igitur cum aliqua anima fuerit vehementer commota ad malitiam, sicut maxime in vetulabus contingit, efficitur secundum modum praedictum aspectus eius venenosus et noxius, et maxime pueris, qui habent corpus tenerum, et de facili receptivum impressionis. Possibile est etiam quod ex Dei permissione, vel etiam ex aliquo facto occulto, cooperetur ad hoc malignitas Daemonum, cum quibus vetulae sortilegae aliquod foedus habent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0065","text":"[I q. 117 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33343)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0066","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod anima corpori humano unitur ut forma, et appetitus sensitivus, qui obedit aliqualiter rationi, ut supra dictum est, est actus alicuius organi corporalis. Et ideo oportet quod ad apprehensionem animae humanae, commoveatur appetitus sensitivus cum aliqua operatione corporali. Ad exteriora vero corpora immutanda apprehensio animae humanae non sufficit, nisi mediante immutatione proprii corporis, ut dictum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0067","text":"## Articulus 4","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0068","text":"[I q. 117 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33344)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0069","text":"Ad quartum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima hominis separata possit corpora saltem localiter movere. Substantiae enim spirituali naturaliter obedit corpus ad motum localem, ut supra dictum est. Sed anima separata est substantia spiritualis. Ergo suo imperio potest exteriora corpora movere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0070","text":"[I q. 117 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33345)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0071","text":"Praeterea, in itinerario Clementis dicitur, narrante Niceta ad Petrum, quod Simon magus per magicas artes pueri a se interfecti animam retinebat, per quam magicas operationes efficiebat. Sed hoc esse non potuisset sine aliqua corporum transmutatione, ad minus locali. Ergo anima separata habet virtutem localiter movendi corpora.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0072","text":"[I q. 117 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33346)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0073","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus dicit, in libro de anima quod anima non potest movere quodcumque corpus, sed solummodo proprium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0074","text":"[I q. 117 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33347)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0075","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod anima separata sua naturali virtute non potest movere aliquod corpus. Manifestum est enim quod, cum anima est corpori unita, non movet corpus nisi vivificatum, unde si aliquod membrum corporis mortificetur, non obedit animae ad motum localem. Manifestum est autem quod ab anima separata nullum corpus vivificatur. Unde nullum corpus obedit ei ad motum localem, quantum est ex virtute suae naturae, supra quam potest aliquid ei conferri virtute divina.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0076","text":"[I q. 117 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33348)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0077","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod substantiae quaedam spirituales sunt, quarum virtutes non determinantur ad aliqua corpora, sicut sunt Angeli, qui sunt naturaliter a corporibus absoluti, et ideo diversa corpora eis possunt obedire ad motum. Si tamen alicuius substantiae separatae virtus motiva determinetur naturaliter ad movendum aliquod corpus, non poterit illa substantia movere aliquod corpus maius, sed minus, sicut, secundum philosophos, motor inferioris caeli non posset movere caelum superius. Unde cum anima secundum suam naturam determinetur ad movendum corpus cuius est forma, nullum aliud corpus sua naturali virtute movere potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0078","text":"[I q. 117 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33349)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0079","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod, sicut dicit Augustinus X de Civ. Dei, et Chrysostomus super Matth., frequenter Daemones simulant se esse animas mortuorum, ad confirmandum gentilium errorem, qui hoc credebant. Et ideo credibile est quod Simon magus illudebatur ab aliquo Daemone, qui simulabat se esse animam pueri quem ipse occiderat.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"8282bd47e0ab486674551396e2155e488199071cdf64fea7d190ca57a0f853a1","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T155111514575Z_9447458d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 117","source_sha256":"8282bd47e0ab486674551396e2155e488199071cdf64fea7d190ca57a0f853a1","sha256":"3fcb2aee9c8c92869c2e1e107ae5492319056eb75f970837e9e8e8ed247fff43","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:51:11.514592+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0001","text":"## Preface","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0002","text":"[I q. 117 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33317)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0003","text":"Afterwards, we must consider those things that pertain to the action of man, who is composed of a spiritual and a corporeal creature. And first, we must consider the action of man; second, the propagation of man from man. Concerning the first, four questions are asked. First, whether one man can teach another, causing knowledge in him. Second, whether a man can teach an angel. Third, whether a man can, through the power of his soul, alter corporeal matter. Fourth, whether the separated soul of a man can move bodies by local motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0005","text":"[I q. 117 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33318)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that a man cannot teach another. For the Lord says, Matt. XXIII, *do not be called Rabbi*; where the Gloss of Jerome says, *lest you attribute divine honor to men*. Therefore, to be a master pertains properly to divine honor. But teaching is proper to a master. Therefore man cannot teach, but this is proper to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0007","text":"[I q. 117 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33319)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0008","text":"Further, if a man teaches another, this is only insofar as he acts through his knowledge to cause knowledge in another. But a quality through which someone acts to make something like himself is an active quality. Therefore it follows that knowledge is an active quality, just as heat is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0009","text":"[I q. 117 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33320)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0010","text":"Further, intelligible light and the species of the thing understood are required for knowledge. But one man can cause neither of these in another. Therefore one man cannot, by teaching, cause knowledge in another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0011","text":"[I q. 117 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33321)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0012","text":"Further, a teacher does nothing to a pupil except propose to him certain signs, either signifying something by words or by gestures. But by proposing signs, no one can teach another by causing knowledge in him. For he either proposes signs of known things or of unknown things. If of known things, then the one to whom the signs are proposed already has the knowledge, and does not acquire it from the master. But if of unknown things, he learns nothing through signs of this kind, just as if someone were to propose Greek words to a Latin speaker whose meaning he did not know, he could not thereby teach him. Therefore, in no way can one man, by teaching another, cause knowledge in him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0013","text":"[I q. 117 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33322)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0014","text":"But on the contrary, the Apostle says, I Tim. II, *in which I was appointed a preacher and apostle, a teacher of the gentiles in faith and truth*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0015","text":"[I q. 117 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33323)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0016","text":"I answer that it must be said that there were diverse opinions concerning this. For Averroes, in his Commentary on book III of *On the Soul*, maintained that there is one possible intellect for all men, as was said above. And from this it followed that the intelligible species are the same for all men. Accordingly, he maintains that one man does not by teaching cause in another a knowledge other than that which he himself has; rather, he communicates to him the same knowledge that he himself has, by moving him to order the phantasms in his soul so that they may be suitably disposed for intelligible apprehension. This opinion is indeed true in this respect: that the knowledge in pupil and master is the same, if identity is considered according to the unity of the thing known; for the truth of the thing is the same which both pupil and master know.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0017","text":"But insofar as he maintains that there is one possible intellect for all men, and the same intelligible species, differing only according to different phantasms, his opinion is false, as was established above. Another opinion is that of the Platonists, who maintained that knowledge is present from the beginning in our souls through participation in separate forms, as was established above; but that the soul, by its union with the body, is impeded from being able freely to consider those things of which it has knowledge. Accordingly, a pupil does not acquire knowledge anew from a master, but is stirred by him to consider those things of which he has knowledge; so that to learn is nothing other than to recollect. Likewise, they held that natural agents merely dispose matter for the reception of forms, which corporeal matter acquires through participation in separate species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0018","text":"But against this it was shown above that the possible intellect of the human soul is in pure potency with respect to intelligibles, according as Aristotle says in book III of *On the Soul*. And therefore it must be said otherwise: that the teacher causes knowledge in the learner by reducing him from potency to act, as is said in book VIII of the *Physics*. For the elucidation of this, it should be considered that among effects which are from an exterior principle, one is from an exterior principle alone; thus the form of a house is caused in matter solely by art. But another effect is sometimes from an exterior principle and sometimes from an interior principle; thus health is caused in a sick person sometimes by an exterior principle, namely, by the art of medicine, and sometimes by an interior principle, as when someone is healed by the power of nature. And in such effects two things must be noted.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0019","text":"First, that art imitates nature in its operation: for just as nature heals the sick person by altering, digesting, and expelling the matter that causes the disease, so also does art. Second, it should be noted that the exterior principle, namely art, does not operate as the principal agent, but as an assisting agent to the principal agent, which is the interior principle, by strengthening it and supplying it with instruments and aids which it may use to produce the effect; just as a physician strengthens nature and applies to it foods and medicines which nature may use for the intended end. Now knowledge is acquired in man both from an interior principle, as is clear in one who acquires knowledge through his own discovery, and from an exterior principle, as is clear in one who learns.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0020","text":"For in every man there is a certain principle of knowledge, namely, the light of the agent intellect, through which certain universal principles of all the sciences are naturally known at once from the beginning. When someone applies such universal principles to certain particulars, the memory and experience of which he receives through sense, he acquires through his own discovery knowledge of those things which he did not know, proceeding from known things to unknown. Hence every teacher leads his pupil, from those things which the pupil knows, to the knowledge of those things which he did not know; according as it is said in book I of the *Posterior Analytics*, that *all teaching and all learning come about from pre-existing knowledge*. Now a master leads a pupil from things previously known to the knowledge of unknown things in two ways.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0021","text":"First, by proposing to him certain aids or instruments which his intellect may use to acquire knowledge—for example, when he proposes to him certain less universal propositions, which the pupil can nevertheless judge from things previously known; or when he proposes certain sensible examples, or similitudes, or opposites, or things of this kind, through which the intellect of the learner is led by the hand to knowledge of an unknown truth. In another way, when he strengthens the intellect of the learner—not indeed by some active power as though of a superior nature, as was said above concerning angels who illuminate, since all human intellects are of one grade in the order of nature—but insofar as he proposes to the pupil the order from principles to conclusions, when perhaps the latter would not of himself have such power of reasoning as to be able to deduce conclusions from principles. And therefore it is said in book I of the *Posterior Analytics* that demonstration is a syllogism that produces knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0022","text":"And in this way, one who demonstrates makes his hearer knowing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0023","text":"[I q. 117 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33324)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0024","text":"Therefore, to the first it must be said that, as has already been stated, a man teaching furnishes only an exterior ministry, as does a physician healing; but just as interior nature is the principal cause of healing, so too the interior light of the intellect is the principal cause of knowledge. But both of these are from God. And therefore, just as it is said of God, who heals all your infirmities, so it is said of him, who teaches man knowledge, insofar as the light of his countenance is signed upon us, through which all things are shown to us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0025","text":"[I q. 117 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33325)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0026","text":"To the second, it must be said that the teacher does not cause knowledge in the pupil in the manner of a natural agent, as Averroes objects. Hence knowledge need not be an active quality, but is a principle by which someone is directed in teaching, just as art is a principle by which someone is directed in working.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0027","text":"[I q. 117 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33326)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0028","text":"To the third, it must be said that the teacher does not cause intelligible light in the pupil, nor intelligible species directly, but by his teaching moves the pupil to form, by the power of his own intellect, intelligible conceptions, the signs of which he sets before him externally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0029","text":"[I q. 117 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33327)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0030","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that the signs which the teacher sets before the pupil are of things known universally and under a certain confusion, but of things unknown particularly and under a certain distinction. And therefore, when anyone acquires knowledge by himself, he cannot be said to teach himself, or to be his own teacher, because complete knowledge of the kind required in a teacher does not pre-exist in him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0031","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0032","text":"[I q. 117 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33328)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0033","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that men can teach angels. For the Apostle says, in Ephesians III, *that through the Church the manifold wisdom of God may be made known to the principalities and powers in the heavens*. But the Church is the congregation of faithful men. Therefore, some things are made known to angels through men.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0034","text":"[I q. 117 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33329)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0035","text":"Further, the higher angels, who are immediately illuminated by God concerning divine things, can instruct the lower angels, as was said above. But some men have been instructed immediately concerning divine things by the Word of God; as is especially clear concerning the apostles, according to Hebrews I, *lastly, in these days, he has spoken to us in the Son*. Therefore, some men could have taught some angels.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0036","text":"[I q. 117 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33330)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0037","text":"Further, lower angels are instructed by higher ones. But certain men are higher than certain angels, since some men are assumed into the highest orders of angels, as Gregory says in a homily. Therefore, certain lower angels can be instructed concerning divine things by certain men.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0038","text":"[I q. 117 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33331)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0039","text":"But on the contrary, Dionysius says, in chapter IV of *On the Divine Names*, that all divine illuminations are conveyed to men through the mediation of angels. Therefore angels are not instructed concerning divine things by men.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0040","text":"[I q. 117 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33332)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0041","text":"I answer that, as was held above, lower angels can indeed speak to higher angels, making their thoughts known to them; but higher angels are never illuminated concerning divine things by lower ones. Now it is manifest that in the way lower angels are subject to higher angels, so the highest men are subject even to the lowest of the angels. This is clear from what the Lord says in Matthew XI: *among those born of women there has not arisen one greater than John the Baptist; yet he who is lesser in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he*. Thus, angels are never illuminated concerning divine things by men. Nevertheless, men can manifest the thoughts of their hearts to angels by way of speech, because to know the secrets of hearts belongs to God alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0042","text":"[I q. 117 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33333)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0043","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that Augustine, in Book V of *On the Literal Meaning of Genesis*, expounds that authority of the Apostle thus. For the Apostle had prefaced: *To me, the least of all the saints, this grace has been given, to enlighten all as to what is the dispensation of the mystery hidden from the ages in God. I say, hidden in such a way, however, that the manifold wisdom of God might be made known to the principalities and powers in the heavens, namely through the Church.* As though he were saying: this mystery was so hidden from men that it was nevertheless known from the ages to the heavenly Church, which is contained in the principalities and powers—not before the ages, because the Church was first there, to which this Church of men also is to be gathered after the resurrection. Yet it can also be said otherwise, that *what is hidden is made known to angels not only in God, but also appears to them here when it is brought about and revealed*, as Augustine adds in the same place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0044","text":"And thus, while the mysteries of Christ and of the Church were fulfilled through the apostles, certain things concerning such mysteries appeared to the angels which had previously been hidden from them. And in this way may be understood what Jerome says, that while the apostles were preaching, angels came to know certain mysteries, namely because through the preaching of the apostles such mysteries were being fulfilled in the things themselves, just as, while Paul was preaching, the Gentiles were converted; and it is of this that the Apostle speaks there.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0045","text":"[I q. 117 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33334)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0046","text":"To the second, it must be said that the apostles were instructed immediately by the Word of God, not according to his divinity, but insofar as his humanity spoke. Hence the argument does not follow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0047","text":"[I q. 117 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33335)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0048","text":"To the third, it must be said that certain men, even in the state of wayfarers, are greater than certain angels, not indeed in act, but in power; namely, insofar as they have charity of such great power that they can merit a greater degree of beatitude than certain angels possess. It is as if we were to say that the seed of some great tree is greater in power than some small tree, although it is much less in act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0049","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0050","text":"[I q. 117 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33336)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0051","text":"We proceed thus to the third. It seems that a human being can alter bodily matter by the power of the soul. For Gregory says, in *Dialogues* II, that *the saints sometimes work miracles by prayer, sometimes by power, as Peter, who by praying raised the dead Tabitha, and by rebuking Ananias and Sapphira for lying delivered them over to death*. But in the working of miracles some alteration of bodily matter takes place. Therefore, human beings can alter bodily matter by the power of their soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0052","text":"[I q. 117 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33337)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0053","text":"Further, on Gal. III, *who has bewitched you, that you should not obey the truth?* the Gloss says that *some have burning eyes, which by their gaze alone infect others, especially children*. But this would not be so unless the power of the soul could alter bodily matter. Therefore, a human being can alter bodily matter by the power of his soul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0054","text":"[I q. 117 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33338)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0055","text":"Further, the human body is nobler than other lower bodies. But through the apprehension of the human soul, the human body is altered as to heat and cold, as is evident in those who are angry and afraid; and sometimes this alteration even reaches illness and death. Therefore, much more can a human soul alter bodily matter by its power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0056","text":"[I q. 117 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33339)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0057","text":"On the contrary is what Augustine says, in *On the Trinity* III, that *bodily matter obeys God alone at a nod*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0058","text":"[I q. 117 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33340)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0059","text":"I answer that, as was said above, bodily matter is not altered to a form except either by some agent composed of matter and form, or by God himself, in whom both matter and form pre-exist virtually, as in the primordial cause of each. Hence it was also said above concerning the angels that they cannot alter bodily matter by natural power, except by applying bodily agents in order to produce certain effects. Therefore, still less can the soul by its natural power alter bodily matter, except through the mediation of certain bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0060","text":"[I q. 117 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33341)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0061","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the saints are said to work miracles by the power of grace, not of nature. This is clear from what Gregory says in the same place: *those who are sons of God by power, as John says, what wonder is it if they are able to perform signs by power?*","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0062","text":"[I q. 117 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33342)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0063","text":"To the second, it must be said that Avicenna assigned the cause of bewitchment on the ground that bodily matter is naturally apt to obey a spiritual substance more than contrary agents in nature. Therefore, when the soul is strong in its imagination, bodily matter is altered in accordance with it. And he says that this is the cause of the bewitching eye. But it was shown above that bodily matter does not obey a spiritual substance at a nod, except the Creator alone. Therefore, it is better to say that, from the soul's strong imagination, the spirits of the body conjoined to it are altered. This alteration of the spirits takes place especially in the eyes, to which the subtler spirits arrive. Now the eyes infect the continuous air up to a determinate distance; in this way mirrors, if they are new and clean, contract a certain impurity from the gaze of a menstruating woman, as Aristotle says in the book *On Sleep and Waking*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0064","text":"Thus, therefore, when some soul has been vehemently moved toward malice, as happens especially in old women, its gaze becomes, in the aforesaid manner, poisonous and harmful, especially to children, who have a tender body and readily receive an impression. It is also possible that, by God's permission, or even through some hidden deed, the malice of demons cooperates in this, with whom old sorceresses have some pact.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0065","text":"[I q. 117 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33343)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0066","text":"To the third, it must be said that the soul is united to the human body as form, and the sensitive appetite, which obeys reason in some measure, as was said above, is the act of some bodily organ. Therefore, at the apprehension of the human soul, the sensitive appetite must be moved along with some bodily operation. But the apprehension of the human soul is not sufficient to alter external bodies except through the mediation of an alteration of its own body, as has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0067","text":"## Article 4","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0068","text":"[I q. 117 a. 4 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33344)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0069","text":"We proceed thus to the fourth. It seems that a separated human soul can at least move bodies locally. For a body naturally obeys a spiritual substance as to local motion, as was said above. But a separated soul is a spiritual substance. Therefore, by its command it can move external bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0070","text":"[I q. 117 a. 4 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33345)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0071","text":"Further, it is said in the *Itinerary of Clement*, as Nicetas relates to Peter, that Simon Magus, by magical arts, retained the soul of a boy whom he had killed, through which he performed magical operations. But this could not have been without some transmutation of bodies, at least local. Therefore, a separated soul has the power of moving bodies locally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0072","text":"[I q. 117 a. 4 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33346)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0073","text":"On the contrary, the Philosopher says, in the book On the Soul, that the soul cannot move just any body, but only its own.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0074","text":"[I q. 117 a. 4 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33347)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0075","text":"I answer that it must be said that the separated soul cannot, by its natural power, move any body. For it is evident that, when the soul is united to the body, it moves no body except one that is made alive; hence, if any member of the body is deadened, it does not obey the soul with respect to local motion. Now it is evident that no body is made alive by the separated soul. Hence no body obeys it with respect to local motion, so far as belongs to the power of its nature, beyond which something can be conferred upon it by divine power.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0076","text":"[I q. 117 a. 4 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33348)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0077","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that there are certain spiritual substances whose powers are not determined to any bodies, as are the Angels, who are naturally free from bodies; and therefore different bodies can obey them in motion. If, however, the motive power of some separated substance is naturally determined to moving some body, that substance will not be able to move any greater body, but a lesser one; just as, according to the philosophers, the mover of the lower heaven would not be able to move the higher heaven. Hence, since the soul according to its nature is determined to move the body of which it is the form, it cannot by its natural power move any other body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0078","text":"[I q. 117 a. 4 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33349)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__109:P0079","text":"To the second, it must be said that, as Augustine says in Book X of The City of God, and Chrysostom on Matthew, demons frequently pretend to be the souls of the dead, in order to confirm the error of the gentiles, who believed this. And therefore it is credible that Simon Magus was deceived by some demon, who pretended to be the soul of the boy whom he himself had killed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__109:P0079"]}],"source_digest":"ec19cd1c833a0f3574cf6f04e24612de3791b52d12ff6df55a757c37c52d1bc9","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 117"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 117","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110","title":"First Part · Question 118","order":119,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC__110","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 118 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33350)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de traductione hominis ex homine. Et primo, quantum ad animam; secundo, quantum ad corpus. Circa primum quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum anima sensitiva traducatur cum semine. Secundo, utrum anima intellectiva. Tertio, utrum omnes animae fuerint simul creatae.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 118 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33351)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima sensitiva non traducatur cum semine, sed sit per creationem a Deo. Omnis enim substantia perfecta quae non est composita ex materia et forma, si esse incipiat, hoc non est per generationem, sed per creationem, quia nihil generatur nisi ex materia. Sed anima sensitiva est substantia perfecta, alioquin non posset movere corpus, et cum sit forma corporis, non est ex materia et forma composita. Ergo non incipit esse per generationem, sed per creationem.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33352)\n\nPraeterea, principium generationis in rebus viventibus est per potentiam generativam; quae, cum numeretur inter vires animae vegetabilis, est infra animam sensitivam. Nihil autem agit ultra suam speciem. Ergo anima sensitiva non potest causari per vim generativam animalis.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33353)\n\nPraeterea, generans generat sibi simile, et sic oportet quod forma generati sit actu in causa generationis. Sed anima sensitiva non est actu in semine, nec ipsa nec aliqua pars eius, quia nulla pars animae sensitivae est nisi in aliqua parte corporis; in semine autem non est aliqua corporis particula, quia nulla particula corporis est quae non fiat ex semine, et per virtutem seminis. Ergo anima sensitiva non causatur ex semine.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33354)\n\nPraeterea, si in semine est aliquod principium activum animae sensitivae, aut illud principium manet, generato iam animali; aut non manet. Sed manere non potest. Quia vel esset idem cum anima sensitiva animalis generati, et hoc est impossibile, quia sic esset idem generans et generatum, faciens et factum. Vel esset aliquid aliud, et hoc etiam est impossibile, quia supra ostensum est quod in uno animali non est nisi unum principium formale, quod est una anima. Si autem non manet, hoc etiam videtur impossibile, quia sic aliquod agens ageret ad corruptionem sui ipsius, quod est impossibile. Non ergo anima sensitiva potest generari ex semine.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33355)\n\nSed contra, ita se habet virtus quae est in semine, ad animalia quae ex semine generantur, sicut se habet virtus quae est in elementis mundi, ad animalia quae ex elementis mundi producuntur, sicut quae ex putrefactione generantur. Sed in huiusmodi animalibus animae producuntur ex virtute quae est in elementis; secundum illud Gen. I, *producant aquae reptile animae viventis*. Ergo et animalium quae generantur ex semine, animae producuntur ex virtute quae est in semine.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33356)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam posuerunt animas sensitivas animalium a Deo creari. Quae quidem positio conveniens esset, si anima sensitiva esset res subsistens, habens per se esse et operationem. Sic enim, sicut per se haberet esse et operationem, ita per se deberetur ei fieri. Et cum res simplex et subsistens non possit fieri nisi per creationem, sequeretur quod anima sensitiva procederet in esse per creationem. Sed ista radix est falsa, scilicet quod anima sensitiva per se habeat esse et operationem, ut ex superioribus patet, non enim corrumperetur, corrupto corpore. Et ideo, cum non sit forma subsistens, habet se in essendo ad modum aliarum formarum corporalium, quibus per se non debetur esse, sed esse dicuntur inquantum composita subsistentia per eas sunt. Unde et ipsis compositis debetur fieri. Et quia generans est simile generato, necesse est quod naturaliter tam anima sensitiva, quam aliae huiusmodi formae, producantur in esse ab aliquibus corporalibus agentibus transmutantibus materiam de potentia in actum, per aliquam virtutem corpoream quae est in eis. Quanto autem aliquod agens est potentius, tanto potest suam actionem diffundere ad magis distans, sicut quanto aliquod corpus est magis calidum, tanto ad remotius calefactionem producit. Corpora igitur non viventia, quae sunt inferiora naturae ordine, generant quidem sibi simile, non per aliquod medium, sed per seipsa; sicut ignis per seipsum generat ignem. Sed corpora viventia, tanquam potentiora, agunt ad generandum sibi simile et sine medio, et per medium. Sine medio quidem, in opere nutritionis, in quo caro generat carnem, cum medio vero, in actu generationis, quia ex anima generantis derivatur quaedam virtus activa ad ipsum semen animalis vel plantae, sicut et a principali agente derivatur quaedam vis motiva ad instrumentum. Et sicut non refert dicere quod aliquid moveatur ab instrumento, vel a principali agente; ita non refert dicere quod anima generati causetur ab anima generantis, vel a virtute derivata ab ipsa, quae est in semine.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33357)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod anima sensitiva non est substantia perfecta per se subsistens. Et de hoc supra dictum est, nec oportet hic iterare.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33358)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod virtus generativa non generat solum in virtute propria, sed in virtute totius animae, cuius est potentia. Et ideo virtus generativa plantae generat plantam; virtus vero generativa animalis generat animal. Quanto enim anima fuerit perfectior, tanto virtus eius generativa ordinatur ad perfectiorem effectum.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33359)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod illa vis activa quae est in semine, ex anima generantis derivata, est quasi quaedam motio ipsius animae generantis, nec est anima, aut pars animae, nisi in virtute; sicut in serra vel securi non est forma lecti, sed motio quaedam ad talem formam. Et ideo non oportet quod ista vis activa habeat aliquod organum in actu; sed fundatur in ipso spiritu incluso in semine, quod est spumosum, ut attestatur eius albedo. In quo etiam spiritu est quidam calor ex virtute caelestium corporum, quorum etiam virtute agentia inferiora agunt ad speciem, ut supra dictum est. Et quia in huiusmodi spiritu concurrit virtus animae cum virtute caelesti, dicitur quod homo generat hominem, et sol. Calidum autem elementare se habet instrumentaliter ad virtutem animae, sicut etiam ad virtutem nutritivam, ut dicitur in II de anima.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33360)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod in animalibus perfectis, quae generantur ex coitu, virtus activa est in semine maris, secundum philosophum in libro de Generat. Animal.; materia autem foetus est illud quod ministratur a femina. In qua quidem materia statim a principio est anima vegetabilis, non quidem secundum actum secundum, sed secundum actum primum, sicut anima sensitiva est in dormientibus. Cum autem incipit attrahere alimentum, tunc iam actu operatur. Huiusmodi igitur materia transmutatur a virtute quae est in semine maris, quousque perducatur in actum animae sensitivae, non ita quod ipsamet vis quae erat in semine, fiat anima sensitiva; quia sic idem esset generans et generatum; et hoc magis esset simile nutritioni et augmento, quam generationi, ut philosophus dicit. Postquam autem per virtutem principii activi quod erat in semine, producta est anima sensitiva in generato quantum ad aliquam partem eius principalem, tunc iam illa anima sensitiva prolis incipit operari ad complementum proprii corporis, per modum nutritionis et augmenti. Virtus autem activa quae erat in semine, esse desinit, dissoluto semine, et evanescente spiritu qui inerat. Nec hoc est inconveniens, quia vis ista non est principale agens, sed instrumentale; motio autem instrumenti cessat, effectu iam producto in esse.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 118 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33361)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima intellectiva causetur ex semine. Dicitur enim Gen. XLVI, *cunctae animae quae egressae sunt de femore Iacob, sexaginta sex*. Sed nihil egreditur de femore hominis, nisi inquantum causatur ex semine. Ergo anima intellectiva causatur ex semine.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33362)\n\nPraeterea, sicut supra ostensum est, in homine est una et eadem anima secundum substantiam, intellectiva, sensitiva et nutritiva. Sed anima sensitiva in homine generatur ex semine, sicut in aliis animalibus, unde et philosophus dicit in libro de Generat. Animal., quod non simul fit animal et homo, sed prius fit animal habens animam sensitivam. Ergo et anima intellectiva causatur ex semine.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33363)\n\nPraeterea, unum et idem agens, est cuius actio terminatur ad formam, et materiam, alioquin ex forma et materia non fieret unum simpliciter. Sed anima intellectiva est forma corporis humani, quod formatur per virtutem seminis. Ergo et anima intellectiva per virtutem seminis causatur.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33364)\n\nPraeterea, homo generat sibi simile secundum speciem. Sed species humana constituitur per animam rationalem. Ergo anima rationalis est a generante.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33365)\n\nPraeterea, inconveniens est dicere quod Deus cooperetur peccantibus. Sed si animae rationales crearentur a Deo, Deus interdum cooperaretur adulteris, de quorum illicito coitu proles interdum generatur. Non ergo animae rationales creantur a Deo.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33366)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in libro de Eccles. Dogmat., quod *animae rationales non seminantur per coitum*.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33367)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod impossibile est virtutem activam quae est in materia, extendere suam actionem ad producendum immaterialem effectum. Manifestum est autem quod principium intellectivum in homine est principium transcendens materiam, habet enim operationem in qua non communicat corpus. Et ideo impossibile est quod virtus quae est in semine, sit productiva intellectivi principii. Similiter etiam quia virtus quae est in semine agit in virtute animae generantis, secundum quod anima generantis est actus corporis, utens ipso corpore in sua operatione. In operatione autem intellectus non communicat corpus. Unde virtus intellectivi principii, prout intellectivum est, non potest ad semen pervenire. Et ideo philosophus, in libro de Generat. Animal., dicit, *relinquitur intellectus solus de foris advenire*. Similiter etiam anima intellectiva, cum habeat operationem sine corpore, est subsistens, ut supra habitum est, et ita sibi debetur esse et fieri. Et cum sit immaterialis substantia, non potest causari per generationem, sed solum per creationem a Deo. Ponere ergo animam intellectivam a generante causari, nihil est aliud quam ponere eam non subsistentem; et per consequens corrumpi eam cum corpore. Et ideo haereticum est dicere quod anima intellectiva traducatur cum semine.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33368)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod in auctoritate illa ponitur per synecdochen pars pro toto, idest anima pro toto homine.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33369)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod aliqui dixerunt quod operationes vitae quae apparent in embryone, non sunt ab anima eius, sed ab anima matris; vel a virtute formativa quae est in semine. Quorum utrumque falsum est, opera enim vitae non possunt esse a principio extrinseco, sicut sentire, nutriri et augeri. Et ideo dicendum est quod anima praeexistit in embryone a principio quidem nutritiva, postmodum autem sensitiva, et tandem intellectiva. Dicunt ergo quidam quod supra animam vegetabilem quae primo inerat, supervenit alia anima, quae est sensitiva; et supra illam iterum alia, quae est intellectiva. Et sic sunt in homine tres animae, quarum una est in potentia ad aliam. Quod supra improbatum est. Et ideo alii dicunt quod illa eadem anima quae primo fuit vegetativa tantum, postmodum, per actionem virtutis quae est in semine, perducitur ad hoc quod fiat etiam sensitiva; et tandem perducitur ad hoc ut ipsa eadem fiat intellectiva, non quidem per virtutem activam seminis, sed per virtutem superioris agentis, scilicet Dei deforis illustrantis. Et propter hoc dicit philosophus quod intellectus venit ab extrinseco. Sed hoc stare non potest. Primo quidem, quia nulla forma substantialis recipit magis et minus; sed superadditio maioris perfectionis facit aliam speciem, sicut additio unitatis facit aliam speciem in numeris. Non est autem possibile ut una et eadem forma numero sit diversarum specierum. Secundo, quia sequeretur quod generatio animalis esset motus continuus, paulatim procedens de imperfecto ad perfectum; sicut accidit in alteratione. Tertio, quia sequeretur quod generatio hominis aut animalis non sit generatio simpliciter, quia subiectum eius esset ens actu. Si enim a principio in materia prolis est anima vegetabilis, et postmodum usque ad perfectum paulatim perducitur; erit semper additio perfectionis sequentis sine corruptione perfectionis praecedentis. Quod est contra rationem generationis simpliciter. Quarto, quia aut id quod causatur ex actione Dei, est aliquid subsistens, et ita oportet quod sit aliud per essentiam a forma praeexistente, quae non erat subsistens; et sic redibit opinio ponentium plures animas in corpore. Aut non est aliquid subsistens, sed quaedam perfectio animae praeexistentis, et sic ex necessitate sequitur quod anima intellectiva corrumpatur, corrupto corpore; quod est impossibile. Est autem et alius modus dicendi, secundum eos qui ponunt unum intellectum in omnibus. Quod supra improbatum est. Et ideo dicendum est quod, cum generatio unius semper sit corruptio alterius, necesse est dicere quod tam in homine quam in animalibus aliis, quando perfectior forma advenit, fit corruptio prioris, ita tamen quod sequens forma habet quidquid habebat prima, et adhuc amplius. Et sic per multas generationes et corruptiones pervenitur ad ultimam formam substantialem, tam in homine quam in aliis animalibus. Et hoc ad sensum apparet in animalibus ex putrefactione generatis. Sic igitur dicendum est quod anima intellectiva creatur a Deo in fine generationis humanae, quae simul est et sensitiva et nutritiva, corruptis formis praeexistentibus.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33370)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ratio illa locum habet in diversis agentibus non ordinatis ad invicem. Sed si sint multa agentia ordinata, nihil prohibet virtutem superioris agentis pertingere ad ultimam formam; virtutes autem inferiorum agentium pertingere solum ad aliquam materiae dispositionem; sicut virtus seminis disponit materiam, virtus autem animae dat formam, in generatione animalis. Manifestum est autem ex praemissis quod tota natura corporalis agit ut instrumentum spiritualis virtutis; et praecipue Dei. Et ideo nihil prohibet quin formatio corporis sit ab aliqua virtute corporali, anima autem intellectiva sit a solo Deo.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33371)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod homo generat sibi simile, inquantum per virtutem seminis eius disponitur materia ad susceptionem talis formae.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33372)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod in actione adulterorum, illud quod est naturae, bonum est, et huic cooperatur Deus. Quod vero est inordinatae voluptatis, malum est, et huic Deus non cooperatur.\n\n## Articulus 3\n\n[I q. 118 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33373)\n\nAd tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod animae humanae fuerint creatae simul a principio mundi. Dicitur enim Gen. II, *requievit Deus ab omni opere quod patrarat*. Hoc autem non esset, si quotidie novas animas crearet. Ergo omnes animae sunt simul creatae.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33374)\n\nPraeterea, ad perfectionem universi maxime pertinent substantiae spirituales. Si igitur animae simul crearentur cum corporibus, quotidie innumerabiles spirituales substantiae perfectioni universi adderentur, et sic universum a principio fuisset imperfectum. Quod est contra illud quod dicitur Gen. II, *Deum omne opus suum complesse*.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33375)\n\nPraeterea, finis rei respondet eius principio. Sed anima intellectiva remanet, destructo corpore. Ergo incoepit esse ante corpus.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33376)\n\nSed contra est quod dicitur in libro de Eccles. Dogmat., quod *simul anima creatur cum corpore*.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33377)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod quidam posuerunt quod animae intellectivae accidat uniri corpori, ponentes eam esse eiusdem conditionis cum substantiis spiritualibus quae corpori non uniuntur. Et ideo posuerunt animas hominum simul a principio cum Angelis creatas. Sed haec opinio falsa est. Primo quidem, quantum ad radicem. Si enim accidentaliter conveniret animae corpori uniri, sequeretur quod homo, qui ex ista unione constituitur, esset ens per accidens; vel quod anima esset homo, quod falsum est, ut supra ostensum est. Quod etiam anima humana non sit eiusdem naturae cum Angelis, ipse diversus modus intelligendi ostendit, ut supra ostensum est, homo enim intelligit a sensibus accipiendo, et convertendo se ad phantasmata, ut supra ostensum est. Et ideo indiget uniri corpori, quo indiget ad operationem sensitivae partis. Quod de Angelo dici non potest. Secundo apparet falsitas in ipsa positione. Si enim animae naturale est corpori uniri, esse sine corpore est sibi contra naturam, et sine corpore existens non habet suae naturae perfectionem. Non fuit autem conveniens ut Deus ab imperfectis suum opus inchoaret, et ab his quae sunt praeter naturam, non enim fecit hominem sine manu aut sine pede, quae sunt partes naturales hominis. Multo igitur minus fecit animam sine corpore. Si vero aliquis dicat quod non est naturale animae corpori uniri, oportet inquirere causam quare sint corporibus unitae. Oportet autem dicere quod aut hoc sit factum ex eius voluntate; aut ex alia causa. Si ex eius voluntate, videtur hoc esse inconveniens. Primo quidem, quia haec voluntas irrationabilis esset, si non indigeret corpore, et vellet ei uniri, si enim eo indigeret, naturale esset ei quod corpori uniretur, quia natura non deficit in necessariis. Secundo, quia nulla ratio esset quare animae a principio mundi creatae, post tot tempora voluntas accesserit ut nunc corpori uniatur. Est enim substantia spiritualis supra tempus, utpote revolutiones caeli excedens. Tertio quia videretur a casu esse quod haec anima huic corpori uniretur, cum ad hoc requiratur concursus duarum voluntatum, scilicet animae advenientis, et hominis generantis. Si autem praeter voluntatem ipsius corpori unitur, et praeter eius naturam; oportet quod hoc sit ex causa violentiam inferente, et sic erit ei poenale et triste. Quod est secundum errorem Origenis, qui posuit animas incorporari propter poenam peccati. Unde cum haec omnia sint inconvenientia, simpliciter confitendum est quod animae non sunt creatae ante corpora, sed simul creantur cum corporibus infunduntur.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33378)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod Deus dicitur cessasse die septimo, non quidem ab omni opere, cum dicatur Ioan. V, *pater meus usque modo operatur*; sed a novis rerum generibus et speciebus condendis, quae in operibus primis non aliquo modo praeextiterint. Sic enim animae quae nunc creantur, praeextiterunt secundum similitudinem speciei in primis operibus, in quibus anima Adae creata fuit.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33379)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod perfectioni universi, quantum ad numerum individuorum, quotidie potest addi aliquid, non autem quantum ad numerum specierum.\n\n[I q. 118 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33380)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod hoc quod anima remanet sine corpore, contingit per corporis corruptionem, quae consecuta est ex peccato. Unde non fuit conveniens quod ab hoc inciperent Dei opera, quia, sicut scriptum est Sap. I, *Deus mortem non fecit, sed impii manibus et verbis accersierunt eam*.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0002","text":"[I q. 118 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33350)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de traductione hominis ex homine. Et primo, quantum ad animam; secundo, quantum ad corpus. Circa primum quaeruntur tria. Primo, utrum anima sensitiva traducatur cum semine. Secundo, utrum anima intellectiva. Tertio, utrum omnes animae fuerint simul creatae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0005","text":"[I q. 118 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33351)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima sensitiva non traducatur cum semine, sed sit per creationem a Deo. Omnis enim substantia perfecta quae non est composita ex materia et forma, si esse incipiat, hoc non est per generationem, sed per creationem, quia nihil generatur nisi ex materia. Sed anima sensitiva est substantia perfecta, alioquin non posset movere corpus, et cum sit forma corporis, non est ex materia et forma composita. Ergo non incipit esse per generationem, sed per creationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0007","text":"[I q. 118 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33352)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0008","text":"Praeterea, principium generationis in rebus viventibus est per potentiam generativam; quae, cum numeretur inter vires animae vegetabilis, est infra animam sensitivam. Nihil autem agit ultra suam speciem. Ergo anima sensitiva non potest causari per vim generativam animalis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0009","text":"[I q. 118 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33353)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0010","text":"Praeterea, generans generat sibi simile, et sic oportet quod forma generati sit actu in causa generationis. Sed anima sensitiva non est actu in semine, nec ipsa nec aliqua pars eius, quia nulla pars animae sensitivae est nisi in aliqua parte corporis; in semine autem non est aliqua corporis particula, quia nulla particula corporis est quae non fiat ex semine, et per virtutem seminis. Ergo anima sensitiva non causatur ex semine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0011","text":"[I q. 118 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33354)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0012","text":"Praeterea, si in semine est aliquod principium activum animae sensitivae, aut illud principium manet, generato iam animali; aut non manet. Sed manere non potest. Quia vel esset idem cum anima sensitiva animalis generati, et hoc est impossibile, quia sic esset idem generans et generatum, faciens et factum. Vel esset aliquid aliud, et hoc etiam est impossibile, quia supra ostensum est quod in uno animali non est nisi unum principium formale, quod est una anima. Si autem non manet, hoc etiam videtur impossibile, quia sic aliquod agens ageret ad corruptionem sui ipsius, quod est impossibile. Non ergo anima sensitiva potest generari ex semine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0013","text":"[I q. 118 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33355)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0014","text":"Sed contra, ita se habet virtus quae est in semine, ad animalia quae ex semine generantur, sicut se habet virtus quae est in elementis mundi, ad animalia quae ex elementis mundi producuntur, sicut quae ex putrefactione generantur. Sed in huiusmodi animalibus animae producuntur ex virtute quae est in elementis; secundum illud Gen. I, *producant aquae reptile animae viventis*. Ergo et animalium quae generantur ex semine, animae producuntur ex virtute quae est in semine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0015","text":"[I q. 118 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33356)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0016","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam posuerunt animas sensitivas animalium a Deo creari. Quae quidem positio conveniens esset, si anima sensitiva esset res subsistens, habens per se esse et operationem. Sic enim, sicut per se haberet esse et operationem, ita per se deberetur ei fieri. Et cum res simplex et subsistens non possit fieri nisi per creationem, sequeretur quod anima sensitiva procederet in esse per creationem. Sed ista radix est falsa, scilicet quod anima sensitiva per se habeat esse et operationem, ut ex superioribus patet, non enim corrumperetur, corrupto corpore. Et ideo, cum non sit forma subsistens, habet se in essendo ad modum aliarum formarum corporalium, quibus per se non debetur esse, sed esse dicuntur inquantum composita subsistentia per eas sunt. Unde et ipsis compositis debetur fieri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0017","text":"Et quia generans est simile generato, necesse est quod naturaliter tam anima sensitiva, quam aliae huiusmodi formae, producantur in esse ab aliquibus corporalibus agentibus transmutantibus materiam de potentia in actum, per aliquam virtutem corpoream quae est in eis. Quanto autem aliquod agens est potentius, tanto potest suam actionem diffundere ad magis distans, sicut quanto aliquod corpus est magis calidum, tanto ad remotius calefactionem producit. Corpora igitur non viventia, quae sunt inferiora naturae ordine, generant quidem sibi simile, non per aliquod medium, sed per seipsa; sicut ignis per seipsum generat ignem. Sed corpora viventia, tanquam potentiora, agunt ad generandum sibi simile et sine medio, et per medium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0018","text":"Sine medio quidem, in opere nutritionis, in quo caro generat carnem, cum medio vero, in actu generationis, quia ex anima generantis derivatur quaedam virtus activa ad ipsum semen animalis vel plantae, sicut et a principali agente derivatur quaedam vis motiva ad instrumentum. Et sicut non refert dicere quod aliquid moveatur ab instrumento, vel a principali agente; ita non refert dicere quod anima generati causetur ab anima generantis, vel a virtute derivata ab ipsa, quae est in semine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0019","text":"[I q. 118 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33357)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0020","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod anima sensitiva non est substantia perfecta per se subsistens. Et de hoc supra dictum est, nec oportet hic iterare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0021","text":"[I q. 118 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33358)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0022","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod virtus generativa non generat solum in virtute propria, sed in virtute totius animae, cuius est potentia. Et ideo virtus generativa plantae generat plantam; virtus vero generativa animalis generat animal. Quanto enim anima fuerit perfectior, tanto virtus eius generativa ordinatur ad perfectiorem effectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0023","text":"[I q. 118 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33359)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0024","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod illa vis activa quae est in semine, ex anima generantis derivata, est quasi quaedam motio ipsius animae generantis, nec est anima, aut pars animae, nisi in virtute; sicut in serra vel securi non est forma lecti, sed motio quaedam ad talem formam. Et ideo non oportet quod ista vis activa habeat aliquod organum in actu; sed fundatur in ipso spiritu incluso in semine, quod est spumosum, ut attestatur eius albedo. In quo etiam spiritu est quidam calor ex virtute caelestium corporum, quorum etiam virtute agentia inferiora agunt ad speciem, ut supra dictum est. Et quia in huiusmodi spiritu concurrit virtus animae cum virtute caelesti, dicitur quod homo generat hominem, et sol. Calidum autem elementare se habet instrumentaliter ad virtutem animae, sicut etiam ad virtutem nutritivam, ut dicitur in II de anima.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0025","text":"[I q. 118 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33360)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0026","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod in animalibus perfectis, quae generantur ex coitu, virtus activa est in semine maris, secundum philosophum in libro de Generat. Animal.; materia autem foetus est illud quod ministratur a femina. In qua quidem materia statim a principio est anima vegetabilis, non quidem secundum actum secundum, sed secundum actum primum, sicut anima sensitiva est in dormientibus. Cum autem incipit attrahere alimentum, tunc iam actu operatur. Huiusmodi igitur materia transmutatur a virtute quae est in semine maris, quousque perducatur in actum animae sensitivae, non ita quod ipsamet vis quae erat in semine, fiat anima sensitiva; quia sic idem esset generans et generatum; et hoc magis esset simile nutritioni et augmento, quam generationi, ut philosophus dicit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0027","text":"Postquam autem per virtutem principii activi quod erat in semine, producta est anima sensitiva in generato quantum ad aliquam partem eius principalem, tunc iam illa anima sensitiva prolis incipit operari ad complementum proprii corporis, per modum nutritionis et augmenti. Virtus autem activa quae erat in semine, esse desinit, dissoluto semine, et evanescente spiritu qui inerat. Nec hoc est inconveniens, quia vis ista non est principale agens, sed instrumentale; motio autem instrumenti cessat, effectu iam producto in esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0028","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0029","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33361)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0030","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod anima intellectiva causetur ex semine. Dicitur enim Gen. XLVI, *cunctae animae quae egressae sunt de femore Iacob, sexaginta sex*. Sed nihil egreditur de femore hominis, nisi inquantum causatur ex semine. Ergo anima intellectiva causatur ex semine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0031","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33362)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0032","text":"Praeterea, sicut supra ostensum est, in homine est una et eadem anima secundum substantiam, intellectiva, sensitiva et nutritiva. Sed anima sensitiva in homine generatur ex semine, sicut in aliis animalibus, unde et philosophus dicit in libro de Generat. Animal., quod non simul fit animal et homo, sed prius fit animal habens animam sensitivam. Ergo et anima intellectiva causatur ex semine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0033","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33363)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0034","text":"Praeterea, unum et idem agens, est cuius actio terminatur ad formam, et materiam, alioquin ex forma et materia non fieret unum simpliciter. Sed anima intellectiva est forma corporis humani, quod formatur per virtutem seminis. Ergo et anima intellectiva per virtutem seminis causatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0035","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33364)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0036","text":"Praeterea, homo generat sibi simile secundum speciem. Sed species humana constituitur per animam rationalem. Ergo anima rationalis est a generante.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0037","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33365)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0038","text":"Praeterea, inconveniens est dicere quod Deus cooperetur peccantibus. Sed si animae rationales crearentur a Deo, Deus interdum cooperaretur adulteris, de quorum illicito coitu proles interdum generatur. Non ergo animae rationales creantur a Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0039","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33366)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0040","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in libro de Eccles. Dogmat., quod *animae rationales non seminantur per coitum*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0041","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33367)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0042","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod impossibile est virtutem activam quae est in materia, extendere suam actionem ad producendum immaterialem effectum. Manifestum est autem quod principium intellectivum in homine est principium transcendens materiam, habet enim operationem in qua non communicat corpus. Et ideo impossibile est quod virtus quae est in semine, sit productiva intellectivi principii. Similiter etiam quia virtus quae est in semine agit in virtute animae generantis, secundum quod anima generantis est actus corporis, utens ipso corpore in sua operatione. In operatione autem intellectus non communicat corpus. Unde virtus intellectivi principii, prout intellectivum est, non potest ad semen pervenire. Et ideo philosophus, in libro de Generat. Animal., dicit, *relinquitur intellectus solus de foris advenire*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0043","text":"Similiter etiam anima intellectiva, cum habeat operationem sine corpore, est subsistens, ut supra habitum est, et ita sibi debetur esse et fieri. Et cum sit immaterialis substantia, non potest causari per generationem, sed solum per creationem a Deo. Ponere ergo animam intellectivam a generante causari, nihil est aliud quam ponere eam non subsistentem; et per consequens corrumpi eam cum corpore. Et ideo haereticum est dicere quod anima intellectiva traducatur cum semine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0044","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33368)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0045","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod in auctoritate illa ponitur per synecdochen pars pro toto, idest anima pro toto homine.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0046","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33369)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0047","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod aliqui dixerunt quod operationes vitae quae apparent in embryone, non sunt ab anima eius, sed ab anima matris; vel a virtute formativa quae est in semine. Quorum utrumque falsum est, opera enim vitae non possunt esse a principio extrinseco, sicut sentire, nutriri et augeri. Et ideo dicendum est quod anima praeexistit in embryone a principio quidem nutritiva, postmodum autem sensitiva, et tandem intellectiva. Dicunt ergo quidam quod supra animam vegetabilem quae primo inerat, supervenit alia anima, quae est sensitiva; et supra illam iterum alia, quae est intellectiva. Et sic sunt in homine tres animae, quarum una est in potentia ad aliam. Quod supra improbatum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0048","text":"Et ideo alii dicunt quod illa eadem anima quae primo fuit vegetativa tantum, postmodum, per actionem virtutis quae est in semine, perducitur ad hoc quod fiat etiam sensitiva; et tandem perducitur ad hoc ut ipsa eadem fiat intellectiva, non quidem per virtutem activam seminis, sed per virtutem superioris agentis, scilicet Dei deforis illustrantis. Et propter hoc dicit philosophus quod intellectus venit ab extrinseco. Sed hoc stare non potest. Primo quidem, quia nulla forma substantialis recipit magis et minus; sed superadditio maioris perfectionis facit aliam speciem, sicut additio unitatis facit aliam speciem in numeris. Non est autem possibile ut una et eadem forma numero sit diversarum specierum. Secundo, quia sequeretur quod generatio animalis esset motus continuus, paulatim procedens de imperfecto ad perfectum; sicut accidit in alteratione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0049","text":"Tertio, quia sequeretur quod generatio hominis aut animalis non sit generatio simpliciter, quia subiectum eius esset ens actu. Si enim a principio in materia prolis est anima vegetabilis, et postmodum usque ad perfectum paulatim perducitur; erit semper additio perfectionis sequentis sine corruptione perfectionis praecedentis. Quod est contra rationem generationis simpliciter. Quarto, quia aut id quod causatur ex actione Dei, est aliquid subsistens, et ita oportet quod sit aliud per essentiam a forma praeexistente, quae non erat subsistens; et sic redibit opinio ponentium plures animas in corpore. Aut non est aliquid subsistens, sed quaedam perfectio animae praeexistentis, et sic ex necessitate sequitur quod anima intellectiva corrumpatur, corrupto corpore; quod est impossibile. Est autem et alius modus dicendi, secundum eos qui ponunt unum intellectum in omnibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0050","text":"Quod supra improbatum est. Et ideo dicendum est quod, cum generatio unius semper sit corruptio alterius, necesse est dicere quod tam in homine quam in animalibus aliis, quando perfectior forma advenit, fit corruptio prioris, ita tamen quod sequens forma habet quidquid habebat prima, et adhuc amplius. Et sic per multas generationes et corruptiones pervenitur ad ultimam formam substantialem, tam in homine quam in aliis animalibus. Et hoc ad sensum apparet in animalibus ex putrefactione generatis. Sic igitur dicendum est quod anima intellectiva creatur a Deo in fine generationis humanae, quae simul est et sensitiva et nutritiva, corruptis formis praeexistentibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0051","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33370)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0052","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ratio illa locum habet in diversis agentibus non ordinatis ad invicem. Sed si sint multa agentia ordinata, nihil prohibet virtutem superioris agentis pertingere ad ultimam formam; virtutes autem inferiorum agentium pertingere solum ad aliquam materiae dispositionem; sicut virtus seminis disponit materiam, virtus autem animae dat formam, in generatione animalis. Manifestum est autem ex praemissis quod tota natura corporalis agit ut instrumentum spiritualis virtutis; et praecipue Dei. Et ideo nihil prohibet quin formatio corporis sit ab aliqua virtute corporali, anima autem intellectiva sit a solo Deo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0053","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33371)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0054","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod homo generat sibi simile, inquantum per virtutem seminis eius disponitur materia ad susceptionem talis formae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0055","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33372)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0056","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod in actione adulterorum, illud quod est naturae, bonum est, et huic cooperatur Deus. Quod vero est inordinatae voluptatis, malum est, et huic Deus non cooperatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0057","text":"## Articulus 3","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0058","text":"[I q. 118 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33373)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0059","text":"Ad tertium sic proceditur. Videtur quod animae humanae fuerint creatae simul a principio mundi. Dicitur enim Gen. II, *requievit Deus ab omni opere quod patrarat*. Hoc autem non esset, si quotidie novas animas crearet. Ergo omnes animae sunt simul creatae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0060","text":"[I q. 118 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33374)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0061","text":"Praeterea, ad perfectionem universi maxime pertinent substantiae spirituales. Si igitur animae simul crearentur cum corporibus, quotidie innumerabiles spirituales substantiae perfectioni universi adderentur, et sic universum a principio fuisset imperfectum. Quod est contra illud quod dicitur Gen. II, *Deum omne opus suum complesse*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0062","text":"[I q. 118 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33375)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0063","text":"Praeterea, finis rei respondet eius principio. Sed anima intellectiva remanet, destructo corpore. Ergo incoepit esse ante corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0064","text":"[I q. 118 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33376)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0065","text":"Sed contra est quod dicitur in libro de Eccles. Dogmat., quod *simul anima creatur cum corpore*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0066","text":"[I q. 118 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33377)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0067","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod quidam posuerunt quod animae intellectivae accidat uniri corpori, ponentes eam esse eiusdem conditionis cum substantiis spiritualibus quae corpori non uniuntur. Et ideo posuerunt animas hominum simul a principio cum Angelis creatas. Sed haec opinio falsa est. Primo quidem, quantum ad radicem. Si enim accidentaliter conveniret animae corpori uniri, sequeretur quod homo, qui ex ista unione constituitur, esset ens per accidens; vel quod anima esset homo, quod falsum est, ut supra ostensum est. Quod etiam anima humana non sit eiusdem naturae cum Angelis, ipse diversus modus intelligendi ostendit, ut supra ostensum est, homo enim intelligit a sensibus accipiendo, et convertendo se ad phantasmata, ut supra ostensum est. Et ideo indiget uniri corpori, quo indiget ad operationem sensitivae partis. Quod de Angelo dici non potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0068","text":"Secundo apparet falsitas in ipsa positione. Si enim animae naturale est corpori uniri, esse sine corpore est sibi contra naturam, et sine corpore existens non habet suae naturae perfectionem. Non fuit autem conveniens ut Deus ab imperfectis suum opus inchoaret, et ab his quae sunt praeter naturam, non enim fecit hominem sine manu aut sine pede, quae sunt partes naturales hominis. Multo igitur minus fecit animam sine corpore. Si vero aliquis dicat quod non est naturale animae corpori uniri, oportet inquirere causam quare sint corporibus unitae. Oportet autem dicere quod aut hoc sit factum ex eius voluntate; aut ex alia causa. Si ex eius voluntate, videtur hoc esse inconveniens. Primo quidem, quia haec voluntas irrationabilis esset, si non indigeret corpore, et vellet ei uniri, si enim eo indigeret, naturale esset ei quod corpori uniretur, quia natura non deficit in necessariis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0069","text":"Secundo, quia nulla ratio esset quare animae a principio mundi creatae, post tot tempora voluntas accesserit ut nunc corpori uniatur. Est enim substantia spiritualis supra tempus, utpote revolutiones caeli excedens. Tertio quia videretur a casu esse quod haec anima huic corpori uniretur, cum ad hoc requiratur concursus duarum voluntatum, scilicet animae advenientis, et hominis generantis. Si autem praeter voluntatem ipsius corpori unitur, et praeter eius naturam; oportet quod hoc sit ex causa violentiam inferente, et sic erit ei poenale et triste. Quod est secundum errorem Origenis, qui posuit animas incorporari propter poenam peccati. Unde cum haec omnia sint inconvenientia, simpliciter confitendum est quod animae non sunt creatae ante corpora, sed simul creantur cum corporibus infunduntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0070","text":"[I q. 118 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33378)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0071","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod Deus dicitur cessasse die septimo, non quidem ab omni opere, cum dicatur Ioan. V, *pater meus usque modo operatur*; sed a novis rerum generibus et speciebus condendis, quae in operibus primis non aliquo modo praeextiterint. Sic enim animae quae nunc creantur, praeextiterunt secundum similitudinem speciei in primis operibus, in quibus anima Adae creata fuit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0072","text":"[I q. 118 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33379)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0073","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod perfectioni universi, quantum ad numerum individuorum, quotidie potest addi aliquid, non autem quantum ad numerum specierum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0074","text":"[I q. 118 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33380)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0075","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod hoc quod anima remanet sine corpore, contingit per corporis corruptionem, quae consecuta est ex peccato. Unde non fuit conveniens quod ab hoc inciperent Dei opera, quia, sicut scriptum est Sap. I, *Deus mortem non fecit, sed impii manibus et verbis accersierunt eam*.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"248f956e7bc03d5a030c5d573a9ce873112d1d63e9caa7b547d53eee853d0482","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T155206686406Z_766c7a68","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 118","source_sha256":"248f956e7bc03d5a030c5d573a9ce873112d1d63e9caa7b547d53eee853d0482","sha256":"ad79f491c67f4183d08631849b81482786a612447bfaec36ef3c2eb218af2d74","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:52:06.686423+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0002","text":"[I q. 118 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33350)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0003","text":"Next, the propagation of man from man must be considered. First, with regard to the soul; second, with regard to the body. Concerning the first, three questions are asked. First, whether the sensitive soul is propagated with the seed. Second, whether the intellectual soul is. Third, whether all souls were created simultaneously.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0005","text":"[I q. 118 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33351)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that the sensitive soul is not propagated with the seed, but comes to be through creation by God. For every perfect substance that is not composed of matter and form, if it begins to exist, does so not through generation but through creation, because nothing is generated except from matter. But the sensitive soul is a perfect substance; otherwise it could not move the body; and, since it is the form of the body, it is not composed of matter and form. Therefore, it does not begin to exist through generation, but through creation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0007","text":"[I q. 118 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33352)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0008","text":"Further, the principle of generation in living things is through the generative power, which, since it is numbered among the powers of the vegetative soul, is below the sensitive soul. But nothing acts beyond its species. Therefore the sensitive soul cannot be caused by the generative power of an animal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0009","text":"[I q. 118 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33353)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0010","text":"Further, what generates generates its like, and thus the form of the thing generated must be actual in the cause of generation. But the sensitive soul is not actual in the seed, neither it itself nor any part of it, because no part of the sensitive soul exists except in some part of the body. But in the seed there is no bodily particle, because there is no particle of the body that is not made from the seed and through the power of the seed. Therefore the sensitive soul is not caused from the seed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0011","text":"[I q. 118 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33354)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0012","text":"Further, if there is in the seed some active principle of the sensitive soul, then either that principle remains once the animal has been generated, or it does not remain. But it cannot remain. For either it would be the same as the sensitive soul of the generated animal, and this is impossible, because then the generator and the generated, the maker and the thing made, would be the same. Or it would be something else, and this too is impossible, because it was shown above that in one animal there is only one formal principle, which is one soul. But if it does not remain, this too seems impossible, because then some agent would act toward its own corruption, which is impossible. Therefore the sensitive soul cannot be generated from the seed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0013","text":"[I q. 118 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33355)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0014","text":"On the contrary, the power that is in the seed is related to the animals generated from seed as the power that is in the elements of the world is related to animals produced from the elements of the world, such as those generated from putrefaction. But in animals of this kind, souls are produced from the power that is in the elements; according to Genesis 1: *let the waters bring forth the creeping thing of living soul*. Therefore, the souls also of animals generated from seed are produced from the power that is in the seed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0015","text":"[I q. 118 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33356)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0016","text":"I answer that it must be said that some have held that the sensitive souls of animals are created by God. This position would indeed be fitting if the sensitive soul were a subsistent thing, having being and operation through itself. For thus, just as it would have being and operation through itself, so it would be due to it to come to be through itself. And since a simple and subsistent thing can come to be only through creation, it would follow that the sensitive soul would proceed into being through creation. But this root is false, namely, that the sensitive soul has being and operation through itself, as is clear from what has been said above; for it would not be corrupted when the body is corrupted. And therefore, since it is not a subsistent form, it is related in being in the manner of other corporeal forms, to which being is not due through themselves, but which are said to be insofar as subsisting composites are through them. Hence coming to be is due also to the composites themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0017","text":"And because the generator is like the generated, it is necessary that, naturally, both the sensitive soul and other forms of this kind be produced into being by certain corporeal agents, which change matter from potency to act through some corporeal power that is in them. Now the more powerful an agent is, the farther it can diffuse its action, just as the hotter a body is, the farther away it produces heating. Therefore non-living bodies, which are lower in the order of nature, generate indeed their like not through any intermediary but through themselves; just as fire through itself generates fire. But living bodies, as more powerful, act to generate their like both without an intermediary and through an intermediary.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0018","text":"Without an intermediary, indeed, in the work of nutrition, in which flesh generates flesh; but through an intermediary in the act of generation, because from the soul of the generator a certain active power is derived to the seed itself of the animal or plant, just as from the principal agent a certain motive force is derived to the instrument. And just as it makes no difference whether one says that something is moved by the instrument or by the principal agent, so it makes no difference whether one says that the soul of the generated is caused by the soul of the generator or by a power derived from it, which is in the seed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0019","text":"[I q. 118 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33357)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0020","text":"Therefore, to the first, it must be said that the sensitive soul is not a perfect substance subsisting through itself. And this was said above, nor need it be repeated here.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0021","text":"[I q. 118 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33358)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0022","text":"To the second, it must be said that the generative power generates not only by its own power, but by the power of the whole soul, of which it is a power. And therefore the generative power of a plant generates a plant, whereas the generative power of an animal generates an animal. For the more perfect the soul is, the more its generative power is ordered to a more perfect effect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0023","text":"[I q. 118 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33359)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0024","text":"To the third, it must be said that the active force which is in the seed, derived from the soul of the generator, is as it were a certain motion of the soul of the generator itself; and it is not a soul, or a part of a soul, except virtually, just as in a saw or an axe there is not the form of a bed, but a certain motion toward such a form. And therefore it is not necessary that this active force have any organ actually; rather, it is founded in the very spirit enclosed in the seed, which is foamy, as its whiteness attests. In this spirit also there is a certain heat from the power of the celestial bodies, by whose power likewise lower agents act toward the species, as was said above. And because in such a spirit the power of the soul concurs with the celestial power, it is said that man generates man, and the sun. But elemental heat is related instrumentally to the power of the soul, just as also to the nutritive power, as is said in *On the Soul* II.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0025","text":"[I q. 118 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33360)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0026","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that in perfect animals, which are generated from coitus, the active power is in the male's semen, according to the Philosopher in the book *On the Generation of Animals*; but the matter of the foetus is that which is supplied by the female. In this matter, indeed, from the very beginning there is a vegetative soul, not according to second act, but according to first act, just as the sensitive soul is in those who sleep. But when it begins to draw nourishment, then it already operates in act. Matter of this sort, therefore, is transformed by the power which is in the male's semen, until it is brought to the act of the sensitive soul; not in such a way that the very power which was in the semen becomes the sensitive soul, for thus the generator and the generated would be the same; and this would be more like nutrition and growth than generation, as the Philosopher says.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0027","text":"But after, through the power of the active principle which was in the semen, the sensitive soul has been produced in the generated being as regards some principal part of it, then that sensitive soul of the offspring already begins to operate toward the completion of its own body, by way of nutrition and growth. But the active power which was in the semen ceases to be, once the semen has been dissolved and the spirit that was in it has vanished. Nor is this unfitting, because this power is not the principal agent, but an instrumental one; and the motion of the instrument ceases once the effect has already been produced in being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0028","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0029","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33361)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0030","text":"We proceed thus to the second. It seems that the intellective soul is caused from semen. For it is said in Gen. XLVI, *all the souls that went forth from Jacob's thigh, sixty-six*. But nothing goes forth from a man's thigh except insofar as it is caused from semen. Therefore the intellective soul is caused from semen.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0031","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33362)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0032","text":"Further, as was shown above, in man the intellective, sensitive, and nutritive soul is one and the same according to substance. But the sensitive soul in man is generated from semen, as in other animals; hence the Philosopher also says in the book *On the Generation of Animals* that an animal and a man do not come to be at the same time, but first an animal having a sensitive soul comes to be. Therefore the intellective soul too is caused from semen.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0033","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33363)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0034","text":"Further, one and the same agent is that whose action terminates in both form and matter; otherwise, one thing simply would not come to be from form and matter. But the intellective soul is the form of the human body, which is formed by the power of semen. Therefore the intellective soul too is caused by the power of semen.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0035","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33364)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0036","text":"Further, man generates one like himself according to species. But the human species is constituted through the rational soul. Therefore the rational soul is from the generator.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0037","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33365)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0038","text":"Further, it is unfitting to say that God cooperates with sinners. But if rational souls were created by God, God would sometimes cooperate with adulterers, from whose illicit intercourse offspring is sometimes generated. Therefore rational souls are not created by God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0039","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33366)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0040","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in the book *On Ecclesiastical Dogmas* that *rational souls are not sown through coitus*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0041","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33367)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0042","text":"I answer that it must be said that it is impossible for an active power that is in matter to extend its action to the production of an immaterial effect. But it is evident that the intellective principle in man is a principle transcending matter, for it has an operation in which the body does not share. And therefore it is impossible that the power which is in semen should be productive of the intellective principle. Likewise also because the power which is in semen acts by the power of the generating soul, insofar as the generating soul is the act of a body, using that body in its operation. But in the operation of the intellect the body does not share. Hence the power of the intellective principle, insofar as it is intellective, cannot reach the semen. And therefore the Philosopher, in the book *On the Generation of Animals*, says, *it remains that intellect alone comes from without*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0043","text":"Likewise, since the intellective soul has an operation without the body, it is subsistent, as was held above, and thus being and coming-to-be belong to it. And since it is an immaterial substance, it cannot be caused through generation, but only through creation by God. Therefore, to posit that the intellective soul is caused by the generator is nothing other than to posit that it is not subsistent; and consequently, that it is corrupted with the body. And therefore it is heretical to say that the intellective soul is transmitted with the semen.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0044","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33368)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0045","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that in that authority a part is put for the whole by synecdoche, that is, soul for the whole man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0046","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33369)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0047","text":"To the second, it must be said that some have said that the operations of life which appear in the embryo are not from its soul, but from the soul of the mother; or from the formative power which is in the semen. Both of these are false, for the works of life cannot be from an extrinsic principle, such as sensing, being nourished, and growing. And therefore it must be said that the soul pre-exists in the embryo: at first nutritive, afterward sensitive, and finally intellective. Some therefore say that above the vegetative soul which was present first, another soul supervenes, which is sensitive; and above that again another, which is intellective. And thus there are three souls in man, one of which is in potency to another. This was disproved above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0048","text":"And therefore others say that the same soul which was at first only vegetative is afterward, through the action of the power which is in the semen, brought to the point of becoming sensitive also; and finally is brought to the point that it itself, the same soul, becomes intellective, not indeed through the active power of the semen, but through the power of a higher agent, namely, God enlightening from without. And on this account the Philosopher says that intellect comes from without. But this cannot stand. First, indeed, because no substantial form receives more and less; rather, the addition of a greater perfection makes another species, just as the addition of unity makes another species in numbers. But it is not possible for one and the same form numerically to be of diverse species. Second, because it would follow that the generation of an animal would be a continuous motion, proceeding gradually from the imperfect to the perfect; as happens in alteration.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0049","text":"Third, because it would follow that the generation of a human being or an animal is not generation simply speaking, since its subject would be a being in act. For if from the beginning there is a vegetative soul in the matter of the offspring, and afterward it is gradually brought through to perfection, there will always be an addition of subsequent perfection without the corruption of preceding perfection. This is contrary to the notion of generation simply speaking. Fourth, because either that which is caused by God's action is something subsisting, and thus it must be essentially other than the pre-existing form, which was not subsisting; and thus the opinion of those positing several souls in the body will return. Or it is not something subsisting, but a certain perfection of the pre-existing soul, and thus it necessarily follows that the intellective soul is corrupted when the body is corrupted; which is impossible. There is also another way of speaking, according to those who posit one intellect in all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0050","text":"This was refuted above. And therefore it must be said that, since the generation of one thing is always the corruption of another, it is necessary to say that both in man and in other animals, when a more perfect form arrives, the prior one is corrupted, yet in such a way that the subsequent form has whatever the first had, and still more. And thus through many generations and corruptions one arrives at the ultimate substantial form, both in man and in other animals. And this appears to sense in animals generated from putrefaction. Thus, therefore, it must be said that the intellective soul is created by God at the end of human generation, and that it is at once both sensitive and nutritive, the pre-existing forms having been corrupted.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0051","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33370)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0052","text":"To the third, it must be said that that argument holds concerning diverse agents not ordered to one another. But if there are many agents ordered [to one another], nothing prevents the power of the higher agent from extending to the ultimate form, while the powers of the lower agents extend only to some disposition of matter; just as the power of the seed disposes matter, whereas the power of the soul gives the form, in the generation of an animal. But it is clear from what has been said that the whole of corporeal nature acts as an instrument of spiritual power, and especially of God. And therefore nothing prevents the formation of the body from being from some corporeal power, while the intellective soul is from God alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0053","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33371)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0054","text":"To the fourth, it must be said that a man generates one like himself, insofar as, through the power of his seed, matter is disposed to receive such a form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0055","text":"[I q. 118 a. 2 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33372)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0056","text":"To the fifth, it must be said that in the action of adulterers, that which belongs to nature is good, and God cooperates with this. But that which belongs to disordered pleasure is evil, and God does not cooperate with this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0057","text":"## Article 3","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0058","text":"[I q. 118 a. 3 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33373)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0059","text":"Thus one proceeds to the third. It seems that human souls were created simultaneously at the beginning of the world. For it is said in Gen. II, *God rested from all the work that He had accomplished*. But this would not be so if He created new souls daily. Therefore all souls were created simultaneously.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0060","text":"[I q. 118 a. 3 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33374)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0061","text":"Further, spiritual substances pertain most of all to the perfection of the universe. Therefore, if souls were created simultaneously with bodies, daily innumerable spiritual substances would be added to the perfection of the universe, and thus the universe would have been imperfect from the beginning. This is contrary to what is said in Gen. II, that *God completed all His work*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0062","text":"[I q. 118 a. 3 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33375)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0063","text":"Further, the end of a thing corresponds to its beginning. But the intellective soul remains when the body is destroyed. Therefore it began to exist before the body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0064","text":"[I q. 118 a. 3 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33376)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0065","text":"But on the contrary, it is said in the book *On Ecclesiastical Dogmas* that *the soul is created simultaneously with the body*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0066","text":"[I q. 118 a. 3 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33377)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0067","text":"I answer that it must be said that some have held that it happens accidentally to the intellective soul to be united to a body, maintaining it to be of the same condition as spiritual substances that are not united to a body. Therefore they held that the souls of men were created simultaneously with the angels at the beginning. But this opinion is false. First indeed, as to its root. For if it belonged accidentally to the soul to be united to a body, it would follow that man, who is constituted from this union, would be a being by accident; or that the soul would be man, which is false, as was shown above. That the human soul is not of the same nature as the angels is also shown by its different mode of understanding, as was shown above: for man understands by receiving from the senses and by turning himself toward phantasms, as was shown above. And therefore he needs to be united to a body, which he needs for the operation of the sensitive part. This cannot be said of an angel.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0068","text":"Second, the falsity is apparent in the position itself. For if it is natural to the soul to be united to a body, to be without a body is contrary to its nature, and, existing without a body, it does not have the perfection of its nature. But it was not fitting that God should begin His work from imperfect things, and from things that are contrary to nature; for He did not make man without a hand or without a foot, which are natural parts of man. Much less, therefore, did He make a soul without a body. But if someone says that it is not natural to the soul to be united to a body, one must inquire into the cause why they are united to bodies. It must, however, be said that either this is done by its will or by another cause. If by its will, this seems unfitting. First indeed, because this will would be irrational if it did not need the body and yet wished to be united to it; for if it needed it, it would be natural to it to be united to a body, because nature does not fail in necessities.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0069","text":"Second, there would be no reason why, after souls had been created at the beginning of the world, the will should have arisen after so many times for one now to be united to a body. For a spiritual substance is above time, as surpassing the revolutions of heaven. Third, it would seem to be by chance that this soul should be united to this body, since for this there is required the concurrence of two wills, namely, that of the arriving soul and that of the generating man. But if it is united to a body apart from its own will and apart from its nature, this must be from a cause inflicting violence, and thus it will be penal and sorrowful to it. This accords with the error of Origen, who held that souls are embodied because of the punishment of sin. Hence, since all these things are unfitting, it must simply be confessed that souls are not created before bodies, but are created and infused simultaneously with bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0070","text":"[I q. 118 a. 3 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33378)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0071","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that God is said to have ceased on the seventh day, not indeed from every work, since it is said in John V, *My Father works even until now*; but from establishing new kinds and species of things that had not pre-existed in some way in the first works. For in this way the souls that are now created pre-existed according to the likeness of the species in the first works, in which the soul of Adam was created.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0072","text":"[I q. 118 a. 3 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33379)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0073","text":"To the second, it must be said that something can be added daily to the perfection of the universe, as regards the number of individuals, but not as regards the number of species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0074","text":"[I q. 118 a. 3 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33380)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__110:P0075","text":"To the third, it must be said that the fact that the soul remains without the body occurs through the corruption of the body, which followed from sin. Hence it was not fitting that the works of God should begin from this, because, as it is written in Wisdom I, *God did not make death, but the ungodly summoned it by their hands and words*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__110:P0075"]}],"source_digest":"99e3e7ec0213bf08ad2c6b280de911db6f635dbcffc14619a2222a7355d8c357","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 118"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 118","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111","title":"First Part · Question 119","order":120,"chapter_id":"CH_GGVC__111","text":"## Prooemium\n\n[I q. 119 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33381)\n\nDeinde considerandum est de propagatione hominis quantum ad corpus. Et circa hoc quaeruntur duo. Primo, utrum aliquid de alimento convertatur in veritatem humanae naturae. Secundo, utrum semen, quod est humanae generationis principium, sit de superfluo alimenti.\n\n## Articulus 1\n\n[I q. 119 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33382)\n\nAd primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nihil de alimento transeat in veritatem humanae naturae. Dicitur enim Matth. XV, *omne quod in os intrat, in ventrem vadit, et per secessum emittitur*. Sed quod emittitur, non transit in veritatem humanae naturae. Ergo nihil de alimento in veritatem humanae naturae transit.\n\n[I q. 119 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33383)\n\nPraeterea, philosophus, in I de Generat., distinguit carnem secundum speciem, et secundum materiam; et dicit quod caro secundum materiam advenit et recedit. Quod autem ex alimento generatur, advenit et recedit. Ergo id in quod alimentum convertitur, est caro secundum materiam, non autem caro secundum speciem. Sed hoc pertinet ad veritatem humanae naturae, quod pertinet ad speciem eius. Ergo alimentum non transit in veritatem humanae naturae.\n\n[I q. 119 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33384)\n\nPraeterea, ad veritatem humanae naturae pertinere videtur humidum radicale; quod si deperdatur, restitui non potest, ut medici dicunt. Posset autem restitui, si alimentum converteretur in ipsum humidum. Ergo nutrimentum non convertitur in veritatem humanae naturae.\n\n[I q. 119 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33385)\n\nPraeterea, si alimentum transiret in veritatem humanae naturae, quidquid in homine deperditur, restaurari posset. Sed mors hominis non accidit nisi per deperditionem alicuius. Posset igitur homo per sumptionem alimenti in perpetuum se contra mortem tueri.\n\n[I q. 119 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33386)\n\nPraeterea, si alimentum in veritatem humanae naturae transiret, nihil esset in homine quod non posset recedere et reparari, quia id quod in homine generatur ex alimento, et recedere et reparari potest. Si ergo homo diu viveret, sequeretur quod nihil quod in eo fuit materialiter in principio suae generationis, finaliter remaneret in ipso. Et sic non esset idem homo numero per totam vitam suam, cum ad hoc quod aliquid sit idem numero, requiratur identitas materiae. Hoc autem est inconveniens. Non ergo alimentum transit in veritatem humanae naturae.\n\n[I q. 119 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33387)\n\nSed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro de vera Relig., *alimenta carnis corrupta, idest amittentia formam suam, in membrorum fabricam migrant*. Sed fabrica membrorum pertinet ad veritatem humanae naturae. Ergo alimenta transeunt in veritatem humanae naturae.\n\n[I q. 119 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33388)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod, secundum philosophum, II Metaphys., *hoc modo se habet unumquodque ad veritatem, sicut se habet ad esse*. Illud ergo pertinet ad veritatem naturae alicuius, quod est de constitutione naturae ipsius. Sed natura dupliciter considerari potest, uno modo, in communi, secundum rationem speciei; alio modo, secundum quod est in hoc individuo. Ad veritatem igitur naturae alicuius in communi consideratae, pertinet forma et materia eius in communi accepta, ad veritatem autem naturae in hoc particulari consideratae, pertinet materia individualis signata, et forma per huiusmodi materiam individuata. Sicut de veritate humanae naturae in communi, est anima humana et corpus, sed de veritate humanae naturae in Petro et Martino, est haec anima et hoc corpus. Sunt autem quaedam, quorum formae non possunt salvari nisi in una materia signata, sicut forma solis non potest salvari nisi in materia quae actu sub ea continetur. Et secundum hunc modum, aliqui posuerunt quod forma humana non potest salvari nisi in materia quadam signata, quae scilicet a principio fuit tali forma formata in primo homine. Ita quod quidquid aliud praeter illud quod ex primo parente in posteros derivatur, additum fuerit, non pertinet ad veritatem humanae naturae, quasi non vere accipiat formam humanae naturae. Sed illa materia quae in primo homine formae humanae fuit subiecta, in seipsa multiplicatur, et hoc modo multitudo humanorum corporum a corpore primi hominis derivatur. Et secundum hos, alimentum non convertitur in veritatem humanae naturae, sed dicunt quod alimentum accipitur ut quoddam fomentum naturae, idest ut resistat actioni caloris naturalis, ne consumat humidum radicale; sicut plumbum vel stannum adiungitur argento, ne consumatur per ignem. Sed haec positio est multipliciter irrationabilis. Primo quidem, quia eiusdem rationis est quod aliqua forma possit fieri in alia materia, et quod possit propriam materiam deserere, et ideo omnia generabilia sunt corruptibilia, et e converso. Manifestum est autem quod forma humana potest deficere ab hac materia quae ei subiicitur, alioquin corpus humanum corruptibile non esset. Unde relinquitur quod et alii materiae advenire possit, aliquo alio in veritatem humanae naturae transeunte. Secundo, quia in omnibus quorum materia invenitur tota sub uno individuo, non est nisi unum individuum in una specie, sicut patet in sole et luna, et huiusmodi. Sic igitur non esset nisi unum individuum humanae speciei. Tertio, quia non est possibile quod multiplicatio materiae attendatur nisi vel secundum quantitatem tantum, sicut accidit in rarefactis, quorum materia suscipit maiores dimensiones; vel etiam secundum substantiam materiae. Sola autem eadem substantia materiae manente, non potest dici quod sit multiplicata, quia idem ad seipsum non constituit multitudinem, cum necesse sit omnem multitudinem ex aliqua divisione causari. Unde oportet quod aliqua alia substantia materiae adveniat, vel per creationem, vel per conversionem alterius in ipsam. Unde relinquitur quod non potest aliqua materia multiplicari, nisi vel per rarefactionem, sicut cum ex aqua fit aer; vel per conversionem alterius rei, sicut multiplicatur ignis per additionem lignorum; vel per creationem materiae. Sed manifestum est multiplicationem materiae in humanis corporibus non accidere per rarefactionem, quia sic corpora hominum perfectae aetatis essent imperfectiora quam corpora puerorum. Nec iterum per creationem novae materiae, quia secundum Gregorium, *omnia sunt simul creata secundum substantiam materiae, licet non secundum speciem formae*. Unde relinquitur quod multiplicatio corporis humani non fit nisi per hoc, quod alimentum convertitur in veritatem humani corporis. Quarto, quia cum homo non differat ab animalibus et plantis secundum animam vegetabilem, sequeretur quod etiam corpora animalium et plantarum non multiplicarentur per conversionem alimenti in corpus nutritum, sed per quandam multiplicationem. Quae non potest esse naturalis, cum materia secundum naturam non extendatur nisi usque ad certam quantitatem; nec iterum inveniatur aliquid naturaliter crescere, nisi per rarefactionem, vel conversionem alterius in ipsum. Et sic totum opus generativae et nutritivae, quae dicuntur vires naturales, esset miraculosum. Quod est omnino inconveniens. Unde alii dixerunt quod forma humana potest quidem fieri de novo in aliqua alia materia, si consideretur natura humana in communi, non autem si accipiatur prout est in hoc individuo, in quo forma humana fixa manet in quadam materia determinata, cui primo imprimitur in generatione huius individui, ita quod illam materiam nunquam deserit usque ad ultimam individui corruptionem. Et hanc materiam dicunt principaliter pertinere ad veritatem humanae naturae. Sed quia huiusmodi materia non sufficit ad quantitatem debitam, requiritur ut adveniat alia materia per conversionem alimenti in substantiam nutriti, quantum sufficiat ad debitum augmentum. Et hanc materiam dicunt secundario pertinere ad veritatem humanae naturae, quia non requiritur ad primum esse individui, sed ad quantitatem eius. Iam vero si quid aliud advenit ex alimento, non pertinet ad veritatem humanae naturae, proprie loquendo. Sed hoc etiam est inconveniens. Primo quidem, quia haec opinio iudicat de materia corporum viventium, ad modum corporum inanimatorum; in quibus etsi sit virtus ad generandum simile in specie, non tamen est virtus in eis ad generandum aliquid sibi simile secundum individuum; quae quidem virtus in corporibus viventibus est virtus nutritiva. Nihil ergo per virtutem nutritivam adderetur corporibus viventibus, si alimentum in veritatem naturae ipsorum non converteretur. Secundo quia virtus activa quae est in semine, est quaedam impressio derivata ab anima generantis, sicut supra dictum est. Unde non potest esse maioris virtutis in agendo, quam ipsa anima a qua derivatur. Si ergo ex virtute seminis vere assumit aliqua materia formam naturae humanae, multo magis anima in nutrimentum coniunctum poterit veram formam naturae humanae imprimere per potentiam nutritivam. Tertio, quia nutrimento indigetur non solum ad augmentum, alioquin terminato augmento necessarium non esset, sed etiam ad restaurandum illud quod deperditur per actionem caloris naturalis. Non autem esset restauratio, nisi id quod ex alimento generatur, succederet in locum deperditi. Unde sicut id quod primo inerat, est de veritate humanae naturae, ita et id quod ex alimento generatur. Et ideo, secundum alios, dicendum est quod alimentum vere convertitur in veritatem humanae naturae, inquantum vere accipit speciem carnis et ossis et huiusmodi partium. Et hoc est quod dicit philosophus in II de anima, quod *alimentum nutrit inquantum est potentia caro*.\n\n[I q. 119 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33389)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod dominus non dicit quod totum quod in os intrat, per secessum emittatur, sed omne, quia de quolibet cibo aliquid impurum per secessum emittitur. Vel potest dici quod quidquid ex alimento generatur, potest etiam per calorem naturalem resolvi, et per poros quosdam occultos emitti, ut Hieronymus exponit.\n\n[I q. 119 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33390)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod aliqui per carnem secundum speciem intellexerunt id quod primo accipit speciem humanam, quod sumitur a generante, et hoc dicunt semper manere, quousque individuum durat. Carnem vero secundum materiam dicunt esse quae generatur ex alimento, et hanc dicunt non semper permanere, sed quod sicut advenit, ita abscedit. Sed hoc est contra intentionem Aristotelis. Dicit enim ibi quod, sicut in unoquoque habentium speciem in materia, puta in ligno et lapide, *ita et in carne hoc est secundum speciem, et illud secundum materiam*. Manifestum est autem quod praedicta distinctio locum non habet in rebus inanimatis, quae non generantur ex semine, nec nutriuntur. Et iterum, cum id quod ex alimento generatur, adiungatur corpori nutrito per modum mixtionis, sicut aqua miscetur vino, ut ponit exemplum ibidem philosophus; non potest alia esse natura eius quod advenit, et eius cui advenit, cum iam sit factum unum per veram mixtionem. Unde nulla ratio est quod unum consumatur per calorem naturalem, et alterum maneat. Et ideo aliter dicendum est, quod haec distinctio philosophi non est secundum diversas carnes, sed est eiusdem carnis secundum diversam considerationem. Si enim consideretur caro secundum speciem, idest secundum id quod est formale in ipsa, sic semper manet, quia semper manet natura carnis, et dispositio naturalis ipsius. Sed si consideretur caro secundum materiam, sic non manet, sed paulatim consumitur et restauratur, sicut patet in igne fornacis, cuius forma manet, sed materia paulatim consumitur, et alia in locum eius substituitur.\n\n[I q. 119 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33391)\n\nAd tertium dicendum quod ad humidum radicale intelligitur pertinere totum id in quo fundatur virtus speciei. Quod si subtrahatur, restitui non potest, sicut si amputetur manus aut pes, vel aliquid huiusmodi. Sed humidum nutrimentale est quod nondum pervenit ad suscipiendum perfecte naturam speciei, sed est in via ad hoc; sicut est sanguis, et alia huiusmodi. Unde si talia subtrahantur, remanet adhuc virtus speciei in radice, quae non tollitur.\n\n[I q. 119 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33392)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod omnis virtus in corpore passibili per continuam actionem debilitatur, quia huiusmodi agentia etiam patiuntur. Et ideo virtus conversiva in principio quidem tam fortis est, ut possit convertere non solum quod sufficit ad restaurationem deperditi, sed etiam ad augmentum. Postea vero non potest convertere nisi quantum sufficit ad restaurationem deperditi, et tunc cessat augmentum. Demum nec hoc potest, et tunc fit diminutio. Deinde, deficiente huiusmodi virtute totaliter, animal moritur. Sicut virtus vini convertentis aquam admixtam, paulatim per admixtionem aquae debilitatur, ut tandem totum fiat aquosum, ut philosophus exemplificat in I de Generat.\n\n[I q. 119 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33393)\n\nAd quintum dicendum quod, sicut philosophus dicit in I de Generat. quando aliqua materia per se convertitur in ignem, tunc dicitur ignis de novo generari, quando vero aliqua materia convertitur in ignem praeexistentem, dicitur ignis nutriri. Unde si tota materia simul amittat speciem ignis, et alia materia convertatur in ignem, erit alius ignis numero. Si vero, paulatim combusto uno ligno, aliud substituatur, et sic deinceps quousque omnia prima consumantur, semper remanet idem ignis numero, quia semper quod additur, transit in praeexistens. Et similiter est intelligendum in corporibus viventibus, in quibus ex nutrimento restauratur id quod per calorem naturalem consumitur.\n\n## Articulus 2\n\n[I q. 119 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33394)\n\nAd secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod semen non sit de superfluo alimenti, sed de substantia generantis. Dicit enim Damascenus quod *generatio est opus naturae ex substantia generantis producens quod generatur*. Sed id quod generatur, generatur ex semine. Ergo semen est de substantia generantis.\n\n[I q. 119 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33395)\n\nPraeterea, secundum hoc filius assimilatur patri, quod ab eo aliquid accipit. Sed si semen ex quo aliquid generatur, sit de superfluo alimenti; nihil acciperet aliquis ab avo et praecedentibus, in quibus hoc alimentum nullo modo fuit. Ergo non assimilaretur aliquis avo et praecedentibus, magis quam aliis hominibus.\n\n[I q. 119 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33396)\n\nPraeterea, alimentum hominis generantis quandoque est ex carnibus bovis, vel porci, et aliorum huiusmodi. Si igitur semen esset de superfluo alimenti, homo generatus ex semine maiorem affinitatem haberet cum bove et porco, quam cum patre et aliis consanguineis.\n\n[I q. 119 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33397)\n\nPraeterea, Augustinus dicit, X super Gen. ad Litt., quod nos fuimus in Adam non solum secundum *seminalem rationem, sed etiam secundum corpulentam substantiam*. Hoc autem non esset, si semen esset ex superfluo alimenti. Ergo semen non est superfluo alimenti.\n\n[I q. 119 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33398)\n\nSed contra est quod philosophus probat multipliciter, in libro de Generat. Animal., *quod semen est superfluum alimenti*.\n\n[I q. 119 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33399)\n\nRespondeo dicendum quod ista quaestio aliqualiter dependet ex praemissis. Si enim in natura humana est virtus ad communicandum suam formam materiae alienae non solum in alio, sed etiam in ipso; manifestum est quod alimentum, quod est in principio dissimile, in fine fit simile per formam communicatam. Est autem naturalis ordo ut aliquid gradatim de potentia reducatur in actum, et ideo in his quae generantur, invenimus quod primo unumquodque est imperfectum, et postea perficitur. Manifestum est autem quod commune se habet ad proprium et determinatum, ut imperfectum ad perfectum, et ideo videmus quod in generatione animalis prius generatur animal, quam homo vel equus. Sic igitur et ipsum alimentum primo quidem accipit quandam virtutem communem respectu omnium partium corporis, et in fine determinatur ad hanc partem vel ad illam. Non autem est possibile quod accipiatur pro semine id quod iam conversum est in substantiam membrorum, per quandam resolutionem. Quia illud resolutum, si non retineret naturam eius a quo resolvitur, tunc iam esset recedens a natura generantis, quasi in via corruptionis existens; et sic non haberet virtutem convertendi aliud in similem naturam. Si vero retineret naturam eius a quo resolvitur, tunc, cum esset contractum ad determinatam partem, non haberet virtutem movendi ad naturam totius, sed solum ad naturam partis. Nisi forte quis dicat quod esset resolutum ab omnibus partibus corporis, et quod retineat naturam omnium partium. Et sic semen esset quasi quoddam parvum animal in actu; et generatio animalis ex animali non esset nisi per divisionem, sicut lutum generatur ex luto, et sicut accidit in animalibus quae decisa vivunt. Hoc autem est inconveniens. Relinquitur ergo quod semen non sit decisum ab eo quod erat actu totum; sed magis sit in potentia totum, habens virtutem ad productionem totius corporis, derivatam an anima generantis, ut supra dictum est. Hoc autem quod est in potentia ad totum, est illud quod generatur ex alimento, antequam convertatur in substantiam membrorum. Et ideo ex hoc semen accipitur. Et secundum hoc, virtus nutritiva dicitur deservire generativae, quia id quod est conversum per virtutem nutritivam, accipitur a virtute generativa ut semen. Et huius signum ponit philosophus, quod animalia magni corporis, quae indigent multo nutrimento, sunt pauci seminis secundum quantitatem sui corporis, et paucae generationis; et similiter homines pingues sunt pauci seminis, propter eandem causam.\n\n[I q. 119 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33400)\n\nAd primum ergo dicendum quod generatio est de substantia generantis in animalibus et plantis, inquantum semen habet virtutem ex forma generantis, et inquantum est in potentia ad substantiam ipsius.\n\n[I q. 119 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33401)\n\nAd secundum dicendum quod assimilatio generantis ad genitum non fit propter materiam, sed propter formam agentis, quod generat sibi simile. Unde non oportet ad hoc quod aliquis assimiletur avo, quod materia corporalis seminis fuerit in avo; sed quod sit in semine aliqua virtus derivata ab anima avi, mediante patre.\n\n[I q. 119 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33402)\n\nEt similiter dicendum est ad tertium. Nam affinitas non attenditur secundum materiam, sed magis secundum derivationem formae.\n\n[I q. 119 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33403)\n\nAd quartum dicendum quod verbum Augustini non est sic intelligendum, quasi in Adam actu fuerit aut seminalis ratio huius hominis propinqua, aut corpulenta eius substantia, sed utrumque fuit in Adam secundum originem. Nam et materia corporalis, quae ministrata est a matre, quam vocat corpulentam substantiam, derivatur originaliter ab Adam, et similiter virtus activa existens in semine patris, quae est huius hominis propinqua ratio seminalis. Sed Christus dicitur fuisse in Adam secundum corpulentam substantiam, sed non secundum seminalem rationem. Quia materia corporis eius, quae ministrata est a matre virgine, derivata est ab Adam, sed virtus activa non est derivata ab Adam, quia corpus eius non est formatum per virtutem virilis seminis, sed operatione spiritus sancti. Talis enim partus decebat eum, qui est super omnia benedictus Deus in saecula. Amen.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0001","text":"## Prooemium","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0002","text":"[I q. 119 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33381)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0003","text":"Deinde considerandum est de propagatione hominis quantum ad corpus. Et circa hoc quaeruntur duo. Primo, utrum aliquid de alimento convertatur in veritatem humanae naturae. Secundo, utrum semen, quod est humanae generationis principium, sit de superfluo alimenti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0004","text":"## Articulus 1","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0005","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33382)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0006","text":"Ad primum sic proceditur. Videtur quod nihil de alimento transeat in veritatem humanae naturae. Dicitur enim Matth. XV, *omne quod in os intrat, in ventrem vadit, et per secessum emittitur*. Sed quod emittitur, non transit in veritatem humanae naturae. Ergo nihil de alimento in veritatem humanae naturae transit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0007","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33383)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0008","text":"Praeterea, philosophus, in I de Generat., distinguit carnem secundum speciem, et secundum materiam; et dicit quod caro secundum materiam advenit et recedit. Quod autem ex alimento generatur, advenit et recedit. Ergo id in quod alimentum convertitur, est caro secundum materiam, non autem caro secundum speciem. Sed hoc pertinet ad veritatem humanae naturae, quod pertinet ad speciem eius. Ergo alimentum non transit in veritatem humanae naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0009","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33384)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0010","text":"Praeterea, ad veritatem humanae naturae pertinere videtur humidum radicale; quod si deperdatur, restitui non potest, ut medici dicunt. Posset autem restitui, si alimentum converteretur in ipsum humidum. Ergo nutrimentum non convertitur in veritatem humanae naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0011","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33385)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0012","text":"Praeterea, si alimentum transiret in veritatem humanae naturae, quidquid in homine deperditur, restaurari posset. Sed mors hominis non accidit nisi per deperditionem alicuius. Posset igitur homo per sumptionem alimenti in perpetuum se contra mortem tueri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0013","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33386)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0014","text":"Praeterea, si alimentum in veritatem humanae naturae transiret, nihil esset in homine quod non posset recedere et reparari, quia id quod in homine generatur ex alimento, et recedere et reparari potest. Si ergo homo diu viveret, sequeretur quod nihil quod in eo fuit materialiter in principio suae generationis, finaliter remaneret in ipso. Et sic non esset idem homo numero per totam vitam suam, cum ad hoc quod aliquid sit idem numero, requiratur identitas materiae. Hoc autem est inconveniens. Non ergo alimentum transit in veritatem humanae naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0015","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33387)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0016","text":"Sed contra est quod dicit Augustinus, in libro de vera Relig., *alimenta carnis corrupta, idest amittentia formam suam, in membrorum fabricam migrant*. Sed fabrica membrorum pertinet ad veritatem humanae naturae. Ergo alimenta transeunt in veritatem humanae naturae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0017","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33388)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0018","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod, secundum philosophum, II Metaphys., *hoc modo se habet unumquodque ad veritatem, sicut se habet ad esse*. Illud ergo pertinet ad veritatem naturae alicuius, quod est de constitutione naturae ipsius. Sed natura dupliciter considerari potest, uno modo, in communi, secundum rationem speciei; alio modo, secundum quod est in hoc individuo. Ad veritatem igitur naturae alicuius in communi consideratae, pertinet forma et materia eius in communi accepta, ad veritatem autem naturae in hoc particulari consideratae, pertinet materia individualis signata, et forma per huiusmodi materiam individuata. Sicut de veritate humanae naturae in communi, est anima humana et corpus, sed de veritate humanae naturae in Petro et Martino, est haec anima et hoc corpus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0019","text":"Sunt autem quaedam, quorum formae non possunt salvari nisi in una materia signata, sicut forma solis non potest salvari nisi in materia quae actu sub ea continetur. Et secundum hunc modum, aliqui posuerunt quod forma humana non potest salvari nisi in materia quadam signata, quae scilicet a principio fuit tali forma formata in primo homine. Ita quod quidquid aliud praeter illud quod ex primo parente in posteros derivatur, additum fuerit, non pertinet ad veritatem humanae naturae, quasi non vere accipiat formam humanae naturae. Sed illa materia quae in primo homine formae humanae fuit subiecta, in seipsa multiplicatur, et hoc modo multitudo humanorum corporum a corpore primi hominis derivatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0020","text":"Et secundum hos, alimentum non convertitur in veritatem humanae naturae, sed dicunt quod alimentum accipitur ut quoddam fomentum naturae, idest ut resistat actioni caloris naturalis, ne consumat humidum radicale; sicut plumbum vel stannum adiungitur argento, ne consumatur per ignem. Sed haec positio est multipliciter irrationabilis. Primo quidem, quia eiusdem rationis est quod aliqua forma possit fieri in alia materia, et quod possit propriam materiam deserere, et ideo omnia generabilia sunt corruptibilia, et e converso. Manifestum est autem quod forma humana potest deficere ab hac materia quae ei subiicitur, alioquin corpus humanum corruptibile non esset. Unde relinquitur quod et alii materiae advenire possit, aliquo alio in veritatem humanae naturae transeunte.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0021","text":"Secundo, quia in omnibus quorum materia invenitur tota sub uno individuo, non est nisi unum individuum in una specie, sicut patet in sole et luna, et huiusmodi. Sic igitur non esset nisi unum individuum humanae speciei. Tertio, quia non est possibile quod multiplicatio materiae attendatur nisi vel secundum quantitatem tantum, sicut accidit in rarefactis, quorum materia suscipit maiores dimensiones; vel etiam secundum substantiam materiae. Sola autem eadem substantia materiae manente, non potest dici quod sit multiplicata, quia idem ad seipsum non constituit multitudinem, cum necesse sit omnem multitudinem ex aliqua divisione causari. Unde oportet quod aliqua alia substantia materiae adveniat, vel per creationem, vel per conversionem alterius in ipsam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0022","text":"Unde relinquitur quod non potest aliqua materia multiplicari, nisi vel per rarefactionem, sicut cum ex aqua fit aer; vel per conversionem alterius rei, sicut multiplicatur ignis per additionem lignorum; vel per creationem materiae. Sed manifestum est multiplicationem materiae in humanis corporibus non accidere per rarefactionem, quia sic corpora hominum perfectae aetatis essent imperfectiora quam corpora puerorum. Nec iterum per creationem novae materiae, quia secundum Gregorium, *omnia sunt simul creata secundum substantiam materiae, licet non secundum speciem formae*. Unde relinquitur quod multiplicatio corporis humani non fit nisi per hoc, quod alimentum convertitur in veritatem humani corporis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0023","text":"Quarto, quia cum homo non differat ab animalibus et plantis secundum animam vegetabilem, sequeretur quod etiam corpora animalium et plantarum non multiplicarentur per conversionem alimenti in corpus nutritum, sed per quandam multiplicationem. Quae non potest esse naturalis, cum materia secundum naturam non extendatur nisi usque ad certam quantitatem; nec iterum inveniatur aliquid naturaliter crescere, nisi per rarefactionem, vel conversionem alterius in ipsum. Et sic totum opus generativae et nutritivae, quae dicuntur vires naturales, esset miraculosum. Quod est omnino inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0024","text":"Unde alii dixerunt quod forma humana potest quidem fieri de novo in aliqua alia materia, si consideretur natura humana in communi, non autem si accipiatur prout est in hoc individuo, in quo forma humana fixa manet in quadam materia determinata, cui primo imprimitur in generatione huius individui, ita quod illam materiam nunquam deserit usque ad ultimam individui corruptionem. Et hanc materiam dicunt principaliter pertinere ad veritatem humanae naturae. Sed quia huiusmodi materia non sufficit ad quantitatem debitam, requiritur ut adveniat alia materia per conversionem alimenti in substantiam nutriti, quantum sufficiat ad debitum augmentum. Et hanc materiam dicunt secundario pertinere ad veritatem humanae naturae, quia non requiritur ad primum esse individui, sed ad quantitatem eius. Iam vero si quid aliud advenit ex alimento, non pertinet ad veritatem humanae naturae, proprie loquendo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0025","text":"Sed hoc etiam est inconveniens. Primo quidem, quia haec opinio iudicat de materia corporum viventium, ad modum corporum inanimatorum; in quibus etsi sit virtus ad generandum simile in specie, non tamen est virtus in eis ad generandum aliquid sibi simile secundum individuum; quae quidem virtus in corporibus viventibus est virtus nutritiva. Nihil ergo per virtutem nutritivam adderetur corporibus viventibus, si alimentum in veritatem naturae ipsorum non converteretur. Secundo quia virtus activa quae est in semine, est quaedam impressio derivata ab anima generantis, sicut supra dictum est. Unde non potest esse maioris virtutis in agendo, quam ipsa anima a qua derivatur. Si ergo ex virtute seminis vere assumit aliqua materia formam naturae humanae, multo magis anima in nutrimentum coniunctum poterit veram formam naturae humanae imprimere per potentiam nutritivam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0026","text":"Tertio, quia nutrimento indigetur non solum ad augmentum, alioquin terminato augmento necessarium non esset, sed etiam ad restaurandum illud quod deperditur per actionem caloris naturalis. Non autem esset restauratio, nisi id quod ex alimento generatur, succederet in locum deperditi. Unde sicut id quod primo inerat, est de veritate humanae naturae, ita et id quod ex alimento generatur. Et ideo, secundum alios, dicendum est quod alimentum vere convertitur in veritatem humanae naturae, inquantum vere accipit speciem carnis et ossis et huiusmodi partium. Et hoc est quod dicit philosophus in II de anima, quod *alimentum nutrit inquantum est potentia caro*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0027","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33389)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0028","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod dominus non dicit quod totum quod in os intrat, per secessum emittatur, sed omne, quia de quolibet cibo aliquid impurum per secessum emittitur. Vel potest dici quod quidquid ex alimento generatur, potest etiam per calorem naturalem resolvi, et per poros quosdam occultos emitti, ut Hieronymus exponit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0029","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33390)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0030","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod aliqui per carnem secundum speciem intellexerunt id quod primo accipit speciem humanam, quod sumitur a generante, et hoc dicunt semper manere, quousque individuum durat. Carnem vero secundum materiam dicunt esse quae generatur ex alimento, et hanc dicunt non semper permanere, sed quod sicut advenit, ita abscedit. Sed hoc est contra intentionem Aristotelis. Dicit enim ibi quod, sicut in unoquoque habentium speciem in materia, puta in ligno et lapide, *ita et in carne hoc est secundum speciem, et illud secundum materiam*. Manifestum est autem quod praedicta distinctio locum non habet in rebus inanimatis, quae non generantur ex semine, nec nutriuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0031","text":"Et iterum, cum id quod ex alimento generatur, adiungatur corpori nutrito per modum mixtionis, sicut aqua miscetur vino, ut ponit exemplum ibidem philosophus; non potest alia esse natura eius quod advenit, et eius cui advenit, cum iam sit factum unum per veram mixtionem. Unde nulla ratio est quod unum consumatur per calorem naturalem, et alterum maneat. Et ideo aliter dicendum est, quod haec distinctio philosophi non est secundum diversas carnes, sed est eiusdem carnis secundum diversam considerationem. Si enim consideretur caro secundum speciem, idest secundum id quod est formale in ipsa, sic semper manet, quia semper manet natura carnis, et dispositio naturalis ipsius. Sed si consideretur caro secundum materiam, sic non manet, sed paulatim consumitur et restauratur, sicut patet in igne fornacis, cuius forma manet, sed materia paulatim consumitur, et alia in locum eius substituitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0032","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33391)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0033","text":"Ad tertium dicendum quod ad humidum radicale intelligitur pertinere totum id in quo fundatur virtus speciei. Quod si subtrahatur, restitui non potest, sicut si amputetur manus aut pes, vel aliquid huiusmodi. Sed humidum nutrimentale est quod nondum pervenit ad suscipiendum perfecte naturam speciei, sed est in via ad hoc; sicut est sanguis, et alia huiusmodi. Unde si talia subtrahantur, remanet adhuc virtus speciei in radice, quae non tollitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0034","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33392)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0035","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod omnis virtus in corpore passibili per continuam actionem debilitatur, quia huiusmodi agentia etiam patiuntur. Et ideo virtus conversiva in principio quidem tam fortis est, ut possit convertere non solum quod sufficit ad restaurationem deperditi, sed etiam ad augmentum. Postea vero non potest convertere nisi quantum sufficit ad restaurationem deperditi, et tunc cessat augmentum. Demum nec hoc potest, et tunc fit diminutio. Deinde, deficiente huiusmodi virtute totaliter, animal moritur. Sicut virtus vini convertentis aquam admixtam, paulatim per admixtionem aquae debilitatur, ut tandem totum fiat aquosum, ut philosophus exemplificat in I de Generat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0036","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33393)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0037","text":"Ad quintum dicendum quod, sicut philosophus dicit in I de Generat. quando aliqua materia per se convertitur in ignem, tunc dicitur ignis de novo generari, quando vero aliqua materia convertitur in ignem praeexistentem, dicitur ignis nutriri. Unde si tota materia simul amittat speciem ignis, et alia materia convertatur in ignem, erit alius ignis numero. Si vero, paulatim combusto uno ligno, aliud substituatur, et sic deinceps quousque omnia prima consumantur, semper remanet idem ignis numero, quia semper quod additur, transit in praeexistens. Et similiter est intelligendum in corporibus viventibus, in quibus ex nutrimento restauratur id quod per calorem naturalem consumitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0038","text":"## Articulus 2","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0039","text":"[I q. 119 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33394)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0040","text":"Ad secundum sic proceditur. Videtur quod semen non sit de superfluo alimenti, sed de substantia generantis. Dicit enim Damascenus quod *generatio est opus naturae ex substantia generantis producens quod generatur*. Sed id quod generatur, generatur ex semine. Ergo semen est de substantia generantis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0041","text":"[I q. 119 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33395)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0042","text":"Praeterea, secundum hoc filius assimilatur patri, quod ab eo aliquid accipit. Sed si semen ex quo aliquid generatur, sit de superfluo alimenti; nihil acciperet aliquis ab avo et praecedentibus, in quibus hoc alimentum nullo modo fuit. Ergo non assimilaretur aliquis avo et praecedentibus, magis quam aliis hominibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0043","text":"[I q. 119 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33396)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0044","text":"Praeterea, alimentum hominis generantis quandoque est ex carnibus bovis, vel porci, et aliorum huiusmodi. Si igitur semen esset de superfluo alimenti, homo generatus ex semine maiorem affinitatem haberet cum bove et porco, quam cum patre et aliis consanguineis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0045","text":"[I q. 119 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33397)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0046","text":"Praeterea, Augustinus dicit, X super Gen. ad Litt., quod nos fuimus in Adam non solum secundum *seminalem rationem, sed etiam secundum corpulentam substantiam*. Hoc autem non esset, si semen esset ex superfluo alimenti. Ergo semen non est superfluo alimenti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0047","text":"[I q. 119 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33398)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0048","text":"Sed contra est quod philosophus probat multipliciter, in libro de Generat. Animal., *quod semen est superfluum alimenti*.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0049","text":"[I q. 119 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33399)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0050","text":"Respondeo dicendum quod ista quaestio aliqualiter dependet ex praemissis. Si enim in natura humana est virtus ad communicandum suam formam materiae alienae non solum in alio, sed etiam in ipso; manifestum est quod alimentum, quod est in principio dissimile, in fine fit simile per formam communicatam. Est autem naturalis ordo ut aliquid gradatim de potentia reducatur in actum, et ideo in his quae generantur, invenimus quod primo unumquodque est imperfectum, et postea perficitur. Manifestum est autem quod commune se habet ad proprium et determinatum, ut imperfectum ad perfectum, et ideo videmus quod in generatione animalis prius generatur animal, quam homo vel equus. Sic igitur et ipsum alimentum primo quidem accipit quandam virtutem communem respectu omnium partium corporis, et in fine determinatur ad hanc partem vel ad illam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0051","text":"Non autem est possibile quod accipiatur pro semine id quod iam conversum est in substantiam membrorum, per quandam resolutionem. Quia illud resolutum, si non retineret naturam eius a quo resolvitur, tunc iam esset recedens a natura generantis, quasi in via corruptionis existens; et sic non haberet virtutem convertendi aliud in similem naturam. Si vero retineret naturam eius a quo resolvitur, tunc, cum esset contractum ad determinatam partem, non haberet virtutem movendi ad naturam totius, sed solum ad naturam partis. Nisi forte quis dicat quod esset resolutum ab omnibus partibus corporis, et quod retineat naturam omnium partium. Et sic semen esset quasi quoddam parvum animal in actu; et generatio animalis ex animali non esset nisi per divisionem, sicut lutum generatur ex luto, et sicut accidit in animalibus quae decisa vivunt. Hoc autem est inconveniens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0052","text":"Relinquitur ergo quod semen non sit decisum ab eo quod erat actu totum; sed magis sit in potentia totum, habens virtutem ad productionem totius corporis, derivatam an anima generantis, ut supra dictum est. Hoc autem quod est in potentia ad totum, est illud quod generatur ex alimento, antequam convertatur in substantiam membrorum. Et ideo ex hoc semen accipitur. Et secundum hoc, virtus nutritiva dicitur deservire generativae, quia id quod est conversum per virtutem nutritivam, accipitur a virtute generativa ut semen. Et huius signum ponit philosophus, quod animalia magni corporis, quae indigent multo nutrimento, sunt pauci seminis secundum quantitatem sui corporis, et paucae generationis; et similiter homines pingues sunt pauci seminis, propter eandem causam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0053","text":"[I q. 119 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33400)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0054","text":"Ad primum ergo dicendum quod generatio est de substantia generantis in animalibus et plantis, inquantum semen habet virtutem ex forma generantis, et inquantum est in potentia ad substantiam ipsius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0055","text":"[I q. 119 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33401)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0056","text":"Ad secundum dicendum quod assimilatio generantis ad genitum non fit propter materiam, sed propter formam agentis, quod generat sibi simile. Unde non oportet ad hoc quod aliquis assimiletur avo, quod materia corporalis seminis fuerit in avo; sed quod sit in semine aliqua virtus derivata ab anima avi, mediante patre.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0057","text":"[I q. 119 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33402)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0058","text":"Et similiter dicendum est ad tertium. Nam affinitas non attenditur secundum materiam, sed magis secundum derivationem formae.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0059","text":"[I q. 119 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33403)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0060","text":"Ad quartum dicendum quod verbum Augustini non est sic intelligendum, quasi in Adam actu fuerit aut seminalis ratio huius hominis propinqua, aut corpulenta eius substantia, sed utrumque fuit in Adam secundum originem. Nam et materia corporalis, quae ministrata est a matre, quam vocat corpulentam substantiam, derivatur originaliter ab Adam, et similiter virtus activa existens in semine patris, quae est huius hominis propinqua ratio seminalis. Sed Christus dicitur fuisse in Adam secundum corpulentam substantiam, sed non secundum seminalem rationem. Quia materia corporis eius, quae ministrata est a matre virgine, derivata est ab Adam, sed virtus activa non est derivata ab Adam, quia corpus eius non est formatum per virtutem virilis seminis, sed operatione spiritus sancti. Talis enim partus decebat eum, qui est super omnia benedictus Deus in saecula. Amen.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"2d85a94627241fc621a8b648545dec277db0bee7c9cc73cce8a4c96c73294fc6","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261019T155256039596Z_9a0610ea","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 119","source_sha256":"2d85a94627241fc621a8b648545dec277db0bee7c9cc73cce8a4c96c73294fc6","sha256":"9dbb646b4ef58ee0fd3368e7f89a251083f137a8ff295fdb59b8c08e20ce509b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:52:56.039653+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0001","text":"## Prologue","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0002","text":"[I q. 119 pr.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33381)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0003","text":"Next, we must consider the propagation of man with respect to the body. And concerning this, two questions are asked. First, whether anything from nourishment is converted into the truth of human nature. Second, whether semen, which is the principle of human generation, is from the superfluity of nourishment.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0004","text":"## Article 1","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0005","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33382)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0006","text":"We proceed thus to the first. It seems that nothing from nourishment passes into the truth of human nature. For it is said in Matt. XV, *everything that enters the mouth goes into the belly and is cast out through the privy*. But that which is cast out does not pass into the truth of human nature. Therefore, nothing from nourishment passes into the truth of human nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0007","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33383)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0008","text":"Further, the Philosopher, in I *On Generation*, distinguishes flesh according to species and according to matter; and says that flesh according to matter comes and goes. But what is generated from nourishment comes and goes. Therefore, that into which nourishment is converted is flesh according to matter, but not flesh according to species. But what pertains to the truth of human nature is what pertains to its species. Therefore, nourishment does not pass into the truth of human nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0009","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33384)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0010","text":"Further, radical moisture seems to pertain to the truth of human nature; and if it is lost, it cannot be restored, as physicians say. But it could be restored if nourishment were converted into that moisture itself. Therefore, nourishment is not converted into the truth of human nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0011","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33385)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0012","text":"Further, if nourishment passed into the truth of human nature, whatever is lost in man could be restored. But the death of man occurs only through the loss of something. Therefore, by consuming nourishment, man could preserve himself against death forever.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0013","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 arg. 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33386)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0014","text":"Further, if nourishment passed into the truth of human nature, there would be nothing in man that could not depart and be repaired, because what is generated in man from nourishment can both depart and be repaired. Therefore, if a man lived for a long time, it would follow that nothing which was materially in him at the beginning of his generation would remain in him at the end. And thus he would not be numerically the same man throughout his whole life, since, for something to be numerically the same, identity of matter is required. But this is unfitting. Therefore, nourishment does not pass into the truth of human nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0015","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33387)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0016","text":"On the contrary, Augustine says in the book *On True Religion*: *the foods of the flesh, when corrupted, that is, losing their own form, pass over into the structure of the members*. But the structure of the members pertains to the truth of human nature. Therefore, foods pass into the truth of human nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0017","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33388)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0018","text":"I answer that, according to the Philosopher, in II *Metaphysics*, *each thing is related to truth in the same way as it is related to being*. Therefore, that belongs to the truth of a thing's nature which is of the constitution of that nature itself. But nature can be considered in two ways: in one way, in common, according to the account of the species; in another way, insofar as it is in this individual. Therefore, to the truth of a nature considered in common belong its form and matter taken in common; but to the truth of a nature considered in this particular belong designated individual matter and the form individuated through such matter. Thus, of the truth of human nature in common are the human soul and body; but of the truth of human nature in Peter and Martin are this soul and this body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0019","text":"Now there are certain things whose forms cannot be preserved except in one designated matter, just as the form of the sun cannot be preserved except in the matter actually contained under it. And in this manner, some have held that the human form cannot be preserved except in a certain designated matter, namely, that which was from the beginning formed by such a form in the first man. Thus, whatever else besides that which is derived from the first parent into descendants may be added does not pertain to the truth of human nature, as though it did not truly receive the form of human nature. But that matter which in the first man was subject to the human form is multiplied in itself, and in this way the multitude of human bodies is derived from the body of the first man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0020","text":"And according to these thinkers, nourishment is not converted into the truth of human nature; rather, they say that nourishment is taken as a certain support of nature, that is, so that it may resist the action of natural heat, lest it consume the radical moisture; just as lead or tin is added to silver, lest it be consumed by fire. But this position is irrational in many ways. First, because the possibility that some form may come to be in another matter and the possibility that it may abandon its proper matter are of the same account; and therefore all generable things are corruptible, and conversely. But it is evident that the human form can fail from this matter which is subject to it; otherwise the human body would not be corruptible. Hence it follows that it can also come to another matter, with something else passing into the truth of human nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0021","text":"Second, because in all things whose whole matter is found under one individual, there is only one individual in one species, as is clear in the sun and moon and the like. Thus, there would be only one individual of the human species. Third, because multiplication of matter cannot be understood except either according to quantity alone, as occurs in things that are rarefied, whose matter receives greater dimensions; or according to the substance of matter as well. But while the same substance of matter alone remains, it cannot be said to have been multiplied, because the same thing does not constitute a multitude in relation to itself, since every multitude must be caused by some division. Hence some other substance of matter must come to it, either through creation or through the conversion of another thing into it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0022","text":"Hence it follows that matter cannot be multiplied except either through rarefaction, as when water becomes air; or through the conversion of another thing, as fire is multiplied by the addition of wood; or through the creation of matter. But it is evident that the multiplication of matter in human bodies does not occur through rarefaction, because in that case the bodies of men of perfect age would be more imperfect than the bodies of children. Nor again does it occur through the creation of new matter, because according to Gregory, *all things were created together according to the substance of matter, although not according to the species of form*. Hence it follows that multiplication of the human body occurs only through nourishment being converted into the truth of the human body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0023","text":"Fourth, because, since man does not differ from animals and plants in respect of the vegetative soul, it would follow that the bodies of animals and plants also would not be multiplied through the conversion of nourishment into the nourished body, but through some sort of multiplication. This cannot be natural, since matter does not by nature extend beyond a determinate quantity; nor again is anything found naturally to grow except through rarefaction or the conversion of another thing into itself. And thus the whole work of the generative and nutritive powers, which are called natural powers, would be miraculous. This is altogether unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0024","text":"Hence others said that the human form can indeed come to be anew in some other matter, if human nature is considered in common, but not if it is taken as it exists in this individual, in whom the human form remains fixed in a certain determinate matter, upon which it is first impressed in the generation of this individual, such that it never abandons that matter until the final corruption of the individual. And they say that this matter principally pertains to the truth of human nature. But because such matter is not sufficient for the due quantity, it is required that other matter come to it through the conversion of nourishment into the substance of the nourished thing, in an amount sufficient for due growth. And they say that this matter secondarily pertains to the truth of human nature, because it is required not for the first being of the individual, but for its quantity. But if anything else comes from nourishment, it does not, properly speaking, pertain to the truth of human nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0025","text":"But this too is unfitting. First indeed, because this opinion judges concerning the matter of living bodies after the manner of inanimate bodies; in which, although there is a power for generating what is specifically similar, there is nevertheless not in them a power for generating something similar to themselves according to the individual; and this power in living bodies is the nutritive power. Therefore nothing would be added to living bodies through the nutritive power if food were not converted into the truth of their nature. Second, because the active power which is in the seed is a certain impression derived from the soul of the generator, as was said above. Hence it cannot be of greater power in acting than the soul itself from which it is derived. Therefore, if through the power of the seed some matter truly assumes the form of human nature, much more will the soul be able, by the nutritive power, to impress the true form of human nature upon nourishment conjoined to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0026","text":"Third, because nourishment is needed not only for growth—for otherwise, once growth had ended, it would not be necessary—but also for restoring that which is lost through the action of natural heat. But there would be no restoration unless that which is generated from food succeeded into the place of what has been lost. Hence, just as that which was present at first belongs to the truth of human nature, so also does that which is generated from food. And therefore, according to others, it must be said that food is truly converted into the truth of human nature, insofar as it truly receives the species of flesh and bone and parts of this kind. And this is what the Philosopher says in *On the Soul* II, that *food nourishes insofar as it is potentially flesh*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0027","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33389)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0028","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that the Lord does not say that everything which enters the mouth is emitted through the evacuation, but all [is emitted], because from every food something impure is emitted through the evacuation. Or it may be said that whatever is generated from food can also be dissolved by natural heat and emitted through certain hidden pores, as Jerome explains.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0029","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33390)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0030","text":"To the second it must be said that some have understood by flesh according to species that which first receives the human species, which is received from the generator, and they say that this always remains as long as the individual endures. But by flesh according to matter they mean that which is generated from food, and they say that this does not always remain, but that, as it comes, so it departs. But this is contrary to Aristotle's intention. For he says there that, just as in each of those things having species in matter, for example in wood and stone, *so too in flesh, this is according to species and that according to matter*. But it is manifest that the aforesaid distinction has no place in inanimate things, which are not generated from seed, nor are nourished.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0031","text":"And again, since that which is generated from food is joined to the nourished body by way of mixture, just as water is mixed with wine, as the Philosopher gives the example in the same place; the nature of that which comes to it and of that to which it comes cannot be different, since one thing has already been made by a true mixture. Hence there is no reason why the one should be consumed by natural heat and the other remain. And therefore it must be said otherwise: that this distinction of the Philosopher is not according to different fleshes, but is of the same flesh according to a different consideration. For if flesh is considered according to species, that is, according to that which is formal in it, then it always remains, because the nature of flesh and its natural disposition always remain. But if flesh is considered according to matter, then it does not remain, but is gradually consumed and restored, as is evident in the fire of a furnace, whose form remains, but whose matter is gradually consumed and another is substituted in its place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0032","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33391)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0033","text":"To the third it must be said that radical moisture is understood to include all that in which the power of the species is founded. If this is taken away, it cannot be restored, as when a hand or foot, or something of this kind, is amputated. But nutritive moisture is that which has not yet arrived at perfectly receiving the nature of the species, but is on the way to this; such as blood and other things of this kind. Hence, if such things are taken away, the power of the species still remains in the root, which is not removed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0034","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33392)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0035","text":"To the fourth it must be said that every power in a passible body is weakened by continuous action, because agents of this kind also undergo passion. And therefore the converting power is at first so strong that it can convert not only what suffices for the restoration of what has been lost, but also for growth. But afterward it cannot convert except as much as suffices for the restoration of what has been lost, and then growth ceases. Finally, it cannot even do this, and then diminution occurs. Thereafter, when such power wholly fails, the animal dies. Just as the power of wine converting the water mixed with it is gradually weakened through the admixture of water, so that at last the whole becomes watery, as the Philosopher exemplifies in *On Generation* I.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0036","text":"[I q. 119 a. 1 ad 5](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33393)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0037","text":"To the fifth it must be said that, as the Philosopher says in *On Generation* I, when some matter is converted into fire through itself, then fire is said to be newly generated; but when some matter is converted into pre-existing fire, fire is said to be nourished. Hence, if the whole matter should at once lose the species of fire, and other matter be converted into fire, it will be another fire numerically. But if, as one piece of wood is gradually burned, another is substituted, and so on until all the first things are consumed, the same fire always remains numerically, because that which is added always passes into what pre-exists. And the same is to be understood in living bodies, in which that which is consumed by natural heat is restored from nourishment.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0038","text":"## Article 2","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0039","text":"[I q. 119 a. 2 arg. 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33394)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0040","text":"To the second [question] one proceeds thus. It seems that seed is not from the superfluity of food, but from the substance of the generator. For Damascene says that *generation is a work of nature producing that which is generated from the substance of the generator*. But that which is generated is generated from seed. Therefore seed is from the substance of the generator.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0041","text":"[I q. 119 a. 2 arg. 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33395)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0042","text":"Moreover, a son is made like his father insofar as he receives something from him. But if the seed from which something is generated is from the superfluity of food, no one would receive anything from his grandfather and predecessors, in whom this food was in no way present. Therefore one would not be made like one's grandfather and predecessors more than other human beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0043","text":"[I q. 119 a. 2 arg. 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33396)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0044","text":"Moreover, the food of a man who generates is sometimes from the flesh of an ox or a pig, and other such things. Therefore, if seed were from the superfluity of food, a man generated from seed would have greater affinity with an ox and a pig than with his father and other blood-relatives.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0045","text":"[I q. 119 a. 2 arg. 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33397)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0046","text":"Moreover, Augustine says, in *On Genesis according to the Letter* X, that we were in Adam not only according to the *seminal principle, but also according to bodily substance*. But this would not be so if seed were from the superfluity of food. Therefore seed is not from the superfluity of food.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0047","text":"[I q. 119 a. 2 s. c.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33398)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0048","text":"But on the contrary, the Philosopher proves in many ways, in the book *On the Generation of Animals*, *that seed is the superfluity of food*.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0049","text":"[I q. 119 a. 2 co.](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33399)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0050","text":"I answer that this question depends in some way on what has been set forth previously. For if there is in human nature a power to communicate its form to foreign matter, not only in another, but also in itself, it is manifest that nourishment, which is at first dissimilar, becomes similar in the end through the communicated form. Now there is a natural order whereby something is gradually reduced from potency to act; and therefore in things that are generated, we find that each is first imperfect, and afterwards is perfected. But it is manifest that the common stands to the proper and determinate as the imperfect to the perfect; and therefore we see that in the generation of an animal, an animal is generated before a man or a horse. Thus, therefore, nourishment itself first receives a certain common power with respect to all the parts of the body, and in the end is determined to this part or that.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0051","text":"But it is not possible that that which has already been converted into the substance of the members should be taken as seed by some resolution. For that which is resolved, if it did not retain the nature of that from which it is resolved, would then already be departing from the nature of the generator, as though existing on the way to corruption; and thus it would not have the power to convert another into a similar nature. But if it retained the nature of that from which it is resolved, then, since it would be contracted to a determinate part, it would not have the power to move toward the nature of the whole, but only toward the nature of the part. Unless perhaps someone should say that it is resolved from all the parts of the body, and that it retains the nature of all the parts. And thus seed would be, as it were, a certain small animal in act; and the generation of animal from animal would be only by division, just as mud is generated from mud, and as happens in animals which live when cut apart. But this is unfitting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0052","text":"It remains, therefore, that seed is not cut off from that which was actually a whole; but rather that it is potentially a whole, having a power for the production of the whole body, derived from the soul of the generator, as was said above. But that which is in potency to the whole is that which is generated from nourishment before it is converted into the substance of the members. And therefore seed is taken from this. And according to this, the nutritive power is said to serve the generative power, because that which is converted by the nutritive power is taken by the generative power as seed. And the Philosopher gives as a sign of this that animals of large body, which need much nourishment, have little seed in proportion to the quantity of their body, and generate little; and likewise fat men have little seed, for the same reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0053","text":"[I q. 119 a. 2 ad 1](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33400)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0054","text":"To the first, therefore, it must be said that generation is from the substance of the generator in animals and plants, insofar as the seed has power from the form of the generator, and insofar as it is in potency to its substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0055","text":"[I q. 119 a. 2 ad 2](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33401)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0056","text":"To the second it must be said that the assimilation of the generator to the generated does not occur because of matter, but because of the form of the agent, which generates something like itself. Hence, for someone to be like his grandfather, it is not necessary that the bodily matter of the seed was in the grandfather; rather, it is necessary that there be in the seed some power derived from the soul of the grandfather, through the father.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0057","text":"[I q. 119 a. 2 ad 3](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33402)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0058","text":"And the same must be said in reply to the third. For kinship is considered not according to matter, but rather according to the derivation of form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0059","text":"[I q. 119 a. 2 ad 4](https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/sth1103.html#33403)","kind":"passage","beautiful_source_detail":"source-locator","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_GGVC__111:P0060","text":"To the fourth it must be said that Augustine's statement is not to be understood as though in Adam there were actually either the proximate seminal principle of this man or his corporeal substance, but both were in Adam according to origin. For both the bodily matter supplied by the mother, which he calls corporeal substance, is derived originally from Adam, and likewise the active power existing in the seed of the father, which is this man's proximate seminal principle. But Christ is said to have been in Adam according to corporeal substance, but not according to seminal principle. For the matter of his body, which was supplied by the Virgin Mother, was derived from Adam; but the active power was not derived from Adam, because his body was not formed through the power of male seed, but by the operation of the Holy Spirit. For such a birth befitted him who is God, blessed above all things for ever. Amen.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_GGVC__111:P0060"]}],"source_digest":"a85d925669f3e951fc746c3f8d02b2eccf40149282a3efb322f1d988768382f3","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 119"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 119","title_language_code":"en"}],"units":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Prologue","order":1,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_PPP0"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PPP0@R001","sha256":"c0e279c7a3a61b3e9f4a5d29bd5ac4a7221eebb73ad04329d492ccbba20e68d9","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AS1","text":"Quaestio centralis est quomodo doctrina ad religionem Christianam pertinens incipientibus erudiendis apte tradatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AS2","text":"Nova quaestionis constitutio ponit impedimenta eruditionis non tantum in difficultate materiae, sed etiam in vitiosa ordinatione et dispositione doctrinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AS3","text":"Fundamentum operis est accommodatio doctrinae ad statum incipientium, salva eius relatione ad veritatem catholicam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AS4","text":"Probatio procedit per diagnosin impedimentorum: quaestiones inutiles, inordinata traditio et repetitio doctrinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AS5","text":"Motus argumentationis transit ab impedimentorum detectione ad propositum doctrinae breviter, dilucide et secundum ordinem tradendae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AS6","text":"Genus argumentationis est diagnosticum et reconstructivum, cum intentione disciplinam doctrinae ad usum incipientium redigendi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:A1","text":"Doctor veritatis catholicae debet non solum provectos instruere, sed etiam incipientes erudire.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:A2","text":"Doctrina Christianam religionem pertinens ad eruditionem incipientium accommodari potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:A3","text":"Novitii doctrinae impediuntur cum quaestiones, articuli et argumenta inutiliter multiplicantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:A4","text":"Novitii doctrinae impediuntur cum necessaria non secundum ordinem disciplinae traduntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:A5","text":"Expositio librorum et occasio disputandi possunt ordinem doctrinae determinare contra necessitatem disciplinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:A6","text":"Frequens repetitio eorundem doctrinalium fastidium et confusionem in auditorum animis generat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:A7","text":"Doctrina ad incipientes directa breviter ac dilucide prosequi debet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:A8","text":"Exsecutio huius propositi auxilio divino nititur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:A9","text":"Ordo doctrinae a materia ipsa determinatur quatenus materiae proprietas id patitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:NX1","text":"Eruditio incipientium non debet restringi ad instructionem provectorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:NX2","text":"Doctrina ad incipientes directa non debet a multiplicatione inutilium quaestionum, articulorum et argumentorum constitui.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:NX3","text":"Necessaria doctrinae non debent casu occasioneque disputationis ordinari.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:NX4","text":"Traditio doctrinae non debet primario formam expositionis librorum sequi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:NX5","text":"Repetitio doctrinalis non debet fastidium et confusionem generare.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:NX6","text":"Propositum operis non est plenitudo indiscriminata, sed brevitas et claritas secundum materiam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:D1","text":"Si quaestiones, articuli et argumenta inutiliter multiplicantur, capacitas incipientium ad doctrinam recipiendam impeditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:D2","text":"Si necessaria non secundum ordinem disciplinae traduntur, scientia doctrinae fit dependens ab ordine externo expositionis vel disputationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A4","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:D3","text":"Si eadem frequenter repetuntur, traditio doctrinae in experientiam fastidii et confusionis convertitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:D4","text":"Remotio multiplicationis inutilis, inordinationis et repetitionis constituit condicionem preliminarem eruditionis incipientium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A3","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A4","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:D5","text":"Ordo disciplinae efficacior est quam ordo occasionis ad ea quae incipientibus necessaria sunt tradenda.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A4","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:D6","text":"Brevitas et claritas non sunt ornamenta expositionis, sed instrumenta accommodationis doctrinae ad novitios.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A1","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A2","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:SD1","text":"Doctrina ad incipientes erudiendos requirit reordinationem totius materiae, non simplicem diminutionem difficultatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A2","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A4","CH_GGVC_PPP0:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:SD2","text":"Structura doctrinae fit pars ipsius actus pedagogici, quia ordo tradendi determinat possibilitatem cognitionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A4","CH_GGVC_PPP0:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:SD3","text":"Eliminatio inutilis, inordinati et repetitivi constituit novam oeconomiam doctrinae, in qua necessarium a superfluo separatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A3","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A4","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:SD4","text":"Auctoritas traditionis subicitur principio disciplinae, cum expositio librorum et occasio disputandi non iam sufficiant ad totum ordinem determinandum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A4","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:SD5","text":"Praescriptum breviter ac dilucide prosequendi coniungit finem epistemicum, disciplinarem et pastoralem in una forma expositionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A1","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A2","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:DNX1","text":"Eruditio non identificatur cum accumulatione quaestionum, articulorum et argumentorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:DNX2","text":"Scientia non supponitur ex sola comprehensione textuum antecedentium vel controversiarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:DNX3","text":"Ordo doctrinae non habetur tamquam effectus neuter ex occasione disputandi productus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:DNX4","text":"Difficultas doctrinae non tractatur tamquam proprietas immutabilis quae accommodationem incipientium excludat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:DNX5","text":"Claritas non reducitur ad simplicitatem informem, sed dependet ab ordine disciplinari.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:DNX6","text":"Novitius non tractatur tamquam auditor cui eadem forma tradendi ac provecto sufficiat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:SNX1","text":"Impeditur possibilitas doctrinae quae sine distinctione inter necessarium et inutile omnia tradit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:SNX2","text":"Impeditur possibilitas disciplinae quae ordinem suum ab accidentalitate disputationis accipit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:SNX3","text":"Impeditur possibilitas cognitionis quae repetitione et accumulatione confundit potius quam instituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:SNX4","text":"Impeditur reductio doctrinae ad provectos, quia initium doctrinale proprium obiectum operis constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:SNX5","text":"Impeditur separatio inter veritatem catholicam et modum eius tradendi, quia forma transmissionis ad finem doctrinae pertinet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:FGS1","text":"Necessitas erudiendi incipientes → recognitio impedimentorum doctrinae → distinctio inutilis, inordinati et repetitivi a necessario.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:FGS2","text":"Multiplicatio inutilis + ordo occasionis + repetitio frequens → impedimentum cognitionis sub formis impeditionis, fastidii et confusionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:FGS3","text":"Remotio impedimentorum → instauratio ordinis disciplinae → accommodatio doctrinae ad incipientium statum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:FGS4","text":"Ordo disciplinae + brevitas + claritas → traditio sacrae doctrinae apta ad eruditionem incipientium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:FGS5","text":"Propositum pedagogicum → exsecutio sub confidentia divini auxilii → continuatio expositionis secundum tolerantiam materiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AF1","text":"Officium doctoris → necessitas incipientes erudiendi → defectus formarum traditarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AF2","text":"Defectus formarum traditarum → diagnosis multiplicationis, inordinationis et repetitionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AF3","text":"Diagnosis impedimentorum → nova constitutio ordinis doctrinae secundum disciplinam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AF4","text":"Ordo disciplinae → brevitas et claritas → aptatio ad novitios.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AF5","text":"Aptatio doctrinae → propositum totius operis sub fiducia divini auxilii.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:ME1","text":"Incipiens fit subiectum structuraliter distinctum doctrinae, non simplex gradus minor provecti.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:ME2","text":"Ordo disciplinae fit criterium principale quo necessaria doctrinae a dispositionibus accidentalibus separantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:ME3","text":"Quaestiones, articuli et argumenta denuo classificari possunt secundum utilitatem eruditionis, non secundum solam existentiam in traditione.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:ME4","text":"Repetitio, inordinatio et multiplicatio mutantur ex proprietatibus materialibus in impedimenta formalia transmissionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:ME5","text":"Brevitas et claritas constituuntur ut operationes ordinantes, non ut simplices qualitates styli.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:ALT1","text":"Surface — Traditio doctrinae Christianae ad incipientes breviter ac dilucide dirigitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:ALT2","text":"Structural — Efficacia traditionis pendet ab ordine disciplinae et a remotione multiplicationis, inordinationis atque repetitionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Textus disciplinam doctrinae separat ab ordine expositionis librorum et occasione disputandi, ut modum cognitionis denuo constituat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Doctrina distinguit statum incipientium et provectorum tamquam diversos modos receptivitatis, dum progressio scientiae per ordinem disciplinarem disponitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad restituendam necessitatem operis, diagnosin impedimentorum et praescriptionem ordinis novi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:MRT2","text":"Structura interne cohaeret, quia propositum pedagogicum directe ad vitia formarum traditarum refertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:MRT3","text":"Firmissima est connexio inter statum incipientium, ordinem disciplinae et necessitatem brevitatis ac claritatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:MRT4","text":"Nondum plene determinatur quid ipse ordo disciplinae materialiter contineat; prooemium eius principium tantum statuit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:MRT5","text":"Tensio manet inter brevitatem et amplitudinem doctrinae, necnon inter claritatem incipientium et tolerantiam quam materia requirit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:MRT6","text":"Exsecutio nititur praesuppositione theologica divini auxilii, quae rationem operis sustinet sed methodo disciplinari non reducitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:MRT7","text":"Restitutio servat motum verum textus: a munere doctoris ad diagnosin impedimentorum, inde ad reordinationem et propositum operis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T155318063898Z_c102614d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Prologue","source_sha256":"c0e279c7a3a61b3e9f4a5d29bd5ac4a7221eebb73ad04329d492ccbba20e68d9","sha256":"3d2d020c3b200ccdc45c30ed7d419a0568cbdf7948df9ea29d9162a55130d1d7","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:53:18.065362+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AS1","text":"The central question is how doctrine pertaining to the Christian religion may be suitably imparted for the instruction of beginners.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AS2","text":"The new formulation of the question locates the obstacles to instruction not only in the difficulty of the subject matter, but also in the defective ordering and arrangement of doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AS3","text":"The foundation of the work is the adaptation of doctrine to the condition of beginners, while preserving its relation to Catholic truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AS4","text":"The proof proceeds through a diagnosis of obstacles: useless questions, disordered transmission, and repetition of doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AS5","text":"The movement of the argument passes from the detection of obstacles to the aim of transmitting doctrine briefly, clearly, and according to order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AS6","text":"The mode of argumentation is diagnostic and reconstructive, with the intention of reducing the discipline of doctrine to the use of beginners.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:A1","text":"The teacher of Catholic truth ought not only to instruct the advanced, but also to educate beginners.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:A2","text":"Doctrine pertaining to the Christian religion can be adapted to the instruction of beginners.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:A3","text":"Novices in doctrine are impeded when questions, articles, and arguments are multiplied uselessly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:A4","text":"Novices in doctrine are impeded when necessary matters are not transmitted according to the order of the discipline.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:A5","text":"The exposition of books and the occasion for disputation can determine the order of doctrine contrary to the necessity of the discipline.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:A6","text":"Frequent repetition of the same doctrinal matters generates weariness and confusion in the minds of hearers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:A7","text":"Doctrine directed to beginners ought to proceed briefly and clearly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:A8","text":"The execution of this aim rests upon divine assistance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:A9","text":"The order of doctrine is determined by the matter itself insofar as the nature of the matter permits it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:NX1","text":"The instruction of beginners ought not to be restricted to the instruction of the advanced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:NX2","text":"Doctrine directed to beginners ought not to be constituted by the multiplication of useless questions, articles, and arguments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:NX3","text":"The necessary matters of doctrine ought not to be ordered by chance and the occasion of disputation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:NX4","text":"The transmission of doctrine ought not primarily to follow the form of the exposition of books.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:NX5","text":"Doctrinal repetition ought not to generate weariness and confusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:NX6","text":"The aim of the work is not indiscriminate completeness, but brevity and clarity according to the matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:D1","text":"If questions, articles, and arguments are multiplied uselessly, the capacity of beginners to receive doctrine is impeded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:D2","text":"If necessary matters are not transmitted according to the order of the discipline, knowledge of doctrine becomes dependent on the external order of exposition or disputation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A4","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:D3","text":"If the same things are frequently repeated, the transmission of doctrine is transformed into an experience of weariness and confusion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:D4","text":"The removal of useless multiplication, disorder, and repetition constitutes a preliminary condition of the instruction of beginners.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A3","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A4","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:D5","text":"The order of the discipline is more effective than the order of occasion for transmitting those things necessary to beginners.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A4","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:D6","text":"Brevity and clarity are not ornaments of exposition, but instruments for adapting doctrine to novices.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A1","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A2","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:SD1","text":"Doctrine for educating beginners requires a reordering of the whole subject matter, not a simple diminution of difficulty.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A2","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A4","CH_GGVC_PPP0:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:SD2","text":"The structure of doctrine becomes part of the pedagogical act itself, because the order of transmission determines the possibility of knowledge.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A4","CH_GGVC_PPP0:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:SD3","text":"The elimination of the useless, the disordered, and the repetitive constitutes a new economy of doctrine, in which the necessary is separated from the superfluous.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A3","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A4","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:SD4","text":"The authority of tradition is subjected to the principle of discipline, since the exposition of books and the occasion of disputation no longer suffice to determine the whole order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A4","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:SD5","text":"The precept to proceed briefly and clearly joins the epistemic, disciplinary, and pastoral end in a single form of exposition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPP0:A1","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A2","CH_GGVC_PPP0:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:DNX1","text":"Instruction is not identified with the accumulation of questions, articles, and arguments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:DNX2","text":"Knowledge is not presumed from the mere understanding of preceding texts or controversies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:DNX3","text":"The order of doctrine is not regarded as a neutral effect produced by the occasion of disputation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:DNX4","text":"The difficulty of doctrine is not treated as an immutable property that excludes adaptation for beginners.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:DNX5","text":"Clarity is not reduced to formless simplicity, but depends on disciplinary order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:DNX6","text":"The novice is not treated as a hearer for whom the same form of transmission as for the advanced would suffice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:SNX1","text":"The possibility of a doctrine that transmits everything without distinction between the necessary and the useless is prevented.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:SNX2","text":"The possibility of a discipline that receives its order from the contingency of disputation is prevented.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:SNX3","text":"The possibility of a knowledge that confuses by repetition and accumulation rather than instructing is prevented.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:SNX4","text":"The reduction of doctrine to the advanced is prevented, because the doctrinal beginning constitutes the proper object of the work.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:SNX5","text":"The separation between Catholic truth and the manner of its transmission is prevented, because the form of transmission pertains to the end of doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:FGS1","text":"Necessity of educating beginners → recognition of obstacles to doctrine → distinction of the useless, disordered, and repetitive from the necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:FGS2","text":"Useless multiplication + order of occasion + frequent repetition → impediment to knowledge in the forms of obstruction, weariness, and confusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:FGS3","text":"Removal of obstacles → restoration of the order of the discipline → adaptation of doctrine to the condition of beginners.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:FGS4","text":"Order of the discipline + brevity + clarity → transmission of sacred doctrine suited to the instruction of beginners.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:FGS5","text":"Pedagogical aim → execution under confidence in divine assistance → continuation of exposition according to the tolerance of the matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AF1","text":"Office of the teacher → necessity of educating beginners → defects of traditional forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AF2","text":"Defects of traditional forms → diagnosis of multiplication, disorder, and repetition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AF3","text":"Diagnosis of obstacles → new establishment of the order of doctrine according to the discipline.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AF4","text":"Order of the discipline → brevity and clarity → adaptation to novices.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:AF5","text":"Adaptation of doctrine → aim of the whole work under trust in divine assistance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:ME1","text":"The beginner becomes a structurally distinct subject of doctrine, not a merely lesser stage of the advanced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:ME2","text":"The order of the discipline becomes the principal criterion by which matters necessary to doctrine are separated from accidental arrangements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:ME3","text":"Questions, articles, and arguments can be classified anew according to their usefulness for instruction, not according to their mere existence in tradition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:ME4","text":"Repetition, disorder, and multiplication are transformed from material properties into formal obstacles to transmission.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:ME5","text":"Brevity and clarity are constituted as ordering operations, not as simple qualities of style.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:ALT1","text":"Surface — The transmission of Christian doctrine to beginners is directed briefly and clearly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:ALT2","text":"Structural — The efficacy of transmission depends on the order of the discipline and on the removal of multiplication, disorder, and repetition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text separates the discipline of doctrine from the order of the exposition of books and the occasion of disputation, in order to reconstitute the mode of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Doctrine distinguishes the condition of beginners and of the advanced as diverse modes of receptivity, while the progression of knowledge is arranged through disciplinary order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the necessity of the work, the diagnosis of obstacles, and the prescription of a new order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:MRT2","text":"The structure coheres internally, because the pedagogical aim is directly related to the defects of traditional forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:MRT3","text":"The connection between the condition of beginners, the order of the discipline, and the necessity of brevity and clarity is strongest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:MRT4","text":"What the order of the discipline itself materially contains is not yet fully determined; the prologue establishes only its principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:MRT5","text":"A tension remains between brevity and the scope of doctrine, as well as between the clarity of beginners and the tolerance required by the matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:MRT6","text":"The execution rests upon the theological presupposition of divine assistance, which sustains the rationale of the work but is not reducible to disciplinary method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPP0:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement of the text: from the office of the teacher to the diagnosis of obstacles, thence to reordering and the aim of the work.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPP0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PPP0@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_PPP0:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 01","title":"First Part · Prologue","context":"Chapter 01","original_title":"Prima pars · Prooemium"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Prooemium","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 1","order":2,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ1"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1@R001","sha256":"80cf557f11ca9a090426bc56a229d3408a5ecf3021c8417f1a2634e67f77b7f1","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS1","text":"Quaestio determinat quo iure sacra doctrina praeter disciplinas philosophicas constituatur, qualis scientia sit et ad quae se extendat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS2","text":"Sacra doctrina constituitur ut scientia divinitus revelata, ad salutem necessaria, una, principaliter speculativa, ceteris superior, sapientialis et Deum pro subiecto habens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS3","text":"Argumentatio est reconstructiva et architectonica in constitutione scientiae, polemica in dissolutione obiectionum, atque apologetica in circumscriptione usus rationis et auctoritatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS4","text":"Motus initialis procedit a fine humano rationem excedente et ab insufficientia cognitionis naturalis ad necessitatem revelationis, deinde a revelatione ad statutum scientiae subalternatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS5","text":"Motus medius procedit a ratione formali divinitus revelabilis ad unitatem doctrinae, principalitatem Dei, coniunctionem speculationis et praxis, atque supremam dignitatem sapientiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS6","text":"Motus finalis procedit a principiis fidei ad modos argumentandi, ab infirmitate cognitionis humanae ad metaphoram, et ab auctoritate divina ad pluralitatem sensuum super litteram fundatam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A1","text":"Homo ordinatur ad Deum sicut ad finem cuius comprehensio naturalem rationem excedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A2","text":"Finis ad quem intentiones et actiones ordinantur homini praecognitus esse debet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A3","text":"Veritas de Deo naturali ratione paucis, longo tempore et cum admixtione errorum provenit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A4","text":"Diversa ratio cognoscibilis, etiam circa easdem res, diversitatem scientiarum inducit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A5","text":"Scientia potest procedere ex principiis lumine superioris scientiae notis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6","text":"Principia sacrae doctrinae a Deo revelata sunt et in scientia Dei et beatorum nota.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","text":"Deus est principale subiectum sacrae doctrinae, cetera autem considerantur secundum ordinem ad eum ut principium et finem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A8","text":"Sacra doctrina principaliter de rebus divinis agit, de actibus autem humanis secundum ordinem ad perfectam Dei cognitionem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A9","text":"Dignitas scientiae ex certitudine, dignitate materiae et ultimitate finis aestimatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A10","text":"Certitudo sacrae doctrinae provenit ex lumine divinae scientiae, quod decipi non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A11","text":"Sapientia inferiora ordinat et iudicat secundum cognitionem causae altissimae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A12","text":"Sacra doctrina disciplinis philosophicis ad manifestationem uti potest sine receptione principiorum ab eis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A13","text":"Scientia ex principiis ad conclusiones argumentatur, non autem sua principia demonstrat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A14","text":"Vis argumenti ab auctoritate secundum fontem humanum vel divinum auctoritatis variatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A15","text":"Gratia naturam non tollit sed perficit, ideoque ratio naturalis fidei subservire potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A16","text":"Cognitio humana a sensu initium habet et per sensibilia ad intelligibilia ascendit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A17","text":"Cognitio Dei in hac vita non procedit ex comprehensione quidditatis, sed ex effectibus et remotione eorum quae Deo convenire non possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A18","text":"Deus, ut auctor Scripturae, non solum voces sed etiam res ipsas ad significandum ordinare potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX1","text":"Sacra doctrina non redditur superflua eo quod philosophia de ente et etiam de Deo tractet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX2","text":"Quae cognitionem humanam excedunt non sunt naturali ratione temere investiganda, sed, si revelantur, fide suscipienda.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX3","text":"Scientia non restringitur ad principia quae discenti per se nota sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX4","text":"Singularia in sacra doctrina non traduntur tamquam principale eius obiectum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX5","text":"Unitas sacrae doctrinae non requirit ut Creator et creatura sub uno genere materiali contineantur aut ex aequo considerentur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX6","text":"Sacra doctrina neque mere practica neque aequaliter practica et speculativa est, sed principaliter speculativa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX7","text":"Dubitatio humana circa articulos fidei non constituit incertitudinem rei vel fontis revelantis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX8","text":"Usus philosophiae non significat quod sacra doctrina ab ea tamquam a scientia superiori dependeat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX9","text":"Sapientia sacrae doctrinae non est idem habitus ac sapientia infusa quae per inclinationem de divinis iudicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX10","text":"Deum esse subiectum scientiae non requirit comprehensionem eius quidditativam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0090","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0099","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX11","text":"Ratio humana non adhibetur ad articulos fidei demonstrandos.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX12","text":"Auctoritates canonicae, ecclesiasticae et philosophicae non eandem vim argumentativam habent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX13","text":"Metaphorae Scripturae neque propter solam delectationem poeticam adhibentur neque proprietatem corporalem Deo attribuunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0130","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0152","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX14","text":"Pluralitas sensuum Scripturae non est aequivocatio, nec sensus spiritualis a litterali solutus est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX15","text":"Sensus parabolicus non constituit sensum a litterali separatum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0152","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D1","text":"Revelatio divina ad salutem necessaria est, quia finis supernaturalis cognosci debet, dum ratio naturalis eum neque comprehendit neque veritates divinas universaliter et sine errore tradit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D2","text":"Theologia philosophica et sacra doctrina de eisdem rebus tractare possunt quin eadem scientia sint, quia diversis luminibus et mediis cognoscunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D3","text":"Sacra doctrina est scientia subalternata, quia principia quae ipsa credit in superiori scientia Dei et beatorum nota sunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D4","text":"Materiae heterogeneae unam sacram scientiam constituunt quatenus una ratione formali divinitus revelabili et uno ordine ad Deum considerantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D5","text":"Sacra doctrina speculationem et praxim comprehendit, sed speculativa principaliter est quia actus humanos sub ordine ad cognitionem Dei considerat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D6","text":"Sacra doctrina scientias speculativas transcendit certitudine et materia, practicas autem ultimitate finis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D7","text":"Minor certitudo quoad nos cum maiori certitudine secundum naturam consistere potest, quia defectus est in intellectu recipiente, non in lumine divino.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D8","text":"Sacra doctrina maxime sapientia est quia Deum ut causam altissimam non solum per creaturas, sed etiam per eius revelationem considerat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0080","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D9","text":"Sacra doctrina principia aliarum scientiarum non demonstrat, sed de conclusionibus earum iudicat et quidquid veritati revelatae repugnat falsum pronuntiat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D10","text":"Philosophia sacrae doctrinae ministrat ad manifestationem, non ad constitutionem principiorum vel supplementum defectus doctrinae revelatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D11","text":"Sacra doctrina ex articulis fidei alia demonstrat, quamvis ipsos articulos non demonstret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D12","text":"Disputatio positiva contra negantem fidem tantum procedit ex principiis revelatis ab adversario concessis; nulla concessione manente, doctrina solum rationes contra fidem dissolvit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D13","text":"Scriptura canonica argumentum necessarium praebet, doctores Ecclesiae argumentum proprium sed probabile, philosophi autem argumentum extraneum et probabile.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D14","text":"Metaphora corporalis est accommodatio necessaria qua intellectus humanus a sensibilibus ad spiritualia elevatur et doctrina omnibus proponi potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D15","text":"Figurae rerum vilium aptiores esse possunt figuris nobilium, quia proprietatem corpoream evidentius removent et excessum Dei supra omne conceptum manifestant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D16","text":"Significatio vocum constituit sensum litteralem, significatio autem rerum per voces significatarum constituit sensum spiritualem allegoricum, moralem vel anagogicum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0146","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D17","text":"Omnis sensus spiritualis litteralem supponit, et sola littera argumentum necessarium fundat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D18","text":"Nihil fidei necessarium solo sensu spirituali continetur quin alicubi litteraliter tradatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D19","text":"Sensus parabolicus litteralis est quatenus littera intendit rem figuratam, non proprietatem imaginis adhibitae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A17","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0152","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D20","text":"Singularia sacrae doctrinae scientialitatem non tollunt, quia ad exemplum vitae et ad declarandam auctoritatem mediatorum revelationis introducuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD1","text":"Revelatio simul necessitatem, principia, rationem formalem, unitatem et certitudinem sacrae doctrinae generat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD2","text":"Deus ut subiectum, causa altissima, principium et finis eodem actu unitatem scientiae, dignitatem sapientiae, primatum speculationis et directionem praxis constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD3","text":"Ordo epistemicus systematis distinguit certitudinem obiectivam fontis a claritate subiectiva recipientis, ita ut altissima veritas naturaliter minus accessibilis sed formaliter certior sit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD4","text":"Ratio naturalis intra doctrinam revelatam instrumentum manifestationis et deductionis est, numquam autem fons principiorum aut tribunal revelationi superius.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D10","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D11","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD5","text":"Vis externa argumentationis decrescit secundum diminutionem principiorum communiter admissorum, quamvis certitudo interna fidei immutata maneat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D11","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD6","text":"Accommodatio sensibilis et cognitio apophatica unam ascensionem constituunt, in qua figura intellectum movet sed prohibetur ne terminus cognitionis fiat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D14","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D15","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0132","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD7","text":"Auctoritas divina semanticam rerum ipsarum instituit, sed pluralitas inde orta per dependentiam sensuum spiritualium a littera restringitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D16","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D17","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D18","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D19"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD8","text":"Sacra doctrina est simul ordo cognitionis et ordo salutis, quia cognitio finis supernaturalis actiones regit et in beatitudinem dirigit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX1","text":"Ratio naturalis non est principium sufficienter universale, celeriter efficax et infallibile cognitionis salutaris.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX2","text":"Evidentia immediata principiorum apud scientem non est condicio omnis scientialitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX3","text":"Communitas vel diversitas materiae non determinat per se identitatem vel pluralitatem scientiarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D2","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX4","text":"Accessibilitas intellectui humano non est mensura ultima certitudinis vel dignitatis veritatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX5","text":"Catalogus rerum tractatarum non sufficit ad subiectum scientiae definiendum sine ratione formali qua tractantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX6","text":"Fides et ratio non constituunt oppositionem in qua altera alteram aut excludat aut absorbeat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D10","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D11","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX7","text":"Argumentatio fidei non reducitur neque ad demonstrationem rationalem principiorum neque ad absentiam omnis rationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D11","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX8","text":"Sermo univocus et immediate positivus non est modus adaequatus cognitionis divinae in statu humano praesenti.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D14","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D15","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX9","text":"Figura sensibilis non est terminus semanticus revelationis, sed medium quod ad intelligibile remittit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D14","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D15","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D19"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0132","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX10","text":"Pluralitas sensuum non licentiam significationum independentium vel aequivocarum constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D16","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D17","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX11","text":"Nulla regio creaturarum in sacra doctrina formaliter autonoma ab ordine ad Deum manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0101","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SNX1","text":"Comprehensio directa quidditatis divinae intellectui humano in statu praesenti indisponibilis manet, atque hanc absentiam supplent revelatio, effectus, remotio et figura.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A17","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D14","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SNX2","text":"Tribunal epistemicum neutrum supra scientiam divinam, revelationem et rationem naturalem in systemate non admittitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SNX3","text":"Demonstratio articulorum fidei adversario nihil revelatum concedenti impossibilis habetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SNX4","text":"Demonstratio vera contra fidem structuraliter excluditur, quia verum contrario vero demonstrative opponi non potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SNX5","text":"Communicatio divina omnino nuda et sensibilibus mediis carens humanae naturae cognitivae non accommodatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SNX6","text":"Sensus spiritualis a littera independens atque ad doctrinam necessariam solus sufficiens praecluditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D17","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SNX7","text":"Finis creaturae vel actionis humanae qui ad Deum ulterius non ordinetur intra ambitum sacrae doctrinae inadmissibilis est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SNX8","text":"Fallibilitas auctoritatis canonicae excluditur ut incompatibilis cum fundamento divino principiorum doctrinae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0117","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:FGS1","text":"Series teleologica operatur hoc modo: finis supernaturalis → necessitas praecognitionis → insufficientia rationis → revelatio → doctrina salutaris.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:FGS2","text":"Series epistemica operatur hoc modo: principia revelata → notitia in scientia superiori → subalternatio → scientialitas → certitudo a fonte divino.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:FGS3","text":"Series formalis operatur hoc modo: lumen revelationis → una ratio cognoscibilis → reductio materiarum heterogenearum ad Deum → unitas scientiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:FGS4","text":"Series hierarchica operatur hoc modo: Deus ut causa altissima → sapientia → iudicium de inferioribus → usus ministerialis philosophiae → supremum ordinis doctrinalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D8","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:FGS5","text":"Series argumentativa operatur hoc modo: principia fidei non demonstrata → conclusiones ex fide → disputatio secundum concessa → dissolutio obiectionum ubi nulla principia communia manent → gradatio auctoritatum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D11","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D12","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:FGS6","text":"Series accommodationis operatur hoc modo: cognitio a sensu → figura corporalis → remotio proprietatis → elevatio ad intelligibile → cognitio apophatica Dei.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A17","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D14","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0132","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:FGS7","text":"Series hermeneutica operatur hoc modo: auctor divinus → voces significant res → res significant alias res → sensus spirituales → reductio ad litteram → firmitas argumenti.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A18","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D16","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D17","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0146","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AF1","text":"Quaestio de necessitate doctrinae → finis rationem excedens → defectus rationis naturalis → necessitas revelationis → constitutio scientiae subalternatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AF2","text":"Scientialitas revelata → ratio formalis una → Deus ut subiectum → coniunctio speculationis et praxis → superioritas → sapientia iudicans.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AF3","text":"Principia fidei → argumentatio condicionata → hierarchia auctoritatum → ministerium rationis → accommodatio metaphorica → pluralitas sensuum litteraliter stabilita.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D11","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D12","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D13","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D14","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D16","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME1","text":"Notio scientiae dilatatur ut scientiam subalternatam complectatur, cuius principia non sunt per se nota scienti sed superiori cognitioni innituntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME2","text":"Identitas scientiae a materia tractata ad rationem formalem et lumen cognitionis transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D2","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME3","text":"Philosophia et sacra doctrina non ut scientiae propter idem obiectum concurrentes, sed ut cognitiones eiusdem rei sub diversis luminibus describuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D2","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME4","text":"Certitudo dividitur in certitudinem fontis secundum naturam et evidentiam recipientis quoad nos.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME5","text":"Oppositio simplicis speculationis et praxis solvitur per inclusionem utriusque sub uno ordine ad cognitionem et fruitionem Dei.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME6","text":"Auctoritas in ordinem triplicem reclassificatur: canonica et necessaria, ecclesiastica et probabilis, philosophica et extranea.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME7","text":"Metaphora ex defectu poetico in instrumentum accommodationis cognitivae, correctionis apophaticae et tutelae contra praedicationem corpoream convertitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D14","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D15","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D19"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0130","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0152","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME8","text":"Res historicae et actus Scripturae fiunt signa secundi ordinis, quibus tempora legis, actio moralis et gloria futura inter se connectuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A18","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0146","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME9","text":"Ager disputationis non secundum solam oppositionem credentis et non credentis, sed secundum quantitatem principiorum revelatorum communiter concessorum describitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D11","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME10","text":"Deus simul ponitur ut subiectum cognitionis et ut terminus qui quidditative comprehendi non potest, unde effectus et negationes functionem quasi-definitoriam accipiunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ALT1","text":"In gradu superficiei proprietates sacrae doctrinae examinantur: necessitas, scientialitas, unitas, modus, dignitas, sapientia, subiectum, argumentatio, metaphora et sensus Scripturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ALT2","text":"In gradu structurali revelatio constituit principia et lumen, ratio formalis unit materiam, Deus ordinat subiectum, et auctoritas distribuit vim argumentorum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD2","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ALT3","text":"In gradu metastrategico fides certamen argumentativum secundum principia concessa ordinat, rationem sibi ministram constituit et fontes auctoritatis hierarchice disponit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D10","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D12","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ALT4","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali Deus est principium et finis omnium, creaturae relatione ad eum intelliguntur, historia fit ordo signorum, et cognitio humana a sensu praesenti ad beatitudinem futuram dirigitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D16","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0146","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:MRT1","text":"Corpus axiomatum sufficit ad necessitatem, scientialitatem, unitatem, dignitatem, methodum argumentativam et structuram hermeneuticam sacrae doctrinae restituendas.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:MRT2","text":"Cohaerentia systematis firmissima est ubi ratio formalis divinitus revelabilis ex diversis materiis unam scientiam efficit et eas ad Deum reducit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D2","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:MRT3","text":"Analogiam inter scientiam subalternatam et sacram doctrinam minus determinatam relinquit differentia inter principia evidenter cognita in scientia superiori et principia ab homine fide tantum recepta.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0020","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0028","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:MRT4","text":"Distinctio inter certitudinem secundum naturam et incertitudinem quoad nos tensionem solvit per mutationem gradus, sed modum participationis certitudinis divinae ab intellectu humano non explicat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0066","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:MRT5","text":"Usus philosophiae ut ancilla stabilitur per negationem dependentiae principiorum, sed limes inter manifestationem instrumentalem et dependentiam conceptualem non plene definitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D10","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0068","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:MRT6","text":"Argumentatio interne firma est ex principiis fidei, sed adversus negationem radicalem ad dissolutionem obiectionum restringitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D11","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D12","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:MRT7","text":"Pluralitas sensuum per fundamentum litterale continetur, sed regula determinandi omnes intentiones litterales quas intellectus divinus simul complectitur non formaliter exhibetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D16","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D17","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0146","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:MRT8","text":"Deus ut subiectum manet cognoscibilis per effectus et negationem, quamvis proportio inter talem quasi-definitionem et scientiam de subiecto non ulterius solvatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A17","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0099","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:MRT9","text":"Restitutio servat motum verum textus a necessitate salutari ad constitutionem scientiae, inde ad eius hierarchiam, argumentationem et semanticam scripturalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS5","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AF1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AF2","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T155356139371Z_483c299c","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 1","source_sha256":"80cf557f11ca9a090426bc56a229d3408a5ecf3021c8417f1a2634e67f77b7f1","sha256":"34aa64d4f1a1379efd02788df858555c26e0f7b5d27d5969cc0ee50020abd499","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:53:56.140758+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS1","text":"The question determines by what right sacred doctrine is constituted alongside the philosophical disciplines, what kind of science it is, and to what it extends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS2","text":"Sacred doctrine is constituted as a divinely revealed science, necessary for salvation, one, principally speculative, superior to the others, sapiential, and having God as its subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS3","text":"The argumentation is reconstructive and architectonic in the constitution of the science, polemical in the dissolution of objections, and apologetic in circumscribing the use of reason and authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS4","text":"The initial movement proceeds from the human end exceeding reason and from the insufficiency of natural cognition to the necessity of revelation, then from revelation to the status of a subalternated science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS5","text":"The middle movement proceeds from the formal ratio of what is divinely revealable to the unity of doctrine, the primacy of God, the conjunction of speculation and praxis, and the supreme dignity of wisdom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS6","text":"The final movement proceeds from the principles of faith to modes of arguing, from the weakness of human cognition to metaphor, and from divine authority to a plurality of senses grounded upon the letter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A1","text":"Man is ordered to God as to an end whose comprehension exceeds natural reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A2","text":"The end to which intentions and actions are ordered must be known beforehand by man.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A3","text":"Truth about God is attained by natural reason by few, after a long time, and with an admixture of errors.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A4","text":"A diverse ratio of knowability, even concerning the same things, induces a diversity of sciences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A5","text":"A science can proceed from principles known by the light of a higher science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6","text":"The principles of sacred doctrine are revealed by God and are known in the science of God and of the blessed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","text":"God is the principal subject of sacred doctrine; the rest are considered according to their order to him as principle and end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A8","text":"Sacred doctrine deals principally with divine things, but with human acts according to their order to the perfect knowledge of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A9","text":"The dignity of a science is assessed from certainty, the dignity of its matter, and the ultimacy of its end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A10","text":"The certainty of sacred doctrine proceeds from the light of divine science, which cannot be deceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A11","text":"Wisdom orders and judges lower things according to knowledge of the highest cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A12","text":"Sacred doctrine can use philosophical disciplines for manifestation without receiving principles from them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A13","text":"A science argues from principles to conclusions, but does not demonstrate its own principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A14","text":"The force of an argument from authority varies according to the human or divine source of the authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A15","text":"Grace does not abolish nature but perfects it; therefore natural reason can serve faith.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A16","text":"Human cognition begins from sense and ascends through sensible things to intelligible things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A17","text":"Knowledge of God in this life does not proceed from comprehension of the quiddity, but from effects and the removal of those things that cannot belong to God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A18","text":"God, as author of Scripture, can order not only words but also things themselves to signify.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX1","text":"Sacred doctrine is not rendered superfluous by the fact that philosophy treats of being and even of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX2","text":"Things that exceed human cognition are not to be rashly investigated by natural reason, but, if they are revealed, to be received by faith.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX3","text":"Science is not restricted to principles that are self-evident to the learner.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX4","text":"Singulars are not handed down in sacred doctrine as its principal object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX5","text":"The unity of sacred doctrine does not require that Creator and creature be contained under one material genus or be considered on equal terms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX6","text":"Sacred doctrine is neither merely practical nor equally practical and speculative, but principally speculative.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX7","text":"Human doubt concerning the articles of faith does not constitute uncertainty of the thing or of the revealing source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX8","text":"The use of philosophy does not mean that sacred doctrine depends on it as on a higher science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX9","text":"The wisdom of sacred doctrine is not the same habit as infused wisdom, which judges divine things through inclination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX10","text":"For God to be the subject of a science does not require comprehension of his quiddity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0090","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0099","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX11","text":"Human reason is not employed to demonstrate the articles of faith.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX12","text":"Canonical, ecclesiastical, and philosophical authorities do not have the same argumentative force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX13","text":"The metaphors of Scripture are neither employed for poetic delight alone nor attribute bodily property to God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0130","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0152","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX14","text":"The plurality of senses of Scripture is not equivocation, nor is the spiritual sense detached from the literal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:NX15","text":"The parabolic sense does not constitute a sense separate from the literal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0152","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D1","text":"Divine revelation is necessary for salvation, because the supernatural end must be known, whereas natural reason neither comprehends it nor conveys divine truths universally and without error.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D2","text":"Philosophical theology and sacred doctrine can treat of the same things without being the same science, because they know through diverse lights and means.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D3","text":"Sacred doctrine is a subalternated science, because the principles which it believes are known in the higher science of God and the blessed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D4","text":"Heterogeneous matters constitute one sacred science insofar as they are considered under one formal ratio of divine revealability and one order to God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D5","text":"Sacred doctrine comprehends speculation and praxis, but is principally speculative because it considers human acts under their order to the knowledge of God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D6","text":"Sacred doctrine transcends speculative sciences in certainty and matter, and practical sciences in the ultimacy of its end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D7","text":"Lesser certainty with respect to us can coexist with greater certainty according to nature, because the defect is in the receiving intellect, not in the divine light.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D8","text":"Sacred doctrine is wisdom in the highest degree because it considers God as the highest cause not only through creatures, but also through his revelation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0080","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D9","text":"Sacred doctrine does not demonstrate the principles of other sciences, but judges concerning their conclusions and pronounces false whatever is repugnant to revealed truth.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D10","text":"Philosophy ministers to sacred doctrine for manifestation, not for the constitution of principles or the supplementation of a defect in revealed doctrine.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D11","text":"Sacred doctrine demonstrates other things from the articles of faith, although it does not demonstrate the articles themselves.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D12","text":"Positive disputation against one denying the faith proceeds only from revealed principles conceded by the adversary; when no concession remains, doctrine only dissolves arguments against the faith.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D13","text":"Canonical Scripture provides a necessary argument, doctors of the Church a proper but probable argument, while philosophers provide an extraneous and probable argument.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D14","text":"Bodily metaphor is a necessary accommodation by which the human intellect is elevated from sensible things to spiritual things and doctrine can be proposed to all.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D15","text":"Figures of lowly things can be more fitting than figures of noble things, because they more evidently remove bodily property and manifest God's excess above every concept.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D16","text":"The signification of words constitutes the literal sense, whereas the signification of things signified through words constitutes the spiritual, allegorical, moral, or anagogical sense.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0146","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D17","text":"Every spiritual sense presupposes the literal, and the letter alone grounds a necessary argument.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D18","text":"Nothing necessary to faith is contained in the spiritual sense alone without being handed down somewhere literally.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D19","text":"The parabolic sense is literal insofar as the letter intends the thing figured, not the property of the image employed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A17","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0152","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D20","text":"Singulars do not remove the scientific character of sacred doctrine, because they are introduced as examples of life and to declare the authority of the mediators of revelation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD1","text":"Revelation simultaneously generates the necessity, principles, formal ratio, unity, and certainty of sacred doctrine.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD2","text":"God as subject, highest cause, principle, and end constitutes in one act the unity of the science, the dignity of wisdom, the primacy of speculation, and the direction of praxis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD3","text":"The epistemic order of the system distinguishes the objective certainty of the source from the subjective clarity of the recipient, such that the highest truth is naturally less accessible but formally more certain.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD4","text":"Natural reason within revealed doctrine is an instrument of manifestation and deduction, but never a source of principles or a tribunal superior to revelation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D10","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D11","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD5","text":"The external force of argumentation decreases according to the diminution of commonly admitted principles, although the internal certainty of faith remains unchanged.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D11","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD6","text":"Sensible accommodation and apophatic cognition constitute one ascent, in which the figure moves the intellect but is prevented from becoming the term of cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D14","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D15","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0132","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD7","text":"Divine authority establishes a semantics of things themselves, but the plurality arising from it is restricted through the dependence of spiritual senses upon the letter.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D16","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D17","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D18","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D19"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD8","text":"Sacred doctrine is at once an order of cognition and an order of salvation, because knowledge of the supernatural end governs actions and directs toward beatitude.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX1","text":"Natural reason is not a sufficiently universal, swiftly effective, and infallible principle of salvific cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX2","text":"Immediate evidence of principles in the knower is not a condition of all scientific character.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX3","text":"Community or diversity of matter does not in itself determine the identity or plurality of sciences.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D2","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX4","text":"Accessibility to the human intellect is not the ultimate measure of the certainty or dignity of truth.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX5","text":"A catalogue of the things treated does not suffice to define the subject of a science without the formal ratio under which they are treated.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX6","text":"Faith and reason do not constitute an opposition in which one either excludes or absorbs the other.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D10","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D11","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX7","text":"Argumentation of faith is reduced neither to rational demonstration of principles nor to the absence of all reason.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D11","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX8","text":"Univocal and immediately positive speech is not an adequate mode of divine cognition in the present human state.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D14","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D15","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX9","text":"A sensible figure is not the semantic terminus of revelation, but a medium that refers to the intelligible.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D14","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D15","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D19"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0132","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX10","text":"A plurality of senses does not constitute permission for independent or equivocal significations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D16","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D17","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:DNX11","text":"No realm of creatures remains in sacred doctrine formally autonomous from order to God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0101","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SNX1","text":"Direct comprehension of the divine quiddity remains unavailable to the human intellect in the present state, and revelation, effects, removal, and figure supply for this absence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A17","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D14","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SNX2","text":"No neutral epistemic tribunal above divine science, revelation, and natural reason is admitted in the system.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SNX3","text":"Demonstration of the articles of faith to an adversary conceding nothing revealed is held to be impossible.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SNX4","text":"A true demonstration against the faith is structurally excluded, because truth cannot demonstratively be opposed to contrary truth.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SNX5","text":"Divine communication wholly naked and lacking sensible media is not accommodated to human cognitive nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SNX6","text":"A spiritual sense independent from the letter and alone sufficient for necessary doctrine is precluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D17","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SNX7","text":"An end of a creature or human action that is not further ordered to God is inadmissible within the scope of sacred doctrine.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SNX8","text":"The fallibility of canonical authority is excluded as incompatible with the divine foundation of the principles of doctrine.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0117","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:FGS1","text":"The teleological series operates in this way: supernatural end → necessity of prior knowledge → insufficiency of reason → revelation → salvific doctrine.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:FGS2","text":"The epistemic series operates in this way: revealed principles → knowledge in a higher science → subalternation → scientific character → certainty from the divine source.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:FGS3","text":"The formal series operates in this way: light of revelation → one ratio of knowability → reduction of heterogeneous matters to God → unity of science.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:FGS4","text":"The hierarchical series operates in this way: God as highest cause → wisdom → judgment concerning lower things → ministerial use of philosophy → supreme status of the doctrinal order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D8","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:FGS5","text":"The argumentative series operates in this way: undemonstrated principles of faith → conclusions from faith → disputation according to concessions → dissolution of objections where no common principles remain → gradation of authorities.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D11","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D12","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:FGS6","text":"The series of accommodation operates in this way: cognition from sense → bodily figure → removal of property → elevation to the intelligible → apophatic cognition of God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A17","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D14","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0132","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:FGS7","text":"The hermeneutic series operates in this way: divine author → words signify things → things signify other things → spiritual senses → reduction to the letter → firmness of argument.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A18","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D16","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D17","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0146","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AF1","text":"Question concerning the necessity of doctrine → end exceeding reason → defect of natural reason → necessity of revelation → constitution of a subalternated science.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AF2","text":"Revealed scientific character → one formal ratio → God as subject → conjunction of speculation and praxis → superiority → judging wisdom.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AF3","text":"Principles of faith → conditioned argumentation → hierarchy of authorities → ministry of reason → metaphorical accommodation → plurality of senses established literally.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D11","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D12","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D13","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D14","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D16","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME1","text":"The notion of science is expanded so as to include subalternated science, whose principles are not self-evident to the knower but rest upon higher cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME2","text":"The identity of a science is transferred from the matter treated to the formal ratio and the light of cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D2","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME3","text":"Philosophy and sacred doctrine are described not as sciences competing on account of the same object, but as cognitions of the same thing under diverse lights.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D2","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME4","text":"Certainty is divided into certainty of the source according to nature and evidence in the recipient with respect to us.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME5","text":"The opposition of simple speculation and praxis is resolved by the inclusion of both under one order to the knowledge and fruition of God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME6","text":"Authority is reclassified into a threefold order: canonical and necessary, ecclesiastical and probable, philosophical and extraneous.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME7","text":"Metaphor is transformed from a poetic defect into an instrument of cognitive accommodation, apophatic correction, and protection against bodily predication.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D14","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D15","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D19"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0130","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0152","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME8","text":"Historical events and acts of Scripture become second-order signs, by which the times of the law, moral action, and future glory are interconnected.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A18","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0146","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME9","text":"The field of disputation is described not according to the mere opposition of believer and nonbeliever, but according to the quantity of commonly conceded revealed principles.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D11","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME10","text":"God is posited at once as the subject of cognition and as the terminus that cannot be comprehended quidditatively, whence effects and negations assume a quasi-definitional function.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ALT1","text":"At the level of the surface, the properties of sacred doctrine are examined: necessity, scientific character, unity, mode, dignity, wisdom, subject, argumentation, metaphor, and the senses of Scripture.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ALT2","text":"At the structural level, revelation constitutes principles and light, the formal ratio unites matter, God orders the subject, and authority distributes the force of arguments.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD2","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ALT3","text":"At the metastrategic level, faith orders argumentative contest according to conceded principles, establishes reason as its minister, and hierarchically arranges the sources of authority.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D10","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D12","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ALT4","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, God is the principle and end of all things, creatures are understood through relation to him, history becomes an order of signs, and human cognition is directed from present sense to future beatitude.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D16","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0146","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:MRT1","text":"The body of axioms suffices to restore the necessity, scientific character, unity, dignity, argumentative method, and hermeneutic structure of sacred doctrine.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:MRT2","text":"The coherence of the system is strongest where the formal ratio of divine revealability makes one science from diverse matters and reduces them to God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D2","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:MRT3","text":"The difference between principles evidently known in the higher science and principles received by man through faith alone leaves the analogy between subalternated science and sacred doctrine less determined.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0020","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0028","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:MRT4","text":"The distinction between certainty according to nature and uncertainty with respect to us resolves the tension through a change of level, but does not explain the mode of participation in divine certainty by the human intellect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0066","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:MRT5","text":"The use of philosophy as handmaid is established by denying dependence of principles, but the boundary between instrumental manifestation and conceptual dependence is not fully defined.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D10","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0068","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0115","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:MRT6","text":"Argumentation is internally firm from the principles of faith, but against radical denial it is restricted to the dissolution of objections.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D11","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D12","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0111","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:MRT7","text":"The plurality of senses is contained by the literal foundation, but no formal rule is set forth for determining all the literal intentions simultaneously comprehended by the divine intellect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D16","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D17","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:D18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0146","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0148","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:MRT8","text":"God as subject remains knowable through effects and negation, although the proportion between such a quasi-definition and science concerning the subject is not further resolved.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:A17","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:ME10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0096","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0099","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0134","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1:MRT9","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement of the text from salvific necessity to the constitution of science, thence to its hierarchy, argumentation, and scriptural semantics.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS4","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS5","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AS6","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AF1","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AF2","CH_GGVC_PPQ1:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ1:P0145","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PPQ1@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_PPQ1:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 02","title":"First Part · Question 1","context":"Chapter 02","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 1"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 1","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 2","order":3,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ2"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2@R001","sha256":"cda969e6616cd88088d31e86e8a8b5f96f534424f2a714b253e5b9fc122e8182","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AS1","text":"Quaestio de Deo non primum quaerit quid Deus sit, sed utrum eius esse sit homini per se notum, demonstrabile, et ex effectibus necessarie statuendum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AS2","text":"Textus distinguit veritatem propositionis secundum se ab eius evidentia quoad nos, ut identitas divinae essentiae et esse non transeat immediate in cognitionem humanam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AS3","text":"Ignorantia quidditatis divinae non impedit cognitionem existentiae, quia demonstratio quia ab effectibus notioribus quoad nos ad causam procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AS4","text":"Quinque viae effectus mundanos—motum, causalitatem efficientem, contingentiam, gradus perfectionis et ordinationem ad finem—in exigentias primi principii convertunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AS5","text":"Obiectiones ex malo et ex sufficientia naturae vel rationis humanae solvuntur, quia malum sub permissione boni supremi ponitur et principia mutabilia ad causam immobilem reducuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AS6","text":"Motus argumentationis est: apparentia vel negatio → distinctio epistemica → demonstratio ex effectibus → positio primi principii → subordinatio contrariorum apparentium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A1","text":"Propositio est per se nota secundum se quando praedicatum in ratione subiecti includitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A2","text":"Propositio per se nota secundum se non est necessario per se nota quoad eos qui rationem subiecti vel praedicati ignorant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A3","text":"Deus est suum esse; ideo propositio «Deus est» secundum se est per se nota.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A4","text":"Homo non cognoscit quid Deus sit; ideo divinum esse non est ei immediate notum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A5","text":"Quod est notius quoad nos potest esse minus notum quoad naturam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A6","text":"Effectus manifestus et a causa dependens requirit causam suam praeexistere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A7","text":"Demonstratio quia ex effectibus potest demonstrare causam esse sine perfecta cognitione essentiae eius.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A8","text":"Nihil reducitur de potentia in actum nisi per ens actu existens; idem non est secundum idem simul movens et motum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A9","text":"Series causarum efficientium ordinatarum sine primo termino effectum ultimum et causas medias evacuaret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A10","text":"Ens contingens, quod potest esse et non esse, non est per se ratio ultima existentiae entium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A11","text":"Necessarium cuius necessitas ab alio causatur non est ultimum principium necessitatis; series talium necessariorum in infinitum procedere non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A12","text":"Gradus bonitatis, veritatis, nobilitatis et entitatis referuntur ad id quod est maxime tale et causale respectu eorum quae participant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A13","text":"Agens cognitione carens non tendit determinate in finem nisi per directionem alicuius cognoscentis et intelligentis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A14","text":"Natura, ratio humana et voluntas humana sunt mutabilia et defectibilia; ideo non sistunt reductionem ad primum principium immobile et per se necessarium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A15","text":"Bonum infinitum potest permittere malum solum sub condicione quod ex malo bonum eliciat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:NX1","text":"Propositio «Deus est» non est per se nota quoad nos, quamvis sit per se nota secundum se.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:NX2","text":"Cognitio naturalis beatitudinis sub ratione confusa non est cognitio explicita Dei existentis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:NX3","text":"Conceptus eius quo maius cogitari non potest non sufficit ad concludendum rem extra intellectum exsistere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:NX4","text":"Cognitio veritatis in communi non est cognitio primae veritatis quoad nos.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:NX5","text":"Divinum esse non ponitur intra articulos fidei ut obiectum ratione naturale inaccessible, sed intra praeambula quae ratio naturalis attingere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:NX6","text":"Demonstratio divina non procedit propter quid ex divina quidditate, quia divina quidditas nobis ignota manet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:NX7","text":"Effectus finiti non tradunt cognitionem perfectam causae infinitae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:NX8","text":"Deus non est causa sui, ens mobile, principium mutabile, nec necessarium cuius necessitas ab alio pendet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:D1","text":"Eadem propositio divina potest esse ontologice immediata et epistemice mediata, quia eius praedicatum cum subiecto coincidit in re sed non in cognitione humana.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:D2","text":"Definitio nominalis Dei dirigit inquisitionem, sed non constituit probationem existentiae divinae nisi effectus causae exigantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:D3","text":"Cognitio Dei ex creaturis est realis quantum ad an sit, sed imperfecta quantum ad quid sit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:D4","text":"Motus sensibilis exigit primum movens immobile, quia potentia non seipsam secundum idem in actum reducit et motus subordinatus sine primo motore non agit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:D5","text":"Ordo causarum efficientium exigit causam efficientem primam, quia remotione principii causalitas media et effectus ultimus simul cadunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:D6","text":"Contingentia entium exigit ens per se necessarium quod sit causa necessitatis aliis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:D7","text":"Natura et actio rationalis humana non sunt explicationes ultimae mundi, quia ipsa principia ad superiorem causam reducuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:D8","text":"Malum non refutat bonum infinitum, si malum non habet statum principii autonomi sed intra ordinem boni elicibilis permittitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:SD1","text":"Systema separat ordinem essendi ab ordine cognoscendi: Deus est principium maxime immediatum in re, sed terminus cognitionis humanae mediate attingendus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:SD2","text":"Quinque viae non tantum quinque indicia cumulant, sed diversos ordines dependentiae—actualem, efficientem, necessarium, participativum et teleologicum—ad unum nomen principii convertunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:SD3","text":"Theologia naturalis hic non possidet essentiam divinam, sed universum tamquam ordinem effectuum ontologice dependentium legit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:SD4","text":"Malum, casus apparens et causalitas creata reclassificantur non ut principia rivalia, sed ut realitates sub ordine causae primae intelligendae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A14","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet quod conceptus mentis, etiam conceptus summi entis, per se existentiam rei extra mentem producat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet quod ens contingens, mobile vel mutabile sui ipsius ultima explicatio esse possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet quod causae ordinatae possint causaliter operari absque fundamento primo a quo virtus earum dependeat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet quod malum sit principium aequipollens bono infinito aut impedimentum absolutum providentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:SNX1","text":"Systema excludit cognitionem divinam quae esset simul immediata, quidditativa et naturaliter communis intellectui humano.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:SNX2","text":"Systema excludit mundum ontologice clausum intra naturam, rationem humanam, voluntatem humanam vel seriem causarum mutabilium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:SNX3","text":"Systema excludit pluralitatem principiorum ultimorum, quia omnis via terminatur in primo quod non ab alio dependet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:FGS1","text":"Identitas Dei cum suo esse + ignorantia quidditatis divinae → distinctio inter per se notum secundum se et per se notum quoad nos.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:FGS2","text":"Non-evidentia quoad nos + manifestatio effectuum creatorum → demonstratio quia ab effectibus ad causam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:FGS3","text":"Motus / causalitas / contingentia / gradatio / ordinatio finalis → primum movens immobile / causa efficiens prima / necessarium per se / maximum causale / intellectus ordinans.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:FGS4","text":"Convergentia terminorum causalium → positio Dei ut principii universalis esse, perfectionis, ordinis et finis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:FGS5","text":"Malum permissum + principia creata mutabilia → negationes non destruunt primum principium, sed intra ipsum ordinem reducuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AF1","text":"Praetensa notitia naturalis Dei → distinctio cognitionis confusae et cognitionis explicitae → negatio evidentiae quoad nos.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AF2","text":"Definitio nominalis vel veritas universalis → separatio intellectus et rei, veritatis communis et primae veritatis → negatio probationis a solo conceptu.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AF3","text":"Ignorantia essentiae divinae → imminutio demonstrationis propter quid → institutio demonstrationis quia per effectus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AF4","text":"Effectus mundani dependentes → quinque rationes primi principii → nomen commune Dei.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AF5","text":"Malum et apparentia sufficientiae causarum creatarum → permissio boni et reductio mutabilium → clausura in principio immobile et necessario.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:ME1","text":"Distinctio «secundum se / quoad nos» facit visibilem discrepantiam inter structuram rei et accessum cognoscentis ad rem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:ME2","text":"Oppositio fidei et rationis dissolvitur in relationem praeambuli et complementi: demonstrabile potest credi ab eo qui demonstrationem non capit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0025","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0035","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:ME3","text":"Effectus finiti reclassificantur ex rebus solum apparentibus in signa dependentiae causalis, etiamsi causam perfecte non manifestant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:ME4","text":"Diversa genera ordinis mundani reclassificantur ut modi unius reductionis ad principium primum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:ME5","text":"Natura, voluntas humana et malum desinunt esse termini explicativi ultimi et fiunt momenta subordinata ordini superiori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus examinat utrum Deus sit per se notus, demonstrabilis, et realiter existens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:ALT2","text":"Structural — Textus explicat relationes potentiae et actus, causae et effectus, contingentiae et necessitatis, gradus et participationis, finis et directionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Textus mutat quaestionem a possessione divinae quidditatis ad legitimam reductionem effectuum manifestorum in causam primam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Textus ponit ens primum ut immobile, per se necessarium et non ab alio dependens, a quo mobilia, causata et defectibilia dependent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad restituendam distinctionem fundamentalem inter veritatem divini esse in se et eius cognitionem humanam per effectus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:MRT2","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad restituendas quinque vias ut variationes reductionis a dependentia effectus ad principium non dependens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:MRT3","text":"Systema firmissime cohaeret ubi ignorantia quidditatis divinae non in agnosticismum transit, sed methodum demonstrationis quia determinat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:MRT4","text":"Commissura fragilis manet in transitu ab impossibilitate regressus infiniti ad necessitatem termini primi, quia forma ordinis causalium praesupponitur ut conditio sine qua effectus non stent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:MRT5","text":"Commissura fragilis manet in quarta via, quia relatio inter gradus perfectionis, maximum in genere et causalitatem universalem ponitur ut nexus generativus sine ulteriori distinctione.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:MRT6","text":"Tensio manet inter affirmationem Dei ut ipsius esse et prohibitionem cognitionis quidditativae: systema eam non solvit per comprehensionem essentiae, sed per distinctionem ordinum cognitionis et entitatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:MRT7","text":"Restitutio servat motum textus, si conclusio non legitur ut cognitio exhaustiva Dei, sed ut positio causaliter necessaria primi principii ex effectibus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T155418169193Z_1a42d978","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 2","source_sha256":"cda969e6616cd88088d31e86e8a8b5f96f534424f2a714b253e5b9fc122e8182","sha256":"77c6ab8ced3ea381dca5b52ef3a84a34a5c2c30ad7ca50dae135f69759a7ccb8","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:54:18.171256+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AS1","text":"The question concerning God does not first ask what God is, but whether his existence is self-evident to man, demonstrable, and necessarily to be posited from effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AS2","text":"The text distinguishes the truth of a proposition in itself from its evidence with respect to us, so that the identity of the divine essence and existence does not pass immediately into human knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AS3","text":"Ignorance of the divine quiddity does not impede knowledge of existence, because a demonstratio quia proceeds from effects better known with respect to us to the cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AS4","text":"The five ways convert worldly effects—motion, efficient causality, contingency, degrees of perfection, and ordering to an end—into requirements of a first principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AS5","text":"Objections from evil and from the sufficiency of nature or human reason are resolved, because evil is placed under the permission of the highest good and mutable principles are reduced to an immutable cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AS6","text":"The movement of the argument is: appearance or negation → epistemic distinction → demonstration from effects → positing of the first principle → subordination of apparent contraries.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A1","text":"A proposition is self-evident in itself when the predicate is included in the account of the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A2","text":"A proposition self-evident in itself is not necessarily self-evident with respect to those who are ignorant of the account of the subject or predicate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A3","text":"God is his own existence; therefore the proposition «God exists» is self-evident in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A4","text":"Man does not know what God is; therefore divine existence is not immediately known to him.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A5","text":"What is better known with respect to us can be less known with respect to nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A6","text":"A manifest effect dependent on a cause requires its cause to preexist.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A7","text":"A demonstratio quia from effects can demonstrate that a cause exists without perfect knowledge of its essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A8","text":"Nothing is reduced from potency to act except by a being existing in act; the same thing is not, in the same respect and at the same time, mover and moved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A9","text":"A series of ordered efficient causes without a first term would eliminate the ultimate effect and the intermediate causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A10","text":"A contingent being, which can be and not be, is not through itself the ultimate account of the existence of beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A11","text":"A necessary being whose necessity is caused by another is not the ultimate principle of necessity; a series of such necessary beings cannot proceed to infinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A12","text":"Degrees of goodness, truth, nobility, and being are referred to that which is most such and causal with respect to those which participate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A13","text":"An agent lacking knowledge does not tend determinately toward an end except through the direction of some knowing and intelligent being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A14","text":"Nature, human reason, and human will are mutable and fallible; therefore they do not halt the reduction to a first immutable and through-itself necessary principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A15","text":"Infinite good can permit evil only on the condition that it draws good from evil.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:NX1","text":"The proposition «God exists» is not self-evident with respect to us, although it is self-evident in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:NX2","text":"Knowledge of natural beatitude under a confused account is not explicit knowledge of the existing God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:NX3","text":"The concept of that than which nothing greater can be thought does not suffice to conclude that a thing exists outside the intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:NX4","text":"Knowledge of truth in general is not knowledge of the first truth with respect to us.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:NX5","text":"Divine existence is not placed among the articles of faith as an object naturally inaccessible to reason, but among the preambles that natural reason can attain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:NX6","text":"Divine demonstration does not proceed propter quid from the divine quiddity, because the divine quiddity remains unknown to us.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:NX7","text":"Finite effects do not convey perfect knowledge of an infinite cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:NX8","text":"God is not the cause of himself, a mobile being, a mutable principle, nor a necessary being whose necessity depends on another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:D1","text":"The same divine proposition can be ontologically immediate and epistemically mediated, because its predicate coincides with its subject in reality but not in human knowledge.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:D2","text":"The nominal definition of God directs inquiry, but does not constitute proof of divine existence unless effects require a cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:D3","text":"Knowledge of God from creatures is real as to whether he is, but imperfect as to what he is.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:D4","text":"Sensible motion requires a first unmoved mover, because potency does not reduce itself, in the same respect, to act, and subordinated motion does not act without a first mover.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:D5","text":"The order of efficient causes requires a first efficient cause, because upon removal of the principle, intermediate causality and the ultimate effect simultaneously fall away.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:D6","text":"The contingency of beings requires a being necessary through itself that is the cause of necessity for others.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:D7","text":"Nature and rational human action are not ultimate explanations of the world, because their very principles are reduced to a higher cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:D8","text":"Evil does not refute infinite good, if evil does not have the status of an autonomous principle but is permitted within the order of good that can be drawn forth.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:SD1","text":"The system separates the order of being from the order of knowing: God is the most immediate principle in reality, but the terminus of human knowledge to be attained mediately.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:SD2","text":"The five ways do not merely accumulate five indications, but convert diverse orders of dependence—actual, efficient, necessary, participatory, and teleological—into one name of a principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:SD3","text":"Natural theology here does not possess the divine essence, but reads the universe as an order of ontologically dependent effects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:SD4","text":"Evil, apparent chance, and created causality are reclassified not as rival principles, but as realities to be understood under the order of the first cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A14","CH_GGVC_PPQ2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:DNX1","text":"It must be false that a concept of the mind, even the concept of the highest being, by itself produces the existence of a thing outside the mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:DNX2","text":"It must be false that a contingent, mobile, or mutable being can be the ultimate explanation of itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:DNX3","text":"It must be false that ordered causes can operate causally without a first foundation upon which their power depends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:DNX4","text":"It must be false that evil is a principle equal in power to infinite good or an absolute impediment to providence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:SNX1","text":"The system excludes divine knowledge that would be at once immediate, quidditative, and naturally common to the human intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:SNX2","text":"The system excludes a world ontologically closed within nature, human reason, human will, or a series of mutable causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:SNX3","text":"The system excludes a plurality of ultimate principles, because every way terminates in a first that does not depend on another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:FGS1","text":"God's identity with his own existence + ignorance of the divine quiddity → distinction between self-evident in itself and self-evident with respect to us.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:FGS2","text":"Non-evidence with respect to us + manifestation of created effects → demonstratio quia from effects to cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:FGS3","text":"Motion / causality / contingency / gradation / final ordering → first unmoved mover / first efficient cause / necessary through itself / causal maximum / ordering intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:FGS4","text":"Convergence of causal termini → positing of God as universal principle of being, perfection, order, and end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:FGS5","text":"Permitted evil + mutable created principles → negations do not destroy the first principle, but are reduced within its very order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AF1","text":"Claimed natural knowledge of God → distinction between confused knowledge and explicit knowledge → negation of evidence with respect to us.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AF2","text":"Nominal definition or universal truth → separation of intellect and thing, common truth and first truth → negation of proof from concept alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AF3","text":"Ignorance of the divine essence → limitation of demonstratio propter quid → establishment of demonstratio quia through effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AF4","text":"Dependent worldly effects → five accounts of the first principle → common name of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:AF5","text":"Evil and the appearance of the sufficiency of created causes → permission by the good and reduction of mutable things → closure in an immutable and necessary principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:ME1","text":"The distinction «in itself / with respect to us» renders visible the discrepancy between the structure of the thing and the knower's access to the thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:ME2","text":"The opposition of faith and reason is dissolved into a relation of preamble and complement: what is demonstrable can be believed by one who does not grasp the demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0025","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0035","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:ME3","text":"Finite effects are reclassified from merely apparent things into signs of causal dependence, even if they do not perfectly manifest the cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:ME4","text":"Diverse kinds of worldly order are reclassified as modes of one reduction to a first principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:ME5","text":"Nature, human will, and evil cease to be ultimate explanatory termini and become moments subordinated to a higher order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text examines whether God is self-evident, demonstrable, and really existent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text explains the relations of potency and act, cause and effect, contingency and necessity, degree and participation, end and direction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text shifts the question from possession of the divine quiddity to the legitimate reduction of manifest effects to the first cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The text posits the first being as immutable, necessary through itself, and not dependent on another, upon which mobile, caused, and defective things depend.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to restore the fundamental distinction between the truth of divine existence in itself and its human knowledge through effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:MRT2","text":"The axioms suffice to restore the five ways as variations of reduction from the dependence of an effect to a non-dependent principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:MRT3","text":"The system coheres most firmly where ignorance of the divine quiddity does not pass into agnosticism, but determines the method of demonstratio quia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:MRT4","text":"A fragile joint remains in the transition from the impossibility of infinite regress to the necessity of a first term, because the form of causal order is presupposed as a condition without which effects would not stand.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:MRT5","text":"A fragile joint remains in the fourth way, because the relation among degrees of perfection, the maximum in a genus, and universal causality is posited as a generative nexus without further distinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:MRT6","text":"A tension remains between the affirmation of God as existence itself and the prohibition of quidditative knowledge: the system does not resolve it through comprehension of the essence, but through a distinction between the orders of knowledge and being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text if the conclusion is read not as exhaustive knowledge of God, but as the causally necessary positing of a first principle from effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PPQ2@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_PPQ2:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 03","title":"First Part · Question 2","context":"Chapter 03","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 2"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 2","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 3","order":4,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ3"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3@R001","sha256":"15d1e5972013c481326399b9bf4093a91bfcb1c25c9b89fdfe0a6750fbaaafa9","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS1","text":"Postquam ex effectibus constitutum est Deum esse, quaestio transfertur a quidditate divina ad modos essendi qui ab eo removendi sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS2","text":"Essentia Dei positive comprehendi non potest; cognitio praesentis gradus procedit per remotionem eorum quae Deo non conveniunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS3","text":"Thesis principalis est Deum omni modo simplicem esse, ita ut nulla compositio intra ipsum aut inter ipsum et alia admittatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS4","text":"Argumentatio est apophatica et metaphysice reconstructiva: prioritas divina in actum purum, identitatem intrinsecam et exclusionem compositionis resolvitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS5","text":"Motus procedit ab incorporeitate ad remotionem materiae, distinctionum intrinsecarum, generis et accidentium, deinde ad simplicitatem totalem et non-commixtionem cum creaturis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS6","text":"Apparentes instantiae ex Scriptura et praedicatione creata solvuntur distinguendo similitudinem actus vel effectus a constitutione ontologica Dei.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0105","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A1","text":"Ex effectibus sciri potest propositionem «Deus est» veram esse, quamvis essentia et actus essendi divinus quidditative non cognoscantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A2","text":"Deus est primum movens omnino immobile.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","text":"Deus est primum ens omnino actuale et nullo modo potentiale.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A4","text":"Deus est prima causa efficiens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A5","text":"Bonitas et perfectio divina sunt per essentiam, non per participationem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A6","text":"Nullum corpus movet quin ipsum moveatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A7","text":"Omne corpus, quatenus continuum divisibile est, potentialitatem implicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A8","text":"Materia est principium potentialitatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A9","text":"Primum et per se agens oportet esse per essentiam formam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A10","text":"Forma materiae irreceptibilis et per se subsistens ex se individuatur, ita ut suppositum et natura non differant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A11","text":"Si esse rei ab essentia eius differt, esse illius rei ab alio causatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A12","text":"Esse est actualitas omnis formae vel naturae; essentia ab esse distincta ad ipsum se habet sicut potentia ad actum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A13","text":"Quod est species in genere compositionem generis et differentiae atque distinctionem essentiae ab esse requirit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A14","text":"Accidens subiectum praesupponit, quod ad ipsum sicut potentia ad actum ordinatur, atque ex principiis subiecti vel causa extrinseca causatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A15","text":"Omne compositum est posterius componentibus et ab eis dependet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A16","text":"Principia secundum se diversa in unum non conveniunt nisi per causam adunantem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A17","text":"Quod intrat compositionem alterius non est primo et per se agens; compositum potius per partem agit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A18","text":"Praedicata ex rebus corporalibus vel creatis ad Deum transferuntur secundum similitudinem actus, effectus vel virtutis, non secundum univocam constitutionem rei.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0105","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX1","text":"Quid Deus sit positive in praesenti ordine cognosci non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX2","text":"Deus non est corpus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX3","text":"In Deo non est compositio materiae et formae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX4","text":"In Deo non differunt suppositum et natura.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX5","text":"In Deo non differunt essentia et esse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX6","text":"Deus neque ens commune neque species generis substantiae neque aliquo modo in genere contentum est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0091","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX7","text":"Deus genus, differentiam et definitionem quidditativam non habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX8","text":"In Deo nullum accidens est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX9","text":"Deus nullo modo compositus est neque pars compositionis alterius esse potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX10","text":"Deus non est anima mundi, principium formale intrinsecum omnium rerum aut materia prima.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX11","text":"Dimensiones, figura, membra, situs, motus localis, passiones et anima Deo non attribuuntur secundum sensum corporeum vel creatum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D1","text":"Deus corpus esse non potest, quia corpus vel motui subicitur vel potentialitatem divisibilitatis includit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D2","text":"In Deo materia et forma componi non possunt; Deus est per essentiam forma, non forma in materia recepta.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D3","text":"Deus ex se individuatur, et eius suppositum est ipsa eius essentia vel natura.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","text":"Deus est ipsum suum esse, quia esse neque ab alio recipit neque ad essentiam suam sicut actus ad potentiam comparatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5","text":"Deus in genere esse non potest, ideoque genus, differentiam, definitionem et demonstrationem per definitionem non admittit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D6","text":"In Deo accidens esse non potest, quia nullum subiectum potentiale aut principium causatum in ipso est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A14","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7","text":"Deus est omnino simplex, quia omnes compositionis formae removentur et quaelibet compositio priora componentia ac causam unionis requireret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A15","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D2","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D8","text":"Deus neque materialiter neque formaliter in compositionem rerum venit, sed eas effective et exemplariter causat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A17","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0139","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D9","text":"Praedicata corporalia et passionalia de Deo significant virtutem, actum, effectum vel affectum spiritualem secundum similitudinem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A18","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D2","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D10","text":"Distinctio nominum concretorum et abstractorum de Deo diversitatem acceptionis intellectus significat, non diversitatem rei divinae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A18","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D11","text":"Esse divinum non est esse commune: illi additio ex ipsa ratione excluditur, huic additio tantum non includitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD1","text":"Quod in Deo unum et per essentiam est, in causatis deficienter per participationem, pluralitatem et compositionem repraesentatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0122","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD2","text":"Deus non est primum membrum ordinis categorialis, sed principium extra omne genus respectu totius esse.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D8","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0107","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD3","text":"Multiplicitas nominum divinorum multiplicat modos conceptionis humanae, non principia constituentia Deum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0105","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD4","text":"Universalitas causalitatis divinae requirit praesentiam effectivam et exemplarem sine commixtione constitutiva cum effectibus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0139","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD5","text":"Cognitio Dei est asymmetrica: veritas propositionis de existentia ex effectibus tenetur, actus autem essendi et essentia non comprehenduntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD6","text":"Simplicitas divina non significat defectum perfectionis, sed perfectionem quae in uno per essentiam continetur, ubi creaturae eam tantum distributam recipiunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0122","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:DNX1","text":"Systema tacite excludit planum ontologicum univocum in quo Deus et creaturae eiusdem generis membra essent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D11","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0105","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:DNX2","text":"Nihil Deo prius esse potest quod eum contineat, definiat, componat, mensuret aut in unitatem adunet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A15","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:DNX3","text":"Receptivitas, actualizatio ab alio et mutabilitas structuraliter excluduntur, quia quaelibet earum potentiam in Deo poneret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:DNX4","text":"Nulla perfectio Deo advenire aut ab ipso participari potest quasi essentiae iam constitutae additamentum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D6","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:DNX5","text":"Distinctio grammatica vel conceptualis non sufficit ad inferendam distinctionem realem in Deo.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D10","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:DNX6","text":"Simplicitas non cogit simplicia ad identitatem: Deus et materia prima sunt diversa seipsis, non differentiis compositivis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SNX1","text":"Negatio intima systematis est absentia omnis alteritatis intrinsecae: Deus non habet esse, formam, vitam vel bonitatem quasi alia a se, sed est ea per essentiam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D2","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SNX2","text":"Praecluditur quaelibet totalitas ontologica Deo prior, intra quam Deus locus, species, pars, mensura homogenea aut summum exemplar eiusdem ordinis esset.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D8","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0084","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0093","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SNX3","text":"Mundus non potest concipi ut compositio, explicatio partium aut materia corporis divini; dependentia creaturarum causaliter, non mereologice, constituitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX10","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D8","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0139","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SNX4","text":"Possibilitas sermonis de Deo pendet ab impossibilitate comprehensionis quidditativae: effectus, remotiones et similitudines cognitionem ordinant ubi definitio positiva deest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A18","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:FGS1","text":"Machina epistemica procedit hoc ordine: effectus → veritas propositionis «Deus est» → inaccessibilitas quidditatis → inquisitio per remotionem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:FGS2","text":"Fundamentum metaphysicum procedit hoc ordine: primum movens et primum ens → immobilitas et actus purus → exclusio corporis et materiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:FGS3","text":"Series intrinseca est: nulla materia → nulla distinctio suppositi et naturae → nulla distinctio essentiae et esse → nullum genus → nullum accidens.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D2","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:FGS4","text":"Clausura metaphysica est: remotio omnium modorum compositionis + posterioritas et causalitas cuiuslibet compositi → simplicitas totalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A15","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:FGS5","text":"Clausura cosmologica est: simplicitas totalis + causalitas prima et per se → impossibilitas Dei ut partis → causalitas effectiva et exemplaris.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A17","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0139","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:FGS6","text":"Circuitus semanticus est: praedicatum creatum → apparens compositio divina → reductio ad similitudinem actus vel effectus → conservatio simplicitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A18","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AF1","text":"Existentia cognita ex effectibus → quidditas incomprehensibilis → remotio corporis → remotio compositionum intrinsecarum → simplicitas absoluta → exclusio compositionis cum aliis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D2","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D6","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AF2","text":"Praedicatio scripturalis vel creata → interpretatio litteralis obiecta → distinctio rei a modo significandi → translatio ad virtutem, actum vel effectum → dissolutio consequentiae compositivae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A18","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:ME1","text":"Quaestio theologica a descriptione positiva essentiae ad disciplinam remotionum transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:ME2","text":"Simplicitas ex proprietate rerum minimarum in condicionem metaphysicam primi entis et summae perfectionis reclassificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0122","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:ME3","text":"Deus e tabula generum et specierum removetur atque ut principium totius esse extra ordinem categorialem collocatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D11","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0107","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:ME4","text":"Oppositio fundamentalis fit inter esse per essentiam et esse per participationem, non inter duo membra eiusdem generis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:ME5","text":"Causalitas divina a compositione separatur: causa universalis non ingreditur effectus ut materia vel forma intrinseca.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D8","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0139","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:ME6","text":"Corporea et affectiva praedicatio in indicem virtutum, actuum et effectuum convertitur, quo multiplicitas sermonis a compositione rei distinguitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D10","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:ALT1","text":"Gradus superficiei octo modos compositionis examinat atque singulos a Deo removet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX2","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX6","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX8","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:ALT2","text":"Gradus structuralis oppositionibus actus et potentiae, essentiae et esse, participationis et essentiae, causae et partis regitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:ALT3","text":"Gradus metastrategicus cognitionem et praedicationem Dei ordinat per effectus, remotionem et similitudinem, ne formae cognitionis creatae in rem divinam transferantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A18","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:ALT4","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis prioritatem simplicem actus statuit: quamvis potentia in eodem transeunte tempore actum praecedere possit, actus simpliciter prior est, unde primum ens nec fit nec ab alio actualizatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad seriem octo exclusionum restituendam, si conclusiones antecedentes de primo movente, primo ente et prima causa admittuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:MRT2","text":"Cohaerentia interna maxime nititur conversione prioritatum divinarum in actum purum, esse per essentiam et independentiam a componentibus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:MRT3","text":"Firmissima commissura est concursus trium viarum: potentia compositionem excludit, causalitas prima dependentiam excludit, esse per essentiam participationem excludit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:MRT4","text":"Minus determinata manent propositiones de Deo ut nobilissimo ente, primo bono et principio totius esse, quia earum probationes partim ex prioribus assumuntur vel ad posteriora remittuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0020","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0089","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:MRT5","text":"Solutio praedicationum creatarum similitudine et non-univocitate nititur, sed plena ratio analogiae in hac quaestione nondum formaliter explicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0105","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:MRT6","text":"Tensio epistemica inter ignorantiam actus essendi divini et demonstrationem identitatis essentiae cum esse solvitur distinctione inter comprehensionem actus et veritatem propositionis «Deus est»; haec distinctio structuram sustinet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:MRT7","text":"Locutiones quibus Deus «forma» et «ipsum esse» dicitur fragiles fiunt nisi forma exemplaris a forma partis et esse divinum ab esse communi stricte distinguantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0042","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0073","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0075","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0139","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0141","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:MRT8","text":"Restitutio servat motum verum textus: cognitio per effectus → remotiones ordinatae → simplicitas totalis → causalitas sine commixtione; contradictio formalis non restat, sed nexus pendet a distinctionibus semanticis et metaphysicis indicatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0139","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T155446678906Z_0bd6e5eb","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 3","source_sha256":"15d1e5972013c481326399b9bf4093a91bfcb1c25c9b89fdfe0a6750fbaaafa9","sha256":"14f3038bac437bb5d6b6018e97eb1d440052f51e4fba263f79642e75bf6e3e6b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:54:46.681102+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS1","text":"After it has been established from effects that God exists, the question shifts from the divine quiddity to the modes of being that must be removed from him.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS2","text":"God's essence cannot be positively comprehended; cognition at the present level proceeds by removing those things that do not belong to God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS3","text":"The principal thesis is that God is altogether simple, such that no composition is admitted either within himself or between himself and other things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS4","text":"The argumentation is apophatic and metaphysically reconstructive: divine priority is resolved into pure act, intrinsic identity, and the exclusion of composition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS5","text":"The movement proceeds from incorporeality to the removal of matter, intrinsic distinctions, genus, and accidents, then to total simplicity and non-mixture with creatures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS6","text":"Apparent instances from Scripture and created predication are resolved by distinguishing the likeness of act or effect from God's ontological constitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0105","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A1","text":"From effects one can know that the proposition «God is» is true, although the divine essence and act of being are not known quidditatively.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A2","text":"God is the first mover altogether unmoved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","text":"God is the first being, altogether actual and in no way potential.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A4","text":"God is the first efficient cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A5","text":"Divine goodness and perfection are through essence, not through participation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A6","text":"No body moves unless it is itself moved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A7","text":"Every body, insofar as it is a divisible continuum, implies potentiality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A8","text":"Matter is the principle of potentiality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A9","text":"The first and per se agent must be form through essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A10","text":"A form unreceptive of matter and subsisting through itself is individuated through itself, so that the supposit and nature do not differ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A11","text":"If a thing's act of being differs from its essence, that thing's act of being is caused by another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A12","text":"Being is the actuality of every form or nature; an essence distinct from being stands to it as potency to act.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A13","text":"What is a species in a genus requires the composition of genus and difference, as well as the distinction of essence from being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A14","text":"An accident presupposes a subject, which is ordered to it as potency to act, and is caused from the principles of the subject or by an extrinsic cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A15","text":"Every composite is posterior to and dependent upon its components.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A16","text":"Principles diverse in themselves do not converge into one except through a unifying cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A17","text":"What enters into the composition of another is not first and per se agent; rather, the composite acts through a part.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A18","text":"Predicates from bodily or created things are transferred to God according to likeness of act, effect, or power, not according to a univocal constitution of the thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0105","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX1","text":"What God is cannot be positively known in the present order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX2","text":"God is not a body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX3","text":"In God there is no composition of matter and form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX4","text":"In God, supposit and nature do not differ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX5","text":"In God, essence and being do not differ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX6","text":"God is neither common being nor a species of the genus of substance, nor is he in any way contained in a genus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0091","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX7","text":"God has neither genus, difference, nor a quidditative definition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX8","text":"There is no accident in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX9","text":"God is in no way composite, nor can he be a part of another's composition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX10","text":"God is not the soul of the world, the intrinsic formal principle of all things, or prime matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX11","text":"Dimensions, figure, members, position, local motion, passions, and soul are not attributed to God according to a bodily or created sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D1","text":"God cannot be a body, because a body is either subject to motion or includes the potentiality of divisibility.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D2","text":"Matter and form cannot be composed in God; God is form through essence, not form received in matter.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D3","text":"God is individuated through himself, and his supposit is his very essence or nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","text":"God is his own being itself, because he neither receives being from another nor is being related to his essence as act to potency.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5","text":"God cannot be in a genus, and therefore admits neither genus, difference, definition, nor demonstration through definition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D6","text":"There can be no accident in God, because there is in him no potential subject or caused principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A14","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7","text":"God is altogether simple, because all forms of composition are removed and every composition would require prior components and a cause of union.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A15","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D2","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D8","text":"God enters into the composition of things neither materially nor formally, but causes them efficiently and exemplarily.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A17","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0139","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D9","text":"Bodily and passional predicates of God signify power, act, effect, or spiritual affection according to likeness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A18","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D2","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D10","text":"The distinction between concrete and abstract names concerning God signifies a diversity in the intellect's apprehension, not a diversity in the divine reality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A18","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D11","text":"Divine being is not common being: addition is excluded from the very notion of the former, whereas it is merely not included in the latter.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD1","text":"What is one and through essence in God is represented defectively in caused things through participation, plurality, and composition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0122","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD2","text":"God is not the first member of a categorical order, but a principle outside every genus with respect to the whole of being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D8","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0107","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD3","text":"The multiplicity of divine names multiplies modes of human conception, not principles constituting God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0105","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD4","text":"The universality of divine causality requires effective and exemplary presence without constitutive mixture with effects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0139","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD5","text":"Knowledge of God is asymmetrical: the truth of the proposition concerning existence is held from effects, but the act of being and essence are not comprehended.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD6","text":"Divine simplicity does not signify a defect of perfection, but perfection contained in one through essence, whereas creatures receive it only as distributed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0122","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:DNX1","text":"The system tacitly excludes a univocal ontological plane in which God and creatures would be members of the same genus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D11","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0105","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:DNX2","text":"Nothing can be prior to God that contains, defines, composes, measures, or unifies him.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A15","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:DNX3","text":"Receptivity, actualization by another, and mutability are structurally excluded, because each of them would posit potency in God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:DNX4","text":"No perfection can accrue to God or be participated by him as an addition to an already constituted essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D6","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:DNX5","text":"A grammatical or conceptual distinction is not sufficient to infer a real distinction in God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D10","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:DNX6","text":"Simplicity does not compel simple things to be identical: God and prime matter are diverse through themselves, not through compositional differences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SNX1","text":"The innermost negation of the system is the absence of every intrinsic otherness: God does not have being, form, life, or goodness as though they were other than himself, but is them through essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D2","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SNX2","text":"Every ontological totality prior to God is precluded, within which God would be a place, species, part, homogeneous measure, or supreme exemplar of the same order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D8","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0084","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0093","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SNX3","text":"The world cannot be conceived as a composition, an unfolding of parts, or the matter of the divine body; the dependence of creatures is constituted causally, not mereologically.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX10","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D8","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0139","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SNX4","text":"The possibility of discourse about God depends upon the impossibility of quidditative comprehension: effects, removals, and likenesses order cognition where positive definition is lacking.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A18","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:FGS1","text":"The epistemic machine proceeds in this order: effects → truth of the proposition «God is» → inaccessibility of quiddity → inquiry through removal.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:FGS2","text":"The metaphysical foundation proceeds in this order: first mover and first being → immobility and pure act → exclusion of body and matter.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:FGS3","text":"The intrinsic series is: no matter → no distinction of supposit and nature → no distinction of essence and being → no genus → no accident.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D2","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:FGS4","text":"The metaphysical closure is: removal of all modes of composition + the posteriority and causality of every composite → total simplicity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A15","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:FGS5","text":"The cosmological closure is: total simplicity + first and per se causality → impossibility of God as part → efficient and exemplary causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A17","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0139","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:FGS6","text":"The semantic circuit is: created predicate → apparent divine composition → reduction to likeness of act or effect → preservation of simplicity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A18","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AF1","text":"Existence known from effects → incomprehensible quiddity → removal of body → removal of intrinsic compositions → absolute simplicity → exclusion of composition with others.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D2","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D6","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AF2","text":"Scriptural or created predication → literal interpretation objected → distinction between the thing and the mode of signifying → transfer to power, act, or effect → dissolution of the compositional consequence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A18","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:ME1","text":"The theological question is transferred from positive description of essence to a discipline of removals.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:ME2","text":"Simplicity is reclassified from a property of minimal things into a metaphysical condition of the first being and supreme perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D7","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0122","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:ME3","text":"God is removed from the table of genera and species and situated as the principle of the whole of being outside the categorical order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D11","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0107","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:ME4","text":"The fundamental opposition becomes that between being through essence and being through participation, not that between two members of the same genus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:ME5","text":"Divine causality is separated from composition: the universal cause does not enter effects as matter or intrinsic form.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D8","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0139","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:ME6","text":"Bodily and affective predication is converted into an index of powers, acts, and effects, by which the multiplicity of discourse is distinguished from composition in the thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D10","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:ALT1","text":"The surface level examines eight modes of composition and removes each from God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX2","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX4","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX5","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX6","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX8","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:ALT2","text":"The structural level is governed by the oppositions of act and potency, essence and being, participation and essence, cause and part.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:ALT3","text":"The metastrategic level orders knowledge and predication of God through effects, removal, and likeness, lest created forms of cognition be transferred to the divine reality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A18","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:ALT4","text":"The ontological and temporal level establishes the simple priority of act: although potency can precede act in the same passing time, act is simply prior, whence the first being neither comes to be nor is actualized by another.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ3:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ3:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to restore the series of eight exclusions, if the antecedent conclusions concerning the first mover, first being, and first cause are admitted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:MRT2","text":"Internal coherence depends most of all on converting divine priorities into pure act, being through essence, and independence from components.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:MRT3","text":"The strongest joint is the concurrence of three ways: potency excludes composition, first causality excludes dependence, and being through essence excludes participation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:MRT4","text":"Less determinate remain the propositions concerning God as the most noble being, the first good, and the principle of the whole of being, because their proofs are partly assumed from prior matters or referred to later ones.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0020","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0041","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0089","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:MRT5","text":"The resolution of created predications depends upon likeness and non-univocity, but the full account of analogy is not yet formally explained in this question.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0105","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:MRT6","text":"The epistemic tension between ignorance of the divine act of being and demonstration of the identity of essence with being is resolved by the distinction between comprehension of the act and the truth of the proposition «God is»; this distinction sustains the structure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0072","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0077","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:MRT7","text":"The expressions by which God is called «form» and «being itself» become fragile unless exemplary form is strictly distinguished from the form of a part, and divine being from common being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0042","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0073","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0075","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0139","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0141","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3:MRT8","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement of the text: cognition through effects → ordered removals → total simplicity → causality without mixture; no formal contradiction remains, but the nexus depends on the indicated semantic and metaphysical distinctions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0135","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ3:P0139","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PPQ3@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_PPQ3:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 04","title":"First Part · Question 3","context":"Chapter 04","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 3"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 3","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 4","order":5,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ4"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4@R001","sha256":"9a68224c52a67a171421039bcfd259f88bfdba68fd4eb204cc31b38e84fa527b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:AS1","text":"Quaestio examinat utrum perfectio Deo competat, utrum omnes perfectiones rerum in eo eminenter contineantur, et utrum creaturae ei similes dici possint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:AS2","text":"Perfectio a conditione facti separatur et ad plenitudinem actualitatis reducitur, ita ut illud perfectum sit cui nihil deest secundum proprium modum perfectionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:AS3","text":"Oppositio inter principium materiale et principium activum constituit transitum: materia est potentia et imperfectio; primum agens est actus et perfectio summa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:AS4","text":"Deus, ut prima causa efficiens et ipsum esse per se subsistens, omnes perfectiones effectuum continet non compositive sed eminenter et uniformiter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:AS5","text":"Similitudo creaturae ad Deum non fundatur in identitate generis, speciei aut formae receptae, sed in analogica participatione essendi a primo principio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:AS6","text":"Motus argumentationis procedit ab apparentia imperfectionis primi principii ad primatum actus, inde ad universalitatem perfectionis, postremo ad similitudinem analogicam et non reciprocabilem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A1","text":"Perfectum est cui nihil deest secundum modum propriae perfectionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A2","text":"Perfectio rei mensuratur secundum actualitatem, non secundum originem per factionem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A3","text":"Materia, inquantum materia, est in potentia et ideo imperfecta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A4","text":"Agens, inquantum agens, est in actu et ideo principium perfectionis effectivae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A5","text":"Ens in potentia non reducitur in actum nisi per aliquod ens in actu.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A6","text":"Ipsum esse est actualitas omnis rei et ipsarum formarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A7","text":"Deus est primum principium non materiale sed in genere causae efficientis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A8","text":"Quidquid perfectionis est in effectu praeexistit in causa effectiva vel eadem ratione vel eminentiori modo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A9","text":"Deus est ipsum esse per se subsistens et ideo totam perfectionem essendi in se continet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A10","text":"Omnis perfectio rerum pertinet ad perfectionem essendi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A11","text":"Diversa et opposita in effectibus possunt in causa universali praeexistere unite et uniformiter sine compositione causae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A12","text":"Similitudo consequitur communicationem formae secundum modum convenientiae qui potest esse univocus, inaequalis vel analogicus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A13","text":"Deus est ens per essentiam; creaturae sunt entia per participationem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A14","text":"Similitudo inter causam et causatum non est reciproca sicut similitudo inter ea quae sunt eiusdem ordinis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:NX1","text":"Perfectio divina non significat Deum esse factum, quamvis nomen “perfecti” a rebus factis translatum sit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:NX2","text":"Deus non est principium materiale neque primum principium imperfectum in potentia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:NX3","text":"Ipsum esse non est receptaculum indeterminatum perfectionum, sed actus quo omnia actualitatem habent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:NX4","text":"Simplicitas divina non excludit multitudinem perfectionum, quia illae non insunt Deo sicut partes distinctae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:NX5","text":"Oppositiones specificae effectuum non ponuntur in Deo ut oppositiones reales simul coexistentes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:NX6","text":"Deus non est ens tantum, quasi vita et sapientia ei extrinsece superadderentur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:NX7","text":"Creatura Deo non assimilatur per identitatem generis, speciei aut essentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:NX8","text":"Deus creaturae similis dici non potest, etiamsi creatura aliquo modo Deo similis dicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:D1","text":"Deus est maxime perfectus, quia, ut primum agens, est maxime in actu.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:D2","text":"Principium materiale imperfectum non potest esse simpliciter primum, quia suam actualizationem ab ente in actu requirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:D3","text":"Deo nulla perfectio rei deest, quia perfectiones effectuum in prima causa effectiva eminenter praeexistunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:D4","text":"Vita, sapientia et ceterae perfectiones in Deo non sunt additiones ad esse, sed intra plenitudinem ipsius esse subsistentis continentur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:D5","text":"Multiplicitas perfectionum creatarum in Deo unitatem simplicem non destruit, quia ibi praeexistit secundum modum causae universalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:D6","text":"Creatura Deo similis est inquantum participat esse, sed dissimilis manet quia esse divinum non participat secundum eandem rationem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:D7","text":"Similitudo creaturae ad Deum est causaliter ordinata et asymmetrica, non comparatio inter duo entia coordinata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:SD1","text":"Perfectiones creaturarum non constituunt gradus autonomos ultra esse, sed modos limitatos quibus una perfectio essendi participatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:SD2","text":"Relatio causalis inter Deum et creaturas simul fundat similitudinem et dissimilitudinem: causalitas producit participationem, transcendens causalitas impedit aequiparantiam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:SD3","text":"Simplicitas divina exigitur ut principium unitivum diversitatis perfectionum, ne causae primae attribuatur compositio ex formis effectuum acceptis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:SD4","text":"Ordo perfectionum creatarum est ordo participationis et limitationis, non ordo quo creaturae aliquod exemplum extra divinum esse perficiunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet quod primum principium sit indeterminata potentia, quia talis potentia actualitatem ab anteriori principio postularet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet quod perfectio divina sit aggregatum perfectionum creatarum, quia aggregatio compositionem et dependentiam a differentiis effectuum induceret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet quod esse sit minimum commune et ideo pauperrimum, quia omnis actualitas formarum ab esse dependet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet quod similitudo omnem comparationem ad mutualitatem obliget, quia causa prima non intra ordinem effectuum collocatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:SNX1","text":"Deus structuraliter indisponibilis est ut obiectum generis, speciei vel coordinationis comparativae, quia omnis talis collocatio ipsum sub principio communi poneret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:SNX2","text":"Perfectiones divinae non sunt multipliciter possessae sicut proprietates subiecti, quia ipsum esse subsistens omnem perfectionem uniformiter praeaccipit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:SNX3","text":"Creatura non potest Deum perfecte imitari, quia participatio causati necessario deficit a modo essendi causae primae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:FGS1","text":"Potentia materialis → imperfectio principii materialis → necessitas prioris actualitatis → exclusio Dei a ratione materiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:FGS2","text":"Actualitas agentis → primum agens maxime in actu → perfectio divina secundum plenitudinem actus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:FGS3","text":"Ipsum esse subsistens + causalitas effectiva universalis → praeexistentia eminentior perfectionum → universalitas perfectionis divinae sine compositione.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:FGS4","text":"Participatio essendi creati + transcendentia causae extra genus → similitudo analogica → dissimilitudo essentialis → non-reciprocitas relationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:AF1","text":"Apparens imperfectio Dei ex origine vocabuli, primatu principii et communitate essendi → distinctio inter potentiam materialem et actualitatem agentem → affirmatio perfectionis divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0017","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0019","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0021","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:AF2","text":"Obiectio ex simplicitate, oppositione et gradu perfectionum → causalitas eminens et esse subsistens → affirmatio perfectionum omnium in unitate divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0024","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0026","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0028","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0032","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0040","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:AF3","text":"Obiectio ex incomparabilitate Dei et creaturae → distinctio modorum similitudinis → analogia participationis → similitudo creaturae sine similitudine reciproca Dei.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0043","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0045","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0047","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0049","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0053","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0057","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0059","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0063","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:ME1","text":"Perfectio reclassificatur ab eventu productionis ad mensuram actualitatis, ita ut increatum non excludatur a perfectione.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:ME2","text":"Esse reclassificatur ab indeterminato communissimo ad actum fontalem omnium formarum et perfectionum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:ME3","text":"Diversitas perfectionum creatarum reclassificatur ut effectus multiplicatus unius causalitatis simplicis, non ut multiplicitas intrinseca in Deo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:ME4","text":"Similitudo reclassificatur ab aequalitate inter comparabilia ad relationem analogicam participationis inter causatum et causam transcendentem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:ME5","text":"Dissimilitudo non destruit similitudinem, sed definit modum finitum quo creatura causam suam imitatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus affirmat Deum perfectum, universaliter perfectum, et creaturas ei analogice similes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:ALT2","text":"Structural — Actualitas, causalitas efficiens, participatio et esse subsistens constituunt mechanismum quo perfectiones creaturarum ad primam causam referuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Obiectiones solvuntur per translationem quaestionis ab imaginibus materialibus, genericis et reciprocis ad ordinem causalitatis et analogiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Realitas ordinatur secundum dependentiam potentiae ab actu et secundum processionem participatam effectuum a primo esse subsistente.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:MRT1","text":"Systema restitui potest ex nexus actualitatis, causalitatis efficientis, esse subsistentis, eminentiae causalis et analogiae participativae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:MRT2","text":"Firmissimum centrum argumentationis est conversio perfectionis in actum et conversio omnis actualitatis in dependentiam ab esse.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:MRT3","text":"Transitus a perfectionibus effectuum ad earum praeexistentiam eminentem in causa supponit principium causalitatis quod hic adhibetur sed non ulterius demonstratur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:MRT4","text":"Compositio inter multiplicitatem perfectionum creatarum et unitatem simplicem divinam formaliter per “praeexistentiam uniformem” solvitur, sed modus positivus huius unionis non determinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:MRT5","text":"Analogia similitudinis sufficienter prohibet identitatem et reciprocitatem, sed eius determinatio pendet ab antecedenti distinctione inter esse per essentiam et esse per participationem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:MRT6","text":"Restitutio servat motum textus, quia ab exclusione potentiae materialis procedit ad plenitudinem actus, ab hac ad causalitatem universalem, et ab ea ad similitudinem creatam limitatam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T155512158595Z_1f5f185d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 4","source_sha256":"9a68224c52a67a171421039bcfd259f88bfdba68fd4eb204cc31b38e84fa527b","sha256":"6bec5b6bcb534d17b7e48ea2d037e13568c28517ee2bf9a4d1a6ea9de81598dd","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:55:12.159549+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:AS1","text":"The question examines whether perfection belongs to God, whether all the perfections of things are contained in Him eminently, and whether creatures can be called similar to Him.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:AS2","text":"Perfection is separated from the condition of having been made and reduced to the fullness of actuality, such that that is perfect which lacks nothing according to its proper mode of perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:AS3","text":"The opposition between material principle and active principle establishes the transition: matter is potency and imperfection; the first agent is act and supreme perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:AS4","text":"God, as first efficient cause and subsisting being itself, contains all the perfections of effects not compositionally but eminently and uniformly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:AS5","text":"The creature's likeness to God is founded not on identity of genus, species, or received form, but on analogical participation in being from the first principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:AS6","text":"The movement of the argument proceeds from the appearance of imperfection in the first principle to the primacy of act, thence to the universality of perfection, and finally to analogical and non-reciprocal likeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A1","text":"Perfect is that which lacks nothing according to the mode of its proper perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A2","text":"The perfection of a thing is measured according to actuality, not according to its origin through being made.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A3","text":"Matter, insofar as it is matter, is in potency and therefore imperfect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A4","text":"An agent, insofar as it is an agent, is in act and therefore a principle of effective perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A5","text":"A being in potency is not reduced to act except through some being in act.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A6","text":"Being itself is the actuality of every thing and of the forms themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A7","text":"God is the first principle not materially but in the genus of efficient cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A8","text":"Whatever perfection is in an effect pre-exists in the efficient cause either in the same respect or in a more eminent mode.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A9","text":"God is subsisting being itself and therefore contains in Himself the whole perfection of being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A10","text":"Every perfection of things belongs to the perfection of being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A11","text":"Diverse and opposed things in effects can pre-exist in a universal cause unitedly and uniformly, without composition in the cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A12","text":"Likeness follows from the communication of form according to a mode of agreement that can be univocal, unequal, or analogical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A13","text":"God is being through essence; creatures are beings through participation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A14","text":"Likeness between cause and caused is not reciprocal like the likeness between things that are of the same order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:NX1","text":"Divine perfection does not signify that God has been made, although the name “perfect” has been transferred from things made.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:NX2","text":"God is neither a material principle nor an imperfect first principle in potency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:NX3","text":"Being itself is not an indeterminate receptacle of perfections, but the act by which all things have actuality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:NX4","text":"Divine simplicity does not exclude a multitude of perfections, because they are not in God as distinct parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:NX5","text":"The specific oppositions of effects are not posited in God as real oppositions coexisting simultaneously.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:NX6","text":"God is not merely being, as though life and wisdom were added to Him extrinsically.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:NX7","text":"The creature is not assimilated to God through identity of genus, species, or essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:NX8","text":"God cannot be called similar to the creature, even if the creature is in some manner called similar to God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:D1","text":"God is most perfect because, as the first agent, He is most fully in act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:D2","text":"An imperfect material principle cannot be simply first, because it requires its actualization from a being in act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:D3","text":"No perfection of a thing is lacking to God, because the perfections of effects pre-exist eminently in the first efficient cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:D4","text":"Life, wisdom, and the other perfections are not additions to being in God, but are contained within the fullness of subsisting being itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:D5","text":"The multiplicity of created perfections does not destroy the simple unity in God, because there it pre-exists according to the mode of a universal cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:D6","text":"The creature is similar to God insofar as it participates in being, but remains dissimilar because it does not participate in divine being according to the same respect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:D7","text":"The creature's likeness to God is causally ordered and asymmetrical, not a comparison between two coordinated beings.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:SD1","text":"The perfections of creatures do not constitute autonomous grades beyond being, but limited modes by which the one perfection of being is participated.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:SD2","text":"The causal relation between God and creatures simultaneously founds likeness and dissimilarity: causality produces participation; transcendent causality prevents equivalence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:SD3","text":"Divine simplicity is required as the unifying principle of the diversity of perfections, lest composition from received forms of effects be attributed to the first cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:SD4","text":"The order of created perfections is an order of participation and limitation, not an order by which creatures perfect some exemplar outside divine being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:DNX1","text":"It must be false that the first principle is indeterminate potency, because such potency would require actuality from a prior principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:DNX2","text":"It must be false that divine perfection is an aggregate of created perfections, because aggregation would entail composition and dependence upon the differences of effects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:DNX3","text":"It must be false that being is the least common and therefore the poorest, because all actuality of forms depends upon being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:DNX4","text":"It must be false that likeness obliges every comparison to mutuality, because the first cause is not situated within the order of effects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:SNX1","text":"God is structurally unavailable as an object of genus, species, or comparative coordination, because every such placement would place Him under a common principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:SNX2","text":"Divine perfections are not possessed multiply as properties of a subject, because subsisting being itself uniformly precontains every perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:SNX3","text":"The creature cannot perfectly imitate God, because the participation of the caused necessarily falls short of the mode of being of the first cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:FGS1","text":"Material potency → imperfection of the material principle → necessity of prior actuality → exclusion of God from the account of matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:FGS2","text":"Actuality of the agent → the first agent most fully in act → divine perfection according to the fullness of act.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:FGS3","text":"Subsisting being itself + universal efficient causality → more eminent pre-existence of perfections → universality of divine perfection without composition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:FGS4","text":"Participation in created being + transcendence of the cause beyond genus → analogical likeness → essential dissimilarity → non-reciprocity of relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:AF1","text":"Apparent imperfection of God from the origin of the term, the primacy of principle, and the commonness of being → distinction between material potency and the actuality of the agent → affirmation of divine perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0017","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0019","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0021","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:AF2","text":"Objection from simplicity, opposition, and the grades of perfections → eminent causality and subsisting being → affirmation of all perfections in divine unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0024","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0026","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0028","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0032","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0040","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:AF3","text":"Objection from the incomparability of God and creature → distinction of modes of likeness → analogy of participation → likeness of the creature without reciprocal likeness of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0043","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0045","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0047","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0049","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0053","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0057","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0059","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0063","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:ME1","text":"Perfection is reclassified from the event of production to the measure of actuality, such that the uncreated is not excluded from perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:ME2","text":"Being is reclassified from the most common indeterminate to the fontal act of all forms and perfections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:ME3","text":"The diversity of created perfections is reclassified as the multiplied effect of one simple causality, not as intrinsic multiplicity in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:ME4","text":"Likeness is reclassified from equality between comparable things to the analogical relation of participation between the caused and the transcendent cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:ME5","text":"Dissimilarity does not destroy likeness, but defines the finite mode by which the creature imitates its cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text affirms God to be perfect, universally perfect, and creatures to be analogically similar to Him.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:ALT2","text":"Structural — Actuality, efficient causality, participation, and subsisting being constitute the mechanism by which the perfections of creatures are referred to the first cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Objections are resolved by translating the question from material, generic, and reciprocal images into the order of causality and analogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Reality is ordered according to the dependence of potency upon act and according to the participated procession of effects from the first subsisting being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:MRT1","text":"The system can be reconstructed from the nexus of actuality, efficient causality, subsisting being, causal eminence, and participatory analogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:MRT2","text":"The firmest center of the argument is the conversion of perfection into act and the conversion of all actuality into dependence upon being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ4:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:MRT3","text":"The transition from the perfections of effects to their eminent pre-existence in the cause presupposes a principle of causality that is employed here but not further demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:MRT4","text":"The composition between the multiplicity of created perfections and simple divine unity is formally resolved through “uniform pre-existence,” but the positive mode of this union is not determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:MRT5","text":"The analogy of likeness sufficiently prohibits identity and reciprocity, but its determination depends upon the antecedent distinction between being through essence and being through participation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4:MRT6","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text, because it proceeds from the exclusion of material potency to the fullness of act, from this to universal causality, and from that to limited created likeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PPQ4@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_PPQ4:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 05","title":"First Part · Question 4","context":"Chapter 05","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 4"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 4","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 5","order":6,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ5"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5@R001","sha256":"13dd5fb97140f35eb7ff709331b1cee4d467b6378b47c0ac843ed42aa9d3f439","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AS1","text":"Quaestio determinat quomodo bonum ad ens referatur secundum rem, rationem, causalitatem, constitutionem et divisionem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AS2","text":"Thesis principalis bonum et ens secundum rem identificat, sed bonum ratione appetibilitatis ab ente distinguit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AS3","text":"Argumentatio metaphysica et reconstructiva est, dialectice per obiectiones, distinctiones ordinum et restitutiones conclusionum procedens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AS4","text":"Motus principalis ab identitate boni et entis procedit ad prioritatem entis in intelligendo, universalitatem boni, causalitatem finalem, structuram perfectionis et divisionem analogicam boni.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AS5","text":"Contradictiones apparentes solvuntur distinguendo rem a ratione, simpliciter a secundum quid, praedicationem a causalitate, ordinem causandi ab ordine causati et res oppositas a rationibus oppositis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0124","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","text":"Bonum et ens sunt idem secundum rem, sed differunt secundum rationem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","text":"Ratio boni consistit in appetibilitate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A3","text":"Unumquodque est appetibile secundum quod est perfectum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","text":"Unumquodque est perfectum secundum quod est actu, et esse est actualitas omnis rei.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A5","text":"Ens primo cadit in conceptione intellectus, quia unumquodque est cognoscibile secundum quod est actu.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A6","text":"Bonum, quia appetibile est, rationem finis importat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A7","text":"In causando finis movet efficientem, et efficiens materiam ad formam movet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A8","text":"In causato forma praecedit virtutem effectivam, quam ratio boni consequitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A9","text":"Primum esse substantiale constituit ens simpliciter, sed ultima perfectio debita constituit bonum simpliciter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A10","text":"Extensio secundum praedicationem distinguitur ab extensione secundum causalitatem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11","text":"Malum est privatio cuiusdam esse debiti, non ens inquantum ens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A12","text":"Forma constituit rem in specie, praesupponit commensurationem principiorum et infert inclinationem ad finem vel actionem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A13","text":"Modus significat commensurationem formae praeviam, species ipsam formam, et ordo inclinationem ex forma consequentem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A14","text":"Motus appetitus terminari potest medio ad aliud, re ultima per se desiderata aut quiete in re desiderata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A15","text":"Divisio propria boni fit secundum rationes appetibilitatis oppositas, non secundum res necessario oppositas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0124","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A16","text":"Bonum de honesto, delectabili et utili analogice secundum prius et posterius praedicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX1","text":"Bonum ab ente secundum rem non separatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX2","text":"Bonum ens non contrahit per additionem novae quidditatis vel naturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX3","text":"Prioritas boni in causalitate non constituit prioritatem boni in conceptione intellectus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX4","text":"Non esse secundum se appetibile non est, sed tantum per accidens quatenus mali ablationem importat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX5","text":"Nullum ens malum est inquantum ens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX6","text":"Materia prima neque ens neque bonum actu est, sed propter potentiam et aptitudinem ad bonum ab ordine boni non excluditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX7","text":"Mathematica rationem boni non excludunt tamquam entia subsistentia, sed ab ea abstrahunt secundum rationem, quia a motu et fine abstrahuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX8","text":"Bonum proprie neque rationem causae formalis neque rationem causae efficientis importat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX9","text":"Pulchrum et bonum, quamvis in eadem re et forma fundentur, eandem rationem non habent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX10","text":"Modus, species et ordo non sunt bona subsistentia quae aliis modis, speciebus et ordinibus indigeant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0102","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX11","text":"Malus modus, mala species vel malus ordo non sunt mala inquantum talia, sed propter defectum debiti vel incongruitatem ad subiectum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX12","text":"Honestum, delectabile et utile bonum neque per genera praedicamentalia neque ut membra univoca et res mutuo exclusae dividunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0122","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0124","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D1","text":"Omne ens, inquantum est ens, bonum est, quia omnis actualitas aliquam perfectionem et appetibilitatem constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D2","text":"Bonum enti solam rationem perfectionis appetibilis addit, non determinationem naturae contrahentem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D3","text":"Ens est prius bono secundum rationem, quia intellectus actualitatem prius ut ens quam ut appetibile concipit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D4","text":"Bonum est prius ente in causando, quatenus finis efficientem movet antequam forma in effectu constituatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D5","text":"Nihil per se appetitur nisi secundum aliquod esse actuale; non esse appetitur tantum quatenus removet privationem alterius esse desiderati.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D6","text":"Secundum primum actum res est ens simpliciter et bonum secundum quid; secundum ultimum actum est ens secundum quid et bonum simpliciter.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D7","text":"Gradus bonitatis sequuntur gradus actuum supervenientium et perfectionum, non gradus ipsius esse substantialis ut talis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D8","text":"Malum omne aliquod bonum praesupponit et totum bonum auferre non potest nisi simul subiectum privationis destruat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D9","text":"Materia prima bonum participat secundum potentiam, ordinem et aptitudinem, quamvis appetibilis actu non sit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D10","text":"Bonum potentiam nondum actu existentem causaliter attingit ut finem, quamvis de ea ut bono actu non praedicetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D11","text":"Perfectio integra requirit principiorum commensurationem, formam determinantem et inclinationem congruam ad finem; ideo ratio boni in modo, specie et ordine consistit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D12","text":"Utile est appetibile ut medium, honestum ut res ultima per se desiderata, et delectabile ut quies appetitus in re desiderata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A14","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D13","text":"Honestum rationem boni primo possidet, delectabile secundario, utile tertio, quia medium et quies ab ultimo appetibili dependent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A14","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D14","text":"Pulchrum ad cognitionem et formam refertur, bonum autem ad appetitum et finem, quamvis utrumque in eadem forma fundetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD1","text":"Ontologia et ratio boni realiter coextensivae sunt, sed conceptibiliter irreducibiles manent: ens dicit actualitatem, bonum eandem actualitatem ut perfectam et appetibilem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD2","text":"Actualitas est cardo communis ex quo entitas, intelligibilitas, perfectio et appetibilitas diverso ordine generantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD3","text":"Prioritas non est unica: ens est prius ordine intelligendi, bonum autem prius ordine causandi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD4","text":"Malum statum ontologicum parasiticum habet, quia tantum in bono subsistente ut defectus actus debiti consistere potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD5","text":"Teleologia enti extrinsecus non imponitur, quia forma actualis inclinationem ad operationem vel finem ex propria constitutione infert.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD6","text":"Potentia et abstractio rationis universalitatem boni non infirmant: potentia a bono finaliter ordinatur, abstractio autem rationem finis omittere potest sine productione entis actualis neutri.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD7","text":"Bonum intrinsecum, quies in bono et medium ad bonum constituunt ordinem hierarchicum, non tres species aequaliter primas.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD8","text":"Bonum est simul consequens actualitatem in effectu et principium actualizationis ut finis, ita ut reciproca prioritas secundum ordines distinctos oriatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:DNX1","text":"Nullum ens actuale axiologice neutrum esse potest, quia actualitas ipsa perfectionem saltem secundum quid inducit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:DNX2","text":"Malum non potest constitui ut substantia positiva vel principium enti contrarium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D8","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:DNX3","text":"Purum nihilum neque finis neque obiectum appetitus per se esse potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:DNX4","text":"Ordo intelligendi, ordo causandi et ordo perfectionis in unam seriem prioritatis reduci non possunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:DNX5","text":"Bonitas non potest esse valor extrinsecus et arbitrarie enti adiunctus, quia ex actu, forma et perfectione rei oritur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:DNX6","text":"Ratio boni nullo genere praedicamentali exhauritur, quia ens universaliter comitatur nec naturam aliquam contrahentem addit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:DNX7","text":"Utilitas et delectatio non possunt constituere primum principium bonitatis, quia a bono per se desiderato dependent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SNX1","text":"Systema nullum actuale extra ambitum boni relinquit; exceptiones apparentes ad potentiam, privationem, non esse per accidens aut abstractionem rationis reducuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SNX2","text":"Malum absolutum praecluditur, quia totalis ablatio boni simul tolleret ens in quo malum inesse posset.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D8","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SNX3","text":"Iudicium quo ipsum esse inquantum esse malum pronuntiaretur intra systema inadmissibile est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SNX4","text":"Prioritas absoluta boni vel entis indisponibilis est; unumquodque tantum sub ordine determinato primum esse potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD8","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SNX5","text":"Bonum ut genus univocum vel regio entium separata cogitari non potest, quia cum ente coextenditur et secundum rationes analogicas distinguitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS1","text":"Nucleus generativus hac serie exprimitur: actualitas → perfectio → appetibilitas → bonum; actualitas → ens; ergo identitas secundum rem et distinctio secundum rationem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS2","text":"Bifurcatio prioritatis hac serie producitur: actualitas → intelligibilitas → ens primum ratione; appetibilitas → finis → bonum primum in causando.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS3","text":"Structura mali hac serie producitur: esse actuale → bonum; defectus actus debiti → privatio; privatio in subiecto bono → malum limitatum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS4","text":"Structura perfectionis hac serie constituitur: commensuratio principiorum → forma → inclinatio ad finem; modus → species → ordo; integritas trium → bonum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS5","text":"Divisio boni hac serie generatur: medium ad finem → utile; terminus per se desideratus → honestum; quies in termino → delectabile; dependentia a termino → praedicatio analogica.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A14","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS6","text":"Modus dialecticus recurrens hac serie operatur: contradictio apparens → distinctio ordinum vel rationum → restrictio praedicationis → conservatio thesis universalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AS5","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX6","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AF1","text":"Motus principalis est: relatio boni ad ens → identitas realis et distinctio rationalis → prioritas entis in intellectu → bonitas omnis entis → causalitas finalis boni → constitutio per modum, speciem et ordinem → divisio analogica in honestum, delectabile et utile.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AS1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AS2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AF2","text":"Motus probationum est: actualitas → perfectio → appetibilitas → bonitas → universalitas, cuius limites apparentes per potentiam, privationem, abstractionem et distinctionem ordinum resolvuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AF3","text":"Motus finalis est: bonum ut finis → appetitus ut motus → triplex terminatio motus → triplex ratio boni → subordinatio analogica utilitatis et delectationis bono per se desiderato.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A14","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ME1","text":"Oppositio inter ontologiam et bonitatem in distinctionem rationum intra eandem rem convertitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ME2","text":"Malum ex genere entis positivi in defectum localem actus debiti reclassificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ME3","text":"Prioritas ex relatione simplici in ordines distinctos intelligendi, causandi et perficiendi distribuitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ME4","text":"Bonum ex qualitate superaddita in dimensionem finalem ipsius actualitatis et formae transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ME5","text":"Modus, species et ordo non ut ornamenta bonitatis, sed ut strata structuralia perfectionis apparent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ME6","text":"Honestum, delectabile et utile ex rebus separatis in positiones functionales intra motum appetitus convertuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A14","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ME7","text":"Potentia, non esse et abstractio mathematica ex exceptionibus ad universalitatem boni in casus limitaneos distinctorum modorum attributionis reducuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D10","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ALT1","text":"Surface — Disputatio explicita identitatem boni et entis, prioritatem eorum, universalitatem boni, causam finalem, modum-speciem-ordinem et triplicem divisionem determinat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ALT2","text":"Structural — Actualitas per perfectionem appetibilitatem generat, forma per commensurationem et inclinationem structuratur, et privatio ex defectu actus debiti oritur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Obiectiones neutralizantur non negando apparentias, sed distribuendo eas inter ordines rei, rationis, causalitatis, praedicationis et analogiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AS5","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Realitas secundum actum et potentiam, formam et privationem, finem moventem et effectum formatum ordinatur, ita ut idem bonum ante effectum causaliter et post formam perfective compareat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:MRT1","text":"Axiomata extracta sufficiunt ad sex conclusiones principales et earum connexiones restituendas.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A14","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A15","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:MRT2","text":"Pars firmissima est nexus actualitatis, perfectionis et appetibilitatis, ex quo identitas realis boni cum ente et bonitas omnis entis derivantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:MRT3","text":"Cohaerentia interna servatur per distinctionem ordinum; si ordo intelligendi cum ordine causandi confunditur, reciproca prioritas boni et entis contradictionem apparentem parit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:MRT4","text":"Norma qua perfectio dicitur debita et qua ultimus actus determinatur in hac quaestione non plene explicatur, sed ex naturis rerum praesupponitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:MRT5","text":"Doctrina mali universalitatem boni tuetur, sed tota pendet ab asymmetria inter esse positivum et privationem esse debiti.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D8","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:MRT6","text":"Transitus a motu corporis naturalis ad motum appetitus et a forma ad inclinationem structuraliter necessarius est, sed analogice et compresse probatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A14","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:MRT7","text":"Divisio triplex res mutuo exclusas non constituit; vis eius pendet a distinctione rationum appetibilitatis et a praedicatione analogica.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A15","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0124","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:MRT8","text":"Restitutio motum verum servat: ab actualitate communi ad universalitatem boni, inde ad causalitatem et constitutionem internam, demum ad ordinationem formarum appetibilitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AF1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T155542070274Z_1edb542c","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 5","source_sha256":"13dd5fb97140f35eb7ff709331b1cee4d467b6378b47c0ac843ed42aa9d3f439","sha256":"6096a1ee6d90d39924e28c8d2d583e07d117455d31dbc68ac58ae433bfa264ef","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:55:42.071224+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AS1","text":"The question determines how the good is related to being according to reality, notion, causality, constitution, and division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AS2","text":"The principal thesis identifies the good and being according to reality, but distinguishes the good from being by the notion of desirability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AS3","text":"The argumentation is metaphysical and reconstructive, proceeding dialectically through objections, distinctions of orders, and restorations of conclusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AS4","text":"The principal movement proceeds from the identity of good and being to the priority of being in understanding, the universality of the good, final causality, the structure of perfection, and the analogical division of the good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AS5","text":"Apparent contradictions are resolved by distinguishing reality from notion, simply from in a certain respect, predication from causality, the order of causing from the order of what is caused, and opposed things from opposed notions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0124","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","text":"The good and being are the same according to reality, but differ according to notion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","text":"The notion of the good consists in desirability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A3","text":"Everything is desirable insofar as it is perfect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","text":"Everything is perfect insofar as it is in act, and being is the actuality of every thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A5","text":"Being first falls within the conception of the intellect, because everything is knowable insofar as it is in act.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A6","text":"The good, because it is desirable, imports the notion of an end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A7","text":"In causing, the end moves the efficient cause, and the efficient cause moves matter toward form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A8","text":"In the caused thing, form precedes effective power, which the notion of the good follows.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A9","text":"The first substantial being constitutes being simply, but the final due perfection constitutes good simply.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A10","text":"Extension according to predication is distinguished from extension according to causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11","text":"Evil is the privation of some due being, not being insofar as it is being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A12","text":"Form constitutes a thing in species, presupposes the commensuration of principles, and entails an inclination toward an end or action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A13","text":"Mode signifies the prior commensuration of form, species the form itself, and order the inclination consequent upon form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A14","text":"The movement of appetite can terminate in a means to another thing, in an ultimate thing desired for its own sake, or in repose in the desired thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A15","text":"The proper division of the good is made according to opposed notions of desirability, not according to necessarily opposed things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0124","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A16","text":"The good is predicated analogically according to prior and posterior of the honorable, the pleasurable, and the useful.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX1","text":"The good is not separated from being according to reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX2","text":"The good does not contract being by the addition of a new quiddity or nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX3","text":"The priority of the good in causality does not constitute the priority of the good in the conception of the intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX4","text":"Non-being is not desirable in itself, but only accidentally insofar as it entails the removal of evil.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX5","text":"No being is evil insofar as it is being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX6","text":"Prime matter is neither being nor good in act, but is not excluded from the order of the good on account of its potency and aptitude for the good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX7","text":"Mathematicals do not exclude the notion of the good as subsisting beings, but abstract from it according to notion, because they abstract from motion and end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX8","text":"Properly speaking, the good imports neither the notion of formal cause nor the notion of efficient cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX9","text":"The beautiful and the good, although founded in the same thing and form, do not have the same notion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX10","text":"Mode, species, and order are not subsisting goods requiring other modes, species, and orders.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0102","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX11","text":"A bad mode, bad species, or bad order are not evil insofar as such, but because of a defect of what is due or an incongruity with the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX12","text":"The honorable, pleasurable, and useful good do not divide either through categorial genera or as univocal members and mutually exclusive things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0122","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0124","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D1","text":"Every being, insofar as it is being, is good, because every actuality constitutes some perfection and desirability.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D2","text":"The good adds to being only the notion of desirable perfection, not a determination contracting nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D3","text":"Being is prior to the good according to notion, because the intellect conceives actuality first as being rather than as desirable.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D4","text":"The good is prior to being in causing, insofar as the end moves the efficient cause before form is constituted in the effect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D5","text":"Nothing is desired through itself except according to some actual being; non-being is desired only insofar as it removes the privation of another desired being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D6","text":"According to the first act, a thing is being simply and good in a certain respect; according to the last act, it is being in a certain respect and good simply.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D7","text":"Degrees of goodness follow the degrees of supervening acts and perfections, not the degrees of substantial being itself as such.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D8","text":"Every evil presupposes some good and cannot remove the whole good unless it simultaneously destroys the subject of the privation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D9","text":"Prime matter participates in the good according to potency, order, and aptitude, although it is not actually desirable.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D10","text":"The good causally reaches potency not yet existing in act as an end, although it is not predicated of it as an actual good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D11","text":"Integral perfection requires the commensuration of principles, a determining form, and an inclination fitting to the end; therefore the notion of the good consists in mode, species, and order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D12","text":"The useful is desirable as a means, the honorable as an ultimate thing desired for its own sake, and the pleasurable as the repose of appetite in the desired thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A14","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D13","text":"The honorable possesses the notion of good first, the pleasurable secondarily, and the useful thirdly, because the means and repose depend upon the ultimate desirable.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A14","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D14","text":"The beautiful is referred to cognition and form, whereas the good is referred to appetite and end, although both are founded in the same form.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD1","text":"Ontology and the notion of the good are really coextensive, yet remain conceptually irreducible: being signifies actuality, the good that same actuality as perfect and desirable.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD2","text":"Actuality is the common hinge from which entity, intelligibility, perfection, and desirability are generated in diverse orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD3","text":"Priority is not single: being is prior in the order of understanding, but the good is prior in the order of causing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD4","text":"Evil has a parasitic ontological status, because it can subsist only in a subsisting good as a defect of due act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD5","text":"Teleology is not imposed extrinsically upon being, because actual form entails an inclination toward operation or end from its own constitution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD6","text":"Potency and abstraction of notion do not weaken the universality of the good: potency is ordered to the good finally, whereas abstraction can omit the notion of end without producing an actually neutral being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD7","text":"Intrinsic good, repose in good, and means to good constitute a hierarchical order, not three equally primary species.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD8","text":"The good is at once consequent upon actuality in the effect and the principle of actualization as an end, such that reciprocal priority arises according to distinct orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:DNX1","text":"No actual being can be axiologically neutral, because actuality itself induces perfection at least in a certain respect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:DNX2","text":"Evil cannot be constituted as a positive substance or a principle contrary to being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D8","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:DNX3","text":"Pure nothingness can be neither an end nor an object of appetite through itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:DNX4","text":"The order of understanding, the order of causing, and the order of perfection cannot be reduced to one series of priority.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:DNX5","text":"Goodness cannot be a value attached extrinsically and arbitrarily to being, because it arises from the act, form, and perfection of the thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:DNX6","text":"The notion of the good is not exhausted by any categorial genus, because it accompanies being universally and adds no nature contracting it.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:DNX7","text":"Usefulness and pleasure cannot constitute the first principle of goodness, because they depend on the good desired through itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SNX1","text":"The system leaves no actual being outside the scope of the good; apparent exceptions are reduced to potency, privation, non-being accidentally, or abstraction of notion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SNX2","text":"Absolute evil is precluded, because the total removal of the good would simultaneously remove the being in which evil could inhere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D8","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SNX3","text":"A judgment by which being itself insofar as being would be declared evil is inadmissible within the system.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SNX4","text":"Absolute priority of either the good or being is unavailable; each can be first only under a determinate order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD8","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SNX5","text":"The good cannot be conceived as a univocal genus or a separate region of beings, because it is coextensive with being and is distinguished according to analogical notions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS1","text":"The generative nucleus is expressed by this series: actuality → perfection → desirability → good; actuality → being; therefore identity according to reality and distinction according to notion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS2","text":"The bifurcation of priority is produced by this series: actuality → intelligibility → being first by notion; desirability → end → good first in causing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS3","text":"The structure of evil is produced by this series: actual being → good; defect of due act → privation; privation in a good subject → limited evil.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS4","text":"The structure of perfection is constituted by this series: commensuration of principles → form → inclination toward end; mode → species → order; integrity of the three → good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS5","text":"The division of the good is generated by this series: means to end → useful; terminus desired through itself → honorable; repose in the terminus → pleasurable; dependence on the terminus → analogical predication.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A14","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS6","text":"The recurrent dialectical mode operates by this series: apparent contradiction → distinction of orders or notions → restriction of predication → preservation of the universal thesis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AS5","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX6","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AF1","text":"The principal movement is: relation of the good to being → real identity and rational distinction → priority of being in the intellect → goodness of every being → final causality of the good → constitution through mode, species, and order → analogical division into honorable, pleasurable, and useful.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AS1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AS2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AF2","text":"The movement of proofs is: actuality → perfection → desirability → goodness → universality, whose apparent limits are resolved through potency, privation, abstraction, and distinction of orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AF3","text":"The final movement is: good as end → appetite as motion → threefold termination of motion → threefold notion of good → analogical subordination of usefulness and pleasure to the good desired through itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A14","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ME1","text":"The opposition between ontology and goodness is converted into a distinction of notions within the same thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ME2","text":"Evil is reclassified from a genus of positive being into a local defect of due act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ME3","text":"Priority is distributed from a simple relation into distinct orders of understanding, causing, and perfecting.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ME4","text":"The good is transferred from a superadded quality into the final dimension of actuality and form themselves.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ME5","text":"Mode, species, and order appear not as ornaments of goodness, but as structural strata of perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ME6","text":"The honorable, pleasurable, and useful are converted from separate things into functional positions within the movement of appetite.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A14","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ME7","text":"Potency, non-being, and mathematical abstraction are reduced from exceptions to the universality of the good to boundary cases of distinct modes of attribution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D5","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D9","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D10","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ALT1","text":"Surface — The explicit disputation determines the identity of good and being, their priority, the universality of the good, final cause, mode-species-order, and the threefold division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ALT2","text":"Structural — Actuality generates desirability through perfection, form is structured through commensuration and inclination, and privation arises from a defect of due act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Objections are neutralized not by denying appearances, but by distributing them among the orders of reality, notion, causality, predication, and analogy.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AS5","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Reality is ordered according to act and potency, form and privation, moving end and formed effect, so that the same good appears causally before the effect and perfectively after form.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms suffice to restore the six principal conclusions and their connections.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A14","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A15","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:MRT2","text":"The strongest part is the nexus of actuality, perfection, and desirability, from which the real identity of the good with being and the goodness of every being are derived.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:MRT3","text":"Internal coherence is preserved through the distinction of orders; if the order of understanding is confused with the order of causing, the reciprocal priority of good and being produces an apparent contradiction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0034","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0036","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:MRT4","text":"The norm by which perfection is called due and by which the ultimate act is determined is not fully explained in this question, but is presupposed from the natures of things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:MRT5","text":"The doctrine of evil safeguards the universality of the good, but depends wholly on the asymmetry between positive being and the privation of due being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D8","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:MRT6","text":"The transition from the motion of the natural body to the motion of appetite and from form to inclination is structurally necessary, but is proved analogically and compactly.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A12","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A14","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0098","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0120","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:MRT7","text":"The threefold division does not constitute mutually exclusive things; its force depends on the distinction of notions of desirability and on analogical predication.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A15","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:A16","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0124","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5:MRT8","text":"The restoration preserves the true movement: from common actuality to the universality of the good, thence to causality and internal constitution, and finally to the ordering of the forms of desirability.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ5:AF1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS1","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS2","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS3","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS4","CH_GGVC_PPQ5:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ5:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PPQ5@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_PPQ5:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 06","title":"First Part · Question 5","context":"Chapter 06","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 5"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 5","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 6","order":7,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ6"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6@R001","sha256":"ce273522177c56bc037fb7bc9b183e9d369d255c92b544f7d7797fd9f853fe7f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AS1","text":"Quaestio bonitatis divinae solvitur distinguendo bonum causatum et bonum quod causaliter omnes perfectiones fundat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AS2","text":"Deus dicitur bonus quia, ut prima causa efficiens, est appetibilis sub ratione similitudinis perfectionis quam effectus participant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AS3","text":"Deus dicitur summum bonum non per participationem generis bonorum, sed per excessum causalitatis aequivocae et extragenericae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AS4","text":"Solus Deus est bonus per essentiam, quia in eo esse, perfectiones operativae et finis ultimus non sunt ab essentia distincta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AS5","text":"Omnia sunt bona simul bonitate divina causaliter participata et bonitate propria formaliter inhaerente.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AS6","text":"Motus argumentationis progreditur a ratione boni ut appetibilis ad causalitatem divinam, deinde ad eminentiam, essentialitatem et participationem bonitatis creatarum rerum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A1","text":"Bonum est id quod est appetibile.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A2","text":"Unumquodque appetit suam perfectionem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A3","text":"Perfectio et forma effectus similitudinem agentis participant, quia agens agit sibi simile.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A4","text":"Deus est prima causa effectiva omnium rerum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A5","text":"Effectus, appetendo perfectiones proprias, appetunt Deum secundum similitudines divini esse in eis participatas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A6","text":"Deus causat perfectiones creaturarum non univoce, sed ut causa quae nec specie nec genere cum effectibus convenit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A7","text":"Quod in causa aequivoca est, excellentiori modo in ea est quam in effectibus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A8","text":"Comparatio Dei ad creaturas per summum importat excessum causalitatis, non communionem generis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A9","text":"Perfectio rei includit constitutionem in esse, perfectiones ad operationem requisitas et assecutionem finis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A10","text":"Solius Dei essentia est suum esse, nullis accidentibus perficitur et ad nullum finem ulteriorem ordinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A11","text":"Essentia rei creatae non est ipsum esse eius.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0049","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A12","text":"Bonitas creata est esse, perfectio superaddita vel ordo ad finem, et non identitas absoluta essentiae et bonitatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A13","text":"Bonitas divina est principium exemplare, effectivum et finale omnis bonitatis creatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A14","text":"Similitudo divinae bonitatis creaturae inhaerens est formaliter bonitas propria qua creatura denominatur bona.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:NX1","text":"Bonitas Dei non est bonitas causata quae modum, speciem et ordinem recipit; est causalitas quae haec aliis imponit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:NX2","text":"Appetere Deum non requirit ut omnis appetens Deum secundum se cognoscat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:NX3","text":"Bonum non significat quod unum idemque bonum ab omnibus appetatur, sed quod quidquid appetitur sub ratione boni appetatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:NX4","text":"Summum bonum non addit Deo rem absolutam nec compositionem realem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:NX5","text":"Deus non est in alio genere bonorum nec cum creaturis sub eodem genere comparatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:NX6","text":"Unitas per essentiam non infert bonitatem per essentiam, quia unum importat indivisionem, bonum vero perfectionem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0047","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:NX7","text":"Bonitas creaturae non est ipsa bonitas divina ut forma immediate denominans creaturam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0058","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:NX8","text":"Species separatae subsistentes, quibus individua absolute denominarentur, non sunt admittendae ad explicandam bonitatem rerum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0060","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:D1","text":"Deus est bonus et appetibilis, quia prima causalitas efficit perfectiones quae eius similitudinem participant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:D2","text":"Appetitus naturalis rerum cognitione carentium ad Deum ordinatur per inclinationem a cognitione superiori institutam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:D3","text":"Deus est summum bonum simpliciter, quia omnes perfectiones appetibiles ab eo effluunt excellentiori modo quam in effectibus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:D4","text":"Praedicatio summi de Deo significat defectum creaturarum ab ipso, non differentiam intrinsecam Deo additam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:D5","text":"Solus Deus est bonus per essentiam, quia solus omnes tres perfectionis rationes essentialiter possidet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:D6","text":"Creatura est bona, sed non per essentiam, quia eius esse et bonitas eius ab essentia distinguuntur vel ei superveniunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:D7","text":"Una bonitas divina et multae bonitates creatae simul affirmantur secundum causalitatem participatam et denominationem formalem inhaerentem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:SD1","text":"Bonitas creaturarum constituit ordinem participativum: eadem res ad Deum refertur ut ad principium, sed bona dicitur per formam sibi propriam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:SD2","text":"Eminentia divina non destruit bonitates creatas, sed earum realitatem subordinat causaliter et exemplariter primae bonitati.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:SD3","text":"Finis ultimus rerum et causa efficiens earum perfectionum coincidit in Deo, ita ut teleologia creaturarum ex causalitate originis intelligatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:SD4","text":"Praedicatio boni de Deo et creaturis non est univoca neque mere aequivoca sine ordine, sed ordinatur per causalitatem et similitudinem deficientem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet quod bonum divinum et bonum creatum eandem rationem genericam uniformiter participant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet quod creaturae perfectionem suam ex seipsis absolute possideant vel ad finem ultimum ex se ordinentur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet quod esse creatum sit identicum essentiae creatae, si bonitas per essentiam creaturae tribuatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0049","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet quod bonitas divina sola formaliter in creaturis inhabitet, quia hoc bonitatem propriam creaturarum evacuaret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0058","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse debet quod causalitas prima sit una causa inter alias, quia Deus ponitur extra genus et principium omnis generis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:SNX1","text":"Argumentatio excludit possibilitatem boni increati quod sit finitum, compositum, accidentale vel ad finem superiorem ordinatum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:SNX2","text":"Argumentatio excludit possibilitatem bonitatis creatae omnino independentis, quia omnis bonitas creata intelligitur ut similitudo primi boni.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:SNX3","text":"Argumentatio excludit aequationem inter cognitionem explicitam Dei et ordinationem realem ad Deum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:SNX4","text":"Argumentatio excludit unam formam denominationis boni omnibus entibus sufficientem: causalitas divina et forma creata diversas rationes denominationis exigunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:FGS1","text":"Appetibilitas → perfectio appetita → similitudo agentis → bonitas causae primae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:FGS2","text":"Causa prima non-univoca → perfectiones effectuum causaliter praecontentae → eminentia divina → summum bonum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:FGS3","text":"Esse subsistens + absentia accidentium + absentia finis ulterioris → omnimoda perfectio essentialis → bonitas per essentiam soli Deo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:FGS4","text":"Participatio similitudinis divinae → causalitas exemplaris, effectiva et finalis → bonitas divina communis causaliter → bonitas propria formaliter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:FGS5","text":"Negatio univocationis et identitatis formalis → conservatio transcendentiae divinae simul cum realitate bonitatis creatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AF1","text":"Bonum ut appetibile → perfectio ut obiectum appetitus → Deus ut prima causa similitudinum perfectionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AF2","text":"Bonitas Dei → appetitus creaturarum → distinctio cognitionis explicitae, cognitionis participatae et inclinationis naturalis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AF3","text":"Prima causalitas non-univoca → modus excellentior perfectionum in Deo → Deus ut summum bonum extra genus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AF4","text":"Triplex perfectio → identitas divinae essentiae et esse → exclusio bonitatis essentialis creaturarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AF5","text":"Participatio divinae bonitatis → similitudo inhaerens → unitas principii bonitatis et pluralitas bonitatum formalium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:ME1","text":"Bonitas transfertur ab aestimatione morali ad structuram metaphysicam appetitus, perfectionis, causalitatis et finis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:ME2","text":"Oppositio inter transcendens bonum divinum et bona creata dissolvitur per distinctionem inter principium causale et formam denominantem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:ME3","text":"Comparatio summae bonitatis desinit esse comparatio quantitativa intra genus et fit relatio excessus principii ad participata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:ME4","text":"Appetitus rerum irrationalium fit intelligibilis non ut cognitio deficiens, sed ut ordinatio naturalis ad perfectionem et finem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:ME5","text":"Multiplicitas bonorum non opponitur unitati boni primi, quia pluralitas formalium bonitatum in una causalitate exemplari ordinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:ALT1","text":"Surface — Deus bonus, summum bonum, solus bonus per essentiam, et causa bonitatis omnium affirmatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:ALT2","text":"Structural — Bonitas explicatur per nexum causae efficientis, similitudinis participatae, formae inhaerentis, perfectionis et finis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Obiectiones resolvuntur per reclassificationem praedicatorum divinorum: modus, summum, comparatio, essentia et participatio non eodem modo de Deo et creaturis dicuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Creatura est ens perfectibile et ad finem ordinatum; Deus est esse subsistens, non recipiens perfectionem neque ad finem ulteriorem tendens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:MRT1","text":"Axiomata de appetibilitate, perfectione, similitudine agentis et causalitate prima sufficiunt ad restitutionem conclusionis quod Deus est bonus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:MRT2","text":"Axiomata de causalitate non-univoca, excellentia et extragenericitate sufficiunt ad restitutionem praedicationis summi boni sine compositione divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:MRT3","text":"Axiomata de triplici perfectione et identitate divinae essentiae cum esse sufficiunt ad exclusivam bonitatis per essentiam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:MRT4","text":"Distinctio inter bonitatem divinam causalem et bonitatem creatam formalem necessaria est ad conservandam simul participationem et proprietatem creaturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:MRT5","text":"Commissura principalis manet in transitu ab assimilatione causae ad appetitum Dei: nexus valet intra ordinem causaliter et finaliter institutum, quem textus supponit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:MRT6","text":"Commissura secunda manet in formula unitatis et pluralitatis bonitatis: textus eam ordinat per distinctionem causae et formae, sed non ulterius explicat modum precisum participationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:MRT7","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat, quia a bonitate divina ut causalitate procedit ad eius eminentiam, essentialitatem et communicationem creatam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T155604184186Z_7255ec45","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 6","source_sha256":"ce273522177c56bc037fb7bc9b183e9d369d255c92b544f7d7797fd9f853fe7f","sha256":"4cb97633ca3c479467990e7c4a8760a0c75f5563e5af1984e30b3aee35274825","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:56:04.185228+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AS1","text":"The question of divine goodness is resolved by distinguishing caused good from good that causally grounds all perfections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AS2","text":"God is called good because, as the first efficient cause, He is appetible under the aspect of the likeness of perfection that effects participate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AS3","text":"God is called the highest good not through participation in a genus of goods, but through the excess of equivocal and extra-generic causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AS4","text":"God alone is good through essence, because in Him being, operative perfections, and the ultimate end are not distinct from essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AS5","text":"All things are good both by divinely participated goodness causally and by their own goodness formally inhering.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AS6","text":"The movement of the argument proceeds from the notion of the good as appetible to divine causality, then to eminence, essentiality, and the participation of the goodness of created things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A1","text":"The good is that which is appetible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A2","text":"Each thing seeks its own perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A3","text":"The perfection and form of an effect participate in the likeness of the agent, because an agent acts producing something like itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A4","text":"God is the first efficient cause of all things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A5","text":"Effects, by seeking their own perfections, seek God according to the likenesses of divine being participated in them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A6","text":"God causes the perfections of creatures not univocally, but as a cause that agrees with its effects neither in species nor in genus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A7","text":"What is in an equivocal cause is in it in a more excellent manner than in its effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A8","text":"The comparison of God to creatures through the highest implies an excess of causality, not a community of genus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A9","text":"The perfection of a thing includes its constitution in being, the perfections required for operation, and the attainment of its end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A10","text":"The essence of God alone is His being, is perfected by no accidents, and is ordered to no further end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A11","text":"The essence of a created thing is not its very being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0049","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A12","text":"Created goodness is being, a superadded perfection, or an order to an end, and not the absolute identity of essence and goodness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A13","text":"Divine goodness is the exemplary, efficient, and final principle of all created goodness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A14","text":"The likeness of divine goodness inhering in a creature is formally the creature's own goodness by which the creature is denominated good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:NX1","text":"The goodness of God is not caused goodness that receives mode, species, and order; it is the causality that imposes these upon others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:NX2","text":"To seek God does not require that every seeker know God according to Himself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:NX3","text":"Good does not signify that one and the same good is sought by all, but that whatever is sought is sought under the aspect of the good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:NX4","text":"The highest good adds neither an absolute thing nor a real composition to God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:NX5","text":"God is not in another genus of goods, nor is He compared with creatures under the same genus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:NX6","text":"Unity through essence does not entail goodness through essence, because one implies indivision, whereas good implies perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0047","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:NX7","text":"The goodness of a creature is not divine goodness itself as a form immediately denominating the creature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0058","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:NX8","text":"Separate subsisting species, by which individuals would be denominated absolutely, are not to be admitted in explaining the goodness of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0060","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:D1","text":"God is good and appetible because first causality produces perfections that participate in His likeness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:D2","text":"The natural appetite of things lacking cognition is ordered to God through an inclination established by a higher cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:D3","text":"God is the highest good simply, because all appetible perfections flow from Him in a more excellent manner than in effects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:D4","text":"The predication of highest concerning God signifies the deficiency of creatures from Him, not an intrinsic difference added to God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:D5","text":"God alone is good through essence because He alone essentially possesses all three notions of perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:D6","text":"A creature is good, but not through essence, because its being and its goodness are distinct from its essence or supervene upon it.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:D7","text":"One divine goodness and many created goodnesses are simultaneously affirmed according to participated causality and inhering formal denomination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:SD1","text":"The goodness of creatures establishes a participatory order: the same thing is referred to God as to a principle, but is called good through its own proper form.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:SD2","text":"Divine eminence does not destroy created goodnesses, but causally and exemplarily subordinates their reality to the first goodness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A13","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:SD3","text":"The ultimate end of things and the efficient cause of their perfections coincide in God, so that the teleology of creatures is understood from the causality of origin.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:SD4","text":"The predication of good concerning God and creatures is neither univocal nor merely equivocal without order, but is ordered through causality and deficient likeness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ6:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:DNX1","text":"It must be false that divine good and created good uniformly participate in the same generic notion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:DNX2","text":"It must be false that creatures absolutely possess their perfection from themselves or are ordered to the ultimate end from themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:DNX3","text":"It must be false that created being is identical with created essence, if goodness through essence is attributed to the creature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0049","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:DNX4","text":"It must be false that divine goodness alone formally inheres in creatures, because this would empty creatures of their own goodness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0058","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:DNX5","text":"It must be false that first causality is one cause among others, because God is posited outside genus and as the principle of every genus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:SNX1","text":"The argument excludes the possibility of an uncreated good that is finite, composite, accidental, or ordered to a higher end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:SNX2","text":"The argument excludes the possibility of wholly independent created goodness, because every created goodness is understood as a likeness of the first good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:SNX3","text":"The argument excludes the equation of explicit knowledge of God with real ordering to God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:SNX4","text":"The argument excludes one form of denomination of the good sufficient for all beings: divine causality and created form require diverse notions of denomination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:FGS1","text":"Appetibility → sought perfection → likeness of the agent → goodness of the first cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:FGS2","text":"Non-univocal first cause → perfections of effects causally precontained → divine eminence → highest good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:FGS3","text":"Subsisting being + absence of accidents + absence of a further end → all-round essential perfection → goodness through essence in God alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:FGS4","text":"Participation in divine likeness → exemplary, efficient, and final causality → divine goodness common causally → proper goodness formally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:FGS5","text":"Negation of univocity and formal identity → preservation of divine transcendence together with the reality of created goodness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AF1","text":"The good as appetible → perfection as the object of appetite → God as the first cause of likenesses of perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AF2","text":"Divine goodness → appetite of creatures → distinction of explicit cognition, participated cognition, and natural inclination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AF3","text":"Non-univocal first causality → more excellent mode of perfections in God → God as the highest good outside genus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AF4","text":"Threefold perfection → identity of divine essence and being → exclusion of essential goodness in creatures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:AF5","text":"Participation in divine goodness → inhering likeness → unity of the principle of goodness and plurality of formal goodnesses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:ME1","text":"Goodness is transferred from moral estimation to the metaphysical structure of appetite, perfection, causality, and end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:ME2","text":"The opposition between transcendent divine good and created goods is dissolved through the distinction between causal principle and denominating form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:ME3","text":"The comparison of highest goodness ceases to be a quantitative comparison within a genus and becomes a relation of the excess of the principle to what is participated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:ME4","text":"The appetite of irrational things becomes intelligible not as deficient cognition, but as natural ordering to perfection and end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:ME5","text":"The multiplicity of goods is not opposed to the unity of the first good, because the plurality of formal goodnesses is ordered in one exemplary causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:ALT1","text":"Surface — God is affirmed to be good, the highest good, alone good through essence, and the cause of the goodness of all.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:ALT2","text":"Structural — Goodness is explained through the nexus of efficient cause, participated likeness, inhering form, perfection, and end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Objections are resolved through a reclassification of divine predicates: mode, highest, comparison, essence, and participation are not said of God and creatures in the same way.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The creature is a perfectible being ordered to an end; God is subsisting being, neither receiving perfection nor tending toward a further end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:MRT1","text":"Axioms concerning appetibility, perfection, likeness of the agent, and first causality suffice for reconstructing the conclusion that God is good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:MRT2","text":"Axioms concerning non-univocal causality, excellence, and extra-genericity suffice for reconstructing the predication of the highest good without divine composition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:MRT3","text":"Axioms concerning threefold perfection and the identity of divine essence with being suffice for the exclusivity of goodness through essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:MRT4","text":"The distinction between causal divine goodness and formal created goodness is necessary in order to preserve both participation and the propriety of the creature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:MRT5","text":"The principal joint remains in the transition from assimilation to the cause to the appetite for God: the nexus holds within the causally and finally instituted order that the text presupposes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:MRT6","text":"The second joint remains in the formula of the unity and plurality of goodness: the text orders it through the distinction of cause and form, but does not further explain the precise mode of participation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text, because it proceeds from divine goodness as causality to its eminence, essentiality, and created communication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ6:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PPQ6@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_PPQ6:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 07","title":"First Part · Question 6","context":"Chapter 07","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 6"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 6","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 7","order":8,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ7"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7@R001","sha256":"fdc69991a8e03a0b2cc8835154218d64b32bf7d09db6067aaea8e5af437f7934","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:AS1","text":"Quaestio investigat quomodo infinitas Deo conveniat, et utrum eadem ratio infinitatis creaturis, magnitudini aut multitudini actu tribui possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:AS2","text":"Argumentatio mutat intellectum infiniti ab indeterminato materiali ad immunitatem ipsius esse a receptione et contractione.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:AS3","text":"Deus infinitus demonstratur quia est ipsum esse subsistens, non esse in aliquo receptum; infinitas igitur eius cum perfectione coincidit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:AS4","text":"Creaturae infinitas simpliciter negatur, quia etiam forma a materia separata esse receptum et ad naturam determinatam retinet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:AS5","text":"Infinitum actu excluditur a magnitudine et multitudine creatis, sed infinitum potentiae et successionis conceditur secundum divisionem, additionem et processum non-totalem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A1","text":"Infinitum dicitur quod finitum non est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A2","text":"Materia terminatur per formam, quia potentia materiae ad multas formas per formam receptam determinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A3","text":"Forma recepta in materia terminatur, quia amplitudo formae ad hoc individuum contrahitur per materiam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A4","text":"Infinitum ex parte materiae rationem imperfecti habet, quia materia sine forma actu perfecta non est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A5","text":"Infinitum ex parte formae non determinatae per materiam rationem perfectionis habet, quia materia formam non perficit sed contrahit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A6","text":"Ipsum esse est maxime formale.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","text":"Esse divinum non est receptum in aliquo, sed Deus est suum esse subsistens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8","text":"Omne ens creatum, etiam forma creata per se subsistens si ponatur, habet esse receptum et contractum ad naturam determinatam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9","text":"Quod est totum simul actu et sub forma determinata, secundum quantitatem finitum est; figura enim quantitatem terminat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10","text":"Tempus et motus non sunt tota simul actu, sed actum potentiae admixtum successive habent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A11","text":"Omnis multitudo actualis in aliqua specie numeri consistit, et omnis numerus est multitudo per unum mensurata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A12","text":"Omne creatum sub certa intentione creantis et sub certo numero comprehenditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13","text":"Potentia in actum reducitur secundum modum essendi proprium, non necessario ut totum simul.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:NX1","text":"Infinitas divina non est infinitas materialis, quae privationem formae, potentiam indeterminatam et imperfectionem significat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:NX2","text":"Infinitas divina non est quantitas, extensio corporalis, locus aut indeterminatio figurae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:NX3","text":"Deus non fit finitus quia hoc est et non aliud; distinctio ab aliis sequitur ex esse subsistente non recepto, non ex limite eius.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:NX4","text":"Virtus infinita Dei non importat potentiam efficiendi creaturam simpliciter infinitam, quia creatum ipsum esse subsistens esse non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:NX5","text":"Comprehensio intellectualis universalis non probat intellectum creatum esse infinitum secundum essentiam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:NX6","text":"Infinitas mathematicae demonstrationis non requirit lineam actu infinitam, sed lineam finitam a qua quantumlibet necessarium subtrahi potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:NX7","text":"Infinitas multitudinis actu non admittitur neque per se neque per accidens; sola infinitas potentiae successive procedentis admittitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0075","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D1","text":"Deus est infinitus et perfectus, quia ipsum esse subsistens non recipitur in subiecto quod eius amplitudinem contrahat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D2","text":"Deus ab omnibus aliis distinguitur non per limitationem essentiae, sed quia esse eius non participatur receptive sicut esse creaturarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D3","text":"Nihil praeter Deum est infinitum simpliciter, quia omne creatum esse determinatum et receptum habet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D4","text":"Creatura potest esse infinita secundum quid, si sub uno respectu potentiam indeterminatam retinet, sed sub alio respectu forma vel natura eam finit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D5","text":"Materia prima neque ens actu per se neque infinita simpliciter est, quia est potentia tantum et ad formas naturales tantum ordinata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D6","text":"Corpus naturale actu infinitum esse non potest, quia forma substantialis determinata accidentia determinata, inter quae quantitatem, consequitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D7","text":"Magnitudo actu infinita esse non potest, quia quaelibet magnitudo actu existens formam quanti, id est figuram terminatam, requirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D8","text":"Divisio continui infinitum potentiae exhibet, quia procedit versus partes materialiter se habentes; additio autem totum formaliter constituit et infinitum actu non producit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D9","text":"Infinitas temporis, motus et numeri non est totalitas infinita simul data, sed continuabilis successio post quam ulterius procedi potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D10","text":"Multitudo actu infinita impossibilis est, quia nulla multitudo actualis extra omnem speciem numeri et omnem mensuram unius consistere potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D11","text":"Ordo creationis excludit multitudinem creatam actu infinitam, quia creatio sub intentione determinata et numero certo intelligitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:SD1","text":"Infinitas univoce non praedicatur de Deo et creaturis: in Deo est modus perfectionis essendi, in creaturis autem solum potentia indeterminata sub aliquo respectu.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:SD2","text":"Ratio absolutae infinitatis convertitur cum aseitate essendi: esse simpliciter infinitum esse debet ipsum esse subsistens, et ideo creatum esse non potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:SD3","text":"Omne infinitum creatum, si conceditur, mutatur ex proprietate rei totaliter datae in regulam indefiniti processus: post datum finitum semper aliud sumi potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:SD4","text":"Ordo naturalis et ordo creatus eandem clausuram contra infinitum actuale exercent: forma determinat quantitatem, numerus determinat multitudinem, intentio creantis determinat universitatem creatorum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:SD5","text":"Distinctio inter infinitum simpliciter et secundum quid impedit transitum a capacitate infinita, divisibilitate infinita vel multiplicabilitate infinita ad existentiam alicuius infiniti actu.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet quod omnis carentia termini idem valeat: carentia termini materialis est imperfectio, carentia receptionis in ipso esse est perfectio.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet quod determinatio individui et determinatio essendi sint eiusdem rationis: creatura determinatur per receptionem, Deus ab aliis distinguitur sine receptione limitante.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0021","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet quod potentia infinita possibilitatem contradictorii aut productionem ipsius esse subsistentis contineat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet quod abstractio mathematica obiectum actu infinitum in rerum natura aut etiam in quantitate mathematice actuali constituat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse debet quod indefinita continuatio operationis, divisionis aut numerationis constituat totum infinitum actu completum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:SNX1","text":"Systema excludit possibilitatem tertii modi essendi inter esse subsistens increatum et esse creatum receptum: nihil potest esse simul creatum et simpliciter infinitum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:SNX2","text":"Systema excludit universum creatum ut totalitatem actu interminatam, quia universitas creata debet sub numero, mensura et intentione comprehendi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0075","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:SNX3","text":"Systema excludit aequationem inter infinitum divinum et infinitum quantitative extensum, ita ut transcendens non per maximum corpus sed per non-receptum esse intelligatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0019","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:FGS1","text":"Non-finitum → quaeritur unde terminus oriatur → materia terminatur per formam, forma per materiam → duplex infinitatis ratio separatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:FGS2","text":"Infinitum materiale → potentia sine forma → imperfectio; infinitum formale non receptum → imminutio contractionis → perfectio.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:FGS3","text":"Ipsum esse maxime formale + esse divinum non receptum → Deus est infinitus simpliciter et perfectus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:FGS4","text":"Esse creatum receptum et contractum → finitas simpliciter creaturae → infinitas secundum quid tantum secundum potentiam vel immunitatem partialem a materia.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:FGS5","text":"Quantitas tota simul actu → figura et terminus → impossibilitas magnitudinis infinitae actualis; successio actui permixta → possibilitas infiniti potentialis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:FGS6","text":"Multitudo actualis → species numeri et mensura unius → exclusio infiniti actualis; multiplicatio successiva → infinitum potentiae post omnem multitudinem finitam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:AF1","text":"Ambiguitas infiniti → analysis termini per materiam et formam → distinctio infiniti imperfecti et perfecti → esse subsistens divinum → infinitas Dei.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:AF2","text":"Infinitas Dei → examen creaturae → esse creatum receptum → negatio infiniti simpliciter creati → concessio infiniti secundum quid.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:AF3","text":"Creatura finita secundum essentiam → distinctio essentiae et magnitudinis → forma et figura quantitatis → negatio magnitudinis infinitae actualis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:AF4","text":"Infinitum divisionis et successionis → potentia non-totalis → separatio a toto simul actu → concessio processus infiniti sine re infinita actuali.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:AF5","text":"Multitudo actualis → numerus mensuratus → intentio creantis → exclusio multitudinis infinitae per se et per accidens.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:ME1","text":"Infinitas reclassificatur ex sola negatione termini in proprietatem analogice divisam inter indeterminationem materialem et eminentiam esse non recepti.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:ME2","text":"Oppositio inter infinitum et perfectum dissolvitur: oppositio valet de infinito materiali, non de infinito formali quod Deo attribuitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:ME3","text":"Distinctio Deus-creatura describitur per modum essendi, non per gradum quantitatis, virtutis aut extensionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:ME4","text":"Infinitum mathematicum, naturale, temporale et numericum non sub eadem categoria ponuntur: quantitas tota actu finitur, successio et potentia indefinita procedunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:ME5","text":"Multitudo infinita non iam aestimatur ex sola non-repugnantia singularium inter se, sed ex condicione formaliter numerabilis et causaliter ordinatae totalitatis creatæ.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0069","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus determinat utrum Deus, creatura, magnitudo et multitudo infinita esse possint, distinguens actum a potentia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:ALT2","text":"Structural — Finitas producitur per receptionem, contractionem, formam, figuram, numerum et ordinationem creatam; infinitas divina producitur per absentiam receptionis ipsius esse.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Argumentatio objectiones de quantitate, virtute intellectus, mathematica abstractione et multiplicabilitate convertit in errores transitum facientes ab ordine potentiali vel intentionali ad esse actuale simpliciter.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Esse subsistens stat contra esse receptum; simultanea totalitas stat contra successionem; infinitum proprie divinum stat contra indefinitum creatum procedurale.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:MRT1","text":"Axiomata de termino materiae et formae, de formalitate ipsius esse, et de esse divino non recepto sufficiunt ad conclusionem quod Deus infinitus et perfectus sit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:MRT2","text":"Axioma de esse creato recepto sufficit ad negandam infinitatem simpliciter creatam, sed status formarum separatarum conditionaliter tractatur per formulam “ut quidam de Angelis opinantur.”","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:MRT3","text":"Negatio infiniti actualis quantitatis cohaeret si omne quantum actu formam figuramque determinatam requirit; haec condicio est cardo reconstructionis physico-metaphysicae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:MRT4","text":"Negatio multitudinis infinitae actualis dupliciter sustinetur, per rationem numeri mensurati et per rationem intentionis creatricis; utraque ratio systematis clausuram roborat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:MRT5","text":"Commissura fragilis manet in transitu ab infinitate operationis mathematicae vel successionis ad impossibilitatem totalitatis infinitae actualis, quia distinctio potentiae et actus totam exclusionem regit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0056","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:MRT6","text":"Quaestio prooemialiter infinitatem cum existentia Dei in rebus conectit, sed haec unitas argumentativa hic solum fundamentum de incircumscriptibilitate praebet, non conclusionem distinctam de praesentia Dei in rebus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T155633230360Z_e72157c3","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 7","source_sha256":"fdc69991a8e03a0b2cc8835154218d64b32bf7d09db6067aaea8e5af437f7934","sha256":"fcb2afc9e098c604fab98e329b703b8ed0a68b38843454bcf862853d0bfff3c8","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:56:33.232105+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:AS1","text":"The question investigates how infinity befits God, and whether the same account of infinity can be attributed in act to creatures, magnitude, or multitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:AS2","text":"The argument shifts the understanding of the infinite from material indeterminacy to the immunity of being itself from reception and contraction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:AS3","text":"God is demonstrated to be infinite because He is subsisting being itself, not being received in anything; therefore His infinity coincides with perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:AS4","text":"Infinity simply speaking is denied to creatures, because even a form separated from matter retains received being and [being] contracted to a determinate nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:AS5","text":"The infinite in act is excluded from created magnitude and multitude, but the infinite of potency and succession is granted according to division, addition, and non-total process.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A1","text":"The infinite is said to be that which is not finite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A2","text":"Matter is terminated by form, because the potency of matter for many forms is determined through the form received.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A3","text":"A form received in matter is terminated, because the amplitude of the form is contracted to this individual through matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A4","text":"The infinite on the part of matter has the character of the imperfect, because matter without form is not actually perfect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A5","text":"The infinite on the part of form not determined by matter has the character of perfection, because matter does not perfect form but contracts it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A6","text":"Being itself is maximally formal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","text":"Divine being is not received in anything, but God is His own subsisting being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8","text":"Every created being, even a created form subsisting through itself, if such be posited, has being received and contracted to a determinate nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9","text":"That which is wholly actual all at once and under a determinate form is finite according to quantity; for figure terminates quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10","text":"Time and motion are not wholly actual all at once, but have act mingled with potency successively.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A11","text":"Every actual multitude consists in some species of number, and every number is a multitude measured by one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A12","text":"Every created thing is comprehended under the determinate intention of the creator and under a determinate number.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13","text":"Potency is reduced to act according to its proper mode of being, not necessarily as a whole all at once.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:NX1","text":"Divine infinity is not material infinity, which signifies privation of form, indeterminate potency, and imperfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:NX2","text":"Divine infinity is not quantity, bodily extension, place, or indeterminacy of figure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:NX3","text":"God does not become finite because He is this and not another; distinction from others follows from subsisting unrecepted being, not from a limit of His.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:NX4","text":"God's infinite power does not entail the power to effect a creature simply infinite, because a creature cannot be subsisting being itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:NX5","text":"Universal intellectual comprehension does not prove that the created intellect is infinite according to essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:NX6","text":"The infinity of mathematical demonstration does not require an actually infinite line, but a finite line from which whatever is necessary can be subtracted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:NX7","text":"Infinity of multitude in act is admitted neither per se nor per accidens; only the infinity of potency proceeding successively is admitted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0075","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D1","text":"God is infinite and perfect, because subsisting being itself is not received in a subject that contracts its amplitude.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D2","text":"God is distinguished from all others not through limitation of essence, but because His being is not participated receptively as is the being of creatures.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D3","text":"Nothing besides God is simply infinite, because every creature has determinate and received being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D4","text":"A creature can be infinite in a certain respect, if under one respect it retains indeterminate potency, but under another respect form or nature makes it finite.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D5","text":"Prime matter is neither a being actually through itself nor simply infinite, because it is potency only and ordered only to natural forms.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D6","text":"A natural body cannot be actually infinite, because a determinate substantial form is followed by determinate accidents, among which is quantity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D7","text":"Magnitude cannot be actually infinite, because every actually existing magnitude requires the form of quantity, that is, a determinate figure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D8","text":"Division of the continuum displays an infinite of potency, because it proceeds toward parts having themselves materially; addition, however, formally constitutes a whole and does not produce an infinite in act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D9","text":"The infinity of time, motion, and number is not an infinite totality given all at once, but a continuable succession after which one can proceed further.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D10","text":"An actually infinite multitude is impossible, because no actual multitude can consist outside every species of number and every measure of one.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:D11","text":"The order of creation excludes an actually infinite created multitude, because creation is understood under a determinate intention and a certain number.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:SD1","text":"Infinity is not predicated univocally of God and creatures: in God it is a mode of perfection of being, whereas in creatures it is only indeterminate potency under some respect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:SD2","text":"The account of absolute infinity is convertible with aseity of being: being simply infinite must be subsisting being itself, and therefore cannot be created.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:SD3","text":"Every created infinite, if it is granted, is transformed from a property of a thing wholly given into a rule of an indefinite process: after a given finite [thing], another can always be taken.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:SD4","text":"Natural order and created order exercise the same closure against the actual infinite: form determines quantity, number determines multitude, the creator's intention determines the totality of creatures.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:SD5","text":"The distinction between the infinite simply speaking and the infinite in a certain respect prevents transition from infinite capacity, infinite divisibility, or infinite multiplicability to the existence of something actually infinite.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:DNX1","text":"It must be false that every lack of a limit amounts to the same thing: lack of a material limit is imperfection, lack of reception in being itself is perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:DNX2","text":"It must be false that determination of the individual and determination of being are of the same account: a creature is determined through reception, God is distinguished from others without limiting reception.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0021","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:DNX3","text":"It must be false that infinite power contains the possibility of a contradiction or the production of subsisting being itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:DNX4","text":"It must be false that mathematical abstraction constitutes an actually infinite object in the nature of things or even in mathematically actual quantity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:DNX5","text":"It must be false that indefinite continuation of operation, division, or numbering constitutes a whole infinitely complete in act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:SNX1","text":"The system excludes the possibility of a third mode of being between uncreated subsisting being and received created being: nothing can be both created and simply infinite.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:SNX2","text":"The system excludes the created universe as an actually unbounded totality, because created totality must be comprehended under number, measure, and intention.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0075","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:SNX3","text":"The system excludes the equation of the divine infinite with the quantitatively extended infinite, so that the transcendent is understood not through the greatest body but through unrecepted being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0019","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:FGS1","text":"Non-finite → it is asked whence the limit arises → matter is terminated by form, form by matter → the twofold account of infinity is separated.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:FGS2","text":"Material infinite → potency without form → imperfection; unrecepted formal infinite → removal of contraction → perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:FGS3","text":"Being itself maximally formal + unrecepted divine being → God is simply infinite and perfect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:FGS4","text":"Created being received and contracted → simple finitude of the creature → infinity in a certain respect only according to potency or partial immunity from matter.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:FGS5","text":"Quantity wholly actual all at once → figure and limit → impossibility of actual infinite magnitude; succession mingled with act → possibility of the potential infinite.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:FGS6","text":"Actual multitude → species of number and measure of one → exclusion of the actual infinite; successive multiplication → infinite of potency after every finite multitude.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:AF1","text":"Ambiguity of the infinite → analysis of limit through matter and form → distinction of imperfect and perfect infinite → divine subsisting being → infinity of God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:AF2","text":"Infinity of God → examination of the creature → received created being → denial of the simply infinite creature → concession of the infinite in a certain respect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:AF3","text":"Creature finite according to essence → distinction between essence and magnitude → form and figure of quantity → denial of actual infinite magnitude.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:AF4","text":"Infinite of division and succession → non-total potency → separation from the whole actual all at once → concession of infinite process without an actually infinite thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:AF5","text":"Actual multitude → measured number → intention of the creator → exclusion of infinite multitude per se and per accidens.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:ME1","text":"Infinity is reclassified from mere negation of limit into a property divided analogically between material indetermination and the eminence of unrecepted being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:ME2","text":"The opposition between the infinite and the perfect is dissolved: the opposition holds for the material infinite, not for the formal infinite attributed to God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:ME3","text":"The God-creature distinction is described through mode of being, not through degree of quantity, power, or extension.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:ME4","text":"The mathematical, natural, temporal, and numerical infinite are not placed under the same category: quantity wholly in act is finite, while succession and indeterminate potency proceed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:ME5","text":"Infinite multitude is no longer assessed from the mere non-repugnance of singulars among themselves, but from the condition of a formally numerable and causally ordered created totality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0069","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text determines whether God, creature, magnitude, and multitude can be infinite, distinguishing act from potency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:ALT2","text":"Structural — Finitude is produced through reception, contraction, form, figure, number, and created ordering; divine infinity is produced through the absence of reception of being itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument converts objections concerning quantity, power of intellect, mathematical abstraction, and multiplicability into errors making the transition from a potential or intentional order to being actual simply speaking.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Subsisting being stands against received being; simultaneous totality stands against succession; the properly divine infinite stands against the procedural created indefinite.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:MRT1","text":"The axioms concerning the limit of matter and form, the formality of being itself, and unrecepted divine being suffice for the conclusion that God is infinite and perfect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:MRT2","text":"The axiom concerning received created being suffices to deny simply created infinity, but the status of separated forms is treated conditionally by the formula “ut quidam de Angelis opinantur.”","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:MRT3","text":"The denial of the actual infinite of quantity coheres if every quantity in act requires a determinate form and figure; this condition is the hinge of the physical-metaphysical reconstruction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:MRT4","text":"The denial of actually infinite multitude is supported in two ways, through the account of measured number and through the account of creative intention; each account strengthens the system's closure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A11","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:MRT5","text":"A fragile juncture remains in the transition from the infinity of mathematical operation or succession to the impossibility of actual infinite totality, because the distinction between potency and act governs the entire exclusion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A9","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0056","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0062","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0077","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7:MRT6","text":"The question prooemially connects infinity with the existence of God in things, but this argumentative unity here provides only a foundation concerning uncircumscribability, not a distinct conclusion concerning God's presence in things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ7:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PPQ7@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_PPQ7:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 08","title":"First Part · Question 7","context":"Chapter 08","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 7"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 7","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 8","order":9,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ8"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8@R001","sha256":"04a334afc63271fab0fee242718f13166b772623b17c54243abe08a0528a3439","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:AS1","text":"Quaestio centralis examinat utrum infinitas divina exigat praesentiam Dei in omnibus rebus et omnibus locis sine inclusione locali vel compositione cum creaturis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:AS2","text":"Textus notionem esse in re ab inclusione continenti ad praesentiam causae agentis, esse creatum immediate conservantis, transfert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:AS3","text":"Argumentatio est metaphysico-distinctiva: praesentia divina explicatur per causalitatem essendi, virtutem, scientiam et relationem obiecti ad operationem rationalem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:AS4","text":"Argumentum ab esse creato ut effectu proprio Dei procedit ad intimitatem causalitatis, deinde ad ubiquitatem virtualem et ad distinctionem modorum praesentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:AS5","text":"Motus completur cum esse ubique ab extensione actuali ad necessitatem per se et primo universalem convertitur: ubicumque aliquid esse posset, ibi Deus totus causaliter adesset.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A1","text":"Deus est ipsum esse per suam essentiam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A2","text":"Esse creatum est proprius effectus Dei.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A3","text":"Deus esse creatum non solum producit initio, sed efficit et conservat quamdiu res esse habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A4","text":"Omne agens immediate operans effectui suo coniungitur eumque sua virtute contingit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A5","text":"Esse est formaliter intimum et profundissimum omnibus quae in re sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A6","text":"Deus est in rebus sicut causa agens, non sicut pars essentiae earum neque sicut accidens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A7","text":"Incorporale locum contingit per virtutem, non per contactum quantitatis dimensivae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A8","text":"Totalitas essentiae non commensuratur totalitati loci; Deus igitur totus est in omnibus et singulis rebus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A9","text":"Omnia divinae potestati subduntur, divinae cognitioni patent, et divinae essentiae ut causae essendi immediate adsunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A10","text":"Deus in creatura rationali cognoscente et diligente ipsum est sicut obiectum operationis; hic modus per gratiam constituitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A11","text":"Scientia et voluntas rem in Deo ut cognitam vel volitam ponunt, potentia vero agens ad rem exteriorem applicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A12","text":"Nulla perfectio substantiae superaddita, praeter gratiam, Deum constituit obiectum cognitum et amatum in creatura.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A13","text":"Quidquid esse potest, per Deum esse oportet; ideo, quibuscumque locis positis, Deus in singulis totus adest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:NX1","text":"Deus esse super omnia per excellentiam naturae non significat eum ab omnibus rebus causaliter abesse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:NX2","text":"Esse Deum in rebus non significat Deum a rebus contineri; Deus potius res continet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:NX3","text":"Actio divina non procedit ad rem distantem sine mediorum ordine, quia Deus immediate in omnibus agit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:NX4","text":"Deformitas culpae non est a Deo; ideo Deus in daemonibus non absolute dicitur esse nisi secundum naturam eorum creatam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0025","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:NX5","text":"Ubiquitas divina non est existentia Dei in loco sicut corporis locati.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:NX6","text":"Deus loca replens non alia corpora excludit, sicut corpus aliud corpus excludere dicitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:NX7","text":"Esse Deum per essentiam in rebus non significat Deum esse de essentia rerum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0060","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:NX8","text":"Praesentia secundum conspectum non est idem ac praesentia secundum substantiam causaliter adstantem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:NX9","text":"Modus specialis per gratiam non oritur ex quacumque perfectione accidentali creaturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:NX10","text":"Esse ubique primo et per se non convenit enti quod locis per partes distribuitur vel quod solum supposita conditione ubique esset.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:D1","text":"Omnis res, quamdiu esse habet, in continua dependentia causali a Deo manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:D2","text":"Praesentia Dei est intima omnibus rebus, quia causalitas eius directe attingit ipsum esse quod est omnibus intimum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:D3","text":"Deus est in omni loco per contactum virtutis, quia dat ipsi loco esse et dat rebus locatis esse quo locum replent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:D4","text":"Deus omnem locum replet sine exclusione corporum, quia praesentia causalis non competit cum praesentia quantitativa.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:D5","text":"Potentia, praesentia et essentia tres rationes irreductibiles divinae in rebus adstantiae constituunt: subiectionem, manifestitatem et causalitatem essendi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:D6","text":"Scientia et voluntas non sunt modi quibus Deus in rebus dicitur esse, quia secundum eas res magis in Deo sunt quam Deus in rebus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:D7","text":"Gratia non auget communem causalitatem divinam, sed instituit praesentiam specialem qua Deus fit obiectum cognitionis et amoris rationalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:D8","text":"Ubiquitas Dei primo et per se exigit praesentiam totius Dei in quolibet loco possibili, non praesentiam secundum partes neque sub conditione cosmologica contingenti.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:SD1","text":"Omnipraesentia divina est forma ontologica conservationis: esse finitum non subsistit post causationem, sed permanet dum causatio divina immediate perseverat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:SD2","text":"Spatium non constituit ambitum externum Deo datum, sed ordinem rerum quarum esse et virtus locativa a Deo dependent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:SD3","text":"Praesentia divina et praesentia creaturarum in eodem loco non sunt competitivae, quia una est causalis et virtualis, altera quantitativa et localis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:SD4","text":"Triformis praesentia divina ordinat universum sub uno principio tripliciter: ut subiectum potestati, manifestum cognitioni, et dependens a causa essendi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:SD5","text":"Gratia mutat genus relationis creaturae ad Deum, non quia Deus aliter causat esse creaturae, sed quia creatura rationalis Deum ut obiectum actu vel habitu attingit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:SD6","text":"Proprietas divinae ubiquitatis est modalis, non merely cosmographica: non ex amplitudine mundi, sed ex necessitate causalitatis divinae sequitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse oportet creaturam posse esse per se aut sine actuali influxu divino conservante.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse oportet inferiores res a Deo tantum mediate pendere, quasi primae creaturae ultimam originem et conservationem reliquorum sufficienter supplere possent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse oportet locum vel universum esse receptaculum ontologice externum divinae praesentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse oportet praepositionem in univoce localem esse, cum causalitas, cognitio, obiectum operationis et situs localis diversas relationes constituant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0064","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse oportet culpam vel deformitatem moralem naturam creatam a causalitate essendi separare.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:DNX6","text":"Falsum esse oportet quamlibet perfectionem superadditam specialem inhabitationem divinam efficere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:SNX1","text":"Nulla regio entis, nulla res, nullus locus possibilis extra ambitum causalitatis divinae consistere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:SNX2","text":"Nulla magnitudo, extensio vel distributio partium potest esse exemplar proprium totalitatis divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:SNX3","text":"Nulla divisio creaturarum, locorum vel effectuum essentiam divinam dividere, circumscribere vel localiter determinare potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:SNX4","text":"Nullus terminus mundi actualis mensuram divinae ubiquitatis praebet, quia praesentia Dei non a locis datis, sed a possibilitate cuiuslibet esse dependet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:FGS1","text":"Ambiguitas esse in re disiungitur in inclusionem localem, praesentiam causalem, praesentiam cognitivam et praesentiam obiecti operationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:FGS2","text":"Deus est ipsum esse → esse creatum est effectus proprius Dei → Deus esse creatum continue causat et conservat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:FGS3","text":"Causalitas immediata essendi → coniunctio virtualis agentis ad effectum → praesentia Dei intima in omni re existente.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:FGS4","text":"Praesentia virtualis incorporalis → locus per virtutem contingitur → Deus est in omni loco sine occupatione quantitativa vel exclusione.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:FGS5","text":"Communis causalitas essendi → potentia, praesentia et essentia → distincta subiectio, manifestitas et dependentia ontologica omnium creaturarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:FGS6","text":"Cognitio et amor rationalis per gratiam → Deus ut obiectum operationis in sanctis → modus specialis praesentiae non reducibilis ad causalitatem communem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:FGS7","text":"Quidquid esse potest per Deum esse oportet → quaelibet positio locorum Deum requirit → esse ubique primo et per se est proprium Dei.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:AF1","text":"Obiectiones ex transcendentiâ, continentia, distantia et deformitate → distinctio praesentiae causalis a situ corporali.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:AF2","text":"Esse creatum ut effectus proprius → conservatio immediata essendi → praesentia Dei intima in omnibus rebus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:AF3","text":"Praesentia in omnibus rebus → contactus virtutis ad loca → ubiquitas sine extensione quantitativa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:AF4","text":"Ubiquitas communis → distinctio potentiae, praesentiae et essentiae → modus specialis gratiae in creatura rationali.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:AF5","text":"Candidati creati ad ubiquitatem examinati → exclusio praesentiae partitivae et accidentalis → proprietas divina esse ubique primo et per se.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:ME1","text":"Relatio in mutatur ex relatione continentis et contenti in relationem causae essendi ad effectum conservatum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:ME2","text":"Locus reclassificatur ut effectus ordinis creati et virtutis locativae, non ut terminus qui divinam praesentiam mensuret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:ME3","text":"Praesentia divina dividitur in tres dimensiones non coincidentes: dominium potentiae, aperturam cognitioni et immediatam causalitatem essendi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:ME4","text":"Oppositio inter praesentiam Dei et corruptionem creaturae dissolvitur per distinctionem naturae creatae a deformitate culpae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:ME5","text":"Differentia inter praesentiam communem et inhabitationem gratiae fit visibilis ut differentia inter causalitatem universalem et relationem intentionalem obiecti cogniti atque amati.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:ME6","text":"Ubiquitas desinit significare maximam extensionem et significat necessitatem praesentiae divinae in quolibet ente vel loco possibili.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus directe quaerit utrum Deus sit in omnibus, ubique, per essentiam potentiam et praesentiam, atque utrum ubiquitas ei sit propria.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:ALT2","text":"Structural — Mechanismus constitutivus est continuus influxus divinus quo esse cuiuslibet rei immediate causatur et conservatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Argumentatio obiectiones spatialiter formatas solvit per distinctiones relationum: pars, accidens, causa, virtus, essentia, praesentia, obiectum et conditio per accidens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Res finita non habet esse ut possessionem semel acquisitam, sed ut effectum cuius permanentia praesentiam causae transcendentis et intimioris requirit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:MRT1","text":"Systema axiomatum sufficit ad conclusionem principalem restituendam: Deus est intime in omnibus rebus et ubique per causalitatem essendi, non per extensionem corporalem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:MRT2","text":"Nucleus argumenti maxime pendet ab identitate Dei cum ipso esse et ab immediata conservatione omnis esse creati.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:MRT3","text":"Transitus a causalitate ad praesentiam nititur principio quod agens immediate operans effectui coniungi debet; hoc principium assumitur ex physica prioriter stabilita.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:MRT4","text":"Distinctio contactus virtutis a contactu quantitativo sustinet transitum ad ubiquitatem, sed requirit retentionem metaphysicae incorporalis quam textus non hic iterum demonstrat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:MRT5","text":"Tensionem apparentem inter Deum in omnibus et Deum non absolute in daemonibus solvit distinctio naturae creatae et deformitatis culpae; sine hac distinctione universalitas propositionis vacillaret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0025","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:MRT6","text":"Distinctio potentiae, praesentiae et essentiae cohaeret tantum si subiectio, cognitio et causalitas essendi relationes realiter diversas designant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:MRT7","text":"Proprietas ubiquitatis primo et per se firmatur per extensionem contrafactualem ad quaelibet loca possibilia; vis probationis pendet ab universalitate principii quod nihil esse potest nisi per Deum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:MRT8","text":"Modus singularis unionis tantum indicatur et differtur; ideo classificatio modorum specialium praesentiae intra hanc quaestionem deliberate incompleta manet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T155702568257Z_cda84704","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 8","source_sha256":"04a334afc63271fab0fee242718f13166b772623b17c54243abe08a0528a3439","sha256":"7747014a893b4404fdac6cc1822eafdcf20ef4578c5c13f5fa669402fc4b1d1f","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:57:02.569229+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:AS1","text":"The central question examines whether divine infinity requires the presence of God in all things and all places without local inclusion or composition with creatures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:AS2","text":"The text shifts the notion of being in a thing from inclusion by a container to the presence of an agent cause immediately conserving created being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:AS3","text":"The argumentation is metaphysical-distinctive: divine presence is explained through the causality of being, power, knowledge, and the relation of an object to rational operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:AS4","text":"The argument proceeds from created being as God's proper effect to the intimacy of causality, then to virtual ubiquity and to the distinction of modes of presence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:AS5","text":"The movement is completed when being everywhere is converted from actual extension to a necessity that is per se and primary-universal: wherever anything could be, God would be wholly present there causally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A1","text":"God is being itself through His essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A2","text":"Created being is God's proper effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A3","text":"God not only produces created being at the beginning, but brings it about and conserves it as long as a thing has being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A4","text":"Every agent operating immediately is conjoined to its effect and touches it by its power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A5","text":"Being is formally the most intimate and deepest of all things that are in a thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A6","text":"God is in things as an agent cause, not as part of their essence nor as an accident.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A7","text":"What is incorporeal touches place through power, not through the contact of dimensive quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A8","text":"The totality of essence is not commensurate with the totality of place; therefore God is wholly in all and each individual thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A9","text":"All things are subject to divine power, lie open to divine knowledge, and are immediately present to the divine essence as the cause of being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A10","text":"God Himself is in the rational creature knowing and loving as the object of operation; this mode is constituted through grace.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A11","text":"Knowledge and will place a thing in God as known or willed, whereas acting power is applied to the external thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A12","text":"No superadded perfection of substance, apart from grace, constitutes God as the known and loved object in the creature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A13","text":"Whatever can be must be through God; therefore, whatever places are posited, God is wholly present in each.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:NX1","text":"God's being above all things through the excellence of His nature does not signify that He is causally absent from all things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:NX2","text":"God's being in things does not signify that God is contained by things; rather, God contains things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:NX3","text":"Divine action does not proceed to a distant thing without an order of intermediaries, because God acts immediately in all things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:NX4","text":"The deformity of guilt is not from God; therefore God is not said to be in demons absolutely except according to their created nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0025","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:NX5","text":"Divine ubiquity is not God's existence in a place as that of a located body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:NX6","text":"God, filling places, does not exclude other bodies, as one body is said to exclude another body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:NX7","text":"God's being in things through essence does not signify that God is of the essence of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0060","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:NX8","text":"Presence according to sight is not the same as presence according to substance causally standing by.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:NX9","text":"The special mode through grace does not arise from any accidental perfection whatsoever of the creature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:NX10","text":"Being everywhere primarily and per se does not belong to a being distributed through places by parts or that would be everywhere only under a supposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:D1","text":"Every thing, as long as it has being, remains in continuous causal dependence upon God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:D2","text":"God's presence is intimate to all things, because His causality directly reaches being itself, which is most intimate to all things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:D3","text":"God is in every place through the contact of power, because He gives being to place itself and gives being to located things by which they fill place.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:D4","text":"God fills every place without excluding bodies, because causal presence does not compete with quantitative presence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:D5","text":"Power, presence, and essence constitute three irreducible accounts of divine presence in things: subjection, manifestness, and the causality of being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:D6","text":"Knowledge and will are not modes by which God is said to be in things, because according to them things are more in God than God is in things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:D7","text":"Grace does not increase common divine causality, but establishes a special presence by which God becomes the object of rational knowledge and love.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:D8","text":"God's ubiquity primarily and per se requires the presence of the whole God in every possible place, not presence according to parts nor under a contingent cosmological condition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:SD1","text":"Divine omnipresence is an ontological form of conservation: finite being does not subsist after causation, but remains as long as divine causation immediately perseveres.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:SD2","text":"Space does not constitute an external boundary given to God, but an order of things whose being and locative power depend on God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A2","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:SD3","text":"Divine presence and the presence of creatures in the same place are not competitive, because one is causal and virtual, the other quantitative and local.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:SD4","text":"The threefold divine presence orders the universe under one principle in three ways: as subject to power, manifest to knowledge, and dependent on the cause of being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:SD5","text":"Grace changes the kind of the creature's relation to God, not because God causes the creature's being differently, but because the rational creature reaches God as an object actually or habitually.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:SD6","text":"The property of divine ubiquity is modal, not merely cosmographical: it follows not from the magnitude of the world, but from the necessity of divine causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ8:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:DNX1","text":"It must be false that a creature can be through itself or without the actual conserving divine influx.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:DNX2","text":"It must be false that lower things depend upon God only mediately, as though first creatures could sufficiently supply the ultimate origin and conservation of the rest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:DNX3","text":"It must be false that place or the universe is a receptacle ontologically external to divine presence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:DNX4","text":"It must be false that the preposition in is univocally local, since causality, knowledge, the object of operation, and local situation constitute diverse relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0064","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:DNX5","text":"It must be false that guilt or moral deformity separates created nature from the causality of being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:DNX6","text":"It must be false that any superadded perfection whatsoever produces a special divine indwelling.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:SNX1","text":"No region of being, no thing, and no possible place can subsist outside the scope of divine causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:SNX2","text":"No magnitude, extension, or distribution of parts can be the proper model of divine totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:SNX3","text":"No division of creatures, places, or effects can divide, circumscribe, or determine the divine essence locally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:SNX4","text":"No boundary of the actual world provides the measure of divine ubiquity, because God's presence depends not on given places, but on the possibility of any being whatsoever.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:FGS1","text":"The ambiguity of being in a thing is divided into local inclusion, causal presence, cognitive presence, and the presence of the object of operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:FGS2","text":"God is being itself → created being is God's proper effect → God continuously causes and conserves created being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:FGS3","text":"Immediate causality of being → virtual conjunction of agent to effect → God's intimate presence in every existing thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:FGS4","text":"Incorporeal virtual presence → place is touched through power → God is in every place without quantitative occupation or exclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:FGS5","text":"Common causality of being → power, presence, and essence → distinct subjection, manifestness, and ontological dependence of all creatures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:FGS6","text":"Rational knowledge and love through grace → God as the object of operation in the saints → a special mode of presence not reducible to common causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:FGS7","text":"Whatever can be must be through God → any positioning of places requires God → being everywhere primarily and per se is proper to God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:AF1","text":"Objections from transcendence, containment, distance, and deformity → distinction of causal presence from bodily situation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:AF2","text":"Created being as proper effect → immediate conservation of being → God's intimate presence in all things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:AF3","text":"Presence in all things → contact of power with places → ubiquity without quantitative extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:AF4","text":"Common ubiquity → distinction of power, presence, and essence → special mode of grace in the rational creature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:AF5","text":"Created candidates for ubiquity examined → exclusion of partitive and accidental presence → the divine property of being everywhere primarily and per se.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:ME1","text":"The relation of in is changed from the relation of container and contained into the relation of the cause of being to the conserved effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:ME2","text":"Place is reclassified as an effect of the created order and locative power, not as a boundary measuring divine presence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:ME3","text":"Divine presence is divided into three non-coincident dimensions: the dominion of power, openness to knowledge, and the immediate causality of being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:ME4","text":"The opposition between God's presence and the creature's corruption is dissolved through the distinction between created nature and the deformity of guilt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:ME5","text":"The difference between common presence and the indwelling of grace becomes visible as the difference between universal causality and the intentional relation of the known and loved object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:ME6","text":"Ubiquity ceases to signify maximal extension and signifies the necessity of divine presence in every possible being or place.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text directly asks whether God is in all things, everywhere, through essence, power, and presence, and whether ubiquity is proper to Him.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:ALT2","text":"Structural — The constitutive mechanism is the continuous divine influx by which the being of every thing is immediately caused and conserved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argumentation resolves spatially formulated objections through distinctions of relations: part, accident, cause, power, essence, presence, object, and condition per accidens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — A finite thing does not have being as a possession acquired once and for all, but as an effect whose permanence requires the presence of a transcendent and more intimate cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:MRT1","text":"The system of axioms suffices to restore the principal conclusion: God is intimately in all things and everywhere through the causality of being, not through bodily extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:MRT2","text":"The core of the argument depends most on the identity of God with being itself and on the immediate conservation of all created being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:MRT3","text":"The transition from causality to presence rests on the principle that an agent operating immediately must be conjoined to its effect; this principle is assumed from previously established physics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:MRT4","text":"The distinction of the contact of power from quantitative contact supports the transition to ubiquity, but requires retention of the metaphysics of the incorporeal, which the text does not demonstrate again here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:MRT5","text":"The apparent tension between God being in all things and God not being absolutely in demons is resolved by the distinction between created nature and the deformity of guilt; without this distinction, the universality of the proposition would waver.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0025","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:MRT6","text":"The distinction of power, presence, and essence coheres only if subjection, knowledge, and the causality of being designate really diverse relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:MRT7","text":"The property of ubiquity primarily and per se is established through counterfactual extension to any possible places; the force of the proof depends on the universality of the principle that nothing can be except through God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0083","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8:MRT8","text":"The singular mode of union is only indicated and deferred; therefore the classification of special modes of presence within this question deliberately remains incomplete.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ8:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PPQ8@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_PPQ8:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 09","title":"First Part · Question 8","context":"Chapter 09","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 8"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 8","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 9","order":10,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_PPQ9"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9@R001","sha256":"95dcb234ddf824d37be816a28578f25bc4862f2fe842acbd19b70b0dc54268fb","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:AS1","text":"Quaestio examinat utrum immutabilitas absoluta Deo competat et utrum talis immutabilitas soli Deo sit propria.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:AS2","text":"Argumentatio a ratione motus transit ad structuram entis: mutatio potentiam, compositionem aut perfectionem nondum possessam requirit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:AS3","text":"Deus, ut primum ens, actus purus, simplex et infinitus, nullam conditionem motus recipere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:AS4","text":"Voces motus Deo attributae distinguuntur a mutatione proprie dicta per analogiam operationis, processionis causalis et effectus in creaturis percepti.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:AS5","text":"Exclusivitas divinae immutabilitatis demonstratur per universalem contingentiam creaturae ad potentiam creantis et per potentias creaturis intrinsecas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:AS6","text":"Textus resolvit apparentem immobilitatem angelorum, beatorum et formarum distinguendo immutabilitatem secundum aliquem respectum ab immutabilitate omnino.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A1","text":"Deus est primum ens et actus purus absque omni potentiae permixtione.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A2","text":"Omne quod mutatur quocumque modo est aliquo modo in potentia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A3","text":"Omne motum aliquid retinet et aliquid transit; motus igitur compositionem implicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A4","text":"Deus est omnino simplex et in eo nulla compositio est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A5","text":"Omne motum per motum aliquid acquirit vel ad id pertingit ad quod prius non pertingebat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A6","text":"Deus infinitam totius esse perfectionem comprehendit; nihil igitur ei acquirendum vel attingendum restat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A7","text":"Creatura ex nihilo producitur et non subsistit nisi per continuam donationem esse a Deo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A8","text":"Quod ab alio esse accipit, in potentia illius alterius manet ad esse et ad non esse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A9","text":"Creaturae, praeter dependentiam a creatore, secundum proprios modos potentias activas vel passivas habent ad perfectionem, finem, locum vel variationem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A10","text":"Motus, processus et accessus Deo attributi non semper mutationem subiecti significant, sed interdum operationem, causalitatem exemplariter diffusivam aut effectum receptum in creatura.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:NX1","text":"Immutabilitas divina non est quies rei potentiam retinentis, sed absentia omnis potentiae ad alium actum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:NX2","text":"Deus non est compositum in quo idem secundum aliquid maneat et secundum aliquid transeat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:NX3","text":"Deus non est ens perfectionem novam acquirens neque ad terminum nondum possessum se extendens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:NX4","text":"Intelligere, velle et amare divina non sunt motus proprie dicti, quamvis motus latiore significatione nominari possint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:NX5","text":"Processus sapientiae divinae non est migratio Dei in res, sed diffusio similitudinis divinae secundum gradus participationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:NX6","text":"Accessus Dei ad creaturam et recessus ab ea non sunt motus locales Dei, sed diversitas perceptionis influxus bonitatis vel defectus creaturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:NX7","text":"Nulla creatura est omnino immutabilis, etiam si aliqua secundum esse, electionem vel aliquem alium respectum immobilis dicitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:D1","text":"Deus mutari non potest, quia mutatio potentiam requirit, Deus autem est actus purus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:D2","text":"Deus moveri non potest, quia motus compositionem implicat, Deo autem simplicitas absoluta competit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:D3","text":"Deus nullo motu proficere potest, quia motus acquisitionem importat, infinita autem plenitudo divina nihil ulterius recipere potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:D4","text":"Creatura est mutabilis saltem secundum potentiam creatoris, quia esse eius nec originarie nec conservate sibi ipsi sufficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:D5","text":"Diversae creaturarum immutabilitates sunt partiales, quia ad unum genus mutationis exclusum semper alius ordo potentiae vel dependentiae remanere potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:D6","text":"Attributiones scripturales motus divinae immutabilitati non adversantur, quia praedicatio analogica effectum vel operationem sine mutatione divina significare potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:D7","text":"Esse omnino immutabile soli Deo proprium est, quia solus nec intrinseca potentia nec extrinseca dependentia ad non esse vel ad alium actum subiacet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:SD1","text":"Immutabilitas divina non est proprietas isolata, sed nexus inseparabilis inter primatum essendi, purum actum, simplicitatem et infinitatem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:SD2","text":"Creatio et conservatio non constituunt eventum semel praeteritum, sed dependentiam ontologicam continuam qua creatura semper ad esse revocabilis manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:SD3","text":"Differentiae inter corpus corruptibile, corpus caeleste, substantiam incorpoream et beatum non sunt differentiae inter dependentem et independentem, sed inter diversos modos creaturae mutabilis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:SD4","text":"Linguarum motus applicatio ad Deum exigit transitum ab ordine physico subiecti mutabilis ad ordinem causalitatis et participationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet quod immaterialitas vel carentia materiae sufficiat ad immutabilitatem absolutam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet quod adeptio ultimi finis omnem modum mutabilitatis creaturae auferat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet quod invariabilitas formae significet subsistentem formam extra omnem variationem et dependentiam positam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet quod creatura conservetur per principium essendi sibi intrinsecum et ab actione divina separabile.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse debet quod praedicata motus de Deo univoce cum motu creaturae dicantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:SNX1","text":"Systema excludit in Deo omnem potentiam nondum actuatam; aliter infinitas perfectionis et immutabilitas simul stare non possent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:SNX2","text":"Systema excludit creaturam cui esse suum non sit semper pendens a potestate conservantis; talis creatura finitum et increatum confundere cogeret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:SNX3","text":"Systema excludit conceptum mutationis qui solum alterationem corporalem respicit, quia mutabilitas etiam secundum finem, electionem, locum et dependentiam causaliter describitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:FGS1","text":"Potentia ad actum ulteriorem → capacitas mutationis → negatio potentiae in actu puro → immutabilitas divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:FGS2","text":"Permanentia unius respectus cum transitu alterius → compositio in mobili → simplicitas divina → impossibilitas motus divini.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:FGS3","text":"Motus ut acquisitio → terminus prius non possessus → plenitudo infinita divina → exclusio omnis profectus divini.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:FGS4","text":"Creatio ex nihilo → conservatio continua → potentia creatoris ad non esse creaturae → mutabilitas universalis creaturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:FGS5","text":"Mutabilitas extrinseca communis + potentiae intrinsecae diversimode distributae → immutabilitates creaturae restrictae → immutabilitas absoluta soli Deo propria.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:FGS6","text":"Praedicatum divinum apparenter mobile → distinctio motus proprii et analogici → relatio ad operationem vel effectum creatum → conservatio immutabilitatis divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:AF1","text":"Obiecta mobilitatis divinae → definitio motus per potentiam, compositionem et acquisitionem → exclusio harum condicionum a Deo → immutabilitas omnino divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:AF2","text":"Praedicata scripturaria motus → reinterpretatio analogica secundum operationem, processionem et effectum receptum → dissolutio contradictionis semanticae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:AF3","text":"Apparentes creaturae immutabiles → distinctio mutabilitatis intrinsecae et extrinsecae → taxonomia modorum creatorum → proprium Dei esse omnino immutabilem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:ME1","text":"Motus reclassificatur: non solum translatio localis aut alteratio corporalis, sed omnis ordinatio potentiae ad actum vel ad finem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:ME2","text":"Immutabilitas distinguitur in partialem, secundum esse vel electionem, et absolutam, quae omnem potentiam et dependentiam excludit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:ME3","text":"Creaturae classificatio transit ab oppositione materiali/incorporali ad pluralitatem modorum mutabilitatis: corruptio substantialis, variatio accidentalis, motus localis, electio et dependentia creativa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:ME4","text":"Praedicatio de Deo reordinatur per distinctionem inter mutationem in Deo et manifestationem vel influxum Dei in creaturas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:ALT1","text":"Surface — Deus dicitur omnino immutabilis, et esse omnino immutabile soli Deo attribuitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:ALT2","text":"Structural — Mutabilitas ordinatur per potentiam, compositionem, acquisitionem perfectionis, finitudinem virtutis et dependentiam a conservatione divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Obiectiones non negantur simpliciter, sed termini motus, mobilitatis et invariabilitatis distinguuntur secundum rationem qua dicuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Deus constituitur ut actus non dependens, creatura vero ut ens cuius esse continuative a causa creatrice procedit et cuius stabilitas numquam independentia fit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:MRT1","text":"Axiomata de actu puro, potentia, simplicitate et plenitudine perfectionis sufficiunt ad conclusionem quod Deus nullo modo mutari potest restituendam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:MRT2","text":"Axiomata de creatione ex nihilo et conservatione continua sufficiunt ad exclusivitatem divinae immutabilitatis stabiliendam, quia omnem creaturam sub potentia creatoris constituunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:MRT3","text":"Taxonomia creaturarum argumentum complet, ostendens quod imminutio unius modi mutabilitatis non tollit omnes alios modos.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:MRT4","text":"Commissura fragilis manet in transitu ab eo quod creatura a Deo ad non esse reduci potest ad assertionem quod creatura propter hoc mutabilis dicitur, quia hic mutabilitas extrinseca latius sumitur quam mutatio in ipso subiecto.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:MRT5","text":"Commissura ulterior pendet a distinctione motus proprii et analogici: haec contradictiones linguisticas dissolvit, sed validitas eius ex doctrina praedicationis analogicae praesupposita dependet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:MRT6","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat, si immutabilitas non ut immobilitas physica, sed ut exclusio potentiae, compositionis, acquisitionis et dependentiae intelligitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T155728486447Z_119cf7cc","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 9","source_sha256":"95dcb234ddf824d37be816a28578f25bc4862f2fe842acbd19b70b0dc54268fb","sha256":"a9f5ab4f8b4322e4ccc4fd28a19480ebf72754c583bc2bfa90e8f93a3b0acb93","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:57:28.489959+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:AS1","text":"The question examines whether absolute immutability belongs to God and whether such immutability is proper to God alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:AS2","text":"The argument proceeds from the account of motion to the structure of being: change requires potency, composition, or a perfection not yet possessed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:AS3","text":"God, as the first being, pure act, simple and infinite, cannot admit any condition of motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:AS4","text":"Terms of motion attributed to God are distinguished from change properly so called through the analogy of operation, causal procession, and effect perceived in creatures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:AS5","text":"The exclusivity of divine immutability is demonstrated through the universal contingency of the creature upon creative power and through powers intrinsic to creatures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:AS6","text":"The text resolves the apparent immobility of angels, the blessed, and forms by distinguishing immutability in some respect from immutability absolutely.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A1","text":"God is the first being and pure act without any admixture of potency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A2","text":"Everything that is changed in any way is in potency in some way.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A3","text":"Everything moved retains something and passes away from something; motion therefore implies composition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A4","text":"God is altogether simple, and there is no composition in Him.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A5","text":"Everything moved acquires something through motion or attains that which it did not previously attain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A6","text":"God comprehends the infinite perfection of all being; therefore nothing remains to be acquired or attained by Him.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A7","text":"The creature is produced from nothing and does not subsist except through the continuous bestowal of being by God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A8","text":"That which receives being from another remains in the power of that other with respect to being and to non-being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A9","text":"Creatures, besides their dependence on the creator, according to their proper modes possess active or passive powers toward perfection, an end, place, or variation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A10","text":"Motion, procession, and approach attributed to God do not always signify a change of subject, but sometimes operation, causality diffusively exemplary, or an effect received in the creature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:NX1","text":"Divine immutability is not the rest of a thing retaining potency, but the absence of every potency toward another act.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:NX2","text":"God is not a composite in which the same remains according to one respect and passes away according to another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:NX3","text":"God is not a being acquiring a new perfection or extending Himself toward an end not yet possessed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:NX4","text":"Divine understanding, willing, and loving are not motions properly so called, although they can be called motions in a broader sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:NX5","text":"The procession of divine wisdom is not God's migration into things, but the diffusion of divine likeness according to degrees of participation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:NX6","text":"God's approach to the creature and withdrawal from it are not local motions of God, but a diversity of perception of the influx of goodness or of the creature's defect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:NX7","text":"No creature is altogether immutable, even if something is called immobile with respect to being, choice, or some other respect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:D1","text":"God cannot change, because change requires potency, whereas God is pure act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:D2","text":"God cannot be moved, because motion implies composition, whereas absolute simplicity belongs to God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A3","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:D3","text":"God cannot advance by any motion, because motion entails acquisition, whereas infinite divine fullness cannot receive anything further.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A5","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:D4","text":"The creature is mutable at least according to the power of the creator, because its being is not sufficient to itself either originally or preservatively.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:D5","text":"The diverse immutabilities of creatures are partial, because for one excluded kind of change another order of potency or dependence can always remain.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:D6","text":"Scriptural attributions of motion do not conflict with divine immutability, because analogical predication can signify an effect or operation without divine change.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:D7","text":"To be altogether immutable is proper to God alone, because He alone is subject neither to intrinsic potency nor to extrinsic dependence with respect to non-being or another act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A6","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:SD1","text":"Divine immutability is not an isolated property, but an inseparable nexus among the primacy of being, pure act, simplicity, and infinity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A1","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A4","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:SD2","text":"Creation and conservation do not constitute an event once past, but a continuous ontological dependence by which the creature remains always capable of being reduced to being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A7","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:SD3","text":"The differences among a corruptible body, a heavenly body, an incorporeal substance, and the blessed are not differences between dependent and independent, but among diverse modes of mutable creature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A8","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:SD4","text":"The application of the language of motion to God requires a transition from the physical order of the mutable subject to the order of causality and participation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PPQ9:A10","CH_GGVC_PPQ9:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:DNX1","text":"It must be false that immateriality or lack of matter suffices for absolute immutability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:DNX2","text":"It must be false that attainment of the ultimate end removes every mode of mutability from the creature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:DNX3","text":"It must be false that invariability of form signifies a subsisting form posited outside every variation and dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:DNX4","text":"It must be false that the creature is conserved through a principle of being intrinsic to itself and separable from divine action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:DNX5","text":"It must be false that predicates of motion are said of God univocally with the motion of the creature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:SNX1","text":"The system excludes in God every potency not yet actualized; otherwise infinity of perfection and immutability could not stand together.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:SNX2","text":"The system excludes a creature whose being is not always dependent upon the power of the conserver; such a creature would compel the confusion of the finite and the uncreated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:SNX3","text":"The system excludes a concept of change that concerns only bodily alteration, because mutability is also described causally according to end, choice, place, and dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:FGS1","text":"Potency toward a further act → capacity for change → negation of potency in pure act → divine immutability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:FGS2","text":"Permanence of one respect with the passing away of another → composition in what is mobile → divine simplicity → impossibility of divine motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:FGS3","text":"Motion as acquisition → an end not previously possessed → infinite divine fullness → exclusion of every divine advance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:FGS4","text":"Creation from nothing → continuous conservation → the creator's power over the creature's non-being → universal mutability of the creature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:FGS5","text":"Common extrinsic mutability + intrinsically distributed powers in diverse ways → restricted creaturely immutabilities → absolute immutability proper to God alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:FGS6","text":"Apparently mobile divine predicate → distinction between proper and analogical motion → relation to operation or created effect → preservation of divine immutability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:AF1","text":"Objections concerning divine mobility → definition of motion through potency, composition, and acquisition → exclusion of these conditions from God → divine immutability altogether.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:AF2","text":"Scriptural predicates of motion → analogical reinterpretation according to operation, procession, and received effect → dissolution of the semantic contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:AF3","text":"Apparently immutable creatures → distinction between intrinsic and extrinsic mutability → taxonomy of created modes → God's proper characteristic of being altogether immutable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:ME1","text":"Motion is reclassified: not only local translation or bodily alteration, but every ordering of potency toward act or toward an end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:ME2","text":"Immutability is distinguished into partial, according to being or choice, and absolute, which excludes every potency and dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:ME3","text":"The classification of creatures passes from the material/incorporeal opposition to a plurality of modes of mutability: substantial corruption, accidental variation, local motion, choice, and creative dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:ME4","text":"Predication concerning God is reordered through the distinction between change in God and the manifestation or influx of God in creatures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:ALT1","text":"Surface — God is said to be altogether immutable, and being altogether immutable is attributed to God alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:ALT2","text":"Structural — Mutability is ordered through potency, composition, acquisition of perfection, finitude of power, and dependence on divine conservation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Objections are not denied simply, but the terms motion, mobility, and invariability are distinguished according to the account under which they are said.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — God is constituted as act not dependent, whereas the creature is constituted as a being whose being proceeds continuously from the creative cause and whose stability never becomes independence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:MRT1","text":"The axioms concerning pure act, potency, simplicity, and fullness of perfection suffice to restore the conclusion that God can in no way change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:MRT2","text":"The axioms concerning creation from nothing and continuous conservation suffice to establish the exclusivity of divine immutability, because they place every creature under the power of the creator.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:MRT3","text":"The taxonomy of creatures completes the argument, showing that the diminution of one mode of mutability does not remove all other modes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:MRT4","text":"A fragile juncture remains in the transition from the fact that a creature can be reduced by God to non-being to the assertion that the creature is called mutable on that account, because here extrinsic mutability is taken more broadly than change in the subject itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0033","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:MRT5","text":"A further juncture depends on the distinction between proper and analogical motion: this resolves linguistic contradictions, but its validity depends upon the presupposed doctrine of analogical predication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0017","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0019","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0021","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9:MRT6","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text if immutability is understood not as physical immobility, but as the exclusion of potency, composition, acquisition, and dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PPQ9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PPQ9@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_PPQ9:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 10","title":"First Part · Question 9","context":"Chapter 10","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 9"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 9","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 10","order":11,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_PP_2"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PP_2@R001","sha256":"8cdc3f4c3c5faee7dd592d9b1d7aa21c340439668ae40163e7bc26fa53cc4a2b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AS1","text":"Quaestio centralis est quomodo modi durationis ex habitudine ad motum et immutabilitatem definiantur, Deo et creaturis distribuantur atque ad unitatem mensurae reducantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AS2","text":"Aeternitas constituitur non ut tempus infinitum, sed ut interminabilis permanentia successione carens et tota simul existens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AS3","text":"Argumentatio est metaphysica, reconstructiva et distinctiva: a mensura motus ascendit ad rationem permanentiae, deinde gradus participationis ordinat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AS4","text":"Motus epistemicus procedit a tempore sensibili ad aeternitatem simplicem per remotionem prioris, posterioris, principii et finis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AS5","text":"Motus divinus procedit ab immutabilitate Dei ad aeternitatem propriam, ac deinde ab identitate Dei cum suo esse ad identitatem cum sua aeternitate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AS6","text":"Motus classificatorius distinguit tempus, aevum et aeternitatem secundum transmutabilitatem intrinsecam, transmutabilitatem adiunctam et plenam immobilitatem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AS7","text":"Clausura argumenti unitatem aevi ex ordine graduato aeviternorum et primatu simplicissimi aeviterni conditionaliter deducit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0127","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A1","text":"Intellectus humanus simplicia cognoscit per composita et per remotionem compositionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A2","text":"Tempus est numerus prioris et posterioris in motu.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A3","text":"Quod omnino immutabile est successione, principio et fine caret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A4","text":"Aeternitas est duratio interminabilis, successione carens et tota simul existens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A5","text":"Ratio aeternitatis immutabilitatem consequitur sicut ratio temporis motum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A6","text":"Solus Deus est omnino immutabilis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A7","text":"Deus est suum esse uniforme.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A8","text":"Quod non est suum esse, non est sua duratio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A9","text":"Creatura aeternitatem participat secundum immutabilitatem a Deo receptam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A10","text":"Vera et necessaria aeterna sunt quatenus in intellectu divino aeterno sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","text":"Aeternitas est propria mensura esse permanentis, tempus autem propria mensura motus et esse transmutabilis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A12","text":"Differentia per se aeternitatis et temporis est simultaneitas totalis contra successionem, non absentia contra praesentiam principii et finis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13","text":"Aevum mensurat esse intransmutabile cui transmutatio actu vel potentia adiungitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A14","text":"Tempori prius et posterius intrinsece insunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A15","text":"Aevo prius et posterius intrinsece non insunt, sed ei coniungi possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A16","text":"Aeternitas prius et posterius neque habet neque compatitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A17","text":"Mensura communis plurium unitatem simpliciter accipit a primo et simplicissimo mensurato eiusdem ordinis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A18","text":"Secundum opinionem quam textus veriorem accipit, substantiae spirituales gradu et ordine disponuntur, ita ut unum primum aeviternum ceteris simplicius sit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0127","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX1","text":"Definitio negativa simplicis neque defectum rei neque negationem essentiae ponit, sed compositionem ab apprehensione intellectus removet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX2","text":"Totalitas aeternitatis partes non significat, sed absentiam defectus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX3","text":"Aeternum verum ad nudum esse non reducitur, quia vita operationem includit, secundum quam duratio apprehenditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX4","text":"Nomina temporalia et pluralia aeternitati attributa successionem realem non inferunt, sed translationem sermonis temporalis efficiunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX5","text":"Locutiones tota simul et perfecta non sunt redundantes: prior tempus, posterior nunc temporis imperfectum excludit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX6","text":"Possessio aeternitatis non dominium extrinsecum significat, sed stabilem, quietam et indeficientem habitudinem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX7","text":"Deus aeternitate non mensuratur quasi mensura ab eo distincta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX8","text":"Verba praesentis, praeteriti et futuri de Deo dicta mutationem divinam non important.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX9","text":"Creaturae, poenae et res diuturnae aeternae dictae veram aeternitatem eo solo nomine non possident.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX10","text":"Principium et finis differentiam per se neque inter aeternitatem et tempus neque inter aeternitatem, aevum et tempus constituunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0101","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX11","text":"Tempus et aeternitas non sunt mensurae unius generis neque partes unius quantitatis durationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX12","text":"Nunc temporis aeternitas non est, quia idem subiecto sed variabile ratione manet, dum aeternitas utroque modo eadem est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0086","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX13","text":"Aevum neque tempus diminutum neque aeternitas creata est; prioris et posterioris absentiam intrinsecam cum mutatione adiuncta coniungit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0101","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX14","text":"Prius et posterius sine innovatione et veteratione in ipsa mensura poni non possunt, quia successio alterius partis recessum et alterius adventum requirit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0102","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX15","text":"Unitas numeri abstracti, unitas primi principii remoti et unitas materiae primae unitatem temporis simpliciter non efficiunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0125","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX16","text":"Communis mensura non requirit ut primum mensuratum sit causa omnium aliorum mensuratorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0136","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX17","text":"Infinitas aevi ex parte posteriori infinitatem creatam actu totaliter possessam non significat, sed non-terminationem per tempus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0112","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D1","text":"Conceptus aeternitatis ex tempore generatur per remotionem motus, successionis et terminorum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D2","text":"Deo maxime competit esse aeternum, quia soli Deo competit plena immutabilitas.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D3","text":"Deus non tantum aeternus est, sed est sua aeternitas.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D4","text":"Aeternitas vere et proprie soli Deo convenit, creaturis autem secundum participationem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D5","text":"Interminabilitas sola participationem minimam constituit, intransmutabilitas autem esse vel operationis pleniorem participationem aeternitatis efficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D6","text":"Aeternitas veritatum necessariarum nullum ens aeternum extra Deum constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D7","text":"Etiamsi tempus initio et fine careret, aeternitas non fieret, quia successio maneret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D8","text":"Tempus etiam quietem mensurat, quatenus quies est rei naturaliter transmutabilis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D9","text":"Aevum est medium non per quantitatem durationis, sed per gradum recessionis ab esse omnino permanente.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D10","text":"Eadem substantia spiritualis secundum esse naturale aevo, secundum operationes successivas tempore, secundum visionem gloriae participatione aeternitatis mensurari potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D11","text":"Unitas temporis ex unitate primi motus, non ex multitudine motuum mensuratorum, procedit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0126","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D12","text":"Unum aevum sequitur si aeviterna graduantur sub uno primo et simplicissimo aeviterno; aequalitas plurium primorum pluralitatem aevorum inferret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0127","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD1","text":"Taxonomia durationis qualitativa est: modus essendi et mutabilitatis, non longitudo continuationis, genus mensurae determinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD2","text":"Aeternitas ex tempore per extensionem infinitam generari nequit, quia successio quantitatively extensa successionem non aufert.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD3","text":"Deus intra durationem communem non collocatur, quia esse divinum, duratio divina et mensura aeternitatis realiter non distinguuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD4","text":"Praedicatio aeternitatis de creaturis gradus analogicos exprimit, non possessionem univocam eiusdem modi essendi divini.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD5","text":"Mensura durationis subiectum simpliciter non classificat, sed aspectum subiecti cui mutabilitas vel permanentia convenit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD6","text":"Unitas mensurae ex ordine ad primum mensuratum, non ex aggregatione recipientium neque ex communitate causae remotae, constituitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX1","text":"Systema tacite excludit genus durationis univocum sub quo Deus, aeviterna et temporalia aequaliter collocarentur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX7","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX2","text":"Duratio non est vas autonomum in quo entia collocantur, sed ratio consequens modum essendi rei mensuratae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX3","text":"Sempiternitas successiva non potest plenitudinem simultaneam aeternitatis supplere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX10","CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX4","text":"Nulla creatura ex seipsa permanentiam aeternam possidet; omnis stabilitas creata influxum immutabilitatis divinae praesupponit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX5","text":"Forma grammatica temporalis nullam mutationem ontologicam in re significata necessario probat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX4","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX6","text":"Aevum per mixtionem partium temporis et aeternitatis constitui non potest; medium est secundum habitudinem esse ad mutationem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX13","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0101","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX7","text":"Unitas aevi aequalitatem planam substantiarum spiritualium tacite excludit et ordinem prioris ac simplicioris requirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A18","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0127","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SNX1","text":"Inadmissibilis est prospectus neuter ex quo Deus et creaturae eadem mensura extrinseca commensurentur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX1","CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SNX2","text":"Systema nullam sempiternitatem creatam autonomam relinquit: quidquid extra Deum permanet, participatum et dependens permanet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX4","CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SNX3","text":"Possibilitas fieri temporalis in Deo praecluditur, quia variationes temporum ad sermonem et ad res inclusas, non ad esse divinum, referuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SNX4","text":"Impossibile habetur medium quod successionem intrinsecam possideat sine recessu prioris et innovatione posterioris.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX14","CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0102","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SNX5","text":"Conclusio unius aevi vim accipit ex possibilitate tacite praeclusa plurium aeviternorum aequaliter primorum; hac exclusione remota, conclusio non manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A18","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D12","CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0127","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS1","text":"Machina epistemica sic operatur: compositum sensibile → motus numeratus → tempus → remotio successionis et terminorum → apprehensio aeternitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS2","text":"Machina ontologica constituit has correspondentias: motus → prius et posterius → tempus; immutabilitas adiuncta mobilitati → aevum; immutabilitas absoluta → aeternitas.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS3","text":"Applicatio ad Deum procedit: plena immutabilitas → aeternitas propria → identitas esse et durationis → Deus est sua aeternitas.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS4","text":"Applicatio ad creaturas procedit: immutabilitas recepta → participatio aeternitatis → gradus secundum permanentiam esse vel operationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS5","text":"Operator classificationis est: esse transmutabile → tempus; esse intransmutabile cum mutatione adiuncta → aevum; esse nec mutabile nec mutabilitati adiunctum → aeternitas.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS6","text":"Operator aspectualis permittit ut una res diversis mensuris subiaceat secundum esse, operationem et participationem finis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS7","text":"Operator unitatis procedit: primum simplicissimum mensuratum → mensura communis → multiplicitas mensuratorum sine multiplicatione mensurae; in aevo haec deductio ordine graduato substantiarum spiritualium condicionatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A18","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0127","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS8","text":"Obiectiones dissolvuntur distinguendo proprium a participato, per se a per accidens, intrinsecum ab adiuncto, idem subiecto ab eodem ratione, atque praedicationem temporalem a mutatione reali.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX4","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX9","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX10","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX12","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF1","text":"Catena initialis est: quaestio de aeternitate → exemplar cognoscibile temporis → remotio successionis → definitio aeternitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF2","text":"Definitio aeternitatis → objectiones contra terminos negativos et metaphoricos → distinctiones semanticae → stabilitas definitionis Boethianae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX1","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX2","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX3","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX5","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF3","text":"Immutabilitas → applicatio ad Deum → aeternitas divina → identitas Dei cum sua aeternitate.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF4","text":"Proprietas divina → difficultas praedicationum aeternarum de creaturis → distinctio proprietatis et participationis → gradus aeternitatis participatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF5","text":"Aeternitas et tempus ut mensurae → reiectio differentiae ex solis terminis → differentia ex simultaneitate et successione.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF6","text":"Polaritas aeternitatis et temporis → quaestio de creatura stabili sed mutabili secundum adiuncta → constitutio aevi ut medii.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0101","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF7","text":"Pluralitas aeviternorum → quaestio de unitate mensurae → analogia primi motus → primum aeviternum → unum aevum conditionaliter positum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A18","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0126","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0127","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ME1","text":"Tabula quantitativa durationis, in qua aeternitas esset tempus longissimum, substituitur tabula modali secundum gradus mutabilitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD1","CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ME2","text":"Sempiternitas et aeternitas separantur: prima continuationem sine termino, altera absentiam successionis significat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ME3","text":"Mensura durationis reclassificatur ex quantitate extrinseca in rationem consequentem modum essendi mensurati.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ME4","text":"Praedicata aeterna de creaturis dividuntur in diuturnitatem, interminabilitatem, intransmutabilitatem esse, intransmutabilitatem operationis et participationem visionis divinae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ME5","text":"Aevum aperit tertium campum inter temporalitatem et aeternitatem, definitum non mixtione sed distinctione esse permanentis et mutationis adiunctae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:D9","CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0101","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ME6","text":"Subiectum idem in plures ordines durationis distribui potest, quia mensura aspectum essendi potius quam rem indivisam absolute sequitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ME7","text":"Unitas temporis et aevi fit effectus ordinis ad primum simplicissimum, non effectus similitudinis, simultanei initii vel causalitatis mutuae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD6","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0132","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ME8","text":"Sermones scripturales temporales et plurales ex testimoniis mutationis ontologicae in modos metaphoricam vel participatam praedicationem exprimendi transferuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX4","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX8","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ALT1","text":"Gradus superficiei definit aeternitatem, attribuit eam Deo, distinguit tempus et aevum atque quaerit unitatem aevi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:AS1","CH_GGVC_PP_2:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ALT2","text":"Gradus structuralis durationis genera ex relatione inter esse, motum, successionem et immutabilitatem producit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS2","CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ALT3","text":"Gradus metastrategicus oppositiones dissolvit mutando principium classificationis: a longitudine ad modum essendi, a praedicatione ad participationem, ab absoluto subiecto ad aspectum mensuratum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS8","CH_GGVC_PP_2:ME1","CH_GGVC_PP_2:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ALT4","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis praesupponit realitatem ordinatam a puro esse immobili per esse stabile cum mutatione adiuncta ad esse transmutabile et successivum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:MRT1","text":"Axiomata extracta sufficiunt ad restituendas definitionem aeternitatis, attributionem divinam, participationem creatam, distinctionem trium mensurarum et conclusionem conditionalem de uno aevo.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:MRT2","text":"Duae praemissae principales intra quaestionem non probantur: absoluta immutabilitas Dei ex disputatione antecedenti assumitur, ordo graduatus substantiarum spiritualium ad probationem subsequentem remittitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:MRT3","text":"Cohaerentia firmissima est in correspondentia motus–successionis–temporis et immutabilitatis–simultaneitatis–aeternitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:MRT4","text":"Commissura semantica manet in notione mensurae: aeternitas esse divinum mensurat, sed Deus non mensuratur ab alio; difficultas identitate Dei cum sua aeternitate solvitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX7","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:MRT5","text":"Structura aevi distinctione stricta inter mutationem intrinsecam esse et mutationem adiunctam sustinetur; hac distinctione remota, medium in contradictionem relaberetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX14","CH_GGVC_PP_2:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:MRT6","text":"Compatibilitas aevi totius simul cum initio, cessatione possibili et mutationibus adiunctis extrinsecam dependentiam a structura intrinseca durationis separare requirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX17","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0101","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:MRT7","text":"Conclusio unius aevi est pars infirmior machinae, quia ex opinione hierarchica nondum hic demonstrata pendet et sub hypothesi plurium primorum aequalium cessaret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A18","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D12","CH_GGVC_PP_2:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0127","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:MRT8","text":"Solutio de veritatibus necessariis doctrinam veritatis in intellectu et exclusionem aeternorum abstractorum extra intellectum divinum praesupponit, sed eas intra quaestionem non explicat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:MRT9","text":"Responsiones ad locutiones scripturales vi distinctionum inter proprietatem, participationem, metaphoram et aequivocationem lexicalem nituntur; argumentum eas non tam negat quam ontologice reclas­sificat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX4","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX8","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:MRT10","text":"Restitutio motum verum textus servat: tempus ut via cognitionis → aeternitas ut immobilitas tota simul → Deus ut sua aeternitas → participationes creatae → aevum medium → unitas mensurae ex ordine ad primum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF1","CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF3","CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF4","CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF6","CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T155800828719Z_ddcb16f9","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 10","source_sha256":"8cdc3f4c3c5faee7dd592d9b1d7aa21c340439668ae40163e7bc26fa53cc4a2b","sha256":"be95fee812e217e4ce7f3d8a612db92bdee692d09e893a806fec298e3a92dabf","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:58:00.829750+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AS1","text":"The central question is how modes of duration are defined from their relation to motion and immutability, distributed to God and creatures, and reduced to the unity of measure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AS2","text":"Eternity is constituted not as infinite time, but as interminable permanence lacking succession and existing all at once.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AS3","text":"The argumentation is metaphysical, reconstructive, and distinctive: from the measure of motion it ascends to the account of permanence, then orders the degrees of participation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AS4","text":"The epistemic movement proceeds from sensible time to simple eternity through the removal of before, after, beginning, and end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AS5","text":"The divine movement proceeds from God's immutability to eternity proper, and then from God's identity with His own being to His identity with His own eternity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AS6","text":"The classificatory movement distinguishes time, aevum, and eternity according to intrinsic mutability, associated mutability, and complete immobility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AS7","text":"The closure of the argument conditionally deduces the unity of aevum from the graded order of aeviternal things and the primacy of the simplest aeviternal thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0127","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A1","text":"The human intellect knows simple things through composite things and through the removal of composition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A2","text":"Time is the number of before and after in motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A3","text":"What is altogether immutable lacks succession, beginning, and end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A4","text":"Eternity is interminable duration, lacking succession and existing all at once.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A5","text":"The account of eternity follows immutability as the account of time follows motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A6","text":"God alone is altogether immutable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A7","text":"God is His own uniform being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A8","text":"What is not its own being is not its own duration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A9","text":"A creature participates in eternity according to the immutability received from God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A10","text":"True and necessary things are eternal insofar as they are in the eternal divine intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","text":"Eternity is the proper measure of permanent being, whereas time is the proper measure of motion and mutable being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A12","text":"The per se difference between eternity and time is total simultaneity as opposed to succession, not absence as opposed to presence of beginning and end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13","text":"Aevum measures immutable being to which change is joined in act or potency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A14","text":"Before and after are intrinsically present in time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A15","text":"Before and after are not intrinsically present in aevum, but can be joined to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A16","text":"Eternity neither has nor admits before and after.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A17","text":"The common measure of several things receives its unity simply from the first and simplest measured thing of the same order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:A18","text":"According to the opinion that the text takes to be truer, spiritual substances are disposed by grade and order, such that one first aeviternal thing is simpler than the rest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0127","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX1","text":"The negative definition of the simple posits neither a defect of the thing nor a negation of essence, but removes composition from the intellect's apprehension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX2","text":"The totality of eternity does not signify parts, but the absence of defect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX3","text":"The true eternal is not reduced to bare being, because life includes operation, according to which duration is apprehended.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX4","text":"Temporal and plural names attributed to eternity do not entail real succession, but effect a transference of temporal speech.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX5","text":"The expressions all at once and perfect are not redundant: the former excludes time, the latter the imperfect now of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX6","text":"The possession of eternity does not signify extrinsic dominion, but a stable, tranquil, and indeficient relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX7","text":"God is not measured by eternity as though by a measure distinct from Him.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX8","text":"Words of the present, past, and future said of God do not imply divine change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX9","text":"Creatures, punishments, and long-lasting things called eternal do not thereby possess true eternity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX10","text":"Beginning and end do not constitute a per se difference either between eternity and time or between eternity, aevum, and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0101","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX11","text":"Time and eternity are not measures of one genus nor parts of one quantity of duration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX12","text":"The now of time is not eternity, because it remains the same in subject but variable in account, whereas eternity is the same in both ways.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0086","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX13","text":"Aevum is neither diminished time nor created eternity; it combines the intrinsic absence of before and after with associated change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0101","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX14","text":"Before and after cannot be posited in the measure itself without innovation and aging, because succession requires the departure of one part and the arrival of another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0102","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX15","text":"The unity of abstract number, the unity of the first remote principle, and the unity of prime matter do not simply effect the unity of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0125","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX16","text":"A common measure does not require that the first measured thing be the cause of all other measured things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0136","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX17","text":"The infinity of aevum on the side of what follows does not signify a created infinity totally possessed in act, but non-termination through time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0112","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D1","text":"The concept of eternity is generated from time through the removal of motion, succession, and limits.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D2","text":"To God it belongs most of all to be eternal, because complete immutability belongs to God alone.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D3","text":"God is not only eternal, but is His own eternity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D4","text":"Eternity belongs truly and properly to God alone, but to creatures according to participation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D5","text":"Interminability alone constitutes minimal participation, whereas immutability of being or operation effects fuller participation in eternity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D6","text":"The eternity of necessary truths constitutes no eternal being outside God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D7","text":"Even if time lacked beginning and end, it would not become eternity, because succession would remain.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D8","text":"Time also measures rest, insofar as rest belongs to a naturally mutable thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D9","text":"Aevum is intermediate not through the quantity of duration, but through the degree of recession from altogether permanent being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D10","text":"The same spiritual substance can be measured by aevum according to natural being, by time according to successive operations, and by participation in eternity according to the vision of glory.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D11","text":"The unity of time proceeds from the unity of the first motion, not from the multitude of measured motions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0126","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:D12","text":"One aevum follows if aeviternal things are graded under one first and simplest aeviternal thing; equality of several first things would entail a plurality of aevums.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0127","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD1","text":"The taxonomy of duration is qualitative: the mode of being and mutability, not the length of continuation, determines the kind of measure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD2","text":"Eternity cannot be generated from time by infinite extension, because quantitatively extended succession does not remove succession.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD3","text":"God is not placed within common duration, because divine being, divine duration, and the measure of eternity are not really distinct.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD4","text":"The predication of eternity of creatures expresses analogical degrees, not univocal possession of the same divine mode of being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD5","text":"The measure of duration does not classify the subject simply, but the aspect of the subject to which mutability or permanence belongs.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD6","text":"The unity of measure is constituted from order to the first measured thing, not from aggregation of recipients nor from community of a remote cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX1","text":"The system tacitly excludes a univocal genus of duration under which God, aeviternal things, and temporal things would be equally placed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX7","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX2","text":"Duration is not an autonomous vessel in which beings are placed, but an account consequent upon the mode of being of the measured thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX3","text":"Successive sempiternity cannot supply the simultaneous fullness of eternity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX10","CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX4","text":"No creature possesses eternal permanence from itself; every created stability presupposes an influx of divine immutability.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX5","text":"A temporal grammatical form does not necessarily prove ontological change in the thing signified.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX4","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX6","text":"Aevum cannot be constituted by a mixture of parts of time and eternity; it is intermediate according to the relation of being to change.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX13","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0101","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX7","text":"The unity of aevum tacitly excludes a flat equality of spiritual substances and requires an order of the prior and simpler.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A18","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0127","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SNX1","text":"Inadmissible is a neutral standpoint from which God and creatures are commensurated by the same extrinsic measure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX1","CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SNX2","text":"The system leaves no autonomous created sempiternity: whatever remains outside God remains participated and dependent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX4","CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SNX3","text":"The possibility of temporal becoming in God is precluded, because variations of tenses are referred to speech and to included things, not to divine being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SNX4","text":"Impossible is held to be an intermediate that possesses intrinsic succession without the departure of what is prior and the innovation of what is posterior.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX14","CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0102","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:SNX5","text":"The conclusion of one aevum receives force from the tacitly precluded possibility of several equally first aeviternal things; once this exclusion is removed, the conclusion does not remain.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A18","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D12","CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0127","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS1","text":"The epistemic machine operates thus: sensible composite → numbered motion → time → removal of succession and limits → apprehension of eternity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS2","text":"The ontological machine establishes these correspondences: motion → before and after → time; immutability joined to mobility → aevum; absolute immutability → eternity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS3","text":"The application to God proceeds: complete immutability → eternity proper → identity of being and duration → God is His own eternity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS4","text":"The application to creatures proceeds: received immutability → participation in eternity → degrees according to permanence of being or operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS5","text":"The operator of classification is: mutable being → time; immutable being with associated change → aevum; being neither mutable nor associated with mutability → eternity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS6","text":"The aspectual operator permits one thing to be subject to diverse measures according to being, operation, and participation in the end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS7","text":"The operator of unity proceeds: first simplest measured thing → common measure → multiplicity of measured things without multiplication of the measure; in aevum this deduction is conditioned by the graded order of spiritual substances.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A18","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0127","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS8","text":"Objections are dissolved by distinguishing the proper from the participated, per se from per accidens, the intrinsic from the associated, the same in subject from the same in account, and temporal predication from real change.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX4","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX9","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX10","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX12","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF1","text":"The initial chain is: question concerning eternity → knowable model of time → removal of succession → definition of eternity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF2","text":"Definition of eternity → objections against negative and metaphorical terms → semantic distinctions → stability of the Boethian definition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX1","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX2","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX3","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX5","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF3","text":"Immutability → application to God → divine eternity → God's identity with His own eternity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF4","text":"Divine property → difficulty of eternal predications concerning creatures → distinction of property and participation → degrees of participated eternity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF5","text":"Eternity and time as measures → rejection of difference from limits alone → difference from simultaneity and succession.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF6","text":"Polarity of eternity and time → question concerning a creature stable but mutable according to associated features → constitution of aevum as intermediate.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0101","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF7","text":"Plurality of aeviternal things → question of unity of measure → analogy of the first motion → first aeviternal thing → one aevum conditionally posited.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A18","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0126","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0127","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ME1","text":"A quantitative map of duration, in which eternity would be the longest time, is replaced by a modal map according to degrees of mutability.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD1","CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ME2","text":"Sempiternity and eternity are separated: the former signifies continuation without limit, the latter absence of succession.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ME3","text":"The measure of duration is reclassified from extrinsic quantity into an account consequent upon the mode of being of what is measured.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ME4","text":"Eternal predicates concerning creatures are divided into long duration, interminability, immutability of being, immutability of operation, and participation in the divine vision.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ME5","text":"Aevum opens a third field between temporality and eternity, defined not by mixture but by the distinction between permanent being and associated change.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:D9","CH_GGVC_PP_2:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0101","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ME6","text":"The same subject can be distributed among several orders of duration, because the measure follows an aspect of being rather than the absolutely undivided thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ME7","text":"The unity of time and aevum becomes an effect of order to the first simplest thing, not an effect of likeness, simultaneous beginning, or mutual causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD6","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0126","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0132","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ME8","text":"Temporal and plural scriptural expressions are transferred from testimonies of ontological change into modes of expressing metaphorical or participated predication.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX4","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX8","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ALT1","text":"The surface level defines eternity, attributes it to God, distinguishes time and aevum, and seeks the unity of aevum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:AS1","CH_GGVC_PP_2:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ALT2","text":"The structural level produces kinds of duration from the relation among being, motion, succession, and immutability.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS2","CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ALT3","text":"The metastrategic level dissolves oppositions by changing the principle of classification: from length to mode of being, from predication to participation, from absolute subject to measured aspect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:FGS8","CH_GGVC_PP_2:ME1","CH_GGVC_PP_2:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:ALT4","text":"The ontological and temporal level presupposes a reality ordered from pure immobile being through stable being with associated change to mutable and successive being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms suffice to restore the definition of eternity, divine attribution, created participation, the distinction of the three measures, and the conditional conclusion concerning one aevum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:MRT2","text":"Two principal premises are not proved within the question: God's absolute immutability is assumed from prior disputation, and the graded order of spiritual substances is deferred to subsequent proof.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:MRT3","text":"The strongest coherence lies in the correspondence of motion–succession–time and immutability–simultaneity–eternity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:MRT4","text":"A semantic juncture remains in the notion of measure: eternity measures divine being, but God is not measured by another; the difficulty is resolved by God's identity with His own eternity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX7","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:MRT5","text":"The structure of aevum is sustained by the strict distinction between intrinsic change of being and associated change; with this distinction removed, the intermediate would fall back into contradiction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX14","CH_GGVC_PP_2:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:MRT6","text":"The compatibility of aevum existing all at once with a beginning, possible cessation, and associated changes requires separating extrinsic dependence from the intrinsic structure of duration.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX17","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0101","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:MRT7","text":"The conclusion of one aevum is the weaker part of the machine, because it depends upon a hierarchical opinion not yet demonstrated here and would cease under the hypothesis of several equal first things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A18","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D12","CH_GGVC_PP_2:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0127","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:MRT8","text":"The solution concerning necessary truths presupposes the doctrine of truth in the intellect and the exclusion of eternal abstractions outside the divine intellect, but does not explain them within the question.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_2:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:MRT9","text":"Responses to scriptural expressions rely on the force of distinctions among property, participation, metaphor, and lexical equivocation; the argument does not so much deny them as ontologically reclassify them.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX4","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX8","CH_GGVC_PP_2:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_2:MRT10","text":"The reconstruction preserves the text's true movement: time as a path of cognition → eternity as immobility existing all at once → God as His own eternity → created participations → intermediate aevum → unity of measure from order to the first.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF1","CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF3","CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF4","CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF6","CH_GGVC_PP_2:AF7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_2:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PP_2@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_PP_2:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 11","title":"First Part · Question 10","context":"Chapter 11","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 10"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 10","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 11","order":12,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_PP_3"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PP_3@R001","sha256":"d3979f88610251b04de8f49915c95ca1a8452ed92d295ff285f7c8aa18f2d2c6","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AS1","text":"Quaestio unitatem divinam constituit per distinctionem inter unum transcendens, quod ens indivisum significat, et unum numericum, quod quantitatem fundat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AS2","text":"Negatio divisionis explicat cur unum cum ente convertatur sine additione rei, quamvis supra ens aliquid secundum rationem addat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AS3","text":"Oppositio unius et multorum resolvitur per duplicem ordinem: unum numericum opponitur numero ut mensura mensurato, unum transcendens multitudini ut indivisum diviso.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AS4","text":"Ordo cognitionis transit ab ente ad negationem identitatis, divisionem, unitatem et multitudinem, ita ut multitudo unitatem praesupponat etiam si divisio prius concipiatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AS5","text":"Unitas Dei demonstratur ex identitate naturae et suppositi divini, ex infinitate perfectionis et ex reductione ordinis mundani ad unum principium per se.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AS6","text":"Deus maxime unus concluditur quia est simul maxime ens et omni modo indivisus, non quia unitas ut mera privatio per se gradus recipiat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A1","text":"Unum convertibile cum ente nihil reale supra ens addit, sed ens indivisum significat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A2","text":"Esse cuiuslibet rei in indivisione consistit; res suum esse servans suam unitatem servat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A3","text":"Unum quod est principium numeri rem quantitatis addit supra substantiam entis et non convertitur cum ente.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A4","text":"Eadem res potest secundum diversos respectus una et multa esse; unum simpliciter ex indivisione essentiali vel actuali, multa simpliciter ex divisione essentiali sequuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A5","text":"Multitudo sub ente esse non potest nisi aliquo modo sub uno contineatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A6","text":"Unum transcendens multitudini opponitur privative, ut indivisum diviso; unum numericum numero opponitur ut mensura mensurato.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A7","text":"Privatio non totaliter esse tollit, quia negatio in subiecto fundatur; ideo multitudo unum aliquod retinet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A8","text":"Multitudo ex unitatibus constituitur secundum entitatem earum, non secundum indivisionem qua multitudini opponuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A9","text":"Intellectus ens prius apprehendit, deinde non-identitatem, divisionem, unitatem et postremo multitudinem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A10","text":"Deus est sua natura, ita ut Deus et hic Deus secundum idem sint; quod individuat Deum pluribus communicari non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A11","text":"Perfectio essendi infinita nullam differentiam realem inter plures deos admittit, quia differentia perfectionem uni adderet vel alteri subtraheret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A12","text":"Ordinatio plurium in unum ordinem causam unam per se requirit, quia multa unum causant tantum per accidens quatenus aliquo modo unum sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A13","text":"Praedicata privativa de Deo non imperfectionem divinam ponunt, sed modum cognitionis humanae per remotionem exprimunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A14","text":"Maxima unitas requirit maximam entitatem et maximam indivisionem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A15","text":"Deus est ipsum esse subsistens et omni modo simplex; neque actu neque potentia secundum ullum divisionis modum dividitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:NX1","text":"Unum transcendens non est res addita enti neque differentia contrahens ens ad genus determinatum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:NX2","text":"Unum convertibile cum ente non est unum numericum, nec principium numeri ad Deum transfertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:NX3","text":"Multitudo non est pura imminutio entis, quia privatio in subiecto fundatur et multitudo aliquod unum participat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:NX4","text":"Unum et multa non ita opponuntur ut alterum de altero nullo modo praedicari possit; praedicatio fit secundum simpliciter et secundum quid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:NX5","text":"Unitas ex qua multitudo constituitur non operatur formaliter ut indivisio opposita multitudini, sed materialiter quatenus unitas est ens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:NX6","text":"Unitas divina non est imminutio vel privatio realis in Deo, licet a cognitione humana per negationem divisionis enuntietur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:NX7","text":"Maxime unum non significat punctum aut unitatem mathematicam, quae esse in subiecto habent et propterea maxime entia non sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:D1","text":"Convertibilitas entis et unius unitatem constituit transcendentalem proprietatem omnis entis, non genus particulare entium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:D2","text":"Compositum non est in actu dum partes manent divisae; unitas compositi est conditio ipsius esse compositi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:D3","text":"Distinctio inter unum simpliciter et unum secundum quid impedit ne divisio accidentalis, potentialis vel numerica identitatem rei simpliciter destruat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:D4","text":"Multitudo definiri per unitatem potest, sed unitas multitudinem in sua definitione non requirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:D5","text":"Prioritas divisionis ad unitatem non est prioritas rei, sed prioritas quoad apprehensionem intellectus composita per simplicia cognoscentis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:D6","text":"Plures dii esse non possunt, quia pluralitas aut naturam divinam communicabilem supponeret aut perfectionem infinitam distributam et imminutam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:D7","text":"Unitas ordinis mundani non est tantum concordia effectuum, sed signum causalitatis primi principii unius per se.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:D8","text":"Praedicatio divina unius est metaphysica, non mathematica, et negativa secundum modum cognoscendi sine negatione perfectionis divinae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:D9","text":"Deus maxime unus est quia ipsa plenitudo essendi et simplicitas absoluta coincidunt in eodem subsistente.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:SD1","text":"Unitas transcendentalis non est proprietas extrinseca rei, sed nomen ontologicum ipsius stabilitatis qua res est et in se manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_3:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:SD2","text":"Pluralitas non constituit alterum principium contra unitatem, sed modum entium divise existentium qui unitatem totius, subiecti, speciei, generis vel principii retinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:SD3","text":"Demonstratio unius Dei connectit ordinem metaphysicum, perfectionem absolutam et ordinem cosmicum in unam exclusionem pluralitatis principiorum primorum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:SD4","text":"Gradus unitatis mensurantur non per intensionem privationis solam, sed per gradum essendi et immunitatis a divisione.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:DNX1","text":"Systema negat rem indigere principio extrinseco quo una fiat, quia tale principium rursus unum esse deberet et regressus in infinitum sequeretur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:DNX2","text":"Systema negat multitudinem posse intelligi ut absolutam dispersionem, quia omnis multitudo unitatem aliquam participare debet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:DNX3","text":"Systema negat causam primam esse unum membrum intra seriem plurium principiorum coordinatarum; primum ordinans est unum per se.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:DNX4","text":"Systema negat cognitionem humanam posse perfectionem divinam directe positive comprehensam possidere; unitas divina per remotionem divisionis enuntiatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:SNX1","text":"Impossibilis est conceptus Dei tamquam individui numerabilis inter alia individua, quia numerus quantitatem et materiam secundum esse implicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:SNX2","text":"Impossibilis est pluralitas absolutorum infinite perfectorum, quia differentia inter absoluta perfectionem necessario limitaret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:SNX3","text":"Impossibilis est maxima unitas quae solum absentiam divisionis dicat et plenitudinem essendi non includat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0072","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:FGS1","text":"Ens → negatio divisionis → unum transcendens, quod ens non contrahit sed indivisionem eius manifestat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:FGS2","text":"Distinctio unius transcendentalis ab uno numerico → exclusio confusionis metaphysicae et mathematicae → praedicatio divinae unitatis non-quantitativa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:FGS3","text":"Ens apprehensum → non-identitas → divisio → unum → multitudo, ita ut multitudo rationem unius structuralius praesupponat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:FGS4","text":"Simplicitas divina + infinitas perfectionis + unitas ordinis mundani → exclusio plurium deorum → Deus unus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:FGS5","text":"Ipsum esse subsistens + simplicitas omni modo indivisibilis → maximum ens et maxime indivisum → Deus maxime unus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AF1","text":"Ambiguitas unius → distinctio transcendentis et numerici → negatio additionis realis supra ens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AF2","text":"Indivisio entis → convertibilitas entis et unius → subordinatio multitudinis ad participationem unius.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AF3","text":"Divisio et indivisio → oppositio privativa unius et multorum → ordinatio cognitionis ab ente ad multitudinem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AF4","text":"Unitas transcendentalis → simplicitas, perfectio infinita et causalitas ordinans → demonstratio Dei unius.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AF5","text":"Deus unus → esse subsistens et simplicitas absoluta → affirmatio Dei maxime unius.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:ME1","text":"Unitas reclassificatur ex accidente quantitativo in proprietatem transcendentalem convertibilem cum ente.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:ME2","text":"Oppositio unius et multorum reclassificatur ex contradictione simplici in oppositionem privativam compatibilem cum praedicatione secundum quid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:ME3","text":"Multitudo fit intelligibilis non ut negatio pura unitatis, sed ut pluralitas fundata in entitate et unitate participantium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:ME4","text":"Unitas divina reclassificatur ex singularitate numerica in incomcommunicabilitatem ipsius naturae subsistentis et perfectionis infinitae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:ME5","text":"Maxima unitas reclassificatur ex indivisibilitate geometrica in plenitudinem essendi simplicis et a divisione actuali ac potentiali immunis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus quaerit utrum unum enti addat, utrum unum multitudini opponatur, utrum Deus unus sit, et utrum maxime unus sit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:ALT2","text":"Structural — Unitas rei ex indivisione esse derivatur, multitudo ex divisione intelligitur, et ordo rerum ad causam unam per se reducitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Quaestio de pluralitate deorum solvitur per translationem a numero et nominatione communi ad condiciones metaphysicas individuationis, perfectionis et causalitatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Ordo apprehensionis ab ente ad multitudinem distinguitur ab ordine ontologico, in quo unitas esse constituit et divisio rem compositam praesupponit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:MRT1","text":"Axiomata de indivisione entis, distinctione unius numerici et transcendentis, atque de prioritate unitatis ad multitudinem sufficiunt ad duos primos articulos reconstruendos.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:MRT2","text":"Axiomata de identitate Dei cum sua natura, perfectione infinita et causalitate ordinante sufficiunt ad conclusionem quod Deus unus sit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:MRT3","text":"Axiomata de ipso esse subsistente et simplicitate absoluta sufficiunt ad transitum a Deo uno ad Deo maxime uno.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:MRT4","text":"Commissura principalis consistit in distinctione inter privationem realem et modum privativum cognitionis: unitas negationem divisionis significat, sed in Deo nullam imperfectionem realem ponit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:MRT5","text":"Tensio ordinativa manet inter divisionem priorem quoad apprehensionem et unitatem priorem in definitione multitudinis atque in fundamento essendi; textus eam per distinctionem ordinum dissolvit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:MRT6","text":"Articulus de unitate Dei demonstrat unitatem naturae, perfectionis et causae primae, dum testimonium de unitate Trinitatis intra hanc quaestionem non ulterius explicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T155828807064Z_4050d06d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 11","source_sha256":"d3979f88610251b04de8f49915c95ca1a8452ed92d295ff285f7c8aa18f2d2c6","sha256":"4a2a3ce3a364807360777a3c8dab5eb2e2ae150090364372f33601cf7a163091","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:58:28.810070+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AS1","text":"The question establishes divine unity through the distinction between the transcendent one, which signifies undivided being, and the numerical one, which grounds quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AS2","text":"The negation of division explains why the one is convertible with being without adding a thing, although it adds something above being according to reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AS3","text":"The opposition of one and many is resolved through a twofold order: the numerical one is opposed to number as measure to measured, and the transcendent one to multitude as the undivided to the divided.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AS4","text":"The order of cognition proceeds from being to the negation of identity, division, unity, and multitude, so that multitude presupposes unity even if division is conceived first.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AS5","text":"The unity of God is demonstrated from the identity of the divine nature and supposit, from the infinity of perfection, and from the reduction of worldly order to one principle per se.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AS6","text":"God is concluded to be maximally one because He is at once maximally being and undivided in every way, not because unity as a mere privation itself admits of degrees.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A1","text":"The one convertible with being adds nothing real above being, but signifies undivided being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A2","text":"The being of any thing consists in indivision; a thing preserving its being preserves its unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A3","text":"The one that is the principle of number adds the thing of quantity above the substance of being and is not convertible with being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A4","text":"The same thing can be one and many according to diverse respects; one simply follows from essential or actual indivision, and many simply from essential division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A5","text":"Multitude cannot be under being unless it is in some way contained under one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A6","text":"The transcendent one is opposed privatively to multitude, as the undivided to the divided; the numerical one is opposed to number as measure to measured.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A7","text":"Privation does not wholly remove being, because negation is grounded in a subject; therefore multitude retains some one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A8","text":"Multitude is constituted from unities according to their entity, not according to the indivision by which they are opposed to multitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A9","text":"The intellect first apprehends being, then non-identity, division, unity, and finally multitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A10","text":"God is His nature, such that God and this God are the same; that which individuates God cannot be communicated to many.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A11","text":"Infinite perfection of being admits no real difference among several gods, because difference would add perfection to one or subtract it from another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A12","text":"The ordering of many into one order requires one cause per se, because many cause one only per accidens insofar as they are in some way one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A13","text":"Privative predicates concerning God do not posit divine imperfection, but express through removal the mode of human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A14","text":"Maximal unity requires maximal entity and maximal indivision.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:A15","text":"God is subsistent being itself and altogether simple; He is divided neither actually nor potentially according to any mode of division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:NX1","text":"The transcendent one is neither a thing added to being nor a difference contracting being to a determinate genus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:NX2","text":"The one convertible with being is not the numerical one, nor is the principle of number transferred to God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:NX3","text":"Multitude is not a pure diminution of being, because privation is grounded in a subject and multitude participates in some one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:NX4","text":"One and many are not opposed in such a way that neither can in any way be predicated of the other; predication occurs according to simply and according to something.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:NX5","text":"The unity from which multitude is constituted does not operate formally as indivision opposed to multitude, but materially insofar as unity is being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:NX6","text":"Divine unity is not a real diminution or privation in God, although it is enunciated by human cognition through the negation of division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:NX7","text":"Maximally one does not signify a point or a mathematical unity, which have being in a subject and therefore are not maximally beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:D1","text":"The convertibility of being and one establishes unity as a transcendental property of every being, not as a particular genus of beings.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:D2","text":"A composite is not in act while its parts remain divided; the unity of the composite is a condition of the composite's very being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:D3","text":"The distinction between one simply and one according to something prevents accidental, potential, or numerical division from destroying the identity of the thing simply.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:D4","text":"Multitude can be defined through unity, but unity does not require multitude in its definition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:D5","text":"The priority of division to unity is not a priority of the thing, but a priority with respect to the apprehension of an intellect knowing composites through simples.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:D6","text":"There cannot be several gods, because plurality would either presuppose a communicable divine nature or infinite perfection distributed and diminished.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:D7","text":"The unity of worldly order is not merely a concord of effects, but a sign of the causality of the one first principle per se.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:D8","text":"The divine predication of one is metaphysical, not mathematical, and negative according to the mode of knowing, without negation of divine perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:D9","text":"God is maximally one because the very fullness of being and absolute simplicity coincide in the same subsistent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:SD1","text":"Transcendental unity is not an extrinsic property of a thing, but an ontological name for the very stability by which a thing is and remains in itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_3:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:SD2","text":"Plurality does not constitute another principle opposed to unity, but a mode of beings existing separately that retains the unity of whole, subject, species, genus, or principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:SD3","text":"The demonstration of one God connects the metaphysical order, absolute perfection, and cosmic order in one exclusion of a plurality of first principles.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:SD4","text":"Degrees of unity are measured not by the intensity of privation alone, but by the degree of being and immunity from division.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_3:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:DNX1","text":"The system denies that a thing needs an extrinsic principle by which it may become one, because such a principle would in turn have to be one and an infinite regress would follow.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:DNX2","text":"The system denies that multitude can be understood as absolute dispersion, because every multitude must participate in some unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:DNX3","text":"The system denies that the first cause is one member within a series of several coordinated principles; the first ordering principle is one per se.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:DNX4","text":"The system denies that human cognition can possess divine perfection directly comprehended positively; divine unity is enunciated through the removal of division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:SNX1","text":"The concept of God as a numerable individual among other individuals is impossible, because number implies quantity and matter according to being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:SNX2","text":"A plurality of infinitely perfect absolutes is impossible, because difference among absolutes would necessarily limit perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:SNX3","text":"A maximal unity that signifies only the absence of division and does not include fullness of being is impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0072","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:FGS1","text":"Being → negation of division → transcendent one, which does not contract being but manifests its indivision.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:FGS2","text":"Distinction of the transcendental one from the numerical one → exclusion of metaphysical and mathematical confusion → non-quantitative predication of divine unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:FGS3","text":"Being apprehended → non-identity → division → one → multitude, such that multitude structurally presupposes the account of one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:FGS4","text":"Divine simplicity + infinity of perfection + unity of worldly order → exclusion of several gods → one God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:FGS5","text":"Subsistent being itself + simplicity indivisible in every way → maximal being and maximally undivided → God maximally one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AF1","text":"Ambiguity of one → distinction of transcendent and numerical → negation of real addition above being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AF2","text":"Indivision of being → convertibility of being and one → subordination of multitude to participation in one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AF3","text":"Division and indivision → privative opposition of one and many → ordering of cognition from being to multitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AF4","text":"Transcendental unity → simplicity, infinite perfection, and ordering causality → demonstration of one God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:AF5","text":"One God → subsistent being and absolute simplicity → affirmation of God as maximally one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:ME1","text":"Unity is reclassified from a quantitative accident into a transcendental property convertible with being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:ME2","text":"The opposition of one and many is reclassified from simple contradiction into a privative opposition compatible with predication according to something.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:ME3","text":"Multitude becomes intelligible not as the pure negation of unity, but as plurality grounded in the entity and unity of the participants.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:ME4","text":"Divine unity is reclassified from numerical singularity into the incommunicability of the very subsisting nature and of infinite perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:ME5","text":"Maximal unity is reclassified from geometrical indivisibility into the fullness of simple being, immune from actual and potential division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text asks whether one adds to being, whether one is opposed to multitude, whether God is one, and whether He is maximally one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:ALT2","text":"Structural — The unity of a thing is derived from the indivision of being, multitude is understood from division, and the order of things is reduced to one cause per se.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The question of the plurality of gods is resolved through a translation from number and common naming to the metaphysical conditions of individuation, perfection, and causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The order of apprehension from being to multitude is distinguished from the ontological order, in which unity constitutes being and division presupposes a composite thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:MRT1","text":"Axioms concerning the indivision of being, the distinction of the numerical and transcendent one, and the priority of unity to multitude suffice to reconstruct the first two articles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:MRT2","text":"Axioms concerning the identity of God with His nature, infinite perfection, and ordering causality suffice for the conclusion that God is one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:MRT3","text":"Axioms concerning subsistent being itself and absolute simplicity suffice for the transition from God being one to God being maximally one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:MRT4","text":"The principal joint consists in the distinction between real privation and the privative mode of cognition: unity signifies the negation of division, but in God posits no real imperfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:MRT5","text":"An ordering tension remains between division prior with respect to apprehension and unity prior in the definition of multitude and in the foundation of being; the text resolves it through the distinction of orders.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_3:MRT6","text":"The article on the unity of God demonstrates the unity of nature, perfection, and first cause, while the testimony concerning the unity of the Trinity is not further explained within this question.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_3:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PP_3@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_PP_3:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 12","title":"First Part · Question 11","context":"Chapter 12","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 11"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 11","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 12","order":13,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_PP_4"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PP_4@R001","sha256":"fe02b9458a11d6b2199846f81e53e2c26d839c8582aecd9652774ebb2a09642c","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AS1","text":"Quaestio determinat quibus modis Deus intellectui creato cognoscibilis sit, a visione essentiae usque ad cognitionem naturalem et gratuitam status praesentis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AS2","text":"Intellectus creatus essentiam Dei videre potest, sed neque naturaliter, neque per sensum, neque per speciem creatam, neque comprehendendo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0131","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AS3","text":"Argumentatio metaphysico-reconstructiva per distinctiones obiecti, potentiae, medii, modi et status cognitionis procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AS4","text":"Motus initialis transit ab intelligibilitate Dei in se et capacitate finita intellectus ad necessitatem possibilitatis beatificae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AS5","text":"Possibilitate visionis concessa, textus eius mechanismum constituit per unionem essentiae divinae et supernaturalem elevationem potentiae intellectivae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AS6","text":"Ex hac structura derivantur gradus visionis, incomprehensibilitas, cognitio finita effectuum in Deo et simultaneitas eorum quae per unam essentiam videntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0152","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AS7","text":"Argumentatio demum distinguit visionem beatificam a cognitione praesentis vitae, quam in ordines naturalem et gratuitum distribuit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A1","text":"Unumquodque cognoscibile est secundum quod actu est; Deus igitur, cum sit actus purus, in se maxime cognoscibilis est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A2","text":"Cognitum recipitur secundum modum cognoscentis, ideo excessus intelligibilis capacitatem intellectus finiti superare potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A3","text":"Ultima perfectio rationalis creaturae postulat ut intellectus primum essendi principium attingere possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A4","text":"Intellectus creatus essentiam Dei videre potest tantum Deo se ei supernaturaliter coniungente.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A5","text":"Visio in actu requirit virtutem visivam et unionem rei visae cum vidente.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A6","text":"Nulla species creata divinam essentiam ut in se est repraesentare potest, quia divina essentia est ipsum esse incircumscriptum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","text":"In visione essentiae, divina essentia ipsa intellectui creato unitur ut forma intelligibilis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A8","text":"Omnis potentia sensitiva, cum sit actus organi corporalis, ad corporalia terminatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A9","text":"Cognitio naturalis intellectus creati proprio modo essendi determinatur; ipsum esse subsistens soli intellectui divino connaturaliter cognoscitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10","text":"Intellectus creatus ad visionem essentiae lumine gloriae, dispositione creata et supernaturali, roboratur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A11","text":"Lumen gloriae potentiam videntis perficit, sed essentiam visam non repraesentat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A12","text":"Gradus visionis divinae determinatur participatione luminis gloriae, quae secundum caritatem et desiderium variatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0113","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0114","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13","text":"Nullum lumen gloriae in intellectu creato receptum infinitum esse potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A14","text":"Effectus in causa cognoscuntur secundum perfectionem qua ipsa causa cognoscitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0152","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A15","text":"Multa quae per unam formam intelligibilem cognoscuntur simul cognoscuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A16","text":"Cognitio humana naturalis in hac vita a sensu et phantasmatibus incipit atque ad ea terminatur quae per sensibilia manifestari possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0226","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A17","text":"Effectus causae divinae non adaequantes manifestant an Deus sit et quae ei ut primae causae conveniant, non quid sit eius essentia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0222","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0224","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A18","text":"Revelatio gratiae cognitionem naturalem excedit, quia lumen intellectus confortat et aptiora signa vel phantasmata divinitus praebere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0244","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX1","text":"Maxima intelligibilitas obiecti in se non importat eius naturalem accessibilitatem cuilibet intellectui.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX2","text":"Auctoritates quae visionem Dei negant comprehensionem negant, non omnem visionem essentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX3","text":"Infinitas Dei non est infinitas materiae indeterminatae et secundum se ignotae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX4","text":"Deum esse supra existentia non significat eum nullo modo esse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX5","text":"Proportio creaturae ad Deum non est commensuratio quantitativa, sed habitudo effectus ad causam et potentiae ad actum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX6","text":"Similitudo requisita ex parte potentiae videntis non est species creata in qua essentia divina videatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX7","text":"Deus neque sensu neque imaginatione per essentiam videri potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX8","text":"Cognitio Dei per similitudinem in Angelo refulgentem non est visio essentiae divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX9","text":"Lumen gloriae non est medium in quo Deus repraesentetur, sed perfectio sub qua intellectus immediate videt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0100","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX10","text":"Identitas essentiae visae non inducit aequalitatem modi quo diversi intellectus eam vident.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX11","text":"Deum totum videre non est eum totaliter videre neque comprehendere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0137","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0139","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX12","text":"Visio divinae essentiae non confert intellectui creato cognitionem omnium quae Deus facit vel facere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0152","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0153","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX13","text":"Desiderium naturale sciendi non est desiderium omnium singularium, cogitationum, factorum et possibilium divinorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0161","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX14","text":"Res in Deo visae non cognoscuntur per proprias species creatas intellectum informantes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0170","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0171","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX15","text":"Simultaneitas visionis in Deo non tollit successionem cognitionis naturalis quae per diversas species exercetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0188","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX16","text":"Locutiones scripturales de visione sensibili vel imaginaria Dei non per se significant visionem essentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0064","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0204","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX17","text":"Cognitio naturalis et cognitio gratiae in statu viae non praebent cognitionem quidditativam divinae essentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0224","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0242","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D1","text":"Deus simul est maxime cognoscibilis ex parte obiecti et naturaliter inaccessibilis intellectui creato ex parte capacitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D2","text":"Possibilitas visionis essentiae est conditio perfectionis beatificae, sed eius actus a dono divino dependet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D3","text":"Visio beatifica immediata manet, quamvis potentia videntis per lumen creatum medietur et perficiatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D4","text":"Visio essentiae divinae est actus solius intellectus, quia obiectum incorporeum proportionem potentiae sensitivae excedit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D5","text":"Nulla excellentia naturalis intellectus creati, etiam angelici, sufficit ad visionem ipsius esse subsistentis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D6","text":"Diversitas beatitudinis ex diversa capacitate gloriosa videntium provenit, non ex divisione obiecti divini.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D7","text":"Intellectus creatus totam essentiam divinam videt, sed modum infinitum eius cognoscibilitatis non adaequat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0137","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0139","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D8","text":"Numerus effectuum in Deo cognitorum crescit cum perfectione visionis, sed numquam ad totam virtutem divinam extenditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0153","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0159","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D9","text":"Res in Deo cognitae per ipsam essentiam divinam et non per proprias species videntur; ideo ea quae sic videntur simul cognoscuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0171","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D10","text":"Beatitudo ex sufficientia Dei ut ultimi obiecti provenit, non ex comprehensione Dei neque ex encyclopaedica cognitione creaturarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0161","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D11","text":"Impossibilitas visionis in vita mortali ordinem communem cognitionis corpori subiectae respicit, unde elevationem extraordinariam sine usu sensuum non excludit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0206","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D12","text":"Cognitio naturalis Dei est causalis, analogica et negativa: ex effectibus ponit causam, necessaria ei attribuit et imperfectiones creaturarum removet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0222","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D13","text":"Cognitio gratiae in via altior est cognitione naturali, sed manet cognitio mediata et non quidditativa.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0242","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SD1","text":"Totum systema asymmetria inter sufficientiam infinitam obiecti et capacitatem finitam subiecti ordinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SD2","text":"Mediatio ex parte potentiae et immediatio ex parte obiecti simul consistere possunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SD3","text":"Gratia capacitatem creatam transformat sine abolitione finitudinis vel conversione creaturae in naturam divinam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SD4","text":"Participatio unius luminis generat hierarchiam beatitudinis sine multiplicatione vel partitione essentiae visae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SD5","text":"Beatitudo est plenitudo desiderii per praesentiam finis, non exhaustio infinita cognoscibilitatis finis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0161","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SD6","text":"Simultaneitas beatifica non est abolitio omnis successionis creatae, sed effectus unius principii intelligibilis respectu eorum quae in Deo videntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0188","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SD7","text":"Ontologia Dei ut ipsius esse subsistentis cogit epistemologiam visionis ultra exemplar repraesentationis creatae constitui.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:DNX1","text":"Transcendentia Dei cum absoluta incognoscibilitate identificari non potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:DNX2","text":"Omnis cognitio intellectualis ad repraesentationem per speciem creatam reduci non potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:DNX3","text":"Cognitio Dei commensurationem finiti et infiniti non requirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:DNX4","text":"Elevatio super naturam ad sufficientiam latentem facultatis naturalis reduci non potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:DNX5","text":"Integritas obiecti cogniti cum exhaustione modi eius cognoscibilitatis identificari non potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0137","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0139","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:DNX6","text":"Impletio desiderii rationalis cognitionem omnium singularium et possibilium non requirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0161","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:DNX7","text":"Successio non est proprietas necessaria omnis cognitionis intellectus creati.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0188","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:DNX8","text":"Ignorantia quidditativa quae in cognitione gratiae manet aequalitatem eius cum cognitione naturali non inducit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0224","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0239","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0242","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SNX1","text":"Nulla creatura ad modum quo Deus seipsum infinite cognoscit pervenire potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0139","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SNX2","text":"Nulla forma creata spatium repraesentativum capax essentiae divinae constituere potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SNX3","text":"Omniscientia creaturae nec effectus necessarius visionis nec pars constitutiva beatitudinis est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0153","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0161","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SNX4","text":"Ascensus autonomus intellectus creati ad essentiam divinam structuraliter praecluditur; divina sui communicatio est condicio visionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SNX5","text":"Deiformitas participata identitatem ontologicam creatoris et creaturae admittere non potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0096","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0102","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SNX6","text":"Absentia cognitionis quid est in statu viae utrumque ordinem, naturalem et gratuitum, circumscribit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0224","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0242","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:FGS1","text":"Ordo obiectivus est: actus purus → maxima intelligibilitas in se → excessus super modum intellectus creati → inaccessibilitas naturalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:FGS2","text":"Ordo possibilitatis est: desiderium primi principii → exigentia perfectionis beatificae → possibilitas visionis → necessitas divinae coniunctionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:FGS3","text":"Ordo visionis est: exclusio sensus et speciei repraesentativae → unio essentiae ut intelligibilis → lumen gloriae ut perfectio potentiae → visio immediata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:FGS4","text":"Ordo limitationis est: participatio luminis → gradus visionis → lumen finitum → incomprehensibilitas → cognitio finita effectuum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0153","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:FGS5","text":"Ordo contentorum est: essentia divina unica ratio intelligendi → res cognitae in causa → pluralitas sine speciebus propriis → cognitio simultanea.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0171","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:FGS6","text":"Ordo statuum est: sensus et phantasmata → cognitio naturalis ex effectibus → elevatio gratiae in via → lumen gloriae → visio essentiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0239","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AF1","text":"Intelligibilitas Dei in se → excessus capacitatis creatae → exigentia beatitudinis → concessio visionis essentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AF2","text":"Concessio visionis → exclusio speciei creatae → exclusio sensus → exclusio sufficientiae naturalis → positio luminis gloriae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AF3","text":"Lumen gloriae → diversitas graduum → incomprehensibilitas → cognitio limitata effectuum → cognitio per essentiam unam et simultanea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0153","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AF4","text":"Visio beatifica → exclusio status ordinarii vitae mortalis → cognitio naturalis causalis → cognitio gratiae altior sed non quidditativa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0239","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0242","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ME1","text":"Quaestio cognoscibilitatis Dei in quinque variabiles distribuitur: obiectum, potentia, medium, modus et status cognoscentis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:AS3","CH_GGVC_PP_4:FGS1","CH_GGVC_PP_4:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ME2","text":"Oppositio simplicis cognitionis et ignorantiae dissolvitur per distinctionem visionis, comprehensionis et cognitionis ex effectibus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:D7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:D12","CH_GGVC_PP_4:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0131","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0137","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0242","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ME3","text":"Similitudo creata dividitur in perfectionem subiectivam potentiae et repraesentationem obiectivam rei, quarum prima admittitur et secunda excluditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ME4","text":"Infinitas divina ex causa ignorationis absolutae in principium maximae intelligibilitatis et simultaneae incomprehensibilitatis reclassificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_4:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0022","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ME5","text":"Beatitudo a scientia exhaustiva separatur et per sufficientiam ultimi obiecti definitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:D10","CH_GGVC_PP_4:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0161","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ME6","text":"Sermones sensibiles, imaginarii et scripturales de visione Dei in repraesentationes divinae praesentiae, non in visiones essentiae, reordinantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0204","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0208","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ME7","text":"Pluralitas cognitorum et eorum successio non ex sola pluralitate rerum, sed ex pluralitate formarum intelligibilium explicantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_4:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ME8","text":"Ager cognitionis divinae in tres ordines distinguitur: naturalem ex effectibus, gratuitum per revelationem et gloriosum per essentiam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A18","CH_GGVC_PP_4:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ALT1","text":"Gradus superficiei complectitur possibilitatem, medium, subiectum, gradum, extensionem et statum visionis vel cognitionis Dei.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ALT2","text":"Gradus structuralis cognitionem per relationes inter modum essendi, potentiam cognoscitivam, formam intelligibilem et lumen participatum constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ALT3","text":"Gradus metastrategicus quaestionem ab oppositione cognoscibilis et incognoscibilis ad distinctiones visionis, comprehensionis, repraesentationis et participationis transfert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0131","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0137","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ALT4","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis Deum ut ipsum esse subsistens, creaturam ut esse participans, cognitionem viae ut sensibus et successioni subiectam, atque visionem gloriae ut supernaturalem et simultaneam praesupponit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0188","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad restituendam seriem a possibilitate visionis usque ad distinctionem cognitionis naturalis, gratuitae et gloriosae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0239","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:MRT2","text":"Restitutio verum motum textus servat, quia mechanismum visionis ante eius gradus, limites, contenta et statum temporalem constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:AF1","CH_GGVC_PP_4:AF2","CH_GGVC_PP_4:AF3","CH_GGVC_PP_4:AF4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0152","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:MRT3","text":"Cohaerentia systematis maxime nititur distinctione inter intelligibilitatem obiecti et capacitatem cognoscentis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_4:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:MRT4","text":"Commissura firmissima est distinctio inter essentiam ut obiectum immediate visum et lumen gloriae ut perfectionem potentiae videntis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:MRT5","text":"Tensionem nondum plene explicitam constituit desiderium naturale visionis primi principii cuius impletio tamen facultatem naturalem omnino excedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0018","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:MRT6","text":"Nexus inter maiorem caritatem, maiorem participationem luminis gloriae et perfectiorem visionem asseritur potius quam intra quaestionem demonstrative explicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:MRT7","text":"Exceptio raptus commissuram fragilem constituit inter impossibilitatem visionis in vita mortali et elevationem hominis adhuc in carne viventis sed sensibus non utentis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0206","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:MRT8","text":"Terminus exactus eorum quae quilibet beatus in Deo cognoscit non determinatur ultra principium proportionis ad gradum visionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0152","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0153","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0159","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0161","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:MRT9","text":"Unio essentiae divinae ut formae intelligibilis mechanismum centralem praebet, sed modus huius unionis sine informatione substantiali vel abolitione finitudinis hic tantum compresse definitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T155901375169Z_cbd5f30a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 12","source_sha256":"fe02b9458a11d6b2199846f81e53e2c26d839c8582aecd9652774ebb2a09642c","sha256":"37c505c8114fbd48c16106cc309826b5cdc1e684ee98c086919aae1cf69e4fbb","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:59:01.377482+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AS1","text":"The question determines in what ways God is knowable to the created intellect, from the vision of the essence to the natural and gratuitous knowledge of the present state.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AS2","text":"The created intellect can see the essence of God, but neither naturally, nor through sense, nor through a created species, nor by comprehending.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0131","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AS3","text":"The metaphysical-reconstructive argument proceeds through distinctions of object, power, medium, mode, and state of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AS4","text":"The initial movement proceeds from God's intelligibility in itself and the finite capacity of the intellect to the necessity of the possibility of beatitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AS5","text":"Once the possibility of vision has been granted, the text establishes its mechanism through the union of the divine essence and the supernatural elevation of the intellective power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AS6","text":"From this structure are derived the degrees of vision, incomprehensibility, finite knowledge of effects in God, and the simultaneity of those things seen through one essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0152","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AS7","text":"Finally, the argument distinguishes beatific vision from the knowledge of the present life, which it distributes into natural and gratuitous orders.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A1","text":"Everything is knowable insofar as it is in act; therefore God, since He is pure act, is in Himself maximally knowable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A2","text":"The known is received according to the mode of the knower; therefore intelligible excess can surpass the capacity of a finite intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A3","text":"The ultimate perfection of the rational creature requires that the intellect be able to attain the first principle of being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A4","text":"The created intellect can see the essence of God only if God supernaturally unites Himself to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A5","text":"Vision in act requires a power of seeing and the union of the thing seen with the seer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A6","text":"No created species can represent the divine essence as it is in itself, because the divine essence is unbounded subsisting being itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","text":"In the vision of the essence, the divine essence itself is united to the created intellect as an intelligible form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A8","text":"Every sensitive power, since it is the act of a bodily organ, terminates in corporeal things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A9","text":"The natural knowledge of the created intellect is determined by its proper mode of being; subsisting being itself is connaturally known only to the divine intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10","text":"The created intellect is strengthened for the vision of the essence by the light of glory, a created and supernatural disposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A11","text":"The light of glory perfects the power of the seer, but does not represent the essence seen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A12","text":"The degree of divine vision is determined by participation in the light of glory, which varies according to charity and desire.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0113","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0114","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13","text":"No light of glory received in a created intellect can be infinite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A14","text":"Effects are known in a cause according to the perfection with which the cause itself is known.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0152","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A15","text":"Many things that are known through one intelligible form are known simultaneously.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A16","text":"Natural human knowledge in this life begins from sense and phantasms and terminates in those things that can be manifested through sensibles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0226","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A17","text":"Effects not adequate to the divine cause manifest whether God is and what belongs to Him as first cause, not what His essence is.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0222","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0224","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:A18","text":"The revelation of grace exceeds natural knowledge, because it strengthens the light of the intellect and can divinely provide more fitting signs or phantasms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0244","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX1","text":"The maximal intelligibility of an object in itself does not entail its natural accessibility to every intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX2","text":"Authorities that deny the vision of God deny comprehension, not every vision of the essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX3","text":"The infinity of God is not the infinity of indeterminate matter and unknown in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX4","text":"That God is above beings does not mean that He is in no way.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX5","text":"The proportion of creature to God is not quantitative commensuration, but the relation of effect to cause and of potency to act.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX6","text":"The likeness required on the part of the power of seeing is not a created species in which the divine essence is seen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX7","text":"God can be seen through His essence neither by sense nor by imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX8","text":"Knowledge of God through a likeness shining forth in an angel is not vision of the divine essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX9","text":"The light of glory is not a medium in which God is represented, but a perfection under which the intellect sees immediately.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0100","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX10","text":"The identity of the essence seen does not entail equality in the mode by which diverse intellects see it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX11","text":"To see God whole is not to see Him wholly nor to comprehend Him.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0137","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0139","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX12","text":"The vision of the divine essence does not confer upon the created intellect knowledge of all that God does or can do.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0152","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0153","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX13","text":"The natural desire to know is not a desire for all divine singulars, thoughts, deeds, and possibilities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0161","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX14","text":"Things seen in God are not known through their proper created species informing the intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0170","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0171","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX15","text":"The simultaneity of vision in God does not remove the succession of natural knowledge exercised through diverse species.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0188","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX16","text":"Scriptural expressions concerning the sensible or imaginary vision of God do not in themselves signify the vision of the essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0064","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0204","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX17","text":"Natural knowledge and the knowledge of grace in the state of wayfarerhood do not provide quidditative knowledge of the divine essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0224","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0242","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D1","text":"God is at once maximally knowable on the part of the object and naturally inaccessible to the created intellect on the part of its capacity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D2","text":"The possibility of the vision of the essence is a condition of beatific perfection, but its act depends upon a divine gift.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D3","text":"Beatific vision remains immediate, although the power of the seer is mediated and perfected through a created light.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D4","text":"The vision of the divine essence is an act of the intellect alone, because an incorporeal object exceeds the proportion of the sensitive power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D5","text":"No natural excellence of the created intellect, even an angelic one, suffices for the vision of subsisting being itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D6","text":"The diversity of beatitude proceeds from the diverse glorious capacity of those who see, not from a division of the divine object.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D7","text":"The created intellect sees the whole divine essence, but does not attain the infinite mode of its knowability.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0137","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0139","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D8","text":"The number of effects known in God increases with the perfection of vision, but never extends to the whole divine power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0153","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0159","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D9","text":"Things known in God are seen through the divine essence itself and not through their proper species; therefore those things thus seen are known simultaneously.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0171","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D10","text":"Beatitude proceeds from the sufficiency of God as ultimate object, not from comprehension of God nor from encyclopedic knowledge of creatures.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0161","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D11","text":"The impossibility of vision in mortal life concerns the common order of cognition subject to the body, and therefore does not exclude extraordinary elevation without the use of the senses.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0206","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D12","text":"Natural knowledge of God is causal, analogical, and negative: from effects it posits a cause, attributes necessities to Him, and removes the imperfections of creatures.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0222","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:D13","text":"Knowledge of grace in the way is higher than natural knowledge, but remains mediated and non-quidditative knowledge.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0242","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SD1","text":"The whole system is ordered by the asymmetry between the infinite sufficiency of the object and the finite capacity of the subject.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SD2","text":"Mediation on the part of the power and immediacy on the part of the object can coexist.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SD3","text":"Grace transforms created capacity without abolishing finitude or converting the creature into the divine nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SD4","text":"Participation in one light generates a hierarchy of beatitude without multiplication or partition of the essence seen.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SD5","text":"Beatitude is the fullness of desire through the presence of the end, not the infinite exhaustion of the knowability of the end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0161","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SD6","text":"Beatific simultaneity is not the abolition of every created succession, but the effect of one intelligible principle with respect to those things seen in God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0188","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SD7","text":"The ontology of God as subsisting being itself requires that the epistemology of vision be constituted beyond the model of created representation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:DNX1","text":"The transcendence of God cannot be identified with absolute unknowability.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:DNX2","text":"Not all intellectual knowledge can be reduced to representation through a created species.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:DNX3","text":"Knowledge of God does not require commensuration of the finite and the infinite.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:DNX4","text":"Elevation above nature cannot be reduced to the latent sufficiency of a natural faculty.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:DNX5","text":"The integrity of the object known cannot be identified with exhaustion of the mode of its knowability.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0137","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0139","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:DNX6","text":"The fulfillment of rational desire does not require knowledge of all singulars and possibilities.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0161","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:DNX7","text":"Succession is not a necessary property of all knowledge of the created intellect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0188","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:DNX8","text":"The quidditative ignorance that remains in the knowledge of grace does not entail its equality with natural knowledge.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0224","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0239","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0242","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SNX1","text":"No creature can attain the mode by which God knows Himself infinitely.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0139","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SNX2","text":"No created form can constitute a representative space capable of the divine essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SNX3","text":"A creature's omniscience is neither a necessary effect of vision nor a constitutive part of beatitude.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0153","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0161","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SNX4","text":"The autonomous ascent of the created intellect to the divine essence is structurally precluded; divine self-communication is the condition of vision.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SNX5","text":"Participated deiformity cannot admit the ontological identity of Creator and creature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0096","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0102","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:SNX6","text":"The absence of knowledge of what God is in the state of wayfarerhood circumscribes both orders, natural and gratuitous.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0224","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0242","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:FGS1","text":"The objective order is: pure act → maximal intelligibility in itself → excess over the mode of the created intellect → natural inaccessibility.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:FGS2","text":"The order of possibility is: desire for the first principle → requirement of beatific perfection → possibility of vision → necessity of divine union.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:FGS3","text":"The order of vision is: exclusion of sense and representative species → union of the essence as intelligible → light of glory as perfection of the power → immediate vision.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:FGS4","text":"The order of limitation is: participation in light → degrees of vision → finite light → incomprehensibility → finite knowledge of effects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0153","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:FGS5","text":"The order of contents is: the divine essence as the one intelligible ground → things known in the cause → plurality without proper species → simultaneous knowledge.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0171","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:FGS6","text":"The order of states is: sense and phantasms → natural knowledge from effects → elevation of grace in the way → light of glory → vision of the essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0239","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AF1","text":"God's intelligibility in itself → excess over created capacity → requirement of beatitude → concession of the vision of the essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AF2","text":"Concession of vision → exclusion of created species → exclusion of sense → exclusion of natural sufficiency → positing of the light of glory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AF3","text":"Light of glory → diversity of degrees → incomprehensibility → limited knowledge of effects → knowledge through one essence and simultaneous knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0153","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:AF4","text":"Beatific vision → exclusion of the ordinary state of mortal life → causal natural knowledge → higher but non-quidditative knowledge of grace.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0239","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0242","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ME1","text":"The question of God's knowability is distributed into five variables: object, power, medium, mode, and state of the knower.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:AS3","CH_GGVC_PP_4:FGS1","CH_GGVC_PP_4:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ME2","text":"The opposition between simple knowledge and ignorance is dissolved through the distinction of vision, comprehension, and knowledge from effects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:D7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:D12","CH_GGVC_PP_4:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0131","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0137","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0242","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ME3","text":"Created likeness is divided into the subjective perfection of the power and the objective representation of the thing, of which the former is admitted and the latter excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ME4","text":"Divine infinity is reclassified from a cause of absolute ignorance into a principle of maximal intelligibility and simultaneous incomprehensibility.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_4:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0022","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ME5","text":"Beatitude is separated from exhaustive knowledge and defined through the sufficiency of the ultimate object.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:D10","CH_GGVC_PP_4:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0161","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ME6","text":"Sensible, imaginary, and scriptural discourses concerning the vision of God are reordered into representations of the divine presence, not into visions of the essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:NX16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0204","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0208","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ME7","text":"The plurality of things known and their succession are explained not by the plurality of things alone, but by the plurality of intelligible forms.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_4:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ME8","text":"The field of divine knowledge is distinguished into three orders: the natural from effects, the gratuitous through revelation, and the glorious through the essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A18","CH_GGVC_PP_4:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ALT1","text":"The surface level includes the possibility, medium, subject, degree, extent, and state of the vision or knowledge of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ALT2","text":"The structural level constitutes knowledge through relations among mode of being, cognitive power, intelligible form, and participated light.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ALT3","text":"The metastrategic level transfers the question from the opposition of knowable and unknowable to distinctions of vision, comprehension, representation, and participation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0131","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0137","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:ALT4","text":"The ontological and temporal level presupposes God as subsisting being itself, the creature as participating being, knowledge in the way as subject to senses and succession, and the vision of glory as supernatural and simultaneous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0184","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0188","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to restore the sequence from the possibility of vision to the distinction of natural, gratuitous, and glorious knowledge.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0221","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0239","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement of the text, because it establishes the mechanism of vision before its degrees, limits, contents, and temporal state.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:AF1","CH_GGVC_PP_4:AF2","CH_GGVC_PP_4:AF3","CH_GGVC_PP_4:AF4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0152","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:MRT3","text":"The coherence of the system rests most greatly upon the distinction between the intelligibility of the object and the capacity of the knower.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_4:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:MRT4","text":"The firmest joint is the distinction between the essence as the object immediately seen and the light of glory as the perfection of the power of seeing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_4:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:MRT5","text":"A tension not yet fully made explicit is constituted by the natural desire for the vision of the first principle, whose fulfillment nevertheless wholly exceeds natural faculty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0018","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0077","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:MRT6","text":"The connection among greater charity, greater participation in the light of glory, and more perfect vision is asserted rather than demonstratively explained within the question.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:MRT7","text":"The exception of rapture constitutes a fragile joint between the impossibility of vision in mortal life and the elevation of a man still living in the flesh but not using the senses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0201","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0206","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:MRT8","text":"The precise limit of those things that each blessed person knows in God is not determined beyond the principle of proportion to the degree of vision.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0152","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0153","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0159","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0161","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_4:MRT9","text":"The union of the divine essence as intelligible form provides the central mechanism, but the mode of this union without substantial informing or abolition of finitude is here defined only in compressed form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_4:P0132","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PP_4@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_PP_4:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 13","title":"First Part · Question 12","context":"Chapter 13","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 12"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 12","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 13","order":14,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_PP_5"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PP_5@R001","sha256":"9cbba374dcf7bb38a23f71cc169948ebaf8e954388ed56a6e21aae517d1e2c36","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:AS1","text":"Quaestio est quomodo Deus vere nominari et affirmative praedicari possit, quamvis essentia eius intellectui viatoris incomprehensibilis sit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:AS2","text":"Nomina ex creaturis accepta Deum substantialiter sed imperfecte significant, quia perfectiones creatas in eo unite et eminenter praeexistere repraesentant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:AS3","text":"Argumentatio est reconstructiva, distinctiva et simul affirmativa atque apophatica: sermonem de Deo non abolet, sed eius referentiam, modum et limites distinguit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:AS4","text":"Mediatio conceptionis intellectualis efficit ut veritas nominis a comprehensione quidditativa rei distinguatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:AS5","text":"Motus procedit a possibilitate nominationis ad significationem substantialem, proprietatem, pluralitatem rationum, analogiam, ordinem praedicationis, relationes temporales et genera nominum divinorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:AS6","text":"Conclusio est quod propositiones affirmativae de Deo verae sunt, dum pluralitas rationum intellectarum uni rei divinae simplici respondet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A1","text":"Vox rem significat mediante conceptione intellectus, ideoque nominatio mensuram cognitionis sequitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A2","text":"Essentia divina in statu viae non cognoscitur secundum quod in se est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A3","text":"Deus ex creaturis cognoscitur secundum causalitatem, excellentiam et remotionem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A4","text":"Omnes perfectiones creaturarum in Deo simpliciter, unite et eminenter praeexistunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A5","text":"Effectus virtutem causae non adaequans similitudinem causae deficienter recipit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0093","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A6","text":"Nomina absoluta et affirmativa substantiam divinam significant, sed eam imperfecte repraesentant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7","text":"Id quod nomen significat distinguitur a modo quo illud significat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A8","text":"Modus significandi humanus cognitioni rerum compositarum, materialium et temporalium conformatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A9","text":"Multiplices conceptiones imperfectae uni simplici rei divinae vere respondent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A10","text":"Praedicatio analogica rationes diversas per ordinem ad unum primum coniungit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0115","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A11","text":"Creatura realiter ad Deum ordinatur, sed in Deo relatio realis ad creaturam non constituitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A12","text":"Denominatio relativa ex tempore sequi potest mutationem creaturae sine mutatione Dei.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A13","text":"Principium a quo nomen imponitur distingui potest ab eo quod nomen significat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A14","text":"Nomen secundum totam significationem proprie communicatur tantum ubi res significata multiplicari potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0183","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A15","text":"Natura divina non multiplicatur; nomen tamen per similitudinis participationem aut secundum opinionem multipliciter praedicari potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0186","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0187","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A16","text":"Esse divinum, essentiae identicum et successione temporali carens, per nomen indeterminatum qui est significatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0226","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0227","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A17","text":"Veritas affirmationis compatitur identitatem rei cum diversitate rationum subiecti et praedicati.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A18","text":"Intellectus potest rem simplicem modo composito intelligere quin eam compositam esse iudicet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0253","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX1","text":"Innominalitas absoluta Dei reicitur; supra nominationem esse non idem est ac nullo modo nominari posse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX2","text":"Nullum nomen humanum essentiam divinam secundum quod est definitive exprimit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX3","text":"Nomina affirmativa non sunt tantum formulae removentes imperfectiones a Deo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX4","text":"Nomina affirmativa non significant tantum relationem causae divinae ad effectus creatos.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX5","text":"Non omnia nomina de Deo metaphorice dicuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX6","text":"Identitas rei divinae non reddit omnia nomina divina synonyma.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX7","text":"Nullum nomen de Deo et creatura simpliciter univoce praedicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0093","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0094","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX8","text":"Nomina de Deo et creaturis non praedicantur pure aequivoce.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0094","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX9","text":"Prioritas nominum non uniformiter ex ordine cognitionis humanae determinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0117","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX10","text":"Praedicatio relativa ex tempore mutationem realem in Deo non requirit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX11","text":"Hoc nomen Deus non imponitur ad operationem divinam tamquam terminum significationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0167","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX12","text":"Participatio divinae naturae multiplicationem ipsius naturae divinae non constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0186","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0189","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX13","text":"Compositio grammatica propositionis compositionem realem in Deo non infert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0253","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D1","text":"Deus nominari potest, sed nomen eius non exprimit essentiam divinam eo modo quo definitio essentiam creatam exprimit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D2","text":"Nomina abstracta simplicitatem, concreta subsistentiam, verba temporalia aeternitatem sub modo humano, et demonstrativa obiectum intellectum significare possunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D3","text":"Nomina bonitatis, vitae et sapientiae aliquid positive in ipsa substantia divina ponunt, quamvis nec quidditative nec exhaustive.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D4","text":"Nomen perfectionem absolute significans Deo proprie convenit quantum ad rem significatam, sed non quantum ad modum significandi; nomen vero modum creatum in significato includens metaphorice convenit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D5","text":"Plura nomina divina unam rem significant sub rationibus diversis et ideo synonymiam non constituunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D6","text":"Communitas nominum inter Deum et creaturas analogica est, quia nec ratio eadem nec ratio totaliter diversa servatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D7","text":"Nomina perfectionum absolute dictarum prius Deo secundum rem, prius creaturis secundum impositionem conveniunt; nomina metaphorica prius creaturis dicuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D8","text":"Nomina dominus, creator et salvator ex tempore de Deo dici possunt propter relationem vel effectum temporalem, manente Deo immutato.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0145","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0146","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0150","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D9","text":"Hoc nomen Deus ab universali providentia imponitur, sed ad naturam divinam significandam terminatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0167","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D10","text":"Hoc nomen Deus appellativum est secundum modum cognitionis, sed secundum rem incommunicabile; communicatur tantum per similitudinem aut opinionem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0186","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0187","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0191","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D11","text":"Deus per naturam, per participationem et secundum opinionem analogice dicitur, quia significatio veri Dei in significationibus derivatis includitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0206","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0207","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D12","text":"Veritas vel falsitas praedicationis per se significationem nominis non mutat, unde contradictio de idolo eandem significationem principalem nominis Dei retinere potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0209","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D13","text":"Hoc nomen qui est maxime proprie Deum nominat, quia ipsum esse indeterminate et praesentialiter significat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0226","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0227","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D14","text":"Gradus proprietatis nominum variatur secundum criterium: qui est praecellit ex esse et modo significandi, Deus ex natura significata, tetragrammaton ex supposito incommunicabili, bonum ex causalitate.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0229","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0231","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D15","text":"Propositiones affirmativae de Deo verae sunt, quia distinctio subiecti et praedicati pluralitatem rationum, compositio autem identitatem rei repraesentat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD1","text":"Ordo cognitionis et impositionis a creaturis ad Deum ascendit, dum ordo perfectionis et causalitatis a Deo ad creaturas descendit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD2","text":"Affirmatio et negatio non sunt viae aemulae: affirmatio perfectionem retinet, negatio modum creatum et comprehensionem excludit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD3","text":"Simplicitas divina pluralitatem nominum non destruit, sed eam ordinat, quia perfectiones divisae in effectibus uni principio simplici respondent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD4","text":"Analogia demonstrationem de Deo possibilem reddit sine reductione Dei et creaturae ad genus aut definitionem communem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD5","text":"Aeternitas subiecti divini cum temporalitate denominationum compatitur, quia tempus ex mutatione termini creati, non ex mutatione Dei, oritur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD6","text":"Veritas sermonis de Deo consistit in referentia reali sine comprehensione quidditativa et in compositione intellectuali sine compositione ontologica.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0253","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX1","text":"Veritas nominis non requirit adaequationem comprehensivam intellectus ad essentiam divinam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX2","text":"Communitas vocabuli non requirit communitatem generis, speciei aut definitionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX3","text":"Identitas realis significati non requirit identitatem rationum intellectualium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX4","text":"Modus quo intellectus cognoscit rem non necessario est modus quem rei cognitae attribuit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A18","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0252","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0253","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX5","text":"Origo nominis ab effectu vel operatione non claudit significatum intra effectum vel operationem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX6","text":"Denominatio realis non requirit relationem realem intrinsecam in utroque extremo.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0152","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX7","text":"Distinctio inter praedicationem veram et falsam non est identica distinctioni inter significationem univocam, analogicam et aequivocam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D11","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0206","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0209","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX8","text":"Perfectio simplicitatis divinae non exigit sermonem unius rationis, sed excludit compositionem rei sub pluralitate rationum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SNX1","text":"Conceptus quidditativus essentiae divinae in statu viae structuraliter indisponibilis est et totam doctrinam nominum circumterminat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0226","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0227","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SNX2","text":"Systema neque silentium absolutum neque sermonem comprehensivum admittit; utrumque terminum analogia simul praecludit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0094","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SNX3","text":"Nullum planum semanticum neutrum praesupponitur in quo Deus et creatura sub eadem mensura proportionata comprehendantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SNX4","text":"Nulla mutatio intradivina ponenda est ut omnis novitas praedicationis temporalis explicetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SNX5","text":"Nomen humanum omnino proprium, quod simul essentiam comprehendat et modum divinum adaequate significet, intra hanc cognitionis condicionem non datur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0226","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0227","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:FGS1","text":"Prima series est: creatura cognita → causalitas, excellentia et remotio → conceptio imperfecta Dei → nomen verum sed non definitivum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:FGS2","text":"Secunda series est: perfectio divisa in effectu → praeexistentia unita in causa → significatio substantialis → correctio apophatica modi significandi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:FGS3","text":"Tertia series est: defectus identitatis rationis excludit univocationem → ordo realis effectus ad causam excludit aequivocationem → analogia constituit communitatem mediam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:FGS4","text":"Quarta series est: mutatio creaturae → relatio realis creaturae ad Deum → relatio rationis ex parte Dei → denominatio temporalis sine mutatione divina.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:FGS5","text":"Quinta series est: operatio cognita → impositio nominis → natura significata → distinctio communicabilitatis secundum rem, similitudinem et opinionem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0166","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0167","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0183","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0186","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0187","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:FGS6","text":"Sexta series est: una res simplex → conceptiones plures → subiectum et praedicatum ratione distincta → compositio significans identitatem → affirmatio vera.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:AF1","text":"Primus motus procedit a nominabilitate Dei → ad significationem substantialem → ad distinctionem proprietatis et metaphorae → ad pluralitatem non synonymam nominum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:AF2","text":"Secundus motus procedit ab exclusione univocationis et aequivocationis → ad analogiam → ad duplicem ordinem prioritatis → ad praedicationes relativas ex tempore.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:AF3","text":"Tertius motus procedit a distinctione operationis et naturae in nomine Deus → ad communicabilitatem eius limitatam → ad usus analogicos → ad praestantiam nominis qui est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D9","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D10","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D11","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0167","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0186","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0206","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0226","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:AF4","text":"Motus finalis procedit a pluralitate rationum sub una re → ad compositionem subiecti et praedicati → ad veritatem propositionum affirmativarum de Deo.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ME1","text":"Nominatio Dei a definitione directa essentiae ad cognitionem mediatam per effectus transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ME2","text":"Distinctio rei significatae et modi significandi permittit idem nomen esse verum secundum perfectionem et deficiens secundum modum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ME3","text":"Oppositio inter proprietatem et metaphoram secundum inclusionem vel exclusionem modi creati in ipso significato reordinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ME4","text":"Dilemma univocationis et aequivocationis solvitur per analogiam fundatam in ordine effectus ad causam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ME5","text":"Pluralitas nominum non amplius ut multiplicatio divinae essentiae, sed ut multiplicitas conceptionum eiusdem principii classificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ME6","text":"Relationes Dei ad creaturas in relationes reales ex parte creaturae et relationes rationis ex parte Dei distribuuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ME7","text":"Analysis nominis in originem impositionis, terminum significationis, modum significandi et gradum communicabilitatis dividitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0183","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ME8","text":"Compositio propositionis a compositione rei separatur et in operationem intellectus unitatem sub pluralitate rationum repraesentantem convertitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D15","CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0253","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ALT1","text":"Surface: investigantur nominabilitas Dei, proprietas nominum, eorum communitas, prioritas, temporalitas, communicabilitas et capacitas affirmationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:AS1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ALT2","text":"Structural: systema distinguit rem significatam, conceptionem intellectus, modum significandi, originem impositionis et ordinem analogicum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: quaestio sermonis de Deo extra oppositionem inter silentium apophaticum et praedicationem univocam constituitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:SNX2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0094","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: argumentatio praesupponit causam eminentem et simplicem, perfectiones in ea unitas, relationem creatoris et creaturae asymmetricam atque aeternitatem sine successione.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0226","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0227","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:MRT1","text":"Axiomata extracta sufficiunt ad restituendam progressionem a cognitione per creaturas ad veritatem affirmationum de Deo.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:MRT2","text":"Cohaerentia systematis pendet praecipue a distinctionibus inter significatum et modum significandi, atque inter identitatem rei et diversitatem rationum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:MRT3","text":"Firmissima commissura est analogia, quae simul similitudinem causalem retinet et adaequationem univocam excludit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:MRT4","text":"Forma exacta analogiae inter Deum et creaturam atque inter Deum verum, participatum et opinatum non uno schemate logico plene determinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0206","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0207","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:MRT5","text":"Commissura inter significationem substantialem et absentiam cognitionis quidditativae manet dependens a distinctione inter repraesentationem imperfectam et definitionem essentiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:MRT6","text":"Assertio quod Deus realiter dominus est, quamvis relatio in ipso sit tantum secundum rationem, nititur denominatione ab ordine reali creaturae et est nexus relationis maxime delicatus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0134","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0152","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0154","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:MRT7","text":"Praestantia unius nominis non est absoluta sub omnibus rationibus, quia qui est, Deus, bonum et tetragrammaton secundum diversa criteria ordinantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D13","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0226","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0227","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0229","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0231","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:MRT8","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat: cognitio mediata fundat nominationem, analogia eius ambitum regit, apophasis modum corrigit, et distinctio rationum affirmationem legitimam reddit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:FGS1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:FGS3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T155931802384Z_fd5afb4e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 13","source_sha256":"9cbba374dcf7bb38a23f71cc169948ebaf8e954388ed56a6e21aae517d1e2c36","sha256":"92f5f60301d697f134e97e43e13571d4c3ba904696f55fc000a950d065900bc4","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T15:59:31.803466+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:AS1","text":"The question is how God can truly be named and affirmatively predicated, although his essence is incomprehensible to the intellect of the wayfarer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:AS2","text":"Names taken from creatures signify God substantially but imperfectly, because they represent created perfections as pre-existing in him unitedly and eminently.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:AS3","text":"The argumentation is reconstructive, distinctive, and at once affirmative and apophatic: it does not abolish discourse about God, but distinguishes its reference, mode, and limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:AS4","text":"The mediation of intellectual conception makes it possible to distinguish the truth of a name from quidditative comprehension of the thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:AS5","text":"The movement proceeds from the possibility of naming to substantial signification, propriety, plurality of rationes, analogy, the order of predication, temporal relations, and the kinds of divine names.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:AS6","text":"The conclusion is that affirmative propositions about God are true, while a plurality of understood rationes corresponds to the one simple divine thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A1","text":"A word signifies a thing through the mediation of an intellectual conception, and thus naming follows the measure of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A2","text":"The divine essence is not known, in the state of wayfaring, according to what it is in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A3","text":"God is known from creatures according to causality, eminence, and removal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A4","text":"All perfections of creatures pre-exist in God simply, unitedly, and eminently.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A5","text":"An effect not equaling the power of its cause receives the likeness of the cause deficiently.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0093","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A6","text":"Absolute and affirmative names signify the divine substance, but represent it imperfectly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7","text":"What a name signifies is distinct from the mode in which it signifies it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A8","text":"The human mode of signifying is conformed to the cognition of composite, material, and temporal things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A9","text":"Multiple imperfect conceptions truly correspond to the one simple divine thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A10","text":"Analogical predication joins diverse rationes through an order to one first.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0115","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A11","text":"The creature is really ordered to God, but in God no real relation to the creature is constituted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A12","text":"A relative denomination can follow temporally upon a change in the creature without a change in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A13","text":"The principle from which a name is imposed can be distinguished from that which the name signifies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A14","text":"A name is properly communicated according to its whole signification only where the thing signified can be multiplied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0183","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A15","text":"The divine nature is not multiplied; nevertheless, the name can be predicated in multiple ways through participation by likeness or according to opinion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0186","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0187","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A16","text":"Divine being, identical with essence and lacking temporal succession, is signified by the indeterminate name qui est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0226","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0227","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A17","text":"The truth of an affirmation is compatible with the identity of the thing and the diversity of the rationes of subject and predicate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:A18","text":"The intellect can understand a simple thing in a composite mode without judging it to be composite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0253","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX1","text":"God's absolute unnameability is rejected; to be above naming is not the same as being unable to be named in any way.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX2","text":"No human name definitively expresses the divine essence according to what it is.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX3","text":"Affirmative names are not merely formulas removing imperfections from God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX4","text":"Affirmative names do not signify merely the relation of the divine cause to created effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX5","text":"Not all names are said of God metaphorically.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX6","text":"The identity of the divine thing does not render all divine names synonymous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX7","text":"No name is predicated simply univocally of God and creature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0093","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0094","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX8","text":"Names are not predicated of God and creatures purely equivocally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0094","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX9","text":"The priority of names is not determined uniformly from the order of human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0117","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX10","text":"Temporal relative predication does not require a real change in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX11","text":"The name Deus is not imposed with divine operation as the terminus of its signification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0167","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX12","text":"Participation in the divine nature does not constitute a multiplication of the divine nature itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0186","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0189","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX13","text":"The grammatical composition of a proposition does not entail real composition in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0253","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D1","text":"God can be named, but his name does not express the divine essence in the way that a definition expresses a created essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D2","text":"Abstract names can signify simplicity, concrete names subsistence, temporal verbs eternity under a human mode, and demonstratives an object understood.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D3","text":"The names of goodness, life, and wisdom posit something positively in the divine substance itself, although neither quidditatively nor exhaustively.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D4","text":"A name signifying perfection absolutely belongs properly to God as regards the thing signified, but not as regards the mode of signifying; whereas a name including a created mode in what is signified belongs metaphorically.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D5","text":"Several divine names signify one thing under diverse rationes and therefore do not constitute synonymy.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D6","text":"The community of names between God and creatures is analogical, because neither the same ratio nor a totally diverse ratio is maintained.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D7","text":"Names of perfections said absolutely belong first to God according to the thing, and first to creatures according to imposition; metaphorical names are said first of creatures.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D8","text":"The names lord, creator, and savior can be said of God temporally on account of a temporal relation or effect, while God remains unchanged.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0145","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0146","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0150","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D9","text":"The name Deus is imposed from universal providence, but terminates in signifying the divine nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0167","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D10","text":"The name Deus is appellative according to the mode of cognition, but incommunicable according to the thing; it is communicated only through likeness or opinion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0186","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0187","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0191","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D11","text":"Deus is said analogically by nature, by participation, and according to opinion, because the signification of the true God is included in the derivative significations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0206","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0207","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D12","text":"The truth or falsity of predication does not of itself alter the signification of the name; hence a contradiction concerning an idol can retain the same principal signification of the name Deus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0209","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D13","text":"The name qui est names God most properly, because it signifies being itself indeterminately and presentally.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0226","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0227","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D14","text":"The degree of propriety of names varies according to the criterion: qui est excels in being and mode of signifying, Deus in the nature signified, tetragrammaton in the incommunicable supposit, and good in causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0229","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0231","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:D15","text":"Affirmative propositions about God are true, because the distinction of subject and predicate represents a plurality of rationes, while composition represents the identity of the thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD1","text":"The order of cognition and imposition ascends from creatures to God, while the order of perfection and causality descends from God to creatures.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD2","text":"Affirmation and negation are not rival ways: affirmation retains perfection; negation excludes created mode and comprehension.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD3","text":"Divine simplicity does not destroy the plurality of names, but orders it, because perfections divided in effects correspond to one simple principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD4","text":"Analogy makes demonstration about God possible without reducing God and creature to a common genus or definition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD5","text":"The eternity of the divine subject is compatible with the temporality of denominations, because time arises from the change of the created terminus, not from a change in God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD6","text":"The truth of discourse about God consists in real reference without quidditative comprehension and in intellectual composition without ontological composition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0253","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX1","text":"The truth of a name does not require a comprehensive adequation of the intellect to the divine essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX2","text":"Community of a word does not require community of genus, species, or definition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX3","text":"The real identity of what is signified does not require identity of intellectual rationes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX4","text":"The mode in which the intellect knows a thing is not necessarily the mode that it attributes to the thing known.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A18","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0252","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0253","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX5","text":"The origin of a name from an effect or operation does not confine what is signified within the effect or operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX6","text":"Real denomination does not require an intrinsic real relation at both extremes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0152","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX7","text":"The distinction between true and false predication is not identical with the distinction between univocal, analogical, and equivocal signification.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D11","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0206","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0209","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX8","text":"The perfection of divine simplicity does not require discourse of one ratio, but excludes composition of the thing under a plurality of rationes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SNX1","text":"A quidditative concept of the divine essence is structurally unavailable in the state of wayfaring and circumscribes the whole doctrine of names.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0226","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0227","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SNX2","text":"The system admits neither absolute silence nor comprehensive discourse; analogy simultaneously precludes both extremes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:NX2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0094","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SNX3","text":"No neutral semantic plane is presupposed in which God and creature are comprehended under the same proportionate measure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SNX4","text":"No intradivine change need be posited in order to explain every novelty of temporal predication.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:SNX5","text":"A wholly proper human name, which both comprehends the essence and adequately signifies the divine mode, is not given within this condition of cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0226","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0227","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:FGS1","text":"The first series is: creature known → causality, eminence, and removal → imperfect conception of God → a true but non-definitive name.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:FGS2","text":"The second series is: perfection divided in the effect → united pre-existence in the cause → substantial signification → apophatic correction of the mode of signifying.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:FGS3","text":"The third series is: lack of identity of ratio excludes univocation → the real order of effect to cause excludes equivocation → analogy constitutes an intermediate community.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:FGS4","text":"The fourth series is: change of creature → real relation of creature to God → relation of reason on God's side → temporal denomination without divine change.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:FGS5","text":"The fifth series is: operation known → imposition of a name → nature signified → distinction of communicability according to thing, likeness, and opinion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0166","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0167","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0183","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0186","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0187","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:FGS6","text":"The sixth series is: one simple thing → multiple conceptions → subject and predicate distinct in ratio → composition signifying identity → true affirmation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:AF1","text":"The first movement proceeds from the nameability of God → to substantial signification → to the distinction between propriety and metaphor → to the non-synonymous plurality of names.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:AF2","text":"The second movement proceeds from the exclusion of univocation and equivocation → to analogy → to a twofold order of priority → to temporal relative predications.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:AF3","text":"The third movement proceeds from the distinction between operation and nature in the name Deus → to its limited communicability → to analogical uses → to the preeminence of the name qui est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D9","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D10","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D11","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0167","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0186","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0206","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0226","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:AF4","text":"The final movement proceeds from a plurality of rationes under one thing → to the composition of subject and predicate → to the truth of affirmative propositions about God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ME1","text":"The naming of God is transferred from direct definition of the essence to cognition mediated through effects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ME2","text":"The distinction between the thing signified and the mode of signifying allows the same name to be true according to perfection and deficient according to mode.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ME3","text":"The opposition between propriety and metaphor is reordered according to the inclusion or exclusion of the created mode in what is itself signified.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ME4","text":"The dilemma of univocation and equivocation is resolved through analogy founded on the order of effect to cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ME5","text":"The plurality of names is no longer classified as a multiplication of the divine essence, but as a multiplicity of conceptions of the same principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ME6","text":"God's relations to creatures are distributed into real relations on the side of the creature and relations of reason on the side of God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ME7","text":"The analysis of a name is divided into the origin of imposition, the terminus of signification, the mode of signifying, and the degree of communicability.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0183","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ME8","text":"The composition of a proposition is separated from the composition of the thing and converted into an operation of the intellect representing unity under a plurality of rationes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D15","CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0253","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ALT1","text":"Surface: the nameability of God, the propriety of names, their community, priority, temporality, communicability, and capacity for affirmation are investigated.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:AS1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ALT2","text":"Structural: the system distinguishes the thing signified, intellectual conception, the mode of signifying, the origin of imposition, and analogical order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: the question of discourse about God is constituted outside the opposition between apophatic silence and univocal predication.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:SNX2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0094","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: the argumentation presupposes an eminent and simple cause, perfections united in it, an asymmetric relation of creator and creature, and eternity without succession.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0143","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0226","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0227","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms suffice to reconstruct the progression from cognition through creatures to the truth of affirmations about God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:MRT2","text":"The coherence of the system depends chiefly on the distinctions between what is signified and the mode of signifying, and between the identity of the thing and the diversity of rationes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:MRT3","text":"The strongest articulation is analogy, which simultaneously retains causal likeness and excludes univocal adequation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:MRT4","text":"The exact form of analogy between God and creature, and between the true, participated, and imagined God, is not fully determined by a single logical schema.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0206","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0207","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:MRT5","text":"The articulation between substantial signification and the absence of quidditative cognition remains dependent on the distinction between imperfect representation and definition of essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:MRT6","text":"The assertion that God is really lord, although relation in him is only according to reason, rests on denomination from the real order of the creature and is the most delicate relational nexus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_5:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_5:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0134","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0152","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0154","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:MRT7","text":"The preeminence of one name is not absolute under every ratio, because qui est, Deus, good, and tetragrammaton are ordered according to different criteria.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:D13","CH_GGVC_PP_5:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0226","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0227","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0229","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0231","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_5:MRT8","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text: mediated cognition grounds naming, analogy governs its scope, apophasis corrects its mode, and the distinction of rationes makes legitimate affirmation possible.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_5:FGS1","CH_GGVC_PP_5:FGS3","CH_GGVC_PP_5:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0244","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0245","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_5:P0246","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PP_5@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_PP_5:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 14","title":"First Part · Question 13","context":"Chapter 14","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 13"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 13","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 14","order":15,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_PP_6"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PP_6@R001","sha256":"a49e06386545400f23aecf9a2ff22cd05cab6a2d553dd6e94dbcc1f373b9f7dd","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS1","text":"Quaeritur quomodo scientia Deo attribuatur sine potentialitate, compositione, dependentia vel limitatione cognitionis creatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS2","text":"Scientia in Deo perfectissime est, quia summa immaterialitas eius summam cognitionem constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS3","text":"Argumentatio est metaphysica, reconstructiva et apophatica: perfectionem cognitionis retinet, modos autem creatos eius successive excludit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS4","text":"Motus primus procedit ab immaterialitate ad actum purum, deinde ad identitatem intellectus, intelligibilis, speciei, actus intelligendi et essentiae divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS5","text":"Perfecta cognitio sui per cognitionem propriae virtutis ad cognitionem omnium effectuum, modorum participationis et distinctionum creatarum extenditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS6","text":"Unica cognitio divina dividitur secundum obiecta et rationes causalitatis, non secundum compositionem, discursum vel successionem in Deo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0125","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS7","text":"Aeternitas cognitionis futura contingentia praesentialiter et infallibiliter complectitur, quin contingentiam causarum proximarum tollat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0240","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0242","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS8","text":"Scientia sui est tantum speculativa; scientia aliorum secundum diversos respectus speculativa, practica, visionis, simplicis intelligentiae vel approbationis est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0292","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0293","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A1","text":"Immaterialitas rei est ratio cognitionis, et modus cognitionis modum immaterialitatis consequitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A2","text":"Deus est summe immaterialis et actus purus, omni potentialitate carens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A3","text":"Perfectiones creaturis communicatae in Deo eminentiore modo sunt, remoto omni modo imperfecto creaturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A4","text":"Cognitum est in cognoscente secundum modum cognoscentis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A5","text":"Intelligere est operatio immanens quae in intelligente manet ut actus et perfectio eius.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0065","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A6","text":"Essentia Dei est ipsum eius esse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A7","text":"Substantia divina omni compositione, mutatione atque successione caret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0161","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0275","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A8","text":"Unumquodque cognoscibile est secundum modum quo est in actu.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0049","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A9","text":"Perfecta cognitio rei cognitionem totius virtutis eius et omnium ad quae illa virtus extenditur includit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A10","text":"Omnes perfectiones creaturarum, tam communes quam distinctivae, in Deo eminenter praeexistunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0105","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A11","text":"Causalitas divina ad omnes entitates et ad omnia principia constitutiva rerum, etiam ad materiam individuantem, extenditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0201","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12","text":"Essentia divina est species intelligibilis intellectus divini et similitudo eminens omnium quae causari vel participari possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A13","text":"Scientia non causat effectum determinatum nisi quatenus voluntas inclinationem ad effectum adiungit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0142","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A14","text":"Quod actu non est, quatenus in potentia activa, passiva, cognitiva vel significabili est, modum essendi et veritatis cognoscibilem habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0163","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A15","text":"Malum est privatio boni et non est per se cognoscibile nisi per bonum cuius privatio est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A16","text":"Aeternitas est tota simul et totum tempus absque successione comprehendit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0161","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A17","text":"Contingens in praesentia actuali determinatum est, sed respectu causae proximae ad opposita manet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A18","text":"Scientia speculativa et practica distinguuntur secundum rem scitam, modum cognoscendi et finem cognitionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0292","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX1","text":"Scientia divina non est qualitas, habitus medius inter potentiam et actum, neque cognitio ab alio causata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX2","text":"Scientia divina non est universalis, particularis, habitualis vel potentialis secundum modum scientiae creatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX3","text":"Cognitio sui in Deo non est motus, passio, reditus post exitum neque perfectio recepta ab obiecto.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX4","text":"Comprehensio sui non significat quod Deus a distincto intellectu includatur vel finiatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX5","text":"Intelligere divinum non est operatio ab essentia procedens neque accidens secundarium quod regressum infinitum cognitionum inducat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0072","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX6","text":"Res aliae non perficiunt, non specificant neque mensurant intellectum divinum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0091","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX7","text":"Deus res alias non cognoscit tantum sub communi ratione entis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0103","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX8","text":"Scientia divina non procedit successive ab uno obiecto ad aliud neque causaliter a noto ad ignotum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0125","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX9","text":"Scientia secundum solam rationem cognitionis non est causa rerum sine voluntate determinante.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0142","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX10","text":"Aeternitas scientiae causalis non requirit aeternitatem creaturarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0147","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX11","text":"Non omne quod Deus cognoscit est actu, fuit, erit, vult aut causat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0167","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX12","text":"Malum non est ens positivum, similitudo divina neque effectus scientiae divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0184","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0186","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX13","text":"Singularia non cognoscuntur sufficienter ex solis causis universalibus neque ex applicatione universalium ad particularia iam incognita.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0200","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0201","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX14","text":"Infinita non cognoscuntur a Deo enumeratione partium vel transitu successivo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0223","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX15","text":"Infallibilis cognitio futuri contingentis non facit effectum absolute necessarium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0240","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0242","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0248","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX16","text":"Enuntiabilia non cognoscuntur a Deo per compositionem et divisionem intellectus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0262","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX17","text":"Variatio rerum vel veritatis enuntiabilium non inducit variationem scientiae divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0283","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX18","text":"Scientia speculativa non recipitur necessario per abstractionem a rebus, neque omnis cognitio operabilium est simpliciter practica.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0293","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0298","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D1","text":"Scientia in Deo perfectissime est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D2","text":"Deus seipsum per seipsum intelligit, quia intellectus, species intelligibilis et obiectum in actu non distinguuntur in actu puro.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D3","text":"Deus se perfecte comprehendit, quia se tantum cognoscit quantum cognoscibilis est, nullo sui latente.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D4","text":"Intelligere Dei est eius essentia et esse, atque intellectus, intellectum, species intelligibilis et actus intelligendi omnino unum sunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D5","text":"Deus alia a se cognoscit in seipso, quia perfecta cognitio propriae virtutis omnes effectus eius complectitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6","text":"Deus res omnes propria cognitione cognoscit secundum modos quibus perfectionem eius distincte participant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D7","text":"Deus omnia simul et sine discursu cognoscit, quia omnia in unica essentia sua cognoscit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D8","text":"Scientia divina, voluntate coniuncta, est causa rerum et secundum hunc respectum scientia approbationis dicitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9","text":"Deus praeter praesentia cognoscit praeterita et futura scientia visionis, possibilia autem numquam actualia scientia simplicis intelligentiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0161","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D10","text":"Deus mala perfecte cognoscit cognoscendo bona quibus mala privative opponuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D11","text":"Deus singularia secundum singularitatem cognoscit, quia causalitas et species intelligibilis divina ad principia individuantia extenduntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0201","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0204","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D12","text":"Scientia Dei ad infinita etiam ut ab invicem distincta extenditur, nec successiva enumeratione indiget.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0219","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0221","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0223","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D13","text":"Futura contingentia infallibiliter cognoscuntur ut aeternitati praesentia, sed contingentia manent secundum causas proximas.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0240","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0242","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D14","text":"Deus omnia enuntiabilia formabilia cognoscit simplici intelligentia essentiarum et eorum quae eis convenire possunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0260","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D15","text":"Scientia Dei est omnino invariabilis, quamvis res variabiles et enuntiabilia temporaliter vera vel falsa cognoscat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0275","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0283","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D16","text":"Deus de seipso scientiam tantum speculativam habet, quia ipse operabilis non est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0293","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D17","text":"Deus de rebus aliis scientiam speculativam secundum modum habet et scientiam practicam de iis quae ad operationem temporalem ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0293","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D18","text":"Possibilia numquam efficienda non cadunt sub scientia practica secundum finem, mala autem practice cognoscuntur quatenus permittuntur, impediuntur vel ordinantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD1","text":"Simplicitas divina non minuit amplitudinem cognitionis, sed efficit ut tota multiplicitas cognitorum uno actu absque interna multiplicatione cognoscatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0127","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD2","text":"Cognitio sui in Deo non est clausura reflexiva, sed principium cognitionis omnium modorum quibus essentia divina participari vel imitari potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D3","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD3","text":"Ordo mensurationis epistemicae inter Deum et creaturas convertitur: res mensurant scientiam humanam, scientia autem divina mensurat et causat res.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0149","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD4","text":"Omniscientia divina non est totalitas indifferens, sed comprehendit distinctiones inter actuale, possibile, privativum, causatum, permissum et mere cognoscibile.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0167","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD5","text":"Necessitas cognitionis, necessitas rei ut praesentialiter cognitae et necessitas rei secundum causas proprias sunt ordines distincti.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D13","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0242","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0248","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD6","text":"Non ens et malum cognitionem divinam non terminant sicut obiecta positiva sui iuris, sed per potentiam vel privationem in ordine ad esse et bonum cognoscuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0163","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0188","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD7","text":"Scientia practica omnium aliorum intra scientiam speculativam sui continetur, quia Deus alia operatur et cognoscit per essentiam quam speculative intuetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D16","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0300","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX1","text":"Modi cognitionis creatae non possunt univoce in Deum transferri.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX2","text":"Pluralitas cognitorum non requirit pluralitatem specierum, actuum vel partium in cognoscente divino.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0127","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX3","text":"Nulla creatura potest esse ratio formalis, perfectio recepta vel mensura cognitionis divinae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0091","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX4","text":"Actualitas praesentis mundi non constituit limitem cognoscibilitatis divinae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0219","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX5","text":"Certitudo cognitionis non est convertibilis cum necessitate absoluta obiecti.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D13","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0248","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX6","text":"Index temporalis enuntiabilis non introducit successionem intrinsecam in scientiam divinam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D14","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0283","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX7","text":"Malum non potest constituere principium positivum cognitionis, causalitatis vel oppositionis ad essentiam divinam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0184","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0186","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX8","text":"Transcendentia cognitionis divinae non significat cognitionem confusam vel solum universalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX9","text":"Universalitas causalitatis divinae nullam determinationem creatam relinquit structuraliter extra cognitionem principii causantis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0201","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SNX1","text":"Nulla exterioritas epistemica admittitur ex qua Deus speciem, perfectionem vel notitiam recipiat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0091","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SNX2","text":"Nullum residuum essentiae, virtutis vel participabilitatis divinae cognitionem Dei effugit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D3","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SNX3","text":"Nullum prius et posterius epistemicum inter divina cognita locum habet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0125","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SNX4","text":"Nulla res actualis, possibilis, singularis, infinita vel enuntiabilis extra ambitum unicae speciei intelligibilis divinae relinquitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0204","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0219","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SNX5","text":"Oppositio inter simplicitatem cognitionis et articulationem omnium differentiarum creatarum praecluditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SNX6","text":"Nulla necessitas extrinseca ex scientia divina ad actualizationem omnium possibilium procedit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0142","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0167","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SNX7","text":"Nulla entitas positiva mali requiritur vel admittitur ad plenitudinem cognitionis divinae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0188","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS1","text":"Praedicatio divina hac operatione generatur: perfectio creata → remotio modi potentialis et compositi → retentio perfectionis → attributio eminens Deo.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS2","text":"Structura cognitionis sui est: summa immaterialitas → actus purus → nulla distinctio inter intellectum et speciem → identitas intelligere-essentia-esse → comprehensio perfecta sui.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D3","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS3","text":"Extensio cognitionis est: perfecta cognitio sui → cognitio virtutis → cognitio causalitatis → cognitio omnium modorum participationis → cognitio propria omnium rerum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS4","text":"Distributio modalis est: actuale temporale → scientia visionis; possibile non actuale → scientia simplicis intelligentiae; volitum vel permissum ad effectum → scientia approbationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0161","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0167","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS5","text":"Cognitio privativi generatur per ordinem: bonum positive cognitum → defectus boni possibilis → malum ut privatio → cognitio mali sine similitudine positiva mali in Deo.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0188","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS6","text":"Concordia praescientiae et contingentiae generatur per distinctionem: aeternitas tota simul → eventus praesens divino intuitui → infallibilitas cognitionis → contingentia servata in causa proxima.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0240","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0242","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS7","text":"Cognitio enuntiabilium generatur per ordinem: essentia divina omnia repraesentat → essentiae et habitudines rerum uno actu cognoscuntur → veritas enuntiabilis cognoscitur → nulla compositio in intellectu divino ponitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D14","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0260","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0283","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS8","text":"Classificatio finalis procedit per tres axes: operabilitas obiecti → modus considerationis → ordinatio ad effectum; inde scientia sui est tantum speculativa, scientia aliorum varie speculativa et practica.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A18","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D16","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D17","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0292","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0293","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AF1","text":"Motus principalis est: scientia divina quaesita → cognitio ex immaterialitate → identitas cognitionis et essentiae → perfecta cognitio sui → cognitio omnium aliorum in seipso.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0085","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AF2","text":"Expansio obiectiva est: alia in communi → alia secundum proprias differentias → singularia → infinita → non entia → mala → enuntiabilia.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0201","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0219","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AF3","text":"Expansio modalis est: cognitio simplex et simultanea → causalitas voluntate determinata → praesentia aeternitatis → infallibilitas sine necessitatione absoluta → invariabilitas → distinctio speculativi et practici.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D13","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D15","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D16","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0239","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0275","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0292","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ME1","text":"Scientia ex habitu acquisito in actum substantialem et subsistentem reclassificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ME2","text":"Simplicitas, quae primo obstaculum cognitionis multiplicis videbatur, in principium cognitionis universalis convertitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0127","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ME3","text":"Multiplicitas cognitorum ex interiori compositione cognoscentis ad multiplicem imitabilitatem unicae essentiae transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ME4","text":"Oppositio inter cognitionem sui et cognitionem aliorum solvitur, quia cognitio aliorum intra perfectam cognitionem causalitatis propriae continetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ME5","text":"Ordo cognoscendi divinus ab ordine humano distinguitur: homo a rebus ad scientiam procedit, Deus a scientia et voluntate ad res.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0142","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0149","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ME6","text":"Ens actuale desinit esse unicus terminus scientiae, quia possibilia et privationes secundum diversos modos cognoscibilitatis includuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0188","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ME7","text":"Oppositio universalis et singularis relativizatur ad intellectum abstrahentem, non ad intellectum causaliter omnia principia continentem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0201","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0204","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ME8","text":"Oppositio infallibilitatis et contingentiae solvitur distinctione inter praesentiam obiecti cognitioni et modum eius in causa proxima.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D13","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0240","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0242","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ME9","text":"Speculativum et practicum ex generibus exclusive oppositis in respectus distinguibiles secundum rem, modum et finem convertuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A18","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D16","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D17","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0292","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0293","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ALT1","text":"Gradus superficiei constituitur assertionibus quod Deus se, alia, possibilia, mala, singularia, infinita, contingentia et enuntiabilia perfecte cognoscit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D13","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0199","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0219","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ALT2","text":"Gradus structuralis cognitionem divinam in identitate essentiae, actus intelligendi, speciei intelligibilis, causalitatis et praesentiae aeternitatis fundat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0239","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ALT3","text":"Gradus metastrategicus modos creatos cognitionis prius removet et deinde distinctiones obiectivas sine multiplicatione divina restituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS3","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ALT4","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis praesupponit realitatem ut participationem actus divini, causalitatem ut mensuram veritatis rerum, et aeternitatem ut praesentiam non successivam totius temporis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD3","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0161","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0165","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0149","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:MRT1","text":"Axiomata extracta sufficiunt ad restituendam progressionem a perfectione cognitionis divinae ad amplitudinem obiectivam et distinctiones modales scientiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A18","CH_GGVC_PP_6:AF1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:AF2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0201","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0219","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0292","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:MRT2","text":"Structura interne cohaeret per regulam constantem quod cognitum recipitur secundum modum cognoscentis, unde multiplicitas rerum non multiplicat Deum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:MRT3","text":"Firmissima commissura est nexus inter actum purum, identitatem intelligere-esse et cognitionem simplicem ac simultaneam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:MRT4","text":"Probationes simplicitatis, aeternitatis, universalitatis causalitatis et identitatis essentiae cum esse hic praesupponuntur ex superioribus, non intra quaestionem integre restituuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0275","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:MRT5","text":"Commissura fragilior est transitus ab eminentia perfectionum divinarum ad cognitionem propriam omnium differentiarum, quia nititur doctrina participationis et continentiae eminentis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0105","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:MRT6","text":"Concordia praescientiae et contingentiae distinctione inter praesentialitatem aeternam et causalitatem proximam servatur, sed modus concursus causae primae cum causa contingenti ulterius non explicatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D13","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0239","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0242","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:MRT7","text":"Distinctio inter scientiam causaliter approbantem et scientiam mere cognoscentem voluntatem divinam requirit, cuius structura in hac quaestione nondum explicatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0142","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:MRT8","text":"Inclusio mali sub cognitione practica per permissionem, impeditionem et ordinationem a posteriori doctrina providentiae et voluntatis pendet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:MRT9","text":"Restitutio servat verum motum textus: purificatio praedicati scientiae → identitas cognitionis et essentiae → extensio ad omnia cognoscibilia → distributio causalitatis, temporalitatis et praxis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS3","CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:AF1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:AF2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0292","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T160006049137Z_ee28fe79","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 14","source_sha256":"a49e06386545400f23aecf9a2ff22cd05cab6a2d553dd6e94dbcc1f373b9f7dd","sha256":"f8e6cff9cd579403504c4675168ec9f1866575856f9db874333cf4cb84eadcce","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T16:00:06.050612+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS1","text":"It is asked how knowledge is attributed to God without potentiality, composition, dependence, or limitation by created knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS2","text":"Knowledge is most perfectly in God, because His supreme immateriality constitutes supreme cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS3","text":"The argumentation is metaphysical, reconstructive, and apophatic: it retains the perfection of cognition, but successively excludes its created modes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS4","text":"The first movement proceeds from immateriality to pure act, then to the identity of intellect, intelligible object, species, act of understanding, and divine essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS5","text":"Perfect knowledge of Himself extends, through knowledge of His own power, to knowledge of all effects, modes of participation, and created distinctions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS6","text":"The single divine knowledge is divided according to objects and modes of causality, not according to composition, discursion, or succession in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0125","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS7","text":"The eternity of cognition comprehends future contingents as present and infallibly, without removing the contingency of proximate causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0240","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0242","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS8","text":"Knowledge of Himself is only speculative; knowledge of other things, according to diverse respects, is speculative, practical, of vision, of simple intelligence, or of approval.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0292","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0293","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A1","text":"The immateriality of a thing is the ground of cognition, and the mode of cognition follows the mode of immateriality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A2","text":"God is supremely immaterial and pure act, lacking all potentiality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A3","text":"The perfections communicated to creatures are in God in a more eminent mode, with every imperfect mode of the creature removed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A4","text":"The known is in the knower according to the mode of the knower.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A5","text":"To understand is an immanent operation which remains in the one understanding as his act and perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0065","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A6","text":"The essence of God is His very being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A7","text":"The divine substance lacks all composition, change, and succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0161","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0275","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A8","text":"Everything is knowable according to the mode in which it is in act.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0049","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A9","text":"Perfect knowledge of a thing includes knowledge of its whole power and of all things to which that power extends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A10","text":"All perfections of creatures, both common and distinctive, pre-exist eminently in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0105","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A11","text":"Divine causality extends to all entities and to all constitutive principles of things, even to individuating matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0201","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12","text":"The divine essence is the intelligible species of the divine intellect and the eminent likeness of all things that can be caused or participated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A13","text":"Knowledge does not cause a determinate effect except insofar as will adds an inclination toward the effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0142","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A14","text":"What is not in act, insofar as it is in active, passive, cognitive, or signifiable potency, has a knowable mode of being and truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0163","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A15","text":"Evil is the privation of good and is not per se knowable except through the good of which it is the privation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A16","text":"Eternity is all at once and comprehends all time without succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0161","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A17","text":"A contingent thing is determinate in actual presence, but with respect to its proximate cause remains open to opposites.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:A18","text":"Speculative and practical knowledge are distinguished according to the thing known, the mode of knowing, and the end of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0292","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX1","text":"Divine knowledge is not a quality, a habit intermediate between potency and act, nor cognition caused by another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX2","text":"Divine knowledge is not universal, particular, habitual, or potential according to the mode of created knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX3","text":"God's knowledge of Himself is not motion, passion, a return after going forth, or a perfection received from an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX4","text":"His comprehension of Himself does not signify that God is contained or bounded by a distinct intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX5","text":"Divine understanding is not an operation proceeding from the essence, nor a secondary accident that induces an infinite regress of cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0072","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX6","text":"Other things neither perfect, specify, nor measure the divine intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0091","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX7","text":"God does not know other things only under the common notion of being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0103","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX8","text":"Divine knowledge does not proceed successively from one object to another, nor causally from what is known to what is unknown.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0125","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX9","text":"Knowledge according to the mere notion of cognition is not the cause of things without a determining will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0142","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX10","text":"The eternity of causal knowledge does not require the eternity of creatures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0147","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX11","text":"Not everything that God knows is actual, has been, will be, He wills, or He causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0167","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX12","text":"Evil is not a positive being, a divine likeness, or an effect of divine knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0184","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0186","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX13","text":"Singulars are not sufficiently known from universal causes alone, nor from the application of universals to particulars still unknown.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0200","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0201","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX14","text":"Infinities are not known by God through enumeration of parts or successive passage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0223","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX15","text":"Infallible knowledge of a future contingent does not make the effect absolutely necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0240","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0242","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0248","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX16","text":"Enuntiables are not known by God through composition and division of the intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0262","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX17","text":"The variation of things or of the truth of enuntiables does not induce variation in divine knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0283","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX18","text":"Speculative knowledge is not necessarily received through abstraction from things, nor is every cognition of operable things simply practical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0293","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0298","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D1","text":"Knowledge is most perfectly in God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D2","text":"God understands Himself through Himself, because intellect, intelligible species, and object in act are not distinct in pure act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D3","text":"God perfectly comprehends Himself, because He knows Himself only as much as He is knowable, with nothing of Himself hidden.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D4","text":"God's understanding is His essence and being, and intellect, the understood, intelligible species, and act of understanding are altogether one.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D5","text":"God knows things other than Himself in Himself, because perfect knowledge of His own power comprehends all its effects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6","text":"God knows all things by proper knowledge according to the modes in which they distinctly participate in His perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D7","text":"God knows all things simultaneously and without discursion, because He knows all things in His single essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D8","text":"Divine knowledge, conjoined with will, is the cause of things and according to this respect is called knowledge of approval.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9","text":"Besides present things, God knows past and future things by knowledge of vision, but possibles never actual by knowledge of simple intelligence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0161","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D10","text":"God knows evils perfectly by knowing the goods to which evils are privatively opposed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D11","text":"God knows singulars according to their singularity, because divine causality and intelligible species extend to individuating principles.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0201","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0204","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D12","text":"The knowledge of God extends to infinities even as distinct from one another, and does not require successive enumeration.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0219","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0221","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0223","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D13","text":"Future contingents are known infallibly as present to eternity, but remain contingent according to proximate causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0240","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0242","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D14","text":"God knows all formable enuntiables by simple intelligence of essences and of what can belong to them.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0260","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D15","text":"The knowledge of God is altogether invariable, although He knows variable things and enuntiables temporally true or false.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0275","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0283","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D16","text":"God has only speculative knowledge of Himself, because He Himself is not operable.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0293","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D17","text":"God has speculative knowledge of other things according to one mode and practical knowledge of those which He orders to temporal operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0293","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:D18","text":"Possibles never to be effected do not fall under practical knowledge according to its end, but evils are known practically insofar as they are permitted, impeded, or ordered.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD1","text":"Divine simplicity does not diminish the breadth of cognition, but makes it such that the whole multiplicity of known things is known by one act without internal multiplication.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0127","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD2","text":"God's knowledge of Himself is not reflexive closure, but the principle of knowledge of all modes in which the divine essence can be participated or imitated.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D3","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD3","text":"The order of epistemic measurement between God and creatures is reversed: things measure human knowledge, whereas divine knowledge measures and causes things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0149","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD4","text":"Divine omniscience is not an indifferent totality, but comprehends distinctions between actual, possible, privative, caused, permitted, and merely knowable.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0167","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD5","text":"The necessity of cognition, the necessity of a thing as known present, and the necessity of a thing according to its proper causes are distinct orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D13","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0242","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0248","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD6","text":"Non-being and evil do not terminate divine cognition as positive objects in their own right, but are known through potency or privation in relation to being and good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0163","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0188","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD7","text":"Practical knowledge of all other things is contained within speculative knowledge of Himself, because God operates and knows other things through the essence which He speculatively beholds.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D16","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0300","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX1","text":"The modes of created cognition cannot be transferred univocally to God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX2","text":"The plurality of known things does not require a plurality of species, acts, or parts in the divine knower.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0127","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX3","text":"No creature can be a formal principle, received perfection, or measure of divine cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0091","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX4","text":"The actuality of the present world does not constitute a limit of divine knowability.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0219","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX5","text":"The certainty of cognition is not convertible with the absolute necessity of the object.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D13","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0248","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX6","text":"The temporal index of an enuntiable does not introduce intrinsic succession into divine knowledge.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D14","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0283","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX7","text":"Evil cannot constitute a positive principle of cognition, causality, or opposition to the divine essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0184","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0186","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX8","text":"The transcendence of divine cognition does not signify confused or merely universal cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX9","text":"The universality of divine causality leaves no created determination structurally outside the cognition of the causing principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0201","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SNX1","text":"No epistemic exteriority is admitted from which God might receive species, perfection, or knowledge.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0091","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SNX2","text":"No remainder of divine essence, power, or participability escapes God's cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D3","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SNX3","text":"No epistemic prior and posterior obtains among divine known things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0125","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SNX4","text":"No actual, possible, singular, infinite, or enuntiable thing is left outside the scope of the single divine intelligible species.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0204","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0219","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SNX5","text":"The opposition between simplicity of cognition and articulation of all created differences is precluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SNX6","text":"No extrinsic necessity proceeds from divine knowledge to the actualization of all possibles.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0142","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0167","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:SNX7","text":"No positive entity of evil is required or admitted for the fullness of divine cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0188","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS1","text":"Divine predication is generated by this operation: created perfection → removal of potential and composite mode → retention of perfection → eminent attribution to God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_6:NX1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS2","text":"The structure of self-knowledge is: supreme immateriality → pure act → no distinction between intellect and species → identity understanding-essence-being → perfect self-comprehension.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D3","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS3","text":"The extension of cognition is: perfect self-knowledge → knowledge of power → knowledge of causality → knowledge of all modes of participation → proper knowledge of all things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS4","text":"The modal distribution is: temporal actual → knowledge of vision; non-actual possible → knowledge of simple intelligence; willed or permitted unto effect → knowledge of approval.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0161","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0167","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS5","text":"Knowledge of the privative is generated through the order: positively known good → possible defect of good → evil as privation → knowledge of evil without a positive likeness of evil in God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0188","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS6","text":"The concord of foreknowledge and contingency is generated through the distinction: eternity all at once → event present to divine intuition → infallibility of cognition → contingency preserved in the proximate cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0240","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0242","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS7","text":"Knowledge of enuntiables is generated through the order: divine essence represents all things → essences and relations of things are known by one act → enuntiable truth is known → no composition is posited in the divine intellect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D14","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0260","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0283","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS8","text":"The final classification proceeds through three axes: operability of the object → mode of consideration → ordination to effect; hence knowledge of Himself is only speculative, while knowledge of other things is variously speculative and practical.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A18","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D16","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D17","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0292","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0293","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AF1","text":"The principal movement is: divine knowledge sought → cognition from immateriality → identity of cognition and essence → perfect self-knowledge → knowledge of all other things in Himself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0085","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AF2","text":"The objective expansion is: other things in common → other things according to their proper differences → singulars → infinities → non-beings → evils → enuntiables.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0201","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0219","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:AF3","text":"The modal expansion is: simple and simultaneous cognition → causality determined by will → presence of eternity → infallibility without absolute necessitation → invariability → distinction of speculative and practical.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D13","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D15","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D16","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0239","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0275","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0292","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ME1","text":"Knowledge is reclassified from an acquired habit into a substantial and subsistent act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ME2","text":"Simplicity, which first appeared an obstacle to multiple cognition, is converted into the principle of universal cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0127","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ME3","text":"The multiplicity of known things is transferred from the interior composition of the knower to the manifold imitability of the single essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ME4","text":"The opposition between knowledge of self and knowledge of others is resolved, because knowledge of others is contained within perfect knowledge of one's own causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ME5","text":"The divine order of knowing is distinguished from the human order: man proceeds from things to knowledge, God from knowledge and will to things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0142","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0149","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ME6","text":"Actual being ceases to be the sole terminus of knowledge, because possibles and privations are included according to diverse modes of knowability.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0188","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ME7","text":"The opposition of universal and singular is relativized to the abstracting intellect, not to the intellect causally containing all principles.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0201","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0204","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ME8","text":"The opposition of infallibility and contingency is resolved by the distinction between the presence of the object to cognition and its mode in the proximate cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D13","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0240","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0242","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ME9","text":"The speculative and practical are converted from exclusively opposed genera into distinguishable respects according to thing, mode, and end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A18","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D16","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D17","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0292","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0293","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ALT1","text":"The surface level is constituted by assertions that God perfectly knows Himself, other things, possibles, evils, singulars, infinities, contingents, and enuntiables.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D13","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0199","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0219","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ALT2","text":"The structural level grounds divine cognition in the identity of essence, act of understanding, intelligible species, causality, and the presence of eternity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0239","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ALT3","text":"The metastrategic level first removes created modes of cognition and then restores objective distinctions without divine multiplication.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS3","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:ALT4","text":"The ontological and temporal level presupposes reality as participation in divine act, causality as the measure of the truth of things, and eternity as the non-successive presence of all time.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD3","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0161","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0165","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0149","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms suffice to restore the progression from the perfection of divine cognition to the objective breadth and modal distinctions of knowledge.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A18","CH_GGVC_PP_6:AF1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:AF2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0201","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0219","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0292","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:MRT2","text":"The structure coheres internally through the constant rule that the known is received according to the mode of the knower, whence the multiplicity of things does not multiply God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:MRT3","text":"The strongest joint is the nexus between pure act, the identity of understanding-being, and simple and simultaneous cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:MRT4","text":"The proofs of simplicity, eternity, universality of causality, and identity of essence with being are here presupposed from what precedes; they are not fully reconstructed within the question.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0275","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:MRT5","text":"The weaker joint is the transition from the eminence of divine perfections to proper knowledge of all differences, because it rests on the doctrine of participation and eminent containment.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0105","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:MRT6","text":"The concord of foreknowledge and contingency is preserved by the distinction between eternal presence and proximate causality, but the mode of concurrence of the first cause with a contingent cause is not further explained.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D13","CH_GGVC_PP_6:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0239","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0242","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:MRT7","text":"The distinction between causally approving knowledge and merely knowing knowledge requires the divine will, whose structure is not yet explained in this question.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0142","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:MRT8","text":"The inclusion of evil under practical knowledge through permission, impediment, and ordination depends on the subsequent doctrine of providence and will.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:D10","CH_GGVC_PP_6:D18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0294","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_6:MRT9","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement of the text: purification of the predicate of knowledge → identity of cognition and essence → extension to all knowables → distribution of causality, temporality, and praxis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS3","CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS4","CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS5","CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS6","CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS7","CH_GGVC_PP_6:FGS8","CH_GGVC_PP_6:AF1","CH_GGVC_PP_6:AF2","CH_GGVC_PP_6:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0143","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0160","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0180","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0238","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0259","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_6:P0292","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PP_6@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_PP_6:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 15","title":"First Part · Question 14","context":"Chapter 15","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 14"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 14","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 15","order":16,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_PP_7"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PP_7@R001","sha256":"55e2255ba98dfe17615cec70f5673e8bbc4b95bf931333ee9af2601c36e0cef5","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:AS1","text":"Quaestio investigat utrum ideae in Deo sint, quomodo una essentia simplicissima plures rationes rerum contineat, et quatenus idea ad omnia divinae cognitionis obiecta se extendat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:AS2","text":"Argumentatio ideam reconstituit non ut rem separatam, sed ut formam rei in intellectu divino existentem, quae simul exemplar productionis et ratio cognitionis esse potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:AS3","text":"Ex creatione mundi per intellectum et ex intentione ordinis universi infertur Deum non solum formam universalem, sed proprias rationes plurium rerum intelligere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:AS4","text":"Multiplicitas idearum non introducit compositionem in Deo, quia non multiplicat essentiam nec species quibus Deus intelligit, sed rationes intellectas quibus una essentia imitabilis ad diversas creaturas comparatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:AS5","text":"Ambitus idearum dividitur secundum duplicem munus: exemplar respicit ea quae divina actione producuntur; ratio cognoscitiva etiam ea comprehendit quae non producuntur, dummodo propria ratione cognoscantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A1","text":"Idea est forma alicuius rei praeter rem ipsam existens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A2","text":"Forma extra rem vel exemplar eius est vel principium cognitionis eius.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A3","text":"Agens intellectuale agit ad formam cuius similitudo intelligibiliter in ipso praeexistit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A4","text":"Mundus a Deo non casu, sed per intellectum agentem producitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A5","text":"Essentia divina est similitudo omnium rerum et principium operativum earum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A6","text":"Bonum ordinis universi est finis proprie intentus a principali agente.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A7","text":"Ratio totius non habetur sine propriis rationibus eorum ex quibus totum constituitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A8","text":"Simplicitas intellectus non excludit multitudinem obiectorum intellectorum; excludit multitudinem formarum quibus intellectus actuatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A9","text":"Deus suam essentiam perfecte cognoscit secundum omnes modos quibus est cognoscibilis et imitabilis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A10","text":"Quaelibet creatura propriam speciem habet secundum proprium modum participationis similitudinis divinae essentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A11","text":"Ideae multiplicantur per respectus intellectos quibus Deus essentiam suam ad res comparat, non per respectus reales a creaturis causatos.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A12","text":"Idea ut exemplar est principium factionis; idea ut ratio est principium cognitionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A13","text":"Malum non cognoscitur a Deo per propriam rationem, sed per rationem boni.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A14","text":"Materia secundum se nec esse nec cognoscibilitatem habet; cognoscitur et ideam habet solum cum ordine ad compositum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:NX1","text":"Idea divina non est forma separata extra intellectum Dei existens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:NX2","text":"Idea non est medium extrinsecum quo Deus res cognoscit praeter suam essentiam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:NX3","text":"Pluralitas idearum non est pluralitas essentiarum, artium, sapientiarum aut specierum intellectualium in Deo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:NX4","text":"Relationes ideales non sunt relationes reales in Deo eius simplicitatem multiplicantes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:NX5","text":"Malum non habet ideam propriam neque ut exemplar neque ut ratio cognoscitiva.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:NX6","text":"Genus non habet exemplar distinctum ab exemplari speciei, quando non producitur nisi in specie.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:NX7","text":"Materia non habet ideam distinctam ab idea compositi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:D1","text":"Idea divina est ipsa essentia divina non absolute sumpta, sed ut similitudo et ratio determinatae rei.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:D2","text":"Cum mundus per intellectum divinum fiat, forma mundi in mente divina praeexistens necessario est exemplar mundi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:D3","text":"Intentio divina ordinis universi exigit rationes proprias partium universi, non solam rationem primi creati.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:D4","text":"Plures ideae sunt plures rationes propriae creaturarum intellectae ex una essentia divina.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:D5","text":"Aeternitas pluralitatis idearum non dependet ab aeternitate creaturarum, quia relationes ideales ex actu intellectus divini procedunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:D6","text":"Eadem ratio divina potest esse exemplar rei fiendae et ratio speculativa rei non fiendae, sed hae functiones non sunt coextensivae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:D7","text":"Omnia quae Deus cognoscit per proprias rationes habent ideam ut rationem, sed non omnia habent ideam ut exemplar effectivum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:D8","text":"Privatio mali ex ordine idearum sequitur, quia malum intelligitur per bonum et non ut ratio positiva propria.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:SD1","text":"Systema simul servat unitatem ontologicam Dei et multiplicitatem intelligibilem creaturarum, ponendo distinctionem non in esse divino sed in rationibus intellectis essentiae imitabilis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:SD2","text":"Ordo universi non est effectus emergens ex successione causarum secundarum, sed obiectum primarium intentionis divinae quod rationes singularium sub se ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:SD3","text":"Cognitio divina superat ordinem actualitatis temporalis, quia ratio cognoscitiva potest se extendere ad possibilia non futura, dum exemplar restringitur ad id quod cadit sub factione divina.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:SD4","text":"Individuatio, materia, genera et accidentia non recipiunt eandem rationem idealem, sed classificatio eorum pendet ex modo quo ad productionem compositi et ad providentiam divinam referuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet quod divina causalitas sit actio sine forma intellecta, quia tunc mundus per intellectum factus exemplar intelligibile non requireret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet quod creaturae sint tantum effectus indistincti unius causae, quia intentio ordinis universi proprias rationes constituentium exigit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet quod omnis distinctio intelligibilis multiplicem rem vel compositionem ontologicam importet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet quod cognosci idem sit ac positive propriam rationem habere, quia malum cognoscitur sine idea propria.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse debet quod omne cognitum sit producibile eodem modo quo cognoscitur, quia ratio speculativa excedit exemplar practicum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:SNX1","text":"Systema excludit autonomiam formarum ab intellectu et essentia divina: nulla ratio rerum subsistit ut principium per se positum extra Deum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:SNX2","text":"Systema excludit contingentiam radicalem ordinis universi: multitudo rerum intelligibiliter ordinata praesupponit intentionem divinam antecedentem omnem successionem creaturarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:SNX3","text":"Systema excludit positivam intelligibilitatem mali ut termini primi: malum intra cognitionem manet dependens a bono cuius privatio dicitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:SNX4","text":"Systema excludit materiam ut principium completum et per se cognoscibile, quia materia tantum sub forma et in composito intrat ordinem ideae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:FGS1","text":"Creatio non casualis → actio per intellectum → forma intellecta praeexistens → exemplar divinum → necessitas idearum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:FGS2","text":"Intentio ordinis universi → ratio totius → rationes propriae partium → multiplices rationes intellectae → pluralitas idearum sine pluralitate essentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:FGS3","text":"Distinctio exemplar/rationis → discretio factionis a cognitione → extensio rationum ad possibilia → exclusio ideae propriae mali → classificatio limitata materiae, generum, accidentium et singularium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:AF1","text":"Sufficientia apparens essentiae divinae → definitio ideae ut forma extra rem → creatio intellectualis mundi → essentia ut exemplar rerum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:AF2","text":"Unitas essentiae divinae → intentio ordinis universi → rationes propriae constituentium → pluralitas idearum intellectarum → conservatio simplicitatis divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:AF3","text":"Identificatio ideae cum exemplari et ratione → distinctio practicae et speculativae cognitionis → delimitatio ambitus idearum → negatio ideae propriae mali et idearum separatarum quarundam universalium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:ME1","text":"Idea reclassificatur ab ente separato ad modum relationis intelligibilis quo essentia divina significatur ut ratio determinatae creaturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:ME2","text":"Oppositio inter unitatem divinam et multiplicitatem rerum dissolvitur per distinctionem inter unitatem rei intelligentis et multitudinem rationum intellectarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:ME3","text":"Cognitio divina ordinatur secundum duo axes non coincidentes, factionem exemplarem et intelligentiam rationalem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:ME4","text":"Entia, possibilia, privationes, materia, genera, accidentia et singularia secundum diversum modum participationis rationis divinae distinguuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:ME5","text":"Ordo universi fit primum obiectum causationis divinae, cuius partes non per successionem fortuitam sed per rationes proprias intelliguntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus statuit ideas in mente divina esse, plures esse, et ad cognita divina diversimode pertinere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:ALT2","text":"Structural — Ideae operantur ut nexus inter essentiam divinam, actum intellectus, productionem creaturarum et ordinem universi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Obiectiones de superfluitate idearum et de pluralitate in Deo solvuntur mutando sensum ideae ab instrumento cognoscendi ad rationem intellectam unius essentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Essentia divina est una et aeterna, creaturae temporales sunt, sed rationes earum aeternaliter in cognitione divina consistunt sine dependentia causali ab ipsis creaturis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:MRT1","text":"Axiomata de forma intellecta, creatione per intellectum et intentione ordinis sufficiunt ad necessitatem idearum et pluralitatem earum reconstruendam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:MRT2","text":"Distinctio inter essentiam unam et rationes multas est commissura principalis qua pluralitas idearum cum simplicitate divina conciliatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:MRT3","text":"Systema firmissimum est ubi pluralitatem idealem reducit ad actum unius intellectus, non ad multitudinem formarum realium in Deo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:MRT4","text":"Tensionem relinquit transitus inter respectus in Deo intellectos et rationes proprias rerum: distinctio plurium rationum affirmatur, dum omnis pluralitas realis in Deo negatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0042","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:MRT5","text":"Altera tensio in ambitu ideae manet: ratio extenditur ad non-fiendum, sed malum, quamvis cognitum, a ratione propria excluditur; ita universitas cognitionis non coincidit cum universitate idearum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0061","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:MRT6","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat, quia ex negatione idearum separatarum procedit ad ideam ut essentiam relative intellectam, deinde ad multiplicitatem ordinatam et ad limites eius cognoscitivos.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T160029134247Z_a5670f97","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 15","source_sha256":"55e2255ba98dfe17615cec70f5673e8bbc4b95bf931333ee9af2601c36e0cef5","sha256":"595eb0e74ddcd3c10070e2d71600bab6c4d7c198950be0a5dc1aa252f789482e","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T16:00:29.136275+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:AS1","text":"The question investigates whether ideas are in God, how one most simple essence contains multiple accounts of things, and to what extent idea extends to all the objects of divine knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:AS2","text":"The argument reconstructs idea not as a separate thing, but as the form of a thing existing in the divine intellect, which can at once be an exemplar of production and an account of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:AS3","text":"From the creation of the world through intellect and from the intention of the order of the universe, it is inferred that God understands not only a universal form, but also the proper accounts of multiple things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:AS4","text":"The multiplicity of ideas does not introduce composition in God, because it does not multiply the essence or the species through which God understands, but the understood accounts by which one imitable essence is compared to diverse creatures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:AS5","text":"The scope of ideas is divided according to a twofold function: exemplar concerns those things produced by divine action; cognitive account also comprehends those not produced, provided that they are known by a proper account.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A1","text":"An idea is the form of some thing existing apart from the thing itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A2","text":"A form outside the thing is either its exemplar or the principle of its cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A3","text":"An intellectual agent acts toward a form whose likeness preexists intelligibly in it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A4","text":"The world is produced by God not by chance, but through an acting intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A5","text":"The divine essence is the likeness of all things and their operative principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A6","text":"The good of the order of the universe is an end properly intended by the principal agent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A7","text":"The account of the whole is not possessed without the proper accounts of those from which the whole is constituted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A8","text":"Simplicity of intellect does not exclude a multitude of understood objects; it excludes a multitude of forms by which the intellect is actualized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A9","text":"God perfectly knows His essence according to all the modes in which it is knowable and imitable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A10","text":"Every creature has its proper species according to its proper mode of participation in the likeness of the divine essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A11","text":"Ideas are multiplied through understood relations by which God compares His essence to things, not through real relations caused by creatures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A12","text":"Idea as exemplar is the principle of making; idea as account is the principle of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A13","text":"Evil is not known by God through a proper account, but through the account of the good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:A14","text":"Matter, considered in itself, has neither being nor knowability; it is known and has an idea only in relation to the composite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:NX1","text":"A divine idea is not a separate form existing outside God's intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:NX2","text":"An idea is not an extrinsic medium through which God knows things apart from His essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:NX3","text":"A plurality of ideas is not a plurality of essences, arts, wisdoms, or intellectual species in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:NX4","text":"Ideal relations are not real relations in God multiplying His simplicity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:NX5","text":"Evil has no proper idea, neither as exemplar nor as cognitive account.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:NX6","text":"A genus does not have an exemplar distinct from the exemplar of the species when it is produced only in the species.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:NX7","text":"Matter does not have an idea distinct from the idea of the composite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:D1","text":"The divine idea is the divine essence itself, taken not absolutely, but as the likeness and account of a determinate thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:D2","text":"Since the world is made through the divine intellect, the form of the world preexisting in the divine mind is necessarily the exemplar of the world.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:D3","text":"The divine intention of the order of the universe requires the proper accounts of the parts of the universe, not merely the account of the first created thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:D4","text":"Multiple ideas are multiple proper accounts of creatures understood from one divine essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:D5","text":"The eternity of the plurality of ideas does not depend on the eternity of creatures, because ideal relations proceed from the act of the divine intellect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:D6","text":"The same divine account can be the exemplar of a thing to be made and the speculative account of a thing not to be made, but these functions are not coextensive.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:D7","text":"All things that God knows through proper accounts have an idea as account, but not all have an idea as effective exemplar.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:D8","text":"The privative character of evil follows from the order of ideas, because evil is understood through the good and not as a proper positive account.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:SD1","text":"The system simultaneously preserves God's ontological unity and the intelligible multiplicity of creatures, locating distinction not in divine being but in the understood accounts of an imitable essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:SD2","text":"The order of the universe is not an emergent effect from the succession of secondary causes, but the primary object of divine intention, which orders under itself the accounts of singular things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:SD3","text":"Divine knowledge surpasses the order of temporal actuality, because a cognitive account can extend to possible things not future, whereas the exemplar is restricted to that which falls under divine making.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:SD4","text":"Individuation, matter, genera, and accidents do not receive the same ideal account, but their classification depends on the manner in which they are referred to the production of the composite and to divine providence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_7:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_7:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:DNX1","text":"It must be false that divine causality is action without an understood form, because then a world made through intellect would not require an intelligible exemplar.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:DNX2","text":"It must be false that creatures are merely indistinct effects of one cause, because the intention of the order of the universe requires the proper accounts of its constituents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:DNX3","text":"It must be false that every intelligible distinction entails a multiple thing or ontological composition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:DNX4","text":"It must be false that to be known is the same as to have a proper positive account, because evil is known without a proper idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:DNX5","text":"It must be false that everything known is producible in the same manner in which it is known, because speculative account exceeds practical exemplar.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:SNX1","text":"The system excludes the autonomy of forms from the divine intellect and essence: no account of things subsists as a principle posited through itself outside God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:SNX2","text":"The system excludes the radical contingency of the order of the universe: an intelligibly ordered multitude of things presupposes a divine intention antecedent to every succession of creatures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:SNX3","text":"The system excludes the positive intelligibility of evil as a primary term: within cognition, evil remains dependent on the good of which it is called the privation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:SNX4","text":"The system excludes matter as a complete and knowable-through-itself principle, because matter enters the order of idea only under form and in the composite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:FGS1","text":"Non-chance creation → action through intellect → preexisting understood form → divine exemplar → necessity of ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:FGS2","text":"Intention of the order of the universe → account of the whole → proper accounts of the parts → multiple understood accounts → plurality of ideas without plurality of essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:FGS3","text":"Distinction of exemplar/account → separation of making from cognition → extension of accounts to possibilities → exclusion of a proper idea of evil → limited classification of matter, genera, accidents, and singulars.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:AF1","text":"Apparent sufficiency of the divine essence → definition of idea as form outside the thing → intellectual creation of the world → essence as exemplar of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:AF2","text":"Unity of the divine essence → intention of the order of the universe → proper accounts of the constituents → plurality of understood ideas → preservation of divine simplicity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:AF3","text":"Identification of idea with exemplar and account → distinction of practical and speculative knowledge → delimitation of the scope of ideas → denial of a proper idea of evil and of certain separate ideas of universals.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:ME1","text":"Idea is reclassified from a separate being to a mode of intelligible relation by which the divine essence is signified as the account of a determinate creature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:ME2","text":"The opposition between divine unity and the multiplicity of things is dissolved through the distinction between the unity of the understanding thing and the multitude of understood accounts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:ME3","text":"Divine knowledge is ordered according to two non-coincident axes: exemplary making and rational understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:ME4","text":"Beings, possibilities, privations, matter, genera, accidents, and singulars are distinguished according to diverse modes of participation in the divine account.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:ME5","text":"The order of the universe becomes the primary object of divine causation, whose parts are understood not through fortuitous succession but through proper accounts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text maintains that ideas are in the divine mind, that they are multiple, and that they pertain in diverse ways to divine objects of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:ALT2","text":"Structural — Ideas operate as a nexus between the divine essence, the act of intellect, the production of creatures, and the order of the universe.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Objections concerning the superfluity of ideas and plurality in God are resolved by changing the sense of idea from an instrument of knowing to an understood account of one essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The divine essence is one and eternal, creatures are temporal, but their accounts subsist eternally in divine knowledge without causal dependence upon the creatures themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:MRT1","text":"The axioms concerning understood form, creation through intellect, and the intention of order suffice to reconstruct the necessity of ideas and their plurality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:MRT2","text":"The distinction between one essence and many accounts is the principal joint by which the plurality of ideas is reconciled with divine simplicity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:MRT3","text":"The system is strongest where it reduces ideal plurality to the act of one intellect, not to a multitude of real forms in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:MRT4","text":"It leaves a tension in the transition between relations understood in God and the proper accounts of things: the distinction of multiple accounts is affirmed, while every real plurality in God is denied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0038","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0042","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0044","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:MRT5","text":"Another tension remains in the scope of idea: account extends to what is not to be made, but evil, although known, is excluded from a proper account; thus the universality of knowledge does not coincide with the universality of ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0059","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0061","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_7:MRT6","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text, because it proceeds from the denial of separate ideas to idea as essence understood relatively, then to ordered multiplicity and to its cognitive limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_7:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PP_7@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_PP_7:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 16","title":"First Part · Question 15","context":"Chapter 16","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 15"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 15","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 16","order":17,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_PP_8"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PP_8@R001","sha256":"36f0d0e93f8f2848a03f2f0109cd730b2a1ead383ef32db630ce3871b3db84b8","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AS1","text":"Investigatur ubi veritas proprie consistat, quomodo ad ens et bonum referatur, et quo modo unitas, aeternitas atque immutabilitas eius intelligendae sint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AS2","text":"Veritas principaliter est conformitas intellectus ad rem, secundario autem in rebus secundum ordinem earum ad intellectum a quo dependent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AS3","text":"Argumentatio est metaphysica et reconstructiva: aequivocationes loci, prioritatis, unitatis et mutationis per distinctiones rationis, causalitatis et analogiae dissolvit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AS4","text":"Motus procedit a diversitate cognitionis et appetitus ad conformitatem, a conformitate ad iudicium, a iudicio ad transcendentales, deinde ad primam veritatem divinam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AS5","text":"Ex identitate esse et intelligere in Deo deducuntur fundamentum unitatis veritatum, sola aeternitas veritatis divinae et eius immutabilitas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AS6","text":"Opposita apparentia reconciliantur distinguendo veritatem divinam, veritatem intellectuum creatorum, veritatem rerum et veritatem enuntiabilium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A1","text":"Verum nominat id in quod tendit intellectus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A2","text":"Cognitio fit secundum quod cognitum est in cognoscente, appetitus autem secundum quod appetens inclinatur in rem appetitam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","text":"Veritas intellectus consistit in conformitate eius ad rem intellectam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","text":"Veritas principaliter est in intellectu et secundario in rebus secundum ordinem earum ad intellectum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","text":"Res per se ordinatur ad intellectum a quo secundum esse dependet, per accidens autem ad intellectum a quo tantum cognoscibilis est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A6","text":"Intellectus proprie cognoscit et dicit verum quando conformitatem suam ad rem iudicio componendi vel dividendi apprehendit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A7","text":"Unumquodque inquantum habet esse, intantum est cognoscibile.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A8","text":"Non ens cognoscibile fit solum ut ens rationis ab intellectu apprehensum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A9","text":"Verum cum ente secundum substantiam convertitur, sed ratione differt quia respectum ad intellectum addit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A10","text":"In ordine apprehensionis ratio entis est prima, ratio veri secunda, ratio boni tertia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A11","text":"In Deo esse et intelligere sunt idem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","text":"Intellectus divinus est mensura et causa omnis alterius esse atque intellectus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A13","text":"In praedicatione analogica ratio proprie invenitur in primo analogato, alia autem ab eo denominantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A14","text":"In intellectibus creatis veritates multiplicantur secundum pluralitatem intellectuum et cognitorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A15","text":"Enuntiabile vocale dicitur verum quia significat veritatem intellectus, non quia veritatem in se sicut in subiecto possidet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16","text":"Solus intellectus divinus aeternus est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A17","text":"Conformitas intellectus creati ad rem variari potest mutatione intellectus vel mutatione rei.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A18","text":"In intellectu divino neque opinionum alternatio contingit neque aliqua res cognitionem subterfugit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX1","text":"Veritas non est principaliter in rebus potius quam in intellectu.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX2","text":"Veritas absoluta rei non constituitur ex eo quod res ab intellectu humano videatur vel cognoscatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX3","text":"Apparentia cuiuslibet cognoscentis non est mensura veritatis, quia inde contradictoria simul vera sequerentur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX4","text":"Sensus et simplex apprehensio possunt conformitatem habere, sed veritatem ut cognitam proprie non apprehendunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX5","text":"Ens apprehendi potest quin ratio veri explicite apprehendatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX6","text":"Non ens ex se neque cognoscibilitatem neque veritatem habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0122","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX7","text":"Bonum non est simpliciter prius vero, quamvis prius sit in ordine appetibilium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX8","text":"Veritas divina compositionem et divisionem in intellectu divino non requirit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0083","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX9","text":"Veritas divina non est similitudo ad principium extrinsecum a quo Deus dependeat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0085","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX10","text":"Quod enuntiatio de peccato sit vera non infert actum peccati a Deo causari.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0087","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX11","text":"Omnia vera non participant unam veritatem creatam numero eandem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX12","text":"Abstractio universalium ab hic et nunc aeternitatem realem eis non confert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX13","text":"Immutabilitas veritatis divinae ad omnem veritatem creatam transferri non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0136","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D1","text":"Res absolute vera dicitur per conformitatem ad intellectum a quo esse recipit; ideo res naturales mensurantur intellectu divino, artificiales intellectu artificis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D2","text":"Definitiones veritatis ut manifestationis ad intellectum, definitiones autem rectitudinis vel similitudinis principii ad veritatem rei referuntur; adaequatio utrumque ordinem complectitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D3","text":"Relatio rei ad intellectum divinum impedit ne diversae apparentiae humanae contradictoria simul absolute vera efficiant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D4","text":"Sensus et intellectus simplex veri sunt per similitudinem rei, sed solus intellectus iudicans verum ut verum cognoscit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D5","text":"Verum ad non ens extenditur tantum quia intellectus non ens constituit ut ens rationis; fundamentum veri manet ens.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D6","text":"Verum est ratione prius bono quia immediate respicit esse et cognitionem, quae appetitum naturaliter antecedit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D7","text":"Deus non tantum veritatem habet, sed est prima et summa veritas, quia in eo esse, intelligere et mensura omnis conformitatis idem sunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D8","text":"Veritates creatae sunt multae secundum proprios actus intellectuum, sed res omnes una veritate divina denominantur verae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D9","text":"Veritas aeternitatem habet solum in intellectu divino, ubi non distinguitur realiter a Deo.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D10","text":"Rationes universales et veritates de futuris non sunt veritates creatae aeternae; earum aeternitas est tantum in mente vel causa divina.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0118","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D11","text":"Veritas intellectus divini est immutabilis, veritas autem intellectus humani mutabilis per transitum intellectus a vero in falsum vel e converso.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D12","text":"Mutatio rerum non tollit omnem veritatem, sed substituit aliam veritatem determinatam illi quae mutatione cessavit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0140","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D13","text":"Propositio potest retinere veritatem qua est vox secundum ordinationem divinam, dum amittit veritatem significationis propter mutationem rei.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0142","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D14","text":"Deus causat quidquid veritatis est in apprehensione privationis vel peccati, non autem privationem vel culpam ut defectum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0087","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SD1","text":"Systema realismum veritatis servat sine mente humana mensuram ultimam constituere, quia obiectivitas rerum ex dependentia earum ab intellectu divino oritur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SD2","text":"Eadem res veritatem intellectus humani per suum esse causat, quamvis ratio formalis veritatis rei ex ordine ad intellectum divinum proveniat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SD3","text":"Extensio veri et entis est eadem, sed ordo rationum est asymmetricus: ens fundat cognoscibilitatem, verum exprimit relationem cognitionis, bonum exprimit perfectionem appetibilem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SD4","text":"Pluralitas epistemica veritatum creatarum et unitas ontologica primae mensurae non opponuntur, quia diversis gradibus analogicae attributionis pertinent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SD5","text":"Aeternitas et immutabilitas veritatis non proprietates abstracti enuntiabilis sunt, sed consequuntur modum intellectus in quo veritas formaliter existit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SD6","text":"In Deo mensura, intellectus, esse et veritas identificantur; in creaturis autem res, conformitas, iudicium et signum distinctis ordinibus distribuuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:DNX1","text":"Veritas non est tertia res subsistens inter intellectum et cognitum, sed conformitas formaliter in intellectu et denominative in re.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:DNX2","text":"Res creata non est sui ipsius mensura ultima neque ex sola praesentia materiali absolute vera efficitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:DNX3","text":"Intellectus humanus neque veritatem rerum producit neque earum ordinem absolutum determinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:DNX4","text":"Veritas non praedicatur univoce de Deo, intellectibus creatis, rebus et enuntiabilibus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:DNX5","text":"Purum non ens non potest esse fundamentum sui ipsius veritatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:DNX6","text":"Forma semantica propositionis non sufficit ad aeternitatem vel immutabilitatem veritatis creatae constituendam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0122","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0142","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:DNX7","text":"Adaequatio non est relatio causaliter symmetrica: res causat veritatem intellectus humani, intellectus autem divinus causat et mensurat rem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:DNX8","text":"Mutatio veritatis determinatae non importat interitum veritatis communiter sumptae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0140","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SNX1","text":"Nullus ordo veritatum aeternarum extra Deum tamquam regio subsistens admittitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SNX2","text":"Oppositio inter veritatem omnino obiectivam et veritatem omnino subiectivam praecluditur, quia veritas intellectualis et veritas rei relatione asymmetrica cohaerent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SNX3","text":"Nulla mensura veritatis communis supra Deum et creaturas ponitur, quia prima veritas cum esse et intelligere divino identificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SNX4","text":"Nullus intellectus creatus centrum aeternum et invariabile totius veri esse potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SNX5","text":"Privatio, falsitas et culpa non constituuntur principia positiva Deo causaliter aemula.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0087","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:FGS1","text":"Cognitio recipit cognitum in cognoscente → intellectus conformatur rei → conformitas fit terminus cognitionis → veritas principaliter in intellectu constituitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:FGS2","text":"Intellectus originans dat vel mensurat formam rei → res concordat suo principio → veritas ab intellectu ad rem derivatur → res denominative vera dicitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:FGS3","text":"Similitudo cogniti → comparatio implicita → iudicium componens vel dividens → conformitas ut cognita → verum proprie dictum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:FGS4","text":"Esse → cognoscibilitas → relatio ad intellectum → verum; esse perfectum → appetibilitas → bonum; inde ordo rationum est ens → verum → bonum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:FGS5","text":"Identitas divini esse et intelligere + causalitas universalis intellectus divini → identitas mensurae et veritatis → Deus ut prima et summa veritas.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:FGS6","text":"Prima veritas divina → denominatio analogica rerum → multiplicatio veritatum in intellectibus creatis → sola aeternitas et immutabilitas in intellectu divino.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AF1","text":"Scientia divina → quaestio veritatis → distinctio cognitionis ab appetitu → collocatio primaria veri in intellectu.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AF2","text":"Conformitas intellectus et rei → distinctio veritatis possessae a veritate cognita → iudicium componens et dividens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AF3","text":"Cognoscibilitas entis → convertibilitas veri et entis → distinctio rationum → prioritas veri ad bonum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AF4","text":"Identitas esse et intelligere in Deo → prima veritas → fundamentum analogicum omnium veritatum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AF5","text":"Pluralitas veritatum creatarum → reductio aeternitatis ad intellectum divinum → distinctio mutabilitatis creatae ab immutabilitate divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0136","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:ME1","text":"Oppositio inter veritatem in rebus et veritatem in intellectu in ordinem prioritatis convertitur: formaliter in intellectu, denominative in rebus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:ME2","text":"Ager obiectivitatis denuo describitur per tres gradus: intellectus divinus mensurans, res mensurata et mensurans cognitionem humanam, intellectus humanus mensuratus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:ME3","text":"Verum, ens et bonum non amplius ut genera separata ordinantur, sed ut convertibilia secundum supposita et distincta secundum rationem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:ME4","text":"Distinctio inter simplicem apprehensionem et iudicium facit visibilem differentiam inter conformitatem habitam et conformitatem cognitam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:ME5","text":"Quaestiones unitatis, aeternitatis et immutabilitatis transferuntur ab abstracto vero ad intellectum in quo veritas formaliter existit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:ME6","text":"Non ens et privatio ex ordine rerum per se cognoscibilium in ordinem entium rationis transferuntur, quo causalitas veritatis a causalitate defectus separatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:ALT1","text":"Altitude 1 — Surface: Examinantur locus veritatis, actus quo cognoscitur, eius comparatio ad ens et bonum, identitas divina, pluralitas, aeternitas et mutabilitas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:ALT2","text":"Altitude 2 — Structural: Veritas ordinatur per relationes mensurantis et mensurati, per distinctionem formalem inter intellectum et rem, et per analogiam primi ad secundaria.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:ALT3","text":"Altitude 3 — Metastrategic: Obiectiones dissolvuntur non negando data earum, sed distinguendo sensum, ordinem, subiectum et causalitatis directionem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0085","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0142","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:ALT4","text":"Altitude 4 — Ontological/Temporal: Omnis veritas creata dependens, multiplicabilis et mutabilis est, dum prima veritas cum esse et intelligere divino identificata sola est aeterna et immutabilis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad octo articulos restituendos, quia locum, actum, extensionem, principium et statum temporalem veritatis continent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:MRT2","text":"Structura firmissima est nexus inter conformitatem, dependentiam rei ab intellectu originante et intellectum divinum ut mensuram universalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:MRT3","text":"Natura ipsius conformitatis non ulterius resolvitur; textus eam ut relationem fundamentalem assumit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:MRT4","text":"Commissura inter veritatem proprie in compositione et divisione positam et veritatem intellectus divini simplicis servatur per iudicium simplex de complexis, sed modus huius attributionis non plene explicatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0034","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0083","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:MRT5","text":"Prioritas formalis veritatis in intellectu et causalitas rei respectu veritatis humanae cohaerent tantum si causalitas efficientis a loco formali rationis veri stricte distinguitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0023","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:MRT6","text":"Unitas et pluralitas veritatis reconciliantur per analogiam, sed solutio pendet ex mutatione gradus praedicationis inter veritatem propriam et denominativam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:MRT7","text":"Convertibilitas veri cum ente et veritas de non ente cohaerent per notionem entis rationis, quae extensionem entis ultra esse reale requirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:MRT8","text":"Veritas futurorum in causa aeterna fundatur, sed relatio huius fundamenti ad contingentiam futurorum in hac quaestione nondum determinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0120","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:MRT9","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat: a loco formali veritatis per ordinem transcendentalem ad fundamentum divinum unitatis, aeternitatis et immutabilitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:FGS1","CH_GGVC_PP_8:FGS4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:FGS5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T160107196174Z_14634543","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 16","source_sha256":"36f0d0e93f8f2848a03f2f0109cd730b2a1ead383ef32db630ce3871b3db84b8","sha256":"cbbf8a197ef341ba2d317562a4503ad7ef61b85bebfd8fdf314ce979fbce2d80","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T16:01:07.197222+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AS1","text":"It is investigated where truth properly consists, how it is related to being and the good, and how its unity, eternity, and immutability are to be understood.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AS2","text":"Truth is principally the conformity of the intellect to the thing, and secondarily in things according to their order to the intellect on which they depend.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AS3","text":"The argumentation is metaphysical and reconstructive: it dissolves equivocations concerning locus, priority, unity, and change through distinctions of reason, causality, and analogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AS4","text":"The movement proceeds from the diversity of cognition and appetite to conformity, from conformity to judgment, from judgment to the transcendentals, and then to the first divine truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AS5","text":"From the identity of being and understanding in God are deduced the foundation of the unity of truths, the eternity of divine truth alone, and its immutability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AS6","text":"Opposed appearances are reconciled by distinguishing divine truth, the truth of created intellects, the truth of things, and the truth of enunciables.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A1","text":"The true names that toward which the intellect tends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A2","text":"Cognition occurs insofar as the known is in the knower, whereas appetite occurs insofar as the appetitive subject is inclined toward the appetized thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","text":"The truth of the intellect consists in its conformity to the thing understood.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","text":"Truth is principally in the intellect and secondarily in things according to their order to the intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","text":"A thing is ordered per se to the intellect on which it depends according to being, but per accidens to the intellect by which it is merely knowable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A6","text":"The intellect properly knows and speaks the true when it apprehends its conformity to the thing by a judgment of composition or division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A7","text":"Each thing, insofar as it has being, is knowable to that extent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A8","text":"Non-being becomes knowable only as a being of reason apprehended by the intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A9","text":"The true is convertible with being according to substance, but differs in notion because it adds a relation to the intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A10","text":"In the order of apprehension, the notion of being is first, the notion of the true second, and the notion of the good third.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A11","text":"In God, being and understanding are the same.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","text":"The divine intellect is the measure and cause of every other being and intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A13","text":"In analogical predication, the notion is properly found in the primary analogate, while the others are denominated from it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A14","text":"In created intellects, truths are multiplied according to the plurality of intellects and of things known.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A15","text":"A vocal enunciable is called true because it signifies the truth of the intellect, not because it possesses truth in itself as in a subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16","text":"The divine intellect alone is eternal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A17","text":"The conformity of the created intellect to the thing can vary through a change of the intellect or a change of the thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:A18","text":"In the divine intellect neither alternation of opinions occurs nor does any thing escape cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX1","text":"Truth is not principally in things rather than in the intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX2","text":"The absolute truth of a thing is not constituted by the thing's being seen or known by the human intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX3","text":"The appearance to any knower is not the measure of truth, because contradictory propositions would thereby follow to be true simultaneously.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX4","text":"Sense and simple apprehension can have conformity, but do not properly apprehend truth as known.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX5","text":"Being can be apprehended without the notion of the true being explicitly apprehended.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX6","text":"Non-being has from itself neither knowability nor truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0122","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX7","text":"The good is not simply prior to the true, although it is prior in the order of appetibles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX8","text":"Divine truth does not require composition and division in the divine intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0083","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX9","text":"Divine truth is not a likeness to an extrinsic principle on which God depends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0085","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX10","text":"That an enunciation concerning sin is true does not entail that the act of sin is caused by God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0087","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX11","text":"All true things do not participate in one numerically identical created truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX12","text":"The abstraction of universals from the here and now does not confer real eternity upon them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:NX13","text":"The immutability of divine truth cannot be transferred to every created truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0136","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D1","text":"A thing is called absolutely true through conformity to the intellect from which it receives being; therefore natural things are measured by the divine intellect, artificial things by the intellect of the artificer.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D2","text":"Definitions of truth as manifestation to the intellect refer to intellectual truth, while definitions of rectitude or likeness to a principle refer to the truth of the thing; adequation includes both orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D3","text":"The relation of the thing to the divine intellect prevents diverse human appearances from making contradictory propositions simultaneously absolutely true.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D4","text":"Sense and the simple intellect are of the true through likeness to the thing, but the judging intellect alone knows the true as true.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D5","text":"The true extends to non-being only because the intellect constitutes non-being as a being of reason; being remains the foundation of the true.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D6","text":"The true is prior in notion to the good because it immediately regards being and cognition, which naturally precede appetite.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D7","text":"God not only has truth, but is the first and highest truth, because in him being, understanding, and the measure of all conformity are the same.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D8","text":"Created truths are many according to the proper acts of intellects, but all things are denominated true by one divine truth.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D9","text":"Truth has eternity only in the divine intellect, where it is not really distinct from God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D10","text":"Universal notions and truths concerning future things are not eternal created truths; their eternity is only in the divine mind or cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0118","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0120","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D11","text":"The truth of the divine intellect is immutable, whereas the truth of the human intellect is mutable through the passage of the intellect from truth to falsity or conversely.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D12","text":"A change of things does not remove all truth, but substitutes another determinate truth for that which ceased through the change.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0140","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D13","text":"A proposition can retain the truth by which it is a vocal expression according to divine ordination, while losing the truth of signification because of a change in the thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0142","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:D14","text":"God causes whatever truth there is in the apprehension of privation or sin, but not privation or fault as defect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0087","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SD1","text":"The system preserves realism about truth without making the human mind the ultimate measure, because the objectivity of things arises from their dependence on the divine intellect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SD2","text":"The same thing causes the truth of the human intellect through its being, although the formal notion of the truth of the thing derives from its order to the divine intellect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SD3","text":"The extension of the true and of being is the same, but the order of notions is asymmetric: being founds knowability, the true expresses the relation of cognition, and the good expresses appetible perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SD4","text":"The epistemic plurality of created truths and the ontological unity of the first measure are not opposed, because they belong to different levels of analogical attribution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SD5","text":"The eternity and immutability of truth are not properties of an abstract enunciable, but follow the mode of the intellect in which truth formally exists.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SD6","text":"In God, measure, intellect, being, and truth are identified; in creatures, however, thing, conformity, judgment, and sign are distributed among distinct orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:DNX1","text":"Truth is not a third subsisting thing between the intellect and the known, but conformity formally in the intellect and denominatively in the thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:DNX2","text":"A created thing is not its own ultimate measure, nor is it rendered absolutely true by material presence alone.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:DNX3","text":"The human intellect neither produces the truth of things nor determines their absolute order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:DNX4","text":"Truth is not predicated univocally of God, created intellects, things, and enunciables.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:DNX5","text":"Pure non-being cannot be the foundation of its own truth.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:DNX6","text":"The semantic form of a proposition does not suffice to constitute the eternity or immutability of created truth.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0122","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0142","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:DNX7","text":"Adequation is not a causally symmetrical relation: the thing causes the truth of the human intellect, whereas the divine intellect causes and measures the thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:DNX8","text":"The change of determinate truth does not imply the destruction of truth taken generally.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A17","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0140","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SNX1","text":"No order of eternal truths outside God is admitted as a subsisting realm.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SNX2","text":"The opposition between wholly objective truth and wholly subjective truth is precluded, because intellectual truth and the truth of the thing cohere through an asymmetric relation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SNX3","text":"No common measure of truth above God and creatures is posited, because the first truth is identified with divine being and understanding.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SNX4","text":"No created intellect can be the eternal and invariable center of the whole true.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:SNX5","text":"Privation, falsity, and fault do not constitute positive principles causally rival to God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0087","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:FGS1","text":"Cognition receives the known in the knower → the intellect is conformed to the thing → conformity becomes the terminus of cognition → truth is principally constituted in the intellect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:FGS2","text":"The originating intellect gives or measures the form of the thing → the thing accords with its principle → truth is derived from the intellect to the thing → the thing is called true denominatively.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:FGS3","text":"Likeness of the known → implicit comparison → judgment composing or dividing → conformity as known → the true properly so called.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:FGS4","text":"Being → knowability → relation to the intellect → true; perfect being → appetibility → good; hence the order of notions is being → true → good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:FGS5","text":"Identity of divine being and understanding + universal causality of the divine intellect → identity of measure and truth → God as first and highest truth.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:FGS6","text":"First divine truth → analogical denomination of things → multiplication of truths in created intellects → eternity and immutability in the divine intellect alone.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AF1","text":"Divine science → question of truth → distinction of cognition from appetite → primary location of the true in the intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AF2","text":"Conformity of intellect and thing → distinction of possessed truth from known truth → judgment composing and dividing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AF3","text":"Knowability of being → convertibility of the true and being → distinction of notions → priority of the true to the good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AF4","text":"Identity of being and understanding in God → first truth → analogical foundation of all truths.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:AF5","text":"Plurality of created truths → reduction of eternity to the divine intellect → distinction of created mutability from divine immutability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0136","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:ME1","text":"The opposition between truth in things and truth in the intellect is converted into an order of priority: formally in the intellect, denominatively in things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:ME2","text":"The field of objectivity is redescribed through three levels: the measuring divine intellect, the thing measured and measuring human cognition, and the measured human intellect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:ME3","text":"The true, being, and the good are no longer ordered as separate genera, but as convertible according to supposita and distinct according to notion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:ME4","text":"The distinction between simple apprehension and judgment makes visible the difference between possessed conformity and known conformity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:ME5","text":"Questions of unity, eternity, and immutability are transferred from the abstract true to the intellect in which truth formally exists.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A15","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:ME6","text":"Non-being and privation are transferred from the order of things knowable per se to the order of beings of reason, whereby the causality of truth is separated from the causality of defect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:ALT1","text":"Altitude 1 — Surface: The locus of truth, the act by which it is known, its comparison to being and the good, divine identity, plurality, eternity, and mutability are examined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:ALT2","text":"Altitude 2 — Structural: Truth is ordered through the relations of measurer and measured, through the formal distinction between intellect and thing, and through the analogy of the first to the secondary.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:ALT3","text":"Altitude 3 — Metastrategic: Objections are dissolved not by denying their data, but by distinguishing sense, order, subject, and direction of causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0085","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0142","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:ALT4","text":"Altitude 4 — Ontological/Temporal: Every created truth is dependent, multipliable, and mutable, while the first truth, identified with divine being and understanding, alone is eternal and immutable.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0136","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the eight articles, because they contain the locus, act, extension, principle, and temporal state of truth.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:MRT2","text":"The firmest structure is the nexus between conformity, the dependence of the thing on the originating intellect, and the divine intellect as universal measure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:MRT3","text":"The nature of conformity itself is not further resolved; the text assumes it as a fundamental relation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:MRT4","text":"The juncture between truth properly placed in composition and division and the truth of the simple divine intellect is maintained through simple judgment concerning complexes, but the mode of this attribution is not fully explained.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0034","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0083","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:MRT5","text":"The formal priority of truth in the intellect and the causality of the thing with respect to human truth cohere only if efficient causality is strictly distinguished from the formal locus of the notion of the true.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0023","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:MRT6","text":"The unity and plurality of truth are reconciled through analogy, but the solution depends on a shift in the level of predication between proper and denominative truth.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A13","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A14","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:MRT7","text":"The convertibility of the true with being and truth concerning non-being cohere through the notion of being of reason, which requires the extension of being beyond real being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:MRT8","text":"The truth of future things is founded in the eternal cause, but the relation of this foundation to the contingency of future things is not yet determined in this question.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:A12","CH_GGVC_PP_8:A16","CH_GGVC_PP_8:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0120","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_8:MRT9","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement: from the formal locus of truth through the transcendental order to the divine foundation of unity, eternity, and immutability.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_8:FGS1","CH_GGVC_PP_8:FGS4","CH_GGVC_PP_8:FGS5","CH_GGVC_PP_8:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_8:P0135","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PP_8@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_PP_8:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 17","title":"First Part · Question 16","context":"Chapter 17","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 16"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 16","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 17","order":18,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_PP_9"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PP_9@R001","sha256":"ade2014d47bf710b1f7045205a037bc52025b57ba85f6f70772dcf0b4268c2e0","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:AS1","text":"Quaestio determinat ubi falsitas primo et proprie consistat, cum verum et falsum eodem ordine oppositionis collocentur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:AS2","text":"Falsitas a rebus non secundum se incipit, sed ex comparatione rei ad intellectum divinum vel humanum diversimode attribuitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:AS3","text":"Cognitio graduata distinguit obiecta propria, circa quae potentiae cognoscitivae per se non deficiunt, ab his quae solum consequenter vel accidentaliter iudicantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:AS4","text":"Falsitas in sensu est deficientia apprehensionis vel iudicii de re; in intellectu autem est proprie cognoscibilis, maxime in compositione et divisione.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:AS5","text":"Verum et falsum constituuntur contraria, quia utraque ponunt determinatam acceptionem rei, alteram adaequatam, alteram non adaequatam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A1","text":"Veritas et falsitas primo sunt in intellectu; in rebus sunt solum per ordinem ad intellectum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A2","text":"Id quod rei per se convenit simpliciter dicitur; id quod solum per accidens convenit secundum quid attribuitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A3","text":"Res naturales per se dependent ab intellectu divino et solum per accidens comparantur ad intellectum humanum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A4","text":"Omne quod in rebus naturalibus est ex ordinatione intellectus divini procedit; ideo res naturalis simpliciter falsa esse non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A5","text":"Res potest dici falsa secundum quid vel quia falso significatur, vel quia nata est causare falsam opinionem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A6","text":"Similitudo deficiens falsitatem non constituit nisi quando nata est opinionem falsam facere ut in pluribus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A7","text":"Virtus cognoscitiva per similitudinem rei cognoscit et circa obiectum quod eam directe informat per se non deficit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A8","text":"Sensus circa sensibilia propria recte dispositus non fallitur per se, sed circa sensibilia communia et per accidens falli potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A9","text":"Intellectus circa quidditatem simplicem non fallitur; falsitas eius per se oritur in compositione vel divisione.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A10","text":"Falsum est acceptio rei non adaequata, quae apprehendit esse quod non est vel non esse quod est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A11","text":"Contrarium aliquid positive ponit et subiectum determinat; negatio autem neque aliquid ponit neque subiectum determinat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A12","text":"Falsitas fundatur in subiecto vero, sicut privatio in ente et malum in bono, non autem in vero sibi contrario.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:NX1","text":"Falsitas non est proprietas absoluta rei naturalis secundum comparationem ad intellectum divinum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:NX2","text":"Res non fallit per se, quia non ostendit nisi suam speciem; fallacia oritur solum per accidens ex similitudine inadaequata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:NX3","text":"Sensus non est falsus circa proprium sensibile, nec deceptio circa ipsum sentire constituitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:NX4","text":"Intellectus non est falsus in simplici apprehensione quidditatis, sed aut verum apprehendit aut nihil intelligit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:NX5","text":"Verum et falsum non opponuntur ut mera affirmatio et negatio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:NX6","text":"Deo in se considerato falsitas vel contrarietas non opponitur, quia falsitas in intellectu divino esse non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0076","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:D1","text":"Falsitas rerum artificialium simpliciter attribui potest, quia opus artificiale per se ordinatur ad intellectum artificis et deficere potest a forma artis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:D2","text":"Falsitas rei naturalis est relativa ad intellectum humanum, quando res vel significatur aliter quam est vel ex apparentia sua opinionem falsam inducere nata est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:D3","text":"Peccatum inter res voluntarias specialiter habet rationem falsitatis, quia agens voluntarius se subducere potest ab ordine intellectus divini.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:D4","text":"Deceptio sensibilis non refertur ad certitudinem ipsius passionis sensus, sed ad transpositionem ab eo quo sensus afficitur ad rem qualis esse iudicatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:D5","text":"Phantasia falsitatis sedem latiorem habet quam sensus, quia similitudinem rei etiam absentis repraesentat et pro re praesenti sumi potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:D6","text":"Error intellectualis oritur cum intellectus quidditati intellectae attribuit praedicatum non consequens vel oppositum, aut partes definitionis incompossibiles componit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:D7","text":"Principia per se nota ab errore immunia sunt, quia praedicatum eorum in definitione subiecti continetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:D8","text":"Falsum, quamvis vero opponatur, non subsistit sine fundamento veri vel entis in quo defectus adaequationis recipitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:SD1","text":"Ordo falsitatis est hierarchicus: simpliciter spectat ad dependentiam rei a causa intellectuali; secundum quid spectat ad comparationem contingentem rei cum cognitione finita.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:SD2","text":"Error non producitur ex sola praesentia similitudinis, sed ex excessu functionis repraesentativae, quando similitudo transit in iudicium de re cui non adaequatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:SD3","text":"Cognitio ordinatur per zonam immunitatis et zonam defectibilitatis: obiectum proprium potentiam informat; relatio, compositio, accidens et absentia locum errori aperiunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:SD4","text":"Oppositionem veri et falsi textus adaequationis oppositionem facit, non entis et non-entis simplicem divisionem; falsum est actus determinatus cognitionis deficientis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:DNX1","text":"Systema requirit ut ens creatum non sit per se mendacium vel chaos ontologicum, quia tota falsitas simpliciter a divino ordine excluditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:DNX2","text":"Systema requirit ut similitudo non sit identitas rei, alioquin neque apparentia fallax neque phantasia neque falsa significatio intelligi possent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:DNX3","text":"Systema requirit ut apprehensio simplex et iudicium compositivum sint operationes formaliter distinctae, alioquin immunitas quidditatis ab errore consistere non posset.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:DNX4","text":"Systema excludit opinionem qua error aequaliter omnibus cognitionis gradibus diffunditur, quia falsitas distribuitur secundum modum obiecti et operationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:SNX1","text":"Argumentatio circumagit impossibilitatem falsitatis absolutae extra ordinem cognitionis: quod omnino nulli intellectui comparatur nec verum nec falsum formaliter dici potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:SNX2","text":"Argumentatio excludit possibilitatem erroris divini; aliter veritas rerum naturalium, dependentia causalis et imminutio falsitatis simpliciter simul corruerent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0076","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:SNX3","text":"Argumentatio non admittit falsum purum et sine subiecto, quia falsitas semper parasitice in ente, vero, bono vel similitudine fundatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:FGS1","text":"Oppositio veri et falsi → quaestio de sede primaria veritatis → intellectus constituitur locus formalis primus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:FGS2","text":"Ordo rei ad intellectum divinum vel humanum → distinctio per se et per accidens → falsitas simpliciter ex rebus naturalibus removetur, falsitas secundum quid rebus attribuitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:FGS3","text":"Similitudo cognoscitiva → distinctio obiecti proprii, communis et accidentalis → distributio defectus inter sensum, phantasiam et intellectum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:FGS4","text":"Inadaequatio apprehensionis → compositio vel divisio → falsum determinatur ut contrarium veri, non ut mera negatio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:FGS5","text":"Contrarietas veri et falsi → dependentia falsi a subiecto vero → falsitas ponitur defectus realiter fundatus sed formaliter cognoscitivus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:AF1","text":"Falsitas quaesita → intellectus ut sedes prima veri → res sub relatione ad intellectum distinguuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:AF2","text":"Res naturalis ordinata ad Deum → falsitas simpliciter negata → apparentia vel significatio fallax → falsitas secundum quid admissa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:AF3","text":"Obiectum proprium → cognitio per se recta → obiectum consequens vel compositio → possibilitas erroris.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:AF4","text":"Inadaequatio determinata → falsum positivum → contrarietas veri et falsi → fundatio falsi in vero subiecto.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:ME1","text":"Falsitas reclassificatur ab attributo ontologico absoluto rei ad relationem ordinatam inter rem, similitudinem et intellectum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:ME2","text":"Distinctio inter rem falsam, cognitionem falsam et causam falsae opinionis separat defectum rei, defectum repraesentationis et defectum iudicii.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:ME3","text":"Oppositio simplicis apprehensionis et compositionis facit intelligibilem cur cognitio quidditatis sit stabilior quam iudicium praedicativum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:ME4","text":"Oppositio veri et falsi transfertur a divisione esse/non esse ad structuram adaequationis et inadaequationis intellectus ad rem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:ME5","text":"Idola, imagines, somnia et similitudines non ponuntur ut falsa ex se, sed ut loca in quibus repraesentatio sine re correspondenti falsam acceptionem generare potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:ALT1","text":"Surface — Quaestio immediate examinat utrum falsitas sit in rebus, sensu, intellectu, atque qualiter vero opponatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:ALT2","text":"Structural — Falsitas distribuitur per relationem causalem et cognoscitivam inter intellectum divinum, rem naturalem, sensum, phantasiam et intellectum humanum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Argumentatio solvit aporias non removendo apparentias fallaces, sed distinguendo ordinem per se ab ordine per accidens et obiectum proprium a iudicio derivato.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Ens creatum sub ordine intellectus divini verum manet, dum falsitas apparet ut defectus contingentis adaequationis in cognitione finita vel voluntate deviante.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad restituendam conclusionem principalem: falsitas formaliter ad intellectum pertinet, rebus autem solum relative attribuitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:MRT2","text":"Structura maxime cohaeret per distinctionem ordinis divini et humani, per divisionem per se et per accidens, atque per differentiam apprehensionis simplicis et compositionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:MRT3","text":"Commissura fragilis manet inter affirmationem quod res per se non fallunt et attributionem rei quae nata est causare falsam opinionem; solutio pendet ex distinctione inter causam occasionis et causam formalem falsitatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0021","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:MRT4","text":"Tensio permanet inter falsitatem rerum artificialium simpliciter et falsitatem rerum naturalium tantum secundum quid, quia utraque ex defectu respectu intellectus explicatur, sed diversa dependentiae ratio postulatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:MRT5","text":"Systema verum et falsum contraria facit, simul tamen falsum in vero subiecto fundari affirmat; contrarietas igitur est formaliter in adaequatione, non materialiter in subiecto recipiente.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0074","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:MRT6","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat, si falsitas non ut res positiva autonoma, sed ut inadaequatio cognitionis realiter fundata et gradatim distributa intelligatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T160130404460Z_43aa5405","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 17","source_sha256":"ade2014d47bf710b1f7045205a037bc52025b57ba85f6f70772dcf0b4268c2e0","sha256":"77614afc18e80a00effc8e8a0c009f9b41fad2c559bf44689e63dce369ad51ba","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T16:01:30.405674+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:AS1","text":"The question determines where falsity first and properly resides, since truth and falsity are placed in the same order of opposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:AS2","text":"Falsity does not begin from things according to themselves, but is attributed differently from the comparison of a thing to the divine or human intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:AS3","text":"Graded cognition distinguishes proper objects, concerning which cognitive powers do not fail through themselves, from those that are judged only consequently or accidentally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:AS4","text":"Falsity in sense is a deficiency of apprehension or judgment concerning a thing; in the intellect, however, it is properly cognizable, especially in composition and division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:AS5","text":"Truth and falsity are constituted as contraries, because each posits a determinate apprehension of the thing, one adequate, the other inadequate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A1","text":"Truth and falsity are first in the intellect; in things they are only by relation to the intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A2","text":"That which belongs to a thing through itself is said simply; that which belongs only accidentally is attributed relatively.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A3","text":"Natural things depend through themselves on the divine intellect and are compared to the human intellect only accidentally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A4","text":"Everything that is in natural things proceeds from the ordering of the divine intellect; therefore a natural thing cannot be false simply.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A5","text":"A thing can be called false relatively either because it is falsely signified, or because it is apt to cause a false opinion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A6","text":"A deficient likeness does not constitute falsity unless it is apt to produce a false opinion in most cases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A7","text":"A cognitive power knows through the likeness of a thing and does not fail through itself concerning the object that directly informs it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A8","text":"Sense, if rightly disposed, is not deceived through itself concerning proper sensibles, but can be deceived concerning common and accidental sensibles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A9","text":"The intellect is not deceived concerning a simple quiddity; its falsity arises through itself in composition or division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A10","text":"The false is an inadequate apprehension of a thing, which apprehends to be what is not or not to be what is.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A11","text":"A contrary posits something positively and determines a subject; negation, however, neither posits anything nor determines a subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:A12","text":"Falsity is founded in a true subject, as privation in being and evil in good, but not in truth as its contrary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:NX1","text":"Falsity is not an absolute property of a natural thing according to comparison with the divine intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:NX2","text":"A thing does not deceive through itself, because it displays only its own species; deception arises only accidentally from an inadequate likeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:NX3","text":"Sense is not false concerning its proper sensible, nor is deception constituted concerning the act of sensing itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:NX4","text":"The intellect is not false in the simple apprehension of a quiddity, but either apprehends truth or understands nothing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:NX5","text":"Truth and falsity are not opposed as mere affirmation and negation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:NX6","text":"To God considered in himself, falsity or contrariety is not opposed, because falsity cannot be in the divine intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0076","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:D1","text":"Falsity can be attributed simply to artificial things, because an artificial work is ordered through itself to the intellect of the artisan and can fall short of the form of the art.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:D2","text":"The falsity of a natural thing is relative to the human intellect, when the thing is either signified otherwise than it is or is apt, by its appearance, to induce a false opinion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:D3","text":"Among voluntary things, sin has in a special way the character of falsity, because a voluntary agent can withdraw itself from the order of the divine intellect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:D4","text":"Sensory deception does not concern the certainty of the passion of sense itself, but the transference from that by which sense is affected to the thing as it is judged to be.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:D5","text":"Imagination has a broader seat of falsity than sense, because it represents the likeness of a thing even when absent and can be taken for the present thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:D6","text":"Intellectual error arises when the intellect attributes to an understood quiddity a predicate that does not follow from it or is opposed to it, or composes incompatible parts of a definition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A9","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:D7","text":"Self-evident principles are immune from error, because their predicate is contained in the definition of the subject.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:D8","text":"The false, although opposed to the true, does not subsist without a foundation of truth or being in which the defect of adequation is received.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A11","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:SD1","text":"The order of falsity is hierarchical: simply, it concerns the dependence of a thing on an intellectual cause; relatively, it concerns the contingent comparison of a thing with finite cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A2","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A3","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:SD2","text":"Error is not produced by the mere presence of a likeness, but by an excess of representative function, when a likeness passes into judgment about a thing to which it is not adequate.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A5","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A6","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:SD3","text":"Cognition is ordered through a zone of immunity and a zone of defectibility: the proper object informs the power; relation, composition, accident, and absence open a place for error.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A7","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A8","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:SD4","text":"The text makes the opposition of truth and falsity an opposition of adequation, not a simple division of being and non-being; the false is a determinate act of deficient cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_PP_9:A1","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A10","CH_GGVC_PP_9:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:DNX1","text":"The system requires that created being not be through itself a lie or ontological chaos, because all falsity simply is excluded from the divine order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:DNX2","text":"The system requires that likeness not be the identity of the thing; otherwise neither deceptive appearance nor imagination nor false signification could be understood.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:DNX3","text":"The system requires that simple apprehension and compositive judgment be formally distinct operations; otherwise the immunity of quiddity from error could not stand.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the opinion according to which error is diffused equally through all grades of cognition, because falsity is distributed according to the mode of object and operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:SNX1","text":"The argumentation turns around the impossibility of absolute falsity outside the order of cognition: what is compared to no intellect whatsoever can formally be called neither true nor false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:SNX2","text":"The argumentation excludes the possibility of divine error; otherwise the truth of natural things, causal dependence, and the diminution of falsity simply would collapse together.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0076","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:SNX3","text":"The argumentation does not admit a pure falsehood without a subject, because falsity is always parasitically founded in being, truth, good, or likeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:FGS1","text":"Opposition of truth and falsity → question concerning the primary seat of truth → the intellect is established as the first formal locus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:FGS2","text":"Order of a thing to the divine or human intellect → distinction of through itself and accidentally → falsity simply is removed from natural things, falsity relatively is attributed to things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:FGS3","text":"Cognitive likeness → distinction of proper, common, and accidental object → distribution of defect among sense, imagination, and intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:FGS4","text":"Inadequation of apprehension → composition or division → the false is determined as contrary to the true, not as mere negation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:FGS5","text":"Contrariety of truth and falsity → dependence of the false on a true subject → falsity is posited as a really founded but formally cognitive defect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:AF1","text":"Falsity sought → intellect as the first seat of truth → things are distinguished under their relation to intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:AF2","text":"Natural thing ordered to God → falsity simply denied → deceptive appearance or signification → falsity relatively admitted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:AF3","text":"Proper object → cognition correct through itself → consequent object or composition → possibility of error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:AF4","text":"Determinate inadequation → positive falsehood → contrariety of truth and falsity → foundation of the false in a true subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:ME1","text":"Falsity is reclassified from an absolute ontological attribute of a thing to an ordered relation among thing, likeness, and intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:ME2","text":"The distinction between a false thing, false cognition, and a cause of false opinion separates the defect of the thing, the defect of representation, and the defect of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:ME3","text":"The opposition of simple apprehension and composition renders intelligible why cognition of quiddity is more stable than predicative judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:ME4","text":"The opposition of truth and falsity is transferred from the division of being/non-being to the structure of adequation and inadequation of intellect to thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:ME5","text":"Idols, images, dreams, and likenesses are not posited as false in themselves, but as places in which representation without a corresponding thing can generate a false apprehension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:ALT1","text":"Surface — The question immediately examines whether falsity is in things, sense, or intellect, and how it is opposed to truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:ALT2","text":"Structural — Falsity is distributed through the causal and cognitive relation among the divine intellect, natural thing, sense, imagination, and human intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argumentation resolves aporias not by removing deceptive appearances, but by distinguishing the order through itself from the order accidentally, and the proper object from derivative judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Created being under the order of the divine intellect remains true, while falsity appears as a defect of contingent adequation in finite cognition or deviating will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to restore the principal conclusion: falsity formally belongs to intellect, but is attributed to things only relatively.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:MRT2","text":"The structure is most coherent through the distinction between divine and human order, through the division of through itself and accidentally, and through the difference between simple apprehension and composition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:MRT3","text":"A fragile juncture remains between the assertion that things do not deceive through themselves and the attribution of a thing that is apt to cause a false opinion; the solution depends on the distinction between a cause of occasion and a formal cause of falsity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0021","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:MRT4","text":"A tension remains between the falsity simply of artificial things and the falsity only relatively of natural things, because both are explained from defect with respect to intellect, but a different account of dependence is required.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:MRT5","text":"The system makes truth and falsity contraries, yet at the same time affirms that the false is founded in a true subject; contrariety, therefore, is formally in adequation, not materially in the receiving subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0074","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_PP_9:MRT6","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text if falsity is understood not as an autonomous positive thing, but as an inadequation of cognition really founded and gradually distributed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_PP_9:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_PP_9@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_PP_9:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 18","title":"First Part · Question 17","context":"Chapter 18","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 17"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 17","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 18","order":19,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_P_10"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_10@R001","sha256":"2d48acce29f695c0cc091bea1082c942e4a3d5d10d2b62424d57fc9cc106fac9","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:AS1","text":"Quaestio inquirit cui vivere proprie attribuatur, quid vita essentialiter significet, utrum Deo conveniat, et quo sensu omnia facta sint vita in ipso.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:AS2","text":"Argumentatio motum naturalem communem ab eo motu separat quo res seipsam ad operationem agit, ut vita propria a similitudine vitae discernatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:AS3","text":"Nomen vitae ab exteriori manifestatione motus procedit, sed definitio eius ad esse substantiale naturae operativae reducitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:AS4","text":"Gradus vitae mensurantur secundum quantum principium executionis, formae et finis in ipso agente consistit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:AS5","text":"Deus summam vitam habet quia eius intelligere est natura sua et ab alio non determinatur; immobilitas igitur imperfecta vitae privatio non est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:AS6","text":"Omnia facta, quatenus in intellectu divino sunt intellecta, sunt vita divina; quatenus vero in propriis naturis continentur, non propter hoc motus vel viventia fiunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:AS7","text":"Argumentatio diagnostica et reconstructiva est: apparentia motus → definitio substantialis vitae → hierarchia autodeterminationis → identitas divinae vitae et intellectionis → distinctio modorum essendi in Deo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A1","text":"Vivere proprie convenit ei quod seipsum agit ad aliquem motum vel operationem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A2","text":"Motus dicitur vel actus imperfecti existentis in potentia vel actus perfecti operantis, sicut intelligere et sentire.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A3","text":"Motus naturalis qui a motore extrinseco dependet vel ad dispositionem naturalem recuperandam ordinatur non est propria automotio viventis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A4","text":"Nomen vitae ab exteriori proprietate imponitur, sed principaliter essentiam rei significat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A5","text":"Vivere est esse in natura cui secundum se competit se agere ad operationem; vivum igitur substantialiter, non accidentaliter, praedicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A6","text":"Perfectio vitae augetur secundum extensionem autodeterminationis ab executione motus ad formam motus et ad finem motus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A7","text":"Plantae formam et finem a natura determinatos habent et seipsas solum quantum ad executionem motus agunt; animalia formam motus per sensum accipiunt, sed finem sibi non praestituunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A8","text":"Intellectus proportionem finis et eorum quae sunt ad finem cognoscit et unum in alterum ordinat, sed intellectus creatus prima principia et ultimum finem a natura praestitutos habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A9","text":"Actio immanens, quae in agente manet, est perfectio agentis; actio transiens est perfectio rei motae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A10","text":"Summus gradus vitae convenit ei cuius natura est ipsum intelligere et cuius id quod naturaliter habet ab alio non determinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A11","text":"Deus est ipsum suum esse, suum intelligere et suum vivere; ideo vivendi principium receptum non habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A12","text":"In Deo idem sunt intellectus, id quod intelligitur et ipsum intelligere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A13","text":"Omnia quae a Deo fiunt in ipso sunt ut intellecta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A14","text":"Quidquid in Deo ut intellectum est, est ipsum vivere vel vita divina, quia essentia divina est vita.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A15","text":"Creaturae sunt in Deo vel ut virtute divina contentae et conservatae vel ut a Deo cognitae per proprias rationes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A16","text":"Exemplatum exemplari conformatur secundum rationem formae, non secundum modum essendi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A17","text":"Mala, licet a Deo cognoscantur, non sunt vita in Deo, quia per rationes bonorum cognoscuntur nec ut creata, conservata aut propria ratione divina constituta sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:NX1","text":"Vita non est omnis motus naturalis; motus universi, caeli aut elementorum vitam per similitudinem tantum designat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:NX2","text":"Vita non est motus corporis gravis aut levis ad locum proprium, quia talis motus a dispositione extrinseca et motore extrinseco dependet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:NX3","text":"Vita non est mera operatio neque accidens rei viventis, quamvis operationes vitae nomen aliquando recipiant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:NX4","text":"Vita divina non est mutatio imperfecti neque motus localis, sed actus immanens perfecti intelligentis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:NX5","text":"Vita Dei non est vita animata per principium distinctum, quia Deus est ipsum suum vivere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0060","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:NX6","text":"Res in Deo ut intellectae non sunt motus neque vivunt modo creaturarum in propriis naturis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:NX7","text":"Conformitas rei ad exemplar divinum non requirit identitatem materialis, sensibilis aut corruptibilis modi essendi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:NX8","text":"Malum non ingreditur vitam divinam per hoc solum quod divina scientia ipsum comprehendit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D1","text":"Motus qui formam, finem vel virtutem movendi ab extrinseco recipit potest similitudinem vitae exhibere, sed rationem vitae propriae non constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A1","CH_GGVC_P_10:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D2","text":"Animal mortuum dicitur quando motum ex se iam non habet, etiamsi ab alio moveri possit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A1","CH_GGVC_P_10:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D3","text":"Alimentum, sensus, motus localis et intellectio sunt divisiones vitae quia diversas naturas ad tales operationes dispositas manifestant, non quia vita formaliter sit collectio operationum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A4","CH_GGVC_P_10:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D4","text":"Ordo plantarum, animalium et intellectualium non secundum solam mobilitatem, sed secundum maiorem interioritatem principiorum formae et finis constituitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A6","CH_GGVC_P_10:A7","CH_GGVC_P_10:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D5","text":"Intellectus creatus vitam perfectiorem quam sensus habet, sed non vitam absolutam, quia in suis primis principiis et ultimo fine ab alio determinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A8","CH_GGVC_P_10:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D6","text":"Immobilitas Dei vitae non repugnat, quia eius intelligere est actus perfectus immanens, non motus imperfecti.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A2","CH_GGVC_P_10:A9","CH_GGVC_P_10:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D7","text":"Deus non vivit per participationem animae aut principii vitae, sed per identitatem essentiae, esse, intellectionis et vitae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A10","CH_GGVC_P_10:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D8","text":"Omnia facta sunt vita in Deo non quia in se viventia sint, sed quia ut intellecta identitatem cum divina vita habent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A12","CH_GGVC_P_10:A13","CH_GGVC_P_10:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D9","text":"Formula rerum esse in Deo duas ordinationes disiungit: causalem, qua creaturae in propriis naturis conservantur, et intelligibilem, qua rationes earum sunt vita divina.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A14","CH_GGVC_P_10:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D10","text":"Res non vivens extra Deum potest in Deo vita esse, quia forma eius in exemplari divino modum essendi divinum, non modum essendi creatum, habet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A14","CH_GGVC_P_10:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D11","text":"Comparatio veritatis rerum dividitur inter esse simpliciter et esse ut haec natura materialis: res in Deo esse increatum habet, sed in propria natura materialem rationem speciei actu exercet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A13","CH_GGVC_P_10:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D12","text":"Ea quae numquam fiunt possunt dici vita in Deo secundum intellectum divinum, non secundum vitam ut principium operationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A14","CH_GGVC_P_10:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:SD1","text":"Vita reordinatur ut gradus interioritatis causalis: non corpus, non motus, non sensus solus, sed capacitas principia propriae operationis intra se continendi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A1","CH_GGVC_P_10:A5","CH_GGVC_P_10:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:SD2","text":"Transcendentia divina non exceptionem ad regulam vitae constituit, sed consummationem regulae, quia actus purus intelligendi perfectius est quam motus corporalis aut operatio recepta.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A2","CH_GGVC_P_10:A9","CH_GGVC_P_10:A10","CH_GGVC_P_10:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:SD3","text":"Praedicatio rerum esse vitam in Deo non transfert vitam creaturarum in Deum, sed modum essendi rerum ab esse creato ad esse intellectum divinum distinguit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A12","CH_GGVC_P_10:A13","CH_GGVC_P_10:A14","CH_GGVC_P_10:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:SD4","text":"Exemplaritatis relatio est asymmetrica: res exteriores formam exemplaris participant, sed materialitatem, temporalitatem et sensibilem modum exemplaris divini non exigunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A15","CH_GGVC_P_10:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:SD5","text":"Scientia divina non aequat cognitionem et positivam constitutionem rei: malum cognoscitur sine ratione propria et possibile non futurum cognoscitur sine operatione procedente.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A14","CH_GGVC_P_10:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse oportet quod proprietas exterior rei, velut motus apparens, essentiam eius sufficienter definiat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse oportet quod motus localis sit mensura primaria vitae, quia intelligere et sentire perfectiores modi motus sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse oportet quod creatura vivens absolute sibi fundamentum suae intellectionis et finis sit, cum gradus creati vitae semper principia aliqua praestituta retineant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse oportet quod vita sit species vitae corporalis, vegetabilis aut corruptibilis, cum vita summa incorporea et sempiterna sit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse oportet quod omne cognitum a Deo eodem modo positivam rationem divinam, creationem et vitam habeat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:DNX6","text":"Falsum esse oportet quod veritas et actualitas rerum uno tantum sensu comparentur inter intellectum divinum et naturas materiales.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:SNX1","text":"Systema excludit principium vitae vel intellegibilitatis creaturae omnino independens a prima intelligentia divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:SNX2","text":"Systema excludit mensuram univocam quae Deum, rationes divinas et res materiales eodem genere motus, esse aut actualitatis metiatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:SNX3","text":"Systema excludit malum tamquam exemplar positivum vel proprium contentum vitae divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:SNX4","text":"Systema excludit interpretationem indifferentiatae formulae “omnia in Deo sunt vita,” quia creatio, conservatio, cognitio, motus et principium operationis modis diversis praedicantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:FGS1","text":"Motus apparens in animalibus → distinctio motus a se et motus ab alio → criterium vitae propriae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:FGS2","text":"Criterium motus a se → extensio motus ad actus immanentes perfectos → exclusio motus naturalis mere extrinsece determinati.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:FGS3","text":"Proprietas exterior a qua nomen imponitur → regressus ad naturam rei → vita ut esse substantiale naturae operativae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:FGS4","text":"Autodeterminatio executionis → acquisitio formae motus → ordinatio finis et mediorum → gradatio plantarum, animalium et intellectualium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:FGS5","text":"Actus immanens perfectus + intelligere non ab alio determinatum → vita perfectissima et sempiterna → Deus maxime vivens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:FGS6","text":"Identitas intellectus divini, intellecti et intellectionis + omnia facta ut intellecta → omnia facta in Deo ut vita divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:FGS7","text":"Distinctio continentiae causalis et esse intellecti + exclusio mali a propriis rationibus → limitatio formulae de vita rerum in Deo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:AF1","text":"Motus communis naturae → insufficientia motus externi → automotio ad operationem → viventia proprie distincta a non viventibus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:AF2","text":"Automotio observata → esse substantiale naturae operativae → gradus executionis, formae et finis → intellectus creatus → vita divina perfectissima.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:AF3","text":"Deus ut ipsum intelligere → identitas intellectus et intellecti → facta ut intellecta → vita divina, salvo discrimine inter cognitionem, creationem, malum et possibile.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:ME1","text":"Motus a mera mutatione physica ad ordinem causalitatis internae reclassificatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:ME2","text":"Vita ab operationibus visibilibus ad esse substantiale naturae ordinatae ad operationem transfertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:ME3","text":"Hierarchia viventium per interioritatem formae et finis, non per maiorem tantum mobilitatem aut complexitatem corporalem, disponitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:ME4","text":"Immobilitas divina a signo carentiae ad signum actus perfecti immanentis convertitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:ME5","text":"Esse rerum in Deo bifariam cartographatur: ut causata et conservata in propriis naturis, vel ut rationes intellectae quae sunt vita divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:ME6","text":"Cognita distinguuntur inter res exemplatas, possibilia numquam futura et mala per bona cognita; sola cognitio non uniformem statum ontologicum constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus discernit viventia a non viventibus, vitam ab operationibus, Deum viventem a re immota corporee, et res in Deo a rebus in propriis naturis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:ALT2","text":"Structural — Vita per structuram principiorum motus explicatur: aliud est exsequi, aliud formam recipere, aliud finem ordinare, aliud ipsum intelligere esse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Obiectiones solvuntur per distinctiones regulantes inter motum proprium et analogicum, operationem et naturam, esse causatum et esse intellectum, exemplar et exemplatum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Vita divina est actus semper in actu et increatus; res temporales, possibilia numquam futura et mala diversis modis ad divinam cognitionem referuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:MRT1","text":"Criterium automotionis, definitio substantialis vitae, gradatio principiorum motus et identitas divinae vitae cum intellectione sufficiunt ad quattuor quaestiones generandas.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A1","CH_GGVC_P_10:A5","CH_GGVC_P_10:A6","CH_GGVC_P_10:A10","CH_GGVC_P_10:A11","CH_GGVC_P_10:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:MRT2","text":"Systema cohaeret si motus imperfectus et actio immanens perfecta non univoce sumantur, et si esse in Deo causale ab esse in Deo intelligibili distinguatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A2","CH_GGVC_P_10:A9","CH_GGVC_P_10:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:MRT3","text":"Firmissimum nodum constituit transitus ab intellectione divina identica sibi ad conclusionem quod omnia ut intellecta sint vita divina.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A12","CH_GGVC_P_10:A13","CH_GGVC_P_10:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:MRT4","text":"Commissura fragilis manet inter motum ex se manifeste observatum et praedicationem substantialem vitae, quia transitus a signo exteriori ad essentiam theoriam nominationis praesupponit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A1","CH_GGVC_P_10:A4","CH_GGVC_P_10:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0032","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:MRT5","text":"Gradatio vitae nititur suppositione quod maior ordinatio formae et finis intra agens sit maior perfectio vitae; ipsa mensura non ulterius demonstratur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A6","CH_GGVC_P_10:A7","CH_GGVC_P_10:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0052","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:MRT6","text":"Tensio manet inter universalitatem rerum ut intellectarum et exclusionem mali: solutio postulat discrimen stabile inter cognosci, rationem propriam habere, creari et conservari.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A13","CH_GGVC_P_10:A14","CH_GGVC_P_10:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:MRT7","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat, si formula “omnia in Deo sunt vita” semper ad modum essendi intellectum referatur nec ad identitatem creaturarum cum essentia divina simpliciter transferatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A14","CH_GGVC_P_10:A15","CH_GGVC_P_10:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T160201851044Z_5c134ddc","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 18","source_sha256":"2d48acce29f695c0cc091bea1082c942e4a3d5d10d2b62424d57fc9cc106fac9","sha256":"d9cd821b7b392f6879241a7d47d160c33988b5b095f89a9a224795959359ac5e","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T16:02:01.852479+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:AS1","text":"The question inquires to what living is properly attributed, what life essentially signifies, whether it belongs to God, and in what sense all things made are life in Him.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:AS2","text":"The argument distinguishes common natural motion from that motion by which a thing moves itself to operation, so that life proper may be discerned from a likeness of life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:AS3","text":"The name of life proceeds from the outward manifestation of motion, but its definition is reduced to the substantial being of an operative nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:AS4","text":"The degrees of life are measured according to how much the principle of execution, form, and end resides in the agent itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:AS5","text":"God has the highest life because His understanding is His nature and is not determined by another; immobility, therefore, is not an imperfect privation of life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:AS6","text":"All things made, insofar as they are understood in the divine intellect, are divine life; but insofar as they are contained in their own natures, they do not thereby become motions or living things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:AS7","text":"The argument is diagnostic and reconstructive: appearance of motion → substantial definition of life → hierarchy of self-determination → identity of divine life and understanding → distinction of modes of being in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A1","text":"Living properly belongs to that which moves itself to some motion or operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A2","text":"Motion is called either the act of the imperfect existing in potency or the act of the perfect operating, such as understanding and sensing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A3","text":"Natural motion that depends on an extrinsic mover or is ordered to recovering a natural disposition is not the proper self-motion of a living thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A4","text":"The name of life is imposed from an external property, but principally signifies the essence of a thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A5","text":"To live is to be in a nature to which it belongs of itself to move itself to operation; therefore living is predicated substantially, not accidentally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A6","text":"The perfection of life increases according to the extension of self-determination from the execution of motion to the form of motion and to the end of motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A7","text":"Plants have a form and end determined by nature and move themselves only with respect to the execution of motion; animals receive the form of motion through sense, but do not set the end for themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A8","text":"The intellect knows the proportion of the end and of those things that are for the end and orders one to the other, but the created intellect has first principles and the ultimate end pre-established by nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A9","text":"Immanent action, which remains in the agent, is the perfection of the agent; transient action is the perfection of the thing moved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A10","text":"The highest degree of life belongs to that whose nature is understanding itself and whose natural possession is not determined by another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A11","text":"God is His own being, His own understanding, and His own living; therefore He has no received principle of living.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A12","text":"In God, the intellect, that which is understood, and understanding itself are the same.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A13","text":"All things that are made by God are in Him as understood.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A14","text":"Whatever is in God as understood is living itself or divine life, because the divine essence is life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A15","text":"Creatures are in God either as contained and preserved by divine power or as known by God through their proper intelligible accounts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A16","text":"The exemplified is conformed to the exemplar according to the intelligible account of form, not according to the mode of being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:A17","text":"Evils, although known by God, are not life in God, because they are known through the intelligible accounts of goods and are not constituted as created, preserved, or by a proper divine intelligible account.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:NX1","text":"Life is not every natural motion; the motion of the universe, heaven, or elements signifies life only by likeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:NX2","text":"Life is not the motion of a heavy or light body toward its proper place, because such motion depends on an extrinsic disposition and an extrinsic mover.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:NX3","text":"Life is not mere operation, nor is it an accident of the living thing, although operations sometimes receive the name of life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:NX4","text":"Divine life is not the change of an imperfect thing nor local motion, but the immanent act of a perfect knower.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:NX5","text":"The life of God is not life animated by a distinct principle, because God is His own living.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0060","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:NX6","text":"Things in God as understood are not motions and do not live in the manner of creatures in their own natures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:NX7","text":"The conformity of a thing to the divine exemplar does not require identity of material, sensible, or corruptible mode of being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:NX8","text":"Evil does not enter divine life merely because divine knowledge comprehends it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D1","text":"Motion that receives from without its form, end, or power of moving can display a likeness of life, but does not constitute the proper account of life.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A1","CH_GGVC_P_10:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D2","text":"An animal is called dead when it no longer has motion from itself, even if it can be moved by another.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A1","CH_GGVC_P_10:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D3","text":"Nutrition, sense, local motion, and understanding are divisions of life because they manifest diverse natures disposed toward such operations, not because life formally is a collection of operations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A4","CH_GGVC_P_10:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D4","text":"The order of plants, animals, and intellectual beings is constituted not according to mobility alone, but according to the greater interiority of the principles of form and end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A6","CH_GGVC_P_10:A7","CH_GGVC_P_10:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D5","text":"The created intellect has a life more perfect than sense, but not absolute life, because it is determined by another in its first principles and ultimate end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A8","CH_GGVC_P_10:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D6","text":"God's immobility is not incompatible with life, because His understanding is a perfect immanent act, not the motion of the imperfect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A2","CH_GGVC_P_10:A9","CH_GGVC_P_10:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D7","text":"God does not live through participation in a soul or principle of life, but through the identity of essence, being, understanding, and life.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A10","CH_GGVC_P_10:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D8","text":"All things made are life in God not because they are living in themselves, but because, as understood, they have identity with divine life.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A12","CH_GGVC_P_10:A13","CH_GGVC_P_10:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D9","text":"The formula of things being in God separates two orders: the causal, by which creatures are preserved in their own natures, and the intelligible, by which their intelligible accounts are divine life.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A14","CH_GGVC_P_10:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D10","text":"A nonliving thing outside God can be life in God, because its form in the divine exemplar has a divine mode of being, not a created mode of being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A14","CH_GGVC_P_10:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D11","text":"The comparison of the truth of things is divided between being simply and being as this material nature: a thing has uncreated being in God, but in its own nature it actually exercises the material account of the species.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A13","CH_GGVC_P_10:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:D12","text":"Those things that never come to be can be called life in God according to the divine intellect, not according to life as a principle of operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A14","CH_GGVC_P_10:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:SD1","text":"Life is reordered as a degree of causal interiority: not body, not motion, not sense alone, but the capacity to contain within itself the principles of its own operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A1","CH_GGVC_P_10:A5","CH_GGVC_P_10:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:SD2","text":"Divine transcendence constitutes not an exception to the rule of life, but the consummation of the rule, because the pure act of understanding is more perfect than bodily motion or received operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A2","CH_GGVC_P_10:A9","CH_GGVC_P_10:A10","CH_GGVC_P_10:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:SD3","text":"Predicating that things are life in God does not transfer the life of creatures into God, but distinguishes the mode of being of things from created being to divine intelligible being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A12","CH_GGVC_P_10:A13","CH_GGVC_P_10:A14","CH_GGVC_P_10:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:SD4","text":"The relation of exemplarity is asymmetrical: external things participate in the form of the exemplar, but do not require the materiality, temporality, and sensible mode of the divine exemplar.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A15","CH_GGVC_P_10:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:SD5","text":"Divine knowledge does not equate cognition with the positive constitution of a thing: evil is known without a proper intelligible account, and a possible that will not be is known without proceeding operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A14","CH_GGVC_P_10:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:DNX1","text":"It must be false that an external property of a thing, such as apparent motion, sufficiently defines its essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:DNX2","text":"It must be false that local motion is the primary measure of life, because understanding and sensing are more perfect modes of motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:DNX3","text":"It must be false that a living creature is absolutely the foundation of its own understanding and end, since created degrees of life always retain some pre-established principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:DNX4","text":"It must be false that life is a species of bodily, vegetative, or corruptible life, since the highest life is incorporeal and everlasting.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:DNX5","text":"It must be false that everything known by God has in the same way a positive divine intelligible account, creation, and life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:DNX6","text":"It must be false that the truth and actuality of things are compared in only one sense between the divine intellect and material natures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:SNX1","text":"The system excludes a principle of life or intelligibility of the creature wholly independent of the first divine intelligence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:SNX2","text":"The system excludes a univocal measure that measures God, divine intelligible accounts, and material things under the same genus of motion, being, or actuality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:SNX3","text":"The system excludes evil as a positive exemplar or proper content of divine life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:SNX4","text":"The system excludes an interpretation of the undifferentiated formula “all things in God are life,” because creation, preservation, cognition, motion, and the principle of operation are predicated in different modes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:FGS1","text":"Apparent motion in animals → distinction between motion from itself and motion from another → criterion of proper life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:FGS2","text":"Criterion of motion from itself → extension of motion to perfect immanent acts → exclusion of natural motion determined merely extrinsically.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:FGS3","text":"External property from which the name is imposed → return to the nature of the thing → life as the substantial being of an operative nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:FGS4","text":"Self-determination of execution → acquisition of the form of motion → ordering of end and means → gradation of plants, animals, and intellectual beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:FGS5","text":"Perfect immanent act + understanding not determined by another → most perfect and everlasting life → God as most living.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:FGS6","text":"Identity of divine intellect, thing understood, and understanding + all things made as understood → all things made in God as divine life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:FGS7","text":"Distinction of causal containment and intelligible being + exclusion of evil from proper intelligible accounts → limitation of the formula concerning the life of things in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:AF1","text":"Common motion of nature → insufficiency of external motion → self-motion to operation → living things properly distinguished from nonliving things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:AF2","text":"Observed self-motion → substantial being of operative nature → degrees of execution, form, and end → created intellect → most perfect divine life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:AF3","text":"God as understanding itself → identity of intellect and thing understood → things made as understood → divine life, with the distinction maintained among cognition, creation, evil, and possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:ME1","text":"Motion is reclassified from mere physical change to the order of internal causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:ME2","text":"Life is transferred from visible operations to the substantial being of a nature ordered to operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:ME3","text":"The hierarchy of living things is arranged through the interiority of form and end, not through merely greater mobility or bodily complexity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:ME4","text":"Divine immobility is converted from a sign of deficiency into a sign of perfect immanent act.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:ME5","text":"The being of things in God is mapped in two ways: as caused and preserved in their own natures, or as understood intelligible accounts that are divine life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:ME6","text":"The known are distinguished among exemplified things, possibilities never to be future, and evils known through goods; cognition alone does not constitute a uniform ontological status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text distinguishes living things from nonliving things, life from operations, the living God from a thing bodily immobile, and things in God from things in their own natures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:ALT2","text":"Structural — Life is explained through the structure of the principles of motion: it is one thing to execute, another to receive form, another to order the end, another for understanding itself to be.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Objections are resolved through governing distinctions between proper and analogical motion, operation and nature, caused being and intelligible being, exemplar and exemplified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Divine life is act always in act and uncreated; temporal things, possibilities never to be future, and evils are referred to divine knowledge in different ways.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:MRT1","text":"The criterion of self-motion, the substantial definition of life, the gradation of principles of motion, and the identity of divine life with understanding suffice to generate the four questions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A1","CH_GGVC_P_10:A5","CH_GGVC_P_10:A6","CH_GGVC_P_10:A10","CH_GGVC_P_10:A11","CH_GGVC_P_10:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:MRT2","text":"The system coheres if imperfect motion and perfect immanent action are not taken univocally, and if causal being in God is distinguished from intelligible being in God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A2","CH_GGVC_P_10:A9","CH_GGVC_P_10:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:MRT3","text":"The strongest nexus is constituted by the transition from divine understanding identical with itself to the conclusion that all things, as understood, are divine life.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A12","CH_GGVC_P_10:A13","CH_GGVC_P_10:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:MRT4","text":"A fragile juncture remains between motion from itself manifestly observed and the substantial predication of life, because the transition from external sign to essence presupposes a theory of naming.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A1","CH_GGVC_P_10:A4","CH_GGVC_P_10:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0032","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0033","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:MRT5","text":"The gradation of life rests on the supposition that greater ordering of form and end within the agent is greater perfection of life; this measure itself is not further demonstrated.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A6","CH_GGVC_P_10:A7","CH_GGVC_P_10:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0051","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0052","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0053","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:MRT6","text":"A tension remains between the universality of things as understood and the exclusion of evil: the solution requires a stable distinction between being known, having a proper intelligible account, being created, and being preserved.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A13","CH_GGVC_P_10:A14","CH_GGVC_P_10:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0075","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0084","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_10:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text if the formula “all things in God are life” is always referred to the intelligible mode of being and is not transferred to the identity of creatures with the divine essence simply.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_10:A14","CH_GGVC_P_10:A15","CH_GGVC_P_10:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_10:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_10@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_P_10:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 19","title":"First Part · Question 18","context":"Chapter 19","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 18"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 18","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 19","order":20,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_P_11"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_11@R001","sha256":"e005d1e93ca30897d6c4dfd608d22fce197ac9b19e45d3d570369f308c304a84","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AS1","text":"Quaestio determinat quomodo voluntas Deo attribuenda sit salva simplicitate, necessitate, immutabilitate, efficacia, contingentia creaturarum, permissione mali et libertate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AS2","text":"Voluntas divina est ipsa essentia Dei, bonitatem divinam necessario vult, alia libere et immutabiliter vult, atque effectus cum propriis modis causaliter producit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0205","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AS3","text":"Argumentatio dialectico-reconstructiva est: contradictiones ex notionibus creatis voluntatis removet distinctionibus modalibus, causalibus et semanticis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AS4","text":"Fundamentum est consecutio voluntatis ad intellectum coniuncta identitati reali inter esse, intelligere et velle divinum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AS5","text":"Motus procedit ab existentia voluntatis ad obiecta eius, inde ad necessitatem et causalitatem, postea ad efficaciam, immutabilitatem, modos effectuum, malum, libertatem et significationem voluntatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AS6","text":"Machina solvendi innititur distinctionibus inter rem et rationem, necessitatem absolutam et ex suppositione, voluntatem per se et per accidens, voluntatem simpliciter et secundum quid, atque beneplacitum et signum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0132","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0189","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A1","text":"Omnis natura intellectualis habet voluntatem ut inclinationem ad bonum intellectu apprehensum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A2","text":"In Deo esse, intelligere et velle sunt idem secundum rem, quamvis ratione et modo significandi distinguantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A3","text":"Bonitas divina, quae est essentia Dei, est obiectum principale et finis voluntatis divinae atque necessario a Deo amatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A4","text":"Omne agens actu perfectum inclinationem habet ad communicandam aliis similitudinem boni sui.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A5","text":"Deus alia a se vult ut bonitatem suam participantia et ad ipsam tamquam ad finem ordinata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A6","text":"Bonitas divina est perfecta sine creaturis, neque ex eis aliqua perfectio Deo accrescit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A7","text":"Necessitas absoluta ex intrinseca habitudine terminorum differt a necessitate ex suppositione rei iam positae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A8","text":"Substantia et scientia divina sunt omnino immutabiles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0150","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A9","text":"Agens naturale recipit determinationem finis ab intellectu superiori; primum igitur agens per intellectum et voluntatem agit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0088","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A10","text":"Effectus finiti praeexistunt in Deo intelligibiliter et ab infinita perfectione divina procedunt secundum determinationem intellectus et voluntatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A11","text":"Deus uno simplici actu vult finem et ea quae sunt ad finem; intra actum divinum nulla causalitas sui ipsius vel successio actuum est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A12","text":"Deus vult effectus ex causis certis provenire, ut ordo causarum in rebus servetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A13","text":"Voluntas divina est causa universalis sub cuius ordine omnes causae particulares comprehenduntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A14","text":"Causa perfecte efficax producit non solum effectum, sed etiam modum necessarium vel contingentem quo effectus evenit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A15","text":"Voluntas refertur ad res secundum quod in seipsis, particulariter et cum circumstantiis suis sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0132","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A16","text":"Malum, inquantum malum et privatio boni est, nulli appetitui potest esse obiectum per se.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0189","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A17","text":"Liberum arbitrium respicit ea quae non necessario voluntur, non autem necessario importat facultatem peccandi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0205","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0207","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A18","text":"Ea quae apud homines sunt signa volendi possunt Deo metaphorice attribui ut voluntas signi, distincta a voluntate proprie dicta sive beneplaciti.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0218","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX1","text":"Voluntas divina non est appetitus ex carentia tantum, quia voluntas etiam amorem et quietem in bono habito complectitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX2","text":"Deus nullum finem extrinsecum habet quo perficiatur; ipse est finis eorum quae facit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX3","text":"Voluntas Dei ab obiecto exteriori non movetur, quia bonitas quae eam movet est ipsa essentia divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX4","text":"Pluralitas volitorum non multiplicat actum voluntatis divinae, quia omnia per unam bonitatem vult.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX5","text":"Aeternitas volitionis non infert necessitatem absolutam rei volitae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX6","text":"Habitudo non necessaria ad effectum creatum non significat defectum, contingentiam intrinsecam vel determinationem externam voluntatis divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX7","text":"Deus non agit per necessitatem naturae quae unum effectum determinatum exigit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX8","text":"Carentia causae divinae voluntatis neque irrationalitatem divinam neque supervacuitatem causarum secundarum infert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0112","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX9","text":"Defectus causae secundae voluntatem universalem Dei frustrare non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0136","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX10","text":"Mutatio effectuum, praeceptorum vel sententiarum temporalium non est mutatio voluntatis divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0149","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0155","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0157","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX11","text":"Efficacia irresistibilis voluntatis divinae non imponit omnibus effectibus necessitatem absolutam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0176","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX12","text":"Ordo mali ad bonum per accidens non facit malum per se obiectum divinae voluntatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0192","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX13","text":"Deus malum culpae neque vult fieri neque simpliciter vult non fieri; vult permittere illud fieri.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX14","text":"Libertas divina non consistit in possibilitate deflectendi a bonitate divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0207","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0209","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX15","text":"Voluntas signi non est pluralitas realium voluntatum neque signum infallibile beneplaciti divini.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0216","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0223","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D1","text":"In Deo est voluntas, et actus volendi non est accidens essentiae divinae, sed ipsa essentia secundum rem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A1","CH_GGVC_P_11:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D2","text":"Deus se vult ut finem, alia vero ut participantia et manifestantia similitudinem bonitatis suae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A3","CH_GGVC_P_11:A4","CH_GGVC_P_11:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D3","text":"Deus bonitatem suam vult necessario absolute; creaturas non vult necessario absolute, sed, supposita volitione, necessario eas vult propter immutabilitatem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A3","CH_GGVC_P_11:A6","CH_GGVC_P_11:A7","CH_GGVC_P_11:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D4","text":"Voluntas divina est omnino immutabilis, quia neque bonum divinum neque cognitio eius mutari potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A2","CH_GGVC_P_11:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0149","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0150","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D5","text":"Voluntas Dei est causa rerum: scientia formam dirigit, voluntas eius productionem determinat, potentia exsecutionem efficit, quamvis haec in Deo unum sint.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A2","CH_GGVC_P_11:A9","CH_GGVC_P_11:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0098","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D6","text":"Nulla res est causa qua Deus velit, sed Deus vult unum esse propter aliud secundum ordinem inter volita.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D7","text":"Causae secundae verae et investigabiles manent, quia divina voluntas vult effectus per eas ordinate provenire.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A12","CH_GGVC_P_11:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D8","text":"Quidquid Deus simpliciter vult necessario impletur; quod ab uno ordine particulari recedit sub alio ordine causae universalis continetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A13","CH_GGVC_P_11:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0132","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D9","text":"Effectus necessarii et contingentes eadem efficacia divina producuntur secundum diversos modos a Deo intentos et per causas congruas dispositos.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A12","CH_GGVC_P_11:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D10","text":"Voluntas antecedens respicit bonum absolute et est secundum quid; voluntas consequens respicit omnes circumstantias particulares et est voluntas simpliciter.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A13","CH_GGVC_P_11:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0132","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D11","text":"Deus malum culpae nullo modo vult, defectum naturalem et poenam vult solum per accidens volendo ordinem naturae vel iustitiae, atque culpam permittit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A3","CH_GGVC_P_11:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0190","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D12","text":"Deus respectu creaturarum non necessario volitarum liberum arbitrium habet, sine facultate eligendi malum culpae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A3","CH_GGVC_P_11:A6","CH_GGVC_P_11:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0205","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0207","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0209","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D13","text":"Voluntas signi dividitur in operationem directam, permissionem per non impedire, praeceptum et prohibitionem per inductionem necessariam, atque consilium per inductionem persuasoriam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0234","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D14","text":"Praeceptum, prohibitio et consilium rationalem creaturam ad actum proprium ordinant; operatio et permissio latius patent, et sola operatio semper beneplacito congruit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A12","CH_GGVC_P_11:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0241","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D15","text":"Identitas realis scientiae et voluntatis non aequat earum habitudines: scientia res respicit ut in cognoscente, voluntas autem ut in seipsis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A2","CH_GGVC_P_11:A10","CH_GGVC_P_11:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SD1","text":"Simplicitas divina pluralitatem relationum intentionalium et effectuum non tollit, quia multiplicitas terminorum sub uno actu et uno fine continetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D1","CH_GGVC_P_11:D2","CH_GGVC_P_11:D5","CH_GGVC_P_11:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SD2","text":"Libertas et immutabilitas divina componuntur, quia libertas in non necessaria habitudine ad creaturas, immutabilitas autem in invariabilitate actus semel aeternaliter positi consistit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D3","CH_GGVC_P_11:D4","CH_GGVC_P_11:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0205","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SD3","text":"Contingentia et causalitas secunda non sunt limites providentiae, sed modi ab efficacia prima causaliter instituti.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D7","CH_GGVC_P_11:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0172","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SD4","text":"Universalis impletio voluntatis divinae includit operationem boni, permissionem culpae et ordinationem consequentium poenalium sine univoca voluntate omnium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D8","CH_GGVC_P_11:D10","CH_GGVC_P_11:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0190","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0235","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SD5","text":"Rationalitas voluntatis divinae consistit in ordine volitorum inter se, non in causa priori, deliberatione temporali vel discursu interno.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D4","CH_GGVC_P_11:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SD6","text":"Praecepta, prohibitiones, consilia et permissiones non sunt transcriptio directa decreti divini, sed modi analogici quibus relatio Dei ad actiones creatas significatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D13","CH_GGVC_P_11:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0218","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0223","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SD7","text":"Necessitas causae divinae et necessitas consequentiae non transferuntur in necessitatem absolutam cuiuslibet effectus creati.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D3","CH_GGVC_P_11:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0174","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0178","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:DNX1","text":"Systema excludit translationem univocam psychologiae appetitivae creatae ad Deum, quia carentia, passio et motus receptivus voluntati divinae convenire non possunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A2","CH_GGVC_P_11:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_11:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:DNX2","text":"Identitas realis attributorum divinorum non exigit identitatem rationum formalium vel habitudinum ad obiecta.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A2","CH_GGVC_P_11:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:DNX3","text":"Determinatio libera ad creaturas non requirit potentiam passivam, mutationem vel causam extrinsecam in Deo.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D3","CH_GGVC_P_11:D4","CH_GGVC_P_11:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0149","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0205","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:DNX4","text":"Veritas causalitatis secundae non requirit independentiam a causalitate prima.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D5","CH_GGVC_P_11:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:DNX5","text":"Contingentia rei creatae non significat exitum ab ordine universali neque defectum efficaciae divinae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D8","CH_GGVC_P_11:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0172","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:DNX6","text":"Permissio mali non est productio positiva defectus moralis, neque ordinatio per accidens facit malum partem per se bonitatis universi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D11","CH_GGVC_P_11:NX12","CH_GGVC_P_11:NX13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0192","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:DNX7","text":"Signum normativum vel historicum voluntatis divinae non permittit simplicem illationem ad beneplacitum aeternum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D13","CH_GGVC_P_11:D14","CH_GGVC_P_11:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0223","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0239","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SNX1","text":"Nulla indigentia, complementi absentia vel bonum externum potest rationem ultimam creationis constituere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A3","CH_GGVC_P_11:A6","CH_GGVC_P_11:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SNX2","text":"In voluntate divina nullum ante et post, nulla deliberatio successiva et nulla revisio consilii admittitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A8","CH_GGVC_P_11:A11","CH_GGVC_P_11:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0130","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0155","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SNX3","text":"Nullus eventus, defectus vel finis creatus potest extra ordinem causae universalis aut tamquam ratio ultima divinae volitionis subsistere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D6","CH_GGVC_P_11:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0112","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SNX4","text":"Totalitas providentiae non admittit necessitatem modaliter planam quae differentiam inter necessarium et contingens absorbeat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D9","CH_GGVC_P_11:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_11:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0178","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SNX5","text":"Malum nullam rationem positivam habet qua cum bono ut obiectum aequale divinae electionis comparetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A16","CH_GGVC_P_11:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0189","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0190","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SNX6","text":"Manifestatio temporalis voluntatis divinae non est perspicua duplicatio actus aeterni beneplaciti.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D13","CH_GGVC_P_11:D14","CH_GGVC_P_11:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0218","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0223","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0239","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:FGS1","text":"Constitutio voluntatis sequitur: intellectus → bonum apprehensum → inclinatio voluntatis; in Deo autem intelligere, velle et esse uniuntur secundum rem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A1","CH_GGVC_P_11:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:FGS2","text":"Bonitas propria necessario amata → communicabilitas perfectionis → creaturae volitae ut participationes → nulla necessitas absoluta creaturarum, quia nihil perfectionis Deo addunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A3","CH_GGVC_P_11:A4","CH_GGVC_P_11:A5","CH_GGVC_P_11:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:FGS3","text":"Primum agens intellectuale → effectus intelligibiliter praesentes → determinatio voluntatis → exsecutio potentiae → ordo causarum secundarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A9","CH_GGVC_P_11:A10","CH_GGVC_P_11:A12","CH_GGVC_P_11:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:FGS4","text":"Actus aeternus immutabilis → causa universalis efficax → impletio infallibilis → productio effectus cum modo necessario vel contingenti.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A8","CH_GGVC_P_11:A13","CH_GGVC_P_11:A14","CH_GGVC_P_11:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0149","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:FGS5","text":"Bonum ut obiectum → exclusio mali per se → admissio defectus tantum per accidens vel permissionem → libertas circa bona non necessaria sine peccabilitate.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A16","CH_GGVC_P_11:A17","CH_GGVC_P_11:D11","CH_GGVC_P_11:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0189","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0190","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0205","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0207","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:FGS6","text":"Voluntas proprie dicta → analogia signorum humanorum → voluntas signi → operatio aut permissio per seipsum / praeceptum, prohibitio aut consilium per alium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A18","CH_GGVC_P_11:D13","CH_GGVC_P_11:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0218","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0234","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AF1","text":"Intellectus divinus → voluntas divina → bonitas ut obiectum necessarium → creaturae ut volita non necessaria.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D1","CH_GGVC_P_11:D2","CH_GGVC_P_11:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AF2","text":"Prima causalitas → actio per intellectum et voluntatem → voluntas ut causa rerum → negatio causae ipsius voluntatis → affirmatio ordinis inter volita.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D5","CH_GGVC_P_11:D6","CH_GGVC_P_11:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0112","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AF3","text":"Causa universalis → impletio voluntatis → immutabilitas actus → necessitas ex suppositione → diversitas modorum necessariorum et contingentium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D3","CH_GGVC_P_11:D4","CH_GGVC_P_11:D8","CH_GGVC_P_11:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0149","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AF4","text":"Bonum ut obiectum → malum exclusum per se → malum permissum vel poena volita per accidens → liberum arbitrium sine facultate peccandi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D11","CH_GGVC_P_11:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0189","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0190","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0205","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0207","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AF5","text":"Voluntas beneplaciti → praedicatio metaphorica → voluntas signi → divisio operationis, permissionis, praecepti, prohibitionis et consilii.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D13","CH_GGVC_P_11:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0218","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0234","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ME1","text":"Appetitus reordinatur a pura carentia ad structuram duplicem qua bonum absens quaeritur et bonum habitum amatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A1","CH_GGVC_P_11:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ME2","text":"Oppositio inter simplicitatem Dei et pluralitatem volitorum solvitur per distinctionem unius actus a multiplicibus terminis intentionalibus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A2","CH_GGVC_P_11:A11","CH_GGVC_P_11:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ME3","text":"Aeternitas, immutabilitas et necessitas separantur: actus potest esse aeternus et immutabilis sine necessitate absoluta obiecti creati.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D3","CH_GGVC_P_11:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0149","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ME4","text":"Causalitas prima et causae secundae non amplius ut causae concurrentes eiusdem ordinis, sed ut ordo fundans et causae dependentes describuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D5","CH_GGVC_P_11:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ME5","text":"Contingentia ex signo defectus providentiae in modum positive intentum efficaciae divinae convertitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D9","CH_GGVC_P_11:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0170","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0172","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ME6","text":"Malum distinguitur in culpam nullo modo volitam, defectum vel poenam per accidens volitam, atque culpam permissam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0190","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ME7","text":"Vocabulum voluntatis dividitur inter actum proprie dictum et systema signorum, quo praecepta vel permissiones a decretis effectivis discerni possunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D13","CH_GGVC_P_11:D14","CH_GGVC_P_11:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0234","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ALT1","text":"Surface — Examen immediatum determinat existentiam, obiecta, necessitatem, causalitatem, efficaciam, immutabilitatem, modos effectuum, relationem ad malum, libertatem et signa voluntatis divinae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_11:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ALT2","text":"Structural — Unus actus simplex causat ordinem multiplicem effectuum, causarum secundarum et modorum necessariorum vel contingentium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A11","CH_GGVC_P_11:A12","CH_GGVC_P_11:A13","CH_GGVC_P_11:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Distinctiones modales et semanticae dissolvunt falsas oppositiones inter libertatem et immutabilitatem, efficaciam et contingentiam, providentiam et causalitatem secundam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_11:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_11:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_11:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0205","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Realitas divina est actus necessarius, simplex et aeternus, cui effectus temporales et contingentes adveniunt sine mutatione intrinseca causae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A2","CH_GGVC_P_11:A7","CH_GGVC_P_11:A8","CH_GGVC_P_11:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0149","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:MRT1","text":"Duodeviginti axiomata sufficiunt ad restituendas conclusiones duodecim articulorum et nexus inter voluntatem, causalitatem, modalitatem, malum, libertatem et significationem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A1","CH_GGVC_P_11:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:MRT2","text":"Cohaerentia interna maxime pendet a distinctionibus inter identitatem realem et rationes formales, necessitatem absolutam et conditionalem, atque beneplacitum et signum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A2","CH_GGVC_P_11:A7","CH_GGVC_P_11:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:MRT3","text":"Pars firmissima est coniunctio causalitatis universalis cum productione intentionali ipsius modi necessarii vel contingentis effectuum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A13","CH_GGVC_P_11:A14","CH_GGVC_P_11:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:MRT4","text":"Modus quo actus simplex et immutabilis se determinat ad unum ordinem creatum non necessarium affirmatur per autodeterminationem, sed ulterius non explicatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A7","CH_GGVC_P_11:A8","CH_GGVC_P_11:A11","CH_GGVC_P_11:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0063","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0073","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0149","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:MRT5","text":"Transitus a perfectione boni ad communicationem sui innititur analogiae agentium perfectorum, cuius vis generativa assumitur potius quam separatim demonstratur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A4","CH_GGVC_P_11:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:MRT6","text":"Concordia inter voluntatem universalem salutis et eventum contrarium obtinetur voluntate antecedente ad voluntatem secundum quid redacta; pondus solutionis in hac distinctione semantica manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0130","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0131","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0132","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:MRT7","text":"Distinctio inter permissionem culpae et causalitatem universalem impedit attributionem directam mali Deo, sed commissura inter causalitatem totalem et defectum moralem manet in oppositione per se et per accidens.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D8","CH_GGVC_P_11:D11","CH_GGVC_P_11:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0190","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:MRT8","text":"Realitas causarum secundarum servatur earum ordinatione intenta, quamvis autonomia earum numquam a causalitate prima separetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D7","CH_GGVC_P_11:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:MRT9","text":"Restitutio servat motum verum textus ab attributione voluntatis ad constructionem systematis causalis, modalis, moralis et semantici.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_11:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_11:AF1","CH_GGVC_P_11:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T160241777897Z_36a84df1","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 19","source_sha256":"e005d1e93ca30897d6c4dfd608d22fce197ac9b19e45d3d570369f308c304a84","sha256":"1fa0c3a9bd906c38869a09789687a512044ed9ae533f311de4306c97224a1152","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T16:02:41.779966+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AS1","text":"The question determines how will is to be attributed to God while preserving simplicity, necessity, immutability, efficacy, the contingency of creatures, the permission of evil, and freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AS2","text":"The divine will is God's very essence, necessarily wills divine goodness, freely and immutably wills other things, and causally produces effects together with their proper modes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0205","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AS3","text":"The argumentation is dialectical-reconstructive: it removes contradictions arising from created notions of will by modal, causal, and semantic distinctions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AS4","text":"The foundation is the consequence of will upon intellect, joined to the real identity between divine being, understanding, and willing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AS5","text":"The movement proceeds from the existence of will to its objects, thence to necessity and causality, thereafter to efficacy, immutability, the modes of effects, evil, freedom, and the signification of will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AS6","text":"The resolving mechanism rests on distinctions between thing and reason, absolute and conditional necessity, will per se and per accidens, will simply and in a certain respect, and good pleasure and sign.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0132","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0189","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A1","text":"Every intellectual nature has will as an inclination toward the good apprehended by the intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A2","text":"In God, being, understanding, and willing are the same in reality, although they are distinguished in reason and mode of signifying.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A3","text":"Divine goodness, which is God's essence, is the principal object and end of the divine will and is necessarily loved by God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A4","text":"Every agent perfect in act has an inclination to communicate to others the likeness of its own good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A5","text":"God wills things other than Himself as participating in His goodness and ordered to it as to an end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A6","text":"Divine goodness is perfect without creatures, nor does any perfection accrue to God from them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A7","text":"Absolute necessity from the intrinsic relation of terms differs from necessity from the supposition of a thing already posited.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A8","text":"Divine substance and knowledge are altogether immutable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0150","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A9","text":"A natural agent receives the determination of its end from a higher intellect; therefore the first agent acts through intellect and will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0088","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A10","text":"Finite effects pre-exist intelligibly in God and proceed from infinite divine perfection according to the determination of intellect and will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A11","text":"God wills the end and the things ordered to the end by one simple act; within the divine act there is no self-causality or succession of acts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A12","text":"God wills effects to arise from determinate causes, so that the order of causes in things may be preserved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A13","text":"The divine will is the universal cause under whose order all particular causes are comprehended.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A14","text":"A perfectly efficacious cause produces not only the effect, but also the necessary or contingent mode by which the effect occurs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A15","text":"Will is related to things insofar as they are in themselves, particularly, and together with their circumstances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0132","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A16","text":"Evil, insofar as it is evil and a privation of good, cannot be the object per se of any appetite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0189","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A17","text":"Free choice concerns those things that are not willed necessarily, but does not necessarily entail the capacity to sin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0205","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0207","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:A18","text":"Things that among humans are signs of willing can be attributed metaphorically to God as a will of sign, distinct from will properly so called, or will of good pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0218","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX1","text":"The divine will is not an appetite arising from mere lack, because will also includes love and repose in the possessed good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX2","text":"God has no extrinsic end by which He is perfected; He Himself is the end of the things He makes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX3","text":"God's will is not moved by an external object, because the goodness that moves it is the divine essence itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX4","text":"The plurality of things willed does not multiply the act of the divine will, because He wills all things through one goodness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX5","text":"The eternity of volition does not entail the absolute necessity of the thing willed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX6","text":"A non-necessary relation to a created effect does not signify defect, intrinsic contingency, or external determination of the divine will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX7","text":"God does not act by a necessity of nature that demands one determinate effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX8","text":"The lack of a cause of the divine will entails neither divine irrationality nor the superfluity of secondary causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0112","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX9","text":"A defect of a secondary cause cannot frustrate the universal will of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0136","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX10","text":"A change in effects, precepts, or temporal judgments is not a change in the divine will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0149","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0155","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0157","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX11","text":"The irresistible efficacy of the divine will does not impose absolute necessity upon all effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0176","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX12","text":"The ordering of evil to good per accidens does not make evil per se the object of the divine will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0192","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX13","text":"God neither wills evil of fault to occur nor simply wills it not to occur; He wills to permit it to occur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX14","text":"Divine freedom does not consist in the possibility of turning away from divine goodness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0207","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0209","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:NX15","text":"The will of sign is neither a plurality of real wills nor an infallible sign of divine good pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0216","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0223","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D1","text":"There is will in God, and the act of willing is not an accident of the divine essence, but is the essence itself in reality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A1","CH_GGVC_P_11:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D2","text":"God wills Himself as end, but other things as participating in and manifesting the likeness of His goodness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A3","CH_GGVC_P_11:A4","CH_GGVC_P_11:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D3","text":"God wills His goodness with absolute necessity; He does not will creatures with absolute necessity, but, given the volition, He necessarily wills them on account of immutability.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A3","CH_GGVC_P_11:A6","CH_GGVC_P_11:A7","CH_GGVC_P_11:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D4","text":"The divine will is altogether immutable, because neither the divine good nor the knowledge of it can change.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A2","CH_GGVC_P_11:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0149","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0150","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D5","text":"God's will is the cause of things: knowledge directs the form, will determines its production, power effects its execution, although these are one in God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A2","CH_GGVC_P_11:A9","CH_GGVC_P_11:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0098","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D6","text":"No thing is a cause on account of which God wills, but God wills one thing to be for the sake of another according to the order among the things willed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D7","text":"Secondary causes remain real and investigable, because the divine will wills effects to arise through them in an ordered manner.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A12","CH_GGVC_P_11:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D8","text":"Whatever God wills simply is necessarily fulfilled; what departs from one particular order is contained under another order of the universal cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A13","CH_GGVC_P_11:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0132","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D9","text":"Necessary and contingent effects are produced by the same divine efficacy according to diverse modes intended by God and disposed through suitable causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A12","CH_GGVC_P_11:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D10","text":"Antecedent will regards good absolutely and is will in a certain respect; consequent will regards all particular circumstances and is will simply.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A13","CH_GGVC_P_11:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0132","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D11","text":"God in no way wills evil of fault; He wills natural defect and punishment only per accidens, by willing the order of nature or justice, and He permits fault.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A3","CH_GGVC_P_11:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0190","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D12","text":"With respect to creatures not willed necessarily, God has free choice, without the capacity to choose evil of fault.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A3","CH_GGVC_P_11:A6","CH_GGVC_P_11:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0205","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0207","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0209","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D13","text":"The will of sign is divided into direct operation, permission through not impeding, command and prohibition through necessary inducement, and counsel through persuasive inducement.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0234","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D14","text":"Command, prohibition, and counsel order the rational creature toward its proper act; operation and permission extend more broadly, and operation alone always accords with good pleasure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A12","CH_GGVC_P_11:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0241","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:D15","text":"The real identity of knowledge and will does not equate their relations: knowledge regards things as in the knower, but will as in themselves.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A2","CH_GGVC_P_11:A10","CH_GGVC_P_11:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SD1","text":"Divine simplicity does not remove the plurality of intentional relations and effects, because the multiplicity of terms is contained under one act and one end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D1","CH_GGVC_P_11:D2","CH_GGVC_P_11:D5","CH_GGVC_P_11:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SD2","text":"Divine freedom and immutability are reconciled, because freedom consists in a non-necessary relation to creatures, while immutability consists in the invariability of the act once eternally posited.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D3","CH_GGVC_P_11:D4","CH_GGVC_P_11:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0205","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SD3","text":"Contingency and secondary causality are not limits of providence, but modes causally instituted by the first efficacy.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D7","CH_GGVC_P_11:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0172","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SD4","text":"The universal fulfillment of the divine will includes the operation of good, the permission of fault, and the ordering of consequent punishments, without a univocal willing of all.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D8","CH_GGVC_P_11:D10","CH_GGVC_P_11:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0190","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0235","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SD5","text":"The rationality of the divine will consists in the order of things willed among themselves, not in a prior cause, temporal deliberation, or internal discursus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D4","CH_GGVC_P_11:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SD6","text":"Commands, prohibitions, counsels, and permissions are not a direct transcription of the divine decree, but analogical modes by which God's relation to created actions is signified.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D13","CH_GGVC_P_11:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0218","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0223","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SD7","text":"The necessity of the divine cause and the necessity of consequence are not transferred into the absolute necessity of any created effect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D3","CH_GGVC_P_11:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0174","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0178","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:DNX1","text":"The system excludes the univocal transfer of created appetitive psychology to God, because lack, passion, and receptive motion cannot belong to the divine will.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A2","CH_GGVC_P_11:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_11:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:DNX2","text":"The real identity of divine attributes does not require identity of formal reasons or relations to objects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A2","CH_GGVC_P_11:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:DNX3","text":"Free determination toward creatures does not require passive potency, change, or an extrinsic cause in God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D3","CH_GGVC_P_11:D4","CH_GGVC_P_11:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0149","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0205","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:DNX4","text":"The reality of secondary causality does not require independence from first causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D5","CH_GGVC_P_11:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:DNX5","text":"The contingency of a created thing does not signify departure from the universal order or a defect of divine efficacy.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D8","CH_GGVC_P_11:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0172","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:DNX6","text":"The permission of evil is not the positive production of moral defect, nor does ordering per accidens make evil a per se part of the goodness of the universe.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D11","CH_GGVC_P_11:NX12","CH_GGVC_P_11:NX13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0192","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:DNX7","text":"A normative or historical sign of the divine will does not permit a simple inference to eternal good pleasure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D13","CH_GGVC_P_11:D14","CH_GGVC_P_11:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0223","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0239","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SNX1","text":"No need, absence of completion, or external good can constitute the ultimate reason for creation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A3","CH_GGVC_P_11:A6","CH_GGVC_P_11:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SNX2","text":"In the divine will, no before and after, no successive deliberation, and no revision of counsel is admitted.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A8","CH_GGVC_P_11:A11","CH_GGVC_P_11:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0130","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0155","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SNX3","text":"No event, defect, or created end can subsist outside the order of the universal cause or as the ultimate reason of divine volition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D6","CH_GGVC_P_11:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0112","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SNX4","text":"The totality of providence does not admit a modally flat necessity that absorbs the difference between the necessary and the contingent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D9","CH_GGVC_P_11:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_11:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0178","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SNX5","text":"Evil has no positive basis by which it may be compared with good as an equal object of divine choice.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A16","CH_GGVC_P_11:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0189","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0190","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:SNX6","text":"The temporal manifestation of the divine will is not a perspicuous duplication of the eternal act of good pleasure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D13","CH_GGVC_P_11:D14","CH_GGVC_P_11:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0218","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0223","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0239","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:FGS1","text":"The constitution of will follows: intellect → apprehended good → inclination of will; but in God understanding, willing, and being are united in reality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A1","CH_GGVC_P_11:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:FGS2","text":"One's own goodness necessarily loved → communicability of perfection → creatures willed as participations → no absolute necessity of creatures, because they add no perfection to God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A3","CH_GGVC_P_11:A4","CH_GGVC_P_11:A5","CH_GGVC_P_11:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:FGS3","text":"First intellectual agent → effects intelligibly present → determination of will → execution of power → order of secondary causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A9","CH_GGVC_P_11:A10","CH_GGVC_P_11:A12","CH_GGVC_P_11:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:FGS4","text":"Eternal immutable act → efficacious universal cause → infallible fulfillment → production of the effect with necessary or contingent mode.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A8","CH_GGVC_P_11:A13","CH_GGVC_P_11:A14","CH_GGVC_P_11:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0149","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:FGS5","text":"Good as object → exclusion of evil per se → admission of defect only per accidens or by permission → freedom concerning non-necessary goods without sinfulness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A16","CH_GGVC_P_11:A17","CH_GGVC_P_11:D11","CH_GGVC_P_11:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0189","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0190","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0205","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0207","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:FGS6","text":"Will properly so called → analogy of human signs → will of sign → operation or permission through oneself / command, prohibition, or counsel through another.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A18","CH_GGVC_P_11:D13","CH_GGVC_P_11:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0218","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0234","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AF1","text":"Divine intellect → divine will → goodness as necessary object → creatures as non-necessary things willed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D1","CH_GGVC_P_11:D2","CH_GGVC_P_11:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AF2","text":"First causality → action through intellect and will → will as cause of things → denial of a cause of will itself → affirmation of order among things willed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D5","CH_GGVC_P_11:D6","CH_GGVC_P_11:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0112","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AF3","text":"Universal cause → fulfillment of will → immutability of act → necessity from supposition → diversity of necessary and contingent modes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D3","CH_GGVC_P_11:D4","CH_GGVC_P_11:D8","CH_GGVC_P_11:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0149","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AF4","text":"Good as object → evil excluded per se → evil permitted or punishment willed per accidens → free choice without the capacity to sin.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D11","CH_GGVC_P_11:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0189","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0190","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0205","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0207","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:AF5","text":"Will of good pleasure → metaphorical predication → will of sign → division of operation, permission, command, prohibition, and counsel.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D13","CH_GGVC_P_11:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0218","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0234","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ME1","text":"Appetite is reordered from pure lack to a double structure in which absent good is sought and possessed good is loved.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A1","CH_GGVC_P_11:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ME2","text":"The opposition between the simplicity of God and the plurality of things willed is resolved through the distinction of one act from multiple intentional terms.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A2","CH_GGVC_P_11:A11","CH_GGVC_P_11:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ME3","text":"Eternity, immutability, and necessity are separated: an act can be eternal and immutable without the absolute necessity of a created object.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D3","CH_GGVC_P_11:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0149","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ME4","text":"First causality and secondary causes are no longer described as concurrent causes of the same order, but as a founding order and dependent causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D5","CH_GGVC_P_11:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ME5","text":"Contingency is transformed from a sign of deficient providence into a positively intended mode of divine efficacy.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D9","CH_GGVC_P_11:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0170","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0172","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ME6","text":"Evil is distinguished into fault in no way willed, defect or punishment willed per accidens, and fault permitted.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0190","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ME7","text":"The term will is divided between the act properly so called and a system of signs, by which commands or permissions can be distinguished from efficacious decrees.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D13","CH_GGVC_P_11:D14","CH_GGVC_P_11:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0234","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0239","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ALT1","text":"Surface — Immediate examination determines the existence, objects, necessity, causality, efficacy, immutability, modes of effects, relation to evil, freedom, and signs of the divine will.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_11:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ALT2","text":"Structural — One simple act causes a multiple order of effects, secondary causes, and necessary or contingent modes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A11","CH_GGVC_P_11:A12","CH_GGVC_P_11:A13","CH_GGVC_P_11:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Modal and semantic distinctions dissolve false oppositions between freedom and immutability, efficacy and contingency, providence and secondary causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_11:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_11:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_11:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0205","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Divine reality is a necessary, simple, and eternal act, to which temporal and contingent effects accrue without intrinsic change of the cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A2","CH_GGVC_P_11:A7","CH_GGVC_P_11:A8","CH_GGVC_P_11:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0149","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:MRT1","text":"Eighteen axioms suffice to reconstruct the conclusions of twelve articles and the connections among will, causality, modality, evil, freedom, and signification.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A1","CH_GGVC_P_11:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:MRT2","text":"Internal coherence depends especially on distinctions between real identity and formal reasons, absolute and conditional necessity, and good pleasure and sign.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A2","CH_GGVC_P_11:A7","CH_GGVC_P_11:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0219","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:MRT3","text":"The strongest part is the conjunction of universal causality with the intentional production of the very necessary or contingent mode of effects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A13","CH_GGVC_P_11:A14","CH_GGVC_P_11:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:MRT4","text":"The manner in which a simple and immutable act determines itself to one non-necessary created order is affirmed through self-determination, but is not further explained.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A7","CH_GGVC_P_11:A8","CH_GGVC_P_11:A11","CH_GGVC_P_11:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0062","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0063","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0073","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0149","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:MRT5","text":"The transition from the perfection of good to self-communication rests on the analogy of perfect agents, whose generative force is assumed rather than separately demonstrated.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:A4","CH_GGVC_P_11:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:MRT6","text":"The concord between the universal will of salvation and the contrary outcome is obtained by reducing antecedent will to will in a certain respect; the weight of the solution remains in this semantic distinction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0130","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0131","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0132","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:MRT7","text":"The distinction between permission of fault and universal causality prevents direct attribution of evil to God, but the joint between total causality and moral defect remains in the opposition between per se and per accidens.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D8","CH_GGVC_P_11:D11","CH_GGVC_P_11:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0128","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0190","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0196","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:MRT8","text":"The reality of secondary causes is preserved by their intended ordering, although their autonomy is never separated from first causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:D7","CH_GGVC_P_11:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0171","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_11:MRT9","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement of the text from the attribution of will to the construction of a causal, modal, moral, and semantic system.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_11:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_11:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_11:AF1","CH_GGVC_P_11:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_11:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_11@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_P_11:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 20","title":"First Part · Question 19","context":"Chapter 20","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 19"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 19","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 20","order":21,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_P_12"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_12@R001","sha256":"861dd5eade544088b52216333d9e9ce74899c4bed26aeff36eab80a2128cd516","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:AS1","text":"Textus quaerit utrum amor Deo proprie conveniat, cum passio, indigentia, compositio et mutabilitas ab eo excludantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:AS2","text":"Quaestio reformatur per distinctionem inter passionem appetitus sensitivi et actum voluntatis intellectivae: ex voluntate divina amor necessario sequitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:AS3","text":"Amor definitur ut velle bonum; quia voluntas Dei est causa esse et bonitatis, amor divinus non bonitatem inventam sequitur, sed eam in rebus producit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:AS4","text":"Universalitas amoris derivatur ex convertibilitate entis et boni; gradus amoris derivatur ex diversitate bonorum quae Deus diversis vult.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:AS5","text":"Apparentes contradictiones solvuntur per distinctiones secundum passionem et voluntatem, naturam et defectum, actum et obiectum, amorem amicitiae et concupiscentiae, statum praesentem et bonum tempore conferendum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A1","text":"Amor est primus actus voluntatis et cuiuslibet appetitus, quia bonum in communi respicit et reliqui motus appetitivi eum praesupponunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A2","text":"Voluntas in Deo est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A3","text":"Passio nominat actum appetitus sensitivi transmutatione corporali coniunctum, non actum voluntatis intellectivae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A4","text":"Praedicata appetitiva quae formaliter imperfectionem implicant Deo proprie non conveniunt; amor autem et gaudium talem imperfectionem non implicant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A5","text":"Amare aliquem est velle bonum ei.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A6","text":"Vis unitiva et concretiva amoris in Deo compositionem non ponit, quia bonum sibi volitum est ipse, aliis autem bona vult.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A7","text":"Omne ens et omnis eius perfectio, qua ens, bonum sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A8","text":"Voluntas Dei est causa esse et cuiuslibet boni creati.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A9","text":"Actus voluntatis divinae est unus, simplex et invariabilis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A10","text":"Magis amare significat maius bonum velle, non actum voluntatis intensiorem exercere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A11","text":"Amicitia proprie rationalem redamationem et communicationem vitae requirit; creaturae irrationales ordinari possunt amore quasi concupiscentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A12","text":"Peccatum, qua peccatum, est defectus ab esse neque a Deo est; natura peccatoris, qua ens, ab eo est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A13","text":"Comparatio bonitatis amati attenditur secundum tempus quo maius bonum ex divina voluntate ei datur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A14","text":"“Intelligere” et “velle” actum tantum significant, “amare” vero bonum amato volitum in sua ratione includit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:NX1","text":"Amor Dei non est passio, quia non est motus sensitivus transmutatione corporali affectus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:NX2","text":"Amor Dei non importat indigentiam, motum receptivum, vel translationem divinae perfectionis in creaturam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:NX3","text":"Amor ut vis unitiva vel concretiva non significat compositionem in Deo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:NX4","text":"Desiderium, tristitia et ira Deo non dicuntur proprie, quia in sua ratione formali defectum vel imperfectionem includunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:NX5","text":"Dilectio universalis non est amicitia univoca cum omnibus creaturis, neque amor irrationalium supponit eorum redamationem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:NX6","text":"Deus peccatum non amat, nec peccatorem qua peccatorem odit; naturam amat, defectum ab esse odit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:NX7","text":"Unitas et simplicitas actus divini non exigunt uniformitatem bonorum creaturis volitorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0058","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:NX8","text":"Cura divina aequalis non est distributio bonorum aequalium, nec signa externa familiaritatis aut fervoris mensuram simplicem maioris dilectionis constituunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:D1","text":"Amor in Deo proprie affirmatur, sed sine passione corporali et sine imperfectione appetitiva.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A1","CH_GGVC_P_12:A2","CH_GGVC_P_12:A3","CH_GGVC_P_12:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:D2","text":"Amor divinus bonitatem rerum infundit et creat, dum amor humanus bonitate vera vel aestimata provocatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A5","CH_GGVC_P_12:A7","CH_GGVC_P_12:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:D3","text":"Deus omnia existentia amat, quia unicuique enti aliquod bonum vult et ipsum esse eius a voluntate divina procedit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A5","CH_GGVC_P_12:A7","CH_GGVC_P_12:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:D4","text":"Locutio qua Deus per amorem “extra se” fit significat providentem boni collationem, non exitum a se aut mutationem in aliud.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A5","CH_GGVC_P_12:A6","CH_GGVC_P_12:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:D5","text":"Creaturae irrationales diliguntur ut bona ordinata ad creaturas rationales et ad divinam bonitatem, non ut sociae vitae intellectualis et beatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A5","CH_GGVC_P_12:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:D6","text":"Eadem res secundum naturam amari et secundum defectum odiri potest, quia ens et peccatum non eandem rationem habent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A7","CH_GGVC_P_12:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:D7","text":"Deus quaedam magis quam alia amat secundum maiorem bonitatem volitam, quamvis uno simplici actu omnia velit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A8","CH_GGVC_P_12:A9","CH_GGVC_P_12:A10","CH_GGVC_P_12:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:D8","text":"Meliora sunt magis amata, quia eorum melioritas consistit in maiori bono eis a Deo volito.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A8","CH_GGVC_P_12:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:D9","text":"Casus Christi, angelorum, hominum, poenitentium et praedestinatorum regulam maioris dilectionis non infirmant, quia comparationes diversa bona, ordines, tempora vel modos manifestationis respiciunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A10","CH_GGVC_P_12:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:SD1","text":"Ordo causalis amoris invertitur respectu amoris creati: bonitas creaturae non est norma quae amorem divinum movet, sed effectus quo amor divinus creaturam constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A5","CH_GGVC_P_12:A7","CH_GGVC_P_12:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:SD2","text":"Universalitas amoris et inaequalitas creaturarum non sunt propositiones oppositae, quia una causalitas divina omnibus esse confert sed bona secundum gradum distribuit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A7","CH_GGVC_P_12:A8","CH_GGVC_P_12:A9","CH_GGVC_P_12:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:SD3","text":"Malum morale non constituit alterum ens contra creationem, sed defectum intra naturam quae, qua ens, manet obiectum amoris divini.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A7","CH_GGVC_P_12:A8","CH_GGVC_P_12:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:SD4","text":"Hierarchia bonitatis creaturae est simul ontologica, gratialis et temporalis, quia natura, gratia, gloria et tempus collationis boni non coincidunt necessario.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A8","CH_GGVC_P_12:A10","CH_GGVC_P_12:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet bonitatem creaturae amorem Dei antecedere atque eum quasi obiectum causans determinare.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet Deum ab amato perfici, moveri, diminui vel ad creaturam realiter transferri.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet malum vel peccatum positivam naturam habere quae a Deo creetur aut eodem modo ac ens diligatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet praesentem conditionem naturalem, moralem vel affectivam simpliciter ostendere quis a Deo magis diligatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0084","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0086","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse debet hominem ex indiciis particularibus ultimam comparationem spirituum certo diiudicare posse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:SNX1","text":"Possibilitas bonitatis creaturae sibi ipsi fundatae et ab amore divino independentis structuraliter excluditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:SNX2","text":"Possibilitas relationis simmetricae, in qua creatura divinae perfectioni aliquid addat vel voluntatem divinam reconfiguret, structuraliter excluditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:SNX3","text":"Possibilitas mensurae creatae quae electionis divinae gradus exhauste et simpliciter manifestet structuraliter excluditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:FGS1","text":"Voluntas divina → amor ut primus actus ad bonum communiter ordinatus → amor proprie affirmatus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:FGS2","text":"Amor voluntarius − transmutatio corporalis − imperfectio formalis → amor divinus impassibilis et simplex.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:FGS3","text":"Amare = velle bonum; ens = bonum; voluntas Dei = causa esse → omne ens est effectus amoris divini.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:FGS4","text":"Actus divinus unus et invariabilis + bona creaturis diversa volita → dilectio maior secundum obiectum, non secundum intensionem actus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:FGS5","text":"Maius bonum volitum → res melior → res magis amata; distinctio naturae, defectus, ordinis et temporis → dissolutio apparentium exceptionum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:AF1","text":"Impassibilitas divina → distinctio passionis sensibilis ab amore voluntatis → amor in Deo proprie affirmatus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:AF2","text":"Amor = velle bonum → ens = bonum → voluntas divina causat esse → Deus omnia existentia amat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:AF3","text":"Universalitas amoris → distinctio amoris amicitiae et quasi concupiscentiae → creaturae irrationales in ordine finali diliguntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:AF4","text":"Actus divinus simplex → obiecta bona inaequalia → maior dilectio sine maiori intensione divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:AF5","text":"Maius bonum volitum → maior bonitas → maior dilectio; casus contrarii → distinctio secundum bonum, ordinem et tempus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:ME1","text":"Praedicata affectiva reclassificantur secundum materiam corporalem et rationem formalem, ita ut amor et gaudium a passione separari possint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:ME2","text":"Bonitas creaturae reclassificatur ab obiecto praesupposito amoris in effectum amoris divini.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:ME3","text":"Oppositio inter amorem universalem et ordinem inaequalem solvitur, quia amor universalis esse omnibus tribuit et amor maior bona maiora quibusdam confert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:ME4","text":"Oppositio simplicis amoris et odii dissolvitur per distinctionem inter naturam entis et defectum peccati.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:ME5","text":"Comparatio melioris et magis dilecti transfertur a signis exterioribus, natura sola, vel condicione praesenti ad bonum divinitus volitum et tempore conferendum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:ALT1","text":"Surface — Deus amat, omnia amat, quaedam magis amat, et meliora magis amat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:ALT2","text":"Structural — Amor divinus est causalitas voluntatis quae esse, bonitatem, ordinem et gradus perfectionis creaturarum producit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Obiectiones non per negationem amoris solvuntur, sed per translationem quaestionis a motu divino ad rationem amoris et ad effectus boni voliti.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Ens et bonum convertuntur; malum privative intelligitur; gradus bonitatis etiam secundum tempus futurae collationis divini boni aestimatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:MRT1","text":"Nucleus voluntas → amor → bonum → causalitas sufficit ad generandas affirmationes de amore divino, universalitate eius et graduum inaequalitate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:MRT2","text":"Distinctio inter intensionem actus divini et magnitudinem boni voliti est commissura firmissima, quia simplicitatem divinam cum ordine creaturarum inaequali componit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:MRT3","text":"Systema pendet a praemissis antea ostensis, scilicet Deum habere voluntatem et voluntatem eius esse causam rerum; haec quaestio eas assumit potius quam iterum demonstrat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:MRT4","text":"Commissura fragilis manet inter propositionem quod Deus unicuique enti bonum vult et descriptionem amoris irrationalium ut amoris quasi concupiscentiae ordinati ad alia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0043","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:MRT5","text":"Regula quod meliora magis diliguntur vim explicativam habet solum si distinctio inter bonum causaliter volitum et bonum iam apparens servatur; aliter melioritas ex maiori dilectione definita regulam circulariter confirmat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0077","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:MRT6","text":"Solutio de peccato cohaeret per distinctionem naturae et defectus, sed tensionem servat, quia idem suppositum simul amatur ut ens et odio habetur secundum privationem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:MRT7","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat, sed comparationes particulares non omnino mechanice determinat, quia ipse textus de quibusdam gradibus dilectionis iudicium creatum praesumptuosum esse declarat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T160308259965Z_b69da386","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 20","source_sha256":"861dd5eade544088b52216333d9e9ce74899c4bed26aeff36eab80a2128cd516","sha256":"0fcdb35f2b398e3964d3c394cbe39042aec7677eb3f980577ad9e54908134c72","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T16:03:08.262906+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:AS1","text":"The text asks whether love properly belongs to God, since passion, need, composition, and mutability are excluded from him.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:AS2","text":"The question is reformulated through the distinction between a passion of the sensitive appetite and an act of the intellectual will: love necessarily follows from the divine will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:AS3","text":"Love is defined as willing good; because the will of God is the cause of being and goodness, divine love does not follow goodness found, but produces it in things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:AS4","text":"The universality of love is derived from the convertibility of being and good; the degrees of love are derived from the diversity of goods that God wills for different things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:AS5","text":"Apparent contradictions are resolved through distinctions concerning passion and will, nature and defect, act and object, love of friendship and love of concupiscence, present state and good to be conferred in time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A1","text":"Love is the first act of the will and of every appetite, because it regards good in general and the remaining appetitive movements presuppose it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A2","text":"Will is in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A3","text":"Passion denotes an act of the sensitive appetite conjoined with bodily transmutation, not an act of the intellectual will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A4","text":"Appetitive predicates that formally imply imperfection do not properly belong to God; love and joy, however, do not imply such imperfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A5","text":"To love someone is to will good to him.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A6","text":"The unitive and consolidating power of love does not posit composition in God, because the good he wills for himself is himself, whereas he wills goods for others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A7","text":"Every being and every one of its perfections, insofar as it is a being, are good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A8","text":"The will of God is the cause of being and of every created good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A9","text":"The act of the divine will is one, simple, and unchangeable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A10","text":"To love more signifies to will a greater good, not to exercise a more intense act of will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A11","text":"Friendship properly requires rational reciprocal love and a sharing of life; irrational creatures can be ordered by love as though by love of concupiscence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A12","text":"Sin, insofar as it is sin, is a defect from being and is not from God; the nature of the sinner, insofar as it is a being, is from him.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A13","text":"The comparison of the goodness of the beloved is considered according to the time at which a greater good is given to it by the divine will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:A14","text":"“To understand” and “to will” signify only an act, whereas “to love” includes in its meaning the good willed for the beloved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:NX1","text":"The love of God is not a passion, because it is not a sensitive movement affected by bodily transmutation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:NX2","text":"The love of God does not imply need, receptive movement, or a transfer of divine perfection into the creature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:NX3","text":"Love as a unitive or consolidating power does not signify composition in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:NX4","text":"Desire, sadness, and anger are not said of God properly, because in their formal meaning they include defect or imperfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:NX5","text":"Universal love is not univocal friendship with all creatures, nor does love of irrational beings presuppose their reciprocal love.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:NX6","text":"God does not love sin, nor does he hate the sinner insofar as he is a sinner; he loves nature and hates the defect from being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:NX7","text":"The unity and simplicity of the divine act do not require uniformity of the goods willed for creatures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0058","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:NX8","text":"Equal divine care is not a distribution of equal goods, nor do external signs of familiarity or fervor constitute a simple measure of greater love.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:D1","text":"Love is properly affirmed in God, but without bodily passion and without appetitive imperfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A1","CH_GGVC_P_12:A2","CH_GGVC_P_12:A3","CH_GGVC_P_12:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:D2","text":"Divine love infuses and creates the goodness of things, whereas human love is provoked by real or estimated goodness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A5","CH_GGVC_P_12:A7","CH_GGVC_P_12:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:D3","text":"God loves all existing things, because he wills some good to each being and its very being proceeds from the divine will.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A5","CH_GGVC_P_12:A7","CH_GGVC_P_12:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:D4","text":"The expression by which God through love becomes “outside himself” signifies a provident bestowal of good, not an exit from himself or a change into another.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A5","CH_GGVC_P_12:A6","CH_GGVC_P_12:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:D5","text":"Irrational creatures are loved as goods ordered to rational creatures and to divine goodness, not as companions in intellectual and blessed life.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A5","CH_GGVC_P_12:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:D6","text":"The same thing can be loved according to its nature and hated according to its defect, because being and sin do not have the same account.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A7","CH_GGVC_P_12:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:D7","text":"God loves some things more than others according to the greater goodness willed, although he wills all things by one simple act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A8","CH_GGVC_P_12:A9","CH_GGVC_P_12:A10","CH_GGVC_P_12:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:D8","text":"Better things are more loved, because their superiority consists in the greater good willed for them by God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A8","CH_GGVC_P_12:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:D9","text":"The cases of Christ, angels, human beings, penitents, and the predestined do not weaken the rule of greater love, because the comparisons concern diverse goods, orders, times, or modes of manifestation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A10","CH_GGVC_P_12:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:SD1","text":"The causal order of love is inverted with respect to created love: the goodness of the creature is not the norm that moves divine love, but the effect by which divine love constitutes the creature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A5","CH_GGVC_P_12:A7","CH_GGVC_P_12:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:SD2","text":"The universality of love and the inequality of creatures are not opposed propositions, because one divine causality grants being to all but distributes goods according to degree.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A7","CH_GGVC_P_12:A8","CH_GGVC_P_12:A9","CH_GGVC_P_12:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:SD3","text":"Moral evil does not constitute another being contrary to creation, but a defect within nature which, insofar as it is a being, remains an object of divine love.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A7","CH_GGVC_P_12:A8","CH_GGVC_P_12:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:SD4","text":"The hierarchy of creaturely goodness is at once ontological, gracious, and temporal, because nature, grace, glory, and the time of the bestowal of good do not necessarily coincide.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_12:A8","CH_GGVC_P_12:A10","CH_GGVC_P_12:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:DNX1","text":"It must be false that the goodness of the creature precedes the love of God and determines it as though as a causing object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:DNX2","text":"It must be false that God is perfected, moved, diminished, or really transferred to the creature by the beloved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:DNX3","text":"It must be false that evil or sin has a positive nature that is created by God or loved in the same way as being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:DNX4","text":"It must be false that a present natural, moral, or affective condition simply shows who is loved more by God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0084","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0086","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:DNX5","text":"It must be false that a human being can certainly judge from particular indications the ultimate comparison of spirits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:SNX1","text":"The possibility of a creaturely goodness founded in itself and independent of divine love is structurally excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:SNX2","text":"The possibility of a symmetrical relation in which the creature adds something to divine perfection or reconfigures the divine will is structurally excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:SNX3","text":"The possibility of a created measure that exhaustively and simply manifests the degrees of divine election is structurally excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:FGS1","text":"Divine will → love as the first act ordered to good generally → love properly affirmed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:FGS2","text":"Voluntary love − bodily transmutation − formal imperfection → impassible and simple divine love.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:FGS3","text":"To love = to will good; being = good; the will of God = cause of being → every being is an effect of divine love.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:FGS4","text":"One and unchangeable divine act + diverse goods willed for creatures → greater love according to the object, not according to the intensity of the act.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:FGS5","text":"Greater good willed → better thing → more loved thing; distinction of nature, defect, order, and time → dissolution of apparent exceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:AF1","text":"Divine impassibility → distinction of sensible passion from love of the will → love properly affirmed in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:AF2","text":"Love = to will good → being = good → divine will causes being → God loves all existing things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:AF3","text":"Universality of love → distinction of love of friendship and quasi-concupiscence → irrational creatures are loved in the final order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:AF4","text":"Simple divine act → unequal good objects → greater love without greater divine intensity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:AF5","text":"Greater good willed → greater goodness → greater love; contrary cases → distinction according to good, order, and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:ME1","text":"Affective predicates are reclassified according to bodily matter and formal account, so that love and joy can be separated from passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:ME2","text":"The goodness of the creature is reclassified from a presupposed object of love into an effect of divine love.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:ME3","text":"The opposition between universal love and unequal order is resolved, because universal love grants being to all and greater love confers greater goods on some.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:ME4","text":"The opposition of simple love and hatred is dissolved through the distinction between the nature of being and the defect of sin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:ME5","text":"The comparison of what is better and more loved is transferred from external signs, nature alone, or present condition to the good divinely willed and to be conferred in time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:ALT1","text":"Surface — God loves, loves all things, loves some things more, and loves better things more.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:ALT2","text":"Structural — Divine love is the causality of will that produces the being, goodness, order, and degrees of perfection of creatures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Objections are resolved not through the negation of love, but through a translation of the question from divine movement to the account of love and to the effects of the good willed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Being and good are convertible; evil is understood privatively; degrees of goodness are also assessed according to the time of the future bestowal of divine good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:MRT1","text":"The nucleus will → love → good → causality suffices to generate affirmations concerning divine love, its universality, and the inequality of degrees.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:MRT2","text":"The distinction between the intensity of the divine act and the magnitude of the good willed is the firmest joint, because it combines divine simplicity with the unequal order of creatures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:MRT3","text":"The system depends on premises shown previously, namely, that God has will and that his will is the cause of things; this question assumes them rather than demonstrates them again.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:MRT4","text":"A fragile joint remains between the proposition that God wills good to each being and the description of love of irrational beings as love of quasi-concupiscence ordered to other things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0036","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0043","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:MRT5","text":"The rule that better things are loved more has explanatory force only if the distinction between causally willed good and already apparent good is maintained; otherwise, superiority defined from greater love circularly confirms the rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0056","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0077","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:MRT6","text":"The solution concerning sin coheres through the distinction of nature and defect, but preserves a tension, because the same supposit is simultaneously loved as a being and held in hatred according to privation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0045","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_12:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text, but does not determine particular comparisons wholly mechanically, because the text itself declares created judgment concerning certain degrees of love to be presumptuous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_12:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_12@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_P_12:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 21","title":"First Part · Question 20","context":"Chapter 21","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 20"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 20","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 21","order":22,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_P_13"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_13@R001","sha256":"fb9be418e85f7fcc5073f4e7b2b3a21e469ee0eea0213ce62e2ce731c117cd31","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AS1","text":"Quaestio examinat quomodo iustitia, veritas et misericordia Deo attribui possint, salva eius impassibilitate, simplicitate et libertate absoluta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AS2","text":"Praedicata moralia a modo creaturali passionis, commutationis et subiectionis separantur, ut earum rationes causales Deo convenientes retineantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AS3","text":"Argumentatio reconstructiva procedit ab ordine universi et defectuum remotione ad rationem divinae sapientiae, bonitatis et voluntatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AS4","text":"Iustitia divina constituit ordinem rerum rationi sapientiae conformem; misericordia divina defectum removet per communicationem perfectionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AS5","text":"Omne opus divinum simul iustitiam ostendit secundum proportionem ordinatam et misericordiam secundum originem gratuitam omnis boni creati.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AS6","text":"Motus textus est: obiectio ex anthropomorphismo → distinctio rationum iustitiae et misericordiae → subordinatio debiti creati ad sapientiam divinam → radicatio omnis operis in bonitate gratuita.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A1","text":"Deus nulli creaturae debitor est, quia ad alia non ordinatur, sed omnia alia ad ipsum ordinantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A2","text":"Sapientia divina est ratio et lex secundum quam voluntas divina recta et iusta operatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A3","text":"Iustitia distributiva consistit in tribuendo unicuique quod competit secundum naturam, dignitatem, ordinem et finem eius.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A4","text":"Omne debitum creaturae dependet ab ordine priore divinae sapientiae et voluntatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A5","text":"Ordo universi, naturalis et voluntarius, est effectus manifestans iustitiam gubernantis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A6","text":"Veritas causalis consistit in conformitate rei effectae ad intellectum qui eam regulat et mensurat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A7","text":"Misericordia divina non est tristitia de miseria aliena, sed effectus quo defectus per perfectionem bonitatis removetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A8","text":"Eadem collatio perfectionum potest formaliter ad bonitatem, iustitiam, liberalitatem et misericordiam pertinere secundum diversas rationes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A9","text":"Misericordia non agit contra iustitiam, sed aliquid supra debitum iustitiae confert atque ipsam ad plenitudinem ducit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A10","text":"Omne opus divinae iustitiae praesupponit opus misericordiae, quia nullum debitum creaturae subsistit nisi ex dono priore.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A11","text":"Creatio, quamvis nihil in rebus praesupponat, praesupponit rationem rei in cognitione, sapientia et bonitate divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:NX1","text":"Iustitia divina non est iustitia commutativa, quia Deus nihil recipit et nullus prior ei dare potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:NX2","text":"Iustitia divina non est obedientia legi superiori, quia Deus sibi ipsi est lex.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:NX3","text":"Iustitia divina non est virtus politica vel civilis Deo univoce translata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:NX4","text":"Misericordia divina non est passio tristitiae neque affectio ex miseria alterius suscepta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:NX5","text":"Misericordia divina non est imminutio, suspensio vel negatio iustitiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:NX6","text":"Veritas iustitiae divinae non est sola conformitas signi ad significatum neque sola adaequatio intellectus recipientis ad rem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:NX7","text":"Creatio non est opus carens iustitia et misericordia propter absentiam materiae vel debiti praecedentis in rebus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0064","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:D1","text":"Voluntas divina, quia bonum intellectum necessario respicit sub ratione sapientiae, non operatur arbitraria licentia sed ordine rationis suae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A2","CH_GGVC_P_13:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:D2","text":"Quod creaturae debetur non antecedit voluntatem divinam ut ius independens, sed sequitur rationem naturae et finis a Deo constitutam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A1","CH_GGVC_P_13:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:D3","text":"Iustitia divina veritas nominatur, quia res effectae conformantur intellectui divino sicut artificiata arti.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A2","CH_GGVC_P_13:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:D4","text":"Misericordia in Deo maxime attribuitur, quia omnis remotio defectus dependet a prima origine bonitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A7","CH_GGVC_P_13:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:D5","text":"Donum supra proportionem rei non destruit ordinem iustitiae, sed bonitatem divinam manifestat ut fontem ordinis excedentem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A3","CH_GGVC_P_13:A8","CH_GGVC_P_13:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:D6","text":"Poena, remissio, iustificatio et afflictio temporalium bonorum sub eodem schemate iustitiae et misericordiae collocantur, quamvis altera ratio in singulis vehementius appareat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A3","CH_GGVC_P_13:A7","CH_GGVC_P_13:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:D7","text":"Creatio est iusta secundum convenientiam rei productae ad sapientiam divinam et misericors secundum transitum rei de non esse ad esse.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A7","CH_GGVC_P_13:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:SD1","text":"Ordo creatus non fundatur in limitatione externa divinae potentiae, sed in immanenti conformitate voluntatis, sapientiae et bonitatis divinae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A1","CH_GGVC_P_13:A2","CH_GGVC_P_13:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:SD2","text":"Iustitia divina reconstituitur non ut relatio aequalitatis inter Deum et creaturam, sed ut architectura teleologica qua res proprium esse, ordinem et finem recipiunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A1","CH_GGVC_P_13:A3","CH_GGVC_P_13:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:SD3","text":"Misericordia est prior iustitiae secundum radicem causalitatis, non secundum abolitionem ordinis: donum gratuitum instituit ipsum campum in quo debitum postea intelligitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A4","CH_GGVC_P_13:A9","CH_GGVC_P_13:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:SD4","text":"Omnis distinctio operum divinorum in opera iustitiae vel misericordiae est distinctio apparentiae et rationis formalis, non separatio realis principiorum operantium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A5","CH_GGVC_P_13:A8","CH_GGVC_P_13:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet quod creatura possit possidere ius contra Deum quasi principium Deo prius, externum vel coordinatum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet quod libertas divina sit indifferentia sine ratione boni et sapientiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet quod misericordia requirat participationem affectivam in passione alterius.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet quod iustitia et misericordia sint causae rivales quarum una alteram necessario minuat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse debet quod creatio sit pura facticitas sine mensura intelligibili et sine ordine ad bonitatem divinam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:SNX1","text":"Argumentatio excludit possibilitatem ordinis creati omnino sui-fundati, quia omnis ratio debiti resolvitur in bonitatem voluntatis divinae ut ultimum finem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:SNX2","text":"Argumentatio excludit Deum ut subiectum morale inter alia subiecta, cui virtutes univoce, passive vel civiliter attribuantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:SNX3","text":"Argumentatio excludit defectum ut principium positivum autonomum, quia intelligitur solum ut privatio perfectionis per bonitatem divinam removenda.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:FGS1","text":"Translatio creaturalis praedicatorum → exclusio passionis, commutationis et legis superioris → retentio rationis causalis et ordinativae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:FGS2","text":"Sapientia divina ut lex → voluntas conformis sapientiae → ordo rerum secundum proportionem → iustitia distributiva.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:FGS3","text":"Intellectus divinus ut mensura rerum → conformitas effectus ad rationem causalem → veritas iustitiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:FGS4","text":"Defectus creaturae → collatio perfectionis → remotio defectus → misericordia secundum effectum sine passione.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:FGS5","text":"Debitio creaturae → resolutio in conditionem priorem → regressus non infinitus → bonitas divina gratuita ut radix → misericordia fundans iustitiam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:FGS6","text":"Bonitas fontalis + sapientia ordinans + proportio creaturae → omne opus divinum simul misericors et iustum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AF1","text":"Attributio moralium praedicatorum Deo → negatio modorum creaturalium → distinctio iustitiae commutativae et distributivae → iustitia ut ordo sapientiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AF2","text":"Sapientia ut regula rerum → effectus conformis regulae → veritas ut nomen iustitiae divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AF3","text":"Misericordia ut passio reiecta → misericordia ut remotio defectus → collatio perfectionis ut effectus bonitatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AF4","text":"Debitum creaturae → dependentia ab ordine priore → regressus ad bonitatem gratuitam → misericordia ut radix omnis iustitiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AF5","text":"Diversitas apparentiae operum → unitas principiorum divinorum → iustitia et misericordia in quolibet opere divino.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:ME1","text":"Iustitia transfertur a reciproca aequalitate inter agentes ad ordinem teleologicum rerum sub ratione sapientiae divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:ME2","text":"Misericordia transfertur ab affectu compassivo ad efficaciam ontologicam qua defectus per boni communicationem removetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:ME3","text":"Oppositio inter libertatem divinam et iustitiam dissolvitur, quia libertas divina per sapientiam suam regulatur et sibi ipsi lex est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:ME4","text":"Oppositio inter iustitiam et misericordiam dissolvitur per ordinem causalem in quo misericordia constituit radicem gratuitam iustitiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:ME5","text":"Creatio reclassificatur ut actus simul intelligibilis, iustus et misericors, et non ut actus extra omnem rationem debiti positus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus attribuit Deo iustitiam, veritatem et misericordiam atque affirmat utramque rationem in omnibus operibus divinis inveniri.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:ALT2","text":"Structural — Ordo creaturarum, earum proportiones, fines et perfectiones efficiunt structuram in qua debitum et iustitia intelliguntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Obiectiones solvuntur per mutationem generis attributionis: non quaeritur utrum Deus habeat virtutes humanas, sed qua ratione effectus divini earum perfectiones causaliter exhibeant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Ens creatum, debitum eius et omnis perfectio eius ab anterioritate logica bonitatis et cognitionis divinae dependent; defectus intelligitur ut privatio ab hac origine reparabilis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad restitutionem nexus inter sapientiam, voluntatem, ordinem, debitum, iustitiam, veritatem et misericordiam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:MRT2","text":"Centrum firmissimum systematis est dependentia omnis debiti creati ab ordine divinae sapientiae et ab ultima bonitate voluntatis divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:MRT3","text":"Cohaerentia interna servatur quia iustitia et misericordia distinguuntur formaliter, sed in una causalitate divina componuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:MRT4","text":"Commissura fragilis manet in transitu ab eo quod sapientiae divinae convenit ad id quod creaturae debetur, quia ratio convenientiae non independenter determinatur a voluntate divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:MRT5","text":"Tensio manet inter gratuitatem radicalem bonitatis divinae et necessitatem ordinis iusti, quam textus componit per identitatem sapientiae, voluntatis et bonitatis divinae potius quam per regulam externam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0070","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:MRT6","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat, si iustitia non ut limitatio Dei sed ut forma ordinativa effectuum, et misericordia non ut exceptio sed ut radix ontologica ordinis intelligatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T160331057366Z_53eff76e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 21","source_sha256":"fb9be418e85f7fcc5073f4e7b2b3a21e469ee0eea0213ce62e2ce731c117cd31","sha256":"0878aa48809362aadf22dfefb44176d58440eae5f3436634dea334afda53aebf","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T16:03:31.058450+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AS1","text":"The question examines how justice, truth, and mercy can be attributed to God, while preserving His impassibility, simplicity, and absolute freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AS2","text":"Moral predicates are separated from the creaturely mode of passion, exchange, and subjection, so that their causal notions befitting God may be retained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AS3","text":"The reconstructive argument proceeds from the order of the universe and the removal of defects to the notion of divine wisdom, goodness, and will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AS4","text":"Divine justice establishes an order of things conformed to the notion of wisdom; divine mercy removes defect through the communication of perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AS5","text":"Every divine work simultaneously manifests justice according to ordered proportion and mercy according to the gratuitous origin of every created good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AS6","text":"The movement of the text is: objection from anthropomorphism → distinction between the notions of justice and mercy → subordination of created debt to divine wisdom → rooting of every work in gratuitous goodness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A1","text":"God is debtor to no creature, because He is not ordered to other things, but all other things are ordered to Him.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A2","text":"Divine wisdom is the notion and law according to which the divine will operates rightly and justly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A3","text":"Distributive justice consists in rendering to each what befits it according to its nature, dignity, order, and end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A4","text":"Every debt of the creature depends on a prior order of divine wisdom and will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A5","text":"The order of the universe, natural and voluntary, is an effect manifesting the justice of the governor.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A6","text":"Causal truth consists in the conformity of the effected thing to the intellect that rules and measures it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A7","text":"Divine mercy is not sorrow over another's misery, but the effect by which defect is removed through the perfection of goodness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A8","text":"The same bestowal of perfections can formally pertain to goodness, justice, liberality, and mercy according to diverse notions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A9","text":"Mercy does not act against justice, but confers something beyond what justice is owed and brings justice itself to fullness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A10","text":"Every work of divine justice presupposes a work of mercy, because no debt of the creature subsists except from a prior gift.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:A11","text":"Creation, although it presupposes nothing in things, presupposes the notion of the thing in divine knowledge, wisdom, and goodness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:NX1","text":"Divine justice is not commutative justice, because God receives nothing and no one prior can give to Him.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:NX2","text":"Divine justice is not obedience to a higher law, because God is law to Himself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:NX3","text":"Divine justice is not a political or civil virtue transferred univocally to God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:NX4","text":"Divine mercy is not a passion of sorrow nor an affection assumed from another's misery.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:NX5","text":"Divine mercy is not a diminution, suspension, or negation of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:NX6","text":"The truth of divine justice is not merely the conformity of sign to signified, nor merely the adequation of the receiving intellect to the thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:NX7","text":"Creation is not a work lacking justice and mercy on account of the absence of matter or of a preceding debt in things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0064","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:D1","text":"The divine will, because it necessarily regards the understood good under the notion of wisdom, does not operate by arbitrary license but by the order of its own reason.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A2","CH_GGVC_P_13:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:D2","text":"What is owed to the creature does not precede the divine will as an independent right, but follows the notion of nature and end established by God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A1","CH_GGVC_P_13:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:D3","text":"Divine justice is called truth because effected things are conformed to the divine intellect as artifacts to art.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A2","CH_GGVC_P_13:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:D4","text":"Mercy is attributed most of all in God, because every removal of defect depends upon the first origin of goodness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A7","CH_GGVC_P_13:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:D5","text":"A gift beyond the proportion of the thing does not destroy the order of justice, but manifests divine goodness as a source exceeding order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A3","CH_GGVC_P_13:A8","CH_GGVC_P_13:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:D6","text":"Punishment, remission, justification, and the affliction of temporal goods are placed under the same schema of justice and mercy, although one notion appears more forcefully in each.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A3","CH_GGVC_P_13:A7","CH_GGVC_P_13:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:D7","text":"Creation is just according to the fittingness of the produced thing to divine wisdom, and merciful according to the passage of the thing from non-being to being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A7","CH_GGVC_P_13:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:SD1","text":"Created order is not founded on an external limitation of divine power, but on the immanent conformity of divine will, wisdom, and goodness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A1","CH_GGVC_P_13:A2","CH_GGVC_P_13:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:SD2","text":"Divine justice is reconstituted not as a relation of equality between God and creature, but as the teleological architecture by which things receive their proper being, order, and end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A1","CH_GGVC_P_13:A3","CH_GGVC_P_13:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:SD3","text":"Mercy is prior to justice according to the root of causality, not according to the abolition of order: gratuitous gift establishes the very field in which debt is later understood.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A4","CH_GGVC_P_13:A9","CH_GGVC_P_13:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:SD4","text":"Every distinction of divine works into works of justice or mercy is a distinction of appearance and formal notion, not a real separation of operative principles.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_13:A5","CH_GGVC_P_13:A8","CH_GGVC_P_13:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:DNX1","text":"It must be false that the creature can possess a right against God as though a principle prior to, external to, or coordinate with God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:DNX2","text":"It must be false that divine freedom is indifference without a notion of goodness and wisdom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:DNX3","text":"It must be false that mercy requires affective participation in another's suffering.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:DNX4","text":"It must be false that justice and mercy are rival causes, one of which necessarily diminishes the other.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:DNX5","text":"It must be false that creation is pure facticity without an intelligible measure and without order toward divine goodness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:SNX1","text":"The argument excludes the possibility of a created order wholly self-founded, because every notion of debt is resolved into the goodness of the divine will as ultimate end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:SNX2","text":"The argument excludes God as a moral subject among other subjects, to whom virtues are attributed univocally, passively, or civilly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:SNX3","text":"The argument excludes defect as an autonomous positive principle, because it is understood only as a privation of perfection to be removed by divine goodness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:FGS1","text":"Creaturely translation of predicates → exclusion of passion, exchange, and higher law → retention of causal and ordering notion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:FGS2","text":"Divine wisdom as law → will conformed to wisdom → order of things according to proportion → distributive justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:FGS3","text":"Divine intellect as measure of things → conformity of effect to causal notion → truth of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:FGS4","text":"Defect of the creature → bestowal of perfection → removal of defect → mercy according to effect without passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:FGS5","text":"Debt of the creature → resolution into a prior condition → non-infinite regress → gratuitous divine goodness as root → mercy founding justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:FGS6","text":"Fontal goodness + ordering wisdom + proportion of the creature → every divine work simultaneously merciful and just.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AF1","text":"Attribution of moral predicates to God → negation of creaturely modes → distinction of commutative and distributive justice → justice as the order of wisdom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AF2","text":"Wisdom as rule of things → effect conformed to rule → truth as a name of divine justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AF3","text":"Mercy as passion rejected → mercy as removal of defect → bestowal of perfection as effect of goodness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AF4","text":"Debt of the creature → dependence on prior order → regress to gratuitous goodness → mercy as root of all justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:AF5","text":"Diversity in the appearance of works → unity of divine principles → justice and mercy in every divine work.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:ME1","text":"Justice is transferred from reciprocal equality between agents to the teleological order of things under the notion of divine wisdom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:ME2","text":"Mercy is transferred from compassionate affection to the ontological efficacy by which defect is removed through the communication of good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:ME3","text":"The opposition between divine freedom and justice is dissolved, because divine freedom is ruled by its own wisdom and is law to itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:ME4","text":"The opposition between justice and mercy is dissolved through the causal order in which mercy constitutes the gratuitous root of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:ME5","text":"Creation is reclassified as an act simultaneously intelligible, just, and merciful, and not as an act posited outside every notion of debt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text attributes justice, truth, and mercy to God and affirms that both notions are found in all divine works.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:ALT2","text":"Structural — The order of creatures, their proportions, ends, and perfections produce the structure in which debt and justice are understood.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Objections are resolved through a change in the kind of attribution: the question is not whether God has human virtues, but in what manner divine effects causally exhibit their perfections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Created being, its debt, and every one of its perfections depend on the logical priority of divine goodness and knowledge; defect is understood as a privation reparable from this origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice for reconstructing the nexus between wisdom, will, order, debt, justice, truth, and mercy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:MRT2","text":"The firmest center of the system is the dependence of every created debt on the order of divine wisdom and on the ultimate goodness of the divine will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:MRT3","text":"Internal coherence is preserved because justice and mercy are formally distinguished, but composed in one divine causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:MRT4","text":"A fragile juncture remains in the transition from what befits divine wisdom to what is owed to the creature, because the notion of fittingness is not determined independently of the divine will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0024","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:MRT5","text":"A tension remains between the radical gratuity of divine goodness and the necessity of just order, which the text composes through the identity of divine wisdom, will, and goodness rather than through an external rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0070","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_13:MRT6","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text if justice is understood not as a limitation of God but as the ordering form of effects, and mercy not as an exception but as the ontological root of order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_13:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_13@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_P_13:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 22","title":"First Part · Question 21","context":"Chapter 22","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 21"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 21","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 22","order":23,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_P_14"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_14@R001","sha256":"3f8b09556f4f694c6d7eef7212e174cf27eaccf6383e9dc36b662aa6d28f7667","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:AS1","text":"Argumentatio metaphysica et reconstructiva quaerit utrum providentia divina Deo conveniat, quid complectatur, quo modo causaliter operetur, et utrum certitudinem cum contingentia componat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:AS2","text":"Providentia reconstituitur non ut consilium dubitans vel cura temporalis, sed ut ratio divina qua res ad finem ordinantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:AS3","text":"Ex universalitate causalitatis divinae infertur quod omne ens, etiam singulare, corruptibile, liberum et fortuitum respectu causarum proximarum, ordini providentiae subiacet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:AS4","text":"Distinctio inter rationem ordinis immediate in Deo existentem et executionem ordinis per causas secundas servat simul immediatam providentiam divinam et veram causalitatem creaturarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:AS5","text":"Certitudo infallibilis providentiae non uniformem necessitatem imponit, quia ipsa providentia praeordinat quosdam effectus necessario et alios contingenter evenire.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A1","text":"Providentia divina est ratio ordinis rerum in finem praeexistens in intellectu divino.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A2","text":"Deus creat non solum substantias rerum, sed etiam ordinem earum ad finem ultimum, qui est bonitas divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","text":"Ordinatio effectuum in finem tantum se extendit quantum causalitas primi agentis se extendit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A4","text":"Causalitas Dei se extendit ad omne ens, secundum principia universalia et individualia, incorruptibilia et corruptibilia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A5","text":"Providentiae ratio est aeterna, dum executio eius temporalis gubernatio vocatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A6","text":"Deus immediate habet rationem ordinis omnium effectuum, etiam minimorum, quia virtutes causarum quas instituit ad effectus producendos ipse praehabet intellectu.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A7","text":"Causae secundae ordinem providentiae exsequuntur, quia Deus dignitatem causalitatis creaturis communicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A8","text":"Provisor universalis aliquos defectus particulares permittere potest ne bonum perfectum universi impediatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A9","text":"Actus liberi arbitrii, quamvis a creatura rationali consilio et electione procedat, reducitur in Deum sicut in causam et providentiae divinae subditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A10","text":"Perfectio universi requirit diversos gradus entium et diversos modos causalitatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A11","text":"Providentia divina praeparat causas necessarias ad effectus necessarios et causas contingentes ad effectus contingentes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A12","text":"Infallibilitas providentiae pertinet ad eventum effectus simul cum modo eveniendi quem providentia disposuit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:NX1","text":"Providentia divina non est consilium ut inquisitio de rebus dubiis, quia Deus rationem rectam ordinandorum in finem certissime habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:NX2","text":"Providentia non est ipsa executio temporalis ordinis, quae gubernatio dicitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:NX3","text":"Providentia divina non introducit compositionem in Deo, quamvis intellectum respiciat et voluntatem finis praesupponat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:NX4","text":"Nulla regio entis, nec singulare corruptibile, nec creatura irrationalis, nec actus liberi arbitrii, providentiae divinae subtrahitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:NX5","text":"Casus et fortuna non sunt effectus sine ordine simpliciter, sed effectus qui ordinem causae particularis effugiunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:NX6","text":"Permissio mali non significat defectum potentiae divinae neque absentiam curae, sed ordinationem universalem quae bonum totius particulari defectui non subordinat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:NX7","text":"Creatura rationalis sibi relicta non est a providentia divina exclusa, sed virtute operativa ad unum non praefixa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:NX8","text":"Immediata providentia in ratione ordinis non excludit media causalia in executione ordinis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:NX9","text":"Immobilitas providentiae non est necessitas uniformiter imposita omnibus effectibus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:D1","text":"Quidquid quocumque modo esse participat, eo ipso ordinatur a Deo in finem et divinae providentiae subditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A1","CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","CH_GGVC_P_14:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:D2","text":"Eventus fortuitus respectu causae particularis est provisus respectu causae universalis, quia nulla causa particularis extra universalem causalitatem consistit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","CH_GGVC_P_14:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:D3","text":"Malum particulare non cadit extra providentiam, sed sub permissione qua bonum universi et bona ex defectibus consequentia servantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A2","CH_GGVC_P_14:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:D4","text":"Libertas creaturae rationalis est modus providentialiter ordinatus, non imminutio extensionis providentiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A4","CH_GGVC_P_14:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:D5","text":"Creatura rationalis specialiter sub providentia stat, quia eius actus imputabilis ad culpam vel meritum est et poenae vel praemii ordinem recipit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:D6","text":"Divina cognitio immediata omnium et gubernatio mediata per causas secundas sunt ordines diversi eiusdem providentiae, non causae contrariae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A5","CH_GGVC_P_14:A6","CH_GGVC_P_14:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:D7","text":"Necessitas et contingentia sunt ipsi modi effectuum a providentia dispositi, non eventus qui providentiam limitant vel frustrantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A10","CH_GGVC_P_14:A11","CH_GGVC_P_14:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:SD1","text":"Ordo providentialis est hierarchicus: causalitas universalis continet causalitates particulares sine earum ordines proprios abolendo.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","CH_GGVC_P_14:A4","CH_GGVC_P_14:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:SD2","text":"Immediatio divinae providentiae ad cognitionem et rationem ordinis pertinet, mediatio vero ad executionem; ideo directio divina et actio creata non in eodem plano competunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A5","CH_GGVC_P_14:A6","CH_GGVC_P_14:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:SD3","text":"Malum intelligitur ut defectus particularis permissibilis intra perfectionem ordinatam universi, non ut principium quod universalem ordinem evertit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A2","CH_GGVC_P_14:A8","CH_GGVC_P_14:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:SD4","text":"Contingentia non est imminutio perfectionis universi, sed gradus entis cuius proprius modus eveniendi ad perfectionem universi pertinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A10","CH_GGVC_P_14:A11","CH_GGVC_P_14:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:SD5","text":"Providentia universalis non aequat omnes creaturas moraliter, quia rationales creaturae, propter dominium suorum actuum, ordinem meriti et culpae recipiunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A4","CH_GGVC_P_14:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:DNX1","text":"Non datur ens providentialiter neutrum, quia participatio essendi et subordinatio ad finem eandem extensionem causalitatis divinae sequuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","CH_GGVC_P_14:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:DNX2","text":"Non datur casus absolutus, quia fortuitum solum ex defectu coordinationis inter causas particulares denominatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","CH_GGVC_P_14:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:DNX3","text":"Non datur libertas quae a causalitate prima sit independens, quia liberum arbitrium quidem se dirigit, sed eius actus in Deum sicut in causam reducitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:DNX4","text":"Non datur malum tamquam contra-principium ordinativum, quia permissio defectus sub bonum universi et sub bonum ex malo faciendum comprehenditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:DNX5","text":"Non datur immediatio divina quae creatas causas superfluas faciat, quia communicatio causalitatis est ipsa ratio medii gubernativi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A6","CH_GGVC_P_14:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:SNX1","text":"Impossibilis fit exterioritas absoluta ad ordinem divinum: si aliquid est, iam intra causalitatem, cognitionem et ordinationem primi agentis ponitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A1","CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","CH_GGVC_P_14:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:SNX2","text":"Impossibilis fit mundus omnino defectu carens ut norma primaria providentiae, quia perfectio universi non cum conservatione cuiuslibet boni particularis identifice habetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A8","CH_GGVC_P_14:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:SNX3","text":"Impossibilis fit mundus unius modi modalitatis, sive pure necessarius sive pure contingens, quia providentia perfectionem universitatis per pluralitatem causarum et effectuum instituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A10","CH_GGVC_P_14:A11","CH_GGVC_P_14:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:SNX4","text":"Impossibilis fit Deus qui cum causis creatis ut causa rivalis certat, quia divina ratio universalis eas instituit, ordinat et per eas executionem gubernationis producit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A6","CH_GGVC_P_14:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:FGS1","text":"Creatio substantiarum et ordinis ad finem → praeexistentia rationis ordinis in intellectu divino → providentia ut ratio aeterna.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A1","CH_GGVC_P_14:A2","CH_GGVC_P_14:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:FGS2","text":"Universalitas causalitatis divinae ad omnia principia entis → extensio ordinationis ad omnia entia → subiectio universalis providentiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","CH_GGVC_P_14:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:FGS3","text":"Ordo universalis causae primae → relativizatio casus ad ordinem causae particularis → inclusio fortuiti, mali et liberi actus intra providentiam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A4","CH_GGVC_P_14:A8","CH_GGVC_P_14:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:FGS4","text":"Ratio immediata omnium effectuum in Deo → communicatio virtutis causalis creaturis → executio mediata gubernationis sine remotionibus causarum secundarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A6","CH_GGVC_P_14:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:FGS5","text":"Perfectio universi per gradus entium → praeparatio causarum necessariarum et contingentium → certitudo effectus conservans modum necessarium vel contingentem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A10","CH_GGVC_P_14:A11","CH_GGVC_P_14:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:AF1","text":"Obiectio ex consilio dubitante, temporalitate et compositione → providentia definitur ut ratio intellectiva ordinis ad finem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:AF2","text":"Ratio ordinis creati → causalitas primi agentis universalis → providentia omnium singularium entium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:AF3","text":"Universalitas ordinis → reclassificatio casus, mali, necessitatis naturalis et libertatis ut modi intra providentiam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:AF4","text":"Immediata ratio divina omnium → executio per superiora et causas secundas → conservatio causalitatis creatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:AF5","text":"Certitudo immobilis providentiae → diversitas causarum proximarum → necessarium et contingens certo eveniunt secundum modum provisum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:ME1","text":"Oppositio inter providentiam et casum dissolvitur, quia casus transfertur e plano universalis ordinis ad defectum ordinis respectu causae particularis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:ME2","text":"Oppositio inter curam divinam et existentiam mali dissolvitur, quia bonum particulare et bonum perfectum universi non eadem regula aestimantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:D3","CH_GGVC_P_14:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:ME3","text":"Oppositio inter causalitatem divinam et causalitatem creatam dissolvitur, quia ratio immediata et executio mediata in diversis ordinibus collocantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:D6","CH_GGVC_P_14:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:ME4","text":"Oppositio inter providentiam infallibilem et contingentiam dissolvitur, quia contingentia ipsa fit modus effectus infallibiliter provisi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:D7","CH_GGVC_P_14:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:ME5","text":"Creatura rationalis non per exclusionem a providentia, sed per inclusionem specialem sub ordine meriti, culpae, poenae et praemii classificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:ALT1","text":"Surface — Quaestio explicita statuit utrum providentia Deo conveniat, utrum omnia complectatur, utrum immediate provideat, et utrum necessitatem imponat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:ALT2","text":"Structural — Providentia structuratur per relationem inter causalitatem universalem, causas particulares, bonum universi, gubernationem et diversos modos causalitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","CH_GGVC_P_14:A7","CH_GGVC_P_14:A8","CH_GGVC_P_14:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Obiectiones non removentur per negationem apparentium contrariorum, sed per translationem eorum ad diversos ordines causalitatis, executionis et modalitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:D2","CH_GGVC_P_14:D6","CH_GGVC_P_14:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Ens, finis, causalitas et modus essendi constituunt obiectum providentiae; ratio ordinis est aeterna, executio autem temporaliter in rebus procedit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A4","CH_GGVC_P_14:A5","CH_GGVC_P_14:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:MRT1","text":"Axiomata de ratione ordinis, creatione ordinis, universalitate causalitatis, causis secundis et modalitate effectuum sufficiunt ad seriem principalium conclusionum restituendam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A1","CH_GGVC_P_14:A2","CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","CH_GGVC_P_14:A7","CH_GGVC_P_14:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:MRT2","text":"Firmissimum nexus est extensio providentiae ex extensione causalitatis primi agentis, quia hinc simul sequuntur universalitas providentiae et relativitas casus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","CH_GGVC_P_14:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:MRT3","text":"Permissio mali pendet ex praelatione boni universi bono particulari, sed ipsa ratio qua hic ordo bonorum necessario statuitur in quaestione non amplius demonstratur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A8","CH_GGVC_P_14:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:MRT4","text":"Compatibilitas libertatis et causalitatis divinae per subordinationem causae particularis ad universalem affirmatur, sed mechanismus interior huius reductionis in praesenti quaestione non explicatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:MRT5","text":"Distinctio inter rationem ordinis immediate habitam et executionem mediatam efficaciter removet contradictionem apparentem inter immediatam providentiam et causas secundas.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A5","CH_GGVC_P_14:A6","CH_GGVC_P_14:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:MRT6","text":"Solutio determinismi consistit in eo quod providentia non solum eventum, sed modum eventus disponit; tensio residua est quod certitudo huius dispositionis modaliter asseritur potius quam ulterius demonstratur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A11","CH_GGVC_P_14:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:MRT7","text":"Restitutio conservat motum textus: definitio providentiae → extensio ad omne ens → distinctio rationis et executionis → conservatio contingentiae sub certitudine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T160357131054Z_314819dd","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 22","source_sha256":"3f8b09556f4f694c6d7eef7212e174cf27eaccf6383e9dc36b662aa6d28f7667","sha256":"7829d5df17fd093258ec57a3a89fb20bf8a30cc71b249f996e2b4b09093fe0be","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T16:03:57.132468+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:AS1","text":"The metaphysical and reconstructive argument asks whether divine providence befits God, what it encompasses, how it operates causally, and whether it reconciles certainty with contingency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:AS2","text":"Providence is reconstructed not as doubtful deliberation or temporal care, but as the divine reason by which things are ordered to an end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:AS3","text":"From the universality of divine causality it is inferred that every being, even the singular, corruptible, free, and fortuitous with respect to proximate causes, is subject to the order of providence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:AS4","text":"The distinction between the reason of order existing immediately in God and the execution of order through secondary causes preserves at once immediate divine providence and the true causality of creatures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:AS5","text":"The infallible certainty of providence does not impose uniform necessity, because providence itself preordains some effects to occur necessarily and others contingently.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A1","text":"Divine providence is the reason of the order of things to an end, pre-existing in the divine intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A2","text":"God creates not only the substances of things, but also their order to the ultimate end, which is divine goodness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","text":"The ordering of effects to an end extends only as far as the causality of the first agent extends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A4","text":"God's causality extends to every being, according to universal and individual principles, incorruptible and corruptible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A5","text":"The reason of providence is eternal, whereas its execution is called temporal governance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A6","text":"God immediately possesses the reason of the order of all effects, even the least, because He Himself possesses intellectually beforehand the powers of the causes that He instituted to produce effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A7","text":"Secondary causes execute the order of providence, because God communicates the dignity of causality to creatures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A8","text":"The universal provider can permit certain particular defects so that the perfect good of the universe may not be impeded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A9","text":"The act of free choice, although it proceeds from a rational creature through deliberation and choice, is reduced to God as to a cause and is subject to divine providence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A10","text":"The perfection of the universe requires diverse grades of beings and diverse modes of causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A11","text":"Divine providence prepares necessary causes for necessary effects and contingent causes for contingent effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:A12","text":"The infallibility of providence pertains to the occurrence of an effect together with the mode of occurring that providence has disposed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:NX1","text":"Divine providence is not deliberation as inquiry into doubtful matters, because God most certainly possesses the right reason of things to be ordered to an end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:NX2","text":"Providence is not the temporal execution itself of order, which is called governance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:NX3","text":"Divine providence does not introduce composition into God, although it concerns the intellect and presupposes the will of the end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:NX4","text":"No region of being, neither the singular corruptible thing, nor the irrational creature, nor the act of free choice, is withdrawn from divine providence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:NX5","text":"Chance and fortune are not simply effects without order, but effects that escape the order of a particular cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:NX6","text":"The permission of evil does not signify a defect of divine power or an absence of care, but a universal ordering that does not subordinate the good of the whole to a particular defect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:NX7","text":"The rational creature, left to itself, is not excluded from divine providence, but is not fixed by operative power to one thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:NX8","text":"Immediate providence in the reason of order does not exclude causal means in the execution of order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:NX9","text":"The immutability of providence is not necessity imposed uniformly upon all effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:D1","text":"Whatever participates in being in any manner is thereby ordered by God to an end and subject to divine providence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A1","CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","CH_GGVC_P_14:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:D2","text":"An event fortuitous with respect to a particular cause is provided for with respect to the universal cause, because no particular cause exists outside universal causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","CH_GGVC_P_14:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:D3","text":"Particular evil does not fall outside providence, but under the permission by which the good of the universe and the goods consequent upon defects are preserved.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A2","CH_GGVC_P_14:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:D4","text":"The freedom of the rational creature is a providentially ordered mode, not a diminishment of the extension of providence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A4","CH_GGVC_P_14:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:D5","text":"The rational creature stands specially under providence, because its act is imputable to fault or merit and receives the order of punishment or reward.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:D6","text":"Immediate divine knowledge of all things and mediate governance through secondary causes are distinct orders of the same providence, not contrary causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A5","CH_GGVC_P_14:A6","CH_GGVC_P_14:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:D7","text":"Necessity and contingency are themselves modes of effects disposed by providence, not events that limit or frustrate providence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A10","CH_GGVC_P_14:A11","CH_GGVC_P_14:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:SD1","text":"The providential order is hierarchical: universal causality contains particular causalities without abolishing their proper orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","CH_GGVC_P_14:A4","CH_GGVC_P_14:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:SD2","text":"The immediacy of divine providence pertains to cognition and the reason of order, whereas mediation pertains to execution; therefore divine direction and created action do not obtain on the same plane.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A5","CH_GGVC_P_14:A6","CH_GGVC_P_14:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:SD3","text":"Evil is understood as a particular defect that may be permitted within the ordered perfection of the universe, not as a principle that overturns universal order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A2","CH_GGVC_P_14:A8","CH_GGVC_P_14:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:SD4","text":"Contingency is not a diminishment of the perfection of the universe, but a grade of being whose proper mode of occurring pertains to the perfection of the universe.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A10","CH_GGVC_P_14:A11","CH_GGVC_P_14:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:SD5","text":"Universal providence does not morally equalize all creatures, because rational creatures, on account of dominion over their acts, receive the order of merit and fault.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A4","CH_GGVC_P_14:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:DNX1","text":"There is no providentially neutral being, because participation in being and subordination to an end follow the same extension of divine causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","CH_GGVC_P_14:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:DNX2","text":"There is no absolute chance, because the fortuitous is denominated only from a defect of coordination among particular causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","CH_GGVC_P_14:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:DNX3","text":"There is no freedom independent of the first causality, because free choice indeed directs itself, but its act is reduced to God as to a cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:DNX4","text":"There is no evil as a counter-ordering principle, because the permission of defect is comprehended under the good of the universe and under the good to be made from evil.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:DNX5","text":"There is no divine immediacy that renders created causes superfluous, because the communication of causality is itself the reason of the means of governance.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A6","CH_GGVC_P_14:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:SNX1","text":"Absolute exteriority to divine order becomes impossible: if something is, it is already posited within the causality, cognition, and ordering of the first agent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A1","CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","CH_GGVC_P_14:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:SNX2","text":"A world wholly lacking defect becomes impossible as the primary norm of providence, because the perfection of the universe is not identified with the preservation of every particular good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A8","CH_GGVC_P_14:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:SNX3","text":"A world of one mode of modality, whether purely necessary or purely contingent, becomes impossible, because providence establishes the perfection of the universe through a plurality of causes and effects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A10","CH_GGVC_P_14:A11","CH_GGVC_P_14:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:SNX4","text":"A God who competes with created causes as a rival cause becomes impossible, because the universal divine reason institutes them, orders them, and through them produces the execution of governance.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A6","CH_GGVC_P_14:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:FGS1","text":"Creation of substances and of order to an end → pre-existence of the reason of order in the divine intellect → providence as eternal reason.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A1","CH_GGVC_P_14:A2","CH_GGVC_P_14:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:FGS2","text":"Universality of divine causality to all principles of being → extension of ordering to all beings → universal subjection to providence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","CH_GGVC_P_14:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:FGS3","text":"Universal order of the first cause → relativization of chance to the order of a particular cause → inclusion of the fortuitous, evil, and free act within providence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A4","CH_GGVC_P_14:A8","CH_GGVC_P_14:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:FGS4","text":"Immediate reason of all effects in God → communication of causal power to creatures → mediate execution of governance without the removal of secondary causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A6","CH_GGVC_P_14:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:FGS5","text":"Perfection of the universe through grades of beings → preparation of necessary and contingent causes → certainty of the effect preserving the necessary or contingent mode.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A10","CH_GGVC_P_14:A11","CH_GGVC_P_14:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:AF1","text":"Objection from doubtful deliberation, temporality, and composition → providence is defined as the intellectual reason of order to an end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:AF2","text":"Reason of created order → universal causality of the first agent → providence over all singular beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:AF3","text":"Universality of order → reclassification of chance, evil, natural necessity, and freedom as modes within providence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:AF4","text":"Immediate divine reason of all things → execution through higher causes and secondary causes → preservation of created causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:AF5","text":"Immutable certainty of providence → diversity of proximate causes → the necessary and contingent occur certainly according to the foreseen mode.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:ME1","text":"The opposition between providence and chance is dissolved, because chance is transferred from the plane of universal order to a defect of order with respect to a particular cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:ME2","text":"The opposition between divine care and the existence of evil is dissolved, because particular good and the perfect good of the universe are not evaluated by the same rule.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:D3","CH_GGVC_P_14:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:ME3","text":"The opposition between divine causality and created causality is dissolved, because immediate reason and mediate execution are situated in distinct orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:D6","CH_GGVC_P_14:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:ME4","text":"The opposition between infallible providence and contingency is dissolved, because contingency itself becomes the mode of an infallibly foreseen effect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:D7","CH_GGVC_P_14:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:ME5","text":"The rational creature is classified not through exclusion from providence, but through special inclusion under the order of merit, fault, punishment, and reward.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:ALT1","text":"Surface — The explicit question establishes whether providence befits God, whether it encompasses all things, whether it provides immediately, and whether it imposes necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:ALT2","text":"Structural — Providence is structured through the relation between universal causality, particular causes, the good of the universe, governance, and diverse modes of causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","CH_GGVC_P_14:A7","CH_GGVC_P_14:A8","CH_GGVC_P_14:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Objections are not removed by denying apparent contraries, but by transferring them to distinct orders of causality, execution, and modality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:D2","CH_GGVC_P_14:D6","CH_GGVC_P_14:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Being, end, causality, and mode of being constitute the object of providence; the reason of order is eternal, whereas execution proceeds temporally in things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A4","CH_GGVC_P_14:A5","CH_GGVC_P_14:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:MRT1","text":"The axioms concerning the reason of order, the creation of order, the universality of causality, secondary causes, and the modality of effects suffice to restore the series of principal conclusions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A1","CH_GGVC_P_14:A2","CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","CH_GGVC_P_14:A7","CH_GGVC_P_14:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:MRT2","text":"The strongest connection is the extension of providence from the extension of the causality of the first agent, because from this follow simultaneously the universality of providence and the relativity of chance.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A3","CH_GGVC_P_14:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:MRT3","text":"The permission of evil depends upon the precedence of the good of the universe over particular good, but the very reason why this order of goods is necessarily established is no longer demonstrated in the question.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A8","CH_GGVC_P_14:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0043","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:MRT4","text":"The compatibility of freedom and divine causality is affirmed through the subordination of the particular cause to the universal, but the inner mechanism of this reduction is not explained in the present question.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0048","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:MRT5","text":"The distinction between the immediately possessed reason of order and mediate execution effectively removes the apparent contradiction between immediate providence and secondary causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A5","CH_GGVC_P_14:A6","CH_GGVC_P_14:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:MRT6","text":"The solution to determinism consists in this: providence disposes not only the event, but also the mode of the event; the residual tension is that the certainty of this disposition is asserted modally rather than further demonstrated.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_14:A11","CH_GGVC_P_14:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0082","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0086","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_14:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text: definition of providence → extension to every being → distinction of reason and execution → preservation of contingency under certainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_14:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_14@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_P_14:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 23","title":"First Part · Question 22","context":"Chapter 23","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 22"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 22","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 23","order":24,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_P_15"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_15@R001","sha256":"94aed4baaeef7217ce9ec37fd6a21150430cb25f59c7a704e3d0c645fd6507c4","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:AS1","text":"Quaestio est quomodo ordinatio divina rationalium ad salutem supernaturalem constitui possit sine necessitatione actuum, abolitione libertatis aut iniustitia distributionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:AS2","text":"Praedestinatio est ratio aeterna ordinis aliquorum ad salutem, in mente divina existens, cuius exsecutio temporalis per gratiam, actus liberos et gloriam infallibiliter impletur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:AS3","text":"Reprobatio constituitur ut pars providentiae permittens defectum a fine et ordinans poenam, non ut causa culpae symmetrica praedestinationi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:AS4","text":"Dilectio causans bonum antecedit electionem, electio antecedit praedestinationem, neque merita humana causant totum ordinem salutis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:AS5","text":"Certitudo praedestinationis et definitio numeri electorum conservantur per necessitatem conditionatam, non per necessitatem absolutam effectuum proximorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0123","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:AS6","text":"Preces et bona opera non mutant praeordinationem aeternam, sed intra eam ordinantur tamquam causae secundae exsecutionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0156","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:AS7","text":"Argumentatio metaphysica, reconstructiva et polemica procedit a providentia universali ad distinctiones inter ordinem et exsecutionem, causam primam et secundam, necessitatem absolutam et conditionatam, decretum et media temporalia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0154","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A1","text":"Omnia divinae providentiae subiacent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A2","text":"Providentia est ratio ordinis rerum in finem in intellectu divino; gubernatio est eius exsecutio in rebus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A3","text":"Finis creaturae aut naturae proportionatur aut facultatem omnis naturae creatae excedit, sicut vita aeterna in visione divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A4","text":"Creatura rationalis ad finem supernaturalem pervenire non potest nisi a Deo secundum rationem in ipso praeexistentem transmittatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A5","text":"Praedestinatio, quantum ad obiecta, est pars providentiae et ratio divina ordinis aliquorum, etiam nondum existentium, in salutem aeternam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A6","text":"Praedestinatio ipsa est tantum in praedestinante; eius exsecutio active est in Deo et passive in praedestinatis per vocationem, gratiam et gloriam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A7","text":"Quia voluntas divina bonum electi causat, dilectio secundum rationem antecedit electionem, et electio praedestinationem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A8","text":"Totus effectus praedestinationis comprehendit quidquid hominem ordinat in salutem, etiam praeparationem, usum liberi arbitrii et merita.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A9","text":"Effectus particulares praedestinationis possunt inter se ordinari, gratia ad meritum ut principium, meritum ad gloriam ut causa meritoria.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","text":"Causa prima effectus producit per causas secundas, quarum proprium modum agendi non excludit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A11","text":"Reprobatio est pars providentiae includens voluntatem permittendi casum a fine et inferendi poenam propter culpam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A12","text":"Praedestinatio causat gratiam et gloriam, reprobatio autem derelictionem et poenam, non culpam, quae ex libero arbitrio reprobati provenit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A13","text":"Ordo praedestinationis infallibilis est, quamvis effectus eius secundum causas proximas contingenter et libere proveniant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A14","text":"Numerus praedestinatorum est Deo certus formaliter et materialiter per electionem et principalem praefinitionem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0137","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A15","text":"Divina bonitas in diversitate effectuum repraesentatur per misericordiam in praedestinatis et per iustitiam in puniendis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A16","text":"Cur hic eligatur et ille reprobetur nullam rationem ulteriorem habet praeter simplicem voluntatem divinam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A17","text":"In bonis ex gratia datis inaequalitas non est iniusta, dummodo nulli subtrahatur debitum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A18","text":"Preces, bona opera et alia media salutis sub ordine praedestinationis cadunt ut causae secundae quibus effectus eius impletur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0156","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX1","text":"Praedestinatio non est praedeterminatio quae necessitatem imponit aut virtutem compellit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX2","text":"Creaturae irrationales ad finem supernaturalem incapaces non proprie praedestinantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX3","text":"Miseria antecedens non pertinet ad rationem praedestinationis, quae a termino ad quem specificatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX4","text":"Praedestinatio propria non omnibus revelatur, quia desperatio reproborum et negligentia securorum sequerentur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX5","text":"Praedestinatio non est passio aut dispositio in praedestinato, quamvis eius exsecutio ibi effectum ponat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX6","text":"Gratia temporalis non ingreditur essentiam praedestinationis, sed se habet ad eam ut effectus et obiectum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX7","text":"Reprobatio non est praescientia nuda defectus futuri.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX8","text":"Reprobatio non causat culpam eo modo quo praedestinatio causat gratiam et gloriam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX9","text":"Voluntas antecedens qua Deus omnes homines salvos vult non est voluntas consequens qua salutem omnium simpliciter decernit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX10","text":"Merita in vita priore supposita non sunt causa effectus praedestinationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0094","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX11","text":"Initium benefaciendi non est residuum Pelagianum a solo homine praestitum ante auxilium divinum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0094","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX12","text":"Usus gratiae praescitus non causat collationem gratiae nisi secundum rationem causae finalis particularis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0099","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX13","text":"Certitudo praedestinationis non est necessitas absoluta neque abolitio contingentiae et libertatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0123","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX14","text":"Numerus praedestinatorum non est certus tantum formaliter aut indifferens quoad identitates singulares.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0134","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX15","text":"Preces neque superfluae sunt propter certitudinem decreti neque capaces sunt decretum aeternum mutandi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0154","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:D1","text":"Praedestinatio proprie convenit omni creaturae rationali capaci vitae aeternae, sive humanae sive angelicae, non autem irrationalibus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A3","CH_GGVC_P_15:A4","CH_GGVC_P_15:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:D2","text":"Praedestinatio potest respicere nondum existentem et manere aeterna, quia futuri effectus ratio in agente non est ipse effectus temporalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A5","CH_GGVC_P_15:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:D3","text":"Electio divina non respondet bono electi iam praesupposito, sed procedit ex dilectione quae ipsum bonum causat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:D4","text":"Nullum meritum vel initium humanum potest causare totum effectum praedestinationis, quia ipsum ordinari ad meritum iam intra illum effectum continetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:D5","text":"Meritum humanum veram causalitatem particularem retinet sine constitutione principii a gratia independentis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A9","CH_GGVC_P_15:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:D6","text":"Salus comprehensive a praedestinatione procedit, sed perditio quoad culpam creaturae imputatur, quamvis derelictio et poena sub reprobatione ordinentur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A11","CH_GGVC_P_15:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:D7","text":"Necessitas salutis praedestinati est conditionata ex supposita immutabilitate ordinis divini, non absoluta ex natura actuum particularium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0123","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:D8","text":"Certitudo singularum praedestinationum requirit numerum electorum materialiter definitum, non solam quantitatem abstractam salvandorum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A13","CH_GGVC_P_15:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0134","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:D9","text":"Diversitas communis misericordiae et iustitiae rationem accipit ex bonitate divina, sed distributio singularis terminatur in voluntate, sine iniustitia quia gloria non debetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A15","CH_GGVC_P_15:A16","CH_GGVC_P_15:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0105","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:D10","text":"Preces et bona opera valent non quasi causae decreti, sed quasi media ab eodem decreto praefinita ad effectum certum producendum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0156","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SD1","text":"Tota doctrina structuram duplicem constituit: ordinem aeternum et immanentem in Deo, atque exsecutionem temporalem et transeuntem in creaturis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A1","CH_GGVC_P_15:A2","CH_GGVC_P_15:A5","CH_GGVC_P_15:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SD2","text":"Libertas non collocatur extra causalitatem divinam, sed definitur ut modus contingens causae secundae ab ordine causae primae comprehensae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A8","CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SD3","text":"Causalitas salutis et causalitas perditionis deliberate asymmetricae sunt: bonum salutare totaliter reducitur ad Deum, culpa autem ad defectum voluntatis creatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A8","CH_GGVC_P_15:A11","CH_GGVC_P_15:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SD4","text":"Certitudo finis actionem temporalem non evacuat, quia eadem certitudo media libera et contingentia includit quibus finis attingitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A13","CH_GGVC_P_15:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0156","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SD5","text":"Explicatio electionis duplicem clausuram habet: diversitas ordinum rationem universalem ex bonitate accipit, sed identitas singulorum nullam causam ulteriorem praeter voluntatem recipit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A15","CH_GGVC_P_15:A16","CH_GGVC_P_15:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0105","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SD6","text":"Electi ut pars principaliter praefinita universi constituuntur; reprobi autem, secundum cautam textus formulam, «videntur» ordinari ad bonum electorum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A14","CH_GGVC_P_15:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0137","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SD7","text":"Certitudo divina cum obscuritate status singularis creaturae coniungitur, unde praxis exigitur sine accessu ordinario ad cognitionem propriae praedestinationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A13","CH_GGVC_P_15:A14","CH_GGVC_P_15:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0156","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:DNX1","text":"Causalitas divina et causalitas creata non sunt causae concurrentes quarum una tantum admitti possit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A9","CH_GGVC_P_15:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:DNX2","text":"Libertas non requirit independentiam actus a causa prima universali.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:DNX3","text":"Infallibilitas ordinis non requirit ut causae proximae necessario agant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:DNX4","text":"Iustitia non requirit aequalitatem gratuitorum ubi nullum debitum antecedens violatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0106","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:DNX5","text":"Permissio defectus non significat defectum providentiae, sed subordinationem defectus ordini universaliori.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A1","CH_GGVC_P_15:A11","CH_GGVC_P_15:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0103","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:DNX6","text":"Causa temporalis non potest retroactive constituere aut mutare actum aeternum, quamvis ab illo actu ut medium exsecutionis ordinetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A6","CH_GGVC_P_15:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0154","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:DNX7","text":"Praescientia nuda non sufficit ad praedestinationem vel reprobationem, quia utraque voluntatem et ordinationem causaliter addit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A5","CH_GGVC_P_15:A7","CH_GGVC_P_15:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0058","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0093","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SNX1","text":"Nullum principium salutare extra totum effectum praedestinationis relinquitur quo homo causam suae electionis antecedenter constituat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A8","CH_GGVC_P_15:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SNX2","text":"Nulla mensura meritorum vel aequalitatis divinae voluntati antecedens singularum electionum distributionem regit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A16","CH_GGVC_P_15:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0105","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0106","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SNX3","text":"Creaturae ordinario modo indisponibilis est prospectus ex quo proprium decretum aeternum directe cognoscat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_15:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SNX4","text":"Nulla causa secunda extra ordinem causae primae stat ut praedestinationem impedire aut corrigere possit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0162","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SNX5","text":"Systema excludit attributionem symmetricam qua Deus eodem modo causa culpae esset quo causa gratiae est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A11","CH_GGVC_P_15:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS1","text":"Subiectio universalis providentiae, finis supernaturalis et insufficientia naturae generant rationem divinae transmissionis, quae praedestinatio nominatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A1","CH_GGVC_P_15:A3","CH_GGVC_P_15:A4","CH_GGVC_P_15:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS2","text":"Ratio aeterna in agente distinguitur ab exsecutione temporali, quae per vocationem, gratiam, actum liberum, meritum et gloriam progreditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A2","CH_GGVC_P_15:A6","CH_GGVC_P_15:A8","CH_GGVC_P_15:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS3","text":"Voluntas causans bonum producit ordinem dilectio → electio → praedestinatio, quo meritum antecedens ut causa totalis excluditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A7","CH_GGVC_P_15:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS4","text":"Effectus particulares inter se causaliter ordinantur, sed totus nexus ex bonitate divina ut primo principio et ultimo fine procedit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A8","CH_GGVC_P_15:A9","CH_GGVC_P_15:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS5","text":"Providentia defectum permittit; permissio defectus generat reprobationem, quae derelictionem et poenam ordinat, culpa autem libero arbitrio imputatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A11","CH_GGVC_P_15:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS6","text":"Ordinatio causarum proximarum contingentium producit simul infallibilitatem ordinis, libertatem effectus et necessitatem conditionatam finis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0123","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS7","text":"Electio singularis infallibilis generat numerum materialiter definitum; ratio diversitatis communis ex bonitate, assignatio singularis ex voluntate simplici sumitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A14","CH_GGVC_P_15:A15","CH_GGVC_P_15:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS8","text":"Causae secundae intra ordinem inclusae efficiunt ut preces et opera exsecutionem iuvent, quamvis praeordinationem neque constituant neque impediant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0156","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0162","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:AF1","text":"Motus materialis procedit per seriem: providentia universalis → definitio praedestinationis → locus eius in Deo → reprobatio asymmetrica → dilectio et electio → exclusio meritorum antecedentium → certitudo → numerus definitus → causalitas precum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:AF2","text":"Motus probativus recurrenter transit ab obiecto fundato in sensu univoco ad distinctionem ordinum, sensuum vel causalitatum, deinde ad reinsertionem utriusque termini sub providentia universali.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_15:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_15:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_15:NX13","CH_GGVC_P_15:NX15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ME1","text":"Praedestinatio ex imagine fati necessitatem imponentis in rationem providentiae finem supernaturalem ordinantis transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A5","CH_GGVC_P_15:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ME2","text":"Unum obiectum apparens dividitur in praeordinationem aeternam et exsecutionem temporalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A2","CH_GGVC_P_15:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ME3","text":"Libertas et gratia desinunt esse fontes causales mutuo exclusivi et fiunt gradus eiusdem ordinis primae et secundae causalitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A9","CH_GGVC_P_15:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ME4","text":"Meritum ex causa antecedente electionis in effectum internum praedestinationis et causam particularem gloriae reclassificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A8","CH_GGVC_P_15:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ME5","text":"Reprobatio ex imagine causationis directa culpae in permissionem defectus, derelictionem et ordinationem poenae distinguitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A11","CH_GGVC_P_15:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ME6","text":"Oppositio inter certitudinem et libertatem dissolvitur per distinctionem ordinis infallibilis, effectus contingentis et necessitatis conditionatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0123","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ME7","text":"Numerus electorum ex quantitate cognita in partem universi per electionem materialiter praefinitam convertitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0137","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ME8","text":"Preces et opera ex causis decretum mutaturis aut actibus superfluis in media interna exsecutionis transferuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A18","CH_GGVC_P_15:NX15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0154","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0156","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ME9","text":"Ordo humanus electionis ex bono praeexistente invertitur in Deo, cuius dilectio bonum electi antecedenter causat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ALT1","text":"In gradu superficiei determinantur convenientia, natura, locus, causa, certitudo, numerus et adiuvabilitas praedestinationis atque relatio eius ad reprobationem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ALT2","text":"In gradu structurali providentia dividitur in rationem ordinis et gubernationem, causalitas autem in primam et secundam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A2","CH_GGVC_P_15:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ALT3","text":"In gradu metastrategico oppositiones inter gratiam et libertatem, certitudinem et contingentiam, decretum et orationem per distinctionem graduum causalium reconstituuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_15:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ALT4","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali actus aeternus atque immanens effectus temporales, contingentes et liberos per causas secundas producit sine mutatione sui.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A6","CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A13","CH_GGVC_P_15:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0123","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad restituendam seriem a providentia et fine supernaturali usque ad electionem, reprobationem, certitudinem, numerum et media exsecutionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A1","CH_GGVC_P_15:A2","CH_GGVC_P_15:A3","CH_GGVC_P_15:A4","CH_GGVC_P_15:A5","CH_GGVC_P_15:A6","CH_GGVC_P_15:A7","CH_GGVC_P_15:A8","CH_GGVC_P_15:A9","CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A11","CH_GGVC_P_15:A12","CH_GGVC_P_15:A13","CH_GGVC_P_15:A14","CH_GGVC_P_15:A15","CH_GGVC_P_15:A16","CH_GGVC_P_15:A17","CH_GGVC_P_15:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0134","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:MRT2","text":"Structura firmissima est distinctio inter praeordinationem in Deo et effectum in creatura, quia ea plures difficultates temporales et causales simul regit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A2","CH_GGVC_P_15:A5","CH_GGVC_P_15:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:MRT3","text":"Concordia certitudinis et libertatis interne cohaeret tantum si causalitas prima et secunda non competitive atque necessitas conditionata admittuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A13","CH_GGVC_P_15:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0123","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:MRT4","text":"Ratio electionis singularis nondum ulterius determinatur, quia regressus explicativus ad simplicem voluntatem divinam terminatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A16","CH_GGVC_P_15:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:MRT5","text":"Commissura reprobationis fragilis manet inter voluntatem divinam permittendi casum, causationem derelictionis et attributionem culpae soli libero arbitrio.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A11","CH_GGVC_P_15:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0058","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0064","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:MRT6","text":"Distinctio voluntatis antecedentis et consequentis compatibilitatem amoris universalis cum reprobatione formaliter tuetur, sed rationem distributionis singularis non auget.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_15:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0082","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:MRT7","text":"Transitus a diversitate cosmologica ad misericordiam et poenam rationem communem praebet, sed analogia universi identitates singulares non demonstrat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A15","CH_GGVC_P_15:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0103","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0105","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:MRT8","text":"Adiumentum precum cohaeret per aequivocationem ordinatam: praeordinatio nullo modo iuvatur, effectus autem per causas secundas iuvatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A18","CH_GGVC_P_15:NX15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0158","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:MRT9","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat, quia singulae conclusiones ad eandem structuram providentiae, distinctionis ordinum et inclusionis causarum secundarum reducuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS1","CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS2","CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS3","CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS4","CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS5","CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS6","CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS7","CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T160428692342Z_7e04338f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 23","source_sha256":"94aed4baaeef7217ce9ec37fd6a21150430cb25f59c7a704e3d0c645fd6507c4","sha256":"c03d5c440fc2284f7c725e580fddc301b1aaf4f141a39fec446c6005d9059159","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T16:04:28.693758+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:AS1","text":"The question is how the divine ordering of rational beings to supernatural salvation can be established without necessitating acts, abolishing freedom, or injustice in distribution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:AS2","text":"Predestination is the eternal plan of the ordering of certain persons to salvation, existing in the divine mind, whose temporal execution is infallibly fulfilled through grace, free acts, and glory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:AS3","text":"Reprobation is constituted as a part of providence permitting defection from the end and ordering punishment, not as a cause of fault symmetrical to predestination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:AS4","text":"Love causing good precedes election, election precedes predestination, and human merits do not cause the whole order of salvation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:AS5","text":"The certainty of predestination and the determination of the number of the elect are maintained through conditional necessity, not through the absolute necessity of proximate effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0123","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:AS6","text":"Prayers and good works do not change eternal preordination, but are ordered within it as secondary causes of execution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0156","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:AS7","text":"The metaphysical, reconstructive, and polemical argument proceeds from universal providence to distinctions between order and execution, first and secondary cause, absolute and conditional necessity, decree and temporal means.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0154","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A1","text":"All things are subject to divine providence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A2","text":"Providence is the plan of the ordering of things to an end in the divine intellect; governance is its execution in things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A3","text":"The end of a creature is either proportioned to nature or exceeds the capacity of every created nature, as eternal life in the divine vision.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A4","text":"A rational creature cannot attain a supernatural end unless it is conveyed by God according to a plan pre-existing in Him.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A5","text":"Predestination, as regards its objects, is a part of providence and the divine plan of the ordering of certain persons, even not yet existing, to eternal salvation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A6","text":"Predestination itself exists only in the predestining agent; its execution is actively in God and passively in the predestined through calling, grace, and glory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A7","text":"Because the divine will causes the good of the elect, love precedes election in account, and election predestination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A8","text":"The whole effect of predestination encompasses whatever orders a human being to salvation, including preparation, the use of free choice, and merits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A9","text":"The particular effects of predestination can be ordered among themselves: grace to merit as principle, merit to glory as meritorious cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","text":"The first cause produces its effect through secondary causes, whose proper mode of acting it does not exclude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A11","text":"Reprobation is a part of providence including the will to permit falling away from the end and to inflict punishment on account of fault.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A12","text":"Predestination causes grace and glory, whereas reprobation causes abandonment and punishment, not fault, which proceeds from the free choice of the reprobate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A13","text":"The order of predestination is infallible, although its effects proceed contingently and freely according to proximate causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A14","text":"The number of the predestined is certain to God formally and materially through election and principal predefinition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0137","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A15","text":"Divine goodness is represented in the diversity of effects through mercy in the predestined and justice in those punished.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A16","text":"Why this one is chosen and that one reprobated has no further reason beyond the simple divine will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A17","text":"In goods given from grace, inequality is not unjust, provided that what is due is not withheld from anyone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:A18","text":"Prayers, good works, and other means of salvation fall under the order of predestination as secondary causes by which its effect is fulfilled.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0156","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX1","text":"Predestination is not predetermination that imposes necessity or compels virtue.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX2","text":"Irrational creatures incapable of a supernatural end are not properly predestined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX3","text":"Antecedent misery does not belong to the account of predestination, which is specified by the terminus toward which.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX4","text":"Predestination properly speaking is not revealed to everyone, because the despair of the reprobate and the negligence of the secure would follow.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX5","text":"Predestination is not a passion or disposition in the predestined, although its execution places an effect there.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX6","text":"Temporal grace does not enter into the essence of predestination, but stands in relation to it as effect and object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX7","text":"Reprobation is not bare foreknowledge of future defection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX8","text":"Reprobation does not cause fault in the way predestination causes grace and glory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX9","text":"The antecedent will by which God wills all human beings to be saved is not the consequent will by which He simply decrees the salvation of all.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX10","text":"Merits supposed in a prior life are not the cause of the effect of predestination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0094","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX11","text":"The beginning of doing good is not a Pelagian remnant rendered by human beings alone before divine aid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0094","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX12","text":"The foreknown use of grace does not cause the conferral of grace except according to the account of a particular final cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0099","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX13","text":"The certainty of predestination is not absolute necessity or the abolition of contingency and freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0123","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX14","text":"The number of the predestined is not certain only formally or indifferent with respect to singular identities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0134","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:NX15","text":"Prayers are neither superfluous because of the certainty of the decree nor capable of changing the eternal decree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0154","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:D1","text":"Predestination properly belongs to every rational creature capable of eternal life, whether human or angelic, but not to irrational creatures.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A3","CH_GGVC_P_15:A4","CH_GGVC_P_15:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:D2","text":"Predestination can concern one not yet existing and remain eternal, because the plan of a future effect in the agent is not itself the temporal effect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A5","CH_GGVC_P_15:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:D3","text":"Divine election does not answer to an already presupposed good of the elect, but proceeds from love that causes that very good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:D4","text":"No human merit or beginning can cause the whole effect of predestination, because being ordered to merit itself is already contained within that effect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:D5","text":"Human merit retains true particular causality without establishing a principle independent of grace.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A9","CH_GGVC_P_15:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:D6","text":"Salvation proceeds comprehensively from predestination, but perdition as regards fault is imputed to the creature, although abandonment and punishment are ordered under reprobation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A11","CH_GGVC_P_15:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:D7","text":"The necessity of the salvation of the predestined is conditional upon the supposed immutability of the divine order, not absolute from the nature of particular acts.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0123","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:D8","text":"The certainty of singular predestinations requires a materially determinate number of the elect, not merely an abstract quantity of those to be saved.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A13","CH_GGVC_P_15:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0134","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:D9","text":"The diversity of mercy and justice in common receives its account from divine goodness, but singular distribution terminates in the will, without injustice because glory is not due.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A15","CH_GGVC_P_15:A16","CH_GGVC_P_15:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0105","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:D10","text":"Prayers and good works avail not as causes of the decree, but as means pre-defined by the same decree for producing a certain effect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0156","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SD1","text":"The whole doctrine constitutes a double structure: an eternal and immanent order in God, and a temporal and transitive execution in creatures.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A1","CH_GGVC_P_15:A2","CH_GGVC_P_15:A5","CH_GGVC_P_15:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SD2","text":"Freedom is not situated outside divine causality, but is defined as the contingent mode of a secondary cause comprehended by the order of the first cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A8","CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SD3","text":"The causality of salvation and the causality of perdition are deliberately asymmetrical: salvific good is wholly reduced to God, but fault to the defect of the created will.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A8","CH_GGVC_P_15:A11","CH_GGVC_P_15:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SD4","text":"Certainty of the end does not evacuate temporal action, because that same certainty includes the free and contingent means by which the end is attained.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A13","CH_GGVC_P_15:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0156","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SD5","text":"The explanation of election has a double closure: diversity of orders receives a universal account from goodness, but the identity of singulars admits no further cause beyond the will.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A15","CH_GGVC_P_15:A16","CH_GGVC_P_15:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0105","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SD6","text":"The elect are constituted as the principally pre-defined part of the universe; the reprobate, however, according to the cautious formulation of the text, «videntur» to be ordered to the good of the elect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A14","CH_GGVC_P_15:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0137","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SD7","text":"Divine certainty is joined with obscurity concerning the singular status of the creature, whence practice is required without ordinary access to knowledge of one's own predestination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A13","CH_GGVC_P_15:A14","CH_GGVC_P_15:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0156","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:DNX1","text":"Divine causality and created causality are not concurrent causes of which only one can be admitted.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A9","CH_GGVC_P_15:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:DNX2","text":"Freedom does not require the independence of an act from the universal first cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:DNX3","text":"The infallibility of the order does not require proximate causes to act necessarily.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:DNX4","text":"Justice does not require equality of gratuitous gifts where no antecedent due is violated.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0106","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:DNX5","text":"Permission of a defect does not signify a defect of providence, but the subordination of the defect to a more universal order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A1","CH_GGVC_P_15:A11","CH_GGVC_P_15:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0103","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:DNX6","text":"A temporal cause cannot retroactively establish or change an eternal act, although it is ordered by that act as a means of execution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A6","CH_GGVC_P_15:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0154","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:DNX7","text":"Bare foreknowledge does not suffice for predestination or reprobation, because each adds will and causal ordering.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A5","CH_GGVC_P_15:A7","CH_GGVC_P_15:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0058","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0093","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SNX1","text":"No salvific principle outside the whole effect of predestination is left by which a human being might antecedently establish the cause of his own election.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A8","CH_GGVC_P_15:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SNX2","text":"No measure of merits or equality antecedent to the divine will governs the distribution of singular elections.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A16","CH_GGVC_P_15:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0105","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0106","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SNX3","text":"The perspective from which a creature might directly know its own eternal decree is ordinarily unavailable to the creature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_15:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SNX4","text":"No secondary cause stands outside the order of the first cause such that it can impede or correct predestination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0162","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:SNX5","text":"The system excludes a symmetrical attribution by which God would be cause of fault in the same way as He is cause of grace.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A11","CH_GGVC_P_15:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS1","text":"Universal subjection to providence, the supernatural end, and the insufficiency of nature generate the account of divine conveyance, which is called predestination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A1","CH_GGVC_P_15:A3","CH_GGVC_P_15:A4","CH_GGVC_P_15:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS2","text":"The eternal plan in the agent is distinguished from temporal execution, which proceeds through calling, grace, free act, merit, and glory.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A2","CH_GGVC_P_15:A6","CH_GGVC_P_15:A8","CH_GGVC_P_15:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS3","text":"The will causing good produces the order love → election → predestination, by which antecedent merit as total cause is excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A7","CH_GGVC_P_15:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS4","text":"Particular effects are causally ordered among themselves, but the whole nexus proceeds from divine goodness as first principle and ultimate end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A8","CH_GGVC_P_15:A9","CH_GGVC_P_15:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS5","text":"Providence permits defect; permission of defect generates reprobation, which orders abandonment and punishment, while fault is imputed to free choice.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A11","CH_GGVC_P_15:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS6","text":"The ordering of contingent proximate causes produces at once the infallibility of the order, the freedom of the effect, and the conditional necessity of the end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0123","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS7","text":"Infallible singular election generates a materially determinate number; the account of common diversity is drawn from goodness, singular assignment from simple will.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A14","CH_GGVC_P_15:A15","CH_GGVC_P_15:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS8","text":"Secondary causes included within the order make prayers and works aid execution, although they neither establish nor impede preordination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0156","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0162","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:AF1","text":"The material movement proceeds through the sequence: universal providence → definition of predestination → its location in God → asymmetrical reprobation → love and election → exclusion of antecedent merits → certainty → determinate number → causality of prayers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:AF2","text":"The probative movement repeatedly passes from an objection founded on a univocal sense to a distinction of orders, senses, or causalities, then to the reinsertion of both terms under universal providence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_15:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_15:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_15:NX13","CH_GGVC_P_15:NX15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ME1","text":"Predestination is transferred from the image of fate imposing necessity into the account of providence ordering a supernatural end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A5","CH_GGVC_P_15:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ME2","text":"One apparent object is divided into eternal preordination and temporal execution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A2","CH_GGVC_P_15:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ME3","text":"Freedom and grace cease to be mutually exclusive causal sources and become levels of the same order of first and secondary causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A9","CH_GGVC_P_15:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ME4","text":"Merit is reclassified from an antecedent cause of election into an internal effect of predestination and a particular cause of glory.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A8","CH_GGVC_P_15:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ME5","text":"Reprobation is distinguished from the image of direct causation of fault into permission of defect, abandonment, and ordering of punishment.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A11","CH_GGVC_P_15:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ME6","text":"The opposition between certainty and freedom is dissolved through the distinction of infallible order, contingent effect, and conditional necessity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0123","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ME7","text":"The number of the elect is converted from a known quantity into a part of the universe materially pre-defined through election.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0134","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0137","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ME8","text":"Prayers and works are transferred from causes changing the decree or superfluous acts into internal means of execution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A18","CH_GGVC_P_15:NX15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0154","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0156","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ME9","text":"The human order of election from a pre-existing good is inverted in God, whose love antecedently causes the good of the elect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ALT1","text":"At the surface level are determined the suitability, nature, location, cause, certainty, number, and aidability of predestination, as well as its relation to reprobation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ALT2","text":"At the structural level, providence is divided into the plan of order and governance, while causality is divided into first and secondary.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A2","CH_GGVC_P_15:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ALT3","text":"At the metastrategic level, oppositions between grace and freedom, certainty and contingency, decree and prayer are reconstituted through the distinction of causal levels.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_15:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:ALT4","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, an eternal and immanent act produces temporal, contingent, and free effects through secondary causes without change in itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A6","CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A13","CH_GGVC_P_15:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0123","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to restore the sequence from providence and the supernatural end through election, reprobation, certainty, number, and means of execution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A1","CH_GGVC_P_15:A2","CH_GGVC_P_15:A3","CH_GGVC_P_15:A4","CH_GGVC_P_15:A5","CH_GGVC_P_15:A6","CH_GGVC_P_15:A7","CH_GGVC_P_15:A8","CH_GGVC_P_15:A9","CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A11","CH_GGVC_P_15:A12","CH_GGVC_P_15:A13","CH_GGVC_P_15:A14","CH_GGVC_P_15:A15","CH_GGVC_P_15:A16","CH_GGVC_P_15:A17","CH_GGVC_P_15:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0134","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:MRT2","text":"The firmest structure is the distinction between preordination in God and effect in the creature, because it governs several temporal and causal difficulties at once.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A2","CH_GGVC_P_15:A5","CH_GGVC_P_15:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:MRT3","text":"The concord of certainty and freedom coheres internally only if noncompetitive first and secondary causality and conditional necessity are admitted.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A10","CH_GGVC_P_15:A13","CH_GGVC_P_15:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0123","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:MRT4","text":"The account of singular election is not further determined, because explanatory regress terminates in the simple divine will.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A16","CH_GGVC_P_15:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:MRT5","text":"The junction of reprobation remains fragile between the divine will to permit falling away, the causation of abandonment, and the attribution of fault to free choice alone.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A11","CH_GGVC_P_15:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0058","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0062","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0064","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:MRT6","text":"The distinction of antecedent and consequent will formally safeguards the compatibility of universal love with reprobation, but does not augment the account of singular distribution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_15:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0082","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:MRT7","text":"The transition from cosmological diversity to mercy and punishment provides a common account, but the analogy of the universe does not demonstrate singular identities.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A15","CH_GGVC_P_15:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0103","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0104","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0105","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:MRT8","text":"The assistance of prayers coheres through an ordered equivocation: preordination is aided in no way, but the effect is aided through secondary causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:A18","CH_GGVC_P_15:NX15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0158","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_15:MRT9","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement, because the individual conclusions are reduced to the same structure of providence, distinction of orders, and inclusion of secondary causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS1","CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS2","CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS3","CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS4","CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS5","CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS6","CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS7","CH_GGVC_P_15:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_15:P0155","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_15@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_P_15:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 24","title":"First Part · Question 23","context":"Chapter 24","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 23"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 23","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 24","order":25,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_P_16"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_16@R001","sha256":"aa69455f43b4336e950a80d3eb68558ec29bef3305c1e86f458e21b1a84156d2","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AS1","text":"Quaestio determinat utrum liber vitae sit praedestinatio, quem finem respiciat, et quomodo deletio ei attribui possit sine mutabilitate divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AS2","text":"Nomen libri vitae ex conscriptione humana transfertur ad notitiam divinam firmiter retinentem ordinatos ad vitam aeternam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AS3","text":"Argumentatio distinguit rationem libri vitae a praedestinatione: liber est notitia vel conscriptio praedestinationis, non ipsa praedestinatio formaliter accepta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AS4","text":"Ambitus proprius libri vitae restringitur ad gloriam, quia electio proprie terminatur ad finem supernaturalem, non ad vitam naturalem neque ad gratiam ut medium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AS5","text":"Difficultas deletionis solvitur distinguendo inscriptionem simpliciter ex praedestinatione indefectibili ab inscriptione secundum quid ex gratia amissibili.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AS6","text":"Motus argumentationis procedit ab ambiguitate metaphorica nominis ad distinctionem ordinum divinorum, creatorum et apparentium, ut immutabilitas Dei et mutabilitas creaturae simul serventur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:A1","text":"Liber vitae metaphorice dicitur ex consuetudine qua electi ad officium vel finem in libro conscribuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:A2","text":"Liber vitae proprie est divina notitia qua Deus retinet quos ad vitam aeternam praedestinavit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:A3","text":"Liber vitae a praedestinatione ratione distinguitur, quia notitiam praedestinationis importat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:A4","text":"Electio proprie respicit id quod naturae electi non competit et rationem finis habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:A5","text":"Vita gloriae est finis supernaturalis et ideo proprium obiectum electionis ac libri vitae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:A6","text":"Vita gratiae est ordinatio ad gloriam, non finis ultimus simpliciter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:A7","text":"Ordinatio ad vitam aeternam ex praedestinatione divina numquam deficit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:A8","text":"Ordinatio ad vitam aeternam ex gratia habita potest deficere per peccatum mortale.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:A9","text":"Res divinae immutabiliter cognitae in seipsis mutabiles manere possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:A10","text":"Deletio libri vitae mutationem rei ordinatae significat, non mutationem scientiae divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:NX1","text":"Liber vitae non est liber materialis neque instrumentum quo Deus oblivioni vel defectui memoriae subveniat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:NX2","text":"Liber vitae non significat univoce Scripturam, memoriam iudicii, conscriptionem electorum et artem ducendi ad vitam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:NX3","text":"Liber vitae proprie non respicit vitam divinam, quia haec Deo naturalis est et electionem non requirit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:NX4","text":"Liber vitae proprie non respicit vitam naturae, quia haec naturae convenit nec terminus electionis est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:NX5","text":"Liber vitae non respicit gratiam secundum se, nisi quatenus gratia ad gloriam ordinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:NX6","text":"Reprobatio non producit librum mortis correspondentem, quia conscriptio electionem, non repudium, significat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:NX7","text":"Deletio non est divina praescientia prius habita et postea amissa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:NX8","text":"Explicatio deletionis per solam opinionem humanam non sufficit ad rationem praemii promissi iustis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:D1","text":"Nomen libri vitae habet usus subordinatos: potest designare electos, media ad vitam ordinantia, vel facta ad iudicium revocanda.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A1","CH_GGVC_P_16:A2","CH_GGVC_P_16:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:D2","text":"Soli praedestinati sunt scripti simpliciter, quia soli vitam aeternam habituri ordinantur in ipsa re finali.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A2","CH_GGVC_P_16:A5","CH_GGVC_P_16:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:D3","text":"Habentes gratiam qui a gloria deficiunt scripti sunt secundum quid, quia ordinatio eorum ad vitam aeternam consistit in causa mutabili, non in fine possessuro.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A6","CH_GGVC_P_16:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:D4","text":"Deletio realis attribui potest scriptis secundum quid, quia gratia quae eos ad gloriam ordinabat amitti potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A6","CH_GGVC_P_16:A8","CH_GGVC_P_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:D5","text":"Deletio praedestinatis simpliciter attribui non potest, quia eorum ordinatio divina ad finem numquam deficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A7","CH_GGVC_P_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:D6","text":"Eodem sensu quo aliquis per gratiam de novo ordinatur ad vitam aeternam, dici potest de novo scribi, licet divina cognitio non de novo accrescat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A8","CH_GGVC_P_16:A9","CH_GGVC_P_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:D7","text":"Immutabilitas libri vitae servatur non per negationem mutationis creaturae, sed per relocationem mutationis ad rem divinitus cognitam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A9","CH_GGVC_P_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:SD1","text":"Systema duplicem rationem salutis constituit: una est finalis et indefectibilis ex praedestinatione, altera est dispositiva et defectibilis ex gratia.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A5","CH_GGVC_P_16:A6","CH_GGVC_P_16:A7","CH_GGVC_P_16:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:SD2","text":"Contingentia moralis creaturae non limitat scientiam divinam, quia contingentia in re cognita ponitur, non in cognitione cognoscentis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A9","CH_GGVC_P_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:SD3","text":"Praemium perseverantiae intelligitur ut manifestatio ordinis simpliciter stabilis, non ut mera confirmatio opinionis humanae de iustitia praesentis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A7","CH_GGVC_P_16:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:SD4","text":"Asymmetria inter librum vitae et librum mortis ordinem salutis describit per electionem positivam, non per binariam catalogorum oppositionem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A1","CH_GGVC_P_16:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:DNX1","text":"Systema falsum esse requirit quod omnis ordinatio ad vitam aeternam sit eiusdem firmitatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:DNX2","text":"Systema falsum esse requirit quod gratia praesens per se identica sit cum gloria futura.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:DNX3","text":"Systema falsum esse requirit quod mutatio status creati mutationem internam in Deo inferat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:DNX4","text":"Systema falsum esse requirit quod electio divina ad quidquid boni vel vivi pertinet sine distinctione naturae, medii et finis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:DNX5","text":"Systema falsum esse requirit quod reprobatio sit positiva conscriptio eodem modo quo praedestinatio est electio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:SNX1","text":"Argumentatio excludit imaginem Dei cuius cognitio temporali successione rerum informatur aut corrigitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:SNX2","text":"Argumentatio excludit salutem cuius veritas ex sola conditione morali praesenti concludi possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:SNX3","text":"Argumentatio excludit symmetriam ultimam inter electionem vitae et repudium mortis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:FGS1","text":"Consuetudo humana conscriptionis → translatio metaphorica ad intellectum divinum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:FGS2","text":"Electio ad finem supra naturam → vita gloriae ut terminus proprius → liber vitae ut notitia electorum ad gloriam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:FGS3","text":"Distinctio finis et medii → gloria ut obiectum proprium → gratia ut ordinatio subordinata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:FGS4","text":"Distinctio praedestinationis et gratiae → inscriptio simpliciter indefectibilis / inscriptio secundum quid defectibilis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:FGS5","text":"Immutabilis scientia divina + mutabilis res creata → deletio attribuitur ordini creato, non cognitioni divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:FGS6","text":"Multiplicitas usus vocabuli libri vitae → subordinatio sensuum impropriorum ad definitionem propriam praedestinationis cognitae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AF1","text":"Ambiguitas nominis libri vitae → distinctio metaphorae et rei significatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AF2","text":"Conscriptio electorum → notitia divina praedestinationis → liber vitae proprie dictus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AF3","text":"Electio ad finem supernaturale → gloria → exclusio vitae divinae, naturalis et gratiae separatim sumptae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AF4","text":"Textus de deletione → distinctio opinionis humanae et rei → insufficientia explicationis mere apparentialis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AF5","text":"Duplex ordinatio ad vitam aeternam → duplex modus inscriptionis → deletio conditionalis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AF6","text":"Immutabilitas Dei + mutabilitas creaturae → deletio rei scitae sine mutatione scientiae divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:ME1","text":"Liber vitae reclassificatur a obiecto scripturali vel memoriali ad signum intellectuale electionis divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:ME2","text":"Oppositio simplex inter scriptum et non scriptum resolvitur per distinctionem inter inscriptionem simpliciter et inscriptionem secundum quid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:ME3","text":"Oppositio inter immutabilem Deum et mutabilem salutis statum dissolvitur per distinctionem inter modum cognitionis et modum rei cognitae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:ME4","text":"Gratia reclassificatur non ut finis electionis, sed ut causa et via contingens ad gloriam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:ME5","text":"Reprobatio desinit intelligi ut terminus catalogicus symmetricus electioni et intelligitur per absentiam conscriptionis electivae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:ALT1","text":"Surface — Liber vitae definitur ut conscriptio vel notitia eorum qui ad vitam aeternam ordinantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:ALT2","text":"Structural — Ordo salutis dividitur inter praedestinationem indefectibilem, gratiam amissibilem et gloriam finalem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Contradictio apparentis deletionis et divinae immutabilitatis solvitur transferendo mutationem a cognoscente ad cognitum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Deus res mutabiles immutabiliter cognoscit; mutatio temporalis rerum non constituit successionem vel innovationem in scientia divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad restituendam definitionem propriam libri vitae, restrictionem eius ad gloriam, et rationem deletionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:MRT2","text":"Distinctio inter praedestinationem et librum vitae est firma, quia diversitatem formalem inter ordinem et notitiam ordinis servat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:MRT3","text":"Distinctio inter inscriptionem simpliciter et secundum quid est commissura necessaria, quia sola deletio realis cum praedestinationis indefectibilitate conciliatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:MRT4","text":"Tensio manet quia liber vitae primum definitur per notitiam praedestinationis, deinde etiam de iis dicitur qui ex sola gratia ordinantur nec finaliter gloriam consequuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:MRT5","text":"Tensio ulterius manet quia nomen inscriptionis eodem vocabulo usurpatur pro ordine finali certo et pro dispositione causali amissibili.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:MRT6","text":"Systema interne cohaeret si analogia inscriptionis et distinctio simpliciter-secundum quid retinentur; sine eis immutabilitas divina et deletio simul asseri non possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:MRT7","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat, quia ab interpretatione metaphorae per determinationem finis ad solutionem quaestionis de mutatione procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T160453650762Z_5a5b17e6","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 24","source_sha256":"aa69455f43b4336e950a80d3eb68558ec29bef3305c1e86f458e21b1a84156d2","sha256":"4160a7f0618897117ba3ae0e4024c2fb7add3afa093a3de94726d29ba33af648","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T16:04:53.652667+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AS1","text":"The question determines whether the book of life is predestination, to what end it is directed, and how deletion can be attributed to it without divine mutability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AS2","text":"The name book of life is transferred from human registration to divine knowledge firmly retaining those ordered to eternal life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AS3","text":"The argument distinguishes the account of the book of life from predestination: the book is knowledge or registration of predestination, not predestination itself formally considered.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AS4","text":"The proper scope of the book of life is restricted to glory, because election properly terminates in the supernatural end, not in natural life nor in grace as a means.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AS5","text":"The difficulty of deletion is resolved by distinguishing inscription simply speaking from indefectible predestination and inscription in a qualified respect from grace that can be lost.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AS6","text":"The movement of the argument proceeds from the metaphorical ambiguity of the name to the distinction of divine, created, and apparent orders, so that the immutability of God and the mutability of the creature may both be preserved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:A1","text":"The book of life is spoken of metaphorically from the custom whereby those chosen for an office or an end are registered in a book.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:A2","text":"The book of life properly is the divine knowledge by which God retains those whom He has predestined to eternal life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:A3","text":"The book of life is distinguished from predestination in account, because it imports knowledge of predestination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:A4","text":"Election properly concerns that which does not belong to the nature of the one elected and has the character of an end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:A5","text":"The life of glory is a supernatural end and therefore the proper object of election and of the book of life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:A6","text":"The life of grace is an ordering to glory, not the ultimate end simply speaking.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:A7","text":"The ordering to eternal life from divine predestination never fails.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:A8","text":"The ordering to eternal life from grace possessed can fail through mortal sin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:A9","text":"Divine things immutably known can remain mutable in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:A10","text":"Deletion from the book of life signifies a change in the thing ordered, not a change in divine knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:NX1","text":"The book of life is not a material book, nor an instrument by which God remedies forgetfulness or a defect of memory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:NX2","text":"The book of life does not signify univocally Scripture, memory of judgment, registration of the elect, and the art of leading to life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:NX3","text":"The book of life properly does not concern divine life, because this is natural to God and requires no election.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:NX4","text":"The book of life properly does not concern the life of nature, because this belongs to nature and is not the terminus of election.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:NX5","text":"The book of life does not concern grace in itself, except insofar as grace is ordered to glory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:NX6","text":"Reprobation does not produce a corresponding book of death, because registration signifies election, not rejection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:NX7","text":"Deletion is not divine foreknowledge first possessed and afterwards lost.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:NX8","text":"An explanation of deletion by human opinion alone does not suffice for the account of the reward promised to the just.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:D1","text":"The name book of life has subordinate uses: it can designate the elect, the means ordering to life, or deeds to be recalled for judgment.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A1","CH_GGVC_P_16:A2","CH_GGVC_P_16:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:D2","text":"Only the predestined are written simply speaking, because only they are ordered to eternal life in the final reality itself which they will possess.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A2","CH_GGVC_P_16:A5","CH_GGVC_P_16:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:D3","text":"Those possessing grace who fall short of glory are written in a qualified respect, because their ordering to eternal life consists in a mutable cause, not in the end they will possess.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A6","CH_GGVC_P_16:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:D4","text":"Real deletion can be attributed to those written in a qualified respect, because the grace that ordered them to glory can be lost.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A6","CH_GGVC_P_16:A8","CH_GGVC_P_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:D5","text":"Deletion cannot be attributed to the predestined simply speaking, because their divine ordering to the end never fails.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A7","CH_GGVC_P_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:D6","text":"In the same sense in which someone is newly ordered to eternal life through grace, he can be said to be newly written, although divine knowledge does not newly increase.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A8","CH_GGVC_P_16:A9","CH_GGVC_P_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:D7","text":"The immutability of the book of life is preserved not by denying the change of the creature, but by relocating the change to the thing divinely known.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A9","CH_GGVC_P_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:SD1","text":"The system establishes a twofold account of salvation: one is final and indefectible from predestination; the other is dispositive and defectible from grace.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A5","CH_GGVC_P_16:A6","CH_GGVC_P_16:A7","CH_GGVC_P_16:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:SD2","text":"The moral contingency of the creature does not limit divine knowledge, because contingency is posited in the thing known, not in the knowledge of the knower.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A9","CH_GGVC_P_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:SD3","text":"The reward of perseverance is understood as the manifestation of an order stable simply speaking, not as a mere confirmation of human opinion concerning present justice.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A7","CH_GGVC_P_16:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:SD4","text":"The asymmetry between the book of life and the book of death describes the order of salvation through positive election, not through a binary opposition of catalogues.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_16:A1","CH_GGVC_P_16:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:DNX1","text":"The system requires it to be false that every ordering to eternal life has the same firmness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:DNX2","text":"The system requires it to be false that present grace is of itself identical with future glory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:DNX3","text":"The system requires it to be false that a change of created state entails an internal change in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:DNX4","text":"The system requires it to be false that divine election pertains to whatever is good or living without distinction of nature, means, and end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:DNX5","text":"The system requires it to be false that reprobation is a positive registration in the same way that predestination is election.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:SNX1","text":"The argument excludes an image of God whose knowledge is informed or corrected by the temporal succession of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:SNX2","text":"The argument excludes a salvation whose truth can be inferred from present moral condition alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:SNX3","text":"The argument excludes an ultimate symmetry between election to life and rejection to death.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:FGS1","text":"Human custom of registration → metaphorical transfer to the divine intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:FGS2","text":"Election to an end above nature → life of glory as the proper terminus → book of life as knowledge of those elected to glory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:FGS3","text":"Distinction of end and means → glory as proper object → grace as subordinate ordering.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:FGS4","text":"Distinction of predestination and grace → indefectible inscription simply speaking / defectible inscription in a qualified respect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:FGS5","text":"Immutable divine knowledge + mutable created thing → deletion is attributed to the created order, not to divine knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:FGS6","text":"Multiplicity of uses of the term book of life → subordination of improper senses to the proper definition of known predestination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AF1","text":"Ambiguity of the name book of life → distinction of metaphor and the thing signified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AF2","text":"Registration of the elect → divine knowledge of predestination → book of life properly so called.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AF3","text":"Election to a supernatural end → glory → exclusion of divine life, natural life, and grace separately taken.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AF4","text":"Texts concerning deletion → distinction of human opinion and reality → insufficiency of a merely apparent explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AF5","text":"Twofold ordering to eternal life → twofold mode of inscription → conditional deletion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:AF6","text":"Immutability of God + mutability of the creature → deletion of the thing known without change in divine knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:ME1","text":"The book of life is reclassified from a scriptural or memorial object to an intellectual sign of divine election.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:ME2","text":"The simple opposition between written and unwritten is resolved through the distinction between inscription simply speaking and inscription in a qualified respect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:ME3","text":"The opposition between immutable God and mutable state of salvation is dissolved through the distinction between the mode of knowing and the mode of the thing known.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:ME4","text":"Grace is reclassified not as the end of election, but as a contingent cause and way to glory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:ME5","text":"Reprobation ceases to be understood as a terminus catalogically symmetrical to election and is understood through the absence of elective registration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:ALT1","text":"Surface — The book of life is defined as the registration or knowledge of those ordered to eternal life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:ALT2","text":"Structural — The order of salvation is divided between indefectible predestination, grace that can be lost, and final glory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The contradiction between apparent deletion and divine immutability is resolved by transferring change from the knower to the known.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — God knows mutable things immutably; the temporal change of things does not constitute succession or innovation in divine knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the proper definition of the book of life, its restriction to glory, and the account of deletion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:MRT2","text":"The distinction between predestination and the book of life is firm, because it preserves the formal diversity between the ordering and knowledge of the ordering.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:MRT3","text":"The distinction between inscription simply speaking and inscription in a qualified respect is a necessary joint, because real deletion alone is reconciled with the indefectibility of predestination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:MRT4","text":"A tension remains because the book of life is first defined through knowledge of predestination, then is also said of those who are ordered from grace alone and do not finally attain glory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:MRT5","text":"A further tension remains because the name inscription is used in the same term for a certain final order and for a causal disposition that can be lost.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0040","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:MRT6","text":"The system coheres internally if the analogy of inscription and the distinction simply speaking—in a qualified respect are retained; without them, divine immutability and deletion cannot both be asserted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_16:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text, because it proceeds from interpretation of the metaphor through determination of the end to resolution of the question concerning change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_16:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_16@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_P_16:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 25","title":"First Part · Question 24","context":"Chapter 25","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 24"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 24","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 25","order":26,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_P_17"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_17@R001","sha256":"6415b81748eccb141196fe418756b4c43ddbd829809905eaff27fc120e888415","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:AS1","text":"Quaestio determinat utrum potentia Deo conveniat, quousque extendatur et quibus limitibus apparentibus non minuatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:AS2","text":"Potentia divina, quia in actu puro et esse infinito fundatur, est activa, infinita et omnia absolute possibilia complectitur, sed contradictionem, defectum et abolitionem praeteriti pro obiectis non habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:AS3","text":"Argumentatio formam metaphysicam, reconstructivam et dialecticam habet: difficultates per distinctiones actus et potentiae, possibilitatis absolutae et relativae, atque potentiae absolutae et ordinatae dissolvuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:AS4","text":"Ambitus potentiae ex modo essendi agentis, non ex amplitudine effectuum actualium, mensuratur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:AS5","text":"Motus procedit ab existentia potentiae activae ad infinitatem, ab infinitate ad omnipotentiam, deinde ad probationes eius per contradictionem, tempus, contingentiam ordinis actualis et comparationem bonitatis effectuum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A1","text":"Deus est actus purus, simpliciter et universaliter perfectus, sine imperfectione.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A2","text":"Potentia activa fundatur in actu et perfectione atque rationem principii effectus habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A3","text":"Potentia passiva defectum, potentialitatem et receptionem ab alio praesupponit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A4","text":"Potentia, essentia, actio, scientia, voluntas, sapientia et iustitia in Deo secundum rem idem sunt, sed secundum rationem diversa munera principii significant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A5","text":"Potentia activa tanto maior est quanto perfectior et actualior est forma qua agens agit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A6","text":"Esse et essentia divina sunt infinita quia nullo recipiente limitantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A7","text":"Deus est agens non univocum, ideoque nullus effectus creatus potentiam eius adaequat aut totaliter manifestat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A8","text":"Potentia divina non ordinatur ad effectum sicut ad finem, sed ipsa est finis effectus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A9","text":"Omnipotentia est potentia respectu omnium possibilium absolute.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","text":"Possibile absolute est id cuius termini non repugnant et quod rationem entis habere potest; contradictorium rationem possibilis et factibilis non habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A11","text":"Deus agit per voluntatem, non ex necessitate naturae, neque voluntas eius necessario ad res et ordinem actuales determinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A12","text":"Ordo rebus inditus sapientiam divinam non adaequat, quia bonitas divina res creatas improportionabiliter excedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A13","text":"Potentia absoluta significat posse secundum naturam divinam; potentia ordinata significat posse quatenus imperium voluntatis iustae exsequitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A14","text":"Negatio facti praeteriti coniungit eius fuisse et non fuisse, ideoque contradictionem implicat, quamvis effectus vel corruptiones ex facto ortae removeri possint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A15","text":"Necessitas, contingentia et impossibilitas relativa effectibus secundum dispositionem causarum proximarum conveniunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A16","text":"Bonitas essentialis rem in sua specie constituit, sed bonitas accidentalis salva essentia augeri potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A17","text":"Modus divini agendi sapientior aut melior fieri non potest, sed effectus meliorem modum essendi recipere potest et qualibet re creata alia melior fieri potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A18","text":"Universum his rebus suppositis ordinem meliorem non recipit, sed aliis rebus factis vel additis aliud universum melius constitui potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX1","text":"Potentia passiva nullo modo Deo convenit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX2","text":"Potentia activa non opponitur actui neque imperfectionem potentialitatis importat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX3","text":"Infinitas divina non est infinitas materiae indeterminatae vel quantitatis imperfectae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX4","text":"Potentia infinita neque effectum infinitum producere neque se tota in effectu manifestare debet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX5","text":"Omnipotentia neque per possibilia naturae creatae neque circulariter per ea quae Deus potest definitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX6","text":"Contradictoria sub omnipotentia non continentur, quia non sunt possibilia factibilia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX7","text":"Non posse moveri, pati aut peccare defectum omnipotentiae non constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX8","text":"Omnipotentia necessitatem et impossibilitatem relativam rebus creatis non aufert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX9","text":"Deus efficere non potest ut factum praeteritum factum non fuerit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX10","text":"Cursus rerum actualis ex necessitate naturae divinae non emanat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX11","text":"Sapientia et iustitia divina ad solum ordinem actualem non limitantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX12","text":"Quod Deus non facit, non ideo extra potentiam eius est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX13","text":"Melior effectus non requirit meliorem modum agendi in Deo, neque eadem res essentialiter melior manet eadem specie.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX14","text":"Iustitia divina debitum creaturae a Deo independens non imponit, quia Deus nulli nisi sibi debet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0102","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:D1","text":"Deo maxime convenit potentia activa, dum omnis potentia passiva ab eo excluditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A1","CH_GGVC_P_17:A2","CH_GGVC_P_17:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:D2","text":"Potentia divina est infinita quia actus et essentia quibus Deus agit sunt infinita.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A5","CH_GGVC_P_17:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:D3","text":"Effectus finitus neque potentiam infinitam exhaurit neque eam frustraneam reddit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A7","CH_GGVC_P_17:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:D4","text":"Incapacitas patiendi, movendi passive vel peccandi perfectionem potentiae significat, quia talia defectum actionis vel receptionem ab alio important.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A1","CH_GGVC_P_17:A3","CH_GGVC_P_17:A9","CH_GGVC_P_17:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:D5","text":"Quod naturae creatae impossibile est potest absolute possibile esse, dum necessitas vel impossibilitas secundum causam proximam manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A9","CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:D6","text":"Praeteritum non potest infectum fieri, non ob diminutionem potentiae, sed quia negatio veritatis factae obiectum possibile non constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:D7","text":"Deus alias res et alium ordinem facere potest, quia voluntas eius ad actualem cursum non necessitatur et ordo actualis sapientiam eius non exhaurit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A11","CH_GGVC_P_17:A12","CH_GGVC_P_17:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:D8","text":"Omne quod Deus actu facit praescientiae, voluntati et iustae ordinationi subiacet, quamvis posse eius decretum actuale excedat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A4","CH_GGVC_P_17:A11","CH_GGVC_P_17:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:D9","text":"Eadem res accidentaliter melior fieri potest, sed essentialiter melior facta iam alia res secundum speciem esset.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A16","CH_GGVC_P_17:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:D10","text":"Perfectio ordinis actualis sub rebus datis compatitur possibilitatem melioris universitatis per mutationem vel additionem terminorum ordinis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A12","CH_GGVC_P_17:A17","CH_GGVC_P_17:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SD1","text":"Omnipotentia non est capacitas indeterminata ad quidlibet enuntiabile, sed plenitudo causalitatis respectu omnis rei cui esse non repugnat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A1","CH_GGVC_P_17:A2","CH_GGVC_P_17:A9","CH_GGVC_P_17:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SD2","text":"Mundus actualis est contingens respectu naturae divinae, sed conditionate ordinatus respectu voluntatis et decreti quibus actu producitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A11","CH_GGVC_P_17:A12","CH_GGVC_P_17:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SD3","text":"Nulla creatura nec universitas creata potentiam, sapientiam aut bonitatem divinam exhaurit; possibilitas divina actualitatem creatam semper excedit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A6","CH_GGVC_P_17:A7","CH_GGVC_P_17:A12","CH_GGVC_P_17:A17","CH_GGVC_P_17:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SD4","text":"Irrevocabilitas praeteriti non est necessitas extrinseca Deo imposita, sed consequentia identitatis veritatis factae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SD5","text":"Distinctio potentiae absolutae et ordinatae possibilis est tantum quia identitas realis attributorum divinorum distinctionem rationum principii non tollit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A4","CH_GGVC_P_17:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SD6","text":"Optimalitas localis ordinis et meliorabilitas globalis non contradicunt, quia prima terminis actualibus, altera mutationi terminorum et ordinis respondet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A16","CH_GGVC_P_17:A17","CH_GGVC_P_17:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX1","text":"Actualitas rerum creatarum mensura possibilitatis divinae esse non potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A6","CH_GGVC_P_17:A7","CH_GGVC_P_17:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX2","text":"Effectus creatus testimonium adaequatum infinitatis potentiae praebere non potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A7","CH_GGVC_P_17:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX3","text":"Contradictio non est res difficillima neque obiectum resistens, sed absentia rationis entis, possibilis et intelligibilis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX4","text":"Capacitas deficiendi, peccandi vel patiendi inter perfectiones potentiae numerari non potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A1","CH_GGVC_P_17:A3","CH_GGVC_P_17:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX5","text":"Identitas realis potentiae, voluntatis et sapientiae identitatem ambitus intentionalis secundum rationem non requirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A4","CH_GGVC_P_17:A13","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX6","text":"Immutabilitas potentiae divinae non requirit ut res eandem rationem possibilis ante et post eventum retineat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A14","CH_GGVC_P_17:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX7","text":"Bonitas partium independenter ab ordine totius augeri non potest quin relatio proportionis mutetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A16","CH_GGVC_P_17:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX8","text":"Iustitia divina potentiam non coercet tamquam norma exterior, sed eandem essentiam sub alia ratione exprimit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A4","CH_GGVC_P_17:NX14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SNX1","text":"Ultimus limes omnipotentiae est absentia obiecti possibilis, non praesentia rei potentiae divinae resistentis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A9","CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SNX2","text":"Nulla creatio actualis clausuram modalem constituit qua divina fecunditas ad unum cursum rerum redigatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A11","CH_GGVC_P_17:A12","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SNX3","text":"Nec contradictio nec iustitia quasi principium Deo exterius operantur: contradictio obiectum non est, iustitia autem cum essentia divina secundum rem coincidit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A4","CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SNX4","text":"Nullus effectus creatus est finis cui potentia divina perficienda subordinetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A7","CH_GGVC_P_17:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SNX5","text":"Historia facta non manet materia modaliter retractabilis, quia veritas praeteriti possibilitatem contrariae confectionis claudit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A14","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SNX6","text":"Nulla scala univoca Deum et creaturam, potentiam et effectum, vel modum agentis et perfectionem facti uno genere mensurat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A7","CH_GGVC_P_17:A17","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:FGS1","text":"Actus purus et perfectio universalis → principium activum maximum → potentia activa sine potentia passiva.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A1","CH_GGVC_P_17:A2","CH_GGVC_P_17:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:FGS2","text":"Esse infinitum → forma agendi illimitata → potentia activa infinita.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A5","CH_GGVC_P_17:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:FGS3","text":"Esse infinitum praehabens perfectionem entis → omne non contradictorium ut obiectum → omnipotentia.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A6","CH_GGVC_P_17:A9","CH_GGVC_P_17:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:FGS4","text":"Distinctio possibilitatis absolutae et relativae → subordinatio impossibilium naturalium potentiae divinae → conservatio necessitatum creatarum → exclusio contradictionis et abolitionis praeteriti.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:A14","CH_GGVC_P_17:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:FGS5","text":"Identitas realis attributorum et distinctio rationum → potentia absoluta et ordinata → actualitas necessario praescita, sed possibilitas actualitate non exhausta.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A4","CH_GGVC_P_17:A11","CH_GGVC_P_17:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:FGS6","text":"Distinctio bonitatis essentialis et accidentalis → immobilitas identitatis specificae → meliorabilitas modi creati.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A16","CH_GGVC_P_17:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:FGS7","text":"Ordo optimus sub terminis datis + mutabilitas terminorum → possibilitas alterius universitatis melioris → inexhaustibilitas potentiae creantis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A12","CH_GGVC_P_17:A18","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:AF1","text":"Exsistentia potentiae activae → infinitas potentiae → definitio omnipotentiae → contradictio ut limes obiecti → irrevocabilitas praeteriti → excessus possibilitatis supra actualitatem → meliorabilitas effectuum sub distinctionibus boni.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:D1","CH_GGVC_P_17:D2","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_17:D6","CH_GGVC_P_17:D7","CH_GGVC_P_17:D9","CH_GGVC_P_17:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:AF2","text":"Obiectio ex defectu apparente → distinctio rationum → remotio falsi obiecti vel falsae mensurae → restitutio perfectionis divinae → determinatio consequentiae modalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_17:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_17:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_17:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_17:NX12","CH_GGVC_P_17:NX13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ME1","text":"Potentia a potentialitate passiva separatur et tamquam expressio actualitatis perfectae denuo classificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A2","CH_GGVC_P_17:A3","CH_GGVC_P_17:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ME2","text":"Impossibile dividitur in impossibile absolute per contradictionem et impossibile relative secundum potentiam vel causam determinatam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:A15","CH_GGVC_P_17:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ME3","text":"Omnipotentia ab ambitu omnium formularum ad ambitum omnis rationis entis transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A9","CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ME4","text":"Irrevocabilitas praeteriti a limite causali ad structuram veritatis et contradictionis transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A14","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ME5","text":"Ordo actualis ex unico ordine necessario in unam ordinationem voluntarie institutam inter plures ordines possibiles convertitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A11","CH_GGVC_P_17:A12","CH_GGVC_P_17:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ME6","text":"Notio melioris secundum identitatem rei, accidentia, modum agentis et ordinem totius distribuitur, unde oppositio inter perfectionem actualem et meliorem possibilitatem dissolvitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A16","CH_GGVC_P_17:A17","CH_GGVC_P_17:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ME7","text":"Simplicitas divina compositionem realem attributorum excludit, sed distinctiones functionales necessarias ad structuram causalitatis et modalitatis servat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A4","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ALT1","text":"Gradus superficiei sex quaestiones tractat: existentiam, infinitatem, omnipotentiam, praeteriti irrevocabilitatem, possibilia non actualia et meliorabilitatem factorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ALT2","text":"Gradus structuralis potentiam ex actu, essentia, voluntate, sapientia, causalitate et ordine rerum constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A1","CH_GGVC_P_17:A4","CH_GGVC_P_17:A5","CH_GGVC_P_17:A11","CH_GGVC_P_17:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ALT3","text":"Gradus metastrategicus quaestiones de eo quod Deus non potest in quaestiones de defectu obiecti, defectu agentis vel falsitate mensurae convertit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:D4","CH_GGVC_P_17:D6","CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_17:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ALT4","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis possibilitatem secundum rationem entis, causalitatem secundum modum essendi et irrevocabilitatem secundum veritatem facti ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A6","CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:A14","CH_GGVC_P_17:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad seriem principalem ab actu puro ad infinitatem, omnipotentiam, limites contradictionis et excessum potentiae supra ordinem actualem restituendam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A1","CH_GGVC_P_17:A5","CH_GGVC_P_17:A6","CH_GGVC_P_17:A9","CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:A11","CH_GGVC_P_17:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:MRT2","text":"Restitutio conditionata manet demonstrationibus antecedentibus de actu puro, simplicitate, infinitate essentiae et causalitate voluntatis, quas quaestio praesupponit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:MRT3","text":"Structura interne cohaeret quatenus contradictiones non tamquam opera difficilia sed tamquam non-obiecta potentiae tractantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_17:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:MRT4","text":"Firmissimae commissurae sunt distinctiones potentiae activae et passivae, possibilitatis absolutae et relativae, atque bonitatis essentialis et accidentalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A2","CH_GGVC_P_17:A3","CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:A15","CH_GGVC_P_17:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:MRT5","text":"Commissura fragilis manet inter identitatem realem potentiae, voluntatis et sapientiae atque diversitatem modalem potentiae absolutae et ordinatae; textus eam distinctione rationis continet, non ulterius demonstrat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A4","CH_GGVC_P_17:A13","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:MRT6","text":"Infinitas potentiae ex infinitate essentiae, non ex effectibus, probatur; ideo argumentum ab antecedenti doctrina de essentia divina essentialiter dependet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A5","CH_GGVC_P_17:A6","CH_GGVC_P_17:A7","CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:MRT7","text":"Comparatio inter universitates alternativas minus determinata manet, quia mensura qua alius ordo simpliciter melior dicatur non plene formalizatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A17","CH_GGVC_P_17:A18","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:MRT8","text":"Restitutio verum motum textus servat: potentiam constituit, ambitum eius auget, falsa eius limitationes removet, deinde actualitatem et bonitatem creatam intra illum ambitum reordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:AF1","CH_GGVC_P_17:FGS1","CH_GGVC_P_17:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T160522974720Z_1ecd6ef6","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 25","source_sha256":"6415b81748eccb141196fe418756b4c43ddbd829809905eaff27fc120e888415","sha256":"6a451d5022f35cb029dcd6804b86a650c6891b973c74227d8ce05151f1c29af5","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T16:05:22.975901+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:AS1","text":"The question determines whether power belongs to God, how far it extends, and by what apparent limits it is not diminished.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:AS2","text":"Divine power, because it is founded in pure act and infinite being, is active, infinite, and encompasses all things absolutely possible, but does not have contradiction, defect, and the abolition of the past as objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:AS3","text":"The argumentation has a metaphysical, reconstructive, and dialectical form: difficulties are resolved through distinctions between act and potency, absolute and relative possibility, and absolute and ordained power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:AS4","text":"The scope of power is measured from the mode of being of the agent, not from the extent of its actual effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:AS5","text":"The movement proceeds from the existence of active power to infinity, from infinity to omnipotence, then to proofs thereof through contradiction, time, the contingency of the actual order, and comparison of the goodness of effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A1","text":"God is pure act, simply and universally perfect, without imperfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A2","text":"Active power is founded in act and perfection and has the character of a principle of an effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A3","text":"Passive power presupposes defect, potentiality, and reception from another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A4","text":"Power, essence, action, knowledge, will, wisdom, and justice in God are the same in reality, but according to reason signify diverse offices of principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A5","text":"Active power is greater insofar as the form by which the agent acts is more perfect and more actual.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A6","text":"Divine being and essence are infinite because they are limited by no recipient.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A7","text":"God is a non-univocal agent, and therefore no created effect equals or totally manifests His power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A8","text":"Divine power is not ordered to an effect as to an end, but is itself the end of the effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A9","text":"Omnipotence is power with respect to all things absolutely possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","text":"Absolutely possible is that whose terms are not repugnant and which can have the character of being; the contradictory has neither the character of the possible nor of the feasible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A11","text":"God acts through will, not from necessity of nature, nor is His will necessarily determined to actual things and order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A12","text":"The order imparted to things does not equal divine wisdom, because divine goodness exceeds created things disproportionately.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A13","text":"Absolute power signifies the ability according to the divine nature; ordained power signifies the ability insofar as it executes the command of a just will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A14","text":"The negation of a past fact conjoins its having been and not having been, and therefore implies contradiction, although effects or corruptions arising from the fact can be removed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A15","text":"Relative necessity, contingency, and impossibility belong to effects according to the disposition of proximate causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A16","text":"Essential goodness constitutes a thing in its species, but accidental goodness can be increased while the essence is preserved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A17","text":"The mode of divine acting cannot become wiser or better, but an effect can receive a better mode of being, and any created thing can become better than another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:A18","text":"The universe, given these things, does not admit a better order, but with other things made or added, another better universe can be constituted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX1","text":"Passive power in no way belongs to God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX2","text":"Active power is not opposed to act, nor does it import the imperfection of potentiality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX3","text":"Divine infinity is not the infinity of indeterminate matter or imperfect quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX4","text":"Infinite power need neither produce an infinite effect nor manifest itself wholly in an effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX5","text":"Omnipotence is defined neither through the possibilities of created nature nor circularly through those things that God can do.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX6","text":"Contradictories are not contained under omnipotence, because they are not feasible possibilities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX7","text":"Being unable to be moved, to suffer, or to sin does not constitute a defect of omnipotence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX8","text":"Omnipotence does not remove relative necessity and impossibility from created things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX9","text":"God cannot bring it about that a past fact has not occurred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX10","text":"The actual course of things does not emanate from necessity of the divine nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX11","text":"Divine wisdom and justice are not limited to the actual order alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX12","text":"What God does not do is not thereby outside His power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX13","text":"A better effect does not require a better mode of acting in God, nor does the same thing, made essentially better, remain the same in species.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:NX14","text":"Divine justice does not impose a debt to a creature independent of God, because God owes nothing to anyone except Himself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0102","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:D1","text":"Active power belongs most fittingly to God, while all passive power is excluded from Him.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A1","CH_GGVC_P_17:A2","CH_GGVC_P_17:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:D2","text":"Divine power is infinite because the act and essence by which God acts are infinite.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A5","CH_GGVC_P_17:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:D3","text":"A finite effect neither exhausts infinite power nor renders it superfluous.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A7","CH_GGVC_P_17:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:D4","text":"The incapacity for suffering, passive motion, or sin signifies perfection of power, because such things imply a defect of action or reception from another.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A1","CH_GGVC_P_17:A3","CH_GGVC_P_17:A9","CH_GGVC_P_17:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:D5","text":"What is impossible to created nature can be absolutely possible, while necessity or impossibility according to the proximate cause remains.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A9","CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:D6","text":"The past cannot be made undone, not because of a diminution of power, but because the negation of an accomplished truth does not constitute a possible object.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:D7","text":"God can make other things and another order, because His will is not necessitated to the actual course and the actual order does not exhaust His wisdom.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A11","CH_GGVC_P_17:A12","CH_GGVC_P_17:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:D8","text":"Everything that God actually does is subject to foreknowledge, will, and just ordination, although His power exceeds the actual decree.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A4","CH_GGVC_P_17:A11","CH_GGVC_P_17:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:D9","text":"The same thing can become accidentally better, but if made essentially better it would already be another thing according to species.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A16","CH_GGVC_P_17:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:D10","text":"The perfection of the actual order under given things is compatible with the possibility of a better universe through the alteration or addition of the terms of the order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A12","CH_GGVC_P_17:A17","CH_GGVC_P_17:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SD1","text":"Omnipotence is not an indeterminate capacity for whatever can be stated, but the fullness of causality with respect to every thing to which being is not repugnant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A1","CH_GGVC_P_17:A2","CH_GGVC_P_17:A9","CH_GGVC_P_17:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SD2","text":"The actual world is contingent with respect to the divine nature, but conditionally ordered with respect to the will and decree by which it is actually produced.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A11","CH_GGVC_P_17:A12","CH_GGVC_P_17:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SD3","text":"No creature or created universe exhausts divine power, wisdom, or goodness; divine possibility always exceeds created actuality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A6","CH_GGVC_P_17:A7","CH_GGVC_P_17:A12","CH_GGVC_P_17:A17","CH_GGVC_P_17:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SD4","text":"The irrevocability of the past is not an extrinsic necessity imposed upon God, but a consequence of the identity of accomplished truth.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SD5","text":"The distinction between absolute and ordained power is possible only because the real identity of the divine attributes does not remove the distinction of notions of principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A4","CH_GGVC_P_17:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SD6","text":"The local optimality of the order and global improvability do not contradict each other, because the former corresponds to actual terms, the latter to the alteration of terms and order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A16","CH_GGVC_P_17:A17","CH_GGVC_P_17:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX1","text":"The actuality of created things cannot be the measure of divine possibility.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A6","CH_GGVC_P_17:A7","CH_GGVC_P_17:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX2","text":"A created effect cannot furnish adequate testimony to the infinity of power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A7","CH_GGVC_P_17:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX3","text":"Contradiction is not an exceedingly difficult thing or a resistant object, but the absence of the character of being, of possibility, and of intelligibility.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX4","text":"The capacity to fail, sin, or suffer cannot be counted among the perfections of power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A1","CH_GGVC_P_17:A3","CH_GGVC_P_17:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX5","text":"The real identity of power, will, and wisdom does not require identity of intentional scope according to reason.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A4","CH_GGVC_P_17:A13","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX6","text":"The immutability of divine power does not require that a thing retain the same character of possibility before and after an event.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A14","CH_GGVC_P_17:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX7","text":"The goodness of the parts cannot be increased independently of the order of the whole without the relation of proportion being changed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A16","CH_GGVC_P_17:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX8","text":"Divine justice does not constrain power as an exterior norm, but expresses the same essence under another aspect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A4","CH_GGVC_P_17:NX14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SNX1","text":"The ultimate limit of omnipotence is the absence of a possible object, not the presence of a thing resisting divine power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A9","CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SNX2","text":"No actual creation constitutes a modal closure by which divine fecundity is reduced to one course of things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A11","CH_GGVC_P_17:A12","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SNX3","text":"Neither contradiction nor justice operates as a principle external to God: contradiction is not an object, while justice coincides in reality with the divine essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A4","CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SNX4","text":"No created effect is an end to which divine power is subordinated as something to be perfected.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A7","CH_GGVC_P_17:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SNX5","text":"Accomplished history does not remain matter modally retractable, because the truth of the past closes the possibility of a contrary making.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A14","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:SNX6","text":"No univocal scale measures God and creature, power and effect, or the mode of the agent and the perfection of what is made under one genus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A7","CH_GGVC_P_17:A17","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:FGS1","text":"Pure act and universal perfection → supreme active principle → active power without passive power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A1","CH_GGVC_P_17:A2","CH_GGVC_P_17:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:FGS2","text":"Infinite being → unlimited form of acting → infinite active power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A5","CH_GGVC_P_17:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:FGS3","text":"Infinite being prepossessing the perfection of being → every non-contradictory thing as object → omnipotence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A6","CH_GGVC_P_17:A9","CH_GGVC_P_17:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:FGS4","text":"Distinction between absolute and relative possibility → subordination of natural impossibilities to divine power → preservation of created necessities → exclusion of contradiction and abolition of the past.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:A14","CH_GGVC_P_17:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:FGS5","text":"Real identity of attributes and distinction of notions → absolute and ordained power → actuality necessarily foreknown, but possibility not exhausted by actuality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A4","CH_GGVC_P_17:A11","CH_GGVC_P_17:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:FGS6","text":"Distinction between essential and accidental goodness → immobility of specific identity → improvability of the created mode.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A16","CH_GGVC_P_17:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:FGS7","text":"Best order under given terms + mutability of terms → possibility of another better universe → inexhaustibility of creating power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A12","CH_GGVC_P_17:A18","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:AF1","text":"Existence of active power → infinity of power → definition of omnipotence → contradiction as limit of the object → irrevocability of the past → excess of possibility over actuality → improvability of effects under distinctions of the good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:D1","CH_GGVC_P_17:D2","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_17:D6","CH_GGVC_P_17:D7","CH_GGVC_P_17:D9","CH_GGVC_P_17:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:AF2","text":"Objection from apparent defect → distinction of notions → removal of false object or false measure → restoration of divine perfection → determination of the modal consequence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_17:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_17:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_17:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_17:NX12","CH_GGVC_P_17:NX13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ME1","text":"Power is separated from passive potentiality and reclassified as an expression of perfect actuality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A2","CH_GGVC_P_17:A3","CH_GGVC_P_17:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ME2","text":"The impossible is divided into the absolutely impossible through contradiction and the relatively impossible according to a determinate power or cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:A15","CH_GGVC_P_17:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ME3","text":"Omnipotence is transferred from the scope of all formulas to the scope of every character of being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A9","CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ME4","text":"The irrevocability of the past is transferred from a causal limit to the structure of truth and contradiction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A14","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ME5","text":"The actual order is converted from a single necessary order into one voluntarily instituted ordering among many possible orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A11","CH_GGVC_P_17:A12","CH_GGVC_P_17:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ME6","text":"The notion of the better is distributed according to the identity of the thing, accidents, the mode of the agent, and the order of the whole, whence the opposition between actual perfection and better possibility is dissolved.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A16","CH_GGVC_P_17:A17","CH_GGVC_P_17:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ME7","text":"Divine simplicity excludes real composition of the attributes, but preserves functional distinctions necessary to the structure of causality and modality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A4","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ALT1","text":"The surface level treats six questions: existence, infinity, omnipotence, the irrevocability of the past, non-actual possibilities, and the improvability of things made.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ALT2","text":"The structural level constitutes power from act, essence, will, wisdom, causality, and the order of things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A1","CH_GGVC_P_17:A4","CH_GGVC_P_17:A5","CH_GGVC_P_17:A11","CH_GGVC_P_17:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ALT3","text":"The metastrategic level converts questions about what God cannot do into questions about a defect of the object, a defect of the agent, or the falsity of the measure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:D4","CH_GGVC_P_17:D6","CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_17:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:ALT4","text":"The ontological and temporal level orders possibility according to the character of being, causality according to the mode of being, and irrevocability according to the truth of the fact.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A6","CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:A14","CH_GGVC_P_17:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the principal series from pure act to infinity, omnipotence, the limits of contradiction, and the excess of power over the actual order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A1","CH_GGVC_P_17:A5","CH_GGVC_P_17:A6","CH_GGVC_P_17:A9","CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:A11","CH_GGVC_P_17:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction remains conditioned by prior demonstrations concerning pure act, simplicity, infinity of essence, and causality of the will, which the question presupposes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:MRT3","text":"The structure coheres internally insofar as contradictions are treated not as difficult works but as non-objects of power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_17:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:MRT4","text":"The strongest joints are the distinctions between active and passive power, absolute and relative possibility, and essential and accidental goodness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A2","CH_GGVC_P_17:A3","CH_GGVC_P_17:A10","CH_GGVC_P_17:A15","CH_GGVC_P_17:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:MRT5","text":"A fragile joint remains between the real identity of power, will, and wisdom and the modal diversity of absolute and ordained power; the text maintains it by a distinction of reason, but does not further demonstrate it.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A4","CH_GGVC_P_17:A13","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:MRT6","text":"The infinity of power is proved from the infinity of essence, not from effects; therefore the argument depends essentially on antecedent doctrine concerning the divine essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A5","CH_GGVC_P_17:A6","CH_GGVC_P_17:A7","CH_GGVC_P_17:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:MRT7","text":"The comparison between alternative universes remains less determinate, because the measure by which another order is called simply better is not fully formalized.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:A17","CH_GGVC_P_17:A18","CH_GGVC_P_17:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_17:MRT8","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement of the text: it establishes power, enlarges its scope, removes its false limitations, then reorders created actuality and goodness within that scope.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_17:AF1","CH_GGVC_P_17:FGS1","CH_GGVC_P_17:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_17:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_17@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_P_17:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 26","title":"First Part · Question 25","context":"Chapter 26","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 25"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 25","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 26","order":27,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_P_18"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_18@R001","sha256":"e7bb882e94f92d6a04939ad480da0a16cf804082f31b73a314e9d4a636677ed6","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:AS1","text":"Quaestio determinat utrum beatitudo Deo conveniat, secundum quam rationem ei attribuatur, quomodo ad beatitudinem creatam referatur, et utrum omnes beatitudinum perfectiones complectatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:AS2","text":"Beatitudo reconstituitur non ut aggregatum bonorum, praemium meriti, aut possessio corporalis, sed ut bonum perfectum naturae intellectualis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:AS3","text":"Cum actus intellectus sit perfectio suprema naturae intellectualis, beatitudo formaliter ad intelligere reducitur, quamvis in Deo esse et intelligere re differant non.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:AS4","text":"Distinctio obiecti intellecti et actus intelligentis permittit Deum esse beatitudinem increatam omnibus obiectivam, simulque beatitudinem creatam in beatis esse actum participationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:AS5","text":"Omne desiderabile quod in beatitudinibus veris vel falsis imperfecte apparet, in beatitudine divina eminenter et spiritualiter praeexistit; defectus autem et falsitas excluduntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:A1","text":"Beatitudo est bonum perfectum naturae intellectualis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:A2","text":"Naturae intellectuali competit perfectionem possessam cognoscere, suarum operationum dominari, et sufficientiam in bono habito recognoscere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:A3","text":"Perfectio suprema cuiuslibet naturae intellectualis est operatio intellectus, qua intelligibile quodammodo capit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:A4","text":"Deus est maxime perfectus et intelligens; ideo beatitudo ei maxime convenit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:A5","text":"In Deo esse et intelligere re idem sunt, quamvis ratione intelligentiae distinguantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:A6","text":"In actu intellectus distinguuntur obiectum intelligibile et ipse actus intelligendi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:A7","text":"Solus Deus est summum bonum simpliciter et finis ultimus rationalis naturae ut res cuius fruitio quaeritur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:A8","text":"In creaturis beatis actus intelligendi quo Deus cognoscitur est quid creatum et participatum; in Deo idem actus est increatus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:A9","text":"Acquisitio per meritum vel conditio praemii non constituunt rationem essentialem beatitudinis, sed modum contingentem quo creatura ad ipsam pervenit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:A10","text":"Quidquid perfectionis et desiderabilitatis in beatitudine quacumque reperitur, eminentius in divina beatitudine praeexistit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:NX1","text":"Beatitudo divina non est aggregatio bonorum composita; pluralitas bonorum creaturalium in Deo simpliciter et unite praeexistit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:NX2","text":"Beatitudo divina non est praemium virtutis nec terminus meriti, quia Deus non transit de potentia in actum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:NX3","text":"Beatitudo divina non formaliter secundum rationem essentiae aut voluntatis dicitur, quamvis essentia sit bona et voluntas in beatitudine requiescat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:NX4","text":"Beatitudo creaturae non est Deus secundum actum quo creatura intelligit, licet Deus sit obiectum sine quo beata esse non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:NX5","text":"Beatitudo falsa non includitur in Deo secundum suum defectum a vera beatitudine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:NX6","text":"Bona corporalia non Deo corporaliter attribuuntur; in Deo tantum spiritualiter et eminenter inveniuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:NX7","text":"Multiplicitas graduum beatitudinis creatarum non competit divinae beatitudini, cui nihil maius esse potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:D1","text":"Deus beatus dicitur secundum intellectum, quia beatitudo naturae intellectualis formaliter in actu perfectissimo eius consistit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A1","CH_GGVC_P_18:A3","CH_GGVC_P_18:A4","CH_GGVC_P_18:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:D2","text":"Bonitas divina et beatitudo divina secundum rem non separantur, sed diversa ratio attributionis servanda est: bonitas respicit essentiam, beatitudo actum intellectus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A1","CH_GGVC_P_18:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:D3","text":"Voluntas beatitudine fruitur ut bono obiecto, sed actus quo obiectum beatificum praecognoscitur est intellectualis; ideo intellectus ordine rationis voluntatem praecedit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A3","CH_GGVC_P_18:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:D4","text":"Deus est beatitudo cuiuslibet beati ex parte obiecti, quia creatura beata fit per cognitionem Dei.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A6","CH_GGVC_P_18:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:D5","text":"Beatitudo creaturalis differt a Deo ex parte actus, quia ipsa fruitio vel intelligentia creaturae est effectus creatus et participabilis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A6","CH_GGVC_P_18:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:D6","text":"Finis ultimus rationalis naturae dupliciter dicitur: Deus est finis cuius, actus creatus fruitionis est finis quo.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A7","CH_GGVC_P_18:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:D7","text":"Beatitudines inferiores non aequant beatitudinem divinam, sed desideria quae sub illis latent—contemplatio, gaudium, sufficientia, potestas, regimen, admiratio—per eminentiam in Deo inveniunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A4","CH_GGVC_P_18:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:SD1","text":"Beatitudo divina est identica perfectioni divinae secundum rem, sed intelligibiliter determinata per modum actus quo perfectio a natura intellectuali possidetur et cognoscitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A1","CH_GGVC_P_18:A3","CH_GGVC_P_18:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:SD2","text":"Relatio Dei ad beatitudinem creatam est simul causaliter asymmetrica et obiective intima: Deus non est actus creaturae, sed est obiectum sine quo actus beatificus constitui non potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A6","CH_GGVC_P_18:A7","CH_GGVC_P_18:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:SD3","text":"Omnis pluralitas bonorum beatificantium creaturalium reducitur ad unitatem simplicem divinae perfectionis, sine compositione in Deo neque aequivocatione pura inter Deum et creaturas.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A4","CH_GGVC_P_18:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:SD4","text":"Falsitas beatitudinis mundanae non provenit ex eo quod desideria eius omnino mala sint, sed ex eo quod bonum finitum, corporale vel defectivum pro bono perfecto substituitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A1","CH_GGVC_P_18:A7","CH_GGVC_P_18:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:DNX1","text":"Beatitudo non est status extrinsece additus subiecto iam perfecto, quia in Deo beatitudo exprimit ipsam perfectionem intelligibilem increatam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:DNX2","text":"Perfectio intellectualis non est mera possessio obiecti, sed actus quo obiectum cognitum formaliter perficit intelligentem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:DNX3","text":"Deus non est unum bonum maximum inter bona comparabilia, sed summum bonum simpliciter, a quo omnis ratio boni participabilis dependet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:DNX4","text":"Creatura non possidet Deum ut obiectum exhaustum aut adaequate comprehensum; beatitudo eius est participatio actus finiti circa obiectum infinitum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:DNX5","text":"Translatio bonorum corporalium in divinam beatitudinem non servat modum corporalem eorum, sed solum rationem perfectionis quam imperfecte significant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:SNX1","text":"Systema excludit omnem conceptionem beatitudinis in qua finis ultimus sit res finita, acquisita, commutabilis, vel extra actum intelligentiae constituta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:SNX2","text":"Systema excludit identitatem univocam inter beatitudinem increatam et creatam, sed etiam separationem quae creaturam beatam ab obiecto divino independentem faceret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:SNX3","text":"Systema excludit conceptionem divinae perfectionis ut collectionis attributorum, quia eminentia divina per simplicitatem continet quod creaturae dispersim possident.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:SNX4","text":"Systema excludit falsitatem ut elementum positivum divinae plenitudinis, quamvis omnem tenuem similitudinem perfectionis in beatitudinibus falsis ad Deum referat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:FGS1","text":"Natura intellectualis → bonum perfectum cognitum et sufficienter possessum → ratio beatitudinis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:FGS2","text":"Perfectio naturae intellectualis → actus intellectus ut operatio suprema → beatitudo formaliter secundum intelligere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:FGS3","text":"Deus perfectus et intelligens → esse et intelligere realiter idem → beatitudo divina increata et essentialiter subsistens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:FGS4","text":"Actus intellectus divisus in obiectum et actum → Deus ut obiectum beatificans → creatura ut actum beatificum participans → distinctio beatitudinis increatae et creatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:FGS5","text":"Perfectiones desideratae in beatitudinibus particularibus → remotio defectus, compositionis et corporalitatis → praeexistentia eminens et spiritualis in Deo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:AF1","text":"Obiectio ex compositione et merito → separatio rationis beatitudinis a compositione et acquisitione → affirmatio beatitudinis divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:AF2","text":"Bonum et finis voluntatis → praecedentia obiecti cogniti → intellectus ut ratio formalis beatitudinis divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:AF3","text":"Deus ut summum bonum → distinctio obiecti beatifici et actus beatifici → Deus ut beatitudo increata, creatura ut participatio creata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:AF4","text":"Beatitudines verae et falsae apparent multipliciter → abstractio rationum desiderabilium → comprehensio eminens omnium perfectionum in Deo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:ME1","text":"Beatitudo transfertur ex categoria praemii, fortunae aut possessionis in categoriam perfectionis intellectualis sufficienter cognitae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:ME2","text":"Oppositio inter bonum essentiale et beatitudinem intellectualem dissolvitur per distinctionem inter identitatem realem divinam et diversitatem rationum intelligibilium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:ME3","text":"Relatio Dei et beati creati describitur non per identitatem simplicem neque per exterioritatem, sed per obiectum increatum et actum participatum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:ME4","text":"Beatitudines corporales, activae et contemplativae reclassificantur ut figurae imperfectae diversarum perfectionum divinarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:ME5","text":"Falsitas beatitudinis mundanae localizatur in defectu rationis boni perfecti, non in tota abolitione boni quod desiderium illud partialiter significat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:ALT1","text":"Surface — Deus beatus affirmatur; beatitudo eius secundum intellectum dicitur; creatura beata Deum cognoscendo perficitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:ALT2","text":"Structural — Beatitudo ordinatur per nexus perfectionis, intelligentiae, obiecti, actus, finis cuius, finis quo, simplicitatis et participationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Obiectiones ex compositione, merito, voluntate, corporeitate et pluralitate non negatione simplici solvuntur, sed per distinctionem modi attributionis, obiecti et actus, vel perfectionis et defectus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Deus non acquirit beatitudinem nec transit ad eam, quia beatitudo eius est increata perfectio sine potentia, generatione, merito aut compositione.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:MRT1","text":"Axiomata de bono perfecto intellectualis naturae, actu intellectus, simplicitate divina, distinctione obiecti et actus, atque participatione creaturali sufficiunt ad principales conclusiones restituendas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:MRT2","text":"Structura maxime nititur in distinctione inter identitatem realem et diversitatem rationis in Deo, qua beatitudo simul essentialis et secundum intellectum dici potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:MRT3","text":"Structura etiam nititur in distinctione finis cuius et finis quo, sine qua Deus et beatitudo creata vel identificari vel dissociari nimis facile possent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:MRT4","text":"Commissura fragilis manet in transitu ab eo quod actus intellectus est perfectissimus in natura intellectuali ad conclusionem quod beatitudo formaliter omnino per intellectum, non per voluntatem, attendatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0029","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:MRT5","text":"Tensio ordinata, non plene explicata, manet inter affirmationem quod Deus est beatitudo cuiuslibet beati ex parte obiecti et negationem quod beatitudo creata simpliciter sit Deus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0040","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:MRT6","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat, quia a remotione impedimentorum extrinsecorum procedit ad rationem formalem beatitudinis, deinde ad distinctionem participativam, et ultimo ad eminentem comprehensionem perfectionum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T160549371996Z_da41462e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 26","source_sha256":"e7bb882e94f92d6a04939ad480da0a16cf804082f31b73a314e9d4a636677ed6","sha256":"977083b361cf26db992b46f30224e9bd1ea9b5101ef71fa3c918046432767e9c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T16:05:49.373467+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:AS1","text":"The question determines whether beatitude befits God, according to what account it is attributed to Him, how it is related to created beatitude, and whether it encompasses all the perfections of beatitudes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:AS2","text":"Beatitude is reconstructed not as an aggregate of goods, a reward of merit, or a bodily possession, but as the perfect good of an intellectual nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:AS3","text":"Since the act of intellect is the highest perfection of an intellectual nature, beatitude is formally reduced to understanding, although in God being and understanding do not differ in reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:AS4","text":"The distinction between the object understood and the act of understanding permits God to be uncreated beatitude objective to all, while created beatitude in the blessed is an act of participation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:AS5","text":"Whatever is desirable that appears imperfectly in true or false beatitudes pre-exists eminently and spiritually in divine beatitude; defect and falsity, however, are excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:A1","text":"Beatitude is the perfect good of an intellectual nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:A2","text":"It belongs to an intellectual nature to know the perfection possessed, to be master of its operations, and to recognize sufficiency in the good possessed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:A3","text":"The highest perfection of any intellectual nature is the operation of intellect, by which it somehow apprehends the intelligible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:A4","text":"God is maximally perfect and intelligent; therefore beatitude most befittingly belongs to Him.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:A5","text":"In God, being and understanding are the same in reality, although they are distinguished according to the account of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:A6","text":"In the act of intellect, the intelligible object and the very act of understanding are distinguished.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:A7","text":"God alone is the highest good simply speaking and the ultimate end of rational nature as the thing whose enjoyment is sought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:A8","text":"In blessed creatures, the act of understanding by which God is known is something created and participated; in God, the same act is uncreated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:A9","text":"Acquisition through merit or the condition of reward do not constitute the essential account of beatitude, but a contingent mode by which the creature attains it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:A10","text":"Whatever perfection and desirability is found in any beatitude pre-exists more eminently in divine beatitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:NX1","text":"Divine beatitude is not a composite aggregation of goods; the plurality of created goods pre-exists in God simply and unitedly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:NX2","text":"Divine beatitude is neither a reward of virtue nor the terminus of merit, because God does not pass from potency to act.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:NX3","text":"Divine beatitude is not formally predicated according to the account of essence or will, although the essence is good and the will rests in beatitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:NX4","text":"The creature's beatitude is not God according to the act by which the creature understands, although God is the object without which it cannot be blessed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:NX5","text":"False beatitude is not included in God according to its defect from true beatitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:NX6","text":"Bodily goods are not attributed to God bodily; in God they are found only spiritually and eminently.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:NX7","text":"A multiplicity of degrees of created beatitude does not belong to divine beatitude, than which nothing can be greater.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:D1","text":"God is called blessed according to intellect, because the beatitude of an intellectual nature formally consists in its most perfect act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A1","CH_GGVC_P_18:A3","CH_GGVC_P_18:A4","CH_GGVC_P_18:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:D2","text":"Divine goodness and divine beatitude are not separated in reality, but a different account of attribution must be maintained: goodness concerns the essence, beatitude the act of intellect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A1","CH_GGVC_P_18:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:D3","text":"The will enjoys beatitude as an objective good, but the act by which the beatifying object is foreknown is intellectual; therefore intellect precedes will in the order of account.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A3","CH_GGVC_P_18:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:D4","text":"God is the beatitude of every blessed being on the side of the object, because the creature becomes blessed through knowledge of God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A6","CH_GGVC_P_18:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:D5","text":"Created beatitude differs from God on the side of the act, because the creature's enjoyment or understanding itself is a created and participable effect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A6","CH_GGVC_P_18:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:D6","text":"The ultimate end of rational nature is spoken of in two ways: God is the end which is sought, and the created act of enjoyment is the end by which.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A7","CH_GGVC_P_18:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:D7","text":"Lower beatitudes do not equal divine beatitude, but they find in God eminently the desires latent beneath them—contemplation, joy, sufficiency, power, governance, admiration.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A4","CH_GGVC_P_18:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:SD1","text":"Divine beatitude is identical with divine perfection in reality, but is intelligibly determined according to the mode of an act by which perfection is possessed and known by an intellectual nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A1","CH_GGVC_P_18:A3","CH_GGVC_P_18:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:SD2","text":"God's relation to created beatitude is at once causally asymmetrical and objectively intimate: God is not the creature's act, but He is the object without which the beatific act cannot be constituted.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A6","CH_GGVC_P_18:A7","CH_GGVC_P_18:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:SD3","text":"Every plurality of created beatifying goods is reduced to the simple unity of divine perfection, without composition in God or mere equivocation between God and creatures.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A4","CH_GGVC_P_18:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:SD4","text":"The falsity of worldly beatitude does not arise from the fact that its desires are wholly evil, but from the fact that a finite, bodily, or defective good is substituted for the perfect good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_18:A1","CH_GGVC_P_18:A7","CH_GGVC_P_18:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:DNX1","text":"Beatitude is not a state extrinsically added to an already perfect subject, because in God beatitude expresses uncreated intelligible perfection itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:DNX2","text":"Intellectual perfection is not mere possession of an object, but the act by which the known object formally perfects the knower.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:DNX3","text":"God is not one greatest good among comparable goods, but the highest good simply speaking, on which every account of participable good depends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:DNX4","text":"The creature does not possess God as an exhausted or adequately comprehended object; its beatitude is participation in a finite act concerning an infinite object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:DNX5","text":"The transposition of bodily goods into divine beatitude does not preserve their bodily mode, but only the account of perfection which they imperfectly signify.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:SNX1","text":"The system excludes every conception of beatitude in which the ultimate end is a finite, acquired, changeable thing, or one constituted outside the act of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:SNX2","text":"The system excludes univocal identity between uncreated and created beatitude, but also a separation that would make the blessed creature independent of the divine object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:SNX3","text":"The system excludes a conception of divine perfection as a collection of attributes, because divine eminence contains in simplicity what creatures possess separately.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:SNX4","text":"The system excludes falsity as a positive element of divine fullness, although it refers to God every faint likeness of perfection in false beatitudes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:FGS1","text":"Intellectual nature → perfect good known and sufficiently possessed → account of beatitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:FGS2","text":"Perfection of intellectual nature → act of intellect as supreme operation → beatitude formally according to understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:FGS3","text":"Perfect and intelligent God → being and understanding really identical → uncreated and essentially subsisting divine beatitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:FGS4","text":"Act of intellect divided into object and act → God as beatifying object → creature as participating in the beatific act → distinction of uncreated and created beatitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:FGS5","text":"Perfections desired in particular beatitudes → removal of defect, composition, and corporeality → eminent and spiritual pre-existence in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:AF1","text":"Objection from composition and merit → separation of the account of beatitude from composition and acquisition → affirmation of divine beatitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:AF2","text":"Good and end of the will → precedence of the known object → intellect as the formal account of divine beatitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:AF3","text":"God as highest good → distinction of beatifying object and beatific act → God as uncreated beatitude, creature as created participation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:AF4","text":"True and false beatitudes appear in many ways → abstraction of accounts of the desirable → eminent comprehension of all perfections in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:ME1","text":"Beatitude is transferred from the category of reward, fortune, or possession into the category of sufficiently known intellectual perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:ME2","text":"The opposition between essential good and intellectual beatitude is dissolved through the distinction between divine real identity and diversity of intelligible accounts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:ME3","text":"The relation of God and the created blessed is described not through simple identity or through exteriority, but through an uncreated object and a participated act.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:ME4","text":"Bodily, active, and contemplative beatitudes are reclassified as imperfect figures of diverse divine perfections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:ME5","text":"The falsity of worldly beatitude is located in the defect of the account of the perfect good, not in the total abolition of the good which that desire partially signifies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:ALT1","text":"Surface — God is affirmed to be blessed; His beatitude is said to be according to intellect; the blessed creature is perfected by knowing God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:ALT2","text":"Structural — Beatitude is ordered through the connections of perfection, understanding, object, act, end which is sought, end by which, simplicity, and participation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Objections from composition, merit, will, corporeality, and plurality are not resolved by simple negation, but through the distinction of the mode of attribution, object and act, or perfection and defect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — God neither acquires beatitude nor passes to it, because His beatitude is uncreated perfection without potency, generation, merit, or composition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:MRT1","text":"The axioms concerning the perfect good of intellectual nature, the act of intellect, divine simplicity, the distinction of object and act, and creaturely participation suffice to restore the principal conclusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:MRT2","text":"The structure depends most of all on the distinction between real identity and diversity of account in God, by which beatitude can be said to be at once essential and according to intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:MRT3","text":"The structure also depends on the distinction between the end which is sought and the end by which, without which God and created beatitude could too easily be either identified or dissociated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:MRT4","text":"A fragile joint remains in the transition from the fact that the act of intellect is most perfect in an intellectual nature to the conclusion that beatitude is formally to be considered altogether through intellect, not through will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0029","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:MRT5","text":"An ordered, not fully explained tension remains between the affirmation that God is the beatitude of every blessed being on the side of the object and the negation that created beatitude simply is God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0040","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0042","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_18:MRT6","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text, because it proceeds from the removal of extrinsic impediments to the formal account of beatitude, then to the participatory distinction, and finally to the eminent comprehension of perfections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_18:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_18@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_P_18:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 27","title":"First Part · Question 26","context":"Chapter 27","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 26"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 26","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 27","order":28,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_P_19"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_19@R001","sha256":"98749d931797413ae85894bec19a3cf094f035bac56ed83e9e4ed6071c2f65f3","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AS1","text":"Distinctio personarum divinarum ex relationibus originis posita exigit ut processiones ante relationes et personas examinentur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AS2","text":"Obiectiones ex immobilitate, simplicitate et ratione primi principii oriuntur, si processio necessario ut motus vel exitus in aliud intelligitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AS3","text":"Processio reconstituitur ut emanatio ad intra ex actione immanente, praecipue ex intellectione, non ut causalitas ad extra.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AS4","text":"Verbum ab intellectione divina procedens, quia est a vivente coniuncto et secundum similitudinem eiusdem naturae, rationem generationis et filiationis habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AS5","text":"Amor ex voluntate procedens est processio altera, verbo ordine posterior, sed non generatio, quia voluntas inclinatur in rem amatam potius quam similitudinem eius producat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AS6","text":"Enumeratio actionum immanentium et simplicitas unius actus divini systema claudit ad duas processiones perfectas: Verbum et Amorem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A1","text":"Personae divinae distinguuntur secundum relationes originis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A2","text":"Processio divina tantum secundum actionem in agente manentem accipi potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A3","text":"Nomina divina per similitudinem substantiarum intellectualium, non per modum corporum, intelligenda sunt; similitudo tamen divinum non perfecte repraesentat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A4","text":"Intellegere producit in intelligente conceptionem rei intellectae, quae verbum cordis dicitur et in intelligente manet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A5","text":"In Deo intelligere est idem quod esse et substantia intelligentis, atque est in ultimo perfectionis gradu.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A6","text":"Quidquid est in Deo est Deus; ideo quaelibet processio divina ad intra divinam naturam communicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A7","text":"Generatio proprie est origo viventis a principio vivente coniuncto secundum similitudinem naturae eiusdem speciei.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A8","text":"Voluntas in actu non per similitudinem voliti in se receptam, sed per inclinationem in rem volitam perficitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A9","text":"Amor non procedit nisi ex re prius in intellectu concepta; propterea processio amoris ordinem ad processionem verbi habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A10","text":"In natura intellectuali divina solae actiones immanentes sunt intelligere et velle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A11","text":"Deus omnia uno simplici actu intelligit et vult; ideo in Deo unum Verbum perfectum et unus Amor perfectus sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:NX1","text":"Processio divina non est egressus effectus creati a causa; haec acceptio Arii Filium et Spiritum Sanctum creaturas constitueret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:NX2","text":"Processio divina non est idem Deus in effectibus diversis denominatus; haec acceptio Sabellii Patrem et Filium identificaret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:NX3","text":"Processio divina non est motus localis neque actio in materiam vel effectum exteriorem tendens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:NX4","text":"Processio ad intra non exigit procedens esse diversum vel extraneum a principio; perfectior processio maiorem unitatem importat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:NX5","text":"Procedere ut extraneum et diversum repugnat primo principio; procedere ut intimum intelligibile in ipsa ratione primi principii includitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:NX6","text":"Generatio divina non est mutatio de non esse in esse neque exitus de potentia in actum in materia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:NX7","text":"Verbum intellectus humani non est proprie genitum, quia intelligere humanum non est ipsa substantia intelligentis nec verbum eius naturae identitatem habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:NX8","text":"Processio amoris non est generatio neque filiatio, quamvis procedens divinam naturam accipiat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0072","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:NX9","text":"Potentia, bonitas ut attributum essentiale, et sensatio non constituunt principia processionum personalium divinarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0091","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0093","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:NX10","text":"Fecunditas divina non fert processionem verbi ex verbo vel amoris ex amore, neque seriem infinitam processionum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0085","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:D1","text":"Processio divina intelligitur ut emanatio intelligibilis Verbi manentis in dicente, non ut exitus localis vel productio effectus exterioris.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A2","CH_GGVC_P_19:A3","CH_GGVC_P_19:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:D2","text":"Simplicitas divina et ratio primi principii processionem ad intra non excludunt, quia processio perfectissima est intima et absque diversitate.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A2","CH_GGVC_P_19:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:D3","text":"Verbum divinum procedens est genitum et Filius, quia est viva conceptio a principio coniuncto procedens secundum similitudinem eiusdem naturae subsistentis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A4","CH_GGVC_P_19:A5","CH_GGVC_P_19:A6","CH_GGVC_P_19:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:D4","text":"Esse Filii accipitur a generante sine receptione in subiecto vel materia, quia esse divinum unum subsistens non fit aliud esse in procedente.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A5","CH_GGVC_P_19:A6","CH_GGVC_P_19:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:D5","text":"Processio amoris est processio realiter ordinata ad Verbum, quia amor sequitur rem intellectam, licet voluntas et intellectus in Deo non sint res diversae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A8","CH_GGVC_P_19:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:D6","text":"Amor divinus communicat divinam naturam, sed non generat similitudinem ut terminum processionis; ideo procedit ut Spiritus, non ut Filius.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A6","CH_GGVC_P_19:A7","CH_GGVC_P_19:A8","CH_GGVC_P_19:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:D7","text":"Processiones divinae sunt numero duae, quia tantum intelligere et velle actiones ad intra praebent, atque uterque actus unus et perfectus est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A10","CH_GGVC_P_19:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:D8","text":"Generatio nomen proprium habet quia creaturae communicationem naturae per generationem cognoscunt; processio amoris nomen speciale proportionatum non habet et spiratio vocari potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A3","CH_GGVC_P_19:A7","CH_GGVC_P_19:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:SD1","text":"Pluralitas personalis non ex divisione essentiae, sed ex ordinibus originis intra unam naturam divinam intelligibilis fit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A1","CH_GGVC_P_19:A6","CH_GGVC_P_19:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:SD2","text":"Simplicitas divina non pluralitatem originum destruit, sed prohibet eam fieri per diversitatem essentiarum, actuum separatarum vel receptionum materialium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A2","CH_GGVC_P_19:A5","CH_GGVC_P_19:A6","CH_GGVC_P_19:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:SD3","text":"Fecunditas divina maxima non multiplicatione terminorum vel successione actuum, sed perfectione unius Verbi et unius Amoris definitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A10","CH_GGVC_P_19:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:SD4","text":"Nominatio processionum asymmetrica est: Verbum per similitudinem et generationem positive nominatur, Amor vero per negationem generationis et per nomen imperfecte proportionatum describitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A3","CH_GGVC_P_19:A7","CH_GGVC_P_19:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:SD5","text":"Ordo Verbi ad Amorem est ordo rationis originariae, non successio temporalis aut mutatio in vita divina.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A5","CH_GGVC_P_19:A9","CH_GGVC_P_19:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet omnem originem causalem necessario alteritatem, exterioritatem et effectum separatum producere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet simplicitatem absolutam relationes originis ordinatas necessario dissolvere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet omnem communicationem naturae generationem esse; natura communicata et ratio processionis distinguendae sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet fecunditatem perfectam multiplicationem serialem verborum, amorum vel terminorum exigere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0085","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse debet vocabula creaturarum univoce taxonomiam divinam constituere posse; ratio significata retinenda est, modus creatus removendus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:SNX1","text":"Systema excludit possibilitatem ut divina processio in fieri, mutatione, receptione, loco vel tempore fundetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:SNX2","text":"Systema excludit possibilitatem ut divinae personae ex potentia productiva, bonitate diffusa vel effectibus creatis primario constituantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0091","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:SNX3","text":"Systema excludit tertiam originem personalem irreducibilem praeter Verbum et Amorem, quia nulla tertia actio immanens divina datur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:FGS1","text":"Relationes originis personarum → prior inquisitio de processione → quaestio de compatibilitate processionis cum unitate divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:FGS2","text":"Motus localis et causalitas exterior exclusa → actio immanens requisita → emanatio intelligibilis intra intelligente ponitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:FGS3","text":"Intellegere → conceptio interior → Verbum → identitas divini intelligere et esse → eadem natura subsistens → generatio et Filius.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:FGS4","text":"Velle → inclinatio in bonum intellectum → Amor procedens → dependentia ordinis a Verbo → Spiritus, non genitus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:FGS5","text":"Exhaustio actionum immanentium per intelligere et velle + unitas simplicis actus divini → una processio Verbi et una processio Amoris → clausura binaria.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AF1","text":"Distinctio personarum per origines → prioritas doctrinae de processionibus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AF2","text":"Motus, diversitas et imminutio primi principii → exclusio notionis externae processionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AF3","text":"Actio intelligibilis immanens → Verbum intra dicentem → processio divina ad intra.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AF4","text":"Vita, principium coniunctum, similitudo et eadem natura → generatio Verbi → Filius.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AF5","text":"Voluntas ordinata ad intellectum → Amor intra amante → distincta processio non-generativa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AF6","text":"Duae actiones immanentes et unus actus simplex → duae tantum processiones perfectae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:ME1","text":"Processio a categoria motus et productionis exterioris ad categoriam operationis immanentis transfertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:ME2","text":"Oppositio simplicitas divina versus pluralitas personalis dissolvitur per distinctionem inter unitatem essentiae et ordinem originum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:ME3","text":"Generatio reclassificatur non per materialem receptionem vel transitum temporalem, sed per originem viventem secundum similitudinem naturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:ME4","text":"Distinctio Verbi et Amoris non ex essentia communicata, quam uterque habet, sed ex rationibus intellectualis similitudinis et voluntariae inclinationis constituitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:ME5","text":"Numerus processionum ex inventario actionum immanentium derivatur, non ex quantitate divinae potentiae, bonitatis vel fecunditatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0091","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:ALT1","text":"[Gradus superficiei] Quaeritur utrum in Deo processio sit, utrum generatio sit, utrum Amor procedat, et utrum processiones numero duas excedant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:ALT2","text":"[Gradus structuralis] Processiones distinguuntur per rationes actionum immanentium: intellectionis conceptivae et voluntatis inclinativae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:ALT3","text":"[Gradus metastrategicus] Obiectiones solvuntur per disiunctionem modi creati a ratione significata: retinetur origo, removentur motus, exterioritas, materia et diversitas essentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:ALT4","text":"[Gradus ontologicus/temporalis] Divinum esse unum, subsistens et simpliciter actuosum excludit potentiam, successionem, receptionem et multiplicationem processuum, dum ordinem originis conservat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:MRT1","text":"Systema extractum argumentationem restituit, si processio ad intra, identitas divini intelligere et esse, et distinctio rationum intellectus ac voluntatis simul retinentur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:MRT2","text":"Fundamentum dependet a conclusionibus praemissis de simplicitate divina, subsistentia ipsius esse, identitate attributorum et perfectione intellectus divini.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:MRT3","text":"Cohaerentia interna servatur dum diversitas processionum non ad res diversas in Deo, sed ad ordinem rationum originariarum refertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:MRT4","text":"Commissura firmissima est nexus inter Verbum, similitudinem intellectam, naturae identitatem et generationem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:MRT5","text":"Commissura fragilior est distinctio processionum intellectus et voluntatis, cum utrumque in Deo idem secundum rem esse ponatur et sola ratio ordinis differentiam ferat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0060","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0074","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:MRT6","text":"Transitus ab verbo humano non-subsistenti ad Verbum divinum subsistens postulat analogiam et doctrinas praevias; intra quaestionem ipsam non independenter demonstratur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:MRT7","text":"Nomen spiratio indicat asymmetriam residualem: systema rationem processionis amoris determinat, sed vocabulum proprium creaturis perfecte proportionatum non invenit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T160615468036Z_c3fc9813","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 27","source_sha256":"98749d931797413ae85894bec19a3cf094f035bac56ed83e9e4ed6071c2f65f3","sha256":"94eb0317d6da227d9a16af7d5b361fd3d4abbe0e7db53e434dd8e665b4d2fa4a","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T16:06:15.469293+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AS1","text":"The distinction of the divine persons, posited from relations of origin, requires that processions be examined before relations and persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AS2","text":"Objections arise from immobility, simplicity, and the notion of the first principle, if procession is necessarily understood as motion or an issuing-forth into another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AS3","text":"Procession is reconstituted as an emanation ad intra from immanent action, especially from intellection, not as causality ad extra.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AS4","text":"The Word proceeding from divine intellection, because it is from a living and conjoined source and according to the likeness of the same nature, has the character of generation and sonship.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AS5","text":"Love proceeding from the will is another procession, posterior to the Word in order, but not generation, because the will is inclined toward the beloved thing rather than produces its likeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AS6","text":"The enumeration of immanent actions and the simplicity of the one divine act closes the system to two perfect processions: the Word and Love.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A1","text":"The divine persons are distinguished according to relations of origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A2","text":"Divine procession can be understood only according to an action remaining in the agent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A3","text":"Divine names are to be understood through the likeness of intellectual substances, not in the manner of bodies; yet the likeness does not represent the divine perfectly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A4","text":"Intellection produces in the one understanding a conception of the thing understood, which is called the word of the heart and remains in the one understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A5","text":"In God, to understand is the same as the being and substance of the one understanding, and is at the highest degree of perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A6","text":"Whatever is in God is God; therefore every divine procession ad intra communicates the divine nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A7","text":"Generation properly is the origin of a living being from a conjoined living principle according to the likeness of a nature of the same species.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A8","text":"The will in act is perfected not through the likeness of the willed thing received into itself, but through inclination toward the willed thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A9","text":"Love proceeds only from a thing previously conceived in the intellect; therefore the procession of love has an order to the procession of the Word.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A10","text":"In the divine intellectual nature, the only immanent actions are understanding and willing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:A11","text":"God understands and wills all things by one simple act; therefore in God there are one perfect Word and one perfect Love.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:NX1","text":"Divine procession is not the emergence of a created effect from a cause; this understanding of Arius would make the Son and the Holy Spirit creatures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:NX2","text":"Divine procession is not God himself denominated differently in diverse effects; this understanding of Sabellius would identify the Father and the Son.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:NX3","text":"Divine procession is not local motion, nor an action tending toward matter or an exterior effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:NX4","text":"Procession ad intra does not require that what proceeds be diverse or extraneous from its principle; the more perfect procession entails greater unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:NX5","text":"To proceed as extraneous and diverse is incompatible with the first principle; to proceed as an intimate intelligible is included in the very notion of the first principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:NX6","text":"Divine generation is not a change from non-being into being, nor an emergence from potency into act in matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:NX7","text":"The word of the human intellect is not properly begotten, because human understanding is not the very substance of the one understanding, nor does its word possess identity of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:NX8","text":"The procession of love is neither generation nor sonship, although what proceeds receives the divine nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0072","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:NX9","text":"Power, goodness as an essential attribute, and sensation do not constitute principles of divine personal processions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0091","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0093","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:NX10","text":"Divine fecundity does not bear a procession of word from word or of love from love, nor an infinite series of processions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0085","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:D1","text":"Divine procession is understood as the intelligible emanation of the Word remaining in the speaker, not as a local issuing-forth or the production of an exterior effect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A2","CH_GGVC_P_19:A3","CH_GGVC_P_19:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:D2","text":"Divine simplicity and the notion of the first principle do not exclude procession ad intra, because the most perfect procession is intimate and without diversity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A2","CH_GGVC_P_19:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:D3","text":"The proceeding divine Word is begotten and is the Son, because it is a living conception proceeding from a conjoined principle according to the likeness of the same subsisting nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A4","CH_GGVC_P_19:A5","CH_GGVC_P_19:A6","CH_GGVC_P_19:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:D4","text":"The being of the Son is received from the begetter without reception in a subject or matter, because the one subsisting divine being does not become another being in what proceeds.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A5","CH_GGVC_P_19:A6","CH_GGVC_P_19:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:D5","text":"The procession of love is a procession really ordered to the Word, because love follows the thing understood, although will and intellect in God are not diverse things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A8","CH_GGVC_P_19:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:D6","text":"Divine Love communicates the divine nature, but does not generate a likeness as the terminus of procession; therefore it proceeds as Spirit, not as Son.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A6","CH_GGVC_P_19:A7","CH_GGVC_P_19:A8","CH_GGVC_P_19:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:D7","text":"The divine processions are two in number, because only understanding and willing provide actions ad intra, and each act is one and perfect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A10","CH_GGVC_P_19:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:D8","text":"Generation has a proper name because creatures know the communication of nature through generation; the procession of love has no special proportionate name and may be called spiration.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A3","CH_GGVC_P_19:A7","CH_GGVC_P_19:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:SD1","text":"Personal plurality becomes intelligible not from a division of essence, but from orders of origin within one divine nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A1","CH_GGVC_P_19:A6","CH_GGVC_P_19:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:SD2","text":"Divine simplicity does not destroy plurality of origins, but forbids it from occurring through a diversity of essences, separate acts, or material receptions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A2","CH_GGVC_P_19:A5","CH_GGVC_P_19:A6","CH_GGVC_P_19:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:SD3","text":"The greatest divine fecundity is defined not by a multiplication of termini or succession of acts, but by the perfection of one Word and one Love.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A10","CH_GGVC_P_19:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:SD4","text":"The naming of the processions is asymmetrical: the Word is positively named through likeness and generation, whereas Love is described through the negation of generation and through an imperfectly proportionate name.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A3","CH_GGVC_P_19:A7","CH_GGVC_P_19:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:SD5","text":"The order of the Word to Love is an order of originating intelligibility, not temporal succession or change in divine life.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_19:A5","CH_GGVC_P_19:A9","CH_GGVC_P_19:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:DNX1","text":"It must be false that every causal origin necessarily produces otherness, exteriority, and a separate effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:DNX2","text":"It must be false that absolute simplicity necessarily dissolves ordered relations of origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:DNX3","text":"It must be false that every communication of nature is generation; the communicated nature and the intelligibility of procession must be distinguished.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:DNX4","text":"It must be false that perfect fecundity requires a serial multiplication of words, loves, or termini.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0085","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:DNX5","text":"It must be false that the terms of creatures can univocally constitute a divine taxonomy; the signified intelligibility must be retained, the created mode removed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:SNX1","text":"The system excludes the possibility that divine procession be founded in coming-to-be, change, reception, place, or time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:SNX2","text":"The system excludes the possibility that the divine persons be constituted primarily from productive power, diffused goodness, or created effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0091","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:SNX3","text":"The system excludes a third irreducible personal origin beyond the Word and Love, because no third divine immanent action is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:FGS1","text":"Relations of origin of the persons → prior inquiry into procession → question of the compatibility of procession with divine unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:FGS2","text":"Local motion and exterior causality excluded → immanent action required → intelligible emanation within the one understanding is posited.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:FGS3","text":"Understanding → interior conception → Word → identity of divine understanding and being → same subsisting nature → generation and Son.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:FGS4","text":"Willing → inclination toward the understood good → proceeding Love → dependence of order upon the Word → Spirit, not begotten.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:FGS5","text":"Exhaustion of immanent actions by understanding and willing + unity of the simple divine act → one procession of the Word and one procession of Love → binary closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AF1","text":"Distinction of persons through origins → priority of the doctrine of processions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AF2","text":"Motion, diversity, and diminution of the first principle → exclusion of an external notion of procession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AF3","text":"Immanent intelligible action → Word within the speaker → divine procession ad intra.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AF4","text":"Life, conjoined principle, likeness, and the same nature → generation of the Word → Son.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AF5","text":"Will ordered to intellect → Love within the lover → distinct non-generative procession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:AF6","text":"Two immanent actions and one simple act → only two perfect processions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:ME1","text":"Procession is transferred from the category of motion and exterior production to the category of immanent operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:ME2","text":"The opposition between divine simplicity and personal plurality is dissolved through the distinction between unity of essence and order of origins.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:ME3","text":"Generation is reclassified not through material reception or temporal transition, but through living origin according to likeness of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:ME4","text":"The distinction of Word and Love is constituted not from the communicated essence, which each possesses, but from the notions of intellectual likeness and voluntary inclination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:ME5","text":"The number of processions is derived from the inventory of immanent actions, not from the magnitude of divine power, goodness, or fecundity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0091","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:ALT1","text":"[Surface level] It is asked whether there is procession in God, whether there is generation, whether Love proceeds, and whether the processions exceed two in number.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:ALT2","text":"[Structural level] Processions are distinguished through the intelligibilities of immanent actions: conceptual intellection and inclining will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:ALT3","text":"[Metastrategic level] Objections are resolved through the disjunction of the created mode from the signified intelligibility: origin is retained, while motion, exteriority, matter, and diversity of essence are removed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:ALT4","text":"[Ontological/temporal level] Divine being, one, subsisting, and simply actual, excludes potency, succession, reception, and multiplication of processes, while preserving the order of origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:MRT1","text":"The extracted system restores the argumentation if procession ad intra, the identity of divine understanding and being, and the distinction of the intelligibilities of intellect and will are retained together.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:MRT2","text":"The foundation depends on prior conclusions concerning divine simplicity, the subsistence of being itself, the identity of attributes, and the perfection of the divine intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:MRT3","text":"Internal coherence is preserved so long as the diversity of processions is referred not to diverse things in God, but to an order of originating intelligibilities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:MRT4","text":"The strongest joint is the nexus between the Word, understood likeness, identity of nature, and generation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:MRT5","text":"The more fragile joint is the distinction between the processions of intellect and will, since both are posited in God as the same in reality and the notion of order alone bears the difference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0060","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0074","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:MRT6","text":"The transition from the non-subsisting human word to the subsisting divine Word requires analogy and prior doctrines; it is not independently demonstrated within the question itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_19:MRT7","text":"The name spiration indicates a residual asymmetry: the system determines the intelligibility of the procession of love, but does not find a proper term perfectly proportionate to creatures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_19:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_19@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_P_19:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 28","title":"First Part · Question 27","context":"Chapter 28","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 27"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 27","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 28","order":29,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_P_20"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_20@R001","sha256":"17d48d1ef06713c5f875bcf7f3c699e0abfd97869869faa4db50333c25533d86","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AS1","text":"Quaestio examinat quomodo relationes divinas reales esse possint, salva absoluta simplicitate et unitate essentiae divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AS2","text":"Argumentatio metaphysico-reconstructiva relationem primo dividit in respectum realem et respectum a ratione tantum constitutum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AS3","text":"Processiones intra eandem naturam ponuntur ut fundamentum quo respectus originis in Deo reales fiunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AS4","text":"Realitas relationis cum simplicitate conciliatur distinguendo esse relationis, quod est ipsum esse essentiae, ab eius ratione relativa, quae opponitur alteri.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AS5","text":"Oppositio relativa producit distinctionem realem relationum sine distinctione absoluta essentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AS6","text":"Numerus relationum realium ad duas processiones immanentes et ad binas relationes oppositas per processionem restringitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A1","text":"Relatio secundum propriam rationem respectum ad alterum significat, non rem quae per se subiecto inhaeret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A2","text":"Respectus est realis quando in ipsa natura rerum fundatur per ordinem et inclinationem ad invicem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A3","text":"Respectus est rationis tantum quando intellectus unum alteri comparando ordinem conficit qui in rebus non fundatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A4","text":"Quod ab alio procedit in identitate naturae cum suo principio, cum eo in eodem ordine convenit et respectum realem recipit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A5","text":"Nihil quod realiter est in Deo habet esse accidentale vel esse aliud ab essentia divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A6","text":"Relatio realiter existens in Deo est una res cum essentia divina, sed significat respectum ad oppositum quem nomen essentiae non significat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A7","text":"Ratio relationis realis oppositionem relativam includit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A8","text":"Oppositio relativa distinctionem realem secundum rem relativam requirit, non distinctionem secundum rem absolutam quae est essentia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A9","text":"Omnis relatio realis vel supra quantitatem vel supra actionem et passionem fundatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A10","text":"Quantitas in Deo non est, et relatio Dei ad creaturas non est realiter in Deo, quia creatura ab eo procedit in diversitate naturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A11","text":"Duae tantum processiones immanentes in Deo ponuntur: processio Verbi secundum intellectum et processio amoris secundum voluntatem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A12","text":"Unaquaeque processio immanens duas relationes oppositas requirit: relationem principii et relationem procedentis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A13","text":"Deus uno actu omnia intelligit; ideo actus intelligendi iterati relationes intelligibiles in infinitum in Deo non multiplicant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0080","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:NX1","text":"Relatio divina non est accidens essentiae inhaerens nec aliquid essentiae extrinsecus affixum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:NX2","text":"Non omnis relatio significata est relatio realis; relatio identitatis simpliciter accepta et comparationes mentis sunt rationis tantum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:NX3","text":"Identitas naturae divinae non efficit ut paternitas et filiatio sint relationes rationis tantum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:NX4","text":"Relatio creatoris ad creaturam non est relatio realis in Deo, quamvis creatura realiter a Deo dependeat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:NX5","text":"Pluralitas relationum divinarum non est pluralitas essentiarum, accidentium, aut formarum absolutarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:NX6","text":"Relationes intellectus ad intellectum, voluntatis ad volitum, ideae, aequalitas, similitudo et identitas non addunt relationes reales divinas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0084","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:D1","text":"Paternitas et filiatio sunt in Deo relationes reales, quia generatio divina ponitur processio intra identitatem naturae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_20:A2","CH_GGVC_P_20:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:D2","text":"Creatura ad Deum realiter refertur, sed Deus ad creaturam non realiter refertur, quia dependentia creaturae ad ordinem divinum pertinet sine reciproca Dei dependentia.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_20:A2","CH_GGVC_P_20:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:D3","text":"Relationes divinae possunt simul esse idem essentiae secundum rem et distinctae inter se secundum oppositionem relativam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_20:A5","CH_GGVC_P_20:A6","CH_GGVC_P_20:A7","CH_GGVC_P_20:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:D4","text":"Relationum realium divinarum numerus est quattuor: paternitas, filiatio, spiratio et processio.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_20:A9","CH_GGVC_P_20:A10","CH_GGVC_P_20:A11","CH_GGVC_P_20:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:D5","text":"Diversitas respectuum patris ad filium et filii ad patrem manet, etiam si idem medium vel idem iter inter terminos consideratur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_20:A7","CH_GGVC_P_20:A8","CH_GGVC_P_20:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0086","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:SD1","text":"Trinitas realis collocatur in ordine oppositionum relativarum, dum unitas divina collocatur in ordine essentiae absolutae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_20:A5","CH_GGVC_P_20:A6","CH_GGVC_P_20:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:SD2","text":"Simplicitas divina non tollit omnem pluralitatem realem, sed tantum pluralitatem quae essentiam divideret, accidentibus augeret, aut subiectum compositum constitueret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_20:A5","CH_GGVC_P_20:A6","CH_GGVC_P_20:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:SD3","text":"Realitas relationis divinae ab immanenti origine dependet, non ab extensione causali Dei ad creaturas neque a multiplicatione obiectorum intellectorum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_20:A4","CH_GGVC_P_20:A10","CH_GGVC_P_20:A11","CH_GGVC_P_20:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:SD4","text":"Numerus relationum realium non a copia nominum relativorum, sed a numero processuum immanentium et oppositionum originis deducitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_20:A11","CH_GGVC_P_20:A12","CH_GGVC_P_20:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet quod relatio realis necessario esse accidentale, compositionem, vel additionem ad essentiam inducat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet quod omnis distinctio realis distinctionem essentiarum vel substantiarum absolutarum exigat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet quod identitas rei cum essentia divina oppositiones relationum inter se dissolvat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet quod causalitas exterior aut pluralitas idearum relationes reales novas in Deo constituere possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:SNX1","text":"Essentia divina nullo termino exteriori constituitur, determinatur, vel realiter dependet; relatio divina interna est sine heteronomia divinae naturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:SNX2","text":"Non admittitur alia res absoluta intra Deum quae relationes personales fundet; fundamentum earum est una essentia secundum processiones oppositas intellecta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:SNX3","text":"Non admittitur reflexio intellectualis infinita quae actus, respectus et relationes reales in Deo sine termino multiplicet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0080","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:FGS1","text":"Respectus significatus → fundamentum in natura rerum → relatio realis; respectus significatus → sola collatio rationis → relatio rationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:FGS2","text":"Processio intra eandem naturam → ordo realis principii et procedentis → respectus originis reales.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:FGS3","text":"Relatio realis in Deo + absentia accidentis → esse relationis idem esse essentiae; significatio respectus oppositi → distinctio rationis a nomine essentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:FGS4","text":"Relatio realis → oppositio relativa → distinctio realis relativa; unitas essentiae → exclusio distinctionis absolutae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:FGS5","text":"Relatio realis → fundamentum in quantitate vel actione; exclusio quantitatis et actionis exterioris → duae processiones immanentes → binae oppositiones → quattuor relationes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AF1","text":"Dubium de realitate relationum divinarum → distinctio respectus naturalis et respectus rationalis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AF2","text":"Processio in identitate naturae → relatio originis realis → exclusio paternitatis et filiationis mere nominalis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AF3","text":"Relatio realis in Deo → identificatio eius esse cum esse essentiae → exclusio accidentis et appositionis exterioris.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AF4","text":"Identitas relationum cum essentia → oppositio relativa → distinctio realis personarum sine divisione essentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AF5","text":"Fundamenta relationum realium → exclusio quantitatis et exterioritatis creaturalis → duae processiones → quattuor relationes reales.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:ME1","text":"Relatio realis a conditione accidentis separatur, quia esse accidentale in creaturis translatum in Deo esse substantiale recipit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:ME2","text":"Unitas et pluralitas desinunt esse oppositiones simplices: unitas ad essentiam absolutam, pluralitas ad respectus oppositos refertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:ME3","text":"Ordo internus processionum et ordo externus creationis secundum reciprocam realitatem relationis reclassificantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:ME4","text":"Numerus relationum divinarum a vocabulario relativo ad structuram processuum originis transfertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:ALT1","text":"Superficies: paternitas, filiatio, spiratio et processio ut quattuor relationes reales in Deo determinantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:ALT2","text":"Structura: relatio dividitur secundum fundamentum reale vel rationalem, et realitas divina relationis identificatur cum essentia sine accidentali inhaerentia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:ALT3","text":"Metastrategia: objectiones solvuntur distinguendo modum significandi relativum ab esse rei, identitatem essentiae ab oppositione relativa, et cognitionem plurium ab ontologica multiplicatione divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:ALT4","text":"Ontologia/Tempus: Deus ponitur simplex et non compositus, uno actu intelligens, cum relationibus originis realibus quae essentiam non mutant nec temporaliter multiplicant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad restituendam seriem principalem: fundamentum relationis, realitas processionum, identitas relationis cum essentia, distinctio oppositorum, et numerus quaternarius.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:MRT2","text":"Firmitas maxima systematis consistit in distinctione inter esse relationis et rationem qua relatio ad oppositum significatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:MRT3","text":"Commissura praecipua est transitus ab oppositione relativa ad distinctionem realem relativam, qui distinctionem sine pluralitate absoluta sustinere debet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:MRT4","text":"Quaestio localiter non demonstrat cur processiones divinae sint duae tantum, sed hanc condicionem ut supra ostensam ad numerum relationum concludendum assumit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:MRT5","text":"Numeratio quaternaria pendet ab exhaustione fundamentorum relationis et ab exclusione respectuum idealium, intellectualium, identitatis, similitudinis et aequalitatis ut relationum realium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:MRT6","text":"Distinctio inter relationes et origines manet terminologice tenuis, quia spiratio et processio nominibus etiam ipsas processiones vel origines significare dicuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:MRT7","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat, si error Sabellianus intelligitur ut terminus negativus systematis, non ut demonstratio independens realitatis relationum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T160637523929Z_44579a43","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 28","source_sha256":"17d48d1ef06713c5f875bcf7f3c699e0abfd97869869faa4db50333c25533d86","sha256":"06e7bd8e1d19e170a90955dcf05b50788209fa11f43161a9f79d10e0f2a40e83","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T16:06:37.525212+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AS1","text":"The question examines how divine relations can be real, while the absolute simplicity and unity of the divine essence are preserved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AS2","text":"The metaphysical-reconstructive argument first divides relation into a real respect and a respect constituted by reason alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AS3","text":"Processions within the same nature are posited as the foundation whereby respects of origin become real in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AS4","text":"The reality of relation is reconciled with simplicity by distinguishing the being of relation, which is the very being of the essence, from its relative notion, which is opposed to another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AS5","text":"Relative opposition produces a real distinction of relations without an absolute distinction of essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AS6","text":"The number of real relations is restricted to two immanent processions and to two opposed relations per procession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A1","text":"Relation, according to its proper notion, signifies a respect to another, not a thing inhering in a subject through itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A2","text":"A respect is real when it is founded in the very nature of things through an order and inclination toward one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A3","text":"A respect is of reason alone when the intellect, by comparing one thing to another, forms an order that is not founded in things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A4","text":"What proceeds from another in identity of nature with its principle agrees with it in the same order and receives a real respect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A5","text":"Nothing that is really in God has accidental being or being other than the divine essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A6","text":"A relation really existing in God is one thing with the divine essence, but signifies a respect to an opposite that the name of essence does not signify.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A7","text":"The notion of real relation includes relative opposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A8","text":"Relative opposition requires a real distinction according to the relative thing, not a distinction according to the absolute thing that is the essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A9","text":"Every real relation is founded either upon quantity or upon action and passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A10","text":"There is no quantity in God, and God's relation to creatures is not really in God, because the creature proceeds from Him in diversity of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A11","text":"Only two immanent processions are posited in God: the procession of the Word according to intellect and the procession of love according to will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A12","text":"Each immanent procession requires two opposed relations: the relation of the principle and the relation of that which proceeds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:A13","text":"God understands all things by one act; therefore repeated acts of understanding do not multiply intelligible relations in God to infinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0080","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:NX1","text":"A divine relation is not an accident inhering in the essence, nor something affixed extrinsically to the essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:NX2","text":"Not every signified relation is a real relation; relation of identity taken simply and comparisons of the mind are of reason alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:NX3","text":"The identity of the divine nature does not make paternity and filiation relations of reason alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:NX4","text":"The relation of creator to creature is not a real relation in God, although the creature really depends upon God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:NX5","text":"The plurality of divine relations is not a plurality of essences, accidents, or absolute forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:NX6","text":"Relations of intellect to intellect, of will to what is willed, ideas, equality, likeness, and identity do not add real divine relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0084","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:D1","text":"Paternity and filiation are real relations in God, because divine generation is posited as a procession within identity of nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_20:A2","CH_GGVC_P_20:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:D2","text":"The creature is really related to God, but God is not really related to the creature, because the dependence of the creature pertains to the divine order without a reciprocal dependence of God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_20:A2","CH_GGVC_P_20:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:D3","text":"Divine relations can at once be the same as the essence according to the thing and distinct from one another according to relative opposition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_20:A5","CH_GGVC_P_20:A6","CH_GGVC_P_20:A7","CH_GGVC_P_20:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:D4","text":"The number of real divine relations is four: paternity, filiation, spiration, and procession.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_20:A9","CH_GGVC_P_20:A10","CH_GGVC_P_20:A11","CH_GGVC_P_20:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:D5","text":"The diversity of the respects of father to son and of son to father remains, even if the same medium or the same path between the termini is considered.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_20:A7","CH_GGVC_P_20:A8","CH_GGVC_P_20:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0086","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:SD1","text":"The real Trinity is situated in the order of relative oppositions, while divine unity is situated in the order of absolute essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_20:A5","CH_GGVC_P_20:A6","CH_GGVC_P_20:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:SD2","text":"Divine simplicity does not remove every real plurality, but only the plurality that would divide the essence, increase it by accidents, or constitute a composite subject.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_20:A5","CH_GGVC_P_20:A6","CH_GGVC_P_20:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:SD3","text":"The reality of divine relation depends upon immanent origin, not upon God's causal extension to creatures nor upon a multiplication of objects understood.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_20:A4","CH_GGVC_P_20:A10","CH_GGVC_P_20:A11","CH_GGVC_P_20:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:SD4","text":"The number of real relations is deduced not from the abundance of relative names, but from the number of immanent processions and oppositions of origin.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_20:A11","CH_GGVC_P_20:A12","CH_GGVC_P_20:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:DNX1","text":"It must be false that real relation necessarily introduces accidental being, composition, or addition to the essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:DNX2","text":"It must be false that every real distinction requires a distinction of essences or absolute substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:DNX3","text":"It must be false that the identity of a thing with the divine essence dissolves the oppositions of relations among themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:DNX4","text":"It must be false that external causality or a plurality of ideas can constitute new real relations in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:SNX1","text":"The divine essence is not constituted, determined, or really dependent upon any external term; divine relation is internal without heteronomy of the divine nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:SNX2","text":"No other absolute thing within God that founds the personal relations is admitted; their foundation is the one essence understood according to opposed processions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:SNX3","text":"An infinite intellectual reflection that multiplies acts, respects, and real relations in God without limit is not admitted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0080","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:FGS1","text":"Signified respect → foundation in the nature of things → real relation; signified respect → comparison of reason alone → relation of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:FGS2","text":"Procession within the same nature → real order of principle and that which proceeds → real respects of origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:FGS3","text":"Real relation in God + absence of accident → being of relation identical with being of essence; signification of an opposed respect → distinction in notion from the name of essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:FGS4","text":"Real relation → relative opposition → relative real distinction; unity of essence → exclusion of absolute distinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:FGS5","text":"Real relation → foundation in quantity or action; exclusion of quantity and external action → two immanent processions → twofold oppositions → four relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AF1","text":"Doubt concerning the reality of divine relations → distinction between natural respect and rational respect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AF2","text":"Procession in identity of nature → real relation of origin → exclusion of merely nominal paternity and filiation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AF3","text":"Real relation in God → identification of its being with the being of the essence → exclusion of accident and external addition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AF4","text":"Identity of relations with essence → relative opposition → real distinction of persons without division of essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:AF5","text":"Foundations of real relations → exclusion of quantity and creaturely exteriority → two processions → four real relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:ME1","text":"Real relation is separated from the condition of accident, because accidental being transferred from creatures receives substantial being in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:ME2","text":"Unity and plurality cease to be simple oppositions: unity is referred to absolute essence, plurality to opposed respects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:ME3","text":"The internal order of processions and the external order of creation are reclassified according to the reciprocal reality of relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:ME4","text":"The number of divine relations is transferred from relative vocabulary to the structure of processes of origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:ALT1","text":"Surface: paternity, filiation, spiration, and procession are determined as four real relations in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:ALT2","text":"Structure: relation is divided according to a real or rational foundation, and the divine reality of relation is identified with the essence without accidental inherence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:ALT3","text":"Metastrategy: objections are resolved by distinguishing the relative mode of signifying from the being of the thing, identity of essence from relative opposition, and cognition of many things from divine ontological multiplication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:ALT4","text":"Ontology/Time: God is posited as simple and non-composite, understanding by one act, with real relations of origin that neither change the essence nor multiply temporally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the principal sequence: foundation of relation, reality of processions, identity of relation with essence, distinction of opposites, and the quaternary number.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:MRT2","text":"The greatest strength of the system consists in the distinction between the being of relation and the notion by which relation signifies an opposite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:MRT3","text":"The principal juncture is the transition from relative opposition to relative real distinction, which must sustain distinction without absolute plurality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:MRT4","text":"The question does not demonstrate locally why divine processions are only two, but assumes this condition as shown above in order to conclude the number of relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:MRT5","text":"The quaternary enumeration depends upon an exhaustive account of the foundations of relation and upon the exclusion of ideal, intellectual, identity, likeness, and equality respects as real relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:MRT6","text":"The distinction between relations and origins remains terminologically tenuous, because spiration and procession are also said by their names to signify the processions or origins themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_20:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text if the Sabellian error is understood as the negative terminus of the system, not as an independent demonstration of the reality of relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_20:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_20@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_P_20:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 29","title":"First Part · Question 28","context":"Chapter 29","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 28"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 28","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 29","order":30,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_P_21"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_21@R001","sha256":"09b5818b053fef63f2d44a0715a717021428934fbe5298401491597ea4a1beeb","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:AS1","text":"Quaestio constituit quid nomen personae significet, quibus limitibus a nominibus vicinis distinguatur, et qua ratione de divinis praedicetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:AS2","text":"Argumentatio semantico-metaphysica reconstructiva a singularitate substantiae procedit ad rationem personae tamquam subsistentis rationalis naturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:AS3","text":"Distinctio essentiae, subsistentiae, rei naturae et hypostasis separare debet principia speciei, modum essendi per se, suppositum naturae et subiectum accidentium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:AS4","text":"Applicatio ad Deum nomen personae a materiali, accidentali et discursivo modo creaturarum purgat, retinens perfectionem subsistendi intellectualiter et incommunicabiliter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:AS5","text":"Determinatio ultima personas divinas constituit relationes originis subsistentes, quae essentiam non multiplicant sed personas realiter distinguunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A1","text":"Substantia per seipsam individuatur, accidentia vero per substantiale subiectum individuantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A2","text":"Substantiae rationales dominium sui actus habent et per se agunt, non solum aguntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A3","text":"Persona significat substantiam individuam rationalis naturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A4","text":"Ratio personae requirit suppositum incommunicabile et completum, non universale, non partem, nec naturam alteri assumptibilem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A5","text":"Essentia significat quidditatem definitam per principia speciei, non principia individualia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A6","text":"Subsistentia significat esse per se et non in alio; hypostasis significat suppositum accidentibus substans; res naturae significat suppositum naturae communi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A7","text":"Persona restringit ad substantias rationales id quod subsistentia, res naturae et hypostasis in toto genere substantiae communiter significant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A8","text":"Legitima applicatio nominis de Deo attendit id ad quod nomen imponitur significandum, non solum originem vocabuli aut usum litteralem Scripturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A9","text":"Omne quod perfectionis est Deo attribuitur, quia essentia divina omnem perfectionem continet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0065","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A10","text":"In Deo rationalitas significat naturam intellectualem, individuum incommunicabilitatem, et substantia esse per se.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A11","text":"Distinctio in divinis non fit nisi per relationes originis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A12","text":"Relatio divina non est accidens subiecto inhaerens, sed est ipsa essentia divina et ideo subsistit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A13","text":"Individuum significat quod in se est indistinctum et ab aliis distinctum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0088","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:NX1","text":"Persona non definit hoc singulare vel illud singulare, sed rationem communem singularitatis rationalis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:NX2","text":"Persona non competit generi, speciei vel naturae universali.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:NX3","text":"Persona non competit animae separatae neque alicui parti humanae, quia pars retinet ordinem unionis ad totum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:NX4","text":"Persona non est idem quod essentia in compositis, quia hypostasis et persona principia individualia supra rationem essentiae addunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:NX5","text":"Persona divina non dicitur secundum solam metaphoram scenicam neque prohibetur propter absentiam vocis in textu Scripturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0069","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:NX6","text":"Individuatio divina non est materialis, rationalitas divina non est discursiva, et substantia divina non significat subiectum accidentium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:NX7","text":"Persona divina non significat essentiam solam neque relationem positam ex nudo usu conciliari.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0086","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:NX8","text":"Persona divina relationem non significat per modum relationis accidentalis, sed per modum hypostasis subsistentis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0093","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:D1","text":"Singulare substantiale rationalis naturae nomen proprium personae requirit, quia per dominium sui actus excellit supra cetera singularia.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A1","CH_GGVC_P_21:A2","CH_GGVC_P_21:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:D2","text":"Persona in rebus compositis ab essentia distinguitur, quia suppositum individuatam materiam et formam includit, dum essentia principia speciei significat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A4","CH_GGVC_P_21:A5","CH_GGVC_P_21:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:D3","text":"Nomen personae de Deo vere dicitur, si perfectiones quas significat a conditionibus creatis separantur et eminentius retinentur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A8","CH_GGVC_P_21:A9","CH_GGVC_P_21:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:D4","text":"Persona divina est relatio originis subsistens in natura divina.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A11","CH_GGVC_P_21:A12","CH_GGVC_P_21:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:D5","text":"Pluralitas personarum divinarum exprimit distinctionem relationum originis sine pluralitate essentiarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A11","CH_GGVC_P_21:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:D6","text":"Nomen personae de Deo et creaturis neque pure aequivoce neque univoce praedicatur, quia communis ratio sub diverso modo essendi determinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A3","CH_GGVC_P_21:A10","CH_GGVC_P_21:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0099","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:SD1","text":"Unitas essentiae divinae et pluralitas personarum componuntur, si distinctio personalis ad subsistentes relationes originis refertur potius quam ad essentias distinctas.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:D2","CH_GGVC_P_21:D4","CH_GGVC_P_21:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:SD2","text":"Persona fit instrumentum semanticum quo ratio communis substantiae rationalis transponitur in divinam simplicitatem sine retentione materiae, accidentium aut discursivitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:D1","CH_GGVC_P_21:D3","CH_GGVC_P_21:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:SD3","text":"Controversia de nominibus divinis solvitur non per arbitrium vocabuli, sed per distinctionem inter originem nominis, rem significatam, modum significandi et usum doctrinalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A8","CH_GGVC_P_21:D3","CH_GGVC_P_21:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0091","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:DNX1","text":"Individuatio non potest formaliter reduci ad materiam, quia in Deo incommunicabilitas obtinet sine principio materiali.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:DNX2","text":"Relatio quae personas divinas distinguit non potest esse dispositio extrinseca vel accidens superadditum, quia divina essentia simplex manet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:DNX3","text":"Auctoritas sermonis theologi non pendet a mera praesentia vocis in Scriptura, sed a fidelitate ad rem Scriptura assertam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:DNX4","text":"Singularitas personalis non est solum separatio numerica, sed modus subsistendi quo suppositum est incommunicabile et ab aliis distinctum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:SNX1","text":"Systema excludit tam essentiam divinam indistinctam quae omnem pluralitatem personalem absorbeat quam pluralitatem quae essentiam divinam in plures essentias dividat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0099","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:SNX2","text":"Systema excludit univocitatem creaturae et Dei, quia significatio communis nominis personae non importat identitatem modi essendi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0099","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:SNX3","text":"Systema excludit notionem personae divinae tamquam absoluti solitarii, quia distinctio personalis constituitur relatione originis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:FGS1","text":"Individuatio substantiae per se → imminutio dependentiae ab alio → hypostasis seu substantia prima.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:FGS2","text":"Hypostasis rationalis cum dominio sui actus → substantia individua rationalis naturae → persona.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A1","CH_GGVC_P_21:A2","CH_GGVC_P_21:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:FGS3","text":"Distinctio essentiae a supposito → separatio principiorum speciei a principiis individualibus → persona non reducitur ad essentiam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A4","CH_GGVC_P_21:A5","CH_GGVC_P_21:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:FGS4","text":"Purificatio rationis personae a materia, accidentibus et discursu → retentio intellectualitatis, esse per se et incommunicabilitatis → praedicatio divina eminens.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A8","CH_GGVC_P_21:A9","CH_GGVC_P_21:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:FGS5","text":"Distinctio per originem + relatio essentialis et subsistens → relatio subsistens → persona divina distincta sine divisione essentiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A11","CH_GGVC_P_21:A12","CH_GGVC_P_21:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:AF1","text":"Processiones et relationes praecognitae → quaestio de persona in communi → definitio substantiae individuae rationalis naturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:AF2","text":"Definitio personae → distinctio essentiae, subsistentiae, rei naturae et hypostasis → determinatio suppositi rationalis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:AF3","text":"Perfectio subsistentis rationalis → imminutio conditionum creaturarum → legitima praedicatio personae de Deo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:AF4","text":"Individuum ut distinctum in natura → distinctio divina per relationes originis → persona divina ut relatio subsistens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:AF5","text":"Relatio subsistens → pluralitas personalis → unitas essentiae divinae salva.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:ME1","text":"Singularitas substantialis reclassificatur ut principium proprii modi subsistendi, non ut mera particularitas numerica.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:ME2","text":"Essentia, subsistentia, res naturae et hypostasis distribuuntur secundum diversa obiecta significationis, ita ut identitas vocabularum apparens solvatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:ME3","text":"Persona divina reclassificatur ab absoluto essentiali vel relatione accidentali ad relationem originis subsistentem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0093","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:ME4","text":"Nomen theologicum fit aestimandum ex re significata et congruentia fidei, non ex etymologia, lingua originaria vel sola littera vocabuli scripturarii.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:ALT1","text":"Surface — Persona definitur ut substantia individua rationalis naturae et distinguitur a partibus, universalibus et essentia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:ALT2","text":"Structural — Ratio personae dependet a distinctione inter principia speciei, principia individuationis, esse per se, suppositum naturae et subiectum accidentium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Conflictus de nominibus divinis resolvitur per distinctionem inter rem significatam, modum significandi, originem vocabuli et adaptationem doctrinalem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0091","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Divina simplicitas non excludit distinctionem personalem, quia relationes originis sunt essentiales et subsistentes, non accidentales.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:MRT1","text":"Axiomata de individuatione, dominio actuum, suppositione, significatione nominum et relationibus originis sufficiunt ad restitui motum a persona communi ad personam divinam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:MRT2","text":"Nodus firmissimus systematis est connexio inter individuum ut ab aliis distinctum et relationem originis ut principium distinctionis divinae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A11","CH_GGVC_P_21:A12","CH_GGVC_P_21:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:MRT3","text":"Systema interne cohaeret quia materialis individuationis ratio creaturis propria non transfertur ad Deum, sed incommunicabilitas retinetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A4","CH_GGVC_P_21:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:MRT4","text":"Commissura fragilis manet in transitu a substantia individua communiter significata ad relationem subsistentem divinam, qui ab identitate divinae essentiae et relationis dependet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A3","CH_GGVC_P_21:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0093","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:MRT5","text":"Modus exactus quo nomen personae de Deo et creaturis proportionaliter conveniat non plene intra quaestionem explicatur, quamvis univocitas et aequivocitas simplices excludantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0086","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0087","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0099","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:MRT6","text":"Restitutio servat motum textus, quia definitio, distinctio terminorum, imminutio condicionum creaturarum et determinatio relationis subsistentis ordinem generativum constituunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:FGS1","CH_GGVC_P_21:FGS2","CH_GGVC_P_21:FGS3","CH_GGVC_P_21:FGS4","CH_GGVC_P_21:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T160701634425Z_4e1a8c5c","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 29","source_sha256":"09b5818b053fef63f2d44a0715a717021428934fbe5298401491597ea4a1beeb","sha256":"b9db352a59f38b68d10d408602682bfa0790c09d60e19c5ea9d33a912b855d56","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T16:07:01.635722+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:AS1","text":"The question establishes what the name of person signifies, by what boundaries it is distinguished from neighboring names, and in what way it is predicated of the divine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:AS2","text":"The reconstructive semantic-metaphysical argument proceeds from the singularity of substance to the account of person as a subsistent of rational nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:AS3","text":"The distinction of essence, subsistence, thing of a nature, and hypostasis must separate the principles of species, the mode of being through itself, the suppositum of a nature, and the subject of accidents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:AS4","text":"The application to God purges the name of person of the material, accidental, and discursive mode of creatures, while retaining the perfection of subsisting intellectually and incommunicably.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:AS5","text":"The final determination constitutes the divine persons as subsisting relations of origin, which do not multiply the essence but really distinguish the persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A1","text":"Substance is individuated through itself, whereas accidents are individuated through a substantial subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A2","text":"Rational substances have dominion over their own acts and act through themselves; they are not merely acted upon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A3","text":"Person signifies an individual substance of rational nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A4","text":"The account of person requires an incommunicable and complete suppositum, not a universal, not a part, nor a nature assumable by another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A5","text":"Essence signifies a quiddity defined by the principles of species, not by individual principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A6","text":"Subsistence signifies being through itself and not in another; hypostasis signifies a suppositum underlying accidents; thing of a nature signifies the suppositum of a common nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A7","text":"Person restricts to rational substances what subsistence, thing of a nature, and hypostasis commonly signify in the whole genus of substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A8","text":"The legitimate application of a name to God attends to that which the name is imposed to signify, not merely to the origin of the word or to the literal usage of Scripture.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A9","text":"Everything that pertains to perfection is attributed to God, because the divine essence contains every perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0065","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A10","text":"In God, rationality signifies intellectual nature, individual signifies incommunicability, and substance signifies being through itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A11","text":"Distinction in the divine occurs only through relations of origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A12","text":"A divine relation is not an accident inhering in a subject, but is the divine essence itself and therefore subsists.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:A13","text":"Individual signifies that which is undivided in itself and distinct from others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0088","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:NX1","text":"Person does not define this singular or that singular, but the common account of rational singularity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:NX2","text":"Person does not belong to genus, species, or universal nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:NX3","text":"Person does not belong to the separated soul or to any human part, because a part retains an order of union to the whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:NX4","text":"Person is not the same as essence in composite things, because hypostasis and person add individual principles beyond the account of essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:NX5","text":"A divine person is not said according to mere scenic metaphor, nor is it prohibited on account of the absence of the word in the text of Scripture.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0069","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:NX6","text":"Divine individuation is not material, divine rationality is not discursive, and divine substance does not signify a subject of accidents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:NX7","text":"A divine person signifies neither essence alone nor a relation posited from bare conciliar usage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0086","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:NX8","text":"A divine person does not signify relation in the mode of accidental relation, but in the mode of subsisting hypostasis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0093","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:D1","text":"A substantial singular of rational nature requires the proper name of person, because through dominion over its own act it excels above other singulars.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A1","CH_GGVC_P_21:A2","CH_GGVC_P_21:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:D2","text":"In composite things, person is distinguished from essence, because the suppositum includes individuated matter and form, whereas essence signifies the principles of species.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A4","CH_GGVC_P_21:A5","CH_GGVC_P_21:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:D3","text":"The name of person is truly said of God if the perfections it signifies are separated from creaturely conditions and retained more eminently.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A8","CH_GGVC_P_21:A9","CH_GGVC_P_21:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:D4","text":"A divine person is a subsisting relation of origin in the divine nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A11","CH_GGVC_P_21:A12","CH_GGVC_P_21:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:D5","text":"The plurality of divine persons expresses a distinction of relations of origin without a plurality of essences.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A11","CH_GGVC_P_21:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:D6","text":"The name of person is predicated of God and creatures neither purely equivocally nor univocally, because the common account is determined under a diverse mode of being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A3","CH_GGVC_P_21:A10","CH_GGVC_P_21:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0099","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:SD1","text":"The unity of the divine essence and the plurality of persons are reconciled if personal distinction is referred to subsisting relations of origin rather than to distinct essences.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:D2","CH_GGVC_P_21:D4","CH_GGVC_P_21:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:SD2","text":"Person becomes the semantic instrument by which the common account of rational substance is transposed into divine simplicity without retaining matter, accidents, or discursivity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:D1","CH_GGVC_P_21:D3","CH_GGVC_P_21:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:SD3","text":"The controversy concerning divine names is resolved not by arbitrariness of terminology, but by the distinction between the origin of the name, the thing signified, the mode of signifying, and doctrinal usage.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A8","CH_GGVC_P_21:D3","CH_GGVC_P_21:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0091","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:DNX1","text":"Individuation cannot be formally reduced to matter, because in God incommunicability obtains without a material principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:DNX2","text":"The relation that distinguishes the divine persons cannot be an extrinsic disposition or a superadded accident, because the divine essence remains simple.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:DNX3","text":"The authority of the theologian's speech does not depend on the mere presence of a word in Scripture, but on fidelity to the thing asserted by Scripture.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:DNX4","text":"Personal singularity is not merely numerical separation, but a mode of subsisting whereby the suppositum is incommunicable and distinct from others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:SNX1","text":"The system excludes both an undistinguished divine essence that absorbs all personal plurality and a plurality that divides the divine essence into several essences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0099","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:SNX2","text":"The system excludes the univocity of creature and God, because the common signification of the name person does not entail identity of mode of being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0099","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:SNX3","text":"The system excludes the notion of divine person as a solitary absolute, because personal distinction is constituted by a relation of origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:FGS1","text":"Individuation of substance through itself → diminution of dependence on another → hypostasis or primary substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:FGS2","text":"Rational hypostasis with dominion over its own act → individual substance of rational nature → person.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A1","CH_GGVC_P_21:A2","CH_GGVC_P_21:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:FGS3","text":"Distinction of essence from suppositum → separation of the principles of species from individual principles → person is not reduced to essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A4","CH_GGVC_P_21:A5","CH_GGVC_P_21:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:FGS4","text":"Purification of the account of person from matter, accidents, and discourse → retention of intellectuality, being through itself, and incommunicability → eminent divine predication.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A8","CH_GGVC_P_21:A9","CH_GGVC_P_21:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:FGS5","text":"Distinction through origin + essential and subsisting relation → subsisting relation → distinct divine person without division of essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A11","CH_GGVC_P_21:A12","CH_GGVC_P_21:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:AF1","text":"Processions and relations previously known → question concerning person in common → definition of individual substance of rational nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:AF2","text":"Definition of person → distinction of essence, subsistence, thing of a nature, and hypostasis → determination of rational suppositum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:AF3","text":"Perfection of the rational subsistent → diminution of creaturely conditions → legitimate predication of person concerning God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:AF4","text":"Individual as distinct in nature → divine distinction through relations of origin → divine person as subsisting relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:AF5","text":"Subsisting relation → personal plurality → unity of the divine essence preserved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:ME1","text":"Substantial singularity is reclassified as the principle of a proper mode of subsisting, not as mere numerical particularity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:ME2","text":"Essence, subsistence, thing of a nature, and hypostasis are distributed according to diverse objects of signification, so that the apparent identity of terms is resolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:ME3","text":"Divine person is reclassified from an essential absolute or accidental relation to a subsisting relation of origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0093","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:ME4","text":"A theological name comes to be assessed by the thing signified and the congruence of faith, not by etymology, original language, or the letter alone of the scriptural term.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:ALT1","text":"Surface — Person is defined as an individual substance of rational nature and distinguished from parts, universals, and essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:ALT2","text":"Structural — The account of person depends on the distinction between principles of species, principles of individuation, being through itself, the suppositum of a nature, and the subject of accidents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The conflict concerning divine names is resolved through the distinction between the thing signified, the mode of signifying, the origin of the word, and doctrinal adaptation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0091","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Divine simplicity does not exclude personal distinction, because relations of origin are essential and subsisting, not accidental.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:MRT1","text":"The axioms concerning individuation, dominion over acts, supposition, the signification of names, and relations of origin suffice to restore the movement from person in common to divine person.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:MRT2","text":"The firmest node of the system is the connection between the individual as distinct from others and the relation of origin as the principle of divine distinction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A11","CH_GGVC_P_21:A12","CH_GGVC_P_21:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:MRT3","text":"The system coheres internally because the account of material individuation proper to creatures is not transferred to God, while incommunicability is retained.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A4","CH_GGVC_P_21:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:MRT4","text":"A fragile juncture remains in the transition from an individual substance commonly signified to a subsisting divine relation, which depends upon the identity of divine essence and relation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:A3","CH_GGVC_P_21:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0093","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:MRT5","text":"The exact mode by which the name person belongs proportionally to God and creatures is not fully explained within the question, although simple univocity and equivocity are excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0086","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0087","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0099","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_21:MRT6","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text, because definition, distinction of terms, diminution of creaturely conditions, and determination of the subsisting relation constitute the generative order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_21:FGS1","CH_GGVC_P_21:FGS2","CH_GGVC_P_21:FGS3","CH_GGVC_P_21:FGS4","CH_GGVC_P_21:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_21:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_21@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_P_21:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 30","title":"First Part · Question 29","context":"Chapter 30","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 29"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 29","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 30","order":31,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_P_22"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_22@R001","sha256":"6afe7b21c854bd4ef12398cc883b1a67f5df30574f594c5ebc7b25f0b2c6d3c7","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AS1","text":"Quaestio constituitur utrum pluralitas personalis, numerus ternarius et communitas nominis personae sine multiplicatione essentiae, compositione divina vel universalitate intelligi possint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AS2","text":"Textus affirmat personas divinas esse relationes subsistentes realiter oppositas, quarum ordinatio tres tantum personas constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AS3","text":"Argumentatio metaphysico-semantica est: distinctiones inter essentiam et suppositum, absolutum et relativum, quantitatem et transcendentale, rem et rationem adhibet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AS4","text":"Motus initialis apparentem contradictionem inter divinam simplicitatem et pluralitatem dissolvit per transitum a substantia multiplicata ad relationem subsistentem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AS5","text":"Numerus personarum non ex nuda relationum enumeratione, sed ex oppositione relativa et communicatione relationum non oppositarum determinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AS6","text":"Termini numerales divini a numero quantitativo transferuntur ad multitudinem transcendentem, quae res significat et divisionem removet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AS7","text":"Communitas nominis personae in ratione modi subsistendi ponitur, non in re una communi neque in genere, specie vel universali.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A1","text":"Persona in divinis significat relationem ut rem subsistentem in natura divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A2","text":"Substantia in definitione personae suppositum individuale significat, non essentiam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A3","text":"Proprietates relativae divinae et subsistunt et realiter ab invicem distinguuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","text":"Distinctio realis relationum divinarum non est nisi secundum oppositionem relativam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A5","text":"Relationes oppositae ad diversas personas pertinent, relationes autem non oppositae ad eandem personam pertinent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A6","text":"Paternitas et filiatio sunt relationes relative oppositae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A7","text":"Spiratio et processio sunt relationes sibi invicem oppositae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A8","text":"Spiratio nec paternitati nec filiationi relative opponitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A9","text":"In Deo secundum rem una tantum operatio est, quae est eius essentia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A10","text":"Una eademque bonitas est patris et spiritus sancti.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A11","text":"In divinis praedicatur multitudo transcendens, non numerus qui est species quantitatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A12","text":"Unum transcendentale non addit supra ens nisi negationem divisionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A13","text":"Multitudo transcendens res multas significat cum indivisione circa unamquamque.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A14","text":"Nomen personae est nomen rei subsistentis, non nomen negationis neque intentionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A15","text":"Persona tribus divinae communis est communitate rationis sicut individuum vagum, non communitate rei vel generis et speciei.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A16","text":"Personae divinae unum esse habent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A17","text":"Processio verbi secundum intellectum est ipsa generatio secundum naturam, processio autem amoris generatio non est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A18","text":"Tres personae eandem magnitudinem habent, ideo numerus earum mensuram realem non imponit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX1","text":"Plures personae non sunt plures essentiae neque tres substantiae secundum essentiam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX2","text":"Proprietates absolutae, ut bonitas et sapientia, personas non distinguunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX3","text":"Summa unitas divina omnem pluralitatem absolute dictam excludit, non pluralitatem relationum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX4","text":"Numerus personarum non introducit totum et partes in re divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX5","text":"Quattuor relationes divinae quattuor personas non efficiunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX6","text":"Verbum non est quarta persona praeter Filium, quia processio intellectualis iam est generatio Filii.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX7","text":"Perfectio infinita divina neque infinitas operationes intrinsecas neque infinitas personas postulat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX8","text":"Termini numerales in Deo non significant numerum quantitativum, accidens additum vel mensuram rerum divinarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX9","text":"Termini numerales non sunt merae remotiones sine re significata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX10","text":"Persona communis non est una res tribus communis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX11","text":"Communitas personae nec universalitatem nec genus nec speciem divinam constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:D1","text":"Pluralitas relationum relativarum subsistentium pluralitatem personarum intra unam naturam divinam importat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A1","CH_GGVC_P_22:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:D2","text":"Paternitas et filiatio duas personas constituunt, spiratio utrique competit, et processio personam spiritus sancti constituit; ideo tres tantum sunt personae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A5","CH_GGVC_P_22:A6","CH_GGVC_P_22:A7","CH_GGVC_P_22:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:D3","text":"Spiratio relatio est, sed proprietas personalis non est, quia patri et filio communiter convenit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A5","CH_GGVC_P_22:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:D4","text":"Differentia inter processionem verbi et processionem amoris duas personas procedentes explicat, non duplicem personam intellectualem vel naturalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:D5","text":"Numerus ternarius personarum ordinem relationum indicat, sed immensitatem vel magnitudinem personarum non metitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A11","CH_GGVC_P_22:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:D6","text":"Dicere essentiam unam vel personas plures significat rem de qua dicitur et negationem divisionis ei propriam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A12","CH_GGVC_P_22:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:D7","text":"Unitas multitudinem non removet, sed divisionem; multitudo autem unitatem singulorum constituentium non removet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A12","CH_GGVC_P_22:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:D8","text":"Persona simul incommunicabilis in singulis et communis secundum rationem esse potest, quia communicatur modus subsistendi distincte, non ipsa persona singularis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A14","CH_GGVC_P_22:A15","CH_GGVC_P_22:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:SD1","text":"Pluralitas divina reordinatur ut differentia relationum sine partitione essentiae: unitas essentialis et distinctio personalis diversa principia ordinis sunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A1","CH_GGVC_P_22:A3","CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:SD2","text":"Ternarius non est aggregatum trium unitatum aequalium per additionem, sed clausura topologica oppositionum relativarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A5","CH_GGVC_P_22:A6","CH_GGVC_P_22:A7","CH_GGVC_P_22:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:SD3","text":"Praedicatio numeralis divina structuram duplicem habet: fundamentum positivum in rebus significatis et determinationem negativam per indivisionem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A11","CH_GGVC_P_22:A12","CH_GGVC_P_22:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:SD4","text":"Identitas personalis divina non per naturam individuantem, sed per subsistentiam relativam distinctam constituitur; communitas igitur nomen et rationem attingit, non individuum reale.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A1","CH_GGVC_P_22:A14","CH_GGVC_P_22:A15","CH_GGVC_P_22:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:SD5","text":"Multiplicatio processionalis in infinitum sistitur quia bonitas et operatio non sunt principia numerice distincta, dum relationes oppositae solae personas distinguunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A9","CH_GGVC_P_22:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse oportet omnem distinctionem realem distinctionem essentiarum vel substantiarum exigere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A1","CH_GGVC_P_22:A2","CH_GGVC_P_22:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse oportet unitatem et pluralitatem ut opposita absoluta, quorum alterum alterum destruit, se habere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A12","CH_GGVC_P_22:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse oportet omnem numerum esse quantitatem, mensuram vel aggregationem partium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A11","CH_GGVC_P_22:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse oportet omne commune esse universale, genus, speciem aut rem numero unam communicabilem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A14","CH_GGVC_P_22:A15","CH_GGVC_P_22:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse oportet infinitam bonitatem per se exigere infinitam successionem personarum producentium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:SNX1","text":"Praecluditur divina pluralitas ut compositio additiva entium partialium intra totum superius.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A2","CH_GGVC_P_22:A11","CH_GGVC_P_22:A16","CH_GGVC_P_22:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:SNX2","text":"Praecluditur alternativa quae Deum aut solitariam singularitatem aut pluralitatem deorum essentialiter separatorum facit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A1","CH_GGVC_P_22:A3","CH_GGVC_P_22:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:SNX3","text":"Praecluditur translatio immediata categoriarum creaturarum, quantitatis et universalitatis in praedicationem divinam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A11","CH_GGVC_P_22:A14","CH_GGVC_P_22:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:FGS1","text":"Essentia una + relationes reales subsistentes → persona intelligitur ut relatio subsistens, non ut essentia multiplicata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A1","CH_GGVC_P_22:A2","CH_GGVC_P_22:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:FGS2","text":"Oppositio relativa → distinctio personalis; absentia oppositionis → communicatio relationis in eadem persona.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:FGS3","text":"Paternitas ↔ filiatio + spiratio communis patri et filio + processio opposita spirationi → Pater, Filius et Spiritus Sanctus tantum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A6","CH_GGVC_P_22:A7","CH_GGVC_P_22:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:FGS4","text":"Multitudo transcendens + indivisio singulorum → termini numerales significant res divinas sine quantitate, mensura vel partibus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A11","CH_GGVC_P_22:A12","CH_GGVC_P_22:A13","CH_GGVC_P_22:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:FGS5","text":"Res subsistens distincta + communitas rationis → nomen personae tribus dicitur sine una persona reali vel universali divino.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A14","CH_GGVC_P_22:A15","CH_GGVC_P_22:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:FGS6","text":"Una operatio et bonitas + distinctio per relationes tantum → infinitus processus personarum excluditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A9","CH_GGVC_P_22:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AF1","text":"Simplicitas divina apparenter negans pluralitatem → distinctio essentiae et suppositi → persona ut relatio subsistens → pluralitas personarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AF2","text":"Quattuor relationes enumeratae → oppositio relativa ut regula distinctionis → spiratio communis → tres tantum personae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AF3","text":"Numerus materialis recusatus → multitudo transcendens admissa → pluralitas significans res indivisas → numerus sine mensuratione divinae magnitudinis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AF4","text":"Communitas realis recusata → communitas negationis et intentionis recusata → communitas rationis modi subsistendi → persona communis sine universali.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:ME1","text":"Distinctio divina a multiplicatione substantiarum ad oppositionem relationum subsistentium transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A1","CH_GGVC_P_22:A2","CH_GGVC_P_22:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:ME2","text":"Relatio quae personam constituit a relatione quae pluribus personis convenit distinguitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A5","CH_GGVC_P_22:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:ME3","text":"Numerus a quantitate mensurante ad notationem transcendentem rerum indivisarum reclassificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A11","CH_GGVC_P_22:A12","CH_GGVC_P_22:A13","CH_GGVC_P_22:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:ME4","text":"Communitas a re, genere et specie ad rationem communem modi subsistendi reclassificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A14","CH_GGVC_P_22:A15","CH_GGVC_P_22:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:ME5","text":"Unitas essentialis et pluralitas personalis desinunt apparere contradictoriae, quia ad ordines ontologicos et semanticos diversos referuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A3","CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A12","CH_GGVC_P_22:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus immediate determinat utrum plures sint personae, quot sint, quid termini numerales significent, et quomodo persona communis dicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:ALT2","text":"Structural — Ordinatio relationum per subsistentiam, oppositionem et communicationem constituit mechanismum distinctionis personalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A1","CH_GGVC_P_22:A3","CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Argumentatio contradictiones apparentes per mutationem categoriae dissolvit: substantia in suppositum, numerus in transcendentale, commune in rationem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A2","CH_GGVC_P_22:A11","CH_GGVC_P_22:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Realitas divina ponitur una secundum essentiam, operationem et esse, distincta vero secundum relationes non temporales neque successive productas.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A9","CH_GGVC_P_22:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad restituendas quattuor conclusiones principales: pluralitatem personarum, ternarium numerum, sensum non-quantitativum numerorum, et communitatem rationis nominis personae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:MRT2","text":"Firmissimum cardo systematis est identificatio personae cum relatione subsistente et restrictio distinctionis realis ad oppositionem relativam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A1","CH_GGVC_P_22:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:MRT3","text":"Demonstratio ternarii intra hanc unitatem pendet ex doctrinis praemissis de quattuor relationibus et duabus processionibus, quas textus hic supponit potius quam iterum deducit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:MRT4","text":"Commissura fragilis manet in transitu ab oppositione relationum ad regulam quod relatio non opposita eidem personae convenire debeat; regula ponitur ut necessaria, sed fundamentum eius ex praemissis importatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:MRT5","text":"Semantica numeralis positive res significat et negative divisionem removet; distinctio inter rem significatam et negationem additam systemati necessaria est, sed intra articulum non independenter probatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A12","CH_GGVC_P_22:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:MRT6","text":"Communitas rationis personae incommunicabilitatem singularium conservat, sed stabilitas eius nititur distinctione subtili inter nomen rei et rationem communem modi existendi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A14","CH_GGVC_P_22:A15","CH_GGVC_P_22:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:MRT7","text":"Restitutio servat motum verum textus: ab objectione simplicis unitatis ad ontologiam relationis, inde ad clausuram ternarii, deinde ad disciplinam semanticam numeri et communitatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T160726703707Z_93e82421","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 30","source_sha256":"6afe7b21c854bd4ef12398cc883b1a67f5df30574f594c5ebc7b25f0b2c6d3c7","sha256":"644af8869d5db44d07f6b6dde40e5282b3e4d5ee8009e0354d94afc50052847d","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T16:07:26.704660+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AS1","text":"The question is posed whether personal plurality, the ternary number, and the community of the name person can be understood without multiplication of essence, divine composition, or universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AS2","text":"The text affirms that the divine persons are subsistent relations really opposed, whose ordering constitutes only three persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AS3","text":"The argumentation is metaphysical-semantic: it employs distinctions between essence and suppositum, absolute and relative, quantity and transcendental, thing and reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AS4","text":"The initial movement dissolves the apparent contradiction between divine simplicity and plurality by a transition from multiplied substance to subsistent relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AS5","text":"The number of persons is determined not by a bare enumeration of relations, but by relative opposition and the communication of non-opposed relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AS6","text":"Divine numerical terms are transferred from quantitative number to transcendent multitude, which signifies things and removes division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AS7","text":"The community of the name person is placed in the account of a mode of subsisting, not in one common thing nor in genus, species, or universal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A1","text":"Person in the divine signifies relation as a subsistent thing in the divine nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A2","text":"Substance in the definition of person signifies the individual suppositum, not essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A3","text":"Divine relative properties both subsist and are really distinguished from one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","text":"The real distinction of divine relations is only according to relative opposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A5","text":"Opposed relations pertain to different persons, whereas non-opposed relations pertain to the same person.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A6","text":"Paternity and filiation are relatively opposed relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A7","text":"Spiration and procession are relations opposed to one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A8","text":"Spiration is relatively opposed neither to paternity nor to filiation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A9","text":"In God there is, according to reality, only one operation, which is His essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A10","text":"The goodness of the Father and of the Holy Spirit is one and the same.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A11","text":"In the divine, transcendent multitude is predicated, not number which is a species of quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A12","text":"The transcendental one adds to being only the negation of division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A13","text":"Transcendent multitude signifies many things with indivision concerning each one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A14","text":"The name person is a name of a subsistent thing, not a name of negation or intention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A15","text":"Person is common to the three divine persons by a community of reason, like an indeterminate individual, not by a community of thing or of genus and species.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A16","text":"The divine persons have one being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A17","text":"The procession of the Word according to intellect is generation itself according to nature, whereas the procession of love is not generation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:A18","text":"The three persons have the same magnitude; therefore their number does not impose a real measure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX1","text":"Several persons are not several essences nor three substances according to essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX2","text":"Absolute properties, such as goodness and wisdom, do not distinguish persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX3","text":"The highest divine unity excludes every plurality said absolutely, not plurality of relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX4","text":"The number of persons does not introduce whole and parts into the divine reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX5","text":"The four divine relations do not make four persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX6","text":"The Word is not a fourth person besides the Son, because intellectual procession is already the generation of the Son.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX7","text":"Infinite divine perfection requires neither infinite intrinsic operations nor infinite persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX8","text":"Numerical terms in God do not signify quantitative number, an added accident, or a measure of divine things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX9","text":"Numerical terms are not mere removals without a thing signified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX10","text":"A common person is not one thing common to three.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:NX11","text":"The community of person constitutes neither universality nor divine genus nor species.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:D1","text":"A plurality of subsistent relative relations entails a plurality of persons within one divine nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A1","CH_GGVC_P_22:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:D2","text":"Paternity and filiation constitute two persons, spiration belongs to both, and procession constitutes the person of the Holy Spirit; therefore there are only three persons.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A5","CH_GGVC_P_22:A6","CH_GGVC_P_22:A7","CH_GGVC_P_22:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:D3","text":"Spiration is a relation, but it is not a personal property, because it belongs commonly to the Father and the Son.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A5","CH_GGVC_P_22:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:D4","text":"The difference between the procession of the Word and the procession of love explains two proceeding persons, not a double intellectual or natural person.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:D5","text":"The ternary number of persons indicates an order of relations, but does not measure the immensity or magnitude of the persons.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A11","CH_GGVC_P_22:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:D6","text":"To say one essence or several persons signifies the thing of which it is said and the negation of division proper to it.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A12","CH_GGVC_P_22:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:D7","text":"Unity does not remove multitude, but division; multitude, moreover, does not remove the unity of the individual constituents.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A12","CH_GGVC_P_22:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:D8","text":"Person can be simultaneously incommunicable in each and common according to reason, because the mode of subsisting is communicated distinctly, not the singular person itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A14","CH_GGVC_P_22:A15","CH_GGVC_P_22:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:SD1","text":"Divine plurality is reordered as a difference of relations without partition of essence: essential unity and personal distinction are diverse principles of order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A1","CH_GGVC_P_22:A3","CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:SD2","text":"The ternary is not an aggregate of three equal unities by addition, but the topological closure of relative oppositions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A5","CH_GGVC_P_22:A6","CH_GGVC_P_22:A7","CH_GGVC_P_22:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:SD3","text":"Divine numerical predication has a twofold structure: a positive foundation in the things signified and a negative determination through indivision.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A11","CH_GGVC_P_22:A12","CH_GGVC_P_22:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:SD4","text":"Divine personal identity is constituted not through an individuating nature, but through distinct relative subsistence; community therefore concerns the name and the account, not the real individual.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A1","CH_GGVC_P_22:A14","CH_GGVC_P_22:A15","CH_GGVC_P_22:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:SD5","text":"Processional multiplication to infinity is arrested because goodness and operation are not numerically distinct principles, while opposed relations alone distinguish persons.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A9","CH_GGVC_P_22:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:DNX1","text":"It must be false that every real distinction requires a distinction of essences or substances.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A1","CH_GGVC_P_22:A2","CH_GGVC_P_22:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:DNX2","text":"It must be false that unity and plurality comport themselves as absolute opposites, one of which destroys the other.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A12","CH_GGVC_P_22:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:DNX3","text":"It must be false that every number is quantity, measure, or an aggregation of parts.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A11","CH_GGVC_P_22:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:DNX4","text":"It must be false that everything common is a universal, genus, species, or a numerically one communicable thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A14","CH_GGVC_P_22:A15","CH_GGVC_P_22:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:DNX5","text":"It must be false that infinite goodness of itself requires an infinite succession of producing persons.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:SNX1","text":"Divine plurality as an additive composition of partial beings within a higher whole is precluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A2","CH_GGVC_P_22:A11","CH_GGVC_P_22:A16","CH_GGVC_P_22:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:SNX2","text":"The alternative that makes God either a solitary singularity or a plurality of essentially separated gods is precluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A1","CH_GGVC_P_22:A3","CH_GGVC_P_22:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:SNX3","text":"The immediate transfer of the categories of creatures, quantity, and universality into divine predication is precluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A11","CH_GGVC_P_22:A14","CH_GGVC_P_22:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:FGS1","text":"One essence + real subsistent relations → person is understood as subsistent relation, not as multiplied essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A1","CH_GGVC_P_22:A2","CH_GGVC_P_22:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:FGS2","text":"Relative opposition → personal distinction; absence of opposition → communication of relation in the same person.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:FGS3","text":"Paternity ↔ filiation + spiration common to Father and Son + procession opposed to spiration → Father, Son, and Holy Spirit only.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A6","CH_GGVC_P_22:A7","CH_GGVC_P_22:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:FGS4","text":"Transcendent multitude + indivision of individuals → numerical terms signify divine things without quantity, measure, or parts.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A11","CH_GGVC_P_22:A12","CH_GGVC_P_22:A13","CH_GGVC_P_22:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:FGS5","text":"Distinct subsistent thing + community of reason → the name person is said of three without one real person or a divine universal.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A14","CH_GGVC_P_22:A15","CH_GGVC_P_22:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:FGS6","text":"One operation and goodness + distinction through relations alone → an infinite process of persons is excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A9","CH_GGVC_P_22:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AF1","text":"Divine simplicity apparently negating plurality → distinction of essence and suppositum → person as subsistent relation → plurality of persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AF2","text":"Four relations enumerated → relative opposition as rule of distinction → common spiration → only three persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AF3","text":"Material number rejected → transcendent multitude admitted → plurality signifying undivided things → number without measurement of divine magnitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:AF4","text":"Real community rejected → community of negation and intention rejected → community of reason of the mode of subsisting → common person without universal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:ME1","text":"Divine distinction is transferred from multiplication of substances to opposition of subsistent relations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A1","CH_GGVC_P_22:A2","CH_GGVC_P_22:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:ME2","text":"The relation constituting a person is distinguished from the relation belonging to several persons.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A5","CH_GGVC_P_22:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:ME3","text":"Number is reclassified from measuring quantity to transcendent notation of undivided things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A11","CH_GGVC_P_22:A12","CH_GGVC_P_22:A13","CH_GGVC_P_22:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:ME4","text":"Community is reclassified from thing, genus, and species to the common account of the mode of subsisting.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A14","CH_GGVC_P_22:A15","CH_GGVC_P_22:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:ME5","text":"Essential unity and personal plurality cease to appear contradictory, because they are referred to diverse ontological and semantic orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A3","CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A12","CH_GGVC_P_22:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text immediately determines whether there are several persons, how many there are, what numerical terms signify, and how common person is said.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:ALT2","text":"Structural — The ordering of relations through subsistence, opposition, and communication constitutes the mechanism of personal distinction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A1","CH_GGVC_P_22:A3","CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argumentation dissolves apparent contradictions through a change of category: substance into suppositum, number into transcendental, common into reason.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A2","CH_GGVC_P_22:A11","CH_GGVC_P_22:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Divine reality is posited as one according to essence, operation, and being, but distinct according to relations neither temporal nor successively produced.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A9","CH_GGVC_P_22:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to restore the four principal conclusions: plurality of persons, ternary number, the non-quantitative sense of numbers, and the community of reason of the name person.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:MRT2","text":"The firmest hinge of the system is the identification of person with subsistent relation and the restriction of real distinction to relative opposition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A1","CH_GGVC_P_22:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:MRT3","text":"The demonstration of the ternary within this unity depends on prior doctrines concerning four relations and two processions, which the text here presupposes rather than deduces again.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:MRT4","text":"A fragile juncture remains in the transition from opposition of relations to the rule that a non-opposed relation must belong to the same person; the rule is posited as necessary, but its foundation is imported from the premises.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A4","CH_GGVC_P_22:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0040","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:MRT5","text":"Numerical semantics positively signifies things and negatively removes division; the distinction between the thing signified and the added negation is necessary to the system, but is not independently proved within the article.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A12","CH_GGVC_P_22:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:MRT6","text":"The community of reason of person preserves the incommunicability of singulars, but its stability rests on a subtle distinction between the name of a thing and the common account of a mode of existing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_22:A14","CH_GGVC_P_22:A15","CH_GGVC_P_22:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_22:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement of the text: from the objection of simple unity to an ontology of relation, thence to the closure of the ternary, then to the semantic discipline of number and community.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_22:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_22@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_P_22:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 31","title":"First Part · Question 30","context":"Chapter 31","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 30"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 30","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 31","order":32,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_P_23"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_23@R001","sha256":"625264b14de780b1a460c5883e8f006f6b3509b831811a2a2ed2aff8039ee24a","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:AS1","text":"Quaestio regulas quaerit quibus unitas essentiae divinae et pluralitas personarum simul dici possint, sine multiplicatione essentiae vel absorptione personarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:AS2","text":"Thesis est quod veritas locutionum trinitariarum ex distinctione essentiae communis, suppositorum personalium, numeri personarum, generis grammaticalis et ambitus exclusionis pendet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:AS3","text":"Argumentatio est simul semantica et dogmatica: errores trinitarii non solum ex falsa metaphysica, sed etiam ex vocabulis inordinate prolatis oriuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:AS4","text":"Motus procedit a numero determinato personarum ad disciplinam nominum distinctionis, deinde ad analysim dictionum exclusivarum quae communionem praedicati vel excludunt vel perperam restringunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:AS5","text":"Ordinatio sermonis debet utrumque extremum arcere: pluralitatem substantiarum Arianam et singularitatem personae Sabellianam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","text":"In divinis est una essentia communicabilis et sunt tres personae vel supposita distincta oppositione relativa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A2","text":"Terminus essentialis essentiam communem significans de qualibet persona et de tribus simul praedicari potest; terminus personalis suppositum determinatum significat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A3","text":"Nomen Trinitatis determinate significat numerum trium personarum unius essentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A4","text":"Numerus Trinitatis personas numerat, non unitatem essentiae in tres unitates essentiales dividit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A5","text":"Distinctio personarum non infert diversitatem essentiae, differentiam formae, separationem partium, disparitatem, alienitatem aut dissimilitudinem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A6","text":"Alius masculine distinctionem suppositi importat; aliud neutraliter distinctionem essentiae importat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A7","text":"Lexicon trinitarium ita regulandum est ut nec unitas essentiae in unitatem personae redeat nec pluralitas personarum in pluralitatem substantiarum transeat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A8","text":"Dictio solus categorematice accepta solitudinem subiecti ponit; syncategorematice accepta alia supposita a consortio praedicati excludit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A9","text":"Dictio exclusiva proprie respicit suppositum cui adiungitur et ambitum praedicati, non nudam formam praedicati.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A10","text":"Dictio exclusiva terminum cui adiungitur immobilitat, ita ut distributio ab essentiali termino ad singula supposita non liceat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A11","text":"Locutiones authenticae quae usui ordinario periculosae videntur per unitatem essentiae, implicationem vel expositionem piam interpretandae sunt, non simpliciter extendendae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:NX1","text":"Trinitas non significat essentiam quasi de singulis personis praedicabilem neque ipsas relationes personales; significat personas numeratas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:NX2","text":"Trinitas non est nomen collectivum secundum solam unitatem ordinis, quia pluralitatem personarum cum unitate essentiae coniungit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:NX3","text":"Trinitas non est triplicitas, quia triplicitas proportionem inaequalitatis importat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:NX4","text":"Distinctio personalis non dicenda est diversitas, differentia, separatio, divisio, disparitas, alienitas vel discrepantia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:NX5","text":"Unitas divina non est singularitas, unicitas, confusio vel solitudo, quia haec vel communicationem essentiae vel numerum et ordinem personarum tollunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:NX6","text":"Solus categorematice acceptum nulli termino divino adiungendum est, quia Deum solitarium constitueret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:NX7","text":"Dictio exclusiva termino personali adiuncta cum praedicato essentiali communi secundum proprium usum non est extendenda, quia aliam personam divinam a praedicato excluderet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:D1","text":"Posita pluralitate trium personarum, nomen Trinitatis necessario adhibetur ut pluralitas indeterminata determinate significetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:D2","text":"Formula Trinitas in unitate personas numeratas in una natura ponit, non ternariam unitatem essentialem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A3","CH_GGVC_P_23:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:D3","text":"Filius est alius a Patre, quia est aliud suppositum, sed non est aliud a Patre, quia essentia non distinguitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A5","CH_GGVC_P_23:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:D4","text":"Vocabulum distinctionis retinendum est ad personas discernendas, dum vocabula diversitatis et differentiae vitantur ad unitatem essentiae servandam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A5","CH_GGVC_P_23:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:D5","text":"Solus Deus est aeternus vel solus Deus creat dici potest, si exclusio omnia extra Deum a consortio praedicati removet, non si Deum solitarium facit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A2","CH_GGVC_P_23:A8","CH_GGVC_P_23:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:D6","text":"Ex sola Deo creat non sequitur solus Pater creat, quia exclusio ad terminum essentialem fixa ad singula supposita non descendit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A2","CH_GGVC_P_23:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:D7","text":"Propositio solus Deus est Pater vera est si Pater personam Patris significat, falsa si solam relationem paternitatis significat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A2","CH_GGVC_P_23:A8","CH_GGVC_P_23:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:D8","text":"Propositio solus Pater est Deus secundum proprium sensum falsa est, quia Filium et Spiritum Sanctum, qui alia supposita sunt, a deitate communi excluderet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A2","CH_GGVC_P_23:A6","CH_GGVC_P_23:A8","CH_GGVC_P_23:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:D9","text":"Auctoritates quae Patri vel Filio exclusivum attribuere videntur ad totam Trinitatem aut ad exclusionem alterius neutraliter intellectam reducendae sunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A6","CH_GGVC_P_23:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:SD1","text":"Orthodoxia trinitaria hic constituitur ut disciplina translationis inter ordinem ontologicum et ordinem semanticum: idem verbum secundum essentiam, suppositum, genus vel ambitum diversam veritatem habet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A2","CH_GGVC_P_23:A6","CH_GGVC_P_23:A8","CH_GGVC_P_23:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:SD2","text":"Systema duas dimensiones irreducibiles instituit: essentia una praedicata et supposita plura relativa; haec dimensiones nec confundi nec separari possunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A2","CH_GGVC_P_23:A5","CH_GGVC_P_23:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:SD3","text":"Exclusio non est praedicatum ontologicum de Deo, sed operator logicus qui participes alicuius praedicati determinat; ideo eius veritas a situ syntactico pendet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A8","CH_GGVC_P_23:A9","CH_GGVC_P_23:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:SD4","text":"Auctoritas locutionis authenticae non abolit regulam semanticam ordinariam, sed expositionem specialem exigit ne forma litteralis doctrinam de essentia et personis destruat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A7","CH_GGVC_P_23:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet quod identitas essentiae identitatem suppositi necessario inferat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet quod distinctio relativa personarum distinctionem essentialem vel pluralitatem substantiarum exigat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet quod vocabula ordinaria, sine determinatione significationis et ambitus, theologice innocua sint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet quod societas creaturarum solitudinem divinam absolute tollat; solitudo ex consortio eiusdem naturae aestimatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse debet quod exclusio ad Deum essentialiter dicta sine mutatione sensus ad Patrem, Filium vel Spiritum Sanctum distribui possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:SNX1","text":"Systema excludit omnem constitutionem divinitatis in qua unitas essentiae per singularitatem personalem vel pluralitas personarum per multiplicationem essentiae intelligitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:SNX2","text":"Systema excludit immediatam conversionem formae grammaticae in structuram divinam: genus, numerus et exclusio media semantica sunt, non imagines ontologicae simpliciter translatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:SNX3","text":"Systema excludit sermonem de Trinitate qui auctoritatem textus aut usum verborum supra distinctionem essentiae et suppositi collocet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:FGS1","text":"Una essentia communicabilis + tria supposita relatione distincta → duplex ordo constituitur: unitas essentialis et pluralitas personalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:FGS2","text":"Duplex ordo → termini numerales ad personas, termini essentiales ad naturam, termini personales ad supposita referuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A2","CH_GGVC_P_23:A3","CH_GGVC_P_23:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:FGS3","text":"Relatio terminorum ad ordines diversos → vocabula distinctionis admittuntur, vocabula diversitatis, separationis et singularitatis repelluntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A5","CH_GGVC_P_23:A6","CH_GGVC_P_23:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:FGS4","text":"Dictio exclusiva examinata secundum modum categorematicum vel syncategorematicum → aut solitudo divina negatur aut exclusio participantium praedicati limitatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A8","CH_GGVC_P_23:A9","CH_GGVC_P_23:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:FGS5","text":"Locutio authentica ambiguam exclusionem continens → per unitatem essentiae, distinctionem suppositorum et implicationem contextus pie exponitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A6","CH_GGVC_P_23:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:AF1","text":"Pluralitas personarum → numerus determinatus → Trinitas → personae numeratae in una essentia → exclusio multiplicationis unitatis essentialis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:AF2","text":"Unitas essentiae + oppositio relativa → distinctio suppositorum → alius sed non aliud → exclusio Arianismi et Sabellianismi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:AF3","text":"Solus ambiguum → distinctio solitudinis et exclusionis → terminus essentialis admittitur → terminus personalis restringitur → locutiones authenticae exponuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:ME1","text":"Numerus divinus reclassificatur: non est numerus essentiarum nec proportio inaequalitatis, sed numerus personarum unius naturae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A3","CH_GGVC_P_23:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:ME2","text":"Oppositio alia vel aliud fit index structuralis: masculinum personale pluralitatem suppositorum ostendit, neutrum essentiale unitatem naturae custodit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A2","CH_GGVC_P_23:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:ME3","text":"Exclusio reclassificatur ab attributo solitudinis ad regulam consortium praedicati ordinantem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A8","CH_GGVC_P_23:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:ME4","text":"Haeresis reclassificatur ut imminutio vel confusio unius ex duobus ordinibus, essentiali et personali, per abusum nominum effecta.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A5","CH_GGVC_P_23:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:ALT1","text":"Surface — Quaestio immediate de licitis locutionibus agit: Trinitas, alius, aliud, solus, unus et exclusio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:ALT2","text":"Structural — Mechanismus argumenti distinctionem inter essentiam communem, personas numeratas, supposita determinata et ambitum praedicati instituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Sermonis moderatio fit instrumentum custodiendi medium inter Arianam multiplicationem substantiarum et Sabellianam reductionem personarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Realitas divina praesupponitur ut essentia simpliciter una, intra quam relationes personales non partes, non formae diversae et non substantiae plures constituunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad derivandas decisiones principales de Trinitate, de alius et aliud, atque de dictione solus, quia omnes ex axibus essentiae, suppositi et ambitus procedunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:MRT2","text":"Firmissimum nexus systematis est distinctio inter essentiam communem et suppositum personale, ex qua tam regulae numeri quam regulae exclusionis dependent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:MRT3","text":"Systema distinctionem realem personarum per oppositionem relativam praesupponit potius quam intra hanc quaestionem demonstrat; haec est condicio non a textu hic generata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:MRT4","text":"Commissura fragilis manet ubi eadem locutio exclusiva secundum usum proprium falsa, secundum implicationem vel expositionem auctoritative recepta vera fieri potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:MRT5","text":"Ratio generis grammaticalis ad distinctionem suppositi et essentiae adhibita vim ordinativam habet, sed eius necessitas metaphysica non independenter stabilitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:MRT6","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat, si vocabula non tamquam ornamenta doctrinae sed tamquam operatoria quae unitatem et pluralitatem simul distribuunt intelliguntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T160752399264Z_69d3c107","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 31","source_sha256":"625264b14de780b1a460c5883e8f006f6b3509b831811a2a2ed2aff8039ee24a","sha256":"0949d5a0c169d809fe6bf20fc6bdc471ab752ecbd5b0fd330822852b898105ea","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T16:07:52.400973+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:AS1","text":"The question seeks rules by which the unity of the divine essence and the plurality of persons can be affirmed together, without multiplication of the essence or absorption of the persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:AS2","text":"The thesis is that the truth of Trinitarian expressions depends on the distinction of common essence, personal supposita, the number of persons, grammatical gender, and the scope of exclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:AS3","text":"The argument is at once semantic and dogmatic: Trinitarian errors arise not only from false metaphysics, but also from words uttered without order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:AS4","text":"The movement proceeds from the determinate number of persons to the discipline of names of distinction, then to an analysis of exclusive expressions which either exclude or wrongly restrict the communion of the predicate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:AS5","text":"The ordering of discourse must ward off both extremes: the Arian plurality of substances and the Sabellian singularity of person.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","text":"In divine things there is one communicable essence and there are three persons or supposita distinct by relative opposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A2","text":"An essential term signifying the common essence can be predicated of each person and of the three together; a personal term signifies a determinate suppositum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A3","text":"The name Trinity determinately signifies the number of three persons of one essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A4","text":"The number of the Trinity counts persons; it does not divide the unity of essence into three essential unities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A5","text":"The distinction of persons does not entail diversity of essence, difference of form, separation of parts, disparity, alienness, or dissimilarity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A6","text":"Alius, in the masculine, conveys a distinction of suppositum; aliud, in the neuter, conveys a distinction of essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A7","text":"Trinitarian vocabulary must be regulated so that neither the unity of essence is reduced to unity of person nor the plurality of persons passes into a plurality of substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A8","text":"The word solus, taken categorematically, posits the solitude of the subject; taken syncategorematically, it excludes other supposita from participation in the predicate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A9","text":"An exclusive expression properly concerns the suppositum to which it is joined and the scope of the predicate, not the bare form of the predicate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A10","text":"An exclusive expression fixes the term to which it is joined, so that distribution from an essential term to the individual supposita is not permitted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:A11","text":"Authentic expressions that seem dangerous in ordinary usage are to be interpreted through the unity of essence, implication, or pious exposition, not simply extended.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:NX1","text":"Trinity does not signify the essence as though predicable of the individual persons, nor the personal relations themselves; it signifies the persons as numbered.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:NX2","text":"Trinity is not a collective name according to mere unity of order, because it joins the plurality of persons with the unity of essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:NX3","text":"Trinity is not triplicity, because triplicity conveys a proportion of inequality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:NX4","text":"Personal distinction is not to be called diversity, difference, separation, division, disparity, alienness, or discrepancy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:NX5","text":"Divine unity is not singularity, uniqueness, confusion, or solitude, because these remove either the communication of essence or the number and order of persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:NX6","text":"Solus, taken categorematically, is not to be joined to any divine term, because it would make God solitary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:NX7","text":"An exclusive expression joined to a personal term with a common essential predicate is not, according to its proper usage, to be extended, because it would exclude another divine person from the predicate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:D1","text":"Given a plurality of three persons, the name Trinity is necessarily employed so that indeterminate plurality may be signified determinately.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:D2","text":"The formula Trinity in unity posits numbered persons in one nature, not a ternary essential unity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A3","CH_GGVC_P_23:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:D3","text":"The Son is alius from the Father, because He is another suppositum, but He is not aliud from the Father, because the essence is not distinguished.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A5","CH_GGVC_P_23:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:D4","text":"The term distinction is to be retained for discerning the persons, while the terms diversity and difference are to be avoided in order to preserve the unity of essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A5","CH_GGVC_P_23:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:D5","text":"“God alone is eternal” or “God alone creates” may be said if the exclusion removes all things outside God from participation in the predicate, but not if it makes God solitary.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A2","CH_GGVC_P_23:A8","CH_GGVC_P_23:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:D6","text":"From “God alone creates” it does not follow that “the Father alone creates,” because exclusion fixed to an essential term does not descend to the individual supposita.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A2","CH_GGVC_P_23:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:D7","text":"The proposition “God alone is Father” is true if Father signifies the person of the Father, false if it signifies the relation of paternity alone.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A2","CH_GGVC_P_23:A8","CH_GGVC_P_23:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:D8","text":"The proposition “the Father alone is God,” according to its proper sense, is false, because it would exclude the Son and the Holy Spirit, who are other supposita, from the common deity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A2","CH_GGVC_P_23:A6","CH_GGVC_P_23:A8","CH_GGVC_P_23:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:D9","text":"Authorities that seem to attribute an exclusive term to the Father or the Son are to be reduced to the whole Trinity or to an exclusion of another understood neutrally.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A6","CH_GGVC_P_23:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:SD1","text":"Trinitarian orthodoxy is here constituted as a discipline of translation between the ontological order and the semantic order: the same word has a different truth according to essence, suppositum, gender, or scope.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A2","CH_GGVC_P_23:A6","CH_GGVC_P_23:A8","CH_GGVC_P_23:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:SD2","text":"The system establishes two irreducible dimensions: one essence predicated and multiple relative supposita; these dimensions can neither be confused nor separated.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A2","CH_GGVC_P_23:A5","CH_GGVC_P_23:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:SD3","text":"Exclusion is not an ontological predicate concerning God, but a logical operator that determines the participants in a predicate; therefore its truth depends on syntactic position.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A8","CH_GGVC_P_23:A9","CH_GGVC_P_23:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:SD4","text":"The authority of an authentic expression does not abolish the ordinary semantic rule, but requires a special exposition lest the literal form destroy the doctrine of essence and persons.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A7","CH_GGVC_P_23:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:DNX1","text":"It must be false that identity of essence necessarily entails identity of suppositum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:DNX2","text":"It must be false that the relative distinction of persons requires essential distinction or a plurality of substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:DNX3","text":"It must be false that ordinary words, without determination of signification and scope, are theologically innocuous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:DNX4","text":"It must be false that the society of creatures absolutely removes divine solitude; solitude is assessed from communion in the same nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:DNX5","text":"It must be false that exclusion said of God essentially can be distributed to the Father, the Son, or the Holy Spirit without a change of sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:SNX1","text":"The system excludes every constitution of divinity in which unity of essence is understood through personal singularity or plurality of persons through multiplication of essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:SNX2","text":"The system excludes the immediate conversion of grammatical form into divine structure: gender, number, and exclusion are semantic mediations, not simply transferred ontological images.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:SNX3","text":"The system excludes discourse concerning the Trinity that places the authority of the text or the use of words above the distinction of essence and suppositum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:FGS1","text":"One communicable essence + three supposita distinct by relation → a twofold order is constituted: essential unity and personal plurality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:FGS2","text":"Twofold order → numerical terms refer to persons, essential terms to nature, personal terms to supposita.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A2","CH_GGVC_P_23:A3","CH_GGVC_P_23:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:FGS3","text":"Relation of terms to diverse orders → terms of distinction are admitted; terms of diversity, separation, and singularity are rejected.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A5","CH_GGVC_P_23:A6","CH_GGVC_P_23:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:FGS4","text":"An exclusive expression examined according to the categorematic or syncategorematic mode → either divine solitude is denied or the exclusion of participants in the predicate is limited.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A8","CH_GGVC_P_23:A9","CH_GGVC_P_23:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:FGS5","text":"An authentic expression containing ambiguous exclusion → is piously expounded through unity of essence, distinction of supposita, and implication of context.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A6","CH_GGVC_P_23:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:AF1","text":"Plurality of persons → determinate number → Trinity → persons numbered in one essence → exclusion of multiplication of essential unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:AF2","text":"Unity of essence + relative opposition → distinction of supposita → alius but not aliud → exclusion of Arianism and Sabellianism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:AF3","text":"Solus ambiguous → distinction of solitude and exclusion → essential term admitted → personal term restricted → authentic expressions expounded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:ME1","text":"Divine number is reclassified: it is not a number of essences nor a proportion of inequality, but a number of persons of one nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A1","CH_GGVC_P_23:A3","CH_GGVC_P_23:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:ME2","text":"The opposition of alius and aliud becomes a structural index: the personal masculine displays a plurality of supposita, while the essential neuter preserves the unity of nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A2","CH_GGVC_P_23:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:ME3","text":"Exclusion is reclassified from an attribute of solitude into a rule ordering participation in the predicate.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A8","CH_GGVC_P_23:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:ME4","text":"Heresy is reclassified as the diminution or confusion of one of the two orders, essential and personal, effected through misuse of names.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_23:A5","CH_GGVC_P_23:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:ALT1","text":"Surface — The question deals immediately with permissible expressions: Trinity, alius, aliud, solus, one, and exclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:ALT2","text":"Structural — The mechanism of the argument establishes a distinction between common essence, numbered persons, determinate supposita, and the scope of the predicate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Regulation of discourse becomes an instrument for preserving the mean between the Arian multiplication of substances and the Sabellian reduction of persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Divine reality is presupposed as essence simply one, within which personal relations constitute neither parts, nor diverse forms, nor multiple substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to derive the principal determinations concerning Trinity, alius and aliud, and the word solus, because all proceed from the axes of essence, suppositum, and scope.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:MRT2","text":"The strongest nexus of the system is the distinction between common essence and personal suppositum, upon which both the rules of number and the rules of exclusion depend.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:MRT3","text":"The system presupposes the real distinction of persons through relative opposition rather than demonstrates it within this question; this is a condition not generated here by the text.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:MRT4","text":"A fragile juncture remains where the same exclusive expression can be false according to proper usage and true according to implication or authoritatively received exposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0071","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0085","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:MRT5","text":"The account of grammatical gender employed for distinguishing suppositum and essence has an ordering force, but its metaphysical necessity is not independently established.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_23:MRT6","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text if words are understood not so much as ornaments of doctrine but as operators that distribute unity and plurality simultaneously.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_23:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_23@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_P_23:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 32","title":"First Part · Question 31","context":"Chapter 32","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 31"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 31","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 32","order":33,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_P_24"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_24@R001","sha256":"b796774d958e9530ed776ef8eba839e6b111578a28d9795f5c979e257072d6da","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:AS1","text":"Quaestio a limite cognitionis naturalis incipit: utrum effectus creati ad distinctionem personalem in Deo perducere possint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:AS2","text":"Argumentatio cognitionem essentiae divinae ex causalitate creaturarum separat a cognitione originum quibus personae distinguuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:AS3","text":"Ratio naturalis a probatione Trinitatis removetur, sed retinetur ad congruentias post revelationem positam ostendendas et ad non-impossibilitatem fidei defendendam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:AS4","text":"Distinctio personalis, revelatione cognita, per notiones abstractas ordinatur ut relatio inter essentiam unam, personas tres et rationes distinctivas significari possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:AS5","text":"Numerus notionum ex ordine originum derivatur, dum pluralitas realis restringitur ad oppositionem relativam et pluralitas notionalis a pluralitate personarum discernitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:AS6","text":"Diversitas opinionum de notionibus non immediate haeresis est, sed fit haeretica ubi contradictio fidei intellecta et pertinaciter defensa constituitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A1","text":"Ratio naturalis in cognitionem Dei pervenit ex creaturis sicut ex effectibus in causam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A2","text":"Ex effectibus creatis cognosci potest de Deo solum quod ei necessario convenit ut principi omnium entium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A3","text":"Virtus creativa divina est communis toti Trinitati et ad unitatem essentiae, non ad distinctionem personarum, pertinet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A4","text":"Personae divinae per origines generationis et processionis distinguuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A5","text":"Ratio potest vel radicem rei sufficienter probare vel, radice iam posita, effectus ei congruentes ostendere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A6","text":"Quae fidei sunt auctoritate proponenda sunt recipientibus auctoritatem, aliis vero defendenda sunt ut non impossibilia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A7","text":"Intellectus humanus divina apprehendit et nominat secundum modum cognitionis a sensibilibus acceptae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A8","text":"Essentia divina significatur ut quid, persona ut quis, proprietas sive notio ut quo persona cognoscitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A9","text":"Notiones significantur non ut res distinctae, sed ut rationes cognoscendi personas, licet relationes significatae realiter sint in Deo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A10","text":"Personae divinae multiplicantur secundum originem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A11","text":"Sola oppositio relativa pluralitatem realem in divinis efficit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A12","text":"Relationes originis in una persona concurrentes seorsum abstracte significandae sunt, ne distinctio personarum relatione indistincta evacuetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A13","text":"Principium notionalis cognitionis debet esse speciale et ad dignitatem personalem pertinere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A14","text":"Ordo originum Patrem a nullo ponit, Filium a Patre natum et cum Patre spirantem, Spiritum autem procedentem nec aliam personam producentem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A15","text":"Fides directe continet divina tradita principalia, indirecte vero ea quorum falsitas contradictionem fidei infert; haeresis requirit falsitatem fidei implicantem agnitam et pertinaciter retentam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A16","text":"Cognitio divinarum personarum necessaria est ad creationem per verbum et amorem recte intelligendam atque ad salutem per Filium incarnatum et donum Spiritus cognoscendam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:NX1","text":"Cognitio Dei ex creaturis non transit ad cognitionem Trinitatis personalis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:NX2","text":"Attributiones essentiales personis appropriatae, ternarius numerus, vel formulae philosophicae non sunt cognitio paternitatis, filiationis et processionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:NX3","text":"Rationes ex bonitate infinita, consortio boni vel similitudine mentis non sufficienter probant Trinitatem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:NX4","text":"Fides non debet fundari in rationibus infirmis quae pro demonstrationibus exhibentur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:NX5","text":"Simplicitas divina non vetat proprietates abstractas significari nec paternitatem ad solum nomen concretum Patris redigi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:NX6","text":"Notiones non sunt subiecta actuum essentialium vel personalium; paternitas nec creat nec generat nec intelligit secundum modum suae significationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:NX7","text":"Quinque notiones nec quinque personas nec Deum “quinum” constituunt, quia notiones sunt rationes notificantes et non res numerandae eodem modo ac essentia vel personae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:NX8","text":"Ex eo quod nulla persona procedit a Spiritu Sancto non nascitur sexta notio personalis, quia talis negatio dignitatem personalem non significat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:NX9","text":"Contraria opinio circa notiones non est ex se haeresis antequam eius contradictio fidei manifestetur et pertinaciter teneatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:D1","text":"Theologia naturalis ad unitatem essentiae et ad attributa causaliter necessaria sistit, quia creatio est operatio toti Trinitati communis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A1","CH_GGVC_P_24:A2","CH_GGVC_P_24:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:D2","text":"Trinitas personarum ratione naturali demonstrari non potest, quia distinctiones originum non sunt effectus creaturis proportionaliter manifestati.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A1","CH_GGVC_P_24:A3","CH_GGVC_P_24:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:D3","text":"Rationes trinitariae post revelationem adhibitae sunt rationes congruentiae, non probationes sufficientes ipsius mysterii.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A4","CH_GGVC_P_24:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:D4","text":"Usus rationis theologicae bifurcatur: intus fidem congruentiam explicat, extra fidem impossibilitatis accusationem repellit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A5","CH_GGVC_P_24:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:D5","text":"Revelatio Trinitatis non est superflua, quia creationem a necessitate naturae et indigentia divina liberat atque salutem per Filium et Spiritum determinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:D6","text":"Notiones abstractae necessariae sunt ad simul significandum Deum unum secundum essentiam et tres personas secundum relationes originis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A7","CH_GGVC_P_24:A8","CH_GGVC_P_24:A9","CH_GGVC_P_24:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:D7","text":"Ex ordine originum oriuntur quinque notiones: innascibilitas, paternitas, filiatio, communis spiratio et processio.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A9","CH_GGVC_P_24:A10","CH_GGVC_P_24:A13","CH_GGVC_P_24:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:D8","text":"Quinque notiones distribuuntur in quattuor relationes, quattuor proprietates et tres notiones personales, quia innascibilitas relationem non est nisi per reductionem et communis spiratio duas personas convenit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A9","CH_GGVC_P_24:A11","CH_GGVC_P_24:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:D9","text":"Error circa notiones ab opinione tolerabili ad haeresim transit cum nexus eius ad contradictionem fidei manifestus fit, praesertim per determinationem Ecclesiae, et pertinaciter retinetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:SD1","text":"Cognitio naturalis Dei non est scala continua ad cognitionem personalem, sed ordo limitatus qui causaliter ad essentiam pervenit et ante originum mysterium sistit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:D1","CH_GGVC_P_24:D2","CH_GGVC_P_24:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:SD2","text":"Notiones constituunt apparatum semanticum quo simplicitas divina et distinctio personalis simul retinentur sine compositione rerum divinarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:D6","CH_GGVC_P_24:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:SD3","text":"Revelatio trinitaria non solum cognitionem internam Dei addit, sed structuram causalitatis creatae et structuram salutis humanae reordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:D2","CH_GGVC_P_24:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:SD4","text":"Status haeresis non ex sola propositionis forma pendet, sed ex situ inferentiali, manifestatione consequentiae, receptione ecclesiali et pertinacia tenentis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet quod intelligibilitas Dei ut causae creaturarum eius vitam personalem intrinsecam exhauriat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet quod simplicitas divina omnem distinctionem rationis, relationis et significationis prohibeat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet quod omnis differentia notionalis sit differentia realis, compositio divina, vel multiplicatio personarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet quod fines fidei ex sola enumeratione articulorum pateant sine consideratione consequentiarum doctrinalium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:SNX1","text":"Possibilitas Trinitatis per rationem naturalem autonomam possidendae structuraliter excluditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:SNX2","text":"Possibilitas divinarum personarum tamquam substantiarum separatarum vel graduum emanativorum distinctarum structuraliter excluditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:SNX3","text":"Possibilitas fidei ex probationibus dialecticis infirmis dependentis structuraliter excluditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:FGS1","text":"Effectus creati → causa universalis → attributa essentialia necessaria → unitas essentiae; distinctio personarum non producitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A1","CH_GGVC_P_24:A2","CH_GGVC_P_24:A3","CH_GGVC_P_24:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:FGS2","text":"Mysterium originum revelatum → ratio congruentiae → explicatio intra fidem vel defensio non-impossibilitatis extra fidem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A5","CH_GGVC_P_24:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:FGS3","text":"Verbum et amor divini → creatio non necessitata neque indigentiae subiecta; Filius incarnatus et donum Spiritus → salus recte intelligenda.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:FGS4","text":"Simplicitas divina et modus humanus significandi → abstractum/concretum → essentia ut quid, persona ut quis, notio ut quo.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A7","CH_GGVC_P_24:A8","CH_GGVC_P_24:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:FGS5","text":"Origines personales → rationes speciales cognoscendi → quinque notiones → distributio relationum, proprietatum et notionum personalium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A10","CH_GGVC_P_24:A11","CH_GGVC_P_24:A13","CH_GGVC_P_24:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:FGS6","text":"Propositio de notionibus → examinatio consequentiae ad fidem → ignorantia vel opinio non pertinax / contradictio manifesta et pertinax → licita diversitas vel haeresis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:AF1","text":"Effectus creati → unitas essentialis cognoscibilis → incapacitas naturalis ad origines personales → Trinitas revelata → ratio congruentiae et defensio → notiones relationum → numerus quinarius ordinatus → norma de errore et haeresi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:ME1","text":"Cognitio Dei dividitur in campum essentialium causaliter cognoscibilium et campum personalium originum revelatione tantum cognoscibilium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:ME2","text":"Ratio theologica reclassificatur in probationem radicis, congruentiam consequentium et defensionem non-impossibilitatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:ME3","text":"Vocabulum de Deo reordinatur per tres functiones irreductibiles: quid essentiale, quis personale et quo notionale.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:ME4","text":"Numeratio divina reordinatur, ut quinque notiones, quattuor relationes, quattuor proprietates et tres notiones personales non confundantur cum tribus personis et una essentia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:ME5","text":"Error doctrinalis reclassificatur ex oppositione simpliciter proposita in relationem conditionalem inter propositionem, consequentiam fidei, cognitionem et pertinaciam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:ALT1","text":"Gradus superficiei: quaeritur utrum Trinitas naturaliter cognoscatur, utrum notiones ponendae sint, quot sint, et utrum circa eas dissentire liceat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:ALT2","text":"Gradus structuralis: effectus creati cognoscibilitatem essentialem mediant, origines relationes personales constituunt, et notiones has relationes cognoscibiliter distinguunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:ALT3","text":"Gradus metastrategicus: ratio a munere demonstrandi mysterium ad munus congruentiae internae, defensionis externaе et disciplinae inferentialis transfertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:ALT4","text":"Gradus ontologicus vel temporalis: simplicitas divina relationes reales non excludit, dum periculum haeresis historice mutatur secundum manifestationem consequentiarum et determinationem Ecclesiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:MRT1","text":"Praemissae de cognitione ex effectibus, communitate virtutis creativae et distinctione originum sufficiunt ad conclusionem de impossibilitate demonstrationis naturalis Trinitatis restituendam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:MRT2","text":"Praemissae de modo significandi, de relationibus realibus et de oppositione relativa sufficiunt ad necessitatem notionum et ad taxonomiam quinariam generandam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:MRT3","text":"Structura firmissima est in distinctione inter probationem sufficientem et rationem congruentiae, quia haec simul tuetur transcendens mysterii et usum rationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:MRT4","text":"Commissura fragilis manet inter relationes reales in Deo et notiones ut rationes cognoscendi, quia identitas rei et distinctio significationis simul affirmantur sine ulteriori regula conversionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0046","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0068","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:MRT5","text":"Commissura fragilis manet in asymentria innascibilitatis Patris et negationis originis Spiritus, quia exclusio sextae notionis per dignitatem personalem potius quam per criterium omnino symmetricum stabilitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0057","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0070","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:MRT6","text":"Tensio temporalis manet inter immutabilem veritatem fidei et mutationem status erroris, quae ex clariore perceptione consequentiarum et determinatione Ecclesiae procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:MRT7","text":"Restitutio motum argumentationis servat, si terminus naturalis cognitionis, mediatio notionalis et disciplina consequentiarum doctrinalium tamquam tres nexus unius machinae retinentur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T160816188993Z_ea2cb747","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 32","source_sha256":"b796774d958e9530ed776ef8eba839e6b111578a28d9795f5c979e257072d6da","sha256":"1957f91456b14ec43482b20b8c7ed2eb662096c8b97ef8816c643f4b0b9fd0f5","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T16:08:16.190118+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:AS1","text":"The question begins from the limit of natural knowledge: whether created effects can lead to personal distinction in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:AS2","text":"The argument separates knowledge of the divine essence from the causality of creatures from knowledge of the origins by which the persons are distinguished.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:AS3","text":"Natural reason is excluded from proving the Trinity, but is retained for showing congruities once revelation has been posited and for defending the non-impossibility of faith.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:AS4","text":"Personal distinction, once known by revelation, is ordered through abstract notions so that the relation between one essence, three persons, and distinctive grounds can be signified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:AS5","text":"The number of notions is derived from the order of origins, while real plurality is restricted to relative opposition and notional plurality is distinguished from the plurality of persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:AS6","text":"Diversity of opinion concerning notions is not immediately heresy, but becomes heretical where a contradiction of faith is understood and pertinaciously defended.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A1","text":"Natural reason arrives at knowledge of God from creatures as from effects to a cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A2","text":"From created effects, only that can be known of God which necessarily befits Him as the principle of all beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A3","text":"The divine creative power is common to the whole Trinity and pertains to the unity of essence, not to the distinction of persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A4","text":"The divine persons are distinguished by the origins of generation and procession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A5","text":"Reason can either sufficiently prove the root of a matter or, once the root has been posited, show effects congruent with it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A6","text":"Matters of faith are to be proposed by authority to those receiving authority, but defended to others as not impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A7","text":"The human intellect apprehends and names divine things according to the mode of knowledge received from sensible things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A8","text":"The divine essence is signified as what, the person as who, and the property or notion as that by which the person is known.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A9","text":"Notions are signified not as distinct things, but as grounds for knowing the persons, although the signified relations are really in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A10","text":"The divine persons are multiplied according to origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A11","text":"Relative opposition alone effects real plurality in divine things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A12","text":"Relations of origin concurrent in one person must be signified separately in the abstract, lest the distinction of persons be voided by an indistinct relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A13","text":"The principle of notional knowledge must be special and pertain to personal dignity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A14","text":"The order of origins posits the Father as from no one, the Son as born of the Father and spirating with the Father, and the Spirit as proceeding and not producing another person.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A15","text":"Faith directly contains the principal divine things handed down, and indirectly those whose falsity entails a contradiction of faith; heresy requires a falsity implying faith to be recognized and pertinaciously held.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:A16","text":"Knowledge of the divine persons is necessary for rightly understanding creation through word and love and for knowing salvation through the incarnate Son and the gift of the Spirit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:NX1","text":"Knowledge of God from creatures does not pass into knowledge of the personal Trinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:NX2","text":"Essential attributes appropriated to persons, the number three, or philosophical formulas are not knowledge of paternity, filiation, and procession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:NX3","text":"Arguments from infinite goodness, the fellowship of the good, or the likeness of the mind do not sufficiently prove the Trinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:NX4","text":"Faith ought not to be founded on weak arguments presented as demonstrations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:NX5","text":"Divine simplicity does not prevent abstract properties from being signified, nor does it reduce paternity to the concrete name Father alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:NX6","text":"Notions are not subjects of essential or personal acts; paternity neither creates nor generates nor understands according to the mode of its signification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:NX7","text":"The five notions constitute neither five persons nor a “fivefold” God, because notions are grounds that make known and not things to be numbered in the same way as essence or persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:NX8","text":"From the fact that no person proceeds from the Holy Spirit, a sixth personal notion does not arise, because such a negation does not signify personal dignity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:NX9","text":"A contrary opinion concerning notions is not of itself heresy before its contradiction of faith is made manifest and pertinaciously held.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:D1","text":"Natural theology stops at the unity of essence and at causally necessary attributes, because creation is an operation common to the whole Trinity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A1","CH_GGVC_P_24:A2","CH_GGVC_P_24:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:D2","text":"The Trinity of persons cannot be demonstrated by natural reason, because distinctions of origins are not effects proportionately manifested to creatures.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A1","CH_GGVC_P_24:A3","CH_GGVC_P_24:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:D3","text":"Trinitarian arguments employed after revelation are arguments of congruity, not sufficient proofs of the mystery itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A4","CH_GGVC_P_24:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:D4","text":"The use of theological reason is bifurcated: within faith it explains congruity; outside faith it repels the accusation of impossibility.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A5","CH_GGVC_P_24:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:D5","text":"The revelation of the Trinity is not superfluous, because it frees creation from necessity of nature and divine need, and determines salvation through the Son and Spirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:D6","text":"Abstract notions are necessary for signifying simultaneously God as one according to essence and three persons according to relations of origin.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A7","CH_GGVC_P_24:A8","CH_GGVC_P_24:A9","CH_GGVC_P_24:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:D7","text":"From the order of origins arise five notions: innascibility, paternity, filiation, common spiration, and procession.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A9","CH_GGVC_P_24:A10","CH_GGVC_P_24:A13","CH_GGVC_P_24:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:D8","text":"The five notions are distributed into four relations, four properties, and three personal notions, because innascibility is not a relation except by reduction and common spiration belongs to two persons.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A9","CH_GGVC_P_24:A11","CH_GGVC_P_24:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:D9","text":"Error concerning notions passes from tolerable opinion to heresy when its connection to a contradiction of faith becomes manifest, especially through a determination of the Church, and is pertinaciously retained.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:SD1","text":"Natural knowledge of God is not a continuous ladder to personal knowledge, but a limited order that arrives causally at essence and stops before the mystery of origins.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:D1","CH_GGVC_P_24:D2","CH_GGVC_P_24:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:SD2","text":"Notions constitute a semantic apparatus by which divine simplicity and personal distinction are simultaneously maintained without composition of divine things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:D6","CH_GGVC_P_24:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:SD3","text":"Trinitarian revelation not only adds internal knowledge of God, but also reorders the structure of created causality and the structure of human salvation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:D2","CH_GGVC_P_24:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:SD4","text":"The status of heresy does not depend on the form of a proposition alone, but on inferential situation, manifestation of the consequence, ecclesial reception, and the pertinacity of the holder.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:DNX1","text":"It must be false that the intelligibility of God as the cause of creatures exhausts His intrinsic personal life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:DNX2","text":"It must be false that divine simplicity prohibits every distinction of ground, relation, and signification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:DNX3","text":"It must be false that every notional difference is a real difference, divine composition, or multiplication of persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:DNX4","text":"It must be false that the boundaries of faith are evident from the enumeration of articles alone without consideration of doctrinal consequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:SNX1","text":"The possibility of possessing the Trinity through autonomous natural reason is structurally excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:SNX2","text":"The possibility of divine persons as separate substances or distinct emanative grades is structurally excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:SNX3","text":"The possibility of faith depending on weak dialectical proofs is structurally excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:FGS1","text":"Created effects → universal cause → necessary essential attributes → unity of essence; distinction of persons is not produced.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A1","CH_GGVC_P_24:A2","CH_GGVC_P_24:A3","CH_GGVC_P_24:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:FGS2","text":"Revealed mystery of origins → reason of congruity → explanation within faith or defense of non-impossibility outside faith.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A5","CH_GGVC_P_24:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:FGS3","text":"Divine word and love → creation neither necessitated nor subject to need; incarnate Son and gift of the Spirit → salvation to be rightly understood.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:FGS4","text":"Divine simplicity and the human mode of signifying → abstract/concrete → essence as what, person as who, notion as that by which.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A7","CH_GGVC_P_24:A8","CH_GGVC_P_24:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:FGS5","text":"Personal origins → special grounds of knowing → five notions → distribution of relations, properties, and personal notions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A10","CH_GGVC_P_24:A11","CH_GGVC_P_24:A13","CH_GGVC_P_24:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:FGS6","text":"Proposition concerning notions → examination of its consequence for faith → ignorance or non-pertinacious opinion / manifest and pertinacious contradiction → permissible diversity or heresy.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_24:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:AF1","text":"Created effects → knowable essential unity → natural incapacity regarding personal origins → revealed Trinity → reason of congruity and defense → notions of relations → ordered fivefold number → norm concerning error and heresy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:ME1","text":"Knowledge of God is divided into the field of essential things causally knowable and the field of personal origins knowable only by revelation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:ME2","text":"Theological reason is reclassified into proof of the root, congruity of consequences, and defense of non-impossibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:ME3","text":"Vocabulary concerning God is reordered through three irreducible functions: essential what, personal who, and notional that by which.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:ME4","text":"Divine enumeration is reordered, so that five notions, four relations, four properties, and three personal notions are not confused with three persons and one essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:ME5","text":"Doctrinal error is reclassified from simply proposed opposition into a conditional relation between proposition, consequence for faith, knowledge, and pertinacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:ALT1","text":"Surface level: it is asked whether the Trinity is naturally known, whether notions must be posited, how many there are, and whether dissent concerning them is permitted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:ALT2","text":"Structural level: created effects mediate essential knowability, origins constitute personal relations, and notions distinguish these relations in a knowable manner.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic level: reason is transferred from the task of demonstrating the mystery to the task of internal congruity, external defense, and inferential discipline.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:ALT4","text":"Ontological or temporal level: divine simplicity does not exclude real relations, while the danger of heresy changes historically according to the manifestation of consequences and the determination of the Church.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:MRT1","text":"Premises concerning knowledge from effects, the commonality of creative power, and the distinction of origins suffice to reconstruct the conclusion concerning the impossibility of natural demonstration of the Trinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:MRT2","text":"Premises concerning the mode of signifying, real relations, and relative opposition suffice to generate the necessity of notions and the fivefold taxonomy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:MRT3","text":"The firmest structure lies in the distinction between sufficient proof and reason of congruity, because this simultaneously protects the transcendence of the mystery and the use of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:MRT4","text":"A fragile joint remains between real relations in God and notions as grounds of knowing, because identity of thing and distinction of signification are simultaneously affirmed without a further rule of conversion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0046","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0068","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:MRT5","text":"A fragile joint remains in the asymmetry of the Father's innascibility and the negation of the Spirit's origin, because exclusion of a sixth notion is established through personal dignity rather than through an altogether symmetric criterion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0057","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0070","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:MRT6","text":"A temporal tension remains between the immutable truth of faith and the change in the status of error, which proceeds from a clearer perception of consequences and the determination of the Church.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0082","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_24:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the argument if the natural limit of knowledge, notional mediation, and the discipline of doctrinal consequences are retained as three links of one machine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_24:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_24@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_P_24:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 33","title":"First Part · Question 32","context":"Chapter 33","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 32"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 32","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 33","order":34,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_P_25"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_25@R001","sha256":"dba37b269a681b28feffbf9e827c68773a6c806ff76bee5607b678aa09ff680f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:AS1","text":"Textus quaerit quomodo Pater principium, Pater proprie dictus, prior paternitate personali quam essentiali, et ingenitus dici possit sine causalitate creaturali, successione temporali, subiectione vel divisione naturae divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:AS2","text":"Argumentatio est semantico-metaphysica et reconstructiva: nomina divina distinguuntur secundum rem significatam, modum creaturalem impositionis, perfectionem rationis et relationem originis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:AS3","text":"Confusio principii cum causa, prioritate et effectu creato solvitur per reductionem principii ad id a quo aliud procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:AS4","text":"Relatio originis, quia in divinis subsistit, non solum accidens sed persona est; paternitas igitur Patrem proprie denominat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:AS5","text":"Paternitas personalis Patris ad Filium est ratio perfecta et aeterna; paternitas essentialis respectu creaturarum est participatio derivata similitudinis Filii.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:AS6","text":"Innascibilitas Patrem determinat ut principium non de principio, et unitas naturae excludit pluralitatem personarum innascibilium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A1","text":"Principium significat id a quo aliquid procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A2","text":"Significatio nominis non est idem quod ratio ex qua nomen imponitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A3","text":"Apud Latinos nomen causae, minus commune quam principium, modum creaturae determinat et diversitatem substantiae vel dependentiam videtur importare.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A4","text":"Processio divina originem significat sine ordine prioris et posterioris.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A5","text":"Auctoritas Patris ratione principii nullam subiectionem vel minorationem Filii aut Spiritus Sancti importat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A6","text":"Persona divina est relatio subsistens in natura divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A7","text":"Nomen proprium personae significat id quo illa persona ab omnibus aliis distinguitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A8","text":"Paternitas est relatio qua Pater ab omnibus aliis distinguitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A9","text":"Paternitas generationem completam significat, generatio autem processum ut in fieri.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A10","text":"Verbum divinum subsistit in natura divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A11","text":"Generans et genitus in generatione divina eandem numero formam habent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A12","text":"Nomen per prius dicitur de eo in quo tota ratio nominis perfecte salvatur, et de imperfecto per similitudinem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A13","text":"Filiatio creaturae est participatio similitudinis Filii naturalis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A14","text":"Pater est principium non de principio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A15","text":"Prima et simplicia per negationes significari possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0080","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A16","text":"Ingenitum Patri proprie convenit si intelligitur de persona quae omnino non est ab alio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A17","text":"Personae divinae oppositionibus relationum distinguuntur sine diversitate naturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A18","text":"Processio Spiritus Sancti generationem Filii ordine naturae praesupponit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX1","text":"Principium, in usu Latino de divinis, non est causa creaturalem dependentiam vel diversitatem substantiae importans.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX2","text":"Filius et Spiritus Sanctus non sunt dicendi principiati, si talis dictio subiectionem aut minorationem suggerit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX3","text":"Principium non significat prioritatem temporalem, quamvis nomen a prioritate imponi videatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX4","text":"Pater divinus non est relatio accidentalis personae subsistentis alio modo constitutae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX5","text":"Pater non denominatur principaliter a generatione ut processu, sed a paternitate ut termino completo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX6","text":"Verbum divinum et Filius non metaphorice dicuntur, quia Verbum in natura divina subsistit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX7","text":"Generatio creaturalis non est mensura neque forma perfectior generationis divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX8","text":"Paternitas essentialis Trinitatis respectu creaturae non eandem rationem perfectam habet ac paternitas personalis Patris ad Filium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX9","text":"Innascibilitas non est fontalitas, universalitas auctoritatis, aut complexus paternitatis et spirationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX10","text":"Ingenitum non significat privationem imperfectivam, neque simpliciter idem est quod increatum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0085","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX11","text":"Plures innascibiles non possunt stare cum una natura divina et distinctione mere relativa personarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX12","text":"Improcessibilitas non constituit nomen proprium aeque primarium ac innascibilitas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D1","text":"Pater proprie dicitur principium Filii et Spiritus Sancti, quia ab eo personae procedunt, sine causalitate creaturali aut praecessione temporali.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A1","CH_GGVC_P_25:A2","CH_GGVC_P_25:A3","CH_GGVC_P_25:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D2","text":"Auctoritas Patris ut principii cohaeret cum plena aequalitate Filii et Spiritus Sancti; originis ordo non transit in ordinem dominationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A4","CH_GGVC_P_25:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D3","text":"Nomen Pater est nomen proprium personae Patris, quia paternitatem significat ut relationem subsistentem et distinctivam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A6","CH_GGVC_P_25:A7","CH_GGVC_P_25:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D4","text":"Pater est nomen convenientius quam generans vel genitor, quia denominatio personalis a complemento generationis potius quam a processu incompleto fit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A7","CH_GGVC_P_25:A8","CH_GGVC_P_25:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D5","text":"Generatio divina et paternitas divina proprie ac per prius dicuntur, quia Verbum subsistit et generans cum genito eandem numero formam habet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A10","CH_GGVC_P_25:A11","CH_GGVC_P_25:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D6","text":"Paternitas personalis Patris ad Filium prior est paternitate essentiali Trinitatis ad creaturas, quia filiatio creaturarum per similitudinem Filii naturalis participatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A12","CH_GGVC_P_25:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D7","text":"Filiatio creaturarum ordinatur graduatim secundum vestigium, imaginem, gratiam et gloriam, nec ullus horum graduum aequatur filiationi naturali Filii.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A12","CH_GGVC_P_25:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D8","text":"Innascibilitas est notio propria Patris, quia negative manifestat eum esse principium non de principio.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A14","CH_GGVC_P_25:A15","CH_GGVC_P_25:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D9","text":"Spiritus Sanctus et essentia divina ingenita dici non possunt eo sensu quo ingenitum soli Patri proprium est, quia Spiritus est ab alio et essentia in personis procedentibus a Patre communicatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A14","CH_GGVC_P_25:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0083","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D10","text":"Duo innascibiles ponerent duas naturas divinas et duos deos, quia non possent oppositione relationum distingui.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A16","CH_GGVC_P_25:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D11","text":"Remota generatione passiva a Patre, et posita generatione Filii, consequitur Patrem non procedere processione Spiritus Sancti.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A14","CH_GGVC_P_25:A16","CH_GGVC_P_25:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:SD1","text":"Lingua de divinis fit vera non per retentionem modi creaturalis vocabuli, sed per separationem rei significatae a modo creaturali impositionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A2","CH_GGVC_P_25:A3","CH_GGVC_P_25:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:SD2","text":"Ordo originis potest esse asymmetria realis sine hierarchia perfectionis, temporalitatis aut potestatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A1","CH_GGVC_P_25:A4","CH_GGVC_P_25:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:SD3","text":"Pluralitas personalis divina explicatur per relationes subsistentes, dum unitas divina servatur per exclusionem differentiae naturae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A6","CH_GGVC_P_25:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:SD4","text":"Omnis paternitas creaturalis et omnis filiatio adoptiva reordinatur ut effectus secundarius filiationis naturalis Filii.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A11","CH_GGVC_P_25:A12","CH_GGVC_P_25:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:SD5","text":"Negatio in theologia trinitaria non est mera imminutio conceptus, sed instrumentum cognoscendi principium primum quod nulla origine determinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A14","CH_GGVC_P_25:A15","CH_GGVC_P_25:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:SD6","text":"Unicitas Patris ut innascibilis est clausura systemica quae simul originem personalem, unitatem naturae et impossibilitatem polytheismi tuetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A14","CH_GGVC_P_25:A16","CH_GGVC_P_25:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse oportet omnem originem realem necessario causalitatem creaturalem, distantiam perfectionis aut dependentiam substantialem importare.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse oportet relationem in divinis esse accidens non subsistens quod personam iam aliter constitutam tantum afficiat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse oportet generationem creaturalem esse archetypum a quo veritas paternitatis divinae metiatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse oportet nomen commune et essentiale prius in re esse nomine proprio quod relationem personalem exprimit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse oportet nudam negationem generationis, sine determinatione originis personalis, Patrem sufficienter distinguere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:DNX6","text":"Falsum esse oportet plures personas absolute non ab alio posse una natura divina et sola oppositione relativa distingui.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:SNX1","text":"Systema excludit pluralitatem personarum divinarum ut pluralitatem principiorum absolutorum et independenter innascentium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:SNX2","text":"Systema excludit rationem qua primum principium divinum per praedicata affirmative exhaustiva cognosci possit sine ministerio negationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0080","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:SNX3","text":"Systema excludit filiationem creaturae ut relationem immediatam et autonomam quae similitudine Filii naturalis non mediatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0058","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:SNX4","text":"Systema excludit unitatem divinae essentiae cum distinctionibus personalibus non-relativis et non-originariis componi posse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:FGS1","text":"Modus creaturalis impositionis ≠ res significata → nomen principii liberatur a prioritate temporali et a causalitate creaturali.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:FGS2","text":"Processio ab aliquo → principium; principium sine praecessione et minoratione → Pater principium aequalium personarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:FGS3","text":"Relatio subsistens + relatio paternitatis distinctiva + generatio completa → Pater nomen proprium personae Patris.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:FGS4","text":"Verbum subsistens + identitas numero formae → generatio divina perfecta → paternitas personalis primaria; participatio similitudinis Filii → paternitas essentialis creaturis secundaria.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:FGS5","text":"Principium non de principio + significatio negativorum primorum → innascibilitas ut notio Patris.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:FGS6","text":"Distinctio per relationes + unitas naturae + Spiritus ex ordine generationis procedens → unus innascibilis et exclusio improcessibilitatis ut proprietatis primariae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:AF1","text":"Causa / creatura / prius → distinctio significationis ab impositione → principium ut pura relatio originis → Pater principium sine temporalitate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:AF2","text":"Relatio accidentalis / generatio metaphorica → relatio subsistens / Verbum subsistens → paternitas completa → Pater nomen personale proprium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:AF3","text":"Paternitas communis respectu creaturae → distinctio perfectionis et similitudinis → filiatio naturalis Filii → paternitas personalis per prius.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:AF4","text":"Ingenitum ambiguum → negatio originis personalis absoluta → Pater principium non de principio → unus innascibilis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:ME1","text":"Principium reclassificatur ut relatio originis quae nec causationem creaturalem nec successionem temporalem secum fert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:ME2","text":"Persona reclassificatur ut relatio subsistens, ita ut nomen relativum simul nomen personale esse possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:ME3","text":"Paternitas dividitur in paternitatem personalem naturalem et paternitatem essentialem creaturis participatam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:ME4","text":"Generatio creaturalis reclassificatur ex exemplari veritatis in similitudinem imminutam generationis divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:ME5","text":"Ingenitum reclassificatur ex negatione indeterminata in signum originis personalis absolutae et unicitatis principii.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus de proprietatibus Patris agit: principio, nomine Patris, ordine paternitatis et innascibilitate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:ALT2","text":"Structural — Textus personas per relationes subsistentes et origines distinguit, essentiam autem unam et indivisam retinet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Obiectiones solvuntur non per negationem terminorum, sed per mutationem regulorum quibus termini divini significare censentur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0085","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Origo divina est realis sed aeterna; distinguit personas sine diversitate naturae, sine tempore et sine gradu perfectionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:MRT1","text":"Corpus axiomatum sufficit ad restituendam seriem principalem: principium sine causalitate creaturali, Pater ut relatio subsistens, paternitas divina primaria, innascibilitas unica.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:MRT2","text":"Firmissimum cardo systematis est distinctio inter significationem nominis et modum impositionis, quia per eam vocabula creaturaliter imposita de divinis servantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:MRT3","text":"Distinctio inter causam et principium structuram sustinet, sed eius vis partim pendet a norma semantica Latina quae hic recipitur potius quam demonstratur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:MRT4","text":"Subsistentia relationis divinae est praemissa decisiva, sed intra hanc quaestionem tamquam doctrina iam stabilita adhibetur, non ex fundamento iterum probatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:MRT5","text":"Praelatio paternitatis personalis super essentialem cohaeret, sed tensionem inter vocabula ex creaturis recepta et veritatem divinam per distinctionem rei et modi significandi continere debet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0042","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:MRT6","text":"Innascibilitas proprie negativa manere vult, sed ad solum Patrem restringendum condicionem originis personalis requirit; hic est commissura ubi negatio et positio proxime contingunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0079","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0080","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0083","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:MRT7","text":"Exclusio improcessibilitatis ut proprietatis primariae pendet ab ordine naturae quo processio Spiritus generationem Filii praesupponit; iste ordo hic assumitur et applicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:MRT8","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat: imminutio confusionum semanticarum → constitutio relationum personalium → ordinatio perfectionis et participationis → clausura negativa unitatis principii.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T160847681429Z_c2e34a81","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"First Part · Question 33","source_sha256":"dba37b269a681b28feffbf9e827c68773a6c806ff76bee5607b678aa09ff680f","sha256":"78a24d9e20c2a5df597a60cf9d0ae99b34341bfdbbf00787c6751ad8f4ca78dd","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T16:08:47.683557+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:AS1","text":"The text asks how the Father can be called principle, Father properly so called, prior in personal rather than essential paternity, and unbegotten, without creaturely causality, temporal succession, subjection, or division of the divine nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:AS2","text":"The argumentation is semantic-metaphysical and reconstructive: divine names are distinguished according to the thing signified, the creaturely mode of imposition, the perfection of the notion, and the relation of origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:AS3","text":"The confusion of principle with cause, priority, and created effect is resolved by reducing principle to that from which another proceeds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:AS4","text":"The relation of origin, because it subsists in divinity, is not merely an accident but a person; therefore paternity properly denominates the Father.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:AS5","text":"The Father's personal paternity toward the Son is a perfect and eternal notion; essential paternity with respect to creatures is a derived participation in the likeness of the Son.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:AS6","text":"Unbegottenness determines the Father as principle not from a principle, and unity of nature excludes a plurality of unbegotten persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A1","text":"Principle signifies that from which something proceeds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A2","text":"The signification of a name is not the same as the notion from which the name is imposed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A3","text":"Among the Latins, the name of cause, less common than principle, determines a creaturely mode and seems to imply diversity of substance or dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A4","text":"Divine procession signifies origin without an order of prior and posterior.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A5","text":"The authority of the Father by reason of principle implies no subjection or diminution of the Son or the Holy Spirit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A6","text":"A divine person is a subsisting relation in the divine nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A7","text":"The proper name of a person signifies that by which that person is distinguished from all others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A8","text":"Paternity is the relation by which the Father is distinguished from all others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A9","text":"Paternity signifies completed generation, whereas generation signifies a process as in becoming.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A10","text":"The divine Word subsists in the divine nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A11","text":"The generator and the begotten in divine generation have the same form numerically.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A12","text":"A name is said primarily of that in which the whole notion of the name is perfectly preserved, and of the imperfect by likeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A13","text":"Creaturely filiation is a participation in the likeness of the natural Son.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A14","text":"The Father is principle not from a principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A15","text":"First and simple things can be signified through negations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0080","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A16","text":"Unbegotten properly belongs to the Father if it is understood of the person who is altogether not from another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A17","text":"The divine persons are distinguished by oppositions of relations without diversity of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:A18","text":"The procession of the Holy Spirit presupposes the generation of the Son in the order of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX1","text":"Principle, in Latin usage concerning divine things, is not a cause implying creaturely dependence or diversity of substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX2","text":"The Son and the Holy Spirit are not to be called principiated, if such speech suggests subjection or diminution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX3","text":"Principle does not signify temporal priority, although the name appears to be imposed from priority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX4","text":"The divine Father is not an accidental relation of a subsisting person constituted in some other way.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX5","text":"The Father is not denominated principally from generation as process, but from paternity as completed term.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0038","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX6","text":"The divine Word and Son are not said metaphorically, because the Word subsists in the divine nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX7","text":"Creaturely generation is neither the measure nor a more perfect form of divine generation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX8","text":"The essential paternity of the Trinity with respect to creatures does not have the same perfect notion as the personal paternity of the Father toward the Son.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX9","text":"Unbegottenness is not fontality, universality of authority, or a complex of paternity and spiration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX10","text":"Unbegotten does not signify an imperfective privation, nor is it simply the same as uncreated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0085","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX11","text":"Several unbegottens cannot stand with one divine nature and the merely relative distinction of persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:NX12","text":"Improcessibility does not constitute a proper name equally primary with unbegottenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D1","text":"The Father is properly called the principle of the Son and the Holy Spirit, because the persons proceed from him, without creaturely causality or temporal precedence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A1","CH_GGVC_P_25:A2","CH_GGVC_P_25:A3","CH_GGVC_P_25:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D2","text":"The authority of the Father as principle coheres with the full equality of the Son and the Holy Spirit; the order of origin does not pass into an order of domination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A4","CH_GGVC_P_25:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D3","text":"The name Father is the proper name of the person of the Father, because it signifies paternity as a subsisting and distinctive relation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A6","CH_GGVC_P_25:A7","CH_GGVC_P_25:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D4","text":"Father is a more fitting name than generator or begetter, because personal denomination comes from the completion of generation rather than from an incomplete process.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A7","CH_GGVC_P_25:A8","CH_GGVC_P_25:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D5","text":"Divine generation and divine paternity are said properly and primarily, because the Word subsists and the generator has the same form numerically as the begotten.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A10","CH_GGVC_P_25:A11","CH_GGVC_P_25:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D6","text":"The personal paternity of the Father toward the Son is prior to the essential paternity of the Trinity toward creatures, because the filiation of creatures is participated through the likeness of the natural Son.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A12","CH_GGVC_P_25:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D7","text":"The filiation of creatures is ordered by degrees according to vestige, image, grace, and glory, and none of these degrees equals the natural filiation of the Son.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A12","CH_GGVC_P_25:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D8","text":"Unbegottenness is a proper notion of the Father, because it manifests negatively that he is principle not from a principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A14","CH_GGVC_P_25:A15","CH_GGVC_P_25:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D9","text":"The Holy Spirit and the divine essence cannot be called unbegotten in the sense in which unbegotten is proper to the Father alone, because the Spirit is from another and the essence is communicated in the persons proceeding from the Father.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A14","CH_GGVC_P_25:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0083","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D10","text":"To posit two unbegottens would posit two divine natures and two gods, because they could not be distinguished by opposition of relations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A16","CH_GGVC_P_25:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:D11","text":"With passive generation removed from the Father, and the generation of the Son posited, it follows that the Father does not proceed by the procession of the Holy Spirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A14","CH_GGVC_P_25:A16","CH_GGVC_P_25:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:SD1","text":"Language concerning divine things becomes true not by retaining the creaturely mode of a word, but by separating the thing signified from the creaturely mode of imposition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A2","CH_GGVC_P_25:A3","CH_GGVC_P_25:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:SD2","text":"An order of origin can be a real asymmetry without a hierarchy of perfection, temporality, or power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A1","CH_GGVC_P_25:A4","CH_GGVC_P_25:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:SD3","text":"Divine personal plurality is explained through subsisting relations, while divine unity is preserved through exclusion of difference of nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A6","CH_GGVC_P_25:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:SD4","text":"Every creaturely paternity and every adoptive filiation is reordered as a secondary effect of the natural filiation of the Son.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A11","CH_GGVC_P_25:A12","CH_GGVC_P_25:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:SD5","text":"Negation in Trinitarian theology is not a mere diminution of a concept, but an instrument for knowing the first principle determined by no origin.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A14","CH_GGVC_P_25:A15","CH_GGVC_P_25:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:SD6","text":"The uniqueness of the Father as unbegotten is a systemic closure that simultaneously safeguards personal origin, unity of nature, and the impossibility of polytheism.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_25:A14","CH_GGVC_P_25:A16","CH_GGVC_P_25:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:DNX1","text":"It must be false that every real origin necessarily implies creaturely causality, distance in perfection, or substantial dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:DNX2","text":"It must be false that relation in divine things is a non-subsisting accident that merely affects a person already otherwise constituted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:DNX3","text":"It must be false that creaturely generation is the archetype by which the truth of divine paternity is measured.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:DNX4","text":"It must be false that a common and essential name is prior in reality to a proper name expressing a personal relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:DNX5","text":"It must be false that the bare negation of generation, without determination of personal origin, sufficiently distinguishes the Father.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:DNX6","text":"It must be false that several persons absolutely not from another can be distinguished by one divine nature and relative opposition alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:SNX1","text":"The system excludes a plurality of divine persons as a plurality of absolute and independently unbegotten principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:SNX2","text":"The system excludes the view that the first divine principle can be known through affirmatively exhaustive predicates without the service of negation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0080","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:SNX3","text":"The system excludes creaturely filiation as an immediate and autonomous relation not mediated by the likeness of the natural Son.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0058","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:SNX4","text":"The system excludes the possibility that unity of the divine essence can be combined with non-relative and non-originative personal distinctions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:FGS1","text":"Creaturely mode of imposition ≠ thing signified → the name of principle is freed from temporal priority and creaturely causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:FGS2","text":"Procession from something → principle; principle without precedence and diminution → Father, principle of equal persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:FGS3","text":"Subsisting relation + distinctive relation of paternity + completed generation → Father, proper name of the person of the Father.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:FGS4","text":"Subsisting Word + numerical identity of form → perfect divine generation → primary personal paternity; participation in the likeness of the Son → secondary essential paternity toward creatures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:FGS5","text":"Principle not from a principle + signification of first negatives → unbegottenness as a notion of the Father.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:FGS6","text":"Distinction through relations + unity of nature + Spirit proceeding from the order of generation → one unbegotten and exclusion of improcessibility as a primary property.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:AF1","text":"Cause / creature / prior → distinction of signification from imposition → principle as pure relation of origin → Father, principle without temporality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:AF2","text":"Accidental relation / metaphorical generation → subsisting relation / subsisting Word → completed paternity → Father, proper personal name.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:AF3","text":"Common paternity with respect to creatures → distinction of perfection and likeness → natural filiation of the Son → personal paternity primarily.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:AF4","text":"Ambiguous unbegotten → negation of absolute personal origin → Father, principle not from a principle → one unbegotten.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:ME1","text":"Principle is reclassified as a relation of origin that entails neither creaturely causation nor temporal succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:ME2","text":"Person is reclassified as a subsisting relation, such that a relative name can at once be a personal name.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:ME3","text":"Paternity is divided into natural personal paternity and essential paternity participated by creatures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:ME4","text":"Creaturely generation is reclassified from a model of truth into a diminished likeness of divine generation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:ME5","text":"Unbegotten is reclassified from an indeterminate negation into a sign of absolute personal origin and uniqueness of principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text treats the properties of the Father: principle, the name Father, the order of paternity, and unbegottenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text distinguishes persons through subsisting relations and origins, while retaining the essence as one and undivided.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Objections are resolved not by denying the terms, but by changing the rules by which divine terms are held to signify.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0085","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Divine origin is real but eternal; it distinguishes persons without diversity of nature, without time, and without degree of perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:MRT1","text":"The body of axioms suffices to restore the principal sequence: principle without creaturely causality, Father as subsisting relation, primary divine paternity, unique unbegottenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:MRT2","text":"The firmest hinge of the system is the distinction between the signification of a name and the mode of imposition, because through it words imposed creaturely are retained concerning divine things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:MRT3","text":"The distinction between cause and principle sustains the structure, but its force partly depends on a Latin semantic norm that is received here rather than demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:MRT4","text":"The subsistence of divine relation is a decisive premise, but within this question it is employed as a doctrine already established, not proven again from the foundation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:MRT5","text":"The precedence of personal paternity over essential paternity coheres, but it must contain the tension between terms received from creatures and divine truth through the distinction of thing and mode of signifying.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0042","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0056","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:MRT6","text":"Unbegottenness wishes to remain properly negative, but in order to be restricted to the Father alone it requires the condition of personal origin; this is the juncture where negation and affirmation closely meet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0079","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0080","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0083","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:MRT7","text":"The exclusion of improcessibility as a primary property depends on the order of nature by which the procession of the Spirit presupposes the generation of the Son; this order is assumed and applied here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_25:MRT8","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text: diminution of semantic confusions → constitution of personal relations → ordering of perfection and participation → negative closure of the unity of principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_25:P0087","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_25@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_GGVC_P_25:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 34","title":"First Part · Question 33","context":"Chapter 34","original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 33"},"original_title":"Prima pars · Quaestio 33","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26","layer":"CH","title":"First Part · Question 34","order":35,"parents":["RAW_GGVC_P_26"],"revision":"CH_GGVC_P_26@R001","sha256":"9db87b74ea0c6e111c25615de43557c6cbb70b00877917b6c672ba34e4cf6e7a","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:AS1","text":"Quaestio determinat utrum nomen verbi in divinis sit personale, proprium filii, et ad creaturas relatum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:AS2","text":"Ambiguitas vocis verbum resolvitur distinguendo conceptum interiorem, vocem significantem, imaginem vocis et usum figurativum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:AS3","text":"Verbum proprie dictum est conceptus intellectus ab intellegente procedens; processio igitur originem importat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:AS4","text":"Cum personae divinae originibus distinguantur, verbum proprie dictum non essentiam communem sed personam procedentem significat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:AS5","text":"Processio intellectualis divina generatio est; ideo verbum est proprium nomen filii, non novae proprietatis additio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:AS6","text":"Unicum verbum, ex unica divina intellectione procedens, Patrem exprimit et creaturas cognitas repraesentat atque operative respicit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:AS7","text":"Relatio verbi ad creaturas aeterna manet, quia sequitur actionem intellectualem in Deo manentem, non actionem temporalem in effectum transeuntem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:A1","text":"Verbum proprie dictum est conceptus interior intellectus, non vox exterior neque res voce significata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:A2","text":"Conceptus intellectualis, ut verbum, a notitia concipientis procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:A3","text":"Personae divinae secundum originem distinguuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:A4","text":"In Deo esse et intellegere sunt idem; quicquid divinae naturae inest, subsistit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0047","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:A5","text":"Intellegere directe importat habitudinem ad rem intellectam, dicere autem principaliter importat habitudinem ad verbum conceptum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:A6","text":"Processio divina per modum intellectus est generatio, et persona sic procedens est filius.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:A7","text":"Nomen verbi principaliter relationem ad dicentem significat, relationem autem ad creaturas consequenter includere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:A8","text":"Deus uno actu se ipsum et omnes creaturas cognoscit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:A9","text":"Verbum conceptum repraesentat omne id quod actu intelligitur; unitas actus intelligendi unitatem verbi fundat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:A10","text":"Scientia Dei de se est cognoscitiva tantum, de creaturis autem est cognoscitiva et factiva.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:A11","text":"Relatio ad creaturas ex actione in agente manente non est ex tempore, quamvis relatio ex actione in externum effectum transeunte ex tempore dicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:A12","text":"Verbum Dei entia exprimit et operative respicit; non-entia autem exprimit et manifestat, non factive constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:NX1","text":"Verbum divinum non est vox exterior, imaginatio vocis, nec effectus verbo imperatus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:NX2","text":"Verbum metaphorice dictum non evacuat necessitatem verbi proprie dicti, quia ipsa metaphora manifestationem per verbum praesupponit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:NX3","text":"Verbum non est nomen essentiale commune Patri, Filio et Spiritui Sancto.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:NX4","text":"Verbum non est cogitatio inquisitiva vel mutabilis, quia in Deo non est successiva quaesitio veritatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:NX5","text":"Dicere divinum non est simpliciter idem ac intellegere divinum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0023","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0027","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:NX6","text":"Verbum filii non est accidens, virtus animae, aut effectus separatus a natura divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0047","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:NX7","text":"Verbum non multiplicatur secundum multitudinem creaturarum vel idearum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:NX8","text":"Relatio verbi ad creaturas non significat processionem verbi a creaturis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0076","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:NX9","text":"Relatio verbi ad creaturas non est relatio temporalis creatoris ad effectum iam productum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:D1","text":"Verbum divinum proprie sumptum est nomen personale, quia conceptus procedens originem importat et originis ratio personas distinguit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_26:A1","CH_GGVC_P_26:A2","CH_GGVC_P_26:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:D2","text":"Solus Pater proprie est dicens, quia solus est principium a quo verbum personaliter procedit; tota tamen Trinitas verbo intelligitur et dicitur sicut res intellecta.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_26:A3","CH_GGVC_P_26:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:D3","text":"Verbum divinum subsistens est, quia conceptus divinus non cadit extra esse divinum, quod est ipsum intellegere subsistens.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_26:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0047","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:D4","text":"Verbum est proprium nomen filii, quia eadem processio intellectualis quae verbum constituit generationem filialem constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_26:A2","CH_GGVC_P_26:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:D5","text":"Filius intelligit essentialiter, sed non est dicens ut verbum producens, quia intellegere commune est naturae, dicere autem originem verbi designat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_26:A4","CH_GGVC_P_26:A5","CH_GGVC_P_26:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:D6","text":"Nomen verbi novam proprietatem personalem non addit, sed eandem nativitatem filialem sub ratione immaterialis generationis significat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_26:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:D7","text":"Unicum verbum Patrem et creaturas exprimit, quia una divina cognitio se ipsum et omnia alia uno actu comprehendit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_26:A8","CH_GGVC_P_26:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:D8","text":"Verbum creaturas operative respicit sine mutatione temporali in Deo, quia ratio factiva sequitur aeternam scientiam et actionem immanentem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_26:A10","CH_GGVC_P_26:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_GGVC_P_26:D9","text":"Non-entia in verbo continentur ut cognoscibilia et manifestabilia, non ut effectus producendi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_26:A9","CH_GGVC_P_26:A10","CH_GGVC_P_26:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_GGVC_P_26:P0080","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Der